WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
MEETING MINUTES
This collection contains minutes from January 6, 1998 to March 18, 2025
1998
January 6, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved January 20, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council chamber on Tuesday,
January 6, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, William Meuer, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield,
Lanphier, John Lien. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff and about 22 persons in
the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
   January 13, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Duhl indicated that he would be
unable to attend. President Holland reminded the Trustees about the dinner to
be held at the Historical Society prior to the Council meeting.
   January 20, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Lien indicated that he might be
unable to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
   December 16, 1997, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the
minutes and was seconded by Trustee Derning. Trustee Duhl suggested some
changes to the draft. A voice vote was taken, and the minutes, as amended, were
unanimously adopted.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
   Ordinance No. MC-199-97 - Foxdale GFA Amendments - Public Hearing and
Adoption. Cindy McCammack, Director of Community Development briefly reviewed
the provisions of this ordinance which applies to those properties on the west
side of Foxdale that lost thirty feet of their lots’ depth to the railroad
right-of-way. She noted that the ordinance would not increase the total,
allowable bulk on these lots; it simply provides the property owners with an
additional design option. She also pointed out that the draft ordinance had
been amended by adding language which would allow any bulk that is not used in
the rear-quarter of the lot to be included in the principal building.
   President Holland declared the Public Hearing open. She then recognized Ms.
Jeanne Beckman, 840 Foxdale, who spoke against the proposed amendment. Ms.
Beckman stated that she felt the amendment discouraged the preservation of
existing neighborhoods and older homes. She expressed worry about the potential
ramifications on property values and the implications for future development of
neighborhood properties. She also voiced some concerns about the timing of the
Public Hearing and the procedures employed by the Village to notify neighbors
about it.
   Mr. James Egan, 555 Cherry St., Mr. John Larson, 756 Foxdale, and Mr. Paul
Allen, 812 Lincoln, echoed Ms. Beckman’s concerns. Mr. Egan asked the Council
to initiate an independent review of the situation, and Mr. Larson asked for
further study by and compromise options from qualified land use planners. Mr.
Allen urged the Council to be cautious in its actions.
   Messrs. Jim Henderson and George Friestedt also commented on the issue. Mr.
Henderson urged the Council to vote against this amendment and return to the
zoning ordinance as a whole to study the density issue and determine what can
be quantified and consistently applied throughout the Village.
   Trustee Lanphier asked for clarification of the procedures followed to
notify Foxdale residents of the Public Hearing. Attorney Janega recounted the
notification requirements and said that appropriate procedures had been
followed in this instance.
   President Holland disputed audience comments that the Council was rushing to
make a decision in this matter, noting that it had been in various stages of
discussion for nearly a year. She affirmed the Trustees’ understanding that
this was an important issue affecting many residents and their desire to reach
an equitable solution.
   Mr. Pat Dowdle inquired as to the possibility of the homeowners buying back
the “taken” property. Trustee Duhl commented that this was the hardest issue he
has had to deal with during his tenure on the Council and that in his opinion
the Trustees had deliberated in a responsible fashion. He mentioned the
necessity and difficulty of balancing the interests of the community against
the interests of an individual or group of individuals and the impossibility of
pleasing everyone. He expressed his desire to implement policies in keeping
with the wishes of the community and stated his support for the proposed
amendment, citing it as a fair and rational compromise.
   President Holland closed the Public Hearing and solicited comments from the
Trustees.
   Trustees Derning and Hilton reiterated Mr. Duhl’s remarks and indicated
their support for the proposed amendment.
   Trustee Lanphier noted that although she initially had been opposed to this
zoning text amendment, she had come to realize that it makes sense given the
circumstances and acknowledged her support for the proposal.
   Trustee Meuer agreed with his fellow Trustees in view of the fact that the
affected homeowners cannot purchase the additional property at this time.
   Trustee Lien expressed his disagreement. Citing the lack of resultant
density problems and fairness concerns, he expressed his belief that the
residents on the west side of Foxdale ought to be given full credit for the
“taken” property.
   Trustee Duhl moved to adopt the proposed amendments to the ordinance as
noted in the red-lined version of the ordinance in the agenda packets. He was
seconded by Trustee Meuer. By roll call vote, the amendments were adopted.
Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, and Hilton. Nays: Trustee Lien.
Absent: None.
   Trustee Hilton then moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended, and was
seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: Trustee Lien.
Absent: None.
   Resolution No. 1403-98 - Hahn Subdivision - Adoption. Village Manager
Williams directed the Council's attention to an updated copy of the Resolution
and asked Attorney Janega to enumerate some of the technical changes included
therein. Then, Ms. McCammack briefly reviewed the provisions of the final plat.
She pointed out a substantive revision to the Resolution as originally drafted
which specifies that as a condition of final plat approval, the fence along the
rear (west) property line be replaced.
   President Holland invited Mr. Jeff Greenspan to speak on behalf of the
Hahns. He indicated that he had reviewed the Resolution with the Hahns and that
it was acceptable to them. He asked the Council for an extension of time, to a
year and a half, for the Hahns to remove the tennis court as required in order
to record the plat.
   Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the Resolution as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously, thereby, adopting
the subdivision but denying the Hahns’ request for an extension of time. Ayes:
Trustees Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton and Lien. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
   Trustee Derning commended the staff for its outstanding work on this issue.
   Ordinance No. M-509-97 - Vacation of Rights-of-Way in Crow Island Park -
Adoption. President Holland asked for a motion to adopt this resolution.
Trustee Hilton so moved and was seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier,
Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Ordinance No. M-516-98 - Hibbard Road Jurisdictional Transfer - Introduction
Manager Williams explained that this jurisdictional transfer needs to be
approved in order to permit the Village to complete two utility projects
involving placing pipes underneath Hibbard Road between Winnetka Avenue and
Tower Road. He outlined the improvements to be made and noted that IDOT, under
whose jurisdiction this portion of roadway currently falls, refuses to allow
these installations without the transfer. He pointed out that President Holland
had already signed a letter of intent outlining an agreement wherein the
Village would take over the roadway and IDOT would pay up to $200,000 towards
the Village’s resurfacing of the roadway, in addition to allowing the utility
projects to proceed.
   Trustee Meuer moved to introduce the ordinance which would add Hibbard Road
between Tower Road and Winnetka Avenue to the Municipal Street System. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   Resolution No. R-1406-98 - 1998 MFT Transfer for Maintenance of Streets -
Adoption. Mr. Williams explained that in keeping with past practice this
Resolution would authorize the expenditure of $125,000 in MFT funds for street
maintenance. Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt the Resolution and was seconded by
Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Duhl, Derning, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Resolution No. R-1404-98 - Berlet, Pilipovic and Wengler Subdivision -
Adoption. Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt the Resolution approving the
subdivision. She was seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion
passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Lien, Meuer, Hilton, Derning and
Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Resolution No. R-1407-98 - Electric Strategic Plan - Adoption. Village
Manager Williams reviewed the previously discussed Ten-Year Electric Strategic
Plan. He explained that the attached Resolution would amend that plan by
deleting the early retirement incentive option and adding a 4% rate decrease in
2001. He mentioned that first step toward achieving the goals embodied in the
Plan would be included in the Preliminary Budget to be distributed in late
January.
   Bryan McInturff, Director of Water & Electric added his strong endorsement
of the Electric Strategic Plan.
   Trustee Meuer moved to adopt the Electric Strategic Plan, as amended, and
was seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll call vote, the Resolution was
unanimously adopted. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Lien, Lanphier, Hilton, Duhl and
Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Resolution No. R-1408-98 - Examination and Release of Executive Session
Minutes - Adoption. Mr. Williams explained that pursuant to state law the
Village Council is required to examine and release Executive Session minutes
which no longer require confidentiality. This Resolution would cover those
minutes from December 17, 1996, through November 11, 1997. He noted that there
were eleven sets of minutes which would be retained for confidentiality
reasons.
   Trustee Duhl moved to approve the Executive Session Minutes from December
17, 1996, through November 11, 1997, as drafted. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the Resolution releasing all of the minutes
except those specified therein. He was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By voice
vote, the motion passed unanimously.
      Resolution No. R-1405-98 - Compensation for Village Attorney - Adoption.
Trustee Lien moved to adopt the Resolution setting and approving the salary for
the full time Village Attorney. Trustee Derning seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Lanphier,
Hilton, Meuer, Derning and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
   Warrant Lists Nos. 1059 and 1060. Trustee Derning moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1059 in the total amount of $427,704.94 and Warrant List No. 1060 in
the total amount of $586,939.66. Trustee Lien seconded the motion. Mr. Lien
commented that he had reviewed the lists and found everything to be in order.
By roll call vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, Hilton and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
   Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported that the preliminary budget for
Fiscal Year 1998-99 would be distributed to the Trustees by January 20th and
asked the Trustees to reserve the three tentative dates selected for the review
sessions. He also announced that Ronald Earl, a former Vice President of the
Chattanooga Electric Power Board, had been selected as the next General Manager
of the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency.
XI. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)
(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Hilton seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Duhl, Hilton, Lien, Meuer, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None. Motion
carried unanimously. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:35 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:03 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Appointments.
   President Holland appointed Mr. David Wright to the Forestry Commission to
replace Mr. Howard Haffenberg whose term expired on October 31, 1997.
XIII. Adjournment.
   Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:07 p.m.
January 13, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved February 3, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 13, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, William Meuer and John
Lien. Absent: Trustee Michael Duhl Also present: Plan Commission
representatives, Jack Wentz, John Berwanger, and Phyllis Myers, Village Manger
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Cindy McCammack, and Police Chief William Gallagher.
II. Plan Commission Recommendation re: Height Standards in the Commercial
Zoning Districts.
Ms. Myers reviewed the specifics of the report prepared by the Plan Commission
regarding its recommendations on building heights in the Village’s business
districts. During her presentation she explored the concept of involving
builders in the preplanning process and raised the idea of offering incentives
to builders to participate in such a process. She distributed copies of
memorandums received from Wade Judge, LaSalle Advisors, addressing incentives
employed in the City of Chicago to guide real estate developers.
   Ms. Myers, Mr. Wentz, and Mr. Berwanger responded to questions from the
Trustees and stressed the importance of being able to evaluate each development
proposal on an individual basis in order to maintain flexibility and control.
Mr. Williams expressed his concerns about a development impact fee for off
street requirements. He noted that a developer would only pay for part of the
cost of a parking structure and the Village and/or business district would pay
the remaining amount. Secondly, he stated that if the Village adopted these
fees, the Village should specifically identify off street parking projects that
would be built. Ms. Janega advised Council is was illegal to just collect the
fees without spending them on a specific project.
   Trustee Meuer indicated his support for the height recommendations made by
the Plan Commission but expressed his concern about other aspects of
construction such as style, trim, materials, etc., saying that he doesn’t want
to see the Village control height at the expense of these features.
   After extended discussion of the height recommendations and building
standards, it was decided that Ms. Janega should proceed to draft an ordinance
lifting the current building moratorium and setting 2-1/2 stories, or 35 feet,
as the maximum height limit in the commercial districts. It was noted that
builders could still apply for a Special Use Permit should they so desire.
   Ms. McCammack suggested that the Village employ a consultant to assist in
the development of design guidelines, including standards for special use
permits, which would apply throughout the Village.
III. Staff Recommendations re: Off Street Parking Requirements in the
Commercial Zoning Districts.
Mr. Williams commented that the issue of parking is a key to the development of
regulations and standards for the Village. To assure that the commercial
districts remain economically healthy, he recommended that the Village continue
to provide offstreet parking to meet the needs of businesses on the first floor
(i.e., ground level). He noted that the Village cannot provide parking for any
uses on floors above the ground level. Thus, staff has recommended requiring
the developer to be responsible for providing offstreet parking for the 2nd,
3rd and 4th floors. Ms. McCammack then discussed the staff’s recommendations in
detail and answered questions from the Trustees.
IV. Liquor Service by 18 Year Olds or 21 Year Olds.
Chief Gallagher reported that in the absence of specific legislation by the
Council, state law permits individuals under the age of 21 to serve alcoholic
beverages. He shared his concerns about this policy and urged the Council to
adopt an ordinance requiring individuals serving alcohol to be at least 21. By
consensus the Council agreed to move forward with a public hearing on the
subject in the near future.
V. Review of Green Sheets.
Manager Williams pointed out that the information contained on the green sheets
could be obtained easily elsewhere via other media. Citing the duplication of
work as a waste of Village resources and the fact that in at least one instance
the green sheets were interpreted as being an official Village document,
resulting in substantial confusion, Mr. Williams recommended that the green
sheets be discontinued. He indicated that in the future copies of not only the
Agenda but also the entire Village Council packet would be put on file with the
Reference Librarian and the Agenda also would be posted on the Library’s
bulletin board.
The meeting adjourned at 10:35 p.m.
January 20, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved February 3, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council chamber on Tuesday,
January 20, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield, Lanphier, and John
Lien. Absent: Trustee William Meuer. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Director of Public Works Ken Keene, and about six persons in
the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
   February 3, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they would be able to attend.
   February 10, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
   January 6, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. He was seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
   Ordinance No. M-516-98 - Hibbard Road Jurisdictional Transfer - Adoption.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt this ordinance, and he was seconded by Trustee
Lanphier. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Meuer.
   President Holland called for a motion authorizing her to sign the documents
effecting the transfer. Trustee Hilton so moved, seconded by Trustee Lanphier.
By roll call vote, all of the Trustees voted in favor of the motion. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Lanphier, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Meuer.
   Resolution No. R-1409-98 - Providing for Village Utility Work on    Roads
under State Jurisdiction - Adoption. Director of Public Works Ken Keene
explained that this Resolution could be filed with the State of Illinois in
lieu of individual surety bonds, thereby permitting the Village to perform work
on roads under the state’s jurisdiction and/or on utilities on State rights-of-
way.
   The motion to adopt the Resolution was made by Trustee Lanphier and seconded
by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote the Resolution was unanimously adopted.
Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Meuer.
   Resolution No. R-1410-98 - Authorizing Expenditure of MFT Funds for
Construction Engineering Contract for Oak and Cherry Street Bridge
Rehabilitation - Adoption. Mr. Keene briefly reviewed the provisions of the
contract with Ciorba Group to perform construction engineering and contract
administration for the Oak Street and Cherry Street bridge rehabilitation
projects. He pointed out that the project, including the engineering, was
completed with 80% Federal funding, and explained that this Resolution would
authorize the Village to pay $17,280 from Motor Fuel Tax funds for its share of
the contract, including its extension.
   Trustee Lanphier questioned whether the funds had already been included in
the Budget. Manager Williams confirmed that this item already had been
accounted for. He also announced that the American Public Works Association,
Chicago Chapter, had notified the Village that these bridges had been selected
as the recipient of the “Project of the Year - Transportation” and were
eligible for consideration at the national level.
   Trustee Lien moved to adopt the Resolution. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lien, Hilton, Lanphier, Derning and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Meuer.
   Ordinance No. M-515-98 - Alley Vacation and Plat of Consolidation - 333
Fairview Ave. - Introduction. Mr. Williams reviewed the details of the proposed
plat of consolidation. He explained that the consolidation executes both the
vacation of an improved alley and consolidation of the vacated alley with the
existing parcel of land. He pointed out that this was in keeping with Village
policy and past practice and noted that a public utility easement had been
provided for.
   Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the
Ordinance. By voice vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lanphier, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Meuer.
   President Holland announced that there would be a Public Hearing on the
Ordinance at the next Council meeting scheduled for February 3, 1998.
   Ordinance No. MC-200-98 - Commercial Zoning District Text Amendments
Building Height and Parking Requirements - Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed
the provisions of the proposed ordinance setting the maximum building height in
the C-1 and C-2 Commercial Zoning Districts at 35 feet, or 2-1/2 stories, as
proposed by the Plan Commission and establishing minimum parking requirements
and standards for future developments. She distributed drawings of roof types
and definitions of relevant terms, as provided in the Code, for the Trustees to
review. She presented slides of Village business districts showing the
prevalent size and style of building(s) and the different floor configurations.
The slides demonstrated her point that it is what is seen from the street that
is important, not necessarily the specific number of stories since that is
sometimes difficult to determine.
   Mr. George Noyes, 381 Elder Lane, urged the Trustees to vote in favor of the
proposed introduction and asked them to consider making the same provisions for
the B-2 Zoning District.
   Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lien.
By unanimous voice vote, the motion carried.
   President Holland announced that a Public Hearing on the Ordinance would be
conducted at the February 3, 1998, Regular Meeting of the Village Council.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
   Warrant Lists Nos. 1061 and 1062. Trustee Lien indicated that all of his
questions with regard to the warrant lists had been answered satisfactorily. He
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1061 in the total amount of $230,539.12 and
Warrant List No. 1062 in the total amount of $407,045.88. Trustee Lanphier
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the warrant lists were approved
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, and Hilton. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Meuer.
   DK Recycling Monthly Report. The Council reviewed the monthly report from DK
Recycling Systems, Inc.
   Status Report of Capital Improvements. The Trustees reviewed the status
reports prepared by each of the Department Heads with regard to capital
improvement projects. Manager Williams noted that most of the projects which
had been budgeted for had been completed with the exception of the Pine and
Hibbard Streets pump station. He indicated that this item would be included in
the 1998-99 Budget.
   Upcoming Recycling Bids. The Director of Public Works, Ken Keene, informed
the Council that the Village’s current recycling contract with BFI expires on
March 31, 1998, and that in order to continue the current contract at the
present level of service, BFI would be requiring substantial rate increases. He
explained that this was due to the fact that over 60% of the people recycling
are in violation of the rules for recycling creating extra work and problems
for the collectors. He asked for guidance from the Council as to whether to
continue to provide the current level of service at the substantially greater
cost or to solicit bids from other providers who would strictly enforce the
recycling rules in order to keep their costs down.
   Trustee Lanphier expressed her opinion that because BFI has continued to
provide their services despite the continued violations, there has been no
incentive for residents to sort appropriately or pay attention to the recycling
rules. She voiced her belief that the Village is sending wrong message to
residents and should begin to enforce the rules by not picking up incorrectly
sorted material.
   President Holland and Trustee Hilton concurred and noted that it appears
that people are using recycling as a second garbage pick up.
   Trustee Duhl indicated that given BFI’s excellent service despite the
violations, he would like to see them have the opportunity to make a
competitive bid. He suggested that they strictly enforce the recycling rules
for the next several months in order to better evaluate their projected costs
under those circumstances.
   Manager Williams pointed out that while there was no objection to
negotiating an extension with BFI, it was also important to check out the
marketplace.
   By consensus of opinion, it was agreed that BFI should be instructed to
strictly enforce the recycling rules, and that this policy should be widely
publicized in order to alert residents.
X.Reports.
   Village Trustees. Trustee Hilton reported that the Chamber of Commerce
Commercial Development Subcommittee had met and reviewed the Fire Code. He also
announced that the new issue of Winnetka Shop was now available
   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that he and staff had attended a
general meeting outlining the process for appealing maps and designations for
flood plains in the community. He indicated that the Village intends to file an
appeal based upon several technical issues which have been identified as
incorrect in an effort to minimize the impact of the Federal regulations on the
Village.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)
(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Meuer.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:50 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, and Lien. Absent: Trustee
Meuer. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the motion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 9:45
p.m.
January 27, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 27, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien
and William Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manger Doug Williams,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Police Chief William Gallagher, Hanna Sullivan,
Assistant Finance Director, Gene Kalvitis, Police Lieutenant, Frank Siwak,
Police Lieutenant, and one person in the audience.
II. Review 1998-99 Preliminary Budget.
Mr. George Noyes, 381 Elder Lane, urged the Council to stop funding the
Winnetka Historical Museum. He stated his belief that this was not an
appropriate appropriation of taxpayers’ money.
   Village Manager Williams summarized the Preliminary Budget as follows:
      (1)   It is fiscally conservative as the increases in operating expenses
have been minimized;
      (2)   All utilities (electric, water, sewer, and refuse) have positive
net incomes;
      (3)   There is an increase of one full time employee’s position relative
to last year’s budget.
   Village Manager Williams identified the four following key policy issues:
      (1)   The Budget proposes a 5% decrease in electric rates;
      (2)   The Budget proposes a 3.2% increase in property tax, plus new
development;
      (3)   The Budget authorizes a 3.25% increase in wages;
      (4)   Due to the Pine and Hibbard storm sewer project, the cash in the
General Fund is projected to decrease by approximately $445,000.
   Village Manager Williams briefly reviewed the revenues and expenses for the
General Fund, the net income for the four utilities, as well as the Capital
Improvements Plans for the General Fund and all utilities. He noted that the
Capital Improvements Plan for 1998-99 is in conformance with previous five year
projections.
   Finance Director Ed McKee reviewed the General Fund revenues and the
expenses in the Finance and General Administration area. Mr. McKee also
reviewed recent accomplishments in the Finance Department as well as projected
goals for Fiscal Year 1998-99.
   Police Chief Gallagher reviewed the Preliminary Budget for the Police
Department as well as the department’s recent accomplishments and future goals.
The meeting adjourned at 10:10 p.m.
February 2, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Monday,
February 2, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien and William Meuer.
Absent: Trustee Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manger Doug Williams,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Fire Chief Ron Colpaert, Director of Public Works
Ken Keene, Village Engineer Steve Saunders, and Community Development Director
Cindy McCammack.
II. Fire Department Budget.
Fire Chief Colpaert reviewed the accomplishments of the Fire Department for the
last fiscal year as well as the department’s goals for next year. Chief
Colpaert reviewed the operating and capital expenses for the department.
Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:45 p.m.
   Manager Williams noted that in Fiscal Year 1999-2000 the Village is
projecting to purchase an ambulance for a $138,000, and in Fiscal Year 2000-
2001, the Village will be replacing one of its fire engines for approximately
$290,000.
   Chief Colpaert reviewed the benefits of “Red Center” which is a regional
dispatch center for fire and emergency medical services. Red Center also
provides emergency medical dispatch. Chief Colpaert outlined the benefits of
this consolidated fire and emergency medical dispatch service. By consensus,
the Village Council directed the Village Manager and Fire Chief to proceed with
joining Red Center.
III. Public Works Department Budget.
Public Works Director Keene reviewed the principle accomplishments in Fiscal
Year 1997-98 for the department as well as its goals for the upcoming year. He
reviewed Public Works staffing which has remained stable at thirty-six. (There
were forty-two full time employees in 1992.) Mr. Keene reviewed the operational
cost summaries and capital improvements summaries for the department which also
includes the Motor Fuel Tax Fund, the Sewer Fund, and Refuse Fund.
Village Engineer Saunders reviewed the status of leachate at the Village’s
landfill. He explained how the Village is managing this leachate and identified
potential problems within the next five to ten years.
Trustee Lanphier left the meeting at 10:00 p.m.
IV. Community Development Department.
Community Development Director McCammack reviewed the accomplishments of the
department for this fiscal year as well as its goals for next fiscal year. She
reported that she would be reducing some of the proposed costs in next year’s
budget in order to hire a consultant to develop design guidelines. It is
expected that these design guidelines could cost approximately $20,000.
   The Village Council requested that Staff, during the next fiscal year,
review the zoning fees for simple variances versus very complex rezoning issues
such as the Winnetka Avenue Development project.
V. Squad Car Purchase.
Village Manager Williams distributed a memo from Police Chief Gallagher which
indicated that the cut off date for purchasing 1998 squad cars is March 1,
1998. By consensus, the Village Council authorized the Village Manager to place
an order this fiscal year for squad cars that will be purchased after April 1,
1998.
The meeting adjourned at 11:00 p.m.
February 3, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved February 17, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council chamber on Tuesday,
February 3, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and Bill Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Chief of Police William Gallagher and about 75 persons in the
audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that a new item, ratification of Telecommunications
Infrastructure Maintenance Fee Ordinance, had been added to the Agenda. All of
the Trustees then approved the Agenda, as amended.
IV. Quorum.
February 10, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
planned to attend.
February 17, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees indicated that they
planned to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
January 13, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Lien moved to adopt the Minutes
including suggestions made by Trustees Hilton and Lanphier. Trustee Lanphier
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
January 20, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the Minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-515-98 – Alley Vacation and Plat of Consolidation - 333
Fairview Ave. - Public Hearing and Adoption. President Holland declared the
Public Hearing open and asked Manager Williams to briefly review the history
and contents of the Ordinance. He explained that the Ordinance provides for
executing both the vacation of an unimproved alley and consolidation of the
vacated alley with the existing parcel. There being no comments from the floor,
President Holland closed the Public Hearing.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the Ordinance. he was seconded by Trustee Lien.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Derning, Lanpher, Meuer, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-200-98 – Commercial Zoning District Text Amendments: Building
Height and Parking Requirements – Public Hearing and Adoption. Announcing that
the Council was sitting as a committee of the whole, President Holland declared
open the Public Hearing on the proposed text amendments and whether or not the
commercial building moratorium ought to be extended.
Ms. McCammack read aloud the public notice which had been published regarding
the Public Hearing.
President Holland recognized Mr. Peter, Cummins, 720 Roger Avenue, Kenilworth,
who was sworn in by Village Clerk Williams. Mr. Cummins identified himself as
the developer who had purchased the Caledonian Building. he briefly reviewed
his plans for renovating the building and urged the Council to lift the
moratorium so that he could proceed. He noted that his plans would be in
compliance with the proposed new provisions and that extending the moratorium
would be expensive and make it difficult to complete the project during the
upcoming building season.
There being no other speakers from the floor, President Holland closed the
Public Hearing.
Trustee Derning inquired whether the Village staff felt there was any reason to
extend the moratorium. Manager Williams responded that there was none. Trustee
Derning then moved that the commercial building moratorium be lifted. His
motion was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Duhl.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Attorney Janega reviewed several proposed amendments to the introduced
ordinance and the reasons therefore before Trustee Hilton moved to amend the
Ordinance as suggested. He was seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll call vote,
the amendments were approved. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning,
Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lien, then moved to adopt the Ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Zoning Map Amendment and Variances: 454-462 Winnetka Ave. – Continued Public
Hearing. Announcing that the Council was sitting as a committee of the whole,
President Holland declared open this continued Public Hearing on the proposed
Map Amendment to change the zoning for this property from a C-1 (Limited
Retail) Commercial to B-2 Multi-Family Residential Zoning District.
Ms. McCammack then read aloud the public notice which had bee published
regarding the Public Hearing, and Mr. Williams administered the oath to all
those individuals who indicated that they intended to speak before the Council.
Before proceeding, President Holland clarified a procedural point for the
audience. She explained that under Winnetka’s Zoning Ordinance, the findings of
the Design Review Board and Plan Commission on the proposed development were to
have been considered at this hearing. However, because the Plan Commission was
not able to consider the development proposal until a special meeting scheduled
for the following night, February 4, 1998, the council would not be able to
complete this Public Hearing that evening. She announced that the Council would
proceed to hear all of the other evidence and then continue the Public Hearing
for the sole purpose of receiving and considering the Plan Commission’s
comments and hearing any related testimony.
President Holland asked representatives of the developer of the proposed
project, Conversion Marketing and Management, to make their presentation. The
first speaker was Mr. Jim Hickman, Vice President, who introduced the other
members of the developer’s team who would be speaking:
   1.   Neil Stenholt, President of Conversion Marketing and Management, Inc.;
   2.   James M. Plunkard, of Hartshorne & Plunkard, Ltd., an adaptive reuse
architect;
   3.   Kenneth W. Boxworth, of Horwood, Marcus & Berk, their attorney;
   4.   Jerry Lindgren, of Kening, Lindgren, O’Hara, Aboona, Inc., a traffic
study expert;
   5.   Michael S. Marous, of MaRous & Company, an MAI appraiser specializing
in value impact.
Mr. Hickman went on to provide a broad overview of the project and outline the
developer’s arguments in favor of the proposal. He expressed the developer’s
belief that the status quo cannot be maintained indefinitely as the building
continues to deteriorate and is too expensive to repair. He referred to a
report from Property Technics (at page 4-31 of the Agenda materials) and
pointed out that maintenance of critical building functions had been neglected.
Acknowledging that no single solution will satisfy everyone, he reviewed the
developer’s efforts to be sensitive to the community’s needs and responsive to
the community’s input in evolving the project’s design. Mr. Hickman expressed
the developer’s position that it is seeking to “down-zone” the property to B-2,
a less intense use than under current C-1 zoning. He also stated that the
developer believes that the proposed plan does not create any greater massing
or bulk than that now allowed by the Zoning Ordinance. He further asserted that
the proposed redevelopment is “entirely consistent” with the Village’s
Comprehensive Plan.
Mr. Plunkard explained the design elements of the plan. He acknowledged that
the building is not in compliance with setback requirements on Winnetka Avenue
and Tempel Court and that the developer’s goal was to push the mass of the
proposed building back behind the existing building. He presented a modified
plan that reflects changes made in an effort to respond to the staff’s
recommendation of two parking spaces per unit and to the preliminary comments
of the Design Review Board. He explained that the proposed site coverage was
now 75%. One of the design changes he pointed out was a change from the
bricked-in window openings on the first floor to windows with etching. He also
stated the developer was working on a plan to screen the balconies so that
objects on the balconies would not be visible from the street.
Following Mr. Plunkard’s presentation, Mr. Bosworth addressed the Comprehensive
Plan. He stated the developer’s position that the proposed redevelopment is
consistent with the current Plan.
The next speaker was Mr. Neil Stenholt, President of Conversion Marketing, who
reiterated his commitment to the property and his desire to save the existing
building.
The final speaker on behalf of the developer was Mr. Michael Marous who
explained portions of his market study of the property (at page 4-8 of the
Agenda package). Mr. Marous referred to what he called the “functional
obsolescence” of the property. According to Mr. Marous, the developer’s
proposal reflects a reduction in density on the property, which would increase
the value of other properties in the vicinity. He estimated that there is a
difference in value of the property of approximately one-half million dollars
if developed as multi-family residential rather than commercial property.
President Holland then recognized other members of the audience who wished to
comment on the proposal. She began with Mr. and Mrs. George Noyes, 381 Elder
Lane. Mrs. Noyes presented the Council with copies of a petition opposing the
development signed by numerous Village residents. Mr. Noyes read aloud a letter
he wrote taking issue with petitioner’s arguments and urging the Council to
deny approval of the requested variations and change in zoning classification.
Mr. Socratis Zavitsanos, a tailor currently occupying commercial space in the
existing building expressed his desire to stay in the community and submitted a
petition signed by patrons urging the tailor to stay in Winnetka.
Among the residents who urged the Council to reject the proposal were Pat
Harwood, 255 Poplar, Margo Funk, 397 Sunset, Linda Goodwin, 82 Tempel Court,
Jim Connors, 82 Tempel Court, Lurie Myers, 432 Sunset, Terry Duffy, 1559
Bertling, Chris Jacobsen, 84 Tempel Court, Dan McInture, 86 Tempel Court, and
Jeanne Pence, 330 Walnut. They
expressed the importance to the neighbors of the commercial space in the
building, their desire to maintain the community feeling of the neighborhood,
and their concerns about parking and overbuilding. They pointed out the
desirability of maintaining the availability of small, relatively inexpensive
rental units in the Village and stressed what they described as the
overwhelming opposition by the community to the proposed development.
Rhonda Plunkett, 80 Warwick, questioned the effect of the proposed development
on the trees and wildlife on the Green Bay Trail. She pointed out that the
proposed development would be higher than the railroad embankments, which it
will abut along the west property line.
Mr. Bob Zelinski, 72 Warwick, expressed concerns about the current plans,
especially with regard to lot coverage, and questioned which specific plan was
being pursued by the developer. Mr. Zelinski raised questions about the lot
coverage and distributed copies of an analysis of the proposed plan prepared by
JCS Real Estate Services of Highland Park, which estimated the proposed lot
coverage to be anywhere from 76.2% to 79%.
Mr. Bosworth objected to the introduction of this document as hearsay evidence.
President Holland noted his objection for the record and allowed Mr. Zelinski
to proceed. Addressing the question of whether the property can yield a
reasonable rate of return as zoned, Mr. Zelinski asserted that the developer
was actually looking for a variance on top of what he characterized as an
“already generous allowance.” Mr. Zelinski stated that, by comparison, the
maximum allowable lot coverage was 30% in Glencoe, 33% in Highland Park, and
100% of the gross floor area in Wilmette.
Mr. Kevin Plunkett, 80 Warwick, played a video tape sharing the comments of
project neighbors, the Dolds and Clevengers, who stated in their respective
tapes that they were opposed to the development but were unable to be at the
meeting.
Mr. Plunkett continued by challenging Mr. Bosworth’s assertion that the
proposed development was consistent with the current Comprehensive Plan. He
directed the Council’s attention to the last paragraph of page 20-5 of the Plan
(page 7-10 of the Agenda package), which states: “The area east of the railroad
tracks most closely approximates the neighborhood convenience concept
originally planned for the district. Consequently, no change in the basic land
use pattern is contemplated in this area.”
Mr. Plunkett also addressed the specific impact of the project on his property,
citing his understanding of Illinois law requiring consideration of the impact
on the value of neighbors’ property. He submitted enhanced photos showing the
view from the rear of his house with the proposed building superimposed on the
image (page 4-20 of the Agenda package). Mr. Plunkett then introduced Mary
Bradbury, a local real estate broker who spoke to the impact of the proposed
project on the value of neighboring properties.
After describing her experience in Winnetka’s real estate market, Ms. Bradbury
stated that she had reviewed the developer’s plans and the surrounding
properties. She stated that she was familiar with property in the immediate
area and that she and her real estate firm had sold properties in the immediate
vicinity of the subject. She stated that, in her opinion, the proposed
development will have a negative impact on neighboring properties and will be
considered to be a “location flaw” because of the size of the proposed
buildings and their proximity to the single family residences. She also noted
that the proposed plan would narrow Tempel Court to accommodate parking. She
estimated that the properties in the immediate vicinity of the subject property
would experience approximately a 20% reduction in value if the property were to
be developed as proposed. She also indicated that she felt it was unlikely that
the projected condominiums would sell for prices in the $400,000 to $600,000
range, as had been suggested by Mr. Marous.
Mr. Bob Yale, 500 Hawthorn Lane, expressed his criticism of the proposed
project but indicated that he would not be opposed to an acceptable
rehabilitation of the existing property given its state of disrepair.
Mr. Bosworth asked the Council for a brief break in order to confer with his
clients. In deference to his request, President Holland declared a five minute
break at 10:25 p.m.
The Public Hearing reconvened at 10:30 p.m. with all members of the Council
present.
Messrs. Plunkard, Bosworth, and Stenholt presented rebuttal arguments before
the Trustees commented. Mr. Plunkard stated that the developer has never
envisioned narrowing Tempel Court. He also challenged the accuracy of Mr.
Plunkett’s computer generated picture, stating that the proposed building
addition does not sit in front of the existing trees. Mr. Plunkard further
stated that the developer had drawn six plans in an effort to develop the
building and that lot coverage increased because of increased Village
requirements. he noted, however, that the parking lot will now be primarily
pavers, which count as 80% rather than 100% impermeable, and that five parking
spots are shown on compacted soil.
Regarding the neighbors’ loss of the view to the west because of the proposed
building addition, Mr. Bosworth asserted that there is not constitutional right
to a view.
In response to the neighbors’ contentions that the building was commercially
viable and that the developer had been paying rent to keep space vacant, Mr.
Stenholt acknowledged that as the developer he had been paying rent on a vacant
store since July. He explained that they did not want the current owner to rent
to a new tenant only to have to force the new tenant to vacate the property in
order to accommodate the redevelopment. He added that the vacant space was
being used to conduct project development meetings. Mr. Stenholt added that the
developer would incur about $7 million in hard costs in developing the property
and that the cost of redevelopment was approximately $250 per square foot.
Trustee Duhl expressed his belief that a number of the issues being discussed
are ones about which the Council can do nothing; however, it can decide the
question of rezoning. He noted that the current Comprehensive Plan provides for
the area to include commercial space and that he is inclined to support that
position unless the Plan Commission and the consultants preparing the new
Comprehensive Plan recommend otherwise. He indicated his intention to listen to
their recommendations and his discomfort with overturning whatever they decide.
He also indicated that even if they say that this property should not stay
commercial, he is reluctant to grant the requested zoning variances because he
does not believe that the applicants have met the requirements for those
variances.
Trustee Meuer concurred with Mr. Duhl’s assessment of the situation. He
expressed his own concerns about the parking and overall bulk of the building.
Noting that he felt the proposed project was just too big, he indicated that he
would be unable to defend it.
Trustees Derning and Hilton indicated that they wanted to wait to hear from the
Plan commission before making any decision.
President Holland declared the Public Hearing continued until February 17,
2998, at which time the Council will have the comments from the Plan
Commission. She urged all of the interested parties in the audience to attend
the Plan Commission’s meeting the following evening.
Ordinance No. MC-201-98 – Ratification of Telecommunications Infrastructure
Maintenance Fee Ordinance – Introduction. Mr. Williams explained that the
Illinois Municipal League has recommended that communities which adopted its
model Telecommunications Infrastructure Maintenance Fee ordinance must now
adopt a ratifying ordinance in order to ward off any potential challenges to
the imposition of the fees.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce this ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1063 and 1064. Trustee Lien indicated that Mr. McKee had
satisfactorily answered all of his questions regarding the warrant lists.
Trustee Duhl moved to approve Warrant List No. 1063 in the total amount of
$452,694.60 and Warrant List No. 1064 in the total amount of $327,498.26.
Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call vote the warrant lists were
approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Lien, Lanphier, Derning, Hilton and
Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland announced that the Village would be
hosting a breakfasts for local legislators and representatives from Northwest
Municipal Conference on Friday, February 6th. She invited all of the Trustees
to attend.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported on the Chamber of Commerce meeting.
Village Manager. Village Manager Williams explained that the time line for
improving the traffic signals at Willow and Hibbard Roads needed to be extended
due to the construction of the Pine and Hibbard storm sewer project.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland asked for the approval of the Council for Jon Meyer and Edie
Howell to serve as representatives from the Design Review Board to the Forestry
Commission and Plan Commission, respectively. Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee
Hilton, moved to accept these appointments. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probably litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)
(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lanphier seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, Meuer, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 11:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:38 p.m. Present: Presidetn
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the motion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 11:40
p.m.
         
Deputy Clerk
February 10, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
A record of a legally convened study session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 10, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
President Louise Holland, Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton,
John Lien, William Meuer, and Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Finance Director Ed McKee, Water and Electric Superintendent
Bryan McInturff.
2. Electric Department Budget.
Superintendent McInturff reviewed the proposed Electric Department budget for
fiscal year 1998-99. Major expenditures in the distribution’s system include
the conversion of feeders 10 and 11 to 12.5kv and the placement of additional
load on the Northfield Substation. The major expenditures at the power plant
include new controls for boiler #8 and a turbine repair.
Mr. McInturff noted that it is a goal to convert overhead power lines to
underground on Greenbay Road from Pine Street to Chatfield.
3. Water Department Budget.
Superintendent McInturff reviewed the proposed Water Department budget. He
noted that the major expenditures include installing a variable frequency drive
for high lift pump #2 and the replacement of mixing basin valve.
Manager Williams noted in the five-year capital improvement plan that the
village will not be undertaking a water main project next year. However, four
water main projects are scheduled in the following five fiscal years.
4. Policy Direction.
By consensus, the Village Council directed staff to include the following three
additional expenditures in the 1998-99 budget: 1. Joining “Red Center” for
emergency medical dispatch as well as fire department dispatch; 2. Funding one
additional position in the Police Department as a transitional police officer
position; 3. Increasing the sidewalk program from $35,000 to $100,000 and the
Village will provide 100% funding for the repair of its sidewalks.
The Village Council adopted these additional expenditures by consensus with the
understanding that beautification programs will also be funded in fiscal year
1999-2000.
These additional programs and expenditures will be offset from revenues that
will be generated by the telecommunications tax.
It was the consensus of the Village Council to not establish a user fee, which
would be used to create cash reserves to fund a possible leachate problem at
the sanitary landfill.
The Village Council approved various reductions in expenditures for the
Community Development department in order to hire a consultant to help the
Village develop design guidelines for its commercial areas. Also, the Village
Council approved, by consensus, adjustments in the refuse fund to offset an
accounting problem with motor pool expenditures.
The meeting adjourned at 10:15 p.m.
February 17, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved March 3, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council chamber on Tuesday,
February 17, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien, and Bill Meuer.
Absent: Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack,
Chief of Police William Gallagher and about 40 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
March 3, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the trustees indicated that they planned
to attend.
March 10, 1998, Study Session. Trustees David Hilton and Michael Duhl will not
be attending. Trustees Derning, Lien and Meuer planned to attend. Trustee
Lanphier was absent.
V. Approval of Minutes
January 27, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Lien moved to adopt the minutes;
Trustee Meuer seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
February 2, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Lien moved to approve the minutes;
Trustee Meuer seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
February 3, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Lien moved to adopt the minutes,
with one correction on page 9 as noted by Trustee Hilton. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Meuer. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
February 10, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the minutes;
Trustee Meuer seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Zoning Map Amendment and Variances: 454-462 Winnetka Ave. – Continued Public
Hearing. President Holland declared the public hearing open and explained that
this was a continuation of the hearing that began on February 3, 1998. She then
gave a brief background of the request for variation. Mr. Neil Stenholdt
thanked the council for their time and work and said that he had reviewed the
material in Friday’s packet. He expressed his belief that perhaps the
developers are the victims of circumstances and bad timing, and referred to the
draft reports from Thompson Dyke & Associates and work on the comprehensive
plan. He stated that, because the old plan is currently being redone and the
new plan is only partially drafted, he believes the Council is dealing with two
different plans. He further stated that some ordinances probably should be
changed, and noted the height changes in the C-1 and C-2 zones adopted at the
Council’s last meeting. He also acknowledged that the Village has to apply the
ordinances in effect today. Referring to the letter he delivered to the Council
on February 13, Mr. Stenholdt stated that he was seeking a compromise and asked
the Council to find a mechanism to do so.
After explaining to the audience that they must be sworn in unless they were at
the previous meeting, President Holland asked for comments from the audience.
Mr. Socrates Zavitsanos, a custom tailor located in the subject building, that
he has customers all around the area, in Chicago, Wilmette, Lake Forest, Des
Plaines, Highland Park, Skokie, but that he wants to say in Winnetka.
Bob Zelinski, 72 Warwick, spoke regarding the Plan Commission’s consideration
of the proposal, stating that one of the three options for the property
outlined by Mr. Stenholdt is to construct a new building under the existing
zoning. Mr. Zelinski pointed to the statement from Mr. Stenholdt at pages 6-55
and 6-56 of the agenda packets, in which Mr. Stenholdt stated that this option
would be more profitable. Mr. Zelinski stated that because of this admission by
Mr. Stenholdt, the proposal does not meet the first requirement for the
variance requested. Regarding Mr. Stenholdt’s letter, Mr. Zelinski stated that
the community has given its input, but that they have not seen anything in
response from the developer. Mr. Zelinski further asserted that it is not
necessary to be an expert to know this is not a good plan and that the proposal
does not work. Mr. Zelinski concluded by stating that Mr. Stenholt should come
back with another design and asked the Council to deny the plan.
Alex Klein, 230 Church Rd; stated that he moved into the Village in August of
1997. He related his examination of the Village’s ordinances before he bought
his property, that there was a rule change while his building permit
application was pending and that he is now required to submit a topographical
map of his property. He described his efforts to comply with the Village’s
ordinances rather than seeking variations, even though it increased his costs.
He expressed his belief that zoning is there for a reason, and the rules should
be followed. He contended that the developer was asking for the rules to be
changed so he could do whatever he wants with the property. Noting that
students pass the property everyday, he suggested that there are other
commercial uses available, such as an ice cream parlor. He stated that he has
been very careful that his own project follows the rules and that he won’t
understand if this plan is approved, stating that he wants to encourage the
developer, but that the Council shouldn’t change the rules for him.
President Holland asked for further questions or comments from audience, and,
there being none, she asked for questions from Council.
Trustee Duhl moved that the Council go into executive session at 8:05 p.m. for
the purposes of discussing probably litigation and the possible sale of Village
land, and the consideration of the evidence received before issuing findings.
Trustee Meuer seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning, Meuer, and Duhl. Nays: None.
Absent Trustee Lanphier.
The Council reconvened into regular session at 8:40 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
President Holland again asked if there were any more comments from the audience
before closing the hearing. Hearing none, she asked for any questions or
discussion from the Council.
Trustee Hilton stated that he feels negative about the project. He stated that
it is too high, that it is out of line with the comprehensive plan, that it is
too dense, that there is no buffering. He does not like the balconies over the
sidewalks and finds the proposal inconsistent with the Comprehensive Plan.
Trustee Derning stated that he has serious reservations about the proposal and
stated that he was not convinced that the property is not viable at the present
time. He added that he was concerned about the encroachments on the public ways
and the violations of Village ordinances and stated that he was not inclined to
approve the proposal.
Trustee Meuer stated that he finds it difficult to support the project. He
expressed concern about the bulk, noting the general concerns expressed to the
Ad Hoc Zoning Committee about building bulk in the Village. He also expressed
concerns about parking on the first floor and height and mass of the building.
He sated that he feels strongly that it would impact on the value of homes in
the area and that he cannot support the project as it is presently proposed.
Trustee Duhl agreed with the other trustees and added that he thinks the
proposal in inconsistent wit the Comprehensive Plan, as found by the Plan
Commission, and stated he was reluctant to go against the Plan Commission’s
findings. He also expressed concerns about the density and bulk of the proposal
and that the project should not be supported for the reason even if the current
zoning classification is inappropriate.
President Holland declared the hearing closed at 8:45 p.m.
Trustee Lien moved that the applicant be denied the requested re-zoning from C-
1 to B-2 for the following reasons:
   1.   the applicant hasn’t established that the existing use is not the
highest and best use;
   2.   the proposed use creates new non-conformities as to building mass and
lot coverage;
   3.   the developer does not meet all of the standards for granting the
variations;
      (a)   the developer has not offered any compelling reasons why the
building can’t comply with existing zoning;
      (b)   the developer hasn’t established the existence of practical
difficulties related to the land and the only hardship identified is a
financial one;
      (c)   the proposed variations will alter the existing character of the
neighborhood;
   4.   the proposed use includes several ordinance violations; and
   5.   the proposed re-zoning is not appropriate for the property because the
increase in bulk and mass are inconsistent with the requested downzoning,
because of the number and extent of the variations and the impact in the
neighborhood, because the ordinance violations are an essential part of the
developer’s proposal and because of the change of first floor use to parking.
Trustee Derning seconded the motion.j By roll call vote, the application was
denied. Ayes: Trustee Lien, Trustee Duhl, Trustee Derning, Trustee Meuer, and
Trustee Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier. President Holland then
declared that the request for re-zoning had been turned down by the Council and
that the Council would issue written findings later in the week and would
include them in the minutes as well.
The Council recessed from 8:55 p.m. to 9:00 p.m., at which time President
Holland proceeded with the remainder of the agenda.
Ordinance No. MC-201-98 – Ratification of Telecommunications Infrastructure
Maintenance Fee Ordinance – Adoption. Trustee Hilton moved to adopt this
Ordinance. Trustee Meuer seconded the motion. Village Manager Williams then
explained that this ordinance ratifies the previously adopted ordinance which
established a telecommunications infrastructure maintenance fee. By roll call
vote, the ordinance was adopted. Ayes: Trustee Hilton, Trustee Duhl, Trustee
Meuer, Trustee Derning, Trustee Lien. Nayes: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Ordinance No. M-518-98 – Zoning Variation: 744 Tower Road – Introduction.
Cindy McCammack summarized the variance requested by Dr. and Mrs. Tat Kin Tsang
from the north front yard and south front yard setbacks in order to construct a
second story addition to their home. The property has more than one front yard
because of the Tower Road frontage and because of an adjoining private road,
which is a street under the Zoning Ordinance. Ms. McCammack circulated
photographs of the subject property and referred the Council to the diagram at
page 43 of the agenda packets.
In response to a question from Trustee Duhl regarding the noncomformities, Ms.
McCammack explained that the setback of the south front yard will increase
somewhat because of the removal of part of the existing structure. The size of
the north front setback will not increase, because the expansion of that
nonconformity is due to the vertical extension of that part of the building.
Attorney Steve Engelman, representing the applicants, introduced Dr. and Mrs.
Tsang, as well as their builder, David Dwyer, and their architect, John Banks.
Mr. Engelman went on to explain that the variations are being sought because of
the way the building is situated on the property. The footprint of the building
will not be increased and the size and height of the building will be within
the limitations of the zoning district. The expansion will be a vertical
extension of existing noncomformities. Mr. Engelman further explained that the
applicants desire to bring the house up to modern standards and to accommodate
their family of five. He stated that the neighbor to the west would be most
affected, but showed drawings and distributed photos (which had bee marked as
Exhibit 5), explaining that there is already a 6 foot high fence and quite a
bit of foliage, so the addition would not interfere with the existing views.
Mr. Engelman also showed a shadow study and stated that the addition would not
cast shadows on the house to the west.
Mr. Engelman stated that the applicants propose to build an addition consistent
with the size and quality of homes in the neighborhood, and distributed
Exhibits 3, 3B and 4, which he said showed that the neighboring homes are all
larger than the one on the Tsang’s property.
In response to a question from Trustee Meuer, the Tsang’s architect explained
that there would be little glazing facing the neighbor to the west.
Attorney Peter Friedman stated that he represents the neighboring property
owners at 851, 853, 857 and 859 Sheridan Road. He stated that the Tsang’s lot
is similar to a flag lot because of the narrow sublot that extends to the east,
which shifts the building bulk to the west. However, the lot does not meet the
definition of flag lot, so the protection in the Zoning Ordinance against the
concentration of bulk in the “flag” portion of the lot does not apply here.
Mr. Friedman asserted that the applicants do not have a right to put all of the
bulk in the setbacks. While the proposed house, with the addition, falls within
floor area limitations and may be smaller than neighboring houses, those houses
do not have setback variations. Mr. Friedman pointed out that because the
sublot increases the amount of available floor area of the lot, the setbacks
are the only thing left to protect neighboring properties from the building
bulk.
Mr. Friedman stated that the applicants have not met the standards for the
granting of a variation. He contended that the plight of the owners is not
unique, because many house are on bluffs and private road flanges. Also, the
owners’ plight is not related to the unique characteristics of the land, and
they have not shown that conforming alternatives either are not physically
possible, or are prohibitively expensive. Mr. Friedman stated that the
applicants’ hardship is self-created, pointing to a statement in the ZBA’s
minutes, at page 54 of the agenda packet, that the vertical extension of the
noncomformities is desired for aesthetic reasons. Mr. Friedman stressed that
the opposition of the neighbors is not personal, but that the neighbors are
deeply and sincerely concerned about the impact on their homes and
neighborhood. He added that granting the variation would set a damaging
precedent regarding building bulk, and asked the Council to reject the ZBA’s
recommendations and to deny the variations.
In response to a question from Trustee Duhl, Mr. Engelman stated that the
applicants’ house would be 25 feet high, which is lower than the house at 853
Sheridan. Mr. Engelman then responded to Mr. Friedman’s statements, asserting
that the ZBA had found that additional construction was necessary and no
reasonable alternative was found. He noted that the applicants wanted to adapt
the existing house, not tear it down, and urged the Council to concur with the
ZBA.
In response to questions from Trustee Derning and Trustee Duhl, Mr. Engelman
stated that setting the addition back to comply with the setbacks would shift
the addition to the north and east. Mr. Friedman asserted that this would have
a significant impact on the view from the west.
Trustee Hilton moved to introduce Ordinance M-518-98; Trustee Derning seconded.
Trustee Meuer stated that he could not support the request because it impacts
on other issues that the Council has considered, such as bulk, privacy of
neighbors, impact on property values and view. Trustee Lien agreed, adding that
the request is for a substantial variance and is contrary to actions the
Council has taken in recent past. Trustee Duhl stated that he believes that
setback requirements are second in importance only to floor area limitations.
By roll call vote, the motion failed. Ayes: Trustees Hilton and Derning. Nayes:
Trustees Lien, Duhl and Meuer. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Trustee Meuer the moved to deny the variation; Trustee Lien seconded. By roll
call vote, the motion to deny the variations was carried, unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Derning, Lien, Duhl, and Meuer. Nayes: None. Absent: Trustee
Lanphier. President Holland stated to applicant that any compliant proposal can
be brought to the building department.
Ordinance No. M-519-98 – Zoning Variation: 986 Asbury Court – Introduction.
Trustee Hilton moved to introduce the ordinance; Trustee Lien seconded. Cindy
McCammack explained the request for the increase in gross floor area. She
explained that the house was within floor area limitations when the original
building permit was applied for. Because of amendments to the gross floor area
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance, the attic space is included in the
calculations, so the existing building exceeds the gross floor area
limitations. Although this is a legal nonconformity, a variance is required for
the proposed balcony. Trustee Duhl expressed his disapproval, stating that the
gross floor area has been maxed out, that this is new construction and the
variance is being sought before the construction is even complete. He further
stated granting the requested variance would establish a dangerous precedent.
Trustee Lien and Trustee Derning concurred. By voice vote, the motion to
introduce failed unanimously. Trustee Lien then moved to deny the variance,
seconded by Trustee Meuer. By roll call vote, the motion failed, unanimously.
Ayes: Trustee Lien, Derning, Duhl, Meuer, and Hilton. Nayes: None Absent:
Trustee Lanphier.
Resolution No. R-1411-98 – Authorizing an Intergovernmental Agreement for a
County-wide Computerized Criminal Booking System (Livescan) – Adoption.
President Holland called for a motion to adopt. Trustee Hilton moved to adopt
the resolution; Trustee Derning seconded. Chief of Police William Gallagher
provided background regarding the county-wide booking system stating there was
a federal grant to Cook county and that the Village costs are a small
percentage of the total. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustee Lien, Derning, Duhl, Meuer, Hilton. Nayes: None. Absent: Trustee
Lanpher
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1065 and 1066. Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1065 in the total amount of $253,653.84 and Warrant List No. 1066 in the
total amount of $261,279.61. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Lien, Hilton and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Budget: Trustee Meuer commended Ed McKee on the monthly financial statements
given to the trustees. He noted that copies are available at the Winnetka
Library and encouraged viewers to look for the statements at the library.
Request for Donation of Parking Pass to Village Follies. Trustee Duhl stated
that donating a parking pass is a bad idea and sets a bad precedent. Trustee
Hilton stated that he thinks it makes parking look like a frivolous thing.
Trustee Derning questioned whether the Council had the authority to donate a
pass for private purposes. Trustee Lien moved to deny the request, Trustee
Meuer seconded the motion. by voice vote, the motion was carried unanimously.
Bid No. 98-006 – Traffic Signal Modernization. Village Manager Williams stated
that research on the lowest bidder disclosed a good reputation and
certification by IDOT. Trustee Hilton moved to award the bid as recommended by
staff, Trustee Duhl seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion was
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Derning. Nayes:
None. Abseent: Trustee Lanphier.
Bid No. 98-008 - Copper Wire. President Holland indicated that this was primary
and secondary copper wire used in service connections in homes. Trustee Derning
moved to approve; seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Derning. Nayes:
None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland reported that Sara Tueting of 488 Ash St.
was the goalie for the women’s hockey team that won the gold medal in the
Olympics and expressed hope that there will be some type of ceremony at a later
date.
President Holland also reported that Diane Shannon will receive the Girl
Scouting Gold Award, recognizing her as outstanding at a ceremony in Vernon
Hills. President Holland will be writing a letter to her.
Village Manager. Village Manager Williams announced that the public hearing on
the preliminary budget will be held on March 3. He reported that a copy of the
budget is available for public inspection in the Village clerk’s office.
Village Manager Williams reported that the Insurance Service Office (ISO) rates
communities on fire protection. In 1982, the village received a rating of class
4, on a scale of 1 to 10 with 1 being the best rating. The Village recently
received a letter that ISO will rate Winnetka gain within the next few months.
Mr. Williams said that he will keep the Council informed.
Village Trustees. Trustee Hilton reported on a meeting of the Chamber of
commerce’s Commercial Development Committee with landlords of retailers. Most
were present at the meeting which included discussion on new businesses, the
comprehensive plan and beautification projects. Issues for future meetings
include real estate taxes on commercial property and creating a balance between
retailing and service businesses.
Village Attorney. None
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
None
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:18 p.m.
         
Deputy Clerk
March 3, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved March 17, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council chamber on Tuesday, March
3, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, David Hilton, John Lien, and Bill Meuer. Absent: Michael
Duhl. and Penfield Lanphier,. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Director of Finance Ed McKee and Chief of Police William Gallagher
and about 6 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
   A.   March 10, 1998, Study Session - Trustees Hilton and Duhl will be absent
   B.   March 17, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustees Hilton, Lien, Meuer and
Derning indicated they will be present.
V. Approval of Minutes
   February 17, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning moved to adopt the
Minutes as drafted. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
   Ordinance No. M-519-98 - Zoning Variation: 986 Asbury Court -
   Reconsideration. Manager Williams stated that this matter was placed on the
Council agenda at the last minute. He stated that it is a routine matter for
staff to send out notices informing residents that their items are on the
agenda. A notice was sent to Dr. & Mrs. Lichtenberg but the envelope was
returned by the post office as undelivered. Dr. Lichtenberg contacted the
manager and stated that he didn’t receive the notice. In order to be fair, it
was decided to put this item back on agenda for reconsideration. C. McCammack
stated this request for a variation was due to the applicant’s desire to build
an addition of a balcony on new structure .The applicants submitted a revision
to the permit for approval of the balcony because it increases gross floor
area. The Zoning Board of Appeals met on 1/12/98 and voted 5-2 that Council
approve the variance requested. President Holland then asked if Dr. Lichtenberg
was present and if so, to introduce himself. Robert Kirk, the architect on the
project introduced himself and Dr. Lichtenberg. Mr. Kirk stated that the
project has been in progress for two years. During the review process, the
design was 200 sq. ft under the allotted amount. During construction while
doing a walk through of the house, Dr. Lichtenberg and Mr. Kirk decided another
exit on the second floor was desirable to provide safety for the children. Mr.
Kirk then proceeded to get a revision of permit, but due to a change in the
ordinance, the attic was then considered part of gross floor area and the
addition of a balcony would exceed the allowable square footage. Mr. Kirk then
stated that, even so, the addition of the balcony was within 80 sq ft of
allotted amount. Mr. Kirk and Dr. Lichtenberg surveyed the neighbors and they
do not object to their proposal. Dr. and Mrs. Lichtenberg are requesting that a
second exit for the children and for ease of access to the exterior be
approved.
Trustee Hilton asked about the decision to add a balcony as a security exit for
children. Mr. Kirk said that they did not feel that they were in any violation
of the ordinance. The ordinance specifies a cantilevered structure and he does
not believe that a balcony could technically be considered a cantilevered
structure. The home does have allowable exits via the windows, which are
casement type and not easily operated. When they were considering a second exit
they saw that they did have an opportunity for a balcony in a large window on
the second floor and felt it would be desirable to place an exit there instead.
Dr. Lichtenberg then stated that there is only one staircase and that this
additional exit would be beneficial. He asserted that he did not think this
would be in any way a problem in the gross square footage of the house. He has
heard of no complaints about it. Trustee Hilton then asked if the intention was
that they could jump from the balcony in the event of a fire. Dr. Lichtenberg
replied that it would be easier than a window which may not open. Trustee
Derning stated that there are a number of products made to exit from a second
story in case of a fire or emergency. Had the applicants considered any of
those alternatives. Mr. Kirk said that a rope ladder was possible. Trustee
Meuer asked why the need for another exit did not occur to them when plan was
originally drawn. Mr. Kirk said that they did not think of it then and because
of ordinance change, the addition of the balcony fell into an overage of square
feet. Trustee Meuer then stated that he is opposed to a variance on new
construction because of continued concern about bulk. Mr. Kirk replied that in
light of the fact that the ordinance changed after construction began, he felt
that they should be entitled to the variation. Trustee Hilton also stated that
he would have a problem supporting the variance. It would be nice to have it
for security, but there are other ways to exit the second floor. Trustee
Derning concurred, saying that there were other alternatives to use for life
safety and that the bulk issue is critically important, particularly in new
construction. Mr. Kirk replied that the Zoning Board of Appeals did have a
similar concern. Their consensus was that since the applicant did not seek this
variation in the initial permit application, it had become a burden to the
homeowner. Mr. Kirk stated that this is an issue of 80 sq ft and the ordinance
does not mention balconies. In addition, the balcony is tucked behind the
garage and is an extension of the roof, becoming part of the house. President
Holland asked Mr. Kirk at what point in the construction did the balcony appear
in plans. Mr. Kirk replied that during the framing of the second floor, in
order to support a balcony, they had to decide to put joists in. It was at this
time, that he made a recommendation to the owners to put in cantilevered
girders which is the main supporting structure for the balcony. This is when
they requested revisions in the permit and when they found out about new
ordinance. President Holland then stated that in order to consider Dr.
Lichtenberg’s application, a motion to reconsider was necessary. Trustee Hilton
moved to introduce. Trustee Meuer seconded. By roll call vote, the council
denied the reconsideration. Ayes: None Nayes: Lien, Derning, Meuer, Hilton.
Absent: Trustees Duhl and Lanphier. President Holland then told Mr. Kirk and
Dr. Lichtenberg that if they desired to discuss this further they could speak
with Cindy McCammack.
Ordinance No. M-515-98 - Alley Vacation - 333 Fairview -
Reconsideration. President Holland asked Attorney Janega to give a brief
background on this matter. Attorney Janega stated that this ordinance vacating
the alley was previously adopted at the February 3 Council meeting.
Subsequently, the contract purchasers of the property asked that the utility
easement on the north edge of property be reconsidered. Bryan McInturff and
Attorney Janega inspected the site and Village records and found that there is
a dedicated alley one foot north of the property line, and utilities are strung
over this alley. The northernmost foot of the original lot is owned by the
owner to the north of the alley. The strip apparently was acquired in
anticipation that the alley would eventually be vacated. After confirming that
there were no utilities in the 5-foot easement strip, Bryan McInturff agreed
that it would not be necessary to reserve the 5-foot easement for utilities but
to maintain it for ingress and egress so utilities in the rear can be accessed.
Because of this, it is being brought back to the Council so that the ordinance
can be amended to change the easement along the north lot line. Attorney Janega
also stated that the revised ordinance has one other change, which clarifies
the legal description as requested by the surveyor. Although this is a
housekeeping matter, she feels it is appropriate to bring before the Council as
part of the reconsideration. The Council’s course of action would be to
consider a motion to approve the reconsideration. If that motion passes, then
the Council would move to amend the text of the ordinance and then adopt it.
President Holland asked for a motion. Trustee Hilton moved to reconsider the
ordinance, Trustee Lien seconded. Trustee Meuer had questioned whether the
square footage amount is the same as the amount of property being sold.
Attorney Janega said it is exactly the same and so is the compensation. Ayes:
Trustee Hilton, Derning, Lien, Meuer. Nayes: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier and
Trustee Duhl. President Holland then called for a motion to amend the ordinance
reclassifying the north utility easement as one for ingress and egress. Trustee
Meuer so moved, Trustee Derning seconded. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Absent: Trustee Lanphier and Trustee Duhl. President Holland then
called for a adopt ordinance M-515-98 as amended. Trustee Lien so moved,
Trustee Meuer seconded. By roll call vote, motion carried unanimously. Absent:
Trustee Lanphier and Trustee Duhl President Holland declared the ordinance
adopted as amended.
Ordinance No. MC-203-98 - Telecommunications Tax - Introduction. President
Holland called for a motion to introduce. Trustee Meuer moved, Trustee Lien
seconded. Trustee Hilton asked about beautification being included in the
listed reasons for the tax. Manager Williams said that this was a long term
goal and will not be in next year’s budget. President Holland then stated that
there was a letter in the Winnetka Talk addressing the tax, and that a letter
will be sent to the Winnetka Talk this week in response, explaining the taxes
and why Winnetka does not have an abundance of taxes. She indicated that an
important part of this letter is a list of our taxes, the increases, the
changes, and the decreases. President Holland stated that she hopes that all
residents read this letter carefully because the Council certainly does not
introduce taxes lightly and this letter will explain them. Trustee Meuer had a
question for Ed McKee, referring to the agenda packets, and the list of uses
for the Telecommunications Tax. Ed McKee replied that these are partial
allocations, for 9 months of the year. The tax would generate $187,000 for a 9
month period, and the three programs are RED Center, allocated $80,000,
sidewalk repair, $65,000 and the six-month cost of a transitional police
officer of $27,500; this totals $172,500, less than the revenue being received
of $187,000. Trustee Meuer then asked if the figure was prorated over a full
year and if it was safe to assume that prorated over a year, the
telecommunications tax would take care of all of it. Ed McKee indicated that it
would. Manager Williams stated that all of the costs have been annualized in
the agenda report. President Holland stated that the three items that this tax
will provide are RED Center, sidewalk repairs, and the increase of one police
officer to cover the pending retirements of several other officers. Trustee
Hilton then indicated that there will be a follow up to all residents assuming
that the tax passes. President Holland asked for any questions from audience.
Trustee Hilton moved to introduce the ordinance. Trustee Meuer seconded. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1412-98 - Reduction of Electric Rates - Introduction. Trustee
Lien moved to introduce the resolution to reduce electric rates. Trustee Hilton
seconded. Manager Williams stated that this is the first step of a ten year
plan to reduce rates that will position our electric utility to compete in
deregulation. If it is implemented, the rates will actually be 7% lower.
Wholesale prices have been coming down and the Village has been automatically
crediting residents. Trustee Hilton asked if the 5% decrease is based on
original rate. Manager Williams indicated that it was. By voice vote, the
resolution was introduced unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1414-98 - Resolution for Sarah Tueting - Adoption. President
Holland stated that as Winnetkans know, Sarah was the goalie on the Olympic
hockey team that won the gold medal. Trustee Derning moved to adopt the
resolution. Trustees Hilton and Meuer said that they would like to see an
addition to the resolution indicating that Sarah’s schooling was in Winnetka.
President Holland asked for those in favor of adopting the resolution. By voice
vote, the Council adopted the resolution unanimously. President Holland stated
that they will be presenting Sarah with this resolution at the event being
planned by the Park District.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
Findings - 454-462 Winnetka Avenue. . Approval. President Holland called for
motion to approve the findings. Trustee Lien moved to approve, Trustee Derning
seconded. Trustee Hilton asked if the findings were now officially public
record. President Holland said that they were, and because Council went into
executive session, to discuss the evidence from the hearing, it was required to
follow the announcement of its decision with written findings stating the
particular reasons for denying the rezoning and variation. By voice vote,
motion to approve the findings was carried unanimously. Absent: Trustees
Lanphier and Duhl.
Public Hearing - 1998/99 Budget. President Holland asked Ed McKee to address
this item. Mr. McKee stated that the staff had made the changes suggested by
Council during the budget hearings and these revisions are now at the Library.
President Holland declared the public hearing open at 8:00 p.m. and asked for
any questions or comments from anyone in attendance. There were none. President
Holland declared the public hearing closed at 8:02 p.m. She announced that the
budget figures are still available at the Library or in the Village Manager’s
office.
Operating Budget Projections. President Holland asked Ed McKee to give a
summary of the projections. Mr. McKee stated that the staff had put together 5-
year projections to look at trends affecting Village budget. Page 65 of the
agenda packet shows revenues are growing at a slightly slower rate than
expenditures. More dollars tend to be needed to provide the same services year
to year and operating costs are rising faster than inflation. Mr. McKee
explained that if the economy goes down a little, property taxes are fixed and
this helps. If the economy is booming, property taxes don’t help. Some
weaknesses on revenues are shown, but overall revenues are solid. Manager
Williams summarized that basically the general fund is in a delicate balance
and each year it takes a bit of tweaking to even it out. One or two events
during the year could tip that balance. Predicting variables in the future is
difficult. The general fund really doesn’t have the capacity to absorb major
problems. Trustee Meuer asked a question about reassessments and how they
impact a budget of this sort. Ed McKee said that it would not affect this type
of budget. Manager Williams said that the tax levy is $6.5 or 7 million dollars
and that this is all that we can generate. Trustee Hilton asked why the
property tax figure went down. Mr. McKee replied that everything is funneled
through the general fund and that this used to include police and fire
departments, but now this is handled separately and that is why the figure is
lower. Manager Williams asked Mr. McKee to address the electric department
projections. Mr. McKee indicated that this department is following the
projections made on the strategic plan. Sales are a bit stronger than
predicted, generating a significant amount of money for capital improvements
for another year. Mr. McKee said that going forward, we will have the rate
reductions next year and the year after of 5%. The electric fund shows a long
term growth factor of 1.5%. The water fund has a slight downward trend in terms
of sales. This fund generates $500,000 per year necessary to pay for capital
improvements. Sewer fund is in the best shape and shows a positive cash
position, will be adding cash for the next couple of years, and has a balanced
budget. The refuse fund will be affected by the recycling contract that is up
for renewal this year, the membership in SWANCC and what we pay for tipping
costs, and landfill costs. In terms of rates, it is heavily subsidized by the
general fund, one third from general fund. Trustee Hilton asked about the cash
and investments at the top of the spreadsheet. Ed McKee explained that this is
a simple cash projection. Manager Williams said that the utility funds are
doing okay, that we may have to increase rates for water, but refuse and sewer
are doing great. President Holland then thanked Ed McKee.
IX. New Business
Women’s Club Croquet Tournament Request. Kitty Shin, philanthropy chairman for
the Winnetka Women’s Club, Chris Fockler chairman of the event, and Liz
Barretta, president of the club introduced themselves. Ms. Barretta said that
they had had a similar event in Florida and described the croquet courts
surrounded by pavilions. The tents are decorated and people come for an elegant
afternoon dressed in croquet attire. She understood that one concern is the
contest for “Most Wicket Punch”. It will be up to each tent to produce punch
for their people. The punch may be alcoholic or not, and there is an entry fee
for the dinner and to play croquet. The club is hoping to schedule the event
for the last Saturday in July. Because it is the Club’s 90th anniversary, they
felt this event has a vintage appeal, and they want to make it very elegant.
The club would also like to make it a greater Winnetka community event and is
attempting to draw participation from various Village circles, such as a real
estate company of couples, the Village Council, and a tennis club for a feeling
of community rivalry. It would be for adults only. Trustee Hilton asked if the
event itself needs to be approved by Council or if it is just because liquor is
being served. Manager Williams said this is a policy call. Trustee Hilton asked
if the event needed to be insured. Attorney Janega said that she thought the
Village was protected by the Park District contract. Trustee Hilton asked if
the Village had ever had liquor served on the Green before. Trustee Hilton
asked if liquor was necessary to the event. Ms. Barretta said that she thought
it should be permitted. She also stated that the Women’s Club is covered on the
insurance matter. Trustee Hilton asked for Trustee Derning’s opinion. Trustee
Derning said that he understands what the club is trying to do and understands
the concern about alcohol. If the presence of alcohol on the Green is legal,
then he feels that appropriate adult behavior should not be prohibited. Based
on his prior experience, he assumes Winnetka residents will behave responsibly
and the liquor should be permitted. President Holland asked about the hours of
the event. Ms. Barretta said the hours are from 3 p.m. to 8 p.m. and that by
the time everything is set up it would be 3:30 p.m. to 4:00 p.m. before the
event began. She also clarified that non-alcoholic beverages would also be
served. The club will try to make this a nice event, because they want it to be
an annual occasion, elegant and subdued. President Holland asked if each tent
will be instructed that the contest must be a punch, not a full-service bar.
Ms. Barretta stated that they could be very specific about that when the rules
are decided. President Holland explained that she would like them to have a
wonderful event, but Council is concerned that no one gets hurt and that the
event is safe. Trustee Meuer asked about liquor in the parks. Chief Gallagher
stated that with a permit, alcohol is allowed in parks. Trustee Hilton stated
that he thought the event should be limited to punch, not hard liquor.
President Holland then presented Village Attorney Janega’s suggestions of
possible conditions for approval, such as a special assignment police officer
at the event, limiting the hours when punch is served to dinner so that the
time for the consumption of alcohol was limited or that the contest be renamed
so that the use of alcohol is not encouraged. President Holland then asked
about the use of the word, “wicket”, and how it would be interpreted. Ms.
Barretta said that the definition of the word hadn’t been a problem at the
Florida event. Trustee Derning indicated that he thought the Council should
just decide yes or no about the event. He felt that they should go ahead with
it and see how it develops. Trustee Meuer said that he would like to see the
alcohol be limited to punch, not liquor or an open bar, and that he would like
to see the Park District’s rule on alcohol. Trustee Hilton also said that he
would like to limit it to punch. Trustee Lien said that he was willing to go
along with Trustee Derning to see how the event goes, although he said that he
had a minor problem with using the park to the exclusion of other residents.
However, he is willing to try it for this year. President Holland stated that
the consensus was restricting the hours to 3:00 p.m. to 8:00 p.m. and that only
punch would be alcoholic. The Council approved the use of the Village Green for
the Women’s Club Croquet Tournament.
Warrant Lists Nos. 1067 and 1068. Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1067 in the total amount of $373,349.53 and Warrant List No. 1068 in the
total amount of $173,812.40. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lien, Hilton and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and Lanphier. Trustee
Lien said he had discussed several items with Ed McKee, including the glock
pistol for $410 which Mr. McKee said will be used for a training tool. Trustee
Hilton asked about the livescan equipment listed at the top of page 17 in the
packet. Ed McKee stated this is fingerprint equipment that was approved by
council. Trustee Hilton also asked about the water testing figure. Mr. McKee
said this is for the water plant and is an annual fee which has been
significantly increased by the State’s water regulations.
1997 Police Department Community Survey. Chief Gallagher introduced Sgt. Glen
Florkow, who was one of the motivating factors behind the survey and who also
did the majority of the work on it. Chief Gallagher explained that the survey
was first done several years ago for police accreditation and thereafter the
Council had requested an annual survey. Although, the initial reason for the
survey was the accreditation process, it covers overall department performance,
and the concerns of the community on safety and, as a result, the department
has since used the survey for policy and structural procedures. For this year’s
survey, the Finance Department provided the Police Department with 550 random
addresses to better sample the community and unincorporated areas. The actual
survey process is articulated in the first part of the survey. Chief Gallagher
stated that the results of this year’s survey are very positive and show
continued support from the community regarding the interactions with the
public, how the police are perceived, and how the safety of the community is
perceived. He said the residents’ comments are particularly interesting. One
point that Chief Gallagher wanted to clarify is confusion about solicitation in
the community. The Police Department does not solicit from the community. Chief
Gallagher listed several organizations who solicit but that do not directly
involve any employee of the Police Department. Trustee Hilton then asked if
this should be included in the Winnetka Report. Manager Williams said that it
had been in the Report within the last year. Chief Gallagher said he receives a
number of questions about this and stated that organizations often suggest that
they work for Winnetka Police Department, but they do not. Other comments in
the survey indicated a belief that some activities the police were involved in
have had some remote racial connotations. Chief Gallagher said that the Police
Department has looked at all complaints for the last two years and citations
issued in the last 6 months and has determined that this is not remotely true.
They have not received any complaints of a racial nature or from people in any
protected classes. Another area covered in the survey was parking which has
been a recurring theme over the past 27 years that Chief Gallagher has been
aware of. He said the responses to these questions were surprising, because the
majority of residents seem to feel that there is adequate parking. There are
opposing positions in the survey but the survey doesn’t provide much guidance
on the issues because most residents indicated there was no problem. Chief
Gallagher said that the Village needs to look at parking in the Village
regarding the addition of spaces, reallocation of spaces, and continuation of
enforcement. He also explained that the appeal process for parking tickets was
formalized this year and as a result of this, there is more consistency with
how the tickets are handled. However, parking continues to be a major problem.
There has been an increased enforcement program over the last year, and the
Police Department will try to redirect some of that enforcement as a result of
the survey. There will be more focus on stop sign and traffic control
enforcement. The Police Department has also purchased a speed sign and has put
this out in areas where people have indicated there is speeding. Next year’s
survey will include some of the changes that were made in response to the
residents’ comments in this survey. Manager Williams then directed the Council
to page 12 of survey where residents rated the services they have received. He
noted that the residents surveyed rate the services higher each year. He was
also stunned to see that 74% of commuters said that there was adequate parking
and that shoppers said there was adequate parking, because he receives so many
complaints in his office. Trustee Hilton said that the Chief should be
congratulated on the outstanding results. Chief Gallagher said that the survey
was designed to be anonymous, but that the Department would have liked to
discuss some of the comments made in the survey with the residents. Trustee
Derning expressed his appreciation of the officers and personnel.
   Monthly Compost Report. No comments or questions.
X. Reports.
   Village President. President Holland thanked Michelle Osman from TCI, who
created a video on recycling so that residents can return to the type of
recycling that we have done in the past. The tape is available at the library
and also the Village Manager’s office. She thanked Michelle for doing this all
on her own and said that it is very appreciated. President Holland also
mentioned that Diane Shannon will be awarded the Girl Scout Gold Award by the
Girl Scouts of America and wished Diane congratulations.
   Village Trustees. None.
   Village Attorney. None.
   Village Manager. Manager William reported on the traffic signals at the
Public Safety Building and on bridge construction.
   Public Safety Building Traffic Signals: Staff put together a sketch of the
traffic signal layout included in Council’s agenda packets. There will be two
mast arms, five standards (poles) and eight traffic signal heads.
   Elm Street Bridge Timetable: The Illinois Department of Transportation is
taking longer than anticipated for approval of the project, so construction
will not start until July, 1999 and we will receive 80% funding.
   Tower Road Bridge: The bid has been awarded to the same contractor who did
Oak and Cherry Street, Areatha Construction. Construction will start in mid-
March and will be done by October 31. The Village’s cost is very minor.
   Manager Williams also stated he had good news about floodway revisions,
because the Illinois Department of Natural Resources has signed off in writing
on redrawing the floodway boundary west of Hibbard Road. It goes to FEMA next.
   He also reported that the Village has been named as a 1997 Tree City USA.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland expressed her thanks to Randy Larimore for his 6 years of
service on the Landmark Preservation Committee, with his term ending March 31
this year. She said he has given more than his share, is the owner of Lloyd
House and has put many hours of restoration into that construction and has been
a tremendous contributor to landmark preservation. President Holland stated she
would like to appoint Trish Early to replace Mr. Larimore. Ms. Early is the
first curator of the Winnetka Historical Museum and the Village will be
fortunate to have her on this committee. President Holland called for a motion
to confirm the appointment. Trustee Lien moved to confirm the appointment of
Trish Early, Trustee Hilton seconded. Motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)
(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Hilton, Lien, Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and Lanphier.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:57 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:10 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Lanphier.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:11 p.m.
Findings of the Village Council
Following the Hearing by the Committee of the Whole
Regarding 454 – 462 Winnetka Avenue
Approved by the Village Council on March 3, 1998
1.   The applicant has not demonstrated that the existing zoning of C-1 is not
the highest and best use of the property. The existing zoning, development and
use of the property is established in the neighborhood and is consistent with
the current Comprehensive Plan.
2.   The proposed use creates new nonconformities in addition to the ones
currently existing on the property, in order to accommodate additional building
mass and lot coverage.
a.   Granting the applicant’s request for rezoning requires the granting of a
variation in the Tempel Court and Winnetka Avenue setbacks, reducing them from
25 feet to zero, to allow the existing building to remain in place.
b.   In addition, the proposed rezoning would require the following variations
to allow for the proposed 4-story, flat-roofed addition to the south and west
sides of the existing building: (a) reduction of the Winnetka Avenue setback
from 25 feet to 3 feet for a distance of approximately 54.69 feet; (b) an
increase in building height from 42 feet to approximately 44.5 feet to align
the flat roof of the addition with the ridge line of the existing gabled roof;
(c) increasing the lot coverage from 60 % to 75%; (d) reducing the west side
yard setback to zero to allow the addition to abut the railroad embankment; and
(e) increasing the number of dwelling units from the permitted maximum of 27 to
28.
3.   The developer does not meet all of the standards for granting the
variations.
a.   The developer has not offered any compelling reasons why the new
construction cannot comply with the applicable zoning. The building height, set
backs and lot coverage requirements could all be met by reducing the size of
the proposed addition.
b.   The developer has not established the existence of practical difficulties
or particular hardships related to the land, with the sole exception of the
railway embankment, which warrants only the reduction of the west side yard
setback.
c.   The only hardship that may exist is a financial one, created by the
developer’s desire to get a return on its investment. However, the developer’s
market study by MaRous establishes that the property currently has a market
value of approximately $990,000, and the developer has admitted paying $2.7
million for the property, approximately 3 times its market value. Consequently,
any hardship has been self-created and does not warrant the granting of
variances.
d.   The requested variations will alter the essential character of the
existing neighborhood.
(1)   The flat-roofed mass of the addition will be visible from Winnetka
Avenue, Tempel Court, Green Bay Road and the adjoining residential properties,
and will create a wall approximately 30 feet high and approximately 163 feet
long along the edge of the Green Bay Trail.
(2)   The addition along Winnetka Avenue will increase the Winnetka Avenue
frontage by approximately 50% and reduce open space along the Winnetka Avenue
frontage from approximately 88.21 feet to approximately 33.52 feet and will
jeopardize several large diameter trees.
(3)   The proposal will also change the appearance of the building, by changing
the fenestration on the first floor to camouflage parking areas, adding a 3-
foot high “garden wall,” adding balconies where windows now exist, and changing
dormers from single gables to double gables.
(4)   The proposal removes existing open space that now serves as a buffer
between the current building and the adjoining single family residences and
streets, with building mass increasing rather than decreasing as it approaches
the adjoining single family properties.
4.   The proposed use includes several additional ordinance violations: the
enclosure of a portion of the Winnetka Avenue and Tempel Court rights-of-way;
balconies extending over the Winnetka Avenue sidewalk and encroaching over the
Tempel Court right-of way; a portion of the parking lot is shown as “compacted
dirt,” in order to stay within the limits of the lot coverage variation that
has been requested; and the use of at least one parking space in the Tempel
Court right-of-way to meet the parking requirements.
5.   Several aspects of the proposed use demonstrate that the proposed rezoning
to B-2 is not appropriate for the property. Those aspects include:
a.   the proposed use results in an increase in lot coverage, bulk, and
density, which is inconsistent with the request to down-zone the property to a
less intense zoning classification, and provides less of a transition to the
adjoining residential uses than does the existing use;
b.   the number and extent of the variations requested, and the negative impact
of those variations on the neighborhood;
c.   the ordinance violations that are an essential part of the developer’s
proposal; and
d.   the change of a substantial portion of the first floor from a principal
use (currently commercial) to an accessory use (parking).
March 10, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved April 7, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
10, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Penfield Lanphier, and John Lien. Absent: Trustees
Michael Duhl, Bill Meuer, and David Hilton. Also present: Village Manger Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and Director of Community
Development Cindy McCammack.
II. B-2 Zoning Regulations – Possible Changes.
President Holland introduced the suggestions from the Comprehensive Plan
subcommittee noting that these recommendations had been endorsed by the whole
Plan Commission. Manager Williams indicated that the Village had specifically
asked Thompson Dyke and Associates when they were first hired to assist with
the development of the Comprehensive Plan to respond to concerns about density,
especially in the B-2 zoning district. He then asked Ms. McCammack to review
each of the recommendations from the Plan Commission enumerated in the meeting
materials. In doing so she noted that Thompson Dyke & Associates felt that the
B-2 zoning district ought to be eliminated as it has too much impact on the
single family districts. She also indicated that TD&A had recommended a
reduction in impermeable surface to 35% and an increase in permissible building
height.
   Trustee Meuer arrived at 8:00 p.m.
   The Trustees engaged in extended discussion about the recommendations.
President Holland expressed concern about the impact of increased height on
neighboring single family homes vis-a-vis lack of privacy. She also stated that
she didn’t think that decreasing the impervious coverage ratio from the current
60% to 35% was realistic. Trustee Lanphier, who sits on the Plan Commission,
clarified some of the issues raised during the commission’s meetings. She
addressed the need to define what a smooth transition between zoning districts
looks like and the need to retain a diversity of housing alternatives. Attorney
Janega pointed out that there are certain kinds of multi-family units, i.e.
townhomes, which do work well to serve both purposes.
   President Holland recognized Mr. Dick Busscher, a local developer, who also
addressed the Plan Commission’s recommendations. He applauded the Council’s
actions but urged the Trustees to proceed carefully cautioning that they cannot
legislate exactly what is built because, ultimately, consumer demand and
favorable economics dictate what builders will build. He also expressed his
opinion that a reduction in the impervious coverage ratio to 35% might be too
much but indicated that he didn’t perceive the parking or building height
requirements to pose much of a problem.
III. Attic FAR Analysis – The Results.
Ms. McCammack reviewed her findings regarding the impact of the adoption of
Ordinance MC-160-96 amending the zoning ordinance to include attic area that
contributes to the mass of a residence in gross floor area (GFA) calculations.
She indicated that overall staff had not seen a significant hardship placed on
existing structures, and that, therefore, her recommendation is to maintain the
1% attic GFA allowance. However, she did suggest that the language of the
ordinance be amended to replace the word “rafters” with “decking” in order to
include those framing methods that do not use roof rafters.
IV. Federal Funding Analysis for Pine and Eldorado Street Bridges.
Manager Williams referred Council to the report prepared by Assistant Village
Engineer Steven Saunders. He explained that it was likely that Federal funding
could be obtained for the Pine Street Bridge rehabilitation project, but the
Eldorado Street Bridge was not eligible for Federal funding. Council directed
Mr. Williams to have staff do what is necessary to pursue Federal assistance.
V. Proposed Gift of Sculpture to the Village.
The Trustees discussed the possible disposition of the proposed gift of an
Abbott Pattison screen sculpture. President Holland volunteered to contact
representatives from the Design Review Board and Historical Society for their
thoughts and discuss possible alternatives with the present owner.
VI. Appointment of Member to Canvassing Board.
Trustee Lanphier volunteered to serve on the Canvassing Board for the upcoming
election.
VII. Review Plans for the Old Caledonia building: 562-572 Lincoln.
Manager Williams shared the preliminary drafts of the proposed plans for the
renovation of the old Caledonia building with the Trustees. He noted that the
plans validate the viability of restricting height in the commercial zoning
districts to 2-1/2 stories.
VIII. Manager’s Report.
Mr. Williams reminded the Trustees about the presentation to Sarah Tueting this
coming weekend. He also reviewed the events of the recent major storm and the
Village’s response thereto. He commended all Village personnel who pitched in
wherever needed to keep systems operational.
   Manager Williams requested that in recognition of continued service to the
Village over and above the call of duty, the Trustees approve a paid leave of
absence for Ken Keene, Director of Public Works, who is to have major surgery
next week. There was unanimous agreement among the Trustees to do so.
The meeting adjourned at 9:25 p.m.
March 17, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved April 7, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 17, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, and John
Lien. Absent: Bill Meuer. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack,
Assistant Village Engineer Steven Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee, and
about 3 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
   April 7, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they planned to attend.
   April 14, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they planned to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
   March 3, 1998, Regular Meeting. Attorney Janega made a minor correction to
the Minutes as drafted. Trustee Lien moved to adopt the Minutes as amended. He
was seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-203-98 – Telecommunications Tax – Adoption. Mr. Williams
briefly reviewed the programs which would be funded by the approximately
$250,000 received in revenue from the telecommunications tax. In response to a
question from Trustee Lanphier, he explained that the telecommunications tax is
essentially a “sales tax” on telephone calls which many other villages have
already adopted. In view of the Village’s current stagnant revenue sources, the
tax would provide additional assurance that the Village could fund its Five
Year Capital Improvements and maintain its current level of services.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Meuer.
Resolution No. R-1413-98 – A Resolution Adopting the Annual Budget for the
Fiscal Year Beginning April 1, 1998 and Ending March 31, 1999 – Adoption.
Trustee Derning moved to adopt the proposed Budget for 1998-99, and Trustee
Lanphier seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Meuer.
Manager Williams commended the Finance Department, especially Director Ed McKee
and Assistant Director Hanna Sullivan, for their efforts in preparing the
Budget.
Ordinance No. M-517-98 – Alley Vacation and Plat of Consolidation: 325 Fairview
(Montplaisir Consolidation) – Public Hearing and Introduction. President
Holland declared the Public Hearing open. Ms. McCammack explained that approval
of this vacation and consolidation would result in the subject lot being
increased in depth from 160 to 170 feet. As noted in her Agenda Report, she
recommended that the consolidation plat be approved subject to the reservation
of utility easements. Manager Williams noted that the circumstances of this
property are similar to those at 333 Fairview. He indicated that the Village
would be compensated for the land in the amount of $12,500.
There being no comments from the floor President Holland closed the hearing.
Trustee Lien moved to introduce the ordinance approving the vacation and
consolidation. He was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
Trustee Meuer arrived at 7:45 p.m.
Ordinance No. MC-202-98 – Amending Zoning Ordinance Pertaining to Building
Height, Intensity of Use, and Parking in the Multi-Family Residential Zoning
District – Public Hearing and Introduction. Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce
the ordinance. She was seconded by Trustee Hilton.
President Holland declared the Public Hearing open. Manager Williams briefly
outlined for the Council the recommendations from the Plan Commission and
Thompson Dyke & Associates which were previously discussed at the March 10,
1998, Study Session.He reported that TDA had raised concerns about the B-
2 zoning standards when they were first retained at the Village's land use
consultant.
Manager Williams pointed out that the proposed ordinance would amend the zoning
district requirements in the B-1 and B-2 zoning districts. He noted that the
ordinance (1) was evolutionary in nature, an attempt to balance private
property rights with the community interests, (2) strengthens off street
parking requirements, (3) tightens the standards in the B-2 district, (4) adds
to the B-1 district in terms of FAR, and (5) eliminates the prospect of B-
2 projects being built in a C-1 zone, thereby providing a smoother transition
from commercial to residential districts.
Ms. McCammack added comments regarding setbacks stating that she believes
specific minimums should be set. She also noted that the proposed ordinance
does not provide a specific lot coverage percentage for buildings. Mr. Williams
indicated that he expected to have recommendations on these issues for
consideration at the next Council meeting.
Dick Busscher questioned the requirement for two parking spaces per unit and
asked about a bonus for putting all parking underground. Manager Williams
replied that these issues were still under discussion.
There being no further comments from the floor, President Holland closed the
Public Hearing and called for a vote on the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Duhl, Hilton, Derning,
Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-204-98 – Amending Village Code Pertaining to Gasoline Powered
Leaf Blowers -–Introduction. Manager Williams indicated that after reviewing
the first year of enforcement of MC-167-96 banning the use of gasoline-powered
leaf blowers, staff had determined that additional language needed to be
incorporated into the ordinance to prohibit the use of electric leaf blowers
connected to portable gasoline-powered electric generators. He mentioned that
although staff felt that this issue was already covered by the existing
ordinance, enforcement would be easier if the police department could point to
specific language rather than rely on interpretation.
The motion to introduce was made by Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee
Lanphier. A voice vote on the motion was unanimous.
Resolution No. R-1412-98 – Reduction of Electric Rates – Adoption. Manager
Williams explained that this resolution implements the 5% rate reduction for
Fiscal Year 1998-99 as outlined in the Strategic Plan for the Water & Electric
Department already approved by the Council. He noted that the reduction had
been anticipated in the preparation of the 1998-99 Budget.
Trustee Meuer moved to adopt the resolution. He was seconded by Trustee Lien.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Hilton,
Duhl, Lien, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1415-98 – Vancor Consolidation: 83 Locust Road – Adoption. Ms.
McCammack explained that the request would result in the consolidation of an
existing zoning lot which currently consists of two tax parcel identification
numbers. It would also simplify the existing legal description which consists
of numerous small lots and portions of vacated streets and alleys. She
indicated that the subject property is currently in for a building permit for a
new residence and that consolidation is required under the new Code provisions.
The motion to adopt was made by Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: .
Resolution No. R-1416-98 – Woyner’s Subdivision: 93 Green Bay Road – Adoption.
Ms. McCammack explained that this subdivision would result in the consolidation
of an existing zoning lot which currently consists of three tax parcel
identification numbers. She indicated that at present there are no building
plans on file for the property. Mr. Williams commented that this property had
been identified in the current Comprehensive Plan as one which the Village
might want to acquire at some time in the future.
Trustee Derning moved to adopt the resolution, and he was seconded by Trustee
Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
   Warrant Lists Nos. 1069 and 1070. Noting that all of his questions had been
satisfactorily answered by Mr. McKee, Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1069 in the total amount of $269,997.97 and Warrant List No. 1064 in
the total amount of $228,902.24. Trustee Lanphier seconded the motion. By roll
call vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien,
Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Hilton and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Bid No. 98-009 – Henry Pratt Butterfly Valves. Manager Williams informed the
Council that the requested valves were very specific pieces of equipment used
to control the flow of water in the mixing basins of the Water Plant which
would replace the existing ones which are over 65 years old. He noted that the
Fiscal Year 1997-98 Budget provided a total $50,000 for these items, whereas
the lowest bid, from Henry Pratt Co., was for $36,571.
   Trustee Derning moved to award the bid to Henry Pratt Co. Trustee Hilton
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Mr. Williams referred Council to the
reports prepared by the individual department heads which were included in the
Agenda materials. He summarized the capital improvements for this fiscal year
noting that the only work which had not been completed was the drainage project
at Pine and Hibbard. He indicated that the Public Works Department hoped to
have this project underway by early summer.
   BFI Recycling Contract Extension. Steven Saunders, Assistant Village
Engineer, requested that the Council authorize a month-to-month extension of
the current recycling contract with BFI, at the increased rates quoted by BFI,
in order to provide seamless service to residents while bids for continuing the
Village’s recycling program are being sought and evaluated. He indicated that
BFI’s 30% increase in cost is consistent with their 30% increase in quantities
and explained that should a new vendor ultimately be selected, they might
require time to purchase new equipment or hire additional personnel in order to
service the Village. Mr. Saunders said that all vendors would have the
opportunity to conduct a prebid walk through on the heaviest day of the week
before submitting their bids. He pointed out that although any vendor selected
would be encouraged to strictly enforce the Village’s recycling procedures,
ultimately, the choice is the provider’s.
   President Holland inquired if there had been any improvement in the
residents’ response since the recent notice about appropriate recycling
procedures had been sent out. Mr. Saunders indicated that it was too early to
make that determination but noted that BFI had begun tagging the grosser
violations. Trustee Derning confirmed this saying that he had observed that BFI
had stopped picking up inappropriate materials.
   In response to a question from Trustee Hilton, Mr. Williams said that the
Village’s annual recycling costs are approximately $150,000.
   Trustee Lien moved to extend BFI’s contract as outlined by Mr. Saunders. He
was seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, and Hilton.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
   Village President. President Holland reported that on April 9, 1998,
representatives from Metra will be at the train station to perform commuter
safety information.
   She also reported on the Sarah Tueting award presentation and congratulated
the Park District for its efforts in organizing the event.
   Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported on a recent Chamber of Commerce
discussion regarding the provisions of the sign ordinance. He indicated that
members of the Chamber hoped to have the opportunity to share their thoughts
before the adoption of any amendments.
   Trustee Derning also expressed his compliments and appreciation to all
Village personnel for their response during the recent storm.
   Village Manager. Mr. Williams briefly reviewed reports from the Public Works
and Water and Electric Departments regarding the recent storm. He encouraged
any resident ever experiencing power failure to report it directly to Village
Hall.
He also advised the Council that IMEA is hosting a tour of The Trimble County
Power Plant, located outside of Louisville, Ky., to which all of the Trustees
were cordially invited.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland proposed that Pete Cruikshank be appointed to replace Ann
Zimmerman on the Board of Police and Fire Commissioners. Trustee Lien so moved,
seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
President Holland also thanked Peter Wemple for his service on Plan
Commission. She said that Joe Dooley would be canvassing the Park District
Board for a replacement representative to that commission.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)
(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, Meuer, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 8:35 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the motion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 9:47
p.m.
April 7, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved April 21, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
7, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien and Bill
Meuer. Absent: Trustee Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief William Gallagher,
Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack, and about 11 persons in the
audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that there had been an addition to the Agenda, Item
11E, regarding the 911 Indemnification Agreement with ETSB. The Agenda, as
amended, was unanimously approved by the Trustees.
IV. Quorum.
April 14, 1998, Study Session. Trustees Meuer and Lien indicated that they
would be unable to attend.
April 21, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be in attendance.
V. Approval of Minutes
March 10, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the Minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
March 17, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien,
moved to adopt the Minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-517-98 – Alley Vacation and Plat of Consolidation: 325 Fairview
(Montplaisir Consolidation) – Adoption. Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the
ordinance executing the vacation of the west ten feet of the unimproved alley
and consolidation of the vacated alley with the existing parcel. He was
seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Derning, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-202-98 – Amending Zoning Ordinance Pertaining to Building
Height, Intensity of Use, and Parking in the Multi-Family Residential Zoning
District – Adoption.
Ms. McCammack reviewed questions raised at the March 17, 1998, Regular Meeting.
With regard to requiring a minimum front setback for corner lots, Ms. McCammack
reported that staff had determined that although neighboring communities do not
have minimum buildable width requirements as proposed in this Ordinance, they
all have some controlling mechanism resulting in an absolute minimum front yard
setback requirement for corner lots. She recommended that the Village impose a
fifteen (15) foot minimum requirement for the front corner yard setback of
corner lots.
Ms. McCammack also reported that staff had looked at reducing the total
impermeable lot coverage allowance from the current 60% to 30-35% as suggested
by Thompson Dyke & Associates. Her conclusion was that such a reduction was too
drastic, and, therefore, recommended that the percentage be lowered to 45%.
Addressing the question of whether exclusive multiple family residential
developments should be permitted in the C-1 (Limited Retail) Commercial Zoning
District, Ms. McCammack expressed her opinion that until the Comprehensive Plan
has been adopted, it is advisable to maintain the status quo of the existing
predominant uses in this area. She pointed out that this zoning district is
located only within the Indian Hill Business District area. She also noted that
none of the five comparable communities surveyed permit exclusive residential
in their commercial districts.
Finally, Ms. McCammack addressed the question of implementing a density bonus
if parking is located underground and/or if more than the minimum required
parking is provided. Citing the need to closely consider the implications of
such allowances on adjacent single family residential neighborhoods, Ms.
McCammack recommended that a density allowance not be provided by right in this
ordinance and that the Council consider treating each situation on a case by
case basis through the special use permit process.  
Ms. McCammack then responded to further questions from the Trustees regarding
each of the issues.
Trustee Lanphier stated that she did not feel comfortable making a decision
about either the impermeable surface issue or the corner lot setback issue
without further information. With regard to the latter, she indicated that she
did not feel that fifteen feet provided enough of a buffer.
Trustee Lien questioned whether the proposed underground parking requirements
and the impermeable surface restrictions might be working at cross purposes
making development extremely difficult and expensive for prospective builders.
Noting the importance of moving forward with the proposed ordinance, Manager
Williams suggested that the Trustees adopt those amendments to the ordinance
with which they were comfortable and with regard to the outstanding issues
leave the existing standards in place to be amended at a future time.
Therefore, Trustee Derning moved to amend Ordinance M-517-98, as revised, with
the exceptions that the corner lot setback not be changed to 15 feet and the
maximum impermeable lot coverage be maintained at 60%. Trustee Hilton seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-204-98 – Amending Village Code Pertaining to Gasoline-Powered
Leaf Blowers – Adoption. The motion to adopt was made by Trustee Hilton,
seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees, Hilton, Derning, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Duhl.
Resolution No. R-1417-98 – Establishing Department Heads’ Salaries – Adoption.
Trustee Lien moved to adopt the Resolution and was seconded by Trustee Meuer.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Resolution No. R-1418-98 – Establishing Village Manager’s Salary – Adoption.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the Resolution, seconded by Trustee Derning. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
VIII. Unfinished Business
– None.
IX. Seating of New Village Council.
Local Canvass Board: Canvass and Proclamation and
Administration of Oath of Office for Village Trustees-elect Don Derning and
David Hilton. There being no unfinished business for the sitting Council,
President Holland asked the Village Clerk to review the results of the Local
Canvass Board. Village Clerk Williams read the results of the March 17, 1998,
election and reported that the Board had proclaimed Messrs. Derning, Duhl, and
Hilton duly elected to a second term as Village Trustees. He then administered
the Oath of Office to the Trustees-elect.
President Holland announced the Trustees’ different board and commission
assignments, as follows:
President Pro Tem     Bill Meuer
                      Mike Duhl, Chairman
Code Review Committee John Lien
                      Dave Hilton
Chamber of Commerce   Don Derning
                      Dave Hilton
Warrants Review       John Lien
Museum Liaison        Bill Meuer
Youth                 Dave Hilton
Winnetka Report       Dave Hilton
Plan Commission       Penny Lanphier
 
President Holland then called for a motion to approve the appointment of
Trustee Meuer as President Pro Tem of the Council. Trustee Derning so moved,
seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton and Lien. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Duhl.
X. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-205-98 - Amends Liquor Ordinance to Require Servers to be 21
Years of Age – Convening of the Liquor Advisory Board and Introduction. Manager
Williams reported that State law grants Villages the right to prohibit persons
under the age of twenty-one (21) from selling, distributing, delivering,
drawing, pouring, mixing or otherwise serving liquor to any person on the
premises of an establishment which has received a liquor license from the
Village. He indicated that as directed by the Trustees at the January 13, 1998
Study Session, staff had prepared such an ordinance for Council’s
consideration. He also indicated that all liquor licensees had been notified of
the proposed ordinance.
Before considering the introduction of the ordinance, President Holland
convened the Liquor Advisory Board and solicited comments from the liquor
licensees.
Patrick O’Neil, owner of O’Neil’s, voiced his strong opposition to the proposed
ordinance. He questioned the genesis of the proposal, noting that he had spoken
with Police Chief Gallagher, who had cited a single incident which occurred
some time ago at a now defunct establishment which was operated by an absentee
owner. Mr. O’Neil indicated that he would have to relocate and/or let go some
of his employees should the ordinance be passed. He said that he would have to
inform a number of local, returning college students to whom he has already
promised summer employment, that they would be unable work because they would
be underage. Mr. O’Neil expressed his opinion that liquor service in Winnetka
is already very restricted and that the local restaurateurs are on-premises
managers and well able to control the service of alcohol.
Robin Taylor and Sarah Sulger, Song O’Sixpence, Marianne Gigiolio, LaBella
Winnetka, and Jim Pomerantz, J. P. McCarthy’s, agreed with Mr. O’Neil, noting
that they rely heavily on 18-21 year olds, especially for seasonal help.
Dave Ruhlman, proprietor of Grand Foods, explained his policy with regard to
handling alcohol and expressed his belief that the liquor licensees in Winnetka
are extremely responsible and that no further controls are needed.
Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry Street, spoke as a local homeowner and parent in support
of the position taken by the restaurateurs. He pointed out that many local
students between the ages of 18 and 21 earn money for college by waiting tables
in local restaurants. In his opinion, there is no current problem which needs
correcting, and it is important for the community to support its youth by
making it possible for them to find this kind of employment.
President Holland then asked Police Chief Gallagher to comment. Mr. Gallagher
thanked all of the attendees for their input. He noted that he had queried
neighboring communities on their liquor policies and found Glenview,
Northbrook, Wilmette, and Glencoe have selected to follow the State of
Illinois. In those communities 18-21 year olds may serve, but must be 21 to
draw or pour; only Northfield is more restrictive. Chief Gallagher referred the
Council to a letter included in their packets from former Trustee Perry Fuller
and urged them to adopt the proposed ordinance.
President Holland called for a motion to adjourn the Liquor Advisory Board
meeting. Trustee Lien so moved. He was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice
vote, the meeting adjourned and returned to the regular Council meeting.
Trustee Hilton indicated that he thought that in view of the disparity of
opinion, the Council should not move to introduce the proposed ordinance.
Trustee Derning concurred.
Trustee Lien and Trustee Meuer both indicated that they would vote against the
ordinance should it be introduced. There was consensus among the Trustees that
some kind of a compromise ordinance should be considered which makes a
distinction between the various operations involved handling liquor. Trustee
Meuer urged that this issue be dealt with before the return of students for the
summer.
Ordinance No. M-520-98 - Disposition of Property Owned by the Village –
Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that the Village planned to dispose of eight
vehicles and/or items of equipment which are past their useful life and are
being replaced with other equipment. In keeping with past practice, the
vehicles are scheduled to be sold at the Northwest Municipal Conference’s
auction on May 9, 1998. He noted that in order for this to proceed, the Council
needed to declare the equipment as surplus and to authorize its sale by
auction.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Derning. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
XI. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1071 and 1072. Trustee Lien indicated that he had spoken
with Mr. McKee and found everything to be satisfactory. Trustee Lanphier moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1071 in the total amount of $607,867.64 and Warrant
List No. 1072 in the total amount of $428,303.86. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lanphier, Derning, Lien, Hilton and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Duhl.
Bid No. 97-013 – Electric Meters. Manager Williams reported that bid notices
for remote electric meters to be used for consumer metering for the remote
metering program for Fiscal Year 1998-99, with optional pricing to purchase the
same amount of meters for Fiscal Year 1999-00, had been mailed to three
prospective bidders. The successful bidder was Electric Laboratories & Sales in
the total amount of $81,360.00 for FY 98/99 and an additional $81,360 in FY 99/
00. He noted that the items had been provided for in the 1998-99 Budget.
Trustee Derning moved that the contract be awarded to Electric Laboratories &
Sales and a new Purchase Order in April, 1999, be issued for the same amount to
fund the FY 99/00 purchase. He was seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton
and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Winnetka Women’s Club Banners. Manager Williams explained that the Winnetka
Women’s Club has requested permission place banners on street light poles along
Green Bay Road between Elm and Oak Streets from May 4 to June 1, and from July
6 to July 27, 1998. The banners would be similar in appearance to those
approved in earlier years. Marsha Rhodes from the Women’s Club passed a picture
of former banners around for the Trustees to see. Trustee Lien moved to approve
the proposal, seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
165 Thorntree Sewer Easement. Manager Williams reviewed the genesis of the
dispute over the location of a sanitary sewer line between the Village and the
owners of the referenced property. He explained that the proposed Agreement and
Grant of Easement sets out the agreed upon terms for relocating the general
service line in a new easement and transfers maintenance responsibility for the
individual service line to the property owners. It also contains mutually
releasing claims, thereby, resolving the dispute.
Trustee Derning moved to adopt the Agreement as drafted, seconded by Trustee
Lien. By roll call vote the Trustees unanimously agreed to accept the proposed
Agreement.
911 Indemnification Agreement with ETSB. Attorney Janega explained that this
Agreement would enable the Village to continue its current practice of having
911 calls from unincorporated areas served by the Village relayed directly to
the Village. Trustee Hilton moved to approve the Agreement substantially in the
form as submitted to the Council, seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning,
Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
XII. Reports.
Village President. President Holland urged the Trustees to attend an upcoming
lecture by Richard Eyre sponsored by Forestry Commission and the Historical
Society. She also asked for and received a consensus among the Trustees to
participate in the annual Fourth of July parade.
Village Attorney. Ms. Janega invited the Trustees to inspect the municipal law
library which she has established in her office and thanked the Trustees and
Village Manager for their support
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported on the status of FEMA’s floodplain
maps, noting that an article covering the topic would be included in the
upcoming edition of Winnetka Report. He also reported on the damage to parkway
trees as a result of the winter storm on March 9, 1998. Finally, he updated the
Council on IDOT’s Five Year Highway Plan.
XIII. Appointments.
President Holland announced that Joe Dooley had suggested the appointment of
Ken Roffe as the Park District’s representative to the Plan Commission to
replace Peter Wemple who has resigned because of conflicting obligations. She
called for a motion to confirm the appointment of Mr. Roffe. Trustee Lanphier
so moved, seconded by Trustee Meuer. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
XIV. Executive Session.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)
(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Hilton seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:40 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XV. Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:41 p.m.
April 14, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved April 21, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
14, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Penfield Lanphier, Michael Duhl and David Hilton. Absent:
Trustees John Lien and Bill Meuer. Also present: Village Manger Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water
& Electric Bryan McInturff, and Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack.
II. Swearing in of Trustee Michael Duhl.
Manager Williams administered the oath of office to Trustee-elect Michael Duhl.
III. Pre-Audit Meeting – Irwin Lyons.
Mr. McKee introduced Irwin Lyons, a partner at Miller, Cooper and Company, the
Village’s independent auditor, who briefly explained the upcoming audit
process. Mr. Lyons reviewed comments in the Management Letter and indicated
that the auditors would be following up on the implementation of what has
already been accomplished. Mr. McKee stated he had also asked the auditors to
review the Village’s billing practices in an effort to assure timely
collections. Mr. Lyons added that the review of billing practices would also
include a review of the Village’s collection practices. Mr. Williams, Mr. McKee
and Mr. Lyons also responded to questions from the Trustees.
IV. Possible Building Code Amendment – Smoke Detectors.
Manager Williams explained the current conflict between the Village’s Building
Code and State law. He pointed out that in applying the stricter standards
required by local ordinance, which incorporates the CABO model standards, it
has appeared inequitable to require a resident to install hardwired,
interconnected smoke detectors throughout their home when isolated improvements
are proposed in unrelated areas of their residence. He explained that the Fire
Department has been using a 50% rule which seems to be a reasonable compromise
solution.
Ms. McCammack added her comments. She indicated that neighboring communities
had been surveyed and that only Northfield strictly enforced the CABO
standards. She suggested that the Council balance cost with reason and consider
an ordinance which would define “substantially remodeled” to establish an
appropriate minimum level of improvements necessary before accessing internal
wiring to make permanent smoke detector installations. She also stated that she
and the Fire Chief agreed that requiring a smoke detector in each sleeping room
as well as on each floor, with hardwiring being required only if the 50%
threshold is met, would strike a reasonable balance.
There was consensus among the Trustees to pursue amending the Village Code as
suggested.
V. Sign Code Draft.
Attorney Janega referred the Trustees to the Discussion Draft of the Sign Code
distributed with the meeting materials. She enumerated the items identified as
policy issues to be considered by the Council. She indicated that very little
had been done since 1980, making this a comprehensive rewrite of the policies.
She noted that most of the substantive changes were generated by staff, while
she had added structure more in keeping with the newer portions of the Village
Code. Ms. McCammack added that she did not think that there was a lot of
substantive change; the biggest change is reorganization and grouping
everything together.
   Trustee Derning requested a matrix comparing the current and proposed
policies. He said that this would be helpful not only to the Trustees but to
other interested parties, especially members of the Chamber of Commerce.
   The Trustees also discussed possible amortization provisions and requested
further information before making any decision with regard to this issue.
VI. Underground Electric Service – Cost Distribution.
Mr. McInturff presented a historical perspective of the Village’s electric
services and pointed out some of the service disparities which have resulted.
He reviewed possible options available and asked for direction from the
Trustees.
Manager Williams indicated that it was common practice for Villages to take
maintenance responsibility for utilities up to the meter and recommended that
the Village adopt such a policy.
After discussing the alternatives illustrated in Mr. McInturff’s Agenda Report,
the Trustees agreed that the Village ought to continue to install underground
service at no charge to the homeowner so long as that installation was
performed in conjunction with the Village’s scheduled program of putting the
distribution feeders underground. The customer will be required to pay the
costs of putting the service underground if it is for new construction or for
an upgrade of an existing service that is not part of the Village’s scheduled
program.
VII. Fairview Water Main Extension.
Mr. McInturff outlined the Water and Electric Department’s request for approval
of the installation of an 8” water main on Fairview Avenue from Willow Road to
Orchard Lane. The estimated the cost of the work is $45,500. He explained that
the timing was propitious because of new homes being constructed on the street.
Mr. Williams added that this was work which would eventually have to be done
anyway, when the 12” main planned for Willow Road is installed in the next 5
years. The new main would also reduce the length of some of the service
connections.
Mr. Duhl inquired as to where the money would come from since this was not
included in the Budget. Mr. Williams indicated that funding would come from the
Water Fund.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed with the plan as presented
by Mr. McInturff.
VIII. Request for Raffle License.
Manager Williams brought to Council’s attention a request from a resident to
conduct a raffle on behalf of an injured NTHS student. Ms. Janega explained
that although such a raffle was legal under the State statutes, it could not be
authorized under the existing Village Code, which limits raffles to charitable,
religious, veterans’ or educational organizations which have been in existence
for a minimum of five years. The Trustees agreed to amend the Code accordingly
so as to permit this type of raffle.
IX. Executive Session.
It was moved by Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Duhl, that the Trustees
adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of discussing the possible sale
of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(6) of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. On roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Duhl, Hilton and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and Meuer. The
Trustees adjourned into executive session at 9:15 p.m.
The Trustees returned to open session at 9:46 p.m. Still present were Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton and Lanphier and Village President Holland.
X. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:47 p.m.
April 21, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved May 5, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
21, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and Bill
Meuer. Absent: Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Fire Chief Ron Colpaert. There were no
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
   May 5, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer indicated that he will not be in
attendance.
   May 12, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Hilton indicated that he will not be in
attendance.
V. Approval of Minutes
   April 7, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning moved to adopt the Minutes
as drafted, seconded by Trustee Lien. Trustee Hilton noted a correction to be
made on page 3 of the minutes, stating that the Oath of Office was not
administered to Trustee Duhl who was absent. By voice vote, the motion to
approve the minutes as amended was carried unanimously.
   April 14, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the Minutes as
drafted, seconded by Trustee Derning. Trustee Lien requested a change to page 3
to indicate that he was not present at this meeting and to renumber the
paragraphs on page 2. By voice vote, the motion to approve the minutes as
amended was carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-520-98 – Disposition of Property Owned by the Village –
Adoption.
This ordinance was introduced at the April 7, 1998 Council meeting to dispose
of eight vehicles and other items of equipment owned by the Village. These
items are being disposed of because they are past their useful life and have
been or are being replaced with other equipment.
   Trustee Meuer moved to adopt the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lanphier.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien, Hilton, Meuer, Lanphier
Nayes: None
Absent: Trustee Duhl
Ordinance No. MC-206-98 – Residential Smoke Detector Standards – Introduction.
Village Manager Williams explained that this ordinance amends the Building and
Fire Prevention Chapters of the Village Code to require hard-wired smoke alarms
in a home if remodeling exceeds 50% of the value of the home and to define the
term, “substantially remodeled,” of the Illinois Smoke Detector Act. Manager
Williams gave an example of a resident who was remodeling his basement but was
required to put in hard-wired smoke detectors on the second floor under the
current Code provisions. After discussing the issue with staff, it was decided
to request the Code be amended to require hard-wired smoke detectors if
remodeling is in excess of 50%.
Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce the ordinance, Trustee Lien seconded. By
voice vote, the ordinance was introduced unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1419-98 – Unincorporated Charges for Fire and Ambulance
Service – Adoption. Fire Chief Colpaert summarized the increase of
approximately $56.00 per residence in charges to Kenilworth and unincorporated
New Trier Township residents for fire and ambulance services and asked the
Council to adopt the resolution. The cost to these residents increased due to
the increase in the number of calls to these areas, an increase in assessed
valuation, and an increase in the Fire Department budget because of the
responses to these areas. Trustee Hilton asked if the charges were per home.
Chief Colpaert indicated that they are. Trustee Lanphier asked if Kenilworth
and unincorporated Northfield knew of us joining RED Center. Manager Williams
said he was attending a meeting to advise them later this month. President
Holland asked if Woodley Road was included in this, and Manager Williams
indicated that those residences are included.
   Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt the resolution, seconded by Trustee Derning.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien, Hilton, Meuer, Lanphier
Nayes: None
Absent: Trustee Duhl
Ordinance No. MC-208-98 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to Gambling and
Raffles – Adoption.
Village Attorney Janega summarized this amendment to the Village Code to permit
raffles by not-for profit organizations for the purpose of providing financial
assistance to individuals suffering extreme financial hardship as a result of
illness, disability, accident or disaster. This type of raffle is permitted by
State statute and will also be permitted by local ordinance once the amendment
is adopted. Also recommended is that rules be waived in order to allow this
ordinance to be introduced and adopted at one Council meeting.
Council unanimously approved proceeding with this and to waive the rules.
Attorney Janega stated that this would be reflected in the findings in the
ordinance. Trustee Derning asked what the fee was; Manager Williams explained
that the fee has already been set by resolution and is $25.00.
Trustee Derning moved to waive the rules, seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll
call vote, motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lien, Lanphier, Meuer.
Nayes: None
Absent: Mike Duhl
Trustee Lien moved to adopt the amendment to the ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Lanphier. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. .
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lien, Lanphier, Meuer.
Nayes: None
Absent: Mike Duhl
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
   Warrant Lists Nos. 1073 and 1074. Trustee Hilton moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1073 in the total amount of $218,602.76 and Warrant List No. 1072 in
the total amount of $265,213.57. Trustee Lanphier seconded the motion. By roll
call vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier, Lien, Hilton and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Michael Duhl.
   Trustee Lien stated that he had asked Ed McKee to do a report on telephone
line item; Manager Williams indicated that it will be in the next packet. Ed
did advise Trustee Lien that there did not appear to be any trend during the
last year. Trustee Lien also questioned the item on Pete the Painter next to
Village Hall. Manager Williams said that Pete the Painter had the contract to
remove and replace the stairs between the Village Hall and the Harris Bank
after a village employee had fallen there. Trustee Lien also questioned the
item on page 19, the Oakton Community College training session. Manager
Williams stated that this item covered computer training for several people,
not just one person, and that approximately 13 employees had been sent to this
training session.
Bid No. 98-0111- Tree Trimming, Removal & Disposal: 
Bid notices were mailed out for the purchase of tree trimming, removal and
disposal on February 22; Staff recommends Council award lowest bidder, Robert
Hendricksen at $62,670. President Holland asked if this contract includes
trimming around electric lines, and Manager Williams replied that it does.
Trustee Lanphier then asked how the trimming was coming and if we have worked
with this company before. Manager Williams replied that the Village has worked
with this company before and President Holland said that they had done the
Village trimming last year and Nels Johnson had done the electric trimming.
Trustee Meuer commented that he remembered about a year ago that Nels Johnson
had been before the board because of complaints. President Holland replied that
2 or 3 years ago there were numerous complaints on Nels Johnson’s work and it
was unacceptable; Trustee Meuer asked if there was a reason why this bid wasn’t
broken out into two separate contracts as was done last year. Manager Williams
replied that he would need to get more information for the Council and asked if
this item could be tabled until the next meeting.
   Trustee Derning moved to continue this item for next meeting, seconded by
Trustee Lanphier. Motion passed unanimously by voice vote.
Bid Award - Bid No. 98-002 – Street Rehabilitation:
Bid notices were sent out April 9 for street rehabilitation. Manager Williams
stated that the low bidder was Johnson and the staff recommendation is to award
contract to the low bidder. Trustee Hilton asked if rehabilitation and
resurfacing were the only components in this contract. Manager Williams said
this award was just for street rehabilitation.
Trustee Lanphier moved to award the bid to Johnson Paving, seconded by Trustee
Hilton. Motion was carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Meuer and Lien
Nayes: None
Absent: Trustee Duhl
X. Reports.
   Village President. None.
   Village Trustees. Trustee Hilton reported that the Chamber of Commerce met
last Thursday and the vacancy list is now at 10. There is good news that
Lakeside Shoes has a new tenant. Frances Heffernan is going to move from the
Caledonian building to this location for approximately 10 months. The Chamber
of Commerce also discussed the Caledonian site and members were excited about
it. President Holland was there, as was Cindy McCammack who reviewed the
drafted sign code amendments.
   Village Attorney. None.
   Village Manager.
   RED Center: The fire chiefs of RED Center have voted to recommend to RED
Center’s Board that Winnetka be allowed to join.
   Revised Estimate for Elm Street Bridge: Due to the revised estimate for the
decorative railing, the cost estimate for the Elm Street Bridge rehabilitation
has been increased from $650,000 to $810,000. Since the Village’s share of
these costs is 20%, the Village’s estimated costs have increased from $130,000
to $162,000 or about $32,000.
   The special Council Meeting on April 28 has been canceled. This meeting was
scheduled to address the New Trier expansion, but since the Zoning Board and
Plan Commission are not meeting until April 29, the Council’s meeting will be
rescheduled.
   Electronic Utility Bill Payments: At the request of the Village Council,
staff members prepared a report on the status of the automatic utility bill
payment program. The report indicates that 844 customers, or 15% of all Village
customers are participating in the program. Trustee Hilton asked if a bonus
could be given to residents who sign up for automatic bill payment. Manager
Williams replied that he would discuss this with staff.
   President Holland congratulated Manager Williams and staff on the Winnetka
Report, and reminded residents of clean up week in April. Trustee Hilton
formally thanked Ursula Hoeft for her work on the Winnetka Report.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lanphier seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Duhl. The
Council adjourned into executive Session at 7:55 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:38 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lanphier seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
8:40 p.m.
May 5, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved May 19, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 5,
1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, and John Lien. Absent:
Trustees Michael Duhl and Bill Meuer. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Assistant Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Finance
Director Ed McKee, and about five persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
May 12, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
May 19, 1998, Regular Meeting. Mr. Lien indicated that he would be unable to
attend and Mr. Hilton indicated that he expected to arrive late.
V. Approval of Minutes
April 21, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Lanphier made two minor corrections to
the draft before Trustee Lien moved to adopt the minutes, as amended, seconded
by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
MollyBelle Berish commented that Glencoe had installed a very good sign
regarding the leashing of dogs on the Green Bay Trail. She also expressed her
compliments to BFI for the job they have done as the Village’s recycling
provider. Mr. Williams responded to questions from Mrs. Berish regarding the
distribution of “green sheets,” the safety of the antique desk on display in
the Village Hall lobby, and the timing of the stop light at Green Bay Road and
Elm Street.
   Mr. Sturgis Chadwick requested that the Council expedite consideration of
his request for a vacation of property.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
   Ordinance No. MC-206-98 – Residential Smoke Detector Standards – Adoption.
Attorney Janega noted that some of the language had been changed from the
original draft in order to clarify the intent of the Council, but that the
standards remained the same. Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the amendments to
the drafted ordinance which was introduced on April 21, 1998. He was seconded
by Trustee Derning.    By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Meuer.
   Trustee Derning then moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended, seconded by
Trustee Lanphier. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Hilton and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl
and Meuer.
   Ordinance No. MC-207-98 – Amending the Village Code to Clarify the Authority
of the Village Manager as it Pertains to Parking Regulations – Introduction.
Manager Williams commented on a recent court challenge which warrants amending
the Code to clarify the authority of the Village Manager to establish parking
standards and regulations in parking lots.
   Trustee Hilton moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lien.
By roll call vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and Meuer.
   Resolution No. R-1422-98 – Adoption of the Prevailing Rate of Wages for the
Construction of Public Works – Adoption. Manager Williams explained that under
the provisions of the State of Illinois Prevailing Wage Act, the Village is
required annually to investigate and ascertain the prevailing rate of wages for
laborers, mechanics, and other workers in the Village employed in performing
construction of public works for the Village. The findings of that
investigation are incorporated into and made a part of this resolution.
   Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt Resolution No. R-1422-98 which establishes
prevailing wage rates for the Village of Winnetka. She was seconded by Trustee
Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lanphier, Hilton, Lien, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Meuer.
   Ordinance No. MC-522-98 – Zoning Variations: 756 Foxdale – Introduction.
Cindy McCammack, Director of Community Development, reviewed the history of
this request by the applicants to permit an increased building size (GFA) and
responded to questions from the Trustees. Trustee Lanphier clarified that it is
the design of the house which creates the hardship, not its location on
Foxdale.
   The applicant, John Larson, spoke in support of his petition and presented
photos and documents for Council’s consideration.
   Trustee Lien indicated that he was in favor of allowing the request citing
the modest size of the variation requested and the significant improvement to
the home for future use. Trustees Hilton and Derning concurred.
   Trustee Lanphier pointed out that it has been procedural issues which have
made this such a difficult case for the Council and Zoning Board of Appeals to
resolve. She explained that the variation process has to be taken seriously;
homeowners should not get the message that they can proceed without going
through appropriate procedures and expect to win approval. However, she
indicated that in this instance, she supports the request.
   Trustee Hilton moved to introduce the ordinance permitting the requested
zoning variation, seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and Meuer.
   Ordinance No. MC-521-98 – Zoning Variation: 1177 Ash St. – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack explained that the proposed variation would permit the subdivision of
a single lot into two lots with reduced lot widths. Each of the two new lots
would have a width of fifty (50) feet, representing a reduction from the
minimum lot width of sixty (60) feet, as required in the A-1 Zoning District.
She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted in favor of this variance
and pointed out that Introduction of the ordinance would require a majority
vote of the Trustees present.
   The applicant Richard Radclifffe spoke in support of his request.
   Attorney Janega suggested that the Council might wish to impose a condition
upon the granting of the subdivision requiring that no building be done on the
property until the subdivision is actually recorded, so as to prevent the
possibility that the owners could change their minds, not record the
subdivision and then proceed to build an oversized house.
   Trustee Hilton moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lien.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   Resolution No. R-1421-98 – A Resolution Pertaining to the Pledge of
Securities for the Benefit of the Village of Winnetka – Adoption. Mr. McKee
explained that this resolution facilitates some of the banking changes approved
by the Council last fall. In particular this safekeeping resolution pertains to
the pledge of securities for the benefit of the Village. Pursuant to this
resolution an account will be established which will be used to hold collateral
for Village deposits.
   Trustee Lien moved to adopt Resolution No. R-1421-98, and he was seconded by
Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lien, Derning, Lanphier, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl
and Meuer.
   Ordinance No. MC-209-98 – Revision of Chapter 27 Pertaining to the Sign Code
– Public Hearing. President Holland declared the Public Hearing open. Ms.
McCammack briefly reviewed the documents included in the Agenda materials. She
also distributed a list of nonconformities which would be created by the
proposed revisions. The Trustees discussed some of the provisions of the new
ordinance including a possible amortization clause.
   President Holland expressed her objection to the inclusion of an
amortization clause indicating that it could create an unreasonable financial
burden, particularly for some of the smaller businesses. She said she thought
it could be counterproductive at a time when some merchants are struggling to
stay viable and the Village is trying to encourage merchants to remain in the
community.
   Trustee Lanphier asked for feedback from the Chamber of Commerce on the
proposed ordinance.
   Joan Noyes, 381 Elder Street, thanked the Village Trustees for taking this
issue under consideration . She expressed her belief that Winnetka has the
opportunity to be leader among communities by enacting an ordinance which
prevents the obliteration by signage of its natural and architectural beauty.
   There being no further comments from the floor, President Holland closed the
Public Hearing saying that the proposed ordinance would be back on Council’s
Agenda after further study could be conducted of the proposed amortization
clause and the receipt of comments from the Chamber of Commerce.
   Manager Williams thanked Mrs. Noyes for spearheading this process, and
Trustee Derning complimented the Village staff on the technical data compiled.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
   Bid No. 98-011 – Tree Trimming, Removal and Disposal. Manager Williams
addressed the issues outstanding from the last Council meeting. He noted that
the bid was for public works trees only and did not involve any electric line
work. He indicated that in the past the Village has been pleased by the work of
Robert Hendricksen who submitted the lowest bid.
   Trustee Hilton moved to accept the bid of Robert Hendricksen in the total
estimated amount of $62,670.00 for Fiscal Year 1998-99. The motion was seconded
by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl
and Meuer.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1075 and 1076. Trustee Lien indicated that he had reviewed
the lists with Mr. McKee and found everything to be in order. He then moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1075 in the total amount of $404,800.08 and Warrant
List No. 1076 in the total amount of $224,933.96. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lien, Derning, Lanphier, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl
and Meuer.
   Zoning Variation: 807 Burr Avenue. Mr. Williams asked that it be noted in
the minutes that this item was removed from the Agenda at the request of the
applicant.
   Recycling Proposal Analysis. Assistant Village Engineer, Steven Saunders
reviewed the proposals received by the Village for providing curbside recycling
services. He compared the three bids received with regard to legal compliance,
financial data, technical merit, and cost effectiveness. He noted that the FY
1998-99 Budget contains $163,300 for contracted recycling services. However,
the Village’s costs for the year project out to $194,798, a shortfall of
$31,498. Mr. Saunders indicated that staff was looking at several options for
covering this shortfall. In summary, he recommended that the Trustees authorize
a five year contract with Browning-Ferris Industries to provide curbside
recycling services for a total of $1,044,744.
   Trustee Hilton inquired as to the history of recycling in the Village and
asked if costs would be less if there was no recycling. Manager Williams
indicated that they would not because SWANCC’s tipping fees are so high. He
also pointed out that recycling is mandated by State law.
   Trustee Lanphier questioned whether the new enforcement of recycling rules
were having the desired effect. Mr. Saunders indicated that they were working.
   Trustee Derning moved to approve the proposal by Browning Ferris Industries,
seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Hilton and Lien. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Duhl and Meuer.
   Equipment Disposal. Mr. McKee detailed the equipment, primarily computer
components, to be disposed of and outlined suggested disposal methods. He asked
that the Trustees approve the equipment for disposal as noted in his Agenda
Report and indicated that an ordinance approving same would follow.
   Trustee Hilton moved to accept Mr. McKee’s recommendation. He was seconded
by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl
and Meuer.
   Bid No. 98-010 – 1998 Tree Planting Program. Mr. Saunders explained that
this bid is for the purchase and planting of approximately 186 parkway trees
within the Village for 1998. He noted that the Fiscal Year 1998-99 Budget
contains $50,000 for parkway tree planting and recommended to the Council that
they award the bid to the four vendors noted in the Agenda Report in the total
amount of $46,523.30.
   Trustee Lien moved to award the bids, as noted, seconded by Trustee Hilton.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien,
Derning, Lanphier, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and Meuer.
   Bid No. 98-014 – Conduit Installation. Manager Williams explained that this
bid is for the installation of conduit by the directional bore method for
Fiscal Year 1998-99, and noted that $200,000 was contained in the Budget to
cover these costs. Staff’s recommendation is to accept the bid of Archon
Construction Co., Inc. in the amount of $107,572.50 for the performance of such
work, on an “as needed and when directed” basis.
   Trustee Derning moved to award the bid as recommended. He was seconded by
Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Lanphier, Hilton and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Meuer.
   Bid No. 98-007 – Storm Sewer & Junction Chamber. Mr. Saunders explained that
this bid is for the construction of storm sewer on Pine Street east of Hibbard
Road and arch storm sewer on Hibbard Rd. from Pine St. to Willow Road as a part
of the drainage relief project. He noted that $450,000 had been allocated in
the Fiscal Year 1998-99 Budget for this project. He recommended that the
contract be awarded to the lowest bidder, Glenbrook Excavating & Concrete,
Inc., for a total amount of $387,549.06.
   Trustee Hilton moved to award the bid as recommended. He was seconded by
Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Meuer.
   Bid No. 98-017 – 165 Thorntree Lane Sanitary Sewer Relocation. Mr. Saunders
reviewed the history of this project and indicated that funds for this project
had been included in the current Budget. He recommended that the Trustees
accept the bid of Glenbrook Excavating in the amount of $44,140.
   Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Lanphier, Hilton and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Meuer.
   Bid No. 98-003 – 1998 Street Reconstruction. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids
for the 1998 Street Reconstruction Project, covering sections of Prospect
Avenue, Lincoln Avenue, and Provident Avenue in addition to the Duke Child
Field parking lot.. He recommended a motion awarding the contract to Glenbrook
Excavating in the amount of $574,831.03.
   Trustee Hilton moved to award the bid as recommended. He was seconded by
Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Meuer.
X. Reports.
   Village Manager.
Woodley Road Agreements. Mr. Williams advised the Council that all of the
formal details pertaining to providing police service to the Woodley Road
residents have been completed and that the three year service agreement with
the homeowners’ association has been signed.
Certificate of Achievement of Excellence in Financial Reporting. Mr. Williams
announced the receipt of this award for the Village’s comprehensive annual
financial report. Noting that this represents a significant accomplishment by
the Village, he thanked Mr. McKee and his staff for their efforts in attaining
this achievement.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland announced with regret that Susan Connor has resigned from the
Zoning Board of Appeals and proposed Mary-Liz Lichtenfels to replace her. She
also proposed Rachel Blank, who has been filling the term of Steve Gomberg on
the same board, to serve her own term. Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee
Hilton, moved to approved the appointments proposed by President Holland. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lanphier seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Lien,
Derning, Hilton, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and Meuer. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 9:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:23 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, and Lien. Absent: Trustees Duhl
and Meuer. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:25 p.m.
May 12, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved June 2, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
12, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, and John Lien. Absent:
Trustees Penny Lanphier and Bill Meuer. Also present: Village Manger Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Police Chief William Gallagher, Assistant Village Engineer
Steven Saunders, and about 25 persons in the audience.
II. Application for Zoning Variance and Special Use Permit by New Trier High
School.
President Holland welcomed representatives from the Board of Education, New
Trier High School staff and members of the audience and explained the
procedures for the evening’s meeting.
Trustees Lanphier and Meuer arrived at 7:45 p.m.
   Mr. James Nowacki, President of the School Board, thanked the Village
Council on behalf of the Board and New Trier High School administration,
faculty and staff for the opportunity to make their presentation. In doing so,
he recognized that each body has its own sphere of responsibility and that the
goal was to achieve a satisfactory conclusion through intergovernmental
cooperation. He acknowledged that the Special Use Permit and variations
requested are for only one of the four models studied by the School Board for
meeting the enrollment challenges of the future, and that ultimately, that body
must decide which option to pursue. He pledged to work with the Council to
resolve every issue of concern and to foster a continuing amiable relationship
between the high school and the community.
   Dr. Hank Bangser, Superintendent of New Trier Township High School, echoed
Mr. Nowacki’s thanks. He briefly reviewed the history of the project and the
projections for the future. He also provided an abbreviated analysis of the
four models studied for housing the anticipated additional students, pointing
out some of their respective strengths and weaknesses.
Then Dr. Bangser addressed the proposed single school concept in greater
detail, emphasizing the need for an additional 95,000 sq. ft. and illustrating
the proposal with various architectural renditions. In doing so, he reiterated
that this is but one of the alternatives available to the School Board and that
the opinions among the Board, the Administration and the community are not
unanimously in favor of any one of the alternative models.
Specifically acknowledging concerns about increased vehicular traffic resulting
from the increased enrollment, Dr. Bangser outlined components incorporated
into the single school plan addressed directly to this problem. These include
the following: (1) building a new parking area on school property on the east
side of Essex where the old spectator stands formerly stood; (2) constructing a
new road connecting Essex to Woodland on the north side of the high school
which would bring traffic around the high school on its own property; and (3)
considering the construction of a parking garage at Winnetka and Green Bay
Roads, which would depend upon negotiations with the Village.
   In response to Trustee Hilton’s question as to why one campus is considered
the most viable option when there is, in fact, another school facility which
can be used, Dr. Bangser indicated that the single school model was one of the
two most favored in the community survey. This choice was echoed by the staff,
students and faculty. He stated that the tremendous synergies would result from
having all of the staff working together and would provide students with
broader access to curriculum.
   New Trier High School principal, Wes Baumann, pointed out some of the issues
involved in trying to create two equal schools so as to provide equal access to
equal resources of the community. He also noted that there was the possibility
that enrollment would once again decline after the predicted surge.
   Trustee Derning inquired as to the extent of modifications required to the
east campus buildings regardless of an increase in enrollment. Dr. Bangser and
John Nieweem, Director of Physical Plant Services, responded pointing to
examples of deteriorating conditions, deferred maintenance and life safety
work, in addition to an outdated cafeteria, and the inability to maximize
effective use of existing space. Mr. Nieweem expressed his personal opinion
that it was not wise to invest significantly in the existing inefficient
building footprints, but stressed that this should not be taken to mean that
the buildings are unsafe.
   In introductory comments, Village Manager Doug Williams stated one of the
major policy components of the single school proposal is whether or not to
construct a parking deck at Winnetka Avenue and Green Bay Road. He noted that
New Trier High School has made it clear that if the community opposes such a
parking facility, the school could shift existing parking from students to
staff and limit the number of parking permits issued to the existing number of
parking spaces available. He pointed out that in this event, it was the
considered opinion of both Village staff and the Police Department that a
minimum of two additional Community Service Officers would need to be employed
to enforce parking.
Mr. Williams then explained that Steve Saunders, Assistant Village Engineer,
would present a brief report on traffic circulation and parking and that Police
Chief Bill Gallagher would review parking enforcement.
Trustee Derning asked Village Attorney Janega to clarify the options available
to the Council given the recommendations with contingency conditions received
from the Plan Commission and the Zoning Board of Appeals. Ms. Janega responded
that the Council could accept or reject any or all of the conditions
recommended by the Plan Commission and the Zoning Board of Appeals. She also
stated that the Council could impose additional conditions if the Council found
they were necessary. She added that the recommendations of the Plan Commission
and Zoning Board were just that, and did not bind the Council to a particular
course of action.
Mr. Williams summarized saying that the Village Council must make the ultimate
determination, but that conditions may be incorporated in the final ordinance.
Trustee Duhl expressed his belief that any conditions ought to be specific and
measurable or not noted at all.
III. Village Staff Analysis of Traffic Circulation Around NTHS and Staff
Analysis of Parking Demand in NTHS Area.
Mr. Saunders reviewed his report to the Council summarizing traffic and
pedestrian congestion and neighborhood traffic activity. He outlined Staff’s
recommendations for the improvement/mitigation of traffic congestion,
pedestrian safety and parking in the New Trier area. He mentioned additional
alternatives deserving of further study and noted others which had been
considered but rejected for a variety of reasons. Mr. Saunders distributed a
projection of the increased parking demand needed to accommodate the increased
enrollment and a comparison of the student parking allocations provided by nine
neighboring high schools.
   Trustee Duhl inquired as to whether the Plan Commission and the Zoning Board
of Appeals had been given all of this information prior to making their
recommendations. Mr. Saunders replied that they had.
   Addressing one of the possible solutions to the parking problems, a parking
deck for the corner of Green Bay and Winnetka Avenue, Manager Williams noted
that it was possible that some funds might be obtained from METRA and/or ISTEA,
in proportion to the number of commuter spaces provided. However, the remainder
of the funding would need to come from New Trier. He pointed out that
discussion about the desirability of such a parking lot was currently being
studied as a part of the comprehensive planning process and that a report would
be forthcoming in the near future.
   Trustee Meuer asked whether new Community Service Officers would be a cost
to the Village or to New Trier. Mr. Williams replied that in his opinion, any
such costs ought to be borne by the school.
   Trustee Lien questioned whether a parking deck wouldn’t make for additional
traffic congestion problems of its own, particularly at ingress and egress. He
also raised the question of security issues associated with a parking deck. Mr.
Saunders replied that any traffic problems could be easily managed, and Chief
Gallagher mentioned a couple of options for addressing security concerns.
   Police Chief Gallagher reviewed parking enforcement procedures and problems.
He pointed out that issuing parking tickets, which are intended to change
behavior, prove to be ineffective, particularly in the New Trier area. He said
that parking tickets are routinely perceived merely as the cost of parking. He
explained that because of the way the system is established there is no
accurate way of determining how many tickets are issued to students. He also
noted that Winnetka’s increased enforcement has pushed the parking problem into
the neighboring communities of Kenilworth and Wilmette to the south.
   John Nieweem, Director of Physical Plan Services at New Trier, stated that
the school had placed video cameras at six roof top locations during the
morning and afternoon peak periods to determine traffic counts and the number
of students being dropped off. He said that information would be provided to
the Village. He then thanked the Village for its assistance and suggestions and
indicated that the school would be working to make improvements regardless of
the outcome of this request.
   President Holland announced that this issue would be placed on the Agenda
for next Tuesday’s regular Council meeting, at which time residents will have
further opportunity to voice their opinions. She then declared that there would
be a five minute break before proceeding with tonight’s public comment.
V. Public Comment.
   MollyBelle Berish spoke in support of two four year high schools, saying
that there would be more opportunities for more students to pursue their
talents and interests. She also offered a historical perspective of the one
campus vs. two campus model.
Mike and Sondra Hannafan, 547 Roslyn, Kenilworth, whose property backs onto
Winnetka Avenue, echoed Ms. Berish’s sentiments and vigorous opposition to the
issuance of the special use permit and the construction of one “megaschool.”
They cited some of their experiences with parking and student behavior
problems.
Bob Fuller, 162 Fuller Lane, also spoke in opposition to this zoning issue. He
specifically addressed traffic congestion and associated safety concerns and
loss of green space. He also asked if there was a backup plan should NTHS’s
number projections be too low. He urged the Board to consider the community
impact of their decision and to set clearly defined, measurable conditions for
the granting of the requested special use permit.
Tom Buck, 350 Winnetka Avenue, asked to the Board to deny the school’s request.
He pointed out that the vote from the Zoning Board of Appeals does not
accurately reflect how close the vote actually was. He commented that the
public turnout for tonight’s meeting was poor and not reflective of public
sentiment.
Art Bloom, 138 Woodland, stated his belief that the parking and traffic
problems will only be exacerbated by larger enrollment and that whatever
solutions the traffic engineers devise will only divert traffic and parking
from one street to another. He also brought up concerns about problems
associated with the proposed construction, particularly noise.
Wes Baumann, 445 Sunset, specifically addressed the Sunset Road issue stating
that it is his belief that no major traffic problem exists. He said he had
videotaped morning and afternoon traffic on Susset and found a very short peak
traffic period, no hot rodding, and minimal littering.
Kevin Plunkett, 80 Warwick, noted that the proposed parking garage is an
integral part of the one school model and asked the Council to wait on a
decision until the Comprehensive Plan has addressed both this issue and the
Indian Hill district as a whole.
Jennifer Weaver, 136 Chestnut, spoke in support of the two school concept
saying that it might be a more viable alternative to serve the best interests
of the community and the children. She says she hears the Village moving toward
making Herculean efforts to meet the request from the School Board which don’t
seem to solve bigger problems created for the community.
Dale Cleavenger, 82 Warwick Road, said that the lack of turnout for tonight’s
meeting resulted from the fact that neighbors are frustrated with the process
as a whole; that their expectation is that the Village Board will give the
School Board whatever it wants, regardless of the community’s feelings. On
behalf of the neighbors, he voiced strong opposition to a parking garage on
Green Bay at Winnetka Avenue.
Lauren Tazlitz, 1210 Broadmeadow, noted that the true increase in demand for
parking as a result of increased enrollment can only be projected; it is, in
reality, a complete unknown. She also expressed security concerns about a
parking deck, especially after school hours and nights.
Peter Kelly, 366 Winnetka, stated that traffic problems need to be addressed,
regardless of the model chosen. He urged delay of any vote on the special use
permit until a satisfactory resolution to the traffic problems can be devised.
Steve Treacy, Chicago, spoke in favor of the single school model. He cited the
long relationship between NTHS and the Village of Winnetka, and pointed out
that regardless of which model was chosen, there were deferred maintenance
issues at the east campus which had to be attended to.
Barry McAllister, 108 Fuller Lane, asked the Council to vote against the
special use permit and variance. He said he was in favor of the two school
model because of density concerns and the negative impact on the neighbors, as
well as nighttime security and safety issues resulting from the many activities
which already take place at the school during the evening.
Keith and Beth Groebe, 133 Bertling, reiterated others’ comments regarding
density, traffic congestion and safety, and greenspace. Mr. Roby also echoed
neighbors’ disappointment in the representative governmental process. He
acknowledged the difficulty of balancing the interests of the community with
the needs of the high school which is such an integral part of the Village, and
requested that the Board represent its constituency.
Roger Knight, 163 Bertling, relayed his experiences as a neighbor of New Trier.
He questioned the ability of the high school to control the behavior and
community impact of an increased number of students when they are unable to
control the current number.
President Holland reminded everyone that this issue would again be on the
Agenda next Tuesday night and that a complete copy of the Agenda materials was
available for review from the reference desk at the Library.
The meeting adjourned at 10:50 p.m.
May 19, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved June 2, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
19, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier and Bill
Meuer. Absent: Trustee John Lien. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Director of Water and Electric Bryan McInturff and about 67 persons
in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
June 2, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton indicated that he would be unable
to attend.
June 9, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Hilton indicated that he would be unable
to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
May 5, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved
to adopt the Minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Presentation of Landmark Preservation Awards by Nan Greenough, Chairman,
Landmark Preservation Commission.
President Holland recognized Nan Greenough and thanked her and the committee
for all of their efforts to put the awards together. She presented the
Preservation Awards to the recipients and thanked them for their sensitive
contributions to their Village. Awards were presented to the following:
Michael Edwards & Melissa Mizel 939 Tower Road
Christ Church                   470 Maple
Jack Shapiro                    810 Elm Street
Michael & Colleen Campbell      518 Sunset
Matthew & Ashley McCall         686 Hill Road
Ray & Trish McLaughlin          815 Rosewood
Warren & Amy Warren James       1083 Cherry Street
Jim & Carol Hansen              822 Bryant
John & Nancy Dore               286 Sheridan Rd.
 
   Ordinance No. M-524-98 – Landmark Designation for 930 Fisher Lane –
Introduction.
   Ms. Greenough reviewed the committee’s recommendation that the Council
designate 930 Fisher Lane a local landmark pursuant to the System for
Evaluation of Landmarks. She commented on the history of the house and
highlighted some of its distinctive features.
   President Holland then called for a motion to introduce the ordinance
designating the residence on Fisher Lane as a landmark. Trustee Hilton so
moved, seconded by Trustee Meuer. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VII. Public Comment and Questions.
MollyBelle Berish inquired as to the status of the request for payment from the
Democratic National Committee or Mr. Brandt with respect to President Clinton’s
visit to Winnetka. She urged that the Council enact an ordinance which in the
future would hold appropriate parties responsible for the payment of funds
expended by the Village to provide services under similar circumstances.
VIII. Ordinances and Resolutions
    Ordinance No. MC-207-98 – Amending the Village Code to Clarify the
Authority of the Village Manager as it Pertains to Parking Regulations –
Adoption. Trustee Derning moved to adopt this ordinance which was introduced at
the May 5, 1998, Regular Meeting. He was seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier,
Meuer, Hilton and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
   Resolution No. 1420-98 – Establishing a Policy for the Allocation of the
Costs of Installing, Operating and Maintaining Underground Electric Services –
Adoption. Mr. McInturff reviewed Council’s discussions concerning the practice
of installing underground electric services at no charge. He explained that
this Resolution would formalize the policy providing for continued installation
of underground service at no charge to the homeowner so long as that
installation is performed in conjunction with the Village’s scheduled program
of putting the distribution feeders underground. In the case of new
construction or an upgrade of existing service that is not part of the
Village’s scheduled program, however, the customer will be required to pay the
costs of putting the service underground. He pointed out that the Village would
continue to operate and maintain the service in order prevent a disparity of
service among residences.
   Manager Williams clarified that the division of ownership now will be at the
meter in all instances.
   Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the Resolution, seconded by Trustee Lanphier.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Duhl,
Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
   Ordinance No. M-523-98 – Authorizing the Sale or Other Disposition of
Certain Personal Property Owned by the Village – Introduction. Mr. Williams
explained that the proposed ordinance would authorize the disposal of the
computer equipment and PC’s that are no longer needed by the Village and were
declared surplus at a previous meeting. He mentioned that this ordinance would
also give an umbrella authorization to dispose of other computer equipment that
is retired during the current fiscal year.
   Trustee Derning moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-523-98, seconded by
Trustee Lanphier. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
IX. Unfinished Business
   NTHS Special Use Permit and Variations. Ms. McCammack briefly reviewed the
request from New Trier High School for zoning approvals to enlarge their
facility at 385 Winnetka Avenue. The zoning applications consist of a request
for a Special Use Permit to construct a three story addition, replacing the
one-story Tech/Arts Building, the loading dock and the cafeteria, and a request
for zoning variances to permit an increase to the existing nonconforming
building size (GFA) and building lot coverage (intensity of use of lot). Ms.
McCammack also summarized the actions of the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan
Commission and the conditions attached to their respective recommendations for
approval of the requests.
   In response to an issue raised during the May 12, 1998, Study Session, Ms.
McCammack explained a Staff study of pedestrian traffic on the “short path,”
which extends from Hill Road and Bertling to Woodland Avenue. Manager Williams
added that Staff had reviewed this issue and recommended that the Village
consider vacating this path. He pointed out that several issues would need to
be resolved in order to do so, including the provision of a utility easement,
but that this would mitigate the impact of migrating students on neighbors and
alleviate the law enforcement challenges associated with the path.
   Trustee Duhl inquired as to whether this was Staff’s recommendation
regardless of whether the one or two school model was accepted. Mr. Williams
responded in the affirmative.
   Trustee Derning asked whether there was a way to block the path on a trial
basis in order to determine if there are any significant unintended
consequences. Manager Williams indicated that Staff had been unable to devise a
means to effectively do so.
   In response to a question from Mr. Duhl, Mr. Williams clarified the vacation
process.
   John Nieweem, Director of Physical Plant Services, addressed the Council and
responded to previously raised issues. In doing so, he indicated that the
school would be amenable to closing off the “short path.” He distributed and
explained the results of a recent video taped school study summarizing Parent/
Student Drop-off Data. He acknowledged that the study had been performed on a
nice day but that it would be repeated on a bad weather day for purposes of
comparison. He pointed out that the peak traffic periods are extremely short in
duration, from 20 to 30 minutes in the morning and afternoon.
   Mr. Nieweem emphasized the school’s desire to cooperate with the Village in
order to resolve the traffic, parking and other neighborhood impact issues
regardless of the model chosen. He indicated the school’s willingness to
further explore the possibility of a parking structure, to limit the number of
student parking permits, actively promote carpooling, increase enforcement and
contribute to additional CSO officers, and support the Village’s efforts to
close loopholes in the parking regulations. He also mentioned that the school
was considering busing some service personnel, and possibly students, from Duke
Child’s Field.
   In closing, he pointed out that NTHS has been where it is for many years and
can continue to operate as they have been. He reiterated the school’s desire to
achieve a mutually satisfactory resolution with the Village. He said that in
his opinion, it would be wrong to take away from this community the option of a
single campus which has proven in the past that it can support enrollment in
excess of 4,000 students.
   President Holland then invited comments from the floor.
   Bob Owen, 499 Sunset, pointed out that students are dropped off throughout
the surrounding neighborhood, not just at the points observed in the NTHS
study. Mr. Nieweem confirmed this. Mr. Owen said that when he bought his home,
he always assumed that should student enrollment increase to maximum capacity,
the school district would revert to the two campus configuration.
   Ann Knight, 163 Bertling, noted that her objections were not directed to the
proposed development itself, but to her perceived lack of interest shown by the
high school in controlling student behavior in the neighborhood. She submitted
pictures of her yard and driveway showing students smoking, loitering and
littering.
   Alice Smith, 140 Bertling, inquired whether the high school study could be
truly representative given the number of seniors participating off campus in
senior projects. Dr. Bangser stated that approximately 120 seniors participate
in the senior projects program.
   Lloyd Owens, 922 Elm, expressed his opinion that the neighborhood should
come first, particularly because there is already another campus well suited to
accommodate the increased enrollment. He urged the Council to preserve the
beauty of the area as it is.
   Debby Buck, 350 Winnetka Ave. and Gaylene Vasaturo, 42 Abbotsford Road,
repeated concerns about traffic issues, especially safety and illegally parked
cars. Ms. Vasaturo indicated that when her family purchased their property,
within the last couple of years, they had been given no reason to consider or
expect possible expansion of the high school.
   John Gustauson, Woodland Avenue, indicated that at no time did he expect
that the high school would prove to be such a bad neighbor. He complained not
only about traffic problems, but about noise from construction activities and
nightly garbage pick ups, as well as student loitering and smoking. He urged
the Council to wait until the School Board makes a decision before taking any
action on the requested Special Use Permit and variations.
   Donna Gann, 176 Fuller Lane, also asked the Council to wait for complete
information before taking any action, saying that there are far reaching
consequences for Winnetka since this community bears the burden of the location
of the school. She expressed her belief that the proposed four-plus story
building would be out of character in the residential neighborhood and in
opposition to the findings of the Village’s 2020 Survey which favored a maximum
of 2-1/2 stories, even in the commercial districts.
   Jeff Hudson, 79 Abbotsford, shared personal experiences resulting from the
traffic congestion around the school and noted loitering and safety concerns.
   Mack Reynolds, 231 Winnetka Avenue, urged the Council to deny the requests
from NTHS. He represented that the neighbors are strongly opposed to a parking
garage. He also noted that his own children are not allowed to ride their bikes
to school because of the traffic congestion and undesirable behavior on the
part of high school students.
   Liz Nesler, 491 Hill Rd., related a story about student smoking and drinking
and the response by police officers.
   Keith Groebe, 133 Bertling, showed the Council pictures of traffic on
Winnetka Avenue near the high school on a recent rainy day. He submitted for
consideration petitions signed by neighbors requesting denial of the school’s
applications for a Special Use Permit and variances and read excerpts of the
petition to the Trustees. He cited the fact that the proposed expansion is not
necessitated by hardship to the school in that there are acceptable alternative
models, diminished property value, and undue burden on the Village’s
infrastructure resulting in hardship to the neighbors which constitute
impediments to their health, safety and general welfare.
   Robert Bell, 120 Woodland, shared experiences of living across the street
from the high school since 1960, and stated that he did not think the school
should be allowed to expand as proposed.
   Enrico Plati, 822 Humboldt, commented on Mr. Nieweem’s presentation, saying
that in his opinion, the school had not shown any hardship, since they
acknowledged that other viable models were available. He noted that he
frequently runs into NTHS traffic when delivering and picking up his children
from Greeley School, a significant distance away from the high school. In his
opinion, it would be a major disaster take an action which could only increase
traffic on the east side.
   Dr. Hank Bangser, Superintendent of New Trier Township High School,
responded to some of the residents’ comments. He acknowledged that students do
things off campus that shouldn’t be done and affirmed the school’s commitment
to addressing these problems which move out into the neighborhood. He pointed
out evidence confirming the school’s willingness to be responsive to
complaints.
   Dr. Bangser noted that the reason this issue was now before the Village
Council was because the School Board was making every good faith effort to work
in tandem with the Village and the community to resolve this difficult issue in
a mutually satisfactory manner. He pointed out that the difficulty of the issue
is exacerbated by the mixing of school issues and administration with community
issues and administration.
   Dr. Bangser further explained that in the beginning there had only been one
option, the expansion of the east campus, and that only later were creative,
alternative models developed. He indicated that now it would be very difficult
for the School Board should the single campus option be removed from the table,
since the whole Township community is more or less equally divided in its
opinions about the alternative models.
   On behalf of the Trustees, President Holland thanked the representatives
from the School Board, high school and community for repeatedly attending
meetings and sharing their information and concerns with the Council. She then
called for questions and comments from the Trustees.
   Trustee Hilton inquired whether the Lab School model had been eliminated.
Dr. Bangser indicated that it had.
   Trustee Duhl stated that he had found the discussions very informative, but
disturbing because of the process. Noting that he did not feel that both sides
of the issues had been represented at this point, he proposed the idea of
tabling consideration of the issue until after a decision is made by the School
Board. He said that he would be uncomfortable being forced to make a decision
without hearing complete arguments from both sides. He noted that at this
point, the Trustees had only heard comments demonstrating the detriments to the
Village which were persuasive. However, his belief that New Trier High School
is one of the major benefits existing in this community leads him to be
extremely cautious about doing anything which might compromise that institution
or lead to the diminution of their product. He stated that he did not believe
that the School Board had yet produced evidence as to why one campus would be
superior educationally and why the benefits of that model outweigh the
hardships created to the Village. He expressed his fear that if the Village
Council were to deny the School Board’s request at this point in the process,
the most educationally sound model might unintentionally be removed from
consideration. He requested that the School Board present some of the positive
arguments in favor of the single campus so that the benefits in education might
be weighed against the detriments to the Village.
   Trustee Hilton inquired about the ramifications for the School Board, should
the Trustees decide to table the issue. Dr. Bangser responded that time was
important because of the need to be prepared to place a referendum on the
ballot in November.
   Trustee Derning, however, expressed his opinion that the School Board had
come to the Village Council to determine the viability of one of its options,
and that it was the Council’s responsibility to let them know if this option is
not available to them.
   Stating that all decisions are a balance of pros and cons, Trustee Duhl
reiterated his belief that the School Board had not yet presented its case
based on the educational benefits of a single campus. He noted that although
the burden of the location falls on the Village of Winnetka, the benefits that
might accrue to the high school significantly affect all of the students from
the entire Township and that this factor could not be ignored. He noted that if
the educational interests of the school are equally well served by any of the
three models, then there would be no question in his mind that the east campus
should not be expanded; however, in the absence of evidence attesting to the
models’ educational neutrality, he is uncomfortable doing anything which might
jeopardize the high school’s standards.
   Trustee Lanphier questioned Dr. Bangser about the demographics. She asked
about the planned options in the event student enrollment exceeds the predicted
4,250 level. Dr. Bangser stated that the Board had used the mid-range number in
the enrollment projections and said that that would have to be determined
depending upon how high the enrollment went and how long it was predicted to
stay at a peak level. He added that one of the solutions to a higher enrollment
would be to put freshmen at the west campus.
   Trustee Hilton stated that at the moment he was hesitant to table the issue
and voiced his belief that academics are not the concern of the Council.
   Dr. Bangser explained that because of the diversity of opinion on the School
Board itself, he would not be comfortable presenting the arguments in favor of
the single school model until that decision has been made by that Board.
   Trustee Lanphier expressed her belief that the Council owes it to the School
Board to let it know the nature of the obstacles they would need to overcome if
they select the single campus model, so that they can consider that information
in their decision making process.
   Dr. Bangser asked for direction as to what information or criteria the
Council wished to see before it voted on the issue. He reiterated his point
that at this time, he can only speak in hypotheticals and cannot represent the
position of the School Board until after they have voted. He also assured the
Council that the School Board had very seriously considered the negative impact
of putting more people on the east campus.
   Manager Williams confirmed Dr. Bangser’s statement that he had recommended
that the School Board focus its presentation on traditional land use issues,
but mentioned that issues of general welfare, into which category educational
excellence could be interpreted to fall, could constitute hardship for the high
school.
   Noting that there were obviously significant obstacles to overcome in order
to gain approval, Mr. Duhl reiterated his request for information as to why the
School Board believes that one campus is the better option.
   Trustee Meuer expressed his support for Mr. Duhl’s position, stating that he
has numerous unanswered questions relating to the educational program, which he
thinks could prove to be a hardship in this instance. He said he was bothered
by having to make a decision one way or another without having the educational
merits of the single school model addressed.
   The Trustees discussed the relative merits of tabling the issue until after
the decision by the School Board, ultimately deciding to convene again next
week to decide upon a course of action. President Holland announced that there
would be a Special Meeting of the Council on Wednesday, May 27, 1998. She
invited the audience to attend to hear the discussion if they so wished, but
stressed that no comments would be received from anyone outside of the Village
Council.
X. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1077 and 1078. Trustee Derning moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1071 in the total amount of $ 228,967.15 and Warrant List No. 1072 in the
total amount of $228,298.79. Trustee Duhl seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Lanphier, Hilton and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
    Sidewalk Café Permits. Manager Williams asked Council to approve the
applications for 1998 Outdoor Seating Permits received from six local
restaurants. He noted that the restaurants had submitted certificates of
insurance which had been reviewed by the Director of Finance and were found to
be acceptable. Trustee Lanphier moved to approve the applications as requested,
seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Duhl and Derning. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Lien.
   Bid No. 98-018 – Pad Mount Transformers. Manager Williams reviewed the bids
for 1-phase and 3-phase pad mount transformers to be purchased on an “as
needed” basis for FY 98/99. He noted that the bids had been examined from the
standpoint of initial costs as well as the to operate over a twenty year period
using standards generally accepted in the industry. He said that Staff
recommended that the bid be awarded to Wesco, the low bidder for the purchase
of 1-phase transformers, and Resco, the low bidder for the purchase of 3-phase
transformers. Trustee Meuer moved to approve the award of the bids as noted. He
was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
   Bid No. 98-019 - Sidewalk and Curb Repair and Replacement Program. Manager
Williams reviewed the bids for furnishing all labor, materials, services and
equipment to construct concrete sidewalks, curbing, handicapped ramps and other
related work as a part of the Sidewalk and Curb Repair and Replacement Program.
He relayed Staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to Schroeder &
Schroeder, the lowest bidder in the total amount of $96,115.00. Trustee Derning
moved to accept the bid, seconded by Trustee Duhl. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton and
Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
   Engineering Services Contract. Mr. Williams outlined the provisions of the
proposed contract for professional engineering services with Ken Keene. Ms.
Janega explained one small change to the document as drafted. Trustee Hilton
moved to approve the contract, seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer
and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
X. Reports.
   Village President. President Holland announced the passage by the State
legislature of the Religious Restoration Act. She expressed her disappointment
that a proposed statement of “legislative intent,” which would have ameliorated
the concerns of many communities of the NWMC, had been omitted. Mr. Williams
added that the had requested Ms. Janega to research this topic and evaluate its
potential impact on the Village.
   Village Trustees. Trustee Derning noted that he had met with the Chamber of
Commerce. He indicated that interest had been expressed in the status of the
Comprehensive Plan. He also indicated that the members were in favor of
removing the amortization provisions from the proposed new Sign Ordinance.
   Village Manager. Mr. Williams announced that Ordinance No. MC-562-98 and MC-
521-98 requests for zoning variations at 756 Foxdale and 1177 Ash,
respectively, which were introduced at the May 5, 1998, Regular Meeting, would
be docketed for future meetings. The Foxdale ordinance was delayed at the
request of the applicant and is now scheduled for consideration at the June 2,
1998, meeting, and the Ash Street ordinance needs to go back to the plan
Commission for final plat approval.
   Mr. Williams also called Council’s attention to a summary analysis of the
preliminary financial results for the fiscal year ending March 31, 1998,
included in the Agenda packets, and stated that the preliminary results
indicate that the Village had a very good year.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lanphier seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 10:40 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:24 p.m.. Present:
President Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier and Meuer. Absent:
Trustee Lien. Also present Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney
Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
11:25 p.m.
June 2, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved June 16, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
2, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and Bill
Meuer. Absent: David Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about eight persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland asked that the Council amend the Agenda by moving
consideration of Item 7(I), Introduction of a Request for Special Use Permit
and Zoning Variance for Hubbard Woods School, to the top of the Ordinances and
Resolutions portion of the Agenda in order to permit representatives from the
school to attend graduation ceremonies which conflict with this meeting.
Trustee Duhl moved to amend the Agenda as requested, seconded by Trustee Lien.
By voice vote the motion carried.
IV. Quorum.
June 16, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
June 9, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
May 12, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Derning moved to adopt the Minutes as
drafted. He was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion carried.
May 19, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved
to adopt the Minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
   Ordinance No. M-527-98 – Special Use Permit and Zoning Variance: Hubbard
Woods School, 1110 Chatfield Road – Introduction. Ms. McCammack explained the
requests from the Winnetka Public School district for zoning variations and a
Special Use Permit to permit the construction of three, one-story additions to
Hubbard Woods School. She indicated that the additions would result in
increases to the existing non-conforming building size (GFA) and intensity of
use of lot (lot coverage).
   Trustee Meuer asked whether these changes would provide for the future
removal of the mobile classrooms. Ms. Shelly Clark, Business Manager for the
School District, indicated that it was hoped that these mobile classrooms could
eventually be removed, but that it is presently impossible to determine when
that might take place. Attorney Janega mentioned that this issue was not
affected in any way by the proposed ordinance. Trustee Derning clarified that
the school would house Kindergarten through fifth grade and that the sixth
grade would be moving to Skokie School. Ms. Clark confirmed that that was the
plan.
   Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lien.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   Ordinance No. M-524-98 – Landmark Designation for 930 Fisher Lane –
Adoption. Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt the ordinance designating 930 Fisher
Lane a local landmark. She was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By roll vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Lien, Duhl, and
Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
   Ordinance No. M-522-98 – Zoning Variations: 756 Foxdale – Adoption. Ms.
McCammack briefly reviewed the applicants’ request for an increased building
size (GFA). Ms. Janega then explained language which has been added to the
introduced ordinance pursuant to Council’s discussions at the time of
introduction providing for the payment of all outstanding fees within thirty
(30) days of adoption as a condition of the variation.
   Trustee Derning moved to amend the ordinance as outlined by Ms. Janega. He
was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Lien and Duhl. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
   Trustee Derning then moved to adopt the ordinance approving an increased
building size (GFA), seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote the motion
passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Lien and Duhl.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
   Ordinance No. M-523-98 – Authorizing the Sale or Other Disposition of
Certain Personal Property Owned by the Village – Adoption. Trustee Meuer moved
to adopt this ordinance which was introduced at the May 19, 1998, Regular
Meeting, providing for the disposal of certain pieces of older equipment and
furniture no longer needed by Village operations. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Lanphier. By roll call vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Meuer, Lien, Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Hilton.
   Resolution No. R-1425-98 – Examination and Release of Executive Session
Minutes – Adoption. Manager Williams explained that pursuant to State law, the
Village Council is required to examine and release Executive Session minutes
which no long require confidentiality. This resolution covers minutes from June
17, 1997, through May 5, 1998. He indicated that the minutes also needed to be
approved before they were released. Trustee Meuer moved to approve the
Executive Committee Minutes as drafted, seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
   Trustee Lien moved to adopt Resolution No. R-1425-98, seconded by Trustee
Duhl. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien,
Duhl, Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
   Resolution No. R-1423-98 – Authorizing Participation in the Regional
Emergency Dispatch (RED) Center – Adoption. Manager Williams explained that
this Resolution would effect membership in the RED Center, a conglomeration of
several municipalities who have pooled their resources to perform critical life
safety functions and provide emergency medical dispatch services. Membership in
the organization would also serve to provide seamless mutual aid in the event
of a major incident in Winnetka.
   Ms. Janega pointed to language incorporated by means of a supplemental
letter of agreement requesting a limitation of the Village’s indemnification
obligations, saying that the issue was still under study for all members.
Manager Williams noted that the target date for activating the system is August
1, 1998, with a back-up date of September 1, 1998, and indicted that the
Council needed to appoint a Trustee to serve on the RED Center’s Board.
   Trustee Meuer moved to adopt the resolution authorizing participation by the
Village in the RED Center. He was seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call
vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Lien, Duhl, Derning
and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
   President Holland suggested the appointment of Trustee Derning to serve as
the Council’s representative to the RED Center Board. Trustee Meuer so moved,
seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote the Trustees unanimously approved this
appointment.
   Ordinance No. M-525-98 – Zoning Variation: 653 Blackthorn – Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed this request for a variation from Zoning Ordinance to
permit a reduced rear yard setback to allow for the relocation of window and
door openings in the existing exterior walls and construction of an open,
roofed porch. She indicated that it was important to remember that the house
had been built in 1916 prior to the zoning ordinance.
   Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Derning. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   Ordinance No. M-526-98 – Zoning Variances: 731 Prospect – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed this request for zoning variations to permit an increased
building height and building size (GFA) and a reduced side yard setback.. She
noted that the existing residence exceeds the maximum permitted GFA and has a
height of three stories, instead of the maximum permitted height of 2.5
stories. She explained that the applicants are proposing a third floor dormer
which will not increase the existing calculated GFA, but will add to the
existing bulk and volume of the house as well extending the degree of
nonconforming building height. Ms. McCammack noted that a second request for a
variance to permit three existing air conditioning condensers to observe a
reduced side yard setback had been resolved. The applicant presented
architect’s drawings and photos of the house for Council’s examination
explaining that their intent was to balance the front of the house and add
light to an existing room.
   Trustee Duhl moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-526-98, permitting
construction of the dormer addition. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien.
By voice vote the motion carried.
   Ordinance No. M-528-98 – Special Use Permit: Village Green – Introduction.
Ms. McCammack outlined a request from the Winnetka Park District for a Special
Use Permit to permit improvements to the play area located in the southeast
quadrant of the Village Green Park. She pointed out that the proposal does not
create any zoning ordinance violations and that the Village Engineer had
reviewed the plans and determined that the improvements will not increase
parking or roadway demands in the vicinity of the park..
   Trustee Meuer moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Derning.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   President Holland expressed appreciation to the Park District for their
efforts to choose equipment and plans which are compatible with the
neighborhood.
   Resolution No. R-1424-98 – Page-Flannery Subdivision: 120 Thorntree Lane –
Adoption. Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt this resolution. She was seconded by
Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Lien, Duhl and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Hilton.
VIII. Unfinished Business
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1079 and 1080. Noting that he had reviewed the lists to his
satisfaction with Mr. McKee, Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List No.
1079 in the total amount of $353,656.78 and Warrant List No. 1080 in the total
amount of $203,875.04. Trustee Lanphier seconded the motion. By roll call vote
the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning,
Duhl, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
   Capital Improvement Quarterly Reports. Manager Williams briefly reviewed the
reports submitted by the Department Heads.
   DK Recycling Systems Monthly Report. Manager Williams referred the Trustees
to the report prepared by DK Recycling contained in their Agenda packets.
   Cherry Street Alley Improvements. At 8:10 p.m. Trustee Lien moved to adjourn
from the Regular Council meeting to convene as the Board of Local Improvements
for the purpose of considering a petition for improvements to the public alley
between Ash and Cherry Streets, from Locust to Rosewood. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   The Trustees reconvened into Regular Session at 8: 15 p.m. Present: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee Hilton. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
X. Reports.
   Village President. President Holland mentioned a recent article in North
Shore Magazine, and indicated that she would be writing a letter to the editor
refuting some of the claims made therein.
   Village Manager.
   NTHS Zoning Variance and Special Use Permit Applications. Mr. Williams
reported that this agenda item remains pending until the School Board makes its
final decision on June 8, 1998.
   Equipment Auction Results. Mr. Williams reviewed the results of the auction
with the Trustees.
   He also announced that the Plan Commission proposed a joint Study Session on
June 23rd to discuss the Comprehensive Plan, and invited the Trustees to attend
the Plan Commission’s meeting scheduled for June 10th to review the plan’s
preliminary goals. As a result of these meetings, the Trustees agreed to meet
on June 23rd and cancel the Study Session originally scheduled for June 9,
1998.
XI. Appointments.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the motion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 9:47
p.m.
