VIII. Unfinished Business
   Application for Special Use Permit and Zoning Variations by New Trier High
School. Manager Williams announced that the Board of Education had requested
that the Application for a Special Use Permit and Variances for New Trier High
School be tabled until further notice. Attorney Janega explained the tabling
process.
   Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to table the issue. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1081 and 1082. Trustee Lien briefly reviewed the questions
and answers he and Mr. McKee had exchanged. Trustee Lien moved to approve
Warrant List No. 1081 in the total amount of $243,878.61 and Warrant List No.
1082 in the total amount of $377,525.45. Trustee Derning seconded the motion.
By roll call vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Meuer inquired whether the Village was pursuing study of the
telephone service. Manager Williams responded that money for a consultant had
been authorized, and it was hoped to begin the project in the fall. Trustee
Meuer also raised the possibility of purchasing copy machines instead of
renting them.
   DK Recycling Systems Monthly Report. Manager Williams referred the Trustees
to the report prepared by DK Recycling contained in their Agenda packets, and
President Holland pointed out that there had been no odor complaints since
operations began this spring.
   Leaf Blowers. Manager Williams informed the Trustees that numerous letters
had been received opposing the current ban on the use of leaf blowers during
the month of June and requesting that during the first part of June the
cleaning of gutters by gasoline powered leaf blowers be allowed. He asked Gary
Lindeman, the local contractor who was instrumental in organizing the request,
to explain the need for such a ban and speak about available new equipment. Mr.
Lindeman explained that gutter cleaning is most effective when done after the
trees have finished dropping their seeds, somewhere around June 20th. He also
indicated that he had brought one of the newer model leaf blowers to
demonstrate the noise level for the Council if they so desired.
   Trustee Lanphier concurred that quieter technology was available, but noted
that the Village cannot regulate decibel levels, nor can it require contractors
to only use specific equipment. Attorney Janega noted that State statutes
preempt local regulation of noise as to intensity. She suggested that one
possible way to address the newer technology would be to examine the
specifications to see if there is some way to distinguish between older,
noisier models and the newer, quieter ones.
   Trustee Duhl stated that he had a problem with opening up the ban for any
purpose. He expressed his belief that an absolute rule was necessary and
pointed to the enforcement difficulties currently being encountered. He
objected to creating an exception for gutters because it would only make it
more difficult to enforce the ordinance, would still interrupt the quiet, and
encourage abuse of the machines for other uses.
   Trustee Derning pointed out that in any event, no change could be effected
for this year. He asked that Mr. Lindeman provide specifications to Staff for
study.
   President Holland asked about the use of electric blowers for gutter
cleaning. Mr. Lindeman replied that they were neither practical nor safe for
roof and ladder work.
   Bid No. 98-021 – Underground Cable. Manager Williams explained that this bid
was for the purchase and delivery of 15KV and 600V cable on an “as needed”
basis for FY 98/99. He noted that staff’s recommendation was to purchase all of
the cable from the lowest responsible bidder, The Okonite Co., in the total
amount of $141,481.50.
   Trustee Hilton moved to accept the bid as recommended, seconded by Trustee
Lanphier . By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
   Village President. President Holland distributed copies of the 1921 and 1935
Comprehensive Plans pointing to the numerous accomplishments which have been
achieved over the years.
   Village Manager.
   List of Actions to Mitigate Impact of New Trier High School on the
Neighborhood. Mr. Williams reviewed a list of strategies developed during the
NTHS application process to mitigate the impact of the high school on the
immediate neighborhood. He indicated that despite the tabling of New Trier’s
application, the Village intended to move forward with the suggested changes to
determine their consequences.
   Facilities Tour. Mr. Williams announced that a tour of Village facilities
for the Trustees had been scheduled for September 26, 1998.
   Special Study Session. Manager Williams reminded Trustees of the special
Study Session scheduled for Tuesday, June 23rd to review the status of the
Comprehensive Plan with the Plan Commission.
   Manager Williams announced the appointment of President Holland to Co-chair
the Legislative Committee of the Northwest Municipal Conference.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 8:18 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:25 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: None. Also
present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Duhl moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:26 p.m.
June 23, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
AND
SPECIAL MEETING
WINNETKA PLAN COMMISSION
(Approved July 7, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
23, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, William Meuer, Michael Duhl
and David Hilton. Also present: Village Manger Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Cindy McCammack, and Rich Williams, Tom Dyke, and Drew
Petterson of Thompson Dyke & Associates and about 4 persons in the audience.
Plan Commission Members Present: Stewart Early, Penfield Lanphier, Jack Wentz,
Bernard Sergesketter, Ann Alexander, Edith Howell, Nan Greenough, John
Berwanger, Ed Woodbury. Absent: Phyllis Myers, Dorothy Anderson, Ken Roffe, and
Maureen Mitchell.
II. Comprehensive Plan Update by Thompson Dyke & Associates and the Plan
Commission Comprehensive Plan Subcommittee.
   Chairman Early said that the purpose of the meeting was to obtain the
Village Council’s support on community goals and objectives before taking the
matter to the public and also so that the Council can give the Plan Commission
a sense of direction regarding certain details of the Plan. He said the Plan
will include community goals, objectives, recommendations, and the economic
feasibility of the recommendations. The Plan Commission is not looking for
assistance from the Council at this meeting on the goal and objective documents
nor are they seeking final approval or a redrafting of the zoning and building
ordinances. They are here to get a shared vision of Winnetka 2020.
   Chairman Early gave a general and brief history of the Comprehensive Plan’s
revisions and of the processes completed to date on the Comprehensive Plan.
Importantly, the processes included the Plan Commission’s counsel to the
Village Council on the proposed Winnetka Avenue (White’s Drugs) redevelopment,
New Trier High School’s expansion plans, and development of standard changes in
the commercial zoning districts. He said that they need to develop what to
expect from the Plan, what they want to preserve of Winnetka and an
introductory vision statement.
   Mr. Sergesketter summarized the function of a good vision statement and read
the vision proposed by the subcommittee for the Comprehensive Plan. Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Hilton and President Holland voiced support of the statement.
   Ms. Greenough summarized the 1921 Plan with respect to the recommendations
made that have since been realized, such as the below grade railroad tracks and
the purchase of the Crow Island property for a school and park, etc.
   Chairman Early addressed the cost of implementing the plan and said that
Thompson Dyke and Associates will prepare financing options for the Council to
review. Mr. Early said that the Village has some major assets, such as the post
office block. Mr. Early stated that, at this point, the recommendations will
not be packaged with the community goals and objectives so that the process
would not get bogged down.
III. Discussion of Draft Community Goals and Objectives.
   Mr. Early and Ms. Greenough presented the potential key issues for
discussion:
   Community
   1.   Power lines
   2.   Density/Bulk (residential)
   3.   Open spaces (vegetation)
   4.   Municipal utilities
   5.   Underground parking
   6.   Owner/employee parking
   
   Green Bay Road/Business Districts
   7.   Underground parking, new developments
   8.   Use public/private funds for study of maintenance and development of
Green Bay Road/Business Districts
   Hubbard Woods
   9.   Landmark Structure - Green Bay Road, southbound at Tower
   Elm
   10.   Train station/underground parking
   11.   Post office site development
   12.   Bratschi Plumbing site
   13.   Redevelopment of Community House
   14.   North Elm/West Lincoln development
   Indian Hill
   15.   Multi-use or Multi-family
   16.   Parking (New Trier High School)
   Plan Commission and Council Members discussed burying power lines. Manager
Williams stated that the cost would be prohibitive to accomplish this goal by
2010.
   Another issue discussed was the residential bulk and that the density for
replacement homes are more than double the size of the original house. Manager
Williams gave an update on a request sent to Thompson Dyke & Associates to
conduct a detailed analysis of gross floor area. They will also be looking at
revising the floor area ratio formula to reduce gross floor area on corner lots
and also to reduce total gross floor area in each district. The members present
then discussed the issues regarding maintaining the scale of existing
neighborhoods.
   Mr. Berwanger said that there is a need to discuss with the public the
impact of reducing the floor area ratio.
   Trustee Duhl said that there is a need for balance of the private and public
interests.
   Ed Woodbury said that he believes that not only gross floor area should be
discussed, but also setbacks, the size of permitted lots and the subdivision
ordinance.
   Ann Alexander also sees a need to consider existing SFR and the FAR impacts
when additions are needed.
IV. Discussion of Draft Strategic Plans for the Green Bay Road Corridor and
Business Districts.
   Mr. Early and Mr. Rich Williams presented a sub-area plan (map) for the
Indian Hill area and reviewed the shallowness of the commercial district along
Green Bay Road, south of Winnetka Avenue and the suggested five point
intersection. Also reviewed was the possibility of commercial or multiple
family lot assembly and closure of Brier.
   Rich Williams stated, on the other hand, it is possible to determine land
use by the market but that densities should be determined by the Village. At
the southern end of the district, there are 2 ½ story townhouse developments
with parking at the rear.
   Ms. Lanphier said there is a need for developments that will improve the
area, not just maintain the status quo.
   Rich Williams discussed the possibility of a parking structure for 800+ cars
northeast of Winnetka and Green Bay Road. He said that policy decisions should
be made for uses south of the Winnetka Avenue intersection as to multi-family,
mixed uses or if a vibrant business district is desired.
   Ms. Lanphier said that they must consider the social and economic importance
of resurging business districts.
   President Holland stated that she believes it would be more appropriate to
have Winnetka and Green Bay Road develop as a mixed use because the buildings
would not be setback and landscaped to the extent of multi-family and those
uses would provide more vitality to the area.
   Rich Williams reviewed the Elm Street sub-area plan. The parking deck at the
Community House may be needed in 20 years because of increased parking
requirements. Some other items to consider would be parking underground west of
the train station; replacing the Village parking lot with a park; underground
parking at the post office site; townhomes to the south and west of the post
office site as transitional land uses.
   Trustee Derning said he was concerned about the overall loss of on-street
parking and asked what the net change would be.
   Ms. Alexander suggested that corner urban elements work to reduce the urban
sprawl appearance of parking lots.
   In Hubbard Woods, Rich Williams displayed urban design feature examples that
will be offered for the Village to determine specific styles. He also presented
a linear park concept to run south from the Elm Street station and into
Kenilworth. This park would consist of the existing undeveloped path that is
owned by the Village and would contain a pedestrian trail.
   In addition, on Tower Road at the Green Bay Road intersection, consider a
landmark style building; additional landscaping for auto service uses; improve
the alley along the industrial corridor and a covered drop off at the train
station.
   Other issues discussed were housing for all ages; limiting the use of Tower
Road as a cut-through for the Edens Expressway; using calming techniques to
discourage through traffic; revising the subdivision ordinance.
   Rich Williams presented a map showing several lots of minimum size which
have the potential of being subdivided with respect to establishing minimum
street frontage and lot width requirements and prohibitive irregular shaped
lots.
VII. Adjournment
   Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting, seconded by Trustee Derning.
The meeting adjourned at 10:47 p.m.
July 7, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved July 21, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
7, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and Bill
Meuer. Absent: Trustee Don Derning. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Bryan
McInturff, and about seven persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
July 14, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
July 21, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Lanphier who expects to be out of town, indicated that they would be able to
attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
June 16, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved
to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried.
June 23, 1998, Study Session and Special Meeting of the Winnetka Plan
Commission. Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Ms. Mollybelle Berish read a letter raising numerous questions, noting that
copies had been distributed to each Trustee. Mr. Williams responded briefly to
each of the items in Ms. Berish’s letter.
   Ms. Debby Clinch, Director of the Winnetka Community Nursery School,
expressed her concern for the safety and welfare of the nursery school’s
children during the tear down and rebuilding of the Caledonian Building. In
particular she voiced her worries about construction traffic using Killian
Court as a thoroughfare during the project and asked that the trucks be
rerouted to enter and exit on Lincoln Avenue. She also noted the problems of
noise, fumes and dust.
   Ms. Laurie Kaplan, the mother of one of the children spoke in support of Ms.
Clinch’s position.
   Mr. Mike Wurzberg, owner of Minna Hart, voiced his objection to the removal
of parking spaces from the Community House parking lot in order to accommodate
the construction. He expressed his concerns about the present and potential
impact on his business from reduced parking availability.
   Trustee Duhl inquired about other available options to handle the truck
traffic and the parking problems. Manager Williams explained three possible
scenarios, citing the advantages and disadvantages of each. He said that staff
had chosen to use the Killian Court option because it minimized the impact on
Lincoln Avenue businesses and parking lot users. He indicated that the builder
of the Caledonian project was willing to work with the Village to insure safe
procedures for the use of Killian Court. He mentioned the coordination of
schedules, the use of flaggers, and the possibility of building a guard rail
and/or jersey barriers, if necessary. Mr. Williams pointed out that this issue
presents very difficult policy choices and suggested that if the Council wanted
to discuss it further, a study session should be set aside for its
consideration.
   Trustee Lanphier asked how long the construction project was anticipated to
take. Mr. Williams indicated that a year was not an unlikely estimate. Ms.
Lanphier said that she thought that the topic merited further discussion.
   Trustee Meuer agreed with Trustee Lanpher and inquired whether the
Congregational Church would be willing to allow use of their parking lot. He
also pointed out that the dust from the old building should be checked for
traces of lead and/or asbestos.
   Trustee Lien inquired whether the builder would be paying rent for the use
of the blocked off parking spaces on the Village owned lot?
   After further discussion, the Trustees agreed to set this item on the agenda
for the Study Session on Tuesday, July 14, 1998. President Holland announced
that residents, business owners, and representatives from the various parties
would be notified of the upcoming meeting.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
   Resolution No. R-1430-98 – Approving and Establishing Salary for New
Director of Public Works – Adoption. In accordance with Section 3.09(b) of the
Village Code, Trustee Lien moved that the Council establish and approve the
salary of the new Public Works Director, Steven M. Saunders, as set forth in
the proposed Resolution. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll
call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Duhl,
Hilton, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Derning.
   President Holland expressed the Trustees’ endorsement of and support for Mr.
Saunders.
   Ms. Berish asked about the professional services contract for Mr. Keene. Mr.
Williams outlined the projects which Mr. Keene would be completing and informed
her that the Council had supported extending the contract to Mr. Keene during
an open meeting.
   Ordinance No. M-529-98 – Zoning Variance: 2 Golf Lane - Introduction.
President Holland recognized the applicants who explained that the requested
variation would permit reduced front yard setbacks. They indicated that there
would be no change in the building footprint and that potential parking issues
could be handled. Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded
by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote the motion carried.
VIII. Unfinished Business
   Leafblowers. Attorney Janega explained that she had examined the materials
received regarding the Echo brand of leafblowers and determined that there is
insufficient information about distinguishing characteristics to allow for
carving an exception out of the current ordinance. She reviewed her findings
and recommended that if the Council wishes to extend the time period in which
leafblowers can be used, the extended period should apply across the board for
all types of leafblowers, rather than for one particular type.
   Gary Lindeman who initiated the request to reconsider the timing in the
ordinance in order to permit gutter cleaning commented on Ms. Janega’s remarks.
He referred the Trustees to information which he had prepared and distributed
to them.
   Trustee Lanphier noted that the industry is moving in the right direction,
but that for the time being, discerning the difference between models is
difficult and makes enforcement too cumbersome. Trustee Hilton concurred and
suggested that the Council reconsider the matter again next year.
   Thanking Mr. Lindeman for his efforts, President Holland spoke for the
Council saying that for the time being they wished to give the ordinance as it
currently stands some time before making any adjustments to see if the
community will adapt to it. She asked Mr. Lindeman to transmit to the Council
any future information regarding new, quieter technology.
   Cherry Street Alley Improvements. At 8:30 p.m. Trustee Duhl moved to adjourn
from the Regular Council meeting to convene as the Board of Local Improvements
for the purpose of continuing consideration of improvements to the public alley
between Ash and Cherry Streets, from Locust to Rosewood. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Derning.
   The Trustees reconvened into Regular Session at 8:45 p.m. Present Trustees
Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, Hilton and Meuer. Absent. Trustee Derning. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and Director
of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1083 and 1084. After addressing the questions he had raised
with and had answered by Mr. McKee, Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1083 in the total amount of $499,210.18 and Warrant List No. 1084 in the
total amount of $469,912.90. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl,
Lanphier, Hilton and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Derning.
   Professional Service Agreement for Analysis for Residential Bulk
Regulations. Manager Williams explained that in response to growing resident
concern about residential bulk, and the extent of new construction activity in
the Village, Thompson Dyke & Associates had been asked to submit a proposal to
conduct a detailed study of building size in Winnetka’s residential
neighborhoods. He said the goal was to prepare a comprehensive report outlining
the variety of options available regarding residential bulk, especially in 1/
6 acre zoning district.
Mr. Williams referred the Trustees to TD&A’s proposed agreement for fees not to
exceed $18,500 and reviewed the included scope of services. He mentioned that
the 1998-99 Budget does not contain an appropriation for this expenditure. He
recommended that in view of the importance of the issue, the contract be
awarded, and if necessary, a supplemental appropriation be made at the end of
the fiscal year using General Fund reserves to offset the expenditure.
   Trustee Lanphier expressed her support for the proposal saying that Thompson
Dyke & Associates would be able to provide information which cannot be
determined in house.
   Trustee Meuer agreed and suggested that the next Winnetka Report inform the
residents of the study and its results.
   Trustee Lanphier moved to approve the proposal from Thompson Dyke &
Associates. She was seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion
passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Duhl.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Derning.
   Bid No. 98-023 – Sewer Relining. Mr. Williams explained that the bid is for
trenchless relining of the existing sanitary sewers under Merril Street, Sunset
Road, Green Bay Road and Ridge Avenue. Staff’s recommendation is that the bid
be awarded to the low bidder, Kenny Construction Co., in the total amount of
$36,255.
   Trustee Hilton moved to accept the bid from Kenny Construction. He was
seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent:
Derning.
   Lake Michigan Water Allocations. Mr. McInturff explained that at
approximately ten year intervals, the State of Illinois reviews the water
allocations for communities withdrawing water from Lake Michigan and that the
Department of Natural Resources, Office of Water Resources is responsible for
allocating the use of the withdrawal from Lake Michigan. He pointed out that as
a settlement to a Supreme Court case, the State of Illinois is allocated a
3,000 cubic feet per second withdrawal from the lake. He noted that Winnetka’s
current allocation is 3.021 million gallons per day (MGD) and grows to 3.739
MGD in 2020. He informed the Trustees that the State proposes to reduce
Winnetka’s allocation to 2.385 MGD in 1998, growing to 2.410 MGD in 2020.
Pointing to historical data which shows that Winnetka has exceeded the 1998
allocation in three of five historic years and that the five-year average also
exceeds the new allocation, Mr. McInturff expressed Staff’s belief that the
proposed allocation is inadequate to meet the needs of the Village. He
indicated that other neighboring communities face similar problems with the
proposed new allocations and recommended that the Council authorize Staff to
protest the proposed Lake Michigan water allocations for Winnetka.
   Trustee Duhl noted that it was his understanding that this was an attempt to
bring the State of Illinois into compliance with the Supreme Court decision. He
also expressed his desire to be sure that Winnetka is not being discriminated
against unfairly.
   Manager Williams recommended that the Council authorize the Village to join
together with neighboring communities to protest the proposed allocations.
   President Holland noted the consensus of the Trustees and called for a
motion to officially object to the proposed water allocation. Trustee Hilton so
moved, seconded by Trustee Meuer. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Derning.
Ameritech Vault Installations. Mr. McInturff reviewed the history of
installations in the Village by Ameritech of cabinets on jointly owned poles,
the purpose of which is to facilitate conversion from fiber optics to copper
wire. He also said that Ameritech had indicated a willingness to install
underground vaults in lieu of the additional cabinets if suitable locations
within the Village could be found. Mr. McInturff referred the Trustees to
pictures of four potential sites on Village owned land or rights of way,
located by the Water & Electric Department, acceptable to Ameritech and asked
the Council to authorize the negotiation of license agreements with Ameritech
for the location of these vaults. He noted that Ameritech had indicated a
willingness to pay the Village between $7,000 and $10,000 per site for a
license to install the vaults and that it might be possible to negotiate for
the removal of those pole mounted cabinets already installed..
   Trustee Lanphier moved to authorize negotiations with Ameritech. She was
seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Derning.
X. Reports.
   Village President.
   President Holland reviewed the current status of the Religious Freedom
Reformation Act.
   Village Manager.
   Warrants Questions. Mr. Williams reported on two outstanding questions
raised at the last Council meeting.
   Police and Fire Pension Reports. Mr. Williams referred the Trustees to
copies of the report prepared by the Illinois Department of Insurance for the
Village’s Police and Fire Pension Funds and commented on the implications
thereof.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland announced the nomination by the Winnetka-Northfield Public
Library Board of Dorothy Anderson as the new Library representative to the Plan
Commission and asked for a motion to approve the Board’s nomination. Trustee
Meuer so moved, seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote the motion carried
unanimously.
   She also announced the appointment of Jon Meyer, representative from the
Design Review Board to chair the newly created Ad Hoc Committee on Design
Guidelines. Other appointments to the committee include: John Berwanger, Plan
Commission; Joan Evanich, Landmark Preservation Committee; John Lien, Village
Council.
   Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the
appointments as noted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Derning.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:10 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Derning. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:11 p.m.
July 14, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved July 21, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
14, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien and William Meuer.
Absent: Trustee Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manger Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders,
Police Chief William Gallagher, and about 22 persons in the audience.
II. Caledonian Project – Lincoln Avenue.
   Presentation by Developer. Mr. Peter Cummins, the developer of the Winnetka
Galleria, thanked the Council for the opportunity to share his plans for the
property and presented an overview of the proposed project. He introduced John
Myefski, the architect, who reviewed a three dimensional model, floor plans and
elevations with the Trustees. He also explained some of the specific design
features of the building.
Mr. Cummins then spoke to the tenancy of the building. He noted that upon
completion Frances Heffernan would be moving back into the Lincoln Avenue
location and mentioned that three of the eight condominium units were already
under contract. He emphasized his desire to enhance the Lincoln Avenue business
district and to minimize the disturbance and inconvenience to the neighborhood
created by the project. Mr. Cummins also distributed to the Trustees copies of
the initial construction schedule highlighting certain periods of intense
activity. He said that overall he expected the project to take from 13 to 16
months to complete.
Proposed Truck Route. Steven Saunders, Director of Public Works, discussed the
proposed plan to provide construction access to the construction site. He
explained that the original plan had been to provide access via the Lincoln
Avenue Parking Lot, rather than by Lincoln Avenue itself, but that concern over
the structural capacity of the Pine Street Bridge, as it relates to the
anticipated type of loads, has necessitated modifying the plan. He explained
that the modified plan uses the Lincoln Avenue entrance to the parking lot for
both the construction entrance and an exit. It also involves reconfiguring the
temporary fence that is currently in place so that semi-trailers can access the
site and staging area from Lincoln Avenue, and exit the site as well, without
having to perform complex maneuvers. This would result in the removal of an
additional 10-12 parking spaces, and relocation of several trees and shrubs.
Mr. Saunders outlined his recommended truck route, noting that it minimizes the
presence of semi-trailers in residential areas, maximizes the use of State
roads, and places semi-trailers on the Oak Street Bridge which was recently
rehabilitated.
Mr. Saunders also explained the proposed reassignment of available parking
spaces in the Lincoln Avenue lot.
Manager Williams spoke to the difficulty of balancing the need for parking
among the various user groups, commuters, employees, customers and Community
House users. He also commented on possible available options to minimize the
impact on parking, including the use of the Congregational Church parking lot,
use of the railroad cut, and use of the south side of Oak Street along the
Village Green.
The Trustees discussed at length the consequences, intended and unintended, of
reallocating available parking and considered various alternatives.
Parking Impact – Discussion. President Holland opened the floor for questions
and comments.
   Debby Clinch, Director, Winnetka Community Nursery School, thanked Mr.
Saunders and the Council for reevaluating the situation and rerouting the truck
traffic off of Killian Court. She reiterated the school’s willingness to
cooperate in any way possible during the construction period.
   Jim Williams, 2227 Birchwood Avenue, Wilmette, a WCNS parent, expressed
concern for the numerous elderly who use the short term parking in the
Community House lot and suggested that fewer people would be disrupted if
commuter spaces were reassigned to other locations than if the short term
parking was reassigned. President Holland noted that with the relocation of the
Senior Center, the age of Community House users had dropped, except for the
Friday bridge game. She encouraged the nursery school to make its employees and
parents aware of the problems.
   Leslie Bullock, Winnetka Travel, 551 Lincoln Ave., voiced her worries about
cutting back on employee parking. She pointed out that many employees already
park on Lincoln Avenue, moving their cars from space to space, and said she
feared that the elimination of what little legal employee parking there is will
only encourage more of this behavior, making it even more difficult for
customers to find suitable parking. She said most clients are not willing to
walk to spend their money; they expect to be able to park near to their
destination.
   President Holland emphasized that the parking regulations would be strictly
enforced, and Chief Gallagher commented on the number of tickets issued along
Lincoln Avenue from Elm to Pine Streets.
Andy Bullock, President of the Lincoln Avenue Townhome Association raised
questions from the property owners with Messrs. Cummins and Saunders regarding
the proposed parking structure ingress, the staging area, and the construction
timeline.
   Eve Bremen, President of the Chamber of Commerce and Manager of Coldwell
Banker, commented on the potential loss of employee parking, stating that if
retailers don’t have a place to park, then consumers don’t either.
   Mike Wurzberg, owner of Minna Hart, expressed his opinion that the Community
House users will simply use the spaces on Lincoln Avenue, thereby preventing
parking by customers. He pointed out that parking is already very difficult
even though the key retail season, August 15th to October 15th,  has not begun.
He concurred with Ms. Bullock’s comments about employee abuse of parking on
Lincoln Avenue and said that strict enforcement would be very important.
   Maggie Barrett, the owner of Frances Heffernan, spoke in support of Mr.
Cummins and the Winnetka Galleria project as well as her fellow retailers. She
suggested that the business owners meet together with all of their employees to
explain the situation and encourage them to use parking on the outskirts of the
business district rather than on Lincoln Avenue.
   Diane Phelan, a local business owner, spoke about the present difficulty of
finding parking, saying that she has to get to work earlier and earlier in
order to get a legal spot.
   On behalf of Council, President Holland thanked all of the participants in
the discussion for their input. She acknowledged that parking is, and will
continue to be, a problem in the Village, regardless of the upcoming
construction project. She asked everyone for patience and understanding during
the project and urged all business owners to encourage their employees not to
park on Lincoln Avenue.
   Finance Director Ed McKee arrived at 8:30 p.m.
   Trustee Duhl repeatedly cited the availability of parking along Pine,
Prospect and Lincoln Avenue past the Community House.
   Trustee Meuer asked about distinguishing between resident commuters and
nonresident commuters. Manager Williams responded that because the Village has
used State and Metra funds for the construction of streets, it cannot
discriminate between categories of commuters. Trustee Meuer also suggested
assigning commuters to the lot behind Grand Food, thereby retaining as many
parking spaces as possible in the Lincoln Avenue lot for employees and
shoppers. He urged the Village Manager to continue to explore the possibility
of providing parking in the railroad cut for construction employees.
   Trustee Duhl questioned whether it was time to institute a zoned parking
policy along Lincoln Avenue.
Trustee Derning asked Chief Gallagher to explain zoned parking concept and its
potential consequences. Chief Gallagher did so stating that there would be
significant impact on local businesses.
   President Holland asked Staff to continue to investigate alternative
solutions to the parking dilemma and indicated that enforcement would be
stepped up, especially during the construction. She urged business owners to
educate their employees as to how they can help alleviate the problem. She also
asked Mr. Cummins to employ flag men during the construction to alert normal
traffic to the entering and exiting of big trucks. Mr. Cummins indicated that
he would make every effort to ensure that adequate safety procedures are
followed.
III. Liquor Service at Golf Course.
Mr. Joe Dooley, President, Winnetka Park District, presented a request from the
Park District for the issuance of a liquor license which would permit the sale
of wine and beer through the food service operation at the golf clubhouse. He
explained that over the last several years the District has received repeated
requests for beer and wine service from the men’s golf club as well as from
individual members. In addition to the service and convenience issues, he
mentioned that it was becoming increasingly difficult to find food vendors that
are interested in bidding on the food service contract because it is very
difficult to make a profit without the service of liquor, particularly when the
business is so weather driven.
   He emphasized that the District is only interested in the service of beer
and wine and only interested in serving and allowing the consumption of such
within the golf clubhouse; there would be no service of beer or wine on a
beverage cart. He indicated that the District would be willing to comply with
any restrictions imposed by the Village. In support of the request, he referred
the Trustees to a survey of neighboring public golf courses which indicates
that only Winnetka and Glencoe restrict the service of liquor.
Trustee Duhl inquired whether any of the other golf courses had had any
problems resulting directly from the sale of alcohol. Mr. Dan Newport, Director
of the Park District, responded that none had reported any issues except when
individuals were served on the course itself. He also cited the lack of
problems during private events held on the Winnetka course when liquor was
available.
Trustee Lanphier arrived at 9:30 p.m. She voiced concern about the potential
for a bad accident resulting from over consumption and driving, especially
given the proximity of the golf clubhouse to schools and other Park District
activity centers.
Trustee Hilton expressed some concern about teenagers being served and asked
during what hours the Park District proposed to make service available. Mr.
Newport reiterated his finding that other golf clubs had not encountered any
problems. He said that they were hoping to make the service available from
approximately 11:00 a.m. until 7:30 p.m., from mid-April through mid-October,
when food service is available. He pointed out that customers will have to go
up to a counter to purchase a beverage and that it will not be a full service
bar.
Chief Gallagher commented on the request. He expressed his belief that it would
be a step toward loosening up the liquor regulations generally throughout the
Village. He also pointed out that in this situation it would be particularly
difficult to define what constitutes a “full meal” and enforce the purchase of
such should that be required.
In response to a question from Trustee Derning about whether the Village would
be required to treat other businesses in the same manner, Attorney Janega
indicated that it was possible to create a new category of license into which
no organization other than the Park District golf clubhouse could fit. She said
the question was entirely one of policy, not legality. The Village has the
legal authority to carve out a special area for a publicly operated entity, and
pointed out that there are no other similar facilities, either public or
private, within the corporate limits of the Village. She said there would be no
problem in recognizing the golf course as a separate facility and restrict the
beverage service to times when they have an operating food service.
The Trustees discussed the feasibility of various possible regulations. Trustee
Meuer inquired of Messrs. Dooley and Newport whether the Park District would be
willing to accept the sale of beer only. They answered in the affirmative.
Trustees Hilton, Lien and Duhl indicated support for the Park District’s
request, limiting the beverage for sale to beer only, without requiring the
purchase of food. Trustee Derning noted some concerns, but expressed general
support. Trustee Lanphier dissented.
Trustee Meuer asked whether it would be possible to limit consumption, but Ms.
Janega indicated that doing so would make enforcement extremely difficult.
President Holland asked Ms. Janega to prepare an ordinance permitting the sale
of beer only by the Park District, at the golf clubhouse only, during specific
hours, without the purchase of food, but in conjunction with food service.
IV. Fisher Lane Sanitary Sewer.
Due to the lateness of the hour, this discussion was postponed until a later
meeting.
V. Stillerman Proposed Zoning Text Amendments.
Ms. Janega briefly reviewed this appeal from the decision of the Zoning Board
of Appeals to uphold the average front setback determination for 333 Fairview
Avenue and a subsequent request from the property owner for an amendment to the
zoning standards of the A-1 (one-sixth acre) Zoning District. She pointed out
that Staff had found no evidence of a wide-spread problem with average front
setbacks and had determined that the limitations on the size of the home that
can be constructed on the subject property are related more to a restrictive
covenant on the property than to setback requirements. Therefore, Staff does
not believe that an amendment to the setback provisions in the A-1 (one-sixth
acre) Zoning District is warranted.
   The applicant, Mr. Stillerman, presented his arguments in favor of his
position. He stated his belief that Staff’s interpretation of ordinance will
ultimately result in a “hodge podge” of setbacks on the block in question. He
said that the ordinance, as interpreted by Staff, doesn’t produce results
consistent with Village’s standards, and suggested eliminating the inclusion of
the anomaly on the block from the required calculations.
   Trustee Derning supported Staff’s interpretation of the ordinance, as did
Trustee Duhl. Trustee Duhl expressed hesitation about changing the ordinance
overall.
   Attorney Janega pointed out that the lot is buildable, but that the size of
the home permitted to be built is affected.
   Trustee Lanphier inquired what the consequence throughout the Village would
be if the ordinance were changed. Mr. Williams replied that the Director of
Community Development could better address this issue, but that it would
require further staff study. He said such a study could be done but that it
could not be completed for some time given the current work load of the
Department and other competing priorities.
Trustee Derning asked about the possibility of the applicant getting a variance
in order to accomplish his goal. Mr. Williams pointed out that the applicant
wished to demolish the whole building and start over again. He indicated that
under such circumstances it was extremely difficult to prove the hardship
necessary to get a variance.
   Attorney Janega voiced her belief that it is not good legislative policy to
change the policy for an entire district in order to address only one or two
properties. She said this situation did not constitute a consistent problem and
pointed out that a mechanism to prevent the extreme staggering of houses is
already in place.
   President Holland asked the effect of the setback restriction on the size of
the house which could be built. Mr. Stillerman replied that he would be
restricted to approximately 2,400 – 2,500 sq. ft. which would limit his
construct to a three bedroom home, which is not considered a desirable size in
this neighborhood.
   Mr. Duhl commented that the problem in this case is really the restrictive
covenant attached to the property, not the ordinance.
   Trustee Derning encouraged Mr. Stillerman to seek a variance instead of a
text amendment. Trustee Duhl said he thought that would be establishing a bad
precedent.
VI. Collection Policies.
Due to the lateness of the hour, discussion of this item was postponed until
another meeting.
VII. Appointments.
President Holland recommended the following persons as representatives from
their current boards to membership on the Ad Hoc Committee on Design
Guidelines: Ann Alexander, Plan Commission; Nan Greenough, Landmark
Preservation Commission; Christine Robb, Building Review Committee; and Phyllis
Thomas, Zoning Board of Appeals.
   Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the above
appointments. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Hilton, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 10:37 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:05 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Hilton, Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
IX. Adjournment.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Lanphier seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
11:06 p.m.
July 21, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved August 4, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
21, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien and Bill Meuer.
Absent: Trustee Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Director of Water and Electric Bryan McInturff and about eight
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
August 4, 1998, Regular Meeting. Of the Trustees present, Mr. Hilton and Mr.
Meuer indicated that they would not be able to attend.
August 11, 1998, Study Session. Of the Trustees present, Mr. Hilton and Mr.
Meuer indicated that they would not be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
July 7, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
July 14, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
   Ordinance No. M-529-98 – Zoning Variation: 2 Golf Lane – Adoption. Trustee
Derning moved to adopt the ordinance permitting reduced front yard setback and
improvements which fall within the existing nonconforming building lines. His
motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
   Ordinance No. MC-210-98 – Zoning and Building Moratorium in the Indian Hill
Business District - Introduction. Mr. Williams explained that the proposed
moratorium on building, zoning, and demolition permits would prevent
potentially inconsistent redevelopment of the area while the goals and
strategies of the Comprehensive Plan are being finalized. He noted that while
work on the Comprehensive Plan has progressed significantly since the adoption
of Ordinance No. MC-200-98 reducing the height limitations in the C-1 and C-
2 zoning districts, it is not yet complete. However, based on the present
status of the Comprehensive Plan, it appears that the final product will lead
to certain changes in the zoning standards, particularly in the C-1 commercial
district, in which the Indian Hill business district is located. This
moratorium would assure that the final goals and objectives of the
Comprehensive Plan can be meaningfully implemented.
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the ordinance as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Mr. Williams announced that there would be a Public Hearing during the August
18, 1998, Regular Council meeting to discuss the proposed moratorium.
   Resolution No. R-1431-98 – Bern’s Consolidation – 1074/1076 Pine Street -
Adoption. Ms. McCammack explained the request to consolidate two existing, non-
conforming, zoning lots into a single lot of record. She said that each of the
two lots, which until recently were under separate ownership, presently has a
residential dwelling located upon it. She noted that consolidation of the
required parcels requires the demolition of the residence on the south lot in
order to assure compliance with Zoning Ordinance limitations and recommended
that approval be conditioned upon this demolition.
   Trustee Duhl commented that he was very much in favor of this consolidation
and asked if there were other similar situations throughout the Village. Ms.
McCammack replied that she was not aware of any others.
   Trustee Meuer inquired whether at some future time, subsequent owners of the
consolidated property would be able to resubdivide it. Ms. Janega indicated
that under the provisions of the current Zoning Ordinance, such a move would be
nearly impossible due to the need to obtain numerous variances.
Trustee Meuer then moved to adopt the proposed Resolution, seconded by Trustee
Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Cherry Street Alley Improvements – Public Hearing. At 7:40 p.m. President
Holland convened the Council as the Board of Local Improvements for the purpose
of holding a Public Hearing in connection with the proposed improvements to the
public alley between Ash and Cherry Streets, from Locust to Rosewood.
The Trustees reconvened into Regular Session at 8:20 p.m. Present: Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Hilton, Lien and Meuer. Absent: Trustee Lanphier. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of
Public Works Steven Saunders and Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1085 and 1086. Mr. Lien reviewed the questions he had raised
with Finance Director Ed McKee before moving to approve Warrant List No. 1081
in the total amount of $235,607.68 and Warrant List No. 1086 in the total
amount of $507,251.25. Trustee Hilton seconded Mr.Lien’s motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl,
Hilton, Derning and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Bid No. 98-025 – Pole Mount Transformers. Mr. McInturff reviewed the bid for
the purchase and delivery of various size single phase conventional overhead
transformers to be purchased on an “as needed” basis for FY 98/99 and FY 99/00.
He pointed to Staff’s recommendation that the award be made to Wesco
Distribution.
Trustee Hilton moved to accept Staff’s recommendation and approve the bid to
Wesco Distribution. He was seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the
motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,, Duhl, Derning and
Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
DK Recycling Systems Monthly Report. President Holland noted that once again
there had been no odor complaints from the compost site and cited the tipping
fees paid for the month.
Inventory Study. Manager Williams reviewed a proposal prepared by the Village’s
auditors, Miller Cooper & Co., Ltd., to perform an inventory study in the Water
& Electric Yard, motor pool and Public Works operations, as discussed during
the annual budget process. He cited the purpose of the study as (1) to make
sure that there are appropriate controls over physical inventory, (2) to
summarize current practices, and (3) to make cost effective recommendations to
improve inventory management. He noted that this project is scheduled to begin
in early August and that the proposed cost of $5,600 was covered by an
appropriate budget allocation
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the project as
outlined by Mr. Williams. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl and Derning. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Lanphier.
SCADA Equipment Purchase. Director of Water and Electric Bryan McInturff
explained the request for approval of the purchase of new SCADA equipment and
software from QEI, without bids, and summarized the reasons for the request.
Manager Williams supported Mr. McInturff’s recommendation.
Trustee Derning suggested that Mr. McInturff explore the possibility of
expanding the capacity of the hard drive, a suggestion with which Mr. Duhl
concurred. Mr. McInturff indicated that he would do so.
Trustee Derning moved to approve the award of the equipment purchase to SCADA
in the amount of $110,000. Trustee Lien seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and
Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
X. Reports.
   Village President. President Holland announced that she had received a
letter from the Illinois Historical Preservation Agency notifying her of the
nomination of the Kuppenheimer House for inclusion on the National Register.
   She also thanked all of the Trustees for participating in Winnetka Days and
thanked those residents who responded to her letter regarding the Religious
Freedom Reformation Act.
   Village Trustees. Trustee Derning indicated that he had met with the Chamber
of Commerce and that they had expressed continuing interest in the status of
the Sign Code amendments. Mr. Williams indicated that he expected the issue to
be on Council’s agenda again in September.
   Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported that RED Center should be activated
on August 2, 1998.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland asked the Trustees to affirm the appointment of Ann
Alexander, Nan Greenough, Christine Robb and Phyllis Thomas to the Ad Hoc
Committee on Design Guidelines. She also proposed the appointment to that
committee of John Kollar as a representative from the Design Review Board.
   Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to approve these
appointments. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:15 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:16 p.m.
August 4, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved August 18, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
4, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, and John Lien. Absent: Trustees David
Hilton Penfield Lanphier, and William Meuer. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and about 25 persons
in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that Agenda Items Nos. 7(G)(2) and 7(G)(4) had been
postponed for consideration until the August 18, 1998, Regular Meeting. The
Council then approved the Agenda as amended.
IV. Quorum.
August 18, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
September 1, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
July 21, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved
to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
   Mr. Martin Kenny, 510 Meadow, inquired about the discussion on the proposed
building moratorium for the Indian Hill Commercial District which had been
announced in the Winnetka Talk. President Holland informed Mr. Kenny that the
article in the newspaper had been in error and that the Public Hearing is
scheduled for August 18, 1998. Mr. Kenny stated that he thought the Council
ought to consider the affected residential properties in the Indian Hill area
as well as the commercial properties.
President Holland encouraged Mr. Kenny and his neighbors to attend the August
18th meeting.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-530-98 – Zoning Variation: 984 Hill Road - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed this request to permit a reduced rear yard setback. She
pointed out that the Zoning Board of Appeals had recommended that the Council
grant the variation as requested.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-531-98 – Zoning Variation: 466 Poplar - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed this request to permit an increase in permitted building
size (Gross Floor Area). In doing so, she noted that the existing residence
currently exceeds the maximum permitted Gross Floor Area and that the requested
addition would increase the nonconformity. She also mentioned that in this
case, the Zoning Board of Appeals voted 4-2 to recommend granting the variation
requested.
Mr. Konstant, architect for the applicant, explained the proposed addition. He
stated that a hardship was created when 750 square feet of the property was
sold off by a previous owner, thus considerably lowering the allowable Gross
Floor Area. He also pointed out that 324 square feet of the attic is unusable
due to the steeply pitched roof, a portion of which, however, must be counted
towards Gross Floor Area. Mr. Konstant noted that the addition will not exceed
the existing footprint of the house and expressed his belief that without
improvements this house will become a candidate for a tear down. He stated his
opinion that the addition will permit the applicant to build a kitchen suitable
for the house without changing the overall character of the home or the
neighborhood.
In response to questions from Trustees Duhl and Lien, Ms. McCammack reviewed
the discussions of the Zoning Board of Appeals as well as discussions by the
Council earlier this year with regard to roof forms. She also clarified the
rules for calculating FAR.
Trustee Derning asked if the addition would be invisible from the street. Ms.
McCammack answered that it would be.
President Holland expressed her opinion that this was the kind of house and
roof line that the Village wished to encourage, even though the design of the
roof adds square footage to the FAR calculations.
Trustees Duhl and Lien stated that they would vote to introduce the ordinance,
but that they were not certain that they ultimately would be able to vote in
favor of its adoption. Mr. Duhl said that it was difficult for him to see a
legitimate legal hardship in this instance. He added that the applicable rules
were carefully considered before adoption and that he has a problem now taking
exception to those rules. He cited the difficulty in distinguishing this
request from the host of other requests for FAR variations that might come
before the Council. Mr. Lien noted that he has difficulty with the entire GFA
calculations concept and therefore, was keeping an open mind pending further
study and consideration.
Mr. Konstant replied that the applicants could tear down the existing house and
build a larger home with a less steep roof line which would cause a greater
infringement on the neighbors’ air, light and green space.
Mr. Duhl pointed out that at present, steep roofs do count in the FAR
calculations and that he could not reasonably make a distinction between this
case and the cases on Foxdale which came before the Council earlier this year.
He said he saw no reason to create an exception in this instance and, as
previously stated, he is generally against exceptions. He expressed his belief
that bulk is bulk, regardless of the form it takes. He acknowledged that
perhaps the ordinance needs to be amended, but for the present it must serve as
the objective standard by which all cases are measured.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-532-98 – Zoning Variation: 1311 Scott Avenue - Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed this request to permit an increased building size (Gross
Floor Area) to a residence which is already not in conformity. The variation
would allow for the construction of four dormers on the upper floor of a single
family residence. Ms. McCammack pointed out that this is a split lot, a portion
of which is in Glencoe, and that the Village of Glencoe has already approved
the request. She mentioned that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to
recommend granting the variation requested.
President Holland invited the applicant, Mr. Ebersole, to comment on his
request, which he did.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-534-98 – Zoning Variation: 955 Old Green Bay Road –
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed this request to permit a reduced rear yard
setback in order to construct an addition to connect the existing detached
garage to the residence. She explained that the current detached garage is
considered an accessory building, but once it is attached, it becomes part of
the primary structure and setbacks apply. She noted that in this case, the
Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend granting the variation.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-536-98 – Special Use Permit: Christ Church, 784 Sheridan Road –
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed this request from Christ Church for a
Special Use Permit which would allow the expansion of their existing facility
and include the consolidation of the church lot and building with the
neighboring residential lot and house at 739 Humboldt. She noted that both the
Design Review Board and the Plan Commission had recommended approval of the
permit and mentioned that the plat of consolidation would be on Council’s
agenda on August 18, 1998.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Mr. Scott Hargadon, attorney for Christ Church, introduced Mr. Craig Fowler,
Church Warden, and Mr. Steve Corchran, Traffic Engineer, Metro Traffic
Consultants, who each made presentations and answered questions from the
Trustees.
Mr. Fowler explained that the goal of the project was to physically unify what
has been a divided congregation for many years. He briefly reviewed the
specifics of the proposal and stated that the Church had worked with both the
Village and its neighbors to minimize the impact of this consolidation on the
neighborhood.
Trustee Lien asked Ms. McCammack to explain the retrofitting required to make
Humboldt House useable as an educational building, which she did.
Citing its heritage, President Holland expressed her pleasure that Humboldt
House was to be preserved.
Mr. Corchran summarized the traffic and parking studies which his firm had
conducted in connection with the transformation of the Church and the Humboldt
House into a connected entity.
Mr. Duhl inquired whether the study had taken into consideration the competing
traffic and parking needs from attendees at Winnetka Congregational Church. Mr.
Corchran answered in the affirmative.
By roll call vote, the motion to introduce passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Duhl and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Hilton, Lanphier and
Meuer.
Ordinance No. MC-211-98 – Establishing Liquor License Classification for the
Park District – Introduction. President Holland asked Dan Newport, Director of
the Park District, to discuss their request for an amendment to the Liquor
Ordinance allowing the sale of beer at the Park District Golf Course clubhouse
food service facility. He briefly reviewed the discussion of the item at the
July 14, 1998, Study Session and outlined the proposed service. Mr. Newport
emphasized that there would be no beer service outside of the clubhouse.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl and
Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Hilton, Lanphier and Meuer.
Winnetka Galleria, 562-572 Lincoln Avenue
(1)   Ordinance No. M-535-98 – Zoning Variation – Introduction. Ms. McCammack
distributed materials from the Zoning Board of Appeals record and reviewed the
history of the applicant’s request for a variation to permit a commercial
building height of three stories, instead of the maximum permitted height of
two and one half stories, in order to accommodate the development of a three
story, mixed use building. She pointed out that although the proposed
development complies with the district’s height requirements, it does not with
regard to the number of stories. During her review, Ms. McCammack mentioned
that both the Design Review Board and the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted in
favor of the request.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Manager Williams asked Ms. McCammack to discuss the definition of a half story
for the Trustees, which she did, using a diagram in their Agenda packets for
clarification. Mr. Williams expressed his belief that that this problem has
arisen as a result of the application of the residential definition of a half
story to a commercial project. He stated that he did not consider this a
dangerous precedent and thinks that it is in keeping with the intent of
Council’s policy when it reduced the height of new commercial buildings from
four stories to two and one half stories.
Trustee Duhl asked whether the ordinance needed to be amended. Mr. Williams
replied that this idea had been discussed by Staff, with the consensus being
that it was preferable to finish the commercial design guidelines before making
any text amendments. Mr. Duhl stated his belief that once design guidelines are
determined, a text amendment should be passed in order to make the Code clear.
President Holland asked questions about some of the design features to which
Mr. Peter Cummins, managing partner of Winnetka Galleria, LLC, responded.
President Holland then clarified that tonight’s vote was only on the variation
and did not encompass any of the proposed design aspects.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl and
Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Hilton, Lanphier and Meuer.
(2)   Resolution No. R-1433-98 – Sale of Property to Winnetka Galleria –
Adoption. This item was postponed until the August 18, 1998, Regular Meeting,
because two-thirds of the Council members were not present.
(3)   Resolution No. R-1434-98 – Parking Lot License Agreement – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that the proposed agreement provides compensation and
protection of the Village’s interests in the Lincoln Avenue parking lot during
construction of the Winnetka Galleria. She pointed out differences between the
draft agreement distributed at tonight’s meeting from the one included in the
Agenda packet, noting that they were not substantive in nature, but intended
for clarification. The License Agreement, the term of which expires on January
1, 2000, grants a license to use the parking lot and specifically defines both
the license area and the access routes. The license fee is $25,000 and the
Agreement provides for total restoration of the parking lot.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien and Duhl
and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Meuer, Hilton and Lanphier.
(4)   Resolution No. R-1435-98 – Plat Consolidation – Adoption. This item was
postponed until the August 18, 1998, Regular Meeting, because two-thirds of the
Council members were not present.
VIII. Unfinished Business
- None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1087 and 1088. Trustee Lien stated that all of his questions
had been answered satisfactorily and moved to approve Warrant List No.1087 in
the total amount of $560,538.96 and Warrant List No. 1088 in the total amount
of $322,615.41. Trustee Duhl seconded the motion. By roll call vote the warrant
lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl and Lien and
President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Hilton, Lanphier and Meuer.
Bid No. 98-022 – Truck and Snow Plow Bids. Public Works Director, Steven
Saunders, explained that this bid was for the purchase and delivery of three
dump trucks with mounted snowplow equipment to replace three trucks that will
then go to auction upon the delivery of the new equipment. In response to a
question from Manager Williams, he said that this equipment would complete the
Snow Plow Replacement Plan for pieces of major, heavy duty equipment. He
reviewed his recommendation to award the bid to Northwest Trucks, Inc., in the
total amount of $260,302.00.
Trustee Duhl moved to accept the bid of Northwest Trucks, Inc. He was seconded
by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Duhl, Derning and Lien and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Hilton, Meuer and Lanphier.
Willow Road Pedestrian Underpass Repair. Mr. Saunders presented various
alternatives, as outlined in his Agenda Report, for the rehabilitation of the
Willow Road pedestrian subway under Green Bay Road and the Union Pacific
Railroad tracks. Following a discussion of the options, Trustee Derning moved
to approve Mr. Saunders’ “Strategy 2” and award the contract bid to Schaefges
Bros. in the amount of $105,799. The motion was seconded by Trustee Duhl. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien and
Duhl and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Hilton, Lanphier and
Meuer.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland announced that she had received a letter
from Patty Sullivan, Chairman of the Winnetka Garden Clubs, detailing all of
the projects throughout the Village undertaken by the various clubs. She
thanked all of the volunteers for their efforts and indicated that an
acknowledgment would appear in the next edition of the Winnetka Report. She
also commended Public Works Supervisor Bill Willing for his and his staff’s
support of the garden clubs’ efforts.
Village Manager.
Cancellation of Study Session. Mr. Williams announced that the August 11, 1998,
Study Session had been canceled.
Village Facilities Tour. Manager Williams reminded the Trustees of the Village
facilities tour scheduled for Saturday, September 26, 1998.
Fire Department 2nd Quarterly Report. Mr. Williams highlighted some of the
points in the report distributed to the Trustees with their Agenda packets.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl and Lien,. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Hilton, Lanphier and
Meuer. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:35 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl and Lien,. Absent: Trustees Hilton, Lanphier
and Meuer. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:36 p.m.
August 18, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved September 1, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
18, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, John Lien, and Bill Meuer. Absent: Trustees Duhl,
Lanphier and Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and about 75 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
September 1, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
September 8, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
August 4, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-530-98 – Zoning Variation: 984 Hill Road – Adoption. Trustee
Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting a
reduced rear yard setback. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Derning and Lien and President Holland. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
Ordinance No. M-532-98 – Zoning Variances: 1311 Scott – Adoption. Trustee
Derning, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting
increased building size (gross floor area). By roll call vote, the motion
passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien and Meuer and President
Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
Ordinance No. M-534-98 – Zoning Variation: 955 Old Green Bay Road – Adoption.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting reduced rear yard setback. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien and Meuer and President Holland.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
Christ Church, 784 Sheridan Road and 569 Humboldt
Ordinance No. M-536-98 – Special Use Permit – Adoption. Trustee Derning,
seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting the church
to expand its existing Sheridan Road church facilities and functions to the
existing house on Humboldt Avenue and construct additions.
Attorney Janega reported on a conversation held with the attorney for the
Church and enumerated minor changes in the text of the draft ordinance upon
which they had agreed.
Trustee Derning moved to adopt the amendments as outlined by Ms. Janega. His
motion was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Holland then asked if there were any comments from the floor. There
being none, she called for a vote on the motion to adopt the ordinance, as
amended.
For the benefit of the audience, Trustee Meuer explained that although it
appeared that the Council was moving very quickly through this issue, it had,
in fact, been under consideration for some time. He expressed his full support
for the project and thanked the Church for its sensitivity and cooperation.
President Holland echoed Mr. Meuer’s remarks, adding special thanks for saving
the Humboldt House.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien,
Meuer and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and
Hilton.
Resolution No. R-1432-98 – Plat of Consolidation – Adoption. Trustee Meuer,
seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt this resolution consolidating the two
separate parcels of Christ Church and Humboldt House into a single lot of
record, pursuant to Ordinance No. M-536-98. By roll call vote, the motion
passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Derning, Lien and President Holland.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
President Holland wished the Church good luck with its project.
Ordinance No. MC-211-98 – Establishing Liquor License Classification for the
Park District – Adoption. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to
adopt this ordinance creating a separate “Class P” license for the Park
District and adopting text amendments to Chapter 35 setting out the conditions
under which liquor service will be provided.
President Holland recognized Jerry Burke, 1201 Pine Street. Mr. Burke read a
prepared letter requesting that the Council table consideration of this issue
until October in order to permit further study and discussion. He cited his
belief that the Park District’s request was motivated more by convenience for
golfers than by the need to encourage vendors to bid on the food service
operation. He pointed out that delaying the decision would not adversely affect
the Park District because bids for the 1999 vendor would not be sought until
next year. Mr. Burke suggested that comments from neighbors and other
interested groups and organizations be solicited before taking a final vote.
President Holland invited Howie Clinch and Mary Garrison, both Park District
Commissioners, to comment on their request. Mr. Clinch agreed with Mr. Burke’s
comments. Ms. Garrison noted that this is not a new issue and has been
requested for a long time. She said that it was a service issue for golfers and
that she can’t conceive of there being an incident over service of beer.
Trustee Meuer pointed out that there has already been ample opportunity for
community input because the question was discussed at a Council Study Session
with all of the Trustees present as well as appearing on the Council’s agenda
for introduction. He pointed out that the proposed service would be on a trial
basis and would be evaluated at the end of one year of operation. Mr. Meuer
indicated his continued support for the proposal.
Trustee Derning responded to some of Mr. Burke’s comments and concurred with
Trustee Meuer’s remark that there had already been opportunities for interested
parties to convey their positions to the Council.
Trustee Lien recounted his recollection that the Park District had presented
its request primarily based on the necessity to have more vendors bid on the
food service operation. He expressed his concern that the Park District’s
request now appears to be motivated more by convenience for golfers. He stated
that while he had been in favor of the proposal based on the argument of
necessity, he now was disturbed by the commissioners’ remarks that convenience
is the driving force. Mr. Lien indicated that he would not be able to vote in
favor of the proposal at this time and suggested that it be tabled for future
study.
Attorney Janega confirmed Mr. Lien’s recollection of the Park District’s
presentation. She also pointed out that in order to pass, the motion would
require the affirmative vote of all four Council members present at the
evening’s meeting.
Trustee Derning noted that the Park District commissioners had not said
convenience was the overriding consideration and had not excluded the argument
of necessity.
Mr. Lien moved to table the motion. Trustee Derning seconded the motion. By
roll call vote the motion failed. Ayes: Trustee Lien and President Holland.
Nays: Trustees Derning and Meuer. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
Attorney Janega explained that although the motion to table had failed, it was
clear that the standing motion to adopt would not pass since it required the
positive vote of all four Council members. Therefore, the issue had not been
tabled, but would be taken off the agenda for further consideration this
evening.
Trustee Meuer moved to withdraw his motion to adopt and was seconded by Trustee
Derning. By voice vote the motion passed.
Trustee Meuer then moved to schedule the item for the September 8th Study
Session agenda. His motion was seconded by Trustee Derning.
Trustee Lien objected on the grounds that September was too early to permit
thorough study and suggested the discussion be scheduled for the October 13th
Study Session.
Trustee Meuer moved to amend his motion to reflect October 13th, seconded by
Trustee Derning. By voice vote the motion passed. By roll call vote, the motion
set the item on the agenda for October 13th passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Derning and Lien and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
Resolution No. R-1438-98 – Approval of Cook & Cummins Subdivision, 503 Rosewood
– Adoption. Trustee Derning moved to adopt this ordinance. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Meuer.
Attorney Janega explained replacement pages distributed to the Trustees in
which the language had been amended to clarify the recommended conditions upon
which the subdivison is dependant.
Trustee Lien expressed some concern about approving the resolution given the
Plan Commission’s discussion about potential water and flooding. He asked
whether the proposed detention facility was designed to accomplish what the
ordinance proposes.
Manager Williams replied that staff believes that it can be accomplished,
although it might require the building of a smaller house. He confirmed his
comments with Director of Public Works Steve Saunders.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien
and Meuer and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Hilton and
Lanphier.
Ordinance No. MC-210-98 – Imposing a Moratorium on Development in the C-1
(Indian Hill) Commercial Zoning District – Public Hearing and Adoption.
President Holland opened the Public Hearing.
Manager Williams announced the receipt by the Community Development Department
late Tuesday afternoon of an application for rezoning for properties on
Winnetka Avenue and 80 Brier Street, and asked Attorney Janega to review the
options available to the Council in light of the receipt of this application.
Ms. Janega said that the mere fact that something had been submitted that
afternoon did not mean that it would not be governed by an ordinance enacted
this evening. Consequently, all of the options available to the Council
remained open. She suggested that because of the large attendance at the
meeting, the Trustees proceed with the Public Hearing as scheduled and then
decide whether to continue with the vote on the moratorium.
President Holland announced that the Public Hearing would continue and
explained the procedures for speakers.
Manager Williams swore in members of the audience who wished to comment.
He explained that the proposed ordinance would impose a six month moratorium on
new construction and additions to buildings in the C-1 zoning district in order
to allow time for the Plan Commission, working with Thompson Dyke & Associates
(TD&A), to complete the draft strategic plan for the commercial zoning
districts. He noted that the Plan Commission and TD&A are continuing to work on
the Comprehensive Plan vis-à-vis community goals and objectives, land use
issues, and strategic plans for the Green Bay Road Corridor and the business
districts but that the plan is still in rough draft form pending further
discussions with village residents. He pointed out that at present, it appears
likely that the final Comprehensive Plan may recommend changes in the zoning
standards for the C-1 commercial district. The proposed moratorium on building,
zoning and demolition permit activity would prevent potentially inconsistent
redevelopment.
Manager Williams also mentioned that the Plan Commission has scheduled a
workshop session for September 2nd and a Public Hearing on September 9th to
further explore with the Indian Hill neighborhood possible solutions to achieve
shared goals and objectives as well to discuss the various land use issues.
President Holland recognized David Grossberg, an attorney and resident,
representing the petitioners in the rezoning application. Mr. Grossberg asked
the Council to consider the possibility that the proposed development would
have positive benefit to the community. He indicated that he felt sure that if
the developer was allowed to proceed and present their development plan, the
Council would be persuaded by the quality of the proposed development. Mr.
Grossberg explained the plights of the respective property owners and pleaded
that a moratorium would inflict hardship on these individuals. He pointed out
that if the rezoning application was allowed to proceed on course, the Council
would have an opportunity at a later date to turn down the development.
Irv Jobst, owner of New Trier Service Station, explained it was necessary for
him to sell his property in a timely fashion because he would otherwise be
required to comply with environmental regulations in order to continue his
business. He asked the Council not to act in a manner that would prevent him
from doing do.
Jim Hurtel, 95 High Street, urged the Council to adopt the moratorium. He
argued that the neighborhood was opposed to the proposed draft plans for the
Indian Hill District and needed time to make their views known to the Plan
Commission.
An unidentified member of the audience asked that the letter contained in the
rezoning application from Dr. Murphy indicating his intent to sell his property
at 530 Winnetka Avenue be read aloud. Mr. Williams did so.
President Holland reminded the audience that this Public Hearing was not about
the proposed development but about the moratorium. She also indicated that in
her experience any such request for rezoning of this magnitude would take
longer than six months to process.
Bill Stevens, 142 Church Street, also urged approval of the moratorium. He said
that he did not think that the moratorium would prevent anyone from selling
their property.
Al Day, 85 High Street, voiced his support for the moratorium as well. He
indicated that neighbors had only recently received copies of the draft plan
for the area, which shows a tremendous amount of development encroaching into
the residential areas. In response, he said that the neighborhood was
organizing and would be working with the Plan Commission to develop a plan
which was more consistent with the residents’ wishes.
On behalf of the Council, President Holland said that they were pleased that
the residents were going to become more involved in the process. She indicated
that the she had assurances from the Plan Commission that nothing would be
presented to the Council until there was agreement among the parties.
Eugene Clarke, 554 Meadow Road, expressed compassion for the sellers’ interests
but reiterated that the Indian Hill residents are a part of this community,
too, and need to be satisfied as well. He expressed his strong support for the
moratorium and presented the Council with petitions supporting the moratorium
signed by residents. Mr. Clarke stated his belief that the key to Winnetka is
the residential community and asked that the proposed moratorium be extended to
include the Indian Hill residential area as well.
Jennifer Vincent, 514 Meadow Road, voiced her shock and dismay at the draft
plan. She asked for full disclosure from the Council and inclusion of the
residents in the planning process in order to avoid future animosity.
President Holland again stated that the Plan Commission’s documents were in
draft form only, saying that even the Village Council had not yet seen them.
She explained that the consultants had generated the maps which were included,
not the Plan Commission. Then President Holland reiterated the fact that this
Public Hearing concerned the proposed moratorium in the Indian Hill commercial
district and asked Attorney Janega to explain why no changes to the zoning
districts covered by the proposed ordinance could be made.
Ms. Janega explained that once a matter is published for purposes of notice,
the scope of the action cannot exceed what was published in the notice. In
order to expand the scope, a separate publication of notice and a new hearing
would be required. She also explained that State statutes require that any
proposed plan put together by the Plan Commission be sent to the Village
Council for review and Public Hearing before adoption.
Ms. Vincent inquired as to how the Indian Hill district had come to be under
discussion.
Cindy McCammack, Director of Community Development, reviewed the process for
the audience.
Richard Yaunt, 706 Cummings Avenue, Kenilworth, expressed displeasure on the
part of nearby Kenilworth residents and asked that they be included in the
planning process.
Manager Williams indicated that notice of future meetings would be sent to
those Kenilworth residents whose names appeared on a list provided by the
Village of Kenilworth.
Martin Kenny, 510 Meadow Road, stated his total support of the commercial
moratorium saying that it would permit the neighbors time to respond to the
draft documents prepared by the consultants and Plan Commission.
Tom Shanaham, 651 Brier, Kenilworth, recounted his views about the properties
involved in the rezoning application. He stated his belief that there would be
private harm to many neighbors, while the private good would only benefit three
individuals, and the harm to those individuals should the moratorium be adopted
was negligible given that it would not preclude their selling their property.
Sarah Hull, 580 Sunset Road, added her voice to those of the others in support
of the moratorium and who expressed concerns about the draft development plan.
She said that she believed that the moratorium was essential to maintaining the
unique and dynamic character of the community.
Jim Holland, 741 Brier Street, asked for further explanation of the reason for
the moratorium.
Attorney Janega repeated earlier comments that the purpose is to stave off
rezoning and development in the area so that the planning process can be
completed and any resulting zoning changes put into place.
President Holland provided further explanation.
Mr. Holland expressed concerns for the well being of Mr. Jobst and suggested
that perhaps the Village should purchase his property for resale at a later
time.
President Holland said that the moratorium would not prevent Mr. Jobst from
selling his property.
Ida Sugden noted her concern that the moratorium would only affect commercial
property.
President Holland pointed out that the moratorium process had been initiated
long before receipt of the rezoning application in order to provide the time
for completion of the applicable section of the Comprehensive Plan.
Mr. Grossberg reminded the Trustees that if the moratorium is passed, they
would not have the opportunity to view the plans for the primarily residential
development being proposed in the rezoning application.
President Holland closed the Public Hearing and than thanked the audience for
their input. She urged residents to attend the Plan Commission meetings
scheduled for September 2 and September 9.
Trustee Lien indicated that he supported the moratorium because the Plan
Commission needs to finish its job. He emphasized that the plan was still very
much in draft form and that resident input was critical to its completion. He,
too, urged all interested parties to participate in the upcoming meetings.
Trustee Derning concurred with Mr. Lien. He pointed out that the Council and
the residents work in complementary roles, not adversarial ones, and that the
moratorium was instituted by the Council itself, which has never been in
opposition to the concept.
Trustee Meuer echoed the comments of the two other Trustees, saying that the
moratorium would provide time to see that the community’s interests are best
served and would not prevent anyone from selling any piece of property if they
chose to do so. He then moved to adopt the moratorium, seconded by Trustee
Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Derningand Lien and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
President Holland introduced Andrew Ash and Sam Grossberg from Boy Scout Troop
18 who are pursuing their citizenship and community badge by attending the
meeting.
Winnetka Galleria, 562-572 Lincoln Avenue
Ordinance No. M-535-98 – Zoning Variation – Adoption. Attorney Janega announced
that both this item and the Resolution approving the Plat of Consolidation were
predicated upon the sale of Village property as provide for in Resolution No.
R-1433-98. She explained that since two thirds of the Council was not present,
the resolution approving the sale could not be passed tonight. Therefore, she
recommended amending this drafted ordinance, as well as the Plat of
Consolidation resolution, to make their approval conditioned upon completion of
the sale of property and commencement of the proposed construction.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee, moved to adopt the amended language. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Lien moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended, permitting a commercial
building height of three stories. Trustee Meuer seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Meuer, Derning
and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
Resolution No. R-1433-98 – Sale of Property to Winnetka Galleria – Adoption.
Because this item requires the vote of two-thirds, or five members, of the
entire Council, it was postponed to a later meeting.
Resolution No. R-1435-98 – Plat of Consolidation – Adoption. In accordance with
Attorney Janega’s remarks regarding Ordinance No. 535-98, Trustee Derning moved
to amend the Resolution’s language to make it conditioned upon completion of
the sale of property and commencement of proposed construction. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Meuer. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien, then moved to adopt this resolution,
as amended, approving the consolidation of six separate tax parcel numbers into
a single lot of record in order to facilitate the development of the Winnetka
Galleria.
By roll vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien,
Meuer and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and
Hilton.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1089 and 1090. Trustee Lien reported that all of his
questions had been answered satisfactorily and moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1089 in the total amount of $259,030.67 and Warrant List No. 1090 in the
total amount of $446,195.32. Trustee Meuer seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning
and Meuer and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier
and Hilton.
Bid No. 98-027 – Irrigation Systems. Mr. Williams explained the bid to provide
a total price to furnish and install a complete underground irrigation system
and to place it in an approved operating condition at the Public Safety
Building. Staff recommends awarding the bid to Halloran & Yauch, Inc., in the
total amount of $27,800.
Trustee Meuer moved to approve the bid as recommended. He was seconded by
Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Derning and Lien and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton.
Pine Hibbard Drainage Project Change Order. Mr. Saunders explained the
necessity for the change order, noting that the bulk of the cost increase is
attributable to pavement replacement and required extra work requests.
Mr. Williams pointed out that overall this project had been completed
significantly under budget.
Trustee Meuer moved to approve the change order. He was seconded by Trustee .
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Derning
and Lien and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and
Hilton.
Mr. Saunders replied to several questions from the Trustees regarding other
Public Works projects throughout the Village.
DK Recycling Systems Monthly Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to
the report submitted by Tom Scheidt of DK Recycling Systems.
Tower Road Bridge Reconstruction Project. Mr. Williams added this item to the
Agenda and asked Mr. Saunders to provide background information for the
Trustees. Mr. Saunders explained that several complications have arisen which
drastically affect the project schedule, delaying its anticipated completion
until late May, 1999. He indicated that the contractor thought that there was a
possibility of completing it by late December (weather permitting), if they
were allowed to work on weekends beginning at 7:00 a.m. Mr. Saunders
recommended that the Council grant this request under these extraordinary
circumstances in order to permit the contractor to finish as quickly as
possible thereby minimizing the length of time the Village would experience
construction induced traffic delays around the bridge.
Trustee Meuer inquired whether there would be any additional cost to the
Village. Mr. Saunders replied that there would not be.
Trustee Lien commented that there were only a few houses and a few apartments
in the immediate vicinity.
It was the consensus of the Trustees to allow the construction activity on the
weekends in the hopes of getting the project completed as swiftly as possible.
Trustee Meuer asked that the Public Works Department notify the immediate
neighbors of the change in construction hours advising them of the reasons
therefor.
MollyBelle Berish raised a question about the sidewalk on Spruce Street. Mr.
Williams indicated that the sidewalks are being redone according to the
Sidewalk Replacement Program schedule. He also informed the Council that he was
aware of some opposition to the building of a new sidewalk on the east side of
Church Street.
X. Reports.
Village Manager.
Village Facilities Tour. Mr. Williams reminded the Trustees of the tour of
village facilities scheduled for September 26, 1998.
Leaf Blower Report. Mr. Williams referred the Council to the report submitted
by Police Chief Gallagher regarding the enforcement activities during the
months of June and July. PD has been issuing citations.
New Compost Regulations. Mr. Williams reported that it appears that the
Illinois Pollution Control Board’s final ruling on the location of composting
facilities will not affect the Village’s composting operation, however, the
Village will be prevented from future expansion to the front areas of the
landfill.
Zoning Variation Request: 466 Poplar. Mr. Williams noted that at the request of
the applicant, this request was postponed until the September 1st Council
meeting.
XI. Appointments.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Lien, and Meuer and President Holland. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Duhl, Lanphier and Hilton. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:10
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:45 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustees Duhl, Lanphier and
Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:46 p.m.
                  ________________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
September 1, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved September 15, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 1, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and Bill Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about 5 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
A corrected copy of the agenda was presented to the Council and was approved as
corrected.
IV. Quorum.
September 8, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Hilton indicated that he would not be
present.
September 15, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Duhl indicated that he would not
be present.
V. Approval of Minutes
August 18, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Dr. Glen Sizemore, 807 Prospect, a resident there since 1983,addressed the
Council as a representative of his neighbors along Prospect, regarding the
street repaving project on Prospect Avenue. He said that for the last 3 weeks
and after curb and gutters were in place, it became apparent to 6 or 7 of his
neighbors that there was a problem with the level of the street. He said that
the village attorney responded to the residents’ concerns on Friday evening.
Essentially, they have asked that the project be stopped until a solution can
be reached to the problem the neighbors believe they have. He said that they
want engineers to redesign the street so it will not affect them adversely,
regarding the drainage of water. He said that the yards at 809 Prospect and 811
Prospect have always been low. He said that the Village was asked to add a
storm drain but that was not done until two years ago and it has helped. Dr.
Sizemore explained that with the current project, the street will now be 10 to
12 inches higher than his driveway, which has always drained into the street,
and his driveway will drop down from the street. To solve that a small drainage
site has been placed on the Morgan’s parkway at 811 Prospect to catch water off
of the Sizemore’s and Morgan’s yards, but he believes there will be quite a bit
of water. He said that there were six other neighbors that met with the village
manager and village attorney last night with mixed results.
Trustee Hilton asked what happened at the meeting.
Village Manager Williams said that Dr. Sizemore reported things correctly and
that the Village staff had taken grade shots today and will meet with the
neighbors further with some ideas that the staff has had. The manager said he
would keep the council informed.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-533-98 – Zoning Variation: 821 Foxdale – Introduction. Cindy
McCammack said that this request was for a second story addition of 224 square
feet on a non-conforming first floor. The required side yard is 6 feet. She
said that the house was constructed prior to the adoption of a zoning ordinance
and explains the nonconformity. The zoning board voted 6 to 0 to recommend
approval of the variance. The property on the north side adjacent to the
setback has a driveway that would separate the two structures. The distance
between them is 10 feet, which the fire chief has said is a comfortable
distance.
Trustee Derning moved to introduce the ordinance permitting a reduced side yard
setback. He was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and
Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-531-98 – Zoning Variances: 466 Poplar – Adoption. Ms. McCammack
briefly reviewed this request to permit an increase in permitted building size
(Gross Floor Area). In doing so, she noted that the existing residence
currently exceeds the maximum permitted Gross Floor Area and that the requested
addition would increase the nonconformity. She said that the ordinance was
introduced on August 4 following a favorable recommendation of the Zoning Board
of Appeals by a vote of 4 to 2. The petitioner is asking to permit the
construction of a one-story 140-square foot addition to the rear of the
property. The two persons on the Zoning Board who voted nay said that they
found it difficult to find a hardship. The hardship was stated to be that the
previous owner had exceeded the maximum allowable area.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt the ordinance, Trustee Derning seconded.
Mr. Alex Moore and Mrs. Moore addressed the Council. Mr. Moore said that they
are currently 70 feet over and with the additional 140 square feet it is 213
square feet, not 287 square feet as Ms. McCammack stated. They are asking for a
5-foot by 28-foot addition to the first floor at the back edge of the house.
The current kitchen accommodates a table only the size of a small card table.
They have 3 children who are growing and they want to increase the size of the
kitchen to allow them to have a regular kitchen table. This addition would also
create a passageway from the den to the kitchen instead of making a circular
route through the house to get to the kitchen. Mr. Moore said that they rented
the house for 4 years before purchasing it and love the house despite its
limitations. This addition does not change the bulk or streetscape and it does
not impact the sight views of their neighbors. The neighbors affected have
written letters to say they have no objection. The remainder of the first floor
and second floor remain unchanged One of the alternatives is to put a large
window into the back of the house for 65 feet, but it would not address the
problem of the flow through the house. They have considered changing the front
of the house, but feel it would change the façade and have also considered the
possibility of moving the den from one side of the house to another but it
would hurt the neighbors view.
Manager Williams pointed out that two letters were received in favor of the
addition from 470 Poplar and from the Egans. He clarified with Cindy McCammack
that there are really no differences in the square footage of the addition. The
difference is in the fact that the house was over the maximum allowable area
before the addition.
Ms. Lanphier asked about the useable attic space. Mr. Moore said that his
architect had said that if someone else took this house they could opt to put
in a flatter roof that would allow more space to be used at the attic level,
but it would change the bulk of the house. The attic was, in their context, not
useable space.
Trustee Duhl said that he has a difficult time supporting this request because
the gross floor area issue is so important. He said that it bothers him that
300 sq. ft. was sold off and put the house in the position that it is in. He
said that he does not like to disappoint anyone but he cannot support this
variance.
Mr. Moore replied that they are able to conform, but that it would take away
from the charm of this home.
Trustee Derning said that when the presentation was made, it was said that the
house might be destroyed and something built in its place. He said that doesn’t
make sense to him. He believed that the Zoning Board members had pointed out
that this request is not in violation of the intent of the ordinance and he
tried to look at it in this respect. He is concerned about the numbers
involved, but he doesn’t think there is any reason that this variance should
not be granted and is in support of it.
Trustee Lanphier said that she had looked at the property today and it presents
a classic case of trying to maintain the housing stock that they have. She
believes that granting the variation has less of an effect on the neighborhood
than a conforming alternative. The Moores could do the conforming things but it
would make the house less attractive and that is why the Council needs to weigh
these things carefully. She said that she is in favor of this particular
variation request.
Trustee Hilton stated that he agrees with Trustee Lanpher and Trustee Derning
and is in support of it.
Trustee Meuer said that he takes Trustee Duhl’s comments seriously, as well as
Trustee Lanphier’s and Derning’s, but would like to see the property himself
and asked if it would be a hardship for the Moores to come back September 15.
Mr. and Mrs. Moore said that they would not be able to attend that meeting, but
their architect could.
Trustee Lien said that he tends to agreed with Trustee Duhl for a couple of
reasons. Although this is modestly over the limit, most of the cases are. He
said that he thinks the Council can be accused of being arbitrary in deciding
these things. The ordinance is supposed to provide a rule and not have
individual cases come before the Council. He has a difficulty with the
ordinance itself because it includes the unusable attic space in the gross
floor area. He said that in this case, the house is already over and is going
to go over some more. He doesn’t think that he can support it, but thinks Mr.
Meuer should have the opportunity to see it in the light of day.
Trustee Lanphier said that the attic space relative to the Foxdale question
earlier this year, is not relevant in this case.
Trustee Lien said that the ordinance benefits houses of some types and not
others.
Trustee Lanphier said that when they put the ordinance into effect to include
attics in gross floor area, one of the concerns was that existing houses could
be penalized, but the feeling was that this is the reason there is a variation
process.
Trustee Lien said that he remembers they denied a balcony in one house and the
Council was unanimous on that. He has a hard time granting this variation
because the ordinance is the rule. He doesn’t see the hardship but is willing
to have it delayed to the 15th.
Trustee Meuer asked that it be tabled to September 15 giving any other trustee
an opportunity to look at the house.
Trustee Meuer moved to table this agenda item until September 15 and Trustee
Lien seconded. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer.
Nayes: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-537-98 – IMEA Membership for Allendale, Fairfield and
McLeansboro, IL – Introduction. President Holland explained that the By-Laws of
the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency require that a majority of its members
each pass an ordinance approving the addition of new communities to the IMEA.
The Village of Allendale, the City of Fairfield and the City of McLeansboro
have applied for membership and the IMEA is requesting that the existing
members pass the requirement ordinance so that membership can be granted.. She
noted that approval of this ordinance would have no impact on the Village of
Winnetka or on its relationship with the IMEA.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Holland asked if all of these towns produce electricity and Village
Manager Williams said that they purchase power now. He said they are downstate
communities and tend to be small in size.
Resolution No. R-1433-98 – Sale of Property to Winnetka Galleria, 562-572
Lincoln Avenue – Adoption. Mr. Williams explained that in the course of
obtaining title information in preparation for closing on the 562-572 Lincoln
Avenue property, it was discovered that the 10-foot deep landscaped area that
lies between the existing building and fence along the public sidewalk behind
the property actually belongs to the Village rather than to the owners of the
562-572 Lincoln Avenue. The purchasers, Cummins Winnetka Galleria LLC,
approached the Village and offered to purchase the property.
Attorney Janega explained that because of its location and the area involved,
the only viable private use of the property is as a part of the 562-572 Lincoln
Avenue development and reviewed the process for determining the property’s
value. The proposed Resolution authorizes the sale of the subject property
subject to the reservation of utility easements and the consolidation and
development of the subject property with the principal parcel at 562-572
Lincoln Avenue.
Trustee Duhl asked if the buyer had asked for a reduction in price from the
original seller and if the Village was selling it for more. Village Attorney
Janega said that the buyer was paying more for the land. She said the appraisal
for this land is part of the agenda packet and the appraiser reviewed the
purchase of the property for Winnetka Galleria, the contract price, and the
declarations and made adjustments accordingly. Trustee Duhl said that it wasn’t
clear to him that the buyer did get a rebate from the seller.
Trustee Meuer asked if we knew the amount of legal fees. Ms. Janega replied
that the recording fee will be paid by the buyer and the Village will prepare
the deed. The appraisal fee was paid by the Village, but they can have the
developer pay that. She said that the amount of the sale price was more than
double what was anticipated so she did not push the appraisal fee to be paid by
the buyer.
Trustee Lien moved to adopt the resolution permitting the sale of the property.
Trustee Lanphier seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Derning, and Meuer.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Village Manager Williams complemented the Village attorney in the handling of
this matter.
Ordinance No. 521-98 – Zoning Variation: 1177 Ash Street – Adoption. Ms.
McCammack said this request is to approve a zoning variation requesting the
division of an existing 100 foot wide single lot into two 50 foot wide new lots
of record. She noted that both the Plan Commission and the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted in favor of permitting the reduced lot widths.
Cindy McCammack said that this request for the variation and subdivision has
been before the Plan Commission twice and because of the zoning variation
required, staff brought it to the Council on May 5 at which time the zoning
variation was introduced. She said they went back to the Plan Commission with
the subdivision which was approved.
Trustee Duhl asked Ms. McCammack if she was in favor of this as a village
planner.
Ms. McCammack replied that she is, given the size of a house that could be
built on a 100 foot lot.
Village Manager Williams said that this was two 50 ft lots that were previously
joined and now that they are subdivided the properties will fit into the
neighborhood better.
Trustee Lien said that a two-lot subdivision bothers him and also that joining
two lots bothers him.
Village Attorney Janega said that in this instance, the lots in this area were
all platted at 50 feet but were shifted over 10 feet so that the Radcliffes
owned parts of three lots, of 40 feet, 10 feet and 50 feet, or 100 feet total.
She said there are other places in the Village where the Village has allowed
property to revert to the 50 foot lot lines, but there was no previously
platted lot line here at the 50-foot mark. The shifting of the lot lines was
not something that was under the residents control.
Trustee Lien said that he understands from this that they were then caught in a
technicality.
Trustee Lanphier said she thinks that the two 50 foot lots is in keeping with
the rhythm of the street.
Trustee Lien said that all of these things are to be considered but he would be
against two 50 foot lots elsewhere.
Trustee Meuer said that this property is in a floodplain and the Village is
going to have to monitor what is built on that lot because of the water. He
asked that Ms. McCammack watch for this.
Ms. McCammack said that she will bring it to the attention of the engineers.
President Holland asked if anyone was in the audience to address this.
Mr. and Mrs. Radcliffe, 1177 Ash, said that there is a covenant on this
property because of the water drainage. Mr. Radcliffe said he understands that
Mr. Meuer put a drain in his yard and the calculations for the Radcliffe’s
property are to put in significant storage for water.
President Holland said that there is an ordinance that requires compensatory
storage so that none of the water from their property can go onto anyone
else’s.
Mrs. Radlciffe said that the water is actually coming from the Skokie Lagoon.
Trustee Meuer said that once they begin building on the property, he is
concerned about the water, especially with the construction on Willow Road. He
said that he had been asked to express these concerns by his neighbors.
Mrs. Radcliffe said that this is why the Village engineer put the covenants on
the plat.
President Holland asked for any other questions. There were none.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt the ordinance. Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution R-1428-098 Approving Plat of Subdivision for 1177 Ash - Adoption.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the resolution, seconded by Trustee Derning. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Cherry Street Alley Improvements. At 8:25 p.m. President Holland convened the
Council as the Board of Local Improvements for the purpose of adopting
resolution SA-2-98 in connection with the proposed improvements to the public
alley between Ash and Cherry Streets, from Locust to Rosewood.
The Trustees reconvened into Regular Session at 8:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer. Absent: None. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega and Community
Development Director Cindy McCammack.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1091 and 1092. Trustee Lien reported that he had talked to
the finance director about the Simpson Construction Company payment of
$205,900, noting that this is the final payment for the Public Safety Building.
The remaining dollars in the account will be for lawn sprinklers and fire
training towers. Trustee Lien also had questioned the item on Hometown Electric
and reimbursement for the Elm Street project and all of his questions had been
answered satisfactorily. Trustee Meuer moved to approve Warrant List No. 1091
in the total amount of $683,576.71 and Warrant List No. 1092 in the total
amount of $728,961.88. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call vote
the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Hilton, Lien, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance M-538-98 - Harris Bank Lease - Introduction. Mr. Williams explained
that Harris Bank-Winnetka has been leasing a portion of the Village Hall site
since 1968 in order to facilitate access to its drive-through facility from the
public alley west of Village Hall. He noted that the most recent lease had
expired on July 31, 1998, and that a renewal of the lease must be authorized by
ordinance. The proposed ordinance provides for the payment of $2,500 in ten
equal annual installments for lease of the property from August 1, 1998,
through July 31, 2008.  
Trustee Lanphier asked if the Village requires any proof of insurance.
Village Attorney Janega said that there has been none required in past years
and that from a statutory standpoint, the Village is released from liability.
Trustee Meuer said that he noted a correction to be made on page 6 of the
ordinance: $250 should be changed to $2500.
Trustee Lien said that he looked at the plat and had determined the property
was about 300 square feet and at $65.00 a square foot, he came up with
approximately $19,000 for this property. He said that the Village hasn’t raised
the rent in 10 years and the bank should be paying us something for the use of
the property. He said that he thinks that the Village should apply the same
methods with this property that were used for the sale of property to the
Winnetka Galleria.
Village Manager Williams said that he will get more information and come back
in two weeks to report to the Council.
Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce the ordinance to approve the lease of
Village property to Harris Bank-Winnetka. Trustee Derning seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
Village President.
President Holland announced a meeting September 2, 1998 at 10:00 a.m. at the
Village Hall with Jeff Schoenberg to preserve and maintain land for
agricultural activity and protecting private property rights.
There will be an event at the Village Green sponsored by many Winnetka groups
on September 26 from 1-5pm. All organizations will have tables and discussion
about youth and family awareness.
Village Manager.
Insurance Services Office Report. Manager Williams apprised the Trustees of the
new ISO report reflecting a Building Code Effectiveness Grading Classification
of 4 for one and two family residential property and 4 for commercial and
industrial property. The scale is 1 to 10, with 1 representing exemplary
enforcement. He reported that Glencoe, Northfield, and Glenview have not yet
received their ISO rating and that Wilmette received a 5.
Manager Williams also reported that he was asked about the concrete across from
the Public Safety Building that was to be removed and it is gone as of today.
The trees on Willow Road will be replaced, as will the poles.
President Holland said that she has noticed a collection of snow and salt on
the trees at Green Bay Road and they are also dying like the trees on Willow
Road. She asked that the Village try to find other places to pile the snow.
Manager Williams said that places to pile snow in the winter are getting fewer
and that this may be something the Council could talk about it in study
session.
Village Facilities Tour. Manager Williams reminded the Trustees about the tour
of Village facilities scheduled for September 26th (Saturday) from 8:30 a.m. to
noon.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 8:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:10 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:11 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
September 8, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 8, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien and William
Meuer. Absent: Trustee David Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee,
and about fifteen persons in the audience.
II. Church Street Sidewalk Review.
Mr. Saunders reviewed a survey performed by Village staff where the current
Comprehensive Plan recommends sidewalks, but where no sidewalks presently
exist. He identified the “top five” areas to be considered, first among them
being the east side of Church Street from Winnetka Avenue to Green Bay Road,
and explained that these locations had been identified and ranked according to
a number of factors including proximity to parks, schools, traffic, street
classification, etc. He informed the Trustees, however, that the responses
received from affected residents to notification of the proposed project were
overwhelmingly opposed to the proposed improvement. He referred the Trustees to
a petition submitted by a group of neighbors.
Mr. Saunders also explained the two primary reasons for having a sidewalk: (1)
to ensure pedestrian safety, and (2) to encourage neighborhood activities. He
commented that while a sidewalk on the east side of Church Street would benefit
some pedestrians, he could see no major safety drawbacks should the project not
proceed. He also mentioned that although the lack of a sidewalk somewhat
isolates the homes on the east side of the street, based upon responses
received from residents, it appears that other considerations, such a property
appearance, are more important to the homeowners.
   President Holland opened the floor for comments from interested parties.
Numerous residents spoke against the proposed sidewalk, including Church Road
residents Neal Fulk, Bill Stevens, Jack Spears, Ed Hermann, Bill and Mary
Goodrich, Patty Smart, Jim Stavros, and Monica Lynch Dutmers. Among their
arguments against the proposed sidewalk, they cited potential increased safety
hazard from numerous alleys, disruption to property and character of the
neighborhood, and lack of sufficient evidence demonstrating need. They urged
the Council not to authorize this project.
   Bonnie Phemister, Steve Kindra, Eric Mogentale, and Maria Kernahan, all
residents of Hill Terrace, argued in favor of the proposed sidewalk, citing
safety, treacherous walking surfaces, and awkward street configurations.
   Following the residents’ comments, Trustee Lanphier, noting that she had
recently moved from Hill Terrace, confirmed the volume of pedestrian traffic
through that neighborhood, saying that the specific area of concern is very
small, but that the duration or the problem extends throughout the course of
the day, not just for brief, specific times.
   Trustee Derning comments that there is no way that the Council can guarantee
that there will absolutely not be an accident in this area, regardless of what
action is or is not undertaken.
   In response to residents’ comments about traffic failing to stop at the
already established stop signs, Manager Williams indicated that the Village
takes traffic enforcement very seriously and he would bring these comments to
the Police Chief’s attention. He pointed out, however, that there has been a
significant increase in recent months in the number of moving violation
citations issued throughout the Village.
   Mr. Saunders responded to some of the concerns raised by neighbors. He
pointed out that in all likelihood only one tree would need to be removed since
the sidewalk could be curved and indicated that the number of people who
ultimately use a sidewalk is not necessarily the overriding factor in planning
one.
   Trustee Derning asked Mr. Saunders to clarify the size of the Village’s
right of way and the average front setback of the affected homes.
   Trustee Duhl expressed his opinion that since there appears to be no
overwhelming argument in favor of the proposed sidewalk, the neighbors ought to
have some say in whether or not one is installed. He said that he would like to
see the Village look for other solutions to the problem first.
   Trustee Lanphier reiterated her belief that there is a significant safety
concern but that it affects only a very short area.
   In stating his position, Trustee Meuer pointed out that the Trustees have a
responsibility to the Village as a whole and that just because one neighborhood
is opposed to some action, the Trustees cannot necessarily support that
position. He asked for further information in order to be better able to
address the street design and safety issues involved.
   President Holland announced that there was a consensus among the Trustees to
look into the problem further before making any decision. She indicated that
residents of both Church Road and Hill Terrace would be notified when the item
returned to Council’s Agenda.
III. Fisher Lane Sanitary Sewer.
Mr. Saunders explained the status of the sewer line that serves 970, 1060 and
1065 Fisher Lane and which has been the cause of some recent problems at 919
Fisher Lane. He noted that at the present time the line is under private
jurisdiction, but that the Metropolitan Water Reclamation District of Greater
Chicago (MWRDGC) does not permit private sewer lines that serve more than one
building. Therefore, this line must become a public sewer under the Village’s
jurisdiction. He then reviewed options for accomplishing this transfer of
jurisdiction and outlined recommended steps.
   Trustee Duhl asked for an explanation as to why this was the Village’s
problem to solve and whether or not the homeowners would be required to pay for
the project.
   Manager Williams explained that it is in everyone’s best interest to have
this line be the Village’s public sewer and that the homeowners would
participate in paying for the work.
   There was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed with the project as
outlined.
IV. Compost for Winnetkans Only.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village currently provides compost free of
charge at a pickup area located at the Village Yards. Although there is a sign
stating that the compost is for Winnetka residents only, weekend and evening
enforcement is non-existent. He explained that if the Village wished to
restrict bulk pickup to residents only, the only way to prohibit non-residents
from collecting it would be to police the pickup area. This could only be
effectively accomplished by moving the location of the pickup area inside the
fence, thus restricting the times for pickup to the operating hours of the
Village Yards. He noted that such a move would likely be unpopular with
residents who wish to have compost available when they are home, namely
evenings and weekends, and that keeping the Village Yard open for a period of
time on weekends in order to monitor the compost pile inside the fence would
require paying employee overtime.
   Trustee Lien noted that he had witnessed contractors loading their trucks
with the compost and expressed concern about giving a valuable Village resource
away without any control.
   Trustee Duhl summarized the available options before the Trustees agreed to
move the compost pile behind the Village Yard fence, without keeping the Yards
open for hours on the weekends, beginning October 1, 1998.
V. Collection Policies.
Mr. McKee reviewed the Village’s collection procedures and outlined recommended
changes, as noted in the Agenda materials, including the implementation of a
consistent approach to billing, modifying the Village Code to require payment
of outstanding balances prior to receiving non-emergency services, adjusting
utility service procedures, and strengthening parking ticket enforcement
procedures.
   The Trustees expressed their support for all of Mr. McKee’s suggestions. Mr.
Williams indicated that there would be an ordinance implementing the changes on
Council’s Agenda in the near future.
V. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of the
possible sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(6) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Duhl seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier,
Lien and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:35 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Hilton.
Also present: Village Manager Williams.
VI. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:37 p.m.
September 15, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved October 6, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 15, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and Bill Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about twelve persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
October 6, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees, except Trustee Duhl,
indicated that they would be able to attend.
October 13, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees, except Trustees Duhl and
Lien, indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
September 1, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
September 8, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Meuer,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Mr. Sturgis Chadwick, 1324 Asbury, requested that the Council reconsider its
decision to offer the remaining 24 feet of right-of-way of Randolph Steet to
his neighbors, Mr. and Mrs. Richard Busscher. He recited for the Council the
history of the property and raised several issues, saying that he objected
strongly to the sale of the property to Mr. Busscher.
Tom Mather, 115 Bertling Ln., requested that the Council removed the barricades
at the intersection of Winnetka Avenue and Bertling Ln. His request was
supported by his daughter, Mary, who expressed concern about the barricades
impeding emergency access to the street. She also noted that because of the
barricades, large vehicles are required to back down the street in order to
exit.
President Holland announced that this topic would be on Council’s Agenda for
the October 20, 1998.
Jim Kruempelstaedter, 178 Myrtle, also voiced his objections to the closure of
Bertling Lane and the “short path,” and enumerated his reasons therefor. He
said that he believes the closure is unfair to the majority of Winnetka
taxpayers because it accommodates only a few people to the detriment of many.
He urged the Council to find other solutions to the traffic problems.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Kruempelstaedter whether there had been an increse
in pedestrian and automobile traffic on Myrtle since Bertling Lane had been
blocked off. Mr. Kruempelstaedter replied in the affirmative.
Mary Lawlor, 471 Hill Road, pointed out how busy the area is on weekends,
especially when there is a special event at Indian Hill Park or activities
scheduled at New Trier High School. She encouraged the Council to urge stricter
enforcement of the existing traffic laws in an effort to resolve the problems
without closing off Bertling Lane.
President Holland reiterated that this topic would be discussed at Council’s
October 20, 1998, regular meeting.
Roger Knight, 163 Bertling Lane, voiced his belief that the barricades had
resulted in a significantly safer neighborhood and thanked the Village for
closing off the street. He said that there is less traffic, fewer young
drivers, and fewer problems with speeding. He asked that the Council maintain
the changes.
Elaine Kruempelstaedter, 178 Myrtle, suggested that perhaps the barricades
could be made temporary and placed only before and after school, leaving the
street open for the remainder of the day.
Ronnie Tesar, 146 Bertling, said that she did not think that Mrs.
Kruemplestaedter’s suggestion would work because New Trier is an open campus
and students come and leave throughout the day. She pointed out that thirteen
of the fifteen families living on Bertling Lane are in favor of the barricades
and expressed her appreciation to the Village for installing them.
Terry Duffy, 159 Bertling, reiterated earlier comments in favor of the
barricades. He said that is has made it a much nicer and safer street for the
residents and indicated that he believed that motorists were beginning to learn
to reroute so as not to impose so heavily on neighboring residential streets.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-533-98 – Zoning Variation: 821 Foxdale – Adoption. Trustee Lien
moved to adopt the ordinance permitting a reduced side yard setback. He was
seconded by Trustee Hilton. Trustee Hilton confirmed with Ms. McCammack that
the building would retain its current footprint and setback. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier,
Meuer and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-531-98 – Zoning Variances: 466 Poplar – Adoption. Trustee
Lanphier moved to adopt the ordinance permitting an increase in permitted
building size. He was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton and Derning. Nays: Trustees
Duhl and Lien. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-537-98 – IMEA Membership for Allendale, Fairfield and
McLeansboro, IL – Adoption. Trustee Meuer moved to adopt the ordinance
approving the admittance of the three communities into IMEA. He was seconded by
Trustee Lanphier. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Ordinance No. M-539-98 – Zoning Variation: 159 Sheridan Road – Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the request for a variation permitting a reduced front
yard (through lot) setback. The applicants are requesting the variation to
allow for the construction of an accessory building, deck and boat ramp within
a required front yard. She explained that because the lot is situated on Lake
Michigan with Sheridan Road as its more or less parallel, opposite street line
that the Zoning Ordinance requires there to be a front yard provided from both
Sheridan Road and Lake Michigan. Ms. McCammack noted that the Zoning Board of
Appeals voted 6-0 to recommend that the Council grant this request.
Trustee Derning pointed out that this request was contingent upon the granting
of a number of permits from other agencies and asked what effect this might
have. Attorney Janega replied that it might be necessary to readdress the time
limits noted in proposed ordinance, extending them to 24 months, instead 12,
because of the potential for a lengthy permitting process.
Ms. McCammack described the proposed boathouse, noting that the plan was to
construct it into the face of the bluff, rather than have it sit atop the
bluff, where it would be a permitted use, but where it would impede more light
and air.
David Grossberg, attorney for the applicants, explained that in its proposed
location the boathouse is less obvious and presents fewer safety problems.
Trustee Lanphier noted that riparian properties had been discussed at length
during the review of the zoning ordinance and that this seemed to be in keeping
with the intent of those discussions.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-539-98. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-540-98 – Zoning Variation: 656 Ardsley Road – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed the request to permit structural alterations to window
openings in a legal nonconforming room addition located within the required
front yard setbacks. She pointed out that Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-
0 to recommend approval of the request for the Village Council.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-540-98. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Ordinance No. M-541-98 - Cherry Street Alley Improvements – Introduction.
President Holland read the first paragraph of the proposed ordinance and
explained that the ordinance establishes a special assessment district for the
purpose of providing funding for the proposed improvements to the public alley
between Ash and Cherry Streets, from Locust to Rosewood. She noted that the
ordinance is in the form recommended by the Board of Local Improvements and
incorporates the detailed specifications for the proposed improvements, giving
a detailed estimate of costs and defining the special assessment district.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
541-98. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1093 and 1094. Trustee Lien reported that all of his
questions had been answered satisfactorily and moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1093 in the total amount of $167,876.43 and Warrant List No. 1094 in the
total amount of $222,007.88. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Lien, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
DK Recycling Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to the most recent
report from DK Recycling. She pointed out that there had been only one odor
complaint and mentioned that the Illinois EPA had found no violations and that
the site had received its annual compliance letter.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland reminded the Trustees about the “Forward
Youth” exposition, presented by the Interfaith Council of Winnetka, to be hald
on the Village Green on September 26, 1998.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning commented that the Chamber of Commerce
remained interested in the progress on revising the Sign Ordinance.
Village Manager.
Village Facilities Tour.  Manager Williams reminded the Trustees about the tour
of Village facilities scheduled for September 26th (Saturday) from 8:30 a.m. to
noon.
also tour water and power plants.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 8:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:35 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:36 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
October 6, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved October 20, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 6, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m.
Present: Trustees Don Derning, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and
Bill Meuer. Absent: Trustee Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, and about 13 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
October 13, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Lien indicated he would not be present
as well as Trustee Duhl.
October 20, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
September 15, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton requested that the times
that the regular meeting reconvened after executive session and for adjournment
be inserted in the minutes. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to
adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-539-98 – Zoning Variation: 159 Sheridan Road – Adoption.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt this ordinance permitting the construction of
an accessory building, deck and boat ramp within the east 50 foot required
front yard setback. As discussed at the September 15, 1998 meeting the
ordinance would be in effect for 24 months to allow time for State and Federal
permitting authorities to review the request. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Meuer.
By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Hilton,
Meuer, Lanphier, Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-540-98 – Zoning Variances: 656 Ardsley Road – Adoption. The
motion to adopt this ordinance permitting structural alterations to a legal
nonconforming room addition was made by Trustee Hilton and seconded by Trustee
Lien.
By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Hilton,
Meuer, Lanphier, Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-538-98 – Harris Bank Lease - Adoption. Manager Williams
reported that in response to Council’s discussions at the September 1, 1998,
Regular Meeting, the Village had negotiated a new lease with Harris Bank -
Winnetka establishing a lease rate of $1,000 per year for a period of ten
years. The lease would permit Harris Bank to continue to use a 375 sq. ft.
portion of the Village site to facilitate access to its drive-through facility
from the public alley west of Village Hall. Manager Williams stated that he
would recommend that the lease amount be amended to $1,000.00.
Trustee Derning moved to amend the proposed ordinance to reflect the new lease
rate of $1,000 per year. He was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Meuer then moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended. His motion was
seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-542-98 – Zoning Variation: 512 Orchard - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack explained this request to permit an increase in accessory building
height, building size (GFA) and roofed and total impermeable lot coverage. The
applicants wish to construct a two-car garage in the rear quarter of the lot to
replace an existing garage erected in 1979. The proposed new garage would
contribute additional footage to the GFA and roofed and total impermeable lot
coverage. The proposed height of the garage also exceeds the permitted height
for an accessory building. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had made
two separate motions for these variations requests. They had voted 4-2 to
recommend granting the variations for building height and building size, and 6-
0 to grant the variations for roofed and total impermeable surfaces coverage.
Trustee Lanphier asked if the applicant was in attendance.
Mrs. Wyman replied that she was.
Ms. McCammack said that this proposal is for a replacement of a garage and the
existing garage exceeds the maximum permitted gross floor area. The proposed
garage will contribute 141 additional square feet, so that the main floor of
the garage is 462 square feet. Ms. McCammack also said the height of the new
garage is 14.25 feet instead of the 14 feet permitted. Lot coverage with the
addition is 12.8 square feet for both the roof and impermeable surfaces. At the
Zoning Board of Appeals hearing on August 17, the members were not concerned
with the 12 square feet of lot coverage, but did have difficulty with the
height and GFA, so they split the motions approving lot coverage unanimously
and voting 4-2 in favor of granting variations.
Mrs. Janet Wyman, 512 Orchard said that the reason for changing the height is
to match the architecture of the home and that she understands that the
footprint is larger by 12 square feet. Mrs. Wyman said that there are two
garage doors proposed instead of one garage door and this makes the garage a
little wider.
President Holland asked Mrs. Wyman how the extra garage space would be used.
Mrs. Wyman replied that it would be for storage.
Trustee Derning asked about the impermeable surface and Mrs. Wyman explained
that the proposed changes would be removing a lot of impermeable surface that
is there currently.
Trustee Lanphier asked Mrs. Wyman if they had considered something that was
conforming.
Mrs. Wyman said that the high pitched roof was more in keeping with the style
of the home and so instead of flattening the roof to conform, they thought this
would be more in character with home.
Trustee Lanphier then asked if they had considered hip roofs.
Mrs. Wyman said that to keep in character with their home they need a steeper
pitch to the roof.
Trustee Lanphier stated that the design is very attractive.
Trustee Derning clarified that the height is actually three inches higher.
Trustee Lien said that only six inches were added to the width, but that makes
the proposed garage actually 12 square feet larger.
Trustee Meuer asked if one garage door could be used.
Ms. McCammack asked if Mrs. Wyman had considered lowering the height of the
walls.
Mrs. Wyman said that this would not accommodate one of their cars but they
could look into that.
Trustee Lanphier suggested that if the whole roof form was brought down to a
lower level that it would save square footage.
Mrs. Wyman said that she really didn’t think that their car would fit and since
cars are bigger now, she thinks the extra square footage is necessary.
Trustee Lanphier said she just wondered if there wasn’t a conforming option.
Mrs. Wyman said that the only thing would be to soften the pitch of the roof.
President Holland said there had been a garage at 560 Elm that required a
variation but by bringing down the ceiling very slightly, the attic space
didn’t count at all.
Mrs. Wyman said that she would look into bringing it down one foot and asked
what steps she would need to take next for Council approval.
Ms. McCammack told her that she would need to submit a revised plan.
President Holland told Mrs. Wyman that it would be on the agenda again in two
weeks when Mrs. Wyman expressed concern about the scheduled building of the
garage.
Mrs. Wyman said that she was familiar with the garage on Elm and the resident
had raised the ceiling, but she didn’t think that would work in this case. She
said she would consult her architect before the next meeting.
Trustee Hilton moved to introduce the proposed ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Lanphier. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-543-98 – Zoning Variation: 1175 Whitebridge Hill Road -
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed this request to make structural
alterations to the garage roof located within the required front yard, thereby
reducing the front (corner) yard setback. She noted that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend granting this application.
President Holland asked for any discussion. There was none.
Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce the proposed ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Lien. By voice vote, the motion to introduce carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-544-98 – Zoning Variation: 788 Cherry Street – Introduction.
Ms. McCammack explained this request to permit an increase in roofed lot
coverage, and a reduction in the rear and side yard. The variation is requested
in order to permit the construction of a new covered stoop and an addition to
widen the existing garage. She noted that the existing roofed lot coverage
already exceeds the amount permitted and that the proposed new covered stoop
would increase that nonconformity. She pointed out that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend approval of this variation.
Trustee Lanphier questioned whether the applicants had considered any
conforming plans for constructing a detached garage.
Trustee Hilton made the motion to introduce, seconded by Trustee Derning. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1440-98 – Peters Subdivision: Green Bay Court Townhouses, 778-
788 Green Bay Road – Adoption. Ms. McCammack explained that this subdivision
was proposed in order to carry out the proposed division and individual sale of
what are currently six rental apartments. The proposal involves the division of
a single zoning lot into seven separate lots, six “dwelling lots,” and the
seventh dedicated for purposes of ingress, egress and utilities. She indicated
that townhouse association declarations had been submitted and reviewed to
assure that sufficient provisions are included to address the Village’s
concerns pertaining to operation, maintenance, easements and potential
redevelopment of the property.
Mildred Peters introduced herself as the representative of the owner. She
stated that there has been several revisions of the declarations and to the
plat of subdivision that had been reviewed by the staff and the Village
attorney and she will answer any questions from the Council.
President Holland said that she believes the Council had looked at this
proposal before and asked if there was a motion to introduce the Resolution.
The motion to adopt this Resolution was made by Trustee Lanphier, and seconded
by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien. Nayes: None. Absent: Trustee
Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-545-98 – Special Use Permit: SprintCom, 911 Green Bay Road –
Introduction. Ms. Janega stated that this is the first of two applications to
install wireless telecommunication antennas pursuant to Ordinance No. MC-195-
97. SprintCom, Inc. has entered into a lease agreement with the property owners
at 911 Green Bay Road to permit installation and maintenance of the proposed
equipment on top of the penthouse. Under the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, WTSF
are permitted as accessory special uses in the C-2 (General Retail) Commercial
Zoning District. She pointed out that the proposed roof location is a fifth
priority location under the ordinance, which provides that no WTSF will be
permitted at any site unless all sites having a higher location priority have
been fully utilized or the applicant submits engineering data to establish that
the use of the higher priority location is not technically feasible. In
addition, the applicant must also meet the standards for the granting of a
special use.
In addition to permitting the WTSF, SprintCom is also seeking several
variations. They are requesting a height variation from the 35 foot permitted
height to 56.58 feet. In addition, the proposed location is located within
39.94 feet of a nonconforming single family residence and 230 feet from a
second residence, thereby requiring a variation from the 250 foot minimum
distance requirement. Following these findings, the Plan Commission also
considered SprintCom’s request and voted 6-1, including one abstention, to find
that the request was consistent with the Village’s Comprehensive Plan.
Ms. Janega reported that the Village had retained Dr. Allen Taflove of
Northwestern University to perform a third party, independent review of the
application as mandated by the ordinance. She indicated that Dr. Taflove
recommended that that his preliminary conclusions be confirmed by actual field
testing since SprintCom’s data had relied solely on propagation studies.
Following this recommendation, the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-0 to
recommend granting of the special use permit and zoning variations, subject to
the conditions that the applicant conduct tests under Dr. Taflove’s supervision
and that the test finding support SprintCom’s projected data. Specifically,
SprintCom was to test to determine whether wall-mounted antennas could be used
instead of the requested roof-mounted antennas and whether or not a higher
priority site, as set out in the WTSF ordinance, could achieve the same
coverage and/or serve to consolidate a proposed Northfield site and the
proposed Winnetka site into a single location. Ms. Janega said that the goal
was to get as much seamless coverage as possible. The testing validated the
projections that are shown on the exhibits in the materials. The signal
strength improved dramatically with the roof-mounted antennas. She indicated
that Dr. Taflove’s final report had concluded that the roof-mounted antennas
are necessary and that the higher priority sites would not provide the coverage
to the Hubbard Woods area that SprintCom was attempting to achieve. The key to
the testing was that the roof-mounted antennas would be above the tree line.
The second site tested was at the landfill. Topographical maps were used to
determined how high they could get with the antenna and the test site was
actually higher than the priority location, but the signal strength dropped off
and showed that the site could not be used. Ms. Janega said that there was no
map of this particular test, but Dr. Taflove reported that the accuracy of
SprintCom’s data in their projections factored in the drop off from tree
interference so that the results from test runs were virtually the same as the
projections. SprintCom was also concerned that a site at this location would
interfere with another site location in Northfield. Based on the testing at the
landfill, Dr. Taflove felt no further testing was necessary. In addition to
testing done by SprintCom, the Village received test results from AT & T for
the Hubbard Woods site. AT & T submitted propagation maps and did not test at
the landfill, but did test at Hubbard Woods. Dr. Taflove concluded that AT & T
did not have to do further testing at the landfill, and that SprintCom’s was
sufficient. Ms. Janega said that the report submitted by Dr. Taflove determined
that the locations proposed were appropriate and warranted by engineering
necessity.
Trustee Hilton asked about the height variance and if it wasn’t higher than
allowed.
Ms. Janega replied that it is higher than the maximum building height in that
it exceeds the parapet of the building but it is necessary to clear the tree
line.
Trustee Lanphier commented that it had not been discussed as to why the wall-
mounted antennas wouldn’t work.
Ms. Janega said that she would let SprintCom talk to that. The reason why the
Zoning Board of Appeals did not push to have two locations was that the cost of
two sites to get signal coverage is $750,000 per site. The fact would remain
that the coverage from the landfill would not get to the Hubbard Woods area and
so the signal strength coming from wall-mounted antennas on the penthouse would
still not be sufficient to cover Hubbard Woods because of the heavy tree
impact. Ms. Janega said that she could ask Dr. Taflove to address the Council
to discuss this.
Trustee Lanphier said that she doesn’t understand why the wall-mounted antennas
won’t work and asked if the technology has changed since the Council discussed
this a year ago.
Ms. Janega replied that when the ordinance was originally created, there was no
testing done as to whether sites were viable from an engineering standpoint
because it was cost prohibitive for the Village. Ted Kreines was looking for
likely places for the types of antennas available but no testing was done as to
whether a given antenna would work at a particular site. Dr. Taflove has
underscored the impact of trees on signal strength. Ms. Janega said that
because everything works on line of sight, the signal going through trees drops
off in strength.
Trustee Lanphier said that she wasn’t persuaded, after reading the material,
that this is the only choice and she would like to have more information.
Trustee Lanphier asked if it was feasible to have wall mounted antennas
installed for a cost of $750,000 each. She would like more information before
she makes a decision.
Manager Williams clarified that the applicants did not retain Dr Taflove, but
that the Village had consulted him pursuant to the ordinance requirements.
Mr. Richard Nikchevich, 333 W. Wacker, attorney for SprintCom said he would
answer the question about the height of the antenna. He said that the cost is a
factor, but not the predominant one. The network operates at the same power
level, but the height of the antenna provides a broader ability to serve a
larger geographic area. Height is impacted also by the tree line. He said that
Dr. Taflove pointed out in his report, that they are not above the roof with a
roof mounted antenna, it is that they are going on a trajectory through the
trees and the trees absorb the radio waves. As a general principal, height has
a direct correlation with the cell area served, and also the tree line issue,
as Dr. Taflove’s report states, is greatly ameliorated by the increase in
height. Multiple sites are a factor, but cost is not the predominant factor. If
there are two radio stations at the same frequency that are close to each
other, there will be interference. The same phenomenon occurs with the cell
signal.
Trustee Lanphier said that therefore none of the towers can overlap each other.
Mr. Nikchevich said that locations cannot be too close to one another because
of signal interference but there can be slight variances due to site
availability. It is basically like a grid in that they overlap a little so that
when a person goes from one site to another there is no interruption in signal,
but if they are too close together, there will be interference. He said that in
earlier testimony at the zoning board meeting with one of Winnetka’s priority
sites there would be that problem, and that is the site at Northfield. Mr.
Nikchevich said that the one thing about 911 Green Bay is that it is one of the
tallest buildings in the Hubbard Woods area, backs up to the railroad tracks
and would serve the greatest area.
Trustee Lanphier said she would like to see what the antennas would look like.
Mr. Nikchevich said there is a depiction of the antenna on the last page of the
exhibit.
Trustee Lanphier replied that this is technical information and she would like
a photograph.
Mr. Nikchevich showed the Council a photograph that had been provided to the
Zoning Board of Appeals.
Trustee Lanphier said that the Council has been going through great pains to
eliminate clutter and thinks there may be another option than the roof-mounted
antennas.
President Holland asked about the equipment room and what is presently there in
the building.
One of AT & T Wireless’ engineers replied that currently it is a storage room.
President Holland also asked if the air conditioner was overhanging the
driveway.
The engineer answered that it overhangs a roof.
President Holland asked if that was shown on the west elevation on sheet A-3.
The engineer replied that this was correct.
President Holland noted that it overhangs the roof below. She said that she
assumes that the storage room floor is secure enough to hold the equipment
which she has heard can sometimes weigh a ton.
The engineer said that right now there are auto body parts there and he has
full documentation and calculations from engineers that it will support the
equipment.
Trustee Lanphier said that the site is in a very visible spot and asked if
there were any other options available.
Mr. Nikchevich said that the visual is misleading because it shows one of four
angles. He said that the antenna will not be seen from three of the angles. He
pointed out that there are also overhead power lines and other things that
visually clutter the area.
Trustee Lanphier replied that the Village is actively pursuing putting power
lines underground and wants to reduce clutter.
President Holland said this was especially so in the Hubbard Woods area.
Trustee Lanphier repeated that she is bothered by SprintCom saying that this is
the only option available and wonders if they can work with the Village to find
another location and reduce the visibility.
Mr. Nikchevich said that what gets lost sometimes is that a lot of work has
already gone on to select a site and SprintCom has worked with the Village
staff who have been very thorough. They feel they have worked carefully to
minimize the visually impact. He said that he cannot say there is no impact,
but it is very minimal. He pointed out that the monopole on the Public Safety
Building is much more visible so, in terms of visibility, the site at 911 Green
Bay Road is much better. He said SprintCom has tried to work within the
parameters of the ordinance and they are in the most priority site they can
use. The Village’s expert has said that this is the only way to get the desired
signal strength and the height is needed to get the signal coverage.
President Holland said that she also has a problem with the look of the antenna
and asked if it could be put on another side of the building to minimize the
visual impact.
Mr. Nickchevich said that the antenna will be painted an overcast gray color
and is designed to make it blend into the background as much as possible.
Trustee Lanphier again asked Mr. Nikchevich if this was the only site and the
only option they can use.
Trustee Lien said that his recollection when the ordinance was created, was
that they had been under the impression that wall mounted antennas would work
and he also said that he understands that the expert said that this would not
work. He asked Mr. Nikchevich if a monopole would work and if so, where would
they put it. He asked what would be the optimum site in the Village for the
most coverage. Mr. Nikchevich said that it would be adjacent to 911 Green Bay
Road.
Trustee Lien clarified that this proposed site would then be the optimum site
for a monopole.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Nikchevich if they had considered concealment of any
kind.
Mr. Nikchevich said that they had considered a fiberglass cast around the
antenna but they felt that it would create a greater controversy and would
increase the visual mass of what is already a tall building for the vicinity.
His understanding of the opinions of the Zoning Board of Appeals was that
increasing visual mass was to be avoided.
Manager Williams asked if there are concealment strategies to make the
fiberglass look like clock towers, or something similar.
Mr. Nikchevich said that the form they had considered made it look even taller
because the wall height was taller.
Manager Williams asked if the antennas were 10 feet higher than the roof.
Mr. Nikchevich said they would be going to 57 feet instead of 50 feet.
President Holland then asked if there were four of them.
Mr. Nikchevich replied that there are three sets of three.
President Holland asked if there was further discussion.
Ms. Janega said that Dr. Taflove could be at the next meeting and questions
could be prepared.
Trustee Meuer said that height of this building is 50 feet and asked if there
are other buildings in that area that are the same height.
Dr. Aseed Shad, SprintCom’s engineer, said that 911 Green Bay is the tallest
building.
Trustee Meuer asked if you could accomplish the same thing with a higher
antenna on a lower building in that area.
Dr. Shah said that they could.
Mr. Nikchevich said that effectively that would be starting over and they have
a launch date of December 3 to turn on the service. It would be difficult to
begin again.
Trustee Lanphier commented that the priority scheme led them to that area.
President Holland asked for a motion to introduce the ordinance.
Trustee Hilton asked if they had addressed the proximity to residences.
Manager Williams said that staff had overlooked the one house existing there
when the ordinance was created, but the house is zoned commercial.
Trustee Meuer moved to introduce the ordinance granting the Special Use Permit
and zoning variations. The motion was seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-546-98 – Special Use Permit: AT&T Wireless, 874 Green Bay Road
– Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed this second request for a WTSF by AT&T
Wireless Services. She noted that this application also seeks several
variations, including height and reduced side yard and rear yard setbacks.
Again Dr. Taflove was asked to review this application and the Zoning Board of
Appeals ultimately voted 5-0 to recommend granting of the Special Use Permit
and zoning variations, subject to tests supporting AT&T’s projected data being
conducted under the supervision of Dr. Taflove.
She reported that following the required tests, Dr. Taflove’s final report
concluded that higher priority sites would not provide the coverage in the
Hubbard Woods area that AT&T was attempting to achieve. Subsequently, the Plan
Commission also considered the application and voted 6-1, including one
abstention, to find that the request is consistent with the Village’s
Comprehensive Plan.
Brian Barrett introduced himself as representing AT & T Wireless and also
introduced the engineer, Orlando Davilla. He said that there are some slight
differences in AT & T’s proposal from SprintCom’s. They have already been
providing service for 1½ years and their data is based on sites that are up and
running. He said that this is the reason Dr. Taflove was satisfied with their
material because it is based on real life locations. They are proposing nine
panel antennas to be installed on the roof which are 13 inches in height and
are the smallest antennas possible to use. He referred to the site plans
provided to the Council. The proposed site is located at Tower and Green Bay
Road, immediately north of the McDonalds, and is a three-story building with a
penthouse on the southwest corner. There has been discussion about wall-mounted
vs. roof mounted antennas. AT & T’s first proposal was submitted with antennas
mounted on the wall, but the building sits on the property lines so they were
forced to move the antennas to the roof. There are three sectors of antennas
and each sector is comprised of three panels. The first sector is roof mounted
on the east portion of the roof and will peek over the top of the wall. The
other two sectors are both located on the roof of the penthouse. Mr. Barrett
said AT & T could have settled with the wall mounted antennas, but the roof
mounted antennas enable them to get over the tree tops. He said the antennas
will be left in their natural color which is a light gray to minimize their
effect. The equipment will be housed on the third floor within the building.
The only exterior equipment will be an air conditioning unit and the antennas.
Trustee Lanphier asked then if they will all be wall mounted.
Mr. Barrett referred Council to the south elevation which shows the panels. He
said all antennas will be roof mounted.
Ms. Janega said that the drawings they have show wall mounted antennas.
Trustee Lanphier then clarified that the south elevation drawing is not
correct.
President Holland asked how high the antennas will extend.
Mr. Barrett said that the antenna panels themselves are only 13” in height and
the total overall height will be 50 ft.
Trustee Lanphier asked if they were using a different technology from
SprintCom.
Mr. Barrett said that AT & T is using TDMA technology instead of CDMA as
SprintCom is using.
Trustee Lanphier said that this is the type of information she would like to
get in order to understand.
Mr. Barrett said that they have a slightly different parameter than SprintCom
does because they already have sites in existence. Dr. Taflove concurred with
their explanation at the Zoning Board of Appeals meeting that in order for the
panels to work they must be in close proximity to each other.
Trustee Lanphier asked him to explain the maps that were provided in the
packet.
Orlando Davilla, 8700 W. Bryn Mawr, engineer with AT & T Wireless, showed the
Council the map of current coverage provided by AT & T and explained the
different areas of coverage. He then explained the current map of actual road
data from the existing sites. He pointed out that the Hubbard Woods district
coverage is inadequate for residential and vehicular coverage. He then showed a
map of the predicted coverage for the proposed location at Tower Realty. He
then showed the map of actual road data collected by using a test transmitter
and stated that this map shows the increase in coverage with the new location.
Manager Williams asked about a location point at the very north of the map and
it was determined that this was in Glencoe around Hawthorn & Vernon, possibly
at the Optima building.
President Holland then asked if they have done a test for the load capacity for
the equipment in that building.
   Mr. Davilla said that this was not something he could answer as it was not
his field.
President Holland asked him to continue and said she would ask her question at
another time.
Mr. Davilla then showed a map with the Public Works Yard location and explained
that coverage would not be ideal if this location was used. He also showed the
Council the map of the water plant location and golf course locations and
explained that they also would not provide sufficient coverage. The park
district driving range map also showed coverage would be insufficient. He said
that he may be able to explain to Trustee Lanphier the reason why the park
district site would not work. The map showed that the Northfield site was only
about 1 mile away. The ideal site would be to put the new site in a location
somewhere in the middle of the existing sites so as to have the seamless
coverage desired. If they were to put a site at the park district driving range
location, the FCC only allows them to use a frequency within certain distances
and the same frequency may be used two or three miles away. If the sites are
too close, they cannot use as many frequencies or the same frequency because of
interference.
Trustee Lanphier noted that even with the Tower Realty location, the southwest
end of Winnetka is not well covered.
Mr. Davilla said that what the maps do not show is the site west of the Edens
in Northfield and if the Council were to see that, it would show that the
remaining area is covered by this site.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Davilla what would be happening in the years to
come, and if these sites will be subdivided.
Mr. Davilla said that he didn’t know, but if there are more users, they will
need to increase the sites. He said that there cannot be too many sites without
having to go lower unless there is different technology.
Manager Williams asked about the status of project angel and if Chicago is one
of the test sites.
Mr. Davilla said that it is going to happen next year and the construction
phase will begin next. He said that Chicago is the test site.
Manager Williams asked if the goal is to put a cell phone in each home.
Mr. Davilla said that they are focusing on homes and he is not involved in the
design, but it is coming and Chicago is the test market. He said that some of
their monopoles are already constructed for that possibility and there is a box
that will be on a person’s house that will have a direct signal.
President Holland asked if there could be a new A-3 site plan for the next
meeting and Trustee Lanphier requested a picture of what the antennas at the
site will look like.
President Holland asked about the weight loads and if the building can support
the equipment. The AT & T engineer replied that it would be comparable to
SprintCom’s and weigh about 600 lbs. President Holland then asked what the
total weight would be and the AT & T engineer said the total would be 2400 lbs.
President Holland asked Manager Williams if the fire department looks at the
interior installations. Mr. Williams answered that if there are fire code
issues, the fire department would be involved.
Trustee Hilton moved to introduce the proposed ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Lien. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Winnetka Galleria, 562-572 Lincoln Avenue
Resolution No. R-1436-98 – Access Agreement – Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed the proposed resolution which would grant a permanent right of access
across the Lincoln Avenue parking lot to the Winnetka Galleria. She explained
that because the redevelopment of the Caledonia Building includes an
underground parking area, which will be accessed down a ramp located near the
rear of the building, vehicular access from the rear is not possible unless
access is allowed via the parking lot. She pointed out that although access
rights would result in the loss of several parking spaces in the parking lot,
these would be offset by the creation of two on-street parking spaces in front
of the building and the number of underground parking spaces built would exceed
the amount required by Village Code. She also noted that the general right of
ingress and egress best serves the Village’s needs because it allows the
Village greater flexibility and control over its property than would be
possible if specific easement locations were defined. She distributed copies of
the site plan that shows the parking spaces.
Trustee Lanphier asked if the Council had talked about this at a previous time.
Ms. Janega replied that it had been discussed at the time the License Agreement
was done for the use of the parking lot. The Access Agreement follows that
agreement by allowing a permanent use of the parking lot. Ms. Janega then
explained the highlighted areas of the site plan showing the parking spaces
lost and new parking spaces created. She said that the terms provide for a
permanent right of continuing access to the property with the Village
maintaining control over the access route. She said that this was not done as
an easement purposely . The Access Agreement protects the rights of the Village
to continue to use the parking lot or reconfigure it as long as a reasonable
access is allowed for vehicular access.
Trustee Lanphier asked how many spaces were underground and how many were for
the use of the residents.
Ms. Janega said there are 33 total, 18 allocated for residential use and 10 for
commercial use, leaving an excess of 5.
Trustee Lanphier said that she hadn’t realized that there would be a loss of
five spaces.
Manager Williams said that because of gaining the two spaces on Lincoln Avenue,
they are really only losing two spaces which may be made up by re-striping.
Trustee Lanphier asked how the extra five spaces would be used.
Mr. Cummins explained that the old building covered 2200 sq. ft. and had no
parking. They are offering the new building occupants 16 spaces for residential
use, and the extra are for visitor parking or to offer residents an extra
space.
Trustee Hilton stated that the effect after the project is completed is that
the Village will be losing two spaces.
Mr. Cummins said that part of the difficulty with the extra spaces is to
maintain the lot as a limited area. It isn’t designed to be a public in and
public out parking lot.
Trustee Lanphier said that there is a problem in the business district with
employees parking in that area and asked if Mr. Cummins could require employees
working in the building to park in the underground lot.
Mr. Cummins said that the agreement with Frances Heffernan gives them two
spaces, but they may have 5 or 6 people working.
Trustee Lanphier commented that it would be a shame, if there is space
available in the underground parking, that employees would park on the street.
Mr. Cummins said that they are compensating for spaces that were never there
before and he suggested further that they may actually compound the problem by
enhancing retail activity which might make the parking problem worse.
President Holland said that this leads to her next question that if everything
becomes healthy, and the Village should consider a parking deck she wants to
make sure that they would not have a problem doing that and that Steve Saunders
has considered this.
Manager Williams said that Village Attorney Janega has been careful to address
this in the agreement.
Ms. Janega then pointed out the language in the agreement addresses this issue
so that the use can be changed if necessary, but that access is still
reasonable. This is so that the Village has some flexibility.
Manager Williams agreed that there is a lot of flexibility built into the
agreement.
Mr. Cummins said that this is the one agreement that matters most to the
project as it pertains to the future. He said that he hopes the Village would
enhance the retail in that area.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the proposed resolution. The motion was seconded
by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Duhl.
Resolution No. R-1437-98 – Development Agreement – Adoption. Ms. Janega
explained that this resolution would authorize the execution of a Development
Agreement between the Village and Winnetka Galleria to establish the terms and
conditions under which the development of the property must proceed. She also
said that this Agreement is the last piece of the puzzle and is required by the
building code and zoning ordinance and protects the Village during the
construction project to insure that public improvements are constructed in a
safe and timely fashion. The Agreement defines specific requirements as to site
features, building height, number of dwelling units, architectural features and
landscaping, in addition to defining required infrastructure improvements. The
Agreement also sets out standards for construction parking and construction
traffic as well as required elements of the Condominium Declaration. Ms. Janega
said that this building will be in condominium ownership ultimately and the
Agreement will change somewhat to bring it into conformity with the condominium
statutes. The Agreement is to substantially provide the Village with a party to
go to in order to make sure that the building is in compliance with codes. She
said the Agreement has been reviewed by the Water and Electric Department and
the Village Engineer and the goal is to make sure the construction process is
as orderly as possible. There are also insurance requirements in this Agreement
that will protect the Village.
Mr. Cummins presented a drawing of the project to the Council. He said that the
development agreement gives the Village more control than the Zoning Ordinance
does.
President Holland asked Mr. Cummins if there is a completion date. Mr. Cummins
said that they are about 32 days behind schedule because of removing the four
underground storage tanks that were discovered, but he intends to make that
time up as they go forward. The Caledonian building predated the buildings to
the north and south and when it was put in it was affixed to the buildings next
to it and because of that it requires underpinning. He said that it is a steel
frame building and they will begin setting steel January 1. The commercial unit
objective is July 15 and the residential is ?????
President Holland asked about the urns against the fence.
Mr. Cummins said that there is a replacement of the urns on the top of the
clock tower. They are not in good shape and the architects want to implant some
of the older material into the new building. He said that the clock tower does
not conform to the building height and they are up before the Zoning Board of
Appeals again next week.
Ms. Janega said there is one last point about construction schedules. One
factor in this agreement is that there is no schedule built into it and this is
because the license agreement for the parking lot controls the construction
process and helps to prevent delays.
Trustee Lanphier asked if the preschool has been satisfied with the truck
traffic.
Manager Williams said there has been no complaints from them. He said that some
complaints had been received for early morning noise, but the complaints were
resolved between he and Mr. Cummins.
Mr. Cummins said that he thought that people should call the police although he
is willing to provide his home phone number if they have complaints.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt the proposed resolution as outlined by Ms.
Janega. She was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Mr. Cummins said that he had one last thing. He said he has entered into an
agreement to develop the property at 808 Oak. He said they have a lot of work
to do with staff but hopefully when they come back with this, they will have
the same relationship for that project as they have had with this one. He
presented a rendering of a proposed plan and said there are a variety of
difficult issues that relate to this project. He said they hope to file a
proper plat of subdivision by October 15 and they hope this will be successful.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1095 and 1096. Trustee Lien reported that all of his
questions had been answered satisfactorily and moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1095 in the total amount of $590,370.35 and Warrant List No. 1096 in the
total amount of $565,779.06. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lien,
Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Bid No. 98-028 – Boiler Conversion. Mr. Williams reviewed this bid for the
conversion of Boiler #8 Combustion Controls from pneumatic to electronic,
thereby upgrading the existing flame management system with all new
instrumentation on a total turnkey installation basis. He said that this is the
last boiler over the past three years to be converted. He reported Staff’s
recommendation to award the bid to independent Mechanical Industries in the
total amount of $213,988.00. He said that the Village has used this contractor
in the past and results have been good.
Trustee Lanphier asked if the contract amount was set or if there are other
elements to this.
Manager Williams replied that they are very comfortable with the amount the
contractor bid for the project.
Trustee Derning moved to approve the award of the bid to Mechanical Industries
and was seconded by Trustee Lien . By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer and Hilton. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Agreement for Lining of Sewer, 915 Sheridan Road. Attorney Janega reviewed the
Agreement and Grant of Easement proposed to resolve the dispute between the
Village and the owners of the property at 915 Sheridan Road over the location,
operation and maintenance of the storm sewer line on the property. She
indicated that the Village Engineer has estimated that the cost of the work to
be approximately $100,000.00.
Manager Williams said that this item had been in discussion for about a year
and referred to the materials in the Council’s agenda packet that show the
existing sewer line. He said that the issues have been worked through with the
resident and the Village will put in the northern Y of this line. He said it is
important to note that this is a public drainage way, not a private one.
because it drains water from Sheridan Road. He said there is some erosion that
occurred in the ravine area and the property owner will be repairing that.
Manager Williams said that he believes they have arrived at a good resolution
to this problem and in answer to Trustee Hilton’s question, thought they would
be about $25,000 over the budgeted amount of $75,000 for this project.
Ms. Janega then went over the revisions to this Agreement with the Council.
Trustee Hilton asked what would happen if consent was not received from Glen
Oaks.
Ms. Janega said that if consent is not received by the Village, then the
property owners will try to obtain it. If they cannot, then the Village will
let that one leg continue to drain into the ravine.
Trustee Lanphier moved to approve the Agreement authorizing execution of the
Agreement Regarding Stormwater Sewer System Repairs and the Agreement for Storm
Sewer and Drainage Easements in substantially the form proposed. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Hilton . By roll call vote the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Duhl.
Trustee Meuer then moved to authorize the expenditure of up to $100,000.00,
and, if necessary a corresponding amendment of the Village’s current sewer
lining contract, to cover the proposed repairs and improvements to the storm
sewer at 915 Sheridan Road. This motion was seconded by Trustee Derning. By
roll call vote the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer,
Hilton, Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
X. Reports.
Village President.
Village Trustees.
Village Attorney.
Village Manager.
Funding Status for Elm Street Bridge. Manager Williams informed the Trustees
that Staff had recently learned from IDOT that the Congress rail for the Elm
Street bridge is not eligible for Federal funding. Thus, it is the Village’s
responsibility to fund that portion of the railing that is above and beyond the
customary expense. He reported that it is possible that this might result in an
additional expense to the Village of between $170,000 and $394,000 and might
delay the rehabilitation of the Eldorado or Pine Street bridges for one year.
Water Allocation Modification.
Manager Williams reported that the Village Attorney and the Director of Water
and Electric are working on this and arguing an appeal before the authorities.
Zoning Application Withdrawn. Mr. Williams announced that the applicants for a
zoning variation for increased GFA at 217 Woodland had withdrawn their variance
request in light of the fact that the Zoning Board of Appeals had recommended
that Council deny it.
Fire Department Open House. Manager Williams announced that the Fire Department
will host an open house on October 10, 1998 from 10:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m.
XI. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lanphier seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Trustee Duhl: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 9:52 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:55 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Appointments.
President Holland appointed Margaret Bensen as Chairperson of the Zoning Board
of Appeals for a term of two years. A motion to approve the appointment was
made by Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
   President Holland appointed Ed Woodbury to the Plan Commission. The motion
to approve the President’s appointment was made by Trustee Derning, seconded by
Trustee Meuer. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
11:00 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
October 13, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved October 20, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 13, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:42 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Penfield Lanphier, David Hilton and William Meuer.
Absent: Trustees Michael Duhl, John Lien. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Director of Water and
Electric Bryan McInturff, and about 21 persons in the audience.
II. Liquor Service at the Golf Course.
At its August 18th Council meeting, the Village Council tabled this request
from the Park District to serve liquor at the golf course clubhouse food
service facility in order to give notice to all residents via the Winnetka
Report that this issue would be considered at the October 13th meeting. The
Park District has made a request that the Village amend its liquor ordinance to
allow them to serve beer and wine at the golf course. After discussion, the
Council directed the preparation of an ordinance that creates a separate “Class
P” license for the Park District only that would allow the service of beer,
between 11:00 a.m. and 7:30 p.m.
Trustee Lanphier moved to take this item off the table, seconded by Trustee
Hilton. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Joe Dooley, President of the Park Board, said that the two reasons this request
was brought to the Council in July are that golf course users have requested it
and that the park board has had some trouble getting concession operators to
serve food because liquor is not served to make up for days when food service
is poor. They are asking for a one year license, that would be renewable, to
serve beer only when the food service operation is open from 11:00 a.m. to 7:30
p.m. The golf course is closed in the winter and there would be no concession
during that time. Mr. Dooley listed the golf courses who serve beer and
commented that the country clubs also serve liquor where there are other
activities, such as pools, golf, children playing, etc. It will not be a tavern
atmosphere, and they simply want to offer beer at the clubhouse. It would
remain the same as it is now, except that a person could get a beer after a
round of golf.
Trustee Hilton asked if the service would be from April 1 to November 1.
Trustee Meuer asked if it would be served on the course itself.
Mr. Dooley said that the beer would only be served in the clubhouse and only
while the golf course is open from April to November.
President Holland asked if anyone from the audience wished to speak.
Jerome Burke, 1201 Pine Street distributed a handout to the trustees of
observations he has made about the history of the request to sell alcohol at
the golf course. He thinks the council should consider if it is really
necessary and in the best interest of Winnetka. He prepared a history of the
requests to sell beer and the only time a vendor mentioned that they would like
to sell alcohol was in May, 1995. He stated that the golf board advisory
committee met with J.P. McCarthy and that’s when they suggested selling beer.
He found that no other concessionare had mentioned that they would like to sell
beer, nor after they received the concession did they say they wanted to sell
beer. Mr. Burke does not believe that the request is as strong as presented. He
cited Chicken Charlies who asked to receive the concession again without
mentioning selling beer, but the Park District was not pleased with their
service. He thinks that many of the users come from outside Winnetka. Mr. Burke
stated that there have been requests to increase the children’s facilities and
he believes this is not the type of thing Winnetka wants at the golf course.
Judy Burke, 1201 Pine St., said she is concerned from a different angle and
that is to look at the idea of what is the greater good. She distributed a
handout she had prepared to the Council. She believes Winnetka’s golf course is
very unique and referred to a map she had prepared marked with the golf courses
who serve liquor in the area. She said that none of these Park Districts have
the facilities that Winnetka does. She also said that Wilmette and Highland
Park have full service restaurants with their golf courses. Mrs. Burke stated
that the Par 3 golf course in Winnetka attracts young persons and the other
areas for children’s use will be open at the same time the golf course wants to
serve beer. Because she believes that Winnetka is a unique situation, she asked
the Council to consider the request very carefully.
Trustee Lanphier asked if the Park District currently has a license to serve
beer and wine with food. The Park District representatives indicated they have
no license to serve any alcohol.
Mr. Curt Krueger said that he has observed the area around the Park District
golf course and the activities there. He said that Skokie Playfield is a family
oriented center where children participate in most activities. He said the
users of the 18 hole golf course make up 18% of all people who use Skokie
Playfield. He asked if Winnetka wants to encourage consumption of alcohol
during family-oriented activities. He said that on prom night, etc, parents go
to the trouble of stressing not being in an alcohol environment to their
children and wonders what kind of example serving alcohol will set at the golf
course. He believes that the beer consumers may not just be golf course
attendees, that the clubhouse will be open to all of the sports and activities
at Skokie Playfield, and there is no way to limit this to just golfers. He
thinks that this will also lead to an increase in accidents in an area where
there are already too many accidents, if council approves this license. He
considers this a landmark request. He said there has been no alcohol for 60
years served at the golf course. He said that the Village has a Plan Commission
who will be coming up with overall plan for Village and this issue has
ramifications for the whole Village. He asked that this issue should be turned
over to the Plan Commission to insure that this kind of decision fits who
Winnetkans are and what they want to become.
Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce St., asked if the continued strong attendance at the 18
hole and Par 3 golf course belies the reason given by the Park District for
wanting to have liquor served. He said that unless he is missing something,
there has been no diminution of people using the course.
Mr. Dooley replied that he was correct, that golfers will come even if beer is
not sold, but have asked if it could be served since they would like it after
they play golf. He said that they are not requesting beer to attract more
golfers, the golfers will still come.
Glenn Cray, 1295 Sunview Lane. He is familiar with the clubhouse and he said
that the Park District will not be putting up beer signs anywhere on the
grounds. He understood that there will only be beer sold inside the clubhouse.
He said that he thinks that people are worrying about that, but it would be
served in a specific location.
Trustee Lanphier asked if anyone who is 21 can get a beer there or if it would
only be golfers.
Mr. Newport said the issue is that if someone comes in how do they distinguish
if that person is a golfer or a tennis player or someone else. He said that it
would not be possible to limit beer consumption to golfers. He said that there
is a 1 year limit on the liquor license and if it is not working out, it can be
changed. He said that it is an adult option, and the Park District is not
encouraging the sale of liquor to minors. He feels that it would be an
appropriate thing, while recognizing that there are other activities going on
at the same time. They have had liquor served at special events for many years
with no problem.
Trustee Hilton said that it seems that some of the residents feel that the
service will be abused and asked if there were any controls set up for that.
Mr. Newport said that they would work with the Police Department and oversee as
a landlord that things did not get out of hand. He said that one of the things
that helps is that the customers are known to the employees and to other adults
from the community who will recognize underage consumers.
Mr. Derning said that he realized that this facility did not come about by
accident. it evolved over a long time with the careful concern of the park
board and this request is being made by the same board. He does not believe
that granting this request is going to change the board or its members or that
problems will go unchecked. He thinks that the track record by the staff and
park board is commendable and this request should be supported, especially for
a trial year.
Manager Williams said that the issue can be docketed for next Tuesday for
official action to be taken.
The resident at 577 Sumac, asked the Council to be careful to consider the
liability. He asked who at the Park District will have the authority to
determine who has had enough to drink. Some golfers may go beyond the .8 limit
for driving drunk and if an accident occurs he believes that it would be very
bad for the Board.
Jim Pomerantz, J.P. McCarthy, said that he would like to clarify that they did
not request service of beer when they applied for the concession because they
were told it was not an option. He thinks the key points with this are that
they accept the responsibility for serving the liquor, that the business is run
according to the laws of the Village, and that it is a one year trial. He
doesn’t take the service of liquor lightly at J.P. McCarthy’s which is a family
place and he would not take it lightly at the golf course concession. He is not
speaking for the park board, but believes the reason they are requesting this
is that it is an overwhelming topic of conversation by the users of the golf
course and the community.
Trustee Lanphier commented that she is in the middle on this issue. She knows
the request asks to serve beer without food, but that is contrary to the other
Village licenses. She believes that food helps mitigate the affect of alcohol
in the body. She is concerned about the mixed land uses and serving alcohol at
the golf course would be at the bottom of her list for this area. She also
feels it is the responsibility of the liquor commissioners to understand that
people have been asking for this and she knows the Park District would be
responsible, but wants to be able to minimize any tragedy that may occur from
this. She said that she would prefer to limit it to service with food. As a
general matter, she feels it is the same as any other place that wants to serve
liquor and that it should be with food.
Trustee Hilton said that to answer Trustee Lanphier, he believes that requiring
food to be served is impractical. He said that to have to order a hamburger at
3:00 p.m. just to get a beer would not be reasonable. He said that either the
Council should approve the request or not.
Trustee Lanphier said that maybe it should not be served at all. The response
that she received seemed to be that the people supported serving liquor with
food.
Trustee Hilton said that people may not want to eat at 3:00 or 4:00 p.m.
because they are going home to dinner.
Mr. Burke asked if there was any other evidence other than the anecdotes that
have been mentioned. He has just heard that people want beer and that Jim
Pomerantz says that people say they want beer, but it doesn’t make sense to him
in view of the large attendance. He asked if a survey had been taken.
Several members of the audience said no, that it is just what has been heard
over the years.
One audience member said that he personally requested beer service, but it is
anecdotal, not evidence.
Mr. Dooley said that the beer is not the overriding concern of the golfers.
They are coming to Winnetka to play golf and after that, they want a beer. The
Park District is not losing golfers to Wilmette because they serve beer and
Winnetka doesn’t.
Mr. Burke said he is trying to get a sense of why it is important to change
what is working at the Playfield. He doesn’t think the reason is there.
Mr. Newport said that they try to provide general servicing and amenities to
the users, whether it is beer or something else. The Park District is trying to
provide the best service they can. He said that there was a request like this
several years ago of the Council that was put to the caucus and passed. The
Park District is trying to respond to their users by attempting to provide this
service.
One of the audience members was concerned that the ordinance, when it is
adopted, is forever.
Manager Williams replied that the liquor license is renewable on an annual
basis.
The audience member said that the ordinance is on the books forever and he has
read that the caucus response was that people did not want service of beer at
the golf course. He remembered that Chief Gallagher said that having food with
liquor would make enforcement very difficult. He wonders if the convenience of
the golfers is so important that they must change Skokie Playfield. He said
that one of the reasons given was that they could not get a good vendor with no
beer service, but there already is a good vendor. He asked if they are
considering the convenience of the golfers only or of the community as a whole.
Trustee Meuer asked if Mr. Williams had heard from the schools or churches or
any other people who indicated he had not. He said that he knows Trustee Lien
has questions and needs further discussion.
President Holland said that the Council is missing two trustees who have said
that this is important enough an issue that they would like to have a vote. She
said that next Tuesday they will take a vote on this issue when the other
trustees are present.
Trustee Meuer and Trustee Lanphier then asked Jim Pomerantz questions about who
is at the golf course during the day and if a two year limit is practical as
well as enforcement questions and the proximity to children’s activities.
Mr. Burke asked if the consumption of alcohol is allowed on Skokie Playfield.
Trustee Lanpher replied that only if there is a special event, but not on a
daily basis. President Holland said there are regulations about people bringing
alcohol onto the property.
III. Policy Direction Regarding the Use of Special Assessment Procedures to
Place Electric Lines Underground – Sunview Lane.
Manager Williams reviewed this request from several residents on Sunview Lane
who are interested in establishing a Special Assessment District to offset the
cost of implementing the placing of electric utility lines underground. Mr.
Williams also discussed other financing options and expressed the Staff’s
concern about how to prioritize this work with the goal of the Comprehensive
Plan to place underground electric lines along all major streets in the
Village. Policy direction is needed to determine whether the Village wants to
authorize neighborhoods to place their electric lines underground through the
special assessment process or prepaid direct payments, how to determine the
priority of the work, and if special assessment districts should be set up or
the prepayment of costs required before the project is started.
Trustee Lanpher asked about the method used for the recent alley improvement
that was approved. Manager Williams said that this was the Cherry Street Alley
special assessment.
   Mr. Williams said that it has been interesting to work with Bryan McInturff
on the undergrounding program with goal of electric lines along major streets
being put underground by 2010. He has also had conversations with residents of
Sunview about undergrounding on their street who said they may be willing to
fund that project. As they worked through this, Mr. Williams became concerned
about how they would be able to accomplish all of the work and how to
prioritize it. He and Bryan McInturff believe they can underground the lines on
the major streets by 2010 and one neighborhood of the Sunview type per year. He
said there are a couple of ways to fund these projects and one is the special
assessment. With this method the cost is divided between the residents
proportionately. The negative side of this is that it is very bureaucratic, as
the Council has seen with the Cherry Street alley project which involved the
Board of Local Improvements, the Village Council and eventually the court to
set up of special assessment district. He said that the second method is a
special service area. This establishes a project and is repaid by property tax.
The benefit is that it is very simple and you don’t get into court issues. The
negative is that it is not always the fairest way to divide the cost. Another
method which was used on Boal Parkway, was that all residents were required to
contribute their share and when the Village received the money, the work was
done. The negative side of this is that everyone has to agree to the project.
   Mr. Williams said that if they decide on special assessments, the funding
needs to be decided and priorities need to be set. This type of special
assessment may be very popular and his concern is that the other Village
undergrounding work will not be done. Neighborhood work would be a priority
because the residents have paid for the work and this may prevent other village
goals to underground from getting completed. He said that it is important that
Winnetka lineman do key electrical work because it is to be maintained by
Winnetka. Mr. Williams said he was excited about this program, but there needs
to be some balance and policy direction. He said that it can be done, but
funding methods have to be determined, and priorities established about how to
choose which neighborhoods go first.
   Trustee Derning asked if the service meant undergrounding the lines or the
whole resident’s service.
   Mr. McInturff said it would be the whole service to the residence and it has
taken up to year in the past to get the telephone and cable companies to put
their lines underground at the same time.
Trustee Lanphier asked on what terms the other companies use our poles.
Mr. McInturff said that it says that if our lines are underground then the
cable company has to be underground. The telephone company owns the poles
jointly with the Village.
Mr. Cray said that his service is underground and it amazed him how difficult
it was to get Com Ed to put the lines underground, but they finally did. He
wondered why some of the work couldn’t be subcontracted out, which might also
save the village money.
Trustee Hilton asked how much could be contracted out.
Mr. McInturff said that they could contract out enough work that it would save
them 2/3 of the work.
An audience member asked if that would also save 2/3 of the money.
President Holland replied that dollars aren’t the issue. The Village’s goal is
that by 2010 all major streets will be underground and to meet that goal, the
issue is how much availability does that leave the staff.
Mr. Williams said that subcontracting will work, but he believes that Winnetka
lineman need to be used because out of town contractors do not have the
standards that Winnetka does.
Trustee Meuer asked how many residents are on Sunview.
An audience member said there are 26 homes.
Another audience member said that all but two residents want to put the lines
underground.
Trustee Lanphier asked Mr. McInturff what the per household cost would be to
put the lines underground.
Mr. McInturff said that it is about $6000 or $7000 per house with about $4,500
of that the basic service to the home.
Trustee Meuer asked about the $109,000 alluded to in the letter.
An audience member replied that some of the homes have already paid to have
service from street to house underground, so this figure of $109,000 would be
the basic amount of $4,500 per home.
Trustee Lanphier asked about the special assessment process.
Mr. Williams said there would be a lot of bureaucratic process such as there
was for the Cherry Street alley improvements.
Mr. Derning asked if the majority of the residents want this and can finance
this, can it be done and can the people who do want it done, pay for the
remaining people who don’t.
An audience member said that they are requesting that the Village provide this
service to the residents at the residents cost.
Trustee Lanphier said that given that there is a program to underground the
lines, this idea sounds great to her. The problem is that if the revenue
received is not enough to fund the extra linemen that would be needed and would
the Village need to hire additional workers.
Mr. Williams said that the Village can do a project like Sunview each year -
that is doable. The problem he believes is what happens down the road if five
neighborhoods want this service done in one year. How do they determine which
neighborhood gets the work done.
Trustee Lanphier asked at what point would it make sense to add the personnel
to accomplish this work.
Mr. Williams said that he could not justify hiring more workers, and that the
work would have to be contracted out but would not be up to the standard of the
village.
Trustee Meuer asked if farming out the work would set a precedent. He stated
that there is a neighborhood that is very interested in doing this and more
power to them. He thinks the total cost should be the homeowners
responsibility, that some work should be farmed out and our linemen should do
their work. He believes that they are on a course they should stay on.
Trustee Derning said that the only way to choose what work will be done is to
do it chronologically.
The Council continued to discuss how to prioritize the work. Mr. McInturff
listed some of the projects that are already under way and that are planned for
the future. He said that there are only so many man hours and essentially all
of the conduit work is being farmed out currently, so there is not that much
more to be gained by farming out more work.
Trustee Hilton said that he thought they will never get everyone to agree for a
special service area. He asked if a special assessment is used, can all costs
be built in.
Mr. Williams said that all of the costs can be built into it, but it is
possible that we may have to contract out the legal work because the village
attorney is already very busy.
Trustee Lanphier said that they need to think in terms of a general policy as
far as a special assessment is concerned.
Trustee Derning commented that in a special service area the money comes in
over years and asked how the work is funded, if the Village did not have the
capital.
Mr. Williams said that special assessment bonds would be issued. This is a
judgment against the properties and residents pay over time.
Trustee Derning said that if the village did not have the capital, then what.
An audience member suggested that the bonds could finance more projects at a
higher amount.
Mr. Williams said the number one project next year is Sheridan Road and Mr.
McInturff has to keep up with the new houses being built who have to have
underground service.
Trustee Lanphier said that if there is anyway to accommodate the residents now,
it should be done.
Mr. Williams repeated that one neighborhood a year can be done, but they need
direction or policy for more than one project a year.
Trustee Meuer suggested that Mr. Williams and Mr. McInturff bring the issue
back at the next study session.
Mr. Williams replied that the Sunview paperwork needs to begin now if the
project is to be done next summer.
An audience member suggested that there would be a savings to Winnetka to do
the undergrounding as far as maintenance goes.
IV. Class A Liquor License Application – 1062 Gage.
Manager Williams reviewed this application for a Class A Liquor License for a
new restaurant at 1062 Gage, Tai Fuko Japanese Restaurant and Jewelry.
   Teddy Chu said that he plans to open a Japanese restaurant at 1062 Gage to
serve lunch and dinner, 11:30 a.m. to 9:30 p.m. and Friday and Saturday to 10:
30 p.m. They are requesting to serve liquor with dinner. President Holland
asked if this is a request for full service liquor. Mr. Chu said yes and that
jewelry will also be sold in one part of the restaurant. Trustee Hilton asked
if there was anything unusual about the application. Mr. Williams said that
there is nothing unusual, there would be no bar, and a menu was provided in the
Council’s agenda materials that shows a full menu. Mr. Williams told Mr. Chu
that there will be an ordinance done for this and it will take another two
meetings. President Holland told the applicant that he should have his license
in about three weeks.
V. Street Improvement Report.
Steve Saunders reviewed the results of the annual street program for the years
1993-1998 and the projected program for the years 1999-2005. He said that
within this time frame, it is feasible that nearly all Village streets will be
rehabilitated or reconstructed, with the exception of a few streets.
Mr. Williams added that one of the things in Ken Keene’s contract was to update
the street program and they are right on track with the 13 year program.
   Steve Saunders said that the program as outlined will result in the
rehabilitation or reconstruction of every street in the village. He said that
this has been possible because of an enlightened Village Council, the
cooperation of the residents and a Village Manager who is dedicated to making
capital improvements. The program will continue at $870,000 per year and
reconstruction work will end in 2001. Some streets that are shown as needing
rehabilitation may actually require reconstruction when they get to them
depending on what the pavement and subconditions are. Also some of the costs
may go up depending on the construction of new houses.
President Holland asked if the Village monitors the contractors who are
building new houses and tearing up the street.
Mr. Saunders said yes and that the contractors pay a fee, but the Village
patches the road. He said that sometimes there may be a lag time between when
the contractor is finished and when the Village can contract the work to be
done. The costs are paid by the contractor and some of the cost was built into
the increases in building permit fees. Construction prices have been budgeted
at a 3% inflation rate.
Trustee Lanphier asked if Mr. Saunders felt comfortable with the program or if
they should they be putting more annual dollars into it.
Mr. Saunders said that for the next five years or so, he is comfortable with
it. He said that it is reviewed annually and there is room to make an
adjustment.
President Holland asked if he intends to show the residents the type of process
they will be using.
Mr. Saunders said that he thinks it is the best thing to do. He wants to get a
95% final design and have the residents look at it. He does not want to have
the residents designing the streets, but wants to present them with a final
design that will work. He also said that he will take into account the
residents needs.
Mollybelle Berish asked about the work to be done on her street. She said that
she thought the way that the work is going to be done would be more expensive,
than the way it was done on another street where there was no curb and gutter
put in.
Mr. Saunders replied that the primary reason that curb and gutter was not put
in on some streets is for drainage reasons.
   Mr. Williams said that he would like to talk about area drains between the
sidewalk and curb. He said this is only one area where capital dollars are
used. Engineers are making decisions on where to replace curbs and then
residents ask why their curbs were not replaced. He said that there is only so
many dollars for replacements and the engineers make the decisions about which
curbs to replace. He said that sometimes only a two inch curb is needed instead
of a four inch curb and it is still very effective. Sealcoating also stretches
the life of a street until it can be scheduled for work. He said that the area
drains have worked very well. There are some aesthetic issues they have had to
work through, but they have actually come out ahead.
Trustee Hilton commented that he knows of a resident on Mt. Pleasant who was
very happy with the paving.
Mr. Saunders said that the residents didn’t want curbs there and it has worked
out very well.
President Holland asked when they will let people know about the
reconstructions.
Mr. Saunders said that the designs are usually finalized in February, so the
meeting will probably be in March. He said that completion dates for next year
will include set times for all stages of the work.
Trustee Derning said that on the basis of what Mr. Saunders has submitted, he
sees no reason to change what is being done. He said that he has even seen
Steve on Saturdays supervising the work.
VI. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:30 p.m.
October 20, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved November 5, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 20, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and Bill Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Fire Chief Ron Colpaert and
Deputy Chief Eric Bennett and about 35 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
November 5, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton indicated that he would not
be able to attend this meeting.
V. Approval of Minutes
October 6, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as corrected. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Trustee Lanphier had one correction to her comment at the bottom
of page 4 and President Holland noted that the address in the heading of R-
1440-98 on page 5 was incorrect
October 13, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-211-98 – Provides for Sale of Beer at the Park District
Clubhouse Food Service Facility – Adoption. At the August 4, 1998 council
meeting, the Winnetka Park District requested an amendment to the Village
Liquor Ordinance to allow the service of beer and wine at the golf course
clubhouse. After discussion, the Village Council directed the preparation of an
ordinance that would allow the service of beer only from 11:00 a.m. to 7:30
p.m. The ordinance as drafted creates a separate Class “P” license for the Park
District Only. The ordinance was tabled at the August 18 meeting, removed from
the table at the October 13 meeting, and is being considered for adoption.
Trustee Meuer moved to adopt this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Derning.
Joe Dooley, 428 Cedar, President of the Park District, reviewed the request to
serve beer at the golf course clubhouse from April to November during the hours
of 11:00 a.m. to 7:30 p.m. without food. The reasons for the request are that
the service of beer was requested by the users of the golf course and also
because food service vendors are becoming difficult to find. The only location
to serve the beer would be at the clubhouse.
George Noyes, 381 Elder Ln., voiced his opinion that the establishment of this
ordinance would set precedent and secondly, service of adult beverages without
food opens the Village to other applications from people who would like the
same privilege. He encouraged the Council not to approve this ordinance. He
said that the current ordinance is fine the way it is, no other vendor is
permitted to serve liquor without food and asked that the Council vote no.
Dr. Bob Winn, Hadley School, Dr. Hersch of 1578 Hazel, Mr. Marc Hecht, 1096
Spruce Street, and Mr. Kurt Krueger, 1215 Elm, who distributed his written
opinion to the Council, also spoke against the adoption of this ordinance.
Mr. Ike Oberman, 468 Ash, said that 15 years ago, he spoke before caucus group
on this issue. At that time, the Village Council and the Park Board agreed that
they would take an informative referendum to find out if the people in the
Village supported the service of beer with the results being 56% to 44% of the
600 people responding in favor. He said that the Village Council turned the
request down on the grounds that there are 4000 families in Winnetka and 3400
of them did not vote. He suggested that if a similar referendum were taken
today, there would be a similar result. He is an avid golfer, not a drinker,
and the clubhouse is not a place where people congregate. He said he expects
they would not be having 3 or 4 beers there after a round of golf. He hoped the
Council would take this all into consideration.
Trustee Derning said that at the last meeting, he made the observation that he
had confidence that Park District could administer this and still believes
this. He has had 17 phone calls at his home since then and 16 were opposed and
1 in favor. He said that the only plus is that there is a minor advantage to
having a beer at the golf course and having to weigh the two, he thinks the
time is not right for this ordinance.
Trustee Lien had many phone calls also and said that he could not find a
compelling reason why beer should be served, but could find reasons why it
should not be served. He said he has sons that played golf on the course and
that youths being what they are, he would have wondered if they really would
have been prevented from obtaining a beer. He said that it does set precedent
and he will vote against this ordinance.
Trustee Lanphier said she understands the Park Board’s request and that it has
been a long standing issue. She said her concern from phone calls she has
received, is that there is a high level of community concern. She said that
this is a heavily trafficked area by adults and children and not the safest
from a pedestrian standpoint. As a Village Trustee, she is not able to see the
necessity of changing the philosophy of serving liquor with food. She said that
if you take the orientation of the clubhouse with the other facilities into
consideration, although she believes that the vast majority of users would be
responsible, she does not see the compelling necessity to grant this request.
Trustee Hilton said he initially favored the sale of beer but because of the
strong feelings of the residents, changed his vote and votes no.
Trustee Duhl said he thinks this is the kind of issue which is reflective of
what the community wants and the right thing to do is to go to the caucus and
have them survey the Village on this subject so the Village can truly speak. He
said he could not vote for this in the absence of knowing what the Village
thinks.
Trustee Meuer said he also had a number of calls and agrees with Trustee Duhl.
He has faith that the Park District could oversee this and suggested that the
Park District revisit the caucus. He said he appreciated that all residents
left their full names and addresses with their messages.
President Holland said that she does not vote, but would like to voice her
opinion. She said that the December, 1997 caucus questionnaire asked to what
extent residents would support a referendum for liquor without food. She listed
the numbers from survey answers in response to this question. She said the
ordinance has changed gradually from liquor with dessert and liquor sold in
grocery stores to what it is now, but the issue needs a mandate from the
community and from the survey numbers, the support is just not there. She does
not think there would be any problem with the Park District overseeing this but
she would not vote for it, if she could.
By roll call vote, the motion to adopt this ordinance was denied. Ayes: None.
Nayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-543-98 – Zoning Variance: 1175 Whitebridge Hill Road –
Adoption.
This request is for a variation from the front and corner yard setback in order
to make structural alterations to the garage roof. The existing garage has a
non-conforming setback from the south roadway easement and the current request
would result in a variation of 15.27 feet from the required setback of 50 feet.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier,
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-544-98 – Zoning Variance – 788 Cherry Street - Adoption.
This request is for variations to permit an increase in roofed lot coverage and
a reduction in the rear and side yards in order to construct a covered entry
and an addition to widen the existing garage.
Trustee Derning moved to adopt this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, by
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-545-98 – Special Use Permit: SprintCom, 911 Green Bay Road –
Adoption. Kathy Janega reviewed this request for a special use permit by
SprintCom to install wireless telecommunications antennas and related equipment
on the roof at 911 Green Bay Road. This location is the lowest priority
category established by Section 22.12A of the Zoning Ordinance. SprintCom is
also seeking variations from the maximum permitted height and the distance from
single family residences. Public hearings were held on August 20 and September
14 and after consideration, the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended 4-0 that
the Village Council grant the special use permit and zoning variations, subject
to the test findings of Dr. Allen Taflove of Northwestern University who was
consulted by the Village in this matter.
Trustee Lien moved to adopt this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Derning.
Ms. Janega said that at the last meeting, questions were posed to SprintCom
regarding the capability of their system and the use of an antenna at a lower
height and were forwarded to Dr. Allen Taflove to provide answers to the
questions. Ms. Janega circulated these answers and a letter from SprintCom that
followed up on her questions about other locations in their network. She said
that SprintCom brought photo renderings in response to questions about the
possibility of using a shroud or other visual mitigation. She said there are
color photographs and transparencies in the agenda materials.
Rich Nikchevich, SprintCom, 333 Wacker, Chicago, introduced himself and
explained that he had returned with additional information about frequency
testing and methods of changing the external appearance of antennas.
Mr. Shah, senior RF engineer, reviewed tests he did with Dr. Taflove and showed
transparencies of the network coverage and predicted coverage from the landfill
site. He said that the tests performed with Dr. Taflove showed that coverage
stops between Elm and Oak to the north and just before Glendale Avenue to the
East.
Trustee Lanphier asked why there is better coverage from the Hubbard Woods site
than from the landfill site.
Mr. Shah said that because of interference from the trees, coverage is better
at the Hubbard Woods site. He said that at 911 Green Bay Road the rooftop
antenna versus the wall mounted antenna coverage difference is about 10 dB
which is a 75-90% decrease in coverage.
Trustee Lanphier asked at what point the acceptable coverage ended.
Mr. Shah said it would end ½ mile from the site or east of Hibbard Road and
Tower.
Trustee Lanpher then asked if that was a broader area than from the site at the
landfill.
Mr. Shah said there was a slightly bigger coverage area from Green Bay Road
because there are less trees to interfere.
Mr. Nikchevich said SprintCom had prepared various alternatives since the last
meeting and distributed copies to the Council that showed roof mounted antennas
without shielding, also additional photo simulations attached to the plans, and
a proposal based on the creation of a fiberglass shroud which would extend up
the roof line of the existing penthouse. He said the antennas would be located
behind the fiberglass and the signals can pass through without significant
disruption. He said that this is a very rare option for them to propose and
showed a sample of the fiberglass to the Council. He said the material can be
painted to match the brick on the penthouse and the antennas would be behind
the fiberglass. He said that another proposal is that they would try to obtain
brick that would match that on the buildings. The antenna panels would be
mounted on the exterior of the brick in this option.
Trustee Derning asked about wall mounted antennas on the brick.
Mr. Nikchevich said they would still need the antenna panels as well as the
antennas whether on the wall or on the roof.
Trustee Lanphier questioned if the antennas would be enclosed on three or four
sides with the shroud and brick wall options.
Mr. Nikchevich said that the shroud would completely enclose the antennas.
Trustee Hilton said that with the fiberglass option, it doesn’t seem as though
anything is visible, but with the brick option there does.
Mr. Nikchevich said that the antennas would not be seen with the fiberglass.
Trustee Lanphier asked about the lifespan of the fiberglass.
Mr. Nikchevich referred to an engineer from SprintCom who said that he doesn’t
have an answer. Mr. Nikchevich said that water or other elements should not
create disintegration.
Mr. Williams said that he had obtained information from the Internet on
shrouding and gave Trustee Lanphier this information.
Trustee Hilton asked why SprintCom’s antennas were higher than AT & T’s.
Mr. Nikchevich said that AT & T’s operate at a higher level than SprintCom’s
CDMA technology. They did look at going to TDMA but the area served and the
capacity of the antennas was less.
Dr. Allen Taflove, Northwestern University, Professor, retained as consultant
to the Village to investigate SprintCom’s and AT & T’s antennas, said that Ms.
Janega had forwarded the Council’s questions and he had provided a five-page
response. He summarized the answers he had written to the Council’s questions
and concluded that the wall-mounted antennas and antennas at the golf netting
poles would probably not work. He also concluded that the site proposed would
provide the best coverage because of the interference from the tree line from
other locations. Dr. Taflove also explained the difference between TDMA and
CDMA technology to the Council and said that for future expanded uses, the CDMA
technology is more capable than TDMA. He said he is not an expert in shrouds,
but referred to Andrews Corporation in his letter. In summary, the situation
demands the antennas be rooftop mounted in order to serve Winnetka residents.
The antennas mounted on the wall system would work poorly.
Trustee Derning asked Mr. Nikchevich if the three alternatives presented are
all acceptable to SprintCom.
Mr. Nikchevich replied that each of them is something that Sprintcom is
prepared to move forward with.
Ms. Janega suggested that the Council decide which alternative they prefer
before adopting ordinance.
Trustee Derning moved to choose the shroud, Trustee Hilton seconded.
Mr. Nikchevich said that the building owner is agreeable to this alternative,
which is alternative B.
Trustee Meuer said that his concern is that they are not sure how this will
wear over time. The engineer from SprintCom said that he was sure that the
integrity of the material would not falter, but he did not know what the
manufacturer recommended and thought it would be about 20 years.
Todd Meldy, RF manager for SprintCom said that it is the same material as is
used in buildings swimming pools and is very durable.
Trustee Lanphier said that since she is not familiar with these materials, if
it is next to a brick material how would it age differently.
Mr. Meldy said that if the material is aging, he thought they could replace it.
Mr. Williams said that staff did some research on this and it is a standard
application in other communities.
Ms. Janega said a condition could be made in the ordinance that SprintCom
maintain the shroud.
Trustee Lien said that he thought there would be a noticeable difference
between a painted fiberglass shroud and brick. He thinks it will add to the
height and will be more intrusive. There is already a tower and that will add
several feet and it will look worse than just the antenna.
By voice vote, all in favor of option B: Ayes: Trustees Derning and Hilton.
Nayes: Trustee Lanphier, Meuer, Duhl and Lien.
Trustee Meuer said that he doesn’t think the fiberglass will wear as well as
the brick and would have a higher upkeep. He would rather consider the brick.
Trustee Lanphier agreed.
Mr. Meldy said that SprintCom feels that just the antennas are the least
intrusive.
Trustee Meuer moved to amend the ordinance to approve Alternative C which is
the brick, seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By voice vote, Alternative C was
approved with President Holland voting yes.
Trustees Lien and Derning accepted the modification to their motion.
Ms. Janega said the ordinance would be modified to refer to Alternative C, with
an additional provision to require the construction of a brick extension on the
penthouse as shown in Alternative C.
By roll call vote, the motion to adopt the ordinance was passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl. Nayes: None.
Absent: None.
Bob Moochy asked about the study of the two sites at the golf course and the
landfill. He said his recollection was that there were two sites at the
landfill and the test they did was at ground level. He asked why the test was
not done at the top of the site.
Ms. Janega replied that the testing was done with a portable telescoping
antenna and rather than driving it to the top of the landfill, it was placed to
the north of the compost heap in close proximity to Tower and Hibbard Road. The
antenna was extended to exceed the maximum height stated in the ordinance which
was actually more optimal for measuring the signals. Because of this, Dr.
Taflove concluded that it was not necessary to test at the golf course site.
Ordinance No. M-546-98 – Special Use Permit: AT & T Wireless, 874 Green Bay
Road – Adoption.
Ms. Janega reviewed this request for a special use permit to install wireless
telecommunication antennas at 874 Green Bay Road, which is in the lowest
priority category location established in Section 22.12A of the Zoning
Ordinance. AT & T is also seeking variations from the maximum permitted height,
side and rear yard setbacks. Public hearings were held on this request as well
as SprintCom’s on August 20 and September 14 and the Zoning Board of Appeals
recommended to the Village Council by a vote of 5-0 that they grant the special
use permit and variations, with the condition that the test findings of Dr.
Taflove support the projected data of AT & T.
Trustee Meuer moved to adopt this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lien.
Ms. Janega said that at the last council meeting, the Council noticed that the
drawings supplied by AT & T Wireless did not include the change to the roof
mounted from the original proposal of wall mounted antennas. She said that this
drawing was the only outstanding issue for AT & T, has since been provided, and
has been circulated with the agenda materials.. The conclusion reach by Dr.
Taflove was that the actual field testing done by AT & T was dead on with their
projections and their propagation projections did factor in the trees on signal
strength. The data submitted by AT & T supported their position that it is a
matter of engineering necessity to have the antennas in the Hubbard Woods area.
President Holland said that the main concern is the look of the antennas on the
building and that they extend slightly above the roof. They understand the
necessity of the location of the site.
Brian Barrett, representing AT & T Wireless, referred the Council to drawing A-
3, the exterior elevation of the building. He said the penthouse mounts at the
top of the coping are 48 feet 4 inches, and the top of the antenna is 50 feet,
which is a difference of 1 foot, 8 inches. The antennas they are using are 13.7
inches in height and are 6 inches wide. They do not require the larger antennas
and the only clearance they need above the coping is 6 inches to avoid
interference from the coping itself. They have to roof mount due to
restrictions on the encroachment of the neighbors’ property. He referred to
drawing A-2, the western and southerly property lines that the building sits
on. He said the initial proposal was to side mount the antennas, but because of
property lines and because they were unable to come to an agreement with the
neighbor, they were forced to put the antennas on the roof. He proposed that
the mountings they are suggesting are better looking than the wall mounts. He
said that if you were looking at the antennas from the ground, the six inch
height would be hidden. The third sector to the east is different, 1 foot 7
inches, and moved closer to the edge of the coping to use the peak to the south
and to the north to hide the antennas. He said that when driving north that
sector of antennas won’t be seen and from the ground they will be virtually
invisible.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1439-98 – McKinven Subdivision, 642 and 656 Maple Street -
Adoption 
This request for a proposed subdivision will reconfigure two existing
irregularly shaped lots. The Plan Commission at its October 1 meeting voted 10-
0 to approve the subdivision and also granted a variation from the subdivision
ordinance to permit a side lot line between the two lots which is not entirely
perpendicular to the street. They also found that the existing nonconforming
setbacks would have no adverse impact as a result of the proposed subdivision.
Plan Commission approval was granted subject to modifications of the plat and
that the current garage at 642 Maple be demolished prior to recording the plat
of subdivision.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt this resolution, seconded by Trustee Lanphier.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
President Holland said that at this time they would move to the Unfinished
Business agenda items.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Bertling Lane Cul-de-sac Experiment. Manager Williams reviewed the experiment
that temporarily made Bertling Lane a cul-de-sac following changes requested to
improve traffic and pedestrian flow as a result of discussions last spring
during the New Trier High School hearings for a special use permit for
expansion to accommodate an increased enrollment. Barricades were placed on
Bertling Lane in late August for a two-month experiment.
Steve Saunders said that as part of the debate on the New Trier expansion, the
high school hired a consultant to do a traffic study. He said there was a
considerable amount of comments from the neighborhood at the public meetings.
He said that the Village staff also did observations and collected data and the
traffic count showed 5 cars exiting Bertling Lane during the peak morning hour
and 20 cars during the peak afternoon hour. From his observations of the
intersection, it was primarily in the afternoon that cars would circle Indian
Hill Park and primarily driven by students. That, combined with existing narrow
pavement, the right of way there is only 30 feet wide and the two sidewalks are
four feet wide which is less than standard, led to the closing of Bertling Lane
as an experiment. The results of the experiment are that phone responses were
8-6 opposed to the cul de sac and written responses 16-2 in favor of cul de
sac. Concern was expressed about emergency access, garbage truck access and
other comments were that it was quieter on the street and there was less
traffic. Mr. Saunders said the design of the permanent cul de sac would be a
half-T cul de sac. The turn around area for the cul de sac would be on the
public right of way, but would be in Indian Hill Park. It would be designed
with lower curbs to facilitate emergency access. There would also be minimal
landscaping. The cost estimate is $17,500, plus any landscaping. Mr. Saunders
said that if the pros and cons were weighed, the benefits are reduced through
traffic which would almost entirely eliminate outside traffic, increased
pedestrian safety as a consequence of the reduced traffic, and the cul de sac
creates a quieter, more peaceful neighborhood. The drawbacks are that it would
increase Village service and maintenance costs. It is easier to plow snow and
pick up leaves on through streets, although it is not impossible. As an
engineer the construction of the turn around is not perfect, but there is not
the right of way to do a perfect turn around. The design will handle most
vehicles, but not all. The residents would only have one exit, and there is the
possibility of traffic increasing on other streets. Mr. Saunders said that one
of the residents reported increased traffic on Myrtle. He was unable to verify
this because there were no traffic counts done on Myrtle Street from the New
Trier study before the cul de saccing. Another drawback is that from the
neighborhood to the north, if you want to go east, the left turn on Wilson is
more difficult than from Bertling Lane. That, combined with the hill, limits
the sight distance. There are other issues such as emergency access, garbage
and recycling collection. Although scooters would pick up the garbage, a
recycling truck would have to back up. Other streets may request cul de sacs
for similar reasons and if we were to proceed with this, Mr. Saunders would
like to develop standards stating why this would be implemented. He said that
as Director of Public Works he would like to see fewer cul de sacs because they
are more difficult to maintain.
Trustee Derning asked about emergency vehicles accessibility.
Mr. Saunders said that the curbs would be about three inches high and that in
the future there would be a grass block put in that most vehicles could go
over.
Trustee Lanphier asked if it would be enough to discourage reckless drivers.
Mr. Saunders said that a utility sport vehicle could certainly go over the
curb.
Trustee Lanphier asked about a gate that could be closed.
Mr. Saunders said that the only gate in the Village now is on Hill Terrace. His
concern with a gate would be having a clear authority on who would be opening
and closing the gate and there is also a liability question. He wouldn’t
recommend gating the street because of the possibility of accidents.
President Holland asked Chief Colpaert and Deputy Chief Bennett to address the
exit and entrance to Bertling Lane.
Chief Colpaert said that the low curb does not prohibit the fire department
from access.
An audience member asked if the access was blocked, would their first option be
to go through Indian Hill.
Deputy Chief Bennett said that the police department has looked at the
situation and does not feel that closing the street would impact their ability
to respond to that area.
Mr. Williams said that staff would recommend whatever the Council decides. The
staff does recommend that they remove the temporary barricades at the end of
the month to be able to plow snow and so that the fire department can access
it. If Council chooses to go forward with this, although the turnaround would
be on Village right of way, the Park District should be included in a
discussion since it is in the park.
Trustee Lanpher asked about parking restrictions on Bertling and Hill.
Deputy Chief Bennett said that kids congregate in the park and the cul de sac
would limit access to larger vehicles and they would look at changing the
parking restrictions if cul de sac goes in.
Mr. Williams said that staff would monitor and take care of any parking
problems that arise.
Thomas Mathers, 115 Bertling, and his daughter, spoke against the cul de sac
stating that there aren’t that many cars and because cars from the north will
have to turnaround in a driveway or back all the way down the street there
would be an increase in accident possibilities. Mr. Matthers also said in his
letter to the Council that he believes the cost of $17,500 would be assessed to
each resident.
Mr. Williams said that the cost would not be assessed to residents of Bertling
Lane, but would be paid by the Village so that the cost would be from every
Winnetka resident.
Mollybelle Berish said that she is concerned that the money for this would be
taken from the budget. She would rather it be spent on sidewalks or to a better
use. She suggested the Village spend their money more wisely.
Jan Gaines, 107 Bertling, spoke in favor of the cul de sac stating that the cul
de sac has kept the kids off of the street and has made Bertling safer.
Another resident of Bertling Lane also spoke in favor of the cul de sac. She
said that within two tenths of a mile there are 38 children, 18 of whom are
under 7 years old and people used to drive down the street very fast. She asked
if the issue is a question of inconvenience or saving lives of little people
and hopes that the Council will make a decision for the children and people
living there.
Mike Hedge, 139 Bertling Lane, said the cul de sac is an inconvenience for him
but well worth it to avoid accidents and also stated the number of children
living on the street. He suggested that the no left turn sign could be
eliminated.
Terry Duffy, 159 Bertling, said that he thinks that Wilson can handle the
traffic rather than the narrower Berling Lane. He suggested that as people get
use to the cul de sac on Bertling, they will find other routes. He said he
lives in the second house from the stop sign and if half the people stop at the
sign, he would be surprised. He asked the Council to take into consideration
all of these things.
Marlene Tesar, 146 Bertling, said that a few streets are severely affected by
the thousands of students and because of that feels they are owed special
consideration.
Elaine Kruemplestaedter, 178 Myrtle, suggested a gate that could be opened and
closed by police as an alternative or by the paraprofessionals who are at that
corner every morning. She said that twenty-five cars does not seem like
thousands of teenagers going and coming from New Trier.
Ms. Gaines said that the paraprofessionals watched the high school students
tear down the gate in the park and cannot monitor the kids. The kids would pull
down a gate there and she does not think it is a viable solution.
Jim Kruempelstaedter, 178 Myrtle, spoke against the cul de sac and objected to
the lack of notice when the barricades were put up. He said he is not against
doing something to control the traffic problem but thinks it is excessive to do
it 365 days a year 24 hours a day when there are times when people should be
able to drive on Bertling Lane. He also presented a petition from 51 residents
that live north and east of Winnetka Avenue and a petition from 295 high school
students complaining about the closing of Bertling Lane and the walkway. He
suggested there may be other means to regulate traffic, such as placing
paraprofessionals at the corner with a van or a police car that could be pulled
across to block the street. He urged the Council to be cautious that what they
do with the closing on a permanent basis is setting a precedent for the future.
Mr. Kruempelstaedter said he also had a petition signed by 75% of the people on
Myrtle requesting the closing of their street for the same reasons that
Bertling Lane is to be closed.. He said the Village’s studies on pedestrian
traffic said that a minimum of 150 high school students use the walkway between
Woodland Ave and Bertling Lane and believes closing the walkway will penalize
the students.
Mary Lawler, 471 Hill Rd, said that there had never been an accident involving
a child between Hill and Bertling and they believe they are supporting their
children in a safety matter.
Trustee Lanphier asked if speed bumps have been considered.
Mr. Williams said they are illegal by fire code and it has been shown that
there are no satisfactory results.
Trustee Lanphier suggested that they need to think carefully about the street
grid because it is important. She sees that there may be an impact in terms of
quality of life on the street.
Trustee Meuer said that if he were living on Bertling he would want a cul de
sac too, but the issue of precedent setting is what concerns him. It is easy to
make a case around the school and parks. He said he doesn’t disagree with
residents on Bertling but as a trustee he has difficulties because of the next
group that comes in for the same reasons.
President Holland said that this cul de sac came out of studies made by Mr.
Saunders on how to alleviate traffic in this neighborhood
Trustee Meuer said there was a time when we didn’t want to use stop signs to
control traffic, and one of the things they have heard is that people are
coasting through stop signs. He agrees with the neighbors, but there is a great
deal of traffic on Hibbard Road, too.
Trustee Hilton said that even though that is a factor it should not be a reason
not to deal with it and maybe cul de sacs should be considered on other
streets.
Trustee Lanphier said that a mitigating factor with this road is that it is so
narrow and cannot accept the traffic and that makes this a different situation.
It does force traffic onto another street and has impact on surrounding
neighborhoods
Trustee Lien said that he sympathizes with the people on Bertling but sees
Trustee Meuer’s concern also. He lives on Pine Street where there are 1800 cars
a day, or about 100 cars an hour and would love to see it made into a series of
cul de sacs. He doesn’t think a clear case has been made. He has more concern
about the large vehicles backing out of the street and thinks that would be a
greater inconvenience to the Village for snow removal, trash pick up,
maintenance, etc. and agrees that it would not be a good idea. He said that it
also takes away park land. He does not favor making Bertling a cul de sac.
Trustee Duhl said that originally this experiment was done to improve traffic
around New Trier. He hasn’t heard whether it has improved the traffic there or
not. He said it has helped the people on Bertling. There was a speed bump
discussion last year and it was concluded that it is not a good idea. He is
concerned about closing off the street and about the petition from the
residents on Myrtle. He said he is not prepared to vote to continue this
experiment and would like to see if there is something short of this as a
solution. Not convinced experiment fulfilled request to improve traffic
situation around New Trier.
President Holland said that they had wanted to keep the cars from going into
the neighborhoods and to keep them on the main street.
Trustee Lien said that has not been demonstrated.
Trustee Duhl asked if that has produced a significant benefit to the New Trier
neighborhood.
Mr. Saunders said that the traffic recommendations in his report in the spring
were outlined in three broad areas, traffic congestion, pedestrian safety, and
neighborhood concerns. The cul de sac was under neighborhood concerns. He said
that nothing done on Bertling Lane is going to make a difference on Winnetka
Avenue. His comment on the gate was that, if there was any sort of temporary
blockage at one end or the other, there would still be some sort of turn around
needed.
Trustee Meuer said that at National Louis in Evanston, there are signs that say
residents only are permitted to enter the street – He asked if some
consideration could be given to that on certain streets. He suggested posting
that cars could not enter from the north and south and with residents entering
from one way during school hours. He said this appears to be working at
Washburne. He said that maybe New Trier needs to give them more information in
terms of parking.
Trustee Duhl said that they have a request from people on Myrtle and feels
uncomfortable making decision on Bertling.
Mr. Williams said staff did these recommendations based on Bertling because
they could see the cul de sac and still keep traffic on major roads. They had
even talked about cul de sacing Woodland, but on Bertling and Fuller with the
narrow lanes, the traffic could be kept on Wilson and Sheridan, etc. If the cul
de sac was done, you would not be forcing residential traffic on Bertling or
Myrtle. He said that there had been a similar request at Chestnut and Willow
and a lot of time was spent on that because traffic would just shift over one
residential street. In staff’s mind, they felt there might be a way to cul de
sac and force traffic to major streets. He said that the thought was that maybe
there is a way to minimize some of the impact but he can’t begin to predict all
of the consequences.
Trustee Meuer said that he believed that an outline should be developed as to
what should go into a cul de sac for consideration. The staff can then let
residents know if a particular street meets them.
Trustee Derning was concerned about safety issues, the recycling truck on that
street trying to maneuver its way back out, about precedent in the absence of
standards, and sees a series of repercussions down the road. He suggested
putting this on the table for a bit in order to look at alternatives and
suggested the possibility of one way streets.
President Holland said that this could be tabled until after the first of the
year to consider standards and other options. She said they will gather more
information, but the barricades have to be removed by November 1. There would
be no construction until Spring. She asked for a consensus of the Council to
table this until after first of the year. It was the consensus to table this
item.
Experimental Closures of Pedestrian Path between Bertling Lane and Woodland
Avenue.
Manager Williams reviewed this experiment to change pedestrian flows and reduce
the impact of New Trier High School on its neighborhoods, to temporarily block
off the pedestrian path from Bertling Lane to Woodland Avenue. As a result of
the analyses made for this experiment, public input and policy direction is
needed.
President Holland said that Mr. Saunders had said that the path can remain
temporarily closed. Trustee Duhl suggested that this could be put over to next
meeting. Trustee Meuer asked for more information from New Trier. Trustee
Hilton said that it was not necessary to wait until they discuss the cul de sac
issue again. Mr. Williams requested that the short path discussion be at the
November 17 meeting because of the many agenda items on the November 5 agenda.
Robert Cannon, 170 Woodland, asked if testimony would still be taken at the
November 17 meeting because he is affected by the closure of the short path.
Ordinance M-558-98 – Sale of Surplus Electric Equipment – Introduction
This ordinance will formally authorize the disposal of distribution
transformers that are no longer needed by the Water & Electric Department and
also those that are no longer needed during the current fiscal year. Disposal
is recommended either by sending them out to bid for sale to the highest bidder
or by bid for disposal.
Trustee Meuer moved to introduce this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lanphier.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustee Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance MC-213-98 – Additional Class A Liquor License (Tai Fuko Restaurant) –
Introduction
Manager Williams reviewed this request for a liquor license for the Tai Fuko
Japanese Restaurant at 1062 Gage Street.
Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce this ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1097 and 1098. Trustee Lien reported that all of his
questions had been answered satisfactorily and moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1097 in the total amount of $236,277.10 and Warrant List No. 1098 in the
total amount of $344,268.47. Trustee Lanphier seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Lien, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Quarterly Report: Capital Projects. Manager Williams discussed the status
reports of the capital improvement projects from the various Village
departments. He said that the street resurfacing and rehabilitation program is
under control and nearly wrapped up. He also said that the project of the
traffic signal at Willow and Hibbard had been frustrating because of a six week
delay due to a change of standards by IDOT. He reported that staff is ready to
go to underground the electric lines on Green Bay Rd., north of Pine, and are
just waiting for the contractor.
Report: Leafblower Enforcement. Manager Williams reviewed the enforcement
activity for leafblower violations that occurred during the months of June,
July, August and September. The annual report shows100 tickets for violations,
and 76 complaints, 18 of which resulted in tickets. There were 82 complaints
initiated by the police officers who are still getting challenges in terms of
enforcing this. Mr. Williams believes there will be more and more compliance as
enforcement continues. Trustee Lanphier said that she had gone around her
neighborhood and informed some people that they were not allowed to use them
and it has worked.
Monthly Compost Report. Manager Williams reviewed the monthly report from DK
Recycling Systems, Inc.
X. Reports.
Village President.
Village Trustees.
Trustee Derning reported that he had attended the Chamber of Commerce meeting.
They discussed the Winnetka Vision Statement and were concerned that there was
no mention of the business community, so they may be bringing that up. He said
that one suggestion put forth was the noted lack of public restrooms in the
business district and that person was invited to get more detail and bring it
back.
Village Attorney.
Ms. Janega reported that she had received an order from the Illinois Supreme
Court a week ago who denied the Harper’s Petition for Leave to Appeal. She said
this means that the dismissal obtained by the Village has been sustained by the
Supreme Court. She believes the Village will be in a position in a month or so
to move this case along.
Village Manager.
Mr. Williams reported that at the last meeting a zoning variation had been
requested at 512 Orchard, and the Council had asked for a revision to the plan.
He said the staff did not receive the revised plan and that is why it is not on
tonight’s agenda.
PCB Issues New Compost Rules. Manager Williams reported that the Illinois
Pollution Control Board has issued a final decision, modifying the rules on the
siting of compost sites. For facilities developed or expanded after January 1,
1999, a setback of 1/8 mile must be maintained from health care, preschool and
child care, primary and secondary school facilities and adjacent recreation
areas used by the schools. Mr. Williams said that the new rules will not impact
Winnetka because they do not apply to existing sites and only to adjacent
recreation areas.
Water Allocation Summary. Manager Williams reported that the new allocations
for water are set and the Village can use up to 115% of its allocation in a
year or average 105% above its allocation over a 5 year running period without
being in violation of State rules.
XI. Appointments.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 11:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:45 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
11:46 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
November 5, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved November 17, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 5, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and Bill
Meuer. Absent: Trustee Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about 14 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that at the request of the applicant consideration
of Item 7(F) had been postponed until November 17, 1998. The Council then
approved the Agenda as amended.
IV. Quorum.
November 10, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present except Trustee
Duhl indicated that they would be able to attend. Trustee Lien indicated that
he expected to be late arriving.
November 17, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present except Trustee
Duhl indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
October 20, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-558-98 – Sale of Surplus Electric Equipment - Adoption. Trustee
Meuer moved to adopt this ordinance authorizing the disposal of distribution
transformers that are no longer needed by the Water & Electric Department and
giving the Department umbrella authorization to dispose of any other
distribution transformers that are retired during the current fiscal year. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Lanphier.
By roll call vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Lien,
Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. MC-213-98 – Adds Additional Class A Liquor License for Tai Fuko
Restaurant – Adoption. The motion to adopt was made by Trustee Meuer, seconded
by Trustee Lien.
By roll call vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Lien,
Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. M-542-98 – Zoning Variation: 512 Orchard - Adoption. President
Holland asked Ms. McCammack to explain the changes which had been made to the
proposed plans in order to bring the project more into conformance with
ordinance standards. Ms. McCammack responded that the height of the garage had
been reduced to make it compliant with the height standard of 14 feet and that
all of the attic GFA has been eliminated. She noted that there was still a
slight net increase in GFA, but that it was equal to the increase in lot
coverage.
Trustee Lanphier asked for an explanation of the increased GFA. The applicant,
Janet Wyman, provided the requested information.
President Holland called for a motion to amend the ordinance to incorporate the
proposed changes. Trustee Lanphier so moved, seconded by Trustee Derning. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Lanphier and President Holland each thanked the applicant for changing
their plans.
Trustee Duhl expressed his opinion that internal structural changes ought not
to make a difference in the GFA, and that, therefore, the Council ought to
reexamine the GFA concept. Ms. McCammack indicated that Staff shared this
concern and said that she expects recommendations regarding the GFA issue from
the Village’s consultants, Thompson Dyke & Associates. Mr. Duhl continued
saying that although this instance is a de minimus request, overall he doesn’t
see justification for GFA exceptions. He noted that the Zoning Ordinance is
aimed at controlling visual bulk, which is not affected by an internal change
such as the one employed here.
Trustee Derning pointed out that the interior changes were not made to benefit
the application but to conform to the ordinance.
Trustee Lanphier asked the applicant to explain why further adjustments
couldn’t be made to bring the building into complete compliance. After Mrs.
Wyman answered, Ms. Lanphier commented that she thought that it was important
to draw the distinction here between an accessory building and home per se. She
also pointed out that the Village encourages the use of two garage doors as
proposed here, rather than one, and that that is the design feature the
applicants are trying to accommodate with their plan.
Trustee Derning moved to adopt this ordinance permitting an increase in
building size (GFA) and roofed and total impermeable lot coverage. His motion
was seconded by Trustee Meuer.
By roll call vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. M-550-98 – Zoning Variation: 575 Sheridan Road - Introduction.
Ms. McCammack explained this request for a variation to permit an increase in
building size (GFA). She pointed out that the residence already exceeds the
permitted GFA by 1,926.87 square feet, and that the demolition and addition
proposed will contribute an additional 132 square feet to the existing GFA
making the property 31.13% above the permitted GFA. Ms. McCammack noted that
the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-1 to recommend granting this variation
as requested.
Trustee Lien moved to introduce this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lanphier.
After asking several questions of the applicant, the Trustees took a voice
vote, and the motion passed unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-551-98 – Zoning Variation: 687 Cherry Street – Introduction.
President Holland announced that although there had been a request to waive the
first reading of this ordinance, the Council would not be considering that
request. She then asked Ms. McCammack to review the application for a variation
permitting an increased building size (GFA), intensity of use of lot (roofed
and impermeable lot coverages) and a reduced side yard setback. She said that
all of the requested variances concern existing nonconformities. The zoning
relief is being sought to permit the retrofitting of an existing attached three
car garage, removal of the second floor of an existing two-story staircase, and
reconstruction of the remaining first floor area. The proposed changes will
result in a 27.34 square foot increase to the existing nonconforming Roofed and
Total Impermeable Lot Coverages. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 6-0 to recommend granting this request.
Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce the ordinance as drafted. Her motion was
seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-552-98 – Zoning Variation: 380 Poplar – Introduction. As
previously announced by President Holland, consideration of this item was
postponed at the request of the applicant until November 17, 1998.
Ordinance No. M-553 - Zoning Variation: 1138 Scott Ave. – Introduction.
President Holland announced that although there had been a request to waive the
first reading of this ordinance, the Council would not be considering that
request. She then asked Ms. McCammack to review this application for a reduced
side yard setback in order to permit conversion of an attached garage into a
sunroom and remodeling of the existing kitchen as well as the addition of a new
deck. She pointed out that these changes would not change the existing
nonconforming Lot Coverage and GFA and that there would be no increase to the
building’s foot print. The Zoning Board of Appeals voted 5-0 to recommend
granting this request.
Trustee Lanphier then moved to introduce the ordinance as drafted. Her motion
was seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-554-98 – Zoning Variation: 323 Locust – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack opened her comments by thanking the Village Attorney for all of her
work in drafting the numerous proposed ordinances. She then explained this
request for a reduced average front yard setback in order to construct an
enclosed front entry with an associated roofed entry feature. She noted that
the applicants had agreed to revise their plans for the roofed entry to bring
it into compliance with permitted encroachment provisions and that plan
revisions would be required prior to issuing a building permit. Ms. McCammack
said that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to recommend grating this
variation, subject to compliance with the setback provisions by the covered
entry.
Trustee Meuer moved to introduce this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lien.
Trustee Duhl asked for clarification of the plans and summarized his
understanding that for the most part this is an extension of an existing
encroachment rather than the addition of a new encroachment and that the Zoning
Ordinance does not preclude such an extension of an already existing violation.
Ms. McCammack and Ms. Janega responded in the affirmative.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-555-98 – Zoning Variation: 1045 Pine Street – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed this request to permit an increase to the existing
nonconforming building size (GFA) in order for the applicants to construct two,
one-story additions on the rear side of the house. One addition involves moving
the garage doors; the other proposes to remodeling the existing garage space
into a family room. These additions will result in an increase above the
maximum permitted GFA of 456.34 square feet. She noted that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted on the additions separately, 5-0 in favor of the first
addition and 4-1 in favor of the second.
After hearing from Ms. McCammack and the applicant, Mr. Duhl expressed his
concerns about granting a variation for the reasons stated. He said that he
would vote in favor of introduction but could not be counted on to vote in
favor of adoption.
By voice vote, the motion to introduce carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-556-98 – Zoning Variation: 832 Foxdale – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack explained this request for permission to retain a 60.00 square foot
attached room addition, used as a storage shed, which was installed without the
required permits, to remain on the property in violation of the GFA standards.
She also reviewed the history of Council actions on this property. She pointed
out that the “shed” in question represents a 30.38 square foot, or 1.18%,
increase from the maximum permitted building size, but results in a Gross Floor
Area Ratio of 51.1%, which is in violation of the Building Size provisions of
the Code. Ms. McCammack noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-1 to
recommend Council’s approval.
Trustee Meuer moved to introduce this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Lien.
Trustee Derning inquired whether the shed in question would be approved if it
were a stand alone shed, removed from the building proper. Ms. McCammack stated
that it would not.
Trustee Lanphier asked the applicant to explain how this situation had
occurred.
Mr. Charatz indicated that it had resulted from a misunderstanding of the
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance.
Trustee Duhl asked whether the shed had been a part of the original expansion
plans. Mr. Charatz responded that it had not; it was added as the project
progressed. He indicated that he had discovered that he needed some storage on
the property and thought that the addition of an attached shed, rather than a
free standing one which he understood would be permitted, would be more
visually pleasing because it creates more open space. He added that his
neighbors have no objections to this addition.
Trustee Lanphier expressed her reservations given Council’s previous extensive
deliberations about Foxdale properties.
Mr. Charatz pointed out that those previous decisions did not preclude him from
seeking a variance.
By voice vote, the motion to introduce carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-557-98 – Randolph Street Vacation – Introduction. Manager
Williams briefly reviewed the history of this issue. He explained that this
ordinance vacates the remaining 24 feet of the Randolph Street right-of-way
south of Asbury Avenue and provides for the owner of the property abutting the
right-of-way to the west, at 1330 Asbury, pay the Village the value of the
vacated property. The value of the property in question has been established at
$154,000. He noted that as a result of the acquisition of this property, the
formerly undersized property at of 1330 Asbury would become only about one foot
wider than the lot immediately to the east of the right-of-way, thereby
ensuring greater conformity with that lot and providing greater consistency in
lot sizes in the immediate area. In addition, both abutting lots will no longer
be classified as corner lots.
Mr. Dick Busscher, the owner of the property at 1330 Asbury, raised some minor
questions regarding the proposed agreement to which Attorney Janega responded.
Mr. Sturgis Chadwick, the owner of the other abutting lot commented on
Council’s actions.
Responding to Mr. Chadwick’s comments, Trustees Lanphier and Duhl pointed out
that the Council’s overriding consideration had been the creation of lots of
equal size which is in the best interests of both the neighborhood and the
Village.
President Holland supported Council’s decision saying that it was a reflection
of good city planning.
Trustee Lien moved to introduce this ordinance, seconded by Trustee Derning. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. MC-212-98 – Vehicle Impoundment Procedure Amendments –
Introduction. Manager Williams explained recommended changes to the existing
provisions of the Village Code designed to close an existing loophole and
clarify that responsibility for paying accrued parking fines, impoundment and
removal fees attaches to the license plate, rather than to the vehicle itself.
After discussing the issue, Trustee Duhl suggested that, if possible, the
proposed ordinance amendment be changed to indicate that responsibility for
fines and fees attach to the owner or lessee of a vehicle.
The motion to introduce the ordinance was made by Trustee Derning and seconded
by Trustee Meuer. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ordinance No. MC-209-98 – Sign Code Amendments – Introduction and Public
Hearing. 
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning moved to introduce the proposed
changes to the Sign Code.
President Holland opened the Public Hearing and asked Ms. McCammack to
comment on the revisions to the Village’s Sign Code. Ms. McCammack stated that
this comprehensive rewrite and reorganization of the Sign Code makes it more
user friendly. She noted that the changes clarify the intent and applicability
of the regulations, expand definitions to include previously undefined terms,
and establish new limitations on the type, size and appearance of signs in
addition to clarifying the limitations on those signs which are exempted from
the Sign Code. She said that the draft is essentially the same as the
Discussion Draft previously considered by Council, except that, as directed by
Council after that discussion, the amortization provisions had been removed.
Manager Williams and Attorney Janega highlighted some of the revisions and
responded to specific questions from Trustees.
Ms. McCammack pointed out that in order to avoid perpetuating nonconforming
signs, the applicable provisions have been patterned after similar Zoning
Ordinance provisions regarding nonconformities explicitly defining the extent
to which nonconforming signs can be maintained and repaired. She commented that
the underlying philosophy of the nonconformities provision is to allow the
natural life span of the nonconforming sign to determine when it is to be
replaced by a conforming sign. Manager Williams commented that this was a
kinder and gentler approach to nonconforming signs.
Trustee Lanphier raised a question regarding “Sale” signs placed in store
windows, and possible remedies were discussed.
Ms. Pat Horn, owner of Multiple Choices and a Director of the Chamber of
Commerce, endorsed the gentle approach to sign enforcement. She suggested that
the Chamber might host a seminar on window display and that directors might fax
comments about offenses directly to the Community Development Department. She
indicated that certainly some enforcement of violations would be appreciated.
Ms. Eve Breman, President of the Chamber of Commerce, represented that the
Chamber supported direction from the Council with regard to signs, but that
regulation needs to be encouraging to merchants. She urged the Council not to
be so restrictive as to make it discouraging to business owners who otherwise
might want to locate in the Village.
Trustee Lanphier asked Ms. Breman if the proposed changes were too strict. Ms.
Breman replied that they were not, but reiterated the need for a friendly,
flexible approach to enforcement.
President Holland closed the Public Hearing and called for a vote on the
motion.
By voice vote, the motion to introduce carried unanimously.
Property Taxes. Mr. McKee reviewed the Agenda Report on the Property Tax Levy
and Public Safety Building Bonds. He noted that existing property tax payers
will see a 3.2% increase and that new development will be taxed at a rate
similar to existing taxpayers. He said that the property tax revenue from new
development will go into the General Fund to pay for increased operating costs.
Ordinance No. M-547-98 – 1998 Property Tax Levy – Introduction. Trustee
Lanphier moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-547-98, seconded by Trustee Duhl.
By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-548-98 – 1994 Public Safety Building Bonds – Introduction.
Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-547-98, seconded by Trustee
Derning. By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-549-98 – 1996 Public Safety Building Bonds – Introduction.
Trustee Lanphier moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-547-98, seconded by Trustee
Meuer. By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business
IX. New Business
Zoning Variation: Winnetka Galleria, 562-572 Lincoln – Policy Direction. Ms.
McCammack explained the request for permission to increase the building height
of the clock tower to 40.50 feet, instead of the maximum permitted height of 35
feet. In doing so, she also reviewed the history of actions taken by Village
Boards with regard to the project. Ms. McCammack pointed out that the
noncompliant feature is an architectural feature and not one that screens
mechanical elements. She noted that the vote on the question by the Zoning
Board of Appeals had been 2-3, and that without an affirmative vote by the
majority, no recommendation was given to the Council.
President Holland invited Mr. Cummins and Mr. Myefski to comment on their
request and respond to questions from the Trustees. Using a model and
elevations, Mr. Myefski discussed possible alternatives and expressed his
belief that the requested variation serves a public purpose and public good in
that the desired element makes the best sense architecturally and is more
esthetically pleasing than any of the available options.
Trustee Lien stated his strong support for the variation, saying that the clock
tower is not only a desirable design feature but also serves to mask the
elevator tower and equipment.
Trustee Lanphier expressed her opinion that since this building is new
construction, a conforming alternative ought to be employed.
Trustee Duhl supported Mr. Lien’s position saying that in this instance the
rule doesn’t make sense and that he was prepared to vote in favor of the
variation.
Trustee Derning said that initially he had been disinclined to vote in favor of
the variation, but that after seeing the reality of the situation, he would
support it because it significantly enhances the esthetic compatibility of the
structure.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to direct the Village Attorney to
prepare an ordinance granting the requested variation. By voice vote, the
motion carried with all of the Trustees present voting in favor, except Trustee
Lanphier.
Web Page Posting Policy. Manager Williams reported that the Village expects to
post its web page on the Internet in the near future. The page will provide
important information to and about the community. He expressed his belief that
this will be yet another means of opening up Village Hall and the legislative
process to residents. He asked for policy direction from the Council regarding
the type of information that will be posted on the “front page,” indicating
that Staff recommends that information which is being considered by boards and
committees to establish policy be included. Mr. Williams also suggested that
the Council appoint one of its members as liaison with Staff to review the
information posted.
Trustee Duhl moved that the front page include pending policy issues which are
being considered by various Village boards and that Trustee Lien act as
liaison. His motion was seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Warrant Lists Nos. 1099 and 1100. Trustee Lien reported that all of his
questions had been answered satisfactorily and moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1099 in the total amount of $491,435.00 and Warrant List No. 1092 in the
total amount of $575,030.56. Trustee Derning seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning,
Duhl, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Street Change Order. Manager Williams reported that the final cost of the 1998
Street Reconstruction Program was $627,043.90, $52,212.87 over the contract
award of $574,831.03. He cited the areas to which the cost increases could be
attributed and recommended approval of the authorization.
Trustee Lanphier moved to authorize the expenditure of an additional $52,212.87
to cover the final cost of the Program. She was seconded by Trustee Lien. By
roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer,
Lien, Duhl and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Rock Salt. Mr. Williams noted that it is estimated that the Village will need
to purchase 1,075 tons of rock salt for use during the coming winter season. He
explained that the Village participates in the purchase of rock salt through
the State of Illinois bid and that the successful bidder had been IMC/North
American Salt Co. at $27.92/ton. He pointed out that the FY 98/99 Budget
contained a total of $30,000 for this purchase.
Trustee Derning moved to authorize the purchase of rock salt from North
American Salt Co. at $27.92/ton. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By
roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Scada Fiber Optic Communications Equipment. Mr. Williams reported on the bids
solicited for the purchase of Scada Fiber Optic Communications Equipment used
to collect and transmit data from all substations to Village Hall and the
electric Plant. He stated that both Staff and the project consultant
recommended award of the bid to Northern Telecom Utility Networks (“Nortel”) in
the total amount of $77,784 for purchase of the equipment with an additional
cost of “not to exceed” $3,330 for field support services.
Trustee Meuer moved to award the bid as recommended, seconded by Trustee Lien.
By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Lien,
Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Bid No. 98-029 – Water Treatment Plant Improvements. Mr. Williams reported on
the bids received for the labor, materials and equipment necessary to effect
the removal and replacement of High Lift Pump #1, for which the FY 98/99
contains $120,000 to cover the costs. He stated that both Staff and the project
consultant recommended award of the bid to Independent Mechanical Industries in
the total of $103,800.
Trustee Meuer moved to award the bid as recommended, seconded by Trustee Lien.
By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Lien,
Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
X. Reports.
Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported that the new traffic signal at Hibbard
and Willow Roads had been activated and that it works.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland announced that Tom Hermes was retiring as Chairman of he
Forestry Commission. She indicated that Jim Fox would be moving up to occupy
that position. She also indicated reappointment of Liz Crowe and Loretta Speis
to second terms on the committee and appointment of Mary Robbins to a first
term. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the
appointments as proposed by President Holland. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl,Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Hilton. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:05 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:06 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
November 10, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved November 17, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 10, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7: 38 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Penfield Lanphier, David Hilton and William Meuer.
Absent: Trustees Michael Duhl and John Lien. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Police Chief William
Gallagher, Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack and about three
persons in the audience.
II. Liquor License Application: Class A-1 – Espresso Pacifico.
Manager Williams briefly reviewed the application from Espresso Pacifico for a
new Class A-1 Liquor License. He then introduced the applicant, Barbara
Horowitz, who discussed the specifics of her plan with the Trustees.
By consensus the Trustees instructed Mr. Williams to have the Village Attorney
draft the appropriate ordinance and joined President Holland in wishing Ms.
Horowitz good luck with her new venture.
III. Storage of Plowed Snow.
Mr. Saunders explained the Village’s current policy regarding the storage of
plowed snow, which is to haul it out of the business districts to remote
locations in order to improve the appearance of the district and conserve
parking spaces. Historically, snow has been hauled to Duke Child Field,
however, with the recent re-paving of this lot, the High School District has
indicated that this location will no longer be available to the Village except
in extreme emergency. The School District has been working very hard to
beautify the area, and the storage of snow would not only damage the new
pavement, it would also endanger the new trees, shrubs and grass which have
been planted.
Mr. Saunders then reviewed other potentially available options, outlining their
advantages and disadvantages, and asked for guidance from the Council as to
which would be most acceptable.
   Manager Williams pointed out that the options for snow storage were becoming
more and more limited.
   After discussing the available options, the Council concluded that
conditions permitting, it would like to see snow distributed over several of
the possible locations in order to minimize the impact on any one site.
IV. Possible Distributor Pump Resale.
Mr. Williams added this item to the Agenda, asking Mr. Saunders to explain to
the Trustees the possible opportunity to sell the distributor pumps originally
purchased for the Skokie River Pump Station. Mr. Saunders said that he had been
approached by a potential buyer for the pumps and needed Council’s approval to
pursue the option. By consensus the Trustees agreed that Mr. Saunders should
continue to do so.
   Trustee Lien arrived at 8:15 p.m.
V. Public Comment.
   MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Lane, brought two issues before the Council
for consideration: (1) the need for licensing cats as well as dogs, and (2) the
imposition of a fine for piling leaves in the street rather than on the
parkway, as required by ordinance. With regard to the former, she cited the
need for cats to be properly inoculated against diseases. In support of the
latter, she said that leaves in the street create a narrowed passageway, are
slippery when wet, entice children to play in the streets, and clog drains.
   After briefly discussing both issues, Mr. Williams indicated that he would
take them to Staff for consideration.
IV. DARE Report.
Chief Gallagher reviewed the Police Department’s report on the DARE program. He
indicated that despite some negative publicity about the program nationwide, he
felt the program was doing a good job for Winnetka. He cited the fact that the
program helps youngsters to establish a nonadversarial relationship with the
Police Department and provides an opportunity for parents to find support
within the community. He emphasized that the program is designed to be only one
component of drug education and needs continual support by churches, schools
and parents.
   Trustee Derning supported Chief Gallagher’s comments saying that the program
provides a positive outreach into the community and opens up important lines of
communication between the Police Department and other entities.
   By consensus the Trustees endorsed continuing the program as it currently
exists.
V. Corner Lot Subdivisions.
Ms. McCammack reviewed her Agenda Report regarding the subdivision of corner
lots. She noted that typically these subdivisions result in the formation of
two square, “box” lots which intensify the building cadence along the block
face and increase the overall density of the neighborhood. In addition, the
interior of the two newly created lots shuts out light and air to the rear
yards of existing properties and may create setback requirements for an
adjoining rear yard where none previously existed. She then outlined possible
remedies which could be employed to prevent future box lot subdivisions,
recommending that any changes be made to the Zoning Ordinance.
   The Trustees discussed the various alternatives at length, finally deciding
that changes should focus on lot area and lot depth. They asked the Village
Manager to return with Staff’s suggestions as quickly as possible.
VI. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:15 p.m.
November 17, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved December 1, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 17, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and Bill
Meuer. Absent: Trustee Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief William Gallagher,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack,
and about twenty persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
December 1, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
December 8, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present except Trustee
Derning indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
November 5, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved
to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
November 10, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-556-98 – Zoning Variation: 832 Foxdale - Adoption. Trustee
Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance.
President Holland recognized the property owner, Jeff Charatz, and asked the
Trustees if they had any questions for him. Mr. Charatz briefly reviewed the
circumstances surrounding his request and referred the Trustees to a petition
and letters of support filed by neighbors.
Trustee Derning commented that presenting the Council with a fait accompli
complicated the decision making process and that Mr. Charatz was fortunate to
have such supportive and involved neighbors.
Trustee Lanphier expressed her appreciation to Mr. Charatz for his patience
with the process, saying that this was an extremely difficult issue for her.
She indicated that she did not think that she would be able to support his
request, particularly in light of all of the effort that went into the recently
adopted text amendment pertaining to the Foxdale properties. She said that
because this variation involves new construction, she feels the need to be
consistent in applying the newly established standards. She added that she does
not see there being a hardship in this situation.
Mr. Charatz responded to Trustee Lanphier’s comments, pointing out features
which he considers constitute a hardship.
Trustees Hilton, Lien and Meuer indicated that even though the request came to
the Council after completion of the construction, they considered the variance
to be so small as to be de minimus, and that they would support it.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer,
Hilton, Lien, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-550-98 – Zoning Variation: 575 Sheridan Rd. – Adoption. Trustee
Derning, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt the ordinance.
The applicant answered questions from the Trustees before the vote was taken.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-551-98 – Zoning Variation: 687 Cherry Street - Adoption.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-553-98 – Zoning Variation: 1138 Scott Avenue – Adoption.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer,
Hilton, Lien and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-554-98 – Zoning Variation: 323 Locust Street – Adoption.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt the ordinance.
The property owner responded to questions from the Trustees, and Attorney
Janega explained conditions which had been incorporated into the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Meuer, Hilton
and Lien. Nays: Trustee Lanphier. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-555-98 – Zoning Variation: 1045 Pine Street – Adoption. Trustee
Hilton recused himself from consideration of this matter. Attorney Janega
explained that this action would not affect how the vote was counted; it would
be as if Mr. Hilton were absent.
The applicant, Jeff Lipe, responded to questions from the Trustees explaining
his motivation for adding to the existing residence rather than modifying the
existing interior.
After discussing the proposed variations, Trustee Lanphier inquired whether
consideration of the two additions could be severed and voted on separately.
Attorney Janega indicated that because both had been approved by the Zoning
Board of Appeals, they had been incorporated into one ordinance, but that it
would be possible to sever them and amend the ordinance accordingly.
Trustee Derning asked Mr. Lipe whether he would proceed with one addition if
the other was not granted. Mr. Lipe indicated that he would have to consider
the matter.
Trustee Meuer indicated that he could not support the proposed variation given
that the house is already significantly over the allowed GFA. He suggested that
Mr. Lipe rethink the project and return to the Council with modified plans.
Trustee Lien concurred with Trustee Meuer.
Trustee Lanphier also agreed saying that from a policy standpoint, she could
not support the proposed family room addition, but that she could support the
garage portion.
Manager Williams outlined the options available to the Council and Mr. Lipe.
Ms. Janega pointed out that if the vote was taken, and the request denied, the
applicant would be foreclosed from returning to the Council on the same matter
for a period of one year.
In order to give Mr. Lipe time to consider his alternatives, Trustee Lanphier
moved to table the issue until the December 1, 1998 Regular Meeting. Her motion
was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Lien and Derning. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-547-98 – 1998 Property Tax Levy – Adoption. Trustee Meuer,
seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the ordinance. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Derning and
Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-548-98 – Tax Abatement – 1994 PSB Bonds – Adoption. Trustee
Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt the ordinance. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-549-98 – Tax Abatement – 1996 PSB Bonds – Adoption. Trustee
Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt the ordinance. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-557-98 – Randolph Street Vacation – Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that the easement issues had been finalized and that amendments
specifying the particulars had been incorporated into the proposed ordinance.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt the amendments to the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lanphier moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-212-98 – Boot Procedure Amendments – Adoption. Attorney Janega
reported that the proposed ordinance has been revised in order to attach
responsibility for fines to the individual owner or lessee, instead of to the
automobile or license plate.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt the amendments to
the proposed ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt the proposed
ordinance, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-209-98 – Sign Code Amendments – Adoption. Mr. Williams
explained that there had been a suggestion made by a member of the Chamber of
Commerce to amend the proposed ordinance to include an amortization clause
regarding neon signs which would require the removal of all such signs
currently in existence by the year 2001. He said that inclusion in the new Sign
Code of such a provision would require an amendment to the proposed ordinance.
Trustee Lanphier asked whether the entire Chamber of Commerce supported this
change. Manager Williams indicated that the suggestion had come after the
Public Hearing from one member and that to the best of his knowledge the
Chamber had not officially considered it.
President Holland voiced her opposition to amortization clauses in general and
to this one in particular. She pointed out that there are only a few existing
neon signs in the Village and that some of them are antiques and of historical
interest. She said that she did not oppose prohibiting their replacement, but
that the owners ought not to be required to remove them.
George Noyes, 381 Elder, expressed his support for the proposed amendment.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the suggested
amendment to the Sign Code. By unanimous voice vote the motion failed.
Trustee Hilton moved to adopt the proposed new Sign Code as drafted. His motion
was seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Manager Williams expressed appreciation to the Community Development Department
and Brian Norkus, in particular, for their work on this project.
Ordinance No. M-552-98 – Zoning Variation: 380 Poplar – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack briefly reviewed this request to permit an increase in existing
nonconforming building size (GFA) and intensity of use of lot (roofed lot
coverage). The applicant proposes to construct a roof above an existing front
entry, a first floor breakfast room expansion in the rear of the house, and
four dormers on the front elevations of the roof. She noted that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 5-1 to recommend granting this variation.
The applicant’s architect was in attendance to answer questions from the
Trustees.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Hilton dissented.
Ordinance No. M-541-98 – Cherry Street Alley Special Assessment – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that this ordinance would establish the special
assessment district for the purpose of providing funding for the proposed
improvements to the public alley between Ash and Cherry Streets, from Locust to
Rosewood. She noted that the ordinance, as introduced on September 15, 1998,
had been changed to incorporate detailed specifications for the proposed
improvements, give a detailed estimate of the costs, and define the special
assessment district. She also pointed out that the ordinance has been modified
to set a maximum interest rate of 7% and a maximum term for the bonds of six
years.
Trustee Meuer moved to amend the ordinance to set the interest rates and term
of bonds. The motion was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By unanimous voice vote,
the motion carried.
Trustee Hilton then moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended. This motion was
seconded by Meuer. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-215-98 – Addition of a Class A-1 Liquor License – Espresso
Pacifico – Introduction. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to
introduce this ordinance permitting the issuance of a Liquor License to
Espresso Pacifico, increasing the number of A-1 licenses in the Village from
four to five. By voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Lien dissented.
Ordinance No. M-559-98 – Zoning Variation: Winnetka Galleria, Height of Clock
Tower, 562-572 Lincoln Avenue – Introduction. Trustee Meuer, seconded by
Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Short Path Report. Mr. Williams briefly reviewed the history of the
experimental closure of the pedestrian path between Bertling Lane and Woodland
Avenue. In doing so he referred Council to reports prepared by both the
Director of Public Works and the Police Chief.
Before recognizing members of the audience to speak, President Holland asked
that remarks be kept short, to the point, and nonrepetitive, since a great deal
of time had already been devoted to this issue.
Speakers in support of reopening the pathway included Jim Kruemplelstaedter,
178 Myrtle; George Noyes, 381 Elder Lane; Wes Bauman, 445 Sunset and Principal
of New Trier; and two New Trier students, David Macey and Pat Dupree.
Speakers opposed to the reopening of the pathway included Anne and Roger
Knight, 163 Bertling; Jo Meikle, 443 Hill Rd.; Liz Nesler, 491 Hill Rd.; Robert
Cannon, 170 Woodland; Terry Duffy, 159 Bertling Ln.; Marlene Tisor, 146
Bertling Ln.; and Alice Smith, 140 Bertling Ln.
After extended discussion of the issues and alternative solutions, it was the
consensus of the Trustees that it is in the best interests of the neighborhood
to keep the short path closed.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to keep the pathway
permanently closed until such time as a satisfactory solution to the problems
associated with keeping it open is submitted. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer and Hilton.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1101 and 1102. Trustee Lien reported that all of his
questions had been answered satisfactorily and moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1101 in the total amount of $199,422.03 and Warrant List No. 1102 in the
total amount of $401,916.33. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning,
Hilton, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to the
report providing a brief overview of operations at the Winnetka compost site
during the month of October. She noted that there had been no complaints about
odors and complimented DK Recycling on their job.
Resident’s Proposed Text Amendment. James Pinkerton, 333 Willow Road, briefly
reviewed his request for a text amendment to the Zoning Ordinance regarding
side yard requirements in residential districts.
Ms. McCammack explained that the request was to change a specific definition
within the Code, which is typically revised only when it is determined that the
definition creates loophole that prevents the intent of the ordinance from
being met. She commented that definitions lay the foundation for standards
within any zoning district and that the requested change would impact every
residential zoning district within the Village. Ms. McCammack noted that over
the past 35 years there have been no difficulties in employing the existing
side yard definition and voiced her opinion that the current definition works
well given the many irregularly shaped lots in the Village. She expressed
concern that the proposed amendment would affect numerous lots throughout the
Village, each in a different way.
Mr. Williams complimented Mr. Pinkerton on his well reasoned and well
researched arguments. However, he added that if the Trustees choose to go
forward with consideration of the request, he would be compelled to recommend
that an outside consultant be employed to do the necessary calculations on
every irregularly shaped lot in the Village that would be affected by the
proposed amendment in order to fully understand the intended and unintended
consequences of adopting such an amendment.
Trustee Lien expressed appreciation of the problem and support for the concept
of treating all like lots comparably. He said, however, that he was reluctant
to upset 35 years of interpretation and reliance upon the existing standards
and noted that, in his opinion, the issue required further study.
Attorney Janega addressed the procedural questions raised by Mr. Pinkerton. She
said that text amendments typically result from the identification, over time,
of an issue which repeatedly arises, as recognized by the filing of numerous
petitions for variations from affected property owners. She said that
ordinarily when a zoning ordinance is challenged, it starts with a zoning
interpretation or variation request tied to a specific property. She pointed
out that neither situation exists in this instance. She emphasized that it is
important to remember that zoning ordinances are designed to fit as many
parcels of property as possible and that there is, by definition, an existing
escape mechanism in the variation process.
Ms. Janega went on to say that in this instance, the Village has a formula
which has been in place for at least 35 years and that there is no record of
repeated variation requests to identify a flaw. She added that the issue had
been raised with the Village’s consultants, Thompson, Dyke & Assoc., and that
they did not find it significant enough to flag as needing work.
She indicated that procedurally, no action has been taken before now because
the problem has not been identified via the usual channels. Therefore, it is
now a policy decision as to whether the Council find the issue worthy of
further study.
Mr. Pinkerton responded to Ms. Janega’s comments. He acknowledged that he was
not seeking a variance for his property, but expressed his belief that pursuant
to the existing Zoning Ordinance, a hearing on the issue is mandated in light
of his filing of the request for a text amendment.
Ms. Janega stated that in her opinion, it is up to the Council to determine to
what extent the issue should be pursued, since the amendment would affect all
residential properties. The Council requested that she prepare a written
opinion regarding the procedural issues.
President Holland asked for a motion to table the issue until the next meeting.
The motion was made by Trustee Meuer and seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice
vote it carried unanimously.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland displayed the 1998 Chamber of Commerce
Christmas ornament depicting Winnetka’s business district. She urged everyone
to purchase an ornament to support the Holiday Lighting Fund.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported that the Chamber of Commerce was
delighted with the early installation of holiday lights.
Village Manager.
Storm Reports. Mr. Williams complimented Village crews on their outstanding job
responding to problems resulting from the November 10th windstorm. He also
reported that the Pine and Hibbard drainage project performed extremely well
during the recent rains.
Web Page. Mr. Williams reported that the Village’s web page had been activated
on November 11th. It’s address is www.villageofwinnetka.org. He thanked the
Winnetka Public Library for their assistance in preparing the page.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland announced with regret the resignation of Stuart Early from
the Plan Commission. She thanked him for his exceptional job as Chairman and
wished him well in his new position as Vice President of Lehigh University. She
also mentioned that Trish Early would also be leaving the Landmark Preservation
Committee. Mrs. Holland suggested the appointment of Bernie Sergesketter as
Acting Chairman until the completion of the Comprehensive Plan and Nan
Greenough as Acting Vice Chairman. She recommended that Joan Fragen, be
appointed as a new member to the Plan Commission.
Trustee Lanphier moved to approve the proposed appointments, and she was
seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:31 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:32 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
December 1, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved December 15, 1998)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 1, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien, and Bill Meuer.
Absent: Trustee Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about 21 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
December 8, 1998, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Derning, indicated that they would be able to attend. However, Trustee Duhl
said that he expected to be late arriving.
December 15, 1998, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
November 17, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Martin Kenny, 510 Meadow Rd., complimented the Village on its new web site. He
said that it makes complete information more readily available to the
community, thereby, allowing for full, free discussion of issues. Mr. Kenny
also raised several questions regarding actions by the Plan Commission and
asked that Council review them.
George Noyes , 381 Elder Ln., read a letter he had written to the Park District
regarding unnecessary signage and its negative impact on the community. He
asked that they, too, consider the issues raised in his letter. [A copy of his
letter may be obtained from the Village Manager’s office.]
Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:45 p.m.
Arthur Zech, 1515 Tower Rd., asked that the Village prohibit parking on the
Tower Road parkway. Manager Williams indicated that he would have the police
and the traffic engineer look into the situation.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-552-98 – Zoning Variation: 380 Poplar – Adoption. Trustee
Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting an
increase in the existing, nonconforming building size and intensity of use of
lot.
Trustee Lanphier inquired of the project’s architect whether or not a bay
window had been considered as an alternative to the proposed design. The
architect said that it had, but that it would not create the desired additional
functional floor space.
Trustee Hilton indicated that he was not inclined to vote in favor of this
variance, saying he is disturbed by the repeated exceptions to the Zoning
Ordinance being granted by the Council.
Trustee Lien asked the architect whether rather than adding on to the existing
building, there had been an attempt to redesign the existing, interior space.
The architect said such changes had been precluded due to the nature of the
space beyond the kitchen area.
Trustee Duhl agreed with Trustee Hilton, saying that it is always possible to
make an argument for a variation. He stated his belief that because the Village
has a GFA rule, it should be either abided by or removed from the Zoning
Ordinance. He indicated that he would also vote against this variance.
Trustee Derning expressed his belief that the rule is in place to protect the
community as a whole, but that if a variance does no harm, but rather enhances
the property for the owner and the community, he has no difficulty in voting in
favor of it.
A roll call vote was taken, resulting in a tie. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: Trustees Duhl, Lien and Hilton. Absent: None.
President Holland explained that when a variance comes to the Council with a
positive recommendation from the Zoning Board of Appeals, she is allowed to
vote in the event of a tie. So being, she voted in favor of the proposed
variance and announced that it was, therefore, adopted.
Ordinance No. MC-215-98 – Addition of a Class A-1 Liquor License – Espresso
Pacifico – Adoption. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to
adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Lanphier asked Trustee Lien to explain his concerns about the adoption
of this ordinance. Trustee Lien stated that he believed the proposed business
to be essentially different from others in the Village who hold similar liquor
licenses. The Trustees then reviewed the proposed menu and concurred that they
needed more information.
Trustee Meuer moved to table the motion. He was seconded by Trustee Derning. By
voice vote, the motion was tabled.
Ordinance No. M-559-98 – Winnetka Galleria, 562-572 Lincoln Avenue – Height
Variation - Adoption. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt
this ordinance permitting an increased building height in order to accommodate
the construction of a clock tower.
Attorney Janega explained that in order to pass, the motion required the
affirmative vote of four of the Trustees and that President Holland could not
vote in the event of a tie.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the progress of the project, saying that it is
proceeding on schedule and that the builder has been responsive to issues
raised by the Community Development Department.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-560-98 – Zoning Variation: 1228 Pine Street – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed this request for a variance permitting a reduction in the
total side yard setback. The applicant proposes to remove an existing one and
one-half story screened porch and garage and replace them with a one and one-
half story family room and garage addition. The addition would increase the net
foot print of the house but would not create any additional impermeable
surface. She said that in all other aspects the proposal is in compliance with
existing standards and noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had recommended
approval of the variation by a vote of 5-0.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. M-561-98 – Zoning Variation: 915 Elm Street – Introduction.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Ms. McCammack reviewed this request for a variance to permit structural changes
to an existing basement in a residence that has an existing nonconforming front
setback. The variation would allow for the construction of a submerged garage
at the existing basement level of the home. She said that the applicant had
considered alternative locations for the garage, but they were not viable. She
noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-1 to recommend approval of
the variance.
In response to a question from President Holland, the applicant, Eric Anton,
indicated that a large Oak tree in the yard would not be disturbed by the
construction.
Trustee Derning recused himself from voting because of the proximity of his
home to the subject property.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Neighborhood Electrical Underground Standards. Mr. Williams reviewed the
history of the issue. He referred to Council’s prior directive to implement the
placement underground of electric lines in residential neighborhoods, outside
of those formerly identified in the Village’s Five Year Capital Plan, at the
rate of one neighborhood per year. He explained that such work would be
undertaken subject to the neighborhood paying for the cost of the work and said
that the work would neither delay nor supersede those distribution projects
identified in the Village’s Plan. He indicated that in the event of competing
projects, the order of priority would be determined on a first come, first
served basis.
Mr. Williams also briefly reviewed previously discussed possible methods of
funding the program, including (1) collecting up front contributions from the
neighborhood, (2) establishing a special service area, and (3) implementing a
special assessment. He then outlined a newly devised plan, approved by the
Village Attorney, which would set special electric rates for the specific
neighborhoods benefiting from an underground project. He recommended that
Council consider this method because it could be efficiently administered,
would distribute the costs of the program equitably, and would avoid the
problems associated with the other alternatives.
Mr. Williams identified additional issues requiring direction from the Council
and presented Staffs’ recommendations with regard thereto. These include (1)
the determination of how costs for the projects will be assigned; (2) the
allocation of primary, secondary and service costs; (3) the suggestion that a
“participation petition” be required to be submitted by any neighborhood
seeking to take part in the program; (4) the suggestion that a guarantee from
individual participants indicating their willingness to place their individual
services underground be required; and (5) the method used for calculating the
electric rates implemented to recover project costs.
Trustee Duhl clarified that these projects would only be for neighborhoods that
choose to place their service underground outside of the Village’s already
established timetable. Mr. Williams answered in the affirmative, saying that
the Village is operating on a directive to have all lines in the Village placed
underground by 2010.
Trustee Derning asked how “neighborhood” would be defined. Manager Williams
indicated that this would have to be determined by the Director of Water &
Electric based upon the way in which the electric distribution system is laid
out.
Mr. Williams explained that it has been a social goal of the Village since 1910
to get all of the electric lines underground. He pointed out that there is a
public benefit to the Village to do so in that it results in fewer call outs
and reduces associated maintenance work.
The Trustees continued to discuss the issue and concluded that before
ordinances could be properly prepared and adopted, Council needed to provide
specific policy direction with regard to the outstanding questions. Therefore,
it was agreed to place the issue on the Agenda for the next Study Session,
December 8, 1998.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1103 and 1104. Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1103 in the total amount of $236,347.16 and Warrant List No. 1104 in the
total amount of $501,764-56, with one item being held pending further
information. Trustee Lanphier seconded the motion. By roll call vote the
warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lien,
Hilton, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Zoning Variation: 333 Fairview – Policy Direction. Ms. McCammack reviewed the
application for a variance to permit the construction of a new residence with a
reduced average front yard setback. She explained that the issue had previously
been considered for Administrative Appeal, and that the applicant’s request had
been denied. She indicated that Staff had found no evidence that the few
similar situations in the A-1 zoning district would not be able to comply with
the current average setback standards. On a motion to recommend approval of the
applicant’s request, the Zoning Board of Appeals voted 2-3.
Samuel Lawton, representing the applicant, presented arguments in support of
the variation. He said conditions imposed on the property severely restrict his
client’s opportunity to build a marketable house. Therefore, he argued that
denying the variation would not be in the best interests of the public welfare
and contrary to the character of the community.
Larry Hossack, 310 Fairview, expressed his increasing concern about
redevelopment and overbuilding in the village. He urged the Council to consider
carefully the impact on the neighborhood of this request.
Gary Kemp, 335 Fairview, stated his belief that the proposed structure would be
much larger than those in the rest of the neighborhood and would, therefore, be
deviant from other standards on the street.
Mike Humpal, 329 Fairview, voiced concern about the new construction
overshadowing his home and asked that the Trustees consider the small home
owners who are caught between two large, new homes.
The applicant, Mr. Stillerman, pointed out that he was not seeking a variance
on any construction elements except the setback.
Attorney Janega and Ms. McCammack addressed questions from the Trustees
regarding the reason for employing average setback standards, saying that their
purpose is essentially aesthetic.
Trustee Lien expressed concern about using averages.
Trustee Meuer asked Mr. Stillerman if he had been aware of the deed restriction
on the property when he purchased it. Mr. Stillerman said that he had been, but
that he had not be aware of how the application of the front yard setback
requirement would affect his building options. He indicated that had he been
aware, he would not have purchased the property.
Trustee Lanphier spoke in support of the average setback provisions, saying
that they serve a purpose, especially in the smaller zoning districts where an
existing structure can be blocked in so effectively. She indicated that she
could not support this request because the property is buildable without the
variation.
Trustee Meuer agreed with Trustee Lanphier’s position. He said that the Council
has heretofore never granted a variation on new construction and that he was
not prepared to vote in favor of one now.
Laurie Stone, 463 Orchard, mentioned that the lot in question has particularly
lovely gardens which would be destroyed by the construction, thereby, having a
negative effect on the neighborhood.
Ruth Woods, 470 Orchard, urged the Council to reject the application, saying
that she is very disturbed by overbuilding throughout the Village.
Manager Williams explained that there were two options before the Council: (1)
to overturn the Zoning Board’s recommendation and draft an ordinance granting
the variation; or (2) to adopt a motion concurring with the Zoning Board’s
recommendation, thereby denying the request.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to concur with the Zoning
Board’s recommendation. By roll call vote, the motion failed. Ayes: Trustees
Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl and Derning. Absent:
None.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to overturn the Zoning Board’s
recommendation and direct the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting
the variation. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Lien,
Duhl, Derning and Hilton. Nays: Trustees Lanphier and Meuer. Absent: None.
President Holland announced that an ordinance would be drafted for introduction
at the December 15th regular meeting of the Council.
X. Reports.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported that he and Fire Chief Colpaert had
attended a meeting of the RED Center members at which the budget for the coming
year had been adopted, including provision for increased staffing resulting
from Winnetka’s membership. He expressed his support for the Center and its
administration.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 9:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:07 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:08 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
December 8, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved December 15, 1998)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 8, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien and William
Meuer. Absent: Trustee Don Derning. Also present: Village Manger Doug Williams,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Director of Water and Electric Bryan McInturff, and about 14
persons in the audience.
II. Church Street Sidewalk.
Mr. Saunders provided the additional engineering information requested by
Council at its September 8th Study Session, concentrating on the area between
Sunset Road and Hill Terrace. He reported on a pedestrian count conducted in
the area and a review of the sight distance near the identified intersection.
Mr. Saunders discussed the potential path of the proposed sidewalk, indicating
that it would necessitate the removal of one tree on the right-of-way and might
affect the root zone of several others. He said, in addition, that several
bushes and shrubs would need to be either trimmed or removed. Citing the lack
of any record of pedestrian accidents at this site and the strong opposition
expressed by neighbors, he recommended that the Village abandon the plan to
create a sidewalk in favor of requiring the removal of the bushes at the corner
of Church Road and Hill Terrace and the installation of signs and cross walks
to warn motorists of crossing pedestrians.
As an alternative to the above, Mr. Saunders suggested that since the focus in
this instance is on pedestrians crossing Church Road, rather than walking on
it, it also would be possible to provide a sidewalk from Hill Terrace just to
the intersection of Hill Road. This option might encourage pedestrians to cross
at that controlled intersection. He indicated that he expected the cost for
building this sidewalk would be approximately $4,700.
President Holland recognized members of the audience who presented their views
regarding the proposal. Those who spoke against the installation of a sidewalk
included: Bill Stevens, 142 Church Rd., Baird Smart, 112 Church Rd., John
Hedden, 171 Church Rd., George Douaire, 165 Church Rd., Anne and David Loucks,
155 Church, and Paul Davis, 235 Church Rd. Christine Goss, 259 Church Rd., Dan
Yih, 576 Hill Terrace, and Steve Kindra, 568 Hill Terrace spoke in support of
the installation of some sort of sidewalk. All of the audience members agreed
that it would be beneficial to trim the shrubbery at the intersection of Hill
Terrace and Church to clear sight lines.
The Council discussed the problem and various options for resolution at length.
Trustee Duhl expressed his belief that although the installation of a sidewalk
would provide an option, it would not, necessarily, change residents’ walking
patterns.
Trustee Lanphier cited community values and expectations safe passageways as
reasons to build the sidewalk.
Trustee Lien agreed with Trustee Duhl, and Trustee Meuer agreed with Trustee
Lanphier.
Trustee Hilton indicated that he did not think that a short sidewalk was going
to affect safety significantly.
All of the Trustees, however, agreed that the vegetation should be trimmed and
crosswalks installed.
Trustee Lanphier proposed that the Council endorse the construction of a
sidewalk on the east side of Church from Sunset to Hill Terrace, and Trustee
Meuer concurred.
Trustees Lien and Hilton disagreed.
Trustee Duhl indicated that he was undecided on the issue and suggested that
since no definitive action could be taken at this meeting, the issue be slated
for a future Council meeting when all members would be present.
President Holland announced that the item would be placed on a future Council
agenda, but that in the meantime, the foliage would be trimmed.
III. Two Lot Subdivisions.
Ms. McCammack reported on Staff’s study of ways to reduce the number of new
subdivisions focusing on lot area and lot width requirements. She discussed
recommended proposed changes which include: (1) increasing minimum lot area
requirements; (2) requiring a minimum lot depth; (3) requiring an increased lot
area and average lot width for corner lots; and (4) prohibiting the creation of
lots that would result in a side yard that abuts a rear yard. She pointed out
that, if adopted, none of these proposed changes would have an impact on
existing properties; they would only affect new lots.
There was a consensus among the Trustees that all of the suggestions should be
adopted.
IV. Neighborhood Electrical Underground Standards.
Manager Williams reviewed the policy issues which need to be resolved before
Council’s directive to place underground the electric lines in residential
neighborhoods, outside of those identified in the Village’s Capital Plan, can
be implemented. These included: (1) method of funding; (2) determination of
costs; (3) allocation of primary, secondary, and service costs; (4)
participation petition(s); (5) calculation of the special electric rate; (6)
affect on other existing and future utilities.
Mr. Williams indicated that Staff recommended the implementation of the special
electric rate option and that they also agreed that the primary, secondary, and
service costs be rolled together to be divided among the users. He said that
since this way all users would be paying for the service, it was hoped that
there would be minimal hold outs among residents.
The Manager also pointed out to the Trustees that the proposed ordinance
contained provisions (1) prohibiting the addition or extension of any existing
overhead lines. and (2) requiring that if the Village’s lines are underground,
all present and future utilities must also go underground.
President Holland recognized Julie Hanna, 1357 Sunview. Representing her
neighborhood, which has requested that their lines be buried, Ms. Hanna
answered questions about her neighborhood’s experience and asked questions
about the proposal.
The Trustees discussed the inclusion of indirect costs, which it was estimated
would add approximately 15-20% and the threshold level required for the
participation petition. There was general agreement that the Village should
absorb the indirect costs and that the required participation threshold be very
high.
President Holland indicated that the item would appear on Council’s December
15th Agenda.
V. Meter Pedestal Policy.
Mr. Williams and Mr. McInturff reviewed the status of the current project to
convert Green Bay Road from overhead to underground electrical service. Mr.
McInturff noted that there are several properties being held for future
development whose owners are reluctant invest the money necessary to complete
having the overhead service lines placed underground. He estimated the cost of
installing the meter pedestal to be about $500. Mr. Williams indicated that the
Council could opt to put in the meter pedestals at its expense, assuming
permission is obtained from the property owner, however, this could set an
undesirable precedent. He also noted that it would be unfair to those property
owners who have already spent their money on this project.
After discussing the issue, the Council directed the Manager to have the
Village Attorney investigate means of requiring residents to install the meter
pedestal.
VI. Y2K Report.
Manager Williams reported that Staff has been working diligently to bring all
Village computer systems into compliance with Y2K standards well in advance of
the Millenium and referred Council to individual department status reports. He
enumerated critical Village functions which could be affected as well as
contingency back up plans. He also outlined current plans for administration
and operations on December 31, 1999.
The meeting adjourned at 10:35 p.m.
December 15, 1998
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved January 5, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 15, 1998, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and Bill Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of
Community Development Cindy McCammack, and about 41 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
January 5, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
January 12, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
December 1, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
December 15, 1998, Study Session. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Meuer,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Arthur Zech, 1515 Tower Rd., repeated his request of December 1st that the
Village do something about parking on parkway on the north side of Tower Rd.
Mr. Williams indicated that the Director of Public Works and the Police Chief
were analyzing the situation and hoped to have the issue on Council’s Agenda in
January.
VII. Village Auditor’s Report: Irwin Lyons, Miller, Cooper & Co., Ltd. 
Mr. Irwin Lyons, the Village’s auditor, briefly reviewed the audit report. In
doing so, he noted that the Village had once again received the Government
Finance Office Certificate for Excellence in Finance Reporting and mentioned
that the Village also had received an unqualified opinion from Miller, Cooper &
Co. Mr. Lyons responded to questions from the Trustees and commented on the
Management Letter.
Manager Williams then highlighted various sections of the report for the
Council. He concluded by saying that the Village had experienced a good year;
revenues were up, and expenses had been held to within budget.
President Holland formally thanked Finance Director Ed McKee for his
recommendations and diligent oversight of the Budget and Mr. Lyons for his
firm’s thorough report.
VIII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-560-98 – Zoning Variation: 1228 Pine St. - Adoption. Trustee
Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting
a reduced total side yard setback. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl and Derning.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-561-98 – Zoning Variation; 915 Elm Street – Adoption. Trustee
Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting
structural changes to the existing nonconforming front setback. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl
and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None. Trustee Derning recused himself.
Ordinance No. M-214-98 – Amending Chapter 9 of the Village Code as it Pertains
to Underground Utility Facilities - Introduction. Trustee Hilton, seconded by
Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Manager Williams reviewed the proposed ordinance which would establish a policy
for placing electric lines under ground upon agreement by the neighborhood to
pay for these improvements. He outlined the key elements of the ordinance which
include: (1) method of funding; (2) determination of costs; (3) allocation of
primary, secondary, and service costs; (4) participation petition; (5)
determination of special electric rate to recover costs of undergrounding; (6)
provisions regarding other present and future utilities.
President Holland recognized James Swartchild, 1333 Sunview Lane, who expressed
his opinion that the 90% threshold established for the participation petition
was stringent. He also voiced concern that it would still be possible for a
resident to refuse to place the service lines under ground.
Manager Williams responded that the Village was trying to address that problem
by providing a strong financial incentive for residents to cooperate by having
individuals pay for the service whether they choose it or not.
After further discussion on the issue, President Holland called for a vote on
the motion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
Ordinance No. M-562-98 – Equipment Disposal – Introduction. Mr. Williams
reviewed the request for authorization to dispose of a damaged police squad
car. He indicated that a replacement squad car would not be purchased at this
time, because three new cars, as already proposed in the 1999/2000 Budget,
should be available in April or May, 1999. He also requested that he be
authorized to order these three cars prior to final approval of the Budget.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce the ordinance
authorizing disposal of the damaged squad car. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to order three new police cruisers under the State of Illinois bid,
prior to formal adoption of the 1999/2000 Budget. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and
Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1442-98 – Examination and Release of Executive Session Minutes
- Adoption. Manager Williams explained that pursuant to State law the Village
Council is required to examine and release Executive Session minutes which no
longer require confidentiality. He pointed out that certain sets of minutes
would be retained due to continuing litigation matters. The minutes reviewed by
Council cover the period from November 18, 1997 to November 17, 1998.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to approve the Executive
Session minutes from November 18, 1997 to November 17, 1998. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Lanphier then moved to adopt the resolution releasing certain sets of
the minutes. Trustee Derning again seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl
and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-563-98 – Zoning Variation: 333 Fairview – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack briefly reviewed this request for variation which was discussed by
Council at its December 1, 1998, Regular Meeting, at which Council voted 4-2 to
direct the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting the requested
front set-back variation which the ZBA had rejected. She explained that Council
could grant relief in this instance by either granting the proposed variation
or by amending the text of the Zoning Ordinance’s average front setback
formula. Ms. McCammack then outlined Staff’s specific recommendations for
amending the zoning ordinance, which would affect the A-1, A-2, and A-5 zoning
districts.
President Holland recognized members of the audience who wished to speak to the
matter.
Gary Kemp, 325 Fairview, stated his belief that a suitable residence could be
built on the property in question without any variation and noted that the
proposed building would significantly impact the light and air to the adjacent
homeowner. He also cited the overwhelming objections of the neighborhood as
reason to deny the variation.
Trustee Duhl replied saying that it is not the Council’s responsibility to make
a decision which is popular with the neighborhood, but one which is consistent
with the governing ordinance and its intent. He said that in this instance, in
his opinion, the specific ordinance makes no sense and that if there were no
bulk issue in the Village, an acceptably sized house could be built without
problems. However, because bulk is such an overriding concern, he indicated he
felt it necessary to take a stand now against overbuilding by voting against
this variation until the issue of bulk has been studied and resolved.
Trustee Hilton also indicated that he was not favorably disposed at this time
to vote in favor of the variation. He said that in his opinion, if Council was
to take any action, it should be in the form of a text amendment to resolve the
issue overall.
Larry Hossack, 310 Fairview, a professional city planner, commented that he
felt that the Village’s bulk ordinance is too weak and added that the average
front setback formula serves a reasonable purpose. He used blocks to
demonstrate that the front setback formula in this instance staggers the
building masses and that the variation should not be granted.
Trustee Lanphier agreed with Mr. Hossack’s arguments.
Trustee Derning agreed with Trustee Hilton, but pointed out that the variation
process is a legitimate part of the zoning ordinance.
President Holland pointed out that a neighboring house was built conforming to
the ordinance. She indicated that she failed to see any hardship in this
instance.
Bob Woods, 470 Orchard, expressed his concern about overbuilding in the
neighborhood. He submitted to the Council a petition signed by 79 residents on
Oakdale, Willow and Fairview opposing this variation request and urged that
Council deny it.
Other neighbors who spoke against the variation included: Bill Krebbs, 315
Poplar; Dean Polales, 463 Orchard; Mike Humple, 329 Fairview; Jack Hillery, 458
Willow; and Al Day, 85 High Street.
Manager Williams commented that the Village’s consultants, Thompson, Dyke &
Associates, had been authorized to study the GFA issue, particularly with
regard to the one-sixth acre lots, and would be presenting their findings to
the Council early in the year. He invited all interested parties to attend that
meeting.
Samuel Lawton, on behalf of the applicant, responded to the comments made by
members of the audience. He represented that the applicant had not purchased
the property with the expectation of receiving a variation and reiterated his
position that the variation is needed because one house on the street is
flagrantly nonconforming, thereby significantly skewing the calculations with
respect to setback.
The applicant, Richard Stillerman, also addressed the Council. He indicated
that had he known there would be a fifty foot front setback requirement, he
would not have purchased the property because, in his opinion, given the
existing rear yard restrictions imposed by deed, a marketable home could not be
developed.
Trustee Lanphier stated her belief that there is no ground for granting a
variation in this situation. The land was purchased with known difficulties and
must be developed to suit the property. She said that she is adamantly against
granting variations for new construction and would vote against this request.
Trustee Lien announced that he intended to change his vote and would vote
against introduction of this variance until after he has seen the report from
the consultants on bulk and floor area ratios.
Trustee Derning concurred, saying that he saw no hardship in this instance, and
therefore, he also would vote against introduction. He agreed with Trustees
Duhl, Hilton and Lanphier that should the matter be pursued in the future, a
text amendment would be the proper format.
Trustee Meuer reiterated his opposition to the variation.
President Holland called for a roll call vote on the matter. Trustee Lien,
seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-563-98 permitting
the construction of a new residence with a reduced average front setback. By
roll call vote, the motion was unanimously defeated. Ayes: None. Nays: Trustees
Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, and Hilton. Absent: None.
IX. Unfinished Business.
Comprehensive Plan Update. President Holland introduced Bernie Sergesketter,
Acting Chairman of the Plan Commission, who made a few preliminary remarks
before introducing Drew Petterson and Tom Dyke from Thompson, Dyke & Assoc.
Messrs. Petterson and Dyke then presented an overview of the planning process
and current status report on the Comprehensive Plan.
Mr. Petterson noted that the Comprehensive Plan is for the entirety of the
village and includes a strategic plan for the Green Bay Road corridor where
there may be substantial land use changes in the future, whereas in other parts
of the village, few changes are anticipated. He and Mr. Dyke used overlays and
exhibits to review the progress of the planning process and present possible
conceptual scenarios for future development.
President Holland thanked Messrs. Peterson and Dyke, as well as the members of
the Plan Commission and residents, for all of their efforts to draft a plan
which will help to make Winnetka an even better place in which to live.
X. Zoning Amendment Request from James Pinkerton Regarding the Definition of
Average Lot Width – Public Hearing and Policy Direction.
President Holland opened the Public Hearing and asked Ms. McCammack to read the
Notice of Public Hearing and swear in presenters, which she did. President
Holland then recognized Mr. James Pinkerton, 333 Willow Road, who explained his
proposal to change the Village’s definition of side yards and method of
calculating Average Lot Width, saying that application of the current method
results in side yards of varying depths for lots that are similarly situated.
(The text of Mr. Pinkerton’s presentation and his exhibits are attached hereto
as Exhibit A.) Following his presentation, Mr. Pinkerton responded to questions
from the Trustees and the Village Manager.
Mr. Williams asked Mr. Pinkerton to comment on his impression of the effect of
the side yard issue globally throughout the Village. Mr. Pinkerton indicated
that given his study of the issue, he believes the definition of average lot
width causes side yards throughout the Village to be less than they should be.
He said that the larger the lot, the bigger the problem, because on small lots
there are minimum side yard requirements.
President Holland and Trustee Lanphier thanked Mr. Pinkerton for his
presentation.
Ms. McCammack commented on Mr. Pinkerton’s analysis and proposal. She said that
it is important to remember that side yards are not the only limitation
affecting the placement of homes on a lot. She also pointed out that the
Village has relied on the existing method of calculation for an extended period
of time without difficulty, and that given the uniqueness of many village lots,
the method is reasonable.
Drew Petterson, Thompson, Dyke & Associates, commented that he had reviewed Mr.
Pinkerton’s proposal and agreed with his conclusions, saying that the existing
method favors the property owners and provides for lesser side yards than might
otherwise be required. He did, however, also point out that any change could
create even more problems because a significant number of homes would suddenly
become nonconforming. He noted that to implement such a major change in a
village that is already 99% built out may not be entirely reasonable.
Trustee Duhl asked Mr. Petterson why this issue was different than the GFA
issue. Mr. Petterson responded that in the latter case, based upon the history
of requests for variation, there are a large number of people who view it as a
problem, whereas in this instance there hasn’t been the same experience.
Attorney Janega noted that the amendments that established GFA limitations were
the result of extensive study and that the formula was designed to create as
few nonconformities as possible. She pointed out that the proposed amendment
could also cause many nonconformities and that the unknown and unintended
consequences of a change could be significant. She recommended that no change
be adopted, unless a thorough study was first conducted to determine the
consequences.
Mr. Duhl clarified his understanding of the situation, saying that there
appeared to be agreement that Mr. Pinkerton’s analysis is correct, but that the
risk in making a change is that an enormous number of nonconformities could be
created resulting in an enormous number of variance requests. Mr. Duhl
acknowledged his agreement with both Mr. Pinkerton and Ms. Janega, saying that
the Council cannot move forward without studying the issue.
Trustee Derning concurred with Mr. Pinkerton’s statement that we need to treat
citizens equally, but that deciding what “equally” means is the key. He added
that, although Mr. Pinkerton’s solution seems simple on its face, he could not
identify the unintended consequences that might result from the proposed
amendment. He expressed his belief that the Council should study the
consequences in a reasonable time frame and then move forward to a solution.
Trustee Duhl expressed his opinion that the definition of a side yard ought to
be changed immediately and that the feasibility of implementing a revised
method of calculating side yards ought to be investigated.
Manager Williams pointed out that there are already four major planning
studies, which he enumerated, underway and that should Council wish to direct
Staff to conduct this additional study, Council will need to direct Staff as to
the priorities to apply to each.
Trustee Lanphier reiterated Ms. McCammack’s remarks that there are still other
fundamental aspects of a lot which control building and said that although this
clearly is an issue, she did not believe it to be a major determining one.
After further discussion, there was consensus among the Trustees that the
definition of “side yard” could be modified as proposed as soon as possible.
Trustee Duhl noted that, in his opinion, the question of how to calculate side
yards should be pursued, but the issues of Gross Floor Area and the processing
of building permits had to take priority.
The Trustees, Ms. Janega and Mr. Pinkerton discussed the procedural options
available to the Council.
President Holland closed the Public Hearing and again thanked Mr. Pinkerton for
his presentation, saying that the Council would get back to him within a few
weeks.
XI. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1105 and 1106. Trustee Lien reported that all of his
questions had been answered satisfactorily and moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1105 in the total amount of $165,242.60 and Warrant List No. 1106 in the
total amount of $498,193.18. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Lien, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to the
report providing an overview of operations at the Winnetka Compost Site during
the month of November.
XII. Reports.
Village Trustees. Trustee Hilton reported to the Council on the Chamber of
Commerce meeting he attended. He indicated that the Chamber would like to see
the Village more actively involved in encouraging commercial development.
President Holland suggested that an informal meeting between the Chamber and
the Trustees be scheduled to discuss the issue.
XIII. Appointments.
None.
XIV. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 11:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:30 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XV. Adjournment.
Trustee Duhl moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the motion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 11:31
p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
1999
January 5, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved January 19, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 5, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien, and Bill Meuer.
Absent: Trustee Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and about 16 persons in the
audience.
Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:40 p.m.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced the removal of Item 7(b) from the Agenda. The
Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
IV. Quorum.
January 12, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend. President Holland reminded the Trustees that
there would be a dinner at the Historical Society preceding the meeting.
She also reminded the Trustees of the joint meeting with the Chamber of
Commerce scheduled for Thursday, January 7, 1999.
January 19, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Hilton, indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
December 15, 1998, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Meuer,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-214-98 – Amending Chapter 9 of the Village Code as it Pertains
to Underground Utility Facilities – Adoption. Manager Williams briefly reviewed
the electric distribution policy established by this ordinance. He pointed out
that there were several minor technical amendments and one substantive
administrative one to the drafted ordinance. The latter provides that the final
authorization for an undergrounding project come directly from the Village
Council.
Trustee Duhl inquired whether this would permit the Council to waive the fees
if it found sufficient cause. Manager Williams answered in the affirmative.
Trustee Hilton supported the concept of providing Council with an escape
mechanism should individual circumstances require it.
Attorney Janega explained that the language of the ordinance is very general
and includes no specific criteria dictating Council’s actions.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to amend the drafted
ordinance as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1441-98 – Establishing Electric Utility Rate Surcharges for
Undergrounding Transmission and Distribution Lines – Adoption. As previously
announced by President Holland, this item was removed from the Agenda.
Ordinance No. M-562-98 – Equipment Disposal - Adoption. Trustee Meuer, seconded
by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt this ordinance authorizing the disposal of a
police squad car. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Ordinance No. 215-98 – Class A-1 Liquor License: Espresso Pacifico – Adoption.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to remove this ordinance
from the table. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Trustee Derning
then moved to adopt the ordinance. He was seconded by Trustee Meuer.
Attorney Janega discussed her review of the application to determine if the
proposed operation meets the standards required by the Village Code for the
operation of a “restaurant,” and whether the area for bar service falls within
the prescribed limitations for an A-1 liquor license. She outlined the key
elements of the definition and explained how each pertains to the applicant’s
proposal. These elements include: (a) preparation of meals; (b) kitchen
equipment; (c) seating of patrons; (d) taking and serving of orders; (e) meal
service at bar or counter; (f) definition of a meal; (f) size of bar area; and
(g) hours of operation. Ms. Janega concluded by saying that Council needed to
address (a) the definition of a “fully equipped,” kitchen as required by the
definition of a “restaurant”; (b) what constitutes “on the premises” food
preparation; and (c) whether the 10% limitation on bar space is an issue. She
indicated that with regard to all of the issues, the applicants had expressed
their desire to work with the Council to meet all requirements.
Trustee Meuer requested clarification of the hours during which liquor could be
served. Ms. Janega responded that the Code permits service between the hours of
11:00 a.m. and 11:00 p.m.
Responding to a question from Trustee Duhl regarding the definition of a full
service kitchen, Ms. Janega said that she understood that industry standards
have changed. She explained that it is now common for certain aspects of the
food preparation process to occur off premises. She also noted that the
Village’s definition of a restaurant follows State statute and was not
originally written to reflect any specific intent.
Trustee Duhl inquired whether other restaurants in the Village carrying liquor
licenses perform some of their food preparation off site. President Holland
answered in the affirmative. He then asked for confirmation that the proposed
operation would function no differently than other similar restaurants in town.
Attorney Janega confirmed this.
Finally, Mr. Duhl suggested that the ordinance be amended to reflect actual
practice.
Trustee Hilton raised a question about the proposed bar area. Ms. Janega
replied that there would be no problems after design modifications, already
agreed upon, had been made.
Trustee Lanphier asked whether both coffee and liquor service would be
permitted at the same time. Again, Ms. Janega responded in the affirmative,
noting that after 11:00 a.m., no counter service would be allowed, only table
service, but that carry-out coffee service would be available.
The applicants also responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Lien voiced his opinion that the applicant does not meet the criteria
outlined in the ordinance and likened granting the proposed license to amending
the ordinance. He said that he would be more comfortable allowing a restaurant
of this type if the ordinance were, in fact, amended.
Trustee Derning concurred with the idea of amending the ordinance, but said
that he felt it was unfair for the Council to tie up this application during
that process. He recommended proceeding with this application and amending the
ordinance at a later date.
Trustees Hilton and Lanphier agreed that it was inappropriate for the Council
to continue to use a meaningless definition.
Attorney Janega pointed out that by passing the ordinance at hand, the Council
only creates a license which may be issued in the future by the Liquor
Commissioner. Therefore, it would be possible to amend the ordinance before
actually issuing the license.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer,
and Hilton. Nays: Trustees Lien and Duhl. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1443-99 – 1999 MFT Transfer for Maintenance of Streets –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders, Director of Public Works, explained that historically
the Village has authorized the allocation of $125,000 from the MFT Funds
towards typical street maintenance activities.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1444-99 – Townsend Consolidation: 394 Chestnut St. – Adoption.
Ms. McCammack explained the proposal to consolidate an existing zoning lot into
a single lot of record in order to complete the requirements of the Building
Code, which require that a new residence be constructed on a single lot of
record. The property currently has a “metes-and-bounds” lot description
resulting from its division prior to adoption of local subdivision regulations
in 1959. She recommended that the consolidation be approved, subject to certain
provisions, which she outlined for the Trustees.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1445-98 – Lutz Consolidation: 4 Indian Hill Rd. – Adoption.
Ms. McCammack explained the proposal to consolidate an existing, irregularly
shaped zoning lot into a single lot of record in order to complete the
requirements of the Building Code, which require that a new residence be
constructed on a single lot of record. The property currently has a “metes-and-
bounds” lot description resulting from its division prior to adoption of local
subdivision regulations in 1959. She recommended that the consolidation be
approved, subject to certain provisions, which she outlined for the Trustees.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1446-99 – Elm Street Bridge Refunding – Adoption. Mr. Saunders
briefly reviewed the history of the Elm Street Bridge project. He explained
that the Village’s share of the project’s cost was substantially greater than
initially projected because of the style of railing selected, which was
specified by the Council. According to the Federal Highway Administration, the
type chosen is considered an aesthetic feature and, therefore, is considered a
non-participatory expenditure and ineligible for reimbursement. This resolution
would authorize the expenditure of $364,776 of Motor Fuel Tax (MFT) Funds to
cover the Village’s portion of the costs for rehabilitation of the bridge.
Trustee Duhl inquired whether there was any means of appeal from the Highway
Administration’s decision. Mr. Saunders indicated that he would pursue the
matter.
President Holland asked whether the cost included decorative lighting and/or
watering systems. Mr. Saunders replied the latter was included, but lighting
fixtures were not.
Trustee Derning stated that this is a very expensive undertaking and that the
issue of the additional expense should be aired and considered.
Trustee Lanphier pointed out that while this is very expensive, the bridge has
been identified as the focal point of the central business district and is
integral to its cohesion. She expressed her opinion that this is a long term
investment in the future of the community.
Her comments were echoed by President Holland, who expressed her belief that
the Village should proceed with the project as planned. She indicated that
other Village organizations might be willing to invest in the project as well.
She then asked Mr. Saunders if the Village would be in charge of the project.
He said it would be. He added that he expected bids to go out in April, which
would allow the construction to begin in June.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Lanphier,
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-555-98 – Zoning Variation: 1045 Pine St. – Adoption. Ms.
McCammack reviewed this variation request which first came to Council on
November 5, 1998. At Council’s request, the applicants submitted revisions to
their original proposal, removing one proposed addition. She explained that the
remaining addition would result in a 6% increase from the maximum permitted
total Gross Floor Area. She noted that the existing GFA already exceeds the
maximum permitted by 272.91 square feet.
Trustee Hilton recused himself from consideration of this issue.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to amend Ordinance No. M-
555-98 to reflect the revisions made in applicant’s proposal. By voice vote,
the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Derning moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended to allow the
construction of the proposed addition to the existing attached garage. He was
seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, and Lien. Nays: Trustee Duhl. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-216-99 – Amendments to Chapters 21 and 22 as They Pertain to
Certain Two-lot Subdivisions – Public Hearing and Introduction. President
Holland opened the Public Hearing and asked Ms. McCammack to comment.
Ms. McCammack briefly reviewed the history of this issue. She explained that
Ordinance No. MC-216-99 would incorporate recommendations made to Council at
its December 8th Study Session. She referred Council to information provided by
Thompson, Dyke & Associates as a part of the Comprehensive Plan process
identifying village lots that could, based solely on their lot areas, be
divided into at least two new lots. She also pointed out that all of the
proposed amendments would impact only new subdivisions; there would be no
implications for existing properties.
President Holland called for comments from the floor and recognized Ed
Woodbury, 1169 Cherry Street, former Chairman of the Zoning Board of Appeals.
Mr. Woodbury stated his support for the proposed amendments, saying that they
moved the Village toward preserving and enhancing the essential character of
neighborhoods. He also raised questions for Staff’s and Council’s
consideration, which the Trustees discussed, including the concept of
establishing a maximum lot size for any given zoning district.
Mr. Williams mentioned that these revisions will stop the creation of “box”
lots, especially in the one-sixth zoning district.
President Holland closed the Public Hearing and called for a motion to
introduce the changes. The motion was made by Trustee Meuer and seconded by
Trustee Hilton. By voice vote, it carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Tower Road Parking Bays – Policy Direction. Mr. Saunders addressed comments
made by a resident during the December 1, 1998, Regular Meeting, expressing
concern about potential safety problems caused by vehicles parking in parking
bays along Tower Road. He reviewed the history of the use of these parking bays
and commented on their current impact on traffic. He confirmed that their uses,
in fact, present a significant safety hazard. Mr. Saunders noted that a review
of historical documents revealed the existence of authority to remove the
parking bays should there be deemed necessity to do so. He asked the Trustees
for policy direction.
Carol Hayes, 1487 Tower Rd., commented in support of the original complaint.
Trustee Duhl commented that the dynamics of traffic patterns have changed
significantly since the original construction of the parking bays; there is now
a marked increase in both the amount and speed of the traffic rolling over
Tower Road. He said that he thought the Council ought to offer those residents
directly affected by their potential removal an opportunity to comment.
Trustee Lanphier said she believed that Mr. Saunder’s representations about
diminished site distances presented compelling safety arguments in favor of
removing the parking bays.
Trustee Derning concurred, saying that the only rationale for their existence
is convenience and that safety must take priority.
Manager Williams suggested that a letter be written to those homeowners
directly affected saying that the parking bays would be removed within the next
six months and that if they wished to appeal the decision, they could come
before the Council.
There was consensus among the Trustees that the parking bays should be removed
and a letter written, as noted above.
Policy Decision on Church Street Sidewalk. Manager Williams summarized previous
meetings on the topic and outlined the four policy options available to
Council. He pointed out that a comprehensive analysis of the issue had been
conducted and extensive comments from neighbors received.
Trustee Lien stated that he supported a minimum approach of cutting back
foliage and installing crosswalks and was not in favor of constructing any
sidewalk at this time.
Trustee Hilton concurred, saying that the Village should wait to see if the
situation improves after these initial steps are taken.
Trustee Derning disagreed. He stated his belief that the Village has been lucky
thus far that there have been no accidents and that there is an obligation to
take action when a hazardous situation has been identified. Recognizing that it
would not be a popular decision, he expressed his support for the construction
of a sidewalk from Hill Terrace to Sunset Road.
President Holland recognized members of the audience. Those speakers who
opposed the construction of any sidewalk were: Ann Loucks, 155 Church Rd.; John
Hedden, 171 Church Rd.; George Douaire, 165 Church Rd.; and, Bill Stevens, 142
Church Rd.
Daniel Yih, 576 Hill Terrace, spoke in favor of a sidewalk. He presented to
Council a petition signed by numerous residents supporting that position. He
argued that the issue is one of safety and is of widespread concern among the
neighbors.
Trustee Meuer indicated support for Trustee Derning’s point of view, saying he
was struggling for a satisfactory compromise solution which would meet the
needs of homeowners as well as address safety concerns.
Trustee Duhl also said that he was struggling with the issue. He expressed the
belief that if the Village is going to alter the status quo, there ought to be
compelling evidence in support of the change. He said he did not believe such
evidence had been presented and voiced his strong reluctance to infringe on an
individual’s property in its absence.
Trustee Lanphier, speaking as a former resident of the neighborhood and as
Council’s representative to the Plan Commission, supported construction of a
sidewalk. She cited safety, as well as the importance of completing a missing
link from the Village’s pedestrian network, as arguments in favor of a
sidewalk.
Trustee Derning reiterated his position, saying that Council has committed to
reviewing the sidewalk question throughout the Village and that over time, it
inevitably will encounter repeatedly conflict among neighbors, especially those
most directly affected. In his opinion, however, issues of safety must take
higher priority.
Trustee Duhl again disagreed, saying the evidence of the existence of hazardous
situation in this instance is minimal.
Trustee Derning moved that a sidewalk be installed from Hill Terrace to Sunset
Rd. His motion was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By roll call vote, there was a
tie among the Trustees. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, and Duhl. In view of the tie, President Holland was
required to vote; she voted against the motion, thereby, defeating it.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1107 and 1108. Trustee Lien reviewed questions he had
resolved with Finance Director McKee and moved to approve Warrant List No. 1107
in the total amount of $433,618.69 and Warrant List No. 1108 in the total
amount of $349,931.03. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call vote
the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Lanphier,
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Contract Amendment – Thompson, Dyke & Associates. Citing unanticipated work in
conjunction with the development of the Comprehensive Plan, Manager Williams
recommended that the contract with Thompson, Dyke & Associates be amended to
reflect an increase of $20,000.
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to authorize the additional
expenditure. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Elm Street Bridge Rehabilitation Funding Agreement. Mr. Saunders explained that
in order to proceed with the rehabilitation of the bridge, the Village must
enter into an agreement with IDOT governing the construction and funding of the
project. He asked for Council’s approval of the proposed agreement.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to approve the agreement and
authorize the Village President to execute it on behalf of the Village. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl,
Derning, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
Village Trustees. Trustee Meuer complimented the Public Works Department on
their performance during the winter storm and expressed gratitude on behalf of
the entire Village. He also distributed copies of the Winnetka Historical
Society’s report reviewing its activities during the past year.
Village Manager. Mr. Williams reiterated Trustee Meuer’s remarks about the
Public Works Department.
Budget Meeting Dates. Manager Williams announced that meetings to review the
preliminary 1999-00 Budget have been scheduled for the following dates: Monday,
February 1, 1999; Wednesday, February 3, 1999; Monday, February 8, 1999; and
Thursday, February 11, 1999
Amusement Device. Mr. Williams reported on the operation of the mechanical
amusement device at J. P. McCarthy’s which was authorized last year for a
period of one year. He noted that no problems associated with its operation had
been reported.
Brochure. Mr. Williams distributed the draft of a new brochure about Village
services which will be made available to new residents when they sign up for
utilities.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland recommended the appointment of Kevin Plunkett to the Zoning
Board of Appeals.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the above
appointments. By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 10:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:47p.m.
January 12, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved January 19, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 12, 1999 at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and William Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about six persons in the audience.
II. Gross Floor Area Study Regarding the Bulk of Houses.
Manager Williams provided a brief overview of the issue. He said that the items
to be considered were on the Agenda for discussion purposes only at this time
and indicated that Staff hoped to have text amendments ready for introduction
in February, with the hope of adopting them before April 1, 1999. Mr. Williams
suggested that the Council begin by focussing on the Floor Area Ratio
calculations and distributed a summary comparing Winnetka to neighboring
communities. He pointed out that for the average lot in the one-sixth acre
zoning district, Winnetka currently permits the construction of a home with
substantially greater Gross Floor Area than any other comparable communities
surveyed.
He then asked Ms. McCammack to summarized the current requirements, including
several allowances permitted in order to obtain perceived desirable design
features, such as (1) attic allowance, (2) bonus for excessive side yards, and
(3) the detached garage/bay/chimney allowances. Ms. McCammack reviewed the
calculation process and commented on the implications of each. She indicated
that Staff felt that allowances (1) and (3) act as incentives for the inclusion
of design features that benefit the community with respect to appearances.
However, the bonus for excessive side yards has a negative impact on the
community and presents an inequity in favor of new construction over existing
homes. She recommended that this bonus be eliminated.
Ms. McCammack proceed to review two possible alternative proposals for
calculating FAR which would ratchet down the permissible GFA. Both of the
alternatives suggest lowering the absolute 0.50 FAR, permitted when allowances
are factored back into the calculated FAR, to 0.43. Each also suggests changing
the permitted building size formula to reduce the guaranteed GFA .
The Trustees discussed at length the various allowances, the alternative
proposals, and their implications.
Mr. Williams urged the Council to think about what kinds of numbers it thinks
are appropriate for Winnetka, relative to its comparable communities. He
confirmed the Trustees’ consensus to eliminate the bonus for excessive side
yards, reduce the maximum GFA to a number in the median/average range, and make
sure that people take advantage of what allowances are offered for desirable
design features in order to reach the maximum GFA.
Mr. Williams then asked Ms. McCammack to review the components of height and
design review, as covered in her Agenda Report, and explain her recommendations
with regard thereto.
After an extended discussion of these two elements, Manager Williams suggested
that in view of the lateness of the hour, a second Study Session be scheduled
for January 26, 1998 in order to permit further consideration of this issue. He
also recommended that discussion of the next item on the Agenda be postponed
until the Regular Meeting on January 19, 1999. The Trustees agreed.
III. Possible Zoning Text Amendments for the C-1 Commercial District – Indian
Hill.
Postponed.
IV. Distribution of Preliminary 1999-00 Budget.
Mr. Williams distributed copies of the preliminary budget to the Trustees.
The meeting adjourned at 10:10 p.m.
January 19, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved February 2, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 19, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and Bill
Meuer. Absent: Trustee David Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff and about six
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
President Holland announced that the joint meeting between the Village Council
and the Chamber of Commerce scheduled for Wednesday, January 20, 1999, had been
cancelled and would be rescheduled, probably for Thursday, February 4, 1999, at
7:30 p.m.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
January 26, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend. President Holland, however, noted that she would
be out of town and that Trustee Meuer would serve as President Pro Tem.
February 2, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
January 5, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
January 12, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved
to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-216-99 – Two-lot Subdivisions – Adoption. Trustee Lanphier,
seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt this ordinance. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Lien, Duhl, and
Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Resolution No. R-1441-99 Establishing Electric Utility Rate Surcharges for
Undergrounding Transmission and Distribution Lines - Introduction. Trustee
Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to introduce this ordinance.
Trustees Lanphier and Meuer asked for clarification of a couple of points, and
Attorney Janega explained a slight modification to the formula which would be
made before adoption.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
Resolution No. R-1447-99 – Ameritech Vault Installations - Adoption. Mr.
Williams briefly reviewed the history of this issue. He explained that this
resolution provides the authority for the Village to enter into a license
agreement with Ameritech for four sites within the Village for the installation
of underground equipment vaults at an agreed upon price of $10,000 per site. It
also grants the easements necessary for the cable that will extend from the
CEV’s to Ameritech’s lines in the public rights-of-way. He said that after the
installation of the CEV’s, Ameritech has agreed to remove the existing
cabinets.
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt this ordinance.
Trustee Lanphier asked for clarification of the expiration date of the lease,
the lease price and the payment schedule.
Manager Williams commented that an informal survey indicates that the terms are
toward the upward end of what other villages are getting.
Trustee Duhl questioned the advisability of locking in a fixed amount for the
extension of the lease after its expiration and suggested that it be removed.
Manager Williams indicated that Staff would attempt to delete the renewal
option from the contract before finalizing it.
President Holland called for a vote on the motion to approve the Resolution,
amending out the renewal provision. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Manager Williams commended Mr. McInturff for his handling of this issue and
successful resolution of the problem.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Possible Zoning Text Amendments for the C-1 Commercial District – Indian Hill.
Manager Williams briefly reviewed the history of the current moratorium,
expiring on February 18, 1999, prohibiting the processing of any applications
for zoning relief, demolition permits, or building permits in the C-1 (Limited
Retail) Commercial Zoning District. He explained that the moratorium has
permitted the Village, its consultants and residents to thoroughly study and
evaluate the impact and issues surrounding commercial development within this
district. He summarized three possible text amendments recommended by Staff and
asked Ms. McCammack to explain each in greater detail.
The text amendments she reviewed included: (1) requiring a maximum front yard
setback of one foot along any front property line; (2) requiring certain C-
1 land uses that are currently permitted by right to be permitted by Special
Use Permit only; and (3) reducing the current permitted residential density
from 36 units per acre to a lower number. She said that enacting these
amendments would help to preserve the existing nature of the neighborhood and
increase the comfort level about the propriety of future development once the
moratorium expires. She noted that upon final approval of the Comprehensive
Plan by the Village Board, further Code amendments may be necessary.
Ms. McCammack indicated that the first recommendation stemmed from concerns
about both the appearance of the Indian Hill area as a gateway to the Village
and ongoing traffic circulation problems. She expressed her opinion that
establishing a maximum setback would deter “strip mall” type development, with
parking in front of the buildings, and promote a pedestrian friendly atmosphere
in what is, essentially, a neighborhood convenience area.
President Holland suggested that the maximum setback might be increased from
one to three feet in order to allow for the installation of ornamental planting
along the edge of the sidewalk.
In explaining the second recommendation, Ms. McCammack referred the Trustees to
a chart of existing uses permitted by right and uses which require a Special
Use Permit. She suggested that some of those uses which are currently permitted
by right could have a significant impact on the surrounding, adjacent
properties and should, perhaps, be moved into the category of those permitted
only by Special Use Permit, where there are certain standards which must be
satisfied before they can be approved. She explained that these businesses tend
to have a high impact on traffic and intensity of use, especially during peak
periods.
Trustee Lanphier pointed out that it was important to achieve a balance because
the Village does not want to shut small business owners out of the area by
making the process of locating in the neighborhood onerous. Trustee Derning
concurred.
Manager Williams noted that Staff had attempted to identify those types of
businesses which would have a negative spillover impact on the neighborhood
while acknowledging those viable businesses that are working well in the area
and have zero impact.
Trustee Duhl voiced concern that requiring Special Use Permits for a large
number of types of businesses constitutes too much central planning, and would
ultimately result in additional empty space.
Ms. McCammack responded that there would be a catch all category in both the
permitted by right and the Special Use Permit sections which would provide a
safety net in order to weigh any proposed use in order to accommodate those
that do work in the area. She stated that she did not believe that this change
would, ultimately, prohibit many uses. She said that she makes a distinction
between those uses permitted by right which have no impact on the neighborhood,
and those which do have an impact and need to be well detailed before being
granted, saying that inevitably, a development which goes through the Special
Use Permit process is improved before it gets approved.
Next, Ms. McCammack addressed the issue of density, saying that the objective
is to reduce the maximum density for multi-family units. She suggested lowering
the currently permitted 36 dwelling units per acre to 32, noting that density
is also a function of other standards. She expressed concern about dropping the
number too low because the availability of small housing units is already
perceived to be an issue.
In closing Ms. McCammack commented that parking and loading difficulties which
plague the Indian Hill commercial district are not as profound in the Hubbard
Woods area because there are accessible alleyways. She encouraged the Village
to maintain its alley ways.
President Holland recognized Al Day, 88 High Street, who spoke in support of
the suggested text amendments, in particular enhancing the Special Use Permit
process for the Indian Hill area. He asked the Council to consider requiring a
Special Use Permit for every new commercial venture coming into the area and
cited Laguna Beach, California, as an example of a community where such a
requirement exists.
Martin Kenny, 510 Meadow, agreed with Mr. Day’s comments. He urged the Council
to protect the Indian Hill neighborhood.
Manager Williams explained that for timing purposes, the Village had advertised
for a Public Hearing on February 2, 1999, for consideration of the proposed
text amendments, which could then be adopted on February 16th, prior to the
expiration of the moratorium. He noted that the Village had also advertised for
a Public Hearing for an extension of the moratorium for up to thirty days,
should Council decide that that is a desirable course of action.
There was consensus among the Trustees to direct the Village Attorney to
prepare an ordinance for discussion on February 2, 1999 incorporating the three
proposed text amendments, as well as one requiring rear landscaping plans for
shallow lots.
President Holland thanked all of the neighbors representing Indian Hill for
their continued loyal and dedicated involvement in the planning process.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1109 and 1110. Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1091 in the total amount of $197,910.20 and Warrant List No. 1110 in the
total amount of $200,942.43. Trustee Derning seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl,
Lien, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Quarterly Capital Improvement Reports. Manager Williams referred the Trustees
to the reports prepared by the Department Heads summarizing the status of
capital improvement projects. He noted that all of the projects were on
schedule except for the major repairs to the Public Works Yards, which were
being reexamined. He also pointed out that the undergrounding of electrical
wires along the Green Bay corridor had begun.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland commented on the report for the
month of December from DK Recycling Systems.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland mentioned that she had learned that there
might be Federal relief available for snow removal. Manager Williams confirmed
this and indicated that the Village might expect to receive about 80% of its
base costs. By consensus, the Council approved applying for the Federal
Disaster Relief Funds.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported on a recent meeting with the Chamber
of Commerce during which the Chamber had complimented the Village for its
excellent job during the recent major snow storm.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reminded the Trustees of the scheduled dates
for meetings to review the 1999-00 Budget.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:07 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:08 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
January 26, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
DRAFT
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 26, 1999 at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
Trustee Meuer called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees Don
Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and William
Meuer. Absent: President Louise Holland. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, , and about 7 persons in the
audience.
II. Gross Floor Area Study Regarding the Bulk of Houses.
Manager Williams summarized the points discussed at the January 12 meeting. Mr.
Williams said that according to the caucus recommendations, the community seems
to feel that houses on 1/6 acre lots are out of proportion. He referred to the
analysis in the agenda packet for comparisons of other communities for gross
floor area, floor area ratio, garage allowances and bonuses for design
elements. He said that discussion would be on a building height change from 35
feet to 30 feet, the inclusion of 266 feet of basement wall height towards GFA
if walls are 3.5 feet above grade, allowances for garages on corner lots, ways
to encourage new houses to be built with front porches, bay windows, etc. and
incentives to put garages in rear quarter lot. He said that it had been
suggested that for houses on alleys, the access to the residence should be
through the alley. Another issue is the impermeable coverage of turnarounds or
parking pads. Neighborhood complaints are that these change the character of
the neighborhood.
Mr. Williams said that there is a public hearing scheduled for February and
that staff recommends a second public hearing in March because this is such an
important issue. He said that Winnetkans would be notified in the Winnetka
Report sent to all residences in February. A preliminary draft of an ordinance
would be done for the February meeting and then the ordinance could be
introduced and adopted possibly in March.
Trustee Meuer asked for comments from the audience:
Lauren Cohen, 978 Spruce, expressed her concern that new construction may block
the sun and light of existing houses. She suggested considering staggered
setbacks, window placement and a law in Glencoe that she referred to as the
“Sun Law” which protects the amount of light and air that can be blocked. She
also suggested that there be some control or approval of the design of houses
and asked if there was a way to have different requirements for new
construction and additions to existing homes. She said that she was not adverse
to the scaling back of houses but was concerned that it may lower property
values.
Trustee Duhl’s comments were that the community feels very strongly about
private property rights and does not want to be told what to do with their
property. He does not want to lose support of community but believes there has
to be some control keeping in mind the best interest of the community at large.
He agreed that scaling back will affect property values. He would like to see
more flexibility provided; such as more allowances if design elements they want
to encourage are used rather than going through the variation process.
Trustee Lanphier said in response to the staggered setback suggestion that the
average setback is to create green space in the back of the house so that you
can enjoy your back yard as well as your neighbor’s back yard. She also said
that she thought the zoning regulations have served well to support the housing
values in the Village. If people build to the maximum, the use is too intense
for the community, and so a reduction in size would mean that you would accept
a short term loss for a long term gain. She said that Glencoe felt that the sun
law works but they were disappointed with the design results and that it is not
achieving what they thought it would.
Ms. Janega agreed that Glencoe is finding that the sun law doesn’t work,
because garages or additions may not be architecturally the same as the houses.
She commented that staggered setbacks can have drawbacks such as the shadows
cast on green space. She said that when houses are more closely aligned, the
lights and shadows seem to fall in the same spaces. She said that design review
for residential purposes only has not held up in court. One of the dilemmas is
that new construction is able to take more advantage of the maximum square
footage than existing houses. Ms. Janega also said that to create different
requirements for new construction and existing houses is possible, but
difficult. She said that land use regulations should be applied in a relatively
uniform fashion. Design review standards that have held up are the ones for
existing houses and what we are looking at is more a question of mass than
style.
Trustee Lien commented that the caucus questionnaire came out strongly for
reducing the mass of houses, but this translates to a reduction in property
value.
Sharon Christiansen, 377 Sunset Road, was concerned that the floor area ratio
and allowances would be reduced so that it is not economically feasible to
renovate older homes. She said that some renovations or improvements that would
bring an existing house up to a standard that would meet today’s requirements
and last for another 100 years, would not be allowed and residents would just
move to larger houses. Her question is that if the present resident doesn’t
find the house economically feasible, who will. Ms. Christiansen said that we
should reduce the floor area ratio and allowances, but felt that with the new
formula no one would ever reach the figure.
Trustee Duhl said that she raised a very interesting issue that had also been
addressed by Ed Woodbury, the former Zoning Board of Appeals chairman, that
reducing the floor area ratio may penalize older houses.
Trustee Lanphier added that the Zoning Board of Appeals variation process for a
major addition would not be appropriate, but if there are minor problems, such
as the floor plan, or for modernization of a house, the variation process looks
at individual situations and would want to support existing housing stock.
Steve Strange, Oak and Provident, voiced a strong objection to the corner lot
reductions and garage requirements.
Trustee Duhl replied that his view was that corner lots are different and the
purpose of a bonus for a garage in the back of a lot is to put it away from
visual perception. With a corner lot, you don’t get that benefit.
Mr. Strange disagreed and said that garages can be seen from the street. He
also said that smaller lots should have a lot of leeway because you can’t do as
much. His lot will support 3000 square feet, but with a reduced FAR it would
only be 2600 square feet. He believes he would not realize the value of the
property with such a small house. He asked that the Council do the same for A
as they do for B, C, and D. He thinks that people are being short sighted and
that Winnetka may have a different look, but will ultimately realize the
profit.
Trustee Derning agreed with Mr. Strange saying that different requirements for
corner lots is only because of aesthetics and he believes that is improper. He
stated that people with corner lots have the same rights as everyone else.
Sandy McCollough, 217 Woodland Avenue, said that she believes a 3000 square
foot structure in her area is smaller than most of the houses around her. Most
of the issues are associated with new construction and there is not a lot of
comment about reconstruction or additions to existing houses. She believes that
by requiring a reduced FAR, it would be more difficult to make a house meet
today’s needs and has the potential for negatively affecting the value of the
house. She knows that design review boards have been implemented successfully
in other places, such as Greenwich, Connecticut, Short Hills, New Jersey and
Palisades Park in California and she offered to try to get information from
them. She said that these municipalities have the same the socio-economic
position as Winnetka and are looking at the same issues.
The Trustees questioned how to legislate design review, what guidelines would
be followed and designs that would be approved in years to come.
Mrs. Finger, 77 Church, presented her concerns about hillside homes. She
distributed a list of her ideas to Manager Williams and the Trustees. She
explained that her home is almost at the peak of Indian Hill and her basement
is above grade which would count towards FAR under the new figures. She
believes that the basement height should not apply to hillside properties on
natural grades. She believes that builders are putting in higher basement
ceilings and using the grading levels to create more space.
Trustee Duhl replied that he thought the new requirement might actually help
her in regards to height because the measurement would be from the highest
point instead of the lowest point, but that this would definitely effect her.
The Trustees then held a discussion on the gross floor area allowances for
detached garages and agreed that the goal is to push garages to the rear yard
and discourage garages in the front. They discussed requiring that no garages
be allowed in front, whether to encourage use of the 400 square foot allowance
for creativity in design instead, and reducing the allowance to 200 square
feet. There was some concern that the allowance would be used for garages only
so that the desirable design features would not be realized and a suggestion
was made that the design features be in addition to the maximum allowable floor
area ratio.
Trustee Hilton requested an explanation of the floor area ratio formula that
reduces the gross floor area allowance base from 3,630 square feet to 3,000
square feet.
Trustees Duhl and Lien suggested that if the base is lowered, the more it will
affect existing houses.
Manager Williams said that he would refer this to staff who is working on the
formula.
A discussion was then held on the reduction of the building height maximum from
35 feet to 30 feet. Trustee Derning asked Manager Williams how this would
affect the hillside residences and suggested that consideration be made for
natural grade levels as opposed to man-made grade levels designed to create
more living space. The consensus of the Trustees was that since most of the
homes in Winnetka are at 30 feet, that allowing a 35 foot height for new homes
could be out of the norm and all were in favor of the reduction in height.
Trustee Duhl said that in discussing the height of basement walls they need to
deal with the hardships caused by natural topography. He referred to the issue
raised regarding the natural slope of hillside homes and wants a rule that will
not send residents through the variation process. The Trustees asked Manager
Williams to check into this further.
The discussion continued regarding corner lots and eliminating the allowance
for all garages on corner lots. Trustee Derning commented that a property owner
on a corner lot has as much right to a garage as any other person. Trustee Duhl
suggested that by reducing the total floor area ratio, it may become less of an
issue for corner lots. Trustee Lanphier suggested that there may be another way
to address this problem than eliminating the allowance for all garages on
corner lots. Manager Williams said that the corner lots in other communities
are larger to account for the set back needs, but Winnetka’s are not. He
understood that the Council was leaning toward treating corner lots the same as
interior lots.
Trustee Duhl questioned the incentive for front porches.
Manager Williams said that this is a design feature that is felt to encourage a
sense of community and was suggested as a bonus. He said that items on attic
height and the definition for attic are changes to prevent builders from
circumventing the rules.
The Trustees then indicated that they were generally not in favor of requiring
people whose property abuts alleys to use the alley for access.
Manager Williams explained that the item on impermeable coverage had been
raised because of the turnarounds and parking pads created around New Trier. He
said that he sees this as a trend in the high school area, but that there are
also other areas, such as Tower Road to consider. After discussion, the
consensus of the Trustees was to generally prohibit turnarounds or parking pads
on lots with nonconforming lot widths, with the exception of certain streets
where entering traffic is a safety concern.
The Trustees asked for an explanation of the interior side set back item.
Trustee Lanphier suggested a required minimum distance between homes. There was
some discussion of the light and air affected by too little side yards.
Attorney Janega suggested that she include notes in the draft of the ordinance
regarding a minimum side yard to allow adequate light and air for neighboring
homes, especially if one home has a nonconforming side yard.
Trustee Meuer acknowledged a member of the audience.
Mrs. Aleshire, 777 Burr, who asked why there are no parameters when a house is
torn down as to what construction materials can be used on the replacement
homes. She asked if it was possible to keep property values up by asking for
construction of brick or masonry homes.
Trustee Meier said that he understood Mrs. Aleshire to ask if there could be
different standards for spec homes.
Ms. Janega replied that this would not be possible based on new versus old. She
said a better justification would have to be articulated from a legal
standpoint for required materials and that gets into the design review area.
The meeting adjourned at 10:17 p.m.
February 2, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved February 16, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 2, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien, and Bill Meuer.
Absent: Trustee Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about 42 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:40 p.m.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
February 9, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
February 16, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
January 19, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-564-99 – Zoning Variation: 217 Woodland - Introduction. Trustee
Derning, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the request for a variation to permit an increase in the
existing, nonconforming building size (GFA). She explained that the applicants
are proposing to remove the existing screened port and construct a one-story
addition, consisting of a new kitchen, covered porch and mud room, in its
place. The existing kitchen is to be remodeled into a family room. She noted
that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to recommend that the Village
Council approve the variation as requested.
The applicant, Sandy McCullough, urged the Council to approve the request
saying that they had worked very hard to make the changes necessary for modern
living while preserving the integrity of the existing structure. She also
indicated that they had the support of all of their immediate neighbors.
Trustee Lanphier pointed out that this is an instance in which the current
third floor/attic calculations cause a problem for the homeowner. She cautioned
that the Council needs to be careful not to penalize existing, older homes with
large third floors.
Manager Williams noted that Staff is currently reviewing the effectiveness of
the rules for calculating third floor/attic space when determining maximum GFA.
He indicated that it may be that these rules need to be changed in order to
avoid excessively penalizing older homes.
Trustee Derning mentioned that he had visited the site and determined that the
addition would in no way add to the overall bulk of the home, and that,
therefore, he would vote in favor of the variation.
Trustee Duhl indicated that although he has voted against every request for an
increase in GFA, if, in fact, it is likely that the rules will be changing, he
would be inclined to vote in favor of this one.
Trustee Lien recused himself from voting on this issue.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-565-99 – Zoning Variances: 475 Poplar - Introduction. Trustee
Duhl, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the request for variations to permit increases in
building size (GFA) and roofed lot coverage, as well as a reduction from the
minimum side yard requirement. She pointed out that the side yard setback is an
existing nonconformity. She explained that the applicants are proposing to
construct a new family room and vestibule at the rear of the residence and to
raise the roof of the existing residence. Ms. McCammack noted that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 4-1 to recommend that the Village Council approve
the variations requested.
Seymour Turner, representing the applicants, spoke in support of the variation,
saying that the existing house is out of proportion with other homes on the
block and that the proposed changes bring it more into keeping with its
neighbors. He added that the rear addition does not impose additional bulk from
the curb.
The applicant, Mrs. D’Amico, asked the Council to approve the requested
variations so that the existing home could be improved to include modern living
space in order to raise a family.
Trustee Derning inquired whether raising the roof line would create additional
living space. Mr. Turner replied that it would not as there would be no
provisions for heat or electricity and that the space would not be load
bearing.
Trustee Lanphier expressed reservations because the applicant would be
receiving a GFA bonus for a detached garage even though the garage is separated
from the main house by only one foot.
Mr. Turner used elevations to support the applicant’s request.
Trustee Derning suggested that the ordinance be introduced and that during the
interim period, the Trustees visit the property to determine for themselves the
impact of the proposed changes.
Referring to a discussion at a previous Study Session, Trustee Duhl noted that
this appears to be an instance where a smaller, existing home could be torn
down and a bigger, new house built in its place if the homeowners so desired.
He said that he did not believe that existing housing stock ought to be
penalized for features over which the homeowner has no control.
By voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Hilton dissented.
Ordinance No. M-566-99 – Zoning Variation: 559 Oak - Introduction. Trustee
Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the request for a variation to permit an increase to the
existing nonconforming accessory building height. She explained that the
applicant is requesting the height variance to allow an increase in the height
of the garage walls, which will result in a raised eave line. She mentioned
that , in addition, the two-story garage will exceed the maximum permitted
accessory building height of one-story. Ms. McCammack noted that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to recommend that the Village Council approve
the requested variation.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-568-99 – Zoning Variation: 984 Hill – Introduction. Trustee
Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Ms. McCammack reviewed this request for a variation to permit a reduced rear
yard setback to allow the construction of upper floor dormers, necessitated by
revised plans for which the applicants have already received a variation. She
noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to recommend that the
Council grant the requested variation.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-567-99 – Zoning Variation: 548 Cherry Street – Introduction.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Ms. McCammack reviewed this request for variations to permit increased building
height to three stories and increased building size (GFA). She explained that
the applicants are proposing to reconstruct a portion of the rear of the
residence and add to it a three-story addition. They also plan to construct a
wrap around porch and one story addition under the new porch roof deck. She
noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to recommend that the
Council grant the requested variation.
The applicant, Sheilah Johnson, and the architect, Healy Rice, responded to
questions from the Trustees.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. MC-218-99 – Amending Liquor Ordinance Definition of “Restaurants”
– Introduction. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce
the ordinance.
Attorney Janega explained that in the course of considering the recent
application by Espresso Pacifico for a Class A-1 liquor license, questions had
been raised within the context of the definition of the term “restaurant” in
the Village’s Liquor Ordinance as to what constitutes a fully equipped kitchen
and what is considered to be a meal. As a result, the proposed amendment has
been drafted to expand the definition of “restaurant” to incorporate language
from the Illinois Liquor Control Act and add the requirement that the principle
business be for the service of complete meals, with orders taken from
individual preprinted menus, utilizing non-disposable dishes and napkins for
service. She added that, the exclusions from the definition of restaurant have
been expanded to specifically include fast food restaurants, and language
requiring the preparation of meals on the premises in a fully equipped kitchen
has been deleted.
Trustee Duhl took exception to requiring cloth napkins and suggested that this
requirement be removed.
Trustee Lanphier inquired as to whether there was a clear definition of a
“complete meal” included. Attorney Janega responded that several alternatives
had been considered, but that a specific definition is difficult to come by
given the diversity of restaurants and the personal preferences of diners. She
said that currently each situation is judged on a case-by-case basis.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1441-99 Establishing Electric Utility Rate Surcharges for
Undergrounding Transmission and Distribution Lines – Adoption. Manager Williams
summarized the issue before Trustee Meuer moved to adopt the Resolution. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning and Lanphier.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-219-99 & MC-220-99 – C-1 Zoning Regulations – Introductions
and Public Hearings. Attorney Janega explained that Ordinance No. MC-219-99 had
been drafted to amend the district purpose and bulk regulations of the C-
1 Zoning District to reflect Council’s discussions of January 19, 1999, as well
as the concerns expressed by both the Plan Commission and Indian Hill residents
that redevelopment be done in such a way as to preserve the residential
character of the surrounding neighborhood. Accordingly, the drafted ordinance
restructures the permitted and special uses so that uses that tend to generate
more traffic, increase density, or have a greater visual impact on the
surrounding residential districts are treated as special uses. It also reduces
the current permitted residential density from 36 units per acre to 32 units
per acre, provides for a front yard set back from one to three feet, and
requires landscaping buffers at the rear lot lines.
She noted that Ordinance No. MC-220-99 had been prepared for the sole purpose
of allowing Council to extend the current moratorium for up to 30 days, should
it determine that a brief additional period of time is needed to reach a final
decision on Ordinance No. MC-219-99. She explained that since both are zoning
amendments, both require public hearings, which can be conducted
simultaneously.
President Holland opened the Public Hearing, and Cindy McCammack read the
public notice for the record.
President Holland called for comments from the floor. There were none.
The Trustees and Ms. Janega discussed the types of businesses which ought to be
included in the “by right” and “special use” categories. Mr. Williams pointed
out that the focus is to preserve the neighborhood’s residential character.
Trustee Duhl suggested that both ordinances be introduced and the public
hearings be continued until the next meeting. He, then, moved to introduce
Ordinance No. M-219-99 and continue its Public Hearing until February 16, 1999.
His motion was seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Hilton moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-220-99 and continue its
Public Hearing until February 16, 1999. His motion was also seconded by Trustee
Derning. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. MC-221-99 – Amending Gross Floor Area Requirements – Public
Hearing and Introduction. Manager Williams reviewed the history of the issue.
He explained that the drafted ordinance was for discussion purposes only
because several policy issues remain to be researched before a complete
ordinance can be considered for introduction. Among the outstanding issues are:
(1) ways to provide more flexibility for additions to existing homes; (2) how
to treat the third floor/attic in GFA calculations and the subsequent impact on
design of roof form; (3) natural grades versus manmade grades; and (4) ways to
encourage construction of front porches. Staff also needs to explore Glencoe’s
use of sight lines, and discuss when new GFA regulations would become
effective.
He emphasized the importance of insuring maximum public participation in the
policy making process, and recommended that this Public Hearing be the first of
two, with the second being scheduled for March 2, 1999. He also noted that an
article on the possible GFA changes would be included in the Winnetka Report to
be mailed to all Winnetkans before February 25th, and an updated analysis would
be posted on the Village’s web site.
President Holland opened the Public Hearing, and Ms. McCammack read the public
notice for the record.
Mike Graham, 528 Maple, asked the Council to enact a moratorium on all
teardowns and new construction until resolution of this issue. He urged Council
to do what is necessary to preserve and enhance existing housing stock. He also
suggested that notice of the next Public Hearing be posted on merchants’
windows and bulletin boards.
Mr. Williams responded that such a moratorium had been tried elsewhere and
would not stand up to a legal challenge.
John Myefski, a resident and architect, who has served on Glencoe’s Zoning
Board commented on the Glencoe ordinances. He encouraged the Council to clearly
specify in any new ordinance not only what is not desired by the Village, but
also what is desired.
Trustee Lanphier pointed out that Council continually struggles with
redevelopment issues, trying to maintain a balance between the old and the new,
when the standards for everyone have to be consistent.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to continue the Public Hearing
until March 2, 1999. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business
Tower Road Parking Bay Appeals. Manager Williams briefly reviewed the history
of the issue. The Council then heard comments from homeowners wishing to
respond to its January 5, 1999 decision to affect removal of several parking
bays in the 1500 block of Tower Road.
Ms. Carol Douglas, 1505 Tower and Ms. Maureen Evans, 1507 Tower, addressed the
Council regarding parking alternatives and parkway maintenance.
Manager Williams indicated that Staff would work individually with affected
homeowners to reach satisfactory resolution of the problems.
Appeal: 808 Oak Street. Mr. Peter Cummins presented his appeal of the Plan
Commission’s 8-1 vote, on December 16, 1998, to deny preliminary approval of
the subdivision of the property at 800 and 808 Oak Street.
President Holland asked Trustee Lanphier to comment on the Plan Commission’s
deliberations and decision, which she did.
Trustee Duhl indicated that he would be unable to support any further ventures
by The Cummins Group until such time as the Lincoln Avenue project is under
control.
Trustee Derning said that although he had initially been in favor of the
project, he was no longer sure that it would fit appropriately onto the
property. He moved to sustain the findings of the Plan Commission. The motion
was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the appeal was denied. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Findings on Application of James Pinkerton for Zoning Text Amendment. Attorney
Janega explained the history of Mr. Pinkerton’s application for a proposed
zoning text amendment regarding side yard requirements and the manner in which
lot width is determined for the purpose of establishing side yards. She
commented on the procedural history and findings that pertain to both requested
text amendments and concluded by reviewing the drafted findings that pertain
solely to each of the requested amendments. She explained that, as drafted, the
findings would deny the amendment to the definition of average width of lot and
direct the preparation of an ordinance amending the definition of side yard.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to adopt the findings as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1111 and 1112. Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1111 in the total amount of $376,880.76 and Warrant List No. 1112 in the
total amount of $460,666.46. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl,
Derning, Lanphier, Hilton, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
 Elm Street Bridge Rehabilitation – Construction Engineering Contract. Manager
Williams explained that because of the enormous amount of documentation and
paperwork involved in administering a Federal-aid construction project the size
of the Elm Street Bridge Rehabilitation, it is recommended that a third party
be hired to handle the completion of this project. The competitive bidding
process resulted in the selection of Graef, Aanhalt, Schloemer & Associates for
design engineering services. In keeping with past procedure, it is recommended
that they also perform the construction engineering services. The agreement
provides for a not to exceed figure of $81,311.44, of which the Village’s 20%
share of the engineering costs equals $16,262.29.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to approve the agreement for
construction engineering services on the Elm Street Bridge Rehabilitation with
Graef, Anhalt, Schloemer and Assoc. for an amount not to exceed $81,311.44. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland read aloud a letter from a grateful
resident thanking the Village for the “comforting” response received to a 911
call.
Village Manager.
Budget Meeting Dates. Manager Williams reminded the Trustees of the remaining
dates scheduled for review of the preliminary 1999-2000 Budget.
Police Re-Accreditation. Mr. Williams announced that the Winnetka Police
Department had recently completed an on-site assessment by the Commission on
Accreditation for Law Enforcement Agencies. The independent assessment team
determined that the Department meets all criteria for re-accreditation, which
is a high mark of excellence for the Department.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
President Holland announced that there were no items of business to be
discussed at an Executive Session.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lanphier seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:15 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
February 9, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved March 2, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 9, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and William Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water
& Electric Bryan McInturff, and Public Works Director Steven Saunders. Trustee-
elect John Powell was also in attendance, along with eleven persons in the
audience.
II. Inventory Study.
Mr. Williams introduced the Village’s auditor, Irwin Lyons and his associate,
Bill Wiersema, from Miller, Cooper & Co., Ltd., who recently completed an
Inventory Study to evaluate the procedures, controls and reporting of the
inventories in the Water & Electric yard motor pool and Public Works
operations. Messrs. Lyons and Wiersema provided an overview of their report,
pointing out that although a major benefit of automating systems is control and
accountability, the improvements necessary to implement that automation cannot
always be cost justified. They mentioned that other potential increased
efficiencies might include: (1) automation of current manual records; (2)
inventory management; (3) scheduling; (4) inventory aging; (5) time
accountability; and (6) generation of key operating indicators.
   Mr. Lyons indicated that their review had been conducted against an ideal
system and that the cost benefit may be limited in a municipal environment vis-
a-vis a traditional business environment. He said that their comments were of a
general nature and had to be considered by the Council within the context of
the amount of effort involved in implementation and the overall importance and
value of the information generated. He noted that in a municipal environment
there is a need to maintain a certain level and continuity of service,
especially in the event of an emergency situation.
   Mr. Wiersema then reviewed their findings for each department studied,
outlining recommendations and possible options for implementation.
   Messrs. Lyons, Wiersema, and McKee then responded to questions from the
Trustees.
   Trustee Lanphier asked Mr. Lyons if the study had identified anything which
they felt caused alarm and required immediate attention. Mr. Lyons responded
that they identified no overriding concerns and that the current system
functions adequately; it just needs refinement to provide better control.
   Manager Williams identified five important issues which Staff hopes to
address during the coming 1-2 years: (1) moving forward with vehicle
maintenance software; (2) reviewing the possibility of computerizing the
inventory of major items in the Electric Department; (3) exploring the
possibility of setting up a central shop inventory system and fund; (4)
investigating the feasibility of establishing a central warehouse; and (5)
examining a new work order system.
   Trustee Lien expressed his appreciation for the thoroughness of the
auditors’ report, commenting that updating inventory management systems is,
obviously, a work in progress, but that it is important to become more
efficient over time. He said he supported the Village taking steps to take
advantage of modern technology.
   The Trustees thanked Mr. Lyons, Mr. Wiersema, and Mr. McKee for their
efforts on this issue.
II. Point of Access Request: Northshore Community Bank.
Manager Williams opened the discussion on this issue by briefly reviewing the
request from Northshore Community Bank to construct a new building at 576-582
Lincoln Avenue to serve as their new bank. He commented on part of their
proposal which is to install a drive-through banking facility behind the new
building. This would mean that cars would need to access this facility from the
Village’s parking lot and exit directly onto Lincoln Avenue. This proposal
raises zoning, design review, and vehicular access issues. Mr. Williams
indicated that the vehicular access issue needs to be considered before other
zoning and design review issues can be addressed.
President Holland then turned the floor over to representatives from the bank
who discussed various aspects of their proposal.
Lem Tate provided an overview of the bank’s projected expanded program,
including the addition of the drive-through facility, vaults, and an
underground parking garage designed to better meet the needs of their Winnetka
customers.
John Myefski, the project’s architect, reviewed elevations of the proposed
building, saying that it had been designed to complement the other buildings
throughout the neighborhood. He also responded to questions from the Trustees
regarding both design features and possible options for traffic circulation.
Mary Koberstein, the bank’s attorney for this project, and Jerry Lindgren, the
traffic engineer for the bank, addressed several of the issues raised by the
Trustees. Mr. Lindgren, in particular, addressed concerns about pedestrian
safety at the proposed Lincoln Avenue exit.
The Trustees concurred that the proposed building is a beautiful building and
would be a positive addition to the neighborhood. They agreed that they foresaw
little objection to the bank’s using the parking lot as access to an
underground garage, but all expressed serious concern about the feasibility of
a drive-through facility in this location with an exit onto Lincoln Avenue. The
major problems being pedestrian safety and increased traffic and intensity of
use.
Ms. Koberstein, on behalf of the bank, expressed the desire to move ahead with
the approval process and asked what the Village wanted to see from the bank.
Mr. Williams indicated that a solid traffic engineering study, showing hard
data, would provide some of the necessary information.
Given the consensus of the Trustees, it was agreed that the project be sent on
through the zoning process.
The meeting adjourned at 9:27 p.m.
February 16, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved March 2, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 16, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien, and Bill Meuer.
Absent: Trustee Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and about 29 persons in the audience.
Trustees-elect John Powell and Barbara Aquilino were also in attendance.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
February 23, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
March 2, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Hilton, indicated that they would be able to attend.
March 9, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustees
Meuer and Duhl, indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
February 2, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
February 1,3, and 8, 1999 Budge Review Meetings. Trustee Hilton, seconded by
Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:45.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Dorothy Gordon, 707 Prospect, urged the Trustees to pass a complete ban on leaf
blowers, saying that they contribute significantly to both noise and air
pollution in the Village. She cited articles from magazines and the Internet.
President Holland voiced the Council’s support of the existing ordinance. She
asked for the cooperation of all homeowners and encouraged residents to assist
its enforcement by informing landscapers using gas powered leaf blowers that
they are in violation of the ordinance, and if necessary, calling police.
Trustee Duhl suggested that a question regarding the use of leaf blowers be
included in the next Caucus questionnaire. President Holland agreed and
indicated that the issue would be referred to the Caucus.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-564-99 – Zoning Variation: 217 Woodland – Adoption. Trustee
Derning, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting
an increase in the existing, nonconforming building size (GFA). By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer,
Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-565-99 – Zoning Variation: 475 Poplar – Adoption. Trustee
Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting
increases in building size (GFA) and roofed lot coverage, as well as a
reduction from the minimum side yard requirement.
Trustee Lanphier asked to see the builder’s street elevations and footprint
plans. She expressed her discomfort with the overall size of the project on the
lot, saying the intensity of use is too much, and indicated that she could not
support it.
Trustee Hilton clarified that it is the raising of the roofline, causing the
attic to be counted at 100%, which creates the GFA problem.
Trustee Derning pointed out that the rear addition does not require a variance.
Trustee Duhl stated his belief that the Village needs to consistently either
encourage rehabilitation of existing houses by allowing variations or strictly
apply the zoning rules in each instance. He said that it doesn’t make any sense
to him that the homeowner could tear down the existing home and build a new,
larger one, but that they could not improve the existing structure.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Meuer, Hilton,
Lien and Duhl. Nays: Trustee Lanphier. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-566-99 – Zoning Variation: 559 Oak – Adoption. Trustee Lien,
seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting an
increase in the existing nonconforming accessory building height. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-567-99 – Zoning Variation: 548 Cherry – Adoption. Trustee Lien,
seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting an increased
building height and increased building size (GFA).
The Trustees discussed some aspects of the proposal with the applicant, Joe
Johnson, before voting. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, and Hilton. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-568-99 – Zoning Variation: 984 Hill – Adoption. Trustee Hilton,
seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting a reduced
rear yard setback. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Ordinance No. MC-218-99 – Amending Liquor Ordinance Definition of “Restaurants”
– Adoption. Attorney Janega explained changes which had been made to the
drafted ordinance in response to the discussion at the last meeting. Then,
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-570-99 – IMEA Membership for Albany, IL – Introduction. Manager
Williams explained that the by-laws of the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency
require that a majority of its members each pass an ordinance approving the
addition of new communities to the IMEA. The City of Albany, IL has applied for
membership and the IMEA is requesting that the existing members pass the
required ordinance so that membership can be granted. He noted that approval of
this ordinance would have no impact on the Village of Winnetka or on its
relationship with the IMEA.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1448-99 – J. Christopher Clifford Consolidation: 1026 Pine -
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that the proposed consolidation would
officially consolidate two 50’ foot wide lots into a single lot of record. She
noted that the property historically has been treated as a single zoning lot
due to occupancy of both lots by a single residence, but that it must be
consolidated in order to be brought into compliance with the Building Code for
purposes of constructing a new primary residence on the property.
Trustee Lanphier inquired about the limit on the number of construction sites
on any given block, saying that there are already a number of sites on Pine
Street. Ms. Janega responded that the limit is 2 or 3 per block, as defined by
intersecting streets.
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1449-99 – Myefski, Cook & Cummins Consolidation: 535 Orchard –
Adoption. Ms. Janega explained that the proposed consolidation would officially
consolidate a property which historically has been treated as a single zoning
lot, but which actually exists as a “metes and bounds” tax parcel division,
rather than as a lot created by a recorded plat of subdivision. She said that
this consolidation would prevent any possible future subdivision of the
property. The consolidation is required in order to bring the lot into
compliance with the Building Code for purposes of constructing a new primary
residence.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1450-99 – Winslow Consolidation: 735 Sheridan – Adoption. Ms.
Janega explained that the requested consolidation of lakefront property would
eliminate an existing, vacant flag lot, consolidating it with its adjacent lot,
thereby returning it to its prior configuration as a single lot.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance Nos. MC-219-99 and MC-220-99 – C-1 Zoning Regulations – Continued
Public Hearing and Adoption. President Holland reconvened the Public Hearings
begun on February 2, 1999.
Attorney Janega explained that pursuant to discussions at the time of
introduction, specified uses had been further modified to add grocery and
convenience stores to the list of special uses and to allow standard
restaurants and drug stores as permitted uses, rather than special uses. She
also noted that the set back requirement had been modified to clarify its
application to courtyard buildings, which, in turn, had necessitated the
addition of “courtyard” as a defined term. Also, further definition of the
landscaping requirement was added to clarify its intent to provide a buffer
zone.
Marty Kenny, 510 Meadow, expressed some concern about restaurants being a
permitted use. He said he fears an establishment, which minimally meets the
requirements for a restaurant, will open up and function more as a carry out
business than a regular restaurant, thereby significantly increasing the amount
of traffic and impacting the intensity of use of the neighborhood.
Dean Kondelis, a neighborhood business property owner, expressed concern that
he and other business owners had not been notified of the meetings concerning
the drafted ordinance. He asked Council to extend the moratorium so that he and
the others would have time to review the proposed changes and, if necessary,
seek counsel.
John Lewis, 40 Green Bay, agreed with Mr. Kondelis. He said that the business
property owners have significant investments in their properties and want to be
certain that their interests are not overlooked in the process.
President Holland assured the business owners that it is the desire of the
Council to protect both the businesses and residents of the Indian Hill
neighborhood and insure careful maintenance of properties.
Trustee Lanphier pointed out that the proposed ordinance is the result of
months of conversation and study and that there are no proposed zoning changes.
Al Day, 85 High Street, voiced support for an extension of the moratorium until
the work of the Plan Commission is completed. He expressed concern about
unresolved density issues.
Mr. Kondelis inquired about restrictions on residential accommodations above
first floor commercial space. Ms. Janega responded that if the drafted
ordinance were to be adopted, this would now become a special use. Mr. Kondelis
replied that this would constitute a major change in policy and significantly
affect the property rights of the property owners, materially changing the
value of their properties.
Manager Williams noted for the record that the Village had met all of the legal
notification requirements.
In consideration of the representation by Messrs. Kondelis and Lewis that the
commercial property owners had been unaware of Council’s consideration of this
matter, Trustee Derning moved to table Ordinance No. MC-219-99 until March 16,
1999. The motion was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Hilton then moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-220-99 extending the
present moratorium for thirty days. His motion was seconded by Trustee
Lanphier. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-217-99 – Side Yard Amendment – Introduction. Attorney Janega
reviewed the history of the issue, noting that the issue had been brought to
Council by James Pinkerton, 333 Willow Rd., in an application for a proposed
zoning text amendment regarding side yard requirements and the manner in which
lot width is determined for the purpose of establishing side yards. She
explained that the proposed amendment had been drafted at Council’s direction
after its February 2, 1999, adoption of findings setting out the procedural
history and denying the request to amend the definition of average width of
lot. The proposed amendment redefines side yard by inserting language to make
the side lot line the point of reference for measuring the required side yard,
which is consistent with the manner in which side yards have been determined
and with language used in the Zoning Ordinance definitions for other yards.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business
– None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1113 and 1114. After commenting on certain items on the
warrant lists, Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List No. 1113 in the total
amount of $168,732.70 and Warrant List No. 1114 in the total amount of
$408,211.00. Trustee Lanphier seconded the motion.
Trustee Meuer repeated his concern about the Village’s telephone service
expenses and asked that the matter be explored over the next year.
By roll call vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Boiler Valve Change Order. Mr. Williams explained the necessity for the change
order in the amount of $22,390, awarded to Independent Mechanical Industries,
to replace all four feedwater pump valves.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the change order
as specified. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
Village President. Noting that many other communities represented in the
Northwest Municipal Conference have expressed significant uncertainty about
electric reliability, President Holland commented how fortunate the Village is
to have its own electric plant. She suggested that the Village educate the
community about what a great asset this really is.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported on a recent meeting with the Chamber
of Commerce. He said that their two points of inquiry were about: (1) when the
Lincoln Avenue project was going to get underway, and (2) the schedule for
bridge reconstruction and completion.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland proposed the following appointments to the Landmark
Preservation Commission: Robert Zelinski to complete the term of Trish Early,
and John Cook to complete the term of Paul Konstant. She also said that Phyllis
Thomas would be serving as the Zoning Board’s representative to the Plan
Commission.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the appointments
as noted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:16 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:17 p.m.
