                  Deputy Clerk
February 23, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved March 2, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 23, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, and John
Lien.. Absent: William Meuer. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Acting Director of Community Development
Brian Norkus, and about fifteen persons in the audience.
II. Discussion of Gross Floor Area and Other Bulk Regulations for Single Family
Zoning Districts.
Manager Williams went through a lengthy presentation highlighting the concerns
and issues associated with bulk regulation. A copy of his presentation slides
and Agenda Report are attached hereto and made a part hereof. Mr. Williams
described zoning regulation as being a combination of both art and science,
with no available clear cut formula for determining exactly what is right or
wrong. He said that, essentially, issues of bulk boil down to what the
community wants to set as its standards for preserving and enhancing the
character of the Village. He pointed to the importance of reaching a balance
among the community goals of: (1) minimizing any potential impact on property
values; (2) meeting the space needs of today’s families; (3) encouraging
interesting and excellent architecture; (4) reducing real and perceived bulk;
and (5) encouraging renovations and additions to the existing housing stock in
lieu of new construction.
Mr. Williams compared Winnetka’s current standards to those of neighboring
communities and discussed numerous possible modifications to the Zoning
Ordinance which might be considered in order to control and mitigate the
effects of bulk. These include: (1) modifying Attic Gross Floor Area
calculations; (2) eliminating the side yard bonus for homes in the 1/6 acre
zoning district; (3) changing the GFA bonuses for garages; (4) reducing the GFA
formula for the larger lots in the 1/6 acre zoning district; (5) implementing a
setback plane requirement; (6) providing a bonus for traditional, open porches;
(7) requiring the submission and review of landscaping plans for the issuance
of a building permit; and (8) exempting older homes from some of the GFA
calculations in order to encourage renovation rather than tear down.
President Holland thanked Mr. Williams for his presentation and asked for
comments from the floor.
Nancy Levine, 1541 Asbury, expressed some concern that the Village appeared to
be considering legislating design features. She said she feared that such
action would inhibit diversity of design and indicted that she thinks that most
of the new homes are an improvement over their predecessors.
Trustee Lanphier responded to some of Ms. Levine’s remarks, reviewing the
history of some of the discussed design features, i.e., porches and garages.
She pointed out that the Village has no intention of requiring any specific
design features, but wishes to encourage their construction by providing both
the opportunity and an incentive for a homeowner to include them.
Manager Williams pointed out that in the past the Council considered requiring
design review but ultimately decided that it was not something in which the
Village wanted to be involved.
Ann Montgomery, a local realtor, complimented the Village on its efforts
overall, but voiced some worry that detached garages unintentionally encourage
people to leave their cars parked on driveways. She also noted that most buyers
express the desire for an attached garage.
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce Street, a local architect, commented on Mr. Williams’s
presentation and the options outlined therein. He used pictures of some of his
recent renovation projects to illustrate his points. He indicated that he felt
that the proposed changes would not encourage renovations, but, in fact, would
encourage demolitions. He also said that, in his opinion, simply reducing the
permitted GFA would not, in and of itsel, eliminate the undesirable, bulk
effect, which he sees as an issue of artistic design as much as one of gross
floor area.
Mr. Frank then responded to questions from all of the Trustees regarding
renovation vs. tear down decisions.
Manager Williams pointed out that some of the recommendations, specifically
changing attic and basement calculations, are critical components aimed at
leveling the playing field between new construction and the existing housing
stock.
Ed Woodbury, 1169 Cherry, an architect, member of the Plan Commission and
former Chairman of the Zoning Board of Appeals complimented Mr. Williams and
his staff on their work distilling down the points and problems of this complex
issue. Mr. Woodbury expressed his overall support for the proposed changes. He
voiced some concern that Village is losing its diversity in terms of dollar
value, because it is on its way to becoming a community composed predominantly
of very expensive homes. He said residents need to recognize that in some
instances, it is a better option to move up, rather than to renovate. He
supported the proposed attic changes to encourage pitched roofs as well as
efforts encourage the construction of front porches, but he opposed any
incentive permitting encroachment into the required front yard setback. He
suggested that the Village require an additional side yard setback, at some
specified point after the initial side setback, to encourage greater
articulation in walls, thereby, ameliorating the “Queen Mary” effect. Mr.
Woodbury urged the Council to employ a single GFA formula throughout the 1/
6 acre zoning district and not make a distinction between the larger and the
smaller lots therein. He also voiced his strong opposition to the use of
basements as garages, saying that it is detrimental to the overall land use
patterns in the Village.
Mark Kurensky, 1034 Pine Street, a professional land planner also applauded
staff’s efforts and the overall goals of the project. He said he generally
agrees with most of the proposals, but cautioned the Trustees about certain
aspects which might be conducive to monotony and defeat the goal of maintaining
variety.
John Webber, 415 Berkeley, also commented on the proposal. He questioned the
desirability of allowing encroachment into the front yard setback for front
porches and suggested requiring a specified distance between houses and
garages.
President Holland thanked all of the meeting’s participants for their
contributions and complimented Mr. Williams and his staff on their efforts. She
announced that there would be a Public Hearing on the topic at the March 2,
1999, Regular Meeting and encouraged all interested parties to attend.
III. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:10 p.m.
March 2, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved March 16, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
2, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, John Lien, and Bill Meuer. Absent: Trustees
David Hilton and Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief William Gallagher,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Acting Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, and about 55 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
March 9, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee Duhl,
indicated that they would be able to attend.
March 16, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
February 9, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved
to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
February 16, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Lien,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
February 23, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Presentation of IACP Certificates.
On behalf of the Illinois Association of Chiefs of Police, President Holland
presented Certificates of Recognition to Stephanie Harper, Kirsten Spanjer, and
Taylor McGuire, New Trier High School students, who came to the aid of Glenview
Police Chief David Kelly last December. She commended them for their courageous
actions, calm resourcefulness and quick responsiveness to the emergency
situation. President Holland then introduced Chief Kelly who recounted the
story of the incident and personally thanked the girls for their efforts,
saying that they had, literally, saved his life.
Chief Kelly, the Trustees, and the audience gave the three girls a standing
ovation.
Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:40 p.m.
VII. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VIII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-570-99 IMEA Membership for Albany, IL – Adoption. Trustee Lien,
seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt this ordinance. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier
and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. MC-217-99 Side Yard Amendment – Adoption. Trustee Lanphier,
seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt this ordinance. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Lien, Duhl and
Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Resolution No. R-1451-99 – Increase in Water Rates by 3% - Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that this resolution would implement, effective
April 1, 1999, the 3% increase in water rates proposed in the 1999-2000 Budget.
He noted that it has been two years since the last water rate increase and that
the increase is needed in order to keep pace with inflation and maintain the
necessary level of funding for capital projects.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Meuer, moved to introduce Resolution No.
R-1451-99. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1452-99 – Decrease in Electric Rates by 5% - Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that this resolution provides for the second of the
four rate decreases anticipated in the Strategic Electric Plan designed to
maintain the competitiveness of the Village’s Electric Utility.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce Resolution No.
R-1452-99. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1454-99 – License and Permit Fees – Adoption. Mr. Williams
summarized the proposed changes to the Village’s general fee schedule for
permits and licenses, including liquor, construction and parking, as discussed
during the Council’s Budget meetings.
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt this resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Lien,
Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. MC-221-99 – Amending Gross Floor Area Requirement – Continued
Public Hearing. President Holland opened the Public Hearing continued from the
February 2, 1999, Regular Meeting.
Manager Williams briefly reviewed the history of the issue and the array of
proposed options designed to mitigate the effects of bulk as discussed during
the February 23rd Study Session. He also reiterated the five community goals
which need to be carefully balanced when considering any changes to the Zoning
Ordinance.
Lloyd Owens, 922 Elm St., complimented staff on its work on the issue. He spoke
against jamming large houses onto small lots, saying that they impede light and
air. He expressed concern about comparing Winnetka’s standards to those of
other communities and about the concept of grandfathering, or exempting,
certain properties from any new legislation. He also voiced his opinion that it
is not the Village’s responsibility to guarantee property values or the
opportunity to develop and that aesthetics cannot be legislated.
Rick Berlet, 992 Ash St., recounted his personal experience, saying that the
proposed reduction of GFA would have signficant economic consequences on
property values, particularly the larger lots in the 1/6 acre zoning district.
He expressed his opinion that redevelopment raises the ceiling for the rest of
the neighborhood and, therefore, does not necessarily constitute a negative
impact.
Sherry LaPorte, 1072 Pine St., relayed her experience with a builder on a
neighboring lot and asked the Council take steps to protect existing homeowners
from abuse by builders. She also asked that the Council to consider making
construction activity on Sundays and holidays a violation of the Village Code.
Tim Iida, 464 Sunset, asked for change in the definition of a half story,
saying that the current definition is severely limiting to existing homes.
John Dragie, 907 Oak, indicated that as a builder he has no problems with
proposed changes, but as a Winnetka homeowner, he does. He said that people
move to the Village expecting to be able to make changes to their homes as
their situations warrant and economic conditions change. They have made
investments and decisions based on reasonable expectations of being able to
modify their homes, and that, therefore, some relief ought to be offered to
these individuals.
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce St., an architect and resident, reiterated his comments
from the February 23, 1999, Study Session. He commented that simply downsizing
GFA would not, necessarily, mitigate the effect of bulk or improve the façade
of new homes. He argued that reducing GFA would, in fact, promote tear downs
and adversely affect the character of renovations in the community. He urged
the Council to conduct further study before making any changes.
President Holland asked Mr. Frank if he could suggest better ways to attack the
scale of houses. He responded that there should be ways of encouraging better
architecture, perhaps by the means of bonuses.
Marjorie Trobaugh, 465 Poplar St., encouraged the Council to enforce any
ordinances consistently throughout the Village without permitting any
variations. She said that she believed that if the regulations were more
stringent, developers would be less likely to tear down the smaller homes,
thereby leaving some places available for less wealthy and/or older people who
also wish to live in the Village.
Michael Graham, 528 Maple, also recounted his personal experience, saying that
although he has a large lot, he will lose a significant amount of potential GFA
if the proposed changes are enacted. He expressed his belief that older homes
are more inefficient than newer ones and, therefore, need to be allowed
additional GFA inorder to facilitate the construction of usable space. He also
urged the Council to be certain to well publicize any changes and alert
residents to their effective date.
David Gelfand, 319 Fairview, voiced concern about the effective date of any
ordinance changes, noting that any changes would dramatically affect residents
who are currently in the planning process.
Jerry Burke, 1201 Pine St., a real estate developer and builder, agreed with
earlier comments that good architecture cannot be legislated by simply reducing
GFA and urged the use of bonuses to encourage desirable design features. He
pointed out that some existing homes inevitably will be torn down because there
is no feasible way to renovate them, but that new homes with good architectural
design can be built and comfortably assimilated into the neighborhood.
Allen Smith, 1322 Wilmette Ave., Wilmette, spoke about some of the methods
employed in Wilmette to encourage desirable architectural design.
Mr. Williams asked Mr. Smith whether his experience throughout the North Shore,
would indicate whether there are more tear downs or more renovations taking
plae in neighboring communities. Mr. Smith replied that at least in Glencoe and
Wilmette he believed that remodeling was outpacing demolition and tear down. In
response to a question from Trustee Lanphier, he indicated that he believed, if
studied, Winnetka would find the same to be true.
Alan Molitor, 893 Ash St., expressed concern that the Village is losing its
essential character; middle class dwellings are being eliminated and replaced
by new homes that are priced well out of reach of all except the very wealthy.
June O’Donoghue, 1208 Ash St., a new resident, voiced concern about plans
currently in progress which would be severely impacted by any ordinance
changes.
Bill Kasten, 1034 Westmoor, urged the Council to delay any decisions, saying
that he felt that residents of larger lots had not been given sufficient
notification of the proposed changes which, ultimately, will adversely affect
their property values. He said that it is new construction which is primarily
causing the problem, and that he did not believe that an overall reduction in
GFA would accomplish the goal of reducing bulk.
Trustee Derning pointed out that the issue of bulk had come directly from the
Village Caucus, which is the voice of the people. He noted that builders of new
construction are not breaking any regulations, but, in fact, are conforming to
them to the extreme. President Holland added that no new construction has ever
been granted a variation, and Manager Williams mentioned that the Caucus
surveys and Village questionnaire identifying this issue as an overwhelming
community concern all preceded the construction of some of the homes considered
to be most intrusive.
Stewart Shepherd, 870 Bryant, expressed support for the concept of renovations,
saying that many of the older homes are not designed for today’s life style. He
pointed out, however, that there are undoubtedly a lot of residents in the
planning process who will be significantly impacted, and urged to Council to
make its proposals widely known.
Charlie Hoffman, 315 Woodland, complimented Village staff on its work on this
issue. Mr. Hoffman spoke in support of the proposed changes and asked the
Council to enact them as quickly as possible in order to prevent further
destruction of the Village’s character and circumvention of the community’s
standards by abusive builders.
Sarah Carey, 1153 Oakley, commented that she was in the process of planning an
addition to her home which would not be permitted if the proposed changes are
adopted. She asked for an explanation of the schedule for processing and
implementing any of the proposed changes.
Manager Williams explained that traditionally new ordinances go into effect
immediately, but that the Council would have to make a policy decision about
the effective date of any new legislation.
Chuck Boehrer, 1310 Forest Glen North, remarked that reducing the GFA will
substantially affect property values and encourage tear downs because new homes
can be built more efficiently. He commented that Council is trying to make too
many small changes all at the same time and suggested that it impose only those
which seem reasonable and noncontroversial and wait to see what effect those
changes have over some specified time period.
President Holland closed the Public Hearing and called for a five minute break
in the proceedings.
Upon return from the break, Trustee Lanphier indicated that she was not yet
prepared to make a decision on the issue and suggested that the Council
continue its discussion at a later date.
Trustee Duhl said that the Trustees have learned an enormous amount through the
Public Hearing process, and that both the Council and staff need to address
some of the issues raised. He indicated that it is clear to him that there is
some housing stock that will end up being demolished regardless of what action
the Council takes. However, it is equally clear that steps need to be taken to
reduce the size of new construction. He said that he would like to see the
Village concentrate on drawing whatever distinctions it legally can between
renovations and new construction, perhaps by providing bonuses for renovations.
He mentioned that he had been impressed by the comments made during the
February 23rd Study Session by Messrs. Woodbury and Webber regarding requiring
wall articulation and not pushing front porches into the front yard setback.
Trustee Derning asked for recommendations from both staff and local experts in
the construction field as to what architectural standards might be employed to
minimize the perception of bulk.
Ann Orwig, 322 Woodland, asked the Council to consider imposing a moratorium on
all new construction until such time as all of these issues can be resolved.
Mr. Williams and Attorney Janega explained that such a moratorium would be
legally indefensible as well as requiring as much time to implement as it will
take the Council to reach a conclusion on the subject.
By consensus it was decided to resume the discussion of the issue at the March
16, 1999, Regular Meeting. President Holland noted that the discussion would be
among the Trustees only and that there would be no further public comment. She
urged all interested parties to attend that meeting to hear the Trustees’
deliberations.
Resolution No. R-1455-99 – Increase Boot Prepayment to 75% - Adoption. Trustee
Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to adopt this resolution which
increases the amount of the Collateral Fee imposed pursuant to the Parking
Ticket Enforcement Procedures, from 50% of the amount of unpaid fines specified
in the final notice to 75% of the specified amount. In addition, it establishes
the maximum amount of the Collateral Fee at $750, thereby setting it to
correspond to the maximum fines of $750 established by the Village Code. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer,
Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. MC-225-99 – No Parking on Sidewalks – Introduction. Trustee
Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce this ordinance which
would add parking on sidewalks and the obstruction of sidewalks and driveways
to the provisions of the Village Code regarding prohibited parking. This will
permit citations for such violations to be issued via the Village’s parking
computer, which, in turn, will facilitate both processing and tracking for
purposes of implementing the Village’s booting procedures. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. MC-223-99 – Requiring Temporary Electric Service – Introduction.
Manager Williams briefly explained that the proposed ordinance will require
that contractors obtain a utility source of temporary power for new
construction or remodels requiring the removal of existing service. He noted
that this would prevent contractors from either employing portable generators
at construction sites, which create a source of constant noise, or running
extension cords to outlets on neighbor’s property, which, in effect, amounts to
theft of electricity from the adjoining neighbors.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce this ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1453-99 – FY 1999-2000 Budget – Public Hearing and Adoption.
President Holland opened the Public Hearing on the proposed 1999-2000 Budget.
Manager Williams commented that minor, technical revisions had been made to the
proposed Budget, but no substantive changes had been made as a part of the
budget process. He indicated that there was an overall 3.4% increase, but that
the Village would be maintaining the existing levels of service as well as
continuing its aggressive capital improvements program. He said that the Budget
is in balance and projected continued financial health for the Village.
There being no questions from the floor, President Holland closed the Public
Hearing.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
1453-99. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lanphier, Meuer, Lien, Duhl and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
IX. Unfinished Business.
None
X. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1115 and 1116. Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1115 in the total amount of $388.289.50 and Warrant List No. 1116 in the
total amount of $250,193.65. Trustee Duhl seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl,
Derning, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
XI. Reports.
Village Manager.
Tree City USA Recognition.  Manager Williams reported that the National Arbor
Day Foundation has named the Village as a 1998 Tree City USA. This award is in
recognition of the Village’s effective community forestry and tree-care
program.
Snow Storm Reimbursement. Mr. Williams informed the Trustees that the Village
had applied to FEMA for reimbursement of approximately $31,000 in expenses
incurred as a result of the early January snow storm.
XII. Appointments.
None.
XIII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:24 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:56 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:57 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
March 16, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved April 6, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
16, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien, and Bill
Meuer. Absent: Trustee Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Acting Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, and about 26 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that Item No. 7(e) had been removed from the Agenda
at the request of the applicant. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
IV. Quorum.
April 6, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
April 13, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
March 2, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved
to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Resolution No. 1451-99 - Increase in Water Rates by 3% – Adoption. Trustee
Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt this Resolution. By roll call
vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Lien,
Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Resolution No. 1452-99 – Decrease in Electric Rates by 5% – Adoption. Trustee
Lien, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt this Resolution. By roll
call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning,
Lanphier, Meuer and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-225-99 – No Parking on Sidewalks - Adoption. Trustee Derning,
seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt this Resolution. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton
and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-223-99 – Requiring Temporary Electric Service – Adoption.
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt this Resolution.
Attorney Janega noted that language had been added to the draft ordinance to
specify Council’s intent to exempt Village Public Works projects.
Trustee Lien inquired as to why the Public Works Department needed to be
exempted. He expressed concern that the ordinance provides no way for a private
contractor to obtain an exemption, even for a few days, and that the ordinance
might have the unintended consequence of significantly increasing the cost of
remodeling projects.
Manager Williams replied that the ordinance is targeted toward contractors
building new houses who leave a private generator running for weeks at a time.
He said that because of the transient nature of the Public Works Department’s
work, it would be extremely difficult for them to comply with the proposed
ordinance. Mr. Williams suggested that staff come back with revised language
giving the Community Development Director the discretion to permit, or not, a
private generator for no longer than some specified short period of time.
After discussing what might be an appropriate short period of time, Trustee
Lien moved to table the motion at hand and asked for a redrafted ordinance
leaving the time period to the discretion of the Community Development
Director. Trustee Lien’s motion was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1456-99 – Hackberry West Subdivision (1361-1363 Hackberry) –
Adoption. As previously announced by President Holland, this item was removed
from the Agenda at the request of the applicant.
Resolution No. R-1457-99 – Alwan’s Subdivision (1314 Scott) – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that the proposed resolution would consolidate an
existing zoning lot into a single lot of record as required in order to permit
the construction of a new residence. Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee
Hilton, moved to adopt this Resolution. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Derning. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-221-99 – Amending Gross Floor Area Requirements – Preliminary
Policy Direction. Manager Williams said that it  1 has become evident that the
current bulk regulations have had the unintended consequence of encouraging the
replacement of existing homes with new construction. The roof forms of older
homes, with their steeper pitches and gables, often lead to greater calculated
building heights and floor areas, while providing less usable space, than those
employed in new homes.
He continued by saying that by definition, new construction starts with a
"clean slate" and can be designed to take advantage of all the available floor
area bonuses and allowances provided under current Village ordinances.
Therefore, it is much easier to take advantage of these bonuses and allowances
when planning new construction than when remodeling an existing house. The
general result, however, has been boxier buildings, with less articulation on
the exterior walls and flatter roof forms.
He noted that on February 2, 1999 and March 2, 1999, the Village Council held
Public Hearings regarding the possibility of reducing GFA. There was
overwhelming testimony by home owners and architects alike that, due to
existing floor plans, homes that were built before GFA regulations were enacted
cannot effectively use the maximum allowed GFA if they wish to add an addition.
This problem is especially difficult for houses on the smaller lots, which have
limited GFA. Whereas, new construction on the smaller lots can maximize the GFA
by incorporating numerous design features which are in compliance with existing
GFA regulations.
Further, he said that there appears to be overwhelming community support for
encouraging renovations to existing homes in order to avoid tear downs. Some of
the benefits of encouraging renovation versus tear downs are:
   (1)   Renovation minimizes the damage to trees, mature landscaping and
topsoil through the use of less intensive construction methods.
   (2)   Renovation reduces the impact on natural drainage patterns and
flooding by maintaining existing natural grades.
   (3)   Renovation provides efficient use of existing housing
“infrastructure.”
   (4)   Renovation minimizes the disposal costs associated with demolition and
excavation.
   (5)   Renovation preserves the historic architectural fabric of the Village.
   (6)   Renovation maintains the range of architectural styles which provide
visual interest and diversity.
   (7)   Renovation maintains a diversity of housing types, providing a range
of housing options and values.
   (8)   Renovation has less visual impact on neighboring properties because it
is more likely to preserve existing landscaping and screening.
   (9)   Renovation has less visual impact on open space, views, natural light
and air, because interior renovations maintain the existing building volume
while the volume resulting from exterior renovations is generally has higher
basement elevations and vaulted ceilings.
   (10)   Interior renovation has no impact on the appearance and scale of the
existing blockface; and
   (11)   Exterior renovation tends to maintain the appearance and scale of the
existing streetscape, because the typical exterior renovation consists of an
addition at the rear of the existing building and, normally, does not exceed
either the existing building height or width.
He summarized the discussion ideas previously developed to reduce residential
bulk, which have been updated since Council’s March 2nd meeting, to add the
following options:
   (1)   An FAR formula that recognizes the differences between houses built
before and after the GFA regulations were established. This alternate formula
accomplishes the following:
      (a)   Maintains the current GFA for all homes built prior to 1989;
      (b)   Eliminates the side yard bonus;
      (c)   Reduces FAR for new homes by 2% on lots up to 12,000 square feet;
      (d)   Is seamless as it would treat a 9,350 square foot lot the same
whether it falls in the 1/6 acre zoning district or the ¼ acre zoning district;
      (e)   Preserves the original intent of the 1989 GFA formula to reduce the
FAR as the lot size increases.
   (2)   A reduced setback plane;
   (3)   Not allowing porches to encroach into the minimum front yard setback;
   (4)   Articulation requirements;
   (5)   Ways to encourage dormers;
   (6)   Reduction of basement heights; and
   (7)   Denial of any future GFA variances.
Mr. Williams referred the Trustees to the Decision Matrix, included in their
Agenda packets for purpose of providing policy direction on these issues. (A
copy of the Decision Matrix is attached hereto as Attachment A.) He indicated
that the Council also had the option of making no changes whatsoever and
reminded them that in the future text changes could be made if something
unforeseen developed.
In addressing the subject of when any changes might take effect, the Village
Manager said that staff proposed an effective date of May 1, 1999.
President Holland announced that the meeting’s discussion would be conducted
solely among the Trustees, but that there would be a third Public Hearing on
April 6, 1999, where the Council would hear comments from residents.
The Trustees then reviewed and discussed each of the possible changes, item by
item, taking a consensus on each.
At the conclusion of the discussion, President Holland again announced the
Public Hearing scheduled for April 6th and encouraged all interested residents
to attend and/or submit their written comments to the Trustees either by mail
or via email. She also said that a letter outlining the proposed changes would
be sent to all residents during the following week.
Trustee Derning thanked Mr. Williams and the staff for devising the Decision
Matrix in order to facilitate the discussion.
Ordinance No. MC-219-99 – C-1 Zoning Regulations – Continued Public Hearing and
Adoption. President Holland called for a motion to reopen the Public Hearing
begun on February 2, 1999, and continued to February 16, 1999. The Ordinance
amends the district purpose and bulk regulations of the C-1 zoning district.
Trustee Lien so moved, and he was seconded by Trustee Meuer. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Manager Williams explained that the drafted ordinance now reflects concerns
raised by property owners at the February 16th Public Hearing about requiring a
special use permit for residential uses above the ground floor. Pursuant to
those concerns, the text of the ordinance has been amended to allow residential
uses above the first floor. Presently, all such uses are subject to the
issuance of a Certificate of Appropriateness by the Design Review Board.
Because of the potential impact on the adjoining single family residential
districts, language has been added to the draft ordinance to amend the Building
Code in order to make the Design Review Board’s decisions on properties in the
C-1 Zoning District advisory only, and to make final design review decisions in
this zoning district subject to Council approval. This will assure that the
Village Council makes the final determination as to whether the building
design, site plan and landscape plan, all of which fall within the Building
Code’s definition of “external architectural feature,” minimize the impact of
the physical presence of the larger structures to the extent reasonably
possible. There is also an amendment to provide that neighbors within 250 feet
of such use be notified when a proposal comes before the Village Council for
consideration.
Dean Kondelas, 650 Exmoor, thanked the Council and Village staff for taking
property owners’ concerns into consideration and said that he thought an
acceptable compromise had been reached.
There being no further comments from the floor, Trustee Derning, seconded by
Trustee Hilton, moved to close the Public Hearing. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the amendments to
the drafted ordinance as discussed. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Derning, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl
Attorney Janega explained that by virtue of the adoption of Ordinance No. MC-
219-99, the moratorium on the issuance of demolition and building permits and
on requests for rezoning, special uses and variations in the C-1 Zoning
District was automatically lifted.
 
Notes
  The purpose of this note is to provide more of a rational for the legislative
1 action: the goal is to level the playing field, so it’s important to note the
  disparate impact of the existing regulations.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1117 and 1118. Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1117 in the total amount of $165,018.21 and Warrant List No. 1118 in the
total amount of $576,088.39. Trustee Meuer seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning,
Hilton, Lanphier, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Appeal of A-1 Liquor License Fees. President Holland recognized Jim Pomerantz,
who on behalf of himself and Patrick O’Neil, requested that the Council
reconsider the fee set for a Class A-1 Liquor License. He referred the Trustees
to the results of a recent survey, included in their Agenda packets, showing
that with the exception of Northfield, Winnetka’s fees are the highest of any
of the neighboring communities, while its restrictions on the service of
alcohol are the most stringent. Saying that based upon the restrictions, the
fees seem out of line with the revenue generated, he asked for a reduction in
the fee.
Manager Williams remarked that the Village’s fees had been established to
reflect the unique provisions of the Village’s ordinance and to recapture the
costs associated with the processing of an application.
Trustee Lien commented that the Village is not trying to raise revenue on the
issuance of liquor licenses, but that the fees must be high enough to cover the
associated costs of reviewing and processing. He said that he would be in favor
of reducing the fees if it was practicable to do so.
After asking Mr. Pomerantz what he thought would be an appropriate fee, Trustee
Derning suggested that staff return to the Council with a recommendation on a
reduced fee. The Trustees agreed to revisit the issue at their April 6th
Regular Meeting.
Bid No. 99-004 – 1999 Tree Planting Program. Mr. Williams reviewed the bid for
the purchase and planting within the Village during 1999 of approximately 190
parkway trees. Staff’s recommendation is to award the bid to three vendors, as
follows: St. Aubin Nursery, $37,557; Weiler Nursery, $8,380; Mike Greco
Nursery, $3,985, for a total of $49,922.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to accept the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Lien,
Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Census Proclamation. President explained that the purpose of the Census 2000
Proclamation is to introduce to the community the Village’s commitment to and
partnership with the U. S. Census Bureau to complete the Census 2000 as well as
encourage active participation by all Winnetkans.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the Proclamation.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland responded to an anonymous letter received
by the Trustees regarding vacancies in downtown commercial space.
Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported that IDOT had taken bids for the
renovation of the Elm St. bridge. He indicated that the lowest had come in
approximately $100,000 over the initial estimates; IDOT has concurred with it,
and the Village will need to do the same. It is expected that this will amount
to an additional expense to the Village of about $20,000.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent: Trustee
Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:47 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
April 6, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved April 20, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
6, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien, and Bill Meuer.
Absent: Trustee Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Acting Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, and about 52 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that item 7(e) had been removed from the Agenda at
the request of the applicant. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
IV. Quorum.
April 13, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
April 20, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
March 16, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:40.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-221-99 – Amending Gross Floor Area Requirements – Public
Hearing and Introduction. President Holland opened the reconvened Public
Hearing and thanked all members of the audience for attending.
Manager Williams explained that the drafted ordinance would codify the
tentative policy changes specified by the Village Council at its March 16th
meeting. He noted that a special mailing had been sent to all residents
notifying them of this hearing and the proposed changes.
Then Mr. Williams outlined three, new provisions, not previously discussed by
the Trustees, which had been added to the preliminary draft. They are as
follows:
1.   Allowing a GFA bonus of 30 square feet for a storage shed. Currently, many
Winnetkans want to build a small shed for the storage of lawn equipment, but
they must submit very complicated GFA calculations before being allowed to do
so.
2.   Specifying that attached garages with front-facing garage doors may not
exceed 50% of the front building line. This would limit the aesthetic impact of
front-facing garage doors.
3.   Establishing a minimum permitted encroachment length of 20 feet for gable
ends and dormers in the setback plane for all lots up to 50 feet wide. This is
the same minimum encroachment allowed by Glencoe.
He noted that the draft ordinance also would codify existing Zoning Code
interpretations and change the designation of residential zoning districts to
the more conventional “R” designation, with numbering to indicate the relative
sequence of minimum lot sizes.
In addition, Manager Williams reviewed two additional possible Zoning Code
changes which had been identified by staff: (1) Side yard setbacks of 8 feet
and 6 feet for 1/6 and ¼ acre zoning districts, and (2) No GFA variances or “de
minimis” variances.
Following the presentation by Mr. Williams, President Holland invited comments,
questions and testimony from persons in the audience.
Elizabeth Crowe, 1228 Westmoor Road, expressed surprise at the 3-foot
limitation on basement height and stated that most newer homes have lower
basements.
Anne Finger, 77 Church Road, referred to correspondence that she had addressed
to the Village Council, pertaining to the impact of regulations on properties
located on hillside.
Irv Footlick, 1548 Tower Road stated that he came to learn more about the
proposed zoning ordinance changes and stated that he might have comments to
make later in the hearing.
Roger Bishop, 1290 Scott Avenue, asked whether the ordinance on the Internet is
the full text, and Mr. Williams replied that it is. Mr. Bishop also commented
that his lot slopes from front to back.
Lois Bishop, 1290 Scott Avenue, also inquired about the impact of the proposed
changes on sloping lots. She also stated that she opposes special treatment of
dormers and front porches, stating that “they take up space, too.”
Veronica Jiganti, 325 Linden, stated that a new home is being constructed next
to her lot, which measures 50’ x 187’. She expressed concern in general about
the large size of new construction and expressed concerns about the impact of
the construction on her driveway.
Joanna Karatzas, 476 Provident, expressed concern about the proposed setback
plane. She stated that she is trained as an architect and that she believes
that the proposed setback plane would force people to do odd-shaped roof forms.
She stated that her husband is also an architect and that he would be preparing
some additional information for the Council.
Wayne Schoenmakers, 968 Pine Street, expressed concern about too much
construction going on at one time along Pine Street. He also stated that some
new homes have no windows on the street side and felt that windows should be
required. He also raised questions regarding requiring the use of alleys and
prohibiting driveways where an alley exists.
Gertrude Niehoff, 557 Orchard Ln., expressed her strong objections to the
proposed revisions, saying that they would create an economic hardship for her.
Mrs. Niehoff stated that her lot measures 50’ x 165.75’ and that her home is
smaller than the homes around her. She expressed her belief that she would not
be able to build a new house that meets contemporary needs on her property and
asked the Trustees to exempt her property or reject the amendments.
Trustee Duhl responded saying that the Council and staff have made every effort
to balance the clear mandate from the community to control overbuilding with
the understanding that there may be some adverse effects on property values in
the short term. He said that the long term goal is to maintain Winnetka’s
desirability as a residential community.
Trustee Derning agreed, saying that the mandate from the community is to reduce
the size of homes and that he feels no obligation to accommodate the maximum
desires of builders and future homebuyers.
Trustee Hilton suggested that Mrs. Neihoff meet with the Village Manager
regarding her claim of potentially lost square footage to make sure that her
calculations are correct.
Richard Radcliffe, 1177 Ash, stated that his property is located in the
floodplain, where basements cannot be located. He stated that the Village
should accommodate the loss of basement square footage on such properties by
allowing more square footage above ground.
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce, a resident and architect, stated that he supports
distinguishing between renovations and new homes. He expressed concern,
however, that the plane of light regulations could pose difficulties for
existing homes. He explained that the side building line articulation
requirement might provide sufficient relief from bulk in the side yards so that
the daylight plane regulations might not be necessary. Regarding other
provisions of the draft ordinance, Mr. Frank stated that he does not favor a
“no variance” policy, and stated that he thought the proposed limits on garage
doors is a good idea.
E.B. Jorgensen, 985 Ash Street, commented that issues regarding roof line are
important.
Ed Woodbury, 1169 Cherry, a member of the Plan Commission and former Chairman
of the Zoning Board of Appeals, stated that he felt it appropriate for the
Council to use the proposed zoning ordinance to encourage porches but expressed
concern about allowing encroachments into the average front yard. In his
opinion, the lot coverage bonus is sufficient to encourage front porches. He
also stated that he sees the side building line articulation as an additional
setback and considers it to be important in that the articulation provisions in
the proposed ordinance needs to be maintained. He also asked the Council not to
overlook issues about basement garages and recommended that there be continued
debate on the availability of variations.
Midge Powell, 693 Walden Road, asked the Council to limit the number of
construction sites, noting that there are currently 6 construction sites on
Pine Street in a 4 block area, which is too much for the neighborhood to
absorb.
David Webster, 596 Arbor Vitae, stated that he has a vacant lot adjoining his
property and inquired whether undeveloped lots would be protected by a
grandfather provision.
Irv Footlick, 1548 Tower Road, also inquired about the impact on vacant lots,
noting that he has an additional ½ acre adjacent to his lot.
There being no further comments from the floor, President Holland continued the
Public Hearing until Tuesday, April 13, 1999, stating that persons who had not
been able to speak tonight could make their comments known to the Council at
that time. She added that, following the hearing the Trustees will discuss the
individual components of the proposed ordinance. No action to introduce the
ordinance was made at this time.
Ordinance No. M-569-99 – Special Use Permit: Station Park - Introduction.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the ordinance
which would permit improvements to Station Park, bounded by Elm Street, Oak
Street, and Green Bay Road. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. MC-223-99 – Electricity at Construction Sites - Adoption. Manager
Williams explained that in response to concerns raised at the March 16th
Regular Meeting, amendments to the proposed ordinance had been drafted. These
amendments (1) clarify that exceptions for Public Works projects only apply to
work being done on or in the public right-of-way; (2) expand existing
exceptions to include the use of electric generators; and (3) add an exception
for temporary use of generators, for up to five (5) days, upon a determination
by the Community Development Director that it is necessary to temporarily
disconnect from the Village’s distribution system.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to amend the drafted
ordinance as proposed. By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Meuer, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1462-99 – Appeal of Liquor License Fees – Adoption. Manager
Williams reviewed the modifications to the Village’s liquor license fees,
noting that Winnetka’s are, in fact, higher than neighboring communities and
recommended that the major fees be reduced by approximately 30%.
Trustee Duhl expressed his strong support for reducing the fees even further
than proposed in the hopes of encouraging and supporting the Village’s
restaurants, which ultimately benefit the community by generating revenue and
sales tax. He said he would like to see Winnetka’s fees be the lowest in the
surrounding area, with no license costing over $1,000.
Trustees Hilton and Lien concurred with Trustee Duhl.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to amend the proposed ordinance
to reflect the following fee changes: $1,000 for Class A-1; $750 for Class A;
$150 for a Packaged Meal Rider; $750 For Class B; $150 for Class D; and $500
for Class E. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the resolution
amending liquor license fees. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Lanphier and Meuer.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1456-99 – Hackberry West Subdivision (1361-1363 Hackberry) –
Adoption. As announced by President Holland, this item was removed from the
Agenda at the request of the applicant.
Resolution No. 1459-99 – Approving and Establishing Fee for Provision of Fire
Suppression and Emergency Paramedical and Rescue Service to Unincorporated
Areas – Adoption. Manager Williams reviewed the proposed charges, as determined
by a combination of workload, assessed valuation and population, to Kenilworth
and Unincorporated New Trier Township for fire and emergency paramedical and
rescue services.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1458-99 – Renewal of Sidewalk Replacement Contract for 1999 –
Adoption. Manager Williams reviewed the resolution proposing extension of the
Village’s current contract with Schroeder and Schroeder, Inc. for sidewalk
replacement during calendar year 1999 for an amount not to exceed $100,000.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier, Meuer,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1460-99 – Establishing Salary of Department Heads – Adoption.
Trustee Meuer moved to adopt the Resolution and was seconded by Trustee
Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning and Lanphier. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1461-99 – Establishing Salary of Village Manager – Adoption.
Trustee Derning moved to adopt the Resolution. He was seconded by Trustee
Lanphier. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Duhl, and Lien.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1119 and 1120. Trustee Lien commented that there continues
to be an issue with landscapers who take months before presenting bills for
services rendered. Manager Williams indicated that in the future contracts
would include a provision for the submission of bills within a reasonable time
period.
Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List No. 1119 in the total amount of
$376,070.61 and Warrant List No. 1120 in the total amount of $379,535.40.
Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call vote the warrant lists were
approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, Lanphier, and
Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Capital Improvements Quarterly Reports. Manager Williams referred the Trustees
to the reports summarizing the status of capital improvement projects for the
various departments. He noted that the Village has accomplished all that it set
out to do during the past fiscal year with the exception of refurbishing the
Public Works Facility. This project has been postponed pending the development
of a coordinated plan.
Trustee Lanphier complimented the Public Works Department on the completion of
the Willow underpass.
Bid No. 99-002 – Street Rehabilitation. Mr. Williams reviewed the Agenda Report
recommending the award of the bid to Curran Contracting Company for
$429,200.07. Trustee Derning moved to approve the award of the bid as
recommended. His motion was seconded by Trustee Lanphier. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, and
Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland reported on her attendance at the award of
CALEA accreditation to the Police Department. She congratulated Chief Gallagher
and his entire department for their efforts.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported that Highwood has now been admitted
to RED Center. He said that the Center is moving toward the acquisition of
their own location and indicated that he was encouraging them to find an
autonomous site.
Village Manager.
Y2K Preparations: Manager Williams reported that Village staff continues to
prepare for possible Y2K computer problems and that most preparations have been
completed.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 9:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:26 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:27 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
April 13, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved April 20, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
13, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and Bill Meuer. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Acting Director of Community Development
Brian Norkus, and about 25 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced the addition of the appointment of a Canvassing
Board to Council’s Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
IV. Appointment of Canvassing Board.
The Trustees approved the appointment of President Holland, Village Manager
Williams and Trustee Don Derning to the Canvassing Board and scheduled a
meeting for Tuesday, April 20, 1999 at 10:00 a.m. to canvass the results of the
April 13, 1999 Consolidated Election
V. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-221-99 – Amending Gross Floor Area Requirements – Continued
Public Hearing and Introduction. President Holland reopened the Public Hearing
opened on February 2, 1999, and since continued to March 2, 1999, April 6, 1999
and this date. She noted that after hearing remarks from Manager Williams and
Trustee Lanphier the Council would hear from any residents who had not been
able to speak at the previous meeting before deliberating among themselves.
Manager Williams reviewed correspondence regarding the proposed ordinance
received from residents in response to the public mailing. He then highlighted
items from the Agenda Report, noting that after discussions with staff, the
changes in the drafted ordinance needed to be made with regard to the
following: (1) the proposed GFA bonus for storage sheds, play houses, etc.,
should be increased from 30 sq. ft. to 50 sq. ft.; (2) the proposed language
regarding attached garages needed amending; and (3) the procedures for
calculating height, especially on a sloping lot, needed to be clarified. Mr.
Williams also commented on calculations cited in a letter received from Mrs.
Niehoff, 557 Orchard Ln.
Mr. Williams then asked Attorney Janega to explain staff’s current thinking
with regard to renovations, saying that staff wanted to include language that
would prevent someone from demolishing all but a small feature of a pre-1989
home and calling it a “renovation” in an attempt to take advantage of the GFA
allowances for existing buildings.
Attorney Janega explained that she had researched the Code of Federal
Regulations for examples of definitions and standards regarding rehabilitation,
renovation and additions. She walked the Trustees through suggested changes to
definitions and the proposed language defining “maximum building size” as
contained in the drafted ordinance, so as to address staff’s concerns.
Trustee Duhl expressed some concern that the rules would not be liberal enough
to include substantial additions. Ms. Janega stated that the sample definitions
would be modified as necessary to allow existing homes with substantial
additions to be treated as existing buildings as long as they maintained the
style, scale and architectural features of the original building.
Following Ms. Janega’s discussion, Trustee Lanphier distributed photographs of
Village homes, of various architectural designs, in the 1/6 acre zoning
district, and explained how they demonstrate some of the issues under
consideration. She noted that in many instances, design dictates a de facto
setback plane, which works effectively to preserve light and air and said that
in her opinion, articulation is a more important design feature than an
enforced set back plane. Trustee Lanphier also mentioned some design features
that she hopes the Village will choose to encourage in the future, such as
detached garages matched to the construction of the home.
President Holland thanked Trustee Lanphier for her time and efforts. Then, she
recognized members of the audience wishing to address the Council.
John Snyder, 592 Cherry, expressed his concern about overbuilding, but said
that some of the older homes in the Village, particularly smaller ones, may
need to be torn down and replaced with homes designed to fit today’s needs. He
said that in his opinion it would be a mistake to dictate that these homes be
renovated instead of replaced. Mr. Snyder also said that, in his opinion, the
GFA issue is different and apart from the design issues. He voiced some concern
that more restrictive regulations might inhibit the attractive design features
that the Village is, in fact, attempting to encourage. He suggested that the
Village consult with independent architects to determine the consequences of
the proposed changes. Overall, he said it was important to maintain the
character of the Village.
Veronica Jiganti, 325 Linden, reviewed the problems resulting from the
construction of a large home next door to her property. She urged the Council
to take steps to protect existing property owners from builders, particularly
with regard to side yard regulations.
Jim Collins, 1005 Ash, remarked on Mr. Snyder’s comments, saying that there are
homes in the Village which are difficult, if not impossible, to renovate, and
that the Village does not want to stymie development. In his opinion,
homeowners ought to have the option of selling to developers if they so desire
because renovation is a long, involved process that many don’t choose to
endure. Mr. Collins also raised questions about (1) off-street parking places
on the public right of way, which he suggested the Village ought to require to
be grassed over if the home is purchased by a developer; (2) driveways that
remain after an attached garage is converted to living space, leaving a
driveway that does not lead to a garage, but serves as an unsightly parking
area; and (3) alignment of garages with houses, especially on corner lots, and
the point from which setbacks are measured when there is no sidewalk.
Trustee Lanphier explained that the proposed requirement for 20-foot setback
for garages on corner lots is to provide sufficient space for a car to pull in
without overhanging the sidewalk or public walkway and creating a safety
hazard.
Trustee Derning explained that it is necessary to deal with lot lines when
determining set backs, not the location of a sidewalk or roadway, because
public property cannot be combined with private property.
Phil Enquist, 476 Provident, an architect, expressed his overall support for
the Trustees’ efforts to reduce the bulk of new construction. He, however,
voiced concern about the setback plane concept and spoke to sketches he
submitted previously to the Council illustrating the potential problems he
foresees if a setback plane is implemented. He said that he feared that the
imposition of setback planes would contribute to the use of unusual, complex
roof forms designed simply to solve the setback puzzle. He commented that, in
his opinion, the proposed set back planes are too restrictive to work
effectively on traditional Colonial or Georgian architecture, in which gables
face the side lot line and extend into the setback plane.
President Holland thanked Mr. Enquist for his time and drawings saying they
were very helpful in understanding his point.
Mary Felise, 350 Linden, spoke in support of the Village’s efforts to control
overbuilding as well as of her neighbor’s interests on Linden. She urged the
Village to be diligent in its protection of homeowners’ rights and residents’
privacy, gardens and yards. She also asked that the Village focus attention on
larger lots where homes are being demolished in favor of new construction,
saying that she had noted several recent tear downs of architecturally
significant homes.
Manager Williams clarified that at present the zoning changes will not affect
the regulations in effect regarding new construction on larger lots. At present
the FAR ordinance is designed so that the larger the lot, the smaller the house
gets relative to the size of the lot.
Charlie Hoffman, 315 Woodland, commended the Council on its endeavor to develop
a meaningful ordinance, saying that the Trustees and Village staff have done an
excellent job of encouraging residents to participate in the legislative
process as well as making every effort to preserve the sense of community. With
that in mind, he suggested amending the porch area requirements to permit the
construction of a one story, open, wrap around porch, which would encourage
neighborliness.
Mrs. Niehoff, 557 Orchard Ln., responded to Mr. Williams’s earlier comments
addressing her letter to the Council. She said that her 110 year old home
cannot structurally support a renovation, so inevitably, it will be torn down.
She believes that the proposed ordinance negatively impacts the redevelopment
prospects for her property and reiterated her request for an exemption from any
new regulations.
Joanna Karatzas, 476 Provident, clarified several of her husband’s (Mr.
Enquist’s) earlier comments regarding existing homes, particularly noting that
an eight foot ceiling is extremely low for today’s construction.
There being no further speakers from the floor, President Holland closed the
Public Hearing and the Trustees proceeded to discuss the issues.
The first issues to be discussed were the two additional possible zoning code
changes identified by staff in the Agenda Report: (1) Requiring total side yard
setbacks of 14 feet for the 1/6 and ¼ acre zoning districts; and (2)
Eliminating GFA variances altogether, or permitting only “de minimis”
variations.
With regard to the first issue, there was consensus among the Trustees to
impose the 14 foot setback requirement, with minimums of six feet and eight
feet. In conjunction with this, it was agreed that if the side yards are six
feet and eight feet, a two-foot articulation to the interior of the lot should
be required along the six foot side yard after the first 30 feet of the
building.
With regard to the variance issue, Trustee Duhl said that he was vehemently
opposed to not permitting variations for existing homes. He also expressed
opposition to the concept of a specified de minimis variation, saying it would
have to be an arbitrary number that could come to be viewed as an automatic
variation. He noted that, historically, variances for new construction are
never granted, and that, since it is impossible to predict future situations,
there is no need to eliminate the opportunity for existing homeowners to seek a
variance. He indicated that he believed that if a homeowner can prove a
legitimate hardship, they ought to be allowed to do so.
Trustee Hilton agreed, saying that he believed there ought to be an avenue of
appeal for all existing homeowners.
In defense of eliminating variances, Trustee Lanphier pointed out that the
Council has been lenient in granting GFA variations because of inequities
incorporated into the GFA calculations, particularly with regard to roofs and
basements. Under the proposed ordinance, these inequities would be removed and
the playing field leveled for all homeowners. She remarked that it is very
difficult to substantiate a true claim of hardship when pleading for a GFA
variance.
Trustee Derning indicated that he had no problem with incorporating a de
minimis variance into the ordinance, saying that the option to apply to the
Council for a greater variation would still exist.
Trustee Meuer suggested that if there is no limitation imposed, Councils will
continue to grant variations without any clear-cut limits. He said he believes
the proposed changes will level the playing field and could support the concept
of saying, essentially, “this is the playing field, and these are the rules.”
Manager Williams recommended that if the Council decides to continue to permit
GFA variances, it should make it clear that any such variance must be tied to a
hardship that runs with the land.
Trustee Derning reminded the others that in each instance of a variance being
granted, the request had been considered by numerous individuals and
legislative entities, and that he believed that the ultimate decision of the
many is usually better than the decision of a few.
The Trustees then discussed with Attorney Janega the most effective means of
tying any request for a variance directly to the land and whether or not
explicit language should be incorporated into the ordinance. She explained that
the standard is part of the body of law on variances, that it is possible to
incorporate such language into the standards for “practical difficulty” and
“particular hardship.” She noted, however, that such language could limit the
scope of the Council’s discretion in looking at the totality of a request.
Following the discussion, President Holland called for a consensus from the
Trustees as to whether or not specific language should be included. Trustees
Hilton, Meuer and Lanphier indicated support for its inclusion; Trustees Duhl,
Lien and Derning were opposed. Citing her experience that few hardships truly
run with the land, and that including specific language might have unintended
consequences, President Holland voted against adding specific language,
therefore allowing a certain amount of flexibility as individual situations
arise.
The Trustees then discussed the key points raised during the April 6th Public
Hearing, as enumerated in the Agenda Report, and proceeded on to the Policy
Issue Decision Matrix provided therein.
After an extended discussion of the issue of height measurements, Manager
Williams suggested that staff return with a further in-depth analysis of the
ramifications of implementing the proposed procedures.
With regard to requiring front windows and requiring new roof lines to conform
with the existing neighborhood, there was consensus among the Trustees that
these were design features and should not be legislated.
The consensus of the Trustees was opposed to imposing a setback plane, but in
favor of requiring articulation, as discussed earlier in the meeting. Manager
Williams suggested that after a year of implementing this regulation, staff
report back to the Council in a year with an analysis of its effectiveness.
The Trustees also decided not to grant a bonus for houses located in the
floodplain that are not allowed to have basements.
The Council discussed whether covered, but unenclosed areas, such as a
cantilevered second story over an open area should be counted as part of gross
floor area. The members of the Council were in agreement that the recently
adopted provision to include such areas should not be changed. The Council also
determined that the scope of the proposed 275 square foot roofed lot coverage
bonus for front porches should be expanded to include a porch that wraps around
from the front of the house to the side, but that the amount of the bonus
should not be increased.
The consensus of the Trustees was that the proposed changes for attics, gross
floor area, and dormers should be included in the ordinance, but that the
prohibition against shared driveways, landscaping between driveways and setback
planes should not be included. The Council further confirmed that the
provisions requiring garages on corner lots to have a minimum setback of 20
feet, and allowing a second story above a garage attached to the back of a
house should remain in the proposed ordinance. The Council agreed that the
proposed limitation on the width of attached garages should be included, but
that the permitted width should be sufficient to allow a two-car garage on a
50-foot wide lot. The Council also agreed that the height bonus in the 1/6 and
¼ acre districts should be eliminated and that the ordinance should be revised
to eliminate height penalties or bonuses resulting from measuring building
height from the sidewalk rather than at the grade at the building.
In discussing articulation, the Council again reiterated that a two-foot
articulation towards the center of the lot should be required in the smaller
side yards after a 30-foot building length, provided that the building is more
than 40 feet long. The Council also agreed that the two-foot articulation
should be for the remaining length of the building.
In discussing basements, the Council agreed that the allowable basement height
for exclusion from gross floor area calculations could be higher for basements
built before 1989 than the 3-foot limitations being proposed for basements
constructed since that time. There was a lengthy discussion on whether
basements of homes built before 1989 should be excluded entirely from gross
floor area calculations, and Attorney Janega noted that the proposed draft
would include basement gross floor area for such homes if the exposed basement
wall is more than 3.5 feet above grade. The Council expressed concern that the
formula in the ordinance would create “phantom” floor area in basements as the
current zoning ordinance creates “phantom” gross floor area for attics and
directed the staff to develop a formula that would not penalize the higher
basements that were often typical of older construction.
Trustee Lanphier questioned why the point of reference for lots along Lake
Michigan was set at the water’s edge, when the water’s edge could fluctuate.
Attorney Janega explained that the proposed changes clarify the existing
ordinance language and codify the written zoning interpretations of the current
zoning ordinance. She explained that the consistent and long standing practice
of the Village has been to measure lot area and setbacks of such lots based on
the location of the water’s edge, since, by statute, the bed of Lake Michigan
is owned by the State of Illinois.
Finally, Attorney Janega informed the Council that she had discussed the draft
amendments pertaining to driveway turnarounds with Public Works Director Steve
Saunders, who expressed some concerns about the draft language. Ms. Janega
suggested that the provisions in question were not essential to the overall
bulk regulations and recommended that they be excluded from the proposed
ordinance and considered at a later time.
President Holland asked if there was a motion to introduce Ordinance No. MC-
221-99. The introduction was moved by Trustee Derning and seconded by Trustee
Lanphier. On voice vote, the motion passed.
VI. Executive Session.
Trustee Lanphier moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Meuer seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 10:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:50 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien, and Meuer. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
VII. Adjournment.
Trustee Meuer moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lanphier seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:51 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
April 20, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved May 4, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
20, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, Penfield Lanphier, John Lien,
and Bill Meuer and Trustee-elect Barbara Aquilino. Absent: None. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Acting Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and
about eleven persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
May 4, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, and Trustee-elect
Aquilino indicated that they would be able to attend. Trustee Lien said that he
expected to be out of town.
May 11, 1999, Study Session. Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Trustee-
elect Aquilino indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
April 6, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lanphier,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
April 13, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Barbara Watt, a resident of Northbrook, speaking on behalf of her aunt, Grace
Erikson, 809 Oak St., asked the Village to investigate an incident which
occurred on her aunt’s property on April 13, 1999. Both President Holland and
Manager Williams indicated that they would do so and respond accordingly.
VII. Presentation of Award to Ed McKee.
President Holland presented Finance Director Ed McKee with an award for
financial reporting achievement from the Government Finance Officers’
Association. In accepting the award, Mr. McKee thanked the Council for its
continuing support and the fine staff afforded him.
VIII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-221-99 Amending Gross Floor Area Requirements – Adoption.
Manager Williams briefly reviewed the six amendments incorporated into the
draft ordinance as a result of Council’s discussions at the April 13th meeting,
including the following:
1.   Increasing the minimum side yard requirements in the “1/4” and “1/6”
zoning districts to a total of 14 feet, with at least one 8-foot side yard;
2.   Deleting the setback plane requirements;
3.   Deleting the prohibition against shared driveways and the requirement that
specified an one-foot landscaping buffer between shared driveways;
4.   Requiring an articulation of at least 2 feet on one side wall adjacent to
the narrowest side yard. The articulation must be within 30 feet of the front
façade if a side wall is at least 40 feet long;
5.   Allowing the 275 square-foot bonus for roofed lot coverage to be applied
to a "wrap around" porch provided that the portion of the porch adjacent to the
side yard meets all setback requirements;
6.   Deleting the proposed language regarding driveway turnarounds.
He, then, discussed the following additional amendments which also had been
included in the revised draft in the agenda materials.
1.   Adding a specific definition for the term "rehabilitation";
2.   Increasing the height of basements for pre-1989 homes to 4 feet above
grade before the basement is counted as part of GFA;
3.   Deleting the reference point of the sidewalk for height measurements, so
that all building heights will be measured at the average grade around the
house, thereby assuring that all houses will be treated equally vis-à-vis
height calculations;
4.   Modifying the 50% of front building line limitation on front-facing
attached garages to allow a width of up to 22 feet (for a two-car garage) where
the maximum allowable width of the building is less than 44 feet.
Following Mr. Williams’s comments, Attorney Janega addressed technical changes
made to the draft ordinance. She pointed out the new recitals that had been
added to the revised draft, which summarize the legislative history of the
issue as well as the purpose and intent of the amendments, saying that these
could be used to clarify interpretations of the ordinance and provide grounds
for factual findings and evidence. Ms. Janega also explained four additional
changes to the revised draft, adding one additional paragraph to the recitals
and modifying the language of Section 22.02(b)1 and the definitions of building
height and rehabilitation.
Mr. Williams, Ms. Janega, and Brian Norkus, Acting Director of Community
Development, then responded to questions from the Trustees regarding specific
provisions of the proposed ordinance.
President Holland called for a motion to accept the new recitals and the first
six amendments enumerated earlier by Mr. Williams and contained in the revised
draft in the agenda materials. Trustee Derning so moved, seconded by Trustee
Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Then President Holland called for a motion to incorporate the additional
amendments outlined by Ms. Janega. The motion was made by Trustee Meuer and
seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Lanphier. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Finally, President Holland called for a motion to adopt Ordinance No. MC-221-
99, as amended. Trustee Lanphier made the motion and was seconded by Trustee
Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Holland thanked Manager Williams, Ms. Janega, and the entire
Community Development staff for their efforts in bringing about these changes
to the Zoning Ordinance. She expressed the hope that these changes will keep
the character of the Village intact and reflect the desires of its residents
for years to come.
Manager Williams concluded with comments that this ordinance, in conjunction
with the new subdivision regulations and the changes to the commercial zoning
district regulations, were major legislative accomplishments which would,
indeed, preserve the character of Winnetka for future generations.
Ordinance No. M-569-99 – Special Use Permit: Station Park – Adoption. Trustee
Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt the ordinance. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl,
Derning, Lanphier and Meuer. Nays: None. Absent: None.
IX. Unfinished Business.
None
X. Seating of New Village Council.
Local Canvass Board: Canvass and Proclamation and
Administration of Oath of Office for Village President Louise Holland and
Trustees-elect Barbara Aquilino, John Lien and John Powell. There being no
further unfinished business for the sitting Council, President Holland asked
the Village Clerk to review the results of the Local Canvass Board. Village
Clerk Williams read the results of the April 13, 1999, election and reported
that the Board had proclaimed Louise Holland duly elected to a second term as
Village President, John Lien duly elected to a second term as Village Trustee,
and Barbara Aquilino and John Powell duly elected to a first term as Village
Trustees. He administered the Oath of Office to Trustees Lien and Aquilino and
then to President Holland.
XI. Ordinances and Resolutions
Resolution No. R-1466-99 – Honoring Trustee Lanphier for Village Service -
Adoption. Resolution No. R-1467-99 – Honoring Trustee Meuer for Village Service
- Adoption.
President Holland read aloud the Resolutions honoring Trustees Lanphier and
Meuer for four years of service as Village Trustees. Trustee Derning moved for
adoption. He was seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Hilton, Lien and Duhl.
Nays: None. Absent: Powell.
Each of the retiring Trustees thanked Village residents for the opportunity to
serve and their fellow Council members and Village staff for their experience,
expertise, and support.
Resolution No. R-1463-99 – Appointing Ed McKee as Village Treasurer – Adoption.
Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the resolution appointing Ed McKee as Village
Treasurer, a position historically filled by the Finance Director, pursuant
provisions of the Village Code. The motion was seconded by Trustee Derning. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl,
Derning, Aquilino, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
Resolution No. R-1464-99 – Appointment SWANCC Representatives – Adoption.
Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the resolution appointing Louise Holland as
Director and Douglas Williams as Alternate Director of SWANCC for the next two
year term, beginning on May 1, 1999. Trustee Derning seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
Ordinance No. M-571-99 – Disposal of Village Equipment – Introduction. Trustee
Duhl, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the ordinance, consistent
with past practice, allowing for the disposal of surplus firefighting equipment
to the joint training facility in Glenview, as well as the disposal of property
recovered by the Police Department. The ordinance also gives the Finance
Director umbrella authorization to dispose of any computer equipment during the
current fiscal year. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Hilton and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Powell.
Resolution No. R-1465-99 – Adopting Investment Policy for Village – Adoption.
Manager Williams explained that during last year’s legislative session, the
State Legislature adopted a new section to the Public Funds Investment Act
which requires the adoption of a written investment policy. Since the Village
already has such a policy, the proposed resolution authorizes the adoption of a
slightly revised version of the investment policy that meets all of the
necessary requirements.
After Mr. McKee responded to questions from the Trustees, Trustee Hilton moved
to adopt the resolution. His motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl,
Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
Ordinance No. M-572-99 – Designating Depositories for the Village –
Introduction.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1121 and 1122. Trustee Lien pointed out that the Village’s
accountants had been instrumental in the collection of substantial overdue fees
from one of the telephone companies. Then, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1121 in the total amount of $178,491.54 and Warrant List No. 1122 in the total
amount of $510,104.41. Trustee Duhl seconded the motion.
Trustee Hilton inquired as to why the warrant lists showed that refuse tipping
fees were being paid for the month of May. Mr. McKee responded that these fees
are paid in advance and then proved up once a year.
By roll call vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Duhl, Hilton, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
Bid No. 99-005 – Parkway Tree Maintenance and Utility Line Clearance. Manager
Williams explained that the project had been broken into four sections and
reviewed the bids, noting staff’s award recommendations as follows:
 
Tree Trimming/Line Clearance Nels Johnson      $111 per hr.
Tree & Stump Removal         R. W. Hendricksen $29,970.00
Parkway Trimming             R. W. Hendricksen $17,315.00
Emergency Work               Bartlett Tree     $40 per hr.
  
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the recommended
award of the bids. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Powell.
DK Recycling Systems Monthly Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to
the report filed by DK Recycling reviewing the operations at the Winnetka
Compost Site for the month of March. She noted that now compost will be
available only during the week from behind the Public Works Yards gates.
X. Reports.
Village President. President Holland advised the Trustees of a Chamber of
Commerce meeting being held on Thursday morning regarding suggested
beautification techniques to improve the look of retail space throughout the
Village.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning noted that he had attended a meeting of the
Chamber of Commerce and updated them on Council’s activities. He indicated that
an upcoming meeting would be devoted to commercial development and invited any
interested persons to attend.
Village Manager.
Hibbard Road Funding. Manager Williams reported that the Illinois Department of
Transportation has officially approve its share of the funding, $200,000, for
the rehabilitation of Hibbard Road from Tower to Winnetka Avenue.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland announced the appointment of Trustee Michael Duhl as
President Pro Tem of the Council and called for a motion to approve the
appointment. Trustee Lien so moved, seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call
vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Derning, Aquilino, and Hilton.
Nays: Trustee Duhl. Absent: Trustee Powell.
XIII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Derning, Duhl, Lien, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIV. Adjournment.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
8:55 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
May 4, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved May 18, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 4,
1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, and John
Powell. Absent: Trustee John Lien. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Director of Public
Works Steven Saunders, and about eight persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Administration of Oath of Office to Trustee-elect John R. Powell, Sr.  
Village Clerk Doug Williams administered the Oath of Office to Trustee-elect
John Powell.
IV. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
V. Quorum.
May 11, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
May 18, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
VI. Approval of Minutes
April 20, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VII. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VIII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-571-99 – Disposal of Village Equipment – Adoption. Trustee
Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance allowing
for the disposal of surplus firefighting equipment, property recovered by the
police, and computer equipment. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, and Duhl. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Ordinance No. M-572-99 – Designating Depositories for the Village – Adoption.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the ordinance
designating the following as depositories for the Village: Amalgamated Bank of
Chicago, Bank One, the Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago, The Illinois Funds, and
Harris Trust and Savings Bank. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Hilton. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Ordinance No. M-573-99 – Zoning Variation: 1310 Forest Glen North –
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed the specifics of a request to allow an
increased maximum permitted building size (GFA) resulting from a proposal to
construct a kitchen addition. She explained that the home in question is
already nonconforming and that the construction would increase the existing
nonconformity, but that the owners also proposed to create a larger lot by
subdividing an existing zoning lot. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals
and the Plan Commission had voted 4-0 and 7-3, respectively, in favor of the
proposals before them.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Trustee Duhl confirmed that the subdivision of the two commonly owned lots
would result in one lot being substantially smaller than most others in the
neighborhood. Ms. McCammack said that moving the existing lot line to increase
the lot area for the house would, in fact, result in the second lot’s being
reduced in size. The smaller lot would be of minimum conforming size and would
be the fourth smallest relative to other lots in the area.
Trustee Hilton inquired as to what kind of development could be put on the
smaller of the two new lots should it be sold off. Ms. McCammack indicated that
any development would have to conform with all zoning regulations, but that she
did not believe that the smaller lot would be a saleable lot.
Trustee Derning pointed out that the proposed construction on the home on the
larger lot was the issue at hand and that it would, in fact, reduce the degree
of nonconformity of that house should the subdivision be granted. He stated
that he would be in favor of supporting the recommendation of the Zoning Board
of Appeals, saying that the totality of the argument is persuasive, especially
when that decision creates no harm to anybody.
Manager Williams clarified that by subdividing the property as proposed and
constructing the proposed new kitchen, the building on the larger lot would
still be expanding its nonconformity relative to GFA.
Attorney Janega addressed some questions from the Trustees regarding
interpretation of the Zoning Ordinance. She explained that there are currently
two adjoining parcels owned by the applicant and the two parcels are used
together as a single zoning lot, so the proposed addition could be built
without a variation on that existing zoning lot.
Trustee Hilton indicated that he saw no necessity for permitting the requested
addition for which the variation is being sought.
President Holland recognized the applicant, Chuck Bohrer, who reviewed the
history of their petition and their plans. He disputed the fact that the
smaller lot would not be a saleable property and stated that his proposal, if
granted, would result in less bulk in the neighborhood because the second lot
would be smaller than it is now.
President Holland explained that the Bohrers could keep the two parcels as one
lot and add on to their residence as they wish, or subdivide the property and
leave their home intact, without any addition, and could do either without a
variation.
Trustee Duhl expressed disagreement with the Zoning Board’s recommendation,
saying that he does not see creating the smaller lot as a good alternative.
Trustee Aquilino agreed with Trustees Duhl and Hilton because without the
subdivision, the applicant has sufficient property to make any desired
additions to his existing home.
Mr. Bohrer stated that he believed the second parcel is a buildable lot as it
stands today, and that if it could not be sold off at some time, he would be
losing a substantial amount of the value of his property since he had purchased
the second lot under the belief that it was buildable.
Trustee Powell indicated that he believed that the addition was attractive and
nonintrusive to the neighbors and ought to be permitted.
President Holland explained the procedural options open to Mr. Bohrer given
that the consensus of the Trustees was not in favor of the variation when a
majority vote was required for introduction. Trustee Duhl indicated that
although he was opposed to granting the variation, he would vote in favor of
introduction if that was what the applicant desired.
Upon consideration, Mr. Bohrer, asked to defer the decision on introduction,
pending resolution of some of the outstanding issues in question. Therefore,
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to table the matter until May
18, 1999. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Ordinance No. M-574-99 – Zoning Variation: 480 Oak Street – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed the specifics of a request to allow an increased maximum
permitted building size (GFA) resulting from a proposal to build an addition on
the existing residence. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-
0 to recommend approval of the variation as requested.
Trustees Duhl and Powell discussed with Ms. McCammack the residence’s location
on the shoreline of Lake Michigan and the effects of erosion on the size of the
property.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-575-99 – Zoning Variations: 220 Poplar Street – Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the specifics of a request to permit construction of a
two-car garage that will exceed the permitted roof lot coverage and that will
be placed within the required front yard setback. She noted that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 4-0 to recommend approval of the variations as
requested.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1468-99 – Prevailing Rate of Wages – Adoption. Manager
Williams explained that pursuant to the provisions of the State of Illinois
Prevailing Wage Act, the Village is required annually to investigate and
ascertain the prevailing rate of wages for laborers, mechanics, and other
workers in the Village employed in performing construction of public works.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing the Village’s Prevailing Rate of Wages. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and
Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Resolution No. R-1456-99 – Hackberry West Subdivision – Adoption. Ms. McCammack
reviewed the proposal to reconfigure two existing irregularly shaped lots. She
noted that as a result of extensive discussions between the applicant and his
attorney and Village staff, the easement requirements have been modified.
Trustee Duhl inquired whether all of the easement issues had been resolved with
the applicant, and Attorney Janega replied that further assurances and
agreements from the Village had been requested, but that those are inconsistent
with established practices and have not been included in the proposed
resolution.
Manager Williams added that the Village is not requiring anything which it does
not absolutely need.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
approving the Hackberry West Subdivision, subject to the conditions stated
therein. The motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino,
Powell, Hilton and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
IX. Unfinished Business.
None
X. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1123 and 1124. Trustee Duhl moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1123 in the total amount of $348,283.22 and Warrant List No. 1124 in the
total amount of $191,055.41. Trustee Derning seconded the motion. By roll call
vote the warrant lists were approved unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Derning,
Aquilino, Powell, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Bid No. 99-001 – Hibbard Road Rehabilitation & Water Main Replacement. Mr.
Williams reviewed the bids received for the rehabilitation of Hibbard Road from
Tower to Hill Roads and the replacement of the existing water main on Hibbard
Road from Tower to Elm. He noted that staff recommended the award of the bid to
the lowest bidder, DiPaolo Company, in the total amount of $717,717.00. Mr.
Williams also commented on the low bid for the water main portion of the
project, saying that it would exceed the budgeted amount by $70,108.50, and
that at end of fiscal year, the capital improvements budget may be exceeded.
Trustee Hilton moved to accept the bid of DiPaolo Company. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Bid No. 99-003 – Street Reconstruction. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids received
for street reconstruction, explaining that the work would encompass excavation
of pavement and replacement of curbs and gutters. He enumerated the steps which
were being taken to ensure timely completion of the work and explained why the
bid exceeded staff’s original estimate. Finally, he recommended that the bid be
awarded to Arrow Road Construction in the amount of $483,722.84.
Trustee Powell moved to accept the bid of Arrow Road Construction. The motion
was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Duhl, Derning and Aquilino. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Bid No. 99-010 – S&C Switchgear Units. Mr. McInturff reviewed the bids received
for equipment which will provide switching capabilities for the underground
electrical system. He recommended that the bid be awarded to Border States
Electric Sup. in the amount of $29,713.00.
Trustee Hilton moved to accept the bid as recommended. The motion was seconded
by Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Lien.
Public Works Yards Architectural Study. Mr. Saunders explained that pursuant to
direction provided by the Village Council, the Village has solicited proposals
to provide architectural services related to a space needs analysis and
preliminary design for rehabilitation or replacement of the Village’s Public
Works and Water & Electric Maintenance facility located at 1390 Willow Road. He
reviewed the scope of the proposed work and the process used to evaluate the
competing bids. He reported that as a result of the process, Legat Architects
of Oak Brook, Illinois, received staff’s recommendation for award of the
contract for a Space Needs Analysis and Preliminary Design Study for a proposed
fee of $16,000. He pointed out that staff also recommended that the scope of
work be modified to include the use of an independent cost estimating service.
Legat has indicated their willingness to provide this additional service at a
fee not to exceed $6,500.
Trustee Derning moved to approve the award of the bid as recommended, including
the independent cost estimating service. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Duhl. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Cul de Sac Standards. Manager Williams reviewed the history of this issue,
noting that the possibility of creating a cul-de-sac at Bertling Lane and
Winnetka Avenue had been considered during the fall of 1998 as one of seven
possibilities for controlling traffic and mitigating the impact of New Trier
High School on the surrounding neighborhood. In response to direction from
Council at it’s October 20, 1998, meeting, he outlined the criteria, as
follows, which have been identified by staff during the interim as possible
standards for creating a new cul-de-sac. He emphasized the importance of making
it a priority to retain Winnetka’s grid system which spreads traffic out
throughout the neighborhoods instead of concentrating it in a few areas. The
standards for the creation of a new cul-de-sac would require that it:
1.   Not divert significant volumes of traffic into other residential areas;
2.   Would be supported (by petition) by (significant) 75% of residents of the
proposed cul-de-sac;
3.   Not jeopardize public safety vis-à-vis police, fire, and ambulance
services;
4.   Not create new traffic hazards;
5.   Respond to an identifiable traffic hazard;
6.   Require that a turn around be built according to Village standards.
Mr. Williams indicated that staff suggested that a Public Hearing be held
whenever a petition for the creation of a new cul-de-sac is received.
Mr. Saunders then commented that the key component of traffic calming is that
you don’t want to provide solutions at the expense of general traffic safety or
at the expense of diverting traffic into adjoining neighborhoods.
In response to Trustee Duhl’s question as to whether the ideas proposed were
the result of staff’s brainstorming or whether they were specific, recognized
standards, Mr. Saunders replied that no such standards are available.
Jim Kruemplestaedter, 178 Myrtle, commented on the proposed cul-de-sac at
Bertling Lane and asked questions of Mr. Williams and Mr. Saunders about the
identified criteria.
Manager Williams suggested that if there was consensus among the Trustees to
proceed on the question of establishing standards, they ought to be set down by
resolution at a meeting in the near future. The Trustees concurred unanimously.
XI. Reports
Village President.
Village Trustees. Trustee Hilton reported on his attendance at a Chamber of
Commerce meeting on commercial development. He said that the Chamber identified
building obsolescence and absentee ownership as two of the major factors
deterring new businesses from locating in the community. President Holland also
commented that a good marketing tool for prospective tenants had been
identified as a need and pointed out that numerous experts commented that
downtown areas are being redesigned to look like Winnetka’s.
Village Manager.
Revised Preliminary Plans for FEMA. Manager Williams reported that the Village
had received a letter from FEMA concerning the appeal information sent to them
last June. He indicated that revised maps, stamped “Revised Preliminary”
reflect the changes requested by the Village, restricting the floodway to the
area west of Hibbard Road and removing numerous “island” areas from the
proposed re-mapping of the floodplain.
Mr. Williams also reported that the Village had clearly benefited substantially
from the installation of the new pump stations, as evidenced by the reduction
in flooding during the recent heavy rainfall. 
XII. Appointments.
President Holland announced that Robert Geraghty would be leaving the Fire and
Police Commission and proposed the appointment of Donna Pinsof in his stead.
She also announced the retirements of Sandra Berger from the Police Pension
Board and Edie Howell from the Design Review Board.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the appointment of
Donna Pinsof to the Fire and Police Commission. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
Then President Holland announced the formation of a Beautification Committee,
chaired by Trustee Duhl. The members will be: Elise Covey, 1503 Edgewood Ln.;
Iris Miller, 1373 Westmoor Trail; Pat Horne, 791 Bryant and Betty Skalski, 611
Ash, as representatives of the Chamber of Commerce; Donna Pinsof, 25 Indian
Hill Rd.; Michael Levitin, 507 Cedar; Byram Dickes, 505 Hoyt Ln., Manager
Williams and Director of Public Works Saunders, and representatives from the
Design review Committee, Forestry Commission, Plan Commission, Landmark
Preservation Commission, and the Winnetka Garden Guild.
Finally, President Holland announced the Trustees’ different board and
commission assignments, as follows:
President Pro Tem        Mike Duhl
                         John Lien , Chairman
Code Review Committee    Mike Duhl
                         Dave Hilton
Chamber of Commerce      Don Derning
                         Dave Hilton
Warrants Review          John Powell
Museum Liaison           John Powell
Youth                    Barbara Aquilino
Winnetka Report          Dave Hilton
Plan Commission          John Lien
Red Center               Don Derning
Web Page                 John Lien
Beautification Committee Mike Duhl
                         Barbara Aquilino
 
XIII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:38 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, and Powell. Absent: Trustee
Lien. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIV. Adjournment.
Trustee Duhl moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:39 p.m.
