                  Deputy Clerk
May 11, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved May 18, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
11, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Finance Director Ed McKee, and Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff.
II. Bond Options.
Manager Williams reviewed the history of the existing outstanding bond issues
and financial structures. He noted that with the enactment of tax cap
legislation, the Village is no longer allowed to reallocate revenue from the
payment of the Hubbard Woods Parking Deck Bond to pay the debt of the Public
Safety Building. He pointed out that unless the Village issues new debt before
December 1999, it will lose its ability to continue to levy $138,000 which was
used to pay the 1989 parking lot debt and, then, earmarked for additional debt
retirement for the Public Safety Building. Manager Williams explained that the
Village can preserve the $138,000 levy by issuing debt of about $1.4 million,
which will be needed to implement the plans to renovate, and possibly expand,
the Village Yards. Saying that the Village’s property tax rate already contains
$138,000 for debt retirement, Mr. Williams pointed out that the issuance of
$1.4 million in general obligation bonds would not increase the Village’s
property tax rate, in keeping with representations made to residents in 1994.
He asked Council to authorize staff to begin the process to issue the $1.4
million in general obligation debt.
Kevin McCanna from Speer Financial, Inc., discussed the bond rating process in
general, as well as the procedures required to issue the subject bond. He said
that the Village currently has a AAA rating and that he expected there would be
no difficulty in retaining that rating.
President Holland pointed out that the Village has very low debt in comparison
to other communities.
Manager Williams expressed his opinion that it was important to preserve the
$138,000 tax levy.
There was consensus among the Trustees to begin the process to issue $1.4
million in general obligation debt.
III. State Gift Act.
Manager Williams briefly reviewed a memorandum prepared by Attorney Janega
discussing the implications and issues raised by the enactment of the State
Gift Ban, Public Act 90-737, effective January 1, 1999. He noted that
compliance with the Act raises three principal policy issues: (1) how and to
what extent the prohibition against the solicitation and acceptance of gifts
should be stated; (2) whether an ethics officer should be appointed and, if so,
who should be so designated; and (3) whether to establish an ethics commission
and, if so, who should serve on that commission.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees agreed to proceed as recommended by Ms.
Janega, as follows: (1) adopt a specific ordinance establishing the gift ban
prohibitions, incorporating specific provisions that reflect the Village’s
particular circumstances; and (2) designate the Village Manager as the ethics
officer and the Village Council as the Ethics Commission.
IV. Tree Planting Policy.
Mr. Williams reviewed the response of the Public Works Director, including a
survey by the Forester and the recommendation of the Forestry Commission, to
requests from residents on Bertling Lane that the Village plant parkway trees.
He explained that on Bertling Lane the right-of-way is not wide enough to
accommodate any trees whatsoever, and that, therefore, any tree planting would
have to be done on private property. He noted that Mr. Saunders recommended
that if the Trustees choose to consider planting trees on private property, it
should be within a set distance of the right of way and at the cost of the
resident. This would be in keeping with the policies of some of the other
municipalities and could act as a foundation for a private tree-planting
program in the future. Mr. Williams added that he was uncomfortable with the
concept of going on to private property with public monies.
Trustee Derning expressed concern about homeowners perceiving that the Village
would be liable for the well being of the trees after they were planted. He
suggested that if the Village proceeds with this program, there be a written
agreement between the homeowner and the Village clearly stating the terms of
responsibility.
Trustee Duhl indicated that he believed that by accommodating the request from
Bertling Lane residents, the Village would be establishing a precedent which
could not be limited to streets with narrow rights-of-way. He questioned
whether it was really wise for the Village to take such an action.
Trustee Lien agreed with Trustee Duhl, saying that this kind of tree planting
program could end up being very expensive for the Village .
It was the general consensus of the Trustees to not support the planting of any
trees by the Village on private property.
V. Council Handbook.
Manager Williams distributed copies of the new Council Handbook to each Council
member and briefly commented on each of the sections therein.
V. Preliminary Analysis of Financial Results for Fiscal Year 1998-99.
Manager Williams reviewed the preliminary analysis of the Village’s financial
status as of the end of FY 1998-99. He reported that the Village had
experienced an exceptionally strong financial year and highlighted some of the
preliminary figures demonstrating that point. On the negative side he pointed
out a deficit of approximately $300,000 in the health insurance fund and
expenses exceeding the Budget in the Water Fund, where operating expenses
exceeded the Budget by $56,241, and capital expenses exceeded the Budget by
$63,851. He cited the significant operating expenses and capital improvements
accounting for the excess spending.
Mr. Williams then responded questions from the Trustees and discussed possible
funding options for future Village projects.
VI. Adjournment .
The meeting adjourned at 8:45 p.m.
May 18, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved June 1, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
18, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien,
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Village Planner Brian Norkus, and about 36
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that there would be amendments proposed to
Ordinance No. M-574-99 to be considered before the drafted ordinance could be
considered. She also noted that Bid No. B-99-012 had been removed from the
Agenda. The Council, then, approved the Agenda as amended.
IV. Quorum.
June 1, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
June 8, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
May 4, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
May 11, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Landmark Preservation Awards.
President Holland introduced Nan Greenough, Chairman of the Landmark
Preservation Commission and expressed her sincere thanks to Ms. Greenough and
the other members of the Commission for their hard work. Then, in recognition
of Preservation Week, President Holland presented the Village’s annual awards
for demonstrating what careful building can accomplish. She noted that each of
the projects receiving an award showed sensitivity to the community and respect
for their neighborhoods. Awards were presented to the following:
For Restoration:
ArLynn & Stephen Presser         785 Willow Rd.
For Rehab or Sensitive Addition:
Pam & Bill Gunlicks            630 Willow Rd.
Mr. and Mrs. Peter Abuls         509 Cherry St.
Skokie School               520 Glendale Ave.
Roche Bobois               908 Green Bay Rd.
Diane Curtis & Charles McDonald      720 Willow Rd.
For New Construction:
Donna & Curt Voges            946 Oak St.
Donna & Leslie Pinsoff         25 Indian Hill
VIII. Irwin Lyons – Village Auditor.
Mr. Lyons addressed the Council to review the audit process. He also responded
to questions from the Trustees.
IX. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-574-99 – Zoning Variation: 480 Oak Street - Adoption. Manager
Williams commented on new amendments being proposed by staff which require the
removal of a fence and other obstructions to make sure that it is understood
that the right-of-way is public land. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee
Hilton, moved to adopt the proposed amendments. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien and
Duhl.
Trustee Hilton then moved to adopt the ordinance permitting increased GFA, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-575-99 – Zoning Variation: 220 Poplar Street - Adoption.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting increased roofed lot coverage and a reduced front yard setback. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-573-99 – Zoning Variation: 1310 Forest Glen North –
Introduction. Mr. Norkus briefly reviewed the particulars of this request for a
plat of resubdivision and a building size (GFA) variance, introduction of which
was deferred at the May 4, 1999 Regular Meeting. He indicated that during the
interim, staff has determined that the existing 22,020 square foot property
consists of two buildable lots of record and discussed different possible
scenarios should Council grant the applicants’ requests. He also responded to
questions from the Trustees.
President Holland recognized the applicant, Chuck Bohrer, who reiterated the
points of his position and, in turn, introduced his architect who reviewed the
specifics of the proposed addition.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
A roll call vote which resulted in a tie was taken on the motion. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Powell and Hilton. Nays: Trustees Aquilino, Lien and Duhl.
The Village Attorney ruled that the President could vote to break the tie.
President Holland said that if the Village had not spent so much time studying
the bulk issue in response to overwhelming Village-wide concern and rewriting
the Zoning Ordinance, the Bohrers’ situation might be viewed in a different
light. However, given the changes made and the reasons therefor, she voted
against the variance and subsequent resubdivision requests. Therefore, the
motion failed.
X. Unfinished Business.
None
XI. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1125 and 1126. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1125 in the total amount of $181,607.92 and Warrant List No. 1126 in the
total amount of $368,588.99. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Bid No. 99-012 – Recloser. This item was removed from the Agenda.
Bid No. 99-006A – Conduit Installation. Manager Williams explained that this is
for the installation of conduit by the directional bore method for FY 99/00. He
noted that the FY 99/00 Budget contains a total of $203,800 for directional
boring and that the lowest responsible bidder was Archon Construction Co., in
the amount of $176,930.
Trustee Derning moved to approve the bid of Archon Construction as noted above.
The motion was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien and
Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Sidewalk Café Permits. Mr. Williams referred the Trustees to applications by
six area restaurants for outdoor sidewalk seating permits, as follows:
Starbucks Coffee, Caffe Buon Giorno, St. Louis Bread, Song O’Sixpence, Sweet
Shop, and Expresso Pacifico.
Trustee Hilton moved to approve the applications for outdoor seating permits.
Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Monthly Compost Report – DK Recycling. President Holland commented on the
report on operations at the Winnetka Compost Site for the month of April.
XII. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported the Chamber of Commerce’s continuing
interest in the Elm Street Bridge project. He also indicated that in view of
the bridge project, the Chamber planned to scale back their Winnetka Days
activities.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Illinois Department of
Transportation has awarded the contract for the Elm Street Bridge
Rehabilitation to Areatha Construction of Chicago, for a total of about
$968,000. The Village’s share of this work will be approximately $360,000. He
noted that work is scheduled to begin the project during the second week of
June. Completion is anticipated in October. The bridge will be closed to
vehicle and pedestrian traffic for this period. Vehicle traffic will be
detoured via Green Bay, Oak, and Maple, while pedestrian traffic will be
detoured via the station bridge. Advance warning signs will be placed in the
next week or two. This project includes removal of the existing metal handrails
and replacement with a decorative precast concrete railing, upgrading the
structural support members, painting, and resurfacing. Lighting and planter
boxes will be added by the Village upon completion of the bridge work.
XIII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:39 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIV. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:40 p.m.
