June 1, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved June 15, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
1, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien,
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and about four persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
June 8, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
June 15, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Hilton, indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
May 18, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Tom Wilson, 927 Forest Glen West, on behalf of neighborhood of Forest Glen,
presented a petition signed by residents supporting the zoning variations,
denied by Council, which were requested by the owners of 1310 Forest Glen
North. Mr. Wilson asked the Council to reconsider its decision.
Jack Minkow, 1323 Forest Glen North and Dr. Sanford Roth, 920 Forest Glen West,
also spoke in support of the requested variations and Mr. Wilson’s request for
reconsideration.
President Holland addressed the concerns of the residents of Forest Glen and
suggested that they contact the Village Attorney for a thorough explanation of
the Council’s ruling. She pointed out that although the Zoning Board of Appeals
and the Plan Commission voted to recommend approval of the variations, they are
only quasi-judicial boards and do not have the right to make the final
determination in cases involving Floor Area Ratio.
Attorney Janega confirmed President Holland’s comments saying that the Zoning
Board of Appeals and the Village Council are bound by statutory requirements in
making their determinations. In this instance there was no evidence or finding
of fact coming from Zoning Board of Appeals as to hardship or practical
difficulties.
Brian Morrow, 503 Chestnut, voiced concern about the role of the Federal
government in the community. Trustee Derning commented that the solution to
Federal bureaucracy could not be found at the local level. He told Mr. Morrow
that if he chose to return to the Council with explicit, documented
information, the Council would address the specific issues, if appropriate.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-576-99 – Disposal of Village Property - Introduction. Mr.
Williams explained that the proposed ordinance would authorize the disposal of
vehicles and equipment that are past their useful life and are being replaced
by other equipment. The vehicles will be put up for sale at the Northwest
Municipal Conference’s auction on June 19, 1999.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. MC-226-99 – Adding a Section to the Village Code to Implement the
Requirements of the State Gift Ban Act - Introduction. Attorney Janega
explained that in accordance with the newly enacted State Gift Ban Act,
municipalities must “prohibit the solicitation and acceptance of gifts, and . .
. enforce those prohibitions in a manner substantially in accordance with the
requirements of [the] Act.” Pursuant to the Act, the local prohibition and
enforcement must be in place by July 1, 1999. The proposed ordinance states the
prohibition against the solicitation and acceptance of certain gifts,
establishes an Ethics Commission, creates the office of Ethics Officer, and
provides for the Village Council to serve as the Ethics Commission and for the
Village Manager to serve as the Ethics Officer.
Ms. Janega further pointed out that the Act permits officers serving without
compensation to be exempt from its requirements, but that if the Council
determines that such persons should be exempt, then minor revisions to the text
of the drafted ordinance would be required.
Trustee Duhl expressed his opinion that it would be better to have an absolute
ban on gifts, applicable to all parties. The other Trustees concurred.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce Ordinance No. MC-
226-99. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien,
Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1470-99 – Water Service Taps and Meter Fees – Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that new water service taps and meter fees are
needed because the fees currently charged are inadequate to cover the direct
staff costs, as well as the materials costs, to the department. Adoption of
this resolution will result in new construction being responsible for covering
the costs of providing these taps and meter.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce Resolution No.
R-1470-99. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1471-99 – Release of Executive Session Minutes - Adoption.
Manager Williams explained that pursuant to State law the Village Council is
required to examine and release Executive Session minutes which no longer
require confidentiality. He pointed out that certain sets of minutes would be
retained due to continuing litigation matters. The minutes reviewed by Council
cover the period from May 19, 1998 to May 4, 1999.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to approve the Executive Session
minutes from May 19, 1998 to May 4, 1999. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Lien, then, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-1471-99, releasing certain
of the approved minutes. The motion was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning,
Aquilino, Powell and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-1469-99 – Cul de Sac Standards - Adoption. Manager Williams
briefly reviewed the history of this issue. The drafted resolution was prepared
in response to Council’s direction at the May 4, 1999 Regular Meeting, to
establish standards and procedures for the possible creation of new cul de
sacs.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Derning,
Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1127 and 1128. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1127 in the total amount of $316,333.63 and Warrant List No. 1128 in the
total amount of $336,134.26. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Additional Capital Improvements. Manager Williams reviewed two additional
capital improvement projects identified by staff for Council’s consideration.
They are as follows: (1) Additional Sidewalk Repair for $100,000. He explained
that the Village has already completed its sidewalk repair program for this
year, and that due to a lag in the street reconstruction program, the Village
engineers now have time to identify additional sidewalk repairs that could be
made this construction season. The benefits of doing these additional repairs
this year are two fold. First, it would reduce liability exposure to the
Village for possible trip and fall lawsuits, and second, it would speed up the
Village's sidewalk repair program which had been slated to be completed in four
years. (2) Headwall Repair for $35,000. The concrete on the headwall that
serves as the storm water outfall for the Sheridan Road ravines area is in need
of repair. With Lake Michigan at such a low level, these repairs would be
relatively easy. Mr. Williams indicated that it may be possible to absorb these
additional expenditures within the current budget, but, if not, there are
sufficient cash reserves to offset them.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the additional
capital expenditures as outlined by Mr. Williams. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and
Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Bid No. 99-013 – One Aerial Platform Lift Truck. Mr. Williams reviewed this bid
for the purchase and delivery of one 30,000 GVWR insulated 40 ft. aerial
platform lift truck to replace an existing vehicle to be used for service and
repair of electrical distribution lines throughout the Village. He noted that
although the bid exceeds the budgeted amount for the vehicle, the Water and
Electric Department will defer other purchases so that approval of this item
will not cause the Budget to be exceeded. Staff recommends that the bid be
awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, Mid-America Truck & Equipment Co. in
the total amount of $116,222.
Trustee Hilton moved that the bid be awarded as recommended. Trustee Powell
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Bid No. 99-014 – One Telescoping Aerial Platform Lift Truck. Mr. Williams
reviewed this bid for the purchase and delivery of one 17,500 GVWR telescoping
31 ft. aerial platform lift truck to replace an existing vehicle to be used for
troubleshooting and emergency services in confined areas throughout the
Village. He noted that although the bid exceeds the budgeted amount for the
vehicle, the Water and Electric Department will defer other purchases so that
approval of this item will not cause the Budget to be exceeded. Staff
recommends that the bid be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, Mid-
America Truck & Equipment Co. in the total amount of $77,798.
Trustee Hilton moved that the bid be awarded as recommended. Trustee Powell
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
X. Reports
Village President. President Holland thanked Jim Hoynes and the Park District
for providing the Memorial Day program on the Village Green. She also urged all
residents to attend the Caucus meeting next Wednesday.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that a new brochure listing various
Village services has been developed for distribution to new residents when they
sign up for new utility service. He also notified the Trustees of the receipt
of two petitions which will be put on future agendas: (1) asking for repair of
the Cherry Street pier and beach access, and (2) asking for revision of the
cost sharing program for 400 amp service.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland announced the appointment of Karen Fell to the Building
Review Committee. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to
approve the appointment. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:29 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:20 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:21 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
June 8, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved June 15, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
8, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien,
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and
Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-578-99 – Zoning Variation: Hubbard Woods School, 1110 Chatfield
- Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that Winnetka Public School, District
36, has requested a variance in order to permit the replacement of play
equipment that is proposed to be located within the required Hamptondale front
setback. Because the playground equipment is accessory to the school and its
replacement will not increase the intensity of use of the permitted primary
Special Use, the proposed improvements do not require Special Use Permit
consideration.
Melanie Stall, Hubbard Woods School PTA President, presented a drawing of the
new playground design for review by the Trustees.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-577-99 – Zoning Variation: Greeley School, 275 Fairview -
Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that Winnetka Public School, District
36, also has requested a variance in order to permit play apparatus to be
located within the required front yard setbacks at Greeley School. Again,
because the playground equipment is accessory to the school and its replacement
will not increase the intensity of use of the permitted primary Special Use,
the proposed improvements do not require Special Use Permit consideration. She
noted that in this instance the proposed setbacks reduce the existing degree of
nonconformity.
Linda Hoffman, the school’s outgoing PTA chairman presented a drawing of the
new playground design for review by the Trustees.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
V. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
7:45 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
June 8, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved June 15, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
8, 1999, at 7:46 p.m., immediately following a Special Meeting of the Village
Council.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:46 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and
Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
II. Zoning 101.
Attorney Janega reviewed fundamental zoning concepts for the Trustees. She
provided a brief overview of zoning in general and how zoning administration
works. She, then, discussed some of the specifics of Winnetka’s Zoning
Ordinance. She covered the decision making standards for both zoning variations
and special use permits and commented on some of the frequently heard
arguments, pro and con. She also spoke to the importance of findings of fact.
President Holland thanked Attorney Janega for her extensive review.
III. Inventory Status Report.
Manager Williams reviewed staff memorandums updating the Council on the status
of inventory control systems as a follow up to the Village auditor’s
recommendations.
III. Natural Gas Franchise.
Manager Williams explained that the Village, like many other villages, has been
operating without a franchise agreement with North Shore Gas since 1995 because
of an inability to agree to terms. He said that recently, however, the Village
of Glencoe has negotiated a compromise agreement with the gas company which
staff deems to be appropriate for Winnetka as well. He asked for direction from
Council as to whether or not to pursue a comparable agreement with the gas
company on behalf of the Village.
The Trustees agreed that the Village should pursue finalizing an agreement with
the gas company similar to Glencoe’s, with minor changes.
The meeting adjourned at 9:45 p.m.
June 15, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved July 6, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
15, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, John Lien, and John
Powell. Absent: Trustee David Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Village Engineer Steven Saunders,
Village Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about 35 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
July 6, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustees Lien and Duhl indicated that they did
not expect to be able to attend.
July 13, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee Lien,
indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
June 1, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
June 8, 1999, Special Meeting. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
June 8, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
President Holland recognized Chuck Boehrer, 1310 Forest Glen North, who
appealed to the Council to reconsider their denial at the May 18, 1999, Regular
Meeting, of a motion to introduce his application for a zoning variation. Mr.
Boehrer indicated a willingness to amend his proposal and asked for direction
from the Council as to what alternatives might be available to him.
Attorney Janega and Ms. McCammack discussed the options open to Mr. Boehrer.
Ms. Janega indicated that there were two possible approaches: (1) for one of
the Trustees who was on the prevailing side of the original vote to move to
reconsider, which would reopen the issue, or (2) for the Boehrers to file
another application.
President Holland polled the three Trustees present who voted against the
original zoning variation request. Trustee Duhl said that he still doesn’t see
the hardship in this case that is required to justify a GFA variance. Trustee
Lien agreed saying that the variance for which the Boehrers asked was simply
too significant to consider given the comprehensive review and revision of the
Zoning Ordinance recently undertaken by the Council. Trustee Aquilino
concurred. She indicated that perhaps she would be able to consider a different
application for a substantially reduced variation more favorably because in her
opinion, the Boehrers have the option to accomplish what they want and remain
within the requirements of the Zoning Code.
Saying that she had cast the deciding, tie breaking vote, President Holland
summarized the others’ comments. She said that the problem is that there is no
inherent objection to the Boehrers improving their property, but that for the
variation requested, there would have to be a hardship or practical difficulty
running with the land. If they could have scaled back their request to
something much more reasonable, it might have been doable, but at the requested
size, it was too large. She indicated that if the Boehrers wish to come in with
a new application for a different sized addition, with a different set of
facts, the Council would be willing to entertain it. However, by Code the
existing petition must wait one year for reconsideration.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-578-99 – Zoning Variation: 1110 Chatfield, Hubbard Woods School
– Adoption. Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance permitting the
replacement of play equipment that is proposed to be located within the
required Hamptondale front setback. The motion was seconded by Trustee Duhl. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl,
Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. M-577-99 – Zoning Variation: 275 Fairview, Greeley School –
Adoption.
Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance permitting play apparatus to be
located within the required front yard setbacks at Greeley School. Trustee
Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. M-576-99 – Disposal of Village Property - Adoption. Trustee Duhl,
seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance authorizing the
disposal of vehicles and equipment that are past their useful life and are
being replaced. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Hilton.
Ordinance No. MC-226-99 – Adding a Section to the Village Code to Implement the
Requirements of the State Gift Ban Act - Adoption. Attorney Janega briefly
reviewed the proposed ordinance, which states the prohibition against the
solicitation and acceptance of certain gifts, establishes an Ethics Commission,
creates the office of Ethics Officer and provides for the Village Council to
serve as the Ethics Commission and for the Village Manager to serve as the
Ethics Officer.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Aquilino, Powell, Lien, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Resolution No. R-1470-99 – Water Service Taps and Meter Fees – Adoption.
Manager Williams commented on the resolution which will increase fees in order
to recover the Village’s costs of providing water service taps and meter.
The motion to adopt was made by Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. MC-227-99 – Amending Chapter 1 of the Village Code Regarding
Delinquent Accounts – Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that the proposed
ordinance implements some of the policies recommended by the Finance Director.
It clarifies the authorization of a collection fee, defines delinquent
accounts, provides for the assessment of a collection charge for delinquent
accounts and authorizes the Finance Director to enter into payment plans. It
also specifically authorizes the denial of the issuance or renewal of licenses,
permits or privileges to persons with delinquent accounts. However, it does not
impose limitations or conditions on the provision of police and fire services.
She further noted that a provision requiring the imposition of a late charge
and interest has not been included and explained the reasons therefor.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-1472-99 – Amendment to Hackberry West Subdivision Plat –
Adoption. President Holland announced that this was the rehearing of a
previously decided issue.
Attorney Janega explained that the proposed resolution would amend the
Hackberry West Subdivision resolution, R-1456-99, which was originally approved
by Council’s adoption of resolution R-1456-99, at the May 4, 1999, Regular
Meeting. The amendment would revise the easement language on the plat by adding
three points to clarify the nature, timing and method of work in the easements.
The proposed resolution would still accept and approve the amended plat with
the same lot configuration and same easements, but would supercede the prior
resolution because of the specific easement language.
Manager Williams stated that the revised resolution merely affirms the
Village’s current standard utility practices. He said that both the Public
Works Director and the Director of Water and Electric are in concurrence.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Ordinance No. M-579-99 – Natural Gas Franchise Agreement – Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that the Village, like many other villages, has been
operating without a franchise agreement with North Shore Gas since 1995 because
of an inability to agree to terms. Recently, however, after more than a year’s
study and negotiation, the Village of Glencoe reached a compromise agreement
with the gas company which staff deemed to be an appropriate model for Winnetka
as well. The proposed ordinance is an agreement based upon the Glencoe model
with a few minor changes.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
North Shore Community Bank Special Use Permit. Manager Williams summarized the
three fundamental policy issues confronting the Council: (1) the use of public
land for ingress and egress and the loss of public parking spaces; (2) the
three requested variances; and (3) the special use permit in and of itself,
which Council has to decide whether or not the requested use is appropriate for
the proposed location.
Ms. McCammack then briefly reviewed the request from North Shore Community Bank
to construct a new bank facility at 572-582 Lincoln Avenue. This request
involves the use of public property to accommodate a ramp to access the lower
level parking structure and to provide access to, through, and from the drive-
through banking element. It also proposes site alterations to the Village-owned
public parking lot immediately west of the subject property and proposes to
eliminate eight existing public parking spaces. She explained that the
applicant also hopes to construct a two-lane drive-through facility located at
the rear of the building which would be accessed from, and exit through the
Village owned public parking lot. It would have a roofed canopy over the drive-
through teller area which would project onto Village property, as would a ramp
to the proposed basement level parking structure.
Ms. McCammack noted that the Plan Commission and Zoning Board of Appeals
considered the applicant’s request for Special Use Permits (SUP) to allow the
drive-through facility and the surface parking area. Both boards recommended
that the Village Council deny the request for a drive-through banking facility.
With regard to the surface parking area, the Plan Commission recommended that
the Village Council approve the surface parking area and the Zoning Board gave
no recommendation to the Council for this.
She said that the Zoning Board of Appeals also considered three zoning
variation requests for the new structure. The Board recommended that the
Council deny zoning variances to allow the construction to exceed the
impermeable surface limitation and to have a reduced (0 foot) west rear yard
setback, and offered no recommendation to the Council for the variance
requested to allow the drive-through without access to a primary street.
President Holland then recognized Randolph Hibbon, President and CEO of North
Shore Community Bank, who introduced other representatives of the applicant who
would be making presentations. Mr. Hibbon discussed the process used to
determine the need for the proposed facility and commented on the relationships
which the Bank has established throughout the Village. He also addressed some
of the general objections to the project raised during other committee
meetings. He expressed the belief that the proposed facility would enhance the
neighborhood, diminish the demand for local parking spaces, and increase foot
traffic to the area, which, in turn, would benefit local retailers. He said
that if the Bank is denied the drive-through facility, it would have to
reevaluate its options as it would be difficult to justify the considerable
expense of the new building alone. Mr. Hibbon requested that the Council
favorably consider its application to the benefit of their constituents, the
Bank’s customers, and the community as a whole.
Lemuel Tate, Chairman of North Shore Community Bank, reviewed the Bank’s
numerous attempts over the past few years to find a suitable new location which
could accommodate a drive through facility. He pointed to the Bank’s other
branches in Glencoe and Wilmette, which include drive through facilities, as
examples of the Bank’s success at providing neighborhood friendly operations.
Tolbert Chisolm, past president of the Bank, also urged the Council to consider
the request favorably, saying that the Bank has worked diligently both to
establish significant relationships within the community and to find a suitable
location from which to better serve the community.
Saying that the Bank has listened closely to the concerns and comments of the
various Village boards, the project’s architect, John Myefski, then reviewed
the evolution of the current project design. He responded to questions from the
Trustees regarding design features and the Bank’s suggestion to minimize the
loss of parking spaces by restriping the Village’s parking lot.
Mary Koberstein, the Bank’s attorney, then addressed some of the standards and
concerns raised at earlier hearings. She indicated that the Bank’s plans are
aimed at preserving the pedestrian character of the neighborhood, with as
little disruption of pedestrian traffic on Lincoln Avenue as possible. She
described it as also being designed to preserve redevelopment options for
properties surrounding the subject property. Ms. Koberstein pointed out to the
Council that the building itself does not require a variation. She emphasized
that all alternatives have been thoroughly explored; there is no other
available property which will accommodate the Bank’s needs.
Trustee Powell inquired as to whether consideration had been given to fitting
the entire project onto the Bank’s land. Mr. Myefski said that that would still
require access either off of Lincoln Avenue or from an access ramp to
underground parking from the rear.
Trustee Powell also asked how customers would be expected to locate the
proposed drive through facility in the back. Mr. Myefski indicated that they
would propose some sort of attractive, low signage on Lincoln Avenue.
Ms. Koberstein reiterated the Bank’s willingness to enter into a lease
agreement with the Village so as to preserve the Village’s ownership rights
over its property or to entertain any type of option that the Village might
consider.
Manager Williams asked Ms. Koberstein and Mr. Myefski to address the
impermeable surface variance request, which they did.
Ms. Koberstein said that the applicant is caught in a dilemma in that the Code
requires that there be underground parking, but that because there is no alley,
there is no way to access that parking without either removing parking spaces
from Lincoln Avenue to accommodate a front entry access ramp, or using Village
property in the rear to access a ramp located in the back. She likened the
circumstances to those of the Galleria.
Manager Williams pointed out that the significant difference between the two
situations is that the Galleria only uses Village property to access its
property, while the Bank has requested its use for a drive through as well.
Trustee Derning asked Attorney Janega for a clarification of the policy
underlying the requirement that access is to be from a primary street. Ms.
Janega replied that her understanding is that the requirement was added to the
Zoning Ordinance to prevent potential conflicting traffic patterns. She said
that it was also her understanding that the requirement was added not long
after a drive-through with alley access opened, and that the only drive-through
that has been added since then is access off Green Bay Road, which is a primary
street.
Trustee Duhl said that he did not see anything about the nature of the specific
property that creates a hardship which would allow for the variance for the
drive through. He suggested that a request for a text amendment would be the
proper format for this situation because the land at issue does not abut a
primary street and the applicant is seeking to change the ordinance’s language.
Ms. Koberstein replied that the Bank has searched extensively, and
unsuccessfully, for a location on a primary street.
Village Engineer, Steve Saunders, addressed the question of whether or not the
proposed design of the access ramp and drive through would impact access to any
at or below grade parking deck which the Village might locate near the proposed
facility in the future. He said that, given the current proposal, there would
be options available, but that careful planning would be required and costs
allocated and assigned.
Mr. Saunders also commented on the Bank’s traffic study, noting that it showed
an increase of traffic on the street system of 12.5% to 23% during peak hours.
He said this would be noticeable, but would not cause the street system to fail
to function. However, he expressed concern for pedestrians because the parking
lot would function like a street in many ways, but not look like one, so
pedestrians would not be expecting the traffic flow.
Mr. Saunders said that he was not able to comment on the Bank’s suggested plan
for restriping the Village lot, but said that the Village does not normally
stripe spaces less than 8’6”.
Trustee Derning asked for clarification of figures provided in the Bank’s
traffic study, and President Holland noted that the study projects 55 cars
going through the drive through facility on any average day. Jerry Lindgren,
the Bank’s traffic consultant responded to Trustee Derning’s question and
President Holland’s comments.
Attorney Janega asked whether the drive through facilities in Glencoe and
Wilmette had been added after the banks were built and, if so, how much of the
banks’ business shifted from inside to outside. Mr. Tate responded that they
were added after the banks were built and that about 25% of the transactions
could be expected to shift to the drive through.
President Holland then invited comments from members of the audience.
Lauren Polin, 978 Spruce Street; Harold Egan, 370 Rosewood; Judi Geale, a
Northfield resident; Curtis Tate 1063 Cherry St.; and Mary Bradbury, 574
Lincoln, all spoke in support of the Bank’s application and asked the Council
to consider it favorably. Among other things, they cited convenience, the
beauty of the proposed building, and the potential for increased foot traffic
in the Lincoln Avenue business district as reasons for their support.
Speakers opposing the application included: Debby Clinch, Director of the
Winnetka Community Nursery School; Geoff Fox, 879 Cherry St.; Jane Brower, 956
Vernon; and Gus Knight, 598 Lincoln. Ms. Clinch expressed concern for the
safety of the many children that play and walk in the area. Mr. Fox noted that
the building itself would be a great asset to the neighborhood, but that the
drive through facility was of grave concern. Ms. Brower said that the parking
lot and driveway already serve as thoroughfares and present a hazard to all who
use them. Mr. Knight concurred with the others’ comments
Andrew and Leslie Bullock, 612 Lincoln, also spoke against the proposal. On
behalf of the Lincoln Avenue Townhome Association, Mr. Bullock noted four
objections to the proposal: (1) necessary signage for the Bank’s drive through
would change the character of the neighborhood; (2) no matter what design,
spaces would be lost in the Village’s parking lot, which is already
overcrowded; (3) the use of public land for private use is not an appropriate
policy decision; (4) because of the ATM machine, the proposed drive through
facility, by necessity, would be a 24 hour operation, which would mean night
time traffic and lights which would directly impact the neighboring townhomes.
Trustee Lien said that he had no objection to the concept of negotiating an
agreement for the use of public land for private purposes. He pointed out that
for this piece of property, the only realistic access to any building is from
the rear and that some kind of an agreement would have to be reached, the terms
of which would have to be the subject of negotiation. He, however, expressed
grave concern about the long term impact the proposed plan would have on the
Village’s parking lot, both now and in the future, and questioned whether this
particular plan was an appropriate use of public land.
Trustee Powell raised concern about the proposed extensive use of public land.
He noted that with a reduction in the overall size of the Bank’s building, they
could construct a drive through without using so much of the Village’s property
or impacting the parking to such an extent.
Trustee Derning expressed ambivalence about the plan, noting that it is
ingenious from a logistic standpoint, but saying that he, too, was concerned
about its being so dependent upon the use of public land. He asked Attorney
Janega to comment on whether or not this would amount to a precedent setting
decision on the part of the Council. Ms. Janega replied that any action
potentially establishes the groundwork for precedent. She noted that the Bank
is relying on the precedent of allowing access to the Galleria project via
public property but said, however, that the circumstances are different in this
instance.
Trustee Duhl voiced support for the positions of Trustees Lien and Powell. He
said that he is concerned about establishing a precedent. He added his opinion
that in this case, the property in question simply is not large enough to do
all that the Bank wishes to do, so they are asking the Village to let them use
the Village’s land in order to accommodate their desires. Mr. Duhl said that he
hopes the Bank will proceed with erecting their building without the drive
through, but that he cannot support the use of public land to facilitate the
business of the Bank.
Trustee Aquilino agreed, saying that the drive through just does not fit onto
the property and is not an appropriate use of Village property, especially in
such a congested area.
President Holland called for a motion to concur with the findings of the Zoning
Board of Appeals and the Plan Commission. The motion was made by Trustee Lien,
seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
President Holland announced that the Bank’s application for the use of public
property for a drive through had been denied. She urged the Bank to proceed
with their plans for the building without the drive through or to return to the
Council with something that is less intrusive on public land.
General Obligation Limited Bonds, Series 1999.
At 9:50 p.m., President Holland announced that the next agenda item for the
Council was a Public Hearing to receive public comments on the proposal to sell
not to exceed $1,500,000 General Obligation Limited Bonds, Series 1999, for the
payment of the cost of reconstruction, resurfacing and rehabilitation of
Village streets, and explained that all persons desiring to be heard would have
an opportunity to present written or oral testimony with respect thereto. She
declared the hearing open.
President Holland opened the discussion and explained that the reasons for the
proposed issuance of the Bonds was to permit the Village to continue levying
the amounts that have been levied to pay off the debt on the Hubbard Woods
parking deck and the Public Safety Building. The issuance of the bonds makes it
possible to offset the cost of the street improvement program and reallocate
other funds for the renovation and expansion of the Public Works Yards.
Whereupon she asked Manager Williams to provide additional detail for the
benefit of the Trustees.
Manager Williams explained that with the enactment of tax cap legislation in
1995, the Village is no longer allowed to reallocate the revenue from the
payment of the Hubbard Woods Parking Deck Bond to pay the debt of the Public
Safety Building, as originally proposed. Unless the Village issues new debt
before December 1999, the Village will lose its ability to continue to levy the
$138,000 which was used to pay the 1989 debt and, then, earmarked for
additional debt retirement for the Public Safety Building. He noted that the
Village can preserve this $138,000 levy by issuing debt of about $1.5 million
this year for street improvements. This will enable the Village to reallocate
other funds and implement its plans to renovate, and possibly expand, the
Village Yards which are utilized for water, electric, street, sewer, refuse and
mechanic functions. He pointed out that since the Village's property tax rate
already contains $138,000 for debt retirement, the issuance of $1.5 million in
general obligation bonds will not increase the Village's property tax rate.
Manager Williams referred the Trustees to a special Winnetka Report mailed to
all Winnetkans before the 1994 referendum to finance the renovation and
expansion of the Public Safety Building, which clearly defined the Village’s
financial structuring plan. It stated that the Village would not be reducing
taxes when the Hubbard Woods parking structure debt was retired, but would be
reallocating those funds to the Public Safety Building project.
He said staff recommends that council entertain issuing these bonds this year.
President Holland then asked for additional questions or comments from the
Trustees. There were none.
Next, President Holland asked if there was any written testimony concerning the
proposed issuance of the Bonds to be read into the record. None was received.
President Holland called for any further comments from Village staff or written
comments from the audience. Again, there were none.
Manager Williams announced that this was just the first step in the process and
that no final action would be taken at this meeting.
Whereupon the President asked for oral testimony or any public comments
concerning the proposed issuance of the bonds. None was forthcoming.
The President then announced that all persons desiring to be heard had been
given an opportunity to present oral and written testimony with respect to the
proposed issuance of the Bonds.
Trustee Duhl moved and Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion that the Public
Hearing be adjourned. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Hilton.
Whereupon President Holland declared the motion carried and the Hearing was
adjourned at 10:00 p.m.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Duhl, then moved to direct staff to
proceed toward issuing the General Obligation Limited Bonds, Series 1999. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Aquilino, Powell, Lien, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1129 and 1130. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1129 in the total amount of $185,451.56 and Warrant List No. 1130 in the
total amount of $284,715.75. Trustee Duhl seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Duhl,
Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
Bid No. 99-007 – Sewer Relining. Mr. Saunders explained this bid for trenchless
relining of the existing 8”, 10” and 12” sanitary sewers under Winnetka Ave.,
Brier St., Meadow Rd., and High St. He noted that staff recommends the award of
the bid to Kenny Construction Co., in the amount of $67,744.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to approve the award of the
bid to Kenny Construction. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Hilton.
Bid No. 99-016 – Pad Mount Transformers. Manager Williams explained that this
bid was for the purchase and delivery of single-phase and 3-phase pad mount
transformers to be purchased on an “as needed” basis for FY 99/00. He said most
of these were required for the Village’s project to place electric lines
underground. He noted that staff recommends the award of the bid for single-
phase transformers to Wesco Distribution in the amount of $136,205.10 and for
3-phase transformers to Resco in the amount of $323,800.00.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to approve the award of the
bids as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Powell, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Hilton.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to the
report on operations at the Winnetka Compost Site during the month of May.
X. Reports
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported on the number of demolition permits
issued by the Village annually for the last ten years. He also announced that
there would be a tour of Village facilities for new Trustees on June 23rd,
starting at 9:00 a.m., and commented on the performance of the pump station
during the recent rain storm.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland asked the Trustees to appoint Charles Hoffman to serve out
the term of Peter Gould on the Building Review Committee, beginning July 1,
1999. Trustee Duhl moved to appoint Mr. Hoffman. He was seconded by Trustee
Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Hilton.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:00 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
Trustee Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney
Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Duhl moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
11:01 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
July 6, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved July 20, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
6, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, David Hilton, John Lien, and John
Powell. Absent: Trustee Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, and one
person in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
July 13, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
July 20, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
June 15, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved
to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Fred Askanos, 1310 Asbury Ave., voiced a complaint about the response of the
Public Works Department to a sewer problem after the recent heavy rainfall.
Manager Williams acknowledged the Village’s error, saying that the Public Works
Department has subsequently checked the sewer line and found no blockage. He
indicated that the Public Works Director would contact Mr. Askanos to suggest
possible solutions to his problem.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-227-99 – Amending Chapter 1 of the Village Code Regarding
Delinquent Accounts – Adoption. Manager Williams reviewed the provisions of the
ordinance which clarifies the authorization of a collection fee, defines
delinquent accounts, provides for the assessment of a collection charge for
delinquent accounts, and authorizes the Finance Director to enter into payment
plans. It also specifically authorizes the denial of the issuance or renewal of
licenses, permits or privileges to persons with delinquent accounts.
In response to a question from Trustee Aquilino, Mr. McKee explained the
procedures for determining an account to be delinquent.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-579-99 – Natural Gas Franchise Agreement – Adoption. Trustee
Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt this ordinance which
would authorize North Shore Gas to use the public rights-of-way to provide
natural gas service in the Village. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton and Lien. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
   Ordinance No. M-580-99 – Zoning Variation: 466 Poplar - Introduction. Mr.
Williams reviewed this request for variations to permit the reconstruction of
an existing addition which will result in an increased building size (GFA) and
a reduced side yard setback and building line articulation. He explained that
the need for the requested variations was discovered during construction of an
already approved addition and pointed out that the completed project will be no
different than the one previously approved. Because work is currently underway
on the property, he noted a request by staff that the Council consider
introduction of this ordinance prior to consideration by the Zoning Board of
Appeals.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-581- 99 General Obligation Limited Bonds, Series 1999 –
Introduction. Manager Williams briefly reviewed this issue. He noted that the
proposed ordinance, which was prepared by the Village’s Bond Counsel, provides
for the authorization of this bond issue which will not exceed $1.5 million.
The bonds will not be sold until July 20, 1999. The ordinance specifies that
the monies from this bond issue will be allocated for the payment of the cost
of reconstruction, resurfacing and rehabilitation of Village streets for this
year and next year. He explained that the savings from the Budget for street
rehabilitation will be transferred to a fund for the renovation of the Public
Works Facility.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1131 and 1132. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1131 in the total amount of $366,731.36 and Warrant List No. 1132 in the
total amount of $341,356.25. Trustee Lien seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Bid No. 99-009A – Capacitor Bank. Mr. Williams reviewed the bid for the
purchase and delivery of one (1) metal enclosed capacitor bank, which will
balance kVAR readings in the Village’s electrical system, for the Northfield
Substantion. He reported that staff recommended awarding the bid to Wesco
Distribution in the amount of $61,700.00.
Trustee Derning moved to approve the award of the bid to Wesco Distribution as
recommended. The motion was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton,
and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Capital Improvements Quarterly Reports. Manager Williams reviewed the Quarterly
Capital Improvements Reports from each of the departments.
Winnetka Days – July 16 & 17. President Holland presented a request from the
Chamber of Commerce to hold their annual, Winnetka Days sidewalk sales on July
16th and 17th.
Trustee Hilton moved to approve the request, and Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Reports
Village Trustees.
Trustee Lien reported on a recent meeting of the Plan Commission regarding the
Comprehensive Plan. He indicated that the subcommittee would be meeting to put
together the final draft for presentation to the Council.
Trustee Hilton reported on a recent Chamber of Commerce Subcommittee on
Commercial Development meeting to discuss better ways and means to market
Winnetka to potential merchants.
X. Appointments.
President Holland announced the temporary reappointment of Sandra Berger to the
Police Pension Board until her replacement recovers from an illness. She also
proposed the appointment of Ms. Berger to the Zoning Board of Appeals and John
McLinden to the Design Review Board.
She called for a motion to approve the above appointments and to thank Mary-Liz
Lichtenfels, Peter Gould, and Penny Lanphier for their service to the
community. Trustee Derning so moved, seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
XI. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:50 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
8:51 p.m.
