                  Deputy Clerk
July 13, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved August 3, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
13, 1999, at 7:37 p.m., immediately following a Special Meeting of the Village
Council.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack.
Review of Draft of Comprehensive Plan. President Holland recognized Nan
Greenough, representing the Plan Commission. Noting that the old comprehensive
plan needed updating because it wasn’t internally consistent, lacked a clear
vision, and failed to provide guidance, Ms. Greenough outlined the process used
to develop the current draft, emphasizing the high degree of resident
participation. She then explained the draft’s organizational structure and
briefly reviewed each chapter, highlighting some of the salient points. She
also noted issues which the Plan Commission feels require further study. In
closing, Ms. Greenough thanked the members of the Plan Commission for their
time and dedication to the project and both the Council and Village staff for
their support and help throughout the planning process.
President Holland noted that the Council expected to address the Plan in the
fall after the Trustees had had an opportunity to thoroughly review the draft,
with the hope of adopting it in November after several public hearings.
Ms. Greenough responded to questions from the Trustees regarding some of the
specific inclusions and exclusions in the Plan.
President Holland recognized Lloyd Owens, 922 Elm St., who expressed his
appreciation for the unique qualities of the Village and his desire to see the
character of the neighborhoods maintained. He urged the Trustees to carefully
consider the recommendation discouraging front loading garages at the expense,
in his opinion, of larger, more attractive back yards.
Manager Williams pointed out that in considering the Plan, it is important for
the Council to carefully think through what it wishes to accomplish in the long
term and separate the ideas into categories of desirability. There will be some
things that the Village simply cannot implement because of limited resources.
Trustee Duhl concurred saying that the final Plan is designed to give
management guidance, but that ultimately, management still has to determine
what goals are feasible for the Village to tackle.
President Holland praised the draft plan and thanked the Plan Commission for
their time and diligence in putting the document together.
The meeting adjourned at 8:45 p.m.
July 20, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved August 3, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
20, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien,
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, and about four
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
August 3, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustees
Lien and Aquilino, indicated that they would be able to attend.
August 10, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Derning, indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
July 6, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-581-99 – General Obligation Limited Bonds, Series 1999 -
Adoption. Village Manager Williams explained that the ordinance had been
amended by inserting the details of the bonds, including the face value and
interest, following the sale of the bonds this morning. He informed the Council
that the Village’s financial adviser, Kevin McCanna from Speer Financial, Inc.,
and the Village’s bond counsel, David G. Williams of Chapman and Cutler, were
both present and available to answer any questions the Council may have.
Mr. McCanna reviewed the results of the bond bids and answered questions from
the Trustees. He informed the Council that $1,380.000 in bonds had been
purchased by Bernardi Securities, Inc. at a net interest rate of 4.7299%. There
were no questions for bond counsel.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to amend the draft ordinance
to reflect the results of the bids. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance which
provides for the issuance of General Obligation Limited Bonds, Series, 1999. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-228-99 – Comprehensive Amendments to Chapters 3 through 6, 8,
9, and 24 - Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that the proposed ordinance
would enact comprehensive revisions to the chapters of the Village Code
covering Village administration, the Police and Fire Departments, and the Water
and Electric Department. The amendments would also add a new Chapter 24, which
covers fire alarms and security systems. She reviewed the revision process and
noted that the ordinance had been prepared by the Code Review Committee, after
extensive discussion and input from Village staff. Ms. Janega pointed out that
the revisions were both organizational and substantive in nature. In doing so,
she highlighted the changes as outlined in the Summary included in the Agenda
materials and responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1133 and 1134. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1133 in the total amount of $205,761,06 and Warrant List No. 1134 in the
total amount of $452,650.95. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Monthly Compost Report. President Holland reviewed the report submitted by DK
Recycling.
Request to Use Public Sidewalk for Merchandise Display – 1048 Gage Street.
President Holland recognized Susan Afryl, a local merchant, who has requested
special permission from the Village Council to permit her to use the public
sidewalk outside, in front of her store to display merchandise for sale. Ms.
Afryl presented her request and responded to questions from the Trustees.
The Trustees discussed the comparison between using the sidewalk to provide a
service, as in sidewalk cafes, and using it for purposes of displaying
merchandise for sale. Trustee Duhl noted that although the sidewalk outside of
her premises might be able to accommodate such a use, not all sidewalks in the
Village would be able to do so. Trustee Aquilino pointed out that there is no
way to legislate and or monitor the aesthetic appropriateness of any display.
On behalf of the Trustees, President Holland suggested that Ms. Afryl first
determine for certain whether the property in question is, in fact, public
property and that she also speak to representatives from the Chamber of
Commerce to ascertain whether, in their opinion, such action would have the
support or opposition of other area merchants. She indicated that the Trustees
would consider the issue once these determinations had been made.
X. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported on a recent meeting with the Chamber
of Commerce. He noted that the merchants were very pleased with the Village’s
halt of construction on the Elm Street bridge during Winnetka Days and with the
prompt response to their request for signage directing pedestrians to the
walkway across the railroad tracks. He also expressed on their behalf continued
concern over the slow progress of the Galleria construction project.
Village Manager. Mr. Williams announced that there would be a tour of the
Public Safety Building for the two new trustees on Wednesday July 28th,
beginning at 9:00 a.m., and added that the public was welcome to attend.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:50 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
8:52 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
August 3, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved August 17, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
3, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, and John
Powell. Absent: Trustee John Lien. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about five persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
August 10, Study Session. President Holland announced that this meeting had
been cancelled.
August 17, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
July 13, 1999, Special Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
July 13, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
July 20, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-582-99 – Zoning Variation: 775 Lincoln Ave. - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack briefly reviewed the request for a variation to permit the
construction of additions to an existing residence that will result in a
reduced front yard setback. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 5-1 to recommend approval of the request.
Trustee Powell questioned the architect, Paul Konstant, about possible
alternative designs which would not require a variation. Mr. Konstant addressed
the suggested options from both structural and economic considerations.
Trustee Derning noted some hesitation about granting the variance because he
could not see where the hardship goes with the land, as is required. Mr.
Konstant pointed out that the property is a corner lot and, therefore, greater
setbacks are required.
Trustees Duhl and Aquilino expressed appreciation for the problems associated
with owning a corner property. Mr. Duhl said that although, technically, the
hardship may not run with the land, the house in question was sitting in its
current location on the lot before the rules were created, and that, therefore,
he was prepared to vote in favor of the variation.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-585-99 – Zoning Variation: 545 Sheridan Rd. - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack briefly reviewed the request for variations to permit the
rehabilitation of an existing attached two car garage which is located within
the required front and side yards and an increased garage door width. She noted
that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend approval of the
variations.
The project’s architect, Paul Konstant, explained that the current footprint of
the garage would remain the same. He also responded to questions about
procedures and design from President Holland and Trustee Aquilino.
Trustee Duhl clarified the fact that if the Council were to require two garage
doors in accordance with the Code, because of the architectural standards
needed to accommodate the two doors, one of the exterior walls would have to be
moved in order to make the garage larger. Mr. Konstant confirmed this.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-583-99 – Special Use Permit: Corwin Park, 1550 Edgewood –
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed the request from the Winnetka Park
District for a Special Use Permit to allow replacement of existing playground
equipment. The proposal includes an increase in the size of the playground. She
noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend approval by
the Village Could subject to the proposal having no negative impact on drainage
or on the two willow trees interior to the play area. Both the Public Works
Director and the Village Forester have reviewed the proposal and concluded that
the impact would be negligible. Although the Plan Commission has not yet
considered this request, she recommended consideration of introduction in order
to avoid delay.
Rick Schram, the landscape architect for the Park District, explained that most
of the redesign was being done in order to be in compliance with new
governmental standards and recommendations for access and the placement of
playground equipment.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-584-99 – Special Use Permit: Hubbard Woods Park, 939 Green Bay
Rd. - Introduction. Ms. McCammack noted that this request from the Winnetka
Park District for a Special Use Permit to allow replacement of existing
playground equipment at Hubbard Woods Park is very similar in nature to the
preceding one. The proposal includes an increase in the size of the playpit.
She said that both the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Design Review Board had
voted in favor of the improvements. Although the Plan Commission has not yet
considered this request, she recommended consideration of introduction in order
to avoid delay.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. MC-228-99 – Comprehensive Amendments to Chapters 3 through 6, 8,
9, and 24 - Adoption. Attorney Janega highlighted changes made to the ordinance
since the time of introduction reflecting comments received from both the
Trustees and department heads.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend Ordinance MC-228-99
as reviewed by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
On behalf of the Code Committee, Trustee Duhl thanked Ms. Janega and Mr.
Williams for the tremendous effort invested in updating the Code.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
Cherry Street Alley Improvements. At 8:15 p.m. Trustee Hilton moved to recess
from the Regular Council meeting to convene as the Board of Local Improvements
for the purpose of discussing the competitive bidding and contract process for
improvements to the public alley between Ash and Cherry Streets, from Locust to
Rosewood and authorizing publication, opening, and review of bids. The motion
was seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
   The Trustees reconvened into Regular Session at 8:25 p.m. Present: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton and Powell. Absent. Trustee Lien. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and Director
of Community Development Cindy McCammack.
   George Pappas, 1024 Cherry Street, said that he was very pleased with the
Village’s response to this issue and asked when the residents could expect the
project to begin. Mr. Williams replied that, hopefully, work would begin early
this fall, but that the Village would have a better idea after opening the
bids.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1135 and 1136. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1135 in the total amount of $541,962.19 and Warrant List No. 1136 in the
total amount of $630,551.18. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Duhl, and
Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Bid No. 99-017A – Underground Cable. Manager Williams explained that the bid is
for the purchase and delivery of cable to be used for various projects
throughout the Village on an “as needed” basis for the period August 1, 1999 to
April 30, 2000. The low bidder for the 15KV cable was Okonite Co. at $46,171,
with Resco making the low bid at $14,700 for the 600V cable.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Hilton, Duhl, Derning and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Reports
Village President. President Holland commented on the design of the railing for
the Elm Street bridge.
Village Trustees. Mr. Duhl reported on the first meeting of the Beautification
Committee saying that there was consensus to focus attention on the Indian Hill
area and Green Bay Corridor. Specific suggestions included replacing the trash
cans throughout the Village and removing or redesigning the newspaper racks at
the train stations.
Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported the statistics on the use of the
Village’s web page since its inception in January, 1999. He also commented on
the status of numerous projects throughout the Village, saying that
construction projects are going very well this summer.
In response to a question from Trustee Duhl, Mr. Williams reported that during
last week’s heat wave the Village had pumped over 60 million gallons of water,
more than three times the normal quantity, and had hit all time new peaks in
electrical consumption.
X. Appointments.
President Holland announced the reappointment of Keith Groebe to the Building
Review Committee. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino moved to approve
the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Holland then announced the appointment of Nan Greenough to the
Beautification Committee. The motion to approve the appointment was made by
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Duhl. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
XI. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:26 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:27 p.m.
