August 17, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved September 7, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
17, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m.
Present: Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, David Hilton, John Lien, and
John Powell. Absent: President Holland. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and two people in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
September 7, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Duhl, indicated that they would be able to attend.
September 14, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Powell, indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
August 3, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Lien,
moved to adopt the minutes with one minor change. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Public Comment and Questions. Martin Kenny, 510 Meadow Rd., asked the Council
to take action to correct the parking and traffic problems associated with the
defunct gas station at the corner of Green Bay Rd. and Winnetka Ave. President
Pro Tem Duhl informed Mr. Kenny that the Council was aware of the problems and
that the issue will be addressed.
VI. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-582-99 – Zoning Variation: 775 Lincoln Ave. - Adoption. Trustee
Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the ordinance permitting a
reduced front yard setback. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent:
President Holland.
Ordinance No. MC-585-99 – Zoning Variation: 545 Sheridan Rd. - Adoption.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting an increased garage door width. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, and
Lien. Nays: None. Absent: President Holland.
Ordinance No. MC-229-99 – Class E Liquor License for Corner Cooks –
Introduction. Attorney Janega briefly reviewed the request for a Class E liquor
license for Corner Cooks. She noted that wine would be served only to class
participants and that the cost of the wine would be included in the cost of the
class. Brent Shaphren, on behalf of the applicant, explained the concept to the
Trustees and responded to questions. After a brief discussion of the proposal,
there was consensus among the Trustees that the applicant meet with the Village
Attorney to further clarify the appropriate license category being sought
before returning to the Council for adoption. However, in order to keep the
licensing process advancing, Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
moved to introduce the ordinance increasing the number of Class E liquor
licenses from one to two. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VII. Unfinished Business.
Cherry Street Alley Improvements. At 7:45 p.m. Trustee Hilton moved to recess
from the Regular Council meeting to convene as the Board of Local Improvements
for the purpose of ratifying the opening and reviewing the sealed bids for the
improvements to the public alley between Ash and Cherry Streets, from Locust to
Rosewood. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By roll call vote, the
motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and
Powell. Nays: None. Absent: President Holland.
   The Trustees reconvened into Regular Session at 7:50 p.m. Present: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent. President Holland.
Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney Katherine
Janega.
VIII. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1137 and 1138. Trustee Powell commented on a couple of items
on the warrant lists and, then, moved to approve Warrant List No. 1137 in the
total amount of $154,028.39 and Warrant List No. 1138 in the total amount of
$572,537.58. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: President Holland.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Pro Tem Duhl referred the Trustees to
the report filed by DK Recycling for the month of July, noting that the annual
IEPA annual inspection has been conducted and a compliance letter was received
by the company on July 29th.
IX. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Powell reported that the Historical Society has three
major issues to resolve during the coming year: (1)finding a new location; (2)
hiring a new museum director; and (3) acquiring and moving the Village’s log
cabin.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported on the progress of numerous Village
projects.
X. Appointments.
None
XI. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
President Holland. The Council convened into Executive Session at 7:57 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:21 p.m. Present: President Pro
Tem Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
President Holland. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney
Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
8:22 p.m.
