September 7, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved September 21, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 7, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien,
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and about 14 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
September 14, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend. President Holland announced that the Plan
Commission would be presenting its completed Comprehensive Plan for the first
in a series of Public Hearings.
September 21, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
August 17, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Martin Kenny, 510 Meadow Rd., reiterated complaints and comments about the
parking problems in the Indian Hill area and asked that the Village stringently
enforce its parking ordinances.
Manager Williams informed Mr. Kenny that a Notice of Violation had been issued
and that the parties were currently in the process of negotiating through
attorneys.
Al Day, 85 High Street, confirmed Mr. Kenny’s comments saying that the
neighborhood continues to be plagued by heavy vehicular traffic which poses a
threat to the neighborhood, particularly just following the end of the school
day.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-229-99 – Class E Liquor License for Corner Cooks - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the application and a meeting held with the applicant
regarding the proposed operation of the establishment. She confirmed that the
applicant plans to sell specialty tools, wines and food items needed for at-
home preparation of the meals prepared in classes. The items the applicant
proposes to sell meet the definition of foods and food-related products
required for a Class E liquor license. She noted that she had explained to the
applicant that the sale of wine must be accompanied by the sale of a food item
to be consumed off the premises.
A representative of the applicant responded to questions from the Trustees.
Although not opposed to this license request, Trustee Duhl cautioned that the
Village needs to insure that it can distinguish this type of operation from
that of a White Hen Pantry, for example, so that it does not open the door for
package liquors to be sold by limited food products stores.
Ms. Janega expressed her opinion that this operation, as described in the
application, falls within the purview of a Class E license, but that at some
time in the future it might be necessary to redefine the license or reclassify
the operation.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting the issuance of a second Class E liquor license for Corner Cooks. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien, Duhl,
Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-583-99 – Special Use Permit: Corwin Park, 1550 Edgewood -
Adoption. Trustee Derning moved to amend the ordinance to reflect the Plan
Commission’s determination. The motion was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, issuing a Special Use Permit to allow the replacement of existing
playground equipment. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Hilton,
Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-584-99 – Special Use Permit: Hubbard Woods Park, 939 Green Bay
Road - Adoption. Trustee Powell moved to amend the ordinance to reflect the
Plan Commission’s determination. The motion was seconded by Trustee Derning. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, issuing a Special Use Permit to allow the replacement of existing
playground equipment. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Powell,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1139 and 1140. Trustee Aquilino moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1139 in the total amount of $597,062.51 and Warrant List No. 1140 in
the total amount of $567,492.10. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None
Winnetka Presbyterian Church Proposal. Representatives from Winnetka
Presbyterian Church briefly previewed proposed expansion plans which in the
near future they plan to bring before the Village’s appropriate boards for
action. They also responded to questions from the Trustees.
Display of Merchandise on Sidewalks. Susan Afryl owner of For Kids Only, 1048
Gage Street reiterated her request for permission to display merchandise on the
Village sidewalk. She presented the Trustees with a compilation of surveys
which she had mailed to neighboring business owners indicating that few
objected to her request.
Trustee Derning inquired whether, if granted, the owner would have to comply
with insurance requirements. Manager Williams indicated that it would be
strongly recommended.
There was some discussion as to what might be an appropriate limitation on the
intrusion of the merchandise onto the sidewalk, with Manager Williams saying
that outdoor cafes are required to maintain a five foot sidewalk clearance.
Trustee Hilton asked whether this would be setting a precedent for future
similar requests. Mr. Williams indicated that it would, but that Attorney
Janega had established that there are unique circumstances which can be used to
distinguish this property from other situations. He recommended that, if
granted, a permit be granted on an annual basis, similar to that for sidewalk
cafes, which would permit the Council to deny the request should the need
arise.
The Council asked the Village Manager to return at its next meeting with a
written policy plan for permitting this use.
X. Reports
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported on the progress of various Village
Projects.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Duhl, Derning, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:20 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent: Trustee
Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:21 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
September 14, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved October 5, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 14, 1999, at 7:30 p.m., immediately following a Special Meeting of
the Village Council.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and about 26 persons in the audience.
II. Comprehensive Plan – Public Hearing.
Ms. McCammack read the Public Notice for the Public Hearing.
President Holland introduced Bernie Sergesketter, Acting Chairman of the Plan
Commission. Mr. Sergesketter thanked the Council for its continued support
throughout the planning process. He also thanked the many community
participants who participated in focus groups, completed the 2020
Questionnaire, and attended meetings. Finally, he thanked his committee
members, especially noting the contributions of Nan Greenough and Penny
Lanphier.
Ms. Greenough briefly reviewed the planning process for the Trustees and
members of the audience. She noted that some minor, essentially non-substantive
revisions have been made to the Plan submitted to the Trustees on July 14th,
and that there are several in-house modifications that still need to be made to
the maps, as well as the pending incorporation of Appendix 6.
On behalf of the Council, Trustee Hilton thanked all of the members of the Plan
Commission for their contributions.
President Holland then called for comments from the audience.
Al Day, 85 High Street, expressed his thanks to the Commission for their
efforts, especially to the subcommittee for its concern for and responsiveness
to the Indian Hill neighborhood.
Lois Bishop, 1290 Scott St., expressed some concern about the possible
incorporation of design standards because they ultimately might deter the
variety of styles which currently exist. Ms. Bishop went on to comment about
numerous other elements of the draft Plan.
John Berwanger and Bernie Sergesketter informed Ms. Bishop that the design
standard component of the Plan had been eliminated.
Richard Kates, 1526 Tower Rd., complimented the Commission on its overall
effort, but saying that public input and refinement continue to be important,
went on to discuss several points, which in his opinion, require further
consideration. He remarked upon the future use considerations for the landfill
and post office sites and commented upon items dealing with fences, front
porches, and traffic flow.
Marty Weil, 983 Vine St., expressed his belief that the Village ought not
encourage further townhouse developments as the neighboring properties tend to
be degraded.
Joan Noyes, 381 Elder Lane, voiced concern for the maintenance of open space
within the Village.
President Holland praised the draft Comprehensive Plan and noted its importance
for the future development of the Village. She stressed that the Plan was
developed especially for and written by the residents of this Village. She
reiterated her praise for the Plan and thanked the Plan Commission members for
their many, many hours of time devoted to perfecting the document. Then, she
announced that further Public Hearings would be held on October 12th and 21st,
with an eye toward introducing the new Comprehensive Plan on November 2nd and
adopting it on November 16th.
The meeting adjourned at 8:25 p.m.
September 21, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved October 5, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 21, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien,
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief William Gallagher, and about
six persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
October 5, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
October 12, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend. President Holland reminded everyone that this
meeting would be devoted to discussion of the draft Comprehensive Plan.
V. Approval of Minutes
September 7, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee
Aquilino, moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln., complimented the Village on the newly
painted crosswalks at Hubbard Woods School and asked that the same be done in
the area around Sacred Heart School. Manager Williams explained that the
striping machine had been put away for the season. He said that the Public
Works Director had been consulted on this issue and determined that this could
safely wait until next spring.
The Trustees asked Mr. Williams to have staff contact the school to ascertain
their position on this issue.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-589-99 – Changing Name of Alley from Tower Road to
Tower Court - Introduction. Bill Hiscott, a representative of the applicant
explained the request that the Village rename the alley that runs along the
west side of the railroad cut in Hubbard Woods, extending from Tower Road to
Gage Street. He noted that all of the other businesses on the affected alleyway
have joined in the request and that the name requested is “Tower Court.”
Manager Williams said that this situation is analogous to the renaming of part
of Chestnut Street to Chestnut Court and that the appropriate department heads
had approved the request.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the ordinance
effecting the name change. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Manager Williams mentioned to the applicants that once approved, the name
change would not take effect until 30 days after notice had been filed with the
correct authorities, which also would provide time to update the Village’s
emergency and utility records and prepare proper signage.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1141 and 1142. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1141 in the total amount of $170,277.28 and Warrant List No. 1142 in the
total amount of $669,394.77. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Display of Merchandise on Sidewalks. Mr. Williams reviewed this issue. He
briefly explained the conditions enumerated in the Agenda Report which could be
attached to any use of the public sidewalk for merchandise display. He
commented that these were merely ideas, suggested by staff pursuant to
Council’s discussions at the September 7th meeting, and that the Council needed
to decide which, if any, it wished to adopt.
After an extended discussion on the recommended criteria, the Trustees agreed
unanimously that:
1)   The operator of the business should meet the same insurance requirements
as sidewalk cafes: $2,000,000 with a carrier acceptable to the Finance
Director, with the Village named as a primary, non-contributory additional
insured, and with the wording of the certificate of insurance being subject to
approval by the Finance Director.
2)   A display may extend up to 3 feet from the building into the public way,
except that there must be at least 5 feet of unobstructed sidewalk space
between the display and the curb for pedestrian traffic. Tree, hydrants, street
lights, sign posts and the like should be considered obstructions. Tree grates
should also be considered obstructions, as the Village has consistently taken
the position in litigation that they are not intended for pedestrian use.
3)   An annual permit should be required, as with sidewalk cafes.
4)   All displays must be removed for the winter, no later than the day of the
first snowfall. They should not be put out again the next spring until the next
annual permit has been issued.
With regard to whether the proposed display should be reviewed and approved by
the Design Review Board, Trustees Derning and Lien felt that it would
complicate the process and was unnecessary given that the agreed upon annual
permit could be withheld if there were flagrant violations of aesthetic
guidelines. The remaining Trustees, however, felt that some sort of review
process was desirable and that the Design Review Board was the appropriate body
to conduct that review. President Holland called for a vote on the matter which
resulted in a vote of 4 to 2 in favor of having the review option included in
the criteria.
With regard to the final condition, whether an applicant must establish unique
circumstances that distinguish the location of the applicant’s business from
other business locations in the Village, the Trustees were again split.
President Holland called for a vote. Trustees Lien, Derning and Duhl were
opposed, while Trustees Hilton, Aquilino, and Powell were in favor. Voting in
order to break the tie, President Holland voted in favor of requiring an
applicant to establish the existence of unique circumstances.
Manager Williams then explained that it was staff’s recommendation that the
process and criteria be incorporated into a resolution and brought back to the
Council for approval at the next regular meeting.
MollyBelle Berish reiterated her concerns about the implications of permitting
merchandise to be displayed on sidewalks.
Design Engineering for Tower Road Water Main, Storm Sewer, and Pavement
Rehabilitation. Mr. Williams explained that the Village has programmed the
rehabilitation of Tower Road in its Capital Improvements Plan for FY 2001-02
and is currently discussing implementation of this project with the Illinois
D.O.T., in conjunction with jurisdictional transfer of the roadway from IDOT to
the Village. He said that due to the scope of the project and the workload of
the Village’s engineering staff, it is desirable that an outside consultant be
employed to prepare the project plans. He advised the Council that a proposal
to provide the required engineering services for this project had been
requested and received from Ken Keene, the former Public Works Director, who
possesses both the experience and the qualifications to perform this work. Mr.
Williams indicated that in staff’s opinion, Mr. Keene’s proposed fee of not-to-
exceed $30,550 was fair in light of the estimated construction cost of
$1,450,000 given that, historically, fees paid to consultants tend to fall into
the range of 4 to 8 percent of the construction cost.
Trustee Hilton questioned the advisability of accepting a jurisdictional
transfer from IDOT to the Village. Manager Williams responded that while in the
long run, it might not be prove to be as economical for the Village, it does
put the Village in control and allows it to do things with the street that it
might not otherwise be able to do.
Trustee Lien asked some questions about Mr. Keene’s insurance situation and
expressed some concern about the Village’s not seeking other proposals before
settling on a consultant. Trustee Duhl concurred and moved to table the
proposal and proceed with a formal RFP process. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland commented on the report of
operations during the month of August at the Winnetka Compost Site.
X. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported on the Chamber of Commerce’s
continued search for a new director. He also reminded the Trustees about the
upcoming meeting devoted to improving the viability of the business districts
and stressed the Chamber’s desire to work in closer conjunction and cooperation
with the Village, especially on special events.
Trustee Duhl reported on the recent meeting of the Beautification Committee.
Village Manager.
Manager Williams reported on the status of construction projects throughout the
Village and provided an overview of other critical projects which are underway.
He reminded the Trustees that additional Public Hearings regarding the draft
Comprehensive Plan had been scheduled for October 12th and October 21st.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland recommended the appointment of Betty Skalski as a
representative from the Chamber of Commerce to the Ad Hoc Committee on Design
Guidelines. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to make the
appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:47 p.m.
