                  Deputy Clerk
October 5, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved October 19, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 5, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien, and John Powell.
Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Finance Director Ed
McKee, and about 12 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
October 12, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
October 19, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Duhl, indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
September 14, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Powell,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
September 21, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
Trustee Aquilino arrived at 7:40 p.m.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-589-99 – Changing Name of Alley from Tower Road to Tower Court
- Adoption. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the
ordinance effecting the name change. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien, and Duhl.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-590-99 – Zoning Variation: 333 Rosewood Avenue - Introduction.
Mr. Williams reviewed a request to permit construction of a one-story room and
one car garage addition that will result in reduced north and total side yard
requirements. He noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-0 to
recommend approval of the requested variance.
The applicant, Nancy Marcus, responded to questions from the Trustees, and
Trustee Derning commented that the architecture of the proposed addition is in
consonance with that of neighboring homes.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-591-99 – Zoning Variation: 680 Locust Street – Introduction.
Mr. Williams reviewed a request to permit the construction of a roofed front
entry porch, or stoop, which will result in increasing the existing,
nonconforming building size (GFA). He noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals
had voted 4-0 to recommend approval of the requested variance.
Jeff Harting, 680 Locust, a representative of the architects displayed boards
showing the proposed construction.
Trustee Derning clarified that although the house is currently undergoing
renovation, the stoop is part of the original structure. Mr. Harting responded
in the affirmative.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. Williams explained that the next ordinances were a series of ordinances
done each year and asked the Finance Director to briefly explain each. Mr.
McKee reviewed the Agenda Report on the Property Tax Levy and Tax Abatements on
the Public Safety Building Bonds. He noted that existing property tax payers
could see a 3% increase, provided that the CPI supports such an increase. He
said that the additional funds would primarily be used to offset inflation and
the rising cost of employee wages. Mr. McKee also responded to questions from
the Trustees.
Ordinance No. M-586-99 – An Ordinance Levying Taxes for the Year 1999 -
Introduction. Trustee Powell moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-586-99,
seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-587-99 – Tax Abatement on General Obligation Bonds, Series 1994
- Introduction. Trustee Hilton moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-586-99,
seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-588-99 – Tax Abatement on General Obligation Refunding Bonds,
Series, 1996 – Introduction. Trustee Powell moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
586-99, seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote the motion carried
unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
Adopt Policy for Displaying Merchandise on Public Sidewalks. President Holland
introduced this item by reading a letter received from the Board of Directors
of the Chamber of Commerce noting the recent passage of a resolution opposing
the display of merchandise on public sidewalks except for food, beverages, and
flowers.
Trustee Derning expressed some question about the intent of the Chamber’s
resolution resulting from their use of the word “sale” as opposed to the use of
“display.”
Jean Wright, a representative from the Chamber clarified the Chamber Board’s
action, saying that it had not been their intent to distinguish between sale
and display and that the resolution was meant to encompass both activities.
The applicant, Susan Afryl, reiterated her request and her supporting
arguments.
Trustee Derning noted the significant support received by the applicant from
neighboring merchants, saying that he felt that was a persuasive argument in
favor of the proposal.
Trustee Lien concurred, saying that there appeared to be a significant
difference of opinion between the Chamber’s Board and the Chamber members. He
said that he was in favor of adopting the policy as outlined in the Agenda
Report on an experimental basis to see how it worked, noting that the Council
could always choose at a later time not to renew it.
Trustee Aquilino voiced her general opposition to the policy, and inquired as
to the intent of the original ban.
Aside from the obvious safety concerns where the sidewalks were less wide than
on Gage Street, President Holland pointed out Police concern about
rubbernecking by passing drivers and a desire to prevent the streets from
looking like a “flea market” as possible reasons for the original ban.
Trustees Hilton and Powell noted that although initially they had been in favor
of granting the request, they had changed their positions. Trustee Hilton said
that he felt it would be a mistake for the Village to allow the display in
light of the Chamber’s resolution.
Trustee Duhl reiterated his opposition based upon the difficulty of defining
what constitutes “unique circumstances” as required by the policy. He also said
that while he doesn’t see much of an issue if only a few merchants were to
pursue permits, he would not want to see merchandise displayed outside by
numerous merchants.
Ms. Afryl responded to the comments by the Trustees and thanked the Trustees
for their careful consideration.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the proposed
Statement of Policy Regarding the Display of Merchandise on Public Sidewalks.
By voice vote, the motion failed. Ayes: Trustees Lien and Derning. Nays:
Trustees Aquilino, Duhl, Hilton, and Powell.
Trustee Derning commended staff for formulating the policy and expressed regret
that the Village is unable to support its merchants in this manner.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1143 and 1144. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1143 in the total amount of $286,841.00 and Warrant List No. 1144 in the
total amount of $245,568.38. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Request to Vacate a Portion of Elm Street. Mr. Williams presented a request
from adjacent property owners to vacate the end of Elm Street abutting Lake
Michigan and sell the property to them. He noted that, historically, the
Village has denied similar requests to vacate and sell the ends of streets
abutting Lake Michigan and pointed out major public policy questions that arise
from the consideration of this request. Mr. Williams also mentioned that there
had been some objections to the request on the part of neighbors.
David Grossberg, attorney for the applicants, presented their request and
explained the reasons therefor. He noted their desire to improve the security
of their property because of undesirable activities that take place on the
secluded land, along with their desire to build a new garage set into the
hillside. He said that this street end was significantly different from others
in the Village where there is a usable beach front. Mr. Grossberg also said
that the applicants would be pleased to modify their proposal to permit the
purchase of half of the street end by the other neighboring property owners. He
urged the Council to act favorably on this issue to solve a Village security
problem while affording the Kubickas an opportunity to improve their property.
Nancy Kubicka presented a historical perspective of the security issues
encountered during their ownership of the property adjoining the street end.
James Murray, an attorney for a neighboring property owner, voiced an objection
to the request. He cited the vacation of this property as being the loss of a
substantial public asset and urged the Council to summarily deny the request.
Trustee Hilton asked Mr. Williams to review the reasons for the Village to
retain the street ends. Manager Williams explained that the issue was basically
a philosophical one, because once the Village loses the point of access, it is
gone forever. He also mentioned that from a pragmatic point of view, the land
was needed to handle maintenance issues, i.e. storm sewers, etc., but that
these, in fact, could be handled through easement agreements.
Trustee Aquilino pointed out that the Kubicka’s present garage is hardly
visible from the street and, therefore, not an eyesore to passing motorists or
pedestrians. Alan Kubicka replied that the present garage was inadequate and
needed replacement. He said that if they are not permitted to build a new
garage into the hillside, they will be forced to build a new one, which will
end up being significantly higher, and therefore, more visible than the current
one.
Trustee Powell indicated that the Council should review the policy issues
surrounding the retention of its street ends along with its historical position
of retaining them as public property. He suggested that they might provide
beautification opportunities in keeping with goals mentioned in the new
Comprehensive Plan.
Trustee Derning said that while the use of the street ends might be an
appropriate subject of study, he could not support the sale of the property.
Trustee Lien agreed that the issue merits more study. He said that although he
could not support the sale of the property at this time, a solution might be
devised which would be a benefit to both the public and the private landowners.
President Holland affirmed the consensus of the Trustees that they would not
consent to the requested vacation and sale of the property. She indicated that
the Council might undertake further study of the issue, but that any action
would be taken very slowly in view of the fact that the street ends are of
critical importance and value to the Village.
Manager Williams indicated that staff had contacted an erosion expert who was
expected to report on all the street ends and provide ideas for their use and
improvement.
Bid No. 99-020 – Power Plant Roof Bids. Mr. Williams explained that the bid
would cover the repair and replacement of the roof above Boilers #5-#7 at the
Electric Plant. The successful low bidder was American Roofing & Repair in the
amount of $68,425.00.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the bid of American
Roofing. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Rock Salt Bids. Mr. Williams explained that the Village participates in the
purchase of rock salt through the State of Illinois bid and will require
approximately 1,075 tons for the coming winter season. The successful bidder
for this requirement is IMC/North American Salt Co. at $26.24/ton.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to approve the bid of IMC/
North American. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
NTHS Centennial Vehicle Sticker. Manager Williams reviewed a request from New
Trier High School, Centennial Celebration Steering Committee to have the
Village use New Trier’s Centennial logo on the Village’s vehicle stickers for
January, 2001- January, 2002 year. He indicated that the high school had
approached all five communities in the township asking them to honor the
anniversary in a similar manner. He reported that the projected cost for
redesigning the Village’s vehicle sticker would amount to approximately $5,000.
Wes Baumann, Principal, displayed the logo designs for the Trustees. He
indicated that Wilmette and Winnetka, from which the school draws the majority
its students, are the most likely communities to be able to participate in this
aspect of the celebration. He said that the high school would be willing to
work with the Village to achieve a mutually acceptable agreement about covering
the increased costs.
Trustee Aquilino asked whether the words “Village of Winnetka” could be
incorporated into the logo design. Mr. Baumann indicated that they could be.
After discussing the request, Trustee Derning moved to approve the request,
leaving the negotiation of the cost issue to the Village Manager. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
X. Reports
Village President. President Holland announced the receipt by LaBella Winnetka
of awards from Chicago Magazine and NorthShore Magazine for “Best Italian Food
and Best Romantic Dining Where the Locals Eat” and “Best Outdoor Dining,”
respectively.
Village Manager.
Comprehensive Plan Schedule. Mr. Williams reviewed the schedule for public
hearings and adoption of the Comprehensive Plan.
Construction Update. Manager Williams reported on the status of current
construction projects throughout the Village.
He also noted that staff has just learned that IDOT has scheduled the
reconstruction of the Tower Road bridge over the Skokie River for the spring of
2001. He indicated that to minimize the inconvenience to motorists, staff
believes that the rehabilitation of Tower Road in the Village should be
coordinated with this bridge reconstruction schedule.
Electric Rate Comparisons.   Mr. Williams reported that according to an
American Public Power Association report, the ComEd average charge for
residential service in 1998 was 10.66 cents per kWH, while the average
residential kWH charge for Winnetka residents was 9.95 cents in fiscal 1998-99.
He pointed out that although ComEd will not reduce rates this year, the Village
of Winnetka has reduced its residential rates another 5%.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland recommended the appointment of Carter Page to the Landmark
Preservation Commission. A motion to appoint Mr. Page was made by Trustee
Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:49 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:50 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
October 12, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved October 19, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 12, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Director of Finance, Ed McKee, and about 7 persons in the audience.
II. Comprehensive Plan – Continued Public Hearing.
President Holland opened the continued public hearing.
Cindy McCammack noted that at the public hearing on September 14, the Village
Council received public comment on the Plan Commission’s draft Comprehensive
Plan. After reviewing the comments received at the hearing, the Plan Commission
amended Section 2.7, on Parks, Open Space, Recreation and Environment and
approved Appendix 6 to the Plan at its September 22 meeting. Presently, the
Plan Commission is continuing its work preparing the front cover of the Plan
document and staff is still working on the final format of the color maps. In
response to questions from President Holland and Ms. Janega about Appendix 6,
Ms. McCammack said that the appendix is the priority recommendations for the
Comprehensive Plan and will be updated annually as part of the Commission’s
annual report to the Council. She also noted that the Table of Contents will be
revised to correctly reflect the title and placement of Appendix 6 in the Plan.
Trustee Aquilino questioned if it was the intent of the Commission to impose
landscaping requirements as part of zoning requirements. She also asked about
the reference in Chapter 4 of the Plan to create streetscapes in residential
neighborhoods.
Nan Greenough responded that the Commission had not discussed requirements as
such but that landscaping would be something to look at for new construction to
maintain the mature landscaping that is a distinguishing feature of Winnetka.
She said that the streetscape idea discussed was that if a neighborhood wished
to do a decorative street lighting project with their own funds, for example,
the Village would have certain lights that the residents would have to use for
that project. Ms. Greenough indicated that this issue will be addressed further
in Design Review and will include public spaces as well.
Trustee Aquilino referred to section 4, page 14 of the Plan and said that she
is uncomfortable with the idea that the Village create a foundation to solicit
donations from private people for public use.
Ms. Greenough explained that the Plan Commission had intended that the
donations would be for projects that would be in addition to the projects set
up by the Village and that the Commission never perceived it as an active
fundraising activity for the Village. She said the Commission’s concern was
that if there was a rogue entity orchestrating a project, the Commission would
want that overseen by the Village. The Commission had also wanted to provide an
avenue for people who would like to donate money for a particular project.
After further discussion regarding the creation and administration of a
foundation or a fundraising group similar to Friends of the Library, the
Trustees agreed instead that publishing a list of Village-approved projects,
possibly by gift catalogue, would allow people to donate to specific projects
if they wished and directly to the Village.
Trustee Lien suggested that the Plan Commission use the term “vehicle” instead
of foundation to encourage gifts and donations.
Trustee Hilton, referring to the first page of Appendix 6, asked Manager
Williams about stormwater drainage and how much has been done this year.
Manager Williams replied that the reality is that the most Winnetka can do is
relieve a two to five-year storm. He said that many of the problems have been
relieved and the last two drainage projects are Tower Road, which is slated for
two years from now, and the golf course. He said there is also discussion about
a levee project, the possibility of increasing pumping capacity, and stormwater
detention at Crow Island Woods, which has been recommended by staff but has
been resisted by residents in that area. In response to a question from Trustee
Powell, Manager Williams said that the only detention area other than the
Skokie Playfield is at Crow Island Woods, although any park can act as one. He
further stated that some of the property south of Tower Road near Heather Lane
may be designated as wetlands, as is Butler Pond, and could not be used for
detention.
Trustee Hilton commented that it seemed there had been a lot of progress made.
Trustee Aquilino asked about Section 5.7, paragraph C, regarding general plans
and landscaping improvements along the Green Bay Road corridor, which refers to
a map that does not seem to be included with the Plan document.
Ms. McCammack replied that this map was very small in scale and had no detail,
so was excluded and noted to amend the document to reflect this change.
Manager Williams asked the Council to refer to Appendix 6, on the second page,
to a list of things to study under priority #2. He said that the second item is
to study the need for standards for bluff stability along the lakefront. After
speaking with Ms. McCammack earlier in the day and concluding that there is
nothing in the Village Code that addresses this issue, Mr. Williams recommended
that this item be moved to the priority #1 category.
President Holland commented that there is a current project off of Maple Street
beach that is being built into the bluff that has raised concern for this
issue. She suggested that the Council members view the site and asked if they
would agree that this item be included as a priority #1.
President Holland announced the next public hearing on the draft Comprehensive
Plan will be Thursday, October 21 and that introduction of the Plan is
scheduled for the Council’s November 2 meeting, with adoption on November 16.
Ms. Greenough said that all of the comments from each of the meetings have been
considered and discussed by the Plan Commission, many of which had already been
addressed in the draft of the Plan. She said the Plan Commission would prepare
a memo listing the changes made to the draft to present to the Village Council.
President Holland requested that Ms. Greenough ask all of the members of the
Plan Commission to be present at the meeting on November 16 because the Council
has a presentation to make to them. She then declared the Comprehensive Plan
hearing continued to Thursday, October 21, 1999.
III. Fell Request to Use Public Sidewalk.
President Holland recognized Mr. David Fell and asked him to present his
request to use the public sidewalk in front of his business on Lincoln Avenue
for a promotional event on November 6th.
Mr. Fell said that they are planning a promotion in November and hope to use
space on the street to have displays and a climbing wall in front of their
store on Lincoln Ave. He said there will be a public appearance by a world-
renowned rock climber, displays of literature and a van from KISS FM radio
station. The Land Rover dealer will also be there with vehicles on display and
promotional literature, so he is requesting the use of space on the east side
of the street and parking spaces on the west side to re-route traffic.
Manager Williams also explained that no merchandise will be displayed or sold
on the public right-of-way, but that staff had requested policy guidance from
the Village Council. He said that Public Works and the Police Department can
accommodate the traffic flow and believe the present traffic flow can remain
with barricades separating the other areas. Mr. Williams mentioned that there
is also an event at the Community House at the other end of Lincoln Avenue that
may increase traffic that day. He said that the main concern is that the
Village’s insurance will not cover this event. The Village requires insurance
from Fell Company and will also ask the rock climbing company for insurance,
but there will be no back up of our own insurance.
After questioning Mr. Fell about who will be climbing the wall, the set up of
the wall and supervision of the climbers and discussing further the issue of
the Village’s liability in case of injury with input from Ms. Janega, Mr.
Williams and Mr. McKee, President Holland stated that the consensus of the
Trustees was that it would be irresponsible to subject the residents of the
Village to such a risk. She asked Mr. Fell to investigate the possibility of
erecting the climbing wall on his own property because, although the Council
would like to encourage the event, the liability is too great.
Mr. Fell thanked the Council members for their consideration and said that they
are still asking for the use of parking spaces on the street. Manager Williams
said that the standard $5,000,000 insurance would be asked of the Fell Company.
After further discussion among the Trustees, he summarized the comments of the
Council members that using the parking spaces in the street is approved, but
not the climbing wall.
Manager Williams announced that the United States Seventh Circuit Court of
Appeals affirmed the Federal District Court’s decision that the balefill site
is under the jurisdiction of the federal Clean Water Act. Effectively, SWANCC
will not be able to construct the balefill in Bartlett in accordance with this
decision. He indicated that the next SWANCC meeting will be in two weeks to
discuss this.
The meeting adjourned at 8:45 p.m.
October 19, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved November 2, 1999)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 19, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, David Hilton, John Lien, and John
Powell. Absent: Trustee Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and about four persons in the
audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that Item 9(a), Request for Cul de Sac on Arbor
Vitae, and Item 9(b), Ambulance Bid, had been removed from the Agenda. The
Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
IV. Quorum.
November 2, 1999, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Derning, indicated that they would be able to attend.
November 9, 1999, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
October 5, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
October 12, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Lane, requested that the Trustees consider saving
all of the video tapes of past Council meetings for an indefinite period rather
than for only the past six months. Manager Williams advised the Trustees that
this would be a change from a policy decision made several years ago. President
Holland indicated that the Council would consider putting the matter on the
agenda for a future study session.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-590-99 – Zoning Variation: 333 Rosewood Ave. – Adoption.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting reduced north side yard and total side yard requirements. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino,
Powell, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-591-99 – Zoning Variation: 680 Locust St. – Adoption. Trustee
Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting
an increased GFA. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien and Derning. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-586-99 – An Ordinance Levying Taxes for the Year 1999 –
Adoption.
Manager Williams explained that this was the annual tax levy ordinance, noting
that it is in compliance with the Budget adopted last March. He said that Mr.
McKee was available to answer any questions from the Trustees. There being
none, Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the
ordinance levying taxes for the Year 1999. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning, Aquilino, and
Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-587-99 – Tax Abatement on General Obligation Bonds, Series 1994
– Adoption. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aqulino, moved to adopt the
ordinance abating taxes on the Series 1994 General Obligation Bonds. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino,
Powell, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. M-588-99 – Tax Abatement on General Obligation Refunding Bonds,
Series 1996 – Adoption. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to
adopt the ordinance abating taxes on the Series 1996 General Obligation
Refunding Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Duhl.
Resolution No. R-1473-99 – Pertaining to the Collection of Funds to Pay Losses
and Costs Related to Real Estate Tax Collections – Adoption. Manager Williams
explained staff’s recommendation that in addition to the tax levy ordinance and
related abatements, the lost and cost collection factor for taxes collected for
purposes other than debt service be reduced from 1.0% to 0.0%, as the item can
be a source of tax objections. The lost and cost collection factor for taxes
levied for bond principal ad interest would remain at 5%. Trustee Powell,
seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt this resolution. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Duhl.
Ordinance No. MC-230-99 - Pertaining to Location of Newspaper Bins –
Introduction.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce this ordinance
which would regulate the location of newspaper racks throughout the Village.
Manager Williams explained that this ordinance had been drafted as a result of
discussions by the Ad Hoc Beautification Committee, which has identified the
placement and proliferation of newspaper racks, especially the “freebies” which
contribute significantly to litter problems, as an eyesore. He asked Attorney
Janega to review the particulars of the document, which she did. She said that
the ordinance would provide tighter controls over the placement of newsracks by
imposing specific, enforceable standards. She noted that as drafted, the
ordinance would fall under the Village Code’s provisions governing the issuance
of permits and licenses and the provisions pertaining to nuisances. She
explained that the draft ordinance does not specify that newspaper racks be
placed where they cannot be seen, but that it effectively moves them out of the
most visible spots without having to go through a detailed regulatory process
with the Design Review Board. It also prohibits their presence on sidewalks and
in locations which might endanger the safety of persons or property.
After extended discussion by the Council, there was a consensus among the
Trustees that the issue be slated for consideration at a study session so that
the Council can obtain more information before voting on a draft ordinance. In
response thereto, Trustee Powell withdrew his motion and Trustee Aquilino
withdrew her second.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Request for Cul de Sac - Arbor Vitae. As noted earlier by President Holland, at
the request of the petitioners, this items has been removed from the Agenda.
