November 9, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved November 16, 1999)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 9, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, David Hilton, John Lien and John
Powell. Absent: Trustee Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and one person in the audience.
II. Discussion of Draft Ordinance MC-230-99 Establishing Standards for the
Location of Newsracks.
Manager Williams noted that the draft ordinance had been prepared in response
to a recommendation by the Ad Hoc Beautification Committee in an effort to stem
the visual blight that results from the uncontrolled location of newsracks and
to protect the Village from damage to public property and from exposure to
liability from the presence of obstructions in the public ways or on public
property. In order to provide the Trustees with the Committee’s perspective,
Mr. Williams presented slides showing the current allocation of newsracks at
the three Village train stations. He then asked Attorney Janega to review the
key features incorporated into the ordinance.
Ms. Janega said that first, the draft ordinance requires a permit for the
lawful placement of newsracks. Secondly, it recognizes that, historically,
newsracks have been located near the train stations rather than on sidewalks
throughout the Village, which provides an additional basis for restricting the
presence of newsracks on public sidewalks. Thirdly, the ordinance limits the
placement of newsracks on both public and private streets and sidewalks, which
provides the authority to require the newsracks currently located on the
sidewalks outside of the train stations, facing the street, to be moved to an
out-of-the-way location. And finally, in addition to regulating the size,
number, appearance and placement of newsracks, it requires that newsracks fully
enclose their contents and that either a key or coins be used to access the
contents.
She explained that the locational standards are detailed enough that decisions
on permit applications can be made with a minimum of administrative effort, and
without requiring approval by the Design Review Board. The ordinance also
authorizes the summary removal of non-complying newsracks, as well as providing
for a simple and direct appeal process.
She also pointed out that the ordinance amends the Nuisance provisions of
Chapter 12 and the Handbill provisions in Chapter 45 of the Code, to cover the
visual blight and littering aspects that also come into play.
The Trustees discussed the issue at length. There was consensus among them to
rewrite the draft with a focus toward addressing the litter and visual blight
issues caused by the “free” newspapers and to further investigate the
possibility of procuring “designer” newsracks, similar or identical to those
recently installed in the City of Chicago. It was agreed to discuss the matter
again after the first of the year.
III. NTHS Sidewalk Lease.
Manager Williams reviewed a request from New Trier High School to enter into a
non-exclusive lease agreement that would allow it to use the sidewalk adjoining
the school property for school purposes. He explained that by so doing, New
Trier would be able to exercise greater control over student activities on
those sidewalks. He noted that because the School District’s authority over
student activities on school property is broader than the Village’s general
police powers, the lease arrangement would benefit both the school and the
Village by providing more alternatives for handling loitering, disorderly
conduct and other forms of misbehavior on the sidewalks. He also mentioned that
New Trier High School had entered into a similar lease agreement with Winnetka
Park District in 1996, in order to control activity at Indian Hill Park.
Attorney Janega explained that the drafted lease agreement is virtually
identical to the lease agreement between New Trier and the Park District. It
provides for a 5 year term with 2 successive 5-year renewal terms. It allows
New Trier to use the leased premises for its school activities, but prohibits
putting up any signs or structures without Village permission, and requires
that the sidewalks always be available for use by the general public. It states
unequivocally that New Trier’s use of the premises is subject at all times to
the Village’s ordinances, rules and regulations relating to streets, sidewalks
and public ways, and reserves the Village’s right to enact ordinances and to
promulgate rules and regulations regarding the use of the premises at any time
during the term of the lease.
In addition, she said that the lease provides that the Village will continue to
maintain the sidewalks in the same manner as it maintains its other sidewalks.
Any additional costs resulting from more frequent or more extensive maintenance
would be borne by New Trier. Similarly, New Trier would be responsible for any
damage to the sidewalks resulting from its use under the lease agreement.
She further explained that there are several provisions that serve to protect
the Village from liability. First, New Trier waives claims against the Village.
Second, it provides broad indemnification for the Village. Third, the
indemnification is backed by insurance requirements. There is also protection
against the use of hazardous materials.
The termination provisions are flexible, allowing for termination of the lease
if the use of the premises is abandoned, if a default is not cured within 30
days or if New Trier places hazardous materials on the sidewalks. In addition,
either party can terminate the agreement for any reason by giving 60 days
written notice. The indemnification and hazardous waste protections survive the
agreement, as does the insurance protection, since it is provided on an
occurrence basis rather than on a claims-made basis.
She noted that there is a property tax provision that would require New Trier
to assume the responsibility for paying property taxes if the lease triggers
that liability.
Finally, the lease contemplates that it will be administered by the Village
Manager and the Superintendent of Schools, with the key activity in that regard
being the annual development of a memorandum of understanding detailing the
specific times that the sidewalks would be used by New Trier during that year.
Manager Williams indicated that this lease could become the model for the
Winnetka, District 36, schools as well, which are also very interested in the
concept.
Trustee Powell asked about possible application to the private and parochial
schools in the Village. Ms. Janega responded that they are not covered by the
School Code so their situations are different. Trustee Derning said that he did
not believe that these schools were experiencing the same kinds of problems as
the public institutions.
There was consensus among the Trustees to proceed with the lease. Manager
Williams said that after polishing the draft, it would be mailed to the
Superintendent of Schools for review before being brought back before the
Council for action.
IV. Appointment:
President Holland requested approval of her appointment of Keith Groebe,
Chairman of the Building Review Committee, as representative to the Ad Hoc
Committee on Design Guidelines. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell,
moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
The meeting adjourned at 8:27 p.m.
