reduced GFA results from removing the screens and opening the rear porch. She
mentioned that revised drawings were to be submitted, which might further
reduce the proposed GFA. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted
6-0 to recommend approval of the variation request.
Trustee Derning suggested that a provision be added to the draft ordinance
specifically stating that screens would not be permitted to be added to the
open, roofed area on the property. The Trustees concurred with this
recommendation.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-596-99 - Special Use Permit: 530 Hibbard Rd., Winnetka Park
District, Temporary Offices - Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed a request
from
the Park District for a temporary Special Use Permit to locate modular office
units at the rear, north side of the A. C. Neilsen Tennis Center. The two units
will serve as administrative offices for those employees currently located at
Skokie School. It is expected that these units will not be needed for more than
three years.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the ordinance
to allow location of modular office units for a period of not to exceed three
(3) years. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1149 and 1150. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1149 in the total amount of $155,913.17 and Warrant List No. 1150 in the
total amount of $614,669.00. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Videotape Policy. Manager Williams commented on a request to consider the
possibility of retaining the videotapes of Council meetings permanently instead
of the current six month period adopted by Council in 1994. He reviewed for the
Trustees the history of checkouts and enumerated potential problems that could
result from permanent retention of the tapes. Based upon the usage pattern, he
recommended either retention of the current policy or a reduction in the time
frame.
Trustee Duhl remarked that in his opinion the purpose of the video tapes is
community service, and as they become quickly obsolete and the Council minutes
constitute the official record of proceedings, he believes that the detriment
of keeping the tapes outweighs the benefit for which they are intended.
Therefore, he moved to shorten the retention period to two months. The motion
was seconded by Trustee Derning. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland directed the Trustees' attention
to the report filed by the Village's compost operator for the month of October.
Reports
Village President. President Holland asked Nan Greenough to explain her ideas
for a plaque dedicating the Elm Street bridge to Frank Windes, a former Village
Engineer, for his contributions to the Village and whose design ideas have
coincidentally been incorporated into the new bridge.
Trustee Derning,, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the placement of
a plaque on the Elm Street bridge dedicating it to Frank Windes. By voice vote
the motion carried unanimously. Manager Williams said that staff would return
to the Council with a formal resolution dedicating the bridge to Mr. Windes.
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning, reported that he had met with the Chamber of
Commerce and that they are encouraged by the progress on the Elm Street bridge
and were contemplating a ribbon cutting ceremony. He also reported that they
hoped to pursue the concept of a Chamber web site.
Trustee Powell reported that the Historical Society has postponed finding a new
director until they find a new home. He said that they were moving aggressively
on getting a new location and hope to have something soon.
Village Manager. Manager Williams updated the Trustees on the status of the Elm
Street Bridge and briefed the Council on other major projects throughout the
Village.
X. Appointments.
President Holland announced that Margaret Benson had agreed to continue to
serve in the capacity as Chairman of the Zoning Board of Appeals and asked for
a motion to reappoint her to that Board. Trustee Hilton made the requested
motion. It was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Holland then proposed Gwen Trindl, former Village President, as the
new Chairman, of the Plan Commission. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee
Aquilino, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Finally, President Holland thanked John Kollar for his service on the Design
Review Board and suggested the appointment of Gary Frank as his replacement.
Trustee Derning,, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to approve the appointment.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Holland also thanked Max Malmut for his service on the Board of Fire
and Police Commissioners.
XI. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Hilton seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Duhl, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:43 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:15 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
Adjournment. Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Aquilino
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting
adjourned at 9:16 p.m.
