                  Deputy Clerk
December 14, 1999
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved January 4, 2000)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 14, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, John Lien and John
Powell. Absent: Trustee Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of
Public Works Steve Saunders, Village Forester Jim Stier, and one person in the
audience.
II. Audit Report - Irwin Lyons, Miller, Cooper & Co., Ltd. 
Mr. Irwin Lyons, the Village’s auditor, briefly reviewed the Audit Report. In
doing so, he noted that the Village had once again received the Government
Finance Office Certificate for Excellence in Finance Reporting and mentioned
that the Village also had received an unqualified opinion from Miller, Cooper &
Co.
Mr. Lyons responded to questions from the Trustees and commented on the
Management Letter, which he noted is negative by nature, although there are no
critical items requiring attention. He pointed out that some of the issues are
carryovers from previous years and need not be repeated in the future if the
Trustees are comfortable with current procedures. With regard to one of those
items, accounting for checks, the Trustees concurred that they were satisfied
with the current procedures and, therefore, advised Mr. Lyons that this issue
need not be raised in subsequent Management Letters.
Manager Williams then highlighted various sections of the Report for the
Council. He concluded by saying that the Village had experienced another very
good year; revenues were up, and expenses had been held to within budget. He
noted that steps were being taken to deal with the ever increasing costs of
health insurance.
President Holland formally thanked Finance Director Ed McKee for his
recommendations and diligent oversight of the Budget and Mr. Lyons for his
firm’s thorough report.
III. Forestry Ordinance Update.
Mr. Williams introduced this item saying that the discussion draft had been
prepared with an eye toward strengthening the forestry ordinance in an effort
to address some of the challenges faced by the Village Forester.
Attorney Janega provided an overview of changes to the Village Code proposed by
the Forestry Commission and Village Forester. She explained that in general,
the legal issues involve determining the nature and extent of the Village’s
police powers, identifying and striking a balance between the public health,
safety and welfare interests and the private property interests it seeks to
protect, and identifying regulatory standards and administrative procedures for
implementing the Village’s policy. She noted that the practical issues include:
(a) recognizing that trees in public locations are often in close proximity to
utility infrastructure and public streets; (b) assuring that the regulations
established do not hinder public works and utilities operations; and (c)
assuring that the administration and enforcement of the regulations can be
easily understood and effectively implemented. She also noted that with the
pending recodification, the forestry provisions of the Village Code would be
reorganized to provide a more logical structure.
Mr. Saunders expressed the need to address a number of overall policy issues
that need to be articulated so that an ordinance can be prepared that has teeth
and is enforceable. One example he gave was the question of whether trees trump
building, or vice versa,
Manager Williams explained that currently Village staff does whatever it can to
save a tree, but if a developer or homeowner can’t live with the resolution,
the Village comes down on the side of private property rights.
President Holland stated her belief that trees should come first and expressed
her frustration with builders who abuse the Village’s trees by failing to
adequately protect them during construction.
Trustee Powell noted that he would like to see aggressive enforcement of the
Village’s laws. However, he went on to say that as a practical matter, he
didn’t think that the Village could always come down in favor of saving a tree,
especially when the presence of a tree interferes with building plans and all
reasonable, alternative designs have been exhausted.
Trustee Derning noted that it is important that the issue of abuse not be
commingled with that of what to do when building plans and trees conflict, in
which case, in his opinion, a homeowner’s property rights need to prevail. The
Trustees concurred.
After discussing examples of violations and the subjects of fencing and
reforestation, it was the consensus of the Trustees that staff should examine
ways to better protect trees from willful abuse as well as ways to facilitate
better administration and enforcement of regulations. It was agreed that
Attorney Janega would confer with the Village Forester, the Forestry
Commission, and the Code Committee before revising the draft.
IV. Reconsideration of Policy Regarding Tree Planting on Private Property.
Mr. Williams reviewed the history of the issue and a request by the Forestry
Commission that the Council reconsider the question of establishing a private
property tree-planting program.
James Fox, Chairman of the Forestry Commission, explained that while the
committee had originally approached the Council on this issue with regard to
Bertling Lane, they had not intended that it only apply there. He noted that
Winnetka is the only community in the neighborhood that doesn’t have a private
tree planting program for reforestation and that the goal of the program would
be to engage residents in beautifying their private property. He indicated that
it was never the intent of the committee that the Village accept any kind of
risk or liability as a result of this program. Rather, it was proposed that the
Village would merely serve as a clearing house, affording residents the
opportunity to take advantage of price breaks given to the Village.
Trustee Duhl asked Dr. Fox and Mr. Stier to explain how the Committee
envisioned the Village implementing such a policy, which they did. Trustee Duhl
noted that when considering the prior proposal, in addition to liability
issues, the Council had felt it inappropriate to offer a program only to some
residents and had been concerned about whether Village staff was sufficient to
support such a program. Dr. Fox reiterated that there would be no liability to
the Village, as participants would be required to sign a release, and said that
the Village Forester had indicated to the Committee that he would be able to
support a limited program.
Manager Williams commented that if the Council wants to institute such a
program, the rules need to be clearly defined.
The Trustees agreed that they were conceptually in support of a program if it
could be accomplished within the constraints of the Forester’s other job
requirements and asked staff to return with a specific proposal for
consideration.
V. Miscellaneous Items.
Mr. McKee distributed a memo regarding property insurance proposals and noted
that he would be bringing the issue before Council at the next meeting. He
pointed out that there had been a significant jump in the cost, from about
$37,000 to approximately $90,000.
President Holland showed the Trustees the proof of the plaque dedicating the
Elm Street Bridge to Frank Windes.
The meeting adjourned at 9:23 p.m.
December 21, 1999
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved January 18, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 21, 1999, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien, and John Powell.
Absent: Barbara Aquilino. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, and about four persons in
the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
January 4, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
January 11, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
December 7, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-597-99 – Zoning Variation: 5 Indian Hill Road - Adoption.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting structural alterations. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning, Powell, and Hilton. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
Ordinance No. M-598-99 – Zoning Variation: 757 Locust Street - Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting replacement of an existing roof and construction of new dormers. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton,
Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
Resolution No. R-1476-99 – Dedicating Elm Street Bridge to Frank Windes -
Adoption. In accordance with Council’s decision at the November 16, 1999,
Regular Meeting, Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt
this resolution dedicating the Elm Street Bridge to Frank A. Windes, former
Village Engineer, in recognition of his many contributions to the Village of
Winnetka from 1898 to 1940. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1153 and 1154. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1153 in the total amount of $476,629.57 and Warrant List No. 1154 in the
total amount of $668,631.28. Trustee Derning seconded the motion.
Trustee Aquilino arrived at 7:43 p.m.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Request for Public Meeting – Water Plant Chlorine Risk Management Plan. Mr.
Williams explained that pursuant to the Clean Air Act, facilities that at any
time house more than a threshold amount of specified hazardous substances are
required to prepare and submit a Risk Management Plan. Because at times the
Water Plant has more than the threshold amount of chlorine gas on hand, it has
prepared such a plan and is further required to share the information therein
with the public via a public meeting, which pursuant to the federal law must be
held no later than February 1, 2000. He asked the Council to schedule the
public meeting for discussing the Water and Electric Department’s Risk
Management Plan and its attendant Off-site Consequences Analysis during the
Regular Council meeting on January 4, 2000.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to schedule the requested
meeting for January 4, 2000. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Property Insurance Proposals. As Mr. McKee explained at the December 14, 1999
Study Session, the Village’s current property insurance policy will expire on
December 31, 1999. Mr. McKee introduced the Village’s insurance consultant,
Bill Lienhieser of Risk Resources, who reviewed the reasons that the current
provider no longer provides a viable option, explained why there has been a
substantial increase in the cost of the insurance, and appraised the Trustees
about available alternatives. Mr. Lienhieser’s recommendation, supported by Mr.
McKee, was that the Village accept the proposal of AIG/Kemper through Lamb,
Little for coverage in the amount of $79,081,600 costing $91,266.00.
President Holland inquired whether the Village had ever made a claim on its
insurance. Mr. Lienheiser responded in the negative and said that this
information had been included in the Request for Proposals.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the recommended
proposal. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland directed the Trustees’ attention
to the monthly report filed by the Village’s composting operator. In addition,
she formally thanked the Public Works Department for its efficient job removing
leaves this fall.
Request to Extend Winnetka Galleria License Agreement. Ms. Janega reviewed a
request from the developer of the Winnetka Galleria to extend the License
Agreement that allows it to enclose and use a portion of the Lincoln Avenue
parking lot in conjunction with the development of the Winnetka Galleria
building. The request seeks to extend the Agreement, which expires on January
1, 2000, through the first quarter of the new year. She noted that the original
License Agreement was authorized by Resolution No. R-1434-98 and that any
extension or other modification of the terms of the Agreement must, therefore,
be made by resolution. She summarized key terms of the Agreement and outlined
options available to the Council.
President Holland recognized Heather Higgins, resident and merchant, who asked
the Trustees to consider the impact this construction is having on the
neighborhood. She said that the Developer has taken up an excessive number of
parking spaces during construction which has been particularly hard on local
merchants, especially during this holiday season. She suggested that if the
Council approves an extension, that they do so only with the limitation that
the Developer use no parking spaces on Lincoln Avenue.
Leslie Bullock, another Lincoln Avenue merchant, also commented on the effect
the construction has had on area businesses.
Thomas Corrano, a representative of the general contractor, expressed his
sympathy for the merchants. He indicated the Developer’s desire to work with
the merchants to minimize the impact of the construction on their business and
on traffic. He explained that in order to avoid damage to nearby parked cars,
they prefer to block off more than the minimum amount of space necessary for
truck traffic.
President Holland expressed her strong dismay and frustration over the lack of
progress on this project and at the Developer’s apparent disregard for the
impact of the project on the merchants, especially during the holiday season.
She pointed out that, ultimately, this directly affects the Village in the form
of lost sales tax revenue. She acknowledged that the Council has no choice but
to come to terms on an extension in order to facilitate the completion of the
project, but she recommended that in order to avoid further inconvenience and
disruption to the Village, the Developer be barred from the use of Lincoln
Avenue until it is ready to proceed with the building façade.
Trustee Powell noted that although he had initially been very supportive of the
project, based upon the Developer’s performance thus far, he would now have a
hard time supporting any extension whatsoever.
Trustee Derning noted that the Developer has the Village over a barrel because
the project must be completed. He supported President Holland’s recommendation
that Lincoln Avenue be returned to the Village.
The Developer, Peter Cummins, commented briefly on the situation.
Trustees Lien and Duhl echoed President Holland’s dissatisfaction with the
Developer’s performance and expressed a lack of confidence in its ability to
complete the project in a timely fashion. Both indicated that they would like
to deny any extension of the Agreement but recognized that, ultimately, that
would not be in the Village’s best interest. They supported the return of
Lincoln Avenue to the Village and suggested significantly increased rental for
the parking lot spaces, including a $50,000 cash deposit payable before January
1, 2000.
Trustee Derning agreed that complete use of Lincoln Avenue needed to be
returned to the Village immediately.
Trustee Lien moved that the Developer be required to deposit $50,000 as a cash
bond as security for the parking lot rental fee, with the actual rate to be
determined by negotiations between staff and the Developer, and that the
Lincoln Avenue area be immediately vacated, saw horses removed and fences moved
back to the sidewalk not later than January 1, 2000, with the deposit to be
forfeited if that is not done.
Trustee Duhl seconded the motion and reiterated that Lincoln Avenue has to be
unobstructed.
Trustee Powell said that the barricades had to be removed immediately.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Duhl,
Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Manager Williams indicated that staff would work out the details and return
with a proposal at the next meeting.
X. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Duhl reported on the recent meeting of the
Beautification Committee. He told the Council that the Committee had approved
of the selection of Teska & Associates as landscape design consultants for the
area around the Indian Hill parking lot and hoped to discuss specifics of the
design with the consultant at its next meeting.
Trustee Derning reported that the Chamber of Commerce was delighted with the
opening of the Elm Street Bridge. He also indicated that they would be coming
to the Village to formalize the holiday lighting program for the future and to
request a review of the lighting levels throughout the Village’s business
districts and associated areas.
Village Manager. Mr. Williams asked that the Trustees to set aside the nights
of February 7, 14, and 28, 2000 for Budget Review meetings. He also informed
them that it was expected that the undergrounding of the electric lines on the
Village Green would be completed by April 1, 2000.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:19 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:20 p.m.
         
         _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
2000
January 4, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved January 18, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 4, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien,
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian
McInturff, Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack, and about
eighteen persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
January 11, 2000, Study Session. President Holland announced that this meeting
had been cancelled.
January 18, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
December 14, 1999, Study Session. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Powell,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-1-2000 – Winnetka Village Code - Introduction. Ms. Janega
explained that the proposed ordinance would adopt the amended Village Code,
which has been comprehensively recodified. She pointed out that during the
recodification process, the Code was totally restructured, using Titles and
Chapters organized by subject matter so that the Code is more logically
organized, more flexible, and more easily accessible to both Village staff and
the general public. She said that the new Code would be made effective on
February 1, 2000. Copies were distributed to the Trustees.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
adopting the new Code. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-1-2000 – Special Use Permit – Winnetka Presbyterian Church,
1255 Willow Road - Introduction. Ms. McCammack summarized the request for a
Special Use Permit to allow for the expansion of the existing church building
and reconfiguration of the parking lot. She briefly explained the Church’s
plans and noted that four zoning violations would result from the proposed
redevelopment, as follows: (1) roofed lot coverage, (2) impermeable lot
coverage, (3) gross floor area, and (4) front setback.
Ms. McCammack noted that the Village’s Traffic Engineer, Steven Saunders, had
commented on the traffic study provided by the applicant and found no
exceptions to the remarks made therein. She said that Mr. Saunders did not feel
that the proposed addition would significantly impact traffic circulation or
add to congestion. With regard to the proposed decrease in on-site parking, she
reported that she had polled three similar communities, Glencoe, Lake Forest
and Wilmette, in order to determine what their on-site parking requirements
would be under similar circumstances and found that even with the proposed
reduction, the Church would meet the requirements in all three instances.
Ms. McCammack also reported on the recommendations of the Plan Commission,
Zoning Board of Appeals, and Design Review Board, all of which supported the
issuance of the Special Use Permit.
Manager Williams commented on the problems attendant to applying residential
standards to institutional uses within a residential district.
Pete Cruikshank, 710 Lincoln Avenue, spoke on behalf of the Church and
introduced each of the individuals who would be making presentations. He also
remarked on the success of the Church’s fund raising efforts.
Jack Chapin, Chairman of the Church’s Architectural Design Committee, reviewed
the processes the Church has gone through to determine the size and scope of
the necessary expansion. He said that the Church had (1) developed and followed
a master plan; (2) met with and responded to neighbors; (3) completed a
successful capital fund drive; and (4) met with all appropriate Village boards
and committees
Martha Greene, Senior Minister, provided a historical perspective of the Church
and its design. She explained their need for a parish hall, or Christian Life
Center, to accommodate the programmatic needs of an ever increasing, diverse
congregation. Among the needs she cited were the needs for a space in which to
(1) serve 250 people a sit-down meal; (2) have a full kitchen in which to
prepare such a meal; (3) have a site in which 50 young people can engage in
gross motor movement; and (4) have an air conditioned space in which to hold
summer worship.
Mr. Chapin presented a video which had been prepared for their fund raising
campaign, entitled “A Time to Build Up,” about the Church and its activities.
Chris Mekus, the Church’s architect, reviewed the specific expansion plans
using the architectural elevations of the project. He said that the Christian
Life Center would act as an anchor on the east end of the campus and that the
aesthetics of the building would remain the same in order to preserve its
existing character.
Mr. Williams inquired as to the location of the air conditioner compressors and
their relative proximity to the closest neighboring house. Mr. Mekus responded
that they would be located behind a wooden fence on the north side of the
proposed Christian Life Center and that he would guess that there would be a
distance of about 100 feet to the nearest house.
Trustee Aquilino asked whether or not there were plans to expand and/or air
condition the sanctuary. Mr. Mekus replied that there were none at this time.
Trustee Powell expressed concern about the potential impact on drainage. Mr.
Mekus indicated that it was the Church’s desire to work with both the Village
Engineer and the neighbors to be certain that there are no detrimental effects
from the construction. Manager Williams said that the Village would require
detailed elevations showing where all of the drainage would go and that if the
project was not completed according to specifications, the Village would
require that it be razed and redone.
Trustee Derning commented that the existing retaining wall on the north and
east of property appears to be in good condition. He stated his belief that
drainage from the Church’s property could not adversely affect the neighbors
and that it is not the Church’s responsibility to provide drainage for the
neighbors. He also confirmed with Mr. Chapin that modification to the sanctuary
is not a part of this proposal.
Trustee Duhl referred to correspondence from neighbors expressing concerns
about the project. Mr. Chapin indicated that the Church had responded to the
neighbors on each of the points raised.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the ordinance
which would allow for the expansion of the church. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
President Holland thanked the representatives from the Church for their
presentations and indicated that the matter would be up for further discussion
at the next Council meeting on January 18th.
Resolution No. R-1-2000 – Motor Fuel Tax Maintenance Transfer - Adoption. Mr.
Williams explained that historically, the Village authorizes the allocation of
$125,000 from the MFT Fund towards street maintenance, which is a permitted use
of MFT funds.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution continuing past practice and authorizing expenditure of $125,000 for
street maintenance. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Resolution No. R-2-2000 – Release of Executive Session Minutes - Adoption. Mr.
Williams explained that pursuant to state law, the Council is required to
examine and release Executive Session minutes which no longer require
confidentiality. The proposed resolution would cover Executive Session minutes
from December 1, 1998, through December 7, 1999. He noted that the resolution
specifies the retention of certain sets of minutes for confidentiality due to
ongoing litigation.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the Executive
Session minutes from May 18, 1999, through December 7, 1999. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Powell then moved to adopt Resolution No. R-2-2000. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1155 and 1156. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1155 in the total amount of $257,607.39 and Warrant List No. 1156 in the
total amount of $456,916.70. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Public Meeting – Water Plant Chlorine Risk Management Plan. President Holland
opened the Public Meeting
Mr. McInturff reviewed remarks from the last meeting regarding the requirements
for this meeting. He also commented briefly on the Risk Management Plan, the
attendant analysis of operating procedures, and the consequences of a “worst
case” scenario.
In response to a question from Ms. Janega, Mr. McInturff said that the Village
had been using chlorine since 1922 and that the last incident was a minor one
that happened approximately three years ago.
There being no further comments or questions, President Holland closed the
meeting.
Request for Ban on Cell Phone Use While Driving. Mr. Williams presented a
request from a resident for Council to consider enactment of an ordinance
barring the use of cell phones while driving a motor vehicle. He remarked that
the Village attorney had reviewed the issue and determined that it is probable
that a way could be found to do so, but that she also had concluded that it is
likely that any such act would be challenged. He indicated that both the Police
Chief and the Village Prosecutor had been consulted, and both recommended that
a regional approach was, likely, to be more effective, and mentioned that the
Northwest Municipal Conference had referred the matter to its legal committee
for study. Mr. Williams suggested that the issue be proposed to the Caucus for
inclusion on its questionnaire in order to determine the extent of community
support, as, undoubtedly, it would be a controversial one. He concluded by
saying that if the Council wished to pursue this avenue, it needed to be
prepared to expend time and dollars on defending it.
Irwin Askow, 830 Hibbard Road, spoke in support of his request. He cited
health, safety, and welfare of residents as the reason for the proposal and
commented on various studies performed regarding the hazards of using a cell
phone while driving.
Trustee Duhl asked Mr. Askow whether he felt a distinction should be made
between hands-free and hand-held phones. Mr. Askow responded in the
affirmative.
Pete Cruikshank, 710 Lincoln, supported Mr. Askow’s comments, saying that he
felt that the use of cell phones, particularly hand-held ones, contributed to
making walking and driving around the Village hazardous.
Trustee Derning agreed that there is a problem but remarked that enforcement
could create its own problems. He said that, in his opinion, a regional
approach would be more effective and suggested contacting the Northwest
Municipal Conference to determine the status of their study.
Trustee Duhl concurred, commenting that the Village acting alone will be
ineffective, whereas, with a broader base of support, positive, corrective
behavior might be successfully encouraged.
President Holland noted that the Village would pursue the topic on several
fronts and bring it back for discussion at a later date.
Holiday Lighting and Lighting Levels. Mr. Williams presented a request from the
Chamber of Commerce to extend this year’s holiday lighting period through
January 2000 and to establish the period for the coming year to be from Friday,
November 17, 2000 through January 2001. He mentioned that traditionally, the
Village turns the holiday lights on the Wednesday before Thanksgiving and turns
them off the Monday after the weekend after New Years.
In addition, Mr. Williams noted that the Chamber had requested that the Village
review its year-round lighting levels of Village streets in the business,
shopping, and related parking areas, with an eye toward raising the levels,
especially during the dark days of winter. He remarked that, historically, the
Village’s philosophy has been to keep the lighting levels low out of respect
for neighboring residences and said that the issue has been one of ongoing
controversy throughout the years.
Eve Bremen, President of the Chamber of Commerce spoke in support of the
Chamber’s requests.
Trustee Duhl suggested that the issue of lighting levels be addressed in a
study session so that the Village could determine whether there is a group of
residents who would be adversely affected by raising the lighting level. He
also recommended that the Village extend the holiday lighting period for this
year but wait to see if any there is any negative reaction before formalizing a
regular, holiday lighting schedule.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the Chamber’s
request to extend the holiday light period through January 2000. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
President Holland assured the Chamber that the Village would come to a final
decision about the holiday lighting before Thanksgiving of 2000 and slate a
discussion of the overall lighting levels for an upcoming study session.
Private Tree Planting Program. Mr. Williams explained that further to Council’s
discussion at it’s December 14, 1999 Study Session, and pursuant to a
recommendation by the Forestry Commission, staff developed a pilot program,
outlined in the Agenda materials, whereby residents can have trees planted on
their private property, at their own cost, through a Village tree-planting
program. He pointed out that the experimental program would be limited in scope
in order to evaluate its popularity, effectiveness, and impact on staff
resources and noted that the Village would recover both its direct and indirect
costs. He recommended that the program be approved for a one year period, with
a follow up report to be made to Council at its conclusion.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to authorize staff to
implement the pilot tree-planting program, as outlined. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Zoning Variation: 425 Birch Street – Policy Direction. Ms. McCammack reviewed a
request for zoning variations to permit the construction of a second story
addition. She explained that the requested variations would increase the
existing, nonconforming roofed lot coverage, as well as the existing,
nonconforming GFA. In addition, the existing nonconforming side and rear
setbacks would be reduced. She also reviewed the history of previously
permitted variances on the property and action by the Zoning Board of Appeals
which voted 5-0 to recommend denial of the variances.
Attorney Janega explained that since this request came to the Council with a
negative recommendation from the Zoning Board, it would require the affirmative
vote of four Trustees and that the Village President could not vote to break a
tie.
The applicant, Jim Pomerantz, spoke in support of his request. He cited the
fact that the proposed addition would not change the foot print of the house
and has no impact on the neighbors as reasons to grant the request. He stated
his desire to improve the property while preserving the architectural integrity
of the home and the character of the older neighborhood.
Trustee Duhl inquired as to whether all of the proposed work was internal,
since Mr. Pomerantz represented that the foot print would not change? Ms.
McCammack indicated that there would, however, be a substantial change in the
roof line, adding height. Trustee Duhl commented that the construction would,
thereby, substantially increase the bulk of the building which was contrary to
the recently enacted Gross Floor Area ordinance which was aimed at controlling
bulk.
Trustee Derning explained to Mr. Pomerantz that the Council has conflicting
messages from the community; one being that they want to restrict the bulk of
building, the other being that they want to encourage redevelopment of older
homes. He said that it is frequently impossible to satisfy both. He indicated
that in this instance, he did not see that the requested variance would
negatively impact the neighborhood and that, therefore, he would support the
request. In addition, Mr. Derning suggested that the Council schedule a study
session aimed at reviewing the standards applied to zoning variations.
Trustee Lien spoke against the variances, saying that the fact remains that the
lot size does not support the requested variances and that the existing house
already exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area, as defined by the
Village’s ordinance which was enacted after thorough study and careful
crafting. He expressed concern that by permitting the requested variances, the
Village would be setting a precedent which would, effectively, loosen the
standards and run counter to general sentiment of the Village.
Trustees Powell, Hilton, and Aquilino supported Trustee Lien’s comments, saying
that the requested variances considerably exceed the standards, and that,
therefore, they could not support the request.
President Holland informed the applicants that although no formal vote was
taken, it was apparent that there was a consensus among the Trustees not to
support the request.
Mrs. Pomerantz responded to the Council’s decision.
Winnetka Galleria Extension of License Agreement. Manager Williams outlined a
proposed agreement negotiated with the Developer pursuant to Council’s
direction at the December 21, 1999 Regular Meeting. He reviewed the key
elements of the proposed agreement and noted that its fundamental principle is
to penalize the Developer significantly, by drawing up to $50,000 against
Letters of Credit, if the parking lot is not returned to the Village in a
timely fashion. The proposed extension would expire on April 1, 2000. Mr.
Williams reported that the Developer had agreed to the negotiated terms.
Trustee Duhl complimented staff on the tentative agreement.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to ratify the proposed
agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports
Village Manager. Manager Williams reminded the Trustees of the dates scheduled
for Budget Study Session dates meetings. He also reported on the Village’s
smooth transition into the new year without any Y2K problems.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:58 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:47 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
January 18, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved February 1, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 18, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m.
Present: Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, David Hilton, John Lien, and
John Powell. Absent: President Holland. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Public Works Director Steven Saunders, and about ten persons
in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
February 1, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustees
Powell and Duhl, indicated that they would be able to attend.
February 7, 2000, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
February 8, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
December 21, 1999, Regular Meeting. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee
Aquilino, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
January 4, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-1-2000 – Special Use Permit – Winnetka Presbyterian Church,
1255 Willow Road - Adoption. President Pro Tem Duhl announced that the Council
would hear testimony from both the Church and neighboring residents, but that
no action on the item would be taken at this meeting.
Pete Cruikshank, 710 Lincoln Avenue, spoke on behalf of the Church. He
indicated the Church’s desire to be a good neighbor. He expressed concern over
the proposed revisions to the ordinance regarding landscaping and lighting and
asked that the language be further modified.
Mr. Cruikshank addressed the issue of landscaping, noting the difficulties and
consequences of requiring additional landscaping on the north side of the
property, because the presence of the curbs and footings there leaves only
about 20” for planting between the curb and property line. He noted that
landscaping inside the curb would eliminate parking spaces along the north side
of the property and also expressed concern about the cost of providing a row of
trees along the full length of the north property line. Regarding the lights,
Mr. Cruikshank stated that the proposed language should be modified because the
church may consider simply purchasing the lights from the Village. Mr.
Cruikshank also presented a brief video showing the existing property lines and
landscaping of the properties abutting the Church’s property to the north. He
concluded by saying that the proposed expansion would result in minimal
substantive change to the existing visions of the property owners.
Jack Chapin, a member of the Church’s Building Committee, addressed a question
raised at the last meeting about the Church’s long term, master plan. He stated
that, eventually, the Church hoped to make a small increase to the footprint to
the west, but that it would not include an increase in elevation, nor was any
increase in seating capacity or parking anticipated. Mr. Chapin indicated that
there were no current plans to seek funding for such a project as the
congregation was already heavily committed to funding the expansion under
discussion.
Mr. Duhl inquired as to how many parking spaces would have to be removed to
accommodate an additional landscaping buffer. Mr. Chapin responded that twenty-
two parallel parking spaces along the driveway would have to be removed,
reducing the total number of parking spaces to forty-seven.
Trustee Derning asked whether parking along the driveway to the north would
permit the passage of emergency vehicles. Mr. Williams responded that the
Deputy Fire Chief had reviewed and approved the plans.
Mr. Williams asked the Church’s representatives if they had considered offering
to help the abutting property owners pay for additional landscaping on their
own property in order to alleviate their concerns. Mr. Chapin said that they
had not.
James Marcus, 1212 Ash Street, addressed letters filed by himself and other
neighbors in opposition to the proposed expansion plans. Speaking for himself,
he said that if additional landscaping were provided to block the view and
enhance beautification, he would remove his objections regarding the adequacy
of parking. In addition, he requested the location of an 8’ fence, which would
require a variance, along the north property line, since the grade of the
parking lot is raised above that of the neighboring yards. He also asked that
if the Council approves the Zoning Board’s recommendation, that (1) the chronic
drainage problems experienced by the neighbors be addressed; (2) the HVAC units
with their associated noise be removed from proximity to the neighbors; (3) the
proposed basketball facilities be eliminated; and (4) the garbage and refuse
facility be located away from the northern neighbors.
In response to Mr. Marcus’s comments urging additional landscaping
requirements, Mr. Williams asked how he would feel if the cars that would be
eliminated from parking along the driveway were to be parked in the
neighborhood.
Mr. Marcus replied that he, personally, would rather have trees than parking
spaces, and that the occasional use of the street for parking would not present
a problem.
Trustee Duhl asked Mr. Marcus his position should the Church offer financial
assistance to help pay for additional plantings on his property. Mr. Marcus
said that, speaking for himself, he didn’t think he would oppose the option.
Jim Farmer, 388 Berkeley, asked the Council to carefully consider the fact that
all of the Church’s immediate neighbors oppose the expansion not only for
visual reasons, but for reasons of drainage, increased traffic and parking in
an already congested area, and potential effect on property values. He also
expressed his opposition based upon the use of different zoning standards for
an institutional property vis-a-vis a residential property. He objected to
residents being required to demonstrate a high level of hardship, whereas, the
Church was not.
Trustee Duhl responded that Mr. Farmer’s observations were not misplaced,
saying that the Village does, in fact, apply different standards to
institutional requests. He said that the Zoning Ordinance is aimed at single
family residences but pointed out that the Village does have institutional
buildings throughout its residential neighborhoods. He explained that the
Village consciously chose not to adopt different standards for institutional
uses, so that each individual case would have to be heard and considered on its
merits. He pointed out that the Council is charged with the responsibility of
trying to best accommodate the interests of both types of users, but noted that
if the Village is going to grow, it has to allow its institutions to grow as
well.
Bill McAdam, 1234 Ash Street, urged the Council to require the provision of a
buffer between the residential neighborhood and the Church. He expressed no
concern with the significant reduction in the number of parking spaces and
resulting increase in on-street parking, should landscaping be mandated.
Trustee Duhl inquired as to his position on a possible offer from the Church to
help with additional landscaping on his property. Mr. McAdam responded that he
would consider it as a choice of last resort.
Trustee Powell asked Mr. McAdam his view of the drainage problems. Mr. McAdam
noted that, although he does not experience a significant problem, some of his
neighbors have flooding.
John Stauffer, 1255 Ash Street, supported Messrs. Marcus and McAdam’s position
regarding parking, saying that the Church only contributes a few cars to the
already congested on-street parking. He reiterated concerns about HVAC noise
and appropriate facilities to house garbage and refuse.
Mr. Cruikshank responded to the comments from the neighbors, and in response to
a question from Trustee Powell about the drainage issues, indicated that the
architects and engineers needed to reexamine the issue. He also said that the
Church would strictly comply with regulations regarding HVAC and make every
attempt to make it as inoffensive as possible.
Chris Mekus, the Church’s architect, remarked that the Church had been given
the Village’s HVAC requirements and would abide by them. He also mentioned that
fencing would be provided around both the HVAC units and the garbage/refuse
area. In response to comments from the audience, he noted that it would be
difficult to locate any mechanical equipment on the front façade of the Church.
Ms. McCammack stated that prior to issuing a permit for the installation of the
HVAC equipment, certification by a qualified engineer would have to be
provided, stating that the sound levels are in compliance with state standards
officially setting the thresholds for noise spillover.
Trustee Duhl questioned Village Engineer Steve Saunders about the stormwater
run-off. Mr. Saunders indicated that he could see how water could flow around
the ends of the retaining wall. He noted that surface water run-off is a
Building Code compliance item and that, by ordinance, no building activity can
increase the amount of surface water runoff, create ponding, or divert natural
draining patterns. However, the Code does not require the repair of existing
drainage problems. He, too, pointed out that if compliance can’t be
demonstrated, no building permit would be issued.
Trustee Hilton asked Mr. Saunders whether the retaining wall could be extended.
Mr. Saunders answered affirmatively.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to comment on remarks made about the
traffic analysis and the repaving of Ash Street. Mr. Saunders indicated that
Ash Street, from Hibbard to Green Bay, is on the street reconstruction program
for next summer. He also said that it is Village policy not to widen streets in
residential neighborhoods as that tends to encourage speeding.
Trustee Duhl remarked that although the Church’s projected original loss of 19
parking spaces would probably be offset by the proposed switch to two services,
the elimination of additional parking in order to accommodate a landscape
buffer certainly would push cars into the neighborhoods for parking.
Trustee Aquilino asked Mr. Saunders whether drainage would be affected by the
installation of a planting buffer. Mr. Saunders said that it should not be.
Trustee Duhl thanked the presenters for their input and asked Council for their
comments.
Trustee Hilton noted that he saw the key issue as being landscaping vs.
parking, since Mr. Saunders had represented that the drainage would not be
allowed to be changed.
Trustee Lien expressed concern about the loss of parking spaces and noted that
it is only the very back of the residential lots that abut the Church that
would be affected and that the houses are some distance away. Pointing out that
there is no specific evidence to support the representations that the Church’s
project would affect drainage to neighboring properties or that there would be
any effect on property values, he voiced his support for the Church’s plan, as
submitted.
Trustee Derning concurred, while Trustee Duhl stated his opinion that a
landscape buffer would, in fact, be a benefit to the neighborhood.
In his capacity as President Pro Tem, Trustee Duhl noted that the item would
appear again on Council’s Agenda for its February 1st meeting and encouraged
all interested parties to attend that meeting.
Ordinance No. MC-1-2000 – Winnetka Village Code - Adoption. Attorney Janega
noted that the ordinance was the formal vehicle for replacing the old version
of the Code with the recodified and restructured version and reviewed changes
to the drafted ordinance. Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved
to approve the amendments as outlined. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-1-
2000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: President
Holland.
Resolution No. R-3-2000 – Ratifying Winnetka Galleria Extension Agreement -
Adoption. Attorney Janega briefly summarized the provisions of the Agreement as
approved at the last meeting and reviewed proposed amendments to the drafted
document. Manager Williams noted that the Developer had reviewed the amended
Agreement and agreed to the terms therein. He pointed to the Developer’s
compliance to date with all of the established deadlines and conditions in the
extension Agreement.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
authorizing the extension of the License Agreement, and approving the extension
Agreement with the amendments outlined by Ms. Janega. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton,
Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: President Holland.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1157 and 1158. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1157 in the total amount of $153,191.87 and Warrant List No. 1158 in the
total amount of $244,045.77. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Duhl, Hilton,
Lien, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: President Holland.
Quarterly Capital Improvement Reports. Manager Williams referred the Trustees
to the reports from the various departments included in their Agenda packets.
He pointed out that the Village experienced a very good construction season and
that almost all of the major projects were completed, with the exception of the
undergrounding of electrical wires on Sheridan Road which is expected to be
finished by the end of February.
X. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Derning reported that he had met with the Chamber of
Commerce and that they had been very pleased by the Village’s response to their
request to extend holiday lighting and the Village’s attention to the Galleria
project. He mentioned that the Chamber had developed a marketing plan for the
coming year and was planning to have an official ceremony to mark the opening
of the Elm Street bridge.
Trustee Duhl reported on the last meeting of the Beautification Committee
saying that the Committee had identified the Indian Hill area for immediate
attention and would be conferring with the designated landscape architect at
its next meeting.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reminded the Trustees about the scheduled
meeting dates for review of the Budget and noted that the February Study
Session would be dedicated to the Village Yards project.
XI. Appointments.
At the request of President Holland, President Pro Tem Duhl recommended the
appointment of Ken Behles to Design Review Board to replace Jim Antell whose
term has expired. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to
approve the appointment of Mr. Behles. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
President Holland. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:01 p.m. Present: President
Pro Tem Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
President Holland. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney
Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:02 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
February 1, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved February 15, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 1, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, David Hilton, and John Lien. Absent:
Trustees Michael Duhl and John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and about 12 persons
in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
February 7, 2000, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
February 8, 2000, Study Session. President Holland announced that this meeting
would now be devoted to review of the Budget. All of the Trustees present
indicated that they would be able to attend.
February 14, 2000, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
February 15, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
February 28, 2000, Study Session. President Holland announced that the Study
Session originally scheduled for February 8, 2000, to be devoted to the
discussion of a new Public Works Yards facility had been rescheduled for this
date. All of the Trustees present indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
January 18, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee
Aquilino, moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-1-2000 – Special Use Permit – Winnetka Presbyterian Church,
1255 Willow Road - Adoption. President Holland announced that due to the
absence of Trustees Duhl and Powell, the Council would hear comments from any
interested parties, but no action would be taken on the ordinance.
Ms. Janega noted changes to the draft ordinance, which was revised to reflect
key policy issues identified by the Council at the time of introduction and
additional issues identified by Village staff following the Council’s
discussion and public input at the January 18th meeting. She stated that the
key policy issues are (1) stormwater drainage, (2) landscape and parking, (3)
exterior lighting, (4) garbage enclosure, and (5) air conditioning.
President Holland then recognized Pete Cruikshank, who presented the Church’s
written comments on the proposed changes to the draft ordinance. He noted that
the Church took no exception to most of the changed language, but did have
concerns about some of the wording regarding landscaping. The complete text of
Mr. Cruikshank’s comments is attached hereto as Attachment A. He remarked on
the drainage issue, as well as the basketball court, the lighting, the garbage/
air conditioning enclosure, and the landscaping and parking issues. With regard
to the latter, he asked the Council to consider three points: (1) the cost of
the requested landscaping, which the Church estimates to be around $100,000;
(2) the effect of further reducing the number of parking places; and (3) the
fact that only two properties on Ash Street will directly face anything other
than what they now face. He stated that in the Church’s opinion, it ought not
be required to bear the unreasonable and unnecessary burden required to rectify
existing conditions that will not be impacted by the application. Mr.
Cruikshank concluded by requesting that the Council modify the language of the
draft ordinance to reflect the Church’s concerns about the requested
landscaping buffer along the Church’s north property line.
James Marcus, 1212 Ash Street, complimented the Village on the ordinance as
proposed, saying that he and the neighbors feel that the buffer is an important
component of the ordinance. He asked that the ordinance be adopted as presently
drafted.
Bill McAdam, 1234 Ash Street, reiterated his comments from the last meeting,
adding that in his opinion, as a part of the neighborhood, it is incumbent upon
the Church to provide a suitable buffer and perform up to the same standards as
established for other property owners. He stated his belief that the Village
has a compelling interest to see that the Church does so in order to preserve
property values.
Trustee Aquilino expressed continued concern about the impermeable surface. She
said that she would like to see some landscaping incorporated into the northern
area to create a small green area, perhaps by removing just a few of the
parking spaces.
Trustee Derning noted that it is important to recognize that this church group
is growing in size and that it is not unreasonable to assume that their parking
needs will increase as well, even with the change to two services. He expressed
opposition to the concept of removing any more parking.
Mr. Marcus reiterated his remarks from earlier correspondence and prior
meetings regarding the possibility of requiring the Church to construct a high
fence along the north property line. He said that a fence would not impact the
Church’s parking situation, but would satisfy the buffer concerns of the
neighbors.
There being no further comments, President Holland announced that the Council
would consider the ordinance for adoption at its next meeting on February 15,
2000. Manager Williams stated that he would provide more information to the
Council on the fence questions.
Ordinance No. M-2-2000 – Zoning Variation: 1165 Hamptondale - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed a request to permit the structural alteration of an existing
roof to accommodate a skylight which would require a reduced rear yard setback.
She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 7-0 to recommend that the
Council grant the requested variance.
The applicant, Robert Grant, spoke briefly in support of confirmation of the
ZBA’s recommendation.
Manager Williams remarked that, although the Code clearly provides that the
variation process be completed in this instance, in the future the Council
might wish to look at amending the Code to eliminate the need for a homeowner
to endure such onerous performance standards for such a small change. The
Trustees concurred.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-3-2000 – Zoning Variation: 301 Sheridan Rd. – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed a request to permit the construction of a front entrance
that will result in a reduced south setback. She noted that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 7-0 to recommend that the Council grant the requested
variance.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-4-2000 – IMUA Emergency Aid Agreement - Adoption. Mr. Williams
explained that this resolution would approve and authorize execution of the
Illinois Municipal Utilities Association’s Electric Utility Emergency Aid
Agreement. He said the Agreement as originally submitted by IMUA had been
modified by the Village Attorney to make it consistent with the Village’s other
mutual aid agreements.
Trustee Aquilino inquired as to the Village’s responsibilities under the
contract should the Village and other members experience problems concurrently.
Mr. Williams responded that each member would take care of itself first.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to adopt the Resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Aquilino, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and Powell.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1159 and 1160. Trustee Lien moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1159 in the total amount of $389,951.83 and Warrant List No. 1160 in the
total amount of $169,384.12. Trustee Derning seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Hilton, Derning, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and Powell.
Bid No. 99-028 –Packer Body Bids. Mr. Saunders explained the bids received for
the packer bodies of two refuse trucks, the chassises of which have already
been awarded. He noted that only one bidder, United Equipment Service with a
Pak-Mor body, met the necessary payload specifications, which are performance
based. He pointed out that it is very important that the vehicles be able to
pack as much refuse as possible while remaining within the roads’ load limits
because there are heavy fines for being overweight. He recommended that the bid
be awarded to United Equipment in the amount of $80,100.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Derning, Aquilino, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Powell.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to the
monthly report contained in the Agenda packets noting that all of the fall
leaves had been processed during the month of December and that the facility
had been inspected by the Illinois EPA with no problems found. She also thanked
the Public Works Department for their excellent job of collecting leaves this
fall season.
Software Proposals. Ms. McCammack explained the Village’s solicitation of
proposals for Building Permit Software and the services related to its
installation and operation. She recommended that the project be awarded to
Sierra Computer Systems in the total amount of $94,525. She said that their
product, Permits Plus, would enable the Village to provide true “one stop
permitting” and quickly process certain permit “fast track applications” by
automating the plan approval and permit activity tracing mechanisms. She noted
that immediate information access by other departments involved in the permit
process will expedite the handling of applications and provide a safety net to
ensure that the requirements of each involved department are met by tracking
their requirements and procedures.
Manager Williams remarked that this is the next step in the Community
Development Department in terms of streamlining to improve turnaround time and
improve the efficiency of information management. He noted that between the
monies budgeted for the current and next fiscal years, there are sufficient
funds to cover the expenditure.
President Holland called for a motion to approve the purchase. Trustee Hilton,
seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to authorize an agreement with Sierra
Computer Systems, Inc., for permit tracking software, in an amount not to
exceed $95,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Hilton, Lien, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl
and Powell.
X. Reports
Village President. President Holland announced that the Village had received a
letter of commendation from the Illinois Recycling Association in recognition
of the tenth anniversary of the start of the Village’s curbside recycling
program. The letter notes that the Village, as one of the pioneering
communities, has helped to divert thousands of tons of material from landfills
and to establish recycling as a service that is now expected by most Illinois
residents.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reminded the Trustees of the dates for the
upcoming Budget Review meetings and announced that the Budget has been
available for public review in the Manager’s office in Village Hall and at the
Winnetka Public Library since January 25th.
Mr. Williams also made note of an award received by NORTAF, the North Regional
Major Crimes Task Force, whose Executive Board is chaired by Chief Gallagher,
for the role it played in the investigation of a homicide last summer.
XI. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Duhl and
Powell. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, and Lien. Absent: Trustees Duhl
and Powell. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Derning seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:47 p.m.
         _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
February 15, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Adopted March 7, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 15, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, David Hilton, John Lien,
and John Powell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and about nine persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
March 7, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Powell, indicated that they would be able to attend.
March 14, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
February 1, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
February 7, 2000, Budget Review Meeting. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee
Hilton, moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
February 8, 2000, Budget Review Meeting. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee
Duhl, moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-1-2000 – Special Use Permit – Winnetka Presbyterian Church,
1255 Willow Road - Adoption. Pete Cruikshank, 710 Lincoln, spoke briefly on
behalf of the Church. He reported that a basketball locking device had been
installed and that conversations had begun between the Church’s landscape
contractor and the property owners of 1228 and 1234 Ash Street. He said that
the Church would be willing to accept any of the four options outlined in the
draft ordinance but indicated that, even if all four were eliminated, the
Church would continue to work with the neighbors at 1228 and 1234 Ash in order
to come to a mutually acceptable resolution to mitigate, at the Church’s
expense, the impact of the project on their views.
Bill McAdam, 1234 Ash, advocated a landscaping solution rather than a fence. He
said that all discussions so far have been conceptual and that there still are
no specific plans.
Trustee Derning echoed support for a landscaping option, and some discussion
ensued as to whether it would be better to place the landscaping on the
Church’s property or on the adjoining sides of the owners’ property.
Trustee Lien moved to delete Section 4(E) and (F) from the revised draft of
ordinance M-1-2000.
Attorney Janega suggested retaining some form of approval of a landscaping
plan, saying that it is a normal part of the process on this type of
application for the Council to exercise some kind of control over the final
plan.
Trustee Lien then amended his motion to retain the first sentence of Section 4
(E) and to delete everything thereafter, through 4(F). Trustee Derning seconded
the motion.
In explaining his motion, Trustee Lien said that although he had taken the
comments of the neighbors very seriously, there was not unanimity among the
neighbors in asking for screening and noted that, in fact, the initial
objections had been aimed at the project as a whole. He pointed out that over
the forty years that the Church building has been where it is, it appears that
none of the neighbors has objected to the view strongly enough to install their
own screening, of either the parking lot or the existing building, and that in
reality, only two of the property owners will have their view changed
significantly. He also noted that landscaping only provides a temporary
solution, not a permanent one, as plantings are not always successful, require
care and, frequently, need to be replaced. Trustee Lien also voiced serious
concern about any landscaping plan that would result in the removal of parking
spaces, which, in his opinion, would only serve to aggravate an already busy
intersection’s problems. Finally, he stated his belief that it was not within
the prerogative of the Council to force a substantial expenditure on the Church
when overwhelming public interest could not be shown.
Trustee Powell moved to further amend Trustee Lien’s motion to include 4(E)2(b)
which would provide for the landscaping to be placed “on the north side of the
concrete curb retaining wall, opposite the new addition, adjacent to or at the
rear of the properties located at 1228 and 1234 Ash.”
Trustee Duhl clarified that putting any landscaping on the Church’s side of the
retaining wall curb would, in fact, require the removal of some parking spaces.
He voiced concern about imposing by ordinance any unnecessary restrictions and
suggested that the parties work out the solution without specific direction
from the Council.
Trustee Aquilino suggested leaving all of the options open in order to give the
parties flexibility.
Trustee Duhl objected to including any option about which there was a consensus
that it is undesirable. He proposed a more general revision of the text to
simply require the provision of a landscaping screen to be agreed upon by the
parties.
Trustee Lien said that he did not believe that the Council had the knowledge to
work out what the design should be. The parties needed to work something out
and bring it to the Design Review Board before Council considered it.
President Holland said that she did not think it would be appropriate to
exclude the Village Forester from the process. She then called for a vote on
Trustee Lien’s motion.
Trustee Powell withdrew his motion to amend the motion on the floor.
By roll call vote, the motion failed. Ayes: Trustees Lien and Derning. Nays:
Trustees Duhl, Aquilino, Powell, and Hilton. Absent: None.
Trustee Duhl moved to delete subparagraphs a through d of Section 4(E)2, to
amend the language in Section 4(E)2 to , “It shall provide a buffer between the
church building and the single-family residential property located at 1228 Ash
and 1234 Ash,” and to add provisions requiring the landscape plan to be
mutually agreeable, to require a good faith effort to reach an agreement, to
allow the Church to submit a plan if it is unable to reach an agreement with
the two property owners, and to require the Church to be bound by the Council’s
final determination on the landscape plan. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl,
Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Holland then called for a motion to adopt the other proposed
amendments to the draft ordinance. The motion was made by Trustee Hilton,
seconded by Trustee Derning. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Finally, Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt Ordinance
No. M-1-2000, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien, and Duhl. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-2-2000 – Zoning Variation: 1165 Hamptondale - Adoption. Trustee
Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance permitting
the structural alteration of an existing roof to accommodate a skylight
resulting in a reduced rear yard setback. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, and Powell.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-3-2000 – Zoning Variation: 301 Sheridan Rd. – Adoption. Trustee
Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance permitting
the construction of a front entrance resulting in a reduced south setback. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Hilton,
Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1161 and 1162. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1161 in the total amount of $163,960.82 and Warrant List No. 1162 in the
total amount of $453,538.78. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Hilton,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Preauthorization for Purchase of Automobile for Water & Electric Department.
Manager Williams explained that because of the cut off date for participating
in the state bid process, which affords the Village an advantageous price on
cars, staff was requesting permission to commit early to an order for a car
that is in the FY 2000-2001 Budget.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to authorize the purchase as
requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Hilton, Lien and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Preauthorization for Purchase of Automobiles for Public Works Department. Mr.
Williams explained that, for the same reasons cited above, the Public Works
Department was requesting permission to commit to the purchase of two
automobiles included in its FY 2000-2001 Budget.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to authorize the purchase as
requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Hilton, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports
Village President. President Holland commented on the impact internet sales are
having on local businesses and indicated that she was continuing to pursue the
institution of an internet use tax with the Northwest Municipal Conference. 
Village Manager. Manager Williams informed the Trustees that staff and the
architect were continuing to review plans and cost estimates for updating the
Village Yards and that a report will not be submitted to the Council until
April. Therefore, the meeting to discuss the project, originally scheduled for
February 28th, was postponed to a later date.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland announced that Ken Behles has agreed to serve as the Design
Review Board’s representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee Aquilino,
seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve this appointment. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
8:47 p.m.
         _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
March 7, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved March 21, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
7, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, and David Hilton. Absent:
Trustees John Lien and John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, Trustees-elect Stephen Presser and Edmund Woodbury, and about five
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that Item 9(e) on the Agenda would be moved up to 9
(a) in order to accommodate neighbors who might be attending. The Council
approved the amended Agenda.
IV. Quorum.
March 14, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Duhl indicated that he might not be able
to attend. All of the other Trustees present indicated that they expected be
able to attend.
March 21, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
February 15, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee
Hilton, moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-4-2000 – Disposal of Transformers – Introduction. Mr. Williams
explained that this was a typical Village disposal ordinance that would
formally authorize the disposal of surplus distribution transformers that are
no longer needed by the Water & Electric Department. It also gives an umbrella
authorization to dispose of any other distribution transformers that are
retired during the next current fiscal year. He noted that in order to minimize
the Village’s costs of disposing of the equipment, while getting a fair return
on the items, the recommended methods of disposal are (1) send all transformers
containing no PCB’s to transformer vendors for bids, to be sold to the highest
bidder; and (2) send all PCB contaminated or PCB transformers to bid for
disposal and dispose of remaining material via means that produce no remains
that need to be landfilled.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-5-2000 – Zoning Variation: 445 Cedar St. – Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed a request for a variation to permit the location of an air
conditioning unit and the alteration of an existing nonconforming roof
resulting in a decreased rear yard. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals
had voted 4-0 to recommend that the Council grant the variances as requested.
Trustee Hilton inquired as to whether the proposed placement of the air
conditioning unit was more desirable than the old one. Ms. McCammack answered
affirmatively.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-6-2000 – Zoning Variation: 900 Greenwood Ave. – Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed a request for variations to permit the construction of a
second floor bay window which would result in increased roofed lot coverage and
a decreased rear yard. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-
0 to recommend that the Council grant the variances as requested.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-5-2000 – Fiscal Year 2000-01 Budget – Public Hearing and
Adoption.
President Holland opened the Public Hearing on the Fiscal Year 2000-2001 Budget
at 7:40 p.m. Manager Williams discussed the derivation of the sales forecast
for the Electric Department, noting that the figure contained in the Budget is
conservative and could be raised somewhat. He also said that if the Council
were to agree, it would be possible to include two underground conversion
projects in the Budget and explained how the financing would be accomplished.
The two projects recommended by staff are (1) Tower Court, and (2) Hibbard
Road, from Elm Street south to Willow Road.
President Holland called for questions or comments from the floor. There being
none, she closed the Public Hearing, at 7:50 p.m.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to include the two
projects in the Budget. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Hilton, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien
and Powell.
Trustee Aquilino remarked that undergrounding the wires on Tower Court will be
a big improvement to the neighborhood.
President Holland thanked Mr. Williams and his staff for their excellent work
on the Budget.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the Fiscal Year
2000-2001 Budget, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Lien and Powell.
Utility Rates Resolutions. Manager Williams explained that the three
resolutions establish rates for water, electric, and sewer service, effective
as of the beginning of the new fiscal year, April 1, 2000. In addition to
implementing the changes recommended in the course of budget discussions, the
resolutions also consolidate all rates applicable to each particular utility
service, regardless of whether a change is proposed or not. He noted that the
only substantive changes were the 2% water rate increase and the 5% electric
rate decrease already approved by the Council.
Resolution No. R-6-2000 – Water Rate Resolution – Introduction. Trustee Duhl,
seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the resolution establishing
rates for water service. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-7-2000 – Electric Rate Resolution – Introduction. Trustee
Hilton, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to introduce the resolution
establishing rates for electric service. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Resolution No. R-8-2000 – Sewer Rate Resolution – Introduction. Trustee
Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to introduce the resolution
establishing rates for sewer service. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Zoning Variation: 800 Green Bay Road – Policy Direction. Ms. McCammack reviewed
a request for variations from the Zoning Ordinance to permit the conversion and
enlargement of an existing single family residence into a two-unit, multifamily
dwelling. She noted that the existing single family residence in the B-
1 Multifamily Residential Zoning District is a nonconforming land use but
pointed out that single family residences do exist within its boundaries. She
mentioned that a previous application for a similar variance had been withdrawn
based upon a unanimous recommendation by the ZBA that the Council deny the
requested variances.
Ms. McCammack discussed the proposed changes that would not comply with Code
requirements and pointed out that the Village Code provides that any
nonconforming use, such as the existing single family residence, cannot be
changed to another use, except to a permitted use, and in such case, the change
to a conforming use must also comply with all zoning requirements and Village
ordinances.
She said that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-0 to recommend that the
Council deny both requested variances.
President Holland inquired whether capping the chimney in order to facilitate
the construction of a balcony, as proposed, would present a fire hazard. Ms.
McCammack responded that the chimney would not be functional.
In response to a question from Manager Williams, Ms. McCammack confirmed that
the distance of the proposed balcony overlooking the property next door would
be only two feet from the property line.
The applicant, Suzette Ng, read a statement in support of her petition and
presented an artist’s rendering of the proposed new construction. Her
architect, Chun Chan, read a statement from Ms. Ng’s attorney stating that the
Village’s setback requirements are so stringent as to make it impossible to
economically expand and improve the property.
Robert Dini, the owner of 790 Green Bay Rd., urged the Council to affirm the
finding of the Zoning Board of Appeals. Mr. Dini argued that the picture
supplied by Ms. Ng doesn’t relate to the east elevation shown in the agenda
materials, that the north side yard setback does not take into account the
chimney and is, in reality, less than one foot from the property line. He added
that the applicant is merely planning to use the existing north wall by
erecting a completely new building around it.
Connie Webster, 808 Green Bay Rd., expressed strong opposition to the proposed
plans. Ms. Webster contended that the proposed balcony would look directly into
her yard and intrude on trees planted along the property line for privacy. She
noted that neither the lot size nor the zoning has changed since the property
was purchased by the present owner and that the proposed structure would be out
of character with the rest of the homes in the neighborhood. She argued that
there is no reason to permit the owner to enhance her property, and therefore,
her potential financial gain upon sale, at the expense of the neighbors.
Trustee Hilton stated that he was in favor of upholding the Zoning Board’s
decision because the proposed project encroaches too close to the neighbors.
After the Trustees asked questions of the presenters, President Holland
explained to Ms. Ng that because her variations had been denied by the Zoning
Board of Appeals, it would take the positive vote of four Trustees to instruct
the Village attorney to proceed with preparing an ordinance.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to approve the
recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals and affirm their findings. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Hilton,
Acquilino and Duhl. Nayes: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and Powell.
Warrant Lists Nos. 1163 and 1164. Trustee Aquilino moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1163 in the total amount of $415,618.51 and Warrant List No. 1164 in
the total amount of $272,547.36. Trustee Hilton seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Duhl,
Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and Powell.
Trustee Duhl inquired about the Nextel system and whether or not the Village
was taking advantage of packages where available. Mr. Williams and Mr. McKee
answered affirmatively.
Final Floodplain Map Revisions. Mr. Saunders provided background information
and discussed the status of the revision of FEMA’s floodplain maps. He
explained that the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) has been working
on a countywide revision of the 100-year floodplain maps since 1997 and plans
to announce its final determination on these maps sometime in March or April of
2000, which means that all of the appeals, including the Village’s, have been
resolved and the new maps will become effective.
He noted that the Village had successfully appealed FEMA’s initial map
proposal, and that appeal had resulted in removal of the floodway from the
Village. It also resulted in a scaling back of the number of additional homes
in the floodplain from the number in FEMA’s initial re-mapping.
Mr. Saunders explained that FEMA’s rules require that the Village regulate the
floodplain based upon “the best available information,” which, in FEMA’s
opinion, are the new maps. The revised maps contain a new, somewhat raised Base
Flood Elevation (BFE) which results in the floodplain boundaries expanding such
that a number of additional homes are shown in the floodplain. These expanded
floodplain boundaries are significant because the FEMA requires certain
construction standards and flood insurance coverage for structures located
within the floodplain.
He noted that although the Village is not technically required to regulate with
the new maps until they are officially adopted by ordinance, FEMA suggests and
“encourages” that such regulation begin immediately. Therefore, in order to
provide a smooth regulatory transition for homeowners, architects, and
builders, he recommended that the Village begin regulating under the new map on
May 1, 2000. All applications for building permits received after that date
will be regulated based upon the new map.
President Holland inquired whether FEMA had taken in account all of the money
the Village has spent to alleviate flooding problems. Mr. Saunders replied that
because the floodplain is defined relevant to river elevations, our work within
the Village has little impact, as the Skokie River water shed takes in the
upstream areas of western Highland Park and Lake Forest where there is more
impervious surface.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to brief the Council on his investigations
into the possibility of raising the levee. Mr. Saunders explained that the east
side of the Skokie River waterway system is protected by a levee, which
pursuant to federal requirements must have a certain amount of freeboard in
order to reduce the calculated flood risk. The Village has been looking into
the possibility of raising the levee system by the addition of fill in order to
meet FEMA’s requirements and, thereby, reduce the area of floodplain in the
Village. One of the problems which must be overcome is that the Village would
not be permitted to do anything that would have a negative effect on
neighboring communities. He emphasized that this is a long term project under
study.
Bid No. 00-003 – Tree Planting Program. Mr. Saunders explained that this bid is
for the purchase and planting of approximately 190 parkway trees and 24 private
trees within the Village for FY 2000. He noted that the private, front yard
tree planting is a part of the pilot program in which participating residents
will be responsible for their own total cost. As a result of the bidding
process, staff recommends that St. Aubin Nursery and Arthur Weiler Nursery be
awarded the bids in the amounts of $50,636 and $450, respectively.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Hilton, moved to award the tree planting
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Hilton and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien
and Powell.
President Holland complimented the Forester, Jim Stier, for seeing to it that
the newly planted trees on the Village Green are protected from mower damage.
Bid No. 00-004 – Sidewalk & Curb Replacement Program. Mr. Saunders explained
that this bid is for the furnishing of all labor, materials and equipment to
construct concrete sidewalks, curbing, handicapped ramps and other related work
for FY 2000. He recommended award of the bid to Schroeder & Schroeder, Inc., in
the total amount of $85,134.50.
Trustee Hilton, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to award the sidewalk and
curb replacement program as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Hilton, Duhl, Derning and Aquilino. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Lien and Powell.
X. Reports
Village President. President Holland announced that the Village had received a
Century Award from the American Public Power Association for 100 years of
service to customers and community in providing water and electricity. She
commented on the wisdom of the Village’s founding fathers, who had the
foresight to establish the Village’s own water and electric service.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that the final Y2K event, February
29, 2000, had passed without incident. The Village experienced no problems
throughout the computer date changeover.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland suggested the appointment of Trustee Derning to the
Canvassing Board for the upcoming March 21st election. Trustee Duhl moved to
approve the appointment. He was seconded by Trustee Hilton. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, and Hilton. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and
Powell. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:55 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, and Hilton. Absent: Trustees Lien
and Powell. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Hilton moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Duhl seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:56 p.m.
         _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
March 14, 2000
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved March 21, 2000)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
14, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, John Lien, and John
Powell. Absent: Trustee David Hilton. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Police
Chief William Gallagher, Trustees-elect Hugh Brower and Steven Presser, and
nine persons in the audience.
II. WYO Funding Request.
John Thomas, a member of the Winnetka Youth Organization Board, requested that
the Council reconsider its policy about providing direct funding for the WYO.
Historically, the Village has not provided any funds toward WYO’s operation.
Mr. Thomas cited the results of the Caucus 2000 platform question regarding the
issue and pointed out that Glencoe and Wilmette each provide some funding for
their youth centers. He noted that a similar request had been made of the Park
District and that they had agreed to re-examine their similar, previous refusal
to provide direct aid to WYO. In support of his request, he distributed to the
Trustees copies of the New Trier Township 2000-2001 Agency/Program Evaluation
and Funding Request prepared by WYO’s Executive Director and Program Director.
John Ishu, Program Director, highlighted some of the information, including the
fact that usage has already exceeded that of the last fiscal year. In an effort
to show the quantitative impact of the organization, he pointed to statistics
about the ongoing involvement in volunteer service of program participants.
Trustee Powell asked Mr. Ishu to comment on the format of the program. Mr. Ishu
replied that the WYO is a drop in center, which anyone can attend, with an
average attendance of between 4-50 youths. It is a recreational based setting
and provides both volunteer and recreational opportunities, as well as study
nights.
Mr. Brower inquired about the organization’s use of the Community House.
Trustee Duhl asked about the size of any core group of regular users, and Mr.
Presser asked whether they were primarily Winnetka residents. Mr. Ishu replied
that the core group consisted of about 67 individuals who were mostly Winnetka
residents.
Mr. Duhl also asked about the possibility of combining the services of
neighboring youth organizations. Mr. Ishu explained that most of the
participants are more comfortable in their own communities, and reiterated that
some other communities provide direct funding and or space.
Two program participants, Logan and Helena, spoke in support of the
organization. Logan said that the WYO provided a comfortable place and a
supportive environment in which to relax among his peers outside of the school.
Helena said that WYO offers a safe, friendly place to go and opportunities to
be involved with peers in positive activities in a smaller milieu than the high
school.
Amy Trogden, a parent and new WYO Board member, also spoke in support of the
funding request, saying that she found it distressing that other communities
are willing to fund similar programs while Winnetka is not.
After a further exchange of questions and answers, President Holland thanked
all of the participants for their presentations and indicated that although
granting the request would be a departure from existing policy for the Council,
the issue would be placed on an agenda for further consideration after the
seating of the new Trustees in April.
III. Audit Contract.
Mr. McKee explained that the current contract with Miller Cooper and Company
expires after the completion of the March 31, 2000 audit. He noted that there
is nothing to suggest that the Village needs to go out for bids, but that it is
up to the Council to decide whether to negotiate an extension or go out for
bids.
Trustee Lien said that in the absence of reasons to the contrary, he would be
in favor of staying with the present auditors.
Trustee Duhl concurred, citing the importance of a strong relationship between
a client and a service provider.
Trustees Derning, Powell, and Aquilino also agreed. Therefore, there was a
consensus to instruct Mr. McKee and Mr. Williams to negotiate an extension to
the contract with Miller Cooper and Company.
IV. Historical Society Annual Report.
Joan Evanich, President of the Historical Society, updated the Council on
changes since the Annual Report and reported on the progress of their move to
Champion House and their search for new, permanent space. She said that the
Society is still trying to service the public but, for the time being, has
stopped taking research requests.
On behalf of the Trustees, President Holland wished the Society continued
success and good luck in their search for a new home.
V. Report on Police Department Community Survey.
Chief Gallagher provided a brief history of the survey which began as a part of
the accreditation process. He remarked that the survey process, in which
approximately 500 surveys are randomly mailed to residents, has evolved over
the years and is very labor intensive, but provides a valuable tool for
focusing in on issues of particular significance, planning, and directing the
Department. The survey is now conducted on a bi-annual basis and the Chief
commented that the results are similar to those received to Caucus survey
questions, which, in his opinion adds a significant element of credibility.
Chief Gallagher and Sergeant Glenn Florkow reviewed some of the positive and
negative comments reported in the survey.
Mr. Brower noted that speeding and stop signs violations are recurrent themes.
Chief Gallagher responded that direct enforcement operations are based upon
experience and complaints, and that the perception of speeding is not always
the reality.
After the officers responded to further questions from the Trustees, President
Holland thanked them for their presentation and remarked that the survey
provides an excellent vehicle for individuals to make comments.
VI. Needed Repairs.
Manager Williams reported to the Council that the end of Spruce Street was
going to require extensive storm sewer repairs during the next year due to
tremendous erosion. He presented a videotape prepared by Village Engineer
Steven Saunders showing the problems and discussing alternative plans for
repair. Mr. Williams said that staff would be bringing a proposal regarding the
repairs and funding to the Council. He noted that the Village would be doing a
complete study of all of the street ends in terms of erosion and similar
issues.
VII. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Aquilino, Derning, and Duhl. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Hilton. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:
15 p.m.
The meeting adjourned at 10:08 p.m.
March 21, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved April 4, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
21, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Don Derning, Michael Duhl, John Lien, John Powell
and one vacancy. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Trustees-elect Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury, and two persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag. Following
the Pledge, President Holland asked the attendees to remain standing for a
moment of silence in memory of Trustee David Hilton who passed away last week.
Saying he would be sorely missed, she commended Mr. Hilton for his dedicated
service on the Council and his devotion to the community.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
April 4, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees and Trustees-elect present,
except Trustee Aquilino, indicated that they expected be able to attend.
April 11, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees and Trustees-elect present
indicated that they would be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
March 7, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
March 14, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-4-2000 – Disposal of Transformers – Adoption. Trustee Derning,
seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Lien,
and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-5-2000 – Zoning Variation: 445 Cedar St. – Adoption. Trustee
Duhl, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance permitting a
zoning variation to allow for the location of an air conditioning unit and the
alteration of an existing nonconforming roof resulting in a decreased rear
yard. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl,
Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-6-2000 – Zoning Variation: 900 Greenwood Ave. – Adoption.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting a zoning variation to allow for the construction of a second floor
bay window which would result in increased roofed lot coverage and a decreased
rear yard. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Lien, Duhl, and Derning. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Rate and Fee Resolutions. Attorney Janega explained that the proposed
resolutions implement changes recommended in the course of budget discussions
and consolidate all rates applicable to each particular utility service,
regardless of whether a change is proposed or not. She said that all rates will
be effective as of the beginning of the new fiscal year, April 1, 2000.
Resolution No. R-6-2000 – Water Rate Resolution – Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed the proposed amendments to the draft resolution, which do not
substantively alter its content. Most of the suggested changes are for
clarification and facilitate efficient bookkeeping.
Manager Williams noted that the resolution still implements Council’s budget
decision to increase water rates by 2%.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to amend the draft
resolution as suggested. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the resolution, as
amended, establishing rates for water service. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-7-2000 – Electric Rate Resolution – Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed proposed amendments to the draft resolution, noting that there had
been no change to the proposed 5% reduction in fees. She said that the other
changes were similar to those made in the preceding resolution relating to
housekeeping measures.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the resolution as
suggested. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing rates for electric service. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Lien. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-8-2000 – Sewer Rate Resolution – Adoption. Ms. Janega
explained that this resolution restates, without change, the current rates for
sewer services. As in the two preceding resolutions, proposed amendments to the
draft document relating to returned checks, payment period and effective date
provisions have been included.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to amend the resolution as
suggested. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing rates for sewer service. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-9-2000 – Refuse Service Rate Resolution – Adoption. Noting
that this ordinance requires no introduction, Attorney Janega explained that
this resolution consolidates and reorganizes all of the rates and fees
pertaining to refuse services. It also contains the same provisions regarding
returned checks, payment period, and effective date as the other rate
resolutions.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing rates for refuse service. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Lien, and Duhl. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
President Holland mentioned that the NWMC has been fighting for additional
funds to be put back into the state budget to cover more hazardous waste pickup
days throughout the year.
Resolution No. R-10-2000 – General Permit and License Fees – Adoption. Ms.
Janega explained that this reorganized and consolidated fee schedule contains
cross-references to the new Village Code and adds charges currently imposed for
miscellaneous service fees. She noted that the only substantive changes relate
to parking passes, where the daily fees have been increased to $4.00 and a
provision has been added stating that daily passes will not be refunded
beginning July 1, 2000. Manager Williams pointed out that this represented a
substantial increase and would be a departure from current policy change. He
explained staff’s reasons for recommending the specific change in amount and
said that it represents an attempt to control nonresidents using daily parking
passes in Winnetka. In response to a question from Trustee Duhl, he said that
this would make Winnetka’s the most expensive daily pass of the comparable,
north shore communities.
Trustee Duhl expressed support for the proposed increase.
President Holland pointed out that the Village does not want to discourage
parking to the point that ridership on Metra is diminished to a level where
Metra might choose to close down either the Indian Hill or Hubbard Woods
stations.
Trustee Powell thanked Ms. Janega for all of her efforts to consolidate the
fees.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing the general permit and license fees. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-11-2000 – Carroll Consolidation – 533 Hawthorne – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that this resolution would consolidate an existing
irregular shaped zoning lot into a single lot of record as required by the
Building Code for the construction of a new residence. In response to a
question from President Holland, Ms. Janega indicated that this was merely a
housekeeping matter and that there would be no changes to the property’s outer
boundaries.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien,
Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-12-2000 – Ash Consolidation – 563 Ash Street – Adoption. Ms.
Janega explained that this request to consolidate was similar to the preceding
one, and is, basically, a housekeeping action required by the Building Code to
permit the construction of a new residence. It does not change the outer limits
of the lots, but eliminates the lengthy metes and bounds description
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the consolidation. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Derning,
Aquilino, Powell, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-13-2000 – Establishing Fees for Fire and Emergency Paramedical
and Rescue Services to Unincorporated New Trier Township. Manager Williams
reviewed the annual resolution establishing the charges to Kenilworth and
Unincorporated New Trier Township for emergency services. He said that the fees
are calculated based on the population of the area served, the equalized
assessed valuation, and the number of calls received from each area. The
proposed charge for the upcoming fiscal year will be $583.91 per household, up
$12.38 from last year.
Trustee Aquilino inquired how the charge was billed. Manager Williams replied
that the fee is tied to the water bill and noted that the Finance Department
had been able to achieve 100% collection.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Derning,
Aquilino, Powell, Lien, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Unfinished Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1165 and 1166. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1165 in the total amount of $181,607.39 and Warrant List No. 1166 in the
total amount of $465,834.15. Trustee Duhl seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Duhl,
Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Bid No. 00-001 – Tree Removal, Tree Trimming and Disposal. Manager Williams
reviewed the bid for the annual trimming, removal and disposal of parkway trees
for FY2000. The bid is divided into three sections: tree and stump removals,
parkway tree trimming, and emergency work. He distributed some supplemental
materials to support staff’s recommendation to award the bid to R. W.
Hendricksen for the first two portions at $30,620 and $18,425, respectively,
and Bartlett Tree Service for the last section at an hourly rate of $41.50 per
hour.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Bid No. 00-005 – Chlorine Gas Feed Equipment. Manager Williams explained that
this bid was for the purchase and delivery of equipment which is used to
disinfect the Village’s water supply. Staff recommends award of the bid to
Smith Ecological Systems, Inc., in the total amount of $27,895.00.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Lien, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Bid No. 00-006 – Fan Discharge Silencer. Manager Williams explained that this
bid was to furnish, deliver and install a silencer at the Electric Plant to
mitigate noise experienced by neighbors. Staff recommends award of the bid to
Sound Technologies, Inc., in the total amount of $60,154.00.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Lien, Duhl, Derning, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Bid No. 00-007 – Overhead Transformers. Manager Williams reviewed the bid for
the purchase and delivery of various sizes of single-phase conventional
overhead transformers on an “as needed” basis for FY2000, with an option to
extend for FY2001. He explained that the bids were evaluated on the lifecycle
cost of the transformers and noted staff’s recommendation to award the bid to
Resco in the amount of $79,962.00.
Trustee Duhl, seconded by Trustee Derning, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Duhl, Derning, Aquilino, Powell, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Chief Local Officials Agreement. President Holland introduced Michael Spiers,
Assistant Director of the Workforce Development Council, who provided an
overview of the Council’s work and explained that due to the new Workforce
Investment Act, the Northwest Municipal Conference is required by the State of
Illinois to have a new Chief Local Officials Agreement as part of the
restructuring of the Workforce Development Council. In response to a question
from Manager Williams regarding liability exposure to the Village, Mr. Spears
said that the organization had never had any costs disallowed by either state
or federal authorities and reviewed the organization’s liability policies.
Trustee Derning, seconded by Trustee Duhl, moved to authorize President Holland
to sign the CEO Agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Lien, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
X. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Aquilino reported on the recent meeting of the
Beautification Committee. She indicated that the Committee was progressing with
its development of a landscaping plan for the Indian Hill parking lot.
Village Manager. Manager Williams announced a tour of the Village Yards
Facilities tentatively scheduled for April 29th. Following the tour, a bus will
take attendees to a recently constructed Public Works facility in another
community. He said that the architect’s study of our Village Yards should be
completed by mid-April and that staff’s recommendation regarding the various
options should be submitted to the Council in time for the May 9th study
session.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Derning moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Derning, Aquilino, Powell, Lien, and Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 8:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Lien, Powell and one vacancy.
Absent: None.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Duhl moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the motion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 9:46
p.m.
