                  Deputy Clerk
April 11, 2000
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved April 18, 2000)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
11, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Fire Chief Ron Colpaert, Director
of Community Development Cindy McCammack, and no persons in the audience.
II. New Rescue/Medical/Engine Purchase.
Chief Colpaert reviewed the information regarding the purchase of a new rescue/
medical/engine from Pierce Manufacturing, Inc. through the Northwest Municipal
Conference purchasing consortium. He noted that by going through the
consortium, the Village would save approximately $8,000. Manager Williams said
that this item was being raised with the Trustees before presenting a formal
request for approval because it is non-bid item.
III. Miscellaneous Zoning Issues.
Manager Williams explained that the following four issues have arisen as a
result of administration of the Zoning Ordinance. He asked Ms. Janega and Ms.
McCammack to walk the Council through the policy and technicalities involved in
each.
a)   Commercial Half-story Definition.
Ms. Janega explained that the definition of half-story was originally designed
to apply only in residential zoning districts, where the height limitations
allowed the upper floor to be a half-story. The current version of the
definition of half-story is the result of a series of amendments that have
refined the definition to reflect staff interpretations and to eliminate, to
the extent possible, loopholes that had previously allowed oversized beams and
trusses to increase the real height and exterior bulk of single-family
residences while keeping the calculated height within the limits of the Zoning
Ordinance.
She explained that defining the half-story in relation only to the area of the
part of the lower floor directly below it, also helps to assure that every
individual section of a building meets the 2-1/2 story limit.
She noted, however, that setbacks in commercial properties tend to be smaller
than in residential properties, and that developers typically attempt to
maximize the amount of space used, which frequently results in more monolithic
structures. Consequently, when the current half-story definition is applied to
commercial structures, an upper floor that is set well back from the edge of
the building might be considered a full story, even it its actual area is less
than half of the area of the full story below it. This was not an issue before
commercial building heights were reduced. However, staff’s recent experiences
in applying the residentially oriented definition of half-story to proposed
commercial developments indicates that the current definition poses design
problems for developers who attempt to design a conforming building that is
also architecturally compatible with its surroundings.
Therefore, when applied in the commercial zoning districts, the existing half-
story definition has had the unintended effect of creating more massive roof
forms, so that buildings still loom over the adjoining residential
neighborhood, which is inconsistent with the reduction in size that was
intended when the Council amended the Zoning Ordinance to reduce the maximum
building height in the commercial zoning districts to 2-1/2 stories.
Ms. McCammack cited examples and referred to illustrations to explain the
problems experienced by staff with designs submitted by developers. She
recommended that a modification of the definition of "half story" be made to
address commercial buildings, by creating one definition for residential
properties and one for commercial properties. She also suggested adding
standards that would make the upper half-story less visible from sidewalks
adjoining commercial buildings and keep open balconies or decks at that level
from looming over adjoining residential properties.
Manager Williams pointed out that both major commercial projects undertaken in
the Village since the adoption of the new regulations had to seek variations in
order to get approval for a building that was aesthetically pleasing.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that any changes to the regulations be written so as
to permit a developer to comply by selecting the most aesthetically pleasing of
the two definitions.
Trustee Powell supported the concept of permitting some flexibility, as did the
other Trustees.
b)   Residential Articulation Clarification.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the history of the Village's modifications to the gross
floor area requirements and other bulk regulations in the single-family
residential zoning districts. These modifications resulted in a provision
requiring articulation for side building walls in the R-5 and R-4 Zoning
Districts (formerly the One-Sixth and One-Fourth Acre Districts). The purpose
of the building line articulation requirement was to prevent a “Queen Mary”
effect, or a lengthy expanse of a 2 or 2.5 story high wall located only six
feet from the side lot line.
She noted that, while the articulation requirements have worked well on new
construction, the Community Development staff has found the provision difficult
to interpret and administer with regard to older buildings, which often have
non-conforming side yards.
The Trustees discussed the examples cited and the potential solutions
identified by staff. There was some consensus among the Trustees to exclude
roofed, unenclosed front porches from the computation of building length, as
well as general agreement that both old and new homes should be required to
incorporate some kind of articulation, although there could be different
standards for old and new homes, as was done with the gross floor area
amendments.
c)   One Year Waiting Period Between Applications for Similar Zoning Variations
if the First is Denied.
Ms. Janega explained that a common provision in most zoning ordinances is to
prohibit applying for a variance for a year after a request for the same
variance has been denied. Although the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance has such a
provision to bar repeated requests for special uses, it does not have a
provision limiting reapplying for variations.
Trustee Powell inquired whether such a provision would prevent a variation from
returning to the Zoning Board of Appeals. Ms. Janega answered in the
affirmative.
There was general support for the concept among the Trustees. Trustee Woodbury,
however, pointed out that sometimes the Council takes no formal action and
merely indicates to the applicant that there is insufficient support to pass
the requested variation. Ms. Janega agreed, saying that this would mean that
the Council would be required to take a formal vote on all variations brought
before it, including negative recommendations from the Zoning Board of Appeals.
d)   Elimination of Need to Obtain Zoning Variation for "Sky Light" Type
Situations.
Ms. McCammack suggested an amendment to the Zoning Ordinance to permit certain
types of de minimus structural activity, i.e., small modifications to existing
legally nonconforming walls and/or roofs located within required side yards,
rear yards, and front setbacks, by permit, without triggering the full
variation process.
The Trustees supported the concept and discussed exactly what types of work
could be handled this way. They concluded that they would support allowing the
replacement of existing elements on a one-to-one basis, such as windows and
doors on the same plane within an existing wall, and allowing skylights within
an existing roof.
The meeting adjourned at 9:27 p.m.
April 18, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved May 2, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
18, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works, Steve Saunders and 8
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
Manager Williams asked that the Council remove item (d) under New Business, the
jurisdictional transfer of Tower Road, as staff has revisions to this item. The
Council approved the Agenda as amended.
IV. Quorum.
May 2, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees indicated that they expected
to be able to attend.
May 9, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they would be
able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
April 4, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved
to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
April 11, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Brower,
moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Winnetka Caucus Presentation.
President Holland asked Becky Hurley, a member of the 2000 Caucus Platform
Committee, to introduce herself and present the Caucus’ Platform.
Ms. Hurley said that this year the Caucus had been asked to follow up on
questions from the previous year, as well as to survey new items. She reported
that, due to an effort to improve communications, responses to the Caucus
questionnaire increased from 400 to 1100, so they consider this survey very
useful. Prior to the survey, the Caucus members met with the Village Council
members and Chamber of Commerce and reviewed the Plan Commission survey of a
few years ago.
Ms. Hurley then reported on specific issues that the Caucus questionnaire
addressed year.
Regarding the attractiveness and convenience of the East and West Elm shopping
areas, vacant storefronts and the Indian Hill commercial district, the Caucus
recommends a study of the needs of each area and how to bring in diverse
retailers that service the needs of the town. Ms. Hurley gave the closing of
the hardware store as an example of services that the Village is lacking.
The Caucus advocates searching for parking solutions, particularly in Hubbard
Woods.
The responses to the questionnaire also seemed to indicate a renewed interest
in building a parking deck behind the Community House.
The questionnaire asked if there would be support to review the current liquor
policy if the rules might be construed as disincentives and put Winnetka
business at a disadvantage. Ms. Hurley said responses were interesting in that
58% were in favor of reviewing the liquor law.
The Caucus recommends residential design guidelines and the creation of a
design review board that will consider residential building plans to promote
architectural integrity as well as sensitivity to surroundings. Ms. Hurley
indicated that the responses showed broad support for design restrictions: 80%
support having guidelines; 74% support a design review board; 68% support this
for all residential construction; and 32% support design review for new
construction.
The Caucus recommends that the Village continue to allocate funds to maintain
the current level of Village services. Ms. Hurley said that this was difficult
to evaluate responses on this issue because the services are all varying
degrees of very good. The responses indicated some dissatisfaction with
streets, sidewalks and drainage systems and the recommendation is to expedite
improvements in these areas.
The Caucus recommends the coordination of Village services with neighboring
communities wherever possible with the goal of controlling costs, citing the
library as an example. The results of the survey indicated that people are
generally supportive, as long as service isn’t reduced.
The Caucus also asked for continued effort in burying utility lines, but has
found that at the cost of $10,000 - $15,000 per home, it does not have as high
a level of support.
Ms. Hurley noted there were lengthy written comments in the surveys and that
she would give a copy of them to Manager Williams for the Council's review.
President Holland said that she would be responding once again this year to the
Caucus Platform at the Town meeting in June to tell them how the Village would
implement things, what the Village’s recommendations are, how the Council is
moving along, and what the Council has or has not decided. She thanked Ms.
Hurley for the Platform and her presentation.
VIII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance MC-2-2000 - Amending the Village Code Pertaining to Liquor Licenses:
Packaged Meal Rider for Espresso Pacifico - Introduction. Village Attorney
Katherine Janega said that Expresso Pacifico has applied for a packaged meal
rider liquor license to allow the sale of beer and wine, in its original
package, to accompany full meals packaged for consumption off the premises. She
explained that currently the restaurant has a Class A-1 license, which allows
the service of liquor with full meals for consumption on the premises. She said
the amended ordinance also reduces the number of Class A-1 liquor licenses by
one because of the permanent closing of Jacky's Bistro due to a fire.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the resolution
as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Manager Williams explained that the Village’s liquor regulations are structured
so that the Council must amend the Village Code before a liquor license can be
issued by the Village President, who is ex-officio Liquor Control Commissioner.
A short discussion followed as to how the sale of packaged goods is controlled,
the distinction between Class E licenses and the package meal rider, the
requirements of the store owners to keep detailed records and the quarterly
inspections of liquor license holders that is done by the Police Department.
Ordinance No. M-8-2000 - Zoning Variation at 687 Cherry Street - Introduction.
Ms. Janega reviewed the request for variations from intensity of use of lot and
maximum building size to convert this two-unit structure into a single-family
residence. She stated that this request is similar to a variation requested in
1998 for the same property. She noted that, although the improvements will
slightly increase the gross floor area and roofed lot coverage, the increase is
less than what the Council previously approved and the impermeable surface will
be reduced, making the home more compatible with surrounding homes and bringing
it closer to compliance with the Zoning Ordinance.
Trustee Powell asked for a clarification of the landscaping plan along the west
lot line.
Mr. Todd Stephens, applicant’s attorney, said that the neighbors had requested
that some type of bushes be planted along the driveway on the west side to
replace the existing hedge between the townhouses and the residence. The owners
are currently working this out with the townhouse association, but believe it
will be beneficial to all.
Trustee Aquilino moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Presser.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-9-2000 - Disposal of Personal Property Owned by the Village -
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that four vehicles owned by the
Village are past their useful life and are being replaced with other equipment.
He requested that the Council authorize the sale of the vehicles at the
Northwest Municipal Conference's auction on May 6, 2000. He explained that the
auction sells equipment from many towns, which saves each Village the cost of
conducting auctions individually.
Trustee Lien moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-10-2000 - Disposal of Miscellaneous Surplus Property -
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that this ordinance allows for the
disposal of property, mainly bicycles and cash, recovered by the Police
Department, whose owners could not be found. It also authorizes the Finance
Director to dispose of any computer equipment retired during this fiscal year,
in accordance with past disposal practices.
Trustee Aquilino moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Brower.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
IX. Old Business.
a) New Rescue/Medical/Engine Purchase. Manager Williams requested permission to
purchase a Rescue/Medical/Engine from Pierce Manufacturing of Wisconsin through
the bidding process of the Northwest Municipal Conference. He reported that the
Conference's bidding process appears to be working well and the savings to the
Village is projected to be approximately 4.5% on the purchase of a new engine.
Trustee Powell moved to approve the purchase of one model year 2000 Rescue/Med/
Engine from Pierce Manufacturing of Appleton, Wisconsin for $269,858.00,
seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
b) Renewal of Audit Contract. President Holland explained that, pursuant to the
Council's discussion at its March 14 study session, the staff has received a
proposal from Miller Cooper to extend the existing auditing and accounting
contract.
Trustee Powell moved to accept the proposal of Miller Cooper & Co., Ltd., to
extend the existing auditing and accounting contract and to authorize President
Pro-Tem John Lien to sign the contract rather than President Holland, who is
unable to sign due to a broken hand. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Presser. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser and
Edmund Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
X. New Business
a) Warrant Lists Nos. 1169 and 1170. Trustee Aquilino moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1169 in the total amount of $425,268.57 and Warrant List No. 1170 in
the total amount of $265,260.15. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Manager Williams directed the Council's attention to the Harris Bank item and
notation of “w” which indicates a wire-transfer transaction, and explained that
the notation was added to the warrant lists at the request of Trustee Powell as
a way to show electronic transfers.
b) RFP for Design Engineering - Pine and Eldorado Street Bridges. Steve
Saunders, Director of Public Works, asked that the Council consider awarding
contracts to Ciorba Group for Phase I Engineering for the Pine Street Bridge
and Phase I and Phase II engineering for the Eldorado Street Bridge as
proposed. Mr. Saunders explained the process used to rank the proposals. In
response to questions from the Council members on the selection process, he
stated that the projects would use motor fuel tax funds solely, that the
contract was on a not-to-exceed basis and that based on the experience gained
from the construction of the Elm Street and Oak Street bridges, he was
satisfied with the amount proposed.
Trustee Powell moved to award the contracts to Ciorba Group in an amount not to
exceed $29,527 for Phase I Engineering for the Pine Street Bridge and in an
amount not to exceed $49,754 for Phase I and Phase II Engineering for the
Eldorado Street Bridge, seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c) RFP for Underwater Valve Replacement. Manager Williams explained the need
for the replacement of underwater valves at the Water Plant, which had been
included in the 2000-2001 budget, and recommended the contract be awarded to
Kadinger Marine Service.
Trustee Lien moved to award Kadinger Marine Service the contract for the
underwater valve replacement project at the Water Plant in the amount of
$109,250.00, seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d) Jurisdictional Transfer for Tower Road. Removed from agenda.
e) Assignment of Recycling Contract. Attorney Janega explained the assignment
of the Village's recycling contract to Superior Services, Inc. which is now in
the process of acquiring BFI Waste Systems. She explained that the Agreement
between BFI and the Village requires written consent of the Village before an
assignment can be made.
Trustee Lien stated that he is a partner at Foley and Lardner, the attorneys
for Superior Services, Inc., and said that he will recuse himself from voting
and will not participate in the discussion.
In response to a question concerning the possibility of rates being raised,
Manager Williams clarified that this was an assumption of the original
contract, which set prices and is in force until 2003, and the assignment does
not change the charges as originally set. He also said that SWANCC has dealt
with Superior Services in other communities and reports have been satisfactory.
Trustee Brower moved to authorize Trustee Lien as President Pro Tem in lieu of
President Holland to execute the customer consent portion of the Assignment and
Assumption of Contract between BFI Waste Systems of North America and Superior
Services, Inc. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell and, by roll call
vote, passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser,
Woodbury. Nayes: None. Recusal: Trustee Lien.
f) DK Recycling Monthly Report. Village Manager Williams discussed the report
of DK Recycling Systems for the months of January to March.
President Holland commented that a resident had asked her why compost was
locked up and explained that before it was decided to lock up the compost,
landscapers and others would back trucks up and take most of it so that none
was left for residents. It was decided to limit hours so that there would be
compost available for residents.
Mr. Saunders said that the cost of compost depends upon the amount delivered.
He added that the majority of the compost is generally consumed right away.
g) Capital Improvement Quarterly Reports. Village Manager Williams reviewed the
status of capital improvement projects for the various Village departments,
noting that the undergrounding of utility lines was almost complete along
Sheridan Road. He also said that the Village has been notified by the light
manufacturer for the Elm Street bridge that the lights would not be delivered
until August. He said he and the staff consider this unacceptable and has asked
Mr. Saunders and the Village Attorney to work on it.
Mr. Saunders said they would be looking at legal and technical options to get
satisfaction. He said an alternative would be to go to another vendor who will
follow through, but that it would probably still be August before delivery.
President Holland commented that they were very disappointed and that this was
not a fly-by-night company, noting that it had actually supplied lights to
Winnetka in the 1920's and has a long history of service along the North Shore.
XI. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Aquilino reported that she and Trustee Brower had
attended their first Chamber of Commerce meeting, that the Chamber is in the
process of changing officers and that Jean Wright will be the new President.
She said some of the discussion was about the Elm Street bridge lights, the
holiday lights and the sidewalk sale, which will be held on June 23 and 24. She
asked Manager Williams to include these dates in the next issue of the Winnetka
Report.
Village President. President Holland reported that the Landmark Preservation
Commission has received more nominations for landmark awards this year than
ever before. She said that although it has taken a number of years, this award
system seems to have gained a foothold and has a real prestige attached.
Village Manager. Village Manager Williams informed the Council that the
demolition of the house at 894 Sunset damaged by fire has been tentatively
scheduled for May 8, pending receipt of a demolition permit from Cook County.
Village Manager Williams announced that there will be a Council meeting and
tour of the Village Yards facilities at 1390 Willow Road on April 29 at 8:30
a.m. and that the public is invited to attend. The Council will be reviewing
various options to renovate and expand the Village Yards at its May 9th study
session.
XII. Appointments.
President Holland asked for confirmation of the appointment of Gary Frank as
the Design Review Board's representative to the Ad Hoc Design Guidelines
Committee. Trustee Powell moved to approve the appointment, seconded by Trustee
Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
XIII. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:10 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent:
None.
XIV. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:10 p.m.
