                  Deputy Clerk
May 2, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved May 16, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 2,
2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and about seven persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
May 9, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees, except Trustee Lien, indicated
that they would be able to attend.
May 16, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees indicated that they expected
to be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
April 18, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower noted a minor correction to the
draft, after which Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the minutes as drafted. Her
motion was seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance MC-2-2000 - Amending the Village Code Pertaining to Liquor Licenses:
Packaged Meal Rider for Espresso Pacifico - Adoption. Trustee Presser moved to
adopt the ordinance changing the number of liquor licenses in the Village to
permit Expresso Pacifico to have a packaged meal rider allowing for the sale of
beer and wine, in its original package, to accompany full meals packaged for
consumption off the premises and to reduce the number of Class A-1 licenses to
reflecting the closing of Jacky's Bistro.
The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien
and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-8-2000 - Zoning Variation at 687 Cherry Street - Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting variations from intensity of use of lot and maximum building size to
convert this two-unit structure into a single-family residence. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Lien,
Brower, Presser and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-9-2000 - Disposal of Personal Property Owned by the Village -
Adoption. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the
ordinance authorizing the sale of the vehicles at the Northwest Municipal
Conference's auction on May 6, 2000. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and
Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-10-2000 - Disposal of Miscellaneous Surplus Property -
Adoption. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the
ordinance authorizing the Finance Director to dispose of any computer equipment
retired during this fiscal year, in accordance with past disposal practices. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-11-2000 - Zoning Variation: 976 Sunset Rd. - Introduction. Ms.
Janega explained that this variation would increase an existing nonconforming
front setback in order to permit the complete removal of an existing second
story and replacement with a new, larger one. She noted that the Zoning Board
of Appeals had voted 4-0 to recommend approval by the Council.
The applicant's architect, Gary Beyerl, was present to discuss the project and
respond to questions from the Trustees. He showed elevations of the proposed
building and enumerated the criterion justifying the request for variance. He
noted that the home is currently in the flood plain and that the goal is not to
increase the first floor area which might be susceptible to flooding, but
merely, to add a second floor on top of the existing structure. He pointed out
that the modern character of the home would remain in tact, that the building
would not move any closer to the property lines than the current building, and
that there would be no impact on neighbors' light and air.
President Holland inquired about the sanitary sewer than runs under the
setback. Ms. Janega explained that in accordance with the Zoning Ordinance,
when a property is served by an easement for ingress and egress, the setbacks
have to be determined from that easement, even if it is not constructed.
Trustee Powell asked if the plan was to maintain the roof portion over the
center of the building. Mr. Beyerl indicated that the applicants were hoping to
remove it. Trustee Powell commented that the home was small relative to its
larger neighbors and that the proposal represented a logical addition to the
property.
Trustee Aquilino asked what the highest point of the building would be. Mr.
Beyerl responded that the new structure would be approximately 27 feet at its
peak, with a midpoint of about 22-1/2 feet, or well below the required limit.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-12-2000 - Zoning Variation: 851 Sheridan Rd. - Introduction.
Ms. Janega explained that this variation would permit the structural alteration
and enlargement of window openings that are within an already legally existing
nonconforming wall. She pointed out that this was an example of one of the
anomalies previously discussed by Council in terms of streamlining the variance
process and noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-0 to recommend
approval by the Council.
Charles Cook, the applicant's architect, was available to answer questions from
the Trustees. He said that the windows are being changed because they are old,
not energy efficient, and do not meet current life safety egress requirements.
Trustee Aquilino inquired about the increase in GFA. Mr. Cook explained that
this was the result of an addition to the rear of the house for which no
variance was required.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Holland suggested a move out of order in the Agenda to consider Item
9(d) Winnetka Summer Festival, in order to accommodate the members of the
Chamber of Commerce who were in attendance. The Trustees agreed.
Chamber of Commerce Summer Festival. Manager Williams explained that the
Chamber of Commerce was requesting permission to conduct its annual Winnetka
Days/Sidewalk Sale, under the new name of Winnetka Summer Festival, on Friday,
June 23 and Saturday, June 24. He noted that this year they requested an
extension of their hours on Friday until 9:00 p.m. and permission to block off
Lincoln Avenue from Oak to Elm. With regard to the former, he said that staff
had no objections, but recommended that the streets remain closed until 9:00
p.m. Regarding the latter, he said that it was staff's recommendation that only
the east side Lincoln Ave. be blocked off in order to minimize the impact on
commuter parking.
The Chamber Executive Director, Pam Boemi, explained the Chamber's requests and
indicated that it had no objections to the recommendations made by staff.
President Holland inquired whether this festival would incorporate the
traditional sidewalk sale. Ms. Boemi said that it would.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the Chamber's
requested date and street closures, as amended. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-21-2000 Prevailing Wage Resolution - Adoption. Manager
Williams explained that pursuant to the provisions of the State of Illinois
Prevailing Wage Act, the Village is required annually to investigate and
ascertain the prevailing rate of wages for laborers, mechanics, and other
workers in the Village employed in performing construction of public works.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing the Village’s Prevailing Rate of Wages. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Old Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1171 and 1172. Trustee Brower moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1171 in the total amount of $391,609.23 and Warrant List No. 1172 in the
total amount of $376,605.86. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Bid No. 00-009 - Street Reconstruction. Mr. Williams reviewed the bids received
for street reconstruction, explaining that the work would encompass excavation
of pavement and replacement of curbs and gutters. He also explained that the
bids are all based on unit prices based on the engineers' best estimates of
each component. He pointed out that this year the Budget contains more than the
estimated total cost, so that staff would be considering an extension of the
Ash Street project. He pointed out that this year's lowest and best bid came
from Arrow Road Construction, a contractor with a favorable, proven record in
the Village, in the amount of $761,882.52.
Trustee Woodbury moved to accept the bid of Arrow Road Construction. The motion
was seconded by Trustee Brower. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and
Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Winnetka Women's Club Banners. Mr. Williams explained that beginning in 1995,
the Women's Club has requested and received approval for the placement on the
seven Village light poles on Green Bay Road between Oak and Elm Street of
canvas banners advertising their housewalk in May and croquet tournament in
July. In prior years, the design of the banners, white lettering on a blue
background, has been approved by the Design Review Board, however, this year
the timing of their request did not permit the issue to come before that body
before coming to the Village Council. The Club is requesting permission to
display their banners again this year from May 8 to May 19, and again in July.
Placement of the banners on the Village's light poles requires approval by the
Village Council.
Trustee Aquilino inquired who installs the banners and apparatus. President
Holland responded that the Women's Club hires a private contractor to do the
job.
Pointing out that the organization is a worthwhile one that it working to
revitalize itself, Trustee Brower moved to approve the request to hang banners
in May and July. He was seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Chamber of Commerce Summer Festival. As noted above, this item was discussed
earlier in the meeting.
X. Reports
Village Trustees.
Trustee Presser reported that the next edition of the Winnetka Report, would be
published soon. He noted that there was so much material that not all of it
could be included and suggested that the Council might want to consider
increasing the publication frequency.
Trustee Woodbury reported that the Ad Hoc Design Guidelines Committee had been
activated and was ready to get started.
Trustee Aquilino reported that the Beautification Committee had agreed on its
first project, the Indian Hill parking lot, and that bids should be received
before its next meeting.
Trustee Powell commented on the Village's participation in RED Center and the
outstanding service provided.
Village President. President announced that the Village had once again received
a Certificate of Achievement for excellence in financial reporting from the
Government Finance Officers’ Association for its annual financial report ending
March 31, 1999. She complimented Finance Director Ed McKee and his staff for
their continuing superior performance.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland asked for confirmation of the reappointment of John Berwanger
to the Plan Commission, as well as the Plan Commission's representative to the
Ad Hoc Design Guidelines Committee. Trustee Lien moved to approve the
appointment, seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:27 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:27 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent:
None.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:28 p.m.
