                  Deputy Clerk
May 9, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved June 6, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 9,
2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser, and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustees John Lien and John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and about five persons
in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda.
IV. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-13-2000 - Zoning Variation: 550 Oak St. - Introduction.
President Holland invited the applicant, Mary Allen, to present her proposal.
She explained that she wanted to construct a classic, historical fence
appropriate for the style and age of her house. She added that the design is
one copied from a fence in Southport, Connecticut and has been reviewed for
safety, without objection, by the Village Engineer. She expressed her belief
that it is consistent with other fences in the neighborhood and will enhance
the beauty of the area.
Marjorie Troubough and Nan Greenough spoke in support of the fence. Ms.
Greenough noted that the fence is an appropriate addition in keeping with the
era of the home, and given the absence of any public safety issues should be
permitted despite the fact that it is not strictly in keeping with Village
policy.
Ms. Janega explained that required yard setbacks would normally apply to
fences, but that they are permitted as one of the listed exceptions/
obstructions in required yards if they satisfy right-of way visibility
requirements when located at the intersection of two streets. She noted that
the proposed fence is to be located within the required corner sight triangle,
a 25 ft. setback along each property line that is connected to form a triangle.
If the fence is to be located within this sight triangle, it is required to be
90% open when measured at right angles to the surface of such fence. The
proposed fence, however, is only 69.4% open and, therefore, does not comply
with the requirements. She pointed out that the Zoning Board of Appeals found
no hardship to permit this variance and, consequently, had voted to deny the
application.
Manager Williams commented that the proposed variation makes sense from both a
technical and an aesthetic point of view, noting that it would be possible to
plant a wall of trees such as arbor vitae right on the property line, all the
way to the corner, without a variance, even though that could obstruct sight
lines more than a low, relatively open fence. He suggested that the Council
consider a text amendment to resolve future fence issues, so that, in the
absence of sight obstructions, homeowners can construct fences into the sight
triangle without going through the entire variation process.
Trustees Woodbury and Aquilino concurred. Trustee Woodbury suggested that Ms.
Allen submit the dimensions between the pickets on her fence and that those of
the other examples cited be determined to be used in designing guidelines for a
possible text amendment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
V. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
7:50 p.m.
                  _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
May 9, 2000
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved June 6, 2000)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
9, 2000, at 7:51 p.m immediately following a Special Meeting.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:51 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser, and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustees John Lien and John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and no persons in the
audience.
II. Village Yards Strategy Discussion.
During a brief introduction, Manager Williams emphasized that staff was not
looking for a decision on any of the specific proposals from the evening's
discussion. He, then, turned the floor over to Mr. Saunders, Director of Public
Works, who introduced the representatives from the architects, Legat, Jeff
Sronkoski and Jim Petrakos.
Mr. Saunders provided an overview of the Village's process thus far. He
reviewed the four options considered by staff to meet both current and
anticipated future needs, as well as to solve the numerous deficiencies of the
present facility, as follows:
A.   Construction of New South Wing for Mechanics' Bays: This option proposes
constructing a new mechanics' wing just south of the current lunch room for
Public Works crews. This wing would then be connected to an addition to be
built to the east of, and adjacent to, the lunch room. Finally, a new support
services wing would be constructed on the Willow Road side of the facility. The
remainder of the existing buildings would be renovated.
This option was eliminated due to the traffic flow problems it created. Trucks
would no longer be able to circle the facility without a great deal of
difficulty, causing major disruptions to the flow of traffic, equipment and
employees.
The initial cost estimate for this option, for construction in 2001, is
$9,013,000.
B.   Enclosing the Court Yard: This option projects enclosing the existing
court yard between the two wings of the current complex and constructing a new
support services wing on the Willow Road side of the facility. Again, the
remaining existing buildings would be renovated.
This option was also eliminated because large areas inside the enclosed space
would have been needed to accommodate the maneuvering of trucks and equipment,
which was an inefficient use of the space and drove up the cost without any
additional benefits.
This is the most expensive option, with an initial cost estimate for 2001
construction of $10,097, 000.
C.   Demolition, Renovation, and New Construction: This option incorporates the
following five components:
1)   Demolition of the garage housing the garbage trucks and construction of
new mechanics' bays in this area;
2)   Renovation of the garage housing Public Works equipment;
3)   Renovation of the warehouse area for Water and Electric;
4)   Construction of a new support services facilities; and
5)   Construction of a new garage to house garbage trucks.
The initial cost estimate of this option for construction in 2001 is
$7,615,000. Mr. Saunders indicated that this was one of the two options
considered feasible by both Village staff and the architects.
D.   Construction of a New Facility: This option proposes that all current
buildings would be demolished, and a completely new facility would be
constructed.
The initial cost estimate of this option for construction in 2001 is
$8,487,000. Mr. Saunders said that this option was also considered feasible.
Manager Williams pointed out that all four options share one common element:
the construction of a new Support Services Building, which would serve two
primary functions. First, it would provide locker rooms, meeting rooms, lunch
rooms, etc. for the crews. Secondly, it would house all of the administrative
staff, who are currently located in Village Hall, for both the Public Works
Department and the Water and Electric Department. He noted that such a move
would benefit the Village by:
1)   Freeing up sorely needed office space in Village Hall ;
2)   Reducing the demand for daily parking spaces in the downtown area by about
10 spaces; and
3)   Providing a stronger management presence over the day-to-day operations of
both departments, which should result in increased productivity and efficiency.
Trustee Presser commented that, in his opinion, there is a strong argument to
be made for constructing a new building. Mr. Saunders agreed there were
certainly some efficiencies to be gained by having flexibility of design, but
Mr. Williams pointed out that the any advantages to be gained with regard to
workflow cannot be accurately quantified.
Trustee Woodbury asked for an elevation showing the age of each of the existing
buildings. Mr. Saunders indicated that one would be provided at the next
meeting.
Noting that inventory control had been an issue raised by the Village's
auditors, Trustee Aquilino inquired whether it is a problem because currently
there are several different inventory sites, or whether it could be better
managed with a different system. Mr. Saunders replied that both factors
contributed to the issue. Having different locations spreads out who has access
to inventory and who has control of its check in and check out. If they were
consolidated into a single location, accurate record keeping would be
facilitated. Mr. Williams pointed out that the Village does not experience a
serious inventory shrinkage problem but better design would certainly help.
Trustee Presser inquired if Option C would require updating again in the not-
too-distant future. Mr. Williams responded that staff believes that either of
the two options would meet the Village's needs for approximately the next
thirty years.
Trustee Woodbury noted that there are really two separate issues: (1)
functional efficiency, and (2) repair of the state of disrepair. With regard to
Option C, he asked whether with the funds allocated, the physical problems can
be solved, or whether it would be, merely, a patch. Mr. Williams said that
functionally, a new building could be designed to solve all of the identified
problems, whereas, the definition of 'renovation' is doing the best you can
with what you've got. He noted that although Option D presents a more perfect
solution with an $8.5M price tag, Option C has advantages. Mr. Saunders added
that operationally speaking, the face lift provided by Option C is very
workable in that the technology for the functions contained in the remodeled
buildings is not likely to change.
Mr. McInturff, Director of Water & Electric, also addressed Mr. Woodbury's
question. He noted that all of the areas serving his department where there are
structural problems will disappear in either scenario, with the resulting
structure being both functional and sound. He said that the options present the
alternatives of optimal vs. workable.
Trustee Aquilino inquired about the building's façade. Manager Williams said
that sufficient funds had been allocated to upgrade the Willow Road façade so
that the building(s) would be aesthetically pleasing from that perspective.
Mr. McInturff then reviewed the effect of the project on Village's services
during the construction phase. He noted that under either option, construction
would be conducted in phases, with the new Support Services Building being one
of the first areas to be completed so that the people and people functions can
be moved to a central location. He indicated that it was hoped that the timing
of construction could be coordinated so that all of the mechanics' bays would
never have to be completely closed in order to avoid referring heavy lift jobs
outside of the Village, adding to the expense. He said that in terms of
transitional expenses, Option C would be somewhat less expensive because it
probably would not involve costs associated with the leasing of storage space
for equipment and transportation back and forth.
Then, Manager Williams highlighted some of the financial contingencies
associated with each of the options and reviewed the proposed funding. He
expressed his opinion, supported by the Finance Director, that the $8.5 million
proposed budget is the comfortable limit for the Village in terms of cash
reserves and capital improvements. He emphasized that neither option would
impact long term capital improvements, although some might be postponed for a
short period. He pointed out that once the current cash reserves are spent,
given the current revenue structure, the Village cannot anticipate replenishing
its coffers quickly. Finally, he mentioned that the next major capital project
could be a parking deck at the Community House, with a rough estimated cost of
$3-4 million dollars, and anything saved on this project could become seed
money for that one.
After reviewing a decision matrix with the Trustees, Mr. Williams concluded
that the Village has two very viable options, each with its own pros and cons.
The representatives from Legat made a brief presentation and responded to
questions from the Trustees.
In response to a question from Trustee Aquilino, Mr. Williams estimated that it
would be approximately one year before any construction could begin, and that
would be assuming the Village encountered no problems in obtaining a permit to
build on the landfill from the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency. From
beginning to end, he said he expected that the construction process itself
would last from 12-16 months.
The Trustees briefly discussed each of the alternatives as well as the best
ways to notify villagers of the impending project.
On behalf of himself and Trustee Lien, Trustee Presser expressed initial
support for Option D, citing increased efficiencies of design.
Trustees Woodbury, Aquilino, and Brower expressed initial ambivalence about the
options. Trustee Brower commented that Option D certainly appears to be the
more efficient and user friendly. He mentioned that residents expect immediate
gratification and have a very high level of service which would be facilitated
by Option D. He, however, said that the Village must pay attention to its
financial constraints.
President Holland mentioned that initially she had been in favor of Option C,
but that after viewing a recent remodel job elsewhere, she feels that Option D
might be more economical in the long run as perceived economies can quickly
disappear over a short period of time.
It was decided to hold a second study session on May 23rd to further discuss
the options with hope of deciding upon one so that feedback from residents can
be taken over the summer. Public meetings, then, could be held in September
with an eye toward starting construction next summer.
The meeting adjourned at 9:40 p.m.
May 16, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved June 6, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
16, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Cindy McCammack, Director of Community
Development, Jim Stier, Village Forester, and about fifty persons in the
audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
May 23, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they would be
able to attend. Trustee Aquilino said that she expected to arrive late.
June 6, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees indicated that they intended
to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
May 2, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Landmark Preservation Awards.
President Holland introduced Nan Greenough, Chairman of the Landmark
Preservation Commission, and extended a special thank you to her and the other
members of the Commission for all of their effort to put the awards together.
She commented on how the awards' prestige has grown over the years.
Ms. Greenough remarked that this is the sixth year for the awards and that
there had been a superb crop of nominees. She noted that the judges are always
preservation professionals known throughout the Midwest and that throughout the
years, she has seen a consistency to the selections in that they all rely on
basic underlying principles.
Then, in recognition of Preservation Week, President Holland presented the
Village’s annual awards for demonstrating what careful building can accomplish.
As she presented each award, Ms. Greenough showed a slide of the subject
property. President Holland commented that each of the projects receiving an
award showed sensitivity to the community and respect for their neighborhoods.
Awards were presented to the following:
For Rehabilitation/Sensitive Addition:
Sheilah & Joe Johnson                  548 Cherry St.
Becky & Jeff Cohen                     1028 Cherry St.
Lynn & Rich Mathias                    514 Oak St.
Cathy & David Durning                  838 Locust Rd.
For Restoration:
Patricia Kenney & Jeffrey Moon         19 Warwick Rd.
Melanie & Greg Stahl                   680 Locust Rd.
Sharon & James Beyl                    887 Oak St.
Andrea & Marc Katz                     491 Elder Ln.
Michael Sachs & Alice Rapoport         731 Prospect
For Restoration & Rehabilitation
Molly & John Lien                      921 Pine St.
For Rehabilitation:
Eleanor & Christopher Land             1045 Dinsmore Rd.
Judy & George Harmon                   1376 Asbury Ave.
Beth & Erik Anton                      915 Elm St.
Cate & Tom Barron                      1071 Ash St.
For New Construction
Joan Golder                            1235 Whitebridge Ln.
 
VIII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance M-11-2000 - Zoning Variation: 976 Sunset Rd. - Adoption. Trustee
Aquilino moved to adopt the ordinance permitting an increase in an existing
nonconforming front setback. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, Lien, Brower and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-12-2000 - Zoning Variation: 851 Sheridan Rd. - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting the structural alteration and enlargement of window openings that
are within an already legally existing, nonconforming wall. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, Lien and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-13-2000 - Zoning Variation: 550 Oak St. - Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed proposed amended language incorporated into introduced
ordinance reflecting the Council discussion at its May 9th Special Meeting and
responded to a question from Trustee Woodbury about one of the recitals.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the ordinance as
suggested. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, which would permit the construction of a wooden, picket fence along
the property line adjacent to Poplar and Oak Streets. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-14-2000 - Zoning Variations: 381 Poplar Street - Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the request to permit variations from the roofed lot
coverage provisions of the Zoning Ordinance, in addition to north side yard and
set back requirements, to allow for the construction of a rear addition and
replacement of the upper floor. She noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 4-0 to recommend that Council grant these variances and responded to
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-15-2000 - Zoning Variations: 1124 Gage St. - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack explained the request to permit variations from the building height,
gross floor area, and setback provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to allow for
the construction of a third floor addition. She reviewed prior action by both
the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Village Council pertaining to previous
application for the same variations. She pointed out that the existing GFA is
nonconforming, in that it is above what is permitted, and that although with
the proposed changes there would be a net decrease in the building size, the
resulting FAR would still be above that permitted by the Zoning Ordinance. In
closing, she noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-0 to recommend
that the Council grant the requested variances.
Seymour Turner, the architect, presented elevations showing the applicant's
plans and spoke in support of their request. He also responded to questions
from the Trustees.
Manager Williams mentioned that although this is an already nonconforming
building and there are unusual circumstances, to the best of his knowledge,
this would be the first time the Council would approve an FAR over 50%.
Attorney Janega pointed out that the proposed ordinance contains a provision
that requires the commencement of the construction to begin within 12 months of
the effective date of the ordinance and be contingent upon taking away
measurable GFA by the permanent removal of the existing knee walls and screens
enclosing the existing back porch, without replacement thereof.
Trustee Powell commented that since this would result in a net decrease to the
floor area ratio, he could support the application.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
IX. Old Business.
None.
X. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1173 and 1174. Trustee Brower moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1173 in the total amount of $636,043.14 and Warrant List No. 1174 in the
total amount of $368,908.24. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Annual Outdoor Seating Permits. Manager Williams explained that seven local
restaurants have submitted applications and certificates of insurance seeking
outdoor sidewalk seating permits for the coming year. They are: St. Louis
Bread/Panera, Sweet Shop, Espresso Pacifico, Caribou coffee, Starbucks, Caffe
Buon Giorno, and Song O'Sixpence.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the
applications. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Sheridan Road/Ravine Tree Removal & Planting. Mr. Stier, the Village Forester,
explained the proposed disposition of hazardous trees along Sheridan Road and
the tree trimming, removal and replanting plans for the public right-of-way in
the "ravines" in accordance with his recommendations which were reviewed by the
Forestry Commission.
After some discussion, Trustee Lien moved to approve the plans as submitted by
the Village Forester. He was seconded by Trustee Powell. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Bid No. 00-008A - Underground Cable. Manager Williams reviewed the annual bids
for 15kv and 600v cable to be used for various projects throughout the Village
to be purchased on an "as needed" basis for the period May 1, 2000 to April 30,
2001. He noted staff's recommendation was that the bids be awarded to Okonite
Co. for $27,440 for the 600v cable and Wesco for $121,489 for the 15kv cable.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Bid No. 00-010 - Conduit Installation. Manager Williams reviewed the bids for
the installation of conduit by the directional bore method for the period June
1, 2000 through May 31, 2001, with an option to extend for one additional year.
He pointed out that bidders were requested to provide unit prices using the
estimated quantities that were provided in the bid document, which allowed for
comparisons of the bids. He noted that staff recommended that the bid be
awarded to Directional Solutions for $179,045.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the award of the
bid as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland commented briefly on the report
filed by the operator of the Village's composting site.
XI. Reports
Village Trustees.
Trustee Woodbury said that the Design Guidelines Committee had met and was
scheduled to interview planning and architectural firms in June, after which
they hoped to have a recommendation to present to the Council. He noted that
the budget for the project appears to be leaner than its scope.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village's annual spring
cleanup total had taken in 335.33 tons of rubbish, the second highest amount in
the last seven years. He also noted that the four vehicles sent to the
Northwest Municipal Conference Auction had netted the Village $11,115.
XII. Appointments.
None.
XIII. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:03 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent:
None.
XIV. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower moved to adjourn the meeting and Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:04 p.m.
