                  Deputy Clerk
May 23, 2000
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved June 6, 2000)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
23, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manger Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Finance Ed McKee, Director of
Public Works Steven Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and
no persons in the audience.
II. Village Yards Project Financing.
Mr. McKee summarized his recommendations regarding the financing of a new
Village Yards. He said that the Village is proposing to pay for the project
with transfers from the five larger operating funds that benefit from the
project. Contributions to the Yards Fund would be based on ability to pay and
percentage of use and projected benefits.
He explained that the Village has adopted a philosophy of setting target cash
balances to aid in determining revenue and expenditure decisions. Because the
Village funds most of its capital expenses out of cash reserves, it is
sometimes necessary to build up dollars over many years for a project like the
one under consideration. Both Mr. McKee and Manager Williams commented that is
unlikely that given the current revenue sources, the Village would be able to
replenish its cash reserves to the current level in the near future. They noted
that although projected revenues are flattening, as budgeted, the Yards project
would not deplete reserves below a conservatively comfortable level.
Mr. McKee also reviewed a spreadsheet summarizing the cash balances for the
last four years for those funds that will contribute to the Yards Project. He
noted that after transfers in 2001 and 2002, because most of the funds would be
at the very low end of their target cash amounts, and the Village's financial
flexibility could become constrained.
Mr. Williams called attention to the fact that if the financing plan is
approved, the Village would be diminishing its Electric Fund to a balance
somewhat below that established by prior Councils, although not below comfort
level. President Holland pointed out that the same situation occurred when the
new substantion was built.
President Holland inquired as to what other major capital improvement projects
might be on the more immediate horizon which might require funds from the
General Fund. Mr. Williams replied that he foresaw the possibility of a new
transformer and, perhaps, a parking deck at the Community House. He noted that
the Village's capital funding is very high on an annual level, and that should
necessity arise, certain projects could, in fact, be temporarily deferred.
Mr. McKee responded to additional questions from the Trustees regarding funding
and the Village's fund structure.
Village Yards Strategy Discussion. Trustee Lien spoke in favor of the option
for a completely new structure, saying that for the additional cost, the
increased efficiencies would be well worthwhile. He also mentioned that there
are ways to structure the contract to encourage a design which would come in
line with the projected budget.
Trustee Woodbury concurred with Mr. Lien's position. He suggested that the
Village consider employing a professional construction manager to help control
costs and, perhaps, serve as a general contractor.
Trustee Powell expressed some initial ambivalence about the expense of funds on
this project at this time, thereby depleting the Village's cash reserves for
other possible projects. He did, however, indicate support for the new office
structure.
Manager Williams pointed out that at the present time the Village's mechanics
are working in less than optimal conditions which must be corrected in one way
or another and must take precedence over new office space. He said that in his
estimation the Yards Project is a valid project that will increase efficiency
and productivity in addition to creating additional space for use in Village
Hall.
President Holland noted that the Village's mission is to keep its facilities
and services operating at efficient levels consistent with the expectations of
the residents.
Trustee Lien said that the working conditions at the Yards are currently
disgraceful and that he would be in favor of spending money to correct those
before spending any funds to bury another electrical line, especially in view
of the fact that the Village would not be drawing down its reserves outside of
its comfort level.
Trustee Presser concurred, saying that he was in favor of a new facility in
order to increase employee efficiency and morale.
Manager Williams reiterated that under either proposed scenario, there would be
increased efficiencies and that employees will have significantly upgraded
facilities.
Trustee Brower expressed his belief that it is not the Village's job to store
away accumulated funds, but to spend them for the overall, long term benefit of
the Village.
The Trustees then discussed the chronology of the decision making process and
decided to vote on which option they preferred at the June 6th Council meeting,
then proceed to publicize it throughout the summer.
Manager Williams suggested the appointment of a Technical Committee to include
Trustees Lien and Woodbury, once the option has been chosen.
III. Unpaid Parking Tickets Program.
Mr. McKee noted that because the Village has only a limited number of parking
spaces, substantially more parking issues arise and more parking tickets are
issued than in many communities of a similar size. He explained that during the
upcoming renewal of employee parking passes (A&B) and commuter parking passes,
the Finance Department proposes to exercise some of the expanded powers granted
to the Finance Director by the new Village Code, to enhance parking enforcement
and collect on as many outstanding parking tickets as practical.
The proposal is that all outstanding parking tickets must be paid prior to
being able to purchase a new parking permit. In addition, an applicant will be
required to fully complete and sign the permit application, conditions which
have not necessarily been enforced in the past.
Mr. Williams remarked that this would be in keeping with approved policy
whereby nonessential Village services can be withheld from individuals who have
failed to pay their bills.
Mr. McKee outlined the advantages for the Village of pursuing this course. In
doing so, he noted that the current outstanding balance of uncollected parking
tickets for the last three years is $139,736.
The Trustees unanimously agreed to the proposal.
The meeting adjourned at 9:23 p.m.
June 6, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved June 20, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
6, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, and Stephen
Presser. Absent: Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and about eighteen
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
June 13, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Aquilino, indicated that they expected to be able to attend.
June 20, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Aquilino, indicated that they expected to be able to attend. Manager Williams
said that he would be unable to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
May 9, 2000, Special Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
May 9, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
May 16, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
May 23, 2000, Study Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance M-14-2000 - Zoning Variation: 381 Poplar St. Trustee Brower moved to
adopt the ordinance permitting roofed lot coverage, north side yard, and
setback variances. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
Ordinance No. M-16-2000 - Zoning Variation and Special Use Permit: 518 Winnetka
Ave. - Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed the proposal by Metropolitan
Development Corporation to develop a mixed-use building on the subject
property. The proposed development would require a Special Use Permit to allow
for interior and exterior street level parking and variations from the building
height and setback provisions of the Village Code. She reported on the findings
of the Design Review Board, Plan Commission, and Zoning Board of Appeals, the
latter two of which recommended that the Council approve the applicant's
requests.
Manager Williams asked Ms. McCammack to explain how the setback was being
calculated. She said that the setback was being measured from the wall of the
arcade, instead of from the columns of the arcade. Mr. Williams pointed out
that if the measurement was taken from the columns, no setback variation would
be required.
Trustees Powell and Brower and President Holland asked questions about the
location of the traffic signal. Mr. Saunders indicated that at this time there
are no plans to move it.
Steve Friedland, the applicant's attorney, discussed the proposed development,
pointing out that applicant's first proposal complied fully with the Zoning
Code and that changes which were made to that proposal in response to concerns
raised by neighbors. Among the changes he mentioned were (a) reducing the
amount of available commercial space by adding the arcade to the first floor;
(b) redirecting parking ingress and egress in order to keep users of the
commercial space out of the adjacent neighborhood; and (c) providing
articulation and exterior modifications to the third floor.
Manager Williams commented that the applicant proposed to provide 23 parking
spaces for 10 living units, in compliance with the 2.25 spaces per unit
required by the Code. He remarked that if each building resident had two cars,
that would only leave three spaces for users of the commercial space and asked
Mr. Friedland whether the applicant had considered that this might limit the
types of businesses that would be interested in the space. Mr. Friedland
responded that they had.
Trustee Brower asked where the business employees would park and whether the
nine proposed outdoor spaces would be open for anyone to park. Mr. Friedland
replied that employees would be directed to park in nearby employee parking
lots and that the outdoor spaces would be restricted to use by users of the
commercial space, depending upon how many were assigned to residents of the
building. He pointed out that there is no Code requirement that a developer
provide employee parking.
With regard to the outdoor parking spaces, President Holland asked whether they
had been designed to accommodate today's oversized vehicles. Voy Madeyski, the
architect, responded that although the situation was not optimum, it was
workable according to their traffic consultant. President Holland asked how
wide the parking spaces would be. Mr. Madeyski said that they would be 19' x
8'6".
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to comment on these dimensions from a
technical standpoint. Mr. Saunders said that 8.5 ft. is the width used in the
Village's commuter lots and that the length can be anywhere from 16 to 19 feet,
so in his estimation the parking space size is sufficient. He remarked,
however, that the aisle is a little tight and that some vehicles might be
required to execute a three-point turn in order to exit.
Trustee Brower expressed some concern about traffic exiting the building onto
Winnetka Avenue across a narrow sidewalk.
The architect indicated that this plan had evolved from meetings with residents
and planners in response to worries about the impact of traffic on the
neighborhood. Mr. Friedland pointed out that this would not be a high volume
traffic generator because the underground parking would be assigned to
residents. He added that appropriate light and sound signals would be provided
to indicate that a car was exiting the building.
Mr. Saunders commented that the Village had investigated this issue and found
no research to indicate that this should be considered a serious problem. He
noted that there are similar situations throughout the Village with no accident
history where there is probably higher pedestrian traffic.
Continuing to express concern about the outdoor parking area, President Holland
asked where snow would go. Mr. Saunders responded that the building would
probably wind up losing some parking spaces to accommodate plowed snow. The
other alternative would be to cart it out on a truck.
Trustee Brower inquired about the status of the remediation letter from the
Environmental Protection Agency. Mr. Friedman indicated that the developer was
working with an environmental consultant and that they hoped to have a final
letter within 90 days of final project approval.
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 8:15 p.m.
President Holland then invited members of the audience to speak.
Al Day, 85 High Street, thanked the developer and the architect for trying to
work with the neighborhood to modify the original plan to address concerns
raised. He stated, however, that the overall building plan is inappropriate to
the neighborhood; that it is too big and negatively impacts air, light, and
green space. He expressed his opinion that the problem results from two
factors: (1) the Zoning Code which allows for a building of this size to be
developed next to a residential neighborhood, and (2) the fact that at some
point in the past the Village picked up an additional turning lane by cutting
back on the parkway, which resulted in an especially narrow sidewalk to begin
with. On behalf of the neighbors, he voiced serious concerns about the impact
this development will have on the neighborhood, saying that the nature of this
development immediately adjacent to a single family residential neighborhood is
contrary to the Comprehensive Plan.
Marty Kenny, 510 Meadow, agreed with Mr. Day's comments.
Baird Smart, 112 Church St., urged the Council to examine the development in
the context of the available space. He pointed out that there would be a wall a
mere 5 ft. from moving traffic, creating a traffic hazard, and that the mature
trees would be destroyed with only minimal vegetation being proposed as
replacement. He concurred with the previous speakers that the project is too
massive for the available space. He asked the Council to use a negative vote on
the variations to send a message to all developers that only something in
keeping with the Village's best interests would be approved.
In response to questions from Manager Williams, Mr. Madeyski stated that the
arcade is 15 ft wide and is designed as both a visual feature and a functional
asset.
Trustee Powell observed that the arcade could be continued across the garage
door. Mr. Madeyski responded that such a design would mean the loss of one
inside parking space and remarked that building is already set back farther
than is required by the Zoning Code and, in fact, could be markedly larger than
is proposed.
Trustee Aquilino voiced concern about the street setback on the north side and
asked Mr. Madeyski whether the developer had considered reducing the size of
the building in order to provide a greater setback. Mr. Madeyski pointed out
that the building has to be large enough to support its mixed use concept . He
said that the size of the apartments cannot be reduced, especially in light of
the fact that the developer is already providing fewer than permitted by the
Zoning Ordinance.
Trustee Woodbury asked for an explanation of remarks made by the architect and
developer that the project's design is still a work in progress. Mr. Friedland
indicated that these comments go to what the building might ultimately look
like, what materials might be used and what specific articulation might be
incorporated. He said that with regard to structure, height, and massing, the
project would be exactly as proposed.
President Holland asked what would be done to protect the mature trees on the
west side. Paul Hardej, the developer, responded that every attempt would be
made to save as many of the trees as possible.
Citing the experience of the intrusion of the construction process into the
neighborhood, President Holland raised the question of where the developer
planned to stage the construction. Mr. Hardej said that they would work with
the Village to build as efficiently as possible and that the configuration of
the site itself, along with the construction type, would help to minimize
problems.
President Holland called for a motion to introduce the ordinance. Trustee Lien
so moved. He was seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-17-2000 - Zoning Variation: 595 Orchard - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed the request for variations from the setback and side yard
provisions of the Village Code in order to permit the replacement of
deteriorating foundations and the rebuilding of an existing chimney and
screened porch to provide a foundation system under existing bay windows. She
noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to recommend approval of
this request.
Trustee Woodbury clarified that requested variations were simply to rebuild
what is presently there. Ms. McCammack responded affirmatively.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting the requested variations. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Resolution No. R-23-2000 - Approving Agreement with Metropolitan Alliance of
Police - Adoption. Manager Williams reported on the renegotiation of the
collective bargaining agreement with the union representing Winnetka's patrol
officers. He summarized the key changes to the contract and said that the union
members had recently voted to ratify this Agreement. Noting that it was a good
agreement for both parties, he said that both he and the Police Chief
recommended that the Resolution be approved.
Trustee Brower congratulated Manager Williams on the conclusion of the
successful negotiations.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt Resolution No. R-23-2000 approving the
Agreement between the Village and the Winnetka Police Association, Metropolitan
Alliance of Police, Winnetka Chapter 54 for April 1, 2000 through March 31,
2003. Her motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower,
and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Old Business.
Village Yards Strategy. Manager Williams briefly reviewed the two feasible
options identified by the Village's architect and staff. He also outlined the
next steps in the process once Council has selected one option for further
study, including the completion of preliminary designs and drawings, selection
of a Technical Committee, and hiring of a construction manager to assist in the
development of accurate costs estimates. Saying that no final decision needed
to be reached at this time, he recommended that the Council narrow the focus of
further study to one of the two options.
Trustee Woodbury inquired whether any plans produced as a result of further
study would be sufficient to begin the permitting process with the
Environmental Protection Agency. Mr. Saunders responded that the study should
produce a reasonably complete site plan which would include most of the
necessary information about foundations and gradations that would be of the
most interest to the EPA.
Trustee Lien expressed his preference for review of the option calling for
construction of a completely new facility, and his belief that architectural
costs can be controlled through contract language.
Trustee Aquilino supported Trustee Lien's position, as did Trustee Woodbury.
Trustee Powell voiced some continuing reservations, but indicated that overall,
the idea probably made sense.
Trustee Presser moved that the Village proceed with: (1) the study of the new
construction option under the direction and supervision of the Village
President and Village Manager; (2) the appointment of a Technical Committee and
its authorization to negotiate a preliminary contract with the architect,
subject to Council's approval; and (3) the authorization of the Technical
Committee to seek proposals from a Construction Manager to assist in developing
accurate cost information. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1175 and 1176. Trustee Brower explained some of the items on
the warrant lists and then moved to approve Warrant List No. 1175 in the total
amount of $801,621.89 and Warrant List No. 1176 in the total amount of
$538,871.45. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Zoning Variation: 911 Euclid Ave. - Policy Direction. Ms. McCammack reviewed a
request for a side yard variance to permit the construction of a new single
family residence. She noted that the issue was being brought to the Council
because there was no recommendation from the Zoning Board of Appeals in light
of the fact that it is very difficult to meet the standard of hardship with new
construction. She explained that that the proposed new residence would satisfy
the minimum side yard requirements, but that the resulting total of the two
side yards would be less than that required by the Village Code. She noted that
the subject property is a flag lot and that when the Council reviewed and
revised the standards pertaining thereto, it was determined that no changes
were necessary for setbacks or yards.
Mr. Williams asked Ms. McCammack to review the history of oddities that occur
with flag lots, how required yards are calculated on flag lots, and
particularly how the calculations for the subject property were made. Ms.
McCammack did so.
Trustee Powell commented that he saw no reason for a resident to have to seek a
variance and go through the appeals process because of what appear to be
irrational calculations. He suggested that the issue be addressed as a part of
the upcoming Study Session devoted cleaning up the Zoning Ordinance.
Attorney Janega explained that this anomaly exists in the Zoning Ordinance and
that the issue had been addressed by a prior Council in the form of a text
amendment that would address the concept of average side yards. She explained
that the problem arises out of the location of the street frontage on the
Subject Property, which is the narrow end of the “flagpole” portion of the lot.
She noted that, because the street frontage places the location of the front
lot line on the north side of the lot, the front yard has been established on
the west side of the lot which is the narrower side of the body of the lot. The
aligns the front yard with the other front yards on Euclid Avenue. As a result,
she explained, the average lot width, which is used in determining side yard
requirements, is based on the dimension that functions as the depth of the lot.
Ms. Janega pointed out that a text amendment is ordinarily considered only when
there have been numerous requests for variances and noted that this is only the
second time such a request has been sought based upon this particular anomaly.
She further explained that one of the reasons that the Council had chosen not
to pursue amending the Zoning Ordinance was that there was not, and still is
not, any way to gauge the unintended consequences of any changes that might be
made, as there is no way of determining how many lots might be affected. Ms.
Janega observed that on the subject property, it is very clear what impact
revising the formula would have, but that on more irregularly shaped
properties, of which there are many in the Village, the consequences are less
obvious. She remarked that when changes are made to multiple definitions, one
has to look at their impact on every lot in every zoning district, whereas, the
variation process is intended to address individual situations.
Erick Mollman, the applicant, discussed the plans for the new house and
responded to questions from the Trustees.
Tom Healy, 1173 Tower Rd., voiced concern that the minimum requirements
governing a zoning variance have not been observed in that there was no finding
of hardship. Therefore, he objected to Council's directing the Village attorney
to draft an ordinance introducing the requested variation.
Jim Pinkerton, 333 Willow Road, addressed the Council. His comments are
attached hereto as Attachment #1.
Trustee Woodbury said that he is generally not in favor of granting variations
for new construction and said that he would like to listen to the discussion of
the merits of side yards with regard to flag lots before a variation is
granted. He said that if there is an issue with the rule, then the rule needs
to be addressed directly.
President Holland reiterated the fact that this is a clean flag lot and that
any discussion would necessarily have to extend to all irregularly shaped lots.
Trustee Aquilino pointed out that if the Council concludes that there is an
issue with the rule, then the rule should be changed. In the meantime, the
applicant will be forced to wait until the deliberations are over before
proceeding with their project. She voiced support for the permitting the
proposed variation, citing the seeming logic of the plan and the preservation
of the lovely back yard.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to direct the Village Attorney
to prepare an ordinance permitting the requested variation. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
Certificate of Appropriateness: 100 Green Bay Rd. Ms. McCammack explained a
request from First National Bank & Trust of Evanston for an illuminated 30-foot
flagpole to be located in front of their facility at 100 Green Bay Road. She
noted that the Design Review Board had voted 5-0 to recommend issuance of a
Certificate of Appropriateness.
President Holland inquired about the design of the light that would illuminate
the flagpole. Ms. McCammack replied that it was designed to illuminate only the
flagpole.
Trustee Brower mentioned that two other banks in town have flagpoles, so there
is precedent to grant this request.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to issue a Certificate of
Appropriateness as requested. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Vacuum Leaf Machines. Mr. Saunders reviewed a recommendation to waive the
formal bid process for the purchase of two leaf machines because Standard
Equipment Co. is the sole source dealer in the Chicagoland area for the three
manufacturers of equipment meeting the Village's specifications.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to award the bid to Standard
Equipment Co. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Bid No. 00-011 - Indian Hill Parking Lot Beautification. Mr. Saunders reviewed
the bids for the Indian Hill Parking Lot beautification project, identified by
the Ad Hoc Beautification Committee to be the target of funds budgeted for the
Committee's use. He recommended that the bid be awarded to the low bidder,
Church Landscaping, in the amount of $151,436.70.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the award of
the bid as recommended and to authorize the Village President to sign a
contract on behalf of the Village. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and
Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Spruce Street Storm Sewer Outfall Rehabilitation. Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed
the history of this issue. He explained the two options identified for long-
term repairs to the facility: (1) remove the existing pier and pipe from the
base of the bluff to the Lake and construct a runoff channel across the beach,
at a cost of approximately $109,000; or (2) remove and replace the entire
existing pipe and pier structure, at an approximate cost of $190,000. He noted
that either option would address the technical issues involved, i.e., providing
a working storm sewer outfall that would be physically stabilized from lake
action.
Mr. Saunders pointed out that there are some policy questions revolving around
the issue concerning: (1) budget impact; (2) public use/amenity desired; and
(3) effect upon neighboring properties. With regard to the first item, he
explained that there are currently no funds in the FY 2000-01 Budget for this
project, but he outlined possible sources of funds that could be used.
Regarding the second item, he commented that while Option 2 would replace a
previously existing amenity, Option 1 would not. Finally, he pointed out that
repairs to the Village's facility would affect the two, privately owned,
neighboring properties as to littoral drift and aesthetics/use of beach. In
concluding his remarks, Mr. Saunders recommended that based upon budget
constraints and technical issues, the Village proceed with Option 1.
Mr. Saunders then responded to questions from the Trustees. After some
discussion, it was decided that the issue be addressed in connection with the
broader discussion as to disposition and policies regarding the Village's
street ends scheduled for later in the summer.
X. Reports
Village President. President Holland announced an upcoming hazardous waste
pickup in Glenview.
Village Trustees.
Trustee Brower reported that he and Trustee Aquilino had attended the business
forum sponsored by the Chamber of Commerce where most of the discussion
revolved around the upcoming Summer Festival.
Trustee Woodbury reported that the Ad Hoc Design Guidelines Committee would be
meeting this week with four prospective planners and hoped to have a
recommendation to the Council in early July.
Trustee Powell reported that he had attended a recent open house at the SWANCC
facility and been very impressed. He encouraged the other Trustees and
interested residents to attend a future open house.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland proposed the appointment of Gwen Trindl as the Plan
Commission's representative to the Forestry Commission, with Bob Horne serving
as her alternate should she be unable to attend.
Trustee Powell moved to approve the appointments. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:47 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:34 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
11:35 p.m.
