                  Deputy Clerk
June 20, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved July 6, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
20, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Barbara Aquilino and Village Manager Douglas
Williams. Also present: Acting Village Manager William Gallagher, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and about twelve persons in the
audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that Item IX(b), a request from Winnetka
Presbyterian Church, had been removed from the Agenda. The Trustees approved
the Agenda as amended.
IV. Quorum.
July 6, 2000, Regular Meeting. President Holland reminded the Trustees that due
to the Fourth of July holiday, the normally scheduled meeting for July 4, 2000
would be held on the following Thursday. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Presser, indicated that they expected to be able to attend.
July 11, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Presser, indicated that they expected to be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
June 6, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance M-15-2000 - Zoning Variation: 1124 Gage St. - Adoption. Trustee Lien
moved to adopt the ordinance permitting roofed lot coverage, north side yard,
and setback variances. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. President
Holland clarified that the porch on the south side would be, and remain,
completely open. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Aquilino.
Ordinance No. M-17-2000 - Zoning Variation: 595 Orchard - Adoption. Trustee
Brower moved to adopt the ordinance permitting variations from setback and side
yard provisions in order to permit the replacement of deteriorating foundations
and the rebuilding of an existing chimney and screened porch. Trustee Presser,
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Aquilino.
Ordinance No. M-18-2000 - Zoning Variation: 911 Euclid Ave. - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack briefly reviewed the request that discussed at the last Council
meeting to permit a side yard variance in order to allow for the construction
of a new single family residence on a flag lot. Trustee Powell, seconded by
Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the ordinance. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-22-2000 - Addendum to Ameritech Vault Agreement - Adoption.
Ms. Janega explained that pursuant to R-1477-99, adopted in January, 1999,
Ameritech was grated licenses to use four Village owned sites for
communications equipment. Ameritech is now requesting a minor change of sites
at the Hubbard Woods parking garage to facilitate construction. The change
would result in no significant change in the appearance of the completed
installation. She reported that neither the Water & Electric and Public Works
Departments had any objections to the shift in sites.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the Resolution as
proposed. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Aquilino.
Resolution No. R-24-2000 - Release of Executive Session Minutes - Adoption. Ms.
Janega explained that pursuant to state law, the Council is required to examine
and release Executive Session minutes which no longer require confidentiality.
The proposed resolution would cover Executive Session minutes from May 18,
1999, through June 6, 2000. She noted that the resolution specifies the
retention of certain sets of minutes for confidentiality due to ongoing
litigation.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the Executive
Session minutes from December 21, 1999, through June 6, 2000. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Powell, Woodbury,
Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
Trustee Lien then moved to adopt Resolution No. R-24-2000. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Brower. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Aquilino.
Resolution No. R-25-2000 - MFT Funds: Pine Street Bridge Engineering -
Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that this resolution would authorize the
expenditure of $11,400 of Motor Fuel Tax Funds for Phase I Engineering of the
Pine Street Bridge. He noted that this project is federally funded and that,
therefore, the Village's portion of the cost would amount to 25% of the total
cost.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt Resolution R-25-2000.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
Resolution No. R-26-2000 - MFT Funds: Eldorado Street Bridge Engineering -
Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that this resolution would authorize the
expenditure of $50,000 of Motor Fuel Tax Funds for all of the engineering of
the Eldorado Street Bridge. He noted that this project was not eligible for
federal funding so that all of the costs would be paid by the Village.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt Resolution R-26-
2000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
VIII. Old Business.
Ordinance No. M-16-2000 - Zoning Variation and Special Use Permit: 518 Winnetka
Ave. Ms. Janega briefly reviewed Council's discussion at its June 6, 2000,
meeting at which time Ordinance M-16-2000 was introduced. She noted that during
those discussions, the Council expressed general concern about the lack of
detail on design elements and about an apparent lack of finality to the
building design. She also enumerated four additional specific concerns about
the proposed redevelopment raised by the Council, as follows:
(1)   The first concern was the narrowness of the sidewalk, particularly along
Winnetka Avenue. Questions were raised about the possibility of removing the
traffic signal standard currently in the sidewalk at the Green Bay Road corner
of the property, and about the possibility of widening the sidewalk by reducing
the width of the right turn lane on Winnetka Avenue.
(2)   The second concern pertained to the proposed parking spaces and whether
the spaces and aisles are sufficient for the safe maneuverability of vehicles
trying to enter and exit the parking areas. Other parking concerns included the
direction of the traffic and the parking lot exit on Winnetka Avenue.
(3)   The third area of concern pertained to the proposed arcade, its aesthetic
impact on the streetscape and potential safety issues because the area is not
readily visible from the street. The Council also questioned whether the
proposed commercial space behind the arcade is viable, due to its lack of
visibility, no apparent location for signage and general lack of parking.
(4)   Finally, the Council raised questions about the landscaping, including
the proposed landscape buffer, the impact of the development on the trees
adjoining the property to the west, and whether some landscaping or parkway
area is possible at street level.
   Steve Friedland, the applicant's attorney, indicated that the applicants
were prepared to discuss any of these issues even though they felt that they
had responded to them at the last meeting. He reiterated that the proposed plan
had evolved as a result of a long and arduous process involving staff,
neighbors, and public bodies and that the original proposal had been fully
compliant with the Zoning Ordinance. He pointed out that the developer had been
prepared to build their "as of right" plan but had agreed to go through the
variation process in order to respond to issues raised by neighbors and staff.
   President Holland commented on the importance of this particular corner to
the Village and the impact that its development will have on the whole area.
She said that the Council wanted to be sure that it was fully informed and
entirely comfortable with the plan before taking any action.
   Trustee Woodbury remarked that he felt that the width of the sidewalk on
Winnetka Avenue, if the building is built as proposed was a significant issue.
He said that there is just too much pedestrian traffic, particularly during
school hours, and that he thought that the arcade concept was an unsatisfactory
compromise for a less than optimal sidewalk situation.
   Mr. Friedland responded that the developer would be satisfied with a
building that met the one- to three-foot setback requirement and that the
arcade had been proposed to deal with the sidewalk, which was not conditioned
by this property, but by the fact that the lanes of Winnetka Avenue had been
widened earlier by the Village.
   Trustee Brower mentioned some concern that the arcade would become a
gathering place similar to the White Hen, and Trustee Presser concurred, noting
that there was little enthusiasm among the Council for the arcade concept.
   Mr. Friedland expressed concern about removing the arcade. He said that any
attempt to move the building would raise other significant issues and would
require a variation from the Council as well. He pointed out that the developer
has gone to great lengths to minimize the impact of the building on the
neighborhood and does not have the time, assets, or desire necessary to begin
the process all over again.
   Trustee Powell expressed a preference to see the exit from the underground
parking, which is to be designated for residential parking, shifted onto Brier
St. rather than onto Winnetka Avenue.
   Mr. Friedland replied that the proposed traffic pattern was established in
response to concerns about sending traffic through the neighborhood as
expressed by the Design Review Board and the community. He noted that the
developer views the traffic impact of the building as being quite minimal
overall as there is not the potential for a tremendous number of automobiles.
He also pointed out that the current proposal creates some parking for the
commercial space.
   President Holland inquired as to whether the developer was committed to the
arcade concept given the concerns about sidewalk safety and aesthetics. Mr.
Hardej responded that they were not, but that it certainly represented one
possible solution.
   Trustee Woodbury asked Marty Kenny, a neighborhood representative, whether
he foresaw a significant problem should the traffic from the development's
residences be routed through the neighborhood. Mr. Kenny responded that he,
personally, did not feel that it would create a major problem.
   Mr. Friedland pointed to Mr. Kenny's comments saying that there will be less
traffic than with the current situation and that it will be more controlled.
   Trustee Lien echoed Trustee Woodbury's concern about the narrowness of the
sidewalk. He said that the arcade does not solve the problem because it does
not go the length of the building. He also expressed some reservations about
deviating from the standards established by the new Comprehensive Plan calling
for underground parking for the development of multifamily units along Winnetka
Avenue.
   President Holland inquired whether the arcade could be eliminated in favor
of additional landscaping between the building and the sidewalk and expressed
serious concern about preserving the existing mature trees on the west side of
the property. With regard to the former, Mr. Hardej indicated that it was a
possibility, and with regard to the trees, Mr. Hardej promised that every
effort would be made to save as many of the trees as possible.
After further discussion, President Holland thanked the developers for their
cooperation and participation and said that staff would get back to them with
clear directions for revising the project in accordance with what the Village
would like to see.
Mr. Hardej thanked the Council for its consideration and said that he would
like to proceed as soon as possible.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1177 and 1178. Trustee Brower explained some of the items on
the warrant lists and then moved to approve Warrant List No. 1177 in the total
amount of $599,908.24 and Warrant List No. 1178 in the total amount of
$269,767.03. Trustee Lien seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Brower, Presser, Powell, Woodbury, and
Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
Request for Change of Implementation Date - Winnetka Presbyterian Church. This
item was removed from the Agenda.
X. Tower Road Engineering.
Mr. Saunders explained the bid analysis process used to evaluate the proposals
for the Tower Road engineering to rehabilitate the pavement, install a new
water main and provide other drainage improvements. In response to a question
from President Holland, he said that the engineering would be done this year
and construction would begin next year. He recommended that the bid be awarded
to Oates Associates for $63,120.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to award the bid as recommended
to Oates Associates. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Aquilino.
XI. Reports
Village President. President Holland announced that SWANCC would be holding a
household hazardous waste collection at the transfer station in Glenview and
encouraged residents to take their hazardous waste products there for
appropriate disposal.
She also announced that the Trustees would be available to answer residents'
questions on the corner in front of Koenig & Strey during the Chamber of
Commerce's Summer Festival
Finally, President Holland noted the commendation received by Village Forester
Jim Stier on the development and installation of the Barbara Short Memorial
Tree Walk, which she said would be a wonderful addition to the Village's open
space.
Village Trustees. Trustee Powell reported that he and President Holland had
attended a meeting of the NWMC recognizing outgoing officers and newly elected
officials. He noted that President Holland had been recognized for her
participation and leadership on the Legislative Committee. He also displayed a
plaque presented to the Village for its outstanding performance in Census 2000.
Trustee Brower reported on his attendance at the Chamber of Commerce's After
Hours held at Caribou Coffee/The Book Stall.
XII. Appointments.
President Holland proposed the appointment of Luvie Myers to a full term on the
Plan Commission to replace Trustee Woodbury. Trustee Woodbury moved to approve
the appointment. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and
Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
XIII. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:45 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
XIV. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
10:46 p.m.
         _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
July 6, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved July 18, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday, July
6, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams and Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and two persons in the
audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that Item IX(c), a request to install in-ground
lighting on the public right-of-way had been removed from the Agenda. The
Trustees approved the Agenda as amended.
IV. Quorum.
July 11, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
expected to be able to attend.
July 18, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that they
expected to be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
June 13, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
June 20, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-18-2000 - Zoning Variation: 911 Euclid Ave. - Adoption. Trustee
Lien, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance permitting a
side yard variance to allow for the construction of a new single family
residence on a flag lot. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees . Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
VIII. Old Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1179 and 1180. After commenting on a few of the items on the
warrant lists, Trustee Brower moved to approve Warrant List No. 1179 in the
total amount of $873,104.40 and Warrant List No. 1180 in the total amount of
$289,207.02. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
Resident Concern about Parkway - Policy Direction. Manager Williams briefly
explained the issue about the placement of patio blocks on the parkway. He
noted that historically, the Village has had a "hands off" policy so long as
anything placed on the parkway causes no hazard and does not interfere with
sight lines. However, since this complaint was specifically addressed to the
Council, the issue was brought up for policy direction.
Karen and Joe Fell, the property owners, reported that they had already removed
the patio blocks. Mr. Fell, however, remarked that that was irrelevant to
establishing formal policy. Mrs. Fell pointed out that there is no consistent
policy regarding the parkways; Some are covered in weeds, some in wildflowers,
and others paved over or covered with gravel.
Mr. Williams explained that at one time the Village allowed parking on the
parkways, but that that was prohibited a number of years ago as it creates a
hazard.
Trustee Lien voiced his opinion that since this particular situation is no
longer an issue and the problem does not seem to be widespread, there is no
necessity to establish a formal policy at this time.
Trustee Woodbury expressed his preference for parkways being the traditional
green space.
The other trustees concurred, resulting in a consensus that parkways should be
kept green and growing, filled with planting materials of some type.
Request to Install In-ground Lights on Public Right of Way - Policy Direction.
Due to the applicant's request, this item was removed from the Agenda.
Capital Improvements Reports. Manager Williams briefly reviewed the quarterly
capital improvements reports prepared by the Department Heads. He pointed out
that most of the projects are just getting started for this fiscal year and
that, thus far, the road construction projects are proceeding well.
X. Reports
Village President. President Holland thanked Village staff for expediting the
new plantings around the electrical boxes on the Village Green.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:00 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees, Brower, Lien, Powell, Aquilino and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Brower seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
9:01 p.m.
         _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
July 11, 2000
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved August 1, 2000)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
11, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manger Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Director of Water
and Electric Bryan McInturff, members of the Building Review Committee and
Landmark Preservation Commission, and no persons in the audience.
II. Roundtable Discussion with Building Review Committee and Landmark
Preservation Commission.
President Holland recognized Keith Groebe, Chairman of the Building Review
Committee who presented an overview of the issues for discussion. Mr. Groebe
explained that over the last year the Committee has become increasingly aware
of and concerned about what appears to be a growing trend in the Village.
Specifically, buyers purchasing residential property with the sole intent of
tearing down the existing structure to replace it with new construction. He
commented that a notable number of the recent demolition permit requests
involved properties that in the Committee's opinion did not warrant being torn
down and, in one case, concerned an architecturally significant property. In
the latter instance, the Committee, in fact, recommended a delay in the
issuance of the permit. He also noted that due to the strong economy, the
average total per square footage price of teardowns has increased approximately
9% in the last year, to $42.42. He opined that many of the purchasers appear to
be moving to Winnetka solely for the land value and location with little
appreciation for the character or heritage of the Village. In addition, he
pointed to an increasing number of teardowns on larger lots, further
exacerbating the Committee's concern that an ever-growing percentage of the
Village's housing stock is vulnerable to demolition. Finally, he indicated that
it is the Committee's impression that buyers have become very savvy as to the
Village's requirements to justify a teardown and are, therefore, able to
circumvent the system and any potential holdups in the process.
Mr. Groebe explained that in response to these observations, the Committee
investigated the regulations of other similar communities to determine whether
there might be some more effective means of dealing with the issue than
currently available in the Winnetka. Among those studied were Lake Forest,
which basically approaches the issue from a design review perspective and Oak
Park, which addresses the issue from a historical preservation perspective. He
pointed out that both communities' ordinances are of interest and valuable from
an educational standpoint, but neither is in and of itself totally appropriate
for Winnetka because Winnetka has neither the vast open land of west Lake
Forest, nor the consolidated historical districts of Oak Park.
Mr. Groebe said that the Committee had discussed hypothetically several ideas,
including some sort of a development impact fee, an adjustment to increase the
length of the delay in issuance of a permit allowed to be imposed by the BRC,
some type of architectural review process, and/or some increased financial
burden for simply obtaining a demolition permit.
Karen Fell, also a member of the Building Review Committee, reiterated the
Committee's desire to find some means of stopping unwarranted demolitions. She
pointed out that the process is structured so that the Committee usually does
not have an opportunity to get involved until prospective buyers are deeply
rooted in the demolition mind set, and it is, essentially, a fait accompli. She
suggested that, perhaps, there might be some way for the BRC to become involved
early on in the process with an eye toward counseling buyers as to available
alternatives to demolition and offering resources to help view the property
from a different perspective. Ms. Fell stated that virtually every house in the
Village is a candidate for teardown.
The group discussed the possibility of surveying the community to identify
homes of particular significance. Nan Greenough, Chairman of the Landmark
Preservation Commission pointed out that in order to do a proper survey, all
houses in the Village would need to be catalogued and suggested a couple of
possible ways to accomplish this.
President Holland raised the idea of establishing a private architectural
foundation in the Village.
Manager Williams pointed out that the total number of teardowns amounts to only
approximately one percent of the total housing stock.
Referring to Mr. Groebe's comments about an increasing number of demolitions on
larger lots, Trustee Aquilino inquired as to the effect of the amended Zoning
Ordinance on the number of demolitions on the one-sixth acre lots.
Manager Williams indicated that staff was currently preparing a report on that
topic. He said it would be completed in time for Council's consideration during
discussions about possible similar changes relative to the larger lots.
Attorney Janega pointed out that since last year there have been fewer requests
for demolitions on the small lots. She reminded everyone that the changes
enacted had been structured not only to reduce gross floor area, but also to
encourage renovation.
Ms. Fell asked whether the BRC could be authorized to impose a waiting period
on a demolition in excess of current limit of three months, similar to Lake
Forest. Ms. Janega indicated that she would research the question.
Manager Williams pointed out Lake Forest’s extended waiting period is coupled
to a design review process. He asked Mr. Hoffman whether he thought some sort
of design review process would slow down the number of demolitions. Mr. Hoffman
responded that he did not think that it would.
Trustee Aquilino asked whether the 90-day waiting period had deterred any
demolition permits. Mr. Groebe replied that it had not.
Trustee Woodbury summarized the discussion saying that there appear to be three
separate issues: (1) slowing down the number of demolitions and educating
buyers as to renovation possibilities; (2) stopping the tear down of
“significant” structures, i.e. some sort of conservancy program; and (3)
establishing design guidelines to regulate the quality of replacement homes. He
noted that while the issues are related, each has a different answer and
requires thoughtful study. He pointed out that given current economics and the
dynamics in the real estate market, it is unlikely that the trend will slow
down in the foreseeable future and that any action that would negatively impact
the positive movement in real estate values would not be supported by the
community.
Mr. Groebe concurred but pointed out that all three issues fall within the
purview of the same ordinance. He questioned whether the ordinance provided
adequate tools to discourage undesirable activity as a matter of due course.
Trustee Powell indicated that while he was in favor of identifying significant
properties and educating owners and buyers, he would not be in support of
infringing upon a property owner's rights or enacting any kind of punitive
“hammer” in the form of an ordinance.
Mr. Groebe inquired about the possibility of imposing some sort of development
impact fee.
Ms. Janega responded that it would be very difficult to do because the only two
impact fees specifically authorized by statute are for schools and parks. In
addition, the standard for a sustainable impact fee is that it must be
specifically attributable to the development in question, and this has been the
basis for striking down street and road impact fees. She also noted that the
impact that we would be trying to quantify here is something that hasn’t been
quantified before, which would also increase the difficulty of making the fee
sustainable. She added that an increase in the demolition fee may also be
possible, although generally a fee has to be reasonably related to the burden
imposed on the Village. She suggested that it might be possible to increase the
demolition fees, for example based on the number of trees on the property, but
that overall, this would probably not be financially significant enough to
deter a tear down.
Ms. Greenough expressed her opinion that there is no point in doing a survey to
identify historically significant properties unless the Village is willing to
draw a line as to which specific houses it is unwilling to lose and provide
some sort of mechanism to prevent their demolition.
Manager Williams made three points: (1) that the crux of this problem revolves
around the issue of community standards vs. private property rights, an issue
which has historically generated a lot of friction in the community; (2) that
to the best of his knowledge there is no legal way to stop demolitions; and (3)
that a couple of years ago the Council had rejected the concept of design
review. He suggested that the Council might want to revisit the question of
residential design review but emphasized that it would need widespread support
from the community.
President Holland thanked the members of the visiting committees for their time
and input. She indicated that the Council and staff would continue to study the
issue and explore available options.
III. Street End Study.
Manager Williams commented briefly on the scope of the issue, noting that it
was tied into questions of riparian rights as well as public access and use.
Mr. Saunders reviewed his report detailing the existing conditions and repair
options for the six public street rights-of-way that end at the shore of Lake
Michigan. These are: (1) Fisher Lane Extension; (2) Spruce Street; (3) Elm
Street; (4) Oak Street; (5) Cherry Street; and (6) Willow Road. He noted that
all of these locations are Village-owned property, and with the exception of
Cherry Street, are all undeveloped street rights-of-way. Whereas, Cherry Street
consists of property dedicated to the Village, but configured along the lines
of a utility easement.
Using visual aides, Mr. Saunders addressed each location individually,
commenting on its current condition, level of public use, necessary repairs,
and projected costs of repair/replacement. During his presentation, he noted
that (1) Cherry Street is where the Village experiences the most friction
between the public that uses the street end and the adjoining property owners;
(2) that the repair of the Fisher Lane headwall is the only item currently
provided for in the budget; and (3) that, as discussed at earlier meetings,
Spruce Street is at present the most critically in need of repair. He, then,
outlined the two options for its repair. The first being to replace the pier
and re-extend the storm sewer, and the second being to remove the pier,
truncate the sewer and construct an erosion resistant channel across the beach.
He pointed out that the second option is less expensive than the first, does
not involve any construction into the lake or have any environmental
consequences, and could probably be completed this year, while the permitting
process for the first option would undoubtedly take its construction into next
year.
During the discussion Bill Sick, 565 Sheridan Rd., and Jean Wright, 555
Sheridan Rd., expressed concern on behalf of the Spruce Street end neighbors
about any construction that might adversely impact the adjoining property
values by damaging the present beach. They voiced their preference for the
option that would replace the deteriorated pier, but strongly suggested that
the pier not be designed as a solid structure.
Mr. Saunders enumerated the four policy issues before the Council, as follows:
(1) What level of public use should be intended or permitted at each site? (2)
What repairs or upgrades are necessary to bring each facility to the condition
needed to support the intended level of public use? (3) How should access be
controlled to support the Village’s policy objectives for facility use? (4) How
should the Village assign priorities to the improvements desired by the Council
and fit them into the capital improvement plan?
Manager Williams remarked that if the Council decides to maximize public
access, at any one or all of the street ends, a pier is probably a better
solution than an open channel, and indicated that the Village could seek
outside assistance in pier design. He pointed out, however, that with regard to
the Spruce Street situation, funds for such a project are not budgeted at this
time, while money for the channel project could probably be found. He said that
whichever overall policies staff is directed to follow, the Village would need
to begin to programming street end improvements into the Five Year Capital
Plan.
Trustee Powell asked why there are no signs indicating that this is public
land. Manager Williams responded that the issue had never been raised.
Trustee Woodbury said that if these are to be improved for public access, and
not simply to protect the storm sewers, using public funds, then they ought to
be open to all residents.
Manager Williams cautioned that opening all of the street ends could present a
policing problem.
With regard to the Spruce Street situation, there was consensus among the
Trustees to pursue the pier concept, obtain more information about pier design,
and then hold public hearings.
IV. Cost Sharing for Underground Projects.
In response to questions raised by Trustee Powell, Manager Williams provided a
brief historical perspective of the Village’s undergrounding policies. He also
reviewed the three basic scenarios under which a customer is either required
to, or may request to, put electrical service underground. He commented on
residents’ general acceptance of the Village’s policies and concluded that if
costs to residents are increased, resistance would, in all likelihood, go up as
well. This, in turn, could prove to be counterproductive to achieving the
Village’s articulated goal of having all of its electrical service buried by
2008.
   Trustee Powell voiced some concern about the existing policy that ultimately
provides buried service for residents who live along arterial streets for free
while all others are required to pay for it at one time or another.
The meeting adjourned at 10:55 p.m.
July 18, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved August 15, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday, July
18, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Powell, and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustees John Lien and Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Cindy McCammack, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and 26
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
August 1, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to be able to attend.
August 8, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
July 6, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-16-2000 - Special Use Permit and Variances: 518 Winnetka Avenue
- Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed the revised draft before the Council
saying that it reflected Council’s discussions at previous meetings as well as
the latest revisions proposed by the applicant. These include a reduction in
the total number of parking spaces from 23 to 21 to accommodate the agreed upon
four foot setback for the building and the elimination of the arcade, while
maintaining the configuration of the second and third floors. She indicated
that subsequent to drafting the ordinance, further recommendations had been
made and summarized those suggested revisions, including a further reduction in
the number of parking spaces from 21 to 20.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to comment on the proposed changes to
parking, sidewalk and curb design, light standards, and planting strips. Mr.
Saunders did so concluding that the revised plans met all necessary standards
and that it was his recommendation that the parking be further reduced to 20
spaces.
Ms. Janega pointed out that the landscaping requirements in the draft ordinance
include specific definition of the planting strip and that the depth thereof
would have to be specified. She also noted that the draft incorporates
conditions of the revised parking plan and limits the duration of parking in
the exterior parking spaces to 90 minutes during business hours to assure that
the purpose of the outside parking, to support commercial use, be met. She
indicated that this would also prevent potential use of the parking spaces by
NTHS student.
Trustee Brower clarified that this restriction would attach to the building
forever. Ms. Janega said that it would, under the current wording of the
revisions.
Steve Friedland, attorney for the applicant, objected to the restriction with
regard to the outside parking spaces. He indicated that the applicant could
agree to remove the third parking space and to the other general parameters and
conditions noted by Ms. Janega as a part of the variations and special use
permit. He said, however, that placing a 90 minute limitation on all of the
outdoor parking spaces would pose a very real problem should those places be
conveyed to resident owners. He said that the developer could agree to having
the use of three exterior spaces restricted, but added that the limitation
should not be by restrictive covenant.
Trustee Aquilino asked if there were current elevations showing the proposed
changes, including the removal of the arcade. Mr. Friedland said that there
were not. He pointed out that once the final plan has been determined, the
applicant will have to return to the Design Review Board and the Council for
approval of the specific design elements. He described the building saying that
the first floor would be set back four feet from the property line while the
second floor would only be set back one foot, resulting in an overhang of three
feet. He said that that the applicant wished to have Council's final
determination as to whether this plan with its modifications, variations, and
special use is acceptable, so that they can move on to the next stage. He
indicated that if it was not, they applicant would be forced to return to its
original plan which required no variations.
Trustee Powell clarified that the applicant was proposing eight outdoor parking
spaces, with at least three not allocated to resident parking. Mr. Friedland so
confirmed. He explained that the developer did not feel that every building
resident would have an interest in having a second parking space.
Trustee Aquilino asked about the size of the commercial space. Mr. Friedland
responded that it would be 1,840 sq. ft. Mr. Hardej, the developer, expressed
his belief that it would not be occupied by a major retailer, but rather by a
professional enterprise.
President Holland invited members of the audience to comment.
Marty Kenny, 510 Meadow, objected on behalf of the neighborhood to the lack of
formal revised plans for review. He stated that he didn’t think the sidewalk
was wide enough and that there should be a parkway. He also voiced concern
about the proposal to narrow Winnetka Avenue, which he characterized as already
congested and dangerous.
Baird Smart, 112 Church Rd., commented that all of the problems that continue
to crop up with regard to this project revolve around the fact that it is
simply too large for the property. He noted that there is already abundant,
vacant commercial space in Indian Hill, especially with no parking, and that
the community has no further need for luxury condominiums. He urged the Council
to vote against this project in favor of waiting for one that is
environmentally in sync with the community and complements the neighborhood.
Gene Clark, 534 Meadow, reiterated Mr. Kenny’s comments about the neighbors not
having an opportunity to review the revised plans and voiced concern about the
impact the proposed development will have on the safety and security of
neighborhood homes and residents, especially children.
At the request of President Holland, Ms. Janega gave a detailed review of the
changes to the proposal.
Al Day, 85 High Street, said that, as a neighborhood, their conflict does not
arise from the deisgn of the proposed development, but from its magnitude
relative to the size of the property. He pointed out that the current plans
show no access from the building to the dumpster, or from the commercial space
to the building. He urged the Council not to vote on the matter until everyone
has an opportunity to examine a final plan.
President Holland reminded the neighbors that there is still an arduous, long
process with the Design Review Board before the developer’s plans are
finalized. However, she pointed out that the Council has only to consider the
variations and special use permit at this time. She encouraged the neighbors to
continue to be involved.
Manager Williams remarked that Winnetka’s Zoning Code does not have GFA
requirement for commercial properties. Therefore, the Village has no tool other
than setback, height, etc. to control the development’s size. He also pointed
out again that the applicant’s first proposal for a significantly larger
building complied fully with the Village’s ordinances.
Attorney Janega commented on the proposed project relative to the Comprehensive
Plan, noting that its use is consistent with the C-1 commercial zoning and with
the Plan’s suggested development plans for both the subject property and the
area.
Mr. Friedland pointed out the lengths to which his client has gone to
accommodate both staff and the neighborhood. He reaffirmed his client’s comfort
level with the original, fully compliant building which could be built “as of
right,” and would not have triggered any of the process that they have gone
through thus far. He noted that from his client’s perspective, there is a
practical difficulty associated with the land that allows for appropriate
modifications through the variation process. He also stated that the project
would not be viable if the number of residential units was reduced.
Trustee Woodbury expressed disappointment at not seeing revised elevations. He
commented that any development ultimately will impact and evoke changes in the
neighborhood. However, he pointed out that both the Council and the developer
have to work within the context of the existing Zoning Ordinance and that the
process has resulted in a better building than the original “of right”
scenario. He said that he believes that all of the technical problems will be
solved in due course by the developer and urged the Design Review Board to be
very firm with the applicant in order to get the best end product available.
John Higgins, 534 Meadow Rd. and Doris Wolf, 91 High Street, addressed concerns
about increased traffic and traffic and parking enforcement. Ms. Wolf asked who
would monitor the open parking spaces.
Ms. Janega suggested that the building tenants may be able to monitor the
spaces sufficiently on their own, and Manager Williams mentioned that if they
are not, they could contract with the Police Department to monitor the area.
President Holland told Mr. Friedland that it was Council’s expectation that all
eight outdoor parking spaces would be dedicated to open parking during business
hours.
Manager Williams pointed out that if the Village requires that all eight spaces
be open, residents could buy ‘C’ passes to enable them to use the nearby
commuter lots during the day, thereby, transferring part of the parking burden
to the Village.
Trustee Powell remarked that while he would like to see more spaces allocated
for general use, he was inclined to support the developer because he does not
visualize that the commercial space will draw the kind of business with rapid
turnover.
Trustee Woodbury countered that he would prefer to err on the side of having
more spaces dedicated to general use first because that could be corrected at a
later time. If the spaces are deeded to residents, then they are gone, and
other users are thrown onto the street or into the Village lots.
President Holland directed Council’s attention back to the planting strip. She
noted that the more of the four feet that is used for the planting strip, the
less there will be available for the sidewalk.
Attorney Janega suggested that the Village require no more than a two foot
planting strip, because the wider setback was requested for the purpose of
widening the sidewalk. There was general consensus among the Trustees that a
planting strip of up to three feet would be acceptable.
President Holland called for a motion to amend Ordinance M-16-2000 to reflect
the revisions discussed and imposition of certain conditions, including the
allocation of the eight outdoor parking spaces for general use with restricted
parking limits.
Mr. Friedland asked to be heard again on the topic of the parking spaces. He
strongly objected to the outdoor parking restrictions, pointing out that the
Zoning Ordinance does not require that parking spaces be provided for
commercial space. He said that this would present a tremendous problem for the
developer.
Trustee Aquilino commented that the residential requirement is, in fact, 2.25
spaces per unit, which the draft ordinance amends to two (2). She said that she
has a problem reducing the requirement at all and that the proposal seems like
a reasonable compromise.
Trustee Woodbury stood firm in his position that all eight of the parking
spaces have parking restrictions. He said that pushing parking into the
neighborhood was not acceptable.
Mr. Friedland responded that the restrictions would not be acceptable to the
developer. He pointed out that the Zoning Ordinance would allow for 10,000 sq.
ft. of commercial space to be built with no parking requirements whatsoever.
Mr. Saunders noted that the applicant's original parking and traffic study
projected peak parking needs of eight spaces for 2,000 sq. ft of commercial
space.
Since the parties could not come to agreement on the parking issue, President
Holland called for a motion to table the ordinance until the August 1st
meeting. Trustee Powell so moved. He was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury,
Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and Presser.
Ordinance No. M-19-2000 - Special Use Permit: North Shore Community Bank -
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed the request from North Shore Community
Bank and Trust Company for a Special Use Permit to allow five of the required
20 parking spaces to be located at street level. The proposal is to redevelop
the site at 576-582 Lincoln Avenue into a 2.5 story commercial building. The
plans comply with all zoning requirements. She noted that the current request
came to the Council with positive recommendations from the Zoning Board of
Appeals, Plan Commission and Design Review Board.
Manager Williams provided an historical perspective. He reminded the Council
that in April 1999, the applicant requested zoning relief and a Special Use
Permit for a similar facility that included street level parking and a drive-
through banking facility. At that time, the Council concurred with the
recommendations of the Plan Commission and Zoning Board of appeals and denied
the drive-through banking facility. The Council then did not proceed with
consideration of the street-level parking request. He pointed out that one of
the key aspects to both the prior and this proposal is access to the proposed
underground parking, which the applicant is proposing be achieved by the use of
Village owned land.
Mr. Williams asked Mr. Saunders to provide an overview of future plans for the
area and comment from a technical point of view on how the Bank's proposal
might impact those plans. Mr. Saunders briefly described the potential future
development of a parking structure in the Lincoln Avenue parking lot and
addressed some of the difficulties that would have to be addressed in order to
interface the two developments.
Finally, Mr. Williams pointed out the three issues that need to be addressed:
(1) this specific zoning and special use permit proposed; (2) a policy decision
about the private use of public land; and (3) how the proposed project will
overlay with some sort of possible future parking structure.
Randy Hibben, President of the NSCB, commented on the Bank's process to date,
and John Myefski, the architect, reviewed the elevations and discussed the
proposed parking and access.
Trustee Aquilino asked why the proposed access to the development is from the
north rather than from the west. Mr. Myefski explained that there is not enough
distance to ramp down.
Mr. Williams noted that last year the Bank had proposed three separate
scenarios with the access from the west. Mr. Myefski responded that regardless
of the direction of the access, the Bank would need to use Village property.
Trustee Aquilino expressed concern about the impact of development on the area
in general and the consequences of any decisions made on future development in
the area.
Trustee Powell remarked that he continues to find it objectionable to use
public property as proposed. He said he would like to see the bank use its own
property.
Mr. Hibben commented that the Bank has already spent a substantial amount of
time on this project and that their alternative is to build a one story
building which would not require any parking.
Manager Williams said that there was still a lot of technical work to be done
on the access design, as well as on some kind of access agreement. He pointed
out that any additional costs incurred by the Village in connection with
construction of a parking facility as a result of special engineering
requirements, would need to be transferred to the Bank. He also reiterated the
Village's strong preference for a plan that provides access from the west.
Gus Knight, 598 Lincoln, commented on the project. He expressed no particular
objection to the Bank and complimented them on their overall design. He did,
however, opine that the Village needs to study the entire area with a
particular eye toward resolving the ongoing parking problems before approving
any additional development. He suggested that the Village buy the property in
question in order to facilitate the future construction of a parking facility.
A copy of Mr. Knight's comments is attached as Attachment #1.
Bill Ensing, the attorney for Glenn Weaver, 574 Lincoln Ave., cautioned the
Council against overdevelopment of the area and the resulting impact of
increased traffic flow. He voiced his client's belief that the narrow alleyway
will become a high volume thoroughfare to the parking facility in the back and
have a significant negative impact on the value of his client's property. He
also expressed his opinion that the proposal constitutes a taking of Mr.
Weaver’s easement for the benefit of the Bank.
Leslie Bullock, 612 Lincoln, a resident and local business owner, spoke about
the current struggles of the merchants with construction and parking. She
expressed concern about the impact of ongoing construction and increased
traffic.
Andy Bullock, 612 Lincoln, President of the Lincoln Avenue Townhome
Association, expressed support for anything that would alleviate the demand for
parking in the Lincoln Avenue lot. He asked that, if the Bank's proposal is
approved, the five on grade parking spaces should not be restricted use spaces,
but should be available for general use. He also reiterated opposition to the
future development of any kind of drive-through facility.
President Holland called for a motion to introduce the ordinance and table it
until the second meeting in September. She noted that there remain a number of
major, fundamental issues to be thoroughly studied and resolved before Council
can take any kind of final action. She advised the applicant that because of
the enormity of the issues involved, the Council would proceed deliberately and
cautiously, with full disclosure to the community.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved that the ordinance be
introduced and tabled until the September 19th regular meeting. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Brower,
and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and Presser.
VIII. Old Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1181 and 1182. Trustee Brower moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1181 in the total amount of $598,921.71 and Warrant List No. 1182 in the
total amount of $319,538.12. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Brower, Aquilino, Powell,
and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and Presser.
Electric Power Supply Study. Manager Williams gave a brief overview of the
Village’s contract with the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency (IMEA) to
provide wholesale electric power for the Village. He noted that the contract
expires in February 2006, with an automatic renewal unless a five-year formal
notice of termination is given. He noted that as a result of staff’s
recommendation that a consultant be retained to assist in determining whether
the Village should terminate its current contract and pursue other available
options or allow the contract to renew itself, RFP’s had been sent to
consulting firms. Of the responses received, staff recommended the project be
awarded to Burns-McDonnell based on their technical qualifications and Midwest
experience.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to authorize Burns-
McDonnell to perform an electric power supply study for a fee of $29,500. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and Presser.
X. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Aquilino reported that she and Trustee Brower had
attended the most recent Chamber of Commerce meeting where the major topic of
discussion was the ongoing parking problems in the Village.
Trustee Woodbury reported that the Design Guidelines Committee was continuing
to work on its recommendation of a consultant and hoped to have one to the
Council in the near future.
Village Manager. Manager Williams informed the Council that the Village would
be upgrading the sound system in the Council Chambers in the next couple of
months.
He also mentioned that in the fall, the Village will begin using the services
if IRMA, Intergovernmental Risk Management Agency, to handle Workers’
Compensation claim administration. He said that staff anticipates that this
move will improve communications with injured employees, facilitate the
monitoring of claims for the Village’s claims administrator, redistribute
responsibilities for handling claims and clarify procedures, and obtain better
purchasing power for medical services, excess insurance, etc.
Manager Williams reported that the Council’s Technical Committee had met and
concluded that it makes sense to retain a construction manager to assist with
the Village Yards project and that an RFP would be prepared in the near future.
He indicated that staff has recommended a change in architects to Sente & Rubel
and that Trustee Lien had agreed to review the construction contracts.
Finally, Mr. Williams provided a brief review of the Village’s undergrounding
and street reconstruction projects.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Woodbury seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Lien and
Presser. The meeting adjourned at 10:50 p.m.
______________________________________
Deputy Clerk
August 1, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved August 15, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
1, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, Stephen Presser and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: John Powell Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Chief of Police William Gallagher, Director
of Community Development Cindy McCammack and Director of Public Works Steve
Saunders, and 34 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
August 15, 2000, Regular Meeting. President Holland and Trustee Woodbury
indicated they would not be able to attend.
September 5, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
July 11, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Mr. John C. Wilkinson, 870 Sheridan Road, questioned the fee to residents for
undergrounding electrical service. He said that the amount is only $20, but on
principle, does not think that he should pay a penalty when the Village has
ordered this work to be done and asked for an explanation.
Manager Williams replied that at recent meetings, the Council had decided that
the customer has to pay for the pedestal and also for a permit fee that is set
by resolution. He said that staff has been asked to waive the fees but cannot
without a motion from the Council.
Mr. Wilkinson then asked that the Council waive the fee to residents.
President Holland thanked Mr. Wilkinson and said that the Council will discuss
the issue. She said that the direction the Council has been given by the
residents is to bury the lines by 2010, that the Village is 3 years ahead of
that deadline and the project should be completed by 2007. In response to Mr.
Wilkinson’s question as to why he has to buy a permit when the Village has
ordered the work to be done, President Holland said that it was the feeling of
the Council that the $40 permit fee is not that terrible a burden for the
homeowners and offered to provide documentation of the discussions on this
issue. She also told Mr. Wilkinson that he would be notified when this item
would be scheduled for further discussion by the Council.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-16-2000 – Special Use Permit and Variances: 518 Winnetka Avenue
- Adoption.
President Holland explained that this item had been tabled at the July 18th
meeting of the Council and asked that a motion be made to remove it from the
table. Trustee Presser moved to remove the 518 Winnetka Avenue request for
special use permit and variances from the table, seconded by Trustee Aquilino.
Village Attorney Janega explained the additional revisions to the ordinance,
which reflect discussions held at the last Council meeting and read aloud the
new language for each of the modifications.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the amendments
in the revised draft of the ordinance. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
Trustees Woodbury and Brower asked Village Attorney Janega several questions
about the review of the Design Review Board and the development process.
Ms. Janega replied that the project would go before the Design Review Board and
then return to the Council. She said the development agreement would be the
next hurdle and would address how the work progresses. She said that there is
rarely a time when the Council has the final, complete development package to
review at one time and that the Council has the authority to approve the final
design. She explained that the unusual thing about this case is that it went to
the Design Review Board first because the initial plan did not require any
zoning relief from the Zoning Board of Appeals.
President Holland stated that the Council had received a letter from Barbara
Hull that questioned if there was a conflict of interest in this matter because
Mr. Friedland was with the law firm of Schiff Hardin and Waite, which has been
retained to represent the Village in a different matter.
Village Attorney Janega responded by saying that as part of retaining Schiff
Hardin and Waite and Attorney Jim Clark, the Village had received a letter from
the law firm requesting that the Village sign a conflict waiver. She read the
letter that set forth the nature of the potential conflict and contained
assurances that confidentiality would be maintained. After reviewing the letter
and discussing it with Manager Williams, she advised him to sign it. She
further stated that the two matters are totally unrelated and there had been no
disclosures made to Mr. Clark regarding the 518 Winnetka Avenue matter.
President Holland added that prior to this, Mr. Clark had represented the
Village in other matters when Dean Cameron was Village Attorney. She said that,
from the Council’s standpoint, there is no conflict.
Mr. Friedland addressed the Council on behalf of Paul Hardej and said that
after hearing Ms. Janega’s changes to the ordinance, he feels the ordinance
strikes a fair compromise.
Mr. Al Day , 85 High Street, commented on Trustee Aquilino’s suggestion at a
previous meeting to close Brier Street and stated that his concern is that the
original 2020 plan had suggested demolishing homes and putting in a shopping
area at this location. Mr. Day said that although he still thinks this project
is too big for the area, he appreciates what the Board does and would like them
to consider the neighborhoods.
Ms. Aquilino clarified that she had only suggested a small barricade, not a
shopping area.
Mr. Brower said that he received a package from Mr. Clark, 555 Meadow,
containing a letter about traffic enforcement in the area. He said Mr. Clark
asked that it be looked at to make sure that the Council is doing everything
they can for traffic enforcement.
Manager Williams said that he has met with the neighbors from the area and the
Chief will be sending monthly reports to them on the traffic.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended, permitting variations
from building height, setback and parking space requirements and granting a
special use permit to allow the construction of a multi-use building. Trustee
Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Powell.
Resolution No. R-27-2000 – MFT Resolution: Pine Street Bridge – Adoption.
Steve Saunders, Director of Public Works, explained that this resolution
authorizes the expenditure of $45,000 of Motor Fuel Tax funds for Phase I
Engineering for the Pine Street Bridge. He said that this amount represents the
remainder of the $57,000 total for this phase of the project and IDOT requires
that the Village pay the full amount of the contract to the consultant, 80% of
which they will then reimburse.
Trustee Lien moved to adopt Resolution No. R-27-2000 authorizing $45,000 of
Motor Fuel Tax funds to be paid for Phase I Engineering for the Pine Street
Bridge, seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the resolution passed.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes: None.
Absent: Trustee Powell.
VIII. Old Business.
None.
IX. New Business
Resident Concern About Trucks on Willow Road.
Village Manager Williams explained that the Village Council had received a
request from Linda Kramer, 370 Glendale, to discuss possible solutions to
increased truck traffic on Willow Road. He said that the Police Department has
been enforcing the laws this summer and staff has outlined some options with
corresponding pros and cons.
Linda Kramer, 370 Glendale, a resident for 35 years, has observed along with
her neighbors that the truck traffic has become extremely difficult on Willow
Road. She listed concerns about safety for the children going to Crow Island
School, the heavier weight of trucks allowed by IDOT on Willow Road, the noise
and vibration from the trucks, and the increased amount of truck traffic. She
said her neighbors have signed petitions listing the same concerns and
presented them to President Holland. She asked how some of the heavy traffic
could be eliminated and suggested working with the other communities to reach a
solution. She acknowledged that the problem is complicated and said there were
other people in the audience who would like to speak.
President Holland replied that she knows that it is a very complicated matter.
She said that she, as a resident, is also frustrated. She explained that the
Council could discuss it at a study session and that she could also mention it
at the meetings she attends with other communities’ trustees. She asked Steve
Saunders, Director of Public Works, to review the options that staff had
prepared.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the pros and cons of the four options staff had prepared:
assigning permitted truck routes, banning through truck traffic on individual
streets, developing a regional strategy to control through truck traffic by
working with other communities, and enforcing existing speed limits and traffic
control devices.
A short discussion followed regarding the Village’s control over portions of
Willow Road and alternate routes, such as Sheridan Road. Manager Williams
indicated that one possibility was to train police officers to weigh trucks in
the areas under the Village’s jurisdiction.
Mr. Saunders said that the Village could talk to IDOT about the traffic on
Willow Road and relay the comments of Mrs. Kramer and the neighbors.
Chief Gallagher then addressed the Council and explained that he had discussed
the problem of construction traffic with Kenilworth, Wilmette and Glencoe
police chiefs who were not concerned about addressing the issue. He said that
he assumes that drivers are choosing not to use Lake Avenue because of the back
up at Green Bay Road and that rerouting the trucks simply would move them to
other local streets.
Chief Gallagher continued to say that he had discussed training for overweight
trucks with the Northfield police, but it has been their experience that when
they have stopped trucks coming from Winnetka, they are not finding them to be
overweight. He said that Northfield does have their own scale and qualified
officers who would be willing to work with our officers. He also said that it
would mostly be construction vehicles that would be overweight, not ordinary
delivery trucks, and that Winnetka doesn’t have enough truck traffic to warrant
overweight training.
Mrs. Rogal, 1143 Willow Road, expressed her concern about the speed of the
trucks as they travel Willow Road causing her house and her neighbors’ houses
to shake. She requested that speed limit signs be cleared of trees, etc., so
they can be seen. She complained that nothing is being done about the speed of
the trucks.
Karen Templeton, 889 Willow Road, said that one Saturday morning when children
were playing in the neighborhood, there was at least one truck every 5 minutes
going 40 miles an hour and on that occasion, the residents stood in the middle
of the street to slow them down. She asked that the enforcement be stepped up
and suggested that the Village put a double yellow line down Willow Road to
prevent passing.
Greg Swenson, 808 Willow, said he felt that he made a mistake buying a home on
Willow Road and invited the trustees to spend an afternoon in his yard on a
lawn chair. He said that the noise is incredible and the shaking in the house
is more dramatic than what would be expected on a residential road. He said
there are no stop signs to slow down the speeding cars and trucks and suggested
that enforcement from Provident to Green Bay may help alleviate the problem.
Chris Shea, 365 Birch, asked that the Council look at short term and long term
problems and solutions. He said that enforcement of the speed limit could be an
immediate decision.
Trustee Woodbury also asked Mr. Saunders to look into traffic calming
techniques as part of the future discussion.
President Holland said that the Council would discuss this issue at a study
session later in the fall and all names on the petition would be contacted when
this item is next on the agenda.
Go-peds.
Chief Gallagher explained that Manager Williams had asked his office to look
into regulations pertaining to go-peds (motorized scooters) in response to a
request from Diana Tucker, 1082 Elm Street, to discuss permitting their use on
public sidewalks.
Chief Gallagher said that any time there is a motor put on a vehicle, it then
falls under the Illinois Vehicle Code and that there is also a Village
ordinance prohibiting motorized vehicles on sidewalks. His direction to the
police officers on the use of these vehicles has been to give warnings
initially, but then tickets may be written for subsequent offenses. He has
talked to surrounding communities and they are taking the same approach. He
reported that Schaumburg is discussing an ordinance to prohibit them from bike
paths and sidewalks. He has checked with his staff, who reported that a number
of people have asked about the use of go-peds and those people have been
advised that using them on public property is illegal.
Diana Tucker, 1082 Elm Street, showed the Council what a motorized go-ped is.
She explained that it has a motor that is similar to a lawnmower’s. She said
that when calling the Police Department, they were told a number of different
things and that their purpose is to find a way for the children to ride the go-
peds safely. She has talked to Dan Newport of the Park District, who said he is
open to proposals and that there might be some parking lots available to them.
She indicated that the families are willing to send the boys to a school to
learn rules for safety, but would like them to have a place to ride the go-
peds.
Shane Tucker, 1082 Elm Street, Alex Berghorst, 12 Kent Road, Greg Van Zuiden,
474 Berkeley, and Mark Shelton, 1340 Westmoor Trail, presented their comments
to the Council. They each reported that when contacting the Police Department
they were told that it was okay to ride the go-peds, but had experienced or
knew several people who had been stopped by the police and told it was illegal.
They suggested a school to learn rules for safety and where they would be
issued a permit to ride the go-peds. They also described features of the go-
peds regarding speed and stopping. They requested that the Village find
somewhere safe and legal for them to use the go-peds.
President Holland said in response that the Council understands the effort the
boys have made to work to pay for the go-peds, but there are two sides to the
argument. She explained the potential liability of the Village in suits for
injuries sustained by persons who fall on a public sidewalk or public way, not
necessarily to the person riding the go-ped but to other pedestrians who may be
run into accidentally. She then explained that the Village might not be able to
disclaim liability and suggested that the boys write a letter to their State
representative to see if they could change the law. She said that the Council
would speak with the Park District to see if there is any place that they can
be used that is safe and that Village liability is not an issue.
Manager Williams said that he would contact Mrs. Tucker with any information
collected.
Request from French Institute – Policy Direction.
Village Manager Williams explained the request from the French Institute of the
North Shore to obtain the Council’s approval to close a portion of Green Bay
Road from Spruce to Elm on Sunday, September 17, from 2:00 – 7:00 p.m. to hold
a grand opening celebration. He said that there is a resolution that IDOT would
require the Village to adopt that transfers all liability to the Village. He
said that staff recommends that if the Council agrees to do this, that all of
Green Bay Road be closed. The Village Council has been asked to provide policy
direction on the liability issue.
Maria Kurt, Director of the French Institute of the North Shore, explained that
she had purchased the building at 562 Green Bay Road and had transformed an
eyesore into a beautiful structure. She asked to be allowed to celebrate with a
grand opening where an estimated 200 past and present students, the Chamber of
Commerce members and others would attend. She proposed closing a portion or all
of Green Bay Road for the celebration and will provide a $5 million insurance
policy for liability.
Barbara Wood, and Maureen Ryan, both students of Ms. Kurt’s, recommended that
the Council view the building and approve the closing of the street for the
opening.
Trustee Brower asked if the liability problems can be resolved.
Ms. Janega responded by saying that the Council would have to adopt a formal
resolution to allow the road to be closed and that the form required by the
State would make it difficult for the Village to shift liability to the person
holding the event. She said the staff recommendation that all of the street be
closed reduces the potential for liability, but she is concerned that there is
no way to assess the exposure to liability and that $5 million in insurance
probably would not be enough. She said that the other concern is that the
Council would be setting a precedent and reminded the Council that the Village
had turned down similar requests in the past.
Manager Williams said that moving the traffic to Spruce Street and Elm Street
does keep people a distance away and that the Village closes Green Bay Road for
brief ceremonies on Veteran’s Day. He said that the Village attorney’s job is
to tell the Council the risks, but only the Council members can make the
decision.
President Holland and Trustee Presser then asked Ms. Kurt if there was another
location to hold the event or if she would consider closing the road for the
ribbon cutting ceremony only and hold the rest of the event in nearby parking
lots.
Ms. Kurt said that if the ribbon cutting ceremony was held in another location
the effect would be lost and she thought that holding the event in nearby
parking lots would create more of a traffic problem along Green Bay Road for
the pedestrians.
Trustee Aquilino said that she is very concerned about the liability and that 5
hours is a long time to shut down Green Bay Road.
Ms. Kurt replied that the road had been closed for the pet parade and traffic
was allowed to come very close to the pedestrians at that time. She said that
because the French Institute is in the middle of the block it would be safer
for pedestrians because traffic would be kept farther away.
Trustee Lien indicated that he was willing to close Green Bay Road for a Sunday
afternoon.
President Holland asked if the Council was prepared to vote on the policy
direction.
Trustee Lien moved to close the road, seconded by Trustee Brower.
President Holland asked for any further discussion. She asked Ms. Kurt if any
damage to the street would be taken care of by the French Institute.
Ms. Kurt indicated that it would and said that the tent would not be attached
to the street, but held down with weights and sandbags.
Trustee Presser asked that the Council discuss this in executive session before
voting on it because of the potential for liablity.
Trustee Lien withdrew the motion to close the road, and Trustee Brower withdrew
his second. The Council unanimously agreed to discuss the matter in executive
session after the regular meeting. President Holland told Ms. Kurt that the
Council would go into executive session to discuss this further and would
reconvene to vote and announce their decision.
Zoning Variation – 889 Sheridan Road – Policy Direction.
Cindy McCammack presented a request from Patrick O’Neil to subdivide the
property at 889 Sheridan Road into two lots, one of which requires variances
from the lot area, rectangular area and lot width requirements of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance. Ms. McCammack summarized a history of the advisory boards’
actions and asked that the Village Council provide policy direction.
Trustee Aquilino asked if the front yard of the existing house is on Tower
Road, if it had always been the front yard, and if it was in compliance with
the Zoning Ordinance requirements.
Village Attorney Janega replied that the current front yard is on Sheridan
Road, but there is a second front yard, or corner yard, on Tower Road because
it’s a corner lot. She stated that there is a nonconforming setback on Tower
Road and that, if the property is subdivided as proposed, Lot 2 will remain on
Sheridan Road, and Lot 1 will have a front yard on Tower Road.
Trustees Aquilino and Woodbury asked further questions about the calculations
for the requirements of both lots and if they would result in a buildable lot.
Ms. McCammack replied that there would be a buildable lot.
Village Attorney Janega responded that this is the first corner lot subdivision
since the code was amended in January, 1999. She said the history of that
ordinance is that there had been a subdivision at Linden and Ash which resulted
in two very square shaped lots instead of the traditional rectangular lots and
a subsequent similar request by the Cummins Group was denied because it
required taking some land from an adjacent lot. She said that staff found that
there were at least 200 other lots in Winnetka that would lend themselves to
this type of subdivision which, would create the same problems as flaglots and
would have a single-house block face, with one house oriented differently from
the other houses on the block. Ms. Janega explained that the interior lot
minimum requirements for this zoning district is 24,000 square feet and the
corner lot requirement is 28,000 square feet. She said that this proposal seeks
to apply the interior lot requirement to the corner lot. She also said that, as
it exists now, the side lot line is along the north property line, but if the
subdivision is granted, there is a side lot line for Lot 1 that will abut the
rear lot line for Lot 2, and the rear lot line of Lot 1 would abut the side lot
line of the property to the north, which would be contrary to the Subdivision
Ordinance.
Ms. Janega said she researched other subdivisions for precedents and found that
the only lot width variance was for the Radcliffe subdivision, which divided a
100-foot lot into two 50 foot lots. She said this was granted because every
other house on that block was on a 50-foot lot. She cited the Hahn subdivision
as another example, in that it was a trapezoidal lot and the requested
subdivision aggravated an existing non-perpendicular line. In that case, the
Council required that the buildable area be limited so that it assured that any
development of the lot would fall within the requirements. She said that this
could also be done in this case, should the Council decide to grant this
subdivision. Ms. Janega suggested that the Council also consider whether
granting this subdivision will set precedent for other similar requests in that
if the increased lot size requirement for a corner lot is disregarded, anyone
could identify several properties in the immediate area that they could attempt
to subdivide and claim that a precedent had been set on those types of lots.
She also cautioned that the 12-foot setback on Lot 1 would need to be verified
if the Council wishes to grant the subdivision.
Manager Williams pointed out that this is a significant issue to be decided. He
reminded the Council that lot area requirements were increased to prevent
subdivisions because of the many lots in Winnetka that could be subdivided. He
said that if the Council grants the subdivision, they need to ascertain if this
would be a true exception to the ordinance passed in 1999. He suggested that
one way to look at it is that, although Lot 2 is less than required; if you
look at the total lot area of 60,000 square feet, there is certainly enough lot
area for two lots; and, that Mr. O’Neill is renovating an existing house to
retain in the Village. He said that the home is located next to the power plant
and that may make the property unique, but this case could set the tone for
future subdivision requests.
Patrick O’Neill, Robsart Avenue, Kenilworth, explained that he has an acre and
a half on this property and could build smaller houses on the subdivided lots,
but wants to preserve the existing house. He said that in order to reduce the
size of the existing house by 6,000 square feet, he needs to sell off the other
part of the lot. He said he has hired an architect who has designed many houses
on the north shore in order to preserve the character of the neighborhood. He
said that the existing home is not one of the nicest homes in Winnetka, but it
has great qualities that he is planning to restore, although much of it would
be demolished. Mr. O’Neill emphasized that his plan would make the houses look
more in character with the neighborhood than the existing house does. He said
that he feels strongly that he will be improving this lot so that it fits in
better with the neighborhood and asked that the Council consider this when
making their decision.
Manager Williams referred to the Hahn subdivision and suggested that the
Council put in a covenant that the house could not be built larger than 5000
square feet so that it would comply.
Trustee Lien commented that he was on the Council when they voted on the side
yards and said that he thought the concern was the subdivision of the corner
lots. He said that he would favor the subdivision if he had an assurance that
the house on Lot 2 was less than 7500 square feet and said that he does not see
a violation of the side lot ordinance if this could be done. He said that if
Mr. O’Neill could come back with this assurance, he would support the
subdivision.
Trustee Aquilino said that she does not support a nonconforming new
subdivision. She said that the lot itself would be nonconforming.
Keith Shay, 857 Sheridan, and neighbor of Mr. O’Neill’s subdivision, said in
this particular case, what he has heard is that a prospective buyer has bought
a house with the idea of changing it and selling off property to a builder to
create a new lot that doesn’t meet zoning requirements and requires variations.
He also has heard Mr. O’Neill say that to make it financially possible to
restore the existing house, he needs to sell the second lot. Mr. Shay said he
believed this to be a corruption of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, that
granting the variations does not appear to be necessary or required by law, and
asked that the Council refuse the request for subdivision and reject the
request for variations.
Mr. Pinkerton, 333 Willow Road, said that this request presented another
example of the flaw in the zoning lot width requirements of our ordinance. He
submitted his written comments to the Council on the issue of calculations for
setbacks and average lot width to be attached to this meeting’s minutes. They
are attached at Attachment #1.
President Holland responded to Mr. Pinkerton’s comments by saying that the
Council will be discussing average lot width in the next study session and Mr.
Pinkerton will be notified of that meeting.
Nan Greenough, Plan Commission member, expressed her support for the
subdivision. She said she has been driving past this home for years and it is
out of character with the neighborhood. Economically, she said that she could
not see that anyone would buy the property and rebuild, but was encouraged when
she thought it might be subdivided and enthusiastic because she saw it as a
potential good. She believes the changes to the subdivision ordinance have been
successful because no one has come forward requesting a subdivision like the
Melanie subdivision, however, she does not see any similarity between this
subdivision and Melanie. She said that because of the surrounding properties,
this is not a normal corner lot and that the Council could not find another
subdivision like this in the Village. She hoped that the Council would look at
this request favorably.
Manager Williams commented that his recollection is that the Plan Commission
had boards showing all of the large lots in town that they were concerned could
potentially be subdivided and that it was not just the Melanie subdivision that
caused changes in the subdivision ordinance.
In response to Trustee Aquilino’s question about property lines, Mr. O’Neill
said that he has hired structural engineers to build up the lot and the ravine,
putting soil into it and adding more yard.
Trustee Woodbury said that a map did exist of the larger lots in town and he
was struck by the number that could be subdivided. He said he is concerned
about the minimum lot size, and creating another irregularly shaped lot from an
existing irregularly shaped lot. He said he would like to consider further the
issue of setting precedent and also explore whether there is a conforming
alternative before setting policy.
President Holland suggested tabling this item until the August 15 meeting since
Mr. Woodbury did not feel prepared to vote. She explained to Mr. O’Neill that
he needs a vote of 4 from the Council because there was no favorable
recommendation from the Plan Commission.
Flood Plain Variation – Winnetka Presbyterian Church – Public Hearing.
President Holland opened the public hearing at 10:10 p.m.
Steve Saunders explained that the Winnetka Presbyterian Church had requested a
one-foot variation from the floodplain restrictions in the Winnetka Village
Code. The variation does not affect the requirements of the National Flood
Insurance Program, and the Village Council has been asked to evaluate the issue
and hold a public hearing on the application for variance.
Mr. Saunders said that the concrete wall of the Church and the front door
threshold would have be to elevated one foot above the existing floor level in
order to comply with the Village Code, however, it meets the requirements of
the National Flood Insurance Program. He then read the findings required by the
Village Code that must be met to grant the variance.
Trustee Aquilino asked if the ordinance had changed since the Church was
originally built.
Mr. Saunders replied that it had and that it is most likely the Church was in
compliance at the time.
Mr. Chris Mekus, Mekus Studios, Chicago, concurred with Mr. Saunders findings
and addressed the hardship and uniqueness issues. He said that an exceptional
hardship would be created due to the substantial ramping that would be
necessary to connect the floor levels. He referred to a floor plan to
demonstrate the placement of ramps and the flow from the new addition to the
existing rooms in the Church. He explained that ramps would also require
handrails, would have to extend 12 feet into the space, would reduce the 8’6”
ceiling height and create an inhabitable space. He said that the requested
variation would allow the floors and ceiling in the existing building to be at
the same level as the new addition.
Jack Chapin, a church member who lives in Wilmette, explained that the Church
is higher than the roads that run by it and also Skokie Playfield across the
street from the Church. He said that the worst condition that could happen to
the church is that the cement slab would get wet. He said he had been a member
there for 35 years and has never seen a water problem around the church.
President Holland asked for public comment on the request.
Kathy Stauffer, 1255 Ash, who lives north of the Church, said she is very
concerned about the compensatory storage. She said that although it has been
said that the Church itself will act as compensatory storage, she is not sure
that that is right, and believes it will cause problems for all of the houses
in that area because the water will run to the lower ground. She asked to be
notified when this item is on the agenda again.
Mr. Mekus replied that the issue tonight is not compensatory storage, but a
request for a flood elevation level variance. He said that they are providing
compensatory storage, but are not prepared to discuss it tonight.
Rachelle Brisk, 1215 Willow Road, lives east of the parking lot, which is 6
feet from her house. She said that she doesn’t get water in her house now, but
does have water standing in her yard when there is a rain. She is very
concerned that she will begin to get water in her house and would like to be
kept appraised of the meetings.
There being no further comments, President Holland closed the public hearing at
10:30 p.m.
President Holland asked the Trustees if they would like a resolution prepared
for the variation. It was the sense of the Council that the conditions for a
variance had been met and, by voice vote, the Trustees agreed to have the
Village Attorney prepare a resolution for the variation.
Warrant Lists Nos. 1183 and 1184. After commenting on a few of the items on the
warrant lists, Trustee Brower moved to approve Warrant List No. 1183 in the
total amount of $969,771.97 and Warrant List No. 1184 in the total amount of
$362,862.30. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Aquilino, Brower, Presser,
Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell
Certificate of Appropriateness – Hadley School.
Manager Williams presented a request by the Hadley School for the Blind to
allow the placement of 72” x 30” banners on Village light poles along Green Bay
Road between Elm and Oak Streets for six weeks in September and October to
commemorate the school’s 80th anniversary.
After a short discussion and with the agreement of the representative from the
Hadley School who was present, Trustee Brower moved to approve the request for
a total of 4 weeks instead of 6 weeks, seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll
call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees: Aquilino, Brower, Presser,
Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell
Change Order – Tower Road Engineering.
Steve Saunders, Director of Public Works, explained that at the Council’s June
6, 2000, meeting a contract for engineering services was awarded to Oates
Associates for the Tower Road Rehabilitation in an amount not to exceed
$63,120. He further explained that a math error had been discovered and the not
to exceed amount should have been $66,410. He asked that the Village Council
consider revising the approved amount for the Tower Road Engineering Contract
to a total not to exceed figure of $66, 410.00.
Trustee Presser moved to approve the amended change order, seconded by Trustee
Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motions passed. Ayes: Trustees: Aquilino,
Brower, Presser, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell
DK Recycling Report.
Village Manager Williams reported on the monthly DK Recycling operations and
noted that DK Recycling had forwarded a copy of a letter from the Illinois
Environmental Protection Agency reporting that an inspection of the recycling
site and its operating permit revealed no violations and was in compliance with
the requirements of the IEPA.
X. Reports
Village President.
President Holland report that Village Forester, Jim Stier, has been working
with the Electric Department to screen the large transformers where they are
undergrounding electric lines and she thanked him for working so diligently.
Village Manager.
Manager Williams reported that the Cedar Street sanitary sewer problem has been
resolved by Mr. Saunders and the Village will be installing a new sanitary
sewer main to service the three houses at this location.
Leaf Blower Enforcement – June 2000.
Village Manager Williams reported that there were 13 complaints of violations
of the leafblower ordinance and officers issued 9 citations in response, found
one complaint to be unfounded and that the alleged violators were gone in 3 of
the instances. Officers issued 32 additional citations upon observation of
violators during June.
CATV Consultant RFP.
Village Manager Williams reported that the current cable TV franchise is
renewable April 5, 2003 and that AT & T had contacted the Village recently to
begin negotiations. He said the Village had also received a request from
another cable TV company regarding the possibility of installing a second cable
TV system in the Village. Manager Williams explained that there are complicated
technical and legal issues to be considered and staff will be requesting
proposals from qualified consultants to assist the Village and will also be
contacting neighboring Villages to determine the feasibility of a regional
approach.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Brower moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Aquilino, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Powell. The Council convened into Executive Session at 11:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:45 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino Brower, Lien, Woodbury, and Presser. Absent: Trustee
Powell. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega
Request of the French Institute of the North Shore.
Trustee Presser moved to deny the request of the French Institute to close a
portion or all of Green Bay Road to hold a grand opening celebration, seconded
by Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion to deny was passed. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Presser, and Woodbury. Nayes: Trustee Lien. Absent:
Trustee Powell.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at
11:50 p.m.
         _______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
August 15, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved September 5, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday,
August 15, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem John Lien called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Powell, and Stephen Presser.
Absent: President Louise Holland and Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of
Community Development Cindy McCammack, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, and 15 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Lien led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
September 5, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Presser, indicated that they expected to be able to attend.
September 12, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to be able to attend.
V. Approval of Minutes
July 18, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
August 1, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Linda Kramer, 370 Glendale, reiterated concerns expressed at the August 1st
meeting about truck traffic on Willow Road. In particular she mentioned her
worry that many of the trucks are overweight, shaking houses and causing cracks
in walls and foundations. She urged the Village to work diligently with
neighboring communities to arrive at a comprehensive plan to control the
problem.
Mr. Saunders repeated his comments at the last meeting saying that the Village
cannot take any unilateral action because Willow Road is under IDOT
jurisdiction.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. MC-3-2000 - Floodplain Ordinance Amendments - Introduction. Mr.
Saunders explained that the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) has
issued updated maps of floodplain areas within the Village. Therefore, in order
to maintain its eligibility for the National Flood Insurance Program, the
Village is required to modify its Flood Hazard Area regulations, contained in
Chapter 15.68 of the Village Code. The modifications are necessary to replace
references to the prior Flood Insurance Study and Flood Insurance Rate Maps
with references to the new Study and Maps. The modifications also correct
references to certain state agencies that have undergone reorganizations and
name changes. He indicated that there are no regulatory impacts of this
ordinance modification and noted that the Village began enforcing the new base
flood elevations on June 1, 2000.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-03-2000, modifying definitions, map references and agency names in Chapter
15.68 of the Village Code - Flood Hazard Areas. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-20-2000 - Consolidation and Zoning Variations: 680-690 Elder
Lane - Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed a request for variations from
Building Height and Rear Yard provisions by the contract purchaser/owner of the
two above-mentioned properties who wishes to consolidate them. She explained
that the applicant wishes to convert the residence at 680 Elder Lane, which was
originally the coach house of 690 Elder, into a detached three-car garage and
alter portions of the existing 690 Elder residence to permit a one-story
kitchen addition with an open front porch. The former requires a variation from
the building height limitations to allow an existing accessory building to
maintain its existing height of 23 feet and 2 stories, which exceeds the
accessory building height limitations of 14 feet and 1 story. The latter
requires a variation from the rear yard setback requirements to allow the
existing single-family residence and attached wood deck to encroach upon the
rear yard. She also reviewed the recommendations of the Plan Commission and the
Zoning Board of Appeals, which both recommended approval of the zoning
variations.
Manager Williams clarified that the applicant is not presently the owner of the
second lot, but is trying to acquire it. He pointed out that: (1) both parcels
were originally a single lot and the coach house was designed and constructed
in the same architectural character and detailing as the main house; (2) the
nonconformities created by the proposed consolidation result from existing
structures that predate the Village's Zoning Ordinance and from the prior
subdivision of the subject property into parcel; and (3) the proposed
consolidation will restore the subject property to its original configuration
thus eliminating an undesirable flag lot. He also explained that the rear yard
variance is required because the orientation of required yards to existing
buildings would be changed.
The property owner, Mark Haller, commented briefly on the history of the
property.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance
permitting a variation from the building height limitations of the Zoning
Ordinance to allow an existing accessory building to maintain a height in
excess of that allowed and a variation from the rear yard setback requirements
to allow the existing single-family residence and attached wood deck to
encroach upon the rear yard. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-21-2000 - Zoning Variation: 1155 Oak St. - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed a request from the Winnetka Public School Nursery for a
variance from the requirements of the Maximum Building Size and Building Height
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance. The requested variance would allow for the
construction of two replacement storage sheds in connection with the renovation
of an existing outdoor play area. The variation would increase the existing
nonconforming GFA, and the proposed new storage sheds would exceed the height
requirement for storage sheds.
Manager Williams pointed out that this is another example of the problem with
applying the standards for residential GFA to institutional properties within a
residential zone. He suggested that because this has occurred on a number of
occasions, the Council might want to consider amending the Zoning Ordinance on
this point at some time in the future.
Mark Evans, the landscape architect for the project was available to answer
questions about the proposal.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-22-2000 - Zoning Variation: 475 Ash St. - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed a request for a variance from the Maximum Building Size
requirements of the Zoning Ordinance in order to permit the reconstruction of a
porch. The applicants propose to replace an existing temporary one-story screen
porch, which is removed and re-installed annually with a permanent structure.
The replacement porch would be the same size, height, and in the same location
as the current temporary one. Ms. McCammack reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 5-1 to recommend approval of the request.
Attorney Janega called the Council's attention to paragraphs in the draft
ordinance regarding the criteria for variations that were taken directly from
the ZBA findings and conclusions. She indicated that she had not found any
additional supporting facts in the ZBA record.
The applicant, Bernard Sergesketter, commented on the proposal.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-23-2000 - Zoning Variation: 567 Hill Terrace - Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed an application for a variation from the Maximum Building
Size and Building Line Articulation provisions of the Zoning Ordinance in order
to permit the construction of a second story addition. She pointed out that in
calculating the new GFA, the applicant was not permitted an allowance for the
existing garage because no all of the structure is located within the rear
quarter of the lot as required to qualify for the allowance. Ms. McCammack
reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend approval
of the requests.
Ms. McCammack responded to questions from the Trustees and the applicant,
William Kernahan, was also in attendance.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-24-2000 - Zoning Variation: 446 Provident - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed an application for a variation from the Intensity of Use of
Lot, Side Yard, and Building Line Articulation provisions of the Zoning
Ordinance in order to permit the construction of first and second story
additions. She reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to
recommend approval of the requests. In response to a question from Trustee
Brower, she indicated that the requested increase applies to roofed lot
coverage and that the side yard and building line variations involve the
extension of the existing nonconforming building line.
Mike Klein, a representative of the builder, responded to a question about
structural changes from Trustee Powell.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-25-2000 - Zoning Variation: 275 Auburn - Introduction. Ms.
McCammack reviewed an application for a variation from the Building Line
Articulation provision of the Zoning Ordinance to permit construction of an
addition. The applicants propose to remove an existing addition before building
the new one. Ms. McCammack noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-
0 to recommend approval of the request.
The applicants, Harold and Betsy Rosenzweig, responded to questions from
Trustees Aquilino and Presser. They indicated that they had had no objections
from their neighbors about extending the existing wall.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
permitting the variation. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Resolution No. R-29-2000 - Presbyterian Church Floodplain Variation - Adoption.
Mr. Saunders reviewed a request from the Winnetka Presbyterian Church for
variance relief from certain provisions of the Village Code, specifically those
sections that require that non-residential buildings be structurally
floodproofed below the flood protection elevation. He noted that the relief
requested would still result in compliance with the minimum federally-mandated
requirements of the National Flood Insurance Program.
Ms. Janega summarized the resolution and hardship findings, saying that the
criteria are stated in the draft of the floodplain ordinance introduced
earlier. In reciting the findings, she noted that they establish the uniqueness
of the property and support the variance because they are related to the actual
use of the property, particularly because this is an institutional property.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the proposed
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees .
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
VIII. Old Business.
Zoning Variation: 889 Sheridan Rd. - Policy Direction. Manager Williams
reviewed the proposal from Patrick O'Neil to subdivide the property at 889
Sheridan Road into two lots. He explained that there are two fundamental policy
issues to be considered. The first is the determination of whether or not to
grant a variance to allow a reduction in the lot area requirements for the
second lot, when the combined lots are of sufficient size to create two
conforming lots. He pointed out that in January 1999, the Council adopted
ordinance, MC-216-99, which increased the lot area requirements for
subdivisions in order to discourage two-lot subdivisions on properties
identified as having the potential for subdivision under the old standards. He
noted that since the adoption of that ordinance, no subdivisions have been
approved. The second issue concerns the proposed design of the subdivision. The
same ordinance also adopted a subdivision design standard prohibiting a side
lot line from abutting the rear lot line of an adjoining lot. The subdivision
under consideration does not meet this standard.
Mr. Williams also reviewed the technical details of the request and noted that
approval of the subdivision would require variances from the minimum standards
of the Zoning Ordinance for Lot Area, Rectangular Area, and Lot Width as they
pertain the second lot. It would also require three variances from the
Subdivision Design Standards.
Finally, Mr. Williams discussed the positives and negatives of the proposed
subdivision as identified by staff and reviewed legislative history of the
issue.
The applicant, Patrick O'Neil, spoke in support of his application and
responded to questions from the Trustees.
Keith Shay, 857 Sheridan, reiterated his previous opposition to the proposed
subdivision.
After further discussion, Trustee Brower commented that one of the critical
considerations is the precedent that would be set by allowing this subdivision
to occur in direct contradiction to the relatively new Subdivision Ordinance.
Trustee Aquilino expressed her opinion that although she thinks the plans for
the new home are lovely, after previous Councils spent many months reviewing
and studying the issues associated with subdivisions, she would have a problem
supporting a nonconforming lot subdivision and, therefore, would not be able to
vote in favor of this application.
Trustee Powell moved to reject the request for subdivision. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino.
French Institute - Request for Reconsideration. The applicant, Maria Kurt,
spoke in support of her request, as did Susan Dunne and Janice Schneider,
students of the Institute.
Trustee Aquilino reported that the Chamber of Commerce Executive Board,
although thrilled with the Institute's presence in the Village, was very
sensitive to the Village's concerns about liability and precedent, and
therefore, would be in support of whatever decision is reached by the Council.
She pointed out that many merchants consider themselves to be unique and that
it is difficult to differentiate between the Institute's claim to unique status
and any others that might be made.
Trustee Brower reiterated that one of the chief concerns is liability. He noted
that the Chamber Board felt there was a distinction between closing off one of
the smaller, east-west streets, i.e., Chestnut or Elm, and closing off one of
the three major north-south arteries to the Village. He also made the
distinction between doing so for what is, essentially, a private function and
doing so for something that involves the whole Village.
Manager Williams briefly reviewed the original request and discussed an
alternative plan developed by staff in view of Council's denial of that request
and the Institute's subsequent request for reconsideration. The alternative
would provide some additional space for the Institute's grand opening, but
would not require the complete closure of Green Bay Road. He did note, however,
that anytime something like this is done, there is some risk involved and that
ultimately this had to be a policy decision.
Ms. Kurt voiced initial objection to the alternative plan.
President Pro Tem Lien reviewed the action heretofore on the issue and the
necessary procedural action required to bring the issue to another vote.
Manager Williams noted that if the issue were to come up for vote again, it
would be the Police Chief's preference to close off all of Green Bay Road. Mr.
Saunders concurred.
The Trustees and Ms. Kurt discussed the suggested alternative along with other
possible solutions without coming to a consensus on a suitable one.
President Pro Tem Lien called for a motion from the prevailing side to
reconsider the original request. No such motion was made.
He, then, called for a motion to approve the alternative plan, with a
restricted time limit. Trustee Powell moved to approve the alternate plan to
close one south bound lane and the parking lane of Green Bay Road, as outlined
by staff, for two hours for the French Institute's celebration. There being no
second to the motion, the motion was declared failed.
IX. New Business
Warrant Lists Nos. 1185 and 1186. Trustee Brower moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1185 in the total amount of $582,266.10 and Warrant List No. 1186 in the
total amount of $257,383.08. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell,
Lien, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
Bid No. 00-013 - 600 Volt Cable. Mr. Williams reviewed the bid for the purchase
and deliver of 600 volt cable and staff's recommendation that the bids be
awarded to Okonite Co. for $19,213 and Pirelli Cable for $9,696.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the award of
the bids as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Woodbury.
Doble Test Equipment Purchase Request. Mr. Williams reviewed the request from
the Water & Electric Department to waive the bid process for the purchase of
equipment needed to test and verify protective relay functions that provide
protection for the generators, ties lines, distribution circuits, and water
system high lift pumps. The request is made because there are no alternative
suppliers whose equipment is compatible with existing equipment. If another
supplier's equipment were utilized, the other related test equipment would have
to be replaced as well.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to waive the bid process
and approve the purchase of Doble testing equipment for $34,335. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Lien, Brower,
Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
DK Recycling Monthly Report. Mr. Williams referred the Trustees to the report
for the month of July filed by the Village's recycling contractor, saying that
it was a typical month without problems.
Architectural Contract for Village Yards. Mr. Saunders provided a brief
overview of the project to date. He explained that at Council's direction,
staff is prepared to proceed with further study of the option to replace the
entire existing Village Yards facility with a new facility, specifically by
pursuing refinement of the project feasibility report, including cost
estimates, a site plan, and preliminary elevations. In order to do this,
further architectural services are required. A proposal for these services,
based on detailed discussions of the project's requirements, has been received
from the architectural firm of Sente & Rubel. Mr. Saunders reviewed the key
provisions of a drafted, proposed contract, including provision for a
construction manager, and recommended awarding the contract for architectural
design services for the Village Yards to the firm. At Mr. Williams's request,
Mr. Saunders reviewed the projected time frame for the project.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the award of
the contract to Sente and Rubel in accordance with the contract. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Lien,
Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
X. Reports
Village Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the new issue of the Winnetka
Report would be delivered to residents soon.
Trustees Aquilino and Brower reported on the most recent meeting of the Chamber
of Commerce. Trustee Aquilino announced that the official opening of the Elm
Street Bridge was scheduled for September 9th. She also remarked that
MollyBelle Berish had brought up the subject of benches on the train platform.
Trustee Brower noted that the downtown lighting level had again been addressed
and that the issue of Christmas lights on the trees had also been a topic of
discussion. Mr. Saunders said that the latter would be on the agenda for the
next Forestry Commission meeting.
Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that as a member of NORCOM, the
Police Chief, along with other member chiefs, has entered into an agreement
with RCC Communications Consultants to evaluate the existing communications
system with the goal of replacing outdated equipment and upgrading the
network's communications without creating deficiencies on the main NORCOM
system. Winnetka's portion of the cost is anticipated to be $8,305.
Mr. Williams also informed the Council that the Village and the Park District
are continuing to explore possible alternative locations for the use of go-
peds. He said that the Green Bay Trail is not an option and that there have
been general discussions about using a couple of parking lots. However, he
noted that it is important to be sensitive to the impact on neighbors.
XI. Appointments.
None.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Woodbury. The meeting adjourned at 10:10 p.m.
______________________________________
Deputy Clerk
September 5, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved September 19, 2000)
1.   A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday,
September 5, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
2. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Assistant Director of Public Works Alex Attiah, Fire Chief Ron
Colpaert, and seven persons in the audience.
3. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
4. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
5. Quorum
a)   September 12, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to be able to attend.
b)   September 19, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Lien indicated that they expected to be able to attend.
6. Approval of Minutes
a)   August 15, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2000 - Floodplain Ordinance Amendments - Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance
modifying definitions, map references and agency names in Chapter 15.68 of the
Village Code - Flood Hazard Areas. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
b)   Ordinance No. M-21-2000 - Zoning Variation: 1155 Oak St. - Adoption.
President Holland called the Council’s attention to a letter received from
neighbors voicing concern about the height of the structures and their view of
the sheds and the play equipment, especially when the trees lose their leaves.
In their letter the neighbors requested that the school provide some evergreen
screening and consider repainting the equipment. President Holland noted that
she had pointed out to the neighbors that their comments had been received very
late in the process.
Mike Eberle, the landscape architect, confirmed that the new poles are exactly
the same height as the old ones were and noted that all of the concerns
expressed in the letter from the neighbors had been addressed during DRB
hearings.
Ann Kelly, the outgoing school board president, asked that it be noted that no
complaints had been received heretofore from the neighbors and that they were
invited to participate in a series of meetings throughout the planning process.
Ann Balusek, another Board member, noted that the landscaping has not yet been
completed and that there will be some smaller trees planted but that there is
no budget for large evergreens.
Trustee Powell voiced his support for the ordinance, as drafted, calling
attention to the fact that the neighbors had plenty of opportunity to comment
on the proposal early in the process but failed to do so. He moved that the
ordinance be adopted, as drafted. His motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
President Holland asked the school to work with the neighbors to achieve a
mutually agreeable resolution to their concerns.
c)   Ordinance No. M-22-2000 - Zoning Variation: 475 Ash St. - Adoption.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the proposed ordinance
granting a variance from the Maximum Building Size requirements of the Zoning
Ordinance in order to permit the reconstruction of a porch.. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Aquilino, Powell,
and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser
d)   Ordinance No. M-23-2000 - Zoning Variation: 567 Hill Terrace - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed one amendment made to the draft ordinance in which two
new points were added to the findings regarding practical difficulties and
particular hardships associated with the property.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved approve the amendment as
outlined by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt proposed
ordinance, as amended, permitting a variation from the Maximum Building Size
and Building Line Articulation provisions of the Zoning Ordinance in order to
permit the construction of a second story addition. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser
e)   Ordinance No. M-24-2000 - Zoning Variation: 446 Provident - Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the proposed ordinance
permitting a variation from the Intensity of Use of Lot, Side Yard, and
Building Line Articulation provisions of the Zoning Ordinance in order to
permit the construction of first and second story additions. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
f)   Ordinance No. M-25-2000 - Zoning Variation: 275 Auburn - Adoption. Trustee
Lien, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance permitting a
variation from the Building Line Articulation provision of the Zoning Ordinance
to allow for the construction of an addition. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
g)   Ordinance No. M-26-2000 - Sale of Surplus Property - Introduction. Mr.
Williams explained that this ordinance would authorize the Village to dispose
of five vehicles owned by the Village. These vehicles are past their useful
life and are being replaced with other equipment. The ordinance authorizes the
Village to dispose of the vehicles at the Northwest Municipal Conference
auction on September 23, 2000.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
h)   Ordinance No. M-27-2000 - Zoning Variation: 851 Sheridan Rd. -
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed a request for a side yard variance to
permit structural improvements to an existing one-story, four season sunroom.
The variance would allow for the existing, nonconforming screened porch to be
reconstructed in the same location, but with a 15-inch increase in height and
with a new ornamental rooftop and window railings. She then answered questions
from the Trustees. Ms. Janega added an historical perspective.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
i)   Ordinance No. M-28-2000 - Zoning Variation: 614 Willow Rd. - Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed a request for a variation from the building line
articulation requirements of the Zoning Code to allow an existing porch to be
replaced with a somewhat larger one-story family room which would extend the
nonconforming east building line from 60.53 feet to 66.03 feet.
Trustee Aquilino inquired whether an articulation variance would still be
required if the structure was not extended. Ms. McCammack responded in the
affirmative.
Looking at photos of the existing house, President Holland asked whether the
building conformed with the 35' height requirement. Ms. McCammack indicated
that the zoning analysis only considered proposed changes, therefore, if the
existing structure exceeds limits, it would not be cited.
Trustee Brower asked Ms. McCammack to comment on staff's assessment of this
request. Ms. McCammack indicated that she did not believe that the request was
compliant with the intent of articulation. She added, however, that there had
been considerable discussion about the extension onto the rebuilt porch at the
Zoning Board meeting and that the Board, ultimately, had voted to recommend
approval. She noted that it was her understanding that the applicant hoped to
maximize its family living space on the first floor. She said that the Zoning
Board had discussed complying alternatives with the applicant, but because of
their building plans, a number of the compliant alternatives had been
eliminated.
Trustee Aquilino expressed uncertainty as to the application of the
articulation requirement. She said that she didn't have any initial objection
to the size, but was somewhat confused seeing as how a variance would still be
required just to enclose the existing porch.
President Holland suggested that the issue be tabled until the next meeting
since there was no representative from the applicant present to answer some of
the Trustees' outstanding questions.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to table introduction until the
next meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Brower, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
j)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2000 - Fees for Municipal Services - Introduction. Ms.
Janega explained the provisions of this ordinance that establishes the
legislative authority to assess charges for the use and availability of extra
Village services, i.e. personnel, equipment, and property, in conjunction with
special events. She noted that it is a well established pubic policy that
public funds should not be spent to support political purposes or agendas and
pointed out that the proposed ordinance is similar to the Village's film
regulation ordinance. The ordinance requires the advance payment of 110% of the
projected costs and reserves rights to the Village, including the right to deny
a request under certain circumstances and the imposition of penalties.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
k)   Resolution No. R-28-2000 - Transfer of Funds to Village Yards Fund -
Adoption. Manager Williams explained the creation of a Public Facilities Fund
and the transfer of monies thereto. The Fund would serve as the cost enter for
the construction and/or renovation of the Village Yards. He noted that
establishing a single accounting cost center for this project will simplify and
centralize the accounting of all costs, make it easier to track all of the
costs through at least two budget cycles, and provide critical management
information on a timely basis.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
9. Old Business.
None
10. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1187 and 1188. Trustee Brower moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1187 in the total amount of $1,050,243.35 and Warrant List No. 1188 in
the total amount of $991,029.35. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino,
Powell, Lien, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
b)   Public Hearing: Zoning Ordinance Clean Up Issues. President Holland
declared the Public Hearing open, saying that discussion would take place
tonight and continue at the Study Session on Tuesday, September 12th.
Ms. Janega used overhead projections of drawings and recent variation requests
to illustrate each of the issues under review, including: (a) commercial half-
story definition, (b) residential articulation clarification, (c) setbacks for
commercial buildings, (d) Timing requirements for variation and special use
requests, (e) One year waiting period after denial of a zoning variation, (f)
minor structural changes to nonconforming buildings, and (g) open fences on
corner lots. The clean up would allow some minor variations that do not extend
nonconformities to take place without going through the variation process. She
noted that the draft ordinance reflects Council's discussions at its April 11,
2000 and June 13, 2000, study sessions.
After some discussion among the Trustees, President Holland closed the Public
Hearing.
c)   Sale of Reserve Engine. Chief Colpaert explained that with the delivery of
its new Rescue/MED/Engine in October, the Fire Department will be retiring its
1983 engine. That engine has been requested in a letter by the Austin Tracy
Volunteer Fire Department, purchasers of the Village's last surplus engine, in
the hopes that the Village would waive the bid process. He said that he had
been in contact with a fire apparatus broker to see if they would be willing to
advertise and sell the engine for a fee, but his request was declined due to a
surplus of vehicles of this type on the market. Therefore, he requested that
the Council waive the bid process and approve the sale of the 1983 engine to
the Austin Tracy Volunteer Fire Department for the sum of $15,000.000.
President Holland mentioned that the Northwest Municipal Conference had been
discussing ways in which to help these small, volunteer fire departments.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to waive the bid process
and approve the sale as requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
d)   Cedar Street Sanitary Sewer Extension. Mr. Attiah explained that for a
number of months, the Village has been working with the property owners at 410,
418, and 428 Cedar Street to resolve a dispute over a private sanitary sewer
service line that crosses these properties without recorded easements. At one
time the residences, which were all built at about the same time, were
connected to a common, private sewer service line. However, when the houses at
410 and 418 were subsequently demolished, the house at 428 was left without a
viable sanitary sewer service line. He outlined the particulars of a solution
brokered by the Village after several rounds of discussions with the three
property owners and requested that the Council approve the award of the bid for
the construction work necessary to implement the agreement to Lampignano & Son
for $41,147.
Saying that reaching this solution had been an arduous process, Mr. Williams
voiced his support for the agreement and recommended that the bid be awarded as
suggested.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Brower, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
President Holland congratulated the Public Works Director on the formulation
and negotiation of a fair solution.
11. Reports.
a)   Village President.
Mrs. Holland reviewed the dates and location of upcoming hazardous waste
collections in the suburbs and urged residents to take advantage of these
opportunities to rid themselves of old paint, pesticides, etc. She also
announced that The Fell Company had been selected as an Illinois Retailer of
the Century in recognition of its more than 90 years in business. Next,
President Holland reminded everyone that the official opening of the Elm Street
Bridge was scheduled for Saturday, September 9th at 10:00 a.m. Finally she
encouraged residents to plan to tour the existing Public Works Yards to gain an
understanding of the necessity for rebuilding the facility.
12. Appointments.
President Holland called for a motion to reappoint Richard Glenn to the Police
Pension Board. The motion was made by Trustee Brower and seconded by Trustee
Powell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Brower seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:32 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:32 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Presser. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Lien, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser. The meeting adjourned at 10:33 p.m.
______________________________________
Deputy Clerk
September 12, 2000
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved October 3, 2000)
A record of a legally convened Study Session of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 12, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, Chief of Police William J. Gallagher, members of the Plan Commission
and Building Review Committee and approximately 19 persons in the audience.
II. Demolition Permit Analysis and Options.
Ms. McCammack reviewed the current practices for demolition permits, issues or
concerns and possible policy alternatives. A discussion was held on the impact
of single family residential home demolition and new home construction. Gwen
Trindl of the Plan Commission and Charles Hoffman of the Building Review
Committee joined the Council for the discussion.
   Ms. McCammack listed the methods used by staff to reduce the impact of
demolitions and new construction: limiting permitted construction hours,
monitoring construction site conditions, delaying the issuance of demolition
permits until the replacement home’s construction documents are approved, and
restricting the number of simultaneous demolition projects along either side of
a block face. She said that in the case of restricting the number of permits
issued, staff has found that neighbors prefer that the work progresses quickly
and without delay and that delaying a permit for 90 days merely prolongs the
disruption in the neighborhood and creates an economic hindrance for the
developer or homeowner. She stated that this is different from the intent of
the ordinance and does not provide the Building Review Committee with any tools
to work with the homeowner to try to save historic homes. She recommended that
the Building Review Committee develop a plan for working with homeowners and
developers during the demolition delay period. She also identified other
possible changes, such as increasing the delay period, requiring public
hearings, posting signs on properties for public hearings and requiring
construction documents to be submitted with the application for demolition
permit.
Ms. McCammack then commented on solutions to the transformation of neighborhood
character due to demolitions, and indicated that the staff was beginning to
study and evaluate building size limits for larger lots, reducing midpoint
building heights, developing design guidelines and developing minimum
landscaping requirements. She listed several proactive solutions to the
disruption of neighborhoods: temporarily restricting parking to one side of the
street in certain circumstances where significant construction is occurring,
temporarily restricting traffic to one-way, locating temporary toilets as far
away from the street as possible, requiring 48 hours notice to neighbors before
demolition and a 48-hour pre-demolition inspection by the Village of the site.
   Charles Hoffman, Building Review Committee, commented that he has found the
demolition ordinance to be ineffective. He said the Committee has focused on
the intent to maintain character of neighborhoods, but has found that as
properties change, all housing stock has the potential for development. He
stated that the Committee has found that the 90 day delay period is purely an
inconvenience to builders and suggested that demolition permits be separated
from building permits before there is a great deal of money invested by the
property owner in completed construction documents, so that there would be time
to communicate, educate, and suggest possible avenues for rehabbing instead of
new construction. He believes that this would give the Building Review
Committee an earlier opportunity to work with people. He also believes that
that 180 days might be a more appropriate delay period to create an opportunity
to invite people to come to public hearings. He said that the Committee feels
limited in their ability to serve and is sometimes in a quandary as to what the
original intent was, and suggested that interior and exterior design review
also be considered.
   Manager Williams summarized Mr. Hoffman’s recommendations as follows:
      1.   Separate demolition permits from construction permits
      2.   Key permit date to review board meeting findings
      3.   Hold public hearings or informal meetings to receive public comment
for complex requests
   Gwen Trindl, Plan Commission, said that the Plan Commission is asked to look
at demolition because of budget and to determine if it conforms to the
Comprehensive Plan only, but the Commission thinks they should address this
issue in the future.
   Village Attorney Janega stated that she has concerns about extending the
delay, due to decisions in court cases during the past year. She explained the
concept of property rights as being a combination such rights as the right to
put a house where he or she wants it, a right to quiet enjoyment, etc., and
that as the law has evolved, if one of those rights is taken away, even for a
very short period of time, it is possible for someone to have a cause of action
against the Village. Ms. Janega explained that in order to establish this, a
property owner has to prove that a regulation imposed on a property has the
effect of taking away the value of that property. She explained that the term,
“takings,” is based on the concept of inverse condemnation, and generally means
that when a zoning or land use regulation has the effect of limiting property
use or development to the point that property value is reduced, then an owner
is entitled to compensation. She cautioned that anything the Council imposes
has to be related to a legitimate public purpose and that there must be a nexus
between what is being done and what you want to accomplish. She said that for
example, you cannot deny use of property for several years just to maintain
quiet in the neighborhood. She said that her concern about Mr. Hoffman’s
comments is that interior space analysis and exterior design review differ from
historic preservation aesthetic standards, which address the parts of a
building that face the street and how they relate to the appearance of the
neighborhood.
   Trustee Presser asked if it is a legitimate community concern to preserve
architecture and Ms. Janega affirmed that it is.
After further discussion, President Holland stated that there was a consensus
to proceed with the separation of the demolition permit and building permit
processes and to wait until there is a plan of action for the Building Review
Committee to effectuate before proceeding with extending the delay period. She
then asked for comments on Mr. Hoffman’s recommendation to hold public
hearings.
   Village Attorney Janega explained that the significance of a public hearing
is that it entitles a person to be heard. She said that an open public meeting
does not require that a person be allowed to participate unless it is allowed
by the Board. She said that in this context public participation could begin
right away with Building Review while it develops a plan and then a decision
could be made in the future about whether there should be a public hearing or a
public meeting.
   Trustee Lien suggested that the Plan Commission is the perfect vehicle for
this issue. He felt that more information was needed and that it could be a
short term project, discussed in depth by the Plan Commission with input from
the Building Review Committee and then reported to the Council.
   Manager Williams said that he thought the recommendation of separating the
demolition and construction permits could be done in the interim, since this is
currently just a policy decision, but does require a text amendment.
   President Holland announced that the consensus was that the Code be amended.
She asked that the Plan Commission meet on the demolition issue and invite the
Building Review Committee to the discussions.
III. Zoning Ordinance Issues.
Village Attorney Janega explained that an ordinance had been drafted based upon
the Council’s discussions in previous meetings and comments made at the public
hearing regarding commercial half-stories, residential articulation, setbacks
for commercial buildings, timing requirements for variation and special use
requests, one year waiting periods after denial of zoning variation, minor
structural changes to nonconforming buildings and open fences on corner lots.
President Holland invited Kevin Plunkett and Margaret Benson of the Zoning
Board of Appeals to join the discussion and announced that item 2(g) of the
Agenda would be considered first because of the many people attending the
meeting for this item.
Open fences on corner lots: Manager Williams explained that Council had asked
staff to brainstorm ideas about fence issues.
Brian Norkus explained that staff had devised a list of options for fences on
corner lots: prohibiting fences outright or limiting the height with exceptions
for major arterial streets or Design Review Board approval; reducing the
visibility requirement, requiring appearance review and landscaping, requiring
finished side to face the street, requiring a minimum setback from the sidewalk
and requiring a staked survey.
Manager Williams said that in 1996 an ad hoc committee looked into this and the
concern at that time was that homes on Hibbard, Tower, etc. wanted to erect
fences that would block traffic. He said that the deer problem was addressed at
that time also.
Margaret Benson said that ZBA felt that there should be a change, noting that,
based on the variation standards, the ZBA recently had to make a negative
recommendation on a fence variation request to build a fence to the corner, but
that they would have liked to have granted a variation.
After hearing comments from members of the audience opposed to making changes
to the ordinance, Manager Williams attempted to clarify that at the last
meeting staff was asked to develop a list of options on fences in corner lots,
which they have done. He said that the only requirement that is actually in the
ordinance is the 25-foot set back. He also said that existing fences would not
be affected unless they were taken down completely and rebuilt.
President Holland explained that the Council had asked for recommendations to
make the ordinance more lenient and to allow people to go to the corner with an
open fence. She said that the discussions in 1996 had included prohibiting
fences in the front yards. She said that 6-foot stockade fences in front yards
just haven’t occurred and trying to define types of fencing on Cherry Street as
opposed to Willow Road is not possible.
Trustee Brower said the only issue for discussion is the degree of openness on
a corner lot.
Trustee Aquilino said that this change was meant to allow the process to be
completed at the building permit stage and eliminate the need to go through the
process of requesting a variation and coming before the Village Council.
A member of the audience said that from an aesthetic standpoint it might be
rather bizarre-looking if you had a solid fence coming to the point that is set
25 feet back and then a picket or open split rail fence. He said that the
Village may begin to see some odd corner fence configurations where now most
people just cut the corner diagonally and continue with the solid fence.
John Weber, 415 Berkeley, remembers the discussions in 1996 and the concerns
for people who live on Hibbard and Tower Roads. He thinks that visibility on
corners is important, but that each person is also concerned about the
appearance of the front of their property.
Trustee Presser asked if it could be drafted that fence be required to be of
uniform materials.
President Holland said that it was the Council’s opinion that when the Village
Engineer determines that safety requirements have been met it should be
approved at the Building Department counter.
Manager Williams said that what they are also asking is that the Village
Engineer be given the discretion to determine that life safety requirements are
met.
Trustee Lien said that there will be cases where the Village will be sued if
decisions are made at the discretion of the Village Engineer. He said he would
be more comfortable leaving the ordinance as is or retaining a percentage of
visibility.
Attorney Janega said that the Village Engineer refers to standards to come up
with a percentage for visibility.
President Holland concluded the discussion by asking that Ms. Janega look into
changes to the ordinance for fences on corner lots.
Commercial half-story definition: President Holland asked for questions from
the Council. There were none.
Residential articulation: In response to a question from Trustee Aquilino, Ms.
Janega stated that the Council had decided not to include chimneys, dormers,
bay windows, etc. as articulation. She explained that these elements encroach
towards the outside of the lot and Council wanted articulation to go toward the
interior of the lot.
Ms. Janega noted further changes made to the draft language, and said that
rather than making the point of reference the side yard line, it would be at
the side building line for existing buildings.
Kevin Plunkett asked why that couldn’t be the same for new construction and
existing housing and why the ordinance could not simply say that articulation
would be 2 feet from the existing building line for new or existing buildings.
He also asked for an explanation of staff’s interpretation of the smaller side
yard and how it is made.
Ms. Janega replied that existing buildings are already measured from the
building line but nonconforming properties could not always be measured in this
way. She said that new construction has no building as yet, so measuring must
start from the setback requirement. She further explained the 2 foot
articulation requirements. In response to Mr. Plunkett’s question about
determining the smaller side yard, Ms. Janega explained that when the side
yards are close in size, they look to what gives the best chance of reducing
the extent of nonconformity.
Setbacks for commercial buildings: President Holland said that the current
ordinance requires a minimum of 1 foot and a maximum of 3 feet set back along
any street frontage.
Ms. Janega explained that proposed language lifts the maximum of 3 feet for
anything above the ground floor, so that the half-story requirement and
articulating features such ass terracing on larger buildings can be done
without a variation.
Timing requirements for variation and special use requests: Manager Williams
explained that he had asked the Village Attorney to revise this so that the
Council receives a request 30 days after all review boards have considered it.
One year waiting period after denial of zoning variations: There was no
discussion.
Minor structural changes to nonconforming buildings: Manager Williams said that
this change is being made so that homeowners of nonconforming homes can install
skylights and windows, etc. without requiring a variance.
Ms. Janega also explained that replacing windows with larger ones or different
configurations would still require variations. She said that it is difficult to
make a hardship in the instance of replacing deteriorating structures or
ornamental features and that a variation need not be necessary.
IV. Average Side Yard Definition.
Village Attorney Janega gave a brief summary of the history of this request to
re-examine the average lot width concept in the Zoning Ordinance. Ms. Janega
said that the owner of the property at 333 Willow Road had originally made the
request, and had raised the issue again when 680 Elder was before the Council.
She explained that, as originally proposed, total recalculation would be
required on all lots with any kind of non parallel lot line. She said that the
Council had authorized a minor change to the ordinance, but it was, in effect,
left essentially alone. At the time, it was not possible to determine what the
effect of this would be. Ms. McCammack and Mr. Norkus illustrated Ms. Janega’s
proposed amendments and how average lot width would be determined on flag lots
and irregularly-shaped lots. Ms. Janega explained that the amendment proposed
by staff requires that all of the calculations be made from where the box of
the lot begins, in an effort to reduce the number of variations necessary.
Mr. James Pinkerton, 333 Willow Road, commented on the zoning issues presented
and said that all of them should be easily adopted and will support staff’s
decisions on many lots. He asked that they not overlook the last paragraph of
Ms. Janega’s agenda report referring to flag pole lots and that changes be made
to make the ordinance consistent. He said that all of these changes are
significant, but feels that definition of average lot width is still distorted.
He distributed a handout with examples that illustrate the basic principles
involved.
   Manager Williams commented that to go forward, a public hearing would be set
for the proposed amendment. He recommended that the Council proceed. He then
asked President Holland to defer the remaining agenda items until the next
study session due to the late hour.
V. Results of Staff “Brainstorming” Concerning Parking Improvement Ideas.
Manager Williams presented several suggestions made by staff for parking
improvements within the Village. Further discussion was deferred to the
November 14, 2000 study session.
VI. Benches for Train Stations.
Manager Williams presented a suggestion from a resident that the Village
install benches for commuters using the Metra train service and asked that the
Council provide policy direction regarding spending taxpayer dollars for
improvements on private property. Further discussion was deferred to the
November 14, 2000 study session.
VII. Executive Session.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Presser,
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 10:39 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:29 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees, Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Absent: None.
VIII. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:30 p.m.
Respectfully Submitted,
Mary Ivins
Recording Secretary
September 19, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved October 3, 2000)
1.   A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday,
September 19, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
2. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7: 33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser, John Powell, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: Trustee John Lien. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, Police Chief William Gallagher, Village Planner Brian Norkus, and 14
persons in the audience.
3. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
4. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
5. Quorum
a)   October 3, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to be able to attend.
b)   October 10, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to be able to attend.
6. Approval of Minutes
a)   September 5, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
7. Commendation
a)   Resolution No. R-30-2000 - Commending Aaron Thomas - Adoption.
At President Holland's request Police Chief Gallagher recounted the events of
August 3, 2000, occasioning the commendation of postal worker Aaron Thomas. She
then read the resolution commending Mr. Thomas for the role he played in
rescuing an elderly Village resident who had fallen in her home and could not
get up. Mr. Thomas noticed that the resident's mail had not been collected and
fearing for her well being, alerted public safety employees of a possible
problem. President Holland presented a signed copy of the resolution to Mr.
Thomas and, on behalf of the Village, expressed high praise and sincere thanks
for his alertness and concern.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
30-2000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Lien.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln. read a statement regarding recent discussions
about fence policies. (Attachment #1)
9. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. M-27-2000 - Zoning Variation: 851 Sheridan Rd. - Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting a side yard variance to allow for structural improvements to an
existing one-story, four season sunroom. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
b)   Ordinance No. M-28-2000 - Zoning Variation: 614 Willow Rd. - Introduction.
President Holland called for a motion to remove this issue from the table and
introduce the drafted ordinance. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser so
moved. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. Norkus briefly reviewed the request for a variation from the building line
articulation requirements of the Zoning Code to allow an existing porch to be
replaced with a somewhat larger one-story family room which would extend the
nonconforming east building line.
The applicant, Douglas Shand, responded to questions from Trustee Woodbury
regarding the existing foundation and basement access.
President Holland informed Mr. Shand that the item would appear for adoption at
the next Council meeting on October 3, 2000.
c)   Ordinance No. M-19-2000 - Special Use Permit: North Shore Community Bank -
Adoption. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to remove the
motion from the table. By unanimous voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Brower recused himself from consideration on this issue.
President Holland asked Mr. Saunders for any additional comments he might have
regarding the impact of the Bank's proposed new development on the Village's
possible future construction of an underground parking facility. Mr. Saunders
referred the Trustees to materials in the Agenda packet showing the applicant's
proposed ramp and underground parking, noting that the proposed driveway would
cut through a portion of the available envelope for construction of a parking
facility. He explained that this intrusion into the space would necessitate
either scaling back the size of the Village's proposed facility or doing some
creative, and probably expensive, engineering work to connect the two
structures. He added that it is not possible to estimate the cost of such work
at this time. He pointed out that in addition to considering a connection
between the two structures, should the Village build a parking facility, it
would also have to provide service access to the existing and new buildings,
and that the proposed location of the Bank's proposed ramp also conflicts with
such access.
Mr. Norkus indicated that the Bank had also submitted an application for a
Certificate of Appropriateness to redevelop the existing site as a bank without
a drive-through banking facility. Mr. Norkus said that these plans call for the
façade of the building to remain essentially as it is, but the applicant would
remove some of the building in the rear to accommodate a small addition. He
pointed out that these plans comply with all zoning requirements. Manager
Williams indicated that, therefore, the alternative proposal's facility would
be there "by right" and would not be attached to any parking requirements.
President Holland asked the applicant if it had any comments at this time.
There were none. She, then, invited comments from the audience.
Bill Ensing, attorney for Glenn Weaver, the owner of 574 Lincoln, reiterated
his client's objections to the Bank's proposed development based upon increased
traffic flow and potential negative impact on his client's property value.
Leslie Bullock, 612 Lincoln, a resident and business owner, requested that any
decision made by the Council include a provision that at no time in the future
would a drive-through facility be permitted due to the small, pedestrian nature
of the neighborhood and business district.
Blanche Romey, 744 Prospect, a resident and future tenant of the Bank's
proposed new facility, spoke in support of the new development, which, in her
opinion would be an enhancement to the beauty and safety of the neighborhood.
She questioned the logic of requiring underground parking for the proposed new,
two story building when none would be required for the renovation of the
existing building.
Geoff Fox, 879 Cherry, a resident and local business owner, expressed agreement
with Ms. Bullock.
Randy Hibben, President of North Shore Community Bank, reiterated his comments
from previous meetings. He said that the Bank is running out of time and
alternatives and must pursue some course of action on behalf of the Bank and
its prospective tenants. He urged the Council to vote on the petition before
it.
Trustee Woodbury asked if staff had discussed the Village's issues about access
and service with the applicant and identified possible alternatives for the
Bank. Mr. Williams said that it had, with the identified alternative being to
rotate the Bank's proposed entry to its facility 90 degrees to have its access
off the west. He indicated that since the Bank's proposal had not been amended,
it appears that this solution had been rejected.
Mr. Williams pointed out that there were two applications before the Council:
(1) the proposed new development of a two story building requiring the
provision of some parking; and (2) the rehab of the existing building, which
does not require parking. He explained that parking is a serious problem in the
Village, especially the Elm Street business district, which is one of the major
reasons that parking requirements were imposed for the development of new
buildings. While the Village wants to encourage first floor development and
provides for the parking needs of retail merchants on the first floor, it
cannot absorb either the cost or the demand for parking to accommodate
businesses located on second and third floors.
Trustee Powell commented that it was unfortunate that the Bank's and Village's
timing did not coincide, but that in his opinion, the Bank is trying to build a
larger facility than is possible on the property, and that the overall public
interest has to be considered over any private interests. He said that he would
have difficulty supporting the Bank's proposed new development.
Trustee Aquilino concurred.
President Holland called for a motion to adopt Ordinance No. M-19-2000 granting
a Special Use Permit to allow five (5) of the required twenty (20) parking
spaces to be located at street level.. Trustee Woodbury so moved, seconded by
Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion failed. Ayes: None. Nays:
Trustees Woodbury, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Absent: None. Trustee Brower
abstained.
President Holland indicated that the request for a Certificate of
Appropriateness for the conforming, one-story alternative would continue to
move through the Village's processes.
d)   Ordinance No. M-26-2000 - Sale of Surplus Property - Adoption. Trustee
Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance that would
authorize the Village to dispose of five vehicles that have passed their useful
life by selling them at the NWMC auction. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien.
Ordinance No. MC-4-2000 - Fees for Municipal Services - Introduction. Mr.
Williams asked the Council that this item be removed from the agenda in order
to assimilate and incorporate additional staff comment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to table the ordinance,
which would establish the legislative authority to assess charges for the use
and availability of extra Village services, i.e. personnel, equipment, and
property, in conjunction with special events. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2000 - Amendment to Demolition Provisions of Building
Code - Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that the draft ordinance
reflects Council's discussions at the last Study Session and the joint Study
Session held earlier this summer with the Building Review Committee, which
resulted in a directive to eliminate the requirement that either a detailed
site restoration schedule and site restoration plan or a complete building
permit application be submitted as part of a demolition permit application.
This revision is in response to Council's determination that requiring an
applicant to incur the cost of a complete set of plans in order to demolish an
existing building has the unintended consequence of reducing the effectiveness
of the advisory demolition review procedure, which is intended to encourage
preservation of architecturally or historically significant structures by
evaluating alternatives to demolition. She pointed out, however, that this
ordinance would prohibit the issuance of a demolition permit until all permits
for the replacement home have been approved. She also noted that the ordinance
proposes a few non-substantive changes to the demolition permit requirements.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance as
drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2000 - Clean Up of Zoning Ordinance - Introduction.
Attorney Janega explained that this ordinance would amend various provisions of
the Village's Zoning Ordinance revising the definition of half-story, and
modifying provisions pertaining to commercial building setbacks, single-family
building line articulation, fences on corner lots, changes in nonconforming
buildings, and procedures for variations and special uses. She noted that the
text of the hearing draft considered at the September 5 and 12, 2000, public
hearing and discussions had been modified to reflect Council's discussions and
further comment received from the Community Development Department.
Ms. Janega reviewed those changes in detail and explained that the articulation
requirements may need further, minor modification prior to adoption. She also
pointed to additional staff comments pertaining to work on nonconforming
buildings.
After further discussion among the Trustees and staff, particularly with regard
to setbacks, it was agreed to proceed with introduction, with the expectation
that additional language regarding doors and windows in nonconforming buildings
would be incorporated into the draft for consideration prior to adoption.
On behalf of a resident who contacted him but could not attend personally, Mr.
Norkus expressed concern about the proposed changes to the ordinance pertaining
to fences stemming from the fact that the Village now has a strict numerical
standard and what is proposed would be based on case-by-case review, which
could be construed as arbitrary.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-5-2000, Amending the Zoning Ordinance as it pertains to building line
articulation, half-stories, setbacks for commercial buildings, encroachments in
required yards, fence setbacks, and procedures for special use and variation
applications. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
10. Old Business.
a)   Consideration of Landscape and Lighting Plan - Winnetka Presbyterian
Church, 1255 Willow Rd. Mr. Norkus explained that pursuant to Ordinance M-1-
2000, which granted a Special Use Permit and zoning variations to the Winnetka
Presbyterian Church, the Church has submitted for Village Council approval (a)
a site lighting plan, which includes Church acceptance of the Village's removal
of existing Village light fixtures along the North property line; (b) a site
landscape plan, supplemented by a landscape "study" of existing plant materials
on adjoining properties at 1228 and 1234 Ash Street; and (c) proposed
relocation of air conditioning condensers to the building rooftop.
Mr. Norkus then briefly reviewed the specifics of each element. He explained
that the Design Review Board had reviewed the plans and recommended approval of
the lighting plan. He noted that the Design Review Board had also approved the
fence for the enclosure of refuse and the proposed relocation of the air
conditioning condensers. It had also voted to issue a Certificate of
Appropriateness as to the remaining building design elements considered.
Mr. Norkus explained that the landscape plan had been reviewed and approved by
Jim Stier, the Village Forester. The Design Review Board had also considered
the plan and recommended approval, but expressed concern about the Church's
request to defer consideration of a mutually agreeable buffer between the
Church and the properties at 1228 and 1234 Ash Street until after the building
is completed.
Trustee Powell noted that the September 7 letter from the Church suggested that
the parking lot would be lit from dusk to midnight and asked if it had any
impact on the Church's application.
Mr. Norkus responded that the September 6 letter from the Church's architect
stated that lighting is proposed from dusk to dawn.
Trustee Brower noted that the plan and discussions were consistent with a dusk
to dawn operation.
Jack Chapin, representing the Presbyterian Church, asked if the Church would
have to come to the Village Council for approval if it wished to modify the
existing lighting plan.
Attorney Janega stated that Council approval would not be required to reduce
the hours of operation, but that approval would be required to increase the
hours, which would expand the intensity of the use.
Mr. Chapin stated that the Church would prefer to proceed with approval for
dusk to dawn operation, which would make amendment easier later, and withdrew
the dusk to midnight comment in the Church's letter.
Trustee Brower asked whether the lighting was needed for longer hours to
provide protection.
Mr. Chapin responded that the lot is not used after 11:00 p.m. and that the
purpose of the lighting is to provide security and to facilitate safety for
those attending functions at the Church.
Trustee Woodbury noted that the Village now had responses to six out of seven
of the issues left open for final approval after adoption of the original
ordinance. He asked whether drainage and grading are still open.
Mr. Chapin stated his understanding that the plan had been approved and is
currently under review by the Village Engineer due to changes made by FEMA.
Ms. Janega and Mr. Norkus noted that the ordinance required Village Engineer
approval for drainage and grading and added that the Council had approved the
variance from FEMA's floodplain requirements.
In response to a question from Trustee Aquilino, Mr. Chapin stated that the
Church’s lights would be 14 feet tall. He noted that the current lights are
owned by the Village and are 30 - 35 feet high.
Trustee Presser asked whether the neighbors had any issues regarding lighting.
The neighbors present in the audience, from 1228 Ash and 1234 Ash, confirmed
that the lighting was not a problem.
Trustee Woodbury noted that the Church's request stated there was an agreement
to agree on landscaping and asked how the neighbors intended to resolve the
issue so that the Council would not have to be involved, noting that there is
some disagreement between the neighbors and the Church as to what constitutes
an adequate buffer.
Village Manager Williams suggested that the Council assess where we are, from
all perspectives.
Mr. Chapin stated that he chairs the Church's architectural design committee
and that he considers tonight's meeting a critical one, because the Church has
experienced delays, which have increased the cost of construction. He related
the manner in which the Church has attempted to communicate with the neighbors,
asserting that the Church has made every effort to be a good neighbor. Mr.
Chapin stated the Church has agreed to work with the neighbors and to be guided
by the Village. He pointed to the ordinance provision allowing the Council to
make a final determination on the landscape buffer and expressed the need to
avoid further delay of the work. Mr. Chapin added that he feels that Mr.
McAdam, of 1234 Ash, is now asking for more than was originally agreed to.
Sarah Phillips, of 1234 Ash, stated that their purpose is not to dissuade
expansion efforts but to protect their property values and to make sure that
everything is being handled in a professional and appropriate manner. She feels
that the wording being used to reflect the agreement on the landscaping is
vague and wants to be sure that all parties are on the same page. She also
expressed concern about whether funds would be available for landscaping at the
end of the project. She added that the landscaping plan they have received is
not entirely accurate, making a decision about the appropriateness of the
proposal difficult. She stated that she and her husband want to make sure that
their interests are being looked after and that the intent of the ordinance is
followed.
Mr. Williams noted that the Church had requested that the buffering landscape
plan not be developed until after construction and asked Ms. Phillips whether
she agrees.
Ms. Phillips stated she thinks doing the final landscape buffer plan sooner is
not realistic. She added that they don't want to hold the Church up, but they
would like more specific wording, so that the mutually agreed landscaping will
be on their property because the Church doesn't want to relinquish parking
spaces. She noted this was not mentioned in the ordinance and that, with the
new structure, she will be looking at a brick wall, not at the open space that
had previously been there.
Trustee Presser noted that it appears the neighbors are not asking the Council
to deny approval, but they just want to make sure their concerns are on record.
Mr. Williams explained that one option would be to hold back on the landscaping
for the time being, but to move the rest of the project forward, ask the
neighbors and Church to meet and then come back to the Council on the final
landscaping issues if necessary.
President Holland asked if that met with the approval of the parties.
Mr. Williams asked for clarification of the time frame.
Mr. Chapin stated that he still maintains that they won’t know what additional
landscaping is needed until the building is completed and the landscaping shown
in the plans has been installed. He added that it makes no sense to make a
decision before then.
Trustee Aquilino asked whether the building has been changed.
Mr. Chapin stated that they have moved the building south and back, so there is
more of a green buffer between the building and the property. Chris Mekus, the
Church's architect, added that they have also shortened the building so that it
comes further to the west.
Trustee Aquilino asked where the landscaping would be and inquired as to the
likelihood that it would be on the neighbors' property.
Mr. Chapin added that it appears that, should a buffer be required and agreed
upon, it would more than likely go on the neighbors' property if it is to
survive, although there is an opportunity to put some of the landscape buffer
on the Church's property. He reiterated that it is necessary to evaluate what
is proposed on the Church's property before discussing what landscaping should
be put on the neighbors' property.
President Holland noted that the building elevation shows an unrelieved wall
without any green in front of it, and asked if Church intends to use that strip
for planting. President Holland also asked about the picture of the north
elevation and the placement of AC units.
Mr. Mekus stated that they intended to use the strip for planting and that they
have an enlarged planting plan that he can make available for Council review.
Trustee Aquilino asked whether the AC units could be seen from the south side
of the property.
Mr. Mekus responded that the air conditioners would be located over the
existing courtyard and would not be seen from the south.
Trustee Powell stated that, regarding the landscaping, it looks as if the real
issue is that the neighbors don't want to look at a solid wall and asked
whether landscaping could totally block the building from view.
Mr. Chapin responded that he does not think the Church can afford a solid wall
of any kind of foliage that would prevent the neighbors from viewing the new
Christian Arts Center.
Trustee Presser asked whether that meant the Church was backing off from its
stated intention of doing something satisfactory for the neighbors.
Mr. Chapin responded that his answer specifically addressed whether the Church
can entirely prevent the neighbors from seeing the new structure. He believes
it would be impossible to do that, and the Church would have to plead a
hardship if that were the requirement.
Mr. Williams asked what it would cost to do a first class landscaping for the
two properties in question.
Mr. Mekus stated that it would be $5000-$10,000 for each property.
Mr. Chapin noted that the shorter building now proposed neither goes past nor
invades the property at 1228 Ash. Ms. Janega pointed out that at the time the
ordinance was introduced, these two parcels of property were specifically
included because there would be a clear view of the building addition from
their property.
Trustee Aquilino noted that, in her opinion, the changes to the building are
improvements over what had been approved. She added that perhaps what was
necessary to assure performance on the landscaping was an escrow.
Mr. Williams suggested that the Village could take a deposit on the
landscaping, which would allow the parties time to work together and reassure
the neighbors that funds are on deposit while the situation is being resolved.
Trustee Brower added that the Village could also accept a letter of credit for
the remaining work.
Ms. Janega suggested granting preliminary approval of the landscape plan as it
exists today, so that the Church could put that landscaping in, and reserving
the final landscaping approval until after the parties assess the impact of the
completed development and landscaping.
Mr. Chapin asked about deposits required by the Forester to guarantee
transplanted trees and objected to being required to make such a deposit.
Both President Holland and Trustee Aquilino stated that tree deposits are a
standard procedure under the Village's tree preservation ordinance.
Mr. Norkus noted that Jim Stier had identified 4 trees as being protected and,
therefore, subject to deposit requirements.
Trustee Powell stated that the deposits don't begin to amount to enough to
replace a tree.
President Holland confirmed that the Village does not ask the Church to replace
a tree with a like tree of the same size, but to replace a certain number of
inches.
Mr. Chapin stated that he had misunderstood the requirement, but that he does
object to having Church funds tied up by the Village.
President Holland reiterated that the requirement of a deposit to secure
replacement inches to be put back on the property is a requirement for any
homeowner under the Village's tree ordinance.
Mr. Williams suggested that one solution would be to give approval to the
lighting, air conditioning and existing landscaping, and then to require the
Church to post either a cash deposit or a letter of credit for $15,000,
approximately $7,500 per property, and then have the parties work out the final
landscape buffering when the project is completed.
Mr. Chapin again objected to the amount of deposit.
Trustee Brower noted that the deposit follows the usual procedures.
Ms. Janega added that the deposit or letter of credit is to secure performance
in accordance with Section 4E of the special use ordinance and does not require
that specific amount to be spent on each property.
Trustee Woodbury stated that it is necessary to get on with the project, but
that having the Church and neighbors agree upon a plan is an important
component.
Mr. Chapin recommended a $5,000 deposit for 1234 Ash and $3,000 for 1228 Ash,
only to insure that the Church meet and attempt to work out a plan, but not to
commit to spending that amount of money on landscaping.
Trustee Aquilino noted that posting of the letter of credit should give the
neighbors an additional level of comfort and asked whether the amount of money
in the agreement would bind anyone to anything. Trustee Presser asked whether
this would assure that the landscape plan would be mutually agreeable.
Ms. Janega responded that the motion could be phrased so that the security
would require that the obligation of the ordinance be met, without requiring
the expenditure of that specific amount.
Mr. McAdam asked that the record note that he agrees to be committed to the
process.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the lighting plan
as submitted. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser, Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the relocation
of the air conditioning condensers, as requested. By roll call vote, the motion
passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser, Woodbury.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to partially approve the
landscaping plan to allow the Church to proceed with construction, and put in
the landscaping shown in the plans submitted, subject to the Church’s posting a
letter of credit or cash deposit with the Village in the amount of $10,000 to
secure performance of the requirement to work with the neighbors to agree on a
landscape plan as provided in Section 4E of Ordinance M-1-2000, with the
understanding that the cost of the landscape buffer may be less than the
deposit amount, and that the deposit is separate from and in addition to any
deposits required under the Village's tree ordinance. By roll call vote, the
motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser,
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lien
11. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1189 and 1190. Trustee Brower moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1189 in the total amount of $608,563.38 and Warrant List No. 1190 in
the total amount of $531,763.96. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino,
Powell, Lien, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
DK Recycling Systems Monthly Report. President Holland directed Council's
attention to the monthly report for the month of August included in the agenda
packets.
12. Reports.
a)   Village President.
President Holland reported that the Elm Street bridge was dedicated a week ago
and thanked Michelle and TCI Cable for running dedication of the bridge on
Channel 19, Monday - Friday at 9:30 p.m. Mrs. Holland further reported that she
attended the opening of the new Christ Church addition and commented on the
wonderful job that was done in restoring an architecturally significant home.
Mrs. Holland urged residents to take a look at the Public Works Yards, noting
that this was a large public project and wanted all residents to understand its
necessity.
Mrs. Holland then expressed her thanks to Art and Steve Schmidt at The Bellowes
for their work in restoring the chandeliers and sconces in the Chambers. She
stated that the fixtures had not been touched in 75 years and that the
restoration work, which included re-wiring, was almost complete. She invited
all residents to come to the Council Chambers to see this beautiful work.
b)   Village Trustees. Trustee Woodbury reported that he attended a fundraiser
sponsored by the Historical Society. He stated he also attended the monthly
meeting and noted continuing progress by the Historical Society on a possible
acquisition.
Trustee Brower reported that he had attended the Chamber of Commerce meeting.
The meeting included discussion about benches at the train stations and
approval of that concept. There was also discussion about the visual clutter
from newspaper machines at train stations. Trustee Brower stated there was also
a long discussion on Christmas tree lights and that the Chamber has requested
that the Council light the lights on November 16, earlier than last year in
order to tie in with Jim Henderson's TV show on cable. In response, Mr.
Williams reported that he had spoken with Bryan McInturff, who had indicated
that the deadline can be met and requested direction from the Council.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino moved that Christmas lights be lit
on November 16. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Finally, Trustee Brower noted that the Chamber again expressed that parking is
a real concern which needs attention.
Trustee Powell reported that he attended a meeting of the RED Center Board. He
reported that the Board approved agreements for the construction of a new
dispatch center in Northbrook, with completion expected by this time next year.
He noted, however, that Glenview and Wheeling have withdrawn from RED Center,
but that the matter was handled cordially and that a transition plan is being
worked out to insure that all communities are well served during the
transition. He added that RED Center will proceed with efforts to get
additional Villages to join.
c)   Village Manager.
Manager Williams referred the Trustees to reports included in the Agenda
packets from the Police Chief on enforcement of the leaf blower ban during the
summer months and on enforcement of traffic and parking laws around the White
Hen Pantry.
He also reminded everyone about the open house at the Public Works Yards on
Saturday, from 10 a.m. – 2 p.m.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
13. Appointments.
None.
14. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Presser, and Brower Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Lien. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:35 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Presser, Powell, and Woodbury. Absent:
Trustee Lien. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega
15. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Brower seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Presser, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Lien. The meeting adjourned at 10:36 p.m.
______________________________________
Deputy Clerk
October 3, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved October 17, 2000)
1.   A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday,
October 3, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
2. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7: 31 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, Stephen Presser, John
Powell, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee,
Director of Community Development Cindy McCammack, and 11 persons in the
audience.
3. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
4. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
5. Quorum
a)   October 10, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to be able to attend.
b)   October 17, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to be able to attend.
6. Approval of Minutes
a)   September 12, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
b)   September 19, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln., repeated her previously expressed concern
about the lack of a sidewalk on one side or the other of Pine Street between
Rosewood and Hibbard Roads. She expressed her opinion that the absence of a
sidewalk constitutes a significant safety issue and should be addressed by the
Council. (Ms. Berish's comments are attached as Attachment #1.)
President Holland responded that the Village had examined the sidewalk
situation throughout the Village and prioritized projects needing attention.
She also mentioned that Ms. Berish's previous request for reprioritizing the
Pine Street sidewalk had been considered by a former Council and denied. She
suggested that perhaps the issue could be slated for a future agenda if the
Trustees felt that it warranted further reconsideration.
Curt Voges, 946 Oak St., commented on a letter that he submitted to the
Trustees regarding the Zoning Code amendments concerning the addition,
relocation or enlargement of doors and windows in nonconforming walls as
addressed in Item 8(b). Mr. Voges voiced support for allowing windows and doors
to be moved in nonconforming walls without going through the variation process
as proposed, but asked that the scope of the amendments be expanded to include
side yard walls.
Penny Lanphier, 250 Birch Street, expressed concern about the demolition of so
many older homes of historical or architectural significance to the Village,
and remarked on the effect the loss of such a home has on a neighborhood. She
also commented that the Village does not get involved in the demolition process
until it is nearly at an end, and that, at that point, there is little that can
be done to salvage a property. She urged the Council to examine the process and
work to develop one that provides for greater and earlier involvement on the
part of Village boards and staff, particularly toward educating homeowners
about the uniqueness of their properties.
President Holland commented briefly on the Landmark Preservation Ordinance and
suggested that residents interested in the history of their homes should
contact the Historical Society.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. M-28-2000 - Zoning Variation: 614 Willow Rd. - Adoption.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting a variation from the Building Line Articulation provisions of the
Village Code in order to permit the reconstruction and extension of a one-story
addition.
Trustee Woodbury reiterated his concern about this request. He said that he
couldn't see where there was a hardship that goes with the property involved,
and therefore, would have to cast a negative vote.
One of the applicants, Suzanne Shand, responded to questions from the Trustees.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: Trustee Woodbury. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No.MC-5-2000 - Clean Up of Zoning Ordinance - Adoption.
President Holland called for a motion to amend the ordinance as shown in the
revised draft in the Agenda packet. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien,
so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Attorney Janega reviewed the language that was added to the ordinance as a
result of Council's discussions at the time of introduction. She noted that the
changes would allow for the addition, relocation, or enlargement of doors and
windows in nonconforming front and rear walls of buildings without obtaining a
variation, as well as permitting the ZBA to grant variations for new, moved or
enlarged windows and doors in walls facing side yards except under certain
circumstances. Addressing Mr. Voges's earlier comments, she explained that the
rationale for requiring the review of requests to change nonconforming walls in
side yards is that changes in these walls can result in an intrusion into
neighbors' privacy.
Trustee Woodbury pointed out that these situations already involve
nonconforming properties which, by definition, are closer together than
required.
After further discussing whether to allow changes in nonconforming side walls
without requiring a variation, the Trustees decided to continue to require
review of such alterations.
Trustee Powell said that he favors letting the Zoning Board make the final
decision in many of the cases that heretofore have come before the Council.
Ms. Janega remarked that, although variations would still be required in many
instances, the proposed changes would significantly reduce the processing time,
and suggested that a meaningful test of the effectiveness of the new language
would be to monitor applications for a specified period of time to determine
the kinds of variance requests that keep coming back up to the Council. She,
then, suggested an additional, minor modification in the draft language to
further clarify the scope of the ZBA’s authority.
President Holland inquired whether Trustees Brower and Lien accepted the
additional amendments as a part of their motion. Each answered affirmatively.
By roll call vote, the ordinance was amended. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
President Holland thanked Mr. Voges for his input, but told him that the
Council had decided not to further relax the procedures to allow the addition,
enlargement or relocation of windows or doors in legally nonconforming side
walls, because of the privacy issue. She noted, however, that the process would
now stop at the Zoning Board level, so that the processing time would be
reduced.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2000 - Amendment to Demolition Provisions of Building
Code - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed minor, nonsubstantive changes to the
introduced ordinance, which uncouples the restoration plan/building permit
requirement from the demolition application. She pointed out that the ordinance
still requires that the demolition permit not be released until construction
documents have been approved. The submittal of restoration plans is retained as
a specific example of the types of additional information that the Director of
Community Development may require in support of a demolition application.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Powell moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser,
and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Presser remarked that this ordinance goes some distance towards meeting
the concerns expressed by ex-Trustee Lanphier.
d)   Ordinance No. M-29-2000 - Zoning Variation: 1183 Scott Ave. -
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed a request for a variation from the Side
Yard and Total Side Yard and Building Line Articulation provisions of the
Zoning Ordinance in order to permit construction of a 2-1/2 story addition,
replacement of a flat roof with the same roof form as the rest of the house,
and extension of the existing 2 foot deep eaves. She reported that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 4-0 to recommend approval of this variation.
Manager Williams explained that this particular lot is one of those that is
split between Winnetka and Glencoe, so that, technically, the land use is
regulated by two jurisdictions. He mentioned that residents in this situation
receive all of their municipal services from Winnetka, but only pay one-third
of their property taxes to Winnetka. He noted that by administrative agreement,
it has been agreed that Winnetka will solely conduct the zoning analysis and
issue building permits for the primary structure on the property, while Glencoe
will handle any accessory structures and grading issues. He indicated that the
Village has been trying to resolve this duality for some time, but thus far has
been unsuccessful.
The owner's architect, Paul Janicki, responded to questions from the Trustees
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance,
by voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-30-2000 - Zoning Variation: 1294 Scott Ave. -
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed a request for a variation from the
Building Line Articulation requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the
construction of a two-story addition. She reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 4-0 to recommend approval of this variation.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2000 - Sign Code Amendment - Introduction. Attorney
Janega reviewed an ordinance prepared at the recommendation of the Village's Ad
Hoc Beautification Committee, which seeks to prohibit construction signs on
residential properties, as is done in neighboring communities, such as Glencoe.
She noted that these signs differ from real estate signs, which offer a parcel
of property for sale or lease, because they serve as advertisements for the
contractors and interject a commercial element into the residential zoning
districts, especially because they are often posted for the duration of a
project, which can exceed a year. The proposed ordinance would prohibit
construction signs only on properties in residential zoning districts and would
not affect real estate signs. In addition, it would require the removal of
nonconforming temporary signs within seven days after the adoption of the
ordinance that makes them nonconforming.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance.
Trustee Aquilino asked how this ordinance would be enforced. Ms. McCammack
indicated that it would be enforced through the Community Development
Department by the Village's field inspectors.
Manager Williams suggested that a notice prohibiting the posting of signs could
be put on the building permit.
Trustee Lien said that the failed to see the difference between real estate
signs and contractor signs as both contribute to the clutter. Further, he said
that he saw no reason to ban advertising signs, which would unnecessarily
penalize residents and contractors, without banning real estate signs. He
pointed out that contractor signs serve the public by letting them know who is
working in the neighborhood, so that neighbors can evaluate such things as the
quality and timeliness of a contractor's work.
Trustee Powell agreed with Trustee Lien about the real estate signs, but spoke
in support of the ordinance, saying that it would, at least, go part of the way
toward clearing the neighborhoods of unsightly signs.
Trustee Brower agreed with Trustee Lien about the public disclosure aspect of
the signs, saying that they provide valuable information to the public.
Trustee Aquilino asked whether the Village could distinguish between the types
of contractor signs. Attorney Janega responded that the Village could not
control on the basis of content and that the ordinance had been drafted to
regulate on a land use basis. She indicated that it might be possible to
rewrite it to say that anything not requiring a permit, i.e. painters, cannot
be displayed.
Trustee Lien suggested that, perhaps, the Village could regulate the design,
size, content, and location of the signs on lots without banning them
altogether. Ms. Janega indicated that that was the policy in Glenview. Mr. Lien
said that he would be in favor of such an ordinance.
Trustee Presser expressed support for the ordinance as drafted, and suggested
that some kind of yard container might be designed and made available to enable
architects and other contractors to make their business cards available.
Trustee Woodbury agreed with the Beautification Committee's desire to minimize
clutter, but indicated that he was more in favor of the Glenview model than
banishment.
By roll call vote, the motion tied. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, and
Powell. Nays: Trustees Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. President Holland, then,
voted in favor of introduction, and the motion carried. She urged residents to
let the Trustees know their opinions before the matter comes up for final vote.
g)   Ordinance No. M-26-2000 - Disposal of Personal Property of the Village -
Reconsideration. Manager Williams explained that only two of the five vehicles
authorized for disposal at auction received bids at or above the minimum value
established in this ordinance, adopted on September 19, 2000. He said that
because the original ordinance contained authorization for only the one
auction, further authorization is required to put the remaining vehicles up for
bid at future auctions. This authorization can best be accomplished by
reconsidering the ordinance and adding a new provision authorizing the property
to be submitted at subsequent auctions, and also permitting the Village Manager
to sell the property in any other manner deemed reasonable so long as the
vehicles are not sold for less than the minimum value established.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to reconsider adoption of
Ordinance No. M-26-2000. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend Ordinance No. M-
26-2000 to add additional authority to sell the specified property as explained
above. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
9. Old Business.
10. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1191 and 1192. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists and recommended approval. Trustee Aquilino moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1191 in the total amount of $1,066,122.40 and Warrant List No. 1192 in the
total amount of $357,334.19. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell,
Lien, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Estimated Tax Levy. Finance Director Ed McKee explained that due to
changes in state law, the Village's process for adopting the property tax levy
has changed. He then reviewed the new procedures and explained that the first
step requires formal action by the Council to set the proposed property tax
levy. He explained that, as part of the 2000/2001 Budget process, the amount
was set at 3% plus new growth. He explained that the Village waits until the
1999 property tax is extended to set the actual dollar amount of the levy. He
referred the Council to the Proposed 2000 Property Tax Levy spreadsheet in the
Agenda, which arrives at a total proposed property tax levy amount of
$9,141,503 and recommended that the Council set the proposed property tax levy
at that amount. Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to set the
proposed property tax levy for the year 2000 at $9,141,503. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Manager Williams announced that because of the new legislation, the Village is
required to hold a Special Meeting on Monday, December 4, 2000, to conduct a
public hearing on the proposed tax levy. He said that all of the business
slated for the Regular Meeting on December 5th would be conducted at the
December 4th meeting, so the December 5th meeting would be cancelled.
c)   Certificate of Appropriateness: 118/120 Green Bay Rd. Ms. McCammack
reviewed an application for a Certificate of Appropriateness for a proposed
renovation of the referenced property including removal of existing box signs
and hardware, replacement of storefront windows in existing openings, a new
wood entry door, signage, and lighting. She noted that the Design Review Board
had voted unanimously to recommend issuance of the Certificate by the Council.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the issuance of
the Certificate of Appropriateness, as depicted in the plans. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Purchase of Rock Salt. Mr. Williams explained that annually the Village
participates in the purchase of rock salt through the State of Illinois bid,
which this year went to IMC/North American Salt Co. at $26.76/ton. He said that
the Village estimates that it will require 1,500 tons of rock salt this season.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the purchase of
rock salt from IMC/North American Salt Co., as above. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
11. Reports.
a)   Village President. President Holland announced that the week of October 8-
14 is Fire Prevention Week and that the Fire Department would be holding an
open house on October 14th from 10:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m. She urged residents to
attend to tour the station house.
President Holland also mentioned that the Village had received a letter from
NiCor indicating that they would be beginning to inspect homes in the Village
in which mercury regulators were removed. She said those residents affected
would be contacted by NiCor.
Finally, she announced that there would be a hazardous waste collection in
Glencoe on October 28th and urged residents to participate. Mr. Williams added
that more information on the collection could be found on the Village's
website.
b)   Village Trustees.
Trustee Lien reported that at the recent meeting of the Plan Commission a
subcommittee had been appointed to study the demolition process and develop
ideas that might assist the Landmark Preservation Committee in carrying out its
mission.
Trustee Aquilino reported on the last meeting of the Beautification Committee.
She explained that the Committee's plan to plant on the embankment between the
railway and the parking lot along the Indian Hill parking lot had been delayed
pending resolution of some issues with Union Pacific. However, she said that
the planting on the strip between Green Bay Road and the parking lot would
proceed this fall as scheduled. She said that the Committee had decided that
its next project, scheduled for the spring planting season, would be the
parkway on the east side of Green Bay Road, between Spruce and Eldorado.
Finally, she announced that a subcommittee had been appointed to develop a gift
list, i.e. newsbins, from which residents could choose gifts to be donated to
the Village. She expressed hope that the list would be available and the
program in place before the holiday season.
Trustee Presser reported that he, Mr. Brower and Mr. Williams had attended the
dedication of the Winnetka Garden Clubs' butterfly garden.
c)   Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported on the results of the recent NWMC
auction. He also announced that an open tour of the Water & Electric Plant has
been scheduled for Saturday, October 21st, at 9:00 a.m. Finally, he announced
that an updated version of the Village's Resident Information Brochure had
recently been mailed to every household and that the initial response was very
positive.
d)   Village Attorney. Ms. Janega reported on her attendance at an IICLE
seminar on employment law and that she had recently completed authoring a
chapter on utility systems for the IICLE Handbook on Municipal Law and Practice
in Illinois. She added that she would be part of the presentation panel in the
attorneys' program at Illinois Municipal League's meeting later in the week.
12. Appointments.
None.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Lien, Woodbury, Presser, and Brower Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:31 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:40 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Presser, Powell, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Brower seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Presser, Lien, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The meeting adjourned at 10:41 p.m.
