Deputy Clerk
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
October 10, 2000
(Approved October 17, 2000)
1. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders and
approximately 33 persons in the audience.
2. Public Hearing – Street Ends.
President Holland declared the public hearing open at 7:33 p.m.
Steve Saunders, Director of Public Works and Village Engineer, described the
publicly owned street rights of way that extend to Lake Michigan at the east
end of Cherry Street, Fisher Lane, Elm Street, Spruce Street, Oak Street and
Willow Road. He explained the current conditions at each location, including
the level of development, the extent of any general public use, and the
condition of the property. He then outlined policy issues as observed by the
staff regarding the level of public use, control of access to the beaches, and
the types of repairs or upgrades required. He explained that any improvements
would be prioritized and worked into a five-year capital improvements plan.
Mr. Saunders then summarized for the Council what the estimated costs of
repairs might be at each street end , with alternatives being provided, based
on the level of public access permitted, and said that the only project that is
currently in the budget for this year is the repairs needed at Fischer Lane.
In response to questions from trustees, Mr. Saunders explained that the Village
has stormwater outflows at five of the six street ends and that the damage to
existing pipes is probably due to ice and wave action when the lake level was
higher. He stated that if any of the outflows are closed off, new storm drains
would have to be run to connect to remaining outflows. He also explained the
pros and cons of open channels and pipe extensions.
Comments from residents in attendance:
Elm Street
Leonard Gugler, 523 Hoyt Lane, strongly opposes a beach at the foot of Elm
Street and believes it is destructive to property values.
David Grossberg, 773 Prospect, speaking also for Nancy and Alan Kubicka, Sam
and Nancy Isaacson and Mr. and Mrs. Dewey Crawford, also opposes use of Elm
Street as a beach and expressed concern about liability.
Lloyd Ferguson, 519 Hoyt, was concerned about liability as well and suggested
that both Elm Street and Oak Street ends be kept private.
Spruce Street
Jean Wright, 555 Sheridan, related a drowning incident at this location and
said that she thinks a pier is attractive to children and is concerned about
safety.
Cherry Street
Joe Johnson, 548 Cherry
Tom Eilers, 520 Cherry
John Cravens, 556 Cherry
Bill Tueting, 488 Ash
Susan Nelson, 504 Ash
Roger Miller, 620 Ash
Cherie Gables, 509 Cherry
Cynthia D'Amico, 475 Poplar
Jill Zapton, 603 Elm
Dean Cameron, 568 Cherry
The residents listed above made comments that support keeping this beach open
to public use, considered it a benefit to Winnetka and a positive effect on
property values, but expressed concerned about the condition of the Cherry
Street end and liability, requested repairs to increase safety and the
installation of an emergency phone or life preservers.
General Comments
Joe Johnson asked that life preservers be placed at each street end, and asked
that the Village construct a barrier in the lake to create a safe swimming area
at Cherry Street.
John Cravens also asked the Village to provide life preservers at the six
locations under discussion and also the public beaches when lifeguards are not
on duty and said he would personally make a donation for the life preservers.
Roger Miller commented further that a solution to neighbor complaints was found
by putting up a gate that is locked at the entrance to Cherry Street beach and
suggested that this could be done at the other street ends for a minimal amount
of money.
Dean Cameron stated that as public property, the street ends are a precious
resource and should be identified as public property and taken care of, or they
should be sold. He said that if the Village were to abandon these properties,
he hoped they would consider the long term use and public access.
Stephanie Sick, 565 Sheridan, spoke against opening the beaches to the public
and also said her husband had written a letter to the Board to that affect.
Mark Crane, 520 Hoyt, expressed concern about safety and liability, the cost to
the Village to protect and police the beaches, as well as services required to
pick up trash. He stated he doesn’t see why the Village should take on
additional responsibility and liability for a sixth beach at Cherry Street,
which is only two blocks from a public beach.
After a thorough discussion of each street end and referring to the matrix of
repair options suggested by Mr. Saunders at each location, the Council made the
following preliminary recommendations:
1.   Erect appropriate and uniform signage at each location to reduce
liability.
2.   At Fisher Lane: Change current use to recreational public access; replace
the railing repair the concrete wall, and add steps; estimated cost $35,000 in
current budget; Council consensus was to proceed with these repairs.
3.   At Spruce Street: Change current use to recreational public access; remove
and replace pier; estimated cost $185,000.
4.   At Elm Street: Continue utility access with only incidental public access;
reconfigure stormwater outlet; estimated cost $50,000.
5.   At Oak Street: Retain no access; no repairs.
6.   At Cherry Street: Change current use to recreational public access; repair
pier, replace steps; estimated cost $70,000.
7.   At Willow: Change current use to utility access only with incidental
public access: remove pipe, replace steps, construct channel, reinforce bluff;
estimated cost $105,000.
In response to additional questions from the audience, President Holland stated
that before any expenditures are made, each item would be incorporated into a
five-year capital improvement plan and revisited by the Council with another
opportunity to receive public comments and concerns.
President Holland closed the public hearing at 9:23 p.m.
3. MC-4-2000 Special Events Permits.
Ms. Janega explained the revisions she had made to the ordinance as a result of
the comments received and further research into ordinances adopted by other
communities.
Manager Williams commented that when the ordinance was introduced it was
basically no pay, no services, but after long discussions with the Police
Chief, they found that they could not refuse services where public safety is
concerned, so staff determined that a permit procedure would be beneficial. He
explained that if the event sponsor violates the ordinance, they can be taken
to court, but services will still be provided.
Mr. Lien stated that as currently written, the definition of special event
would be unenforceable and suggested a minor change be made so that private
functions whose hosts do not hire special police but whose event could affect
the Village’s streets would not be required to go through the permit process.
He said that his concern is that these types of things would trigger the permit
process and that instead of receiving reimbursement from political candidate’s
functions, local events would be penalized.
Mr. Powell requested that a change be made to allow the requests to come to the
Manager who would bring them to the Council.
Ms. Janega noted these changes.
4. Landfill Briefing.
Steve Saunders distributed a memo to Council regarding permit applications for
a Groundwater Management Zone at the Winnetka Landfill and a Proposal to
Establish a Groundwater Management Zone. He explained that after the landfill
was closed, groundwater is forced out of it. He has been notified by the EPA
that they believe that this is the cause of elevated concentrations of
inorganic chemical compounds in the ground water around the landfill. He said
the EPA has required that the Village conduct groundwater samplings to
determine the extent of any contamination, which the Village has done and there
were indications of elevated concentrations of chlorides, sulfates, iron and
dissolved solids. He said that other compounds found were within groundwater
quality standards or were non-detectable. Mr. Saunders stated that the Village
has used these sampling results to develop a Groundwater Management Zone
proposal and a corrective action plan that will maintain the landfill cover so
as to cause the landfill to shed rainwater, rather than absorb it, remove the
leachate by installing five pumping wells, which was done as part of the
closure in 1994 and continue monitoring the groundwater by taking samples
quarterly and submitting the results to the EPA. Mr. Saunders said that the
best scenario would be that the groundwater would stabilize and the worst
scenario would be that a sewer pipe would have to be installed around the
landfill to create an impermeable barrier which would be very expensive.
President Holland asked if we were allowed to put the leachate into the sewer
system. She also asked if this would affect the proposed construction at the
Village Yards.
Mr. Saunders replied that we are allowed to put it into the sanitary sewer
system, where it is filtered at the Water Reclamation District, and that it is
a very mild amount that is sometimes less than what is in groundwater in other
locations in the Village. He said that it is possible that permit applications
for the construction at the Village Yards could be held until this is cleared
by the EPA.
President Holland then said that this is one of the reasons why building cannot
be done at this site for several years to come. She received assurance from Mr.
Saunders and Mr. Williams that staff would be talking to the Park District, the
Forest Preserve and the schools about this issue.
5. Results of Staff “Brainstorming” Concerning Parking Improvements Ideas.
Mr. Williams explained that after discussions with staff, 61 ideas to improve
parking were identified. The two areas of most concern are the areas around New
Trier High School and Lincoln Avenue between Elm and the Community House. He
advised the Council that it costs about $20,000 to create one parking space in
the Village and to keep that in mind as the discussion progresses. He said that
one thing that other Village’s do, that Winnetka does not, is distinguish
between residents and non-residents. He explained that because some of the land
used for parking is State property or railroad property, the Village cannot
distinguish between resident and non-resident in those areas, but where the
property is entirely Village-owned, these designations can be made.
Mr. Williams further explained that the Village has created the C+ parking
passes, which means a person must show a train pass and a Village vehicle
sticker to obtain them. He said that this suggestion was made to try to
encourage non-residents to use the Hubbard Woods parking lot. He offered a
second suggestion to establish a two-tier fee system to encourage use of the
Hubbard Woods lot which would begin by not raising current fees and a third
suggestion to review the fee structure for all parking passes. Mr. Williams
then said that at Indian Hill, the daily passes do not seem to be working out
and six month passes were suggested. He stated that since the renewal of the
lease with New Trier High School is coming up, this may be the Village's
greatest opportunity to change parking. He also said that one new suggestion is
to use the north side of the railroad right-of-way to create parking spaces and
another is the possibility of creating angle parking on Wilson Avenue. He said
other suggestions were to have parking at the landfill with shuttle service,
but that after the Village Yards project is completed, the idea that makes the
most sense is to revisit the Lincoln Avenue parking garage and discuss various
ways to help defray the costs.
6. Benches for Train Stations.
Manager Williams explained that after speaking with Metra, they indicated that
they would help us with benches at the train stations. He said that before
going ahead, the Council must consider a fundamental policy decision about
making improvements on private property using public funds.
Trustee Brower indicated that he considers this to be different, since the
community benefits from this project.
Trustee Aquilino said that the Beautification Committee is coming up with a
gift catalog that would allow people to purchase benches and donate them to the
Village which would help with the cost of the project. The committee is waiting
for the Design Guidelines Committee to recommend a style for the benches.
Mr. Williams reported that because of the upcoming general election, he wanted
to inform the Council that the procedure for political signs is that they
cannot be on the public right-of-way and that if a sign is between the sidewalk
and the street, then it is simply moved to the other side of the sidewalk off
of public property.
7. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:50 p.m.
Respectfully Submitted,
Mary Ivins
Recording Secretary
October 17, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved November 9, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 17, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, Stephen Presser, John
Powell, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, and 6 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   November 9, 2000, Regular Session. Trustee Powell indicated he would not
be present.
b)   November 14, 2000, Study Meeting. Trustee Powell indicated he would not be
present.
5. Approval of Minutes
a)   October 3, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   October 10, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
Judy McKiernan, 384 Hawthorn, related an incident that occurred two years ago
at the intersection of Green Bay Road and Winnetka Avenue that involved a car
accident in which a teenager was injured. She considers this intersection to be
very dangerous and suggested changing the timing of the traffic signals,
prohibiting right turns on red lights and increasing the time to allow
pedestrians to cross the street safely. She reported two accidents that had
occurred two weeks ago, both with injured persons. She also commented that
garbage trucks slow down traffic during peak times. She said she had contacted
the Village Manager's office and had asked that the Village take some action to
make this intersection safe.
Manager Williams responded by saying that the staff has done some research
since receiving her call on October 13, 2000. He said that the staff has talked
to employees on the garbage trucks and they do not pick up garbage during peak
traffic hours at Winnetka and Green Bay, but suggested that the recycling truck
may and Staff is in the process of determining whether that is so. Manager
Williams further reported that there had been 11 vehicle accidents at the
intersection since the first of the year. After speaking with Steve Saunders,
who is also the traffic engineer for the Village, they determined that a left
turn arrow was needed and also increased the green time for the "walk" signal
for pedestrians. He advised Mrs. McKiernan that these traffic signals are due
to be upgraded next year and said staff is hopeful that traffic will decrease
in this area when the New Trier freshmen begin attending school at the west
campus.
Mrs. McKiernan replied that students are all over the intersection during
school hours and hold up traffic by walking in front of cars. She commended the
Police Department, who do caution the kids who are not in the crosswalk, and
thanked Manager Williams for answering her concerns.
Fern Simon, 220 Chestnut, introduced herself as the owner of a business in
Winnetka for 12 years and a participant in the Winnetka Modernism Show for 11
years. She expressed her profound concern about the amount of demolitions in
Winnetka. She said that when she learned that a Howard Van Doren Shaw home at
200 Chestnut was in danger of being destroyed, she was horrified. She feels
that the proposed demolition of this house is an irresponsible and contemptuous
act and that a valuable educational tool is being destroyed. She stated that we
have to be accountable for this kind of destruction and said that if it is
illegal to stop it, maybe there could be one final attempt to preserve the
home. She likened it to an endangered species to be preserved for future
generations. She said she is not against teardowns and not against progress,
but doesn’t believe this home could ever be considered a teardown. Mrs. Simon
said she had many documented articles and essays about the significance of the
work of Howard Van Doren Shaw and believes that the teardown of this home is
destroying primary evidence and future opportunities to study his work. She
stated her belief that the homeowners are participating in the diminishing of
the reasons people want to live in Winnetka. She said she appreciated the
opportunity to express her views and will participate in any way she can to
extend the restrictions for demolishing this home.
President Holland said that the Council's hands are tied in many ways on the
demolition issue and they cannot restrict an individual's right to tear down a
home. She said that unless the homeowner can be convinced to change his plans,
there is not much that can be done. She said the Council hope to educate people
in the Village who own homes of this quality to learn the significance of
preservation, perhaps through the Landmark Preservation Commission.
7. Irwin Lyons, Auditor - Report on Comprehensive Annual Financial Report and
Management Letter.
Irwin Lyons, Miller Cooper & Co., auditor for the Village was introduced by
Finance Director, Ed McKee
Mr. Lyons began his report by pointing out that the Village had been given an
unqualified opinion, which is the highest level that can be received and
referred the Council to the detailed information contained in the footnotes of
the report.
He then reviewed the Management Letter, which he explained is negative by its
nature, but noted that there were only two points issued this year, including
one that is still in the process from last year. He said that all other items
from last year's report had been addressed by Village staff and resolved.
Trustee Aquilino asked if the Village has the option of not implementing GASB
34.
Mr. Lyons replied that the Village's implementation of GASB 34 is a few years
away and currently there are a number of key issues surrounding the use of
auditing or accounting standards that have not been settled. He said that if
the infrastructure portion of GASB 34 is not implemented, then the Village
could receive an adverse opinion. He stated that the bond market is the driving
force. His recommendation is to implement GASB when it is required and to begin
moving in that direction.
Trustee Brower asked if any other municipalities had implemented the plan.
Mr. Lyons said that he thought that possibly Flossmoor had put it together to
see what it would look like, and also Hoffman Estates Park District. However,
he said a park district is an entirely different entity and does not have as
much infrastructure.
Mr. Williams then commented on key points of the audit. He noted that the
Village had invested $44 million in various securities that are Category 1
investments, which are the safest recommended. He also noted that the Illinois
Municipal Retirement Fund is 69% funded and has improved tremendously over the
last few years; that the Police Pension Fund is 97.1% funded and the Fire
Pension Fund is 86.7% funded, which is significantly higher than IMRF, and
shows that the Village is very well funded and moving in right direction. He
also pointed out that the general fund realized $15.5 million of revenue versus
a budget of $13 million; that the electric fund had a net income of $1.2
million, which is 300% more than the projections; that the water fund income
was $757,000 which is $250,000 more than was budgeted; and that the sewer fund
had a slightly better positive net income than was anticipated.
Mr. Williams then noted that of the Village's insurance, the key one is the
general liability policy which has $1 million in retention and that the Village
is a member of the High Level Access Liability Pool with 14 other communities
where risk is shared. He said that in the ten year analysis of tax levies from
1989 - 1998, the Winnetka tax levy has gone up an average of 4.3% per year. Mr.
Williams also reported that the electric rates, which were at 10.84 cents per
Kwh two years ago, had decreased to 9.44 cents per Kwh this year reflecting
that the strategic plan to reduce rates by 5% has been followed. He ended his
comments by stating that it had been a very good year for the Village
financially and that the total bottom line is that the Village is 3% under
operating expenses.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. M-29-2000 - Zoning Variation: 1183 Scott Ave. - Adoption.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting variations from provisions of the Village Code in order to permit a
2 1/2 story addition and replacement roof.
Trustee Powell expressed concerned about the encroachment into side yard and
asked the homeowner if it could be excluded from the project.
Mrs. Briggs answered that it is a decorative feature that matches the main part
of the house and the home would look off-balance if it was not done. She showed
the Council pictures and drawings of the proposed design. She said that after a
member of the Zoning Board looked at the site, she felt that the house would
look better as planned and that the variation would not be much of an intrusion
into the side yard.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-30-2000 - 1294 Scott Avenue - Adoption. Trustee Aquilino,
seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting a
variation from the Building Line Articulation provisions of the Village Code in
order to permit the construction of a two-story addition.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Lien, Brower, and Presser, Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2000 - Sign Code Amendment - Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed changes to the introduced ordinance, which prohibits construction
signs only on properties in residential zoning districts and would not affect
real estate signs nor signs in commercial districts. It would also require the
removal of nonconforming temporary signs within seven days after the adoption
of the ordinance that makes them nonconforming.
President Holland asked that the Council's table this item, because of numerous
conversations with architects and others concerned with construction, who
believe the signs to be the products of their businesses. She said that the
Design Guidelines Committee would be recommending guidelines on this issue.
After a short discussion, Trustee Lien moved to table this item and Trustee
Aquilino seconded. By voice vote, the motion to table passed unanimously.
d)   Public Hearing: Average Side Yard Definition - Introduction. Village
Attorney Janega explained that Ordinance MC-8-2000 was drafted after
discussions held on average lot width at the September 12, 2000, Study Session
and amends various definitions of the Zoning Ordinance pertaining to lot
dimensions, yards and setbacks.
President Holland opened the public hearing at 8:20 a.m. She read a letter from
Mr. Pinkerton, who was unable to attend the meeting, voicing his approval of
the changes to the Zoning Ordinance.
Attorney Janega called attention to the issues specific to the public hearing:
average lot width, the impact of flag lots on yards and setbacks, and the
terms, "set back" vs. "setback." She explained that the significant cause of
irrational results in calculations of average lot width arise in instances
where a lot is oddly configured or is a flag lot that requires an
administrative decision to determine the front lot line. She said that the
amendment changes the definition so that once the front lot line is identified,
the rear lot line is opposite to it and the side yards are then perpendicular
to it. Ms. Janega stated that this one amendment will address most of the
issues that have arisen in calculating lot width. She added this amendment also
addresses flag lots and removes the flag pole portion of the lot from the
equation so that the calculation of lot width is measured according to the body
of the lot. She said that staff had noticed that one unintended consequence of
this change may be an increase in the subdivisions of flag lots. She said she
hoped to have an analysis completed for the Council at the next meeting or a
proposed solution. Ms. Janega then described the need to make the terms
"setback" vs. "set back" consistent throughout the Village Code.
Trustee Woodbury questioned the meaning of absolute width in the draft
ordinance.
Ms. Janega stated that she will ask the staff how this term is used and then
will clarify it, if necessary.
President Holland closed the public hearing at 8:32 p.m.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-8-
2000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Old Business.
None.
10. New Business
a)   Request by the Fell Company for Temporary Use of the Sidewalk for Trunk
Show. Manager Williams received a letter from Mr. David Fell requesting the
temporary use of the sidewalk in front of his store at 511 Lincoln Avenue for
their 9th annual North Face and Patagonia Trunk Show on Saturday, November 4,
2000. He explained that Mr. Fell wishes to use the sidewalk to set up tables
and racks and hang banners and decorations. He is also requesting the use of 8
parking spaces on the east side of Lincoln Avenue in front of the store. Mr.
Williams said that this request was similar to one approved by the Village
Council last year. The request has been reviewed with the Public Works Director
and the Police and Fire Departments, all of whom had no objections.
Trustee Presser asked if additional police officers would be required for the
event.
Manager Williams replied that it would not be necessary.
Trustee Brower moved to approve the request, seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1193 and 1194. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists and recommended approval. Trustee Powell moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1193 in the total amount of $634,027.26 and Warrant List No. 1194 in the
total amount of $909,962.61. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell,
Lien, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Tower Road Jurisdictional Transfer. Public Works Director Steve Saunders
explained that the Village plans to rehabilitate Tower Road from Green Bay to
Forestway Drive with a water main replacement, drainage improvements,
underground utility installation and pavement repairs. He explained that a
permit must be obtained from IDOT to do these improvements and placing the
utilities under the roadway requires the transfer of jurisdiction of Tower Road
from IDOT to the Village. He also said that IDOT has agreed to the transfer and
will fund up to $200,000 for pavement repairs. He explained that the transfer
runs from Vernon Avenue west to the Village's corporate limits and would
include 50% control of the intersection of Tower Road and Forestway Drive. Mr.
Saunders further stated that during verbal discussions with IDOT, they had
indicated that the jurisdiction of the intersection of Tower and Forestway
could be excluded from the transfer.
Trustee Aquilino asked if there was an alternative to putting the utilities
under the roadway.
Mr. Saunders replied that the parkways were not wide enough to provide the
required distance from the placement of the utility lines.
After discussion of the advantages and disadvantages of maintaining the
intersection, the Council agreed that it would be to the Village's benefit to
assume 50% jurisdiction of Tower Road and Forestway Drive.
Trustee Lien moved to approve the letter agreement with IDOT, seconded by
Trustee Brower. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
d)   DK Recycling Systems Monthly Report. Manager Williams summarized the
September report from DK Recycling Systems operations at the Winnetka Compost
Site.
11. Reports.
a)   Village President.
President Holland reported that SWANCC would be conducting a household
hazardous waste collection on October 28, 2000 from 8 a.m. to 3 p.m. in the
commuter parking lot, east of the railroad tracks at Park and Green Bay Road in
Glencoe. She urged residents to take advantage of this service to get rid of
old paint, chemicals, batteries, and other materials that may cause fire
hazards.
b)   Village Trustees.
Trustee Woodbury reported that the Design Guidelines Committee during its
meeting October 16, 2000, recommended that an architectural and planning
consultant be engaged for the Committee's first meeting in November.
Trustee Brower and Trustee Aquilino attended a meeting of the Chamber of
Commerce last week where a discussion was held on the holiday lighting. Trustee
Brower said a meeting of the Forestry Commission thereafter ended amicably,
with the agreement that the lights will come down after the Christmas season.
Manager Williams added that the Forestry Commission's recommendation will come
before the Council at its November 9th meeting.
c)   Village Manager.
Manager Williams announced that the Council will be taking a tour of the Water
and Electric plant at 9:00 a.m. this Saturday, October 21, 2000, and urged the
public to attend.
d)   Village Attorney.
12. Appointments.
President Holland nominated Fern Simon to replace Marie Harris who has served
on the Landmark Preservation Commission since 1992 and has asked to be relieved
of her duties. She thanked Mrs. Harris for her service and then informed the
Council that Mrs. Simon has a masters degree in architectural history from
Northwestern and shows much enthusiasm for educational projects on historic
preservation in the Village.
Trustee Aquilino moved to nominate Fern Simon to the Landmark Preservation
Commission, seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
President Holland also thanked Kurt Karmin, a member of the Board of Fire and
Police Commissioners whose time is limited during the winter months and who has
agreed to step down. She nominated Don Derning, a former Village Trustee and
Police Chief, who has been of immeasurable service to the Village and who has
indicated that he would happy and honored to serve on the Board.
Trustee Brower moved to nominate Don Derning to the Board of Fire and Police
Commissioners, seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:05 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent:
None. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn the meeting, and Trustee Lien seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Presser, Lien, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The meeting adjourned at 10:06 p.m.
______________________________________
Deputy Clerk
November 9, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved November 21, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday,
November 9, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Cindy McCammack, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and ten persons in
the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
a)   November 14, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Powell, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 21, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
V. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 17, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-33-2000 - Landmark Designation: 535 Cherry - Introduction.
Nan Greenough, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission briefly
explained the rating system used to evaluate properties. Then she reviewed the
Commission's findings with regard to this property and presented slides showing
some of the home's special architectural features. Ms. Greenough noted that,
based upon the System for the Evaluation of Landmarks, the home had received
85.2 points and had been rated "unique."
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-34-2000 - Landmark Designation: 687 Cherry - Introduction.
Nan Greenough again reviewed the Landmark Preservation Commission's finding and
presented slides of the home. Ms. Greenough noted that, based upon the System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks, the home had received a score of 71.2 and had
been rated "significant."
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-31-2000 - Special Use Permit: 566 Chestnut St. -
Introduction. Ms. McCammack reviewed a request from Cook Inlet/Voice Stream for
a Special Use Permit to allow for installation of a Wireless Telecommunications
Service Facility (WTSF). She explained that the proposed antenna array would be
installed and concealed on top of the existing chimney at 566 Chestnut Street
("Laundry Mall"), located in the C-2 Commercial Zoning District. All WTSF
installations in the C-2 Zoning District are permitted only by Special Use
Permit. She noted that although this is a Level 3 priority location, as
determined by the WTSF ordinance, all of the higher priority sites had been
eliminated because they are either fully utilized or engineering data indicated
that they are not technically feasible. She said that both the Zoning Board of
Appeals and the Plan Commission had voted favorably on the request for the
Special Use Permit, with the Zoning Board also recommending that the Council
not permit the existing chimney height to increase. She pointed out, however,
that this recommendation does not apply because a nonconforming height for a
concealed antenna array is permitted by ordinance.
Trustee Powell asked for clarification of the Zoning Board's recommendation.
Ms. McCammack indicated that the Board's recommendation carried no official
authority, but was intended to point out to the Council that the applicants
would be extending the height of the chimney. She repeated, however, that no
variance is required because the proposed facility would be completely
concealed.
Terry Sullivan, appeared on behalf of the applicant, Voice Stream, to respond
to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Powell then inquired as to where the applicant intended to house all of
its necessary support equipment.
Ms. Sullivan replied that self-enclosed cabinets would be placed inside of the
existing penthouse.
President Holland asked for confirmation that the applicant would not be adding
any new structures, except the extension of the chimney, on top of the roof.
Ms. Sullivan so confirmed.
Manager Williams inquired about the length of the antennas inside of the shroud
and asked whether the applicant had considered placing them on the outside of
the chimney structure and painting them the same color as the chimney. He also
inquired about the possibility of using shorter antennas.
Ms. Sullivan responded that the proposal was to place nine antennas, each
approximately 6 feet in length and 5 inches in diameter, atop the chimney
within the shroud. She passed around a sample of the shroud material for
inspection and said that the site would be more concealed if the antennas were
on the inside of an appropriately painted shroud than if they were mounted in
the open on the outside of the chimney. She said Voice Stream felt that the
added height was necessary to serve the anticipated volume of traffic, which
could not be served by shorter antennas. She indicated that if Voice Stream
were to attach antennas to the outside of the chimney, the antenna array might
extend out further from the chimney. She also pointed out that, if the company
operates from within the chimney, the chimney stays intact, whereas, if they
mount the array onto the chimney, the integrity of the bricks and mortar might
be compromised.
Manager Williams noted that in order to get the building permit necessary to
attach antennas to the chimney, the applicant would have to agree to improve
the chimney's structural integrity to accommodate the array.
Mr. Williams requested that computer generated images depicting both options be
prepared for consideration by the Council before final action is taken on this
request. He pointed out to the Council that there is a policy decision to be
made on this issue since Voice Stream has an option.
Attorney Janega asked whether the cables would be inside or outside of the
chimney. Ms. Sullivan replied that they would be inside the chimney.
Attorney Janega explained staff's determination that pursuant to the existing
WTSF ordinance, no height variation is required. However, she said that, given
this experience, Council might wish to consider amending the WTSF ordinance in
order to prevent a second carrier from piggybacking onto this already existing,
nonconforming chimney by merely extending the shroud.
Trustee Brower inquired as to whether the Village required any financial
information from the applicant.
Manager Williams said that the applicant was required to post a $5,000 bond,
but that beyond that, this issue is strictly one of zoning and aesthetics. Ms.
Janega added that all special use permits (SUPs) are conditioned upon the
completion of the work within a prescribed timeframe. She indicated that a
condition could be added to the SUP that would require the removal of the
equipment should the applicant not be fully in operation within the prescribed
time period.
In response to a question from Trustee Aquilino, Manager Williams explained why
no suitable Level 1 or 2 sites could be located by the applicant. He pointed
out that this chimney previously had been identified as an ideal cell site
location by the Village's consultant.
Ron White, 434 Berkeley, commented on the location and suggested that the
applicant pay careful attention to the lightning rods embedded in the top of
the chimney. He also spoke in support of Council's decision in another instance
to not permit a fiberglass shroud.
Trustee Woodbury voiced support for the chimney location, but expressed concern
with the prospect of extending a nonconforming height any further. In addition,
he pointed out that computer generated images can be suspect.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-32-2000 - Zoning Variation: 191 Linden - Adoption. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance. Ms.
McCammack reviewed a request from Saints Faith, Hope and Charity for a zoning
variation to permit construction of a handicapped accessible ramp, which is
necessary to comply with the Illinois Accessibility Code. She explained that
because the ramp will be obscured from visibility from the public right-of-way,
no Design Review Board consideration is necessary, and noted that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted unanimously to recommend approval of the request.
Manager Williams noted that staff's interpretation was that this was not a
significant zoning issue, but merely a safety feature for the church. He
indicated that if Council wished to do so, it could waive introduction of this
ordinance so that the church could proceed with construction during the
favorable weather.
Attorney Janega explained that introduction can be waived by a unanimous vote
of the Council members present, if they find that the procedures do not, in
this instance, serve the best interests of the Village, because it would
unnecessarily delay accessibility compliance.
Trustee Brower withdrew his motion. Trustee Presser concurred. Trustee Brower
then, moved to waive introduction of the ordinance for the reasons stated by
Manager Williams and Attorney Janega. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Presser. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien.
Finally Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the
ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2000 - Special Events Permits - Adoption. Attorney
Janega commented briefly on the history of this action and reviewed changes
made to the document since the October 10th Study Session. She noted that the
ordinance presented for adoption addresses all comments made by Council and
staff. With regard to Council's expressed desire to specifically exclude the
annual "Winnetka Days" sidewalk sale and the Children's Fair from the special
events requirements, she explained that the Council could specifically exclude
these events and/or permit the requirements to be waived in certain specified
circumstances. She noted that, as redrafted, the ordinance contains language
covering both options.
Trustee Presser moved to amend the draft ordinance to reflect all of the
changes highlighted in the revised draft, without exception. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Powell. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: Trustee Lien.
Absent: None.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, then moved to adopt the draft, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: Trustee Lien. Absent: None.
VIII. Old Business.
IX. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1195 and 1196. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists and recommended approval. In doing so, he noted that the appraisal of
Village property had come in at approximately $73,000,000. He then moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1195 in the amount of $1,018,814.32 and Warrant List
No. 1196 in the amount of $319,114.04. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Aquilino, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Staff Update: Construction Manager. Mr. Saunders reviewed the process used
to select a Construction Manager firm for the Yards Rehabilitation Project. He
informed the Council that negotiations were currently underway with Riley
Construction Company and expressed hope that the Technical Committee would have
a contract to bring before the Council for approval within the coming month.
As a member of the Technical Committee, Trustee Woodbury remarked that there
had been general consensus among the members and staff that Riley was the best
choice.
President Holland noted that she had had some reservation, but was sure that
Riley would perform well because both Mr. Saunders and the Technical Committee
would be carefully overseeing the project.
Irv Footlick, 1548 Tower Rd., volunteered the services of his firm should the
Council desire its services.
c)   Street Reconstruction Change Order. Mr. Saunders informed the Council that
the 2000 Street Rehabilitation Project has been completed. He noted that the
final cost of the improvements to Ash, Cherry, Elm, Oak, and Chatfield had been
$22,523.71 above the bid amount due to additional quantities of asphalt used to
resurface an extra block of Ash Street, but pointed out that the total work is
still below the budgeted amount.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve a change order
in the amount of $22,523.71. The motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Forestry Commission Recommendation Holiday Lighting on Trees. Trustee
Brower reported that the Chamber of Commerce wished to discuss this issue with
the Council before any decision is made. He suggested that consideration of the
item be deferred until after the holidays. Trustee Aquilino concurred.
In response to the Chamber's request President Holland set the issue for
discussion at the January Study Session.
e)   Policy Direction: 1548 Tower Road. Ms. McCammack reviewed a request to
permit the installation of an attached greenhouse at the referenced property.
The applicants propose to install a one story greenhouse, which will be
attached to and accessed through the home, on the existing concrete patio. This
addition would increase the total roofed lot coverage and the existing
nonconforming gross floor area beyond the permitted maximums. She noted that
the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted unanimously to recommend that these
requests be denied.
Irv Footlick, the applicant, and Art Catrell of FW Associates, spoke in support
of the request citing the small size of the proposed addition and saying that
because of the age of the home, it shouldn't be held to the same standards as
new construction. Mr. Catrell mentioned that if the home were to be built
today, it could be made to comply simply by manipulating the ceiling height.
Mrs. Klein, a neighbor, also spoke in support of the request.
Mr. Patrick O'Donoghue, 1208 Ash Street, voiced objection based on Council's
rejection of a similar request to add onto his property.
Ms. McCammack clarified the details of the existing site and the proposed
addition. She pointed out that staff had prepared an analysis of the subject
property and the immediately adjacent vacant lot, also owned by the Footlicks,
to determine how much would have to be carved out of the adjacent lot to bring
the proposed addition into compliance. She noted that doing so would reduce the
adjacent lot below the size permitted as a buildable lot.
Mr. Footlick disputed the Village's calculations. In addition he said that he
has no intention of adding further on to his home or of selling the adjacent
piece of property. However, he objected to this solution saying that it would
be a fiscally irresponsible decision.
Trustee Lien expressed his opinion that this situation represents a typical
example of an oversized home and cannot be differentiated from similar
situations in which the Council has unanimously denied requests for variations,
in particular, a fairly recent instance on Forest Glen North.
Trustee Woodbury concurred, saying that this home is already more than 19% over
the permitted FAR and that the request adds too much.
Trustee Presser clarified that the two lots are presently separate lots of
record that could be combined to accommodate the construction of the greenhouse
without a variance but that removing a sufficient portion of the second lot
would render it unbuildable. Ms. McCammack so confirmed.
Trustee Brower voiced severe reservations about the request in view of the fact
that there is a complying alternative, which the applicant has rejected.
Citing the case on Forest Glen North as precedent, President Holland affirmed
Council's consensus to deny this request. She explained that there was an
appeal process available to the Footlicks if they chose to pursue it.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to accept the recommendation
of the Zoning Board of Appeals to deny the variation. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid No. 00-018: Water Main Contract. Mr. Williams reviewed the bids for
the replacement of the existing water main on Mt. Pleasant between Locust and
Glendale and Staff's recommendation that the bid be awarded to the lowest
bidder, Lampignano & Son Construction Co., in the total amount of $77,650.00.
He noted that the Village had been very pleased with their work on the Cedar
Street sewer line.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the bid, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
g)   Design Guidelines Consultant Recommendation. Ms. McCammack reviewed the
selection process and recommendation from the Ad-hoc Design Guidelines
Committee that Bauer Latoza be retained to assist in the development of
evaluation criteria for the Design Review Board. In addition, she asked that
the Village Council authorize the execution of a contract with that firm for
professional services in the amount of $73,570. She noted, however, that this
amount exceeds that available in the Community Development Department's current
year contractual services budget, which is $20,000. Manager Williams indicated
that the shortfall could be made up from the contingency fund already budgeted
by Council.
Trustee Woodbury commented on the decision of the Design Guidelines Committee
and expressed its belief that this firm would provide the necessary leadership
to develop a long overdue, effective tool to assist the Village in managing
growth and the design process within the community.
Trustee Aquilino concurred. She inquired whether the timeline was realistic.
Mr. Woodbury responded that, while it is an aggressive schedule, it is the goal
of the committee to complete the project by spring. He then moved to approve
the recommendation. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien.
Ron White, 434 Berkeley, commented on the consultants' approach, proposal, and
qualifications.
Manager Williams pointed out that the firm's selection was very much community
based, in that it had been made by a committee composed of representatives from
numerous other Village boards and committees, and Trustee Woodbury remarked
that the Committee anticipated a collaborative effort by, and for, Village
residents.
President Holland invited Mr. White and other interested residents to join in a
walking tour of the Village's commercial districts scheduled for Saturday,
November 18th and urged all interested residents to attend public meetings
scheduled for December 7th and January 11th.
By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Mr. Williams called the Council's
attention to the Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports contained in the Agenda
packets. He reported that the undergrounding project was proceeding on
schedule.
X. Reports.
a)   Village Trustees. Trustee Presser commented on the current edition of the
Winnetka Report and mentioned the article on the SWANCC balefill case on the
front page.
Trustee Woodbury reported on a recent meeting of the Historical Society Board
saying that they are working on plans which would complement the Village's
study of its demolition process. In addition, he noted that they hope to move
the log house to its permanent location in the coming year.
b)   Village Manager. Manager Williams announced that the Illinois Municipal
Electric Agency had recently presented the "Generating Member of the Year
Award" to the Village in recognition of "outstanding professionalism and
commitment in the operation" of the Village's power plant.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland announced that Joan Evanich has resigned from Landmark
Preservation Commission and nominated Jane Burns to complete the remainder of
her term, until March 31, 2001. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
moved to approve the appointment of Ms. Burns. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and
Section 2(c)(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Presser and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Powell. The Council convened into Executive Session at
10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:58 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent:
None. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:59 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
November 14, 2000
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved November 21, 2000)
1. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7: 32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, Stephen Presser and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Public Works and Village Traffic Engineer Steve Saunders,
Deputy Police Chief Eric Bennett and approximately 24 persons in the audience.
2. Truck Traffic.
Manager Williams summarized the history of the issue, referring the Trustees to
the Agenda Report outlining possible courses of action identified by Mr.
Saunders. He pointed out that the Council needed to take into consideration not
only local concerns, but also, the impact that any policy decision might have
on regional needs.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the five options, as follows:
(a)   Assign permitted truck routes in the village, ban trucks from certain
specific streets, and educate drivers and trucking companies in the routes and
their use.
(b)   Ban through truck traffic on individual streets in response to individual
requests.
(c)   Develop a regional strategy to control through truck traffic by working
with surrounding communities.
(d)   Do nothing, other than continue enforcing existing speed limits and
traffic control devices.
(e)   Seek State Police Assistance for Vehicle weighing on certain streets such
as Tower and Willow Roads.
He also commented on the pros and cons of each option. He emphasized that there
is no way to altogether eliminate truck traffic from Winnetka's streets because
many of the trucks traveling the roads are providing goods and/or services to
Winnetka residents and businesses.
Manager Williams pointed out that should the Council choose option (a), there
are essentially only three east-west routes through the Village. Choosing the
one(s) to designate presents a Hobson's choice because all three go through
residential neighborhoods, from which there would likely be significant
opposition, and bypass schools.
Trustee Woodbury inquired about the extent of the Village's control over each
of these routes. Mr. Saunders replied that at present IDOT exercises some
authority over both Tower and Willow Roads, but that Winnetka-Hill Road is
mostly under the jurisdiction of the Village. Tower Road, he noted, would come
under the control of the Village by the end of next year.
Trustee Brower expressed his belief that option (c) was the optimal solution.
President Holland, however, remarked that thus far, she had been unsuccessful
in garnering any support from neighboring communities for developing a regional
solution. Mr. Saunders suggested that a good approach would be for the
communities to cooperatively develop suggested routes for trucks heading toward
broad directional destinations. He pointed out, however, educating truck
drivers as to the desired routes and enforcing infractions would still be
significant problems.
Trustee Aquilino observed that other communities have designated truck routes.
Mr. Saunders acknowledged that this is the case and could be done, but
reiterated that in this community, selecting that route is an extremely
difficult choice.
President Holland asked about the possibility of installing additional traffic
control devices.
Citing one of the suspect causes of the increased traffic flow in general as
the jamming of the Edens Expressway, Mr. Williams mentioned that the Village
might consider making it less convenient to use Winnetka as a short cut. Among
the possibilities that he mentioned were (1) narrowing Tower Road to make it
slower; (2) putting a stop sign at Vernon; and (3) putting unsynchronized
signal lights on Willow and/or Tower Roads. Among the consequences of such
actions, however, would be that residents living where signs/lights are
installed would have their problems increased and Winnetkans traveling these
routes would be inconvenienced as well as transients.
In response to complaints about late night truck traffic noise, Trustee Lien
suggested that if it can legally be done, the Village ban truck traffic during
nighttime hours. Further he suggested that the Village might consider other
traffic control measures, such as installing calming strips, reducing speed
limits as far as permitted, and making both Willow and Tower Roads 'no pass'
zones. He expressed little hope that neighboring communities would cooperate as
all want to push the problem outside of their boundaries, but supported the
concept of makes it inconvenient to use Village streets so that trucks would
seek out more expedient routes.
Trustee Woodbury concurred. He strongly supported the installation of a traffic
signal at Crow Island School for safety's sake and said that he thought the
other options were worthy of review.
Mr. Saunders pointed out that so long as Willow and Tower Roads are under split
jurisdictions, traffic control matters are complicated, as the Village would
have to obtain IDOT's permission before doing anything. He said that IDOT has
strict rules and regulations which determine the placement of traffic control
devices and speed limits. He also mentioned that research indicates that stop
signs at intersections actually increase the incidence of certain types of
accidents. Further, Mr. Saunders discussed with the Trustees the Village's
experience with a trial speed hump, the likelihood of being able to lower speed
limits, and the ramifications of making both Tower and Willow 'no pass' roads.
Manager Williams noted that when the Village takes of jurisdiction of Tower
Road, it will be totally under the Village's control permitting the Village to
effect any means of traffic control it deems desirable. He noted, however, that
this control does not come without a cost to the Village in terms of street
maintenance and repairs.
President Holland then called for comments from the audience.
The following residents shared their views and suggestions about truck traffic
throughout the Village, particularly along Willow and Tower Roads. Many related
personal experiences and witnessed incidents.
Tricia & John Hartner, 1215 Tower Rd.
MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln.
Lolly & Cy Rosengarten, 1460 Tower
Arlyn Presser, 785 Willow Rd.
Melba Gold, 1250 Willow Rd.
Tom Healy, 1173 Tower
Carol Hayes, 1487 Tower
Hal Miller, 1107 Tower
Chester Rogal, 1143 Willow
John Cook, 765 Willow
Bob Doepel, 1210 Willow
Navweed Anwar, 1195 Tower
Greg Swenson, 808 Willow
Linda Kramer/Mary Kennedy, 370 Glendale
Robert Bertram, 877 Auburn Rd.
Thomas Izo 969 Willow
Jean Brainerd 910 Willow
C. J. Beckerman, 705 Willow (by letter)
Repeatedly the residents cited concern for pedestrians, especially children,
and complained about noise pollution and excessive speed. There was a general
consensus among the speakers that the Police Department does not enforce its
speed and traffic laws effectively. In response to the latter, Deputy Chief
Bennett reported that the Village issues more than 2,000 tickets for traffic
violations per year. He also said that the annual Police Department Survey
shows an overall general satisfaction rate among residents with the Police
Department's traffic enforcement activity. Mr. Williams said that he would have
the Police Department prepare a report on the location and issuance of tickets
for the last several years.
The extended discussion produced the following ideas to reduce/discourage truck
traffic and otherwise calm traffic:
a)   Brick Road
b)   Speed Humps
c)   Double Yellow Lines for both roads
d)   Unsynchronized new, traffic lights
e)   Ban trucks from midnight till 5 a.m.
f)   Install blinking red light at Tower and Gordon Terrace
g)   Weight restrictions for trucks
h)   Specify truck routes for demolition projects
i)   Regional approach with neighboring villages
j)   Move up jurisdictional transfer of Willow Road (reduce four lanes to three
lanes for western section and reduce speed limit from 30 mph to 25 mph)
k)   Reduce width of Willow Road at Green Bay Road
l)   Stop signs on Tower at Vernon
m)   President Holland’s “Welcome to Winnetka” signs
n)   Reduce speed limit on Tower Rd. to 25mph after we take over from IDOT
Manager Williams indicated that Staff would analyze these suggestions and
return in December with suggestions as to how best to improve the situation.
President Holland slated further discussion for the Monday, December 4th,
Regular Meeting.
3. Proposed Signage for Street Ends.
Manager Williams reported that this agenda item was a follow up from the
previous meeting where Council had directed staff to develop uniform signage
for the street ends that abut lake Michigan. He indicated that the versions
included in Council's packet were merely suggestions and that they could
ultimately be sent to the DRB.
Before opening the discussion to the audience, President Holland pointed out
that the Council had already made its decision about how to proceed on the
street end issue and that this conversation was about appropriate signage only.
Donald Harnack, 459 Sheridan Rd., expressed disappointment at Council's
decision to continue the status quo at the Cherry Street street end. He said
that although that decision resolves the issue for the Cherry Street neighbors,
it does nothing to address the concerns of the riparian property owners. He
recited incidents of accidents, abuse and trespass experienced by neighboring
property owners. Mr. Harnack urged the Council to be sure that appropriate
signage is installed to insure that people understand what the use limitations
of the street ends are and make a clear distinction between public and private
property. In addition, he requested that the Cherry Street access be regularly
patrolled and the gate consistently locked after hours. Finally, he voiced
concern about the Village's liability, even with signage, should there be a
serious accident resulting from improper use of the street end.
Bill Sick, 565 Sheridan, and Denise Ottaviano, 455 Sheridan supported Mr.
Harnack's comments. Mr. Sick indicated that he had been advised by private
counsel to inform the Village of his concerns about permitting access to the
unattended beachfront via the street ends and, thereby, increasing his personal
liability.
Pam Harnack requested that the Council (1) attend to the dog issue, (2)
identify the difference between public and private property (3) clarify
regulations and penalties, and (4) enforce the rules regularly and
consistently.
Manager Williams acknowledged that the dog problem resulted from a loophole in
Village ordinances and suggested that, perhaps, Council might want to change
this. He emphasized that there exist strongly polarized, competing points of
view about the use of the street ends.
Trustee Aquilino pointed out that any signage language needs to be carefully
constructed because it is enforceable, e.g. "Swim at own risk" is different
than "No swimming." She said that it had been her understanding that Council's
decision was that this is an "own risk" situation.
The Trustees discussed possible wording for the signs. Manager Williams
suggested that he, staff and the neighbors get together to identify possible
wording and bring those back ideas to the Council for consideration.
President Holland objected to any action that would hold up the repair of the
unsanitary conditions at the Spruce Street and Fisher Lane street ends. She
said that the Council could revisit its decision about the use of the street
ends at another meeting if it chose to do so, but that these repairs could not
wait. There was no disagreement about proceeding with these repairs as quickly
as possible.
Manager Williams said that the Village would investigate possibilities to
clearly separate and identify public vs. private property and return with the
ordinance tools necessary to enforce restrictions on the use of the street
ends.
4. Analysis of Discrepancies Between Comprehensive Plan and Existing Sidewalk
System.
Mr. Williams explained that this item had been placed on the agenda in response
to a request from MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln., to consider reprioritizing
the installation of a sidewalk on Pine Street between Rosewood and Hibbard. In
doing so, he referred Council to a memorandum prepared in 1997 analyzing the
Village's sidewalk system. He also reviewed two previous instances in which the
Council had considered the same request and turned it down.
Trustee Brower indicated that he had spoken with several residents, even on
Pine Street, who were supportive of the idea.
Manager Williams said that the Village certainly could install such a sidewalk
but warned that the most property owners on whichever side of the street is
chosen for the sidewalk would be irate.
MollyBelle Berish reiterated previously expressed concerns about the safety of
pedestrians on Pine Street.
Trustee Lien opined that there probably should be a sidewalk on one side of the
street, but pointed out that (1) it would cause the destruction of a lot of
mature trees and landscaping that would take a long time to replace, and (2)
that it makes little sense to put a sidewalk on either side when it would
deadend at Hibbard where there is no sidewalk on the east side.
Manager Williams suggested that since traditionally this Village is heavily
invested in community-based decision making, a question be posed for inclusion
on next year's Caucus questionnaire to see if there is widespread support for
changing the priority of a Pine Street sidewalk. In the interim, he indicated
that staff would re-examine and re-evaluate the entire list to see if changed
circumstances required an overall shuffling of priorities.
5. Reports.
Mr. Williams reported that he had given Land Rover permission, on a temporary
basis only, to use the property at 518 Winnetka Ave. for storing and washing
vehicles, but had made it clear that they are not to drive through the adjacent
neighborhoods or park vehicles so that they overhang the sidewalk.
6. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:40 p.m.
November 21, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL MEETING
(Approved December 4, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 21, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, and two persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
IV. Quorum.
a)   December 4, 2000, Special Meeting. President Holland reminded the Trustees
that this meeting would be on a Monday. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Presser, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   December 11, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
V. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 9, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
b)   November 14, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Sarah Carey, 1153 Oakley, expressed concern about the demolition of
historically significant housing in the Village, particularly the Howard Van
Doren Shaw home on Mt. Pleasant, and the tear down issue overall. She urged the
Council to take steps to slow down the demolition process so as to permit
owners more time to consider restoration options.
Ms. Carey also voiced objection to the Village's removal, without any notice to
the neighborhood, of an architectural element marking the entrance to Oakley
Ln.
With regard to the first issue, Manager Williams replied that the Village Code
clearly set 90 days as the limit for delaying a demolition. With regard to the
second, Mr. Williams acknowledged that the Village had removed the brick pillar
because it was believed to be a potential hazard. He indicated that the Village
would be getting quotes on reconstructing the pillar, but said that the old
materials were in such poor condition that they could not be reused. He pointed
out that the expenditure for any reconstruction activity would have to be
authorized by the Council and noted that there are numerous similar structures
in varying states of disrepair throughout the Village that staff would
inventory for Council's consideration.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-33-2000 - Landmark Designation: 535 Cherry - Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
designating this home as a Village landmark. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser,
and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-34-2000 - Landmark Designation: 687 Cherry - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance
designating this home as a Village landmark. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien,
and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2000 - Amending Lot Dimension, Yard and Setback and
Related Provisions of the Zoning Ordinance, and Amending Related Provisions of
the Subdivision and Tree Preservation Regulations - Adoption. Ms. Janega
explained that this ordinance amends various definitions of the Zoning
Ordinance pertaining to lot dimensions, yards and setbacks. She noted that
these amendments, in turn, triggered the need for corresponding amendments to
various provisions of the Zoning Ordinance, Subdivision Regulations and Tree
Preservation Ordinance, where those redefined terms are used. Since its
introduction, the draft has been amended extensively to add recitals, amend
additional definitions, and add amendments to the various regulatory provisions
to reflect the amended definitions. She pointed out that despite these
amendments, the ordinance still addresses the same substantive areas -- average
lot width, flag lots and the term "setback" -- and the manner in which they are
handled remains unchanged. She added that the revised ordinance also makes
nonsubstantive, technical amendments to clean up the language in the various
provisions affected by the substantive amendments. Finally, Ms. Janega
summarized the key substantive changes to each item.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend Ordinance MC-8-2000
as outlined by Ms. Janega. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
VIII. Old Business.
IX. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1197 and 1198. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists and recommended approval. He moved to approve Warrant List No. 1197 in
the amount of $637,450.49 and Warrant List No. 1198 in the amount of
$554,052.99. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Lien, Powell, Presser and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Replacement Sewer Jet Machine. Mr. Saunders discussed the options
available to the Village for the replacement of the sewer jet, equipment used
to clean and maintain storm and sanitary sewer lines, which is outdated and
unreliable. He explained that a combination sewer jet that meets the Village's
specifications could be purchased via the State of Illinois Cooperative
Purchase Program, however, the cost would be $29,529 over the budgeted amount.
Alternatively, the specifications could be rewritten for the purchase of a jet
only, rather than a combination machine, or the Village could proceed with bids
of its own on a combination machine. He outlined the pros and cons of each
option and recommended that the Village proceed with the State low-bid purchase
price of $179,529.
Manager Williams noted that because the purchase of leaf machines had come in
under budget, the excess cost could be absorbed into the Department's budget.
Trustee Woodbury inquired whether the Village had anticipated purchasing a
combination machine. Mr. Saunders replied affirmatively.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve staff's
recommendation to proceed with the purchase of a combination machine at the
State low-bid purchase price. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Composting Contract. Mr. Saunders explained that in order to comply with
state law that prohibits the disposal of yard waste in landfills, the Village
has a composting site located at the (closed) landfill at 1390 Willow Road.
Currently that site is operated by DK Recycling Systems under a contractual
arrangement with the Village, which expires on March 31, 2001. In order to
provide a seamless transition to a new contract at the time of expiration, the
Village solicited price proposals to provide composting services upon
expiration of the current contract. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bid process and
the evaluation of the two low bidders, DK Recycling and Thelen Sand & Gravel.
Based upon cost and land use criteria Mr. Saunders recommended that the Village
cease operating the composting center at the end of the existing contract and
enter into a contract with Thelen S&G to operate a transfer site and hauling
yard waste from a location at the landfill at a cost of $6.16 per cubic yard,
and to provide up to 500 cubic years of finished compost at a cost of $4.00 per
cubic yard. In further support of this recommendation, he cited Wilmette's
experience with the same contractor.
Trustee Woodbury inquired about how difficult it would be for the Village to
obtain the necessary permit from the IEPA to become a transfer site. Mr.
Saunders replied that he did not think that the Village would encounter a
significant problem.
Trustee Lien asked if there were any costs associated with getting the transfer
permit. Mr. Saunders said that there would be no cost for the permit, but that
there would be some associated costs in the way of staff and equipment time.
Manager Williams explained that land use is becoming an increasingly
significant issue for the Village. He pointed out that allowing a private
contractor to use Village owned land, which might be put to other uses, is
essentially providing the private contractor with a hidden property tax subsidy
because they are not paying for the cost of using that land. He said that
Village needed to free up the land currently used for composting for future
Village uses, e.g., fire tower, parking, etc. He added that changing the
operation to a transfer station would have the added benefit of decreasing the
number of odor complaints by neighbors.
Trustee Powell asked if making the change would be difficult to reverse. Mr.
Saunders replied that it probably would not be since the composting site permit
is good until 2003.
Trustee Brower inquired whether the Village would be changing its policy about
providing compost to residents. Mr. Saunders said that it would not.
President Holland recognized Charlie Pick, a former associate with DK
Recycling, who commented that the composting facility has proved to be a
benefit to both the Village and DK. He pointed out that IEPA composting permits
are hard fought because the regulations are very onerous, and if the Village's
permit is allowed to lapse, the current site would never be repermitted because
of the physical requirements for a site. He suggested that the requirement that
waste be removed from a transfer station within 24 hours would create
operational inefficiencies for the Village because its volume is so small. Mr.
Pick acknowledged that transferring is certainly a viable option, but expressed
concern that it might not be the best option available, proposing, perhaps, a
hybrid option of some type instead which would allow the Village to retain its
composting permit.
Trustee Powell expressed interest in the concept of keeping the Village's
composting permit.
Trustee Aquilino asked if Mr. Pick was suggesting that DK's price might be
negotiable. Mr. Pick responded that he thought that it would be.
Trustee Woodbury inquired whether, if the Village retained its composting
permit, the Village would be required to continue to be in compliance with IEPA
regulations. Mr. Pick replied that it would, but that the operator would be
responsible for any compliance activities. He opined that the Thelen bid does
not include any administrative duties.
Manager Williams commented that there was a time when many thought that the
composting center would be a profit center for the Village but that has not
been the case. He also said that there are many residents who want the Village
to get out of the composting business altogether. He indicated that the
transfer option would allow the Village to break even, possibly even save, as
well as remove the issue from the concern of residents.
Trustee Woodbury supported the transfer concept, but said that it makes sense
for the Village to keep the composting permit viable if possible because it may
be of value in the future. He moved that Thelen S&G be awarded the contract for
the operation with a review of keeping the current permit in place and
operational on whatever minimal basis might be required. There was no second to
the motion.
Trustee Powell suggested that no decision be made immediately. Trustee Lien
agreed. He asked that staff return at the next meeting with information about
what the Village would have to do in order to keep the composting permit
viable.
Trustee Aquilino added that detailed information as to exactly what assumptions
each of the providers made in preparing their proposals as well as more
specific land use details.
Manager Williams pointed out that if there was a change in the specifications
of the job, both contractors needed to be given an opportunity to review and
reevaluate their proposals. He also mentioned that because historically this
issue is a sensitive one, that the Council might consider notifying the
neighbors in the area.
Trustee Presser disagreed, pointing out that, because whatever decision is made
will reduce the impact on the neighbors, there is no need to involve residents.
Trustee Lien concurred.
It was the consensus of the Council to set the issue aside until next month in
order to give staff an opportunity to gather more information.
d)   DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to
the report included in the Agenda packet.
X. Reports.
a)   Village Trustees. Trustee Aquilino reported on the Beautification
Committee project at Indian Hill. She said that much of the planting has been
done, but that some of the ornamentals would have to wait until spring.
President Holland thanks Mr. Saunders and Ms. Janega for negotiating an
agreement with Union Pacific for the use of the land on the embankment.
Trustee Brower reported on the recent Chamber of Commerce meeting noting that
the Christmas kickoff would be on December 3rd with an After Hours at Harris
Bank on December 13th. He also mentioned that he had attended a meeting of the
Winnetka Youth Organization where there had been discussion of the impact of
the Organization's possible tenancy at the Winnetka Community House should the
Community House's capital campaign go forward.
Mr. Saunders reported briefly on WYO's clean up project on the Green Bay Trail.
b)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Comprehensive Plan is
now available on the Village's web site. President Holland commented that she
had received several requests from other communities for copies of our Plan.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and
Section 2(c)(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously:
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:21 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent:
None. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:22 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
December 4, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved December 19, 2000)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Monday,
December 4, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Police
Chief William Gallagher, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of
Community Development Cindy McCammack, and ten persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that Item 8(b), Reconsideration of the Composting
Contract, had been removed from this Agenda and would be placed on the Agenda
for the December 12th Study Session. The Council approved the Agenda, as
amended.
IV. Quorum.
a)   December 12, 2000, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   December 19, 2000, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
V. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 21, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-35-2000 - Tax Levy for Year 2000 - Public Hearing and
Introduction. President Holland opened the Public Hearing.
Mr. McKee explained that the amount of money requested in the property tax levy
is based on the projected needs of the Village to continue to provide services.
He referred the Trustees to agenda documents detailing the particular items
property taxes are requested to fund and explained the main factors in
determining the amount of property taxes to request for each line item. He said
that given the proposed net property tax levy of $9,141,503, most residents
will see a 2.7% increase in the Village portion of their tax bill. As the
Village's portion is about 16% of a typical property tax bill, this would
translate into approximately $43 on a $10,000 property tax bill.
Mr. McKee then explained the two property tax abatements arising from the
Village's issuing debt to be paid from property taxes for the construction of
the Public Safety Building.
There being no comments from the floor, President Holland closed the Public
Hearing. She reiterated the point that on a $10,000 tax bill, approximately
$1,600 goes to the Village, which represents an increase of about $43, or 2.7%.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
35-2000, Levying Property Taxes for the Year 2000. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
b)   Ordinance No. M-36-2000 - Tax Abatement for 1994 GO Bonds - Introduction.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
36-2000, Abating the Property Tax Levy for the 1994 Public Safety Building
General Obligation Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Presser.
c)   Ordinance No. M-37-2000 - Tax Abatement for 1996 GO Bonds - Introduction.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-37-2000, Abating the Property Tax Levy for the 1996 Public Safety Building
General Obligation Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Presser.
d)   Ordinance No. M-31-2000 - Special Use Permit: 566 Chestnut - Wireless
Telecommunication Service Facility - Adoption. Terry Sullivan, a representative
of the applicant, VoiceStream, reviewed additional information submitted in
response to Council's request regarding the feasibility of mounting antennas on
the outside of the existing chimney in order to minimize height instead of
installing an eight foot shroud to conceal the antennas. She referred the
Trustees to photo simulations of both the concealed and external mounting
alternatives and informed the Council that their structural engineer had
verified that the exterior mounting would be structurally sound. She noted that
VoiceStream's radio frequency engineers had also provided a supplemental
explanation of the engineering necessity for the higher mounting height allowed
through the concealment option and the negative impacts of either lowering the
antennas from four to six feet, or using six rather than nine antennas. Ms.
Sullivan also referred the Trustees to a supplemental analysis of the same
alternatives submitted by the Village's consultant. That analysis also
concluded that shorter antennas or a lower mounting height would downgrade the
system to a lower quality signal, particularly in the "fringe" of coverage
zones, and would introduce seasonal variability to the signal strength
experienced by users. Therefore this option would have a noticeable effect on
VoiceStream's system design to provide reliable coverage within commercial
buildings.
Trustee Brower inquired whether there would be lightening road attached. Ms.
Sullivan answered affirmatively.
Manager Williams remarked that before VoiceStream receives a building permit,
the Village will require its plans to be stamped by a structural engineer and
will also have our own structural engineer review them.
Trustee Aquilino asked whether VoiceStream had a preference for which design it
used. Ms. Sullivan replied that it would prefer height, as the 85' is already a
compromise for their system.
Manager Williams and Ms. Sullivan discussed different antenna options and their
impact on the operation of the system.
One of Voice Stream's engineers commented on that impact, saying that reducing
the antenna height and/or shortening the antennas diminishes the coverage
significantly.
Trustee Woodbury asked the engineer if VoiceStream uses its preferred
alternative, would they need another cell site in Winnetka. The engineer stated
probably not, given today's numbers and technology.
Manager Williams confirmed with the engineer that the company's goal is to
provide service inside commercial buildings, which requires additional strength
via height and length.
The engineer also responded to questions from the Trustees and residents.
Trustee Brower asked if the Village has an economic interest in this. Mr.
Williams replied that the Village receives a telecommunications tax for every
phone registered in the Village.
Trustee Brower pointed out that the proposed operation could provide a higher
level of service in some areas of town than is currently available.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the ordinance granting
the Special Use Permit to VoiceStream. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
VIII. Old Business.
a)   Traffic Calming Report. Mr. Williams provided an overview of staff's
discussions before turning the floor over to Mr. Saunders and Chief Gallagher.
He noted that approximately twenty ideas had been examined and broken down into
three categories: (1) short term (6 month) recommendations, (2) long term
options (more than 6 months), and (3) not recommended. He also pointed out that
traffic calming is not an exact science and that a lot of value judgment had
been applied. He reported that all of the ideas had been evaluated by the Fire
Chief and the Police Chief, for public safety issues, as well as by the
Village's Traffic Engineer.
In addressing the recommendation to enhance traffic enforcement, Chief
Gallagher emphasized that traffic enforcement continues to be one of the
Department's major priorities. He commented that both Willow and Tower Roads
are major arteries for the Village, making them among the most heavily traveled
thoroughfares in the community. Therefore, they already attract most of the
traffic enforcement attention. He cited the number of tickets issued in the
last three years on the two roads. He pointed out to the Trustees and audience
the fact that new technology installed in all of Winnetka's police squad cars
permits speed to be tracked from a moving vehicle. He also referred to the
recently purchased, shared mobile speed sign and the annual traffic study.
Chief Gallagher remarked that it is very difficult for an observer to judge the
speed of a moving vehicle and said that frequently speeds are not as fast as
some think.
Trustee Brower asked if the mobile speed signs can record the speed on passing
cars. Chief Gallagher replied that they can, but that the Village heretofore
has not set them up to do so. Mr. Saunders added that, because they are
nondiscriminatory in that they don't distinguish between a speeding automobile,
a turning car, or a slow moving garbage truck, when the speeds are averaged,
the result is not necessarily representative of the real, average speed of
traffic traveling the roads.
Trustee Powell suggested that the Department decrease the amount of cushion
given drivers before issuing a speeding ticket and asked about judgements in
court against speeders. Chief Gallagher reported that there is a fine line
between too much cushion and not enough. He added that a community had to have
a reputation for being realistic or its tickets were given less credence by the
judge, thereby causing them to be thrown out except in the most egregious of
instances.
Robert Dini, 1056 Gage St., asked if the Chief considered the mobile speed
signs to be effective. Chief Gallagher indicated that there were no official
studies, but that observation would indicate that they are.
Mr. Saunders then referred the Trustees to the Agenda Report and reviewed
staff's remaining recommendations individually, noting the feasibility, pros,
and cons of each.
President Holland commented on her unsuccessful efforts to unite neighboring
communities in a regional approach. She mentioned that she had been in touch
with groups opposing the widening of Willow Road west of the Edens Expressway.
She said that heretofore Winnetka has not taken an official position on this
issue, but that seeing the current problems, she is inclined to agree that the
fewer trucks on Willow west of Edens, the fewer there will be heading east
through Winnetka.
An audience member asked about making Tower Road 'no passing.' Mr. Saunders
replied that staff had discussed that option, but that, ultimately, it can
create a conflict between drivers and the Police Department because drivers
technically would be breaking the law if they passed a slow moving vehicle,
such as a garbage truck, even if it was safe to do so. In addition, he
mentioned that for the present it would not be an option because IDOT controls
the roadway.
Trustee Woodbury said he thought the concept of a double yellow line had some
merit and asked if there was a way to try it along some portions of the road.
Mr. Saunders said he could investigate it, but reiterated his professional
opinion that it was a bad idea because it would run the risk of confusing
drivers and set up confrontational situations with the police. He expressed his
belief that the problem is more of speeding issue.
Referring to the Agenda Report, Trustee Powell asked why the crossing guard at
Gordon Terrace and Tower thinks that the stop signs make her/his job more
difficult. Chief Gallagher explained.
Trustee Brower suggested escalating enforcement even further and Trustee
Woodbury suggested collecting some specific data about the speed of passing
cars.
In commenting on the truck ban option, Trustee Brower asked if the hours could
be adjusted. Manager Williams said that any hours could be established, but
pointed out that some trucks have to get through, i.e. Post Office trucks.
Linda Kramer, 370 Glendale, commented on following a truck crossing Willow to
service a business in Wilmette and expressed consternation that trucks serving
businesses outside of the community are using Village streets.
Carol Hayes, a resident, voiced her belief that the big speed signs are
ineffective and asked for information about how many tickets have been issues
on Tower and Willow Roads since this issue was discussed at the last meeting,
which Chief Gallagher indicated he could supply at another time.
Robert Dini, 1068 Gage, expressed some concern for businesses within the
community that depend upon trucks to deliver their merchandise. He said that he
did not believe that a permit process would work well because many times a
merchant has no idea who the actual overland carrier will be. He called for the
police to enforce the existing regulations before the Council enacts new
ordinances that require even more staff to enforce.
Bob Bertram, 877 Auburn Ave., queried the number citations issued on the east
side of Hibbard as opposed to the west side, and suggested that more warnings
be issued as a deterrent. He also suggested that one street be routed eastbound
and the other westbound. Manager Williams said that this idea had been
considered, but discarded.
Trustee Aquilino asked about the effectiveness of signs reading "No through
Truck Traffic." Chief Gallagher pointed out that the only way to enforce such
signs was to actually follow a truck from the point of entry until it leaves
the Village.
After further discussion, there was consensus among the Trustees that the
Village should initiate implementation of all of the short term options: (1)
heavily patrol both Tower and Willow Roads, (2) begin to work on an ordinance
that would ban all but essential trucks from traversing Village streets during
some specified nighttime hours; (3) check the intersection of Tower Rd. and
Gordon Terrace to see if it meets the warrants for installation of a blinking
red light or removal of the existing stop sign; and (4) work out a means to
apply weight restrictions or restrict trucks exceeding a certain license class
from certain portions of Willow and/or Tower Roads, while continuing to examine
the seven long term options.
b)   Reconsideration: Composting Contract. As announced by President Holland at
the beginning of the meeting, this item has been slated for the Agenda of
December 12, 2000 Study Session.
IX. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1199 and 1200. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. He commented on a couple of the items and, then, moved to approve
Warrant List No. 1199 in the amount of $699,669.74 and Warrant List No. 1200 in
the amount of $330,250.65. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Lien,
Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
b)   Update on Private Tree Planting Program. Mr. Saunders reported that the
first year of the Village's Private Property Tree Planting Program had been
successful and posed no serious problems for either the contractor or the
Village. He recommended that the Village again offer the program in the spring
of 2001, increasing the number of trees and the selection of species to add
more diversity and beauty.
President Holland congratulated Mr. Saunders and the Forester, Jim Stier, on
the success of the pilot program.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to authorize the
continuation of the tree planting program for the next year. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
X. Reports.
a)   Village Trustees. Trustee Aquilino reported that she and Trustee Brower
had attended the Chamber of Commerce's quarterly forum, at which the discussion
had centered on when to hold next year's Winnetka Days. Last year the sidewalk
sale was held in June on a trial basis. She said that the consensus had been to
move the event back to the third weekend in July, and that the scope and format
of the sale was still under discussion
Trustee Woodbury reported that the first community outreach meeting for the
Design Guidelines Committee was scheduled for Thursday, December 7th, with the
second meeting scheduled for January 11th.
b)   Village Manager. Manager Williams referred the Trustees to a report from
Mr. McKee regarding the Village's property insurance renewal. He noted that the
cost would be going up about 10% and that the key issue is providing coverage
for the power plant. He said that staff would be continuing to evaluate other
options for the power plant.
Trustee Brower commented that despite the price increase the coverage was still
very good given the current market.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Brower moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and
Section 2(c)(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Presser. The Council convened into Executive Session at
9:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Presser. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: Trustee Presser. The meeting
adjourned at 10:47 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
December 12, 2000
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved December 19, 2000)
1. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, and Stephen
Presser. Absent: Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams and two persons in the audience.
2. Policy Direction: Composting Contract.
Manager Williams referred the Trustees to the Agenda report reviewing the
history of the Village's composting operation and the current issues under
discussion, as well as a recently received memorandum from DK Recycling. He
spoke positively about the experience with DK, but indicated firmly that staff
continues to advocate that it is time to move on to a different option. He
pointed to three key factors leading to this recommendation: (1) a cheaper
alternative has been identified; (2) given the scarcity of land in the Village,
the land at the landfill can be put to a better and higher use; and (3)
eliminating the composting operation removes incompatible adjoining land uses.
Representatives from Thelan responded to questions from the Trustees. In
response to a question from President Holland about the necessity to remove
materials every 24 hours, one of the representatives indicated that this
requirement was dependent upon the permit received and that it could be up to
every five days. He recommended that the Village seek a five day permit because
it does not generate enough grass to warrant daily removal. Trustee Brower
commented that this seemed to make some sense.
President Holland pointed out that the Village didn't want to be the position
of having to bring in employees on a weekend or holidays to facilitate the
removal of waste. She also remarked that five days might be too long because
the grass would have started to decompose and emit an odor. The Thelan
representatives assured President Holland that in that eventuality, it could be
effectively handled.
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:55 p.m.
Trustee Brower returned to the concept discussed at the last meeting of merely
suspending the Village's current permit for a period of time before giving it
up altogether to determine whether the new operation is successful. Mr.
Williams referred to Mr. Saunders's memo indicating that the Village's current
composting permit does not expire until April 2, 2003, and that it would be
possible to make modifications to it during the interim.
Manager Williams responded to questions from Trustees Powell and Aquilino about
the quantity of materials, tipping, and the assignment of labor costs. He
pointed that no contract had been negotiated as yet and suggested that the
Village begin to discuss specific terms with Thelan. He noted that the term of
any contract could be defined so as to allow the Village to revisit this issue
before the expiration of the Village's current operating permit.
Trustee Presser asked whether there was any reason to expect that the
composting business would turn into the previously anticipated "cash cow" in
the future. Mr. Williams replied that he could not see that happening.
At the end of the discussion, the Council asked Manager Williams to obtain
information regarding the permit allowing for a five day pick up interval, the
control of potential odor, the number of yards of compost to be generated and
returned, employee cost, and maintaining the current permit.
Then, President Holland informed the Thelan representatives that the Council
would be making its final decision next Tuesday.
3. Appointment of Ad Hoc Façade focus Committee for Village Yards Complex.
President Holland recommended the appointment of the following persons to this
advisory committee, with the final decision on the façade to be determined by
the Council:
Nan Greenough, Landmark Preservation Representative
Carter Page, Landmark Preservation Representative
Jon Meyer, Design Review Board Representative
Ken Behles, Design Review Board Representative
Gwen Trindl, Plan Commission Representative
Barbara Aquilino, Village Trustee
Ed Woodbury, Village Trustee
Louise Holland, Village President
Michael Duhl, Incoming Village President
Trustee Woodbury suggested that the architects prepare options for review by
the committee in order to save time and effort. The Trustees concurred that
this would be an appropriate approach.
4. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Presser,
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 8:16 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:26 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees, Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Absent: None.
5. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:27 p.m.
December 19, 2000
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved January 2, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 19, 2000, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, and Stephen
Presser. Absent: Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Police
Chief William Gallagher, and four persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland announced that Item 8(a), Reconsideration: Composting
Contract, and Item 9(e), Policy Direction: 421 Rosewood, had been removed from
this Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:38 p.m.
IV. Quorum.
a)   January 2, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   January 9, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend. President Holland indicated that she would be unable
to attend.
V. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 4, 2000, Regular Meeting. After Trustee Powell suggested a minor
revision, Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the minutes as amended. The motion
was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   December 12, 2000, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-35-2000 - Tax Levy for Year 2000 - Adoption. President
Holland called for a motion to adopt the annual tax levy. Trustee Brower,
seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-35-2000. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-36-2000 - Tax Abatement for 1994 GO Bonds - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
36-2000, Abating the Property Tax Levy for the 1994 Public Safety Building
General Obligation Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-37-2000 - Tax Abatement for 1996 GO Bonds - Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
37-2000, Abating the Property Tax Levy for the 1996 Public Safety Building
General Obligation Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2000 - Taxpayer Rights & Responsibilities -
Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that Ordinance MC-9-2000 adds a new
chapter to the Revenue and Finance Title of the Village Code, entitled “Rights
and Responsibilities Related to Certain Local Taxes.” It implements the Local
Government Taxpayers’ Bill of Rights Act (“Act”), P.A. 91-920, which was
approved by the governor on July 7, 2000 and becomes effective January 1, 2001.
The stated intent of the Act is to grant “various rights and protections to
taxpayers and tax collectors with respect to the administration and enforcement
of local government tax laws” and to provide “a minimum level of consistency”
in local tax procedures as taxpayers “do business in multiple locations within
this State.”
She noted that there are actually few taxes that are considered “locally
imposed and administered taxes” under the Act, which specifically excludes all
property taxes and all fees collected by municipalities other than municipal
infrastructure fees. Therefore, the only Village taxes that fall within the
scope of the Act are the telecommunications tax and the telecommunications
infrastructure maintenance fee. She commented, however, that notwithstanding
the narrow scope of the Act, its requirements are substantial.
Ms. Janega said that Ordinance MC-9-2000 meets all of the Act’s requirements,
with much of the language of the new Chapter 4.40 being drawn directly from the
Act. Chapter 4.40, which is set out in Section 2 of the ordinance, is also
based on a model ordinance that was prepared by a committee of attorneys for
members of the Illinois Municipal League, so it will further the statewide
uniformity goal.
Ms. Janega pointed out that, although the Act leaves little to the discretion
of municipalities in enacting their implemented ordinances, it does allow the
Village to set procedural deadlines, statutes of limitation, and interest and
penalty rates. She added that the Village may also decide whether to allow
installment agreements for the payment of taxes, and to allow the tax
administrator to serve as the hearing officer, or to provide for a separate
hearing officer. She pointed out that the draft ordinance provides for
installment payments and designates the hearing officer.
Finally, she explained why, although not mandated by the Act, it is recommended
that the Village enact this ordinance by January 1, 2001.
Trustee Powell inquired whether this ordinance would prevent the Village from
doing anything that it felt was in the Village’s best interests. Attorney
Janega responded that she did not think that it would create any serious
obstacles, but indicated that it does create substantially more administrative
work, which imposes an additional burden on the Finance Department. She
believes the best route is to comply with the Act and make certain that the
procedures are as clear-cut and as favorable as possible to the Village. Mr.
McKee concurred.
Mr. McKee added that, although language providing for installment payments had
been included in the draft for reference, it was his recommendation that the
Village not allow installment contracts. Ms. Janega agreed, saying that these
would not provide any real benefit to the Village and would impose additional
work.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance, as
drafted, excluding the language providing for installment payments. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
(e) Ordinance No. MC-10-2000 - Amending the Village Code to Regulate Truck
Traffic. Mr. Williams explained that he, Chief Gallagher, the Director of
Public Works and Attorney Janega had met on several occasions to review similar
ordinances and craft an ordinance to control the noise and disturbance caused
by increased truck traffic. He emphasized that this ordinance was designed to
be an initial step in addressing the problem. As drafted, it would prohibit all
commercial truck traffic, over 2.5 tons, on Village streets between midnight
and 5:00 a.m., except for deliveries within the Village. He pointed out that
the ordinance would not apply to fire and emergency vehicles or to vehicles
used for snow and ice removal. He also noted that, contrary to the Caucus
platform to reduce regulatory signage, the Illinois Vehicle Code would require
the posting of appropriate signage to inform drivers of the prohibition.
Trustee Powell suggested implementing an earlier start time, 8:00 p.m., for the
prohibition. Manager Williams responded that the start time could be discussed,
but that for a variety of reasons, midnight had been selected as being the most
appropriate.
Chief Gallagher pointed out that there is generally more traffic and more
police business before midnight. He noted that in order to enforce this
ordinance, squad cars would be required to follow a truck on its route through
the Village, and that it would take more resources to enforce an earlier start
time.
Attorney Janega expressed reluctance to significantly move up the start time to
avoid the creation of a nuisance for local merchants who legitimately conduct
business during the evening hours. She also pointed out that an earlier hour
would dilute the noise prevention rationale. She said that this ordinance is
aimed at large delivery trucks more than truck traffic connected to
construction, whose activities already fall within the construction activity
provisions of the Code.
Trustee Presser suggested changing the language to clearly reflect the
exemption of any trucks doing business “by and on behalf of the Village.”
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Old Business.
a)   Reconsideration: Composting Contract. As previously noted, this item was
removed from the Agenda pending resolution of contract negotiations with Thelan
Sand & Gravel.
IX. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1201 and 1202. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a couple of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1201 in the amount of $475,410.17 and Warrant List No. 1202 in the
amount of $325,718.41. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Lien, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid No. B-00-012 - Sanitary Sewer Lining. Mr. Williams reviewed the bid to
provide for trenchless relining to existing sanitary sewers located throughout
the Village and recommended that it be awarded to the lowest responsible
bidder, Instituform Technologies USA, In., in the total amount of $74,103.90.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the award of the
bid as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Sidewalk & Curb Replacement Program - Change Order. Manager Williams informed
the Council that construction on the Sidewalk and Curb Replacement Program for
calendar year 2000 had been completed. He explained the process for obtaining
and awarding the initial contract for the work to Schroeder & Schroeder in the
amount $85,134.50. He noted that this year, the low bidder’s bid unit prices
were lower than staff’s estimate, so the total bid was below the amount
budgeted, which made it possible to perform more work than the quantities
contained in the original bid documents. As a result, the amount expended
overall on this program exceeded the amount of the bid. He noted that this
year, the amount of work done exceeded the bid amount by $35, 577.53, however,
all but $5,229.77, was budgeted for expenditure this year, and staff
recommended that a change order of $35,577.53 be approved.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the change order
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Alarm Panel Bid. Chief Gallagher presented an historical perspective on
the existence and operation of the alarm monitoring system currently in
existence at the Public Safety Building. He also reviewed the RFP and bid
process for a new system operator to replace the existing one whose contract
has expired. He pointed out that the Village is not purchasing anything, but
rather providing a service to residents by allowing the vendor to install and
maintain its alarm panel in the Public Safety Building. This permits residents
to choose to connect directly to the Public Safety Building if they so desire.
Finally, he recommended approval of the Alarm Detection Systems, Inc. (ADS)
bid. He explained the two customer pricing options available and recommended
approval of the option that would provide for an up front payment of $40,000 to
the Village to defray the cost of necessary maintenance and pagers for police
personnel, as is provided by the current vendor, for the next five years. In
closing, he pointed out that regardless of which fee option is selected, the
ADS bid is still the lowest.
Manager Williams provided the Trustees with a spreadsheet, attached hereto as
Attachment #1, analyzing the two options.
Trustee Lien clarified that if the ADS bid is approved, the Trustees were being
asked to decide whether or not to authorize the vendor to charge an additional
$4.50 per customer, per month so that the Village can continue to receive funds
from the vendor to maintain the current level of services. Mr. Williams
responded affirmatively. Trustee Lien, then, expressed support for permitting
the additional charge. Trustee Aquilino concurred.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the award of the
bid to ADS and to authorize an increase of $4.50 in the amount charged by the
vendor on behalf of the Village. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Policy Direction: 421 Rosewood. As announced by President Holland, this
item was removed from the Agenda.
e)   DK Recycling Monthly Report. President Holland referred the Trustees to
the report included in the Agenda packet.
X. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Manager Williams announced that four budget meetings had
been scheduled, as follows:
January 29, 2001, 7:30 p.m. at the Village Hall
February 5, 2001, 7:30 p.m. at the Village Hall
February 12, 2001, 7:30 p.m. at the Public Safety Building
February 22, 2001, 7:30 p.m. at the Village Hall (and if necessary)
   He also reported on the number of traffic violations issued on Tower and
Willow Roads between November 19th and December 2nd.
b)   Village Trustees. Trustee Brower reported that he had met with the
Historical Society to discuss possible Village financial support. He indicated
that no conclusion had been reached and that there were a number of issues that
would require resolution. In addition, there was some discussion by the
Trustees of possible solutions to the Society’s need for a permanent home.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland requested appointment of Robert Vanden Bosch to the Zoning
Board of Appeals to replace Phyllis Thomas who has moved out of the Village.
She mentioned that Mr. Vanden Bosch had also volunteered to serve on Design
Guidelines Committee.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to appoint Mr. Vanden
Bosch. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Brower moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and
Section 2(c)(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:35 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:36 p.m.
