                  Deputy Clerk
2001
January 2, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved January 16, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 2, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Cindy
McCammack, and five persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland noted that an amended Agenda had been distributed to the
Trustees showing the addition of Item XIII, Resolution No. R-1-2001 Amending
the Schedule of Parking Fees. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
IV. Quorum.
a)   January 9, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   January 16, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
V. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 19, 2000, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Brian Morrow, 503 Chestnut Street, asked some questions about Winnetka's Power
Plant generation and its fuel sources. He also commented on the acidity level
of the water supply. Mr. Williams informed Mr. Morrow that Winnetka's water is
carefully monitored and meets all Federal standards.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2000 - Taxpayer Rights & Responsibilities - Adoption.
Attorney Janega briefly reviewed this item. She pointed out that at the time of
introduction, the ordinance included an installment contract provision, but
noted that it had been removed given Council's acceptance of the recommendation
of the Finance Director to exclude that provision.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-9-
2000, adding Chapter 4.40 to the Village Code the Define Taxpayers' Rights and
Responsibilities Related to Certain Taxes Imposed by the Village. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2000 - Amending the Village Code to Regulate Truck
Traffic - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed the ordinance making note of the
three categories of vehicles excluded from its scope: (1) fire and emergency
vehicles owned or operated by any unit of government; (2) any vehicle owned or
operated by or on behalf of the Village; and (3) any vehicle operated under the
terms of a special permit under Section 15-301 of the Illinois Vehicle Code.
She pointed out that the second exception had been changed since introduction,
by removing the specific references to snow, ice, solid waste and recycling,
which are now included within the scope of the broader reference to “any
vehicle owned or operated by or on behalf of the Village.”
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend the ordinance as
noted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance
Amending Chapter 10.28 of the Village Code to Regulate Truck Traffic. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2001 Amending Section 2.04.020 of the Winnetka Village
Code as It Pertains to the Schedule of Regular Meetings of the Village Council
- Introduction. Manager Williams explained that the Village Code establishes
the first and third Tuesday of each month as the dates for regular meetings of
the Village Council. He noted, however, that there has always been an exception
that reschedules the meeting to Thursday of the same week if New Year’s Day or
the Fourth of July fall on a Tuesday. He said that in 1994, at the request of a
citizen, the Council adopted Ordinance MC-127-94, which codified a similar
exception for regular meeting nights that coincide with a General Election.
Pursuant to the schedule set by that ordinance, this year the Village Council
met on Thursday, November 9, 2000, two days after the General Election. Then,
in December, pursuant to the newly enacted Cook County Truth in Taxation Law,
the Council held a special meeting on Monday, December 4, 2000, to conduct a
hearing on the proposed tax levy, resulting in the cancellation of the regular
meeting of December 5, 2000. At that time, concern was expressed about the
back-to-back departures from the normal meeting schedule, and staff was
directed to prepare an amendment to remove the election related schedule
change. The ordinance under consideration amends the “Time and Day of Meetings”
provision of Section 2.04.020(A) of the Winnetka Village Code. It deletes the
election exception, but retains the exceptions for New Year’s Day and the
Fourth of July.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-01-2001- Zoning Variations: 391 Sheridan Road -
Introduction. Manager Williams reviewed the history of work on the subject
property, including the issuance of a stop work order in July 1995 and the
filing of a lawsuit against the owners and their contractors for the
performance of work without the proper permits, litigation which is still
pending.
He explained that since spring of 2000, the owners have been working with
Village staff in an effort to bring the property into compliance. As part of
those efforts, the owners submitted a site plan showing the location of all
improvements on the property. He reported that a subsequent zoning analysis of
the property concluded that the only zoning nonconformities are in the front
yard setback required along the water’s edge, the south side yard setback and
in the total side yard requirement (the sum of the north and south side yards),
and that the owners have now applied for the variances necessary to allow the
structures to remain as built. He said that in addition to bringing the
property into zoning compliance, the zoning variations, if granted, provide the
necessary prerequisite for the owners to apply for building permits, which, in
turn, provide the basis for conducting necessary inspections to determine what
is necessary to bring the property into compliance with all other Village
codes.
   The three variations requested by the owners are as follows:
   (1)   from the front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) to allow
an existing elevated concrete deck and boat ramp to remain in their current
locations;
   (2)    from the minimum side yard requirements of Section 17.24.010(I) to
permit an existing storage shed to remain in its current location, 6.14 feet
from the south lot line, and to allow an existing wooden deck to remain in its
current location; and
   (3)    from the total side yard requirement of Section 17.24.010(I), to
permit the total of the two side yards to be 12.45 feet (12.45-foot north side
yard + 0.00-foot south side yard).
Mr. Williams reported that following a Public Hearing, the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted to recommend approval of the requested variations.
Ms. Janega distributed photographs of the subject property.
The Trustees discussed the desirability of retaining or removing the small
wooden deck. Mr. Williams reported that Village staff had no strong opinion one
way or another, but that neighbors had pointed out that the deck assisted
pedestrians walking the beach. Trustee Woodbury expressed a preference, from a
zoning point of view, for removing the deck. Citing its use by beach walkers,
Trustee Powell spoke in favor of retaining it.
Manager Williams explained that the only issue of concern to the Village about
the deck is whether or not to allow the variance for the zero side lot line
necessary to accommodate it. He said that any issue about whose property the
deck is on is between the neighboring property owners.
Noting that the negotiation of the settlement of this matter had been long and
arduous, Trustee Lien spoke in support of the requested variations and moved to
introduce the ordinance. His motion was seconded by Trustee Presser. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-02-2001- Zoning Variations: 714 Park Lane - Introduction.
Ms. McCammack explained this request for a variation to permit the location of
a two-story addition within the required front yard setback, which would
accommodate an additional garage bay for the existing two car attached garage
and habitable space on the second floor. She reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted to recommend approval of the variance as requested.
The applicant, Ed Mekertichian, and a representative from his design group
commented on the proposed plans.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
VIII. Old Business.
IX. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1203 and 1204. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a couple of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1203 in the amount of $783,869.45 and Warrant List No. 1204 in the
amount of $168,511.53. Trustee Lien seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Lien, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
b)   Plan Commission Annual Progress Report on the Winnetka 2020 Comprehensive
Plan. Ms. McCammack reported that the Plan Commission has completed its review
of Appendix 6 of the Comprehensive Plan for the year 2000 and is pleased that
58% of the identified high and first level priorities of the Plan's
recommendations are either nearing completion or are programmed to begin. She
explained some of the comments made by the Commission, but noted that after its
review, the Plan Commission recommended that no text amendments be made to the
official document. She indicated, however, that at some time in the future it
might be appropriate to make some amendments to the Plan.
The Trustees briefly discussed the Commission's comments.
X. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Preliminary Budget is
available for inspection at the Library. He said that the Council would be
meeting to study this document on:
January 29, 2001, 7:30 p.m. at the Village Hall
February 5, 2001, 7:30 p.m. at the Village Hall
February 12, 2001, 7:30 p.m. at the Public Safety Building
He also announced that the Council would be meeting after the Executive Session
to consider Resolution No. R-1-2001 regarding parking fees.
b)   Village Trustees. Trustee Woodbury reported that the Design Guidelines
Committee has received the first of the proposed Design Guidelines and would be
meeting on Thursday, January 4th to talk through that draft, with a second
meeting scheduled to be held on January 11th.
Trustee Powell reported on his recent attendance at the annual meeting of RED
Center. He confirmed that upon receiving approval from each of the member
villages a new Center would be built in Northbrook. He also confirmed that at
the end of September 2001 the villages of Glenview and Wheeling would leave RED
Center to form a dispatching center of their own and that Lincolnwood has
expressed a desire to join the Center. He noted that in order to proceed with
the new Center, each member community is being asked to approve a Regional
Emergency Dispatch Center Intergovernmental Transition Plan and Service
Agreement. He mentioned that this document would be presented to the Council at
its meeting on January 16th. Citing the excellent, high quality service
received by the Village from RED Center, Mr. Powell recommended approval of the
Agreement.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Brower moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and
Section 2(c)(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lien
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:05 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
XIII. Resolution No. R-1-2001 - Parking Fees - Adoption.
Ms. Janega explained that the proposed resolution revises the Scheduling of
Parking Fees by reducing the fee for daily parking permits from $4.00 to $3.00,
which is the highest amount that can be charged for a daily fee without being
subject to the $0.50 tax that Cook County has imposed on parking fees.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adopt the Resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
XIV. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:07 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
January 9, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved February 6, 2001)
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Lien called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: President Holland. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Village Forester Jim
Stier, Chief of Police William Gallagher and approximately 15 persons in the
audience.
2. Holiday lighting.
Trustee Lien explained that the Council had been asked to consider three
recommendations regarding the holiday lights on Village trees. He said that the
lights are currently being left on year round and the purpose of this study
session is to consider removing them after each holiday session. He asked Jim
Stier to comment.
Mr. Stier explained that the holiday lights were brought to his attention by
concerned citizen groups early last year because of possible damage to trees
that could be affecting their overall health and growth. He said that he did an
inspection of the trees in August and September and found that the lights had
become wrapped too tightly because the trees had grown. During meetings with
the Forestry Commission and representatives of the Chamber of Commerce, Mr.
Stier related that the Chamber had said that they wanted to get their own
contractor for a second opinion and also to put up the lights at the beginning
of the holiday season and take them down afterwards. Mr. Stier suggested that
it might be best for the Village to reevaluate its current policy or develop a
new one that addresses the amount of time lights are on the trees. He said the
Forestry Commission recommends that the lights remain on the trees for no more
than 3 months and be taken down each year, the lights should be loosely
wrapped, and the installation and removal of the lights should be done
carefully following good forestry practices.
The Trustees asked Mr. Stier several questions regarding the current policy of
removing lights from half the trees one year and the other half the next, the
costs involved, and the amount of damage to the trees. Mr. Stier’s
recommendation was to remove the lights since the diameter of trees continues
to grow.
Manager Williams and Joan Moreland of the Chamber of Commerce commented on the
cost of the program, estimating the figure to be between $14,000 and $17,000
per year which includes inspecting, replacing and removal of all of the lights
every 3 years.
In response to a question from the audience, Trustee Brower said that the
Chamber had decided a couple of years ago to cut back the lights by about 50%
when they started re-doing the trees in 1995, and work with the stringers to
hang the lights more loosely. He also said he doesn’t think anyone is opposed
to this or to requiring worker's compensation insurance for outside
contractors.
Debbie Ross said that the Garden Guild of Winnetka is in support of wrapping
the lights more loosely and removing them after the holiday season. She
distributed pictures to the Council of trees from Winnetka and Glencoe and
pointed out the difference in foliage on the loosely wrapped trees in Glencoe.
Trustee Aquilino commented that she thought the lights make a great
contribution to the Village and asked Mr. Stier about poling vs. wrapping.
Mr. Stier replied that in speaking to other Villages who do poling, they have
found it easier to cut the lights out of the trees at the end of the season and
throw them out, rather then to remove and re-use them.
Jean Wright, President of the Chamber of Commerce, read aloud a letter she sent
to the Council that afternoon from the Chamber of Commerce recommending that
the Village take over the lighting project.
Manager Williams asked that this part of the discussion be deferred because of
personnel issues.
Betty Skalski said that because the Chamber has hired Village employees at time
and a half, she thinks that if the Village does this project the cost would be
less. She also suggested that the Village’s trees be poled or strung from
branch to branch in a pleasing manner rather than the wrapping which takes more
time and more lights. She said that with these types of compromises, she hopes
that the Village would now take over the tree lighting because she feels it is
so important to the business district to have the wonderful spirit of the
lights.
Manager Williams clarified that currently the Chamber is paying the standard
hourly rate for employees, but that if the Village were to do the project, it
would have to pay time and a half and that is one of the personnel issues.
Trustee Lien asked for a vote on the first recommendation of the Forestry
Commission concerning the installation of the holiday lights for a period of
three months, that the lights should be loosely wrapped and installed and
removed carefully following good forestry practices.
Trustee Powell objected that the Council is being asked to recommend something
that has no costs attached to it.
Joan Moreland said that she had figures for the last three years, but to start
over and to pole the lights, the cost would be about $18,000 to $20,000 and
does not include removing the lights. She indicated that the ornament sales
bring in about $4,000 of this.
After discussion of the various estimated costs for the holiday lighting
program, Trustee Presser commented that he thought most of the Council favors
year to year removal of the lights, but that cost is a factor.
Trustee Lien asked Manager Williams to develop the cost of what the annual
removal and installation would be. He said he assumes that Council would agree
that workers compensation and liability insurance would be required for outside
contractors. The consensus of the Council was that this was correct.
Trustee Lien asked about the third recommendation to consider the selection of
dates for the lighting program. After a short discussion, the consensus of the
Council was to have the lights taken down at the end of this season.
3. Policy Direction Pillars.
Steve Saunders explained that a stone pillar at Gordon Terrace and Oakley
Avenue that was in very poor condition had been removed after consulting with a
stone mason without notifying the neighbors. He said that parts of the pillar
had been saved and the estimated cost to replace the pillar is about $6700. Mr.
Saunders said that they had inventoried the other pillars in town and the
condition of each and as a result, found that a number of the pillars are in
need of repair or are in poor condition. He referred to a spreadsheet in the
agenda materials that staff had prepared which shows each pillar’s location,
its condition, a cost estimate for repairs, and whether they are on public or
private property with most being on public property. He said he also looked at
sight distance concerns for drivers and found that none of the pillars are a
major safety concern.
Mr. Saunders listed the options available for maintaining the pillars; the
Village could maintain them all which would provide the Village with control
over liability; the adjacent homeowners could maintain the pillars after the
Village has inspected them; the residents could maintain the pillars without
any involvement by the Village; and the fourth option is to remove all of the
pillars, but this is not favored by the residents. Mr. Saunders recommended
that the Village maintain the pillars on public property and to have adjacent
property owners maintain those on private property. He said that to annualize
the cost, the pillars would be maintained on a 15 year schedule and would
probably cost about $2000 per year for maintenance.
Trustee Brower asked if the Village had built any of these pillars.
Mr. Saunders replied that he could find no evidence of that and that he thought
they were put in when the subdivisions were developed.
Trustee Brower said that he was of that opinion as well and raised the question
of why the Village should maintain them.
Trustee Powell said that he feels this list is only a small portion of the
pillars that are in the Village, with the rest on private property. He said
that it is clear to him that the pillars were intended to discourage use of the
street by anyone other than the property owners. He also asked Mr. Saunder’s
opinion on the blockage of view and if it is a real concern.
Mr. Saunders replied that strictly speaking, the pillars should probably be
truncated or removed, but that in most circumstances they are on small streets
with low traffic and there is no accident history to support that there is a
major safety hazard. He said that the pillar at Gordon Terrace and Oakley was
removed because it was in such poor condition that a few people could have
pushed it over.
Several residents expressed their concern that no notice was given before the
stone pillar was removed and asked that this type of incident not happen again.
They also agreed that because of the historical value of the pillars and the
relatively low cost of maintaining them, the Village should maintain and repair
the pillars.
Following a discussion about the Village or private property owners maintaining
the pillars, the consensus of the Council was that the Village should maintain
the pillars.
Trustee Lien acknowledged that the pillars are historical monuments that are
part of the atmosphere of the Village and add to the Village’s character. He
said he would like to see the removed pillar rebuilt and an annual maintenance
program begun to restore and maintain the existing pillars. He also stated that
since this would be part of the budget, the Council would vote on each of these
items beginning with the replacement of the removed pillar and an annual
maintenance item thereafter.
4. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
sale of Village owned land, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and
Section 2(c)(6), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Presser, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and
Powell. Nays: None. Absent: President Holland. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Study Session at 9:16 p.m. Present: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent: President
Holland.
5. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:17 p.m.
January 16, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved February 6, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 16, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders,
Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Fire Chief Ron Colpaert, and
twenty persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
President Holland noted two changes to the evening's Agenda, as follows: (1)
Item VII(b), Ordinance No. M-01-2001, was removed, and (2) Item VII(e),
Resolution No. R-3-2001, is up for adoption rather than introduction. The
Council approved the Agenda as amended.
IV. Quorum.
a)   January 29, 2001, Budget Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   February 5, 2001, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Aquilino, indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   February 6, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Aquilino, indicated that they expected to attend.
d)   February 13, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
V. Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 2, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Ike Oberman, 468 Ash St., expressed his frustration over the lack of enough
commuter parking, especially in the neighborhood of the Elm Street station and,
particularly, for individuals taking later trains. He asked that the Council
place the issue on an upcoming agenda and suggested that a committee be
established to help resolve the problem. He also proposed that some of the
spaces around the Village Green be specifically dedicated to commuters, or that
a cap be put over the railroad tracks to enable cars to park on top.
James and Stephanie Cavanaugh, 1210 Spruce St., supported Mr. Oberman's
comments. Mr. Cavanaugh stated his opinion that the number of "A" spaces in the
Winnetka Community House should be reduced. Mrs. Cavanaugh voiced her opinion
that there is an increasing number of two wage earner families in the Village
with both adults commuting downtown which contributes significantly to the
escalating problem.
Saying that it is very difficult to achieve a balance among the four, competing
user groups, Manager Williams reported that following recent adjustments to the
Community House lot, the allocation of commuter spaces is the same for
commuters as it was in 1995. He said that the availability picture changes
repeatedly throughout the day and recounted the results of a recent mini-survey
of availability. Whatever way the limited number of spaces are divided, there
will be a group of users that are unhappy; the Community House parking lot is
oversubscribed. Mr. Williams told the Council that the only place that there is
parking consistently available for commuters is the Hubbard Woods parking deck.
He said that staff had recently studied the issue and the feasibility of
numerous ideas, including a cap over the railroad tracks, and concluded that
the only long term solution is the construction of a parking deck to handle the
Lincoln Ave./Elm Street area.
President Holland mentioned that one of the problems with specifically
dedicating too many spaces around the Village Green to any one user group would
be that it would infringe upon the parking needs of the Women's Club and Willow
Wood Preschool.
Bernard Hammer, 1455 Tower, objected to the increase in the price of a daily
parking pass, saying that Village fees favor commuters over daily parkers. He
also voiced concern about the impact on the Village of the new Cook County tax
on daily parking costs.
Manager Williams responded that the daily parking fee had been reduced to $3.00
to avoid any impact by the Cook County tax.
Ted Reed, 411 Glendale, commended the Village for its handling of parking over
the years. He suggested, however, that more of the spaces be designated as A/C.
Bringing up a new topic, Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., expressed concern about
the overall cost to residents for undergrounding the electrical wires and a
perceived lack of communication with residents about those costs. He voiced his
opinion that any project of such financial magnitude ought to be put before the
Villagers for a formal vote.
He also commented on the pedestal issue, slated for discussion later on the
Agenda. He expressed his belief that homeowners should not be charged anything
for the installation of pedestals required as a result of the Village's
undergrounding project. He contended that because the undergrounding project
ultimately benefits the entire Village, the Village ought to pay all of the
costs attendant thereto, and that the current policy of charging residents $750
- $1,200 unfairly penalizes residents who happen to live along main streets.
In addition, Mr. Kates asked about specific plans for the upcoming Tower Road
rehabilitation.
Irv Footlick, 1458 Tower, Arthur Zeck, 1515 Tower, and Bernard Hammer 1455
Tower Rd., also asked specific questions about the rehabilitation project.
Mr. McInturff responded that the overall cost for the undergrounding project is
hard to estimate as it is spread over at least a thirty year period.
Manager Williams added that monies are budgeted annually for the undergrounding
as a part of the Capital Improvements Budget. He noted that both Budget
meetings and the Council meeting at which the Budget is approved are open,
public meetings.
President Holland commented that the undergrounding project is not primarily a
beautification project, but also a means of insuring reliable electric service
to residents. She reiterated Mr. McInturff's remark that the expenditure takes
place over many, many years.
Mr. Kates expressed his belief that Winnetka has no reliability problems and,
therefore, undergrounding for the sake of reliability is unnecessary. He
requested a cost-benefit study to confirm the necessity for the project. In
addition he asked whether the pedestal cost to residents is a fixed policy or
subject to further consideration.
Mr. McInturff pointed out that undergrounding prevents damage/breakage to wires
from snow and ice storms or falling limbs.
Trustee Powell remarked that the $750 - $1,200 cost to residents constitutes a
very small percentage of the total cost of each job.
President Holland responded that the policy regarding the pedestal cost had
been revisited by the Council previously and the decision to charge residents
reaffirmed.
Zonnie Alshire, 777 Burr, Lolly Rosengarten, 1460 Tower Rd., and William
Sievers, 925 Elm Street, also commented on the undergrounding project.
With regard to the specific plans for the Tower Road project, Mr. Williams said
that the plans are still under being developed and that no bids have been let.
He said that the next Winnetka Report would feature an article on the project
and that the Council would be reviewing all of the issues at a study session
scheduled for February 13th.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2001 Amending Section 2.04.020 of the Winnetka Village
Code as It Pertains to the Schedule of Regular Meetings of the Village Council
- Adoption. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt this
ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Lien. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-01-2001- Zoning Variations: 391 Sheridan Road - Adoption.
As previously noted, this item was removed from the agenda.
c)   Ordinance No. M-02-2001- Zoning Variations: 714 Park Lane - Adoption.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance
permitting the location of a two-story addition within the required front yard
setback. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Resolution No. R-2-2001 - Release of Executive Session Minutes - Adoption.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the Executive
Session minutes from June 13, 2000, through December 19, 2000. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser then moved to adopt Resolution No. R-2-2001. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Brower. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-3-2001 - RED Center Transition Plan and Agreement -
Adoption. As the Council's representative to RED Center, Trustee Powell
reported that he had read through the resolutions and supported their adoption.
Saying that the negotiation of the development of the Transition Plan and the
negotiation of the Agreement had taken many months, Chief Colpaert also
expressed his support for the adoption and answered questions from the
Trustees.
President Holland requested that Trustee Powell describe RED Center for the
benefit of cable viewers and audience members, which he did. He cited it as a
model of efficient dispatching service and a prime example of intergovernmental
cooperation.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the Resolution
authorizing execution of the "Intergovernmental Transition Plan and Service
Agreement" between existing members of RED Center and the Villages of Glenview
and Wheeling. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
f)   Resolution No. R-4-2001 - Adoption of Amended Intergovernmental Agreement
and Bylaws for RED Center - Adoption. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien,
moved to adopt this Resolution authorizing execution of the "Joint Fire
Department Communications Agreement." By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and
Lien. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Resolution No. R-5-2001 - Annual MFT Resolution - Adoption. Mr. Saunders
explained that historically, the Village authorizes the allocation of $125,000
from the MFT Fund towards street maintenance, which is a permitted use of those
funds. He said that the remaining funds go to the ongoing bridge improvements.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
h)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2001 - Amend Village Code to Require Police and Fire
Applicants to be Registered Voters - Introduction. Ms. Janega explained that
the proposed ordinance would amend the Village Code by adding new provisions to
the chapters pertaining to the Fire and Police Departments. She said that if
the ordinance is adopted, both chapters will require applicants for sworn
positions in the two departments to be registered to vote in any state or
federal election. An additional amendment will require all members of the Fire
Department to be sworn in before entering active duty.
She noted that the ordinance was prepared at the request of the Board of Fire
and Police Commissioners (BFPC), which is responsible for examining and
determining the qualifications of candidates for sworn positions in the police
and fire departments, because with increasing frequency, the BFPC has
encountered difficulties in obtaining satisfactory verification of eligibility,
as required by Federal immigration laws. This often has required that the BFPC
determine the legal sufficiency of various Immigration and Naturalization
Service documents. She said that because some of these documents are valid for
limited periods of time, it is possible for a person not born in the United
States to be eligible for employment at the time of the written examination,
but to be no longer eligible by the time he is considered for final employment.
In order to avoid these difficulties and to streamline the process of
determining the eligibility of applicants, the BFPC requested that the Village
limit applicants to citizens of the United States. She said that this ordinance
would accomplish this in the manner authorized by the Illinois Municipal Code,
which allows municipalities to adopt ordinances requiring applicants to be
electors in the municipality, the county, the state or the country.
Ms. Janega pointed out that none of the proposed new provisions would impact
the current Police and Fire Departments as all members of both forces are
citizens, and the new oath requirement for the Fire Department would merely
codify the existing practice.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
Trustee Lien asked whether a passport would not constitute more reliable
evidence of citizenship.
Attorney Janega replied that the Village does not have the necessary authority
to require a passport.
Trustee Lien then said (1) that he has no faith that a voter registration card
is reliable proof of citizenship, although ostensibly they can only be obtained
by citizens, and (2) that he is opposed to requiring an individual to register
to vote for employment purposes.
President Holland suggested that the issue be put off for discussion at some
future study session. Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to
table the matter. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Old Business.
None.
IX. New Business.
a)   Reconsideration: Pedestal Costs. Manager Williams briefly reviewed the
history of this issue reiterating that the Council has previously revisited it
and deemed the sharing of costs to be appropriate. As noted earlier, he
commented that a homeowner typically pays $40 for a permit and between $750 to
$1,250 to install a meter pedestal, which is the connection point between a
home's electrical system and the Village's system.
Trustee Presser expressed support for the policy saying that eventually, over
an extended period of time, all Village homeowners will end up paying for the
conversion. He asked whether there was any way to make the cost tax deductible,
a question by Mr. Hammer.
Attorney Janega replied that the only two ways to accomplish that would be to
(1) absorb the expense by corporate funds, thereby diminishing the amount
available for other services, or (2) do each undergrounding project as a
special service area, which would be a complicated and extremely cumbersome
process.
Trustee Aquilino pointed out that conceptually this project has been a part of
the Village's Comprehensive Plan for a long time and that, therefore, a lot of
people feel that it is important.
Dick Kates reiterated earlier comments that although the Comprehensive Plan
envisions the undergrounding of the wires, it is not a legislative document,
but merely a blue print for the future. In addition to which, it does not
discuss individuals paying from their pockets for any part of the project. He
also objected again to the perceived lack of communication with residents about
its cost.
Trustee Lien disputed Mr. Kates' representation that this issue is being raised
without either community with the public or public input. He noted that the
specifics of the Comprehensive Plan had been discussed repeatedly in detail
during Plan Commission and subcommittee meetings and at public hearings. He
also pointed out that the Council votes annually at an open meeting whether it
is going to fund the program taking into account current considerations. He
expressed his belief that it is not unfair to charge a minimal amount to the
homeowner for the installation of the pedestal because, ultimately, it is the
homeowner who benefits as it enhances the property's value. In addition to
which, to change the program now would be unfair to those homeowners who have
already paid. He said he was not in favor of reconsidering a change to the
existing policy.
Trustee Aquilino commented that the procedure for obtaining the necessary
permit should be made simple and, perhaps, the $40 fee should be reconsidered.
All of the Trustees agreed.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved that the Village retain its
present policy with regard to pedestal installation, minus the permit
application.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1205 and 1206. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a couple of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1205 in the amount of $724,189.19 and Warrant List No. 1206 in the
amount of $563,119.42. Trustee Lien seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Lien, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
c)   Preauthorization for Purchase of Squad Car. Manager Williams explained
that the Police Department expects to purchase two new squad cars from the FY
2001-2002 budget to replace two vehicles that will have exceeded 65,000 miles
by April 1, 2001. He said that the Department recently received the State of
Illinois bid, which is out of sync with the Village's budgeting process, for
the vehicles and that in order to insure both the price, approximately $21,000
per car, and delivery times, orders must be placed before March 1, 2001. The
Department, therefore, is requesting preauthorization to place this order.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to authorize the purchase
of the squad cars as requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and
Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid No. 00-019 - Automatic Control of High Lift Pumps. Mr. Williams
reviewed the bids for the design, fabrication, and installation of a control
panel to automatically turn on or off the four high lift pumps which satisfy
the demand for water and will also maintain a pressure setpoint. He noted
staff's recommendation that the bid be awarded to Metropolitan Industries,
Inc., in the total amount of $42,852.00.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Approval of Construction Manager Contract. Mr. Saunders reviewed the
status of the draft construction manager contract with Riley Construction
Company for the construction of the proposed new Public Works Facility at 1390
Willow Road. He enumerated some of the important provisions incorporated into
the document and noted that the contract form, as amended, has been reviewed by
the Technical Committee and found to be satisfactory. He pointed out that
although the terms of the draft contract are virtually complete, the requested
authorization at this time is for $40,000.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the contract. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. In response to questions raised at the last Council
meeting Manager Williams reported that Village's water supply has a pH level of
about 7.5, which is in the center of the desirable range of 6.5 to 8.5. He also
reported that the Village's road salt supply is secure for the remainder of the
winter season and that its supplier does not expect any shortages or delivery
problems.
He informed the Trustees that the Village has been notified by RCN that their
plans install a second cable TV service to Winnetka have been put on indefinite
hold.
In addition, he explained that although the plans for the rehabilitation of
Tower Rd. are not yet complete, it appears that the Village will be unable to
complete the project in a single summer, so it is likely that the proposal will
be to break it down into two segments, east and west of Hibbard Road, over the
next two summers.
Finally, he reminded everyone that this year's Budget Meetings would be held on
January 29th, February 5th and February 12th, with the first meeting being
devoted to a general overview.
b)   Village Trustees.
Trustee Brower reported on the most recent meeting of the Chamber of Commerce
and reported that Winnetka Days would be held July 20-21. He said that the
Chamber is looking into developing a map of businesses for the central Winnetka
area.
Trustee Woodbury reported that the Design Guidelines Committee was making
progress and expected to complete its mission before the Council's changeover
in April.
c)   Village President. President Holland reported that she had been approached
by a council on tourism seeking to promote shopping up and down the North
Shore. She said that she would attend their initial meeting to see what the
concept entails and determine whether participation would be of benefit to the
Village.
XI. Appointments.
None
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:31 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:32 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
February 6, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved February 20, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 6, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser and Edmund
Woodbury, and President-elect Michael Duhl, and Trustee-elect Nan Greenough.
Absent: Trustee Barbara Aquilino. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development, and 22
persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda.
IV. Quorum.
a)   February 12, 2001, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Lien, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 13, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
c)   February 20, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that expected to attend.
V. Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 9, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   January 16, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   January 29, 2001, Budget Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
Richard Thomas, 638 Elder, voiced concern about an experience with the
Community Development Department and alleged Code Violations which passed the
Department's inspection processes. He asked that the Village address the issue.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-01-2001- Zoning Variations: 391 Sheridan Road - Adoption.
Manager Williams briefly reviewed the history of this issue. He noted that
passage of this ordinance would bring resolution to a long-standing issue and
bring all buildings on the property into zoning compliance.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Lien,
Brower, Presser, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
b)   Resolution R-6-2001- North Shore Community Bank Parking Lot License -
Adoption. Manager Williams explained the License Agreement with North Shore
Community Bank for the use of Village property during its remodeling and
construction project at 576-582 Lincoln Avenue. He referred the Trustees to a
map of the Lincoln Avenue Parking Lot and the Bank’s property. He noted that
the project would be staged from a portion of the Village’s parking lot and
entails fencing off eight parking spaces, all designated "A," in that lot,
along with some of the open space in the Village lot adjacent to the Bank's
property. By staging the project from the rear, Mr. Williams pointed out that
there would be minimal need to close off the Lincoln Avenue sidewalk.
Ms. Janega then summarized the key points of the Agreement, including the term,
fee, penalties, restrictions, etc. and responded to questions from the
Trustees.
Trustee Brower mentioned that the issue of parking had been raised at the
Chamber of Commerce meeting, particularly with regard to realtors.
Mr. Williams suggested parking out into the neighborhoods or behind Grand
Foods. He said that this example illustrates the ever increasing pressure from
competing user groups for parking and underlines the need for the Village's
next major capital project to be a parking facility.
Trustee Powell raised a question about the adequacy of the rate being charged
for the eight spaces. Manager Williams explained the rationale and noted that
using one of the two other rate-setting methods would result in a lower rate.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the Resolution
authorizing the execution of the License Agreement with North Shore Community
Bank. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
c)   Ordinance No. M-3-2001 - Zoning Variation: 850 Hill Rd. - Introduction.
Ms. McCammack reviewed an application for zoning variances from Permitted Use,
Nonconforming Use of a Nonconforming Building, Building Height, and Corner
setback provisions for an accessory (coach house) structure to permit the
construction of a one-story workroom and upper floor dormers to an existing,
nonconforming dwelling/garage. She explained that the coach house is an
existing, nonconforming use because part of it is a second dwelling on a parcel
of property zoned "single family residential." She noted that the Zoning Board
of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend approval of the variances as requested
and that the ordinance itself is drafted to require that the number of sleeping
rooms be limited to the remaining two currently proposed.
The applicant, Joseph Cacciatore, explained that he wished to make the changes
in order to improve the vehicular storage area. He presented elevations to
illustrate that the plans were in keeping with the aesthetics of the existing
coach house and would not significantly alter its character.
Trustee Brower expressed support for the proposed improvements, but expressed
some concern about its continued use for nonconforming purposes. He asked if
the property would continue to be occupied by the same number of tenants.
Mr. Cacciatore answer affirmatively. He remarked that he would be enhancing the
conforming use of the property, not be intensifying the nonconforming portion
of the use, and in fact, would be reducing the sleeping area and baths.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
VIII. Old Business.
None.
IX. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1207 and 1208. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a couple of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1207 in the amount of $904,388.26 and Warrant List No. 1208 in the
amount of $412,486.98. Trustee Lien seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
b)   Request for Preapproval of Van Purchase. Manager Williams explained that
in accordance with their Budget presentation, the Fire Department wishes to
purchase a window van. The new van would provide staff officers with a more
efficient and effective vehicle from which to command emergency incident scenes
than can currently be done with the existing vehicle, which is old and too
small. If the bid for the van is placed before March 1, 2001, the price and
delivery times received in the State of Illinois bid are guaranteed. Therefore,
the Department is requesting authorization to purchase the designated vehicle
to be charged to the FY 2001-2002 Budget.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to authorize the purchase of
the van as requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Aquilino.
c)   Policy Direction: 518 Winnetka Ave. Ms. McCammack noted that the requested
variation for a vertical building height in excess of 35 feet had been removed
from the table by virtue of a new plan ("C") submitted that afternoon. She then
briefly reviewed the history of the property issues and referred the Trustees
to a matrix summarizing the issues and proposed alternatives.
Ms. McCammack explained that the applicant has worked extensively with the
Design Review Board to obtain a favorable recommendation. However, thus far, it
has been unable to obtain final, favorable comment on either design ("A"),
based on the building envelope established under M-16-2000, granting previously
requested zoning variations and a special use permit for the property, or the
revised plan ("B") that generated the additional height variation requests.
In response to a question from Trustee Woodbury, Ms. McCammack indicated that
although the maximum 35 ft. building height was no longer an issue in view of
the third plan, under either "B" or "C" there remained an issue with regard to
the size of the top story. In the first instance the proposed coverage of the
second floor is 72%, and in the latter, it is 65%.
Attorney Janega explained that both plans “B” and “C” exceed the maximum upper
floor area of 50% contemplated in the original variation adopted by Council,
making the proposed building a three-story structure as it is now defined. A
variation from the 2 ½ story height limitation would therefore still be
necessary, even though the vertical height has been reduced.
Steve Friedland, the applicant's attorney, commented on the applicant's efforts
to appease the various interests represented by the boards and the standards
applied by those boards. He explained the applicant's understanding from the
DRB that it did not wish to consider yet another design requiring a zoning
variation until such variation had been granted by the Council. Mr. Friedland
also responded to questions from the Trustees and requested that a variation be
introduced so that final action could be taken by the Council as soon as
possible.
Voy Madeyski, the architect, explained the evolution of the building design and
the differences between Plans "B" and "C". In response to a question from Mr.
Williams, he said that in each of the plans succeeding "A," there had been no
increase in the number of individuals that can be housed in each of the third
floor's four units; the only increases were in living space.
Trustee Presser remarked that no ordinance could be introduced because none had
been prepared as yet, and Attorney Janega explained the procedural options
available to the Council.
President Holland called for comments from the audience.
Ann Lyon, 97 High St., expressed concern about all of the designs saying that
there seemed to be too many projections and too many materials for such a small
space. She said that she would like to see the design simplified overall.
Al Day, 85 High St., reiterated prior objections to the design on the grounds
that this is the first major construction project for the Indian Hill district
since the adoption of the guidelines incorporated into the Comprehensive Plan,
and will, therefore, set the tone for future projects. He expressed the desire
that no further variations be granted, especially any that would increase the
size of the top floor, thereby increasing the bulk of the building.
Trustee Presser inquired whether the neighborhood would be satisfied with a
third floor of 50%. Mr. Day said that residents understand that the developer
has been granted the right to build up to that limit. He expressed his opinion
that the Village's boards are doing their jobs in limiting the development to
that in order to comply with the guidelines set forth in the Comprehensive
Plan.
Dennis Murphy, 530 Winnetka Ave., voiced concern that any increase in the
height or mass of the building would block additional light from his property,
thereby increasing the chance of damage to the mature trees between the
properties.
Manager Williams clarified that the developer is no longer requesting a
variation for a building height in excess of 35 ft.
Marty Kenny, 510 Meadow, commented on the use of the property. He said that he
fails to see any justification for the developer's representation that the
property can't yield a reasonable rate of return without the requested
variances. He expressed his opinion that the Council has already given the
developer significant financial relief in the form of variations and that
further relief would merely set a precedent for future development, contrary to
Comprehensive Plan guidelines.
Trustee Presser urged the developer refine its design by incorporating the area
of Plan A and the appearance of Plan C, so that no further variations would be
required. He encouraged the developer to devise an aesthetically pleasing
development involving as little a variance as possible for the third floor.
Mr. Friedland and Mr. Madeyski addressed Mr. Presser's suggestion, saying that
it was impossible to achieve the vertical look desired by the Design Review
Board and maintain economic feasibility without increasing the size of the top
floor.
Trustee Powell supported Trustee Presser's suggestion and indicated that he
would not be in favor of granting a variation without a positive recommendation
from the Design Review Board.
Trustee Woodbury concurred. He acknowledged that there may be further,
appropriate variations required for the project. He said, however, that he
would not support approval until the developer returns to the Council with the
favorable recommendation of the Design Review Board. He encouraged the
developer to move back toward its original concept and bring forth a plan
balancing both design and zoning requirements.
Trustee Lien indicated that he would not be in favor of granting further relief
on the third floor beyond what has been previously approved.
Trustee Brower agreed with the other Trustees' remarks.
Summarizing the Trustees' comments, President Holland told the applicant that
the Council is willing to consider further variations but not until a design
that meets the criteria of the Design Review Board is presented.
In response to Mr. Friedland's comment that the DRB seemed reluctant to review
another design requiring a variation without first having Council's approval,
Attorney Janega indicated that, normally, the Council requires that all
processes be completed before considering an issue. She indicated that the
Board could be specifically directed to consider further plans presented by the
developer.
Mr. Hardej thanked the Council for its comments and said he would work with
staff and the Design Review Board in an effort to address them.
d)   Policy Direction: 333 Sunset. Ms. McCammack reviewed a request for a
variation from the Maximum Building Size (GFA) provisions of the Village Code
to permit the construction of additions to the subject residence. The
applicants are proposing to add a second story master bath above the existing
one-story structure and to increase the size of the family room and add a
laundry.
Ms. McCammack pointed out that if the detached garage were located in the rear
quarter of the property, it would be given a 400 sq. ft. GFA allowing, bringing
the building size into compliance. She said that the applicant, however, had
objected to that alternative, based on the belief that it would be cost
prohibitive, require the removal of trees, and increase flooding problems. Ms.
McCammack indicated that the Village Forester and Director of Public Works had
disagreed on the tree and flooding issues.  
Finally, Ms. McCammack reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 2-
3 on a recommendation to approve the variance, based largely upon the
consideration that complying alternatives had been identified.
Using photographs and elevations for illustration, the applicant, Katherine
King, extensively explained the reasons for the requested variations. She also
addressed in detail each of the complying alternatives, explaining why she
believed they were not workable.
Each of the Trustees present voiced support for the requested variation of 354
sq. ft., so the Village Attorney was directed to prepare an ordinance for
introduction at the next meeting.
X. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that in accordance with
Council's direction at its January 16th meeting, and until such time as all of
the building permit fees are revised, he has waived the charge to residents,
who are required to install a new pedestal as a result of the Village's ongoing
electrical undergrounding project.
b)   Village Trustees. Trustee Brower reported on the recent Chamber of
Commerce meeting. He mentioned the formation of a new committee to study
promotion of the Winnetka area and to devise ways in which to enhance retail
business in the Village. He also noted that lighting and parking had been
topics of discussion.
Trustee Powell announced that the official groundbreaking for RED Center had
been scheduled and that the project was moving along as expected.
Trustee Presser announced that the new Winnetka Report, including an extensive
article on the Tower Road project, had been mailed.
XI. Appointments.
President Holland called for motions to appoint Sandy Berger and Kevin Plunkett
as the ZBA's representatives to the Plan Commission and Design Guidelines
Committee, respectively, to replace Phyllis Thomas. She also announced the
replacement of Pete Cruikshank by Andy Lubetkin as the School Board
Representative to the Plan Commission.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to approve the appointments as
proposed by President Holland. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
XII. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:43 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:25 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
XIII. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Presser,
Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: Trustee Aquilino. The meeting adjourned at
10:26 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
February 13, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved February 20, 2001)
1. Call to Order.
President Holland called the meeting to order at 7: 30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Bryan
McInturff, Distribution Engineer Rich Ciesla, Trustee-elect Nan Greenough, and
approximately 22 persons in the audience.
2. Preliminary Tower Road Improvement Plans.
President Holland welcomed all of the attendees and outlined the format for the
evening's presentations.
In his introductory remarks Mr. Williams emphasized that the entire Tower Road
project is still under development, that no plans have been finalized, and that
no bids have been let as yet. He informed the attendees of a recently
discovered conflict on the west end between the storm sewer and the natural gas
line which has necessitated changing the order in which the project will be
completed. Instead of doing the western section (Hibbard to Forestway) during
the summer of 2001 and the eastern portion (Green Bay to Hibbard) during the
summer of 2002, as previously planned and announced, the order will be
reversed. He noted, however, that it is expected that all of the electrical
undergrounding will be completed during the 2001.
Mr. Saunders gave a Power Point presentation summarizing the preliminary plans
and various issues concerning the project. He explained each of the reasons
necessitating the rehabilitation, i.e., inadequate drainage, water main
replacement, deteriorating pavement, undergrounding of utilities, and traffic
concerns. He also discussed each of the construction phases in detail and
summarized tentative traffic control plans. He noted that consideration of the
construction of a possible sidewalk on the north side of Tower would be held in
abeyance until spring. In closing, Mr. Saunders reviewed the budget for the
project along with the plans for maintaining up-to-date communication with
residents via email, telephone, web site, bulletin boards, and door hangers.
During Mr. Saunders's presentation, he responded to numerous questions from
audience members. He stressed that completion of the storm sewer work will not
eliminate flooding and water problems in the area, but will reduce the
frequency and severity of those problems.
President Holland acknowledged that this project will be disruptive to many
residents but pointed out that maintaining a sound and up-to-date
infrastructure is extremely important. She asked everyone for patience, good
will, and cooperation during this time. Then, she called for comments from the
floor.
Bernard Hammer, 1455 Tower, and Irv Footlick, 1548 Tower, asked questions about
the purpose and cost of undergrounding the utility wires. Mr. McInturff
explained that undergrounding wires prevents damage from falling limbs,
squirrels, and windstorms, which can cause outages and conditions that pose a
hazard to citizens. In response to the question about cost, he said that when
wires are undergrounded at the Village's choice, the only cost to affected
residents is for the installation of the meter pedestal, which runs
approximately $750 - $1,250.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, asked whether if once the wires are undergrounded, it
would cost more to upgrade service than if the wires were overhead. Mr.
McInturff responded that it would not because by Village ordinance any time
service is upgraded, it must be put underground.
Mr. Kates also asked if the Village had computed the cost of taking over Tower
Road from IDOT. Mr. Williams indicated that there were no exact calculations
but acknowledged that in the long run it will cost the Village money to take
over the maintenance. He said, however, that it is the only way that the new
storm sewer can be installed because IDOT would not permit the Village to do so
otherwise.
Finally, Mr. Kates suggested that the Village confirm its legal standing on the
proposed nighttime truck ban relative to interstate commerce laws. Manager
Williams indicated that the issue had been studied by the Village Attorney.
Carol Hayes, 1487 Tower Rd., expressed concern about the effect of the
installation of the larger water main on existing mature trees along its route.
Her concern was echoed by Bernard Hammer. Mr. Saunders responded that the
Village Forester would be actively involved throughout the project and will
review all plans.
Mr. Hammer also suggested that the Village require everyone affected to install
a 200 amp pedestal so as to permanently upgrade all of the affected residences
at the same time in order to avoid future charges should upgraded service be
desired. Manager Williams indicated that the Village would study that
suggestion.
Tom Evans, 1507 Tower Rd., voiced some concern about the effect that the
project would have on activities and parking at Corwin Park, in particular AYSO
soccer games. He also inquired about refuse pick up and parking when driveways
are inaccessible and about street lighting throughout the rehabilitation
project. With regard to the soccer schedule, Mr. Saunders said that the Village
would work with the Park District to coordinate activities and make suitable
parking arrangements, but that some conflict could not be avoided. He indicated
that refuse pick up would continue as regularly scheduled and that, as
necessary, overnight parking would be accommodated on adjacent streets. In
answer to the question about lighting, Mr. McInturff indicated that for the
most part, there would be no interruption or change in the street lighting
except for an occasional night or two. Mr. Evans asked that the Village check
the location, and perhaps move, the light pole at Tower and Boal Parkway, which
he believes makes maneuvering a vehicle difficult.
David and Sheila Dolkart, 1524 Asbury, cited severe drainage problems in the
Vernon and Asbury area and asked that the Village reviews its drainage plans
for this area and extend the 36" storm sewer up Grove to Asbury. Mr. Saunders
indicated that the Village's drainage analysis shows that the proposed plan
will address the problem, but said that the Village would reexamine it.
After some discussion among audience members of suggested alternatives,
including retention ponds, President Holland commented that the Village has
taken over two years to thoroughly study and engineer this project. She said
that the professional opinion is that the proposed work will help to noticeably
alleviate the problems, but that there is no way to completely eliminate them.
Manager Williams mentioned that in the past the Village has proposed two
different retention areas, only to have them ultimately rejected by neighbors.
Elise Covey, 1503 Edgewood, expressed disappointment over the fact that
residents in her area would now have to wait another year for the improvements
which they had been led to believe would occur this year. She also voiced
concern about parking for construction vehicles working on projects in the
neighborhoods and about adequate policing of detour routes to avoid excessive
traffic problems on nearby neighborhood streets.
Jeff Hoch, 1320 Westmoor Trail, urged the Village to make certain that safe
passage on sidewalks or the parkway is provided for pedestrians, especially
children, throughout the construction process. He voiced particular concern
about the intersection of Tower and Hibbard.
Bob Burton, 1274 Tower, agreed that the Tower and Hibbard intersection needs
attention and suggested that the Village take action to make it safer during
this project.
President Holland then turned to the Trustees for their comments and questions.
Trustee Woodbury asked whether the intersection of Tower and Hibbard would be
involved during both phases of the project, or just one, and whether each phase
would take approximately the same amount of time. Mr. Saunders replied that he
expected to get to just beyond the intersection during the first phase so that
it would only be torn up once. In response to the latter question, he indicated
that because the second (western) phase was more complicated overall, it would
probably take a little longer to complete than the first phase.
Trustee Woodbury also asked in which direction the undergrounding would proceed
and approximately how long it was expected to take to complete. Mr. McInturff
responded that it would probably go from east to west and that he expected the
project to be completed between approximately the first of June and the first
of the year.
President Holland explained to the audience that just because the Village gets
its wires underground, that does not mean that the poles will immediately
disappear because AT&T and Ameritech also have to get their wires down. She
said that both are slow in complying and that this can sometimes take a year or
more.
The Trustees briefly commented on some of the suggestions put forth by the
audience and echoed concerns for safety and trees.
President Holland thanked the audience for its participation, urged them to
continue to be interested and to comment, and repeated her request for
patience, cooperation, and good will.
3. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:00 p.m.
February 20, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved March 6, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 20, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
I. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury, and Trustee-elect Nan Greenough. Absent: President-elect
Michael Duhl Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega and 2 persons in the audience.
II. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
III. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda.
IV. Quorum.
a)   March 6, 2001, Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Powell indicated that he
would not be able to attend.
b)   March 13, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Lien indicated that he would not be
able to attend.
V. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 5, 2001, Budget Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to approve the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
b)   February 6, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino noted that she was not
present for this meeting and that the motion to adjourn in these minutes should
be corrected. Trustee Presser moved to approve the minutes as corrected. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   February 12, 2001, Budget Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to approve the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Presser By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
d)   February 13, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
VI. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
VII. Ordinances and Resolutions.
 
a)   Ordinance No. M-03-2001- Zoning Variations: 850 Hill Road - Adoption.
This ordinance for zoning variances from Permitted Use, Nonconforming Use of a
Nonconforming Building, Building Height, and Corner setback provisions to
permit the construction of an addition and dormers to an existing coach house
that is used as both a dwelling and a garage was introduced at the Council’s
February 6 meeting. There was no further discussion.
Trustee Presser seconded by Trustee Brower moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-04-2001- Zoning Variation: 333 Sunset - Introduction.
This request is for variation from the Maximum Building Size limitations of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of small additions to the
first floor of the residence to reconfigure the side entrance, increase the
size of the family room and to add a laundry room on the first floor, and to
permit a second floor addition to add a master bathroom.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. There was no further discussion.
VIII. Old Business. None.
IX. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1209 and 1210. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on several of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1209 in the amount of $598,416.48 and Warrant List No. 1210 in the
amount of $259,771.61 Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell,
Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Traffic Signal Bids: Scott and Green Bay.
Mr. Williams reviewed the bids for replacement of the traffic signal at the
intersection of Scott and Green Bay Road and recommended awarding the contract
to Pinner Electric, the lowest bidder and a qualified contractor.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the recommendation
of the Village staff to award the contract for traffic signal replacement at
Scott Avenue and Green Bay Road to the low bidder, Pinner Electric, for
$109,800.00. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
X. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported that the Fire Chief had been
contacted by the Insurance Service Office (ISO), who will be inspecting the
Fire Department. Mr. Williams said he was delighted to hear this because the
rating received from the ISO affects insurance rates. He explained that they
evaluate everything from training, personnel on duty, and dispatching to water
supplies.
Mr. Williams announced that the budget study session for Thursday, February 22,
2001 has been cancelled.
b)   Village Trustees.
Trustee Woodbury reported meeting with the Historical Society, who expressed
appreciation for the Village’s support and that they are embarking on a new
strategic analysis in the search for a home for the Society.
Mr. Woodbury also reported that the third public hearing on design guidelines
had been held and discussions are progressing well for the March 13th date with
the Village Council. Trustee Brower commented on a letter received from Mr.
Marty Kenny and Trustee Woodbury said that the Design Guidelines Committee had
spent time on Mr. Kenny’s points regarding height, etc. He said the committee
is focusing on gaps in the design guideline sections of the Village Code and is
trying not to repeat things that are already in the Zoning Ordinance. However,
he added that some suggestions have been made which may lead to text
amendments. He indicated that they are meeting with several community groups
and committees, as well as with design groups and architects who have worked in
the Village to obtain input on the guidelines.
President Holland said she was very impressed with the Village's consultants,
BauerLatoza Studios and The Lakota Group, and that the draft guidelines were
well written. She said that after attending a meeting to learn about guidelines
for commercial property, she was pleased to note that Winnetka has a great deal
of input compared to other communities that allow developers to dictate the
design of commercial developments.
Appointments.
XI. Executive Session.
Trustee Lien moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 7:52 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:45 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, Woodbury and
Trustee-elect Nan Greenough. Absent: None. Also present: Manager Williams and
Attorney Janega.
XII. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Lien, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 8:46 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
March 6, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Adopted March 20, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
6, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, Stephen Presser and Edmund
Woodbury, and President-elect Michael Duhl, and Trustee-elect Nan Greenough.
Absent: Trustee John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of
Public Works Steven Saunders, and three persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3.   Recognition of Award. President Holland acknowledged the receipt from the
Loyola University School of Law by Village Attorney Katherine Janega of the
Francis J. Rooney-St. Thomas More Award. She congratulated Ms. Janega on this
prestigious award in recognition of her outstanding service to the school.
4. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
5. Quorum
a)   March 13, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustees Lien and Woodbury, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   March 20, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
6. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 20, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Lien. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. M-4-2001- Zoning Variations: 333 Sunset - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting the addition of a second story master bath and two small additions
to the first floor to reconfigure the rear entrance, add a laundry and mud
room, and provide enclosed, conforming access to the basement of the residence.
There was no further discussion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
b)   Resolution R-7-2001- Adopting Annual Budget for Fiscal Year 2001-2002 -
Public Hearing and Adoption. President Holland opened the Public Hearing. Mr.
McKee provided a brief summary of the Budget, saying that homeowners could
expect to see approximately a 1.4% increase. There being no comments from the
floor, President Holland closed the Hearing. There was no further discussion
among the Trustees.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the Resolution
adopting the Annual Budget for Fiscal Year 2001-2002. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
c)   Ordinance No. M-5-2001 - Authorizing Disposal of Surplus Village Property
- Introduction. Mr. Saunders explained that in preparation for moving the
operation, equipment, and materials to an off-site location before construction
of the new Public Works facility, staff has identified surplus inventory that
has either exceeded its useful life or is of little value. In order to ensure
an efficient move and to avoid unnecessary storage and moving costs, this
ordinance would authorize the Village Manager to dispose of the property in
whatever manner is deemed appropriate.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to brief the Council on the current status
of temporary warehousing and the projected timetable. Mr. Saunders indicated
that although no final plans have been made, a possible warehousing site has
been located in Glenview at a cost of approximately $200,000 for a year, which
fits into the projected budget. He said that staff hopes that demolition will
begin late in the summer.
d)   Resolutions Establishing Rates and Fees Related to Water Service, Service
Taps and Meters, Electric Service and Sewer Services - Introduction. Mr. McKee
explained that as part of the budget process the ordinances that govern user
fees are reviewed annually to make sure that they reflect any rate changes and
still meet the operational needs of the staff. He said that the proposed
ordinances reflect no change to the actual rate schedules, but do include one,
small substantive change. Recently, the Village has noticed an increase in the
number of customers on the automatic payment plan who do not have sufficient
funds for payment of the bill on the due date. Heretofore, staff has been
interpreting the returned payment fee as being applicable in these cases, but
wording has been drafted to clarify this policy in all rate schedules. He noted
that similar changes would be incorporated into the refuse service and
miscellaneous fees scheduled for adoption at the March 20th meeting.
i)   Resolution R-8-2001- Establishing Rates and Fees Related to Water Service,
Service Taps and Meters.
ii)   Resolution R-9-2001 - Establishing Rates and Fees for Electric Service.
iii)   Resolution No. R-10-2001 - Establishing Rates and Fees for Sewer
Services.
There was no discussion about the specific resolutions. Trustee Presser,
seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce Resolutions Nos. R-8-2001, R-
9-2001, and R-10-2001. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Powell.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1211 and 1212. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a couple of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1211 in the amount of $788,425.34 and Warrant List No. 1212 in the
amount of $374,849.96. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Lien, Aquilino, Presser,
and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
b)   Landscape Maintenance Agreement. Mr. Saunders reported that the Village's
landscape maintenance contract with Anthony Scopelliti Landscaping, Inc. will
end on March 31st, 2001. He said that Scopelliti Landscaping had been awarded a
two (2) year landscape maintenance contract in 1998, with an option to renew
for a 3rd year. He said that Scopelliti is interested in extending the contract
for the additional year and commented that they have provided quality landscape
maintenance services to the Village for five (5) consecutive years. The current
monthly fee for the contract is $2,819.50. Scopelliti proposes to extend its
contract with a minimal monthly increase of 2.5%, or $70.49, equaling
$2,889.99. The increase is necessary due to increases in fuel and labor costs.
Mr. Saunders recommended that the contract be extended for the year.
Trustee Brower inquired whether there would be any problem about not going out
for competitive bids. Attorney Janega responded that there would not be because
the option had been built into the original contract.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to extend the contract as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Lien, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Powell.
c)   Parkway Tree Trimming and Emergency Maintenance Contracts. Mr. Saunders
reported that the Village’s parkway tree trimming and tree and stump removal
agreement with Robert W. Hendricksen Company and the Village’s emergency
parkway tree maintenance agreements with Bartlett Tree Expert Company,
respectively, will end on March 31, 2001. He noted that both companies have
been awarded the contract and have been the lowest bidders for three
consecutive years. Both companies have submitted letters of intent to extend
their current agreements with the Village for one year at last year’s fees. He
remarked on the Forester's pleasure with the performance of both companies and
recommended approval of the extensions.
Manager Williams noted that last year the competitive bids for this contract
had been extremely close. He supported the extension saying that next year
another contract for tree trimming for the Water & Electric Department would be
coming up for bid and it would be advantageous to rebid the entire package at
that time.
Trustee Aquilino asked whether this contract included all of the parkway trees
and the commercial districts and what the current trimming cycle is. Mr.
Saunders replied that it did. He said that presently these trees are trimmed on
a seven year cycle but that it is the Village's goal to reduce the cycle to
every six years.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to extend the contracts as
recommended, with the current fees to remain in effect during the extension
period. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Powell.
d)   Bid No. 01-001: Tree Planting. Mr. Saunders explained that this bid was
for the purchase and planting of approximately 170 parkway trees and 30 private
trees within the Village for FY 2001 from St. Aubin Nursery and Arthur Weiler
Nursery in the amounts of approximately $45,975 and $3,025, respectively. He
noted that these bids include the trees requested by residents participating in
the Private Tree Planting Program, and pointed out that while they will be able
to take advantage of the Village's purchasing power and guarantee, they will be
fully responsible for their own costs.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the bids as
noted. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Powell.
e)   Landscape Waste Transfer Contract with Thelen. Mr. Saunders briefly
reviewed the history of this issue as well as the general provisions and
responsibilities incorporated into the proposed Agreement, including the fact
that the Village will be named as the permanent operator of the site with
Thelen as its private hauler.
Trustee Brower asked about the status of the application for transfer of the
site permit. Mr. Saunders replied that an inspection was scheduled for the
coming week.
President Holland inquired about the adequacy of the frequency of pick up from
the site. Mr. Saunders responded that Thelen intends to remove waste daily
except during slow periods so that there should not be an odor problem.
Manager Williams pointed out that this is only a one year contract with
provisions for renewal.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to authorize execution of
the Agreement with Thelen Sand & Gravel, Inc. for landscape waste removal
services from the Village Yards. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Woodbury, and Lien.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
f)   Quarterly Reports. Manager Williams referred the Trustees to the Quarterly
Reports prepared by the Department Heads wrapping up the year's capital
improvements. He said that although he was disappointed that as much
undergrounding hadn't been accomplished as hoped, overall it had been a very
successful year.
10. Reports.
11. Appointments.
President Holland suggested the appointment of Trustee Aquilino as the
Alternate Representative to RED Center. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee
Brower, moved to approve this appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
She then recommended the appointment of Bill Meuer to replace Pete Cruikshank
on the Board of Fire and Police Commissioners. Trustee Brower, seconded by
Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve this appointment. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
For the Landmark Preservation Commission, President Holland suggested the
appointment of Carter Page to replace Nan Greenough as Chairman and Jill Zapton
to replace Mary Badgley. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved
to approve the appointment of Carter Page. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve
the appointment of Ms. Zapton. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Finally President Holland recommended the appointment of Hugh Brower to the
Canvassing Board for the April 3rd election. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by
Trustee Presser, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Powell. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:50 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Brower, Lien, Aquilino, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent:
Trustee Powell. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Presser, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: Trustee Powell. The meeting
adjourned at 9:51 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
March 13, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved April 3, 2001)
1. Call to Order.
President Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Powell, and Stephen Presser. Absent:
Trustees Lien and Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
President-elect Michael Duhl, Trustee-elect Nan Greenough, and approximately
one person in the audience.
2. Project Introduction, Overview, and Presentation.
Joanne Bauer from BauerLatoza Studio used a Power Point presentation to provide
an overview of the draft Design Guidelines, highlighting the architectural
aspects. She also reviewed the steps the consultants had taken in developing
the Guidelines and noted that they were developed with the hope of assisting
developers, architects and the Design Review Board with the design and
assessment of future projects. She said that the consultants had pulled apart
the components of commercial, mixed use, and multi-family buildings that are
considered to be in keeping with the desired character of the Village. Among
these components, she discussed building style, setbacks, height, window types,
entries, roof forms, shapes of roof, building massing, proportion, scale,
articulation, signage, etc.
Following her presentation, Ms. Bauer responded to questions about some of the
specific elements from the Trustees.
Scott Freres from The Lakota Group then provided an overview of the Guidelines
for public spaces, which encompass everything outside of the building. These
elements generally address the streetscape, pedestrian zones and pedestrian
circulation, vehicular zones, lighting, and signage. Among these components are
benches, trash containers, water fountains, paper bins, planters, fences, and
the like. He noted that the consultants had considered many options for each of
the elements before narrowing each down to a family of alternatives from which
to establish the standards. Mr. Freres pointed out that while retrofitting
buildings would be very expensive, many of the standards could be incorporated
into new development.
Manager Williams relayed the Public Works Director’s comments on the proposals
for street and sidewalk design, noting that some would affect parking
throughout the Village.
Following Mr. Freres's presentation, the Trustees discussed features of the
proposed Guidelines.
Manager Williams explained that once the Guidelines are completed and we have
policy direction, the Village Code would have to be amended in order to provide
consistency.
Attorney Janega noted that it is up to the Council to prioritize the
Guidelines. She suggested that it first identify those things that it wants to
treat as absolute requirements, which could, then, be built into the Code to
give them the force of law. She added that items that fall somewhere in the
middle could be adopted by resolution, which would allow the Council to retain
control while still providing guidance for the Design Review Board. The final
tier of priorities, Ms. Janega explained, could simply be adopted as design
criteria, with the Design Review Board being authorized to amend or modify as
necessary. She said that this would provide a progressive scheme of design
regulation, assisting the Design Review Board in enforcing the Village’s design
policies, while giving the Board the flexibility to weigh cases as they arise
and fine-tune the guidelines as it sees fit.
3. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:40 p.m. .
March 20, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved April 3, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
20, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury, and President-elect Michael Duhl. Absent: Trustee-elect
Nan Greenough. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, and twelve persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   April 3, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   April 10, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Powell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 6, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
President Holland welcomed Mr. Grossberg and his Scout troop.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. M-5-2001 - Authorizing Disposal of Surplus Village Property
- Adoption. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the
ordinance authorizing the Village Manager to dispose of surplus property from
the Village Yards in whatever manner is deemed appropriate. Mr. Williams
mentioned that both the Foreman and the Department Head would have to approve
any items to be disposed. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
b)   Resolutions Establishing Rates and Fees Related to Water Services,
Electric Services, Sewer Services, Refuse Service, Miscellaneous Permits,
Licenses and Services, and Building and Zoning Fees - Adoption. Ms Janega
explained that the proposed resolutions reflect no change to the actual rate
schedules, but do include language clarifying the fee for returned payments.
She pointed out that Resolutions R-8-2001, R-9-2001 and R-10-2001, which make
changes to water, electric and sewer rate schedules, were introduced on March
3, 2001. Resolutions R-11-2001, R-12-2001, and R-13-2001, which make the
corresponding changes to the refuse service rate schedule, to the miscellaneous
permit, license and service fee schedule, and to the building and zoning permit
fee schedule do not require introduction. She also noted that R-12-2001
formalizes Council's decision to waive the permit fees associated with
modifications to existing service entrance equipment if those modifications
result from the Water & Electric Department's scheduled undergrounding program.
i)   Resolution R-8-2001- Establishing Rates and Fees Related to Water Service,
Service Taps and Meters.
ii)   Resolution R-9-2001 - Establishing Rates and Fees for Electric Service.
iii)   Resolution No. R-10-2001 - Establishing Rates and Fees for Sewer
Services.
iv)   Resolution No. R-11-2001 - Refuse Service Rates.
v)   Resolution No. R-12-2001 - Miscellaneous Permits, Licenses and Services.
vi)   Resolution No. R-13-2001- Building and Zoning Fees.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the six rate
resolutions. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Resolution No. R-15-2001 - Commending Rescuers of Woman and Dog. President
Holland read the resolution commending David Husted, Bryce Radke, Lee Habben,
Steve Kolmin and Joanne Hennessy for their alertness, courage and compassion in
responding to an incident that occurred at Elder Lane Park on February 23,
2001.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-6-2001 - Zoning Variation: 420 Elder Ln. - Introduction.
Ms. Janega reviewed the request for a variation from the rear yard setback
requirements to permit construction of a 1-story detached garage 3 feet from
the west property line. This request requires a variation of 3 feet from the 6
foot setback because the rear yard abuts the side yard of the lot to the west.
She explained that the applicants wish to place the garage as close to the
south and west lot lines as possible so as to not jeopardize the health of
existing mature trees, including a 250-year old, 30-inch diameter maple tree
and a triple-stem clump ash tree. The requested placement of the garage would
allow more valuable root space for both trees. She noted that the Zoning Board
of Appeals had voted unanimously to recommend that the Council grant the
variation.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
adopting the requested variation. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-7-2001 - Zoning Variation: 1036 Spruce Street -
Introduction. Ms. Janega reviewed this request for a variation from the side
yard and building line articulation requirements to allow the vertical
extension of the existing second floor walls and roof. She explained that the
side yard variation is also necessary to allow new windows to be installed in
the nonconforming west wall on the first floor level. She noted that the
vertical extension of the unarticulated west wall on the second floor also
requires a variation and pointed out that the entire project results in a
completed total building size that is in compliance with the gross floor area
limitation for this property. Ms. Janega finished by mentioning that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted in favor of recommending that the variations be
granted.
Trustee Powell voiced initial opposition to the request, expressing concern
that although it looks like a nice improvement, the applicants are taking
advantage of where the house is positioned in order to build a wider home than
would normally be permitted on the lot. He noted that there are other similarly
situated houses on the block and said that it would be very difficult to
justify refusing like requests, which could result in a row house effect. He
said he would like to see the plan modified.
Attorney Janega pointed out that the applicants are permitted by right to
extend their house to the east.
Trustee Lien concurred with Trustee Powell. He said that if this were new
construction, no variance would be granted for the placement of the house, and
that the substantial increase in the GFA results in essentially a new house. He
noted that set backs are designed to protect neighboring property owners and
said that these variations would permit this house to take up more than its
fair share of what the Zoning Ordinance allows. He indicated that he was not
inclined to grant either variation.
Trustee Aquilino agreed that if it were new construction no variance would be
granted but spoke in support of this request, saying that the Village is trying
to encourage rehabilitation, and in her opinion, this request falls within the
line of reasonableness.
Trustee Brower supported Trustee Aquilino's point of view. He said that the
plans would greatly improve the home, that the house would not be out of
character with others in the area, and it would enhance the neighborhood
without another tear down.
The applicants, Timothy and Marianne Marino, spoke in support of their
proposal, saying that they had spent a great deal of time trying to create as
compact a design as possible that would require a minimum of zoning variances.
Trustee Woodbury joined Trustees Aquilino and Brower in support of the proposed
variations.
Saying that it was a very difficult choice, Trustee Presser indicated that he
would support the motion.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
adopting the requested variations. By voice vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Woodbury, Brower, and Presser. Nays: Trustees Powell and
Lien. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-16-2001 - Annual Fee for Fire & Emergency Services -
Introduction. Mr. Williams explained that annually the Council must approve the
charges to unincorporated New Trier Township for fire and emergency paramedical
and rescue services. He noted that the new charge is $573.59 for each premises,
and pointed out that this amount is down $10.32 from last year's fee because
the amount is very formula driven, based on (1) assessed valuation, (2) number
of calls, and (3) population.
Trustee Brower inquired whether the Village had any difficulty collecting this
fee. Manager Williams responded that any problems had been resolved by using
the water system as leverage.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
setting the annual fee. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Lien, and Brower.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Policy Direction: Espresso Pacifico. The applicant, Barbara Jacobson,
explained her request to serve liquor at the establishment's sidewalk café
tables. She expressed her belief that the service enhances the ambiance and
character of the community in addition to be a service desired by patrons. She
asked that the Council support her request and help her business continue to
grow.
President Holland noted that the history of this prohibition has very little to
do with ambiance but has been imposed because of serious concerns about
liability.
Manager Williams mentioned that previously, pursuant to prior Council
discussions, acting in its capacity as Liquor Advisory Board, the Village
President, as Liquor Control Commissioner, promulgated a rule defining
"licensed premises" as excluding any part of the public way. Therefore,
permission to serve liquor on the sidewalks, which are public property, has
been routinely denied.
Trustee Lien expressed serious concerns about liability as well as enforcement
issues. He cited examples in which a problem might occur. He also pointed out
that the Village does not permit other merchants to use public property for
mercantile activity. Saying that this is an analogous situation, he expressed
his belief that all merchants should be treated equitably and that sales of
liquor should be restricted to inside the premises.
Trustee Powell voiced some support for the concept if some kind of special,
readily terminable, license can be established.
Trustee Presser supported the concept and suggested additional indemnification.
In response to a question from Trustee Aquilino, Attorney Janega explained the
various liability issues. She said that the premises liability is much more of
a concern that the dram shop liability because of the trip and fall issues.
Trustee Brower voiced concern to about serving liquor on Village property and
echoed Trustee Lien's worry about enforcement.
Saying that there are persuasive arguments on both sides of the issue, Trustee
Woodbury asked for further information about the policies of neighboring
communities.
Manager Williams suggested that the topic be slated for the April Study Session
at which time research from other communities could be presented and a series
of options discussed. The Trustees concurred.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1213 and 1214. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a couple of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1213 in the amount of $612,320.80 and Warrant List No. 1214 in the
amount of $528,739.32. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Lien, Aquilino, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Bid No. 01-004: Backhoe Replacement. Mr. Williams explained this bid for
the purchase and delivery of a new backhoe to replace the one currently used by
the Public Works Department. He discussed the various specifications for the
item and noted that of the five bids received, three were deficient with
respect to the critical specifications. He pointed out that of the other two
bids, the only machine that meets all of the major specifications as well as
the critical specifications is the Komatsu machine proposed by Roland Machinery
for $65,193, which staff believes would provide more flexibility and be a
better machine.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the
recommendation to accept the bid of Roland Machinery for a Komatsu backhoe. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Abstain: Trustee Lien. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
a)   President. President Holland commented on the recent fatal automobile
accident at the corner of Elm and Locust and extended condolences to the family
of Lenore Shapiro on behalf of all Winnetkans. She urged all drivers to
exercise extreme caution when driving and to avoid distractions and excess
speed.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Powell reported on his attendance at a recent Northwest
Municipal Conference meeting and commented on the importance of keeping up with
state legislation that has an impact on local municipalities.
Trustee-elect Nan Greenough arrived at 8:41 p.m.
Trustee Aquilino reported on the recent meeting of the Beautification Committee
and the Chamber of Commerce with the representative of the Lakota Group
regarding the proposed Design Guidelines. She said that the group had focused
on the streetscape aspects of the Guidelines and that the feedback from the
meeting had been positive.
Trustee Aquilino also commented on the most recent Chamber of Commerce meeting
and urged all of the Trustees to attend the Chamber's 'After Hours' scheduled
for March 29th at the French Institute.
Trustee Brower mentioned that he had attended the most recent WYO meeting at
which the group reported that they had a successful winter, would be employing
a new program director, and were looking for board members with business
experience.
Trustee Woodbury reported that the meeting between the Design Guidelines
Committee and the Design Review Board had been very productive and that a
meeting with architects was scheduled for March 22nd, with a final meeting of
the Ad Hoc Design Guidelines Committee set for April 19th.
c)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the National Arbor Day Foundation
recently names Winnetka as a 2000 Tree City USA, a recognized standard of an
effective community forestry program, the basis of which is a continuing
commitment to ongoing improvement and renewal.
He also reported that according to the recently released statistics Winnetka's
Census 2000 population is 12,419.
11. Appointments.
President Holland suggested the appointment of Bernard Hammer to the Forestry
Commission to replace David Wright whose term has expired. Trustee Powell,
seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:55 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present: Manager Williams and Attorney Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:16 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
April 3, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved April 17, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
3, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Louise Holland called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, John Powell, Stephen Presser
and Edmund Woodbury, President-elect Michael Duhl, and Trustee-elect Nan
Greenough. Absent:. None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, and 5 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Holland led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   April 10, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   April 17, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5.   Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 13, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   March 20, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution R-14-2001 - Annual Fee for Fire & Emergency Services -
Adoption. Mr. Williams explained that this resolution, which was introduced at
the March 20 Council meeting, establishes the annual charges to Kenilworth and
unincorporated New Trier Township for fire and emergency paramedic and rescue
services. He noted that the new charge is $573.59 for each premises, and
pointed out that this amount is down $10.32 from last year.
Trustee Brower noted an inconsistency in that the quarterly amounts shown in
the resolution do not total the annual amount.
Attorney Janega responded that in past years only the annual amount is billed
for this service and that Section 3 of the resolution should therefore be
deleted.
Trustee Brower moved to amend the resolution, seconded by Trustee Powell. By
voice vote, the motion to amend passed unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt Resolution R-14-
2001 as amended, approving the annual fee for fire and emergency services. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. 
b)   Ordinance No. M-6-2001 - Zoning Variation; 420 Elder Lane - Adoption. Ms.
Janega reviewed this ordinance, which was introduced at the March 20 Council
meeting and will grant a variation from the rear yard setback requirements to
permit the construction of a one-story detached garage three feet from the west
property line.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance M-6-
2001, granting a zoning variation to 420 Elder Lane. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower,
Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-7-2001 - Zoning Variation 1036 Spruce Street - Adoption.
Ms. Janega reviewed this request for a variation from the side yard and
building line articulation requirements to allow the vertical extension of the
existing second floor walls and roof. The ordinance was introduced at the March
20 Council meeting.
Trustee Aquilino asked about the side yard requirements of 6 feet and 8 feet.
Attorney Janega replied that the side yard requirements had been amended and
articulation requirements had been added to the Zoning Ordinance about two
years ago to address the bulk issue.
Manager Williams further explained that at that time, builders were taking
advantage of the allowance for garages by building homes with both side yards
at six feet.
Trustee Aquilino stated that, although she is going to vote to approve this
variation, a valid point was raised at the last meeting about the addition
being very close to the lot line. She suggested that some formula be developed
so that, if one side yard was less than the requirement, the other side yard
would be increased in order to provide for a total of 14 feet of side yards.
Trustee Lien commented that his mind had not changed since the last meeting and
that he will vote against the variation because this variation on the side yard
allows the house to be expanded where it’s located almost at the lot line.
Since the house is being increased substantially, he considered this to be
virtually new construction and a variance would not be allowed for new
construction. He added that he thought this rule should be applied to this case
since the addition is so large, although he didn’t think a case should be made
only for this one house.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance M-7-
2001, granting a zoning variation to 1036 Spruce Street. By roll call vote, the
motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Woodbury, Brower, and Presser. Nays:
Trustees Lien and Powell. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-8-2001 - Disposal of Miscellaneous Property -
Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that this ordinance allows for the disposal of
property recovered by the police, mainly bicycles, and also gives the Finance
Director authorization to dispose of any computer equipment that is retired
during the current fiscal year. He noted that the proposed disposal methods are
consistent with past practice and that traditionally, bicycles are taken to the
Community House for their auction, which will be April 28th this year.
Trustee Powell urged people who are interested to attend the auction because
there is a good assortment of bicycles.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Resolution No. R-18-2001 - Awarding Contract Extension for Sidewalk
Replacement - Adoption. Manager Williams explained that the Village's sidewalk
replacement contract with Schroeder and Schroeder, Inc. provides for three one-
year extensions so long as the Village and contractor agree on adjusted prices
based on the CPI rate of change, which was +4.6%. He said that the contractor
has agreed to the adjustment and, based upon Schroeder and Schroeder's
performance in 2000, the staff recommends extending this contract.
President Holland added that the Village has not received any complaints in the
years that Schroeder has been working for the Village.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt Resolution R-18-
2001, authorizing the extension of the Village's contract with Schroeder and
Schroeder, Inc. for sidewalk replacement for calendar year 2001. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell,
Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8. Old Business.
a)   Sidewalk Liquor Service. Manager Williams reported that at the Council's
March 20th meeting, the Council had directed the staff to survey neighboring
communities to determine their policies on the service of liquor at sidewalk
cafes. After summarizing the results of the survey, Mr. Williams asked the
Council to determine if service of liquor should be allowed on public
sidewalks, to specify how it is to be implemented and to determine if any
special conditions would be required.
After hearing comments from Barbara Jacobson of Espresso Pacifico and comments
from the Trustees, the majority of the Council agreed to allow sidewalk liquor
service on an experimental basis with annual reviews when liquor licenses are
renewed, subject to the following special conditions:
      Ø   Service is of beer and wine only and with full meals;
      Ø   Service will not be after 9:00 p.m. on weekdays nor after 10:00 p.m.
on weekends;
      Ø   The area where liquor is served is to be defined by either fencing or
planters, so that the pedestrian path is not obstructed and is to be contiguous
to the store front;
      Ø   The restaurant must have dram shop insurance of $5,000,000 -
$10,000,000 and general liability insurance with the Village named as
additional insured to cover the sidewalk area;
      Ø   The sidewalk area where liquor is served is to be supervised by an
employee of the restaurant at all times; and
      Ø   Implementation would be by a rider to the restaurant’s liquor
license, with annual reviews.
President Holland directed the Village Attorney to draft an ordinance for the
April 17th meeting that would create a rider to the liquor licenses for the
service of beer and wine at outdoor sidewalk cafes and would incorporate the
special conditions listed above.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1215 and 1216. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1215 in the amount of $777,640.88 and Warrant
List No. 1216 in the amount of $263,245.16 Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Lien, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
Trustees. Trustee Woodbury reported that the Ad Hoc Design Guidelines Committee
had held discussions with architects who had offered good comments for design
guidelines.
Manager. Manager Williams announced that the public hearing on the proposed
Design Guidelines would be held on May 1, with ordinances to be introduced at
that time, followed by adoption at the Council’s May 15 meeting.
Manager Williams also reported that the Police Chief had advised him on the
Department’s efforts to enforce the truck ban. He said they have found that
trucks are making deliveries to gas stations, Grand Foods and the Post Office,
and therefore are exempt from the ban. He also said that there were no other
trucks other than these going through the Village.
Trustee Brower commented that it is not known, then, whether trucks are
diverting around Winnetka or not.
President Holland said she had asked the Village President in Wilmette if she
would like to participate in a meeting about the trucks, but she felt that
there were so few that it was not worth the time. President Holland expressed
her hope that truck traffic in Winnetka had decreased and said that the Police
Department will continue to monitor truck traffic.
11. Appointments.
President Holland said that Jane Burns, whose temporary term as a member of the
Landmark Preservation Commission expires, had asked to be appointed to her own
term on the Landmark Preservation Commission. Trustee Brower moved to approve
the appointment of Jane Burns to the Landmark Preservation Commission, seconded
by Trustee Powell. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:34 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:30 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury and
President-elect Mike Duhl and Trustee-elect Nan Greenough. Absent: None.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Lien, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Lien, Woodbury, and Brower. Nayes: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:31 p.m.
