                  Recording Secretary
April 10, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved May 1, 2001)
1. Call to Order.
President Holland called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Lien, Stephen Presser, and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
President-elect Michael Duhl, Trustee-elect Nan Greenough, and one person in
the audience.
2. Power Supply Study.
Mr. McInturff explained that if Winnetka intends to terminate its agreement
that expires in 2007 with IMEA to purchase power, it must give IMEA a five-year
notice of its intention to do so. Then he introduced Scott Feuerborn and Brian
Duncan, representatives from the engineering firm of Burns and McDonnell that
was hired to perform the power supply study, who presented their study and
analysis to the Trustees.
Trustee Powell arrived at 7:40.
Mr. Duncan reviewed the existing power supply situation, noting the Village’s
steady load growth since 1996. Based on previous consumption data from 1996
through 2000, Burns and McDonnell projected a peak demand of about 40,000
kilowatts in 2010.
Mr. Duncan reported that the Village has about 31,000 kilowatts of local
generation capacity. Thus, to meet current and future peak demands, the Village
must rely on purchase power contracts or add future local generation.
Burns and McDonnell analyzed the following four options for the Village to meet
the electrical needs of the Village:
1.   Continue with the current agreement with the Illinois Municipal Electric
Agency, which is obligated to meet all of the Village’s electrical needs. The
projected net present value in 2007 of this option is $22.5 million.
2.   Install new generation at the Village’s power plant or Village Yards. The
projected net present value in 2007 of this option is $26.9 million.
3.   Purchase Village’s base load from the market and self-generate peaking
load. The net present value of this option in 2007 is $33.1 million.
4.   Must take contract and call options in which the Village purchases base-
load energy, self-generates intermediate energy and purchases call options for
peaking energy. The net present value in 2007 of this option is $33.8 million.
Thus, the least cost alternative for the Village is to continue with the IMEA
contract. The consultant also tested the results by creating high and low load
growth scenarios as well as high and low fuel prices. This sensitivity analysis
indicated that under all conditions, the IMEA contract is the least costly
alternative for the Village.
Mr. Duncan concluded that Burns and McDonnell recommends that the Village
maintain the current IMEA contract, but the Village must continue to monitor
IMEA’s costs and the cost of natural gas.
Manager Williams reviewed a staff analysis, focusing on the transmission system
in northern and central Illinois. He noted that for the first time the power
plant cannot meet all of the Village's electrical needs 100% of the time, and
therefore, the Village is slowly becoming more and more dependent upon the
transmission system to provide power to Winnetkans. Based on the following
factors, Manager Williams concurred with the consultants' recommendations to
continue with the IMEA contract:
1.   IMEA has the transmission agreements in place to assure that the Village
will receive power from IMEA;
2.   IMEA’s generation assets (both owned and contractual) use several
different fuel sources (49% diesel; 25% natural gas; 19% coal; 3% hydro and 3%
jet fuel). This fuel diversity helps to insulate IMEA’s customers from rapid
price increases if the price of one fuel increases rapidly (for example the
recent price escalations in natural gas can be mitigated by switching to
generating resources that use other fuels).
3.   When the Village joined IMEA in 1990, the Village committed itself to pay
for its share of a coal-fired plant in Kentucky. In 2006, the Village makes its
last payment for this plant, which will reduce the Village’s annual bill to
IMEA by about $1,000,000.
4.   The Village has some control over the future destiny of its power costs as
Winnetka is a member of IMEA’s Board of Directors and has also served on the
Executive Board.
After posing questions to the consultants and Manager Williams, the Trustees
agreed to accept their recommendations and continue the IMEA agreement with
regular, careful monitoring of the situation.
3. Change Order: Mt. Pleasant Water Line.
Mr. McInturff explained the increased costs associated with the installation of
a replacement 8" water main on Mt. Pleasant from Locust to Glendale. He said
that because the condition of the pipe when it was exposed indicated that it
should be replaced, the decision was made to replace all of the old main on Mt.
Pleasant. This required additional pipe and increased the amount of work, but
eliminates the possibility of any failures at the tap. The estimated cost of
the additional work is $60,500, which was not included in the bid and increases
the total cost to $138,150, which is more than the $125,000 included in the
2000-01 budget for the project.
4. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Study Session at 9:46 p.m. Present: President
Holland, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury and
President-elect Mike Duhl and Trustee-elect Nan Greenough. Absent: None.
5. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:47 p.m.
May 1, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved May 15, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 1,
2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Acting Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus and six persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 7(f) had been removed from the Agenda. The
Council approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   May 8, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend, but Trustee Powell said that he might be late.
b)   May 15, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Presser, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 10, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
b)   April 17, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln., voiced objection to Council's passage
earlier this year of Ordinance No. MC-1-2001 amending policy pertaining to the
scheduling of regular Council meetings during years in which there is a General
Election.
Manager Williams explained the reasons for the change, and President Duhl
indicated that the Council would discuss reconsidering the ordinance at a later
time.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. M-9-2001 - Zoning Variation: 795 Foxdale - Adoption. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt this ordinance permitting
variances from the Maximum Building Size and Intensity of Use of Lot provisions
of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of a one-story addition at
the rear of the residence. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-10-2001 - Zoning Variation: 885 Hill Rd. - Adoption. Kevin
Panek appeared on behalf of the applicant. He explained that the garage doors
are merely being relocated from the rear of the house to the front and that the
change would, in fact, reduce the overall impermeable surface.
In response to a question raised at introduction, Mr. Norkus explained that the
work which is already underway is in full compliance with a building permit
issued in January on a variation to narrow the existing garage. The variation
currently under consideration would be to delete that approved variation.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting a variation from the Width of Attached Garage provision of the
Zoning Ordinance, in order to allow for the modification of an existing
attached garage. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: None..
c)   Ordinance No. M-11-2001 - Zoning Variation: 757 Locust Rd. - Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting variations from the Front Yard and Corner Yard Setbacks of the
Zoning Ordinance to permit construction of a one-story front entrance addition.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-12-2001 - Zoning Variation: 1230 Westmoor - Adoption.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt this ordinance
permitting variations from the Front Setback and Side Yard provisions of the
Zoning Ordinance to allow additions at the front and rear of the existing
residence. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Resolution No. R-21-2001 - Prevailing Wages - Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that pursuant to the provisions of the State of Illinois Prevailing
Wage Act, the Village is required annually to investigate and ascertain the
prevailing rate of wages for laborers, mechanics, and other works in the
Village employed in performing construction of public works.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing the Village's Prevailing Rate of Wages. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-23-2001 - Approving IAFF Agreement - Adoption. As noted
above, this item was removed from the Agenda.
g)   Ordinance No. M-13-2001 - Alley Vacation: 329 Fairview - Introduction.
Attorney Janega reviewed an offer from the owner of the subject property to
purchase the west 10 feet of the unimproved public alley that abuts the rear
lot line of the property. She explained that in 1998 similar alley vacations
were adopted by the Council for the properties located at 325 and 333 Fairview.
This vacation would align the rear lot lines of the three properties. She
reported that in keeping with Village policy, Village staff obtained
information on the value of the property and reported the fair market value of
the alley strip to be $27,000. She said the owner had tendered that amount to
the Village with his purchase offer. Ms. Janega also reported that the proposed
consolidated lot conforms with the Subdivision Ordinance and that the Acting
Director of Community Development has recommended approval, subject to the
reservation of utility easements, as recommended by the Director of Water &
Electric. She pointed out that a public hearing is not required by the Illinois
Municipal Code, because the alley is located within the Village's corporate
limits.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance
authorizing the vacation of the alley right-of-way at 329 Fairview and
accepting the Plat of Consolidation. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
h)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2001 - Sidewalk Liquor Service - Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed the language amending the draft ordinance discussed at the last
meeting. She noted the following clarifications:
      §   To be eligible for the sidewalk restaurant rider, the applicant must
have a sidewalk café license as well as a Class A or Class A-1 liquor license.
The license is limited to the sale of beer and wine for consumption on the
premises.
      §   As with all liquor licenses, the sale of beer or wine must be
incidental to the sale and service of a complete meal. The prohibition against
issuing a bill for beer or wine only, without a charge for food, has been
relocated to the front of the section , so that it is closer to the provisions
that have similar content.
      §   The revisions emphasize that the service is to be to customers who
are seated at the outdoor tables and that having a permit to operate the
sidewalk restaurant is a prerequisite to the sidewalk restaurant rider.
      §   Pursuant to discussions with the Finance Director, the amount of
insurance has been set at $2 million for general liability, with excess
coverage adding at least another $2 million. The amount of dram shop insurance
is set at $1,000,000 unless more is required by law or applicable rule.
      §   The term of the license has been more clearly stated, with December 1
of the year the rider is issued being the automatic expiration date. The
revisions also emphatically state that there is no right or expectation to a
renewal of the sidewalk restaurant rider, and that each year’s application is
treated de novo.
      §   As discussed at the Council’s April 17 meeting, the provision that
allows for a licensee who violates the terms of a sidewalk restaurant rider to
be disqualified from obtaining another sidewalk restaurant rider for 5 years,
has been moderated, so that the disqualification is permissive rather than
mandatory, and the disqualification period is up to 5 years.
      §   There has been no change to the hours of operation or to the
requirement to delineate the licensed area using planters or fencing.
      §   Constant supervision of the licensed area by a person old enough to
serve alcohol is still required.
      §   A new provision has been added, to authorize the Local Liquor Control
Commissioner (the Village President) to impose additional conditions before
issuing a sidewalk restaurant license.
She noted that editorial changes had also been made to eliminate some of the
more outdated phraseology, and that the revised draft provides for a single
sidewalk restaurant rider to be issued, so that a separate ordinance will not
have to be prepared, introduced and adopted when Espresso Pacifico’s license is
ready to be issued.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to amend the ordinance as
outlined. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
i)   Resolution No. R-22-2001 Setting Fee for Sidewalk Liquor Service License -
Adoption. Ms. Janega explained that the proposed resolution amends the existing
fee resolution by adding a charge for the sidewalk restaurant rider liquor
license. The fee suggested is $150, which is intended to be modest but allows
the Village to recover the costs generally related to the application of the
new license fee. She noted that the draft resolution also corrects the fee
references for film production permits and special event permits, with the
exact amount of each varying depending on the nature and extent of Village
services needed.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
setting the fees as suggested. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Request for Sidewalk Liquor License - Espresso Pacifico. Manager Williams
briefly reviewed the request from Espresso Pacifico for a sidewalk restaurant
rider liquor license. He noted that as requested at the last meeting, Espresso
Pacifico had provided a letter stating its intent to comply with all of the
provisions of the new ordinance and its Certificate of Insurance, accompanied
by a drawing of its proposed table size and layout, so that the Council could
approve her application concurrently with the adoption of MC-3-2001.
Mr. Williams highlighted three policy issues for Council's consideration, as
follows: (1) The ordinance requires that a 5' pedestrian clearance be
maintained at all times. He pointed out that in this location there are certain
spots where this might be difficult, especially around the tree grate. He
stated, however, that he did not expect this to be a major problem. (2) The
ordinance requires full time supervision, which is challenging given that the
café spans portions of both Elm and Lincoln. He noted that no maitre d' stand
had been placed on the proposed layout. (3) The ordinance requires clear
demarcation, or barriers, between the café area and the pedestrian walkway. As
drawn, Espresso Pacifico's proposal does not provide for any obvious barrier
elements. He suggested that the planters along Lincoln be moved from their
proposed, staggered locations to be equidistant to the east from the building
to establish clearer delineation between the walkway and the café.
President Duhl asked the applicant if there had been any problems in the past.
Ms. Jacobson responded that the only problems occurred when bicycles are parked
along the walkway.
Manager Williams noted that the Village was looking into different options to
accommodate bicycles.
Trustee Woodbury voiced some continued concern about the lack of a clear line
of demarcation and intermingling pedestrian passage with the service of liquor.
Trustee Powell commented that a fence of any type would make it impossible to
serve the tables. Trustee Woodbury responded that there might be fewer tables,
set parallel to the windows which would allow for more barrier elements.
President Duhl noted some reservations about the planters proposed around the
corner round table posing an impediment to pedestrians.
Trustee Brower expressed some reservation about serving alcohol on a sidewalk
where children pass by on a regular basis. He also said that he believed the
proposal to be aggressive use of the sidewalk, but indicated a willingness to
pass on it on a trial basis.
Trustee Presser suggested granting the permit subject to staff's satisfaction
with the set up, granting discretion to the Liquor Commissioner and the Village
Manager to require changes in lay out as deemed appropriate.
Attorney Janega noted that the Council has already created one license in this
category, and could by motion say what conditions it thinks should be imposed.
Trustee Aquilino asked about the location of the maitre d' stand. The applicant
responded that an appropriate staff member will always be present outside, but
that she would prefer not to have the maitre d' stand be a requirement.
After further discussion, Trustee Presser moved to authorize the issuance of
the Sidewalk Liquor Permit under the condition that a 5' walkway be maintained,
including a 5' arc around the tree, and all of the planters on Lincoln be moved
equidistant from the building to provide for a better line of demarcation,
leaving the ones around the corner table as proposed for the time being. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and
Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ms. Berish inquired as to why only the service of beer and wine was authorized.
President Duhl responded that that was all that was requested.
b)   Public Hearing - Preliminary Design Guidelines Report. Manager Williams
described the nature of the Public Hearing. He explained that the Notice of
Public Hearing that was published had been broadly phrased to permit
consideration of zoning issues if the Council decided to do so. However, he
reported that, after review of the preliminary report, it is still difficult to
envision where all of the zoning changes might be and what unintended
consequences might ensue. Therefore, he stated that it is staff's
recommendation that the Council hold off on any zoning amendments until after
the Design Review Board has had an opportunity to work with the drafted
Guidelines for a while and to refine them from experience.
President Duhl opened the Public Hearing, and asked Trustees Woodbury and
Greenough for any comments that they might have.
Both trustees expressed thanks to the two consulting groups for their efforts
and to all of the residents who also participated in the process. Trustee
Woodbury said that the draft Guidelines represent a major step forward and that
the process afforded the ad hoc committee an opportunity to clarify all of the
things they perceived to be missing from the design review process. He said
that, ultimately, the Guidelines, in conjunction with the Comprehensive Plan
and the Zoning Ordinance, will result in designs that are more in keeping with
what the Village, as a whole, desires.
The consultants, Joanne Bauer and Scott Freres, each made brief opening
remarks.
There being no further comments from the floor, President Duhl thanked Ms.
Bauer and Mr. Freres and closed the Public Hearing.
Attorney Janega then explained the Council's two policy options: (1) to
formally adopt the Guidelines itself, or (2) to refer the Guidelines to the
Design Review Board (DRB) so the DRB could adopt them, apply them, and then
report back to the Council after a few months. Ms. Janega stated that the
Council needed to carefully consider what kind of force it wanted to give to
the Guidelines and recommended refraining from formally adopting them at this
time, which could give them the force of law and might create unanticipated
conflicts with current Village Code provisions. She added that the design
review provisions of the Village Code contemplate that the DRB will adopt
design guidelines. She, therefore, suggested that the Council pass the draft
Guidelines on to the Design Review Board to implement and apply for a period of
time. This would provide an opportunity for the Board to fine-tune the
Guidelines as they work with them, and the DRB’s report would assist the
Council in determining what, if any, Code provisions should be changed. She
explained that this would give the Council the most flexibility. She also
mentioned that referring the Guidelines to the DRB would also provide an
opportunity to consider the Guidelines within the context of the Comprehensive
Plan.
Trustee Woodbury concurred with Ms. Janega's suggestion that the Guidelines be
passed on to the Design Review Board for a trial period. He noted that the
Guidelines in and of themselves are not a prescription for good design and that
a design must be considered in its totality. He added that the DRB needs the
freedom to approve a good design when it is presented. He said that having
their experience and advice after they’ve worked with the Guidelines will help
to make the final document work in the best interests of the Village.
After further discussion, the Council agreed to send the Guidelines to the
Design Review Board for a six month trial period.
c)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1219 and 1220. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1219 in the amount of $884,416.62 and Warrant
List No. 1220 in the amount of $329,105.28. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Woodley Road Police Contract. Manager Williams explained that since 1988,
the Village has been providing basic police services to the properties located
in unincorporated Cook County that make up the Woodley Road Association. He
reported that the most recent three-year contract expired on March 31, 2001,
and a new contract has been executed for the term April 1, 2001 through March
31, 2003. He noted that no action by Council was required.
e)   Residential Gross Floor Area Study. Manager Williams reviewed a proposal
from Trkla, Pettigrew, Allen & Payne, Inc. to conduct a residential gross Floor
Area Study for the Village. The focus of the study would be twofold: (1) to
analyze gross floor area and floor area rations, particularly in larger lots,
while also considering other factors that contribute to the overall "visual
bulk" of a home, especially building height, and (2) compare and contrast what
Winnetka is doing in relation to its neighbors and comparable communities. He
explained that this would be an informational study only and that as with the
GFA changes for smaller lots, there would be numerous public hearings before
any Zoning Ordinance changes were made.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to sign the proposed Professional Services Agreement in an amount not-
to-exceed $11,500. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid No. 01-007: Electric Plant Pond Dredging. Manager Williams reviewed
the bids to provide labor, materials and equipment required to mechanically
dredge the intake and cooling ponds at the Electric Plant. He said that staff's
recommendation is to award the bid to Professional Diving Services, Inc.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Government Finance Officers
Association has notified the Village that our comprehensive annual financial
report for the fiscal year ended March 31, 2000 qualifies for a Certificate of
Achievement for Excellence in Financial Reporting. The Certificate of
Achievement is the highest form of recognition in governmental accounting and
financial reporting.
Mr. Williams also reported that FEMA has reimbursed $23,500 for expenses
incurred as a result of the major snow storm in December.
b)   Trustees.
Trustee Powell reported on his recent visit to Springfield and alerted the
Council to the fact that numerous pending bills seek to usurp the power of
local governments.
Trustee Aquilino reported that the Beautification Committee's project at Indian
Hill is nearly finished and that work would begin shortly on the Committee's
next project, the Green Bay corridor, particularly the section north of Spruce
Street where the guardrail is.
Trustee Presser reported that the Code Committee would begin meeting again,
starting on May 22nd.
c)   President. President Duhl advised the Trustees that the Village Caucus
Platform Committee Meeting would be held at the Library on Monday evening at 7:
30, and encourage any who wanted to attend to do so.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:46 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
The meeting adjourned at 9:47 p.m.
