                  Deputy Clerk
May 8, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Adopted June 5, 2001)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Bryan
McInturff, and six persons in the audience.
2. Plan Commission Report on Building Demolition Process.
President Duhl invited Gwen Trindl, Chairman of the Plan Commission, to review
the Report that was prepared in response to concerns expressed by the Building
Review Committee about the increasing number of demolition applications and the
ineffectiveness of the 90-day demolition delay period as a tool for finding
alternatives to demolition. (The Report is attached hereto as Attachment #1.)
Ms. Trindl said that the Committee had focused on ways to (1) manage the
rapidly increasing number of permit applications, (2) support the original
purpose of the Demolition Ordinance by providing the Building Review Committee
with adequate tools to try to affect the demand for teardowns, and (3) adjust
the speed of demolitions to allow sufficient time to research, and therefore,
understand the impact of proposed demolitions.
After reviewing the Report, Ms. Trindl summarized the Committee’s
recommendations saying that the proposed revisions were aimed at accomplishing
two things:
a)   Modifying the BRC procedures to improve management of its workload.
b)   Modifying the permit procedure to provide more information about
structures to be demolished early in the process and to allow more time for
discussion with the applicant of alternatives to demolition.
Ms. Trindl cited the solicitation of significantly more information in the
initial demolition application as the most noteworthy recommended change in the
process. In addition to which, in certain instances, an Historical and
Architectural Impact Study (HAIS) might be required.
She emphasized that the suggested revisions fully recognize the rights of an
applicant to proceed with a demolition, providing the requirements are
completed, but serve to make the role of the Building Review Committee more
effective than is now the case.
Ms. Trindl complimented all of the individuals who helped to prepare the Report
on their efforts and thanked them for their time.
President Duhl then asked Attorney Janega to comment on the Report. Ms. Janega
explained that what the Committee is recommending is a bifurcated process that
would allow the Village staff to focus on public safety, convenience, and
welfare issues, while vesting all determinations of the architectural/
historical value of a building in a public body rather than in Village staff.
The two-step process would allow the first meeting to be a triage meeting,
bringing all of the issues to the attention of the property owner. Ms. Janega
pointed out that every demolition application has two potential impacts, the
first being the loss of architecturally or historically significant structures,
the second being the noise, dirt, and traffic surrounding a demolition project,
which can disrupt a neighborhood and interfere with public works that might be
going on in the area.
She noted that in most instances, people requesting demolition permits would
probably get them in approximately the same amount of time that they do now.
However, in instances when a full HAIS is required, the timing of the
processing could take 4-6 months, but the timing would be largely under the
control of the owner.
Ms. Janega also commented on possible changes in the Code that would allow for
a more consistent, regular schedule for Building Review Committee meetings. As
it is now, the timeframe for the demolition review process needs to be adjusted
to alleviate the frequent meeting schedule and the overwhelming workload.
Ms. Trindl indicated that the Committee expects that between 5-10% of the
applications would require extensive study, but said that that number could
change given the proposed intensive initial application.
Becky Hurley, another member of the Plan Commission, pointed out that the
object of the proposed revisions is to slow down the process to afford the
Village, the buyer, the seller, and the community time to entertain other
options to tearing down significant buildings.
Trustee Aquilino inquired whether the Committee had discussed placing a sign in
front of properties for which a demolition permit application has been filed.
Ms. Hurley said that it had, but decided not to make that recommendation
because it might be perceived as punitive. Ms. Janega added that to do so would
require a Code amendment and suggested that procedures be kept consistent with
what is done in other zoning matters.
The Trustees discussed the processing and evaluation of a full HAIS as well as
associated costs associated therewith and possible alternatives.
Trustee Aquilino remarked that the process by its very nature continues to be
an adversarial one.
Ms. Janega concurred, saying that there will never be a way to dissuade someone
from demolishing a house if they are determined to do so, but that the revised
process, although time consuming, will provide a last opportunity to see if
something can be done to preserve the structure.
Trustee Presser voiced his satisfaction with the recommendations, saying that
they do the best they can while recognizing that any house can still be
demolished.
President Duhl thanked Ms. Trindl and the rest of the Committee.
3. Request from Corner Cooks.
President Duhl invited Betsy Simson, owner of Corner Cooks, to explain her
request. She explained that she would like to use the Chestnut Court courtyard
for grilling classes a couple of times over the summer months. She also
indicated that she wished to expand her operation by beginning to serve dinner,
accompanied by alcoholic beverages, on Friday nights, on a reservation only
basis, for a prix fixe menu.
President Duhl suggested that the Council consider the two issues separately.
After a brief discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees to direct
the Village Attorney to draft language that would create a separate liquor
license category that would allow Ms. Simson to implement her plan to serve
dinners on Friday nights.
With regard to the use of Chestnut Court, Manager Williams cited three policy
concerns: (1) it would expand the scope of what has been considered commercial
use by allowing public property to be used for solely commercial activity other
than as a sidewalk café; (2) it would permit commercial activity on property
that is not adjacent to the business in question; and (3) other entrepreneurs
might request similar use of the mall.
Trustee Powell arrived at 8:30 p.m.
Manager Williams and Trustee Greenough suggested a couple of alternative spaces
that might serve the purpose.
President Duhl commented that in his experience, the Council has consistently
refused requests from merchants to use public property for entrepreneurial
purposes. He expressed his belief that with the exception of sidewalk cafés
that create an ambiance through the Village as a whole, and are, therefore, of
benefit to all, use of public property for individual commercial enterprises
should be avoided. The Trustees agreed.
Trustee Presser suggested that Ms. Simson pursue some kind of similar activity
in conjunction with the Chamber of Commerce’s Sidewalk Sale.
President Duhl mentioned that the Chamber was also planning a fall event and
that the Council’s position might be different in the context of a Village-wide
activity.
4. C-2 Overlay Districts: Continuing Legal Nonconformities.
Attorney Janega explained that the Overlay District creates two areas in the
Village in which commercial uses at street level are reserved strictly for
retail uses. The C-2 Overlay Districts were established to encourage and
preserve retail uses in an effort to prevent service industries from taking
over the retail tax base of the Village, as many of those businesses do not
generate sales tax. At present, such businesses are allowed only by Special Use
Permit, although some currently in existence were permitted uses that
automatically became lawful special uses when the Overlay District was
established.
Currently, for the ground floor special uses in the Overlay Districts to
expire, the use for which the Special Use Permit was issued must cease for at
least six consecutive months. Therefore, because the Zoning Ordinance refers to
the specified “use” rather than to the specified “user,” it is possible for one
tenant with a non-retail use to be replaced by another tenant for substantially
the same use. As a result there has been little opportunity to reduce the
number of non-retail uses in the Overlay Districts.
Ms. Janega then reviewed steps that could be taken to encourage the replacement
of the non-retail special uses with the preferred, permitted uses. These
include:
a)   Amend the Zoning Ordinance to require that all special uses granted in the
Overlay Districts be limited to the use of the premises by the proposed
occupant and that the special use expires when the occupant vacates the
premises.
b)   Amend the Zoning Ordinance to limit the length of time that a special use
in the Overlay Districts can exist as a permitted special use if the special
use is created by virtue of an amendment to the permitted uses in the Zoning
Ordinance.
c)   Amend the Zoning Ordinance so that the uses that are now special uses in
the Overlay Districts become legal nonconformities that must be amortized over
a certain period of time and/or that may not continue from one user to another.
d)   Amend the standards for the granting of special uses in the C-2 Overlay
Districts to require evidence of a good faith effort to lease the space in
question for retail purposes.
The Trustees discussed these and other options. Afterwards, President Duhl
asked if there was any disagreement that the special use ought to run with the
user, instead of with the property as a whole. There was no disagreement. The
Trustees also agreed that while successor tenants should not be permitted,
entities considered legal successor entities should be. There was a consensus
to eliminate the six month window and amend the Village Code to provide that
there be no special uses permitted in the Overlay Districts.
Ms. Janega pointed out that these changes would require a Public Hearing, and
Manager Williams recommended proceeding slowly so that all residents and
merchants have an opportunity to make their positions known.
5. Clarification of Bid No. 01-007: Electric Plant Pond Dredging.
Manager Williams clarified the bid information distributed at the last Council
meeting. He confirmed that although the bid had not been clearly presented, it
had been correctly awarded.
6. Change Orders: Power Plant Roof.
Mr. McInturff reviewed the bid process and explained the need to request an
additional $11,119 for the removal and installation of roof ventilators,
replacement of roof decking, repair of the turbine room roof, and the purchase
of excess tiles.
July 3rd Council Meeting: The Council discussed alternatives to the regularly
scheduled meeting date of July 3rd and decided to leave meeting date as is.
7. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:32 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Study Session at 10:06 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney
Janega.
8. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:07 p.m.
May 15, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Adopted June 5, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
15, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega and seven persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   June 5, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend. 
b)   June 12, 2001, Study Session. President Duhl noted that the Village Caucus
had scheduled its meeting for this evening and suggested that an alternative
date be designated for the Study Session. After discussing the options, the
Trustees agreed to meet on Thursday, June 14th.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 1, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
Sheila Geraghty Dolkart, 1524 Asbury, expressed continued concern about flood
control and drainage problems in the Asbury/Edgewood Ln. area, in particular a
drain that backs up in her back yard. She said that she and her neighbors were
worried that the upcoming work on the Tower Road project would not correct the
problems and asked that the alternatives be explored. Manager Williams offered
to arrange a meeting between Ms. Dolcart and the Village Engineer, Steven
Saunders.
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln., objected to the rescheduling of the June
Study Session in view of the Council’s recent decision not to reschedule its
meetings when there is a general election. Then, Ms. Berish read a letter
detailing her efforts to get benches and trash cans located on the platforms at
the Village’s train stations. She expressed her opinion that the Village ought
to use some of the money derived from commuter parking passes to fund these
items.
President Duhl thanked Ms. Berish for her efforts and suggested that she give
the information she had gathered to Trustee Aquilino, Chairman of the
Beautification Committee, and work in conjunction with that committee.
Trustee Aquilino mentioned that the Beautification Committee was in the process
of putting together a list of items, such as benches and trash cans, that might
be donated by residents for placement throughout the Village.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. M-13-2001 - Alley Vacation: 329 Fairview - Adoption. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance authorizing
the vacation of the alley right-of-way at 329 Fairview and accepting the Plat
of Consolidation. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Powell, Aquilino, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Presser.
b)   Resolution No. R-23-2001 - Approving IAFF Agreement - Adoption. Manager
Williams noted the conclusion of the Village’s negotiations with the
firefighters’ union and their recent ratification of the first collective
bargaining agreement. On behalf of the negotiating team, he recommended
approval of the contract.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
approving the Agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser
c)   Ordinance No. M-14-2001 - Special Use Permit: Elder Lane Park -
Introduction. Ms. Janega reviewed the Park District's application for a Special
Use Permit for park and playground renovations and beach house replacement at
Elder Lane Park. The Park District is proposing to remove and replace existing
play equipment, picnic tables, benches, patios, walkways, stairways, fencing,
lighting and landscaping. They are also proposing the demolition and
reconstruction of the existing beach house and provision of a disabled parking
space adjacent to the beach house. Ms. Janega called attention to the fact
that, although there are practice fields and sports areas depicted in the
proposal as potential uses, the drafted ordinance does not include permission
for those uses at these locations.
Pointing out that no zoning variations are required for the proposed
improvements, Ms. Janega reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to
recommend approval of the Special Use Permit, with some recommendations
regarding lighting and fencing. She also reported that Design Review Board had
issued generally favorable comment on the proposed improvements but voiced some
concern over the proposed materials and metal roof of the beach house and
suggested that its architectural style be reviewed further. In addition she
said that the Plan Commission found the proposed Special Use Permit to be
consistent with Winnetka 2020 Comprehensive Plan, but also had recommended that
particular attention be paid to the quality of building materials.
With regard to the metal roof, Liza McElroy, Director of the Park District,
explained that the maintenance of the metal roofs is much easier and less
costly than other alternatives and asked for approval of this feature.
Trustee Brower spoke in support of the metal roof, saying that in this instance
practicality needed to trump architectural correctness.
Trustee Powell inquired about the exterior options.
Ms. McElroy responded that the Park District would like to use all stone
construction, but can’t settle on any option until it sees the price
differences, as economy will dictate the choice.
Trustee Woodbury voiced some concern about the proposed light fixtures. He
suggested that the Park District employ the type identified in the interim
Design Guidelines, or perhaps, use some kind of low, walkway light instead.
After discussing procedural issues, Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee
Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance granting the Special Use Permit to
the Park District, but providing for the final design to be brought back before
the Design Review Board for final recommendation before returning to the
Council for final approval. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2001 - Creating New E-1 Liquor License Category -
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the Liquor
Ordinance establishing a Class E-1 license, as discussed by the Council at its
May 8th Study Session in response to a request from Corner Cooks. She explained
that the new category would permit Class E licensees to serve liquor in
conjunction with full service meals, on a reservation-only basis, for a prix
fixe, single-offering, single-seating meal. She then reviewed the key features
of the proposed new category, as follows:
      ·   The license is limited to the sale of only beer and wine for
consumption on the premises. She noted that although the applicant, Corner
Cooks, had indicated that it would like to have full service liquor service,
its Class E license allows only the sale of wine. However, she pointed out that
a full service bar may be more consistent with a full service restaurant than
with meal.
      ·   As with all liquor licenses, the sale of beer or wine must be
incidental to the sale and service of a complete meal. Pursuant to the
discussions at the Study Session, the meal service is for a fixed menu, prix
fixe offering.
      ·   The days for service are limited to Friday and Saturday nights
between the hours of 6:00 p.m. and 11:00 p.m. This makes the closing time
consistent with restaurant service and limits the time to dinner hours.
Although only a single seating on Friday night was requested, the draft
includes both Friday and Saturday allows two seatings on both nights, to afford
the licensee more flexibility.
      ·   Unlike any other liquor license classification, the E-1 license would
allow service of both food and drink at a counter. However, because patrons
must reserve a seat for a specific meal serving, and because the number of
patrons is specifically limited to the number of seats at the tables and
counter, the counter service becomes part of the meal service itself, thereby
reducing the likelihood that patrons would walk in off the street for cocktails
only.
      ·   The Local Liquor Control Commissioner (the Village President) is
authorized to impose additional conditions before issuing a sidewalk restaurant
license.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1221 and 1222. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list,
Trustee Aquilino moved to approve Warrant List No. 1221 in the amount of $
594,690.35 and Warrant List No 1222 in the amount of $433,553.81. Trustee
Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
b)   Annual Requests for Sidewalk Cafes. Manager Williams commented that the
Village had received applications from seven local restaurants for sidewalk
café permits, noting that staff recommended that these permits be issued for
the period May 1 through November 30, to coincide with the dates approved for
the Sidewalk Café Liquor License. He indicated that regular cleanup of the café
area by the operator would be added as a condition of maintaining the permit.
Mr. Williams pointed out that Song O'Sixpence had requested an expansion of its
sidewalk café area to incorporate the enlarged parkway area at Elm and Arbor
Vitae. He enumerated staff’s concerns about this request, as follows:
i)   A relatively dangerous location: Locating a café on a bump-out raises a
serious public safety concern for its patrons. Current sidewalk cafes, which
are located adjacent to a building, are protected from passing vehicles by a
row of parked cars. A bump-out café would be immediately adjacent to the travel
lane and would not be protected by parked cars.
ii)   Reduced Sight Distance: One purpose of this bump-out is to improve sight
distance for motorists and pedestrians at this intersection. If tables and
chairs are placed on this bump-out, the sight distance will be diminished.
iii)   New Precedent: All current sidewalk cafés are required to be adjacent to
their restaurants. The bump-out area in question is not adjacent to Song
O’Sixpence. If this request is allowed, other restaurants could make a similar
request to expand their cafés into areas that would not be adjacent to their
business. Recently, the Council denied a similar request where a business
wanted to expand into a non-adjacent public area.
Trustee Powell expressed concern about the safety of the proposal and said that
even if the Village charged rent for the use of the property, it would
establish an undesirable precedent.
President Duhl concurred saying that he saw no difference in substance between
this request for off-site tables and a recent request from Corner Cooks to use
the Chestnut Court mall for purposes of promoting business.
After a brief discussion of possible alternatives for Song O’Sixpence,
President Duhl noted the Council’s consensus to deny the request to expand its
sidewalk café.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the sidewalk café
applications. By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Request for Sidewalk Liquor License - J.P. McCarthy's. Manager Williams
reviewed the request from J. P. McCarthy's for a Sidewalk Café Liquor License.
He noted that if Council approved of the request, the addition of a second
holder of this rider would be added to the amendments to MC-4-2001 and be up
for adoption at the next meeting.
Mrs. Pomerantz explained the reason for the request. She said that it would
provide for additional, necessary seating to replace tables removed from the
top tier of the seating area to make a play area for children.
Trustee Woodbury commented that as proposed, the café space does not conform
with the recently enacted ordinance in that there is no separation of
pedestrian space from the café space.
Phil Hoza, a neighboring merchant spoke in support of the application.
President Duhl asked if there the sidewalk was of sufficient size. Manager
Williams responded that it was.
Noting a consensus among the Trustees to permit the sidewalk liquor service,
President Duhl asked the applicant to amend its proposal to provide for
appropriate separation of the pedestrian from the café space. He also directed
the Village Attorney to add this license rider to the amendment to MC-4-2001 up
for adoption at the next meeting.
d)   Elm Street Bridge Planters. Manager Williams reported that the Winnetka
Rotary Club has requested permission to place decorative planters at the four
corners of the Elm Street Bridge and reviewed comments with regard thereto made
by the Director of Public Works.
There was unanimous consensus among the Trustees to permit the placement of the
planters on the bridge in accordance with Mr. Saunders’s recommendations.
e)   Bid No. 01-002: Tower Rd. Rehabilitation - Phase I. Manager Williams
reviewed the bids for Phase I of the Tower Road Rehabilitation and Water Main
Replacement. He recommended awarding the bid to Lampignano & Son for
$501,731.60.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
f)   Change Order: Power Plant Roof. Manager Williams explained the need, as
reviewed at the May Study Session, to add $11,119 to the cost for the removal
and installation of roof ventilators, replacement of roof decking, repair of
the turbine room roof, and the purchase of excess tiles for the Power Plant.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village had been experiencing
some shaky internet service lately, but said that he expected that all of the
problems to be resolved by the end of the month when the installation of new
DSL service will be completed.
He also reported that 346 tons of materials had been collected during Spring
Clean Up Week, as compared to 335 tons last year.
Finally, Mr. Williams noted that the Police Department reported that thus far
there have been no violations, either through directed patrol or standard
patrol techniques, of the truck ban since it was enacted.
b)   Trustees.
Trustee Woodbury reported on a recent meeting of the Historical Society Board
at which the Board had been preparing to request a Special Use Permit for its
new home at 411 Linden. He noted that they would be embarking on an extensive
capital program to raise funds from the community.
Trustee Aquilino reported that she and Trustee Brower had attended the Chamber
of Commerce at which they briefly discussed the overlay district. She said that
the issue of loitering by school age children in the business district,
especially by Lakeside and The Sweet Shop, had been raised by several members,
who were looking to the Village for help in controlling the problem.
Manager Williams said that the best tool to control the problem was education,
as writing a loitering ordinance is very difficult constitutionally. He also
indicated that the Village would be looking into the possibility of placing
bike racks in strategic locations.
Trustee Brower mentioned that the Chamber was planning a three week event in
the fall to promote the merchants and the business district.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the reappointment of Charlie Hoffman to the Building
Review Committee for a full term from the date of the expiration of his
previous term. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, so moved. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl suggested the reappointment of Robert Vanden Bosch to the Zoning
Board of Appeals for a full term from the date of the expiration of his
previous term. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, so moved. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl reported that he had asked Irv Footlick to serve on the
Beautification Committee.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
purchase of property pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section
2(c)(5), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Presser. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:30 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Woodbury. Absent:
Trustee Presser. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nayes: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
The meeting adjourned at 9:31 p.m.
