                  Deputy Clerk
June 5, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved June 19, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
5, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Stephen
Presser. Absent: Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams and Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and 21 persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that he wished to move discussion of Items 7(c) and 9
(d) up on the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   June 14, 2001, Study Session. President Duhl reminded the Trustees that
this meeting had been scheduled for Thursday, June 14th in order not to
conflict with the Village Caucus meeting on Tuesday.
b)   July 19, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend. 
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 8, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Powell. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   May 15, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
None
7. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Resolution No. R-25-2001 – Commending Mary Grace Ford – Adoption. Fire
Chief Colpaert reviewed the circumstances of an incident April 4, 2001, in
which Mrs. Ford’s efforts to revive a fallen bicyclist resulted in saving the
individual’s life. Chief Colpaert introduced Mrs. Ford, Mr. Bornstein, the
victim, and Marian Henderson from the American Heart Association who presented
Mrs. Ford with a Certificate of Recognition for her lifesaving actions.
President Duhl then read the Village’s resolution commending Mrs. Ford and
called for a motion to adopt. Trustee Presser so moved. The motion was seconded
by Trustee Aquilino. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Saying that this was one of the moments that makes being on the dais a humbling
and momentous experience, President Duhl presented Mrs. Ford with an official
copy of the Resolution.
Mrs. Ford thanked the Village and recognized the efforts and personal concern
shown by Fire Department personnel.
b)   Ordinance No. M-14-2001 - Special Use Permit: Elder Lane Park - Adoption.
Mr. Williams reviewed the changes made to the draft ordinance in response to
issues raised at introduction on May 15, 2001. These included the addition of a
prohibition of programmed activities on ball fields depicted on the site plan
and language requiring Council approval of the final building materials and
landscape plan as conditions of the Special Use Permit
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:40 p.m.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to amend the ordinance as
indicated. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2001 – Flood Hazard Protection Ordinance Revisions –
Introduction. Mr. Saunders reviewed three policy options regarding proposed
revisions to the Flood Hazard Protection Ordinance primarily resulting from
recent policy revisions adopted by the Federal Emergency Management Agency
(FEMA) and the Department of Natural Resources/Water Resources (IDNR/OWR). He
explained that although some of the changes were merely housekeeping in nature,
three substantive ones, enumerated hereafter, required Council’s direction.
i)   The first revision regards repetitive loss within the 100-year flood
plain. If the Village wishes to make it so that a resident owning a structure
that sustains repetitive loss will be eligible to make an Increased Cost of
Compliance claim, the Village must adopt language establishing requirements for
the protection of such a structure. He emphasized that this is strictly a
Village option. The downside of including the language is that if a structure
sustains a repetitive loss, the Village would be required to force the owner to
bring the building into compliance with the new flood plain ordinance, which
could result in repair costs exceeding the amount of additional insurance
coverage to be made available.
ii)   The second revision has to do with the construction of new residential
structures with basements on sites in the flood plain that have obtained
appropriate authorization from FEMA under certain conditions. Mr. Saunders
pointed out that there might be an instance when the requisite conditions
cannot be met for a particular site.
iii)   The remaining change to the Ordinance pertains to Village policy on the
interpretation of the current Ordinance, in particular language having to do
with allowing non-substantial improvements on existing structures. Heretofore,
it has been Staff’s interpretation to require that non-substantial improvements
adhere to the building protection clause of the Ordinance by requiring that the
lowest level of an addition be constructed 12” above the base flood elevation,
as recommended by FEMA. However, Staff has permitted variance to this by
allowing a proposed addition to match the finished floor elevation of an
existing structure if that elevation is above the base flood elevation.
With regard to the first revision, President Duhl clarified that the Increased
Cost of Compliance claim would only be available to a resident if the Village
requires that they meet tougher standards. Should the Village adopt such a
provision, if there is a repetitive loss, the owner would be forced to improve
the structure to meet all flood plain requirements regardless of whether they
choose to make a claim or not. He confirmed with Mr. Saunders that FEMA allows
the Village to remain silent on the issue.
Trustee Brower inquired whether the current ordinance has such a provision. Mr.
Saunders said that it does not.
There was a consensus among the Trustees not to include this provision in the
ordinance.
With regard to the second revision, Mr. Saunders reviewed the process by which
a builder could legally place fill on a lot located in the flood plain, raising
the building pad out of the floodplain, in order to build a house with a
basement.
Trustee Powell inquired whether this was in contradiction to the current
drainage ordinance.
Mr. Saunders responded that it is not, but that the current drainage ordinance
regulates the way in which people may place fill on property. He said that the
grade may be changed as long as it is done in a properly engineered manner. He
added that in practice the idea of adding fill to a lot and creating
compensatory storage, in compliance with FEMA’s directives, would not be
feasible for all lots, i.e., small lots and heavily treed lots, but it does
provide a way for a basement to be installed.
Manager Williams pointed out that grading plans would have to be submitted for
all building permits.
Trustee Greenough expressed concern about increased overall building height as
a result of raising the grade. Mr. Saunders pointed out that all construction
still has to meet the building height requirements.
President Duhl mentioned that someone currently can employ fill to raise their
grade, regardless of whether they are in the flood plain or not. He suggested
that should the Council decide to add this provision, the building height
restrictions be reviewed so as to avoid increasing the adverse impact of some
new construction.
Trustee Aquilino confirmed with Mr. Saunders that if the Council does not adopt
this revision, then no basements are permitted in the flood plain.
Tanya Dietrich, 824 Boal Parkway and Ray Teborek, 804 Boal Parkway voiced
concern about the proposed revision citing the impact that a change in grade
might have on neighboring properties, particularly with regard to stormwater
runoff.
Bud Van Arsdale, 1137 Cherry, spoke in favor of the revision.
Penelope Berlet, 1093 Ash, added comments about the effect of being in the
flood plain.
All urged the Village to pursue do everything within its power to remove
properties from FEMA’s flood plain boundaries. Manager Williams expressed his
view that FEMA’s flood plain regulations do not work well in Winnetka
Trustee Brower voiced support for this revision as in his opinion, it benefits
both the homeowners and architects.
Trustee Woodbury noted that the real issue is providing sufficient compensatory
storage. He mentioned that building height could be redefined.
During a discussion of several different drainage options and their
consequences, Trustee Aquilino expressed strong objection to the “moat” effect
employed in some new construction.
Trustee Presser noted that the pro basement position results in a pro moat/
higher house position. President Duhl concurred but pointed out that neither
issue is solely indigenous to the flood plain. He asked for a consensus as to
whether the Trustees wished to permit this provision subject to addressing some
of the attendant issues more generally. It was agreed that the Council did want
to see this revision included.
With regard to the final revision regarding staff interpretation of the
Ordinance, Liz Kendall, 1322 Sunview Ln., urged the Council not to formally
adopt FEMA’s regulations so as to give the Village some flexibility and
discretion in considering non-substantial improvements.
The Trustees generally agreed. President Duhl called for a motion to introduce
the drafted ordinance with the instruction that before adoption it be amended
to omit the first and third suggested revisions and include only the second.
Trustee Powell so moved, seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-15-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1005 Pine Tree Lane –
Introduction. Manager Williams reviewed a request for a front setback variation
to permit construction of a new 2-1/2 story residence to be located 25 feet
from the property line that abuts a public street and thus is subject to a 50-
foot setback. He explained that the property is irregularly shaped and has
street frontage on two streets, each requiring the 50-foot setback. He noted
that although a previous request to construct a 2-1/2 story residence located
within 19.08 feet of the north property line had been denied by the Zoning
Board of Appeals, the Board had unanimously recommended approval of the revised
request citing the unusual size and shape of the buildable area. He also noted
that the ZBA had recommended that a landscape buffer be installed.
Both Trustee Brower and Trustee Powell made comments supporting the variation.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2001 - Creating New A-2 Liquor License Category -
Adoption. Mr. Williams reviewed the changes to the draft ordinance as
introduced on May 15, 2001. He explained that as originally introduced, the
ordinance would amend the Village Code by establishing a Class E-1 license,
which would be available only to persons holding a Class E, limited food
products, liquor license. However, since introduction, staff has obtained a
more detailed operational description from the proprietor, and based upon that
information, it appears that the proposed operation will be more akin to that
of a restaurant. Consequently, the draft ordinance has been revised to create a
different liquor classification, which is defined as a “specialty restaurant”
and has been given an A-2 classification. The basic characteristics of the new
license have not changed significantly since introduction, except that the new
classification would allow a full service bar. The prix fixe, limited seating,
fixed menu concepts have been retained, as have the limitations on the hours of
service, but the days of service are no longer restricted to Friday and
Saturday.
He noted that the creation of the new license category required amending the
Liquor Ordinance to define “specialty restaurant,” with one of its
distinguishing features being that meals are allowed to be served at a counter.
The proposed license fee for this new category would be $500, as set forth in
Resolution R-24-2001, immediately following.
Mr. Williams also mentioned that a few other technical amendments had been made
to the draft ordinance to clean up language, and reorganize and rephrase
provisions to make them easier to follow, less redundant, and more consistent
with the structure of the new Village Code.
Finally, Manager Williams referred to changes to the draft ordinance to provide
for the addition of Sidewalk Liquor License Riders for J.P. McCarthy’s and
Corner Cooks.
Trustee Powell expressed some concern about the width of the sidewalk in front
of Corner Cooks. Trustee Aquilino indicated that it was the same as by Espresso
Pacifico, and President Duhl pointed out that regardless of the width of the
sidewalk, the ordinance requires that a five foot pedestrian passage be
provided.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to amend the draft ordinance
to create the A-2 category and add Corner Cooks and J.P. McCarthy’s as new
sidewalk liquor licensees.. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-4-
2001, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-24-2001 – Establishing Fee for New Liquor Category.- Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Resolution R-24-
2001, establishing the fee for the A-2 liquor license category at $500.00. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-26-2001 – Establishing Department Head Salaries –
Adoption. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the
resolution establishing the salaries for department heads for the current
fiscal year. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
g)   Resolution No. R-27-2001 – Establishing Village Manager’s Salary –
Adoption. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the
resolution establishing the salary for the Village Manager for the current
fiscal year. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
8. Old Business
a)   Request for Sidewalk Liquor License Rider: J.P. McCarthy’s. This item was
discussed and acted upon previously in conjunction with Ordinance No. MC-4-
2001.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1223 and 1224. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1223 in the amount of $ 861,338.35 and
Warrant List No 1224 in the amount of $452,809.91. Trustee Woodbury seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Request for Sidewalk Café Permit: Corner Cooks. Manager Williams reviewed
this request for permission to operate a sidewalk café.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the request.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Request for Sidewalk Liquor License Rider – Corner Cooks. This item was
discussed and acted upon previously in conjunction with Ordinance No. MC-4-
2001.
d)   Spruce Street Stormwater Outfall Update. Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed the
history of this issue. He explained that in response to Council’s direction,
Village staff in collaboration with an outside consultant developed a proposed
pier design believed to be consistent with Council’s stated wishes. The
proposed design provides protection for the outfall pipe, minimizes impact upon
the littoral drift, and allows for the area to be used for “recreational public
access.” He said that two construction options had been considered and
circulated to neighboring property owners. The main difference between the two
alternatives being that in the proposed design, the pier commences at the base
of the bluff, while in the other the pier commences roughly 40 to 50 feet from
the bluff. Mr. Saunders reported that staff, the consultant, and all but one of
the neighbors preferred the option having the pier commence at the base of the
bluff, in order to maximize protection and support for the pipe and provide
maximum accessibility to the pier. Finally, Mr. Saunders reported that the
application for the required Army Corps of Engineers construction permit had
been completed and filed so as to permit the completion of construction before
the end of the year.
Warren Guthrie, 575 Sheridan Rd., an adjacent property owner, voiced objection
to the Village’s preferred design, particularly to the railing, as well as to
the promotion of recreational use at the street end. He expressed his opinion
that not only will the Village’s preferred design obstruct his view, but it is
contrary to recreational use because it essentially erects a wall dividing what
little beach there is. He asked the Council to reexamine the project.
Trustee Brower supported Mr. Guthrie’s comments.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to explain the Village’s reasoning for
choosing the longer pier option. Mr. Saunders reported that from an engineering
standpoint, the longer pier is a better option because: (1) it provides more
support for the pipe; (2) should the water level rise, the western edge of the
shorter pier would probably be in the water; and (3) due to the height of the
pier, in order to make it useful in high water an additional stairway that
would susceptible to destruction by the elements would have to be added. He
agreed that the proposed railing was an eyesore added for Code compliance and
indicated that the Village was reviewing options for eliminating it.
William Sick, 565 Sheridan Rd., encouraged the Trustees to reconsider their
decision to construct any pier at all. He reiterated previously expressed
concerns about the promotion of recreational use based on safety and liability
considerations.
Jean Wright, echoed Mr. Sick’s comments urging the Council to eliminate the
pier altogether.
Trustee Greenough asked for an explanation of why the Council decided to focus
on options that supported recreational use. Manager Williams indicated that
Spruce Street was the first of the street end projects to be undertaken because
of the broken stormwater pipe. He explained that during consideration of the
alternatives for repair/replacement of the pipe, the Council had come down on
the side of advocating an open beach area, as has historically been the
Village’s position.
Trustee Powell noted that it had been his understanding that the focus of the
beach use was merely access, not full recreational use, to be in keeping with
the status quo.
Saying that she believed that the concept of beach openness had been well
addressed, Trustee Aquilino voiced support for a pier option.
Trustee Woodbury concurred, but expressed favor for the short pier option, as
did Trustee Presser, despite Mr. Saunders’s comments that it might require
sooner and more expensive repairs.
After further discussion of the pier options, President Duhl noted a consensus
for one of the pier options and asked Mr. Saunders to return with a detailed
cost analysis of the pros and cons of each alternative.
e)   Request from Women’s Club. Manager Williams reviewed a request from the
Women’s Club to hang canvas banners on six Village light poles on Green Bay
Road advertising the Club’s annual Silver Wicket event. He explained that the
banners’ design had been approved in the past by the Design Review Board and
that the Club’s contractor would install and remove the banners.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Bid No. 01-006: Low Profile Truck Chassis I. Manager Williams reviewed the
bids for delivery of one low profile truck chassis to be used by the Water &
Electric Department. He reported staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded
to Northwest Trucks in the total amount of $38,680.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
g)   Bid No. 01-010: Conduit Installation. Manager Williams reviewed the bids
for the installation of conduit by the directional bore method. He reported
staff’s recommendation that the Village Manager be authorized to enter into a
contract with Archon Construction Co. for the performance of such work on an
“as needed” basis for the period of June 1, 2001 to May 31, 2002. He noted that
renewal of the contract would be at the Village’s option.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager.
Manager Williams reported that the Village Code was now available on the web
site.
b)   Trustees.
Trustee Presser reported that the Code Review Committee would begin holding
regular meetings starting this month.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl noted that the Landmark Preservation Commission had proposed
Carter Page as its representative to the Plan Commission and called for a
