                  Deputy Clerk
June 14, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved July 3, 2001)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water and Electric Bryan
McInturff, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Assistant Director of
Public Works Alex Attiah, Acting Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, Assistant Village Planner Ann Klaassen, Keith Groebe of the Building
Review Committee, Gwen Trindl of the Plan Commission and 7 persons in the
audience.
President Duhl announced that the agenda item on Wireless E-911 had been
removed at the request of the Police Chief and the Draft of Revised Demolition
Process would be discussed before the Village Yards Update.
2. Draft of Revised Demolition Process.
Brian Norkus explained that the process to apply for demolition would begin
with a preliminary screening that requests property information compiled by
Village staff for the Building Review Committee (BRC), who would then evaluate
the information and determine whether an HAIS study, done by an architectural
historian with proper credentials, would need to be submitted. He further
explained that once an HAIS study was received, it would be sent to the
Landmark Preservation Committee (LPC) for their evaluation. The LPC would then
report their decision to the BRC as to whether a delay is warranted or further
study is needed. A notice of hearing for public comment would follow and the
BRC would then make a final determination.
Trustee Aquilino asked if a list of recommended architects would be available
to applicants.
Manager Williams said that although it is not a Village practice, he thought
that this particular process warrants that a list of qualified people be made
available to the applicants in order to save time for all of the committees.
President Duhl recommended that the list be offered as strictly voluntary and
that it be made clear that the Village does not sanction only the people on the
list.
Trustee Brower commented that he preferred that the LPC make a recommendation
to the BRC, who would then make the decision. He expressed discomfort that two
commissions would be making decisions on this issue.
Mr. Norkus distributed an analysis of applications that had been received for
demolition permits. He explained the schedule of meetings and deadlines for
submitting applications, and the timeframe allowed for the study to be
completed. He said that the LPC has an alternate meeting date as well as a
regular meeting date and could review studies within 15 days. He estimated that
it would be 30 days from completion of the LPC review to the BRC meeting,
including proper notice.
Mr. Duhl asked Ms. Trindl for the recommendation of the Plan Commission.
Ms. Trindl said that the Plan Commission had not included time for a review by
the LPC, but had recommended that the overall process be no longer than 220
days.
President Duhl suggested that they go through the process step by step and
entertain questions.
After reviewing the proposed application process and the information needed by
the Building Review Committee to evaluate demolition permit applications, the
consensus of the Council was that Village staff would revise the application to
reflect Council’s comments. As revised, the application would have staff
compile the information for the preliminary screening and forward it to the BRC
for review. The Council further recommended that, if it is determined that a
full HAIS study is needed, then the applicant would be directed to have one
prepared and provide copies of the study to the LPC and BRC. The LPC could then
meet to discuss the study, but it would not have a formal advisory role in the
process. Instead, the LP would submit comments or appear at the BRC public
hearing to provide their recommendation. The BRC would then evaluate all
comments and determine whether the study is adequate. If so, it would then
determine if there should be a delay in demolition in order to preserve a
significant property. The Council discussed clarifying the findings the BRC has
to ascertain before making a decision and directed the Village Attorney to
prepare them for discussion at a future meeting. The Council also directed that
one of the outcomes of the BRC be that the HAIS is inadequate, in which case
the BRC could continue the process and require the applicant to return with a
completed HAIS.
President Duhl directed staff to revise the flow charts and application form,
and to develop criteria for findings in order to give the BRC the tools that
would support a decision to delay demolition to save a residence of historic or
significant importance in the Village.
Manager Williams agreed and suggested that another study session would be
necessary to continue the discussion.
3. Village Yards Update.
Steve Saunders summarized the history of the Village Yards project to date and
reported that after the cost estimates were completed by the construction
manager, the cost to build a new building exceeded the $8.5 million approved by
the Council on June 6, 2001. He explained the possible reasons for the increase
in costs and reported that once it became apparent that the budget could not
contain the project as designed, the architects, construction manager and
Village staff had considered other ways to redesign the building, concluding
that a partly new, partly renovated building would meet the needs of all of the
departments and could be done within budget.
After reviewing the options presented by Sente & Rubel and Riley Construction,
the Council agreed that Scheme C, a combination of new construction and
renovation, would be the best alternative for the Village Yards project and
authorized the staff to proceed with detailed design drawings for the
rehabilitation of the Village Yards, for an estimated cost of $8.65 million.
Mr. Saunders then reviewed the schedule for further action needed to begin the
project, noting that a special use permit must be requested and presented to
the appropriate Boards, that the Building Façade Committee must meet so that
the façade of the building could be considered at the Design Review Board’s
July 19 meeting, and that the project would be before the Council at its August
7 meeting.
4. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
sale of Village owned land, and the possible purchase of property pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:55 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Study Session at 10:30 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Williams and Village Attorney
Janega.
5. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
June 19, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved July 3, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
19, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief William Gallagher,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Interim Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, and 44 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 8(a) had been removed from the Agenda
awaiting information from FEMA. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   July 3, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   July 10, 2001, Study Session. President Duhl and Trustee Greenough
indicated that they would not be present. All of the other Trustees indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 5, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Landmark Preservation Awards.
President Duhl introduced Carter Page, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation
Commission, and former Chairman, Nan Greenough.
Mr. Page remarked that this is the eighth year for the awards and that a number
of the awards are for buildings over 100 years old. He explained the different
categories and noted that for the first time awards were being presented to
“things” as well as to houses.
Mr. Page and Trustee Greenough showed slides of the winners, commenting on each
of the projects, and presented the following awards:
For Restoration:
School District 35               Skokie School
Village of Winnetka              Council Chambers Light Fixtures
For Restoration & Rehabilitation
Linda Pedian & Tom Perraud       510 Sheridan Rd.
Sondra & Denis Healy             Roof: 9 Indian Hill
Christ Church                    Show House
For Rehabilitation:
Carole & David Shelby            Barn: 559 Oak St.
Waterworks                       907 Green Bay Rd.
For New Construction
Village of Winnetka              Elm Street Bridge
The Robert Allens.               Fence: 550 Oak St.
Kerry & George Arnold            445 Cedar
Christ Church                    Building Addition
 
Saying that each of the winners had made a significant contribution to the
beauty of the community, President Duhl congratulated all of the winner. He
also thanked Trustee Greenough for inspiring the design of the Elm Street
Bridge.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln., read a letter to the Council from the New
Trier Republican Organization requesting that Ordinance MC-1-2001 be rescinded.
President Duhl reported that the Council had reconsidered the issue and that
the consensus had been to keep the meeting schedule as it is.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2001 – Flood Hazard Protection Ordinance Revisions –
Adoption. This item was removed from the Agenda.
b)   Ordinance No. M-15-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1005 Pine Tree Lane –
Adoption. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the
ordinance permitting a front setback variation to permit construction of a new
2-1/2 story residence. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
9. Old Business
a)   Spruce Street Storm Sewer Outfall Rehabilitation. Mr. Saunders reviewed
budgetary estimates of costs for three design options for the Spruce Street
Pier and Outfall Reconstruction Project that were prepared in response to
Council’s directive at the last Council meeting. He explained that the
estimates for both of the options discussed at the last meeting had exceeded
the amount budgeted for this project, so a third option, removal of the
existing pier and pipe plus construction of an erosion-proof runoff channel,
had been requested. Mr. Saunders reported that each of the three options would
address the necessary technical issues.
President Duhl asked Mr. Williams to summarize the impact of the three options
on the budget. Mr. Williams indicated that, in order to effect either pier
option, the Village would have to consider a supplemental appropriation.
Trustee Brower asked if these were single estimates and if Mr. Saunders had any
expectation that a competitive bidding process would produce significantly
lower costs. Mr. Saunders reported that a well-respected marine contractor had
prepared the estimates and that, while the actual bids might be somewhat lower,
they would not be substantially lower. He explained that Options #1 and #2
employ a different pier design from the original concept in that they rely on
piles that must be driven into the clay layer, which is very costly because the
work must be done from a barge. In addition, those designs call for significant
structural support to hold the pipe out of the water.
President Duhl asked those Trustees who sat on the Council when the decision to
pursue the pier design had been made whether they would view the situation
differently, given the cost.
Trustees Powell and Presser said that they would. Trustee Brower expressed
disappointment, but said the cost could not be ignored. Trustee Woodbury
commented that he had never been in favor of keeping the street end open and
that Option #3 permitted the Village to stay within budget. Trustees Aquilino
and Greenough joined in the consensus to pursue Option #3.
b)   518 Winnetka Avenue. Attorney Janega reviewed the materials found in the
supplemental binders included as a part of Council’s Agenda packets. She then
summarized the various board and Council actions on this issue. She concluded
by pointing out that the subject of the current discussion is limited to the
applicant’s request for a height variation to permit three stories instead of
2-1/2 stories. Ms. Janega emphasized that the variations and Special Use Permit
already granted to the applicant were not affected by the present application
before the Council and were not up for reconsideration. She concluded by
informing the Council that its options were to affirm the Zoning Board of
Appeals’ recommendation or decide to direct the Village Attorney to proceed
with the preparation of an ordinance granting the requested variation.
President Duhl inquired whether the application before the Council this evening
was the same as the one that did not receive a favorable recommendation from
the Zoning Board.
Ms. Janega responded that the applications were the same except that the one
presented to the Zoning Board also requested a 37’ building height in addition
to the three stories. She explained that both vertical height and the number of
stories are components of the height requirements. She noted that since the
application failed to receive a favorable recommendation from the Zoning Board,
the design had been changed to bring the vertical height into compliance and
eliminate the need for that variation.
Trustee Woodbury confirmed that the only issue before the Council was the
request to permit a variation allowing a third story covering 72% of the second
story, as opposed to a half story covering a maximum of 50% of the second
story, as required by the Zoning Ordinance.
Paul Hardej, the developer, and Voy Madeyski, the architect, used elevations to
review the new design. They also presented samples of the proposed building
materials.
Steve Friedland, attorney for the developer, spoke to the standards required
for granting the variation. He addressed the standards of reasonable return and
uniqueness of the site as justification for the variation. In support of the
first, he pointed to other design modifications made by the applicant in
response to Village and neighborhood concerns, which significantly impacted the
amount of usable space in the proposed building, which had been reduced from
approximately 26,500 square feet to 19,500 square feet. With regard to the
second standard, he noted the property’s narrow sidewalks, three street
frontages, and the Ordinance’s requirement that commercial space be provided at
street level.
Martin Kenny, 510 Meadow Rd., Jack Wolf, 91 High St., Barbara Hull, 101 High
St., and Jennifer Hull, 80 Brier Street, expressed continued reservations about
the project, based on its mass, the impact on the residential neighborhood, and
establishing an undesirable precedent. They urged the Council to deny the
variation and require the applicant to abide by Zoning Ordinance.
Jim Haft, 1096 Ash St., spoke favorably about the proposed design and urged
approval of the variation.
MollyBelle Berish commented on the applicants’ selection of materials.
Trustee Greenough disclosed that her husband was a partner in the same law firm
as Mr. Friedland, but that it did not create a legal conflict of interest, so
she would not needed to recuse herself from this decision. She noted that the
current design is significantly different from the first design, which had been
designed in full compliance with the Zoning Ordinance and in keeping with
Winnetka 2020, but was not at all what had been anticipated in the
Comprehensive Plan. She expressed her belief that although not 2-1/2 stories,
the current proposal is much more aesthetically pleasing overall and probably
will not have the looming presence or adverse impact about which neighbors are
concerned.
Trustee Presser noted that, from the beginning, the neighbors’ concerns have
centered around massiveness and congestion, and pointed out that the earlier
designs were significantly more massive than the present design. Saying that it
is inevitable that there will be some building on this site, he believes this
design to be about as good as anything the Village is going to get, and he is,
therefore, inclined to support the variation.
Trustee Woodbury commented that the applicant has done what it has been asked
to do by both the Council and the Design Review Board, and as a result, the
current design is substantially different from the one reviewed by the Zoning
Board of Appeals. He acknowledged that while there will be some unavoidable
impact upon the neighborhood, the property is unique. He expressed support for
the current option, saying that it is preferable to any future one that might
combine this site with the neighboring parcel, which would make it possible to
construct an even larger building.
Trustee Brower agreed with reluctance. He expressed regret that the
neighborhood will be changed somewhat, but said that he believes that the
applicant has made every reasonable effort to accommodate Village concerns.
Trustee Powell indicated that he would have a difficult time supporting this
variation because of his concern about the size of the third floor.
Trustee Aquilino indicated that she had voted in favor of the previous
variations and Special Use Permit because they resulted in a design that was an
improvement over what was allowed under existing zoning. She noted
disappointment that the current design is not smaller, but said that given what
is permitted, this design is very good looking, and therefore, she would be
willing to support it.
Hearing a consensus to approve the variation, President Duhl called for a
motion to direct the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting the
requested variation. Trustee Presser so moved, seconded by Trustee Aquilino. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
10. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1225 and 1226. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1225 in the amount of $ 641,866.06 and
Warrant List No 1226 in the amount of $447,006.80. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Aquilino. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
b)   Wireless 911. Police Chief Gallagher explained that the Police Department
is trying to favorably position itself so that when the Cook County Sheriff’s
Department ceases to provide wireless 9-1-1 service, as it is planning to do,
the Village will be able to make a decision as to whether or not to provide the
service, and if so, enable the Village to receive its portion of applicable
surcharges. He reviewed the steps required to accomplish this goal, one of
which is signing a non-disclosure agreement with all of the cellular service
providers. He said that should the Village ultimately opt in favor of providing
wireless 9-1-1 service, the necessary equipment modifications and upgrades
would be fairly minimal.
Attorney Janega explained the nature of the non-disclosure agreements.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to authorize the execution
of the non-disclosure agreements. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and
Greenough.
c)   Bid No. 01-009: Street Reconstruction. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for
the reconstruction of Ash Street from Wilson Avenue to Sheridan Road. He noted
that the low bid exceeds the budgeted amount by roughly $60,000. He explained
that this is probably due to (1) staffing shortage that resulted in the bids
going out later than usual in the year when the better bid prices are not
available; and (2) the cost of road construction is impacted by the cost of
petroleum and fuel. Mr. Saunders recommended that the bid be awarded to Lenny
Hoffman Excavating in the amount of $460,534.40.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Change Order: Mt. Pleasant Avenue Water Main. Manager Williams reviewed
the reasons for the change order and reported staff’s request that it be
approved.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the change order.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
11. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the ad hoc Façade Committee would
be meeting on Thursday, June 21st at 7:30 p.m. in the Public Safety Building to
consider possible designs for the new building at the Village Yards. He also
informed the Trustees that Winnetka had been designated by the National Arbor
Day Foundation as Tree City USA for the year 2000.
b)   Trustees.
Trustee Presser reported that the next edition of the Winnetka Report would be
mailed soon.
Trustee Brower reported that he had attended the Chamber of Commerce’s
inaugural dinner for the new President, Howard Smith. He mentioned that the
Chamber is working on the July Sidewalk Sale and had recently sponsored a
presentation by an attorney specializing in advising merchants about ADA issues
and procedures.
12. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Ned Leuchtner to the Landmark
Preservation Commission. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved
to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, the possible
purchase of property, and the possible sale of Village owned land, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), Section 2(c)(5), and 2(c)(6), respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:51 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:14 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
The meeting adjourned at 10:15 p.m.
