                  Deputy Clerk
July 10, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved August 7, 2001)
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Barbara Aquilino called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m.
Present: Trustees Hugh Brower, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: President Michael Duhl and Trustee Nan Greenough. Also
present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega,
Police Chief William Gallagher, and one person in the audience.
2. Demolition Permit Application Process.
Ms. Janega reviewed Council’s actions thus far on this issue. She reported that
while discussing a revised discussion draft of Chapter 15.52, which included
changes made in response to the Council’s comments at its June 14, 2001 Study
Session, the Code Review Committee identified the following three additional
policy issues to be resolved:
1.   What qualifications are required of persons who perform the historical and
architectural impact study (HAIS)?
2.   If the Building Review Committee (BRC) can continue the impact hearing
because the HAIS is inadequate or incomplete, what mechanism, if any, should
there be to break the circular pattern that can occur either if the BRC
continues to find the HAIS unsatisfactory or if the applicant refuses to do
anything further on the HAIS?
3.   Should there be an appeal to the Council and, if so, at what point in the
process?
She then reviewed the third discussion draft section by section and addressed
language proposed to deal with each of the above questions, as follows:
Regarding the first question, the new discussion draft requires that the HAIS
be performed by an architectural historian retained by the applicant at the
applicant’s expense and that the names and qualifications of the persons
performing the study be included.
Regarding the second question, the new draft provides for only one continuance
to be imposed by the BRC as a result of an incomplete HAIS. If an applicant
refuses, or still fails to produce a satisfactory HAIS, the applicant would be
directed to deposit funds with the Village so that the Village could complete
the study and the processing of the application could move ahead to the next
step.
Regarding the final question, she noted that no language had been proposed to
provide for an appeal process because the BRC’s authority to continue the
impact determination meeting has been limited. She pointed out that in the end,
an applicant would always receive a demolition permit, regardless of the
determination on significance.
The Trustees discussed the outstanding issues.
Trustees Presser and Aquilino expressed concern about “architectural historian”
being a defined profession and asked if there were recognized credentials make
an individual qualified to perform the requested study.
Trustee Woodbury pointed out that the test for significance might fall under
the purview of any number of fields of expertise. While an architectural
historian might be qualified to determine the architectural significance of a
house, he might not be focused on determining the historical significance, i.e.
famous persons who lived in the house. Or, depending upon whether the
individual was an architect, he might or might not be qualified to evaluate the
structure from a technical standpoint.
Trustee Presser suggested that the Building Review Committee make a
recommendation as to the kind of expert that should be employed. He also voiced
some concern that the HAIS study could impose a significant expense upon the
applicant.
Trustee Brower wondered whether the Village ought to put some kind of cap on
how much it would be willing to invest in completing the study in the event
that the applicant fails to complete it satisfactorily, noting that ultimately,
whether the study is completed or not, the applicant will get its demolition
permit.
Attorney Janega reminded the Trustees that most applicants would not be
subjected to having to complete an HAIS. She suggested that the BRC be
instructed to provide direction as to the areas of potential significance,
architectural or historical, in the preliminary determination, so that an
appropriate, qualified “expert” can be employed to complete the study.
The question was raised as to who determines who qualifies as a “famous”
person. Trustee Presser remarked that the object is just to uncover pertinent
information.
Attorney Janega commented that the Building Review Committee would make the
determination as to both historic and architectural significance, using the
same standards as the Landmark Preservation Commission. She stated that the
goal in running the property through the same type of examination is to educate
the applicant, who could discover that the building might be worth keeping. She
also pointed out that under the revised procedures both the Landmark
Preservation Commission and the Winnetka Historical Society would act only in
an advisory capacity, providing comment or other input for the Building Review
Committee.
Manager Williams pointed out that the new process can significantly affect the
time frame required to obtain a demolition permit.
Trustee Brower expressed some reservations about the process because an
applicant wouldn’t know at the time of application how long the process would
ultimately take.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that, given the shifting and broadening of the
Building Review Committee’s responsibilities, it might be appropriate to
enlarge the committee. The Trustees concurred, and it was agreed to increase
the membership to five.
There was also consensus among the Trustees to allow the Building Review
Committee to impose only one continuance in the event of an incomplete HAIS,
and not to include any kind of appeal process.
The Village Attorney was asked to return with a draft ordinance for
introduction.
3. Possible Lease of Train Stations.
Manager Williams explained that, in response to both the Beautification
Committee and Village Caucus, the Village had contacted Metra officials
regarding upgrading the deteriorated Village train stations. Preliminary
meetings with Metra resulted in Metra’s proposing major renovation projects for
both of the Elm Street and Hubbard Woods stations, subject to available
funding. These renovations, however, are dependent upon the Village’s
negotiating a lease with Union Pacific for the operation and maintenance of the
stations. He noted that several neighboring communities have such leases and
that their budgeted costs vary considerably. He recommended that the Village
continue to explore the lease option and put together a detailed budget. The
Trustees agreed.
4. New Trier High School Parking Lot Lease.
Chief Gallagher reviewed the history of these leases and the status of current
negotiations with the High School in which the Village is proposing to increase
the school’s cost for some of the spaces and to make some of the spaces
historically available to the school available to commuters before 9:00 a.m.
Manager Williams pointed out that the Village would continue to make parking
spaces available for teachers and staff at the same cost, but that some student
spaces would be eliminated while others would be more expensive. He said that
it is in the Village’s best interests to reclaim as many of the parking spaces
as possible because pressure from other user groups continues to increase.
All of the Trustees agreed that it was important to continue to squeeze the
High School’s parking spaces and make it more expensive for students to drive
to school, noting that the current rate charged for students to park in the
Village’s lots is less than the rate paid to park in private driveways. There
was agreement to incrementally raise the parking rates for the student spaces
to $5.00 each over the first three years of the new contract so as to reduce
the incentive for students to drive to school.
5. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:15 p.m.
July 17, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved August 7, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
17, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steven
Saunders, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Community
Development Director Brian Norkus and about fifty persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 7(d) had been removed from the Agenda and
that the Council would discuss Item 9(c) before Item 9(b). The Council approved
the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   August 7, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   August 14, 2001, Study Session. President Duhl announced that this meeting
might be cancelled, but all of the Trustees present, except Trustee Presser,
indicated that they could attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 3, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., raised four issues: (1) He reported that he had
seen passersby taking bricks recovered during the Tower Road project from piles
on the side of the road. Mr. Saunders indicated that these were to be used in
the future for a retaining wall and indicated that steps would be taken to
label the material as Village property and provide better security. (2) He
requested that the Village return to its practice of leaving compost available
outside of the Village Yards complex for individuals who are unable to pick it
up during weekday business hours. (3) He asked whether the Village had any
studies showing that the undergrounding of utility wires increases the value of
property. Mr. Williams replied that it did not. (4) He reiterated concern that
residents had not had not been appropriately notified about the costs imposed
by the Village Council for undergrounding utility wires, voicing objection to
the Council’s taking unilateral action without consulting residents and stating
that the matter may be headed for the courts.
MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln., read a letter from the local committeeman
for the Democratic National Committee objecting to the Village’s policy of
having Council meetings on evenings when there is a national election. Ms.
Berish reiterated her previous requests that this policy be reconsidered.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Ordinance No. M-16-2001 – Zoning Variation: 518 Winnetka Ave. – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance granting a
variance from the maximum building height requirement of 2-1/2 stories to allow
construction of a 3-story building on the subject property, noting in
particular that the pertinent plans had been incorporated as a part of the
ordinance. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to amend the
ordinance as outlined. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Presser, Aquilino,
Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: Trustee Powell. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2001 – Appearance Review Appeals – Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed suggested amendments to the draft ordinance establishing an
appeals procedure for Design Review Board decisions. She explained that this
ordinance permits any decision by the Design Review Board to be appealed to the
Village Council by an applicant before seeking a judicial appeal. Trustee
Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the ordinance as
suggested. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2001 – Flood Hazard Protection Ordinance Revisions –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders reported that after introduction on June 5, 2001, the
draft ordinance had been amended to address Council’s concerns and sent to FEMA
and IDNR/OWR for their review and comment. He explained that the amendment now
presented for adoption reflects both the Council’s and the agencies’ comments.
He, then, summarized those changes, as follows:
   i)   The provision regarding repetitive loss was removed because it was
apparent that the cost of necessary remedial work would, in all likelihood,
exceed the additional insurance benefits available.
   ii)   Regarding the construction of basements below the Base Flood
Elevation, language has been added to address Council’s concerns about creating
a moat effect by placing limits on the allowable depth of compensatory storage
in front and side yards. In addition the ordinance provides for a reduction in
the engineered extension of fill beyond the building wall to allow for drainage
swales on narrower, smaller lots.
   iii)   Language has been added clarifying that the application of building
protection criteria be applied only to in cases of substantial improvements.
   iv)   At the agencies’ direction, a definition of “new construction” and
administration and enforcement provisions have been added.
   v)   New language regarding the construction of detached garages and tool
sheds below the BFE was also added.
Mr. Saunders also commented on a letter received from Mr. and Mrs. Teborek, 804
Boal Parkway. He concluded by saying that in all cases, the ordinance requires
that the required compensatory storage must be able to drain freely to the
available storm drainage system and that, as with any development within the
Village, site drainage must not adversely affect adjacent properties.
Trustee Woodbury expressed concern that an unintended consequence of the
proposed limits on the allowable depth of storage and reduction in the
engineered extension of fill might result in a “bathtub” effect, rather than a
“moat” effect. He suggested that additional language be included to clarify the
issue.
Trustee Aquilino asked how water runoff before and after construction is
determined. Mr. Saunders replied that it is done by a series of calculations.
He reported that a full engineering site plan is required before any building
development takes place and that once built an inspection is made to determine
that what was built is, in fact, what the plans said would be. An “as built”
survey must then be submitted.
Penelope Berlet, 1093 Ash, remarked that she thought this ordinance was
intended to be pro basement. Mr. Williams replied that it is, because as it
stands now, no basements can be constructed in the flood plain. Now those lots
where it is possible to meet the technical requirements will be able to qualify
for construction of a basement. Mr. Saunders, however, emphasized that
regardless of what is permitted in the new ordinance, there may still be lots
in the flood plain, especially smaller ones, on which basements cannot be built
under any circumstances because sufficient compensatory storage cannot be
constructed.
President Duhl suggested that in light of Trustee Woodbury’s comments the issue
be tabled while additional language is developed to address those concerns.
Jim Haft, 1096 Ash, spoke in support of the ordinance. He asked that the
Council be sure that any new language does not foreclose the opportunity to
build a basement on smaller lots.
The Trustees agreed to table the matter until a later date.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2001 – Amending Demolition Permit Procedures –
Introduction. As noted earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1229 and 1230. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1229 in the amount of $620,292.06 and Warrant
List No 1230 in the amount of $236,630.81. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   411 Linden: Historical Society Museum – Special Use Permit and Variation –
Policy Direction. President Duhl announced that the Council would be listening
only to audience comments at this meeting and that the Trustees would discuss
the issue at the next meeting after having had time to consider the issues. In
light of a question raised about possible conflict of interest raised by the
objectors, he asked Attorney Janega to address the issue.
Attorney Janega explained the different types of conflicts of interest. She
noted that to be a current or former member of the Society or contributor
thereto, to be a former officer or former board member not involved in the
decision to purchase the subject site, or to attend occasional meetings or
functions does not represent a conflict. She also explained what would
constitute an improper ex parte communication. She concluded by saying that
there had been no specific charges made and that she was of aware of no action
by any of the Trustees that constitutes a conflict of interest or an improper
ex parte communication and that the Council could proceed to deliberate and
decide this issue.
President Duhl then suggested that each of the Trustees disclose past or
present involvement with the Historical Society, which they did. Each member
also stated that he or she could be impartial and objective in rendering a
decision.
Trustee Presser clarified that the Trustees would be focusing on facts
pertaining to two of the key legal issues (1) whether or not the Historical
Society’s proposed use is, in fact, a nonessential public use, and (2) whether
or not the proposed use would be substantially injurious to neighboring
properties.
President Duhl indicated that the Council did not need to hear a repetition of
arguments that both sides had already presented in written documents on the
issue of whether or not the Society’s proposed use is legal, as the Trustees
would be carefully reviewing those materials and consulting with Village
counsel.
Nancy Judge, 534 Elder Ln., President of the Historical Society, reviewed the
Society’s history and explained why the site at 411 Linden is well suited to
the organization’s mission of collecting, preserving, and presenting the
Village’s history. She contended that the Society provides a cultural use to
the public that constitutes a legal institutional use, just like a school or
library. Ms. Judge also submitted an addendum to their real estate appraiser’s
report indicating that there is no reason to believe that the Society’s
occupation of the site would have any adverse impact on neighboring property
values.
Joan Evanich, 779 Bryant, the Society’s Director, described the organization’s
role in the community. She described services provided to the community by the
Museum, how the Museum has been used historically, future plans, and how the
site at 411 fits into those plans. She emphasized that it is the Society’s
intention to retain the historic residential character of the house. She stated
that its occupation of the house would not only insure preservation of a
historical structure of architectural merit, but also provide continuity and
stability to the neighborhood in a period of transition and teardowns.
Ken Behles, 426 Maple, architect for the project, presented elevations showing
the planned improvements. He noted that the proposed enlarged and attached
garage would act as a storage area, be sympathetic to the Gothic design of the
home and offer secure access to the collection. He asked to reserve the right
to expand the existing driveway should need arise in the future.
Penny Lanphier, 215 Birch, spoke on behalf of the Society’s application,
reviewing major points of the special use and variation applications. She
expressed her belief that it is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan. She
concluded that, in her opinion, the proposed changes are in keeping with a
residential neighborhood, will not dramatically affect traffic, and that,
overall, the Society’s occupation of the site benefits the general welfare of
the Village as a whole.
John Lien, 921 Pine Street, spoke against the variance and special use permit,
saying that he believes that maintaining the integrity of zoning is important.
He said, that although he personally supports the goals of the Historical
Society, this location is not appropriate for their purposes because
residential neighborhoods are sensitive. By putting this use in the midst of a
residential neighborhood, it is changed to a “mixed use” zone and, while the
house might maintain the appearance of a residence, it would, in fact, merely
be a facade. He suggested that permitting the Society to locate on Linden would
set a dangerous precedent should other not-for-profit institutions desire to
locate within a home in the Village, noting that many are well funded and could
outbid single family residential bidders. Mr. Lien also raised a question about
the propriety of the Society’s purchasing and maintaining this property that,
in fact, would be a growing asset for the Society, in part with funds received
from the Village, when the Village has no control over the Society. He urged
the Council to deny the application and the Society to consider moving the
house to another, publicly-owned property.
Dan Kinsella, 420 Linden, speaking on behalf of the Linden Street neighbors,
who filed formal objections, voiced concern and dismay about what he perceived
to be slights shown the objectors at various board and committee meetings, as
opposed to the perceived favored status shown to the Historical Society. He
objected to the Zoning Board’s placing the burden of proof on the neighbors to
establish that there would be a negative impact on property values on the
neighbors, and also objected to the Board’s apparent lack of concern about
intensity of future use. He also complained about remarks made by a Plan
Commission member characterizing the Linden Street neighborhood as lacking
quiet ambiance. Referring to comments made earlier in the meeting by Mr. Kates,
Mr. Kinsella stated that it appears that it will be necessary to take this
matter to the courts.
Mr. Kinsella voiced support for the Historical Society and its mission, but not
at the Linden Street site. He said the chosen site is not large enough for the
proposed use and is completely surrounded by single-family residential
properties, all of whose owners feel that their neighborhood does, in fact,
have a quiet ambiance. Mr. Kinsella also pointed out to the Council that it has
never granted a special use permit to an applicant who did not meet the
eligibility requirements.
Mr. Kinsella opined that the purpose of zoning is not to preserve the
“appearance” of the zone, but to preserve the “use,” and if the Historical
Society’s application is approved, the Council will be introducing a
nonconforming, institutional use into a residential neighborhood. He urged the
Council to table the present application and help the Historical Society find a
suitable, complying option. Emphasizing that the neighbors do not want the
Society on Linden Street, he asked that in the event that the application is
approved, that the Council impose significant restrictions on such issues as
parking, evening and weekend use, the number of meetings, and signage.
In closing, Mr. Kinsella commented on the Historical Society’s real estate
appraisal addendum. He introduced the neighborhood’s appraiser, Kevin Byrnes,
who stated that he had not yet had time to study the addendum. He disagreed
with the Society’s appraiser and asserted that there could potentially be
substantial impairment to neighboring property values. President Duhl told Mr.
Kinsella and Mr. Byrnes that the Council would be happy to consider any
rebuttal comments submitted by Mr. Byrnes in a timely fashion.
Robert Lapp, 414 Linden, expressed concern about what will happen to the house
in the long run, given that the Society hopes to continue growing and may at
some point outgrow this facility. In his opinion, once this home is converted
from a house to a museum, it will never be suitable for a single-family home
again. He asked the Council to defer action until a more suitable location can
be found.
MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Lane, also voiced objection to the intrusion of a
museum into a residential neighborhood, especially one where the neighbors
bought their homes with no expectation that an institutional use would be
imposed upon their neighborhood.
JoAnne Lapp, 414 Linden, addressed the parking issue. She used elevations to
show proposed nearby parking areas. She stated her belief that it is
unreasonable to believe that most guests of the Museum are going to choose to
park two or more blocks away and then walk to the museum. She further expressed
the belief that on-street parking will be the norm on an already busy and
congested street, thereby increasing not only overcrowding but raising concerns
for pedestrian safety. She asked how parking restrictions could be enforced so
residents’ guests would not be forced to find remote parking. Mrs. Lapp also
reviewed the proposed driveway configuration, pointing out that the handicapped
parking is poorly located and that guests who use the Museum’s driveway for
parking would wind up being blocked in by others.
Ruth Ann Buckley Kinsella, 420 Linden, a landscape architect, used elevations
to compare the proposed site to the sites of neighboring communities’
historical museums. Among the differences she pointed out are zoning, lot size,
and mitigating buffering features. She said that none is completely surrounded
by residential use as the Winnetka site is. She also noted that several of
these other museums have been relocated from their original site to a more
appropriate one and that only two are situated on privately owned land. She
said that the Historical Society will continue to grow, and that they need to
do so in an area where they can do so successfully.
Other residents speaking against the application included:
John and Cece Durbin, 418 Ash St.
Trude Smith, 435 Linden Street
Bill and Betty Gutekanst, 770 Cherry
John Sobel, 428 Linden
Brian and Ann Balusek, 390 Linden
Nancy McCarthy, 744 Ash
Martin Kenny, 510 Meadow
Bill Purlitz, 380 Linden
Silas Keehn, 812 Ash
All reiterated concerns about traffic, parking, signage, property values,
precedent, and forever changing the character of an historically stable,
residential neighborhood bordering on the Green Bay corridor.
Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry St., Past President of the Historical Society, spoke in
support of the application, as did Carol Hansen, 822 Bryant, a former
volunteer.
Acknowledging that there are heartfelt feelings on both sides of this issue,
President Duhl thanked all of the speakers for their patience and civility. He
said the item would be back on the Agenda at the August 7th regular Council
meeting and encouraged interested individuals to attend.
c)   Certificate of Appropriateness: Elder Lane Park – Final Design Approval.
Brian Norkus reported on recommendations made by the Design Review Board at its
June 19th meeting regarding the beach house design and park lighting. He
reported that the Design Review Board had expressed a preference for stone
construction as well as light fixtures in keeping with the Village’s Design
Guidelines, although they found those originally proposed by the Park District
acceptable. They also re-evaluated the original beach house design and
suggested various alternative design details. He noted that since the
renovations of the park were subject to issuance of a Special Use Permit,
issuance of the Certificate of Appropriateness is subject to final approval by
the Council.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, commented on the Design Review Board’s
suggestions. She said that cost would be a determining factor but indicated
that she expected that some combination of the Board’s recommendations should
be able to be incorporated into the final design.
After discussing the process and the options, Trustee Woodbury moved to approve
the Certificate of Appropriateness specifying stone construction, a roof design
with eaves supported by brackets, and lighting in keeping with the Design
Guidelines, with the caveat that brick construction and lighting as initially
proposed would be acceptable. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid No. 01-013 – Underground Cable. Manager Williams reviewed a bid for
purchase and delivery of 15kv and 600v cable to be used on various projects
throughout the Village on an “as needed” basis for the period August 1, 2001
through July 31, 2002, with an option to extend for one (1) additional year. He
reported Staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to Englewood Electric
for the 15kv cable and to Wesco for the 600v cable for an estimated total
purchase of $168,000 worth of cable over the year.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that on Thursday the Design Review
Board would be considering the tentative façade design for the new Village
Yards project.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Presser mentioned that the Code Committee planned to
meet next week and the monarch butterfly issue would fall within the scope of
its discussions.
Trustee Brower reported on attendance at the last Chamber of Commerce meeting
where discussion had centered on the upcoming Sidewalk Sale.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and the possible purchase of
property pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(5), respectively, of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 11:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:50 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting and
to reconvene the meeting to complete the Executive Session on July 24, 2001, at
7:30 p.m. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned
at 11:51 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
July 24, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL RECONVENED REGULAR MEETING
(Approved August 7, 2001)
A record of a legally reconvened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
24, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Stephen
Presser. Absent: Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director
Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus,
Finance Director Ed McKee, and no persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Executive Session.
President Duhl stated that the only agenda item for this meeting is an
executive session to discuss pending and probable litigation and the possible
purchase or lease of real estate by the Village, the Council having decided to
continue the regular meeting of July 17, 2001, to today, to complete the
executive session portion of the July 17 agenda.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and the possible purchase or lease
of property pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(5), respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 7:35 p.m.
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:40 p.m. during the executive session.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:30 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
4. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
August 7, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved August 21, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
7, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steven
Saunders, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Planner, Ann
Klaassen, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and about 28 persons in
the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   August 14, 2001, Study Session. President Duhl announced that this meeting
had been cancelled.
b)   August 21, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 10, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   July 17, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   July 24, 2001, Reconvened Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt
the minutes as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously
6. Public Comment and Questions.
Linda Kramer, 370 Glendale, voiced continued concern about heavy truck traffic
on Willow Road and asked for a report on the truck ban ordinance passed last
year. She expressed frustration over the amount of truck traffic, the
congestion, and the noise.
Mr. Williams reported that the police had been carefully monitoring the
situation, but found that most of the trucks using Winnetka’s streets are
destined for businesses or construction within the Village.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2001 – Flood Hazard Protection Ordinance Revisions. –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders reported that the text of the ordinance had not changed
since the discussion at the July 17th meeting. He indicated that in response to
concerns raised by Trustee Woodbury about the appearance and safety of
compensatory storage, a new ordinance, MC-8-2001, which follows, had been
drafted.
Manager Williams noted that this draft had already been reviewed and approved
by FEMA and that the newly proposed MC-8-2001 would not require FEMA’s
approval.
Sallie VanArsdale, 1137 Cherry, urged the Council to pass the ordinance in the
hopes that it would help to dispel some of the fear that some buyers seem to
have about buying property in the floodplain.
In response to a question from Irv Footlick, Mr. Saunders reviewed the
procedures for reviewing drainage plans and commented on suggestions for
potential retention areas.
Trustee Brower inquired whether there were any houses actually in the floodway.
Mr. Saunders responded that there are none.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2001 – Amending Village Code Regarding Stormwater
Detention Requirements for Issuance of Building Permits - Introduction. As
mentioned above, Mr. Saunders explained that in the course of considering MC-5-
2001, which will allow basements in filled areas of the floodplain, the Council
expressed concerns that using retaining walls to construct compensatory storage
areas may, in some instances, create an additional hazard for adjoining
properties. He noted that MC-8-2001, which amends the stormwater drainage
requirements for building permit approval in Section 15.32.080 of the Code, had
been drafted to address these issues.
Attorney Janega explained that at the last meeting Trustee Woodbury had
expressed concern about the possible appearance of the compensatory storage
areas, particularly if retaining walls are placed adjacent to property lines.
She noted that this ordinance would prohibit the use of retaining walls in
compensatory storage or detention areas on the side nearest the property line.
Mr. Saunders pointed out that this ordinance would apply to all building
permits as well as to compensatory storage areas.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2001 – Amending Demolition Permit Procedures –
Introduction. Ms. Janega reviewed the history of this issue. She explained that
it provides for extensive amendments to the “Demolition Permits” chapter of the
Village Code. In addition, it would (1) increase the size of the Building
Review Committee from three members to five; (2) add a new section that
establishes the qualifications for the members of that committee; and (3) set
out the duties of that committee to make them consistent with the new
demolition procedures. She then provided a section-by-section explanation of
the amendments to the “Demolition Permits” chapter. These include: (1)
reorganizing and clarifying the current filing requirement and expanding and
clarifying the contents of the application; (2) adding a new step to the
process providing for a preliminary determination as to significance; (3)
adding a requirement for a full historic and architectural impact study
(“HAIS”) following a preliminary determination of significance; (4) adding a
section identifying the contents of the HAIS and clarifying provisions for its
filing and distribution; (5) addressing the timing and nature of the
determination of significance and identifying the components that form the
record and the standards for determining significance; and (6) modifying and
expanding the delay provisions.
Carter Page, 38 Abbottsford, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission
commented favorably on the draft ordinance.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-17-2001 – Landmark Designation: 20 Fox Lane –
Introduction. Carter Page, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission
showed slides of the subject property, pointing out significant architectural
features. He noted that, based upon the System for the Evaluation of Landmarks,
the home had received an overall score of 84.4 points and had been rated
"unique." Mr. Page concluded by reviewing proposed alterations to the
residence, which the Commission found to be appropriate to the character of the
residence and in keeping with the spirit of the original design.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Village Yards Rehabilitation
   i)   Strategic Direction. Manager Williams reviewed the history of this
issue. He went on to report on the project’s development and current status. He
explained that after working with the architect and construction manager, the
only plan that falls within the budget established by the Council is a
combination of new construction and remodeled areas. The former would primarily
be areas in which personnel will be working and the latter primarily storage
areas. He emphasized that this project will have no impact on the property tax
rate. He reported that although the current cost estimate exceeds the budget by
approximately $302,000, staff recommends proceeding with design work, which
will refine the cost estimate and identify ways in which to choose less
expensive construction methods. Mr. Williams also pointed out that this is
neither the end of the road in terms of process nor the point of no return for
the project.
Mr. Saunders introduced representatives from the project’s architectural firm,
Scott Rubel and Louise Kowalczyk, and construction manager, Anthony Douglas. He
then reviewed the Special Use Permit request.
After Mr. Rubel and Ms. Kowalczyk reviewed elevations of the proposed site and
addressed points raised by the Design Review Board, there was a consensus to
proceed to detailed design drawings.
Manager Williams and President Duhl thanked Irv Footlick, a resident, for his
contribution to the development of the design.
   ii)   Ordinance No. M-18-2001 – Special Use Permit – Introduction. Attorney
Janega explained that the proposed ordinance would amend the Village’s Special
Use Permit for the Municipal Maintenance Facility at 1390 Willow Road, to
permit renovation and expansion of the existing building. She explained that
the Special Use Permit is required because some of the functions of the yards
are considered nonessential public uses under the Zoning Ordinance. She pointed
out that no zoning variations are required. Ms. Janega reviewed concerns raised
by Village Boards and Commissions, including: (1) massing and roof forms, (2)
materials and details, (3) landscaping, and (4) parking, and noted that the
draft contains conditions intended to address those concerns. These conditions
allow for further design review, but reserve the final decision for the
Council, whose final decisions on each issue would be reflected in the final
ordinance by making necessary modifications after introduction and prior to
adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
18-2001, amending the existing Special Use Permit. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
8. Old Business.
a)   411 Linden: Historical Society Museum – Special Use Permit and Variation –
Policy Direction. President Duhl commented that, after further review of the
Historical Society’s request and the evidence and public comment received into
the record, the Council felt it was advisable to further consider the
possibility of moving the house at 411 Linden to an alternative site on
publicly owned land before making a final decision. He said that staff had
identified some possible sites and suggested that Council table the issue in
order to give staff time to do a feasibility study of such a move. Upon
completion of that study, Council would be better able to determine whether the
requested Special Use Permit should be granted and, if so, whether the grant
should be with, or without, conditions.
Angie Davis, 647 Hill Rd. and Becky Hurley, 1027 Spruce Street, Historical
Society Board members, supported the idea of looking at publicly owned land for
a site. They, however, pointed out some of the desirable attributes of the 411
site and some disadvantages to the Society of waiting too long for a decision.
Mr. Williams reviewed potential alternate sites and a timeline for staff
analysis. He emphasized that this is a creative process and that new sites
might come to light while others might prove to be unacceptable. He indicated
that he expected that a feasibility study could be completed in time for
presentation at the September 11th Study Session.
In response to a question from the audience, President Duhl said that the
Village would not pay for moving the house from the 411 Linden site to another
location.
Trustee Aquilino remarked that the Village has limited land resources and that
there are many competing demands for those resources.
Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry Street, and Lloyd Owens, 922 Elm Street, both commented
briefly on the issue
President Duhl called for a motion to table the request for Special Use Permit
to the October 16, 2001 regular Council meeting. Trustee Presser so moved.
Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and
Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1231 and 1232. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1231 in the amount of $887,760.87 and Warrant
List No 1232 in the amount of $607,520.20. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   New Trier High School Parking Contract. Manager Williams reviewed the
terms of the contract, noting that it follows policy direction established by
the Council.
Trustee Presser moved to approve the contract substantially in the form
presented and to authorize the Village President to sign in duplicate. Trustee
Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
c)   Bid No. 01-011 – Aerial Platform Lift. Mr. McInturff reviewed a bid to
furnish and install a fully hydraulic, insulated, telescopic aerial platform
lift with a one (1) man bucket on a truck chassis purchased earlier this year.
He explained why staff’s recommendation was to go with Mid-America Truck &
Equipment Co., Inc., in the total amount of $61,512.00, even though a somewhat
lower bid was received.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Bid No. 01-014 – Pad Mount Transformers. Mr. McInturff reviewed a bid for
purchase and delivery, on an ‘as needed’ basis between August 1, 2001 and July
31, 2002, of single-phase and three-phase pad mount transformers. He reported
staff’s recommendation that the award be made to MC Sales for the single-phase
transformers and RESCO for the three-phase transformers.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams and Mr. Saunders reported on the preceding
week’s storm and power failure incidents.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Powell reported on his recent attendance at a RED Center
meeting. He said that the new facility is essentially completed and is expected
to open on January 1st. He also mentioned that the Deerfield/Bannockburn Fire
Protection District has applied for membership.
Trustee Brower reported on the most recent Chamber of Commerce meeting. He
noted that the Sidewalk Sale had not been as successful as hoped and that the
members were again reexamining the concept
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and the possible purchase of
property pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(5), respectively, of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 10:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:10 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:11 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
August 21, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved September 4, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
21, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve
Saunders, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Fire Chief Ron
Colpaert and about 8 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   September 4, 2001, Regular Meeting. President Duhl said that he would be
absent for this meeting.
b)   September 11, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 7, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Lane, asked how long the Village keeps a record.
Ms. Janega replied that the formal record of the Council is kept forever.
Mrs. Berish asked the Council to consider the design of the street lights on
behalf of some citizens of Winnetka who think they look commercial and non-
traditional. She also distributed a letter to the Council that she did not want
to read for the record and asked that the Council consider it.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2001 – Amending Village Code Regarding Stormwater
Detention Requirements for Issuance of Building Permits. – Adoption. Ms. Janega
reported that the text of the ordinance had not changed since it was introduced
at the August 7th meeting. She explained that the ordinance addresses concerns
raised by Trustee Woodbury about using retaining walls to construct
compensatory storage areas that may, in some instances, create an additional
hazard for adjoining properties. She noted that the ordinance would prohibit
the use of retaining walls in compensatory storage or detention areas on the
side nearest the property line.
Trustee Presser seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2001 – Landmark Designation: 20 Fox Lane –
Adoption. President Duhl explained that this ordinance had been introduced at
the August 7th meeting to designate this unique property as a Winnetka
Landmark.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt Ordinance M-17-2001
designating 20 Fox Lane as a landmark. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and
Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-7-2001 – Amending Demolition Permit Procedures – Adoption. Ms.
Janega said this ordinance was introduced at the August 7th meeting and amends
Chapters 3.36 and 15.52 of the Village Code, as they pertain to building
demolition and the role of the Building Review Committee. She explained that,
pursuant to discussions when it was introduced, the ordinance has been amended.
The amendments are: (1) increase the size of the Building Review Committee from
three members to five; (2) add a new section that establishes the
qualifications for the members of that committee; and (3) modify the duties of
the committee to make them consistent with the new demolition procedures. She
also described a few non-substantive revisions that were made since the
ordinance was introduced.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend Ordinance MC-7-
2001. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Trustee Presser, seconded
by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance as amended. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough,
Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-18-2001 – Village Yards Rehabilitation – Special Use
Permit – Adoption. Ms. Janega explained that this ordinance amends the
Village’s special use permit for the Municipal Maintenance Facility at 1390
Willow Road and will allow the renovation and expansion of the existing
building to correct building deficiencies and to accommodate current and
anticipated space needs of the Public Works and Water & Electric Departments.
She said that since introduction at the August 7th meeting, a few minor
revisions were made to the ordinance and briefly described the changes.
Manager Williams emphasized that this is just one more step in process and will
now go to the EPA for review.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend Ordinance M-18-
2001. By voice vote, the motion was passed unanimously. Trustee Aquilino,
seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance as amended. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
d)   Ordinance No. M-19-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1009 Old Green Bay Road –
Introduction. Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio explained that
this ordinance would grant variations from the front and rear yard setback
requirements of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of a
one-story addition to include kitchen and mud room expansion, improve the
access to the basement and install a bay window in an existing nonconforming
rear yard. Mr. D’Onofrio said the Zoning Board of Appeals held a public hearing
and the members voted 6-1 to recommend approval for the front setback and
recommended denial of the rear yard setback variation request.
Paul Konstant, 16 Old Green Bay Road, the architect for the project, commented
on the expansion of the kitchen and improved access to the basement in the
proposed addition. He maintained that, if measured from the curb rather than
the property line, the front setback would be reduced from 72 to 69 feet and
would still be in keeping with the neighborhood.
Trustee Woodbury confirmed that the Village Code measures the setback from the
property line and that the proposed addition will maintain the character of the
neighborhood.
There were no further comments on the front setback variation request..
Mr. Konstant provided a picture of the proposed bay window requiring a rear
yard setback variation.
Trustee Powell commented that it appeared to be more of a bump-out than a
window.
Mr. Konstant replied that the extension will not actually be a window, but an
extended bookcase inside the house. He said that the room will be extended 1.5
feet for a distance of 12 feet along the length of the house and that it would
project no further than the roof line.
Trustee Aquilino asked about the existing nonconformity and the calculations
that indicate that the rear yard would be reduced further than it is now.
Mr. Konstant said the rear yard setback is 15.33 feet and that they will be
building along the existing wall of the house for 12 feet.
Ms. Janega explained that a bay window would be allowed to encroach into a
required yard as long as the building wall itself is in a conforming location.
She said that, in this case, the building wall is in a nonconforming location
and the allowance would not apply.
The Trustees discussed the findings of the Zoning Board of Appeals and how the
calculations for the rear yard setback were made.
Manager Williams said that he would have Village Staff prepare calculations for
the Council and suggested that the discussion about the rear setback be
reserved until the next meeting.
President Duhl requested a motion to introduce the ordinance with discussion on
the rear setback variation request continuing at the Council’s next meeting and
before adoption of the ordinance.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce Ordinance
M-19-2001, granting variations from the setback requirements of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance. By voice vote, the motion was passed unanimously.
e)   Resolution No. R-30-2001 – Harris Bank Subdivision – Adoption and M-20-
2001 Zoning Variation and Special Use Permit Request - Introduction.
   i)   Mr. D’Onofrio explained that adoption of Resolution R-30-2001 would
approve the proposed resubdivision of the Harris Bank Winnetka property at 1070
Gage Street and 928 Green Bay Road.
   ii)   He then explained that M-20-2001 amends the Harris Bank’s special use
permit for the existing bank facility and requests a variation from the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to increase the impermeable lot coverage of the
proposed south lot to 100%. He said that the property is zoned C-2, and at the
public hearing, the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended approval of the
variation and special use. He said the Plan Commission also voted favorably,
but did make certain conditions, including provisions for easements for ingress
and egress and for utilities.
Trustee Brower recused himself from the discussion and the vote on this
resolution and ordinance.
Sarah Nadelhoffer, attorney for the Harris Bank, said the subdivision request
divides the bank property at 1070 Gage from the south part of the property at
928 Green Bay which is existing storefront retail property. She said that
subdividing the properties resulted in a need for a variation request and for
an amendment to the special use permit. She introduced bank president, Bruce
Osborne to tell the Council why the bank is making these requests.
Mr. Osborne said that the bank had acquired the property in 1975 to build a
branch in Hubbard Woods with a drive through facility. The bank retained two
commercial retail spaces that have been leased out since the date of purchase.
He said the bank’s original intention was to expand the bank space into the
retail property, but it has been decided that this would not be necessary. He
said that their comptroller has asked that banks not retain retail property
that is not for their use, so the bank is selling the property at 928 Green Bay
and this is the reason for the request.
Manager Williams said that he would have the Village Staff prepare a zoning
matrix for this zoning request for the Council’s next meeting and language. He
suggested that the resolution for the subdivision be adopted at that time.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino moved to introduce Ordinance M-
20-2001 amending a special use permit and granting a variation for the Harris
Bank Winnetka property. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously, with one
abstention.
f)   Ordinance No. M-21-2001 – Zoning Variation 503 Orchard Lane –
Introduction. Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio presented this
request for a variation from the maximum building size requirements of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the reconstruction of the second floor of
the residence. He said the total gross floor area will be reduced because of
the installation of an elevator for which an exception is given, but it will
still exceed the maximum building size requirements. He said the residence is
currently nonconforming, but the nonconformity will be reduced by the
reconstruction.
Trustee Brower commented that the reason for the reconstruction is so that the
owner can get to the second floor in a wheelchair.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance M-21-
2001 granting a variation for the property at 503 Orchard Lane. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Ordinance No. M-23-2001 – Sale of Surplus Vehicles – Introduction. Manager
Williams explained that this ordinance authorizes the sale of vehicles and leaf
machines that have been replaced or have exceeded their useful life through the
Northwest Municipal Conference’s auction being held on September 22nd. He
pointed out that Section 5 of the resolution authorizes the Village Manager or
his designee to sell the equipment at a lower bid than the minimum amount. He
said that this provision has been added to prevent the return of the property
to the Village if it is not sold for the minimum amount.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce Ordinance
M-23-2001 for the sale of surplus vehicles. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
h)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2001 – New Liquor License: Glass Onion Café –
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that an application had been received
for a Class A-1 liquor license to allow liquor service in a restaurant at 64
Green Bay Road, named the Glass Onion Café. He said Ordinance MC-9-2001 asks
the Council to consider amending the Village Code to increase the number of A-
1 liquor licenses to five.
Trustee Powell seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-9-
2001 amending the number of A-1 liquor licenses. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
i)   Resolution No. R-32-2001 – Membership in NIPSTA – Adoption. Chief Colpaert
explained that Resolution R-32-2001 would approve the Intergovernmental
Agreement and By-laws of the Northeastern Illinois Public Safety Training
Academy. The Village Council’s approval would allow the Village of Winnetka to
become a founding member in the formation of the Academy. He said the Village’s
commitment would be $11,050 per year for the next two years for both the police
and fire departments. He described changes to the by-laws since the Council’s
agenda materials were distributed and answered Council’s questions pertaining
to indebtedness.
After receiving additional comments from Manager Williams and Village Attorney
Janega, the Council approved the membership to NIPSTA.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt Resolution R-32-
2001 to approve an agreement and by-laws for membership in the Northeastern
Illinois Public Safety Training Academy. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and
Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
j)   Resolution No. R-31-2001 – Elimination of Deposits – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio stated that Resolution R-31-2001 amends the fees for construction by
eliminating several of the permit deposit requirements. He said that after
discussions with his staff, it was his recommendation to increase the amount of
the contractor’s bond to cover the deposits that have been eliminated. He
believed this would simplify the process and reduce staff time in tracking
deposits and refunds. He said the deposits for demolition clean up and
certificates of occupancy would remain so that the Village has the means to
require compliance at construction sites.
Manager Williams reminded the Council that the audit of the Community
Development Department last year recommended that this process be streamlined
and said that he believes that it makes sense to eliminate these deposits.
In response to Trustee Woodbury’s question about how a claim is made against
the deposit, Mr. D’Onofrio said that it is much like an insurance claim. He
said that stop work orders are effective and the certificate of occupancy can
also be held to make contractors comply. He also said that he has advised his
inspectors to tell the contractors when violations are noticed and not to wait
for specific inspections and hopes that as people become educated with the
process, there will be more cooperation.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce Resolution
R-31-2001 amending construction fees. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
k)   Ordinance No. M-22-2001 – Purchase of 93 Green Bay Road – Introduction.
Manager Williams stated that the corner of Winnetka and Green Bay Road has
traditionally been a location that the Village has wanted to improve and the
purchase of this property would give the Village an opportunity to implement
one of the recommendations in the Winnetka 2020 Comprehensive Plan. He said the
owner of 93 Green Bay Road has entered into a tentative agreement to sell the
property to the Village and the Village has the necessary cash reserves to
purchase the property without having to consider any other financing options.
Attorney Janega reviewed the contract for purchase of the property which she
described as straightforward. In response to a question from President Duhl
about the property’s exemption from taxes, she explained that exemption is
based not only on ownership, but that the property would have to be put to
governmental use. She said there will also be appropriate environmental
approval for the property.
Manager Williams explained that the Council has a draft of the contract and the
attorneys are still working on a final version.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
22-2001 for the purchase of property at 93 Green Bay Road. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1233 and 1234. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1233 in the amount of $723,631.42 and Warrant
List No 1234 in the amount of $409,521.03. Trustee Woodbury seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. None.
b)   Village President. None.
c)   Trustees. Trustee Greenough reported the Plan Commission will take another
look at the Comprehensive Plan with the discussion beginning tomorrow night,
August 22, and will report to the Council in the next few months.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and the possible purchase of
property pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(5), respectively, of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:50 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:51 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
September 4, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved September 18, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 4, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Barbara Aquilino called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m.
Present: Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Michael
D’Onofrio, and about 12 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Aquilino led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   September 11, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   September 18, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 21, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Lane, read a letter, (Attachment #1) responding
to a letter from President Duhl regarding holding regular council meetings on
General Election Tuesdays.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-19-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1009 Old Green Bay Road –
Adoption. After clarifying the measurement of the rear yards, noting that there
appeared to have been an administrative error when the structure was built,
Attorney Janega reviewed the options available to the Council. It can accept
the entire recommendation of the ZBA by granting the front setback variation
and denying the rear yard setback variation, requiring that the ordinance as
introduced be amended. Or, it can grant both setbacks, thereby rejecting the
ZBA’s recommendation as to the rear yard setback. This would require amending
the ordinance as introduced.
The applicant had no further comment.
Trustee Powell said that while he could see reasonableness to the rear
addition, he was bothered by compounding an existing mistake. He stated that,
therefore, he was in support of the Zoning Board’s recommendation to deny the
rear yard variance.
Trustee Woodbury said that in light of the fact that there were no new facts,
he was not inclined to overturn the Zoning Board’s recommendation.
Trustee Brower concurred, saying that he couldn’t discern any legitimate
hardship.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
introduced. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Harris Bank: 1070 Gage Street
Trustee Brower recused himself from discussion and vote on this matter.
   i)   Resolution No. R-30-2001 – Harris Bank Subdivision – Adoption. Manager
Williams reported that the Bank had clarified its position on green space by
committing to keep the present green space intact as long as the Bank owns the
property. He said that they were, however, uncomfortable with the inclusion of
any kind of restrictive covenant that might impact the ultimate resale of the
property
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt Resolution R-30-
2001 approving the subdivision of 1070 Gage and 928-930 Green Bay Road. By roll
call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Presser, Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   ii)   Ordinance No. M-20-2001 – Variation Request – Adoption. Trustee
Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Ordinance M-20-2001
approving the amended Special Use Permit and granting an intensity of lot
variation at 928-930 Green Bay Road. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Aquilino.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-21-2001 – Zoning Variation 503 Orchard Lane – Adoption.
The applicant, William Bartlett, explained that the variation was required to
accommodate his wife’s physical condition and would permit them to remain in
their residence and, thereby, in Winnetka.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt Ordinance M-21-2001
granting a variation for the property at 503 Orchard Lane. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-22-2001 – Purchase of 93 Green Bay Road – Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance
authorizing the purchase of this property. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower,
Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-23-2001 – Sale of Surplus Vehicles – Adoption. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt this ordinance authorizing
the sale of vehicles and leaf machines that have been replaced or have exceeded
their useful life through the Northwest Municipal Conference’s auction being
held on September 22nd.  Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-31-2001 – Elimination of Deposits – Adoption. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt this Resolution
amending the fees for construction by eliminating several of the permit deposit
requirements. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Powell, Woodbury, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
g)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2001 – New Liquor License: Glass Onion Café – Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt this ordinance
amending the Village Code to increase the number of A-1 liquor licenses to
five. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
h)   Ordinance No. M-24-2001 – Zoning Variation: 381 Poplar Street – Adoption.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the applicant is requesting variations from the
intensity of use of and front and sideyard setbacks to permit construction of
additions to the residence. He noted that in June of 2000, the Village Council
adopted an ordinance granting the same variations for the same proposed work,
However, due to delays in completing the construction drawings and obtaining
contractor bids, the applicants were unable to commence construction within the
required 12 months after the grant of the original variation. Mr. D’Onofrio
reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted four to zero to recommend
in favor of the variations.
Trustee Woodbury moved that introduction be waived. Trustee Presser seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt the ordinance granting the requested
variations. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough,
Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
i)   Resolution No. R-33-2001 – Boehrer Subdivision: 1320 Forest Glen Dr. -
Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request to subdivide the subject property in
order to accommodate an addition to the existing home that would comply with
the GFA requirements for the R-5 zoning district. He reported that after
hearing testimony at a Public Hearing, the Plan Commission had recommended
approval with certain conditions, including the following:
   (1)   A five (5) foot utility easement is provided along the north lot line
of the two lots, with the standard utility easement language shown on the plat.
   (2)   That if in the future one lot is sold separately from the other, the
existing accessory structure is relocated eliminating the existing 0.41-foot
encroachment.
   (3)   A restrictive covenant be placed on the plat for stormwater control
and drainage.
   (4)   Provide a signature block for Plan Commission approval.
   (5)   Provide a signature block for Community Development Director approval.
Mr. D’Onofrio mentioned that this property was the subject of a subdivision and
variation request in 1999 that was denied by the Council, and noted that both
lots would be buildable.
Mr. D’Onofrio and Ms. Janega also responded to questions from the Trustees
about the accessory structure, a playhouse.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Resolution R-33-
2001. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Noyes Appeal: Transformer Location. Mr. and Mrs. George Noyes, the new
owners of the property at 1555 Tower Road, requested that an electrical
transformer currently situated on the south side of a utility pole, facing
their property, be relocated to the north side.
Ms. Anne Stern, 958 Grove, the resident to the north side of the utility pole
in question stated her objections to the requested movement of the transformer.
She distributed pictures showing the view of the transformer from both
directions.
Manager Williams explained that the location of the transformer had been made
by administrative decision based upon its distance from the respective
properties. He mentioned the possibility of exploring a different color for the
transformer and discussed the Village’s procedures for undergrounding a
neighborhood’s electrical service.
Trustee Presser suggested that review of the administrative decision by the
Council at this time was inappropriate, unless required by the Code. Attorney
Janega said that it was not.
There was a consensus to take no action on this request, thereby, letting the
Manager’s decision stand, and to consider issues of transformer style and color
at another time.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1235 and 1236. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1235 in the amount of $556,593.57 and Warrant
List No 1236 in the amount of $248,119.45. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Bid No. 01-008: Spruce Street Storm Sewer Outfall Pier Removal and
Discharge Channel. Manager Williams briefly reviewed the history of this issue.
He explained that the project consists of the removal of a portion of the
existing, deteriorated storm sewer, the removal of the deteriorated pier, and
the construction of a stone outfall channel. He reported that the bids included
an option to provide construction of a revetment to stabilize the bluff within
the public right-of-way. Mr. Williams noted that staff’s recommendation is that
the bid be awarded to Kadinger Marine Service, Inc. for a base bid of $52,590,
plus $47,120 for the option, for a total sum of $99,710, which is substantially
under budget estimate.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported on a recent Insurance Service Office
evaluation of Winnetka’s Fire Protection. He noted that while the Village’s
overall fire protection rating has improved vis-à-vis the last evaluation, the
Village continues to be classified as Class 4.
Mr. Williams also displayed one of the Village’s historic minute books that has
recently been preserved. He noted that the Village hoped to continue the
preservation program until all of its historic records are appropriately
preserved.
b)   Village President. None.
c)   Trustees. Trustee Presser reported on a recent meeting of the Code Review
Committee.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, the possible purchase of property,
and personnel matters pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(5), and
Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 8:48 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:06 p.m. Present: President
Pro Tem Aquilino, Trustees, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Presser,
Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:07 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
September 18, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Adopted October 2, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 18, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve
Saunders, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Water &
Electric Bryan McInturff and about 40 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
Before leading the group in the Pledge of Allegiance, President Duhl called for
a moment of silence in remembrance of the victims of last Tuesday’s terrorists’
attack.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   October 2, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees, except Trustee
Presser, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 9, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 4, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Historical Society Museum – 411 Linden Street
a)   Staff Power Point Presentation. Manager Williams presented an analysis of
alternate sites for the Museum. His presentation consisted of a series of
slides that identified the key issues pertaining to possible relocation,
pictured the existing site and potential alternatives, and summarized the pros
and cons of each location. The presentation was accompanied by a detailed
written analysis of each site.
Prior to beginning the slide presentation, the Manager noted that the analysis
of the other sites was not required as part of the Historical Society’s case,
and that the study was made at the direction of the Council.
The following sites were analyzed in detail:
1.   Parking lot south of Village Hall (Village-owned)
2.   Parking lot at southeast corner of Pine and Birch (Village-owned)
3.   Sheridan Park (Village-owned)
4.   Library Park (Park District property)
5.   Hill Park (Park District property)
6.   Possible expansion of Elm Street train station (owned by railroad at
present)
Mr. Williams discussed the difficulties associated with moving the 411 Linden
structure, in addition to identifying a suitable location. He explained that,
due to the size of the house and the width of the streets, the house would have
to be cut into at least two, and possibly three, sections to move it. He added
that this would increase the costs and that he was not sure of how moving the
house in sections would affect the structural integrity of the house.
Mr. Williams also noted that all of the alternatives would require some zoning
relief and mentioned that a new building could be built at any of the
identified sites as an alternative to moving the 411 house. He also commented
on the possible future development of the Linden Avenue site if the house is
moved, as well as the future of the 411 Linden house if a new facility is built
for the Historical Society.
In summary, Mr. Williams pointed out that if a park is chosen, the Village
loses open space. If a parking lot is chosen, the Village loses valuable
parking. If the train station expansion is chosen, the Village loses parking
and possibly a business.
President Duhl thanked the Manager and staff for the analysis, noting that it
had raised additional questions for consideration. He said that the Trustees
would hold their comments until the next Council meeting, but invited members
of the audience to make any remarks that they thought relevant.
b)   Audience Comments
Becky Hurley, 1027 Spruce, speaking on behalf of the Historical Society, said
that at first blush the train station seems an exciting prospect, but also
raises questions about funding, timing, and the amount of work to be done.
Manager Williams concurred, but noted that the railroad is very motivated to
work with the Village to explore all options.
Joan Laffey, 320 Linden and Dan Kinsella, 420 Linden, suggested that the
Village ask the Park District to reconsider some of their other properties as
potential locations. Bill Gutekanst, 770 Cherry, agreed and proposed an
alternate site.
Elisha Gray, 672 Maple, Marshall Ash, 662 Maple, and Beverly Mekutichian, 714
Park Lane, all voiced objection to using Sheridan Park. They presented the
Trustees with a petition signed by 106 residents supporting that objection.
Luvie Myers, 432 Sunset Rd., asked whether using the train station would result
in a reconfiguration of Green Bay Road. Mr. Williams responded that there are
no plans to change the road.
Susan Curry, 821 Prospect, expressed concern about the amount of money involved
in implementing any of the alternatives.
Dan Kinsella, 420 Linden, thanked staff for the thorough analysis and inquired
about future plans for the parking lot to the south of Village Hall and the
construction of a parking deck by the Community House. He also asked whether
the Village had considered officially designating the 411 Linden house as a
landmark.
President Duhl responded that the Village’s Landmark Ordinance does not grant
the authority to save the house and that landmark designation would not
necessarily save it.
Ken Behles, 426 Maple, the architect for the Historical Society commented on
the difficulties in separating the house into sections in order to move it. He
said that moving the house may not be feasible because it is neither a simple,
nor an inexpensive project.
Nancy Judge, 534 Elder, the Society’s President, thanked Mr. Williams and staff
for their extensive analysis of alternative sites. She expressed the Society’s
desire to find a solution that is attractive to the Village as a whole and
asked for the full support and cooperation of as much of the community as
possible. She indicated that the Museum Board would meet to discuss and explore
the suggested alternatives
President Duhl thanked everyone for attending and invited all participants to
return on October 2nd to hear the Trustees’ comments and discussion.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish, 1074 Trapp Ln., commented on a rumor that the Landmark
Preservation Commission is seeking to certify the voluntary landmark ordinance
to establish a mandatory designation process.
President Duhl responded emphatically and unequivocally that the rumor is
unfounded and that there is no certification process that can change the
Village’s ordinance, which is totally voluntary. He said only the Council can
amend the ordinance and that there have been no such discussions.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution No. R-34-2001. – Certificate of Appropriateness: 452-456
Winnetka Avenue - Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for exterior
building alterations and related site improvements in conjunction with the
proposed renovation of vacant ground floor commercial space into office space.
He also reviewed action by various Village boards and mentioned that the
applicant has applied for a special use permit for the reconstruction and
expansion of surface parking and a zoning variation to permit a reduced parking
landscape buffer, to be discussed at a later meeting.
The applicant, Dave Kase, asked the Council for favorable consideration saying
that he hoped that they would see the proposed improvements as a great
enhancement to the building. He then responded to questions from Trustees
Powell and Woodbury about the planned renovations.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution
granting a Certificate of Appropriateness for building renovations. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Old Business.
None.
10. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1237 and 1238. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1237 in the amount of $728,690.22 and Warrant
List No 1238 in the amount of $568,287.08. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Telephone System Proposals. Mr. McInturff explained that the Village’s
telephone system is inadequate, obsolete, and provides poor service to both
employees and residents, pointing out some of its shortcomings. He noted that
it is also very expensive. He reviewed the bidding process, the bids and
staff’s recommendation, supported by the consultant’s, that the bid be awarded
to Midco, Inc., for the purchase of a Mitel telephone system for a maximum
expenditure of $235,000 total installed cost.
Mr. McInturff further explained that the request for proposals was sent to
seven vendors, but only two responded. He noted that the other bid was rejected
because it is a relatively new technology tied to the computer network, it does
not meet our current needs and was a higher cost alternative.
He noted that the installation of the new system would result in significant
savings to the Village over time.
President Duhl asked where the savings for the Mitel system would come from.
Mr. Baim, the Village’s consultant, explained that the major savings would come
from the cost of the lines. Presently, the Village has a very complex, unwieldy
system where every line is a premium Centrex line, which is very expensive.
Trustee Presser commented that he had received a call from the competitive
bidder expressing concern that the Village did not appreciate the future
advantages of its system. He stated, however, that Mr. McInturff and Mr. Baim’s
presentation made it clear that the risks of the competitor’s system are too
great and that, therefore, he was comfortable with staff’s recommendation.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Bid No. 01-016 – Precast Concrete Manholes. Mr. McInturff reviewed the
bids for three manholes to house underground switchgear for the Tower Road
Undergrounding Project. He cited staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded
to Universal Utility Supply in the amount of $34,650.00.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Document Preservation. Manager Williams explained that the Village has
begun a program to conserve its historic minute and ordinance books dating back
to the mid-1800’s. He said that three old volumes, had been satisfactorily
preserved by Brown’s River Preservation Services on a trial basis, and that
staff recommends continuing the program with the same vendor in order to insure
consistency. As the cost varies from book to book depending upon the condition
of the original document and the amount of work required, staff also recommends
that Council authorize the use of Brown’s River Records Preservation Services
for an amount not to exceed $25,000 per year. He pointed out that the 2001/2002
Budget includes $50,000 for document storage and preservation.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
11. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that in response to the terrorists’
attack on September 11th, the Village had brought in additional firefighters
and police officers to provide a presence. In addition, the Village
disconnected from the main power grid for a period of time and generated its
own power. He said that staff was also evaluating the security of all Village
operations.
b)   Village President. None.
c)   Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the Code Committee would not be
meeting this month due to a staff scheduling conflict.
Trustee Brower reported that he had attended recent meetings of the Chamber of
Commerce and the Winnetka Youth Organization.
Trustee Powell reported that the Northwest Municipal Conference had announced
its annual legislative awards, one of which will be going to Senator Kathy
Parker.
12. Appointments.
None.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Greenough moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
purchase of property pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section
2(c)(5), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:12 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:15 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:16 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
October 2, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved October 16, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 2, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Finance
Director Ed McKee and about 30 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   October 9, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   October 16, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 18, 2001, Regular Meeting. After Attorney Janega reviewed a
correction made to the distributed draft minutes, Trustee Brower moved to adopt
the minutes as redrafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
7. President’s Announcement.
President Duhl formally announced the retirement of Police Chief William
Gallagher, effective October 15, 2001. He thanked the Chief for his 30 years of
service to the Village and expressed regret at his departure. In doing so, he
noted a number of the Chief’s accomplishments, including obtaining
accreditation for the Department.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-25-2001 – Zoning Variation: 266 Linden Street -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from the maximum
building size to permit the construction of a one-story addition to a legally
non-conforming structure. He explained that the proposed addition would amount
to 244.56 square feet of gross floor area, comprised of (a) enclosure of
existing exterior stairs at the north side of the existing residence, (b) mud-
room expansion, (c) a roofed porch addition at the rear, and (d) a family room
addition.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had given a favorable
recommendation only to the stair enclosure and associated mudroom expansion, a
total of 80.31 square feet.
Attorney Janega clarified that the ordinance as drafted would grant the mud-
room expansion and stairway enclosure, but not the other requested variations.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
as drafted granting a Gross Floor Area variation. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
b)   Ordinance No. M-26-2001 – Zoning Variation: 649 Locust Street –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a side yard variation to
permit the addition of 15 inches to the height of the roof parapet on a wall
that is in a nonconforming location. He explained that permitted work is
currently taking place on the property but that the newly proposed extension of
the parapet wall has not been approved under the current building permit,
because of the legally nonconforming side yard. The current request is made in
order to change the pitch of the existing flat roof on the southerly section of
the home, where water pools and causes damage to the house. He noted that the
Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend approval of the variation.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting a side yard variation. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. M-27-2001- Special Use Permit and Zoning Variation: 452-458
Winnetka Avenue/82-86 Tempel Court – Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a
request for a Special Use Permit to permit the reconstruction and expansion of
surface parking at the subject property. The applicant plans to convert the
first floor of the building into shared office units. The residential units on
the upper floors would remain. They also propose to expand the existing parking
lot to the west of the building to contain a total of 39 spaces. He pointed out
that, although the number of parking spaces would be increased by ten, the
parking standard of 2-1/4 spaces per residential dwelling unit would not be
met, but commented that this standard does not apply since the building was
constructed before the standard was enacted. He noted that no off-street
parking is required for the non-residential uses located at street level. Mr.
D’Onofrio reported that the applicant plans to institute some kind of shared
parking arrangement between the residential and commercial users and had agreed
not to lease any spaces to high school students.
Mr. D’Onofrio further explained that a landscaping variation is requested to
allow a reduced landscape buffer along the east and west property lines with
parking to be located within one (1) foot of the property line, whereas a
landscape buffer of five (5) feet is required. The variation would allow the
configuration of the parking lot to be maximized.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Plan Commission had voted unanimously to find
the proposed special use consistent with the Winnetka 2020 Comprehensive Plan,
and that the Design Review Board had issued favorable comment on the revised
plans and the landscape plan, as well as related exterior building alterations.
He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had also voted to recommend
approval of the variation and the special use permit, but recommended that the
parking lot be shifted to the west so that the full five-foot landscape buffer
could be maintained along the adjacent single-family properties.
In response to questions from Trustee Woodbury and President Duhl, the
applicant, Dave Kase, explained his plans for shared parking, citing experience
with a similar development in Wilmette as an example.
Trustee Powell requested an explanation of the stormwater drainage plans using
an elevation of the plat of survey. Desiree Dolan from Dolan Engineering
described the detention plan uses oversized underground pipes and flow controls
to discharge into the Village’s storm sewer. She noted that there will be
considerably less run off than there is now.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that a prohibition against leasing parking to NTHS
students be incorporated into the ordinance. The other Trustees concurred. Mr.
Woodbury also strongly recommended that the applicant designate more than just
a few spaces for residential parking and that the special use permit be
conditional upon the provision of a specific number or percentage. He asked
that Mr. Kase present the Council with a more finite proposal of the parking
space distribution.
President Duhl suggested that the Council introduce the ordinance as drafted
and that the parties work to achieve a mutually agreeable resolution before
adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting a Special Use Permit and landscape variation. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution No. R-35-2001 – Budget Amendment for G&W Purchase – Adoption.
Mr. McKee explained that this resolution would amend the 2001-2002 budget by
adding a new line item specifically authorizing the purchase of 93 Green Bay
Road for $650,000 because the current budget does not contain a category
authorizing the purchase of real estate.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
9. Old Business.
a)   411 Linden: Historical Society
Before soliciting comments from the Trustees, President Duhl called for
comments from the audience regarding alternative sites for the Historical
Society.
Tom Hulick, 410 Linden, a new resident to the neighborhood, voiced opposition
to the Society’s plan, stating it would have a negative impact on the immediate
neighborhood.
Marjorie Webster expressed her opposition to the use of any of the Village’s
open space.
President Duhl first solicited comments from the Trustees regarding the option
of moving the 411 Linden house. He referred them to a matrix prepared by the
Village Attorney summarizing land ownership, special uses, lot sizes, parking,
and other aspects of each alternative site. Ms. Janega noted that any of the
alternative locations would require a special use permit. Therefore, should
this option be chosen, the Historical Society would be required to file a new
zoning application with the Village.
Trustee Powell voiced his belief that staff’s analysis had made it abundantly
clear that from both a financial and a structural standpoint it is impractical
to move the house.
Trustee Greenough added that it is not a good way to treat an historical
structure unless there was an extraordinary advantage to one of the alternative
locations and that none of the alternatives offered such an advantage.
Trustees Brower, Aquilino, and Woodbury concurred.
President Duhl then asked the Trustees their thoughts about the Elm Street
train station alternative.
The Trustees agreed that, while the concept and location has merit, there are
too many unknowns, i.e., time frame, cost, design, to make it a feasible
option.
President Duhl stated that, since the alternative sites have been ruled out,
the Council should consider the Society’s application, and suggested that they
discuss it in detail at the next study session.
Therefore, Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to remove the
Historical Society’s application from the table. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously. President Duhl then announced that the Council would
discuss the application at the Study Session on October 9th and asked Attorney
Janega to prepare an analysis of the issues requiring Council consideration in
conjunction with the special use permit and variation.
10. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1239 and 1240. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1239 in the amount of $1,089,946.97 and
Warrant List No 1240 in the amount of $366,241.99. Trustee Greenough seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
b)   Bid No. 01-008: Spruce Street Storm Sewer Outfall Change Order. Ms. Janega
explained that at a pre-construction meeting with the contractor and the
Village’s engineering consultant, it had been determined that additional
protection to the stone channel should be provided during construction. She
stated that staff had also determined that additional materials were needed to
construct the proposed revetment across the full width of the right-of-way. She
added that the need for the changes was confirmed by the Village’s consulting
engineer. These two changes require additional quantities of materials, which,
in turn, increase the total cost of the project by $18,426.00.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the change
order as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Presser.
c)   Property Tax Levy. Mr. McKee reviewed the three steps taken to levy
property taxes for 2001. He explained that during the 2001/2002 budget process,
the property tax levy for the Village was set at a 3.5% increase plus the
effect of new development. This would increase the Village portion of an
existing taxpayer’s bill 3.5% or the rate of inflation, whichever is less. He
noted that a homeowner with a $10,000 tax bill would see an increase of about
$60.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to set the 2001 property
tax levy amount, net of abatements, at $9,419,625.00. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
d)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. President Duhl referred the
Trustees to the reports in their agenda packets filed by the Department Heads,
and Ms. Janega pointed out some of the key items.
11. Reports.
a)   Manager. Ms. Janega, as Acting Village Manager, reported that next week is
Fire Prevention Week and that in recognition thereof, the Fire Department would
be holding its annual open house. She noted that she had given the firefighters
permission to collect funds to benefit the families of firefighters killed by
the terrorist attack at the open house and in the parking lot of Grand Foods.
Trustees. Trustee Aquilino reported that the Beautification Committee was
nearing a decision on treatment for the guardrail along Green Bay Road that
they hoped would be completed in the spring.
12. Appointments.
None.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and the possible
purchase of property pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section
2(c)(5), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Presser. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:55 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:50 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Woodbury. Absent:
Trustee Presser. Also present, Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
The meeting adjourned at 9:51 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
October 9, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved November 6, 2001)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and
approximately 30 persons in the audience.
2. Winnetka Historical Society: Discussion of Application for 411 Linden Site.
President Duhl noted that, after thorough study, the Council had decided to
focus directly on the Historical Society’s application and asked Village
Attorney Janega to discuss the procedural and policy issues before the Council.
Ms. Janega stated that a series of public hearings before advisory boards and
commissions; supplementary presentations regarding key issues; and explorations
of alternative sites led up to this final discussion and determination on the
application. She then explained the main policy issues before the Council, the
first being whether the proposed use is one of the enumerated special uses in
the zoning district regulations, i.e., library. If it is not, the next issue is
whether it is a non-essential public use? This issue would also lead to the
Council’s consideration of whether it should amend the Zoning Ordinance to
clarify the non-essential public use provision. She went on to say that if the
Council decides that the use is permissible, whether by right or as a non-
essential public use, then the Council must proceed to evaluate the criteria
for issuance of a Special Use Permit and judge whether the Society has met the
criteria of the Zoning Ordinance. If the Council’s findings are favorable, it
would then determine what, if any, conditions should be imposed upon the
issuance of the Special Use Permit. The final issue is the Society’s variation
request.
President Duhl asked Attorney Janega to address the threshold legal issue
raised by the Objectors of whether or not the Historical Society is eligible
for a Special Use under the Village’s Zoning Ordinance and whether or not the
Council has the authority to make that determination.
Ms. Janega stated that she has concluded that the Zoning Ordinance, as it
presently stands, authorizes the Council to grant the Special Use Permit being
requested, explaining the definitions of “essential public service” and
“essential public use.” She then used those defined terms to explain that
reading the Zoning Ordinance as a whole shows that “non-essential public use”
means any public use that is provided by anyone other than the Village, any
Village service that is not an essential public service, and any essential
public service that is not provided from Village owned and operated land or
buildings. In closing remarks, she recommended that the Council consider
amending the Zoning Ordinance to remove any question about its authority to
grant such a Special Use Permit.
President Duhl then polled the Trustees for their conclusions.
Trustee Presser said that he was uncomfortable with the library concept and
felt that the non-essential public use definition was in keeping with the
purpose and intent of the Zoning Ordinance. He also indicated that he was
disinclined to draft anything new and special.
President Duhl agreed, but pointed out that, just because the Council was
discussing this issue affirmatively, it did not mean that the other issues had
been prejudged favorably because they are separate and distinct from one
another. He then asked Ms. Janega how the ordinance could be amended to clarify
the meaning.
Attorney Janega explained two alternatives, either to add “local historical
society” to the list of permitted special uses in the R-5 district, or to
clarify that a non-essential public use may be owned by a public body or by a
private person or entity. She added that she did not recommend adding a new
special use to the R-5 district or creating a specific list of “non-essential
public uses,” stating that she believed it is important to preserve the
Council’s right to consider each application on a case-by-case basis.
Ms. Janega mentioned that one of the strongest features in support of
identifying the Society as a nonessential public use is the service that it
does, in fact, provide to the community. She also noted that the Illinois
Property Tax Code allows abatement of taxes for qualified tax exempt historical
societies, which affirms that the state legislature recognizes the public
benefit provided by these entities. She also noted that the history of the
relationship between the Society and Village indicates that the Village
recognizes it as a public service, because it was the Village that leased the
Society’s prior location at Skokie School and the Village still appropriates
funds for the Society.
Trustee Powell agreed with Trustee Presser. He also indicated that he saw no
particular reason to amend the Zoning Ordinance because to his thinking, it is
already perfectly clear that non-essential public uses are to be determined by
the Village Council.
Trustee Aquilino concurred.
Trustees Brower and Greenough also expressed discomfort in treating the
Historical Society as a library, but also spoke in favor of correcting any
ambiguity.
Trustee Woodbury said he could support an amendment for purposes of
clarification but felt strongly that case-by-case consideration is an essential
element.
Manager Williams asked Ms. Janega her opinion as to the safest course of action
in light of threatened litigation.
Acknowledging that the lack of a satisfactory definition was one of the reasons
that none had been included in the Zoning Ordinance, Ms. Janega said that, if
satisfactory language can be crafted, it would be best to amend the ordinance.
She explained that any litigation would be decided upon the basis of the
ordinance in effect at the time of consideration by the courts.
President Duhl summarized the Trustees’ comments. He noted a general consensus
that the Historical Society is not a library, but does qualify as a non-
essential public use within the context of the present ordinance. Additionally,
if appropriate language can be drafted to clarify that a public use need not
have anything to do with public ownership of property, the Trustees agreed that
the Zoning Ordinance should be amended. He asked Attorney Janega to prepare a
draft text amendment for consideration at a later meeting, but noted that it
was not necessary for continuing consideration or action on the application at
issue.
President Duhl then turned Council’s attention to consideration of whether or
not the Society has met the standards for issuance of a Special Use Permit, and
Attorney Janega led a discussion of the seven standards, reviewing them one at
a time, as follows:
   a)   Would the Special Use Permit be substantially injurious to single
family residential uses or substantially diminish or impair property values?
   b)   Would the Special Use Permit impede the normal and orderly development
or improvement of other properties in the immediate vicinity?
   c)   Have adequate measures been taken to provide ingress and egress in a
manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular congestion in the public ways?
   d)   Is the proposed parking adequate
   e)   Do utilities, access roads, drainage and other facilities necessary to
the operation of the special use, or will be provided?
   f)   Is the Special Use endanger, or is it detrimental to the public health,
safety, comfort, morals, or general welfare?
   g)   Will the Special Use in all other respects conform to the applicable
regulations of the Zoning Ordinance and other Village ordinances and codes?
In discussing the above, Ms. Janega reviewed evidence contained in the record
regarding details of the Historical Society’s operation, hours, frequency of
events, number of employees, etc. In doing so, she pointed out that the Society
proposed to maintain the single family appearance and design of the building,
which in and of itself limits the numbers and types of activities that can go
on there. She also pointed to the Society’s formalized role in demolition
process, its history of service to and support from the community, and its
recognition in the Comprehensive Plan.
Trustee Brower inquired whether most of the perceived negative impacts couldn’t
be dealt with by restrictions. Ms. Janega said that they could.
Trustee Woodbury commented that ‘use and enjoyment’ and ‘impairment’ are two
separate issues, but concluded that in his opinion, nothing in the record
indicates that the Society plans anything more than modest use of the property.
He said that he sees no evidence to indicate that this use would negatively
impact the use and enjoyment of neighboring property owners.
Trustee Greenough commented that she saw no evidence to indicate that the
proposed use would be substantially different from that of a family in the
house.
Trustee Brower commented that there ought to be some way to monitor what goes
on over time.
Trustee Powell spoke in support of restrictions on usage, particularly parking.
He advocated restricting usage to low intensity, but providing for some
flexibility in order to meet changing needs of the Society.
Trustee Presser voiced concern over the impact issue, questioning whether the
fact that there isn’t going to be a family living in the house doesn’t
fundamentally alter the nature of the neighborhood. He indicated his belief
that carefully calibrated restrictions can maximize the chances of the
Society’s being a good neighbor, but is impossible to insure.
President Duhl summarized the consensus among the Trustees that the Special Use
would not substantially injure the use and enjoyment of other property owners.
In commenting on whether there would be any impairment to property values, Ms.
Janega referred to analyses by both the Society’s and the Objector’s
appraisers. She said that the issue revolved around determination of the
credibility of appraisals and the quality of the evidence presented. She
pointed out that although each appraiser used a different methodology, both
acknowledged that there is no comparable use in the Village, and neither
appraiser presented evidence that there would, in fact, be a negative impact.
She noted that the Objector’s appraiser questioned the methodology applied by
the Society’s appraiser, but did not refute his findings. Finally, she noted
that, if there has been no long-term negative impact on neighboring property
values from even very intense usage of an institutional nature, it follows that
there will not be a substantial impact in this instance.
Trustee Presser commented that based upon recent sales records, there appears
to have been no significant negative impact to value.
Trustee Aquilino remarked that the Society’s appraiser’s approach seemed
reasonable and that the Objectors’ appraiser does not disprove his findings.
Trustees Brower, Powell, Greenough and Woodbury agreed.
There were no comments indicating that any of the Trustees felt that the
Special Use would impede normal and orderly development or improvement of
neighboring property, with the proviso that restrictions be imposed.
With regard to access to the property, Ms. Janega noted that although there is
a limited amount of parking, the manner of ingress and egress is consistent
with that of a residential neighborhood, minimizing vehicular traffic in the
public ways. She commented that there might be somewhat more pedestrian traffic
if there is off-site parking, especially for events, but pointed out that the
driveway will be open for visitor parking, rather than for employee parking.
Trustee Woodbury inquired about the society’s proposal to widen the driveway.
Given the neighbors’ negative response that this would transform the appearance
from that of a residential driveway to a long, narrow parking lot, the Trustees
agreed to keep that proposal off the table. They did, however, agree that
parking restrictions need to be imposed. Trustee Woodbury suggested that
employees be required to park off-site as a condition of the Special Use
Permit. Trustees Powell and Brower expressed the desire to see parking subject
to annual review and oversight.
Attorney Janega returned to the standard of general impact. She explained that,
if the Council finds that there are no insurmountable negatives, that there is
a public benefit, and that conditions can be imposed to offset any negative
consequences, then it could find that there is no detriment to the public
health, safety, comfort, morals, or general welfare. The Trustees had no
dissenting comments.
Finally, Ms. Janega addressed the standard of whether the Special Use would in
all other respects conform to the applicable regulations of the Zoning
Ordinance and other ordinances and codes. She said that, once enacted, the
Special Use Permit with any conditions imposed would be in conformity with all
other Village requirements.
Trustee Presser confirmed that if the conditions are not adhered to by the
Historical Society, the Council has the authority to withdraw the Special Use
Permit. Ms. Janega responded that the option to withdraw the permit is
incorporated as one of the conditions of all special use permits.
Noting that there was a consensus among the Trustees that the standards for the
Special Use Permit, subject to certain conditions, would be met, President Duhl
directed the discussion to the conditions to be imposed in order to complete
the analysis of the Special Use Permit. He referred to the four general
categories of conditions: (1) maintaining the residential appearance of the
building and subject property; (2) reducing the impact of the Society’s
operations on the adjoining residential properties; (3) providing a formalized
channel of communication between the Society and the neighbors; and (4)
providing for the evaluation of the impact after a fixed period of time.
The Trustees turned first to a discussion of lighting. Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Brower, and Presser expressed their belief that exterior lighting
should be kept to residential standards and be required to be extinguished by a
specific hour. Trustee Greenough contended that low level lighting can be
attractive and improves security. She and Trustee Woodbury suggested that the
issues of lighting and signage could be left to the Design Review Board for
determination, although Trustee Woodbury voiced a strong opposition to any kind
of lighting on signage, as did Trustees Presser and Brower. President Duhl said
that he believed that, because these are policy issues of concern to the
neighborhood, the Council needs to make its position on them clear.
The topic of signage was discussed next. Trustee Aquilino indicated that the
issue of signage in general had been discussed at some length in Beautification
Committee meetings, where the consensus was that there are already too many
signs in the Village and that signage ought to be eliminated or made to conform
to minimal standards. She added that the style of a sign is more important than
not having one and indicated that she thought some kind of identifying sign was
desirable for the Historical Society. 
Trustee Presser expressed a strong belief that any signage should be right on
the building so that it does not detract from the residential appearance.
Trustees Powell and Brower agreed.
Trustee Greenough suggested a low, horizontal sign, set back discreetly on the
property. Trustees Woodbury and Aquilino concurred that this would be possible.
President Duhl asked the Society to return with a specific sign design so that
one of the Trustees might be persuaded to change his/her position.
There was a consensus among the Trustees that any proposed outside changes to
the structure be required to go before the Design Review Board.
There was also a consensus that the Special Use Permit be subject to the
condition that the Society never exceed gross floor area limitations. Mr.
Williams commented that this is the key to maintaining residential scale and is
the primary way in which the Village can control any similar future
applications.
The Trustees then discussed limiting the Society’s hours of operation. It was
agreed that there should be no limitation on staff hours, but that there should
be restrictions as to public hours. Trustee Powell commented that the Society’s
concept is predicated on light usage of the facility and that to allow
considerable usage would be contrary to use proposed. He suggested converting
to a specific total number of hours rather than specific daily hours so as to
give the Society some flexibility. After an extensive discussion, it was
concluded that the Special Use Permit should be conditioned upon public usage
of an average of fourteen (14) hours per week, with no more than sixty-four
(64) hours in any given month.
The Trustees also agreed to require the Society to hold its annual meeting off
the premises, but they were not inclined to limit Board or Executive Board
meetings. They also agreed that there should be a prohibition on non-Historical
Society events and outdoor events not in keeping with the residential character
of the neighborhood. In addition, they agreed that an evening curfew of 11:00
p.m. for any public event was reasonable and that there could be no parking of
buses delivering students along Linden Street.
The Trustees agreed not to specifically limit the number of Sunday afternoon
programs, so long as they fall within the Society’s regularly scheduled, hours
of operation. It also agreed not to limit the Society to a specific number of
exhibit openings within a given time frame.
With regard to parking, it was agreed that the Society’s personnel should be
required to park off site during hours the Society is open to the public, but
that the driveway may be used at other times.
Finally, the Trustees agreed that the Society should be required to designate
some individual as a liaison between itself, the neighbors, and the Village,
and to notify all parties on a monthly basis of scheduled events. In addition
to which, the Society’s Special Use Permit would be subject to review and
modification by the Council should problems evolve.
Lastly, the Council turned to consideration of the requested zoning variation.
Mr. Norkus explained that the variation is necessitated by the Society’s
proposal to attach the garage to the house. Ms. Janega noted that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had considered this matter and forwarded a favorable
recommendation.
After a brief discussion, the Council decided that the standards for the
variance were met and that the proposed use is consistent with the mission of
the Historical Society.
President Duhl directed Attorney Janega to prepare a draft ordinance granting
the Special Use Permit and zoning variance as well as a draft text amendment
clarifying the meaning of a nonessential public use.
3. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:00 p.m.
October 16, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Adopted November 6, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 16, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent:.None. Also present: Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and about 36 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   November 6, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   November 13, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. President Duhl introduced Deputy Police Chief Eric Bennett, who will be
serving as the Village’s Interim Chief while the Village Manager conducts a
national search for a replacement for Chief Gallagher.
6. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 2, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish commented on the progress of the installation of benches at
the Village’s train stations. She also inquired whether a meeting scheduled
among the various Village board presidents and the boards’ chief operating
officers constituted a public meeting. Both President Duhl and Attorney Janega
informed her that it is not a meeting, as defined in the Open Meetings Act and,
therefore, is not subject to that Act.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-25-2001 – Zoning Variation: 266 Linden Street - Adoption.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a GFA variation to allow for a stair enclosure addition. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None
b)   Ordinance No. M-26-2001 – Zoning Variation: 649 Locust Street – Adoption.
Trustee Presser , seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a side yard variation. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and
Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-27-2001- Special Use Permit and Zoning Variation: 452-458
Winnetka Avenue/82-86 Tempel Court – Adoption. Attorney Janega briefly
summarized action on this issue and reviewed recent correspondence from the
applicant agreeing to give residents the first priority on parking. She also
commented on amendments drafted into the ordinance as a result of Council’s
discussion at the last meeting.
Trustee Woodbury again urged that the ordinance specify a designated number of
spaces for residential parking. He voiced concern that the number of vehicles
associated with office tenants will exceed that predicted by the applicant,
resulting in residents being forced on to the street where there is no parking
available.
Dave Kase, the applicant, expressed confidence in their projections and
reiterated his commitment to make residential parking the first priority. He
noted that at present no shared parking program has been instituted, but that
there will be numbered, assigned spaces, with specific passes issued for them.
Trustees Aquilino and Greenough voiced concern that resident assigned spaces
might sit unutilized during the day and expressed the belief that market
pricing would probably take care of any problems. Most of the Trustees
concurred.
Following further discussion about whether spaces should be reserved, Attorney
Janega suggested deleting the condition requiring spaces to be reserved for
patrons and guests. She also suggested inserting a new Section 5, to add a
condition to the Special Use Permit reserving the right of the Council to
reexamine and modify the parking conditions in the future if experience makes
that necessary.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance as
shown in the agenda materials, with further changes as discussed. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to the ordinance
granting a Special Use Permit and landscape variation. By roll vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-36-2001 – Remote Parking Lot Fees – Adoption. Manager
Williams explained that this resolution would permit the Village Manager to
issue permits to allow parking on a limited basis at the Village’s landfill
site by businesses located in the Village. He said that the number of spaces
and areas used will not interfere with the Village’s use of the site. The
resolution establishes a semi-annual charge per vehicle of $120.00. He noted
that this is below the threshold that triggers the Cook County Parking Tax.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, Brower, Presser and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2001 – Amends Zoning Ordinance as it Pertains to Non-
essential Public Uses – Policy Direction. Attorney Janega explained the genesis
of the term “non-essential public uses.” She said that this preliminary draft
ordinance had been prepared at Council’s direction after the October 9th study
session, to clarify the use of the term by adding a definition of the term to
the Zoning Ordinance. She noted that amending the Zoning Ordinance requires a
public hearing, which historically has been conducted by the Village Council,
sitting as a Committee of the Whole. She reported that notice would be
published for such a hearing to be held on November 6, 2001, should the Council
decide to proceed with considering the amendment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to designate the Village
Council, sitting as a Committee of the Whole, as the body before which the
proposed zoning amendment should be heard and to set the hearing date for
Tuesday, November 6, 2001. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Ordinance No. M-28-2001 – Special Use Permit & Variation: 411 Linden –
Introduction.
Attorney Janega reviewed the draft ordinance prepared after the October 9th
study session at which Council determined that the Historical Society is
eligible to apply for a special use permit and that it had met the standards
for both the special use permit and the requested rear yard setback variation.
She explained that the draft contains an extensive recital of the Council’s
findings, grants the special use and variation, and imposes several conditions,
as discussed on October 9th. In addition, it provides for ongoing evaluation of
the impact and reserves the right of the Council to modify and revise those
conditions from time to time as necessitated by experience.
President Duhl indicated that the Council would discuss the draft an item at a
time with the goal of introducing an orally amended draft at this meeting. He
pointed out that there will be a new draft, incorporating comments made this
evening, for review at the next meeting. Mr. Duhl invited the audience to make
remarks about the conditions as the discussion progressed.
President Duhl then led a discussion of the conditions, one at a time.
There were no comments with regard to the construction timeframe.
After extensive discussion about exterior lighting and signage, it was decided
to have both aspects be subject to final review by the Village Council after
consideration and recommendation by the Design Review Board. It was agreed that
the condition that exterior security lighting be consistent with that of a
single-family residence and be operated by motion sensors and/or timers, and
the prohibition against off-premises signs should remain as directives to the
Design Review Board. A majority of the Trustees felt that lighting should not
be left on all night, and all agreed that any signage should be inconspicuous.
With regard to requiring the Historical Society to stay within the maximum
gross floor area for the R-5 zoning district, there was agreement that this was
a necessary condition in order to preserve the residential character of the
neighborhood. Ms. Janega explained the effect of the condition, noting that an
insignificant change to the building could be made without amending the special
use. However, if some modification were proposed that would take the building
over the gross floor area limitation, then the Society would not only have to
file for a variance, as any owner would, it would also be required to apply for
an amendment to the special use, thereby imposing a two-step requirement. She
noted that this provides an extra layer of protection to the residential
neighborhood.
Manager Williams pointed out that the Village would be allowing a new
institutional use in a residential neighborhood and stressed that GFA is the
key zoning tool for ensuring that a building is in harmony with its
neighborhood. He said that it was extremely important that the Village also
establish the precedent of requiring any such use to conform to GFA
requirements should there be similar applications in the future.
President Duhl concurred, noting that, historically, GFA variations are the
most difficult to obtain.
Residents who commented on these issues were John Sobol, 428 Linden, and
Kimberly Brya, 1097 Cherry St.
The Trustees supported the prohibition against private events hosted by a third
party.
Discussion followed about what constituted an outdoor event in keeping with the
single-family residential character of the neighborhood. Emphasizing that the
Council is committed to preserving the residential character of the
neighborhood, Trustee Presser urged reasonableness and pointed out that the
ordinance contains a mechanism to revisit the special use permit after a year.
President Duhl said that time would tell what is “reasonable” within the
context of a single-family neighborhood, and the Trustees agreed that this
condition could stand, as drafted.
With regard to the hours of operation and curfew, Ms. Janega explained that the
hours noted in the draft had come out of last week’s meeting, at which the
hours proposed by the Historical Society, 3 hrs. per day, 12 hrs. per week, had
been expanded marginally to permit the Society to have some scheduling
flexibility. After a lengthy discussion over what constituted “public” hours,
the Trustees decided to keep the maximum number of hours as specified in the
draft and to have the limit apply to public hours, which would not include
board meetings.
Regarding the curfew provisions, the Council determined that the curfew should
remain at 11:00 p.m. for Friday and Saturday nights, but that the weeknight
curfew should be changed to 10:30 p.m.
As to parking, the Trustees agreed that the driveway should not be widened,
that two staff cars should be allowed to park in the driveway during non-public
hours, and that, during public hours, employees should be required to park in
an off-site lot parking lot. In addition, buses will only be allowed to drop
visitors off and would be required to leave the area until time for pick up.
All of the Trustees agreed that the Historical Society should designate someone
to be a liaison with the neighbors, being those within 250’ of the subject
property, and that the Society should make a monthly calendar of events
available.
Finally, the Trustees approved the concept of reviewing the use of the property
in January, 2003.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the draft of
ordinance M-28-2001, as orally amended. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
9. Old Business.
None.
10. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1241 and 1242. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1241 in the amount of $615,258.18 and Warrant
List No 1242 in the amount of $400,585.92. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Tower Road Engineering Contract. Mr. Saunders explained that as a result
of dividing the Tower Road Project into two phases, the consultant had been
required to produce additional contract documents and plans. This resulted in
an expense for additional time, increasing the fee cap from $66,410 to $73,210.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the new fee cap.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Reconstruction Contract Change Order. Mr. Saunders explained that during
the replacement phase of the work on Ash Street from Maple to Sheridan, it had
been determined that an additional section of storm sewer was in disrepair and
required replacement, work that is not included in the existing contract. The
cost, based on unit prices contained in the bid, for the extra work is $10,177.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the change
order to the 2001 Street Reconstruction Project in the amount of $10,177. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Rock Salt Bid. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village purchases its rock
salt through the Sate of Illinois bid. He said that the estimate for the coming
winter season is 1,500 tons, with the Village being obligated to purchase at
least 70% of the estimated amount. The successful bidder for the State of
Illinois contract is IMC/North American Salt Co., for a price of $30.77 per
ton.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the purchase of
salt from IMC/North American at $30.77 per ton. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Bid: Street Technician Truck. Mr. Saunders reviewed a bid for the purchase
of a new truck to be used by the street technician. It is equipped to serve for
sign installation, replacement, and striping operations, as well as snow
plowing. Mr. Saunders explained how the purchase would be financed and
recommended buying a 2001 Ford chassis through the State of Illinois
Cooperative Purchasing Program for the amount of $26,585.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the purchase
in the amount of $26,585. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Policy Direction: Which Board/Commission to Hold Public Hearing on
Rezoning Request for 705 Oak Street – Hadley School for the Blind. Mr. Norkus
reviewed an application from The Hadley School for the Blind to rezone its
property at 705 Oak Street from B-1 Multi-Family Residential to R-5 Single
Family Residential. He explained that the rezoning request is based on the
applicant’s desire to construct a new single family residence on the property
to serve as a home for the school’s president. He outlined the land use and
zoning issues that will need to be considered as a result of the request and
requested that in accordance with the Village Code, the Village Council
designate the body to hold the requisite public hearing.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to designate the Plan
Commission as the body to conduct the hearing.
11. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Chamber of Commerce had made
arrangements for this year’s holiday lighting.
Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that there would be no Code Committee
meeting this month.
12. Appointments.
President Duhl recommended the appointment of Jane Dowding to complete the term
of Jim Sheridan on the Zoning Board of Appeals and then serve a full term on
her own. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the
appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
He also suggested that Mary Robbins be reappointed to that committee for a
second full term. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to
approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Finally, Mr. Duhl suggested the reappointment of James Fox as Chairman of the
Forestry Commission for an additional year. Trustee Presser, seconded by
Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 10:58 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:26 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:27 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
November 6, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved November 20, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 6, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, and
about 52 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that in consideration for the audience waiting to
testify at the Public Hearing, the Council would consider Agenda Item 8,
Ordinances and Resolutions, before Items 4, 5, 6, & 7.
4. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-28-2001 – Special Use Permit & Variation: 411 Linden –
Review of Additional Amendments. Attorney Janega reviewed the drafted
amendments to the ordinance that was introduced at the last meeting. She noted
that the revisions modify the recitals as well as the conditions for the
requested zoning relief to reflect Council’s discussion at that time.
John Kessler, 860 Oak St., inquired whether the Historical Society had
considered the Lloyd House at Birch and Cherry. Joan Evanich said that it had,
but that the parties had been unable to reach mutually agreeable terms. She
indicated that the property was in trust with a foundation being operated by
Mrs. Lloyd’s daughter.
George Noyes, 1555 Tower Rd., urged the Council to proceed favorably.
Faith Orlebeke, 1390 Scott, and Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry, also spoke in support of
the Historical Society.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the amendments as
drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2001 – Amends Zoning Ordinance as it Pertains to
Nonessential Public Uses – Public Hearing and Introduction. President Duhl
opened the Public Hearing on the ordinance amending the Zoning Ordinance to
define the term “nonessential public use” and clarifying the section pertaining
to Special Use Permits for nonessential public uses. In doing so, he reiterated
the Village’s interpretation that this amendment does not create any new rights
that have not existed for some time.
Mr. Williams, the Village Clerk, asked all persons who intended to testify to
stand and then swore in all those who so identified themselves as witnesses.
Attorney Janega reviewed the draft of Ordinance MC-10-2001 and its recital of
the history of the terms "essential public use” and “nonessential public use.”
She explained that the term “nonessential public use” had been added to the
Zoning Ordinance in 1997 as part of an enactment establishing standards and
prioritizing locations for wireless telecommunications facilities. At that
time, the terms “essential public services” and “essential public uses” were
defined and made permitted uses, rather than special uses, in all zoning
districts. Ms. Janega also referred to agenda materials pertaining to the 1997
“essential use” amendment and noted that the only change to the special use
provisions that was intended by that amendment was to carve essential public
uses as an exception to the special use process that applied to uses affected
with the public interest under in what was then Section 22.13(b) of the Zoning
Ordinance. She added that there was no intent to change any other application
of that section, which is now Section 17.56.010(B) and that “nonessential
public uses” were not intended to be limited to uses owned by public bodies
because the pre-amendment reference to “public uses” was not subject to that
limitation. She also cited statutory language supporting her interpretation,
and explained that the purpose of the amendment proposed by Ordinance MC-10-
2001 is to clarify the Council’s intent and to remove any questions about the
meaning of the term “nonessential public uses.”
Dan Kinsella, 420 Linden, expressed his belief that the amendment is a bad idea
for the community. He voiced concerns about the constitutionality of Council’s
actions and suggested that enacting the ordinance sets a dangerous precedent in
that it opens the door for institutional uses to encroach into residential
neighborhoods. Mr. Kinsella indicated that regardless of Council’s decision on
this amendment, there will be litigation over the issue of the Historical
Society’s presence at the Linden Avenue site.
Brian Balusek, 390 Linden, and Bill Gutekanst, 770 Cherry, and John Oerlerich,
565 Arbor Vitae, supported Mr. Kinsella.
Robert Lapp, 414 Linden, cited Barrington as an example of what can happen when
a historical museum is located in an older, residential neighborhood, resulting
in the neighboring homes being converted into commercial property. He urged the
Council to leave the ordinance as it stands and not adopt the proposed
amendment. Both he and Mr. Kinsella cited Council’s representations at previous
meetings that this amendment need not be enacted in order for the Council to
permit the Historical Society to locate at the 411 Linden site.
President Duhl closed the Public Hearing and called for comments from the
Trustees. There being none, he asked for a motion to introduce the amendment.
Trustee Aquilino made the motion, seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. Duhl thanked all of the residents who have taken part in this process for
their participation and expressed regret that some are left with negative
feelings over the disagreement. He remarked that all of the Trustees are acting
in good faith, trying to do what is best for all.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-11-2001- Amending Village Code as it Pertains to
Responsibility of Dog Owners and Fines and Penalties for Violations -
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that, as a result of an increasing
number of incidents involving threatening dogs, staff recommended raising the
fines imposed for violations of the ordinance since the current fines are not a
significant deterrent. He pointed out that this simply changes the fine
structure, not the requirements, of the animal control ordinance as an interim
solution to an accelerating problem. He indicated that staff was undertaking a
comprehensive review of the animal control ordinance and reported that the
Village Prosecutor supported the increase in fines.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2001- Amending Residency Requirements for Chief of
Police - Introduction. Manager Williams explained that the current residency
requirement imposes economic hardship on candidates seeking to apply for the
position and recommended that it be removed. He reported that Glencoe,
Wilmette, and Northfield do not require their police chiefs to live in the
village.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Resolution No. R-37-2001 – Commending William J. Gallagher, Police Chief -
Adoption. Noting that the commendation will be presented to Chief Gallagher at
a later date, President Duhl read the resolution recognizing the Chief for his
30 years of service to the community.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Proclamation: Winnetka Community House – Adoption. President Duhl read the
Proclamation designating Saturday, December 1, 2001, as “Winnetka Community
House Day” in recognition of the organization’s 90th birthday.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the
Proclamation. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
5. Quorum
a)   November 13, 2001, Special Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   November 20, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
6. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 9, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   October 16, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1243 and 1244. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1243 in the amount of $2,002,906.67 and
Warrant List No 1244 in the amount of $1,041,896.71. Trustee Greenough seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Certificate of Appropriateness: 64 Green Bay Rd. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed
the application for a Certificate of Appropriateness for proposed alterations
to the subject property that is to be renovated and reopened as a new café. He
reported that the Design Review Board had voted favorably to recommend approval
of the Certificate, subject to modifications that have been accepted by the
applicant. He noted that one issue remains unresolved, but that the Design
Review Board recommended that the Council proceed with approval conditional
upon the Design Review Board’s final review of that issue.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the Certificate
of Appropriateness. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
c)   Auditor’s Report. Finance Director Ed McKee introduced the Village’s
Auditor, Irwin Lyons, from Miller Cooper & Co., Ltd. Mr. Lyons commented on the
Comprehensive Annual Financial Report for the year ended March 31, 2001. He
pointed out that the Village had been given an unqualified opinion, which is
the highest level that can be received. He also noted that the Village had once
again received the Government Finance Office Certificate for Excellence in
Finance Reporting. Mr. Lyons then reviewed the Management Letter, which he
explained is negative by its nature, and responded to questions from the
Trustees.
President Duhl asked Mr. Lyons whether there was anything in particular that
the Village needed to watch out for. Mr. Lyons responded that it just needed to
be aware of the changing economic conditions and monitor them closely. He also
suggested that the Village’s disaster recovery plan relating to computer
systems needs to be tested to assure that it functions.
Manager Williams remarked that staff has been carefully monitoring revenues and
believes that although there has been some softening, the Village ought to be
on target for general revenue. He indicated that the Village overall is very
sound financially, but called Council’s attention to two Internal Service
Funds, Health Insurance and Liability Insurance, that raise flags of some
concern.
d)   Interagency Agreement to Provide Mapping Data to Village. Mr. Williams
explained that this agreement would give the Village access, for free, to a
data set of digital orthophotography of the entire county recently developed by
Cook County. He noted that should the Village decide to develop a GIS, this
data would be very useful.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to authorize execution of
the Interagency Agreement allowing the Village access to Cook County’s digital
orthophotography. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
e)   Change Order Indian Hill Parking Lot. Mr. Williams explained that the
change order resulted from Union Pacific requiring the purchase, installation
and removal of temporary, protective fencing along the length of the parking
lot during work on the re-landscaping project. Trustee Greenough inquired about
the seemingly very high cost of the fence. Mr. Williams suggested that the item
be tabled until the next meeting, so that he could get more information from
staff. The Trustees concurred.
f)   Bid No. 01-017 – Madsen Reservoir Fill Line. Manager Williams explained
that the purchase and installation of this line would permit the reservoir to
continuously pump water to both Winnetka and Northfield while allowing the
reservoir also to serve as a backup to the pumps at the Electric Plant. At
present, there is only one line serving the reservoir, and it cannot perform
both operations at the same time.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the purchase and
installation of a Madsen Reservoir Fill Line from G. W. Kennedy Construction
Co., in the amount of $50,859. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and
Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Bid No. 01-018 – Skid Steer Loader. Manager Williams reviewed the bids for
the purchase and delivery of a new hydrostatic skid steer loader to replace a
1989 asphalt paver that will be sold at later date. He reported staff’s
recommendation that the bid be awarded to Atlas Bobcat, Inc., in the amount of
$33,334.06.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the purchase of
from. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Bid No. 01-012A – Boiler Feedwater Pump. Manager Williams reviewed the
bids for a specialty pump that will supply high-pressure feedwater to power
boilers. The pump will replace one that has worn out. He reported staff’s
recommendation that the bid be awarded to Mack Pump & Equipment Co., in the
amount of $68,896.00.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the purchase
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
i)   Bid No. 01-020 – Watt-Hour Meter Test. Manager Williams reviewed the bids
for a new automated universal watt-hour test and calibration system to test and
calibrate consumer watt-hour meters. He reported staff’s recommendation that
the bid be awarded to Electric Laboratories & Sales Corp., in the amount of
$33,450.00, which includes two days of on-site training.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Woodbury,
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that the Village and Park District
would be undertaking a joint drainage study of the golf course and the
adjoining neighborhoods to the east. This neighborhood has been identified in
the Five Year Capital Plan as the next drainage area to be addressed after
completion of the drainage improvements on west Tower Road.
He also reported that Bryan McInturff had been named as this year’s recipient
of the IMEA Board Member of the Year Award
Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the next Winnetka Report would be
coming out soon.
Trustee Aquilino reported that she had attended a recent Red Center meeting and
tour of the new facility, which they expect to be operational the first of the
year.
10. Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish requested that a bike rack be installed on the southwest
corner of Elm and Chestnut and asked about the progress on upgrading the train
stations.
Manager Williams indicated that he would check with Public Works about the bike
rack and reported that staff continues to pursue the train station project.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Mary Hill to the Forestry
Commission to replace Elizabeth Crowe, whose term has expired. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the appointment. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl also suggested the reappointment of Joan Fragen to the Plan
Commission for a second term. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser,
moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, lease of village
property, and security procedures pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)
(1), Section 2(c)(6), and Section 2(c)(8), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:55 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:56 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
November 13, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved November 20, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 13, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: Trustee. Also present: Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, and about
11 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-29-2001 – Zoning Variation: 950 Green Bay Rd. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from the
Intensity of Use provisions to permit the construction of a building addition
and reconstruction of a parking lot. The applicant is also requesting a
variation from the Parking provisions to permit expansion of the existing
ground floor at the rear of the building. This expansion would reduce the off-
street parking from ten (10) spaces to four (4) spaces. He explained that the
variations are being requested in conjunction with the proposed expansion of
the building in order to accommodate national retail tenants looking at the
space. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the former tenants were medical practitioners,
permitted by a 1995 Special Use Permit to the C-2 overlay District that has now
expired. He also pointed out that the lot currently has 100% impervious surface
coverage and that compliance with the 90% limitation would require part of the
remaining parking lot to be landscaped, in effect further reducing the number
of parking spaces.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals voted to recommend that
the Council grant applicant’s requests.
Herbert Linn, of the law firm Pedersen & Houpt, spoke on behalf of the
applicant. He indicated that the building is old and tired, which discouraged
office tenants from occupying the space, and that it is difficult to allocate
the cost of renovation to two small users. The applicant is seeking to attract
retail clients but, in order to do so feels that these improvements need to be
made. Mr. Linn also commented on the parking, explaining that the Capital
Development Board, which administers accessibility requirements, had determined
that they do not need to provide a handicap accessible parking space at the
rear of the building because there are accessible spaces on the street.
Mr. Pasquinelli, the architect, used an elevation to show the expansion plans
and explained the proposed new elevation to Trustee Woodbury. Mr. Woodbury
expressed the desire to see the Zoning Board’s minutes as well as the Design
Review Board’s comments before approving the plan.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   Ordinance No. M-30-2001 – Zoning Variation: 339 Sheridan - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from Front Setback and Rear
Yard provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of a one-
story conservatory addition. He commented on prior zoning cases for the subject
property, noting that in 1996, the ZBA and the Village Council approved a
variation for a nearly identical plan. Construction, however, did not commence
within the required 12 months, and thus the approval had lapsed.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the matter comes to the Village Council with no
recommendation from the Zoning Board of Appeals as their vote was 3 to 1, and
four affirmative votes are necessary to recommend approval.
David Forte, the architect, distributed drawings demonstrating the hardship of
working with this property, chiefly its unique orientation, which virtually
eliminates the buildable depth of the lot. He asked for the Council to
reinstate the variance granted in 1996.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance as drafted granting a Gross Floor Area variation. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. M-31-2001 – Zoning Variation: 300 Woodlawn. - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from the Front Setback and
Rear Yard provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the expansion of a
single family residence, including construction of a one and two-story addition
and a screened porch and new one-story attached garage.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended that the
requested variations be approved.
Healy Rice, the architect, explained the proposed construction. She pointed out
that the owners are making every effort not to disturb a large maple tree that
occupies a prominent place on the property.
Trustee Woodbury raised some questions about the location of the screened porch
in relation to the other building setbacks on block face as a whole and whether
it would break the typical pattern. Noting that he was not inclined to violate
the pattern of typical setbacks, he asked to see some figures on the typical
setbacks before making a decision.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
as drafted granting a Gross Floor Area variation. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-32-2001 – Zoning Variation: 785 Lincoln. - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from the Maximum Building Size
of the Zoning Ordinance to permit construction of a kitchen expansion and
enclosure of a service porch at the rear of the existing residence, as well as
a request for interior renovations and window replacement. He pointed out that
the structure’s legal non-conforming nature is due to the fact that both the
maximum allowable GFA, as well as the method of calculating the existing home’s
size have changed since the home was constructed.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended that the
requested variations be approved.
Attorney Janega commented on the GFA variance. She explained that the ordinance
had been drafted only to permit the refenestration because she was unable to
find any findings in the Zoning Board minutes identifying a particular hardship
that would allow for the GFA variation. She pointed out that this house had
been constructed in 1989 after maximum GFA limitations went into effect and
that it was therefore subject to the more stringent GFA limitations that the
1999 zoning amendments made applicable to buildings constructed after May of
1989. She added that, even if the less stringent standards that apply to older
homes were applied in this case, this house would still exceed the limits.
The applicant, Jill Ferguson, spoke in support of the variance, saying that the
small existing kitchen imposes a significant hardship on her family’s
lifestyle. The project architect, Chris Rhodes, also commented on the request.
Manager Williams explained to the Council that it could (a) grant the ordinance
as drafted; (b) refer it back to the ZBA for reconsideration and further
findings, or (c) determine that there is, in fact, a significant hardship and
direct that both variances be included in the final draft up for adoption.
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, and Greenough indicated that they could appreciate
that the small kitchen was a hardship and would, albeit reluctantly, approve
the de minimis increase in GFA.
Trustee Woodbury indicated that while he supports the enclosure of the screened
porch, he could not support allowing an increase in GFA.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance as drafted, to be amended before adoption to grant all requested
variations. By voice vote, the motion carried.
4. Old Business.
None.
5. New Business.
None.
6. Reports.
a)   Manager.
Trustees. Trustees Aquilino and Brower reported on their attendance at a recent
Chamber of Commerce meeting. They indicated that representatives from the
Chamber were interested in meeting with the Director of Community Development
to discuss some issues that have arisen with regard to building permits on
commercial properties. Trustee Aquilino noted that the annual Village Christmas
ornament was now on sale and Trustee Brower commented on the holiday lights
currently being hung. He noted that the Chamber wished to extend the lighting
period until February 15th.
Manager Williams reported that it had been a policy decision by a previous
Council to remove the lights the end of January, but that this Council could
certainly alter that decision.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to allow the holiday lights to remain
until February 15th.
7. Appointments.
None.
8. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(5), respectively, of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 8:32 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:30 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
9. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:31 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
November 20, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Adopted December 4, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 20, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, and
about 31 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced the addition of Item 9(c), the bid for Water &
Electric Plant fencing. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   December 4, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   December 11, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend. President Duhl said that he would be unable to
attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 6, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
b)   November 13, 2001, Special Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2001 – Amends Zoning Ordinance as it Pertains to
Nonessential Public Uses – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed revisions to the
recitals at the beginning of the ordinance that have been made since its
introduction on November 6th. She explained that these changes had been made in
order to further clarify the purpose of the amendment and to provide a more
comprehensive legislative history.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the amendments
as outlined by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
defining the term “nonessential public use” to clarify the Zoning Ordinance as
it pertains to special use permits for nonessential public uses. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough,
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-28-2001 – Special Use Permit & Variation: 411 Linden -
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed revisions to the final draft of the
ordinance made to assure that it is consistent both with Council’s discussions
and with the record. She explained that the Special Use Permit would be limited
solely to the Historical Society, rather than running with the land due to the
uniqueness of the circumstances and Council’s desire to preserve the
neighborhood.
Dan Kinsella, 420 Linden, reiterated objections made at previous meetings.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the amendments
as outlined by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Speaking only for himself, Trustee Presser addressed both the neighbors and the
Historical Society. He commented on the difficulty of making a decision about
this matter, as there is right on both sides of the issue. He expressed his
belief that the Council has endeavored to meet the needs of both parties by
granting the Society the privilege of making its home in a residential
neighborhood but imposing upon it restrictions intended to prevent the
neighborhood from losing its residential character. He noted that this imposes
upon the Historical Society the obligation to behave as good neighbors in the
community. Saying that the neighbors should not have to change their behavior
or expectations, he, however, encouraged them to take advantage of the benefits
associated with having the Historical Society in their neighborhood.
Mr. Presser referred to the threats of litigation from the neighbors. He said
that no one wished to remove that right, but discouraged them from pursuing
that avenue, as in his view, the chances of success were very minimal. He
suggested that the neighbors wait to see what happens, and if adjustments to
the restrictions are deemed necessary, make a case to the Council for those
changes before initiating litigation.
Trustee Aquilino commented that the responsibility of a Trustee is to weigh the
issues and make a decision about what he/she believes is best for the Village
as a whole. She expressed concern about allegations that the Village is bailing
out the Historical Society because they purchased the house without coming to
the Village first and that the Trustees have not heard the neighbors. She said
she believed that neither was the case. The Society’s precipitous purchase of
the property was not a factor in the Council’s deliberations, and the Trustees,
in fact, had carefully considered the neighbors concerns, which had resulted in
the restrictions imposed. She expressed her hope and belief that this would be
a good thing not only for the community as a whole, but for the neighborhood as
well.
Trustee Woodbury reinforced the remarks of Trustees Presser and Aquilino,
saying that one of the goals of the Comprehensive Plan was to foster public
dialogue on public issues, which has certainly occurred on this matter. He
asked the Historical Society to be a good neighbor and to complement the
neighborhood in which it will now reside.
Trustee Greenough said that she believed firmly that it would be possible for
the neighbors to work with the institution when neighborhood issues arise, as
she expects that they will.
Trustee Brower echoed Mr. Presser’s comments on the difficulty of this
decision. He acknowledged that it was possible that the Trustees were in error
about property values or intensity of use, but that provisions for review and
recourse had been carefully incorporated into the ordinance. He urged the
neighbors to return to the Council if there is a problem and asked both parties
to work together to make the situation work.
Trustee Powell concurred with the other Trustees’ remarks.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting the Winnetka Historical Society a Special Use Permit, allowing it to
locate its collections and operations in the single-family residence at 411
Linden, as well as, a variation granting a rear yard setback allowing the
existing one-car garage to be extended and attached to the residence. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough,
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None
President Duhl addressed the audience saying that what has transpired in this
matter is an example of the most fundamental controversy faced by all
governments: public rights vs. individual rights. He expressed his belief that
the Council has made every effort to do what is right by constructing a
compromise that accommodates both parties’ interests and makes sense of the
natural tension that exists between individual and community rights. He stated
his opinion that throughout all of law, there are very few absolutes where
variances don’t makes sense in some cases. Mr. Duhl reiterated Trustee Brower’s
comment that if the situation doesn’t work out at first, the problems can be
addressed at that time. He voiced hope that all of the parties will accept that
the Council has acted in good faith and attempt to apply the same principle to
their interactions with one another.
c)   Ordinance No. M-29-2001 – Zoning Variation: 950 Green Bay Rd. - Adoption.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed new information regarding parking in this block and the
findings of the DRB in response to questions raised at the last meeting. With
regard to the parking variation, Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Village had
not conducted any studies on off-street parking in this area. He noted that
while the Comprehensive Plan calls for encouraging on-site parking at the rear
of buildings, for this specific portion of Green Bay Rd., in the C-2 Retail
Overlay district, no parking is required for first floor retail uses. Mr.
D’Onofrio mentioned that the need for off-site parking is mitigated somewhat by
the proximity of the Hubbard Woods parking deck. He also reported that the
Design Review Board had issued favorable comments on the proposed building
design with the suggestion that more thought be given to the design of the rear
of the building.
Ms. Janega reviewed revisions to the draft ordinance introduced at the last
meeting, specifically the incorporation of note of the Design Review Board’s
findings. She also commented on a letter from the Capital Development Board
concerning handicapped accessible parking, noting that none is required of the
owner and there is no burden on the Village to provide additional parking.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the amendments
as outlined. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that in light of the Design
Review Board’s comments, the applicant will be returning to the Council for
final approval of the design.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting zoning variations for intensity of use and parking for the subject
property. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-30-2001 – Zoning Variation: 339 Sheridan - Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting the front setback and rear yard variations. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-31-2001 – Zoning Variation: 300 Woodland. - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio responded to a question about building setbacks raised at the last
meeting. He advised the Council that the setbacks on the north Hawthorn Lane
block face range from 26 to 34 feet. Therefore, the residence on this property
would be the most forward house.
The applicant explained that the design results from their attempt to preserve
a large maple tree in the rear yard.
Manager Williams inquired whether any of the neighbors had expressed any
concern about this setback. The applicant replied that none had.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting side yard and front setback variations. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Ordinance No. M-32-2001 – Zoning Variation: 785 Lincoln. - Adoption. Ms.
Janega reviewed revisions to the draft ordinance made to include not only the
variation for refenestration, but also the variation from maximum building
size, pursuant to Council’s discussion at the time of introduction. She also
responded to questions from the Trustees as to what design features were
included in the GFA calculations and was not.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance as
explained by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting both variations. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: Trustees Powell and Woodbury.
Absent: None.
g)   Ordinance No. MC-11-2001- Amending Village Code as it pertains to
Responsibility of Dog Owners and Fines and Penalties for Violations - Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
increasing the fines imposed for violations of the Code’s sections pertaining
to the responsibilities of dog owners and dangerous dogs. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2001- Amending Residency Requirements for Chief of
Police - Adoption. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt
the ordinance eliminating the requirement for the Village’s Police Chief to
live in the Village. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
i)   Resolution No. R-38-2001 –Emergency Mutual Aid Agreement for Public Works
- Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that for many years Winnetka has
participated in emergency and non-emergency mutual aid agreement through the
Northwest Municipal Conference (NWMC), under which participating agencies may
exchange non-emergency and/or emergency mutual aid services with other members.
The South Suburban Mayors and Managers Association (SSMMA) and the West Central
Municipal Conference (WCMC) also operate similar mutual aid agreements for
their members. The NWMC has recently been working with the SSMMA and the WCMC
to expand the program to include other public agencies in the region. As a
result of these efforts, the NWMC, SSMMA, and WCMC have developed the
Northeastern Illinois Mutual Aid Network (NEIMAN), for the purpose of area-wide
exchange of personnel and equipment in the event of emergency or non-emergency
situations.
Ms. Janega then reviewed the key provisions of both Agreements, which she said
are consistent with the key provisions of the Village’s other mutual aid
agreements.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Brower, moved to adopt the resolution authorizing
the Village to execute the Public Works Non-Emergency Mutual Aid
Intergovernmental Service Agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and
Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
j)   Resolution No. R-39-2001 – Non-Emergency Mutual Aid Agreement for Public
Works – Adoption. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Aquilino, moved to adopt the
resolution authorizing the Village to execute the Public Works Emergency Mutual
Aid Intergovernmental Service Agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and
Powell,. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Lane, read a letter from The Women’s Republican
Club of New Trier Township asking that Ordinance No. MC-1-2001 regarding the
scheduling of Village Council meetings on election days be rescinded
President Duhl responded that his position had not changed on this issue.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1245 and 1246. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1245 in the amount of $201,451.02 and Warrant
List No 1246 in the amount of $487,712.37. Trustee Presser seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Kenilworth Fire Contract. Manager Williams reported that the existing
five-year contract with Kenilworth to provide fire and paramedic services will
expire on March 31, 2002. Kenilworth reimburses Winnetka for these services,
and these funds help to offset the expenses of the Village’s Fire Department.
Thereby, both villages enjoy the economies of scale. He explained that the cost
to Kenilworth is determined by a formula based on the most current equalized
assessed valuation, the latest decennial population, and the number of all fire
suppression and emergency paramedical service calls from the preceding twelve-
month period. As proposed the contract would extend the same terms and
conditions of the existing contract for an additional ten- (10) years, until
March 31, 2012.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve contract as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Water and Electric Plant Fencing. Manager Williams reported that new
fencing is required to secure the Water and Electric Plant sites, noting that
all fencing visible to the public would be decorative wrought iron. He
explained that because it is important to have this installed as soon as
possible and before winter sets in, staff sought quotes outside of the formal
bidding process. He said that staff requested that Council waive the formal
bidding process and approve the bid of All American Fence in the amount of
$37,254.00.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to waive formal bid
process and approve bid of All American. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that although it had been originally
intended
b)    to bring an analysis of the impact and success of the Design Guidelines
back to the Council six months after adoption, staff felt that such an analysis
was premature, as more experience working with the Guidelines was needed.
Therefore, the analysis will be delayed another six months in order to gather
more information.
Trustees. Trustee Presser announced that there would be a Code Review Committee
meeting next week.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Steve Scherer to the Building
Review Committee and called for a motion to approve the appointment. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, so moved. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings
Act. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:30 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:31 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
December 4, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved December 18, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 4, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Director
of Public Works Steven Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee and about 11 persons
in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that regarding Item 6(e), the Truth in Taxation
Hearing would be held prior to the introduction of the Tax Levy ordinance. The
Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
He then introduced Susie Schreiber who reported on activities she has organized
for the morning of January 5, 2002 when Olympic Torch Relay passes through
Winnetka. Afterwards, on behalf of the Council, Trustee Brower expressed the
thanks to Ms. Schreiber for her efforts.
4. Quorum
a)   December 11, 2001, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Powell indicated that they expected to attend. President Duhl
reiterated that he would be unable to attend.
b)   December 18, 2001, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 20, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-37-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1173 Asbury St. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for variation from the
Intensity of Use provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to permit construction of a
new open front porch. The proposed construction would exceed roofed lot
coverage and impermeable surface limitations. He explained that, while the
Zoning Ordinance provides an intensity of use of lot allowance for any one-
story open porch that extends along the full length of the front of the house,
the proposed porch would extend across only a portion of the front of the
residence, thereby disqualifying it from being excepted from the requirements.
He reported that the ZBA had voted unanimously to recommend approval of the
variation.
The applicant explained that they were unable to extend the porch the length of
the residence because there is a large oak tree in the way.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that perhaps at some future time, the Council should
consider a text amendment to eliminate the requirement that an open porch
extend the entire length of the house, as there are some instances in which
that is an inappropriate design and the intent of the ordinance was to
encourage open porches in the front.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   Ordinance No. M-38-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1179 Asbury St. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for a variation from the
Intensity of Use and Side Yard provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to permit
reconstruction of an existing front porch, together with construction of a
mudroom addition, a porte cochere and a new detached garage. He reported that
the ZBA had voted unanimously to recommend approval of the variation.
The applicant’s architect responded to questions from the Council and used a
model to show the Council the proposed changes, which included opening the
first floor area under the existing office addition to create a porte cochere
that will allow driveway access to the proposed garage at the rear of the
property.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. M-39-2001 – Zoning Variation: 333 Sheridan Rd.. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for front and rear yard
setbacks in order to permit the construction of a one-story addition at the
rear and side of the existing residence which is situated on a wide but shallow
lot, which results in most of the existing home being in a nonconforming
location. The applicant also proposes structural alterations to allow an
increased window opening on the east elevation, which encroaches in the side
yard. He reported that the ZBA had voted unanimously to recommend approval of
the variation.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-40-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1092 Spruce St.. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for a side yard variation
to permit a second floor addition that would be above a one-story addition
constructed in 1993 with a 6.29-foot east side yard setback that conformed to
the then required 6-foot setback. In 1999, the Zoning Ordinance was amended to
require an 8-foot setback on one of the two side yards, which made the addition
non-conforming, thus requiring the present variation request. He reported that
the ZBA had voted unanimously to recommend approval of the variation. He also
noted that the applicants had recently received from the Zoning Board a
variation for refenestration of the existing west wall which is within 2 feet
of the west property line.
The applicant, Gary Frank, used elevations and photos to illustrate both the
previous and proposed additions. He explained that in order to bring the
proposed second floor addition into compliance on the east side with the new
setback requirements, its east wall would have to be set in two feet from the
wall directly underneath it, causing hardship and practical difficulties.
Trustee Woodbury confirmed that the requested variation under consideration
only affects the east wall and that the west wall, where there is an existing,
non-conforming situation, would be in compliance.
Attorney Janega reviewed the pertinent 1999 changes to the Zoning Ordinance and
their impact on this situation.
Speaking from an historical perspective, President Duhl remarked that it had
been the intent of those changes to encourage driveways as opposed to front-
facing garages.
Ms. Janega pointed out that this property has access to its garage from the
alley, and therefore, the object of the revision is not relevant in this
instance. She also reviewed a correction to the last paragraph on page 3 of the
proposed ordinance, deleting the statement that no bedrooms would be added, and
adding that the existing garage will remain.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce St., the neighbor to the west of the applicant, raised
procedural and substantive objections to the Council’s granting the requested
variation, as cited in letters submitted to the Council and the Zoning Board.
Mr. Hecht contended that the applicant’s proposed addition will substantially
curtail the light and air to his property, which has already been
significantly, negatively impacted by prior additions to the original
residence. Citing the non-conforming nature of the existing west wall, Mr.
Hecht asked that the Council not let the Zoning Board’s decision stand and that
the Council consider both the proposed addition and the refenestration projects
in their totality.
Trustee Powell noted that the Council had already determined that most
refenestration, issues, such as this one, fall within the purview of the Zoning
Board.
Trustee Presser pointed out that if the applicant set the east wall of the
addition in by two feet, he could proceed to build his new addition without a
variation, because it otherwise is in complete conformity with the Zoning
Ordinance. He also noted that the Zoning Board of Appeals had heard all of Mr.
Hecht’s arguments and ultimately had not supported his position. Mr. Presser
expressed doubt that the Council could, or would want to, set aside the ZBA’s
previous determination about the refenestration.
Trustee Greenough confirmed that the grandfathered, non-conforming section of
the west wall, where the refenestration is to take place, was in existence when
the Hecht’s built their home.
President Duhl commented that he did not see how the proposed addition would
impact Mr. Hecht’s property any more than a similar addition with a complying
6-foot setback would impact a similarly situated neighboring house in another
location. He also indicated that he was not sure that the Council wouldn’t have
granted both variations had they come before it because, as stated earlier, the
intent of increasing the setback on one side to 8 feet was to encourage
driveways. Mr. Duhl pointed out that the size of a house is determined by gross
floor area, not by setbacks.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance as corrected by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
e)   Ordinance No. M-34-2001 – 2001 Tax Levy - Truth in Taxation Hearing and
Ordinance Introduction. President Duhl opened the hearing at 8:50 p.m. and
asked Attorney Janega to review the notice and legal requirement for the
hearing. He then asked Mr. McKee to review the tax levy. Mr. McKee explained
that the tax levy is set by taking into consideration revenue and expenditure
trends to determine what is needed to fund the Village for the coming year. He
recommended that the tax levy be increased by 3.5%, plus new development, in
order to cover increased operating expenses, primarily wage and benefit costs.
Mr. McKee then explained the two property tax abatements would abate the taxes
levied for debt service on the bonds issued for the construction of the Public
Safety Building because sales tax revenues are sufficient to meet the bond
payments.
At the conclusion of Mr. McKee’s comments, President Duhl called for remarks,
questions and/or evidence from the audience. There being none, he closed the
hearing at 9:00 p.m.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-34-2001, Levying Property Taxes for the Year 2001. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Ordinance No. M-35-2001 – Tax Abatement: 1994 PSB Bonds - Introduction.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-35-2001, abating the Property Tax Levy for the 1994 Public Safety Building
General Obligation Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Ordinance No. M-36-2001 – Tax Abatement: 1996 PSB Bonds - Introduction.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the Ordinance
No. M-36-2001, abating the Property Tax Levy for the 1996 Public Safety
Building General Obligation Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1247 and 1248. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1247 in the amount of $745,694.46 and Warrant
List No 1248 in the amount of $550,615.57. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Indian Hill Parking Lot Change Order. In response to the question raised
on this matter at the November 6th meeting, Mr. Saunders explained that the
amount charged by the railroad represents the actual cost to the railroad,
which was passed on the Village’s landscape contractor. Acknowledging that the
price was probably not reasonable, but outside of the Village’s control, he
reported that the landscaper has already paid the bill to the railroad’s
subcontractor.
Trustee Aquilino expressed disappointment over the expense.
Manager Williams mentioned that the Village would bring this matter to the
railroad’s attention during negotiation of the lease for the train stations.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the Change Order
in the amount of $14,605.00. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Request for Commemorative Vehicle Sticker. Manager Williams explained that
the Winnetka Community House has requested that the Village use a depiction of
the facility on its 2003 vehicle sticker in recognition of the organization’s
90th anniversary. He said that staff had no objections to the proposal and
indicated that any cost to the Village would be minimal.
Mert Silbar, who is handling the public relations for the Community House, made
a brief presentation.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to authorize the
Winnetka Community House to be on the 2003 vehicle sticker. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury.
d)   Report on Property Insurance. Mr. McKee explained that the events of
September 11, 2001, have traumatized the insurance markets in complex and
profound ways that impact the Village’s property insurance cost and coverage.
He reported his expectation that the Village’s cost for the necessary amount of
insurance could increase anywhere from 50% to 100% over the current cost of
$100,704. Mr. McKee responded to questions from the Trustees and requested
authorization to negotiate and bind property insurance coverage for the Village
effective January 1, 2002, subject to the approval of the Village Manager.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to authorize the Finance
Director, with the Village Manager’s approval, to negotiate and bind property
insurance coverage as requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and
Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   President. President Duhl reported that he had attended the ceremony
celebrating the 90th anniversary of the Winnetka Community House.
Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the Code Review Committee had met and
completed the items on its agenda and would be meeting again to continue it
review.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl appointed Scott Freres to the Design Review Board to replace Ron
Condon, whose term has expired. Trustee Greenough moved to confirm the
appointment. The motion was seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:05 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:06 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
December 11, 2001
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved January 8, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Barbara Aquilino called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m.
Present: Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee John Powell. Also present: Village
Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development
Brian Norkus, and approximately five persons in the audience.
President Pro Tem Aquilino announced that Item No. 2 on the published Agenda,
Maintenance Responsibility for Water and Sewer Service Lines Serving Private
Residences, had been removed from the Agenda because one of the interested
parties could not attend this meeting.
2. Gross Floor Area Report and Discussion.
President Pro Tem Aquilino announced that notice of this discussion item had
been published in the last issue of the Winnetka Report. She asked Manager
Williams to review the history of this issue, which he did. Mr. Williams
suggested that the Trustees listen to the consultant’s report at this meeting
and then staff would return in January with an array of options for the Council
to consider enacting. He, then, introduced the Village’s consultant, John
Houseal from Trekla, Pettigrew, Allen & Payne, Inc.
Mr. Houseal reviewed the Residential Gross Floor Area Study covering the
following areas of concern:
   (a)   Single Family Homes
      (i)   gross floor area
      (ii)   building height
   (b)   Detached Garages
      (i)   side and rear setbacks
      (ii)   garage height
   (c)   Tableland Issues
      (i)   defining shoreline lot area
Mr. Houseal explained that the study was conducted to better understand how
Winnetka’s residential Gross Floor Area (GFA) regulations compare to various
other “similar” communities and examine what changes should be considered to
improve the existing standards. In particular, the study focused on gross floor
area and building height for single-family residential structures and setback
and height requirements for detached garages. He noted that for purposes of
comparison, other communities facing similar new construction and building
addition pressures and real estate values were examined to determine how
residential “bulk” factors were being handled. These included: Glencoe,
Highland Park, Hinsdale, Lake Forest and Wilmette. Mr. Houseal enumerated the
factors contributing to “visual bulk” as being: gross floor area, setbacks, lot
coverage, building height, FAR bonuses, and any other building articulation
requirements that may be implemented. He said that all of these were considered
in the overall analysis and development of suggested GFA management strategies.
Finally, he noted that a study of tableland issues had been undertaken to
assess how different shoreline communities calculate lot area for properties
located on Lake Michigan.
Mr. Houseal reported that a direct comparison of Winnetka to the other
communities shows that Winnetka generally allows a greater GFA for all lots
when compared to the average allowable GFA of the other communities as a whole.
He pointed out, however, that each community measures GFA in slightly different
ways, therefore preventing the comparison from being a precise measure.
President Pro Tem Aquilino asked when each community last amended its zoning
for purposes of controlling the bulk issue. Mr. Houseal responded that all had
dealt with the matter fairly recently and indicated that he would supply the
exact information at a later date.
Mr. Williams commented that Winnetka’s most recent amendments had been geared
to small lots to allow for construction of a reasonable house given today’s
standards.
Ms Aquilino confirmed that Winnetka’s allowable GFA is still high for small
lots. Mr. Houseal responded in the affirmative.
Attorney Janega explained that when the formula was redone, it was liberalized
for existing homes because new construction was able to take advantage of all
of the available bonuses. This had the biggest impact on smaller lots.
Mr. Houseal remarked that Winnetka is unique in that it has different standards
for existing vs. new construction, and commented again that there is no
standard approach to GFA, because what is palatable in one community would not
be acceptable in another. He also noted that a good indication that Winnetka is
not too far off mark is that Winnetka’s allowable GFA is never the extreme
case, neither the most restrictive nor the most lenient, when compared to other
communities. However, because Winnetka’s allowable GFA is consistently higher
on average, he suggested that consideration be given to reducing the maximum
allowable GFA for single-family residential structures.
Next, Mr. Houseal discussed building height, noting that two components
determine how it is regulated: (a) how it is measured and (b) maximum building
height. He reported that the majority of communities, including those included
in this study, measure building height to the highest point of the structure.
Whereas Winnetka measures building height as the distance from grade to the
mean point between the roof peak and the eaves, which in effect allows houses
to be taller than in any other community in the study and is problematic in
terms of regulating visual bulk.
Trustee Greenough pointed out that the purpose of this was to force a pitched
roof.
Mr. Norkus reported that virtually no new construction is 35 feet high.
Trustee Woodbury asked staff to gather information about typical roof height so
that the maximum could be reestablished. He also commented that raised
basements contribute to height and bulk.
Irv Footlick, 1548 Tower Rd., remarked that height is determined in part by
where the measurement is taken from. Mr. Norkus explained the Village’s
measuring procedures.
Mr. Houseal commented that there is no commonly accepted practice, as all of
the communities do it differently. He suggested that the Village’s maximum
allowable height of 35’ is appropriate, but measuring the height of a building
at the midpoint between the peak and the eaves should be reconsidered. He added
that limiting a single-family structure to 2-1/2 stories was also recommended.
With regard to detached garages, Mr. Houseal pointed out that Winnetka
currently does not require rear or side yard setbacks for detached garages
located in the rear yard. This makes it possible to build directly on the rear
and/or side property line, immediately adjacent to a neighboring detached
garage. All of the other communities studied require some setback, ranging from
2’ to 5’. These setbacks allow some separation between structures and the
property line, providing room for repair and maintenance of the garage from the
property on which it is located and ensuring that adjacent detached garages are
a minimum distance apart, which is an added safety benefit in terms of fire
separation. Mr. Houseal suggested a 3’ setback, which keeps a minimum of 6’
between garages and makes a significant impact on appearance without imposing a
significant impact on the use of the property.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that he believed a 2’ setback was sufficient.
Mr. Houseal recommended that the Village’s present maximum garage height of 14’
be increased to 18’, as measured to the highest point of the structure. This
would accommodate garage design and architecture to match the scale, character
and appearance of the principal structure as well as the overall character of
the community.
Finally, Mr. Houseal addressed the tableland issues. Again, he commented that
all communities deal with this issue in a different manner. Some use the water
line to determine the property size, and Wilmette is currently proposing to
establish the base flood elevation (BFE) as the determining factor. He
suggested that a more detailed analysis be undertaken, but that a “set” rear
lot line, and therefore lot area, be established for properties fronting Lake
Michigan.
Manager Williams remarked that one of the recommendations of the Comprehensive
Plan is to do a study of the bluff edge, one of the possible methods for
establishing a “set” rear lot line. Mr. Houseal agreed that a line, such as
this, defined by a natural feature, is a starting point.
In closing, Mr. Houseal briefly reviewed the study’s conclusions and
recommendations.
President Pro Tem Aquilino thanked Mr. Houseal for his report.
King Embry 985 Grove St., commented on the garage issue, submitting pictures of
a garage built against his property line.
Keith Shay, commended the Village on this study and expressed his concern that
builders are taking advantage of the Village’s liberal height and area
restrictions to construct new homes that are not in keeping with their
surrounding neighborhoods.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Norkus to comment on some of the shortcomings of the
Zoning Ordinance as seen by staff and to review the mechanics of calculating
GFA, which he did. In doing so, he commented that as a rule, builders are not
yet maxxing out on GFA for larger lots, but that there is a concern among staff
that this trend will begin.
3. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:05 p.m.
December 18, 2001
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved January 8, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 18, 2001, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager
Douglas Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Finance Director Ed McKee and about 13 persons
in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
Approval of the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
3. Quorum
a)   January 3, 2002, Regular Meeting. President Duhl announced that this
meeting would be cancelled and rescheduled for January 8th.
b)   January 8, 2002, Rescheduled Meeting. All of the Trustees present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 4, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
5. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-37-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1173 Asbury St. – Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting variations from the roofed lot coverage and impermeable surface
limitations to permit construction of a new, open front porch. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-38-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1179 Asbury St. - Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting variations from the intensity of use of lot and side yard provisions
to permit reconstruction of an existing front porch, and the construction of an
addition and a new detached garage. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-39-2001 – Zoning Variation: 333 Sheridan Rd.. - Adoption.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a variations from the front and rear yard setback provisions to permit
construction of a one-story addition as well as structural alteration of the
existing building to allow for refenestration. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-40-2001 – Zoning Variation: 1092 Spruce St. - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a variation from the side yard provisions to permit a second floor
addition. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-34-2001 – 2001 Tax Levy - Adoption. Mr. McKee highlighted
the fact that the 3.5% increase in the Village’s portion of a $10,000 total
property tax bill would amount to $60. He also responded to questions from the
Trustees.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance
setting the tax levy for the Fiscal Year 2001. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Ordinance No. M-35-2001 – Tax Abatement: 1994 PSB Bonds - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
35-2001, abating the Property Tax Levy for the 1994 Public Safety Building
General Obligation Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
g)   Ordinance No. M-36-2001 – Tax Abatement: 1996 PSB Bonds - Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
36-2001, abating the Property Tax Levy for the 1996 Public Safety Building
General Obligation Bonds. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
h)   Ordinance No. M-33-2001 - Authorizing Lease of 93 Green Bay Road -
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that the Village had recently
purchased this property, which has long been identified as a potential site for
a future entryway park. He noted that the subject ordinance would lease the
property to Land Rover for a term of five years, with a possible five year
extension, for storing and servicing vehicles in conjunction with its retail
sales from its main showroom at 80 Green Bay Rd. In the short term, this
arrangement provides a revenue stream for the Village to offset its cost of
acquiring the land and helps to ensure that Land Rover, the Village’s single
biggest generator of sales tax, will remain in Winnetka.
In response to a question from Trustee Aquilino, Mr. Williams indicated that
Land Rover had agreed to cooperate with the Village to clean up the area.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the ordinance
authorizing the lease to Land Rover. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Old Business.
None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1249 and 1250. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1249 in the amount of $623,597.23 and Warrant
List No 1250 in the amount of $724,416.50. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Certificate of Appropriateness: 518 Winnetka Ave. Ms. Janega reviewed an
application for site improvements such as paving, planting, fencing and
construction site signs for the Winnetka Belvedere. She noted that these items
were not covered by the Certificate of Appropriateness granted by the Council
on June 19, 2001, which pertained only to the building. She reported the
comments of the Forestry Commission and its recommendation that the trees along
Winnetka Avenue be eliminated. She also reported the recommendations of the
Design Review Board, noting its recommendation that additional attention be
given to the materials, dimensions and finish of public sidewalks. With regard
to the latter, Ms. Janega referred to a revised sidewalk plan submitted by the
applicant proposing conceptually the use of brick pavers, as specified by the
Design Guidelines, as a border at the back of the curbs and replacing the
existing red brick pavers in the public sidewalk at the corner with a poured
concrete walk. Ms. Janega pointed out that because this development is the
subject of a Special Use Permit, the Council has the final say in the building
design and the site improvements.
The developer provided samples of the proposed materials and reviewed the site
plan.
Trustee Woodbury voiced three objections to the proposal: (1) lack of a
carriage walk along Brier, (2) failure to provide pavers at the corner as
specified by the Design Guidelines, and (3) failure to provide for trees along
Winnetka Avenue.
Mr. Hardej, the developer, responded to these objections.
President Duhl asked Mr. Hardej whether providing all of the things mentioned
by Trustee Woodbury would be a problem. Mr. Hardej replied that they would not,
but that he was anxious to keep the process moving.
Upon Ms. Janega’s recommendation as to how to avoid a delay, President Duhl
asked the developer to return to the next meeting, when the Development
Agreement will be up for consideration, with a revised proposal addressing all
of Trustee Woodbury’s concerns. He noted that with the exception of these three
items, the Council was prepared to go forward.
c)   Review of Comprehensive Plan, Appendix 6. Gwen Trindl, Chairman of the
Plan Commission, reviewed the report and recommendations prepared by the
Commission from its annual assessment, in accordance with the Comprehensive
Plan, of the 2020 Winnetka Plan, particularly Appendix 6: Priority Assessment
of Plan Recommendations. She noted that the Commission had considered the
Appendix in full and made adjustments and clarified priorities. In addition,
the Committee deleted items now completed and analyzed the year’s
accomplishments. In reviewing Planning Priorities, the Commission highlighted
the urgency of establishing a planning process for the Post Office site and the
need to re-evaluate the regulations for building heights and Gross Floor Areas.
She also mentioned that special attention should be given to increased storm
water runoff caused by the increase in impermeable surfaces resulting from new
construction, particularly in view of the known limitations of the Village’s
storm sewer capacity.
Ms. Trindl then responded to questions from the Trustees.
In response to a question from Trustee Brower about an organized effort to
study the health of the Village’s business districts and ways to attract new
businesses, Ms. Trindl indicated that although traditionally this has been the
purview of the Chamber of Commerce, the Plan Commission could undertake such a
study if asked to do so.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that the Commission could study in a strategic way
what uses would be appropriate and are needed for the Village in order to
develop a master plan as to uses, not specific form and substance, for the
various areas.
Manager Williams reminded the Trustees that the Chamber of Commerce and the
Village had hired a consultant a few years ago to examine the health of the
business districts. That consultant found that overall the state of Winnetka’s
business community was enviable and that there has been a steady increase in
sale tax revenues over the last few years.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to ask the Plan Commission to proceed
with the study suggested by Trustee Woodbury.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the proposed
Appendix 6. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
President Duhl thanked Chairman Trindl and her committee for their efforts to
improve and keep current this very important and useful document.
9. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the following dates had been set
for Council’s review of the Preliminary Budget:
January 29, 2002
February 12, 2002
February 26, 2002.
Mr. Williams also reported that significant progress had been made on the
Village Yards Project and that the Technical Committee would be meeting in
early January. He expressed the Committee’s hope to go out for bids by the end
of January and be under construction by early April.
Trustees. Trustee Aquilino reported that she and Trustee Brower had attended
the recent meeting of the Chamber of Commerce. The Chamber is currently
interviewing for a new Executive Director and putting together a committee to
meet with Mr. D’Onofrio to discuss building and zoning issues and concerns.
Also, the Chamber has set the dates for its annual Sidewalk Sale for the third
week in July.
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Susie Shabica to the Forestry
Commission to replace Luretta Spiess. Trustee Greenough moved to confirm the
appointment. The motion was seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 8:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:46 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
2002
January 8, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved January 15, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 8, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Barbara Aquilino. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders and about seven persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Resolution R-1-2002 had been removed from this
meeting’s Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   January 15, 2002, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   January 28, 2002, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 11, 2001, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   December 18, 2001, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-33-2001 - Authorizing Lease of 93 Green Bay Road -
Adoption. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the
ordinance authorizing the lease to Land Rover. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell, Woodbury, and
Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
b)   Resolution No. R-1-2002 – Development Agreement: 518 Winnetka Avenue –
Adoption. Removed from the Agenda.
7. Old Business.
a)   Certificate of Appropriateness: 518 Winnetka Ave. President Duhl announced
that no revised site plans had been received since Trustee Woodbury raised the
following issues at the last meeting: (1) the absence of a carriage walk along
Brier Street; (2) the lack of pavers in the sidewalk at the corner of Winnetka
and Brier; and (3) the lack of trees along the Winnetka Avenue sidewalk.
Attorney Janega stated that the Developer’s application seeks a certificate of
appropriateness for landscaping and sidewalk and parkway improvements, and for
a temporary sign to be posted during construction. She noted that the Developer
was awaiting more specific direction from the Council with regard to the issues
raised by Trustee Woodbury because the submitted plans had received a favorable
recommendation from the Design Review Board. She referred the Trustees to a
Memorandum from the Director of Public Works commenting on these issues.
Mr. Duhl confirmed with the Developer that they were willing to do whatever the
Council directs them to do.
Mr. Saunders reviewed his Memorandum. He expressed concern that the
installation of a carriage walk along Brier Street might serve to encourage
parking activity connected to use of the nearby White Hen, which neighbors find
objectionable. With regard to the question of pavers vs. concrete in the corner
sidewalk, he reported that the Village has had mixed experience with the
installation of pavers at corners, as they often require replacement, adding
maintenance expense. Finally, he explained that only a few, upright, slow
growing trees could be installed along Winnetka Avenue so as to avoid
compromising sightlines to the traffic signal at the corner.
Trustee Woodbury asked how many trees could reasonably be planted.
Mr. Saunders responded two, perhaps, three, depending on the species, flanking
the driveway.
Trustee Woodbury voiced his belief that this would be better than none. He
supported both the carriage walk along at least a portion of Brier Street, for
aesthetic purposes as well as to protect the groundcover, and the installation
of pavers at the corner.
Regarding the trees, Trustee Greenough suggested that the Village plan for the
future by incorporating the use of structural soil along Winnetka Avenue so
that trees might be planted at a future time after a new signal has been
installed at the corner.
Trustee Presser cautioned against doing anything that might create any kind of
additional risk to traffic at that corner.
Ms. Janega explained the remaining signage issue.
President Duhl called for a motion to approve issuance of the Certificate of
Appropriateness, subject to: a) the Council’s direction to plant as many trees
as possible; b) safety considerations; c) ground preparation for future
planting; d) the installation of pavers on the corner; and, e) an abbreviated
carriage walk along Brier north of the parking lot entrance.
Trustee Woodbury so moved. He was seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1251 and 1252. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1251 in the amount of $1,026,291.90 and
Warrant List No 1252 in the amount of $368,260.16. Trustee Greenough seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
b)   Village Yards Project Review. Manager Williams updated the Council on the
status of the proposed expansion and renovation of the Village Yards. He, then,
introduced Scott Rubel, Sente & Rubel, the Village’s architect, who used
elevations to review the current design plans. Mr. Rubel explained that there
will be five separate structures, including a new administration building and
two new storage facilities.
Anthony Douglas from Riley Construction, the Village’s Construction Manager,
reviewed the projected budget for the project and highlighted three alternate
design options for consideration.
Mr. Williams stated that he wished to give the Council time to consider these
materials before returning at the next meeting to ask for authorization to
proceed with bidding.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the projected timeline for the project. He said that if
bidding is authorized at the next meeting, construction could likely start at
the end of April or first of May, with a total construction time for both
phases of 12-15 months.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Budget Meetings would be in
the Council Chambers at 7:30 p.m. on the following evenings:
Monday, January 28
Tuesday, February 12
Monday, February 25
Mr. Williams also reported that a new telephone system has been installed
throughout the Village and asked for everyone’s patience while installation and
staff training are completed.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that in view of the numerous meetings
this month, there would be no meeting of the Code Review Committee.
Trustee Powell reported that RED Center is now fully operational.
Trustee Brower reported that the Chamber of Commerce has selected a new
Executive Director.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser,
and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 8:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:10 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent:
Trustee Aquilino. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Presser,
Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino. The
meeting adjourned at 9:11 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
January 15, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Adopted February 5, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 15, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Public Works, Alex Attiah and about 18
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that the Public Comment portion of the Agenda has been
moved forward in order to accommodate a group of students from New Trier High
School who wish to bring a matter before the Council. He added that Item 9(b)
Maintenance Responsibility for Water and Sewer Service Lines Serving Private
Residences, and Item 9(c) Tower Road Change Order, have been removed from
tonight’s Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   January 28, 2002, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   February 5, 2002, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 8, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
President Duhl recognized Hank Bangser, Superintendent, and Wes Baumann,
Principal, of New Trier High School, and the students accompanying them.
Rebecca Macy, President of the Student Alliance, said she was there on behalf
of all New Trier students to comment on the Council’s proposal to raise the
parking fees for the senior students. She asked the Council to explain the
reason for the price increase for students, saying they already pay more than
the residents and commuters to use the lot. She also asked that the Council
consider the hardship placed on some students who have to drive because of
family responsibilities or because they need to stay after school. She also
asked that the Council review the use of the Green Bay parking lot to determine
if commuters are using the parking spaces as expected.
Village Manager Williams explained that the Village has a corporate philosophy
of making rates competitive for all parking, not only the spaces used by New
Trier, and that rates in other communities as well as the amounts residents are
charging the students for parking were considered. He said that the Village
decided to charge the high school a competitive rate based on the information
that was collected.
President Duhl pointed out that the Village does not charge the students for
parking. He explained that New Trier leases the parking spaces from the Village
for a certain amount and the New Trier School Board sets the fees for student
parking.
Mr. Bangser replied that this was true, but that the Council did not allow any
discussion when the new lease was signed and the School Board was not given a
chance to be heard on this issue. He said that it is reasonable to ask if the
Council is treating the students as equitably as other users of the lot and
reminded the Council that the spaces can’t be filled unless the school uses
them. Mr. Bangser said that because of the growth of the student population,
the need in future years is going to be even greater and he would appreciate
the opportunity for a Board to Board discussion.
Trustees Woodbury and Presser asked how the spaces are allocated to the seniors
and if the school’s policy encouraged driving to school.
Wes Baumann said that parking spaces are distributed by lottery. He said that
students have to live at least 1.5 miles from school to get a parking space and
spaces are limited to seniors. He said that the school does not have a policy
regarding students driving to school. He further stated that the school is
being charged for 4 months at a higher rate than commuters are charged for 6
months and when school is out, no one is using the lot.
President Duhl said that the students should be asking the New Trier Board, not
the Council, about the fees set by the Board. He stated that, when the new
lease was presented to the school, the Council was not made aware of any
complaints; however, he would not want to suggest that the Council is not
willing to discuss the problem. He reiterated that the issue is between the
School Board and the Village Council.
Mr. Bangser agreed with President Duhl and told the student representative that
he would follow up with the Village Manager to bring the School Board and the
Village Council together for discussions and possible solutions.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2001 – Request for Rezoning: Hadley School for the
Blind, 705 Oak Street - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the application
from the Hadley School for the Blind to rezone the subject property from its
current zoning classification of B-1 Multiple Family Residential to R-5 Single
Family Residential in order to permit the School to replace the existing single
family residence with a new single family one. He explained that, although
there is no proposed change in use for the property, the current B-1 zoning
does not permit the construction of a replacement single family home. He
reported that both the Plan Commission and the Design Review Board commented on
the application, noting a suggestion about brick color by the latter. He said
the petitioner had submitted plans, which are included in the Council’s agenda
materials, but more detailed plans were submitted yesterday. He pointed out
that on the new matrix, all requirements are met except for maximum GFA which
has a discrepancy and a problem with the articulation along the west wall of
the building. Mr. D’Onofrio said that, although they have not been able to work
these issues out as yet with the architect, the petitioner does not plan to ask
for any variations as this is new construction and will do what they can to
comply with the requirements.
Trustee Greenough recused herself from the discussion because she lives next
door to this property.
The School’s architect, Mr. Eric Risinger, commented that after doing an
extensive study, it was determined that it would be more expensive to renovate
the house than to build a new one. He said that, in requesting the down-zoning,
the School is acknowledging that it could not take advantage of this property
if it decided to expand the school. He noted that the school will be building
something that will fit in with the character of the neighborhood and he said
that it is their intention to comply with the zoning requirements in place and
will work to bring the GFA and articulation into compliance. In response to a
question from Trustee Brower about drainage, Mr. Risinger replied that they had
consulted with an expert and will be putting a drain into the yard to accept
storm water.
Trustee Aquilino clarified that the Council is only deciding on the re-zoning
request at this time.
Manager Williams clarified that, if a variation is requested, it would have to
come back before the Board separate from this re-zoning.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
approving the requested rezoning. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   M-1-2002 – Zoning Variation: 975 Private Rd. - Introduction. Manager
Williams explained that the subject variation request is the result of
discussions among the owners, the Building Review Committee, and the Landmark
Preservation Commission Chairman in an attempt to preserve the existing
residence, which has been identified as a Howard Van Doren Shaw coachhouse.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the particulars of the front setback variation that is
being requested to permit the relocation of the existing residence and
subsequent additions.
The homeowner, Mr. John Marzonie, asked the Council to review his plans,
although the second floor has not been finalized, and give him an indication of
how the Council would vote if he were to renovate the existing home instead of
demolishing it and building new. He asked that they consider the three front
facing garages, one of which is set back from the others, and also allow the
20-foot utility easement to be cleaned up so that he can use a portion of it
for additions to the house in order to make it more livable for his family.
After discussing the homeowner’s plans, and after Mr. D’Onofrio reported the
results of the Zoning Board of Appeals meeting, the consensus of the Council
was that they are favorably inclined towards the renovation of the existing
house. President Duhl noted that two of the trustees had commented on the
issues of the garage doors and the setback and also noted that proper
procedures would be followed to comply with state laws regarding any requests
for variation. He assured the homeowner that Village staff would discuss the
options that are available for the utility easement, taking into consideration
the regulations concerning the placement of pipes in proximity to one another.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance granting a zoning variation for a front setback. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   Resolution No. R-3-2002 – 2002 MFT Transfer - Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that, historically, the Village authorizes the allocation of $125,000
from the MFT Fund towards street maintenance, which is a permitted use of those
funds.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Resolution No. R-2-2002 – Release of Executive Session Minutes - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the minutes of
the Executive Sessions held June 19, 2001 through December 18, 2001. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino then moved to adopt Resolution No. R-2-2002. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Greenough. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and
Woodbury Nays: None. Absent: None.
8. Old Business.
a)   Village Yards Project, 1390 Willow Road – Bid Authorization. President
Duhl explained that the Council has been asked to authorize Village staff to
begin obtaining bids for the Village Yards project.
Mr. Irv Footlick commented upon several items that he had discussed with the
Director of Public Works, Steve Saunders, to help cut the costs associated with
this project. He also presented a schematic plan that places parking in the
front of the building, with landscaping and a berm, and suggested that the
Council ask the architects to do a perspective of the whole building with
parking at the front to see how it could be done.
Trustee Woodbury said that he understands that Mr. Footlik has spent a lot of
time on this and with the Public Works Director and hopes that this dialogue
will continue, but that the Technical Committee had been adamant about not
placing parking in front of the building. He said he is against parking at the
front of the building.
Trustee Greenough further explained that, since the building is on the most
prominent entrance to the Village, the technical committee was trying to
preserve the open space of Winnetka when it was decided that there would be no
parking in front of the building.
President Duhl thanked Mr. Footlik for his input and said that the Council
agrees with Trustee Woodbury.
Manager Williams commented that several issues Mr. Footlik had brought to Mr.
Saunders’ attention may enable the Village to reduce the cost of the project
and he is investigating alternatives.
President Duhl called for a motion to authorize staff to obtain bids for the
renovation and expansion of the Village Yards. Trustee Greenough so moved. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Presser. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1253 and 1254. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1253 in the amount of $564,678.18 and Warrant
List No 1254 in the amount of $258,536.27. Trustee Presser seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Maintenance Responsibility for Water and Sewer Service Lines Serving
Private Residences. As announced earlier, this item was removed from the
Agenda.
c)   Change Order: Tower Road Rehabilitation – Phase I. As announced earlier,
this item was removed from the Agenda.
d)   Purchase of New Garbage Truck. Mr. Williams reviewed a request from the
Public Works Department to waive the public bidding process for the purchase of
a refuse truck replacement and approve the purchase of a chassis from Northwest
Trucks, Inc. in the amount of $59,788 and the purchase of the packer body from
R.N.O.W., Inc., in the amount of $47,203.
Alex Attiah, Assistant Director of Public Works, further explained that prices
were obtained from another bidder, but that since the price increased by only
1.8% and since the Village would like to use the same chassis and packer for
all of its trucks for ease of maintenance, etc., they recommend the purchase
from Northwest Trucks and RNOW, Inc.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to waive the competitive
bidding process. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the purchase of
the chassis and packer body as recommended by the Public Works Department. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Quarterly Capital Improvement Reports. Mr. Williams referred the Trustees
to the departments’ quarterly capital improvement reports included in their
agenda packets and said that all of the anticipated work has been completed,
except for the painting of the pedestrian bridges because of negotiations for
lease of the train station with the railroad.
10. Reports.
Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Council will be holding its first
budget study session on January 28 at 7:30 p.m. Budgets are available in the
Village Manager’s office and at the library.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Aquilino,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:32 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:10 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Aquilino, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:11 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
February 5, 2002
(Approved February 19, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 5, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and about 22
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag and welcomed
Boy Scout Troop 18.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 9(a) regarding a request from New Trier High
School had been removed from the Agenda at the school’s request. The Council
approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   February 12, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   February 19, 2002, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5.   Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 15, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Public Comment and Questions.
At the request of Mr. Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., President Duhl moved this
portion of the meeting from its standard position on the Agenda.
Mr. Kates expressed objection to a letter being circulated by the Village
regarding the undergrounding of electrical wires that he perceives forces the
recipient to either pay the costs associated with installing a pedestal and
upgrading their electrical service or pay for a utility pole. No option is
available to those residents who wish to do neither. He voiced doubt that the
Council has the appropriate authority to impose such a choice on residents.
Manager Williams responded that he would get back to Mr. Kates after consulting
with the Water & Electric Department and the Village Attorney.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions
.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2002 - Request for Rezoning: Hadley School for the
Blind, 705 Oak Street – Adoption. Trustee Greenough recused herself from the
vote on this issue because she lives immediately adjacent to the property.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
rezoning the parcel of property owned by Hadley School for the Blind from
Multiple Family Residential (B-1) to Single Family Residential (R-5) in order
to permit the School to replace the existing single family residence on the
property with a new single family residence. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough (recusal).
b)   Ordinance No. M-1-2002 – Zoning Variation: 975 Private Rd. – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a variation from the front setback provisions of the Zoning Ordinance
to permit relocation of the existing residence and subsequent additions
thereto. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-2-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1417 Edgewood Ln. -
Introduction. Trustee Brower disclosed the relative proximity of his house to
the property in question. He indicated that he would participate in the
discussion but abstain from any final vote.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for variances from the side yard, rear yard,
garage, and building line articulation provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to
permit construction of a two and one-half story addition. He explained that the
house is one of the oldest in the immediate vicinity, with both the residence
and the attached garage, added at the rear, predating the Village’s first
zoning ordinance. As the orientation of the existing garage and driveway make
entering and exiting difficult, as proposed the addition would eliminate the
present garage building and add a front-facing, below-grade garage.
David Roeser, the applicant, spoke in support of his request. He explained the
need to expand the functional living space of the home and mentioned that a
number of alternative designs had been considered. He said that the proposed
design seemed to be the most efficient and inexpensive means to meet his
family’s needs, as well as preserve the property’s park-like front yard.
Michael Breseman, the architect, explained the reasoning behind his proposed
design, including the desire to preserve a very large tree on the property. He
also responded to questions from Trustees Greenough, Aquilino and Woodbury
about possible design alternatives.
Trustee Woodbury commented that the existing house is certainly set far back on
the property, but that to increase the bulk so significantly in the rear raises
serious questions about encroachment on the neighbors. The applicant expressed
his belief that the proposed addition really only impacts neighbors’ garages.
Trustee Brower inquired about plans to deal with drainage and runoff to which
the applicant responded.
Brenda Olsen, 1414 Asbury, voiced concern about the effect the proposed
addition would have on drainage into her yard, as well as light to her house.
In addition, she expressed reservations about how, with the very minimal
setback, the addition could be constructed without workmen using her property.
She suggested that the applicant design an addition that works from the body of
the main house, rather than from the back of the existing garage, especially
when there is a substantial amount of property in the front of the house to
accommodate such an addition.
Trustee Powell indicated that he was bothered more by the added bulk to the
rear of the property than by the addition on the east side.
Diantha Olschan, 1436 Edgewood, said that while she is not opposed to the
addition, she thought the issues of mass and density needed to be addressed and
taken into consideration, especially given the fact that the footprint of the
proposed new house will be greater than if a new home were to be built on the
lot. She said that the rear neighbors’ view, particularly if the width of the
house is doubled as proposed, will be of a massive structure, 44’ wide and 2-1/
2 stories high. She also expressed her opinion that no variation be granted on
the east boundary.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that the house would be wider and denser than any of
the others and questioned the necessity to make the setback from the back of
the garage line instead of from the back of the primary structure.
Trustee Brower commented that everyone agrees that the house needs to be
brought up to more liveable standards but there are more palatable alternatives
available to do so.
Steve Jones, 1422 Asbury, indicated that the proposed design would place a lot
of mass at the corner of his backyard where he doesn’t have the screen of a
garage. He also expressed concern about its impact on an already existing
drainage problem.
Mick Girardi, 1411 Edgewood, a licensed architect, noted that there is no way
to improve the property that will not require a variance. He voiced no strong
objection to the proposed plan.
Elise Covey, 1503 Edgewood, asked that the impact on drainage be carefully
examined before proceeding and agreed with Ms. Olsen that a 3’ setback does not
permit for an efficient construction process. She concurred that the back of
the primary residence, at approximately 17 ft., would be a more appropriate
back line for any development.
Trustee Powell expressed serious objections to the proposal on the grounds that
the expansion in the rear greatly increases the bulk in the back of the
property encroaching on neighbors. He said he could see no integrity to the use
of the existing rear building line of the garage for establishing a point of
reference, and that he is opposed to permitting a structure that will end up
being wider than normally allowed. Finally, he remarked that although the
Council has made many decisions in order to preserve trees, permitting the
requested variation on those grounds is going too far.
Trustee Presser concurred with Trustee Powell.
Trustee Greenough applauded the applicant’s creative design, but said she was
troubled by the overall size. She said there seem to be alternative means to
achieve the same goal demonstrating more sensitivity to the overall bulk that
this imposes on the neighborhood.
Mr. Roeser expressed his frustration with trying to save an older home to
improve it when the simplest, and perhaps most inexpensive solution, would be
for him, or someone else, to demolish the residence and build a new home that
would conform both to Code and the rest of the block.
Trustee Presser acknowledged that that was certainly an option.
Trustee Brower voiced his belief that there are still alternatives to be
explored and said that he would be comfortable permitting some variances on the
property in order to permit improvements that are not so intrusive to the rear
lot line.
Trustee Woodbury said that the Council is willing to grant variations where
appropriate in order to improve older homes, but the extent of the variations
requested here are out of bounds. He said he believes there are workable
alternatives to explore and does not believe that the only option is to
demolish the house.
Saying that he hears a consensus among the Trustees that they would deny the
requested variations if the matter were put to a vote, President Duhl explained
the options available to the applicant.
In response to a question from Mr. Breseman as to what the components of any
future, successful design might be, President Duhl noted that all of the
Trustees seemed to agree that at least a 17-foot rear setback would be an
acceptable compromise. Trustee Powell added that he believed staying within the
8-foot setback on the east was also important.
Mr. Roeser informed President Duhl that he did not wish to ask for a vote at
this time, so no action was taken.
d)   Resolution No. R-1-2002 – 518 Winnetka Avenue: Development Agreement -
Adoption. Attorney Janega summarized the key provisions of the proposed
Development Agreement and informed the Council that the final remaining policy
issue, indemnification, had been resolved with the applicant accepting staff’s
proposed language. She pointed out that the Agreement incorporates the
Mobilization Plan approved by the Director of Public Works; sets out standards
for construction parking; and imposes restrictions on construction traffic.
Ms. Janega also explained recent legislation passed by the State of Illinois
regarding the forms of construction security allowed and discussed its
ramifications on the Village’s agreements.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution
authorizing execution of the Development Agreement between the Village and 518
Winnetka, L.L.C., substantially in the form outlined by Ms. Janega. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury and Greenough. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-4-2002 – Authorization of Ravine Outfall Grant
Application – Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that the Department of Natural
resources recently announced a Great Lakes Coastal Restoration Grant Program
that provides funding for certain types of coastal restoration projects along
Lake Michigan. The program provides an 80% Federal match to local funds for
qualifying projects. One of the Village’s projects that could qualify for this
grant is the Fisher Lane Outfall Rehabilitation, with the creation of a coastal
wetland at the base of the outfall. This wetland would provide water quality
benefits by filtering out pollutants before they reach Lake Michigan, as well
as provide an educational and aesthetic environmental amenity. The proposed
resolution is required as a part of the grant application and would authorize
the work, as well as that monies have been budgeted to cover the local share of
the cost. He noted that the Village’s proposed budget contains $35,000 for the
construction of this project, which if the grant is obtained, would allow for a
total project cost of $175,000.
Manager Williams commented that if the grant is approved, the Village would
wind up with a much better project for the same amount of money that has
already been budgeted for it. He mentioned that the Village would be working in
conjunction with Northwestern and Northeastern University and also noted that
any project design plans would come back to the Council for approval.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
authorizing the Village to file for financial assistance and to obligate the
funds required for satisfactory completion of the proposed project in order to
become eligible for reimbursement under the grant program. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
8. Old Business.
None
9. New Business
a)   NTHS Request: Wes Baumann Avenue. As announced previously, this item was
removed from the Agenda.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1255 and 1256. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1255 in the amount of $952,985.01 and Warrant
List No 1256 in the amount of $592,687.71. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Maintenance Responsibility for Water and Sewer Service Lines Serving
Private Residences. Indicating that this item would best be discussed at a
future Study Session, President Duhl suggested that the issue be tabled. The
Trustees concurred.
d)   Change Order: Tower Road Rehabilitation – Phase I. Mr. Saunders explained
that the requested Change Order in the amount of $11,966.70 results from a
final balancing of quantities, plus four additional items of work completed
towards the end of the project.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the Change
Order, as requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
e)   Stormwater Management Study – West of Hibbard Rd. Mr. Saunders explained
that over the past several years, the Village has received numerous complaints
about the effectiveness of the stormwater drainage system serving the area west
of Hibbard Road and south of Tower Road that has been subject to periodic
moderate to severe stormwater flooding over the years. In response to these
complaints, the Village has programmed funds in its FY 2002-2003 and ‘03-’04
Capital Improvements Plans to address the stormwater drainage systems in this
area. He noted that the drainage system in these areas consists partly of
Village operated infrastructure, Park District operated infrastructure, and
partly private storm sewer systems in areas served by private roads.
He explained that as a part of the engineering work for this project the
Village sent out a Request for Proposal in order to determine the type and
level of improvements needed to provide adequate stormwater drainage for the
service area. This RFP called for engineering services to perform hydrologic
and hydraulic modeling and analyses of the drainage basins affected by the
flooding, as well as of the system draining the water away from the
neighborhoods. He mentioned that the Park District also has budgeted funds for
this project, so they will be working with the Village on the engineering and
construction portions of the project.
Trustee Aquilino inquired whether the private roads in the area also would be
participating in the cost. Mr. Saunders said that the study has to be completed
in any event; once that is completed, there might be discussions about their
sharing some portion of the improvement costs.
Of the bids received, staff recommends that the Village enter a contract with
Stanley Consultants for a fee not to exceed $34,408, to perform the study.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to authorize a contract
with Stanley Consultants, as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid No. 02-003 – Sanitary Sewer Lining. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids to
provide trenchless relining to the existing sanitary sewers located throughout
the Village. He recommended that the bid be awarded to Visu-Sewer Clean & Seal,
Inc., in the total amount of $35,935.20.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to award the bid, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
g)   Bid No. 02-002: Pumping Station Improvements. Manager Williams reviewed
the bids to furnish labor, equipment and materials to install complete Willow
Road Pumping Station Improvements. He reported staff’s and the Village’s
consultant’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to Grove Mechanical
Contractors, Inc., in the amount of $46,888.00.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bid, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
h)   RFP-02-001: Breathing Air Compressor with Purification System. Manager
Williams reviewed the bids for a complete breathing air compressor system ,
including installation and training. He reported staff’s recommendation that
the bid be awarded to Air One Equipment, Inc., in the amount of $29,543.00.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to award the bid, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
i)   Fire Department New Staff Car Purchase – FY 2002-2003. Manager Williams
reported that the Fire Department would like to purchase a 2002 GMC Yukon for
$28,536.00 to be charged to the FY 2002-2003 budget via the recently received
State of Illinois bid. In order to insure the price and delivery times, order
must be placed before the start of FY 2002-2003.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
Manager: Mr. Williams reported that the Village’s second budget meeting would
be held on February 12th. The Public Works, Police, and Finance Departments
will be presenting their proposed budgets for the coming year.
Trustees. Trustee Woodbury reported that he had attended a recent meeting of
the Historical Society. The Society will be ramping up its fund raising
campaign, as well as accelerating its efforts to move the Log House.
Trustee Aquilino reported that the Ad Hoc Beautification Committee held its
final meeting on January 16th. She thanked all of the members for their time
and participation, saying that the Committee had successfully accomplished its
mission of initiating a beautification program throughout the Village and, in
general, raising awareness of and sensitivity to beautification efforts.
Trustee Brower reported on a recent meeting of the Winnetka Youth Organization,
saying that he expected they would be returning to the Village for funding.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Aquilino,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:33 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Aquilino, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:34 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
February 19, 2002
(Approved March 5, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 19, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:28 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Deputy Police Chief Eric Bennett, and no persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   February 25, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   March 5, 2002, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Powell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5.   Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 28, 2002, Budget Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   February 5, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   February 12, 2002, Budget Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions
.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2002 – Amending Village Code to Prohibit Use of
Groundwater for Potable Water Supply - Introduction. Attorney Janega explained
that this ordinance along with the following resolution are a part of a package
that would formalize an easement agreement with the Village of Glencoe. That
agreement would allow the Village’s underground electric cable connection to
the transmission grid to remain in its current location, on property that the
Village of Glencoe purchased from the Forest preserve District of Cook County
and is preparing to sell. The proposed easement agreement contains a provision
requiring adoption of an ordinance that prohibits the use of groundwater for a
potable water supply. The intent of the amendment is to allow a lower standard
for environmental cleanup of the property. She pointed out, however, that the
ordinance would also lower standards required for property under development
within the Village as well. Currently, the highest level of residential clean-
up is required in the Village. She noted that one development that could be
affected is the 518 Winnetka property, although the Village has a development
agreement in place with the developer of that property. Ms. Janega indicated
that she is working with Glencoe’s Village Attorney and environmental counsel
to arrive at wording that meets Glencoe’s development needs without
compromising Winnetka’s standards. She recommended that the Council not proceed
with the resolution approving the easement agreement until the groundwater
ordinance issue has been resolved, but suggested that it proceed with
introduction of this ordinance at this time in order to keep the process moving
forward.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance
prohibiting the use of groundwater for potable use. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   Resolution No. R-6-2002 – Approval of Easement Agreement with the Village
of Glencoe - Adoption. Action on this item was deferred to a later date.
c)   Resolution No. R-5-2002 – Byrne-Heller Subdivision (999 Hill Rd. & 101
Thorntree Ln. - Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the proposed subdivision, in
which the actual transfer of property occurred between 1962 and 1965, without a
required resubdivision. He noted that the subject plat of resubdivision was
required by the Community Development Department to update property records to
match ownership and to correct the pervious owners’ failure to have a
resubdivision plat approved and recorded.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Plan Commission had approved the proposed
subdivision and a variation from the Subdivision Ordinance requiring all side
lines of lots on curved streets to be radial to the curved street.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution
approving the relocation of the shared property line between the two
properties. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1257 and 1258. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1257 in the amount of $600,005.71 and Warrant
List No 1258 in the amount of $253,564.90. Trustee Presser seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   NORCOM Bid. Deputy Police Chief Bennett reported that the age of the radio
backbone equipment that makes up the Village’s main public safety and 9-1-
1 communications system is outdated and has become a serious concern. He
explained that the five NORCOM police departments had requested bids for
replacement of the system. The only vendor to respond was Motorola, Inc. The
bid was reviewed subsequently by a technical committee, and some modifications
were incorporated. The total cost of the replacement system will be
$672,414.00, of which Winnetka’s portion, based on number of sworn police
officers and populations, will be 20.92%, or $140,669.00.
Manager Williams expressed strong support for Chief Bennett’s recommendation.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bid, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Reports.
Manager: Mr. Williams reported that records show that use of the Village’s
website has increased substantially and continuously over the last three years.
Trustees. Trustees Aquilino and Brower reported on a recent meeting of the
chamber of Commerce.
Trustee Presser announced that the next edition of the Winnetka Report has gone
to press and should be delivered to residents in the next couple of weeks
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously: Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Aquilino,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 7:52 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:00 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Aquilino, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:01 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                     Deputy Clerk
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
March 5, 2002
(Approved March 19, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
5, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: Trustee John Powell. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Public
Works Steven Saunders and six persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that that Item 6(h), Resolution Establishing Electric
Rates, will be considered at the end of the meeting in conjunction with the
discussion of Item 9(d), Electric Undergrounding Policy. The Council approved
the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   March 12, 2002, Study Session. Trustee Woodbury indicated that he would be
unable to attend. The Trustees decided to reschedule the meeting for some
future date when all could be present.
b)   March 19, 2002, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5.   Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 19, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   February 25, 2002, Budget Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions
.
a)   Resolution No. R-15-2002 – Establishing Police Chief’s Salary – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
setting the salary for the new Police Chief. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2002 – Village Vehicle Licenses - Introduction. Mr.
McKee explained that the proposed ordinance would exempt Village vehicles from
Village license requirements. He indicated that the Village’s payment of its
own licensing fee merely reallocates Village funds rather than generating any
revenue to cover the Village’s administrative costs involved. In addition, the
information gathered during the licensing process duplicates information
already maintained by the Village. Consequently, because of the time involved
in complying with the licensing requirements, an operating cost is imposed that
is not offset by either revenue or a measurable public benefit.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance
exempting Village vehicles from Village licensing requirements. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Resolution No. R-7-2002 – Fiscal Year 2002-2003 Budget – Public Hearing
and Adoption. President Duhl opened the Public Hearing. Mr. McKee briefly
reviewed the budget process and summarized the budget. He indicated that the
proposed budget includes two rate adjustments, a 3% water rate increase and a
10% increase in the sewer rate, amounting to approximately $28 per year for the
typical homeowner. Once finalized and implemented, all the changes in the
Budget will result in a typical homeowner’s paying about $4,514 in 2002/2003, a
$79 or 1.8% increase from the current amount.
President Duhl then asked for public comments. There being no comments from the
floor, President Duhl closed the Public Hearing.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the resolution
adopting the Annual Budget for the Fiscal Year beginning April 1, 2002. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough,
Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
d)   Ordinance No. M-3-2002 – Zoning Variation: 220 Birch Street –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from the Front
Setback provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of dormer
additions to the upper floor of the existing residence. These improvements
would allow improvement of the attic space with a playroom, bathroom and
bedroom. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted unanimously in
favor of recommending that this variation be granted.
The project’s architect, Chip Hackley, explained that the main body of the
existing house sits entirely outside of the setback which makes it impossible
to do anything to the home without requiring a variation.
Trustee Greenough remarked that she was delighted to see this older home being
modernized with such sensitivity.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
ordinance granting the requested variation. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-4-2002 – Zoning Variation: 335 Sheridan Rd. –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for variations from the Side
Yard and Building Height provisions of the Zoning Ordinance in order to permit
the construction of an addition to the third floor level of the existing
residence. The applicant is proposing to enlarge the third floor, which exceeds
a height of 2-1/2 stories, and results in a stair tower being located within
the required 12-foot south side yard. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had unanimously recommended that the variations be granted.
The architect, John Fotsch, commented on the improvements and answered
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting the requested variation. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-5-2002 – Zoning Variation: 690 Elder Ln. – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for variations from the Maximum Building Size
and Intensity of Use of Lot requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to permit
construction of a one-story addition at the rear of the existing residence. The
applicants propose to remove two already existing additions and replace these
with one that would enlarge the existing kitchen and powder room. He reported
that the Zoning Board of Appeals had unanimously recommended that the
variations be granted.
Chip Hackley, the architect, explained that the applicant is trying to salvage
an older home and rework the interior to make it workable for a modern family.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance granting the requested variation. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
g)   Resolution No. R-8-2002 – Establishing Water Rates – Introduction. Mr.
McKee explained that over the past few years, the Council has been adopting
rate and fee resolutions each year, as a part of the annual budget process,
regardless of whether rate changes are proposed. This resolution, along with
the two following for electric and sewer rates, are presented in accordance
with this practice. He noted that the water rate resolution, R-8-2002,
implements the 3% water rate increase he mentioned earlier. He said that this
will increase a typical customer’s annual costs by approximately $15.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the resolution
establishing the water. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
h)   Resolution No. R-9-2002 – Establishing Electric Rates – Introduction. As
noted earlier, discussion of this item will be later in the Agenda.
i)   Resolution No. R-10-2002 – Establishing Sewer Rates – Introduction. Mr.
McKee reported that this resolution implements the 10% sewer rate increase
proposed in the 2002-03 Village Budget. This increase will cost a typical
customer an additional $13 per year
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the
resolution establishing the sewer rates. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1259 and 1260. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1259 in the amount of $843,856.69 and Warrant
List No 1260 in the amount of $296,126.90. Trustee Presser seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
b)   Bid No. 02-001: Phase II, Tower Road Rehabilitation Project. Mr. Saunders
reviewed the bids for Phase II of the Tower Road Rehabilitation and Water Main
Replacement. He recommended that the bid be awarded to G. W. Kennedy
Construction Company in the amount of $1,460,211.70. He remarked that staff has
thoroughly checked the references of this contractor on other similar projects
and found them to be both competent and responsible. He noted that this bid is
below the engineering estimate prepared by the Village’s consultant.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid as
recommended, and to authorize the Village President to sign the contract. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Aquilino, Woodbury, Greenough and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
c)   Bid No. 02-004: Parkway Tree Maintenance and Utility Line Clearance. Mr.
Saunders reviewed the bids for maintenance of the Village’s parkway trees. He
reported staff’s recommendation that the bids be awarded as follows:
 
Bidder:                 R. W.       Nels Johnson Asplundh Tree Service
                        Hendricksen
Tree & Stump Removal    $29,220
Parkway Tree Trimming               $17,025
Trimming/Line Clearance             $132.00/hr
Emergency Work                                   $43.16/hr
 
Mr. Saunders commented that the Village Forester has worked with all of these
companies in the past and is satisfied with their work
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to award the bids, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Woodbury, Greenough and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Powell.
d)   Electric Undergrounding Policy. Attorney Janega reviewed the Village’s
current practice for undergrounding the electric distribution system. She
pointed out that the program implements the Village’s Comprehensive Plan,
Winnetka 2020, which establishes a goal of placing the distribution system
along the main thoroughfares underground by 2010. In addition, in view of
recent challenges to the Village’s current practice, she reviewed three options
for allocating the costs associated with the undergrounding, highlighting the
pros and cons of each alternative. Briefly those options are:
i)   Option A -- Maintaining the existing policy, under which the customer is
required to put his service underground and to pay for the meter pedestal and
to make any other changes to his residence to accommodate the underground
connection, with the Village waiving the permit fee for such changes;
ii)   Option B --Maintaining the existing policy, except that the Village would
pay for the customer’s meter pedestal; and
iii)   Option C -- Maintaining the same allocation of costs and waiver of
permit fees, but allowing the customer to choose between placing the service
connection underground or retaining overhead service, with the Village
undergrounding as much of the service line as possible and installing a pole
near the customer’s weatherhead if overhead service is retained.
She explained that for each alternative there are two corresponding legislative
actions: the first being a Code amendment, Ordinance MC-4-2002, to clarify
procedures and the second being a resolution, R-9-2002, modifying the current
rate resolution to clarify the allocation of costs for undergrounding
electrical service. She pointed out that the only differences occur if the
customer chooses not to proceed with undergrounding; otherwise, there is no
change if the customer does underground. She also noted that there is no change
to requiring undergrounding if a customer upgrades or adds new service. The
corresponding resolutions do not change any of the rates charged to
residential, commercial, governmental, or any other class of customer, and
therefore, does not result in a financial change to most customers.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., thanked the Council for reconsidering the existing
policy, which, he said, imposes a hardship on some residents. He urged the
choice of Option C, saying that it addresses the issue of choice and allows
resident input into what they do with their own property.
Trustee Brower expressed support for Option C.
Trustee Woodbury also voiced support for Option C. He also noted that it is
important for the Village to educate the residents about the advantages of
undergrounding and, thereby, to encourage them to choose that alternative.
President Duhl echoed this idea. He urged residents to understand that
undergrounding improves not only aesthetics, but also reliability. While the
implementation of Option C does give them a choice between undergrounding and
having a pole on their property, refusing to choose undergrounding does have an
impact on neighbors. Therefore, the location of the pole will be different in
most circumstances and may become an issue for both parties.
Manager Williams indicated that staff would work on letters to customers
clarifying the issues involved.
After a brief discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed
with Option C.
Trustee Presser moved to introduce the Option C version of MC-4-2002. Trustee
Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser moved to introduce the Option C version of R-9-2002. Trustee
Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl thanked Mr. Kates for bringing the matter to the Council’s
attention.
10. Reports.
Manager: Mr. Williams reported that has been names as 2001 Tree City USA in
recognition of its commitment to an effective, ongoing community forestry
program.
Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the new edition of the Winnetka Report
would be delivered to homes soon and noted that the dates for Spring Clean-up
are in this issue.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell. The Council convened
into Executive Session at 8:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:30 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent:
Trustee Powell. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
The meeting adjourned at 9:31 p.m.
