@@ITEM 2002.03.19 Regular Council Meeting
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
March 19, 2002
(Approved April 2, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
19, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus and four persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   April 9, 2002, Special Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Presser, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   April 16, 2002, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5.   Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 5, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions
.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2002 – Village Vehicle Licenses - Adoption. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance exempting
Village vehicles from Village licensing requirements. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2002 – Electric Pedestal Ownership and Undergrounding –
Adoption. Attorney Janega briefly reviewed the three policy options discussed
at the last meeting. She noted that the changes amend the relevant chapter to
clarify the lines of ownership of the electric system, particularly the meter
pedestal and to add the option of residents retaining overhead service.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance
amending the Village Code as it pertains to electric service ownership and
undergrounding policy. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower.
c)   Ordinance No. M-3-2002 – Zoning Variation: 220 Birch Street – Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting the variation from the front setback requirements to permit
construction of dormer additions to the upper floor. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower,
Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-4-2002 – Zoning Variation: 335 Sheridan Rd. – Adoption.
Trustee Powell said that the proposed construction appears to be very close to
one of the neighbors’ property. He expressed some concern that since they had
not been heard from that they had not received appropriate notice because they
are out of town. Ms. Janega explained that the Village is only required to mail
the notice to the owner of record at their recorded address, but is not
required to pursue the owner further or confirm receipt.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a variation from the Side Yard and Building Height requirements to
permit construction of an enlarged third floor. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-5-2002 – Zoning Variation: 690 Elder Ln. – Adoption.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting the requested variations from the Maximum Building Size and Intensity
of Use of Lot requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to permit construction of a
one-story addition. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-8-2002 – Establishing Water Rates – Adoption. Trustee
Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution
implementing the 3% water rate increase as anticipated in the 2002-2003 budget.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower.
g)   Resolution No. R-9-2002 – Establishing Electric Rates – Adoption. Attorney
Janega noted that this resolution brings the rate policy into conformance with
the Village’s new undergrounding policy, as adopted above in Ordinance No. MC-
4-2002. She pointed out that there are no rate changes.
Manager Williams responded to questions from Trustees Brower and Powell about
certain charges and phases of service.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough.
h)   Resolution No. R-10-2002 – Establishing Sewer Rates – Adoption. Trustee
Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
implementing the 10% sewer rate increase in accordance with the 2002-2003
Budget. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
i)   Ordinance No. M-6-2002 – Zoning Variation: 975 Private Rd. – Introduction.
Mr. Norkus reviewed a request for an attached garage addition with three front-
facing garage doors in conjunction with a request to relocate the existing
residence. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted unanimously
to recommend that the variation be granted.
Trustee Presser commented that in the drawing the doors appear to be very
elegant.
Trustee Greenough remarked that the proposal seems to be a sensible and
sensitive solution to difficult situation, as the doors will be basically
invisible the way they are set back behind a berm.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
ordinance granting a zoning variation to permit construction of an attached
garage addition with three front-facing garage doors. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
j)   Ordinance No. M-7-2002 – Train Station Leases - Introduction. Manager
Williams explained that this ordinance would authorize execution of agreements
with Union Pacific to lease the Elm Street and Hubbard Woods train stations. He
reported that subject to available funding, Metra proposes major renovation
projects for both stations in 2003 and 2004, respectively. These renovations,
however, are dependent upon the Village’s executing leases to take over the
control and maintenance of the stations. He indicated that the provisions of
the leases are consistent with those afforded other North Shore villages, most
of which lease their stations, and pointed out that there is a maximum
liability for repairs to the Village within any given twelve month period. He
indicated that some of the Village’s costs would be offset by revenue from
tenants.
Trustee Woodbury suggested an amendment to the proposed leases regarding the
posting of advertising signs. He also expressed the hope that the quality of
the renovation materials would be in keeping with the existing or original
character.
In response to a question from Trustee Powell Attorney Janega explained that
the Village would only be responsible, vis a vis liability, for its use of the
premises per se, but that all responsible involving issues of access, i.e.
platforms, stairs, elevators, would remain with the railroad.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the ordinance
authorizing execution of the agreements with Union Pacific. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
k)   Resolution No. R-11-2002 – Refuse Service Rates – Adoption. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution setting
the refuse service rates for the coming year the same as last year’s rate. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough,
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury.
l)   Resolution No. R-12-2002 – Miscellaneous Permits, Licenses and Services –
Adoption. Attorney Janega enumerated a few minor changes to the miscellaneous
fees for the coming year, noting especially the insertion of costs for copying
imposed for FOIA responses.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser,
m)   Resolution No. R-14-2002 – Unincorporated Charges for Fire/Ambulance
Service – Adoption. Manager Williams explained that this resolution would
establish charges to Kenilworth and Unincorporated New Trier Township for fire
and paramedic services for the coming year. The fee is formula driven based on
assessed valuation, population, and the number of calls. He reported staff’s
recommendation that the resolution be approved.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
n)   Resolution No. R-6-2002 – Easement Agreement with Glencoe – Adoption.
Attorney Janega summarized the proposed resolution that would authorize
execution of an easement agreement that will allow Winnetka to keep its
underground connection to ComEd’s Northbrook substation in place. She explained
that as originally requested by Glencoe, the Village would be required to pass
a “Potable Water Ordinance,” which could adversely affect clean-up standards
for development underway in the Village. She indicated that a compromise had
been negotiated with Glencoe that does not require amending the Village Code
unless and until the actual contamination area from the site has been
identified. She indicated that if the Village were to decide not to execute the
agreement, it would have to explore other options.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower
7. Old Business.
None
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1261 and 1262. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1261 in the amount of $606,825.07 and Warrant
List No 1262 in the amount of $596,138.72. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid No. 02-005: Tree Planting Program. Manager Williams reviewed the bids
for the purchase and planting of approximately 170 parkway trees and 30 private
trees, in conjunction with the Village’s private tree planting program, within
the Village for the coming year. He explained that the bids were based on both
the variety and size of the trees. He reported staff’s recommendation that the
bids be awarded to St. Aubin Nursery in the amount of $50,190.00 and Arthur
Weiler Nursery in the amount of $4,825.00.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Bid No. 02-006: Landscape Maintenance Services. Mr. Williams reviewed the
bids for landscape maintenance at various locations throughout the Village for
