                     Deputy Clerk
April 2, 2002
DRAFT MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved April 9, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
2, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff and 15
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   April 9, 2002, Special Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Presser, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   April 16, 2002, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 19, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions
. None.
7. Old Business.
a)   Ordinance No. M-6-2002 – Zoning Variation: 975 Private Road – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting the variation to permit the construction of an attached garage
addition with three front-facing garage doors. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
9. Seating of New Village Council.
Local Canvass Board: Canvass and Proclamation and
Administration of Oath of Office for Village Trustees Hugh Brower, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. There being no further unfinished business for
the sitting Council, President Duhl asked the Village Clerk to review the
results of the Local Canvass Board. Village Clerk Williams read the results of
the March 19, 2002 election and reported that the Board had proclaimed Hugh K.
Brower, Stephen B. Presser, and Edmund C. Woodbury duly elected to second terms
as Village Trustees. He administered the Oath of Office to Trustees Brower,
Presser, and Woodbury.
President Duhl introduced the new Police Chief, Joe DeLopez, to the Trustees
and audience and welcomed him to the Village.
10. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1263 and 1264. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1263 in the amount of $818,132.72 and Warrant
List No. 1264 in the amount of $252,111.23. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   GFA Policy Options. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an analysis prepared by staff
of structures built under the most recently adopted (1999) GFA guidelines
presented via PowerPoint. He explained that staff had analyzed 47 homes
constructed since 2000 in all zoning districts including R-5. The following
factors for each home were considered: gross floor area; building height; floor
to floor height; context with respect to adjoining properties; and design. He
noted that the analysis showed several things about building GFA and height, as
follows:
      i)   A significant percentage, 75% or more, of the new homes are built
very close to the maximum GFA.
      ii)   With respect to building height, if the height were calculated to
the ridge of the roofline, not to the mid-point as is currently done, 15 out of
the 47 homes would exceed 35 feet.
      iii)   One significant factor affecting height is the floor-to-floor
dimensions in many homes.
      iv)   A contributing factor to height is how far above grade the first
floor is.
      v)   Many times the most egregious homes on paper turn out to be more
compatible with the neighborhood than homes that do not push the upper limits
of GFA and/or building height.
Mr. D’Onofrio went on to outline a number of recommendations for the Council’s
consideration.
President Duhl remarked that it has become clear that the GFA changes
implemented in 1999 have not effectively decreased the visual bulk of homes.
Attorney Janega commented that while the 1999 amendments affected all zoning
districts somewhat, their predominant effect was on the R-5 district.
In response to a question from Trustee Powell about the practice of measuring
roof height to the midpoint, Manager Williams responded that to the best of his
knowledge, the Village has been doing it this way for a very long time. Mr.
D’Onofrio indicated that this practice is pretty standard for older zoning
ordinances. Mr. Norkus added that it allows for manipulation of actual building
height by lowering/raising the eaves, whereas, the ridge is an absolute
measurement.
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce Street, a local architect and member of the Design
Review Board said that he did not think that changing the roof pitch or
lowering the height requirements would result in significantly different sized
houses.
Mr. Norkus pointed out that two houses with the same GFA square footage can
vary substantially based upon design and a lot depends upon the floor-to-floor
measurements.
Trustee Presser echoed Mr. Norkus’s comment saying that staff’s presentation
makes it clear that design is a key element.
Auste Vygantas, 915 Forest Glen East, voiced her concerns about the effect of
detached garages that currently have no set back requirements and can destroy a
neighbor’s view and sense of privacy. She urged the Trustees to resolve this
issue in conjunction with its review of the Zoning Ordinance.
President Duhl acknowledged this shortcoming in the Ordinance, saying that it
has only been recently that this issue has been brought before the Council.
Linda Davis, 465 Sunset Rd., voiced dismay over the construction of a new, very
tall house in her neighborhood that she considers totally out of keeping with
the neighborhood’s character. She recommended reducing the number of feet that
a basement is allowed to come out of the ground.
Sylvia Footlick, 1548 Tower Rd., inquired whether the Community Development
Department could inspect a property and a neighborhood to see how a proposed
new house will “fit in” before issuing a building permit.
President Duhl said that would be very difficult to do and would put the
Community Development Department in the position of having to make an aesthetic
decision. He said that what the Council wants to do was create objective rules
so that there are no complaints about subjective decisions. He commented that,
“Beauty is in the eye of the beholder.”
Susan Shabica, 326 Ridge Rd., relayed a personal scenario and urged the Council
to look closely at side and rear yard setbacks. She recommended that some
“concessions for amenities,” such as green space, might be granted and
incorporated into any GFA new formula.
Scott Padiak, 980 Greenwood, raised issues about standards. He suggested
reducing the percentage of floor covering permitted as floors go up, also
reducing the FAR ratio for large lots in the smaller zoning districts.
Mr. Frank suggested that the Council permit smaller front porches as a way to
improve visual amenities. He also cautioned against reducing the FAR on smaller
lots, saying that it is already difficult to create a design that has the
desired living space. In response to a question from Mr. Woodbury, he indicated
that he thought that reducing the height of basements could be positive. He
commented on Glencoe’s “plane of light” solution that seems to be successful in
lowering rooflines and creating some additional setbacks on the second floor.
One of his suggestions was to reduce the permissible percentage coverage of the
footprint. He also recommended that houses that exceed the height limitation be
penalized and indicated that, perhaps, new construction should be required to
take the height of adjacent houses into consideration and be at grade.
Saying that this is a classic example of individual property rights vs.
community property rights, President Duhl pointed out that this latter
suggestion ultimately would affect property values of some individual property
owners. Historically this Village has supported the rights of the individual
property owner who wants to sell his property at the maximum price; imposing
more building restrictions might impact property values.
Trustee Aquilino remarked that small lots call for small houses and that,
maybe, all lots shouldn’t have full-sized homes. She inquired about the
possibility of applying a volume approach.
Manager Williams indicated that he was not aware of any community in which this
had been tried and, therefore, there are no volume standards. He also pointed
out that setting a maximum GFA, regardless of lot size, for any zoning district
goes back to the question of impact on property values.
Trustee Woodbury concurred with Mr. Frank’s suggestion about front porches.
The Trustees discussed the audience comments and suggestions at length.
Due to the lateness of the hour, President Duhl proposed that discussion of the
next two items be postponed to anther date and that the Council schedule a
Special Meeting to continue discussion of the GFA issue. The Council agreed to
meet on Saturday, April 13th at 9:30 a.m. in the Council Chambers. President
Duhl invited everyone interested in the matter to attend that meeting.
c)   Proposed Revisions to the Building Permit Fee Schedule. Due to the
lateness of the hour, this item was postponed to a future meeting.
d)   Analysis of Charges for Service Undergrounding Merchandising & Jobbing.
Due to the lateness of the hour, this item was postponed to a future meeting.
11. Reports.
None.
12. Appointments.
President Duhl called for a motion to appoint of Trustee Aquilino as President
Pro Tem for the coming year. The motion to do so was made by Trustee Brower,
seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl then announced the Trustees' committee assignments for the
coming year, as follows: exactly as before, except for web page review has been
eliminated;
President Pro Tem              Barbara Aquilino
Beautification Liaison         Nan Greenough
Chamber of Commerce            Barbara Aquilino
                               Hugh Brower
                               Stephen Presser, Chairman,
Code Review Committee          Ed Woodbury
                               Nan Greenough
Museum Liaison                 Ed Woodbury
Northwest Municipal Conference John Powell
Plan Commission                Nan Greenough
Red Center                     John Powell
                               Hugh Brower (Alternate)
                               Mike Duhl
Technical Committee            John Lien
                               Ed Woodbury
Warrants Review                Hugh Brower
Winnetka Report                Stephen Presser
Youth                          Hugh Brower
 
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 10:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:10 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:11 p.m.
