                  Deputy Clerk
April 9, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: April 16, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
9, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio and 26 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   April 13, 2002, Special Study Session. All of the Trustees present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   April 16, 2002, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 2, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-12-2002 – Special Use Permit: 1321 Willow Road -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request from New Trier High School
District 203 for a Special Use Permit and Certificate of Appropriateness to
allow construction of four dugouts to replace existing dugouts at Duke Childs
Field. He pointed out that at the request of the Design Review Board the
materials proposed to be used have been revised to utilize a brick to match the
color and size of those to be used at the new Public Works Facility. He also
mentioned that the request is similar to one that was approved by the Zoning
Board of Appeals and the Village Council in 1997 but has since lapsed.
In reporting action by the various Village boards, Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the
DRB had issued favorable comment on the proposed improvements after their
suggested alterations were approved; the Plan Commission commented unfavorably
with regard to the height of the fence and expressed concern about both the
color and material of the roof but did find the proposal consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan; and the Zoning Board of Appeals voted to recommend
approval. Since board action, NTHS staff has reduced the proposed height.
Henry Bangser, Superintendent of the NTHS District, spoke in support of the
application and thanked both the Village staff and the School’s staff for their
support in putting it together. He indicated that the chain link structures
that currently serve as dugouts are inadequate and inferior compared with those
provided by other comparable schools.
Trustee Brower inquired as to where the funding would come from for the new
dugouts. Dr. Bangser responded that one-third would come from each of the
following: the school, private donations, and the Booster Club.
Manager Williams asked how far back the closest dugout is to Willow Road. The
response was 72 ft. from the curb of the road.
Gwen Trindle, 602 Provident, Chairman of the Plan Commission, voiced concern
about the proposal , saying that historically the Village has been committed to
keeping permanent structures from being placed on this property. She stated
that the proposed structures are not small and will have a significant impact
on the now-open site that serves as an entry to the Village.
President Duhl asked Ms. Trindle whether she had expressed her concerns to the
Plan Commission when the matter was raised with them. She indicated that she
had but not as clearly as now. She stated her belief that the Commission had
been influenced by the prior Council approval of the project and did not fully
consider the possibility that it could disapprove of the plan.
Trustee Powell inquired about the proposed landscaping. Mr. Nieweem said that
the School planned to install vines to camouflage the dugouts.
Trustee Greenough opposed the proposal, saying that the dugouts are larger than
a school bus, which she deems to be too big, too permanent, and contrary to the
philosophy of open space that has worked well for many years.
Manager Williams indicated that when the Village sold the field to the School,
there was an article in the agreement saying the School could construct
permanent structures on it, although up until 1997 the Village has denied
requests to do so.
Trustee Aquilino indicated that she was in favor of the permanent dugouts ,
saying that what is there now serves no useful purpose in that they are open to
the elements and have no storage capability.
Carter Page, 38 Abbotsford, commented on the Plan Commission meeting. He stated
that he believes these structures are simply too large and urged the Council to
ask the School to look at alternatives. He indicated that while the Plan
Commission approved of the concept of improved dugouts, they were highly
ambivalent about their size and proportions but that these subjects were not
within that Committee’s purview.
Trustee Woodbury asked why in the 1997 proposal the proposed height was shorter
than currently proposed.
Mike Napoleon, head baseball coach, indicated that given the proposed sloped
roof, they needed to be taller to accommodate the height of the team members,
as well as provide some storage in the back for equipment. He remarked that
with the sloping roof, from the rear, people will see the lowest point, not the
top of the structure
Trustee Woodbury stated that he sees the new dugouts as an improvement over the
present structures but requested that the School submit a complete landscape
proposal before approval.
Dr. Bangser indicated that the School would be willing to appropriately
landscape the dugouts and will return with completed landscaping drawings for
Council’s consideration.
Trustees Brower and Powell commented favorably on the proposal, and Trustee
Brower moved to introduce the ordinance approving the Special Use Permit and
Certificate of Appropriateness. Trustee Powell seconded that motion. By voice
vote, the motion carried; all of the Trustees, except Trustee Greenough, voted
favorably.
b)   Ordinance No. M-11-2002 – Special Use Permit and Certificate of
Appropriateness: 1112 & 1140 Willow Road – Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio
explained the request from the Winnetka Park District and Winnetka Public
School District 36 for front setback and side yard variations to permit
replacement of playground equipment at Crow Island School and Crow Island
Woods. The two entities are applying jointly for the permit and variations as
the play equipment used by the school is actually on Park District land. He
noted that plans are underway for the renovation of additional playgrounds at
the school. Due to the multiple playgrounds serving the same school and the
short time frame during which all will be renovated, the Park District and the
School District have submitted all playground renovation plans as they are
currently developed. He reviewed each of those plans briefly along with the
Village boards’ comments.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the
ordinance granting front setback and side yard variations and a Special Use
Permit to allow for the replacement of playground equipment in the northwest
and southwest playgrounds on the subject property. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-10-2002 – Zoning Variation: 562 Green Bay Road -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from the Parking
section of the Zoning Ordinance to permit expansion of the existing language
school into second floor space without providing the required two on-site
parking spaces. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended the
variation be granted.
Noting that the school is landlocked and there is no place to provide parking,
Maria Kurt, the applicant, explained that she plans to reconfigure the school
more than expand it. Her plan is to eliminate a small apartment on the second
floor and expand the school into that space.
Manager Williams pointed out that when the Zoning Ordinance was amended, it was
intended that parking be required if commercial space was added on the second
floor. He said that this is not the case in this instance where there is no
real change in use because there will now be no residents requiring parking
occupying the space.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-8-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1162 Asbury. – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a side yard variation to permit
construction of an open front porch, enclosure of an existing screened porch,
and expansion of a second floor dormer. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had unanimously recommended granting the variation.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-7-2002 – Train Station Leases - Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that this ordinance would authorize the Village President to sign
lease agreements with Union Pacific for the Village to take over the control
and maintenance of the Elm Street and Hubbard Woods passenger stations
effective May 1, 2002.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
f)   Resolution No. R-18-2002 – Boehrer’s Resubdivision – Amended Approval –
Adoption. Ms. Janega explained that this resolution would amend the conditions
for the previously approved resubdivision for 1310 Forest Glen North. The need
for the amendment resulted from the erroneous inclusion of an unnecessary
restrictive covenant in the original resolution approving the subdivision.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough,
Brower, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
g)   Ordinance No. M-9-2002 – Zoning Variation: 222 Essex Road - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a front setback variation arising out of
the proposed consolidation of two existing adjoining lots into a single lot.
The applicants proposed to demolish the existing residence and detached garage
at 357 Sunset and expand the residence on 222 Essex. This development and
expansion would be made possible by the resulting increased lot size. Upon
consolidation, the increased lot width and area require an increased corner
yard setback of 30 feet, rendering the existing 17.5-foot setback
nonconforming.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended that the
variance be granted, and the Building Review Committee recommended that a
demolition permit be issued. In addition, the Plan Commission voted to
recommend preliminary plat approval for the proposed consolidation. However,
they expressed some concern about the impact of a large lot and larger house on
the neighborhood and about creating an unusually large lot on the block.
Bill Reedy, the applicant, spoke in support of his application, pointing to
favorable comments from neighbors and the fact that it will decrease the
density and amount of impervious surface in the neighborhood, as well as
preserve their existing home’s architectural integrity.
Attorney Janega addressed several drafter’s notes incorporated into the draft
ordinance. She expressed some concern about the Zoning Board’s findings in that
they identified no practical difficulties or unique hardship. In addition,
there was no finding on the issue of reasonable return. She outlined the
options available to the Council, noting that the absence of Zoning Board
findings does not preclude the Council from acting and merely changes the vote
required for approval.
Greg Moon, the architect, also spoke in support of the application. He
expressed his belief that the practical difficulty is that the lots are both
narrow. He pointed out that all of the other proposed changes conform to the
Zoning Ordinance.
Manager Williams highlighted the fact that this situation brings forth an
important policy question: Should the Village allow lot consolidations in order
to permit construction of larger houses, especially when the lots are in the
smaller zoning districts? Does the Village have zoning districts that are
trying to establish a character of the zone? Or, are we zoning by lot? Or, are
we zoning by design?
President Duhl suggested that one option would be not to permit a house on a
consolidated lot to go oversize. He pointed out that the current proposal does
not call for the construction of a house that meets the maximum GFA allowable
by virtue of the increased size of the property. However, as the lots are
consolidated, an owner certainly could propose to max out GFA on the property.
Another suggestion would be to put a cap on the permitted expansion.
Trustee Woodbury reiterated concerns raised at the April 2nd meeting about the
adequacy of the sliding scale for GFA. He indicated that although the
applicant’s design is attractive and appealing, he has a problem with building
a very big house in a zoning district in which smaller lots and smaller homes
predominate. He said that he thinks the breaking up of the pattern of the
neighborhood would be wrong and expressed disagreement with the Plan
Commission’s argument that it is acceptable because the property is close to
the R-4 district.
Trustee Aquilino inquired as to the height of the existing home and was
informed that it was 32’ to the ridgeline. She indicated that she felt that the
fact that this is an improvement to an existing home mitigates the concerns.
Trustee Greenough commented that the Plan Commission had really struggled with
this issue, but was swayed by the articulated design, which minimizes the bulk
visible along sunset. She said that she also felt that had this house been in
the middle of the block face, instead of on the end, it would not have passed.
The design was highly influential because it minimizes bulk and presents a
sensitive street appearance.
Mr. Reedy commented that they had gone to extraordinary lengths to work with
the neighbors to create an acceptable plan.
President Duhl explained the courses of action open to the Council: either
proceed with the introduction of the ordinance as it has been drafted, with the
Council determining the two issues identified earlier by Ms. Janega, or send
the matter back to the ZBA for more definitive findings. He recommended that if
the Trustees do not feel that they want to see as much square footage as
proposed on the combined lot, they make that point clear at this time.
Trustee Powell expressed support for the application, saying that he believes
the particulars of the present house constitute a hardship for this family.
Trustees Brower, Greenough and Aquilino concurred. Trustee Aquilino reiterated
her belief that a distinction can be made between improving an existing home
and building a new home. Trustee Greenough remarked that she believes that the
orientation of the existing house constitutes a hardship in that it makes it
unuseable by today’s standards.
Noting that four Trustees believe that there is a practical hardship affecting
the ability to yield a reasonable return, President Duhl called for a motion to
introduce the ordinance granting a variation permitting a nonconforming front
yard setback.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, so moved. By voice vote, the
motion carried. All of the Trustees, except Trustee Woodbury, voted favorably.
h)   Resolution No. R-16-2002 – Establishing Department Heads’ Salaries –
Adoption. President Duhl called for a motion to approve the department heads’
salaries as recommended by the Village Manager. Trustee Woodbury so moved,
seconded by Trustee Powell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Presser.
i)   Resolution No. R-17-2002 – Establishing Village Manager’s Salary.
President Duhl called for a motion to approve the Village Manager’s salary in
accordance with the Agenda Report. Trustee Powell so moved, seconded by Trustee
Woodbury. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
7. Old Business.
None
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Requested Modifications to Certificate of Appropriateness: 518 Winnetka
Avenue. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for modifications to the south and
west building elevations to allow for the installation of wall louvers to
provide ventilation. In response to a question from Manager Williams regarding
the necessity for these louvers, Mr. D’Onofrio informed the Trustees that the
louvers were required by the Building Code
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the
modifications. By roll call vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Greenough, Brower, Aquilino, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
b)   Women’s Club Banner Request. Manager Williams reviewed a request from the
Women’s Club to hang canvas banners on six Village light poles on Green Bay
Road advertising the Club’ s annual Housewalk. He explained that the Design
Review Board has approved the banners’ design in the past and that the Club’ s
contractor would install and remove the banners. Noting that the Club makes the
same request annually for its Housewalk and the Silver Wicket event, he cited
staff’s recommendation that staff be authorized to approve the installation for
subsequent years.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the
installation of the banners for the housewalk and henceforth authorize staff to
approve the installation. By roll call vote the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Presser.
c)   Pickup Truck Purchase. Manager William explained a request from the Water
& Electric Department to replace a pickup truck originally scheduled for
replacement in 2005 due to its unsafe condition. The cost of the truck from the
state bid is $29,210.00. A lift gate will have to be installed after it is
delivered. The estimate for this gate is $3,000.00.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to award the bid, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Aquilino, Brower, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Presser.
d)   Proposed Revisions to the Building Permit Fee Schedule. Due to the
lateness of the hour, this item was slated for a future agenda.
10. Reports.
Manager: Manager Williams reminded the Trustees of the Special Study Session
scheduled for Saturday morning.
Trustees.. Trustee Aquilino reported that she and Trustee Brower had attended a
recent Chamber of Commerce meeting. She indicated that members of the Chamber
would be meeting with the Garden Council to talk about holiday lighting.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the reappointment of Donna Pinsof to Board of Police
and Fire Commissioners for a second full term. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by
Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, the Executive Session was cancelled.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:45 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                     Recording Secretary
Saturday, April 13, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 7, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 9:35 a.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and
approximately 25 persons in the audience.
2. Continued Discussions on Single-family Residential Bulk Issues, Including
Gross Floor Area, Building Height and Setbacks.
President Duhl welcomed the audience to the study session and recognized Susie
Shabica, 326 Ridge Ave., who read a letter to the Trustees expressing her
opinion about what is happening in the Village vis a vis new construction and
making suggestions as to how to improve the situation.
President Duhl then turned the floor over to Mr. D’Onofrio who continued his
summary and analysis of residential bulk issues. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that as
discussed at the April 2nd Council meeting, there are several variables in
residential construction that affect bulk; these include floor height, roof
pitch and placement on a lot. He suggested that the Council consider addressing
building bulk by examining potential changes to control building height.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported staff’s analysis of building height, noting that
Winnetka is the only municipality of those surveyed by the Village’s
consultants, TPAP, which measures roof height to the midpoint. Staff discovered
that if building height was measured to the ridge, 32% of the homes studied
exceed the 35’ limit. He commented further that the height of the attic floor
above the first floor is the best overall indicator of relative volume, and
recommended that building height be controlled much differently than in the
past. He proposed a system whereby the actual attic floor height is disregarded
and attic volume is measured from an established uniform height of 21 feet
above the first floor level. This would establish a uniform vertical plane and
eliminate the attic height manipulation used by some builders to increase GFA.
He commented that this system is similar to the one used in Lake Forest where
it has not generally affected roof pitch, nor does staff believe that it would.
This method would reduce height and GFA in the same manner as yard setbacks do
because it imposes a vertical control on the structure. He pointed out that in
order to allow some design flexibility, staff was not recommending that floor
heights be capped.
He also recommended that the method for measuring building height should be
changed to be measured to the ridge of the roof, exclusive of chimneys, but not
any other architectural elements, with the maximum of 35’ to the ridge being
set for the R-3, R-4, and R-5 districts, and 40’ for the R-1 and R-2 districts.
In his summary, Mr. D’Onofrio reported that another component impacting
building height is how far above grade the first floor elevation can be.
Currently, the top of the first floor can be at four feet above grade. Staff’s
recommendation is that in the R-2 district the top of foundation maximum be set
at 2 feet and in the R-3, R-4, and R-5 districts it be set at 2.5 feet. By
limiting the height of the top of the foundation, staff believes the overall
height of homes will be reduced without drastically reducing the overall
maximum permitted GFA. He noted, however, that altering this requirement could
impact side yard encroachment for window wells.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that this method recognizes that tall spaces are
bulk, whether they are completely accessible or not.
Manager Williams commented that the 21-ft. maximum would begin to regulate
volume without having to do an actual volume measurement.
Trustee Brower inquired as to how the 21-ft. measurement was arrived at. Mr.
Williams indicated that staff had spoken with several architects who had
generally agreed that 21 ft. was appropriate and workable.
Trustee Greenough commented that staff’s recommendation makes no
differentiation between an R-2 district that is predominantly ranch houses and
one that is not. Mr. D’Onofrio agreed. Trustee Greenough suggested that
different neighborhood character within the same zoning district could be
categorized.
President Duhl acknowledged that such would be possible, but that doing so
would ultimately affect property values, making it much more of concern for the
neighborhood than a Village concern.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that a large lot can clearly accommodate greater
height, but he said that the foundation height needs to be considered as a part
of the equation because the effects of the components are additive and clearly
affect the streetfront visual.
In talking about overall height, the Trustees also discussed at length the
point from which the overall height measurement and the height of foundation
measurements are taken. Mr. Norkus reported that currently the measurements are
taken from the average grade adjacent to the foundation. Alternatives discussed
included the lowest point of the natural grade prior to any site improvements,
sidewalk elevation, or curb elevation.
Trustee Presser voiced some concerns about addressing design by addressing
height and the possible unintended consequence of squat roofs.
Manager Williams mentioned that requiring a certain roof slope could be
considered.
President Duhl called for comments from the audience.
Ted Weldon, 426 Sunset Rd., expressed dismay at the oversized homes being
built, particularly in his neighborhood. He voiced his belief that the Village
Code currently permits the Village to exercise its best judgment to prevent
this from occurring, and likened the Village’s zoning ordinance to the one used
by the City of Chicago to control development of its lakefront property. He
asked that the Village delay the issuance of building permits until proposed
plans meet aesthetic standards.
Trustee Presser asked if Mr. Weldon was suggesting imposition of mandatory
residential design guidelines.
Mr. Weldon responded that he was merely suggesting that the Village consider
the character of an area affected by new construction and judge whether or not
the proposed development plan fits in with the aesthetic style of the existing
neighborhood.
Attorney Janega stated that the law is very clear on this subject and has
established that the Village must rely on specifics rather than the types of
general provisions in the Zoning Ordinance to which Mr. Weldon referred. She
informed the Trustees that if someone comes in with specific plans that conform
to the Zoning Ordinance, the Village does not have the authority to look to
more general provisions to control the design. She said that every time a
municipality has tried a mandatory design review, the courts have overturned it
on the grounds that the general statements in a zoning ordinance are subjective
and non-specific.
President Duhl concurred with Attorney Janega’s statement, saying that in law
when there is a general explanation of purpose followed by specific rules, the
specific rules govern. He pointed out that there could be different
interpretations of what the character of any given neighborhood is.
MaryAnn Powers, a local realtor, expressed her concern that a lot of the new
construction in the Village is changing its overall ambiance. She said that she
is worried that the more restrictive the Village becomes, the less diversity of
design there will be. She suggested that the Village adopt Glencoe’s “plane of
light” proposal as a more gentle approach to the problem of height than a
finite ridgeline measurement. She noted that many of the architecturally
significant homes in the Village could not have been built if such a
restriction had been in place at the time of their construction.
Steve Levedidas, 697 Sheridan Rd., a builder and developer, commented that the
Village cannot legislate design. He agreed with comments by Gary Frank made at
previous meetings encouraging an informal design review process by neighborhood
residents, saying that it frequently results in a better design without
stomping on peoples’ property rights. He also agreed with Ms. Powers that it is
important to preserve older homes and reiterated her remark that many could not
have been built under the existing Zoning Ordinance. He expressed his belief
that a number of new homes have been built out of conformance with the Code and
that if the current Code is strictly enforced, appropriate new homes can be
built.
Sergio Estenssoro, another architect, voiced support for Glencoe’s “plane of
light” approach. He voiced his belief that ratcheting down the height will, in
fact, impinge upon the diversity of design.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that perhaps the “plane of light” approach should be
reexamined in conjunction with the other changes being considered because it
addresses blank wall issues.
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce St., agreed with Mr. Levididas. He urged the Village to
look to strict enforcement of the existing codes before changing what is
already in place.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Norkus to comment on this position.
Mr. Norkus replied that what is in place is administratively difficult to
calculate and allows for manipulation by builders. He indicated that a
straightforward 21-ft. limit would be clear and eliminate inconsistencies.
Trustee Woodbury asked Mr. Frank whether he felt the 21-ft. proposal would
significantly limit the design.
Mr. Frank responded that the more flexibility there is, the more opportunity
there is for better architecture. He indicated that he would like to see at
least a 22-ft. limit. He pointed out that a lot of the visual bulk has to do
with how much attic space is counted in GFA and said that the existing
ordinance might, in fact, be more limiting than the new proposal. He also
commented that the height of the house should be determined from some fixed
dimension of the house itself, i.e., the top of the first floor, because
variations in grade make it too difficult to come up with a relative standard.
Trustee Woodbury agreed. He added, however, that when it comes to measuring the
height of the foundation, it must be measured relative to some grade
calculation.
Mr. Norkus reiterated that enforcing the existing ordinance is complicated. He
explained that the GFA allowance for attic space was instituted in order to
keep from flattening roofs, but said that some adjustments might be required.
He pointed out that the allowance does not necessarily guarantee an
attractively pitched roof. He also mentioned that the Village used to employ a
different method to measure height and suggested that staff revisit that
alternative.
President Duhl commented that it seemed to him that the proposed new standard
would simplify staff’s enforcement significantly. He said the height of a house
can be easily measured from the first floor, but the question the outstanding
question is how to best measure the height of the foundation.
The Trustees and staff then continued their discussion of the various options
for measuring foundation height.
Maureen Mitchell, 460 Elder Ln., urged the Council to act boldly and quickly to
restrict bulk.
Keith Groebe, 133 Bertling, Chairman of the Building Review Committee, reported
that that committee increasingly is hearing comments from neighbors about
design issues, rather than about the historical value of a property slated for
demolition. He indicated that the BRC is becoming a public forum for neighbors
who feel aggrieved by their proximity to proposed oversized construction that
they perceive will jeopardize their property values. He noted that very few
complain about the specific detail or architecture of the new construction, but
rather about the profile, or silhouette, that is not in keeping with existing
structures.
Linda Davis, 465 Sunset, agreed with the comments made by Ms. Mitchell and Mr.
Groebe. She urged to Council to act quickly.
The Council scheduled another Special Study Session for Saturday, April 20th,
at 1:00 p.m. President Duhl invited the audience and all other interested
parties to attend.
3. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 12:08 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 1:10 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
4. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 1:12 p.m.
April 16, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: May 7, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
16, 2002, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff and eight
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   April 20, 2002, 1:00 p.m., Special Study Session. All of the Trustees
present indicated that they expected to attend
b)   April 23, 2002, Special Study Session. All of the Trustees present
indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   April 30, 2002, Meeting with Caucus. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 9, 2002, Special Council Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-12-2002 – Special Use Permit: 1321 Willow Road - Adoption.
Manager Williams reviewed this item, noting that there are two outstanding
issues: landscaping and graphics. He indicated that Village staff and
representatives from the High School had met to discuss landscaping and
determined that there will be very little area between the parking lot and the
dugouts which complicates landscaping plans. He recommended that the Council
proceed with allowing the construction of the dugouts and give the School a
year to come back with a landscaping plan. With regard to logos, he indicated
that at this time, the School was not proposing that any graphics or logos be
placed on the dugouts. They have agreed that if they wish to add them at a
later date, they will come back to the Village with a proposal.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments incorporating these conditions
into the ordinance.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the ordinance by
replacing Sections 2 through 6 with the revised Sections 2,3, and 4, as
recommended by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended, approving the
Special Use Permit to permit construction of four dugouts at Duke Child’s
Field. Trustee Aquilino seconded that motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays:
Trustee Greenough. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-11-2002 – Special Use Permit and Certificate of
Appropriateness: 1112 & 1140 Willow Road – Adoption. Trustee Aquilino, seconded
by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance granting front setback and
side yard variations and a Special Use Permit to allow for the replacement of
playground equipment in the northwest and southwest playgrounds on the subject
property. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-10-2002 – Zoning Variation: 562 Green Bay Road - Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a parking variation to permit expansion of the existing language
school into second floor space without providing the required two on-site
parking spaces. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-8-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1162 Asbury. – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a side yard variation to permit construction of an open front porch,
enclosure of an existing screened porch, and expansion of a second floor
dormer. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-13-2002 – Authorizing Sale or Other Disposition of Surplus
Bicycles and Computer Equipment - Adoption. Manager Williams explained that
this ordinance would allow for the disposal of property recovered by the Police
Department, primarily bicycles, and give the Finance Director blanket
authorization to dispose of any computer equipment that is retired during the
current fiscal year. Because the it is proposed that the bicycles be donated to
the Winnetka Community House for its auction at the end of this month, he
recommended that Council waive the first reading of this ordinance and move
directly to adoption. Attorney Janega explained the standards for so doing.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to waive the first reading.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2002 – Amending the Noxious Weeds Provisions of the
Village Code - Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that in the course of
the Village Code Committee’s review of the provisions pertaining to trees and
plants, it was brought to the Committees attention that common milkweed should
be deleted from the Village’s list of noxious weeds. It was pointed out to the
Committee by Bernard Hammer, a member of the Forestry Commission, that milkweed
serves as an important natural habitat of the State insect, the Monarch
butterfly. She noted that the proposed ordinance also amends the relevant
provisions to add garlic mustard to the list of noxious weeds and adds a
requirement that property owners remove ragweed, poison ivy, and garlic mustard
from their property, changes suggested by the Forestry Commission.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Resolution No. R-19-2002 – Easement Agreement with Weil Hummer, LLC –
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that this is a second easement agreement
with regard to land currently owned by the Village of Glencoe that is being
sold for development. This easement, along with the one adopted by Resolution
No. R-6-2002, allows the Village of Winnetka to run its electric cable under
land that lies between the ComEd transmission right-of-way and Frontage Rod, on
the west side of the Edens, north of Tower Rd. She noted that this easement
agreement is identical to the easement agreement approved on March 19, 2002,
except for the description of the property and the name of the owner of the
underlying property.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution
authorizing execution of the easement agreement with the property owner, Weil
Hummer, LLC. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
7. Old Business.
None
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1265 and 1266. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1265 in the amount of $567,614.29 and Warrant
List No. 1266 in the amount of $327,969.82. Trustee Woodbury seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Analysis of Costs for Undergrounding Services. Mr. McInturff explained
what the merchandise and jobbing charges are and how the Water & Electric
Department arrives at the cost billed to customers. He noted that while the
Village recovers its costs, it is probably undercharging for material and
transportation costs. He suggested that the Village adopt charges that more
accurately reflect the total costs to the Department and increase revenue
accordingly.
After discussing Mr. McInturff’s proposal, Trustee Woodbury, seconded by
Trustee Powell, moved to change the method used to calculate the cost of work
billed to customers as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Wholesale Power Cost Adjustment. Mr. McInturff reviewed the current method
for calculating the power cost adjustment. He recommended that the current
method be changed from one based on average cost to one that recovers dollar
amounts.
After discussing the particulars of Mr. McInturff’s proposal, Trustee Woodbury,
seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to change the way the Power Cost Adjustment
is calculated as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and
Powell.
Manager Williams indicated that staff would return to the Council in a year
with a report on the impact the new system has had on revenues.
d)   Bid No. 02-007: Sidewalk and Curb Replacement Program. Manager Williams
reviewed the bids for labor, materials and equipment as a part of the Village’s
annual sidewalk replacement program. He reported staff’s recommendation that
the bid be awarded to Suburban Concrete, Inc., in the total amount of
$104,045.00. unit price contract
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to award the bid, as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Proposed Revisions to the Building Permit Fee Schedule. Mr. D’Onofrio
reviewed a staff analysis of the building permit fee schedule. In doing so, he
pointed out that (1) for the past three years, the Department’s expenses
exceeded revenue collected, and (2) since permit fees were last raised in 1997,
the CPI has risen over 10.4%. He noted that it is a policy decision whether or
not the Department’s fees should balance out its expenses.
In comparing Winnetka to Glencoe, Highland Park, Hinsdale, Lake Forest and
Wilmette, Mr. D’Onofrio reported that with the exception of Glencoe, all of the
municipalities calculate their permit fees in a manner similar to Winnetka’s.
This means that is there is a base building permit fee and then a number of
individual permit fees added to it as appropriate. This comparison indicated
that over all the types of permits issued, Winnetka falls somewhere in the
middle cost range.
He explained that that the base building permit fee is calculated in one of two
ways depending upon the work to be completed; for new construction it is
calculated using GFA, while the fee for remodeling projects is calculated on
construction value. He recommended that this two-tiered system be maintained.
In addition, he recommended that staff be given the authority, in the form of a
Code amendment, to estimate the construction value for remodeling permits as
these are frequently underestimated in the permit application process. Mr.
D’Onofrio suggested ways in which the Village Code might be amended to
accomplish this goal.
Mr. D’Onofrio discussed two methods of increasing permit fees. The first would
be to raise all permit fees across the board; the second would be to increase
the cost of the base building permit fee. In addition, he noted staff’s
recommendation that the demolition fee, plan review fee, and some zoning-
related fees be increased to better reflect the staff time associated with
each.
Manager Williams pointed out that there is a deficit in the Department budgeted
for next year, a deficit where taxpayers are filling the gap.
Trustee Woodbury expressed his opinion that with regard to the building fees,
particularly for new construction, the Village should be among the highest. He
objected to taxpayers subsidizing the Department.
President Duhl stated that he believes that the Village needs to make up the
deficit, particularly on new construction, which is the most insensitive to
fees. He also said he would like to see higher demolition fees.
Trustees Presser and Greenough concurred.
Trustee Presser stated the consensus that all agreed that fees ought to cover
costs.
After discussing options, the Trustees agreed to raise the demolition fee to
$2,500.00; increase all fees across the board by 10%; increase the plan review
fee 10%; and revise the Village Code to give staff the authority to set
construction cost estimates for remodeling.
Manager Williams indicated that staff would return at the next meeting with a
resolution for Council’s adoption implementing these changes.
10. Reports.
Manager: Manager Williams reported that the Village recently learned that the
Village’s excess medical insurance carrier, Legion Insurance, was in serious
financial difficulty. Consequently, the Village wished to contract with an
alternative carrier, BCS, for this coverage with a specific retention level of
$40,000, for an annual cost of $218,776.32. He reviewed the options available
and asked for approval to implement this option.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve Manager’s
recommendation. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the reappointment of Sandra Berger to the Police
Pension Board. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve
the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
He then suggested the reappointment of John Cook to the Landmark Preservation
Commission for a full term. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser,
moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 10:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:31 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:32 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                     Recording Secretary
Saturday, April 20, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 21, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 1:10 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and
approximately 21 persons in the audience.
2. Continued Discussions on Single-family Residential Bulk Issues, Including
Gross Floor Area, Building Height and Setbacks.
President Duhl said that this was a continuing discussion of proposed changes
to the GFA requirements, with the hopes of holding a public hearing on the
proposed changes at the May 7th Village Council meeting. He suggested that the
Council discuss Policy Options 6, 7 and 8, as outlined in the agenda report. He
added that Policy Option 1 also needed to be discussed because it, too, deals
with building volume. President Duhl also commended Assistant Director of
Community Development Brian Norkus for an excellent agenda report. He asked for
comments from the Trustees.
Trustee Woodbury stated that he was in favor of setting the attic floor height
at 21 feet. With regard to Option 6, Trustee Woodbury stated that it, along
with Policy Option 1, would together affect building heights.
Manager Williams stated that staff is thinking about measuring height, from the
lowest grade adjacent to the building foundation, which would be different than
the method currently employed. There are two benefits to this new method.
First, it is a backdoor way to control development in the ravines. Second, if
new construction could not comply with this standard because of the lay of the
land, a variation could be granted, since the that is a legitimate basis for a
variation.
Mr. Norkus noted that the 40-foot height recommendation in the R-2 district
would affect houses in the ravines, and that relief from the maximum height
requirements could be addressed through the variation process.
Trustee Greenough said that if we measure from the lowest adjoining grade in a
structure that is already 2 feet above grade, the attic floor height should be
set at 19 feet to get the 21-foot limit. She did not see why 40 feet should be
allowed in the R-2 district and that the maximum height should be kept at 35
feet as in the R-5 and R-4 districts.
Mr. Norkus explained Attachment A and said an attic floor height of 21 feet
serves as an effective way to control building volume.
President Duhl asked if it is better to try to control height by setting an
attic floor height minimum or by establishing a maximum building height of 35
feet.
Trustee Greenough said that the analysis in Attachment A indicates that both
limits are needed.
Trustee Presser stated that lowering the attic ceiling appears to have more of
an effect in bringing down the bulk.
Manager Williams noted that, since the 1999 amendments, the maximum height per
floor is 14 feet.
Trustee Woodbury said that since most new homes have 9-foot ceilings, so a 20-
foot attic floor would work. He added that there should be a difference in the
R-5 district so that houses begin to fit in. He suggested that the attic floor
should be somewhere between 19 and 21 feet and that setting the attic floor
height at 21 feet is a good place to start.
Trustee Powell agreed with Trustee Woodbury.
Trustee Aquilino stated that she had a concern about 21 feet and whether it
would make sense when a portion of the basement above grade is included. She
referred to the summary of homes constructed in the R-5 district and added that
she would like to get the height right.
Trustee Woodbury said that whatever the standard, the goal is to keep the
existing character. He added that not all houses are at grade, and some of them
have a stoop. These homes should not be penalized and forced to be lowered.
Trustee Powell noted that 10 such homes are listed.
Mr. D’Onofrio stated that the building code requires the top of foundations to
be a minimum of 6 inches above grade. With a 12-inch floor joist, this brings
the first floor to 18 inches above grade. He added that the lower the top of
the foundation, the larger a window well would be.
Trustee Powell asked how the 6 inch top of foundation maximum could be met if
the lot were sloped. Mr. D’Onofrio stated that a stepped foundation could be
employed.
Mr. Norkus stated that under current regulations a basement could be up to 4
feet above grade and not count as GFA.
Trustee Aquilino asked when a 12-foot ceiling would be used on a second floor.
Trustee Woodbury stated that sometimes a master bedroom might have a vaulted
ceiling that would exceed 12 feet.
President Duhl stated that any attic floor area over 21 feet should be counted
as GFA.
Trustees Aquilino and Presser asked about how 9-foot ceiling heights would
compare with other communities.
Manager Williams stated that local architect, Gary Frank, had suggested the 21-
foot attic floor limit.
Trustee Greenough stated that homes in the R-5 district are closer together and
we should control for light and air if we can. She noted that most of the
houses built since 2000, most are 32 feet high.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that the maximum height in the R-5 be 33 feet.
Trustee Brower said that attic floor height should be graduated by district. He
said he could support 21 feet in the R-5 district and suggested an overall
height of 33 or 34 feet in the R-4 district, and 32 or 33 feet in the R-
5 district. He agreed with Trustee Greenough that R-5 affects more people.
Trustee Presser expressed concern about using attic floor height, i.e., 21
feet, for bulk control. He suggested that the maximum building height remain at
35 feet, with taller heights being permitted in zoning districts with larger
lots.
Trustee Aquilino stated the average height is 30 feet and she would vote for a
30-foot maximum.
President Duhl asked about the impact of setting the attic floor height at 19
or 21 feet if the basement elevation is not factored in. Trustee Aquilino said
she could live with it, but it should be broken down. Trustee Powell said he
would be in favor of a 21-foot attic floor so long as the roof ridge would not
exceed 35 feet.
President Duhl summarized that 19 feet is too low and that the height should be
21 feet from basement to attic. President Duhl polled the Trustees as to what
they believe the maximum height of a home should be.
Trustee Brower favors 32 feet, Trustee Greenough favors 29 feet, Trustees
Woodbury and Presser favor 33 feet, Trustee Powell favors 35 feet and Trustee
Aquilino favors 30 feet.
Trustee Woodbury said in addition to the 33-foot height, changing where the
height is measured from will reduce it a foot. Currently, the measurement is
taken from the average natural grade.
President Duhl proposed a 33-foot building height and a 21-foot maximum to the
attic floor in the R-4 and R-5 districts. He also asked if the 21-foot height
should be applied to other zoning districts.
Trustee Aquilino stated it should apply to the R-3 district. Trustee Presser
stated that the heights should be increased in the other larger lot zoning
districts. Trustee Woodbury said the height should be increased to 35 feet.
Trustee Greenough said 21 feet is acceptable. Trustee Brower favored 33 feet in
larger districts.
Trustee Presser said that allowing greater heights in the larger zoning
districts creates an opportunity for more diversity.
President Duhl stated that he understood there to be agreement that the attic
floor heights should be 21 feet and the overall building height should be 33
feet in the R-2 district.
Trustee Greenough then asked what do about limiting height near the one-story
ranch homes. Manager Williams said this would be a whole new discussion and we
would have to invite the neighbors from those districts in for discussion.
Trustee Powell suggested using a different measurement, by looking at the front
profile of a building, and said that if you go taller you must go narrower.
President Duhl asked if we should think about a separate ranch zoning district
Trustee Powell replied that it was too late, but would have a tremendous
impact.
Trustee Greenough questioned whether reducing height really reduces GFA,
stating a lower profile has nothing to do with GFA.
President Duhl asked, given what is being built in the R-2 district, should we
leave the same height limits as in the R-4 and R-5 districts.
Trustee Greenough recommended that the Lake Forest maximum of 19 feet to the
attic floor be used in the R-2 and R-3 districts. Trustee Presser said it
should be 23 feet to the attic and a total of 40 feet in R-2.
Others suggested attic heights for R-1 ranged from 23 feet to 25 feet. Manager
Williams said that he would have staff bring back some data.
President Duhl asked whether the building height should be measured from the
lowest point adjacent to structure or the street front?
Trustee Woodbury said that whatever standard is used, it should be easy for
staff to look at and mentioned that several years ago the height measurement
was changed from the street front to the average lot grade. President Duhl
noted Staff’s suggestion to use the lowest grade adjacent to the structure and
asked if the Council agreed.
Mr. Norkus stated that 75% of new houses have high first floors in order to get
light into the basement. Mr. Norkus said the recommendation is that the top of
the first floor be lowered from 3.5 feet to 2.5 feet above the lowest adjacent
grade.
Trustee Presser inquired as to the down side of this. Mr. D’Onofrio stated that
the building code requires 3-foot window wells, which is necessary for a means
of egress. Window wells are generally found in the sideyard.
Trustee Greenough suggested that the 2-foot side yard encroachment for window
wells not be changed so that a 3 foot wide window well can be built.
Trustee Woodbury stated that the top of the foundation should be the same in
the R-2 district as in the R-5, 4, and 3 districts.
Trustee Powell disagreed and recommended that it be left at 4 feet for larger
lots.
After further discussion, the Council agreed that all districts should have the
same 2.5-foot limitation.
President Duhl opened the floor to the audience for the discussion of side
yards.
Cynthia and Michael Hara of 285 N. Deere Park East in Highland Park said that
they own the house at 411 Sheridan Road and have been working on drawings for
the new house for four years. The plans are not yet completed, but they plan
10-foot ceilings on the first and second floors. She expressed a concern that
the home that they want to build will not meet the new standards and that if
the Village Council passes the changes, they would have to start all over
again.
Mr. Hara said all the architects he has spoken to recommend building 10-foot
ceilings.
President Duhl explained that when rules get changed hardships are sometimes
created and residents can get caught in the middle of the changes.
Charles Hoffman, 303 Sheridan Road, lives in the R-2 district. He encourages
residents to attend Village Council meetings. What he has heard is that height
is definitely an issue and suggested that there be some type of a circuit
breaker. He added that in approving new home construction, the adjoining homes
and surrounding neighborhood should be taken into account and that where a
structure is placed on a lot has a lot to do with bulk. He said side porches
should be considered, but recommended amending the grade and attic provisions
and then revisiting in a few years. He suggested that the current zoning is not
working and some builders and architects are abusing the building code.
Andrew Kempisty, a builder who lives at 459 Sunset, said that the demolition he
applied for was postponed for 60 days because the neighborhood is concerned
about height. He said that builders try to fit the right kind of homes into the
neighborhood. He would like an envelope of 35 feet because he would like to put
in a cathedral ceiling on the second floor, but this does not change the bulk
of houses.
Steve Scherer 1062 Ash, said it’s hard to describe what is good architecture
and suggested design review by a paid board of consultants working for
Winnetka.
John Vlahos, architect, said he has worked in Winnetka for over a decade and
that most of the owners are looking to maximize buildings on their lots. He
suggested that the Council reconsider the ceiling height, noting that 10’
ceiling heights are becoming the norm. He said the R-2 district height should
be increased to 22 feet with a raised basement. There are some life safety
issues because more homeowners are making basements livable spaces. He added
that the attic floor height should be increased to 22 feet because newer floor
joist systems use TJI’s, which are 16 inches thick.
At the conclusion of the meeting, President Duhl summarized the consensus among
the Trustees:
   §   In the R-5 and R-4 districts, the attic floor height would be 21 feet,
with an overall building height of 33 feet.
   §   In the R-3 and R-2 districts, the attic floor height would be 21 feet,
with an overall building height of 35 feet.
   §   In the R-1 district, the attic floor height would be 23 feet, with an
overall building height of 40 feet. (Staff will bring back data on R-
1 district).
   §   Building height will be measured from the lowest adjacent grade.
   §   Basement height in all districts will be reduced to a maximum of 2.5
feet.
The Council scheduled another Special Study Session for Thursday, April 25th,
at 7:30 p.m.
3. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 3:30 p.m.
4. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough seconded by Trustee Powell moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 4:14 p.m.
Tuesday, April 23, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved May 21, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Director of Public Works, Steve
Saunders and approximately 34 persons in the audience.
2. Discussion of Possible Sidewalk on the North Side of Tower Road from Vernon
Avenue to Forestway Drive.
Mr. Saunders said that the Village has been reviewing and analyzing the
possibility of constructing a sidewalk on the north side of Tower Road from
Vernon Avenue west to Forestway Drive in response to a petition submitted by
residents on Tower Road. He presented slides of a possible route for the
sidewalk on public property that would preserve trees and as many smaller
plantings as possible, all the while trying to maintain the minimum distance
from the curb.
The following residents spoke in favor of the sidewalk citing child and
pedestrian safety as the most important issue as well as the desire for better
access to homes on the north side of the street and additional stop signs and
crosswalks:
Nick and Beth Anaclerio, 1427 Tower
Faith Harris, 905 Grove
Tom Ticknor, 1417 Tower
Rick Borgeson, 1544 Tower
Ann Carey, 1447 Tower
Sally Graver, 1495 Tower
Kris Schriesheim, 1413 Tower
Scott Padick, 980 Greenwood
Cathy Jones, 1519 Tower
Janice Eager, 956 Greenwood
Geoff Moore, 1441 Tower
Maureen Evans, 1507 Tower
Donna Powell, 1620 Tower
Sandy and Mike Charlton, 1423 Tower
Kate Kincaid, 1520 Tower
The following residents spoke against installation of a sidewalk citing
enforcement of speed limits, and installation of stop signs and crosswalks as
better safety measures:
Bill Hayes, 1487 Tower
Bernard Hammer, 1455 Tower
George and Joan Noyes, 1555 Tower
Nancy Abrams, 1575 Tower
Morton Hirsch, 1578 Hazel Lane
Carol Douglas, 1505 Tower Road, also provided additional comments on this issue
stating that, although she was not against it, a sidewalk or lack of it would
not make any difference and expressed her desire for more stop signs between
Forestway and Hibbard Road.
After hearing from all those who wished to speak, President Duhl informed the
audience that this issue would be on the May 21 agenda for decision by the
Council.
3. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:34 p.m.
Thursday, April 25, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved May 21, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Village Planner Brian Norkus
and approximately 9 persons in the audience.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega arrived at 8:05 p.m.
2. Continued Discussion on Single-Family Residential Bulk Regulations,
including Gross Floor Area, Building Height and Setbacks.
President Duhl reviewed the discussion from the April 23rd study session.
Trustee Presser expressed his desire to hear opinions from architects and
builders regarding the Council’s discussions on building height, GFA and
setbacks, so that he is better informed about the kind of houses that would be
built with the new requirements. He suggested that there be more discussion
about building design review as well.
President Duhl announced that a public hearing on proposed amendments would be
held on May 7.
The Council discussed building height and after input from the Trustees and the
audience members, concluded that a uniform attic floor height of 21 feet should
be set for the R-5 and R-4 zoning districts. The attic floor height would be
measured from the top of a maximum first floor of 2.5 feet or 1 foot above
floodplain, whichever is less. The Council also concluded that it should
maintain the present rule of a 7-foot attic height for calculating GFA, and
that these changes would result in a roof height of 31.5 feet or, overall, a
34-foot house.
The Council also discussed how articulation could reduce bulk and how
increasing setbacks might affect the pitch of the roof.
Trustee Greenough said that, typically, the articulation of older houses is an
entire floor or an entire room at the front of the house, with the bulk towards
the back, which reduces the overall appearance of bulk of the house. She went
on to say that only the houses built more recently look like they are too high.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that what will change the size of a house is the grade and
where the height is measured from.
Mr. Norkus said that if you keep the height the same, but lower the first
floor, then you will most likely increase the pitch of the roofs.
President Duhl continued the discussion for R-3, R-2 and R-1 zoning districts,
saying that by keeping the 21-foot attic floor level in the R-3 district, the
roof line would be 33 feet from the top of the first floor. However, he
questioned whether this would be right for the R-2 district.
Trustee Greenough commented that managing bulk in the R-3, R-2, and R-
1 districts would only work if you pull in the setbacks.
Trustee Presser asked Mr. and Mrs. Hara, who are planning to build a new home
at 411 Sheridan, if a 23-foot attic floor height would work for the
construction of their house.
Mrs. Hara replied that 24 or 25 feet would allow the wider joists between
floors that are necessary to put in the mechanicals, such as air conditioning.
She asked that the Council consider the size of lots and houses in her district
and their particular hardships. She said that the rules the Council wants to
impose would prevent her from building a house on her lot.
President Duhl said that he understands Mrs. Hara’s problem and that in her
case, she may have to request a variation if she can prove hardship. He then
asked the Council if, generally, they are comfortable with 23 feet and a 32.5-
foot roof height in the R-2 zoning district.
Trustee Greenough commented that attachment B of the agenda materials indicates
that 9 of the homes in this district are 35 feet or over and 10 of them are
under, so this does not appear to be a hardship since most of them are under 35
feet.
Gary Frank, a local architect who lives in Winnetka, pointed out that if the
Council doesn’t allow more flexibility in the R-2 district, then 9 of the
existing houses do not conform. Mr. Frank suggested that the Council come up
with a formula for a graduated height.
Trustee Aquilino proposed that this might be a situation where an incentive
would be given to increase setbacks so that a greater height could be allowed.
The Council discussed existing houses and the effect of measuring overall
height from grade and attic floor height from a first floor at 2.5 feet above
grade.
Trustee Aquilino said that the complaints from residents are that new houses
are too big and most of the houses in R-2 are smaller than the minimum size
allowed. In her opinion, this supports a decision not to go any higher. She
said that a higher roof will not solve the problem because it will just give
people more space below the roof line and flatten roofs.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that the lots in the R-5 zoning district are fairly standard
in size, but R-2 has many varied lot sizes. He suggested that maybe there
should be some kind of sliding scale, because a 47,000 square foot lot is very
different from a 14,000 square foot lot.
After further discussion, President Duhl said that the Council recognizes the
different sizes of the lots in R-2 and asked the staff to make suggestions for
a sliding scale.
President Duhl then directed the discussion to setback requirements and asked
for comments on requiring larger setbacks or giving bonuses for increased
setbacks.
Trustee Greenough said she would like to think the bonus issue through before
answering. She said that many lots are 50 feet wide with 36-foot wide houses,
but as the lot width increases, setbacks could be increased. She proposed that
as lot size grows, larger setbacks should be required. For example, if the
setback was increased 4 feet for lots 70 feet wide, this would result in a 13-
foot side yard setback. She said the district purpose of R-5 and R-4 should be
kept in mind and any increase should be in keeping with the landscape.
President Duhl said he understood from the discussion that a bonus would be
given for increased setbacks resulting in a higher pitch to the roof, but
Trustee Greenough is proposing more setback as lot size grows.
Trustee Greenough also mentioned the possible overlay district for ranch
houses, noting that a ranch house could be very wide side-to-side, but could be
very much in line with the landscaping front to back and height-wise.
The Council discussed applying wider setback requirements to zoning lots and,
after hearing comments from the audience, President Duhl said that he was
concerned about creating a large number of nonconformities on existing
properties. He asked the staff to research this and give the Council some
parameters for their next discussion.
President Duhl then asked for discussion about the current porch allowance and
whether it is too restrictive. He suggested modifying the allowance to include
side porches.
Comments from both the Trustees and the audience reflected a belief that side
porches could encourage greater articulation and interest in a house. Trustee
Woodbury added that he would like further discussion to include the two-wall
rule and how this counts towards floor area ratio.
Trustee Woodbury then stated that the minimum setback for garages should be
addressed. Trustee Greenough suggested the garage setback should be 3 feet so
that it can be properly maintained.
President Duhl asked for staff recommendations on garage setbacks and garage
height, which is now at a maximum of 14 feet to the midpoint of the roof. He
said that staff proposes 15 feet to the ridge, but allowing up to 18 feet to
make the garage roof match the pitch of the roof on the house.
President Duhl said the remaining issues address houses built on a hill or
ravine that do no have flat land surrounding it, and whether only the non-table
land portion of the property should be considered for such things as measuring
gross floor area and setbacks.
Comments from Mr. Frank and from Trustee Powell were that some sort of ratio
should be applied for non-table land, especially for those houses on the
lakefront. Mr. Frank was concerned that this could negatively impact the value
of what can be built.
Trustee Brower said that it appears from the staff’s report that they recommend
that gross floor area be reduced.
President Duhl explained that on May 7, there would be a proposed ordinance and
a public hearing on heights and possibly setbacks. He said that the Council
will either introduce the ordinance or send it back for further study. If
introduced on May 7, the ordinance would be up for adoption at the Council’s
regular meeting on May 21. He added that gross floor area will not be a topic
for the May 7 public hearing.
Mrs. Hara requested that the Council consider the people in the R-2 district
who have over 48,000 square feet of land when making their ruling on these
lots, so that they all don’t have to ask for variations.
President Duhl encouraged her to submit suggestions and told her the Council
would examine her situation and others in similar situations.
2. Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
3. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:24 p.m.
May 7, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 21, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 7,
2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus and
36 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced the addition of Item 9(i) Tower Road Change Order #1
to the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   May 14, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   May 21, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 13, 2002 Special Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   April 16, 2002 Special Council Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt
the minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2002 – Amending the Noxious Weeds Provisions of the
Village Code - Adoption. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved
to adopt the ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2002 – Zoning Code Revisions – Public Hearing &
Introduction. President Duhl opened the Public Hearing. Attorney Janega briefly
reviewed the background and history of the issue, noting that study revealed
that there are several variables in residential construction that affect bulk.
These include variations in “floor-to-floor” height and in first floor/basement
elevations above grade, as well as variations in actual built first floor
elevations. Thus, it is possible for homes with similar calculated bulk (Gross
Floor Area) to vary widely in their “perception of bulk.” The proposed
revisions to the Zoning Ordinance regarding building height and bulk result
from numerous meetings to discuss policy options. Ms. Janega summarized the
proposed changes that would be enacted by the proposed ordinance, as follows:
1.   Controlling building height and volume by establishing a uniform attic
floor height for calculation of attic gross floor area.
2.   Modifying the maximum permitted height of basements.
3.   Reducing the maximum permitted vertical building height by (a)
implementing a graduated building height based on zoning district and lot size;
(b) changing the point of measurement from above average natural grade to
finished first floor elevation; and (c) measuring building height to the
highest point of the roof (ridge).
4.   Implementing a cap on permitted Gross Floor Area for unusually large lots
in the R-5, R-4, and R-3 zoning districts.
5.   Revising height regulations for garages and providing a minimum garage
setback of two (2) feet.
6.   Providing greater incentives for front porches (by extending lot coverage
bonuses to small front porches).
7.   Increasing the GFA allowance for storage sheds
Mr. D’Onofrio added that the proposed ordinance does not cover certain issues
that require further study. These include:
1.   a general reduction of allowable GFA;
2.   side yard setbacks;
3.   lakefront properties and table land issues; and
4.   an annual review of new construction.
Chuck Boehrer, 1310 Forestway, voiced concern about establishing a cap on
overall building size. He said that this change would affect a large number of
people and was not reasonable, and he opposed this provision.
Cynthia Hara, 411 Sheridan, expressed her fear that a 38-foot height limit in
the R-2 district might be too restrictive and exclude certain styles of homes.
Linda Davis, 465 Sunset, spoke in defense of GFA caps, saying that they will
help to guarantee that a new home fits into the character of an existing
neighborhood.
There being no further comments from the floor, President Duhl closed the
Public Hearing.
Attorney Janega distributed a table summarizing the proposed zoning amendments
in MC-6-2002. The Trustees discussed the proposed amendments at length before
President Duhl polled them regarding each issue. President Duhl then called for
a motion to amend the proposed ordinance as follows:
   ·   Strike the 23-foot attic floor height in the R-2 zoning district
   ·   Strike the provisions dealing with oversize lots entirely
   ·   Amend the language with respect to garage overhangs to allow them to be
no more than one foot in the R-3, R-4, and R-5 districts, and up to three (3)
feet in the R-2 and R-1 districts.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to so amend the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance,
as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-2-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1417 Edgewood - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed an application for variations from the Side Yard, Rear Yard,
Garages, and Building Line Articulation provisions to permit construction of a
two-story addition at the rear and side of the existing residence. He noted
that the Council initially considered variations for this property at its
February 5, 2002 meeting, but no action was taken at that time. Since then, the
applicants have revised their plans to address comments made by the Council and
neighbors. While the original plan called for the entire length of the rear
wall to be set back 3.78 feet off the rear property line, the revised plans
show the rear wall portion of the home to have a setback of 15.75 feet, with
the rear wall of the attached garage having a rear setback of 3.75 feet. The
revised plans call for the bulk of the house to be moved forward. However, the
changes result in a side yard of 2.92 feet on the east side and specify that
the garage will be constructed at grade, instead of below grade as initially
proposed.
The project’s architect, Michael Breseman, described the proposal in detail,
saying that the applicants had made a good faith effort to address the concerns
expressed by the Council and neighbors in February. He said that the design of
the proposed addition is driven largely by the existing house and its placement
on the parcel of land.
Brenda Olsen, 1414 Asbury, expressed continued opposition to the project,
saying that the setbacks are still excessive and she has concerns about
drainage issues.
Elise Covey, 1503 Edgwood, pointed out that the applicant is now asking for a
greater easement on the east than originally requested.
Diantha Olschan, 1436 Edgewood, also voiced opposition to the revised plan.
Mr. Breseman responded to the comments made by the audience. He pointed out
that the design is holding to the lines of the existing house that are already
in place and noted that the applicant is seeking only to add what would be
acceptable on any other lot in the neighborhood, a garage in the rear corner of
the lot. He reiterated that it is the location of the existing house that
creates the hardship.
Trustees Aquilino, Greenough, and Woodbury spoke in favor of the revised
proposal. Trustee Powell said that he was favorably inclined, but wished to see
an 8-foot side yard setback. Trustee Presser indicated that he did not believe
that granting the requested variance was consistent with what the Council is
trying to do with zoning overall. Trustee Brower indicated that he would
abstain from voting because of the proximity of his house to the property, but
voiced some concern about water issues.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance granting Side Yard, Rear Yard, Garages, and Building Line
Articulation variations to permit construction of a two-story addition. By
voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Brower abstained. Trustee Presser voted
against introduction.
c)   Resolution No. R-20-2002 – Prevailing Wage – Adoption. Mr. Williams
explained that the State of Illinois Prevailing Wage Act requires the Village
to investigate and ascertain on an annual basis, the prevailing rate of wages
for laborers, mechanics, and other workers in the Village employed in
performing construction of public works. As in prior years, the Village relies
on the study done by the Illinois Department of Labor.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing the prevailing wage for the Village of Winnetka. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Policy Direction: 1234 Westmoor. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a
Front Setback variation to permit construction of a new two-story residence
that would observe a less than minimal corner setback. He noted that the
existing residence that will be demolished observes a nonconforming corner
setback. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had recommended that the
variation not be granted.
Charles Page, the architect and homeowner, described the proposal. He said that
the new home had been designed to fit over the footprint of the existing house
so as to minimize impact on the neighbors. He pointed out that the proposal has
the support of all of the adjacent neighbors and asked the Council to favorably
consider his proposal and to formalize the existing nonconformity.
Robert and Elizabeth Crowe, 1228 Westmoor, Steve and Joanne Hudson, 1230
Westmoor, and Aaron Gelman, 1232 Westmoor, all neighbors, spoke in support of
the proposal.
In response to a comment made by Mr. Gelman, President Duhl explained that the
Zoning Board of Appeals had denied Mr. Page’s application because it was not
within their authority to grant a variance for new construction; such a
variation can be granted only by the Village Council. He pointed out that to
the extent that any variation is granted, it becomes a precedent and that,
therefore, it is important that all variations are considered within the
context of what will benefit the Village as a whole.
President Duhl polled the Trustees and, hearing a favorable consensus, called
for a motion to direct the Village Attorney to draft an ordinance granting the
requested variation. Trustee Presser so moved, seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Policy Direction: 714 Park Lane. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
Front Setback and Garage variations to permit construction of a two-story
addition at the front of the existing residence and a swimming pool to the
north of the existing residence. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals
had recommended approval of the front setback for the addition and the garage
doors, even though those proposed do not comply with Code, but recommended
against the 30-foot front setback required for the proposed swimming pool.
Paul Konstant, the architect, explained that the proposed pool could be moved
back in the yard to a conforming location but the applicants felt that it would
be in the neighbors’ best interests to keep it closer to the house and save the
nearby sliding hill.
Mark Shiff, attorney for Mrs. A.D. Preston, 730 Sheridan Rd., and Philip
Preston, Mrs. Preston’s son, voiced opposition to locating the swimming pool in
the requested, nonconforming location.
None of the Trustees expressed opposition to the swimming pool, but there was a
consensus that it be located in a conforming location.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to direct the Village
Attorney to draft an ordinance granting the requested front setback variation
for the addition and garage door width variation but concurring with the Zoning
Board’s recommendation that the setback variation for the pool be denied. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Policy Direction: 530 Winnetka Avenue. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request
for Permitted Uses, Limitations on Expansion, Intensity of Use of Lot, and
Front Setback variations to permit construction of a building addition at the
front and side of the existing office building and construction of four parking
spaces in the required front yard. He explained that medical offices are not a
permitted use in this zoning district, so the variation would permit an
expansion of a nonconforming use in a nonconforming building. He reported that
the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend approval of the applicant’s
entire request, but that the Design Review Board had issued unfavorable comment
with respect to the proposed parking lot and additional curb cut. Noting that
the proposed expansion would result in the loss of nearly all green space as
viewed from the street, the DRB had recommended that building materials be
upgraded and that the applicants consider placing the additional parking area
at the rear of the building. The DRB also suggested that the applicant upgrade
the materials used on the addition.
Horatiu Wolff, the project’s architect, reviewed the design with the Trustees.
He said that the changes are being proposed in order to address the issues of
inadequate patient drop-off locations and handicapped accessibility. He pointed
out that the site is unique in that it is surrounded by ‘no parking’ zones
because of its proximity to New Trier High School.
Trustee Powell inquired whether the plan would result in additional parking
spaces. Mr. Wolff indicated that the number would remain the same, but that
there would be one handicapped space.
Manager Williams asked whether the applicant had considered expanding parking
to the rear of the building. Mr. Wolff responded that they had but that, based
upon the existing traffic pattern, rear parking would only exacerbate existing
traffic problems.
Trustee Woodbury indicated that he was in favor of the addition but not in
favor of the necessary additional curb cut or the loss of green space. He also
agreed with the DRB’s suggestion that the applicant improve upon the proposed
materials.
After polling the Trustees, President Duhl noted that the Council was favorably
inclined toward the applicant, particularly with regard to the building
expansion, but that unresolved parking issues remain to be solved. He suggested
that the applicant revise his plans more in keeping with the Design Review
Board’s comments and return to that Board with a new proposal. He also asked
the applicant to readdress the materials issue with the DRB.
d)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1267 and 1268. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1267 in the amount of $1,347,599.38 and
Warrant List No. 1268 in the amount of $878,964.29. Trustee Aquilino seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Change Order: Additional Telephone System Expenses. Mr. Williams reported
that the Village incurred additional charges in the amount of $14,906.49 over
and above the amount authorized by the Council for additions to the Village’s
new telephone system. He explained that these charges result primarily from
additional equipment requests, not as a result of deficiencies in the
specifications. He recommended approval of the Change Order.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the Change Order
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Extension of Conduit Installation Contract. Mr. Williams explained that
the existing conduit installation contract expires on May 31, 2002. When it was
bid last year, unit prices were requested for this year. The Water & Electric
Department has found the work completed by Archon Construction to be
satisfactory and recommends that the contract be extended to May 31, 2003 at
the unit prices provided in last year’s bid, amounting to an approximate total
of $249,701.00.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the extension
of the conduit installation contract with Archon Construction as recommended.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Powell, Woodbury, Greenough,
Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Landscape Waste – Public Hearing. President Duhl opened the Public
Hearing. Mr. Williams explained that the Public Works Department is petitioning
the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency to amend the existing Village
landscape waste transfer station permit to temporarily store landscape waste at
the Public Works Yards Facility. The purpose of the petition is to provide the
Department with operational flexibility and to provide a temporary storage
contingency in cases of severe weather conditions that may cause landscape
damage. It is proposed that landscape waste be temporarily stored for a period
not to exceed 72 hours or three (3) days.
There being no comments from the audience, President Duhl closed the Public
Hearing and called for a motion to set a second Public Hearing to be held in 90
days and directing the Village Attorney to prepare an authorizing resolution
for consideration at the time of the second hearing. Trustee Woodbury so moved,
seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
h)   Capital Improvements Reports. Mr. Williams commented positively on the
various department capital improvement reports included in Council’s packet.
i)   Change Order #1 – Tower Road Rehabilitation and Water Main Replacement.
Mr. Attiah explained that, as the engineering staff was marking the proposed
route of the new storm sewer on Tower Road in the vicinity of Pine tree Lane,
it determined that the location of the existing underground electrical utility
was not correctly denoted on the project plans. Consequently, the proposed
storm sewer must be installed several feet to the north of its originally
planned location and requires the relocation of a sanitary sewer manhole. The
bid from G.W. Kennedy Construction to perform this additional work is
$17,713.00.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the Change
Order as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
None.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, President Duhl cancelled the Executive
Session.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:55 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                     Recording Secretary
May 14, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: June 4, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, and approximately two persons in the audience.
2. 2001-02 Pre-audit Consultation.
President Duhl welcomed Irwin Lyons of Miller Cooper, the Village’s auditor who
gave a brief overview of the upcoming audit and responded to questions from the
Trustees. He discussed some of the changes and requirements imposed by the new
accounting procedures resulting from the implementation of new accounting and
reporting standards, including the fact that infrastructure must now be
depreciated. He noted that the Village will continue to operate as it has, but
the format of the statements will change to a business model. He said that the
auditors will continue to report back to the Council in the form of a
Management Letter and to follow up on points raised in prior such letters.
3. Meeting with Building Review Committee.
President Duhl introduced the members of the Building Review Committee in
attendance, Keith Groebe, President, Charles Hoffman, and Christopher Rintz.
Mr. Groebe remarked briefly on the Committee’s activities since adoption of
Ordinance MC-7-2001 revising the demolition process, noting that the Building
Review Committee has reviewed 31 demolition permit applications. Of those 31
applications, five projects (16%) have been required to complete an HAIS. Of
those five, two (6%) of the demolition permits have been delayed for 60 days
after review of the HAIS.
Additionally, Mr. Groebe said that the primary public concerns raised before
the Building Review Committee have to do with replacement properties,
ultimately coming down to questions of design. Concerns focus on what a
replacement building will look like, how it will affect resale values, air,
light, and fitting in with the character of the neighborhood, etc.
President Duhl commented that the Committee’s job has evolved into one not
anticipated at the time the revised ordinance was adopted. He indicated that
originally the focus of the ordinance had been aimed solely at saving houses
that shouldn’t be lost. At that time recognizing that delay is the only action
open to the Village, it was felt that instituting a significant delay in the
process would slow things down. However, as it has evolved, the historic
significance of a structure has not proven to be the primary concern of
residents, but rather the design of its replacement, which brings the concept
of some kind of design review process up for discussion.
Trustee Powell arrived at 8:15 p.m.
Mr. Groebe expressed his opinion that the amended ordinance did its job in
terms of causing people to step back and to examine alternatives. He indicated
that short of design review, no change to the review ordinance is called for at
this time because (a) if there is any possibility of convincing a reasonable
person of alternatives, the Committee now has the time and vehicle to do so,
and (b) very few of the properties that come before the Committee generate a
ground swell of opposition to their demolition simply to save the existing
structure. The issues really revolve around what is going to replace any given
structure, its “shadow,” size, and bulk, more than its appearance. The Zoning
Code and/or design review, or a combination can address these concerns. He
suggested the concept of some kind of third party design review procedure.
President Duhl noted that in any form, design review is a subjective process,
and Mr. D’Onofrio shared his experiences in Des Plaines and Park Ridge.
The Trustees and the Committee members discussed the concept of some form of
design review at length.
As a developer, Mr. Rintz commented that it is very easy to work in Winnetka
because there are objective standards and clear rules upon which new buildings
are evaluated. He suggested that after the expiration of the 60-day waiting
period, a developer be required to prove up his actions before the Committee
again.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that there are really two levels of design review,
one being that of appearance, the other being that of mass. He suggested that,
perhaps, what is called for is design review in the absence of appearance. How
does the proposed structure fit into a neighborhood? Is its rhythm and
proportionality desirable? He expressed his belief that this review should not
occur at the Council level. He indicated that personally he is not in favor of
appearance review, but would favor some sort of contextual review, particularly
in view of the fact that Caucus has raised the issue of design review.
Attorney Janega pointed out that every time a mandatory design review has been
implemented in another community, it has been challenged in court and,
ultimately, lost, because of the lack of recognized standards.
Mr. Groebe reiterated his comments that the public is most concerned about the
silhouette, rhythm, spacing of replacement structures, a lot of which will be
addressed by the proposed changes to the Zoning Code.
Mr. Woodbury pointed out that one of the big problems is that there is nothing
a resident can do about what a replacement house is going to look like, nor is
there an appropriate forum in which to express concerns. The question is how
does the Village get the best replacement structure possible.
President Duhl suggested that staff return to a later Study Session with an
analysis of the effectiveness of design review processes employed in other
villages and some ideas as to how the Village might set some parameters. He
thanked the Building Review Committee for its input.
4. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
5. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:16 p.m.
May 21, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 4, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
21, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen
Presser, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and 30 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that the variation request for 1417 Edgewood would not
be discussed at this meeting because, by law, the modified application must be
reviewed by the Zoning Board of Appeals before the Council can take final
action. He also announced the addition of Item 9(d) Tower Road Change Order #2
to the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   June 4, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   June 11, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustees Presser and Brower, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 20, 2002 Special Study Session. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   April 23 2002 Special Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   April 25, 2002 Special Study Session. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
d)   May 7, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2002 – Zoning Code Revisions – Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio
reported staff’s recommendations with regard to issues raised during the Public
Hearing on May 7th, specifically, maximum attic floor heights and first floor
heights in floodplain areas. With regard to the former, he reported staff’s
recommendations, as follows:
   i)   Because lot sizes in the R-2 District vary greatly, a two-tiered level
is proposed.
   ii)   In the R-4 and R-5 Districts, a 20 ft. maximum is proposed.
   iii)   In the R-3 District, a 21 foot maximum is proposed.
   iv)   Finally, that attic floor height be calculated from 20 feet or the
actual floor height, whichever is less.
With regard to first floor heights in the floodplain, he said staff recommends
that due to the nearly limitless variables involved, the best means of
addressing the issue is through the zoning variation process.
Attorney Janega discussed possible amendments to the proposed floodplain
language and reviewed other technical and nonsubstantive amendments. She
recommended that the Council not amend the proposed floodplain language at this
time and rely on the variation process.
Hal Francke, a resident and attorney for owners of a large estate, raised a
concern about the unintended consequences of adopting the proposed ordinance on
the few remaining large properties in the Village, specifically the provisions
relating to the attic floor height. He expressed the fear that the new
regulations will, in fact, restrict construction of the types of homes that the
Village would like to see on these large lots, thereby inadvertently
encouraging owners to subdivide them, when the intent really should be to
preserve these large lots. He suggested that the Council consider that for lots
in the R-2 District exceeding two (2) acres in size the maximum building be
raised to 40 feet from the proposed 38 feet and that the maximum distance to
the upper floor be permitted to be 26.5 feet instead of 23 feet.
President Duhl explained that the Council could not make those changes at this
meeting without giving staff an opportunity to consider their consequences but
indicated that staff would do so and come back with an analysis when other
outstanding issues are brought up for discussion.
Tanya Dietrich, 824 Boal Pkwy., spoke in favor of any language that would
provide relief to homeowners in the floodplain.
Gary Frank, urged the Council not to make it anymore difficult for homeowners
in the floodplain by requiring them to go through the variation process. He
said that the proposed ordinance adds one more stipulation and step for
residents and their architects.
After polling the Trustees on the question of reducing the maximum attic floor
height in the R-5 and R-4 Districts from 21 feet to 20, President Duhl called
for a motion to so amend the ordinance. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee
Greenough, so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl then polled the Trustees on whether to leave the floodplain
language as proposed or incorporate the amended language discussed by Ms.
Janega. There was a general consensus to leave the language as proposed.
However, in recognition of the fact that the issue is very complex and
deserving of additional consideration, Mr. Duhl asked staff to return with an
analysis of the question at a later date. He suggested that the Council adopt
the language as proposed, with the recognition that the language may need to be
amended down the road.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower moved to amend the ordinance to
reflect the technical changes discussed by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell moved to adopt Ordinance MC-6-
2002, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-9-2002 – Zoning Variation: 222 Essex - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed an application introduced at the April 9 meeting for a Front
Setback variation arising out of the proposed consolidation of two existing
adjoining lots into a single lot.
Attorney Janega reviewed amended changes to the introduced ordinance providing
detailed findings and adding conditions, including restrictive covenants
providing that:
   ·   If the structure is demolished, the two lots shall revert to the
existing two-lot configuration;
   ·   There will be no further building expansions or new buildings on the
consolidated property other than those specified in the plan presented; and
   ·   No swimming pool may be built on the lot.
Trustee Greenough inquired whether the restrictive covenants exist in
perpetuity. Ms. Janega explained that they exist until the Village releases
them. Although a future Council could change or revoke the covenants at any
time, they serve to put future owners on notice.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the ordinance to
provide detailed findings and to impose the restrictive covenant. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: Trustee Woodbury.
Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-14-2002 – Zoning Variation : 1234 Westmoor – Introduction
and Possible Adoption. Mr. Williams advised the Council that although this and
the next matter had been discussed at the last meeting, there had been no draft
ordinances at that time and neither had been formally introduced. Therefore,
the Council could opt to introduce the draft ordinances for adoption at the
next meeting or waive introduction and move directly to adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser moved to waive introduction. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a Front Setback variation to permit the construction of a new two-
story residence. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-15-2002 – Zoning Variation: 714 Park Ln. – Introduction
and Possible Adoption. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to
waive introduction. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell moved to adopt the ordinance
granting Front Setback and Garage width variations to permit the construction
of a two-story addition. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-13-2002 – Building Permit Fees – Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio
highlighted the major increases to the Building Permit Fee Schedule reviewed by
the Council at its April 16th meeting. In so doing, he noted that there have
been no increases to these fees since 1997. He indicated that he expected that
the increases will add enough revenue to cover the operating budget of the
department, which heretofore has been operating at a deficit. Manager Williams
added that by so doing, taxpayers in general will not be subsidizing new
development.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the resolution
amending the Building Permit Fee Schedule to allow for increases. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough,
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
a)   Tower Rd. Sidewalk. Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed the history of this
issue explaining why staff had laid out two alternative routes for the proposed
sidewalk and engineering location standards.
President Duhl suggested that the discussion be broken down into two parts (1)
whether to construct a sidewalk along the north side of Tower from Forestway to
Vernon, and (2) where the sidewalk should be located. Saying that the Council
had heard many comments from both sides during the Public Hearing on April
23rd, he requested that audience members only contribute new comments.
Zonie Aleshire, 777 Burr, questioned the real need for a sidewalk on both sides
of Tower Rd.
Bernard Hammer, 1455 Tower, suggested a compromise that would dead-end the
proposed sidewalk at 1447 Tower.
Randy Kowalski, 1435 Tower, said that while originally a supporter of the
proposed sidewalk, he was categorically opposed to the alternative route
providing a minimum setback from the street of six feet.
Speaking in favor of a sidewalk, Katherine Jones, 1519 Tower, suggested that it
be taken only to Grove so that it would end at the park where children are
often going.
Joan Gately, 1565 Tower, agreed with the idea of ending the sidewalk at Grove.
Ann Cary, 1447 Tower, advocated the sidewalk extend all the way to Forestway
Dr., as did Nick Anaclerio, 1427 Tower, and Kate Kinead, 1520 Tower, and Kris
Schresheim, 1413 Tower.
President Duhl polled the Trustees. All agreed that a sidewalk should be
installed all the way to Forestway.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve construction of a
sidewalk along the north side of Tower Road all the way to Forestway Dr. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough.
Turning to the question of which of the two alternative routes the sidewalk
should follow, President Duhl again polled the Trustees. There was unanimous
consensus that the sidewalk follow the original, outer route. No motion or vote
was required.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
Tanya Dietrich, 824 Boal Pkway; inquired as to the progress on the Tower Road
project and commented on the inconvenience to residents. Mr. Saunders and Mr.
Williams responded.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1269 and 1270. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1269 in the amount of $596,613.77 and Warrant
List No. 1270 in the amount of $645,697.31. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Guardrail Painting. Mr. Saunders reported that the FY 2002-03 Budget
contains money for beautification of the existing guardrail along the east side
of Green Bay Road between Spruce and Eldorado Streets. This item was identified
both in the Winnetka 2020 Plan and by the Ad Hoc Beautification Committee as a
high priority item for enhancing the appearance of the Green Bay Corridor. He
noted that any project undertaken to modify the guardrail requires IDOT
concurrence and approval since Green Bay Road is a State route and, therefore,
falls under IDOT’s jurisdiction. He explained that staff and the Beautification
Committee reviewed several options for enhancing the appearance of this
guardrail and, ultimately, found the most feasible to be painting the guardrail
and installing appropriate landscaping. He indicated that a contractor
estimated the required sandblasting and painting could probably be accomplished
for approximately $50,000, and an additional
John Fields, 680 Green Bay, encouraged the Council to approve this improvement.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees directed Mr. Saunders to proceed with
obtaining bids for the proposed project.
c)   Budget Carryover. Mr. Williams reported that a few projects have been
carried over from last year’s budget because they were not completed in time to
be included as originally planned. He reported that most all have now been
completed or will be completed soon. He indicated that these may require a
budget amendment toward the end of the year.
d)   Change Order #2 – Tower Road. Mr. Saunders reviewed a change order
necessitated by the discovery of unexpected buried refuse unsuitable for
disposal via normal means during the excavation required for the storm sewer on
Grove Street. He recommended that Council authorize expenditure of up to
$50,300 for disposal of this material to G.W. Kennedy. Mr. Williams commented
that overall, the Village still expected to complete the project under budget.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the Change Order
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the Code Committee would not meet
next week, but was planning to hold its June meeting.
Trustee Brower reported that he had attended a recent meeting of the Chamber of
Commerce.
b)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that on June 1, 2002, SWANCC
would be holding a Household Hazardous Waste collection in Glencoe.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:43 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:25 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:26 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                     Recording Secretary
June 4, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 18, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
4, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Stephen
Presser. Absent: Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio and 13 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 6(e) Resolution R-22-2002 would be removed
from the agenda. The Agenda was approved as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   June 11, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustees Presser and Brower, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   June 18, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Greenough, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 14, 2002 Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   May 21, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions
Ordinance No. M-16-2002 – Special Use and Zoning Variation Request 1112 Willow
Road – Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the School District had
submitted an application for special use and front setback variation to replace
playground equipment that is located along the east side of the property at
Crow Island School. He pointed out that the Council had granted similar
requests in April for two other playgrounds along the west side of the school.
The School District had informed the Village at the time that there were two
playgrounds remaining which would require zoning relief and this request is for
one of the remaining playgrounds. Mr. D’Onofrio also noted that the Plan
Commission, Design Review Board and Zoning Board of Appeals recommended in
favor of the variation.
Trustee Brower seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to introduce Ordinance M-16-
2002, as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-17-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1468 Scott Street - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for variations from the intensity of use,
maximum building size and front setback requirements of the Village Code to
permit the reconstruction of an open front porch and the addition of new bay
windows and a second floor balcony at 1468 Scott Street. He noted that the home
is already nonconforming and the improvements as proposed would add very little
to the gross floor area.
Trustee Powell seconded by Trustee Aquilino moved to introduce the ordinance as
amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. M-18-2002 – Zoning Variation : 779 Bryant – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio explained that the owners of 779 Bryant have requested a variation
from the rear yard setback requirements of the Village Code to construct a new
three-car detached garage in the same location as an existing garage that
currently has a reduced rear yard setback.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
a)   Ordinance No. M-19-2002 – Zoning Variation: 882 Willow Road. –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio summarized the applicants’ request for a variation
from the side yard setback provisions of the Village Code to construct a two-
story addition at the rear of their existing home, together with a new two-car
detached garage and new driveway. He informed the Council that the contract
purchaser of the home has reduced the garage to one car so that the addition
will be within the footprint of the home. Mr. D’Onofrio said a question had
been raised about building articulation, but in the R-2 zoning district there
is no requirement for articulation.
Trustee Powell said that a neighbor had expressed concern about the ability to
back up in the 8.5-foot wide driveway and asked the applicants what the
probability was that they would want a turn around by the garage.
Mr. McCarthy replied that maneuvering in an 8.5 foot driveway is easily done.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Resolution No. R-22-2002 – CATV Transfer – Adoption. Removed from agenda.
7. Old Business.
Petition to Reconsider Tower Rd. Sidewalk from Grove to Forestway. President
Duhl explained that the Village had received a petition from Tower Road
residents to reconsider the construction of a sidewalk on the north side of
Tower Road from Grove to Forestway.
The following residents spoke in favor of reconsideration and against the
construction of the sidewalk on Tower Road, west of Grove Street:
Mr. George Noyes, 1555 Tower Road, who also read a letter in opposition from
Nancy Abrams, 1575 Tower Road
Mike Gately, 1565 Tower
Marvin Robinson, 900 Pine Tree
Joan Noyes, 1555 Tower Road
Mr. Robinson requested on behalf of himself and 4 or 5 of his neighbors that
the parkways be preserved. He complained that the Village had torn down a tree
and spoiled the parkway at Pine Tree Lane because of the construction on Tower
Road.
Manager Williams replied that an anonymous donor had offered a tree to replace
the one that had been taken down and explained that the removal was necessary
for the installation of the storm sewers.
Mr. Williams also stated that the Village had received a letter by fax from
Susan M. Connor, 1417 Tower Road who urged that the sidewalk be built.
After hearing from all those who wished to speak, President Duhl asked the
Council if they wanted to reconsider their decision to install the sidewalk.
The Trustees gave no indication that they wish to reconsider.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
Mrs. Mollybelle Berish, reported two segments of sidewalk missing in front of
1194 Westmoor Road and asked that caution tape be placed there to warn people.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1271 and 1272. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on several items from each warrant list, he moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1271 in the amount of $435,664.59 and Warrant List No.
1272 in the amount of $218,861.73 Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough,
Aquilino, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
b)   Bid No. 02-011, 15KV: Cable. Mr. Williams reported that Bid No. 02-011 is
for the purchase and delivery of 15kv cable which is used for underground
primary distribution. He said the Village had received four separate bids and
the recommendation of the award is to the Okonite Co. who was the lowest bidder
and met all specifications. Trustee Brower moved to award the bid to Okonite
Co., seconded by Trustee Presser. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell and Presser.
Nayes: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
c)   Chamber of Commerce Request: Annual Sidewalk Days. Mr. Williams advised
the Council that the Chamber of Commerce had requested permission to conduct
its annual Sidewalk Sale on July 19 and 20 and that they do not require any
streets to be blocked.
Trustee Aquilino moved to approve the Chamber of Commerce request to conduct
its sidewalk sale on July 19 and 20, seconded by Trustee Brower. By voice vote,
the motion passed unanimously.
10. Reports.
President Duhl announced that the Village’s town meeting would be held at
Washburne School, at 7:30 p.m., tomorrow evening, June 5.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell,
and Presser,. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 8:13 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:15 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:16 p.m.
