                     Deputy Clerk
June 11, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: June 18, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Edmund Woodbury. Absent:
Trustees Hugh Brower and Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Fire Chief Ron Colpaert, Deputy
Fire Chief Scott Smith, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, and no persons in the audience.
2. Emergency Operations Plan.
Chief Colpaert reviewed the organization and major provisions of the Village’s
Draft Emergency Operations Plan and commented briefly on the different
sections. The Plan delineates operational procedures and provides contact
information to get outside help into the Village if such help is deemed
necessary. He explained that this Plan is an updated version of one that has
been in effect for many years and is a living document that will continually be
revised and updated. He added that after the plan is approved by the Council
and the County, the Village will be conducting various training exercises.
3. Residential Design Review.
Mr. Norkus reviewed the basics of the residential design review policies of
Lake Forest and Park Ridge, two communities that have fairly long histories in
this process. He pointed out differences and similarities between the two and
compared them to Winnetka’s existing regulations and practices. He noted that
many of the elements of both the Lake Forest and Park Ridge approaches exist
under current Winnetka requirements.
In the ensuing discussion of the concept of residential design review, Trustee
Woodbury noted that the Lake Forest Building Review Board appears to exercise
its influence in an unwritten, informal manner rather than the more structured
approach taken in Park Ridge. He expressed his belief that in order to be
effective in this community, there would need to be a blending of the two
approaches, including some objective standards/guidelines. He stated his belief
that any kind of residential design review should be contextual in nature as
opposed to directed at the specifics of bricks and mortar. He expressed his
opinion that good taste and design are subjective and cannot be legislated or
dictated by committee. He did, however, say that providing a vehicle for
contextual review would provide an opportunity to influence design changes
without amending the Zoning Ordinance.
President Duhl expressed concern about the lack of neutral principles and the
long term impact on the Village if procedures are introduced that prevent
obsolete housing stock from being replaced.
Attorney Janega explained that the State’s zoning enabling act allows
municipalities to use zoning powers for aesthetic purposes. However, in
instances where design review processes have been adjudicated, ordinances
focused solely on appearance have generally not been upheld. She said that
aesthetics can be a part of the basis for zoning regulations, but not the sole
basis.
Ms. Janega also pointed out that Lake Forest has a two-year waiting period for
a demolition permit if the applicant does not go through the design review
process.
Manager Williams remarked that there are already significant complaints from
residents about the length of the processing time for building permits and that
implementing a design review process cannot be absorbed by the current staff
without increasing processing times. He indicated that it would take not only
additional staff, but time to gear up for a design review process.
After further discussion, the Trustees agreed to appoint an ad hoc committee in
the fall, consisting of representatives from each of the Village’s standing
committees and two Trustees, to study the feasibility of implementing a
residential design review process. In the meantime, Trustees Woodbury and
Greenough agreed to work on a draft of a charge to the committee, for
consideration by the Council.
4. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Brower and Presser None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:03 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:24 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustees
Brower and Presser. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
5. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Brower and
Presser. None. The meeting adjourned at 9:25 p.m.
June 18, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 2, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
18, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Nan Greenough. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and seven persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that the CATV Resolution, Item No. 6(e) had been moved
to the end of the Agenda and Item 9(b), the Village Yards Bid, had been removed
from the Agenda. The Trustees approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   July 2, 2002 Regular Meeting. President Duhl said that he would be unable
to attend, but all of the Trustees present indicated that they expected to
attend.
b)   July 9, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 4, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   June 11, 2002 Study Session. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-16-2002 – Special Use Permit: 1112 Willow Rd., Crow Island
School - Adoption. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt
the ordinance granting a special Use Permit to allow the replacement of
playground equipment at Crow Island School. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough.
b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2002 – Zoning Variation : 1486 Scott Street - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting intensity of use of lot, maximum building size, and front setback
variations to permit the reconstruction of an existing front porch and the
construction of a new bay window and a new second floor balcony. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough.
c)   Ordinance No. M-18-2002 – Zoning Variation: 779 Bryant - Adoption. Trustee
Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance granting a
rear yard setback to permit the construction of a three-car detached garage. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell,
Woodbury, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough
d)   Ordinance No. M-19-2002 – Zoning Variation: 882 Willow Rd. - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1273 and 1274. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1273 in the amount of $619,878.67 and Warrant
List No. 1274 in the amount of $316,115.09. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Greenough.
b)   Village Yards Bids. As announced previously by President Duhl, this item
was removed from the Agenda.
c)   Police Squad Car Purchase. Manager Williams reviewed a request from the
Police Department to purchase three new squad cars via the State of Illinois
bid to replace cars that will have been driven more than 65,000 miles before
January 1, 2003. The state bid price for the vehicles, equipped with the
options that the Village traditionally orders will be approximately $20,000 per
car.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to authorize the purchase of
the squad cars. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Greenough.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Presser announced that the newest edition of the
Winnetka Report would be received by residents this week and encouraged all to
review the article summarizing the manner in which the Council has carried out
the directives of the Village Caucus.
Trustee Woodbury reported that he had attended the recent annual meeting and
celebration of the Historical Society.
Trustees Brower and Aquilino reported on a recent meeting of the Chamber of
Commerce. Trustee Brower noted that the annual holiday ornament would be of the
Log House. Trustee Aquilino commented that the Chamber was devoting a great
deal of effort toward economic development and noted that she had recently
attended a meeting of a special task force studying the issue. She reminded the
Trustees and residents that a joint meeting of the Council and the Chamber was
scheduled for the Study Session on July 9th.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the reappointment of both Keith Groebe as Chairman,
and Karen Templeton to the Building Review Committee.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to reappoint Mr. Groebe
as Chairman. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to reappoint Ms.
Templeton. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 7:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:10 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Greenough. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Resolution No. R-22-2002 and R-23-2003 – CATV Transfer.
Manager Williams reported that as required by the Village’s cable television
franchise agreement, the cable operator has requested that the Village approve
a transfer of its ownership. He explained, however, that there are a number of
outstanding issues between the cable company and the Village having to do with
the cable company’s failure to perform certain obligations pursuant to the
franchise agreement. Therefore, the Village and the cable company have been
negotiating an Agreement to remedy those issues, including the undergrounding
of its cables on Tower Road, before the Village consents to the transfer. Mr.
Williams then reviewed the provisions of the draft agreement which, among other
things, provides for the cable company to return signed copies of the Agreement
to the Village Manager by a specified date. The agreement also requires the
cable company to deposit an irrevocable letter of credit on behalf of the
Village in the amount of $300,000 to secure the company’s performance in
correcting the outstanding issues by a specific date.
Carlo Cavallero, Director of Financing and Local Government Affairs for AT&T
Broadband, responded to questions from the Trustees. After further discussion,
Mr. Cavallero stated that the company agreed that the signed agreement would be
returned to the Village by 5:00 p.m. on Wednesday, June 26, 2002, and that the
letter of credit would be deposited by July 18, 2002. He also stated that the
company agreed to pay the Village’s costs of enforcing the agreement if the
letter of credit is not delivered by the agreed date.
Given the impending deadline for approving the transfer, Attorney Janega
suggested that the Trustees adopt dual resolutions, one approving the transfer
contingent upon receipt of the properly executed Agreement by the agreed upon
time, the other rejecting and objecting to the transfer if the signed Agreement
is not received by the agreed date and time. She then reviewed the provisions
of the two draft resolutions, noting that Agreement and both resolutions would
have to be amended to reflect the final agreement between the Village and the
cable company, as reflected in the evening’s discussions between the Council
and AT&T’s representatives.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to amend Resolution No. R-
22-2002, A Resolution Authorizing Execution of an Agreement with TCI American
Cable Holdings II, L.P. and AT&T Corporation and Approving the Transfer of
Cable Ownership from a Subsidiary of AT& T Corporation to a Subsidiary of AT& T
Comcast, as discussed and agreed to by the parties. By voice vote the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve Resolution
No.R-22-2002, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Greenough.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to amend Resolution No.
R-23-2002, Resolution of the Village of Winnetka Rejecting and Objecting to the
Proposed Transfer of a Cable Television System from a Subsidiary of AT& T
Corporation to a Subsidiary of AT& T Comcast, as discussed and agreed by the
parties. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve Resolution No.R-
23-2002, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Greenough.
14. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to return into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 8:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:18 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Greenough. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
15. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Presser, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough.
The meeting adjourned at 9:19 p.m.
