July 2, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 16, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
2, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Barbara Aquilino called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m.
Present: Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: President Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Assistant Director of
Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders
and 13 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Aquilino led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Pro Tem Aquilino noted the addition of Item 13, Building and Zoning
Enforcement Issues, to the Agenda. The Trustees approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   July 9, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   July 16, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 18, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-20-2002 – Zoning Variations: 1167 Asbury Ave. -
Introduction. Mr. Norkus reviewed the request for Maximum Building Size and
Side Yard Setback variations to permit construction of a second story addition
above the existing residence. He mentioned that the request incorporates eaves
that project 24 inches into the required side yards, whereas such eaves are
limited to a projection of 18 inches into any required yard. Mr. Norkus
reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend approval of
the variations, noting comments that to require one of the two sides to be set
in by 2 ft. would be a hardship and that the proposed improvements would bring
the house into scale with others in the neighborhood.
Trustee Brower inquired whether the house is currently for sale and asked if
the Council would be granting a GFA variance for a potential developer. Manager
Williams indicated that staff would investigate this question.
Mr. Norkus pointed out that the variation would be granted only for the
specific set of plans submitted. Therefore, any new owner would have to want to
build to those plans or begin from scratch with a request for a different
variation.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting the requested variations. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-21-2002 – Zoning Variation : 470 Chestnut - Introduction.
Mr. Norkus reviewed a request for a variation of the Building Line Articulation
provisions to permit construction of a one-story addition at the rear of the
existing residence. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to
recommend approval of the variation.
Andrew Venamore, a representative of the builder, appeared on behalf of the
applicant. He noted that due to the size of the lot, there is very little room
in which to plan any articulation.
Trustees Greenough and Aquilino commented favorably on the design, saying that
it does not create a monolithic block. The windows and chimney provide some
attractive articulation, even though they do not meet the necessary
requirements, and there is some vertical articulation.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting the requested variations. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   Resolution No. R-25-2002 – Essex Consolidation (222 Essex Rd. and 357
Sunset Rd.) – Adoption. Mr. Norkus explained that as a culmination of a process
begun earlier in the year, the necessary plat for final approval of a
resubdivision to consolidate the above-referenced two properties into a single
lot had been submitted. The proposed resubdivision is part of a development
plan that calls for the demolition of the residence at 357 Sunset in order to
accommodate the expansion of the residence at 222 Essex. Mr. Norkus reviewed
actions taken and comments issued to date by the Village Council, Plan
Commission, and Zoning Board of Appeals.
Attorney Janega reviewed restrictive covenants, incorporated as Exhibit B to
the proposed resolution, addressing conditions recommended by the Plan
Commission.
The applicant, Mr. Reedy, expressed some concern about the language of one of
the provisions pertaining to limitations on reconstruction. He said that, in
his opinion, this would make it impossible to rebuild the house in the event
the house should be involuntarily destroyed and voiced worry that he would be
unable to obtain insurance given the restrictions. Under such circumstances, he
indicated that his intent would be to rebuild the exact same house.
Trustee Greenough indicated that she would have no objections to amending the
language to permit reconstruction of exactly the same structure in the case of
involuntary destruction of the property but felt strongly that the restrictive
provisions should remain with regard to voluntary demolition.
After a brief discussion, the other Trustees concurred. Although, Trustee
Woodbury noted that while in support of the point, he was not in favor of the
application.
President Pro Tem Aquilino directed the Village Attorney to redraft the
restrictive covenants to say that if the principal building is destroyed by an
involuntary occurrence, the building may be restored to its condition
immediately prior to the occurrence. She pointed out that this restriction will
run with the property unless overturned at a later time by a future Council.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the restrictive
covenant accordingly. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the proposed
resolution granting the requested consolidation, subject to the conditions
recommended and pursuant to Ordinance M-9-2002 and the execution and recording
of the restrictive covenants. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Powell, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: Trustee
Woodbury. Absent: President Duhl.
d)   Resolution No. R-26-2002 – Release of Executive Session Minutes -
Adoption. Manager Williams explained that this is a Village housekeeping duty
required by State law to be performed every six months. Trustee Woodbury,
seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the minutes of the Executive
Sessions held January 8, 2002 through June 18, 2002. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser then moved to adopt Resolution No. R-26-2002 releasing those
minutes no longer required to be held for reasons of confidentiality. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and
Woodbury Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
e)   Ordinance No. M-22-2002 – Special Use Permit: 540 Hibbard Rd. -
Introduction. Mr. Norkus reviewed a request from the Winnetka Park District for
a Special Use Permit to allow for the construction of a one-story addition at
the north end of Neilsen Tennis Center to accommodate Park District’s
administrative offices and a community room. The proposed building would
replace the temporary office trailers that currently occupy the site.
Park District Director Liza McElroy and Ken Kenik, the architect from Sente &
Rubel, reviewed the elevations, commenting on design features.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting the requested Special Use Permit. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-23-2002 – Landmark Designation: 411 Linden. Mr. Norkus
reviewed the Landmark Preservation Commission’s recommendation that the Village
Council designate 411 Linden Street as a Winnetka Landmark. He reported that
based upon the adopted System for Evaluation of Landmarks, the property
received an overall score of 83 points, resulting in a “Unique” rating. He
noted, as well, that the Landmark Preservation Commission had found all of the
proposed alterations appropriate to the character of the property and
recommended their approval. He pointed out that the Design Review Board had
commented on signage and lighting.
Attorney Janega explained that there were two matters requiring Council’s
attention. The first being the introduction of the ordinance designating the
property as a local landmark, and the second being to comment on the design
review issues required by the ordinance for the purpose of providing direction.
This matter will be brought back to the Council in the form of a resolution
confirming approval of the design elements.
Trustee Presser expressed some discomfort with the proposed signage, saying
that it is larger and dissimilar from neighboring signs. He urged the Society
to find a smaller sign and indicated a preference for a plaque on the house.
Nancy Judge, President of the Historical Society, responded that it is somewhat
larger than other address signs, but is in keeping with signs found in
neighborhoods. She said that mounting a sign on the house might detract from
the architecturally significant entry doors.
Trustee Brower suggested that the Society try to involve the neighbors in the
signage decision.
Ms. Judge pointed out that there had been an opportunity for neighbors to
comment at the various board meetings.
Carter Page, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission commented on the
Commission’s review of the signage, saying that it was compatible with the time
period and that it would not have been unusual for a house to have a commercial
use as well as being a residence.
Trustee Presser remarked that in view of the extremely controversial nature of
this issue, the Council’s obligation to the neighbors is to preserve the
character of the neighborhood, not to restore the structure to what it might
have been.
Trustee Woodbury raised issue with the height of the proposed signpost,
suggesting that a six-foot maximum should be sufficient.
Manager Williams said that staff would research the issue and bring back
alternatives.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the ordinance
designating the property as a local landmark. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1275 and 1276. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1275 in the amount of $1,165,684.27 and
Warrant List No. 1276 in the amount of $244,223.05. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Aquilino, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
President Duhl.
b)   Village Yards Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed bids received by the Village’s
construction manager, Riley Construction Company, for 29 subcontracts for
construction of the Village Yards project. He discussed the total project cost
and cost savings generated by Riley.
Manager Williams noted that it has taken three years of process to get to this
point in the project. He reviewed the history of the project’s evolution and
pointing to the public process involved. He said that the Village is now ready
to move forward with the construction phase. He pointed out that the
Construction Manager, Riley Construction, has a very good track record in this
type of work, and that the Village’s Technical Committee has been deeply
involved throughout the process. He also thanked Irv Footlick, a resident and
engineer, for his valuable suggestions to the Committee, the architects, and
staff.
Mr. Footlick raised some additional points, suggesting further ways in which
the Village might experience some savings.
The Trustees discussed the specifics of the addendum to the Riley’s contract
stipulating a Guaranteed Maximum Price for construction of $7,820,162 at some
length.
Trustee Woodbury noted that although there are always professional differences
of opinion, it is important to keep the project moving forward as these issues
are sorted out. He said that he believed that the major issues have been
resolved, resulting in the right answers for this Village and a good plan
overall. He, therefore, moved to authorize Riley Construction to award
subcontractor bids for the construction of the Village Yards Project. Trustee
Presser seconded his motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: President
Duhl.
c)   Capital Improvements Reports. Manager Williams referred the Trustees to
the reports filed by the Department Heads included in their Agenda packets. He
also asked Mr. Saunders to review the status of the Tower Road project. Mr.
Saunders indicated that the project might be completed before the November 1st
completion date.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that due to the hot, dry summer
weather, sales of water and electricity were high. He also announced that the
signed agreement had been received from AT&T Cable.
11. Appointments.
President Pro Tem Aquilino suggested the appointment of Mary Ellen Swenson to
the Design Review Board to replace Betsy Karp whose term has expired..
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the
appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:40 p.m. Present: President
Pro Tem Aquilino, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: President Duhl. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and
Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Building and Zoning Enforcement Issues.
Attorney Janega recommended that the Council approve the settlement agreement
with Heritage Builders pertaining zoning and building enforcement issues
discussed Executive Session. Trustee Brower so moved, seconded by Trustee
Presser. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Powell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: Trustee Aquilino. Absent: President
Duhl.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough,
Powell, Presser, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl. The
meeting adjourned at 10:45 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                     Recording Secretary
July 9, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: August 6, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Village Planner Ann Klaassen, and eight
persons in the audience.
2. Winnetka Chamber of Commerce: Discussion.
Cicely Michalak, Executive Director of the Chamber, stated that the Chamber’s
mission is “to build an organization based on consensus, cooperation, and
commitment for the purpose of enhancing the integrity, heritage, sense of pride
and unique assets of our community.” She reviewed the Chamber’s objectives and
reviewed an outline of major concerns prepared by Chamber members in the hope
of developing a comprehensive, long-range action plan.
Pat Horne showed slides depicting some of the Chamber’s major concerns, along
with slides of examples of how other villages have addressed similar problems.
She emphasized the group’s belief that there needs to be changes to signage and
lighting.
Manager Williams presented brief technical analysis of lighting levels showing
both existing light patterns and the projected light patterns if more,
decorative poles were installed. He indicated that to change the existing light
standards would be an expensive project, somewhere in the neighborhood of $2-
3 million dollars. Mr. Williams suggested that the Village could increase the
wattage of the existing bulbs on an experimental basis to see how the residents
react to the change.
President Duhl remarked that the technical analysis clearly demonstrates that
the lighting level has nothing to do with the style of the light standard.
The discussion then turned to holiday lighting. Manager Williams explained the
history of the issue, noting that one of the major considerations has been
whether or not the lights need to be removed annually. He indicated that staff
continues to investigate this matter, and he would return to the Council with a
recommendation in the near future.
There was an extended discussion on parking problems throughout the Village
touching on various components of the issue, including availability of
desirable spaces, abuse, poor signage, and possibilities for the future.
Manager Williams indicated that the Village is looking to develop plans for a
parking deck at the Community House, with construction projected for 2005.
Pat Horne asked whether the Village would consider permitting banners
advertising community events to be flown from the light standards. Manager
Williams replied that the Village has permitted such banners for the Women’s
Club and he could see no reason why there would be objection to other
community-wide events being given similar approval, although proposals would
have to go through the regular design review process.
Ms. Horne asked whether the Village would absorb the cost for such banners.
President Duhl indicated that that would depend upon the cost.
Roberta Rubin commented that her experience is that in the past the Village and
the Chamber have not worked efficiently together on improving the health of the
business districts. She said that the Chamber is proposing that an Economic
Development Commission including members from both organizations be formed to
address the issues identified.
President Duhl said that the Village could not accept total responsibility for
any perceived lack of cooperation between the two bodies. He pointed out that
the Council is a reactive organization, not a proactive one, and must deal with
the issues that are brought before it as they arise. As a body, it must
carefully consider Village-wide concerns, as well as resources, before
determining priorities. He indicated that such a committee might be formed, but
its mission and scope of responsibilities would have to be clearly defined so
as not to impinge upon the jurisdictions of any existing committees. He
indicated that the Council would consider the concept of forming the proposed
committee and get back to the Chamber in a couple of months.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that the Comprehensive Plan and Design Guidelines
need to be incorporated into proposed capital improvements, but pointed out
that it cannot all be achieved immediately.
Manager Williams indicated that the Village has been incorporating components
of the Design Guidelines into the Five-Year Capital Plan.
President Duhl commented that everyone needs to recognize that with tax caps,
the Village is not in the same financial position that it was ten years ago.
Costs are increasing faster than revenues, and the Village does not have the
ability to raise millions of dollars for various projects that may not,
necessarily, be vital to the Village’s sustained well being. He suggested that
creative means of funding need to be developed.
President Duhl thanked the Chamber representatives for their input.
3. Landmark Preservation Commission: 358 Winnetka Avenue: Discussion.
Carter Page, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission, expressed the
frustration of both the Landmark Preservation Commission and the Building
Review Committee that the processes currently in place do not adequately
protect architecturally significant homes, such as the subject property, from
inevitable demolition.
Trustee Presser inquired whether the two bodies have developed an alternative
proposal.
Mr. Page suggested either very stringent landmark regulations that impose on
the existing property rights, making them outside the realm of demolition, or
some kind of community effort to provide for preservation of these houses.
President Duhl remarked that those are the two extreme options. He indicated an
unwillingness to resurrect the landmark preservation fight, saying that he
believes it is unrealistic to expect this Village to accept a strong landmark
preservation ordinance.
Several members of both the Landmark and Building Review committees expressed
the belief that the tenor of the community might have changed and that it might
be more receptive to a strong preservation ordinance now than it was in the
past.
Manager Williams pointed out that the fundamental issue is one of private
property rights. Can/should the needs of the Village override owner’s private
property rights, perhaps to the extent of affecting property values?
Trustee Aquilino suggested investigating the possibility of establishing some
kind of a foundation that would be able to purchase these kinds of properties
as they become available, renovate them appropriately, and then sell them to
individuals willing to accept the restrictions associated with owning a
landmark property. She supported the concept of having an inventory identifying
houses that would be comparably categorized as historical treasures.
President Duhl pointed out that any such foundation would need significant
funding, as well as appropriate leadership. He noted that the Council had
agreed previously that an architectural survey should be performed, but that
the time frame for this survey is undetermined and pointed out that, at this
point, there is little the Village can do regarding the ultimate disposition of
the subject property.
All of the Trustees agreed that some sort of private foundation was a highly
desirable concept. All also agreed that the Village needs to take every
opportunity to continue to educate the community about the value of its
architecture.
4. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:15 p.m.
July 16, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 6, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
16, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Stephen Presser.
Absent: Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, and 25 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl noted that Item 6(a), 1167 Asbury, had been removed from the
Agenda at the request of the applicant and that Item 9(e), Policy Direction:
Train Stations, had been removed from the Agenda for administrative purposes.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   August 6, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Aquilino, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   August 13, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend. Mr. Williams noted that Trustee Woodbury had informed
him that he would not be present.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 2, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. After Trustee Greenough made a
correction to the recorded vote on Item 13, Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as amended. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-20-2002 – Zoning Variations: 1167 Asbury Ave. - Adoption.
As noted above, this item was removed from the Agenda at the request of the
applicant.
b)   Ordinance No. M-21-2002 – Zoning Variation : 470 Chestnut - Adoption.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the ordinance granting a variation of the
Building Line Articulation provisions to permit construction of a one-story
addition at the rear of the existing residence. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Woodbury.
c)   Ordinance No. M-22-2002 – Special Use Permit: 540 Hibbard Rd. - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment to the draft ordinance providing
for the removal of the mobile offices upon completion and occupancy of the new
space.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend the ordinance as
explained by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino moved to approve the ordinance, as amended, granting a Special
Use Permit to the Winnetka Park District to allow for the construction of a
one-story addition at the north end of Nielsen Tennis Center to accommodate
Park District administrative offices and a community room. Trustee Powell
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Woodbury.
d)   Winnetka Historical Society: 411 Linden
   i)   Ordinance No. M-23-2002 – Landmark Designation: 411 Linden - Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
designating 411 Linden Street as a local landmark. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell,
and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-24-2002 – Granting Design Approval – Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed the history of the issue. He pointed out that the ordinance
approving the Special Use Permit and variations for the Historical Society’s
occupancy of 411 Linden Street imposed the condition that exterior lighting and
signage be approved by the Village Council in accordance with standards
applicable to single-family, residential buildings. He reported that both the
Landmark Preservation Commission and the Design Review Board had commented
favorably upon the proposed plans.
President Duhl suggested that the Council discuss the issues of lighting and
signage separately. No Trustee expressed any issue with the proposed lighting,
so that matter was set aside.
Trustee Presser voiced strong objection to the size of the proposed sign,
saying that he does not believe it to be consistent with the residential
character of the neighborhood and, therefore, inconsistent with pledges that
were made by the Historical Society to blend into the surrounding single-family
neighborhood. He acknowledged the need for some signage, but suggested a
smaller sign or a plaque placed on the side of the house.
Robert Lapp, 414 Linden, echoed Mr. Presser’s objections.
Responding on behalf of the Historical Society, Joan Evanich and Nancy Judge
supported the proposed size and design, noting that within residential
districts there exists a wide variety of styles and sizes, all of which are
acceptable because there are no standards. They pointed out that because the
Society is an institution serving the public, it wishes to be clearly
identifiable, welcoming, and accessible to all. They urged approval of the
proposed design.
Becky Hurley, 1027 Spruce St., pointed out that the Special Use Permit
indicates that the Council will evaluate the signage based upon residential
standards, of which there are none. She expressed the need for the Society to
operate with a fine sensitivity to its neighborhood but said that it cannot
operate as a residential home, because it isn’t one, and it needs the freedom
to operate as an institution.
Trustee Greenough indicated that she had surveyed various institutional signs
throughout the Village, almost all of which are in residential neighborhoods,
and in her opinion, the proposed design is substantially more subdued and more
aesthetically pleasing than existing signs, particularly because of the
setback. She deemed the black background in keeping with a residential
character and, therefore, appropriate.
Trustee Brower concurred with Trustee Greenough as to the attractiveness of the
sign, but expressed concern about the objections from the neighbors. Like
Trustee Presser, he urged the Society to consider alternative designs.
Trustee Aquilino voiced support for the proposed design, as did Trustee Powell.
Discussion followed as to the exact height of the proposed sign on the
lightpole, after which President Duhl suggested that the resolution be amended
to reflect that the light would extend no more than six (6) feet to the bottom
of the collar of the light fixture. Trustee Greenough so moved, seconded by
Trustee Powell. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the resolution
approving the proposed exterior changes to 411 Linden Street. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Greenough, and
Brower. Nays: Trustee Presser. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2002 – Amendment to Sign Regulations – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a proposed amendment to the Sign Code to allow and
regulate the use of sandwich board and menu board signs. He explained that
currently there are no specific provisions for the use of such signs, so
essentially, they are not permitted. The proposed ordinance would permit their
use as “temporary” signs, not requiring a permit, but there would be certain
limitations to their use. Mr. D’Onofrio enumerated those limitations.
Manager Williams remarked that staff does not have a strong opinion on this
matter, but requires policy direction as there have been administrative
problems associated with their use.
Trustee Powell voiced strong objection to the idea of a sign sticking out from
the building. He said he would prefer to see signs flush with the building
face.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance
permitting the limited use of sandwich board signs so long as they are adjacent
to the building. By voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Powell voted
against the motion.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2002 – Addition of New Liquor License: Kyoto –
Introduction. Manager Williams reviewed the ordinance that would allow the
Liquor Commissioner to issue a liquor license to Kyoto, a new Japanese
Restaurant, once the background investigation is completed by the Police
Department and all attachments to the application are received from the
applicants.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
Public Comment and Questions. MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln. commented on
the order of the Agenda and asked when there would be a discussion on the
possibility of installing a sidewalk on Pine Street. President Duhl explained
that the next sidewalk under consideration will be one proposed for Spruce
Street. He said the Council would address her issue when a petition is received
from residents whose property would be affected.
8. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1277 and 1278. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1277 in the amount of $633,642.60 and Warrant
List No. 1278 in the amount of $469,551.23. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Aquilino, Greenough, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Woodbury.
Mr. Brower also noted the installation a drive-by drop box for the payment of
utility bills located just south of the Post Office’s drive-by boxes. President
Duhl encouraged residents to make use of this new convenience and to consider
the automatic bill payment option offered by the Village.
b)   Policy Direction: Request to Amend Liquor Ordinance. Jim Pomerantz, owner
of J. P. McCarthy’s Restaurant, presented a request to amend the Liquor
Ordinance to extend service hours from 11:00 p.m. to 12:00 midnight and to
permit the service of liquor without the service of food. Mr. Pomerantz
represented that no neighboring community requires the purchase of a meal with
the purchase of an alcoholic beverage and that these communities have not
experienced significant problems.
Trustee Presser expressed sympathy for the concept of serving liquor without
the purchase of a meal, but inquired as to the reasoning for requesting an
extension of the hours of service.
Mr. Pomerantz replied that the current hours are restrictive, especially on
weekends when patrons wish to have service after a movie or event.
President Duhl asked whether Mr. Pomerantz would object to restricting service
to inside between the hours of 11:00 and Midnight. Mr. Pomerantz responded that
he would not.
Mr. Duhl mentioned that the Caucus was intending to place a question on its
annual questionnaire regarding the service of liquor without the purchase of
meals, but that the Council need not wait for those responses to act. He also
pointed out, however, that many restaurants are very close to residential
districts and that if hours of service are extended, it might generate
objections from neighbors. He suggested that a public hearing be held on this
question.
Trustee Presser indicated favor with the concept of allowing a percentage of
the total service area to be available for the service of liquor only, but he
was not immediately in favor of extending the hours of service.
Trustees Aquilino and Greenough expressed support for both concepts.
Trustee Powell and Trustee Brower spoke in favor of permitting service without
a meal, but felt a hearing should be held about extending the hours of service.
Mr. Duhl directed the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance authorizing the
service of alcoholic beverages without the purchase of a meal within certain
parameters to be recommended by staff. He said that a public hearing be
scheduled for the fall to address the question of extending the hours for
liquor service.
President Duhl thanked Mr. Pomerantz for bringing this matter before the
Council.
c)   Policy Direction: Moffat Mall. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed proposed renovation
plans for Moffat Mall submitted by the Winnetka Garden Club, including
replacement and realignment of paved and planted areas, replacement of plant
materials, hardscape surfaces and street furniture, and a new decorative
fountain and decorative surround. He reported that the Design Review Board had
commented favorably on the proposed renovations, but recommended use of coach
light fixtures consistent with the Design Guidelines to replace and augment
existing light fixtures. The Board, however, was receptive to the Garden Club’s
concern that the cost of new, consistent lighting would be prohibitive, and
expressed support for the idea of changing the existing fixture heads rather
than replacing the entire unit. The Board also supported allowing the Club to
use the new, proposed fixture if replacing the existing fixture heads was found
to be too expensive.
Linda Walsh, Chairman Winnetka Garden Club, said the Club is looking for policy
direction from the Village with regard to the lights. She said the Club is
excited about the project and wants conceptual approval so that it can move
ahead with planning and fundraising.
Manager Williams asked when the Club was hoping to begin construction. Ms.
Walsh responded that they were aiming for next spring, depending upon funding.
She indicated that the Club would be seeking some financial support from the
Village.
Trustee Greenough commented that the plan is attractive and sensitive but said
she would like to have the Club explore further alternatives for the light
fixtures that are compatible with the Design Guidelines. She suggested that the
Council approve the Club’s renovation plans subject to the light posts being
consistent with Design Guidelines. Trustee Presser so moved, seconded by
Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Policy Direction: Request from Winnetka Youth Organization. Manager
Williams explained a request from the Winnetka Youth Organization for a
clarification of the agreement between the Village and WYO as to what is
required in order for the organization to obtain Village funding in the amount
of $5,000. Mr. Williams briefly reviewed the history of this issue and outlined
two policy options for the Council to consider. He pointed out that should the
Council provide a direct donation to WYO, as requested, it must be prepared to
receive similar requests from other equally worthy organizations.
MaryEllen Spicer, Director of WYO, explained the organization’s understanding
of the existing arrangement, saying that the WYO had never understood it to be
a contract-for-services, but rather, a voluntary service provided in exchange
for the Village’s donation. She said that there had never been a discussion as
to the number of volunteers who would perform the jobs nor the number of hours
that would be worked. She asked for a clearer mandate from the Council.
Trustee Presser asked specifically what short of an outright contribution the
WYO was requesting.
Ms. Spicer responded that the WYO could continue to provide volunteer services,
but given the nature and mission of the organization, it was unreasonable to
equate the number of hours worked to the amount of money received.
After a brief discussion, President Duhl restated the Council’s consistent
position that the Village cannot and will not make an outright contribution to
the WYO, but will authorize a reasonable payment for services rendered. That
payment, however, must be consistent with the amount and value of the work
performed and what the Village would pay others to do the same task.
Wes Baumann, former Principal of New Trier High School, spoke in support of the
Village’s gifting money to WYO, saying that the WYO Board is not pleased with
the fact that the Village is requiring the young people to work for this money.
Manager Williams suggested that he and Trustee Brower, the Trustee
representative to the WYO, sit down with the WYO to determine a reasonable
scope of services and return to the Council with a recommendation. The Trustees
agreed to that proposal.
9. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Greenough commented that she had attended the most
recent meeting of the Building Review Committee and was very impressed with
their conscientious use of the ordinance; deliberate and thoughtful approach to
each case; and dedication.
Trustees Brower and Aquilino commented on the upcoming Sidewalk Sale.
10. Appointments.
None.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino,
Greenough, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 9:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:02 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Presser. Absent:
Trustee Woodbury. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
The meeting adjourned at 11:03 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                     Recording Secretary
August 6, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 20, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
6, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Assistant Director of
Public Works Alex Attiah and 53 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item No. 8(a), Amendments to the Liquor
Ordinance, had been removed from the Agenda. The Trustees approved the Agenda
as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   August 13, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Woodbury, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   August 20, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 9, 2002 Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   July 16, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Winnetka Preservation Awards.
President Duhl introduced Carter Page, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation
Commission, and former Chairman, Nan Greenough. Mr. Page and Trustee Greenough
showed slides of the winners, commenting on each of the projects, and presented
the following awards:
For Restoration:
Song O’Sixpence                 735 Elm St.
Leslie Graham & Louis Kenter      94 Mary St.
For Restoration & Rehabilitation
North Shore Community Bank        576 Lincoln Ave.
Grace & Jeff Medford              759 Prospect
For Rehabilitation:
Chris & Robert Zabors             526 Willow Rd
Kim & Rick Dsida                  522 Elm St.
Mary Ellen Keenan & Tom Hermanson 1003 Vine St.
Anne & Fred Belmont               631 Walden
Donna & Kurt Voges                946 Oak St.
Christy & Craig Fowler            1055 Sunset
President Duhl thanked all of the recipients for their contributions to the
beauty of the Village. He remarked that it is joyous to see what can be done
with older homes to maintain and enhance the character of the Village.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2002 – Amendment to Sign Regulations – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the ordinance pursuant to
Council’s discussion at the time of introduction, as well as to cure
inconsistencies between the Design Guidelines and the Sign Ordinance. She added
that a new subparagraph had been incorporated reserving the Village’s right to
remove or relocate a sign if it poses a hazard to pedestrian or vehicular
traffic. In addition, language was added to allow for banners meeting specified
standards if approved by the Village Council.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to amend the proposed
ordinance as reviewed by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
permitting the limited use of sandwich board signs so long as they are adjacent
to the building, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2002 – Addition of New Liquor License: Kyoto –
Adoption. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the
ordinance allowing the Liquor Commissioner to issue a liquor license to the new
restaurant. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-24-2002 – Zoning Variation: 530 Winnetka Ave. –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio summarized a request for a variation permitting
expansion of a nonconforming use and a nonconforming building. He noted that
this matter had been before the Council at its May 7, 2002 regular meeting for
policy direction and reviewed subsequent Design Review Board comments and plan
revisions by the petitioners, including the withdrawal of their request for
expanding the parking lot.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting variation for expansion of a nonconforming use and building. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl thanked the petitioner, Dr. Murphy, for his cooperation
throughout the process.
Trustee Brower expressed his disappointment that the Village and Dr. Murphy had
been unable to reach a compromise that would improve the appearance of the
neighborhood and help Dr. Murphy better serve his elderly and disabled
patients.
d)   Resolution No. R-21-2002 – Landscape Waste Siting Approval – Public
Hearing and Adoption. President Duhl declared open the second Public Hearing to
receive comment regarding the Village’s proposal to store landscape waste for
up to 72 hours at the Village Yards. Alex Attiah, Assistant Director of Public
Works, summarized the Village’s request. He explained that the Public Works
Department is petitioning the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency to amend
the existing Village landscape waste transfer station permit to allow for
temporary storage of landscape waste at the Public Works Yards in order to
provide the Department with operational flexibility. He noted that no
objections had been received during the public comment period.
Attorney Janega commented on the addition of specific points necessary to meet
statutory requirements.
President Duhl invited comments from the audience. There being none, he closed
the Public Hearing
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend the Resolution
as discussed by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
21-2002, as amended, authorizing the Village of Winnetka to operate a landscape
waste transfer station at the Village Yards and to store the waste for a period
of 72 hours. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
8. Old Business.
Policy Direction: Amendments to Liquor Ordinance.  As previously noted, this
item was removed from the Agenda.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
MollyBelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln. asked the Public Works Department to consider
iridescent paint on stop sign poles. She also read a letter to the Trustees
voicing her opinion regarding the proposed sign at the Historical Society.
10. New Business.
a)   Policy Direction: Spruce Street Sidewalk. President Duhl announced that
the Council would listen to testimony only at this meeting, saying that no
decision would be made until after the Trustees have had time to consider the
proposal and the testimony further. President Duhl provided background on the
Village’s approach to handling requests for sidewalks, saying that,
historically, the Council listens to the affected neighbors. He pointed out
that in cases like this, there are always strong opinions on both sides of the
issue and referred to letters and calls from proponents of each side. Then, he
asked Mr. Saunders to review the request to install a sidewalk on the south
side of Spruce Street from Glendale to Hibbard Road.
Mr. Saunders reported that the Village received a signed petition from a number
of Spruce Street residents requesting that the Village install the subject
sidewalk along with a stop sign at Spruce and Berkeley. In response to that
petition, the Engineering Staff examined the area in question and suggested a
possible route. Mr. Saunders reviewed the existing conditions of the site,
including the status of the street, curbs, and stop signs.
With regard to the requested stop sign, Mr. Saunders reported that the
warrants, or standards, spelling out conditions for the use of traffic control
signs are not satisfied by experience at the requested intersection. He pointed
out that stop signs are right-of-way control devices and are not intended or
effective for speed control. In fact, where unwarranted, they create a higher
level of contempt among drivers, while increasing the perception of security
for pedestrians. Therefore, he recommended against the installation of the
requested stop sign.
In response to a question from Trustee Brower as to the category of street into
which Spruce Street fall, Mr. Saunders indicated that Spruce Street qualifies
as a minor street, not a collector street or a primary street.
Mike Pyle, 1260 Spruce, spoke against the proposed sidewalk. He commented on
the lack of curbs on Spruce Street and the impact that has on traffic. He
pointed out that the street is heavily trafficked by people using the
facilities at Skokie Playfields He asked whether the Village has plans to
install curbs and gutters on Spruce and suggested that they might serve
essentially the same purpose as installing a sidewalk without compromising the
open space of the right-of-way and possibly depreciating property values.
Manager Williams responded that the Village picks off streets one at a time to
install curbs and gutters. He said that Spruce is on the list, but not towards
the top of the list.
President Duhl asked Mr. Pyle whether he would be in favor of a sidewalk set
against a curb. Mr. Pyle said that he would not be.
James Cavanaugh, 1210 Spruce Street, also spoke against the proposed sidewalk.
He indicated that it is his perception that there are too many cars, going too
fast, but said that these issues will not be solved by the installation of a
sidewalk. He expressed deep concern about the loss of mature trees should the
Village move ahead with this proposal. Dr. Cavanaugh suggested that because the
neighborhood as a whole has had little opportunity to discuss this issue, all
of the neighbors ought to sit down together to see if they can come to a
compromise before asking the Council to intervene.
Janega McVicker, 1161 Spruce Street, commented favorably upon the proposal, but
agreed with Dr. Cavanaugh that the neighborhood needed to explore the problem
further together to see if there are mutually acceptable alternatives.
Steve Sullivan, 1186 Spruce, voiced concern about possible exacerbation of
flooding if a sidewalk were to be installed.
Jim Luxem, 1225 Spruce, Brian Foley, 1245 Spruce, and Janet Ryan, 1174 Spruce,
also spoke in support of the proposal.
Bob and Monica Yolles, 1235 Spruce, Lawrence Yolles, 1252 Spruce, Marian
Hadley, 1155 Spruce, and Stephanie Cavanaugh, 1210 Spruce, voiced their
objections and concerns.
Saying that this testimony was a good first step in the process, President Duhl
thanked the participants, and suggested that the neighborhood meet to consider
the matter and return to the Council at its September 17, 2002 meeting.
b)   Policy Direction: Train Stations. Manager Williams reviewed this matter.
He reported that the consultant hired by Metra to evaluate the condition of the
train station roof systems has determined that they are in badly deteriorated
condition and must be completely replaced, along with extensive repair of the
mortar joints on the limestone coping and tuckpointing of the masonry facades.
In connection therewith, Metra representatives appeared before the Design
Review Board in May to outline renovation plans. He noted that Metra’s
assessment included a cost estimate for various components of the work,
including alternative roof materials, i.e., asphalt shingles, wood shingles,
and natural slate shingles. He explained that due to the historic nature of
both stations, Metra would pay for all restorations up to and including a cedar
shake roof, with any additional cost associated with upgraded roofing material,
such as slate, to be borne by the Village.
Manager Williams reported that after two meetings, during which the train
station roofs had been considered, the Design Review Board issued favorable
comment recommending that the Village accept Metra’s plans to renovate both
stations. However, they stated preference for a slate roof, citing its longer
life span and higher quality appearance. amounting to $23,000 per station. He
also reported that a staff survey shows that Winnetka’s surrounding Northshore
communities do not have slate roofs on their train stations and commented that
slate had been rejected for both the Public Safety Building and the Village
Yards projects.
Mr. Williams pointed out that the Village’s Budget does not contain any
appropriation for upgrades to the train stations. He mentioned that during the
upcoming renovation process, it is likely that there will be numerous
additional items for consideration that could be upgraded depending upon the
Village’s desire and ability to do so.
Josel Gonzalez, Metra’s Project Manager, distributed samples of the alternative
roof materials for Council’s examination.
After an extended discussion, Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury
moved to direct Metra to proceed with slate tiles for the roofing material at
an additional cost to the Village of approximately $46,000. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Woodbury, Greenough, and
Brower. Nays: Trustee Powell. Absent: None.
c)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1279 and 1280. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1279 in the amount of $1,151,947.95 and
Warrant List No. 1280 in the amount of $667,876.03. Trustee Aquilino seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Request to Waive Bid Process: Refuse Trucks. Mr. Attiah reviewed a request
to waive the bid process for the purchase of a refuse truck replacement and
award the purchase of the chassis, with warranty, to Northwest Trucks, Inc., in
the amount of $67,300 and the award the purchase of the packer body to
R.N.O.W., Inc. for $44,697.
Manager Williams added that purchasing these vehicles would be in keeping with
the Village’s decision several years ago to standardize vehicles.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to waive the bidding
process and award the bids as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Village Yards Contract: Guaranteed Maximum Price. Mr. Saunders reviewed
the GMP Addendum and exhibits as contained in Council’s agenda materials. After
doing so, he asked the Council to approve Addendum #1 to the Contract for the
Village Yards Rehabilitation providing for a Guaranteed Maximum Price of
$7,820.162. He reminded the Council that with the Guaranteed Maximum Price, the
Construction Manager is committed to constructing the project as specified.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to walk the Council through the
Assumptions, which he did.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, move to approve the Addendum as
requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
President Duhl thanked Irv Footlick for his contributions to the project.
f)   Quarterly Report: Power Cost Adjustment. Manager Williams reported that
the power cost adjustment is working as intended.
11. Reports.
a)   Trustees: Trustee Presser announced that the new Winnetka Report would be
published shortly.
b)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the owners of 1167 Asbury had
officially withdrawn their application for zoning variations.
12. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the following appointments:
 
Name          Committee                           Status        Term
Don Derning   Board of Police and Fire            Reappointment 3 year term
Gwen Trindle  Chair: Plan Commission              Reappointment 3 year term
Robert Horne  Plan Commission                     Reappointment 3 year term
Beck Hurley   Plan Commission as School Board’s Reappointment 3 year term
              Representative
Richard Glenn Police Pension Board                Reappointment 2 year term
 
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the
appointments as suggested by President Duhl. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
13. Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, President Duhl canceled the Executive Session.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:25 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                     Recording Secretary
August 13, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: September 3, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell and Stephen Presser.
Absent: Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
restaurant owners Pat O’Neil and Jim Pomerantz, and one person in the audience.
2. Policy Options regarding Liquor Service. Discussion.
After extensive discussion with Mr. O’Neil and Mr. Pomerantz, the Village
Council, by consensus, determined it would consider amending the liquor control
ordinance as follows:
a)   All restaurants and Song O’ Sixpence would be allowed to serve liquor
without the service of a meal. There was no consensus about Corner Cooks.
b)   Liquor-only service would not be allowed on public sidewalks.
c)   Liquor-only service would be allowed for outdoor seating located on
private property.
d)   Prior to 9 p.m., only 20% of the seating capacity would be used for
alcohol service only and 80% would be dedicated to meal service. The alcohol-
only service area would be segregated from the remaining portion of restaurant.
e)   After 9 p.m. alcohol-only service would occur anywhere in the restaurant,
including the outdoor seating area if located on private property.
f)   A meal could be served at the bar.
g)   Alcohol service would be allowed up to 11 p.m. The last sale would be
conducted with enough time to complete consumption prior to closing (15 or 30
minutes).
h)   The primary business of the restaurant as a whole would be the service of
food and not alcohol. This change would redefine “incidental and complimentary”
to be applied to the whole business and not on a per customer basis.
i)   The ordinance would contain a sunset provision where these changes would
no longer be effective after one year. After a year, the Village Council would
assess the consequences of these changes.
2. Construction Hour Analysis. Discussion.
Manager Williams reviewed the Village’s laws regulating construction hours for
contractors. Also, the hours of comparable Villages were reviewed.
President Duhl asked the Council’s consensus. The Trustees agreed to limit
construction on Sundays with the exception of commercial areas. They also noted
that special holiday exceptions would be noted, such as major holidays.
3. Police Department – Update – Chief DeLopez Discussion.
Chief DeLopez updated the Council on the recent activities of the Police
Department.
4. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:50 p.m.
August 20, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 3, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
20, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, and five persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 6(c), a zoning variation at 1417 Edgewood,
had been removed from the Agenda at the request of the applicant. The Trustees
approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   September 3, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   September 10, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 6, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-24-2002 – Zoning Variation: 530 Winnetka Ave. – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment to the ordinance clarifying that
the request for parking had been removed from the variation.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the amendment as
outlined by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting variation for expansion of a nonconforming use and building. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-27-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1072 Ash St. – Policy
Direction. Manager Williams pointed out that since this request came from the
Zoning Board of Appeals with no recommendation, the real policy decision before
the Council is whether or not to direct the Village Attorney to prepare an
ordinance granting the requested variation.
Mr. D’Onofrio then reviewed the request for variations for Intensity of Use,
Maximum Building Size, and Building Line Articulation to permit construction of
a permanent screened porch.
The applicant, Carrie Compall, explained that the reason for the request is
that the existing porch leaks repeatedly and is not repairable, making the
space unusable as designed.
Trustee Presser asked Ms. Compall if there were any special circumstance that
applied to the property, some kind of unique circumstance that runs with the
land. She indicated that there was not, except that the porch is unusable as it
now exists.
After discussing the particulars of the request, Trustee Woodbury indicated
that he would not vote in favor of the application because the gross floor area
considerations remain. He said granting the request could set a precedent for
future similar requests, as well as send the wrong message to builders about
the Council’s position on enclosing porches after the passage of a relatively
short period of time.
Trustee Powell concurred saying that the house is already built to the maximum,
as did the remaining Trustees.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to deny the request for
variations for Intensity of Use, Maximum Building Size, and Building Line
Articulation to permit construction of a permanent screened porch. By roll
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustee Brower, Presser, Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-2-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1417 Edgewood Ave. – Adoption.
As noted earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda.
d)   Ordinance No. M-25-2002 – Zoning Variation: 165 Westview - Introduction.
Mr.’ D’Onofrio reviewed the request to for a Maximum Building Size variation
resulting from the fact that the new home was not constructed in accordance
with approved building plans through no fault of the applicant. He reported
that the Zoning Board of Appeals had issued a favorable recommendation.
Manager Williams explained that dropped ceilings could be ripped out and made
technically correct, so that the house would comply with GFA, but doing so
would not change the appearance of the house at all, and would, in fact, harm
the owner, an innocent bystander. He indicated that the issues with the
developer had been resolved.
Susan Connor appeared on behalf of the applicant.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that language be incorporated into the draft
ordinance that would preclude any future expansion of the house.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance
and direct the Village Attorney to include language reflecting the discussion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-26-2002 – Zoning Variation: 180 Ridge - Introduction. Mr.’
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front and Corner Yard Setbacks to allow
replacement of existing play equipment at Saints Faith, Hope and Charity
School. The new playground equipment will be located in the same area as the
existing equipment and would respect a similar setback. He reported that both
the Design review Board and the Zoning Board of Appeals had reviewed the
proposed plans and recommended approval of the variation request.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1281 and 1282. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1281 in the amount of $792,204.69 and Warrant
List No. 1282 in the amount of $427,156.36. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Street Bids. Manager. Williams reported that the low bidder for
rehabilitation of various streets within the Village was Arrow Construction at
$588,875.75, well below both the amount budgeted and staff’s estimate. Saying
that Arrow has performed well for the Village in the past, he recommended that
the bid be awarded to them.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
MollyBelle Berish distributed a letter and photos to the Trustees for their
consideration.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees: Trustee Presser reported that the Code Committee would reconvene
in September.
Trustees Aquilino and Brower reported on their attendance at the Chamber of
Commerce meeting.
11. Appointments.
None:
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) and
personnel matters, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Board
Appointments, Section 2(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Greenough, Powell, and Presser.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury. The Council convened into Executive
Session at 8:11 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:35 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:36 p.m.
