                     Recording Secretary
September 3, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 17, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 3, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees Hugh
Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee Barbara Aquilino. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, and ten persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   September 10, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   September 17, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 13, 2002 Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   August 20, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino arrived at 7:40 p.m.
6. Public Comment and Questions (Part I).
Due to special circumstances, President Duhl recognized Kimberly Brya, 1097
Cherry St., out of order from the published Agenda. Ms. Brya read a letter
proposing revisions to construction codes and regulations pertaining thereto
based upon her negative experiences with a neighboring construction project.
Manager Williams noted that some of Ms. Brya’s suggestions were already under
staff consideration and will probably be brought before the Council in the
fall.
Trustee Brower asked about the Village’s role in repairing damage done to a
neighbor’s property as a result of construction. Manager Williams replied that
such a matter is a civil one, and therefore, the Village does not get involved.
President Duhl thanked Ms. Brya for her suggestions, saying that staff would
review them and advise the Council on their merit.
7. Preservation Award: Oakley Monument.
Carter Page, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Committee, and Trustee
Greenough presented a Preservation Award to the neighbors of the Oakley
Monument for the part they played seeing it faithfully restored.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-26-2002 – Zoning Variation: Sts. Faith, Hope & Charity,
180 Ridge - Adoption. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser moved to
adopt the ordinance granting Front and Corner Yard Setbacks to allow
replacement of existing play equipment at Saints Faith, Hope and Charity
School. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None..
b)   Ordinance No. M-25-2002 – Zoning Variation: 165 Westview - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed amendments made to the draft ordinance in response to
comments at introduction.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to amend the draft ordinance
as outlined by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, granting a variation for Maximum Building Size. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-27-2002 – Tower Road Jurisdictional Transfer. –
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that this ordinance would authorize
the Village to take over jurisdiction of the section of Tower Road from Vernon
Avenue to the western limits of the Village from IDOT. In return for the
Village’s agreement to do so, IDOT agrees to participate in the restoration/
improvement of the pavement in the amount of approximately $200,000. He
explained that this benefits the village in two ways. First, the Village will
be able to proceed with the project on a timely basis, with financial
participation from IDOT. Secondly, by assuming jurisdiction of this section of
roadway, the Village assumes local control of roadway operations that will
facilitate the Village’s efforts to control traffic volumes and speeds. The
drawback is that the Village will forever be financially and legally
responsible for this section of roadway.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the ordinance
authorizing the transfer of jurisdiction. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to authorize the Village
President to sign the IDOT Jurisdictional Agreement. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2002 – Liquor Ordinance Amendments – Introduction.
Attorney Janega reviewed the ordinance drafted at Council’s direction after its
discussion at the August Study Session regarding the service of alcohol without
the service of a full meal. She explained the key provisions of the proposed
ordinance, including the following:
   i)   All restaurants and Song O’ Sixpence would be allowed to serve liquor
without the service of a meal. At drafting, no provision was made for Corner
Cooks.
   ii)   Liquor-only service would not be allowed on public sidewalks.
   iii)   Liquor-only service would be allowed for outdoor seating located on
private property.
   iv)   Prior to 9 p.m., only 20% of the seating capacity would be used for
alcohol service only and 80% would be dedicated to meal service. The alcohol-
only service area would be segregated from the remaining portion of restaurant.
   v)   After 9 p.m. alcohol-only service would occur anywhere in the
restaurant, including the outdoor seating area if located on private property.
   vi)   A meal could be served at the bar.
   vii)   Alcohol service would be allowed up to 11 p.m. The last sale would be
conducted with enough time to complete consumption prior to closing (15 or 30
minutes).
   viii)   The primary business of the restaurant as a whole would be the
service of food and not alcohol. This change would redefine “incidental and
complimentary” to be applied to the whole business and not on a per customer
basis.
   ix)   The ordinance would contain a sunset provision where these changes
would no longer be effective after one year. After a year, the Village Council
would assess the consequences of these changes.
Ms. Janega also noted some proposed non-substantive and structural changes.
President Duhl addressed the question of Corner Cooks, whose business is highly
specialized and does not clearly fit the definition of a restaurant.
After a discussion between the Trustees and Brent Shafran, representing Corner
Cooks, it was decided that Corner Cooks could apply for an A-1 liquor license
in order to qualify under the proposed new ordinance.
Trustee Brower expressed some concerns about (1) the licensing process, (2) the
absence of a requirement that the area of permitted service is not segregated,
and (3) the hours of operation.
President Duhl suggested that the Council wait to hear what the residents have
to say at the scheduled Public Hearing.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, asked about the process for advertising the Public
Hearing, saying that he feels it is very important that all of the community
have the opportunity to participate.
Trustee Powell asked whether the Council could repeal the ordinance if it
caused a problem. Ms. Janega said that they could.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-11-2002 – Simplified Telecommunications Tax –
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that the proposed ordinance would
bring the Village’s local telecommunications tax ordinance into compliance with
new state law. Pursuant to this ordinance, the existing 5% Municipal
Telecommunications Tax and the 1% Telecommunications Infrastructure Maintenance
Fee will be combined into a single 6% tax to be collected by the state and then
distributed to the Village.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2002 – Setting Speed Limits on Hibbard Road & Tower
Road – Introduction. Manager Williams reported that according to Village Code,
speed limits in the Village are automatically set at 30 mph, unless specified
differently by ordinance. He reported that now that the Village has taken over
both roadways, the Village’s Traffic Engineer recommended setting the speed
limit on both Tower and Hibbard Roads at 25 mph, which is the slowest speed
allowed by state law. Noting that traffic enforcement is challenging in the
face of current judicial practices, he indicated that the Village’s interest in
keeping down the speeds on these two roads would be best served by enacting a
reduced speed limit.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., spoke in support of the existing 30 mph speed limit
and urged more rigid enforcement of that speed limit.
Trustees Aquilino and Powell expressed some reservation about lowering the
posted speed limit.
President Duhl asked the Village Manager to slate this issue for an upcoming
study session.
g)   Resolution No. R-27-2002 – Appointment of IMRF Authorized Agent –
Adoption. Manager Williams explained that with the impending retirement of
Gayle Jones, Human Resources Manager, it is necessary to appoint a new IMRF
Authorized Agent. He recommended that the new Benefits Coordinator, Faranza
Ishaq, be so appointed.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the resolution
appointing Faranza Ishaq as the new IMRF Authorized Agent. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Old Business.
a)   Green Bay Road Guardrail Painting. Manager Williams reported that after
reviewing several options for enhancing the appearance of the guardrail along
the east side of Green Bay between Spruce and Eldorado Streets, it was decided
that painting would be the most efficient and economically feasible. He
informed the Trustees that staff recommends that the job be awarded to White
Sands to blast, prime, and paint the guardrail for an amount not to exceed
$18,000. He noted that plans for replanting the area were still under
discussion.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to authorize White Sands
to paint the guardrail as outlined. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and
Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1283 and 1284. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1283 in the amount of $1,248873.09 and
Warrant List No. 1284 in the amount of $374,120.16. Trustee Presser seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Pad Mounted Transformer Bids. Manager Williams reviewed a request from the
Water & Electric Department for Council’s approval of an extension of the two
purchase orders for three-phase and single-phase pad mounted transformers, at
the same prices as provided in last year’s bids, until July 31, 2003. He noted,
however, that there has been an increase in the estimated expenditure to
provide transformers for resulting from an increase in customer initiated
requests for new service.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Bid No. 02-012: Pumping Station Improvements. Manager Williams reported on
bids to furnish labor, equipment, and materials to install improvements at the
Willow Road Reservoir Pumping Station. He reported that staff recommends that
the bid be awarded to Mechanical, Inc., in the total amount of $56,768.00.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
11. Public Comment and Questions (Part II).
Mollybelle addressed the speed of traffic southbound on Hibbard Road. She also
read a letter from Mr. William Alfini commenting on her letter to the Winnetka
Talk regarding the need for a sidewalk on Pine Street.
12. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams commented on the statistics showing increased
usage of the Village’s web site.
13. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Mary Lambert to fill an existing
vacancy on the Plan Commission and John Weber to the Design Review Board to
replace Carolyn Casey upon expiration of her term, both for full terms.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the appointment
of Mary Lambert to the Plan Commission. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the appointment
of John Weber to the Design Review Board. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
14. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) and
personnel matters, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Board
Appointments, Section 2(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and
Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 9:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:35 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Trustee
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and
Village Attorney Katherine Janega
15. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:36 p.m.
