                     Recording Secretary
September 10, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: October 15, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and
26 persons in the audience.
President Duhl announced that because of the number of people in the audience
interested in the Drainage discussion, that agenda item would be covered first.
2. Strategic View: Drainage.
Manager Williams presented opening remarks, noting that the Village Engineer
Steve Saunders was out of town on vacation and, therefore, technical discussion
would have to be deferred to a later meeting. He referred the Trustees to Mr.
Saunders’ Agenda Report, Flooding Resulting from Recent Severe Rain Events,
copies of which were available for the audience.
Mr. Williams explained that the Village has two separate sewer systems, one for
storm water and one for sanitary waste and explained how each works. He noted
that during heavy rain events, storm water enters the sanitary system via
“infiltration” and “inflow” that can cause sewage to back up in basements. He
acknowledged that the Village has experienced an unusual number of such
basement backup problems during the last four major rain events. Consequently,
the Village plans to initiate an infiltration and inflow study to determine
where the water is entering the sanitary sewer system and do a flow monitoring
and analysis to evaluate the capacity of the Metropolitan Water Reclamation
District’s interceptors. These studies should help the Village determine what
is occurring, where it is occurring, and hopefully, why, so that the problem(s)
can be addressed directly.
In his comments on the storm sewer system, Mr. Williams pointed out two key
factors that hamper Winnetka, particularly the west end of the village: (1) the
land is low and flat forcing storm sewers to be very shallow and drain to
waterways whose elevations are only a few feet below the general ground
elevation; and (2) there are no storm water detention areas in the Village. He
noted that the Village has twice attempted to develop storm water detention
areas in Crow Island Park and Duke Childs Field, but both initiatives failed to
receive sufficient public support.
Mr.Williams also emphasized that although steady progress has been made in
drainage improvements over the last ten years, with regard to any and all
future storm water improvements, they will not solve all drainage problems. It
is not possible to design and construct improvements that are invincible to an
overwhelming rain event.
Mr. Williams reviewed the Village’s drainage projects in the Five Year Capital
Plan, including working with the Park District to alleviate some of the
problems in the West Hibbard Road area, and commented on additional possible
actions that the Village might pursue. In closing he noted that drainage
improvements are very expensive and that funding is limited, which may mean
that residents will have to make some difficult choices with regard to future
capital improvements throughout the Village.
Mr. Williams then responded to numerous questions from audience members
regarding specific incidents and general operational procedures.
There was a general consensus among the Trustees and the audience that sewage
presents a more pressing issue than rainwater. There was also agreement that
the Village needs to have a better understanding of how the MWRD operations
affect the Village’s sanitary sewer system.
President Duhl pointed out that both the MWRD and the Park District are
separate and independent governmental units that are outside the control of the
Village and reminded everyone that each unit of government, including the
Village, is also subject to applicable state and federal regulations. He also
pointed out that the Village cannot arbitrarily raise taxes to cover expenses
associated with drainage improvements. He noted that Village-wide there are
competing interests and competing capital projects. He said that the Village
will make this issue one of its highest priorities within given physical and
financial constraints; there is only so much staff time and money.
2. Natural Gas Tax Option.
Mr. McKee reviewed the Village’s overall financial position. He said that three
factors are significantly impacting the budget: (1) declining State shared
revenues, (2) mandates pension costs, and (3) a high level of annual investment
in capital expenditures. He explained that softening revenues, coupled with
lower interest rates, indicate that in the near future the Village will be
required to trim its capital investments in order to balance the budget.
Given the current financial picture and what is expected for the 2003/2004
budget, one revenue enhancement that could be easily adopted by the Village
would be a natural gas tax similar to that currently in effect in Wilmette,
Northfield, Glencoe, Glenview and other area communities. This is a gross
receipts tax that is charged to customers by their natural gas supplier and
remitted to the Village monthly. Mr. McKee indicated that the Village could
expect to generate about $250,000 per year from such a tax. He emphasized that
this would not be new revenue, but would replace revenues lost or diminished
from other sources.
There was a general consensus among the Trustees to proceed with implementing
this tax.
3. Zoning Issues.
Due to the lateness of the hour and the importance of these issues, discussion
was postponed to later meetings.
4. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:45 p.m.
September 17, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 1, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 17, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Distribution Engineer
Rich Ciesla and 18 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   October 8, 2002 Study Session. President Duhl indicated he would not be
present at this meeting.
b)   October 15, 2002 Regular Council Meeting: President Duhl indicated he
would not be present at this meeting.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 3, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Woodbury noted a
correction on Page 5 of the minutes. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as amended. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2002 – Liquor Ordinance Amendments – Public Hearing &
Adoption. President Duhl opened the Public Hearing and called for comments from
the audience. There being no comments from those persons in attendance,
President Duhl closed the public hearing at 7:35 p.m.
President Duhl asked for discussion on issues remaining from the last meeting,
noting that there are two policy issues to be discussed: (1) whether the entire
restaurant should be available for liquor-only service after 9:00 p.m.; and (2)
whether the current hours of liquor service, 11:00 a.m. to 11:00 p.m. should be
changed to 11:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m. and 5:00 p.m. to 11:00 p.m.
Mr. and Mrs. Pomerantz, 425 Birch, and Mr. Phil Hosa, 801 Oak Street spoke in
favor of liquor-only service while restaurants are open.
Mr. David Webster, 596 Arbor Vitae Road, expressed his opinion that if the
restaurant operation is downplayed and the bar aspect enhanced, the industry
may find this hard to resist. He said he believes this creates an opportunity
for business owners to start new bar businesses, rather than enhance the
restaurant business. He respectfully suggested that, the ultimate issue is
whether anyone would want the law changed to allow a bar to operate next to
their home. He urged the Council to limit any change only to existing
restaurants and to limit the time period.
After considering the comments made by the persons in the audience and upon
further discussion, the Trustees agreed that the hours of service should remain
11:00 a.m. to 11:00 p.m. with food available during those times.
President Duhl asked Attorney Janega to describe the safeguards written into
the ordinance.
Ms. Janega explained that the definition of restaurant had not been changed, so
complete meals must still be served. She further explained that the rules and
regulations of the local liquor commissioner impose requirements for
recordkeeping that can be used as a means to audit whether the service is
primarily meals with incidental liquor service. She said the next key provision
pertains to hours of business which, pursuant to the discussion in this
meeting, is 11:00 a.m. to 11:00 p.m., 7 days a week for Class A licensees.
Ms. Janega said new Section 5.09.209 governs the service of liquor without the
service of full meals and, pursuant to the discussion at this meeting, will be
limited to 20% of the restaurant’s seating without limiting hours of service.
She explained that, because the definition of restaurant requires incidental
service of liquor, it is not possible for a restaurant owner to only serve
liquor and disregard meal service. She said that enforcement is based primarily
on information from customers and the proprietors themselves.
Ms. Janega noted that the Class A2 liquor license has been repealed, reflecting
comments and concerns on behalf of Corner Cooks, which holds the only A2
license, but has evolved to function more as a restaurant and so has been
classified as A-1.
Ms. Janega explained that the proposed ordinance requires everyone who wants
liquor-only service to designate the area where this would occur and provide a
seating plan, which must be approved by the Village President. The ordinance
also imposes further restrictions by permitting outdoor service of liquor
without a meal only in seating areas that are on private property.
Trustee Presser moved to amend the language in paragraph D of Section 5.09.205,
by deleting “prior to 9:00 p.m.” and to delete paragraph E; seconded by Trustee
Powell. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent:
None.
President Duhl asked for the Trustees’ comments about the “last call”
provision.
Trustee Aquilino stated she thought this provision could be eliminated. Trustee
Greenough indicated she thought the provision unnecessary.
Trustee Aquilino moved to delete Paragraph 5 in Section 5.09.250, seconded by
Trustee Greenough. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell and Greenough. Nayes:
Trustees Presser, Brower, Woodbury, President Duhl. Absent: None. By roll call
vote, the motion was denied.
Attorney Janega referred to the language proposed for E-1 licensees in
paragraph J(1) of Section 5.09.100 of the ordinance which requires a minimum
size for that type of establishment and table seating for a minimum number of
customers. She asked what requirements the Council would impose.
Manager Williams recommended that the Council reserve this issue for the next
meeting.
Trustee Powell then moved to adopt the ordinance, reserving the amendments to
Paragraph J(1) of Section 5.09.100. The motion was seconded by Trustee Presser.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-27-2002 – Tower Road Jurisdictional Transfer. – Adoption.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance adding
Tower Road to the municipal street system. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-11-2002 – Simplified Telecommunications Tax – Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
making Winnetka’s local ordinance consistent with new state law. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-28-2002 – Disposal of Surplus Vehicles - Introduction. Mr.
Williams described the 6 vehicles to be disposed of at the Northwest Municipal
Conference auction in October.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)    Ordinance No. M-29-2002 – Zoning Variation: 500 Maple - Introduction.
Trustee Greenough noted that she is the owner of this property and will be
recusing herself. She then asked that this agenda item be heard after M-30-2002
to allow her architect time to arrive.
f)    Ordinance No. M-30-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1310 Scott - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for variation from the gross floor area
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of a mudroom
addition at the rear of the residence that exceeds the permitted square
footage. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had considered the
request and voted unanimously in favor of granting the variation. He explained
that when the home was originally built, it conformed to the GFA requirement,
but due to changes in the zoning ordinance, 1,553.71 square feet of the
basement area now counts towards the allowable GFA.
After hearing comments from the architect regarding the proposed construction,
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Woodbury voted against introduction.
g)   Ordinance No. M-29-2002 – Zoning Variation: 500 Maple - Introduction.
Trustee Greenough recused herself from the discussion and vote on this matter,
again noting that she is the property owner.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for variation to permit the construction
of a detached garage with a corner yard setback that is less than the minimum
requirement of 30 feet. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals
recommended approval of the variations. He added that the Landmark Preservation
Committee determined that the detached garage is an appropriate alteration to
the landmark and had also recommended approval.
Mr. Ken Behles, the architect, explained that the design of the garage sets it
into the hill on the property so that it is less visible to the street. He said
that the runoff would now go to the south of the property and the garage is set
further away from neighboring properties. Because the lot is a corner lot, and
has two front setbacks, conforming locations for the new garage are reduced. He
said the design will save all of the trees on the property, reduce bulk and
height, reduce the slope of the driveway and will not impair light and air.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Having recused herself, Trustee
Greenough did not vote.
h)    Ordinance No. M-31-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1274 Tower Rd. –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for variation from the
front setback requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction
of a one-story family room addition. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals voted to recommend granting the variation with one dissenting vote from
a Board member who felt the home already had sufficient space.
Mr. Gillespie, Roberts Architects, explained that the addition would be behind
a 6-foot high concrete wall along Hibbard Road and that only the top 3.5 feet
would be visible above the wall. He said that it could not be placed in a
conforming location behind the house because it would reduce the water
retention on the property and cause problems for neighboring property owners.
Mr. Robert Kates, 1326 Tower Road, interjected with a question about the proper
notice for this property, stating that his house is two houses from this home
and he was not notified of the variation request.
Mr. D’Onofrio referred to the agenda materials and stated that it appears Mr.
Kates’s home is more than 250 feet away from 1274 Tower Road.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
i)    Ordinance No. M-32-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1230 Westmoor. –
Introduction.: Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application for variations previously
requested in May, 2001 and granted by the Council. He reported that the
petitioners had not acted upon their request and the 12-month period for the
commencement of construction had expired. They are requesting a variation from
the setback requirements and front facing garage door requirements of the
zoning ordinance. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to
introduce the ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury asked if the application requests the same variations as in
2001.
Mr. D’Onofrio replied that they are the same.
Trustee Woodbury moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Presser.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1285 and 1286. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1285 in the amount of $694,293.94 and Warrant
List No. 1286 in the amount of $894,178.07. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
Mr. Kates , 1326 Tower, commented on the installation of electric poles vs.
pedestals. He said he thought the purpose of the pole analysis was to benefit
the area and expressed strong disagreement with the administration of the
policy adopted by the Council. He said that his neighbor, Mrs. Ziegler, who is
elderly and a long-time resident received a letter regarding options for
electrical service on her property. He said Mrs. Ziegler chose the pole option
and, although she received a schematic from the Village, she did not understand
that it was the exact place the pole would go. He said that the pole was placed
5 feet away from the house in the center of the property and blocking one of
the main windows in the house. Mrs. Ziegler was told by the Water and Electric
Department that there was no flexibility in the location of the pole and
because of this, she has now paid $1000 to place the service line underground.
Mr. Kates believes that other neighbors won’t consider the pole option because
of the placement of the poles and that the Village is using this policy to get
people to underground their electricity. He said that he doesn’t think this is
what the Council intended and that the electric department needs guidance from
the Council, so that placement of the poles can be in a way that is aesthetic
for the house as well as the neighborhood.
President Duhl said that he remembers at the time the Council adopted the
ordinance they talked about aesthetics. He said that poles were to be as close
to the weather-head as possible and as unobtrusive as possible. He is
personally concerned that the neighbor who elects a pedestal won’t be adversely
affected by a neighbor who wants a pole. He said that he remembers directly
that it be as close to the weatherhead as possible and as aesthetic to the
neighbors as possible.
Manager Williams asked Mr. McInturff to explain the situation for this
particular house.
Mr. McInturff stated that the weatherhead is in the middle of this house, so
the pole was placed there. He said the ordinance says it is to be as close to
the weatherhead as possible so that, should the house convert to underground,
the conduit and wire that is already installed going up the pole could be
installed at the pedestal without having to run new wire. He said that, in this
case, the pole wound up being in the middle of the yard. Mr. McInturff said he
understood the guidelines set by the Council are that the pole be located as
near to the weatherhead as practicable.
Manager Williams said Mr. Kates has asked to set the pole a little closer to
the lot line. The staff is under the impression that the guidance from the
Council is to set the pole as near to the weatherhead as practicable and is
therefore asking for clarification.
Trustee Presser said that it seems that the procedures that were set down by
the Council were followed and it has been the policy of the Board to encourage
undergrounding. He said it sounds to him that the Electric Department is doing
exactly what the Trustees had directed them to do.
Mr. Kates replied that the Council is discouraging the alternative that
residents want, i.e., the poles, and that the Village could work with the
residents to place the poles aesthetically so that the policy is flexible
enough to benefit everybody.
President Duhl said that the Council will reconsider the policy for the
placement of the poles.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported that he and Trustee Aquilino attended a
meeting of the Chamber of Commerce last Tuesday and they are concentrating on
“Celebrate Winnetka”, which will go on for the next week or so.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of John McLinden for the Chairmanship
of the Design Review Board upon the expiration of Jon Meyer’s term in October.
Trustee Woodbury moved to approve the appointment. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:45 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Presser. Absent:
Trustee Woodbury. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:46 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Deputy Clerk
October 1, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 15, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 1, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees Hugh
Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee Barbara Aquilino. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and 19 persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   October 8, 2002 Study Session. President Duhl indicated he would not be
present at this meeting. All of the Trustees indicated that they expected to
attend.
b)   October 15, 2002 Regular Council Meeting: President Duhl indicated he
would not be present at this meeting. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 17, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt
the minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-28-2002 – Disposal of Surplus Vehicles - Adoption. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance-permitting
disposal of six vehicles at the Northwest Municipal Conference auction. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
b)   Ordinance No. M-29-2002 – Zoning Variation: 500 Maple - Adoption. Trustee
Greenough noted that as owner of the property she would recuse herself from
voting on this issue. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to
adopt the ordinance granting zoning variations to permit construction of a
detached garage. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
c)   Ordinance No. M-30-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1310 Scott - Adoption. Trustee
Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance granting a
zoning variation for maximum building size to permit construction of a mudroom
addition. A roll call vote on the motion did not produce the required majority.
Ayes: Trustees Presser, Powell, and Greenough. Nays: Trustees Woodbury and
Brower. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
President Duhl called for a motion to table the issue. Trustee Woodbury so
moved. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-31-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1274 Tower Rd. – Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a variation from the front setback requirements of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit the construction of a one-story family room addition. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
e)   Ordinance No. M-32-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1230 Westmoor. – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting variations to permit construction of a one and two-story addition and
open porch. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
f)   Ordinance No. M-33-2002 – Landmark Designation: 902 Greenwood -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the landmark Preservation Commission
had recommended that this property be designated a Winnetka Landmark based upon
an overall score on the System for Evaluation of Landmarks of 86 points,
resulting in a “unique” rating.
The owner, Julie Windsor, addressed the original design and materials, and
Trustee Greenough commented on the quality of the restoration work.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance designating the property as a local landmark. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
g)   Ordinance No. M-34-2002 – Special Use Permit: Historical Society and Park
District - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a joint request for a Special
Use Permit from the Winnetka Historical Society and the Winnetka Park District
to permit the relocation of the Schmidt Burnham Log House to a site at Crow
Island Park. It is proposed that the site of the log house will be owned by the
Park District, with the Historical Society entering into the land lease with
the Park District for the site. He reported that both the Plan Commission and
the Zoning Board of Appeals had considered the request for the Special Use
Permit and recommended its approval.
Becky Hurley, 1027 Spruce, a member of the Historical Society’s Board and
attorney provided a brief overview of the issue.
The Society’s architect, Ken Behles, 426 Maple reviewed the proposed siting and
associated issues and responded to questions from the Trustees.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director commented on the siting process and the
cooperation between the Park District and the Historical Society.
Attorney Janega reviewed the ordinance noting that the recitals as drafted are
based upon the findings of the Plan Commission and the Zoning Board of Appeals
and will be amended to reflect tonight’s presentation, including the status of
ownership.
Trustee Brower inquired as to the Village’s participation in costs associated
with the move. Manager Williams replied that most of the Village’s expenses
will be indirect, i.e., adjusting electric and utility lines, personnel,
traffic signal adjustments.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
a)   Spruce Street Sidewalk. In introductory comments Manager Williams noted
that there are two issues under discussion: (1) whether to construct a
sidewalk, and (2) whether to install a stop sign at Berkeley and Spruce.
Mr. Saunders reviewed collected traffic statistics and their collection
methods. He concluded that the traffic and speed counts do not meet the
necessary warrants for a four-way stop at Spruce and Berkeley. In reply to a
direct question from President Duhl, Mr. Saunders stated that his opinion as
the Village’s Traffic Engineer was that no stop sign should be installed at
that intersection.
Jan McVicker, 1161 Spruce, reported on a neighborhood meeting held to discuss
the issues. She indicated that upon learning the results of Mr. Saunders’s
traffic study, the neighborhood as a whole had abandoned the idea of a stop
sign at Berkeley. However, she noted that they are now in favor of a stop sign
at Spruce and Glendale. She also reported that the neighborhood was largely
opposed to the installation of a sidewalk from Berkeley to Hibbard, with most
people being neutral about a sidewalk along the park, pending a report from the
Village’s Forester on the impact on the trees.
Bill Ryan, 1174 Spruce, spoke in support of a sidewalk on the grounds of
safety. He stated his belief that sidewalks are a Village-wide, not a
neighborhood, issue and requested that the Council follow the guidelines
established by the Plan Commission in the Comprehensive Plan.
James Cavanaugh, 1210 Spruce, agreed with the summary given by Jan McVicker and
asked that the Council respect the clear majority of the neighbors with regard
to a sidewalk from Berkeley to Hibbard. He professed some support for a
sidewalk through the park.
Steve Sullivan, 1186 Spruce, also spoke in favor of the sidewalk through the
park. However, he expressed concern about possible drainage ramifications, and
suggested stronger enforcement of existing speed and parking restrictions.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, indicated that before any sidewalk could
be installed through the park, a thorough analysis of the impact on the trees
would have to be done. However, barring any significant impact, the Park
District would not impede the process, but expressed a preference for a
sidewalk adjacent to the curb.
Mr. Saunders reported that in the Village Forester’s opinion most of the large
oak trees would not be negatively affected. He also commented on the request
for a four-way stop at Glendale and Spruce, saying that he did not expect that
the necessary traffic warrants would be met.
President Duhl remarked that since no sidewalk could be installed until spring,
there is sufficient time to study whether it would affect drainage. He then
polled the Trustees for their thoughts.
Trustee Presser said that given the absence of a strong majority, he would vote
against both the sidewalk and the stop sign at this time.
Trustee Powell concurred, saying that the issue requires further study.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that he is generally in favor of sidewalks and would
support one through the park, but that anything else should be revisited when
and if a curb is installed.
Trustee Greenough also agreed with Trustee Presser.
Trustee Brower indicated for a support the sidewalk the entire length, but
perhaps not at this time.
There being no support from the Council for a sidewalk either all the way
through from Berkeley to Hibbard or just through the Park, President Duhl said
that given Council’s historical consideration for neighborhood sentiment, it
would not impose a sidewalk at this time. However, in view of the considerable
concern expressed about the possible impact on drainage, the Village would
undertake a study of the potential impact a sidewalk might have.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1287 and 1288. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1287 in the amount of $1,162,306.93 and
Warrant List No. 1288 in the amount of $610,977.57. Trustee Powell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
b)   Quarterly Capital Improvement Reports. Manager Williams reported that
capital improvements are on track for this year, and asked Mr. Saunders to
briefly review the status of the Village Yards Project. Mr. Saunders did so
indicating that it is hoped that the project will be completed by November of
next year.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln. requested that her letter regarding 500 Maple
be entered into the record.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Powell reported that the NWMC legislative committee
meetings have resumed from summer recess and their initiatives for the coming
year.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Greenough moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Aquilino. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:42 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:16 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
The meeting adjourned at 11:17 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Recording Secretary
October 8, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: January 7, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Aquilino called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: President Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, and 25 persons in the audience.
2. West Hibbard Road Area Drainage Study.
President Pro Tem Aquilino explained to the audience that the evening’s
discussion would be focused on the drainage report and presentation prepared by
the Village’s consultant, Stanley Consultants, outlining the causes of flooding
and technical recommendations to address the flooding. She pointed out that the
report brings to light a number of broad policy questions that need to be
addressed by the Council before a specific course of action can be formulated,
i.e., how and when to fund, plan, and construct the recommended improvements.
Ms. Aquilino mentioned that the Village would be scheduling a series of more
in-depth meetings with affected neighbors to discuss details of proposed
improvements to specific areas.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village had contracted with Stanley Consultants
last spring to prepare a drainage study to investigate causes and develop
potential remediation projects for flooding problems in the area west of
Hibbard Road and south of Tower Road. He briefly reviewed the development
history and topography of the area and the problems inherent thereto. He
indicated that the consultants had been asked to survey the existing drainage
system, identify sections of the system that are inadequate, and provide
recommendations to provide protection from a 5-year frequency storm event.
Mr. Saunders reported that that the consultants had recommended a broad program
of improvements, including sewer and pump improvements on the golf course, an
improved drainage line along the east edge of the golf course, and various
drainage improvements in the residential neighborhoods east of the golf course.
He summarized as improvement to the residential drainage system so that water
reaches the golf course more effectively and improvement of the golf course
drainage system to manage the water more effectively, thereby improving
playability. He pointed out, however, that regardless of what improvements are
made, they will not completely eliminate flooding in the area.
Mr. Saunders introduce Melissa Kehoe and Reza Khakpour of Stanley Consultants
who reviewed their study methodology, findings and recommendations.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega arrived at 8:10 p.m.
After their presentation, Manager Williams noted that the two priorities are
upgradingthe storm sewer system between the ponds and the pump system.
Trustee Woodbury identified three separate problems: (1) flooding issues in
homes; (2) flooding of public ways impacting the ability of people/vehicles to
get in/out; and (3) yard flooding. He asked what the expected consequence would
be if the service channels from the neighboring streets are not rebuilt. Mr.
Khakpour responded that there would be little improvement to backyard flooding
because water cannot get to the golf course. He also mentioned that there is no
alternative that will not require significant construction that will
necessarily have an impact landscaping and trees.
Noting that the Village’s storm sewer system intermingles with the Park
District’s, Mr. Saunders reviewed recent preliminary discussions with the Park
District regarding cost sharing.
Mr. Williams commented that the Village hopes to complete the engineering on
the proposed new sewer line on the east side of the golf course and the new
pump to complete the backbone of the proposed improvements this winter in the
hope of getting it in by next year. He noted that there is presently enough
capital in the budget to cover this cost, however, given the enormity of the
entire project and its extremely high cost, the Village trustees will have to
make some tough choices with regard to the next steps.
Trustee Brower noted that individual residents and neighborhoods would also
have to make some difficult choices.
The Trustees then listened to comments from audience members including: Tom
Harney, 1334 Trapp; Joseph Rosengarten, 1360 Westmoor Trail; Nancy Kopelman,
1385 Trapp; Carole Smith, Sunview Ln.; Kurt Oelerich, 128 Sun view Lne.; Arthur
Braun, 850 Bell Ln.; Stephen Brown, 1338 Hackberry Ln.; Tom Barron, 1284 Trapp
Ln.
3. Tree Regulations.
Attorney Janega discussed a provision of the Village Code pertaining to the
interpretation and application of a section that raises both legal and policy
concerns arising between neighboring property owners when trees located near
property lines mature and extend over those property lines. Unless otherwise
specifically authorized in the Code, this provision makes it unlawful for any
person to remove, cut, injure or destroy, without the consent of the owner, any
tree, shrub, vine or flower located upon the private premises of any person
within the Village.
Ms. Janega explained that this provision has been interpreted and applied more
as a statement of private property rights rather than as a regulatory
provision. However, with increasing frequency, this interpretation is being
questioned by residents when neighboring property owners are unable to agree.
Historically, the Village has taken the position that such disputes are solely
the concern of the owners and has not required one property owner to obtain a
Village permit before trimming overhanging branches.
Although the Village Forester tries to mediate disputes between property
owners, sometimes the police are asked to intervene, i.e., when one property
owner, without the tree owner’s consent, either intrudes directly onto the tree
owner’s property to trim a tree or trims the canopy so extensively that the
shape of the tree is altered. In some instances, the tree owner then complains
to the Village that the change to the tree has a negative impact on either
their property value and/or the overall health and wellbeing of the tree.
Ms. Janega noted that since private property owners enjoy common law rights
against damage to property, and since criminal sanctions also exist, this
provision of the Code does little more than provide a vehicle for interjecting
the Village into disputes between neighbors. She recommended its deletion from
the Village Code.
She reported that the although the Code Review Committee agreed with this
recommendation if the Village did not perceive it as a regulatory provisions,
the Committee expressed a somewhat conflicting desire that the Village provide
the same protections for privately owned trees that it affords publicly owned
trees. She recommended that if the Trustees want to maintain the status quo,
the language should be clarified to reflect that one neighbor is not required
to get permission from another. On the other hand, if the Village wishes to
establish a process or permit requirement, then that needs to be specified.
Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce Street, strongly encouraged the Council to retain the
existing provision and to make a firm policy decision to permanently enforce
it. He cited personal experience in support of his position.
Anne Hecht, 1096 Spruce Street, commented on a proposed change to the Village
Code that would add theEuropean Buckthorn tree to a list of those that are
“unlawful to plant, place or maintain anywhere in the Village.”
After some dialogue about both issues, Manager Williams suggested that they be
returned to the Forestry Commission for further recommendation and
clarification and reslated for Council’s consideration sometime during the
winter.
4. Levee Study Update.
Due to the lateness of the hour, this discussion of this item was postponed
until a later date.
5. Outline for Possible Residential Design Review.
Due to the lateness of the hour, this discussion of this item was postponed
until a later date.
6. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Aquilino, Brower, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl. The Council convened
into Executive Session at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:12 p.m. Present: Trustees
Greenough, Aquilino, Powell, Brower, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent: President
Duhl. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
7. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Brower, Presser, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President
Duhl. None. The meeting adjourned at 11:13 p.m.
October 15, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 19, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 15, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Aquilino called the meeting to order at 7: 30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: President Michael Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and
eight persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Aquilino led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   November 5, 2002 Regular Council Meeting. All of the Trustees indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   November 12, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 10, 2002 Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   October 1, 2002 Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-30-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1310 Scott - Adoption. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to remove the issue from the
table. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
The applicants and their architect reiterated the reason for the request and
responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a zoning variation for maximum building size to permit construction of
a mudroom addition. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Powell, Greenough and Aquilino. Nays: Trustees Woodbury and Brower.
Absent: President Duhl.
b)   Ordinance No. M-33-2002 – Landmark Designation: 902 Greenwood - Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance
designating the property as a local landmark. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
c)   Ordinance No. M-34-2002 – Special Use Permit: Historical Society and Park
District - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed changes to the draft
document. Trustee Woodbury inquired as to the status of landscaping and
lighting plans. After a brief discussion of options with Ms Janega and Mr.
Norkus, he suggested that ordinance be approved with a directive to staff that
when these plans are finalized, they come back to the Council for review.
Trustee Brower asked whether the Historical Society intended to allow third
parties to sponsor events in the Log House. Ms. Hurley, the Society’s attorney,
indicated that their lease with the Park District prohibits successor
subleases. Ms. Janega indicated that language pertaining to limitations to
third parties could be inserted in the ordinance.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend the ordinance
pursuant to Ms. Janega’s review and in keeping with the evening’s discussion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to grant the Special Use
Permit to permit the relocation of the Schmidt Burnham Log House to a site at
Crow Island Park. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell, Aquilino, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays:
None. Absent: President Duhl.
7. Auditor’s Report.
a)   Finance Director Ed McKee introduced the Village’s Auditor, Irwin Lyons,
from Miller Cooper & Co., Ltd. Mr. Lyons commented on the Comprehensive Annual
Financial Report for the year ended March 31, 2002. He pointed out that the
Village had received a Government Finance Office Certificate of Excellence on
last year’s report and should receive that honor again this year. He also
mentioned that the independent auditor’s report gives the Village an
unqualified opinion, which is the highest level of assurance available.
Mr. Lyons then reviewed the Management Letter, which he explained is negative
by its nature, and responded to questions from the Trustees. He stated that
there are no major compliance issues and commented briefly on the status of the
Village’s various funds. Mr. Lyons mentioned that key areas to watch are the
Village’s liability insurance and health insurance funds and remarked that the
only item that could have financial significance is the potential landfill
liability. He suggested a periodic review and updating of this item, especially
in light of ever changing EPA requirements.
8. Old Business.
a)   WYO Payment Request. Manager Williams presented a brief overview of the
issue. He explained that the requested payment is for the uncompleted half of
the organization’s spring project for the Village. He reported that he and
Trustee Brower had met with WYO in order to identify alternative projects that
the organization might undertake in the future in lieu of clearing the railroad
banks, such as cleaning up the business districts.
Trustee Brower commented that it became clear during that meeting that there
had been an inherent conflict in terms of the understanding of what the
performance expectations were. In response to the concern expressed by Trustee
Presser that paying for services that were not provided might be construed as a
contribution, Trustee Brower indicated that he believed that WYO recognizes
that on a going-forward basis, this is not the case. He expressed his belief
that the organization understands that it needs to come to a working
arrangement with the Village whereby it receives the money allocated to it for
services performed.
Trustee Powell suggested that a package of chores that provide some flexibility
be developed.
Manager Williams responded that after the meeting with WYO, they agreed to get
back to the Village with a menu of possible services that the Council would
then have to approve.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to grant payment of $1,250
to the Winnetka Youth Organization with the clear understanding that this money
does not constitute a grant. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell Aquilino, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None.
Absent: President Duhl.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1289 and 1290. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1289 in the amount of $621,943.30 and Warrant
List No. 1290 in the amount of $660,856.19. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Aquilino, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
President Duhl.
b)   Community Signage Improvements. Mr. Norkus explained that in July the
Village had received suggestions from the Chamber of Commerce for improvement
of the retail climate of the Village’s commercial areas, along with suggested
priorities for their implementation. One of these suggestions was improved and
enhanced Village signage and improved “identity elements,” such as a community
events kiosk and decorative fabric banners.
Mr. Norkus reported that the Chamber had presented similar information to the
Design Review Board paying particular attention to the current community event
sign located on Ameritech property at Oak, Linden and Green Bay. The Board had
expressed support for upgrading the sign display area, along with the possible
development of an additional sign location in Hubbard Woods. They also
supported the Chamber’s suggestion of introducing fabric banners in the
downtown areas. The Board asked that designs be developed for review, pointing
out that design and implementation of such improvements should not be looked at
as individual projects, but rather, addressed in a comprehensive fashion with a
unified design theme. Preliminary discussions with one design firm indicate the
development of such a unified design theme could probably be completed for
approximately $15,000 to $20,000.
Finally, Mr. Norkus noted that several issues that are either directly related
to or tangential to the projects recommended by the Chamber are included as
recommendations in Appendix Six of the Comprehensive Plan.
Manager Williams commented that this would provide a valuable guide for staff
as the Design Guidelines do not go into this level of detail.
Mr. Woodbury suggested that the study be conducted with broad representation
from both the Design Review Board and Chamber of Commerce, with the DRB being
the body to review the guidelines before they come to the Council for final
approval.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to authorize staff to issue
a Request for Proposals to firms qualified to develop a set of design standards
for construction of entry signs, parking/regulatory signs, wayfinding elements,
banners, and community event kiosks. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Powell, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
c)   Water Plant Settling Basin Roof Contract. Mr. McInturff reviewed a
proposal to engage Riley Construction to serve as Construction Manager for the
project of installing a roof over the Water Plant. He explained that the
contract would be similar to the contract for the Village Yards Project except
for a few small changes, which he outlined.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to authorized the Village
President to sign a contract with Riley Construction for construction
management services for the installation of a roof over the settling basins at
the Water Plant. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Powell and Aquilino. Nays: None.
Absent: President Duhl.
d)   State Bid: Salt Purchase. Mr. Williams explained that the Village
purchases its rock salt through the Sate of Illinois bid. He said that the
estimate for the coming winter season is 1,500 tons, with the Village being
obligated to purchase at least 70% of the estimated amount. The successful
bidder for the State of Illinois contract is IMC/North American Salt Co., for a
price of $30.77 per ton.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the purchase of
salt from IMC/North American at $30.77 per ton. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
e)   Set Tax Levy. Mr. McKee explained that annually at this time, the Village
has to formally set the dollar amount of its tax levy. He reported that a
homeowner with a $10,000 tax bill would see an increase of about $26.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to set the 2002 Village-wide
property tax levy, net of abatements, at $9,876,081. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Presser, Powell, Aquilino,
Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
f)   Public Safety Building Refinancing. Mr. McKee explained that this is an
economical time to refinance bonds issued in 1996 for the Public Safety
Building.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to authorize staff to
proceed with the refinancing of the Public Safety Building bonds. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Greenough, Brower,
Presser, Powell, Aquilino, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
10. Public Comment and Questions.
Robin Thomas, 638 Elder Ln., raised a policy question about the Village’s
participating in paying for a repair to a deep, broken sewer line underneath
her property.
Manager Williams explained that by ordinance it is the Village’s policy that
the homeowner is responsible for maintenance of sewer lines up to where they
connect to the main sewer. He indicated that most communities have the same
policy, but that some have a cost sharing arrangement if the sewer line is
deep. He recommended that staff bring a report back to the Council at the next
meeting for discussion and policy direction.
11. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported on a recent meeting of the Chamber of
Commerce.
12. Appointments.
None.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Powell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: President Duhl. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:
22 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:20 p.m. Present: President
Pro Tem Aquilino, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Absent: President Duhl. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and
Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Woodbury, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: President
Duhl. The meeting adjourned at 10:21 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Recording Secretary
November 5, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 19, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 5, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Aquilino called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Stephen Presser. Absent:
Trustee Woodbury and President Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Director of Water
& Electric Bryan McInturff, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and 19 persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Aquilino led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   November 12, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
b)   November 19, 2002 Regular Council Meeting: All of the Trustees indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-44-2002 – Special Use Permit and Zoning Variation: 600
Hibbard Rd. - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request from the
Winnetka Park District for a Special Use Permit to permit the construction of a
one-story garage building at Skokie Playfield Service Center to replace
existing garages and a storage shed located on the west side of the Service
Center property. The Park District is also requesting a Building Height
variation to permit the garage to be 21 feet high. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that
the need for the special use permit is triggered by the fact that this would be
a public use in a residential area. He reports that the Design Review Board,
Zoning Board of Appeals, and the Plan Commission recommended approval of the
requests, although the DRB expressed a preference for a different roof shingle
color than that chosen by the Park District.
Trustee Brower indicated that he would abstain from voting on this matter since
the Park District does substantial business with Harris Bank.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, introduced Rich Schram, who designed the
building. Mr. Schram reviewed elevations with the Trustees. He explained that
the Park District prefers the cedar colored shingle because it matches an
existing neighboring building and is more heat resistant than the gray
weathered wood color suggested by the DRB. Ms. McElroy noted that the Park
District has no plans to reroof the existing building, so their two buildings
would have unmatched roofs for some time into the future.
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:45 p.m.
Kurt Oelerich, 1280 Sunview Ln., spoke in opposition to the proposed project
and presented a summary of his remarks and a supporting petition signed by
neighbors to the Trustees. Mr. Oelerich expressed his opinion that the site is
already overcrowded before any expansion that will infringe further on the
neighborhood. He voiced concerns about damage to neighbors’ property as well as
concerns about safety.
Mr. Oelerich acknowledged that the Park District needs updated facilities but
requested on behalf of the neighbors that they explore alternative sites with
an eye toward enhancing the community. He pointed out that the Sunview Lane
area has already been targeted as a drainage problem area and that the new
facility could contribute to that already existing problem. He made several
suggestions as to possible alternate sites and responded to questions from the
Trustees.
Trustee Powell inquired whether the neighbors’ primary concern would be
addressed if additional screening between the proposed facility and the
neighbors was required. Mr.Oelerich responded that screening was not the
principal issue; overcrowding is.
Ms. McElroy addressed some of the sites suggested by Mr. Oelerich. She noted
that every time the Park District looks to relocate anything, they end up at
Skokie Playfields because the neighborhood issues are so great elsewhere. She
explained that given that most of the activity and resources that come from the
service center take place at the playfield, it doesn’t seem efficient to locate
most of the equipment away from where it is most used.
In response to a suggestions from Trustees Presser and Woodbury, Ms. McElroy
agreed to revisit the issues with the neighbors as well as do an alternative
site study. She pointed out, however, that the Park District already has been
cited by the Illinois EPA because they lack a rinsing pad, and therefore, there
is some need to expedite the process.
In view of that need, Attorney Janega recommended that the Council introduce
the ordinance and then table approval until after the Park District completes
its alternative site analysis and discussions with the neighbors.
Therefore, Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   Ordinance No. M-35 - 2002 – Zoning Variation: 1447 Edgewood –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum Building Size and
Intensity of Use of Lot variations to permit construction of a two-story
addition. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had recommended approval
of the variation.
Andrew Venamore, representing the builder, appeared to explain the necessity
for the project.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. M-36-2002 – Zoning Variation: 205 Forest – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Rear Yard Setback variation to permit the
construction of a detached garage. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals
had recommended approval of the variation.
Healy Rice, architect for the applicant, appeared to explain the necessity for
the project.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-37-2002 – Zoning Variation: 675 Garland – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum Building Size and Intensity of Use
of Lot variations to permit the construction of a one-story addition. He
explained that the current structure exceeds the maximum allowable GFA and that
the proposed addition would cause the roofed lot coverage to exceed the maximum
allowable. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
recommended approval of the variation.
The applicant, Mr. McKeever, responded to questions from the Trustees. He noted
that there had been no objections from any of the immediate neighbors.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
e)   Ordinance No. M-38-2002 – Alley Consolidation: 334 Ridge – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request to consolidate an existing zoning lot into a
single lot of record pursuant to the requirements of the Village Code that
require that any new principal structure be built on a single lot of record.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2002 – Amending Village Code regarding Construction
Hours and building Permit Issuance - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed
proposed amendments to Chapter 15 of the Village Code to address several issues
related to construction and demolition. Among the proposed changes are limiting
construction hours on Sundays and holidays in residential districts as well as
a few changes to mitigate the negative impact of construction, i.e., noise and
congestion, on residential neighborhoods. Other proposed changes amend language
concerning the delay of issuance of building permits under certain
circumstances.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to
introduce the ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
g)   Ordinance No. MC-13-2002 – New Liquor Licensee – Cosi, 540 Lincoln -
Introduction. Manager Williams reviewed a request from Cosi, Inc. to amend the
number of liquor licenses to add a new restaurant with an A-1 liquor license at
540 Lincoln Ave.
Jennifer Gallery, attorney for Cosi, appeared to explain the applicant’s
concept and answer questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
h)   Ordinance No. MC-14-2002 – Amending Liquor Ordinance - Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that this is a housekeeping matter, to add specific
provisions for the minimum size and seating capacity of a limited food products
store authorized for the retail sale of beer or wine under the Class E-1 liquor
license classification.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
i)   Ordinance No. MC-15-2002 – Snow Emergency Parking Regulations -
Introduction.. Mr. Saunders reviewed proposed changes to the existing snow
emergency parking regulations that would enable the Police and Public Works
Departments to better serve the community during snow and ice control
operations. These changes would reduce man hours spent trying to locate vehicle
owners and help to ensure that snow is completely removed from Village streets.
Trustee Woodbury expressed some concern of the amount of the proposed increase
in the fine. Chief DeLopez commented that it renders an ordinance meaningless
if it carries a fine that people consider is insignificant. Manager Williams
indicated that staff would get back to the Council with additional information
on the fine.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7. Old Business.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1291 and 1292. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1291 in the amount of $1,062,842.05 and
Warrant List No. 1292 in the amount of $934,156.71. Trustee Greenough seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President
Duhl.
b)   Appeal of Forestry Commission Decision: 380 Locust. Mr. Saunders explained
that the property owner had submitted an application to the Village requesting
permission to remove two Elm trees of 44 and 33 inches in diameter located on
his property in order to accommodate compensatory storage required for the
applicant to be in compliance with floodplain requirements. He reported that
the Village Forester had determined that other suitable locations might exist
on the lot that could serve to provide the necessary compensatory storage, and
therefore, he denied the application. This decision was upheld upon appeal to
the Forestry Commission. Consequently, pursuant to Village Code, the applicant
is appealing the denial to the Village Council.
Mr. Saunders noted that the lot in question is located within the 100-year
floodplain, which means that the home the property owner has designed for the
property must comply with FEMA and Village regulations concerning minimum
elevation and compensatory storage. However, as designed, it is not possible to
do so, unless the compensatory storage is located in the area requiring the
removal of the Elm trees. He concluded by saying that both the Forestry
Commission and Village staff determined that locating the required compensatory
storage in the rear of the property is possible and that the two Elm trees are
of such value as to outweigh the property owner’s preferences for his chosen
home design.
Trustee Presser inquired whether there was any flexibility with regard to how
much compensatory storage is required. Mr. Saunders replied that there is none
as it is regulated by the State and FEMA.
The Village Forester, Jim Stier, commented on his assessment of the situation
and the deliberations of the Forestry Committee. He distributed pictures of the
trees under discussion.
Phil Couri, attorney for the petitioner, spoke in support of the request to
remove the trees. He argued that by preserving the integrity of the trees, the
Village is adversely affecting the integrity of the property. He noted that his
clients were willing to meet the Village’s required replacement inches.
President Pro Tem Aquilino commented that given the size of the property and
the size of the trees, it would be very difficult to plant the required number
of replacement inches.
The Trustees discussed various alternative design solutions with Mr. Saunders,
Mr. Stier, and Mr. Couri.
Following the discussion, Trustee Woodbury expressed empathy with the plight of
the petitioners, but stated that he could not agree to authorize removal of the
trees. Trustee Greenough remarked that trees of such size and quality really
add to the property’s value. Trustee Presser agreed, adding that he would be
willing to consider other variances on the property in order to achieve a
reasonable accommodation with the petitioners. Trustees Brower and Powell
concurred.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to sustain the Forestry
Commission’s decision. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Powell, and Aquilino. Nays:
None. Absent: President Duhl.
c)   Policy Direction Tower Road Lights. Mr. McInturff reported that a question
has arisen about the street lights being installed on Tower Road, although they
are the same as those installed on Sheridan Road, north of Tower except that
they are green instead of brown. It has, however, been suggested that a more
ornamental model be used. Mr. McInturff explained that if an ornamental light
is used, there would be significantly more light spillover into residential
property. In addition, the cost of an ornamental light is about $1,800 in cast
aluminum, or $2500 in fiberglass, as compared to $475 for the light fixture
currently being used.
Trustees Woodbury remarked that this is an aesthetic issue and voiced some
frustration that the matter had not been taken to the Design Review Board.
Manager Williams pointed out that these lights are in a residential district
and, therefore, do not fall under the Design Guidelines. He commented that
heretofore staff has received no direction pertaining to such utility fixtures
in residential districts.
Trustee Greenough agreed with Mr. Williams. She suggested several possible
options to mitigate light spillover.
Dick Kates, 1526 Tower Rd., observed that this provides a possible opportunity
for the Village to improve the appearance of its gateways. He recommended that
the Council solicit input from residents, especially those along Tower Rd.
After polling the Trustees, President Pro Tem Aquilino asked staff to install
one ornamental light fixture alongside the existing model so that people can
compare the two and provide feedback.
d)   Policy Direction: Sewer Repair. Mr. Saunders explained that periodically,
there are occurrences where sewer or water service lines serving individual
buildings, including homes, require maintenance, repair, or replacement. The
Village’s current policy is that the Village provides and maintains the main
infrastructure providing sewer and water service to the Village as a whole,
while the pipe that connects an individual building to this Village-provided
service is provided and maintained by the owner of the building being served.
He noted that there have been two instances during the past year or so in which
the Village has been asked to modify this policy to assume maintenance
responsibility for some of the service pipes due to unique circumstances: (1)
deep sewers, and long services.
Mr. Saunders reviewed results of a survey of other towns in the Northwest
Municipal Conference area as to their policies with respect to maintenance of
sewer lines between the main sewer and the home. This survey indicates that the
overwhelming majority of the towns have policies similar to Winnetka’s.
Mr. Saunders then outlined policy options available to the Council and
suggested that the Council consider each option in light of four basic issues:
cost implications, benefit allocation, practicality, and standard practice.
Trustee Presser expressed concern that it would be enormously expensive to
change the Village’s policy at this time when surveys show that its policy is
essentially the same as most other communities.
Mr. Saunders said that he would be least troubled by adopting some sort of cost
sharing program on deep sewers because they are rare, but recommended that the
Village avoid cost sharing on water or sewer mains.
Manager Williams indicated that he thought that adopting the same policy as
Wilmette with regard to deep sewers whereby the Village pays 50% of the cost of
the repair if the sewer is deeper than 12 feet would be viable. He noted if the
Village were to do so, the Trustees would have to decide whether to make the
decision retroactive and the policy would need to be codified. He suggested
that staff return with additional research and a proposed ordinance.
e)   Policy Direction: Revision of Parking Ban Hours. Chief DeLopez explained
that his research indicated that there exists no law enforcement purpose for
the current overnight parking ban and that a great deal of staff time and
energy is expended on administering requests for exceptions. He proposed
instituting a 6-month trial in a specified area during which the 2:00 a.m. to
6:00 a.m. parking ban would be lifted. Enforcement of the parking prohibition
would remain in effect in posted business districts and during declared snow
emergencies.
Trustee Powell expressed concern that this would promote people using the
streets for parking on a continuous basis.
Trustee Woodbury voice strong opposition to the proposal, and Trustees Brower
and Presser indicated that they didn’t see any real reason to change the
existing policy. Therefore, it was the consensus of the Council to leave the
overnight parking ban in effect.
f)   Additional Design Work for West Hibbard Drainage Study. Mr. Saunders
reported that during the course of performing the drainage study for the West
Hibbard Road area, there were three items that the Village asked Stanley
Consultants to do that were not consistent with the contract scope of work.
Therefore, he requested that the Council authorize increasing the previously
approved fee cap from $34,408 to $42,258. In response to a question from
Trustee Brower, Mr. Saunders indicated that the Village was continuing to
discuss cost-sharing arrangements with the Park District.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the increase
in the fee cap for Stanley Consultants. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Powell, Aquilino, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
g)   Bid 02-016 – 600 V Underground Cable. Mr. McInturff reviewed the bids for
600 volt underground cable and recommended that the bid be awarded to the
lowest bidder, WESCO, in the amount of $41,275. Manager Williams pointed out
that this cable is primarily used for new customers.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve award of the
bid to WESCO as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Powell, and Aquilino.
Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
h)   New Stone on Exposed Intake Pipe. Mr. McInturff reported that an
inspection of the Water Plant’s intake pipe revealed that about 500 ft. of it
is either exposed or “suspended,” meaning that it is not properly supported by
the lake bottom. He explained that it is necessary to cover and support the
intake pipe with rock immediately in order to ensure that the pipe does not
break causing further problems.
Although not in the budget, he recommended that the Council approve the bid of
Kadinger Marine in the amount of $31,400 to place 400 tons of rock to cover
approximately 500 feet of exposed pipe.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the expenditure
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Powell, Aquilino, Woodbury, Greenough and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: President
Duhl.
i)   Cell Phone Report. Chief DeLopez reviewed his research on the possibility
of enacting a ban on cell phone usage while driving within the boundaries of
the Village. He reported that after reviewing studies and discussions with the
Village Prosecutor, the critical issue in question is really one of “distracted
driving.” He explained that the existing Village Code has provisions covering
all instances of persons operating a vehicle in a negligent manner without due
caution and with the likelihood of endangering a person or property. He
recommended appropriate enforcement of this ordinance, with the caveat that
either the result of the distracted driving be observed by the complaining
witness or that it contribute to negligent driving or a traffic crash. He also
recommended that the Village continue to pursue effective state legislation
enacting a uniformly applicable law.
Both Trustee Powell and Trustee Brower commented that they supported the
Chief’s suggestion.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the new edition of the Winnetka
Report has been mailed.
11. Appointments.
President Pro Tem Aquilino suggested the appointment of Mary Robbins as
Chairperson of the Forestry Commission to succeed Jim Fox whose term expired on
October 31st. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve
the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino Brower, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None.
Absent: President Duhl. The Council convened into Executive Session at 11:05
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:35 p.m. Present: President
Pro Tem Aquilino, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Absent: President Duhl. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and
Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: President Pro Tem
Aquilino, Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: President Duhl. The meeting adjourned at 11:36 p.m.
                  ______________________________
                  Recording Secretary
November 12, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 3, 2002)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: none. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Village Planner Brian Norkus,
Assistant Planner Ann Klaassen, and 37 persons in the audience.
2.   Zoning Issues.
President Duhl expressed the Council’s appreciation for the number of people in
attendance and for their input on zoning issues. He suggested that the
discussion should begin with side yard setbacks, but was interrupted by a
member of the audience who asked to speak to the overall issue of changes to
zoning.
Doug Harness, 523 Hawthorn, introduced himself as a Winnetka resident and also
as President of Harness Group, which has built homes at 679 Hill, 500 Sunset,
and will be building a home at 502 Willow. He raised the following points:
   ·   He questioned the direction taken by the Council on zoning.
   ·   He believes new home development is a vote of confidence in Winnetka and
increases the tax base which supports Village parks and schools.
   ·   He stated that about 1% of homes are replaced each year which would mean
that housing is replaced every 100 years. He pointed out that a family’s needs
today are different from 100 years ago making new buildings necessary.
   ·   He expressed concern about the significant changes in basement and
ceiling heights which impose greater restrictions on homes built on hills.
   ·   He believes that Winnetka is more restrictive because of the way FAR/GFA
is calculated compared to other communities and suggested that the Council find
another way to reduce bulk.
   ·   He criticized the Caucus survey as being representative of only 2% of
the residents which is not an overwhelming consensus and asked that the Council
consider a referendum to determine the opinions of the voters.
President Duhl commented upon the audience’s favorable response to Mr.
Harness’s comments which seemed to indicate that many of them think that the
Council should not be discussing changes in the zoning ordinance. He said that
in the years since he has been on the Council, the calls received and comments
made at public hearings have been overwhelming about reducing bulk. He said
this is the first time he has heard that anyone was against reducing the gross
floor area.
The following residents also spoke against more restrictive zoning and
requested that in making changes to the zoning ordinance, the Council remember
young families and the types of housing they would be able to afford to
renovate and the plight of seniors with fixed incomes:
Kathy Miller, 484 Hawthorn
Doug Thompson, 385 Ridge
Kathy Almond, New Trier Tax assessor and Winnetka resident
Lynne Hans, 475 Linden
Michael Huff, 611 Orchard
The following residents spoke against a ranch overlay district and did not
believe there was any support for it in the proposed neighborhood:
Chuck McClenahan, 942 Pine Tree
Tony Kambich, 976 Pine Tree
Marvin Levine, 1541 Asbury
Richard J. Daley, 1600 Asbury
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce, commented that a home built on a 50-foot lot has rooms
that are already minimal in size and that by reducing them further, function of
the spaces inside the house would be lost. He described a typical home on this
type of lot as having four bedrooms, the largest of which may be 12 x 14, a
master bath, and a first floor consisting of a living room, family room,
kitchen, powder room, and possibly a study. He invited the Council to walk with
him through a home on a smaller lot so that they can get an architect’s
perspective. He recommended that the Council consult architects, builders and
other experts in planning before making any further changes to reduce bulk.
Village Attorney Janega arrived at 8:40 p.m.
Mark Verbryck, an architect who does not live in the area but works on
additions and alterations to existing housing stock, agreed with Mr. Frank. He
asked the Council to consider the perspective of architects and other
professionals to see how the spaces in homes are inhabited before restricting
setbacks to control bulk.
Mike Spigelman, a realtor in Winnetka, attending on behalf of a client, asked
the Council to consider the effect of the basement ceiling height provisions on
the floodplain regulations which might prevent new homes from being built in
the floodplain.
Jim Clark, 1040 Oak Street, believes that the Council’s goal is to have
architecturally appealing homes in the community, but believes the zoning
changes have made it difficult for architects to build that type of home. He
suggested the Council contact members of the design review committee in Glencoe
for input and also send out questionnaires and have hearings to obtain comments
from the residents.
President Duhl and the trustees responded to the audience’s comments. President
Duhl stated that the Council is attempting to do what it perceives the
community’s views to be and has attempted to implement the Caucus platform in
the past. He said the question is always how much should the community impose
its will on individual property rights. President Duhl suggested that the
Council meet again and generally discuss the comments received at this meeting
before speaking to more specific zoning changes.
3. Discussion Outline for Residential Design Review.
Trustee Woodbury said the purpose of this discussion is to begin a dialogue to
establish a guide for the Design Review Committee. He said that currently,
design review can only be done based on the zoning that is in place to attempt
to make a better project, as was done in the project at Green Bay and Winnetka.
He thought they this may be the link to better designs overall.
Trustee Brower commented that the Council should proceed carefully and that by
the time every Board is represented in the committee, finding a balance will be
difficult.
Trustee Aquilino suggested that, given the opinions Council heard earlier in
the meeting, the Council should have a discussion about whether to proceed,
before concluding that there should be a committee formed to address the issue.
Trustee Powell suggested contacting other communities for their input.
Trustee Presser stated that he is still concerned about bulk, but noted that
there is some resistance to design review.
Trustee Greenough said that some issues can be addressed in zoning, such as
massing, setback, articulations, and roof forms, but others can’t. She
suggested that if there are design guidelines, you may be able to get good
results and still leave personal taste decisions to individuals.
President Duhl was of the opinion that the Council should discuss if any
concept of design guidelines would be functional and whether design review
should be established at all.
The Trustees agreed to table the discussion until the issue could be
investigated further.
Manager Williams indicated that he would place this item on the agenda for the
next study session.
4. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Brower, Presser, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:03 p.m.
November 19, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 17, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 19, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7: 35 p.m. Present: Trustees Hugh
Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Stephen Presser. Absent: Trustees
Edmund Woodbury and Barbara Aquilino. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus,
and 12 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   December 3, 2002 Regular Council Meeting: All of the Trustees indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   December 10, 2002 Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 15, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   November 5, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
Manager Williams explained that due to the press of other legal business, each
of the first three ordinances had been introduced as a shell ordinance, and
therefore, must be amended prior to adoption to reflect specific information.
a)   Ordinance No. M-35-2002 – Zoning Variation: 1447 Edgewood – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to amend the ordinance
from the introduced shell to the final draft as presented on pages 18-21 of the
agenda packet. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, granting a request for Maximum Building Size and Intensity of Use of
Lot variations to permit construction of a two-story addition. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell,
and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Aquilino and Woodbury.
b)   Ordinance No. M-36-2002 – Zoning Variation: 205 Forest – Adoption. Trustee
Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance from the
introduced shell to the final draft as presented on pages 31-34 of the agenda
packet. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance,
as amended, granting a Rear Yard Setback variation to permit the construction
of a detached garage. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Powell, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Aquilino and Woodbury.
c)   Ordinance No. M-37-2002 – Zoning Variation: 675 Garland – Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend the ordinance from
the introduced shell to the final draft as presented on pages 42-45 of the
agenda packet. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to grant Maximum Building
Size and Intensity of Use of Lot variations to permit the construction of a
one-story addition. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Aquilino and Woodbury.
d)   Ordinance No. M-38-2002 – Alley Consolidation: 334 Ridge – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance
approving the consolidation of two existing zoning lots into a single lot of
record pursuant to the provisions of the Village Code that require that any new
principal structure be built on a single lot of record. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Powell. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Aquilino and Woodbury.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2002 – Amending Village Code Regarding Construction
Hours and building Permit Issuance - Adoption. Manager Williams reviewed minor
housekeeping revisions made to the introduced ordinance to reflect Council’s
discussion at the November 5th meeting.
In response to a comment from Doug Harness, 523 Hawthorne, Mr. D’Onofrio
indicated that a letter explaining the changes would be sent to all contractors
doing business in the Village.
Trustee Aquilino arrived at 7:40 p.m.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the ordinance as
reviewed by Mr. Williams. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Powell seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance
addressing several issues related to construction and demolition, as amended.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Greenough,
Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-13-2002 – New Liquor Licensees – Cosi, 540 Lincoln and
Fio, Laundry Mall - Adoption. Manager Williams explained that procedurally the
Council always considers new liquor license applications in a two-step process.
Therefore, the two applications need to be bifurcated, with the Cosi
application being up for adoption, and the Fio application up for introduction.
He distributed copies of the original MC-13-2002, pertaining only to Cosi,
introduced at the last meeting. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser,
moved to amend the ordinance to update the number of A-1 liquor licenses to
reflect the addition of Cosi as a licensee. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the Ordinance MC-
13-2002, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Powell, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Woodbury.
Manager Williams then distributed drafts of Ordinance No. MC-17-2002,
pertaining to the application by Fio for an “A” liquor license. He explained
that Fio would replace the restaurant known as Tanglewood in the Laundry Mall
that has closed. New proprietors plan to reopen the restaurant, maintaining the
same operation as Tanglewood, under the new name, Fio. He noted that although
this change causes no net change in the number of A licenses and packaged meal
riders, legislative action is necessary because, under the terms of the
Village’s Liquor Control Ordinance, the termination of a business automatically
terminates the license.
George Grumley, the applicant’s attorney, appeared on her behalf and responded
to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the ordinance
that would authorize the Liquor Commissioner to issue a Class A license to Fio.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Ordinance No. MC-14-2002 – Amending Liquor Ordinance - Adoption. Manager
Williams explained one small change to the specific provisions for the minimum
size and seating capacity of a Limited Food Products store authorized for the
retail sale of beer or wine under the Class E-1 liquor license classification
to reflect the current seating capacity for Song O’Sixpence.
Due to some confusion over the existing license classification for Song
O’Sixpence, the matter was tabled until the next meeting.
h)   Ordinance No. MC-15-2002 – Snow Emergency Parking Regulations - Adoption..
Manager reviewed proposed revisions to the draft ordinance to reflect Council’s
discussion at the November 5th meeting.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to amend the ordinance as
proposed. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Greenough,
Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
i)   Ordinance No. M-39-2002 – Disposal of Surplus Equipment - Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that this ordinance is a housekeeping matter that
would permit the Village to dispose of two vehicles that have exceeded their
useful life.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
j)   Ordinance No. MC-16-2002 – Amending Village Code Regarding Sewer Repair -
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that pursuant to Council’s discussion
at the November 5th meeting, the proposed ordinance would modify the Village
Code to provide for a 50/50 cost sharing arrangement for repairs to sewer
service lines deeper than 12 feet below ground, provided that the location of
the repair is located within the public right-of-way, as opposed to being
located under private property. He explained that the ordinance provides for
Village reimbursement for underground work only, limited to 50% of the
reasonable and customary cost of such work.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance as
proposed. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
a)   Zoning Report & Residential Design Review. President Duhl suggested that
the Trustees consider each of the possible proposed areas in which zoning
amendments might be made vis a vis whether or not it should be pursued. These
areas include
Reduction to Maximum Permitted GFA
Side Yard Setbacks
Caps on GFA and Yard Setbacks in Certain Zoning Districts
Building Ht. Standards for Large Lots
Ranch Home Overlay District
Articulation
Garages
Arbor Heights
GFA Inclusions and Exclusions
Residential Design Review
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 8:10 p.m.
After a brief discussion of each option, President Duhl polled the Trustees. It
was the consensus of the Council to revisit each option except the Ranch Home
Overlay District, as well as to return to the residential design review
concept.
Manager Williams mentioned that in addition to the above, staff needs
clarification as to application of the articulation standards.
President Duhl noted that Glencoe had recently performed an extensive study
covering several of these issues and suggested that staff explore their study
to see if any of their information could be of use to Winnekta.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1293 and 1294. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1293 in the amount of $612,891.90 and Warrant
List No. 1294 in the amount of $917,959.93. Trustee Powell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Tower Road Change Order #3. Mr. Williams reported that Phase II of the
Tower Road project is now complete. The Council has already approved change
Orders #1 and #2. This final Change Order, in the amount of $44,238.76 will
increase the total project cost to $1,554,719.20, approximately $180,000 below
the budgeted amount. He recommended approval of the Change Order.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve Change Order #3
in the amount $44,238.76. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
An unidentified audience member inquired about the possibility of instituting
