December 3, 2002
DRAFT MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 17, 2002)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 3, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Finance
Director Ed McKee and 9 persons in the audience. Trustee Aquilino arrived at 7:
52 p.m.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   December 10, 2002 Study Session: All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
b)   December 17, 2002 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 12, 2002, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-14-2002 – Amending Liquor Ordinance – Adoption.
Village Attorney Janega explained Ordinance MC-14-2002 has been amended to
provide for the minimum size and seating of a limited food products store
authorized for the retail sale of beer or wine under the Class E-1 liquor
license classification. Pursuant to Staff recommendations at the November 19,
2002 Council meeting, the minimum seating area has been reduced to 3,500 square
feet, and the minimum seating capacity has been reduced to 18 customers.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the amendment to
the ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees, Woodbury,
Brower, Presser, Powell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
b)   Ordinance No. M-39-2002 – Disposal of Surplus Equipment - Adoption.
Manager Williams explained that this ordinance is a housekeeping matter that
would permit the Village to dispose of two vehicles that have exceeded their
useful life.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance
approving the disposal of two Village vehicles. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Powell, Greenough, Brower, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-17-2002 – Fio Liquor License – Adoption. Village Attorney
Janega explained that the ordinance had been amended to reflect the current
numbers of Class A, Class E and Class E-1 licenses and incorporates the Class A
liquor license and packaged meal rider for Fio. Trustee Presser, seconded by
Trustee Powell moved to amend the ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion
passed. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes:
None. Absent: Trustee Aquilino.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower moved to adopt the ordinance as
amended to reflect the current numbers of Class A, Class E and Class E-
1 licenses. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Powell, Greenough and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Aquilino.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-16-2002 – Amending Village Code Regarding Sewer Repair -
Adoption. Village Attorney Janega explained that this ordinance would modify
the Village Code to provide for a 50/50 cost sharing arrangement for repairs to
sewer service lines deeper than 12 feet below ground, provided that the
location of the repair is located within the public right-of-way, as opposed to
being located under private property. She explained that the ordinance provides
for Village reimbursement for underground work only, limited to 50% of the
reasonable and customary cost of such work.
Trustee Woodbury stated his concern that some residents may be penalized for
having sewer lines that are deeper than 12 feet and asked that the cost-sharing
language in the ordinance be clarified so that it is administered consistently.
The Trustees agreed to table the discussion to obtain further recommendations
from the Village Engineer.
d)   Resolution R-28-2002 – O’Brien-Huggard Subdivision: 845/853/861 Prospect –
Adoption. Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus explained that
this subdivision would increase the existing lot area of 845 and 861 Prospect
by demolishing the home at 853 Prospect and splitting that lot between the two
properties to the north and south in order to create two conforming lots in the
R-3 zoning district.
Lishka Blodgett, a Glencoe resident, spoke against the demolishing of this
house because of its historic, architectural value. She stated that she had
been under contract to purchase but before closing, her lender required an
engineering study. She said that the day before the engineer was to go into the
house, the owner canceled her contract and entered into a contract with the
neighbors. She said she is still interested in purchasing the house to preserve
it.
After ascertaining that Mrs. Blodgett did not have a legal claim to the house,
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Woodbury and Presser commented that since
the demolition had not been delayed by the Building Review Committee, and the
preservation ordinance is not mandatory, they would support the subdivision of
this property.
Trustee Greenough commented that she could not support either the demolition of
the house or the subdivision.
Cami Eichar, 862 Prospect, spoke in favor of the subdivision, stating that it
is important that these properties are brought into conformity.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt Resolution R-28-2002, seconded by Trustee
Presser, approving the O’Brien-Huggard Subdivision of 845, 853 and 861 Prospect
Avenue. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: Trustee Greenough. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance M-41-2002 – Zoning Variation: 887 Private Road – Introduction.
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio explained that Ordinance M-41-
2002 would grant a variation in the front setback requirements of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit the construction of a second floor addition to the
residence at 887 Private Road.
Trustee Greenough moved to introduce Ordinance M-41-2002, seconded by Trustee
Woodbury. By voice vote, the motion passed.
f)   Ordinance M-42-2002 – Zoning Variation: 700 Blackthorn – Introduction.
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio explained that Ordinance M-42-
2002 would grant variations to the minimum setback requirements of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit the construction of a one-story addition to accommodate a
kitchen, dining room and mudroom in the residence at 700 Blackthorn.
Trustee Brower moved to introduce Ordinance M-42-2002, seconded by Trustee
Greenough. By voice vote, the motion passed.
g)   Ordinance M-40-2002 – 2002 Tax Levy – Truth in Taxation Hearing and
Ordinance Introduction. President Duhl opened the hearing at 8:45 p.m. and
asked Mr. McKee to review the tax levy. Mr. McKee explained that the tax levy
is set by taking into consideration revenue and expenditure trends to determine
what is needed to fund the Village for the coming year. He recommended that the
tax levy be increased by 3.5%, plus new development, in order to cover
increased operating expenses, primarily wage and benefit costs, and stated that
the Village’s portion of tax bills will increase 1.6% if the tax levy is
approved, which is $26 on a tax bill of $10,000.
Mr. McKee further explained that the net 2002 property tax levy was set at
$9,876,081, but ordinance M-40-2002 is for the gross tax levy of $10,741,215.
He noted that the tax abatement ordinance for the 1994 and 1996 bond levies is
normally adopted right after the tax levy ordinance but that, because the bonds
are being refinanced in January of 2003, the abatements will be done in
conjunction with the bond ordinance.
At the conclusion of Mr. McKee’s comments, President Duhl called for remarks,
questions and/or evidence from the audience. There being none, he closed the
hearing at 8:50 p.m.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-40-2002, Levying Property Taxes for the Year 2002. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Old Business.
None.
8)   New Business.
None.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1295 and 1296. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1295 in the amount of $844,474.67 and Warrant
List No. 1296 in the amount of $384,528.39 Trustee Woodbury seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Bid No. 02-018: Four (4) Wheel Drive Tractor. Manager Williams explained
that Bid No. 021-018 is for the purchase and delivery of a diesel-powered,
