                  Deputy Clerk
December 10, 2002
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: January 7, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Nan Greenough. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director
of Community Development Brian Norkus, Public Works Director Steven Saunders,
and eight persons in the audience.
2. Residential Design Review.
Mr. D’Onofrio introduced Nathan Parch, Community Development Analyst in
Glencoe, who provided a brief overview of that community’s study of residential
design review. He explained that in response to resident concerns over the
increasing number of teardowns and the impact on the community, Glencoe had
formed an Appearance Review Task Force. This committee was tasked with studying
the programs of other communities, particularly Park Ridge, Skokie and Lake
Forest, all of which have mandatory design review programs, and evaluating
possible options for Glencoe. He said that the committee’s report made twelve
suggestions that were reasonable policy options, mostly based in zoning, and
including a voluntary review process with incentives, but ultimately
recommended a mandatory program. The Village Board, however, did not support
that recommendation.
Trustee Woodbury inquired as to what issues Glencoe’s committee found worth of
review. Mr. Parch said that the committee did not want to prepare specific
design guidelines until after the Board’s approval, but the discussions focused
on compatibility with the neighborhood, bulk, and consistency of architectural
style. He mentioned the concern that one set of design guidelines might not be
totally appropriate for an entire community; they might need to be modified by
different neighborhood areas.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed his discussion outline
After listening to both presentations, the Trustees discussed possible
approaches to the problem for Winnetka. There was agreement that the Village
should pursue the formation of a small committee with a defined charge to begin
a dialogue about residential design review and report back to the Council.
There was consensus that this was not being done with an eye toward enacting
any mandatory process.
3. Discussion – Side Yard Set Back Analysis.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained the two components of side yard setbacks, a major
factor affecting the size of a building on a lot. He also reviewed the results
of a survey comparing Winnetka’s requirements to those of similar communities
and discussed possible options to be considered.
President Duhl pointed out that there are many nonconforming lots throughout
the Village. He expressed some concern that if changes are made, the Council,
which already spends a great deal of time on granting variances for individuals
with nonconforming sideyards, an inefficient use of Council time, the number of
variation requests would multiply significantly. He suggested that, perhaps,
side yard set backs should be determined by lot size, rather than by district.
The Trustees discussed this idea at some length. There was agreement among the
Trustees that the side yards for 50-ft. lots should not be changed and that
given their greater understanding of the issues, Staff should come back to the
Council with specific recommendations of changes.
4. Policy Options: Cost Sharing Program for Flood Protection of Residential
Basements.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the possible causes for basement flooding and sewer
backups, as well as village and private actions that might be initiated to
prevent these occurrences. He also outlined several policy issues arising from
the problem, first and foremost being the question of whether the Village
should expend public funds to assist homeowners in protecting their property
from basement backups, or should this responsibility continue to lie with
homeowners.
President Duhl emphasized that this is an important question and that it is
important that the Village take a stand on whether an expenditure of public
funds is appropriate in this area.
Manager Williams explained that the Village sewer system has three components:
private, Village, and Metropolitan Water Reclamation District. Both he and Mr.
Saunders pointed out that even if the Village’s system were airtight, which is
unachievable given the age of the Village, there would still be problems
because the private and MWRD lines have problems. Therefore, he said that the
only solution for homeowners is some for of self-help, as described in notices
sent to all who attended previous meeting regarding flooding problems.
President Duhl pointed out that education of homeowners as to what their
options are is important and expressed strong support for continuing efforts in
this direction. He, however, voiced serious concerns about the Village’s
ability to actively fund any kind of assistance program and stated his
opposition to the idea.
Manager Williams noted that any such program would have to be funded by the
Sewer Fund, which is driven by sewer rates.
Trustees Woodbury and Aquilino expressed some support for creating a pilot
program.
Trustees Brower, Powell, and Presser indicated that they were not in favor of
the Village providing any kind of subsidy.
5. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser,
and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:30 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Brower, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Greenough. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams.
6. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Brower, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough.
The meeting adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
December 17, 2002
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 7, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 17, 2002 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Village Finance Director Ed McKee and 21 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   January 7, 2003 Regular Meeting: All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
b)   January 14, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 19, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   December 3, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-41-2002 – Zoning Variation: 887 Private Road – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance M-41-
2002 granting a variation in the front setback requirements of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit the construction of a second floor addition to the
residence at 887 Private Road. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance M-42-2002 – Zoning Variation: 700 Blackthorn – Adoption. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance M-42-2002
granting a variations to the minimum setback requirements of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit the construction of a one-story addition to accommodate a
kitchen, dining room and mudroom in the residence at 700 Blackthorn. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-16-2002 – Amending Village Code Regarding Sewer Repair -
Adoption. Mr. Williams addressed concerns raised by Trustee Woodbury when this
ordinance was tabled. He explained that staff had re-examined the 12 ft.
threshold and determined it to be appropriate, given that it is at that point
that specialized equipment is required at substantial cost. He mentioned that
this formula has worked well in Wilmette and that administering any kind of
incremental plan would be very difficult.
Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment to the ordinance to clarify that
the cost sharing only occurs when the portion of the pipe to be repaired is
located under the public right-of-way.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to amend the ordinance as
proposed. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt this ordinance
modifying the Village Code to provide for a 50/50 cost sharing arrangement for
repairs to sewer service lines, underground work only, deeper than 12 feet
below ground, if the location of the repair is located within the public right-
of-way, as opposed to being located under private property. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-43-2002 – SUP and Variations: Winnetka Community House,
620 Lincoln Ave. – Introduction. Trustee Brower recused himself from discussion
and voting on this matter due to his relationship with Harris Bank. Trustees
Greenough and Aquilino did not recuse themselves stating that although they are
both former members of the Women’s Board, they have no participation in the
governance of the Community House.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request by the Community House to amend its Special
Use Permit to permit renovation and expansion of the existing facilities,
including construction of a 2-1/2-story addition at the rear and a 1-story
addition on the front. The proposed ordinance would also grant Height, Rear
Yard, and Exterior Wall variations of the Zoning Ordinance. He reported that
the Zoning Board of Appeals, Plan Commission and Design Review Board each had
issued favorable comments with regard to the proposal. The Plan Commission did
note, however, that the proposed expansion would likely lead to both increased
parking encroachment into adjacent residential areas, as well as increased
pressure to provide additional parking facilities. In addition, the Design
Review Board has requested that the architects address alternatives for certain
materials specified in the plans.
President Duhl noted that Harkness House is inexorably intertwined with the
Community House and requested that staff prepare a zoning matrix for review
including that structure in the calculations. He noted that although GFA is
designed for residential, not institutional uses, there is an institutional
limit.
Geoff Fox, 879 Cherry, Chariman of the Community House Board of Governors,
introduced representatives who would be addressing the Council.
Don Van Arsdale, Executive Director of the Community House, reviewed the
process the organization has pursued to get to this point. He said that the
Special Use Permit and variations would enable the Community House to do what
they do better, and he explained why. He noted that the three areas in which
the facility hoped to grow and expand are youth enrichment, fitness an
athletics, and arts and entertainment. In response to a question from Manager
Williams, Mr. Van Arsdale explained how the organization had involved the
neighbors in their planning process.
Chris Mekus, the architect for the expansion, reviewed sketches, elevations,
and material samples. He noted that the estimated period of construction was
expected to be approximately 12 months and that the finishing of the interiors
might be phased.
Trustee Aquilino inquired as to whether there were plans for additional
expansion in the future.
Bob Barnes, 184 Green Bay Rd., Co-chair of the New Facilities Board addressed
this question. He indicated that initially the master plan had been more
ambitious, but that fundraising efforts have shown that those plans are well
beyond what is affordable within any predictable time frame.
Michael Werthmann, the parking and traffic consultant for the Community House,
commented on his studies and responded to questions from the Trustees. In doing
so, he pointed out that the Village’s traffic engineer had expressed no
disagreement with his traffic study, but had concurred with the Plan Commission
that on occasion parking would be pushed further into the surrounding
residential neighborhoods. Mr. Werthmann reviewed suggestions for mitigating
the potential parking problems.
Manager Williams commented on the cost of adding additional parking. He stated
that due to other demands on Village funds, a parking deck was no longer in the
Village’s Five Year Plan. He reminded the Council that the Caucus had come out
against the concept and that the last time the idea had been broached, there
had been strong neighborhood objection.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Powll, moved to introduce the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Woodbury expressed his belief that there exists a need for further
dialogue about parking. The Trustees concurred. Therefore, President Duhl
suggested that should the ordinance be introduced at this meeting, it not be up
for adoption until the second meeting in January in order to allow the affected
neighbors time to understand the ramifications and participate in discussions.
He stated that the issue for be up for adoption at the January 21, 2003 Regular
Meeting.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-18-2002 – Amending Village Code Regarding Special Event
Permits and Fees. – Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that this ordinance
corrects a numbering error in the Village Code and makes no substantive
changes.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance
as proposed. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Resolultion No. R-30-2002 – Osmond/Baker Resubdivision: 1070/1072 Fisher
Ln. - Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request by immediate neighbors to
resubdivide their properties. The proposed resubdivision creates no new lots
and corrects nonconformities pertaining to the westerly lot. He reported that
the Plan Commission recommended approval of the proposal.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the resubdivision.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Ordinance M-40-2002 – 2002 Tax Levy Adoption. – Adoption. Mr. McKee
explained that (1) the Village is levying the maximum amount that it can
receive; (2) the police and fire pension benefits, as mandated by the State,
will consume 30-35% of the new dollars received from the property tax levy; and
(3) in a departure from past practice, the Village will be abating the Public
Safety Building bonds in January instead of at this time. Noting that the
Village cannot recover the amount mandated by the State for police and fire
pensions, President Duhl urged the Village to pursue every avenue available to
seek an exemption from tax caps, at least insofar as funding this mandate is
concerned.
President Duhl called for additional comments or questions from the audience or
the Trustees. There being none, Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough,
moved to adopt the ordinance levying property taxes for the Year 2002 as
proposed. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent:
None
h)   Ordinance M-01-2003 – Public Safety Building Bond Refinancing –
Introduction. Mr. McKee explained that this ordinance would authorize the
Village to retire bonds issued in 1996 for the Public Safety Building and issue
new bonds in January, 2003, at a lower interest rates, thereby lowering the
Village’s interest expense and saving about $265,000. The ordinance also
directs the Village Clerk to prepare abatements for the 1994 and 1996 bond
issues.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
1)   Old Business.
None.
2)   New Business.
None.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1297 and 1298. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1297 in the amount of $642,244.21 and Warrant
List No. 1298 in the amount of $449,521.69. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Policy Direction: 27 Indian Hill. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a
variation to allow for an existing single family dwelling to remain on the
property while a new dwelling is being constructed on the same lot. He reported
that although the Zoning Board of Appeals had reviewed the proposal, it did not
receive the four affirmative votes necessary for a positive recommendation to
the Council.
Ms. Janega commented on possible restrictions that the Council might consider
incorporating into an ordinance to ensure that both dwellings do not remain on
the lot or that the lot is subdivided.
Geoffrey Stringer, the applicant, spoke in support of his request. In response
to a question from President Duhl, he indicated that he had no problems with
any of the restrictions suggested by Ms. Janega.
Trustee Woodbury moved to direct the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance
granting the requested variation, pursuant to conditions 1, 2, 4, and 5
outlined in discussion materials distributed at the meeting. Trustee Powell
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Presser
abstained.
After brief discussion, there was a consensus to waive introduction of the
proposed ordinance and go directly to adoption at the January 7, 2003 meeting.
3)   Public Comment and Questions.
None
4)   Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Aquilino reported on her attendance at a recent Chamber
of Commerce Board meeting.
Trustee Powell reported that he and Chief Colpaert had attended a press
conference with Governor Ryan announcing a major contribution to RED Center,
that would lessen the Village’s rates on a going-forward basis.
b)   Manager: Mr. Williams reported that three Public Works supervisors had
completed a nationally recognized public manager’s program. He also commented
on the engineering bid with Stanley Consultants for a portion of the
implementation of the West Hibbard drainage project, noting that the cost would
be split with the Park District. Finally, he announced the upcoming budget
meeting dates.
5)   Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Anne Wilder to the Forestry
Committee for a three-year term. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino Brower, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:43 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:30 p.m. Present: President
Duhl and Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
7)   Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Woodbury, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
