January 7, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 21, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 7, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Finance Director Ed McKee and 7 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   January 14, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
b)   January 21, 2003 Regular Meeting: All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend. President Duhl indicated that he would be unable to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 8, 2002, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   December 10, 2002, Study Session. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   December 17, 2002, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-18-2002 – Amending Village Code Regarding Special Event
Permits and Fees. – Adoption. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser,
moved to adopt the ordinance correcting a numbering error in the Village Code.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance M-01-2003 – Public Safety Building Bond Refinancing - Adoption.
Mr. McKee updated the Council on the status of the bids opening, noting that
the Village would be saving $324,946.39 in interest expense. He introduced
Barbara Chezalier from Speer Financial who explained that the copies of the
final ordinance distributed before the meeting reflect the results of the bids
and responded to questions from the Trustees.
Manager Williams pointed out that Moody’s had reaffirmed the Village’s Aaa bond
rating.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the ordinance to
reflect the results of the bids for the bonds. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, authorizing the Village to retire bonds issued in 1996 for the Public
Safety Building and issue new bonds at a lower interest rate. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None
c)   Ordinance M-2-2003 – Zoning Variation: 27 Indian Hill Rd. - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed an application for a Permitted Use variation to allow for an
existing single-family, detached dwelling to remain on a single lot while a new
single-family dwelling is being constructed.
Attorney Janega reviewed special conditions set forth in the draft ordinance
pursuant to Council’s discussion at the December 17th meeting to ensure that
the original dwelling does not remain after completion of the new construction.
After a brief discussion the Trustees agreed to remove the condition requiring
that all construction materials be stored inside the existing house.
President Duhl explained that because the Council had discussed this request at
the last meeting, it could choose to waive normal procedures and move directly
to adoption, or it could choose to follow normal procedure.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to waive introduction and
initial posting of the ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt Ordinance M-2-2003
as revised by the preceding discussion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and
Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance M-3-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1195 Asbury - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from the maximum roofed lot
coverage requirement to permit the construction of a two-car detached garage.
He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend approval of
this variation.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved
to introduce the proposed ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-4-2003 – Zoning Variation: 139 De Windt - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for variations from the Front and Corner Yard
Setback and front facing Garage provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to permit
the construction of a two-story addition. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had recommended approval of this variation request.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved
to introduce the proposed ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-5-2003 – Zoning Variation: 151 Fuller Ln. - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Maximum Building Size variation to
permit the construction of a one-story addition. He reported that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted to recommend approval of this variation.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino,
moved to introduce the proposed ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
g)   Ordinance No. M-6-2003 – Zoning Variation: 740 Sheridan Rd. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Rear Yard Setback
variation to permit the construction of a two-story addition. He reported that
the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend approval of this variation.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to
introduce the proposed ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Trustee Brower recused himself from voting on the issue.
h)   Resolution No. R-1-2003 – Release of Executive Session Minutes - Adoption.
Mr. Williams explained that this is a Village housekeeping duty required by
State law to be performed every six months. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by
Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the minutes of the Executive Sessions held
June 18, 2002, through December 17, 2002. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Powell then moved to adopt Resolution No. R-1-2003 releasing those
minutes no longer required to be held for reasons of confidentiality. Trustee
Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
2. Old Business.
None.
3. New Business.
None.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1299 and 1300. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1299 in the amount of $2,019,600.64 and
Warrant List No. 1300 in the amount of $1,096,972.99. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid No. 02-017- Truck Chassis Replacement. Mr. Williams reviewed the bids
for the purchase and delivery of a new truck chassis needed to replace an
existing 1979 truck chassis used by the Public Works Department for various
services. He reported that the Public Works Department recommended approval of
the bid from Northwest Trucks, Inc., in the amount of $80,705. He explained
that although this bid marginally higher than the lowest, it does meet all of
the specifications of the bid and is in keeping with the Department’s efforts
to standardize equipment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bid to
Northwest Trucks, Inc., as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Plan Commission Report: Economic Development. President Duhl invited Gwen
Trindl, Plan Commission Chairman, to review the process the Commission followed
in developing its report. She noted that members of the Chamber of Commerce had
been involved in the discussions and mentioned that throughout the process, the
Commission had been very aware of the fact that significant staff support would
be required to sustain any committee’s efforts to implement the Commission’s
suggestions. She also responded to questions from the Trustees.
In reply to a question from Trustee Woodbury, she indicated that the Commission
had not done an in-depth study of what other, similar communities are doing to
address the economic development issue. Mr. Woodbury stated that he felt such a
study was critical before the creation of any commission. He expressed the need
to explore what alternatives exist and to clearly define and prioritize
objectives. Ms. Trindl indicated that with staff support the Plan Commission
might be able to undertake a more in-depth research study.
President Duhl suggested that while the Plan Commission’s report is what the
Council requested, given how complicated the topic of economic development is,
compounded by the fact that present staff cannot provide the necessary support,
the issue should be set for further consideration after the budget is
completed. He slated the matter for discussion at the March Study Session. He
asked that in the meantime staff investigate what other communities are doing
in this area to determine ways in which the Village might move forward.
d)   Policy Direction: Sidewalks. Mr. Williams briefly reported the Village’s
recent experience with requests for installation of new sidewalks. He suggested
that the Council consider establishing a global, formal policy for handling and
processing such requests. Such a policy would provide a clear guide for
Winnetkans wanting to know the exact procedures for installing new sidewalks,
or stopping their installation, and eliminate any vagueness that currently
surrounds the Village’s decision-making process.
Mr. Williams explained that recent experience indicates that the Village’s
informal policy appears to defer to the majority opinion of the affected
neighborhood, especially those abutting the proposed sidewalk. Using this past
practice as a precedent, he outlined a possible three-step, formal policy that
could be established: (a) a preliminary petition from the neighbors to initiate
an engineering study; (b) neighborhood meetings; and (c) a final petition. In
doing so, he also raised certain key policy questions requiring consideration,
such as the number of signatures required on a preliminary petition, how many
property owners on either both sides or only immediately abutting a sidewalk
would have to concur with the installation of a proposed sidewalk. He explained
that with this kind of a policy, would make the installation of new sidewalks
more neighborhood driven than Village driven.
In closing his comments, Mr. Williams recommended that any formal policy should
apply only to secondary streets where traffic volume is relatively low.
Questions of whether or not to install sidewalks on primary streets would
continue to fall to the Council after consideration of comments by neighbors.
Mr. Williams pointed out that the Council also could continue to handle each
situation as it arises on an ad hoc basis.
Pointing out that the current lack of process consumes an enormous amount of
the Council’s limited time, President Duhl voiced support for the concept of
delineating an administrative process. He reiterated the fact that
historically, the Council has deferred to the wishes of residents abutting a
proposed sidewalk and that given that underlying philosophy, which continues to
prevail, it makes sense to formalize a process.
Trustee Woodbury concurred, saying that something has to change as the current
lack of process is both inefficient and does not work. He voiced the opinion
that the proposed process would likely produce constructive dialogue among
neighbors outside of the Council Chambers.
Trustee Powell expressed concern about formalizing a rigid process, and Trustee
Brower raised the question of some sort of appeal process.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., spoke in opposition to formalizing a policy unless
sufficient flexibility is incorporated to ensure that exceptions can be
identified and appropriately considered.
After extended discussion, there was a consensus to formalize a procedure for
handling sidewalk requests, with the signatures of 25% of affected owners being
required to sign a preliminary petition that, in turn, would initiate an
engineering study and the signatures of 66% of abutting owners being required
to implement the installation of a sidewalk. There was also agreement that the
process should include a mechanism for bringing a matter before the Council in
certain circumstances. The Village Attorney was directed to draft a procedure
for consideration by the Council.
President Duhl raised the question of how to handle the pending petition for a
sidewalk on Pine Street. There was some support among the Trustees for putting
a framework in place and putting Pine Street into that framework, but no
decision was made. They agreed to consider the matter further.
4. Public Comment and Questions.
None
5. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village had received an amount
in excess of that authorized by Council through the sale of used vehicles at
the NWMC auction. He also reviewed the dates set for the upcoming budget
meetings.
6. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of John Weber as the Design Review
