                  Recording Secretary
January 14, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 4, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of
Community Development Brian Norkus, and two persons in the audience.
2. Recommended Modifications to Side Yard Requirements.
Before Mr. Norkus reviewed his Agenda Report to the Council, President Duhl
suggested that the Trustees work to resolve the issue of sideyards before the
election and leave the other outstanding zoning issues to be discussed and
settled by the new Council. There was agreement among the Trustees that this
was a prudent way to proceed.
Mr. Norkus explained that under the current side yard setback requirements,
compliance with both a “minimum” side yard, as well as a “total” side yard
based on a percentage of the lot width is required and that both of these
standards vary from zoning district to zoning district. He noted that this
could result in inconsistent yard requirements when a hypothetical lot is
"relocated" to different zoning districts.
Mr. Norkus reviewed staff’s study of the sideyard requirements of comparable
communities, i.e. Wilmette, Lake Forest, Glencoe, Highland Park, and Hinsdale.
He pointed out that that study indicates that Winnetka’s minimum side yard
requirement for lots up to 60 feet wide is relatively consistent with the
requirements of the other communities. However, for lots between 60-100 ft. and
over 125 ft., the minimum side yard becomes small by comparison. With regard to
total side yard requirements, Winnetka’s are among the more restrictive for
lots 75 feet wide or greater, based upon the assumption that a lot 75-100 ft.
is zoned R-3, and over 100 ft. are zoned R-2. He noted, however, that there are
numerous lots 75-100 feet in width within the R-5 and R-4 zoning districts that
are subject to smaller side yard requirements.
He went on to explain Staff’s recommendation that minimum side yard
requirements be modified to reflect lot width only. He noted that while this
would make calculating side yards slightly more complicated and create zoning
nonconformities, it would better address the diversity of lot widths. He also
recommended that total side yard requirements eliminate any reference to zoning
district.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the proposed changes would have little effect on the
smaller R-4 and R-5 lots but would impact larger lots regardless of zoning
district. He said that there was no recommendation to change the minimum side
yard requirements on the 50 ft. lots, but that the total side yard requirement
would change to 25% of lot with but not less than 14 ft.
Trustee Greenough expressed concern that although the proposed changes are a
step in the right direction, they still do not provide enough separation
between houses. She mentioned that historically, the Village has been guided by
its zoning districts’ descriptions and that structures are supposed to be
subordinate to landscape, not vice versa. She also commented that it is
possible that our comparables’ regulations might not be the best and most
effective and that, therefore, the averages might not be very meaningful.
President Duhl pointed out that Winnetka is not planning undeveloped areas for
future development and is, therefore, not working with a blank slate. Winnetka
cannot start from scratch and must live with what is already in existence. He
noted to the extent that the Village starts to impose significantly larger
sideyards, it may adversely impact a home’s value.
Trustee Powell concurred with President Duhl’s assessment.
Trustee Greenough disagreed, saying that more restrictive regulation would over
time actually enhance property values by securing more separation between
houses. She pointed out that the new norm is to max out on new construction
without consideration for design character or regard for impact on neighbors or
neighborhood.
Manager Williams remarked that historically the Village has taken an
evolutionary approach, making incremental changes, rather than sweeping ones.
After a lengthy discussion, there was a consensus to move forward with a
formula approach, taking the determination of side yards out of the structure
of specific zoning districts. It was agreed to discuss final details at the
February study session as well as to address comments raised by Gary Frank.
3. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 8:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:05 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Powell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams.
4. Adjournment.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:06 p.m.
January 21, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 4, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 21, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Aquilino called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: President Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, and 38 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Aquilino led the group in pledging allegiance to the flag.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Pro Tem Aquilino announced that Item 6(b), a variation request for
139 DeWindt, had been removed from the Agenda at the applicant’s request.
4. Quorum
a)   February 4, 2003 Regular Meeting: All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
b)   February 11, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 7, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-3-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1195 Asbury - Adoption. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt this ordinance granting a
request for a variation from the Maximum Roofed Lot Coverage requirement to
permit the construction of a two-car detached garage. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell, Aquilino,
Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
b)   Ordinance No. M-4-2003 – Zoning Variation: 139 DeWindt - Adoption. As
noted earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda at the applicant’s
request.
c)   Ordinance No. M-5-2003 – Zoning Variation: 151 Fuller Ln. - Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt this ordinance
granting a request for a Maximum Building Size variation to permit the
construction of a one-story addition. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, Powell, Aquilino, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
d)   Ordinance No. M-6-2003 – Zoning Variation: 740 Sheridan Rd. - Adoption.
Trustee Brower recused himself from voting on this issue.
Attorney Janega reviewed some minor corrections to the text of the draft
document. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the
draft as outlined by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt this ordinance
granting a Rear Yard Setback variation to permit the construction of a two-
story addition. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Powell, Aquilino, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Brower (recused), President Duhl.
e)   Ordinance No. M-43-2002 – Special Use Permit and Variations: Winnetka
Community House, 620 Lincoln Ave. - Adoption. Manager Williams commented on
updated parking projections received by staff late in the afternoon. He also
reviewed options to mitigate the impact of parking that staff had been directed
to explore and their results. He said that a number of them might offer some,
small help for the parking problem, but that the best and most feasible way to
have a meaningful impact is to focus on the Community House staff and ways to
encourage them to park away from the facility. He concluded, however, saying
that although all efforts would have some impact, there will still be more
parking in front of homes extending further into the surrounding neighborhood.
Mr. Williams also reported that in response to a number of requests to consider
a stop sign at Lincoln and Pine Streets, the Village’s Traffic Engineer had
studied the intersection. The Engineer concluded that the necessary standards
are not met, and therefore, a stop sign is not warranted.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance. She
explained that the revisions to the parking provisions address life safety
issues while the building is under construction and also assure that the
ordinance preserves the Village Manager’s discretion and authority to designate
parking zones and permit classifications ender the parking provisions of the
Village Code. She also noted that the Village Code specifically requires
development agreements only for commercial and multi-family developments, and
added that the amendments would require that a development agreement be signed
prior to the issuance of any building permits, because of the impact of the
construction on the neighborhood.
Bob Barnes, 184 Green Bay Rd., Chairman of the WCH Building Committee, thanked
the Village for its efforts to achieve a workable solution. He also responded
to questions from the Trustees.
Neighbors who raised questions and concerns about the plans included: Paige
Sakelaris, 639 Lincoln; Cliff Wiener, 566 Lincoln; Andy Bullock, 612 Lincoln;
Jade Witt, and Terry Renn.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that he thought the entire process and dialogue
between the Community House and the neighbors had been excellent and that the
outcome was equally excellent.
Trustee Greenough commented that it is always difficult when the Village must
commingle residential and institutional uses. She expressed support for the
proposed solution, saying that, given the present circumstances, it is the most
sensitive possible.
Both Trustees Greenough and Aquilino expressed hope that the Village could find
some long-range solutions to the overall parking problems in the area.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance
as outlined by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, granting zoning variations and a Special Use Permit to the Winnetka
Community House to permit it to expand its facilities. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Presser,
Powell, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Brower (recused)
f)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2003 – E-1 Liquor License: Song O’Sixpence –
Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that the proposed ordinance would amend
the number of licenses provided in the Village Code to authorize a new Class E-
1 license to be issued to Song O’Sixpence.
Sarah Anderson, one of the applicants, spoke in support of the request.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Resolution No. R-3-2003 – Annual Motor Fuel Tax Resolution - Adoption. Mr.
Williams explained that, historically, the Village authorizes the allocation of
$125,000 from the MFT Fund towards street maintenance, which is a permitted use
of those funds. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt
the resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Powell, Aquilino, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays:
None. Absent: President Duhl.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1301 and 1302. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1301 in the amount of $222,189.95 and Warrant
List No. 1302 in the amount of $315,497.03. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
President Duhl.
b)   Review of Status: 411 Linden. Attorney Janega explained that the ordinance
granting a special use permit and zoning variation to the Winnetka Historical
Society to locate its offices and museum in the single-family residence and
garage at 411 Linden imposed numerous conditions. Among those conditions is one
providing for review in January 2003, to evaluate whether the conditions
imposed in the ordinance have provided adequate protection to the immediate
neighborhood from the potential negative impact of the special use. She noted
that the permit status in the Agenda Report had been updated, and that the
building permit for structural work in the basement had been issued on November
14, 2002, with construction work starting the following day, prior to the
expiration date set out in the ordinance. Commenting on the design issues in
the ordinance, she noted that the Historical Society had obtained the required
reviews and comments from the Design Review Board and Landmark Preservation
Commission and that the Village Council had adopted a resolution approving
exterior lights and signs. Ms. Janega also reported that the Historical Society
had designated a neighborhood liaison and provided requisite notices to both
the neighbors and the Department of Community Development. She added that there
were no reported complaints from the neighbors. She reviewed the remaining
conditions in the ordinance and stated that the Historical Society was in
compliance, but noted that many of the conditions did not yet apply, because
the building was not yet open to the public. She concluded by saying that the
only action required of the Village Council is to consider whether, at the
moment, the conditions are satisfactory and whether the Council wants to set
another date in the future for further review.
Nancy Judge, 534 Elder Ln., President of the Winnetka Historical Society,
commented on the status of development and operations and responded to
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Brower commented that the Society seems to have complied with the
conditions and their intent and proposed that the Council review the Society’s
progress again next year in January. The other Trustees concurred.
c)   Golf Course Drainage Bids – Cost Sharing. Manager Williams reviewed the
terms of an agreement with the Park District to split the cost of golf course
culvert replacement and pond work that would constitute the first improvements
associated with the West Hibbard Drainage Improvements.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to sign the agreement with the Park District agreeing to split the cost
for the drainage study and the first construction phase, with the Village’s
portion amounting to approximately $55,000. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Powell, Presser,
Aquilino, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
d)   Final Change Order: Street Rehabilitation Program. Manager Williams
reviewed the Change Order for the 2002 Street Rehabilitation Project. He noted
that these changes are primarily the result of the addition of three sections
of pavement that were not included in the original bid documents and
recommended approval.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the Change Order
in the amount of $25,594.96. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Powell, Aquilino, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser.
Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
e)   Quarterly Report: Capital Improvements. Manager Williams referred the
Trustees to the individual departments’ capital improvements reports included
in the Agenda packet. He noted that all of the major projects are completed, or
soon will be, and reminded the Council that some projects have been deferred
for budgetary reasons.
No Council action required.
f)   Quarterly Report: Power Cost Adjustment. Manager Williams reviewed the
Second Quarter Power Cost Adjustment Performance Report. He explained that the
power cost adjustment is a mechanism for recovering the difference between the
actual cost of power paid to the IMEA and a base cost that is used in the
budget. He reported that the adjustment formula is working as designed, using
the seasonal changes in power cost to recover most of the lost revenue and
using the adjustment billed to users to augment that process.
No Council action required.
g)   Quarterly Report: Village Yards Status. Manager Williams reported that the
Village Yards construction project is progressing very nicely, although
somewhat behind the original schedule. He noted that the current cost estimate
is about $57,000 below the original total project cost.
No Council action required.
Public Comment and Questions. Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., suggested that the
public comment portion of the meeting be moved to earlier in the meeting.
President Pro Tem Aquilino responded that it had been moved to the end because
so many residents with slated items on the Agenda have waited a long time to be
placed on the Agenda and the Council decided that those individuals should be
heard first:
Irv Footlick, 1548 Tower Rd., encouraged the Village to explore the possibility
of creating additional parking over the railroad cut.
Baird Smart, 112 Church Rd., expressed concern over the general appearance of
the Indian Hill area, in particular the old G&W site currently leased by Land
Rover.
9. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that the Preliminary Budget for the
2003-04 fiscal year is now available at the Library as well as on the Village’s
website. He also announced the upcoming budget meeting dates.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported on a recent meeting of the Chamber of
Commerce, and Trustee Woodbury reported on his recent attendance at a meeting
of the Historical Society.
10. Appointments.
President Pro Tem Aquilino suggested the appointment of John Thomas to the
Zoning Board of Appeals. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, so
moved. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
She also suggested the appointment of James Cavanaugh to the Design Review
Board. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, so moved. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Powell
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino Brower, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None.
Absent: President Duhl. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:30
p.m.
12. The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:20 p.m.
Present: President Pro Tem Aquilino and Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser and Woodbury. Absent: President Duhl. Also present, Village Manager
Douglas Williams and Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Woodbury, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: President
Duhl. The meeting adjourned at 10:21 p.m.
