Recording Secretary
February 4, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 18, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 4, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7: 32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Stephen Presser.
Absent: Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director
of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Trustees-elect David Abell and David
Webster, and 27 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance and a moment of
silence in tribute to the members of the Shuttle Columbia crew.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   February 6, 2003, Special Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   February 11, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
c)   February 13, 2003 Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 14, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   January 21, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2003 – E-1 Liquor License: Song O’Sixpence – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained a proposed amendment to the introduced ordinance
changing the number of E and E-1 licenses noted. Trustee Presser, seconded by
Trustee Brower, moved to amend the ordinance as proposed. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the proposed
ordinance, as amended, changing the number of liquor licenses provided for in
the Village Code to authorize a new Class E-1 license to be issued to Song
O’Sixpence. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Woodbury.
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:38 p.m.
b)   Ordinance M-8-2003 – Special Use Permit and Zoning Variation: North Shore
Country Day School, 310 Green Bay Rd. - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a
request for a Special Use Permit and Maximum Building Size variation to permit
the school to expand its existing school structure by constructing a second
story addition. He noted that the campus buildings are already a legal
nonconforming use. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that, while expressing concern about
traffic and parking, the Plan Commission had recommended approval of the
Special Use. He added that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted in favor of
both the Special Use and the variation, and that the Design Review Board had
also recommended that the project be approved.
Tom Doar, Head of School, North Shore Country Day School, reviewed the School’s
process in developing this expansion plan. He stressed the School’s need for
improved science facilities to accommodate current, interactive teaching
methods as well as the need for an overall upgrade of campus facilities to
address security issues and make the campus ADA compliant. He addressed
concerns about traffic and parking by saying that the School is committed to
resolving some of the parking issues and discussions are ongoing with the
neighbors in an effort to reach a mutually acceptable solution to current
problems. Mr. Doar referred to the School’s traffic and parking analysis
performed by KLOA, noting that their conclusion was that the expansion would
have little or no impact on existing traffic patterns because the School has no
plans for expansion of the student population.
Eric Penny, the project’s architect, reviewed site schemes and elevations of
the proposed expansion. He explained that the addition will add a second story
to the central portion of the buildings located at the southwest corner of the
campus. A smaller addition on the south side adds a lobby, stairs and an
elevator, which will tie building access together. The total additional height
is 11 feet and the façade is mostly glass. A large oak tree along Forest will
remain, which will help to obscure the view from the west. The four trees now
on the south side of the building, facing Elder Lane, will be replaced by two
trees. The lobby and elevator addition will occupy the area where the other
trees were.
Phil Goss, 266 Forest, addressed matters relating to traffic. He pointed out
that the School’s traffic study was limited to traffic flow on Green Bay Road
and did not consider Forest, Elder, or other surrounding streets that are
greatly impacted by the School. He referred to the ongoing discussions between
the School and the neighbors about traffic problems in these areas and asked
the Council to consider commitments by the School to resolve these problems,
particularly their commitment to get their traffic onto their property. Mr.
Goss expressed concerns about recent changes on Forest, including a change in
the signs on the east side of Forest from “no parking” signs to “loading zone”
signs, the addition of a concrete walkway on the parkway at the street and the
change of Forest to one-way traffic northbound during peak hours, with traffic
being monitored by employees of the School. He noted that the changes were made
without input from the neighbors. Mr. Goss also expressed strong opposition to
the Village Engineer’s suggestion that traffic problems might be resolved by
widening a stretch of Forest for a pick up/drop off lane, and stated that the
School should shift traffic to its own property and should not take up Village
green space.
Trustee Presser inquired whether Mr. Goss’s concerns were focused on traffic
from the addition or on the ongoing traffic problem.
Mr. Goss replied that, while he applauds the School on the proposed design, he
feels that the traffic problems need to be resolved as part of the project’s
approval process.
Rick Duran, 342 Forest, agreed that the proposed design is essentially a good
one. He requested, however, that the School get its traffic off of Forest and
Elder and that it not use either street for construction access purposes under
any circumstances. He referred to a letter from the School committing to keep
drop off/pick up on its property and requested that consideration of the
proposed construction be tabled until the School actually presents a solution
to the parking and traffic problems. He argued that any such solution might
impact the final site plan and design for the new construction.
John Roscof, 258 Forest concurred with the views of the preceding speakers, as
did Mark Haller, 690 Elder Lane.
President Duhl explained that, with projects of this type, the Village requires
a traffic study in an attempt to determine whether the new construction will
have an impact on traffic by increasing utilization of the facility. He pointed
out that in this case the School’s project does not in and of itself exacerbate
the traffic and parking problem. He said that Messrs. Goss, Duran, and Roscof
are telling the Council that there is already a problem that the School needs
to address and are asking the Council to withhold its approval of the proposed
project until that problem is resolved.
Mr. Doar reiterated that the School is committed to improving the existing
traffic problems, but that this will take time. He asked that the Council act
on the merits of the proposed request for Special Use and variation and
consider the negotiation of the traffic issues as a separate and distinct
issue.
Trustee Presser asked Mr. Doar if he was aware of any changes to the proposed
design that might improve the traffic problems. Mr. Doar said that he was not.
Trustee Woodbury inquired as to the anticipated construction time. Mr. Doar
responded that they hoped to begin construction this summer, with completion in
approximately 15 to 24 months, depending upon whether the project had to be
staged.
Trustee Aquilino asked Mr. Doar how long he expected it to take to reach a
resolution to the traffic problems. Mr. Doar repeated his commitment to resolve
the problems, but said that it would take thoughtful study over several months.
President Duhl remarked that traffic is an ongoing problem in the Village and
that there are no perfect solutions to be found. He expressed hope that the
School and the neighbors would be able to find a mutually agreeable compromise
solution as to what is best and feasible, and acknowledged that there would
likely be differences of opinion as to what is best. He asked Mr. Doar whether,
recognizing that there would not be a “perfect” solution, the School would
object to the Village’s incorporating a provision into the ordinance that
requires the traffic issue be addressed. Mr. Doar expressed no objection to
that concept.
President Duhl also addressed the concern expressed by the neighbors that the
Village would install a third lane on Forest by assuring them that the Village
would not unilaterally make such a decision. He said he hoped the parties could
come up with a solution without the assistance of the Council.
In response to a question from Trustee Woodbury, Attorney Janega explained that
a development agreement would be required and that it would include all of the
standard conditions used in other, similar developments.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Resolution No. R-2-2003 – Amending NIPSTA Agreement - Adoption. Attorney
Janega explained that Resolution R-2-2003 amends the Village’s resolution
authorizing participation in the Northern Illinois Public Safety Training
Academy to designate the Village’s representatives by position rather than by
name of the individuals holding the office.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Powell,
Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-4-2003 – ILEAS Mutual Aid Agreement. – Adoption. Attorney
Janega explained that the proposed resolution would authorize the Village’s
participation in the new Illinois Law Enforcement Alarm System Mutual Aid Plan,
enabling the Village to receive and/or provide emergency aid and assistance.
Participation also would enable the Village to qualify for additional revenues
that may be made available through the Illinois Emergency Management Agency.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-5-2003 – Private Street Vacation: 152/164 DeWindt Rd. &
1080 Sunset. - Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the proposed resolution
would approve a plat of vacation and dedicate utility easements at 152 and 164
DeWindt Road and 1080 Sunset Avenue. He explained that the private road is
unimproved, does not provide access to any of the three properties, and is not
an optimal location for the utility lines, because it meanders back and forth
across the lot lines.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Aquilino,
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-6-2003 – Miscellaneous Fees. - Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed the resolution, which proposes three amendments to the Village’s
general fee resolution: (1) the addition of a $500 fee for the new Class E-
1 liquor license; (2) an increase in the impoundment and towing fee from $75 to
$200, along with the lifting of the $750 cap on the collateral fee for release
of impounded or towed vehicles; and (3) the addition of a $500 charge for
street cleaning to allow the Village to recover its costs when it is necessary
to clean streets around construction sites or after special events.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Woodbury,
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2003 – 12 Month Review of Design Guidelines; Public
Hearing & Introduction of Related Zoning Amendments. President Duhl opened the
Public Hearing on the proposed amendments.
Mr. Norkus explained that in May 2001, the Village Council requested that the
Design Review Board report back to the Council its experience and
recommendations after applying the interim design guidelines for a period of
time with an eye toward adopting the Design Guidelines in a final form. He
noted that, since adopting the guidelines, the Design Review Board has applied
them to approximately 60 separate applications for Certificates of
Appropriateness for a wide variety of projects.
Mr. Norkus then reviewed the Boards’ comments and its suggested modifications
to the Zoning Ordinance and Sign Ordinance. Changes to the Zoning Ordinance
include amending the C-1 Zoning District minimum setback and the height of
accessory buildings for institutional uses in residential districts. Changes to
the Sign Code include amending the provisions regarding projecting signs, the
threshold for a sign permit and design review, and window sign area limits. He
added that the DRB had commented favorably on the appearance and use of rigid
awnings on a non-retractable framework, but that both the Public Works Director
and the Fire Chief had recommended that retractable awnings continue to be the
only kind of awnings allowed over public sidewalks.
Trustees Woodbury and Greenough expressed the hope that the Fire Chief and
Public Works Director would reconsider their objections
Attorney Janega noted that the draft of Ordinance MC-3-2003 does not include
changes to the height limitations for accessory buildings in residential zoning
districts. She explained that, since virtually all special use applications in
residential zoning districts also require zoning variations, the Council would
retain more control over the height of accessory buildings for institutional
uses if it continues to consider such requests on a case by case basis under
the current provisions.
President Duhl called for comments from the audience. There being none, he
closed the Public Hearing and in light of the lateness of the hour, suggested
that the Trustees resume discussion of this item at the Special Meeting
scheduled for Thursday evening. The Council concurred with this suggestion.
7. Old Business.
a)   Sidewalk Policy. President Duhl explained that the policy to be discussed
had been drafted to reflect Council’s previous discussion and to create a
process that best reflects experience and the majority view of the Trustees
that decisions about the installation of sidewalks should ultimately be
deferred to the abutting neighbors.
Manager Williams reviewed the proposed policy, saying that it would establish
specific thresholds of support needed from the abutting property owners before
a new sidewalk is constructed on a public street. He explained that there are
three primary components: (1) a preliminary petition indicating some level of
neighborhood interest; (2) interactive neighborhood meetings with Village
staff; and (3) a final petition by immediately abutting neighbors.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, expressed objection to the drafted policy, stating that
it does not address concerns other than those of the abutting neighbors. He
referred to the Comprehensive Plan, which encourages the installation of
sidewalks. He suggested the addition of a phrase that would provide for
adequate public comment and allow for Council consideration if broader public
concerns are raised.
President Duhl explained that this is not going to be an ordinance with the
force of law, but rather a guiding procedure to deal with requests for
sidewalks and pointed out that the Comprehensive Plan is just an outline. He
said that issues of safety and children are legitimately raised each and every
time a sidewalk is proposed but, historically, the Council has voted in keeping
with the wishes of the abutting homeowners. He added that the proposed policy
does not deny that differences of opinion exist or that there are other issues
to consider, but it does recognize the historically prevailing sentiment of the
Trustees.
President Duhl also pointed out that, some time ago, the Village established a
sidewalk program that identified and prioritized streets where sidewalks should
be considered. When the first street on the list, Church Street, was considered
by the Council, the proposed sidewalk was not built.
Mr. Kates pressed for the policy to include some statement making it clear
that, where concerns go beyond the neighborhood, the Council would be available
for discussion.
President Duhl noted that to do so would eviscerate any policy and simply
maintain the status quo. He said that the Trustees recognize that there are
other issues to consider, but that the draft policy articulates the position of
the sitting Council. He voiced the hope that the policy would encourage
neighborhood meetings and smooth the process of developing a mutually
acceptable resolution without the intervention of the Council.
Manager Williams pointed out that the Council could except any streets it chose
from the general policy and identify them as primary streets, requiring that
the consideration of a sidewalk on any of those designated streets be brought
before the Council.
Trustee Woodbury noted that both the Comprehensive Plan and the Caucus
acknowledge the ambiguity in the community over this issue and said that the
Council is simply trying to find a way to promote constructive dialogue in
order to facilitate the construction of sidewalks where appropriate.
Mark Karinsky, 1088 Pine St., expressed his belief that sidewalks are a
community issue, not an individual residential issue. Saying that abutting
neighbors should have a strong say, he added that community considerations may
outweigh their position and may need to be evaluated. He said that, as drafted,
the policy empowers a few to the detriment of the community as a whole. Mr.
Karinsky urged the Council to incorporate some kind of an escape clause.
Trustee Presser voiced general support for the policy, saying that the
percentages could be adjusted.
Trustee Aquilino expressed overall discomfort with the policy, saying that she
wouldn’t want a percentage of abutting homeowners to control the final
decision.
Trustee Powell said that he was in favor of some sort of structured policy, but
doesn’t think that abutting neighbors should have sole say.
Trustee Woodbury indicated that he is generally in favor of sidewalks but very
much in favor of some sort of mechanism that gets a constructive dialogue going
among the affected neighbors before the issue comes before the Council.
Trustee Greenough also expressed a predisposition in favor of sidewalks, but
said that she is in favor of the policy. She said that she would be
uncomfortable forcing a sidewalk on any resident unless there is an
overwhelming need.
Trustee Brower said he was not comfortable with a process that sets minimums
and maximums.
In view of the lateness of the hour, President Duhl suggested that the Trustees
resume their discussion on this issue during the Special Meeting scheduled for
Thursday evening. The Trustees agreed.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1303 and 1304. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1303 in the amount of $1,014,114.01 and
Warrant List No. 1304 in the amount of $1,224,585.61. Trustee Presser seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Request from Girl Scouts. Manager Williams reviewed a request from the
Winnetka Girl Scouts for permission to sell cookies on the sidewalks in the
downtown area. Pursuant to Chapter 12.04.070 of the Village Code, only the
Council has the authority to permit any public street or sidewalk to be used
for commercial purposes.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Village Hall Electric Bids. Mr. McInturff reviewed bids for the rewiring
of Village Hall, where much of the wiring is original and out of date and in
need of upgrading. He recommended that the bid be awarded MKD Electric in the
amount of $259,171.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Bid No. 02-023A: Cable Installation. Mr. Williams reviewed the bids and
the bidding process for labor, materials (except cable), and equipment required
for installation of 600 volt and 1240 volt cable in existing conduits,
manholes, and splice boxes for a period of one year. He informed the Council
that, despite some problems encountered in the bidding process, he had been
advised that the bid documents give the Village the flexibility to waive minor
technicalities and that the Village could proceed. He recommended that the bid
be awarded to Archon Construction Co., Inc., at the unit prices stated, not to
exceed $131,075.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reminded the Trustees of the dates for the
upcoming budget meetings.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported on his recent attendance at the Home,
Health & Garden Show sponsored by the Chambers of Commerce of Winnetka,
Northfield, and Wilmette.
Trustee Aquilino commented on attending the Winnetka Intergovernmental Meeting,
at which representatives of governmental bodies of various Villages get
together to exchange information.
Trustee Powell reported on his recent attendance at a RED Center meeting. He
announced that the award from the State had paid for the building, the land,
and much of the electronics for the Center, ultimately reducing the cost to the
Village for RED Center. He also commented on a recent NWMC Legislative
Committee meeting at which a legislative agenda was discussed.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Woodbury
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino Brower, Greenough, Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:45 p.m.
13. The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:30 p.m.
Present: President Duhl and Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell,
Presser and Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas
Williams and Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Woodbury, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:31 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
February 6, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: February 18, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7: 35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Bryan
McInturff, Finance Director Ed McKee, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, representatives from the Illinois Municipal Electric
Agency, and two persons in the audience.
2. Meeting with IMEA Regarding Future Power Supply.
Manager Williams explained that IEMA, of which Winnetka is a member and from
whom Winnetka purchases power, is proposing to move forward as a group with the
purchase of a coal-fired power plant in Kentucky. He said he expected staff to
present Council with a final recommendation by mid March. He then introduced
Ron Earl, CEO of IMEA.
Mr. Earl introduced the staff members who accompanied him and reviewed IMEA’s
operations. He also presented an historical analysis of Winnetka’s costs. Mr.
Earl presented IMEA’s position that it is important to exercise its option to
buy into this new power plant for three key reasons: (1) an anticipated
baseload capacity shortage; (2) a fundamental change in the primary fuel source
from coal to gas; and (3) changes in FERC rules that will change how electrons
are delivered. He indicated that all of these factors would combine to create a
very unstable market. In closing, Mr. Earl solicited the Council’s support for
the project and approval of an extension to the existing power sales contract.
He indicated that IMEA would like to complete the participation agreement by
the end of June.
Other members of IMEA’s team made brief presentations to the Trustees and
responded to questions.
IMEA attorney Troy Fodor circulated a draft amendment to the Village’s
agreement with IMEA, which extends the length of the agreement to allow
sufficient time to bring the new plant on-line and to retire the bonds that
will be issued to finance the purchase.
President Duhl expressed some concern about the reliability of the price
forecasts upon which IMEA’s projections are based. He noted that the viability
of the whole proposal is based on what the cost of energy is going to be.
Rick Soderholm, IMEA’s consultant from R.W. Beck, acknowledged that there would
be some cost risk involved.
Manager Williams enumerated the things that Winnetka has requested from IMEA
before staff makes a recommendation to the Council.
3. Ordinance MC-3-2003 - Continued Discussion Design Guidelines (continued from
February 4, 2003 regular Council meeting) - Introduction.
Picking up from the discussion of Tuesday evening, Mr. Norkus reviewed the
suggested changes to the text of the Interim Design Guidelines.
Noting that there had been no public comment at Tuesday’s meeting, President
Duhl suggested that Attorney Janega review with the Trustees the proposed
amendments to the Zoning Ordinance, the Building Code requirements pertaining
to signs, and the Design Guidelines. After reviewing the proposed amendments,
Ms. Janega again recommended against changing the accessory building height,
stating that retaining the current provisions affords the Council more
flexibility.
Trustees Woodbury and Greenough also asked again that staff revisit the issue
of fixed vs. retractable awnings before finalizing the ordinance. Mr. Norkus
stated that he and Public Works Director Steven Saunders would get together to
work out language needed to address staff concerns about fixed awning so they
could be allowed.
Trustee Woodbury also recommended changes to setback requirements to allow for
the alignment of the front building lines of adjacent buildings and to provide
for appropriate sidewalk widths. Noting the Council’s experience with the
development proposals for 518 Winnetka, he added that a separate standard may
be needed in the Indian Hill commercial district.
Manager Williams directed the Council’s attention to the decorative street
lights in the Guidelines, and requested that the Council also identify a street
light fixture for residential streets. He noted that, although the Guidelines
technically apply only to commercial and multifamily areas, the same decorative
light fixture is now under consideration for Tower Road and that designating a
specific street light fixture for residential streets would assist the Village
staff in planning for improvements.
Attorney Janega then commented on the procedural aspects of implementing the
Guidelines. She explained that the Design Review Board (DRB) is required to
adopt guidelines and that the guidelines define the scope of the discretion
being delegated to the DRB by the Council. She recommended that, after the
Council adopts the various text amendments, the Council should approve the
Guidelines by motion and direct the DRB to adopt them, so that they will govern
DRB proceedings while reserving final authority and discretionary flexibility
for the Council.
Trustee Woodbury moved to introduce Ordinance MC-3-2003, seconded by Trustee
Greenough. By voice vote, the motion carried.
4. Continued Discussion Sidewalk Policy (continued from February 4, 2003
regular Council meeting).
Manager Williams distributed a map showing those sidewalks identified by staff
as being on the list for installation in response to Council’s determination
that a formal, affirmative Sidewalk Program be established.
President Duhl again explained that, because the Council did not approve the
first sidewalk in that program, and had not approved subsequent sidewalk
proposals due to the objections of abutting neighbors, the Sidewalk Program has
lapsed. He stated that, consequently, the Council is now reactive rather than
proactive when it comes to the installation of sidewalks, but noted that this
has also proven to be an unsatisfactory process. He expressed his hope that the
Trustees would be able to craft a policy that encourages neighborhoods to work
together before coming to the Council.
Trustees Brower, Presser, and Powell reaffirmed their opposition to a
unilateral sidewalk program whereby the Village would identify and install a
sidewalk regardless of the neighbors’ position.
Trustee Aquilino brought up the issue of overriding community concern vs.
private property rights. She supported the concept of including a statement in
the policy that would allow any resident to bring a concern about the
installation of a sidewalk, for or against, directly to the Council.
Attorney Janega observed that the Council appears to be discussing a procedures
rather than a policy, and that some of the concerns may be arising because the
draft before the Council establishes a procedure for only one type of request,
i.e., one initiated by abutting property owners.
After extensive discussion, there was general agreement among the Trustees that
25% of the neighborhood was a good threshold for initiating an analysis by
Village staff, and 66% was an appropriate threshold for installing a sidewalk,
but when support falls between 30-66%, the issue be brought before the Council.
Manager Williams suggested that, in the future, residents within 250 feet of
the proposed sidewalk be notified of any Council discussion on the issue.
President Duhl asked Attorney Janega to redraft a procedure in keeping with
Council’s discussion.
5. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:20 p.m.
February 11, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: March 4, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Kathy Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Trustees-
elect David Abell and David Webster, Park District Director, Liza McElroy,
members of the Park District Board and 28 persons in the audience.
2. Side Yards Analysis.
Mr. D’Onofrio briefly reviewed the history of this issue.
Mr. Norkus then reviewed staff’s study and analysis of the issue, noting that,
overall, Winnetka’s current total side yard requirements meet or exceed the
average of comparable communities, except in instances where a lot is zoned R-
4 or R-5. In these zoning districts, where the required side yards amount to
20% of the lot width, resulting sideyards are significantly less than those
required in comparable communities. He reported that, due to the prevalence of
lots ranging from 60 to 75 feet across several zoning districts, staff
recommends that total side yard requirements be modified to be uniform across
all five single family residential zoning districts. This recommendation
includes continuing to require, at a minimum, side yards of 6 feet and 8 feet,
amounting to 28% on the smallest, or 50-ft. lots, but increasing total side
yard requirements to 25% of lot width for all other lots less than 100 ft.
wide. Staff’s recommendation for lots 100 ft wide or greater is that they be
subject to total side yard requirements of 30%.
With regard to minimum side yard requirements, Mr. Norkus reported staff’s
recommendation that they be modified to reflect lot width only without
reference to zoning district. Specifically that the minimum side yard be 6
feet, plus 10% of the lot width in excess of 60 feet, provided that any lot
greater than 100 feet in width shall provide a minimum side yard of 12 feet.
In response to a question from Trustee Aquilino, Mr. Norkus responded that
this, in fact, would make a number of lots in the Village nonconforming. He
also reminded the Trustees that the front yard setbacks would continue to
differ from district to district.
Trustee Greenough expressed concern that these changes are not significant
enough and are an invitation to builders to build out beyond the articulated
purpose of the zoning district.
Trustee Presser indicated that he could support the recommendations but was
somewhat troubled by creating more nonconformities, given that the more
nonconformities there are, the greater the number of variations requests that
have to be considered.
Gary Frank, 723 Elm Street, a local architect, objected to the recommended
changes saying that they will have the dramatic effect of pushing houses
further into the backyard particularly on the smaller lots
Trustees Woodbury, Powell and Brower agreed with Trustee Presser.
Hearing a general consensus from the Trustees to proceed with implementing
staff’s recommendations, President Duhl indicated that a public hearing would
be scheduled for March.
3. Discussion: Park District Request to Serve Liquor at Golf Course.
Mary Garrison, President of the Park District Board presented the Board’s
request that the Village Council reconsider licensing the Park District to
serve beer at the golf course in areas where food is currently served, i.e.,
the clubhouse and the 10th tee shack. She explained that the Park District is
competing heavily with other area golf courses for a limited number of golfers
and that being able to serve beer would help to make the facility more
attractive to players and help it to remain a self-supporting operation. She
said that the intent it to have service be available to golfers only and
reported that the Park District has experienced no problems with private events
at which alcohol is served. She mentioned that while the Park Board is at
present only considering the service of beer, they would like to have the
option to have full liquor service. In closing, Ms Garrison expressed her
belief that since this issue was last considered both state and local laws have
changed, as well as the tenor of the community.
Ian Sherman, Vice President of the Park Board, echoed Ms. Garrison’s comments
in support of their petition. In response to a question from President Duhl, he
indicated that he expected that the latest the clubhouse would ever be open for
the service of beer would be 8:00 p.m.
President Duhl noted that this is a sensitive issue and that while views may
have changed, the Council has to proceed cautiously.
Judy Blunck, 360 Green Bay Road, Molly D’Esposito, 640 Blackthorn, and Barbara
Meador, 1152 Oak Street, spoke in support of the Park Board’s request.
Bill Murphy, 112 Fuller Ln., Park Board Member and Chair of the Golf Committee
reviewed the facility’s recent revenues, noting that they are falling. He said
that he believes that not being able to serve a beer after a round of golf
places the Club at a competitive disadvantage with other courses. If revenues
continue to fall, the Club will be unable to maintain its facilities up to par
with neighboring courses and fall competitively further behind.
Zane Smith, Park District Commissioner concurred with Mr. Murphy’s assessment.
Joe Dooley, former Park Board member, noted that the clubhouse is not a
conducive to becoming a tavern or hangout. He pointed out that country clubs
have managed for many years to successfully and safely provide liquor service
in areas where there are children.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce St., voiced strong opposition to the proposal, saying
that it had been debated at length in 1998 and there have been no substantive
changes of fact since then. He cited the proximity to schools and the already
existing heavy traffic as invitations to a serious accident. He asked that the
Council table the request.
Curt Kruger, 1215 Elm, cited the previous Council’s unanimous rejection of the
idea, saying that the Park District is trying to impose its desires on a
community that has different priorities.
President Duhl pointed out that in 1998 the issue had been one of convenience,
whereas this time the issue is one of economics. He explained that the Council
could set the matter for public hearing, reject the proposal, or decide that it
doesn’t want to take any action without input from the Caucus. He pointed out
that the latter course would delay the Park District by a year.
After a discussion among the Trustees, there was a general consensus to
schedule the issue for a public hearing and publicize it well to determine the
level of interest in the community.
President Duhl suggested that the issue be set for public hearing at the second
meeting in April after the seating of the new Council and the publication of
the next Winnetka Report.
4. Gary Frank’s Zoning Analysis.
Mr. Frank presented slides illustrating his request for changes to the new
zoning regulations regarding changes to the Zoning Ordinance relating to roof
heights, floor-to-floor heights, and floor construction dimensions, as outlined
in his letter to the Trustees. He urged the Council to amend the regulations to
reflect industry standards.
Speaking from his experience as an architect in the Village, Mr. Frank used
drawings to illustrate his contentions that:
a)   Having only a 10 ft first floor limits the flexibility for good design not
only from an exterior standpoint, but also from an interiors standpoint.
b)   The Code should be amended to allow for doors opening to a deck, without
being charged for GFA.
c)   The Code should be amended to allow area wells to project no more than
2’9” from the property line, into a required side or front yard setback.
He made the following suggestions:
a)   Increase the floor-to-floor height and the roof height by 1 ft in the R2
and R3 districts.
b)   Increase the floor-to-floor height and the roof height by 1 ft in the R4
and R5 districts. (Based on the discussion of pre-cuts vs. non-pre-cuts.
c)   Allow the floor structure dimension, i.e., joist, to be no greater than
17” and only the actual dimension of the floor joist and plywood dimension be
used.
Mr. Frank represented that the total change of height of 1’2” to 1’5” would
hardly be noticeable and pointed out that Council decided at the last minute to
lower the heights by one foot despite previously agreeing to 21 feet in the R-
5 and R-4 districts and 22 feet in the R-3 and R-2 districts.
Trustee Woodbury expressed initial support for the requested additional foot in
height, but noted that the goal of the zoning changes was not only to control
size but also to simplify enforcement.
In view of the fact that the Trustees had an lengthy Executive Session ahead of
them, President Duhl suggested that discussion of this issue be deferred until
next Tuesday evening.
5. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, Powell, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened
into Executive Session at 9:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:10 p.m. Present: President
Duhl and Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
6. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Powell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:11 p.m.
February 18, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 4, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 18, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser, and
Ed Woodbury . Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Director of Water &
Electric Bryan McInturff, Trustee-elect David Webster, and 27 persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   February 20, 2003, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Presser, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 27, 2003, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
c)   March 4, 2003 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Powell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 4, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Powell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   February 6, 2003, Special Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-8-2003 – Special Use Permit and Zoning Variation: North Shore
Country Day School, 310 Green Bay Rd. - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed
extensive revisions to the draft ordinance adding factual recitals and findings
to reflect the Council’s discussions at the time of introduction and to support
the relief granted. She explained that the property as it exists now is a
lawful special use and that the proposed use does not contemplate expanding
enrollment but is designed to better serve the current student population. She
pointed to conditions imposed, particularly the requirement that a development
agreement be signed by the School.
President Duhl noted that there were two issues raised by neighbors at the last
meeting for Council’s consideration. The first issue is the perceived
unilateral change to the existing traffic patterns instituted by the School in
the fall. The second is the request that a mutually agreeable long-term plan
for resolving traffic and parking problems in the neighborhood around the
School be developed before the Council approves the proposed ordinance.
Trustees Presser, Powell, and Greenough expressed some concern about tightly
linking the resolution of the traffic and parking issues to the approval of the
development agreement called for in the proposed ordinance.
Trustee Aquilino expressed her view that this is a timely opportunity to
address the parking and traffic issues.
Attorney Janega suggested that language be added to the ordinance reserving the
right of the Council to intervene and impose a solution pertaining to student
drop-off and pick-up areas prior to taking final action on the development
agreement if the parties are unable to reach agreement. There was a consensus
among the Trustees that this would maintain Council’s desired flexibility.
Maureen Toomy, Director of Finance at NSCDS, addressed the traffic changes
implemented by the School and the Police Department, citing children’s safety
as the primary concern.
Greg Raines, an attorney for NSCDS, urged passage of the draft ordinance,
saying that a discussion of the existing traffic concerns is not appropriately
linked to the special use permit. He indicated that the school has already
begun to plan how to address neighbors’ concerns.
President Duhl pointed out that a building permit will not be issued until the
development agreement is signed, and prior to that, the Council needs to be
able to see that a good faith attempt to deal with the traffic issues has been
made by both parties.
Robin Thomas, 638 Elder, stated that the traffic changes made on Elder and
Forest early in the school year have proved to be a small inconvenience of
short duration and that they have, in fact, greatly improved the traffic flow.
Tom Flickinger, 274 Ridge, argued against linking the expansion of the new
facility to the parking and traffic issues, saying that it will be the students
who suffer from any resultant delay by being unable to take advantage of the
new science center. James Colley, 302 Forest, concurred.
Others speaking in support of linking the issues included, Philip Goss, 266
Forest; Frances Kirby, 296 Forest; Chrstine Botica, 291 Forest; Mark Haller,
690 Elder; Lou Ramsdell, 334 Forest; and Rick Duran, 342 Forest.
Trustee Woodbury supported the draft language, saying that it provides an
opportunity for the parties to establish a working dialogue. He moved to amend
the ordinance as outlined by Attorney Janega and to reflect this evening’s
discussion. Trustee Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Trustee Brower recused himself.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance
amending the Special Use Permit and granting a maximum Building Size variation
for North Shore Country Day School to permit construction of a second story
addition to the existing school, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and
Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None. Trustee Brower recused himself.
Before moving on to the next item, President Duhl reminded the parties that the
Council’s view is supposed to be from the community at large and may not
represent either party. He said it is the Council’s responsibility to decide
what is in the community’s best interest even though that decision may not
please either party. He expressed his hope that the parties will have
constructive discussions and come to a mutually satisfactory agreement on their
own rather than having the Council intervene.
b)   Ordinance No. M-7-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1140 Chatfield - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum Building Size and Setback
variations to permit the construction of additions and attaching of an existing
detached garage. ZBA voted in favor of approval of project. He explained that
the variation is required largely as a result of the unique nature of the
property in that it fronts on three public streets.
The applicant, John Dolby, answered questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. M-9-2003 – Landmark Designation: 94 Mary Street. Carter
Page, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission, reviewed the
Commission’s recommendation that this property be awarded local landmark
status. He reported that the house had been rated as “unique” with a score of
82.8 points on the System for the Evaluation of Landmarks.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution No. R-7-2003 – Emergency Operations Plan. – Adoption. Chief
Colpaert explained that this resolution would adopt and approve the updated
Emergency Operations Plan for the Village.
Manager Williams thanked Chief Colpaert for his extensive work on this project.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
a)   Sidewalk Guidelines. Attorney Janega briefly reviewed the history of this
issue. She explained that the current revised draft provides a more
comprehensive statement of policy and procedural guidelines for citizen
requests for the installation of sidewalks. She noted that the revised
guidelines provide a background statement, a statement of the purpose of the
guidelines and a general policy statement. The preliminary statements
acknowledge the Comprehensive Plan and reiterate the Council’s discretion over
determining when and where sidewalks will be constructed. The preliminary
policy statement is followed by procedural guidelines for processing requests
for the construction of sidewalks. The guidelines address the three basic
methods for initiating such projects:
1)   At the initiative of the Village itself;
2)   At the request of adjoining property owners; and
3)   At the request of any other citizen.
Ms. Janega explained that the guidelines for handling requests from adjoining
property owners set certain thresholds of support required for the initial
consideration of the project, automatic approval of the project, and
consideration of the request by the Village Council. Requests initiated by non-
adjoining owners would, if the Council chooses, lead to staff meetings with the
owners of adjoining property and owners of property located within 250 feet of
the proposed sidewalk route to determine whether there is local support for the
proposed sidewalk. The guideline that applies to this category of requests also
lays out examples of alternative procedures that the Council, in the exercise
of its discretion, could apply.
President Duhl addressed the question of the proposed Pine Street sidewalk. He
stated that no decision had been made as yet, but that there are enough
signatures on the petitions, including those from Pine Street residents, to
proceed with the process. In the spring, proposed routes will be identified and
neighborhood meetings will be held before the matter comes back to the Council.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, complimented the Council on its revisions to the
guidelines, saying that the thresholds are more flexible and enable a
neighborhood to make their concerns known.
Sharon King, 1134 Pine St., commented on the guidelines and expressed concern
about how they pertain to Pine Street. She pointed out that a Pine Street
resident did not initiate the request for a sidewalk on Pine Street. She voiced
her opinion that the first step should be that adjoining property owners be
advised of a request and then meet together before the process goes forward.
She pointed out that the Village not only needs the money to build a sidewalk,
it must be responsible for the maintenance of the ones already throughout the
community, some of which she believes are in serious need of repair.
Jessica Tucker, 850 Locust St., commented that the Council is supposed to have
the view of the community at large and not be focused on particular property
owners. She referred to documents, including the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka
2020, that clearly state that the Village should complete its pedestrian
network as well as to the fact that the Caucus has recommended the installation
of sidewalks. These, she said, represent the collective voice of the residents
speaking as to what to do and the guidelines should promote that goal,
encouraging and promoting consensus rather than discouraging it. She expressed
her belief that the community as a whole should be deciding on the installation
of sidewalks, not just the abutting neighbors, and suggested a simple majority
should be sufficient to implement a sidewalk.
Janice McVicker, 1161 Spruce St., stated her belief that Pine and Spruce
streets should be added to the list of those streets that the Council in the
sole exercise of its discretion determine whether or not to install a sidewalk
based upon community need, public safety, and Village finances.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees voiced general support for the revised
guidelines. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the
guidelines. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
In closing President Duhl reiterated the fact that notwithstanding any language
in the guidelines, the Village Council retains the right to install a sidewalk
wherever it feels one is needed.
b)   Tower Road Lights. Mr. McInturff briefly reviewed the history of this
issue. He reported that correspondence, emails, and phone calls from
neighborhood residents and other villagers were overwhelmingly in favor of the
ornamental light fixture, echoing the Design Review Board’s comments. He said
that the new lights would be exchanged one-for-one for the existing light
poles.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the
decorative light fixture for Tower Road. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Discussion: Gary Frank Zoning Analysis. Mr. Frank continued his presentation
from Thursday evening’s meeting regarding requested changes to the Zoning
Ordinance relating to roof heights, floor-to-floor heights, and floor
construction dimensions.
Mr. D’Onofrio countered Mr. Frank’s comments, saying that architects are not
using creatively the flexibility that they already have been given to create
good designs. He expressed his doubt that raising the floor-to-floor height
would result in better designs. He also pointed out that Lake Forest’s
standards are more restrictive than Winnetka’s, yet they capture better
architectural designs. He recommended that the Village wait until it has had a
sufficient time to appropriately evaluate the effect that recent zoning changes
are having on what is being built.
In response to a question from Trustee Presser regarding facilitating the use
of pre-cut lumber, Mr. D’Onofrio explained that zoning ordinances are not
normally reactive to construction materials and standards and are not meant to
take into account typical construction practices.
Trustee Aquilino pointed out that the Village was not seeing better
architecture a year ago before the new rules were put into place. She noted
that raising roof heights isn’t a guarantee of better design. She indicated
that she would support for waiting until the annual review to make any
adjustments.
Trustee Woodbury voiced agreement with some of Mr. Frank’s points, but
expressed concern about evaluating them outside of the context of a thorough
study. He suggested that the Council wait until May when the zoning changes are
scheduled for review.
Trustee Presser indicated that he would support raising the roof heights by one
foot at this time as did Trustee Brower.
Trustee Powell said that he might be convinced to raise the roof heights but
not at this time. He stated that he did not believe that there is a God-given
right to an 8’ door with a transom and crown molding.
There being a majority of the Trustees wanting to wait until after the annual
review, President Duhl told Mr. Frank that his suggestion to raise roof heights
would be reconsidered at that time.
After further discussion, the Trustees agreed with Mr. Frank that windows and
doors opening to a deck should be treated consistently for GFA purposes, and
asked staff to suggest some appropriate language to amend the Zoning Ordinance.
However, the Trustees disagreed with Mr. Frank’s suggestion to increase the
projection of window wells into the side or front yard. President Duhl asked
staff to look into this matter and report back during the annual review.
8. New Business.
None
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1305 and 1306. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1305 in the amount of $614,622.67 and Warrant
List No. 1306 in the amount of $1,290,063.62. Trustee Woodbury seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Furniture Bids. Mr. Saunders explained that in conjunction wit the new
Public Works Yards, new furniture must be purchased, and he reported the bid
results. He recommended that the bid be awarded to Trendway Corp. for
$85,370.80 for workstations, panels, and filing cabinets and to Landgraf’s Inc.
in the amount of $13,844.70 for seating.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Powell, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Landfill Monitoring Contract. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village’s
landfill is currently subject to regulation by the Illinois Environmental
Protection Agency and the Metropolitan Water Reclamation District of Greater
Chicago. Pursuant to regulations, the Village is required to conduct sampling
and chemical testing of groundwater, leachate, and sanitary sewer discharge for
the facility. He reported the results of the bids to conduct this testing and
recommended that the bid for groundwater monitoring services be awarded to
TestAmerica for an estimated annual amount of $53,883.85.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
Hal Frank, 1163 Cherry St., attorney for North Shore Country Day School, asked
for an explanation of Council’s action on this item and stated that he was
disappointed in the Trustees’ decision. He expressed concern about the
precedent that was being created by linking the School’s application to the
traffic problem, saying that throughout the community neighborhoods surround
institutional uses.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the owners of 139 DeWindt have
withdrawn their request for a zoning variation.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported on a recent Chamber of Commerce meeting.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Woodbury, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:25 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
March 4, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 18, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
4, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, and Stephen Presser. Absent:
Trustees John Powell and Ed Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus and
Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Trustees-elect David Abell and
David Webster, and 20 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   March 11, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   March 18, 2003 Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 11, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   February 18, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-7-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1140 Chatfield - Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting Maximum Building Size and Setback variations to permit the
construction of additions and attaching of an existing detached garage to the
residence. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Powell and
Woodbury.
b)   Ordinance No. M-9-2003 – Landmark Designation: 94 Mary Street - Adoption.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the ordinance
awarding local landmark status to 94 Mary Street. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, and Presser. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Powell and Woodbury.
c)   Ordinance MC-3-2003 – Zoning and Sign Code Amendments to Implement Design
Guidelines - Adoption. Mr. Norkus said the Village Council held a public
hearing on February 4, 2003, to receive public comment on proposed amendments
to the Village Code recommended by the Design Review Board, which would
eliminate conflicts with the Design Guidelines and include changes to the
zoning ordinance, the sign ordinance and awning regulations. At the public
hearing, amendments to the Design Guidelines were submitted by the Design
Review Board for final approval as well. Mr. Norkus also asked that the Council
consider extending the Design Guidelines’ selection of a uniform street
lighting fixture, the Union Metal Evanston pole and luminaire as the standard
for residential areas and arterial streets.
With input from Mr. McInturff, Director of Water and Electric, and mindful of
budget constraints, the Council concurred that the uniform standard lighting
fixture would be used only on arterial streets and replaced as needed or as
improvements are made.
Attorney Janega outlined the amendments to MC-3-2003, which include changes to
the zoning ordinance pertaining to front yard setbacks in commercial zoning
districts. She explained that the amendments require new construction to align
with adjacent buildings and allow staff to make the setback determination to
get the most uniformity in building line if the adjoining setbacks are not the
same. She reviewed amendments made to the sign code and to Chapter 12.24, which
has been amended to allow rigid canopies and awnings over public and private
property, provided there is 8 feet of clearance over the sidewalk.
Trustee Aquilino asked that the staff provide notice to existing businesses of
the changes to the sign code and suggested a mailing and meetings with the
merchants in the commercial districts. She and Trustee Brower also suggested
sending a letter and a copy of the ordinance to the Chamber of Commerce.
Trustee Presser moved to approve the Final Design Guidelines and direct the
Design Review Board to adopt the Guidelines as approved, seconded by Trustee
Brower. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend Ordinance MC-3-
2003 as outlined by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Powell and
Woodbury.
d)   Ordinance M-10-2003 – Special Use Permit and Zoning Variation: 600 Hibbard
Rd. – Adoption. Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio reviewed the
Park District’s request for a special use permit and height variation to allow
the construction of a garage building at the Skokie Playfield Service Center,
located on Hibbard Road, south of Sunview. He stated that since introduction of
the ordinance on November 5, 2002, the Park District has conducted an
alternative site analysis and met with neighbors to discuss concerns about the
proposed construction.
Attorney Janega went over amendments to the ordinance since introduction and
pointed out the new sections added due to the results of the site analysis. She
noted the recitals that provide the procedural history. She explained that the
relief granted in Sections 1 and 2 are for the special use and height variance;
Section 4 is the standard 12 month window for commencing construction and
Section 5 clarifies that the special use granted by Ordinance M-22-2002 last
year for the Park District’s administration building will not be affected by
this ordinance. She stated that this was necessary because the legal
descriptions for both sites are the same. She said there were no further
conditions inserted in the ordinance, but added that the ordinance contains the
standard provision preserving Council’s authority to revisit the Ordinance and
to impose additional conditions.
Liza McElroy, Park District Superintendent, provided comments as to the
necessity for the construction of a rinsate pad in order to comply with
Department of Agriculture regulations and the construction of a garage to store
equipment at the Service Center. She said the replacement of the existing
buildings is the most cost effective, would preserve open space and use the
funds allocated for this purpose, so that reserve funds can be retained for
future infrastructure improvements. She further stated that the Park District
has allocated $500,000 for this building and that drawings were presented last
May and August to design review and the zoning board where they received
approval. She reported that the neighbors were notified at that time and did
not voice any objections. She said that meetings were held with the neighbors
as directed by the Council in November and other locations have been
considered, but the logical conclusion was that the replacement building should
be in the same location as the current buildings.
Joe Dooley, Park Board President, commented on the public meetings and the
meetings held with residents to discuss their concerns and suggestions for
alternatives sites. He believes the primary objections are that they do not
want the service operation to be in its current location and that it causes
flooding, but said that the drainage system at the golf course should help with
the flooding problems. He acknowledged that traffic and parking is an issue at
the Service Center and that since there is no new parking lot being built,
there will be trucks parked on service road.
Mr. Dooley reported that the end of Cherry Street and the Morrow property were
considered as alternate sites for the garage building, but would require
construction of roads and reconfiguring the ball fields and that to relocate
the entire Service Center operation, would cost $2-$3 million instead of the
$500,000 estimated for the proposed plan. He stated that the Park District has
to find ways to increase reserved funding and stay within budgeted funds for
future improvements while operating under property tax caps, so that it is not
necessary to go to referendum.
The following residents spoke against the granting of a special use and
variation for this project:
Mr. Kurt Oelerich, 1280 Sunview Lane, presented a document to the Council
containing his objections and supported by 60 signatures. He believes there are
better alternatives that should not be dismissed without a more formal study.
He agreed that having the Park District facility and services at the same
location was best, but stated that if the service center were elsewhere, open
space would be created where it is now.
Mr. Oelerich said that he met with the Park District Administrator to discuss
the costs for the project, which is to expand the 12,000 square feet of storage
that is now under roof to 15,000 square feet. He stated that, after that
meeting, he believes that the figure would be closer to $1.5 million, rather
than the $500,000 allocated for construction. He said that the Park District
has not put anything out for bid and there is nothing to support their numbers.
He said that at the Plan Commission meeting there was reference made to 20 or
30 pieces of equipment at this location, when in reality it’s more like 160
pieces of equipment and the additional square footage proposed is not enough to
house all of the equipment. He said that he doesn’t think a $500,000 building
will be sufficient for the Park District’s needs at this location and that
parking is inadequate.
Trustee Aquilino questioned Mr. Oelerich as to whether he had identified a site
that the Park District could use, since he had acknowledged that it would cost
more to build a new building and that they probably do need more space than
they are asking for.
Mr. Oelerich replied that the two sites that Mr. Dooley mentioned are doable
and don’t infringe on the ball fields or tennis facilities and are not adjacent
to residential properties. He believes the construction of the building to
store equipment is detrimental due to dangerous fertilizers that would be
stored and would be dangerous to children as they go back and forth. He also
reported that one homeowner’s fence along the service road was damaged in July
and has not been repaired. He stated this was evidence of the safety issues
that exist. He is concerned after receiving an inventory of the vehicles at
this location, that traffic will increase because of the design of the garage
and that no plans have been made to address the inadequate parking. He further
stated that relocating the garage building would not eliminate the use of the
service road, but the roads outside the driving range could alleviate some of
the traffic. He stated that the use is changing with the addition of the
rinsate pad. He agreed that the facility has been in this location for many
years, but there are a lot of negatives with the current site and he doesn’t
believe the Park District has met the requirements of a special use permit. He
asked the Council not to approve the Park District’s request.
Mr. Dale Pelletier, 1314 Sunview Lane, requested that the Service Center be
relocated in the interest of the entire community, citing the chemicals used
and the proximity to the ball fields and residences. He asked the Council to
consider the long term and not grant the special use. He is concerned about
costs but is also concerned that the Park District get the facility they need.
He believes that it should go to referendum because it is a cost everyone
should be willing to incur.
Gail Dunn, 1287 Sunview Lane, expressed concern about flooding in the area and
land values and relocating the Park District equipment area. She suggested an
alternative site adjacent to the ice rink or at the end of Cherry Street, and
proposed a parking lot in the place of the Service Center. She also asked that
the Council not approve the Park District’s request.
Michael Mitchell, 1294 Sunview, commented about the parking problems along the
service road, damage to homeowners’ fences, and the possibility of being taxed
again in the future if the Park District’s needs exceed the proposed facility.
He asked the Council to deny the request at this time.
President Duhl informed Mrs. Dunn that the Village will be doing something this
summer to alleviate the flooding problem on Sunview Lane and then asked for
comments from the Council.
Trustee Presser asked Mr. Dooley if it was his position that there would only
be marginal changes to the inconvenience, the view and the use from the current
use of the facility.
Mr. Dooley replied that it was and clarified that the rinsate pad would be in
the enclosed area and rinsate then taken off site, instead of being washed into
the fields as is done now.
Manager Williams asked Ms. McElroy to explain the parking on the service road.
Ms. McElroy said that parking is more congested primarily during the summer
months when they hire seasonal help because there are not enough spaces in the
parking lot for these extra 28 people. She said that alternate parking at Pine
Street and at Skokie Playfield has been considered, but would reduce parking
for patrons.
Manager Williams suggested the extension of Cherry Street for parking and asked
Ms. McElroy if the Park District could find a solution if the Council required
no parking on the service road as a condition of the special use.
Ms. McElroy said that they could work out an alternate plan for parking.
Mr. Pelletier said that it is not only the parking, but the noise in the
morning from back hoes, service carts, and pick-up trucks that serve the golf
course. He asked the Council to remember that the storage sheds were originally
built to serve the golf course, which was located where the ball fields are
now, and that the service vehicles have to go all the way across them now
beginning at 5 a.m. and all day long.
Richard Blust, Superintendent of Parks, said that it is true that the service
road has been there for a very long time, but the number of vehicles has not
increased significantly.
Trustee Greenough asked if the equipment could go south through the tennis
center instead of by the homes away from the service road in the morning.
Mr. Blust replied that if a different route was used, the staff would then have
to be brought in earlier to move the equipment around and that would start
traffic earlier on the service road.
Trustee Aquilino commented that, early in the morning, no matter where the
traffic is, it would still be heard by the residents. She does not see how the
noise issue can be resolved because the golf course has to be serviced and the
use is not being changed. She said that the neighbors want the Council to deny
the special use, but since the use will be the same, it would still be serviced
the same way and the noise would still be there. She said it is difficult for
her to deal with the issue raised by the neighbors, in the sense that the Park
District’s use isn’t really changed, there is only a change to the height of
this one building.
Trustee Brower recused himself from the discussion.
After determining from Mr. Blust that the proposed building would be as far
away from the fence as the existing structure, Trustee Greenough said that that
the increase in height of this building actually might be more aesthetically
pleasing because the pitch would be better and would not be a detriment. She
felt that there must be a creative way to resolve the noise and parking issue
for the neighbors and was supportive of the request.
Trustee Presser said that he is not happy with the burden it places on the
neighbors, but agreed with Trustee Aquilino’s comments. He said that the Park
District’s alternatives are to find $2 million to build elsewhere, or do
nothing and keep things as they are, which meant that the neighbors would still
suffer. Trustee Presser said that the Council must determine that there is no
profound change in use and he said that he is inclined to approve the request.
He then asked what the Park District would do if the requested was denied.
Mr. Dooley replied that there would be no change to the existing building, but
that a rinsate pad would have to be constructed elsewhere and that parking
along the service road would be addressed and a solution found. He addressed
the neighbor whose fence was damaged and said that the Park District has always
paid for damages they have incurred in the past.
Mr. Oelerich said that to approve this without any concrete plan would be wrong
and this is the first time that the fence damage has been addressed even though
they have known about it since last July. He said there are safety concerns as
long as there is equipment along the road and that height is an issue and is
not more aesthetically pleasing.
President Duhl said that the Council could require no parking along the service
road.
Kurt Krueger, 1215 Elm Street, suggested a parking area could be placed in back
of the compost pile.
Mr. Blust said that they would park off the site to accommodate the neighbors.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance as
outlined by Attorney Janega as well as to reflect the prohibition of parking
along the service drive and to correct the vote on the findings of the Plan
Commission. By voice vote the motion carried.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance,
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Greenough, Presser and President Duhl. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Powell and
Woodbury, Trustee Brower recused.
e)   Ordinance No. M-11-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1110 Elm St. – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for variations from the maximum building size
and corner yard setback requirements of the zoning ordinance to expand an
attached garage and construct a two-story addition along the south end of the
single family residence.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
f)   Ordinance No. M-12-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1213 Ash St. – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation that would allow the
enlargement of a window in a nonconforming side yard and would grant a
variation in building articulation to permit the construction of a one-story
addition.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
g)   Ordinance No. M-13-2003 – Zoning Variation: 898 Elm St. – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for variations from intensity of use of lot
and side yard setback requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the
construction of a one-story, 228-square foot family room attached to the rear
of the house.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
h)   Resolution No. R-8-2003 – FY 2004 Budget: Public Hearing. President Duhl
opened the Public Hearing at 9:39 p.m.
Finance Director Ed McKee reviewed the proposed budget adjustments, citing
limited revenue growth and increasing operating costs as factors that led to
the elimination of four positions in the Village and increases in sewer, water,
and electric rates. He stated that in addition there will be a $10 increase in
vehicle sticker fees and the implementation of a new 5% tax on gross receipts
of natural gas. He estimated that the combined effect of the proposed changes
would increase the cost of municipal services by 3.7% or approximately $164
annually per homeowner.
President Duhl asked for comments from the audience.
Kurt Krueger, 1215 Elm, expressed his hope that the appropriation for the sewer
construction on Spruce Street from Hibbard Road east remains in the budget,
stating that there is three feet of water in his yard when it rains.
Manager Williams confirmed that there is $100,000 in the budget for the
construction of a sewer at this location.
President Duhl informed Mr. Kates that the Village had decided not to do any
further underground electric work until the issue with the phone company is
resolved.
There being no further comments, President Duhl closed the public hearing at 9:
45 p.m. He commented that this budget does eliminate the police social worker
position and the Council will be discussing options for replacing this service.
i)   Utility Rate Adjustments.
   i)   Resolution No. R-9-2003 – Water Rate Adjustment - Introduction. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the resolution. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-10-2003 – Electric Rate Adjustments - Introduction.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
resolution. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-11-2003 – Sewer Fee Adjustment - Introduction.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the
resolution. By voice vote, the motion carried.
j)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2003 – Natural Gas Tax – Introduction. Trustee Presser,
seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the ordinance. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
7. Old Business.
8. New Business.
None
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1307 and 1308. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from each warrant list, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1307 in the amount of $967,547.12 and Warrant
List No. 1308 in the amount of $260,779.12. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Powell
and Woodbury.
b)   Policy Direction: 380 Locust. Mr. D’Onofrio requested that the Council
provide direction regarding variation requests for new construction at 380
Locust. He explained that the homeowners had asked several months ago for
relief to cut down two large oak trees to provide compensatory storage for a
new home they were going to build which was denied by the Council. The
homeowners have since revised their plans to provide compensatory storage on
the north side of their lot, but have shifted the house so that a setback
variation is necessary as well as a variation from the garage requirements. A
third variation is being requested to exceed maximum building size because
floodplain regulations require a higher first floor elevation, which results in
the basement area being counted towards gross floor area. Mr. D’Onofrio stated
that he believes that since this is new construction, conforming alternatives
can be found.
Trustee Presser commented that when the homeowners came before the Council
requesting a variation because of the trees, he remembered that he had been
inclined to think about a variation if alternative compensatory storage could
be found. He asked Mr. D’Onofrio if he had taken this into consideration.
Mr. D’Onofrio replied that he had, but that he is concerned about the look of
the new home from the street and also that the setback variation would still be
needed. He stated that the Village Forester would still like to see the home
built further away from the existing trees. Mr. D’Onofrio clarified for Trustee
Brower that he would like to explore a conforming alternative with the
homeowners before the variation requests come before the Council.
Mr. Phillip Couri, attorney for the homeowners, stated that he has not
discussed the floor elevation with Mr. D’Onofrio and is not prepared to speak
to this, nor does he know the reason for the increased floor height. He
complimented the Council on remembering their last meeting and said that the
homeowners did move the foot print of the house to comply with compensatory
storage issues. Mr. Couri stated he believes that the homeowner, Mr. Ragusi,
does not have strong feelings about the elevation level, but would like to
discuss it with Mr. D’Onofrio and the architect. He pointed out that it is
unfair to include the basement in gross floor area in this case, since the
elevation is because of floodplain regulations.
President Duhl asked that the staff arrange meetings with the homeowner and
architect and come to the study session next Tuesday. He said that if a
decision can be made, it may still be possible to do an ordinance for the March
18th meeting.
Trustee Aquilino asked that that the staff also address the tree issue when
they meet.
c)   Village Yards: 2nd Furniture Bids. Mr. Williams explained that this is the
second furniture bid in conjunction with the new Public Works Yards. He
reported staff recommended that the bid be awarded to the lowest bidder,
Landgraff’s, for $62,189.00 for free standing office furniture and lunchroom
furniture.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Powell
and Woodbury.
d)   Bid: Settling Basin Roof. Mr. McInturff explained that, in total, there
will be five contracts awarded for this project, for concrete, masonry,
structural metal, roofing and hatches, with three of the contracts to be
awarded at this meeting. He described the bidding process and the amounts and
reported staff’s recommendation is that the contracts be awarded as follows:
concrete to Riley Construction for $13,000; masonry to Riley Construction for
$8,900; structural metal to Jack Frost Ironworks for $163,500. He informed the
Council that the bids for roofing were thrown out because one of the bidders
did not include the hatches. He said that those bids would be ready for the
Council at their next regular meeting.
Mr. McInturff distributed a drawing of the proposed settling basin roof and the
Trustees asked several questions regarding the roof material and supporting
structure.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Woodbury
and Powell.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that there is now an Emergency
Preparedness Guide available to residents. Copies are in the Manager’s office
and on the Village web site.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Presser announced that there will be a Code Committee
meeting in three weeks. Trustee Brower reported that the Chamber of Commerce
will be electing officers at its next meeting.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Aquilino moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Woodbury and Powell. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:34 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:09 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, and Presser. Absent: Trustees
Woodbury and Powell. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Woodbury and
Powell. The meeting adjourned at 11:10 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
March 11, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: April 1, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, and Stephen Presser.
Absent: Trustees Edmund Woodbury and Stephen Presser. Also present: Village
Manager Doug Williams, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Trustees-elect David Abell and
David Webster and 10 persons in the audience.
2. Policy Direction: 380 Locust.
Mr. D’Onofrio updated the Council on the current status of this issue. He
advised the Council that in response to the continued concern expressed at the
Council meeting on March 4th about the height of the basement above ground, he
had conferred with both the applicants and their architect. He reported that
the applicant was willing to find an accommodation to lower the house from the
street level to first floor. He also reported that the front setback remains an
issue because the Village Forester continues to believe it should be at least
36 ft. and noted that the issue about locating the garage on Willow continues
to be outstanding.
Mr. D’Onofrio introduced James Kalas, architect for the project, who reviewed
two possible, basically conforming alternatives for lowering the first floor
while meeting the floodplain requirements. He distributed foundation section
drawings to illustrate each method and explained that the second option is
preferable based on ease and integrity of construction and cost considerations,
even though it provides for somewhat less reduction in height. He asked that
the Council approve this option.
Manager Williams pointed out that all three plans are the same in terms of
square footage; the height issue, however, determines what is or is not
included in gross floor area.
President Duhl pointed out that if the Council approves the second alternative,
it would have to do so for every subsequent request from homeowners in the
floodplain.
Mr. D’Onofrio expressed concern that approval of the second option would set a
precedent that would ultimately impact the overall height of the homes in the
floodplain. He voiced a preference for the first option.
Trustee Presser arrived at 8:00 p.m.
Trustee Greenough noted that given the proposed design, there is no potential
for the house to have “looming” presence in the neighborhood.
Trustee Powell said that he thought the construction problems associated with
the first option were valid and, therefore, expressed support for the second
option.
Trustees Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino also indicated support for
the second option.
After a brief discussion of the setback and garage issues, there was a
consensus among the Trustees to direct the Village Attorney to prepare an
ordinance for introduction granting the three requested variations.
3. Discussion: Economic Development.
Gwen Trindl, Chairman of the Plan Commission, commented on Mr. D’Onofrio’s
report to the Council on economic development activities and staffing in other
communities. She indicated that the Plan Commission continues to support the
concept of a standing economic development committee of some sort. However, in
recognition of the current budgetary constraints and the amount of staff time
required to support such a committee, realizes that now is not a practical time
to ask the additional staff needed to support such a committee. She suggested
that an ad hoc study committee of appropriate community representatives be
appointed for a very finite time period, perhaps nine months so that its
findings could be considered during the next round of budget discussions, and
charged with a specific mission. This committee would focus on prioritizing
issues identified by the Plan Commission and clarifying the role of any
permanent economic development function that might be established in the
future.
Pat Horne spoke in support of the economic development concept saying that the
Village needs to be proactive in seeking new businesses if it wishes to
maintain its sales tax revenues.
Cicely Michalak, Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce, commented on
Mr. D’Onofrio’s report saying that it was an excellent summary of economic
development activities in similar communities. She also supported Ms. Trindl’s
suggestion that a committee be formed to study the concept and develop a plan
for achieving the desired goal.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed with the formation of an ad
hoc study committee. President Duhl asked Ms. Trindl and Ms. Michalak to
propose specific names for membership on this committee, and pointed out that
the focus of this committee will be separate and apart from consideration of
what is to be done with the Post Office site.
4. Discussion: Demolition Fee Analysis.
In a follow-up to the February 27th budget meeting, Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed
staff’s demolition cost estimate analysis. He summarized each component leading
to staff’s recommendation that the demolition fee be raised to $10,050. Those
components include: manager’s time, attorney’s time, police calls, community
development director’s time, community development office staff time, community
development inspector’s time, wear and tear on public facilities, legal
notices, HAIS legal notices, water/electric utility use, and Historical Society
demolition review.
A member of the audience commented that some of these costs appear to be
redundant to costs incorporated in the construction permit fees or other fines.
President Duhl responded that the Village must recover its costs in order to
remain viable. He pointed out that because construction fees are not achieving
that end, staff had been directed study both the direct and indirect costs
specifically resulting from the demolition process.
Trustee Greenough commented that the Village should not be subsidizing new
construction.
The Council discussed whether to increase the fee all at once or incrementally.
There was a consensus to raise the fee to $8,000 at this time with an
additional $600 tacked on to cover the Historical Society’s costs, leaving the
option to raise the fee to $10,000 next year if it is deemed appropriate.
5. Discussion: Updated Appendix 6 of 202 Comprehensive Plan – Priority
Assessment of Plan Recommendations.
Mr. D’Onofrio briefly reviewed the Plan Commission’s annual review and update
of Appendix Six of the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020. He noted that the
Plan Commission had added the recommendation that the Village address the full
cycle of storm water generation and control, in particular study of means to
reduce stormwater runoff generated by new development, including identifying
innovative means for storage and detention of stormwater runoff. In addition
the Commission recommended that the Village evaluate incentives to promote
restoration and rehabilitation of commercial properties as a part of a larger
effort to improve economic viability of the Village’s business districts.
Gwen Trindl, Chairman of the Plan Commission, commented on their report, noting
that the committee had also raised the issue of how to handle the ravine/bluff
lots, particularly with regard to accessory use structures.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to accept the Plan Commission’s
report.
6. Village Manager Update: IMEA’s Purchase of Trimble County 2 Coal-fired
Plant.
Manager Williams updated the Council on the desirability of remaining a member
of IMEA and becoming involved in the purchase of the Trimble County 2 plant.
After reviewing an analysis of the pros and cons of remaining a member of IMEA,
he recommended that the Village remain a member of the organization. With
regard to TC2, Mr. Williams recommended sending a letter to IMEA expressing
Winnetka’s continued interest in the project but need for further information,
including a final draft of the Participation Agreement. He also recommended
hiring Gerry Keenan to monitor the developments of the LG&E negotiations and
perform other power supply analyses as required. Mr. Williams noted that Mr.
Keenan is Village Attorney Janega’s husband.
7. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:40 p.m.
March 18, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 1, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
18, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, John Powell, Stephen Presser and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director
of Finance Ed McKee, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Trustee candidates David
Abell and David Webster, and 13 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that item 6(e) Resolution No. R-12-2003 has been
removed from the agenda. The Council approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   April 1, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   April 8, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 4, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Aquilino noted one correction to
the minutes. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes as amended. Trustee
Powell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-11-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1110 Elm Street - Adoption.
Village Attorney Janega explained the amendments to the ordinance since
introduction. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to amend the
ordinance and, by voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, then moved to adopt the
ordinance, as amended, granting maximum building size and setback variations to
permit the construction of a two-story addition and to expand an attached
garage at the residence. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Woodbury and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-12-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1213 Ash Street - Adoption.
Ms. Janega explained that the recitals added to the ordinance since
introduction set out the unique circumstances of this property, which requires
a variation from the building articulation requirements of the Zoning Ordinance
to permit the construction of a one-story addition on the residence and allow
the enlargement of a window in a nonconforming side yard.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the ordinance and
by voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino
moved to adopt the ordinance as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Woodbury and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-13-2003 – Zoning Variation: 898 Elm Street – Adoption. Ms.
Janega explained the amendments to the ordinance since introduction, which
describe the unique circumstances surrounding this property and require
variations from roofed lot coverage and side yard setback requirements of the
Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of an addition to the existing
home. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser moved to amend the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower,
Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Woodbury and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Utility Rate Adjustments: President Duhl stated that, as discussed in the
Council’s discussions during the review of the preliminary budget, the three
following resolutions would increase water, sewer and electric rates.
   i)   Resolution No. R-9-2003 – Water Rates – Adoption. President Duhl stated
that this resolution would increase water rates by 2%, or about $10 per year
for a typical customer and asked for comments and questions from the audience
and Trustees. There were none.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Greenough,
Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
   ii)   Resolution No. R-10-2003 – Electric Rates - Adoption. President Duhl
stated that this resolution would increase electric rates by 2%, or about $40
per year for a typical customer and asked for comments and questions. There
were none.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Greenough,
Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
   iii)   Resolution No. R-11-2003 – Sewer Fees - Adoption. President Duhl
stated that this resolution would increase sewer fees by 10%, or about $14 per
year for a typical customer and asked for comments and questions. There were
none.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Greenough,
Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
e)   Resolution No. R-12-2003 – Refuse Service Rates - Adoption.
Removed from the agenda.
f)   Resolution No. R-13-2003 – General Permit & License Fees - Adoption.
President Duhl stated that this resolution would increase a six-month resident
commuter parking pass by $5, a non-resident pass by $20 and the vehicle sticker
fee by $10. He asked for comments and questions from the audience and Council
members. There were none.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Greenough,
Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
g)   Resolution No. R-14-2003 – Building & Zoning Fees - Adoption. President
Duhl stated that this resolution would increase the demolition fee from $2,500
to $8,600.
Attorney Janega explained that the increase in the fee for demolition is to
reimburse the Village for its costs as well as reimburse the Historical Society
for the costs associated with its work researching properties that are
submitted for demolition. She clarified that the demolition fee would be $8,000
and $600 would be the reimbursement to the Historical Society.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Greenough,
Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
h)   Resolution No. R-15-2003 – Annual Fee for Unincorporated Fire & Emergency
Services - Adoption. President Duhl stated that this resolution sets the
ambulance and fire protection fees for the unincorporated areas served by the
Winnetka Fire Department.
Manager Williams explained that the fee formula is based on assessed valuation,
population and the number of calls per year. He said staff calculates the
figures each year to assure that the fee is fair. He said the same method is
used to determine the fee for service to Kenilworth, although there is a
separate contract with Kenilworth and Resolution R-15-2003 would be solely for
unincorporated areas.
There was no further discussion.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Greenough,
Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
i)   Resolution No. R-18-2003 – MFT – Traffic Signals – Adoption.
Manager Williams stated that this resolution would appropriate $125,000 for
traffic signal modernization at Green Bay Road and Sunset Road and is the next
step in a two-year project. He explained that the Village has to obtain
approval from the State of Illinois because Green Bay Road is currently under
State jurisdiction and the adoption of a resolution is required to receive
motor fuel tax funds.
Trustee Brower asked whether it would be necessary to replace this signal again
if jurisdiction over Green Bay Road was transferred to the Village in the
future.
Manager Williams replied that it would not be necessary to replace the entire
signal, but that the signal heads might have to be changed.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Greenough,
Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
j)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2003 – Natural Gas Gross Receipts Tax – Adoption.
President Duhl stated that this ordinance would impose a 5% tax on the gross
receipts of natural gas utilities.
Manager Williams said that this tax would be a key component for balancing the
budget next year. He explained that this tax is needed to make up for the
decline in revenue and that the income will also help to assure that Village
can continue building its infrastructure.
President Duhl commented that Winnetka is the only community in the area that
does not impose this tax as yet.
Attorney Janega noted an amendment to the ordinance since introduction that
makes the tax apply to services billed on or after May 1, 2003. She explained
that this date was determined after contacting the gas utilities.
There was no further discussion. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee
Aquilino, move to amend the ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Nayes: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Greenough, Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
k)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2003 – Zoning Amendments: Side Yards & Fences – Public
Hearing and Introduction. President Duhl announced that this ordinance would
establish new zoning standards for side yard setbacks and opened the public
hearing at 7:52 p.m.
Brian Norkus, Assistant Director of Community Development, stated that on
February 11th, the staff had presented an analysis to the Council comparing
Winnetka’s current side yard zoning requirements to those of comparable
communities. He explained that Winnetka imposes both a minimum side yard and a
percentage total side yard requirement that varies in each of the five single
family zoning districts.
Mr. Norkus reported that the analysis showed that Winnetka exceeds the average
side yard requirement, except in the R-5 and R-4 zoning districts, where
requirements are significantly less than in other communities. He said that
Village staff had previously recommended that side yard requirements be uniform
across all zoning districts, imposing a side yard standard of 25% or 30% of the
total lot, depending on the lot width. He then described how each zoning
district would be affected by the change.
Mr. Norkus stated that the ordinance would also amend the height limit for
arbors and trellises to allow a height of 8 feet, and would also require them
to be 3 feet deep, 6 feet wide, substantially open and located 2 feet from the
public right of way and 2 feet from a property line.
President Duhl asked for comments from the audience.
Steve Kashian, 531 Hoyt, said that he would like to know how this change
affects his property.
President Duhl referred him to the Community Development Director to obtain the
information he needs for his lot.
Manager Williams interjected that the ordinance was being introduced at this
meeting and there would be an opportunity for Mr. Kashian to speak before the
Council at time of adoption, if he discovers something about his property he
would like to address.
There being no further comments, President Duhl closed the public hearing at 8:
02 p.m.
After confirming that the minimum 6 and 8-foot requirement for side yards would
not be changed by the ordinance, Trustee Woodbury asked Mr. Norkus about the
number of nonconformities the ordinance would create, particularly in the area
of Rosewood, Locust and Westwood.
Mr. Norkus replied that 3 lots in that area would become nonconforming upon
adoption of the ordinance and that there are 3 existing nonconforming lots,
which accounts for 37% of the houses in that neighborhood. He said that
although percentage-wise this looks significant, he didn’t think it was out of
line.
Trustee Aquilino referred to the sections of the ordinance pertaining to
trellises and arbors and asked that the language be clarified.
Attorney Janega stated that she will amend the language in this paragraph so
that it correctly reflects the dimensions and placement of trellises and arbors
as proposed.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
l)   Ordinance No. M-14-2003 – Special Use Permit: 901 Sheridan Road, Tower
Road Beach Park – Introduction. Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio
explained that this Ordinance would grant a special use permit to the Winnetka
Park District to permit the renovation of an existing playground and the
addition of another playground at Tower Road Park Beach. He said the Park
District is also requesting a variation from the side yard requirements to
allow the new playground to span the lot lines of the two parcels of property,
one leased from the Village and the other owned by the Park District. He said
there will also be a new synthetic safety surface installed and the playgrounds
will meet all safety standards and requirements of the ADA. He reported that
the Design Review Board, Plan Commission and Zoning Board of Appeals had
approved the plan, with some comment from the Plan Commission and Zoning Board
about the color of the play equipment and whether the beach should be kept in
its natural state rather than placing playground equipment on it.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, stated that the Park District is seeking
approval for the playground as part of the 1997 comprehensive plan to renovate
all playgrounds. She said this playground project would be the last of the
renovations. She said that drawings were prepared last summer and placed at the
beach to get feedback from those who use the beach, and a number of committee
meetings were held to allow the public to provide comment. She said that this
project not only renovates the playground for tots, but will add a playground
for older children.
Rick Schramm, landscape architect for the Park District, displayed a drawing of
the proposed playgrounds to the Council and also described the placement and
types of equipment that would be installed. He said that picnic tables,
additional seating and trees for shade will be provided as well.
After discussing the size of the playground and the colors proposed, the
Trustees requested that the Park District provide pictures of the proposed
playground and drawings of the equipment for their next meeting.
There were no comments from the audience.
Trustee Brower commented that since Winnetka is landlocked, there are only a
few resources available for kids and families to use. He said that, while he
agrees that it would be nice to have a natural setting, this is one Village
asset that can be used for kids to play.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance.: By voice vote, the motion carried with Trustee Greenough voting no.
m)   Ordinance No. M-15-2003 – Zoning Variation: 380 Locust – Introduction. 
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the request to allow building size and setback variances
for the construction of a new four bedroom, three bath, home. He reported that
discussions were first held at the Council’s March 4th meeting and, at the
direction of the Council, his staff and the Village Forester met with the
applicant to determine if the proposed elevation of the home could be lowered
and the setback increased to prevent damage to mature trees. He said the
Council had discussed the results of those meetings at its March 11th study
session where it was determined that the top of the first floor elevation would
be lowered and the front yard setback along Willow Road would be increased. He
said that, in addition to these variations, a variation from the zoning
requirements for attached garages is requested to allow the garage to exceed
the maximum 50% of the front building line. Mr. D’Onofrio said that the
variations are requested because of the floodplain regulations and compensatory
storage requirements for this property.
Trustee Presser confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the Village Forester was
satisfied with the additional 3-foot setback from Willow Road.
Trustee Woodbury asked how the setback was calculated in this case. He
questioned why the garage couldn’t be moved forward with the house to allow for
compensatory storage behind it.
Mr. D’Onofrio replied that the compensatory storage is now on the north side of
the lot and, in order to put the storage behind the house, you could not have a
detached garage. He said the homeowner’s preference is to locate the house
where it is under the current plan.
Trustee Powell was concerned about water runoff to adjacent properties.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that there does have to be some provision so that water
can’t run off onto a neighbor’s property and he would check the plans.
Attorney Janega explained that compensatory storage was placed in the only
location it could be, if it is to be provided by excavating the ground rather
than using oversize pipes under the ground. She said that it is the preference
of the owner to provide compensatory storage in the ground rather than in
underground pipes.
Trustee Greenough said also that the compensatory storage would have to be dug
quite a bit deeper if it were in another location.
Manager Williams said that he thought putting the garage in front of the house
along Willow Road was becoming something of a standard on this street because
it provides a buffer against the traffic on Willow Road.
Attorney Janega said that the ordinance is drafted so that Section 2 describes
specifically the relief that is being granted and refers to the plans submitted
with the application as modified by the conditions set by ordinance. She said
the two conditions are in Section 3 of the ordinance as follows: Paragraph A of
Section 3 specifically states the elevation of the first floor, which is the
elevation approved by the Council at the March 11 study session, and Paragraph
B of Section 3 shows that the building must be shifted 3 feet to the north so
that it is farther from Willow Road. She added that these two conditions are in
addition to the 12 month construction period.
Trustee Greenough commented that, although the house starts higher at grade,
the house is not designed to use all of the allowable height and the roof line
will help to not create a looming presence on that corner.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Duhl announced that the Council will consider the item under Old
Business, the Police Social Services Contract, before discussing adoption of
the budget.
7. Old Business.
a)   Police Social Services Contract.
Chief DeLopez stated that it was the Village’s intent, after eliminating the
social worker position, to provide the community with a continuity of social
services by contracting with an outside provider. He described the process used
to select providers to present proposals for consideration and, as a result,
staff interviewed Hahn and Associates, Family Services of Winnetka and New
Trier Township. He recommended awarding the social services contract to New
Trier Township.
President Duhl said that he understands that New Trier Township will provide
these services through Elan Adler and that they are considering expanding their
services to other villages. He asked for comments.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce Street, asked how much the Village taxpayers will save
under this proposal.
Chief DeLopez replied that it would be approximately $60,000 yearly.
Manager Williams further stated that the cost of the contract with New Trier
Township is $42,000 per year, as opposed to the $105,000 budgeted currently by
the Village. He said that Hahn & Associates were highly recommended and provide
services to other police departments, but it was felt that they did not know
Winnetka as well as Family Services of Winnetka and New Trier Township. He said
that their proposal was a little less than New Trier Township’s, but one of the
benefits of using New Trier Township is intergovernmental cooperation. He said
that he foresees a synergy with other communities as well. He also said that
the Village wanted the transition to be as seamless as possible by using Elan
Adler who has provided outstanding service to the Village over the years. He
said that he also recommends awarding the contract to New Trier Township,
subject to their hiring Elan.
Trustee Woodbury asked if the services would be comparable to what we have
currently with Elan Adler.
Chief DeLopez said that he sent a contract to each provider that included a
scope of service containing such items as response times to incidents at the
Police Department, follow up therapy referrals, assistance with planning and
policy procedures, involvement with young people in community and station
adjustments for minor juvenile offenders. He also said that it allows
cancellation with 60 days notice, if we are not satisfied with the services
provided.
Trustee Presser commented that the Chief had indicated at a study session that
he was attempting to provide the same services but at a substantially lower
cost.
Chief DeLopez confirmed this and said that it would also be possible to use
resources from other communities who are part of the intergovernmental
cooperation.
Trustee Greenough said that she believes this is a good solution; for the
township who will have Elan’s services and hopefully for Elan as well.
Manager Williams indicated that a great deal of credit goes to Clarine Hall at
New Trier Township.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to award the contract for
Police Social Services to New Trier Township, subject to their employment of
Elan Adler. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
b)   Resolution R-8-2003 – FY 2004 Budget – Adoption.
Manager Williams said that the social services contract was the last item to
consider before adopting the budget. He reviewed the adjustments made such as
the natural gas tax and electric rate increase and projects that will occur
next year.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Greenough,
Presser, Powell, Brower and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1309 and 1310. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant list 1310, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1309 in the amount of $638,047.82 and Warrant
List No. 1310 in the amount of $527,794.86. Trustee Aquilino seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Woodbury and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Policy Direction: Rezoning Request 919 Burr.
President Duhl said that the Council must designate a board or committee to
conduct a public hearing on a rezoning request and suggested that the Plan
Commission be designated. He said that the final determination is made by the
Council.
Susan Connor, architect, for 919 Burr, said that in discussions regarding the
Sacred Heart expansion, it was concluded that it would be wise to consolidate
the zoning for the Sacred Heart campus. She agreed that the most likely place
for a public hearing on their rezoning request would be within the Plan
Commission. She also reported that there have been continuing meetings with the
neighbors to keep them informed.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to designate the Plan
Commission to conduct a public hearing on the rezoning request. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
c)   Bid: Settling Basin Roof
Manager Williams reviewed the contracts awarded at the Council’s last meeting
for the settling basin roof. He said that one of the remaining contracts is for
installation of roofing materials over the settling basins and staff recommends
awarding the contract (Contract 4) to the lowest and most responsible bidder,
Regency, for $46,734.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to award the contract for
the settling basin roof to Regency for $46,734. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and
Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid: Settling Basin Roof Scuttles
Manager Williams stated that this contract is for the settling basin roof
scuttles and staff recommends awarding the contract (Contract 5) to the lowest
and most responsible bidder, ICS, for $29,590. He reported that the total
project cost is $342,348 which is about $7,000 less than the budgeted amount of
$350,000. He said that, by splitting the bids for the roof installation and
scuttles, the Village has saved $16,000.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the contract for
the settling basin roof scuttles to ICS for $29,590. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and
Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
e)   Bid No. 03-001: Concrete Restoration
Manager Williams explained that this contract is for replacement of
deteriorating concrete in the settling basins at the water plant. He said that
the lowest bid is the best and most responsible and is from Structural
Preservations Systems for $85,368.50. He further stated that because the budget
contains only $50,000 for this work this year, the work will be spread out over
two years and added that staff recommends that the contract be awarded to
Structural Preservations Systems.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the contract
for the concrete restoration to Structural Preservations Systems for
$85,368.50. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury. Nayes: None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid No. 01-013: Underground Cable
Manager Williams said this contract is for the replacement of stock and is only
so that the Village can move forward to buy cable for the underground
replacement program and new customers. He said that because this product is
sold in certain numbers of units, they will not be buying as much as what is
stated in the agenda packets. He said staff recommends awarding the contract to
the lowest bidder, Wesco, based on the unit prices quoted, for $138,140.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the contract for
underground cable to Wesco for $138,140. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury.
Nayes: None. Absent: None.
g)   Outdoor Seating Permits and Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License Riders
Manager Williams reported that there are 8 restaurants who have asked to have
sidewalk café’s this year, and listed the restaurants requesting permits. He
said that included in the application this year are requirements to clean the
areas daily which were added to address problems that occurred last year. He
said that sidewalk liquor license riders have been requested by Corner Cooks,
Song O’Sixpence, J.P. McCarthy’s, and Kyoto.
Trustee Woodbury asked if there were any problems with the liquor license
riders last year.
Manager Williams replied that there were none.
Trustee Powell noted that insurance information was missing from J.P.
McCarthy’s application.
Manager Williams said that licenses would not be issued until all required
documents were received.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve sidewalk riders
and liquor license riders subject to complete applications being received. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
Susan Connor voiced a complaint about the lack of a right turn lane at the
intersection of Willow and Forestway and requested that the authorities
consider constructing one because of the traffic there every morning. She would
also like to thank the Police Department for enforcing the speed limit on Tower
Road.
Manager Williams responded that, although Willow and Forestway is not in the
Village’s jurisdiction, the Village has been trying to see if something can be
done at this intersection.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that Winnetka was named Tree City
again for the tenth year.
b)   President. President Duhl announced that a mailing had gone out from a
group who is opposed to the District 36 referendum and on the mailing there is
a replica of the Village’s tree emblem. He wanted the public to know that the
Village has not authorized the use of that emblem and has not endorsed the
mailing.
c)   Trustees. Trustee Presser said that there will be a Winnetka Report mailed
soon and it will contain a good description of what is being done about
flooding in the Village.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl nominated Posey Fisher to replace Jill Zapton on the Landmark
Preservation Commission for a three-year term expiring March 31, 2006. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the appointment of
Posey Fisher to the Landmark Preservation Commission. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
President Duhl nominated Suzanne Bush to replace Ned Leuchtner on the Landmark
Preservation Commission for a three-year term, expiring on March 31, 2006.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the appointment
of Suzanne Bush to the Landmark Preservation Commission. By voice vote, the
motion passed unanimously.
President Duhl nominated Hugh Brower to serve on the canvassing board. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to approve the appointment of
Hugh Brower to the canvassing board. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Woodbury, and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:21 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:00 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Presser, Powell, Greenough, and Woodbury.
Absent: None.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Woodbury and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:01 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
April 1, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 15, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
1, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Trustee candidate David
Webster, and ten persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   April 8, 2003 Study Session. President Duhl announced that this meeting
had been cancelled.
b)   April 15, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
Trustee Powell arrived at 7:40 p.m.
5. Village Council Minutes.
6. March 11, 2003, Study Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes as amended. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. March 18, 2003, Regular Meeting.
Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as amended. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2003 – Zoning Amendments: Side Yards & Fences –
Adoption. Mr. Norkus briefly summarized prior action on this ordinance. In
response to a previous question about the impact these changes would have on
the Asbury/Scott/Edgewood neighborhood, he reported that there are 40 lots
between 60’ and 90’ wide. He said that of these lots, 10 lots would become
newly nonconforming, while eight that were already nonconforming would simply
become more nonconforming. The remaining 22 lots are conforming and would
remain so. Of the 15 lots that exceed 100’ wide, only two would become
nonconforming.
Mr. Norkus also commented on proposed amendments to the introduced ordinance
clarifying the language regarding trellises and arbors.
Attorney Janega then reviewed all proposed amendments in the revised draft
ordinance, including additional recitals and findings.
Michael Hughes, 474 Sheridan Rd., expressed concern that the new side yard
requirements would result in unsightly, narrow houses, taking away from the
character of the existing neighborhoods.
President Duhl remarked that the intent of the new rules is to bring our
sideyards more into line with other, comparable communities. The size of
sideyards will now depend upon the overall width of a lot, rather than the
zoning district in which the lot is located, thereby encouraging more green
space in keeping with the Comprehensive Plan.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to amend the ordinance
establishing new zoning standards for side yard setbacks, arbors and trellises
in accordance with the Village Attorney’s draft amendments. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt this ordinance
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-15-2003 – Zoning Variation: 380 Locust – Adoption.
President Duhl noted that this zoning request has been reviewed at three
previous Council meetings and inquired whether there were any additional
comments or questions from the applicant, the audience, or the Trustees.
Trustee Woodbury commented that he remains opposed to the request because he
believes that the garage should be located in the back.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt this ordinance
granting building size and setback variances to permit the construction of a
new home. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greenough,
Brower, Presser, Aquilino and Powell. Nays: Trustee Woodbury. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-12-2003 – Refuse Service Rates - Adoption. Mr. Saunders
reviewed the proposed increased rates for special refuse pick-ups, the removal
of large-diameter logs and tree limbs, and the purchase of recycling bins and
yard waste bags. He noted that the rates were last increased about ten years
ago and are no longer competitive with market standards.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt this resolution
establishing the Village’s refuse services fees. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury,
Greenough and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-17-2003 – Zoning Variation: 139 DeWindt Rd. –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for front and corner yard
setback and garage variations in order to permit the construction of a two-
story addition. He noted that a similar request was brought before and approved
by the Council in January 2003. Following introduction, however, the petitioner
withdrew the plans due to the fact that the addition, as originally proposed,
was not going to be built.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that there is a dedicated, private, unimproved street
running along the east edge of the property that necessitates the need for a
variation. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend
approval of the variations.
Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-16-2003 – Zoning Variation: 440 Elder Ln. – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for Intensity of Use of Lot, Side Yard
Setback and Nonconformities variations in order to permit the construction of a
two-story addition. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the property is currently legally
nonconforming based on the existing roofed lot coverage that is largely due to
the depth of the eaves, as well as impervious surface. He reported that the
Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend approval of the variations.
Trustee Powell inquired whether part of the reason for the nonconformities is
the existence of what appears to be an unusable one-car, detached garage in the
back of the house and whether that couldn’t be removed. He said that he was not
bothered by the overhang of the eaves but was concerned about the excessive
amount of coverage that could be alleviated by tearing down the old garage. He
indicated that he would not be in favor of granting this request without
requiring its removal.
Trustee Brower expressed support for the variation in light of the fact that
the neighbors have voiced no objections to it.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Powell voted against
introduction.
f)   Resolution No. R-16-2003 – Department Head Salaries - Adoption. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the salaries for the
Department Heads. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
g)   Resolution No. R-17-2003 – Village Manager’s Salary - Adoption. Trustee
Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the salary for the
Village Manager. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, and Brower. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
9. Old Business.
a)   Development Agreement: Winnetka Community House. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed
the proposed Development Agreement with the Winnetka Community House in
connection with their upcoming construction project. He pointed to provisions
pertaining to the concerns raised at earlier meetings regarding staging,
traffic, and parking and referred to the proposed Staging and Site Demolition
Plan. He noted that construction vehicles will be prohibited from parking on
any public streets within the vicinity or in the adjacent municipal lot and
said that he is working with the Community House to establish designated
parking areas for construction vehicles and personnel.
Mr. Williams remarked that there will be some construction vehicles on the
site, noting, however, that the Village can issue "stop work” orders if there
are violations to the parameters specified in the Development Agreement. He
also mentioned that staff is making arrangements to designate commuter parking
on Maple, between Oak and Elm with the hope of encouraging commuters to migrate
to that area.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that the provision pertaining to use of the building
during construction be amended to further assure that construction activities
don’t interfere with the use of the building.
Jerry Aulisio of AT Associates, the owner’s representative, indicated that they
are substantially in agreement with the proposed Development Agreement.
Attorney Janega suggested amendatory language reflecting Trustee Woodbury’s
comments. She also proposed an amendment to the parking provisions to clearly
prohibit construction parking in the Lincoln Avenue Parking Lot and recommended
that copies of all notices to the Village be sent to the Director of Community
Development rather than the Village Attorney.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to amend the Agreement in
substantially the form suggested by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Trustee Brower recused himself.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Powell, moved to approve the Agreement, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, Woodbury, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Brower recused himself.
b)   Ad Hoc Committee for Business Community Development. Gwen Trindl, Plan
Commission Chair, commented briefly in support of the proposed plan to
establish an ad hoc committee to serve for a period of nine months with the
charges outlined in the Agenda Report and to report back to the Council by
January 2004.
Trustee Woodbury encouraged this committee to add investigating creative
funding sources to its list of charges so that all funding is not left to the
Village.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the formation
of the committee as proposed. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl thanked Chairman Trindl for the Plan Commission’s efforts on
this matter.
10. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1311 and 1312. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant lists, he moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1311 in the amount of $941,303.40 and Warrant List
No. 1312 in the amount of $1,103,237.96. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Woodbury and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 341 Woodland. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
Maximum Building Size, Intensity of Use of Lot, and Garage variations to permit
the construction of a two-car detached garage in conjunction with the
construction of a new home. He explained that, when the building permit was
issued in November 2002, the applicant’s plans called for a 24’ x 15’ detached
garage located entirely in the rear quarter of the lot. At that time, given its
proposed dimensions and location, the area of the garage was not included in
the gross floor area (GFA). However, the 20’ x 20’ garage now proposed is no
longer confined to the rear quarter of the lot and, therefore, is not eligible
for the detached garage GFA allowance.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to
recommend denial of the variations.
Todd Stephens, attorney for the applicant, reported that the applicants have
modified their plans to eliminate the need for all but the GFA variation and to
reduce the size of the garage from the proposed 20 by 20 feet to 19.5 by 19
feet. Mr. Stephens represented that because of the design of the new home, it
is impossible to locate the desired two-car garage in the rear 25% of the
property. He said that not being able to build a two-car garage constitutes a
hardship for his clients because it will diminish their property. He argued
that their modified garage design is safer, addressing sight-line concerns
raised by neighbors, and will accommodate two cars, taking them off the street.
Mr. Stephens asked for direction as to whether his clients should proceed
before the Council or return to the Zoning Board of Appeals for reconsideration
of the revised proposal.
President Duhl informed Mr. Stephens that heretofore the Council has taken a
strong stand against granting gross floor area variations for new construction.
He said that if the Trustees have not changed their minds, there is no need to
return to the ZBA. On the other hand, if the Trustees have changed their
position, then there would be some reason to return to the ZBA.
Dan Fox, 354 Sheridan Rd., an adjoining property owner, spoke against the
proposal. He pointed out that (1) it is new construction on a rectangular lot;
(2) the building permit was issued on the basis of a one-car garage; (3) the
request for variation was unanimously denied by the Zoning Board; and (4) the
neighbors are opposed.
Trustee Presser asked whether all of the neighbors are opposed. Mr. Fox
indicated that to the best of his knowledge they are.
Trustee Aquilino asked why the applicants had not designed a two-car garage
originally, before they had begun construction on the project, and applied for
any necessary variations at that time.
Trustee Woodbury inquired whether the applicants initially intended to use the
garage as represented on the permitted plans. The applicant’s architect replied
that they had, but had changed their mind and now desire a two-car garage.
In response to a question from Trustee Presser, the Village Attorney reviewed
the Council’s options.
Trustee Brower said that he would like to hear from the Zoning Board of Appeals
regarding the revised plans.
Pointing out that this is new construction, Trustee Aquilino noted that the
change in the modified request only amounts to a total of 1.5 ft., which does
not significantly change the plans.
Trustee Presser voiced a reluctance to overrule the ZBA or to grant a variation
for new construction. He agreed with Trustee Aquilino’s comments about the
minimal nature of the change. However, he said that he would not be opposed to
taking action that would allow the petitioner to return to the ZBA to see if
they could persuade both that Board and the neighbors to render a different
decision about the project.
Trustee Woodbury indicated that he does not think the change to applicant’s
request is substantial and would deny the variation request.
Trustees Greenough agreed with Trustee Woodbury, saying that the applicants
were aware of their choices when they began the project.
Trustee Powell indicated that although he will not get to vote on this matter,
he would be opposed to it.
Trustee Aquilino indicated that although she, too, will not get to vote on this
matter, she would be opposed to the variation since construction has already
begun and since the applicants had the opportunity to make a different choice
when the plans were originally drawn.
Mr. Stephens said that he would like a chance to resubmit the proposal to the
Zoning Board of Appeal.
President Duhl said that the Council would act so as to permit the applicant to
go back to the Zoning Board of Appeals. He, however, pointed out that a
majority of the sitting Council indicated that they were not convinced that the
de minimis change in the size of the proposed garage was significant enough to
sway their vote in favor of the variation request. He noted that the both the
Council and the Zoning Board were focused on the concept of new construction
and that even if the applicant convinces both the neighbors and the Zoning
Board to change their positions, it is not a guarantee that the Council will
change its view.
Attorney Janega again reviewed the options open to the Council and the
applicant.
Mr. Stephens withdrew the application for variation, indicating that the
applicants would resubmit the request to the Zoning Board of Appeals.
c)   GIS Agreement. Mr. Saunders explained that the GIS Consortium (GISCon) is
a public entity consisting of local government organizations working
collectively to achieve the full benefits of Geographic Information Systems
(GIS) technology. The organization’s mission is to reduce the cost and risk of
individual member programs by creating economies-of-scale through the
collective bargaining power of the Consortium. He reported that GISCon has been
very successful in developing GIS’s for Highland Park, Lincolnshire, Park
Ridge, Deerfield, and Glencoe. He explained that GISCon functions by partnering
with a consulting company that provides professional services rather than each
community hiring and retaining in-house staff resources to staff the GIS
effort, resulting in considerable savings to community members.
Mr. Saunders outlined the basic terms and conditions of membership in GISCon
and recommended that the Village enter into an intergovernmental agreement with
GISCon for the purpose of developing a GIS system in the most cost-effective
manner.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved that the Village enter into
the proposed agreement with GISCon and authorize the Village President to sign
said Agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustee Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and Aquilino. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
President Duhl called for a motion to appoint Steve Saunders as the GISCon
Director and Alex Attiah as the alternate. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Mr. Saunders then recommended that the Village enter into a service agreement
with GISCon’s selected service provider, Municipal GIS Partners, Inc., to
provide GIS development services.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to enter into an agreement
for GIS services with Municipal GIS Partners, Inc., for a fee based on the
hourly rates set forth in the Agenda Report, not to exceed $43,950. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower,
Presser, Aquilino, Powell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Finally Mr. Saunders explained that in the course of developing a GIS, the Cook
County Assessor’s office has developed a data set of digital information for
the entire county, including such information as property assessment data,
photographic images, and various other mapping information. The Assessor’s
office has developed a program by which the Village can obtain the use of this
data for the purpose of performing its official business, for no charge to the
Village, by entering into an agreement with the County. He recommended that the
Village authorize staff to enter into an inter-agency agreement allowing the
Village access to Cook County’s digital orthophotography.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to authorize the Village
to enter into this Inter-agency Agreement. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser,
Aquilino, and Powell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Quarterly Report: Village Yards Project. Mr. Saunders updated the Trustees
on the status of the Village Yards project, saying that the project is running
about one month behind the original schedule. He noted that the current cost
estimate is about $73,000 below the original, approved project budget.
Mr. Saunders noted that there had been a number of change orders that have been
allocated from the construction contingency and, therefore, have no impact upon
the overall project budget. Mr. Williams recommended that because state law
requires that any contingency over $10,000 be approved by the Council that the
Trustees approve these Change Orders.
Trustee Powell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve Change Orders 1
through 7, in addition to the pending Change Order noted in the Agenda Report,
in the total amount of $273,796. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Greenough, Brower, Presser, and
Aquilino. Nays: None. Absent: None.
11. Public Comment and Questions.
Michael Hughes, 474 Sheridan Rd., expressed concern about the speed of traffic
on Sheridan Road. Mr. Williams indicated that he would ask the Police
Department to follow up on this complaint.
12. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams asked Ms. Rosenthal to update the Trustees on
the status of the renovation plans for the Elm Street and Hubbard Woods train
stations. She reported that Metra hopes to begin the roof and masonry work late
in the summer and that the major renovation of the Elm Street station is
anticipated in 2004.
Mr. Williams reported on the number of hits to the Village’s web site during
the preceding month and indicated that he would be working with an outside
consultant to update the site.
Mr. Williams also noted that the Village’s Finance Department had been notified
that its Annual Financial Report for FY ending March 31, 2002, qualifies for a
Certificate of Achievement, the highest form of recognition, from the
Government Finance Officers Association
13. Appointments.
President Duhl nominated Robert Zielinski to be reappointed to the Landmark
Preservation Commission for a full five-year term expiring March 31, 2008.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the
appointment. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
14. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Aquilino
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:45 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury.
Absent: Trustees None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and
Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
15. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Aquilino,
Brower, Greenough, Powell, Woodbury and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:46 p.m.
