Recording Secretary
April 15, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 6, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
15, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7: 36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Barbara Aquilino, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee John Powell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Trustees-elect David Abell and
David Webster, and approximately 60 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   May 6, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   May 13, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees indicated that they
expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 13, 2003, Budget Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as amended. Trustee Aquilino seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   February 20, 2003, Budget Meeting. Trustee Aquilino moved to adopt the
minutes as amended. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   February 27, 2003, Budget Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the
minutes as amended. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
d)   April 1, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as amended. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-17-2003 – Zoning Variation: 139 DeWindt Rd. – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Aquilino, moved to adopt this ordinance
approving Front and Corner Yard Setback and Garage variations in order to
permit the construction of a two-story addition. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower, Aquilino, Woodbury, and
Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
b)   Ordinance No. M-16-2003 – Zoning Variation: 440 Elder Ln. – Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt this ordinance
approving Intensity of Use of Lot, Side Yard Setback and Nonconformities
variations in order to permit the construction of a two-story addition. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Woodbury,
Aquilino, Presser, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Powell.
7. Old Business.
a)   Agreement with Palmer Bellevue. Manager Williams explained that in
connection with its possible participation with IMEA in the purchase of a new
power plant, TC2, Winnetka needs to assess the risk of an increase in the cost
of power from TC2 relative to the Participation Agreement. Therefore,
additional professional advice is needed to evaluate the Participation
Agreement and, possibly, other power sources if the TC2 project is not
completed. He recommended that the Village engage the services of Gerry Keenan,
who assisted the Village with its evaluation of TC2, for an hourly rate of $150
with a not to exceed contract amount of $15,000. Although not required, Mr.
Williams asked the Village Council to approve this contract given that Mr.
Keenan is Attorney Janega's husband.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to enter into a contract with Palmer Bellevue for an amount not to
exceed $15,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Aquilino, Greenough, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Powell.
8. Appointments.
President Duhl nominated Jeff Cohen to serve on the Building Review Committee
for a three year term to begin on June 1, 2003, to replace Charlie Hoffman,
whose term will expire on May 31st. Trustee Aquilino, seconded by Trustee
Greenough, moved to approve the request. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Duhl nominated Maureen Mitchel to the Plan Commission for a three
year term beginning on May 1st to replace John Berwanger whose term expires on
April 30th. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve
the appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl, then suggested the appointment of Barbara Aquilino as
Chairperson of the Ad Hoc Committee for Business Community Development and Bob
Horne, Roberta Rubin, Betty Skalski, Bob Braude, Howard Smith, Tim Welter, and
Fraser Clark as committee members. He announced that Trustees Abell and Brower
would be the Council’s representatives to this committee. Trustee Presser,
seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve these appointments. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
9. Seating of the New Village Council.
Local Canvass Board: Canvass and Proclamation and Administration of Oath of
Office for Village President Michael F. Duhl and Village Trustees David Abell,
Nan Greenough and David Webster. There being no further unfinished business for
the sitting Council, President Duhl asked the Village Clerk to review the
results of the Local Canvass Board. Village Clerk Williams read the results of
the April 1, 2003 election. He reported that the Board had proclaimed Michael
Duhl duly elected to a second term as Village President, Nan S. Greenough duly
elected to a second term as Village Trustee, and David R. Abell and David M.
Webster duly elected to first terms as Village Trustees. He administered the
Oath of Office to Trustees Abell, Greenough, and Webster and then to President
Duhl.
10. Ordinances and Resolutions
a)   Commendation Resolutions - Adoption.
   i)   Resolution No. R-21-2003 - Honoring Trustee Powell for Village Service.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-22-2003 - Honoring Trustee Aquilino for Village
Service.
President Duhl read aloud the resolutions honoring Trustees Powell and Aquilino
and thanked each for their service to the community.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
21-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
22-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl added personal comments thanking both for their leadership and
contributions to the Council.
b)   Resolution No. R-20-2003 - Appointing Ed McKee as Village Treasurer -
Adoption. Manager Williams explained that historically the term of Village
Treasurer has coincided with that of the Village President and has been filled
by the Finance Director. He said that the arrangement works well for monitoring
investment policies and providing representation on pension boards. He
recommended the appointment of Ed McKee as Village Treasurer.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to appoint Mr. McKee. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster,
Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-19-2003 - Appointing SWANCC Representatives - Adoption.
Manager Williams explained that traditionally the SWANCC representatives are
the Village President and Village Manager. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
resolution appointing Michael Duhl as Director and Douglas Williams as
Alternate Director of SWANCC for the next two year term, beginning on May 1,
2003. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster,
Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
11. New Business.
a)   Park District: Liquor Service at Golf Course. Trustee Brower recused
himself from the discussion and vote on this matter.
President Duhl invited Mary Garrison, Park Board President, to review the
Board’s request to sell liquor at the golf course. Ms. Garrison provided a
brief historical review of the issue. She explained that the Park District is
requesting permission to serve alcoholic beverages in the area of the golf
course where food is currently served, i.e., the clubhouse and the 10th tee.
She cited falling revenues and stiff competition for golfers as the primary
reason for the request. She said that the Park Board hopes that offering the
amenity of liquor service will both encourage more users and encourage more
users to stay after their game so that the golf course can remain self-
supporting. She indicated that while a full service liquor license is sought,
the Park District would be satisfied with beer and wine service. Ms. Garrison
compared Winnetka’s golf course to the courses of neighboring communities, all
of which, she said, permit some kind of liquor service.
In response to a question from Trustee Presser, Ms. Garrison indicated that the
Park District would like to be able to serve between the hours of 11:00 a.m.
and 8:00 p.m. She stated that it would be extremely difficult to maintain
control if the hours were divided, depending upon when Washburne School is
open.
Bob Linn, 1228 Cherry; Dan Streiff, 1412 Edgewood; Gil Fizgerald, 336 Rosewood;
John Fadden, 480 Elder; Judy Blunck, 360 Green Bay Rd.; and Bob Kiesler, 222
Forest; Ian Sherman, 923 Oak St.; and Joe Dooley, 849 Sheridan Rd., spoke in
support of the Park District’s request.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce, urged the Council to deny the request based largely on
safety concerns, proximity to schools, and negative role modeling. Mr. Hecht
suggested that the matter be put before all of the Village’s residents in the
form of a referendum.
Barbara Padiak, 980 Greenwood; Pet Gelderman, 896 Tower Rd.; Jean Stonier, 780
Green Bay; Joane Noyes, 1555 Tower; and Kurt and Pam Kruger, 1215 Elm St., also
spoke in opposition to the request.
Ms. Garrison responded to some of the points raised by the opponents to the
proposal. She also submitted a petition supporting liquor service signed by
approximately 125 individuals. In closing, she said that the Park District is
not asking for anything different than the privileges granted to numerous
restaurateurs in town.
Trustee Abell asked whether the police or the schools have taken a position on
this matter. Ms. Garrison responded that both had refrained from taking a
stand.
Trustee Presser asked whether the Park District would be satisfied with a
license limiting the service of alcohol without the consumption of food to 20%
of the seating capacity, as is applicable to restaurants. Ms. Garrison
indicated that that would amount to about 20 seats and that they could accept
that limitation.
Trustee Greenough expressed understanding for the concerns raised by opponents,
but said that she hasn’t seen any experience or documentation to buttress those
safety concerns. She voiced her belief that the Park District would handle
things responsibly and her support for their request.
Trustee Abell indicated a preference to limit the service to beer and wine and
to revisit the matter after a year’s time.
Trustee Presser said that he did not believe that the Village ought to
discriminate against the Park District by limiting their license beyond what is
available to other licensees in the Village. He expressed support for granting
the Park District a full liquor license with the same seating restrictions as
other licensees, with a review in one year.
Trustee Webster expressed his belief that the question of whether to permit
alcohol service within the Village, and under what conditions, has already been
decided by the Village. Therefore, the Park District should not have to come up
with a better reason than anybody else under the ordinance to support their
request for a license; this location does not merit special protection against
alcohol sales more than any other location. He pointed out that there are other
liquor licensees in busy locations where children are exposed to the service of
alcoholic beverages.
Trustee Woodbury voiced the opinion that the question of the sale of alcohol at
public facilities is a Village-wide issue. He said he would be in favor of
having the Park District return to the Caucus to specifically raise the
question and defer discussion and decision until after that has been done.
After further discussion, there was a consensus to direct the Village Attorney
to prepare a draft ordinance granting the Park District’s request for a full
liquor license permitting the service of alcoholic beverages in the golf
clubhouse only, consistent with the regulations that govern such service in
Village restaurants.
b)   Park District Request for Commemorative Vehicle Sticker. Manager Williams
reviewed a request from the Park District to commission a vehicle sticker
commemorating its 100th anniversary. He noted that the Council had approved
similar requests from New Trier High School and the Community House.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve a park District
commemorative vehicle sticker for 2004. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   Request from Phototronics. Peter Skalski, owner of Phototronics explained
their request to use the public sidewalk and a portion of Lincoln Avenue during
Winnetka’s Sidewalk Sale to accommodate a Kodak traveling exhibit promoting
photography, “Picture Planet Experience.” Manager Williams reported that staff
had no problems with this request and that similar accommodations had been made
for other merchants during previous sidewalk sales.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Policy Direction: 1417 Edgewood Tree. Attorney Janega addressed the
petition submitted by Bernie Hammer asking the Village Council to determine
whether the Forestry Commission had acted beyond its jurisdiction with regard
to the removal of the tree in question. She also reviewed the history of prior
zoning requests for the subject site and reviewed the process followed by the
property owners in requesting that this tree be removed.
Attorney Janega explained that the Forestry Commission acts in two ways: (1) in
an advisory capacity, upon a request from the Forester, or (2) upon appeal from
the property owner. The Forestry Commission never issues permits and is not
authorized to do so; only the Village Forester has the authority to make a
final determination, as he did in the matter at hand. She concluded that,
therefore, there is no matter before the Council requiring its decision. She
opined that should an appeal or review be entertained in this instance, it
would jeopardize the finality and decision of every board or commission, as
well as administrative staff, by opening up an avenue of appeal to the Council.
Ms. Janega added that there are questions of hardship relating to the subject
property that should the Council overturn the Forester’s decision, could create
significant liability for the Village and recommended that the President move
that the request is out of order and strike it from the Agenda.
President Duhl indicated that while he shared the Village Attorney’s concerns,
he would listen to Mr. Hammer, as a concerned Winnetka resident, before making
any ruling.
Mr. Hammer enumerated the points in his petition. He focused particularly on
perceived inadequacies in the tree removal permit application.
President Duhl pointed out that there is a history of hardship with this
particular property. He noted that the Village has ordinances determining what
can and cannot be constructed on any parcel of property and has protected its
trees as best as possible. However, never has the Council told a private
property owner that they cannot build something equivalent to what is permitted
by Code because of the existence of a tree.
Mary Robbins, Chairperson of the Forestry Commission, related the Commission’s
discussions on this matter. She said that the Commission’s members, with the
exception of Mr. Hammer, felt that there was a hardship in that there was no
possibility of building a viable home, comparable to what neighbors on a
similar lot could build, on the subject lot without taking down the tree in
question.
Suzanne Hillshire, Dick Kates, 1536 Tower Rd., Bob Hilartis, 1446 Edgewood;
Elise Covey, 1503 Edgewood, Bill Hayes, 1487 Tower, and Dianthe Olschan, 1436
Edgewood, voiced support for Mr. Hammer and expressed concerns about the
Village’s protection of trees in general. Ms. Olschan also voiced concern about
the size of the proposed new construction on the site.
Jim Stier, Village Forester, reviewed his responsibilities and involvement with
the subject property. He explained that given the location of the existing
trees on the site and the resultant design limitations, he felt that the
significant tree in the front of the property, being on the streetscape, was
more valuable, and therefore, more important to save.
Attorney Janega addressed audience comments regarding the size of the proposed
new house. She commented that recent studies indicated that in order to achieve
a reasonable return on investment in this market, a typical home, on the
smaller side of the scale, needed to be approximately 3,200 sq. ft. She
remarked that the amount of Gross Floor Area permitted on this property is
3,500 sq. ft. and pointed out that the Council recently granted a GFA variation
on a nearby, comparably sized lot. She said that every decision in land use is
made within the context of balancing individual property rights of owners with
those of the community at large.
Trustee Presser asked Ms. Janega whether in this situation, where the Village
has other building and zoning restrictions, to deny this property owner the
right to build a house in accordance with those restrictions would raise
regulatory taking issues. Attorney Janega stated that she believed that it
would do so. She said that the tree that the Village Forester authorized to be
removed sits within the legal, buildable area of the lot. To require that the
tree be maintained would substantially reduce the value of the property by
virtue of that regulation. However, because the Village has allowed comparably
sized homes to be built within the immediate area and because it did not
approve proposed variation requests that would have saved the tree, to deny the
owner’s conforming application, opens up the Village to substantial liability.
Having heard all of the comments, President Duhl struck this item from the
Agenda, saying that it is clear from reading the minutes and listening to the
discussion that the Village Forester made every effort to save the tree in
question. The tree sits within the building envelope, and while the Village
makes every effort to preserve trees, it does not do so at all costs to the
extent of impinging on private property rights.
There was no dissent from the Trustees.
e)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1313 and 1314. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant lists, he moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1313 in the amount of $678,373.72 and Warrant List
No. 1314 in the amount of $259,284.32. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Abell,
Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Landscape Maintenance Agreement. Manger Williams explained that in keeping
with discussions held during the budgeting process and subsequent staff
cutbacks, the Village needs to contract out some additional landscaping duties
for the coming summer season. He reported recommendation that these be awarded
to Scopelliti Landscaping who already performs he job for the Village.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award Scopelliti
Landscaping the bid for landscaping services for 26 additional sites in the
amount of $27,712. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
g)   Bid No. 03-006: Poles and Luminaires. Due to the lateness of the hour,
President Duhl announced that this item had been removed from the Agenda.
h)   Quarterly Report: Power Cost Adjustment. Due to the lateness of the hour,
President Duhl announced that this item had been removed from the Agenda.
i)   Outdoor Seating Permits: Sweet Shop and Paddy Wagon. Manager Williams
reported that two additional restaurants, The Sweet Shop and Paddy Wagon, are
seeking outdoor seating permits.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
j)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Due to the lateness of the hour,
President Duhl announced that this item had been removed from the Agenda.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
Laurie Peterson, 1026, Elm St., voiced concern over the size and style of new,
neighboring construction, saying that the issue of teardowns is not only about
losing older homes, but also about what replaces them.
President Duhl agreed with her concerns but pointed out that Winnetka does not
have strict design review at this time.
Trustee Woodbury added that the Council had agreed to form an ad hoc committee
to discuss what the next steps toward design review might be.
Bill Hayes, 1487 Tower Rd., expressed dismay over the proposed loss of Elm
trees on Tower Road in order to accommodate moving the Log House.
Sally Graver, 1495 Tower Rd., echoed Mr. Hayes comments and distributed
pictures of the trees that require significant pruning and/or removal.
The Trustees all shared Mr. Hayes’s concerns and discussed possible
alternatives.
Manager Williams explained that two routes, west on Tower and east on Tower,
had been studied and that it had been determined that the route west on Tower
would have the least impact on both the trees and utilities.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak Street, who served on the committee to orchestrate the
move, explained that for a variety of reasons the Historical Society cannot
afford to postpone the move any longer.
After further discussion it was decided to hold a special Council meeting on
Tuesday, April 22nd to further consider this matter.
John Mengel, 1147 Whitebridge Hill, appeared on behalf of the Whitebridge Hill
neighbors to express concern about the demolition permit request for 1150
Whitebridge and the possible size of new construction on that lot, particularly
in view of the fact that nontableland is currently permitted to be used in FAR
calculations.
President Duhl acknowledged that the issues presented by riparian properties
have not yet been addressed by the Council and expressed hope that the Council
would get to them in the coming year.
Pat O’Donohue, 1208 Ash, raised a question about the hiring of summer help.
Manager Williams informed him that due to budget cutbacks, the Village would
not be hiring the usual summer help in the Public Works Department.
9. Reports.
a)   Manager. Mr. Williams reported that number of permits issued by the
Community Development Department is down somewhat from last year, although the
value of the improvements permitted has increased substantially. He added that
based upon the first quarter of 2003, it is estimated that there will be an
increase of approximately 40% over the number of demolition permits issued last
year. Mr. Williams commented that while from a revenue point of view, the
increased income is good for the Village, the increased value, size, and
complexity of projects impacts staff’s workload throughout the permit process.
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Trustee Ed Woodbury as President
Pro Tem for the coming year. The motion to approve the appointment was made by
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of John Powell as the Village’s
representative, with full voting rights, to the Northwest Municipal Conference.
The motion to approve the appointment was made by Trustee Presser and seconded
by Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl then announced the Trustees' committee assignments for the
coming year, as follows:
President Pro Tem                                   Ed Woodbury
Chamber of Commerce                                 Hugh Brower
Ad Hoc Committee for Business Community Development Dave Abell
                                                    Hugh Brower
                                                    Stephen Presser, Ch.
Code Review Committee                               Dave Abell
                                                    David Webster
Museum Liaison                                      Ed Woodbury
NW Municipal Conference                             John Powell
Plan Commission                                     Nan Greenough
RED Center                                          Dave Webster
                                                    Hugh Brower (Alt)
SWANCC                                              Mike Duhl
                                                    Doug Williams
                                                    Mike Duhl
Technical Committee                                 John Lien
                                                    Ed Woodbury
Warrants Review                                     Hugh Brower
Winnetka Report                                     Stephen Presser
Youth                                               Hugh Brower
 
11. Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, Executive Session was cancelled.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned
at 12:06 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
April 22, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 6, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 6:45 p.m on Tower Road. Present:
Trustees David Abell, Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser, David Webster and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Nan Greenough. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Director of
Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders,
Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Village Forester Jim Stier, Village Plan Reviewer
Darab Shahabi and 25 persons in the audience.
2. Inspection of Trees Boal Parkway and Tower Road.
The Village Council met at Boal Parkway and Tower Road to inspect the trees
along Tower Road that would be affected by the relocation of the historic Log
House to Crow Island Park.
President Duhl adjourned the meeting at 7:30 p.m. and announced that the
Council would reconvene in the Council Chambers of the Village Hall at 7:45
p.m. to continue its discussion.
3. Analysis of Log House Move from Tower Road to Crow Island Woods.
President Duhl reconvened the meeting at 7:50 p.m. at Village Hall to continue
the discussion. Present: Trustees David Abell, Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser,
David Webster and Edmund Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Nan Greenough. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Director of Public
Works Steve Saunders, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Village Forester Jim Stier,
Village Plan Reviewer Darab Shahabi and 13 persons in the audience.
President Duhl commented that visiting the location where the most trees would
be affected by the move of the Log House along Tower Road was very helpful. He
asked the Historical Society’s architect, Ken Behles, to discuss the
feasibility of dismantling the house for the move.
Mr. Behles described how the Historical Society had researched the various
aspects of moving the Log House from its current location to Crow Island Park.
He explained how dismantling the roof would destroy the historical value of the
house and damage the materials. He also explained that it was not possible to
separate the house where the 1917 addition begins because the construction of
the addition is supported by the original house and would result in damaging
both sections. He emphasized that the Log House is the oldest in Cook County
that has not been rehabilitated and once the house is taken apart, it cannot be
restored without damaging the original materials and losing the historical
value. He answered several questions from the audience regarding the timber and
peg construction of the original house. He reported that the unanimous opinions
of the experts in preservation, university authorities and the Illinois state
agencies he has contacted, have been that the house should not be dismantled in
any way.
The Trustees then asked Mr. Behles various questions regarding the
possibilities for dismantling portions of the house for the move.
Mr. Behles’ further clarified how the house had been constructed and stated
that, in his opinion, because of the timber and peg construction of the
original house and the way the addition was constructed, dismantling the roof
or any portion of the house would destroy the original materials and damage the
structure. He also said that having more time to contact other moving companies
or experts would not change his opinion.
Trustee Woodbury added that the procedures for moving and preserving houses
like the Log House that the Historical Society is following are set by the
State preservation agencies.
Mr. Behles also stated that the Historical Society does not have the option of
buying the property and keeping the house where it is. The owners will not
allow the house to remain on the site and the Society has until the end of May
to move it.
Some audience members expressed concern about the weight of the house on the
recently rebuilt Tower Road and asked how the moving company will support the
house and avoid the trees.
Mr. Behles replied that the moving company has $2 million in insurance which
should cover any street damage. He said that the mover is very aware of the
trees and will be zigzagging as much as possible to avoid them. He said the
staff and the Historical Society believe that the mover has the right equipment
and experience and will move the house carefully and slowly.
Trustee Brower asked Steve Saunders about the weight of the structure and the
move.
Mr. Saunders said that there would be nine wheels to support the structure and
that the pavement should be able to support it. He said that plating will be
used on the parkways which should be sufficient as long as it is not wet. He
added that the trailer carrying the house is the size of a semi and can zigzag
only so much.
President Duhl turned the discussion to what can be done to preserve the trees
along the route.
Village Forester, Jim Stier, listed the addresses where trees would be affected
and thoroughly explained the methods that would be used to prevent tree and
branch removal. He said that the staff and the mover are working closely with
the company who will be trimming the trees and that the only tree that will
need to be removed is the one at 1510 Tower Road. He said that an alternate
route along Hibbard Road would affect many more trees than the route along
Tower Road. Mr. Stier also said that, even if the house were moved in sections,
the tree at 1510 Tower would have to come down. He assured the Council that the
staff and the tree trimmers would do the least amount of pruning necessary to
keep the canopy affect of the trees over the road. He was of the opinion that
with the pruning and the utility lines going underground, the trees would
eventually branch out even more than they do now.
President Duhl then asked the audience for their comments and concerns.
Comments were that:
   ·   If the house were moved in two parts, less trees would need pruning and
movers could go to the north side of the street to avoid the trees on the south
side;
   ·   More discussion is necessary;
   ·   Both the trees and the house are assets of the Village and if a
sacrifice has to be made, save the 1837 portion of the house;
   ·   View of the street will be ruined by taking down the trees;
   ·   New trees may take 4 to 5 generations to become the size of the current
trees;
   ·   A hope that a compromise could be made to preserve the beauty of trees
and at the same time, preserve the historic significance of the oldest part of
the house.
   ·   The 1917 section of the house is not a shed, but a work of art and
dovetailed into the older portion of the house and the cabin would be destroyed
if taken apart which would be a discredit to the Burnham family who have
carefully saved the structure.
   ·   The 1917 section is an integral part of the house and cannot be taken
apart from the rest of the house;
Nancy Judge, of the Historical Society, commented that Winnetka has a unique
opportunity to preserve an important part of its history and to take the house
apart would damage the history. She said that the house mover will do
everything in his power to wait for the trees to be pulled back as the house is
moved.
Joan Evanich, Historical Society, said that Anita Wilkes Burnham was a pioneer
in historic preservation and it was Mrs. Burnham’s wish that the Historical
Society have the house. Mrs. Evanich has become very familiar with the Burnham
family and they have worked very hard to save this house for the community. She
also said that both parts of the house are very important and the Society has
worked with the mover and the forester to preserve as much as possible.
Mr. Behles added that, if someone was seeking a demolition permit for the back
part of this house, the various Village boards would be working to find a way
to save it and the Historical Society has found a way to save this structure.
He asked that the Village consider this an opportunity to save a non-renewable
resource.
The Trustees ascertained from their questions that:
   ·   the trees that need trimming are healthy and will grow back;
   ·   that significant trimming is only required on one tree and another tree
will be removed;
   ·   that if the roof were reduced by 5 feet, that one tree would be saved
and there would be less trimming;
   ·   that significant damage would be done to the structure if the roof were
disassembled or if the 1917 addition were moved separately and also, that
historic materials could not be preserved.
Trustee Presser commented that he was reluctant about the move before the
meeting and the inspection of the trees, but now realizes that historical
preservation of the building does include the rear as well as the roof. He also
previously thought that the structure and preserving the canopy of the trees
were incompatible problems, but he no longer thinks that. He said that a
beautiful tree on the south side of the road would be given up, but because Mr.
Stier is going to extraordinary lengths to preserve the rest of the trees, he
believes the Historical Society should go ahead and do this. He said that
another consideration is that for the Historical Society to take apart the
house involves serious sums of money and is not within the Society’s budget. He
said the price paid for the removal of a tree does not outweigh the value of
the house.
Trustee Woodbury said extraordinary measures have been taken by the staff to
save the trees and encouraged the Society to continue with that, only trimming
as necessary. He said many professionals have been involved in this project,
including Ken Behles who has donated his time, the state agencies charged with
protecting assets such as this, and the others who gave their collective
opinions on the right thing to do. He stated that allowing the move is the
right thing to do and finds it rewarding that we have a Society that will
follow through on the Burnham’s wish to preserve the home and will allow future
generations to preserve it as well.
Trustee Abell said his first inclination was to remove a part of the house that
wouldn’t be seen, but after the discussion, has decided that as little as
possible to the house should be done to the house, particularly because the
amount of tree trimming is not as much as originally thought. He said he is in
favor of moving the house.
Trustee Brower agreed that it is an opportunity for the Village, the schools,
and for preserving the historical integrity of the Village. He said that he,
too, was looking for a compromise when they started out the evening, but is
convinced that the house cannot be dismembered and that the Forester will do
the best job he can to preserve the trees. He added that there is always a
trade off and this is a tough one, but the Society should go forward with
moving the house.
Trustee Webster said that he thought it significant in tonight’s commentary
that even if the Society would reduce height of the house, the Forester would
only be able to save one part of one tree, which stands to lose 15% of its
canopy if trimming is done. He said that the architects’ professional opinions
are that cutting the house in two will destroy the structure and under the
circumstances, removal of the one tree should be accepted in this case. He
asked that the Forester save as many branches as possible and replace the tree
that is removed with one as large as possible.
President Duhl said that it appears the Council is unanimous that the Society
should be able to go forward with the move. He said that this is one of the
most difficult issues the Council has been confronted with and hopes that those
who are disappointed will understand the Council’s views and that we tried to
be as open and fair as possible.
Mr. Hayes, 1510 Tower, thanked everyone for discussing this and attending the
meeting because he felt responsible for the meeting being called. He stated
that he had never been against moving the house, but wanted to try to preserve
his tree.
Mr. Hammer, 1455 Tower, said he is proud to live in Winnetka and to be able to
participate in the give and take of these discussions. He said it is enriching
to hear everyone contribute and the final result is no one is angry and
something has been accomplished.
Mr. Kates, 1326 Tower, was very appreciative of the Council’s attempt to
preserve the trees.
President Duhl commented that it is an interesting process and through it, the
Council has learned how people feel about the community. He directed Mr.
Saunders and Mr. Stier to do everything they can to preserve the branches of
the trees.
The meeting adjourned at 9:44 p.m.
________________________
Deputy Clerk
May 6, 2003
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 20, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 6,
2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7: 37 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, and approximately 23 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that at the request of the Historical Society, Item 8
(d) had been removed from the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda as
amended.
4. Quorum
a)   May 13, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   May 20, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 15, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   April 22, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2003 – Liquor Service at Golf Clubhouse - Introduction.
Attorney Janega briefly reviewed the history of this item, saying that the
recitals lay out the history of the request, the rationale behind it, and
reflect Council’s discussions regarding the rationale for proceeding with it.
She then commented on the draft ordinance creating a “Class P” license that
applies only to the Park District and allows liquor service only at the
clubhouse. She explained that although the liquor service authorized by this
ordinance would be essentially the same as for Class ‘A’ restaurant licensees,
key distinctions have been incorporated into the draft recognizing the unique
circumstances at the golf course. She enumerated these distinctions as follows:
   i)   To address concerns expressed in the course of the Council’s
considerations, the general terms of the license limit service to persons the
food service attendant “reasonably believes to be at the Golf Course for the
principal purpose of playing golf.”
   ii)   A new provision has been added to Section 5.09.250 of the Liquor
Control Ordinance, limiting the hours of liquor to between 11:30 a.m. and 7:30
p.m. on days that the Golf Course is open and the food service facility is in
operation.
   iii)   A new provision has also been added to the “liquor-only” regulations
of Village Code Section 5.09.205, to restrict such service at the clubhouse to
persons who have completed playing golf on the Golf Course.
   iv)   Because the Liquor Control Act requires the governing board of the
Park District to approve the liquor sales, a provision has been added that
requires the Park District’s liquor license application to include a certified
copy of the Park District ordinance or resolution authorizing the sale of
liquor at the clubhouse.
Ms. Janega noted that the ordinance is drafted as an amendment to Chapter 5.09
of the Village Code, which has a sunset provision that provides for its
expiration in mid-September. This Chapter supercedes the regular provisions of
the liquor ordinance for a one-year period to provide for a trial period of the
liquor-only service. Therefore, Council will have to revisit this chapter upon
its expiration. She pointed out that this ordinance would only authorize the
classification. The Park District would still have to go through the complete
application process and that application would have to be reviewed by the
Liquor Commissioner.
Trustee Webster suggested that the draft text be amended to reflect that
alcoholic beverages must be consumed at the food service facility only. Ms.
Janega explained that the licensed premises would be defined as the food
service area.
Trustee Abell suggested that the draft text be amended to allow purchase and
consumption by those engaged in golfing activities, rather than restricted just
to those who have completed a round of golf.
Mary Garrison, Park Board President, commented on remarks she made during the
April 15th meeting concerning the positions of the Winnetka Presbyterian Church
and apologized for any confusion or misunderstanding that occurred as a result
thereof.
Zane Smith, Park Board member, suggested that the draft be amended to reflect
service would be permitted when the golf clubhouse is open, rather than when
the golf course is open.
Ms. Janega agreed to work with the Park District to develop appropriate
language concerning when the facility is open for golfing activities.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce, objected to some of the draft language on the grounds
that it implies a causal relationship between the decline in business and the
lack of liquor service. He also objected to what he perceives to be procedural
inappropriateness in that the community has not been polled. He urged the
Council to set the matter over for a full public debate and referendum by the
Village’s residents.
Ann Wilder, 1096 Wilder, questioned the constitutionality of limiting the
service of alcohol to golfers only. Ms. Janega responded that there is no
constitutional issue here.
Pam Kruger, 1215 Elm St., voiced concern that the concept might expand to the
beaches and objected to language implying that the Council is “accommodating”
the Park District request, particularly in view of the fact that the residents
have not been polled.
Bill Murphy, 112 Fuller Lane, Park Board member, commented that the momentous
change in the Village’s liquor policy was last fall when the Council voted to
allow liquor-only service. He agreed with the Council’s direction to make the
Park District license consistent with that of other operations in the Village
and pointed to other courses that are near schools.
Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry, supported the draft ordinance, saying that he respects
the commitment and the judgment of the Trustees.
Karl Fink, 558 Provident, shared his perspective that the golf course is a
place for families and kids and, therefore, should be alcohol free. He also
commented on statements relating to the Winnetka Presbyterian Church’s
position.
Barb Padiak, 980 Greenwood, referred to a letter from the Winnetka Bible
Church.
Arturo Castro, 928 Elm, spoke in support of discipline and careful management
by the vendor of any alcoholic beverage service.
Kurt Kruger, 1215 Elm, reiterated earlier objections, citing safety concerns
and pointing to a recent accident at Hibbard & Oak.
On the point of public notice, Trustee Presser pointed out that the Council, to
date, has addressed this issue at three separate meetings, all of which were
noticed to the public, and specifically mentioned the cover article in the
Winnetka Report that is sent to all residents.
At President’s Duhl’s request, Attorney Janega addressed audience comments
about the text of the draft ordinance and reviewed proposed amendments thereto.
There being a consensus among the Trustees to amend the ordinance as outlined
by Attorney Janega, President Duhl called for a motion to introduce the
ordinance incorporating those amendments. Trustee Presser so moved, seconded by
Trustee Greenough. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Webster. Nays: Trustee Woodbury. Absent: None.
Trustee Brower recused himself.
b)   Ordinance No. M-18-6-2003 – Zoning Variation: 159 Bertling Ln. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Front Setback variation to
permit the construction of a single family residence. He explained that the
applicants are seeking a setback of 17.67 ft., instead of the required 36.9
ft., because a portion of the front lot line includes a portion of an
unimproved, dedicated cul de sac. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board
of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend approval of this variation.
Ted Hammersmith, the applicants’ representative, presented an elevation and
photographs to demonstrate the need for this variation. Mr. Hammersmith
reviewed the applicants’ attempts to come up with alternative solutions,
including purchasing the unimproved property from the Village, to facilitate a
conforming design.
Trustee Presser inquired whether neighbors had voiced any objections to the
proposal. The applicant, Terry Duffy, indicated that there had been none.
Trustee Woodbury expressed discomfort with the Council’s rendering a decision
in this matter before the Building Review Committee has reviewed the demolition
permit.
Manager Williams suggested that the Trustees introduce the ordinance and await
the findings of the Building Review Committee.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce this
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl suggested that the Village attempt to find some effective way to
vacate the unimproved cul de sac in order to get the public property back on
the tax rolls.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2003 – Repealing Licensing for Milk Deliveries -
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that the Village Code is somewhat
antiquated in that it requires the licensing of milk dealers in the Village. He
reported that there is no longer any public health or sanitation reason to
require this licensing as State law has superceded local requirements and that
repealing the local licensing requirement will eliminate the possibility of
inconsistent administration and possibility of conflicts with State
regulations.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce this ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution No. R-23-2003 Prevailing Wages - Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that the Village is required to annually investigate and ascertain
the prevailing rate of wages for laborers, mechanics, and other workers in the
Village employed in performing construction of public works for the Village.
This ordinance establishes the prevailing wage rates for the Village of
Winnetka.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt this
resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1315 and 1316. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant lists, he moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1315 in the amount of $1,406,248.32 and Warrant
List No. 1316 in the amount of $734,175.57. Trustee Abell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Abell, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 824 Humboldt. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a
Garage variation in order to permit the construction of a detached garage that
will result in a rear yard setback of 0.5 ft., whereas a minimum of 2 ft. is
required. He explained the property is currently under construction pursuant to
a building permit issued in October 2002 for a new single family residence,
including a detached garage. The applicants are requesting to move the location
of the garage from that originally permitted in order to keep the garage as far
away as possible from the root system of two large trees located in the rear
yard. No other changes in design have been requested. Mr. D’Onofrio reported
that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend denial of the
variation.
After a brief discussion of the alternatives, there was a consensus to direct
the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting this variation request.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, so moved. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   Policy Direction: 1234 Westmoor. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a
Front Setback variation to permit the installation of air conditioner
condensers that would observe a corner front setback of 11.67 ft., encroaching
into the front corner yard, whereas a setback of 27.95 ft. is required. The
subject property is currently under construction, and the approved building
permit plans show that these condensers were to be placed in a conforming
location at the rear of the house. The petitioner now believes that the
originally permitted location is undesirable and is seeking to move them. Mr.
D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted to recommend
denial of the variation.
Charles Page, 100 Evergreen Ln., the applicant, spoke in support of his
request, saying that the condensers will be out of sight and produce minimal
noise.
Trustee Presser confirmed with Mr. Page that the main reason for the requested
move is to preserve the view from the back of the house. Mr. Page indicated
that it was.
Trustee Woodbury reviewed the history of the prior variation for this property.
He indicated that he was reluctant to overturn the recommendation of the Zoning
Board given that there is no rationale to do so and no legitimate hardship that
runs with the land.
Trustees Presser, Greenough, Abell, and Brower concurred.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to affirm the
recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals denying the variation request. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Presser,
Abell, and Greenough. Nays: Trustee Webster. Absent: None.
d)   Historical Society Request to Waive Charges. As noted earlier, this item
was removed from the Agenda.
e)   Sidewalk Replacement Bid. Manager Williams reviewed the bids for this
year’s Sidewalk Replacement Project. He reported that $125,000 was budgeted for
this Project and reported staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to the
low bidder, Suburban Concrete Company, Inc. for the unit prices set forth under
the bid.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
f)   Community Event/Kiosk Sign Design Contract. Manager Williams explained
that in response to discussions between the Council and the Chamber of Commerce
in July 2002, during which strategies for improving the retail climate of the
Village were discussed, staff solicited a proposal from Lakota Group to provide
for the design of a community event kiosk.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that the Council designate specific individuals to
participate in the design planning process with Lakota and Asst. Community
Development Director Brian Norkus so as to minimize confusion and expedite the
project’s completion. There was a consensus among the Trustees to do so.
Trustee Greenough was asked to serve as the Council’s representative to this
group, along with a representative from the Design Review Board and the Chamber
of Commerce.
Trustee Presser confirmed that any design generated would be taken first to the
Design Review Board and then to the Council for final approval.
President Duhl reminded all that cost to the Village is of primary concern and
that cost will play a significant role in deciding what can be with the final
design product.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to authorize staff to
enter into a professional services agreement with Lakota as outlined by Manager
Williams. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
g)   Bid No. 03-006: Poles and Luminaries. Attorney Janega reviewed State law
regarding conflict of interests. Pursuant to her analysis, Trustees Greenough
and Webster disclosed that they own shares, either individually or jointly, in
General Electric Corp., and recused themselves from the discussion and voting
on this item.
Mr. Williams, then, reviewed the bids for the purchase and delivery of poles
with decorative bases and luminaries to be installed along Tower Rd. and
Chestnut Court. He reported staff’ recommendation that the bid be awarded to
General Electric Supply Corp., the lowest bidder, in the total amount of
$60,201.30.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: None. Recusals:
Trustees Greenough and Webster.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., complimented the Village on the planning,
coordination, and work that went into the successful move of the Log House,
saying that the Tower Road residents were especially pleased that their trees
were preserved. He extended congratulations to all, in particular to Forester,
Jim Stier. Tower Rd
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to direct the Village
Manager to convey special thanks and congratulations on behalf of the Village
Council to all staff who participated in the move project.
9. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Woodbury reminded the Trustees about the Historical
Society’s open house at the Log House on Sunday afternoon.
Trustee Brower reported that the Business Community Development Committee had
met for the first time.
Trustee Webster encouraged the Trustees to participate in and attend the
Memorial Day ceremony.
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Jeff Hoch as the School Board’s
representative to the Plan Commission. The motion to approve the appointment
was made by Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Webster
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:15 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Absent:
Trustee Abell. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The meeting
adjourned at 11:16 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
May 13, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 20, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Chief of
Police Joseph DeLopez, Director of Finance Ed McKee, Assistant Director of
Public Works Alex Attiah and 7 persons in the audience.
2. Discussion: Irwin Lyons, Village Auditor.
Mr. Lyons explained his working relationship with the Village over the past 9
years and urged the Council members to contact him directly if they have
questions or wish to add analyses of special projects to the auditor’s report.
He also discussed the progress of converting the accounting system to a
business model, which will require a different type of reporting, although
general accounting procedures will remain the same. Mr. Lyons then responded to
several comments made by the Trustees.
3. Request to hold Farmer’s Market.
Mr. Schwartz, Flowers by Addante, and Mr. and Mrs. Bob Berger presented a
request to hold a Farmer’s Market on Saturday during the growing season on Mr.
Berger’s property at Green Bay Road and Merrill Street. Mr. Schwartz explained
that the market would be selling packaged foods, flowers, plants, herbs, fruits
and vegetables. He discussed the location of the vendors’ booths and licensing,
parking facilities and also answered several questions from the Trustees.
Manager Williams addressed zoning compliance, placement of the vendors’ booths
on private property vs. public property, sanitary issues, pedestrian and
vehicular traffic, parking, and business hours. He said he would continue
discussions with Mr. Schwartz regarding the hours of business and set up, and
staff would work to resolve any other issues.
After further discussion, the consensus of the Board was that they were
comfortable with the concept as long as it is on private property and is in
compliance with local ordinances.
Manager Williams publicly acknowledged and thanked Sandy Berger for her many
years of service on the Fire Pension Board and for saving the Village thousands
of dollars in that capacity.
4. Discussion: Risk Management/Insurance Review.
Ed McKee, Director of Finance, summarized the Village’s risk management and
self-insurance program. He described the steps taken to analyze the cost of
claims to determine insurance needs and compared the benefits of being self-
insured with an insurance cooperative where risk is shared. He answered several
questions from the Trustees and will obtain current quotes for automobile and
general liability premiums in the commercial market for comparison purposes at
the request of Trustee Brower.
5. Parking Fine Analysis.
Chief DeLopez reported that he had surveyed the amount imposed for parking
fines from eight communities and found them to be higher than the amount
imposed in Winnetka for similar parking violations. He suggested that parking
fines be designed so as to discourage violators from repeatedly parking in
violation of Village ordinances. He proposed that the fine for an initial
violation be raised to $20.00 and that the fines for subsequent violations
increase by increments of $20.00.
President Duhl suggested that the fines escalate faster for repeated violations
within a 12 month period. Trustee Abell agreed.
After further discussion, the Council proposed that the fine structure be $20
for the first three violations; $50 for the 4th, 5th and 6th  violations, $100
for the 7th, 8th and 9th violations and $150 for the tenth and each subsequent
violation within a 12 month period. President Duhl asked Chief DeLopez to
revise the parking fine structure for presentation to the Council at its June
3rd meeting.
6. Discussion: Proposed WYO Project.
Manager Williams reported that, in the past and in return for a $5,000 donation
from the Village to the Winnetka Youth Organization, the Council had required a
service project from the WYO. He said that when the Council last talked about
this project, they directed the staff to investigate alternative ideas for
service. He said that the WYO has worked with Assistant Director of Public
Works, Alex Attiah, and has suggested a beautification project for Green Bay
Road, north of the Elm Street bridge.
Mr. Attiah said that the WYO wanted to select a project that would be phased in
over a three year period and has developed a design for trees and beds of
flowers at Elm and Green Bay. He reported that the cost of the materials for
the project would be approximately $1,687 this year and that the WYO would
plaint and maintain the area, including watering.
Michelle May, Executive Director of WYO, explained that the organization feels
this is a project that the teens could maintain an interest in on a long term
basis. She said that it is a safe project and is tailored to the nature of the
drop in center.
Mr. Attiah said that the plantings would be done under the guidance of the
Public Works Department and was directed by President Duhl to work out the
details with the WYO.
Manager Williams reminded Mr. Attiah that the project would have to go before
the Design Review Board.
7. Village Manager Update: Wholesale Water Contract Negotiations.
Manager Williams reported that the negotiations for the contract to supply
water to the Village of Northfield were close to completion. He also explained
several of the major points in the contract and the impact on revenue and the
Village’s water distribution system.
8. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Webster
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:12 p.m.
9. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:45 p.m.
May 20, 2003
DRAFT MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 3, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
20, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David Webster. Absent:
Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
and approximately 22 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 8(b), 519 Cherry Street, had been removed
from the Agenda at the request of the applicant. The Council approved the
Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   June 3, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   June 10, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 6, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   May 13, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:38 p.m.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2003 – Liquor Service at Golf Clubhouse - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed changes made to the ordinance since introduction to
reflect the discussions at that time. She noted that in addition to minor
changes to the recitals, the ordinance had been revised to clarify that liquor
is allowed only in the food service area and that service is permitted only
from April through October on days the food service facility is open. Ms.
Janega also commented on concerns about the revisions raised by the Park
District regarding:
i)   Restricting liquor consumption to the food service area only.
ii)   Limiting liquor service “to persons the attendant at the food service
facility reasonably believes to be at the Golf Course for the principal purpose
of engaging in golfing activities.”
In commenting on the Park District’s suggested changes, she pointed out that
both of these concerns raise policy issues requiring resolution by the Council
before adoption.
Trustee Presser suggested that the language of the pertinent recital be amended
to indicate that the Village Council desires to “authorize the service of
alcoholic beverages to patrons of the Golf Course.”
President Duhl, speaking as the Liquor Commissioner, indicated that at the Golf
Course as a matter of course if the server were to ask a patron whether he/she
was at the Golf Course for the purpose of participating in golfing activities
and receive a positive response, that would be sufficient to establish
reasonable belief. Therefore, absent somebody being patently obviously not
there to play golf, asking the question would fulfil the requirements of the
license requirements. He went on to address the question of service to guests
and/or spouses who might choose to join a golfer. He pointed out that the
testimony had been predicated upon the ability to serve liquor as being a
desired part of the whole golfing experience, not being able to invite guests
in for a drink. Therefore, the service to guests is inconsistent with the
representations made. If individuals wish to gather for a drink, they can do so
elsewhere. The liquor service authorized is intended only for those
participating in golfing activities. He said that while this is somewhat
different from restaurants, there are, in fact, some differences between the
facilities, and given that, neither change suggested by the Park District ought
to be necessary and go beyond the scope of what was contemplated.
Attorney Janega explained that before any license is issued the Park District
must submit an application, including floor plans indicating where liquor will
be served, for review by the Liquor Commissioner. The designated area then
becomes the “licensed premises.”
Marc Hecht and Ann Wilder, 1096 Spruce St. reiterated previously stated
objections, and Pam Kruger, 1215 Elm St., asked questions that were answered by
President Duhl.
At the conclusion of the comments, President Duhl called for a motion to accept
the amendments reviewed by Attorney Janega, including the suggestion by Trustee
Presser. Trustee Presser so moved, seconded by Trustee Abell. By voice vote the
motion carried. Trustee Brower recused himself, and Trustee Woodbury abstained.
Trusee Abell then moved to adopt the ordinance, as amended. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Abell,
Greenough, Webster, and Presser. Nays: Trustee Woodbury. Absent: None. Trustee
Brower recused himself.
b)   Ordinance No. M-18-2003 – Zoning Variation: 159 Bertling Ln. - Adoption.
President Duhl called for a motion to adopt the ordinance granting a request
for a Front Setback variation to permit the construction of a single family
residence.
Trustee Woodbury inquired whether there had been any comment from staff yet
about selling this land pursuant to discussions at the last meeting and asked
whether the applicant was still interested in purchasing it. Manager Williams
indicated that a plan is being developed, and the applicant, Mr. Cassis,
indicated that he remained interested.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Webster, so moved. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster,
Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2003 – Repealing Licensing for Milk Deliveries -
Adoption. President Duhl called for a motion to adopt the ordinance repealing
the provision of the Village Code requiring milk dealers to be licensed.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Abell, so moved. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser,
Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-20-2003 - Zoning Variation: 824 Humboldt - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed the draft ordinance granting a Garage variation to permit
the construction of a detached garage that will result in rear yard setback of
0.5 feet, whereas a minimum of 2 feet is required. He pointed out that at its
last meeting the Council discussed this item because the Zoning Board of
Appeals had recommended against granting the variation. After considering the
facts, however, the Council had directed the Village Attorney to prepare an
ordinance granting the request.
Noting that this issue had been thoroughly discussed at the last meeting,
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to waive introduction and
proceed directly to adoption. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and
Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt this ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-19-2003 – Disposal of Surplus Equipment - Adoption.
Manager Williams explained that this ordinance is a housekeeping matter that
would permit the Village to dispose of four vehicles that have exceeded their
useful life. He requested that the Council waive introduction of this ordinance
so that the vehicles could be taken to the NWMC auction at the end of May.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to waive introduction of
this ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance
approving the disposal of the four Village vehicles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser,
Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1317 and 1318. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant lists, he moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1317 in the amount of $223,687.62 and Warrant List
No. 1318 in the amount of $738,910.32. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Abell, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 519 Cherry St. As noted previously, this item was
removed from the Agenda.
c)   Sidewalk Sale. Manager Williams reported that the Chamber of Commerce was
requesting permission to conduct its annual Sidewalk Sale on July 18 and 19. He
pointed out that the Chamber is not requesting that any streets be blocked to
vehiclular traffic except for a portion of Lincoln Avenue to accommodate the
Kodak exhibit previously approved by the Council.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Book Stall Request. Manager Williams reviewed a request from The Book
Stall for permission to allow author Steven Reichlen and his promotion Barbeque
USA Bus to park on Village property on June 7, 2003, from 3:00 p.m. to 5:00
p.m. In connection therewith, the Book Stall is also seeking permission to sell
copies of the Mr. Reichlen’s book from a location adjacent to the bus. He
reported that Mr. Reichlen plans to hand out free barbequed food products from
his bus.
The Trustees agreed that the logical location for Mr. Reichlen to park his bus
would be in front of the Book Stall. This would permit any book sales to take
place inside the Book Stall, rather than from the public sidewalk. Trustee
Woodbury suggested that the cooking activities could then be performed from an
adjacent parking space instead of from the sidewalk. Manager Williams mentioned
that all cooking would have to be inspected and cleared by the Village
Sanitarian in order to assure compliance with applicable codes.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Parkway Tree Trimming, Removal, and Maintenance Bids. Manager Williams
reviewed the bids for this year’s tree maintenance. He reported staff’s
recommendation that the current contractors’ contracts be extended at their
current rates for FY 2003-04.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to award the bid as
recommended to R. W. Henricksen Company for $29,220, Nels Johnson Tree Experts
for $17,025, and Asplundh, Inc., for $43.16 per hour. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser,
Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Directional Boring Bids. Manager Williams reviewed the bids from
contractors to perform directional boring services for the period June 1, 2003
to May 31, 2004 He explained that the contract award is based upon the
submitted unit price times the estimated quantity. He reported that the budget
contains a total of $497,000 for this service, and staff’s recommendation that
the bid be awarded to Directional Solutions, Inc. He noted that the Village is
not planning any major undergrounding projects for the immediate future, but
that this service is needed for new and existing customers who want to
underground their electrical service.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid to
Directional Solutions, Inc., as recommended, to perform such work on an “as
needed” basis. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported on a recent meeting with the Chamber of
Commerce at which the upcoming Sidewalk Sale and the fall “Winnetka on Parade”
event were discussed. He relayed a request from some members to upgrade the
lighting in the downtown area.
Trustee Webster reported on a recent orientation visit to RED Center. He also
mentioned that he and his daughter had attended the Police Department’s Bicycle
Rodeo and expressed hope that the Department would continue this kind of public
relations activity.
b)   Manager. Mr. Williams reported that there had been 32,600 visits to the
Village’s website last month and noted that, due to servicing, the Village will
be unable to update its site until after June 2nd.
Manager Williams also reported that, during work on the settling basin, several
soft spots in the concrete had been discovered that would require repair and
thus make the project more expensive than originally anticipated.
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Carol Smith as the Library Board’s
representative to the Plan Commission. The motion to approve the appointment
was made by Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Webster. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:55 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Absent:
Trustees Abell. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The meeting
adjourned at 9:56 p.m.
