Recording Secretary
June 3, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 17, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
3, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Police Chief Joseph DeLopez and
approximately 40 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   June 10, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   June 17, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Greenough, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 20, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-14-2003 – Special Use Permit & Variation: 901 Sheridan
Rd., Winnetka Park District - Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a
Special Use Permit and Side Yard variations to permit the renovation of an
existing playground at Tower Road Park Beach. He noted that this ordinance had
been introduced before the Village Council on March 18, 2003. Mr. D’Onofrio
explained the lapse of time between introduction and adoption and mentioned
that there have been no changes whatsoever to either the Special Use Permit or
Variation requests. He described the replacement and expansion plans for the
playground, noting that the new playgrounds will meet all standards for safety
and are compliant with ADA requirements.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the need for the side yard variation is due to the
fact that this area of the beach is comprised of two lots, one owned by the
Park District and the other by the Village. The new playground area bisects the
lots and, therefore, no setback is provided, where a 12-foot setback is
required.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that Design Review Board, the Plan Commission, and the
Zoning Board of Appeals had all recommended approval.
Attorney Janega reviewed amendments incorporated into the introduced ordinance
to reflect changes to the legal descriptions, as well as to include findings
from the Zoning Board meetings.
Rick Schram, the Park District’s representative, showed drawings of the new
equipment and color samples.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend the ordinance as
recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees: Woodbury,
Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-21-2003 – Zoning Variations: 620 Ash Street -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Setback variation to
permit the construction of a two-story addition at the rear of the home and the
replacement of the existing, detached garage with a new detached garage that
would have the same nonconforming corner setback as the building addition. He
mentioned that the home has a legally nonconforming corner yard setback. The
proposed construction involves the removal of a breakfast room and its
replacement with a window well that does not meet existing standards, as well
as the addition of a deck and the two-story addition.
Manager Williams noted that with regard to the garage request a question has
arisen over the interpretation of the ordinance and indicated that staff is
presently working with the applicant to resolve the issue before adoption.
John Myefski, architect for the applicant, provided an overall view of the site
and discussed the reasons for the proposed design. He used elevations to show
how the applicants are attempting to maintain the architectural integrity of
the structure and the neighborhood.
Trustee Presser commented that it appears to him that the applicants are trying
to do exactly what the Council is trying to encourage.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. M-22-2003 – Special Use Permit & Variation: 1112 Willow Rd.,
Crow Island School – Introduction & Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request
for a Special Use Permit and Setback variations to allow for replacement of
playground equipment at Crow Island School, noting that it is for the third
playground and similar to ones reviewed by the Council last year for the other
two playgrounds. He reported that the Design Review Board and the Zoning Board
of Appeals had voted in favor of the requests. Mr. D’Onofrio also reported that
the School had requested that the Council waive introduction of this ordinance
in order to expedite the process of beginning work.
Scott Freres, the landscape designer, reviewed the specifics of the site plan,
mentioning that impermeable surface would, in fact, be decreased and that the
site will be underdrained to avoid flooding.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to waive introduction of the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Sacred Heart School & Parish. Attorney Janega and Trustee Presser
disclosed that they are associated with Sacred Heart Church but hold no
ownership interest.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed requests from Sacred Heart to permit the expansion of
the school, the development of a parish center, and the rezoning of the 919
Burr property.
Susan Connor, the School’s representative, explained each of their requests in
detail, but the project’s architect and the relevant elevations were not
available for this meeting. She reported that the Church had held multiple
meetings with neighbors and parishioners to discuss the proposals. She
emphasized that the needs that the Church is addressing with these proposals
for the school and the parish center are not related to anticipated growth, but
are rather an attempt to accommodate the existing school and parish population.
Ms. Connor commented on the parking and traffic impact study. Following
questions from the Trustees, Manager Williams remarked that the Traffic
Engineer does not have any significant concerns about increased parking
problems and that he feels that the traffic flow control systems are good.
Trustee Woodbury asked Ms. Connor to be certain that she had elevations
available for the next meeting.
   i)   Ordinance No. M-23-2003 – Special Use Permit & Variations: 1095 Gage
St., Sacred Heart School - Introduction. Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee
Greenough, moved to introduce this ordinance that would grant a Special Use
Permit and variations to permit the expansion of Sacred Heart School. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   ii)   Ordinance No. M-24-2003 – Special use Permit & Variations: 1077 Tower/
919 Burr, Sacred Heart Parish - Introduction. Trustee Brower, seconded by
Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce this ordinance that would grant a Special
Use Permit and variations to permit the construction of a one and two-story
parish center. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   iii)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2003 – Rezoning: 919 Burr - Introduction. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce this ordinance that
would grant a zoning map amendment to rezone 919 Burr to B-1 Multi-family
Residential. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2003 – Increase in Parking Fines - Introduction.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed increases to the parking fines in accordance
with Council’s discussion at its May 13th meeting. She noted that the ordinance
provided the greatest incentive for paying early with increasing penalty for
delaying payment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce this ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Resolution No. R-24-2003 – Establishing Fee for Class P Liquor License -
Adoption. Manager Williams explained that Resolution R-24-2003 reflects the
addition of a $500 fee to the Village’s miscellaneous fee resolution for the
newly established Class P liquor license for the Park District. The amount is
50% of the amount for a Class A-1 license, reflecting the fact that the Golf
Course Clubhouse will only be open for liquor service from April 1 through
October 31.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster,
Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Resolution No. R-25-2003 – MFT Funding for Street Rehabilitation -
Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that this resolution would authorize the
Village to use $174,000 of MFT funds for this year’s Street Rehabilitation
Project.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2003 – Class P Liquor License - Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed technical amendments to the Liquor Ordinance as it pertains tot
he Class P liquor license resulting from a drafting oversight during the
composition of the final ordinance.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance MC-10-2003. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Greenough, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: Trustee Woodbury. Absent:
None.
Given that the motion required a unanimous vote by the Council to proceed to
directly to adoption, Trustee Woodbury changed his vote to “aye.”
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-10-
2003. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Abell,
Greenough, Brower, and Webster. Nays: Trustee Woodbury. Absent: None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1319 and 1320. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant lists, he moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1319 in the amount of $1,309,037.17 and Warrant
List No. 1320 in the amount of $192,568.64. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Abell, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   NSCDS Development Agreement. Mr. D’Onofrio informed the Council that
pursuant to their direction at the time the Special Use Permit and variations
for the North Shore Country Day School project were granted, the School and the
neighbors have met several times in an effort to resolve the parking and
traffic issues between them before the approval of the Development Agreement.
He reviewed several recommendations to alleviate the problems, several of which
have already been implemented by the School.
He reported that with the most recent draft of the Development Agreement, the
Village and the School are close to agreement on almost all of the issues,
except for some items specific to parking and traffic circulation and
sprinkling improvements. He noted that in the most recent draft that has not
yet been fully reviewed by staff, the School has agreed to sprinkler the new
addition as well as existing portions of the building contiguous to the newly
developed space. Therefore, only a few items relating thereto remain to be
ironed out. In addition, the latest draft contains proposals regarding
construction/employee parking during construction.
Manager Williams commented that normally the Council conceptually sanctions a
Development Agreement and leaves staff to iron out the final details.
Attorney Janega observed that the amendments received to the draft appear to
address all key concerns, including the sprinkler issue and parking
alternatives. She recommended that if the Council is inclined to approve the
Agreement, it could authorize staff to finalize the details.
Hal Franke, 1163 Cherry, attorney for NSCDS, summarized the School’s efforts to
come to resolution with the neighbors, saying that he believed there had been
fruitful and productive discussions among the parties. He indicated that
programmatic and physical changes are being considered to alleviate some of the
problems and pointed out that the School views this process as an ongoing one.
Mr. Franke commented briefly on the viability of some of the recommendations
and committed to School to continue to pursue long-term and short-term
solutions to traffic and parking problems. In so doing, he indicated that the
School remains in favor of one-way traffic on Forest. Finally, Mr. Franke
highlighted changes incorporated into the latest draft of the Development
Agreement.
John Rexwaller, President of the Board of Trustees of NSCDS, thanked the
Council for its time and consideration. He urged the Council to approve the
Development Agreement so that the School can begin with the much-needed
upgrades to its facilities.
Mr. Williams asked Steve Saunders, Village Traffic Engineer, and Joe DeLopez,
Police Chief to comment on the traffic and parking recommendations, which they
did. In doing so, both strongly supported making Forest one way for brief
periods of time in the morning and afternoon, similar to what is done at other
schools in the Village.
President Duhl asked Mr. Saunders and Chief DeLopez whether they believed the
School had done everything it reasonably could to develop a workable resolution
to the parking and traffic concerns raised by neighbors. Mr. Saunders indicated
that, although not everyone is pleased with the outcome, he believed that from
an engineering standpoint the School has examined every alternative that could
be implemented, some of which had to be rejected for reasons of cost, loss of
green space, internal circulation issues, etc. Chief DeLopez concurred in his
assessment of the School’s efforts and the recommendations implemented.
Lou Ramsdell, 340 Forest, thanked the Village for its efforts in this issue.
Phil Goss, 266 Forest, indicated that overall his opinions had not changed in
that he believes there are on-site solutions that are options, but complimented
the School on the changes it has made to date that have improved the situation
tremendously. He expressed concern about continuing efforts to find solutions
to the parking/traffic problems.
James Lumberg, 672 Elder, John Roskopf, 258 Forest, Jim Colley, 302 Forest, and
Robin Thomas, 638 Elder, were also complimentary of the School’s efforts. Mr.
Goss and Mr. Roskopf spoke against the one-way traffic on Forest; Mr. Colley
and Ms. Thomas voiced support for it.
Given some continuing opposition among the neighbors to making Forest one way
for brief periods of the day, President Duhl suggested that Chief DeLopez and
Mr. Saunders meet one more time with the neighborhood in an effort to convince
them that this is, in fact, the safest and best solution.
President Duhl called for a motion to approve the Development Agreement
substantially in the form presented, to be fully negotiated by staff to reflect
Council’s discussions. Trustee Woodbury so moved. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Abell,
Greenough, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. Trustee Brower
recused himself.
c)   Policy Direction: 519 Cherry St. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a
Maximum Building Size variation to permit the construction of a room addition
at 519 Cherry Street, noting that this property is already nonconforming with
respect to gross floor area. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted to recommend denial of this request.
Healy Rice, architect for the applicant, presented the applicant’s case, citing
the awkward location and design of the garage as hardships.
President Duhl pointed out that for the purpose of a zoning variation, the
hardship has to run with the land, not be a function of the house itself and
noted that it is particularly difficult to justify a GFA variance, especially
when the existing property already exceeds the allowable GFA.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to concur with the
recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury,
Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Village Yards Update. Mr. Saunders reported that the Village Yards project
is progressing well and that the administration building is now occupied. He
said that the project is running about two months behind the original schedule
and is currently about $184,577 below the original approved project budget,
noting that Phase II has not yet started.
e)   2003 Tree Planting Extension. Mr. Saunders reported staff’s recommendation
that the annual parkway tree planting award be made to St. Aubin’s Nursery in
the amount of $30,000. St. Aubin’s has extended their rates from last year to
the current year.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. Reports.
None.
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the reappointment of Luvie Myers to the Plan
Commission for a second three-year term. The motion to approve the appointment
was made by Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously
11. Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, Executive Session was cancelled.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The meeting
adjourned at 11:22 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
June 10, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: June 17, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director
of Public Works Steve Saunders, Village Forester Jim Stier and 30 persons in
the audience.
2. Pine Street Sidewalk.
Manager Williams updated the Council on the number of votes received as of this
afternoon for and against the construction of a sidewalk along Pine Street
which showed that 73% were in favor of a sidewalk on the south side of Pine
Street and 90% were against a sidewalk on the north side of the street.
President Duhl explained that at its February 18th meeting, after hearing
public comment, the Council adopted sidewalk policy guidelines and, in
accordance with those guidelines, a sidewalk will be constructed on the south
side of Pine Street, unless it can be determined that there are extraordinary
circumstances that would warrant different action by the Council. He said that
before polling the Council members, he would ask persons who oppose the
sidewalk to present their opinions to the Council so that the circumstances can
be considered and a decision made.
Dan Kadjan, 1124 Pine, Steve Baird, 1154 Pine, and Sharon King, 1134 Pine,
spoke in opposition of the sidewalk, stating that the neighbors on both sides
of the street in the block between Glendale and Berkeley are 100% against
construction of a sidewalk. They reported that pedestrian use is mainly school
children who turn onto Glendale from Pine or cut through the park to get to
school. They also were concerned about damage to or loss of trees along the
street and requested that the Village exempt their block from sidewalk
construction.
There were no further comments opposed to sidewalk construction.
President Duhl responded to a question about the rationalization for the
sidewalk guidelines and what is provided for in the comprehensive plan by
saying that it contains several general statements about traffic and directs
the Village to continue to monitor safety concerns. He said the Village’s
current policy is to have sidewalks on both sides of major streets and a
sidewalk along one side of minor streets, to protect mature trees and to
maintain a country feeling. He noted that Pine Street is a collector street and
provides for children to get to school safely. He said that the Council tries
to accommodate the neighbors wishes where possible, but can override them if
circumstances warrant.
The Trustees asked for comments from the Village Engineer, Steve Saunders,
regarding pedestrian traffic counts and construction of the sidewalk, and from
the Village Forester, Jim Stier, regarding the methods that would be used to
protect and minimize trees from damage during construction.
Mr. Saunders reported that there was very little pedestrian traffic during the
day, as stated by the homeowners who were against the sidewalk being built in
their block, except for the peak hours when school children were walking to and
from school. However, he felt that forcing pedestrians off the sidewalk for
just one block presented a safety concern. He further stated that the best
possible protection for the trees would be accomplished by choosing a route for
the sidewalk as far as possible from them, to keep the sidewalk raised above
the roots of the trees, and to prune the roots while excavating. He said the
staff is working with Hendriksen, The Care of Trees and an arborist to limit
the damage to trees.
Mr. Stier explained that the sidewalk could be constructed in the fall after
the trees begin to go dormant, an air spade could be used to blow dirt from the
roots rather than digging, and workers could hand dig around roots to minimize
damage. He would also monitor the trees and provide follow up care or replace
them if necessary.
Trustee Presser said he has not heard a compelling need to put in a sidewalk
between Berkeley and Glendale and would not be in favor of constructing a
sidewalk in that block.
Trustee Woodbury said that he is in favor of a sidewalk from Hibbard to
Glendale, but has some reservations about a sidewalk west of Glendale, since
the neighbors in that block are so fundamentally opposed to the sidewalk. He
said he tended to agree with Trustee Presser on this point, but will go with
the majority opinion.
Trustee Abell commented that he agrees with the 20/20 Comprehensive Plan and
the Council’s own policy guidelines that sidewalks should be continuous. He
said it does not make sense to exempt one block of the street from the sidewalk
construction.
Trustee Greenough said the immediate precedent for this question was the Tower
Road sidewalk, where the numbers for and against were approximately the same.
She noted that the evaluation at the time was that discontinuous sidewalks were
potential safety hazards and that, given the majority view of the neighbors on
the street, taking one block out didn’t make any sense.
Trustee Brower thought the main issue was safety and that to leave out a block,
forcing people into the street, would not be in the interest of safety. He said
that, subject to the Village bending over backwards to preserve trees and
accommodate neighbors who have particular needs, such as newly installed
driveways and drains, he would be in favor of constructing the sidewalk all the
way.
Trustee Webster agreed with Trustee Brower’s comments. He added that the
Council had been conservative about cutting down trees in the Village and had
gone to extraordinary lengths to protect them. He asked that the tree that will
be lost at 1134 Pine Street be replaced by the Village. He also expressed his
appreciation of the efforts of persons on both sides of the issue who had
circulated petitions, attended meetings and participated in the public
discussions.
President Duhl announced that, by a majority, the Council had agreed to uphold
the sidewalk policy in this case, without an exception, and directed the staff
to construct a sidewalk along the entire south side of Pine Street. He
reiterated the need for sensitivity to the trees and had confidence that the
Forester and the Village Engineer would do everything possible, including
spending additional funds and following the recommendations of professional
arborists, to minimize the damage to trees on this project.
President Duhl expressed his thanks to everyone involved in the issue. He asked
that residents recognize that the Council had taken the time to read all of the
documents submitted and had given them an opportunity to work out a solution
and provide the Council with guidance. He added that, although the decision may
be disappointing to some, the Council has tried to do what is best for the
community at large.
3. Executive Session.
Trustee Webster moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Brower
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees David Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David
Webster, and Edmund Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened
into executive session at 8:30 p.m.
4. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees David Abell,
Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Edmund
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 9:16 p.m.
June 17, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 1, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
17, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser, and David Webster. Absent: Trustees Nan
Greenough and Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Finance
Director Ed McKee and approximately seven persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that item 8(d), the bid for the loading dock and
stairs, had been removed from the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda as
amended.
4. Quorum
a)   July 1, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   July 8, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 3, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   June 10, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-21-2003 – Zoning Variations: 620 Ash Street - Adoption.
President Duhl called for a motion to adopt a Setback variation to permit the
construction of a two-story addition at the rear of the home. Trustee Abell,
seconded by Trustee Webster, so moved. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Greenough and Woodbury.
b)   Sacred Heart School & Parish. The project’s architect, Bill Sturm,
reviewed elevations for the Trustees and commented on the choice of building
materials. In response to a question raised by Trustee Woodbury via email, Mr.
Sturm, in particular, discussed the merits of the Renaissance stone chosen for
the façade of the parish center, saying that the product represents a premium
to brick and relates contextually to both the rectory and the Church.
Mr. Norkus reviewed the Design Review Board’s process regarding the Church’s
requests and their reliance on the Design Guidelines for guidance. He reported
on the Board’s discussions about the building material, saying that the general
reaction of the Board was that the proposed material is consistent with the
adjacent church and contextually appropriate.
Trustee Presser asked Mr. Norkus whether the Design Review Board had seen
samples of the material and raised no objections. Mr. Norkus confirmed this. He
pointed out that the Design Guidelines leave room for interpretation from one
project to another and that one of the issues with Renaissance stone is that
there is significant variation in quality.
President Duhl inquired whether there had been any discussion of using real
limestone.
Both Mr. Sturm and Ms. Connor responded that limestone would make the project
cost prohibitive.
   i)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2003 – Rezoning: 919 Burr - Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the ordinance. Trustee Brower, seconded
by Trustee Presser, moved to amend this ordinance that would grant a zoning map
amendment to rezone 919 Burr to B-1 Multi-family Residential as recommended by
the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Greenough and
Woodbury.
   ii)   Ordinance No. M-23-2003 – Special Use Permit & Variations: 1095 Gage
St., Sacred Heart School - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed
amendments to the ordinance, and Trustee Webster suggested some minor language
changes. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to amend this
ordinance that would grant a Special Use Permit and variations to permit the
expansion of Sacred Heart School as recommended by the Village Attorney and
Trustee Webster. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Greenough and
Woodbury.
   iii)   Ordinance No. M-24-2003 – Special Use Permit & Variations: 1077
Tower/919 Burr, Sacred Heart Parish - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed
proposed amendments to the ordinance. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee
Presser, moved to amend this ordinance that would grant a Special Use Permit
and variations to permit the construction of a one and two-story parish center
as recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Greenough and
Woodbury.
President Duhl noted that in keeping with Village policy regarding similar
types of projects, a Development Agreement would have to be negotiated before a
building permit is issued. He polled the Trustees to determine whether they
were satisfied with the Renaissance stone, or whether they felt the matter
should be returned to the Design Review Board for further consideration.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Norkus whether he felt that the matter had been
fully vetted at the DRB. Mr. Norkus responded that he believed that it had been
well addressed and that in this case, the Renaissance stone falls within the
spirit and the intent of the Design Guidelines and is acceptable.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to not return the matter to the Design
Review Board for reconsideration.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2003 – Increase in Parking Fines - Adoption. President
Duhl called for a motion to adopt the proposed increases to the parking fines.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, so moved. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Webster, and Presser.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Greenough and Woodbury.
d)   Ordinance No. M-25-2003 – Equipment Disposal - Introduction. Mr. McKee
explained that this ordinance would authorize the disposal of surplus Village
equipment and unclaimed property that is being held by the Police Department.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Request to Redirect Willow Road Truck Traffic. Linda Kramer, 370 Glendale
and Robert Kean, 373 Glendale, presented a request that heavy construction
traffic, particularly cement mixers, be rerouted to prohibit their traveling
north and south on Glendale to get to Willow Road. They cited safety concerns
for children attending Crow Island School as the reason for their request.
Manager Williams reported that both the Police Chief and the Village’s Traffic
Engineer had analyzed the situation and concluded that no change was warranted.
He noted that either a crossing guard and/or Community Service Officer controls
crossing at the intersection at critical hours and neither had reported any
problem with construction trucks or traffic at the intersection. In addition,
there are no recorded traffic accident reports relating to construction traffic
and pedestrian conflict at that intersection. Mr. William pointed out that
rerouting truck traffic here would only serve to push local construction
traffic onto other neighborhood streets.
Mr. Kean suggested that making Glendale one-way southbound might mitigate the
situation somewhat. Manager Williams indicated that he would have Staff look at
this option and report back to the Council.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1321 and 1322. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant lists, he moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1321 in the amount of $641,126.37 and Warrant List
No. 1322 in the amount of $703,442.98. Trustee Webster seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Abell,
Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Greenough and Woodbury.
c)   Bid No. 03-014 Northfield Service Line Water Main. Manager Williams
reviewed the bids for the installation of a water main, valves, and metering to
provide the Village of Northfield with another service point at Hibbard and
Hill Roads. He reported Staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to
Mosele & Associates in the total amount of $49,060.50.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Abell, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Greenough and
Woodbury.
d)   Bid No. 03-015 Loading Dock & Stairs. As previously noted, this item was
removed from the Agenda.
e)   Insurance Review & Risk Management. Mr. McKee reported that because the
insurance market has changed substantially over the past few years, the risk
retention levels of the Village have increased significantly in some areas. He
recommended that the Village engage Nugent Consulting Group to perform a
complete review of the existing insurance policies, risk retention practices of
the Village, and options available to manage risk at an hourly rate of $140,
not to exceed $7,500 in total.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Abell, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Greenough and
Woodbury.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the new Winnetka Report would be
mailed soon.
Trustee Brower reported that the Chamber of Commerce was working on plans for a
fall festival weekend, September 9-11.
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Bob Zielinski as Chairman of the
Landmark Preservation Commission. The motion to approve the appointment was
made by Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Webster. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Presser moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Webster
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Greenough and Woodbury. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:10
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Presser, and Webster. Absent: Trustees Abell, Greenough
and Woodbury. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Presser, and
Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. The
meeting adjourned at 10:16 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
July 1, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 15, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
1, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee Greenough. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
and approximately four persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance and called for a
moment of silence in remembrance of the young adults killed in the recent porch
collapse in Chicago.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   July 8, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   July 15, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 17, 2003, Regular Meeting. Village Manager Williams submitted a
revised draft of the minutes, explaining that certain editing corrections were
made to the previously circulated draft minutes. Trustee Brower moved to adopt
the minutes as revised. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-25-2003 – Equipment Disposal - Adoption. Trustee Abell,
seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance authorizing the
disposal of surplus Village equipment and unclaimed property that is being held
by the Police Department. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Greenough.
b)   Ordinance No. M-28-2003 – Landmark Designation: 739 Elm Street -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the application requesting that the New
Trier Township Building be designated as a Winnetka Landmark. He recounted the
history of the building and some of its notable architectural features. He
reported that, based upon the adopted system for Evaluation of Landmarks, the
property received an overall score of 83.2 points, resulting in a “unique”
rating.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-26-2003 – Zoning Variation: 910 Greenwood - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot (roofed lot
coverage) and Building Line Articulation variations to permit the construction
of a one-story addition. He pointed out that the property is currently legal
nonconforming with respect to roofed lot coverage and reported that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend approval of the variation.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-27-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1255 Westmoor – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front and Corner Yard Setbacks to permit
the construction of an in-ground swimming pool to replace an existing pool that
has outlived its useful life. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 6-0 to recommend approval of the variation.
Bernie Hammer, 1455 Tower Rd., raised a question about whether the root system
of the existing large trees would be protected from damage during the
construction process and pointed to a different instance in which contractors
had not treated existing trees with care.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Village Forester had approved the plans for
this property and that the Community Development Department monitors
contractors throughout the construction process.
Trustee Brower inquired as to whether the Village has any recourse down the
road if trees damaged during construction die several years later.
Manager Williams responded that the Forestry Commission would be looking into
some new proposals and would report its findings to the Council.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ordinance No. R-26-2003 – Release of Executive Session Minutes - Adoption. Mr.
Williams explained that this is a Village housekeeping duty required by State
law to be performed every six months. Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee
Presser, moved to approve the minutes of the Executive Sessions held January 7,
2003, through June 10, 2003. By voice vote, the motion carried. Trustee Abell
abstained.
Trustee Presser then moved to adopt Resolution No. R-26-2003 releasing those
minutes no longer required to be held for reasons of confidentiality. Trustee
Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Greenough. Trustee Abell abstained.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1323 and 1324. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant lists, he moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1323 in the amount of $923,794.32 and Warrant List
No. 1324 in the amount of $833,291.82. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Abell,
Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough.
b)   Recycling Contract Extension. Manager Williams explained that in 1998 the
Village had gone through the competitive bidding process before awarding the
recycling contract to BFI (now Onyx). That contract provided for the
possibility of administrative extension for approximately a CPI adjustment. He
recommended that the Council ratify staff’s extension of this contract for one
more year until a thorough study and complete analysis of options can be
completed before going out again for competitive bids
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to ratify the extension of
the agreement with Onyx Recycling for a period of one year, with fees to be
increased by 1.1%. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Greenough.
c)   Quarterly Report: Power Cost Adjustment.. Manager Williams reported that
the power cost adjustment is performing as designed. He explained that this
adjustment balances power costs over time without creating wild swings in costs
to customers.
d)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Manager Williams directed the
Trustees’ attention to the reports filed by department heads. He reported that
everything is on track and pointed out that the replacement of key splices on
distribution lines was about 50% completed.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
Mr. Hammer raised the issue of implementing a real estate transfer tax that
would bring income to the Village and suggested possible ways to do so without
the community becoming home rule.
President Duhl, Attorney Janega, and Mr. Hammer discussed the matter for a
brief period of time.
9. Reports.
Trustees. Trustee Webster reported that, as the Village’s representative to RED
Center, he had checked with Chief Colpaert and would vote in favor of the
Center’s purchasing its property from Northbrook and in support of hiring an
additional assistant.
Trustee Brower reported that he and Trustee Abell had attended a recent meeting
of the Ad Hoc Business Development Committee and that the Committee was well on
its way toward completing its mission.
Trustee Woodbury reported that he had recently attended the annual meeting of
the Historical Society at which Louise Holland and Nancy Judge had been
appointed co-presidents.
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Charlie Hoffman to the Landmark
Preservation Commission for a full five year term. The motion to approve the
appointment was made by Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Abell. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Greenough. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:23 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Absent: Trustee
Greenough. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower, Presser,
Woodbury, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Greenough. The
meeting adjourned at 10:16 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
July 8, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: August 5, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster and Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development and 30 persons in the audience.
2. Meet with Caucus.
Mr. Joe Fell, of the Winnetka Caucus, asked the Village Council for their input
as to questions for the annual caucus survey that would provide the Council
with direction from the community on policy issues.
The Trustees suggested submitting questions on businesses and shopping in
Winnetka, curbside garbage pick up, demolition and preservation issues,
appearance review for new construction, impact of zoning changes, construction
of a parking lot, a smoking ban and recent changes the liquor ordinance.
Mr. Fell concluded the discussion by saying that the committees for the schools
and parks will also develop questions and will meet with the Village Caucus
Committee in late July. He said questions would be finalized some time in
August.
3. Discussion of Ways to Streamline Zoning Variance Process.
Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio stated that the purpose of
this discussion is to find a way to streamline the zoning variation process but
still meet the expectations of Village residents. He said that, due to the
number of applications, hearings on applications before the Zoning Board are
now being scheduled for November. He would like to consider giving the Zoning
Board greater authority to grant certain variations so that an appearance
before the Council would not be necessary.
Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus reported the results of
an analysis of the types of variations requested and the number that eventually
come before the Village Council. He said that in 2002, 22% of the caseload
involved GFA requests, 18% were for roofed lot coverage, and 30% for front and
corner yard setbacks. He said that the Zoning Board faces proportionally
greater GFA variation requests because the ZBA can make final determinations
for variations up to 20%. He further stated that because of changes in the
zoning ordinance, all building articulation variation requests must go before
the Village Council.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that 77% of the applications for zoning variation requests
are considered by the Village Council for final determination, which translates
to 44 zoning cases per year and 88 agenda items for introduction and adoption.
He said that it takes 9 weeks from the time the request is submitted to
adoption by the Village Council and 4 to 5 weeks if the variation can be
granted by the Zoning Board, however, the backlog is such that a request today
would not be heard until November and construction would not commence until
Spring. In addition, he said that each request can take up to 8 hours of staff
preparation time, which does not include meeting times, nor the Village
Attorney’s time for preparing an ordinance, which can add another 3 to 4 hours.
Mr. D’Onofrio suggested: (1) that variation requests on new homes built from
1989 to the present continue to go before the Council regardless of the
request; (2) that the authority of the Zoning Board be increased to allow the
Board to have the final determination in cases with more than a 20% variation;
and (3) that the Director of Community Development be authorized to have final
determination on minor variation requests, following the same process in terms
of legal notice, notice to neighbors and hearings, with an appeal of the
decision to the Zoning Board possible in the event a variation is denied.
Manager Williams stated that it is key that the Council always review special
use permits, floodplain issues and new construction. He suggested that the
Zoning Board make the final decisions on pre-1989 variation requests.
Village Attorney Janega suggested that the Council consider it from the
perspective of whether the Council would always accept the ZBA’s recommendation
for a particular variation request. She reminded the Council that the Trustees
change and because it is a policy making body, it must not keep future councils
from exercising their right to decide. She recommended that any variation that
accompanies a subdivision or consolidation go before the Council, as well as
variation requests in the floodplain and special uses. She also suggested
setting thresholds in the Village Code chapter on nonconformities for
determining approval of requests for alterations in existing nonconforming
structures, so that they can be handled on an administrative basis.
Zoning Board Chairperson Margaret Benson agreed that Village staff could do
more administratively. She said that many variation requests are for existing
nonconformities for houses built before 1920, and are almost always approved to
encourage remodeling of older houses and that the variations needed for adding
a window are ridiculous. She commented that the Zoning Board should be able to
handle multiple variations and greater percentages with an appeal to the
Village Council possible.
Sandy Berger, a Zoning Board member, recommended that a historical analysis be
done for the Council, listing cases where the Council disagreed with the Zoning
Board’s recommendation. She felt that there was a need to put more pressure on
the architect and the homeowner to focus on hardship.
Jane Dowding, a Zoning Board member, said she was in favor of authorizing the
Village staff to determine minor ZBA cases, such as variations required to
maintain architectural integrity, windows, etc.
Ms. Benson said that she also agrees with Manager Williams that the Zoning
Board have final determination about construction before 1989.
Doug Harkness, 533 Hawthorn Lane, said he was troubled by the number of
variation requests that have been approved. He said that while past proposals
have been good, they are not treating the source of the problem. He commented
that the zoning ordinance has been rewritten to such a degree that 90% of the
homes in Winnetka are nonconforming. He believes that the zoning ordinance
should make most homes conforming and be objective and fair, so that people can
read and understand whether their house meets it or not. He asked the Council
not to deal with the symptoms, but to look at the source of problem, which is
the zoning ordinance.
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce, commented that the changes made to the zoning
ordinance have created more nonconformities and suggested having one zoning
ordinance for new houses and one for existing homes.
President Duhl suggested that Mr. Harkness and Mr. Frank were actually
addressing the zoning changes, which are a later item on the agenda. He stated
that the pending issue is to streamline the procedure for variation requests to
reduce the number of cases going before the Zoning Board and the Village
Council. He directed Village staff to develop procedures reflecting their
recommendations and to bring them back for further discussion at a future study
session.
Trustee Webster asked if there would be any additional cost if these new
suggestions were implemented.
Mr. Norkus replied that new procedures would involve less staff time and less
attorney time and so the fiscal impact should be favorable.
4. Whitebridge Hill Association Letter.
Mr. John Mengel, 1147 Whitebridge Hill, stated he was speaking on behalf of 20
neighbors who are in opposition to a pending demolition permit at 1150
Whitebridge Hill. He said the neighbors have been working through the Building
Review Committee and that process has led them to question some interpretations
of the Village Code. He said that the proposed house would be 75 % over the
floor area ratio of the average home in the neighborhood. He acknowledged that
it is not the job of Village Council to determine if the project is a detriment
to neighborhood, but the Building Review Committee had suggested that a
representative of the neighbors seek the Council’s clarification and direction.
He said that the neighbors are appreciative of this opportunity and that the
existing ordinance language does answer some of the questions and suggests that
existing property owners have more rights than the developing homeowner. He
introduced Mr. Charles Crook, former director of planning and development in
Lake Forest.
Mr. Crook summarized the issues as he understood them and offered suggestions
based on Lake Forest’s zoning ordinances. He stated that Lake Forest will not
issue a demolition permit without knowing what the replacement house will look
like and can impose a 2-year delay in demolition. He said that this has worked
well, but is not perfect and that the question is how to balance the property
rights of new property owners with the rights of neighboring property owners
and the community as a whole.
President Duhl advised Mr. Crook that the Council has struggled with this issue
and that they are legally constrained according to their counsel’s advice.
Village Attorney Janega commented that the Building Review Committee is not
authorized to deny demolition permits and can only impose a delay if it is
determined that there is an impact on the neighborhood and there is historical
or architectural significance. She said that during the delay, the applicant
can be required to meet and explore alternatives to demolition. She advised the
Council not to interfere with the pending permit application, but to reiterate
ordinance language.
President Duhl stated that the Council does not authorize the denial of a
demolition permit and State law does not allow the community to deny a
demolition permit; it can only delay issuance. He said that he understands that
Lake Forest will expedite the permit, if the applicant submits to design
review, but Winnetka does not have that process in place.
Attorney Janega commented that if Winnetka had a mandatory landmark designation
ordinance with binding design review, it could say no to demolition of exterior
features of historic landmark buildings, but Winnetka does not have that type
of ordinance.
President Duhl stated that Winnetka residents are not willing to accept that
type of thing and that discussions on landmarks several years ago split the
community.
Mr. Crook replied to questions posed by the Trustees, saying that in Lake
Forest, the design guidelines are only for replacement structures and are
binding depending on the eye of the beholder. He further stated that if an
applicant is willing to wait out the 2-year delay, they can demolish a home and
build the home they want, without design review.
Trustee Webster asked what action the Council could take in the Whitebridge
Hill agenda item.
Attorney Janega replied that the Council cannot do anything directed at this
particular application, because it has delegated its discretion to the Building
Review Committee. She said the Council has never interfered with current
applications in process, but that changes can be made applicable to future
applications.
Mr. Mengel said that he is mystified by the Village Attorney’s definitive
interpretation of the words in the Code, and suggested that under the existing
Code, the Council clarify that the Building Review Committee have authority to
deny a demolition permit.
President Duhl said that it is not only Ms. Janega’s opinion, but also that of
the Village Council, which has several attorneys on it. He said the Council had
decided that a 60-day delay is what they could impose and the ordinance clearly
does not allow denial of this demolition permit.
Mr. Mengel said that this is unfortunate and hoped that the Council would be
supportive of the Building Review Committee.
Trustee Greenough asked if there is any other vehicle by which a demolition
permit could be denied.
Attorney Janega said the only ones that have been sustained in court are those
in landmark designated districts in the context of landmark preservation.
Trustee Greenough informed Mr. Crook that part of the compilation of the
ordinance took into consideration how to protect the impact on the neighborhood
and she explained that the ordinance was changed so that an owner could
consider renovation rather than demolition before committing funds to
architectural plans.
Mr. Crook replied that Winnetka’s 60-day delay is not long enough for
homeowners to consider renovation rather than demolition. He said that Lake
Forest’s ordinance was to address developers rather than individual homeowners.
He also thinks that there have recently been design review ordinances that have
been upheld in court.
Attorney Janega replied that there may have been some cases in outlying
districts that address anti-monotony ordinances that apply to new subdivisions.
Those ordinances usually don’t allow two houses of the same design to be next
door to each other to prevent the repetition of certain styles. She said that
type of design review has been upheld, but that design review on a lot-by-lot
design review for redevelopment has not.
Trustee Woodbury commented that it might be time to have some discussion about
revising the ordinance to address the impact of the replacement structure
rather than imposing longer delays.
President Duhl said that, in prior discussions, the Council rejected imposing
longer delays, which is what the community indicated they wanted in their
response to the caucus surveys.
Mr. Mengel inquired whether the applicant could be made to talk to the Council
and neighbors.
Attorney Janega said that the Council could urge parties to meet, but it cannot
direct the Building Review Committee to make its decision based on the results
of that discussion. She reiterated that the ordinance provides for further
delay only if historical or architectural significance is found and any action
is taken through the Building Review Committee.
Victoria Granacki, the consultant for the applicant, stated that her study
showed this house was not architecturally or historically significant. Building
review Committee Chairman Keith Groebe stated that the Building Review
Committee is aware of the requirements of the demolition ordinance and added
that the Committee had not yet made a final determination on the issue of
significance.
President Duhl concluded by saying that the Council is urging the parties to
talk, but does not have any power in this matter.
5. Analysis of 2002 Zoning Amendments.
Director of Community Development, Michael D’Onofrio, reported on the effects
of the changes to the bulk standards in the zoning ordinance over the last
year. He showed examples of new construction in each of the districts under the
current zoning regulations, comparing floor heights and roof pitch. He noted
that some of the houses had dormers that peak above the roof or give that
appearance. He compared the differences of new construction in each of the
zoning districts, stating that in most areas, maximum allowances were used. He
also showed homes built before the changes in the ordinance were enacted and
compared them to homes built afterward. He said the most notable differences
are in the slope of the roofs. Mr. D’Onofrio concluded by saying that roofs
have been flattened, the maximum floor height has become the minimum, and attic
floor heights are above the elevation where it would be calculated in the gross
floor area. He said that floor to floor heights have stayed relatively the
same.
Trustee Woodbury asked if Mr. D’Onofrio had some recommendations. He noted that
the unintended effect of the zoning changes are that roofs look like they have
been cut off and said that he would like to see the pre-amendment look without
the negative consequences.
Mr. D’Onofrio replied that the changes were made to prevent roofs from being
lowered but it happened anyway. He said the main difference is the roof pitch.
Manager Williams commented that he has concluded that the zoning ordinance may
not be the appropriate way to address the problems.
President Duhl announced that there would be discussion on this issue at the
next study session.
6. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees David Abell,
Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Edmund
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:40 p.m.
July 15, 2003
DRAFT MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 5, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
15, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David Webster. Absent:
Trustee Ed Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and approximately four persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   August 5, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustees Abell and Presser, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   August 12, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend. After his arrival, Trustee Woodbury advised President
Duhl that he did not expect to be present.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 1, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-28-2003 – Landmark Designation: 739 Elm Street - Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance
designating the New Trier Township Building as a Winnetka Landmark. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Brower,
Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
President Duhl thanked George Noyes for his leadership on this project as well
as for his foresight many years ago to secure the Village’s ownership of the
Post Office property.
b)   Ordinance No. M-26-2003 – Zoning Variation: 910 Greenwood - Adoption.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting Intensity of Use of Lot (roofed lot coverage) and Building Line
Articulation variations to permit the construction of a one-story addition. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Woodbury.
c)   Ordinance No. M-27-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1255 Westmoor – Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a Front and Corner Yard Setback variation to permit the construction
of an in-ground swimming pool to replace an existing pool that has outlived its
useful life. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Woodbury.
d)   Resolution No. R-27-2003 – Release Restrictive Covenant: 1180 Laurel –
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that this resolution constituted a
housekeeping matter to release two restrictive covenants imposed on this
property in order to permit its sale. She noted that the first covenant was
imposed as a condition of the approval of a 1987 subdivision that carved two
lots out of a larger parcel with an existing home. The second covenant was
imposed as a condition for allowing some improvements to the new home that was
built on Lot 2 of the 1987 subdivision. She reported that Village staff has
concluded that all of the conditions of the two covenants have either been met
or waived, and that therefore, the covenants can be released.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-11-2003 – Hours for Class B Liquor Sales – Introduction.
Manager Williams reviewed a request from Grand Food Center to allow the sale of
packaged liquor sales in full service grocery stores to begin at 7:00 a.m.,
instead of 8:00 a.m., to accommodate those customers who wish to complete all
of their shopping when the store opens at 7:00 a.m.
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:40 p.m.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2003 – Parking Distance from Corners and Driveways –
Introduction. Chief DeLopez reviewed proposed changes to the Village Code
pertaining to parking prohibitions designed to standardize enforcement. In
particular the amendments specify standards for required parking distances from
driveways, intersections, and traffic signals.
President Duhl remarked that the Village needs to be somewhat lenient in areas
surrounding institutional uses.
After a lengthy discussion, Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved
to introduce the ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Ordinance MC-13-2003 – Liquor Licenses – Mirani’s Little Ricky’s, Fio –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed three changes in restaurant and liquor
license operations that trigger the need to amend the number of liquor licenses
authorized by the Village Code. These include:
   i)   Little Ricky’s, 540 Lincoln Ave. – New Class A-1 license
   ii)   Mirani’s, 899-1/2 Green Bay Rd. – New Class A license
   iii)   Fio, The Laundry Mall – Upgrade from Class A to A-1 license
She noted that as with all liquor licenses, the Council’s action in adopting
this ordinance would merely create the licenses, thus authorizing the Village
President, acting in his capacity as Local Liquor Commissioner, to approve and
issue them once all other requirements for the licenses have been met.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Policy Direction: 693 Sheridan Road. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
a Front and Corner Yard Setback variation to permit the construction of a new
single family residence. He explained that the new home is to be built in
approximately the same location as the existing home, only slightly narrower
than the present structure, and would result in side yards that are greater
than those currently existing. The variation is necessitated due to the fact
that streets bound this lot on three sides, one being a driveway serving three
other properties that is, therefore, treated the same as any public street for
zoning purposes. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals voted
6-0 to recommend denial of the variation.
President Duhl noted that this situation is similar to one on Westmoor recently
reviewed and granted by the Council and asked whether the ZBA was aware of
this. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that they were, but that they felt that since
this is new construction and there is a conforming alternative, that the
variation request should not be granted.
Trustee Greenough commented that she does not see that this ordinance makes a
lot of sense, in addition to which the conforming alternative would require
removal of several significant trees. She said she was not opposed to the
request.
The applicant, Kipp Lykins, read a letter in support of their request and the
architect, Healy Rice, reviewed pictures and proposed site plans.
Trustee Presser inquired whether there were any objections filed by the
neighbors. Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that none had been submitted.
After further discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees to direct
the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting the requested variation.
President Duhl suggested that the Council consider amending the Village Code to
eliminate this problem in the future.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1325 and 1326. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from warrant lists, he moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1325 in the amount of $644,255.15 and Warrant List
No. 1326 in the amount of $272,390.48. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Abell,
Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Proposed Caucus Questions Regarding Parking Enforcement. Chief DeLopez
reviewed proposed questions to be submitted to the Caucus for inclusion in its
next questionnaire. He said that the Caucus process will help the Village to
better understand the desires of Village residents with regard to enhancing
overall public safety and establishing uniform and consistent procedures for
addressing parking, traffic, and safety flow issues. In addition, Chief DeLopez
reported that so far this year the Police Department has received more than
5,000 requests to grant exceptions to various parking prohibitions
The Trustees discussed the questions at length, particularly the question
concerning the continuation of the 2:00 a.m. – 6:00 a.m. parking ban.
Manager Williams reported that, as noted in the recent Winnetka Report, the
Police Department will be tightening up on enforcement and starting to enforce
the ordinances consistently and uniformly. The Police Department will only
grant permission to park temporarily on the street during nighttime hours due
to exigent circumstances, such as driveway construction activities. Requests to
park on the street for matters of personal convenience, such as not wanting to
move cars in the driveway or the need for additional parking due to students
returning for the summer, will be denied.
President Duhl reminded the Council that the Chief had come before the Council
several months ago requesting that the 2:00 a.m. – 6:00 a..m. ban be dropped.
At that time, the Council decided it wanted to keep the ban in place. However,
the number of requests for exceptions continues to increase geometrically,
taking manpower and attention away from other Police duties, so the Chief has
returned with a question to present to the Caucus in order to determine the
wishes of the community with regard to this issue. He asked the Trustees how
they felt about eliminating the ban.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to enforce the ordinance as published
at this time until such time as there is a clear response from the Caucus
questionnaire.
d)   Bid No. 03-015: Loading Dock & Stairs. Manager Williams reviewed bids for
the labor, materials, and equipment for removal and replacement of the loading
dock and stairs at the Electric Plant. He reported staff’s recommendation that
the bid be awarded to the lowest qualified bidder, Flodstrom Construction, in
the total amount of $33,750.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Bid No. 03-009 – Street Reconstruction & Rehabilitation. Mr. Saunders
reviewed the bids for rehabilitation of various streets within the Village. He
reported staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to Johnson Paving
Company in the amount of $198,571.01, noting that staff’s estimate for the job
had been $231,915.35.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
f)   Village Yards Landscape Plan. Mr. Saunders reviewed the proposed plans to
re-landscape the north face of the closed landfill that was disturbed by the
construction activities associated with the Village Yards Project. He reported
that the plans had been presented to the Design Review Board for comment and
that the Board had agreed with the overall philosophy of the plan, namely the
naturalizing and seasonal color effects produced by a hardwood savanna, native
grasses, and flowers. They did suggest that the planting beds be more
meandering shown on the scheme. He requested that the Council authorize staff
to proceed with bidding and installation of the plans as recommended by the
Design Review Board.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to authorize staff to
proceed as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
g)   Village Hall Remodeling. Manager Williams reported that Public Works and
Water & Electric Departments’ move to the Village Yards provides an opportunity
for the Village to re-organize office space at Village Hall to improve
efficiencies and provide much needed space for the Community Development
Department. He indicated that the plan is to consolidate the Village Attorney’s
and Manager’s office that work as one unit into the same work area on the
second floor and give the Community Development Department additional office
space on the first floor. He reported that Riley Construction has proposed a
construction budget of approximately $66,500 to complete the remodeling and
recommended that the Village enter into a construction manager contract with
Riley Construction for this work. In addition, he noted that while most of the
existing, leftover furniture would be reused, some purchases for furniture and
finishing would be necessary, amounting to approximately $25,000.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to enter into an agreement with Riley Construction for the remodeling
of the 2nd floor of Village Hall for an amount not to exceed $66,500. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster,
Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported that he attended a recent Chamber of
Commerce meeting at which the main topic of conversation was the upcoming
Sidewalk Sale.
b)   Manager. Manager Williams asked Chief DeLopez to report on the Village’s
recent acquisition of a motorcycle to enhance public safety enforcement, which
he did.
10. Appointments.
None.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Brower
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:25 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Abell, Greenough,
Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned
at 11:26 p.m.
