August 5, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 19, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
5, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees Hugh
Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee David Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Assistant Director of Community Development
Brian Norkus, and approximately six persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   August 12, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except Trustee
Woodbury, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   August 19, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 8, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   July 1, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-11-2003 – Hours for Class B Liquor Sales – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance
allowing the sale of packaged liquor sales in full service grocery stores to
begin at 7:00 a.m., instead of 8:00 a.m. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2003 – Parking Distance from Corners and Driveways –
Adoption. Manager Williams explained that based upon Council’s comments at the
last meeting, staff had revised its recommendation to reduce the applicable
standard for parking distance from driveways from 8 feet to 2 feet. He pointed
out that this ordinance would be enforced on a complaint basis only.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments.
The Trustees discussed the issues. Trustee Webster raised a question about the
differences in the applicable distance standards from intersections, stop
lights, and stop signs.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to amend the ordinance as
it pertains to the distance from driveways as reviewed by the Village Manager
but to table the ordinance until outstanding questions can be researched and
resolved. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance MC-13-2003 – Liquor Licenses – Mirani’s Little Ricky’s, Fio –
Adoption. Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the
ordinance amending the number of liquor licenses authorized by the Village
Code, to include the following:
   i)   Little Ricky’s, 540 Lincoln Ave. – New Class A-1 license, and Sidewalk
Restaurant Liquor Rider
   ii)   Mirani’s, 899-1/2 Green By Rd. – New Class A license
   iii)   Fio, The Laundry Mall – Upgrade from Class A to A-1 license
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Webster, Presser, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
d)   Ordinance No. M-29-2003 – Zoning Variation: 693 Sheridan Rd. –
Introduction. Mr. Norkus briefly reviewed this application for a Front and
Corner Yard Setback variation to permit the construction of a new single family
residence, noting that the Council considered this matter at its July 15th
meeting because the Zoning Board of Appeals denied the application.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce this
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-30-2003 – Zoning Variation: 714 Park Lane – Introduction.
Mr. Norkus reviewed an application for a Front Setback variation to permit the
construction of an in-ground swimming pool, pointing out that the Council had
considered a similar request last year, at which time the application was
denied by both the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Village Council. He noted
that the variation is required because a dedicated, unimproved portion of Park
Lane runs along the eastern edge of the subject property. Mr. Norkus explained
that after last year’s denial the applicant devised a plan for a conforming
location for the pool, but that that location was objectionable both to the
applicant and the immediate neighbor, Mrs. Preston, necessitating the current
variation request. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-
2 to recommend in favor of the variation.
President Duhl noted that Mrs. Preston had also been opposed to the 2002
variation request.
Philip Preston spoke on behalf of his mother. He represented that she would
prefer no pool at all, but that given the applicant’s intention to install one,
the current variation location is preferable to the conforming location.
Trustee Woodbury asked Mr. Norkus to explain the objections to the conforming
location, which he did.
Scott Byron, the applicant’s landscape architect, reviewed the plans to screen
the pool from the neighborhood.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce this ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-31-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1127 Sheridan Road –
Introduction. Mr. Norkus reviewed an application for a Garage variation to
permit the construction of a detached garage that will be 29 feet in height,
whereas a maximum of 18 feet is permitted, in order to meet the roof pitch of
the existing principal structure. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals
had voted unanimously to recommend in favor of the variation.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce this
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Resolution No. R-28-2003 – Approval of Additional Driveway: 670 Sheridan
Rd. - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed a request for permission to install a
circular driveway in the front of the subject property. She explained that the
property is a through lot with street frontages on both Maple and Sheridan and
pointed out that several conditions were imposed upon the owner in the
Resolution. She reported that the Village Traffic Engineer had no objections to
the installation of this third driveway.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce this
ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Abell.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1327 and 1328. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1327 in the amount of $1,185,685.70 and
Warrant List No. 1328 in the amount of $743,317.80. Trustee Woodbury seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Abell.
b)   Outdoor Seating Permit: Little Ricky’s. Manager Williams reviewed a
request by Little Ricky’s owner for an Outdoor Seating Permit in conjunction
with the request for a Sidewalk Liquor Rider as approved earlier.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the requests.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Request to Waive Bid Process: Pump. Manager Williams reviewed a request by
the Public Works Department to waive the bid process for purchase of a
stormwater pump for the Sunview Lane drainage improvements. He explained that
as a part of the West Hibbard Drainage Study and Improvements Project, the
Village must purchase a pump to install in the new pump station, and that in
1995 the Village decided to standardize on vertical stormwater pumps
manufactured by Crisafulli, Inc. These pumps are purchased through Marshall-
Bond Pumps at a price of $27,340, plus $1,225 for shipping.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to waive the bid process
and to purchase the stormwater pump as described by Mr. Williams. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Greenough,
Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
d)   Bids: Replacement Squad Cars. Manager Williams reviewed a request to waive
the competitive bid process and approve the purchase two 2003 Police
Interceptors from Morrow Brothers Ford to replace two existing squad cars that
have outlived their usefulness to the Village. He explained that cars meeting
the required specifications could not be obtained in a timely fashion from any
other dealer.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the purchase of
two squad cars from Morrow Brother for a total purchase price of $42,818. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Brower, Webster, Presser, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported on a recent meeting of the Ad Hoc
Business Development Committee meeting, saying that they expected to complete
their mission by the given deadline.
b)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village has begun the process
of revamping its Web site to make it fully searchable and to include
interactive Village forms.
Mr. Williams asked Mr. Norkus to review a new, proposed slate alternative for
the Elm Street and Hubbard Woods train station roofs. After discussing this
alternative, there was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed with it use at
an additional cost of $12,565 to the Village, provided that Metra and their
chosen contractor, Legat, assume all liability for the installation thereof.
10. Appointments.
None.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Brower
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Abell. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:55 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Absent:
Trustee Abell. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The meeting
adjourned at 9:56 p.m.
