Recording Secretary
August 12, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: September 16, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster. Absent:
Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
and approximately 4 persons in the audience.
2. Review of 2002 Liquor Ordinance Amendments.
Village Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments made to the Liquor Ordinance in
2002 and also reviewed a table provided by the Police Department that
summarized the calls received by the police during the calendar years August
through July in 2001-2002 and 2002-2003 from locations that have liquor
licenses. She reported that during this time there were no substantial changes
in the number of calls from year to year. She also noted that the calls to the
Park District include all of the different facilities on Hibbard Road and that
only calls from the last month of the reporting period might be related to
liquor service at the golf course, so that the information in the table is not
particularly useful in this regard. She also said that there were no incidents
of service where it was not permitted and there have been no violations that
required enforcement, so it can be assumed that there is no negative impact
from the service of liquor only.
Manager Williams reported that that the Chief of Police and his command staff
are of the opinion that the amendments to the Liquor Ordinance have had zero
impact.
President Duhl observed that McCarthy’s and the Park District were the only
applicants for liquor-only service and the calls to McCarthy’s decreased. He
clarified for Trustee Webster that the amendment was for being able to serve a
meal at the bar. He said that if there had been any incidents, it would have
been brought to the Village’s attention.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, along with other representatives of the
Park District, said that they have heard no complaints, except for people who
wanted to sit outside the designated area. She said that when asked to move to
the appropriate area, those people were cooperative with the staff. She
commented that she has had no negative comments from anyone, but that it is too
soon to say if there are any issues.
Mr. Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce, asked if McCarthy’s has had a positive benefit
from the change in the liquor ordinance. He said that he recalled the debate on
this issue and in particular, the reasons given in support, which were that it
would increase traffic, benefit businesses, and discourage customers from going
elsewhere because they couldn’t get a drink while they were waiting for their
pizzas. Mr. Hecht said that this suggested that the reason to do this was so
that people would be able to have a couple of drinks while waiting to take food
out and presumably get them closer to whatever the DUI standard is when they go
home. He thought then and continues to think now that this is a bad policy. He
said that given the negative effects of this policy, which is the encouragement
of bars, and without some positive evidence of the benefit of the ordinance, it
should not be continued. He said that it is simply his belief that encouraging
people to come to drink at a bar, without eating, is not good for Winnetka.
President Duhl said that he thinks the two aspects to this issue are the social
benefit and the increase in business, and it has been determined that there
have been no adverse reactions to the amendments to the liquor ordinance.
Sue McDonald, 788 Green Bay, said that she spends a lot of time at the Park
District golf course and for many years was opposed to liquor service there,
but has changed her mind. She has observed that since the ordinance was
amended, there has been no change in the atmosphere at the golf course. She
said that it should certainly be given another year.
Manager Williams explained that the ordinance that adopted the amendments to
the liquor ordinance expires in September unless the Council adopts another
ordinance to extend it another year. He recommended that the amendments be
adopted as part of the Code, so as to repeal the former ordinance and ensure
that an annual review is made.
After further discussion, the Trustees agreed, with Trustee Webster dissenting,
to repeal the former liquor ordinance and adopt the liquor-only amendments as a
permanent part of the Village Code with annual reviews,.
3. Streamlining the Zoning Process.
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio presented a request to the
Village Council that more authority be given to the Zoning Board of Appeals and
the Director to grant or deny certain zoning variations. He said the purpose of
this request is to reduce the number of zoning variation requests that come
before the Village Council and to reduce the amount of time necessary to
complete the zoning variation process which currently is several months. He
reviewed an analysis that staff had prepared of the variations requested over
the last two years and staff’s recommendation of a 3-tiered proposal for
granting or denying variation requests. He said the proposal would authorize
the Director and the ZBA to grant or deny variation requests for gross floor
area, impermeable surface, roofed lot coverage, and setbacks up to a pre-
determined percentage of variation. Mr. D’Onofrio said that staff also
recommends that the ZBA be given the authority to grant height variations for
accessory structures, excluding variation requests from the 2 ½ story rule,
which would still go to the Council, and to take final action on pools, fences
and detached accessory structures. He also proposed eliminating the need for
variation requests for enlargement or installation of windows and doors in
existing nonconforming structures, and one-for-one replacement of deteriorating
structures with same structures.
Village Attorney Janega stated that in the instance of decisions by the
Director on existing nonconforming structures, authority could be given in the
Village Code chapter on nonconformities with appeals made to the Zoning Board.
She also stated that she would have to study the other staff recommendations
further to determine what amendments would be needed to implement them.
The Council discussed the types of variations that might be granted or denied
at each of the 3 levels proposed, listened to comments from the Village
Attorney regarding proper notice to neighbors, and commented on avenues of
appeal from decisions of the Director and the Zoning Board.
Margaret Benson, chairperson of the Zoning Board of Appeals, said that the ZBA
is not casual about the consideration of cases and knows that 99% of the cases
will go before the Council to set policy. She said that in the future, if the
ZBA is the final decision making body, cases will be considered in a different
way.
Mr. Hecht commented that windows and doors in side yards should be put in a
different category because if they are within 6 feet of a lot line,
particularly doors, it can be a serious intrusion on the neighbors. He
suggested that if more variation requests are delegated to the ZBA, all
proceedings should be televised so that the people in Winnetka are informed.
President Duhl said that as there didn’t appear to be any great objection to
the proposal, Attorney Janega should draft an ordinance. He suggested that
further discussion on an appeal process be done after the ordinance is drafted.
Trustee Brower suggested that the Director report to the Zoning Board and
prepare a report to the Council on a monthly basis to keep them informed.
Meeting adjourned to executive session at 9:18 p.m.
Respectfully submitted,
Mary Ivins
Deputy Village Clerk
August 19, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 16, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
19, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7: 30 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders,
and approximately 19 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   September 2, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend. President Duhl, however, indicated that he would
not be present.
b)   September 9, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 5, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2003 – Parking Distance from Corners and Driveways –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders responded to questions raised at the last meeting
regarding parking distances from various kinds of traffic control devices.
After lengthy discussion, it was decided to amend the draft to eliminate
inconsistencies and clarify references to reflect that no parking shall be
allowed within thirty (30) feet of an intersection controlled by flashing
signals, stop signs, yield signs, or other traffic controls. In addition,
parking will not be permitted within 20 feet of any intersection, except to the
extent that such area has been marked and signed as permitted parking area.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-29-2003 – Zoning Variation: 693 Sheridan Rd. – Adoption.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt this ordinance
granting a Front and Corner Yard Setback variation to permit the construction
of a new single family residence. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Greenough, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: Trustee
Abell. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-30-2003 – Zoning Variation: 714 Park Lane – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough , seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt this ordinance
granting a Front Setback variation to permit the construction of an in-ground
swimming pool. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Webster, Brower, Presser, and Abell. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-31-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1127 Sheridan Road – Adoption.
Manager Williams reviewed a revised zoning matrix for the property. Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt this ordinance granting a
Garage variation to permit the construction of a detached garage that will be
29 feet in height. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-14-2003 – Liquor Ordinance Amendments - Introduction.
Attorney Janega explained that on September 17, 2002, the Council enacted
Ordinance MC-9-2002, which amended the liquor regulations of the Village Code
to allow for the sale of alcoholic beverages in certain food establishments
without the purchase of a full meal. The ordinance contained a one-year sunset
provision requiring an evaluation of the liquor-only service within a year.
She reviewed Ordinance No. MC-14-2003 that was drafted to reflect the policy
direction resulting from Council’s discussions at its August 12, 2003 Study
Session to repeal both Chapter 5.08 (the prior liquor regulations) and the
sunset provisions that had been included in Chapter 5.09, thus making the
liquor-only provisions permanent. It also requires the Village Manager to
submit a report on liquor-only service for consideration by the Council at its
September 2004 Study Session.
Finally, she noted that the draft ordinance contains two technical amendments.
The first clarifies the definitions of “alcoholic beverages” and “licensee,”
and the second authorizes the number of liquor licenses to be established by
resolution in order to streamline the process.
Trustee Webster indicated that he was opposed to the policy of serving liquor
without meals and, therefore, would vote against introduction.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce this ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Abell, Greenough,
Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: Trustee Webster. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1329 and 1330. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1329 in the amount of $603,495.68 and Warrant
List No. 1330 in the amount of $394,162.97. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: Design for East Willow Road. Mr. Saunders explained that
as a part of its planned street improvements for 2003, the Village has
programmed Willow Road, from Wilson to the east end of the road, for
rehabilitation. He reported that the Village had initially designed the
pavement to be constructed 27 feet wide, but that staff had received a petition
signed by 28 of the properties along Willow, from Wilson to Sheridan,
requesting that the street be reconstructed to a width of 22 feet.
Subsequently, the Village surveyed all of the affected residents along east
Willow Road soliciting their preference vis-à-vis the pavement width. The
survey results indicated a clear majority in favor of the originally designed
27-foot pavement.
Mr. Saunders noted that because of the diversity of opinion on this issue, as
well as the level of concern over traffic speeds and volumes, Village staff
examined several other traffic calming measures that might be appropriate for
Willow Road. He reviewed those alternatives for the Trustees, noting that with
the exception of curb extensions, most have little or no effect.
Finally, he recommended that the Council authorize staff to construct east
Willow Road at a width of 27 feet back-of-curb to back-of-curb, to permit
parking on both sides of the street. He expressed his opinion as Traffic
Engineer that curb extensions at the intersections with Wilson/Cedar, Myrtle,
and Woodland, could be installed if the neighborhood so desires.
President Duhl remarked that the Council is always influenced by staff’s
recommendation if there is a substantive reason therefor, however, in cases
where the Village doesn’t have a strong view, the Council usually defers to the
majority of the neighbors. He asked the audience to leave the issue of curb
extensions until the question of street width had been decided.
Several audience members spoke in support of the narrower width, while others
spoke in support of the status quo. After the discussion, President Duhl polled
the Trustees, all of whom expressed support for the 27-ft. width. Therefore,
given Mr. Saunders’ recommendation that the street be 27 ft. wide and, by
survey, a majority of the neighbors expressed a preference for the existing 27-
ft. width, it was the consensus of the Council to maintain the status quo.
Scott Pull, 370 Sheridan, raised an issue about parking at the east end of the
street and asked about the possibility of speed humps.
President Duhl responded that the Council had examined the efficacy of speed
humps in depth several years ago and concluded that they were not a good idea.
Jolene Birmingham expressed concern about perceived excessive speed and
children’s safety. She said that she believes that drivers speed because the
street is straight, flat, and wide.
Steve Shepard, 625 Willow, agreed and urged the Council to take safety into
consideration.
Noting that there were a significant number of residents who desired a narrower
street with an eye toward traffic calming, President Duhl asked Mr. Saunders to
explain the possible curb extensions, which he did. In an effort to meet both
objectives of maintaining a 27-ft wide street and adding traffic calming
measures, President Duhl directed the Village to send another survey to
neighbors soliciting their opinion about curb extensions.
Among the neighbors expressing concern about or opposition to curb extensions
were Scott Pull, 370 Sheridan, A. F. Wilson, 604 Willow, Ruth Rayne, 555
Willow, Barbara Gould, 470 Willow, and Herb Ritchell, 548 Willow.
President Duhl indicated that this survey would not delay the project in any
way and explained that if the neighborhood response comes back opposed to the
installation of curb extensions, the matter would be decided once and for all.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported that the Chamber of Commerce had taken
the month off from meetings. He also submitted a letter to be appended to the
minutes relative to his relationship to Harris Bank and his services to the
Village Council
b)   Manager. Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to update the Council on the
status of the Village Yards project, which he did. He reported that the project
should come in at just slightly under the $8.5 budgeted and be completed by the
first of October.
In view of the recent blackout in the East, Manager Williams reported on the
capability of the Village’s electric plant.
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Nan Greenough, as Chairperson,
Posey Fisher, Gary Frank, Maureen Mitchel, Anne Montgomery, Kevin Plunkett, and
Chris Rintz to the Ad Hoc House Design Study Committee.
Trustee Woodbury moved to approve the appointments. Trustee Abell seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl then suggested the appointment of Martha Pickrell to the Design
Review Board to replace Mary Ellen Swenson.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the appointment.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:55 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
