September 2, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 7, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 2, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present:
Trustees David Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David
Webster. Absent: President Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, and approximately twelve persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   September 9, 2003 Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   September 16, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustees Presser and Webster, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
No draft minutes were presented for review.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-14-2003 – Liquor Ordinance Amendments - Adoption. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-14-2003
authorizing the continuation of the policy to allow the sale of alcoholic
beverages in certain food establishments without requiring the purchase of a
full meal. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greenough,
Woodbury, Brower, Presser, and Abell. Nays: Trustee Webster. Absent: President
Duhl.
b)   Resolution No. R-29-2003 – Appendix of Liquor Licenses – Adoption.
Attorney Janega commented briefly on this resolution that amends the manner in
which liquor licenses are authorized an establishes the number of authorized
liquor licenses. Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt the
Resolution. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Presser,
Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent President
Duhl.
c)   Ordinance No. M-32-2003 – Zoning Variation: 680 Elder Ln - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum Building Size and Front and Side
Setback variations to permit the construction of a two-and one-half story
addition. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals voted 5-0 to recommend
approval of the requested variations.
Andrew Venamore, the owner’s representative, explained that they are trying to
modernize the existing residence in keeping with today’s living standards. He
pointed out that there is no basement and that the lot is very small and
irregular, providing little room in which to operate.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to introduce this
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-33-2003 – Zoning Variation: 155 Thorn Tree – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Front Setback variation in order to
permit the construction of an attached garage. He pointed out that the existing
garage is nonconforming and that the new garage will be somewhat closer to the
main structure than the current one. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals voted 5-0 to recommend that the variation be approved.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce this ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-34-2003 – Zoning Variation: 780 Foxdale - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Maximum Building Size variation to
permit the construction of an addition, garage, and open entry porch. He
pointed out that the rear portion of this lot had been taken by the railroad
and reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals voted 5-0 to recommend the
variation be approved.
Ken Behles, the architect, commented on the necessity for the proposed
improvements.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce this
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-35-2003 – Zoning Variation: 727 Hill Rd. - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Maximum Building Size variation to
permit the construction of an addition. He explained that the GFA variation is
required because a portion of the existing basement is counted toward gross
floor area because it exceeds the four foot maximum by a few inches. Mr.
D’Onofrio went on to report that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to
recommend that the variation be approved.
Nigel Stroud, the architect, commented on the necessity for the proposed
improvements.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce this ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1331 and 1332. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1331 in the amount of $1,027,466.64 and
Warrant List No. 1332 in the amount of $440,203.38. Trustee Greenough seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
President Duhl.
b)   Notification of Closure of Local Broadcast Studio. Manager Williams
indicated that the Village had received notice from Comcast of its intent to
close the Hubbard Woods studio at the expiration of the current franchise
agreement on September 20, 2003. He explained that both the current franchise
agreement and the relevant ordinance contemplate the consolidation of
Winnetka’s studio with other regional studios. He also noted that outside
counsel retained by the Village in this matter concluded that upon expiration
of the agreement Comcast is within its rights to close the studio.
Manager Williams reported that there had been a request by a resident that the
Village take over funding the operation of the studio in Hubbard Woods. He
indicated that given the Village’s continuing difficult financial situation, he
could not recommend that the Village undertake these new operating expenses.
Bob Ryan, Vice President of Government Affairs, Comcast, articulated the
company’s position. He explained that with the expiration of the franchise
agreement, the operating expenses for the Hubbard Woods studio would become the
responsibility of the Village as it is collecting franchise fees, while any
capital expenses would be passed on to subscribers. He said that federal law
precludes the pass through of operating expenses to users. Mr. Ryan represented
that consolidation of studios affords Comcast certain savings and that all
users of the Hubbard Woods studio would be accommodated at either the Skokie or
Highland Park studios because they are larger facilities with larger staffs.
Jeff Berkowitz, 519 Willow Rd., who originates a regular program from the
Hubbard Woods studio, spoke in support of keeping the studio open. He
distributed comments by Tribune columnist, Eric Zorn and highlighted remarks in
his letter to the Council included in the Agenda packet. He stressed that the
studio generates a number of opportunities for students, primarily from New
Trier High School, affording them a real-life chance to learn about TV
production and public policy. In response to a question from Trustee Presser,
Mr. Berkowitz indicated that while he would be able to continue at either
Highland Park or Skokie, transportation to/from these locales would be
difficult for a number of the students who participate. He asked that the
Council commit to keeping the studio open and make it a part of any
negotiations with Comcast for a future franchise agreement.
Mike Arroyo, a NTHS student, supported Mr. Berkowitz’s comments, as did Erin
Weller, NTHS teacher. Ms. Weller said that while neighboring high schools have
TV programs, the program at NTHS had been cut because of an inability to invest
the money required to keep a studio running and up-to-date. She said that the
Hubbard Woods studio is the only facility available to teach live broadcast
skills and techniques.
In response to a question from Trustee Presser, Mr. Ryan indicated that Comcast
had no problem keeping the studio open if adequate funding can be secured from
other sources.
Trustee Webster asked if there was a possibility that Comcast could maintain
the status quo for a short period of time until a satisfactory transition can
be made. Mr. Ryan indicated that they would be willing to keep the studio
operating until the end of October.
Trustee Presser pointed out that the issue is an economic one for the Village,
saying that the primary usage of the facility is by Mr. Berkowitz and that
while it benefits NTHS students, not all are Winnetkans, and it shouldn’t be
supported solely by Winnetka taxpayers. He expressed his belief that the
logical body to take over the studio’s funding is NTHS or some private entity.
Trustee Abell indicated that he could not support the Village’s funding of the
studio as the benefit is primarily to NTHS, not to the Village as a whole.
Trustees Greenough, Brower, and Webster concurred, saying that although Mr.
Berkowitz’s accomplishments are impressive, the Village doesn’t have the funds
to take over this project.
President Pro Tem Woodbury concluded the discussion by saying that Comcast has
agreed to extend the Hubbard Woods studio operation until the end of October,
providing an opportunity for new funding sources to be found, but that it is
the consensus of the Council that the Village will not take over the studio’s
funding.
c)   Bid No. 03-020 - Traffic Signal Modernization at Green Bay Rd. and Sunset
Rd. – Manager Williams reviewed the bids and reported Staff’s recommendation
that the bid be awarded to H & H Electric Co. for $108,103.48, based on unit
pricing. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid
as recommended. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and
Presser. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
Phil Hoza expressed appreciation to all of the Trustees for their efforts on
behalf of the Village and its residents. He updated the Council on the status
of the Log House and invited all to attend the Historical Society gala on
September 13th.
10. Reports.
None
11. Appointments.
President Pro Tem Woodbury suggested the appointment of Susan Connor to the
Board of Fire and Police Commissioners to replace Donna Pinsof who has
resigned.
Trustee Brower moved to approve the appointment. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Brower moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: President Duhl. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:40
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:12 p.m. Present: President
Pro Tem Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Webster.
Absent: President Duhl. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and
Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Webster moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl. The meeting
adjourned at 10:13 p.m.
