Recording Secretary
September 9, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: October 7, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Trustee Edmund
Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Nan Greenough. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Village
Sanitarian Shane Sexton and one person in the audience.
2. Food Service Sanitation Code Update.
Village Sanitarian, Shane Sexton, explained that the Village Code is outdated
as it pertains to food service sanitation. She recommended that the current
State code be adopted by reference and to standardize compliance procedures so
that all food service businesses are treated similarly. She said that although
Cook County would actually enforce a food borne illness outbreak here, the
Village needs to establish a procedure for calling the County. She also stated
that any changes made would only be to procedures necessary to ensure
compliance with existing regulations.
After ascertaining that procedures must be set in place to enforce the State’s
food sanitation regulations, the Trustees agreed that the Code Committee should
meet to discuss amendments to Chapter 8.12 of the Village Code.
President Duhl directed that the Committee keep permit fees low to encourage
restaurants and other food businesses in Winnetka.
3. 2002 Zoning Amendment Analysis.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio explained that the staff had
analyzed the zoning amendments made in 2002 and, having reviewed the data,
recommends that no changes be made to the Zoning Ordinance with respect to
those amendments. He said that the staff feels some progress has been made and
that many of the issues may be addressed by the Ad Hoc Committee on Residential
Design Review. He said that after the Council receives the Committee’s report,
it can decide whether further amendments are necessary.
The Trustees discussed the possible effects of increasing the building height
and minimum allowable attic space in order to get a better roof pitch and
design for new construction in the interim, while the Ad Hoc Committee meets.
President Duhl directed the staff to study the gross floor area calculations
for recent new construction to see if there is anything in common that resulted
in poor design and also the effect on GFA if the minimum allowance for attic
space is increased.
4. Zoning Ordinance definition of private streets.
Assistant Director of Community Development, Brian Norkus, reported that at a
recent Council meeting, the Council had raised a question about the definition
of a private street and how it affects corner lots and setback requirements. He
said that the current Zoning Ordinance treats any private easement that gives
access to three or more properties as a public street. For purposes of
discussion, Mr. Norkus suggested the following alternatives: alter the
definition of a private street and increase the number of “accessed lots”
threshold; or, consider changing the corner lot relief factor. Mr. Norkus said
that, in the first instance, changing the number of accessed lots would create
more interior lots that would be subject to smaller setback requirements than
corner lots, which would result in an increase in buildable area and impact
many lots throughout the Village. He further stated that if the corner lot
relief factor were changed to allow building on less then 60 percent of the
lot’s width, it would impact an even larger number of lots by relaxing the
setback requirements for all corner lots.
After further discussion, the Trustees concluded that the definition of a
private street should remain as it is currently.
5. Options to Improve Aesthetics at 93 Green Bay Road.
Manager Williams reported that several residents had requested that the Village
improve the property at 93 Green Bay Road to make it more attractive. In
response to that request, he had asked architects from Sente Rubel to create
drawings that would improve the aesthetics of the building on the property. He
stated that there is a 5-year lease agreement and 5-year extension with Land
Rover and the long-term goal is to demolish the building on the site to build
an entryway park.
The Trustees considered the three sets of drawings and costs associated with
them, and at the suggestion of Trustee Woodbury, requested that Mr. Williams
explore landscaping as an alternative to change the image of the building and
screen the number of Land Rovers in the parking lot.
6. Parking on Village Streets.
President Duhl stated that he had received a letter from Mary Happ, Ash and
Maple expressing her anger with the current Village policy about parking
overnight on the street. He said she had complained that the Police Department
would not give permission for her to park an extra car on the street when her
son was home and asked the Council to reconsider the policy or let her know
what to do with the extra car. He asked the Council to give him some guidance
about what to tell her.
The Trustees discussed specific exceptions to the rule that had been discussed
in the past and recalled former comments made on this issue.
President Duhl reminded the Council that they had asked the Police Chief to
enforce the overnight parking regulations. He stated that the consensus as he
understands it is for staff to discuss this further with the Chief of Police.
7. No executive session
8. Manager’s Report.
Manager Williams updated the Council on the North Shore Country Day School’s
contract for parking spaces.
Meeting adjourned: 9:45 p.m.
September 16, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 7, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 16, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7: 35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, and David Webster.
Absent: Trustee Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Public Works Director Steven Saunders, and four
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced the removal of Item 8(h), the CATV Franchise
Extension, from the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   October 7, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Abell, indicated that they expected to attend. President Duhl indicated
that he might be out of town.
b)   October 14, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 12, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   August 19, 2003, Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt
the minutes as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-32-2003 – Zoning Variation: 680 Elder Ln. - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the shell ordinance
introduced at the last meeting. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell,
moved to adopt the amendments as presented by Attorney Janega. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-32-2003,
as amended, granting Maximum Building Size and Front and Side Setback
variations to permit the construction of a two-and one-half story addition. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster, Abell,
Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
b)   Ordinance No. M-33-2003 – Zoning Variation: 155 Thorn Tree – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the shell ordinance
introduced at the last meeting. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough,
moved to adopt the amendments as presented by Attorney Janega. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-33-2003,
as amended, granting a Front Setback variation to permit the construction of an
attached garage. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Webster, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
c)   Ordinance No. M-34-2003 – Zoning Variation: 780 Foxdale - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the shell ordinance
introduced at the last meeting. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell,
moved to adopt the amendments as presented by Attorney Janega. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-34-2003,
as amended, granting a Maximum Building Size variation to permit the
construction of an addition, garage, and open entry porch. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and
Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
d)   Ordinance No. M-35-2003 – Zoning Variation: 727 Hill Rd. - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the shell ordinance
introduced at the last meeting. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee
Woodbury, moved to adopt the amendments as presented by Attorney Janega. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-35-
2003, as amended, granting Maximum Building Size variation to permit the
construction of an addition. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Presser.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-15-2003 – Amending Construction Regulations -
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the definition of
“construction activity” in the Village Code to include a number of activities
currently not listed in the definition. She explained that these amendments are
being proposed as a result of a recent housing court case in which the builder
was issued a ticket for early work. The ticket, however, was dismissed because
in the Judge’s opinion, the early work performed did not fall within the
definition of construction activity. Among those activities added are servicing
of portable toilets, delivery or removal of equipment and supplies, and
exterior use of equipment pertaining to cleaning/maintenance of a construction
site.
Trustee Webster suggested taking the term “property” out of the draft and
include language to the effect that the prohibited activities cannot take place
“anywhere on or adjacent to a construction site.”
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to introduce the ordinance
amending the definition of “construction activity” including the changes to the
draft ordinance that he suggested. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Insurance Report. Mr. McKee explained that the Village currently uses a
combination of self insurance, commercial insurance, and pools to manage many
risks. He also explained that the excess insurance pool used by the Village
increased their deductible from $1,000,000 to $2,000,000 effective May 1, 2003.
Because of this higher deductible, Mr. McKee recommended that the Nugent
Consulting Group be retained by the Village to review existing insurance
policies and risk management practices.
Mr. McKee introduced Mike Nugent, of the Nugent Consulting Group and Mike
Kelly, of Lamb Little and Co., the Village’s broker. Mr. Nugent explained the
report presented to the Council and indicated overall there were no serious
unidentified gaps in coverage for the Village. Mr. Nugent then reviewed
exhibits 1 and 2 on pages 55 and 56 of the packet. He suggested that it would
be more appropriate for the Village to assume a higher level of risk in some
areas where the current deductible was $15,000 or less. Conversely, it was
suggested that the Village explore options to cap liability in certain lines of
coverage, such as auto liability and general liability, at the $500,000 self-
insured retention level.
The Council asked Mr. Nugent why a $500,000 self-insured retention was
considered. Mr. Nugent explained that based on the dynamics of the insurance
marketplace, that was an attractive area where insurance carriers would have
the most interest in writing the Village’s coverage, and that the cost for
other options such as a $250,000 or $750,000 self-insured retention should also
be explored. The Council also asked about the mechanics of the Village’s
participation in the excess pool (High Excess Liability Pool, or H.E.L.P.) and
how the sub-limit of $5,000,000 would be applied should the Village have
multiple catastrophic claims.
Upon Council questions about the soundness of purchasing insurance with a lower
deductible, Mr. Nugent indicated that the deductible selected is a function of
the price being charged for the insurance coverage. When the insurance market
is competitive, lower deductibles may be a good business decision.
b)   Willow Road Construction Bids. Mr. Williams reviewed the bids for the
rehabilitation of Willow Road. He reported Staff’s recommendation that the bid
be awarded to Johnson Paving Company for $429,931.50, roughly $109,000 under
the budgeted amount.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Brower, Webster, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
c)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1333 and 1334. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1333 in the amount of $743,051.34 and Warrant
List No. 1334 in the amount of $556,943.92. Trustee Abell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Abell, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
d)   Policy Direction: Parking Bay Request 1063 Cherry St. Manager Williams
explained that the Village received a request from the homeowner to install a
parking bay in the same location as one that existed prior to the construction
of the existing residence. In doing so, he briefly reviewed the history of
parking bays in the Village.
Mr. Williams reported that although this matter has been discussed several
times by Village boards, there exists no definitive policy statement, although
the intent appears to be to “discourage” them. In light thereof, Staff has
taken the position that it will not authorize the construction of new parking
bays on the public parkway and that as properties having parking bays are re-
developed, existing bays must be removed. He noted, however, that Staff’s
analysis of the present request finds that the presence of a parking bay would
not constitute a sight distance encroachment, would not interfere with proper
maintenance of the roadway, and would not create adverse drainage impacts. He
also reported that Staff recommends that if the bay is allowed, conditions be
attached to its use, maintenance, construction, and removal similar to those
previously required.
Monica Boyle, the applicant, explained her reasons for requesting that the
parking bay be reinstalled in its original location. She said that the family
needs additional parking and at the time they bought the home, they did not
know that on-street, overnight parking was not permitted in the Village. She
indicated that when the builder submitted his plans the issue of the removal of
the parking bay was not raised and pointed out that in order to place the
garage to allow for adequate parking, the builder would have had to remove an
exceptionally large tree.
President Duhl pointed out that if a parking bay is installed, it becomes
public parking and can be used by anyone. In this instance the issue is one of
preserving a tree by permitting the parking bay, or denying the parking bay,
necessitating the removal of a tree in order to provide the desired parking.
Trustee Woodbury indicated that he believed that the issue is really one of
overnight parking. He expressed concern that permitting the parking bay would
set a precedent and many others would be making similar requests. He voiced
support for Staff’s interpretation and implementation.
Manager Williams agreed that parking bays are not desirable, but said that the
location of the garage and the removal of the bay should have been raised at
the time of plan approval while the home was in the design phase. In light of
the miscommunication between staff and the builder, he suggested that granting
this request would set minimal precedent.
Trustee Abell pointed out that the applicant knew at the time of purchase that
there was no parking bay on the property.
Trustee Greenough countered saying that the applicants, the buyer, were from
out of town and purchased the property with the expectation of being able to
park on the street.
Ms. Boyle expressed regret at the need to install the parking bay, but said
that she would rather do that than remove a tree to relocate the garage. She
said she believed the building process should have been handled differently
because the issue of off-street parking didn’t come up at all during the design
or building phase of the project.
President Duhl pointed out that even had it come up, the builder would have had
to remove a tree, or trees, in order to put the garage in a different location
or install a driveway off the street. Either way trees would have been removed.
Mr. Duhl indicated that while sympathetic to the Boyles’ situation, he and the
Trustees are troubled by the concept of re-installing a parking bay.
Mr. Duhl noted that the Caucus questionnaire contains questions soliciting
residents’ opinions on the overnight parking ban. If there is an overwhelming
response to eliminate the ban, then there will be no need to reinstall the
parking bay. He asked the Boyles to wait until the Caucus responses have been
tabulated. In the meantime, he asked that the Village Forester review the site
plan and report back to the Council on the impact the installation of a
driveway would have on existing trees.
e)   Bid Waiver: Refuse Scooters. Manager Williams reported that the Public
Works Department has requested that the competitive bid process be waived and
that two refuse scooters be purchased from Cushman in an amount not to exceed
$48,000. He explained that this request is based on the fact that only two
vendors are available, and based upon listed specifications and extensive
research, the Cushman model is deemed by Staff to be superior.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the waiver of
the competitive bid process and the purchase of two Cushman refuse scooters as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
f)   Bid No. 03-021: Pad Mount Transformers. Manager Williams reviewed bids for
the purchase and delivery of single-phase and three-phase pad mount
transformers to be purchased on as ‘as needed’ basis from September 1, 2003 to
August 31, 2004. He reported Staff’s recommendation that the purchase of
single-phase transformers to WESCO in an amount not to exceed $36,000 and the
purchase of three-phase transformers from RESCO in a not to exceed amount of
$40,000.
Trustee Webster raised a question about the ownership of WESCO and RESCO.
Manager Williams indicated that he would have the Finance Director confirm that
neither company is a subsidiary of General Electric.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried subject to verification of
ownership of the two companies. Ayes: Trustee Greenough. Ayes with reservations
depending upon ownership of the two companies: Trustees Webster, Abell,
Woodbury, and Brower. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
g)   North Shore Country Day School Parking Lease. Manager Williams reviewed
the lease agreement negotiated with North Shore Country Day School for the use
of fifteen parking spaces in the Sunset Road parking lot. The lease covers the
school year, September 15, 2003 to May 31, 2004, for an agreed upon amount of
$8,925, and contains a provision permitting its extension for an additional
year.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to approve the parking
contract with North Shore Country Day School. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Abell and
Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Presser.
h)   CATV Franchise Extension. As announced earlier, this item was removed from
the Agenda.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Council Reports.
Trustee Brower reported on a recent meeting of the Chamber of Commerce. He
indicated that FY2003 had been a good one for the Chamber, with revenues and
membership being up and Sidewalk Sale being a big success. He said the Chamber
was beginning strategic planning for the coming year and would be focusing on
business development.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Brower
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Presser. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:25 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees, Brower, Greenough, and Webster. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Presser. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Manager’s Report.
Manager Williams reminded the Council about the Village Yards Open House on
October 18th. He also reported that it appears that Comcast and New Trier High
School have come to an agreement whereby Comcast will move the equipment from
its Hubbard Woods studio to NTHS to set up a new studio for student/school use.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Webster moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:, Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Presser. The
meeting adjourned at 10:28 p.m.
