Recording Secretary
October 7, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 2, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 7, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Steven Presser, and David Webster. Absent:
President Michael Duhl and Trustee David Abell. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee,
Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Public Works Director
Steven Saunders, and 3 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   October 14, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   October 21, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 2, 2003, Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt
the minutes as drafted. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   September 9, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   September 16, 2003, Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to
adopt the minutes as drafted. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. MC-15-2003 – Amending Construction Regulations - Adoption.
President Pro Tem Woodbury commented that Ordinance MC-15-2003 amends the
definition of construction activity in the Village Code and since the Council’s
last meeting, the suggestions of Trustee Webster have been incorporated into
the ordinance.
Village Attorney Janega pointed out that the revisions to the text of the
ordinance were made to incorporate changes proposed by Trustee Webster and
that, because the changes were voted on by the Council at the time of
introduction, it was not necessary to vote to amend the ordinance. She added
that her purpose in pointing out the changes was to ensure that the revised
ordinance reflected Trustee Webster’s recommendations, as voted on by the
Council.
Trustee Webster confirmed that the amendments were as he had proposed and moved
to adopt the ordinance as amended, seconded by Trustee Presser. By roll call
vote the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser
and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee Abell.
a)   Ordinance No. M-36-2003 – Zoning Variation: 515 Meadow - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot, Maximum Building
Size, Front and Corner Yard Setback, and Garage variations to permit expansion
of an attached garage to allow a 16-foot front-facing garage door and new
kitchen and mudroom. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 7
to 0 to recommend that the Front Setback and Garage variations be approved, and
4 to 3 that the Roofed Lot Coverage and Maximum Building Size variations be
approved.
The Trustees voiced some concerns about the mass of the house as it would
appear from the street, heard from the homeowner’s representative as to the
particular hardships of the property and discussed possibilities for a
conforming alternative. Noting that no one appeared in opposition to the
variation request, but that they had received a letter from Mr. Martin Kenny
objecting to granting all requests for variation, the Trustees requested that
the homeowner provide the Council with the neighbors’ comments and agreed to
introduce the ordinance pending further discussion at its next meeting when all
Trustees would be present.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance,
M-36-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-37-2003 – Zoning Variation: 143 Fuller Lane -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Maximum Building Size
variation that would increase the maximum gross floor area to permit the
construction of two one-story additions at the rear of the home that would
expand the kitchen and family room. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 5 to 2 to recommend that the variation be approved.
Mr. Douglas Clark, architect for the project, explained that although the
additions are in two locations, they will allow the interior space to be more
functional by expanding the kitchen to connect to the sunroom, creating a great
room affect. He added that the expansion is modest and similar to additions
made to homes on either side of this property.
After discussion, the consensus of the Council was that the additions will
improve the functionality of the home, possibly prevent a potential teardown,
and improve the appearance of the rear of the house.
Trustee Brower commented that there is a risk of overbuilding in this
neighborhood and making exceptions will invite more requests, but added that he
did not oppose this variation request.
Trustee Woodbury said that he would not like to see additions to the second
floor once this variation has been granted, as that would increase the mass of
the house and asked that the minutes reflect this for future Councils.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-
37-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-38-2003 – Zoning Variation: 845 Prospect - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Side Yard Setback variation to permit
the construction of a one-story addition to the home to provide a larger
breakfast room. The addition would replace an existing deck that encroaches in
the required side yard. Mr. D’Onofrio mentioned two factors pertaining to the
side yard setback. First, earlier this year the setback requirements were
changed to be based on lot width, not zoning district. Second, the petitioner
and his neighbor to the north purchased and subdivided the lot between their
two properties, demolishing the house thereon. This increased the width of
applicant’s lot from 50 feet to 107 feet. This new lot width requires a side
yard setback of 12 feet, whereas petitioner is asking for a setback of 5.83
feet. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 7 to 0 to
recommend approval of the variation.
Andrew Venamore, AirRoom, Inc., Lincolnwood, presented a rendering of the
exterior of the proposed addition and explained that the existing breakfast
room was too small for the number of people in the owner’s family. Responding
to questions from the Trustees about expanding the breakfast room into existing
space such as the family room, since the owners were also constructing a large
great room, Mr. Venamore stated that the owners wanted to continue to use the
family room as a homework study area for their children. Mr. Venamore also
responded to questions about reactions from neighbors, noting that they had
been given the same exterior renderings he had circulated to the Council.
Trustee Greenough stated that she could not vote in favor of any variation for
this property since a historically significant home had been demolished to
create the lots.
President Pro Tem Woodbury stated that he is not in favor of this variation
because there appeared to be space in an adjoining room available for expansion
of the breakfast room. He recalled the discussion at the time the consolidation
of the lots was considered and remembered that one of the concerns was the
proximity of the homes immediately to the north. He asked that Mr. Venamore
propose a different layout and that he return with more information, including
dimensioned drawings with furniture layouts.
Trustees Presser, Brower and Webster expressed support for the variation.
Manager Williams requested that readable floor plans be provided.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-
38-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried, with Trustee Greenough opposing.
d)   Ordinance No. M-39-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1004 Elm St. - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Front Setback variation to permit
reconstruction of a porch. He noted that the proposed porch would be a one-for-
one replacement for the existing front porch, but required a variation because
it is a nonconforming structure and the improvements exceed what is considered
normal repair and maintenance. He also reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had received comments from one neighbor, who expressed opposition to
the project, and that the ZBA had voted 7 to 0 to recommend approval of the
variation.
Manager Williams asked Mr. D’Onofrio if this was truly a one-for-one
replacement and if this is an example of a variation request that could be
authorized by the Director of Community Development in the discussion about
streamlining the zoning ordinance.
Mr. D’Onofrio replied affirmatively.
Village Attorney Janega noted that the ordinance had been prepared for the
Council in final form, because it is a type of variation that the Council is
considering simplifying and because the applicant had to wait a few months for
the Zoning Board of Appeals to hear the case, due to its heavy caseload.
President Pro Tem Woodbury asked for comments or questions from the Council and
the audience members and there being none, suggested that first reading be
waived as provided in the Village Code and that the ordinance adopted.
Trustee Presser moved to waive first reading of the ordinance, seconded by
Trustee Greenough. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President
Duhl and Trustee Abell.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt Ordinance M-39-2003, seconded by Trustee
Greenough. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Presser and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and
Trustee Abell.
e)   Resolution No. R-30-2003 – Approving Agreement between the Village and the
Firefighters’ Union - Adoption. Manager Williams reviewed the major provisions
of the new three-year agreement with the Village’s firefighters. He reported
that the firefighters had ratified the tentative agreement and that the
Village’s bargaining team recommends approval of the contract.
President Pro Tem Woodbury thanked the staff and also the firefighters for the
fine work on the contract and for their continued service to the Village.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt Resolution R-30-
2003. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Presser and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee
Abell.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1335 and 1336. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1335 in the amount of $2,175,413.58 and Warrant List No. 1336 in the
amount of $411,013.92. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee
Abell.
b)   Bid No. 03-018: Sunview Lane Drainage Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids
for the Sunview Lane drainage improvements, which are in the 2nd year of a 3-
year program and will replace the pump system on the golf course, improve the
drainage on Sunview Lane and replace curbs and pavement. He recommended
awarding the bid to the lowest bidder, Lenny Hoffman Excavating, Inc., who has
worked on a number of projects in Winnetka, in the amount of $580,570.
President Pro Tem Woodbury asked if this is the same scope of work as
originally proposed for the estimate of approximately $900,000.
Manager Williams replied that it is and that Village fees were reduced
substantially because Mr. Saunders was able to do the engineering.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Presser and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and
Trustee Abell.
c)   Bid No. 03-17A: Leachate Extraction Well. Mr. Saunders explained that the
Village needs to install five new leachate extraction wells and pump assemblies
at the landfill to supplement and replace existing extraction wells that are no
longer functional. He reviewed the bidding process, which culminated in the
Village’s revising the project to bring it within budget, but noted that the
bid is still, $22,900 over the budgeted amount. Saying that this shortfall
could be funded by reallocating monies dedicated to landscaping the landfill
without reducing the approved plans for the landscape work, he recommended that
the bid be awarded to Pioneer Engineering & Environmental Services, Inc. for
the bid amount of $262,900.
Village Attorney Janega commented on procedural issues in the bidding process.
She noted that the work has been put out for bid twice, that Pioneer has been
the only bidder both time, and that the $262,900 was a negotiated reduction of
Pioneer’s bid amount of $293,500. She advised that, to assure full compliance
with contract procedure and avoid challenges to the process, the appropriate
thing to do is to have a motion to reject the bid for Bid 03-17A and, secondly,
to have a motion to waive the bidding process and award the contract to Pioneer
in the amount negotiated with Mr. Saunders, which would require the affirmative
vote of two-thirds of the Trustees to pass.
Trustee Presser moved to reject the bid, seconded by Trustee Webster. By voice
vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Greenough moved to waive the bidding process and to authorize the
Village Engineer to sign a contract with Pioneer in the amount of $262,900 for
the performance of the work proposed in the Pioneer bid opened on September 9,
2003, as modified by the Village Engineers’ letter of September 24, 2003.
Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser and Webster. Nays: None.
Absent: President Duhl and Trustee Abell.
d)   Quarterly Report: Power Cost Adjustment. Manager Williams reported that
the adjustment formula is working as designed, using the seasonal changes in
power cost to recover most of the lost revenue and using the adjustment billed
to users to augment that process. He asked that rather than report quarterly to
the Council, he incorporate this into the annual budget meetings.
The Trustees agreed that the power cost adjustment report be made annually at
budget meetings.
e)   Set Tax Levy. Mr. McKee reviewed the property tax levy process. Based on
the data available at this time, he recommended that the 2003 property tax levy
amount, net of abatements, be set at $10,167,965. He reported that a homeowner
with a $10,000 property tax bill would expect to see a 2.4%, or $36 increase in
the Village portion.
Mr. McKee and Ms. Janega responded to questions from the Trustees concerning
the effect of new development on tax caps and the tax levy.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to set the tax levy as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Presser and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and
Trustee Abell.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Council Reports.
Trustee Presser reported that the Code Review Committee met this month and
expects to meet this month as well. Ms. Janega commented that the result of the
discussion at this meeting will be on the Council’s agenda next month.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Brower moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
President Duhl and Trustee Abell. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 9:28 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Pro Tem Woodbury, Trustees, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Webster. Absent:
President Duhl and Trustee Abell. Also present, Village Manager Douglas
Williams and Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Manager’s Report.
Manager Williams reported that 36 residents were against and 6 were in favor of
installing the curb extensions on east Willow Road between Maple and Sheridan,
so the Village will not be doing this work.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee
Abell. The meeting adjourned at 10:20 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Village Clerk
October 14, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: October 21, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Trustee
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of
Community Development Brian Norkus, and 7 persons in the audience.
2. 1020 Elm Street – Impervious Surface.
Noting that this item had been placed on the Agenda in response to a petition
filed by residents expressing concerns about the construction of a driveway
located within the front yard at 1020 Elm, President Duhl invited interested
audience members to speak before Staff’s report.
Laurie Peterson, 1026 Elm St., addressed a recent letter in the Winnetka Talk
and expressed dismay that the neighbors received no notification from the
Village about the proposed plans to pave the front yard area. She also raised
potential safety concerns created by the obstructed driveway. She urged the
Council to review the current zoning regulations with an eye toward changing
the existing limit on the amount of impermeable surface that can be placed in
residential front yards and to revisit the issue of what triggers notification
to neighbors.
Carol Meynen, 1050 Elm St., concurred with Ms. Peterson’s assessment that the
new home’s front parking area and turnaround present a hazard to children
walking to/from nearby schools. She voiced her opinion that the project is in
direct violation of the Village’s decision not to allow the addition of new
parking bays. In addition, she read a letter from her husband raising concerns
about possible drainage issues associated with the redevelopment. Ms. Meynen
acknowledged that the builder may have been within his legal rights but
requested that the Council enact changes to the Village Code to prevent similar
homes from being built.
Dick Wallace, 1240 Ash St., spoke in support of the new construction and the
developer’s scheme. He suggested that everyone wait until the project is
completed before condemning it.
Michael Hughes, 474 Sheridan Rd., the developer, stood behind his project and
his vision of an English courtyard setting. He said that the house had been
designed for convenience to the homeowner and with attention to the safety of
children in mind. He asked neighbors to be patient until the construction is
finished, saying that it will be an attractive property when completed. He also
explained all that had been done to address any potential drainage problems
Mr. Norkus reported that current zoning limitations restrict driveways and
other impermeable surfaces “in aggregate” to 50% of any given lot. He reviewed
the impermeable surface coverage on the subject property and reported that the
total impervious surface coverage falls well within the maximum permitted by
Code.
In commenting on the current regulations, Mr. Norkus noted that given the
Village’s expressed preference for detached garages located in the rear of a
lot, “typical” development on a property lacking alley access would incorporate
a sizeable driveway, extending nearly the full length of the lot. He reported
that typically, a full-length driveway with customary and relatively modest
patios might easily approach the permitted maximum permitted area, without
necessarily incorporating any “extraordinary” improvements. He pointed out that
the presence of an alley allows for, but does not necessarily require a
significant reduction in impermeable surfaces due to the significant reduction
in pavement necessary for a driveway.
Mr. Norkus mentioned that despite the preference given for detached garages,
many attached garages are located at the front of houses, especially in larger
lot areas. In instances where a garage is attached at the front, a driveway
will typically expand to provide not only access to the garage, but also
provide additional paved area for maneuvering. In addition, circular driveways
with two curb openings are permitted and relatively common. In such instances,
a driveway may cover rather large amounts of a front yard area.
Finally, Mr. Norkus reviewed possible alternatives for limited impermeable
surfaces, as follows: (1) restrict vehicular access to alleys when an alley
abuts a lot; (2) limit the amount of impermeable surface within the required
front yard setback; (3) decrease the impermeable surface limitations. Noting
that none of these would necessarily prevent a repeat occurrence, he voiced
support for the first option, perhaps in combination with the second.
Manager Williams spoke to traffic safety. He pointed out that there are some
heavily traveled streets in the Village where backing on to the street would be
very undesirable. These streets would include such streets as Tower, Willow,
Sheridan.
Trustee Presser clarified that whatever action the Council might or might not
take will have no effect on the subject property.
Trustee Webster inquired about using landscaping to reduce the visible impact
on the neighbors. Mr. Hughes responded that landscaping is planned where
allowed. Ms. Peterson objected, however, saying that landscaping serves to
further obstruct vision and increases the hazards associated with the design.
President Duhl polled the Trustees on whether they thought the Village should
do anything, and if so, what?
Trustee Presser indicated that he was not ready to take any action because
until the project is completed, it can’t be concluded that something needs to
be done. He said he was not persuaded by the safety concerns and expressed his
opinion that overall this is a residential design issue.
Trustee Abell agreed with Mr. Presser, saying that the issue is not one of
impermeable surface, but one of design. He, too, indicated that he was opposed
to taking any action at this time.
Trustee Greenough strongly disagreed, saying that the Council needs to take
action immediately to prevent a repeat occurrence. She opined that this house
is not in keeping with the remainder of the neighborhood and that driveways
have no place on properties where there is access to an alley.
Trustees Woodbury and Brower expressed disappointment with the subject
property’s design and urged the builder to reconsider his vision. Trustee
Woodbury voiced support for implementing some form of Option #1.
Trustee Webster indicated that he was not in favor of the idea of parking cars
in front yard areas, but said that he was not ready to weigh in on whether
Option #1 or #2 would be an appropriate solution.
President Duhl reminded the Council that at the next meeting they would be
considering the question of the installation of a parking pad. He said that he
believed the issue really is to take a direct approach to paving over front
yard space for any purpose. He suggested that Option #2 might be the most
appropriate.
Mr. Norkus commented that Options #1 and #2 were not necessarily mutually
exclusive.
Manager Williams suggested that the Ad Hoc Committee on Residential Design
might be the best forum for this discussion. Trustees Woodbury and Greenough
opposed that suggestion on the grounds that the process would take too long to
come to some kind of resolution.
Noting that four Trustees were in favor of taking some action, he asked staff
to come back to the Council with a recommendation as to the best course of
action and an evaluation of possible consequences.
3. Historical Society Request to Waive Utility Fees for Log House.
Nancy Judge, 534 Elder Ln., CoPresident of the Winnekta Historical Society
presented the Society’s request that the Village waive utility fees associates
with the recent relocation of the Schmidt-Burnham Log House totaling
$14,703.62.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., spoke in opposition to the request and urged the
Council to deny it. Mr. Kates argued that while the Historical Society is a
worthwhile organization, it is a private entity and is already the recipient of
considerable Village support. In addition to which, he pointed out that the
Village had provided substantial support throughout the move process. Mr. Kates
noted that there are many worthy organizations in the community for whom the
Village does not have a policy of waiving fees. He also enumerated the
Village’s budget problems as outlined by the Village Manager during the budget
hearings. Mr. Kates urged the Council to turn down this request despite the
value of the organization.
Trustee Greenough spoke in support of the request, saying that the Log House
move was a community-wide process and that the house, itself, is a valuable
community asset. She said that she did not believe that waiving the fees would
establish a precedent.
Trustee Presser concurred with Mr. Kates and indicated that he believes that to
grant the request would be precedent setting. Trustees Webster, Brower, and
Woodbury agreed. Trustee Woodbury pointed out that by virtue of the new fee for
services arrangement established with the Society, the Society is expected to
net an additional $6,000 during the current fiscal year that could be used to
offset some of the subject fees.
Trustee Abell had no comment.
4. 2002 Zoning Amendment Analysis – 10 Properties.
Mr. D’Onofrio reveiwed Staff’s analysis of ten homes constructed under the
amended 2002 Zoning Ordinance, as directed by the Council at its September 9th
Study Session. He explained that Staff conducted an analysis of these homes
examining them in relation to various zoning requirements. Specifically, staff
analyzed each by looking at gross floor area, roofed lot coverage, impervious
surface, yard setbacks, attic floor height, attic GFA allowance and building
height. He reported that there does not appear to be a consistent correlation
between the zoning requirements and design, nor does there appear to be a
correlation between floor height and the attic GFA allowance. He said that
given the data, it is difficult to predict that an increase in the attic GFA
allowance would result in more homes with peaked roofs, as architects/builders
may be no more inclined to take advantage of such incentives. He pointed out
that of the ten houses, only two took advantage of the maximum attic bonus
allowance available, while five took no allowance at all.
Doug Harness, 523 Hawthorne, a local builder; commented on design and new
Zoning Ordinance regulations from personal experience. He expressed his belief
that there is a relationship between the floor height and the overall height.
He suggested that the Council look at what the actual attic floor height is
rather than the arbitrary, artificial attic floor height currently used in the
regulations.
Trustee Woodbury disagreed with Mr. D’Onofrio’s assessment that the Council
should leave the Zoning Ordinance alone. He indicated that despite the lack of
statistical support, he believes that increasing the absolute building height
by two (2) feet across the board and increasing the attic allowance to 400 ft
would do no harm.
President Duhl asked Mr. D’Onofrio to talk further with Mr. Harness and do some
additional analysis.
5. Draft Northfield Wholesale Water Contract.
Manager Williams reviewed the draft Northfield Wholesale Water Contract,
highlighting the key provisions, as follows:
   1.   Except in an emergency situation, Northfield will purchase 100% of its
water from Winnetka;
   2.   Winnetka will guarantee a water pressure of 55 psi at the three
delivery points to Northfield; and
   3.   Initially the Northfield-Winnetka wholesale rate will be based on the
average of the wholesale rates charged in 6 other wholesale agreements. For 15
years, the Northfield-Winnetka wholesale rate will be indexed to the changes in
the Chicagoland CPI-U. In year 16, there will be a true up in the rate by
setting it to the average of the six other wholesale agreements. For years 17
through 30, the rate will again be indexed to the CPI-U.
Manager Williams advised the Council that the Village had committed to adding a
third interconnect with Northfield and promised that within seven years it
would upgrade and/or replace the interconnect with Wilmette.
There was general consensus that the draft document was agreeable and could be
approved after final review and revision by the Village Attorney.
6. Skokie Lagoon Vision.
Daniel Kielson, representing Friends and Users of the Skokie Lagoons, explained
that the organization’s vision is that the Skokie Lagoons will be formally
designated as The Skokie Lagoons Wildlife and Recreation Area. This area would
then have its own board of control, under the auspices of the Cook County
Forest Preserve District, to be financially supported in part by private
funding, and would include a Wildlife Rehabilitation and Learning Center
facility on the premises. Its mission will be to address the functions
regarding the ecological welfare of the Skokie Lagoon, the offering of
recreational opportunities, the caring for wildlife animals indigenous to Cook
County, and the offering of wildlife education. Mr. Kielson indicated that the
organization plans to propose that the Cook County Commissioners accept the
Vision and establish a Skokie Lagoons Task Force. Mr. Kielson asked for the
Council’s support and endorsement in pursuit of this end. He mentioned that Dr.
Bangser, Superintendent of New Trier High School, had endorsed the project and
that he would be seeking similar support of other abutting communities.
Trustee Woodbury inquired as to the proposed boundaries of the property to be
so designated. He indicated that he believes that a dialogue should be held
with any affected neighbors
President Duhl explained to Mr. Keilson that from a historical standpoint,
neither the Council as a whole, nor the Village President, endorse these kinds
of projects officially.
After a brief discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees that the
project is a worthwhile one because the Skokie Lagoons are an important asset
to the area and prudent management is to be encouraged. However, given the
precedent of past practice, there was agreement that the Council as a corporate
entity should not officially endorse the project.
Trustee Presser suggested that the Minutes reflect that the matter was brought
before the Council for discussion but not for action and that the concept
received enthusiastic support from the Trustees, individually.
8. Executive Session.
None.
9. Manager’s Report.
None.
10. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:46 p.m.
October 21, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 2, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 21, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, Steven Presser, and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Public Works Director Steven Saunders, and 16 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   November 4, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   November 11, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 14, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-36-2003 – Zoning Variation: 515 Meadow - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the shell ordinance
introduced at the last meeting.
Healy Rice, the project’s architect, briefly explained the requested variation.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the amendments
as presented by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-36-
2003, as amended, granting Intensity of Use of Lot, Maximum Building Size,
Front and Corner Yard Setback, and Garage variations to permit the expansion of
an attached garage. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-37-2003 – Zoning Variation: 143 Fuller Ln. – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the shell ordinance
introduced at the last meeting.
Douglas Clark, the architect, commented on the proposed addition and responded
to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the amendments as
presented by Attorney Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-37-
2003, as amended, granting a Maximum Building Size variation to permit the
construction of two one-story additions. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-38-2003 – Zoning Variation: 845 Prospect - Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that no amendments to the shell ordinance had been
prepared because the minutes of the pertinent Zoning Board of Appeals meeting
do not support findings as to:
   i)   the practical difficulties and particular hardships associated with
this zoning variance request;
   ii)   why the property cannot yield a reasonable return; and
   iii)   if the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
he neighborhood.
In the absence of these determinations, she said that the Council could (1)
articulate findings itself, (2) deny the request because there are no
supporting facts, or (3) send the matter back to the Zoning Board for a
rehearing and more complete record.
President Duhl explained to the applicant that based upon the record, the
Council would deny the request and asked whether the applicant would like to
proceed or take the matter back before the Zoning Board.
The applicant, Jim O’Brien, indicated that he would like to make a presentation
to the Village Council, however, Trustee Webster moved to refer the variation
request back to the Zoning Board of Appeals. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried, with all of the Trustees except
Trustee Woodbury voting favorably.
d)   Resolution No. R-31-2003 – Release of Executive Session Minutes. -
Adoption. Mr. Williams explained that this is a Village housekeeping duty
required by State law to be performed every six months. He noted that while the
Council historically has released minutes in December and June, the schedule is
being changed to prevent the overlap of Councils in the spring after the
election of new Trustees. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to
approve the minutes of the Executive Sessions held June 10, 2003, through
September 16, 2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Abell then moved to adopt Resolution No. R-31-2003 releasing those
minutes no longer required to be held for reasons of confidentiality. Trustee
Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and
Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
a)   Policy Direction: Pine Street Sidewalk. Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed the
history of this matter. He reported that in keeping with Council’s direction to
preserve trees and work with neighbors having special needs, Staff had met with
these affected residents to try address their concerns. He noted that Staff had
been specifically directed to work particularly with the three homeowners
between Glendale and Berkeley to route the proposed sidewalk so as to minimize
any negative impact. Subsequent thereto, a route for the proposed sidewalk was
staked and all neighborhood residents were sent a letter informing them of the
route and giving them an opportunity to comment on or modify the route before
construction began.
Mr. Saunders distributed maps of the proposed route, circulated photographs,
and discussed the reasons for the chosen route. He focused primarily on the
section from Glendale to Berkeley, where the route is primarily along the curb.
On October 16th the Village received a letter from Gabrielle and Andrew
Fischer, 1180 Pine Street, expressing concern and disappointment at the
proposed routing, particularly of the section of sidewalk between Glendale and
Berkeley. They requested that it be reconsidered and rerouted to match the
route shown in the document voted on by the affected residents. Mr. Saunders
mentioned that Staff had also received requests from several other residents
requesting that the sidewalk be relocated adjacent to or closer to the curb in
front of their properties.
Mr. Saunders asked for policy direction from the Council as to (i) how the
sidewalk should be located in front of 1124, 1134, and 1154 Pine Street, and
(ii) how Staff should respond to requests from individual homeowners concerning
desired modifications to the route.
President Duhl asked Mr. Saunders whether the Village Forester had approved the
proposed route. President Duhl also inquired as to whether the Forester had
determined that that there would be increased hazard to the trees not to place
the sidewalk along the curb. Mr. Saunders responded affirmatively to both
questions.
Trustee Woodbury asked whether locating the sidewalk next to the curb meets
safety standards. Again, Mr. Saunders replied affirmatively.
Trustee Abell inquired about the possibility of routing around the tree in
front of 1124 Pine. Mr. Saunders responded that the route in front of the tree
had been chosen to minimize root disruption. Mr. Stier confirmed this.
Trustee Brower asked about the impact of the meander staked out on the west
side of 1154. Mr. Saunders replied that this was done because the trees in that
area are reasonably hardy and would likely survive, in addition to which it
meets up with the sidewalk on the other side of Berkeley and provides some
aesthetic softening.
Andrew Fischer, 1180 Pine Street, objected to the proposed routing, saying that
no where in the sidewalk policy does it indicate that the plan upon which
neighbors voted was “conceptual” in nature. He represented that many of the
votes in favor of the sidewalk would, in fact, have been against, had the plan
shown a carriage walk, instead of a sidewalk separated from the street by
parkway.
Dan Kadjian, 1124 Pine, expressed his belief that it is the function of the
Council to honor form over substance, but to do what is right by preserving
this as a wooded area insofar as possible. The Sidewalk Guidelines are not
chiseled in stone and leave it to Staff to determine an appropriate path. He
urged approval of the staked route.
Sharon King, 1134 Pine, spoke in opposition to any rerouting of the proposed
sidewalk. She pointed out that the sidewalk guidelines provide that the Village
Council may waive compliance, depart from, or amend the guidelines at any time
if in its sole discretion, it deems such action is warranted by the
circumstances. She expressed her opinion that the Council has exercised its
discretion and Staff has done as it was directed.
Steve Baird, 1154 Pine, voiced his belief that the Council and Staff have done
their best to accommodate the concerns of residents, meet the needs of the
neighbors, and preserve the trees. The proposed route is one with which all
affected property owners are comfortable and does not pose any safety hazard.
Suzanne Leach, 1094 Pine, indicated that she had never been in favor of any
sidewalk, but from an aesthetic standpoint prefers a carriage walk design to a
separated sidewalk. She also expressed concern about the divisive effect this
issue has had on neighborhood relationships.
Gabrielle Fischer, 1180 Pine, argued in favor of the first design saying that
it would provide greater safety because it has a buffer between road and
sidewalk and more green space overall, without significantly damaging the
trees.
Trustee Presser asked Ms. Fischer if she was saying that the Village’s current
design is not safe. She responded that it was not the safest possible design.
Trustee Webster inquired whether a one-foot separation would alleviate her
concerns. Ms. Fischer replied that any buffer would be safer than none, the
bigger the better.
Trustee Webster asked Mr. Saunders about decreasing the width of the sidewalk
by one foot to provide some separation between curb and sidewalk. Mr. Saunders
responded that a one-foot strip is extremely difficult to maintain. Trustee
Webster then asked about possibility of having a four-foot sidewalk as opposed
to a six-foot sidewalk. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village’s plows could
not accommodate that narrow of a sidewalk.
Trustee Brower inquired whether there have been any problems with the carriage
walk on Westmoor. Manager Williams reported that none have been reported either
there or along Tower Road. Trustee Woodbury pointed out that there are carriage
walks all over the Village.
Susan Baird, 1154 Pine, supported Trustee Woodbury’s comments, saying that
there are carriage walks around virtually every school and that there have been
no significant problems. She asked that the Council approve the route as staked
out.
Trustee Presser pointed out that while the Council had asked for help from
neighbors, the Council had never given up its right to make the final
determination about the route. He said that while there are many issues to be
weighed in this matter that, ultimately, it is Council’s responsibility to
determine the best solution. He expressed support for Staff’s work and their
plan and said he was in favor of approving it.
Trustee Abell remarked that the Village has gone through considerable process
to accommodate all of the interests in this matter. He said that he was
originally disturbed at the prospect of not putting a sidewalk between Glendale
and Berkeley because of the neighbors’ objections. He believes the sidewalk
ought to be continuous and realizes that several of the Pine Street neighbors
have, in fact, appropriated the public parkway as part of their own property.
Consequently, the Village is now constrained in what it can do because of
plantings, curb cuts, driveways, etc. There are two competing considerations:
the first being to keep the sidewalk away from the street, and the second being
to protect the trees. He said he believes that tree preservation has prevailed
and that Staff has devised the best possible solution.
Trustee Greenough said that she believes that all of the parties in this matter
have acted in good faith and that the Village has followed Council’s
instruction and intent to the best of their ability. It’s a difficult and
emotional issue, but she believes the proposed route is the best possible under
the circumstances and is in favor of its approval. She also pointed out that
parked cars along the south side of Pine Street provide some barrier to
pedestrians along the carriage walk portion of the walkway.
Trustees Woodbury and Brower concurred with Trustee Greenough.
Trustee Webster also agreed saying that the Village never entered into a
binding contract with the neighbors and that there was always the understanding
that the sidewalk might have to run along the street in some places in order to
preserve trees. He said he was not persuaded by the safety concern and was not
in favor of reopening the issue.
Hearing a unanimity to leave the final route to Staff’s discretion, President
Duhl thanked all who participated and called for a motion to authorize the
Village Manager to deal with any last minute routing decisions. Trustee
Woodbury so moved. He was seconded by Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Abell asked about the reported requests from other neighbors to move
the stakes closer to the street. Mr. Saunders replied that it was Staff’s
intention to stick with the route as staked with only very minor adjustments.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1337 and 1338. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1337 in the amount of $527,966.82 and Warrant
List No. 1338 in the amount of $899,427.21. Trustee Abell seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Abell, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: Parking Bay Request 1063 Cherry St. President Duhl
explained that this matter had been discussed and tabled at the September 16th
Council meeting, with the expectation that the Caucus would ask a relevant
question on its annual questionnaire. However, in view of the fact that the
question was not included on the questionnaire, the Council must now consider
the matter of parking bays without direction from Caucus.
Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed the request to install a parking bay in the same
location as one that existed prior to the construction of the existing
residence. He also reviewed prior Council policy as gleaned from agenda reports
and minutes. In addition, he reported that the Village Forester had determined
that the only location for a parking bay at the site without negative impact on
the trees is where the former one was located.
Manager Williams reported that the developer had been advised in April 2003
that the parking bay would have to be removed and that the current owners did
not close on the property until the end of June.
Trustee Abell asked whether it made any difference to the Village what kind of
paving material was used for the parking bay. Mr. Saunders responded that from
a street maintenance standpoint, there would be nothing negative about using
pavers instead of asphalt pavement.
Monica Boyle spoke in support of her request, citing the Forester’s finding
that the only place on the lot to put additional parking is the on the parkway.
She indicated that when they purchased the property, although there was no
parking bay in existence, they were unaware of the Village’s restrictions on
on-street parking.
Trustee Presser pointed out that the home has a two-car garage already, and
that the applicant is seeking parking for two additional cars.
Trustee Brower indicated that he is troubled by the lack of real hardship here.
Ms. Boyle countered that had the developer known he was going to have to remove
the parking lot earlier in the design process, the overall design of the home
might have been very different. However, because he did not know until towards
the end of the project, there is no alternative that does not significantly
impact trees.
Trustee Woodbury voiced his objection to parking bays in general and the
possible precedent that might be set by granting this request.
Trustee Webster indicated that although he had previously been inclined to vote
in favor of reinstallation of the parking bay, however, the owner’s recent
letter to the Council persuaded him to vote no.
Trustee Greenough expressed her opinion that the real cause of the problem is
the Village’s ban on overnight parking and suggested that the issue be put
aside until after Council’s further consideration of that restriction.
Trustee Abell indicated that he is opposed to the request to reinstall the
parking bay, particularly in view of the fact that the owners bought the
property without the parking bay.
Trustee Presser concurred with the remarks of his fellow Trustees.
President Duhl indicated to Mrs. Boyle that the Trustees were unanimously
opposed to reinstalling the parking pad. He reviewed available options for her
in the short term and indicated that there is much that can happen to loosen up
the overnight parking policy before her daughters reach driving age.
c)   Policy Direction: 854 Eldorado. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for five
zoning variances to permit the construction of an expanded, attached garage and
second floor addition above the garage. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 6 to 1 to recommend in favor of the Yard Setback and Garage
variations, however, the ZBA’s motion to approve the Roofed Lot Coverage and
Maximum Building Size variations failed by a vote of 5 to 2. Mr. D’Onofrio
indicated that since the Zoning Board’s decision, the petitioner has revised
his plans to reduce both roofed lot coverage and gross floor area.
Trustee Woodbury asked Mr. D’Onofrio to shed some light on the Zoning Board’s
discussion, which he did, indicating that the Board’s vote was basically
against the increased gross floor area.
Jim Kalas, the project’s architect, reviewed the revised sketches and layouts.
He explained the applicants’ goals and enumerated the steps taken to address
the Zoning Board’s concerns.
After a brief discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees that the
applicants have made a good faith effort to follow the suggestions of the
Zoning Board while still maintaining the update of the house. Therefore,
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to direct the Village
Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting the requested variations. By voice
vote the motion carried, with all except Trustee Webster voting in favor of it.
d)   Policy Direction: 875 Gordon Terrace. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
five variances to permit the construction of a two and one-half story addition
and an attached garage. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted
7-0 to recommend denial of the variations. He explained that there is an
existing 31” Burr Oak tree on the property that prevents the petitioner from
finding a conforming location for the proposed expansion.
Eric Johnson, the architect, used to photos and elevations to explain the
petitioner’s request.
Trustee Abell inquired whether the house has any historical significance.
Mr. Spaan, the owner, responded that because of its age, it is undoubtedly of
some historical significance. He voiced his belief that the Zoning Code fails
to take trees into consideration as a hardship. He indicated that they wish to
preserve the current house, rather than tear it down.
Trustee Presser said he was inclined to grant the variations in order to
preserve the trees, particularly in light of the fact that there are no
objections from the neighbors.
Trustee Abell indicated that he is uncomfortable bending the rules for a huge
lot, although he respects what the architect has designed. He voiced no
opinion.
Trustee Greenough stated that she believes that the hardship is the location of
the house on the lot, which predates the Zoning Code. She pointed out that this
is a Burr Oak savanna and that there are no conforming alternatives that do not
disturb sensitive tree roots.
Trustee Webster said that ordinarily a 7-0 vote from the Zoning Board would
weigh heavily, but given the lack of a conforming alternative and the
significant impact that possible alternative designs might have on the tree
roots, he was inclined to approve the requested variation.
Trustee Woodbury suggested some possible alterations to the design that would
make it more palatable to him but said that overall he was in favor of the
plan.
Trustee Brower urged the applicant to make it easier for the Council by
accepting Mr. Woodbury’s suggestions, but said that he, too, was inclined to
vote in favor of the variations.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to direct the Village
Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting the requested variations. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Sacred Heart Development Agreement. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the draft
agreement prepared to cover the expansion of the Sacred Heart School and
construction of a parish center. He noted that while much of the draft contains
language used in other Village development agreements, it was tailored to the
Sacred Heart project. In particular, he noted Sections 5 & 6 that contain
requirements specific to the project, including zoning regulations,
architectural design, use of the building during construction, employee
parking, construction traffic and site staging areas.
Trustee Woodbury inquired whether the off-site parking provisions were similar
to those employed in the Winnetka Community House Development Agreement. Mr.
D’Onofrio indicated that they were.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the agreement in
substantially the form drafted. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, Greenough, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   State Bid: Salt. Manager Williams reported that the Village participates
in the purchase of rock salt through the State of Illinois bid and that it is
estimated that 1,673 tons of rock salt will be required for the coming winter
season. He reported that the successful bidder is Morton International, Inc.
and recommended that the Council approve the purchase of the salt from the
State of Illinois low bidder.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to award the bid as
recommended at $29.89/ton. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
g)   Replacement Dump Truck. Manager Williams explained that the Water &
Electric Department needs to replace an existing dump truck that is in such
poor condition that it is doubtful that it will pass required safety
inspections. He reported that the chassis/cab is being purchased under the
state bid for $28,849.35 from Dennison Corporation and that it will have the
dump body, hydraulic tool circuit, and the snowplowing equipment installed for
$13,934. He noted that this would give the Village one vendor for a completely
equipped truck and, although slightly over the budgeted amount, would not
adversely impact the budget because a forklift truck being purchased is priced
under budget.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Manager Williams directed the
Trustees’ attention to the reports filed by department heads.
i)   Quarterly Report: Village Yards Update. Manager Williams referred the
Trustees to the construction update prepared by Mr. Saunders and included in
the Agenda Packet.
10. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
11. Council Reports.
a)   Trustee Presser reported that the Code Review Committee had met this month
and plans to meet again next month.
b)   Trustee Brower reported on a recent meeting of the Chamber of Commerce.
c)   President Duhl passed around a thank you card presented to him at the
opening of the new Village Yards and signed by all of the Public Works and
Water & Electric employees working at the Yards. He also reported that he had
convened a meeting of past Village presidents to study the feasibility and
desirability of moving toward home rule.
12. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Robert Zielinski as the Landmark
Preservation Commission representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee
Greenough so moved, seconded by Trustee Abell. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
13. Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour Executive Session was cancelled.
14. Manager’s Report.
None.
15. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned
at 10:26 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
November 4, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 18, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 4, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
and six persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance. He then thanked
Michelle Ofstun, our videographer, for her years of service.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 8(b) a request from the Chamber of Commerce
had been withdrawn from the Agenda by the Chamber. The Council approved the
Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum
a)   November 11, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   November 18, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 7, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   October 21, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustees Abell and Webster made minor
corrections to the draft text. Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes as
amended. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-40-2003 – Zoning Variation: 854 Eldorado - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot, Maximum Building
Size, Front, Corner, and Side Yard Setback and Garage variations to permit the
construction of an expanded attached garage and a second floor addition above
it, noting that this matter had been discussed at the last meeting. He reported
that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-1 to recommend in favor of the
Yard and Garage variations and 2-5 to recommend in favor of the Intensity of
Use of Lot and Maximum Building Size variations.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-
40-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   Ordinance No. M-41-2003 – Zoning Variation: 875 Gordon Terrace -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front, Corner, Side, and
Rear Yard Setback and Garage variations to permit the construction of a 2-1/
2 story addition and an attached garage, noting that this matter had been
discussed at the last meeting. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 7-0 to recommend that the variations be denied.
Trustee Brower expressed some concern that the applicants were pushing the
envelope with regard to the garage and urged them reconsider its size.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-
41-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. M-42-2003 – Zoning Variation: 228 Poplar - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front and Side Yard Setback variations to
permit the construction of a detached garage. He reported that the ZBA had
voted 7-0 to recommend the variations be approved.
The applicant, Jean Caponi, responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Greenough noted the presence of a large Oak tree behind the garage and
asked whether the applicant had taken precautions to minimize any impact from
the construction activity. She suggested that Ms. Caponi contact the Village
Forester to solicit his input.
Trustee Woodbury expressed concern about traffic backing out onto Wilson Ave.
and requested that Staff analyze the proposed plan and address any safety
issues.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-42-
2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-43-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1422 Edgewood - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Side Yard Setback variation to permit
the expansion of an existing second floor dormer. He reported that the ZBA had
voted 7-0 to recommend approval of the variation request.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-
43-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
e)   Ordinance No. M-44-2003 – Zoning Variation: 220 Birch - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Front Setback variation to permit enclosing
an existing covered porch. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 7-0 to recommend that the variation be approved.
Trustee Greenough inquired whether the porch was a part of the original
structure. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that there are no building permits in the
property file to indicate otherwise. Ms. Greenough then mentioned that this
house was once the coach house of a Howard Van Doren Shaw estate and as such is
historically significant to the Village as the only remaining portion of that
piece of his work.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-44-
2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1339 and 1340. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1339 in the amount of $950,432.63 and Warrant
List No. 1340 in the amount of $590,034.06. Trustee Webster seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Abell, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Chamber of Commerce Request: Holiday Highlights. As noted earlier, this
item was removed from the Agenda at the request of the Chamber.
a)   Policy Direction: 630 Locust. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum
Building Size and Side Yard Setback variations to permit construction of a two-
story addition. He reported that the ZBA had voted 7-0 to recommend that the
variations be denied.
Trustee Woodbury inquired whether space was being added over the garage. The
applicant, Mark Westhoff, replied that the space currently exists, but is
unused. Pursuant to these plans, it would be finished out as bedroom space. Mr.
D’Onofrio informed Trustee Woodbury that this improved space does count toward
GFA.
Mr. Westhoff explained that he and his architect have spent many hours trying
to find a conforming alternative for adding the desired family room.
Trustees Greenough suggested a couple of possible alternatives. She explained
to Mr. Westhoff that variations are based on need that run with the land, not
upon needs that have to do with house design. Trustee Woodbury concurred that
there are several practical alternatives to improve the design and eliminate
the need for a variation.
After further discussion, Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved
to concur with the recommendation of the Zoning Board. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury,
Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Bid: Fork Lift. Manager Williams reviewed bids for the purchase and
delivery of a new forklift to replace the existing 1986 forklift used by the
Public Works and Water & Electric Departments that has exceeded its useful
life. He reported Staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to Komatsu
Kalmar in the amount of $27,059, which is below the amount included in the
budget for the replacement of this piece of equipment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid: Vacuum Leaf Machine. Manager Williams reviewed the bids for a new
leaf machine designed to fit on the back of the hook lift truck. He reported
that in May 2000, the Village chose to standardize equipment and purchased a
Giant Vac brand leaf machine from Standard Equipment Co. that has proven
reliable. He reported Staff’s recommendation that the bid process be waived and
a similar piece of equipment be purchased from Standard Equipment, the sole
source dealer in the Chicagoland area for this type of equipment. This would
maintain the Village’s fleet standard for leaf machines.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to waive the bid process and
award the bid to Standard Equipment co., in the total amount of $28,139. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster, Presser,
Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Council Reports.
a)   Trustee Woodbury reported that he and Staff had met with representatives
with regard to the renovation of the Elm Street train station. He noted that
all parties agree that because of repeated problems associated with the
elevators, it would be best to develop a plan utilizing ramps, similar to that
used at the Evanston Central Street station.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Webster
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:15 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Manager’s Report.
Manager Williams reported that the Care of Trees would once again be installing
the holiday lights, for a total amount of $20,126, and that the lights will be
turned on November 21st. He also reported that the Village would be giving each
employee a $30 gift certificate to Grand Foods as a holiday “thank you” for
their dedicated service.
Mr. Williams indicated that work continues on the Village’s new web site and
that it is hoped that it will be launched early next year.
Finally, Mr. Williams noted that he would be reviewing the parking arrangements
at the Winnetka Community House.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned
at 9:25 p.m.
