Recording Secretary
November 11, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 2, 2003)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Trustee
Edmund Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director
of Public Works Steven Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee, Police Chief Joe
DeLopez and two persons in the audience.
2. Draft Ordinance – Streamlining Zoning Variance Process.
In reviewing the history of this matter, Attorney Janega noted that at its July
and August study sessions the Council considered the Community Development
staff’s analysis of the Village’s zoning variation processes. In addition to
analyzing the number and types of cases considered by both the Zoning Board of
Appeals and the Village Council, Community Development staff also put forth
several proposals for streamlining the zoning variation process by shifting
some of the final decision-making authority from the Village Council to the ZBA
and Community Development staff.
Attorney Janega referred the Trustees to two tables included with the agenda
materials illustrating the zoning amendments that would be required to
implement the proposed changes. She pointed out that, while the tables attempt
to address the substance of all of the staff’s recommendations, they do not
reflect all of the procedural recommendations. The key difference between the
delineation of authority depicted in the tables and the allocation of decision-
making authority proposed by staff is that the tables do not implement the
proposal that staff screen and make final decisions on many applications using
an administrative process patterned after the zoning variation hearings.
She explained that the reason for the difference in approach is that the
Village of Winnetka is not a home rule unit, and is, therefore, bound to comply
with the variation procedures provided by state statute. Pursuant to those
provisions, only the ZBA and the Village Council are authorized to grant
variations, and only after a public hearing held by the ZBA. She added,
however, that despite the statutory constraints, the Zoning Ordinance can be
amended to accomplish most, though not all, of the staff’s recommended changes
through a combination of amendments, primarily in the provisions pertaining to
nonconformities.
Attorney Janega noted that some of the proposed amendments would change some of
the matters that currently require a zoning variation into matters that merely
require staff to review plans for consistency with the Zoning Ordinance.
In referring to the tables, Attorney Janega explained that they attempt to
define a continuum of gradually increasing Council scrutiny of variation
requests. Most of the authority delegated to staff would involve homes built
before the Zoning Ordinance was first enacted, as these homes tend to have the
most nonconformities in yards and setbacks. On the other hand, virtually all
requested variations for post-FAR homes, as defined in the Zoning Ordinance,
would go to the Council, while most homes built roughly from 1925 to 1990 would
fall primarily under the jurisdiction of the Zoning Board of Appeals. She
indicated that by implementing a process whereby the oldest homes are subject
to an administrative process, the Village would be fostering the purpose and
intent of the Zoning Ordinance to encourage renovation and restoration of
structurally sound older homes.
The Trustees then reviewed and discussed the items in the tables and the policy
issues they raised.
Trustee Woodbury inquired whether the proposed staff administrative process
would be one with public notice and hearing. Attorney Janega responded that,
while that is what staff contemplated, she did not incorporate it into the
proposals in the tables, because the Village is not a home rule unit and thus
cannot implement such a policy. Mr. Woodbury voiced disappointment at this
because the appeal of staff’s concept was that it would still be a public
process.
Manager Williams explained that, because the Village is not home rule, it can
streamline the process either by folding all variations under the Zoning
Board’s authority, or by trying to write specific nonconformities into the
Zoning Ordinance. Or, the Council could choose not to give staff any
discretion, expand the nonconformities section, and wait for a decision on home
rule.
President Duhl expressed some concern about being able to make the
nonconformity option work. He asked Ms. Janega to explain more fully how this
proposal would work, which she did. Following her explanation, Mr. Duhl
indicated that although he is very much in favor of pushing more responsibility
to the Zoning Board, he would rather not risk giving staff discretion until the
Village elects to become a home rule unit so the process can be more public.
Mr. D’Onofrio commented that while the goal was to streamline the process, the
proposed amendments do not do much to achieve that end.
After extended discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees to give the
Zoning Board more authority with regard to (1) the replacement in kind of
nonconforming open porches, desks, and patios in pre-FAR houses, with no change
in footprint, and (2) any structural change that reduces the degree of
nonconformity or does not increase the degree of nonconformity, regardless of
date of construction. The Trustees concluded that these matters have very
little impact on the neighbors.
Manager Williams noted that there are five components to be resolved before
amending the Zoning Ordinance: streamlining the variation process; the height
issue; attic GFA; articulation, and impervious surface. He suggested that staff
continue to flesh out these issues and bring them all together before the
Council sometime after the first of the year.
3. Front Yard Impervious Surface Analysis.
Mr. Norkus reviewed the October 14th Council Study Session at which staff
outlined possible alternatives for placing additional zoning limits on
impermeable surfaces, in addition to limitations on construction of driveways
and associated curb cuts. Options outlined included restricting the standards
for allowing curb cuts when a property is served by an alley, as well as
amending the Zoning Ordinance to further restrict the amount of impermeable
area permitted within the required front yard setback. He then addressed these
recommendations in more specific detail.
With regard to restrictions on curb cuts for blocks served by public alleys,
Mr. Norkus explained that the proposed amendments have been drafted for
discussion purposes with the intent of requiring that any new driveway built on
a block served by an alley be configured to take advantage of the alley for
access. Draft standards for the location of driveways vary depending on the
location of a lot on such a block. Lots located on the interior of a block
would be restricted to allowing driveway access from alleys only. Lots located
at the end of an alley would be permitted driveway access either from the alley
or from the street perpendicular to the alley. He noted that there are a small
number of corner lots located on blocks served by alleys that do not abut the
alley. In such instances, the preferred driveway location, all other factors
being equal, would be to force the driveway to the street perpendicular to the
alley, to preserve the uninterrupted sidewalk along the street parallel with
the alley. He pointed out, however, that factors such as the location of trees,
fire hydrants and utility poles, as well as general public safety concerns,
dictate flexibility with these standards. It is anticipated that the Director
of Public Works would have authority to waive the requirements of this section,
particularly on corner lots.
Mr. Norkus noted that these provisions are not intended to prevent ordinary
repair and replacement of existing non-compliant driveways. However, it is
intended that in the event of construction of a new residence, a nonconforming
driveway must be removed and replaced with one in a conforming location.
Regarding restrictions on curb cuts, Mr. Norkus explained that currently the
Village Code limits the construction of driveways to one per lot, but allows
the Director of Public Works to issue a permit for an additional second
driveway if warranted and if it will not impair public safety. This has led to
circular driveways becoming relatively common, not only on heavily traveled
collector streets, but also on smaller, less heavily traveled streets.
Mr. Norkus referred the Trustees to a memorandum from the Director of Public
Works enumerating those Village streets which, due to the combination of
traffic speed and volume, pose difficulties in backing out of driveways. In
that memo Mr. Saunders mentioned circular driveways, front yard turnarounds,
and backyard turnarounds as possible alternatives that eliminate the need to
back into traffic. In that memorandum, Mr. Saunders also notes that rear yard
turnarounds, while less visible from the street, tend to be more problematic
from a drainage standpoint.
Mr. Norkus reported that given the existence of numerous circular driveways on
lower traffic volume streets and the potential difficulties when additional
pavement is located in the rear yard, it is not recommended to limit circular
driveways to high volume streets. However, the proposed draft provides
additional standards to control the location of driveways when a second curb
cut is proposed.
Finally, with regard to limiting the amount of impermeable surface within the
required front yard setback, Mr. Norkus suggested that any new limits placed on
front yard impermeable surfaces be directed to smaller lots. It is the smaller
lots where size and busy streets combine to make turnarounds more of a
maneuvering necessity than a design statement. Consequently, he reported
staff’s recommendation that in the R-5, R-4, and R-3 zoning districts, the
regulations be amended to allow no more than 30% of the required front yard to
be covered with impermeable surfaces.
Trustee Presser indicated that he believes that he is reluctant to impose
further regulations. He suggested that the issue is really one of design and
expressed a preference to wait until the whole residential design matter is
resolved.
Trustees Greenough and Woodbury objected on the grounds that it will be some
time before the residential design study is completed.
After further discussion, there was a consensus to implement the concept of
limiting impervious surface to 30% of the lesser of either the required front
yard or the actual front yard. The Trustees also agreed to restrict curb cuts
on streets with developed alleys as outlined in the Agenda materials.
4. Police Department Parking Enforcement Program Purchase.
Chief DeLopez reviewed a proposal to purchase new Professional Parking
Solutions software/hardware for parking enforcement and management. He reported
that this purchase would enable the Police Department to create a new, more
manageable, integrated parking management system that will limit tickets
written in error, improve appeal and collection processes, and provide useful
management information. The new system would replace the existing Radix System
that is outdated, cumbersome, and required repeated servicing. Chief DeLopez
reported that the long term cost to the Village will be $10,000 annually once
the equipment is paid for and that the vendor represents that this system
should result in increased revenues due to efficiencies in issuance,
processing, and collections.
Mr. McKee reported that there would be no negative impact on the budget, and
there was a consensus to approve the proposal at the next regular Council
meeting.
5. Automobile Allowance Withholding.
Mr. McKee explained that, as discussed previously this year, the Village’s
treatment of the automobile allowances should be changed because current
practice does not provide for the appropriate Social Security withholding. In
addition, it is not consistent with general practice in other communities. He
reviewed the procedures recommended to bring Village’s policy into compliance
with withholding regulations.
There were no objections voiced from the Trustees, and in order to avoid any
negative impact on employees receiving an auto allowance, they approved adding
an additional $35 to the current $430 automobile allowance to cover the
employee’s share of the Social Security that is deducted for these payments.
Discussion: Construction Regulation and Enforcement. Manager Williams asked Mr.
D’Onofrio and Mr. Saunders to review their departments’ practices with regard
to the enforcement of building regulations and drainage standards of
construction sites, which they did. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Village
currently has 66 construction sites, with 25-30 additional permits already
being processed. He informed the Trustees that his department has established a
set of site maintenance requirements that is given to contractors as well as
neighbors. However, despite the Department’s best efforts, all sites cannot be
monitored on a daily basis, and violations do occur. He said that 48 stop work
orders have been issued this year.
President Duhl inquired whether the Village could raise the penalty fee for
such violations. Attorney Janega indicated that it could.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that the Village might consider hiring an additional
inspector.
President Duhl asked if the Village could raise the $165 penalty for stop work
and Attorney Janega responded that it could probably be increased up to the
statutory maximum of $750.
Mr. Saunders commented on the Village’s policy for addressing drainage issues
at new construction. He explained that the Village’s overall philosophy is to
do no harm, not to expect that last person to work to solve all of the existing
problems. He reported that no one gets a Certificate of Occupancy until the
final drainage plan and full topographic survey at the completion of
construction is approved.
Manager Williams remarked that when the Village holds up a Certificate of
Occupancy, both would-be residents and developers are angered and
inconvenienced.
8. Update on Elm Street Train Station Renovation.
Mr. Woodbury reviewed concept drawings prepared by Metra for the Elm Street
train station renovation. There was a consensus to move forward with the plans
to eliminate the elevators in favor of heated ramps.
9. Executive Session.
None.
10. Manager’s Report.
None.
11. Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:35 p.m.
November 18, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 2, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 18, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Deputy Police Chief Eric Bennett, Police Lieutenant Frank Siwak and three
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that due to several requests from Village residents,
the Manager’s Report will be listed on the agenda before the executive session
at future meetings.
4. Quorum
a)   December 2, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   December 9, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 4, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-40-2003 – Zoning Variation: 854 Eldorado - Adoption.
President Duhl announced that this zoning variation request for intensity of
use of lot, maximum building size, front, corner, and side yard setbacks and
garage variations to permit the construction of an expanded attached garage and
a second floor addition had been introduced at the November 4th meeting.
There being no questions or comments from the audience nor the Council, Trustee
Brower, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-40-2003. By
roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Presser and Woodbury. Nays: Trustee Webster. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-41-2003 – Zoning Variation: 875 Gordon Terrace - Adoption.
President Duhl asked for further comments or questions regarding this request
for front, corner, side, and rear yard setbacks and garage variations to permit
the construction of a 2½ story addition and an attached garage, noting that
this matter had been introduced at the last meeting.
There being no comments or questions, Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee
Greenough, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-41-2003. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-42-2003 – Zoning Variation: 228 Poplar - Adoption.
President Duhl stated that this ordinance would grant front and side yard
setback variations to permit the construction of a detached garage and that
during the discussion at introduction, a question was raised about pedestrian
safety and sight distance in relation to the placement of the garage.
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio said that the Village Engineer
was asked to review the plans and he concluded that the garage as proposed
would cause a sight distance problem. Mr. D’Onofrio said that the Village staff
then surveyed the area and an alternative plan was proposed to the homeowner
that would leave the driveway where it is, but the garage doors would be on the
south elevation instead of the west elevation and would provide better sight
distance. He stated that the staff thinks this is an acceptable alternative
that would allow the two-car garage to be placed in the same location and would
not disturb the oak trees on the property.
Mrs. Caponi, the homeowner, said she had talked with Mr. D’Onofrio, the
architect, and her husband and that they recognize there is a safety issue, but
believe it is because of the fence height, which they will be reducing to 3
feet and not the placement of the garage. She cited four other properties
within three blocks of her property that have 6-foot fences within 2 feet of
the property line: 302 Walnut, 576 Hawthorn, 261 Wilson, and 220 Poplar. She
said that the plan Mr. D’Onofrio proposes is no different than their plan,
except that Mr. D’Onofrio’s proposal cuts out much of their backyard, would
affect one tree, creates more impermeable surface and would make one portion of
the yard unusable. She stated that, although she appreciates the staff’s
looking into alternatives, she feels the proposed change would be unattractive
and would not work for the applicants.
Trustee Abell commented that he is not persuaded that the Council should make
decision based on other homes who may have safety issues as well, and would
like to explore the possibility of a turn around.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that, since there are safety and traffic issues on
other properties in that area so close to New Trier, staff should take these
issues into account before fence permits are issued.
Trustee Greenough said that, as an owner of a formerly dilapidated garage, she
was sympathetic to Mrs. Caponi’s plight and that, when she built a new garage,
she did take a lot of her yard to create a turn around for safety reasons. She
said that reducing the height of the fence in this case doesn’t seem to do much
good because there will be a solid garage wall that would more likely obscure
the driver’s vision.
President Duhl commented that the Council takes the Village Engineer’s opinion
very seriously but, since Mrs. Caponi has new information, the Council may need
to take the matter under further consideration. He tabled the adoption of the
ordinance to give the homeowner and the staff more time to consider both plans
and stated that the Council would make a decision at the next regular meeting,
either to grant the variation or approve the staff’s recommendation.
d)   Ordinance No. M-43-2003 – Zoning Variation: 1422 Edgewood - Adoption.
President Duhl stated that this ordinance, introduced at the November 4th
meeting, would grant a side yard setback variation to permit the expansion of
an existing second floor dormer and asked for comments and questions.
There being no comments, Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to
adopt Ordinance, M-43-2003. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   e)   Ordinance No. M-44-2003 – Zoning Variation: 220 Birch - Adoption.
President Duhl announced that this ordinance would grant a request for a front
yard setback variation to permit enclosing an existing covered porch and was
introduced at the November 4th Council meeting.
There were no comments or questions from the audience or the Trustees.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-44-
2003. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower,
Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
None
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1341 and 1342. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1341 in the amount of $764,498.78 and Warrant
List No. 1342 in the amount of $780,421.70. Trustee Presser seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Abell, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Parking Computers. President Duhl asked Lieutenant Frank Siwak to review
the points of discussion set out at the last study session.
Lieutenant Siwak stated that the Police Department’s current parking ticket
system is old and is no longer supported by the manufacturer. After receiving
bids for a new system, he said that the staff determined that Professional
Parking Solutions would be the best option because it would provide machines,
parking tickets would be kept off site, and booting procedures would be
outsourced.
Manager Williams added that the call centers were checked for response times
and the staff found that the service provided was very good and that the
support people were courteous, answered quickly and gave satisfactory
responses.
Village Attorney Janega said that she has not as yet reviewed the contracts
thoroughly, but there does not appear to be any significant problems. She
recommended that the Council approve the contracts, subject to final approval
by Village Attorney.
Trustee Webster noted some revisions that would be necessary in the contracts
and questioned the length of the contract and cancellation terms.
After further discussion about the length of the contract and collection
procedures, Trustee Presser moved to approve the contracts with Professional
Parking Solutions, subject to the village attorney’s approval of its final
form, seconded by Trustee Abell. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Council Reports.
a)   Trustee Woodbury reported that he and Staff will be meeting this week with
METRA representatives with regard to the design of the Elm Street train
station.
b)   Trustee Brower reported that at the Chamber’s November 11th meeting, it
was reported that 18 stores would be staying open until 8:00 p.m. on the three
Thursday evenings before Christmas and that the Chamber’s ornament of the
Winnetka Library is on sale for $40, of which $25 can be taken as a tax
contribution to help defray the cost of holiday lighting. He also reported that
the Police Department had a seminar for the merchants on shoplifting and
fraudulent checks that was very helpful and that Gwen Trindl had spoken about
the history of the relationship between the Chamber and the community.
11. Manager’s Report.
Manager Williams reported that the Water and Electric Department had created
refrigerator magnets with important telephone numbers that would be going out
to all residents in the utility bills. He also reported that, during the
garbage strike when the Village was picking up recycling, staff had kept track
of Village expenses and Onyx has agreed to reimburse the Village.
12. President’s Report.
President Duhl reported that, at the recent meeting on home rule, Mr. Jim
Banovitz of Northern Illinois University and an expert in the field, spoke to
those in attendance and provided important background information.
13. Appointments.
President Duhl appointed Julie Goodman, the Park District representative to the
Plan Commission, to be the Plan Commission’s representative to the Forestry
Commission, effective May 2003. Trustee Greenough moved to approve the
appointment, seconded by Trustee Abell. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury.
14. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Webster
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:00 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
15. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower,
Greenough, Woodbury, Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:00 p.m.
