Deputy Clerk
December 2, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 16, 2003)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 2, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Finance Director Ed McKee and 14 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   December 9, 2003, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Presser, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   December 16, 2003, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 11, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   November 18, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-42-2003 – Zoning Variation: 228 Poplar - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio briefly reviewed this request for Front and Side Yard Setback
variations to permit the construction of a detached garage. He reminded the
Trustees that at the last Council meeting staff had suggested a plan revision
whereby the garage door would be re-oriented from the east to the south wall to
allow for improved site distance for vehicles existing the garage. At that time
the matter was tabled to permit the applicants time to determine the
feasibility of the garage door reorientation. He reported that since that
meeting, both he and the Village Engineer met with the applicant at which time
an alternative solution was proposed, whereby the garage would be moved from
one foot to the east and one foot to the north. He noted, however, that this
alternative would require the matter to be set for another Public Hearing by
the Zoning Board.
The applicant, Mrs. Caponi, expressed her preference for the latter solution.
Trustee Brower asked her why she objected to Council’s proposal to place the
garage door on the south wall. Mrs. Caponi responded that this would involve
cutting out a large portion of their private yard space.
Trustee Woodbury inquired about the impact on the large tree to the east of the
garage. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that Mr. Saunders believed that the increased
one-foot variation to the east would not unduly harm the tree. Mr. Woodbury
suggested that if the matter is remanded to the Zoning Board again that the
Village Forester evaluate the potential impact.
Trustee Abell remarked that with the alternative solution, the applicants would
still be backing into the street.
Manager Williams reported that the Village’s Traffic Engineer preference was
for the garage door to be on the south wall, but finds the second alternative
acceptable if the applicants remove the existing stockade fence and replace it
with a lower picket fence.
Attorney Janega explained the Council’s options on this matter.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to table the matter and
send it back to the Zoning Board of Appeals with a note that there was a
consensus among the Trustees in favor of the modified solution. By voice vote,
the matter carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-38-2003 – Zoning Variation: 845 Prospect - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a Side Yard Setback variation in order to
permit the construction of a one-story addition. He reminded the Council that
this item had been introduced at the October 7th Council meeting and referred
back to the Zoning Board of Appeals at the October 21st meeting with the
request that the Board identify appropriate findings of practical difficulties
and particular hardships. He reported that upon reconsideration, the Zoning
Board had voted 4-2 to recommend approval of the variation request and more
clearly articulated is findings.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that since the house was constructed in 1993, two factors
have come into play regarding the side yard setback. First, earlier this year,
the setback requirements were changed to have them based on lot width, rather
than zoning district. Second, last year, the petitioner and his neighbor two
lots to the north purchased the lot in between subdividing the lot and
demolishing the house thereon. This resulted in the petitioner’s lot width
increasing from 50 feet to 107 feet, which in turn, now requires a side yard
setback of 12 feet.
The applicant, Jim O’Brien, and his architect, Seymour Turner, reviewed the
existing and proposed floor plans and responded to questions from the Trustees.
Attorney Janega commented that the Zoning Board’s minutes now contain
sufficient information upon which to prepare findings. She noted that although
no particular hardship was determined, a practical difficulty has been
established.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to amend Ordinance, M-38-2003
to reflect the findings of the Zoning Board of Appeals. By voice vote, the
motion carried. Trustee Greenough voted against the motion.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt Ordinance, M-38-
2003 as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Webster,
Presser, Abell, and Brower. Nays: Trustees Greenough and Woodbury. Absent:
None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-47-2003 – Zoning Variation: 592 Cherry - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot and Side and Rear Yard
Setback variations to permit the construction of a detached garage. He reported
that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend approval of the
variations.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-47-
2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-48-2003 – Zoning Variation: 904 Elm St. - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot, Side Yard
Setback, and Building Line Articulation to permit the construction of a room
addition. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to
recommend approval of the variations.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce Ordinance
M-48-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
e)   Ordinance No. M-45-2003 – Tax Levy – Introduction and Truth in Taxation
Hearing. President Duhl opened the Public Hearing on the 2003 Property Tax
Levy. Mr. McKee reviewed the calculations used in determining the levy and
commented that property taxes are always one year behind, meaning that dollars
requested by the 2003 property tax levy will be received by the Village in
March and August 2004. He reported that the Village accounts for only 15% of
the total property tax bill and that the increase in property tax revenue for
the Village is capped at the lesser of 5% or the rate of inflation. Therefore,
given the 2002 CPI of 2.4%, this year’s property taxes will increase only 2.4%,
plus the increment generated from new development. He said that this translates
into an increase of $36 in the Village portion of the tax bill to a homeowner
with a $10,000 property tax bill.
Manager Williams pointed out that it is important to note that the gap between
revenues and expenses continues to widen.
There being no questions from the audience, President Duhl closed the Public
Hearing.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to introduce Ordinance, M-
45-2003. By voice vote, the motion carried.
f)   Ordinance No. M-46-2003 – Tax Abatement - Introduction. Mr. McKee
explained that this ordinance authorized the Village to abate bonds issues for
the construction of the Public Safety Building.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce Ordinance,
M-46. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
None
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1343 and 1344. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1343 in the amount of $981,416.38 and Warrant
List No. 1344 in the amount of $304,116.75. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Abell, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Policy Direction: 530 Sunset. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
Intensity of use of Lot, Front and Corner Yard Setback, and Side Yard Setback
variations to permit the construction of several additions and a detached
garage. He pointed out that the property is a triangularly shaped corner lot
with a large tree in the center of the site plan. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that
due to the nature and number of variation requests, the Zoning Board of Appeals
took four separate votes, ultimately taking no position on the Side Yard
Setback variation and recommending denial of the Front Setback variation. He
noted that following the Zoning Board’s action, the petitioner revised the
building plans in an attempt to address some of the concerns raised. As a
result of these changes, only the Front Setback variation for the addition
along the east side of the house and the Side Yard Setback variations are now
required.
Trustee Webster confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the plans being presented to
the Council are different than those shown to the Zoning Board of Appeals. He
remarked that this being the case, he was uncomfortable making a decision
without their review and suggested that he was inclined to remand to matter
back to the Zoning Board for reconsideration.
The applicant’s architect, Gary Beyerl, showed site plans and elevations to
demonstrate the difficulties associated with improving this property. He
explained that the applicant is not trying to maximize the site but needs to
make it a reasonable home by today’s standards.
Jim Kruempelstaedter, 178 Myrtle, voiced objection to the proposal, citing
concerns about impermeable surface and increased flooding. He asked the Council
to deny the variation request.
Manager Williams referred the Trustees to the zoning matrix that indicates that
the applicant is not proposing to increase the impermeable surface above that
already allowed by the Zoning Ordinance. He also pointed out that the Village
requires elaborate calculations to ensure that construction causes no harm to
neighbors.
Mark Kennedy, 529 Sunset Road, expressed support for the plans to expand to the
west because it preserves the look and feel of Sunset Road and saves the large
tree.
Christine , 525 Sunset, agreed with Mr. Kennedy.
Jennifer Fife, the applicant, spoke in support of their request.
Trustee Presser remarked that while normally the Council would send this matter
back to the Zoning Board, the changes seem to him to be minimal and that,
therefore, he favors approval without remanding the matter to the Zoning Board.
Trustee Greenough concurred with Mr. Presser as did Trustees Woodbury, Abell,
and Brower. Trustee Woodbury, however, suggested that in the future matters
that are substantially revised after going before the Zoning Board, return to
the Zoning Board for reconsideration before being presented to the Village
Council.
Trustee Webster supported the consensus, but agreed with Mr. Woodbury’s
suggestion that matters such as these be returned to the Zoning Board before
coming to the Council.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to direct the Village
Attorney to draft an ordinance granting the requested variations. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Disposition of Cable TV Studio Entry Way & Parking Spaces. Manager
Williams reported that with the closure of the local cable television studio,
the owner of the property in which the studio was located and the property
adjacent to the entry way has raised a question about the disposition of the
entry way and the parking places leased to the cable studio.
Cliff Zimmerman, the owner’s representative, expressed hope that the owner and
the Village could continue the arrangement whereby the entry way is maintained
as landscaped property and the six parking spaces remain with the site. He also
voiced some concern about access to the property should the Village ever decide
to vacate its neighboring property as the landscaped strip would be the only
access to Tower Road.
Manager Williams pointed out that to continue the existing relationship would
raise two major policy questions: (1) should the Village start a precedent of
leasing public parking spaces to private entities, and (2) is this a fair
exchange of value. He noted that heretofore the Village has only entered into
agreements for parking spaces with three entities, two high schools and the
cable company, a public utility, all of which serve a public purpose and that
the approximate cost of a parking space in Winnetka is $30,000. He suggested
that it might be better to designate the spaces as 90 minutes parking for use
by all of the neighboring business customers. He also suggested that the
Village take over the care and maintenance of the landscaped strip because it
presents a nice buffer between the gas station and the public parking area.
President Duhl noted that as a matter of policy, once these spaces come back
under Village control, they become just like other public spaces and it is up
to the Village to decide the best use therefor. He remarked that the parking
issue is the first concern of the business district and that designating places
for private use would be very sensitive and set a precedent.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees agreed that they were opposed to
dedicating public property for private use.
President Duhl suggested that Mr. Zimmerman speak with the Village Manager
about the best designation for the public parking spaces and work out an
arrangement to retain the landscaped strip and access to his client’s property.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Woodbury reported that he and several staff members had
met with Metra regarding the renovation of the Elm Street station. He noted
that initial drawings are expected from Metra’s architects by the end of
January.
Trustee Presser remarked that the next edition of the Winnetka Report would be
mailed to residents soon.
b)   Village Manager. Manager Williams commented that staff continues to work
with IMEA on assessing the feasibility of acquiring a percentage of the TC2, a
new power plant. He reported that after doing due diligence, staff believes
that it makes sense to move forward with the project as it is the least costly
alternative available. The item will be slated for discussion at the January
study session.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:16 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower,
Greenough, Woodbury, Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 10:17 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
December 9, 2003
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: January 6, 2004)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, David Webster, and Trustee Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee Stephen Presser. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus and 8
person in the audience.
2. Ad Hoc Business Development Committee Report.
Barbara Aquilino, chairperson of the committee, summarized her report to the
Council on the Ad Hoc Business Development Committee whose assignment was to
determine if there should be a permanent committee to deal with business issues
in the community and if so, how the committee should proceed in time for the
budgeting process. She said the members reviewed numerous reports on business
concerns from the 1970s forward and learned that although progress had been
made, there had been no follow up on the reports. The committee concluded that
a commission should be formed to deal with ongoing concerns in the business
district and to provide accountability and continuity.
The Council and committee members discussed the creation of the commission, its
authority and the possibility of funding for the projects listed in the
committee’s report.
President Duhl clarified that the commission would not have any authority other
than to make recommendations to the Council.
Trustee Webster also noted that it is an important distinction that the body
would have the scope only to examine, to confer, and to consult and that
creation of the commission would not necessarily mean that the Council is
approving or endorsing the specific projects listed in the committee’s report.
He suggested the committee look into properties only with approval of the
Council.
Trustee Greenough said that all of the issues are extremely important and the
commission would have the time to focus on projects to provide assistance to
the Council, the staff and the Plan Commission. She also said that if the
commission were only to keep better statistics, it would be helpful in making
decisions about the business community.
Trustee Woodbury said that the best description of this commission is that it
is a plan commission with a special focus. He believed that what the commission
would do is to try to enhance the asset base that we have, with the end result
being sales tax dollars for the Village.
President Duhl asked for comments from the audience and there were none. He
then asked for a consensus of the Council to create the commission. The
Trustees then indicated that the commission should be formed.
Village Attorney Janega was directed to draft an ordinance establishing the
commission and defining the scope of its authority.
3.   Limitations on Impermeable Surfaces.
   Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus reviewed the most
recent revisions to the amendments to the Village Code concerning curb cuts
that require properties served by an alley to use the alley instead of a
driveway. He said that as a result of his discussions with Village Engineer
Steve Saunders, a safety valve had been added for those properties on alleys
that would not do well if forced to use the alley, such as the homes on Willow
Road around Crow Island. He also said the revisions now codify some existing
Public Works policies and standards, such as the requirement that before two
driveways are constructed on a single lot, a property must have a 75-foot lot
width.
   The Trustees commented on circular driveways for corner lots, and whether to
include the R-1 and R-2 zoning districts in regulations for the percentage of
impermeable surface allowed. Mr. Norkus provided the Council with drawings of
properties in these districts to illustrate the amount of impermeable surface
that has been used while maintaining the required setbacks. He did not
recommend applying a percentage in these districts.
Village Attorney Janega agreed with Mr. Norkus, saying that there may be
unintended consequences if people begin to see the limitation as an
entitlement.
   President Duhl also expressed his belief that this does not pertain to the
R-1 and R-2 zoning districts. He instructed Ms. Janega to draft an ordinance
amending the impermeable surface provisions of the Zoning Code as discussed.
4. Zoning Amendments.
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio reported that after conducting
an analysis on the properties whose addresses were submitted by Trustee
Woodbury, the staff had concluded that the attic allowance drives the pitch of
the roof. He met with property owners and architects of some of the properties,
who confirmed that the pitch of the roof was determined so that the first and
second floors of the home could have the most advantage from the square footage
allowed.
President Duhl said that it seems clear to him that people will want to make as
much space habitable in their houses as possible and if we want steeper pitched
roofs incorporated into the design, then there will be more bulk. He suggested
that perhaps what should be done is to limit the maximum height, require a
certain roof pitch and not count the attic space.
Trustee Woodbury said that the changes resulting from discussions about attic
allowances three years ago were meant to permit people to finish existing attic
space. He did not agree with staff’s recommendation to do nothing until design
review is in place and asked that they prove to him that something can be done.
He suggested raising the roof height two feet and eliminating the attic
allowance.
Gary Frank, Architect, Spruce Street, said that if there is no restriction for
floor area, all houses will become gabled, but with restrictions, hips will be
put on to reduce the square footage. He said he believes that increasing the
percentage allowed will create gables rather than hip roofs.
Manager Williams reported that staff’s viewpoint is that there would still be a
reasonable amount of buildable area and that the people want the ceiling
heights on the first and second floors instead of good design.
Mr. Norkus commented that he’s not sure it is the height limitation that is the
problem, but that it is the attic allowance. He said that some houses have been
well designed under these regulations.
Trustee Greenough illustrated a roof height with a 3% allowance and said that
the only way she thought builders could accomplish that would be to create one-
story wings on either side.
Manager Williams said that the only way to require the types of design they are
talking about is to have design review.
Mr. Frank said that in his opinion, the 3% allowance would not go to roof
pitch, but to habitable space. He made further comments about what could be
built with a 3% allowance. He said that the smaller lots may benefit from a
greater allowance because they can build up but not too far out.
President Duhl asked Mr. Norkus for his recommendation.
Mr. Norkus said that he favors incrementalism and that most of the problem is
with the attic allowance.
President Duhl asked for a consensus and the trustees agreed to increase the
attic allowance to 3% and keep the maximum height requirements the same.
5. Building Line Articulation.  
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio said that staff has found that
the building line articulation regulations adopted in 1999 have triggered the
need for variation requests for additions to existing homes. He said that one
of the problems is how articulation is measured. He listed the staff’s
recommendations for possible solutions as follows: 1) simplify the language in
the Village Code to deal with articulation as a setback issue; 2) expand the
means by which existing homes could provide articulation other than bump ins or
bump outs; and 3) eliminate the articulation requirements for existing single-
family residences.
The Trustees commented on the number of variations that had been requested and
agreed that there is better construction and design with articulation. Further
discussion was held on creating one standard for existing homes and another for
new construction, but comments were that it was appropriate for both.
Mr. Frank suggested that the Council consider amending the requirement to
increase the 30 foot figure to 33 or 35 feet. He said that when two rooms
piggyback to one another on a 50-foot lot, each room would be 14 feet long
under the 30-foot measurement. He further stated that it is difficult to
furnish rooms of this size and by increasing the requirement to 33 or 35 feet,
it would make the rooms more liveable. Mr. Frank also requested that the
Council consider how GFA is calculated for front porches with open decks above
and also the size of area wells for egress. He advocated 2’9” which would
conform with the building code.
After discussing Mr. Frank’s suggestion for the measurement for articulation,
the Council agreed that 35 feet would be a better figure to use.
Manager Williams noted that the Council had previously decided to change the
GFA calculations for porches with open decks above and that the change will be
in the more comprehensive amendments that will be considered in February. He
said that there would be an opportunity to review Mr. Frank’s other suggestions
at the Council’s January study session.
President Duhl asked for a consensus from the Council on building line
articulation.
Attorney Janega suggested that a middle of the road position would be to keep
the articulation requirements in the Code, but allow the Zoning Board to grant
these variations.
President Duhl replied that because each case can be interpreted differently,
he is not sure it is worth the administrative effort of staff to go through the
process even with the ZBA granting the variations.
Trustee Woodbury said that his suggestion would be to move the decisions on the
variation requests to the ZBA, but at the same time find more ways for people
to comply by clarifying the requirements.
As there were no further comments, Manager Williams said that he would put this
item on the agenda for discussion in January.
6. Streamlining zoning ordinance.
President Duhl announced that Attorney Janega had drafted and distributed an
ordinance for the Council’s review, but there would be no discussion until the
Council’s next study session in January. Attorney Janega briefly described the
draft ordinance and directed Council’s attention to several specific sections
for their review.
7. Manager’s report.
Manager Williams reported to the Council on the following items:
a)   A child had been transported to the hospital by the Fire Department
ambulance and subsequently died from the flu. Information about the flu virus
had been placed on the Village’s web site.
b)   A female body, identified as a woman from Barrington Hills, had been found
on one of the Winnetka beaches and the Police Department had called in NORTAF
to investigate.
c)   The new wholesale water contract with Northfield will be voted on for
approval by Northfield officials tomorrow evening.
d)   WYO has submitted an invoice for the fall planting project, but the
project has not been completed.
The meeting adjourned at 10:00 pm.
Respectfully submitted
______________________
Deputy Clerk
December 16, 2003
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 6, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 16, 2003 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, Finance Director Ed McKee and 8 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   January 6, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   January 13, 2004, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 2, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Brower seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Village Auditor’s Report.
Irwin Lyons, of Miller Cooper & Co., Ltd., the Village’s auditor, reported to
the Council on the results of their audit of the financial statements of the
Village for the year ending March 31, 2003. Mr. Lyons made several
recommendations regarding capital assets, the conversion to the GASB34
accounting method and reconciliation to the general ledger. He also reviewed
the management letter, noting the auditor’s responsibility under auditing
standards, the impact of the adoption of the GASB 34 accounting standard,
unusual transactions, accounting estimates, and audit adjustments as required
under the generally accepted accounting standards.
Mr. Lyons pointed out that, by its nature, the management letter is negative in
tone and that the points in this year’s letter are relatively minor and show
progress on the points raised last year He also stressed that the audit opinion
is unqualified.
Trustee Brower commented that it seems the most significant event this year was
the conversion to GASB34 and asked Mr. Lyons if the conversion went smoothly.
Mr. Lyons replied that it did go smoothly and that there is a lot of work that
goes into it that doesn’t show anywhere. He said it took seven years to get to
this point.
Ed McKee, Village Finance Director, mentioned that the Village has an option
with Miller Cooper to conduct the audit this year and next year, and Council
will be deciding whether to exercise that option. He said that secondly, one of
the things that the GASB34 accounting standard is trying to identify is
communities that do not invest in capital. He also said that the Village has a
strong history of good investments and showing that assets are written off over
their useful life. He concluded by saying that the Village looks very good
under the new reporting model.
President Duhl asked if there were any comments from the audience. There were
none.
Manager Williams commented on the utilities’ funds, pension liability (noting
in particular the enhancements made by State legislators to the firefighter’s
pension that have been challenging to fund), the reduction in capital
improvements after September 11, 2001, an increase in building permit revenues
and figures for the past 10 years on property tax revenue. He said that Mr.
McKee recommends transferring funds to the workers’ compensation account this
next year and also noted that although health insurance costs are going up,
this account looks good due to the efforts of the staff and the employees’
health insurance committee.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-47-2003 – Zoning Variation: 592 Cherry - Adoption.
President Duhl announced that this request for variation from the Intensity of
Use of Lot and setback provisions of the Zoning Ordinance was introduced at the
Council’s December 2nd meeting. He asked for comments or questions from the
audience and the Trustees.
There were no questions for the applicant and Trustee Greenough moved to adopt
Ordinance M-47-2003 granting intensity of use of lot and setback variations for
the residence at 592 Cherry, seconded by Trustee Brower. By roll call vote, the
motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser,
Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-48-2003 – Zoning Variation: 904 Elm - Adoption.
President Duhl reported that this request for variation from the Intensity of
Use of Lot, Building Line Articulation and Setback provisions of the Zoning
Ordinance was introduced at the Council’s December 2nd meeting. There were no
questions from either the audience or the Trustees, and there was no further
discussion.
Trustee Abell moved to adopt Ordinance M-48-2003 granting intensity of use of
lot and setback variations for the residence at 904 Elm Street, seconded by
Trustee Presser. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-45-2003 – Tax Levy - Adoption.
President Duhl asked for comments or questions before adopting the 2003 tax
levy ordinance, which had been introduced at the December 2nd meeting after a
public hearing had been held.
There being no further discussion, Trustee Presser moved to adopt Ordinance No.
M-45-2003 approving the 2003 tax levy, seconded by Trustee Abell. By roll call
vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustee Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Webster and Woodbury.
d)   Ordinance No. M-46-2003 – Tax Abatement - Adoption.
President Duhl asked for comments and questions before adopting this ordinance
which abates the taxes on the General Obligation Refunding Bonds, Series 2003,
for the construction of the Public Safety Building.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-46-2003, seconded by Trustee
Brower. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-49-2003 – Zoning Variation: 530 Sunset - Introduction.
President Duhl stated that this ordinance would grant variations from the side
yard and front yard setback provisions of the Zoning Ordinance and had been
discussed at length at the Council’s last meeting. He asked for comments from
the audience.
Mr. James Krumpelstaedter, 178 Myrtle, said that he had written a letter to the
Board expressing his concern that this ordinance may be approved over a 5-
1 denial by the Zoning Board. He was also concerned that the Council may be
setting precedent by permitting new construction in the setback. He believes
that would directly affect him because neighboring property has been subdivided
and previous owners have submitted plans to build a house. He said that those
plans were denied, but future plans may not.  
President Duhl replied that the plans have been revised since the Zoning Board
meeting and he does not believe that granting this variation request would
create the precedent that Mr. Krumpelstaedter perceives.
Trustee Presser also assured Mr. Krumpelstaedter that each of the zoning
variation requests is considered on a case by case basis, and that the Council
takes the concerns and comments of the neighbors very seriously.
Trustee Woodbury said that, in answer to Mr. Krumpelstaedter’s question about
how the Council could override a decision of the ZBA, he clearly remembers that
the plans were not articulated with prescribed setbacks and the house could go
further to the north, but the applicant has chosen to stay at the side of the
lot to preserve the setback. He said that new evidence is what persuades him to
reconsider and after driving by the lot several times, he believes it has a
truly unique circumstance.
Trustee Brower said he shares Trustee Woodbury’s view and that the applicants
have been meticulous about maintaining street front. He said he would support
the request for variation.
Trustee Greenough added that the Council is able to consider things that the
Zoning Board can’t, such as that the house was built in 1926 and the importance
of retaining an old tree in the back yard. She also said that the addition is
on the west side of the lot and so does not affect any neighbors, which was
another consideration in this case.
Trustee Abell agreed.
President Duhl asked if there were any further comments or questions and there
were none.
Trustee Woodbury moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-48-2003, seconded by
Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Resolution No. R-32-2003 – CBI Subdivision – 630 Pine Lane – Preliminary
Approval – Adoption. Assistant Director of Community Development, Brian Norkus,
reviewed the proposed subdivision of 630 Pine Lane into 3 lots, one of which
would be a flag lot. He reported that the Plan Commission had recommended
approval of the subdivision conditioned upon retaining the existing home which
was built in 1922 and designed by Edwin H. Clark. He said that the discussion
at the Plan Commission meeting also led to concerns about storm water
management, tree preservation and the use of directional boring rather than
trench construction to minimize tree damage. Mr. Norkus discussed the removal
of trees due to the installation of utilities and compensatory storage as
depicted on the preliminary plans. He said that there is no alternative for the
placement of the water line which will also allow Water & Electric to loop an
existing dead end line through to other lines. He then summarized the easements
required for utilities and how the flag lot would be accessed.
Manager Williams said that in the past he had received comments from the
community about people using fill to get the correct elevation which
consequently affects stormwater drainage. He informed the Council that the
Director of Public Works, Steve Saunders, said that Lot 3 of this subdivision
would need about 4 feet of fill to raise it above the base floodplain
elevation.
The Council discussed the issues involving stormwater detention, tree removal
and preservation, landmark designation for the existing home, floodplain
requirements, and required easements.
Barbara Barrett, representing CBI homes, stated that CBI custom homes has done
other work in the Village and won the Glencoe historical society award last
year for constructing a new home in a very old neighborhood and causing it to
fit in with the neighborhood. She said CBI has also won an award this year as
well. She added that this developer recognizes the beautiful site of this
subdivision. She asked Mr. Todd Thorhom, the engineer on the project, to
address the issue of fill.
Mr. Thorhom said that two utilities must be installed along the north side of
the property that require 10-foot easements. He said that the sewer line cannot
be moved, but directional boring will preserve many of the trees that are shown
on the preliminary plans to be removed. He explained that the high water level
shown on the plat of the property is for detention only and the actual
floodplain elevation is 2 feet below this, so 3.5 feet of fill will be
necessary to observe the floodplain elevation requirement.
Ms. Barrett said that it had been determined that the original house could be
saved.
Manager Williams asked if the possibility of a two lot subdivision had been
considered instead of a 3-lot subdivision.
Mr. James Marlow, principal of CBI, replied that a 4-lot subdivision had been
considered, but not just two lots.
Ms. Barrett said that this subdivision as proposed complies with every
provision of the zoning ordinance. She said that Mr. Marlow is willing to agree
to the requirements that Public Works has requested and will preserve the house
that is there.
Trustee Woodbury said that her comments are well advocated but there are
substantial issues.
President Duhl said that it may comply with zoning, but a subdivision has to be
created first, which is under the Council’s jurisdiction.
Mr. Terry Westbrook, 1160 Pelham Road, speaking on behalf of a neighbor as
well, said he had spent many hours with the Plan Commission and said the
neighbors, as a group, believe that this subdivision is wrong and bad for the
community. He referred to a letter written to the Council by Mr. Andy Block,
whose position he supports, and emphasized that it is not economics, but a
quality of life issue. He asked the Trustees to view it in that manner and
hoped the Council would give it further deliberations.
President Duhl replied that the Council confronts this issue frequently and the
fact is that the lot belongs to whoever owns it now. He stated that Mr.
Westbrook would have to recognize that although he has lived next to this
property for a long period of time, it doesn’t mean that the owner can’t do
certain things.
Trustee Webster asked if Mr. Westbrook had alternatives.
Mr. Westbrook said he supports what is in Mr. Block’s letter, suggesting that
the existing residence and approach be left intact and the rear of the property
be developed. He also said that the neighbors have all put in their own systems
to take care of the water and they believe that the subdivision will cause more
problems. He believes he will have more water and will probably end up in
litigation over this. He said the neighbors do not believe the situation will
improve and are fully prepared to deal with it.
Ms. Barrett replied that building at the rear of the property and leaving the
existing home intact was not possible. She answered the neighbors’ concerns
about water in the area by saying that the advantage of the new system is that
it will probably help the neighbors because the compensatory storage will
improve conditions.
Mr. Thorhom said that the more fill there is, the more compensatory storage
would be needed which would have to be behind the existing home if there is
only one house up front and one in the rear.
Ms. Barrett said that the Village has strict engineering requirements for storm
water sewers and compensatory storage and that Mr. Marlow and CBI Homes intend
to comply. She said that CBI has attempted to provide the Council with far more
detail than is usual at this stage of the development which shows that Mr.
Marlow is a fine developer and will comply with all of the requirements to make
it work.
Manager Williams asked if they were aware of the various species of trees shown
for removal because he knows that a few are unique.
Mr. Marlow replied that he will be going over the trees with Mr. Stier to see
which trees are the most important to preserve and he will preserve as many as
possible.
President Duhl commented that Council has three options, the first is simply to
adopt the resolution granting preliminary approval, the second is to deny the
resolution and the third is to table the resolution for further study. He
suggested reasons for tabling the resolution are the creation of a new flag
lot, significant dissatisfaction with the fill and flood issues, the benefit of
looping utilities and the detriment of cutting down trees. President Duhl also
said that he thought the Council owed it to the neighbors to understand whether
this will create additional pressures on flooding and how the compensatory
storage addresses that issue.
Trustee Woodbury said he is also in favor of tabling the discussion until
February and asked that the applicant show how directional boring would save
the majority of trees, noting that the current plan shows 65 trees for removal,
which is significant. He asked that staff look at the stormwater issue,
compensatory storage and the extension of grading which is unacceptable to him.
He said another thing significant to address is the growing dissatisfaction
with the floodplain ordinance.
Trustee Presser said that if this project were to be completed and the
developers did the best job, he would like them to focus on how they can
improve the neighborhood.
Trustee Brower said that he agreed with what had been said so far.
Trustee Webster said that he doesn’t know if he agrees since this is the first
case of subdivision he has been in on. He asked Attorney Janega if owners’
property rights would be taken away if subdivision was denied.
Ms. Janega replied that she had researched Village cases where subdivisions had
met all mathematical requirements, but had not been permitted. She said that
subdivisions are not merely mathematical zoning formulas, but the full
engineering impact, the impact of the Zoning Ordinance and the impact on
existing trees has to be considered. She said not every lot in a floodplain is
suitable for development, but an engineering staff can find ways to make it
work from an engineering standpoint, although not necessarily from other
viewpoints. She said the Council has to consider the development, the
community, consistency with the Zoning Ordinance, compliance with tree
regulations, and floodplain management as a whole package. She recommended that
the resolution be tabled because of the many questions raised and so that she
can prepare a detailed memorandum that would provide guidance for the Council.
Trustee Presser moved to table the discussion to the February study session,
seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
f)   Resolution No. R-33-2003 – Northfield Wholesale Water Contract – Adoption.
Manager Williams said that in March 1929, the Village entered into an agreement
with Northfield to provide water and has done that for the last 80 years. He
was pleased to announce that another 30-year agreement had been reached for
Northfield to purchase all of their water from Winnetka, retroactive for 2003.
He said that the Village has also agreed to install two capital improvements to
Northfield, a third interconnection and a connection to Wilmette through
Winnetka’s system which he believes is good planning for the continuation of
service in the case of a catastrophic event. He recommended approval of the
resolution and contract.
Trustee Abell moved to adopt Resolution R-33-2003, approving the Northfield
Wholesale Water Contract, seconded by Trustee Brower. By roll call vote, the
motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser,
Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1345 and 1346. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1345 in the amount of $642,287.68 and Warrant
List No. 1346 in the amount of $920,943.97. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Abell, Presser, Woodbury, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Bid: Radios.
Manager Williams reported that one of this year’s budget items was to replace
the Water & Electric Department radios in order to improve communications in
the field. He said that the current radios can not be heard in certain areas
and it is critical that lineman in the field have good communication. He said
that the current Nextel phones are very expensive to use and he recommends
phasing them out and moving forward with replacement radios. He also said that
the power plant antenna must be replaced.
Trustee Presser moved to approve the purchase of replacement radios for the
Water and Electric Department, seconded by Trustee Abell. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Change Order: Street Construction.
Manager Williams reported that the budget allowed the addition of Birch Street
to the street construction project, and consequently a change order is
necessary. He said that the original estimate for the number of units required
was also less than actually needed, but the project is still under budget.
Trustee Brower moved to approve the change order, seconded by Trustee Woodbury.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower,
Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
11. Reports.
a)   Trustees.
Trustee Brower reported that the Chamber of Commerce met and reviewed the
results of the holiday sale, which was outstanding. He said that they had also
discussed the meeting between the Ad Hoc Business Committee and the Village
Council and the possibility of a “before hours” meeting with the Village
Manager and Village staff, which might be held semi-annually.
Trustee Abell reported that the Caucus had approved a platform.
b)   Village Manager.
Manager Williams announced that there will be three budget study sessions in
February, the 19th, the 24th and 26th at 7:30 p.m. He also asked the Council to
keep January 22 open in case the January study session discussion on zoning is
not finished and another meeting is necessary.
12. Appointments.
None.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:32 p.m.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower,
Greenough, Woodbury, Presser, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:46 p.m.
