Deputy Clerk
FINDINGS AND CONCLUSIONS
OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
ON THE APPLICATION OF NATIONAL CITY BANK
FOR A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO OPERATE A BANK AT 952 GREEN BAY ROAD
   The Council of the Village of Winnetka, having reviewed and considered the
records of the Zoning Board of Appeals, the Plan Commission and the Design
Review Board on the pending Application, having reviewed and considered the
legal memorandum submitted by Applicant’s attorney, and having heard and
considered the presentations and legal arguments made by and on behalf of the
Applicant and by and on behalf of several persons who oppose the Application,
and being otherwise fully advised, enters the following Findings of Fact and
Conclusions:
I.   FINDINGS OF FACT
   A.   Nature of Application
      1.   This matter involves the application of James Demos and National
City Bank of Michigan/Illinois (“Applicant’), dated August 1, 2003, for a
special use permit to allow it to occupy the northern half of the commercial
space at 952 Green Bay Road.
      2.   The Applicant proposes the subject premises to be used for a full
service bank, which would include lending, deposits, individual and business
accounts, trust services, services to private clients and selling investments.
The original application included a request for an ATM machine to be located on
the east building wall, which is not set back from the public right-of-way.
      3.   The Subject Property is owned by Patricia Halekias and James Denos.
   B.   Description of Applicant
      4.   The applicant is National City Bank of Michigan/Illinois, acting
through its agent, GPD Group of Akron, Ohio.
      5.   National City Bank is a proposed lessee of the northern half of the
commercial space at 952 Green Bay Road.
      6.   National City Bank proposes to remodel the northern half of the
commercial space at 952 Green Bay Road and operate it as a branch bank with an
ATM, but no drive-through service.
      7.   National City Bank is primarily located in Lake County, DuPage
County and Will County.
      8.   The Applicant has a branch bank on Lake Cook Road in Buffalo Grove,
Illinois, and at 921 W. Armitage Avenue in the City of Chicago, and is
attempting to connect a network of sites from Lake County through the Loop.
   C.   Description of Subject Property
      9.   The Subject Property is commonly known as 952 Green Bay Road,
Winnetka, IL, and is legally described as follows:
         Lot 21 (except the Westerly 16 feet) in Block 3 in Jared Gage’s
Subdivision of part of the East ½ of the Northwest ¼ of Fractional Section 17,
also part of the West ½ of the Northwest ¼ of Fractional Section 17 and part of
the East ½ of the Southwest ¼ of Fractional Section 8, Township 42 North, Range
13, East of the Third Principal Meridian in the Village of Winnetka, Cook
County, Illinois.
      10.   The Subject Property is located in the C-2 General Retail
Commercial Zoning District.
      11.   The Subject Property is also located within the C-2 Retail Overlay
District, which requires most non-retail uses to obtain a special use permit
from the Village if they wish to be located on the ground floor.
      12.   The Subject Property is 50 feet wide and 134 feet deep.
      13.   The Subject Property is improved with a brick and stone building
with a main entrance approximately in the center of the building, from Green
Bay Road
      14.   The building on the Subject Property was built in 1926 and was
originally a bank.
      15.   Village records disclose that the building on the Subject Property
has been used continuously for retail commercial purposes for at least 25
years.
      16.   The building on the Subject Property is divided into two spaces.
The south space has a ground floor area of 2,400 square feet, which has been
and continues to be occupied by the owner’s business, Joliet Kitchen and Bath.
The north space, which National City Bank seeks to use for its branch bank
operations, consists of 2,400 square feet on the ground floor and 690 square
feet in the mezzanine. The north space had been occupied by a series of retail
commercial tenants, but is currently empty.
   D.   Description of Immediate Vicinity
      17.   The Subject Property is located on the west side of Green Bay Road,
on the block face between Gage Street and Merrill Street.
      18.   The Subject Property is approximately in the middle of the middle
block of the three blocks in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Zoning District
that extend from Tower Road on the south to Scott Avenue on the north.
      19.   The block face on the east side of Green Bay Road between Gage
Street and Merrill Street is a public park known as Hubbard Woods Park.
      20.   Directly to the east of Hubbard Woods Park is the Hubbard Woods
train station for the Metra/Union Pacific North Line Commuter trains.
      21.   The block face on which the Subject Property is located, between
Gage Street and Merrill Street, is 400 feet long.
      22.   The following uses are located on the first floor of the other
buildings located between Gage Street and Merrill on the west side of Green Bay
Road:
         a.   940 Green Bay Road – Panera Bread (Northwest corner of Green Bay
and Gage) – restaurant and bakery;
         b.   946 Green Bay Road – Hubbard Woods Travel – travel agency;
         c.   948 Green Bay Road – Winnetka Fitness – retail sales of exercise
equipment;
         d.   950 Green Bay Road (south space) – From Afar – retail sales of
oriental furnishings and accessories; recently converted from medical offices
to retail space;
         e.   950 Green Bay Road (north space) – Vacant – recently converted
from medical offices to retail space;
         f.   952 Green Bay Road (south space of Subject Property) – Joliet
Kitchen & Bath – kitchen and bath design and furnishings, currently under
renovation, to reopen as Hubbard Woods Kitchen & Bath;
         g.   952 Green Bay Road (north space of Subject Property) – Vacant –
proposed space for National City Bank;
         h.   954 – 956 – 958 Green Bay Road – Material Possessions – retail
sales of home furnishings and accessories;
         i.   960 Green Bay Road – Anthony Andrews – hair salon;
         j.   962 Green Bay Road – Randoon’s – children’s apparel;
         k.   964 Green Bay Road – Monckton Gallery – picture framing, retail
sales of antique prints;
         l.   964 ½ Green Bay Road – Bicks – small gift and antique items;
         m.   966 Green Bay Road – nonconforming single-family house set back
from Green Bay Road, with recently vacated M & L hot dog stand in the front, at
the southeast corner, near the sidewalk;
         n.   970 Green Bay Road – Salon 970 and Day Spa – hair salon and day
spa; and
         o.   972 Green Bay Road (Southwest corner of Green Bay and Merrill) –
Boris’ Cafe.
      23.   There are three banks in close proximity to the Subject Property:
         a.   the Hubbard Woods branch of Harris Bank, consisting of offices,
ATM and drive-through, is located at the southwest corner of Green Bay Road and
Gage Street, approximately 216 feet south of the Subject Property;
         b.   the Private Bank, with offices, ATM and on-site parking in front,
located at the southwest corner of Scott Avenue and Green Bay Road,
approximately 516 feet north of the Subject Property; and
         c.   Bank of America, with offices, ATM and a front drive for
passenger drop-off, at the northwest corner of Scott Avenue and Green Bay Road,
approximately 66 feet north of the Private Bank.
      24.   The Harris Bank is located within the C-2 Zoning Overlay District,
although its occupation of the southwest corner of Gage Street and Green Bay
Road pre-dates the enactment of the C-2 Overlay District requirements.
      25.   The Private Bank is located at the north edge of the C-2 Overlay
District. It is the second bank to occupy that site, pursuant to a special use
permit that was granted in 1993.
      26.   The Bank of America is located partially in Winnetka and partially
in Glencoe and is just outside of the C-2 Zoning Overlay District.
   E.   Procedural Background and Evidentiary Record
      27.   On September 18, 2003, at a regular meeting, the Winnetka Design
Review Board considered the building alterations associated with the requested
special use. Mike Morrison of GPD Associates presented the case to the Design
Review Board. The Design Review Board provided the following comments on the
proposed building changes:
         a.   the front elevation is acceptable as presented, with the
exception of the proposed ATM;
         b.   the rear elevation is acceptable as presented, but, in the event
the bank uses the rear door as an active entrance to the bank, consideration
should be given towards making the rear of the building more attractive and
pedestrian-friendly with signage;
         c.   the ATM should be located inside of the vestibule, as is done in
other banks in the Village;
         d.   if the ATM is allowed in the front of the building, the building
would be left with a hole that cannot be matched in the event the bank leaves;
      28.   Although the bank's representative stated that they are proposing
to take down the existing limestone and store it in the event the bank leaves
the building, members of the Design Review Board expressed a preference to see
the ATM located somewhere else and that cutting a hole in the limestone façade
of the building would set a bad precedent.
      29.   On October 22, 2003, at a regular meeting, the Winnetka Plan
Commission considered the special use permit application for consistency with
the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020.
      30.   Jim Nolte, of GPD and Associates, presented the case to the Plan
Commission on behalf of the applicant. Mr. Nolte described the proposed new
banking facility, and noted that the building once housed the Hubbard Woods
Trust & Savings Bank. He stated that the bank would provide retail banking
services and small business banking services and stated that the proposal meets
the goals, objectives and vision of the Comprehensive Plan as it pertains to
pedestrian-oriented retail facilities.
      31.   The applicant was also represented by Ann Lazars, a representative
of the bank, and Jim Denos, the owner of the property. Mr. Denos was in
attendance with his associate.
      32.   Mr. Nolte stated that the nearest National City Bank location is
approximately ten miles away.
      33.   Ms. Lazars stated the bank has branches in Buffalo Grove and
Bannockburn, that they are working on additional branches in Glenview and
Highland Park, and that the Glenview Board had approved the Glenview branch.
      34.   In response to questions from the Plan Commission, Village Planner
Brian Norkus stated that the property immediately to the south of the Subject
Property was similar, in that it is also in the C-2 overlay district and it had
at one time been used for non-retail uses.
      35.   According to Village records, the building immediately to the south
of the Subject Property, 950 Green Bay Road, was built in 1954 and expanded in
the 1960's, before the commercial overlay was added to the C-2 General Retail
Zoning District, and has recently been renovated and converted to retail space.
      36.   Mr. Nolte stated that the bank is new to the Chicago area and that
it would be attracting new customers to the business district who were
previously not exposed to the district. He described the bank as a leader in
the industry offering SBA loans and the Women’s Entrepreneurial Loan Program
and asserted that the bank would be a great community partner.
      37.   At the Plan Commission meting, one of the members of the
Commission, Robert Horne, commented that the Economic Development Committee
discussed the success of the Hubbard Woods commercial district and the synergy
created by the home furnishings and antiques businesses tenant mix. He added
that it also created a destination, which is attracting people from a wider
geographic area.
      38.   The Plan Commission found the proposed special use to be consistent
with the following goals and objectives of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan:
         a.   to limit commercial, institutional, and residential development
within the Village to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent
residential neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources;
         b.   to ensure that development proposals minimize the potential
adverse impact they might have on residential neighborhoods, including the
impact on pedestrian character, on-site parking, traffic patterns, congestion,
open space, storm water management and Village infrastructure;
         c.   to encourage the provision of on-site parking at the rear of
buildings, with access via alleys or private driveways, to reduce demand for
on-street parking in business districts;
         d.   to ensure that new development in business districts does not
decrease public parking supply, particularly on-street parking that supports
retail use; and
         e.   to maintain and enhance existing alleys in business districts,
which absorb some parking demand, provide off-street loading and unloading and
accommodate refuse storage and pick-up.
      39.   The Plan Commission found that the proposed special use was not
consistent with the following goals and objectives of the Winnetka
Comprehensive Plan:
         a.   to ensure that commercial, institutional, and residential
development is appropriate to the character of, and minimizes the adverse
impact on, its surrounding neighborhood;
         b.   to provide for a wide range of office/service and retail
commercial land uses and development within the existing business districts in
the Green Bay Road corridor;
         c.   to promote a strong community identity and opportunities to
interact, while building a healthy commercial tax base, and to provide a broad
range of goods and services so that Winnetka can satisfy most of their ordinary
shopping requirements in the Village, and so that non-residents will come to
the Village for specialty goods and services;
         d.   to maintain the essential quality, viability and attractiveness
of Winnetka’s districts while encouraging new economic development consistent
with the character of the Village and the individual business districts;
         e.   to require all commercial buildings to be buffered from
residential areas through the use of landscaping and/or other design
techniques;
         f.   to review Winnetka zoning regulations to protect the quality of
business districts, to promote the compatibility and continuity of retail
activities on ground floors and to control and limit drive-through businesses
and continue to require retail uses on the ground floor in both the Hubbard
Woods and Elm Street business districts.
      40.   After making its findings, the nine members of the Plan Commission
then in attendance adopted a resolution finding that the proposed special use
permit application by National City Bank is not consistent with the Village of
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan.
      41.   The Winnetka Zoning Board of Appeals conducted a public hearing on
the special use application on January 12, 2004.
      42.   The public hearing was held pursuant to all required notices, and
the public notice of the hearing was read into the record.
      43.   The special use permit application does not include a request for
any zoning variations.
      44.   All witnesses were sworn.
      45.   The applicant’s case was presented by: (a) Jim Nolte of GPD Group,
520 South Main Street, Akron, Ohio; (b) Ann Lazars, of 2021 Spring Road,
Oakbrook, Illinois, vice president of planning for National City Bank; and (c)
Yvonne Jones of Zifkin Realty in Northbrook. The applicant was also represented
by legal counsel, Susan M. Connor, who introduced the witnesses and presented
the applicant’s legal argument.
      46.   The owners of the Subject Property, Patricia Halekias and James
Denos, were also present but did not present any testimony.
      47.   Ms. Lazars testified that the proposed branch bank would generate
between 2,000 and 3,000 customers a week when it matured in a five to seven
year period.
      48.   Ms. Lazars testified that the proposal is for a full service branch
that would include lending, deposits individual and business accounts, trust
services, services to private clients, selling investments and ATM service.
      49.   Ms. Lazars testified that National City Bank is attempting to
connect its network of sites from Lake County through the Loop to provide a
more convenient network and that their 26 branches were not enough to cover the
market.
      50.   Ms. Lazars testified that the bank contracted with real estate
brokers, who provided several potential sites, and that the bank considered the
property at Lincoln and Elm, the Subject Property, and the Winnetka Galleria.
      51.   Ms. Lazars testified that the bank was discouraged from locating at
Lincoln and Elm, and decided that the Green Bay Road location offered it the
best traffic and visibility. In response to questions about other branch
locations and similarities to what is planned at this location, Ms. Lazars
referred to branches at 921 Armitage in Chicago and at Grand and State in
Chicago, but stated there are no other branches in a small community.
      52.   Mr. Nolte testified regarding the location of the ATM, handicapped
access at the rear of the building, and the layout of the building.
      53.   Ms. Jones testified that she is familiar with the market in Hubbard
Woods and recent real estate transactions in the area.
      54.   Ms. Jones testified that the owners purchased the property in 1995.
Ms. Jones testified that, in the past, the building included retail space, an
antique store and a cycle shop, who subleased the space to a children’s
furniture store, which never opened.
      55.   Ms. Jones testified that there are two distinct markets in the
Village, and that Hubbard Woods is the preferred market area in the Village
with regard to visibility.
      56.   Ms. Jones testified that rents in the Hubbard Woods area range from
$30 to $40 per square foot.
      57.   Ms. Jones testified that the subject property has been vacant since
2002.
      58.   Ms. Jones testified that fast food establishments are the most
active tenants, along with financial institutions, and described financial
institutions as better tenants because they are more stable.
      59.   Ms. Jones testified that there is no potential for furniture
retailers at the site, that no apparel tenants would be interested in the site,
and that the size of the space does not lend itself toward retail use.
      60.   Ms. Jones stated that the current business in the building is being
remodeled and would be called Hubbard Woods Kitchens and Bath and that the
space is occupied by the owner.
      61.   In addition to the application, the applicants submitted the
following documentary evidence:
         a.   a letter from Michael Morrison of GPD Group, providing a summary
explanation of how the applicant believes the special use permit meets the
Village’s special use standards;
         b.   a separate summary statement addressing the additional
requirements for special use permit requests in the C-2 Overlay Zoning
District;
         c.   drawings and elevations depicting existing conditions at the
Subject Property and the proposed exterior improvements;
         d.    an August, 2003, Parking Demand and Circulation Study prepared
by Metro Transportation Group, Inc. for GPD Group and National City Bank of
Michigan/Illinois; and
         e.   a brochure describing the bank and its community activities.
      62.   The ZBA record also included the following correspondence and
memos:
         a.   a memo from Steven M. Saunders, Director of Public Work/Village
Engineer, setting out his review of the Parking Demand and Circulation Study;
         b.   a November 5, 2003, letter in opposition from Leslie Stevens;
         c.   a January 12, 2004, fax from Winnetka Chamber of Commerce
Executive Director Cicely Clarke Michalak, forwarding objections from members
of the Chamber of Commerce;
         d.   a November 6, 2003, memo from the Winnetka Chamber of Commerce to
the chairperson of the ZBA, requesting that the C-2 Overlay regulations be
enforced;
         e.   a letter in opposition from Jill Meyer, whose business, Woodstock
Gallery, is located at 901 Green Bay Road in Hubbard Woods;
         f.   a November 10, 2003, fax in opposition from Young at Heart, which
has been a retailer in Hubbard Woods since 1957;
         g.   a November 10, 2003, letter in opposition from Howard Smith of
Trooping the Colour, located at 896 Green Bay Road.
      63.   Persons testifying in opposition to the application were: (a)
Leslie Stevens, 950 Green Bay Road, who owns the property immediately south of
the Subject Property; and (b) Jim Swartchild, 1333 Sunview Lane, who has
occupied the commercial space at 954 Green Bay Road for 26 years.
      64.   Ms. Stevens testified regarding the development of her property at
950 Green Bay Road and its conversion from office space to retail space.
      65.   Ms. Stevens testified that her property at 950 Green Bay Road was
originally built as offices, but was converted to retail space after the
special use for office space expired.
      66.   Ms. Stevens testified that the south retail space at 950 Green Bay
Road has been leased.
      67.   At the completion of the ZBA hearing, a motion to recommend
approval of the special use request failed, with two members voting in favor of
the application and four members voting against it, and the special use
application was submitted to the Village Council without a favorable
recommendation from the ZBA.
      68.   The application was considered by the Village Council at its
regular meeting of January 2, 2004.
      69.   The detailed minutes, findings and conclusions of the Design Review
Board, Plan Commission and Zoning Board of Appeals were submitted to the
Council, along with the documentary record.
      70.   The Village Council also received and considered a 16-page legal
memorandum, dated January 27, 2004, from the applicant’s attorney, Susan M.
Connor. The Village Council also heard and considered the legal and factual
arguments of applicant’s attorney in support of the special use request.
      71.   The Village Council also heard public comment from Leslie Stevens,
Pat Horne, Cicely Clarke Michalak, and Roberta Rubin, all of whom had sent
written objections, and from Barbara Aquilino, 546 Elm Street, a real estate
broker and chairman of the Ad Hoc Economic Development Committee, who also
spoke in opposition to the application.
      72.   The property owner and representatives of National City Bank were
also present at the Village Council meeting, but did not speak.
II.   CONCLUSIONS
      73.   The sworn testimony and documentary evidence are insufficient to
establish that the applicant satisfies all of the Zoning Ordinance's
requirements for the granting of a special use in the C-2 overlay district, as
those requirements are set forth in Section 17.40.010.B2 and Section
17.56.010.L of the Winnetka Village Code.
      74.   Public records of the Village and the evidence in the record
establish that the trend of development in the immediate vicinity of the
Subject Property is toward retail use.
         a.   Public records of the Village establish that 950 Green Bay Road,
the property directly to the south of the Subject Property, was originally
built and used as a medical office building.
         b.   Public records of the Village establish that, after the medical
offices had left the property at 950 Green Bay Road and the building had
remained vacant, the Village granted zoning variations to 950 Green By Road in
2001, to allow building modifications that would expand the first floor space
and convert it from office to retail use.
         c.   Pursuant to the 2001 variation, and according to the testimony of
Ms. Stevens, the owner of the property, 950 Green Bay Road has been extensively
renovated and the commercial space on the south side of the building's ground
floor has been leased to a retail business.
         d.   The applicant admits that the south half of the ground floor
space on the Subject Property is occupied by a retail business and that the
space is being renovated.
      75.   The proposed use is contrary to the trend of development in the
area in that it reverts to a non-retail use, although no portion of the Subject
Property has been used for non-retail purposes for years.
      76.   The only evidence in the record pertaining to the nature and effect
of the bank's operations is Ms. Lazars' testimony as to the number of customers
the bank will have when it has matured in 5 to 7 years.
      77.   The testimony of Ms. Lazars regarding the nature and impact of the
proposed use cannot be given any weight. Ms. Lazars did not testify as to the
basis for her forecast of the number of customers, and the applicant has not
offered any. Nor did Ms. Lazars or anyone testifying on behalf of the applicant
offer any evidence to establish that the facilities and communities it used for
its comparables are similar to the facility proposed in Hubbard Woods business
district.
      78.   The applicant has not offered any evidence either as to the number
of customers the bank will have or as to the effect of the proposed use during
to 5 to 7 years that it will take for the bank's operations to mature.
      79.   The applicant has not offered any evidence as to the effect of the
bank on the immediate vicinity. At best, the evidence offered by the applicant
pertains only to the general effect of a bank. No evidence has been offered on
the effect that adding another bank to the current mix of uses will have on the
trend of development in Hubbard Woods toward retail., where there are already
three banks in fairly close proximity, half of the Subject Property is a retail
use and the property immediately to the south of the Subject Property has
invested in converting to retail uses, which are uses permitted as of right.
      80.   The testimony of Ms. Jones cannot be given any weight, as she did
not provide any basis for her conclusions about the market in general. Nor did
she testify that the owners had attempted to market the property for permitted
uses at market rates.
      81.   The applicant has not presented any evidence to establish that the
retail space in the south portion of the building is devoted to active retail
merchandising which maintains typical and customary hours of operation, as
there no evidence about either the specific nature or the business hours of
Joliet (Hubbard Woods) Kitchen and Bath.
      82.   The presence of the ATM on the front of the building will require
customers to stand on the sidewalk in order to transact business, and the
proposed use thus does not meet the special use requirements of Chapter
17.56.010.L or the additional requirements imposed by Chapter 17.40.010.B.2.
         a.   Requiring customers to stand on the sidewalk in order to transact
their business creates an interference with pedestrian traffic, and the record
thus establishes that adequate measures have not been and will not be taken to
provide ingress and egress in a manner that minimizes pedestrian traffic
congestion in the public ways.
         b.   Although the applicant offered, at the Village Council meeting,
to withdraw the ATM component of the special use if it was "a deal breaker,"
the ATM is not the sole factor for finding that the applicant has not met the
special use requirements established by the Zoning Ordinance, and the Council
must therefore conclude that the ATM component of the application has not been
withdrawn.
         c.   The use proposed does not meet the requirement that it conform
with all other applicable provisions of the Village Code. Section 12.04.070
prohibits the conducting of business on any public street or sidewalk without
the prior approval of the Village Council. The daily, around-the-clock
availability of the ATM for business is unlike any other business use
previously allowed on the public streets. Applicant has not presented any
evidence to support a conclusion that the ATM will provide a benefit to the
general welfare of the Village, and allowing the ATM would thus establish an
unfavorable precedent for the use of the public rights of way for private
business purposes.
      83.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the applicant
has not presented evidence to establish that the proposed bank at the proposed
location will encourage, facilitate and enhance the continuity, concentration,
and pedestrian nature of the Hubbard Woods business district in a manner
similar to that of retail uses of a comparison shopping nature.
      84.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that applicant has
not met its burden of establishing by competent, credible evidence that the
establishment, maintenance and operation of the special use will not be
detrimental to or endanger the public health, safety, comfort, morals or
general welfare.
      85.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that applicant has
not met its burden of establishing by competent, credible evidence that the
special use will not be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of
other property in the immediate vicinity which are permitted by right in the C-
2 General retail Commercial Zoning District.
      86.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that applicant has
not met its burden of establishing by competent, credible evidence that the
proposed special use will not substantially diminish or impair property values
in the immediate vicinity.
      87.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that applicant has
not met its burden of establishing by competent, credible evidence that the
presence of the bank will not impede the normal and orderly development or
improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses permitted by
right in the district or districts of concern.
      88.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the Plan
Commission correctly found that the proposed use is not consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan.
FOR ALL OF THE FOREGOING REASONS, the Village Council accepts the
recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals and the special use permit is
accordingly denied.
   ADOPTED this _________ day of __________________, 2004, by the following
roll call vote of the Council of the Village of Winnetka.
