January 6, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 20, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 6, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, and Stephen Presser. . Absent:
Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega and five persons in the audience, including Trustees-
elect Thomas Eiler, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   January 13, 2004, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   January 20, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 9, 2003, Study Session. Trustee Greenough suggested a couple of
minor text changes in order to clarify meaning. Trustee Brower moved to adopt
the minutes, as amended. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   December 16, 2003, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough suggested a minor
text change for clarification. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes, as
amended. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-49-2003 – Zoning Variation: 530 Sunset - Adoption.
President Duhl stated that this ordinance would grant variations from the side
yard and front yard setback provisions of the Zoning Ordinance and had been
discussed at length at the Council’s last meeting. He asked for comments from
the audience or the Trustees. There being none, he called for a motion.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-48-2003, seconded by Trustee
Presser. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
b)   Ordinance M-1-2004 – Landmark Designation: 419 Sheridan Rd. –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed some of the features of this property,
the Henry Windsor, Jr. House, which earned it a “unique” rating on the System
for Evaluation of Landmarks, and Trustee Greenough commented on the evaluation
method itself.
Trustee Brower asked what benefits accrue to the owner of a property so
designated. Trustee Greenough indicated that the house receives honorary
recognition of its significance as well as certain other intangible benefits.
It may also enable the owner to apply for a property tax assessment freeze,
which does not significantly decrease income to the Village given that the
Village’s portion of the property tax bill is comparatively small.
President Duhl remarked that in the time that the Village has had its Landmark
Ordinance only a very few properties have applied for this freeze.
Trustee Brower commented that he could see little public benefit to the Village
in awarding this designation in this instance because the property is largely
hidden from public view. President Duhl responded that the value to the Village
is in the preservation of a unique home and Village history.
Trustee Woodbury asked that pictures of the property be distributed for review
at the next meeting.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. M-1-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2004 – Foodservice Sanitation Regulations –
Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that this ordinance is a follow-up on
previous discussions about the need to incorporate current versions of the
Illinois Code into the Village Code. She noted that the State of Illinois
Department of Public Health sets the standards for the operation of foodservice
establishments and retail food stores and that most municipalities adopt these
codes by reference. These codes establish sanitation standards for design,
construction, equipment, and operation of food establishments. Municipalities
must enforce the standards and may only make amendments to these codes that
would be stricter than the requirements that the State dictates. She noted that
the State sanitation codes do not address administrative functions such as
licensing and permitting, fees, permit/license suspension and revocation,
administrative hearings, and penalties since each municipality is responsible
for adopting ordinances regarding these administrative functions.
She reported that the Code Committee had reviewed the draft language, added
some definitions from the State’s definitions, clarified the Village’s
enforcement authority, and beefed up general provisions so as to give the
Village Sanitarian some rule-making authority. She said that the ordinance
provides for cumulative enforcement powers and allows for summary closures in
the case of critical violations that are also drawn directly from State
regulations.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-1-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution No. R-1-2004 – Annual Motor Fuel Tax Resolution – Adoption.
Manager Williams explained that historically, the Village has made a policy
decision to allocate $125,000 of the MFT Fund towards maintaining streets,
which is a permitted use of MFT funds. This resolution would officially
authorize this allocation of funds for street maintenance.
Trustee Brower, seconded by TrusteeAbell, moved to adopt Resolution R-1-2004.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1347 and 1348. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items from the warrant lists, he
moved to approve Warrant List No. 1347 in the amount of $1,311,792.00 and
Warrant List No. 1348 in the amount of $915,615.96. Trustee Presser seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Abell, Presser, Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
b)   558 Hill Terrace Rezoning Request – Request to Designate Hearing Body.
Attorney Janega reviewed a request from the present owner of the subject
property to rezone the parcel from its current B-1 Multiple Family Residential
classification to R-5 Single Family Residential zoning.
Ms. Janega explained that pursuant to the Zoning Ordinance, requests for
rezoning must be considered in a public hearing by a board or commission
designated by the Village Council, which, in turn, shall report its findings
and issue recommendations to the Council. She noted that recent similar matters
have been referred to the Plan Commission. The Plan Commission, she said would
look at the rationale laid out in the application and address the proposal’s
consistency with the Comprehensive Plan.
Mr. Harms, the property owner submitted a recent survey and pointed out that
given the size of the property, it cannot possibly be developed within the
constraints of the B-1 zoning district.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved that the Plan Commission
be the hearing body for this rezoning request with their recommendations coming
back to the Village Council for final determination. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   Quarterly Report: Village Yards Update. Manager Williams reported that
from a financial aspect, the project is going very, very well, largely due to
the excellent jobs done by the Village’s Director of Public Works and the
construction manager. He indicated that the Public Works director is hoping to
be able to add sealing of the old concrete floors and new garage doors back
into the project.
d)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Manager Williams referred the
Trustees to the Capital Improvements Reports filed by the Department Heads
included in their packets. He said that with the exception of the installation
of the new water main on Willow Road, the Village accomplished all of its major
capital improvements. He indicated that major bridge work is being slated for
the coming year.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reminded everyone that Budget Meetings
have been scheduled for February 19, 24, and 26.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Brower
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Webster. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:45 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Webster. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster. The meeting
adjourned at 8:46 p.m.
