                     Nan Greenough
Recording Secretary
January 13, 2004
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
7:30 p.m.
(Approved February 3, 2004)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser and Trustee Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Bryan
McInturff, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant
Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Finance Ed McKee,
Trustee Candidates Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker, and 5
persons in the audience.
2. Power Plant – Trimble County 2 Participation.
Manager Williams introduced Ron Earl of the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency,
who presented information to the Council for their decision regarding the
Village’s participation in the acquisition of the Trimble County 2 Power Plant.
Mr. Earl provided the Council with background information on IMEA and the work
being done to find economical sources of fuel to provide power for the
communities the agency represents, including Winnetka. He predicted that the
Trimble County 2 power plant would be one of the lowest cost coal fuel
producers in the country. Mr. Earl then introduced Rick Solderholm, consultant
from R. W. Beck, Bob Childers, IMEA’s Director of Finance and Business and Troy
Fodor, IMEA’s general counsel.
Mr. Solderholm reported that IMEA has grown considerably since 1990 and there
is a need to find additional fuel resources. After investigating the options
available, the conclusion was that Trimble County 2 is the best, although IMEA
will continue to investigate other options as well. He reviewed the estimated
costs and associated risks of the project, as well as projected development and
construction dates and the technology that will be used at the plant.
Mr. Fodor reviewed the terms of the participation agreement and discussed the
negotiating process that resulted in significant cost reductions over the
initial terms. He also responded to comments and questions from the Council
regarding the Village’s participation, its ability to withdraw from the
agreement, it’s position if another partner withdraws, and the effect of the
market and regulation.
Manager Williams discussed the financing alternatives with input from Mr.
Solderholm, Mr. Childers and Mr. McInturff.
Mr. Solderholm reviewed IMEA’s long term resource portfolio, explaining that
IMEA will own about 25% of its capacity in coal and 75% in other fuel sources,
such as hydro power, market purchases of gas and oil, and members’ capacity to
generate power.
Mr. Williams commented that it is important to have diversity in the sources
for fuel so that if market prices are high, IMEA can negotiate lower prices
because the member communities have alternate sources of power.
Mr. Solderhom said it was IMEA’s conclusion that ownership of the power plant
would result in immediate benefits over market purchases and still provide the
lowest bidder. He supported the timely development of Trimble County 2, but
also will remain active in discussions about additional baseload acquisitions
and other options that may become available.
Mr. Duhl asked for the recommendation of Mr. Gerald Keenan, the Village’s
consultant.
Mr. Keenan said that Winnetka’s risk without IMEA is much greater than it is
with IMEA. He explained that the further north you go in Illinois, the more
congested it becomes and prices are higher in congested areas. IMEA helps keep
the cost distributed evenly among its members. He also said that Winnetka’s
ability to generate power if called upon by IMEA may become more valuable in
keeping costs competitive as areas become more congested. He recommended that
Winnetka proceed with the agreement because if the power plant is built
according to schedule, it is an excellent opportunity, has the lowest cost and
is relatively low risk. He said that IMEA has done a very good job of providing
the Village with a low cost option and still retain the ability to be involved
in future opportunities. He suggested that the Village limit their agreement to
Trimble County 2 because they may want to investigate other options
independently and because there may be opportunities over the next 2 years for
IMEA to reduce the construction risks.
Mr. Williams said that the staff had three recommendations:
   1)   that it is in Winnetka’s long term best interest to choose a 15-year
financing option;
   2)   that approval should be limited to this project only; and
   3)   to continue Mr. McInturff’s participation as the Village’s
representative on the IMEA Board and continue to use Mr. Keenan as the
Village’s consultant on this project.
President Duhl asked for comments from the Council and the Trustees concurred
with staff’s recommendations. He stated that the consensus, therefore, was to
go forward with the project.
3. Audit/CAFR Contract.
Mr. McKee, Director of Finance, reported that the contract with Miller Cooper
to provide auditing services to the Village is expiring. He said that there has
been no operational problems with their services over the past 8 years and the
cost is reasonable. He suggested that because of the transition to the GASB34
reporting method, there is a slight benefit in using the same company but he
can accommodate a change if the Council wishes. He also suggested that the
Council meet with Mr. Lyons of Miller Cooper before the audit as well as
afterwards to increase communication, especially in light of recent changes in
legislation.
President Duhl and Trustee Abell commented on the advisability of continuing
the relationship between Miller Cooper and the Village and upon further
discussion, the consensus of the Council was to retain the company’s services.
Mr. Brower suggested the creation of an audit committee that would discuss the
Village’s position in detail and then report to the Council.
The Trustees considered Mr. Brower’s suggestion and agreed that instead of a
separate committee, Mr. Lyons would be invited to a study session to allow the
Trustees to discuss matters in detail before the auditor’s final official
report is submitted to the Council at a regular meeting.
4. Discussion: Draft Ordinance Amending Zoning Code.
Attorney Janega informed the Council that there is a public hearing scheduled
at the Council’s February 3rd meeting on the amendments to the Zoning Ordinance
and to meet publication requirements, the public hearing notice will be
published this week. She said that, consistent with past practice, the hearing
will be before the Council sitting as a Committee of the Whole.
President Duhl announced that the discussions on building line articulation and
the draft ordinance amending the zoning code would be continued to a special
study session on January 22nd due to the late hour of this meeting.
The meeting adjourned at 9:58 p.m.
January 20, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 3, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 20, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus and 20 persons in the
audience, including Trustee Candidates Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and
Jessica Tucker.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   January 22, 2004, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Webster, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 3, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustees Brower, Webster, Woodbury,
Abell, and Presser indicated that they expected to attend. Trustee Greenough
expressed some uncertainty, and President Duhl said that he would be unable to
attend.
c)   February 10, 2004, Study Session. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Abell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 6, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Presser moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   January 10, 2004, Special Meeting. After noting corrections to the draft,
Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes, as amended. Trustee Woodbury
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-1-2004 – Landmark Designation: 419 Sheridan Rd. – Adoption.
President Duhl called for additional comments or questions from the audience or
the Council. There being none, he called for a motion to adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-1-2004 designating 419
Sheridan Road as a Winnetka Landmark. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Webster,
Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None. Trustee Brower abstained.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2004 – Food Service Sanitation Regulations – Adoption.
President Duhl called for additional comments or questions from the audience or
the Council. There being none, he called for a motion to adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-1-2004, amending the Village
Code to update existing food service and sanitation regulations. Trustee
Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-03-2004 – Zoning Variation: 66 Abbotsford - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for roofed lot coverage and impermeable lot
coverage variations to permit the construction of a two-story addition and an
open porch. He noted that construction of the addition would require the
removal of a 30” diameter oak tree that is located in the middle of the
building envelope and that a public sanitary sewer that serves three properties
runs underneath the proposed addition. With regard to the sanitary sewer, he
indicated that the Village Engineer believes that careful construction activity
will avoid any damage to the sewer. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 7-0 to recommend that the variations be approved.
The property owner, Stuart Cornew, spoke in support of the variation request.
He indicated family health concerns as the reason for wanting the enclosed
porch.
Attorney Janega suggested that the draft ordinance be amended to include
conditions, including holding the Village harmless should the construction
damage or interfere with the operation of the sanitary sewer. She also
indicated that findings of difficulty or hardship pertaining to the 3-season
porch needed to be included. She indicated, however, that generally speaking
the hardship or practical difficulties have to run with the land, not with the
owner. She remarked that the removal of the tree probably does not constitute a
significant alteration to the essential character of the neighborhood as it is
in the back of the house and cannot be seen from the street. She commented that
it is unlikely that the tree would survive the construction process, even if it
were not slated for removal. By permitting its removal, it allows the Village
to get compensating trees to make up for the loss now.
Manager Williams pointed out that there is significant precedent in the Village
for permitting the removal of a large tree that clearly falls within the
permitted building envelope.
Trustee Abell inquired whether the sanitary sewer needs to be upgraded. Ms.
Janega responded that the Village Engineer says that the sewer is in good
condition, but it can be videotaped prior to commencing construction. Trustee
Woodbury said that that seems to make some sense.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that it is reasonable to expect that at some time in
the future, the 3-season porch would become enclosed. Ms. Janega agreed, saying
that it will probably serve as a family room since no other space in the house
is designed for that purpose. Trustee Woodbury indicated that overall he was
not opposed to the requested variation.
Trustee Greenough expressed some concern about granting an ordinance that
focuses on practical difficulties without findings that specifically run with
the land. She also noted that there is little remaining space on the property
to plant replacement trees.
Trustee Woodbury pointed out that the tree ordinance provides for the
distribution of replacement trees around the affected neighborhood.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-03-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-02-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1336 Scott Avenue -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot,
Maximum Building Size, and Front and Corner Yard Setback variations to permit
the reconstruction of a second floor addition above an existing attached
garage. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that this is basically a one-for-one replacement in
terms of footprint and that the variations are for existing legal non-
conformities. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 7-0 to
recommend that the variations be approved and submitted a letter from the
neighbor at 1340 Scott supporting the application.
Trustee Woodbury inquired about the proposed height increase. The project
architect, Gerald McManus, explained that the owner is trying to maintain the
Tudor character of the house and tie it in to the rest of the house and the
neighborhood.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-02-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1349 and 1350. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1349 in the amount of $1,026,870.10 and Warrant List No. 1350 in the
amount of $511,464.06. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Abell,
Presser, Woodbury and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None
b)   Comprehensive Plan Review of Appendix A-6. Mr. Norkus explained that when
the current Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, was adopted in 1999, it was
agreed that Appendix Six would be reviewed and updated on an annual basis, with
the goal of helping focus the allocation of the Village’s resources. In
December, the Plan Commission completed its annual review and made its
recommendations that are intended to assist the Council in its budgeting
process and directing staff initiatives.
Mr. Norkus mentioned that one change of particular noteworthiness is that the
Plan Commission will work with the new Business Community Development Committee
on shared concerns and issues relative to improvements within the Village’s
commercial districts. In particular, the Plan Commission considers Chapter 5
regarding the Green Bay Corridor due for an update and views this as an
opportunity to cooperate with the new committee.
Gwen Trindl, Chairman of the Plan Commission, echoed Mr. Norkus’ remarks. She
explained that the Comprehensive Plan is intended to provide focus on an on-
going basis. Ms. Trindl pointed out that in addition to recognizing the need to
work with the new Business Community Development Committee, the Commission
targeted stormwater runoff issues and building issues involving the ravine and
bluff areas as priorities.
Manager Williams recommended that under Appendix 6(C), Land Acquisitions, the
property at the northeast corner of Spruce and Birch Streets be moved from
“recommended” status to “highest priority.”
Trustee Webster expressed concern about the Council changing priorities
established by the Plan.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to accept the report of
the Plan Commission, including the modification suggested by the Village
Manager.
Responding to a question from Trustee Webster regarding the legal effect of
voting in favor of accepting the report, Attorney Janega confirmed that
accepting the report does not carry the force of law, in that it does not amend
the Comprehensive Plan, since amendments can only be done by ordinance after
following required procedures. She added that accepting the report also does
mean that the Council agrees with everything in the report.
Following Attorney Janega’s explanation, a voice vote was taken on the pending
motion, and the motion passed.
c)   Policy Direction: 1122 Sunset. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front,
Corner, and Side Yard Setback variations to permit the construction of a one-
story addition. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals forwarded no
recommendation to the Village Council as the vote was 3 in favor and 3 against
with 1 abstention.
Peter Widmer, the architect, spoke in support of the variation, noting that the
existing house is relatively small in terms of the size of the lot. He said the
owners want to have a home work area that is separate from the family living
area.
Trustee Woodbury remarked that the issue with the Zoning Board appeared to be
one of determining a practical difficulty that runs with the land, rather than
use by the owner.
Mr. Widmer responded that the setback on the side is unique given that it is
vacated space.
There was considerable discussion about whether the platted easement was public
or private land, and it was decided that the survey is correct, showing that
the land, in fact, belongs to the property owner, not to the Village.
President Duhl, however, pointed out that regardless of who owns the land, a
side yard variation is required.
Trustee Brower mentioned that there are conforming alternatives that do not
require a variation.
Trustee Presser remarked that in view of the conforming alternatives, the
difficulties in this instance appear to be driven by the owner’s desires not by
the land, and that therefore, he is not inclined to overturn the Zoning Board’s
decision.
Noting a consensus among the Council, President Duhl called for a motion to
accept the Zoning Board’s denial of this variation request. Trustee Brower so
moved. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, Greenough,
and Woodbury.
d)   Designate Hearing Body: Proposed Zoning Text Amendments . Attorney Janega
explained that pursuant to procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance,
requests for amendments are to be considered in a public hearing by a body
designated by the Village Council. After numerous discussions resulting in
proposed zoning text amendments, a hearing has been set for February 3, 2004,
and because of publication deadlines, the Village Council, at its January 13,
2004 Study Session, designated itself, as a Committee of the Whole, as the
hearing body. She recommended that the Council ratify its designation to
provide additional public notice, since the Study Session was not televised.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to designate the Village
Council, sitting as a Committee of the Whole, to hold the public hearings on
the proposed zoning text amendments. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
e)   Review of Status: 411 Linden . Attorney Janega explained that, pursuant to
Ordinance M-28-2001 granting a Special Use Permit and variations to the
Winnetka Historical Society to locate its offices and museum in the single
family residence and garage at 411 Linden Street, the Council is to “review the
use of the Subject Property in January, 2003, to evaluate whether the
conditions imposed in this Ordinance have provided adequate protection to the
immediate neighborhood from the potential negative impact of the special use.”
This report was prepared and considered in January, 2003, at which time an
updated status report was requested for January, 2004.
Ms. Janega reviewed the updated status report for the Trustees. She noted that
the property remains in compliance as to all of the conditions imposed by the
Special Use Permit, although many of them do not pertain as the property is not
yet open to the public.
Nancy Judge, President of the Historical Society, outlined the Society’s
activities over the last year including the steps taken to complete the museum
at 411 Linden.
Trustee Presser inquired whether the Society felt that it would be able to
support and staff both the 411 site and the Smith Burnham Log House.
Joan Evanich, Executive Director of the Historical Society, indicated that they
believed that they will be able to support both operations and explained how
each will serve the community.
Trustee Woodbury asked when the Society plans to open the 411 site to the
public. Ms. Judge responded that they hope to be open by spring, 2005.
Robert and Jodi Lapp, 414 Linden; Silas Keehn, 812 Ash St.; Ken West, 770 Ash
St.; and Marcia Keehn, 812 Ash, voiced objections to the presence of the
Historical Society in a residential neighborhood and urged the Council to
consider revoking the Society’s Special Use Permit.
President Duhl explained that the purpose of this hearing was to see if the
restrictions imposed are working as intended, noting that all of the objections
had been taken into consideration at the time the Special Use Permit was
granted and an attempt was made to address each one. In light of the fact that
the museum has not yet opened, it is difficult to make a final determination as
to the effectiveness of the conditions, but there are no grounds upon which to
reconsider the Society’s Special Use Permit. He suggested that the Council
revisit the matter in January 2005.
Trustee Presser agreed with President Duhl, saying that although there are
certainly some changed circumstances, there appears to be plenty of time to
revisit the matter should the need arise. He reiterated concern that the
Society would be unable to support both the museum and the Smith Burnham House
and indicated that had he known that was the intention, he might have hesitated
in voting in favor of the Special Use Permit.
President Duhl said that having granted the Special Use Permit, the Council
cannot revoke it arbitrarily, but it certainly can change the conditions
attached to it if it finds that the need exists. Ms. Janega noted that to do so
would require the same process as for granting the special use, including
published notice for a public hearing. President Duhl called for a motion to
review the status of the Special Use Permit conditions again in January 2005.
Trustee Presser so moved, seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
f)   Kiosk Study . Mr. Norkus explained that currently there is only one sign
used to promote community events. It is located on the Ameritech property at
Oak and Linden. He said that frequently organizations are unable to use the
existing sign because of conflicting timing of events. The Chamber of Commerce
suggested the development of a kiosk sign that could accommodate more than one
event to the Council.
After the meeting between the Chamber and the Council, the Council authorized
staff to enter into a contract with the Lakota Group to develop alternative
designs for a community event sign kiosk. At the same time a committee was
formed to work with the designers to evaluate alternative designs and
alternative locations for such kiosks. He reviewed the four alternative
designs, noting that the Committee had selected “Concept A,” as the one that
best evokes the character of the community and the business districts and is
most consistent with the objectives of the commercial area design guidelines.
This design would not only upgrade the appearance of the current display but
also permit more than one sign to be displayed at any given time.
He reported that preliminary budget figures prepared by the Lakota Group
estimate that Concept A would cost approximately $16,000 each to fabricate and
install the kiosk.
Mr. Norkus said that the Design Review Board had approved the proposed design
for the Oak and Linden location but requested that the configurations for
alternative, or additional sites, be brought back for further discussion.
Trustee Woodbury suggested that a mock-up be prepared to ensure that whatever
is posted on all three panels can be easily read from the street.
President Duhl supported Mr. Woodbury’s suggestion, but expressed some concern
over how the allocation of signs, given their different sizes, would be
determined.
Trustee Webster inquired as to the ownership of the property on which the kiosk
sits. Mr. Norkus responded that Ameritech owns the land and that he is in
negotiations with them over securing the right to use the land. Mr. Webster
also asked about illuminating the sign. Mr. Norkus explained that while the
Sign Code prohibits internal illumination, indirect illumination, as proposed,
is permitted.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to approve the proposed
“Concept A” design and authorize staff to proceed with finalizing construction
specifications and solicitation of bids for construction. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
g)   Police Utility Vehicle . Manager Williams reviewed a request from the
Police Department to purchase a 2004 four-wheel drive Ford Expedition to be
used as a patrol vehicle, intended primarily for use by evidence technicians.
The vehicle would be purchased through the State of Illinois Cooperative
Purchasing Program at a price not to exceed $28,000.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to authorize the purchase of
a new utility vehicle at a price not to exceed $28,000. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury,
Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Truck Chassis Purchase. Manager Williams reviewed a request from the
Public Works Department to purchase a truck chassis to replace an existing
vehicle chassis that is in poor condition. The existing vehicle body would be
mounted on the new chassis. The Public Works Department recommends that the
chassis be purchased through the State of Illinois cooperative purchasing
program for the bid price of $32,162.35. It will cost approximately $2,000 to
transfer the existing body to the new chassis.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to authorize the purchase
of a new truck chassis, as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, and
Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reminded everyone that Budget Meetings
have been scheduled for February 19, 24, and 26. He reported that the
preliminary budget book is now available for inspection at the Library and that
the Village’s new web site has been launched. Mr. Williams also distributed the
materials for the special study session on Thursday, January 22, focusing on
building articulation. He also commented on the recent sale of water to
Kenilworth.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Greenough reported on her first meeting as the Village’s
representative to the NWMC.
Trustee Presser noted that there would be a Code Review Committee meeting on
Tuesday January 27th.
Trustee Brower reported on the recent meeting of the Chamber of Commerce. He
noted that holiday sales in the Village were pretty good and that the Chamber
is gearing up for the second Home & Garden Show at the Northfield Community
Center
c)   Village Attorney. Ms. Janega reported that the 2003 Supplement to the
Illinois Institute of Continuing Legal Education Municipal Law handbook has
been published, and that she had again authored the chapter on Utility Systems.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Mary Garrison as the Park
District’s representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee Greenough moved to
approve the appointment. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:33 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:55 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned
at 10:56 p.m.
