Recording Secretary
January 22, 2004
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
7:30 p.m.
(Approved February 3, 2004)
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser and Trustee Edmund Woodbury.
Absent: Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Trustee
Candidates Thomas Eilers and Jessica Tucker, and 2 persons in the audience.
2. Discussion of Proposed Zoning Text Amendments.
   a)   Building Line Articulation.
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio explained that at the
Council’s meeting in December, several items were identified for further
discussion about building line articulation:
      1)   simplify the language in the Village Code;
      2)   expand the means of articulation;
      3)   eliminate articulation requirements for single family homes or
provide greater flexibility;
      4)   expand the Zoning Board’s authority;
      5)   establish requirements for R-1, R-2 and R-3; and
      6)   increase point of beginning from 30 feet to 35 feet.
After discussing the six points listed above, the Council directed Village
Attorney Janega to clarify the language in the Village Code to:
         i)   define the point of beginning for the measurement that determines
articulation;
         ii)   increase the maximum unarticulated distance from the point of
beginning from 30 to 35 feet, keeping the purpose of the ordinance in mind
which was to have articulation in 40 foot walls;
         iii)   retain the current means of articulation; and
         iv)   expand the Zoning Board’s authority to grant variations from
building line articulation requirements as long as no other variation requests
accompany them.
The Council also discussed expanding the articulation requirement to the R-1,
R-2 and R-3 zoning districts and concluded that the requirement was not
necessary for the R-1 district, but directed staff to provide further
information on the R-2 and R-3 districts for discussion at the February 3rd
meeting.
Manager Williams said the notice of hearing for the zoning amendments has
already been published and scheduled for the Council’s February 3rd meeting. He
suggested that Mr. D’Onofrio and Mr. Norkus gather information for that meeting
and that if the Council needs more time, it can be scheduled for discussion at
the February study session.
   b)   Attic Allowance. Mr. D’Onofrio stated that staff has concluded from
comments made by architects and builders that minimizing the amount of attic
space counted toward GFA and increasing the attic allowance to 3% would allow
them to use a variety of roof pitches. He said that current regulations allow
250 feet or up to 1% of the lot area and that the Council had discussed
increasing the allowance to 3% in the hope of getting steeper pitched roofs. He
said this translates to an allowance of 270 square feet on a typical 9,000
square-foot lot.
The Trustees discussed raising the minimum square footage allowed and requiring
a certain roof pitch in order to use the 3% allowance. The staff was directed
to create various scenarios that illustrate the effect these changes might have
for further discussion by the Council.
President Duhl suggested that better defining roof pitch and the 3% allowance
to reflect the Council’s purpose and disallowing variation requests from
applicants who don’t comply may result in better plans.
Attorney Janega confirmed that the Council’s wishes were that the attic
allowance would be increased to 3% and that there would be no changes to the
base square footage and height.
   c)   Building Height. The consensus of the Council was that no changes are
required.
   d)   Front Yard Impermeable Surface. Council confirmed staff’s
recommendations to establish a standard that allows 30% of impermeable surface
in front yards.
Attorney Janega added that the materials used for impermeable surface, such as
paver bricks and gravel, are counted differently and recommends that this
reference be rephrased or a new definition be added so that general lot
coverage will not exceed 30% of the lot area.
   e)   Open Porches: Mr. D’Onofrio asked the Council to confirm the
recommendation to eliminate the calculation towards GFA of accessible open
porches and balconies on the second floor of a home. The Trustees indicated
they were all in favor of this.
   f)   Window Wells: Mr. D’Onofrio stated that the current maximum width
allowed for a window well in the Village Code is 2 feet which does not conform
to the Building Code requirements of 3 feet for window wells used as a means of
egress. He said the staff recommends that the Village Code be amended to allow
3-foot projections for window wells.
The Trustees discussed the issues concerning window wells in side yards and
agreed that the provisions for the maximum projection of window wells in side
yards could be increased to 3 feet, but only in side yards 8 feet or greater.
   g)   ZBA authority.
Trustee Woodbury voiced his concern about giving too much discretion to the
Zoning Board on variation requests where the community or the neighbors have
objections. He stated that if there is a dispute, objectors should be able come
before the Council.
Attorney Janega suggested the Council may want to devise a scale that would not
give 100% authority to the Zoning Board in certain circumstances. She said that
the Board would be dealing with existing houses that may already be
nonconforming.
President Duhl said that there had been discussion on this issue previously,
but it seems to him that either the ZBA has authority or it doesn’t.
Trustee Woodbury stated that, in a case where the neighbors are upset, everyone
should be able to come before the Council, the people they elected, to have
their say.
President Duhl asked if a neighbor could come to the Council anyway. President
Duhl said that the Council has always been sensitive to the fact that they are
the elected officials and anybody should be able to come in and speak to the
Council.
Attorney Janega said that the law says that if variations are granted by an
administrative body other than the Council, appeals can only go to court, and
the Council does not have the authority to bypass that. She said that the
procedure could only be modified if Winnetka became a home rule community. She
further stated that the Council does not have to accept the recommendation of
Zoning Board, but if the Board is authorized to make a final decision, then it
is final unless it goes to court. She also noted that the authority delegated
to the Zoning Board now says that anyone requesting more than one variation has
to come before the Council.
Trustee Abell and President Duhl indicated that they are willing to give more
discretion to the ZBA on these kinds of things.
President Duhl stated the consensus of the Council, which was to retain the
changes that had been drafted to have new construction and multiple variation
requests come before the Council.
The meeting adjourned at 9:22 p.m.
February 3, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 2, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 3, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Ed Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present:
Trustees David Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser,
and David Webster. Absent: Village President Duhl. Also present: Village
Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water &
Electric Bryan McInturff, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio
and approximately 15 persons in the audience, including Trustee Candidates
Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
