6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-2-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1336 Scott Street. – Adoption.
President Pro Tem Woodbury announced that this request is for variations from
the intensity of use of lot, maximum building size and setback provisions of
the Zoning Ordinance and had been introduced at the Council’s January 20th
meeting. He called for additional comments from the audience or the Council.
There being none, he called for a motion to adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Brower moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-2-2004, granting variations from
the intensity of use of lot, maximum building size and setback provisions of
the Zoning Ordinance to permit the reconstruction of a second floor addition.
The motion was seconded by Trustee Presser. By roll call vote, the motion
passed. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-3-2004 – Zoning Variation: 66 Abbotsford – Adoption.
President Pro Tem Woodbury announced that this ordinance would grant roofed lot
coverage and impermeable surface variations to permit the construction of a
two-story addition and an open porch, and that it had been amended since
introduction at the Council’s January 20th meeting.
Village Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments made to the ordinance pursuant
to the Council’s discussion at its last meeting.
Mr. Dimitri Criezis, the architect for the property owners, addressed the
Council and reiterated a comment made at the last meeting that the proposed
addition has less square footage than the existing deck. He also stated that
the property owner has also decided that the deck would be replaced by steps
instead of another deck and would like to modify the application to that
affect. In response to a question from Mr. Brower, he also said that it would
still be necessary to remove the tree.
Attorney Janega said that the ordinance can be modified to incorporate this
change, if necessary, but the deck does not count towards impermeable surface
and so should not affect the relief requested. She also noted that she had
added language to the ordinance because of the sewer line.
President Pro Tem Woodbury asked for questions or comments from the audience or
the Council and there being none, called for a motion to amend the ordinance.
Trustee Abell moved to amend Ordinance No. M-3-2004, seconded by Trustee
Presser. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Abell moved to adopt Ordinance M-3-2004 as amended, granting a
variation from the intensity of use of lot provisions of the Zoning Ordinance
to permit the construction of a two-story addition. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Presser. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-06-2004 – Zoning Variation: 228 Poplar - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for variation from front setback and side yard
setback requirements of the zoning ordinance to permit the construction of a
detached garage. He reported that this request was discussed at the Council’s
December 2nd meeting and sent back to the Zoning Board of Appeals for further
consideration. At its January meeting, the Zoning Board of Appeals voted 6-0 to
recommend that the variations be approved after the applicant improved the side
yard setback and agreed to remove a fence along the Wilson Street side of the
lot.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments to the ordinance that incorporate the
changes made by the applicants since the original request for variation. She
also explained that the ordinance number assigned at introduction in December
was changed from M-42-2003 to reflect the appropriate sequential number for
2004 ordinances.
Trustee Abell commented on the removal of the fence and adding it as a
condition of the ordinance.
Ms. Janega replied that the discussion, as she remembers it, was that the fence
would be replaced by one that is 4 feet high instead of the existing 6-foot
high fence. She said that since a building permit would be necessary for this,
the Community Development Director would see that the applicant complies with
this agreement.
President Pro Tem Woodbury called for questions or comments from the audience
and there being none, called for a motion to approve the amendments to the
ordinance.
Trustee Presser moved to amend Ordinance M-02-2004, seconded by Trustee Brower.
By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-02-
2004 granting a variation from the front and side yard setback provisions of
the Zoning Ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-04-2004 – Zoning Variation: 139 DeWindt – Introduction.
President Pro Tem Woodbury stated that this is the introduction of an ordinance
that grant a request for variations from the front and corner yard setback
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance.
Mr. D’Onofrio stated that this request is for the placement of three air
conditioners in the front and corner yard setback of this property, which is
situated along a private road that does not actually exist and has not been
vacated. He said that the Council had considered variation requests at this
address last spring, but the placement of the air conditioners was not included
in that application. He reported that at its January 12th meeting, the Zoning
Board of Appeals had recommended that the variation be granted.
Mr. Stephen Cole, who represents the applicant, said he had nothing further to
add but would answer questions.
President Pro Tem Woodbury called for comments or questions from the audience
or Council.
Trustee Brower inquired as to whether the neighbors to the east, who would be
the only neighbor affected, had voiced any objections to this request, and Mr.
D’Onofrio replied that they had not.
Trustee Greenough moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-04-2004, seconded by
Trustee Abell. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-05-2004 – Zoning Variation: 818 Elm – Introduction.
President Pro Tem Woodbury announced that this request would grant a variation
from the parking requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to permit three second
floor apartments to be converted to commercial offices without providing the
required on-site parking spaces.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the building on the property is at the southeast
corner of Elm and Chestnut and currently has retail space on the first floor
and three apartments on the second floor. He said that the building does not
have any on-site parking because it was constructed before parking requirements
were established. He explained that the petitioners have requested relief from
the current regulations, which would require 3 parking spaces in order to
convert the apartments into three offices. He also reported that, at its
January 12th meeting, the Zoning Board of Appeals voted 6-0 in favor of the
requested variation.
Mr. Woodbury asked if there are enough parking spaces in the Elm Street
business district to accommodate the change from residential to commercial use.
Manager Williams replied that there are enough spaces in the business district,
but some may not necessarily be in close proximity to the building.
The applicant then showed the Council a rendering of the proposed
reconstruction and office layout. Answering a question from Trustee Woodbury
regarding ADA accessibility, the applicant said that-it is not required for low
impact buildings such as this one.
Trustee Presser moved to introduced Ordinance No. M-5-2004, seconded by Trustee
Abell. By voice vote, the motion passed.
f)   Ordinance No. M-07-2004 – Zoning Variation: 117 DeWindt – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio stated this request for variation would permit the construction
of an attached garage with front facing garage doors having a width of 39 feet.
He said that one of the proposed garage doors measures 17 feet and current Code
requirements allow the maximum of any one door to be 9 feet. He said that the
garage doors would be set back 170 feet and cannot be seen from the street. The
Zoning Board recommended by a vote of 6-0 to approve the request for variation.
Trustee Greenough commented that the purpose for establishing this regulation
was for the effect of the garage doors as seen from the street, but these
garage doors will be hundreds of feet from DeWindt Road.
Trustee Presser asked if there were any neighbors who have objected to the
request. Mr. D’Onofrio replied that the closest neighbor is hundreds of feet
away and he had received no objections.
Trustee Greenough moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-07-2004, seconded by
Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the motion passed.
g)   Ordinance No. M-08-2004 – Disposal of Property (Squad Car) – Introduction.
Manager Williams explained that this ordinance would approve the sale of a
squad car that was involved in an accident about a year ago and would also
permit the Village to dispose of obsolete computer equipment when necessary.
Trustee Brower noted a correction to the Ordinance and. there being no further
questions or comments, moved to introduce Ordinance M-08-2004, seconded by
Trustee Webster. By voice vote, the motion passed.
[Note: President Pro Tem Woodbury announced that the agenda item for policy
decision at 952 Green Bay Road would be discussed by the Council before the
public hearing on the zoning text amendments. Minutes for that topic are under
New Business, below.]
h)   Ordinance No. MC-02-2004 – Zoning Text Amendments – Public Hearing and
Introduction. President Pro Tem Woodbury stated that the Council has had
several study sessions to discuss amendments to various sections of the Zoning
Ordinance and a public hearing on those amendments has been scheduled for
tonight’s meeting.
President Pro Tem Woodbury declared the public hearing open at 9:24pm. He
called for any members of audience who would like to speak to the proposed
zoning amendments. No one responded.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the proposed amendments to the Zoning Ordinance that are
intended to streamline the zoning process by giving more authority to the
Zoning Board of Appeals to make final consideration on certain zoning variation
requests.
The Trustees commented on the draft ordinance and suggested some changes in the
wording of several provisions.
Trustee Greenough asked for clarification on the Zoning Board’s authority for
final decision-making on applications that contain multiple variation requests.
Attorney Janega explained that if any one variation requested in the
application falls within the Council’s jurisdiction, then the whole package
goes to the Council for final consideration and the Zoning Board conducts the
public hearing and makes its recommendation to the Council. She further
explained that if a variation request contains more than one variation and all
of them fall within the Zoning Board’s authority, the Zoning Board would make
the final determination whether to grant or deny the variations.
Mr. D’Onofrio stated that the proposed amendment to the attic allowance retains
the 150 square foot minimum, but increases the maximum allowed to 3% of the
total lot area, with the intent of the amendment being to increase the roof
pitch of homes.
Mr. D’Onofrio further stated that the amendments to the articulation provisions
are to grant authority to the Community Development Director to determine the
point of beginning. They also increase the distance from which articulation is
measured from 30 feet to 35 feet and establish definitions for articulation and
articulated wall. He said that the Staff had been asked to make a
recommendation about whether to establish articulation requirements in the R-
2 and R-3 zoning districts and had analyzed approximately 13 permits issued for
homes during the past year. He said that the results of that analysis are that,
in the majority of the homes in these districts, articulation generally began
between 31 and 35 feet and was set back 2 feet.
Trustee Presser commented that the analysis seems to indicate there is no need
for articulation requirements in the R-2 and R-3 zoning districts.
Trustee Abell added that since the majority of the homes in these districts are
being articulated, the occasional home that is not does not concern him.
President Pro Tem Woodbury said he believes that a case has been made that
articulation is not necessary in the R-2 and R-3 zoning districts and the
Council agreed.
Mr. D’Onofrio then reviewed the amendment to the impermeable surface
requirements, which would allow up to 30% of front yard lot coverage. He
replied in the affirmative to a question from Trustee Webster, who had asked if
this would accommodate the homes on busy streets that need turnarounds in their
front yards.
The Council then discussed the amendments concerning window wells in side yards
that would allow a 2.5-foot encroachment onto a side yard that is 8 feet or
less and a 3-foot encroachment in side yards that are more than 8 feet if the
window well is used for egress.
Attorney Janega further explained the amendments, particularly relating to the
changes and additions to the definitions of articulation and maximum front yard
coverage. She said that she would further clarify the term “horizontal plane”
in the definition of articulation at the request of Trustee Greenough.
Ms. Janega stated that she had revised the chapter on nonconformities in its
entirety to make it more cohesive.
Trustee Greenough referred to the paragraphs concerning the removal of
structures that cost more than 50% of their value to repair and asked that an
exception be made for historic buildings.
The Council commented upon the building code requirements and establishing an
exception for buildings with landmark designation and suggested that it be
discussed further at a study session.
President Pro Tem Woodbury closed the public hearing at 10:07 p.m.
Trustee Presser moved to introduce Ordinance No. MC-02-2004, seconded by
Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
i)   Ordinance No. M-09-2004 – IMEA Power Sales Contract – Introduction.
Manager Williams reported that the Village staff has been performing due
diligence for about a year regarding the acquisition of the Trimble County 2
power plant. The staff has concluded that the purchase makes good sense for the
Village and recommends that the Council approve only this project so that the
Village would be able to consider future projects individually. He said staff
also recommends financing the project over a 15-year time period.
Director of Water and Electric Bryan McInturff had nothing further to add,
except to express his opinion that the project is a very good option for the
Village.
Trustees Webster and Brower asked about any additional funds that might be
required to pay off the bonds or after the Village has paid off its debt.
Manager Williams said that if another municipality would drop out, their share
of the debt would be absorbed among all of the members and since membership is
growing, should be okay.
Attorney Janega said that the document has been changed to specifically define
the Village’s participation in the TC2 project and not just the “project,” so
it is not confused with any other IMEA project. She said final changes would be
discussed with IMEA’s attorney and the Village’s consultant, Mr. Keenan, until
all terms were agreed upon.
Trustee Brower moved to introduce Ordinance No. 09-2004, seconded by Trustee
Webster. By voice vote, the motion passed.
