a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1351 and 1352. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1351 in the amount of $553,621.41 and Warrant List No. 1352 in the
amount of $286,938.32. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Abell,
Presser, Woodbury and Webster. Nays: None.
b)   Policy Direction: Bank Special Use Permit Application – 952 Green Bay.
President Pro Tem Woodbury explained that National City Bank has requested a
special use to be allowed to occupy the existing vacant retail space in the
commercial overlay district and that the Council will be providing policy
direction in this matter.
Trustee Brower recused himself because he is employed by a competitor.
Mr. D’Onofrio stated that the request by National City Bank is for a special
use permit to operate a bank at 952 Green Bay Road. He explained that the space
was previously occupied by Alberto’s Cycle Shop and several other businesses.
The special use is being requested because the property is in the C-
2 Commercial Overlay District which requires a special use permit for non-
retail uses on the ground floor.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the retail overlay district was created because of
concern about the viability of retail business districts as a whole if they
were being taken over by non-retail uses, and if those were allowed to
proliferate. He said that the purpose of the commercial overlay district was to
promote and protect retail uses. He explained that the bank is not considered
to be a retail use. Other non-retail uses are service establishes such as
offices, travel agencies, medical offices and the like.
Describing the application, Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the applicant proposes
to convert the north half of 952 Green Bay Road into a bank. He said that the
first floor of the bank would have tellers, offices, conference rooms and
restrooms, and the second floor would have a couple of offices. An ATM would be
housed on the front at the north end of the building, facing Green Bay Road.
Mr. D’Onofrio added that the petitioners stated at the Zoning Board of Appeals
that there would be 6 to 8 employees during the day. The bank would operate 57
hours a week from 9 a.m. to 7 p.m. Monday through Friday and from 9 a.m. to 4
p.m. on Saturdays.
Mr. D’Onofrio stated the proposal was initially reviewed by the Plan Commission
and, on a vote of 9-0, they found that it was not consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan and recommended against the special use. At its January
meeting, the ZBA was 2 for, and 4 against, recommending the special use. The
Design Review Board reviewed the plan for the exterior improvements and
expressed concerns, strongly stating that their preference was for the ATM to
be in an enclosed vestibule and not on the street as proposed. The DRB also
suggested improving the rear of the building, which faces the alley parallel to
Green Bay Road.
Mr. D’Onofrio added that the applicant had submitted a parking traffic
circulation study, which had been reviewed by the Village Engineer, Steve
Saunders. He directed the Council’s attention to Mr. Saunders’ comments at
pages 201 and 202 of the agenda packet, and explained that Mr. Saunders’
comments conclude that the bank would not create parking or traffic patterns
that are inconsistent with what is already found in the Hubbard Woods area.
Trustee Greenough asked Mr. D’Onofrio how he would evaluate this request based
on his professional experience.
Mr. D’Onofrio replied that, as a planner, he looks at the Comprehensive Plan,
and that it’s pretty clear that certain sections of the Plan point strongly
towards keeping Hubbard Woods viable with retail uses. He said the reason for
the overlay was to protect against proliferation of non-retail uses. He said
that looking at various sections of the Comprehensive Plan, it is clear that
the intent is to create a critical mass of retail uses to get pedestrian
traffic and other traffic to draw people to the Village, and that a bank
doesn’t fit into that retail component. He said that while a case can be made
for the occupancy of the space to a certain degree, the Comprehensive Plan is
clear. He also said that he also looks at the trend of current development and
the owner of the building next to it just spent a great deal of money
remodeling and creating more retail space directly to the south. His opinion is
that the Village should keep the use as it is and everything should be done to
retain retail space.
Trustee Abell noted the presence of other non-retail uses and asked if there
have been any other special uses granted for non-retail in this area.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that the Harris Bank subdivided or consolidated, but there
has been nothing since he has been here.
Manager Williams asked if there were any ATMs in town that are right on the
sidewalk, so to speak.
Mr. D’Onofrio said no, that he doesn’t know of any other financial institutions
that do this.
Manager Williams also noted that, when talking about the farmers market last
summer, the Village decided to keep this type of activity off of the sidewalk.
President Pro Tem Woodbury said that, at page 188, it appears that the bank is
only using part of the building and asked the applicant if he was reading that
correctly.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that this is right and, referring to page 190, said the
south side of the building is Joliet Kitchen and Bath which will remain. It
will be remodeled and renamed and will share a common entrance with the bank,
and the bank will have the north half of the building.
Susan Connor, attorney for the petitioners, said she thinks it very important
going into this hearing, to put out front, that they know that this is a highly
political issue, but it’s not so simple as the Chamber and some opponents have
made it out to be, because the Zoning Ordinance does not prohibit non-retail
use on the first floor, although the Comprehensive Plan clearly states that
this be done. The Zoning Ordinance says that it can be conditionally permitted,
specially permitted. She said it is no surprise that the ZBA and the Plan
Commission both gave negative recommendations because one of their primary
tasks is to determine if the proposed use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan, and to the extent that the Plan says no retail on the first floor, this
project does not comply. She added, however, that the Comprehensive Plan is not
a legally binding document.
Ms. Connor went on to say that, in a recent case, the Supreme Court explained
that when a town decides that a use is a permitted special use, they have
necessarily decided that they are not necessarily prohibited. She said that
case is on all fours with this one, so the premise that banks or other non-
retail uses are not ever permitted, is wrong. She continued, saying that the
question is whether this particular bank in this particular location complies
to the maximum extent with the Comprehensive Plan, but also does not deprive
the owner of all reasonable use. She added that a really important point that
some opponents miss is that this Village has legislated that some uses may be
permissible and some uses, in fact, may be good for the synergy that Mr.
D’Onofrio was talking about.
Ms. Connor said that the Council needs to look closely at the evidence
developed below. Virtually, every opponent who spoke or wrote, including the
owner of L & A, who said it in the paper last week, said that there is not
enough foot traffic to keep retail vital. The bank’s vice president testified
in considerable detail about the stability of this bank and the careful study
it did on what the user profile would be and why it picked this location. She
said one reason they decided on this location is that it is in Winnetka and
they had looked at every other conceivable location in town, and this was the
least difficult request for a permit. She said there was no place where an
acceptable site permitted a bank as a right. There were other sites that also
would have required a special use, but they wouldn’t have been so good, not
necessarily for the bank, but for the pattern of the surrounding, existing
retail and non-retail uses on the first floor.
Ms. Connor explained that the third reason the bank chose this location is the
internal space of this particular location was designed to be a bank. She said
that the first floor with the mezzanine makes it just about perfect for the
kind of banking operation that is proposed. She said it is estimated that there
will be 2,000 to 3,000 retail walk-in users every month. She said this is not a
bank like the one at Winnetka and Green Bay where people park in an adjacent
parking lot, only go to the bank and don’t have any transactions at the same
site. At this location, people necessarily park in the neighborhood, coming
from the train, parking on Gage, parking in the garage, and parking up and down
Green Bay Road. She said there will be 70 to 100 people a day who can shop at
the stores they pass by. She added that the point is that the banks and service
uses are not “one size fits all,” and this particular bank with its customer
base, with its not having a parking lot, will bring customers, including from
Glencoe, Northfield and Wilmette, and can not harm the retail. She said they
are not putting in a new building, not destroying an historically significant
site, and the façade will only be improved because the signage will be brought
up to code, it will be handicap accessible, and have parking as well. She said
the back will be quite attractive. She-asks that the Council not be swayed by
the concern of everyone of us in this room, that we have a strong mix of
retail, as a way to turn this down. She said the bank will only help this
retail.
Trustee Presser asked if the legal part of Ms. Connor’s argument is that she is
suggesting we have the discretion to make this a special use based on facts,
and that the opponents have gotten the case wrong?
Ms. Connor said that Mr. Presser is correct, but she also thinks there is no
discretion for the Council to turn this application down. There is no evidence
that this bank, as opposed to banks in general, will cause any harm and there
is massive evidence about how this owner has for years been taking a hit on
reducing rent, and getting tenants to sign long term leases that are broken.
She said that this space is not viable for retail and was chosen partially
because it was designed as a bank. She added that the bank’s expert on market
demand determined that there were 5 retail sectors in the market and that those
sectors are dead or this particular site was not of any interest to them. For
instance, the site is not restaurant ready and it would take too much to
retrofit and traffic would be too much. She said that no Village is free to
deny an owner reasonable use and, on the facts of this case, the Village
doesn’t have any discretion to turn it down. She said there is no contrary
evidence on traffic, no contrary evidence on how many users there will be, no
damage to the limestone building, except for the ATM, no evidence contradicting
that they are doing nice work on the bank, and it will provide for the
handicapped and parking spaces.
Trustee Presser said that, while the Council will be advised by their attorney
on this, he is intrigued by the idea that they don’t have the discretion to
deny this permit.
Ms. Connor responded that, since the Pennsylvania Coal v. Mahon case in 1922,
the rule in land use has been that regulation can not deny an owner reasonable
use.
Trustee Presser said that position would assume that the only reasonable use is
the one the applicant is presenting.
Ms. Connor said the evidence is uncontradicted as to that issue. Regarding the
ATM’s location, she said the bank had looked at two alternative ways to put it
in; behind in the alley, which was not economically viable and not as safe, or
in the front. She said they looked at North Shore Community Bank, the bank
across from the book store and Harris Bank, and those ATMs are in alcoves. She
said that considerable testimony was given that in this building, a location
like that was not good because it would require sharing entry space and also
people who use the ATM need to be secure. People must be able to see outside
and be seen from outside when using an ATM, which cannot be done in this
building. It would not give the user security. It is more secure for the bank
itself outside.
Ms. Connor stated that it is important to her, as a lawyer for these clients
and as a resident of the Village as well, that the Village be commended for
dealing with the larger issue of economic development and doing all we can to
preserve retail, but one shouldn’t throw the baby out with the bath water. It’s
not necessary to turn down a bank that, by all of its history has demonstrated
that it’s a very good citizen and neighbor, and that, when it’s up and running
will bring 70 to 100 people a day to the Hubbard Woods district.
President Pro Tem Woodbury asked if Ms. Connor would address the adjacent space
and the total size of the combined spaces.
Ms. Connor said the part proposed for the bank is approximately 3,000 square
feet, of which 2,400 square feet is on the ground floor and 690 is in the
mezzanine. The adjacent kitchen property, which has been marketed, is an
additional 2,400 square feet.
President Pro Tem Woodbury said the total is then 4800 square feet of property.
Ms. Connor said that portion of the building has been marketed. Ms. Connor said
that the kitchen retail is run by the owners of the building and if they could
find a tenant to use the whole building, they would. They’ve been looking for
such a tenant.
President Pro Tem Woodbury asked about the intended use of the remaining 2400
square feet.
Ms. Connor stated that it will be the owner’s kitchen shop.
Trustee Presser turned to Attorney Janega, saying he’s not convinced by what he
thinks he heard in some of the argument. He said the problem Ms. Connor is
alluding to is a regulatory taking problem. He asked Ms. Janega if the Council
were to determine that retail use that’s specified for this area is appropriate
for this property, would it eliminate regulatory taking problems?
Ms. Janega responded that the short answer to the question is, yes, that is
correct.
Ms. Connor asked Mr. Presser if his question was that, if you determine that
retail is reasonable, does it overcome the takings problem?
Trustee Presser confirmed that was his question.
Ms. Connor said she thinks the answer is “not necessarily.” She said that, if
you have a factual basis for finding that retail use is reasonable, then yes,
that would deal with the takings problem.
Trustee Presser asked Ms. Connor if her position was that there is no factual
basis for that finding and Ms. Connor responded yes.
Manager Williams said the Village has been down this road a couple of times
with banks seeking to expand into vacant space, and the Village has said no to
this type of situation more than twice, once when Citibank wanted to move into
the former Lakeside Shoe premises, and eventually a dress shop moved in. He
said the North Shore Community Bank also tried for a couple of years to locate
in our downtown area and located in the telephone building until it finally
found a place on Lincoln Avenue just outside the overlay district. He said that
he would let the attorneys sort out the legal issues, but this is the history
of where the Village has been and they have been through similar situations a
couple of times.
President Pro Tem Woodbury commented that the fundamental policy question here
is the overlay district itself.
Attorney Janega said that she has framed the issue differently from the
applicant. She said the applicant’s view is that the Village doesn’t have
authority to deny the application and she said that she frames the issue as
whether the applicant has met its burden. She said there are special use
standards in two places in the Winnetka Village Code, in the special use
chapter, Chapter 17.56, and also in Chapter 17.40, which has standards on
special uses in the overlay distsricts.
Ms. Janega stated that, before getting to the factual issues, there are a
couple of legal issues with which she doesn’t agree. First, she noted that on
page 4 of Ms. Connor’s brief, the reference is to the standard as a denial of a
reasonable economically viable use, but added that the statement leaves out a
key word from the Lucas case, and that is the word “all,” which changes the
meaning, to whether all reasonable use is denied. She added that the Supreme
Court case Ms. Connor referred to, Chicago Heights v. Living Word Church, set
the stage for turning all of the special use law in Illinois on its head. She
stated that she did not agree that it’s on all fours with this case. In that
case, as here, the comprehensive plan said they wanted to protect the
commercial corridor, but there the zoning ordinance said nothing about
protecting the commercial corridor, and the special use to allow a church was
denied solely on the grounds that it did not conform with the comprehensive
plan. The Supreme Court said that if all you are relying on is the plan, it’s
not enough. Regardless of how you apply the standard, under Living Word, you
can’t decide only on the comprehensive plan and not apply the zoning ordinance.
Ms. Janega said that unlike the Living Word case, the Village’s Zoning
Ordinance is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan to protect the area for
retail uses, and the Zoning Ordinance reflects that by the creation of the
overlay district. The Supreme Court said that we are not saying you don’t have
the authority to do this through zoning, and the U.S. Supreme Court, on
regulatory takings, has been very clear, as have courts of appeal, to make
certain that they don’t tell local governments that they don’t have the
authority to do this. She said that what the Court in Living Word was saying is
that you don’t have the authority to protect the commercial district only by
relying on the Comprehensive Plan if your zoning law isn’t consistent with it.
Ms. Janega said this then takes us to the factual record. She stated that, in
Winnetka, three bodies looked at the application: the Plan Commission looks at
the application for compliance with the Comprehensive Plan; the Design Review
looks at it under design guidelines; and the Zoning Board of Appeals looks at
it under the Zoning Ordinance. She noted that ZBA member Sandra Berger said
specifically that the ZBA wasn’t looking at the Comprehensive Plan, and that,
while they may look at consistency with the Plan, the key is the elements under
the Zoning Ordinance and their recommendation is based on the Zoning Ordinance.
Turning to the record, Ms. Janega noted that Ms. Connor said that the evidence
in the record states that the bank meets all standards. Ms. Janega stated her
belief that the Council can decide either way based on the evidence before
them. She said that the statements in the record do not preclude the Council
from determining how much weight to give the statements, so the Council can
still make the determination. She added that she believes the Council could
make findings on this record either to grant the special use or to sustain the
ZBA.
Ms. Janega said the last issue is the ATM, which is a zoning issue from the
standpoint that the special use must conform with all other applicable codes of
the Village, and the ATM on the street does not. The Code has a provision
prohibiting the use of public streets for private business. For example, the
farmers’ market, as Mr. Williams said, restaurants placing signs on the public
way, and sidewalk cafes, etc., are always done with special authorization by
the Council, who determine what they want to allow on the streets. She said
there were several layers to go through in analyzing this particular
application and, again, based on the record the Council can articulate findings
that will support either a grant or denial of the request.
Trustee Presser asked if the Council had the discretion to approve the special
use without the ATM.
Ms. Janega replied that it does, and added that the Council also has the
discretion to impose conditions.
Ms. Connor said she didn’t know about the private business on public streets
and so if she can amend the application to eliminate the ATM, she can do that
and so take it off the table, if that’s a deal breaker.
President Pro Tem Woodbury asked what the Council’s criteria is for standards
for special uses.
Ms. Janega said that the standards for the Council are the same standards that
the ZBA uses. The Council is not bound by their decision, and has the
discretion to cure defects or disagree with the ZBA based on its reading of the
facts and interpretation based on the record.
Mr. Williams said that staff would recommend that the Council hear the rest of
the testimony from the audience and then consider two alternatives: to direct
Attorney Janega to do an ordinance for the Council meeting in two weeks, or to
direct the Village Attorney to come back with findings for denial and to look
at that in two weeks.
President Pro Tem Woodbury asked if Ms. Connor had a response to Ms. Janega’s
comments.
Ms. Connor stated that she didn’t.
President Pro Tem Woodbury then asked for comments from the audience.
Leslie Stevens, owner of 950 Green Bay, the building to the south of the
property, said that she had just finished a complete renovation of her property
over the last year because her special use permit was removed and she was told
that their first floor space would have to be retail. She said the building was
originally a medical building and she had to remodel it to accommodate the
retail space, and she spent much money converting it. She added that the market
is terrible right now. They do have a furniture store in one space, but the
other is not rented. She said that it is important to see that the space is
occupied by retail use. She said the bank made a comment that they could bring
in 2,000 to 3,000 people a month to be retail customers, but you have to have
retail stores for people to shop in. She said the Village needs to look very
strongly at what they can do to attract retailers to the area. She suggested
that, if the plan could be made a priority, it could revitalize the area. She
said that the bank is a mistake, noting that there are already 5 banks in this
area and that, when she banks, she goes in and then just leaves.
President Pro Tem Woodbury told Ms. Stevens that the Village has taken a step
and formed a business development commission to address the retail situations.
Ms. Stevens said she would like to talk to them, because she has some ideas
that would help and she would like to see them go forward with this.
Trustee Presser asked if it would be her view that this property could be used
as retail and be viable.
Ms. Stevens said that there has been retail there for a long time, so yes, even
though it was a bank at one point. You have to find the right person for these
spaces. She suggested to maybe do something with the sales tax so retail
businesses would want to come here.
Barbara Aquilino, 546 Elm Street, said she is a real estate broker, both
residential and some commercial, and although she is not aware of the
discussion at the ZBA meeting, she heard the comment about testimony, and
expert testimony, and said she does get a lot of inquiries about retail space
in Winnetka. One issue that comes up is the high rent and that stops merchants
from pursuing it further. She asked how we can close the gap, maybe through the
economic development commission. She said she doesn’t know what landlords find
they need to make a business profitable, but there must be a point at which
they all can be rented. She supports the overlay and said we need to do some
work to bring building owners and merchants together. She added that increasing
sales tax revenue keeps taxes down, which is in all our best interests, and it
would be a mistake to dilute that from what it is now.
Pat Horne, 791 Bryant, said she has been a shop owner at 841 Elm Street for the
last 18 years. She said the new economic development commission will do a lot
of good because there has never been a good mix of retail and no one was
looking after that. She said that one reason that retailers are having a
problem is we don’t have a good mix. She said that the C-2 overlay ordinance
was passed in 1985 because of all of the realtors that had offices in the
Village, noting that there were 18 real estate offices. She said we now have 9
banks in Winnetka for 12,000 people or a number of homes. Village Manager
Williams said there are approximately 4500 homes. Ms. Horne then asked Ms.
Connor how she came up with 2,000 people using this bank. She added that her
shop used to be a Jewel store and she doesn’t think having a bank in the
subject space before is a good argument, adding that many of the current stores
were other things in past lives.
Cicely Clark Michalak, 351 Ridge, Director of the Winnetka Chamber of Commerce,
spoke on behalf of some of the other members and said she would like to make
sure that they get the comments from other members before making their
decision. She said that she has memos from two business owners who worked hard
to build up their businesses and want to respect the overlay. She said one owns
Material Possessions and the other owns Perlie and that both oppose the bank’s
application. Howard Smith also wanted to be represented in the group of Hubbard
Woods retailers who are concerned to preserve retail use and increase retail
traffic, but he had to leave the meeting. In addition, Ms. Michalak pointed out
that Winnetka has been a leader in what other towns have been experiencing,
that is, how to maintain their main street shopping areas. She referred to a
Tribune article of January 30th and said other communities are now trying to
preserve their shopping areas such as Highland Park, which has put a moratorium
on any more banks. Ms. Michalak said she thinks the Village has done a good job
with the overlay district to keep things retail, but said she is not an expert
and does not claim to be.
Ms. Connor responded that how they came up with the figure of 2,000 to 3,000
people is that the bank has 1100 branch banks and they identified ones that are
comparable to this location – no parking lot, demographics, and located in a
retail rich area - and estimated based on this. There were seven or so
comparables that they used to come up with this projection. She said there was
extensive testimony about how rents are too high and so space cannot be rented.
She said this is not really the problem and it was the specifics of the site
and configuration of the building. Even the leases the owners had were
negotiated down, but the problem isn’t that the rents are too high, it is the
lack of foot traffic. She said the overlay zoning district is not in place to
maximize sales tax, it is in place to make a locational decision. The C-
2 overlay does not say only retail is permitted on the first floor, it
specifically says that some other uses may be permitted. She said she didn’t
hear a single opponent speak to this bank and why it doesn’t satisfy standards
for a special use. Ms. Connor stated that, concerning competition, she doesn’t
think it is the role of the Village to say that there is enough of any one kind
of business.
Roberta Rubin, the owner of the Book Stall and a resident of Glencoe, remembers
walking to Hubbard School and the skating rink, and the gas station and the
restaurant on the corner. She said the complexion of Winnetka does bother her
because we seem to be discouraging retail and encouraging banks. She said that,
on her street, she brought Caribou Coffee in a few years ago and now the Book
Stall is able to stay open in the evening. Banks are not open then. She said we
must encourage more retail in the overlay.
President Pro Tem Woodbury said that as Manager Williams pointed out, there are
two ways that the Council can go and we need comments from the Council on
whether they’re inclined to support the special use or it should be denied.
Trustee Presser said that Ms. Connor presented an excellent argument but that
he found it unpersuasive.
Trustee Abell said he’s of the same view. He agrees we must encourage foot
traffic and said the overlay district’s purpose is to have retail. He said he
doesn’t think the bank is an appropriate use at this site.
Trustee Greenough said she hadn’t heard any compelling arguments to have the
bank at this site.
Trustee Webster stated that the overlay has a legitimate purpose and the
restriction does not prohibit reasonable use. He added that he would not vote
for the special use.
President Pro Tem Woodbury said that he would vote against it in support of the
ZBA and Plan Commission findings. He said the issue is: what is the right use?
He said that to have a service use in this location would start to undermine
the purpose of the creation of the overlay district in 1985, which was to
protect the retail uses.
President Pro Tem Woodbury then called for a motion to deny the special use
permit and to direct the Village Attorney to prepare findings in support of the
negative recommendations of the ZBA and Plan Commission. Trustee Presser moved
to deny the special use permit, seconded by Trustee Greenough. By roll call
vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Presser,
Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Brower (recused).
c)   Bid No. 04-004, Sandblasting & Painting.
Manager Williams told the Council that sandblasting brick has become necessary
in the chemical storage room at the water plant and the woodwork would have to
be painted as well. He said that of the bids received, the lowest bidder did
not meet our specifications, so the staff has recommended that the contract be
award to the next lowest bidder, Pannex Painting, in the amount of $27,750. He
said that Mr. McInturff reports that references for this company are good.
Trustee Greenough was concerned that sandblasting would damage the brick and
asked if it was the interior brick that was being sandblasted.
Mr. McInturff replied that that the bricks must be kept dry of moisture and
since the brick is sealed on the outside of the building, it must be kept
unsealed on the inside. He further stated that this is the recommended
procedure for maintaining the brick.
Trustee Presser moved to approve Bid No. 04-004 and to award the contract for
sandblasting and painting in the chemical storage room at the water plant to
Pannex Painting in the amount of $27,750. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Abell and by roll call vote, passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower,
Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported to the Council that he had
authorized the Fire Chief to spend $5,000 for necessary repairs to a Fire
Department vehicle.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Presser reported that the Code Review Committee met last
week and made some accomplishments regarding the items on their agenda.
11. Appointments.
None
12. Adjournment.
President Pro Tem Woodbury announced that there were be no executive session.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned
at 10:34 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
February 10, 2004
MINUTES OF A WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
7:30 p.m.
(Approved March 2, 2004 )
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees Hugh
Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser and Trustee Edmund Woodbury. Absent:
Trustees David Abell and David Webster. Also present: Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus,
Village Forester Jim Stier, and Assistant Village Engineer Susan Chen. Trustee
Candidates Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker, and approximately
15 persons in the audience.
2. Discussion of Proposed CBI Subdivision.
Mr. Todd Thorhom, engineer for the project, addressed the drainage issues on
the proposed subdivision property as well as on the properties surrounding 630
Pine Lane. After describing the locations of the sub-basins, the detention area
and the swale that will be constructed to handle storm water, he said that in
his opinion, the plans comply with all of the Village ordinances and contain
sufficient means to handle the stormwater on site and also off site. In regard
to the trees on the property, he said directional boring will be done along the
north and south side of the lots, with some trees being removed along the north
lot lines. He said that the Village Forester still had concerns about some of
the trees and stated that he will try to work that out with him. He added that
they will widen Pine Lane as requested by the Fire Department.
The Trustees discussed the flow of water across the property and the placement
of the storm sewers and catch basins. Trustee Greenough expressed some concern
about the drainage system, saying it might be inadequate to handle the water
that will be displaced by the new homes being constructed and that it might
then damage some of the trees.
Mr. Thorhom replied that the addition of a house may add a little more water,
but will not damage any trees.
President Duhl asked Mr. Norkus to begin his presentation.
Mr. Norkus stated that, at its December 16th meeting, the Council had asked the
applicant to provide additional information regarding several issues:
      §   drainage and grading;
      §   the impact of the raised building pad on the westerly Lot 1;
      §   the necessity of the proposed water line;
      §   the impact construction will have on trees;
      §   the historic nature of the residence there, and requiring the home to
be landmarked to preserve the house;
      §   the preservation of numerous large trees;
      §   the installation of fire hydrants as part of the water line;
      §   stormwater detention, storm sewers and other storm water issues;
      §   driveway width; and
      §   the construction of a sidewalk.
Mr. Norkus said that the applicants have provided an explanation of the
drainage system on a neighborhood-wide scale. He referred to a memo relative to
the necessity for construction of a water line written by Director of Water &
Electric, Bryan McInturff, whose opinion is that the water line will improve
the water quality and water flow problems that occur occasionally because the
area is currently served by a dead end line. Mr. Norkus also reported that the
applicant has revised the tree inventory to indentify the species of trees and
to indicate those marked for removal. He said that the staff had placed stakes
at the site to determine the placement of the building pad on the proposed Lot
2 and has suggestions for modifying the building pad to protect some of the
trees. He stated that approval would also be conditioned on the installation of
fire hydrants, augering underground utilities instead of open trench
construction, widening the driveway on Lot 2 for Fire Department access and
construction of a sidewalk.
Assistant Village Engineer, Susan Chen, answered questions from the Trustees
regarding the route of the water line, stating that augering will be done where
possible and that there will be a connection to the water line at Hibbard Road
to loop the service. Ms. Chen further said that both Water & Electric and
Public Works had requested the water line.
Trustee Woodbury noted that there will be at least 6 trees affected by the
water line.
Village Forester, Jim Stier, commented that augering the water line will not
affect the trees as much as the swale and grading in some areas, because
cutting the ground may damage tree roots, and fill might be placed in such a
way that it smothers the tree roots.
Mr. Norkus and Mr. Stier presented slides of various views of the property. The
slide presentation illustrated the proposed building pads and showed which
trees would be affected by the building pads on Lots 1 and 3. Mr. Stier also
explained the location of the trees in relation to the color-coded tree survey.
The tree survey assigned a number to each of the trees with a diameter of 10
inches, named the species of the trees, and stated the condition of each. The
trees were color coded to indicate those that would definitely be removed and
those that Mr. Stier believed would be affected by the storm water drainage and
detention system and by the building pad locations. He also identified several
smaller trees that were of rarer species, which he recommended could be
transplanted rather than removed. Mr. Stier stated that the most desirable
trees are in Lot 2 and part of Lot 3, and that the majority of trees being
removed from Lot 1 are because of the detention area.
The Trustees discussed the placement of the building pads and the distance from
the building pads necessary to minimize damage to the affected trees in the
building areas
Ms. Janega clarified with Mr. Stier that the minimum safe distance for a tree
from the building area is at least one-half foot per diameter inch of tree, and
that the trees under discussion are 10 inches or greater in diameter.
President Duhl deduced that there are a number of trees in danger unless the
building pad is decreased. He asked if any trees would be saved if the building
pad was not built up to the floodplain.
Ms. Chen said that if that were the case, and if the building pad were not
built up, the need for compensatory storage would be less, but that the new
home would have to be built with a crawl space instead of abasement, and the
amount of stormwater detention provided for this development would not change
and a swale would still be needed.
Mr. Norkus suggested that by slightly altering the plans for the swale, the
detention area and the driveway, the number of trees to be removed could be
reduced.. He said that in the detention area, 8 of the 13 trees in question
could be saved by adjusting the building pad.
Trustee Greenough suggested that moving the whole building pad 5 feet to the
east would save the majority of these trees.
Mr. Norkus then displayed a photograph of a building pad that had been elevated
to accommodate a full basement on a property at Lindenwood and Hibbard that is
under construction, as well as pictures of existing homes that had used
landscaping or retaining walls to try to minimize the impact of the raised
building pad.
Trustee Woodbury said that the effect is more unobtrusive when it is handled
naturally and is preferable to a retaining block wall, which gives the neighbor
the feeling that they are looking at a mountain.
Ms. Janega said that the Council must consider the subdivision ordinance, tree
preservation, the Comprehensive Plan and zoning as a whole package before
deciding the preliminary approval of the subdivision. She further stated that
there is no question that the engineering issues have been addressed, but there
are still access and overall subdivision issues.
President Duhl asked Ms. Chen if it was her opinion that the applicant had
provided sufficient detention and compensatory storage. Ms. Chen confirmed that
they had, based upon their proposal.
Lee Baumgarden, 1150 Pelham, commented that oak trees can live 900 years and
that it is a shame to remove a 10-inch oak tree that has been around about 100
years.
George Covington, an attorney from Lake Forest, stated that he is representing
Andy Block, who is the owner of 644 Pine Lane. He said that he appreciated the
amount of time the Village Council has spent on this matter and that it is
appropriate that the subdivision approval be considered as carefully as
possible. He said the question is whether the Village will be better off with
this development or without it? He noted that there are significant problems
with the drainage on Pine Lane and expressed concern that building another
house will exacerbate those problems, notwithstanding the stormwater detention,
swales, etc. that have been proposed. He also noted that most of the discussion
at the meeting has been on trees and the great loss of them on Lot 1, because
of the building of the house on a mound and the amount of compensatory storage
that will be required, and also because of the effect on the west side of the
building envelope. He said that oak trees don’t like any disturbances and the
only thing that is predictable is that if there is building, the trees will die
slowly.
Trustee Presser asked Mr. Covington if it was his view that there is no such
thing as a subdivision by right and those that want to build have the burden to
prove that it is necessary.
Mr. Covington replied that the Tree Preservation Ordinance says to maintain or
add to the tree population, unless specifically authorized to remove a tree
with a permit, or if there is an economic hardship. He stated there is no
evidence of this in this case. He further stated that, if the property is
unusual, has hardships and is in conflict with other ordinances, the Council
might be able to impose conditions or deny the subdivision all together. He
pointed lastly to the preservation of the existing home that the Village has
required be designated as a landmark. He asked if meaningful historic
preservation can be accomplished if a "McMansion" is built in front of the
home, since part of preservation is to see the home in the context of the
grounds.
Marcy Love, 1175 Pelham, said her property abuts Lot 3 on the southwest and is
part of the 9 acres that floods. She said that in the past, they have been told
not to worry because new water lines would be installed, but that as houses are
built, there is more of a lake or swamp in her yard and they have lost oak
trees to water damage. She also said that the new homes have destroyed the
trees, although promises were made to protect them. She asked that Mr. Short’s
conservation efforts be respected.
Another resident of Pelham Road commented that, after seeing Mr. Norkus’s
presentation, the information on the trees was worse than he thought it would
be on Lot 1 and Lot 3, and asked that the Council not approve the subdivision.
Don Manhard, Manhard Consulting, stated he has worked with CBI Builders for
several years and they have saved trees on his property, which is in Glencoe at
595 Sheridan Road. He said this is a unique site and more valuable because of
the trees, so it is in the developer’s best interest to save them. He agreed
that there would be no problem transplanting the trees that Mr. Stier has
marked and they can improve the areas where trees have been marked questionable
or for removal. He also said that in Lot 3, they will try to move the building
pad 5 feet to the east to save the trees if they can and will work with the Mr.
Stier to save more of the trees. He added that there had been a misconception
on the depth of the swales, which, in reality, will only be 6 inches deep in a
lot of areas. He said that they can put in just enough drainage so that trees
are not affected and will do hand digging when necessary. He said that, as far
as drainage goes, the water will still pass through the property, but some of
it will be rerouted around the homes that are built. He also said that the
homes have not been designed as yet and they will take care to fit them within
the pad and save the trees where possible. He pointed out on the plans where
some of the trees could be saved if the building plans were altered. He said
the Council’s concern about the raised elevation of the building pad as it is
seen from Hibbard Road can be designed so that it does not look like it is
sitting much higher than the surrounding properties. He believes the conveyance
of storm water will be much better because it will be directed instead of
meandering across the property.
Barbara Barrett, attorney for the developer, responded to Mr. Covington’s
remarks and said that, as the Council knows, the Village’s ordinances set forth
what the Village expects and what the Village wants to see, and that determines
whether development should take place or not. She further stated that the
ordinances are what set forth the public policy and that CBI's proposed
development complies with each of the ordinances.
Trustee Presser asked Ms. Barrett if she thought that if the subdivision
complies with the ordinances, they must approve the subdivision.
Ms. Barrett said she does believe that if the subdivision complies with all
ordinances, the Council has no discretion. She cited a case from 1917 that said
there is no discretion to a City Council to deny if it complies with
ordinances. She said that she thinks the Council must recognize that CBI Custom
Homes is an outstanding developer that properly scales homes to the sites and
has gone far beyond the Village’s requests for water mains, by reconfiguring
lots, augering, and saving trees. She asked that, when the Council considers
preliminary plat approval, it take into account not only the law, but also the
quality of the developer.
President Duhl requested that Attorney Janega consider what has been discussed
and provide further information to the Council as to its discretion and
authority for the Council's March 2nd meeting.
Ms. Janega reminded the Council that at the preliminary plat approval stage,
the Council gives a formal expression to the developer that, if the plan comes
back in final form substantially as it is at the preliminary approval state,
then a decision can be made to go forward. Ms. Janega said that she would still
need to discuss with the staff if there are any conditions they want to
incorporate with preliminary approval so that there are no misconceptions about
how the ultimate subdivision will look. She said she would also assume that on
March 2nd, there will be policy directions with findings denying or directing a
resolution to approve.
Executive Session. Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for
the purpose of discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters,
and board appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and
Section 2(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes:, Brower, Greenough, Presser, , and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Abell and Webster. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:08 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:12 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser and Woodbury. Absent: Trustees Abell
and Webster. Also present, Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Brower, Greenough,
Woodbury, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Webster. The
meeting adjourned at 10:24 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
February 17, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 3, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 17, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Ed Woodbury, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio and approximately six persons in the audience,
including Trustee Candidates Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   February 19, 2004, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Webster, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 24, 2004, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Webster who was not certain, indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   February 26, 2004, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Webster who was not certain, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
None.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-04-2004 – Zoning Variation: 139 DeWindt – Adoption.
President Duhl called for additional comments or questions from the applicant,
the audience, or the Council. There being none, he called for a motion to adopt
Ordinance No. M-04-2004 granting Front and Corner Yard Setback variations in
order to permit the installation of three air conditioner units.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-04-2004, seconded by Trustee
Greenough. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-05-2004 – Zoning Variation: 818 Elm – Adoption. President
Duhl called for additional comments or questions from the applicant, the
audience, or the Council. There being none, he called for a motion to adopt
Ordinance No. M-05-2004 granting a Parking variation to permit the conversion
of three second floor apartments into three commercial offices without
providing the required on-site parking spaces.
Trustee Brower moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-05-2004, seconded by Trustee
Woodbury. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-07-2004 – Zoning Variation: 117 DeWindt – Adoption.
President Duhl called for additional comments or questions from the applicant,
the audience, or the Council. There being none, he called for a motion to adopt
Ordinance No. M-07-2004 granting a Garage variation to permit the construction
of an attached garage with front-facing garage doors 39’ in width.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-07-2004, seconded by Trustee
Woodbury. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-08-2004 – Disposal of Property (Squad Car) – Adoption.
President Duhl called for additional comments or questions from the applicant,
the audience, or the Council. There being none, he called for a motion to adopt
Ordinance No. M-08-2004 authorizing the disposal of one police squad car and
obsolete computer equipment.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-08-2004, seconded by Trustee
Presser. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-09-2004 – IMEA Power Sales Contract – Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the Ordinance and the Second Addendum to
reflect further clarification of Winnetka’s participation in Trimble County
Unit No. 2.
Trustee Abell pointed out that the Village is losing its right to terminate its
arrangement with IMEA for a short period of time, but since the Village has no
desire to do so, the loss is meaningless.
Attorney Janega noted that should TC2 not move forward, the Second Addendum
would no longer be in effect, so the Village would return to the same position
it now holds and would be able to terminate under the same conditions as it has
now.
Trustee Abell moved to amend Ordinance No. M-09-2004 in accordance with the
Village Attorney’s recommendations. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-09-2004, as amended, which
authorizes the execution of the Second Addendum to the Power Sales Contract
between IMEA and the Village of Winnetka. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
7. Old Business.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-02-2004 – Zoning Text Amendments – Amendment & Policy
Direction. Attorney Janega explained that this ordinance was introduced at the
February 3rd regular meeting, following a public hearing. Pursuant to
discussions at that time, further changes have been made to the draft
ordinance. Ms. Janega proceeded to outline those changes, which include
recitals and legislative findings. After further discussion on the building
line articulation requirements and window well allowances, it was decided that
further revisions would be necessary and that Community Development would
attempt to provide illustrations for these provisions. In addition, she raised
three other policy issues that have been identified and recommended that these
issues be resolved prior to adoption, as follows:
   i)   The amendments are all based on discussions of single-family
residential zoning issues, but the zoning variation procedures pertain to all
variations. Is it Council’s intent that the standards for the ZBA’s
jurisdiction of multi-family and commercial building be expanded, and if so, to
what extent?
   ii)   As drafted, the Zoning Board could grant a 100% variation from the
side yard setback requirements, regardless of whether the variation is for a
structural modification to an existing nonconforming wall, or for a building
addition that extends the nonconforming wall. Is it Council’s intent that the
100% variation authority extend to building additions as well as existing
buildings?
   iii)   The need to clarify provisions regarding the limitation on rebuilding
a nonconforming building after a partial demolition due to such things as
structural damage or fires, was intended to cover all nonconforming buildings,
including landmark properties.
With regard to (i) above, there was agreement among the Trustees that the
standards for the ZBA’s final jurisdiction on variations apply only to single-
family residential zoning districts, and that variations in all other districts
should be decided by the Council.
After extended discussion regarding (ii), it was the consensus of the Trustees
to allow the Zoning Board to grant up to a 50% variation from the side yard
setback requirements, regardless of whether the variation is for a structural
modification to an existing, nonconforming wall, or for a building addition.
Finally, regarding (iii), Ms. Janega pointed out that there is no language in
the Zoning Ordinance that would prevent someone from seeking a variation from
the zoning limitations for the purpose of restoration in the unlikely event
that a landmark structure is damaged or destroyed. She suggested that recitals
be added to encourage the restoration of buildings of architectural
significance.
Trustee Greenough said that she would not like to see the Village discourage
people from rebuilding historic structures, since very little of what is
valuable has been officially recognized as a landmark.
President Duhl indicated that the Village would have to rely on the Director of
Community Development to administer this provision because without an official
landmark designation, there is no way of objectively identifying what is
valuable.
Mr. D’Onofrio commented that the Department would make every effort to assist
any one who is seriously interested in restoration.
Trustee Abell moved to amend Ordinance No. MC-02-2004 as indicated in the
revised draft, so that the Village Attorney can prepare a final draft for
consideration. The motion was seconded Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
8. New Business.
None
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1353 and 1354. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1353 in the amount of $651,136.47 and Warrant List No. 1354 in the
amount of $412,466.77. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Abell,
Presser, Woodbury and Webster. Nays: None.
b)   Renewal of Auditor’s Contract. Mr. McKee reviewed a proposal from Miller
Cooper & Co., the Village’s current auditor, to perform the audit of the
Village’s records for the fiscal years ending 2004 and 2005. He called the
Trustees’ attention to two portions of the proposal where the auditors are
proposing to limit liability, noting that they cover a very narrow set of
circumstances that are not really applicable to Village work.
President Duhl confirmed that these provisions are usual and customary
throughout the industry.
However, after a brief discussion, the Trustees directed the Finance Director
and the Village Attorney to prepare some new language eliminating the
limitations to present it to Miller Cooper for their approval.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Manager Williams announced that he would be staying in
Winnetka and thanked the Trustees for the understanding and support while he
interviewed for the Manager’s job in Glenwood Springs, Colorado.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Woodbury reported on a recent meeting of the Historical
Society. He noted that they are making headway in their fundraising efforts,
although the double impact of the Log House move and their move into 411 Linden
is putting strain on their budget than anticipated. He also mentioned the
Society’s plans for a spring fundraising event.
Trustee Brower reported on a recent Chamber of Commerce meeting at which
continued concern about departing businesses was a topic of discussion. He
reported that attendance at the Home Health and Garden Show was up from last
year, even though there were fewer vendors. He also mentioned that Tim Welter
is slated to become the next president.
c)   President. President Duhl announced that the annual Trustee retirement
party has been scheduled for June 12th.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the reappointment of Bill Meuer to the Board of Fire
and Police Commissioners. Trustee Greenough moved to approve the appointment.
Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:25 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Abell, Brower,
Greenough, Woodbury, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:26 p.m.
