Recording Secretary
March 2, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 16, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
2, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees Hugh
Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, and Ed Woodbury. Absent: Trustees
Webster and Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio and approximately 15 persons in the audience,
including Trustee Candidates Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   March 9, 2004, Study Session. President Duhl announced that the March 9th
Study Session has been cancelled.
b)   March 16, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 3, 2004, Regular Meeting.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes, as drafted. Trustee Greenough
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   February 10, 2004, Study Session.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the minutes, as drafted. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   February 17, 2004, Regular Meeting.
Trustee Brower moved to adopt the minutes, as drafted. Trustee Woodbury
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   February 19, 2004, Budget Meeting.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt the minutes, as drafted. Trustee Brower
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution No. R-02-2004 – FY 2004-05 Budget – Public Hearing & Adoption.
President Duhl stated that this resolution would adopt the annual budget for
the Fiscal Year beginning April 1, 2004, and that the Council would be
conducting a public hearing on the budget at this time. He opened the public
hearing at 7:41 p.m.
Mr. McKee reported that the budget has been available for the public to review
at the Village hall and the library since February 20, and Village staff has
met with the Council to discussed the items contained in it. He said there is a
total increase of 1.9% over last fiscal year. He pointed out two changes to the
budget document since it has been available at the library. The first is an
increase of 2% in the water rates, and the second is that there were a number
of projects that were thought would be completed by March 31st, but will not be
done until next year. He said he would be happy to answer any questions and
thanked the Council and the Department Heads for their work on the budget.
President Duhl asked if any persons in the audience wanted to address the
budget agenda item. There were none, and President Duhl closed the public
hearing at 7:44 p.m. He then turned to the Council for their comments or
questions.
Trustee Woodbury clarified that there is no change in electric rates for the
coming year. He also clarified that the figure for special services water rates
is $14.88, which is up from $14.59 over last year.
President Duhl called for a motion to adopt Resolution No. R-02-2004 adopting
the annual budget for the fiscal year beginning April 1, 2004.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt Resolution No. R-02-2004, seconded by Trustee
Brower. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Greenough, Presser and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Webster
and Abell.
b)   Rate and Fee Resolutions. President Duhl announced that the next four
resolutions are on the agenda for introduction and would set the rates for
water, sewer, electric and refuse collection services in accordance with the
Council’s discussions during the review of the preliminary budget.
   i.   Resolution No. R-03-2004 – Water Rates – Introduction. Trustee Presser
moved to introduce Resolution No. R-03-2004, which would increase water rates
by 2%, about $11 per year for a typical residential customer, seconded by
Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
   ii.   Resolution No. R-04-2004 – Electric Rates – Introduction. Mr. Williams
reported that there is no actual increase in electric rates, but the resolution
does revise the rates for space heater customers who have been charged at the
single-phase rate instead of the multi-phase rate.
Village Attorney Janega further clarified the modifications made to the
resolution, saying they affect format and contain no substantive changes. 
Trustee Greenough moved to introduce Resolution No. R-04-2004, allowing the
Village to charge all multi-phase customers $15.30 monthly, versus the $9.18
monthly charge for single-phase service, seconded by Trustee Presser. By voice
vote, the motion passed unanimously.
   iii.   Resolution No. R-05-2004 – Sewer Rates – Introduction. There being no
questions or comments, President Duhl called for a motion to introduce
Resolution R-05-2004. Trustee Brower moved to introduce Resolution No. R-05-
2004, which would increase sewer fees by 10% or about $15 more per year for a
typical customer, seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
   iv.   Resolution No. R-06-2004 – Refuse Rates – Introduction. Mr. Williams
commented that although there are no changes to the refuse rates, but this
resolution is being introduced and adopted to keep rates current annually.
There being no further comments, President Duhl called for a motion to
introduce Resolution No. R-06-2004. Trustee Woodbury moved to introduce
Resolution No. R-06-2004, which reflects no increase in refuse fees, seconded
by Trustee Brower. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-10-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1275 Scott - Introduction.
President Duhl stated that Ordinance M-10-2004 would grant zoning variances for
intensity of use of lot, maximum building size, side yard setback and building
height to allow the construction of a second floor addition.
Mr. Woodbury recused himself because the petitioner is the wife of his business
partner.
Mr. D’Onofrio stated that this request is for anumber of variations for a home
at 1275 Scott Ave. He referred to the Council’s agenda materials for a
depiction of the house and proposed plans to illustrate that, because the
basement is counted as a full story toward gross floor area, the variations
requested are not as substantial as they seem. He reviewed the increase in
square footage to accommodate the first floor reconfiguration and second story
addition and stated that the ZBA had voted 5 to 0 to recommend approval of the
variations. He added that the applicant is also planning to build a detached
garage but the garage is under the jurisdiction of the Village of Glencoe.
Architect, John Vasilion of Vasilion & Associates, representing the petitioner,
Iris McCaffery, said that they had considered removing the house and
constructing a new home, but his client was attracted to the property because
of its unique visual character. They felt it was worthwhile to pursue
preservation to the extent that they could. He said that they plan to extend
the second floor instead of having unusable attic space and will use most of
the same materials as are on the existing house. He added that the variations
were mostly due to the basement square footage that is counted toward gross
floor area and that they didn’t want to come before council with anything more
than was absolutely necessary. Mr. Vasilion further stated that the roof height
is being raised four feet and the total height would be 24 feet, which is well
under the 31-foot building height restriction.
President Duhl asked if anyone in the audience wanted to address the request
for variations, but no one came forward.
Mr. Vasilion stated that the homeowner does not have any current plans to
finish the basement, but that it might be done in the future, in response to a
question from Trustee Brower.
There being no further comments, President Duhl called for a motion to
introduce the ordinance. Trustee Greenough moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
10-2004, seconded by Trustee Presser. By the voice vote of Trustees Greenough,
Presser, Brower and President Duhl, the motion passed unanimously. Trustee
Woodbury (recused).
d)   Ordinance No. M-11-2004 – Zoning Variations: 566 Orchard - Introduction.
President Duhl announced that this ordinance would grant variations from the
intensity of use of lot, maximum building size, side yard setback, garage, and
regulations on nonconformities allow the construction of a two-story addition
at the residence.
Mr. D’Onofrio described the variations requested by the petitioners which would
add approximately 720 square feet of gross floor area in a home that currently
has nonconforming gross floor area and lot size. He said the homeowner plans to
demolish an octagon-shaped room at the rear of the house that had been added by
a previous owner and replace it with two-story addition that would create a
living room and breakfast room on the first floor and add a bedroom, bathroom
and tv room on the second floor. He noted that the home was built in 1967 and
the octagonal-shaped living room was added on in 1979. He stated that the
Zoning Board of Appeals voted 5 to 0 in favor of recommending approval for the
variations for side yard and the installation of windows and doors in the
nonconforming wall, and voted 4 to 1 in favor of the GFA and roofed lot
coverage variation requests.
Bob Acri, owner of 566 Orchard, told the Council that they had looked at
several alternative plans, taking into consideration the current ordinances and
what they wanted to do with the house. He said he had grown up in the area and
his family needs more living space for their four children. He said he feels
they have a fair plan, but after meeting with the ZBA, they know they need more
help and asked if the Council had any specific questions.
President Duhl asked for comments from the audience and there were none.
The Trustees discussed the size of the house in relation to the size of the
lot, particularly the possibility of reducing the gross floor area on the
second floor and were unable to come to a consensus to approve or deny the
request for variations.
The Council agreed to table the matter until March 16th, when they would have
the input of the Trustees who are absent.
e)   Ordinance No. M-12-2004 – Zoning Variations – 1360 Edgewood -
Introduction. President Duhl said this ordinance would grant side yard setback,
nonconformities and garage variations to permit the construction of room
additions and a second floor addition at 1360 Edgewood.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the variations are being requested to make
improvements to the home, which is currently nonconforming and is on a flag
lot. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5 to 0 to recommend
approval of the variations.
Steve Knutson, the petitioner’s architect, distributed photographs of the
existing house, explaining that the footprint of the house will essentially
stay the same, except for the new mud room addition, and that the setback
variations are requested due to an already nonconforming wall and setback. He
noted that the home will be well under the allowed roofed lot coverage and the
closest neighbor is 60 feet away, even with the setback variation. He added
that the variation from the garage door requirements is for the existing garage
door, which they are not going to replace.
President Duhl asked for comments from the audience and there were none.
Trustee Woodbury commented that, although the mud room has been pulled in
toward the house, it still doesn’t meet the setback requirements.
Trustee Brower moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-12-2004, seconded by Trustee
Woodbury. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-13-2004 – Zoning Variations – 224 Sheridan Road –
Introduction. President Duhl announced that this ordinance would grant four
zoning variations for intensity of use of lot, maximum building size, front and
corner setback, and garage provisions of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
construction of two additions on the home at 224 Sheridan Road.
President Duhl noted that the correct address is 224 Sheridan Road.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that the applicant has proposed two additions to the home: a
two-story addition at the northwest corner of the property for a gallery and
living room on the first floor; and a bedroom, bath and laundry room on the
second floor. He said the second addition is for a master bedroom at the front
of the house and that both additions would add 1,380 square feet of gross floor
area and 183 square feet of roofed lot coverage. He added that the variations
for the corner setback and garage setback are necessary, in part, because the
property has a drive that serves five houses and is thus considered a corner
lot.
In response to a question from Mr. Woodbury, Mr. D’Onofrio told the Council
that this project had been before the ZBA in November, when it received an
unfavorable vote, and has since been revised. He said that there had been some
opposition to the revised plans at the Zoning Board meeting, but that there
were letters of support as well, and the ZBA voted to recommend approval of the
variations.
Trustee Greenough asked the petitioner about the master bedroom addition and
suggested that the gross floor area could be reduced and the expansion north
into the courtyard could be reduced by seven feet by moving the master bathroom
to the south west corner over the garage. She indicated she would be in favor
of a plan like that. She also inquired as to whether any neighbors opposed the
project or would have their view obstructed.
Jeff Harding, the architect for the project, responded to Trustee Greenough’s
comments by discussing the original plan. He showed the Council a drawing of
both the original plan and the revised plan, which now reflects a design that
conforms to the Italianate architecture of the home. He said that there have
been no further objections from the neighbors about the project nor from the
neighbor, Mr. Lee, who was concerned about his view. He stated that moving the
bathroom 7 feet as suggested by Trustee Greenough would require the master
bedroom to be moved further toward the front of the house.
Trustee Greenough indicated that she would be in favor of that proposal.
President Duhl asked if anyone in the audience had any comments and there were
none.
Trustee Brower commented on the effect of the easement for the dedicated shared
private driveway on gross floor area, since the property becomes a corner lot.
Mr. Harding said the easement affects the FAR, south side yard setback, lot
coverage, and also the placement of the garage doors, because the narrow space
doesn’t allow for two 9-foot doors and a turning area.
Manager Williams urged the Council not to ignore the easement for calculating
lot coverage, because it would create a precedent.
There were no further comments and President Duhl called for a motion to
introduce. Trustee Presser moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-13-2004, seconded
by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the motion passed, with Trustee Greenough
voting against it.
7. Old Business.
a)   Findings – 630 Pine Lane Subdivision. Attorney Janega explained
Attorney Janega stated that she had prepared a confidential memo, regarding
preliminary approval of the subdivision plan for the property at 630 Pine Lane
incorporating comments from the discussions at Council meetings and case law.
She stated that case law provides that if municipal ordinances have been
complied with, the Council should approve the preliminary subdivision plan. She
said the ordinances that apply are the subdivision ordinance, zoning ordinance,
floodplain regulations and the tree preservation ordinance, as well as the
comprehensive plan. She discussed the requirements under each ordinance and the
recommendations of the Village to the applicant for compliance with the
requirements. She stated that in all instances, it appears the applicant has
complied with the ordinances.
Trustee Presser said that he had also consulted case law and had reached the
same conclusions as Ms. Janega. He added that a determining factor is whether
the subdivision alters the neighborhood locale and because we do not have a
phrase in our Subdivision Ordinance to that effect, the only choice is to grant
the subdivision.
Trustee Woodbury said that previously, Ms. Janega had counseled that
neighborhood character in this context could be the factor.
Ms. Janega replied that the guide to this is in the Comprehensive Plan as well
as the Zoning Ordinance which has a very strong statement about the
preservation of trees. She said that the Council can impose conditions to
protect the trees that would not interfere with the owner’s right to use their
property, but they must be aimed at a reasonable, public purpose.
Trustee Woodbury asked if the only tool available to protect the existing home
on the property is landmark designation and said that, although he is not in
favor of creating flag lots, it would be worth it in this instance to preserve
an historic home.
Ms. Janega replied that because there is a landmark ordinance, the Council can
require that the home be designated as a landmark as a condition of the
subdivision provided that the house warrants it. However, she said that this
condition should not be made lightly.
Trustee Presser asked where, in our ordinances, the Council has been given the
authority to make someone apply for designation of a landmark as a condition of
the subdivision.
Ms. Janega responded by saying that since there are so many things that come
into play with this property, the Council can exercise its discretion to impose
conditions for the most acceptable plan for this subdivision. She said that the
key element is to have a subdivision that does not change the character of the
neighborhood and one of the major characteristics of the parcel is the house
that sits on it and the trees surrounding it. She added that due to the
proximity of some substantial trees to the house, the way to preserve the trees
is to preserve the house.
President Duhl said that based on what he has heard, the Council can not turn
down the subdivision, but can impose conditions such as, placement of
stormwater ponds to protect trees; locating the building pad on lot 3 to
protect trees; protecting trees generally by transplanting unique species;
requiring that utility lines be put in by direct bore; the installation of a
sidewalk on Hibbard Road; landmark designation application; and size of
driveway for access by Fire Department equipment.
Ms. Janega said there are two driveway issues to consider; the first is the
driveway from Hibbard Road that requires the removal of one tree and interferes
with two others. She said the Council may want to require that the driveway
work around the trees. The second issue is on Lot 2, where access is by an
easement over the property of Mr. Block, who opposes the subdivision. She said
the drive is not wide enough to accommodate fire department vehicles and she
would suggest that the applicant provide access on their own property without
the use of an easement over the neighbor's’ property, so that the Council would
make the final determination solely within the confines of this property. She
recommended that the drive be brought towards the south so that it is entirely
on Lot 2.
Trustee Woodbury said that providing protection for trees that are 6 inches in
diameter or more instead of 10 inches would provide greater protection for
existing foliage. He also said that he understood the condition for the
placement of the building pad was for Lot 1 as well as Lot 3. He asked if the
Council can impose a restriction on Lot 1, so that a basement is not allowed or
something to that effect.
Ms. Janega replied that the floodplain ordinance allows the property owner to
excavate, put in an elevated clay building pad and a basement. She said that
she would not be comfortable with a condition that would not allow a basement
under those circumstances.
Manager Williams commented that the installation of a sidewalk on Hibbard Road
would not be connected to any other sidewalk and suggested that the developer
be asked to contribute toward the construction of a sidewalk whenever the
Village decides to construct one in that area.
Mr. Marlow, of CBI Homes, stated that he plans to replace the additions on the
existing home with additions that would be of the appropriate design and
original construction. He said that he would like the home designated as a
landmark, but does not want to be limited by the landmark designation so that
he cannot return the home to what the original architect designed.
Ms. Baron, attorney for CBI Homes, said she would like to discuss the driveway
access/easement issue further before the March 16th meeting
President Duhl asked for comments from the audience.
Mr. Andy Block, neighbor to the north of 630 Pine Lane, expressed concern about
the effect of the construction on the trees, the relocation of trees, and the
possibility of trees dying one to five years after construction. He also asked
if the Council could allow two homes instead of three and referred to an
agreement among property owners that had been in place until 1993 that showed
that the intent was to keep the property as is for a long time.
Attorney Janega replied that the Village Forester is determining the best way
to relocate and replant as many trees as possible on the property, but would
also find other places for them in the Village. She said that the trees that
are threatened by the construction would be re-evaluated based on the new plan
as well as the effect of direct boring, the swale and placement of the building
pads. Ms. Janega said that the slide show the Village Forester had prepared for
the Council’s last study session superimposed the building pad lines on the
property showing the trees that would be affected. She also said that the
Forester will set minimum distances for the building pads from the trees, which
will be one of the conditions of the resolution as well. As to the agreement
between the adjoining property owners that expired in 1993, she stated that,
while it shows the interest of prior owners, for this purpose it is moot,
because it has expired. She further stated that in regard to allowing two homes
rather than 3 homes on the property, the Council cannot impose this condition
and the purpose of the 3-lot subdivision was to preserve the existing home.
President Duhl said that the removal of even one tree from a property concerns
the Council, but the Council does not have the right to deny construction
because too many trees have to be removed. He said the Council spent a great
deal of time at the last study session trying to preserve as many trees as
possible.
Terry Westbrook, 1160 Pelham Road, who is an adjacent property owner, said he
would like to reiterate everything Mr Block said and still believes the bad
drainage problem will get worse. He said that moving the building pad for Lot 3
eight feet closer to his property will make conditions worse for him and urged
the Council to reconsider that point..
Attorney Janega replied to Mr. Westbrook’s comments by saying that, although
the building pad is shown as shifted 8 feet, setback requirements will have to
be observed. She also said that instead of the whole building pad being moved
over, it may be that the building pad can be reduced from the back and not
moved out correspondingly.
President Duhl emphasized that the setback requirements will be enforced. He
asked that Ms. Janega prepare the resolution with the conditions for discussion
at the March 16th meeting and then preliminary approval would be set for the
first meeting in April.
b)   Findings – Special Use Permit Request – 952 Green Bay Road.
Attorney Janega explained that, at the direction of the Council, she had
prepared findings of fact that support a decision to deny the special use
permit request for 952 Green Bay Road. She said that the findings contain 12
pages of detailed recitals beginning with a description of the nature of the
application, the applicant, and the property, as well as a list all of the
properties on the block on the west side of Green Bay Road, the nature of the
business and the status of the property. She identified three banks in close
proximity to the subject property, then recited a detailed procedural
background and the types of evidence that were provided. Ms. Janega also noted
that lengthy minutes for that portion of the February 17th Council meeting were
transcribed from the video tape of the meeting, and stated in the conclusions
that the sworn testimony and documentary evidence are insufficient to support
the special use. She further stated that the public records establish that 950
Green Bay Road was originally used as a medical office building and zoning
variations were granted to allow modifications to the first floor space to
convert it to retail use. The owner of that property testified at the Zoning
Board of Appeals and variation proceedings in 2001, and the south portion of
that property is now leased for retail use. Ms. Janega said that, in her
opinion, this special use is contrary to the development in the area and that
none of the evidence produced by the bank is valid. She said that three other
banks in close proximity to the subject property is a major gap in the evidence
and that other evidence the bank provided had no back up data. She said that
for the reasons stated in the findings, the Council can deny the special use.
President Duhl asked if anyone in the audience wanted to speak and no one came
forward.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt the findings, seconded by Trustee Greenough. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Duhl, Greenough,
Presser, Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Webster and Abell. Trustee
Brower (recused).
c)   Audit Contract Renewal. Ed McKee summarized the revised terms of the
contract renewal for Miller Cooper & Co. to perform the audit of the Village’s
records for the fiscal years ending 2004 and 2005. He stated that they had
taken the Village’s concerns seriously and revised the contract accordingly. He
added that they have done an excellent job for the Village and recommended that
the Council approve the terms of the modified contract.
Trustee Brower moved to approve the revised contract with Miller Cooper &
Company to perform the audit of the Village’s records for the fiscal years
ending 2004 and 2005 for the amounts of $39,350 and $40,650, respectively. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Greenough and by roll call vote, passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Webster.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1355 and 1356. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1355 in the amount of $1,063,573.40 and Warrant List No. 1356 in the
amount of $167,150.85. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Greenough,
Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Webster
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. President Duhl announced that the New Trier Boys
Swimming Team won the State Championship on Saturday and that Trustee
Woodbury’s son, Drew, is the state champion in the 100 yard Butterfly.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported that Mr. D’Onofrio met with the Chamber
of Commerce to make a presentation about the functions of the Community
Development Department and answer questions. Mr. Brower said that the comments
made at the meeting were mainly about how to increase store traffic in
Winnetka, which speaks to the need for the Business Development Commission.
11. Appointments.
a)   President Duhl nominated Molly Lien and Suzanne Timble to replace Sandra
Berger and Kevin Plunkett, whose terms on the Zoning Board of Appeals expire on
March 31, 2004. Trustee Presser moved to approve the appointment. Trustee
Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   President Duhl nominated Bernard Hammer to serve another term on the
Forestry Commission. Trustee Brower moved to approve the appointment. Trustee
Presser seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Brower
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Abell and Webster. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:33 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury. Absent: Trustees Abell
and Webster. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower,
Greenough, Woodbury, and Presser. Nays: None. Trustees Abell and Webster: The
meeting adjourned at 10:34 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
March 16, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 6, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
16, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Ed
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director
of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Director of
Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and approximately 25 persons in the audience,
including Trustee Candidates Thomas Eilers, and Herb Ritchell.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum
a)   April 6, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   April 13, 2004, Study Session. All of the ongoing Trustees present
indicated that they expected to attend, as did Trustee Candidates Eilers, and
Ritchell.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 24, 2004, Budget Meeting.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes, as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded
the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   February 26, 2004, Budget Meeting.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the minutes, as drafted. Trustee Presser
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   March 2, 2004, Regular Meeting.
Trustee Greenough asked that the minutes be amended to more accurately reflect
her comments regarding 224 Sheridan Road. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the
minutes, as amended. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Rate and Fee Resolutions. President Duhl noted that the next seven
resolutions up for adoption would set the rates for water, sewer, electric,
refuse collection, general permit and license fees, building and zoning fees,
and the fee for fire and emergency services in accordance with Council’s
discussions during the budget meetings.
   i)   Resolution No. R-03-2004 – Water Rates – Adoption. Trustee Presser
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-03-2004, which would increase water rates by
2%, about $11 per year for a typical residential customer. Trustee Abell
seconded the motion. By role call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-04-2004 – Electric Rates – Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that although there are no overall changes in the electric rates, the
restructured rate table would assure that the customer charge for all three-
phase customers would be the same, $15.30 per month. He added that a provision
has also been included that would authorize recapture agreements for certain
service extensions and upgrades. He recommended approval of these proposed
amendments.
Trustee Greenough remarked that this latter amendment is a big step forward in
terms of treating customers fairly and moved to amend the resolution as
recommended. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the resolution,
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega arrived at 7:40 p.m.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-05-2004 – Sewer Rates – Adoption. Trustee Brower,
seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-05-2004, which
would increase sewer fees by 10%, about $15 per year for a typical customer. By
roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster,
Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   iv)   Resolution No. R-06-2004 – Refuse Rates – Adoption. Trustee Abell,
seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt Resolution R-06-2004, showing no
change in refuse rates. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
   v)   Resolution No. R-7-2004 – General Permit & License Fees – Adoption.
President Duhl announced that the only change in the general fee schedule is an
increase in the annual vehicle sticker fee from $30 to $40, and noted that, as
this resolution did not require introduction, it is up for adoption at this
meeting.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution R-07-
2004, establishing the General Permit and License Fees. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Woodbury, Brower,
Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   vi)   Resolution No. R-08-2004 – Building & Zoning Fees – Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio explained that, in view of Council’s direction, in the course of the
budgeting process, to see building permit fees cover the Community Development
Department costs of operation of, the Department thoroughly examined its fee
structure. He reported that in 2002 there was an across the board increase of
10% in fees. However, since that time, the operating costs of the Department
have increased by 15.1%, and it is projected that, in the coming fiscal year,
the Department will run a deficit of approximately 15% to 20%.
As discussed during the budget meetings, Mr. D’Onofrio recommended that all
fees be increased by 15%, with the exception being the base building fee
charged for new construction, which he suggested be raised from $0.84 to $1.00
per sq. ft. for finished areas and $0.42 to $0.50 for finished areas.
With regard to Demolition Permit Fees, Mr. D’Onofrio reported that Resolution
R-08-2004 reflects Council’s decision to increase the current fee of $8,600 to
$10,100, $600 of which will continue to be earmarked for the Winnetka
Historical Society as a reimbursement to cover its costs related to demolition
requests.
Finally, regarding Stop Work Orders, Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the
resolution at hand implements a three-tiered fee structure discussed by the
Council at its budget meetings, whereby the fines for the first three stop work
orders are $250, $500 and $750, respectively.
Pat Horne, 791 Bryant, asked whether the 15% increase in building fees would
apply to the business districts as well as to the residential districts, and if
so, the reason for that. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that, as drafted, the fee
increase applies across the board.
President Duhl explained that the Village is attempting to make the Community
Development Department self-sustaining. He noted that, since most of the
Department’s revenue is generated by residential construction, the Council
would reconsider whether it can, and wants to, do anything different for the
business districts.
Trustee Brower suggested that this might be an issue for the new Business
Community Development Committee to study.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt Resolution R-08-
2004, amending the fees for building and zoning applications and for
construction activity. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   vii)   Resolution No. R-09-2004 – Annual Fee for Fire & Emergency Services –
Adoption. Manager Williams explained that this resolution would establish the
fee charged to Unincorporated New Trier Township and Kenilworth for fire and
emergency paramedic and rescue services. He reported that the fee is calculated
by a formula woven into the contract that is based on population, assessed
valuation, and the number of calls. He reported that this year, the charge
would decrease from $708.94 to $676.97, largely due to a decreased number of
calls last year.
Trustee Webster inquired whether the Unincorporated area had not decreased
substantially. Mr. Williams responded that it has, but that this won’t impact
the calculations until next year.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution R-09-
2004. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Resolution No. R-10-2004 – 630 Pine Lane Subdivision – Adoption. Attorney
Janega briefly reviewed the history of this matter. She explained that,
pursuant to the Council's discussion on March 2, the subject resolution grants
preliminary plat approval, with conditions addressing (1) modifications to the
stormwater detention area, (2) shifting the building pad on Lot 3, (3)
requiring that the unique species of trees be transplanted, (4) requiring
utilities to be installed by the direct bore method, (5) requiring submitting
the existing residence for designation as Certified Landmark, (6) construction
of a sidewalk along Hibbard Road, (7) modifying the driveway to Lot 1 to
protect additional trees, and (8) assuring that the driveway along the
"flagpole" access meets the Fire Department's emergency access requirements.
The resolution also contains detailed findings and sets out specific conditions
for final plat approval, reserves the decisions on tree removal for the Village
Council, and contains a clear statement that plat approval is not to be taken
as granting any zoning variations.
She commented that the applicant’s attorney had expressed concern about the
condition that application for landmark status be made for the residence that
currently exists on the subject property, because that would imply that the
applicant can’t make any changes to the existing house that they want to submit
to the Landmark Preservation Commission. She suggested removal of the phrase
“currently exists.”
Ms. Janega then walked the Council through the conditions to be certain that
all of the issues raised in prior discussions had been addressed. She also
reviewed the exhibits, noting that the resolution incorporates the tree survey
that had been the subject of the Village Forester's presentation and
recommended that the plat on which that survey is based also be the one for
which preliminary approval is granted. She also noted that many of the comments
contained in Exhibit C have already been resolved with the applicant and that,
as the parties proceed toward final approval, all items will, ultimately, be
determined.
Barbara Baran, attorney for the applicant, CBI Homes, thanked the Council for
its in-depth analysis, review, and attention to the matter. She urged approval
of the draft resolution so that her clients can proceed with the project,
assuring the Trustees that the applicant has every intention of meeting all of
the conditions and resolving all outstanding issues before final approval.
Andy Block, 644 Pine Lane, an adjacent property owner, reiterated previously
expressed concerns about the development. He expressed his belief that the
proposed subdivision will, in fact, substantially alter the character of the
neighborhood of Pine Lane and the R-2 zoning district.
Trustee Presser indicated that he essentially agreed with Mr. Block. He said,
however, that in view of the Village Attorney’s opinion and his own exploration
of case law, the Council does not have the discretion to turn down the
subdivision merely on the grounds that it will alter the character of the
neighborhood.
Attorney Janega explained that, by its very nature, a subdivision is going to
change the character of the neighborhood because it changes the number of lots
and, therefore, the density of housing. However, the relevant factor for the
Council’s discussion is how the Village ordinances define the character of the
neighborhood in question, which in this case is the R-2 zoning district. The
Zoning Ordinance defines this district as “semi-estate” lots of approximately
one-half acre in size. She said that, while this subdivision creates three lots
where there used to be one, each of the new lots exceeds the minimum
requirement. In her opinion, the Council has no real discretion in this matter
as it would be arbitrary to deny a subdivision that meets the standards of the
Subdivision Ordinance and in view of the fact that neighborhood character
issues are being addressed by all of the conditions imposed by the resolution.
She pointed out that the Council has never been approached to rezone this area.
In response to a question from Mr. Block about possible encroachment onto his
property, Ms. Janega said that, ordinarily, all conditions must be met on the
subdivided property without encroaching onto neighboring property.
Terry Westerbrook, 1160 Pelham Rd., echoed Mr. Block’s remarks about the
changed character of the neighborhood. In particular, he addressed his concern
that the new development plan would allow construction to take place ten feet
closer to his property in order to save three trees.
Attorney Janega explained that, despite the requirement that the building pad
be adjusted to accommodate the trees, nothing in the subject resolution grants
variations from applicable building and zoning regulations. Therefore, any
adjustments to the building pad must be made within applicable building
setbacks for the zoning district, and anything beyond that would require the
owner to go through the regular variation process.
President Duhl noted that, within the confines of the Subdivision Ordinance,
there are parameters and restrictions that are set out, and those restrictions
continue to apply and be consistent with the Zoning Ordinance. This resolution
does not grant approval to move the building on Lot 3 forward in such a way
that it would violate the established front setback requirements.
Trustee Woodbury inquired about the requirement that the applicant submit an
application for landmark status and asked how that relates to the approval of
the application.
Attorney Janega explained, and Mr. Norkus confirmed, that staff anticipates the
two processes will run in tandem.
President Duhl pointed out that the Council does not have the ability to delay
final approval until the conclusion of the landmark process, but expressed
confidence that final approval would not occur until the Council is assured
that the landmark process is proceeding in good faith and in a timely fashion.
Attorney Janega suggested possibly inserting a condition that states that,
while the Council does not drive the landmark process, the process must run its
course before final approval of the plat.
Ms. Baran expressed concern about such a provision, because the developer
cannot control the landmark designation process, and that making final approval
dependent upon the conclusion of that process could slow the developer down
considerably. She asked the Trustees not to tie final subdivision approval to
the final decision of the Landmark Preservation Commission.
President Duhl noted that, so long as the Council has the discretion to set
final approval on its own agenda, the Council can take into account how the
Landmark process is proceeding and whether it believes the condition has been
met.
Trustee Presser suggested that the Village Attorney add language to the effect
that the applicant “submits and pursues the application for landmark status in
good faith.”
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to amend Resolution No. R-
10-2004 in accordance with the Village Attorney’s recommendations and the above
discussion. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
10-2004, as amended, granting preliminary approval of the CBI Subdivision. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury, Brower, Presser,
Abell, Greenough and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. Trustee Webster
abstained on the grounds that he had missed many of the previous discussions on
this matter.
c)   Ordinance No. M-10-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1275 Scott - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the shell ordinance that was
introduced at the March 2nd meeting, fleshing out the practical difficulties
and particular hardships related to the subject property.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance as
proposed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-10-
2004, as amended, granting Intensity of Use of Lot, Maximum Building Size, Side
Yard Setback, and Building Height variations to allow the construction of a
second floor addition. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None. Trustee Woodbury recused himself from voting on this matter.
d)   Ordinance No. M-12-2004 – Zoning Variations – 1360 Edgewood - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the shell ordinance that was
introduced at the March 2nd meeting, fleshing out the practical difficulties
and particular hardships related to the subject property.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to amend the ordinance as
proposed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
12-2004, as amended, granting Side Yard Setback, Nonconformities, and Garage
variations to permit the construction of room additions and a second floor
addition. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-13-2004 – Zoning Variations – 224 Sheridan Road –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the shell ordinance
that was introduced at the March 2nd meeting.
Trustee Greenough reiterated her concerns about the request for a GFA variance,
saying that she believed similar results could be achieved by minor adjustments
to the proposed plan that in her opinion disrupts the harmony of the
subdivision.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to amend the ordinance as
proposed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
13-2004, as amended, granting Intensity of Use of Lot, Maximum Building Size,
Front and Corner Setback, and Garage variations to permit the construction of
two additions. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Woodbury,
Brower, Webster, Presser, and Abell. Nays: Trustee Greenough. Absent: None.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-02-2004 – Zoning Ordinance – Amendments. Attorney Janega
explained that this ordinance, which was introduced on February 3, 2004, is the
culmination of Council’s numerous discussions concerning ways in which the
zoning variation process could be streamlined, and evaluating some single-
family residential zoning regulations. These include such issues as building
height, attic gross floor area allowances, building line articulation, open
porches and lot coverage of impermeable surfaces in front yards. As a result of
these discussions, she proposed several amendments for purposes of clarifying
the ordinance as introduced, including the addition of definitions and further
refinement of the GFA provisions.
Trustee Presser asked Mr. Saunders whether he was comfortable with the proposed
amendments regarding impervious surface. Mr. Saunders said that he had reviewed
the status of the Elm Street property but still had some concerns.
Ms. Janega noted that, while there appear to be some ambiguities in the
language with regard to how the Community Development Department distinguishes
between balconies and porches for purposes of calculating GFA, she believes
that the proposed language correctly reflects the Department’s intentions. She
also pointed out that, with regard to the window well provisions, the
application materials will include clear illustrations.
Responding to Ms. Janega’s remarks about the porch/balcony issue, Mr. Norkus
expressed some concern about the complexity of the language.
In light of Mr. Norkus’ comments, President Duhl suggested that the Council
amend the draft ordinance as proposed by Attorney Janega, with the exception of
the language pertaining to the porch/balcony issue, and that staff resolve any
outstanding concerns and return with the final proposal at the next meeting.
Referring to the table on page 14 of the draft ordinance, Trustee Woodbury
raised a question about the amount of authority granted to the ZBA for deciding
side yard variations. He voiced his belief that, contrary to the draft, the
Council had decided that that authority not exceed 50% in any instance. The
other Trustees concurred, and Ms. Janega indicated that she would amend the
draft accordingly.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to amend the draft as
proposed by the Village Attorney and discussed above. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
Adoption of this ordinance was set for the next Council meeting.
g)   Ordinance No. M-11-2004 – Zoning Variations: 566 Orchard - Introduction.
President Duhl noted that this ordinance would grant Intensity of Use of Lot,
Maximum Building Size, Side Yard Setback, Garage, and Nonconformities
variations to permit the construction of a two-story addition. He reported that
the matter had been tabled at the March 2nd meeting because there had been no
consensus among the Trustees present at the meeting and two were not present.
He called for a motion to remove the matter from the table. Trustee Woodbury so
moved, seconded by Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the requested variations and, as had been requested at
the March 2nd meeting, he distributed an analysis of GFA variations granted in
2003.
Attorney Janega explained that the ordinance had been amended to include
findings for the first floor variation requests in keeping with the findings of
the Zoning Board, noting that there was nothing in the evidentiary record or in
the Zoning Board's discussion that addressed the variation requests for the
second floor space.
Bob Acri, the applicant, spoke in support of his application, noting that
numerous options had been considered to meet the family’s need for additional
living space without creating an eyesore or something contrary to the desires
of the community.
Ann Clark, the architect, reviewed elevations and a massing model to illustrate
the proposed additions and the impact on neighboring properties. She also
reviewed several of the alternatives considered by the applicants.
Trustee Greenough remarked that she is in total agreement with the plan to
rework the first floor, but has several concerns regarding the expansion of the
second floor to include a fifth bedroom, and additional bath and living space.
She stated that she can’t see any practical difficulty with what already exists
on the second floor.
Ms. Clark responded that the proposed new space would provide communal living
space for the family on the second floor.
Trustee Abell, who stated that he had missed the discussion at the last
meeting, expressed concern about the overall bulk of the proposal on a very
small lot. He voiced a preference for only a one-story addition.
Sam Fifer, 560 Orchard, spoke in support of the proposal, but expressed some
concern about its impact on drainage.
Trustee Presser inquired whether Mr. Fifer was concerned about the bulk. Mr.
Fifer responded that, while he was aware of it, he believed the proposed design
to be an improvement to the overall neighborhood.
Bill Frericks, the neighbor to the west at 570 Orchard, expressed concern about
the added GFA, noting that it adds more problems to an already nonconforming
lot. Referring to the drafter’s note on p. 178, which says that “the record
does not contain any evidence for findings pertaining to the second floor of
the proposed addition,” he said that the fifth bedroom and additional living
space are not standard for the neighborhood or for the size of the lot. He
expressed concern that these additions would set a precedent for similar lots
in the neighborhood. He also voiced objections based on standards 2, 4, and 5
of the Standards for Granting of Zoning Variations, in particular that the
variation is sought not to meet hardships associated with the characteristics
of the property but, rather, circumstances related to the occupants. Mr.
Frericks also commented on the impact of this proposed addition on his future
plans for expanding his home.
Geoffrey Heekin, 575 Orchard, echoed Mr. Frericks' concern about setting a
precedent, noting that these lots are small and constrained and that if the
Council grants one exception, others will ask for similar consideration in
order to accommodate changes in family needs.
Mr. Acri responded to these comments by saying that this is the first time in
the course of the process that neighbors have expressed any objections. He
explained that, from an aesthetic standpoint, a one-story addition does not
work well.
Attorney Janega explained that the purpose of the variation process is to
permit additions that make the living of one property similar to that of other
homes in the Village with similar conditions. Typically, GFA variations have
been granted on the first floor to permit the addition of a family room, larger
kitchen, mud room, etc. and on the second floor to permit the addition of a
master suite. She noted that this house already has four bedrooms on the second
floor, including a master suite. She said that during her tenure, this Council
has never allowed a GFA variation to allow this kind of expansion in the R-
5 zoning district and that it would set a precedent indicating that the Council
considers 5 bedrooms and 4 baths to be typical amenities on the second floor in
the R-5 zoning district. She cautioned the Council against such a conclusion,
especially because of the absence of Zoning Board discussion and a lack of
supporting findings.
Trustee Greenough commented that, in the R-5 Zoning District, typically
necessary amenities amount to four bedrooms, including a master bedroom suite
on the second floor. She expressed her belief that the one-story addition to
bring the home up to current standards on the first floor was reasonable and
said that she would support it as drafted. She indicated, however, that she
would not support a two-story addition.
Trustee Presser indicated that, although he liked the look of the proposed
plan, in view of the neighbor’s opposition, he would only be in favor of
approving a one-story addition, as included in the draft ordinance.
Trustee Abell noted that, in the absence of a proposal for a one-story addition
that the applicant would find acceptable, it would be difficult to vote for the
ordinance as drafted.
Trustee Woodbury indicated that he was in favor of introducing the ordinance as
proposed and giving the applicant the intervening time between meetings to
flesh out the proposal.
Trustee Brower said that, despite his previous support of the proposal, in view
of the neighbors’ objections and the possibility of setting a precedent, he now
could only support a one-story addition, as included in the proposed ordinance.
Trustee Webster expressed his general philosophic view pertaining to existing
rules when residents move into their neighborhoods. He said that residents rely
on established rules and regulations when they make a decision to move into any
given neighborhood. When one neighbor wants to change those established rules
and regulations, he is attempting to change the assumptions and living
conditions of his neighbors, akin to changing the rules of the game in the
middle of the game. Generally speaking, he voiced opposition to changing rules
if those changes adversely affect neighboring properties. Therefore, he would
be opposed to the requested two-story addition.
Hearing a consensus among the Trustees to introduce the ordinance, as drafted,
reflecting only a one-story addition, President Duhl called for a motion.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce the ordinance
granting roofed lot coverage, GFA, side yard setback, enlargement of windows
and doors in a nonconforming wall, and garage door width requirements. By voice
vote, the motion carried, with Trustee Abell voting against it.
Manager Williams advised the applicant that the matter would be up for adoption
at the April 6th meeting unless the applicants advise the Village that they do
not want to proceed.
Ms. Clark inquired if there is a definition of “living space” for this zoning
district.
Attorney Janega responded that when the Zoning Ordinance was revised a number
of years ago, it was done to take total exterior bulk into consideration, as
opposed to a particular definition of the kinds of space.
President Duhl confirmed that the issue here is not the nature of the space,
but the total size of the space, and Trustee Woodbury pointed out that the
Council, as the policy-making body in the Village, has to look at the matter
from a precedent setting standpoint, whereas the Zoning Board of Appeals does
not.
h)   Ordinance No. MC-03-2004 – Establishing Business Community Development
Committee – Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio briefly reviewed the history of this
matter and explained that this ordinance would establish and define the powers
and duties of a permanent Business Community Development Commission, as
recommended by the Ad Hoc Committee for Business Development.
Trustee Woodbury raised a question about the size of the committee and the
frequency of the meetings.
After a brief discussion, there was a consensus that the meetings should be
held at least quarterly, or at the call of the chairman.
Attorney Janega noted that the Village Code does not set the frequency of
meetings for similar boards and commissions and suggested that a specific
meeting schedule not be set by ordinance.
In response to a question from Pat Horne, Manager Williams indicated that Mr.
D’Onofrio would be staffing this committee and that $30,000 had been set aside
in the budget to enable the committee to hire an outside consultant, a
specialist in economic development, if it chooses to do so.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance No. MC-
03-2004. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
i)   Ordinance No. MC-04-2004 – Amending Construction Regulations –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that, recently, issues have been raised
relating to the demolition of homes and excavation for new homes, two aspects
of construction that have a significant impact on neighbors because they
generate a lot of noise, truck traffic, dust, and dirt. The proposed amendment
to the Building Code would prohibit demolition and excavation on residential
projects on Saturdays.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. MC-04-2004. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
7. Old Business.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1357 and 1358. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1357 in the amount of $567,505.35 and Warrant List No. 1358 in the
amount of $462,304.75. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Outdoor Seating Permits. Manager Williams noted that applications for
Outdoor Seating Permits were received from the following restaurants: Caffe
Buon Giorno, Caribou Coffee, Corner Cooks, Little Ricky’s, Panera, Song
O’Sixpence, Starbucks, and Sweet Shop. In addition, applications for Sidewalk
Restaurant Liquor License Riders were received from Corner Cooks, Little
Ricky’s and Song O’Sixpence. Pursuant to the Village Code, the Village Council
must approve these applications.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the applications
for Outdoor Seating Permits conditioned upon Staff’s approval of seating
layouts. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to approve the applications
for Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License Riders. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   Recycling Strategy Discussion. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village’s
current recycling contract with Onyx Waste Services, Inc., expires on June 30,
2004, and that the Village is developing a Request for Proposals to obtain a
new recycling agreement. It is anticipated that these proposals will be
received by May 1st. In the interim, Onyx has expressed a willingness to extend
the current contract for an additional three months. Mr. Saunders briefly
reviewed the proposed provisions of the draft RFP, including:
   i)   maintaining once-per-week curbside collection of the current material
list, plus Nos.3 to 5 plastics;
   ii)   maintaining the existing contract term of five (5) years;
   iii)   proposing that collection be accomplished via a single, wheeled
toter, into which residents could deposit all recyclable materials without
presorting;
   iv)   proposing that initial purchase of toters be done by the hauler, with
the Village assuming ownership and maintaining a stock for replacements;
   v)   requesting proposed alternative collection schedules, one keeping the
current schedule and one matching the refuse collection schedule.
Manager Williams noted that all of the surrounding communities are moving in
the same direction with their recycling. He mentioned that the color of the
proposed toter would be an issue that would probably have to go before the
Design Review Board. He said that, as a part of these discussions, other
changes to the garbage/recycling collection process will be considered, but no
decisions will be made without a lot of public process.
There was a consensus to move forward with this proposed Recycling Contract
RFP.
d)   GIS Annual Services Contract. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village is a
member of the Geographic Information Systems Consortium (GISCon), a public
entity formed in 1999 to help small- to-medium-sized communities meet the
challenges of developing effective information system solutions. As such, the
Village is to enter into a service agreement with GISCon’s selected service
provider, Municipal GIS Partners, Inc., to provide GIS development services.
Noting that the Village is in year 2 of a five-year plan for development,
implementation and management of a fully operational GIS, he recommended that
the Village authorize an agreement with Municipal GIS Partners at an hourly
rate, not to exceed $44,430.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to enter into the service
agreement as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
e)   Highway Authority Agreement. Mr. McInturff explained the necessity for a
Memorandum of Agreement with the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency
pertaining to an August leak of oil into a storm sewer from a manhole in the
intersection of Tower and Sheridan Roads that has been cleaned up and repaired
by constructing a barrier around the affected area. The Agreement provides that
the Village will maintain the engineered barrier and that if the Village has to
break into this space, all materials removed will be dealt with in an
environmentally acceptable method and the barrier will be reinstalled.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to authorize execution of the
Memorandum of Agreement with the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Presser, Abell,
Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Manager Williams referred the
Trustees to the departments’ capital improvements reports contained in the
Agenda packet. He said that the Village accomplished all that was set out to do
last year, on to next year.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
Bill Frericks, who participated in the discussion about 566 Orchard, inquired
whether there was an anonymous process by which a neighbor could voice an
objection to a variation request so as to avert an adversarial relationship
between neighbors.
Acknowledging that this is a difficult and uncomfortable situation, President
Duhl responded that the best method is to confront the matter openly and
honestly in a public hearing, where an individual’s appearance does make an
impression.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Brower reported that the Chamber of Commerce was
receiving responses to their survey about what Winnetkans want in their
business districts and encouraged listeners to pick up a form at a local
business and complete it.
Trustee Abell reported that he had attended a RED Center meeting and was very
impressed by the operation. He suggested that the Village encourage them to
amend their current medical benefit plan for their employees to better reflect
market trends.
Manager. Manager Williams announced that the Canvassing Board would meet on
Thursday, March 18th at 4:00 p.m.
11. Appointments.
a)   President Duhl suggested the reappointment of Sandra Berger and Richard
Glenn to the Police Pension Board for additional two-year terms. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Brower, so moved. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
He then suggested the appointment of Hugh Brower to the Canvassing Board.
Trustee Woodbury moved to approve the appointment, seconded by Trustee
Greenough. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12.   Executive Session. Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive
Session for the purpose of discussing pending and probable litigation,
personnel matters, and board appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and
Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Presser seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser,
Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 10:53 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:28 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower,
Greenough, Woodbury, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 11:29 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
April 6, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 20, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
6, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Hugh Brower, Nan Greenough, Stephen Presser, David Webster, and Ed
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director
of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Police
Chief Joe DeLopez, and approximately 18 persons in the audience, including
Trustees-elect Thomas Eilers, Jessica Tucker, and Herb Ritchell.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 16, 2004, Regular Meeting.
Trustee Presser moved to adopt the minutes, as amended. Trustee Abell seconded
the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
5. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-02-2004 – Zoning Ordinance Amendments – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the last proposed amendments to the draft ordinance
pertaining to the table delineating the scope of the ZBA’s decision-making
authority over variation requests and questions regarding how area under an
open porch is used to calculate GFA. She reported that the former matter has
been addressed by revising the table to limit the ZBA’s authority over side
yard setback variations to a maximum of 50% of the required setback. With
regard to the second issue, however, she said that it is the consensus of the
Village Attorney, the Community Development Director, and the Village Planner
that the initial disparity in the impact of balconies on gross floor area arose
from an interpretation by the prior Director of Community Development and that
these are best addressed by administratively rescinding that interpretation.
Accordingly, she recommended that the “balcony amendments” to the definition of
gross floor area be stricken from the final draft of the ordinance and a
corresponding recital added.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to amend the draft as
proposed by the Village Attorney and discussed above. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Brower, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Woodbury, Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-03-2004 – Establishing Business Community Development
Commission – Adoption. Ms. Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft
ordinance adding the Director of Community Development as a non-voting member
of the Commission and clarifying that the Director’s presence or absence does
not count toward a quorum and that only the nine appointed members may vote.
She called attention to the fact that the ordinance calls for three (3) members
of the Commission to be from the local retail community.
With regard to the latter, Trustee Brower remarked that that accurately
reflected his recollection of the Ad Hoc Committee’s recommendations.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the draft as
proposed by the Village Attorney and discussed above. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Brower, Webster, Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
President Duhl announced that he would be appointing the Commission’s members
at the next Council meeting.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-04-2004 – Amending Construction Regulations – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that the proposed amendment would further limit
construction hours so that demolition and excavation work on new homes is
prohibited on Saturdays. This would allow for improved response time from
Community Development staff in the event of a problem and result in less
disruption in neighborhoods on weekends. The amendment would not limit other
types of new home construction activity on Saturdays.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to amend the draft as
proposed by the Village Attorney and discussed above. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Presser, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-11-2004 – Zoning Variations: 566 Orchard - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed discussions between the Village and the applicant since the
ordinance including language permitting only a one-story addition was
introduced on March 16th.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the ordinance, including the
addition of a recital noting that the applicants have modified their
application to eliminate the construction of the second floor addition, thereby
reducing the scope of the variation and reducing the requested gross floor area
variation.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to amend the draft as
proposed by the Village Attorney and discussed above. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
Trustee Presser, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Presser, Abell, Greenough, Woodbury, Brower, Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Duhl and Trustee Woodbury each expressed their thanks to the
applicant for his cooperation and willingness to compromise.
e)   Resolution No. R-17-2004 – Duke Childs Field – Dugout Landscaping –
Adoption. Mr. Norkus briefly reviewed the conditions attached to Ordinance M-
12-2002, granting a Special Use Permit to New Trier High School allowing
construction of new dugouts at Duke Childs Field. Among the conditions imposed
was one stating that the final landscaping plan would be subject to approval by
the Council.
The plan submitted by New Trier incorporates ivy on three of the four dugouts,
as well as a proposed planting plan for the berm between Willow Road and the
paved parking lot. In addition, New Trier proposed to plant the berm with a
combination of shrubs, perennials, and ornamental grasses. However, because of
its proximity to the parking lot, the south dugout cannot accommodate the use
of ivy. Therefore, New Trier proposed to plant the berm adjacent to Willow
Road.
Mr. Norkus reported that the Design Review Board had commented favorably on New
Trier’s design, but had recommended that an attempt be made to grow vines at
the south dugout.
In addition, Mr. Norkus reported that the Public Works Director had reviewed
the proposed landscaping plan to ensure that sight lines remain clear and
approved it.
Manager Williams confirmed with Mr. Norkus that the Village Forester had also
approved the proposed plan.
John Nieweem, Director of Physical Services for New Trier High School,
commented briefly on the High School’s plans.
Trustee Brower inquired whether there would be ivy on the girls’ dugouts as
well. Mr. Nieween responded affirmatively.
Trustee Brower, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Brower, Webster,
Presser, Abell, Greenough, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-18-2004 – Approval of Plat: 518 Winnetka Avenue -
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that this is a final housekeeping matter in
the development of this property. The proposed resolution approves the proposed
plat of subdivision as the first step of a three-step process needed to place
the owner of the commercial unit on equal footing with the residential portion
of the development. She explained that the developer proposes to separate the
single commercial unit from the condominium association by carving out the
commercial space into one parcel (Lot 2) and leaving the remainder of the
building and the underlying real estate and property improvements in the other
parcel (Lot 1). The condominium declaration and by-laws will be recorded
against Lot 1, and a separate Declaration of Covenants, Conditions,
Restrictions and Easements will be recorded against both lots, assuring that
they are jointly subject to all applicable restrictions on the property as a
whole.
She noted that the proposed resolution contains recitals defining the
conditions imposed by the original Ordinance and by the Development Agreement
and findings that the proposed documents comply with those conditions.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the resolution
approving the Winnetka Belvedere Subdivision and finding that the proposed
condominium declarations and restrictions comply with Ordinance No. M-16-2000
and the Development Agreement between the Village and the developer. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough,
Woodbury, Brower, Webster, and Presser. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6. Old Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1359 and 1360. Trustee Brower reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1359 in the amount of $1,558,786.76 and Warrant List No. 1360 in the
amount of $377,961.42. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough,
Presser, Webster, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Village Yards Status Report. Mr. Saunders reported that the project, which
is basically complete except for a few outstanding “punch list” items, came in
about $230,000 below budget. He mentioned that the Village would be going out
to bid on a couple of the items that were removed in the value engineering
process, such as resurfacing garage floors and replacing garage doors, and that
he hoped that these could be added back into the total package.
Manager Williams thanked the Trustees for their support throughout this major
project and Mr. Saunders for his excellent supervision of the project.
Trustee Woodbury thanked Mr. Williams and Mr. Saunders for their fine
management of this project.
7. Seating of the New Village Council
a)   Local Canvass Board: Canvass and Proclamation and Administration of Oath
of Office to Trustees-elect.
There being no further unfinished business for the sitting Council, President
Duhl asked the Village Clerk to review the results of the Local Canvass Board.
Village Clerk Williams read the results of the March 16, 2004 election. He
reported that the Board had proclaimed Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker duly elected to first terms as Village Trustees. He administered the
Oath of Office to Trustees Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Commendation Resolutions – Adoption
   i)   Resolution No. R-13-2004 – Honoring Trustee Brower for Village Service.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-14-2004 – Honoring Trustee Presser for Village
Service.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-15-2004 – Honoring Trustee Woodbury for Village
Service.
President Duhl read aloud the resolutions honoring Trustees Brower, Presser,
and Woodbury and thanked each for their service to the community.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
13-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
14-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
15-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Each of the retiring Trustees remarked briefly on his experience in office.
b)   Resolution No. R-16-2004 – Extending Sidewalk Contract – Adoption. Mr.
Saunders explained that in 2003 the Village had entered into a contract with
Suburban Concrete, Inc. to provide sidewalk replacement services. The contract
contained a provision allowing for a maximum of three one-year extensions, at
an amended rate of change based on the Chicagoland CPI for a specified period.
The CPI rate of change for the specified period was +1.5% to which the
contractor agreed. He recommended approving this resolution authorizing the
extension of the contract with Suburban Concrete.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Webster, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Request for Motion Picture Filming in Village – Ocean’s 12. Mr. Saunders
explained a request from Warner Bros. – Ocean’s 12 for a commercial film permit
pursuant to the provisions of the Village Code to film in three locations in
the Village. He then reviewed the provisions and requirements that must be met
for the permit to be granted and commented on the applicant’s provision
therefore. He reported that the applicant has met all of the application
requirements, and it is staff’s determination that the application is complete
and proper.
Chief DeLopez addressed the traffic closure, public safety, and staff
deployment aspects of the proposal, saying that the Village’s chief goal is to
be certain that there is sufficient personnel to ensure the a safe environment
for children, shoppers, pedestrians, and commuters. He noted that the Village
had reached out to the neighborhood schools as well as merchants and received
very favorable comment back from all
Mike Dunitz, Location Manager for the movie, and Bob Hudgins, Asst. Director of
the Illinois Film Office, spoke in support of the application and thanked
Village for all of the staff time devoted to working out the necessary
logistics to make proposal doable.
Trustee Tucker expressed support for the proposal saying that although
disruptive, it provides an exciting learning experience for the children.
Trustee Ritchell spoke to the professionalism of the filmmakers and the amount
of work that they have already expended in getting this far in the process. He
asked Mr. Williams if any objections had been voiced. Mr. Williams responded
that staff had received none, other than those included in the Agenda packet.
Trustee Greenough asked about the economic impact on Village businesses. Mr.
Hudgins informed the Trustees that the filmmakers had been in contact with all
of the affected businesses about compensation and that they would suffer no
loss. He expressed his belief that any impact will be positive.
Trustee Eilers said that he felt that this activity was good for the community
and tht he was in favor of it given that the neighbors and merchants had been
notified and there were only two objections voiced and those concerns were
being respected.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved that Warner Bros. – Ocean’s
12 be granted a Commercial Filming Permit contingent upon the receipt of final
documentation and an up-front deposit of the Village’s estimated costs, plus a
contingency of 25%. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Eilers, Tucker, Webster, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Bid: Tree Trimming, Removal, and Maintenance. Mr. Saunders reviewed bids
for parkway tree trimming removal and maintenance. He recommended awarding the
bids, as follows: Parkway tree and stump removal to Arbor Green, Inc., in the
amount of $28,559; parkway tree trimming to Steve Piper Tree Care, Inc., in the
amount of $15,781; and emergency time and material to Arbor Green, Inc. at
hourly rates contained in the bid proposal.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eiler, Tucker, Webster, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Bid: 2004 Parkway Tree Planting. Mr. Saunders reviewed bids for parkway
tree planting. He explained that the bids were determined based upon the price
bid for each species of tree and that references were required as a part of the
bidding process. He reported that staff recommends awarding the contract for FY
2004-05 to St. Aubin Nursery for an amount not to exceed $48,544.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Webster, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
11. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Abell reported that he had accompanied a police officer
on his rounds last Friday night and been impressed by the professionalism of
the Department.
b)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Winnetka Report would soon be
in residents’ mailboxes. He also announced that tours of Village facilities
have been scheduled for 8:45 am., April 14 and April 21, and that anyone who
wished to join the new Trustees on these tours should meet at Village Hall.
12. Appointments.
a)   President Duhl suggested the appointment of Carole Smith as the Plan
Commission’s representative to the Forestry Commission. Trustee Greenough,
seconded by Trustee Abell, so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
He then suggested the appointment of Stephen Presser to replace Don Derning on
the Board of Police and Fire Commissions for the duration of Mr. Derning’s term
and a full term of his own. Trustee Tucker moved to approve the appointment,
seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl then nominated Trustee Nan Greenough to serve as President Pro
Tem for the coming year. Trustee Eilers moved to approve the appointment,
seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Finally, President Duhl announced the Trustee assignments for the coming year,
as follows:
                                          2004-05
