Recording Secretary
April 13, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Adopted May 4, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
13, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development, Mike D’Onofrio,
Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Deputy Chief of Police Eric Bennett and two
persons in the audience. The Zoning Board of Appeals members who were present
were Margaret Benson, Mark Crane, Suzanne Timble, Molly Lien, and Jane Dowding.
2)   Zoning Seminar with Zoning Board of Appeals.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega conducted a seminar and discussion for the
benefit of the newly elected Trustees and Zoning Board of Appeals members. She
spoke about land use regulation, statutory regulatory powers, planning and
zoning administration, Village objectives and the practical application of the
Village ordinances to zoning variation requests and code amendments. She
explained the roles of the Zoning Board and the Village Council, their
respective responsibilities during meetings and hearings and the scope of their
decision-making authority. Ms. Janega also discussed recent decisions from the
Illinois Supreme Court and appellate courts and their impact on zoning
procedures.
3)   Proposed Amendment to False Alarm Ordinance.
Chief DeLopez presented a report and proposed code amendments pertaining to
false alarms. He first asked the Council to recall that, during budget
preparation meetings earlier this year he had discussed amending the ordinance
that imposes fees for the number of false alarms. He then reported the results
of a survey his staff had taken of the fees charged by other North Shore
communities. Chief DeLopez recommended that the fee for 10 false alarms or more
be raised from $150 to $200 per false alarm and that only 2 free false alarms
be allowed per year, instead of 2 every 6 months. He presented the Council with
statistics on the number of false alarms vs. the number of alarms that have
actually occurred due to a burglary or other type of trouble at a home. He also
described the type of alarm that has a digital dialing system and noted it is
the least desirable from the Police Department’s perspective and is the most
easily circumvented by burglars. He recommended that these types of alarms not
be allowed to connect to the Police Department’s alarm panel after January 1,
2005. He said that it is the intent of the Police Department to reduce the
number of false alarms by asking that homeowners keep their alarms in good
repair and maintain a security system that is reliable so that when an alarm is
sounded, it is reasonable to expect some type of help is needed at the address.
After discussing the recommendations of Chief DeLopez, the Trustees agreed to
raise the fine for 10 or more false alarms and to require that digital dialing
systems will not be accepted for connection to the Department’s alarm panel
after January 1, 2005. The Trustees asked that each of the homeowners who have
this type of alarm system be notified by the Police Department of the proposed
change in the ordinance.
Trustee Tucker requested that it be stated that the purpose and intent of the
amendments to the ordinance is to reduce false alarms and increase the safety
of residents through means of a reliable security system.
Trustee Abell ascertained from Chief DeLopez that his staff would work with
those homeowners who wish to modify their alarm systems in order to comply with
the change in the ordinance. He also asked if the Fire Department would want to
consider revising the fees charged for false fire alarms.
Manager Williams agreed to discuss this with Chief Colpaert and report back to
the Council.
4)   Parking: General Discussion.
a)   Overnight parking. Chief DeLopez stated that the Police Department’s
enforcement of overnight parking restrictions has been met with resistance from
some residents and that, during a 7-month period in 2002, the Department had
received over 5000 requests from residents for permission to park overnight on
the street due to special circumstances. Because of these two factors, he had
conducted an experiment to allow residents on two streets near New Trier to
park cars in front of their homes overnight as long as the vehicles had a valid
Village sticker registered to that address. He reported that this had worked
well and asked that the community and the Council let his Department know how
to proceed regarding overnight parking.
b)   Indian Hill. Chief DeLopez reported that his officers have been monitoring
the train lot at Indian Hill because of complaints that New Trier students are
using the commuter parking spaces. He said that daily monitoring requires about
5 hours of staff time but did reduce student use. He proposed three separately
designated areas: one for commuter use requiring a C parking pass from 7 am. to
5 p.m.; another for students; and the third area would be a hybrid of students
and commuters for those adults who want to park a car for use by a student
later in the day. He proposed 10 hybrid parking spaces, which would be
permitted for a one-year term at the high school student rate of $1,000 per
year. He said the permit applications would set out both the use and the
ramifications of misuse, of the parking spaces. Chief DeLopez recommended a
one-year pilot program. He also recommended that the signs at the Indian Hill
lot be changed to make regulations clearer.
Manager Williams commented that the Chief’s recommendation is an attempt at
compromise in the interest of being fair to create areas for all types of users
of the lot.
The Council discussed the parking areas for students and commuters and
expressed some concerns about whether charging the same amount for the hybrid
spaces as for the student pass would encourage students not to seek a student
pass. The Trustees also spoke briefly about revisiting the idea of a parking
deck and asked whether there was any possibility for expansion at New Trier for
parking.
Manager Williams suggested that the Village seek input from the Caucus on Chief
DeLopez’s proposal to designate areas for commuters, students and hybrid
parking for a one year period with appropriate passes and fees required.
Manager Williams also suggested that the signage for the existing regulations
be clarified, as recommended by Chief DeLopez.
The Council agreed and directed Manager Williams to prepare proposed caucus
questions for their review and to proceed with upgrading the signage in the
Indian Hill lot.
5)   Manager’s Report.
a)   Mr. Williams advised the Council that the company that has requested a
film permit has requested an additional day and location for filming on Walden
Lane.
b)   Mr. Williams distributed the Council Handbook and reviewed the contents
with the Trustees.
c)   Mr. Williams announced that there would be a tour of the Public Works and
Police Departments April 14th for the newly elected Trustees.
6)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
7)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:50 p.m.
      
   Deputy Clerk
April 20, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 4, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
20, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and
approximately nine persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 8(f) Landscape Maintenance Bids had been
removed from the Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   May 4, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
the expected to attend.
b)   May 11, 2004, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
the expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 6, 2004, Regular Meeting.
President Duhl referred the Trustees to an amended page from the draft minutes
reflecting a change suggested by Trustee Eilers. Trustee Greenough moved to
adopt the minutes, as amended. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-14-2004 – Zoning Variations: 1117 Merrill - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot and Side Yard
Setback variations to permit the construction of two one-story additions and a
second floor addition at the subject property. He reported that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 6 to 0 to recommend approval of the variations.
Trustee Greenough remarked that she believed the variations to be appropriate
because most houses on this block are two-story and these alterations will make
the interior more livable.
Trustee Eilers commented that he was in favor of the variations. He said that
he believes they are good for the Village because they are an improvement to
the existing housing stock without exceeding the maximum allowable GFA
allowance.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-15-2004 – Special Use Permit: 490 Hibbard – Introduction/
Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request from the Winnetka Park District for
an amendment to the existing Special Use Permit to allow for the construction
of a paddle tennis building at the Skokie Playfield platform tennis center. The
new 1,439 square foot facility would replace the existing 496 square foot
paddle hut building and include a meeting room/viewing room, restrooms, a
kitchen, and a new surrounding deck. He reported that the Plan Commission had
voted affirmatively that the paddle hut improvement is consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan. However, they did voice concern over the increased size and
improved amenities, which might be used for non-paddle tennis activities,
thereby causing a parking problem for other patrons of the golf and tennis
facilities.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, and Gary Frank, the architect, were
present to answer questions from the Trustees. Ms. McElroy asked that the
Council waive the first reading of the ordinance so that the Park District can
begin and complete construction as soon as possible. She noted that the paddle
season begins in the fall.
Ms. McElroy addressed the parking concerns expressed by the Plan Commission and
said that she did not believe that there would be a problem because there is
little overlap of seasons and attendance at the Golf Course has decreased
anyway.
Noting that Skokie Playfields is an entryway feature to the Village, Trustee
Ritchell commented that he would like to see an effort made by the Park
District to look at the overall picture of this recreational campus when
designing new facilities so as to avoid a hodgepodge of building designs. Ms.
McElroy commented that the Park District tries to be as consistent as possible
with existing buildings but said that each facility’s design is largely
dictated by its use.
Trustee Tucker asked whether the Paddle Tennis Club, which is donating
extensively to the project, or the Park District would own the building. Ms.
McElroy said that the Park District would own the facility.
Attorney Janega explained to the Trustees the normal process for passing an
ordinance, noting the provision allowing the Council to waive the first reading
in an emergency or if the Council unanimously finds that the public interest is
not served by the first reading.
Noting the Park District’s comments and observing that the paddle hut does not
affect any other properties, the Council expressed unanimous consensus to waive
introduction of the ordinance and move immediately to adoption. Therefore,
Trustee Greenough so moved, seconded by Trustee Abell. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, then moved to adopt the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
c)   Ordinance No. M-16-2004 – Zoning Variations: 340 Auburn – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Side Yard and Garage variations to permit
the construction of a second floor addition and the replacement of an existing
front facing garage door 16 feet in width. He explained that the side yard
variation was necessitated by the Village’s combined side yard requirements and
the fact that the proposed chimney, which exceeds the 7-foot maximum, is not a
permitted encroachment into the side yard. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the
Zoning Board of Appeals voted 6 to 0 to recommend approval of the variations.
The applicant, Rob Oldfield, was present to respond to questions from the
Trustees.
Trustee Greenough remarked that this is a beautiful example of how to respect a
single story neighborhood with the tasteful addition of a second story.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-17-2004 – Special Use Permit & Zoning Variations: 800 Pine
– Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request from the Winnetka Community
Nursery School for an amendment to the existing Special Use Permit to permit
renovation of an existing playground. The School’s plans call for a complete
renovation of the playground equipment in order to meet current ADA
requirements and safety standards. The playground is located at the northwest
corner of the Winnetka Community House property, the southeast corner of Pine
Street and Killian Court. He noted that the renovation would reduce the amount
of impermeable surface and that the new equipment will be made out of natural
wood and materials with neutral colored accents.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Plan Commission had voted affirmatively that
the new playground is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan. In addition, the
Design Review Board had recommended in favor of the playground improvements,
although it had asked that the chain link fence surrounding the site be
replaced with a more attractive fence. Finally, he reported that the Zoning
Board of Appeals voted 6 to 0 to recommend approval of the Special Use Permit.
Attorney Janega referred to an email received from Trustee Webster with regard
to a concern about language in the draft ordinance pertaining to the access of
Killian Court and asking whether that language gives rights to the School that
would supercede the Village’s right to the use of the public right-of-way. She
explained that it does not because, as with all special use ordinances, the
ordinance applies only to the specific private property in question and no
private rights to public property are granted unless the ordinance specifically
says so. She therefore recommended leaving the matter silent in the present
ordinance, just as it is in all other special use ordinances. She added that
the Village has the right to open up the Special Use Permit at any time for
amendment, if necessary, which provides sufficient protection for the Village.
Kathy Hardy, the Director of Winnetka Community House Nursery and Scott Freres,
the landscape architect, were present to answer questions from the Trustees.
Mr. Freres reviewed the conceptual site plan for the Council and explained the
fence modifications that addressed the Design Review Board’s comments.
Manager Williams asked about the circulation of water on the site. Mr. Freres
confirmed that stormwater is controlled on the property and explained the
improved stormwater drainage system.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2004 – 558 Hill Terrace Rezoning – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request from the purchaser of 558 Hill Terrace to rezone
the parcel from its current B-1 Multiple-family Residential zoning
classification to R-5 Single Family Residential zoning. The property is
currently improved with a single-family residence, which is a legal
nonconforming use within the B-1 Zoning District. As such, it cannot be
expanded or improved in any fashion without zoning relief.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Plan Commission had held a public hearing on
the requested rezoning and, in making their recommendation, noted that the
requested rezoning allows the existing single family residential pattern of
development to remain. The Plan Commission also found that the existing parcel
is not a desirable location for a multiple family development and is consistent
with the Future Land Use Plan of the Comprehensive Plan.
The applicant, Mark Harms, responded to questions from the Trustees, saying
that he wishes to use the property as his personal residence but it is sorely
in need of renovation.
Trustee Greenough commented that this is a beautiful house and worthy of
restoration.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to introduce the ordinance
approving the rezoning of the subject property. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-06-2004 – Alarm Ordinance Amendment – Introduction. Chief
DeLopez reviewed a request from the Police Department to amend the current
Village Code provisions regarding the types of permitted alarm devices and the
fees for false alarms. He asked that the Code be amended to:
   i)   allow each subscriber to have only two (2) free alarm responses within
a calendar year, and increase the fine for 10 alarms or more from $150 to $200
each;
   ii)   reset the alarm counter to zero on January 1 of each year instead of
every six months; and
   iii)   prohibit “Digital Dialers” after January 1, 2006.
Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment to the draft ordinance
prohibiting the installation of any new digital dialing systems as of the
effective date of this ordinance and changing the final date for changeover
from digital dialing systems to January 1, 2006.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to amend the ordinance as
recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance,
as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Ordinance No. MC-07-2004 – Revising Non-resident Charges for Fire &
Paramedic Services – Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed
Village Code amendment eliminating the Village’s fire and paramedic
availability charges to the Longmeadow area, which was recently annexed to the
Village of Northfield, but continues to receive its water from the Village of
Winnetka. She explained that, under the current Code provision, nonresidents
who receive water from the Village pay an additional charge for the
availability of fire and emergency services. However, these residents will be
paying for fire and paramedic services through the property taxes levied by
Northfield. In addition, Northfield has recently approved a long-term water
supply purchase contract with the Village, and will be extending a water main
to serve the Longmeadow area. She noted that the proposed amendment would
create an exception to the surcharge for this very limited group of customers.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
h)   Salary Resolutions. President Duhl explained that the Village Code
requires the Village Council to establish the salaries for the Department Heads
and the Village Manager by resolution.
   i)   Resolution No. R-11-2004 – Department Head Salaries – Adoption. Trustee
Greenough, seconded by Trustee Tucker, then moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough,
Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-12-2004 – Village Manager’s Salary – Adoption.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, then moved to adopt the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1361 and 1362. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1361 in the amount of $621,313.74 and Warrant List No. 1362 in the
amount of $442,623.62. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
b)   Policy Direction: 865 Pine Street Rezoning Request . Mr. D’Onofrio
reviewed a request from the present owner of the subject property to rezone the
parcel from its current B-1 Multiple-family Residential classification to R-
3 Single Family Residential zoning.
He explained that, pursuant to the Zoning Ordinance, requests for rezoning must
be considered in a public hearing by a board or commission designated by the
Village Council. The hearing body then reports its findings and issues
recommendations to the Council. He noted that recent similar matters have been
referred to the Plan Commission. The Plan Commission, he said, would look at
the rationale laid out in the application and address the proposal’ s
consistency with the Comprehensive Plan.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved that the Plan Commission be
designated as the hearing body for this rezoning request, with their
recommendations coming back to the Village Council for final determination. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Policy Direction: Request to Prohibit Pigeons and Predatory Birds. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request from Patrick and Debbie Fox, 385 Provident, that
the Council consider whether pigeons and predatory birds should be allowed to
be kept and bred in the Village. This request arises out of the Fox’s concern
that their neighbor at 387 Provident, Kirk Williams, keeps such birds on his
property. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the issue was brought to the attention
of the Community Development Department approximately one year ago when a
complaint was filed about Mr. Williams’ erecting a structure to house young
pigeons on his driveway. He reported that an examination of the Village Code
determined that there was no specific prohibition against keeping these types
of birds and that the Code is silent on the matter. In October 2003, Mr.
Williams built a weathering pen for his hawks, and again, a complaint was
filed.
In his capacity as Zoning Administrator, Mr. D’Onofrio responded to that
complaint in writing, supporting Mr. Williams’ right to erect the weathering
area, as it was not a prohibited structure. He referred the Trustees to the
copy of his response in the Agenda Packet. The Foxes appealed this decision to
the Zoning Board, which voted to deny the appeal, thus upholding the decision
of the Director to allow the weathering pen to remain on the property. Mr. and
Mrs. Fox, therefore, are now requesting that the Village Council consider
adopting regulations prohibiting the keeping and breeding of pigeons and
predatory birds in the Village.
Debbie and Patrick Fox spoke in support of their request, highlighting the
information contained in their materials submitted with the Agenda packet.
Mr. Williams responded, explaining how he keeps his birds, which he identified
as Rock Doves, not pigeons, and what he does with them. He said that the
predatory birds were used for public education purposes, breeding, and hunting
and are never released in the area. He acknowledged that he hires out the doves
for weddings and funerals. He said that they are only out of their keep when
they are released elsewhere and are on their way home. Mr. Williams represented
that they land on the Foxes’ house for only seconds at a time on their way into
their coop.
Trustee Tucker asked about Mr. Williams’ use of a protective mask when entering
the dove coop, as alluded to by the Foxes. Mr. Williams replied that he wears a
particulate screening mask as a precautionary measure. He said that there is an
airborne disease, which occurs in rare instances in bird keepers, that is
believed to result from intense exposure to their dander and dried droppings.
He represented that his neighbors would not be at risk for this disease because
they are in no way exposed to the particulate matter in high enough
concentrations over a long enough period of time. He explained the disease as
an “allergic” type reaction that is metabolized in the system and is not
cumulative.
Trustee Greenough inquired whether the matter is one of the differences between
enclosed spaces as opposed to open air. Mr. Williams responded affirmatively,
saying that he has no ventilation in the shed or coops.
Trustee Eilers stated that he lives a half-mile from Mr. Williams and asked
whether he is at more or less risk than the immediate neighbors, the Foxes. Mr.
Williams replied that he would consider any risk to be the same.
Trustee Ritchell inquired how many birds were presently housed in Mr. Williams’
facilities. He said that at present he has 12 flying doves, four or five young
birds, two Peregrine Falcons, and two Goshawks, and one other predatory birds.
He indicated that if he was forced to do so, he could move the birds to another
location.
Attorney Janega asked where he feeds the predatory birds. Mr. Williams
responded that they were not fed out in the open where they can be viewed.
Trustee Eilers inquired about a letter from the Village’s Sanitarian asking
that the birds be removed. Mr. D’Onofrio explained the circumstances behind
that letter, and said that all that was required was for Mr. Williams to show
the Sanitarian that he had an appropriate permit from the State to keep the
predatory birds. No permits are required from either the State or the Village
for the Rock Doves.
Manager Williams pointed out that the record is clear that the Village has
known that Mr. Williams’ birds have been in Winnetka for over 20 years.
After hearing from both sides in this dispute, President Duhl suggested that
the Council table the matter until the May 11th  Study Session, in order to
give everyone time to digest the matter and review options. He said that he
believes the matter to be one of whether or not Winnetka is an appropriate
place in which to keep these kinds of birds.
d)   Bid: Fisher Lane Outfall. Manager Williams reviewed the bids for the
Fisher Lane Storm Sewer Outfall Rehabilitation Project, which consists of the
rehabilitation of the existing concrete headwall structure and the installation
of a new guardrail on top of the structure. He reported the Village Engineer’s
recommendation that the bid be awarded to Mosele & Associates, Inc., for the
base bid of $125,750, plus a Plunge Pool at $20,625, for a total of
$146,375.00.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
e)   Bid: Replacement Water & Electric Dump Truck. Manager Williams reviewed a
request from the Water & Electric Department to purchase a replacement dump
truck for a 1991 model that is no longer serviceable. He reported staff’s
recommendation that a new truck be purchased through the State bid at a total
price of $43,832.35.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
f)   Bid: Landscape Maintenance. As noted earlier, this item was removed from
the Agenda.
g)   Bid: Public Works Truck Chassis. Manager Williams reviewed a request from
the Public Works Department to purchase a dump truck with a hook lift body, to
replace a 1996 model that is no longer serviceable. He reported staff’s
recommendation that a new 2004 Ford F550 chassis be purchased through the State
bid from Dennison Corporation for a total amount of $60,917.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
h)   Draft Caucus Question regarding Parking. Manager Williams reviewed
questions regarding parking, particularly with regard to the 2:00 a.m. to 6:00
a.m. on-street parking ban and ban around high school that were submitted to
the Caucus last year but were not included in the annual questionnaire. He
suggested resubmitting them to the Caucus again this year because the responses
are important in formulating policy.
Trustee Eilers said that he was in favor of submitting the questions to the
Caucus but raised some concern about the draft wording, particularly with
regard to the phrase, “This parking ban has no law enforcement purpose . . . .”
Chief DeLopez explained that there is a perception that the 2:00 – 6:00 a.m.
ban was implemented at the request of the Police Department for specific law
enforcement purposes. This could not be confirmed. He said that data indicates
that parking or not parking on streets doesn’t affect the types of crimes that
occur.
Mr. Eilers said that he is uncomfortable leading residents to believe that it
has no impact.
President Duhl pointed out, however, that the Police Chief says that there is
no law enforcement impact and said that he thinks residents need to know that.
He said he believes that the question needs to briefly summarize the issue and
ask whether or not residents want to retain the overnight parking ban.
Trustee Eilers suggested that the language be softened and indicated that he
believed it should also accurately reflect the Village’s policy if the ban is
not eliminated.
Trustee Tucker suggested that information about no law enforcement impact
should be attributed to the Police Chief.
Trustee Ritchell remarked that he believes that the question regarding parking
around schools would be better addressed to the affected neighborhoods than to
the entire population because it only impacts certain areas. There was a
consensus among the Trustees to remove this question.
President Duhl referred the questions back to staff to be reworked for
consideration at the next Council meeting.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported on his attendance at a recent Chamber of
Commerce meeting. He reported that Howard Smith had highly complimented the
Village on its sensitivity and efficiency in handling the fire at Trooping the
Colors. He also noted that the Chamber is very excited about the formation of
the Business Community Development Commission and is hoping that they will be
able to resolve some of the issues facing the community. He then mentioned that
the annual Recognition Luncheon is scheduled for tomorrow afternoon.
b)   Manager. Mr. Williams announced that the tour for the new Trustees of the
Water and Power Plants and Fire Department would begin tomorrow morning at 8:45
a.m., beginning in Village Hall. He invited any interested residents to attend.
He also reminded Winnetkans that Clean-up week starts next Monday, April 27th.
11. Appointments.
a)   President Duhl suggested the following individuals be appointed to the new
Business Community Development Commission, for the terms indicated.
Barbara Aquilino, Chair       3 yrs.
Betty Skalski                 3 yrs.
Steve Kardel                  3 yrs.
Roberta Rubin                 2 yrs.
Bob Horne, PC Rep.            2 yrs.
Pat Corrigan                  2 yrs.
Cicely Michalak, Chamber Rep. 1 yr.
Howard Smith                  1 yr.
Tom Eilers, Council Rep.      1 yr.
 
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve these
appointments. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
He then suggested the appointment of John Thomas as the Zoning Board’s
representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee
Greenough, moved to approve this appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:29 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Tucker, Greenough, Ritchell, and Eilers. Absent: Trustee
Webster. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, and Assistant to the Village Manager Liz Rosenthal.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Eilers, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster. The
meeting adjourned at 10:30 p.m.
