Recording Secretary
May 4, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 18, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 4,
2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker, and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and
approximately ten persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   May 11, 2004, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
the expected to attend.
b)   May 18, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Webster and President Duhl
indicated that they did not expect to attend. The remaining members of the
Council indicated that they expected to be present.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 13, 2004, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   April 20, 2004, Regular Meeting. President Duhl announced that the draft
minutes had been amended to reflect the Trustees actually in attendance.
Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the minutes, as amended. Trustee Abell seconded
the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-14-2004 – Zoning Variations: 1117 Merrill - Adoption.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting Intensity of Use of Lot and Side Yard Setback variations to permit the
construction of two one-story additions and a second floor addition at the
subject property. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes.
Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Webster, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-16-2004 – Zoning Variations: 340 Auburn – Adoption.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting Side Yard and Garage variations to permit the construction of a second
floor addition and the replacement of an existing front facing garage door 16
feet in width. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes.
Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Webster. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-17-2004 – Special Use Permit & Zoning Variations: 800 Pine
– Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment clarifying that the
variations are as depicted in the landscape and site plans dated February 19,
2004 that were presented to the Council.
Trustee Webster shared with the representatives of the Winnetka Community House
and the Nursery School his understanding of the ordinance to the effect that
while he understands that these changes will cost the organizations money, the
Council by its approval of the variations and Special Use Permit, makes no
long-term commitment that Killian Court will necessarily and always be used in
the way it is used now. Future uses of the neighboring parking lot on Lincoln,
among other areas, may require different uses for Killian Court.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the ordinance
as suggested by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance
amending the Special Use Permit and Setback variations to permit the renovation
of the existing playground at the Winnetka Community Nursery School. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes. Trustees Abell, Greenough,
Eilers, Tucker, Webster, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2004 – 558 Hill Terrace Rezoning – Adoption. Trustee
Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance rezoning the
subject property from its current B-1 Multiple-family Residential zoning
classification to R-5 Single Family Residential zoning. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes. Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
Tucker, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-06-2004 – Alarm Ordinance Amendment – Adoption. Trustee
Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance amending
Village Code provisions regarding the types of permitted alarm devices and the
fees for false alarms. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes.
Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Webster, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-07-2004 – Revising Non-resident Charges for Fire &
Paramedic Services – Adoption. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Eilers,
moved to adopt the ordinance eliminating the Village’s fire and paramedic
availability charges to the Longmeadow area, which was recently annexed to the
Village of Northfield, but continues to receive its water from the Village of
Winnetka. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes. Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Webster, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
g)   Resolution No. R-19-2004 - Prevailing Wages - Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that the Village is required to annually investigate and ascertain
the prevailing rate of wages for laborers, mechanics, and other workers in the
Village employed in performing construction of public works for the Village.
This ordinance establishes the prevailing wage rates for the Village of
Winnetka.
In response to a question from Trustee Webster, Manager Williams explained that
these wages only apply to contractors for capital improvements within the
Village.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt this resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
a)   Draft Caucus Question Regarding Parking. President Duhl referred the
Trustees to changes to staff’s revised language suggested by Trustee Eilers.
Trustee Eilers explained that he had reordered the paragraphs, eliminated some
words he considered to be weighted, referenced what is done in other
communities, added a paragraph about snow removal, and clarified language about
restrictions for out-of-town guests and college students at home for vacations.
President Duhl asked Chief DeLopez to explain what actually happens during snow
removal operations. The Chief replied that the Police Department works with the
Public Works Department to identify owners of cars parked on the streets. If
the owners cannot be found, the cars are relocated to a street or parking lot
that has already been plowed. Cars are not plowed in behind banks of snow. He
added that removing the on-street parking ban would only mean that there would
be an additional four hours during which owners might have to be contacted and/
or cars relocated. He said he did not expect this to pose a problem. During his
remarks he noted that under any circumstances, cars are not permitted to be
parked in a single spot on a street for more than 10 days.
President Duhl commented that what the Council really wants to know is whether
residents want to keep the overnight on-street parking ban in the Village, or
not. He urged that the question not be framed in such a complicated manner as
to make the point unclear.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees approved Trustee Eilers’ draft with the
exception of the paragraph on snow removal.
Trustee Webster raised a question about the possibility of removing the parking
ban in the commercial districts. He inquired whether this would really be
desirable for the Village given that it would allow parking on the streets in
front of businesses.
Chief DeLopez indicated that the Police Department does write a number of
tickets for illegally parked cars in the commercial districts, particularly
Hubbard Woods. He said that the matter is being raised out of fairness to
residents living in apartments.
Chief DeLopez reported that overall, since the Council instructed the Police
Department to enforce posted parking restrictions, there has been a decrease in
the number of parking exceptions requested.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1363 and 1364. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1363 in the amount of $863,269.54 and Warrant List No. 1364 in the
amount of $510,431.49. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
Tucker, Webster, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 507 Cedar. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
Intensity of Use of Lot and Maximum Building Size variations to permit the
construction of a one-story addition to the rear of the home on the subject
property. He noted that currently the house is nonconforming with respect to
roofed lot coverage, impermeable lot coverage, and gross floor area. He said
that this is largely due to the fact that the house was originally situated on
a corner lot that was subsequently subdivided into two lots, neither of which
meets the size requirements of the current Zoning Ordinance. Mr. D’Onofrio
mentioned that the petitioners had represented before the Zoning Board that it
was their intention to remove the patio in the rear yard, thereby reducing the
impervious surface. In addition, it is their intention to further reduce the
impervious surface sometime in the future by removing the existing asphalt
driveway and replacing it with brick pavers. He reported that the Zoning Board
of Appeals had voted 6 to 0 to recommend denial of the requested variations.
The petitioner, Michael Levitan, and his architect, Steve Knutson, spoke in
support of the requested variations. Mr. Levitan explained that the home has no
informal living space on the first floor and, therefore, cannot yield a
reasonable return. He noted that the roofed lot coverage variation is caused by
the oversized eaves that are part of the early 20th century arts and crafts
style and pointed out that variations have been granted in other instances for
similar architectural features. He also commented that the requested gross
floor variation is, in part, necessitated by the existence of a second floor,
screened-in sleeping porch that counts in the calculations for GFA. He said
that although this porch could be removed to resolve some of the gross floor
area problems, to do so would diminish the historical architectural integrity
of the house.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that if the patio were removed and
the driveway replaced with brick pavers that the impermeable surface problems
would cease to exist. Mr. D’Onofrio said that was, in fact, the case.
Trustee Tucker asked Mr. Levitan whether he intended to remove the sleeping
porch if the variation requests are not granted. Mr. Levitan said that he would
feel some pressure to do so because he believes the need for living space on
the first floor to be so compelling. Trustee Tucker also asked Mr. Levitan when
he intended to replace the driveway with pavers. He responded that he perceived
this work to be a part of the subject project and, therefore, it would be
completed in a timely fashion.
Wendy Graham, 528 Maple, supported the proposed variations, commending Mr.
Levitan and his wife for attempting to preserve an older home.
Richard Lieberman, 625 Oak, the neighbor immediately to the south of the
subject property, voiced objections to the proposed variations, saying that the
lot is small, already overbuilt, and these changes will alter the essential
character of the neighborhood. He urged the Council to affirm the unanimous
holding of the Zoning Board of Appeals.
Trustee Greenough asked Mr. D’Onofrio whether if the sleeping porch were on the
first floor, it would count towards GFA. He said that it would not.
She also asked whether he believed the proposed addition would impact
neighbors’ light and air. He indicated that he did not believe that it would
given the substantial size of the setback.
Finally, Trustee Greenough asked about the eaves. Mr. D’Onofrio confirmed that
in some other instances, the Village has granted roofed lot coverage variations
in part because of oversized eaves.
There being no further comments or questions, President Duhl polled the
Trustees.
Trustee Abell said that he believed this to be a zoning case, not an historical
preservation case. He said this is an already overbuilt lot and to add onto the
house would increase the nonconformity. He, therefore, indicated that he could
not support the variation requests.
Trustee Ritchell concurred with Trustee Abell’s assessment that this is a
zoning matter. He said that while he admired the applicant’s attempts to
preserve the home and understood the problems associated with small lots, he
would not be inclined to overturn the Zoning Board’s unanimous vote on the
matter.
Trustee Greenough said that given the fact that the open sleeping porch eats up
much of the GFA and the oversized eaves contribute significantly to roofed lot
coverage, she would be in favor of granting the variations so as to save the
older home.
Trustee Eilers agreed with Trustee Greenough. He voiced support for preserving
the existing housing stock and said that given that the if you remove the
architectural elements of the sleeping porch and the oversized eaves from the
equation, the variation sought is within boundaries. He, therefore, would vote
in favor of the petitioners.
Trustee Tucker asked whether recognizing architectural features in the granting
of variations would open up a door to a new trend that could be problematic in
the future.
Attorney Janega responded that she was not concerned about setting a precedent
that would be problem because in this instance the Village is looking at
architectural features that are existing and predate the Zoning Ordinance. She
explained that the Council has consistently recognized that prairie style homes
having the large eaves that are indicative of that style creates a unique
circumstance.
Trustee Tucker indicated that given Attorney Janega’s response, she would be
inclined to vote in favor of the variations so long as the applicant agrees to
take the steps outlined to eliminate the need for the impermeable surface
variation.
Trustee Webster noted his belief that neighbors ought to be able to rely on the
Zoning Ordinance to protect their properties, and since to grant these
variations would change those rules, he would vote to uphold the decision of
the Zoning Board.
Given the 3-3 vote of the Trustees, Mr. Levitan asked that the matter be tabled
so as to allow him time to better address the concerns raised.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to table the matter until
the June 1st meeting. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Bid: Switchgear. Manager Williams explained that the Water & Electric
Department recommended the purchase of an automatic throwover switchgear to
replace the existing manual switchgear in order to improve the Water and
Electric Plants’ auxiliary power supply. He reported that after reviewing the
bids, staff’s recommendation is to purchase the switchgear from UUSCO of
Illinois for the amount of $99,162.00.
In reply to a question from Trustee Ritchell, Mr. Williams explained that there
is really no viable interim step to purchasing this switchgear as it is
important to meeting all Environmental Protection Agency requirements and
avoiding the possibility of contaminating the Village’s water supply.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, Webster, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Abell reported on a recent visit to RED Center saying
that he was extremely impressed with the operation. He said that he had arrived
right after the Utica tornado that proved to be the first test of the state-
wide mutual aid center. He also reported that he and Trustee Eilers had
attended the opening of a new men’s clothing store last week.
Trustee Tucker reported that she had attended her first meeting of the Plan
Commission as the Council’s representative. She noted that the Plan Commission
had honored Trustee Greenough for her many years of service to the Commission
and her significant efforts in developing and writing the Comprehensive Plan.
She said that the Plan Commission was looking forward to working with both the
Business Community Development Commission and the soon-to-be-appointed Ad Hoc
Post Office Committee.
b)   President. President Duhl announced that he had received a letter from the
Park District inviting all of the Trustees to participate in the Memorial Day
celebration on the Village Green. Trustee Webster indicated that he had
attended last year’s event and it was a memorable experience.
c)   Manager: Manager Williams reported that 274 tons of materials had been
collected during Spring Clean-up Week. He said the program cost about
$32,000.00 to run.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Webster, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:29 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Tucker, Greenough, Ritchell, Webster, and Eilers. Absent:
None. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, and Assistant to the Village Manager Liz Rosenthal.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Webster seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Elers,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:30 p.m.
