Recording Secretary
May 11, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved June 1, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
11, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees Nan
Greenough, Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell, David Webster and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee David Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and Director of Community Development, Mike
D’Onofrio, and approximately 20 persons in the audience.
2)   Policy Direction: Request to Prohibit Pigeons and Predatory Birds.
Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio stated that, at its April 20th
meeting, the Council had tabled its consideration of the request of Patrick and
Debbie Fox to prohibit pigeons and predatory birds in the Village. Mr.
D’Onofrio had surveyed neighboring communities and found that Northfield
prohibits pigeons by ordinance as well as the City of Chicago. He quoted from
Northfield’s ordinance and referred to information in the agenda materials that
had been provided by Mr. and Mrs. Fox.
President Duhl asked Mr. and Mrs. Fox if they had any comments to add to those
made at the April meeting.
Mrs. Fox added comments regarding health issues related to the droppings and
dander of the birds, and clarifying information pertaining to complaints about
the birds. She also commented that the State has adopted legislation to protect
this hobby of raising homing pigeons so that no community can prohibit raising
pigeons, and noted that the law does not apply to communities within Cook
County because homing pigeons do not belong in a relatively urban area.
Mr. Fox added that Winnetka is not an agricultural community and, as the
newspaper articles show, the bird droppings can corrode property and carry
disease.
Trustee Webster ascertained that the Fox’s complaints were mostly that the
birds come on their property, leave droppings and debris, and that noise is not
an issue.
Kirk Williams, the owner of the birds, responded to the comments made by Mr.
and Mrs. Fox and also answered several questions from the Trustees concerning
the care and habits of the birds. He disputed the comments about health
concerns by saying this arises from the number of birds, not the cleanliness of
the cages, and that a person must handle the droppings to be exposed to
histoplasmosis. He stated that he wears a mask when tending the birds only
because he does not want to breathe in the dust. He also said that, although he
would be moving from his home in Winnetka this summer and the birds will be
gone, he would not like to see a law prohibiting someone from keeping birds. He
asked that the Council consider the facts before taking any action. Mr.
Williams also provided a written list of neighbors who are in support of his
birds.
President Duhl asked if anyone in the audience had comments.
A resident who lives at 942 Ash, said he has been friends of the former
residents of the Fox’s property for years and there were never any issues with
health or safety.
President Duhl then asked Village Attorney Janega to comment on the options for
a decision by the Council.
Ms. Janega recited three options for the Council: one was to pursue the issue
through the Zoning Ordinance, which she did not recommend; another was to treat
it as a nuisance; and the third was to add it to the animal control
regulations. She said the nuisance option has the most control for the Village
and added that the Chicago ordinance is nuisance based.
Manager Williams asked her to explain the enforcement procedure under the
nuisance ordinance.
Ms. Janega explained that the process is that a notice is given to abate the
nuisance. The notice provides a certain amount of time for the matter to be
resolved and then if it is not resolved, a citation is issued and the matter
goes to court. She said that by using the procedures for nuisances, there would
be lag time between the issuance of a citation and the resolution of the
matter.
Manager Williams asked Mr. D’Onofrio how long it took Northfield to enforce
their ordinance in the one instance.
Mr. D’Onofrio replied that it was in and out of court for 18 months.
Trustee Ritchell asked Ms. Janega about the basis for Northfield’s ordinance.
Ms. Janega replied that Northfield’s ordinance is under their animal control
provisions, which is one of the Council’s options, but that the nuisance
approach includes procedures for abatement. She did not recommend the option of
amending the Zoning Ordinance, because it would be more complex and would
affect nonconformities.
Trustee Ritchell commented that he was interested in some sort of provision,
but would like to give it more thought and would rather not make it a vendetta
against pigeons. He said that it seems to be an animal control issue and asked
if it would be appropriate to check the Village Code for changes required for
all types of animals.
Ms. Janega said that the Council could consider more comprehensive amendments
to the animal code provisions of the Village Code.
Trustee Greenough commented that she was not inclined to do anything about the
issue. She said that Mr. Williams pursues his avocation responsibly and there
hasn’t been a problem up until the last two years. She saw no reason why the
Council should get involved in this unless they want to address the
urbanization issue, or set a maximum for the number of birds.
Trustee Eilers said that the issue for him is if this is an appropriate thing
for this neighborhood. He thought it should be pursued as a nuisance.
Trustee Tucker agreed with Trustee Eilers, and added that she is concerned
about the health issue, stating that there had been no evidence to the contrary
that it is not a health issue. She suggested that an all-encompassing
regulation be created, as this is not an appropriate use in this community.
Trustee Webster commented that Mr. Williams had admitted that the birds land on
the neighbor’s property and said that, if there were other animals trespassing
on a neighbor’s property habitually, it would be considered a nuisance and
suitable for regulation. He said that, in this situation, he is torn and is a
little uncomfortable basing a law on a dispute between two neighbors. He said
he would like more evidence that the pigeons are a nuisance, and with Mr.
Williams moving, it does not seem to be critical to ban pigeons at this time.
He said he would rather update the ordinances generally to address animal
control.
President Duhl said that the Trustees’ comments indicate that the Council would
like to move forward on a broader basis by addressing the animal control
issues. He said his own view is that although he worries about the health
concerns, there are certain activities that some people would consider a
nuisance just because they are unusual and this is an unusual situation. He
further stated that, if the community at large considers this to be a nuisance,
then the Council should respect this, and suggested that it may be a suitable
question for the caucus questionnaire. He asked Ms. Janega to consult with the
Police Department on this matter under the context of animal control.
3)   Affordable Housing.
President Duhl began the discussion by stating that the Illinois legislature
had passed the Affordable Housing Act, which requires affordable housing in 10%
of the housing stock of non-home rule communities. He said the law presents
difficulties for built-out communities such as Winnetka. He asked Ms. Janega to
summarize the statute and what the Council needs to do and then the Council
would discuss how to meet their obligations after giving the audience an
opportunity to comment.
Attorney Janega said that there are amendments pending to the statute that
change the compliance date to 2005, but that the law, as written, requires that
all non-home rule communities comply by July 2004. She said the calculations
for determining affordable housing are based on the primary metropolitan
statistical area and communities within that area must have 10% of their
housing stock affordable or have an affordable housing plan in place. She said
that once there is a plan in place and the plan is implemented, the community
is exempt from the review process. She stated that Winnetka is not exempt from
this law. She said she used statistics prepared by the Interfaith Housing
Council that show that currently, 3.3% of the housing in Winnetka falls within
the affordable housing parameters. She said that an affordable housing plan has
to state the number of units needed to comply, which means that Winnetka would
have to increase the housing units from 138 to 410 to be compliant with the
Act. She said the Act applies to developers and contractors, and to land that
is publicly owned, semi-publicly owned or privately owned. She said a community
must try to attract affordable housing and must set one of two goals: a minimum
3 percentage point increase in the number of affordable housing units; or 15%
of all new development or redevelopment shall be affordable housing units. She
said that if a community reaches the statutory 10% goal, the requirements are
satisfied and the community is then exempt.
Attorney Janega further explained that the key is determining what is
affordable and how to develop a model affordable housing plan. She said that in
using the 10% test for single family owner-occupied housing, affordable housing
would be determined by the amount of gross income earned and must be less than
80% of the median household income in the county, or about $41,000, with not
more than 30% of that used for housing expenses, such as mortgage and taxes.
She said this would be about $12,000 per year or $1,000 per month. She said
there is another formula for rental units.
Trustee Tucker asked if there was any flexibility in these numbers or if these
figures must be used because Winnetka is in Cook County.
Attorney Janega replied that the Act is not clear about this because of the
amendments that are pending, but it currently says within the county of the
municipality, so she used the county statistics for her calculations. She said
that housing authority rents under Section 8 are from $600 for a one bedroom
apartment to $1189 for 3 bedrooms, and $1300 for 6 bedroom apartments. She said
that, under the pending amendments, the State must provide a list of exempt
communities, including the basis for the list which may give some guidance. In
response to a question from Trustee Webster, Ms. Janega expressed her opinion
that it would not be necessary to comply with the Affordable Housing Act if
Winnetka was a home rule community.
Trustee Eilers said that in order for Winnetka to be brought before the
statewide Appeals Board, the Village would have had to deny an affordable
housing developer. He also said that in going before the Appeals Board, the
character of the neighborhoods in Winnetka may be irrelevant to them. He then
asked about a possible scenario involving a competitive bid situation on public
land.
Ms. Janega replied that it is possible that the Village would be challenged if
they were to deny a contract even though everyone had an opportunity to bid on
the same construction project. She said the applicant has to establish that he
was denied because he was proposing affordable housing.
Manager Williams referred to the inclusionary zoning threshold coverage levels
on page 47 of the agenda packet, and suggested that on developments of 10 units
or more, the Village could require that 15% be affordable housing. He said the
Council could also consider a density bonus, allowing developments in the
commercial districts to have a full third story, instead of the 2.5 stories
currently allowed under the Zoning Ordinance, as long as 15% of the units were
affordable housing.
President Duhl said that there must be a plan that gets the community to 10%
and he doesn’t see how we’re ever going to reach that number, since Winnetka is
mostly single family homes. He said he thought the plan was meant to be an
incentive to increase affordable housing to 10% and is really for multi-family
developments. He suggested that perhaps Winnetka could require 15% of new
multi-family developments to be affordable to satisfy the requirement. He said
that Winnetka has to set a goal and develop a plan to get to the goal. He
suggested establishing a goal of 15% of multi-family new development and give
an incentive, but he did not know if this fits within the statute, although it
is a goal.
Trustee Eilers said that, if you have adopted the plan and it has been
implemented, you have met the goal as established in the plan. If you have not,
then you may appear before the Appeals Board.
Trustee Webster stated that the immediate requirement is quite simple, as
stated in the statute: (a) a statement of the total amount of affordable
housing units that are necessary to exempt the Village from the Act; (b) an
identification of the lands “most appropriate” for affordable housing; (c)
incentives that the Village “may” provide to attract affordable house; and (d)
a goal that 15% of all new housing be affordable housing. He added that nothing
in the statute requires Winnetka to develop a specific plan for the
construction or development of new or rehabilitated affordable housing and that
it is incorrect to state that the statute requires this.
Attorney Janega said that the statute does say that we have to have a plan by
July 1 and that the Council must keep the definitions found on page 28 of the
Act in mind. She said assuming the goal is to have 15% of all new development
be affordable housing, the development must be subsidized by the government or
20% of the units must be sold at affordable prices for a certain number of
years. She said the Act recognizes that not all developments are going to be
for affordable housing.
President Duhl said that the Act suggests that single-family homes that are re-
habbed would be part of this.
Attorney Janega agreed and said that how the Village can comply is truly a
dilemma, especially because the data being used is regional data and not local.
Assuming we try to have the 15%, you still have to have developers willing to
build and have the land. She said that density bonuses could also be used.
Manager Williams asked Ms. Janega to talk about takings.
Ms. Janega explained the concepts of regulatory takings and inverse
condemnation and how the designation of particular parcels under the Act could
give rise to takings claims. Ms. Janega said that, in her mind, designating
single family residences as affordable housing would be the most risky from a
takings standpoint.
Ms. Janega further noted that it would not be as easy to give incentives for
single family residences, and commented that the Act seems most appropriate in
the multi-family context.
President Duhl asked for comments from the audience.
A member of the audience said that he understands that there are no enforcement
provisions in the Act until 2009. He added that the Act is poorly conceived and
asked why the Village would do anything until the enforcement is in place in
2009.
President Duhl and Trustee Webster replied that it is the law and the Village
must comply. President Duhl also said that the Village has looked at the
communities adjacent to Winnetka and those communities are moving forward with
their plans. He said the Council does not want to ignore the Village’s legal
responsibilities.
A second member of the audience, a resident of Wilmette, expressed strong
opposition to the Act and noted that public hearings on this legislation have
not been held in this area. She said that the Council should ask the state
representatives tough questions before implementing a plan. She said the best
solution is to do nothing and start asking questions.
A third person from the audience responded that the Council has no authority
over this and that the speaker should be commenting in Springfield or should
vote for home rule.
Trustee Tucker said that a great argument has been made for home rule. The
Village does get mandates from the State and must comply with the law.
A fourth person from the audience said that there are a lot of smaller homes
being torn down and young families can’t live here. She suggested that the
property along Green Bay Road could easily have 3 or 4 stories and be offered
to seniors as affordable housing. She’d like to see Winnetka make some housing
affordable for senior citizens who have lived in Winnetka for a long time as
Northfield has done. She said the community has to look out for the young
people as well. She also asked if the Village is subsidized for affordable
housing.
President Duhl responded that the property along Green Bay Road is privately
owned and the Village must be careful about designating a particular property
as affordable housing, because it may limit the value of the property, which
could be considered an inverse condemnation. He said that Winnetka does not
receive any subsidy and the problem is that a community like ours doesn’t own
any land for this purpose, and also doesn’t have any property to designate as
affordable housing.
President Duhl also said that the Council only has 3 meetings before the July 1
compliance date and doesn’t think we would be able to have a plan in place by
then.
Trustee Webster thought that the initial requirements of the Act are not
complicated and that the compliance date for the plan could be met. He said
there are three things listed on pages 56 and 57 of the agenda materials that
are mandatory. He said that no matter what we do, we are not going to be able
to comply and maybe the only thing that can be said is that all land in
Winnetka is appropriate for affordable housing if it complies with zoning
regulations.
President Duhl said that he doesn’t disagree, but the second and third points
require discussion and since the June study session agenda has been set, this
cannot be resolved before July 1.
The previous speaker from the audience said that she thinks it should be opened
up to the community in a public forum to explain what effect it will have on
taxes, public services, and demands on the school base, so that the people who
elect the representatives passing these laws can be informed of what they are
doing. She said that the average person would not be aware of this law or what
affect it will have on the community and thinks the Council could bring
attention to this.
President Duhl said that he doesn’t disagree, but stressed that the Council
must try to meet its obligations to comply with the law. He said the school
board and the park district need to be concerned, and that, initially, what the
Council needs to try to understand is what the obligations are and how best to
meet them. He added that it may be that, if the statute gets pushed back, and
the Village wants to pursue home rule, nothing may be done until that is
decided. President Duhl said he had called State Representative Hamos and State
Senator Schoenberg to find out what they think this means and how we can meet
the goals, given the reality of the built out nature of the community.
The first audience member to speak said that he agrees that the Council should
consider what the Village’s obligations may be, but also consider the
residents’ rights as well. He said the Act is clearly an attempt from outside
the community to force us to do something we don’t want to do. He didn’t see
how taking a stand would be wrong and that he believes there is a point to be
made here.
President Duhl said that Winnetka will not win if we do not allow developers to
build affordable housing. He further said that the State has the right to tell
us to do this even though we may disagree, and a judge will ultimately force us
to do it. He said that the place to fight this is with the State
representatives.
The fourth speaker from the audience said that the Council has a responsibility
to the community to let them know about this and show leadership on this issue
and also to make the representatives answer our questions and elevate the
understanding of the public.
President Duhl concluded the discussion and stated that the Village Attorney
will monitor the situation in the legislature and that the discussion would be
continued to a future study session.
4)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
5)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:19 p.m.
            
Deputy Clerk
May 18, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 1, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
18, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director
Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and approximately
six persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   June 1, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to be present.
b)   June 8, 2004, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
the expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 4, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the minutes, as
drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-18-2004 – Special Use Permit: 1013 Tower Ct. –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed a request for a Special Use Permit to
allow for a health club in existing commercial space formerly occupied by the
local Comcast studio. She explained that the property is zoned C-2 General
Retail Commercial and is located in the retail overlay district where special
use requirements were adopted out of concern over the viability of the
Village’s business districts as a whole. In general the C-2 overall district
requires issuance of a special use permit for non-retail uses, such as service
establishments. She noted that according to the Zoning Ordinance, a health club
is permitted as a special use only. She mentioned that the applicant, Super
Slow Chicago, had submitted a parking study showing that adequate parking is
available to accommodate the needs of the facility and indicated that the
Village’s Traffic Engineer, Steve Saunders, had concurred with the findings of
the parking study. Ms. Janega reported that the Plan Commission had found the
use to be consistent with the Comprehensive Plan and that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted to recommend approval of the Special Use Permit.
Harry Zoberman, Jay Rosenbloom, and Estes Boshes, the applicants, spoke briefly
about their project. They indicated that it is a low traffic operation; there
are no memberships; and all clients are by appointment only.
Trustee Eilers remarked that he thought the use to be entirely appropriate, as
was clearly shown in the record.
President Duhl concurred and called for a motion to introduce the ordinance.
Trustee Eilers so moved. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Resolution No. R-20-2004 - Approval of MAP Contract - Adoption. Chief
DeLopez reported that the Village and the Winnetka Chapter of the Metropolitan
Alliance of Police have completed interest arbitration of their labor contract
for the period April 1, 2003 through March 31, 2006. He noted that the new
Agreement calls for wage increases of 3.75% per year and the establishment of a
random drug and alcohol-screening program.
President Duhl confirmed with Chief DeLopez that the contract had gone to
arbitration over the matter of drug testing as opposed to wages. Chief DeLopez
said that it had. He indicated that there were some officers who felt that they
were giving up a very important right and that there should be some additional
compensation therefor. The wage settlement was what had been originally agreed
to by the parties.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt this resolution
approving the Agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Webster.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-08-2004 – Amending the Ethics Ordinance – Introduction.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the Village’s Ethics Ordinance
in compliance with a comprehensive ethics reform package passed by the State
legislature last fall. She explained that the Legislature’s Act includes
modifications to the previous gift ban regulations, prohibitions to certain
political activities by public employees and public officials, provisions for
whistle-blower protection, and the implementation of steep penalties. The
amendments under consideration by the Council incorporate language and concepts
taken from the Attorney General’s model ordinance as well as one drafted by the
Illinois Municipal League. She noted that, although not required, the Village
would retain its Ethics Advisor and the Ethics Commission, both of which were
established pursuant to the earlier Gift Ban Act.
Trustee Abell asked whether this would apply to the Trustees should the Village
put forth a referendum. Ms. Janega replied that it would not as the Trustees’
time is not compensated. She said that there would be nothing to prohibit the
Village or the Trustees from participating in anything of an educational
nature. She added that as private individuals, each Trustee has the right to
individually maintain their own stands on various issues, but has to be careful
not to do so when speaking on behalf of the Village.
She reported that pursuant to the Act, local governments are supposed to enact
local regulations within six months after the legislation became effective. In
order to meet the required deadline, she recommended that the Council consider
waiving the first reading of this ordinance and proceed directly to adoption.
President Duhl asked for clarification about when an act of the Council is
considered final. After discussing the language briefly, Ms. Janega suggested
that Section 2.40.070(D) be amended to make clear that only the imposition of a
fine shall be considered a final disposition.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to waive the first reading.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend section 2.40.070
(D) in accordance with the above discussion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanomously.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-08-
2004 amending the Village’s Ethics Ordinance as reviewed by the Village
Attorney. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
7. Old Business.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1365 and 1366. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1365 in the amount of $699,861.94 and Warrant List No. 1366 in the
amount of $294,504.34. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
b)   Sidewalk Sale. Mr. Williams reviewed the request from the Chamber of
Commerce for the annual Sidewalk Sale scheduled for Friday, July 16 and
Saturday July, 17. He explained that no commercial activity may take place on
the public right-of-way without the express permission of the Village Council.
He noted that the Chamber is requesting that Lincoln Avenue from the north end
of Fell’s to Elm and all of Chestnut Street between Spruce and Elm be closed to
vehicular traffic.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Contract for Aerial Photgrammetry. Attorney Janega explained that this
request arises in connection with the Village’s membership in the Geographic
Information System Consortium, GISCon, a public entity consisting of local
government organizations that work collectively to achieve the full benefits of
GIS technology. The Village is in the second year of a five-year GIS
implementation plan, and as a part of this year’s work the Village plans to
obtain updated aerial photogrammetric information.
Manager Williams noted that this item was included in this year’s budget.
Trustee Greenough asked that any paper maps that the GIS service might replace
be donated to the Historical Society.
In response to a question from Trustee Ritchell, Mr. McInturff enumerated some
of the uses for the GIS system. Manager Williams added that he hoped that in
the not too distant future, some of the GIS information would be available over
the Village’s web site.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to authorize the Village to
enter into a contract, substantially in the form submitted,f or aerial
photogrammetry with Owen Ayres & Assoc. in an amount not to exceed $25,000. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
d)   Northern Illinois Police Crime Lab Agreement. Chief DeLopez reviewed a
request to enter into an Intergovernmental Agreement with the other member
agencies agreeing to an annual lease cost of $3,000 each, for five years to
establish a new lab/office building for the Northern Illinois Police Crime Lab.
He explained that the lab, which provides forensic and training services for
thirty-eight police agencies including Winnetka, must be relocated from its
current location in the Highland Park Public Safety Building.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the Village to
enter into the Agreement as described by Chief DeLopez. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
e)   Replacement Police Vehicles. Chief DeLopez reviewed a request for the
replacement and installation of equipment of three Police Department vehicles
that will soon have 75,000 miles on them through the State of Illinois approved
bidder. He reported that the approximate cost would be $50,000.
Manager Williams asked Chief DeLopez how the new system of rotating vehicles
out of the Police fleet at 75,000 miles instead of 60,000 miles was working.
Chief DeLopez indicated that there have been no problems.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the purchase of
three new police vehicle as outlined by Chief DeLopez. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell,
Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
f)   Highway Authority Agreement with Illinois Department of Transportation.
Mr. McInturff reviewed the circumstances surrounding an oil leak at the Pine
Street storm sewer outfall into Lake Michigan. He explained that this is the
second agreement required in connection with the clean up, containment, and
control of that leak. It is similar in intent to the agreement approved by the
Council on March 16, 2004, with the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency
requiring that if the area is disturbed, any material removed must be handled
in accordance with environmental law.
After a brief discussion about the indemnification language, Trustee Eilers,
seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to authorize the execution of the agreement
substantially in the form presented after review by the Village Attorney of
paragraphs 6 and 7. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, and
Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
g)   Utility Line Tree Trimming Clearance. Mr. McInturff reviewed a request to
extend the existing contract with Nels Johnson Tree Experts for utility line
tree trimming. This contract was awarded in 2002, with a provision that hourly
crew rate for 2004-2005 fiscal year be $138 per hour, a 4.54% increase.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize extension of
the contract. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported on a recent meeting of the Chamber of
Commerce. He said that the recent Recognition Luncheon had had the same
attendance as last year; that Sidewalk Sale is scheduled for July; and that the
Chamber had had 404 responses to its survey, the results of which will be
reported to the Business Community Development Committee.
b)   Manager: Manager Williams announced that the Village was once again the
recipient of the “Certificate of Achievement for Excellence in Financial
Reporting” from the Government Finance Officers Association, the highest form
of recognition for a village’s audit. He thanked the Finance Department and
Director Ed McKee for their excellent job.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:48 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:35 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Tucker, Greenough, Ritchell, and Eilers. Absent: Trustee
Webster. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Elers,
Greenough, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster. The
meeting adjourned at 9:36 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
June 1, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 6, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
1, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, David Webster, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Tom Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and
approximately six persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   June 8, 2004, Study Session. All of the Trustees present indicated that
they expected to attend.
b)   June 15, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Webster, indicated that they expected to be present.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 11, 2004, Study Session. Trustee Webster asked that the minutes be
revised to better reflect his remarks. There were no objections from the other
Trustees. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the minutes, as amended. Trustee
Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   May 18, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-18-2004 – Special Use Permit: 1013 Tower Ct. – Adoption.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a Special Use Permit to allow for a health club in existing commercial
space formerly occupied by the local Comcast studio. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Tucker, Webster,
Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-09-2004 – Amending the Village Code pertaining to Posting
of Ordinances – Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that the proposed
amendment to the Village Code would change the location of the three public
posting places for new ordinances as required by the Village Charter. The three
places currently designated are: (a) Hubbard Woods Park, (b) Station Park, and
(c) Indian Hill Park. She said that these locations have been fixed for an
extended period of time, probably dating back to a time when there was more
pedestrian traffic to and from the trains. They have, however, become less than
ideal, and the Park District has requested that the bulletin board in Station
Park, in particular, be moved. In looking for new sites, Village staff looked
for high traffic, outdoor pedestrian areas and centrally located, interior
locations so as to maximize public notice and identified Village Hall, the
Winnetka Public Library, and near the main Post Office as feasible locations.
The proposed ordinance would designate these three spots as the new posting
locations.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the proposed
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Resolution No. R-21-2004 – MFT Resolution: Traffic Signal at Winnetka &
Green Bay Rd. – Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that, as a part of the
Village’s Capital Improvement Projects for 2004, the traffic signals at the
intersection of Green Bay Rd. and Winnetka Ave. have been scheduled for
modernization. This project involves the removal and replacement of the
existing traffic signal standards and wiring and will be funded solely with
Motor Fuel Tax funds. It is anticipated that the engineering and construction
costs will not exceed $175,000.00.
He reported that requests for proposals for the engineering of the project were
received and the consulting engineering firm of Clark Dietz, Inc., was selected
based upon their qualifications and their approach to the project. Their
engineering fee for the project will be $27,408.67. This bid appears for
approval later on the Agenda but the use of MFT funds for the project must be
approved by resolution of the Council.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the MFT
Resolution for the traffic signal modernization at Green Bay Rd. and Winnetka
Ave. in the amount of $28,000.00. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Tucker, and Webster.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
7. Old Business.
a)   Policy Direction: 507 Cedar. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker,
moved to put this matter back on the table for consideration. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. D’Onofrio briefly reviewed the issues. He noted that the Petitioner had
agreed to address the impervious surface matter so as to remove it from
consideration. He mentioned that, since the last meeting the Petitioner and
staff had met to examine alternatives to reduce the overall square footage,
including removal of the second floor sleeping porch or converting the first
floor sun room from an enclosed structure to a screened porch. However, neither
of these would eliminate the need for the gross floor area variation.
Mr. Levitin spoke in support of the requested variation and requested that the
Village Council overturn the Zoning Board’s decision. He said that the addition
of the first floor living space is very important to his family and represented
that he could not get a reasonable return on his property without that
additional space. He voiced his opinion that the key issue is one of gross
floor area because it is primarily the large overhanging eaves that necessitate
the roofed lot coverage variation.
Trustee Tucker countered that she continues to be disturbed by the increase in
roofed lot coverage but is more concerned about the GFA issue.
Steve Knutson, the project’s architect, expressed his belief that the requested
variations are within the intent of the Comprehensive Plan, in which
preservation is heavily emphasized. He also referred to the recent Caucus
survey in which respondents indicated a preference for preserving older homes.
Gail Hodges, 622 Oak St., a former Chairman of the Zoning Board of Appeals,
reviewed some of the history of the subject property. She supported the
immediate neighbors’ objections based upon the density of the houses, drainage
issues, and intrusion into private space. She noted that many families either
choose, or are forced, to live in homes that are less than ideal because the
Zoning Ordinance does not allow theirs to be updated. She said that she
believed that the Zoning Ordinance should provide equal protection for all.
Rick and Tina Lieberman, 625 Oak St., spoke in opposition to the proposed
variation requests, saying that the area of Cedar and Oak is already overbuilt,
and the requested variations will only exacerbate the density. They added that
the immediate neighbors do not support the request and that the Zoning Board
exhorted the Council to enforce the existing laws as every exception creates a
precedent. Mrs. Lieberman noted that the subject property has not been
officially designated historic or special in any way and said that if it were,
its integrity should be preserved as is.
In response to a question from Trustee Webster, Mr. D’Onofrio confirmed that
because of the small lot size, even if the entire house were to be moved to the
center of the property, there would still be roofed lot coverage and gross
floor area issues.
Tom and Barbara Alt, 611 Oak, spoke on behalf of themselves and Mrs. Anderson
at 607 Oak, stating that all are opposed to the proposed variations. They cited
drainage and bulk issues as their primary concerns, and said that the proposed
additions would substantially impact surrounding neighbors who want to protect
their quality of life.
Trustee Tucker indicated she found the core question to be one of balancing
bulk, identified in the Caucus survey as the #1 issue, against preserving older
homes that sometimes have architectural features that count toward gross floor
area. She said that in the end, her concern about bulk outweighs her concerns
about preservation. She indicated that she believed that to permit the
variations would, indeed, increase bulk, compromise the intent of the R-
4 zoning district, and not improve the existing situation. Acknowledging that
the house is a beautiful, older home, she expressed her hope that it would be
preserved, but said that after serious deliberation, she could not support the
requested variations.
Trustee Ritchell affirmed his position from the last meeting, saying that he
continues to believe that the Zoning Board voted correctly and he has heard
nothing to change his opinion.
Trustee Abell concurred with Trustees Ritchell and Tucker.
Trustee Greenough indicated her support for the variations, saying that she
believes that there is precedence for such in terms of lots of similar size
where similar amounts of GFA relief have been granted.
Noting that he believes in strict enforcement of the Zoning Ordinance
especially when immediate neighbors are opposed, Trustee Webster indicated that
he would vote against this request. He pointed out to the neighbors, however,
that because of the constraints of the Zoning Ordinance, another owner might
tear this house down and replace it with one more undesirable to them.
Based upon the poll of the Trustees, President Duhl advised Mr. Levitin that if
a formal vote were taken, there would be four negative votes, thereby affirming
the findings of the Zoning Board of Appeals. He suggested to Mr. Levitin that
before the vote, he withdraw his application so that he may return to the
Zoning Board of Appeals with a new proposal without having to wait for the one-
year waiting period.
Attorney Janega pointed out that withdrawal would eliminate the waiting period,
but would not eliminate the applicable fees and procedural requirements.
Mr. Levitin requested that his application be withdrawn, and President Duhl
granted his request.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1367 and 1368. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1367 in the amount of $859,209.79 and Warrant List No. 1368 in the
amount of $519,782.28. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Tucker,
Webster, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
b)   RFP 04-001 – Green Bay Rd./Winnetka Avenue Traffic Signal Modernization.
Mr.Saunders explained that this is the formal authorization to award the bid
for the engineering portion of the Green Bay Rd./Winnetka Ave. traffic signal
modernization project, discussed earlier, to Clark Dietz, Inc., in the amount
of $27,408.67. In response to a request from Manager Williams, he also reviewed
the process followed by the Village when it solicits proposals for professional
engineering services.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid to Clark
Dietz, Inc., as recommended . By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Tucker, and Webster.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
c)   Replacement Cable Truck. Mr. McInturff reviewed the bids for a replacement
for the Water & Electric Department’s cable truck. He reported that the low
bidder that met all of the specifications was Dueco.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid to
Dueco in the amount of $110,283. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Tucker, Webster, Ritchell, and Abell.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
d)   Replacement Backhoe. Mr. McInturff reviewed the bids for a new backhoe for
the Water & Electric Department to replace one that was purchased in 1992,
explaining that its boom has become “loose” and presents a potential hazard to
employees, and that it cannot be repaired without considerable expense compared
to trade-in value. He reported that backhoes are available with GSA pricing and
the vendor offering the lowest price is Westside Tractor Sales.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to authorize the purchase of
a replacement backhoe from Westside Tractor Sales for a price of $60,550. Ayes:
Trustees Tucker, Webster, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Eilers.
e)   Yearly Electric Cable Requirement. Mr. McInturff reviewed bids for the
purchase of 600 volt and 15,000 volt cable requirements for the next year and
explained how the bid analysis was performed. He explained that the quantity of
cable is estimated based on previous years’ purchases but that the actual cable
that will be purchased depends on the number of new residential and commercial
underground services installed. Mr. McInturff recommended that the bid for 600
volt and 15,000 volt cable be awarded to RESCO in an amount not to exceed
$177,000.00.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the cable
purchase as recommended. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Tucker, Webster, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Tucker reported on the recent Plan Commission meeting.
She said that the Commission feels favorably inclined toward the rezoning of
861 Pine Street and continues to examine Chapter 5 of the Comprehensive Plan.
b)   Attorney. Ms. Janega reported that the Judge in the Historical Society
litigation had handed down a ruling in favor of the Village upholding the
zoning variations and the special use permit.
c)   President. President Duhl reported that he would be meeting with Senator
Jeffrey Schoenberg and Representative Julie Hamos to discuss the status of the
Willow Road resurfacing project.
d)   Manager: Manager Williams announced that Todd Stevens would be the new
Village Prosecutor as Jane Muno will be retiring on July 1st. He also reported
that there were over 40,000 hits on the Village’s website last month, a
significant increase over the same period last year.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Lynn Osmond to the Design Review
Board to complete the term of Jim Cavanaugh. Trustee Greenough, seconded by
Trustee Abell, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Tucker, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Eilers. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:48 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:54 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Tucker, Greenough, Ritchell, and Webster. Absent: Trustee
Eilers. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Webster,
Greenough, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers. The
meeting adjourned at 9:55 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
June 8, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: July 6, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
8, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Thomas Eilers, Herb Ritchell, David Webster and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water &
Electric Bryan McInturff, and approximately 15 persons in the audience.
2)   Irwin Lyons, Village Auditor – Pre-audit Comments or Questions.
Mr. McKee introduced the Village’s Auditor, Irwin Lyons. Mr. Lyons commented
briefly on last year’s audit letter and said that he would be available
throughout the audit process to answer any questions that the Trustees might
have.
3)   Home Rule Analysis.
President Duhl explained that he and the past Village Presidents had met
periodically over the last several months to learn more about the Home Rule
powers created by the Illinois Constitution of 1970. The group also explored
whether the concept of Home Rule warrants further consideration by the
community as a whole. President Duhl asked Gwen Trindl, Chair of the
Presidents’ Committee to review their report with the Council, which she did.
(A copy of their Home Rule Analysis is attached hereto and made a part hereof.)
Ms. Trindl reported that the committee had unanimously concluded that Home Rule
authority would have immediate and long-term benefits for Winnetka. Home Rule
would enable Winnetka to better address the issues facing the community and
afford the Village increased flexibility to better govern itself. She said that
the Presidents firmly believe that the Village’s strong tradition of term
limits, which limits Trustees and Presidents to two, 2-year terms, ensures that
the additional powers accorded Home Rule communities, including the ability to
increase property taxes, would not be abused. Saying that the Committee
believes the question of Home Rule to be an important public policy issue, she
asked the Trustees to encourage and facilitate a dialogue throughout the
community for the remainder of this year. She said the Committee also recommend
that the Council hold a Public Hearing regarding Home Rule in January, after
the holidays, to determine if the community wishes to place a Home Rule
referendum on the ballot in the April election.
Jeanne Bradner added that the Committee found that there are a number of
actions that would improve the quality of life in the Village that the Council
is prevented from taking because, as a non-Home Rule community, it does not
have the authority to do so. In addition, there are ways in which revenues
could be enhanced that are not available to non-Home Rule communities. She said
that she believes the Village is fully capable of self-governance and should
not be forced to defer to the State Legislature when other similar communities
are not subject to that control, simply because of their size.
Louise Holland concurred with Ms. Trindl and Ms. Bradner. She said she believes
it is important to educate the residents about the pros and cons of Home Rule,
and to explain what the alternatives mean, so that all know what the issues
are.
Dean Cameron, former Village Attorney, commented that the mandates coming out
of Springfield recently put greater and greater burdens on municipalities,
particularly those that are non-Home Rule. He pointed out that, although the
Special Charter does give the Village some enhanced powers, it can no longer be
counted on to address all of the problems that arise or to provide the
flexibility required to do so. He also remarked that, with the number and
population of municipalities in Illinois that have Home Rule status, the State
Legislature, by sheer number of representatives, is biased in favor of Home
Rule communities.
Ms. Trindl added that Home Rule would afford the community more flexibility in
developing the Post Office site.
The Presidents then answered questions from the audience and the Trustees.
An audience member asked if the Presidents were recommending a community-wide
survey. President Duhl responded that at this time they are not, because there
has not yet been enough information provided for residents to be well informed
on the subject.
Doug Schmidt, 99 Church St., asked who would represent the counter view and
expressed some concern that the process would be one-sided. President Duhl
replied that, historically, the counter view comes from the community on its
own.
Ms. Trindl said it was Presidents’ Committee’s objective to put forth the
necessary information to the community in an educational manner so that
residents can make their own choices.
Elise Covey said that she hoped the process would be transparent and that both
sides of the issue would be presented fairly and completely.
Trustee Tucker thanked the Committee for its efforts, saying that their report
is responsive to last year’s Caucus survey indicating that residents want more
information.
There was unanimous agreement among the Trustees to proceed as suggested by the
Presidents’ Committee and to move forward with promoting dialogue on Home Rule
throughout the community.
4)   Curbside Recycling Proposals.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village’s current recycling contract expires
this summer, so the Village went out for bids. The lowest proposal received was
from the current provider, Onyx, for approximately $215,500 per year. He noted
that the bids are based on the Village moving to curbside collection of either
a 35 or a 65-gallon wheeled, lidded container, in a dark green color, rather
than the current blue bins. Mr. Saunders indicated that the lidded carts have
been used successfully in a number of nearby suburbs, resulting in reduced
litter from blowing paper and cardboard. In addition, the larger carts reduce
the clutter at the curb generated by multiple bins and loose paper. Finally, it
would no longer be necessary for residents to sort their recyclables, because
the larger carts allow commingling of materials, making for simpler collection.
He noted that lightweight plastics could also be recycled with this system. Mr.
Saunders had samples of the two possible cart sizes available for inspection.
In the proposal, the Village requested pricing for two alternates, depending
upon whether the Contractor or the Village procured the carts and whether the
Village continues its current pick-up schedule or moves to a single-day
collection schedule. He reported that staff recommends that the Contractor
procure the carts, with the details of color, lettering, etc. to be negotiated
to provide maximum flexibility to residents without significantly affecting
costs, and that the single-day collection schedule be pursued.
Dick Kates expressed his opinion that having the larger carts on the curbside
for a longer period of time would be an eyesore. President Duhl disagreed,
saying that this way, there would be carts on the curb on only one day a week,
instead of having recycling bins out somewhere in town each of five days per
week.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees agreed to proceed to implement staff’s
recommendations.
5)   Reallocation of Water & Electric Fund Expenses.
In order to keep the Council fully advised, Mr. McKee brought three items to
the attention of the Council that came out of a review of year-end expenditures
for the Water and Electric Funds.
6)   Manager’s Report.
Manager Williams reported that the Electric Department was still trying to
determine the exact cause of several short power outages in the Tower Road
area. In addition, he informed the Council that Ocean’s 12 has requested
permission to return to the Village to reshoot some of the scenes filmed
earlier in the year.
7)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
8)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:40 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
June 15, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 6, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
15, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and approximately six persons in
the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 6(f), Ordinance No. MC-10-2004 – Film
Production, had been removed from the Agenda and would be discussed at the July
13th Study Session. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum.
a)   July 6, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present, except
President Duhl and Trustee Eilers, indicated that they expected to be present.
b)   July 13, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council member present, except
Trustee Eilers and Trustee Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
None.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-09-2004 – Amending the Village Code pertaining to Posting
of Ordinances – Adoption. Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved
to adopt the ordinance changing locations of the three public posting places
for ordinances as required by the Village Charter. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell,
and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
b)   Resolution No. R-23-2004 – Amended Fee Resolution – Parking Fees –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed this resolution that would amend the
Village’s Fee Resolution to establish new rates for commuter parking permits as
projected in the 2004-2005 Budget. The cost of a six-month resident pass would
go from $90 to $100, and the cost of the same pass for a non-resident would go
from $200 to $220. Ms. Janega also noted a couple of other minor, housekeeping
changes being made concurrently.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
c)   Resolution No. R-24-2004 – Log House – Preservation Award – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the resolution, which authorizes the Village to
nominate the Winnetka Historical Society’s project to relocate and restore the
Schmidt-Burnham Log House for a 2004 Driehaus Preservation Award. She explained
that each year the Landmark Preservation Council of Illinois presents the
Driehaus awards to projects that present unique challenges and innovative
approaches, have a long-range preservation impact, and otherwise stand as
exemplary preservation activities.
Trustee Greenough added that no one can overstate the difficulty of completing
this many-faceted project or the amount of effort and cooperation of so many
organizations and individuals.
Joan Evanich, Executive Director of the Historical Society, thanked the Village
for the honor of the nomination and for the Village’s recognition of the
Society’s efforts. Susan Curry, a Board member, also thanked the Village for
the nomination.
Trustee Tucker pointed out what a great educational asset this is for the
community and its children.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker,
Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
d)   Ordinance No. M-19-2004 – Zoning Variation: 381 Poplar – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for maximum Building Size, Intensity of Use of Lot
(roofed lot coverage), Front Setback, and Side Yard Setback variations to
permit the construction of a first floor porch, a second floor addition, and a
detached garage. In doing so, he noted that this is, in fact, the smallest lot
in Winnetka with a single-family home on it. He noted that three years ago,
variations were granted for roofed lot coverage, front setback and side yard
setback variations; however, the approval lapsed, so no building permit was
ever issued. This request comes from a new owner. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that
the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4-1 to recommend in favor of the
variations being granted. In closing his comments, Mr. D’Onofrio remarked that
some concerns had been raised about the proposed location of the garage and
driveway vis-a-vis existing trees. He reported that the Village Forester had
recommended that the driveway use the existing approach and curb cut and that
the garage be shifted further south, thereby reducing the impact on a large Oak
tree located on a neighbor’s property to the rear of the subject property.
The applicants spoke in support of their request and responded to questions
from the Trustees. They indicated that they are agreeable to the Forester’s
suggestions and would move the proposed location of the driveway and garage.
President Duhl called for a motion to introduce the draft ordinance subject to
the proposed changes to account for moving the garage and the driveway.
Trustee Eilers so moved. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-20-2004 – Zoning Variation: 435 Hawthorne – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot, Side Yard
Setback, and Garage variations to allow for the demolition of an existing
detached, three-car garage and replacement with a two-car, attached garage. He
explained that the applicant’s reason for wanting to move the garage is to
eliminate impermeable surface so as to reduce the severe drainage problem in
the back yard. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 5-1 to recommend that the variation be granted.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the flooding problem is a
serious one.
The applicant, James Bleyer, said that the topography of his yard is such that
it remains spongy all season and indicated that at times he has had as much as
12 inches of standing water between the house and the garage. He reported that
he had spoken with experts who believed that the removal of the garage and
impervious surface would alleviate the water problem without diverting it so
that he doesn’t have to walk through standing water to get to his garage. He
noted that his neighbor to the east was in favor of the plan because it would
improve her view.
Trustee Ritchell expressed concern about the proposed changes making the house
so close to the lot line on both sides and asked whether it would present a
fire hazard. He asked whether alternative designs had been considered.
Mr. Bleyer indicated that they had.
Manager Williams reported that the Fire Chief was satisfied that no additional
fire hazard would be created, and Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that the major
problem would be one of inconvenience having to do with moving equipment from
the front to the back.
Trustee Greenough said that she, too, had been skeptical about the need for the
variances. However, when she visited the property, she saw that the lot is, in
fact, much lower than its neighboring lots. She commented that as much as she
was concerned about the side yard variation, there appeared to be no feasible
alternative solution without spending huge amounts of money on an elaborate
drainage system.
Manager Williams indicated that he would have the Village Engineer evaluate the
site before the owner moves ahead with his plans.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2004 – Film Production – Introduction. As announced
earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1369 and 1370. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1369 in the amount of $757,405.57 and Warrant List No. 1370 in the
amount of $425.090.43. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Tucker,
Eilers, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
b)   Landscape Maintenance Bids. Manager Williams reviewed bids for landscape
maintenance for two-year contracts with an option for a third year. He reported
that staff recommends that the bid for regular monthly landscape maintenance be
awarded to Yes We Care for $3,553 per month, with no third-year price
adjustment and for time and material work to Vena Landscaping at $14.00 per
hour, with no third-year price adjustment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
c)   Bid No. 04-012: Directional Boring & Cable. Mr. McInturff reviewed bids
for the directional boring and cable installation as required by the Water &
Electric Department. He said that the bid amounts are based upon the unit price
and the estimated quantity required. He reported staff’s recommendation that
the bid be awarded to GroundHog Utility Construction in accordance with the bid
unit prices.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bids as
recommended and to authorize the Village Manager to enter into a contract for
the performance of such work on an “as needed” basis for the period from June
1, 2004 – May 31, 2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Webster.
d)   Bid: Liquid Chemical Feed System. Mr. McInturff reviewed the proposals for
a liquid chemical feed system for the Water Plant to enable the Plant to use
liquid chemicals instead of dry chemicals. He recommended that the system be
awarded to Panner Sales for a total of $151,600.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported on a recent meeting of the Chamber of
Commerce. He noted that the annual Sidewalk Sale is scheduled for July 17 and
18. He also mentioned that Brian Norkus, Asst. Director of Community
Development, had reported on the status of the new community kiosk. He
indicated that some Chamber members expressed interest in using one of the
three panels for advertising local business and Chamber activities.
Manager Williams suggested that this matter be placed on a study session agenda
for further discussion.
b)   Manager: Manager Williams reported that the Village has completed the
Sunview Lane drainage project and thanked Mr. Saunders for his efforts to make
this happen. He also reported on several recent brief power outages.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11) of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Greenough, Tucker, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 8:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:40 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Tucker, Greenough, Ritchell, and Eilers. Absent: Trustee
Webster. Also present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster. The
meeting adjourned at 9:41 p.m.
