Recording Secretary
July 6, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 20, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
6, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Greenough called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees David Abell, Herb Ritchell, David Webster, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
President Duhl and Trustee Tom Eilers. Also present: Acting Village Manager
Steven Saunders, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Finance Director Ed McKee, and approximately
fourteen persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Greenough led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   July 13, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council member present, except
Trustee Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend. (Note: Trustee Eilers
indicated at a previous meeting that he would not be able to attend.)
b)   July 20, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present, except
Trustees Tucker and Webster, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 1, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   June 8, 2004, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   June 15, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-19-2004 – Zoning Variation: 381 Poplar – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed amendments to the introduced ordinance granting Roofed
Lot Coverage, GFA, Front Setback and Side Yard Setback variations to permit the
construction of a second floor addition and a detached garage. She reported
that there had been some discussion at introduction concerning the location of
the proposed driveway and garage. At that time, the applicants agreed to
relocate the proposed driveway further south on the lot to utilize an existing
curb cut. They also agreed to shift the garage to the south in order to reduce
the potential impact on the root system of a 35-inch oak tree located on the
neighboring property to the east. She explained that the introduced ordinance
was amended to reflect these changes.
Greg Nowesnick, the architect, indicated that revised plans reflecting these
changes had been submitted to the Community Development Department. He also
commented that the impervious surface calculations had been recalculated and
were below the maximum allowable.
Trustee Tucker thanked the applicants for their willingness to compromise their
original design in order to protect the tree canopy.
President Pro Tem Greenough called for a motion to amend the ordinance as
outlined by Ms. Janega. Trustee Ritchell so moved, seconded by Trustee Abell.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Tucker, Webster, Ritchell and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: President
Duhl and Trustee Eilers.
b)   Ordinance No. M-20-2004 – Zoning Variation: 435 Hawthorne – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the introduced ordinance,
clarifying that the proposed attached garage will increase the overall open
area in the rear portions of the subject property and neighboring properties,
and that the proposed garage will not impair the sight lines from the house to
the east. Changes also specify that the distance between the proposed attached
garage and the building on the property to the east provide adequate fire
separation.
There being no additional comments from the applicant, the audience or staff,
Trustee Ritchell moved to amend Ordinance M-20-2004 as outlined by Ms. Janega.
Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting Intensity of Use of Lot, Side Yard Setback, and Garage variations to
permit the construction of an attached garage. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Tucker, Webster, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee
Eilers.
c)   Ordinance No. M-21-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1174 Scott St. – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Height variation to permit the
construction of a third floor addition, by expanding an existing nonconforming
third floor, on the subject property. He noted that the proposed improvements
also call for a two-story addition along the west side of the building.
However, except for the number of stories, the proposed improvements comply
with all zoning regulations, including vertical building height. Mr. D’Onofrio
reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6 to 0 to recommend that
the variation be granted.
Trustee Abell remarked that this appears to be a perfect example of a variation
request that should be granted, as it improves the appearance and function of
the home. Trustees Ritchell and Tucker concurred.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-22-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1128 Mt. Pleasant –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Front and Corner Setback
variation to permit the construction of a new single-family home on the subject
property. He noted that the property is located at the west end of Mt. Pleasant
Rd. and is adjacent to Crow Island Woods. The only improved street adjacent to
the property is Mt. Pleasant. However, along the west side of the lot there is
a platted, unimproved public street, Glendale Ave.. The proposed new home would
not meet the setback requirements along the Glendale side of the lot. He
reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5 to 1 to recommend that
the variation be granted but recommended that action be taken to vacate
Glendale Ave.
Attorney Janega explained the Village’s past practices regarding variations
related to street vacations. She noted that there were several key findings
missing from the Zoning Board’s minutes, noting that the Zoning Board focused
only on the impact of the existence of the street, but did not consider that a
side yard setback variation would still be required if the street were vacated.
She also pointed out that, although the Council has granted somewhat similar
variations, they have never granted one for new construction on a lot of
regular size.
Charles Page, the architect, spoke in support of the requested variation and
pointed out that the affected neighbor was in favor of granting the variation.
Trustee Tucker inquired whether the house could be built within the required
setback. Mr. Page responded that it could.
President Pro Tem Greenough commented that in addition to looking at the
setback issue, there is a policy issue about vacating a dedicated right-of-way.
Attorney Janega concurred, explaining that one policy issue revolves around the
fact that, generally, the Council has not granted any variations for new
construction absent a real demonstrated hardship that runs with the property.
In this instance, no such hardship has been shown, and the apparent hardship is
caused by the design of the proposed home. A second policy issue involves the
unimproved right-of-way and whether allowing a variation without vacating the
property allows one property owner to get the benefit of the land without
paying for it. She added that in this instance, this public policy issue is
somewhat greater because there is a public amenity, Crow Island Park, involved.
President Pro Tem Greenough suggested that it would be better for the applicant
to seek a vacation of the street before seeking the variation, and asked the
applicant whether he had considered this approach.
Mr. Denenberg indicated that he was afraid that it would take too much time to
pursue this avenue and that the plans for the proposed house had already been
drawn.
Trustee Tucker asked the applicant and the architect if a home could be
designed within the required setback requirements so as to avoid the variation
process altogether. Mr. Denenberg responded that doing so would result in a
long, narrow house that would encroach on the rear neighbor’s privacy. He
reiterated that the plans for the home that he wished to build had already been
drawn.
Attorney Janega pointed out that even if the Village were to vacate the
unimproved street, the proposed new home, as designed, would still require a
variation for the west side setback, unless the vacated street was consolidated
with Mr. Denenberg’s lot.
President Pro Tem Greenough remarked that the existing lot, which is not
irregular or unusual, simply does not accommodate the home as designed without
some sort of variation. The fact that the house has already been designed does
not constitute a hardship under the standards for granting variations.
Trustee Ritchell indicated that given the proposed demolition of the existing
house, thereby giving the owner a clean slate upon which to build, and the
absence of a true hardship running with the land, he could not see granting a
variation for construction outside of the allowed building envelope.
Trustee Abell said that he had visited the property and seen that it is clear
that the proposed encroachment would not harm any of the neighbors. However,
there is still the problem of granting a variance for new construction when the
precedent is clear that the Council does not do so. He indicated that, in order
to maintain the integrity of the Zoning Ordinance, he could not justify
granting variance in this case given the absence of a legitimate hardship.
Trustee Tucker and President Pro Tem Greenough agreed.
Trustee Webster indicated that he would be in favor of granting the variation
and moved to introduce the ordinance. There was no second, and the motion
failed.
President Pro Tem Greenough remarked that, given the discussion, even if the
applicant were to redesign his project in any way short of full compliance with
the Zoning Ordinance, there would not appear to be support for any variation by
this Council.
Trustee Abell indicated that he would have serious reservations about vacating
any portion of unimproved Glendale given that it abuts the park, and moved to
deny the request for variation. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion.
Attorney Janega pointed out that if the Council votes to deny the request, the
applicant may not return to the Council with an amended design for twelve (12)
months. She then outlined the applicant’s options.
Mr. Denenberg indicated that he would like to withdraw his request from the
agenda at this time in order to consider possible modifications.
Trustee Abell withdrew his motion to deny, and Trustee Ritchell withdrew his
second.
Ordinance No. MC-11-2004 – Rezoning: 865 Pine St. – Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio
reviewed a request to rezone the subject property from B-1 Multi-family
Residential to R-3 Single-family Residential. He noted that this matter was
discussed at the April 20, 2004 Council meeting, at which time the Council
designated the Plan Commission as the body to hold a public hearing on this
request. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that at that meeting on May 26, 2004, the Plan
Commission voted unanimously to recommend that the property be rezoned as
requested.
Trustee Webster inquired whether this property would be suitable for a
municipal parking lot. Mr. Saunders responded that it would be very difficult
to make use of this space for that purpose because of its small size and
triangular shape.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1371 and 1372. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1371 in the amount of $1,047,805.40 and Warrant List No. 1372 in the
amount of $923,438.27. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Tucker,
Webster, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee Eilers.
b)   Vehicle Sticker Design Request. Mr. McKee reviewed a request from the
Historical Society to have the Smith-Burnham Log House depicted on the
Village’s 2005 vehicle sticker. He noted that the Village has established a
policy of recognizing significant anniversaries of public entities by placing
designs of their choosing on the Village’s vehicle stickers.
Trustee Webster inquired whether the stickers could be made more user friendly
so that they are easier to remove. Mr. McKee indicated that he would
investigate what options might be available.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Policy Direction: 127 Fuller Lane. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
Intensity of Use of Lot, Maximum Building Size, and Rear Yard Setback
variations to permit the construction of a one-story sunroom addition at the
rear of the home on the subject property. He noted that the existing building
is legal nonconforming with respect to roofed lot coverage and GFA, exceeding
the allowable maximums in both, and reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals
had voted 6-0 to recommend that the variations be denied.
Douglas Clark, the architect, addressed the plight of the owners, saying that
significant water accumulates in the neighbor’s low-lying property and becomes
a breeding ground for infectious mosquitoes. The owners want to add the
proposed three-season room to permit them to enjoy their outdoor space without
worrying about mosquitoes. He pointed out that others in the neighborhood have
added on the rear of their homes and that there are no objections from any of
the neighbors.
A neighbor, Mrs. Whitchurch, spoke in support of the variation request.
Trustee Tucker inquired whether the property owners had considered any
alternatives to resolve the drainage problem. Mr. Clark reiterated that the
drainage problem is on a neighbor’s property, over which the applicants have no
control.
Trustee Ritchell expressed concern that so many of the homes in the
neighborhood are quite large. He said that given that this property is already
well above the allowable FAR, he would have difficulty granting an extension of
what is there. He asked whether the applicants had considered simply adding a
screened porch.
Mr. Clark replied that a screened porch would still require a roof and a
foundation.
Mr. Saunders remarked that standing water is a problem throughout the Village
and not unique to this particular lot.
Trustee Abell voiced his concurrence with the Zoning Board’s finding, saying
that there is no hardship in this instance that is particular to this piece of
property and, therefore, insufficient justification for granting the requested
variance.
Trustee Tucker agreed with Trustee Abell, saying that the perceived hardship is
not unique to this particular property.
Trustee Webster indicated that he would vote in favor of granting the variance.
He pointed out that the home predates the GFA requirements and asked about the
additions granted neighbors. Mr. D’Onofrio replied that, in those instances,
the homes were older and lacked the more modern, expected amenities. The house
in question here is relatively new and was built with most amenities.
President Pro Tem Greenough indicated that while sympathetic to the mosquito
problem, she was unaware of any precedent relating to mosquitoes and drainage
that would qualify the applicants for GFA and roofed lot coverage variations.
Noting that after polling the Trustees, three had indicated that they were
opposed to the requested variances, she asked the applicants whether they
wished to proceed. They indicated that they did.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to uphold the Zoning Board’s
recommendation to deny the variation. He commented that, whatever else the
applicant might propose would require an increase in the GFA and, therefore,
would not significantly change the circumstances. He suggested that the
applicants seek a solution to the drainage problem by some other means.
Trustee Webster moved to table the matter. There was no second, and the motion
failed.
President Pro Tem Greenough called for a vote on Trustee Abell’s motion to
uphold the Zoning Board’s recommendation. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Tucker, Ritchell, and Greenough. Nays: Trustee
Webster. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee Eilers.
Mr. Saunders indicated to the petitioners that he would have a member of his
staff inspect the properties to see if an engineering solution to the drainage
problem could be devised.
d)   Policy Direction: 1205 Tower Rd. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a
Front Setback variation to permit the construction of a new, single-family home
on the subject property. He explained that, in this particular case, the
required front yard setback is substantially greater than what is normally
required, because it is based on the average of the other front setbacks on the
same blockface. This particular block has a total of ten lots with front
setbacks ranging from 36.33 to 118.94 feet, with the average being 53.65 feet.
The petitioner is requesting a front setback of 40 feet, the minimum required
in the R-3 zoning district. He said the intent of the front setback requirement
was to align new homes with the existing blockface in order to mitigate the
impact of newer homes. In this instance, however, there are two unusually deep
lots with deep front setbacks, which skews the average setback requirement. He
commented that all other aspects of the proposed development comply with the
Zoning Ordinance and reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4 to 2
to recommend that the variation be denied.
President Pro Tem Greenough remarked that, while the objective of the
requirement is uniformity, in this case, the strict construction of the rule
results in nonconformity.
Mr. King, the applicant, presented depictions of the existing blockface as well
as a model of the proposed house. He indicated that he is seeking to balance
the front and rear space on his relatively short lot. He pointed out that
although the proposed structure does maximize GFA, its façade is no wider than
that of the existing house and that, even with a 53’ front setback, he could
still build to the maximum allowable GFA. However, by doing so, his house would
be set farther back on the lot and would overlook the private space of his
neighbors, whose houses sit at an average of 39 feet from the front lot line.
He noted that, while neighbors had expressed concern about the bulk, the Zoning
Board had found no impact of the proposed construction on their light and air.
President Pro Tem Greenough reiterated to the Trustees that the proposed
construction is in full compliance with the Zoning Ordinance, except for the
front setback. She suggested that the Council focus on whether enforcing the
average setback regulation in this instance would take the Village closer to
the intent of the requirement or farther away. She remarked that pushing the
house back will result in a wider and deeper house that encroaches on the
neighbors’ private space more than the proposed construction.
Trustee Webster commented that there are two competing principles here; one
being that the Council does not grant variations for new construction, the
other being that to enforce the regulation strictly will obviate the intent of
the front setback regulation by creating an uneven blockface.
Mr. King noted that moving the house back on the property might also jeopardize
a large Oak tree that they are hoping to preserve.
Trustee Abell confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that even if the Zoning Board’s
recommendation is upheld, the applicant can still build the same size house.
Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that was, in fact, the case.
Attorney Janega explained that the Council has dealt with average front setback
issues before, but the typical application is to bring houses that are set too
far back on a property forward to establish some consistency. She could not
recall an instance in which the average setback requirement was enforced so as
to move construction away from a relatively uniform blockface. She pointed out
this case is also fairly unique because Tower Road is an arterial street with
significant traffic that could be a hazard to children playing in the front,
which increases the importance of maintaining open space in the rear of the
lot. She stated that concerns about the bulk of the house are immaterial
because the proposed bulk conforms with what is allowed by the Zoning
Ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell voiced concern for trees on the property and Trustee Tucker
urged some compromise on the proposed bulk.
Mr. King indicated that, if he were forced move the house back to 44 feet, the
average of the blockface without the two very deep lots, he would probably be
forced to redesign the house. He asked that, if the Council is unwilling to
grant a 40-foot setback that they grant a 41-foot setback, or that they base
the average on the first six houses on the block.
President Pro Tem Greenough reminded the Trustees that the request under
consideration is for a 40-foot front setback and that bulk was not on the
table. She also reiterated her understanding that the objective of the front
setback regulation is to bring houses on a single block face into as much
conformity with one another as possible.
Trustee Abell indicated that, while he was very reluctant to grant a variance
for new construction, in this instance, he would vote in favor of the request
as presented because the uniformity of the blockface trumps the other
considerations. He added that the Council has no authority to require the
applicant to reduce the bulk of his house, as it fully complies with the Zoning
Ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell said that he was sorry to see the existing home at 1205 Tower
be demolished and would be more inclined to permit a variation for some sort of
rehabilitation. He indicated that he was inclined to confirm the Zoning Board’s
recommendation. He also asked the applicant about the possibility of removing
the existing garage. The applicant responded that the Zoning Ordinance allowed
him to retain the existing garage and said that they would like to keep it for
storage purposes.
Trustee Tucker concurred with Trustee Ritchell, but said she would like to see
some sort of compromise.
Trustee Webster said that ordinarily he would be in favor of strict application
of the Zoning Ordinance but that, under these circumstances, it is silly. He
said that, if conditions pertaining to the protection of the trees were
incorporated into an ordinance, he would be inclined to vote in favor of
granting the variation.
Stating that she believed that the variance should be granted, President Pro
Tem Greenough, however, noted that the required four affirmative votes were not
available at this meeting.
Trustee Abell moved to table the matter until a meeting when all of the
Trustees will be present. After some discussion, it was determined that that
might not occur until September, thereby significantly delaying the project.
President Pro Tem Greenough suggested that, in order to avoid this unreasonable
delay, the Village Attorney be directed to prepare a draft ordinance for the
Trustees’ consideration. In that way, the process would continue to move
forward and there would be sufficient opportunity for all of the Trustees to
review the matter thoroughly and for the applicant to garner the necessary
support.
Trustee Abell withdrew his motion to table and moved to direct the Village
Attorney to prepare a draft ordinance that would take into account tree
preservation and a compromise on the setback. Trustee Webster seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Abell thanked the applicant for his patience.
e)   Street Rehabilitation Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for the Street
Rehabilitation Project to include construction of new curbs and pavement
replacement. He recommended that the bid be awarded to A Lamp Construction for
$556,767.50 and that the Village President be authorized to sign the contract
on behalf of the Village.
Trustee Webster inquired when the work would be finished and asked that, if
possible, the portion of Humboldt near Christ Church be completed during the
summer. Mr. Saunders indicated that he hoped all of the work would be done by
mid-October and indicated that he would see what could be done to accommodate
Mr. Webster’s request.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bid as
recommended and authorize the Village President to sign the contract. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Webster, Ritchell,
Abell, Tucker, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and Trustee
Eilers.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Manager: Mr. Saunders reported that the Village’s Fourth of July
activities had been well attended and passed without any public safety
incidents.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Greenough, Webster, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl and Trustees
Eilers. The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:47 p.m. Present: President
Pro Tem Greenough, Trustees Abell, Webster, Tucker, and Ritchell. Absent:
President Duhl and Trustee Eilers. Also present, Acting Village Manager Steven
Saunders and Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Webster, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Duhl
and Trustee Eilers. The meeting adjourned at 10:50 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
July 13, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: August 10, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
13, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, David Webster. Absent: Trustees Tom Eilers
and Jessica Tucker. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Chief of Police Joe
DeLopez, Police Sgt. Patrick Kreis, and one person in the audience.
2)   ICMA-RC Health Savings Account.
Manager Williams introduced Sgt. Kreis, who presented the Council with
information on a Retirement Health Savings Plan for consideration as a possible
benefit to Village employees. Sgt. Kreis explained that an RHS plan is designed
to help employees pay for post-retirement health care expenses, including
insurance premiums. He pointed out that the RHS plan is funded by employee
contributions and employee participation is entirely optional. He further
explained that the Village would incur no cost in offering the plan other than
the time to administer it and to process employee election forms.
After asking several questions of Sgt. Kreis, the Trustees agreed the
Retirement Health Savings Plan seemed beneficial for Village employees.
President Duhl directed the staff to obtain more information regarding the tax
ramifications of participating in this type of plan, to determine whether the
ICMA’s legal opinions and IRS private letter rulings could be relied on by the
Village, or whether the Village should obtain its own private letter ruling.
3)   Tasers.
President Duhl asked Chief DeLopez to comment on the proposal to purchase
tasers for use by patrol officers of the Police Department.
Chief DeLopez said the Police Department is asking that the Council approve the
purchase of tasers for all patrol officers. He described the taser as a tool
that projects darts that emit an electrical charge into a person’s skin or
clothing to immobilize muscle groups. He said tasers would help to minimize
risk of injury to the public as well as to officers and explained how using the
taser would be preferable to using pepper spray or a baton to subdue a person.
He said that the cost to purchase the tasers is about $1000 per officer and a
surplus in the Police Department’s budget is available to pay the entire
amount. He asked Sgt. Kreis to demonstrate how the taser would be used.
Sgt. Kreis presented power point and video demonstrations on the use of the
taser and also demonstrated the tool for the Council. He commented on the
safety of the device and the lower risk of injury from its use, referring to
statistics that showed not only a reduction of injuries to suspects and
officers, but also less use of deadly force. Sgt. Kreis explained that using a
less aggressive means of subduing people early in a situation reduces the
possibility of the situation escalating into violence.
Chief DeLopez interjected that the use of the taser is not to be a substitute
for deadly force, but it can reduce the need to use deadly force.
Sgt. Kreis explained that the tasers do not interfere with the functions of the
heart, pacemakers or internal organs, and do not rely on pain to obtain
compliance from an individual. He also said that the taser doesn’t cause burns
or nerve tissue damage, there is no threat of electrocution and no deaths have
been documented from its use. He said each taser also records the date, time
and duration of each use, and that data can be downloaded to a computer, which
allows the use to monitored and could be useful against claims that it was used
excessively.
Chief DeLopez explained that the tasers would replace batons or flashlights as
a way of gaining physical control over a subject, and briefly spoke about
litigation. Village Attorney Janega commented on the decisions of civil rights
lawsuits against police departments for the use of batons and flashlights. She
added that her research on tasers disclosed that, although there have been
lawsuits involving tasers over the years, she has not found cases in which a
taser was found to be a direct cause of death.
The Trustees asked several questions and after further discussion and upon
agreement of the Trustees, President Duhl asked that a resolution to authorize
the purchase of the tasers be placed on the Council’s August meeting for
further discussion and possible adoption.
4)   MC-10-2004 Film Production.
Mr. McKee explained that, after working with film production companies this
year, the staff would like to revise the billing rates for film production to
eliminate confusion over the exact rate charged and to take into account such
items as pension costs, sick time, and overhead costs of the Village. Mr. McKee
proposed that rates be set to cover the Village’s full costs, as explained in
the agenda report and drafted in Ordinance MC-10-2004.
President Duhl commented that, in his opinion, the revised rate formulas in the
draft ordinance still do not take into account the disruption to the Village
and its residents, and the staff time incurred to process the applications and
accommodate the use of public property and Village employees. He suggested
creating a more flexible way to charge film production companies on a case-by-
case basis and negotiate a fee based on how long the film company will be in
town and the circumstances involved with each application.
Trustee Abell agreed and suggested that it apply to still photography and the
closing off of roads, etc.
Trustee Ritchell said he preferred that the Village set guidelines rather than
a policy so that the process would not become onerous.
President Duhl replied that he thought that he and Trustee Ritchell were
essentially in agreement that, instead of fixed rates, making the fee flexible
would allow the Village to evaluate the particular circumstances of each permit
application and set a fee based on the use of Village property and personnel
and the amount of staff time required.
Manager Williams said that the staff would review the draft of the ordinance
and asked the Council to consider if there should be exceptions for school
productions, educational films or not-for-profit films.
Trustee Abell asked if the rates must precisely reflect the Village’s costs.
Attorney Janega commented that the costs must be a reasonable reflection of the
Village’s costs, which can consider the Village’s hard costs and can use market
prices for similar services. She said the rates can also attempt to recover the
Village’s soft costs and stated it would be possible to set fees on a case-by-
case basis depending on factors that would have to be determined. She said she
had contemplated that there would be a minimum base fee that would simply be a
processing fee and said that she had added a statement of policy and purpose
that public resources should not be used unless the public is compensated for
it. She also suggested that the Village Code contain a general reference to
rates rather than a statement of specific amounts, and that the actual rates
being set by the rate resolution. She explained that this would allow the rates
to be easily revised as necessary to more accurately reflect the full cost to
the Village. She asked the Council to determine if they would set all of the
fees on a case-by-case basis or if they would like to give the Manager or staff
authority to set fees in some cases.
President Duhl replied that, at some level, applications should come to the
Council, but that not all of them should.
Manager Williams commented that the current ordinance requires that requests
for permits for more than five days go before the Council.
President Duhl asked that the staff revise the draft ordinance to allow the
Village more flexibility in setting the permit fees.
5)   Comprehensive Nuisance Review.
Village Attorney Janega commented on the discussion draft of comprehensive
amendments to the animal control and nuisance provisions of the Village Code,
which had been drafted with input from the Village Sanitarian, the Director of
Community Development, and the Chief of Police. She said that she had clarified
definitions and added language to make violations of the animal control
ordinance and nuisance violations, subject to nuisance remedies. She also
provided the Council with further sample provisions to consider.
President Duhl suggested that Attorney Janega revise the draft by inserting any
additional provisions she recommends and revising the language drafted for
Section 6.04.010.
Trustee Webster added comments about the types of animals covered or prohibited
under the draft amendments.
Trustee Ritchell inquired about birds of prey, boa constrictors and other
species that require a greater standard of care and asked that Attorney Janega
address these in the definitions for different types of animals, etc.
President Duhl asked that Ms. Janega incorporate the Trustees’ comments and
revise the draft for further discussion by the Council.
6)   Manager’s Report.
Manager Williams updated the Council on pending Council agenda items and the
status of construction at the Winnetka Historical Society at 411 Linden.
7)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
8)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:55 p.m.
         
Deputy Clerk
July 20, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 10, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
20, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, and Herb Ritchell. Absent: Trustees Jessica
Tucker and David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, and approximately six persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   August 3, 2004, Regular Meeting. President Duhl and Trustees Abell and
Ritchell indicated that they could not attend. All of the other Council members
present indicated that they could attend.
b)   August 10, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Ritchell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 6, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-21-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1174 Scott St. – Adoption.
President Duhl called for a motion to adopt the ordinance granting a Building
Height variation to permit the third floor of the existing home on the subject
property, which is a nonconforming third story, to be expanded to align with
the floor below. Trustee Eilers so moved. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Tucker and
Webster.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-11-2004 – Rezoning: 865 Pine St. – Adoption. President
Duhl called for a motion to adopt the ordinance rezoning of the subject
property from B-1 Multi-family Residential to R-3 Single-family Residential.
Trustee Abell so moved. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Tucker and Webster.
c)   Ordinance No. M-22-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1128 Mt. Pleasant –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed a request for a Corner Setback
variation, discussed at the last Council meeting, to permit the construction of
a new single-family home on the subject property. She explained that, at that
time, the applicant had withdrawn his request from the agenda in order to
consider his options, so no ordinance was introduced. Subsequent to that
meeting, the applicant submitted revised drawings. The revised plans indicate
that the west wall of the building will have a 15-foot corner setback, instead
of the original 12.5 feet, the window well encroachment has been removed, and
the eaves have been changed from 24 inches to a conforming 18 inches.
Therefore, the request is now to allow a 15-foot corner setback, where a 28.48-
foot setback is required.
Ms. Janega noted that because of the platted, but unimproved, street to the
west of the lot, the subject property is considered a corner lot and is subject
to the zoning requirements pertaining thereto. These include a corner yard
setback, which is comparable to the front yard setback and is larger than the
side yard setback required for interior lots. However, for purposes of
development, the west side of the subject property is the same as the side yard
of an interior lot, and no variation would be required to build the proposed
home, as modified by the applicant, if it were an interior lot. She also
remarked that there are no objections from any neighbors, and, in fact, the
most immediately affected property owner is in favor of the proposed building
plans.
Trustee Greenough remarked that she had been against this proposal at the last
meeting because of the potential of setting a precedent about encroaching into
the right-of-way and also because it exceeded the 15-foot side yard setback
requirement for an interior lot. She indicated that since that problem has now
been addressed, she would vote in favor of introducing the ordinance.
Trustee Abell moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-22-2004. Trustee Ritchell
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2004 – Amendments Pertaining to Appointment of Deputy
Police and Fire Chiefs and Residency – Introduction. Attorney Janega explained
that this ordinance would amend the Village Code to reflect changes in State
law and authorize the Fire Chief and the Chief of Police to select and appoint
their second-in-command officers, with the approval of the Village Manager. In
addition, it would amend the Village Code to exempt the Fire Chief from
residing within the Village limits, just as was done with the Police Chief in
2001. The Fire Chief, however, would be required to reside within a 10-mile
radius of the Winnetka Fire Station.
President Duhl explained that, historically, both the fire and police chiefs
have been required to live within the Village, but that now, given the very
high property values, it is not economically feasible to impose this
requirement without substantial Village subsidy.
Trustee Eilers suggested that giving the new incumbent merely four months to
relocate, as drafted, might be too short. The other Trustees agreed and decided
that 12 months was more reasonable.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1373 and 1374. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1373 in the amount of $715,579.02 and Warrant List No. 1374 in the
amount of $290,305.06. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Webster and Tucker.
Given that Trustee Abell will be absent from the next meeting, Trustee Eilers
agreed to cover the warrant lists for that meeting.
b)   Policy Direction: Request to Waive Demolition Fees – 710 - 724 Green Bay
Rd. Attorney Janega reviewed a request from the owner of the subject properties
to waive the demolition permit fee of $10,100 that would be required for the
demolition of the buildings on each of four properties, because the owner plans
to replace the four single-family buildings with one multi-family development.
She noted that, in order to grant this request, the Council would first have to
amend the fee resolution, since the fee resolution does not currently reserve
the Council’s authority to waive the fees.
Dick Busscher, 1275 Asbury, the property owner spoke in support of his request.
He indicated that it was his intention to demolish one of the residences as
soon as possible, letting the others wait for a while. He asked that the
Council reconsider its demolition fee as it pertains to the multi-family zoning
districts.
President Duhl explained that the Council raised the demolition fees not out of
concern about all of the teardowns in the single-family districts, but in an
effort to make the Community Development Department self-supporting in terms of
all the time and effort its staff invests in redevelopment, regardless of the
zoning district in which that occurs. He expressed his belief that granting a
waiver would set a precedent that would be difficult not to repeat. He said
that he could see no way to distinguish these lots individually from any other
lots, as their demolition will demand as much of Community Development staff
time as any others. He indicated that he might be able to consider the matter
differently if Mr. Busscher were to consolidate the lots before requesting a
demolition fee for all of the properties.
Attorney Janega indicated that even in Mr. Duhl’s scenario, there would still
be four separate fees, as the fee is a per structure requirement for
demolition, not per property. She explained that, even in the event of a
consolidation, the Council would first have to adopt a resolution giving them
the authority to waive the existing demolition fees, as at present that
authority does not exist. She reported that the Community Development
Department frequently receives requests to waive fees because applicants
represent that they do not need all of the Department’s services, and expressed
her belief that a consistent principle needs to be applied.
President Duhl advised Mr. Busscher that, until he was prepared to take down
all four dwellings at the same time, the Council could not consider any request
to waive fees. There was a consensus among the Trustees to this effect.
c)   Sunview Lane Change Order. Noting that the Sunview Lane Drainage Project
is now completed, Manager Williams explained the items that caused the project
cost to exceed the amount awarded last fall of $580,570. He pointed out that
this project was still well under the initial estimates.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve Change Order #1
for the Sunview Lane drainage improvements in the amount of $48,525.80. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Eilers,
Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Tucker and Webster.
d)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Mr. Williams commented on the
departments’ reports that were included in the Agenda packet. He pointed out
that the conversion of Tower Road from overhead to underground service has been
completed; that the splices have been replaced in three out of the four feeders
to the Northfield substation; and that the use of a liquid chemical feeding
system at the Water Plant has begun.
He also reported that the rehabilitation of Moffat Mall had been completed and
that adjoining business owners are pleased with the results of this public-
private partnership.
Finally, Manager Williams noted that, contrary to original plans, the Eldorado
bridge rehabilitation will not take place until next spring.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported the most recent meeting of the Business
Community Development Commission. He mentioned that two local property owners
had attended and that the Committee encouraged public participation. He noted
that a vacant space inventory had been conducted showing that only seven out of
260 commercial spaces are available, not counting second floor offices, and
remarked that this is significantly less than many assume.
In addition, he commented that the Committee is pursuing its information and
intelligence-gathering phase necessary to making future recommendations that
will be embraced by the community. This includes:
   i)   A consumer survey conducted by the Chamber of Commerce.
   ii)   A business owners survey that will be going out the end of July.
   iii)   The vacant space inventory mentioned above will be ongoing.
   iv)   An inventory of potential sites for development will be done.
   v)   A building owner survey.
Finally, he mentioned that the Committee is reviewing whether it should take a
position on Home Rule. He suggested to the Committee that it study the issue
and form an opinion in the context of economic development.
Trustee Abell reported that he had attended a recent meeting of the RED Center
Board, at which the possible new membership of two additional villages was
discussed. He pointed out that this would help to reduce the Village’s payments
to the Center.
b)   Manager
Manager Williams reported that Fire Chief, Ron Colpaert, will be retiring from
Village service effective August 12, 2004. He noted that Ron has served as
Winnetka’s Chief for 17 years.
Mr. Williams also noted that staff continued to meet with Metra to develop
plans for the renovation of the Elm Street and Hubbard Woods train stations. He
indicated that Metra now hopes to be able to fund the project for the 2005
construction season.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, and
Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Tucker and Webster. The Council convened
into Executive Session at 8:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:15 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Ritchell. Absent: Trustees Tucker
and Webster. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Ritchell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Tucker and
Webster. The meeting adjourned at 9:16 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
August 10, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 7, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
10, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees Tom
Eilers, Nan Greenough, David Abell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Trustees David
Webster, Herb Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works, Steven Saunders, and approximately 9 persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   August 17, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the other Council members present
indicated that they could attend, except Trustee Tucker. Manager Williams
reported that Trustee Webster was also not expected to attend.
b)   September 7, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 13, 2004, Study Session. President Duhl said that Trustee Eilers had
phoned in amendments, which had been incorporated into the minutes and Trustee
Eilers indicated he had no further comments. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the
minutes and Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   July 20, 2004, Regular Meeting. President Duhl said that Trustee Eilers
had phoned in amendments to these minutes as well and Trustee Eilers indicated
he had no further comments. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the minutes and
Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-22-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1128 Mt. Pleasant – Adoption.
President Duhl stated that this request for a variation from the Zoning
Ordinance to grant a corner setback variation to permit the construction of a
single family home at 1128 Mt. Pleasant was discussed at the July 6th Council
meeting and introduced at the July 20th meeting. He called for a motion to
adopt the ordinance. There being no comments from the members of the audience
nor the Trustees, Trustee Eilers moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-22-2004,
seconded by Trustee Abell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Webster and Ritchell.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2004 – Amendments to the Village Code Pertaining to
Appointment of Deputy Police and Fire Chiefs and to Fire Chief’s Residency –
Adoption. President Duhl called for a motion to adopt this ordinance, which was
introduced at the July 20th Council meeting and would amend the Village Code to
authorize Fire and Police Chiefs to each appoint one Deputy Chief for their
respective departments and would amend the residency requirements for the Fire
Chief.
Attorney Janega reviewed the revisions made to the draft ordinance since its
introduction, which include an amendment to the relocation requirement from 90
days to 12 months and the insertion of a sentence in Section 2.6.0.035(A) that
incorporates the statutory standards of eligibility for deputy police chief.
Trustee Abell moved to adopt the amendments to the draft ordinance, seconded by
Trustee Eilers and by voice vote, the motion passed unanimously. Trustee
Greenough moved to adopt Ordinance MC-12-2004 as amended, and the motion was
seconded by Trustee Abell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Ritchell and Webster.
c)   Ordinance No. M-23-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1205 Tower Road –
Introduction. Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio reviewed this
request for a Front Setback variation, which was discussed at the Council’s
July 6th meeting, to permit the construction of a new single-family home on the
subject property.
Attorney Janega commented on the extensive recitals in the draft ordinance that
outline the hardships and difficulties particular to this lot and that could
substantiate granting the variation. She directed the Trustees’ attention to a
drawing in the agenda materials which showed the large number of parcels on the
unusually long block, noting the block also contains three different zoning
districts. She said the purpose of requiring a setback is to prevent homes from
being too near the street and noted that the three eastern R-3 lots in this
block have substantially larger setbacks than is required, which skews the
average of the setbacks on the block so that it is farther away from the
standard. She further said that the key reasons to grant a variation are if
there are particular hardships and unique circumstances to the property. She
stated that applying the zoning requirements to this parcel places a burden on
the parcel that outweighs the public benefit and is contrary to the original
intent of the setback requirements.
Mr. Adam King, the applicant, commented that the ordinance does articulate the
purpose of the variation request and that one of the unique characteristics is
that the formula has the opposite effect of protecting the block face, because
of the two houses that are set further back, which throws off the average. He
said that he has requested this setback variation to balance the house in the
block and line it up with its neighbors. He also commented that allowing the
house to be built with a 40-foot setback would satisfy all of the conditions of
preserving the character of the neighborhood and still provide for reasonable
back and side yards. He further stated that he is not asking for a variation
because they want to build a larger house but because they want it to be more
appealing to them and to their neighbors, although he understood that
variations are not usually granted for new construction.
Tom Healy, 1173 Tower Road, spoke against granting the setback variation
stating that it should not even be introduced. He stated that the Zoning Board
had found that the same house could be built without a variation, which would
make the front yard about 50 feet deep. He said the testimony has been that the
same size house can be built within the zoning requirements and variations are
not supposed to be granted if the house can be built within them. He said that
if this is true as testified, then the Council shouldn’t grant the variation.
He commented on the effect on the visual continuity of the block if an English
chateau is placed 40 feet from the street. He also commented on the provisions
in the Zoning Ordinance that specifically state that houses with front yards
larger than 100 feet should be excluded from the setback provisions, which
implies that yards less than 100 feet be included in the formula and also the
provision that the maximum front yard setback may be no more than 15 feet more
than the adjoining lots. He didn’t understand why there has been no discussion
of these two provisions because they apply to this variation request. He
suggested that if the provisions don’t apply, then the Village Code should be
changed.
Trustee Abell asked whether, if this ordinance were granted, there are any lots
on the block face that could have as little as a 30-foot setback and also
whether a smaller setback would be allowed if there should be further teardowns
on this block.
Attorney Janega replied that, if the application of the average front setback
requirement were removed, it would result in the two lots on either end of the
street having 30-foot setbacks. In the instance of this property, the setback
variation would help keep the property in line with the other homes on the
block. She added that if the average is applied, each successive teardown would
then have a larger setback.
Trustee Greenough said that the objective of this rule has always been to bring
houses forward to conform to the block face because it gives more privacy at
the rear of the lot. She said that, in this instance, following the rule
actually ends up being contrary to the goals of the Village and that, if the
rule is forcing a bad result, that is really inappropriate for the block, then
the Council would have to take a look at the rule and the applicant’s case.
Trustee Tucker said that she initially didn’t favor granting this variation
because she is concerned about it being new construction and is sensitive to
the community’s concern about bulk. She said, however, that as she reviewed
this application, she didn’t see that the owner and applicant are maximizing
bulk, but rather that they are trying to preserve open space with the larger
side yards. She felt that the owner and architect had demonstrated concern
about the neighbor’s requests, and is in favor of granting the variation.
Tom Eilers stated that it is his opinion that the consistency of front yard
setbacks is most important and the Council should approve the variation
request.
Trustee Abell said that he was in favor of the variation the last time it was
discussed and also noted that the side yards are wider than what is required.
He said he would approve the variation request.
President Duhl said that the Council had confronted this issue several years
ago with respect to another house and all of the reasons for granting the
variation that were expressed then apply to this property as well. He said that
the Council at that time reviewed the ordinance and decided to look at
individual properties as they come up rather than change the ordinance. He
further said that, although variances are not usually granted for new
construction, in his opinion, the circumstances in this case deserve to be
considered.
Trustee Greenough moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-23-2004. Trustee Abell
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1375 and 1376. Trustee Eilers reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1375 in the amount of $1,098,013.13 and Warrant List No. 1376 in the
amount of $723,592.37. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough and
Tucker Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Ritchell and Webster.
b)   Commercial PUD Study. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the Plan Commission’s
recommendation to amend the Zoning Ordinance to create Planned Unit Development
standards. He said the Plan Commission had looked at Chapter 5 of the
Comprehensive Plan that mentions the Green Bay Road corridor and business
districts as sites for redevelopment and had put together a memorandum listing
some items the Council might want to consider for the Zoning Ordinance.
Plan Commission Chair Gwen Trindl spoke to the Council, mentioning Ken Behles
who wrote the memorandum, and acknowledging Barb Aquilino and Bob Horne of the
Business Community Development Committee (BCDC). Mrs. Trindl said the Plan
Commission had been concerned about this issue because there will be areas in
the Village that will be redeveloped or developed in a different way from their
current use. She said the Commission believes that they can propose an
ordinance which would meet the standards of the Village and respond to
redevelopment concerns. She suggested that the post office site is not the
least of the areas for redevelopment and that the Plan Commission and BCDC,
whose members also have suggestions for redevelopment, will work well together.
She asked the Council to authorize the Commissions to develop some
recommendations for Planned Unit Development standards.
Barb Aquilino commented that this idea was discussed at the last BCDC meeting
and the members are happy to participate in any way the Council would like.
Manager Williams was in favor of the Plan Commission studying this issue and
suggested that they also consider the Grand Foods block, which is currently
outside the overlay district and could support such businesses as a restaurant,
for instance.
Bob Horne addressed the Council next and stated that the BCDC has been talking
about this issue, as well as density in the business district. He recommended
that if the Village could demonstrate that we are flexible, it will invite more
people to participate. He said that it is very difficult to get deals in this
market, but it may be better if there was a more flexible standard, citing a
recent experience he had with that which may have ended differently if he had
known that there was some flexibility.
President Duhl asked that the BCDC and the Plan Commission work together to
move forward.
Trustee Tucker commented that she also thinks the Commissions should move
forward and asked for Ms. Janega’s comments.
Ms. Janega said that the concept of a planned development is very appealing
and, although Winnetka has a very limited area for this type of development, it
allows the community to be creative as property is redeveloped. She said that
usually a planned unit development is thought of as mixed uses on large acreage
parcels, but the Village can define the scope of development up front and then
set aside areas to be developed for apartments or commercial retail, for
example. She echoed the Village Manager’s comments and also asked the
Commissions to consider areas for office space.
Trustee Eilers said that other communities have established standards such as
these and that the Village won’t be a pioneer in this area. He said that, in
his opinion, this is definitely a tool that the Village can use for more
control over development.
Trustee Greenough commented that the more we can plan, the more attractive
buildings will be and she is in favor.
Trustee Abell stated that he is also in favor.
President Duhl said the consensus is unanimously in favor, and thanked Ms.
Trindl and the Plan Commission for raising the issue, studying it and also
doing the homework.
c)   Recycling Options. Mr. Saunders requested that the Council consider
awarding a contract to Onyx Waste Services Midwest, Inc., for curbside
recycling services. He explained that Onyx had submitted the lowest of three
fee proposals for a contract that is based upon the Village’s current contract,
but with modifications to allow the use of wheeled toters for the collection
container, the recycling of additional plastic materials and single day
collection.
Mr. Saunders said that the current recycling contract with Onyx expired in
June, and has been extended on a month-to-month basis. He said that the
proposed contract is for a term of 5 years, with an option for a sixth year and
the cost is $22,000 less than the current contract due to the change to single
day collection. He said that a number of people had tried out the new
containers at the Village yards and while they weren’t unanimously liked, there
were several favorable comments.
Mr. Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Road, expressed his opinion that there has not been
any communication with the residents on the use of the containers, although it
affects every resident, changes the whole recycling system in the town, and
places a piece of equipment in their garages that they’ve never had before. He
said that there has been no public comment since the June meeting and since the
Council is representative of the Village residents, there should be a public
hearing. He suggested that what the Council is considering should be published
in the Village newsletter or the Pioneer Press to inform the residents and ask
for public comment.
Mr. Kates also commented on the contract itself, questioning the terms for
availability and distribution of the 100 small carts, damaged carts and who is
responsible for the cost of replacement, service to the school districts and
park district and suggested that the $5.00 charge for missed pickups is not
high enough to discourage them. He further commented that the contract allows
the Village the option of charging a fee for the carts and wondered if a policy
had been established for this.
President Duhl replied that a policy has not been established.
Mr. Saunders said that as to the issue of the small and large carts, the 100
small carts represent an estimate of how many are needed, and the Village will
be the arbitrator of who gets the smaller carts. He further said that if more
than 100 people want them, then the Village will get more of them for anyone
who wants a smaller cart. In response to questions from Trustee Eilers and
Trustee Abell, Mr. Saunders replied that a homeowner could have a second large
or small cart, but would probably have to pay for it as they do now for a
second blue bin.
Trustee Tucker asked Mr. Saunders how the Village would implement the system
and if there would be information and instructions with the delivery of the
carts.
Mr. Saunders said they would be sending out a flyer when they know the delivery
date of the carts. Onyx will purchase the carts and have them delivered to the
Village’s maintenance facility and then they will be delivered to the residents
with instructions.
President Duhl asked the Council their thoughts about further public hearings.
He said his own view is that there have been discussions on this issue and no
one has come forward, except for Mr. Kates. He said they have also obtained
comments from other communities who have had support for it, but he is willing
to have a public hearing in September, although he would be surprised if it was
well attended. He also thought a $22,000 savings is significant given the
Village’s financial situation.
Trustee Abell said he agreed with President Duhl, although initially, he
thought that there wasn’t a lot of public input necessary because it was a
routine renewal and an upgrade in the way we collect recyclables, but he would
be willing to have a public hearing.
Trustee Greenough commented that Winnetka is behind the curve in terms of where
this industry is going and other communities have switched to the wheeled
toters several years ago, but said that she would be willing to have a public
hearing.
Trustee Abell asked if waiting to hold a public hearing would cause any
difficulties with Onyx.
Mr. Saunders replied that it should not present a problem.
Trustee Eilers commented that he has no problem with holding a public hearing,
but feels this is the way the industry is going.
Trustee Tucker said the spring meeting was well attended and the carts were
demonstrated. She stated that the last two issues of the Winnetka Report,
spring and summer, had articles about the new carts and she has spoken to
several residents and hasn’t heard any objections. She feels it has been
adequately advertised, but if Mr. Kates believes that a further public hearing
is needed, she would agree to that. She also commented upon a Northfield
resident’s letter to Pioneer Press lauding the containers. She said that it
does not make sense to keep an antiquated system that is costing the Village
more, when the new contract will improve service and reduce costs. The comments
she has heard from neighbors are that they welcome the single container and
residents who may have difficulty with the larger carts or who are seniors, may
choose the smaller containers.
President Duhl said that the Council would schedule a public hearing for the
Sept 7th meeting and place an article into the Winnetka Report.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustee Eilers reported that at its meeting, the Chamber of Commerce
discussed holiday shopping, merchants remaining open on Thursday evenings
beginning after Thanksgiving, a holiday shopping day when shoppers will receive
15% off of merchandise and expressed their thanks to the Village for extending
the length of time the holiday lights will be up. He also reported on the Fire
Department’s discussion with the Chamber regarding the necessity of more
frequent inspections of businesses, that sidewalk sales were down, in general,
and that the Chamber is aware of the kiosk issue that is scheduled for the
September 14th study session.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Tucker moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Ritchell and Webster. The Council adjourned to Executive
Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees. Absent: Trustee Abell. Also present, Village Manager Doug
Williams and Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Tucker, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Ritchell and
Webster. The meeting adjourned at 9:45 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
August 17, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 7, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
17, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, and Herb Ritchell. Absent: Trustees Jessica
Tucker and David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, and one
person in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 8(b), Gage Street Water Main, has been
removed from the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum.
a)   September 7, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   September 14, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
None.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-24-2004 – Special Use Permit: 723 Elm St. - Introduction.
Manager Williams reviewed a request to allow a health club in existing vacant
commercial space. He noted that this is similar to the request recently granted
by the Council to permit a health club facility in the old Comcast studio. This
facility, Curves, will provide strength training, stretching, and aerobic
exercise to women. It will be located in the basement of the building which is
in the C-2 Retail Overlay zoning district. The proposed hours of operation are
9:00 – 11:30 a.m. and 3:30 – 6:00 p.m. Monday through Friday. The operator
hopes to expand the hours as the club grows. He reported that both the Zoning
Board of Appeals and the Plan Commission had recommended approval of the
Special Use Permit.
Manager Williams pointed out that a parking study had been conducted and
reviewed the by Village Engineer who is of the opinion that “ . . . the parking
supply is as adequate for curves as it would be for any other business.”
Pam Amatore, 7719 W. Sherwin, Chicago, the applicant, spoke briefly in support
of her application.
President Duhl asked Ms. Amatore how many clients she expected to serve in a
day. She responded 50-70 women.
Trustee Abell inquired as to whether she ever foresaw a need to be open between
11:00 a.m.and 3:00 p.m. When Ms. Amatore replied that she did not believe that
she would need to be open those hours, Mr. Abell suggested that the ordinance
specify that the business be closed between those hours.
President Duhl asked Ms. Amatore to consider whether this would be a hardship
for her. She said that she would think about it but that she didn’t think it
would be.
Trustee Eilers remarked that there will be parking aggravation, and that users
will have to make accommodations. He said he hopes all will cooperate because
the Village wants its businesses to succeed and enjoy the synergy of their
proximity to one another.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-24-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega arrived at 7:40 p.m.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2004 – Film Production - Introduction. Manager
Williams explained that the proposed ordinance amends the Village’s ordinance
establishing regulations for film production activities in the Village.
Attorney Janega went on to explain that current permit fee formula for film
production is the same as for special events. She noted, however, that film
production is significantly different in that in virtually every instance, it
involves a non-resident, private business entity that seeks to use the Village
and its resources for private, commercial purposes, to the exclusion of the use
of those resources by the Village and the general public. Therefore, film
production has direct and immediate impacts on the Village and places greater
demands on the Village’s resources than do special events.
She said that after the recent filming of “Ocean’s 12,” Finance Director Ed
McKee expressed concern about the current compensation formulas, leading the
Council to consider this matter at its July study session at which time the
Council agreed that the current fee structure for film production is not
satisfactory.
After reviewing the Council’s discussions at the July Study Session, Attorney
Janega explained that the proposed ordinance attempts to implement the
Council’s directive to create a more flexible way to charge film production
companies on a case-by-case basis and to negotiate fees for use of the Village
resources based on factors that would typically be considered in establishing
private sector market rates. The draft ordinance also requires the performance
security to be in the form of a cash deposit.
Ms. Janega went on to say that the proposed ordinance divides fees related to
film production into two separate components: (1) an application processing fee
intended to cover the permit processing costs, and (2) film production
activities charges set on a case-by-case basis to recover the Village’s direct
and indirect costs after determining the full impact of the proposed film
production activities. This latter category includes hard costs, soft costs,
and premium costs. Ms. Janega provided examples of each of the categories.
Finally, Attorney Janega noted that, should the Council proceed with
implementing the recommended fee restructuring, a companion resolution amending
the general fee resolution would be prepared for Council’s action at the time
the Code amendment is adopted.
After a brief discussion at which the Trustees suggested some minor changes,
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-10-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1377 and 1378. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1377 in the amount of $629,077.44 and Warrant List No. 1378 in the
amount of $536,002.11. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Webster and Tucker.
b)   Gage Street and Tower Court Water Main Bids. As announced earlier, this
item was removed from the Agenda.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. None
b)   Manager. None.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
President Duhl announced that there would be no Executive Session.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Ritchell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Tucker and
Webster. The meeting adjourned at 8:47 p.m.
