Recording Secretary
September 7, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 21, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 7, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Water &
Electric Bryan McInturff and 12 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced the addition of Item 8(d), Consideration of the Log
House for the National Register, to the Agenda. The Council then approved the
Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum.
a)   September 14, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   September 21, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 10, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   August 17, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-23-2004 – Zoning Variation: 1205 Tower Rd. – Adoption.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting a Front Setback variation to permit the construction of a single
family home on the subject property. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
b)   Ordinance No. M-24-2004 – Special Use Permit: 723 Elm St. - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment to the introduced ordinance that
reflects the Council’s discussions at the time of introduction by specifically
requiring the facility to be closed between the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 3:00
p.m. Monday through Friday.
Trustee Eilers expressed concern about a business agreeing to being closed so
many hours, especially before they have any measure of what their traffic will
be. He said that he could not see that it served any public purpose and he
hoped the applicant had considered this carefully.
Trustee Abell pointed out that at the last meeting, the applicant had agreed to
this restriction.
President Duhl noted that it is not incumbent upon the Council to impose such a
restriction. He said that if the business is not going to have a significant
impact on parking, then there is no real necessity for any restrictions on its
operating hours. He asked the applicant’s representative to address the matter.
She indicated that, although most Curves operations are not terribly busy
during the hours in question, all is dependent upon the location and they hope
to have sufficient clients to justify remaining open throughout the day and on
weekends.
Trustees Abell and Greenough agreed that the restrictions were not particularly
needed.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance by
omitting the restrictions in Section 3 in their entirety. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Attorney Janega suggested that a corresponding paragraph in the recitals be
omitted as well. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, so moved. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adopt, as amended, the
ordinance granting a Special Use Permit to allow a health club in existing
vacant commercial space. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Webster.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2004 – Film Production - Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed proposed amendments to the provisions of the introduced ordinance
pertaining to insurance requirements and reservation of rights, as discussed by
the Council at its last meeting.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the ordinance as
outlined by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt the ordinance as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
Trustee Ritchell suggested that the changes in our ordinance be conveyed to the
Illinois Film Commission.
d)   Resolution No. R-25-2004 – Amending Fee Resolution: Film Permit Fees –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the Village’s fee
resolution to reflect the revised fees for film production and to make one
other minor change, by deleting fees related to milk dealers, which the Village
no longer regulates.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-25-
2004. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
e)   Resolution No. R-26-2004 – National Incident Management System (NIMS) –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed this resolution adopting the National
Incident Management System as required by Homeland Security Directive 5. She
explained that NIMS is intended to provide a consistent nationwide approach for
federal, state, local and tribal governments, and for private sector and non-
governmental organizations to work effectively and efficiently together to
prepare for, respond to, and recover from domestic incidents, regardless of
cause, size, or complexity. She added that, when the new federal fiscal year
begins on October 1, 2004, the adoption of the NIMS will likely be required to
be eligible for federal emergency grant funds.
Trustee Tucker inquired as to the cost of implementation. Manager Williams
responded that it is minimal, mostly a small amount of staff time.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
26-2004. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
f)   Ordinance No. M-25-2004 – Zoning Variations: Bluhm Subdivision – 701-703
Sheridan – Introduction. Mr. Norkus reviewed requests for a proposed
resubdivision to consolidate two existing parcels into a single lot to allow
for construction of a single new home, together with a zoning variation to
temporarily permit two houses to exist on the newly consolidated lot. He
explained that the new residence proposed for the consolidated lot is located
wholly within the confines of the existing eastern, lakefront lot, and that the
owner has requested a zoning variation to permit the temporary existence of two
dwellings on the single consolidated lot, so he can reside in the existing
residence at 703 Sheridan while the proposed new residence is built on the
eastern lot. Upon completion of the new residence, the existing 703 Sheridan
residence would be required to be demolished, and the lot graded and
landscaped. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to
recommend approval of this variation request.
Mr. Norkus added that the proposed resubdivision fully complies with the zoning
standards for lots in the R-2 zoning district, but noted that the consolidation
results in an irregularly shaped lot, the impact of which was reviewed by the
Plan Commission. The Plan Commission voted 6-2 in favor of recommending
approval of the subdivision subject to a restrictive covenant maintaining side
yards of 43.07 feet, rather than the 37.01 feet required by the Zoning
Ordinance for the consolidated lot. Although staff had also recommended that a
restrictive covenant requiring the side yards to be equally distributed between
north and south side yards because of the number of trees located along both
property lines, the Plan Commission voted unanimously to recommend that the
applicant be allowed to maintain the standard north side yard of 12 feet.
Mr. Norkus pointed out that, as sited, the construction of the proposed
residence would result in the removal of twenty-one trees with a diameter of
10” or greater. A tree protection plan was included in the Agenda packet.
Finally, Mr. Norkus reported that in their current configuration, the 703
Sheridan (west) lot is accessed from Sheridan Road, while the 701 Sheridan
(east) lot is accessed by the private easement that crosses the front yard of
adjoining lakefront properties and the rear yard of the adjoining Sheridan Road
properties. The private roadway that provides access to 701, 695 and 693
Sheridan is located in a 20-foot wide easement, however the pavement width is
approximately 15 feet. While the private street serves as access to only three
parcels, its dead-end configuration requires it to function as a two-way lane.
The narrow width of the street and an abrupt turn results in a less than
optimal layout.
In addition, 701 Sheridan accesses the 20-foot wide easement by a strip of land
that is only 8 feet wide. While the pavement width extends beyond 8 feet onto
the neighboring property, the legal width of access from the end of the private
lane is 8 feet, a width that is inadequate for emergency vehicle access.
Accordingly, staff recommended that the Plan Commission consider approval of
the plat be conditioned on the owner/applicant recording a document releasing
all rights of access to the 20’ wide private street easement and further
requiring that driveways be configured to access the consolidated property
directly from Sheridan Road via the 96 foot wide frontage.
The Plan Commission, however, voted 5-3 in favor of allowing the applicant to
continue to access the property by the private street, and did not recommend
requiring access from Sheridan Road. In light of this decision by the Plan
Commission to permit the applicant to continue to rely on the 8’ wide access
way, Acting Fire Chief Scott Smith has requested that the Council consider
requiring the new residence to be equipped with fire sprinklers to alleviate
firefighting access concerns arising out of the 8-foot wide driveway access.
President Duhl mentioned that the Council had had a similar request a few years
ago. Mr. Norkus agreed, saying that at that time the Village conditioned the
Certificate of Occupancy for the new home on the old house being demolished. He
said that in this instance, Staff recommends that there be a cash bond also
required of the applicant.
Trustee Abell indicated that he was in general agreement with the proposal, but
he expressed some concern that there is no outside limit of time placed on the
temporary residency. Mr. Norkus responded that the outside limit is defined by
the length of time in which the building permit is valid. He added that,
generally speaking, there is a financial incentive to complete construction as
quickly as possible.
Trustee Abell expressed his presumption that the 8’ access cannot be widened
without an easement from the neighbor. Mr. Norkus indicated that he believed
there had been some discussions on this matter.
Manager Williams mentioned that this development presents an opportunity for
the Village to improve its water distribution system to the six different
properties in the area. He said that there is now a 6” line that dead ends at
the driveway to the 701 Sheridan property. Working with the developer to
utilize easements, the Village would install a water main that would loop
around, enabling water to be provided from two different directions. He said
that the cost for installing this loop could be absorbed in the Village’s
budget.
Mr. Williams added that, absent some easement, the Fire Department is very
concerned about getting its life safety vehicles, in particular its fire
trucks, back to the 701 residence because of the 8’ access. He said that the
best solution is to figure out some access to the house other than the
existing.
Attorney Janega then referred to drafter’s notes incorporated into the draft
ordinance. She commented that there is nothing in the record to indicate where
the construction vehicles and traffic would be and noted that this is not
exactly the same situation as existed with the temporary dual residence
permitted in Indian Hill, where the owners used the existing house for
construction storage, not as a residence.
Trustee Eilers said that if the applicant was granted permanent access off the
existing easement, the Sheridan Road curb cut to 703 would be lost. .
Trustee Greenough commented that given that there is no record as to where
construction vehicles will be staged and parked, that means that all of this
activity will have to take place along this small, narrow roadway.
Manager Williams suggested that the Council might want to address with
applicant the issue of a temporary construction path across the 703 lot.
Trustee Eilers raised a question about the discrepancy in the information
purportedly received by the applicant regarding the side yard requirements. He
remarked that it is important that applicants be provided with the correct
information. Mr. Norkus reviewed the process and the calculations, and noted
that the possibility of an additional side yard setback requirement had been
stated in a letter to the owner’s architect before any applications were filed.
Trustee Tucker asked whether the proposed house could be built without the
consolidation and whether it would be possible for the owner to resubdivide the
consolidated lot in the future. Mr. Norkus responded that it could not and that
a future resubdivision would be unlikely.
The applicant, Andy Bluhm, introduced his development team and spoke in support
of his requests. He indicated that the design goal of the new home is to
minimize its appearance of bulk from the street. He voiced no objection to
putting up the cash bond, but expressed strong objection to having to provide
access to the property from Sheridan Road. He said they are trying to preserve
as large a park-like front yard as possible and wish to remove as few trees as
possible. Mr. Bluhm presented pictures of the site for the Trustees’ review. He
also remarked that no code requires the installation of any kind of sprinkler
system.
Trustee Tucker asked whether an assessment had been made of the number of trees
along the north and south property lines. Mr. Bluhm indicated that none had
been done.
David Schenk, Mr. Bluhm’s attorney, commented on the drafter’s notes in the
draft ordinance. He attempted to clarify that once the subdivision and tree
survey are approved, that the applicant will not have to come back to the
Council for any kind of additional approval.
President Duhl asked Mr. Norkus to clarify how the different side yard
requirements were calculated.
Mr. Norkus explained that lot area affects all calculations, and that the
numbers originally provided by the applicant did not exactly coincide with the
numbers provided later by the surveyor. In addition, the plans that were
submitted were for a house on a lot that had not yet gone through the
subdivision process.
Trustee Abell asked whether all of the issues have been resolved. Mr. Norkus
responded that at this point, all of the variation issues, except those under
consideration here, have been resolved, and that the proposed house otherwise
conforms to the Zoning Ordinance.
President Duhl asked Attorney Janega to clarify some of the questions raised by
applicant’s attorney about the draft ordinance with regard to tree removal and
fence construction, which she did. Ms. Janega remarked that the language in the
ordinance must be based upon actual language from witnesses attesting to facts
contained in the minutes of the ZBA meeting. The information in the draft
ordinance is based on a detailed reading of the record in the context of the
variation standards that normally apply. She explained that one of the
difficulties in this case is that the ZBA deals with zoning issues, while the
Plan Commission deals with planning issues that are aimed at ensuring
consistency in development, and that sometimes the planning considerations and
the specifics of the Zoning Ordinance are not fully in sync. She said that the
setback issue in this case is a classic example of such an inconsistency.
Trustee Eilers expressed some concern that the setback requirement seems to
have been changed late in the process. He asked that every attempt be made to
provide homeowners with accurate information as early in the process as
possible. Manager Williams responded that in this case, the final determination
could not be made until an accurate measure of the lot area was available.
Trustee Tucker inquired about the staging of equipment and construction
activity. She said she would prefer to see a nice, tree-lined driveway leading
directly to the new home rather than to have it accessed via the off-site
easement.
Mr. Bluhm reiterated his strong objection to any kind of direct access off of
Sheridan Road. He indicated that the neighbor who would be most inconvenienced
by construction equipment and traffic had voiced no objection to the proposed
plan.
The architect, Peter Gluck, reviewed the extensive effort expended by the
applicant to preserve as many trees as possible, while preserving the park-like
environment. He added that it would be very difficult to provide any kind of a
construction road through the 703 property.
Manager Williams asked about the possibility of accessing the lakefront
property via an access road along the south side of the 703 property. Mr. Gluck
indicated that this would not insure the survival of the existing trees.
Trustee Tucker said that she would like to hear from the Village Forester, Jim
Stier, about the impact on the trees of centering the house on the 701 lot. She
also asked for the Forester’s assessment of the impact on trees of providing a
construction driveway through the 703 property.
Trustee Eilers pointed out that since the Fire Department had gone on record as
requiring a 12 foot access to the property from the existing easement, there is
a life safety issue. Therefore, if Mr. Bluhm is unable to secure the necessary
easement from his neighbor to meet this life safety test, then he would be
forced to access off of Sheridan Road. President Duhl responded that that
decision would be up to the Council.
Trustee Greenough raised a question about the impact of construction on the
bluffs.
Mr. Gluck indicated that the house would be built into the bluff, after
significant excavation. He said that when the project is completed, additional
beach area will have been added and the house will be set back about 100’ from
the water’s edge. Mr. Gluck called on Charlie Shabica, a coastal scientist to
respond to questions about the bluff.
Mr. Shabica indicated that the applicant would be building a breakwater and
armored headlands to diffuse the waves’ energy and create a small bay area.
Ultimately this will result in a wider beach area, by approximately 75-100 ft.
He said that in the natural state, bluffs were not covered by trees, and that
contrary to popular theory, trees roots do not, in fact, really help hold the
bluff together. He said that he recommends to coastal property owners that they
get rid of as many trees as possible on the bluff because they were never a
native component of the bluff community.
Trustee Ritchell asked about the impact this would have on neighbors. Mr.
Shabica said that it would be better for all. Mr. Shabica stated that all of
the permits necessary to perform this work have already been obtained from the
respective governing agencies. He recommended beginning construction of the
shore protection before beginning construction on the house, so as to secure
the shoreline.
The Village Forester, Jim Stier, addressed some of the questions raised
regarding trees. He referred to the Tree Removal and Replacement material
contained in the Agenda packet.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Stier whether he could comment on Trustee Tucker’s
question about centering the proposed construction instead of placing it closer
to the north property line.
Mr. Stier said that anything can happen in a construction area. He indicated
that, upon his previous survey of the property, at first blush, there appear to
be more trees worth saving on the north property line. He indicated that he
briefly discussed with the developer’s contractor the possibility of installing
a temporary construction access road along the south property line of the 703
property, despite the owner’s objections to this plan.
Jim True, the Construction Manager, acknowledged this conversation, but said
that they did not need an access drive off Sheridan Road.
President Duhl suggested that this matter, in particular the access issue, be
revisited in greater detail at the September 14th Study Session and suggested
that the draft ordinance be introduced in order to keep the process moving
forward.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to introduce the draft
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Abell thanked the applicant for his patience and understanding.
Mr. Bluhm indicated that in the meantime, he would approach his neighbor about
negotiating an easement.
Trustee Greenough noted that that presupposes that access over the private lane
is appropriate, a determination that the Council has not yet made.
g)   Resolution No. R-28-2004 – Plat of Consolidation: Bluhm Subdivision –
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that this resolution would grant the
consolidation of the two properties, as just discussed. This matter will not be
voted on until the time of adoption of the zoning variation.
h)   Resolution No. M-27-2004 – Commending Ron Colpaert – Adoption. President
Duhl read this resolution commending former Fire Chief Ron Colpaert for his
service to the Village.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
27-2004. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
i)   Resolution No. M-29-2004 – New Trier Technical Cooperative – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that this resolution would authorize an
intergovernmental agreement between the Village and the New Trier Township
Educational Cooperative. The agreement sets the terms under which member
districts can place fiber optic cable in vacant conduits of the Village’s
electric distribution system.
Trustee Ritchell inquired whether there would be any capacity problems. Manager
Williams indicated that there would not.
The Trustees briefly discussed whether the agreement should be left open-ended
or whether an outside limit for the term of the contract should be included.
Manager Williams recommended that it be left open-ended. Attorney Janega
pointed out that there is a built-in evergreen provision that provides for
extension, and automatic renewal in 3-year increments. The Village must also
give a 3-year notice to terminate the agreement. She explained that there was
no outside limit to the length of the contract, but suggested that allowing six
3-year extensions would result in a 21-year agreement, which would closely
match the 20-year basis used by the Village in determining the compensation.
There was a consensus to include some outside limit on the contract.
Trustee Tucker voiced some concern that Hubbard Woods School was omitted from
this agreement. It was explained that this was due to financial constraints and
that the School would be incorporated into the network as soon as funds were
available and when the network is taken into Glencoe.
Manager Williams explained how the fees for the contract were calculated.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
29-2004. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1379 and 1380. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1379 in the amount of $1,562,587.58 and Warrant List No. 1380 in the
amount of $520,195.36. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
b)   Bid No. 04-015: Gage Street and Tower Court Water Main Bids. Mr. McInturff
reviewed the bids for the 8-inch water main extension on Gage Street and Tower
Court to provide water for fire protection to various commercial properties
east of Green Bay Rd. He noted that, as the commercial areas of the Village are
being redeveloped, buildings are required to install sprinkler systems. The
building at the corner of Tower Court has installed a system and requires the
extension of the main from Green Bay in order to supply an adequate amount of
water to the sprinkler system. He reported that staff had met affected
businesses and residents to review the project and initiate the construction
information process.
Mr. McInturff recommended that bid be awarded to Bolder Contractors, Inc. in
the amount of $165,946.00, using the Alternate #2 plan, which would relocate
the storm sewer eight feet to the west, allowing for the installation of the
main without casing.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees:
None.
c)   Bid No. 04-016: Pad Mount Transformers. Mr. McInturff reviewed the bids
for the purchase and delivery of single-phase and three-phase pad mount
transformers to be purchased on an ‘as needed’ basis from September 1, 2004 to
August 31, 2005. He explained that award of the bid is based on the unit price
plus the guaranteed losses on transformers times the estimated quantities that
were listed on the bid document.
Mr. McInturff recommended that the bid for the purchase of single-phase
transformers be awarded to Central Maloney, Inc., in the amount of $80,000,
$43,000 this fiscal year and $37,000 next year, and for three-phase
transformers to RESCO in the amount of $70,000, $40,000 for this year, and
$30,000 next year.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees:
None.
d)   Consideration of Smith-Burnham Log House for National Register. Trustee
Greenough reviewed a request from the Illinois Historic Preservation Agency for
comments on whether the property should be nominated to the National Register.
She read a draft letter proposed for submittal to the Agency on behalf of the
Village, endorsing the nomination.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the draft letter.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported on a recent meeting of the Business
Community Development Commission. He mentioned that two retailers and three
property owners were present and encouraged all interested parties to attend.
He indicated that the Committee had reviewed the preliminary results of the
business retail survey, saying that the primary issues mentioned were parking,
lighting, and signage. Mr. Eilers said a property owner survey was being
drafted. All of the surveys will be combined and evaluated for presentation to
the Village Council. In closing Mr. Eilers said that the Village Manager’s
PowerPoint presentation on Home Rule had been informative and well received by
the Commission.
b)   Attorney.  Attorney Janega provided a status report on the Village’s work
toward meeting the Affordable Housing Act’s requirement to develop a plan. She
explained that staff continues to research and compile information regarding
the Affordable Housing Act and the best ways for the Village to comply with the
law. She noted that, in addition to the initial Council Study Session,
President Duhl had met with Representative Hamos and Senator Schoenberg and, at
their suggestion, submitted questions seeking legal opinions from the Attorney
General. Ms. Janega also reported that she had attended a recent informational
meeting organized by IHDA, that she and Mr. D’Onofrio are scheduled to attend a
second meeting later this month, and that they will attend additional meetings
as they are scheduled. Ms. Janega mentioned that at the meeting she recently
attended, she ran into Village officials from Glencoe, who informed her that a
Glencoe resident had said at an open meeting that Ms. Janega had advised the
Village of Winnetka not to comply with the Affordable Housing Act. She stated
that, because of the public nature of that statement, she felt the need to
respond on the public record and had therefore spoken with President Duhl and
obtained his consent to disclose her communications with the Village. She
stated unequivocally that at no time has she ever advised the Village of
Winnetka to disregard a law. She added that she would be requesting that a
similar statement be added to Glencoe’s public record. President Duhl strongly
affirmed Ms. Janega’s statement and repeated earlier statements to the effect
that as elected village officials it is incumbent upon the Council to follow
the law and that there has never been any thought or discussion about not doing
so. Trustee Eilers and Trustee Tucker confirmed President Duhl’s statements.
In closing, President Duhl announced that an open forum on Affordable Housing
hosted by the League of Women Voters, would be held on Sunday, September 19th,
at Wilmette Junior High School and that he would be part of the panel.
c)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village had once again
received the Certification of Achievement for Excellence in Financial
Reporting, a very high honor and recognition of the Finance Department’s
efforts.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Trucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Tucker,
and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 10:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:35 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Absent: Trustee
Webster. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Tucker, Greenough, Ritchell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
The meeting adjourned at 11:40 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
September 14, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: October 5, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 14, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Tom Eilers, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Water &
Electric Bryan McInturff and approximately 20 persons in the audience.
2)   Kiosk Policy.
Mr. Norkus reviewed the status of the construction plans for an improved
community event sign kiosk, as approved by the Village Council in January. The
kiosk will be located at the intersection of Oak, Linden, and Green Bay on
property owned by SBC Communications. The existing facility allows for the
display of a single sign, but the conceptual design for the new facility
provides for up to three separate signs. Mr. Norkus noted that the multiple
panels would allow for greater flexibility in allocating usage. He distributed
an elevation drawing of the proposed sign and presented a full size mock-up of
a single sign. It is anticipated that construction of the new kiosk would cost
approximately $16,000 to fabricate and install. Mr.Norkus mentioned that there
has been some discussion that ultimately it might be desirable to install a
similar unit in a different location in the Village.
Historically, the signs have been limited to local charitable, civic, and
philanthropic organizations. The Chamber of Commerce, however, has asked the
Council to consider permitting it to have exclusive use of one of the panels
for the purpose of informing the public about business activities and events,
such as the Recognition Luncheon, the Home Health and Garden Show, and Winnetka
Days. Mr. Norkus pointed out that if the Council were to agree to the sign
being used for any commercial purposes, the Sign Code would require amendment,
as well as the approval of SBC.
Trustee Ritchell inquired whether the signs would be back-lighted and who was
funding the sign’s construction, installation, and maintenance. He also asked
the nature of the agreement with SBC.
Mr. Norkus responded that the signs would be illuminated, but not backlit, and
that the Village Council had agreed to provide the funding. He said that
historically the agreement with SBC has been informal, but that efforts are
being made to formalize it. He noted that if necessary, the sign could be
relocated to some location on Village property.
President Duhl noted that the issue at hand is whether to dedicate the control
and use of one panel to the Chamber of Commerce and invited representatives of
the Chamber of Commerce and Business Community Development Commission to
comment.
Barbara Aquilino, Chairperson of the Business Community Development Commission,
reported that the committee had discussed the matter at its August meeting and
concluded that the Chamber’s request to have exclusive use of one panel was
appropriate and recommended that the Council approve it. She added that the
Commission feels that this is an important tool for promoting the ‘Shop
Winnetka’ theme and increasing awareness of the Village’s merchants and
business districts. Ms. Aquilino relayed the Commission’s suggestion that the
Council grant the request on a one-year trial basis to be re-evaluated at the
end of that period.
Cicely Michalak, Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce, also spoke in
support of the request, saying that the Chamber intends to monitor both the
content and the copy to safeguard the presentation and use of the panel space.
She said that the Chamber would like to be able to recognize individual
businesses without promoting individual sales.
Trustee Tucker inquired whether non-Chamber members would be able to use the
dedicated panel. Ms. Michalak responded that no specific policy had been
established but indicated a willingness to work with all to foster a
cooperative relationship for the betterment of the whole community.
Trustee Eilers, also a member of the Business Community Development Commission,
also voiced support for dedicating one panel to the Chamber. He expressed
confidence in the Chamber’s ability to monitor the use of the panel. He said
that it would not be used for advertising, but would enhance the profile of the
breadth and depth of Winnetka’s retailers and highlight in a tasteful way the
businesses in the community. If not done in good taste, the Village can reclaim
the control of the panel.
Trustee Abell said that he was in favor of the proposal.
Trustee Greenough expressed some concern about the definition of “advertising”
as well as about SBC’s response to the concept, given the sign’s historical use
for not-for-profits. She remarked, however, that as explained by Ms. Michalak,
the Chamber does not intend to use its sign for true commercial advertising
purposes, but added that there needs to be a clear understanding of what the
Village is trying to accomplish.
President Duhl commented that it is difficult to articulate specific uses for
the signs in advance. He supported the suggestion of a one-year trial basis to
see what happens as the Council needs to understand both the intended and
unintended consequences. He said it is clear that the Chamber has events worthy
of advertising, but that even a dedicated panel can be preempted should the
need arise.
Roberta Rubin, owner of The Book Stall, said that she hoped that the Council
would trust the Chamber to be tasteful and to comply with the intent of the
Village’s Sign Code.
Trustee Ritchell inquired whether the Chamber would be willing to participate
financially in the construction, installation, and maintenance of the new sign.
Cicely Michalak indicated that the Chamber would be willing to contribute
something to the effort.
Hearing a consensus to proceed with dedicating one panel for use by the
Chamber, President Duhl asked the Village Attorney to proceed with working
through any legislative or administrative details required to enable the
Chamber to use one panel of the kiosk, adding that any agreement should allow
flexibility for the Village to preempt the Chamber’s use if necessary.
3)   Impact of New Development on Private Roads.
President Duhl announced that the Council would discuss Item No. 3 on the
Agenda, the impact of new development on private roads, before the Bluhm
Subdivision. Tom Hermes and Gene Schmidt, residents of Private Road, a private
street, expressed their frustration with the Village’s existing policy whereby
the cost of repairing the damage on a private street becomes the burden of the
neighbors. They explained that the numerous private roads in the Village are
maintained by privately funded associations or groups of neighbors rather than
the Village, so that when construction, particularly the demolition and
replacement of a home, occurs, damage frequently occurs to the private street
and its storm sewer system. They asked that the Village reconsider this policy
and to either raise the existing demolition fee or dedicate some of the funds
currently received by the Village to the private roads to cover their expenses.
President Duhl pointed out that if the street were not private, the Village
would cover any necessary repairs. He said that the Village does not compensate
private property owners for whatever kind of effect might occur because of the
actions of another private property owner. The homeowners who reside on a
private road have entered into covenants agreeing to ownership of the street,
and to ask the Village for compensation would be asking the Village to pay for
private property. He added that there are procedures in place for a private
road to become a public road under the Village’s jurisdiction, and some have
done so.
Manager Williams noted that of the current $10,100 demolition fee, $1,750
offsets the costs of wear and tear to Village-owned property, such as streets
and parkways. He explained how this figure was originally determined.
Mr. Hermes argued that residents of private streets pay taxes just like all
other Village residents but do not receive the same benefits or get any tax
breaks for living on the private streets.
Trustee Abell remarked that the decision to be a private road is made at the
time of subdivision and that the conditions associated therewith had to have
been disclosed at the time of purchase. In addition to which, residents on
private roads actually own the street property, whereas the residents of
Village-owned streets do not.
Mr. Schmidt asked what the Building Department does to protect the residents of
the Village against the utter disregard of builders and developers.
President Duhl responded that the Village does what it can as permitted by law.
He added that if the Village were to become Home Rule, it could be more
proactive in dealing with non-compliant contractors by licensing them.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that Staff spends a great deal of time and effort
trying to control contractors, in addition to which restrictions have been
tightened and new regulations enacted. However, there are approximately 65 to
70 construction sites in the Village at any given time, and the resources for
patrol and enforcement are limited. He added that the Village takes steps, like
videotaping the street before and after construction, to try to ensure that the
street is returned to its original condition, but in the end it is frequently
difficult to determine what damage was caused by what activity. He pointed out
that the Village does attempt to negotiate a settlement between the contractor
and the homeowners on private streets.
President Duhl remarked that the demolition fee had been determined by taking
into account the impact on the Village as a whole, not just upon the residents
of a specific street. He reiterated that he does not see how the Village can
recompense residents of a private street, regardless of how much money it
collects in demolition fees.
Joseph Rosengarden, 1360 Westmoor Trail, echoed Messrs. Hermes and Schmidt’s
remarks.
President Duhl asked Staff to review the matter to see what more, if anything,
the Village can do under these circumstances, within the law, to assist in
making sure that adjoining property owners suffer as little as possible. He
also suggested that Staff explore ways by which residents on a private road
might be able to finance necessary repairs to bring their streets up to Village
standards so that they might, then, be dedicated to the Village. He asked Mr.
Hermes and Mr. Schmidt to poll their neighbors to see whether they could obtain
the necessary majority agreement to do whatever it takes to get the street
under the authority of the Village.
Mr. Duhl pointed out that the earliest this matter would be revisited was the
October 15th Study Session and told the homeowners that there will be no
stipend from the Village. He said the best the Village would be able to do in
the short run is to suggest ways in which to turn the street back over to the
Village. If the neighbors don’t want to do this, they have their own private
course of action against any contractor that causes damage.
An audience member raised a question about holding the Certificate of Occupancy
until the street is restored to its original condition.
Attorney Janega pointed out that the Certificate of Occupancy indicates that
the developer has complied with applicable Village ordinances and applies only
to public streets and parkways over which the Village has control.
Mr. Duhl responded again that the Village can only do what it is permitted by
law to do. He said that the nature of the problem is understood, and the
Village wants to help, but it must do so within the parameters of its rights
and Village policy.
Mr. Norkus commented that private streets are treated no differently than
public streets to the extent that damage is a delineated clear problem
resulting directly from construction activity. He said that the Village
requires that damage be repaired if it definitively results from construction.
However, the system breaks down when a private road is in bad condition prior
to construction, so that it is difficult to determine what damage is clearly
the result of construction activity and what damage is rooted in lack of proper
repair and maintenance over time. The Village cannot require a developer to
contribute to the cost of repairing normal wear and tear.
Trustee Tucker remarked that the Council needs to study the matter further to
determine what it can and cannot legally do to be certain that developers meet
their obligations as well as to determine how best to facilitate the turning
over of private roads to Village control.
4)   Bluhm Subdivision.
President Duhl noted that this matter was continued from the September 7th
Council Meeting, at which time several issues had been held over for further
discussion, including side yard setbacks and location on lot, the water main
loop, construction access, permanent access, zoning variations, procedural
issues, and the entire process and chronology.
Mr. Norkus advised the Council that in the interim between meetings, the side
yard setback matter had been resolved between the parties. He summarized the
paradox that exists with the consolidation of two lots of differing widths. He
said that staff had recommended, and the applicant agreed to, side yard
setbacks above what is required, so that this is no longer a point of
contention.
As to whether the house should be centered on the lot, Mr. Norkus explained
that the Zoning Ordinance allows a side yard as small as 12 ft. on one side and
31 ft on the other side, however, it was suggested that the house be centered.
The applicant expressed opposition to centering the home because of concern for
the well being of trees along the south property line. A further review by
staff of the trees and the tree survey indicated that centering the house would
not, in fact, save any additional trees. Therefore, the 12 ft. setback that has
been proposed by the developer is satisfactory to staff.
Tom Reynolds, the owner of the adjoining property at 695 Sheridan, voiced no
concern about the proposed location of the house.
With regard to the water main issue, Mr. McInturff explained that one of the
goals articulated in the Comprehensive Plan is to eliminate deadends in the
water system. He said that while the current service is adequate, generally, it
is not sufficient for fire protection. Therefore, he would like to have a
complete water main loop on this property to enable water to flow in both
directions and ensure adequate supply for all of the residences on this private
road.
Manager Williams added that in order to install a water main, the Village would
require a different easement than the one currently under consideration. He
confirmed with Mr. Bluhm’s attorney, Mr. Schenk, that there is a general
agreement to such an easement.
Turning to the question of construction access, Mr. Norkus explained that the
applicant has expressed a desire to have construction traffic enter the
property via the private road that runs adjacent to the front yards of the
adjoining properties, rather than directly off of Sheridan Road. He expressed
staff’s continuing concern about the adequacy and appropriateness of using this
private street with its 15-foot wide pavement for construction access, in terms
of the amount and magnitude of the construction traffic required to build the
proposed house. Mr. Norkus voiced concerns about the impact this construction
will have on the adjoining property owners in terms of not only their day-to-
day use of the road, but also in terms of safety. He reported staff’s
suggestion and the applicant’s opposition thereto that construction traffic be
required to come off of Sheridan Road. He acknowledged that the Village
Forester had said that to do so, would impact some of the existing trees on the
703 property, particularly near the opening of the current driveway.
Trustee Eilers pointed out that the private roadway serves three homeowners,
but if the petitioner did not own 703, there would be no opportunity for
construction traffic to come off Sheridan Road.
Attorney Janega responded that if the applicant did not own 703, the project
would be substantially smaller and that the consolidation gives the petitioner
full advantage of the area of 703 as well as access to and from Sheridan Road.
Mr. Reynolds, whose property abuts the subject property and petitioner’s
driveway, objected to having construction traffic use the private roadway. He
advised the Trustees that the pavement slab is 10.5 ft. in width and added
that, therefore, standard size construction equipment could not be used. He
expressed concern about the builder’s ability to get the necessary equipment
down the driveway and around the curve, and about his ability to enter and exit
his own driveway at will. Mr. Reynolds indicated that he was in support of
staff’s recommendation that construction access be off of Sheridan Road. He
said that he could not see how access off of the private roadway would not turn
out to be a huge logjam and jeopardize the safety and welfare of the neighbors.
President Duhl acknowledged that Mr. Reynolds’ concern was legitimate.
David Schenk, the applicant’s attorney, distributed a memorandum prepared by
Mr. Bluhm. He pointed out that even if Mr. Bluhm did not own 703, he would
still be building a 12,000 sq. ft. house that would present the same
construction problems. He added that other neighbors, the Thornes, are
currently under construction and are using the private road for construction
access.
President Duhl responded that the Thornes’ circumstances are different and that
the Council has to consider what can realistically be expected to occur.
Mr. Reynolds noted that the Thornes set up their entrance gate so that
equipment would not be forced to maneuver the tight curve. He added that even
so, there were times during which he could not access or exit his property.
Mr. Norkus commented that staff believes that this situation is different in
that it presents an opportunity to take a nonconforming access point off of a
private street.
Trustee Eilers pointed out that whereas the Thornes have 15 ft. of pavement,
the Bluhms have at best 10.5 ft. of pavement within which to maneuver.
Jim True, the petitioner’s construction manager, described the plans for
staging the construction. He said that they expected that only a very few
building elements would have to get into the property through 703. He explained
that they want to maintain day-to-day access through the private drive for most
equipment and materials so that 703 can be maintained as a livable residence.
Mrs. Bluhm expressed her desire to have construction traffic use the private
drive.
Mr. Schenk said that Mr. Bluhm has agreed to be financially responsible for any
damage to the private roadway. He indicated that the Bluhms see the private
roadway as the gateway to their home. They agree to video the road now so that
it is clear who is responsible for any damage and/or repairs. He added that
they hoped to work out an arrangement for future maintenance.
Mr. Reynolds commented that possible damage is not the issue under
consideration and acknowledged that he had no doubts that the Bluhms would
cover any damages.
Trustee Eilers asked how access would be accomplished if 703 was not in the
picture.
Attorney Janega mentioned that some lakefront owners have had materials for
work adjacent to the shoreline delivered by barge, for example.
Trustee Abell said that when he first saw the plan, it seemed reasonable, but
after reviewing the material and listening to the discussion, he could not
support it.
Trustee Greenough commented that this particular area is one of the most
congested sites in the Village. She said she could not imagine construction
traffic through here. She said that she believes that given the consolidated
lot, this is the appropriate time to make access conform so as to correct a
past planning mistake to address serious emergency access issues and better
serve the public good.
Trustee Ritchell agreed. He said that he sees this as an opportunity to fix an
undesirable situation by requiring construction access off of Sheridan Road
since that option will now be available.
Mr. Schenk suggested bifurcating the matter by requiring only certain equipment
to be moved from Sheridan Road.
Mr. True pointed out that access off of Sheridan Road will impact trees, since
the trees bracketing the existing driveway are only 9.5 ft. apart.
Trustee Tucker concurred with the other Trustees that, since there is already
access off of Sheridan Road, there is no reason not to use it. She said that
the entire consolidation is predicated upon the desire to build a much larger
new home than would otherwise be allowed, and pointed out that historically the
Council does not grant variations for new construction. Therefore, this is the
appropriate time for the Council to require that the property be brought up to
conforming standards.
Trustee Greenough asked whether the access off Sheridan could be moved to
another location on the property.
Mr. True responded that to do so would require a new curb cut, and that given
the present situation only one tree at the entrance would be impacted.
In response to a question from Mr. Williams, Mr. Stier said that he didn’t know
for certain whether any more than the one tree would have to be eliminated. He
said that if the house at 703 were removed, it might be possible to design a
meandering access road that would minimize impact on the trees. However, he
added that whether you use an access way once or one hundred times, the tree
roots will be disturbed and it is impossible to determine in advance the exact
extent of that impact.
Trustee Eilers said that he was uncomfortable allowing the Thornes to use the
private roadway for construction access and not allowing the Bluhms to do so,
but indicated that he would go along with the consensus of the Trustees.
President Duhl informed the applicants that they would be required to use
Sheridan Road for all construction access.
Mr. Schenk objected, saying that it was placing a hardship upon the applicant
and his family to have to live in a construction zone. He asked that the
Council consider a compromise that would allow construction access from both
entryways.
Manager Williams inquired about a possible access path along the northern edge
of the property.
President Duhl noted that it is the sense of the Council that all construction
traffic must enter and exit the construction area from Sheridan Road. He
pointed out that under the best of circumstances, someone is going to be
inconvenienced by the construction activity and that the brunt of that burden
should not fall on the neighbors. He said that, if the petitioners want to
return to the Council with a more specific plan, it would be considered.
Mr. D’Onofrio added that any such compromise plan would have to be very clear
so that staff could enforce it effectively.
Attorney Janega then addressed the issue of permanent access. She pointed first
to the fact that this is a subdivision as defined in the Village Code and that
the Subdivision Ordinance requires access to be off a public street or existing
private street. In this instance, although the private road provides access,
that access is an 8 ft. flagpole that doesn’t meet the zoning requirements and
doesn’t comply with the Subdivision Ordinance. She pointed out that the
petitioner does not have a recorded interest in any portion of Mr. Reynolds
property, so the connection to the private street is only 8 feet wide. She
added that the street is too narrow to permit unobstructed passage of emergency
equipment, and there is no turnaround where the street dead ends. She noted
that the petitioner has not applied for variances from either the Zoning
Ordinance or the Subdivision Ordinance with regard to their basic requirements.
Ms. Janega added that what appears to be happening is that the applicant is
trying to preserve the flag lot as primary access while retaining the benefit
of the subdivision and the additional square footage that it affords without
complying with all requirements of a subdivision. She noted that the Village’s
ordinances protect the rights of property owners to existing legal
nonconformities but that, when the slate is wiped clean by a subdivision, the
property is considered new and all existing ordinances apply. At that point,
the aim is to forget what went before and start with a clean slate.
Trustee Eilers pointed out that given that there is a serious life safety issue
since the private road does not meet the 12 ft. requirement established by the
Fire Chief, permanent access could not be allowed to continue off of the
private roadway.
Mr. Schenk made a distinction between the private road, which is 20 ft. wide,
and the petitioner’s access roadway, which they consider to be a driveway, at 8
ft. wide. He added that the applicant is trying to provide a park-like
environment that will be of benefit to all.
Attorney Janega pointed out that the Zoning Ordinance requires that the width
of the roadway where it meets the street has to be 20 ft.
President Duhl commented that there are many nonconforming lots in the Village,
but that it is the long-term goal of the Village to get rid of these. This lot
doesn’t conform, and there is a life safety issue to be considered. Therefore,
to maintain the status quo is contrary to the public interest.
Trustee Tucker asked Mr. Schenk if he could identify public benefit from
maintaining permanent access off of the private road when there is already a
curb cut on Sheridan Road, noting that she did not see any benefit to the
community from it. She added that overall, ordinances are enacted to help the
Village accomplish what it deems appropriate for the Village as a whole, not
for individual residents.
Mr. Schenk asked why this was not raised earlier when the Plan Commission
debated this matter.
Trustee Greenough responded that Mr. Behles had raised the matter at the Plan
Commission meeting. She added that the Plan Commission does not always make the
right decision and that ultimately it is the Council’s responsibility to
evaluate the Commission’s decisions.
Trustee Abell said that it is the Council that has the final say.
Trustee Tucker added that the Plan Commission is advisory in nature, and that
it is the Council’s responsibility to see that the requirements of the Village
Code are met.
In turning to the zoning variation request, Attorney Janega indicated that it
was primarily associated with construction access and that these issues have
already been discussed.
President Duhl asked whether there was a consensus among the Trustees to tie
the Certificate of Occupancy with the teardown of the existing house within a
specified time frame. There was.
Mr. Bluhm arrived at 10:40 and after a brief review of the preceding discussion
strongly objected to the Council’s decisions.
President Duhl noted the lateness of hour and the length of the preceding
discussions and suggested that further discussion be postponed to a later date.
He pointed out that process and procedural issues had not yet been addressed,
and Manager Williams pointed out that there are matters pertaining to the beach
front improvements that have not been clarified.
Trustee Abell moved to table further discussion to another evening. The motion
was seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion carried.
5)   Home Rule Power Point Presentation.
Due to the lateness of the hour, Mr. Williams did not make his presentation.
6)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
7)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:00 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Recording Secretary
September 21, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 5, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 21, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker and David
Webster. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public
Works Steve Saunders, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez and approximately 15 persons
in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   October 5, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members, except
Trustees Abell and Webster, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 12, 2004, Study Session. Trustees Abell, Eilers, Webster and
President Duhl indicated that they did not expect to attend and it was
established that there would not be a quorum on this date.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 7, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers reported that he had
requested some changes by phone and they had been incorporated into the minutes
before the Council. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the minutes as drafted.
Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-26-2004 –Disposal of Village Property at Auction -
Introduction. Director of Public Works, Steve Saunders asked the Council to
authorize the disposal of 13 vehicles and 6 pieces of equipment that are past
their useful life through a public auction conducted by the Northwest Municipal
Conference. He added that, for the first time, the Village will host the
auction at the Public Works facility.
President Duhl asked for comments from the audience and there being none,
Trustee Eilers moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-26-2004 authorizing the
disposal of Village Property at auction, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice
vote, the motion passed unanimously.
b)   Resolution No. R-30-2004 – Establishing Fire Chief’s Salary - Adoption.
Manager Williams asked the Council to approve this resolution establishing the
salary for Fire Chief Scott Smith, who was appointed on September 10, 2004.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the resolution
establishing the salary of the Fire Chief. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and
Ritchell, Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Bluhm Resubdivision: 701-703 Sheridan Road.
   i)   Resolution R-28-2004 – Plat of Consolidation: Bluhm Subdivision. -
Adoption.
   ii)   Ordinance No. M-25-2004 – Zoning Variations: Bluhm Subdivision –
Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments to the ordinance and resolution as a
result of the discussion during the September 14th study session. She said the
issues presented at the study session were construction access, permanent
access, looping of the water main, increased side yard setbacks, trees, and the
temporary use variation. She said she had spoken with the applicant’
Ms. Janega referred to the ordinance on pages 32 and 35 of the agenda
materials, where changes were incorporated to allow approval by the Director of
Public Works, rather than the Forester because the Forester reports to the
Public Works Director. She also referred to Section 3 of the ordinance where
she had added a provision for the tree removal plan to be subject to approval
of the Forester, without further review by the Forestry Commission, provided
that the owner supplied security. She said the materials she received this
afternoon from Mr. Bluhm’s attorney had changed this language, so she is not
sure that this is the final amended language.
Ms. Janega then referred to page 40, paragraph C, of the ordinance which sets
out the conditions or circumstances under which removal of the second house,
703 Sheridan, would be required. After talking with Mr. D’Onofrio, it was
determined that the house would be removed within 45 days of the provisions in
subparagraphs 1 through 5 and Ms. Janega will add a modification to allow for
weather conditions, as there are certain times of the year when a house can’t
be demolished.
In paragraph H on page 41 of the ordinance, which addresses split access during
the construction, Ms. Janega stated that the applicant’s attorney suggested
that the limit distinction be at 4 tons. She noted, however, that Community
Development staff recommends that there not be split access during construction
because it complicates enforcement.
Ms. Janega also clarified that in the recitals of the ordinance, the Community
Development staff did not intend for there to be any recommendation or
suggestion that Mr. Bluhm sell the 8-foot wide driveway strip and the Council
should disregard this statement. She said staff had simply meant to eliminate
further subdivision proceedings should Mr. Bluhm decide to sell that strip of
property.
Ms. Janega said that regarding the other permits, the Council has been given a
transcript of a voicemail message that was left for staff today, indicating
that the Army Corps permits have not been issued. As to access, she said that
because of questions about how much access is needed for Fire Department
vehicles, she had asked for those specifications, which show a wall-to-wall
turning radius of 38 feet, which is the radius of the smallest circle that the
fire truck can turn. She also said that Mr. Saunders took this data and worked
it out at the site, and the Fire Department took one of the engines and the
truck to the Public Works yards to measure the turning radius. She said those
numbers have been provided to the Council as well and stated that the key issue
in this case is access.
Ms. Janega reported that she believes the issue of the water main has been
resolved and that if the main is bored under the trees, a construction easement
for a boring pit is necessary. She said Mr. Bluhm’s attorney has proposed
language that changes what the staff recommended regarding the tree issues and
access from Sheridan Road, but some of the tree issues are now off the table.
She added that the temporary use is tied to the access issue is related to the
temporary use variation.
She concluded that, given the late hour that the comments from the applicant’s
attorney were received, the best course would be to figure out where we are and
then draft an ordinance and resolution for Council action. She added that the
documents before Council are clearly not finalized.
President Duhl said that he thought they could discuss the six issues but put
the question of access aside for the moment. He asked if he understood that
because of the nature of the water main, a construction easement would be
necessary.
Manager Williams said that staff had discussed putting the main 25 or 30 feet
from the south lot line, but applicant didn’t want to do this, so boring would
be necessary and would require a construction easement.
Mr. Bluhm said that he would work with the Village on this to resolve this
issue.
Mr. David Schenk, the applicant’s attorney, said that the language Ms. Janega
used for the total side yards requirements was ambiguous and asked for
clarification.
Ms. Janega said in response that the language eliminates the 43.07 feet of
total side yards in the future if this house is torn down and replaced. She
explained that the language was meant to keep at least the 43.07 feet of side
yards, even if there is an amendment to the Zoning Ordinance. She added that,
should there be additions to the house or accessory buildings, they would be
subject to the 43.07-foot total as well, unless the Zoning Ordinance is amended
or a variation is granted.
President Duhl commented that it is clear from the ordinance that the totals of
the side yards will be 43.07 feet.
Mr. Schenk agreed, but mentioned the 12-foot side yards.
Ms. Janega responded by saying that the issue is the total of the side yards.
She explained that the minimum requirements in the Zoning Ordinance are 12 feet
and it is not necessary to restate them.
President Duhl asked Ms. Janega to explain the issue about trees.
Ms. Janega replied that she understands that there are two issues. She said the
applicant is amenable to access off of Sheridan Road, but wants to protect the
trees, which create a choke point at the end of the lot. She said that for
purposes of permanent access, it is not necessary for the Council to make this
determination, because it is normally done by Public Works and the staff, and
requires an IDOT permit for the curb cut. She said that it appears that there
is plenty of room for access, but we don’t have a plan, so we don’t know what
is being proposed. She further said that protecting the trees is an issue that
is usually worked out by the staff and the concern about centering the house
and the problem that would cause to the trees was resolved at the Study
Session, where it was determined that the house doesn’t need to be centered.
She said that without a date on a final tree plan, it is hard to say if the
issues about trees have been resolved.
Mr. Norkus informed the Council that the Village Forester, Jim Stier, was
called after 5:00 p.m. today and told that a tree plan had been left for him to
review.
Manager Williams commented that he thinks that the primary issue with trees has
been resolved.
Ms. Janega said that the tree issue is no longer a part of the subdivision
approval; however, she did put a section into the resolution that says that
tree removal is okay per the plan, but we need a specific plan. She said that
if the date from the prior tree plan is stated in the resolution, subsequent
changes in trees proposed for removal would go through the Forestry Commission.
President Duhl then asked if there was an issue on the use variation.
Ms. Janega said that the issue on the use variation is related to the
conditions on pages 40 and 41 of the agenda materials, because granting the use
variation raises the issue of whether they can actually access the property
from Sheridan Road. She said there appears to be a couple of open issues, such
as the staff recommendation that removal of the house be within 45 days with
the Director of Community Development being authorized to extend the time if
necessary to accommodate weather conditions. She said that she had drafted this
ordinance assuming there would be split access for construction vehicles, and
added a requirement that access issues be cured within 2 days of getting a
notice. She said that she is not sure this issue is fully resolved given
Community Development’s concerns about enforcing split access. She added that
staff has to make sure that there is not a conflict with the access request.
President Duhl suggested that there be a clause in the ordinance that says that
access for other properties served by the private access road trumps
construction and the variation should be subject to those rights of access.
Manager Williams said that from a practical point of view, it should be
possible to resolve this issue.
Attorney Janega said she had revised the ordinance to allow for a letter of
credit instead of a cash deposit, but Mr. Schenk has said a performance bond
would be preferred. Ms. Janega explained that the Village no longer takes
performance bonds because it takes so long to enforce one. She said that a
letter of credit is written with conditions clearly set out so that it can be
drawn against easily.
President Duhl said to Mr. Schenk that the house would be removed within 45
days, there would be a cure period for access issues, and the Village would
require a letter of credit rather than a performance bond.
Mr. Schenk agreed to demolishing the house within 45 days, provided that there
is an exception for weather conditions, that the cure period is acceptable, and
that a letter of credit is acceptable as well, provided it can be cancelled
when the house is down.
Ms. Janega said that it would be when the site is restored.
Mr. Bluhm said that if he is required to take the house down, it is obvious
that he will have to restore the site.
Ms. Janega said that this was a good point and she would work it out with Mr.
D’Onofrio.
Mr. Schenk said that the tree plan is with Mr. Stier for review so they can
begin construction.
President Duhl asked if there were any other issues with permits.
Mr. Schenk asked if they could comment further on paragraph G of the ordinance
regarding the storage of materials. He said that, in the event the owner and
his family relocate to another residence, construction could be staged at 703
or both 701 and 703.
Ms. Janega explained that once this ordinance and subdivision are approved, the
subject property will be a single consolidated lot, so this is change is not
necessary.
Mr. Schenk said that there would be small vehicles on the split road.
President Duhl said that since this is an access issue, he will turn to that in
a minute.
Ms. Janega said that for the ordinance on the variation, there are no further
issues and that the resolution addresses access.
Mr. Bluhm said that he has the right to bring SUV’s and other small
construction vehicles on the property. He said the problem is on the private
road because of the neighbor’s use for their own vehicles and the Reynolds’
access. He said that vehicles will be coming off of Sheridan Road and he
believed that having two means of ingress and egress was a fair compromise.
Hope Reynolds, 695 Sheridan, said she is the adjoining owner and it is hard for
vehicles to get to her house. She asked how big a 2-ton vehicle is.
Mr. Saunders replied that it is a pick up truck like one of the small Public
Works trucks.
Mrs. Reynolds said her other issue is enforcement. She said that construction
people are not very gracious when you ask them to move. She wanted to make sure
that the large trucks come off of Sheridan Road. She has had problems before
and has even not had her mail delivered at times. She said that it is not the
homeowner’s fault, but the construction worker is the one who doesn’t care. She
spends a lot of time dealing with that already and does not want it to get
worse. She said that as long as cars and trucks know that they are to be on Mr.
Bluhm’s property and not the private road, she would appreciate it.
Manager Williams told Mrs. Reynolds that if she encounters a problem, she
should call Community Development, who will assess the situation and issue a
stop work order if necessary.
President Duhl said that at the last study session, the compromise suggestion
that was made was that staff would rather not deal with the enforcement aspect
of this, so it is for the Council to try to resolve it if possible.
Trustee Ritchell asked if we knew how many vehicles there would be?
Mr. Bluhm said that the bulk of the large trucks would be during excavation and
they will come off of Sheridan Road.
Trustee Ritchell said that, in his experience, it was not in the beginning that
traffic was bad, it was when the individual contractors came in with their own
vehicles that congestion occurs.
Mr. Bluhm said that there is no parking on the private road, and since they
will be coming in from Sheridan Road, this should not be a problem.
Trustee Ritchell still wanted to know about how many cars there would be.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that if there are 15 contractors, there would be 15
vehicles. He added that one of the staff’s concerns with respect to this
project is that there is another house being built two houses away whose
contractors Mr. Bluhm has allowed to park on his property. He stated that this
is when there will be a problem.
Manager Williams said to Trustee Ritchell that he looks at this property as if
there was an access off the alley and one off the street. He said that when
there is an alley, construction workers could access the site from the alley,
but the main vehicles will be coming off of Sheridan Road. It is not perfect in
this case, but it may be a reasonable compromise.
Trustee Abell said that the daily trades people may come in off the private
road, but big vehicles are coming off Sheridan Road and the owner will have
control and know when the big vehicle will be arriving. He said it is the daily
traffic off the private road that will be a problem.
Trustee Eilers said that the staff seems to be reconciled that this is the best
compromise and when the issue was discussed at the study session, the split
access was a good compromise. He said he is more concerned about the
interpretation of the size of a 2-ton truck, because some 1-ton trucks are very
large.
Chief DeLopez added that he would rather see it go by size and weight, not just
weight.
Trustee Tucker said that while the split access is a compromise, it will be
difficult to enforce. She thought that if large vehicles can use Sheridan, than
the smaller vehicles should use it as well and thinks the Council should
revisit this.
Trustee Webster agreed with Trustee Tucker’s comments and said he thought that
vehicles could come off Sheridan Road, but added that he could live with split
access.
Trustee Greenough said that she thought single access is appropriate off of
Sheridan Road. She didn’t think that the tiny lane is capable of supporting
this kind of congestion and is reluctant to put traffic on the small street.
President Duhl summarized the Trustees’ comments by saying that Trustee
Greenough and Trustee Tucker, as well as Trustee Ritchell are against the split
access. Trustee Eilers said split access is okay, if defined properly and
Trustee Webster would agree to split access. President Duhl said the he thought
they should approve split access, but told Mr. Schenk that he thought it should
go back to the staff for definition.
Mr. Schenk agreed to do so.
President Duhl asked Mr. Bluhm for his comments about permanent access.
Mr. Bluhm said that the turning radius for the fire truck is irrelevant
because, as Chief Smith had said, the trucks would be backed up out of the
property and that Chief Smith said he needed 12 feet for the trucks to pass.
Mr. Bluhm then addressed issues raised by Ms. Janega. He said he didn’t see
that private road access would be a hardship to owners of adjoining land, since
access has been there for 100 years. He added that the access is by virtue of a
recorded instrument and pointed out that the Plan Commission voted in favorite
of allowing access off the private road. He agreed that the access is a dead
end street, but stated that he has provided a y-shaped turn around on his
property, adding that the Fire Chief was okay with this. He said that the
driveway standards are for a maximum width of 16 feet but no minimum is stated,
so there must be proper life and safety width, which he wants as well. He said
the last issue is 12 feet for means of ingress and egress and said he does not
want permanent access off of Sheridan Road because it is his front lawn. He
feels that permanent access should be off the flag lot. He said there is an
issue of whether this would require a variance and he doesn’t know why this
wasn’t raised before. Mr. Bluhm believed he should be able to have primary
access off the flag lot, and if not, then primary off of Sheridan unless they
can get a total of 12 feet for access from the flag lot.
Manager Williams observed that it is not unusual for residents on Sheridan Road
to have a gate and for the Fire Department to have a knox box so they can
access the house.
Mr. Bluhm said they don’t ever intend to use that driveway and it will only be
the minimum width. He said that the private road has been used for large
deliveries for years. He said he understands the Village Code but does not
think this is a subdivision, because it’s a consolidation.
President Duhl asked Mrs. Reynolds to speak.
Mrs. Reynolds asked why split access was necessary when some vehicles would be
coming in from Sheridan Road. She asked why all vehicles could not use Sheridan
Road.
President Duhl replied that the split access was an attempt to address both
Mrs. Reynolds’ concerns and Mr. Bluhm’s request with respect to construction.
He assumed from Mr. Bluhm’s comments that he agrees that, if he does not get 12
feet, he will have to have permanent access from Sheridan Road.
Mr. Bluhm said that he thinks he does get the 12 feet by prescriptive easement,
but he doesn’t have it now.
President Duhl asked if there were two lanes of vehicular travel and Ms. Janega
replied that there were not.
Mr. Bluhm said that he has 20 feet of easements and that there is two lanes of
travel.
President Duhl asked about the Fire Department access.
Manager Williams said that right now a fire truck can get in there and added
that from a practical point of view, Chief Smith says that he needs 12 feet.
Mr. Williams said that there could possibly be a problem in the future if one
of the other houses on the private lane is taken down and reconfigured. He said
that if people exercised their full property rights, the Bluhm’s access lane
would be 8 feet wide.
Mr. Bluhm said the lane is 11 ½ feet from fence post to fence post. He said
that he doesn’t disagree with Mr. Williams, but he is asking the Council that
if he is able to get 12 feet, he would like permanent access off the flag lot.
President Duhl responded that the Zoning Ordinance would require a minimum of
20 feet, but that doesn’t exist. He said that since the Fire Department said
they needed 12 feet and if Mr. Bluhm can get 12 feet, could the Council agree
to allow access from the flag lot?
Ms. Janega said that it is not merely the 12 feet. She explained that when the
zoning analysis was done on this property, it was done by looking at the
reconfigured property, not by looking at what has existed for the last 100
years. She said the one consolidated lot met the 20 foot street frontage
minimum, so the access off the private road was not an issue. The Plan
Commission cannot grant or recommend a subdivision variance unless there is a
hearing and she does not know of any public notice to this effect. She said
that this came up because the Plan Commission said they could continue to have
access from the stub of the lot, but if that is allowed, that becomes the front
street line and under the Zoning Ordinance that requires a 20 foot minimum
frontage. She said an easement by prescription can only be accepted by the
Council if it’s been determined by a court, because the Council doesn’t have
the authority to make that determination. She added that, while this is not a
classic subdivision in that it doesn’t carve smaller lots from a large lot, it
is a subdivision according to the Code of the Village of Winnetka. She said the
Plan Commission did not follow the staff’s analysis and in her opinion, this
does not comply with the Subdivision nor the Zoning ordinance.
Mr. Bluhm said that what he would suggest is that he has the right to go back
to the Zoning Board and let them make the determination.
President Duhl pointed out that, even if Mr. Bluhm went back to the Zoning
Board, it would come to the Village Council for final decision.
Mr. Schenk wanted to clarify that we are talking about primary access from
Sheridan Road.
Mr. Abell said that staff recommended primary access from Sheridan and to close
off the alley access. He said that what has been discussed is that the
applicant would now have two points of access. He said it seems to him that the
requirements are clear in the Zoning Ordinance and the Village wants to
eliminate flag lots, so we should take advantage of opportunities as they come
up to continue in that vein. He stated the Council should require Mr. Bluhm to
comply with the ordinances as everyone else does.
Trustee Tucker said that the Plan Commission vote of 5 to 3 on access, was not
unanimous. She said further that the Village Council has the discretion to
allow or not allow the consolidation of this parcel for the owner to develop,
as long as public safety and welfare are protected. She said that some of the
conditions that have been discussed she can live with, such as allowing a
25,000 square foot home as long as it has appropriate access; allowing
placement 12 feet from the lot line; allowing 21 trees to be removed; allowing
the bluff area to be rebuilt although she still has questions on this; and
allowing temporary living in one house while the other is under construction,
as long as there is adequate access and the house is torn down in a timely
fashion. She said that the Council is allowing all these rights to this
homeowner, but what she doesn’t think can be allowed is permanent access off
the flag lot. She believes access should be from Sheridan Road since once the
lot is consolidated, there will be 96 feet of lot line along Sheridan Road. She
stated that, in her opinion, the Council should not allow permanent access off
flag lots and that the Council is within their authority to ask the homeowner
to give up his right to access off the private street and to require permanent
access off of Sheridan only.
Mr. Eilers said that we have a process in place, and this is to hold a public
hearing, appear before the Zoning Board, and have discussions, and it is not
appropriate to have this Board put in this position. However, for him, he would
deny permanent access at this point in time and will reserve judgment until the
findings are in from the other Boards.
Trustee Webster commented that he thinks permanent access should be from
Sheridan Road and he is not comfortable that the Council will have decided
something that would make it hard for fire equipment to get to the homes along
the private lane. He said that the Village Code says that there has to be 20
feet for access and so access should be off of Sheridan Road.
Trustee Greenough stated that the reason the 20-foot requirement was put into
place was that the flag lots should not have been allowed in the first place.
She said there are life safety issues and a number of other problems as well
with that and the 20-foot requirement was made to correct these types of
circumstances. She agreed with staff that permanent access should be off of
Sheridan Road.
Trustee Ritchell concurred with his fellow trustees in that permanent access
should be off of Sheridan Road.
Mr. Bluhm responded that everything they have done is based on the Code today
and the one issue that is up for discussion is access from the flag lot, which
is purely a fire, life and safety issue. He said he agrees basically with what
everyone has said, but when he gets the 12 feet he will have enough room from
that. He accepts this, but is not happy about it and doesn’t agree with it.
President Duhl invited Mr. Schenk to work with Ms. Janega to come up with a
revised ordinance for the next meeting.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1381 and 1382. Trustee Abell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1381 in the amount of $1,160,297.87 and Warrant List No. 1382 in the
amount of $655,296.42. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
Ritchell, Tucker, and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Public Hearing: Recycling. President Duhl opened the public hearing at 9:
00 p.m. to receive public comment on the use of wheeled carts for recycling.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the contract terms of the proposed recycling contract
with Onyx Waste Systems that include more recyclables, single-day pick up and
35 or 65 gallon wheeled and lidded carts for collection. He explained that all
recyclables go into the cart which is emptied by a mechanical arm on the
recycling truck. In discussing the proposed contract since the last Council
meeting, Mr. Saunders said that it would be possible to transfer ownership of
the carts from the contractor to the Village and the Village would then be
responsible for replacing the carts. He said that over the 5-year life of the
contract, the cost could be $20,000 per year if all carts needed replacing and
that the initial purchase price in bulk for the carts is about $35.00 a piece.
He said the advantage to ownership of the carts is that the Village could
remove the Onyx logo.
Residents who spoke regarding the proposed changes were Wayne Lockwood, 328
Sheridan Road, Marietta Marson, of Sunset Road, Roger Bishop, 1290 Scott, Bob
Hilarides, 1446 Edgewood, Chuck Dowding, 968 Elm, and George Noyes, 1555 Tower
Road. The comments centered on carts left at the curbs during the week, a loss
of flexibility in the containers used for recyclables, the ability to have more
than one cart, storage of the carts, assistance for infirm residents and that
garbage would be mixed in with the recyclables. Some of the speakers wanted to
be able to keep the blue bins that they are accustomed to and continue to use
paper bags for items that don’t fit into the bins. Mrs. Marson and Mr. Noyes
spoke in favor of the wheeled carts, saying that they would not take up more
floor space than the blue bins and are more convenient for older people.
The Trustees echoed some of these concerns and asked that staff speak with the
contractor about them.
Mr. Richard Kates, 1326 Tower Road, stated that he would not take a position on
the proposed change because of his position on the caucus. He pointed out that
changes for the proposed recycling contract had been published in the Winnetka
Report and the Winnetka Talk, but he would like to have seen this done before
the contract was negotiated. He suggested that the Village should compile a
list of people who are physically infirm and use the Public Works Department to
take the recycling out for them. He also suggested that the contract should
provide people a choice between the large and small carts and increase the
minimum amount of carts that may be used, instead of paying for extra carts as
people request them.
Patty Hilarides, 1446 Edgewood Lane, spoke in favor of conservation, stating
that this should help us to conserve, instead of creating waste.
President Duhl closed the public hearing at 9:32 p.m.
Trustee Greenough commented that this began not only because we have a contract
that is coming to an end, but because we have the highest recycling costs of
any nearby community since the bins are manual and men have to lift them into
the trucks. The reason many communities have switched to these carts is because
a mechanical arm from the truck can lift them and throw the recyclable
materials into the truck. She said the budget difference between the old
contract and the proposed one is a savings of $22,000, which will increase over
time because of automation and the nature of the industry.
Mr. Saunders said that recycling rates also have an effect on our prices and in
comparison to other communities, Winnetka’s rates are significantly higher. He
said that because Winnetka is the only area community that limits the number of
garbage cans, the Village has a high volume of material for recycling.
Manager Williams said that this is a much more efficient system for collection
and is where the industry is going. He asked Mr. Saunders to go over again how
Onyx will address those people with physical problems.
Mr. Saunders said that Onyx will work with the Village to collect recyclables
in particular circumstances and will be flexible as long as it is not just a
matter of convenience.
The Trustees asked Mr. Saunders about the locations of public drop areas, a
trial period, if residents’ needs for a small cart could be determined before
ordering and if the lids latch down on the carts.
Mr. Saunders replied that there are public drop areas at Village Hall, the
Public Safety building, the Public Works building, and in Hubbard Woods. He
said that the proposed contract does not contain a trial period, but that he
could speak to the contractor about this and about more closely determining the
number of small carts necessary. He said that the original estimate for the
number of small carts was based on their use in other communities. He also said
that the lids rely on their own weight to stay down, but that a bungee cord
could be used, if necessary.
President Duhl asked the Trustees what they thought about going forward with
this program.
All of the Trustees present indicated they were in favor of going ahead with
the program, as along as those people who are physically infirm are
accommodated.
President Duhl then asked the Council for their comments on Village ownership
of the carts.
Mr. Kates asked about the cost of Village ownership of the carts.
President Duhl said that this cost is simply an advertising cost and would only
be incurred if Winnetka took ownership. He said that Onyx will purchase and
replace the carts if their logo remains on them.
Mr. Saunders explained that if the Village had to replace every cart every
year, it would cost approximately $20,000, but that the carts should last for
this 5-year contract and one-year renewal period. He said that replacement
carts could be built into the next contract. In response to a question from Mr.
Williams, he said that over a ten-year period, the cost of replacement carts
could be $100,000, but thought it likely that this could be negotiated in the
next contract and that the Village would have more flexibility in contract
negotiations if we owned the carts.
After hearing the Trustees’ comments, President Duhl directed Mr. Saunders to
talk to Onyx about Village ownership of the carts and provisions for hardship.
He then asked what the plan was for starting the new collection.
Mr. Saunders said that there is a six-week time period for ordering and
delivering the carts. He said Public Works plans to order the estimated amount
and then find out the residents’ preference during this period.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Mr. Eilers reported that at the Chamber meeting on the October
14th, Manager Williams will make a presentation on home rule. He said the
holiday sale will be on December 5th. The Chamber also discussed a parking
suggestion made by the Police Dept to the Business Community Development
Commission and the Chamber, whose overwhelming opinions were that it would be a
problem. He said that no vote was taken at the Chamber level, so the BCDC did
not make a recommendation. He said that an inventory of the vacant spaces in
the business districts was being compiled by the Director of Community
Development and his staff and that the first meeting of the Planned Unit
Development group will be September 22nd.
Trustee Greenough reported that on Sept 17th, the Illinois Advisory Council met
to discuss the nominations to the National Register and voted to recommend the
log house for National Register status. She said it then goes on to Washington,
D.C. to be voted on.
b)   President. President Duhl reported that the League of Women Voters had a
forum on the Affordable Housing Act, which was attended by approximately 200
people and representatives of the Village of Wilmette and Glencoe. He said that
the forum was filmed and will be shown on the cable channel.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Tucker, Eilers
and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive
Session at 10:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:28 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell and Webster. Absent:
None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Tucker,
Greenough, Ritchell, Eilers and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:28 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
October 5, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 19, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 5, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees Tom
Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Trustees
David Abell and David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director
of Public Works Steven Saunders, and approximately three persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   October 19, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 21, 2004, Study Session. Village Manager Williams noted that it
was necessary to reschedule the October study session because of a lack of a
quorum. All of the Council members present indicated that they expected to
attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 14, 2004, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   September 21, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough noted a
typographical error on page 5 and asked that it be corrected. Trustee Eilers
moved to adopt the minutes, as corrected. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-26-2004 –Disposal of Village Property at Auction -
Adoption. Director of Public Works Steve Saunders explained that the Village
wishes to dispose of 19 items that are longer usable by the Village. The
surplus vehicles and equipment listed in the ordinance will be sold at an
auction sponsored by the Northwest Municipal Conference on October 16. Mr.
Saunders noted that, for the first time, the Village of Winnetka would be the
host community for the auction. He also reported that the Village of Elk Grove
Village is interested in purchasing the surplus leaf catcher that is on the
list of items for auction. He said that, in the interest of intergovernmental
cooperation, the leaf catcher would be sold directly to Elk Grove Village
instead of being put up for auction, if a purchase price can be agreed to by
October 15, 2004, .
Attorney Janega explained the proposed amendments to the draft ordinance, which
would permit the negotiation and sale of the leaf catcher to Elk Grove Village.
Trustee Eilers moved to amend Ordinance No. M-26-2004 authorizing the disposal
of Village Property as proposed by Attorney Janega. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance,
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Webster.
b)   Bluhm Resubdivision: 701-703 Sheridan Road.
   i)   Resolution R-28-2004 – Preliminary Plat of Consolidation: Bluhm’s
Resubdivision – Adoption.
   ii)   Ordinance No. M-25-2004 – Zoning Variations: Bluhm’s Resubdivision –
Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the current status of the application and distributed
the latest revised versions of the proposed resolution and ordinance, saying
that the whole issue has been in a state of flux.
Ms. Janega went over the new draft resolution, which included changes discussed
with applicant’s attorney, David Schenk that afternoon. She indicated that a
correct legal description had been received, and that, in fact, the property to
be consolidated consists of four underlying parcels of land. She added that the
legal description of the Bluhm’s Resubdivision has also been corrected in the
revised resolution. Ms. Janega noted the revised resolution includes a
Declaration of Restrictive Covenants, which establishes the increased side yard
setback requirement, requires permanent access directly off the Sheridan Road
street frontage, prohibits the expansion of the private road access, and
clearly states that the restrictive covenants will run with the land and can’t
be released or modified without the Village’s written consent. She explained
that the revised resolution provides that the permanent access from Sheridan
Road will be located subject to the approval of the Village Forester, under the
supervision of the Director of Public Works. She added that the restrictive
covenant requires the sum of the side yard setbacks to be based on tan average
lot width of 143.57 ft. and an absolute lot width of 153.09 ft., which are the
dimensions of the wider of the two lots to be consolidated, currently known as
701 Sheridan.
Ms. Janega also explained that the recitals have also been expanded to provide
the factual basis for the restrictive covenants and the other conditions that
need to be met to finalize the subdivision. Finally, she noted that, because
there are a number of outstanding issues, staff recommends preliminary, rather
than final, approval of the resubdivision at this stage. The open items include
specifics as to the location of the construction access, the location of the
water main easements, the location of the permanent driveway, and the final
tree removal and tree protection plans. She also pointed out that Village staff
had contacted the Army Corps of Engineers and learned that the Army Corps had
denied a permit request for shoreline and bluff protection work in January, and
that another application had been filed in August. She said the Army Corps had
not completed its preliminary review and has told Village staff that it had
recently been asked not to proceed further because changes would be
forthcoming.
Ms. Janega mentioned that the applicant’s attorney said the applicant has some
concerns about the language of the restrictive covenant and would like to add a
reference to 12-ft. side yards and to eliminate the language that prohibits the
enlargement of the flagpole access. Ms. Janega advised against inserting
reference to the 12-foot side yards, because that is the current zoning
requirement, and that staff is no longer recommending that the house be
centered, which would have increased the size of the minimum side yard.
Regarding the flagpole access, Ms. Janega reported that staff would be
comfortable with removing the prohibition against enlarging the flagpole
access, so long as the restrictive covenant stated that the flagpole may be
maintained solely as a secondary access to the property.
Finally, Attorney Janega noted that the Village and the applicant have agreed
to work cooperatively to locate the easement for the water main. She then
restated staff’s recommendation that, given the fluidity of the situation, the
Council should only grant preliminary approval of the plat of subdivision,
which would give everyone the opportunity for final paperwork to be completed
and reviewed.
Mr. Norkus spoke briefly about the side yard requirements in the Declaration of
Restrictive Covenants, and recommended that only the average lot width
requirement be stated. Ms. Janega agreed that, given Mr. Norkus’
recommendation, the reference to absolute lot width should be deleted from the
restrictive covenants.
The applicant, Andy Bluhm, then explained his objections to the omission of
language specifying the 12-foot side yard, saying that he would like to have it
there in case of some casualty or condemnation to the proposed home. Otherwise,
he stated his agreement to the terms outlined by Ms. Janega.
Ms. Janega responded that, no matter how staff looks at the sum of the side
yards, a 12-foot minimum side yard is currently required by the Zoning
Ordinance. However, if the 12-foot side yard statement is included in the
covenants, should the Zoning Ordinance’s requirement be increased in the
future, the owner would not be required to abide by the new rules. Similarly,
if restrictions were relaxed, the owner would be locked into the standard
stated in the restrictive covenants. She said that staff believes that it is
inappropriate and misleading to this owner and any future owners to put a
specific reference to a 12-foot side yard in the restrictive covenants.
Trustee Greenough inquired about the property’s east lot line. Attorney Janega
replied that with the corrected legal descriptions, the property is now bounded
by the water’s edge on the east, just as all other similar properties are.
Trustee Ritchell asked whether once consolidated, this property could be
resubdivided. Attorney Janega indicated that it was doubtful that such would
occur.
Manager Williams acknowledged Mr. Bluhm’s cooperation in relocating the water
line.
Trustee Tucker expressed continued concern about allowing access from the
private drive. She said she doesn’t see any benefit to the public, the
neighbors, or the village, and said she feared this would set a precedent for
the future.
Manager Williams responded that, from a practical point of view, this is likely
to happen very, very rarely, as the trend is to subdivide, not to consolidate.
Therefore, this is unlikely to establish a dangerous precedent. From staff’s
point of view, the main concern has been emergency vehicle access, and that
issue has now been resolved.
Attorney Janega explained that the way to establish a policy for the future is
to consider amending the Zoning Ordinance to prohibit development of flag lots.
However, thus far, there has not been a consensus to do so. Mr. Williams added
that there is a 100-year history in this Village of allowing flag lots.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R- 28-
2004, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Tucker, Ritchell, and Greenough.
Attorney Janega turned to the revised draft of the amended ordinance M-26-2004.
She indicated that, pursuant to discussions at the previous Council meeting,
the amended ordinance provides for split construction access, with most
construction access being made directly from Sheridan Road and with use of the
private lane being limited to smaller vehicles. Attorney Janega explained that
the remaining amendments to the ordinance clarify the circumstances under which
the use variation will expire and modify the site restoration requirements. She
noted that the applicant has objected to site restoration being required before
release of the letter of credit. She pointed out that the ordinance can be
adopted, because the variation rights are clearly conditioned on the final
approval and recording of the subdivision plat.
Ms. Janega stated that Mr. Bluhm would like to release the letter of credit as
soon as the house at 703 Sheridan is demolished. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that
staff’s concern has to do with the installation of the driveway and that it was
necessary to keep financial security in place. Ms. Janega agreed, explaining
that, if the letter of credit is released, the Village would lose its only
remaining leverage to assure completion of the driveway because the Village has
to allow the new house to be occupied before the house at 703 can be vacated
and demolished.
Mr. Bluhm asked if it would satisfy staff if he agrees not to get rid of the
construction road until permanent access is established off of Sheridan Road.
President Duhl asked Mr. Bluhm if he could agree to a reduction in the amount
of the letter of credit after demolition. Mr. Bluhm indicated that he could.
Trustee Tucker reiterated her objection to having any access off of the private
road.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to amend the ordinance in
accordance with the discussion. By voice vote, the motion passed, with Trustees
Eilers Greenough and President Duhl voting in favor, and Trustees Tucker and
Ritchell voting against.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, then moved to adopt the
ordinance as amended. President Duhl polled the Council and determined that
there were not enough votes among the five present members to adopt the
ordinance, whereupon Trustees Eilers and Greenough agreed to table the matter
until the next meeting, when all of the Trustees are expected to be present,
and the other members of the Council agreed.
President Duhl thanked the applicant for his patience.
Mr. Bluhm thanked the Council for their efforts.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1384 and 1384. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1383 in the amount of $578,759.44 and Warrant List No. 1384 in the
amount of $323,078.94. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Greenough, Eilers,
and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Webster.
b)   Report on Zoning Board Variations. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a status report
on the impact of changes to the Zoning Ordinance adopted by the Council on
April 6, 2004, comparing the numbers of variation requests before and after the
amendments. He explained that one of the major amendments was to grant the
Zoning Board of Appeals greater authority over approval of various zoning
requests, in the hope of reducing the number of routine variation cases that
had to be considered by the Village Council. He concluded that it appears that
the amendments have had the desired result.
Manager Williams asked Mr. D’Onofrio if he has seen any unintended negative
consequences. Mr. D’Onofrio replied that he has not.
c)   Recycling Contract. Mr. Saunders reviewed the status of this matter and
said that the agreement with Onyx has been modified to reflect the Council’s
direction following the public hearing at the September 21st Council meeting.
The agreement has been modified to specify the 35 and 65-gallon containers and
to reflect that the Village will own the containers, thus allowing the Village
to determine their appearance. As a result, there will be no logo on the
containers. In addition, staff has made arrangements with Onyx to develop a
contractual procedure by which individuals with certain physical hardships
relating to health or property conditions may request and appropriate alternate
means of recycling collection. He recommended awarding the contract for
curbside recycling services to Onyx Waste services Midwest, Inc, as outlined in
their proposal of May 7, 2004, with the modifications reflected in the revised
agreement in the agenda materials.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Webster.
d)   Bid 04-017: Water Plan Concrete Restoration Phase II. Mr. Williams
reviewed the bids and the bid process for concrete repairs at the Filter Plant.
He distributed an addendum to the Agenda Report showing the bids and the letter
of recommendation from the Village’s consultant. He reported staff’s
recommendation that the bid be awarded to Structural Preservation Systems, in
an amount not to exceed $75,000, the amount contained in the budget for this
work.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Webster.
e)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Manager Williams referred the
Trustees to the departments’ capital improvements reports included in the
Agenda packet. He reported that the Village is moving forward with design of
the storm water pump station on West Tower Road, has finished replacing the
splices on the out of 4 of the major circuits, completed the installation of
the roof over the settling basin, and almost completed the street
reconstruction and rehabilitation project for this year.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the appointment of Mark Crane as Chair of the Zoning
Board of Appeals, effective November 1, 2004. Trustee Greenough, seconded by
Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
President Duhl then called for a motion to appoint Susie Shabica to serve a
second term as a member of the Forestry Commission. Trustee Ritchell, seconded
by Trustee Tucker, so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
11. Reports.
a)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that during the month of August
there had been approximately 63,600 hits on the Village’s web site.
Mr. Williams then made an informational PowerPoint presentation explaining Home
Rule. He said that this is the presentation that will be taken to the various
Village boards and committees as well as to local civic and not-for-profit
organizations.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Greenough, Tucker, and
Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Webster. The Council convened
into Executive Session at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:14 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Absent: Trustees Abell
and Webster. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker,
Greenough, Ritchell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Webster. The meeting adjourned at 10:15 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
October 19, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 2, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 5, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker and David
Webster. Absent: None . Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Finance Director Ed McKee, and approximately 17 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   October 21, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 2, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Webster, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 5, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-25-2004 –Temporary Use Variation: Bluhm Resubdivision:
701-703 Sheridan Road - Adoption. President Duhl stated that this ordinance
would permit the house currently at 703 Sheridan to remain on the consolidated
lot during the construction of a new single-family home at the east end of the
property and had been discussed at the September 7th, 14th, 21st and October
5th meetings. He reported that at the October 5th meeting, a motion to adopt
the ordinance was made and seconded, but there were not enough votes to carry
the motion and the matter was tabled. He called for a motion to remove the
matter from the table.
Trustee Greenough moved to remove the Ordinance from the table, seconded by
Trustee Eilers. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Village Attorney Janega reviewed the staff’s amendments to the ordinance since
the last meeting and reported that Mr. Schenk, Mr. Bluhm’s attorney, had
requested further changes. She explained that the effect of Mr. Schenk’s
request, which pertained to the reduction and cancellation of the letter of
credit, access, and the temporary and final certificates of occupancy was to
expand, rather than clarify, the owner’s rights under the variation and the
Village Code. She recommended that the Council proceed with the ordinance as
drafted, without further changes.
President Duhl then asked Trustee Eilers and Trustee Greenough if they wished
to withdraw their October 5th motion and second to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, whereupon the two Trustees withdrew the motion and second.
Mr. Schenk responded to Ms. Janega’s comments and summarized where their
opinions differ.
Ms. Janega explained the conditions of the ordinance and clarified the points
for reducing the letter of credit, temporary occupancy and final site
restoration.
After further discussion, Mr. Bluhm indicated that the conditions of the
Ordinance as drafted by Ms. Janega were acceptable to him.
Trustees Abell and Tucker voiced their concerns about dual access to the
property, the weight of construction vehicles on the private road, and granting
the variation before the subdivision plat is finalized.
President Duhl replied that the discussions on this ordinance are not unusual
and the essence of the ordinance is to permit the owner to live in the house on
the lot while the new house is being built. He further stated that primary and
secondary access is for the Village staff to work out and added that, while he
understood Trustee Abell’s and Trustee Tucker’s positions, he did not think it
necessary to revisit the access issue.
Manager Williams commented that, if you consider the project as a whole, the
access issue has been addressed by the staff and the Council as best as it is
possible.
Trustee Webster asked Mr. Reynolds if he was satisfied with the outcome of the
access issue and the traffic that may be using the private access road.
Mr. Reynolds, 695 Sheridan Road, replied that that the use of the private road
by a B class of vehicle gives him some concern because it can be up to 8,000
lbs. He said a loaded pick-up truck should be okay, and if the vehicle is
larger, then the ordinance’s obstruction of roads provisions would deal with
it. He indicated that he is comfortable with the resolution of the issue and
thanked the Council and Mr. Bluhm.
Trustee Eilers moved to amend Ordinance No. M-25-2004 as presented by the
Village Attorney, seconded by Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended, granting a temporary use variation. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker
and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-27-2004 – Landmark Designation: 1479 Tower Road –
Introduction. Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio presented the
request of the owners at 1479 Tower Road to designate the residence as a
landmark. The home is one of 5 houses in the immediate area that are often
referred to as “the storybook homes.” He reported that the Landmark
Preservation Commission had rated the home as unique with respect to rarity of
construction and architectural type and style, and had recommended that the
Council designate the house a Winnetka landmark.
Julie Windsor, the home’s owner, said this is the second storybook house she
has asked to be designated, the first being the coach house at the rear of the
property. She described some of the unique elements of the home and commented
that it would be wonderful for Winnetka to have it as a landmark.
Trustee Greenough said that the grouping of the 5 houses is a treasure and the
homes are astonishing for their workmanship and creative detail. She said she
is grateful that Winnetka has someone such as Ms. Windsor who recognizes this,
and thanked her for her efforts.
Trustee Greenough moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-27-2004, seconded by
Trustee Abell. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
c)   Resolution No. R-31-2004 – Liquor License Application: J.P. McCarthy’s –
Adoption. President Duhl stated that this resolution would re-authorize a Class
A-1 Liquor License with a television rider to B & G Cuisine, LLC, for the
operation of J.P. McCarthy's Restaurant upon the completion of the background
check and the transfer of assets.
Attorney Janega explained that B & G Cuisine has a contract to purchase J.P.
McCarthy’s and the new owners have applied for a liquor license to be in effect
once the business assets are transferred. She said the Council creates the
license and, as the local liquor advisory commission, authorizes the Village
President, as Liquor Control Commissioner, to issue the liquor license if he
determines that all qualifications are met and the fingerprint response is
received.
Manager Williams added that the service of liquor will continue to be
incidental to the primary service of food and there are no changes in the
proposed menu.
In response to a question from Trustee Abell, Ms. Janega stated that it is not
necessary for the applicant to have a packaged meal rider because the
restaurant’s carry out sales are for food only.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the resolution, re-
authorizing the establishment of a Class A-1 Liquor License with television
Rider for J.P. McCarthy’s Restaurant . By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell and Tucker Nays:
None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1385 and 1386. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1385 in the amount of $619,707.46 and Warrant List No. 1386 in the
amount of $432,913.53. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough,
Ritchell, Tucker and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Chamber of Commerce Holiday Banner Request. Community Development Director
Mike D’Onofrio reviewed the request of the Chamber of Commerce to put banners
on the Village’s utility poles in the business districts from November through
April.
Cicely Michalak, Executive Director of the Chamber, said in response to
questions from the Trustees about the design, length and placement of the
banners, that the banners mainly coincide with holiday lighting and would be
placed in similar locations as those used for the sidewalk sale. She added that
she would check on the length of the banners to make sure they fit on the
existing frames. She said the Chamber would like to have the banners up as long
as the holiday lights are on each year, with the Chamber paying for
installation and removal of the banners. She asked whether the Council had any
preference among the banner designs presented, whether the choice of the
banners should be left to the Chamber.
The Council agreed that the choice of banner should be left to the Chamber.
President Duhl asked if the audience had any questions. There were none.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the Chamber of
Commerce’s request to provide and hang holiday banners of their choice from
among the designs presented. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
c)   Establish Amount for Tax Levy. Finance Director Ed McKee said that, based
on the 2005 budget approved in March, the staff recommends that amount of
revenues to be raised by the tax levy be set at $10,542,699, net of abatements.
He described the various components of the proposed tax levy and the impact it
will have on a homeowner’s tax bill. Mr. McKee also reported that the increased
pension benefits for firefighters passed by the legislature must be funded
locally and recommended extending the property taxes for those benefit
enhancements.
Manager Williams further explained that the legislature recently removed the
tax levy for the increased firefighters pension benefits from the tax cap
legislation, so the increases in fire pension benefits are a new cost to the
Fire Pension Fund. He added that the Village is 80% funded.
Trustee Ritchell commented on the increase in pension benefits as did President
Duhl, who added that funding is a problem for all municipalities, as the money
in the pension fund primarily comes from investment returns and contributions.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to set the 2004 property
tax levy amount, net of abatements, at $10,542,699. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell,
Tucker and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid Water Plant Painting. Mr. McKee explained that this bid award is for
sandblasting and painting the water plant. He reported that, although this was
an open bid, the Village had only one responsive bidder; Alpha Paintworks, for
a bid amount of $41,750.00 which is $1,750 over the budgeted amount. He said
that the staff believes this is a reasonable cost and recommends that the
vendor be awarded the job. Mr. McKee also said that, if the Council has
concerns, the Village could re-open the bid.
After a short discussion, Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved
to award the bid to Alpha Paintworks, for $41,750.00 as recommended. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Salt Purchase. Mr. McKee explained that the Village participates in the
purchase of rock salt through the State of Illinois, which has awarded the bid
to the lowest bidder, North American Salt, for $30.64 per ton. He said that the
staff recommends that the Village accept this bid.
In response to a question from Trustee Webster, Manager Williams said that
other substances are used on certain streets, but rock salt is used on the core
streets and is the least expensive. He added that years ago, there was a caucus
question about reducing the salt on secondary streets and the community was
supportive as long as intersections were salted.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bid to North
American Salt for $30.64 per ton. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and
Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
a)   Trustee Tucker welcomed the boys scouts in the audience whose troop leader
told the Council that the scouts are attending the Council meeting to work
towards a badge.
Attorney Janega added that the scouts are working on a merit badge in
citizenship in the community and explained some of the requirements for earning
the badge.
b)   Mr. Frank Neaylon, 888 Mt. Pleasant, Mr. John Nichols, 900 Mt. Pleasant,
and Mr. John Croghan, 909 Mt. Pleasant, complained about the property at 300
Birch Street, which they said has been under construction for 5 years, is
unsightly and an eyesore. He said that the Village Code does not appear to have
any provisions to address a construction project that has no end in sight and
asked that the Council study the situation.
After hearing comments from the neighbors in attendance and from staff,
President Duhl directed Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio to
prepare a chronology of the construction events at the property and to
determine whether there is something the Council can do to remedy the problems.
He asked the neighbors to come to the next meeting, when the Council will
discuss Mr. D’Onofrio’s findings.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Abell said he had received a letter from Mr. George
Noyes about political signs. Manager Williams stated that, with the Council’s
approval, he would like to turn the matter over to the Sign Board.
b)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village auction at the Public
Works building had brought in about $47,000.00.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl appointed Tom Hermanson to the Zoning Board of Appeals. Trustee
Eilers moved to approve the appointment, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice
vote, the motion passed unanimously.
President Duhl then appointed Mary Hill to a second term on the Forestry
Commission. Trustee Ritchell moved to approve the appointment, seconded by
Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell,
Tucker and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:03 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Webster. Absent:
None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees. The meeting adjourned at 10:03 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
October 21, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: November 16, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday,
October 21, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, David Webster, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public
Works Steven Saunders and approximately 16 persons in the audience.
2)   Elm Street Train Station Renovation & Rehabilitation.
President Duhl asked Ed Woodbury, former Trustee and Council Representative to
the project, to review its status. He noted that this is an ideal opportunity
to enhance and beautify this important entryway to the Village, as well as
bring it up-to-date and make it more functional.
Mr. Norkus reviewed the proposed modifications to the existing station. These
include replacing the existing elevators with heated pedestrian ramps; re-
landscaping the embankments on both sides of the tracks; reconstructing and
expanding the platforms; and reconstructing the pedestrian bridge and stairs.
The project will also include replacement of windows and doors, as well as
remodeling of the station’s interior public areas. He noted that the plans also
call for a small plaza area that will also have a sheltered, screened space for
the newspaper boxes, the appearance of which has been the concern of several
individuals and groups. He added that much of the renovation of the station
building itself has already been completed.
Mr. Norkus reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals, the Plan Commission, and
the Design Review Board had all considered the Special Use Permit application
and come back with favorable recommendations. The Zoning Board had, however,
expressed some concern regarding the elimination of the elevators, and the Plan
Commission had voiced some concern that the replacement landscaping, as
depicted on the then available plan, might not be adequate or appropriate. Mr.
Norkus added that the Design Review Board had issued extensive comments
regarding the improvements, most pertaining to options and upgrades which Metra
has agreed in concept to construct, but which, because of their cost, would
require a contribution by the Village to cover the increased cost. These items
include site fencing, platform lighting, and shelter roof material. In
addition, the DRB commented on the visual impact of clearing the existing
vegetation from the slopes. Mr. Norkus then highlighted some of the key points
in the Boards’ discussions.
President Duhl asked Mr. Norkus to address the issue of the ramps versus the
elevators in terms of cost and reliability. Mr. Norkus deferred to Josel
Gonzalez, the representative from Metra, to answer that question.
Mr. Gonzalez reported that Metra has a strong preference against elevators,
citing the maintenance and vandalism problems historically experienced where
there are elevators. He added that the ramps are more expensive for Metra to
install. He, however, noted that Metra would be willing to install either
option during the renovation, noting that the existing elevators would have to
be completely replaced and new vestibules constructed.
Ted Ried, an audience member, asked whether the electrical elevators function
better than the hydraulic ones.
Mr. Gonzales replied that both are prone to problems, particularly over the
long term, pointing out that there is no way to completely insulate them from
the elements, i.e., weather and salt. He said that the ramps are maintenance-
free and that the only instances in which Metra now installs elevators is when
there is no option to construct a ramp.
In response to a question from Manager Williams, Mr. Gonzales confirmed that
Metra would assume responsibility for the maintenance of the ramps, but should
the Village choose the elevator option, the Village would have to assume all
maintenance responsibility.
Trustee Ritchell asked about skateboarders. Mr. Gonzales responded that, while
there is some usage, this is not a very big problem in other facilities, as the
ramps are not steep enough to be very attractive.
Mr. Gonzales proceeded to review the proposed landscaping plans, fencing, and
lighting. He indicated that irrigation had been discussed, but said that the
cost for installing and maintaining such a system would fall to the Village. He
estimated that the cost would be in the neighborhood of $40,000 for an
irrigation system.
With regard to the fencing, Mr. Gonzales noted that the Village standard
defined by the Design Guidelines was significantly more expensive than Metra’s
standard. The same is true for the Village’s decorative lighting fixtures. He
indicated that if the Village wishes to upgrade from what Metra uses as
standard, the Village would be responsible for the costs involved but pointed
out that knock-off lighting fixtures can be obtained. He reviewed the Village’s
options and estimated that it would cost the Village upwards of $200,000 to
install its standard lighting fixture.
Addressing the light fixtures, Trustee Greenough pointed out that the Village
expended a great deal of time and effort in establishing its Design Guidelines
by comparing contrasting styles and appearance. She pointed out that many times
knock-offs are not necessarily equal to the original.
President Duhl asked if the Design Review Board could see a sample of the
available options for the light fixtures.
Manager Williams asked if Metra has a specific lighting standard. Mr. Gonzales
replied that it must be two foot-candles at the platform edge.
Mr. Woodbury noted that, while the Design Review Board had considered the
aesthetics, it had not been asked to evaluate their worth relative to their
actual cost to the Village. He suggested that the issue be returned to the DRB
for reconsideration, with some general direction from the Council.
President Duhl commented that it would be helpful to hear from the Design
Review Board about whether the Council ought to even consider an expenditure of
more than $200,000 on lights.
Manager Williams noted that the Village has set aside $175,000 for the station
renovations and pointed out that it is not anticipated that the project will
commence until 2005 at the earliest.
President Duhl turned the discussion to the question of ramps vs. elevators. He
said that the Council needs to consider the pro’s and con’s of each option,
including costs. If the costs are identical, then it becomes a question of
which is more desirable and beneficial. However, if one is more expensive than
the other, that has to be taken into consideration.
Mr. Gonzales stated unequivocally that Metra would install either option, but
not both. In response to a question from Manager Williams, Mr. Gonzales said
that if the Village chooses the elevator option, the Village would have to
assume all associated costs on a going-forward basis. On the other hand, Metra
would assume maintenance responsibility for ramps.
Chris O’Donnell, an audience member, voiced his opinion that the elevators were
never a good idea and that ramps are appropriate.
Tom Hermes, also an audience member, asked whether someone in a wheelchair
might loose control on the ramps.
Mr. Gonzales replied that the ramps are ADA compliant. He added that the ramps
proposed for Winnetka, in fact, have a more gradual slope than what’s required
and less than the slope of ramps installed in other stations. He noted that
less than 1% of Metra’s riders qualify under the ADA.
Mr. Saunders added that these ramps would be even less steep than the ramp used
at Village Hall for ADA access.
Ted Ried pointed out that Winnetka’s track is lowered, whereas Evanston and
other North Shore stations’ platforms are at grade level. He expressed concern
that skateboarders, runners, and individuals seeking bicycle access to the
Green Bay Trail would heavily use the ramps. He added that the existing
elevators don’t work well because they are exposed to the elements.
Manager Williams pointed out that because all of the other North Shore
communities’ stations are at grade level, disabled commuters could easily go to
Wilmette, Glencoe, or Glenview to access Metra trains.
Another audience member, Lucas Mockaitis, spoke against the ramps on behalf of
his mother, who suffers from severe arthritis and believes that she would not
be able to navigate them.
Mr. Hermes pointed out that these ramps would be much longer and, therefore,
perhaps less easily used.
Trustee Tucker indicated that she was not convinced that the maintenance costs
and hassles of having the elevators made them the best choice.
Trustee Abell pointed out that there are other problems associated with the
elevators, i.e., safety, vandalism, illegal activities, cleanliness, etc. He
said that it is impossible for the Village to police them.
Brian Norkus remarked that the Design Review Board looked at the ramps as more
attractive than the existing elevator towers as a means of accessing the
platforms.
President Duhl said that the Village needs to continue to move forward in the
process so that plans can be completed, but as it does so, it needs to have
some additional information about costs and ridership.
Manager Williams said that Village staff would conduct a survey of elevator
usage.
Mr. Gonzales made it clear that if the Village chooses to proceed with
elevators, with the lower construction cost, it will not get the advantage of
any extra monies to use for its discretionary use for landscaping, fencing, or
lighting.
Mr. Woodbury concluded that once the Council decides between the elevator and
the ramp options, Metra could proceed with the general design and develop more
exact cost estimates for the possible fixture upgrades for presentation to the
Design Review Board.
3)   Private Road Construction Issues.
Mr. D’Onofrio briefly reviewed this matter, which was last discussed at the
September 14th Study Session, when most of the discussion had to do with the
role of the Village with respect to damage to private streets from new
construction. He noted that neighborhood/road associations, not the Village,
privately fund maintenance on these few private streets. At that meeting, a
resident requested that the Village reconsider its policy of limiting its
involvement with respect to damage and that the Village either raise the
demolition fees or dedicate some of those fees to the affected private road
associations. The Council requested that Staff explore what more the Village
might do to assist owners on private streets, as well as ways in which private
street owners could finance improvements in order to bring their streets up to
Village standards, so that, in turn, they could be dedicated as public streets
and turned over to the Village.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that, of the 114 demolition permits since January 2003,
only six have been for new homes on private streets. He added that, of those,
Village staff was asked on two or three occasions to mediate disputes between
developers and the private road associations with respect to street damage.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that under current procedures, the Community
Development Department does a number of things to address the issue of damage
on private streets. First, staff videotapes the condition of the street prior
to any work being done. Second, as work proceeds, if there is damage, it is
inspected and the developer is notified and held responsible for the repairs.
Finally, when the request for a Certificate of occupancy is made, staff
inspects the street to determine if there is any visible damage and requires it
to be repaired before issuance of the occupancy certificate.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that he had surveyed other municipalities to determine
what they do with respect to this issue and found that Winnetka currently has
more involvement with private streets than do others. He recommended that the
Village continue its existing procedures and consider requiring the developer
to post a street opening fee and bond. He added, however, that these steps do
not deal with the issue of “wear and tear” of a street over time.
As discussed at the September 14th meeting, the Village charges a $10,100
demolition fee, $1,750 of which covers the cost of wear and tear to the public
streets. He said that whether this should be turned over to the private road
associations is a policy decision for the Council to decide, but Staff has some
concerns, because some of these associations are formal; others are not. He
said there is really no standard vehicle for the Village to turn over monies to
any such organization. He suggested that one way to address the wear and tear
issue might be to assist the private street associations to become formal
entities so that they might be better able to exert more control over
developers on their streets.
As to the other issue raised by homeowners at the Study Session, how to help
finance improvements to allow private streets to be brought up to Village
standards, Mr. D’Onofrio said that the biggest impediment, regardless of the
financing method chosen, is the willingness of the owners to participate in
such an endeavor. He noted that there is a wide-variety of private road
organizations, some formal and organized, others small and haphazard..
Trustee Abell reiterated Mr. D’Onofrio’s comment that the major problem is with
the homeowners’ willingness to make any option work. He added that the Village
cannot spend public funds for private benefit.
Mr. D’Onofrio added that the same problem exists with regard to bringing a
street up to Village standards.
Attorney Janega described what occurred on Boal Parkway when that street was
turned over to the Village.
Trustee Tucker said that it would be difficult to change the processes when
there is a precedent.
Mr. Hermes reiterated his request for some portion of the money received by the
Village as a demolition fee. He expressed his belief that it is unfair for the
Village to charge its substantial fee and leave the affected homeowners, who do
not receive any Village services, i.e., snow removal, responsible for the costs
of repairs.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. Hermes that the homeowners do, in fact, own
the property to the middle of the road.
Trustee Abell pointed out that the Village’s fee was designed to recover its
costs associated with demolition and construction. The $1,750 from the
demolition fee identified as the portion assigned for wear and tear on Village
streets affects all of the streets in the Village, not just the street upon
which construction occurs. The remainder of the costs are largely
administrative and were designed to recover the costs of the Community
Development Department and make it self-funded.
Trustee Greenough remarked that the Village used to run at a loss and that all
taxpayers were subsidizing development. The fee now reimburses the Village for
its costs.
President Duhl suggested that the homeowners on Private Road form an
association that does not permit development unless the developer pays the
association for damages to the street.
Trustee Tucker remarked that she thought this was a good idea because, with a
formal organization, there can be rules to ensure accountability. She asked
whether the Village could tack on an extra fee for work on a private road.
Attorney Janega indicated that to do so would be very tricky, but added that
the Village could require a fee and bond for the street opening as suggested by
Mr. D’Onofrio.
With regard to bringing a private road up to Village standards and then
dedicating it to the Village, Ms. Janega again referred to Boal Parkway. In
response to a question from Trustee Webster, Attorney Janega said that everyone
who has an interest in a street has to agree to dedicate it to the Village and
that the Village has never exercised its condemnation powers when the adjoining
owners can’t reach a 100% consensus because, in effect, the nonconsenting owner
would receive a payment from the Village while the owners who agreed to
participate would be paying for their portion of the street upgrade. However,
there are other mechanisms to try to come up with the necessary funding to
upgrade a private road. She said that the Village’s standards for its streets
are those that permit it to use MFT (Motor Fuel Tax) funds rather than general
property taxes to maintain that street.
Manager Williams suggested that perhaps some kind of sharing policy might be
arranged.
President Duhl added that Private Road could be a test case if they come to the
Village unanimously, saying that they would like to enter into an agreement to
bring the road up to Village standards so that it could be dedicated to the
Village.
4)   Flood Plain Regulations Review.
Mr. Saunders explained that, by definition, the flood plain is made up of the
flood way and the flood fringe, and noted that the flood fringe portions of the
floodplain affect some of the Village’s residential neighborhoods and Park
District building areas. He added that the Federal Emergency Management Agency
(FEMA) has mapped areas within the Village that are located in both the 500-
year and 100-year flood plain and explained what those terms mean. Mr. Saunders
noted that the Village’s Building Code and Zoning Ordinance, as well as the
Special Flood Hazard Protection Ordinance, control development within the 100-
year flood plain.
Mr. Saunders reported that, of the approximately 3,900 single-family homes in
the Village, approximately 550 are located within the 100-year flood plain, and
that there is a significant reduction in property value for those properties
because they are subject to certain development constraints, such as the
prohibition against basements.
Mr. Saunders explained that, however, that in 2001, the Village amended its
Flood Hazard Protection Regulations to make it possible under certain
conditions to issue permits for a home with a basement in an area located in
the 100-year flood plain. Since that time, the Village has issued seven
building permits for new homes with basements in the Village’s flood plain. He
added that, of the two completed projects, neighborhood concerns about drainage
appear to have been alleviated by the provision requiring compensatory storage,
the storm water detention, and the general required grading of the site.
He explained that there are two distinct permitting phases that can run in
tandem, the demolition permit and the building permit, and reviewed what each
entails. Mr. Saunders went on to enumerate some of the problems that have
resulted from this permitting process and some of the complaints commonly heard
from both the property owner and the neighbors.
In closing, Mr. Saunders said that, although the permitting process for the
construction of new homes with basements within the flood plain is most often
met with complaints, concerns, and a great deal of effort on the part of the
owner and the Village, the process appears to be manageable. He introduced
Susan Chen, Assistant Village Engineer, who is a certified flood plain manager
and is responsible for managing permit issues for the flood plain. He also
distributed maps showing the flood plain and a drawing showing fill/
compensatory storage.
Ms. Chen explained the process whereby an existing home may be demolished, with
the building site being excavated and filled with clay to raise the ground
above the base flood elevation. The clay fill is then excavated for the
foundation and basement construction. She noted that, whether someone builds a
new home with a basement in this manner, or simply builds a clay building pad
and builds the new home on a slab with no basement, the Village Code requires
the first floor to be at least one foot above the Base Flood Elevation. She
also noted that construction in the floodplain will require compensatory
storage, because it’s the clay fill that is displacing the water, not the
basement. Mr. Saunders added that the only way around this provision is to have
a crawl space. He said that there is a discrepancy in property values based
upon having a basement.
Attorney Janega explained why the Village adopted its present policy, relating
issues that FEMA had raised when variances from the floodplain regulations were
granted so basements could be constructed.
Trustee Tucker asked whether other communities do the same thing. Ms. Chen
replied that Winnetka is one of the few that permits a basement option.
Former Trustee Woodbury remarked that the basement option is a good one for
houses that have outlived their useful life, but that the permitting process is
exhausting.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to address some of the zoning issues
involved, which he did, noting that the developer gets no height or FAR
advantage from building on a raised pad, as height is measured from the pre-
existing grade. He added, however, that there might be some sites where a
basement is not feasible, particularly small, narrow lots. He suggested that
the Village might want to consider some type of setback for swales and perhaps
not permit retaining walls in side yards at the property lines.
Mr. D’Onofrio pointed out that there is a drastic difference between what is
seen during construction and what one sees upon completion.
Mr. Woodbury commented that the Village’s current procedures encourage
retaining walls and swales along property lines and said that, in his opinion,
these are not attractive to the neighbors. Mr. Woodbury asked that the Council
and staff consider the basement issue because, if the result of permitting
basements is having neighborhoods full of retaining walls, the Village doesn’t
want that.
President Duhl pointed out that to deny someone the right to construct a home
with a basement goes to a property value issue.
Mr. Williams indicated that staff would look at retaining walls, building pads,
and compensatory storage, and then will develop some suggestions for Council’s
consideration sometime in April.
5)   GIS Demonstration.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village is a member of the GIS Consortium, a
public entity consisting of local government organizations that work
collectively to achieve the full benefits of Geographic Information Systems
technology. Get economies of scale for a number from a number of aspects. GIS
is a tool for managing and working with information in a way that marries
visual, map-based information to a database. It is a powerful tool that allows
for graphical presentation and analysis of data.
Mr. Saunders and representatives from Municipal GIS Partners reviewed the
information currently available and what has yet to be added.
6)   Executive Session.
There was no Executive Session.
7)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:15 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Recording Secretary
November 2, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 16, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 2, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Trustees Herb
Ritchell and David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Bryan
McInturff, Pubic Works Director Steven Saunders, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and
approximately two persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   November 9, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 16, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Eilers, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 19, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Village Auditor’s Report.
Mr. McKee introduced the Village’s auditor, Irwin Lyons, a partner with Miller
Cooper. Mr. Lyons reviewed the Comprehensive Annual Financial Report and
management letter. He noted that the Village had again received the Certificate
of Achievement for Excellence in Financial Reporting from the Government
Finance Officers Association, the highest form of recognition in the area of
governmental accounting and financial reporting, and said that the Village has
no critical items requiring attention.
Manager Williams highlighted some of points in the report, summarized the
status of the four utility funds, and reviewed the pension funds. He concluded
that the Village has had a pretty good year.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-27-2004 – Landmark Designation: 1479 Tower Road –
Adoption. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the
ordinance designating the subject property as a local landmark. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers,
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Ritchell and Webster.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1387 and 1388. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1387 in the amount of $1,670,500.41 and Warrant List No. 1388 in the
amount of $425,271.29. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough,
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Ritchell and Webster.
b)   Annual Report on Liquor Ordinance Changes. Chief DeLopez reported that,
since 2002, when the Council approved the service of alcoholic beverages in
certain establishments without requiring the purchase of a full meal, there
have been no incidents attributable to such service. Additionally, since 2003,
when the Council authorized the sale of alcoholic beverages at the Winnetka
Golf Course, there have been no incidents related to that service. He,
therefore, recommended that the ordinances permitting these two activities
remain in effect.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to leave the two ordinances in effect.
c)   Water Line Change Order. Mr. McInturff explained the need for the change
order, saying that additional asphalt quantities were required that were
incorrectly specified in the original bid documents.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker moved to authorize Change Order #1
for the 8” Gage Street and Tower Court water main in the amount of $33,760.00.
Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Ritchell and Webster.
d)   Village Yards Project Authorization: Floors & Doors. Mr. Saunders reported
that Village Yards project is now complete. Based upon the final billing, the
total cost for the project is about $165,000 below the original approved
project budget of $8,630,472. He noted that, because the first estimates for
the project were considerably over budget, value engineering was used to reduce
the total costs. During this process, several items were identified and removed
from the project, including the repair and resurfacing of the concrete floors
in the portions of the building that would be retained, and replacement of
existing overhead doors. He said that while these two specific reductions were
made, it was recognized that, because of their badly deteriorated condition,
staff would work to manage the construction budget with an eye toward possibly
re-instating the two items laster in the project.
Mr. Saunders outlined the specifics of each repair and roughly estimated the
cost of repairing the floors and replacing the doors at $150,000 to $190,000.
He recommended that that bids be solicited to replace the doors and that the
Council authorize the expenditure of any remaining funds in the original
approvedhe project budget to patch and resurface the most deteriorated sections
of the floors, as contemplated in the original project.
Manager Williams distributed a summary showing how the floors and doors impact
the Facilities Fund. He noted that the Fund can absorb the cost and will have
no effect on the General Fund.
In response to a question from Trustee Greenough, Mr. Saunders noted that
floors are structurally sound and that the repairs to the floors are relatively
superficial in nature. The proposed repair work will make them safer from
further damage.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve staff’s
recommendation. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays. None. Absent: Trustees
Ritchell and Webster.
10. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
11. Reports.
a)   Trustees.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Business Community Development Commission had
met and is completing the language for the Building Owners’ Survey that will
complement the Commission’s other surveys. He said that after the final results
of citizen input are tabulated, the Commission will be coming to the Council
with recommendations. He added that four property owners had attended the
meeting and said that the Commission encourages their continued participation.
Mr. Eilers also mentioned that Attorney Janega had recently met with PUD
committee members, and that her input had been very helpful in focusing
discussions.
12. Appointments.
President Duhl suggested the reappointment of John McLinden to Design Review
Board. Trustee Greenough moved to approve the appointment, seconded by Trustee
Abell. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
President Duhl then suggested the reappointment of Gary Frank to the Design
Review Board. Trustee Eilers moved to approve the appointment, seconded by
Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Finally, President Duhl suggested the appointment of Mark Kurensky to the Plan
Commission to replace Joan Fragen whose term is expiring. Trustee Abell moved
to approve the appointment, seconded by Trustee Greenough. By voice vote, the
motion passed unanimously.
13. Executive Session.
There was no executive session.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays. None. Absent: Trustees Ritchell and
Webster. The meeting adjourned at 8:16 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
November 9, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: November 16, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday,
November 9, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Trustee
David Webster participated via telephone. Absent: None. Also present: Village
Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, and approximately 15 persons in the audience.
2)   Elm Street Train Station Discussion: Ramps v. Elevators.
Manager Williams gave a brief overview of the issue. He noted that the station
renovation project began in 2001 when the Village approached Metra about the
deteriorated condition of Winnetka’s stations. At that time, Metra agreed to
explore funding for a complete renovation if the Village was willing to lease
the stations and assume maintenance responsibilities therefor. He added that
some temporary improvements have been made and that the final roofing and
masonry work has been completed. Manager Williams made it clear that the
remainder of the station rehabilitation project is only in the early design
phase and that changes can be accommodated. He pointed out, however, that
Metra’s representatives have made it clear that, should the Village decide to
pursue the elevator option, the Village would have to assume financial
responsibility for their maintenance.
Mr. Williams explained that, as a result of the discussion at the October 21st
meeting, staff conducted an elevator usage survey. He reported that during a
20-hour period, from 5:30 a.m. to 1:15 a.m. on Wednesday, October 27th, the
elevators were used 49 times. He also reported on a staff survey of surrounding
train stations to determine how disabled individuals access their platforms and
where handicapped parking is located, noting that Wilmette and Glencoe have far
superior accessibility than Winnetka could ever have due to their design.
Trustee Abell asked whether there were pedestrian grade crossings between the
tracks at the other stations. Mr. Williams replied that there were at Wilmette
and Glencoe, but not at Kenilworth, where there is a sidewalk adjacent to the
station.
Trustee Tucker inquired whether the elevators appeared to be functional
throughout the survey period. Mr. Williams said that they had been.
Trustee Ritchell asked whether Metra could provide any statistics on
handicapped ridership.
Saying that the Village did not have that information, Mr. Williams introduced
Clayton Weaver, Director of Metra’s Technical Division.
Mr. Weaver provided Mr. Ritchell with figures indicating that the lifts on
Metra’s handicapped cars are deployed for use by less than one percent of their
ridership.
Mr. Weaver acknowledged that the question of ramps vs. elevators is a difficult
one, because while the ramps may be long for some users, the elevators are
unreliable. He went on to say that Metra’s Accessibility Committee, which is
composed of individuals who are disabled and which advises and evaluates
Metra’s accessibility to disabled riders, has stated that reliability is the
number one issue. The issue is to try to balance convenience of an elevator
with the reliability of something that will be in service all times. He said
that Metra knows that the ramps will be in service at all times and under all
conditions, while the same cannot be said for the elevators. He added that a
certain level of mobility is required to use the system. Metra has very few
stations equipped with elevators and almost all are located where access is
somewhat controlled, so as to deter misuse and vandalism. Metra has learned
that the elevators cannot be exposed to the elements and must be enclosed in
heated vestibules, and that the mechanicals must be heated to ensure optimal
performance.
Mr. Weaver also noted that he had examined the elevator repair records for the
Winnetka elevators and determined that in 2003, one or the other elevator had
been out of service at least once a month, due to either vandalism or
mechanical failures. Some of these problems could be addressed by better
design, but some will not go away, regardless of design. Mr. Weaver indicated
that the costs of maintaining and repairing the existing elevators over the
last 3 years was approximately $40,000; which covered repairs and upgrading as
well as routine maintenance. He said that with thorough rehabilitation the
elevators could probably be in good shape for a few years, but noted that the
rule of thumb is that elevators are replaced in kind with maintenance expenses
about every 7-8 years.
Mr. Weaver also pointed out that, the for a handicapped rider arriving at
Ogilvie Transportation Center, the distance from the train’s cab car, which
contains the lifts, to the terminal’s interior is approximately 350 ft., which
is shorter than the proposed ramps. The same is true during rush hour, where
the length of the proposed inbound ramp is less than the distance from the
elevator at Elm Street to the cab car.
In reply to a question from President Duhl, Mr. Weaver stated that the funding
to complete this project is not now available and probably will not be
available until 2006 at the earliest, so there is plenty of time to resolve the
issue in the best manner possible.
Trustee Webster asked whether an individual needed a motorized wheelchair in
order to use the ramps. Mr. Weaver replied that would depend upon the
individual’s general state of health and upper body strength.
Trustee Tucker inquired about the difference in cost to Metra between
installing ramps and elevators. Mr. Weaver responded that, overall, the
difference would be approximately $500,000.
Trustee Webster asked about the effectiveness of security devices on elevators.
Mr. Weaver replied that there are just a very few elevators, most of which are
on the IC line, where turnstiles and electronic ticket-vending machines control
access. In some cases, there is surveillance because of cash handling
operations.
Trustee Abell pointed out that for someone on crutches an elevator is
preferable to a ramp, but added that there are neighboring stations where easy
handicapped access is readily available. Mr. Weaver agreed.
Trustee Ritchell asked how many times the elevators were down and how long they
take to repair. Mr. Weaver responded that, on average, service was called for
the elevators about once a month. He indicated that their contract with the
repair service requires a two-hour response time, but that frequently the parts
are not on hand and have to be ordered, thereby extending the length of time
the elevator is out of service.
In reply to a question from Trustee Tucker, Mr. Weaver acknowledged that in
some places skateboarders and in-line skaters pose a problem where there are
ramps. He said that they try to put curves into the design to discourage this
kind of use.
Philip Graber and his son, David; residents who are members of the New Trier
Committee on the Disabled, urged the Council to get more input from the
disabled community before making a decision.
Dick Kates also supported the idea of seeking additional input.
Ted Ried, a resident, raised several questions about the elevators and
expressed his strong support for retaining them, as did Mollybelle Berish.
Martha Mockaitis also expressed strong support for the elevators, explaining
that not all disabilities require use of a wheel chair and that she would not
be able to use the station because she is unable to use the ramp.
Trustee Abell said that, at this time, he favors elevators and inquired about
the possibility of repairing and converting the freight elevator inside the
station so it could be used by commuters.
Trustee Ritchell concurred. He suggested that the Village and Metra explore new
technology to determine whether the reliability of the elevators could be
significantly improved with better elevators and better design.
Trustee Tucker said that, if the goal is to provide access for the truly
handicapped, then, in her opinion, the Village should have the most reliable
means, which are the ramps. She pointed out that the ramps also alleviate
security, misuse, and vandalism problems.
Trustee Greenough said that, given that Metra has made clear that elevator
maintenance would become the Village’s responsibility if it chooses that
option, she is concerned about adding that expense to the Village’s budget on a
going-forward basis, especially when the ramps are cost-free to the Village.
Trustee Eilers indicated that he was leaning toward the ramps because of their
reliability, but suggested that, since there is no rush, the Council should get
additional input from the New Trier Committee on the Disabled.
Trustee Webster expressed an inclination at this time for retaining the
elevators. He said that he would like to have additional information on state-
of-the-art elevators and design options.
Hearing no consensus from the Trustees, President Duhl asked Mr. Weaver if it
would be possible for the Council to return to the issue at a later time. Mr.
Weaver indicated that there is plenty of time to do this right, and added that
the goal is to provide the best, most reliable access possible.
President Duhl asked if Metra’s Accessibility Committee and the New Trier
Township Committee on the Disabled could meet to share perspectives and then
get back to the Council. Mr. Weaver volunteered to facilitate such a
discussion. President Duhl also asked Mr. Weaver to return to the Council with
more information on state-of-the art elevators and preliminary designs that
might enable the elevators to better withstand their exposed location, as well
as an assessment of whether the elevator inside the station itself could be
incorporated and used.
Manager Williams indicated that the matter would be tentatively slated for the
February Study Session.
3)   300 Birch Street Chronology, Options, and Comparison.
President Duhl stated that this matter was on the agenda to provide additional
information requested by the Council in response to complaints presented by
neighbors of the property at the October 19th Council meeting.
Mr. D’Onofrio began by reviewing the chronology of the construction activity at
the subject property, from the issuance of the building permit application on
July 30, 1999 until the complaint against the property owner was dismissed on
October 19, 2004.
He then reviewed a staff survey on what time limits neighboring communities
impose on building permits. He said that, in Winnetka, a building permit has a
time limit of 15 months after which an extension of up to 9 months can be
granted. The extension requires payment of a fee equal to 20% of the total
original permit fees. He added that, if work has not begun within 3 months of
issuance of a building permit, or if substantial progress is not made within 6
months, or if work is stopped or abandoned for a three-month period, then the
permit is considered to have lapsed.
Finally, Mr. D’Onofrio addressed a third issue raised by the Village Council
pertaining to construction driveways, explaining that one of the neighbors’
complaints about the subject project has to do with the construction driveway
located on the south side of the property along Mt. Pleasant. He said that, in
reviewing the Village Code, there does not appear to be any language regulating
such temporary driveways. However, if access to a construction site requires
driving over a parkway rather than an existing driveway, it is recommended
that, the owner install a temporary driveway made of gravel, to minimize damage
to curbs and reduce mud deposits on the street.
President Duhl commented that the goal of the discussion is to determine what
the Village can do now, given that there is no way to undo the past. He said he
recognized that the current situation is unfortunate and said that four years
is along time for the neighbors to have had to put up with the subject
construction work but, unfortunately, there are currently no provisions in the
Village Code that allow the Village to take any action that it has not already
taken. He asked the Village Attorney to review some suggestions as to what the
Council can do within its authority to alleviate this kind of problem for the
future.
Attorney Janega commented on a letter from the attorney for one of the
complaining neighbors. She said that the Village’s main concern has to be that
the project is completed, and that issuing a permit is the necessary first step
in getting that process going. She acknowledged that there is a clear gap in
the Building Code, because, under the present Code, Village staff has no
authority to reduce the 15-month permit period. She then reviewed suggested
amendments to the Building Code that would help to prevent a recurrence of this
situation in the future by providing the Village with more leverage to see that
a construction project is completed in a timely fashion and strengthening the
authority of the Community Development Director.
Mr. and Mrs. Nichols expressed their frustration and disputed staff’s recitals
of the facts on performance and existing conditions.
President Duhl explained that the Council plans to take action as soon as it
can to correct the deficiencies in the Village Code and then will have the
authority to help with the current situation.
Ms. Janega explained that, as the Code now stands, the Village cannot go back
and change the terms of the subject building permit after it has been issued.
However, if the proposed Building Code amendments are passed, they will provide
a legal foundation for the Village to revoke the permit and/or require
conditions for the completion of the work if there is no progress under the
current permit. She proceeded to review the suggested Building Code amendments.
In response to repeated objections from Mr. and Mrs. Nichols, President Duhl
explained that the Village wants to amend the Code so that it can do something
in the future. He pointed out that the Village has gone to Court to ask for
assistance, but that the Court refused to impose deadlines. He reiterated his
comments about the project having gone on too long and said that all agree the
Village ought to be able to do something, but it must have the authority behind
it to do so.
Frank Neaylon, also a neighbor, inquired about adding provisions requiring
notification of the neighbors should there be a request to extend a building
permit. President Duhl indicated that the Village Attorney would incorporate
appropriate language into the amendments to be sure that neighbors are
notified.
Manager Williams suggested that some form of neighborhood mitigation should
also be built in.
After Ms. Janega completed her review of the draft amendments, there was a
consensus among the Trustees to put the amending ordinance on the agenda for
introduction at next Tuesday’s Council meeting.
4)   Draft Post Office Committee Charge.
President Duhl introduced former Trustee Ed Woodbury, who, because of his
experience and expertise, was asked to prepare a discussion outline for the
development of the Post Office site, which is owned by the Village, and to
advise the Council on the formation of a committee and on the development of
this property after the Post Office’s lease expires.
Mr. Woodbury reviewed the draft outline that was included in the agenda
materials, emphasizing that the Post Office site offers the Village a unique
opportunity to have a major, positive impact on the central business district,
the social fabric of the community, and the Village’s tax base. He pointed out
that the Village’s Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, identified the
development of this property as one of the most important projects to be
undertaken by the Village. Mr. Woodbury added that, because of the magnitude
and importance of the project, President Duhl had decided that now is the
correct time to form a committee to study the appropriate range of issues that
will lead to the ultimate development of the property.
Mr. Woodbury noted that the Village’s lease with the Post Office expires in
2009. He said that a letter has already been sent to the Postmaster, notifying
it that the Village does not intend to renew the lease but would be interested
in maintaining a retail Post Office facility within the new development.
John Bodenshok, the Winnetka Postmaster, provided some background into the
current lease situation. He indicated that a request for a new carrier annex
had been submitted to and approved by the Great Lakes District Office and has
been put on the Post Office’s Critical List for 2005-2007. The problem is that
no suitable location has yet been identified, given that they need two to three
acres in the Winnetka-Northfield area. He expressed his expectation, however,
that it was unlikely that the facility will be moved much before the lease
expires, due to the very favorable rent of $.60 per square foot.
Mr. Woodbury went on to say that a wide variety of uses for the site have been
proposed, ranging from retail uses, to senior housing, to a civic performing
arts building. He said the full range of uses merits evaluation by the Post
Office Committee, as do the design and financial implications of such uses.
He added that there are myriad issues to be considered and defined by the
Committee when exploring the potential development of the property, and
suggested that the Committee’s goal should be to define the issues, propose
direction, provide for thorough community input, and recommend the Council
action needed to ready the property for development.
Mr. Woodbury identified some of the issues as being:
   ·   What are the precedents?
   ·   What should the Village’s role in the future land ownership be?
   ·   What are the appropriate uses that should be considered (or not
considered)?
   ·   What is the preferred distribution of uses on the property?
   ·   What are the trends in urban design within suburban communities?
   ·   What types of financing structures are available?
   ·   What should a developer selection process include?
He went on to explain how the issues are intertwined and how each affects the
other.
Mr. Woodbury suggested that the proposed Committee be made up of qualified,
interested citizens that have a degree of familiarity with the Village’s
commercial districts, development processes, public dialog, mixed-use real
estate, public-private finance, and land use trends. He added that he believed
that the Committee should have members from the Village Council, the Plan
Commission, and the Business Community Development Commission, as well as
members at-large from the community, totaling no more than 9 individuals. He
noted that the Committee’s task should be to thoroughly vet all aspects of the
project and to be sure that all stakeholders’ interests are represented and
discussed.
Finally Mr. Woodbury reviewed a proposed schedule outlining the timeframe for
developing the property, including the sequence for the Committee’s work.
Trustee Tucker thanked Mr. Woodbury for the thoughtful outline.
Trustee Webster asked whether a development like this always involves hiring a
professional developer, or whether the Village might do it on its own. Mr.
Woodbury replied that a developer is necessary because these are the
professionals who deal with the issues involved on a daily basis. The Village
does not have the staff or expertise to handle the creative, leasing, and sales
aspects, not to mention the day-to-day oversight. He did say that some sort of
public-private partnership might be considered.
Trustee Webster suggested that the committee include a resident of the
immediate neighborhood.
Trustee Tucker inquired about the appropriateness of a Planned Unit Development
(PUD ) for this site. Mr. Woodbury responded that the process for public input
would be the same, but that a PUD probably provides a more natural and cohesive
process.
Trustee Eilers remarked that this project is going to require careful
stewardship and guidance. Mr. Woodbury concurred.
5)   Insurance Report.
Mr. McKee reviewed staff’s investigation into buying commercial insurance for
the general liability and auto liability exposures rather than the Village
self-insuring up to $2,000,000, where the High Excess Liability Pool coverage
takes over. He reported that quotes for policies obtained by the Village’s
broker had deductibles ranging from $250,000 to $1,000,000 and premiums ranging
from $80,000 to $137,000, respectively. He also noted that the carrier offering
these quotes is rated A- (A minus) by the insurance rating agency and expressed
concern that a carrier so rated might not have the necessary financial
strength.
Mr. McKee said that, under these circumstances, he and the Village’s insurance
consultant agree that it doesn’t make sense for the Village to buy the
insurance because it is too expensive relative to the risk being transferred.
He suggested that the one alternative would be to budget and transfer
additional dollars to the Liability Fund to cover losses between $1,000,000 and
$2,000,000, although such contributions would have a negative impact on the
major operating funds.
There was a consensus among the Trustees that, when preparing the 2005-2006
Budget for consideration, Mr. McKee should funnel an additional $54,000 into
the Liability Fund.
6)   Executive Session 
Due to the lateness of the hour, there was no Executive Session.
7)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:45 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Recording Secretary
November 16, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 7, 2004)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 16, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker and David Webster. Absent:
Trustee Tom Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, and
approximately 13 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   December 7, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except for Trustee Webster who was uncertain, indicated that they expected to
attend.
b)   December 14, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council members present,
except for Trustee Webster who was uncertain, indicated that they expected to
attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 21, 2004, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell requested that the
minutes be corrected to indicate that he was present. Trustee Greenough moved
to adopt the minutes as amended. Trustee Ritchell, seconded the motion. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   November 2, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   November 9, 2004, Study Session. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-28-2004 – Tax Levy - Introduction.
President Duhl announced that Ordinance M-28-2004 would levy the property taxes
for 2004 at $10,542,699.00 and that Ordinance M-29-2004 would abate the
property taxes levied for the Series 2003 Bonds for the 2004 tax levy year. He
asked Mr. McKee to address the Council.
Mr. McKee said the proposed increase in the property tax levy is 2.5%, which
means a $38 increase for a homeowner who pays $10,000 a year in taxes. He
explained that these funds will help pay for a 31% increase in the cost of
police and fire pension benefits which are set by the State legislature, and
added that 56¢ of every new tax dollar will go to funding those benefits.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-28-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-29-2004 – 2003 Bond Abatement – Introduction.
Mr. McKee explained that the bonds are funded through sales tax revenues but
that the bond payments are backed by the Village's taxes. He said that this was
done to obtain the lowest interest rate at the time the bonds were issued and
that the Village makes a determination each year that there is enough money to
pay for the bonds out of sales tax revenues. He said that, because the sales
tax revenues are sufficient, this year's abatement in property taxes related to
the 2003 bonds will be $343,612.50.
Trustee Webster, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-29-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-13-2004 – Building Permit Renewal Amendments –
Introduction.
President Duhl said that this ordinance amends the building code as it pertains
to building permit renewals which was discussed at the November 9th study
session.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the preliminary draft of the ordinance
incorporates changes to clarify the Village Code and grant new authority to the
Director of Community Development regarding renewals of building permits. He
said that the new ordinance will also permit conditions to be placed on
renewals pertaining to site management to mitigate the impact of the
construction on neighbors. He explained that a violation of the chapter will
constitute a public nuisance, a tool that will simplify enforcement, and allow
the permit to be revoked or a stop work order issued. He further explained that
the time frame for the building permit would begin when it is approved, instead
of when the permit is picked up.
In response to questions from Trustee Greenough and Trustee Ritchell, Mr.
D’Onofrio stated that requiring the contractor to provide a bond to fund
restoration is the quickest way to obtain compliance, and that he believes that
the new ordinance grants the director enough discretionary judgment to require
fences, grading or landscaping to deal with unsightly construction areas.
The Trustees briefly discussed the distance from the property for notification
of neighbors and agreed that the distance should be consistent with the
requirements of the zoning ordinance or 250 feet. They also agreed that it
should be explicit in the ordinance that if an owner applies for a new permit
after a former one has expired, that it would be considered a revival and
treated accordingly.
Trustee Abell commented on the obligation of the owner to restore the site to
good condition upon expiration of a permit which is not revived, referring to
construction that has stopped but construction fences remain. He said that he
is not sure that a construction fence would be considered a nuisance and
suggested that the owner should be required to restore the property as closely
as possible to how it was before the construction or within the keeping of the
neighborhood.
President Duhl asked Mr. Neaylon for his comments.
Mr. Neaylon said that this has been an unusual circumstance and would like to
see that homes under construction are progressing. He said that what the
neighbors are trying to accomplish is mutual respect and peace in the
neighborhood.
Mr. David Fischer, attorney for the Hofelds, 300 Birch, said that he is
concerned about the effect of the adoption of the ordinance upon his clients’
current building permit and asked if there would be any retroactive aspect in
the changes being made to the Code. He said that the Hofelds had asked him to
tell the Council that they respect the laws and the citizens of Winnetka, but
can’t stand back and see something happen that would effect their rights.
President Duhl told Mr. Fischer that the Hofelds’ situation is the reason that
the Council is considering these changes to the Village Code. He said the
Council has heard repeatedly from people throughout the Village about the
disruption to neighborhoods caused by construction and they want to tighten up
the ordinances to minimize the disruption. He said that it is his understanding
that if the ordinance is adopted, it will apply as of the adoption date and
doesn’t think that this exempts anyone with current building permits. However,
he also said that he doesn’t think the Council has the power to impact existing
rights and will leave that for Attorney Janega to answer at the next meeting.
He asked for a motion to introduce the ordinance.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Webster, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. MC-13-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1389 and 1340. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1389 in the amount of $761,553.83 and Warrant List No. 1390 in the
amount of $531,899.09 Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Ritchell,
Webster and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
None
11. Appointments.
President Duhl nominated Mary Hill to serve as Chair of the Forestry Commission
and asked for a motion to approve the appointment. Trustee Greenough moved to
approve the appointment, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the
motion passed unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Webster moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) and Section 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker
and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 8:34 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:18 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Webster. Absent: Trustee
Eilers. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Webster. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
The meeting adjourned at 9:19 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
December 7, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 18, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 7, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, and two persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   December 14, 2004, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   December 21, 2004, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend. Trustee Eilers mentioned that he might
arrive late.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 16, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-28-2004 – Tax Levy - Adoption.
President Duhl opened the Public Hearing regarding the tax levy for 2004 that
will be set at $10,542,699.00. He added that Ordinance M-29-2004, which is also
up for adoption, would abate the property taxes levied for the Series 2003
Bonds for the 2004 tax levy year.
Mr. McKee briefly reviewed the particulars of the tax levy ordinance. He
pointed out the Village receives only 15% of the property tax bill and said
that with the proposed increase in the property tax levy of 2.5%, the increase
for a homeowner who pays $10,000 a year in taxes would be $38. He once again
mentioned that these funds will help to pay for a 31% increase in the cost of
police and fire pension benefits which are set by the State legislature, noting
that 56¢ of every new tax dollar will go to funding those benefits.
President Duhl closed the Public Hearing.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-28-
2004. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Tucker, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
b)   Ordinance No. M-29-2004 – 2003 Bond Abatement – Adoption. President Duhl
called for a motion to adopt Ordinance No. M-29-2004 abating the Series 2003
Bonds. Trustee Ritchell so moved. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-13-2004 – Building Permit Renewal Amendments – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed changes to this ordinance that amends the
Building Code as it pertains to building permit extensions and renewals. She
explained that the proposed amendments address comments and suggested changes
made when the ordinance was introduced at the November 16th meeting. Among
these is a new section that completes the tie in between the provisions of the
Building Code and the nuisance provisions, revised language that clarifies the
authority of the Director of Community Development, and new text that addresses
the effect of the ordinance on existing permits.
Trustee Ritchell asked for an explanation of the difference between the date of
approval and the date of issuance. Ms. Janega said that typically these are the
same, but said it is possible to wait between approval and issuance. The
amended ordinance, however, would mark time from the date of approval rather
from the date of issuance, thereby, affording the Village as much control as
possible.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to amend Ordinance No. MC-13-
2004 as discussed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance No.
MC-13-2004. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster.
d)   Ordinance No. M-30-2004 – Disposal of Surplus Property – Introduction. Mr.
Williams reviewed the ordinance that would permit the Village to dispose of
unclaimed bicycles and accessories that have been held by the Police Department
for at least six months and for which reasonable efforts to locate the owners
of the property have been unsuccessful. The ordinance authorizes the Village
Manager to determine the value of the bicycles and dispose of them in any
manner he deems reasonable. He noted that in recent years, the Village has
rotated Village charities, and that the plan is to donate the subject items to
the Winnetka Community House for its bicycle auction.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
30-2004. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1391 and 1392. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1391 in the amount of $974,767.54 and Warrant List No. 1392 in the
amount of $668,776.03. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Webster.
b)   Banner Request. Manager Williams reviewed a request from the Winnetka
Alliance to display seven banners within the Village’s business districts from
January 26 through February 17, 2005 advertising the Alliance’s “TV Tun Out”
event. He reported that the Design Review Board had considered the request and
found it appropriate. The Board, however, felt that further consideration
should be given to a broader policy on the use of banners throughout the
Village and requested comments from the Business Community Development
Commission and the Chamber of Commerce.
Cindy Brady, Chairman for “TV Tune Out,” expressed concern that after reviewing
the groups’ reports the Council might adopt a policy prohibiting banners, in
which case the Alliance will have expended limited funds on banners that could
not be used again.
President Duhl advised Ms. Brady that even though there was a general consensus
among the sitting Trustees in favor of the banners, there is no way this
Council can promise that a future Council will not decide against banners.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the installation
of seasonal banners by the Winnekta Alliance, as requested. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that during November, the Village had
38,000 hits on its web site, and the complete Council agenda packet was
downloaded 1,795 times.
Mr. Williams also reported that George Noyes recently wrote a letter to the
Council suggesting that the Village amend its Sign Code to prohibit political
signs from being posted until 30 days before an election. His letter was
referred to the Design Review Board, which also acts as the Village’s Sign
Committee. The Committee has concluded that this question should be submitted
to the Caucus for a future Village questionnaire. He said that Mr. Noyes agrees
with the DRB’s recommendation.
Finally, Manager Williams mentioned the receipt of two letters from Mr. and
Mrs. Barry Barish, 1274 Trapp Lane that were delivered to the Trustees in
Friday’s Council packet. One letter was dated November 11, 2004 and addressed
to Mr. McKee, and one was dated November 24, 2004 to William R. Woodward, Metro
Transportation Group. Both letters pertain to the removal and replacement of
bushes at the Berish home. At Mrs. Barish’s request, he indicated that the text
of both letters would included in the minutes for tonight’s meeting, as
follows:
November 11, 2004
Edward F. McKee, Jr., Finance Director
Village of Winnetka
510 Green Bay Road
Winnetka, IL 60093
Re:   1274 Trapp Lane, Winnetka
Dear Mr. McKee:
Philip Couri forwarded to us a copy of your October 27, 2004 letter to him
regarding our request for reimbursement from the Village for the replacement of
the arborvitae on our property. We strongly disagree with the Village’s
representation of the facts in this matter. Your letter states, “. . . the
Village did not request the bushes be removed” while conveniently ignoring the
February 5, 2003 letter from Mr. Attiah which says, in part, that if we do not
relocate the bushes by February 21, 2003 “. . . the Public Works Department
will remove the plantings. A bill for services rendered will be mailed to your
attention for full payment. In addition you may be subject to a fine of not
less than $5.00 and not more than $750.00 for each day of violation.”
Yes, we were told to “relocate” the bushes onto private property,” where they
were all along, by the way. How did the Village expect us to do that without
“removing” them? One would think that the forester would know the difference
between a shrub and an arborvitae and understand that arborvitae cannot be
“trimmed.” We find it difficult to understand how the Village does not see that
a letter, addressed to us, suggesting that fines of substantial proportions
might be levied against us if we do not comply with certain demands, does not
constitute a “threat.”
In the end, your own Village Forrester [sic] marked the location for the
replanting practically in the same place they had been before. Doesn’t the
Village see an inconsistency here, i.e., take them out because there’s a
visibility problem but replant new ones only inches from the original location?
Heavy traffic on Hibbard Road necessitates the need for privacy and safety and
was a major concern to us when considering the removal and replacement of the
plantings. Our total out of pocket expenses for removing and replacing the
arborvitae and lilac screening was $4,828.75. If the plantings in question had,
indeed, been in violation of section 8.20.010 and 9.16.020 of the Village Code
these would have been legitimate costs that we should bear. However, on further
inspection, we were not in violation. In fact, this entire incident would have
been unnecessary if the stop sign and stop bar at this corner of Trapp Lane and
Hibbard were in the proper location – another error on the part of the Village.
We still believe that the Village should bear at least some of the financial
responsibility for this mess. Mr. Attiah, as an agent of the Village, made a
mistake – and a costly one at that. Were it not for his errors in judgement and
professional ineptitude, we wouldn’t be having this correspondence today. We
feel it would be in the best interest of both the Village and the Berish family
– a 32-1/2 year resident of the community – to reimburse us for the costs
involved in this totally unnecessary and most disturbing on-going incident.
Sincerely,
Mr. & Mrs. Barry Berish
and
November 24, 2004
William R. Woodward, E.I.T.
Senior Transportation Consultant
Metro Transportation Group
3100 W. Higgins Road, Ste. 100
Hoffman Estates, IL 60195-2093
Re:   Trapp Lane Intersection, Winnetka
Dear Mr. Woodward:
It was at my request that the Village of Winnetka authorized a traffic and
visibility study for the intersection of Trapp Lane and Hibbard Road so,
naturally, I anxiously awaited the results. I must admit, I am a little
disappointed with some of the conclusions. For instance, the second
recommendation in the report focuses on the removal of sight obstacles. It
seems clear to me and others exiting Trapp Lane that the trees on the northwest
parkway are the elements causing the most visibility problems. Some years ago I
suggested to the Village that these trees be removed. At the time they were
merely sapplings and removal would have presented no hardship for the Village.
Over the years these trees have begun to mature, and in subsequent years will
become more dense and visibility will continue to be reduced.
Additionally, there was no mention in the report of moving the stop bar closer
to the painted crosswalk, as they are located in most others parts of the
community. I.D.O.T. says, “stop bar should be placed four (4) feet in advance
of and parallel to the nearest crosswalk line.” The Metro Report suggests the
stop sign be relocated but doesn’t indicated where. If the stop sign and stop
bar were properly located, visibility to the south would not be an issue,
however, as long as the trees remain, residents, service providers and anyone
exiting Trapp Lane will still have to be overly cautious about oncoming traffic
from the north.
Although resolution of your recommendations now seems to be in the hands of the
Village, I think it would be extremely helpful if you would add an addendum to
the report that addresses both the tree issue and the location of the stop sign
and stop bar with a little more specificity. Please let me know your thoughts
on this.
Thank you.
Sincerely,
Mollybelle Berish
