Recording Secretary
December 14, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: January 18, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Thursday,
December 14, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, and
approximately four persons in the audience.
2)   Affordable Housing Plan Discussion.
Village Attorney Janega introduced Brian Zises, Director of Public Affairs for
the Illinois Housing Development Authority, the agency responsible for
monitoring the implementation of the affordable housing plans and assisting
communities with the appeals process.
Mr. Zises described the five elements that must be included in an Affordable
Housing plan:
   ·   a statement of the total number of current affordable housing units;
   ·   a statement of the total number of units needed to exempt the Village
from the 10% requirement;
   ·   the identification of land and structures for affordable housing
construction
   ·   local incentives to attract development
   ·   a goal that is at least one of the following: 10% overall affordable
housing, 15% minimum of affordable housing on all new developments or re-
developments; or a 3% increase in the amount of affordable housing
Mr. Zises then explained that the type of housing is completely within the
municipalities’ discretion and can include the elderly or disabled. He stressed
that communities will have local control over development and that developers
must meet several requirements before they can bring an appeal to the Appeals
Board. He said that developers have the full burden of proving that a denial of
a development by the Village was because of its affordable component. He said
that the IHDA’s interpretation of the Act was that as long as the Village has a
plan in place and is attempting to meet its goal, an appeal would not be
successful.
Mr. Zises described the make-up of the Appeals Board, discussed the steps the
Village should take in developing a plan, and defined what is affordable. He
also discussed what is and is not development or redevelopment and the sources
that can be used to track affordable housing units in the future. He specified
that tearing down a $400,000 home to build a $2 million dollar home would not
be considered redevelopment and acknowledged that tracking affordable housing
units would be a problem for municipalities.
Mr. Zises said that possible incentives offered by non-home rule communities
could be amended zoning requirements, planned unit developments with density
bonuses, waiver of fees, reductions in parking requirements, relaxed design
standards, reduced rates for water billing, tax increment financing and
property tax abatements. He added that home rule communities could also have
inclusionary zoning, expedite procedures, and assess tear down fees and taxes,
conversion taxes, impact fees and real estate transfer taxes.
Mr. Zises identified sources of funding and described the resources available
from the IHDA. In response to a question from Trustee Tucker, he said that
these resources can be accessed by either the developer or the municipality but
is usually a developer-driven process. He said that in addition to loans,
grants are provided from the Trust fund and are reviewed by the IHDA Loan
Committee, the State Trust Fund Advisory Commission and the Board of Directors.
President Duhl asked if a developer who is building $1 million condos would be
eligible for some of this money and if any North Shore communities had received
funds.
Mr. Zises replied that it is possible for this type of development to receive
funds, but that most likely this type of project would generate enough cash
flow to support the 15% affordable requirement. He said that Highland Park had
received approximately $80,000 from the IHDA for their affordable townhome
development, but the Mallinckrodt project in Wilmette had not received funds.
He said that in the past, mostly under the Section 8 program, other North Shore
communities had received funds, but those resources are no longer available.
Trustee Ritchell asked Mr. Zises how the Appeals Board comes into play.
Mr. Zises said that in order to bring an appeal to the Board, a developer must
prove that our plan was to prevent affordable housing development, for
instance, if the Village were to deny a development when a similar development
was approved in the past. He said that if Winnetka has a plan to attain the 15%
affordable housing goal, the plan is laid out well, identifies incentives and
types of housing, is following that plan, and the developer’s project falls
outside of that plan, then the developer would not have reason for an appeal.
Trustee Abell asked if the Appeals Board will be deciding if the tests have
been met and suggested that a proactive Board could take a different view from
Mr. Zises.
Mr. Zises replied that the Board does determine if tests have been met, but
stated that the make up of the Board is such that 3 out of the 7 Board members
would be from non-exempt communities, which should be of help.
Attorney Janega said that to bring an appeal, a developer would have to have
control of the property, 20% of which would be affordable housing, and the
burden of proof that the development has been denied because of its affordable
status. She said that it is in the nature of an affirmative defense because the
Village would be exempt from this process if we can establish that we have
adopted, implemented and met the goal of the plan. She asked whether, if there
are no developers interested in building affordable housing units, and Winnetka
does not meet or progress toward whatever goal has been set, Winnetka can claim
to be exempt if someone says there’s been no affordable housing built.
Mr. Zises replied that it would still be up to the developer to prove it and
the Appeals Board will determine if the plan has been implemented.
Trustee Eilers commented that the executive summary suggested discussing
single-family housing, but it seems to be broader than that.
Ms. Janega explained that the Act applies to dwelling units. She said that if a
commercial use replaces an existing commercial property, then the Act would not
apply, but if the new project included residential units, the Act would apply.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Zises if the IHDA will review and certify Winnetka’s
plan after it is submitted on March 31st.
Mr. Zises replied that the IHDA will review the plan to see that it has all of
the required elements and issue a letter to the community that the plan has
been received.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Zises if he was aware of any pending amendments to
the Act and Mr. Zises replied that he was not.
Trustee Ritchell asked what recourse the Village would have to require the
preservation of the affordable standard of rental units if an owner improves
private property and asked what types of funding would be available.
Mr. Zises said that the Village could attempt to discover what would appeal to
the landlords and develop incentives.
Attorney Janega asked Mr. Zises about improvements such as fire alarms and
other safety improvements that will increase rent and therefore become non-
affordable when they were previously affordable.
President Duhl said that he is concerned because, as a non-home rule unit, all
of these incentives are not going to amount to much, because the Village’s
portion of the taxes doesn’t amount to much. The Village really doesn’t have
the ability to control our rentals and rental amounts will increase even
without improvements. He said that even with the tools available to the
Village, because of the lack of land Winnetka may find it impossible to meet
its goal and comply with the Act. He asked if the Village were to adopt the 15%
goal, can it mandate that nobody can put up a new building without 15% of the
units being affordable and if a developer doesn’t want to do that, can the
Village make it a requirement?
Attorney Janega said that as a non-home rule community, she was of the opinion
that the Village could not mandate that because we do not have inclusionary
authority. She said the Affordable Housing Act requires the Village to comply
but doesn’t give it the tools to do that, unless it takes over the land or it
is Village property.
Mr. Zises said that it is the opinion of other attorneys he has spoken to that
the Act does let municipalities require 15% affordable housing.
Ms. Janega said that there will probably be litigation on this.
President Duhl said that all of the IHDA’s data currently is from the census
data of 2000 and asked if it begins with this date or is it from the date a
plan is adopted.
Mr. Zises replied that it is from data at the time the plan is adopted. He
explained that the list for exempt or non-exempt communities was compiled with
2000 census data which will not be used again. He said that the Appeals Board
will be looking at the progress of the plan in the future.
Trustees Ritchell and Abell commented that Winnetka should then have a new
accounting as of the date of the plan.
Manager Williams asked how it would be possible for the Village to accomplish
that.
President Duhl said that it seemed to him the Village can track new units in
the community and find out if 15% are affordable, but the problem would be
determining if they’ve gone from affordable to non-affordable. He said he
thinks IHDA’s data is high, in which case Winnetka is doomed before we get
started.
Trustee Eilers commented that it is not as relevant if the Village chooses the
15% goal.
President Duhl said that if the 2000 census is used, it is possible that units
that were affordable in 2000 may not be affordable now and the Village doesn’t
have a legal way to ascertain data as of the date of Winnetka’s plan.
In response, Mr. Zises said it was his opinion that data should be tracked
going forward and for purposes of compliance with the 15% standard, it would
only be the re-development of units.
Trustee Abell asked if Winnetka will be able to satisfy the first requirement
by using the IHDA’s numbers from the 2000 census when it submits a plan.
Mr. Zises replied in the affirmative to this and said that it would be tough
otherwise for Winnetka.
President Duhl said that if affordable single-family homes are replaced with
non-affordable units, then the only choice the Village has is the 15% goal, but
the Village may still not be able to comply with the Act.
Manager Williams said that a PUD may help us get there.
Ms. Janega agreed, but said it would only apply to someone who would agree to
build 15% affordable housing.
Trustee Abell asked for clarification and Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr.
Zises that the plan must be adopted by April 1st and then filed with the IHDA
within 60 days after its adoption.
Trustee Tucker asked whether the Appeals Board has final say in an appeal or if
it can go to court.
Ms. Janega answered that if a developer was denied because a project contains
affordable housing and if the Appeals Board decides that this is true, then it
could be appealed to the Appellate court. She said that someone could file
within the 45-day period to the Appeals Board and if they lose there, can then
go to court.
Trustee Tucker said this could become very expensive for the Village.
Ms. Janega asked about the guidelines on tracking existing housing that has
been redeveloped. She asked how the Village can do this without doing our own
census.
Mr. Zises said again that we should track going forward.
Ms. Janega said that there is an ambiguity in that an existing unit that has
improvements made to it counts as re-development and can change from affordable
to non affordable. She said that upgrades for safety reasons should not be
counted as redevelopment.
Mr. Zises said that he does not believe this would be considered redevelopment,
but he will take that question to the guidance group.
President Duhl asked Mr. Zises what would happen after 2010 when the Village’s
tracking shows that it has fewer affordable units than in 2000, even though it
met the 15% goal. He asked if someone can still challenge us?
Mr. Zises replied that if Winnetka met the 15% goal, then that would be the end
of it.
President Duhl said that some of this will be driven by the market and that if
the Village has no authority to tell owners to comply with requirements, what
will we do? He said Winnetka has been made a symbol in the past and has been
challenged on other issues. He wanted to be sure that the Village is able to
comply with the Act.
Mr. Zises said that he will find out and let us know.
Manager Williams said that affordable units are being lost with the conversion
of apartments to office space.
Mr. Zises agreed that this would be considered a loss of an affordable unit.
President Duhl asked how the Village could stop this?
Ms. Janega cited an instance where an owner who could not afford to upgrade
apartments obtained a special use to convert the apartments to offices because
of his economic hardship. She said that the affordable apartments are difficult
to maintain.
Manager Williams said that the pool of affordable housing is shrinking in
Winnetka as this example demonstrates.
Mr. Zises said that he has three questions to take back to guidance group, the
loss of affordable units due to conversion to office or commercial space which
Winnetka can do nothing about; what can Winnetka do if a developer refuses to
put in 15% affordable housing; and, if upgrades on existing units are
considered redevelopment.
Manager Williams said that suppose we track 10 new affordable units, does the
income of the people who live in these units have to be tracked as well?
Mr. Zises said that if the units were developed with federal funds someone
would be tracking the income of the people who live there, which would also be
the case if state funding was used. If the state or federal funds were not
used, and municipal funds were, than the municipality could require tracking of
income, but there is nothing in the Act that says the municipality has to do
this. He said he would get in touch with Ms. Janega on this issue.
There were no further questions from the Trustees or the audience.
President Duhl asked Ms. Janega for further comments.
Ms. Janega referred to the agenda materials to explain what the Village needs
to do to develop an affordable housing plan. She said that the bare numbers
have been predetermined by the Act, but Winnetka needs to identify the lands of
the Village for affordable housing. She said it is clear from IHDA’s procedural
guidelines that they don’t consider improvements to single family homes to be
development or redevelopment under the Act. She said that tear downs of non-
affordable homes replaced with other non-affordable homes are not considered
redevelopment, so there are no concerns about single-family development for
purposes of creating a plan. She suggested targeting zoning districts for
development or re-development instead of pieces of property with the
understanding that there is nothing in the Act or in the plan that requires the
Village to establish market values for property.
Trustee Tucker referred to Glencoe’s plan where they have designated their
central business district and asked Ms. Janega if it needs to be more specific
than that.
Ms. Janega replied that it does not have to be more specific and the Trustees
also need to look at areas where the Village will be able to incorporate
incentives. She suggested that they consider the type of development and then
the marketplace. She said it makes sense in a community like Winnetka to focus
on the denser multi-family districts than on the single-family districts. She
said that the Village needs to come up with a plan, not immediate
implementation, and that staff needs to look at the issue of incentives more
closely.
President Duhl directed staff to draft a skeleton of a plan for the February
study session.
Mr. Richard Koenig and Mr. Michael Roane, of the Illinois Housing Development
Corporation, asked to speak to the Council about their company, whose focus is
on developing affordable housing in multi-family and downtown districts.
President Duhl invited Mr. Koenig to tell the Council what his company could do
in Winnetka.
Mr. Koenig said that it is easier to do rental units that are affordable than
it is residential units and that as the developer, his company would monitor
income and track things for the Village.
President Duhl asked Mr. Koenig if he can get funds from IHDA by doing rental
units.
Mr. Koenig replied that they can’t do it without the help of the municipality
and they work hard to develop the kinds of units Winnetka would want. He said
that the hard part is to identify the sites and then find owners who are
interested in selling. He said that he thinks there are enough sources for
funds that the Village would not have to come up with any money.
President Duhl commented that Mr. Koenig seemed to be suggesting that the
Village take an active role in asking owners if they want to sell or, in other
words, take a proactive approach.
Mr. Koenig replied that it would be a matter of working with his company,
instead of the Village doing it alone, and working with many developers and
different pieces of property. He also said that 3 or 4 units could be done per
year to show that the Village is making progress towards the goal of their
plan. Mr. Koenig added that he is a former employee of the IHDA and familiar
with that agency.
3)   Executive Session
Trustee Greenough moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), and Section 2(c)(1), and Section 2
(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Webster. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:10 p.m.
The Study Session reconvened at 9:25 p.m. Present: President Duhl, Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell and Tucker. Absent: Trustee Webster. Also
present, Village Manager Douglas Williams and Village Attorney Katherine
Janega.
4)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:25 p.m.
