December 21, 2004
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 18, 2001)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 21, 2004 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Trustees Tom
Eilers and David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, Fire Chief
Scott Smith, Deputy Fire Chief Steve Laureys and no persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   January 4, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   January 11, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 7, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-30-2004 – Disposal of Surplus Property – Adoption.
President Duhl called for a motion to adopt the ordinance that would permit the
Village to dispose of unclaimed bicycles and accessories that have been held by
the Police Department for at least six months and for which reasonable efforts
to locate the owners of the property have been unsuccessful. Trustee Ritchell
so moved, seconded by Trustee Abell. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Eilers and Webster.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1393 and 1394. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1393 in the amount of $954,176.29 and Warrant List No. 1394 in the
amount of $381,361.58. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and Webster.
b)   Bid No. 04-021: Village Yards Door Replacement. Mr. Saunders reviewed the
bids to replace thirteen (13) sectional overhead doors at the Village Yards. He
explained that the existing doors are at the end of their useful life and were
originally intended to be replaced as a part of the Village Yards
Rehabilitation Project. Their replacement, however, was deleted from the
project as a cost-reduction measure. He said that now that the project has been
completed they could be replaced within the original project budget. The
Council authorized staff to proceed with soliciting bids at its November 2nd
meeting.
Trustee Tucker remarked that she was very much in support of the request. She
said that it is sensible for the Village to replace items as they break and
need repair in order to maintain the Village’s assets. She noted that several
of these doors are totally inoperable and others barely function, so at some
point, they are going to have to be taken care of in any event.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to award the contract for
the replacement of the sectional overhead doors to Pro-Line Door Systems, Inc.,
in the amount of $61,627, as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Greenough, Tucker, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and Webster.
c)   Bid No. 04-020: West Hibbard Road Drainage Improvement, Phase III. Mr.
Saunders reviewed the bids to replace the existing 24” storm sewer line along
the east edge of the golf course with a 36” line, completing the Village’s
planned improvements for the neighborhoods between Hibbard Road and the Golf
Course. He recommended that the bid be awarded to DiMeo Brothers Construction
in the amount of $230,216 and authorize the Village President to sign the
contract on behalf of the Village. He indicated that staff had checked the
bidder’s municipal references and received satisfactory recommenda-tions. He
also reported that the Village had received an extremely favorable bid on this
project, well below the original budgeted amount, in large part because of the
time of year in which it was bid, when contractors want to keep their
experienced workers on a job.
Trustee Ritchell inquired whether this was the end of the project. Mr. Saunders
indicated that this work will complete what the Village planned.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell moved to award the contract as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and
Webster.
d)   Self Contained Breathing Apparatus Replacement (SCBA). Chief Smith
reviewed a request to replace thirty 30-minute air cylinders used by
firefighters to breathe under adverse conditions at emergency incidents. He
explained that the cylinders require replacement due to age and wear, and
pursuant to OSHA requirements must be retired after 15 years of service. The
cylinders provide air for the breathing apparatus, which also requires
upgrading. Chief Smith said that since the scope of the necessary purchases
exceeds the amount budgeted, the Foreign Fire Insurance Board has approved
$32,000 towards the total cost. He recommended that the purchase of the air
cylinders and breathing apparatus from Air One Equipment, the sole source for
the vendor for the most desirable MSA equipment, in the amount of $64,000.
Manager Williams explained that the funds contributed by the Foreign Fire
Insurance Board come from levies by the State on insurance companies selling
insurance in the State of Illinois. These funds are collected by the State and
given directly to the Fire Department. He pointed out that this is a life-
safety issue for the Village’s firefighters and that because of the
contribution from the Foreign Fire Insurance Board, the cost to the taxpayers
is exactly what was originally budgeted.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough moved to approve the purchase as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Abell, Greenough, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and
Webster.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams referred to a letter in the Trustees’ packets
notifying the Village that Moffat Mall in Chestnut Court was selected to
receive an Excellence in Landscape Gold award from the Illinois Landscape
Contractors Association. The project to rehabilitate Moffat Mall was a joint
public-private partnership undertaken by the Winnetka Garden Club and the
Village. It included replacement and realignment of paved and planted areas,
replacement of plant materials, hardscape surfaces and street furniture, and a
new decorative fountain and decorative surround. 
Mr. Williams also reported for the record that Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp
Lane, had sent another letter regarding the sight distance at Trapp and Hibbard
Roads. A copy of Ms. Berish’s letter is attached as Attachment #1.
11. Appointments.
None.
Executive Session. None.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and
Webster. The meeting adjourned at 7:57 p.m.
