2005
January 11, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 1, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 11, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public
Works Steven Saunders and approximately 24 persons in the audience.
2)   Public Hearing and Discussion of Home Rule Referendum.
President Duhl noted that tonight’s public hearing had been on the front page
of the Winnetka Report and mentioned in an article in the Winnetka Talk. He
said that the Council would officially consider the matter at its January 18th
Council meeting.
Bernie Hammer, 1455 Tower Rd., asked for a presentation on why the powers
granted in the Village Charter of 1869 are insufficient to permit the Village
to license trades and occupations and to provide the Village with the same
powers it is seeking under Home Rule.
President Duhl explained that a special charter from the Legislature originally
created the Village, but that there is an issue as to what authority that
charter still has as much of the authority therein has eroded over time. The
Legislature has changed the charter in a number of ways that suggest that it no
longer provides the same level of authority that it once had. He said that the
authorities under Home Rule are much clearer and pointed out that of the six
benefits identified by the Village Presidents’ Committee, five of them have
nothing to do with the Village’s charter. He remarked that it is clear from the
operation of the law over the last 130 years, that what continuing vitality the
Charter has can only be resolved through case-by-case litigation that is not
beneficial to the Village.
Attorney Janega added that when the Charter was created the concept of land use
and zoning was not contemplated. Therefore, zoning issues and matters
pertaining thereto were not addressed by the Charter. She said that Home Rule
status provides a municipality with both greater flexibility and protection.
She pointed out that there are specific steps that must be followed in order
for the Legislature to preempt Home Rule authority, whereas, there are no such
steps guarding the authority granted by the Charter.
Manager Williams recited the language from the Charter enumerating those
businesses that the Village has authority to license, noting that contractors
are not among them. He pointed out the other advantages of Home Rule that are
not addressed by the Charter, including flexibility in redeveloping the Post
Office site, opportunities to enhance the commercial districts, and
streamlining the zoning and building permit process. He noted that local zoning
authority is being eroded by the State and cited the case of Hawthorne v.
Olympia Fields in which the State courts overruled the local zoning ordinance
and permitted a day care facility to be located in a residential neighborhood.
Dick Golden, 1203 Ash St., remarked that the principle of agreement throughout
the community was very much a part of the Village’s history. He said he was in
favor of Home Rule because he wanted to see the authority to regulate
activities rest inside of the Village’s Council Chambers.
An audience member asked President Duhl to review the impact of Home Rule on
the other governmental bodies in the Village. President Duhl responded saying
that Home Rule only applies to the Village and in no way affects the Library,
Park, or School Boards.
Trustee Tucker added that given the long history of cooperation among the
different governmental entities in the Village that she did not believe that
the Village would abuse any powers that it does have over them.
President Duhl referred to the question of whether New Trier School District is
required to abide by the Village’s Zoning Ordinance. He said that historically,
they have come through the Village’s process, but Home Rule status would
solidify the Village’s zoning authority.
Pete Cruikshank, 710 Lincoln, asked Mr. Williams to enumerate some of the
possibilities available to the Village.
Mr. Williams mentioned that any increased sales tax revenues might be used to
update the schools or used to partner with one or more of the other Village
entities for some common goal.
Mr. Hammer asked whether Home Rule authority would permit the Village to enact
impact fees on new construction. He also inquired about a real estate transfer
tax.
Manager Williams replied that he and the Village Attorney believe that an
impact fee might be possible because of the increased flexibility afforded by
Home Rule. He, however, pointed out that it would take a separate referendum to
enact a real estate transfer tax and emphasized that at this time the Village
has not even considered that possibility.
President Duhl added that the beauty of Home Rule is that it opens up the
possibility to think about things that have never been thought about before. It
lets the Council and the community think creatively about how to solve today’s
problems as well as those that haven’t even been contemplated.
John Kelly, a reporter for Pioneer Press asked what practical changes the
residents might expect to see if the a Home Rule referendum passes.
President Duhl said that it was likely that the Village would begin to license
various types of contractors and that the building and zoning processes would
be streamlined. Also, the Post Office Committee would have the flexibility to
consider public/private partnerships in the redevelopment of that site.
Gene Greable, 477 Berkeley, asked whether Home Rule would have any impact on
the Winnetka Caucus. Both President Duhl and Manager Williams responded in the
negative.
There being no further comments from the audience, President Duhl closed the
Public Hearing, saying that the Council at its next meeting on Tuesday, January
18, 2005 would consider putting a Home Rule referendum on the ballot for
approval.
3)   Executive Session.
There was no Executive Session.
4)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 8:34 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Recording Secretary
January 18, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 1, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 18, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee David Webster. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus and 10 persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Declaration of Vacancy – Trustee.
President Duhl declared a vacancy in the Council due to the resignation of
Trustee David Webster received January 17, 2005.
5. Appointment – Trustee.
President Duhl appointed Edmund Woodbury to the Council to complete the term of
David Webster.
Trustee Eilers, moved to approve the appointment of Edmund Woodbury to the
Village Council, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
Manager Williams, as Village Clerk, swore in Mr. Woodbury as Trustee of the
Village Council.
6. Quorum.
a)   January 25, 2005, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   January 27, 2005, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   February 1, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except for President Duhl, indicated that they expected to attend.
7. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 14, 2004, Study Session. Trustee Abell requested that a
clarification be made to page 5 of the minutes concerning his comments about
the dates for adoption and filing of the affordable housing plan. Trustee
Eilers moved to amend the minutes, seconded by Trustee Greenough, and by voice
vote, the motion carried. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the minutes, as
amended. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   December 21, 2004, Regular Meeting. Trustee Woodbury moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   January 4, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
8. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution No. R-05-2005 – Home Rule - Adoption. Village Attorney Janega
explained the proposal before the Council is to adopt the resolution to place a
question on the April 5th ballot to ask voters whether Winnetka should become a
home rule unit.
President Duhl commented that he had initiated a study by a committee of past
Village presidents to determine whether the question of home rule in Winnetka
should be pursued. He said that over the last several months, he had talked
about home rule status at caucus meetings and the Village Manager had met with
other Boards and committees within the community to provide information as
well. Mr. Duhl stated that a public hearing was also held to receive input from
the community on the subject. He then asked for comments from the audience.
Mr. Bernard Hammer, 1455 Tower Road, stated that he believed there is a
difference of opinions about whether the Affordable Housing Act applies to home
rule communities, and asked if the Village would be forced to provide
affordable housing in the future, if the affordable housing in the Village was
insufficient. He also expressed his belief that the Village has the power,
under the Village Charter, to license contractors, and stated he had appellate
court opinions on this subject. He also expressed concern that, if the Village
adopted home rule, it would have the power to go into business and to own and
operate real estate with a private developer, and added that his specific
concern was in regard to the development of the Post Office property.
President Duhl replied that the advice of the Village's legal counsel is that
the Affordable Housing Act does not apply to home rule communities, that the
future would depend on the Council seated at that time, and that, although
opinions differ, it is also the advice of counsel that the Village should not
rely on our charter to accomplish what home rule can. President Duhl said that
discussions had been held with regard to the Village’s authority under home
rule, and it was suggested that the Council should be given whatever authority
is possible, trusting that they will use it wisely.
President Duhl further explained that this Council and all prior Councils have
traditionally operated with public input and public hearings so that if there
is an issue about the specific use of a property, the public will be provided
an opportunity to comment. He also said that nothing is irrevocable and there
is nothing to prevent Village residents from telling the Council to dispose of
its interest in a property. He said that he trusts the Village to investigate
whatever needs to be investigated and to seek input from the community. He
agreed that it is true that we should all be wary of government, but in the
last 20-30 years, the Council has been enormously responsive and has attempted
to do what the community wants. He said that without home rule, the State
legislature tells us what to do. He asked if we were going to trust the State
legislature or trust our own neighbors on the Council, whose terms turn over
every two years. President Duhl said that to him that is a very simple question
to answer.
President Duhl added that he appreciates Mr. Hammer’s comments. and invited him
to attend post office committee meetings the 3rd Thursday of every month.
Ms. Janega asked Mr. Hammer to provide her with the citations of the appellate
cases he mentioned.
Trustee Eilers commented that the passing of this resolution does not mean that
the Village is adopting home rule; it means that the committee will continue to
work until election day, when the residents will vote on this.
Trustee Abell added that he would prefer to have the home rule powers available
to the Village, subject to the Trustees and the Village President who are voted
in every year, rather than having to do what a special interest group tells us
to do. He said that he also believes the Council should adopt this resolution.
Trustee Woodbury said that this Council has done a great service to the
Village, and also thinks it’s time to take the question to the voters.
Trustee Ritchell echoed Trustee Woodbury’s words and thanked the former
presidents and all of the other people involved for their work. He agreed it is
time to put this to the people to vote on it.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
05-2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None
b)   Ordinance No. M-01-2005 – Zoning Variation: 766 Prospect - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio summarized this request for a variation from the intensity of use
of lot and maximum building size requirements of the zoning ordinance to permit
the construction of a mudroom addition and front porch to the home at 766
Prospect Ave. He added that there had been two previous variation requests in
1995 and in 1998 for interior remodeling and that the Zoning Board at their
December meeting voted 7-0 for approval.
The applicant’s architect, Healy Rice, explained that the homeowners are
proposing to add space for a mudroom with a closet, new stoop and steps as well
as a new front porch to replace the existing porch that is in the Queen Anne
style of the home. She said that one of the difficulties is that overhangs on
the house are counted toward gross floor area even though it is really air
space. She said the 29 square feet of additional area is only a 1% increase
overall.
President Duhl ascertained that the audience and the Council had no questions
or comments.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. M-01-2005. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-02-2005 – Zoning Variation: 458 Sunset – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio explained that, if granted, this request for variation from the
intensity of use of lot, building line articulation and side yard setback
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance would permit the construction of a second
floor addition at the rear of the home at 458 Sunset. He said that the
petitioners had previously submitted plans for a substantial addition to the
home which complied with all regulations and that were approved. Subsequently,
he said the design of the laundry was altered to accommodate the washer and
dryer, necessitating variations from the zoning ordinance. Mr. D’Onofrio
summarized each of the variations that had been requested and added that the
Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 7-0 to approve the variations at their
December meeting.
Mrs. Ackerman, the homeowner, stated that her family had lived in the home
since 1955 and had long dreamed of building a second floor addition to add a
master bedroom and bath. She said that it is now very important to her that she
have a laundry room on the second floor rather than in the basement, but the
zoning ordinance regulations only allowed them to have a 5 foot wide space. She
said that after measuring her washer and dryer, they determined that 5 feet of
space was insufficient. In order to make it a safe and functional room, she
hoped that the Council would consider her variation request.
President Duhl asked for comments or questions from the audience and the
Trustees. There were none.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-02-2005. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution No. R-04-2005 – Bluhm Subdivision: Final Plat Approval –
Adoption. Ms. Janega explained that preliminary approval of this subdivision
was granted last fall and at that time, the Council also allowed a variation to
the Zoning Ordinance so that the homeowner could live in the existing house on
the property while the new one was constructed. The subdivision is now before
the Council for final approval but, staff having learned that the homeowners
have vacated the house, Ms. Janega originally drafted the resolution approving
the final plat so that access via the narrow drive and private road would be
prohibited while the property is under construction. She further explained,
however, she had found it necessary to revise the resolution because the
original resolution could be read to amend the zoning variation ordinance's
access provisions, and a resolution can't amend an ordinance. She recommended
that the Council consider the resolution in the revised form. She said that she
had spoken with Mr. Bluhm’s attorney, Mr. Schenk, during the afternoon and he
had agreed to some of the revisions, but did not want to change the access
provisions that were in the ordinance. She added that neither Mr. Schenk nor
Mr. Bluhm was able to attend the Council meeting, but that someone from the
project architect's office was present, and that she and Mr. Schenk had agreed
that, if the Council continued to have questions over construction access, she
would ask that the Council postpone its consideration until Mr. Schenk or Mr.
Bluhm could be present.
After discussing both the revised resolution and the access provisions of the
ordinance, the Council decided to table the adoption of the resolution, and
asked staff to research the best way to proceed with this matter.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to table Resolution No. R-
04-2005 to the Council’s next regular meeting. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
9. Old Business.
None.
10. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1397 and 1398. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, Trustee Ritchell moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1397 in the amount of $678,910.40 and Warrant List No.
1398 in the amount of $324,828.80. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Status Report: 411 Linden. Ms. Janega updated the Council on the status of
the construction at 411 Linden Avenue as required by the conditions of the
special use permit. She informed the Council that the Historical Society
remains in compliance, but is not yet open to the public.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak, president of Winnetka Historical Society, advised the
Council that she hoped construction would be completed by the end of February.
She asked that the Council defer the report on the operation of the museum
until next year since it is not yet open to the public.
Trustee Eilers moved to extend the review of the status of the Historical
Society special use permit to next year, seconded by Trustee Greenough. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
11. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
12. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers attended the Chamber of Commerce meeting and
reported on several new businesses in the community. He also reported that the
Business Community Development Commission had met and is continuing its work.
b)   President. President Duhl announced that the Council will hold meetings on
the budget on January 25, January 27 and February 3 and invited anyone
interested to attend the presentation of the budget to the Council. He also
said that a copy of the proposed budget could be found at the library.
13. Appointments.
None.
14. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:38 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:50 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Absent:
None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
15. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Woodbury, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:51 p.m.
