Deputy Clerk
Recording Secretary
February 8, 2005
(Approved: March 1, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 8, 2005 at 7:44 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:44 p.m. Present: Trustees, Nan
Greenough, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Trustee Ed Woodbury
arrived at 7:48 p.m.. Absent: Trustee Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and approximately 18 persons in
the audience. Trustee Candidate Ken Behles was also present.
2)   Public Hearing and Discussion: Elevators vs. Ramps at Elm Street Train
Station.
President Duhl opened the public hearing to discuss the maintenance and repair
of the elevators vs. the installation of ramps at the Elm Street train station.
Mr. Dave Olson, 1457 Asbury, spoke on behalf of the New Trier Township
Committee on Disabilities and in support of retaining the elevators. He
reported on the number of people with disabilities and the number of requests
for temporary disability stickers per year in Winnetka and stated that
according to the input he has received, the Committee has concluded that the
elevators are the best option for the needs of the community. He further stated
that it was his opinion that after meeting with the Metra officials, there was
no difference in viewpoints and that Metra does not believe the elevators
should be removed. He thanked the Village for asking the committee to be
involved in the discussions on this issue.
Mr. Clayton Weaver, spokesperson for Metra, said that Metra’s goal is to
develop a solution for the best accessibility, with the focus largely on
reliability, because the station that is accessible at all times. After
speaking with an elevator technician from Otis, Mr. Weaver said that Metra
believes that an elevator designed for outdoor use with a heated vestibule and
elevator shaft should withstand inclement weather conditions and reduce the
need for repairs. He also said that the pedestrian bridge will be rebuilt
slightly to the south of its current location. He indicated that regular
maintenance of the elevators is included in the capital project, so their major
concern is not the repair cost, but finding a way to work with the Village so
that Metra is more responsive to the needs of the elevator.
President Duhl commented that it is neither the cost nor providing assistance
to Metra that is of concern, but rather, that he would be reluctant to ask the
Village to take on the primary responsibility for repair because of the
resulting liability. He clarified with Mr. Weaver that Metra recognizes
maintenance is their responsibility because it is their service, and that Metra
will maintain the elevators if the Village decides that they want them.
Mr. Weaver replied that there is no problem with Metra assuming maintenance
responsibility, but he is asking that the Village help them be more responsive
when the elevators need service.
Trustees Tucker, Eilers Woodbury and Ritchell commented upon Metra’s
responsiveness to the issue, were appreciative of Metra’s agreement to assume
responsibility for the maintenance and repair of the elevators, and were
confident that the Village and Metra could work together.
Trustee Greenough added that she had been concerned about the maintenance and
reliability of the elevators, but Mr. Weaver seems to have answered those
concerns and that reliability issues may be resolved with elevators meant for
outdoor use.
Mr. Weaver replied that Metra is responding to the advice that the industry is
giving them.
President Duhl said that the Council’s comments reveal that the Trustees would
like to go forward with the elevators. He added that he is appreciative of
Metra’s willingness to work with the Village,he is proud of the way the process
has worked and he hopes that Mr. Olson and all the others involved agree that
the right answer has been found.
Jean Beckman, a resident who lives on Foxdale, said she would like to take this
opportunity to encourage Metra to consider a plan for installing elevators in
other train stations. She said she knows that there are other people who live
in Winnetka that can get on the train here, but are limited in the places where
they can get off the train. She urged Metra to make other train stations
accessible.
Mr. Ted Reed introduced two people from Barnard Elevator Company who were there
to answer the Council’s questions. One of the representatives from Barnard
commented that many of the shutdowns at the Winnetka station are from weather
related problems
There being no further comments or questions from the audience or the Trustees,
President Duhl closed the Public Hearing at 8:15 p.m.
3)   Discussion: Draft Affordable Housing Plan.
Village Attorney Janega reviewed the draft of the Village’s Affordable Housing
Plan , described the required components of the Affordable Housing Act and
explained that the Affordable Housing Plan is a natural outgrowth from the
goals and objectives of the Comprehensive Plan, which identified the need for
moderately priced housing for its senior citizens. She said the Plan gives a
general history of the development of land uses, identifies places that are
suitable for affordable housing and sets goals to increase the number of
affordable housing units and to have 10% affordable housing overall in the
Village. She explained that the Village-wide goal of having 15% of the units in
new development meet affordability standards is based on statistics for the
whole Village, not just the areas designated for affordable housing.
Ms. Janega also spoke about the Village’s limited authority to provide
incentives to affordable housing developers in the designated areas, but that
the Council could consider reductions in fees as an incentive. She added that
planned unit developments are a potential vehicle for providing incentives and
the Village already has a committee to study areas for PUDs.
President Duhl asked for comments.
Trustee Woodbury said that the draft plan is well done and grows out of the
dialogs they’ve had on the Comprehensive Plan and diversity of housing stock.
He asked Ms. Janega if there are non-conforming uses in some of the designated
districts.
Ms. Janega replied that there are some single-family homes in the commercial
and multi-family zoning districts at Green Bay and Gage, Green Bay and Merrill,
Green Bay between Pine and Eldorado, and at Spruce and Birch.
President Duhl asked Ms. Janega to include young families as well as senior
citizens to the last page of the Plan and confirmed with her that as a non-home
rule community, the Village cannot mandate developers to build affordable
housing. In his opinion, he said this plan is the best the Village can do, but
it won’t necessarily allow us to meet our goal.
Ms. Janega replied that adoption of the plan puts the Village in compliance
with the law. She said it is a planning tool and the goal of the Act is to get
communities to address the issue of affordability. She said the Act has no
requirements to force municipalities to put in an affordable unit, but that
what happens is that it becomes a record-keeping matter as development occurs.
She said the Village will need to document discussions regarding incentives to
show the Appeals Board when it is appointed, that we’ve made a good faith
effort to implement the plan.
Trustee Eilers said that his understanding is that if we don’t meet the goal,
we still could be brought before the Appeals Board.
Ms. Janega replied that this was true, but first the Plan must be adopted. She
said that until we reach the 10% threshold, a denial to an affordable housing
developer would be subject to review by the Appeals Board. If you’re in
compliance with your Plan, or meeting affordability standards, the Board will
not accept an appeal She added that once the Village has reached the 10% goal,
it is exempt from the Act.
Ms. Janega said that the affordable standards are based on sales of houses, so
it isn’t a once a year statistic, but something that changes when transfer of
ownership occurs, which is hard to track. She said that every time there is a
new census, IHDA will put out new statistics and in the meantime between
censuses, the Village will need to compile information.
Trustee Eilers was concerned about adding a definition regarding single-family
development and asked that Ms. Janega include the IHDA’s definition of new or
re-development.
Manager Williams wondered if IHDA’s opinion should be attached to our Plan.
Ms. Janega replied that the IHDA’s interpretation is that one-for-one
replacement of a single-family home is not considered to be new or re-
development. She said that their opinion is published in the guidelines and she
didn’t recommend adding their definition or attaching the opinion to the Plan
because IHDA’s guidelines are a separate document from the Affordable Housing
Act and she would not want the Village to go on record with an interpretation
that may be an erroneous and is subject to change. She said the Act has very
clear standards and the way it works is that you identify the areas for
affordable housing, set your goal and provide incentives.
President Duhl added that he thinks the goal as Ms. Janega has stated it in the
draft and as it is in the Act, is exactly what we should do.
Ms. Janega explained that if a house is purchased at an affordable price and a
developer then buys it to build a more expensive home, that will show up as a
loss of an affordable unit. She further explained that there is nothing in the
Act that says you have to build the units in any one spot; instead, the Act
says that the Village should designate areas that are most suitable. She said
that if a developer wants to restore a house and keep it affordable, the
Village might be able to help that person and count that as an affordable unit.
Trustee Eilers insisted that the definition should be added to the Plan because
if we have 100 teardowns and none of them are affordable, the Village has
failed to comply with its own ordinance. He was concerned about how the Village
shows that we have complied with the Act. He added that without a definition
there is no way to know how compliance will be measured.
President Duhl disagreed. He said that we know how to measure our goal now, but
suppose IHDA says in a year that they were wrong. If the Plan is left as it is,
there is no problem.
Trustee Woodbury said that compliance is shown through the effort we’ve made to
try and meet the goal and added that it is a goal, not a requirement. He said
that if no one wants to develop here, we are not going to be in compliance and
don’t have the ability to comply.
President Duhl added that if the Village stays non-home rule, there is not a
lot of discretion on how we comply with the Act. He said that what is in the
Plan is what the Act requires and we will try to be in compliance according to
the IHDA guidelines.
Trustee Tucker said that we are probably better off with the language the way
it is in the draft plan and thought Ms. Janega had done a really good job. She
said she understands Trustee Eilers’ concerns, but didn’t think we need to go
there right now.
President Duhl suggested that the public hearing be set for March 15th to get
comments from the community comment before adopting the Plan.
Martha Ross-Mockaitis, an ordained minister, encouraged the Council to continue
to think this through and if the clergy can be of assistance in planning and
working on this issue, they would like to help. She said that it is an
emotional issue and would be happy to help temper that, adding that the issue
needs to be presented to the community in a positive way. She also offered to
help identify contractors and builders who want to do moderately priced
housing.
President Duhl responded that as a non home rule unit, the Village doesn’t have
authority to do much, and at this point in time, there is a greater need for
views on what we should be doing.
Trustee Eilers also said that this Council has made a decision to address the
issue, whether the Village is home rule or not.
President Duhl concluded by saying that we all recognize that there is a need
for affordable housing and that it was addressed in the Winnetka 2020
Comprehensive Plan. He said that the Village must discover the best way to go
about it.
4)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greenough seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:04 p.m.
5)   Adjournment.
The Council returned to the open meeting at 10:00 p.m. and the meeting was
adjourned without further business.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
February 8, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: May 3, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 8, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Michael Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker. . Absent:
Trustees Abell and Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and 18 persons in the audience, including
Trustee Candidate Ken Behles.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Ordinances and Resolutions.
Trustee Woodbury came in at 7:48 p.m.
a)   Resolution No. R-04-2005 – Bluhm Subdivision: Final Plat Approval –
Adoption. Trustee Greenough moved to remove the matter from the table. Trustee
Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ms. Janega explained that the issues left outstanding at the last meeting have
been resolved with the applicant and the resolution has been revised to reflect
the results of discussions at the Council meetings. She stated that Mr. Bluhm
has accepted a requirement that the owner provide written confirmation that all
contractors, subcontractors and other persons hired to work on the construction
of the residence will use the Sheridan Road access and there are no other
outstanding issues or substantive changes.
President Duhl asked for comments from Mr. Shenk, the property owner’s
attorney, and from the Council and there were none.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt Resolution R-4-2005 approving the final plat
of the Bluhm Subdivision, seconded by Trustee Eilers. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Woodbury and Abell.
5. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the special
meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Woodbury. The meeting adjourned at 7:44 p.m.
