Deputy Clerk
February 15, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 1, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 15, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker and Ed Woodbury. Absent:
Trustee Tom Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders and 4
persons in the audience, including Trustee Candidates Ken Behles and Sandra
Berger.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that the budget study session scheduled after the
regular meeting has been cancelled. The Council approved the Agenda, as
amended.
4. Quorum.
a)   March 1, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   March 8, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 25, 2005, Budget Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   January 27, 2005, Budget Meeting. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   February 1, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-01-2005 – Fire Alarm Amendment - Adoption. President Duhl
asked Village Attorney Janega to review this ordinance which corrects a
technical error in the Village Code pertaining to automatic fire alarms.
Attorney Janega explained that in the course of recodifying the Village Code,
the codifiers left out one of the provisions of the old Code and this technical
correction simply puts the provision into the Code that should have been
included in the original codification. She recommended that the Council waive
introduction of this ordinance since it is in the best interest of the Village
to rectify the codifier’s error.
Trustee Woodbury moved to waive introduction of Ordinance MC-01-2005, seconded
by Trustee Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Eilers.
Trustee Woodbury move to adopt Ordinance MC-01-2005, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Eilers.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business
.
a)   Music Institute Request – 2006 Vehicle Sticker.
Susan VanDeusen, Director of Communications for the Music Institute of Chicago,
spoke on behalf of this not-for-profit school that has been in Winnetka since
1931 and will be celebrating its 75th anniversary in 2006. She requested that
the Village Council approve their request to celebrate their anniversary by
being on the Village vehicle stickers next year. She explained some of the
services of the school, community events they sponsor and then distributed a
prototype of the vehicle sticker they are proposing.
President Duhl ascertained from the Trustees and Manager Williams that previous
requests of this nature have been approved as they have been received and that
this is the only request received for 2006.
Manager Williams said that a decision has to be made within the next 45 days to
meet deadlines for creating the vehicle sticker so that it is available by
October of this year.
Trustee Abell suggested that in the future, the Council would probably want to
rotate the organizations that receive approval.
President Duhl determined that the consensus of Council was that the Trustees
support the Music Institute’s request and it was approved.
Ms. VanDeusen thanked the Council and reported that the contact person at the
school will be Stephanie Kunkel.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1401 and 1402. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, Trustee Tucker moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1401 in the amount of $670,718.80 and Warrant List No.
1402 in the amount of $523,338.90. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
c)   Bid No. 05-003 - Truck Chassis. Director of Public Works Steve Saunders
reported that the budget for 2004/2005 included an item for the purchase of a
replacement for PW-32, one of the Village’s refuse trucks; however, the staff
believes that instead of replacing this vehicle, the packer body of the truck
is still useful and only the chassis needs replacement. Mr. Saunders said that
bids were received from Northwest Trucks, Chicago International, Northwest
Ford/Sterling and the lowest responsible bid which meets all specifications was
submitted by Northwest Trucks. He recommended that the Council award the bid
for a Freightliner M2 Chassis to Northwest Trucks in the amount of $63,091.
In response to questions from Trustee Ritchell, Mr. Saunders said that this
purchase is not part of the State of Illinois purchasing process and that
because awards are typically to the low bidder, it is not always possible to
use domestic businesses. He also stated that Public Works has been trying to
standardize its equipment, and this purchase will further that effort.
Trustee Tucker said that she appreciates that the staff is trying to get more
mileage out of existing equipment.
Mr. Saunders replied that the staff does try to do this, especially with the
large equipment because it is so expensive and added that this particular truck
has been in use for 13 years.
Trustee Abell moved to award Bid No. 05-003 to Northwest Trucks in the amount
of $63,091 for the purchase of a truck chassis for PW-32, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Eilers.
d)   GIS Service Provider Agreement. Mr. Saunders reported that the Geographic
Information Systems Consortium has selected Municipal GIS Partners in Highland
Park as the service provider for development services. He added that MGP has
been providing these services to members since GISCon’s inception and the
Village is in year 3 of a 5-year plan for development, implementation and
management of a fully operational GIS. He explained that being a member of the
consortium helps the Village’s buying power when looking for service providers
and aerial photographers. He said the staff recommends approval of the service
agreement with MGP in the amount of $45,875.
In response to the Trustees’ questions, Mr. Saunders said that aerial
photography will add approximately $25,000, the GIS committee members are
mostly department heads, that if information is available on smaller properties
in the Village, it will be included in the system and that increases in the
budget for the next few years would be to cover increases in MGP’s hourly
rates. He explained that MGP’s personnel are on call as needed and on site 3
days a week.
Trustee Tucker asked for Ms. Janega’s comments on the contract.
Ms. Janega said that she is satisfied with the contract and it has been
thoroughly reviewed by other village attorneys over the years, contains a
waiver of claims, good notice and cure provisions, a termination provision and
a disclaimer that it is not a joint venture. It also states that the consultant
is an independent contractor, so there is no employment relationship.
Trustee Tucker moved to approve the GIS Service Provider Agreement with
Municipal GIS Partners in the amount of $45,875, seconded by Trustee Woodbury.
By roll call vote, the motion was passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
e)   Recycling Review. Mr. Saunders updated the Council on the new recycling
procedures. He explained that wheeled carts were delivered to residents over a
2-week period around Thanksgiving, and that provisions have been made for
obtaining more of the smaller carts to accommodate persons who are on a waiting
list for these. He said that there were about 2 dozen requests for assistance
with the carts and that a few people had chosen not to use the carts and
cancelled recycling, but overall the transition had gone smoothly. He said that
there had been some questions about excess material, and water and snow in the
carts. He urged that residents smash large containers down before putting them
into the bin to create more space or that they purchase an extra cart. In
response to a question from Trustee Abell, he said that residents could also
switch the size of their carts and that those would be delivered as well.
Trustee Woodbury asked for Mr. Saunders suggestions for the disposal of
seasonal material and if the cost of recycling was more than that of garbage
collection.
Mr. Saunders replied that recycling does cost more than garbage removal and
suggested that extra material be saved for the following week or cut up into
smaller sizes and placed in the cart. He added that depending on the route and
the driver, excess materials may still be picked up for now.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None
10. Reports.
None.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl appointed Joe Adams to the Zoning Board of Appeals to complete
the term of John Thomas, who has resigned to become a Park Board member.
Trustee Woodbury moved to approve the appointment of Mr. Adams to the Zoning
Board, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Eilers. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:07 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:29 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney Katherine
Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
The meeting adjourned at 8:29 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
March 1, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 15, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
1, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker and Ed
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Public Works
Steve Saunders and four persons in the audience, including Trustee Candidates
Ken Behles and Sandra Berger.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   March 8, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
President Duhl, indicated that they expected to attend. President Duhl
announced that this meeting would begin at 8:00 p.m. due to a schedule conflict
commitment on the Village Manager’s calendar.
b)   March 15, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except President Duhl, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 8, 2005, Study Session. Trustee Eilers suggested some minor
revisions to the text. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the minutes, as amended.
Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   February 15, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the
minutes, as drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
Mr. McKee reviewed the proposed changes to the rates and fees, noting that
these had been discussed during the Council’s budget meetings. He explained
that the Village adopts new fee resolutions each March to ensure their annual
review and to make it easy to reference the most current information. The
current fee adjustments have been made to generate the additional cash
necessary to pay for increased operating costs and to fund needed improvements
to the Village’s aging infrastructure. He said that the sum of these changes
will increase the cost to a typical homeowner by 2.5%, or about $114.
Trustee Abell raised a question about the power cost adjustment rider and
methodology. Mr. McKee commented the Village’s power rates have been below
those of Commonwealth Edison for several years. Manager Williams explained that
the Village has historically used this adjustment to determine rates based on a
market study performed by a rate consultant several years ago. He said that
there are many variables involved in the formula.
Trustee Eilers inquired as to why the sewer rates were going up 10% when the
electric and water were being raised considerably less than that. Mr. McKee
responded that there is a deficit in that fund due to changes in operations.
a)   Fee and Rate Resolutions. Mr. McKee reviewed the proposed changes to the
rates and fees, noting that these had been discussed during the Council’s
budget meetings. He explained that the Village adopts new fee resolutions each
March to ensure their annual review and to make it easy to reference the most
current information. The current fee adjustments have been made to generate the
additional cash necessary to pay for increased operating costs and to fund
needed improvements to the Village’s aging infrastructure. He said that the sum
of these changes will increase the cost to a typical homeowner by 2.5%, or
about $114.
Trustee Abell raised a question about the power cost adjustment rider and
methodology. Mr. McKee commented the Village’s power rates have been below
those of Commonwealth Edison for several years. Manager Williams explained that
the Village has historically used this adjustment to determine rates based on a
market study performed by a rate consultant several years ago. He said that
there are many variables involved in the formula.
Trustee Eilers inquired as to why the sewer rates were going up 10% when the
electric and water were being raised considerably less than that. Mr. McKee
responded that there is a deficit in that fund due to changes in operations.
   i)   Resolution No. R-7-2005 – Water Rates – Introduction. Trustee Greenough
moved to introduce Resolution No. R-07-2005, which would increase water rates
by 2%.. Trustee Woodbury seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-8-2005 – Electric Rates – Introduction. Trustee
Eilers moved to introduce Resolution No. R-08-2005, which would increase
electric rates by 2%. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion passed unanimously.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-9-2005 – Sewer Fees – Introduction. Trustee Ritchell
moved to introduce Resolution No. R-09-2005, which would increase sewer rates
by 10%. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
   iv)   Resolution No. R-10-2005 – Refuse Fees – Introduction. Trustee Abell
moved to introduce Resolution No. R-10-2005, which would not increase the
refuse fees. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
passed unanimously.
b)   Resolution No. R-14-2005 – MFT Authorization for Funding Bridge
Engineering – Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village is in the
process of replacing the superstructures on the Eldorado and Pine Street
bridges. Since the Phase I engineering report has received approval from the
Federal Highway Administration, the Village can proceed with Phase II design
engineering and construction documents. Ciorba Group has submitted a scope of
work and fee proposal to complete the engineering for an amount not to exceed
$128, 190.27 using Federal Bridge, funds at an 80% match, thereby making the
Village share $25,638.05. He said that this resolution would authorize the
expenditure of this amount from the MFT funds set aside for the engineering of
this project.
Trustee Greenough inquired about the condition of the underlying
infrastructure. Mr. Saunders replied that he expected that the substructure
would last for some time yet.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Eilers,
Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   2005-2006 Budget – Public Hearing. President Duhl declared the Public
Hearing open.
Mr. McKee summarized the 2005-2006 Budget, noting that expenditures are up by
approximately 3.5% due to higher wage, pension, and benefit costs while income
is expected to increase by 2.5%. He also reviewed some of the major capital
expenditures, including drainage, vehicles, equipment, and street and bridge
work. He also explained the Village’s pay-as-you-go policy. As he noted
previously, the typical homeowner would see an increase in their existing bill
for municipal services of approximately 2.5%, or approximately $114.00. He
concluded by saying that the proposed budget fulfills the mission of
maintaining Village services, rebuilding infrastructure, and controlling costs.
John Thomas, 525 Ash, briefly reviewed the history of funding for the Winnetka
Youth Organization. He said that while he supported a modest contribution for
this year, the issue is muddy at this time. He suggested that in order to get
past the issue, the Council ask the Caucus to readdress the matter in its
annual questionnaire next summer, with the hope that the responses will provide
definitive guidance.
There was general agreement among the Trustees that this was a good idea. Mr.
Eilers suggested that the Council work with the Caucus to phrase the question
so as to solicit clear responses.
President Duhl closed the Public Hearing, saying that the 2005-2006 Budget
would be considered for adoption at the March 15th Council meeting.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1403 and 1404. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, Trustee Tucker moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1403 in the amount of $1,000,558.65 and Warrant List
No. 1404 in the amount of $442,748.09. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Phase II Bridge Engineering Agreement. Mr. Saunders reviewed the proposed
Phase II Design Engineering Agreement with Ciorba Group for the Pine Street and
Eldorado Street bridges for a fee not to exceed $128,190.27, of which the
Village’s share would be $25,638.05 to be paid from MFT Funds. [See item 6(b)
above.] Mr. Saunders recommended approval of the proposed Agreement.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the
Agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Federal Aid Agreement for Federal Funding for Bridge Engineering. Mr.
Saunders reviewed the Local Agency Agreement with the Illinois Department of
Transportation required for Federal reimbursement of the Phase II design
expenditures mentioned above.
Trustee Woodbury asked whether there is any State funding involved. Mr.
Saunders replied that there is not.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the Village
President to sign the Local Authority Agreement authorizing the Federal funding
for the Pine Street and Eldorado Street bridges. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell, Greenough,
Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Tower Road Pump Purchase. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village has
programmed $100,000 in its current fiscal budget for installation of a
stormwater pump and forced discharge line to serve the new storm sewer on west
Tower Road and adjacent neighborhoods. As a first step, the Village must
purchase the pump, and several years ago it was decided to standardize on a
stormwater pump manufactured by SRS Crisfulli, Inc. This particular pump was
selected because they are manufactured with an additional gearbox that permits
them to be operated from our utility tractors in the event of a loss of power
and because they are extremely reliable and function well under adverse
conditions. The local authorized dealer for the required pump is Marshall-Bond
Pumps, Inc., at a price of $28,324.
Manager Williams commented that these pumps have been incredibly reliable and
have worked out extremely well for the Village. President Duhl confirmed these
remarks.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the purchase of
the pump from Marshall-Bond Pumps for $28,324. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell,
and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Duhl welcomed members of Boy Scout Troop 18 who were in the audience.
f)   Tree Planting Contract Extension. Mr. Saunders reviewed a request to
extend the contract for its Parkway Tree Planting program to St. Aubin Nursery,
with whom the Village currently has a contract providing for an extension. He
noted that the contractor has agreed in writing to hold to last year’s bid
price of $48,544 for the coming year.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the award of the
contract extension to St. Aubin. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
Baird Smart, 112 Church St., reiterated previous remarks about the appearance
of the old G & W Station on the southeast corner of Green Bay Rd. and Winnetka
Ave., expressing his belief that the Village needs to make improving this site
a priority.
Manager Williams explained that in order to keep Land Rover in the Village, a
lease had been negotiated with Land Rover to use the site for a car wash and
light maintenance. He said that the Village has looked into this matter several
times, but each time has determined that the necessary investment is not
warranted given that the Village anticipates taking over this property in the
future and turning it into a park. He added that the Village will be realigning
the design of that intersection which will result in some new geometrics and
curb work. He said that he had asked Mr. Saunders to contact Land Rover, the
current lessee, to work out a plan to spruce up the site. Mr. Saunders reported
that as yet there is no agreement on what to do or when.
Trustee Eilers suggested that during his conversations with Land Rover, Mr.
Saunders explore how critical this building is to their operation.
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. Mr. Duhl reported that educational forums on Home Rule
would be held each of the next five Saturday mornings in Village Hall at 10:00
a.m. and at 11:00 a.m. He encouraged interested residents to attend so that
they can make an informed decision.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl appointed Tom Kerwin to the Design Review Board to replace Ken
Behles. Trustee Greenough moved to approve the appointment of Mr. Kerwin to the
Design Review Board, seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Woodbury moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:24 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:32 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Absent: None. Also
present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 8:45 p.m.
