March 8, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: April 5, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
8, 2005 at 8:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Greenough called the meeting to order at 8:03 p.m. Present:
Trustees David Abell, Nan Greenough, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker
and Trustee Ed Woodbury. Absent: President Duhl. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Finance Ed McKee,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Bryan
McInturff, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Fire Chief Scott Smith and no persons
in the audience. Trustee Candidates Ken Behles and Sandra Berger were also
present.
2)   Fiscal 2006 Budget.
a)   Review of Capital Improvements Plan.
Manager Williams reviewed the 5-year capital improvements plan for the Council
and reported on the status of the facilities fund, motor fuel tax fund and the
general fund. Projects that he hopes to complete with the Council’s approval
within this timeframe are a parking deck for the Elm business district,
computer-aided dispatch for the police department, completion of drainage
projects and repairs to two of the bridges in the Village, improvements to
Village Hall and a training tower for the fire department.
Manager Williams and the Department Heads answered several questions from the
Trustees regarding these projects and the sources for funding them. Mr.
Williams also explained that staff endeavors to keep costs down by exploring
ways to cut operating expenses and share costs with other Villages for projects
such as the fire training tower and computer-aided police dispatch.
b)    Review of Electric Fund Operating Budget.
Manager Williams requested that the Council consider a 3% increase in electric
rates this year, in order to obtain a professional rate study in the next year
and begin to replenish cash reserves by 2010 when the Trimble County 2 power
plant debt service begins.
In response to the Trustee’s questions, Mr. Williams stated that the Village is
behind the curve in new customer fees compared to other communities and that
the Village needs to study the cost of power adjustment. He also explained that
the Glencoe electrical interconnect is being done over a 3-year period instead
of in one year as was planned originally. He added, however, that the lowered
purchase power cost budgeted will improve the electric fund cash-flow.
Mr. McInturff described the types of electric service the Village provides and
how he arrived at the requested increase in rates. He explained that part of
the proposal is that homeowners would assume some of the cost for transformers,
just as customers of other municipal electric utilities.
Manager Williams said that in comparison to the cost structures of Naperville,
St. Charles, Geneva and ComEd, the Village is similar to the municipal electric
utilities and our rates are approximately 5% higher than Com Ed, but once the
cash reserves are built up, it could mean fewer rate increases for residents.
Trustee Greenough added that the slightly higher rates are made up in
responsiveness and reliability.
c)   Other Budget Issues.
Manager Williams reported that the water fund will be monitored carefully so
that the interconnect with Wilmette can be completed by 2009 as agreed in the
Village’s wholesale water contract with Northfield. He explained that the
interconnect will provide water to residents should anything happen to
Winnetka’s water plant.
After further discussion, Mr. Williams determined that the consensus of the
Council was to go forward with the capital improvements budget and the proposed
fees for electric service.
3)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:17 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
March 15, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 5, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
15, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker and Ed
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water &
Electric Bryan McInturff and nine persons in the audience, including Trustee
Candidates Ken Behles and Sandra Berger.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   April 5, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present, except
President Duhl and Trustee Ritchell, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   April 12, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
President Duhl, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 1, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution No. R-15-2005 – Affordable Housing Plan – Public Hearing &
Adoption. Noting that a public hearing is not required by law for adoption of
this resolution, President Duhl declared the Public Hearing open. He asked
Attorney Janega to briefly review the components of the Village’s draft plan.
Ms. Janega explained that the proposed plan is a refinement of the preliminary
draft discussed in February. She indicated that rather than identify particular
structures within the Village, this Plan identified the multi-family and
commercial zoning districts as most suitable for affordable housing and sets a
goal of 15% of all new development or redevelopment to be affordable. This
would mean, for example, that if there is a development of 100 units, the goal
would be to have 15 of them meet affordability standards. Attorney Janega
pointed out that, as the Illinois Housing Development Authority interprets the
definitions of new development and redevelopment, the replacement of a single-
family home with another single-family home is not considered redevelopment and
is, therefore, not covered by the Act. She added that there is nothing in the
Village’s Plan authorizing the Village to compel a property owner to build
affordable housing and that compliance is voluntary. Toward this end, the
Village needs to identify and implement some incentives to encourage builders
to develop affording housing. She noted that the Village has long identified
and articulated the need for moderately priced housing that is affordable for
seniors and young families. Finally, Ms. Janega finished by saying that some
language and typographical errors made in the original draft had been corrected
and that the first two paragraphs were redundant and would be revised into a
consolidated, introductory paragraph.
President Duhl called for comments from the audience.
Betsy Landis, 848 Tower Rd., Katie Seigenthaler, 471 Hawthorne, and Joe Dooley,
849 Sheridan Rd., spoke in support of the Plan and complimented the Council on
its efforts to address the affordable housing issue. They expressed their hope
that should the Village vote to become Home Rule on April 5th that it would
still continue to pursue this goal with the objective of developing housing for
a diversity of people.
Bernie Hammer, 1455 Tower Rd., raised several questions about the powers of the
proposed State super zoning board that is to be established. President Duhl
said that those are not clear at this time. He said that a developer who took
an appeal to this board would have to be able to demonstrate that he was denied
approval by the Village because of plans to build affordable units. Mr. Duhl
added that it is important that the Village do what it can to maintain the
integrity of its Zoning Ordinance and that it enforce its ordinance uniformly.
He voiced his opinion that it is important the Village make every effort to
comply with its Plan, but said that there are circumstances outside of its
control that determine whether affordable housing is actually built.
President Duhl then closed the Public Hearing and asked for comments from the
Council.
Trustee Eilers said that he believes the proposed Plan is consistent with the
values and objectives that Winnetka holds dear and should be passed. Trustees
Ritchell and Tucker concurred.
Stating that he, too, was in favor of the Plan, Trustee Abell questioned
whether Section 3 of the Resolution should be deleted, because it would require
the Plan to be filed with the Illinois Housing and Development Authority before
the 60-day filing deadline. He expressed concern that the Plan would be filed
before the referendum on Home Rule, noting that the Plan will be refined over
time and he wasn’t comfortable binding the next Council before it has had a
chance to review and polish it.
President Duhl commented that the statute requires that the Plan be adopted by
April 1st and filed within 60 days, and whatever this Council does cannot be
binding on the next, or any future, Councils. So, if the next Council should
decide to change it, they can. He expressed concern about not meeting the
statutory obligation to adopt the Plan by the designated deadline. He
recommended that the Council adopt the Plan as drafted but recognize that there
is a period of time before it must to be filed.
Attorney Janega also recommended that the Council adopt and file the Plan
regardless of what happens with the Home Rule referendum, saying that nothing
about filing this Plan prohibits the Village from amending it at a future date
if it chooses to do so. She expressed concern that if the Village does not file
its Plan as required by the Act and the Home Rule referendum fails, or the
State Legislature decides to amend the Statute to preempt Home Rule powers,
then there would be gap showing that the Village had not fully complied with
the Act. This could weaken the Village’s position down the line.
Trustee Eilers suggested that the Resolution be amended to include a specific
date in the future by which the Plan be filed with IHDA.
Trustee Abell said that would address his concerns. He then moved to amend
Section 3 of the proposed Resolution to say that the Plan should be filed
within the prescribed 60-day time frame, but that the amendment is being
proposed with the understanding that the Plan would not be filed until the new
Council has had a chance to review it. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adopt the Resolution,
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustee
Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Trustee Woodbury commented that if the Trustees vote in favor of this
Resolution and the accompanying Plan, then they have to support it and make it
clear that it is the Village’s intent to implement affordable housing in one
form or another.
Trustee Greenough added that while there is a filing requirement, the process
doesn’t go into effect for years, so there is plenty of time for the Village to
evaluate its situation and determine what is realistic for Winnetka and then to
amend the Plan accordingly.
Trustee Eilers clarified that while the authority of the State Appeals Board to
override a local zoning ordinance does not go into effect for years, compliance
with the 15% threshold of new development being affordable housing, commences
with adoption of this ordinance.
b)   Ordinance M-03-2005 – Zoning Variation: 211 Birch St. - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front and Corner Yard Setback variations to
permit the construction of one and two-story additions and a chimney at the
subject property. In doing so, he noted that all of the proposed improvements
are outside of the floodplain and pointed out that although GFA is being added,
the house will still be under the maximum allowable. He reported that the
Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to recommend approval of the variations.
Trustee Greenough commented that the location of the lot in the floodplain and
the house’s location on the lot are two hardships that truly run with the land
and, therefore, constitute legitimate grounds for granting the variations. She
added that she was delighted to see this house dealt with so sensitively. She
then moved to introduce the ordinance. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Resolution No. R-06-2005 – 2005-2006 Budget – Adoption. President Duhl
asked Mr. McKee if he had any changes to report. Mr. McKee responded that all
of the changes previously discussed had been incorporated into the materials
for this evening.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-06-
2005 adopting the Budget for the Fiscal Year 2005-2006. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker,
Woodbury, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Fee and Rate Resolutions. Mr. McKee noted that the next six resolutions
would set the rates for water, sewer, electric, and refuse collection, as well
as the fees for general permits, licenses, building permits, and zoning
activities in accordance with the Council’s discussions during their review of
the preliminary budget. He noted that the proposed electric rates reflect a 3%
increase instead of a 2% increase, but that this actually represents a savings
for customers because there have been changes to the power cost adjustment
formula as originally discussed.
   i)   Resolution No. R-7-2005 – Water Rates – Adoption. Trustee Eilers moved
to adopt Resolution No. R-07-2005, which would increase water rates by 2%.
Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell, and
Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-8-2005 – Electric Rates – Adoption. Trustee Woodbury,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend Resolution No. R-8-2005 as explained
by Mr. McKee in his introductory comments. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt Resolution No. R-08-2005, which would increase
electric rates by 3%. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury,
Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-9-2005 – Sewer Fees – Adoption. Trustee Ritchell
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-09-2005, which would increase sewer rates by
10%. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   iv)   Resolution No. R-10-2005 – Refuse Fees – Adoption. Trustee Eilers
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-10-2005, which would not increase the refuse
fees. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Abell, Ritchell, and
Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   v)   Resolution No. R-11-2005 – General Permit & License Fees – Adoption.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt the Village’s
annual general permit and license fees with no changes in these fees. By roll
call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   vi)   Resolution No. R-12-2005 – Building & Zoning Fees – Adoption. Trustee
Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the Village’s annual
building and zoning fees for construction activity with no changes in these
fees. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker,
Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1405 and 1406. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, Trustee Tucker moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1405 in the amount of $705,313.02 and Warrant List No.
1406 in the amount of $147,114.13. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid No. 05-001: Sewer Lining Bids. Mr. Williams reviewed the bids to
provide trenchless relining to the existing sanitary sewers located through the
Village. He explained that this is part of the Village’s ongoing effort to
reduce infiltration and inflow into the sanitary sewers. Manager Williams
reported that staff recommended the award of the bid to Visu-Sewer Clean &
Seal, Inc., in the total amount of $102,056.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees,
Eilers, Greenough Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Parkway Tree Trimming, Removal and Maintenance. Mr. Williams reviewed the
bids for parkway tree trimming, removal, and maintenance. He reported that the
Village Forester was in agreement with staff’s recommendation that the bids be
awarded as follows:
 
Parkway Tree & Stump  Nels Johnson Tree Experts, $55,900.00
Removal               Inc.
Parkway Tree Trimming Kinnucan                   $16,362.50
Emergency (T&M)       Arbor Green, Inc.          Hourly Rates per proposal
Maintenance
 
In response to a question from Trustee Eilers, Mr. Williams indicated that the
time and material are monitored by Mr. Stier, the Forester.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Ritchell, Woodbury, Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Electrical Line Clearance Tree Trimming. Mr. McInturff reviewed bids from
Nels Johnson and Arbor Green to do the parkway tree trimming around the
electrical lines. He recommended that the bid be awarded to Nels Johnson Tree
Experts with the total cost not to exceed $59,000.
Mr. Woodbury asked whether there wasn’t some overlap between this job and the
tree trimming included in the above bid. Mr. McInturff explained that it takes
different skills and equipment to do this type of tree trimming; hence the
different bids.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
e)   Filter Plant Rewiring. Mr. McInturff reported that the work to upgrade the
electrical system and replace most of the wiring at the Water Plant had been
put out to bid earlier this year. Both bids on the project, however, came in
substantially over the budgeted $75,000. Consequently, the scope of work was
reduced so that the work could be done in two separate projects. The subject
bid would cover the upgrade of the dry type transformers to the electrical
panels and the addition of a couple of outlets. He recommended that this part
of the total project be awarded to the low bidder, Kordick Electric, in the
amount of $29,602.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the award of the
contract for this portion of the necessary rewiring of the Water Plant as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Ritchell, Abell, Greenough, Tucker, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
f)   Annual Outdoor Seating Permits and Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License
Riders. Mr. Williams noted that ten local restaurants had applied for Outdoor
Seating Permits and that three had also asked for Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor
License Riders. He reported that the Police Department has not been advised of
any problems with the latter and recommended that all requests be approved.
Trustee Greenough added that she believes the outside restaurants add a great
deal of ambiance to the Village and moved to approve the ten Outdoor Seating
Permits. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the Sidewalk
Restaurant Liquor License Riders for Corner Cooks, Song O’Sixpence, and Little
Ricky’s. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. Mr. Duhl reported that educational forums on Home Rule
would be held on the next three Saturday mornings in Village Hall at 10:00 a.m.
and 11:00 a.m. He encouraged interested residents to attend so that they can
make an informed decision on the April 5th ballot.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the Chamber’s Annual Recognition
Luncheon will be held on April 13th, and the Sidewalk Sale has been scheduled
for the third week in July. He also mentioned that the Chamber Executive Board
has endorsed Home Rule in a letter to the Pioneer Press.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl appointed John O’Malley to replace Sandra Berger on the Police
Pension Board. Trustee Eilers moved to approve the appointment, seconded by
Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Duhl then appointed Trustee Greenough to serve on the Canvassing
Board for the upcoming election. Trustee Tucker moved to approve the
appointment, seconded by Trustee Eilers. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Abell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:44 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:50 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker, Greenough and Woodbury. Absent:
None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:51 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
April 5, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 19, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
5, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Trustees Herb
Ritchell and Ed Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio
and 5 persons in the audience, including Trustee Candidates Ken Behles and
Sandra Berger.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   April 12, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
President Duhl, indicated that they expected to attend. Trustee Tucker said she
would be late to the meeting.
b)   April 19, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 8, 2005, Study Session. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the minutes as
drafted. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   March 15, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers noted a correction to the
minutes and Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the minutes as amended. Trustee
Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-03-2005 – Zoning Variation: 211 Birch - Adoption.
President Duhl announced that this ordinance was introduced at the Council’s
March 15th meeting and would grant variations from the requirements of the
Zoning Ordinance for front and corner yard setbacks to permit the construction
of one and two-story additions and a chimney at the subject property.
Ms. Janega explained that the ordinance, as introduced, was in preliminary form
and that it has been amended since introduction to expand on the recitals and
provide greater detail about the nature of the variations. She noted that
language was also added to reflect Trustee Greenough's comments about the
reduced buildable area because of the property's location in the floodplain.
President Duhl asked for further comments and there were none.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to amend the ordinance as
recommended and by voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Eilers moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-03-
2005 as amended, granting front and corner yard setback variations for the
property at 211 Birch. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Ritchell, Woodbury
Trustee Woodbury arrived at 7:38 p.m.
b)   Ordinance M-04-2005 – Zoning Variation: 879 Cherry. - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed the request for variations from the Zoning Ordinance
provisions for garages and side yard setbacks to construct a detached garage on
the subject property. He explained that the garage would be built between the
residence and the coach house located at the rear of the property and that,
with the exception of the side yard setback, the proposed garage conforms to
all other zoning requirements. He added that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 6-0 in favor of recommending the variation at its March meeting.
In response to questions from Trustee Woodbury and President Duhl, Mr.
D’Onofrio replied that the applicant has reduced the amount of variation
requested in his original application and there have been no objections
received from the neighbors.
President Duhl clarified that if the garage were being built in the rear
quarter of the lot, it would conform to all zoning requirements. He commented
that violating side yard setback provisions is not desirable and that there is
a conforming location for the garage. He also observed that the ordinance
doesn't require the owners to remove the storage shed, as the homeowners had
indicated would, and that they may choose to keep it at the rear of the
property which may be objectionable to neighbors in the future.
Trustee Woodbury said he was concerned about this as well, because there are a
lot of buildings on a fairly small lot, but he noted the figures in the zoning
matrix are within the requirements and it appears that the homeowners went to
extraordinary measures to reduce the amount of the variation.
Trustee Tucker asked if the ordinance could require the homeowner to tear down
the shed and Ms. Janega replied that she would add this provision to the
ordinance.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-04-2005. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Resolution No. R-13-2005 – Fire and Emergency Services Fees – Adoption.
Manager Williams reported that the annual fees for providing fire and emergency
services to residents in Kenilworth and unincorporated New Trier Township have
been calculated to be $749.47, an increase of $72.50 over last year. He
explained that the fee is based upon population, assessed valuation and the
number of fire and ambulance calls. He added that the huge increases in pension
benefits that were mandated by the State legislature were also taken into
consideration when calculating the fees.
In response to a question from Trustee Eilers, Manager Williams explained that
fire prevention services are not provided to residents in this service area and
the amounts set aside in the budget for this are back out of the calculations
for the service fees. He added that the majority of the increase is
attributable to pension benefits and assessed valuation.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adopt Resolution R-13-2005
establishing fire and emergency services fees. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
d)   Resolution No. R-16-2005 – Establishing Department Head Salaries -
Adoption. President Duhl asked for comments on the resolution establishing
salaries for Department Heads and there were none.
Trustee Greenough moved to adopt Resolution No. R-16-2005, establishing
department head salaries. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Eilers, Tucker,
Woodbury, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
e)   Resolution No. R-17-2005 – Establishing Village Manager’s Salary –
Adoption. President Duhl asked for comments on the resolution establishing the
Village Manager’s salary and there were none.
Trustee Eilers moved to adopt Resolution No. R-17-2005, establishing the
Village Manager’s salary. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greenough, Eilers, Tucker,
Woodbury, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
7. New Business.
None.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1407 and 1408. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, Trustee Tucker moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1407 in the amount of $1,401,384.96 and Warrant List
No. 1408 in the amount of $443,112.93 Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
b)   Compost Contract. Manager Williams explained that in 2000 there were
operational problems and a shortage of land to do composting at the Public
Works yards so, after a competitive bidding process, the Council in 2001
approved a contract with Thelen Sand and Gravel to haul yard waste. He reported
that Thelen Sand and Gravel has performed the contract very well and asked that
the Council approve a one-year extension of the contract. He said that staff
would go out for competitive bid proposals next year, but Thelen has done an
excellent job. He said that the cost for this year would be $6.39 per cubic
yard and is in line with the CPI.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to approve the one-year
extension of the contract with Thelen Sand and Gravel as recommended. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees, Eilers, Greenough,
Tucker, Woodbury, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
c)   Quarterly Capital Improvements Reports. Manager Williams commented that
the report on quarterly capital improvements indicates that it has been a good
year and that there are aggressive capital projects lined up for next year. He
said that the landscaping and beautification work at 93 Green Bay Road is
underway and the Village is working to remove the unsightly building on the
property that has residents concerned.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. Reports.
a)   Village President. President Duhl reported that the votes received in the
home rule referendum as of 8:00 p.m. indicate that it is likely the referendum
will pass.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported on the joint meeting of the Plan
Commission and the Design Review Board next month.
c)   Village Attorney. Ms. Janega reported that the Village has been fully
reimbursed by the Republican National Committee for the expenses incurred
during the two fundraising events held in Winnetka during the 2004 presidential
campaign. She said that the Finance Department will be reimbursing other
participating communities after reimbursement amounts are verified.
Trustee Eilers expressed his appreciation to Ms. Janega, Mr. Williams and Mr.
McKee for the successful negotiation of this issue and for avoiding litigation.
10. Appointments.
None.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Ritchell. The Council convened into Executive Session at 8:01 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:41 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Tucker, Greenough and Woodbury. Absent: Trustee
Ritchell. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Woodbury, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The
meeting adjourned at 8:41 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
April 12, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 3, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
12, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Greenough called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees David Abell, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Ed Woodbury. Absent:
President Duhl. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus and
approximately 5 persons in the audience. Trustee-Elect Ken Behles was also
present. Chief of Police DeLopez arrived at 7:42 pm, Trustee-elect Sandra
Berger arrived at 7:43 p.m., Trustee Tucker arrived at 8:17 p.m.
2)   Discussion: Winnetka Caucus Chair Gene Graeble.
Mr. Graeble introduced members of the Caucus committees and expressed his hope
for an active year for the Caucus. He mentioned that the town meeting will be
held in June where Trustee Woodbury will speak on home rule and Julian
D’Esposito will speak about the post office committee.
Joni Johnson, the Chair of the Caucus Committee, said that the committee will
be speaking with the Council, Village officials and former trustees to develop
questions for the Caucus questionnaire before the Caucus meets in the fall. She
said she was already aware of one issue relating to the funding of community
organizations and asked Trustee Woodbury and Mr. Williams to develop a question
that will provide a clear answer on this issue.
Manager Williams commented that the community conversations over the last 6-
months on home rule had shown that this is a community of grass roots democracy
and the Caucus is an important part of that. He informed the Council that he
has learned the Caucus stores its materials in several cardboard boxes that are
passed each year from the outgoing chairperson to the new chairperson. Mr.
Williams offered to have his staff assist the Caucus with organizing the
material for them and work with the library to put it on the Caucus website.
Mr. Graeble agreed that the website needs updating so that it can be used to
educate the community on the functions of the Caucus. He said that the Caucus
has been in existence for 90 years and his committees hope to renew and
energize it this year. He added that the Caucus is supported solely by
donations and he would like to make the community more aware of the Caucus’
financial position because he believes they will help once they understand the
operating expenses. In answer to a question from Trustee-elect Berger, he said
that the questionnaire alone accounts for 30% of their budget or about $4,000
and they currently have $2,000 in their account. He further said that the
Caucus represents all residents of voting age and the questionnaire is key to
knowing what the community thinks about issues. In reply to a question from
Trustee Eilers, Mr. Graeble said that every eligible voter can fill out the
questionnaire and if a household needs additional questionnaires, they can be
obtained from the Caucus. He added that a common misconception is that the
Caucus is a closed committee, but it is made up of volunteers and any input
would be helpful.
Pat Horne, Vice Chair of the Caucus, said that generally the people who answer
the questionnaire are over 50. She said that another of the committee's goals
is to educate the younger residents on the importance of answering the
questionnaire, so that they are represented in the community.
Trustee Greenough commented that the Caucus provides a tremendous benefit to
the community and the goals of this year’s Caucus are very worthy, particularly
updating the website, which could help with the historical aspect of it. She
suggested that taking advantage of the school functions, the PTA and school
newsletters would also help get information to younger residents.
Trustee Abell commented that there are a lot of volunteer hours put in by the
Caucus and Trustee Ritchell said that he would support the Caucus in any way he
could.
3)   Discussion: Irwin Lyons, Auditor.
Irwin Lyons, Village auditor, spoke to the Council about the auditing process
and asked that the Trustees contact him either directly or through the Village
staff if there were any concerns before auditing begins. He explained that the
audit procedures will begin in June and include speaking to members of the
Council, as well as members of the staff, to determine if there are any
problems that might have an affect on the financial output. Mr. Lyons also said
that there was nothing from last year’s report that needs to be addressed this
year, but that they would be planning for the future in areas such as benefits.
He added that a large part of the time needed to complete the audit is in
getting the report together and meeting the requirements for the government
certificate in excellence.
Trustee Tucker arrived at 8:17 p.m.
In response to a question from Manager Williams, Mr. Lyons said that now that
Winnetka is a home rule community, there would be no change from an auditing
standpoint, except for the debt limitation disclosure and there is a box to
check on the annual report to the State financial office.
The Council briefly discussed the renewal of the auditor’s contract this fall,
Mr. Lyons’ eight years as auditor for the Village, his firm’s reputation as one
of the best auditors for public government and if there would be a benefit to
the Village if auditors were changed periodically.
Trustee Ritchell inquired as to whether Mr. Lyons keeps the Council apprised of
any issues that come up which need their attention.
Mr. Lyons replied that they do let the Village know about changes in standards
and other issues, such as post-employment benefits. Mr. Lyons added that a
study had been done about switching auditors periodically and it was found that
it was not necessarily beneficial because of the start up costs involved.
Trustee Tucker said she assumed that Mr. Lyons’ prices are competitive and
mentioned that she thought that when clients are in a long-time relationship
there would be some sort of discount.
Mr. Lyons replied that they do consider that and again, asked the Council to
contact him if they have any concerns.
4)   Cook County Board – Homeland Security Initiative.
Chief DeLopez introduced Daniel Coughlin who spoke to the Council about
Winnetka’s participation in a suburban communications system funded by Cook
County to create the ability for municipalities to communicate with each other
during times of disaster.
Village Attorney Janega informed the Council of some issues regarding insurance
and liability that still would need to be resolved before agreeing to the
contract terms.
Chief DeLopez said that with this contract, Winnetka would be one of 34 law
enforcement agencies to receive funding for communications that would enable
information to be transmitted over a wireless network throughout suburban Cook
County and includes the installation of two in-car cameras. He said that the
intent of this communications system is to share audio and video in the event
of a major terrorist activity or other disaster, so that resources can be
deployed and information can be provided to the units responding to the scene.
He added that the in-car cameras would have multiple and day to day uses as
well, would be installed and paid for by the county with a minimal cost to the
Village in the way of cellular service to each car or approximately $100 per
car. He added that as funding becomes available, all cars would have the county
communications ability, but this contract provides for the replacement of the
current system in two cars.
Mr. Coughlin addressed the Council about the technical aspects of the contract
and said that Homeland Security had asked that Chicago and the surrounding area
develop and implement plans that would prepare for a large regional response to
major types of disasters. He said that in 2003, the County conducted an
exercise to determine where the weak links were in emergency responses and they
found that communications was key. He said that this would be the first step to
link everyone to communications standards and improve on radio and video
communications for all 128 communities in suburban Cook County. He said the
Cook County system is state of the art, is based on Homeland Security
requirements, has the capacity to be very durable and is designed for triple
fall back, so that if one communications tower does not respond, the system
could rely on another cellular provider or satellite. He expected that if
agreements can be done in time, all 128 communities will be connected by
Christmas 2005. Mr. Coughlin stated that he had provided this information for
the Council’s consideration, but would refer legal questions to the State
Attorneys’ office.
Trustee Tucker asked if there was special training involved and if it will
affect all officers.
Mr. Coughlin replied that this is a local decision and funds are set aside to
provide for training and overtime.
Trustee Eilers asked if there was a reason Winnetka was chose as one of the
original 34 communities.
Mr. Coughlin answered that it was not because of security risk, but because
they felt Winnetka was a progressive community and they were looking for quick
involvement to get the ball rolling. He added that Wilmette is voting on their
contract tonight and speaks very highly of all of the officials in Winnetka and
holds the community in high regard. He said that they have contracted with
Wheeling as well as Maywood, Elmwood Park and a number of communities in
southern Cook County and are meeting with Niles very soon. He said every
community has their own uniqueness, and one of the things they consider is what
the impact to surrounding areas would be, if a community could not function. He
gave the example that Winnetka provides water to more than one community, so
that if something happened here, it would have impact more than one community.
Chief DeLopez said that the communications system in the two vehicles not only
will improve communications at the scene of emergencies, but could also be
prototypes for daily operations to see how officers are using proper protocol,
etc., which would be of benefit to the community. .
Manager Williams said that he thinks that Chief DeLopez and he would recommend
that the Village move forward on this after the Village Attorney resolves
outstanding contract issues with Cook County.
5)   Home Rule Follow Up.
Village Attorney Janega reviewed the post-election procedures that are
necessary due to the passage of the home rule referendum. She also explained
that, because all acts of a home rule unit are considered an exercise of its
home rule authority, it is advisable to re-adopt the Village Code after the
election results are certified so that it is clear that the Village Code is
considered an exercise of home rule powers. She added that all future
ordinances and resolutions will contain home rule recitals.
Ms. Janega said that some options for changes in policy under home rule are
zoning procedures and regulating demolition contractors, and that she and
Community Development Director Mike D'Onofrio had already begun to collect
information for proposed Code amendments on those subjects. She added that, as
part of the continuing fiscal responsibility that was noted in the Village's
informational materials, she had contacted the Village's bond counsel to make
sure that the Village will have all necessary home rule documentation for
future bond refinancings.
Trustee Greenough asked about regulating excavation contractors as well as
demolition contractors.
Ms. Janega replied that demolition contractors were intended as a starting
point, but excavation contractors certainly could be included.
Trustee Tucker asked if the Comprehensive Plan would have to be re-adopted.
Ms. Janega said that this could be done in conjunction with the Affordable
Housing Plan. She explained that when the Affordable Housing Plan was adopted,
there was discussion on how to proceed if the home rule referendum passed. She
said she had gone on record that the Affordable Housing Act does not apply to
home rule communities, so the dilemma is what to do with a Plan that was
adopted in March, before home rule, and must be filed within 60 days of
adoption, after Winnetka has become home rule. She said that she would begin
working on an amended Plan and ordinance that contains recitals that state that
Winnetka is home rule and not under the jurisdiction of the IHDA. She said that
the amended Plan could be customized for the Village and felt that the most
logical thing would be to include a directive to the Plan Commission to come up
with recommendations. She then said that the Plan adopted in March could be
filed by May 14th, along with the amended plan, so there would be no questions
about a gap in compliance. In response to a question from Trustee-elect Behles,
Ms. Janega said that after the Plan Commission deliberates on affordable
housing, the Council would simply amend the Plan again.
The Trustees discussed the timing of filing the original Plan and the amended
plan and agreed that it would be best to file both plans at the same time, so
that there would be no confusion over which Plan was in effect on what date.
Trustee Greenough commented that it is her personal feeling that we can do a
better job on affordable housing as a home rule community because we can
consider Winnetka’s economics, not what the State has required. She said it
will take time, but it has always been the intent of the Council to address
affordable housing, and better progress can be made as home rule.
6)   Kiosk Update
Brian Norkus, Assistant Community Development Director, updated the Council on
the construction of a kiosk to replace the current one. He said that the
committees that worked on this project learned that the original estimate of
$16,000 for construction was considerably less than the estimates for the
actual cost of construction which ranged from $27,000 - $58,000. He said that
of the four options considered, the committee selected the least expensive one,
but it is still more than the $25,000 allocated for the project in the 2006
budget. He asked for the Council’s direction on whether to move forward with
this design.
In response to a question from Trustee Tucker, Mr. Norkus said that the pieces
are from stock, not custom made, and one of the reasons that he brought the
plans was to show the Council that there is also not much landscaping that
might add to the cost. He said the most likely option in refining the plans, is
to see if there is a stock fluted column that would be less expensive. He said
that the committee wanted to stay within design guidelines for illumination in
the business districts and he didn’t know how much the lighting contributed to
the cost.
Trustee Greenough added that this plan was the best in design and quality and
was more substantial.
Trustee Ritchell said that he would be in favor of making a plainer design that
wouldn’t cost as much.
Trustee Eilers commented that the Chamber of Commerce had pledged to pay for
one third of the cost up to $10,000.
Trustee Woodbury added that during the bidding process, the Village would be
able to look at the lighting, design, and construction and see if costs could
be reduced.
Trustee Abell asked Mr. Norkus if he thought this was the best option available
to us and Mr. Norkus replied that this design is a significant improvement over
the current kiosk and would allow more than one group to use the display cases.
He added that the less expensive materials required more maintenance.
Mr. Behles commented that if you are going to use different materials to reduce
the cost, you need to assess whether to use permanent materials or not, because
this design is of cast aluminum which should be taken into consideration.
Trustee Greenough said that the kiosks of this kind in Evanston were installed
during the depression and are still in use. She said this was an economic
decision and is not maintenance free, but will require the least amount of
maintenance.
In response to a question from Trustee-elect Berger about the difference in the
first estimate of $16,000 and the $27,000 estimate we now have, Mr. Norkus said
that the first estimate was very rough and based on a conceptual plan, not
specifics.
Manager Williams assured the Council that Mr. Norkus would proceed with bids
that include options for reducing costs where possible.
7)   Cost of Service Study.
Bryan McInturff, Director of Water and Electric, told the Council that, as a
result of the discussions at budget meetings this year, request for proposals
had gone out for a cost of service study. He said the study will help the
Village determine how to meet its financial goals in the future and correct a
problem with the power cost adjustment without a drastic change. He said he had
received responses to the RFP from four firms, all of whom could do a cost of
service study and develop rates for us: He reported that three of the firms
were in the mid $20,000 range and the fourth, by Burns McDonald, was $44,000.
He said that of the four firms who submitted bids, EES worked for the Village
in 1995, did an excellent job working with the staff and the Council and is his
choice to do the cost service study.
Manager Williams said that state law does not require us to take bids for
professional services and allows us to consider the qualifications of firms and
negotiate prices. He said that the process the Village uses is to have two
envelopes, one containing the qualifications of the firms, which are considered
first, and the other containing the costs. He said the first envelope is opened
before the second so that the prices don’t influence the decision which means
that it is not necessarily the lowest bidder who is selected. He added that he
has had long experience with EES and they are one of the best.
Attorney Janega said that this process is consistent with the statutory
contracting process where there is a prior relationship. Because of the
established relationship with EES, the Village is not required to go out for
bid.
Trustee Greenough added that there is also the economy of dealing with someone
who already knows you and understands community issues.
Trustee Eilers said that he is in favor of listening to staff’s recommendations
in this instance and Trustee Tucker agreed.
Manager Williams commented that this is one of the most important decisions the
Village will make for the future of electric prices.
8)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
9)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:25 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
April 19, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 3, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
19, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker, and Ed
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director
of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and 25 persons in the
audience, including Trustee Candidates Ken Behles and Sandra Berger.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 9(h), a bid for Village vehicles, had been
added to the Agenda. The Council approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum.
a)   May 3, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   May 10, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 5, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greenough moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Landmark Preservation Awards
Robert Zielinski, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission, presented
awards to this year’s recipients. He commented that each of the projects
exhibited careful thought with an eye toward achieving consistency with the
architectural character of the community. The winners were as follows:
For Rehabilitation:
333 Sunset – Ed & Catherine King
128 Church Street – Tom & Monica Dutmers
830 Sheridan Rd. – Judy & Randy Larrimore
595 Orchard – Betsy & Thomas Jones
955 Old Green Bay – Edward & Barbara Rouse
1179 Asbury – Carl & Laurie Warchavsky
586 Maple – Dora & John Aalbregtse
426 Maple – Nancy & Ken Behles
950 Hill Road – Teri & Frederick Lowinger
535 Cherry – Lisa & John Benedetto
Fegenheimer Building
For New Construction:
540 Hibbard Road – Winnetka Park District
619 Sheridan Rd. – Mr. & Mrs. Tom Stanley
491 Hill Road – Liz & Joe Nesler
President Duhl congratulated all of the winners saying that their efforts make
the Village a more desirable place to live for all residents.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-04-2005 – Zoning Variation: 879 Cherry. - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a proposed amendment providing for the removal of a shed on
the property in keeping with the Council’s discussion at the time of
introduction. 
Trustee Eilers asked whether this was always the intent of the petitioner. Mr.
D’Onofrio replied that it was.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the ordinance as
discussed. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
04-2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greenough,
Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Duhl welcomed members of Boy Scout Troop 20 to the audience. They
informed the Council that they were working on badges in citizenship and
community.
b)   Resolution No. R-18-2005 – MFT Funding for Supplement to Engineering for
Traffic Signal Modernization at Green Bay Road and Winnetka Avenue – Adoption.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village has been planning for the modernization
of this traffic signal for some time and last June authorized an agreement with
Clark Dietz, Inc., to provide engineering services for its design for a fee not
to exceed $27,408.67. Since that time, however, discussions with the Illinois
DOT have resulted in the scope of the project going beyond that which was
originally contemplated. Therefore, the project’s cost will exceed the amount
originally authorized, requiring the additional expenditure of $18,000 of motor
fuel tax funds.
Trustee Woodbury asked about the changes requested by IDOT. Mr. Saunders
responded that they include a reworking of the intersection and possible right-
of-way transfer at the southeast corner of the intersection, as well as several
geometric changes to improve capacity and safety without significant right-of-
way acquisition.
Trustee Greenough remarked that she would like to see something done about the
vast expanse of distance needed to cross the street and asked about installing
islands. Mr. Saunders replied that there is not enough room to put in a center
island. He said that they are looking at ways to improve safety but still have
enough room for the necessary traffic flow without taking too much of the
right-of-way adjacent to the old G & W garage.
Ms. Greenough also inquired whether landscape improvements were a part of the
plan. Mr. Saunders said that they were.
Trustee Abell asked whether the Village’s lease with Land Rover permitted the
Village to take some of the right-of-way. Mr. Saunders indicated that it did.
Trustee Tucker asked whether this action would cover all of the options and
when the project would be underway. Mr. Saunders said that he believed that
this would cover the scope of getting IDOT’s permit approval. He indicated that
he expected approval by fall of this year, with construction beginning in the
winter/spring of ’06.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
18-2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell,
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1409 and 1410. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, Trustee Tucker moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1409 in the amount of $870,546.00 and Warrant List No.
1410 in the amount of $243,476.99. Trustee Greenough seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Tucker, Ritchell, and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Supplemental Engineering Agreement – Winnetka & Green Bay Traffic Signal
Modernization. Mr. Saunders explained that this is the agreement provided for
in Resolution R-18-2005 above. The total project cost to get the Village to
IDOT design approval amounts to approximately $45,000, considering the residual
balance from the prior contract.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to cancel the balance of the
June 1, 2004 agreement between the Village and Clark-Dietz, Inc. for
engineering services associated with the preliminary design of traffic signal
improvements at Winnetka Avenue and Green Bay Road and authorizing a contract
supplement to the June 1, 2004 agreement for these improvements for an amount
not to exceed $30,931.80. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Tucker, Ritchell, and Woodbury. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
c)   One-year Review: M-15-2004, Paddle Hut SUP, 490 Hibbard Road. Mr.
D’Onofrio explained that one of the conditions of Ordinance M-15-2004 granting
a Special Use Permit to allow for the construction of the paddle tennis
building was to re-evaluate the parking situation after one year in order to
determine its adequacy for the facility. He reported that since the facility
was opened in October 2004, the building has received significant and
consistent use, and both the Park District and the Police Department confirm
that there have been no incidents related to inadequate parking.
Trustee Eilers asked about the timing of this review, since it has not been one
full year since the facility opened. President Duhl said that the Paddle Hut’s
busiest season is winter, so this has been a good test run.
d)   Electric Rate Study. Mr. McInturff reported that Requests for Proposals
had been sent to four firms, all of which were capable of performing the work,
experienced, and had good references. He reported that after a comprehensive
review of the proposals, staff recommends that the rate study be awarded to EES
Consulting.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the project as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Cable Bid. Mr. McInturff explained that the Village annually goes out for
bid for the purchase of 600 and 15,000 volt cable requirements for the coming
year. He reported that staff recommended that the bid be awarded to RESCO in an
amount not to exceed $284,000.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, Abell, and Greenough. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid: Forestry Truck. Mr. Saunders explained that the Public Works Dept.
would like to purchase a replacement truck for the forestry division through
the State of Illinois joint purchasing program. He reported that the existing
truck is in poor condition and has exceeded its useful life.
Trustee Greenough, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the purchase of
a new truck from Dennison Corporation, the State of Illinois’s low bidder, in
the amount of $26,751.35. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
g)   Bid: Street Sweeper. Mr. Saunders reported that the Public Works Dept.
would like to purchase a new street sweeper through the Federal General
Services Administration Department. The new equipment will serve as the
Village’s front line sweeper, thereby relegating the existing one to alternate
and back-up status.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the purchase of a
new street sweeper from Maryland Industrial Trucks, the GSA’s low bidder, in
the amount of $124,344.00 By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
h)   Bid: Vehicles. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village needs to purchase
two cars, one for the Finance Department meter reader and one to replace the
Village pool car, through the State of Illinois joint purchasing program.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the purchase of
two new vehicles from Dennison Corporation, the State of Illinois’s low bidder,
in the total amount of $27,175.00. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Greenough, Eilers, Tucker, Woodbury, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
9. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the Chamber of Commerce had held a
successful annual Recognition Luncheon. He said that the luncheon illustrated
the depth and breadth of retails in Winnetka and that award recipients were
deeply appreciative of the recognition of their contributions.
Mr. Eilers also reported that there would be a joint meeting of the BCDC and
the Plan Commission on Wednesday evening in the Public Safety Building.
b)   Village Manager. Manager Williams announced that the Local Canvassing
Board would meet on Saturday, April 23rd, at 10:00 a.m. to certify the results
of the recent election.
11. Appointments.
President Duhl called for a motion to approve the appointment of Becky Hurley
as the School Board’s representative to the Plan Commission to replace Jeff
Hoch. Trustee Greenough so moved, seconded by Trustee Woodbury. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
President Duhl also called for a motion appointing Susan Connor to a second
three-year term on the Board of Fire and Police Commissioners. Trustee Abell
moved to approve the appointment. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Finally, President Duhl called for a motion reappointing Cicely Michalak,
Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce, to the Business Community
Development Commission for a three-year term. Trustee Eilers moved to approve
the appointment. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Greenough moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and board appointments pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11), and 2(c)(3) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, , Ritchell, Tucker and
Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 8:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:26 p.m. Present: President
Duhl, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Tucker, Greenough, Ritchell, and Woodbury.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Greenough, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:27 p.m.
