Recording Secretary
May 3, 2005
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 17, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 3,
2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Duhl called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees David
Abell, Tom Eilers, Nan Greenough, Herb Ritchell, Jessica Tucker, and Ed
Woodbury. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director
of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff, and eight persons in
the audience, including Trustee Candidates Ken Behles and Sandra Berger.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Duhl led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Duhl announced that Item 4, the Quorum, would be moved to the end of
the Agenda so as to take into account the ability of the incoming Trustees to
attend. The Council approved the Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum.
As noted above, this item was moved to the end of the Agenda.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 8, 2005, Special Meeting. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   April 12, 2005, Study Session. Trustee Abell asked about the item
regarding the re-adoption of the Comprehensive Plan. Attorney Janega explained
that the Comprehensive Plan itself does not need to be changed as a result of
the Village’s having elected to become Home Rule but that she recommended that
the Village re-adopt the Plan as it stands, just as she recommends the re-
adoption of the Village Code. However, she added that the readoption of the
Plan need not be done immediately, as with the Code. Trustee Ritchell moved to
adopt the minutes as drafted. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   April 19, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. Seating of New Village Council
a)   Local Canvass Board: Canvass and Proclamations and Administration of Oaths
of Office.
There being no further unfinished business for the sitting Council, President
Duhl asked the Village Clerk to review the results of the Local Canvassing
Board. Village Clerk Williams read the results of the April 5, 2005 election.
He reported that the Canvassing Board had proclaimed that the referendum on the
public question “shall the Village of Winnetka become a home rule unit pursuant
to Article VII, Section 6 of the Illinois Constitution?” passed by a majority
of votes. In addition, the Canvassing Board declared David R. Abell duly
elected to a second term as Village Trustee, Kenneth L. Behles and Sandra A.
Berger duly elected to first terms as Village Trustees, and Edmund C. Woodbury
duly elected as Village President. He added that the appropriate paperwork
certifying the election results had been submitted to the State of Illinois.
Mr. Williams then administered the Oath of Office to Trustees Abell, Behles,
and Berger and to President Woodbury.
9. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Commendation Resolutions – Adoption.
   i)   Resolution No. R-20-2005 – Honoring Trustee Greenough for Village
Service.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-21-2005 – Honoring President Duhl for Village
Service.
President Woodbury read aloud the resolutions honoring Trustee Greenough and
President Duhl and thanked each for their service to the community.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-20-
2005. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
21-2005. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ms. Greenough and Mr. Duhl each delivered brief comments thanking the Village
residents for the opportunity to serve and Village staff for their support
throughout their tenures.
b)   Resolution No. R-22-2005 – Appointing Ed McKee as Village Treasurer –
Adoption. Mr. Williams explained that the Village Code requires that the office
of Village Treasurer be appointed every two years. Historically, this position
has been held by the Finance Director.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
22-2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-23-2005 – Appointing SWANCC Representatives – Adoption.
Mr. Williams explained that the Solid Waste Agency of Northern Cook County
requires that representatives to their Board of Directors be appointed bi-
annually. Historically, the positions of Director and Alternate Director have
been held by the Village Manager and Village President, respectively.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
23-2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-5-2005 – Variation: 644 Pine Lane – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio explained that this ordinance would grant Side Yard Setback,
Permitted Uses, and Nonconformities variations to permit the expansion of a
legal, nonconforming coach house on the subject property. In response to a
question from Trustee Tucker, he noted that although the coach house has
historically been used as a second residence on the property, the Village Code
only permits one principal structure on a single lot of record. Mr. D’Onofrio
went on to report that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend
approval of the variation request.
Paul Constant, the project’s architect, reviewed the proposed plans for the
property. He added that the owners intend for the coach house to be solely for
family use and do not plan to re-rent it.
Andrew Block, the owner, echoed Mr. Constant’s comments.
Trustee Eilers remarked that since the coach house is a long-established use of
this property and that even with the increased size, the Maximum Gross Floor
area requirement for the property will not be exceeded, he was in favor of
granting the variation request.
Trustee Tucker voiced some concern about setting a precedent with coach houses
whereby bulk is being added to an already legally nonconforming property that
could result in a number of single lots of record could ending up with more
than one principal structure.
Attorney Janega addressed Ms. Tucker’s concerns by saying that there is a
provision in the Zoning Ordinance that provides for the occupancy of coach
houses and their continued use. She indicated that she was not concerned with
setting a precedent because the size of the lot makes this a unique situation,
because the lot is already more than three times the minimum size required and
not close to any of the limits for its zoning district.
Trustee Ritchell asked whether the lot is currently subdividable and whether
the coach house could, or could not be, rented at some future date.. Mr.
D’Onofrio responded that by virtue of its raw size, subdivision would be
possible; however, there are other conditions that would have to be met. With
regard to the renting the coach house, he said that the Village Code is silent
on that matter. President Woodbury added that it could probably be rented later
so long as occupancy has been continuous.
After discussing deletion of some of the draft language proposed by Trustee
Abell, Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. M-5-2005. By voice vote, the motion carried.
e)   Resolution No. R-19-2005 – Extension of Sidewalk Contract – Adoption. Mr.
Saunders reviewed this resolution authorizing an extension of the Village’s
contract with Suburban Concrete for sidewalk replacement for 2005 at an
increased cost of 2.43%, which is the CPI rate of change for the specified
period.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-19-
2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-24-2005 – Prevailing Wages – Adoption. Manager Williams
suggested that this item be removed from the agenda so Village staff could
confirm that prevailing wage requirements still apply Home Rule units. The
Trustees agreed.
g)   Resolution No. R-25-2005 – STP Funding for Willow Road – Adoption. Mr.
Saunders explained that this resolution would authorize staff to submit an
application for federal Surface Transportation Program funds that may be
allocated for approved local uses up for to 70% of the project’s costs,
excluding Phase I engineering, for reconstruction of Willow Road between
Provident Avenue and approximately Forest Way Drive. He enumerated the
anticipated scope of work, highlighting the fact that at this point, there are
no specific plans and noted that the Village would be competing with numerous
other projects for the available funding. Mr. Saunders pointed out that if the
Village’s application were to be accepted, construction would probably not
begin until Summer 2009, and the Village’s portion of the projected $3,000,000
total cost would amount to approximately $970,000.
Trustee Tucker asked if there would be a possibility at that time for the
Village to take over jurisdiction from IDOT of Willow Road and whether IDOT
might pick up some of the project’s cost. Mr. Saunders indicated that the
Village could try to negotiate with IDOT.
Trustee Ritchell inquired about the specifics of some of the items contained in
the scope of work as outlined by Mr. Saunders. He specifically expressed
concern about the possibility of reducing the number of lanes at the
intersection of Willow and Hibbard Roads. Mr. Saunders reiterated that this is
a very, very preliminary plan; there is no final scope of work and such a final
engineering plan would be thoroughly reviewed and would not be developed until
such time as the Village’s application for funding is approved. The purpose of
the purpose of the proposed resolution is simply to say that if funding is
approved for Winnetka, the Village commits to pay the local share of the
project costs so that the funding body is assured that the Village’s
application has the backing of the policy makers.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-23-
2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Resolution No. R-26-2005 – MFT Funding for Supplement to Engineering for
Bridge Work – Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that significant additional time
and effort was expended by Ciorba Group in preparing the preliminary
engineering report, which has now received approval from the Federal Highway
Administration, for the replacement of the superstructures on the Eldorado and
Pine Street Bridges. The proposed resolution is required in order to authorize
the expenditure of Motor Fuel Tax funds to cover the costs associated with the
additional work.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
26-2005, authorizing the additional expenditure of $21,000.00 of the Village’s
MFT funds for its share of supplemental engineering services. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
i)   Ordinance M-06-2005 – Amended Affordable Housing Plan – Introduction &
Adoption. President Woodbury postponed the discussion of this item until the
end of the meeting. When the matter was called, Attorney Janega summarized the
Village’s actions on the Affordable Housing Plan. She noted that on March 15,
2005, the Village adopted Resolution No. R-15-2005, adopting the Village’s
Initial Affordable Housing Plan, as required by the Affordable Housing Planning
and Appeal Act, requiring “non-exempt” communities to file such a Plan to IHDA.
She noted that because of the Village’s limited powers prior to the home rule
referendum, the Initial Plan was a relatively short document focused upon the
four components required by the Act. However, because the Act does not preempt
home rule powers and the April 5th election on the home rule referendum had not
yet been held, the Council had directed the Village Clerk to wait until the
results of the election were known before filing the Plan with IHDA.
In light of the successful home rule referendum, there is now a need to amend
the Initial Plan so that the Village can clearly establish that the Plan is
intended to be an exercise of the Village’s new home rule authority. She
reported that this is best done via an ordinance, instead of a resolution, as
has been done in other municipalities.
The proposed ordinance expresses the Village’s intent that the Amended Plan
adopted pursuant thereto is the exclusive action governing affordable housing
within the Village, notwithstanding any statutory provisions to the contrary.
It also cites the fact that the Act does not preempt home rule powers and
directs the Village Clerk to file only the Amended Plan with IHDA. In addition
it directs the Plan Commission to study various aspects of the Amended Plan,
such as affordability standards, incentives, and implementation. Such a review
affords the Village the opportunity to further customize the Village’s Plan at
a later time to reflect the needs of the Village, instead of binding it to a
one-size-fits-all model.
Ms. Janega then provided a section-by-section review of the changes to the
Initial Plan. Ms. Janega noted that Section V retains mention of the Village’s
affordable housing goal as 15% of new development or redevelopment, as stated
in the Initial Plan, with an ultimate goal of 10% of total housing units.
Section V of the Amended Plan also avoids the Act’s broad definition of
"development" by specifically defining "development" and "principal building."
She proposed an additional revision to Section V, replacing the definition of
"development" with an expanded definition of the term " new development.”
President Woodbury expressed some concern over the new definitions and asked
about their implications should a piece of property be subdivided and
redeveloped into additional housing units. Attorney Janega indicated that she
felt this matter was adequately covered by the additional revision she
proposed.
Trustee Abell questioned the use of the phrase “. . . enjoy the benefits of
community with economic diversity, . . .” President Woodbury suggested that it
be changed to refer to a diversity of housing stock. There was a consensus to
make this change.
Mr. Abell also mentioned that while there were several references to “seniors
and young families,” there was only one mention of local employees carried
through as beneficiaries of affordable housing. Ms. Janega remarked that
affordability standards are usually tied to employee residency requirements and
that the Village does not have such a requirement for all of its employees.
President Woodbury added that one of the reasons for not including employees is
that most of the Village’s employees earn more than would qualify them
according to the definition of “affordability.”
Trustee Tucker asked why the Plan couldn’t be adopted by resolution instead of
an ordinance which really puts it into law. Ms. Janega responded that using an
ordinance is the surest way of asserting the Village’s home rule authority, and
that most other home rule municipalities in the area have adopted their
affordable housing plans by ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell supported the ordinance’s inclusion of the range of values
from IHDA, as well as more realistic statistics, so as to help those beyond our
borders to more fully understand the Village’s dilemma. He also questioned
identifying townhomes as an affordable use. Ms. Janega noted that this use has
already been identified in the Comprehensive Plan. President Woodbury added
that it is the intent to say that townhomes are desirable along the Green Bay
corridor.
Trustee Behles asked about the definition of affordability. Attorney Janega
replied that she didn’t want to define the term in the Amended Plan at this
point, but indicated that Highland Park and Wilmette have more or less made
their own definitions. President Woodbury remarked that consideration of
surrounding communities’ affordable housing plans will be part of what the Plan
Commission is asked to do.
Trustee Eilers commented that another of the tasks delegated to the Plan
Commission will be to recommend realistic definitions of Winnetka’s housing
goals, but emphasized that the filing of the Amended Plan puts the Village
clearly on record as being supportive of the affordable housing concept.
Trustee Berger asked Ms. Janega to review the Council’s options, which she did.
President Woodbury then directed the discussion to wordsmithing and general
comments.
Trustee Ritchell remarked that he believes that the Amended Plan is richer, but
given the additional comments, the Council need to review it again before it is
filed.
Trustee Tucker indicated that she was still uncomfortable with the Plan and
suggested that qualifiers be added so that it is clear that Winnetka needs and
intends to come up with its own plan, as IHDA’s definitions are unworkable
because they don’t take into account the underlying value of land in Winnetka.
She also questioned the necessity of filing a plan by May 15th, given that the
Village is now not bound by the Affordable Housing Act.
Attorney Janega acknowledged that the Act has no penalties for not having a
Plan on file, but said that she was reluctant to miss the designated deadline
for filing of either the Initial or the Amended Plan. She said that the problem
with not filing one is that the longer the Village goes without filing
anything, the more it looks like there isn’t a good faith effort here. She
added that, from a standpoint of the initial legal requirement, there is a gap
between when filing was required and the adoption of home rule status. She
voiced her opinion that the Village should file a plan with IHDA because that
requirement applied when the Village first adopted its Plan and applied until
the Village officially became home rule. She added that the Amended Plan was
drafted as an intermediary step that would continue the original plan in
principle without having the Village become subject to the Act’s requirement or
IHDA, until such time as the Plan Commission can come up with more definitive
recommendations. She added that the Amended Plan is not a regulatory document
and that there is a very low risk of any interim development while the Plan
Commission does its study, because the Amended Plan specifically excludes
single-family districts.
Trustee Eilers commented that the Amended Plan reinforces the Village’s zoning
and land use patterns, as well as the Comprehensive Plan.
Trustee Abell voiced two concerns: (1) that by putting a plan on file it could
be argued that the Act applied, and (2) that to include specific percentages
would give a target number to someone. He said that he did not believe that the
Village is bound to file a plan that was forced upon it just because it adopted
one. He added that he believes that if given a blank slate, the Plan Commission
will come up with an affordable housing plan that is significantly different
from that mandated by the Affordable Housing Act. He proposed that the subject
Amended Plan be much more general in nature, take out specific target numbers,
and not refer to the definitions included in the Act, so that the Plan
Commission could develop goals and definitions that are appropriate for the
Village.
President Woodbury remarked that the Amended Plan is so edited in light of the
Village’s new status as a home rule community that it is virtually entirely
different from the Initial Plan. Once the document goes to the Plan Commission,
they will give the Council direction as to how to flesh out a plan based on
what is appropriate for Winnetka and what the community is thinking about this
issue; this is simply a starting point.
Trustee Eilers spoke in support of filing the Amended Plan and then referring
it to the Plan Commission for its comprehensive review. He voiced concern that
the Village is not served well by not filing a plan. He said that he believes
that the Council expressed its desire when it adopted the Initial Plan to keep
an affordable housing policy, notwithstanding the problems with the Act. He
suggested that the reference to 10% of total housing units be eliminated as the
ultimate goal.
Trustee Berger indicated that she was comfortable with the Amended Plan
including the 10% target, noting that the Council had talked about including a
percentage, which dovetails into what the State requires under the Act.
Trustee Behles said that he thinks that the Village should file a plan in
accordance with the Council’s discussions prior to the Village’s achieving home
rule status; however, it should not be the Initial Plan. The Amended Plan
indicates the community’s intention to have a Plan and acts as a placeholder
until such time as a final plan can be developed.
President Woodbury reminded the Trustees that whatever plan is filed, it is
just a planning tool stating goals. He commented that the Village cannot create
economic diversity within the community, but it should encourage a diversity of
housing stock, which might not be possible, but it needs to try.
After further discussion of language and whether to include specific
percentages, President Woodbury suggested that the Village Attorney redraft the
Amended Plan to reflect alternatives. These can then be considered at a Special
Meeting to be held before next week’s Study Session.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-06-2005. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
10. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1411 and 1412. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, Trustee Tucker moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1411 in the amount of $924,954.93 and Warrant List No.
1412 in the amount of $296,787.72. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Behles, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Supplemental Engineering Agreement #1 – Pine and Eldorado Street Bridge
Work. Mr. Saunders asked the Council to approve the Supplemental Agreements
provided for in Resolution R-26-2005 above with the Ciorba Group for the Pine
Street and Eldorado Street bridge projects in the amounts of $21,556 and
$16,606, respectively.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the Agreements.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Parking Study Consultant. Mr. Saunders explained that the one of the early
issues identified as being critical by the Ad Hoc Post Office Committee during
its discussions is current and future parking conditions. In connection with
the future redevelopment of the Post Office site, the Committee asked for a
complete parking study, recognizing that this development presents an
opportunity to address deficiencies in the East and West Elm Street business
districts. Mr. Saunders summarized the bid process and reported that as a
result of their review staff recommended approval of the proposal by Rich &
Associates.
Trustee Eilers remarked that this study is vital to the work of both the Post
Office Committee and the Business Community Development Commission because
surveys of the community indicate that parking problems are a major issue that
needs resolution.
Manager Williams added that the proposals were evaluated by Chief DeLopez, and
Mr. D’Onofrio, as well as Mr. Saunders, and all agreed, from three different
perspectives, that Rich & Associates were the most qualified to perform the
work.
d)   Directional Boring and Cable Installation. Mr. McInturff reviewed staff’s
recommendation to award the contract to perform directional boring and cable
installation services from the period of June 1, 2005 to May 31, 2006 to the
current provider GroundHog Utility Construction at the second year price quoted
in their bid for the 2004-2005 season. He explained that this is a unit price
bid and is based on the unit price times the estimated quantity.
After a brief discussion during which there was a consensus from the Trustees
that the Village should go out for bid again next year rather than accept
GroundHog’s proposed third year quote, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Eilers, moved to award the project as recommended. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Behles, Ritchell, Abell, and
Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
11. Public Comment and Questions.
Lee Padgitt, 560 Green Bay Rd.; expressed his appreciation to the Trustees for
their dedication and hard work.
12. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported on last night’s joint meeting between
the Post Office Committee and the Business Community Development Commission at
which Kevin McCanna, Speer Financial Group, gave a brief overview of funding
options available to the Village. He noted that there had been some discussion
during the meeting about providing some affordable housing in the development.
b)   Village Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that the Secretary of State had
acknowledged receipt of the Home Rule referendum results, and the Village
should receive its official certificate shortly.
c)   Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported that for the 12th year in a row,
Winnetka had been designated as a Tree City USA.
13. Appointments.
President Woodbury called for a motion to approve the appointment of David
Abell as President Pro Tem for the coming year. Trustee Ritchell so moved,
seconded by Trustee Eilers. By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Woodbury announced the Trustee assignments for the coming year, as
follows:
Business Community Dev. Commission Tom Eilers
Chamber of Commerce                Tom Eilers
Coastal Management                 Jessica Tucker
Museum Liaison                     Herb Ritchell
NW Municipal Conference            Dave Abell
Plan Commission                    Jessica Tucker
P.O. Committee                     Nan Greenough
Red Center                         Sandy Berger
                                   Ken Behles (alt)
SWANCC                             Ed Woodbury
                                   Doug Williams
Train Station Liaison              Ken Behles
Warrants Review                    Herb Ritchell &
                                   Sandy Berger
Winnetka Report                    Ken Behles
 
14. Quorum.
a)   May 10, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   May 17, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
15. Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, no Executive Session was held.
16. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:00 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
May 10, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: June 7, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
10, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Fire Chief Scott Smith, and
approximately 30 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Presentation by International and Illinois Associations of Chiefs of Police
to Fern Derning.
Chief DeLopez announced that a special presentation was to be made in honor of
the late Police Chief Don Derning, by the Illinois and International Chiefs of
Police. He introduced Chief Charles Gruber of South Barrington who presented
Mrs. Derning with a crystal bowl memorializing Chief Derning’s service to the
Illinois and International Associations of Police, the Village of Winnetka and
others around the world.
President Woodbury thanked Mrs. Derning and her family and also said a few
words of appreciation about Mr. Derning’s service to the Village as a Trustee.
5. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-6-2005 – Amended Affordable Housing Plan – Adoption.
Village Attorney Janega reviewed the revisions in the ordinance and the Plan
pursuant to the Council’s discussion at its May 3rd meeting. She said the
amended plan clarifies that the new plan supersedes the initial plan and
reiterates more forcefully that the Village is using its home rule authority.
She discussed amendments to the language in Section 7 of the ordinance, made at
the suggestion of Trustee Abell, that would authorize the clerk to file the
amended plan with the IHDA before May 14th in lieu of the initial plan. Ms.
Janega also created four alternatives for the Village’s affordable housing goal
as a result of the Council’s May 3rd discussion as follows:
   Alternative A: 15% of new development, 10% of all housing as ultimate goal
for affordable housing as required by the Affordable Housing Act and also
defines new development.
   Alternative B: goal is as stated in Alternative A, but deletes the 10% goal
for all housing and retains definition of new development and initial goal to
encourage 15% affordable units in new development.
   Alternative C: retains definition of new development, deletes the 10% and
15% goals, and refers to the initial plan’s goal of 15% of all new development
as affordable housing and the study that the Plan Commission will prepare.
   Alternative D: inserts a note instead of text that states goals will be
developed pending the Plan Commission Study.
President Woodbury asked for comments from the audience and there being none,
asked for the Council’s comments.
The Trustees discussed the four alternatives and came to the consensus that
Alternative C would best serve the Village in that it would provide some
guidance to the Plan Commission and yet allow the Plan Commission, at the
Council’s direction, to develop its own standards and recommendations.
Trustee Tucker raised a question about whether the Village has to file a plan
with the State at all now that the Village is a home rule unit and is not
required to comply with the Affordable Housing Act. She suggested that filing
the plan with the IHDA would make the Village subject to the Act even though
home rule allows us not have a plan. She preferred that the plan be adopted and
sent to the Plan Commission to develop their recommendations, rather than
filing it with the IHDA.
Ms. Janega replied that she felt the prudent thing to do would be to file the
plan to have a public record that the Village is in compliance with the
Affordable Housing Act, since until last week when the election results were
certified, the Village was required to comply.
Trustee Tucker responded that the Village did comply by adopting the plan.
Ms. Janega said that her recommendation to the Council is to file the amended
Plan which refers to the Village’s home rule authority, instead of the initial
plan, in the hopes that this approach would remove Winnetka from the IHDA’s
list of communities who have not complied.
Trustee Abell stated that the Village record is a public record in a sense and
that he initially thought it wasn’t necessary to file the plan, but had come to
agree with Ms. Janega that by following the process, it would be a good way to
demonstrate that we did comply, and the reasons why the initial plan was
modified. He also stated that the public has as certain level of expectation
that a plan will be filed and would prefer that the plan was on file and
completely in the open.
Trustee Eilers commented that Ms. Janega had done an excellent job and he
believes that the residents of the Village want to send a message about housing
diversity and take this opportunity to file the plan with the State to show
that Winnetka is not ignoring the issue.
Trustee Ritchell agreed with Trustee Eilers, but inquired as to whether there
was any liability to the Village if the plan is filed.
Ms. Janega replied that she did not think there was and could not see any down
side to filing the plan.
Ken Behles added that the amended plan asserts our home rule authority and gets
the Village off the IHDA roles, but more importantly, public comment has
indicated that the residents want a plan. He also agreed that the plan should
be filed.
Trustee Berger agreed that filing the plan shows our commitment to this issue
and that by only have a record here with the Village, it does not publicly show
our intent.
President Woodbury commented that he thinks that the Plan will be amended often
as the Plan Commission makes its recommendations and thanked the Council for
their discussions on this issue.
Trustee Eilers moved to amend Ordinance M-6-2005 as revised in the agenda
materials, including the language suggested for Section 7 and using alternative
C as the statement of the Village’s policy in the Affordable Housing Plan,
seconded by Trustee Abell. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Abell moved to adopted the amended ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Eilers. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6. Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 8:34 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
May 10, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: June 7, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
10, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 8:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Chief of
Police Joe DeLopez, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Director of Community Development
Mike D’Onofrio and approximately 10 persons in the audience.
2)   Updated Building Code.
Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio informed the Council that
building construction is regulated through a series of model building codes,
known as the International Codes authored by the International Code Council
(ICC). He stated that the ICC publishes a new set of codes every three years,
but typically municipalities update and adopt new codes on a six year cycle in
order to establish consistent construction standards to protect the public
health, safety and welfare. He added that updating the building code is also
done to maintain the Village’s Insurance Services Office, Inc. rating, which is
used by the insurance industry to set insurance rates. He said that ISO has
given the Village until July 6th to adopt a new building code.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that staff had reviewed the model codes and proposes that
the codes listed in the agenda materials be adopted as well as certain
amendments to the Code to address issues that are particular to Winnetka. He
said that the amendments will incorporate changes to the administrative
sections of the Code, will list the actual codes adopted by the Village, will
make technical additions and deletions to address concerns related to Winnetka
and will incorporate existing Village policies and procedures. Mr. D’Onofrio
added that this code series includes residential, mechanical, gas piping,
energy conservation, fire, national electric and plumbing, but he has deleted
references to a property maintenance code because the Village does not have
one.
President Woodbury commented that property maintenance might be something to
look into and asked Trustee Eilers to mention it to the Chamber of Commerce. He
also asked that third party approvals for historic properties be expanded to
allow for some flexibility when the codes don’t apply to a property.
Trustee Ritchell requested that the placement of construction fencing be set at
least 18 inches from the street and neighboring lot lines so that it allows
safe passage and does not become an intrusion on a neighbor’s property.
After a short discussion, the Trustees agreed that the Building Code should be
updated as outlined by Mr. D’Onofrio.
3)   Forestry Committee’s Proposed Amendments to Tree Preservation Code.
Mary Hill, chair of the Forestry Commission, reported to the Council that the
Commission had taken the best of the codes from other communities along the
north shore, and incorporated their own ideas to draft the proposed amendments
to the Tree Preservation Code. She said that, in drafting the amendments, the
Commission recognized that trees are important to Winnetka, but also did not
want to have tree preservation damage property rights.
Jim Stier, Village Forester, reported that the increase in home construction,
demolitions and tree removal had raised concern with the Forestry Commission.
He said that neighboring communities are constantly revising their codes with
new techniques, new preservation, and tree replacement,, but it has been number
of years since the Commission has updated Winnetka’s Tree Preservation Code. He
said that a sub-committee had been formed a year ago to survey neighboring and
out of state communities to list major concerns and propose amendments that
would protect trees and provide for stronger enforcement.
Public Works Director, Steve Saunders, suggested that the first step would be
to make technical amendments to the Code that will allow Mr. Stier to better
enforce his recommendations to developers and strengthen the ability of the
Public Works Department to resolve tree issues in the Village. He then
summarized four policy issues for the Council to consider: (a) that the current
language of “allowing reasonable use of land” be stricken because it is at odds
with what the Forester can do; (b) the designation of heritage trees, which are
of such a character and nature that are worth extra protection; (c) reasons for
the removal of trees and under what conditions applications are approved or
not; and (d) the role of the Forestry Commission in the appeal process because
the current language is inconsistent with the powers of other boards and
committees.
Bernard Hammer, of the Forestry Commission, said that the Commission had
discussed the appeal process at great length. He said that the concern was that
anyone in the Village could ask that a tree not be removed, so the Commission
limited the ability to appeal to the applicant or one of the Forestry
Commission members, who would consider whether it would go to the Council. He
said the Commission intends that a group who opposes the removal of a tree
would have to go to a Commission member and convince them to appeal for them.
Mr. Saunders stated that an applicant always has the right to appeal under the
Code, but staff would like preliminary direction from the Council on the four
issues, so that staff can fashion the Forestry Commission document into Village
Code revisions.
President Woodbury appreciated that the Commission has been working on the
amendments since 2003, but requested that the policy issues be brought back to
the Council for further discussion in a few months, since this is the first the
Council has seen of the proposed revisions.
Manager Williams suggested that the technical amendments could be done
initially in order to give the Forester more enforcement power during this
construction season and then address the larger policy issues at a later date.
Village Attorney Janega agreed and proposed that the definitions drafted by the
Commission be included in the Code as well as clarification of the desired
species list. She then asked if the standard for measurement of protected trees
had changed.
Mr. Stier explained that the measurement currently is 2 feet from grade, but
the DBH measurement is about 4.5 feet and so to reflect the DBH standard, the
diameter at which a tree becomes protected has been reduced to 8 inches instead
of the current 10 inches.
Ms. Janega said that adopting the DBH standard is important to do quickly.
Mr. Saunders explained that because our Code does not use the DBH standard and
surveyors do, Mr. Stier has to re-measure their measurements and therefore,
conflicts arise as to whether the tree is less than 10 inches in diameter and
therefore, protected.
Trustee Behles said that he hadn’t seen any standards for review of heritage
trees in the proposed amendments and asked what criteria the Forestry
Commission would use to determine whether the tree would be able to be removed.
He added that although the procedure is clear, any developer or contractor
would want to know what kind of review criteria there is to see if it is worth
going through the process.
Ms. Janega agreed that standards should be developed, but said technical
amendments could be made before the Council considers other policy issues and
the standards for heritage tree removal.
Mr. Hammer said that the Commission feels that review of an application for
removal of a heritage tree should be by the Forestry Commission, not the
Village Forester. He also said that the age and size of the species of tree
should also be taken into account because different species grow differently.
He added that trees are a good source of flood control as well.
Ms. Janega suggested that while the staff works on technical amendments and
format, the Forestry Commission could try to define standards for review of
heritage trees to keep the process going on parallel tracks.
President Woodbury added that there may also be concepts in zoning that might
be helpful to the Commission in defining these standards. .
Susan Shabica, a member of the Forestry Commission, said that other towns have
larger staffs who enforce tree regulations and work on amendments monthly. She
said that the Commission’s goal was to clarify the Code to provide regulations
for the protection of trees on construction sites and neighboring properties
and also better inform the community. She thought that the main standard for
tree preservation is whether the tree is healthy or not and that the ordinance
should be based on healthy trees only. She added that there are also zoning
regulations, such as the placement of garages, that can be responsible for the
demise of trees and so, Boards are working against each other.
Trustee Tucker asked the Commission to give the Council an idea of the input
they have received from the public when they next discuss the revisions to the
Code, so that the Trustees can get a sense as to what the feedback is in the
community.
In response to a question from Trustee Berger, Mr. Saunders stated that the
Village receives 80 – 100 tree removal applications per year. He asked the
Commission and the Council to consider whether it is feasible to accomplish all
that is planned with the current staff, since Mr. Stier only has an assistant
to help him part of the time and the department is not in a position to
increase staff.
Trustee Ritchell asked that, again, when drafting the revisions, the staff
include a requirement that protective fencing be placed at least 1.5 feet from
the street to allow safe passage for traffic and pedestrians.
President Woodbury ended the discussion by saying that the Council is committed
to making the ordinance better and recommended that the technical amendments be
done so that the Forester has the power to enforce. Then, within the next few
months, he said the Council will discuss policy issues, standards for review,
and what to do when conflicts arise.
4)   2004 Traffic Enforcement & Crash Analysis.
Chief DeLopez discussed the traffic enforcement & crash analysis report, noting
a 2% increase in the number of crashes occurring at the peak times of 8 a.m. -
8:50 a.m. and at the noon hour, at locations that mirrored last year’s, which
are all under the jurisdiction of IDOT. He said that the Police Department had
passed recommendations along to IDOT last year and again this year, on
engineering changes, traffic calming, alterations in roadways, etc., but IDOT
responded that they only had funds to assist with signage. He further said that
the Village can’t do alterations in IDOT’s roadways or traffic signals without
incurring liability for the residents and Public Works had done most of what
they are able to do, particularly in the ravines.
Chief DeLopez added that speeding complaints continue and so traffic
enforcement has increased for that and for violations of following too closely
as well, which can result in rear-end accidents. He said the Police
Department’s goal is to try to stop some of the accidents from happening at the
most frequent locations. In response to a comment by Trustee Abell, who noted
that there had been an increase in the number of people who were cited for
driving without licenses, Chief DeLopez replied that many of these were drivers
with suspended licenses involved in accidents. He also stated that that the
statistics reflect the metropolitan area and are not statewide in response to a
question from Trustee Ritchell.
5)   Replacement of Secondary Weapons.
Chief DeLopez informed the Council that the department is requesting that the
police officers’ secondary weapons be replaced with a weapon that is safer in
urban environments and is used by NIPAS and departments in the surrounding
areas with whom we respond in mutual aid incidents. He explained that the rifle
currently in use can project a bullet through a wall and injure someone in
another room, but the ammunition in the new weapons does not travel with the
same velocity and is more appropriate for our use. He added that all of the
officers will receive training in the use of the weapon.
Sgt. Christensen narrated a power point presentation on the transition of the
currently used 9mm carbine to the 5.56mm semi-automatic rifle, which has the
same type of qualities as a pistol. He explained the use of the rifle in
several types of incidents, its accuracy, and its ability to stop life
threatening behavior quickly without over-penetrating or passing through
building materials. He further explained that the surrounding departments have
these weapons and the ammunition is interchangeable, which would be beneficial
when our department assists them or their departments assist us. In response to
a question from Trustee Eilers, Sgt. Christensen stated that he was not aware
of any controversy or lobbying effort to block the use of these weapons.
Manager Williams explained that because of the joint response from the
neighboring communities who are using these weapons, he believes that it would
be best to standardize equipment.
Chief DeLopez commented that the department would retain the current weapons
for training and to use as additional weapons. In response to a question from
Trustee Berger, he explained that the weapons for training are locked in a
vault at the police department and all guns are either under the officer’s
direct control or locked in a rack. He said the department has budgeted $20,000
to purchase 8 guns for each squad car and for training purposes, but ideally
would like to have 10 rifles.
Trustee Eilers commented that if the department needs 10 rifles, then they
should not limit themselves to the $20,000 that was budgeted. He understood
that the weapon may be more lethal to the offender, but less harmful if errant
shots are made and asked if the Council needs to know anything else about this
weapon that might be criticized, so that they can be prepared to answer
questions if necessary from the public.
Chief DeLopez replied that it is true that the gun is more accurate and he did
not know of any other information of a critical nature about the weapon.
President Woodbury asked the Council if there were any objections to the
purchase of these weapons and there were none.
6)   Structure of Northern Illinois Police Crime Laboratory
Chief DeLopez explained the intergovernmental agreement that restructures the
police crime lab services to an intergovernmental cooperative. He said this
agreement standardizes the membership agreements currently in place and does
not impact our cost structure which is determined by population.
After a short discussion, President Woodbury determined that the consensus of
the Council was to go forward with this agreement.
7)   Draft Caucus Question.
Manager Williams requested that the Council consider the first draft of a
question for the caucus questionnaire regarding not-for-profit funding. He
explained that similar questions were asked in 1999 and 2000, but the responses
did not provide a clear answer.
Trustee Eilers asked that the reference to other villages be stricken.
Trustee Berger commented that the previous questions were awkwardly phrased and
suggested that we should ask the question that we want answered. She asked if
we are interested in just the Winnetka Youth Organization or other
organizations as well.
President Woodbury said that the question is if we give to the WYO, what do we
do when other organizations want funds. He suggested adding some background and
the mission of the Village to provide essential services as an educational
point.
Trustee Abell commented that President Woodbury’s point was significant.
Gene Graeble, Caucus Chair, said his own view is that the quality of the
question is paramount and have to be honest with ourselves and be prepared for
the answer. .
8)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held..
9)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:15 p.m.
