   Deputy Clerk
May 17, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 7, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May
17, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Tom Eilers, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, and 10 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   June 7, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Ritchell, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   June 14, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Abell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 3, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers suggested a minor text
change. Trustee Behles then moved to adopt the minutes as amended. Trustee
Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-5-2005 – Zoning Variation: 644 Pine Lane – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment to the ordinance language
resulting from the discussion at the May 3rd meeting granting zoning variations
to permit the expansion of a nonconforming coach house on the subject property.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, then moved to adopt the amended
language. By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt this ordinance granting Side
Yard Setback, Permitted Uses, and Nonconformities variations to permit the
expansion of a legal, nonconforming coach house on the subject property.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-5-
2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker,
Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behled. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Resolution No. R-27-2005 – MFT Funding for Winnetka Avenue Rehabilitation
– Adoption. Mr. Williams reviewed this resolution authorizing the expenditure
of $150,000 of Motor Fuel Tax Funds for the resurfacing of Winnetka Avenue from
Church to Sheridan, noting that the amount was slightly below staff’s estimate
for the project.
Trustee Ritchell asked when the Village anticipated undertaking the work. Mr.
Williams replied that after talking to New Trier High School, it was decided to
begin work in late July and anticipated that it would take about three weeks to
complete. He pointed out that the stoplight would not be done at the same time.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
27-2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-24-2005 – Prevailing Wages – Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that even though the Village is now Home Rule, it has been confirmed
that it still is required by law to annually adopt this resolution establishing
the prevailing wages for laborers, mechanics, and other workers in the Village
employed in performing construction of public works for the Village.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution No. 24-
2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1413 and 1414. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1413 in the amount of $735,859.95 and Warrant List No. 1414 in the
amount of $385,985.83. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Tucker,
Ritchell, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 928 Elm Street. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
four (4) variations from the Zoning Ordinance to permit complete remodeling of
the existing home on the property in order convert it from a currently legal,
nonconforming, multifamily structure back into a single-family home. The
improvements include: (1) a two-story addition at the rear of the structure,
(2) an expansion of a covered porch, (3) installation of new windows in
nonconforming sidewalls, and (4) construction of a detached garage. In
addition, he noted that the proposed improvements would result in a reduction
of the total nonconformities. He said that the applicant’s difficulties are, in
part, the result of the existing nonconforming nature of the lot and the
existing structure, which, in turn, mean that virtually any work on the
property would require some sorts of variations.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that in response to action by the Zoning Board of
Appeals the number of variations being requested has been reduced from the
original number submitted. At its meeting on April 11th, the ZBA took two votes
concerning the variation requests. The first vote included all the variations
except for the originally proposed 1.38 foot east side yard setback for the
proposed porch, and those failed to receive a positive recommendation on a 3 to
3 vote. A second vote on the east side yard variation also failed on a vote of
6 to 0. The east sideyard has now been brought into conformity, but a variation
is still required for the west side.
Manager Williams pointed out that given the scope of work, in excess of 50% of
the cost of construction of such a building or new construction, all work would
have to be done in accordance with all applicable Village codes.
Trustee Tucker asked whether the agenda packet materials were the same as those
seen by the Zoning Board. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that they were, except that
the front porch has been made narrower. Ms. Tucker indicated that she was
uncomfortable proceeding without a recommendation from the Zoning Board.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that every variation being
requested is less than exists currently. He also pointed out the benefit of
removing a multifamily dwelling from this property in a single-family zoning
district and asked whether the existing large Oak tree could be saved. Mr.
D’Onofrio said that question would have to be directed to the Village Forester.
Mr. Behles asked Mr. D’Onofrio to comment on the possible width of any
potential house that might be built on the property should the existing
dwelling be demolished. Mr. D’Onofrio said that new construction would be
limited to 26’ in width, which would be difficult to accommodate given that the
driveway would be only 8 feet, as a portion of it, for which there is a
permanent easement, sits on neighboring property.
Trustee Ritchell asked the extent of the proposed interior construction. Mr.
D’Onofrio indicated that it was quite substantial and reviewed the existing
parameters for distinguishing renovation from new construction. He indicated
that given the work proposed, this project would at present be considered
renovation.
President Woodbury explained that given the existing circumstances, the Council
had only three options: to accept, deny, or remand the matter back to the
Zoning Board.
The owner, Mr. John (“Rip”) Suster, reviewed the status of the existing
property and his plans for the Trustees. He indicated that the dwelling is an
unworkable interior design for a single family and is in extremely poor
condition. He said that he intends to retain the front and both side walls and
do all of the construction work from the rear. He distributed pictures of the
existing structure and renderings of what might be built were it to be
demolished and new construction required. He stated his belief that such
possible new construction does not appear to fit the neighborhood or be of
benefit to the neighbors.
After a discussion about possible revisions to the proposed plans, President
Woodbury reiterated the fact that the question of renovation vs. reconstruction
is important to the Council and said that although it is not an issue as it
pertains to this project, it is something that the Council needs to determine.
Trustee Behles noted that the plans call for pouring a new foundation, which
indicates construction of a new house.
Luvie Owens, 922 Elm, asked the applicant and the Council for clarification of
some specific questions.
Mr. Anderson, 930 Elm, stated that he is not in favor of the proposed plans and
that he believes that the project is really a reconstruction, not a renovation.
He expressed concern about the density on the west side and potential fire
hazard.
Don Smith, 511 Provident, voiced concern about the impact on already existing
drainage problems.
Ken Tracy, 916 Elm, inquired whether given the circumstances, allowances
couldn’t be made that would permit the construction of a new home that was
satisfactory to both the applicant and the neighbors.
President Woodbury said that he believes that this project qualifies as new
construction, and suggested that given that the Zoning Board reviewed the
matter as a renovation, the Council table it until it can be reconsidered by
the Zoning Board.
Ms. Janega added that if it is deemed to be new construction, then the entire
matter must go back to the Zoning Board for reconsideration of the entire plan.
Trustee Abell asked whether the Council could then ask for variations for new
construction. Manager Williams responded that although the Council has the
authority to grant such variances, historically, it does not do so. Trustee
Abell commented that he believes there is a need to put something better than
what currently exists on the property and that the Village would be well-served
if it can retain the architecture of the present dwelling and take advantage of
Mr. Suster’s willingness to rehabilitate it. He said that he was not
particularly bothered by the front yard variation because the house has been
where it is for a long time and asked for more input from the Zoning Board.
Trustee Behles remarked that he could understand why there had been a split
decision in the Zoning Board, noting that while the proposed plan is attractive
and a commendable effort to rehabilitate the current structure, when analyzed
it really constitutes new construction, which triggers a whole new set of
findings wherein the plan needs to be more in conformance with the Zoning
ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell concurred.
Mr. Suster indicated that he would not be the owner to do new construction on
the property.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. Suster for his efforts and apologized for
extending the matter. He added, however, that the Council does not want to
encourage new construction, particularly with a house so close to the property
line, so it is important for the matter of renovation vs. reconstruction to be
settled.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to table the matter to June
21st. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Post Office Consultant. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that as part of the
redevelopment of the Post Office block one of the processes involved includes
obtaining public input. The purpose of the public input component is to solicit
opinions from the public as to what form and use(s) the redevelopment should
take. In order to facilitate the public input process, at the direction of the
Ad Hoc Post Office Committee, staff prepared and issued a Request for Proposals
(RFP). Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the RFP process and indicated that staff and the
Post Office Committee had identified several different groups, some of which
might be combined, as potential participants in the workshops. Responding firms
were asked to provide breakdowns dividing their services into two separate
phases, with Phase I consisting of a series of community workshop meetings and
conceptual plan development, while an optional Phase II would, dependent on the
outcome of Phase I, be used to develop specific detailed architectural site and
design criteria for development of the site. The resulting plans and public
comments would ultimately serve as key elements of the Village’s solicitation
of development proposals.
After a comprehensive review of the proposals received, staff recommends that
The Lakota Group awarded the bid for Phase I of the project and Phase II not be
awarded at this time. He added that the Village has budgeted $50,000 for
studies associated with the Post Office block redevelopment.
Trustee Behles asked about the projected costs for Phase II and mentioned that
Phase II costs should be identified before awarding Phase I, because he
believes we may want to continue Phase II work with the same firm that
completes Phase I.
Manager Williams pointed out that the Chair of the Post Office Committee does
not see the redevelopment vision as progressing in a linear fashion, but one
that goes back to the public and the Council several times throughout its
evolution. He said that the Committee hopes to have the focus group meetings
completed by the end of summer so that it can come to the Council with its
preliminary ideas in the fall.
There was a consensus among the Trustees that at least one of these focus
groups should be open to the public at large and that it should be well
advertised.
President Woodbury said that his experience indicates that these “visioning”
sessions are very valuable in that they bring out the creative process; it is
translating the vision into a workable possibility.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the contract to The
Lakota Group to perform the work outlined in the RFP Phase I Redevelopment
Design Workshops for an amount not to exceed $54,739, as stipulated in their
proposal dated April 26, 2005, provided that the Village Attorney approve the
form of the contract used.. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
d)   Proposed Caucus Question Non-for-profit Funding. Manager Williams
explained that the Winnetka Youth Organization has repeatedly requested funding
from the Village. Thus far those requests have been denied by the Village
Council. However, the Council recently agreed to draft a question for possible
inclusion in the upcoming annual Caucus questionnaire as prior questionnaire
questions intended to determine the community’s support for requested funding
of not-for-profit organizations have produced disparate responses.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to present the question as drafted to
the Caucus.
10. Reports.
a)   President.
b)   Trustees. None.
c)   Village Attorney. None.
d)   Village Manager. Mr. Williams announced that there will be a tour of
Village facilities for the new Trustees. The tour will be split in half and
held on the mornings of Wednesday, May 25th and Thursday, May 26th.
11. Appointments.
None
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Abell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:20 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Tucker, Behles, Berger, and, Ritchell.
Absent: None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:21 p.m.
