Recording Secretary
June 7, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 21, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
7, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Tom Eilers, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Herb Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Public Works
Steven Saunders and ten persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that the Agenda had been amended to include
addition of Item 9(g), IDOT Jurisdictional Transfer of Tower Road and to remove
Item 9(c), Unmarked Police Vehicle to the Agenda. The Trustees approved the
Agenda as amended.
4. Quorum.
a)   June 14, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Abell, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   June 21, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 10, 2005, Study Session. Trustee Behles moved to adopt the minutes.
Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   May 10, 2005, Special Meeting. Trustee Tucker moved to adopt the minutes.
Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   May 17, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles suggested a minor language
change. Trustee Berger then moved to adopt the minutes as amended. Trustee
Abell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-8-2005 – Zoning Variation: 322 Sunset – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front and Corner Setback, Rear Yard Setback,
Garage Door Width, and Obstructions in Required Yard variations to permit the
construction of three additions along the rear of the existing house, plus a
masonry wall along the south property line. He pointed out that given the
location of the lot on Sunset Road, Essex Road, and a private drive serving
three properties, the property is considered to have three front yards (north,
east, and west) and one rear yard (south). Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the
Zoning Board of Appeals had taken three separate votes on the variations,
resulting in unanimous recommendations in favor of granting the front and rear
yard setback and the fence/wall variations. However, the vote was tied on the
garage door width variation, meaning that there is no recommendation to the
Council.
Trustee Berger asked whether the wall was required to be articulated. Mr.
D’Onofrio said that it was not because it is considered a fence.
Trustee Eilers inquired as to the genesis of the garage door requirement, which
Attorney Janega provided.
Greg Nowesnik, the applicant’s architect, addressed the variations under
discussion and referred the Trustees to an elevation in the Agenda Packet
depicting the proposed garage door design. He explained the applicant’s
rationale for seeking the garage door variation, the first being to improve
safety and second being convenience. Currently the applicants must fold in
their rear view mirrors in order to enter/exit their garage because the
existing single doors are too narrow for modern vehicles, in addition to which
the turning radius of these vehicles prevents a driver from sweeping easily
into the garage. The proposed door would replicate the current two-door look
but afford full use of the garage without having to fold in mirrors.
Trustee Behles asked whether the garage door jambs could be extended slightly
to permit the installation of two slightly larger, single doors, instead of the
one large door. Mr. Nowesnik indicated that that option had been explored, but
it does not afford as unencumbered ingress and egress as the one proposed.
President Woodbury pointed out that there are three matters under
consideration: (1) the additions, (2) the wall, and (3) the garage door, and
noted that there is a matter of precedence to be considered, as well. He asked
Mr. Nowesnik to elaborate on the proposed masonry wall.
Mr. Nowesnick explained that this variation is being sought in order to provide
additional visual containment of unsightly, ill-kept maintenance facilities on
the property of New Trier High School, as well as a chain link fence topped by
barbed wire. He said that among other things, New Trier has chosen to store a
large, steel storage container along the applicants’ property line, within
their immediate view. He added that the applicants have repeatedly tried to
resolve this issue with the high school, but have failed to raise any response
from them. Therefore, they are trying to be proactive in devising a means to
hide the unattractive view from their home.
Trustee Tucker asked what advantage an 8’ wall would give the applicant. Mr.
Nowesnick said that it would obstruct the view of the steel container and the
barbed wire from the applicants’ first floor living spaces.
Trustee Abell asked whether this view couldn’t be hidden just as well by a wall
of arborvitae. Mr. Nowesnick explained that arborvitae are subject to weather
and disease and that they would have to be planted inside the applicants’
property line, requiring a significant proportion of the limited open space to
accommodate their width.
Trustee Tucker asked the owner, Mr. Henderson, to speak to the matter of the
garage door, which he did.
President Woodbury asked for additional discussion from the applicant and his
architect as to the need for the proposed additions in order to establish
findings and rationale for the additional kitchen space and bedroom above.
Mr. Nowesnick responded that the existing kitchen space is disproportionately
small by today’s standards and the area into which the applicant is proposing
to expand is currently occupied by a greenhouse, which is meaningless to the
applicant. He said that the expansion would not exacerbate the closeness to the
south wall. As to the expansion above, the applicant is looking to provide a
bath for an existing bedroom.
Ms. Tucker indicated that she considers the addition requests to be reasonable
as they comprise a modest expansion. She said that the whole of the property is
unique because of the undue hardship of having three front yards and being
adjacent to New Trier High School on two sides of the property. She remarked
that she was not concerned about setting a precedent with regard to the masonry
wall because this property backs up to the high school, and added that she was
also in favor of the garage door variation because of the safety concerns and
the fact that it has been designed to replicate the existing look.
Trustee Abell concurred with Trustee Tucker’s position and said that he, too,
would vote in favor of all three variations.
Trustee Berger agreed with regard to the additions and the garage door, but
said that she was troubled by the masonry wall and asked for the applicants to
submit some documentation of their attempts to resolve this matter with NTHS.
Trustee Eilers said that he feels this situation is unique and that safety is
of paramount importance, so he would be in favor of all three requests.
Trustee Behles agreed that having three front yards is a significant hardship
and is sufficient reason to approve the addition variations. He indicated that
he was not in favor of the creation of a nonconforming situation with the
garage door, nor was he in favor of the masonry wall, and he expressed dismay
by NTHS’s failure to respond to the applicants’ appeals.
Noting that all of the Trustees were in favor of the addition variations, three
supported the wall variation, and the required four were in favor of the garage
door variation, President Woodbury called for a motion to introduce the draft
ordinance as presented. Trustee Eilers so moved, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-9-2005 – Special Use Permit: R&R Music School, 896 Green
Bay Road - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Special Use
Permit to allow a music teaching studio to be located in commercial space
located on the first floor of the subject property which is located in the C-
2 Retail Overlay Zoning District. The proposed location is in a mixed use
building that has a number of commercial uses and residential apartments on the
second and third floors. He reported that both the Zoning Board of Appeals and
the Plan Commission had recommended approval of the Special Use Permit,
conditioned upon there being a review after the school has been in operation
for six months to determine if there is any negative impact on residents
residing in the building.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that despite much discussion about
noise, the only reason this is before the Council is because of the need for a
special use permit because of the retail overlay constraint.
Trustee Abell voiced some concern about how late the applicant is planning to
operate. Mr. D’Onofrio advised him that the Council could impose conditions on
the special use permit.
Attorney Janega pointed out that as drafted, the ordinance only recites the
hours of operation, but does not make them a condition of use. In response to a
question from Trustee Berger, she also reviewed the process for imposing
conditions.
President Woodbury asked Mr. D’Onofrio to investigate whether the new building
code provisions could help in terms of preventing noise transmission to the
space above.
Trustee Behles commented that although there is no testimony as to the
construction of the building, it is his understanding that the building is
concrete and, therefore, would satisfy any sound transmission requirements.
Manager Williams suggested that on a going-forward basis, requests for special
use permits should be reviewed by the Business Community Development Commission
with an eye toward their potential impact on the business districts. Trustee
Eilers mentioned that Manager Williams had already asked that Committee to
study the C-2 Zoning District.
The applicant, Matt Lundergan, distributed statements from his current
suitemate and landlord testifying to the fact that his business does not cause
noise problems. He also enumerated on the kinds of instruments that he uses and
stated that he expects that most of his teaching would be on a one-to-one
basis. When asked about the impact of a potential cutback in his hours, Mr.
Lundergan said he would be concerned about financial ruin.
Trustee Eilers indicated that he believes that a six-month review period would
take care of addressing any problems that might arise. He said that he would
like to give the petitioner as wide an envelope as possible in which to operate
so as to maximize his opportunity to succeed.
Elena Dreiske, 594 Elm St., commented that if she lived in an apartment
upstairs, she would be upset if a music school moved in below.
John Shepard, a Chicago resident and long-time associate of Mr. Lundergan’s,
testified as to his conscientiousness about noise.
Attorney Janega suggested that a condition be incorporated specifying that no
acoustic drums and cymbals be permitted. She also commented that acoustic
guitars should not be a problem and are significantly softer than an acoustic
piano would be. Mr. Lundergan indicated that he would not object to such a
condition.
Trustee Behles raised the point that the special use in the C-2 Overlay
District is the main issue in this matter. He said that he would support the
proposed non-retail use, as the location under discussion is inside a courtyard
and does not have street frontage. He also indicated that he is not bothered by
the noise generation issue and feels that the applicant’s representations have
been fair. He concurred with Trustee Eilers on affording the applicant a wide
area of operation.
Manager Williams remarked that there is a layer of protection for neighbors in
the Nuisance Ordinance.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the drafted
ordinance with the addition of the suggested condition on acoustic instruments.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-7-2005 – Harris Bank Lease Extension - Introduction.
President Woodbury announced that this item has been tabled until a later date
pending further information.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2005 – Re-adopt Village Code Under Home Rule Powers –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed the draft ordinance amending and re-
adopting the Village Code pursuant to the Village’s home rule powers. She
explained that under Illinois law, every action of a home rule unit is
considered to be an exercise of its home rule powers. However, there is some
case law that raises questions as to whether legislative enactments taken prior
to acquiring home rule status will be considered exercises of home rule powers
if the municipality silently allows the ordinances to stand. The attached
Ordinance MC-2-2005 has been drafted to eliminate such questions and to
unequivocally state the Village's intent to fully exercise its home rule
powers. Ms. Janega then reviewed the specific proposed amendments.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2005 – ISO Building & Fire Code Amendment –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that this item had been thoroughly reviewed
at a recent study session. He explained that the proposed ordinance would amend
various sections of Chapter 15 of the Village Code pertaining to buildings and
construction, in order to update it to be consistent with a series of model
building codes that are used to regulate building construction. He defined a
model code as a written set of standardized regulations that provide a means
for exercising reasonable control over construction, with such changes as may
be desirable or legal for local needs. Model codes address the design,
installation, maintenance, alteration and inspection of various systems,
including buildings, plumbing, mechanical, electrical and fire prevention.
He added that there is also another version of codes regulating construction,
known as building codes. The building code is a legal document adopted by a
local municipality that regulates building. The building code is based on a
model code that is then modified in order to address local needs.
Mr. D’Onofrio went on to say that the main purpose of all model and building
codes is to protect the public health, safety and welfare. This purpose is
achieved in several different ways. First, codes reduce potential hazards; as
safe buildings are achieved through proper design, construction and inspection
practices that are regulated by building codes. Second, codes keep construction
costs down and provide uniformity of requirements for the construction
industry. Third, codes establish predictable and consistent construction
standards, whether it be through construction methods, practices or materials.
In addition to the public health, safety and welfare issues, another reason for
updating the building code is to maintain the Village’s rating from Insurance
Services Office, Inc. (ISO). He told the Trustees that after ISO conducted its
grading of Winnetka last summer, the Village was informed that in order to
maintain a Grade 4 rating, the building code would have to be updated. ISO has
given the Village until July 6, 2005, to have a new building code adopted.
Mr. D’Onofrio then provided an overview of the kinds of amendments being
proposed, saying they have been grouped into four categories:
   1.   Administrative – Those that include changes to the administrative
sections of the Codes.
   2.   Technical – Additions and deletions to model codes made to address
Winnetka-related concerns.
   3.   Different Codes Adopted – Those identifying codes that have not been
adopted, either wholly or in part, and others that have been substituted.
   4.   Policy – Those that incorporate existing policies and procedures.
In conclusion, Mr. D’Onofrio said that staff believes that if the ICC Codes,
2003 Edition, along with the NFPA Codes and Publications, 2000 Edition, NEC
Electrical Code, 2002 Edition, and State of Illinois Plumbing Code, 2004
Edition are adopted, the Village will be equipped to regulate construction and
to ensure that all building is done in accordance with the most current
construction practices, methods and materials.
Trustee Abell confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that these amendments would not
include the property maintenance portion of the ICC Codes, in keeping with
current Village policy. Trustee Eilers mentioned that the Business Community
Development Commission was planning to look into this issue, too.
Trustee Tucker confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that contrary to what the Trustees
frequently hear, Winnetka is really no different in its use of building codes
than its neighbors.
Trustee Behles asked whether the Village would be adopting all of the same
amendments that are currently in use. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that they are
generally the same. There are no substantial changes but some may have been
incorporated in different places.
Village Attorney Janega then walked the Council through the Ordinance,
explaining how the proposed amendments were put into Village Code form.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. D’Onofrio and Attorney Janega for their
extensive work on this item.
f)   Resolution No. R-28-2005 – Historical Society Award Nomination – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the resolution that would authorize the Village to
nominate the Winnetka Historical Society’s project to relocate and restore the
Schmidt-Burnham Log House for a 2005 Driehaus Preservation Award. She explained
that the Village nominated the same project last year for a Driehaus award, but
it did not receive one.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury commented that this is a very prestigious award and one that
is well deserved by the Historical Society.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1415 and 1416. Trustee Tucker reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1415 in the amount of $919,426.47 and Warrant List No. 1416 in the
amount of $342,581.86. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Tucker,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
b)   Policy Direction: Citizen’s Request to Consider Historical District. Susan
Kiphart, 608 Elm Street, read the letter that she wrote to the Trustees,
expressing her concern for the preservation of historical houses in the
Village.
   President Woodbury thanked Ms. Kiphart for her letter and comments. He said
that there might be some additional things that the Council and its committees
should consider, but overall, Winnetka has been quite conscientious in
balancing private property rights and historical preservation. He referred to
the extreme angst throughout the community the last time historic preservation
came before the Council and noted that a lot of what is in place now grew out
of that controversy. He suggested that Winnetka educate itself on what other
communities are doing, but noted that most of the tools that are available
elsewhere are also available in Winnetka. He indicated that the Village is in
the early stages of studying some changes that might be made that could help
the Building Review Committee and the Landmark Preservation Commission find
better balance between their two missions and result in a better process. Mr.
Woodbury explained that any movement for historical preservation should come
from the community as a whole, perhaps through the Caucus, rather than from the
Council itself. He pointed out that many communities are facing the same issues
and that so far there are no reasonable tools available that present an
effective obstacle to the market forces that are driving tear-downs.
Trustee Abell added that this is a matter of concern to many Winnetkans, but
pointed out that it is not only builders who are driving the process, but also
residents who want the maximum that can be gained from the sale of their
property.
c)   Unmarked Patrol Vehicle. As noted earlier, this item has been removed from
the Agenda because the total purchase price fell below the $25,000 bid
threshold.
d)   Unmarked Utility Vehicle. Mr. McKee reviewed a request from the Police
Department to purchase one new utility vehicle through the Northwest Municipal
Conference Suburban Purchasing Cooperative.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the Police
Department to order a new utility vehicle for an amount not to exceed $30,000.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger,
Behles, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
e)   Kiosk Bids. Mr. Norkus summarized the history of this matter. He noted
that the preliminary pricing estimates for construction of the community event
kiosk as designed came in higher than the amount budgeted by the Council,
including the Chamber of Commerce’s contribution, so several possible cost-
saving alternatives were identified. He reviewed these alternatives and
indicated that in order to see some cost savings, staff recommended that
Alternative #1, removal of lattice work covering the rear of the kiosk costing
$1,440.00, be accepted.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees decided to proceed with the complete
original kiosk design.
President Woodbury asked Mr. Norkus when the kiosk was expected to be
completed. Mr. Norkus said that he hoped it would be finished before Christmas.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid for the
construction of the community event sign kiosk to Star Signs for an amount not
to exceed $29,820.00. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Ritchell.
f)   Extension of Banking Agreement with Harris Bank. Mr. McKee explained that
the Village consolidated banking services in 1998 to increase internal
controls, improve efficiency, and improve income earned on short term
investments. With the exception of accounts maintained pursuant to bond issues
or other specified requirements, the Village’s banking relationship was
consolidated with Harris Bank Winnetka. However, the Village’s current
agreement with Harris Bank will expire December 31, 2005, and to go through a
competitive bid process would involve many hours of staff time. He recommended
that the existing agreements be extended until December 31, 2010.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to extend the existing
agreements with Harris Bank Winnetka for consolidated banking services. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
g)   IDOT Jurisdictional Transfer of Tower Road. Mr. Saunders reviewed the
history of this matter, saying that the amended agreement will complete the
jurisdictional transfer process of Tower Road that was begun in 2002 and allow
IDOT to remit the agreed upon $200,000 to the Village.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to reauthorize the Village
President to sign the amended jurisdictional transfer agreement. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
10. Reports.
a)   President. President Woodbury encouraged residents to attend the Caucus
Town Meeting this coming Monday evening.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that Mr. Norkus and an ad hoc group had
been reworking the banner policy, which will go before the Design Review Board
on June 16. He invited any interested persons to attend that meeting.
Trustee Berger reported that she had taken a fascinating tour of RED Center.
c)   Village Attorney. Ms. Janega reported that the oral argument on the 411
Linden case had been postponed. In addition she said that she would be speaking
at an IICLE Conference tomorrow.
Village Manager. Mr. Williams asked Mr. Saunders to report on the MWRD project
beginning on Hibbard Road, which he did. Mr. Williams also reported Winnetka
had once again received the highest form of recognition for financial reporting
and accounting from the Governmental Finance Officers’ Association. Finally,
Manager Williams reported that the Village had partnered with SWANCC on an
electronic recycling project and collected 103,000 pounds of recyclable
electronics.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Alex Moore as the Library
Board’s representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee Abell, seconded by
Trustee Tucker, so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 10:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:20 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Tucker, Behles, and Berger. Absent: Trustee
Ritchell. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The meeting
adjourned at 11:21 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
June 14, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: July 19, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
14, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee David Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio,
Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus and approximately 20
persons in the audience.
2)   Affordable Housing: Joint Discussion with Plan Commission.
President Woodbury explained that during discussions on the drafting of the
Affordable Housing Plan, the Council intended that the Plan Commission work on
a Winnetka plan for the diversity of housing stock that is consistent with the
goals of the Comprehensive Plan. He asked the Commission to consider what the
community will be like as housing prices rise and make a plan that Winnetka can
be proud of that will consider the needs of seniors and young families. He
requested that the Commission report back to the Council by December 1st.
Plan Commission Chairperson, Gwen Trindl, reported that the Commissioners have
studied the minutes of the Council meetings on the affordable housing plan and
have discussed the way the Commission would proceed. She said the discussion
centered around a plan that would retain the diversity Winnetka has, a
statistical review of the housing districts and sizes, and a study of what
other communities have done. She said the Commission would prepare
recommendations for the Council and suggested that the Comprehensive Plan would
require updating as well.
Maureen Mitchel suggested that because of the number of commissioners whose
terms expire soon and the amount of material to read and digest, it might be
beneficial to have more members on the Plan Commission.
President Woodbury replied that the Council will pay attention to the needs of
the Plan Commission when filling vacancies and they will discuss adding more
members.
Trustee Tucker said that it is important for the Plan Commission to have a
group of members who are consistent. She then suggested the Commission conduct
community discussions and focus groups to give them a basis to form opinions
and provide as much public input as possible to the Council.
Trustee Eilers said that he was concerned and believes residents also think
that it is still an open issue after hearing comments at the Caucus meeting
that, in the future, home rule communities would be required to comply with the
Affordable Housing Act. He requested that Village Attorney Janega obtain the
attorney general’
Ms. Janega replied that she would look into this for the Council.
Midge Powell who said she was born and raised in Winnetka and is also a
realtor, was concerned about what is happening to affordable housing for our
own residents, the seniors and young people. She reported that, as of today,
131 houses in Winnetka are for sale for under $1 million, and14 of them are
condos; 50 houses are on the market for $1 - 2 million, 18 between $2 and $3
million, 11 between $3 and $5 million, and so on up to 1 property that is for
sale for $10 million, all of which excludes a lot of people who would like to
live in Winnetka.
President Woodbury replied that the Village is not able to control the market,
but what it does is important to this community and how the community addresses
this is what the Plan Commission has been charged with. He thanked the
Commission for accepting the responsibility.
3)   Planned Development.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio explained that members of the
Business Community Development Committee and Plan Commission had been studying
for a little over a year the possibility of planned development regulations to
encourage more imagination and creativity for building developments and
efficient use of land than is possible under conventional zoning regulations.
He said the following are the most significant issues for the Council’s
consideration:
   ·   A planned development use would only be permitted in the C1, C2, C2
Retail Overlay, B1 and B2 zoning districts;
   ·   The minimum size of the site would be 10,000 square feet;
   ·   The developer would be required to follow PD guidelines on any site
larger than 10,000 square feet;
   ·   Maximum building height would be 3 stories and 45 feet;
   ·   A two-step process for preliminary and final approval.
The Council discussed these issues, particularly whether it is appropriate to
retain an upper limit for building height or explore 4 stories on the post
office site to conform with buildings across Elm Street and whether the
estimated 18-month time frame for the two-step approval process was reasonable.
The Council also discussed the parcels that might be eligible for planned
development.
Manager Williams asked if it was the Committee’s intent that developers will be
able to choose whether or not they want to build through the planned
development regulations.
Mr. D’Onofrio responded that a parcel larger than 10,000 square feet would be
required to go through the planned development regulations.
Mr. Williams said he was also concerned about the loss of parking spaces under
current regulations as it would pertain to the Grand Foods site. He said that
the regulations now are that the Village provides parking for the first floor,
but the developer has to provide parking for any more stories.
Trustee Behles replied that if a development were to occur on that site today,
parking would be a concern, but he felt that there are built-in protections in
the planned development regulations, in that the developer would have to go
through all boards and commissions and must comply with the comprehensive plan
before approval is given. He said that currently a developer could take that
site and eliminate the parking without having to comply with design guidelines,
but in a planned development, this could be a basis for negotiation. He said
that the parking requirements in the zoning ordinance could be amended as well.
Ms. Janega said that zoning regulations that do not require parking for the
first floor were drafted for small developments with a small amount of retail
space. She agreed that this could be addressed in the zoning ordinance, but
stated that it could also be handled in the planned development regulations.
Barbara Aquilino commented that the Committee had discussed requiring
developers to pay for parking that they do not provide, but the Village would
still have to find another site for parking.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that a study would be done as part of the pre-application
process to find out who uses the parking lots and where to put cars if parking
were reduced.
Scott Crohn, 1056 Mt. Pleasant, architect, expressed his opinion that a planned
development should be optional; that as it has been drafted, it is a detriment
because the process increases the number of meetings a developer will have to
go through which will drive up the end cost; and, that incentives have to be in
height and density.
Jim Sayegh, 570 Lincoln, doesn’t think the preliminary approval period of 6
months would work unless it is essentially an approval and said that a
development should come before the Council sooner.
Ms. Aquilino replied that she sees the planned development as an improvement
because a builder will be able to look at the regulations and know what is
possible. She said that preliminary approval should take approximately 6 months
and after that, the project could move forward as long as there are no major
changes.
President Woodbury said he would not disagree with Mr. Crohn’s and Mr. Sayegh’s
comments, but that the process is that the development is reviewed by the
Village boards and commissions, whose members are representatives of the
community, and then comes before the Council.
Ms. Trindl expressed her concern about bringing a development to the Council
for approval before plans are finalized, saying that the Village might then be
held to something that they could not go back on. She believes that the Village
has to have an impact on any development on a larger site, because of the
nature of the Village, and the only way to do that is through planned
development. She added that other towns who have made the planned development
optional find that developers do not want to use it because they can get what
they want the old way.
Ms. Aquilino said the 18 month period was the outside limit of the time it
would take the developer to get everything together and the Committee felt this
was a reasonable amount of time.
Trustee Behles said the Committee also wanted to provide a friendlier way for a
builder to start out by meeting with Community Development to see if a project
is feasible.
Mitch Kiesler commented that the Committee discussed how to speed up the
process and felt that a builder deserves an answer within 6 to 8 months. He
said that if a builder has preliminary numbers when first meeting with
Community Development, then answers could be received more quickly.
Ms. Aquilino said that all of the Committee members felt they had a
responsibility to take their roles seriously, that they understood the process
and the roles they play in that process. She hoped that it would be a win-win
situation for everybody.
Ms. Janega said that it is intended that everything would be done during the
preliminary process and that the time between preliminary and final approval,
is to allow the property owner to put all of the pieces together. She further
said that final approval for a planned development tends to be a rather
perfunctory process and the timeline between the preliminary and final
approvals is driven by the developer moreso than by the process.
Trustee Eilers added that for those reasons, the timeframe was changed from 12
months to 18 months to give the developer more time before the preliminary
approval would expire.
Ms. Janega followed up on a comment made by Ms. Trindl, saying that it is
always tempting to bring a project to the Council for approval before it is
completely fleshed out, but when that is done, it becomes public record. She
said the Village has a fine professional development and planning staff that
should be put to use at the front end of development projects.
President Woodbury encouraged the Plan Commission to let the Council know about
any changes that may be necessary to the zoning ordinance as they finalize the
planned development regulations. He asked that the staff draft standards so
that they are in place before inquiries from developers are received about the
post office site. He also asked whether the Committee had discussed schools and
institutional uses.
Trustee Behles responded that the Committee had discussed institutional uses,
which are now allowed as a special use in the residential districts and
therefore, would revert to the underlying zoning. He said that, although it is
a valid point that planned development encourages design creativity and
flexibility for institutional uses, the Committee didn’t want residential
districts to be subject to planned development regulations. He said the
Committee thought it would be better to amend the zoning ordinance in this
case.
President Woodbury reported that one of the caucus surveys indicated strong
support for special relief from zoning requirements for institutional
development. Because the underlying zoning is single-family, neighbors expect
single family setbacks, etc and both sides think it is unfair.
Manager Williams commented that prior Councils had studied institutional
zoning, but decided to keep it under the special use process, which is similar
to a planned development.
President Woodbury ascertained that the consensus of the Council was to proceed
with the planned development regulations and that the Plan Commission would
hold the public hearing.
Mr. D’Onofrio stated that the outstanding issues of building height and parking
would be discussed by the Planned Development Committee within the next month
and he would forward the results of that discussion to Ms. Janega for the draft
ordinance.
4)   Streamline Zoning Variation Process.
Village Attorney Janega explained that a year ago, by amendment to the Zoning
Ordinance, the Zoning Board of Appeals was given more authority to make final
decisions regarding certain requests for variations, but the Council tabled
discussions about increasing the Director’s authority because the Village
wasn’t a home rule unit. She said that now that the Village is home rule, staff
would like the Council to consider this issue again. She explained that there
would still be notice to neighbors who would have an opportunity to obtain
information and the Director’s decisions could be appealed to the Zoning Board
of Appeals . She further explained that an appeal from a final Zoning Board
decision would then go to the courts. She added that, in effect, the Director’s
authority would be carved from the Zoning Board’s final decision-making
authority. She said that if these points make sense to the Council, then staff
would draft amendments to the Zoning Ordinance.
Manager Williams commented that a critical element to this is that the Director
would not have the final decision, so neighbors could appeal to the Zoning
Board, as could the petitioner.
Trustee Eilers asked if the Zoning Board decision could be appealed to the
Council.
Ms. Janega replied that she believes that would make the Zoning Board decision
advisory.
President Woodbury commented that the Council had wanted to give the Zoning
Board more discretion after looking at how few of their recommendations the
Council overturned.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that in the 14 months since the Zoning Board was given
more authority, zoning cases heard by the Council have been reduced from 75% to
45%. He added that of those zoning cases, 3 of those cases could have been
decided by the Director, if the Council were to give him the authority. He said
that the process would become routine for the staff and ease the process for
the applicant.
Trustee Tucker stated that she thinks we should go forward with this and asked
to have a review in a year to see how it’s going.
President Woodbury determined that the consensus of the Council was to move
forward and that the Council will hold the hearing on the amendments to the
Zoning Ordinance.
5)   Discussion: Possible Building Code Amendments.
Trustee Ritchell presented the Council with the following items for discussion
in relation to construction sites:
   ·   immediate sidewalk replacement
   ·   obstructing sidewalks or monopolizing street parking
   ·   noise
   ·   location of portable toilets
   ·   location of dumpsters and requiring that they be tarped
   ·   street and sidewalk cleanup
   ·   placement of construction and tree fences a minimum of 18 inches from
lot lines
   ·   repaving street cuts made for utilities instead of patching
   ·   property tax relief for residents within 150 feet of home construction
   ·   one-year moratorium on new development within 150 feet of a redeveloped
lot
The Council, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio and Public Works
Director Steve Saunders discussed the remedies already available under the
Village Code, under the permitting process or by Village policy and agreed to
have staff monitor areas under construction and enforce any violations
observed.
Mr. Saunders suggested that Community Development could compile a list of phone
numbers and departments to call for the resolution of complaints that could be
distributed to neighbors adjacent to construction sites. In response to Mr.
Ritchell’
Manager Williams asked Steve to inquire as to whether the repaving of curb cuts
could be added to the street bids, so that all were done once a year.
Mr. D’Onofrio responded to the concerns about construction noise and the
obstruction of sidewalks by saying that citations could be issued under the
appropriate sections of the Village Code, if the Police Department or Community
Development is notified or observes a violation. He said that requiring
construction fences to be 18 inches from lot lines may be possible as well as
requiring that gates on the fences not obstruct the sidewalk.
President Woodbury said, in response to the question about a moratorium on new
development within 150 feet of a redeveloped lot, that a neighbor could
currently petition to have construction stopped on a project if there are three
going on at once, but the result of this is that instead of getting
construction over with in a relatively short time, it is stretched out over a
longer time.
Ms. Janega responded to Mr. Ritchell’s question about tax relief by saying that
as soon as there is no building on the property, the property owner can apply
to the County for tax relief. She added that it is not likely that this
practice could be stopped because there is no inspection process for it by the
County.
President Woodbury suggested that rather than tax relief for neighbors, the
Village could charge an impact fee to recover the property tax revenue that is
lost in these cases.
6)   Harris Bank Lease
This item was removed from agenda.
7)   Backflow Prevention Policy.
Mr. Saunders asked the Council to consider a cost-sharing program that has been
adopted in some other communities, to assist homeowners who have older homes
with gravity sewers and experience flooded basements during heavy rainstorms or
from sewer backups. He said that the Village does what they can to reduce the
incidence of basement backups, but part of the cause is in the homeowner’s
lines. He said the back up devices to prevent flooding can cost up to $6,000
and some Village’s have agreed to help with this expense in order to do as much
as possible to prevent backups and flooding from occurring. He reported that
Glencoe has had the program for a couple of years and has had 3 applications
this year.
Manager Williams stated that he is very supportive of a program such as this,
but when it was presented years ago, the Council was split. He said the core
issue then was that the Village doesn’t do work on private property, but added
that the Village is part of the problem. He said that it will only fix the
problem of those people who have the devices, not everyone, so water may go to
neighboring homes.
Trustee Behles asked if there could be a pilot program for one year to see how
it goes. He didn’t think the Village should do construction on private
property, and instead, liked Glencoe’s idea that the homeowner brings an
estimate from a private contractor in for approval and that there is a set
amount that would be reimbursed by the Village. He said he saw no reason not to
be a part of the solution.
Ms. Janega said that the Village has no liability if it is only cost-sharing
and the Village is not doing the construction.
Trustee Tucker said that she would like to get more feedback from the
community. She asked where the money for this would be coming from and said
that even though Glencoe only has 3 applications a year, it could be many more
for Winnetka. She also asked if staff knew how many homes might be affected by
this.
Manager Williams replied that the money would come out of the sewer fund,. so
it would be a user fee, not an increase in taxes.
Trustee Behles added that as long as we limit the amount we reimburse, the
amount spent on the program should be contained.
Mr. Saunders said that he’d have to check the census to see how many homeowners
might be affected and added that funds for the program would be a line item in
the budget every year for the Council to consider and approve. He further said
that if Public Works receives a call for flooding, a supervisor will look at a
person’s basement to see what remedies there may be, but typically, a homeowner
doesn’t do anything and their basement continues to flood. He said, however,
that some homeowners have put in backup devices on their own.
Trustee Berger felt that feedback from the community would consist of the
opinions of the people who are affected and may not be a true survey. She said
she would like to find out what equipment is available and its cost. She
suggested that it could be offered to people who are the most vulnerable as a
trial and that Public Works could also offer free inspections to see which
homes would need a back up device. She thought that homeowners are probably not
aware of this issue.
Trustee Behles said that in terms of expenditures, it is a limited use, so he
would be in favor of it. He said, however, that he didn’t like the idea of
having the Village find the equipment or do any work on the property. He said
he thought it could be the cheapest, most effective resolution to the backflow
problem.
President Woodbury determined that Trustees Ritchell, Berger and Tucker were
against and that Trustees Abell, Behles and Eilers were in favor of the
program, and added that he would be in favor of the program as well.
Manager Williams said that the staff will get more financial data for the
Council to consider.
8)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
9)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:20 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
June 21, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 19, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, June
21, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Tom Eilers, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders and ten persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted. President Woodbury noted that the
previously announced discussion of the proposed zoning variance at 928 Elm
Street had been removed from tonight’s agenda at the request of the applicant.
4. Quorum.
a)   July 5, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   July 12, 2005 Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustees Tucker and Behles, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 7, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers suggested a small change to
the draft minutes. Trustee Ritchell then moved to adopt the minutes. Trustee
Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-8-2005 – Zoning Variation: 322 Sunset – Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance reflecting Council’s
discussion at the last meeting. She noted that the drafted ordinance includes
all four proposed variations and requires the affirmative vote of four
Trustees.
In response to a question from Trustee Abell, Manager Williams indicated that
New Trier Township High School had been contacted with regard to its unsightly
storage near the subject property.
Trustee Tucker remarked that she was only persuaded to vote in favor of the
garage door variation because the applicants are replicating the look of two,
single garage doors.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to amend the ordinance as
recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Eilers moved to approve the ordinance granting Front and Corner
Setback, Rear Yard Setback, Garage Door Width, and Obstructions in Required
Yard variations to permit the construction of three additions along the rear of
the existing house, plus a masonry wall along the south property line. Trustee
Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and
Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-9-2005 – Special Use Permit: R&R Music School, 896 Green
Bay Road - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft
ordinance, including conditions imposed as to hours of operation, use of
limited types of instruments, and imposition of a review period.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend the ordinance as
recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance M-9-2005
granting a Special Use Permit to allow a music teaching studio to be located in
commercial space located on the first floor of the subject property, which is
located in the C-2 Retail Overlay Zoning District. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2005 – Re-adopt Village Code Under Home Rule Powers –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance
amending and re-adopting the Village Code pursuant to the Village’s home rule
powers. She noted the ordinance contains an unequivocal finding that it is the
Council’s intention that the Village Code be a home rule exercise, with
references to the Village’s home rule powers added to Chapters 1.01 and 1.04.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the ordinance as
recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-2-
2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2005 – ISO Building & Fire Code Amendment – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the ordinance as
recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve Ordinance MC-3-
2005 updating the Village’s building codes, which are based on various model
building codes. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-10-2005 – Special Use Permit: 1140 Willow Rd. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Special Use Permit to make
improvements to Crow Island Woods in order to renovate the property into an
outdoor education center serving the schools and village residents. The
proposed improvements include conversion and expansion of an existing picnic
shelter into an interpretive center, updating restrooms to make them ADA
accessible, installation of a patio area and campfire circle, planting a
pioneer garden, construction of a new boardwalk, and new accessible pathways
and benches throughout.
He reported that the Design Review Board, the Plan Commission, and the Zoning
Board of Appeals had all considered the proposed plans. The Design Review Board
had generally positive comments but expressed some concern about some of the
details of the design. The Plan Commission unanimously recommended approval of
the Special Use Permit. The Zoning Board of Appeals recommended approval of the
Special Use Permit with the exception of the proposed boardwalk through the
northwest portion of the park. A second vote by the Zoning Board on the
proposed boardwalk failed to receive a favorable recommendation.
Trustee Abell asked for clarification about the objections to the proposed
boardwalk. Mr. D’Onofrio commented that several members were concerned about
possible impact on wetlands and felt there should be an environmental study
done. In addition, there were concerns raised about the possible visual impact
of the boardwalk.
Ms. McElroy pointed out that this property is not technically a wetland area
and said that the plans had been reviewed carefully by both the Illinois EPA
and the Army Corps of Engineers. She also noted that the Park District has
received a State grant for up to $250,000 to improve this property.
Rick Schram, the Park District’s landscape architect, reviewed the restoration
plans. With regard to the proposed boardwalk, he explained that it would be
surrounded by tall grasses and new plant materials, it would not be elevated,
there would be no railings, and it would not be a visual intrusion into the
park.
Ms. McElroy remarked that the Park District had met with the immediate
neighbors, many of whom were concerned about the possible addition of hard
surface. She indicated that although the original plan called for more than the
current plan, the Park District did not feel that more impermeable surface area
was necessary. She also mentioned that objections to the backless benches had
been addressed.
In response to concerns raised by Trustees about control of the fire pit, Ms.
McElroy said that the Park District intended to meet with the Fire Chief to
resolve some of the outstanding issues. She said that fire pit usage is planned
to be controlled by permit.
Manager Williams asked Ms. McElroy whether she foresaw a substantial increase
in park usage as a result of the renovations. She said that she did not.
Trustee Behles stated his belief that the proposed improvements are excellent
and well planned and that he is very much in favor of them, including the
boardwalk.
Trustees Berger, Eilers, Abell, and Ritchell agreed, as did Trustee Tucker who
suggested that landscaping and lighting plans be reviewed by the Design Review
Board.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-11-2005 – Zoning Variation: 746-748 Green Bay Rd. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot,
Front Setback, Side Yard Setback, Parking, Uses, and Nonconformities zoning
variations to permit the expansion of a nonconforming building and use in order
to improve the existing parking lot and build a new outdoor sales area on the
subject property. He explained that since the use and the buildings are both
considered to be legally nonconforming, variations to those sections of the
Zoning Ordinance need to be granted in order for the proposed improvements to
be made.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted unanimously
in favor of recommending the variations. He also noted that the Design Review
Board had made some comments regarding adding trees in the parkway, and
removing the first parking space so the fence could be set back and a planting
area could be added at the entrance.
The Trustees asked the applicant, Tim Welters, a number of questions about the
proposed changes. The applicant’s architect, Brian Reno, reviewed drawings of
the proposed project. He noted that the plans had been revised to reflect the
changes recommended by the Design Review Board.
Trustee Eilers pointed out that the variations are necessary in order to
accommodate improved parking that will alleviate some of the parking congestion
on Green Bay Road.
Manager Williams suggested that the Council could require that the final
landscaping and lighting plans go to the Design Review Board for Approval.
Trustee Ritchell asked whether the land could ever revert to its underlying
zoning of B-1. Attorney Janega indicated that the B-1 zoning remains, and the
use would have to revert to multi-family if the current business closed or was
abandoned. Another commercial use could not be put into place without coming to
the Council for relief.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Ordinance No. M-12-2005 – Landmark Designation: 455 Birch – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a recommendation by the Landmark Preservation Commission
that 455 Birch Street be designated as a local landmark. He reported that based
upon the adopted system for Evaluation of landmarks, the property received an
overall score of 89.6 points, resulting in a “Unique” rating.
Trustee Behles remarked that this house is one of the most unique houses in the
Village and is in pristine condition, making it a wonderful candidate for
landmark designation.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
h)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2005 – Forestry Technical Amendments - Introduction.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Forestry Commission had conducted an extensive
review of the provisions of the Village Code pertaining to forestry and trees
with an eye towards strengthening and improving the Village Code’s tree
protection provisions. After initial consideration by the Council, staff was
directed to separate the recommended technical changes from the major policy
questions raised by the Forestry Commission’s other recommendations. He
explained that as a result, the drafted ordinance contains proposed technical
amendments that would provide the Public Works staff with additional tools to
protect the Village’s trees. He emphasized that these amendments are technical
in nature and require no further Council or public discussion. Mr. Saunders
proceeded to enumerate the proposed amendments and said that the Public Works
staff believes that these will provide additional tree protection, particularly
during building construction projects.
After a brief discussion of some of the specific points, Trustee Behles,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury announced that in keeping with his promise at the Caucus
meeting, there will be a well-publicized public hearing when the Council
considers policy amendments to the Tree Preservation Code. In the meantime,
this ordinance will be returned to the Forestry Commission for consideration.
i)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2005 – BFPC Amendments - Introduction. Attorney Janega
explained that one of the benefits of the Village’s new home rule status is
that it affords the Village greater flexibility in testing and screening
applicants for entry level positions in the Police and Fire Departments than is
granted under the application of statutory provisions that provide for the
establishment of a Board of Fire and Police Commissioners (BFPC). Because the
Village Code provisions pertaining to the BFPC have been in place since long
before the successful home rule referendum, it is necessary to amend the Code
to confer broader powers on the BFPC and to authorize it to adopt rules that
might otherwise conflict with the applicable statutory provisions. The proposed
ordinance is a comprehensive amendment to the Village Code chapter pertaining
to the BFPC.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
j)   Resolution No. R-30-2005 – IDOT Maintenance Agreement - Adoption. Mr.
Saunders explained that pursuant to a 1995 agreement with the Illinois
Department of Transportation, the Village maintains a number of roadways that
are under state jurisdiction. Under this agreement, Village maintenance is
limited to sweeping, snow removal, pothole patching, litter control, and other
routine operational maintenance in return for reimbursement at a standard rate
per lane-mile. The new agreement that would be authorized by the proposed
resolution is a 10-year agreement with a year-by-year renewal, with an
escalation provision, from year to year.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
k)   Resolution No. R-31-2005 – Liquor License: Café 64 - Adoption. Ms. Janega
explained that this resolution provides for the authorization of a Class A
Liquor License to Café 64, formerly known as The Glass Onion Café, Inc. In
December 2004, an Order of Revocation was issued to The Glass Onion Café after
their State liquor license was revoked for failure to file returns and pay the
Retailers Occupational Tax. After payment of all outstanding fees to both the
State and the Village, the State has vacated its revocation order, and the
applicant has submitted all of the supporting documentation necessary to
continue operating the restaurant. She noted, however, that the restaurant will
now be known as "Café 64,” although it will be owned and operated by the same
individual who owned The Glass Onion. This resolution would officially delete
The Glass Onion from the liquor license appendix to Chapter 5.09, and will add
the license for Café 64.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the resolution
authorizing a Class A Liquor License to be issued to Café 64. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1417 and 1418. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1417 in the amount of $1,146,541.87 and Warrant List No. 1418 in the
amount of $290,090.18. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Ritchell,
Tucker, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 408 Winnetka Avenue. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request
for Intensity of Use of Lot and Front Setback variations to permit the
construction of a new, open front porch. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted against recommending that the variations be granted. Mr.
D’Onofrio noted that he had just today received a revised set of plans which he
had not had time to review; nor have these revisions been reviewed by the
Zoning Board of Appeals.
Mr. Williams asked whether there had been any comments from the neighbors. Mr.
D’Onofrio said that there had not been. Mr. Williams mentioned that several
years ago the Council had extensive discussions about encouraging front
porches.
The applicant, Michael Millar, and his architect, Scott Prestangen, explained
the need for the requested variations, saying that the applicant wants to
construct a functional, usable porch and at the same time address basement
water problems. Mr. Prestangen noted that the revised plans call for the front
porch to be pulled back 18 inches from the prior proposal. He pointed out that
the depth of the front yard on the subject property and its two neighboring
properties is unusually shallow.
Following a discussion about some of the specifics of the design, Trustee
Eilers said that he is inclined to look sympathetically on this request as
preservation is in the community’s best interests. He indicated that he
believes the applicant has tried to address the concerns raised by the Zoning
Board with the revised plans.
Trustee Behles indicated that he basically concurs with Trustee Eilers, but is
concerned about the lack of findings of practical hardship and unique
circumstances. He pointed out that zoning variations are to be driven by the
plight of the land, not the plight of the owner.
Trustee Tucker agreed with Trustee Behles that there is lack of practical
difficulties and undue hardship. She indicated that she would not be able to
cast a favorable vote on this request without justification, and in this case,
the request is based upon aesthetics and increased living space.
Attorney Janega pointed out that the standard of practical difficulties and
particular hardship is different for the Council than it is for the Zoning
Board, in that the Council acts in a different capacity than the Zoning Board.
In cases like this, the home was built many years ago and a nonconformity can’t
be corrected without tearing it down, which the Council could consider to be a
practical difficulty.
Trustee Berger indicated that she had no problem with the proposed variations.
She said that she was afraid if the matter is sent back to the ZBA, it will
just return to the Council without resolution. She voiced the opinion that the
Council has the opportunity with this decision to make a policy statement about
encouraging front porches and renovations.
Trustee Ritchell expressed his concern that the Zoning Board turned the request
down, given that the Village encourages front porches. He said that while he
would like to see the matter returned to the ZBA with the revised plans, he is
generally in favor of the renovation.
Trustee Abell agreed that the matter should be returned to the ZBA for further
consideration, although he indicated that he was favorably inclined toward
granting the variations.
Noting that the necessary four Trustees had expressed support for the proposed
variations, President Woodbury called for a motion to direct the Village
Attorney to prepare a draft ordinance for Council’s consideration. Trustee
Berger made the motion, seconded by Trustee Eilers. By voice vote, the motion
carried with four affirmative votes and two negative votes.
c)   Chamber of Commerce Request: Winnetka Days. Mr. Williams reviewed a
request from the Chamber of Commerce for permission to hold their annual
Winnetka Days event on Friday and Saturday, July 15 and 16. He noted that the
Chamber is asking that two street sections be closed to traffic this year.
Staff recommends approval.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the Chamber of
Commerce to conduct Winnetka Days as requested. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
d)   Bid: Winnetka Avenue Resurfacing Project. Manager Williams reviewed the
bids for the 2005 Winnetka Avenue Resurfacing project. He noted that the bids
are based on unit pricing and reported that staff recommends that the bid be
awarded to J.A. Johnson Paving Company for the bid amount of $146,707.20,
slightly under the engineer’s estimate of the cost for the project.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   President. President Woodbury reminded the Trustees and the audience about
the public meeting this coming Saturday, June 25, at 10:00 a.m. at Village Hall
to discuss redevelopment ideas for the Post office site.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the June BCDC meeting had been
devoted to participating as a focus group with the representatives from Lakota
with regard to the redevelopment of the Post Office site. He also noted that
the Chamber of Commerce would be conducting the annual sidewalk sale during the
upcoming Winnetka Days.
Trustee Tucker reported that the Plan Commission, together with the Design
Review Board and the Zoning Board of Appeals, would be meeting with Lakota
representatives to discuss the future of the Post Office site.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Bill Johnson to replace Gwen
Trindl on the Plan Commission upon the expiration of her term on August 1,
2005. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to approve the
proposed appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Maureen Mitchel as
Chairperson of the Plan Commission, effective August 1, 2005. Trustee Abell,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the proposed appointment. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 10:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:04 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Tucker, Behles, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Ritchell, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:05 p.m.
