Recording Secretary
July 5, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 19, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
5, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Tom Eilers, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez and 13 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   July 12, 2005 Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustees Tucker and Behles, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   July 19, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend. President Woodbury said that he did not
expect to be present.
5. Village Council Minutes.
None.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-10-2005 – Special Use Permit: 1140 Willow Rd. - Adoption.
President Woodbury asked Fire Chief Scott Smith to address concerns raised at
the June 21 Council meeting about the Park District’s request for a campfire
circle and fire pit, and Attorney Janega distributed revised amended language
pertaining to conditions limiting the use thereof.
Chief Smith reported that he had met with the Park District to discuss numerous
issues, including conditions and restrictions on the use of the campfire circle
and the type of fuel used. He explained that Class A combustibles, which
include wood, charcoal, paper, brush, etc., can create smoke, which in turn,
may become a nuisance. Of greater concern is the fact that they could get out
of control and cause a grass fire that could spread rapidly. In addition, the
necessary State and county permitting process for the use of Class A
combustibles in an outside pit is rigorous and is required for every occasion.
The Chief said that the revised language distributed by Ms. Janega reflects a
compromise reached with the Park District for the installation and use of the
fire pit. It expressly prohibits the use of Class A combustibles and calls for
the use of artificial logs fueled by natural gas, access to which is controlled
by the Park District.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, indicated that should the Park Board
decide not to use the gas logs in the friendship circle, the District would not
permit fires of any kind in that area. She reminded the Trustees that there is
a fireplace in the shelter in which logs and other similar materials may be
burned.
Attorney Janega reviewed additional proposed amendments to the draft ordinance,
which modify the Special Use Permit to require that backs be added on some of
the proposed benches and that the final landscape and lighting plans be
reviewed by the Design Review Board. She noted that the Village Council would
retain the final authority to decide in the case of a disagreement between the
DRB and the Park District. Finally, Ms. Janega noted that the approval of the
proposed boardwalk would require the affirmative vote of four trustees, since
the Zoning Board of Appeals did not recommend its approval.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the ordinance as
outlined by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-11-2005 – Zoning Variation: 746-748 Green Bay Road –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance,
which include expansion of the recitals, withdrawal of the 8-ft. fence,
inclusion of the April 25 revised site plan, and language requiring final
landscape and lighting review by the Design Review Board, reserving the final
decision for the Village Council in the event agreement cannot be reached.
The applicant, Tim Welter, indicated that he is in agreement with all of the
amendments.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the ordinance as
outlined by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2005 – BFPC Amendments - Adoption. Stephen Presser, a
former Trustee and member of the Board of Fire and Police Commissioners, urged
the Council to pass these amendments, saying that they would give the Board
more flexibility in examining and hiring new officers. Trustee Abell, seconded
by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-29-2005 – Cook County Mobil Communications Agreement –
Adoption. Chief DeLopez explained that the Village of Winnetka is one of
thirty-four municipalities to receive Department of Homeland Security funding
for certain communications equipment to be used in Project SHIELD, a county-
wide interoperable communication system for first responders. This will enable
police, fire, and paramedics from Chicago and the suburbs to communicate with
each other regardless of what radio equipment they use. This will enhance the
safety and security of both the Village and the entire area.
Because the Project involves the use of proprietary technical information and
the contract documents refer to confidential security measures, minimal
documentation was presented to the Council at its Study Session on April 12. As
a result, several issues were unresolved, including insurance and
confidentiality. He reported that most of these matters have now been resolved,
and that the documents have been reviewed by the Village Attorney. He reported
that staff recommends the Council approve and authorize execution of the Cook
County Video and Data Communications Network Participation Agreement,
substantially in the form attached to the Agenda materials.
Attorney Janega added that the confidential documents are available for
inspection by the Trustees and stated that she was satisfied during her review
that the Village is adequately protected because the Village is covered by the
same indemnification as the County.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the Resolution R-
29-2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Citizen Letter: Concerns about Coyotes. Bill Glastris, 990 Sheridan Road,
voiced his concerns about the increasing presence of coyotes in Winnetka,
particularly near the ravines. Ciceil Hales, 1000 Sheridan Rd., Emanuel
Tabachnik, 930 Fisher Lane., Karen Zucker, 1060 Fisher Crescent, Erica Kreisa,
582 Provident, Ruth Jennings, 903 Elm, and Jack Keller, 20 Old Green Bay Rd.,
all echoed Mr. Glastris’ comments.
Chief DeLopez said he appreciates residents’ concerns, but pointed out that the
problem is not unique to Winnetka. At present, there is not a definitive
response to address the matter. While the Police Department is conscious of the
issue and works to provide a safe environment, its response is limited by
environmental regulations. He said that either private individuals or the
Village could contract with State-licensed trappers, but to do so is very
expensive and there is no guarantee of results. He pointed out that baited
traps can pose a risk to pets and small children. In addition, there are
restrictions as to where the traps can be set. Coyotes are very intelligent
creatures, and randomly tracking them has proven unsuccessful. Chief DeLopez
said that only under strict, defined emergency circumstances could an officer
shoot a coyote.
Manager Williams distributed an article from Chicago Wilderness Magazine
regarding the presence of coyotes in urban areas. According to the article only
16 cases of coyotes attacking humans have been documented in a 30-year period.
Trustee Eilers asked what options are available. Chief DeLopez said that if an
animal exhibits signs of aggressiveness, a professional trapper can be called.
However, the real problem animal must be identified.
Trustee Behles asked whether there is any data that would suggest what the
population of coyotes is in this area. Manager Williams responded that
according to the article, the population has remained much the same, but the
animals are becoming less afraid of people, so folks are seeing more of them.
Trustee Abell remarked that there is a fine line between a nuisance and a
threat, or what is annoying and what is dangerous.
Trustee Tucker suggested that the Police Department continue to document
reports and continue to explore what can be done to avert any potential hazard.
President Woodbury reminded the audience that there will be other, contrary
voices in the community who would support leaving wildlife alone. He said that
if the Trustees are to be persuaded to take any action, the direction needs to
come from the community as a whole.
Trustee Eilers asked that staff research what authority the Village may have,
and under what specific circumstances.
b)   Policy Direction: 314 Walnut. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum
Building Size and Front and Corner Yard Setback variations to permit a newly
constructed home to have a gross floor area exceeding the maximum allowable and
reduced setbacks for a detached garage. He noted that the proposed new house
would sit on a lot that fronts three public streets. As a consequence, for
zoning purposes, the home has three front yards, and no rear yard. Because
there is no rear yard, no bonus was given for the detached garage. Mr.
D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had taken two separate
votes on the variation requests and voted to recommend denial of the GFA
variation and approval of the setback variation requests for the detached
garage.
The applicant, Tamara Kasey, spoke in support of her request. She said that
they wish to keep the house and the garage in the same place as they are now
located.
Trustee Berger asked the applicant whether the proposed footprint would exactly
match the existing footprint. Mrs. Kasey said that it would not, as the house
will now go from two bedrooms to four.
Neighbors Richard Warnecke, 565, Orchard Ln., and Dave Bender, 561 Orchard,
expressed objections to the proposed plans based on the fact that this is a
new, large house in a neighborhood of smaller homes, and should be built within
the existing zoning guidelines in such a manner as to fit into the environment
and match the character of the neighborhood.
Manager Williams confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the Zoning Ordinance clearly
defines the rules for a multi-street lot, so to argue that the three street
frontages is a hardship is really arguing that the Zoning Ordinance is the
hardship.
Trustee Behles complimented the applicant on the proposed design and indicated
appreciation for the complexity of working with the lot's irregular shape. He
agreed with the Zoning Board that a variation with regard to the setback for
the garage is appropriate, given the shape of the lot. However, he said that he
cannot support the GFA variation because there is no hardship given that the
applicant is starting with a blank, empty lot.
The other Trustees agreed with Trustee Behles.
Attorney Janega reviewed the applicant’s options, after which Ms. Kasey asked
that the matter be tabled.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to table the matter. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1419 and 1420. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After he commented on a number of the items, Trustee Tucker moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1419 in the amount of $560,901.32 and Warrant List No.
1420 in the amount of $173,006.03. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
d)   Quarterly Report: Status of Capital Improvements. Mr. Williams reviewed
the current status of Village capital improvements projects for the first
quarter of the fiscal year.
e)   Bid 05-006: 2005 Street Rehabilitation. Manager Williams reviewed the bids
for the Village’s standard annual street resurfacing and rehabilitation
project. He noted that the bids are based on unit pricing and reported that
staff recommends that the bid be awarded to J. Curran Contracting Company for
the bid amount of $573,542.30. He added that since the bid price is under the
budgeted amount, he expects that staff will be bringing this back to the
Council with a proposal to add one more street to the project.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid to J.
Curran Contracting for $573,542.30 based on unit pricing. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reminded the audience that the Sidewalk Sale is
coming up on July 15-16, and Trustee Behles announced that local design
professionals would be meeting tomorrow night with representatives from the
Lakota Group to discuss the redevelopment of the Post Office site.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Midge Powell to replace Bob
Horne on the Plan Commission effective August 1, 2005. Trustee Abell, seconded
by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the proposed appointment. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Bill Shiel to a full
three-year term on the Business Community Development Commission to replace
Howard Smith, effective immediately. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the proposed appointment. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:53 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:30 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Tucker, Behles, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger , seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Ritchell, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
July 12, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: August 2, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
12, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell. Absent: Trustees Ken
Behles and Jessica Tucker. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio,
Fire Chief Scott Smith and approximately two persons in the audience. Zoning
Board members Joe Adams, Suzanne Timble, Tom Hermanson, Molly Lien and Jane
Dowding were also in attendance.
2)   Discussion – Zoning Board of Appeals.
President Woodbury stated that the purpose of this meeting was to open
communications between the Zoning Board and the Council and encouraged the
members of the Zoning Board to discuss any issues they may have or challenges
they face.
ZBA members and the Council discussed where to find information on the
Council’s decisions on zoning cases, the effect of the amendments to the Zoning
Ordinance that gave the ZBA more decision-making authority, changes to the
zoning ordinance under home rule, and how the zoning standards are applied
under certain circumstances.
3)   Semi-Annual Report: ZBA Variations.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio reported that, as a result of the
amendments to the Zoning Ordinance in April 2004, the number of zoning cases
for the Council’s consideration had been reduced, saving time for the Council,
the Village Attorney, and the applicants whose variations can be approved
approximately 8 weeks sooner than before the changes were made. He added that,
in comparing last year with the previous 4 years, he found that 75% of cases
under the old structure were submitted to the Council, and 45% of them have
gone to the Council since the amendments. He reported that 40% of the
variations requested this past year have been for front and side yard setback
variations and another 20% were for intensity of use of lot. He said his
department is also seeing a reduction in the overall number of cases, which he
attributed to an increase in the number of instances in which either builders
were asked to find confirming alternatives or variations were not allowed. In
answer to a question from Mr. Williams, Mr. D’Onofrio responded that he
believes the changes in the zoning regulations have achieved the intended
results in that he is seeing better design and has not found any unintended
consequences. He added that there has been a decrease in the number of flat
roof designs, so it appears people are taking advantage of the attic allowance.
Trustee Berger complimented Mr. D’Onofrio on the job he and his staff are
doing, saying that she had served on the ZBA with him and his predecessor and
has noticed a difference in that his Department is doing a good job in
screening the variation requests, which results in fewer issues.
President Woodbury also thanked the ZBA for their work on behalf of the Council
who is very appreciative.
4)   Fire Training Facility.
Chief Smith advised the Council that specifications for an addition to the fire
training tower facility had been developed and sent to potential vendors. He
said that he had received responses to the request for proposal from Fire
Facilities and WHP, whose facilities in Lafayette, Indiana and Skokie were
visited by Deputy Chief Laureys and Captain Trausch. After researching the
construction of the facilities built by each of these vendors, he said that it
is staff’s recommendation to use WHP, whose buildings they believe are more
durable as well as safer than those of Fire Facilities.
Deputy Chief Laureys explained how each of the vendors constructs their
facilities and how the construction relates to the training needs of the Fire
Department.
Manager Williams said he had asked the Fire Department to find out which vendor
builds the best facility, not just the least expensive, and after they brought
their research to him, it was clear that a facility constructed by WHP would
best fit the needs of the Department. Mr. Williams emphasized the need to have
trained people at the scene of a fire because it is essential to minimize risk.
He added that due to expected retirements in the next few years, the training
of new employees will be very important and that although training has been
significantly improved over the last few years, this is an opportunity to make
it even better. He said that because of cost savings on other projects this
year, the funds for the training facilities would come out of the capital
improvements budget.
The Trustees asked several questions about training facilities in nearby
communities and at NIPSTA and briefly discussed the possibility of financial
support from other communities who may be using our facilities.
President Woodbury commented that the training facility is an investment that
will last for 20 years or more and it is important that when there is a fire,
our Fire Department is trained well enough that it will be as if fires were an
every day occurrence. He then determined that the consensus of the Council was
to move forward with the project.
5)   Building Code Regulations.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio addressed the issues that Trustee
Ritchell raised at the June 14th study session with respect to building
construction regulations. He informed the Council of remedies that already
exist under the Village Code for construction site management, sidewalk
replacement, parking, noise, portable toilets, dumpsters, construction fencing
and street and sidewalk cleanup.
After discussing the options available under the Code, the Council and Mr.
D’Onofrio made the following recommendations:
   ·   amend site management requirements to require that following the
installation of utilities, the sidewalk must be restored with a hard surface;
   ·   increase enforcement of parking violations and issue stop work orders if
necessary;
   ·   inspect construction sites more frequently, depending on constraints of
staff’s time;
   ·   include location of portable toilets on the site plan;
   ·   amend requirements to include that dumpsters must be tarped;
   ·   fill vacant staff code enforcement position.
President Woodbury concluded that it is less to do with the individual’s
actions and more the way the construction site is managed by the general
contractor. He added that if the Village begins licensing contractors, site
management should improve because the status of their license would be at risk
if they are not in compliance.
6)   Executive Session. No executive session was held.
7)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:35 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
July 19, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 2, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, July
19, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem David Abell called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Tom Eilers, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: President Edmund Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Director
of Public Works Steven Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee and five persons in
the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Abell led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   August 2, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Mr. Abell, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   August 9, 2005 Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustees Tucker, Ritchell, and Abell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 14 2005, Study Session. Trustee Behles moved to adopt the minutes.
Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   June 21, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   July 5, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers moved to adopt the minutes.
Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-12-2005 – Landmark Designation: 455 Birch. - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio briefly reviewed this matter that was introduced at the June 21
Council meeting. He also presented slides showing the subject property. He
noted that since its introduction, the petitioner has requested that the
property become a certified landmark, meaning that once certified, any changes
to the building require approval by the Landmark Preservation Commission.
Language to this effect has been incorporated into the amended ordinance.
Trustee Behles asked whether the landmark designation would extend to the
grounds and the fencing. Mr. D’Onofrio said that it would.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amended language and explained some of
the tax benefits that are available to owners of certified landmarks.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury. [Note:
Because President Pro Tem Abell also serves as Acting Village President and
signs ordinances, resolutions and other instruments in the absence of the
Village President, he does not vote on final matters that require the Village
President's signature.]
b)   Ordinance No. M-13-2005 – Zoning Variation: 408 Winnetka Ave. –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio responded to two concerns raised by the Village
Council during its discussion of this matter on June 21. He reported that the
front setback of the house to the west is 24 feet, and that the existing canopy
has a front setback of 28.2 feet.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amended language.
Trustee Tucker said that although she was originally opposed to this request
because she did not believe there was an actual practical difficulty, upon
further reflection she believes that deference should be given to the older
home based on the fact that it was built in 1908, prior to the existence of any
Zoning Ordinance. She suggested that the Village look into developing some sort
of sliding scale to make it easier for older homes to obtain variations
necessary to encourage rehabilitation.
Trustee Behles indicated that although he, too, was initially opposed, he
believes that the request, as modified, is modest and appropriate, and that it
is in keeping with what the Village is trying to promote. He said he would
rather see this kind of development than to see the house removed and replaced.
Trustee Ritchell concurred with Trustees Tucker and Behles, saying that this is
a fine example of preservation efforts.
Trustee Eilers remarked that preservation is critical to the fabric of this
community and indicated that he was fully in support of this variation request.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-14-2005 – Tower Road Jurisdictional Transfer –
Introduction (Waived) and Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that when the
Village completed the rehabilitation of Tower Road from Green Bay Road to
Forestway Drive in 2002, IDOT agreed to fund $200,000 of the project in return
for the Village’s assuming jurisdiction of the roadway. At that time the
Village approved an ordinance, M-27-2002, officially adding this roadway to the
municipal street system. However, despite the proper submittal of documents to
IDOT, the transfer was never processed. After an exchange of correspondence and
documents with IDOT, the Village has submitted a new agreement to IDOT. In
addition, the Village will submit ordinance M-14-2005, restating the provisions
of the original ordinance. Approval of this ordinance should complete the
jurisdictional transfer process and allow IDOT to remit the agreed-upon
$200,000 to the Village.
Attorney Janega reviewed the ordinance, noting one technical correction in the
draft. She also suggested that the Council, with unanimous consent, could waive
the first reading of this matter and proceed directly to adoption.
Manager Williams urged the Trustees to waive the first reading so that Mr.
Saunders could proceed immediately to finalize the transaction.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the draft
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to waive the first reading
and introduction of this ordinance.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury. [Note:
Because President Pro Tem Abell also serves as Acting Village President and
signs ordinances, resolutions and other instruments in the absence of the
Village President, he does not vote on final matters that require the Village
President's signature.]
d)   Resolution No. R-32-2005 – Intergovernmental Agreement: Northeastern
Illinois Regional Crime Lab – Adoption. Chief DeLopez explained that the Board
of the Northern Illinois Police Crime Laboratory (NIPCL), which provides
forensic and training services for the Village, has decided to change its
structure from a non-profit corporation to an inter-governmental cooperative.
This affords the organization certain advantages available to such
cooperatives. In addition, the name will be changed to the Northeastern
Illinois Regional Crime Lab. He reported that there will be no change in costs
or services to the Village. In order to facilitate this change, all of the
member communities must execute the same Intergovernmental Agreement. The
subject Resolution authorizes the Village of Winnetka to sign the necessary
documents.
Chief DeLopez responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury. [Note:
Because President Pro Tem Abell also serves as Acting Village President and
signs ordinances, resolutions and other instruments in the absence of the
Village President, he does not vote on final matters that require the Village
President's signature.]
7. Public Comment and Questions.
Senator Jeff Schoenberg appeared before the Trustees to tell them that his
office was available to help the Village in whatever way it can.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1421 and 1422. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1421 in the amount of $785,925.84 and Warrant List No. 1422 in the
amount of $389,421.57. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and
Berger. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
b)   Fire Training Tower. Chief Smith briefly reviewed the details of the
proposed Fire Training Tower that was discussed at the July 12 Study Session.
Trustee Behles remarked that the matrix comparing the two products clearly
shows that the WHP building is a qualitatively better structure.
Mr. Saunders responded to questions from the Trustees regarding the excavation
process and expenses.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to issue a Purchase Order to WHP Training Towers in the amount of
$234,358, contingent upon the Village securing the necessary Illinois EPA
permit. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell.
Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
c)   Local and Long Distance Phone Service. Mr. McKee reviewed the history of
the Village’s phone service and his interviews with prospective consultants
with expertise in the field. He reported that based upon his research, he
recommends that the Village retain the firm of Indaplex to (1) advise the
Village on how best to resolve outstanding issues with SBC, and (2) obtain
current market rates for phone service, as the Village’s current phone carrier
contract expires in October.
Mr. McKee reported that based upon its study of the Village’s records,
discussions with SBC, and an understanding of currently available local and
long-distance rates, Indaplex recommends that the Village enter into a three-
year contract with SBC. Indaplex projects that this will reduce the Village’s
annual local and long-distance costs as well as SBC’s penalties.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the Finance
Director to implement the Indaplex report recommendations, switching phone
carrier service for the Village. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles,
Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
d)   Bid: Forest Glen Pillars. Mr. Saunders explained that during the spring of
2003, the Village of Winnetka Public Works Department removed four previously
existing brick pillars, lights, and walls that marked the entrances to the
Forest Glen neighborhood off Tower Road. The pillars were removed because they
had become unsafe, both structurally and with regard to their location. The
pillars were rebuilt, minus the lights and walls, during the summer of 2003,
after receiving approval from the design Review Board. The DRB approved re-
building the pillars using pre-existing brick; however, the brick actually used
was a mixture of architectural brick from the exterior and common brick from
the interior. The resulting appearance was not deemed acceptable to the
neighbors or the DRB. Pursuant to prior direction of the Council, Village staff
has re-designed the project using entirely new brick and based upon further
resident input, has recreated the lighting and walls that were previously
present. He said that staff has attempted to match as closely as possible the
previously existing exterior brick and present sample materials for inspection.
Mr. Saunders reported that at its June 16 meeting the DRB issued favorable
comment on the proposed design.
Mr. Saunders recommended that the bid for the project be awarded to R. Rudnick
& Company in the amount of $58,910.
Trustee Eilers questioned the Village’s policy about replacing these pillars on
public property which really benefit only a few neighborhoods, saying that the
expenditure seems disproportionate for the minimal public benefit.
Trustee Berger concurred. She said that this Council has an opportunity to set
a new policy and precedent.
Mr. Saunders explained that these and other similar entrance pillars have
fallen to the Village to maintain. Most of them were constructed when their
subdivisions were originally laid out, and they were built on public property.
In addition, most are for neighborhoods where the streets are public and there
is no functional homeowners’ association.
Manager Williams explained that the pillars were deteriorating and posed a
potential liability to the Village. He said that although the expenditure is
ultimately the Council’s decision, it has been represented to the neighborhood
that the Village will do the “right” thing.
Trustee Behles said since the Village made a commitment to restore the entrance
gate, he feels the Village should proceed in this situation. He reported that
this matter had come before the Design Review Board during his tenure and
briefly reviewed the history. He added that although Mr. Saunders has worked
diligently to temper the design of the replacement pillars to make them less
expensive, no one realized how ardently the residents felt about them and how
much they wanted to keep them the same as they were. He added, however, that
given the fact that some of these pillars are located on public property and,
therefore, present maintenance and liability issues for the Village, the
decision to on how to handle them is a matter of public policy.
Trustee Ritchell agreed with Trustees Eilers and Berger, and suggested that the
Council hold off awarding the bid pending further discussion. He expressed
concern about setting a precedent.
Trustee Tucker indicated that she believes that since the Village has already
tried once to rebuild the pillars, it would not be appropriate to back out of
the project at this point. She said she was in favor of this expense for this
particular situation, but was not necessarily in favor of similar expenses on a
going-forward basis.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to table the matter. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Ritchell mentioned that he had attended the recent
meeting of the Historical Society. He reported that the Society’s Gala
fundraising event had been very successful.
b)   Manager. Mr. Williams reported that the Insurance Services Office had
notified the Village that the Community Development Department had been rated
Class 4.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President Pro
Tem Abell, Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Behles, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
President Woodbury. Also present Village Manager Doug Williams and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Ritchell, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
The meeting adjourned at 9:46 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
August 2, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 16, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
2, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Tom Eilers, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee David Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director
of Public Works Steven Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff
and fourteen persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   August 9, 2005 Study Session. President Woodbury announced that this
meeting had been cancelled.
b)   August 16, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustees Eilers and Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   September 6, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 12, 2005, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the minutes.
Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   July 19, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles moved to adopt the minutes.
Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-13-2005 – Zoning Variation: 408 Winnetka Ave. - Adoption.
There being no additional comments, Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to adopt this ordinance granting two zoning variations to
permit the construction of a front porch. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
b)   Ordinance No. M-16-2005 – Zoning Variation: 550 Sheridan Rd. –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front and Corner Yard
Setback, Building Line Articulation, and Garage variations to permit the
construction of two one-story additions, expansion of upper floor space, and
rebuilding of an attached garage of the same dimensions as the existing garage.
He noted that the applicant had withdrawn a request for a 14-foot wide garage
door and reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 7-0 to recommend
approval of the variations.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the existing yard variations
are not being extended, and Trustee Tucker confirmed that the garage footprint
will remain the same.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-17-2005 – Zoning Variation: 652 Lincoln – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot, Side Yard
Setback, and Building Line Articulation variations to permit the construction
of a one-story addition and an open deck. He reported that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 7-0 to recommend approval of the variations.
The applicant’s architect, Charles Lee, explained the details of the request
and responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-04-2005 – Tree Preservation Ordinance Amendments – Public
Hearing. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing on the proposed technical
amendments to the Tree Preservation Ordinance giving the Public Works staff
additional tools and provisions to protect the Village’s trees. This ordinance
was introduced at the June 21st Council meeting. .
Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed the history of the issue and enumerated the five
major areas of improvement to the ordinance. He said that the revised draft
contains many revisions suggested by the Forestry Commission, although some
were not included as they were deemed to have broader policy implications and
will be brought to the Council at another time. He emphasized that the proposed
language codifies what the Village Forester already does and will aid
enforcement. He recommended adoption of the revised ordinance.
Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the revised draft. Ms.
Janega noted that if this ordinance is adopted, the Village would need to adopt
a new Building and Fee Resolution, Resolution R-33-2005, increasing the tree
deposit and clarifying tree plan review fees.
Jim Stier, the Village Forester, responded to questions from the Trustees.
Mary Hill, 271 Linden, Chair of the Forestry Commission, and committee members
Bernie Hammer, 1445 Tower, and Susie Shabica, 326 Ridge, urged the Council to
pass the proposed ordinance.
President Woodbury thanked the Forestry Commission for their time, efforts and
careful stewardship to the Village. Referring to the proposed judgment lien, he
remarked that he believes that all penalties throughout the Code should be
balanced.
Trustee Eilers expressed some concern about holding a public hearing during the
month of August when many Village residents are absent and suggested tabling
the matter until the September 6th Council meeting.
Trustee Behles disagreed. He noted that the changes proposed in this ordinance
are primarily technical in nature and are designed to help staff implement
already existing policies; any proposed substantive issues are slated to be
discussed at a public hearing slated for a later date. He pointed out that the
Village is in the height of the construction season and, therefore, any delay
is counterproductive. He suggested immediate passage of the ordinance.
Trustees Tucker and Berger were supportive of Trustee Eilers’ comments.
Forestry Commission members Bernie Hammer and John Weber argued against
postponing a final decision on the matter.
After further discussion, President Woodbury continued discussion of the matter
until the September 6th Council meeting.
e)   Resolution R-33-2005 – Tree Deposits. This matter is to be considered in
conjunction with MC-04-20025.
f)   Ordinance No. M-15-2005 – Disposal of Surplus Equipment: Transformers –
Introduction. Mr. McInturff reviewed this ordinance that would declare certain
transformers as surplus and authorize the Water & Electric Department to
dispose of them. He explained to the Trustees several of the ways in which
transformers become surplus property and said that storing transformers that
are no longer useful to the electric system can be costly for a variety of
reasons.
Manager Williams noted that this draft will be revised to permit the Village to
sell the surplus transformers to other municipalities.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Rezoning Request: 1190 Hamptondale. Mr. Norkus reviewed a request to
rezone the subject property which is currently zoned as two different zoning
classifications, R-4 and R-2. He noted that although the existing lot is a
single zoning lot, its current dual zoning classification is attributed to the
fact that prior to 1959 reconfiguration of property boundaries did not require
subdivision plat approval. Therefore, it is likely that the boundaries of the
subject parcel were shifted prior to the construction of the existing
residence. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the Community Development Department
has maintained that absent a rezoning, the parcel may not be treated as a
single lot for zoning purposes because of the disparate standards that apply to
the two differing districts and the inability to simply choose one set of
standards over the other.
Mr. Norkus went on to suggest that the requested rezoning to the R-4 Single
Family Residential district is appropriate for the property because it would be
consistent with its immediate neighbors, whereas an R-2 designation would make
the property the only such parcel on its block. As rezoning requests are
subject to conducting a public hearing, the Village Council must determine the
body to hold that public hearing. Ordinarily such a hearing would be heard by
the Plan Commission, but due to the relatively minor, technical nature of the
requested map amendment, he recommended that the hearing be conducted by the
Village Council.
President Woodbury noted that this application also affects 1185 Laurel and
asked whether it would remain R-2. Mr. Norkus indicated that he expected that
it would and said that although the subject request only pertains to 1190
Hamptondale, it makes sense to clean up both matters at this time.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to designate the Village
Council as the Village body to conduct the public hearing on this rezoning
request. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The Public Hearing was
set for September 6th.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1423 and 1424. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1423 in the amount of $1,056,479.36 and Warrant List No. 1424 in the
amount of $677,158.86. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and
Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers announced that the Business Community Development
Commission now meets at 7:30 a.m. on the first Thursday of each month. He
invited all interested persons to attend these meetings.
b)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that a copy of the appellate court
opinion on the 411 Linden affirming the judgment in favor of the Village and
the Historical Society was available in the Village Manager’s Office.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:08 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Eilers, Tucker, Behles, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
Trustee Abell. Also present Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Ritchell, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The meeting
adjourned at 10:09 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
August 16, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 6, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
16, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, and Herb Ritchell. Absent: Trustees Tom
Eilers and Jessica Tucker. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Fire Chief Scott Smith, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and one person in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   September 6, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   September 13, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 2, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger moved to adopt the
minutes. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-16-2005 – Zoning Variation: 550 Sheridan Rd. – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the ordinance granting Corner
Yard Setback, Building Line Articulation, and Garage variations to permit the
construction of two one-story additions, expansion of upper floor space, and
rebuilding of an attached garage of the same dimensions as the existing garage.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to amend the ordinance as
recommended by Ms. Janega. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Abell, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and
Tucker.
b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2005 – Zoning Variation: 652 Lincoln – Adoption. There
being no amendments or comments, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell,
moved to adopt the ordinance granting Intensity of Use of Lot, Side Yard
Setback, and Building Line Articulation variations to permit the construction
of a one-story addition and an open deck. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Abell, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Eilers and Tucker.
c)   Ordinance No. M-15-2005 – Disposal of Surplus Equipment: Transformers –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed technical amendment to the draft
ordinance declaring certain transformers as surplus and authorizing the Village
to dispose of them within the framework of the realities of the marketplace.
Manager Williams noted that this would permit the Village to transfer them to
any other Illinois municipal electric utility essentially at wholesale. He
reported that Winnetka had already had one inquiry from Breeze, Illinois.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the ordinance as
proposed. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Ritchell,
Behles, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and Tucker.
d)   Ordinance No. M-18-2005 – Disposal of Personal Property – Introduction.
Mr. Williams explained that this ordinance would authorize the Village to
dispose of ten pieces of equipment owned by the Village that have exceeded
their useful life and are being replaced. Most of the items will be taken to
the NWMC auction.
Attorney Janega noted that although the draft ordinance incorporates home rule
recitals, the body of the ordinance does not reflect similar updates, so the
ordinance will need to be amended prior to adoption.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2005 – Streamlining the Zoning Variation Process –
Public Hearing & Introduction. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing and
said that, although this would begin the process, the matter would be continued
to the September 6th meeting to accommodate residents who might be out of town.
Ms. Janega explained that, when the Council enacted Ordinance MC-2-2004, which
amends various aspects of the zoning variation process to expedite processing,
the Community Development staff requested that the Council consider
establishing an administrative process whereby certain minor zoning variations
could be granted. However, because the Village was not a home rule unit at that
time, the Council could not consider establishing any zoning variation
procedure other than one that went through the Zoning Board of Appeals, as
required by statute. In the wake of the successful home rule referendum, the
proposal was again brought before the Council, and the Council directed the
Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance implementing the Community Development
Department’s proposal. The draft ordinance under consideration establishes the
administrative zoning variation procedure, authorizes the Community Development
Director to act as the Zoning Administrator, and makes related amendments to
the Zoning Ordinance and other provisions of the Village Code.
Attorney Janega reviewed the key provisions of the preliminary draft ordinance,
and Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the Table of Final Decision-making Authority. Mr.
D’Onofrio noted that this would reduce the number of cases that go to the
Zoning Board of Appeals, which should help to eliminate the stacking of cases
awaiting hearings before the Zoning Board. The changes will not affect the
number of matters that go from the Zoning Board of Appeals to the Council.
Trustee Behles commented that he thinks this is a reasonable evolution of last
year’s ordinance. Trustee Abell said that he found nothing in the draft to give
him pause.
Manager Williams mentioned that the issue of streamlining the variation process
for minor variations had resonated with residents during the Village-wide
discussions on Home Rule. He also noted that the Council will be reviewing the
effect of any changes in an annual report from the Community Development
Department.
President Woodbury asked whether the Zoning Board had had an opportunity to
review the draft. When Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that they had not, President
Woodbury said that the matter might have to be continued even after September
6th to allow them to comment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1425 and 1426. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1425 in the amount of $720,727.93 and Warrant List No. 1426 in the
amount of $321,532.51. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Ritchell, and Berger. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and Tucker.
b)   Community Development Department Remodeling. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a
proposed plan for redesigning and updating the Community Development
Department’s offices on the first floor of Village Hall. He reported that as
best as can be determined this space was last remodeled 20 to 25 years ago, and
much of the furniture and work spaces are now outmoded and not ergonomically
efficient. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the estimates did not include painting or
carpeting.
Trustee Berger asked how much those two items were expected to cost. Mr.
D’Onofrio indicated that he anticipated that they would run approximately
$10,000.
Manager Williams remarked that this is the last step in the process of
upgrading Village Hall office facilities and reorganizing staff space.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the expenditure
of $79,410.61 to remodel the Community Development offices as outlined. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Ritchell,
Berger and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and Tucker.
c)   Public Safety Building Locking System. Chief DeLopez explained that the
Public Safety Building’s door control system is outdated and in need of
replacement. The current vendor has advised the Village that they can no longer
guarantee operation of the system due to its obsolescence. Both the Police
Department and the Fire Department need to maintain tight control, and the
Village is running out of options to keep the current system operational. He
said that after a review of proposals and prices, the Police and Fire
Departments recommend that LaMarco System be awarded the project for the amount
of $61,474.00.
Manager Williams noted that the current locking systems for the Police and Fire
Departments are integrated and that it makes sense to keep them that way,
despite the fact that were not both included in the budgeting. Staff is asking
the Council to approve approximately $30,000 over the amount budgeted for the
project, but Mr. Williams noted there are sufficient funds in the budgeted
contingency fund to cover the additional cost.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the project to
LaMarco System as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Abell, Behles and Ritchell. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Eilers and Tucker.
d)   Hill Road Resurfacing Contract Extension. Manager Williams reviewed
Staff’s recommendation to extend the Village’s annual street rehabilitation
project to include the resurfacing of Hill Road, from Hibbard to Chestnut, at
the contractor’s current bit unit price. This would advance this project from
next year’s projected program. The estimated cost would be $112,141.00.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the extension of
the street rehabilitation contract by the amount of $112,141.00. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Abell,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and Tucker.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams asked Chief DeLopez to update the Council on the
coyote issue.
Chief DeLopez said that he had met with residents in the ravine area to discuss
their issues and had also spoken with a naturalist from the Department of
Natural Resources. He said that the Police Department had received only 7 calls
during July, all about sightings of non-aggressive animals, and mentioned that
the Department had applied for a license to permit them to set live-cage traps,
if necessary. Chief DeLopez reminded everyone not to feed wild animals and to
keep their pets on a leash.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Molly Lien as the Zoning
Board’s representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee Berger, seconded by
Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Abell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and Tucker. The
Council convened into Executive Session at 9:37 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:49 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent: Trustees Eilers
and Tucker. Also present Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Eilers and Tucker. The
meeting adjourned at 9:50 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
September 6, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 4, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 6, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Jessica Tucker, and Herb
Ritchell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public
Works Steven Saunders, and 35 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 9(b), the Police Archival Voice Reorder
System, had been removed from the Agenda. Per Village policy, this bid can be
awarded administratively, since the price is less than $25,000. The Trustees
then approved the amended Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   September 13, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   September 20, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Abell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 16, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2005 – Tree Preservation Ordinance Amendments – Public
Hearing & Adoption. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing. Attorney
Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance. She explained that
the ordinance would implement some of the recommendations of the Forestry
Commission to codify the established practices of the Village Forester and
bring our Code into line with generally accepted standards in the tree
preservation field. She said that the language contained in the ordinance is
permissive rather than required, in keeping with the current Code, and supports
the discretion and authority of the Director of Public Works with regard to
tree protection and tree removal.
Trustee Abell confirmed with Attorney Janega that the draft had been reviewed
by the Forestry Commission.
Trustee Tucker asked how the Village would go about letting builders know of
the new regulations. Mr. Saunders responded that the Public Works Department is
working on a flyer. He added that the proposed amendments represent most of the
technical changes submitted by the Forestry Commission in their March draft.
They give staff the administrative tools needed to better enforce the tree
preservation provisions of the Village Code.
Mary Hill, 271 Linden, Chairperson of the Forestry Commission, reiterated her
comments of August 2 urging the Council to save the tree heritage of the
Village, and encouraged passage of the proposed ordinance.
Bernie Hammer, 1445 Tower Road, and Susie Shabica, 326 Ridge, also members of
the Forestry Commission, enumerated some of the policy issues omitted from this
ordinance that still require the Council’s attention.
President Woodbury thanked the Forestry Commission for their efforts. He stated
that the Council is committed to engaging in the second phase of its
conversation regarding tree preservation, which pertains to more significant
policy issues. This discussion is slated to begin in October. Council decided
to break the discussion into two parts in an effort to put into place those
things that are easiest to implement. He said that major policy issues will
require extended discussions over time, as there is likely to be dissension
between tree preservation and property rights advocates. He added that the
Council wishes to be cautious about any amendments that would reflect a change
in policy, since the community’s responses to the Caucus Questionnaire
indicated widespread support for maintaining the Tree Preservation Ordnance as
it stands. The Council must consider policy matters in a thoughtful process
from the standpoint of their impact on the Village as a whole.
President Woodbury closed the Public Hearing.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved
to amend the ordinance as discussed. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt, as amended,
Ordinance No. MC-4-2005 amending the tree preservation provisions of the
Village Code. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and
Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Resolution No. R-33-2005 – Tree Deposits – Adoption. Attorney Janega
summarized the changes incorporated into the Village’s fee resolution for
building and zoning applications and construction activity. These include
amendments to the tree deposit and tree plan review fees, with language
intended to clarify that the tree enhancement plan review is also subject to
the standard $75 review fee. Updated section references reflect the new chapter
organization.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
33-2005. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2005 – Streamlining the Zoning Variation Process –
Public Hearing. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing, which was
continued from the August 16th meeting. He noted that the matter would not be
considered for adoption until after it is reviewed by the Zoning Board of
Appeals at its September 12th meeting.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance, which
streamlines the processing of zoning variations by adding an administrative
process for minor variations. As proposed, it divides zoning variations into
three categories and establishes the office of Zoning Administrator, who would
conduct informal hearings and make final decisions on requests for minor
variations. She pointed out that although newspaper publication of these
informal hearings is not required, neighbors will still receive notice of the
hearing, written findings will be required, and the standards for granting a
variation will not change.
There being no further comment, President Woodbury closed the Public Hearing.
Trustee Eilers remarked that he is comfortable that appropriate checks and
balances have been put into place to permit the Community Development Director,
in his capacity as Zoning Administrator, make decisions on minor variations.
Trustee Ritchell pointed out that this is not a move to liberalize the zoning
variation or approval process, but merely to speed it up for minor projects,
making it more user-friendly. It does not reflect a change in philosophy.
Attorney Janega stressed that in cases of new construction, all zoning
variations will have to be heard by the Zoning Board of Appeals and decided by
the Village Council, regardless of their magnitude. She added that the Council
will receive a report on the new process after one year.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the ordinance as
discussed. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury indicated that the matter would be up for adoption at the
September 20 Council meeting.
d)   Ordinance MC-7-2005 – Rezoning: 1190 Hamptondale & 1185 Laurel – Public
Hearing & Introduction. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing. Brian
Norkus reviewed the matter, which was initially presented at the August 2
Council meeting. This ordinance would eliminate dual zoning classifications on
two adjoining parcels, 1190 Hamptondale Avenue and 1185 Laurel. The Hamptondale
property would be zoned entirely R-2 Single-Family Residential, which would
eliminate the R-4 zoning of a small portion of that parcel. Similarly, the
Laurel property would be zoned entirely R-4 Single Family Residential, which
would eliminate the R-2 zoning of a small portion of that parcel. Mr. Norkus
explained that the proposed rezoning arises from a request made by the owners
of 1190 Hamptondale, and that staff recommended correcting the zoning at 1185
Laurel in the same process. He said that the proposed zoning changes would be
appropriate, given the zoning of other neighboring, abutting properties on the
respective block faces.
Mr. Woodbury asked whether the Hamptondale property was unbuildable given the
current dual zoning. Mr. Norkus responded that the Community Development
Department’s administrative position has been that it cannot decide which of
the two zoning districts controls, so one portion or another of the property
must be removed for the purpose of calculating allowable GFA.
Trustee Behles confirmed with Mr. Norkus that there is no advantage to the
property owner to keep the dual zoning districts. Mr. Norkus responded that in
the case of 1190 Hamptondale, the dual zoning results in a diminution of
allowable GFA.
Karen Frank, 1200 Hamptondale, requested an explanation of the difference
between the R-4 and R-2 zoning districts, which Mr. Norkus provided. He
indicated that the side yard requirements would not change.
Karen Adelman, 1185 Laurel, expressed concern that the action might take away
some of her flexibility. She indicated that she wished to withdraw her property
from inclusion in the proposed ordinance.
Mr. Woodbury said that it was not a requirement that the 1185 Laurel property
be changed; therefore, that piece of the ordinance will be removed. He then
closed the Public Hearing.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-7-2005. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-18-2005 – Disposal of Personal Property – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance. She
indicated that the changes are intended to assure that the subject ordinance
will have the same type of home rule provisions that were recently included in
the ordinance authorizing the disposal of electric transformers, which has
become the new model for such ordinances. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Abell, moved to amend the ordinance as discussed. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adopt, as amended, the
ordinance authorizing the disposal of ten items of Village-owned equipment at
the upcoming Northwest Municipal Conference auction. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker,
Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
a)   Bernie Hammer, 1445 Tower, suggested that the Council amend the Building
Code to require a grab bar in baths and showers in reconstruction and new
construction.
8. Old Business.
a)   Forest Glen Pillars. President Woodbury announced that the Council would
review the general policy options regarding the maintenance of subdivision
entrance pillars at a future Study Session.
Mr. Saunders then provided a brief overview of the matter and reviewed the bid
for the replacement of the four Forest Glen entrance pillars. He recommended
that the Council approve the bid for the Forest Glen Pillar Replacement to R.
Rudnick & Company, in an amount not to exceed $58,910.
Trustee Abell asked whether the proposed new pillars would be located on the
site of the existing pillars. Mr. Saunders responded affirmatively.
The Council discussed issues associated with the location of the pillars.
Mr. Abell then asked why the Village would be replacing pillars constructed of
the wrong brick with pillars with wing walls and lights. Mr. Saunders explained
that the proposed replacement design is more faithful to the design of the
original pillars.
Trustee Behles, formerly a member of the Design Review Board, provided a
historical perspective on that Board’s discussions of the matter. He explained
that, acting on Council’s policy direction at the time, the Village removed the
original pillars and committed to replacing them. However, at the time that
commitment was made, the magnitude of the associated costs was not anticipated.
He said that given the commitment that was made in this instance, he believes
that the Village should replace the pillars so as to maintain the architectural
integrity of the original design.
President Woodbury said that this is a complicated and messy issue. He added
that he believed that it was important to separate the question of replacing
the Forest Glen pillars from the issue of developing an ongoing policy
regarding pillar maintenance, which must be made based on what is the right
thing to do for the entire Village. By bifurcating the two issues, the Trustees
can then decide about the Forest Glen situation without having to consider what
will happen with other pillars in the future.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., expressed concerns about possible hazards
associated with replacing the existing single pillars with the proposed pillars
and wing walls.
Among the Forest Glen neighbors who spoke in support of replacing the existing
pillars with the proposed design were:
Katherine Hunt, 918 Forest Glen
Christine Dulin, 887 Forest Glen
Nick Brown, 935 Forest Glen
Wayne Fickenger, 1244 Forest Glen
Lin Finneke, 1266 Forest Glen
Scott Madlener, 1305 Forest Glen
Nancy Elmer, 1315 Forest Glen
Bob Wetoska, 1295 Forest Glenn
Cathleen Costello, 1308 Forest Glen
Margaret Athes, 1289 Forest Glen (remarks attached)
Dick Wallace, 1240 Ash, and Ruth Rane, 555 Willow, objected to the proposed
expenditure. Mr. Saunders reported that he had also received two phone calls
from residents saying that they believed the cost of the proposed new pillars
was excessive.
At the conclusion of the audience remarks, President Woodbury asked the
Trustees for their comments.
Trustee Abell said that he believed that the Village had made a perceptual
commitment to restore the pillars in their original design, including wing
walls and lights. The Council at the time considered this to be for the greater
good. He said that he does not believe that whatever is done here would set a
precedent, as the circumstances are unique. He expressed support for moving
ahead with staff’s suggestion to approve the bid at hand.
Trustee Ritchell indicated that he could not support this expenditure because
it is an embellishment of private property and not a responsible use of public
funds.
Trustee Tucker said that although she had previously supported the expenditure,
she believed that the original replacement estimate was for all three
components, the pillar, the wing wall, and the lights. She did not understand
that the wing walls and the lights were intentionally removed. She said that
she believed the Village erred in selecting the color of the brick. She noted
that although the Village made a mistake in not notifying neighbors of the
first Design Review Board meeting, prior to that meeting the Public Works
Department sent a letter proposing a meeting to facilitate resident input; that
meeting was cancelled due to lack of interest. Trustee Tucker indicated that
she would support the expenditure to replace the current pillars in appropriate
brick, but without the wing walls or lights.
Trustee Eilers stated that although he was generally not in favor of using
Village funds for a private improvement as stated by Trustee Ritchell, that he
felt the Village had made a commitment in this case. That commitment is
evidenced by the fact that the Village did, indeed, replace the pillars without
lights and wing walls. However, since this pillar reconstruction was not done
in compliance with the standards set forth in the Design Review Board approval
of March 20, 2003, the Village should be required to remove the pillars and
replace them properly, just as a private developer would be made to comply with
Village standards. Trustee Eilers stated that, in his opinion, this was not a
compromise position. This was simply the Village keeping its promise and
complying with its own regulations that it had previously overlooked.
Trustee Berger suggested that the residents work with the Village to develop a
compromise. She said she would not vote in favor of the proposal currently
before the Council.
After the trustees had expressed their opinions, President Woodbury noted that
two trustees were in favor of full pillar replacement, including wing walls and
lights, two trustees were in favor of replacing the pillars only, and two
trustees were not in favor of any pillar replacement.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to restore the pillars
consistent with the initial Design Review Board approval of March 20, 2003.
President Woodbury asked whether Trustee Eilers would amend his motion to
include lights. Trustee Eilers responded in the negative since the lights were
not included in the original Village promise or commitment. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Tucker, Abell. Nays: Trustees Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. President
Woodbury voted in favor of the motion to break the tie, and asked Trustee
Behles to monitor the situation and act as liaison between the Village and the
neighborhood. He then thanked the neighbors for their patience, participation,
and understanding.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1427 and 1428. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1427 in the amount of $950,402.82 and Warrant List No. 1428 in the
amount of $989,521.41. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Police Department Archival Voice Recorder System. As noted earlier, this
item was removed from the Agenda.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Mr. Saunders updated the Council on the status of the Coastal
Management initiative, a voluntary program to preserve and restore the Lake
Michigan shoreline. To date, Illinois is the only non-participating entity and
no application has yet been developed. He reported that no other communities
have expressed any concerns about the program.
Mr. Saunders also reviewed the current status of the proposed Streetscape
program. He reported that staff recommends employing a design consultant to
translate existing Design Guidelines into an actual plan. He expects to draft
an RFP with input from the Business Community Development Commission and the
Design Review Board, then present the plan to the public and the Council, with
an eye toward having realistic cost projections in time for the 2006-2007
budget cycle.
Finally, Mr. Saunders reported that the hands on the Village Hall tower clock
need repair and have been removed. Because the building is a landmark
structure, the proposed repairs will go before the Landmark Preservation
Commission.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the Business Community Development
Commission has changed its meeting date to the first Thursday of the month at
7:30 a.m. He invited all interested persons to attend. He added that the
Commission had reviewed Mr. Saunders’ preliminary streetscape plans and would
be giving their comments to him.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Linda Pedian to replace Brad
McLane on the Plan Commission at the expiration of his term. Trustee Abell,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the appointment. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 11:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:16 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney Katherine
Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:17 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
September 13, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: October 4, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 13, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director
of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, Finance Director Ed McKee, and approximately three
persons in the audience.
2)   Post Office Committee Status Update.
President Woodbury introduced Julian D’Esposito, Chair of the Post Office
Committee, and reviewed the varied backgrounds of the members noting their
experience and expertise relevant to the project and/or the Village.
Chairman D’Esposito provided a brief review of the Committee’s activities thus
far and goals for the future. He said that the Committee has been exploring
three separate lines of inquiry. First, it has been looking at the site and
thinking about design issues with the objective of coming up with a series of
general principles and concepts that respond to what the Committee heard over
the summer from the various focus groups. He indicated that the alternative
design concepts would be presented to the public for discussion at the Post
Office Committee meetings in October and November. The Committee’s second line
of focus will be directed toward examining what the Village’s role in this
project should be from a financial and regulatory perspective. He said the
answers to those questions will, in turn, impact design and use ideas, perhaps
causing some to be discarded and others put in their place. Once each of the
alternative scenarios has been reviewed keeping these influences in mind, the
Committee plans to engage in more community discussions. Finally, the Committee
will develop a plan for moving from the hypothetical to engaging a development
team. He said he the goal is for the Village is in a position by the beginning
of 2007 to select a developer, negotiate agreements and begin design. That way
it will be prepared to move forward as soon as the Post Office moves out in
2009, with the goal of having the project complete by Christmas of 2010. He
emphasized that process is a spiral, additive one in which the key to get a
general consensus of the values that the community as a whole wants to see
reflected in the final project.
Trustee Berger asked whether there was any possibility that the Post Office
will vacate the premises before 2009. Mr. D’Esposito indicated that that was
unlikely, although they have begun looking for a site for a distribution
facility. He added that one of the recurrent themes from the focus groups was
that a Post Office retail center be located in the new development.
Scott Freres, the Lakota Group, presented an outline of common themes and
recurrent development principles heard from the focus groups and identified
some of the issues that the Village will have to address as the process
proceeds. He then reviewed five different conceptual site layouts, pointing out
differences among the schemes and various issues associated with each.
Trustee Ritchell remarked that it is apparent that parking is going to play a
big role in the way the site is developed.
Trustee Behles commented on the importance of Dwyer Park, and therefore the
Park District, as the project moves forward. He expressed a strong preference
for nitrating the development physically in the park.
Mr. D’Esposito concurred, pointing out that this is a four-sided development
and whatever is done has to be integrated with its immediate neighbors.
Trustee Abell expressed delight with the process and alternatives. He indicated
that he was in favor of a small hotel, which could be incorporated into any
plan, and cautioned against creating any civic space that might compete with
the Community House.
Trustee Tucker voiced the hope that any final recommendation from the Committee
would reflect a community consensus derived from extensive resident
participation.
Trustee Eilers added that the Business Community Development Commission is
monitoring the process and paying close attention as it looks to achieve the
same objectives in its pursuits.
Trustee Ritchell pointed out that all of the concept designs are based on
linkage to Moffat Mall and asked whether there is an alternative design that
the community should consider. Mr. Freres responded that this linking was one
of the most overriding principles that came up time and again.
3)   Planned Development.
Attorney Janega explained that the proposed changes to the draft Planned
Development regulations that was brought before the Council on June 14th, 2005,
reflect topics that the Council asked the Planned Development study committee
to re-examine. These changes pertain specifically to building height and
parking. She said that if these modifications are deemed suitable by the
Council, the next step is to designate the body to conduct the public hearing
on the zoning text amendment and suggested that the Council consider
designating itself and hold the public hearing at its second meeting in
October.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the proposed revisions that give a certain amount of
flexibility to increase the height of a building if it is consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan and all some leeway to consider something different parking
requirements based upon the specific uses proposed and their compatibility with
the adjoining neighborhood. In addition, he noted that other changes had been
made ranging from more clearly outlining certain application process
requirements to better defining certain terms, to correcting typographical
errors.
Trustee Abell inquired as to why the Village would limit sites that qualify to
those over 10,000 sq. ft. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that such sites might need
the additional flexibility afforded by Planned Development and the Village, in
turn, would have more input into the overall project.
President Woodbury commented that the other side of flexibility is control, and
planned development provides greater control over for the Village over what can
go on a site.
Attorney Janega added that it decreases the likelihood that the Village will
get a monolithic building on a large site.
Trustee Berger asked how many pieces of property there are to which these
regulations might apply. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that five have been readily
identified in the commercial districts.
Trustee Tucker asked whether a similar notice plan for neighbors would be
followed and whether there would be processing fees. Mr. D’Onofrio replied in
the affirmative to both.
After a brief discussion, there was a consensus to move forward with setting
the hearing date.
4)   Discussion: Rotating Auditors.
Mr. McKee explained that the Village current audit contract will expire after
the March 31, 2005 Audit is completed in October. Therefore, the Council has
the option of deciding whether to continue with Miller Cooper under a new
contract or solicit bids from other qualified firms. He went on to say that
there in terms of deciding whether soliciting bids or changing auditors is
appropriate, there are two different philosophies, with many points in between;
the first is to rotate auditors based on need, and the second is to rotate
auditors regularly. Mr. McKee stated that there is nothing in the current
working relationship with Miller Cooper that would trigger a rotation under the
“rotate based on need”
The Council discussed this matter at some length. There was a consensus that
after 10 years of service from Miller Cooper, the responsible thing to do is go
out to bid. Trustees Eilers and Berger agreed to work with Mr. McKee through
the process.
5)   Discussion: Tax Levy.
President Woodbury opened by saying that he expects this to be a preliminary
discussion of the matter as it is a huge issue with which no Council has had to
deal before. In the past setting the tax levy was a relatively easy decision
because the Village really didn’t have any control over it. However, now that
Winnetka has become home rule giving it more control, the Council needs to
seriously consider how it wishes to proceed. He suggested developing a policy
statement because some of what this Council decides to do will turn into
precedent.
Mr. McKee explained that as a non-home rule community, the annual increase in
the Village’s property tax revenue was limited by State law to the rate of
inflation or 5%, whichever was less. Now that the Village is home rule, the
Council can set the property tax levy at any amount it deems appropriate. The
Village uses its property tax revenues to fund a large percentage of general
operating costs, pension amortization costs, and debt retirement.
Mr. McKee recommended the Council consider the following:
   (a)   Hold property tax increases to the lower non-home rule amount or less;
   (b)   Base the property tax levy on long-range financial projections
   (c)   If there is a need to deviate from the above, the reasons for such
action should be clearly articulated and publicized, including public meetings.
Mr. McKee noted that because the Village is no longer subject to the property
tax cap, the Village must determine how to set the tax levy amount each year.
He proceeded to outline three options:
   (a)   Inflation only;
   (b)   Inflation + Development (3 year average);
   (c)   Inflation + Projected Development.
He reported that staff’s recommendation would be (b) because it captures new
development property tax revenue as is done now and is easy to explain and
administer.
Finally, Mr. McKee raised the issue of pension funding. He suggested that the
Council consider transferring 75% of any general fund revenues in excess of the
budget to the police and fire pension funds until they are 90% funded. He
explained due to State mandates, the Village’s unfunded police and fire pension
liabilities have increased from $2.6 to $8.9 million since 2001.
After a lengthy discussion, President Woodbury suggested that the Council
revisit these matters at a future meeting once the Trustees have had time to
assimilate the information. He asked Mr. McKee to refine the options and
prepare a draft policy statement.
2)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
3)   Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:55 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Recording Secretary
September 20, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 18, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 20, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: Trustee David Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Water and Electric Bryan McInturff, Public Works
Director Steve Saunders and 12 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Council approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   October 4, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 11, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
None
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-07-2005 – Rezoning: 1190 Hamptondale - Adoption. Village
Attorney Janega explained that this ordinance was introduced at the Council’s
September 6th meeting and would rezone the entire parcel of 1190 Hamptondale
from R-2 to R-4 single-family residential. She added that the ordinance
originally included an adjoining parcel that faces Laurel Avenue, but that the
ordinance has been amended to reflect the Council's grant of that owner's
request that the Laurel Avenue property not be rezoned at this time.
President Woodbury ascertained that there were no comments from the petitioner
or the Trustees.
Trustee Ritchell moved to amend the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Ritchell then moved to adopt Ordinance MC-07-2005, seconded by Trustee
Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
b)   Ordinance MC-06-2005 – Streamline Zoning Variation Process - Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reported that, at two previous meetings, the Council had discussed
further changes to the zoning variation process to provide for a Zoning
Administrator to authorize minor variations. At the September 6th meeting, the
Council directed that the amendments be sent to the Zoning Board for final
comment. Mr. D'Onofrio said the ZBA approved of the amendments and noted that
the members were happy to see that some of the minor variation request will be
removed from their docket of cases.
Trustee Behles raised a question about the jurisdictional table, which includes
maximum building size variation requests pre-FAR buildings, but not post-FAR
buildings. Attorney Janega stated that it was inadvertently omitted and that
the table would be corrected so that the decision-making authority for post-FAR
buildings would remain the same as it is now.
There being no comments from the audience or further comment from the Council,
President Woodbury asked for a motion to amend Ordinance MC-06-2005 to reflect
the revision suggested by Trustee Behles.
Trustee Behles moved to amend the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt as amended
Ordinance No. MC-06-2005. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Abell.
c)   Resolution No. R-34-2005 – Zoning Variation Fee – Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio
reported that this resolution is a companion to MC-06-2005 and would amend the
building and zoning fee resolution by adding a fee for processing minor zoning
variations. This fee is lower than the fees for standard and major variations,
since no public hearing required.
There were no comments from the Trustees or the audience on this resolution.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-34-2005
amending the building and zoning fees. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
d)   Resolution No. R-35-2005 –Liquor License: Michael, 64 Green Bay -
Adoption. Attorney Janega stated that this resolution would authorize a Class A
liquor license for a new restaurant, called Michael, at 64 Green Bay Road. She
noted that amendments had been made to the draft resolution, so that it more
clearly states that issuance of the liquor license is conditioned on the
satisfactory completion of the background investigation. She also clarified for
the Council that liquor would not be served from the small service bar area
depicted in the plans.
Manager Williams reported that there is a two-step process for the issuance of
liquor licenses. The first is that the Council needs to approve the number of
available licenses and make sure that the application comports with Village
Code. The second step is that the Village President, acting as Liquor
Commissioner, must wait for the background investigation to be completed, which
can take up to 6 weeks, before authorizing the issuance of the liquor license.
President Woodbury asked for comments from the Council and there were none.
Michael Lachowitz, the owner of the restaurant, explained that he had spent
some time with Sgt. Harrison at the Police Department to complete his
application and thanked the staff at the Village for being helpful.
Trustee Tucker moved to amend the Resolution as suggested by the Village
Attorney, seconded by Trustee Behles. By voice vote, the motion passed
unanimously.
Trustee Eilers moved to adopt as amended Resolution No. R-35-2005, authorizing
the issuance of a liquor license for the restaurant, called Michael, at 64
Green Bay Road, provided the conditions as stated in the resolution have been
met. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion passed
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1429 and 1430. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, Trustee Ritchell moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1429 in the amount of $1,229,110.78 and Warrant List
No. 1430 in the amount of $318,015.67. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. .
b)   Policy Direction: Glendale Sidewalk. Mr. Saunders reported that in August
he had received a petition signed by 64 residents, asking that a sidewalk be
constructed along Glendale between Spruce and Pine. He discussed the possible
routes for the sidewalk, saying that both sides of the street have some issues
with trees and landscaping that would have to be resolved, but that no
engineering has been done as yet to fully evaluate the request. He said that a
rough cost estimate for this length of sidewalk would be well within the
$20,000 in the budget for miscellaneous sidewalk requests.
In answer to questions from the Trustees, Mr. Saunders went on to explain that
about 20 residents were within 250 feet of the area and were notified by letter
of this meeting. He said that the effect of the installation of the sidewalk on
trees would be identified once the engineering was completed, and added that
few homes fronted on the street.
Manager Williams reported that Public Works would also stake out the route for
the neighbors to view before construction.
Dan Kadjan, 1124 Pine Street; Larry Shannon, 1111 Spruce; Sharon King, 1134
Pine; and, Ann Wilder, 1095 Spruce, spoke against the installation of the
sidewalk, stating that many of the signatures on the petition are of residents
whose property and trees would not be affected; that there are alternative
routes for pedestrians; and that the safety issue is about vehicular traffic,
which a sidewalk would not resolve. These residents urged the Council to
consider alternative traffic safety measures instead of a sidewalk, such as
closing Glendale to vehicles in the morning and afternoon; making Glendale one-
way in opposite directions morning and afternoon; allowing parking on just one
side of the street; installing stop signs; and reducing the speed limit.
DeeDee Shields, 780 Locust, spoke in favor of the sidewalk as did Diana Rauner
of 720 Rosewood, pointing out that Glendale is a public street that all
citizens have a right to use. Mrs. Shields and Mrs. Rauner stated that they
have children who use Glendale to get to school and the playfields and would
like a sidewalk installed to increase the safety of the children. Mrs. Browner
urged the Council to conduct a pedestrian traffic study to determine the amount
of foot traffic in the area.
President Woodbury commented that the discussion particular to this sidewalk is
similar to past discussions about sidewalk requests on Pine Street and Church
Street. He said that what the Council is to consider is whether the dialog
should continue about the installation of a sidewalk on Glendale.
After hearing comments from the Trustees, President Woodbury determined that
the Council unanimously favored continuing the dialog and directed the staff to
conduct the necessary engineering study in preparation for a meeting with the
property owners affected and residents within 250’ of this area in the spring.
He asked that the staff pay careful attention to suggestions for stop signs,
one way directional traffic to manage vehicular flow and the comments received
from Mr. Kadjan and others.
c)   Policy Direction: Zoning Variation – 127 Church St.. Mr. D’Onofrio said
that the Council is being asked for policy direction on a request for variation
from the building height restrictions of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a cupola
on a new home being constructed at 127 Church Street. He said that the Zoning
Board of Appeals voted 6-1 to deny. He explained that the cupola was removed
from the original plan because it exceeded the permitted building height, and
then a variation was requested.
Mr. Konstant, builder of the home, presented his request for the variation
which he felt was necessary due to the natural grade of the property and the
placement of the house on the site. He added that the cupola is not merely
decorative but would contain fans that exhaust the air from the home. He said
that he had expected the Zoning Board’s denial of the variation and was aware
that he would have to come before the Council. He believed that the Zoning
Ordinance should be amended to allow cupolas because they are not much larger
in mass than a chimney.
The Trustees asked several questions of Mr. Konstant pertaining to the location
of the house on the site, alternatives that may have been possible and
discussed the language of the ordinance regarding the measurement of building
height.
President Woodbury stated that the Council can consider whether the cupola is
an integral part of the house and relative to that, accept or deny the
requested variation according to the zoning variation process. He suggested
that another option would be to consider a text amendment to define where
encroachments like this would be allowed because this may be an unintended
consequence of the changes made to the Zoning Ordinance.
Attorney Janega advised the Council to focus on the variation application and
said the facts are that this is new construction, the applicant knew that the
house could not be built with the cupola, the cupola was removed from the plan
to get permit approval, and the applicant decided to come in at a later date to
ask for the variation. She said this shows a conforming alternative, and the
Council needs to decide if there is enough in the record to define particular
hardships unique to this property before granting a variance.
Ms. Janega recalled that there was a specific effort to address sloped lots
when the height regulations were discussed. She explained that the two aspects
of a ZBA hearing are making a record and making a recommendation based on that
record. She added that although the ZBA had no alternative but to deny this
variation request, the Council could determine that a hardship or practical
difficulty exists, as long as the Council can give her an idea of what those
findings would be and if they can be found in the record. She cautioned that
since this request is on new construction, granting the variation may invite
manipulation of the process. She said that while this case seems to raise the
question of an amendment, no amendment is being sought, so unless the Council
is certain that it would amend the ordinance, discussion should focus on the
variation case. She suggested that, if this particular home design is
appealing, another possible consideration for the Council would be to determine
if the design provides a public benefit sufficient to offset the minimal
hardship. She said that if that's case, the Council may want to have the staff
gather more information.
Trustee Eilers asked if the lot being unique is enough of a reason.
Attorney Janega replied that in may not be in this case, because applicant
removed the cupola from his design knowing the plans would not be approved,
obtained a permit for a conforming design, and then came in for a variance. She
added it has the appearance of a self-made hardship.
After further discussion, President Woodbury determined that a consensus had
not been reached with Trustee Eilers, Behles and Ritchell appearing to favor
granting the variance, and Trustees Berger and Tucker not in favor. He
explained that granting this variation requires approval of four trustees, the
absent trustee’s vote becomes the deciding factor. He asked the Council to
table the discussion until the October 4th meeting, when there will be a full
Council present.
Trustee Eilers moved to table the issue until the October 4th meeting, seconded
by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, with Trustees Behles, Ritchell and Eilers
voting aye and Trustees Tucker and Berger, voting nay, the motion passed.
d)   Water and Electric Plant Intake Maintenance Work. Bryan McInturff, Water &
Electric Director, reported that after the annual inspection of the primary
intake at the water plant, it was discovered that several repairs are
necessary. He described what repairs are needed, the difficulty of finding a
vendor who would do the work, and what monies would be used to pay for the
repairs. He asked that the Council waive the bidding process and award a
contract to Gillen Co., which was the only company that provided estimates for
the repairs.
After a short discussion, Trustee Eilers moved to waive the bidding process and
authorize the purchase order for the maintenance of the water intake at the
Water & Electric plant to Edward E. Gillen Company in the amount of $58,500
plus an additional $9,246 to clean the intake cones, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
e)   #4 Turbine/Generator Overhaul. Mr. McInturff reported to the Council that
the turbine generator installed in 1954 is due for an overhaul as part of a 10-
year maintenance cycle. He said that the staff prefers to use Siemens Power
System Sales Co. because they have the original drawings and manufacturer
information, and the staff has been satisfied with their work in the past. He
said that there is $250,000 in the budget for this work and although the
estimate from Seimens is $271,000, he believes that some cost savings is
possible by recycling materials and by having staff monitor the project at non-
critical times.
Trustee Berger moved to authorize the Village Manager to issue purchase orders
to Siemens Power System Sales, for an amount not to exceed $250,000 without
prior Council approval, for the overhaul of #4 Turbine/Generator, seconded by
Trustee Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Abell.
f)   Certificate of Appropriateness: 14 Green Bay Road. Mr. Norkus explained
that Council approval is required for the issuance of a Certificate of
Appropriateness for the building and site alterations at 14 Green Bay Road,
formerly the Midas Muffler building, which is being converted to a restaurant.
He asked that Council consider approval, subject to the recommendations of the
Design Review Board that the applicant reduce the south garden wall to a height
not exceeding eight feet and that the applicant also submit awning, lighting
and sign plans for final approval by the Design Review Board.
Trustee Behles moved to approve the issuance of a Certificate of
Appropriateness, subject to the recommendations of the Design Review Board, for
14 Green Bay Road, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion
passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and
Trucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
g)   Bid No. 05-009 – Cherry Street Storm Sewer Outfall Pier Rehabilitation &
Stairs. Steve Saunders, Director of Public Works, reported to the Council that
the Cherry Street storm sewer outfall pier and stairs are in an advanced state
of deterioration and are part of a series of rehabilitations planned for the
outfalls. He said that three companies picked up bid proposals for this
project. The only bid, from Edward Gillen Company, was over the budgeted
amount. The staff then worked with Gillen to develop a modified design that cut
the pier renovation costs and excludes the replacement of the stairs. Mr.
Saunders said staff will solicit separate bids for the stair replacement work
in the hope of obtaining a more favorable price. He said that Gillen Company
has bought out its primary competitor and as a result is the only relatively
local marine contractor licensed for work in Lake Michigan and capable of doing
this type of work. He asked the Council to consider awarding the bid to Edward
Gillen Company.
Trustee Eilers moved to award Bid #05-009, for the Cherry Street Storm Sewer
Outfall Pier Rehabilitation to Edward Gillen Company for the modified base bid
price of $164,000, seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the motion
passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
Bid No. 05-013 – Tandem Axle Rear Loading Refuse Truck. Mr. Saunders reported
that last month he had received bids for the replacement for a 1996 refuse
truck that has reached the last of its life. He said staff received responses
from four companies, but the lowest bidder did not meet the Village’s minimum
specifications in a number of areas. He asked that the Council consider
awarding the bid to the second lowest bidder, R.N.O.W., Inc., which factors in
the trade-in value of the vehicle and complies with all of the Village’s
specifications. He also asked that the Council approve the costs of the
extended warranties for the vehicle and parts and service manuals.
After a short discussion, Trustee Ritchell moved to award Bid #05-013 for the
purchase of one tandem axle rear loading refuse collection truck to R.N.O.W.,
Inc. of West Allis, WI for $94,900, plus the additional warranty and service
manuals, for a total of $98,961, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
h)   Designation of Hearing Body for Planned Development. Attorney Janega
reported that she will begin formatting the proposed planned development
ordinance for a Zoning Ordinance amendment. She said the amendment requires a
public hearing that would ordinarily be held by the Plan Commission, but that
staff recommends that the Council be designated as the hearing body because
planned developments are a new concept for the Village and the Plan Commission
was involved in working on the proposed ordinance.
Trustee Behles moved to designate the Village Council Committee of the Whole as
the hearing body for the public hearing on the proposed Planned Development
Ordinance to be held on October 18, 2005, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
10. Reports.
a)   Manager. Manager Williams reported to the Council on a recent hire to the
Police Department through the lateral entry process, which was made possible
when the Council used the Village's new home rule powers to grant that
authority to the Board of Fire and Police Commissioners. He said the new
officer was formerly a Winnetka police officer and will be a huge benefit for
our department.
11. Appointments.
a)   President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Mr. Scott Freres as the
Design Review Board’s representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee Tucker
moved to approve the appointment, seconded by Trustee Behles. By voice vote,
the motion passed unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), Section 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 10:43 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:51 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Absent: Trustee
Abell. Also present, Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The meeting
adjourned at 10:51 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
October 4, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 18, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 4, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Jessica Tucker, and Herb Ritchell.
Absent: Trustee Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders, and seven
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 9(d) was a copier lease instead of a
copier purchase as shown on the Agenda. The Trustees approved the Agenda as
amended.
4. Quorum.
a)   October 11, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 18, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 6, 2005, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury pointed out revised
language previously distributed for the Trustees’ review. Trustee Abell moved
to adopt the minutes as amended. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   September 13, 2005, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-19-2005 – Zoning Variation: 1337 Asbury - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use, Maximum Building Size
and Front and Corner Setback variations to permit the construction of a two-car
detached garage to replace an existing two-car garage in a state of disrepair.
The new garage would change the existing nonconforming front and side yard
setbacks slightly, adding 177 sq. ft of nonconforming roofed lot coverage and
GFA. The new garage would have approximately the same setbacks as the existing
garage and the increase in size would enlarge the existing nonconforming roofed
lot coverage, as well as increase the existing nonconforming corner setback. He
reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5 to 0 to recommend
approval of the three variation requests.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
John Nichols, 900 Mt. Pleasant, reiterated complaints about the construction
project at 300 Birch Street. He expressed concern that another building permit
extension has been given to the homeowner.
Mr. D’Onofrio agreed that this project has taken an extremely long time. He
said that the Community Development Department’s goal is to get the job
completed, given the tools that it has, but not to manage the project. He
indicated that he has met with the owner of this property on numerous
occasions, made the expectations clear, and pursued legal action to the extent
available.
Trustee Abell pointed out that when this issue first surfaced a year ago, the
applicable ordinance was amended to prevent a similar occurrence, but the
amended restrictions are not retroactive.
President Woodbury commented that ordinarily homeowners are self-motivated to
finish a renovation project, but in this case, the owners do not seem to be so
motivated. He assured Mr. Nichols that the Village would continue to monitor
the situation and take such actions as are available to ensure that the project
is completed.
8. Old Business.
a)   Policy Direction: Zoning Variation – 127 Church St. Trustee Tucker,
seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to remove this item from the table. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the petitioner is requesting a Building Height
variation to permit a newly constructed home to accommodate a proposed cupola.
The Zoning Ordinance defines building height as the vertical distance measured
from the top of the finished first floor to the highest point of the building,
including any cupola, tower, or similar roof feature. He also reviewed the
circumstances surrounding the variation request and reported that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 6 to 1 to recommend denial of the variation.
Trustee Abell confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that construction of the cupola
began after the building permit was granted based on plans that did not include
a cupola.
Paul Konstant, the architect, reviewed the sequence of events. He explained
that the panelized construction pieces, including the cupola, had been ordered
based upon the original design before the building permit was issued. He said
that although he did not alter the original order to remove the cupola, he had
no intention of constructing it until after a variation had been granted, and
pointed out that he had removed it immediately upon being requested to do so by
the Community Development Department.
Mr. Konstant contended that cupolas should have been excluded from inclusion in
the height restrictions of the Zoning Ordinance because they are a decorative
element that enhances the overall architectural design of a house. He added
that they are also a source of natural light and ventilation.
Trustee Berger pointed out that the issue before the Council is not one of
amending the Zoning Ordinance. She added that by submitting a complying
alternative, Mr. Konstant had demonstrated that there is no practical hardship
or particular difficulty with the property and that a reasonable return could
be expected. She asked Mr. Konstant whether he had considered reducing the
overall building height to accommodate the cupola when he originally designed
the home. He said that to do so would not have been true to the architectural
design.
President Woodbury asked Mr. Konstant to respond to questions raised at the
last meeting about the placement of the house on the parcel. Mr. Konstant
explained that the house had been placed where it worked best with the existing
grade and where it would do the least damage to existing trees. He said that
the placement conforms and is consistent with the Zoning Ordinance and where it
makes the most sense in relation to the neighborhood.
Trustee Ritchell remarked that he has the sense that the Council feels that if
this is allowed, others will take advantage of the exception. He said that he
believes that the cupola belongs architecturally on the house and is in keeping
with the type of things that the Village should support and applaud. He
indicated that he would support this variation request.
Attorney Janega clarified the chronology of events regarding submission of the
design and construction. She also confirmed with Mr. Konstant that the house
meets all light and ventilation requirements of the Building Code without the
addition of the cupola. He said that it did, but maintained that there would be
no height issue if the same house were on flat ground. There was some
disagreement on this point among Mr. Konstant, Ms. Janega, and Mr. D’Onofrio.
Ms. Janega explained to the Council that from a land use and zoning standpoint,
there is no evidence of a practical difficulty or particular hardship. What
does exist is a self-created hardship. The submission of a conforming plan
without the cupola is evidence of a lack of hardship and the applicant’s
determination that a reasonable return could be expected. She explained that in
terms of uniqueness, the uniqueness must go to a characteristic of the land,
not the circumstances of how the land is handled or the particulars of design.
She added that the applicant’s references to the neighboring properties do not
really pertain because they are legal nonconforming, and pointed out that prior
Councils have said no to similar requests.
Trustee Berger asked why Mr. Konstant had not gone through the variation
process or pursued a text amendment at the time he submitted the original
plans. He replied that it was a matter of time and money. Ms. Berger reiterated
that none of the circumstances exist in which the Council can grant an
exception.
Trustee Abell said that he has a real problem with this application,
particularly in view of the fact that cupolas are specifically covered in the
Zoning Ordinance, and reiterated the idea that this is not the procedure to
change the zoning ordinance. He expressed his belief that it would be a mistake
to set a precedent by allowing an exception from the rules when everyone else
had to abide by them. He said that he could not support this request.
Trustee Tucker concurred with Trustees Abell and Berger. She noted that there
have been other instances in which applicants have asked for variations because
of a perceived problem with the Code, but noted that this is not the forum to
make those arguments. She indicated that she could see no practical
difficulties or particular hardships, only self-imposed and self-serving
issues. She said she would support the Zoning Board’s denial of the variation
request.
Trustee Behles said that he believes that Mr. Konstant has raised some
interesting questions that would make for a good discussion for a text
amendment, but that is not the business currently before the Council. At this
time, the Trustees are being asked to test this case against the existing
standards. Since this instance does not meet those standards, he indicated that
he would support the Zoning Board’s decision.
President Woodbury pointed out that it was clear that Mr. Konstant could not
receive the four votes required to grant the variation request and thanked him
for his patience and cooperation throughout the process. He remarked that at
the next Study Session the Council would begin discussing residential design
guidelines and that this case would be an appropriate example of where design
guidelines and the zoning ordinance might conflict and perhaps point out some
of the places where Zoning Ordinance amendments might be appropriate.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to concur with the Zoning
Board’s decision to deny the variation request. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Berger, Behles, and Tucker. Nays: Trustee
Ritchell. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1431 and 1432. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1431 in the amount of $680,270.99 and Warrant List No. 1432 in the
amount of $878,272.11. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
b)   TV Truck Replacement. Mr. Saunders reviewed a request to waive the formal
bid process and authorize the purchase of a 2004 Gas GMC Step Van equipped with
a CUES CCTV unit, for $152,000 from CUES, Inc. to replace the existing CATV
truck used by the Public Works Department. He explained that the current
camera/data recorder unit, which is used to inspect storm/sanitary sewers, is
outdated and technical support for it is no long available. Then Mr. Saunders
reviewed the process used for selecting the recommended vendor and van.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to waive the formal bid
process and authorize the purchase as recommended. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
c)   Salt Bid. Mr. Saunders reviewed the proposal to purchase rock salt through
the State of Illinois bid, which is a joint purchasing program that affords
municipalities the opportunity to obtain lower prices than they might otherwise
be able to secure. This year the State’s low bidder is North American Salt at
$33.57/ton. He said that the Village estimates needing 1130 tons of salt this
coming winter. Participation in the State program requires that the Village
purchase at least 70% of that amount, up to 125%, at the contract price.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to purchase rock salt
through the State of Illinois bid from North American Salt at $33.57/ton. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
d)   Copier Lease Proposal. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a proposal to lease a new,
multifunctional Xerox copy machine for use in the Community Development
Department for $747.00 per month, or $44,820 over the term of the lease, to
replace the existing copier, which costs $873.00 per month. The Village will
realize additional savings because the lease of this machine will permit the
Village to phase out the use of two existing stand-alone printers and a fax
machine.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the lease of the
copier as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Eilers.
e)   Taxicab Deregulation. Manager Williams commented that the Police
Department has recommended changes to the applicable provisions of the Village
Code regarding taxicabs. He said that if the Council concurs with the proposed
changes, the Village Attorney will draft appropriate amended language for the
Council’s consideration at a future date.
Chief DeLopez explained that changes in the operation of taxicab businesses
necessitate revisions to the requirements for their operation that are
currently imposed by the Village Code. The taxicab business has evolved over
time from major companies owning the vehicles, garages, and office facilities,
to multiple proprietary businesses run by individuals. This evolution has
caused several small communities similar to Winnetka to cease trying to license
and/or regulate the taxi business. In addition, the Police Department cannot
inspect and test in keeping with the current requirements in an efficient and
cost effective manner.
Chief DeLopez said that the goal is to ensure that residents receive the
highest quality of service. Therefore, the Police Department will continue to
license the drivers to ensure that they do not have criminal records, and that
they have proof of appropriate insurance.
President Woodbury remarked that the proposed changes would essentially rescind
the local licensing of the business entities themselves, but not of the
drivers.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed, so President Woodbury
asked the Village Attorney to draft an appropriate ordinance amending the
Village Code.
10. Reports.
a)   President. President Woodbury said that he had been interviewed by Channel
5 and expected a report to appear on that station in the near future.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Ritchell reported on his attendance at the recent NWMC
Auction, held at the Village Yards, at which several pieces of surplus
equipment had been sold.
Trustee Berger reminded the audience that the Fire Department would be holding
its annual open house on October 15th from 10:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m.
c)   Village Attorney: Attorney Janega reported that she had met with the Plan
Commission last week and said that the Committee is making progress on its
report regarding affordable housing standards, pursuant to the Council’s May
directive. She added that she would be meeting with the Commission again to
review its role in the land use and development function of the Village.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:42 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:52 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent: None.
Also present Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney Katherine
Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers. The meeting
adjourned at 9:53 p.m.
