Recording Secretary
October 11, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: November 1, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 11, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director
of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Village Forester Jim Stier and approximately 12 persons in
the audience.
2)   Report: Residential Design.
Nan Greenough reported on the Ad Hoc Design Study Committee’s mission to
explore whether design guidelines would make sense for new home construction
and recommend whether or not there should be a dialog among residents. She
explained the process the Committee followed in defining the issue, identifying
the needs of the community and then making recommendations based on what was
discovered. She said that the Committee’s recommendation is to develop a
pattern book containing design elements and styles thought to be desirable in
Winnetka that would help to educate residents, home purchasers and developers
before construction begins on a property. She said the Committee had concluded
that the process of creating the pattern book would be the best way to obtain
the public’s comments before an attempt was made to develop design review.
Following a lengthy discussion between the Trustees and committee members Gary
Frank, Anne Montgomery, Maureen Mitchell and Posey Fisher, President Woodbury
asked the Committee to give the Council an answer as to whether the members
want to stay in place and see the development of the pattern book through to
completion. He said that the Council would then want the Committee to develop a
request for proposal to retain a consultant to produce the pattern book, devise
a schedule for completion and depending on the results of the Caucus survey,
undertake a survey on this question. He also asked the Committee to provide the
Council with recommendations for changes to the Zoning Ordinance as they
prepare their report.
3)   Forestry Commission Policy Issues.
Mary Hill, Chair of the Forestry Commission made some opening remarks and
thanked the Council for approving the technical amendments to the tree
ordinance. She added that the report prepared by the Public Works Director,
Steve Saunders, addresses many of the Commissions recommendations, but not all.
Mr. Saunders replied that his report is based upon the Council’s policy
direction at the May study session to separate the smaller issues from the more
substantive ones. From the Forestry Commission’s recommendations for proposed
changes to the tree ordinance, he had identified the following five major
policy issues: the automatic designation of heritage trees; the removal of
economic hardship language from the grounds for tree removal in the Village
Code; authorizing the Commission to have broader oversight of the permitting
process for tree removal and to allow a Commission member to appeal a staff
decision; the notification of neighbors by building permit applicants; and, a
tree preservation deposit to guard against the loss of trees impacted by
construction.
After discussing the issues raised by the staff and listening to the comments
of the members of the Forestry Commission, President Woodbury informed the
Commission that on the issue of grounds for tree removal and economic hardship,
the Council agrees with the Village Attorney’s recommendation that this
language be retained in the Village Code. On the remaining issues, he directed
the Commission to meet with the staff, particularly the Village Attorney, to
discuss the process further and resolve some of the legal aspects of the issues
before returning to the Council for decisions on policy.
4)   Continued Discussion: Property Tax Levy.
Manager Williams outlined a proposal for setting procedures under the Village’s
home rule authority to maintain a high level of Village services, a sound
financial foundation and remain within the Council’s goal of levying taxes
within the tax cap. He said that under this plan, the Council would be provided
an update of revenues at the November study session, as well as data from
Community Development on trends, teardowns and commercial projects, and data on
changes to the police and fire pension funds. He said that the tax levy would
be calculated based upon this information and an ordinance drafted for
introduction and subsequent adoption at the Council’s meetings in December. He
explained that the difference from past policies is that the Village would be
determining whether or not it is necessary to levy a tax on new development to
increase revenue, which may help the Village’s share of property taxes slowly
decrease over time. He said that services would be reviewed on a 3-year
rotating basis at the rate of two Village departments per year, to determine if
the services are still needed and if they are being provided efficiently.
In response to a question from Trustee Berger, Manager Williams said that
historically, the staff analyzes the same information annually and then big
items are brought to the Council. Under the new procedures, he said it will be
a more formal process to ensure that we remain within the goal of setting the
tax levy within the tax cap. He explained that the Council will decide in
December whether the levy needs to be made for development and if not, the
Village would keep a running tally and in an emergency, refer to the tally to
determine how much tax to levy. He further explained that if the Council
chooses not to levy taxes for development, the levy will be below CPI because
it will be spread over a longer base, but can be recovered in future years by
assessing taxes in arrears.
Trustees Abell and Behles commented that expenses are increasing over and above
the CPI, so at some point, the Village will have to compensate for that.
Director of Finance, Ed McKee, explained that the general fund gives the
Council more flexibility to meet expenses.
Manager Williams suggested that the Council may want to memorialize the
property tax levy philosophy in a resolution to set a precedent for future
Councils. He said that although this Council can’t bind future councils, by
doing a resolution, a future council that chooses to change the philosophy
would have to do a resolution as well and it sets the bar a little higher.
Village Attorney Janega commented to the Council that a resolution is not
necessary, because there is still a record in the minutes and the agenda
materials.
Trustee Berger replied that Manager William’s plan has a lot of merit and a
resolution should be discussed.
President Woodbury added that prior Councils have not made these decisions
before and whether it’s in a resolution or not, it is important to set a policy
as a legacy for future councils and it is a good idea.
5)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:20 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
October 18, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 1, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
October 18, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Jessica Tucker, and Herb
Ritchell. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders,
Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff and five persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   October 22, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 1, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   November 8, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 20, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles moved to adopt the
minutes as amended. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   October 4, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the
minutes. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Audit Report.
a)   Mr. McKee introduced the Village’s auditor, Irwin Lyons, a partner at
Miller Cooper & Co., Ltd. Mr. Lyons reviewed the Comprehensive Annual Financial
Report (CAFR), the auditing standards and the Management Letter, and reported
that the Village had once again been awarded the Certificate for Excellence in
Financial Reporting. He added that the Auditors’ Opinion is “unqualified,”
which is the highest level of assurance that can be given.
Manager Williams pointed out that all of the Village’s investments are in a
secure category in keeping with the Village’s very conservative approach. He
also noted that the funding ratio for the IMRF pension fund has increased over
the years. However, the funding ratio for the police and fire pension funds has
declined somewhat because of the tremendous benefit increases mandated by
Springfield and also because of the current difficult investment environment.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. Lyons that the Village could receive no
stronger positive opinion letter.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-19-2005 – Zoning Variation: 1337 Asbury - Adoption.
President Woodbury called for any additional comments on this matter. There
being none, Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt the
ordinance granting three zoning variations to permit the construction of a two-
car detached garage. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees: Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2005 – Planned Development – Introduction & Public
Hearing. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing and asked Attorney Janega
to comment.
Ms. Janega explained that this ordinance would amend the Village Code to
implement regulations that were recommended by the Planned Development Study
Committee. The proposed regulations are designed to govern the redevelopment of
larger sites within the Village’s commercial areas, and would apply to any
property in the B-1, B-2, C-1, and C-2 zoning districts that has an area of
10,000 sq. ft. or more and is owned by a single entity. Any property under that
minimum would continue to be subject to the existing standards for the
particular zoning district in which it lies. Ms. Janega said that Planned
Developments would soften some of the Zoning Ordinance’s bulk regulations in
order to enable and encourage redevelopment while retaining Village control
over the development. She added that this process would allow the Village to
provide incentives to builders to produce more favorable architectural designs
and layouts.
Attorney Janega then reviewed the draft ordinance section by section. She noted
that it is important to check the proposed amendments against the rest of the
Village Code to make certain that there are no internal conflicts.
Trustee Ritchell asked how many existing properties would fall into the Planned
Development category. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that staff has identified five
such properties, mostly in the C-2 zoning district.
Trustee Eilers commented that the Business Community Development Commission has
identified nine or ten parcels that could be affected.
Ms. Janega went on to explain that once an applicant gets through the
preliminary approval, the final process is straightforward and should be fairly
routine. There are hearings built into the final process because the
preliminary plan approval may have been conditional. In the end, however, the
Village Council will have final authority.
Mr. D’Onofrio remarked that the Community Development Department has been
having preliminary discussions with the developer of a piece of property on
Green Bay Road with an eye on the pending adoption of this ordinance. He
reported that so far the response is positive and that staff views this
ordinance as a sound step because it integrates design with zoning in order to
achieve a development that looks good and serves the community well.
President Woodbury closed the Public Hearing and called for comments from the
Trustees.
Trustee Behles said that he is very pleased that this issue has finally come
before the Council, as he feels strongly that the Village needs this as a tool
to prepare for development of the Post Office site. At present, the Zoning
Ordinance has no provisions to handle the development of such sites and the
Village wants to be in a position to manage this kind of development in a
flexible way for both the Village and the developer.
Trustee Eilers remarked that the Village has more control over the development
of the Post Office site because it owns the property, but that these
regulations will put it in a better position to control development of the
other potential sites.
Trustees Abell, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger concurred.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance
as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9. Old Business.
a)   Bid No. 05-012: Forest Glen Pillar Replacement. Mr. Saunders briefly
reviewed the history of this item and the bids for replacement of the four
brick pillars marking the entrance to the Forest Glen subdivision. He explained
that as directed by the Council at the September 6th Council meeting, the bid
covers the replacement of the four pillars without lights and wing walls. Mr.
Saunders recommended that the bid be awarded to R. Rudnick & Company at the
revised amount of $29,600. He noted that the pillars would be constructed so
that lights could be added later, should the residents decide to do that on
their own.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees: Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Abstain: Trustee Ritchell.
Absent: None.
10. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1433 and 1434. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1433 in the amount of $760,351.36 and Warrant List No. 1434 in the
amount of $317,685.56. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 314 Walnut. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front
and Corner Setback zoning variations to permit the construction of a garage at
a newly constructed home. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the petitioners were
originally asking for a Gross Floor Area variation as well, but that request
has been withdrawn. The size of the house has been reduced and is now in
conformity. He explained that the property fronts three public streets, which
creates the necessity for setback variations.
After a brief discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees to move
forward with granting the requested variations. Trustee Berger, seconded by
Trustee Ritchell, moved to direct the Village Attorney to prepare the necessary
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Bid No. 05-014: All Purpose Utility Tractor. Mr. Saunders reviewed a bid
to purchase a Holder 4WD utility tractor to replace a multi-purpose utility
tractor that has reached its program replacement time. The tractor is used for
leaf collection, sidewalk and business district snow removal and landfill
mowing, and is equipped to provide backup power to storm water pumps at the
pumping station. He recommended that the bid be awarded to Temco Machinery in
the amount of $95,940.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid No. 05-019: Traffic Signal LED Bulb Bids. Mr. McInturff explained that
the Village had applied for and received a grant offered by the State as an
energy conservation measure to replace the existing incandescent bulbs in
traffic signals with LED traffic signal lamps. The grant for $18,840 must be
expended before the end of the calendar year. Mr. McInturff reviewed the cost
savings associated with the LED bulbs’ energy savings and recommended that the
bid for replacing all of the bulbs be awarded to Brown Traffic Products for
$33,460, which after the grant is received will result in a cost to the Village
of $14,620. The LED’s will pay for themselves in less than a year.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Change Order #1: Cherry Street Outfall. Mr. Saunders reviewed the first
Change Order for the Cherry Street Storm Sewer Outfall project. He recommended
that the Council approve the Change Order in the amount of $22,000, increasing
the total cost of the project from $164,000 to $186,000. He said the additional
amount would be obtained from the Street Rehabilitation funds.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the Change Order
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid No. 05-015: Water Plant Concrete Repairs. Mr. McInturff reviewed the
bid for the third year of concrete repairs required at the Water Plant, where
water penetration and freeze/thaw cycles have damaged the old concrete over
time. He recommended that the bid be awarded to Lifco Concrete Contractors in
an amount not to exceed $75,000.
After a brief discussion, President Woodbury tabled the matter so that staff
could obtain some additional information requested by the Trustees.
g)   Capital Improvement Reports. Manager Williams referred the Trustees to the
individual departments’ reports and said that the Village is making good
progress on its capital improvements. He noted that problems have arisen
regarding the addition of a storm water pump station on Tower Road and that the
Village is still negotiating with IDOT about the proposed new water main down
Hibbard Road, which will not be under construction until next summer. He
reported that the liquid chemical feed system at the Water Plant has been
successfully installed, the annual street resurfacing projects have been
completed, and the door security system for the Public Safety Building has been
ordered.
11. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported on the recent Chamber of Commerce
meeting. He said that plans are moving ahead for their Winterfest celebration
on December 10th.
Trustee Tucker commented on her visit to the Fire Department’s Fire Prevention
Week Open House at which the new Hazmat vehicle was on display, along with
other pieces of equipment. She said she believed that the tax dollars
contributed to the purchase of this vehicle were well spent.
Trustee Abell raised the issue of improving the walkway across the Village
Green and announced that he would be attending the NWMC Board meeting on
Wednesday evening.
b)   Village Attorney: Attorney Janega reported that she had become a member of
the Home Rule Attorneys Committee of the Illinois Municipal League.
12. Appointments.
None.
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:19 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney Katherine
Janega.
14. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:20 p.m.
