Recording Secretary
October 22, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: November 1, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Saturday,
October 22, 2005, at 9:00 a.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 9:03 a.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Public Works Steve Saunders, and no persons in the audience.
2)   Discussion: Flood Plain – Constructing Lowest Floor Below the Base Flood
Elevation.
Mr. Saunders explained that there are approximately 550 homes in the 100-year
flood plain as mapped out by FEMA. Development in the flood plain must comply
with the Village’s Special Flood Hazard Protection Ordinance, which requires
that all new construction must have its lowest floor at least one foot above
the 100-year base flood elevation. In 2001, FEMA relaxed their guidelines and
published a technical bulletin providing design criteria for the construction
of homes with their lowest floor below the base flood elevation. Builders are
required to take the footprint of the house out of the flood plain by means of
constructing a clay building pad and then obtaining a permit from FEMA taking
that portion of the property out of the flood plain. The Village used the
bulletin’s guidelines to adopt an ordinance that outlined conditions in which
you could build a basement in the flood plain.
Mr. Saunders then showed the Council some photos of houses that have been
completed under the new guidelines. He explained that builders complain that
the process takes too long and is too expensive. Permit approval from FEMA can
take three to four months or more and the neighbors are not happy that they
have to look at a hole in the ground sitting idle until FEMA approves the
permit. There is some question as to whether or not the finished results are
acceptable. Some of the structures are better than others at fitting in with
neighboring houses.
President Woodbury pointed out that the process requires that you basically
build a mountain and put a hole in it and put the house inside the hole.
Engineers monitor the whole process. If there’s no good clay under the house,
it has to be brought in, so the material is trucked in to build the pad and
then trucked out again when the hole is dug for the basement. Trustee Tucker
commented that that’s a lot of extra wear and tear on the roads.
Mr. Williams said that people want basements because it increases the value of
their homes. Mr. Saunders told the Council to keep in mind that whether or not
someone chooses to build a basement, the first floor will have to be one foot
above the flood plain, so the house will still be built up and compensatory
storage will still be required. The advantage of not having a basement is that
the builder has more choices in handling compensatory storage and landscaping.
The compensatory storage can go below the house and the grade can be sloped
instead of using a retaining wall. Another advantage of compensatory storage
below the house is that you don’t have to create holes or drainage swales
outside the house.
Trustee Behles remarked that on a small lot you don’t have the same
opportunities for compensatory storage. Building it underground is very
expensive but ultimately leaves a better appearance.
Trustee Eilers asked whether the issue before the Council was whether to
continue to allow basements in the flood plain. Mr. Saunders replied in the
affirmative.
President Woodbury said the issue seems to be the overall policy decision about
allowing basements in flood plains. He explained that the change to allow
basements was made in response to concerns about the limits on property values
of houses without basements. The concerns of the Council at that time were
centered around the impact of letting people build basements in the flood plain
and about how well the houses would blend into the surrounding neighborhood.
Neighbors don’t want a house that is built on an island and towers above them.
It’s possible that not allowing basements might encourage renovation. He added
that the Caucus Survey results indicate that residents believe the Council acts
too slowly with regard to reconstruction guidelines.
Mr. Williams informed the Council that staff is working on a summary report
that will show all the changes made in the last 15 years on zoning. The report
will allow the Council to look at all the steps they’ve been through and see
where they stand currently.
3)   Report: Sanitary Sewer Backflow Prevention.
Mr. Saunders explained that at the June 14, 2005, Study Session the Council
discussed implementing a program to provide homeowners with financial
assistance in installing backflow prevention systems. He then explained several
different ideas outlined in his agenda report. In response to questions from
the Council, Mr. Saunders explained that basement sewer backups are somewhat
resistant to global solutions. The Village would have to spend a lot of money
on its sewer system as opposed to spending a smaller amount on the houses that
back up. He confirmed that the backups can happen anywhere and they can be very
localized He added that these are mostly sanitary sewer backups. The system is
old and it’s not airtight. But when MWRD interceptors back up, the Village
system backs up even if it is airtight.
The Trustees discussed the pros and cons of getting into a program that
benefits only some in the Village, and whether there is discretionary money in
the budget for a program of this type. Mr. Williams explained that the money to
pay for the program would not come out of tax revenue, but rather from sewer
user fees. After a brief discussion, the consensus was that money might be
saved on sewer repairs if the Village helps residents with backflow prevention.
Mr. Williams suggested that money be put in the budget and the Council can
hammer out the details of the program next year.
4)   Policy Direction: Pillar Maintenance Policy.
Mr. Saunders explained that in January of 2001, the Council directed staff to
pursue a policy of maintaining several decorative brick pillars located on
public property throughout the Village. Since that time, the Village has
removed and replaced several such pillars, at a total cost of approximately
$40,000. He briefly described the Village’s options with regard to maintenance
and/or removal of the pillars.
Trustee Berger pointed out that the decision by the Council in 2001 was based
on information that now appears inaccurate. The cost to maintain the pillars is
much higher than originally estimated. She stated that what contributed to the
problem at Forest Glen was a lack of communication and lack of a clear policy
direction. She suggested that the Village should give the residents warning if
the pillars are unsafe, give them some options, and if they do nothing, then
make it clear what steps the Village will take. It seems a fair solution to
communicate the problem, outline remedies, be fiscally responsible and still be
sensitive to the needs of a neighborhood.
Trustees Ritchell and Eilers agreed with Trustee Berger and asked about
liability issues.
Mr. Williams stated that the Council will have to deal with those issues on a
case-by-case basis. If staff determines that the pillars are a hazard, the
Village should notify the neighbors and give them the option of maintaining
them or having the Village take them down.
President Woodbury agreed, saying he thought the Council should rescind the
2001 policy and then notify the neighbors that the pillar upkeep is their
responsibility.
Trustee Eilers said he thought it would be fairer to notify the neighbors and
give them the option to repair the pillars before they deteriorate to the point
that they have to be removed.
5)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
6)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:20 a.m.
   ____________________________________
   Recording Secretary
November 1, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 15, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 1, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Jessica Tucker, and Herb
Ritchell. Absent: Attorney Janega. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Director of Water & Electric Bryan McInturff and one person in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   November 8, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 15, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend. President Woodbury mentioned that he
might be out of town.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 11, 2005, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt the
minutes as amended. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   October 18, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles moved to adopt the
minutes. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   October 22, 2005, Special Study Session. Trustee Abell moved to adopt the
minutes. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-20-2005 – Zoning Variation: 314 Walnut - Introduction.
Noting that this matter was discussed at the July 5th and the October 18th
Council meetings and that the Council directed the Village Attorney to prepare
an ordinance granting the requested front and corner setback variations,
President Woodbury called for additional comments. There being none, Trustee
Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance as drafted.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2005 – Taxicab Deregulation – Introduction. Manager
Williams explained that given the Village Attorney’s absence and the draft
nature of the ordinance, the item could either be introduced as drafted for
amendment later or tabled until a later date.
After a couple of comments from the Trustees, Trustee Eilers, seconded by
Trustee Behles moved to table the matter until a later meeting. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Tucker suggested that once the ordinance is revised the local taxicab
companies be notified as best as possible of the changes.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
Gene Greable, the Caucus Chairman, reported that the annual surveys have been
tabulated and that the Caucus is in the process of drafting its platforms for
the coming year.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1435 and 1436. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1435 in the amount of $893,725.36 and Warrant List No. 1436 in the
amount of $862,145.20. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid No. 05-015: Water Plant Concrete Repairs. Mr. McInturff reviewed the
bid for the third year of concrete repairs required at the Water Plant, where
water penetration and freeze/thaw cycles have damaged the old concrete over
time. He also responded to questions raised by the Trustees at the last
meeting. Mr. McInturff explained that this is an ongoing project, and each year
the Village evaluates the work that needs to be completed. All essential work
is slated to be finished first, before any lower priority items are addressed.
Some of these may be deferred until the following year, depending upon budget
considerations. He said that since this is an ongoing project, there is no
concern that needed work will be ignored in the following year and that the
costs for deferred work will not go up significantly in the interim. Mr.
McInturff recommended that the bid be awarded to Lifco Concrete Contractors in
an amount not to exceed the annual budgeted amount of $75,000.
Manager Williams added that time constraints also impact the amount of work
that can be completed in any given year as it must be done during the winter so
that the Water Plant can be ready for peak summer operations by early spring.
Mr. Williams introduced Wayne Brunzell, the Village’s consultant on this
project and confirmed with him that it had been he who mapped out and evaluated
the Village’s project, wrote the specifications for the bids, and ultimately
supervises the work. He also asked Mr. Brunzell to share his background and
credentials with the Council, which he did.
Mr. Brunzell confirmed Mr. McInturffs assessment and recommendation and
responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Abell asked Mr. Brunzell whether in his professional judgment the
Village is approaching the Water Plant repairs appropriately in terms of
deferred maintenance and whether it is gaining on the project or incurring more
costs. Mr. Brunzell responded that the Village is, in fact, gaining on the
overall project and said that it is not detrimental to the program to do a
fixed dollar amount of work annually.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell moved to approve the bid to Lifeco
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker.
10. Reports.
Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that he and Mr. Saunders had met with the
Forest Glen neighbors to review the revised drawings for the new entrance
pillars. He said the neighbors seemed receptive and satisfied with the
materials samples presented. He said that it is hoped that the replacement
pillars will be finished in the next week or so and noted that one of the
residents is contributing light fixtures that will be added in the future.
Trustee Abell reported on his attendance at a recent NWMC meeting.
Trustee Berger reported that she and Trustee Eilers would be meeting with Mr.
McKee in the morning to review the bids from the firms qualified to undertake
the Village’s audit and decide which ones to interview.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of John Jansson as the Library
Board’s representative to the Plan Commission to replace Alex Moore. Trustee
Eilers so moved. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 8:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 8:50 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
Village Attorney Katherine Janega. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 8:51 p.m.
