Recording Secretary
November 8, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 6, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 8, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Director of Community Development Mike
D’Onofrio, Water & Electric Director Rich Ciesla, Distribution Engineer Bryan
McInturff, Finance Director Ed McKee, and approximately 8 persons in the
audience.
2)   Analysis of Building Review Committee and Landmark Preservation Committee.
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio presented the staff’s analysis
about the possibility of combining the Building Review and Landmark
Preservation Committees. He said that the staff had developed a list of pros
and cons and concluded that the costs of combining the two committees outweigh
the benefits. Staff’s recommendation, and also the Committees’, was to keep the
committees separate to preserve the distinction between preservation and
demolition. Mr. D’Onofrio suggested that as discussions are held about
residential design guidelines, the Council could consider an expanded role for
these committees.
After discussing the responsibilities and focus of each of the committees, the
Trustees asked that the committees study the preservation issues, hire a
consultant if necessary to prepare a report and possibly develop a catalog of
homes, explore what types of incentives might be offered, and the creation of a
privately funded foundation that might provide economic incentives to
homeowners and developers. The committees were also charged with studying the
ordinances for demolition and preservation and to meet jointly to discuss
amendments and the role of staff in the demolition process.
3)   Cost of Service Study – Electric Rates.
Ann Falcon , Economic Engineering Services, reported that the Cost of Service
Study for setting electric rates showed that existing rate levels should not be
changed significantly. While the results of the study recommended a 1.45% rate
increase to cover a $160,000 shortfall, Winnetka’s rates are well within the
plus or minus 10% recommended by the industry.
Manager Williams commented upon how the current rates compare to the market and
predicted that other utilities will face significant rate increases. He had
requested the rate study before the market changes to determine whether
Winnetka’s customers are being charged fairly. He added that because of the
retirement of the debt for the Trimble County 1 power plant, the Village will
be able to put funds in the electric fund for future capital improvements
without increasing rates.
The Trustees asked several questions pertaining to the study and agreed with
the recommendation that no significant rate changes are necessary at this time.
4)   2005 Property Tax Levy Analysis.
Manager Williams reported that the 2005 property tax levy would be the first
under home rule. If non-home rule, the tax levy figure would be capped at 4.8%
or $11,000,200 based on the CPI and new development.
In response to a question from Trustee Eilers, Mr. McKee explained that new
development is reported by the assessor’s office and is calculated by averaging
the last 3 years and dividing the average by the equalized assessed valuation.
For 2005, according to his calculations, the levy on new development would be
1.5%.
Manager Williams further explained that the CPI is 3.3%, so that if new
development is 1.5%, the increase to residents would be 1.8% to equal the rate
of inflation. He listed some of the major projects the Village is considering
for improvements in the next few years and concluded by saying that the Village
is in a strong position and it would be reasonable to set the levy at 3.3%. He
added that the staff’s recommendation is to set the levy at 3.3% at the least,
although the Council could set the levy at the tax cap of 4.8%.
Trustee Behles commented that if the Council chooses to set the levy well below
the tax cap, it may have the effect of having to set future tax levies above
the tax cap or accelerate the need to create other revenue-generating taxes or
fees. Mr. Behles was concerned that what may be seen as a coup now, may risk
future tax revenues. He suggested that the levy be set closer to the upper end
of the tax cap, but not at the minimum number recommended.
President Woodbury agreed that that he doesn’t want to feel good this year only
to have to bounce the tax levy in future years.
Trustee Eilers said that even though the Council has full discretion to set the
tax levy, his opinion would be to set the levy within the tax cap.
Trustee Tucker stated that setting the levy below the tax cap should be based
on an accurate projection of the Village’s needs and would put pressure on
government to be more efficient. She was of the opinion that the levy should be
set at 3.3%.
Trustee Ritchell said that he would not be in favor of changing tax levy
amounts significantly from year to year and suggested that the levy be set
under 4.8%, but not as low as 3.3%. He added that it is the Council’s fiscal
duty as well as a show of good faith to set the levy below the tax cap.
Trustee Behles added that people will react to an increase in sales tax and
service fees and that over time, a reduction in the tax levy may be hard to
catch up. He also said the Council must be concerned over pension funds as
well.
In response to a question from Trustee Abell regarding how much of the levy
goes into the pension funds and from Trustee Eilers regarding a reserve fund
for pensions, Mr. McKee said that other than what the actuary has recommended,
no additional funds for pensions have been allocated, and that it was possible
to create a reserve fund to set aside money for this. He suggested a modest
amount over a number of years would be the best course of action.
Manager Williams added that the investment portfolio has done well, but there
should be a discussion with the investment consultant before putting aside
additional funds for pensions.
Trustee Berger commented that the Council’s conclusion on the tax levy should
be based on the figures and have a rational basis.
Manager Williams suggested that the Council consider the lowest of the new
development figures over the past few years which was 1.2%, added to the CPI of
3.3%, to equal 4.5%
Trustee Abell added that the actual number levied for 2004 was 4.5% which is
below this year’s 4.8% tax cap. He suggested setting the levy at 4.5% which he
thought would be a good long-term figure. He said that looking at this figure
in a way most beneficial to the taxpayers, the Village would be capturing the
estimated 1.5% for new development and would be setting the inflation portion
at 3.0% (less than the actual inflation rate of 3.3%).
Trustee Behles said that a 4.5% levy this year would ease having to do a higher
increase in the future, although there should be a rationale for this figure.
President Woodbury determined that the consensus of Council, excepting Trustee
Tucker, was to set the property tax levy at 4.5%, but also that the decision
should be unanimous. He said the discussion would continue at the Council’s
next regular meeting.
5)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:00 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
November 15, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 6, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
November 15, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Abell called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Jessica Tucker, and Herb
Ritchell. Absent: President Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant
Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and four persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Abell led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Pro Tem Abell announced that Item No. 6(d), Release of the Executive
Session minutes, had been removed from the Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   December 6, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend. Trustee Ritchell said that he might be
late.
b)   December 13, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 1, 2005, Regular Meeting. Mr. Abell suggested a minor change
before Trustee Eilers moved to adopt the minutes, as amended. Trustee Behles
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-20-2005 – Zoning Variation: 314 Walnut - Adoption.
President Pro Tem Abell noted that this item had been discussed at three
previous Council meetings. After Attorney Janega pointed out that the date of
the plans had been added to the corrected draft, Mr. Abell called for a motion
to adopt the ordinance. Trustee Tucker so moved, seconded By Trustee Ritchell.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker,
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: President
Woodbury.
b)   Ordinance No. M-21-2005 - Zoning Variation: 639 Spruce – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed this request for Maximum Building Size and Side Yard Setback
variations to permit the construction of an addition and a detached garage. He
explained that the room addition would be located at the rear of the house
where there is currently a one-story enclosed porch and the detached garage
would be located in approximately the same location as an existing two-car
attached garage. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the house is currently nonconforming
with respect to GFA and reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted
unanimously in favor of granting both the GFA and the side yard setback
variations.
Trustee Behles confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the applicant did not qualify
for the garage bonus because it is not located in the rear quarter of the lot
since the situation of the house on the property makes it impossible to locate
a garage in the rear quarter.
Trustee Tucker asked the applicant, Ken McAtamny, whether there had been any
objections from the neighbors. He replied that there had not been any and
explained that they are hoping to improve both the form and functionality of
the house.
Trustee Behles asked why the detached garage is not being attached to the
house. The applicant’s architect responded that it is because they are trying
to be sensitive to the light and air of the house.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Resolution No. R-36-2005 – Janssen’s Re-subdivision – Adoption. Mr. Norkus
reviewed this request to approve the reconfiguration and re-subdivision of two
existing lots at 781 and 785 Walden Road. Both properties are owned by the
applicant, who is proposing to move the boundary line between the two
properties so as to enlarge the north lot. He noted that the proposed
subdivision and existing improvements upon the proposed lots have been reviewed
for compliance with the Zoning Ordinance, and added that both structures and
associated improvements will continue to comply. Total development on two
parcels is not being increased or decreased, just being reallocated, and no
nonconformities are being created. Mr. Norkus reported that the Plan Commission
had voted unanimously to recommend approval of the proposed re-subdivision
subject to certain technical modifications; the expansion of an existing
sanitary sewer easement along the north lot line, and the granting of a water
and electric utility easement along the front of both lots.
Attorney Janega distributed and reviewed a new draft resolution making some
administrative corrections and incorporating the conditions discussed by Mr.
Norkus.
Trustee Tucker commented on the Plan Commission’s deliberations on this matter
and indicated that she supported the request.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
d)   Resolution R-37-2005 – Release of Executive Session Minutes – Adoption. As
noted previously, this item was removed from the Agenda.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1437 and 1438. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1437 in the amount of $693,833.38 and Warrant List No. 1438 in the
amount of $285,956.05. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
b)   Policy Direction: 382 Fairview. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
Maximum Building Size, Side Yard Setback, and Garage variations to permit the
construction of a new two-car attached garage and to convert the existing
attached garage into a family room. He reported that this request had received
an unfavorable recommendation from the Zoning Board of Appeals meaning that a
positive vote by a supermajority of the Trustees would be required to overturn
the ZBA decision. He pointed out that after the Zoning Board’s denial, the
applicants had revised their plans in order to make them more compliant,
removing the need for three of the originally requested variations. These are
the plans that are now before the Council and they have not been reviewed by
the Zoning Board of Appeals.
Trustee Abell inquired whether any consideration had been given to have two,
separate garage doors instead of one large one. Mr. D’Onofrio responded that in
his opinion, there will be a very tight turning radius with the one door and
that two doors would only aggravate that problem.
Trustee Behles asked whether the applicant had looked at reconfiguring the
driveway. Mr. D’Onofrio replied that it had been considered but that would
require an additional variation for impermeable lot coverage.
Trustee Eilers asked Mr. D’Onofrio to confirm that the Zoning Board had been
sympathetic to the petitioner’s request but didn’t think that enough
alternatives had been explored. Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that he believed that
to be true.
Mr. Behles noted that his biggest concern is about the increase in gross floor
area. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that although the existing home is under the
allowable GFA, it is only minimally so, and any addition would put the house
out of compliance.
Manager Williams confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the Council could (1) uphold
the ZBA decision, (2) ask the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting
the requested variations, or (3) send the matter back to the Zoning Board for
its review of the new plans.
The applicant, Randy Hano, and his representative from Airoom, Andrew Venamore,
explained that the goal of the project is to obtain a little more floor space
with the least disruption to the house itself as well as to the neighbors. Mr.
Hano said that some of the neighbors had expressed concern about building out
to the rear of the existing house.
Trustee Tucker asked whether the applicants would be amenable to making the
requested single garage door look like two doors, as other applicants have
agreed to do. Mr. Hano responded that he would be happy to agree to that.
Trustee Ritchell inquired whether the applicant was comfortable with the
turning radius for the garage. Mr. Hano and Mr. Venamore both stated that they
believe that it would be workable. Mr. Venamore indicated that the architects
on staff at Airoom had carefully reviewed the plans and were confident that
there would be sufficient turning room.
Trustee Ritchell remarked that it is unusual property and a uniquely shaped
lot, which presents a real problem to the owners. He said that while he is
interested in preservation and this is a relatively modest change, he is
concerned about the increased gross floor area. He indicated that he would like
to see the matter referred back to the Zoning Board.
Trustee Tucker said that she wants to encourage and preserve older homes, but
the additional bulk and density are issues, there being a limit to what can
reasonably be accommodated on this lot. She indicated that regardless of
whether it is returned to the Zoning Board or not, she was inclined to vote
favorably on the GFA variation but is troubled by the garage door issue.
Trustee Behles expressed a preference to see the matter referred back to the
Zoning Board, saying that the garage door issue is solvable, but even given the
undeniable difficulty of the site, additional bulk is a concern.
Trustee Berger agreed with Trustee Behles.
Trustee Eilers voiced support for the requested variation whether the matter is
returned to the Zoning Board or not, noting that given the configuration of the
lot, there is no way the petitioner can take advantage of the garage bonus.
President Pro Tem Abell indicated that he would like to have the Zoning Board
consider the matter again in view of the changed plans.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to send the matter back to
the Zoning Board. By voice vote, the motion passed unanimously.
10. Reports.
Trustees. Trustee Berger reported on her attendance at the Northwest Municipal
Conference meeting on November 9th. She said that updates were provided on such
issues as the Illinois State Toll Highway Authority improvements, current
legislative issues, cable franchising, and pandemic flu.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Winnetka Chamber of Commerce would be holding
Winterfest on December 10th and urged residents to shop locally.
11. Appointments.
President Pro Tem Abell suggested the appointment of Linda Pedian as the Plan
Commission’s representative to the Forestry Commission. Trustee Berger so
moved. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, and review of Executive Session Minutes pursuant to Sections 2(c)
(11), 2(c)(1), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(21) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 8:47 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:21 p.m. Present: President Pro
Tem Abell, Trustees Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
President Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
The meeting adjourned at 9:22 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
December 6, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 20, 2005)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 6, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Abell called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
President Woodbury and Trustee Herb Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee,
and three persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Abell led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   December 13, 2005, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   December 20, 2005, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 8, 2005, Study Session. President Pro Tem Abell suggested an
amendment and distributed copies to the Trustees. Trustee Eilers moved to
approve the minutes as amended. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   November 15, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger moved to approve the
minutes. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Woodbury arrived at 7:39. After he arrived he took the chair and
presided for the remainder of the meeting.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-21-2005 - Zoning Variation: 639 Spruce – Adoption. There
being no additional comments, President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt
the ordinance granting Maximum Building Size and Side Yard Setback variations
to permit the construction of an addition and a detached garage. Trustee Berger
so moved, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2005 – Planned Developments - Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed the substantive amendments to the introductory draft of the ordinance
that would implement regulations to govern the redevelopment of larger
commercial and multi-family sites.
Trustee Abell suggested and the Trustees discussed some minor language changes,
to add reference to the Green Bay Road corridor to the first recital and to
delete the 3-story height limitation.
President Woodbury remarked on the importance of retaining the restrictions
that are a part of the C-2 Overlay District. He said that he wants to be
certain that it is clear and understood that it is the intent of the Council
that the underlying zoning and the overlay stay in place.
President Woodbury also asked whether Ms. Janega foresaw that the Plan
Commission’s role would be to simply outline findings or to create a separate
body of findings that include recommendations and conditions for any planned
development project. Ms. Janega responded that what is contemplated by the
ordinance is that it not be a simple checklist, but that the Plan Commission
consider all aspects of a project as a whole to determine whether it is in
keeping with the Comprehensive Plan and that it also make recommendations as to
conditions, if appropriate.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend the draft ordinance
as suggested by the Village Attoreny and discussed by the Council. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Attorney Janega stated that the amended language will be incorporated into a
final draft for review and adoption at the next meeting.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2005 – Taxi Cab Regulations - Introduction. Attorney
Janega reviewed the draft of an ordinance that would amend the Village Code as
it pertains to taxi cabs to simplify the regulatory process but still protect
the public safety. The item was originally discussed at the October 4th Council
meeting, at which time the Council directed that the Code’s cleanliness
standards and quality of service provisions be retained. She explained that the
amended language establishes a new administrative process in which the cab
driver must obtain a certificate of qualification from the Village. In order to
do so, the driver must be appropriately licensed by the State and must maintain
certain liability insurance. She said that the provisions governing required
vehicle signage, the use of taxi stands, and solicitation of passengers have
been retained.
Ms. Janega explained that the focus of the new ordinance is on the driver.
Safety inspections are required, but to eliminate liability concerns, the
Village is not required to perform any testing as it no longer has the staff or
expertise to do so.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution R-37-2005 – Release of Executive Session Minutes – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that this resolution would authorize the release of
Executive Session minutes for which confidentiality is no longer required and
would permit the destruction of audio tape recordings of closed meetings held
from January 6 to May 18, 2004. She pointed out that the resolution
specifically states that the Council is not authorizing or consenting to the
public disclosure of the verbatim recordings of Executive Session meetings.
Trustee Abell remarked that it is important to have a consistent policy.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the resolution. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Ritchell arrived at 8:15 p.m.
e)   Ordinance No. M-22-2005 – 2005 Tax Levy - Introduction. Mr. McKee reviewed
the proposed tax levy for 2005. He pointed out that the Village’s share of the
total property tax bill has gradually decreased from 17.1% in 1993 to 13.9% in
2004 and noted that the Village’s new home rule status permits the Council to
levy taxes above tax caps. He said that staff estimates that if the Village
were still non-home rule, a 4.8% property tax increase would have been
possible. However, based on the policies, financial position, and projected
Budget, staff recommends a 2005 property tax levy, net of abatements, of
10,969,000, a 4.5% increase. Mr. McKee explained property tax mechanics, how
new development is calculated, and how it impacts the property tax.
Manager Williams reiterated that the tax levy will be slightly less than would
have been permitted under tax caps.
President Woodbury remarked that the Village’s percentage of the total property
tax bill will probably continue to decline as other taxing bodies’ portions
will continue to rise. He noted that whereas in previous years the setting of
the tax levy has been pro forma, because of becoming home rule, the Council has
spent a considerable amount of time examining income, expenses, and trends in
order to establish a thoughtful, appropriate policy. Moving forward, the
Village will continue to review what services are provided and how best to pay
for them. He pointed out that the Village’s continuing careful stewardship of
finances has made it possible to stay within tax caps even without their
constraints.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-23-2005 – 2003 Bond Abatement - Introduction. Mr. McKee
explained that this ordinance abates the taxes levied to pay the principal and
interest on the General Obligation Refunding Bonds, Series 2003, in keeping
with the Village’s intent at the time the bonds were issued.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
Bill Kasten, 1034 Westmoor Rd., raised concerns arising out of his experience
with developers in his neighborhood and distributed a letter outlining his
complaints. He asked the Council to send a clear message to all developers that
there are rules in Winnetka and that they need to be respected.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1439 and 1440. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1439 in the amount of $558,280.38 and Warrant List No. 1440 in the
amount of $1,202,588.28. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
President. President Woodbury reported on a recent meeting with Sen. Schoenberg
regarding the proposed expansion of Willow Road that was also attended by
elected officials of Northfield, Glenview, and Northbrook. He indicated that a
productive dialogue had begun on this ongoing issue and said that Winnetka
would be willing to participate in future discussions as they occur.
Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the next edition of the Winnetka Report,
with a new, updated format, would be mailed shortly.
Attorney. Attorney Janega reported on the recent favorable decision by the
Illinois Supreme Court regarding the 411 Linden case, bringing this litigation
to a close.
Trustee Abell congratulated Ms. Janega on her successful representation of the
Village in this matter.
Manager: Manager Williams announced that the IMEA had received its Certificate
of Convenience of Necessity for its proposed new electric facility, TC2, so it
can begin to move forward with the project.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury proposed the appointment of Janet Shen to complete the term
of Martha Pickrell on the Design Review Board. Trustee Behles so moved. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, and review of Executive Session Minutes pursuant to Sections 2(c)
(11), 2(c)(1), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(21) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 9:37 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:00 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:01 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
December 13, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: January 17, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 13, 2005 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Herb Ritchell. Also present: Assistant Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Director of Public
Works Steve Saunders, and approximately 5 persons in the audience.
2)   Discussion with Plan Commission on Affordable Housing.
Maureen Mitchell, chair of the Plan Commission, presented the Council with the
Commission’s plan to gather facts on affordable housing and initiatives, review
the information and involve the public, and make recommendations to the Council
at the conclusion of its study. Ms. Mitchell further outlined the Plan
Commission’s two-year plan to define affordable housing, set standards and
goals, determine techniques to facilitate affordable housing and keep the
community informed.
The Council discussed the Plan Commission’s timeline and determined that it
would be helpful to hire a consultant to assist the Commission in assessing the
needs of the community and in gathering facts. President Woodbury also asked
that the Commission work with the staff to plan for funds for the consultant
and try to dovetail their schedule into the Post Office Committee’s timeline
for development of the Post Office site.
Lucy Knight, a resident of Evanston who grew up in Winnetka, said that there
are many Winnetkans who may have need of affordable housing such as those who
have had a job loss, the loss of a spouse or who are seniors with medical costs
who cannot afford to remain in Winnetka. She suggested that the interfaith
housing group director, Gail Schecter, could be of help in locating a
consultant.
3)   Streetscape Update.
Director of Public Works, Steve Saunders, stated that the Council had directed
the staff to work the Business Community Development Committee to develop a
streetscape master plan for the business districts which is a conceptual idea
of the pedestrian traffic environment, the function of sidewalks and
intersections. He cited Moffat Mall as an example of a streetscape which is
friendly and pedestrian. He said that staff had solicited proposals from design
teams to provide the planning, design and engineering services and, out of 9
proposals received, the staff had selected 3 that had the qualifications, past
experience, best understanding of the project, and also met the project
schedule and the four goals that had been set to promote a unified design
theme, to unify all four business districts, to enhance the physical appearance
of commercial areas and to enhance the pedestrian environment and safety. Mr.
Saunders said that of these three design teams, Hitchcock/Civiltech, Planning
Resources/Baxter & Woodman and Lakota/SpaceCo/Christopher Burke, Lakota/SpaceCo
submitted the lowest fee proposal and in the staff’s opinion, also provides the
best fit for this project.
The Trustees discussed the project goals, the master plan, and the use of
LakotaSpaceCo as the design team and agreed to proceed with the streetscape
design plan.
4)   Replacement of Unimog Truck.
Mr. Saunders reported that the Village’s 1988 Mercedes Benz Unimog must be
replaced because parts for repairs can no longer be obtained and it can no
longer be used for sweeping or plowing. He explained that the funds to pay for
a replacement will come from the capital improvements budget which was
underspent.
Manager Williams added that the Unimog is a critical piece of equipment because
it operates the pump station if the electricity goes out and can also run
electric and hydraulic pumps. He said that through GSA pricing, the staff has
come up with a cost effective way to replace this equipment.
After a short discussion, the Trustees agreed that funds should be authorized
for the replacement of the Unimog.
5)   CodeRED Community Notification System.
Chief DeLopez explained that CodeRed is a internet-based emergency telephone
messaging system capable of delivering customized, pre-recorded emergency
telephone messages to 60,000 telephone numbers per hour. He said that the
communities of Winnetka, Wilmette, Glencoe, Northfield and Kenilworth propose
to jointly contract with Emergency Communications Network, Inc. through NORCOM
for this service and each Village will be responsible for a proportionate share
of the cost; Winnetka’s share of the $20,0000 annual fee would be $4,300. Chief
DeLopez and Fire Chief Smith recommend that the police and fire departments be
authorized to participate in the joint agreement for CodeRED.
The Trustees discussed the current system of notifying residents of
emergencies, the ability of the system to use GIS to contact residents in
specific areas, the designation of telephone numbers per household, and sharing
information about the system with New Trier and the elementary school district.
President Woodbury determined that the consensus of the Council was to proceed
with the joint contract for CodeRED once it has been reviewed by the Village
Attorney and the expenditure has been approved by the Village Manager.
6)   Turbine #4 Overhaul Status Report.
Interim Water and Electric Superintendent Rich Ciesla explained that while
Turbine #4 was being overhauled it was discover that additional repairs are
needed. He described the types of repairs and parts that need replacing,
stating that the work is necessary for safety reasons.
Manager Williams added that there was more wrong with the turbine than at first
thought and the repairs are needed to prevent anyone from getting hurt. He said
that this is considered an operating expense and the funds will come from the
electric fund.
President Woodbury determined that the consensus of the Council was to approve
the additional expense.
7)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
8)   Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The meeting
adjourned at 9:22 p.m.
