   Deputy Clerk
December 20, 2005
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 17, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
December 20, 2005, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Dave Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community
Development Mike D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, and two
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   January 3, 2006, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury announced that this
meeting has been cancelled.
b)   January 10, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 6, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell suggested the addition of
a sentence indicating that President Woodbury had assumed the Chair after his
arrival. Trustee Behles moved to approve the minutes, as amended. Trustee Abell
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-24-2005 - Zoning Variation: 442-444 Winnetka Ave. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Front Setback variation to
permit the construction of a six-car detached garage to serve the townhouses
situated on this property.
Trustee Ritchell asked whether there were any apartments in the neighboring
building whose view would be obstructed by the proposed garage. Douglas Clark,
the applicant, responded that there were not, as they would look out above the
ridge line of the proposed garage. Both he and Mr. D’Onofrio reported that they
had received no formal or informal objections from neighbors.
Mr. Clark reviewed the site plan and reported that Temple Court functions as a
driveway/alley for the residents of the townhouses and the neighboring mixed-
use building.
President Woodbury inquired whether the proposed garage would in any way impact
any public parking along Temple Court or pose any kind of a safety hazard. Mr.
D’Onofrio indicated that he would check this out before the next meeting.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce this ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2005 – Planned Developments - Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed the final draft amendments to the proposed ordinance that would
implement regulations to govern the redevelopment of larger commercial and
multi-family sites.
Trustee Eilers remarked that in his experience he has not encountered another
Village where there is a public hearing for final plan approval. He suggested
that after Winnetka has had some experience, the Council may want to take a
second look at this provision.
Attorney Janega commented that the ordinance had been drafted to be in keeping
with the provisions of the subdivision and special use requirements.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the ordinance as
discussed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2005 – Taxi Cab Regulations - Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed proposed amendments to the draft of the ordinance that would amend the
Village Code as it pertains to taxi cabs made pursuant to discussions with
Chief DeLopez and the Council’s discussion at the last meeting. She explained
that the ordinance would simplify the regulatory process but still protect the
public safety.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the ordinance as
discussed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance, as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-22-2005 – 2005 Tax Levy - Adoption. There being no further
comments, President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt the proposed 2005
property tax levy of $10,969,000, net of abatements, a 4.5% increase. Trustee
Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, so moved. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance No. M-23-2005 – 2003 Bond Abatement - Adoption. There being no
further comments, President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt the ordinance
abating the taxes levied to pay the principal and interest on the General
Obligation Refunding Bonds, Series 2003, in keeping with the Village’s intent
at the time the bonds were issued. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, so moved. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1441 and 1442. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1441 in the amount of $892,042.10 and Warrant List No. 1442 in the
amount of $611,566.24. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Streetscape Consultant. Mr. Saunders reviewed proposals to provide
planning, design, and engineering services needed to develop a master plan for
streetscape improvements in the Village’s four business districts. He explained
said that the plan will be a roadmap for future implementation of such
improvements by providing a template for final design and award of contracts
for constructing the improvements. Mr. Saunders identified the four goals of
the plan as being to: (1) promote a unified design theme across all of the
business districts; (2) unify the four business districts, visually and
programmatically; (3) enhance the physical appearance of commercial areas; and
(4) enhance pedestrian environment and safety.
After explaining the bid process, Mr. Saunders recommended that staff be
authorized to negotiate a contract with Lakota Group/Space Co. to complete a
Streetscape Master Plan in accordance with their proposal dated October 14,
2005, for a fee not to exceed $114, 579. He noted that although the project was
not included in the FY 2005-2006 budget, it could be accommodated because other
projects have come in under projected expenses.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Unimog. Mr. Saunders reviewed the Village’s efforts to procure a new
Unimog, a 4-wheel drive truck that features interchangeable rear bodies and
front attachments. He explained that the existing Unimog, which has now reached
the end of its service life, performs several valuable functions and is central
to the Village’
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the purchase as
outlined by Mr. Saunders. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
d)   Auditor Selection. Mr. McKee noted that at the September 13th Study
Session, the Council had discussed the benefits and drawbacks of rotating
auditors, given that Miller Cooper and Co., Ltd. has served as the Village’s
auditor since 1995. As a result of that discussion, Trustees Eilers and Berger
were asked to work with Mr. McKee to obtain proposals from other firms, and Mr.
McKee reviewed the process employed for selecting the proposed new auditor. He
reported that the group’s recommendation is to accept the proposal of Sikich
Gardner and Company, LLP to provide auditing services for the fiscal years
ending March 31, 2006, 2007, and 2008 at the price of $28,975, $30,425, and
$31,950, respectively.
Trustee Berger pointed out that the bid price is less than the Village is now
paying for the same services, and added that Sikich Gardner had just rotated
out of a similar position in Wilmette. Both she and Trustee Eilers commented
that they feel it is very important and appropriate to rotate auditors.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the selection of
Sikich Gardner and Company, LLP, as presented. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   President. President Woodbury encouraged the Trustees and all Village
residents to attend the Town Meeting on January 5th at 7:30 p.m. in the Skokie
School Auditorium.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the Business Community Development
Commission will begin to look into the concept of implementing a property
maintenance code for commercial and/or residential properties. He said that the
initial response to the idea from the Chamber of Commerce had been positive.
Trustee Abell reported that the NWMC would be holding a legislative brunch on
January 14th.
Trustee Berger inquired about attending Legislative Days in Springfield.
Manager Williams encouraged her to attend if she is interested in seeing the
process.
c)   Manager: Manager Williams reported that the Council would be holding
Budget Meetings to review the preliminary budget on February 15, 22, and 23. He
encouraged all interested persons to attend.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury proposed the reappointment of Keith Groebe to serve another
full term as Chair of the Building Review Committee. Trustee Abell so moved.
The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
He then proposed the reappointment of Karen Templeton to serve another full
term on the Building Review Committee. Trustee Behles so moved. The motion was
seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Finally, President Woodbury proposed the reappointment of Ann Wilder to serve
another full term on the Forestry Commission. Trustee Abell so moved. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and board
appointments, and review of Executive Session Minutes pursuant to Sections 2(c)
(11), 2(c)(1), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(21) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 9:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:15 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:16 p.m.
