2006
January 10, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 7, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 10, 2006 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Director of Community Development Mike
D’Onofrio, Interim Superintendent of Water and Electric Rich Ciesla,
Distribution Engineer Bryan McInturff, and approximately 6 persons in the
audience.
2)   Review of Parking Study.
Director of Public Works, Steve Saunders, introduced Mr. Rick Rich of Rich &
Associates, Southfield, MI, whose firm had conducted a study of the current
parking conditions in the East and West Elm business districts.
Mr. Rich described the process used to determine the parking needs in the study
area. He reported that the study concluded there is currently a deficit of 293
parking spaces in the two areas; 66 stalls for commuter parking and 230 for
employee and long term. He made the following recommendations:
   ·   develop a parking signage program to identify parking areas;
   ·   increase parking enforcement to encourage turnover of existing spaces;
   ·   advertise available parking with maps;
   ·   clarify permit parking; and
   ·   encourage employees to park off street
Mr. Rich said the study also considered options for additional parking at
Village parking lots, and identified the post office site and Lincoln Avenue as
the two most likely locations for parking structures.
The Trustees discussed whether there was a deficit of parking, the need for a
parking structure, funding, policy decisions related to additional parking, the
impact of the development of the post office site on existing parking, and
pedestrian access to parking. The Council then considered the option of not
building a parking structure, and instead, increasing enforcement, using
residential neighborhoods for employee parking and adding spaces where
possible. The Council also heard comments from Cecily Clark Michalak, Executive
Director of the Chamber of Commerce, Barbara Aquilino, Chair of the Business
Community Development Committee and Julian D’Esposito, Chair of the Ad Hoc Post
Office Committee.
Bob Horne, member of the Business Community Development Commission, asked the
Council to consider the idea of building senior housing above a parking
structure. Mr. Horne thought it possible to build parking below grade and
housing above grade on the Lincoln Avenue site within a 45’ height limitation
that could provide affordable housing for seniors. He said this would add
residents to the tax rolls and solve a housing need as well.
Manager Williams suggested that for the purpose of continuing discussions,
copies of the study should be provided to the Post Office Committee, Business
Community Development Committee, Plan Commission, Chamber of Commerce and
Community House. He said staff will continue to work with the committees on
details and funding and bring the discussion back to the Council by May.
President Woodbury asked the Post Office Committee to meet with Mr. Saunders to
discuss the impact of the post office development on the parking study, the
loss of parking spaces during construction of the post office site, and to
report to the Council as to how many parking spaces they think the site will be
able to provide. He also asked the Council to think about the fact that a
decision will probably be made before construction begins at the post office
site, noting that if the decision is to build a parking deck, the deck could be
completed in time to alleviate the parking deficit created by the development
of the site.
3)   Discussion: Modification of Power Adjustment..
Manager Williams explained the calculations for adjusting the cost of power to
continue to recover the actual costs and to also allow the Village to set aside
funds to pay the future debt for the Trimble County II power plant once the
current IMEA debt is paid. He said that staff recommends that the base rate be
reduced from 3.75 cents to 2.96 cents per kwh and that the rate of recovery be
changed from one-eleventh to one-eighth of the net outstanding balance of the
actual cost of power. He added that there would be no change to the consumer
but this adjustment would provide for a faster recovery.
The Council determined that the base rate be reduced as recommended, but that
the rate of recovery be changed to one-tenth of the outstanding balance in
order to more gradually recover the actual cost of power and agreed to re-visit
the adjustment in one year’s time.
Manager Williams said that a resolution to incorporate these changes would be
introduced in March at a regular Village Council meeting.
4)   Turbine #4 Overhaul.
Interim Superintendent of Water and Electric, Rich Ciesla, reported on the
progress of Turbine #4’s overhaul and informed the Council of an additional
expenditure of $11,000 for an unexpected and necessary repair which brings the
total cost of the project to $289,000.
5)   Executive Session 
No executive session was held.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:50 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
