January 17, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 7, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 17, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Acting Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, and
nine persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   February 7, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Trustees present, except
Trustee Abell, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 14, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 13, 2005, Study Session. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   December 20, 2005, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-24-2005 - Zoning Variation: 442-444 Winnetka Ave. -
Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio commented on several issues raised at the last meeting
regarding this request for a Front Setback variation to permit the construction
of a six-car detached garage to serve the townhouses situated on this property.
He reported that the Village’s Traffic Engineer is not concerned about this
request from a traffic safety perspective, as the request simply replaces
existing surface parking with garage parking in much the same location, so
there is no additional encroachment and no significant change to what now
exists. He also indicated that from a policy standpoint, it would not be in the
Village’s best interests to vacate Tempel Court because it provides important
life safety access to adjacent properties.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-1-2006 – Zoning Variation: 1422 Edgewood - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Side Yard Setback and Nonconformities
variations to permit the expansion of an existing second floor dormer and
window replacement in a nonconforming wall that encroaches in a side yard as a
part of a larger home remodeling project. He noted that a variation for the
same work was granted in 2003 but construction did not begin within the
required 12-month time frame and that all of the other improvements meet all
other applicable zoning regulations. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 5 to 0 to recommend approval of the variations.
Attorney Janega informed the Council that this ordinance and the other two
ordinances on the agenda for introduction were "shell" ordinances that should
be considered preliminary drafts, and that all three will be amended to bring
them into final form for adoption.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance No. M-2-2006 – Zoning Variation: 382 Fairview - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum Building Size, Side Yard Setback,
and Garage variations to permit the construction of a one-story addition. He
noted that this case was originally considered for policy direction at the
Council’s November 15, 2005, meeting and was referred back to the Zoning Board
because the applicant had made changes to the plans that were not reviewed by
that board. Subsequently, the Zoning Board voted 5-0 to recommend approval of
the variations.
Andrew Venamore, the applicant’s representative from Airoom, indicated that the
requested modifications to the appearance of the garage door had been
incorporated into the plans so that although it will, in fact, be a single
door, it will appear to be two separate doors.
There was a consensus among the Trustees that this is the best way to
accommodate the need for changes given the unusual configuration of the lot but
that the property is now maxed out in terms of gross floor area.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-3-2006 – Park District Special Use Permit: 939 Green Bay
Rd. - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request from the Park District to
demolish the existing shelter in Hubbard Woods Park and construct a new shelter
in approximately the same location as the existing shelter, including two
handicapped accessible bathrooms. The primary use of the building would be as a
shelter for users of the Hubbard Woods ice skating rink. He noted that a
parking study commissioned by the Park District concluded that there is
adequate parking on the surrounding streets and in the parking deck not only
for the ice rink shelter users but also for occasional larger events that might
be held in the park. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Design Review Board, the
Plan Commission, and the Zoning Board of Appeals had approved the Park
District’s request for a Special Use Permit.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, explained that this project has been in
the District’s long range plans since the late 1990’s. As it stands now, the
building needs a new roof, the bathrooms are not ADA compliant, the plumbing
doesn’t meet Code, and water and sewer upgrades are needed. In response to a
question from President Woodbury, she indicated that the bids had not yet been
opened but would be reported with recommendations to the Board at its February
9th meeting.
Trustee Abell expressed some concern about increased traffic turning off of
Merrill and its impact on pedestrian and vehicular safety. He confirmed with
Ms. McElroy that the Park District was not anticipating a significant increase
in the park’s utilization. Ms. McElroy said that there would be some new
programs and that the Park District is working with the Chamber of Commerce to
enhance existing events, but that they did not anticipate noticeable change in
usage.
Trustee Eilers pointed out that the Plan Commission had mentioned that perhaps
the Special Use Permit should address conditions relating to traffic concerns.
Trustee Tucker remarked that it was the Plan Commission’s intent to make the
Council aware of a potential problem with an eye toward perhaps attempting some
traffic calming to be monitored and adjusted as needed.
Manager Williams commented that the Green Bay/Merrill intersection does not
meet the State’s standards for signalization, which would be required given
that Green Bay Road is under IDOT control. He noted that in conjunction with
the streetscape study, the Village might look at the possibility of
constructing pedestrian bump-outs at the intersection.
Mr. Eilers remarked that the traffic problems at the intersection will exist
regardless of the proposed improvements in the Park.
George Regal, the project architect from Green Associates, reviewed the site
plan and the proposed plans.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, spoke in opposition to construction of a new shelter.
Acknowledging that the Council is limited in its decision-making by the land
use functions it performs, he focused his comments on issues that fall within
the purview of the Council, i.e., the parking impact on commuters and customers
and pedestrian and vehicular traffic safety. He urged the Council to couple the
Special Use Permit to safety improvements.
Attorney Janega commented on the scope of the Council’s review, noting the
special use standards and adding that the ultimate finding is whether or not
the proposed use complies with all of the Village's ordinances. She indicated
that the final ordinance would clearly reflect any findings, such as traffic,
that are consistent with the evidentiary record. She noted that advisory bodies
often express concerns about various issues, but that the Council has to look
at the specific impact the use will have and what conditions can be imposed to
address situations that arise from that use. In this case, most of the issues
relate to vehicular/commuter traffic, rather than to the use of the Park
itself. In addition, the Council has to consider that the Village does not have
independent authority to control the crossing over a State-controlled road.
What the Council can do is ask for further study and monitoring in conjunction
with the streetscape project planned for the area.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Resolution No. R-1-2006 – Annual MFT Resolution - Adoption. Manager
Williams reviewed the annual Motor Fuel Tax Resolution, which would authorize
the transfer of $125,000 from the Motor Fuel Tax Fund, Winnetka’s share of the
State gasoline tax, to the General Fund for street maintenance. He explained
that it is a transfer that is budgeted annually in order to offset maintenance
expenses; the remainder of the funds is earmarked for capital improvements.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the resolution. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1445 and 1446. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1445 in the amount of $629,769.90 and Warrant List No. 1446 in the
amount of $424,109.69. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Annual Vehicle Sticker. Mr. McKee reviewed a request from the Winnetka
Woman’s Club to designate its logo on the 2008 Village vehicle sticker. Trustee
Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the request. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Auditor Contract. Mr. McKee reported that the finalized Engagement Letter
from the Village’s new auditor, Sikich Gardner, had been received. He said that
in response to the Village's request, Sikich Gardner has deleted the last two
sentences of the final paragraph of the Management Responsibilities section,
which had limited the auditor's liability. He indicated that the Village
Attorney had reviewed and approved the Agreement.
Trustee Eilers raised a question about the fact that the proposal was for three
years but the Engagement Letter only addresses one year. Mr. McKee stated that
he did not believe that the Letter needs to be amended or re-approved on an
annual basis because the approved bid was for three years, but he indicated
that he would confirm that before the letter is signed.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the Engagement
Letter from Sikich Gardner substantially in the form submitted. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid No. 05-020: Backwash Repair. Mr. Ciesla reviewed the bids for a
replacement backwash line to ensure that the Village’s Water Plant continues to
operate without interruption. He reported staff’s recommendation that the bid
be awarded to Independent Mechanical, Inc. in the amount of $93,848.00.
Manager Williams added that it is critical that the backup, secondary system be
replaced.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Water Department Cost of Service Study Award. Mr. Ciesla reviewed the bids
and the bidding process for a cost-of-service and rate study for the Water
Department. He noted that this work was not included in the FY 2005-2006 budget
but that the Park District had requested rate relief, so it was concluded that
now was an appropriate time to perform such a study. Mr. Ciesla reported
staff’s recommendation that the award be made to HDR in the amount of $26,857.
Manager Williams commented that the Village hasn’t done a water rate study in
at least fifteen years and it is important to make sure that each class of
customer is paying the appropriate amount. He indicated that while he was not a
part of the process and that there is some subjectivity involved in evaluating
the different bids, he believes that HDR is the best-qualified bidder to
perform the work.
President Woodbury remarked that this is consistent with the Council’s
commitment to the citizens to take a careful look at the structure of cost and
services.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Electric Plant Window Replacement. Mr. Ciesla reviewed bids to replace the
remaining old windows on the east side of the Electric Plant that do not
function well and have no screens. He reported staff’s recommendation that the
bid be awarded to Auburn Corporation in the amount of $44,375.
After some discussion as to the brand and performance level of the proposed
windows, this matter was tabled until the next meeting.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Tucker reported that the Plan Commission is continuing
to gather facts on Affordable Housing. She also mentioned her recent attendance
at the NWMC January meeting at which State and regional issues affecting local
municipalities were discussed. Topics included such issues as smoking bans,
eminent domain, bicycle legislation, and stormwater management. She said that
she thinks it is important for Winnetka to be consistently represented and to
participate in their programs.
Trustees Berger and Abell concurred.
Trustee Abell reported on their recent attendance at a legislative branch
meeting.
Trustee Behles reported that the lights in the Forest Glen pillars are now
operational.
Trustee Eilers reported that the BCDC and the Chamber have established a joint
committee to study the feasibility and desirability of implementing a property
maintenance code. He said that in anticipation of future developments the BCDC
will also be looking at development incentives used in other communities.
b)   Village Attorney. Ms. Janega reported that she will be speaking on the
topic of Affordable Housing at the upcoming January 30th meeting of the League
of Women Voters.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, board
appointments, and review of Executive Session Minutes pursuant to Sections 2(c)
(11), 2(c)(1), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(21) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 9:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:30 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
