Recording Secretary
February 7, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 21, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 7, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Trustees
David Abell, Tom Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public
Works Steve Saunders, Acting Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, Village
Forester Jim Stier and 20 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   February 14, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 15, 2006, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   February 21, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 10, 2006, Special Meeting. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   January 10, 2006 Study Session. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   January 17, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-1-2006 – Zoning Variation: 1422 Edgewood – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments made since introduction of this
ordinance on January 17th to grant side yard setback and nonconformities
variations to permit the expansion of an existing second floor dormer and
window replacement in a nonconforming wall that encroaches in a side yard as a
part of a larger home remodeling project.
President Woodbury asked for comments and there being none, called for a motion
to adopt Ordinance M-1-2006.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the ordinance and
by voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles moved to adopt Ordinance M-2-2006
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
b)   Ordinance No. M-2-2006 – Zoning Variation: 382 Fairview - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments made to this ordinance since
introduction on January 17th that would grant maximum building size, side yard
setback, and garage variations to permit the construction of a one-story
addition with front-facing garage doors. She explained that because a revised
drawing has not yet been submitted by the applicant, the ordinance contains a
condition that the variations be granted subject to its receipt and also gives
the Director the authority to determine whether the revised design complies
with the condition that the 16-foot garage door be designed so as to have the
appearance of two single doors.
President Woodbury asked for comments and there being none, called for a motion
to amend Ordinance M-2-2006.
Trustee Tucker moved to amend the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt Ordinance M-2-2006 as amended, seconded by
Trustee Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent. Trustees Abell and Eilers.
c)   Ordinance No. M-3-2006 – Park District Special Use Permit: 939 Green Bay
Rd. - Adoption. Mr. Norkus reviewed a request from the Park District to
demolish the existing shelter in Hubbard Woods Park and construct a new shelter
in approximately the same location as the existing shelter, including two
handicapped accessible bathrooms.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments to the ordinance since introduction.
She noted that there had been substantial amendments, and that the recitals now
begin by describing the Park District as a corporate entity, followed by a
description of activities at the park and field house. The revised ordinance
also contains a reference to ADA standards, notes that the restrooms currently
do not meet ADA accessibility standards and that the Park District has included
the renovation of the field house in their long range plans since 1988. Ms.
Janega stated that the rest of the recitals in the ordinance are taken from the
record and the Village’s Comprehensive Plan. She stated that the recitals
describe the existing facility and how the new one compares with it, and then
describe the proceedings of the Plan Commission, ZBA and Design Review Board,
the KLOA parking study and the Village Engineer’s report on accident and
traffic counts. The recitals address the comments made in the course of the
proceedings, and then explain how the record shows the lack of substance to
those opposing claims. The recitals describe the park and state that the field
house has been present for some 50 years. The recitals also state that the park
is a central feature of the Hubbard Woods shopping district. In addition, the
recitals state the traffic volume on Green Bay Road is about 11,000 cars per
day, which helps provides a basis for finding that the Hubbard Woods shopping
district has existed for some time with the park being no detriment. The
findings also state why using the restrooms at the train station is not a
suitable alternative for the restrooms at the park. The ordinance then recites
additional facts pertaining to the special use application, including its
consistency with the Comprehensive Plan and a determination that the use falls
within the scope of "nonessential public use" under Chapter 17.56 of the
Village Code. The ordinance also contains the general condition requiring that
construction begin within 12-months. However, because the use already exists,
the conditions specifically state that failing to proceed with the new facility
will not negate the entire special use. The revised draft also requires final
landscaping review by the DRB, with appeal to the Council if it's not resolved
by the DRB. Finally, as was done with the 411 Linden special use permit, there
is a 12-month reporting requirement, although this 12-month period is measured
from date of the certificate of occupancy.
President Woodbury asked for questions or comments from the Council and the
Park District representatives and as there were none, called for comments from
the audience.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower Road, objected to the draft of the ordinance,
questioned the findings of fact, and stated that the Council should have
conducted a hearing before the ordinance was drafted. He also raised the
following points pertaining to the facts recited in the ordinance:
   §   Mr. Kates stated that the current structure is not in violation of the
ADA because it is grandfathered in and nothing is required to be done. He added
that by federal regulations, there is a 10% limitation of the expenditure, so
that if there is $40,000 dollars of repairs to the structure, the Park District
is only required to spend $4,000 for ADA compliance.
   §   Mr. Kates said that there are no classes currently being held and that
it's senseless to put this in the ordinance.
   §   Mr. Kates objected to the comments about the train station saying that
they are in opposition to what the Council has previously done. He said that
the Village took over the leases so that something could be done with the train
station, but the ordinance states that any expanded use of it would violate the
Metra lease.
   §   In reference to the traffic study, Mr. Kates commented that the minutes
of the Plan Commission hearing say that Merrill and Green Bay should be
monitored for traffic safety. He said that the ordinance is requiring that the
intersection be monitored for a year, but traffic problems exist now and are
not only pedestrian in nature as once stated by Trustee Abell. He maintained
that the type of traffic study conducted by the Village staff leaves a lot to
be desired, explaining that he once worked for an engineering firm and that
someone sitting at the intersection for 3 to 6 hours during the week and then
for a while on Saturday doesn’t make any sense when most of the commuter trains
come in after 6:00 p.m. He suggested that if a traffic study is going to be
done, that it should be done right.
   §   Mr. Kates stated that the findings as written go into economics in
reference to the question of a restroom for the public, which is normally
something that wouldn’t be in the ordinance. He noted that the 1996 ordinance
for Indian Hill is not as detailed as the one before the Council now. He added
that, when President Woodbury was chairman of the Zoning Board, there was a
hearing on the issue of having a bathroom facility for Indian Hill Park. At the
hearing, the vice chair said that the building was to be open by permit only
and when there are activities in the day time or in the summer when there are
more hours. However, Mr. Kates said that the facility is usually locked.
   §   Mr. Kates stated that, in reference to the Hubbard Woods Park, the ZBA's
minutes show that the Park District represented to the ZBA that the facility
would be open from 6 a.m. to 10 p.m. Mr. Kates stated that when the Plan
Commission met, the hours changed to 9 a.m. to 8 p.m. during certain months. He
said that it remains to be seen whether this will be a repeat of Indian Hill
Park. He suggested that this could be prevented by posting the hours and also
imposing a condition that it be reviewed after a year. He then stated that if
the same thing is done here as was done with Indian Hill, then why is it being
done and why is the money being spent to do it. He added that the Council has
it in their power to put this into the ordinance to assure public access to the
facility.
Mr. Kates summarized by saying that the Council hasn’t heard the evidence to
support the supposed findings of fact and they are doing a disservice to the
residents as far as the train station goes. He hoped that the Council would do
something to assure that that it serves the purpose it claims to serve.
President Woodbury stated that Attorney Janega would respond to Mr. Kates'
comments and the Park District would address access and safety.
Attorney Janega responded to Mr. Kates’ comments. She first explained the
procedure, saying that the Plan Commission, Zoning Board and Design Review
Boards are advisory boards to the Council, and are charged with holding the
public hearings on zoning requests and making recommendations to the Council.
She further explained that the whole record, including the minutes of those
hearings are sent to the Council, which can accept or amend or ask for
clarification of the information. The Council is not required to hear all of
the evidence again, and the minutes and records from the Plan Commission,
Zoning Board of Appeals and Design Review Board are used to draft the findings
in the ordinances. Ms. Janega added that, in addition to the record, the
Council can also take notice of the Comprehensive Plan and its other official
acts and records.
Ms. Janega stated that when she drafts an ordinance, she attempts to recite
facts based on the record and on the standards for special use permits and that
she sees her role as providing the Council with as much detail as she can, and
then the Council is free to modify the draft ordinance and to eliminate or add
things as they think necessary.
Turning to Mr. Kates' comments about the ADA, Ms. Janega stated that the
ordinance does not say that the existing structure violates the ADA, and that
what it says is that the current restrooms don't meet the ADA's standards. She
added that Mr. Kates' observation about the 10% standard for repairs doesn’t
apply here, because this will be a new building, and new construction must
comply with ADA.
Regarding Mr. Kates' comments on the traffic study, Attorney Janega stated
that, while the Plan Commission had expressed concern about traffic, the
Village's engineering staff had studied the traffic and concluded that the
traffic problem in that area is not because of the park. She added that the
additional 12 months of study was included in the ordinance to provide an
opportunity to study this. She also noted that the Village's engineering staff
had analyzed accidents at the intersection of Merrill and Green Bay and
concluded that none were related to park traffic.
Addressing Mr. Kates' observations about Indian Hill Park, Ms. Janega stated
that Indian Hill Park is not relevant to the Hubbard Woods special use permit
application and that the hours and usage of that facility are matters for the
Park District's board to consider. She added that, if the Council wants her to
incorporate something on hours for Hubbard Woods Park, she would do that,
although she cautioned against it because it would require oversight of the
Park District's activities.
Explaining the factual findings and recitals in the draft ordinance, Ms. Janega
said they are typical of recitals she has prepared for the Council in the past.
She noted that the law on special uses has changed since the Indian Hill
special use permit was approved, and the Illinois courts now treat the granting
of a special use permit as a quasi-judicial act of the Council, so it requires
detailed factual findings pertaining to the six standards that are in the
Zoning Ordinance. She explained that the ordinance's recitals therefore address
the objections to the proposed new field house and include a statement that the
Village Council defers to the judgment of the Park District, which is charged
by law with determining how funds are used. She said she included a recital in
this ordinance that one of the objections to the special use was based on the
cost of the project. Ms. Janega then offered to go over all of the recitals and
explain to the council where they appear in the findings of the various boards
that held hearings.
Responding to Mr. Kates' comments that the figures in the ordinance on current
usage of the field house are incorrect and that many of the uses cited don't
exist now, Ms. Janega explained that she took the numbers from pages attached
to the KLOA study and that, if she misread the record and the numbers of
activities are actually proposed for the future rather than being in existence
now, she would correct the ordinance. She suggested letting the Park District's
representatives clarify the issue.
President Woodbury asked Ms. Janega if there was anything in the points Mr.
Kates raised on that issue that was cause for concern about the findings.
Ms. Janega replied that there is not, explaining that the recitals are drawn
from the record, including the KLOA study and the Public Works staff's
engineering study. She added that the reference to trips per day on Green Bay
Road is drawn directly from a map in the Comprehensive Plan, and pointed out
that Green Bay Road is a State road identified in the Comprehensive Plan as a
primary road. She added that the numbers in the Comprehensive Plan are
consistent with the numbers in the traffic counts done by the Village's
engineering staff, so she is confident that those numbers are correct.
Referring to the finding that responds to the objection that the KLOA study
didn't consider parking at the 26-unit building at Merrill and Green Bay, Ms.
Janega stated that the building's parking needs were necessarily considered,
because the KLOA study addresses current parking, and the building's residents
have to park in the neighborhood somewhere because the building does not have
off-street parking. Concluding, Ms Janega said the only comment raised that
concerns her is the comment about the numbers for current programs, and
suggested that the Park District clarify the record.
President Woodbury asked for questions from the Council on the recitals in the
ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell stated he had no questions, noting that Ms. Janega
consistently gave the Council more detail in the draft ordinances and that the
findings in this ordinance reflect the record and other discussions.
Trustee Behles said that Ms. Janega’s comment that the testimony from the
hearings is incorporated into the minutes and is then incorporated into the
findings is responsive to how the Council gets the information.
Trustee Tucker said that the Plan Commission minutes accurately reflect the
discussion during the hearing and she had no concerns.
Trustee Berger commented that she would like to make sure that Mr. Kates'
concerns were addressed about the use of the facility.
Trustee Tucker asked if it was representative of the area if the traffic study
was done from 3-6 p.m. on one day.
Mr. Saunders replied that traffic counts are taken by setting automated traffic
markers in place. The markers record the traffic and pick up the peak hours and
then, during the week following the automated count, a manual count is done. He
said that the automated count identified the hours of 3 p.m. to 6 p.m. on
weekdays and Saturday as peak traffic hours. He added that the use at other
times would be picked up in the 12-month study period.
Mr. Saunders further stated that nothing indicates that the intersection at
Merrill and Green Bay Road is hazardous, although sometimes left turns at this
location can be difficult. He said that the site distances are okay and there
are sufficient gaps in traffic. He did, however, suggest that improvements
could be made as part of the streetscape project, such as shortening the
distance pedestrians have to walk across the street by moving the curb out to
the end of the parking spaces. He said that this would increase the line of
site for pedestrians.
Ms. Janega pointed out that the relevant standard for the special use is
whether the proposed use has a negative impact on traffic and congestion in the
public ways and whether adequate measures have been taken to minimize those
negative impacts. She noted that for this particular application, where there
is already a facility in place, the question is whether the proposed new
facility will have negative impact on pedestrian and vehicular congestion and
if so, have adequate measures been taken to address this. Ms. Janega explained
that the findings say that evidence shows that traffic incidents are not
related to the park, but to the general traffic in area supports the conclusion
that the proposal adequately addresses the impact of this use.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, addressed Mr. Kates' concerns about the
scheduled activities and informed the Council that the activities in the
ordinance are programs they plan to offer, but do not offer currently, as are
the weekend activities. She said that the existing uses are special events, the
plant sale, concerts in the park, the sidewalk sale, Winterfest and Winter
Carnival, rentals of the facility (which totaled 14 last year), and the outdoor
ice rink, which is about 10 to 15 days per year subject to the weather. She
confirmed that no classes are currently offered there. Ms. McElroy said that
park hours are posted throughout the district and are from 6 a.m. to 10 p.m.
and that access to the building will be provided from the outside with a lock
programmed for the hours it is open. She added that the proposed hours from
April to October are 9 a.m. to 8 p.m. In answer to a question from Trustee
Tucker, Ms. McElroy said that it would be no problem to post the hours.
Ms. McElroy explained that in 1992, the Park District put together a plan with
the intention that all park facilities be made ADA compliant and it is a
responsibility that the Park board believes they owe to the public. She said
that, if the restrooms are repaired to provide the ADA accessibility, the
upgraded restrooms would be larger than the current bathrooms, and would
encroach on the space for the rest of the facility. She said the Board felt is
would be better to construct a new building.
In response to Mr. Kates' comments about access to the train station bathrooms,
Ms. Janega said she would check on whether it may be contrary to what was
intended in the Metra leases. She then clarified what amendments to the
ordinance were necessary as a result of the discussion. She asked if the
Council wanted a condition added regarding the posting of hours.
Trustee Berger replied that, although it would be nice for the public to know
when the bathrooms are open, she didn’t think the Council should tie the Park
District to certain hours.
Trustee Behles agreed and suggested that instead of imposing a condition, the
ordinance should state the hours would be posted as determined by the Park
Board.
Ms. Janega suggested that the Council amend the ordinance as provided in the
agenda materials, and that she would then prepare further amendments, as
discussed tonight, for final action at the next meeting.
Trustee Tucker moved to amend the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, and
by voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury stated the ordinance would be continued to the Council’s
meeting on February 21st for adoption.
d)   Ordinance No. M-4-2006 – Zoning Variation: 194 Fuller Lane - Introduction.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a
variation from the maximum buildings size requirements of the Zoning Ordinance
to permit the construction of a second floor dormer addition at 194 Fuller
Lane. He explained that the second floor shed dormer will expand the master
bedroom to accommodate a walk-in closet and master bath and that at its
December meeting, the ZBA voted unanimously to recommend that the variation be
granted.
In response to questions from the Trustees, Mr. D’Onofrio stated that the
existing footprint of the home will remain the same, the basement exposure is
at the back of the house and there will be one full bathroom both upstairs and
downstairs.
President Woodbury asked for any further discussion and there being none,
called for a motion to introduce the ordinance.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance M-4-
2006. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-5-2006 – Zoning Variation: 1128 Mt. Pleasant –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a variation from the
maximum building size requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the
construction of a 34.38-foot wide, front facing garage at a new home at 1128
Mt. Pleasant. He explained that the property is unique in that it is considered
a corner lot, although the street along one side of the lot is not used. He
said that this variation request is specific to that side of the property and
the garage doors will face the unused Glendale Avenue.
Manager Williams added that Glendale is a fictitious street in this location
and clarified with Mr. D’Onofrio that if this property were an interior lot, no
variation would be needed.
Attorney Janega further stated that this would be a problem for any home on a
corner lot and is not a stretch for the relief that is sought. She added that
the intent of the zoning ordinance is to keep garage doors off the block face
and in this instance it is a hardship to find a conforming location for the
garage because the property is considered a corner lot.
President Woodbury asked for comments.
Trustee Berger said that in general she would vote against a variation for new
construction, but in this case, there are particular circumstances. She
objected to a comment in the record that was made by Mrs. Birnoff who said that
this variation would give the owner an increase in the reasonable return and
asked that the record reflect that the variation, if granted, would be based on
the applicant having a reasonable return. Trustee Berger did not want to give
the impression that she is voting for this in order to give the owner a
reasonable return.
Trustee Tucker agreed with Trustee Berger as did Trustee Ritchell and added
that she also did not want to give that impression. She emphasized that a
distinction has been made in this case because the property is unique in that
Glendale is an unused street. She stated that she would not vote for a
variation for a side yard setback, for example, for new construction.
Attorney Janega explained that the language in the ordinance does not state
that the variation would give a reasonable return. She further explained that
for the Council’s purposes, they only have to consider particular hardships and
practical difficulties related to the land, which is the case in this instance.
She added that it is not necessary to include the ‘reasonable return” language
in the ordinance.
A member of the audience asked to have it clarified that this is new
construction and said that she thought that new construction was not supposed
to have garage doors face the street.
President Woodbury replied that although it is new construction, in this
particular situation, the street the garage doors would face is not used as a
street and this variation request is only one of two exceptions the Council has
considered.
There being no further comments, President Woodbury called for a motion to
introduce the ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker moved to introduce Ordinance M-5-
2006. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-6-2006 – Zoning Variation: 545-L Hill Terrace –
Introduction. Mr. D;Onofrio reviewed this request for a variation from the
front yard setback requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the
installation of an air conditioner condenser at 545-L Hill Terrace. He said
that a conforming location in the courtyard is not possible because the
condominium by-laws prohibit air conditioner condensers there because of noise.
He added that landscaping would conceal the condenser and that the ZBA
recommended unanimously to approve this request.
In response to questions from Trustee Behles and Tucker, Mr. D’Onofrio stated
that the condenser will be next to the unit on the ground floor and that the
condominium association has voiced no objections.
President Woodbury asked for comments from the audience and there being none,
called for a motion to introduce the ordinance.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles moved to introduce Ordinance M-6-
2006. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Winngate Condominiums: 710, 714, 718, 724 Green Bay Road. President
Woodbury announced that the Council would consider the approval of a
certificate of appropriateness, adoption of a plat of consolidation and a
request for waiver of demolition permit fees on this condominium proposal.
   1)   Certificate of Appropriateness. Mr. Norkus reviewed the proposal,
explaining that the condominium development involves the demolition of 4
residences, each on separate lots, a plat of consolidation to join the lots
into a single 32,000 square foot lot and the construction of a 2.5-story, 12-
unit condominium building with parking beneath it. He reported that the Design
Review Board had voted unanimously that the revised plan is consistent with
design guidelines and the Forester’s recommendations, subject to the condition
that the applicant address the concerns of the neighbor at 659 Walden for
landscaping improvements.
The Trustees discussed the allowable FAR, building height, provisions for
drainage and stormwater runoff, parking, landscaping and tree preservation and
noted that all zoning requirements had been met.
John Myefski, architect for the project, presented drawings of the condominium
proposal, and responded to questions from the Council. He noted that the
architecture is in the Tudor style particular to Winnetka and that he and the
owner had worked with the neighbors and the Village Forester to address
concerns about drainage, the removal and replacement of trees and landscape
screening. Mr. Myefski also described the system designed for stormwater runoff
and retention which he believes will improve drainage for the neighboring
properties. In response to a question from Mr. Williams, he said that in the
case of a power failure, the storage system will work off of the Village’s own
water pressure or if that fails, will fill the lower level of the parking area.
He then answered questions from the Trustees about the targeted market for the
condominiums, noting that they may be attractive to people who are downsizing.
President Woodbury then asked for comments from the audience.
Brian Spier, 699 Walden, and Mrs. Midge Powell, 693 Walden, stated their
appreciation for the cooperation they had received from everyone and had the
following concerns and comments:
   §   how the project will look at the rear of the building;
   §   which trees will be jeopardized and which will be moved;
   §   that the plan is not as thorough as it could be;
   §   that the swale will be detrimental to an old oak;
   §   drainage;
   §   the height of the fence that borders 693 Walden; and
   §   requested that taller trees be planted to create a good buffer
Mr. Myefski stated that he and Mr. Busscher will continue to work with the
neighbors to address their concerns and added that they do not intend to
replace the fence that is there currently which is 6 feet tall. He also said
that they are working with the Village Forester, the neighbor’s arborist and
their own arborist to see that the plantings are a mixture of species.
Upon further discussion, the Council determined that the approval of the
Certificate of Appropriateness should be conditioned upon a final landscape
plan approved by the Council. There was also some discussion about the
management of the construction site and sidewalk access that Attorney Janega
said would be addressed in a development agreement between the owner and the
Village which is required for these types of projects.
Trustee Behles moved to approve the issuance of a Certificate of
Appropriateness for building and site alterations at 710-724 Green Bay Road,
subject to plans approved by the Design Review Board on December 14, 2005, and
as further modified by the revised landscape plans dated January 25, 2006, and
further subject to a final landscape plan to be approved by the Council.
Trustee Tucker seconded the motion and by voice vote, the motion carried.
   2)   Resolution R-2-2006 – Plat of Consolidation - Adoption. Mr. Norkus
reported that the Plan Commission met twice to consider the plat of
consolidation. After receiving satisfactory information on the tree removal and
tree preservation on the site as well as information on traffic safety along
Green Bay Road, the Plan Commission voted favorably for approval, subject to
conditions such as the provision of utility easements and signature blocks. The
Plan Commission also amplified comments made at the Design Review Board to add
landscaping to address the perceived height of the building.
There being no further discussion or comments, President Woodbury called for a
motion to adopt the Plat of Consolidation.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt Resolution R-2-2006 as submitted approving the
proposed Winngate Consolidation, subject to the provision of a 5-foot utility
easement along the east lot line, and subject to the provision of signature
blocks for the Water and Electric Department and Public Works Department and
subject to the condition that the consolidation approval does not approve the
site plan nor the development plan. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion and by
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
   3)   Waiver of Demolition Fees. Mr. Busscher addressed the Council
requesting that the Council adjust the individual demolition fees for the 4
houses since the demolitions will be completed at the same time and will only
require the Village staff’s services once.
Mr. Norkus explained that Mr. Busscher had requested relief in 2004 from the
demolition fee for the house on one of the lots and the Council did not approve
the request at the time. He said that the staff is not aware of any other
requests for a waiver of these fees, but added that this is a unique
circumstance.
Attorney Janega commented that the fee resolution does not include the right to
waive fees in any circumstance. She said that if the Council wants to adjust or
waive the fees, it must be incorporated into the fee resolution. She also
pointed out that this is a typical incentive used to encourage development.
Trustee Tucker recalled from past discussions that the fees are tied into the
wear and tear of the property individually.
Attorney Janega replied that it is important to realize that the demolition fee
is separate from the construction fee and is structured so that it relates to
the cost of the Village in overseeing the demolition. She said that the type of
building doesn’t matter and suggested that this be viewed from the standpoint
that the Village’s costs are being recovered.
Trustee Behles said that he views this as a development fee and the Council
must consider the impact of this for future developments. He added that the
cost to the Village is the same for this project as it would be if each house
were being demolished separately and is not sure the fees should be reduced
just because the demolition takes place simultaneously.
In response to a question from Trustee Berger, Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the
fee for demolition was determined by taking into account the Village utility
services, staff time, attorney time, inspections and other justifiable costs.
He said that the demolition permit fees have risen from $250 in 2001 to a
little over $10,000 currently and that the equipment needed for demolition
applies whether there is one house or 3 houses.
Trustee Tucker expressed her concern that if the formula is changed now, it may
be difficult using this as an incentive for affordable housing developments in
the future. She said that she is not in favor of waiving the fees.
President Woodbury then determined that it was the consensus of the Council
that the demolition permit fees not be waived or adjusted. He thanked Mr.
Busscher for the care and attention that was put into this project and added
that it was his wish that some of the single family developers would take as
much care and time with the neighbors concerns.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1447 and 1448. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1447 in the amount of $1,664,304.77 and Warrant List No. 1448 in the
amount of $684,196.67. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, , Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
b)   Village Yards Fuel System Improvements. Mr. Saunders reviewed this request
for fuel system island upgrades that would replace the current 1960’s
technology, manual recording of fuel records, and bring the system into
compliance with state and federal requirements.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid to Metro
Environmental in the amount of $72,900. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
Mr. Saunders then reported that the Public Works Department had received an
award from the greater metropolitan chapter of the American Public Works
Association on the renovation of the Public Works building and that this is the
second award that has been received. He said that the APWA has also asked that
the Department submit an application for the national award.
c)   Police Department Xerox Copier Replacement. Manager Williams reviewed this
request to authorize a lease contract with total monthly payments that exceed
the manager’s approval amount of $25,000 for a Xerox copier for the Police
Department to replace an out-of-date copier that is used in the central records
division.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the lease of a
Xerox copier for the Police Department in the terms stated on the tendered
contract. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Eilers.
d)   Electric Plant Window Replacement. As directed by the Council at its
January 17th meeting, Mr. Ciesla reported that staff had investigated the brand
and performance level of the windows and as a result, Quaker windows, which
match the windows on the east side of the plant, will be installed. He
recommends that a purchase order be issued to the lowest bidder, Auburn
Corporation, for replacement of the windows.
Trustee Behles added that the Council’s discussion had been about keeping the
integrity of the building. He said that Mr. Ciesla had forwarded the
specifications to him and he is satisfied that his comments have been
addressed.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to authorize the issuance of
a purchase order to Auburn Corporation in the amount of $44,375 for replacement
windows at the Electric Plant. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury reported on a meeting concerning
Willow Road improvements between the village presidents and school board
presidents of Northfield, Northbrook and Glenview, Northfield’s park district
director, the area’s state senators and representatives and the secretary of
transportation. He said that as a result of the meeting, IDOT clarified its
position and he feels that some solutions will be found within this generation
of talks.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers. The
meeting adjourned at 10:55 p.m.
