Deputy Clerk
February 14, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: March 7, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 14, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of
Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Engineer Susan Chen, and 1 person in the
audience.
2)   Discussion: Interim Banner Policy.
Manager Williams reviewed the banner program, which is administered by the
Chamber of Commerce. He explained that the Chamber, the Winnetka Woman’s Club,
and the Winnetka Alliance for Early Childhood are currently permitted to hang
banners on Village light poles in the business districts and along Green Bay
Road. He went on to say that the purpose of this discussion was to consider
letting other organizations hang banners on the light poles. He expressed his
concern that these other organizations would be wasting money on banners that
may become obsolete in a few years when the Village’s streetscape plan calls
for changing and adding new light poles.
The Council invited Joni Johnson, who was in the audience, to participate in
the discussion. The Council discussed the pros and cons of leaving the present
policy in place until the light poles are replaced. It was the consensus of the
Council to keep the current plan, which allows the Woman’s Club and the
Winnetka Alliance to use seven poles for a period of up to four weeks each per
year. The Chamber will have use of the poles when they are not otherwise
occupied, and other organizations will not necessarily be excluded from using
them if they want to expend the resources.
3)   Discussion: Revisions to Floodplain Regulations.
Mr. Saunders briefly recapped the Council’s previous discussions on the topic,
which centered around FEMA’s guidelines for constructing a basement below the
base flood elevation by means of a clay building pad. He reminded the Council
that their main concern had been with how the grade change was accomplished
from the pad to the existing grade. He added that staff has been working on a
way to minimize the impact of new construction in the flood plain on the
surrounding neighborhood.
Mr. Saunders discussed the history of the flood plain with the Trustees,
confirming that houses that were built before 1980 had basements because FEMA
had not yet declared those areas to be in the flood plain. Manager Williams
added that the Village has to follow the FEMA regulations or residents risk not
qualifying for the Federal flood insurance program. The Trustees discussed
different options available to mitigate the grade change problem that the clay
building pad poses. Mr. Saunders and the Council explored the implications of
limiting the height of retaining walls in the front and side yards.
After discussing the various options, the Council directed Mr. Saunders to
draft ordinance revisions limiting the height of retaining walls to two feet
and minimizing the amount of slope permitted to accomplish grade changes from
the building pad. The Council also directed Mr. Saunders to draft revisions
that would restrict the construction of basements to lots where the combination
of lot width and elevation would permit gentler transitions from the pad
elevation to the existing grade.
Mr. Saunders then brought up the amount of time the Engineering Department
spends reviewing construction plans for homes in the flood plain. He explained
that staff can spend up to ten hours performing engineering reviews on these
plans, compared to approximately four hours for a home outside the flood plain.
He suggested that a permit review fee be implemented to defray the Village’s
review process costs. The Trustees agreed, and directed Manager Williams to add
the item to the Fee Resolution for 2006.
4)   Discussion: Licensing Contractors.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed staff’s efforts to examine what other neighboring/
comparable municipalities were doing with regard to regulating contractors
through some type of licensing program.
Manager Williams informed the Council that Mr. D’Onofrio had recently hired a
code enforcement officer, who is already nipping a lot of problems in the bud.
He recommended that the Council set the issue aside for 6 months, and revisit
it again at that time to see if more enforcement tools are needed.
After discussing the range of options available to the Village in dealing with
errant contractors, the Council agreed to let the code enforcer develop a work
history with the contractors before moving ahead with licensing them.
5)   Executive Session 
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, and possible
purchase of land pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), 2(c)(2), and 2(c)(5)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:20 p.m.
6)   Adjournment.
The Council returned to the open meeting at 10:04 p.m. and the meeting was
adjourned without further business.
   ____________________________________
   Recording Secretary
February 21, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 7, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
February 21, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee and 14
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that the order of the Agenda had been changed, and
the Executive Session will be held following Item 7(c).
4. Quorum.
a)   February 22, 2006, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 23, 2006, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   March 7, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
d)   March 14, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 7, 2006, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury referred the
Trustees to amended language suggested by Trustee Berger. Trustee Behles,
seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the minutes as amended. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6.   Landmark Preservation Awards.
Robert Zielinski, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission, presented
awards to this year’s recipients. Mr. Zielinski remarked that each of the
projects reflects the owners’ careful efforts to move into the modern age with
up-to-date amenities while remaining sympathetic to the original style and
architectural character of the community. The homes winning awards for
rehabilitation or new construction were as follows:
      650 Blackthorn      Mr. & Mrs. John Palmer
      495 Ash         Mr. & Mrs. Michael Campbell
      19 Warwick         Jeff Moon & Pat Kenney
      903 Elm         Ruth & Michael Jennings
      822 Bryant         Jim & Carol Hansen
      500 Maple         Wally & Nan Greenough
      814 Boal Parkway      Mr. & Mrs. Peter Kelly
President Woodbury thanked all of the winners for their fine work and
contributions toward bettering the appearance of the Village and its housing
stock.
7. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-4-2006 – Zoning Variation: 194 Fuller Lane - Adoption.
There being no further comments or questions pertaining to this request for a
variation from the maximum building size requirements of the Zoning Ordinance
to permit the construction of a second floor dormer addition at 194 Fuller
Lane, President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance M-4-
2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-5-2006 – Zoning Variation: 1128 Mt. Pleasant – Adoption.
There being no further comments or questions pertaining to this request for a
variation from the maximum building size requirements of the Zoning Ordinance
to permit the construction of a 34.38-foot wide, front facing garage at a new
home at 1128 Mt. Pleasant, President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt the
ordinance.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance M-5-
2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-6-2006 – Zoning Variation: 545-L Hill Terrace – Adoption.
There being no further comments or questions pertaining to this request for a
variation from the front yard setback requirements of the Zoning Ordinance to
permit the installation of an air conditioner condenser at 545-L Hill Terrace,
President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance M-6-2006.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
***************
President Woodbury moved the Public Comment portion of the Agenda ahead of
Executive Session to accommodate two residents waiting in the audience.
8. Public Comment and Questions.
Frank Neaylon, 888 Mt. Pleasant and John Nichols, 900 Mt. Pleasant expressed
concern that when the Council discusses the possibility of implementing a
property maintenance code, the need for such a code for residential properties
will be subjugated to the needs and desires of the retail community. Mr.
Neaylon pointed out that residential property owners are not represented on the
Business Community Development Commission, the body that has been studying the
desirability of having a property maintenance code. Messrs. Neaylon and Nichols
voiced the opinion that the two sectors are very different and, therefore, the
issues are very different. They urged the Council to examine similar ordinances
from other communities and to separately consider a property maintenance code
for each type of property.
President Woodbury agreed that the two sectors are different and have different
issues. He noted that in the past strong opinions have been expressed on both
sides of the property maintenance code issue. He suggested that the Council
discuss all aspects at its upcoming Study Session, saying that he hopes to
build a consensus within both sectors.
***************
13. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11) of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 8:00 p.m.
Return to Regular Open Session. The Council reconvened into Regular Session at
9:05 p.m. Present: President Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker,
Ritchell, and Berger. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee,
Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works
Steven Saunders, and one person in the audience.
***************
7. After reconvening into Regular Session, President Woodbury returned to item
7(d) on the Agenda of the Regular Meeting.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2006 – Amending Special Events Permits & Fees –
Introduction/Adoption. Attorney Janega distributed a revised draft of the
proposed ordinance amending the special events provisions in Chapter 5.66 of
the Village Code. She then reviewed the proposed, revised amendments to the
provisions of the Village Code pertaining to special events to clarify the
intent of the Village to impose a content-neutral policy for being compensated
when an individual or organization hosts or sponsors an event that requires the
Village to provide special services. Ms. Janega explained that the pending law
suit has given the Village an opportunity to revisit the chapter, which was
added to the Code in late 2000, and to strengthen the provisions of the chapter
and clarify its purpose. The goal is to confirm that the Village’s purpose is
not to make money on private events or choose who may have such events, but to
provide a vehicle for the Village to recoup the expenses it incurs when it has
to provide special services for private events, such as weddings, private
parties, etc. She explained that the Village's expenses may include such costs
as those associated with traffic control, event security, special police
details, standby emergency service and special garbage pickups, and noted that
one would ordinarily be required to pay for such services if they were obtained
from a private source. Ms. Janega also noted that the proposed ordinance
modifies the definitions of "special event" and "official appearance." The
proposed amendment also adds a definition of "special services" and replaces
the "permit fee" concept with a “user fee,” to further clarify that the amount
billed to the event's host or sponsor is based on the actual cost of services
used. Finally, Ms. Janega explained that, in view of the pending litigation,
the Village Council could find that cause has been presented to waive
introduction and initial posting because the normal procedures do not serve the
Village's best interests in this instance. She added that Section 2.04.040 of
the Village Code requires that determination to be unanimous and to be made by
motion, and recommended that the Council do so and move directly to adoption.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to waive introduction of the
ordinance and proceed directly to adoption for the reasons explained by the
Village Attorney. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-
1-2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Manager Williams explained that as a part of the budgeting process, staff would
look at the fees and bring back an analysis for Council’s consideration.
***************
9. Old Business.
None.
10. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1449 and 1450. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1449 in the amount of $611,928.05 and Warrant List No. 1450 in the
amount of $373,050.04. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   GIS Service Provider Agreement. Mr. Saunders reviewed this request to
enter into a service agreement for GIS services with Municipal GIS Partners,
Inc., for a fee based on hourly rates noted in the Agenda Report, in an amount
not to exceed $48,171. Mr. Saunders explained that Winnetka is currently a
member of the Geographic Information Systems Consortium, a public entity that
was formed in 1999 for whom Municipal GIS Partners provides GIS developmental
services. Among other things, these services include: aerial photography,
purchasing and installing the necessary hardware and software, obtaining
available existing data from Cook County, obtaining aerial photography, making
the GIS available on the Internet, and populating various maps and databases.
Mr. Saunders indicated that the new service fees represent slightly over a 3%
increase. He said that after one more year, the services required for updating
the GIS system will amount to maintenance and upgrading of the system.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to authorize the agreement
with Municipal GIS Partners as presented by Mr. Saunders. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   2007 Vehicle Sticker Design. Mr. McKee reviewed a request from the
Winnetka Public School Nursery to designate its logo on the 2007 Village
vehicle sticker. Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve
the request. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
11. Reports.
c)   Village President. President Woodbury commended the North Shore Senior
Center on the occasion of their 50th Anniversary for their outstanding service
to area seniors.
12. Appointments.
None.
13. Adjournment of Regular Meeting into Budget Study Session.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the Regular
Meeting into Budget Study Session. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 9:30 p.m.
