March 7, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 4, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
7, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Acting
Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, and one person in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 6(a), 804 Lincoln Ave., has been removed
from the Agenda at the request of the applicant.
4. Quorum.
a)   March 14, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   March 21, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 14, 2006, Study Session. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   February 15, 2006, Budget Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   February 21, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Eilers, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
d)   February 21, 2006, Budget Meeting. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
e)   February 22, 2006 Budget Meeting. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-7-2006 – Zoning Variation: 804 Lincoln Ave. -
Introduction. As noted above, this item was removed from the Agenda at the
request of the applicant.
b)   Resolution No. $-3-2006 – 2006-07 Budget – Public Hearing & Introduction.
President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing on the 2006-2007 Village Budget.
Mr. McKee reported that revenues and transfers for 2007 are up 5.9% from the
current budget, cash expenses are up 5.1%, and $9.82M has been budgeted for
capital expenditures. He also summarized how the budget changes would impact a
typical homeowner, saying that staff estimates a typical homeowner’s cost to be
$4,585 for municipal services, an increase of $86, or 1.9% over the current
year.
Trustee Tucker expressed concern about the line item for the additional
firefighter. She suggested that the funds be kept in the budget but that any
action to hire this individual be postponed until after the Council’s in-depth
study of the department in the summer.
Trustee Eilers concurred citing the significant pension ramifications, as did
Trustees Berger and Ritchell.
Trustee Behles agreed, but said that it is important to keep the allocation in
the budget because the Village doesn’t want to be scrambling for funding if it
decides to proceed with hiring an additional firefighter. There was a consensus
among the Trustees to do so.
President Woodbury closed the Public Hearing.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Resolution R-3-
2006 adopting the budget for FY 2006-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   Updated Fee and Rate Resolutions. Mr. McKee reviewed the Village’s annual
fee ordinances that would implement the changes discussed during the budget
meetings. He indicated that these reflect no change in water, electric, or
refuse fees, and a 5% increase in the sewer rate that will cost the typical
customer about $9 per year more.
i)   Resolution No. R-4-2006 – Water Rates - Introduction. Trustee Eilers,
seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Resolution R-4-2006 adopting
water rates for FY 2006-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
ii)   Resolution No. R-5-2006 – Electric Rates – Introduction. Manager Williams
pointed out that although the methodology for calculating the cost of power
adjustment is changing to reflect the payoff of the Trimble County One Electric
Plant, there is no rate change to customers.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Resolution R-5-
2006 adopting the electric rates for FY 2006-2007. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
iii)   Resolution No. R-6-2006 – Sewer Rates – Introduction. Trustee Ritchell,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Resolution R-3-2006 adopting a
5% increase in the sewer rates for FY 2006-2007. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
iv)   Resolution No. R-7-2006 – Refuse Rates – Introduction. Trustee Behles,
seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Resolution R-3-2006 adopting
the refuse rates for FY 2006-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1451 and 1452. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1451 in the amount of $1,302,407.48 and Warrant List No. 1452 in the
amount of $513,539.64. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 1096 Ash – Maximum Building Size Variation. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Maximum Building Size variation to permit
the construction of a single-family, two-story home with a basement in the
floodplain. He explained that after being denied the variation by the Zoning
Board, the plans are being revised to look for alternatives that would avoid
having the basement included in the GFA calculations. Therefore, he recommended
that the matter be referred back to the Zoning Board.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to refer this variation
request back to the Zoning Board of Appeals. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   Health Services Agreement. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that for several years
the Village had its own in-house Sanitarian, but that individual’s retirement
has forced the Village to seek Sanitarian services elsewhere. He recommended
that the Village contract with the Village of Wilmette for part time services
from their Sanitarian for a fee of $13,916 per year, prorated to the effective
date of the Agreement.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the Health
Services Agreement with the Village of Wilmette as presented. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker,
Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Yard Waste Hauling and Removal Contract. Mr. Saunders explained that in
2005, the Council authorized renewal of a contract with Thelen Sand and Gravel
to provide hauling services for removal of yard waste and leaves from the
Village. At that time, the Council directed staff that the contract should be
competitively bid at the end of the extension term. Following this direction,
bids were solicited from six firms, but only Thelen Sand and Gravel, the
current provider, submitted a response. He reported that the bid received
represents a 10.3% increase over the current contract. The contract is
structured as an annual agreement subject to renewal each year. He recommended
its approval.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the contract for
Landscape Waste Transfer Services to Thelen Sand and Gravel, at the unit rates
set forth in the Agenda Report. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and
Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Streetscape Master Plan Contract. Mr. Saunders reviewed the contract
negotiated and developed with The Lakota Group at Council’s direction for a
Streetscape Master Plan. He explained that this Plan would be a master plan
that takes guidance from the commercial design guidelines and applies them to
the four business districts, but the Plan will not result in construction
documents. He recommended that the Council award the contract to Lakota for an
amount not to exceed $114,579.
President Woodbury mentioned that the Village would also receive estimates of
feasibility and proposed work costs.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the contract for a
Streetscape Master Plan to The Lakota Group as recommended. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles,
Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid 06-002: Transformers. Mr. Ciesla reviewed the bids for single-phase
and three-phase transformers for FY 2006-2007, the number of which varies
according to usage. He explained that the prices were obtained on all
transformers that the Village thinks will be needed during the coming year; the
specific amounts are determined at a later time, but the bid evaluation
quantities are the quantities that the Village actually used in the last twelve
months. He noted that rapidly changing and increasing prices for the raw
materials in transformers, oil, copper, and steel, have made the manufacturers
index the prices to changes in the market price of those items. He recommended
that the bid be awarded to Wesco in an amount not to exceed $57,000.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bid for
transformers as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Number of transformers varies according to usage
g)   Bid 06-003: Blanket Cable Purchase 2006-2007. Mr. Ciesla reviewed the bids
for primary and secondary cable for FY 2006-2007. He noted that the bid price
for the primary cable is substantially more expensive than the budget estimate,
given increases in the raw cost of the metal. He recommended that the bid be
awarded to Okanite, the low bidder for both primary and secondary cable, in an
amount not to exceed $450,000.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the bid for primary
and secondary cable to Okanite as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell,
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Bid 06-008: Electric Pond Dredging. Mr. Ciesla reviewed bids for dredging
sand that has accumulated in the intake and cooling ponds of the Electric
Plant. He recommended that the word be awarded to O’Bryan Consulting, Inc., in
the amount of $57,980.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid for pond
dredging at the Electric Plant as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury announced that the Village had
received notification that The Lloyd House has been placed on the National
Register of Historic Places.
b)   Trustees.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Business Community Development Commission has
recommended reconsideration of the Overlay District with an eye toward how best
to serve the community. He also pointed out that there are a number of major
projects on the horizon that impact the business districts, i.e., streetscape,
parking, and the Post Office redevelopment, all of which will require
significant funding; therefore, the Village will have to carefully consider and
prioritize its spending.
Manager Williams mentioned that consideration of the Overlay District has been
docketed for the April Study Session.
Trustee Abell reported that the Illinois Municipal League has advised members
that a bill authorizing significant pension benefit enhancements for
firefighters and police officers is being considered by the State Legislature.
Based on the information received, he as asked that the matter be placed on the
agenda of the Northwest Municipal Conference.
c)   Village Manager.
Following up on Trustee Abell’s comments, Manager Williams noted that the
proposed bill, which would permit qualifying public safety employees to double
dip at the end of their careers, has far-reaching consequences for the Village.
He pointed out that since 1999 public safety employees have received three
significant pension benefit enhancements adding millions of dollars of unfunded
liabilities to municipal pension plans, which the State Legislature does not
have to pay for, but the local taxpayers do. He recommended that the Council
authorize the Village President to write a letter to our representatives in
opposition to the proposed bill.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of George Pappas to replace Chris
Rintz on the Building Review Committee. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to appoint Mr. Pappas. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, board
appointments, and review of Executive Session Minutes pursuant to Sections 2(c)
(11), 2(c)(1), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(21) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into
Executive Session at 9:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:35 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Abell, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:36 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
March 14, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: April 4, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
14, 2006 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, and Herb Ritchell. Absent:
Jessica Tucker. Also present: Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Chief of
Police Joseph DeLopez, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and approximately 30
persons in the audience.
President Woodbury announced that agenda item #4, Sanitary Sewer Backflow
Prevention had been removed from the agenda and that the cell phone ordinance
discussion would be moved to the first item of business.
2)   Caucus Request for Cell Phone Ordinance.
In response to the results of the Caucus survey, Chief DeLopez presented a
proposal to generate discussion about amendments to the current provisions of
the Village Code which would prohibit the operation of a vehicle while
distracted by the use of cell phones, entertainment systems, pets, personal
grooming, or eating and drinking. Chief DeLopez based his proposal on the
Illinois Secretary of State’s definition of distracted driving and the City of
Chicago’s ordinance and expressed his opinion that, although it is important to
address the Caucus’ request, legislation should also be made at the state level
in order to facilitate enforcement.
The Trustees raised questions about the difficulty of enforcing such an
ordinance, civil liberties issues, using existing laws for reckless driving,
traffic accident statistics, and similar legislation in other towns and states.
Trustee Eilers expressed serious civil rights and civil liberty concerns in
granting any law enforcement agency the authority to stop citizens in their
cars because they were drinking coffee, adjusting the radio, or glancing back
at a child in a car seat.
Chief DeLopez answered that statewide legislation would be most effective for
enforcement, but that locally, a more specific ordinance defining distracted
driving would be helpful. He explained that enforcement could be secondary to
other offenses or primary, so that an officer could stop someone who was
talking on a cell phone, but driving within the traffic laws, much like the
seat belt law. His proposal would amend the current ordinance on reckless or
negligent driving to include behavior that distracts a driver from the safe
operation of a vehicle and would be the only other ordinance, besides
Chicago’s, in the state. He added that traffic accident statistics are not of
use because most drivers do not admit that the use of a cell phone may have
contributed to a crash, unless there is a witness. In response to the question
raised about civil liberties, Chief DeLopez said that the intent of the
amendments would be to address inattentive drivers who are doing things that
are dangerous to the community, although the Village could face some court
challenges. He said that he is not advocating that an ordinance be enacted, but
has proposed amendments to our current ordinance in response to the Caucus
survey results, that would address hands-free use of cell phones as well as
other types of distracted driving.
Members of the audience who spoke in favor of enacting an ordinance were Elissa
Mizel, 939 Tower; Louise Holland, 545 Oak, who also spoke for Pete Cruikshank
and Irwin Askow who could not attend the meeting; Ron Allen, 265 White Oak
Lane; and, Joni Johnson, 808 Tower Road. They cited the record number of
responses to the Caucus survey in favor of greater regulations of cell phone
use while driving, encouraged education to respect traffic signs and observe
traffic safety regulations, applauded the Council for initiating the discussion
and suggested an ordinance for hands-free cell phone use, if not for all
distracted driving.
Jeanne Beckman, 840 Foxdale, said that the distracted driver issue is
important, but spoke against further regulations. She suggested better
enforcement of the current negligent driving laws.
After further discussion, President Woodbury determined that the consensus of
the Council was to consider an ordinance requiring hands-free cell phone use.
He requested that Chief DeLopez provide the Council with more information on
how the Police Department would deal with primary vs. secondary enforcement. He
also encouraged the Trustees to write to the neighboring communities
encouraging them to consider a similar ordinance, stating that by joining
together, it may be more effective in getting the state legislature to act. He
added that a public hearing to discuss hands-free cell phone use would be held
in September.
3)   Introduction and Conversation with New Caucus Officers.
Joni Johnson introduced the new Caucus officers to the Village Council and
reported that the Spring meeting would be on May 31st. Ms. Johnson said topics
the Caucus will address are affordable housing, the post office site
development, extending the length of the terms of the trustees from 2 years to
4 years, design guidelines, historic preservation and demolitions, and parking
in the Village.
The Council responded to questions about the diversity of skills, occupational
backgrounds and disciplines of trustees; advocated that a trustee’s length of
service be limited because of the talent and innovative ideas frequent turnover
brings to the Council and the commitment of time to prepare for and attend
meetings; listed the current projects being considered, such as affordable
housing, a parking deck, and a streetscape plan which will have a positive
impact on the business districts; and, talked about the fiscal responsibility
to the community so that the Village has the means to maintain infrastructure,
pension funding, etc. without going into debt. The Trustees also commented that
the trustees’ terms don’t limit accomplishments, as that is driven by the
community.
Manager Williams added that the terms of office can be institutionalized at two
years now that Winnetka is a home rule community, so that it need not only be a
Caucus tradition. He also said that the Council has responded to the Caucus
survey results over the past few years by making changes in the zoning
ordinance and a zoning analysis report is to be presented at tonight’s meeting
on the effect of those changes.
President Woodbury provided an update on current projects to the Caucus members
and said that the Council will hold discussions in June and July to set
direction on the parking deck issue. He added that the overwhelming response on
the Caucus survey provides the Council with the framework to hold these
discussions and encouraged Caucus members to attend Council meetings whenever
possible.
4)   Chronology of Zoning Changes, 1989-2005.
Brian Norkus reported on the effect of zoning changes during the last 15 years
using photographs to emphasize the detailed information contained in his
report. The main focus of the report was residential construction since the
advent of gross floor area regulations. Mr. Norkus concluded by saying that he
and Mr. D’Onofrio believe that the zoning regulations have been amended to the
extent possible to reduce bulk, but that zoning amendments can not make a house
look better. Mr. Norkus suggested that alternatives would be design guidelines
and a pattern book for builders and residents.
Comments from the audience were to move toward design guidelines or inform the
community that nothing more can be done with zoning regulations; the loss of
architectural styles could be significant to future generations who would be
very grateful for preservation; and to improve communication to let the
community know what corrective actions can be taken during home construction
projects, such as stop work orders, code enforcement, and citations.
President Woodbury reported that he had asked that the pattern book be
completed in the next 12 months and a consultant hired to assist the Design
Guidelines Committee. He added that this process will help to educate the
community and will foster conversations to define what the community thinks is
best. He said that it may also be possible to provide an incentive in the
zoning regulations to allow the construction of a larger house if the design
follows recommended guidelines.
The Trustees commented on the community’s concern about lot coverage,
amendments to floodplain regulations which could make a difference in the
appearance of houses, and the pattern book which has tremendous appeal in that
it will show people what fits into the neighborhoods.
5)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
6)   Adjournment.
trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Tucker. The meeting
adjourned at 11:30 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Deputy Clerk
March 21, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 18, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
21, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Public Works
Steve Saunders, Acting Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, Fire Chief
Scott Smith, and approximately 9 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   April 4, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   April 11, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Eilers, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
None.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution No. R-3-2006 – 2006-07 Budget - Adoption. Finance Director
McKee reviewed the budget process and stressed that the Village’s goal of
operating under the tax caps has been met, adding that the only rate increase
of significance is the 5% sewer rate increase, which would add $9 per year for
a typical resident.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-3-
2006 adopting the Budget for the Fiscal Year 2006-2007. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Updated Fee and Rate Resolutions. Mr. McKee noted that the next six
resolutions would set the rates for water, sewer, electric, and refuse
collection, as well as the fees for general permits, licenses, building
permits, and zoning activities in accordance with the Council’s discussions
during their review of the preliminary budget.
   i)   Resolution No. R-4-2006 – Water Rates - Adoption. Trustee Eilers,
seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-4-2006, which would not
increase the water rates for residents of the Village but would increase the
rates for unincorporated areas from 133% to 143% of the rate paid by residents.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-5-2006 – Electric Rates – Adoption. Trustee Abell,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-5-2006, which does not
increase the basic electric rates, but approves a change in the power cost
adjustment formula and updates the charges for undergrounding to reflect higher
material costs. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-6-2006 – Sewer Rates – Adoption. Trustee Behles
moved to adopt Resolution R-6-2006, which would increase sewer rates by 5%.
Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   iv)   Resolution No. R-7-2006 – Refuse Rates – Adoption. Trustee Berger,
seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-7-2006, which
maintains the current refuse rates, without an increase. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   v)   Resolution No. R-8-2006 – General Permit, License & Registration Fees -
Adoption. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution
R-8-2006 adopting the general permit, license and registration fees, which adds
a $10 per day vehicle storage fee and increases the cost for impounding a dog
from $20 to $55. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   vi)   Resolution No. R-9-2006 – Building, Zoning & Construction Fees –
Adoption. President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt Resolution R-9-2006,
which would increase most of the building, zoning and construction fees by
approximately 5%, with more significant increases for development agreements,
permit renewals, temporary certificates of occupancy, street replacements and
returned payments, all to better reflect the Village's increased costs. Trustee
Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution R-8-2006. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-10-2006 – Charges for Fire Service to Unincorporated
Areas – Adoption. Fire Chief Smith explained that the Village provides fire
suppression services outside its corporate limits to Kenilworth, which has no
fire department, and to its water service customers located in unincorporated
New Trier Township. He said that the services to Kenilworth are administered
through an intergovernmental agreement that remains in effect through the end
of the 2006-07 fiscal year. Chief Smith noted that staff has been working to
improve the administration of the annual non-resident fire suppression service
agreements and that staff recommends replacing the lump sum annual agreements
with annual charges that would be pro-rated over the course of the year and
billed on the customer’s utility bills.
Village Attorney Janega explained that the proposed resolution would be the
first step in changing the rate formulation, and that it would be necessary to
amend the Village Code to fully implement the staff's recommendation.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees reached a general agreement that annual
fees should be established, and directed Attorney Janega to bring the necessary
Code changes before the Council later this year.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-10-2006
setting the fire service fees for unincorporated areas. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
Sherry Gormanous and John Nye of the Winnetka Interfaith Council, spoke with
regard to the group’s desire to erect a peace pole near the cenotaph on the
Village Green. Ms. Gormanous explained that a peace pole is a remembrance for
peace in the world, and that this particular pole would be 8 feet tall, with
each side containing the message “Let Peace Prevail” in a different language.
Mr. Nye then asked the Council to consider a Memorial Day installation of the
peace pole on the Village Green.
Manager Williams commented that the Village Green has a covenant in the deed
that no “structure” can be placed there, and added that the Village Green is
incredibly special to residents and that substantial public hearings would have
to be held to even consider the idea. He suggested that a better plan would be
to put the peace pole on private property.
The Trustees voiced their appreciation for the message of peace, but felt that
public property is probably not the place to erect the proposed pole. President
Woodbury offered the Village’s cooperation with permits and zoning should a
private location be chosen.
Mr. Nye then remarked that the upcoming Memorial Day might be an excellent time
for a rededication ceremony of the Veterans Memorial on the Village Green.
Manager Williams pointed out that he had inspected the monument on the Village
Green and it needs rehabilitation work. He added that the 2006-07 budget
contains funds for renovations or rebuilding, that the work will most likely be
done in 2007, and that a rededication would be perfectly appropriate at that
time.
Old Business.
None.
8. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1453 and 1454. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1453 in the amount of $434,242.85 and Warrant List No. 1454 in the
amount of $838,473.31. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Status Report on Use of 411 Linden – Winnetka Historical Society. Attorney
Janega reviewed the history of the special use permit that was granted to the
Winnetka Historical Society in November of 2001 for their facility at 411
Linden. One of the conditions in the ordinance granting the special use was
that the Historical Society come before the Council a year later so the Council
could assess the effectiveness of the mitigating conditions contained in the
ordinance. Ms. Janega then directed the Council’s attention to the table in the
agenda report, which lists the status of each of the conditions imposed by the
special use ordinance. She explained that the one-year review had been
continued for successive years because work on the property hadn’t been
completed, but that all work under the building permits had since been
completed, the new facility is operational and the Historical Society remains
in compliance with the conditions.
Louise Holland, president of the Winnetka Historical Society, informed the
Trustees that last May the Society moved their collection to 411 linden. She
explained that the society has adhered to every stipulation in the special use
permit, and reported that there have been no problems with the neighbors.
Trustees Abell and Tucker thanked the society for having facilitated the
defusing of a tricky situation with the neighbors. Trustee Abell then added
that he thought the Council should waive further reports. The Council agreed
that there is no further need to keep reviewing the special use at 411 Linden.
c)   Policy Direction: Forestry Commission Recommended Changes to Village Code.
Public Works Director Steven Saunders reviewed his agenda report, which
contains the Forestry Commission’s recommended changes to the tree preservation
ordinance. The Commission's recommendations include adding the designation of
“Heritage Tree” for trees with over 24 inches DBH; modifying the definition of
economic hardship; and requiring tree preservation deposits in order to provide
protection for the owners of trees in properties adjacent to the construction
site.
Mr. Saunders then walked the Council through the suggested code changes item by
item. Regarding the tree deposit recommendation, he said that staff is troubled
by the additional liability the Village would take on as a result of
administering the program. He recommended that the Council consider relying on
the neighbor notice provisions to get people talking and resolving problems,
and also expressed support for higher fines as a useful tool. He added that the
definition of “hardship” needs more discussion, and noted that there is an
appeal process provided for in the current Code, under which residents have the
opportunity to have their case heard by the Council.
In response to a question about the need for such intense regulation, Village
Forester Jim Stier replied that he thought more regulations would help, but
added that he couldn’t guarantee that every outcome would be a good one.
Mr. Saunders added that the most compelling problems occur when someone is
intent on removing a significant tree, or when construction affects a tree on a
neighbor’s property. He explained that when the ordinance was originally
adopted, the issue of property rights versus the protection of the tree was
intentionally left to be handled on a case-by-case basis, and the issue is how
the Village balances protecting the trees and letting people do what they want
with their property.
The Trustees debated the issue of weighing in with their comments before the
public has had a chance to speak about the issue, with some not feeling
comfortable with voicing any opinion until after a public hearing, and others
wanting to refine the ordinance before bringing it to the public.
In response to a question about pinning down the definition of hardship,
Attorney Janega replied that when trying to define the hardship standard, a
cause for concern is that the proposed wording is patterned after the Zoning
Ordinance's variation standards, which considers whether there is a practical
difficulty and whether the property can produce a reasonable return. She
pointed out that the Commission's proposed definition of hardship makes
reasonable use of the property the controlling factor and it doesn’t delve into
the circumstances that require the removal of the tree. She advised the Council
to weigh the significance of the Commission's proposal in the context of
overall land use regulation in the Village, noting that the proposed amendments
would make the Tree Preservation standards more stringent than the Zoning
Ordinance, and that the proposed language would take the tree regulations
closer to regulatory taking concepts than the Zoning Ordinance.
President Woodbury agreed, saying that in the context of zoning, the concept of
reasonable return is one of the most elusive of standards.
There was more discussion concerning the issues of trees versus new
construction, the feasibility of tree deposits, the appeals process and the
definitions of hardship and reasonable use.
Mary Hill, Chairman of the Forestry Commission, and Susie Shabica and Bernie
Hammer, two of the Commission's members, spoke in support of the Commission's
proposed amendments. They profiled their extensive efforts at developing the
Commission’s proposed amendments, which included researching other tree
preservation ordinances, consultation with other villages and many talks with
concerned residents and Village staff. The Commission members expressed concern
about the number of trees that are over 100 years old that the Village is
losing to construction.
President Woodbury thanked the Commission for its hard work and advocacy for
the trees and stressed that the issue is very important to the community. He
added that the Council takes very seriously the liability issues involved with
the proposed ordinance and pointed out that staff time and budget concerns also
factor into their decisions.
The Council then discussed the pros and cons of the heritage tree designation,
with the consensus being that it would add more steps for builders to go
through in removing large trees, and that getting builders talking to the
neighbors would be a good step. The conversation then turned back to the need
for a public hearing in order to gather community input on the issues.
Manager Williams suggested that he and Attorney Janega meet with the Forestry
Commission to discuss the amendments, carve out the policies that are feasible,
and to package the information so the Council could hold the public hearing.
d)   Bid 06-007: Handholes. Acting Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla
explained what handholes are in the electrical system. He stated that bids were
mailed out to four suppliers and that Utility Pipe and Supply was the lowest
bidder. He recommended that the Council approve the bid in the amount of
$47,000. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to authorize the
Village Manager to award a purchase order for the purchase of handholes for the
period of April 1, 2006, through March 31, 2007, to Utility Pipe and Supply in
an amount not to exceed $47,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Turbine #4 Overhaul. Mr. Ciesla explained that the overhaul is almost
completed, and noted that the total cost of the project has gone over the
initial projection, since once the turbine was dismantled, other problems were
detected that couldn’t have been anticipated. After summarizing the accounting
for the turbine overhaul costs, Mr. Ciesla asked the Council to approve the
total cost of the project.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the final project
cost of $354,899.04 for the rebuild of Turbine #4. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Parkway Tree Planting Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for the parkway
tree planting program and explained that the bids are awarded by tree species.
After eliminating one bidder, KGI, because of unfavorable references, the next
lowest bidder, St. Aubin Nursery and Landscaping, qualified to win 65 of the 69
species offered. Mr. Saunders explained that, while the Village saves money on
the tree costs by splitting up the bids, there comes a point at which the
savings is too small to outweigh the complications of managing multiple
contracts. He therefore recommended awarding the entire contract to St. Aubin
Nursery and Landscaping.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the contract for
the parkway tree planting for FY 2006-2007 to St. Aubin Nursery & Landscaping
for an amount not to exceed $46,430. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None
g)   Parkway Tree Trimming, Removal and Maintenance. Mr. Saunders reviewed the
bids for the parkway tree maintenance contracts, explaining that one
contractor, Autumn Tree Care, asked to withdraw their bid and that staff
honored their request. He recommended that the contracts be awarded at the unit
and hourly rates contained in the bid proposals received March 1, 2006.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the contracts at the
unit and hourly rates contained in the bid proposals to the following vendors:
   •   Parkway Tree & Stump Removal to Nels Johnson Tree Experts, Inc., in an
amount not to exceed $62, 900.00
   •   Parkway Tree Trimming to Arbor Green, Inc., in an amount not to exceed
$17,474.00.
   •   Emergency T&M Maintenance to Arbor Green, Inc., at the rate of $195 per
crew hour.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None
h)   Bid Number 06-001- Landscape Maintenance Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed bids
for landscape maintenance services to maintain various landscaped and parkway
areas in the Village. He recommended that the bid be awarded to the lowest
bidder, Ray Pollina Landscaping, Inc.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the contract to
Ray Pollina Landscaping, Inc. to perform landscape maintenance for a two-year
period beginning April 1, 2006, with an option to extend the contract for a
third year, as set forth in the bidding documents for bid 06-001. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
i)   Annual Outdoor Seating Permits and Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License
Riders. Manager Williams reviewed the agenda report on the outdoor seating
permits and sidewalk restaurant liquor license riders. As there were no changes
from last year, he recommended that the permits be approved.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the Outdoor
Seating Permits for the 10 applicants for 2006. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
approve the Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License Riders for Corner Cooks, Little
Ricky’s and Song O’Sixpence. By voice vote the motion carried unanimously.
9. Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury made a brief reference to a memo he
had distributed to the trustees.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the Winnetka Report is coming out
around the first week of April, Trustee Ritchell reported that the Historical
Society benefit is coming up, and Trustee Abell noted that former Village
President Mike Duhl has been named the Chamber of Commerce’s Man of the Year.
c)   Village Attorney. None
d)   Village Manager. Manager Williams informed the Council that the City of
Springfield sent out a call for help after the recent tornado devastated much
of the area and that he authorized an electric crew to be sent down there. He
also shared that the Illinois Historic Preservation Agency has put 151 Sheridan
Road on the National Historic Register, and that the Park District officially
withdrew their application for a special use permit for the Hubbard Woods Park.
10. Appointments.
None.
11. Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, possible sale of
Village real estate, and board appointments pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)
(1), 2(c)(6), and 2(c)(3) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 10:58 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:23 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
12. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:24 p.m.
