Recording Secretary
April 4, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 18, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
4, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Acting Director of Water & Electric
Rich Ciesla, and six persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 9(b), Policy Direction for 480 Oak St.,
has been withdrawn from the Agenda at the request of the applicant.
4. Quorum.
a)   April 11 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   April 18, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 7, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Abell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   March 14, 2006, Study Session. Trustee Eilers suggested two changes.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as
amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-7-2006 – Zoning Variation: 804 Lincoln Ave. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot
variations to permit the construction of additions to the first and second
floor of the existing home and to replace the existing two-car detached garage
with a new two-car detached garage with additional storage area. He explained
that when bonuses and reductions are factored into the calculations, both the
roofed lot coverage and the amount of impermeable surface. Mr. D’Onofrio noted
that the current building is nonconforming with respect to intensity of use of
lot and that one of the contributing factors to the roofed lot coverage are
eaves greater than 18 inches. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 4-0 to recommend approval of the variations.
Mr. D’Onofrio distributed copies of a letter from the adjacent landowners, Mr.
and Mrs. Adams, who had previously objected to the proposed plans. Mr. and Mrs.
Adams’ letter indicates that the applicants have agreed to certain conditions
regarding grading and drainage and in consideration thereof, the Adams would
feel obliged to agree to the proposed plans. These conditions include (1)
installing at the applicants’ expense a drainage swale on the applicants’ side
of the property line between the two houses, (2) pointing all of their gutter
downspouts to the West, and (3) allowing the Adams the right to review and
consult on the final drainage plans.
Mr. D’Onofrio responded to questions from the Trustees about previous zoning
variations on the property, GFA calculations, and grading and drainage
requirements.
Attorney Janega pointed out that one of the conditions for obtaining building
permit approval is that all of the increased drainage be taken care of on the
property so as to not impact neighboring properties. Adequate provision must be
made to connect to storm sewers and such water control mechanisms as needed as
determined by the Village Engineer. She indicated that the first two conditions
could be incorporated into the ordinance, but recommended against giving the
Adams’ the right to approve the drawings because while the two parties could
consult about the proposals, approval of the drawings is not an objective
standard.
Mark ver Bryck, the applicants’ architect, pointed out that the applicants are
not requesting a GFA variation, but rather only one for roofed lot coverage and
impervious surfaces. He explained that although the large eaves provide
character to the home, they present a hardship from a design standpoint because
they must be extended to the design of any addition in order to keep it
sympathetic and harmonious with the existing house. He said that except for Mr.
and Mrs. Adams all of the neighbors support the proposed plans and indicated
that the applicants will do whatever is required by the Village to be in
compliance with its engineering requirements.
Trustee Behles complimented the applicants on the design but said that he had
to take exception to the finding of the Zoning Board that the property can’t
yield a reasonable return. He said that he does not believe that this is true
or that the applicants’ plight is due to unique circumstances. He added that
there is no guarantee that a property can maximize GFA in every circumstance as
there are other tests. He said that in this instance, he sees excessive size in
the garage and back porch and believes that there is a more conforming
alternative. Therefore, he is opposed to the request.
Trustee Berger concurred with Trustee Behles.
Trustee Eilers agreed that this is a close call, but said that he would support
the request because the petitioner and the architect have been creative in
trying to preserve existing housing stock.
Trustee Abell said that he, too, would support the request because of the
effort made to preserve the existing structure, although this will certainly
max out the property.
Trustee Tucker said that she would like to see a reduced place, but is
persuaded to support the request.
Trustee Ritchell said he was undecided and expressed concern about GFA
calculations and how small the back yard will be.
President Woodbury indicated that he would be in favor of this request because
of the Village’s long-standing policy of supporting renovation. He said that
given today’s technology and staff’s more sophisticated reviews he believes
that the flow of water from property to property can be controlled.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Tucker, and
Abell. Nays: Trustees Behles and Berger. Abstain: Trustee Ritchell. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-8-2006 – Special Use Permit: 546 Lincoln Ave. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Special Use Permit to
allow a health club to be located in the rear of the subject building, which is
within the C-2 Retail Overlay zoning district. He explained that the plans
envision dividing the available space in the building into four smaller spaces,
including the subject use. Mr. D’Onofrio also noted that although most special
use requests require a traffic and parking study, and although no on-site
parking is available, Village Engineer Steve Saunders has determined that such
a study is not necessary because he believes that employee and client parking
needs can be accommodated in the Lincoln Avenue lot. Mr. D’Onofrio reported
that both the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Plan Commission voted in favor of
issuing the Special Use Permit.
The applicant, Steven Mills, reviewed his plans and his business model.
Trustee Tucker asked whether dividing the available space up into so many
spaces makes sense.
Trustee Eilers commented that 100-ft deep spaces are very difficult to lease,
therefore, subdividing the space will probably enhance the ability to lease the
front space. He voiced his support for the request.
Trustee Behles concurred, saying that the proposed use in no way limits the
viability of the retail rentals in the front of the building. He added that it
also serves to activate the pedestrian way between Elm Street and the Village
parking lot.
Attorney Janega remarked that there is precedent for granting this request
because as in other, similar situations, there is no street frontage.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2006 – Amending Hours for Class C Liquor Licenses -
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment to the regulations
for Class C liquor licenses that would permit the sale of alcoholic beverages
until 2:00 a.m. at special events held in certain types of facilities. She
explained that the amendments have been narrowly tailored to allow the expanded
service only for certain types of events and noted that they preserve the 10:00
p.m. limitation for the sale and consumption of alcoholic beverages outdoors,
or in booths, concession stands, and certain tents. She emphasized that
permission to sell until 2:00 a.m. would be granted on an event-by-event basis,
and added that any necessary corrective action could be taken immediately when
it occurs, and that license hours could then be reduced accordingly for future
applications.
Manager Williams noted that there may be situations in which it doesn’t make
sense to grant extended hours, such as for an organization in a residential
neighborhood.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution No. R-11-2006 – Motor Fuel Tax Funding for Phase III
Engineering and Construction for the Pine and Eldorado Street Bridges –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village is in the process of
replacing the superstructures on the Eldorado and Pine Street Bridges. Both
projects are eligible for 80% Federal funding reimbursement. The remaining 20%
of the costs, which is the Village’s portion of the costs, will be funded
through Motor Fuel Tax dollars and these expenditures must be authorized by
resolution. Resolution R-11-2006 would authorize the expenditure of $412,382 of
the Village’s MFT funds for its share.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-11-
2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-12-2006 – Motor Fuel Tax Funding for Supplement #1 for
Preliminary Engineering on Pine and Eldorado Street Bridges – Adoption. Mr.
Saunders explained that this resolution would authorize the expenditure of
$2,820 of the Village’s MFT funds to cover the Village’s share of additional
costs over and above the originally approved amount incurred in connection with
the engineering for the two new bridges.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-12-
2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1455 and 1456. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1455 in the amount of $482,091.64 and Warrant List No. 1456 in the
amount of $457,340.65. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 480 Oak St. As noted earlier, this item was withdrawn
from the Agenda and consideration by the applicant.
c)   Directional Boring and Cable Installation Contract. Mr. Ciesla reviewed a
request to extend the existing directional boring and cable installation
contract with GroundHog Utility Construction for the period from June 1, 2006,
to May 31, 2007, at the 2005-06 prices. The work would be performed on an "as
needed" basis and would not exceed the approved budget amount of $805,000. Mr.
Ciesla noted that given recent increases in costs for petroleum-based products,
it is highly unlikely that bidding this work would result in a reduction in
cost to the Village.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the recommended
third year extension of the contract with GroundHog Utility Construction at the
2005/06 prices. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell Nays: None.
Absent: None.
d)   Replacement Backhoe Purchase. Mr. Ciesla reviewed a request to purchase a
replacement for the Water & Electric Department’s backhoe from Westside Tractor
and Sales at GSA contract pricing. He explained that the existing backhoe is 13
years old, unusable and unsafe and would require significant expense to repair.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to authorize the purchase
of a John Deere Co. backhoe from Westside Tractor Sales in the amount of
$80,200. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Electrical Line Clearance (Tree Trimming). Mr. Ciesla reviewed a request
to extend for a second year the contract with Nels Johnson Tree Experts to
perform electrical line clearance tree trimming work for the coming year.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to extend the contract as
recommended with the total cost not to exceed $65,000. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Post Office Site: Scope of Services Contract Economic Analysis. Mr.
D’Onofrio explained that one of the components of the redevelopment process for
the Post Office site is to conduct an economic analysis of redevelopment
scenarios. The purpose of this process is to assist in determining the economic
and market feasibility of some of the concept plans prepared by The Lakota
Group that were developed from the public input-concept design phase of the
project as overseen by the Ad Hoc Post Office Committee.
In order to facilitate this economic analysis, the RFQ process was used to hire
a firm that would have the ability to assist the Post Office Committee on a
number of issues related to the redevelopment. At the conclusion of the
process, it was agreed that the most qualified firm was S.B. Friedman, and they
were asked to prepare a Scope of Services. The scope of Friedman’s work will
include:
   ·   Project Initiation
   ·   Absorption and Pricing Potential
   ·   Estimates of Construction Costs
   ·   Preparation of Economic Analysis Model
   ·   Sensitivity Tests
   ·   Additional Consultation on Ownership and Development Strategy
   ·   Presentation to the Village Board.
Also included is a detailed cost breakdown for each of the eight tasks and
subtasks.
Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that it is anticipated that this work will take five to
six months to complete at a total estimated project cost of $99,308.
President Woodbury commented that in the course of this study the economic
realities will emerge about what is realistic for the Post Office site.
The Trustees suggested several modifications to the proposal: (a) by including
"Civic Uses" as part of the consideration of the Site Capture Potential
delineated in part e. on page 5 of the proposal; (b) by considering the
Village's parking study in the course of analyzing the Site Capture Potential;
(c) by specifically including the Post Office Committee, Village Council and
Business Community Development Commission in the meeting called for in the last
paragraph of page 5 of the agreement; and (d) by modifying the second sentence
of the first paragraph of the section titled "Limitations of Our Engagement" to
clarify that the data collected under Point 2 of the Scope of Services will be
included in the report.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to authorize the Village
President to sign an agreement substantially in the form of the acceptance of
the March 16, 2006 Scope of Services proposal of S. B. Friedman & Co. to
perform economic analysis services for the Post Office Site Redevelopment, in a
total contract amount not to exceed $99,308, subject to the Village’s receipt
of a duly executed Compliance Affidavit from S. B. Friedman, and further
subject to revisions to reflect the Council's comments. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Replacement of 1995 John Deere Loader. Mr. Saunders reviewed a request to
purchase a replacement loader for snow removal and loading, material handling,
storm cleanup, and yard waste removal through the GSA contract pricing.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize the purchase of a
John Deere Co. loader from Westside Tractor Sales through GSA contract pricing
in the amount of $156,627.25. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Bid 06-010: 2006 Street Rehabilitation. Mr. Saunders reviewed bids for the
2006 Street Rehabilitation project and recommended the award of the bid to A
Lamp Concrete Contractors for the bid amount of $753,373.40.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
i)   Contract Extension – Landfill Groundwater and Leachate Sampling. Mr.
Saunders reviewed a request to extend for one year the contract with Test
America, Ltd. for groundwater monitoring and leachate sampling and testing
services for the Village’s closed landfill. He said the requested extension
would increase the rates bid in 2003 by 2%, which is still below the cost
proposal of the next lowest bidder from the 2003 bids.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize the requested
one-year extension in accordance with the contract bidding documents for an
estimated annual amount of $54,962. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and
Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
j)   Supplement #1 to Preliminary (Design) Engineering for Pine and Eldorado
Street Bridges. Mr. Saunders summarized this and the following two agreements,
saying that they are IDOT boiler plate agreements formalizing the contractual
relationship between the Village and the consultant and the Department of
Transportation for the reconstruction of the two bridges. The first would
authorize Supplement #1 for preliminary Engineering for the Pine and Eldorado
Street Bridges, increasing the engineering cost by $14,099, as discussed
earlier.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the increased
engineering costs as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
k)   Local Agency Federal Aid Agreement – Phase III Construction and
Engineering for Pine and Eldorado Street Bridges. As reviewed by Mr. Saunders,
in order to obtain Federal reimbursement for its expenditures for engineering
and construction costs, the Village must enter into a Local Agency Agreement
with the Department of Transportation that contains provisions and conditions
for the use of Federal funding for the bridge projects.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the Village
President to sign the Local Agency Agreement authorizing Federal funding for
Phase III Engineering and Construction for the Pine and Eldorado Street
Bridges. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
l)   Engineering Agreement with Ciorba Group for Phase III Construction
Engineering for Pine Street and Eldorado Street Bridges. As reviewed by Mr.
Saunders, this agreement provides for Ciorba Group to supply construction
engineering for both bridges for a fee not to exceed $179,195.34, of which the
Village’s share would be $35,839.07, which will be paid out of Motor Fuel Tax
Funds.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to enter into an agreement
with Ciorba Group, Inc. to perform Phase III Design Engineering services for
the Pine Street and Eldorado Street Bridges for an amount not to exceed
$179,195.34. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury commented on a recent coyote issue
in the neighborhood. He said that Winnetka’s Police Department is working with
the Village of Glencoe to remediate the situation.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the Winnetka Report should be
received by residents within the next week.
c)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village’s Police
Department had received accreditation for the fourth time from the Commission
on Accreditation for Law Enforcement Agencies (CALEA), a highly regarded and
broadly recognized body of law enforcement standards.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Gene Graeble to the Zoning
Board of Appeals to replace Robert vanden Bosch upon the expiration of his term
on May 1, 2006. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger moved to so appoint
Mr. Greable. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Rick Taft to the Fire
Pension Board, effective April 17, 2006. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to so appoint Mr. Taft. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Pending and Probable Litigation, Board Appointments, and Collective
Bargaining, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(2), respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 10:20 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:13 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Abell, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:14 p.m.
