                  Deputy Clerk
April 11, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 2, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
11, 2006 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:53 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers,
and Herb Ritchell. Also present: Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Chief of
Police Joseph DeLopez, Acting Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, Fire
Chief Scott Smith, Finance Director Ed McKee, and approximately 4 persons in
the audience.
2)   Educational Presentation: Residential Fire Sprinklers.
Chief Smith and Deputy Chief Laureys presented the Council with information and
videos about residential fire sprinklers and the lives saved due to this
technology. Deputy Chief Laureys dispelled some myths about home sprinklers by
saying that:
   sprinklers are designed to react to temperatures in individual rooms and
usually one sprinkler will control a developing fire;
   water damage caused by a sprinklered fire is substantially less than water
damage caused by fire department hoses;
   the installation cost in homes under construction can be as little as $1,500
- $2,000, or less than 1% of the value of a home;
   sprinklers can match ceiling and wall colors, and can be recessed or
partially recessed, depending on the décor of the home;
   sprinklers are tested at 2 to 3 times the water pressure of a regular
household and one in 16 million discharge accidentally;
   major insurance companies discount homeowners insurance when homes have
residential sprinklers;
At the conclusion of the presentation, Deputy Chief Laureys stated that the
combination of smoke detectors to give early warning and residential sprinklers
to control the fire before the fire department arrives, people are able to
escape to safety and damage to property is minimized.
Chief Smith cited examples of fires in the commercial districts that did not
affect or disrupt neighboring business because they were contained by
sprinklers. He added that most of the fires in Winnetka during the last ten
years have been in new construction and that, although Winnetka has had no
fatalities from fire in this time period, the surrounding communities have had
seventeen. He said that residential sprinklers are designed for life safety and
to give people time to get out of the house.
The Trustees held a short discussion and those present generally favored
sprinklers in new homes citing safety as the primary reason.
President Woodbury commented that the presentation was for educational purposes
and that the Council could open discussions about enacting an ordinance, but
suggested that the question be taken to the Caucus.
3)   Discussion of Photovoltaic Billing – Solar Panels.
Acting Director of Water & Electric, Rich Ciesla, explained that Mr. Thomas
Flannigan, 240 Sheridan Road, would like to install photovoltaic cells on his
home as an alternative source of power and has requested that the Village allow
him to use a net metering system that charges the customer only for the power
used. Mr. Ciesla stated that with the net metering method, the meter would run
backwards as power is delivered to the system. He said that the staff
recommends an avoided cost billing method instead which would credit the
customer for the cost of the energy that is supplied by the Village but is not
used by the customer.
Manager Williams explained the different components for providing electricity
to homeowners and that the amount that should be credited to this customer is
the Village’s cost for electricity purchased through IMEA. He said this method
would prevent the rest of the residents from subsidizing one person’s
electricity.
Trustee Behles added that another factor is that this customer would remain on
the grid, which means that if his system goes down, the Village needs to be
able to supply his full power needs and that doesn’t reduce the cost of
distribution or the capacity to supply power. Trustee Behles also said that the
customer should get credit for the amount of power he has provided and that the
question is if the Village wants to subsidize residents for exploring
alternative energy options.
Mr. Flannigan spoke about alternative sources of power, tax credits the State
of Illinois is offering for the installation of photovoltaic cells this year,
environmental concerns and net metering in other areas of the country. He said
he is not asking for a subsidy, but said that this type of system would keep
energy prices down for residents and is a small way to push things forward to
leave a better world for the future.
President Woodbury commented that the Village Council runs the affairs of the
Village, that this is a business issue, and the Council must look out for Mr.
Flannigan as well as the other 4000 customers in the Village.
Trustee Abell was in favor of providing an incentive to people who have the
photovoltaic systems in addition to a credit for the IMEA cost.
Village Attorney Janega stated that few municipal utilities are required to
provide net metering and the municipal utilities with net metering have a much
larger customer base than Winnetka and can offset the loss better. She
suggested that before discussing incentives, the Village would need to project
how many residents would take advantage of this and what impact it would have
on purchase power costs. She added that the Village may want to also explore
other alternatives for incentives.
Trustee Behles said that electricity is a taxpayer-based business in Winnetka
and suggested that the taxpayers should weigh in on this. He was in favor of
offering incentives, even if there is only one customer, but not net metering,
and thought that the Council should make a policy decision after considering
all of the implications.
President Woodbury said that from an environmental standpoint this may be the
right thing to do and asked that Manager Williams look into the environmental
issues.
At the conclusion of the discussion, Manager Williams ascertained that the
Council would like input from the public before shifting costs for just one
customer.
4)   New Trier High School Parking Lot Agreement.
Manager Williams reported that the Village would be entering into a three-year
contract with New Trier High School for the lease of parking spaces and the
staff would like to renegotiate the terms so that more of the Village’s costs
are recovered.
After discussing several points regarding staff time, costs of parking spaces,
use of the spaces and simplifying the contract, the Trustees authorized the
staff to make revisions to the contract before beginning negotiations.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn the meeting, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 9:57 p.m.
April 18, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 2, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, April
18, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:39 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Fire Chief Scott Smith, and 12 persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance and welcomed
members of Scout Troop 20 to the audience.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   May 2, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   May 9, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend. President Woodbury said that he might arrive
late.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 21, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   April 4, 2006, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury noted that the draft
minutes had been amended to clarify that Item 9(b), Policy Direction: 480 Oak
Street, had been withdrawn from consideration by the applicant. Trustee Eilers
commented on the phrasing of the motion to approve the Scope of Services
Agreement for S. B. Friedman & Co. Attorney Janega indicated that the minutes
were drafted from the actual written wording of the motion.  There being no
other comments, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve
the minutes as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   April 11, 2006, Special Meeting. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-7-2006 – Zoning Variation: 804 Lincoln Ave. - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance granting
two zoning variations to permit the construction of a room addition and
detached garage. She noted that the proposed ordinance does not contain
findings of particular hardship because there does not appear to be any
evidence thereof in the Zoning Board’s minutes. The ordinance does refer to
practical difficulties, since the standards for the Council to grant variations
are practical difficulty or particular hardship.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the ordinance as
reviewed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
Berger, and Ritchell. Nays: Trustee Behles. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-8-2006 – Special Use Permit: 546 Lincoln Ave. - Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed a proposed amendment to the draft ordinance granting a
Special Use Permit to allow a health club to be located in the rear of the
subject building, which is within the C-2 Retail Overlay zoning district. She
noted that a new recital had been added to make it clear that the recitation of
proposed hours is not to be seen as a limitation on the hours of business.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the ordinance as
reviewed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Old Business.
None.
8. Seating of the New Village Council.
Village Clerk, Douglas Williams, administered the Oath of Office to Trustees-
elect Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker, elected in the March 21st
election.
9. Ordinances and Resolutions.
None.
10. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
11. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1457 and 1458. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1457 in the amount of $990,637.67 and Warrant List No. 1458 in the
amount of $308,326.04. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Replacement Vehicles. Chief DeLopez reviewed a request to replace two
Police Department vehicles through the State of Illinois approved vendors. The
Chief proposed the purchase of two Dodge Chargers from Bob Ridings, Inc., with
the anticipation that if these vehicles perform as dependably and reliably as
expected, the Department will purchase a third later in the year.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the purchase
of the two vehicles through the State Bid Contract from Bob Ridings, Inc., for
the total amount of $41,735. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Police Department Property Disposal System. Chief DeLopez reviewed the
Police Department’s proposal to participate with “propertyroom.com” to more
efficiently dispose of recovered property and obsolete equipment designated for
disposal by the Village Council. He explained that the Department recovers
annually hundreds of lost or abandoned items whose owners cannot be identified
for return of the property. Many resources are expended in the recovery,
inventory, and storage of this property. In addition, there is no efficient
system for recovery of any value remaining in the property. He indicated that
although in the past the Village has donated this property to local
organizations for sale or auction, it is becoming increasingly difficult to
find a group that is willing to take it.
Propertyroom.com will pick up the property designated for disposal and auction
the items on their website for a percentage of the sale price and one-half of
the credit card processing costs.
President Woodbury inquired as to the normal holding period for lost and
abandoned property. Chief DeLopez said that normally it is kept for six to
twelve months. Attorney Janega explained that any time the Village disposes of
such property, or surplus property, it must be turned over from the Police
Department to the Village, which is typically done once or twice a year. She
indicated that she could come back to the Council with an annual authorization
of disposal amended to incorporate this new technique which would provide one
more formal avenue available to the Village under the authority of the Village
Manager.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to direct the Village
Attorney to prepare an appropriate ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Foundation Bid Approval for Fire Training Tower. Chief Smith reviewed the
bids for the construction of the foundation for the new residential-style
structure and annex to be built by WHP, pursuant to Council authorization on
July 12, 2005, adjacent to the four-story brick tower currently used by the
Fire Department for training. He recommended that the bid be awarded to
Schaefges Brothers, Inc., in the amount of $53,000.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
12. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Ritchell reported on a recent dinner meeting of the
Winnetka Historical Society, at which the Society expressed their appreciation
to the Council for their support throughout the 411 Linden matter.
Trustee Abell announced that he would be attending the upcoming meeting of the
NWMC.
13. Appointments.
President Woodbury called for a motion to appoint Trustee Abell as President
Pro Tem. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, so moved. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. Woodbury next suggested the appointment of Katie Stevens to the Forestry
Commission to replace Beth Jernigan, effective immediately. Trustee Abell,
seconded by Trustee Ritchell, so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Mark Fuller to the Fire
Pension Board, effective April 18, 2006. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to so appoint Mr. Fuller. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Next President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Stacy Tabachnik to the
Landmark Preservation Commission to replace Jane Burns, effective immediately.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell moved to so appoint Ms. Tabachnik. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Then President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Louise Holland to the
Landmark Preservation Commission to replace Susanne Bush, effective
immediately. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Abell moved to so appoint Ms.
Holland. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. Woodbury then called for a motion to reappoint Maureen Mitchell to a second
full term on the Plan Commission and to serve as its Chair. Trustee Behles,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Finally, President Woodbury called for a motion to reappoint Bob Horne to the
Business Community Development Commission for a full three-year term to replace
Pat Corrigan. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, so moved. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
14. Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, personnel matters, board
appointments, and review of Executive Session Minutes pursuant to Sections 2(c)
(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Tucker, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 8:41 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:18 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
15. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Abell, Eilers, Tucker, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:19 p.m.
