May 2, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 16, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, May 2,
2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:39 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Interim Director of Water & Electric
Rich Ciesla, and five persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   May 9, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend. President Woodbury reiterated that he might
arrive late.
b)   May 16, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   April 11, 2006, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   April 18, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-9-2006 – Zoning Variation: 251 White Oak Ln. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Corner Yard Setback
variation to permit the construction of a one-story garage and mudroom addition
on an existing portion of the building that is already nonconforming. He noted
that this is one of two additions planned for the home, but that the other is
in compliance with zoning requirements and does not require a variation. He
reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-0 to recommend approval
of the variation.
The applicant, William Doyle, and his architect, William Lang, reviewed the
plans for the Council, explaining that they had tried to plan the modifications
to minimize the existing nonconformities and to be sympathetic to the home’s
aesthetics. He pointed out that the proposed plans call for pulling the garage
away from the northern neighbor and away from the street while still preserving
existing large trees on the property.
Ron Allen, 265 White Oak, voiced support for the plan but expressed some
concern about his stake fence.
President Woodbury urged both parties to work together with the Community
Development Department to resolve any issues.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Eilers announced that Michael D’Onofrio had been elected to membership
in the Lambda Alpha Fraternity, an international organization recognizing
individuals who have excelled in careers in land development.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2006 – Sanitary Sewer Backflow Prevention -
Introduction. Mr. Saunders reviewed the proposed ordinance adding a new section
to the Village Code establishing a cost-sharing program for the installation of
sanitary sewer backflow prevention systems. He noted that the installation of
such a system is the most effective single step a homeowner can take to protect
their basement from sanitary sewer backups. He, however, pointed out that the
age of the Village’s system, the current level of technology, and limitations
of Village’s fiscal resources, make it impossible to eliminate back-ups
entirely.
Mr. Saunders explained that the proposed ordinance contains provisions
pertaining to the eligibility for program participation, conditions for
participation, permits and inspections, reimbursement limits and procedures,
application process, and program publicity. Summarizing these provisions, he
said that eligibility is limited to homes built before 1970 and reimbursement
would be limited to 50% of the project’s eligible costs up to a maximum of
$2,500, with any payment made directly to the homeowner after completion of the
work.
Ms. Janega reiterated that as drafted, the ordinance only applies to single-
family homes built prior to 1970, since during prior discussions it was the
Council’s direction not to include commercial properties. She also responded to
a question raised by Trustee Abell about the possibility of recording the
required waiver of liability against the property, saying that she did not
believe that to be necessary.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
introduce the ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Resolution No. R-13-2006 – Prevailing Wages – Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that State law requires that the Village annually pass a resolution
establishing and specifying the prevailing wages for Village contracts for the
construction of public works.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-13-
2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-14-2006 – Department Heads’ Salaries – Adoption.
President Woodbury called for comments on the resolution establishing the
salaries for the department heads for the 2006-2007 fiscal year. There being
none, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution
No. R-14-2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-15-2006 – Village Manager’s Salary – Adoption. Mr.
Woodbury then called for comments on the resolution setting the Village
Manager’s salary for the 2006-2007 fiscal year. There being no comments,
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
15-2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1459 and 1460. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1459 in the amount of $859,865.48 and Warrant List No. 1460 in the
amount of $249,458.72. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Cost Update on Water Plant Emergency Backwash Replacement. Mr. Ciesla
reviewed a request to approve a $12,716 change order for additional work
required to complete the new backwash line at the Water Plant.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the request. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker,
Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Bid No. 06-011: 2006 Sidewalk Replacement Program. Mr. Saunders reviewed
the bids for the 2006 Sidewalk Replacement Program. He recommended that the bid
be awarded to Schroeder and Schroeder, Inc., the second low bidder, for the
unit prices set forth in the bid. He explained that the low bidder, Suburban
Concrete, was deemed to be an unqualified bidder because of their failure to
perform work in a timely manner and failure to respond satisfactorily to
Village scheduling requirements.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid No. 06-012: PW 2 Replacement. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for the
purchase of a replacement vehicle for the Public Works Department. He
recommended that the bid for a 2006 Ford Escape Hybrid be awarded to Sessler
Ford in the amount of $28,381, with acceptance of the bid from Landmark Ford of
Springfield in the amount of $29,006 followed by Oakfield Ford for $29,300 as
the alternate sources if the vehicles specified have already been sold.
After a brief discussion about the use of hybrid vehicles, Trustee Berger,
seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid as recommended. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker,
Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Outdoor Seating Permit Application: Chocolate Box. Manager Williams
reviewed a request from the Chocolate Box for an Outdoor Seating Permit. He
explained that as a new business to the Village, the owners were heretofore
unaware of the requirement to file an application for Council approval. He
noted that the required certificate of insurance had been submitted and that
the Village Engineer had reviewed the proposed table layout and approved with
the recommendation that the tables be placed so as to not hide the standpipe
connection from the Fire Department. Mr. Williams indicated that the Fire Chief
expressed no objection to issuance of the Permit.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the Outdoor
Seating Permit conditioned on the tables being placed so as to not hide the
standpipe connection. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
