Recording Secretary
May 9, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: June 6, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
9, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Abell called the meeting to order at 7:39 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: President Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve
Saunders, Engineer Susan Chen, and approximately 11 persons in the audience.
2)   Discussion: Flood Hazard Protection Ordinance Revision.
Director of Public Works, Steve Saunders, noted that there have been two
previous Study Session discussions about the issue of basements in the flood
plain, and that staff has been working on developing an amendment to the Flood
Protection Ordinance in keeping with policy direction from the Council. He
indicated that staff has come up with a reasonably simple but effective formula
for determining where it might make sense to use a building pad, and where it
would be better to use a floodable crawl space.
Mr. Saunders then described how the formula works, referring to the graph that
was included in his agenda report. He explained that the narrower the lot and
lower the elevation, the less chance there is for building a basement. He said
that staff tested the formula by applying it to the various illustrative homes
that were included in the February Study Session packet, and the two homes that
had seemed the most objectionable would not pass the basement test.
Mr. Saunders then asked the Council for some additional direction with regard
to limitations on the height of retaining walls and side slope elevations,
explaining that staff was unsure whether it would be a good idea to restrict
these items only in the flood plain or whether it should also be used in the
zoning code.
Village Attorney Janega pointed out that, while the proposed formula is based
on assumptions as to retaining wall height, slope and elevation, the purpose of
the proposed formula is to determine whether a basement can be built and does
not actually set the maximum dimensions for retaining walls. She explained that
maximum dimensions could be set by amending the Zoning Ordinance.
After a short discussion about the pros and cons of using the zoning ordinance
as another tool to make sure the retention wall and side slope restrictions
would be effective, it was decided that the limitations should apply only to
lots in the flood plain and that only the flood plain regulations should be
amended at this time.
When concerns about FEMA were brought up by Manager Williams, Mr. Saunders
reported that there will be leeway for the Council to grant a variance if one
is applied for, so long as the minimum FEMA requirements are met. Attorney
Janega confirmed that the proposed formula is a more stringent standard than
what FEMA requires, so the Village will have the discretion to grant variances
without varying FEMA's minimum requirements. She added that the Village can use
the number of variance applications as an indicator of how well the formula is
working.
Mr. Saunders informed the Council that homes with floodable crawlspaces will
still be considered in the flood plain and thus will need flood insurance, and
reminded them that the new restrictions will merely put some lots back in the
same position they were in before FEMA started permitting construction of
basements using clay building pads.
President Pro Tem Abell then suggested that Mr. Saunders work on a draft of the
changes to the Flood Protection Ordinance for the Council to review at a future
meeting.
President Woodbury arrived at 8:30 p.m. and presided for the remainder of the
evening.
3)   Discussion: Possible Parking Improvements in Elm Street Business
Districts:
Manager Williams reviewed the Village’s efforts over the years to deal with
parking deficiencies, closing with a run-down of the different Village
committees and civic groups that have attended presentations of the most recent
parking study conducted by Rich & Associates. He distributed two memos to the
Council, one from the Business Community Development Commission recommending
construction of a parking structure at the Lincoln Avenue parking lot, and the
other from the Post Office Committee, calling for 25 parking spaces over and
above the replacements and those newly created by demand, when redevelopment of
the Post Office site takes place.
Manager Williams acknowledged that the Village has both the financial resources
to finance a major project, and staff who are experienced in managing multi-
million dollar contracts. He then turned the floor over to Mr. Saunders, who
described the half-dozen new parking possibilities identified in the parking
study. Using a power point presentation, Mr. Saunders showed the audience a
virtual rendering of what a new parking deck on Lincoln Avenue might look like.
There was some debate about how a new parking structure might be utilized, with
Mr. Saunders and Manager Williams stressing that commuter parking would have to
be moved to the new structure from Green Bay Road and Lincoln Avenue, to assure
that the parking structure would be used. Mr. Saunders talked about the various
funding possibilities, citing multiple sources that could be used, including
grants, bonds or possibly setting up a Special Service Area, with the Village
possibly contributing up to $3.9 million out of its contingency fund.
The Council deliberated about the merits of building a new, multi-level
facility on Lincoln Avenue and examined different configuration ideas, after
which President Woodbury called for comments from the audience.
Ellen Abell, Chairman of the Board of Governors of the Winnetka Community
House, applauded the council for addressing the parking issue, and indicated
that the Community House is very much in support of a new parking deck.
Pat Horne, 791 Bryant, also spoke in support of a new parking structure and
asked the Council to consider using the space above it as affordable housing
for older residents looking to downsize but stay in Winnetka.
Bob Horne, 162 Fuller Lane, a member of the BCDC, expressed support for a new
facility on the grounds that it would help revitalize the Elm Street business
districts. He said that he had acquired properties adjoining the Lincoln Avenue
parking lot and added that he also favored examining the air rights and
affordable housing. He stated that while he believed that the consultants have
been very thorough, he is concerned that spaces need to be added to their
deficit figure for future development demand, because a lot of buildings can’t
support parking underneath if they are demolished for redevelopment. He
suggested that developers could pay into a fund to support construction of new
parking facilities.
Manager Williams commented that he agreed with Mr. Horne when it comes to
taking a long-term view of Winnetka’s parking situation, and about a possible
parking fund for developers to pay into.
President Woodbury observed that in his view, parking is the main issue to be
decided at this point, with the motivation being that the Post Office spaces
will be lost during the site’s redevelopment.
After a brief discussion about the construction timeframe for completion of a
new parking structure and the Post Office redevelopment, the Trustees came to
an understanding that it is time for the Village to address the parking issue.
Manager Williams agreed to take the analysis of the new parking deck to a
deeper level and bring it before the Council during the summer months.
4)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn the meeting, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 9:44 p.m.
   ____________________________________
   Recording Secretary
May 16, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 6, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
16, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:39 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, and
approximately 25 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   June 6, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   June 13, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 2, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell suggested a minor change to
page 4 of the minutes concerning his report on the NWMC meeting. Trustee
Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as amended.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-9-2006 – Zoning Variation: 251 White Oak Lane – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments made to this ordinance, introduced on
May 2, granting a corner yard setback variation to permit the construction of a
one-story garage and mudroom addition at 251 White Oak.
She explained revisions to the ordinance since introduction, and noted that
references to the need for zoning relief related to the proposed two-story
addition at the rear of the house were deleted, since the encroachment at that
point is a small sliver that Community Development staff had determined does
not require a zoning variation.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend the ordinance and by
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-9-
2006, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2006 – Sanitary Sewer Backflow Prevention – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments that were made to this ordinance,
introduced on May 2, which would add a new section to the Village Code to
establish a cost-sharing program for the installation of sanitary sewer anti-
back-up devices.
She explained that in addition to several technical amendments, a new paragraph
was added to confirm that the program is voluntary on the part of the Village
and is not to be construed either as a waiver of defenses or immunities, or as
an assumption of liability. She noted that a new subsection was added to
clarify that the Director of Public Works will administer the program, subject
to the direction of the Village Manager.
After a few brief questions were answered by Attorney Janega, Trustee Eilers
moved to amend the ordinance as presented. Trustee Abell seconded and the
motion passed unanimously by voice vote.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-3-
2006 establishing a cost-sharing program providing partial reimbursement for
installation of overhead sewers or anti-back-up devices in single family homes
built before 1970. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-16-2006 – MFT Resolution: Street Resurfacing – Adoption.
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders reported that, as part of the Village’s
Capital Improvement Projects for 2006, the Village has scheduled various street
resurfacing projects that will be funded solely with Motor Fuel Tax funds. He
asked the Council to consider adopting Resolution R-16-2006 granting
authorization to utilize the MFT funds.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-16-2006
authorizing the use of Motor Fuel Tax funds for resurfacing of various streets
in the amount of $200,000.00. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1461 and 1462. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items and noting that the Village is
purchasing its first hybrid vehicle, he moved to approve Warrant List No. 1461
in the amount of $775,508.66 and Warrant List No. 1462 in the amount of
$623,608.37. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Glendale Sidewalk. Mr. Saunders reviewed the history of the Glendale
sidewalk request. He reported that the Council first considered the matter at
its September 20, 2005, meeting. At that time, Council directed staff to
perform a traffic and pedestrian study on Glendale Avenue during peak hours,
schedule some meetings with the neighbors, and return in the spring for further
discussions.
Mr. Saunders explained the results of the engineering study, which identified
significant conflict between pedestrians, bicyclists and vehicles. He indicated
that Village staff met with interested neighbors twice to discuss the proposed
routing of the sidewalk, and met once with the homeowners directly affected by
the sidewalk to review some alternatives. He then gave a detailed analysis of
the alternative options contained in his agenda report, concluding with staff
’s recommendation that the sidewalk is the most straightforward option with
regard to cost, safety, and traffic regulation.
The Trustees questioned Mr. Saunders about various aspects of the alternatives
to sidewalk construction. Mr. Saunders noted that the main concern is
eliminating conflicts between pedestrians, bicyclists and vehicles, while
maintaining traffic flow. Trustee Abell brought up that the Village’s
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, sets forth guidelines prioritizing the
connection of the sidewalk network and installing sidewalks near schools.
Mr. Dan Kadjian of 1124 Pine, Ann Wilder of 1096 Spruce and Sharon King of 1134
Pine each informed the Council that they were not in favor of a sidewalk, and
that one of the other alternatives should be considered first. Mr. Kadjian
presented the Council with a petition against the sidewalk signed by neighbors
within 250 feet of the proposed sidewalk. Ms. King called on Trustee Tucker to
recuse herself from voting on the issue because she had signed the petition
asking for a sidewalk installation.
Nine students from Skokie School each came forward to express their support for
the sidewalk, saying they didn’t feel safe on Glendale Avenue when oncoming
cars forced them to jump out of harm’s way. Eight concerned residents also took
turns speaking to the Council to endorse a sidewalk as an easy solution to the
safety problems now faced by the children on that section of Glendale Avenue.
After the public comment came to an end, Trustee Tucker stated she would not
recuse herself from the issue unless the Village Attorney advised her to do so.
She commented that she believed she had signed the petition before she was
elected Trustee, and that furthermore, at the time she was nominated, a plank
in the Village Caucus platform strongly urged more sidewalks for
schoolchildren. She noted that as an elected official, she has a responsibility
to speak for all concerned constituents.
Attorney Janega stated that Trustee Tucker doesn’t have a property interest in
this matter and she therefore would not instruct Trustee Tucker to recuse
herself. She added that, as legislators, Trustees have the right to take public
stands on issues without having to remove themselves from making decisions on
those issues. Trustee Eilers concurred, saying that each Trustee is of the
community and shouldn’t be made to surrender their convictions once elected.
Ms. King replied that her objection still stands and that she still doesn’t
believe a sidewalk will solve all the pedestrian and traffic problems.
President Woodbury thanked all who had come forward to participate in the
discussion. He revealed that the process of determining where to install
sidewalks had historically been a divisive one until 2003, when the Council
created guidelines intended to diminish discord and create more agreement. He
noted that it is Council policy to install sidewalks on at least one side of
the street, with priority status given to heavily traveled properties or those
close to schools.
Mr. Saunders reiterated Staff’s position that, of all the options studied, a
sidewalk is the best and most cost effective method to address the problem of
vehicles and pedestrians sharing the roadway.
President Woodbury asked the Trustees to decide if they needed more dialog or
if they were ready to make a decision.
Trustee Behles observed that since the sidewalk proposal is consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan and the need was clearly demonstrated, he would be in favor.
Trustee Berger agreed that the best solution for separating vehicles and
pedestrians was a sidewalk, and thanked staff for doing an excellent job of
researching the alternatives.
Trustee Eilers remarked that he favored a sidewalk, as it’s consistent with
prior policy, and Trustee Abell said he would fully support a sidewalk, as it
is consistent with the aims of the Comprehensive Plan.
Trustee Tucker concurred with the previous statements and added that the most
cost-effective and safety-oriented position is to support the sidewalk. Trustee
Ritchell questioned the notion that Glendale becomes a quiet country lane for
only one block and expressed his support for the sidewalk. He also thanked
staff for their research and the community for their input.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. Kadjian and Ms. King for their attendance and
diligence. He acknowledged that the issue affects their properties, but
stressed that the public property the roadway sits on has a relation to Skokie
and Washburne Schools, where attendance levels are currently increasing. He
applauded the students who came forward and thanked them for their courage in
speaking up.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the
construction of a sidewalk on the west side of Glendale Avenue between Pine and
Spruce streets. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None
c)    Amendment to NTTEC Agreement. Manager Williams reported that in 2004, the
Village entered into an intergovernmental agreement with New Trier Township
Educational Cooperative (NTTEC) to connect its schools through a fiber optics
network utilizing the Village’s electrical conduits. He indicated that the
Village charges an annual fee of $17,500 for the use of its conduits. He went
on to say that as a member of NTTEC, Winnetka School District 36 connected
Skokie, Washburne, Greeley and Crow Island schools to this fiber optics system,
but has now decided to separate their system from New Trier High School’s
system and NTTEC.
Mr. Williams said that, as a result of this decision, New Trier has proposed a
reduction of its annual fee to reflect the fact that it will only be using 60%
of the cables from this point forward. He added that Village staff agrees that
the proposed new fee of $10,500 accurately reflects the percentage of the
system that will be used by the high school under this new arrangement.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to sign a letter of agreement amending the original Intergovermental
Agreement setting a new conduit rental rate of $10,500 per year. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Ritchell reported that the Winnetka Historical Society
will hold its annual dinner and auction on June 10, and encouraged all
listeners to attend.
Trustee Abell noted that he found the Historical Society exhibit on the Village
Green to be very interesting. He announced that he had gathered information on
the status of leash laws in the community to help develop a questionnaire in
the fall, and mentioned that he thought the NWMC’s Clean Air Communities
campaign could be something for Winnetka to become involved with.
Trustee Behles reported that Metra has not met their due date to complete the
schematic design of the station renovation, and the meeting to review the
schematic design has not yet been scheduled, but that Liz Rosenthal is pressing
them for a date.
Trustee Eilers reported that the BCDC will use its next several meetings to
look at the commercial overlay district, its history, boundaries and uses, with
an eye towards making recommendations to the Council.
c)   Village Attorney. None.
d)   Village Manager. Manager Williams announced that he and Attorney Janega
have met with several residents on Scott Avenue to start getting signatures for
the petition and intergovernmental agreement with Glencoe. He added that he
hopes to have an agreement to annex the split lots before the Council by mid-
to-late summer.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Roberta Rubin to the Business
Community Development Commission for a second full term. Trustee Tucker,
seconded by Trustee Ritchell moved to so reappoint Ms. Rubin. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Bill Johnson as the Plan
Commission’s representative to the Business Community Development Commission,
effective immediately. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to
so appoint Mr. Johnson. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, Board appointments, personnel
matters and possible purchase of land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3),
2(c)(1), and 2(c)(5) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 10:02 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:30 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
June 6, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 6, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
6, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, and approximately 15 persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4. Quorum.
a)   June 13, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   June 20, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend, except Trustee Berger, who said that
she might be out of town.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 9, 2006, Study Session. After Trustee Abell suggested some minor
changes, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the
minutes as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
a)   May 16, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-10-2006 – Special Use Permit: 557-559 Chestnut -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Special Use Permit to
allow the expansion of a real estate office in the C-2 Overlay Zoning District
into the rear of the adjacent retail space. The applicant, Jean Wright,
proposes to reconfigure the space to reduce The New England Trader’s retail
sales space at the front of 557 Chestnut to 565 square feet; break through the
wall between 557 Chestnut and 559 Chestnut to connect the current Jean Wright
Real Estate space at 559 Chestnut with an additional 982 sq. ft. square feet at
557 Chestnut; and create a 450-square foot space consisting of a corridor from
the retail space to the rear of the building. The corridor would include the
toilet and will be part of The New England Trader lease space. The rear office
space would be permanently connected to the Jean Wright Real Estate space at
559 Chestnut, and the remaining retail space at 557 Chestnut would continue to
be occupied by The New England Trader, which would operate with a reduced
inventory.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals voted unanimously in
favor of the Special Use Permit. However, the Plan Commission, by a split vote,
found the application to be inconsistent with the Comprehensive Plan.
Therefore, the matter comes to the Village Council without a favorable
recommendation.
Trustee Abell noted that there is a 400 sq. ft. minimum for retail space in the
Overlay District, and Trustee Tucker mentioned that the Business Community
Development Commission had commented that 1,000 sq. ft. is the minimum for
retail space.
Attorney Janega explained that there are some blanks in the draft Ordinance,
because the Zoning Board’s findings, which address the basic standards for
special uses, are not supported by testimony or documentary evidence in the
record, and there are no findings on the additional standards required for
office uses in the C-2 Overlay District.
Ted Argiris, the owner of The New England Trader, explained that he is only
looking to change his business plan by reducing his inventory because the
current amount of square footage is too expensive for him.
President Woodbury asked Mr. Argiris whether he had looked for other smaller
spaces in the Village. Mr. Argiris responded that he had, but that they were
considerably more expensive. In reply to a follow-up question from Trustee
Behles, Mr. Argiris indicated that he was only aware of one space available in
the size for which he is looking, but that it is still more expensive than what
he is paying now.
Trustee Tucker asked Mr. Argiris whether he had explored other business
arrangements, such as sharing space with other retailers in the Village. He
replied that he had not.
Trustee Ritchell asked Mr. Argiris if he was currently open for business. Mr.
Argiris responded that he was not, pending resolution of the special use
application.
Jean Wright spoke in support of her application. Ms. Wright said that she would
only be adding conference rooms and storage in the expanded space, not
expanding the existing real estate business or adding more realtors. She
expressed her desire to accommodate a good tenant who serves a niche in the
community.
David Schrauth, attorney for the applicant who also represents the Chamber of
Commerce, summarized the applicant’s case.
Elise Covey, 1503 Edgewood Ln., urged the Council to vote favorably on the
application.
President Woodbury inquired of Ms. Wright whether it would be possible to
condition the special use permit on the existence of the existing tenant. Ms.
Wright responded that the cost of reconfiguring the space is approximately
$100,000, and it could not be reversed.
President Woodbury also asked Ms. Wright if the space could be leased for
retail as is. Ms. Wright stated that another Winnetka merchant had called to
inquire about the availability of the space.
President Woodbury commented that while there is a great deal of support in the
community for New England Trader and Ms. Wright, the matter at hand is not
about the individuals or businesses involved, but about the Overlay District.
He outlined the Council’s options and asked for the Trustees’ views.
Trustee Ritchell voiced appreciation for all that has been done by the parties
involved, but said that this is not about the people, but about the bricks and
mortar. He expressed concern that what is being proposed will change the space
and result in a reduction in the amount of the overall retail space and, in
turn, sales tax, and would set a precedent for future similar requests.
Trustee Tucker indicated that she could not support the request because it
chips away at the Village’s precious, but limited, retail space.
Trustees Abell and Berger concurred, as did Trustee Behles, who disagreed with
the Zoning Board’s finding that this would not be a change in use. He voiced
strong support for protecting the C-2 Overlay District.
Trustee Eilers commended Ms. Wright for her efforts to preserve a unique
merchant, but also expressed concern about setting a precedent. He said that if
the Council grants this request, it will begin to erode the Village’s C-
2 Overlay District. He added that the Business Community Development Commission
is looking into the C-2 Overlay District, its history and future in Winnetka,
and would be providing a report to the Council. He said that in his
professional experience, 500 sq. ft is small for a retail space and 21 ft. deep
is too short. Therefore, he would have to vote against granting the special use
application.
Noting the negative consensus of the Council, President Woodbury thanked Ms.
Wright and Mr. Argiris for going through the process and expressed his hope
that Mr. Argiris would find another suitable location in town for his business.
He remarked that the C-2 Overlay District is now about 20 years old, and it is
now time to look at it in light of today’s retail environment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to deny the request for a
Special Use Permit and to direct the Village Attorney to prepare findings and
conclusions in support thereof. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and
Behles.
b)   Ordinance No. M-11-2006 – Zoning Variation: 983 Vine – Introduction &
Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Nonconforming Use variation to
permit the construction of a new covered entry porch and the addition of a
roof, which would be an expansion of a nonconforming use.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
introduce the ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
The applicant, Julie Erst, noted that they had submitted a request to proceed
directly to approval at this meeting. In consideration thereof, Trustee Eilers,
seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to reconsider the original motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees, Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, Abell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustees Eilers and Behles then withdrew the original motion and Trustee Abell,
seconded by Trustee Ritchell, then moved to waive introduction of the proposed
ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Woodbury. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Finally, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adopt Ordinance
M-11-2006 granting the requested variation. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-12-2006 – Disposal of Surplus Village Property –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed an ordinance that would authorize the
disposal of surplus Village property by a variety of methods, including
internet auction. The authorization would extend to the end of the current
fiscal year.
President Woodbury inquired whether the ordinance would permit the Village to
donate property to the Community House or other local organization if it wished
to do so. Ms. Janega responded that it would.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1463 and 1464. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1463 in the amount of $909,128.79 and Warrant List No. 1464 in the
amount of $405,458.96. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Chamber of Commerce Request: Winnetka Days. Manager Williams reviewed the
request from the Chamber of Commerce to hold its annual Winnetka Days
celebration on Friday, July 14th and Saturday, July 15th.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Policy Direction: CBI Subdivision of 630 Pine Lane. Mr. Norkus reviewed a
request from CBI Custom Homes to divide the single parcel at 630 Pine Lane into
three lots, two of which would be accessed from Pine Lane and the third from
Hibbard Road. He noted that the existing property is improved with a residence
built in 1922, which would be largely retained. The applicants are proposing to
build two new homes to either side of the existing house. He explained that the
current proposed plans are similar to the prior subdivision plans, but said
that there are some significant differences, i.e., the number of trees impacted
and the redefinition of compensatory storage and storm water detention areas to
include retaining walls.
Mr. Norkus noted that the applicant has also submitted an application for
landmark status, pursuant to the conditions of the prior subdivision request
which, although granted by the Council, was never finalized by the applicant.
He reported that the Landmark Preservation Commission had recommended approval
of the proposed alterations to the existing residence in connection with the
landmark application. In addition, Mr. Norkus reported that the Plan Commission
had recommended denial of the proposed subdivision.
Finally, Mr. Norkus reported that the applicants had also just submitted an
application for demolition of the existing residence.
John Senica and Joanne Angarola, attorneys for the applicant, summarized the
applicant’s case and provided further detail about the application. Mr. Senica
represented that the applicant has been working diligently to satisfy all of
the requirements and expressed his belief that the Plan Commission took what
should have been a rigid process and imposed their own subjective criteria on
the Village Code, making up new rules as it went along when the sole question
should have been whether the application meets the specific legal requirements
of the subdivision ordinance. He said that the applicant believes it has met
all of the legal requirements needed for approval in compliance with the
subdivision and engineering requirements and the Zoning Ordinance, as well as
the supplemental conditions imposed by the Council during the 2004 subdivision
process, and that the only open issues are compensatory storage and tree
preservation.
Mr. Senica indicated that CBI has examined the recommendations of the Plan
Commission and believes that there are some things that can be done to further
modify the plans, such as reducing the size of the detention area on Lot 1,
putting all of the detention on that lot underground, eliminating most of the
retaining walls, and reducing the overall size of the new homes. However, the
cost of the continued delay is exorbitant and the applicant is looking for a
commitment from the Council of a good faith effort to reach a compromise on the
outstanding issues so that a firm decision can be reached at the June 20th
meeting. He stated that CBI is not planning to withdraw its present plan, but
neither would it withdraw its demolition application.
President Woodbury took exception to the implication that the Village has not
been working in good faith and offense at the submission of the demolition
application. He pointed out that the original subdivision approval lapsed not
because of any action on the part of the Village but because the applicant
failed to submit the final subdivision plans and to otherwise fulfill the
requirements and conditions of the relevant resolution within the requisite 12-
month period. He said that the Council would not negotiate with the applicant
and suggested that Mr. Senica explore the outstanding issues with his client.
Mr. Senica stated that the applicant is trying to preserve its options.
After a brief discussion during which Attorney Janega explained that it was not
feasible to resolve anything and expect action by the Council on June 20th, Mr.
Woodbury called for a break in this discussion for Mr. Senica and his clients
to confer while the Council continued with other items of business.
d)   Village Landscape Maintenance Contract. Mr. Saunders explained that the
contractor to whom the Village’s landscaping contract was awarded on March 21,
2006, has failed to meet the contractual requirements and has not performed
adequately on the contract. Consequently, Village staff has informed the
contractor, Ray Pollina Landscaping, that they are in default of the contract
and that the contract is terminated. Meanwhile, staff met with the other two
bidders for the contract to ascertain whether their bid proposals were still
valid and to discuss the possibility of awarding portions of the contract to
each firm. He reported that both contractors agreed to this course of action.
Therefore, Mr. Saunders recommended that the Council award landscape
maintenance contracts to KGI Landscaping, for a monthly total of $3,503.00, and
to Scopelitti Landscaping at the hourly rates and charges specified in their
response to Bid 06-001, for a two-year period ending March 31, 2008, with an
option for a third year, as specified in the bid documents for Bid 06-001.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Bid No. 06-015: Chatfield Road Sanitary Sewer Construction and Repair and
Pavement Restoration. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for the construction of a
new sanitary sewer with service stubs and the rehabilitation of the existing
pavement on Chatfield Road, from the West End to Gordon Terrace. He recommended
that the bid be awarded to Lenny Hoffman Excavating for the base bid amount of
$251,686, with a reduction of $32,567.50 for the deletion of all pavement
resurfacing line items, amending the contract to $219,118.50.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   2006 Street Rehabilitation Program – Change Order #1. Mr. Saunders
reviewed a request to increase the contract amount awarded to A Lamp Concrete
Contractors for street rehabilitation by $50,000.00 so as to include the
section of Cherry Street between Rosewood Avenue and Glendale Avenue in the
project.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the Change Order
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
g)   Bid No. -6-016: Replacement of Fire Department Utility Vehicle. Manager
Williams reviewed a request for authorization to purchase a 2007 F250 4WD to
replace an existing vehicle that has reached its life expectancy. The vehicle
is used for a variety of purposes such as transporting supplies and personnel
and snow removal. He reported staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to
Elmhurst Form in the amount of $27,935.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
**************
The Council returned to the discussion of Item 9(c), CBI Subdivision of 630
Pine Lane.
Ms. Angarola indicated CBI’s willingness to hold the application for demolition
in abeyance with no further action to see whether the parties can reach
agreement.
After a discussion about time lines, the parties agreed that by June 23rd CBI
would submit its revised plans incorporating the modifications referred to
earlier by Mr. Senica. Staff would then review the revisions and bring them
back to the Council for consideration, hopefully by the July 18th meeting. Ms.
Janega indicated that if in the meantime the applicant wants to proceed
directly with its application for Landmark status, separate and distinct from
the subdivision application, the Village would be willing to consider it by
itself. She accepted Ms. Angarola’s representation that there will be no
further action on the demolition permit at this time and that the timeline for
that will proceed from the date of agreement or impasse.
Andy Block, 644 Pine Lane, expressed his belief that CBI has not been a good
neighbor as it represented it would be. He voiced concern about the care of the
trees and neglect of the existing residence during the construction period.
Norris Love, 1175 Pelham Rd., and Terry Westbrook, 1160 Pelham Rd., supported
Mr. Block’s comments and spoke in opposition to the proposed subdivision.
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury reported that the Village had been
informed by State Senator Schoenberg that the section of Willow Road from
Forestway to the Edens will be resurfaced during the course of this year and
the section from Forestway to Provident, which the State still owns, will be
reconstructed in 2007. He added that he had indicated to Senator Schoenberg
that if IDOT follows through, the Village would be interested in pursuing the
takeover of Willow Road.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that after a recent meeting with Metra
and Union Pacific regarding the rehabilitation of the Elm Street Train station,
several issues about the construction project remain unresolved. Therefore, it
is unlikely that construction will begin as hoped in 2007.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Business Community Development Commission
would soon be submitting its recommendations on a property maintenance code.
c)   Village Attorney. None.
d)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Illinois Municipal
League has embarked on a comprehensive study of public pensions in Illinois;
and NWMC managers are forming a rapid response team to get information to the
Legislature when there are changes proposed in pension laws.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Jeff Cohen to the Building
Review Committee for a second full term. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to so reappoint Mr. Cohen. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Bill Shiel as the
Chairperson of the Business Community Development Commission to replace Barbara
Aquilino, effective immediately. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger,
moved to so appoint Mr. Shiel. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Abell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, Board appointments, and personnel
matters, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(1), respectively, of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:55 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:02 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:03 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
June 13, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: July 6, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
13, 2006 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:45 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and approximately 2 persons in the
audience.
2)   Discussion: Historic Preservation & Impact of Residential Redevelopment.
Trustee Behles announced that in an effort to address the 2005 Caucus planks
concerning historic preservation and the impact of demolitions on Village
character, he and Trustee Ritchell had met with various Village groups engaged
in those issues. He explained that the dialogues included members from the 2006
Winnetka Caucus Council, Landmark Preservation Commission, Building Review
Committee, Residential Design Guidelines Committee, Winnetka Historical Society
and residents from the Village Green neighborhood.
Trustee Behles indicated that two main study issues that were identified during
the course of the discussions: investigate the formation of historic districts
and explore measures that could be put in place to encourage rehabilitation and
discourage redevelopment. He said that with regard to establishing historic
districts, there are local precedents to look to for guidance, then noted that
the Building Review Committee has formed a subcommittee to explore both
positive and negative incentives to encourage preservation and discourage
teardowns, and recommended that a committee be formed to study both issues
further.
Trustee Ritchell observed that the members of the BRC are quite authoritative
about Winnetka architecture, but they feel powerless to effect change without
further guidance from the Council and more effective tools to work with.
Trustee Behles noted that it was not their intention to call for the formation
of a brand-new committee, but rather to form a subcommittee made up of members
pulled from committees already involved in the issues of historic preservation
and residential redevelopment, as there is some overlap of the missions these
different groups.
President Woodbury asked Trustee Behles what advantages a historic district
would offer to homeowners.
Trustee Behles replied that it offers some tax freeze incentives to preserve
the property, and makes it easier to win approval of the tax benefits. He
explained that establishing historic districts is as much about public
relations as it is about anything else, and that in Wilmette, which has two
historic districts, the establishment of the first one had a little opposition
when it was first proposed, but the second was unopposed, because word went out
to the community that this is a non-threatening activity. He said at the same
time, the neighborhoods benefit from the distinction that there is something
special to preserve and the architectural heritages is worth saving. He
commented that the same could hold true for Winnetka, once residents overcome
their fear of restrictions and become aware of the impact of architecture on
the quality of life for all in the Village.
After a discussion about the concept of preserving the character of the Village
and about who would staff the new committee, it was the sense of the Council
that the issues of historic districts and the impact of redevelopment merited
further study, and that the process might re-energize the Building Review
Committee.
Manager Williams cautioned that the Community Development staff is stretched
very thin for the foreseeable future, with the streetscape, Post Office
redevelopment, affordable housing, residential design and just the day-to-day
operation of the department. There was some discussion among the Trustees as to
whether an increase in staffing was called for, and how it would be funded.
3)   Discussion: Caucus Platform re: Enhancing Rehab Opportunities.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio reported that a caucus platform
recommended that the Village establish a clearinghouse to match the owners of
older homes with potential buyers who wish to renovate such homes.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that staff investigated the concept of a clearinghouse,
and identified a number of policy issues that surfaced as a result:
   ·   Should the Village be involved in private real estate matters?
   ·   Should the clearinghouse be voluntary or should the Village require all
houses for sale to register with the clearinghouse?
   ·   If the clearinghouse is to be mandatory, how should that be done?
   ·   What is the role of the clearinghouse with regard to the real estate
community?
   ·   How would data be kept current?
   ·   Would the clearinghouse be an information source or a facilitator
between the buyer and seller?
   ·   How would the clearinghouse be staffed and funded?
   ·   What criteria will be used to determine if a property is historically or
architecturally significant?
Mr. D’Onofrio said that the Landmark Preservation Council of Illinois is not
aware of any such clearinghouses in the Chicago area and could not locate any
private foundation that was established to buy, rehab and re-sell homes. He
acknowledged that the Historic Landmark Foundation of Indiana does do that type
of work, but that it has a staff of 120 and receives substantial support from
the Lilly Co.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the clearinghouse idea dovetails with the issue of
preservation that had just been discussed. He said that if residents knew a
house was on the market they would have the option of trying to save it.
Manager Williams conceded that there are some major planning and policies
issues to be dealt with before progress could be made on establishing a
clearinghouse and that the issue would not be appropriate for the new
subcommittee to tackle. He said that although the Caucus has raised concern
about the issue, it would have to be a back-burner project.
The Trustees discussed the problems involved with moving forward with the
clearinghouse idea, and President Woodbury invited Gene Graeble, chair of the
2005 Caucus, to comment.
Mr. Graeble observed that he was pleased to note the Village Council has
diligently addressed the needs of the community as expressed in the Caucus
planks, and said that he understood staffing can be an issue and that projects
have to be prioritized.
President Woodbury said he recognized that the topic is an important one for
the community, and that it is something that could take a great deal of time to
sort out. He then asked Manager Williams to create a list of possible
candidates to contribute to the new subcommittee.
4)   Draft Intergovernmental Agreement re: Scott Avenue Split Lots.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega reported on the progress of the
intergovernmental agreement with Glencoe to annex the rear portions of the
split lots on the north side of Scott Street to the Village. She called the
Council’s attention to the draft agreement and said that Glencoe had made
mostly technical changes, and that the only change of substance was made with
regard to the property tax reimbursements, which the draft would limit to
annexations done before next spring’s elections, while Glencoe wants that to be
open-ended, which could complicate calculating the reimbursements, because the
five-year periods could start at different times.
Attorney Janega remarked that the process is a straightforward one. She
explained that the two villages would first approve the agreement, acting as
close to simultaneously as possible. Glencoe would then first pass its
disconnection ordinance, and Winnetka would prepare a plat and pass its
annexation ordinance.
Manager Williams observed that the issue would likely be on an agenda in July.
Trustee Ritchell asked what would happen in subsequent years if people don’t
petition for disconnection from Glencoe at this time.
Attorney Janega indicated that the Village would be receptive to them at any
time. She added that the petition is drafted with detailed language that gives
people the option of going to court if Glencoe does not agree to disconnect,
and that the possibility of Glencoe succeeding in court seems unlikely.
5)   Continued Discussion: Financial Analysis of Possible New Parking
Structures.
Manager Williams reported that staff has done some more investigating regarding
funding for a new parking deck, and said that it seems clear that the
likelihood of obtaining Congestion Mitigation and Air Quality (CMAQ)
improvement funding is very small and the grant application process could add
up to two years to the project.
In response to questioning from the Trustees, Mr. Williams responded that the
amount of CMAQ funding could be about $2 million, and the reason it couldn’t be
counted on was that the Village was not adding a sufficient net gain of new
commuter parking.
Public Works Director Steve Saunders added that parking structures are at the
bottom of the list for funding and CMAQ has funded only 3 or 4 in the 14 years
they’ve been in existence. He explained that the reason it would drag out the
construction time is that it is a federal aid program that is run through IDOT
and the additional layer of oversight results in a 24 to 30-month process just
to get from design to construction. He added that applications for the grant
are only accepted once per year in January and that it would be difficult if
not impossible to get an application in for January 2007, because the Village
would have to complete a preliminary design and hire a consultant before the
application could be completed. He also pointed out that trying to meet the
deadline for January of 2007 would rush the Council’s decision-making process
on the issue.
In response to questions from the Council, Mr. Saunders explained that the
Village could start with the construction process while its application to CMAQ
was being considered, because CMAQ publishes a draft of program recipients in
July and a final list in November. He said that the cost of preparing the grant
application would be about $80,000 to $90,000 and noted that, while some of
that money would be part of the project costs in any event, some of it is lost
to the application efforts.
The Trustees conveyed that they were uncomfortable just giving up on such a
large piece of the construction costs, and Manager Williams suggested that they
leave the financial discussion for a later time and focus on the three parking
options that Mr. Saunders had prepared.
Managers Williams referred the Council to the Walker Consultants cost survey of
parking structures in the agenda report, drawing their attention to the fact
that construction costs have gone up 20% in just the last two years. He
continued that the parking option discussed at the last Study Session could
cost $8-$9 million, and if CMAQ funding is taken out of the picture, even with
special service area funding, it is a big bite out of Village reserves for just
one project. He said that he had asked Mr. Saunders to come up with ways to
bring the cost down while still building a structure at Lincoln Avenue.
Mr. Saunders distributed photos of the different above-ground options he had
worked out. As he discussed the designs with the Council, he stressed that
because the construction costs are rising at a steep rate, he tried to put a
cushion into his cost projections to account for that increase, and added that
no figures for landscaping were included.
The discussion centered around the pros and cons of the two new designs, and it
was agreed that one option was visually overbearing and the other brought the
entrance ramp too close to the Community House playground. A consideration was
brought to light about how the new options don’t take advantage of the
underground potential, since the underground portion would have to be
constructed first and possible levels added later as funds became available,
but that once an above-ground structure was in place, there is no getting that
lower level back.
In response to a question about moving the structure to the west into the
railroad cut, Mr. Saunders replied that there would be a new round of
negotiations and money involved, since the railroad owns everything about
halfway up the cut between Elm and Pine Streets.
Manager Williams noted that staff has done their best to cover all the options,
and that from a financial point of view the Village has the capacity to handle
a $4-$6 million project, but that to take on a $9 million project would impact
other important upcoming projects. He told the Council to keep in mind that
doing nothing is always an option for the time being.
The Council’s discussion then turned to the option of using a special service
area to help fund the project, as was done in Hubbard Woods. The trustees felt
that a new parking structure might benefit both Elm Street business districts
because it would move commuters out of West Elm, and noted that the Post Office
redevelopment will also benefit the West Elm district as well. They agreed that
employees and owners of the business establishments need to be persuaded to
park their cars in the Lincoln Avenue facility, so they become part of the
parking solution in efforts to keep the business districts healthy.
Mr. Saunders then showed the Council two draft project schedules, one with a
CMAQ application and one without. There was a brief, additional discussion
about applying for grants from both CMAQ and Metra, and of potential for a
special service area. No final decisions were made.
6)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
7)   Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 11:20 p.m.
June 20, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 6, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
20, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Acting Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and one person in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   July 6, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Eilers, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   July 11, 2006, Study Session. Trustees Eilers, Tucker, and Behles
indicated that they would not be able to attend. All of the Trustees indicated
that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
None
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-12-2006 – Disposal of Surplus Village Property – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed the ordinance authorizing the disposal of surplus
Village property by a variety of methods, including internet auction. She
explained that the ordinance provides blanket authority for the disposition of
Village property that becomes surplus property during the remainder of the
current fiscal year, excepting surplus vehicles to be sold at Northwest
Municipal Conference auctions. She noted that the ordinance includes detailed
authorizations and conditions for disposal. Following the passage of this
Ordinance, the authorization of the disposal of surplus property will be put on
an annual schedule, so that a single, annual authorization can be presented
each year in conjunction with other fiscal year action items.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Resolution No. R-17-2006 – Release of Executive Session Minutes –
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that this resolution would authorize the
release of Executive Session minutes for which confidentiality is no longer
required and would permit the destruction of audio tape recordings of closed
meetings held from June 1, 2004 through December 14, 2004. She pointed out that
the Resolution specifically states that the Council is not authorizing or
consenting to the public disclosure of the verbatim recordings of Executive
Session meetings
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt the resolution as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Road, addressed the Council’s Study Session discussions
about building a parking garage in the east Elm Street area. He urged the
Trustees to examine the matter carefully in light of prior surveys and other
studies, some of which he believes have reached conclusions that differ from
those in the Rich & Associates report. He stated that he did not believe moving
commuters to the Lincoln Avenue lot would be a good idea because they will not
want to walk from there to the Elm Street station and the Village does not want
to do anything that might discourage use of the train stations which are
valuable assets to the community. He also questioned the wisdom of investing
$7-9 million in the east Elm Street area. He pointed out that the area has not
changed in size or character since the studies for the 1999 Comprehensive Plan
and is a relatively small business area. Mr. Kates suggested more directional
signs, more parking around the Village Green, zoned parking, and stepped- up
enforcement to mitigate some of the parking problems.
President Woodbury said that the Council has not committed to do anything
except to continue studying the issue. He noted that the Rich & Associates
study was performed in connection with the proposed development of the Post
Office site, and there is still plenty of time for things to evolve. He noted
that there are a number of major projects competing for the Village’s funds to
be designated toward enhancing the downtown tax base, and it is a question of
priorities.
8. Old Business.
a)   555-561 Chestnut Street – Request for Special Use Permit. Attorney Janega
reviewed the draft Findings and Conclusions pertaining to the application for a
Special Use Permit to reduce the retail space at 557 Chestnut Street, which is
in the C-2 Overlay Zoning District, so the first floor office space at 559
Chestnut Street, also in the overlay, can expand into the rear of 557 Chestnut.
She explained that the Findings were the result of the Council’s lengthy
discussion at its June 6th meeting, at which the Council unanimously concluded
that the requested change would be inconsistent with the purposes of the C-
2 Overlay District and denied the requested Special Use. She explained that
these detailed Finding are required under current Illinois case law and are
similar to the findings entered when the Council denied a bank’s application
for a special use in the Hubbard Woods C-2 overlay.
After a brief discussion resulting in minor changes (i) to describe the
proposed building changes as permanent, and (ii) to correct descriptions of on-
street parking configurations, Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved
to adopt the draft Findings and Conclusions as modified. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Eilers noted that although the detailed findings may at some time no
longer be required by State law, he believes that it is important that the
Village have such findings on file in the event of future cases of a similar
nature.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1465 and 1466. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1465 in the amount of $961,402.01 and Warrant List No. 1466 in the
amount of $400,577.76. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Turbine #7 Overhaul Technical Field Engineer. Mr. Ciesla reviewed a
request for the services of a Technical Field Engineer to assist in the
overhaul of Turbine #7. The work performed will be similar to the work
performed earlier this year on Turbine #4. He recommended that Siemens Demag
Delaval Turbomachinery, Inc., be awarded the purchase order in the amount of
$70,000.00 for said services.
Trustee Abell suggested that the purchase order be increased to $100,000 to
cover possible additional time and material costs incurred because of
conditions that cannot be determined until the turbine is actually dismantled,
in light of the Village’s prior favorable experience and the expertise of
Siemens, and moved to authorize the purchase order in that amount. Trustee
Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Replacement Vehicle for Truck #54. Mr. Ciesla reviewed a request to
purchase a line truck to replace an existing truck that has outlived its
usefulness and is exhibiting significant deterioration. He added that the boom
for lifting equipment on the existing truck cannot meet the weight requirements
of the transformers now being installed in the Village. Manager Williams added
that this presents some safety concerns for the Village and pointed out that
two low bidders did not meet specifications. He recommended that the bid be
awarded to Dueco, Inc., for $185,320.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Squad Car Video Recording System Replacement. Chief DeLopez reviewed a
request to purchase a replacement squad car video recording system. He
explained that the current VHS system requires a cumbersome manual tape
management system, and some reliability and repair issues associated with the
early technology have recently occurred. He recommended that the purchase be
awarded to Digital In-Car Video System from L3 Communications/Mobile Vision,
Inc., in the amount of $45,342. Lt. Frank Siwak added that, although L3
Communications/Mobile Vision, Inc. was not the lowest bidder, it was the only
one that met all three of the Department’s most critical system criteria.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
None.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Rich Chesley to the Plan
Commission to replace Luvie Myers upon the expiration of her term. Trustee
Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to so appoint Mr. Chesley. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Peggy Swartchild to the
Business Community Development Commission to replace Barbara Aquilino,
effective immediately. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to so
appoint Ms. Swartchild. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, Board appointments, and personnel
matters, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(1), respectively, of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:16 p.m.
