July 6, 2006
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 18, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday, July
6, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee Tom Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development, Michael
D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, and approximately eight
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   July 11, 2006, Study Session. President Woodbury announced that this
meeting has been cancelled.
b)   July 18, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 6, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell noted a spelling correction
before moving to approve the minutes. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   June 13, 2006, Study Session. Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell,
moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   June 20, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-13-2006 – Zoning Variations: 445 Sheridan Rd. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Permitted Uses,
Nonconformities, and Building Height variations to permit the expansion and
remodeling of an existing coach house into a second principal structure to be
used as separate living quarters. He explained that the proposed project
includes the conversion of the first floor to living space with a 2-car garage
and a porch addition, as well as the remodeling of the second floor, including
the addition of two dormers and a terrace above the new porch. All of the
proposed changes are considered to be an expansion of a nonconforming use
because only one single-family detached dwelling per zoning lot is permitted.
In addition, the construction of the dormers requires a height variation
because the maximum allowable height of an accessory structure is 15 feet. He
pointed out that the requested variations are similar in nature to those
previously granted for 644 Pine Lane and reported that the Zoning Board had
voted 6-0 to recommend their approval.
In response to a question from Trustee Tucker about the definition of a coach
house, President Woodbury commented that coach houses are a permitted use so
long as they continue to be used for “domestic help.”
David Grossberg, the applicants’ attorney, clarified that the applicants intend
to use the renovated coach house as quarters for visiting relatives. He
remarked that the applicants are taking a great old house and bringing it
forward in time.
President Woodbury encouraged the applicants to consider seeking landmark
status for the house.
Trustee Berger confirmed with Mr. Grossberg that the applicants have no
intention of subdividing the property. Mr. Grossberg so confirmed, saying that
to do so would result in the main dwelling becoming nonconforming.
Trustee Behles inquired as to whether the coach house could turn into rental
property. Attorney Janega indicated that it would have had to continue to be
rental property, which it has not. Therefore, its rental use has expired.
President Woodbury thanked the applicants for their efforts to renovate this
older home.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance as
drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-14-2006 – Zoning Variations: 902 Spruce Street -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot and
Front Setback variations to permit reconstruction of a front entry and deck. He
explained that in addition to constructing a new deck and front entry, the
petitioner is proposing a number of hardscape improvements, including new brick
paver sidewalks. Mr. D’Onofrio mentioned that one of the unique characteristics
of this lot is its very small size. He reported that the Zoning Board had voted
6-0 to recommend approval of the variations.
Denise Shuty, the applicant’s landscape architect, reviewed the improvements
and noted that the applicant hopes to achieve relief from water damage to
buildings and upgrade the home’s outdoor amenities to increase its curb appeal
and overall market value.
Trustee Tucker inquired whether there had been any concerns raised by
neighbors. The applicant indicated that there had not.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance as
drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-15-2006 – Special Use Permit & Zoning Variations: 874
Green Bay Rd. - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Special
Use Permit and Building Height and Rear Yard Setback variations to permit the
installation of a wireless telecommunication service facility on the existing
three-story commercial building located in the C-2 Zoning District. The
applicant, T-Mobile, also seeks permission to place wireless antennas on the
walls of the existing penthouse and main building that will exceed the maximum
permitted height. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the Village Code identifies and
prioritizes permitted locations for the installation of wireless facilities,
with the C-1 and C-2 Commercial Zoning Districts falling last in that list of
priorities. He noted that the Village’s consultant, who was hired to assist in
determining the merits of such applications, has concluded that the proposed
site is, in fact, the highest priority site able to cover the applicant’s
existing coverage gaps. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that both the Plan Commission
and the Zoning Board had voted in favor of recommending approval of the Special
Use Permit and the Zoning Board had voted 6-0 to recommend approval of the
variations.
The applicant’s representative, Ray Shinkle, passed around photo simulations
and exhibited radio frequency maps showing existing and proposed coverage and
responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Behles asked whether after the proposed installation, the applicant
would continue to look for additional sites. Mr. Shinkle indicated that they
would.
Trustee Tucker expressed the community’s concern about proliferation.
Attorney Janega remarked that virtually all local control of telecommunications
has been preempted by federal regulations and that the only tool available to
individual municipalities is land use regulation.
Trustee Behles commented that it is very important that the Village treat
applicants for such facilities by the same standards.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance
as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2006 – Amending the Flood Hazard Area Regulations
Pertaining to the Use of Retaining Walls – Introduction. Mr. Saunders briefly
summarized the history of the issue. He then commented on the draft ordinance
addressing several issues that have arisen when basements are constructed on
certain lots located in the floodplain. He explained that experience shows that
in some cases, new homes with basements that have been constructed in
compliance with the Village’s most recent regulations have relied on retaining
walls or steep slopes to achieve the finished building pad elevation required
by floodplain development regulations. As a result, these homes do not always
fit well into existing neighborhoods with respect to appearance or the function
of the transition zonings in front and side yards that are needed to provide
appropriate drainage patterns for the existing new structures. This is
particularly true on narrower lots and those lots that are significantly below
the base flood elevation. He said that the proposed ordinance addresses these
issues by (1) placing a lower limit on the ground elevation where basement
construction would be permitted, varying with the width of the lot; (2)
limiting the height of retaining walls to 24” total height; and (3) limiting
slopes used to achieve grade changes made necessary by the elevation of the
building pad to 6 to 1. Mr. Saunders reported that based upon the best
information available to the Village at this time, the approximate upper limit
on the number of lots affected by this ordinance would be 176. These would be
lots that are mapped in the floodplain with an area less than 11,200 square
feet. He pointed out that not all of these lots would be prohibited from
building a basement, because the ordinance requirements are based not only on
size and location, but also on elevation, precise determination of which is not
available without detailed survey information.
Trustees Behles and Tucker indicated that they believe this to be a positive
step. Trustee Tucker added that she would like to see the Village consider not
only a height limitation on retaining walls in the floodplain, but across all
zoning districts. She also expressed some concern about maintaining these
grading and drainage controls over time.
President Woodbury suggested that staff give some thought as to how the Village
can make these permanent over time.
Manager Williams remarked that historically when the Council adjusts its land
use laws, their impact is evaluated over a period time and then reported back
to the Council. It was agreed that because this is an evolutionary process and
involves a lengthy FEMA permitting process, a 2-3 year period would be
appropriate for such a review.
Attorney Janega called the Trustees’ attention to a few provisions in the draft
ordinance.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the ordinance as
drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1467 and 1468. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1467 in the amount of $605,563.95 and Warrant List No. 1468 in the
amount of $226,777.20. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
b)   Bid No. 06-018: 2006 Resurfacing Various Streets. Mr. Saunders reviewed
the bids for the 2006 street resurfacing project. He recommended that the bid
be awarded to Peter Baker & Son Company in the amount of $176,895.80.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Eilers.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustee Behles reported that there would be a meeting with Metra this week
regarding the proposed renovation of the Elm Street Train station.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Richard Glenn to the Police
Pension Board. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to so appoint
Mr. Glenn. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Abell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, Board appointments, and personnel
matters, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(1), respectively, of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers. The Council
convened into Executive Session at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Tucker, Ritchell, and Berger. Absent: Trustee
Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers. The meeting
adjourned at 9:46 p.m.
