Recording Secretary
August 1, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 5, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 1, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Tom Eilers, and Jessica Tucker, Absent: Trustees Sandra Berger,
David Abell and Herb Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director
of Public Works Steve Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee and approximately six
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   August 8, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend. President Woodbury said
that he would not be available.
b)   August 15, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Behles, indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 18, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles moved to approve the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Berger arrived at 7:40 p.m.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-16-2006 – Zoning Variations: 73 Locust St. - Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for front yard and rear yard setback
variations to permit the construction of a new home on a lot located in the R-
1 zoning district. He pointed out that the subject lot is particularly small
for that district and the lot depth poses a problem. He noted that the proposed
home would meet all required zoning regulations with the exception of the front
and rear yard setback requirements. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the petitioners
applied for front and rear setback variations in 2005, but the Zoning Board of
Appeals voted to recommend denial, so the petitioners withdrew their
application in order to revise their building plans. Subsequently, these
revised plans were reviewed by the Zoning Board of Appeals, which then voted to
recommend 5 to 1 to approve the requested variations.
Trustee Tucker thanked the applicants for their efforts to revise their plans
but expressed concern about setting a precedent by granting a variation for new
construction should other applicants with a unique lot configuration ask for
the same consideration.
Attorney Janega remarked that there really is no conforming alternative
available on this lot and said that she feels there is enough evidence in the
record to permit the Council to grant this variation without setting a
dangerous precedent. She noted that the revised plans maintain the side yard
setbacks required in the zoning district and bring the front building lines of
adjacent properties into line.
The applicant, David Dunkin, spoke in support of his request. In response to a
question from Trustee Berger about the original plans to renovate the house, he
explained that the house is very old, with a brick foundation, and after
further study it became clear that structural problems make renovation an
unviable solution.
Trustee Berger expressed support for the plans, saying that the applicants have
checked out all of the alternatives and presented a reasonable solution, given
that the property does not belong in the R-1 zoning district.
Trustee Behles concurred, saying that the required large front setback imposes
a hardship on the house and that the applicant has demonstrated that he has
tried to comply with zoning requirements.
Trustee Eilers agreed. He said that the circumstances in this case are
compelling and unique.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury skipped to the Public Comment portion of the Agenda in order
to accommodate a resident waiting in the audience. (See below.) Thereafter, the
Council returned to the original order of the Agenda.
b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2006 – Zoning Variations: 1258 Scott St. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for three zoning variations to
permit the construction of a new roof structure over the entire building which
would allow for the expansion of the second floor. As proposed, the project
would result in a net decrease in the roofed lot coverage and a net increase in
GFA and no change in the sum of the side yard setbacks. He noted that the
existing structure is legal nonconforming with respect to all three
requirements. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that after breaking the proposal into two
parts, the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6 to 0 to recommend approval of
the roofed lot coverage and sum of side yard setback variations and 4 to 2 to
recommend in favor of the GFA variation.
Trustee Behles remarked that he could not find where the applicant had
demonstrated a particular hardship in this situation. The side yard issue goes
with the land, but the house is already over the bulk limit.
Trustee Tucker agreed.
Attorney Janega pointed out that the draft ordinance does not include a finding
on reasonable return or particular hardship.
Trustee Berger commented that the reasonable return requirement seems to be
mitigated by the fact that the house just sold in 2006, proving that
improvements are not needed in order to realize a reasonable return. She said
that it is critical that the GFA issue be addressed. The current house was
built in 1978 and current lifestyles are not significantly different than they
were then.
Since neither the applicanst nor their representative was present, President
Woodbury suggested that the matter be tabled.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to table the issue. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-18-2006 – Zoning Variations: Hubbard Woods School, 1110
Chatfield Rd. - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for zoning
variations to permit the construction of an Outdoor Environmental Learning Area
at Hubbard Woods School as a part of improvements to an outdoor play area. He
reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6 to 0 to recommend
approval of the variations.
Scott Freres, the project’s landscape architect, provided a quick overview and
responded to questions.
Trustee Tucker commended the applicants for putting the plan together and said
that the school community is excited about its potential.
Trustee Eilers commented on the need to address and serve the ever-increasing
school population.
Trustee Behles noted that it is quite common for institutional uses to have to
go through this process because they do not readily conform to the residential
zoning standards for the districts in which they are located. He expressed his
support for this project saying that it is very appropriate and noting that
there is an actual reduction in the coverages.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution No. R-19-2006 – Supplemental Agreement with Union Pacific
Railroad for Eldorado Street Bridge Replacement – Adoption. Mr. Saunders
explained that the Village has been working for several years on a
reconstruction project for the Pine Street and Eldorado Street bridges over the
Union Pacific Railroad. The final issue to be settled is an Agreement with
Union Pacific for the air rights for the additional two feet of width required
for the reconstructed bridge. The details of the conditions placed by the
Railroad for the Village to proceed with the project are enumerated in the
Agreement covered by the subject Resolution. Mr. Saunders summarized the major
conditions and noted that the additional, unanticipated cost to the Village to
proceed with this project that is currently scheduled to begin construction
early next spring, as well as the Pine Street bridge project, is $44,826.
Manager Williams pointed out that if the Village doesn’t approve the Agreement,
the Railroad will not proceed.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-19-2006
approving the Agreement with Union Pacific Railroad. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Berger, Behles, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Ritchell.
e)   Winngate Condominiums: Approval of Final Landscape Plan and Adoption of
Resolution No. R-20-2006 Approving the Development Agreement: – Mr. Norkus
explained that on February 7, 2006, the Council approved a Plat of
Consolidation for 710-724 Green Bay Rd. and granted a Certificate of
Appropriateness for a twelve (12) unit development, subject to obtaining the
Village Council’s approval of a final landscape plan. He explained that the
Design Guidelines discourage parking areas in front of buildings and require
landscaped screening if it is used. He commented that at the February 7th
meeting, the Village Council had indicated that appropriate landscaping was
critical to this project and asked that certain changes be incorporated into
the originally submitted landscaping plan, including better screening along the
back property line and increased quantity and variety of plant materials along
Green Bay Road.
President Woodbury acknowledged that the proposed development is at odds with
the Design Guidelines because of the proposed parking in front, and added that
when the economics and realities of a site demand such placement, as in this
case, the site must be heavily screened. Therefore, in lieu of the concession
being given, a revised landscaping plan was requested.
Mr. Norkus stated that he had requested revised landscape plans on numerous
occasions since the February 7th meeting, and reviewed the only revision staff
had received, noting that most of the changes on the revised plan, dated April
5, 2006, had been made along the back property line to satisfy the rear
neighbors’ concerns about satisfactory screening, but that little substantial
change had been made to the front of the property.
Trustee Behles remarked that, given the concept of a paved turnaround in front,
the developer only has what is left of the property with which to develop the
landscaped area. He said it appears that some of the improvements that were
requested were not included.
John Myefski, the project’s architect, reviewed the developer’s process and
gave the Council and Village staff a new plan, prepared the afternoon of August
1, 2006, by Jacobs/Ryan Associates Landscape Architects. He said that overall
the new changes are very similar to the prior plan, but that some species have
been changed and some trees and plantings in the front of the building have
been modified. He added that the goal is to screen the cars, create buffer, and
soften the area.
Trustee Berger expressed concern about pedestrian safety with the turnarounds
being immediately adjacent to the sidewalks. Mr. Myefski indicated that the
plan provides for appropriate sightlines, which Mr. Saunders later confirmed.
He also stated that there are numerous places in the Village where sidewalks go
over driveways and there are ways in which the two areas can be visually
delineated.
Trustee Behles said that he believes the applicant is doing what it can to
effectively screen the cars from the street, although it will take a while for
the plantings to grow in place.
After a lengthy discussion, the Trustees decided to consider the Development
Agreement before taking action on the newly revised landscape plan.
Mr. Norkus then reviewed the Development Agreement, noting that it covers the
Developer’s request to increase the number of dwelling units from twelve to
thirteen. He pointed out that this is being accomplished without any increase
in the size of the building or any changes to its appearance and noted that the
building will be under the density requirements for the property and that the
parking remains exactly as originally proposed.
Attorney Janega addressed specific provisions in the Development Agreement,
highlighting modifications made to the standard agreement’s provisions on
improvements and fence requirements in response to points raised by Trustees
Ritchell and Abell during the discussion of the Westmoor Commons development at
the last meeting.
Mr. Saunders then reviewed the provisions of the Mobilization Plan covering how
the site will be protected and governed during construction.
After further discussion as to how to address the new revisions to the August
1, 2006, revised landscape plan, the Council determined that it should be
considered a conceptual plan. The Council also determined that, since the
revised plan had not been previously submitted to Village staff for review,
approval of a final landscape plan should be left to the Village staff. At the
suggestion of Attorney Janega, the Council confirmed that the final landscape
plan should be consistent with the August 1, 2006, plan.
Trustee Tucker moved to approve the conceptual landscape plan prepared by
Jacobs/Ryan Associates Landscape Architects dated August 1, 2006, subject to
Village staff's review and approval of a final landscape plan consistent with
the August 1, 2006 conceptual plan. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Ritchell.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the Development
Agreement between the Village and Winngate Development LLC, including the
proposed increase to thirteen (13) dwelling units, in a form substantially in
the form of the draft presented in the agenda materials. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Tucker, and Eilers.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Ritchell.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
Melba Gold, 1250 Willow Rd., expressed concern about the condition of an
overgrown alleyway behind her property belonging to the Village. She asked for
the Village to help keep the area clean and clear of weeds.
Mr. Saunders explained that the property is an undeveloped but platted alley.
He indicated that the property would be put on the Village’s regular
maintenance list on a going-forward basis.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1471 and 1472. In Trustee Ritchell’s absence, Trustee
Tucker reviewed the Warrant Lists. After commenting on a number of the items,
she moved to approve Warrant List No. 1471 in the amount of $936,032.60 and
Warrant List No. 1472 in the amount of $245,648.94. Trustee Eilers seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Ritchell.
b)   Bid No. 06-019: Truck Chassis and Body Replacement (PW-13). Mr. Saunders
reviewed the bids for the purchase of a new chassis and body to replace an
existing dump/plow truck in poor condition. He recommended the purchase of new
equipment meeting the Village’s specifications from Northwest Truck and Lindco
Equipment in the amount of $149,456.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Tucker, Eilers, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Ritchell.
c)   Capital Budget Status Report. Manager Williams explained that this report
has been reformatted to provide a better sense of the status of each major
capital project of $50,000 or greater. Mr. McKee enumerated some of the
changes, highlighting some specific projects. He emphasized that the document
is a “living” document and not static.
The Trustees thanked staff for their efforts and asked that a column be added
to the new format to show projects and amounts already authorized.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the Village would be meeting this
coming Friday with representatives from Union Pacific regarding the
rehabilitation of the Elm Street station.
b)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Electric Plant hit a new all-
time peak of 40,700 kw, and the system is proving to be stout and stable. He
then asked Mr. Saunders to provide an update on the Emerald Ash Borer and a
recent meeting on the subject sponsored by Senator Durbin.
Mr. Saunders confirmed that infested trees had been discovered in northwest
Evanston and that the Illinois Department of Agriculture will have inspectors
in parts of Winnetka for the next few days looking at ash trees on public
property. Thus far no Winnetka afflicted trees have been found. He noted that
at the moment the Illinois DOA has no concrete management plan in place but one
is being developed.
Manager Williams added that Sen. Durbin was clear that local governments
shouldn’t count on significant funding from the Federal government to combat
the problem.
Mr. Saunders reminded residents to not transport firewood from outside of the
area.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Chris Rintz to Chair the Ad Hoc
Residential Design Committee, saying that this committee was formerly known as
the Ad Hoc House Design Study Committee. Trustee Tucker moved to appoint Mr.
Rintz. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Mr. Woodbury then suggested that King Poor be appointed to the Ad Hoc
Residential Design Committee. Trustee Tucker so moved, seconded by Trustee
Berger. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Finally President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Posey Fisher to the
Landmark Preservation Commission for a full term and her appointment as Chair
of that Committee. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve
both appointments. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and Board appointments pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Abell and Ritchell. The Council convened into Executive
Session at 10:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:55 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Berger, Behles, Eilers, and Tucker. Absent: Trustees Abell
and Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles,
Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Ritchell. The
meeting adjourned at 10:56 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
August 8, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: September 5, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 8, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Eilers called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandy Berger, and Herb Ritchell. Absent: President Edmund
Woodbury and Trustees David Abell and Jessica Tucker. Also present: Village
Manager Doug Williams, Interim Superintendent of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla,
Distribution Engineer Bryan McInturff, and one person in the audience.
2)   Discussion: Hibbard Road Water Main Replacement and Pump Station
Project.  
Manager Williams reported that although the water main replacement was included
in the budget, the pump station project is a new item, and he wanted the
Council to have some time to review the project proposal before making a
decision early next month.
He noted that the Village’s water system interconnects with Northfield,
Wilmette, Kenilworth and Glencoe, but that due to pressure and elevation
factors, the Glencoe interconnect is the only one that can provide Winnetka
with any significant amount of water, approximately 3.5 million gallons per
day. He said that in the event of a catastrophic situation at the plant, or if
the plant ever needs to be shut down to replace aging infrastructure, upgrade
to new technologies, or to bring it into compliance with future Federal
regulations, the current interconnections do not have the capacity to supply
adequate water to the system.
Manager Williams indicated that the issue came to the forefront during the
Village’s last water contract negotiations with Northfield, when Northfield
insisted that an upgrade be implemented to improve the connection to Wilmette
by the year 2010, and he added that the Council approved the contract with the
condition that Northfield’s water rate will be frozen after 2010 until the work
is completed. He reported that after evaluating numerous options and running
several computer engineering analyses, staff came to the conclusion that the
current 8-inch water line under Hibbard Road between Winnetka Avenue and
Illinois Road should be replaced with a 16-inch line. He observed that the line
upgrade alone won’t substantially increase Winnetka’s interconnect capacity
with Wilmette, due to lower elevation and pressure in the Wilmette system, and
therefore a new underground pumping station is also needed to significantly
enhance the capacity of the interconnection.
Distribution Engineer Bryan McInturff then reviewed the status of the Village’s
IDOT and EPA permit applications. He indicated that the EPA has approved a
permit to upgrade the water line, but they have some questions about the pump
station, and they need to be reassured that the project is for backup and
contingency purposes only. He noted that the IDOT permit has been applied for,
and that they want a bid bond from the contractor before they will issue a
permit.
Manager Williams asked Mr. McInturff to give an overview of some of the
challenges that have been faced with regard to choosing a path down Hibbard
Road.
Mr. McInturff explained that because of the positioning of storm sewers in the
utility rights-of-way, the Village had no option but to get permission from
IDOT to lay the pipe down the middle of the roadway, acknowledging that the
middle of Hibbard Road is asphalt over an 8-inch concrete base, which is
expensive and complicated to repair.
In response to a question about alternate plans or routes, Manager Williams
replied that other options were studied, but that there was no better alternate
route.
Mr. McInturff noted that the Wilmette water line is in the middle of the street
at the intersection of Hibbard and Illinois, where it dives down under a storm
sewer on the east side of Hibbard, which precludes putting the pump station in
the utility right-of-way. He continued that the only corner at that
intersection which is not residential is the northeast corner, occupied by
Winnetka Covenant Church, and that the Village has been negotiating with the
church for an easement on their property, with a cost appraised at $35,000 for
the easement and $15,000 for relocating their sign and landscaping.
Manager Williams added that the Village of Wilmette is very supportive of the
project, and that the interconnection will work both ways if Wilmette ever
needs water from Winnetka.
The Trustees questioned Mr. McInturff and Manager Williams about various
aspects of the project, and the water rate contract with Northfield. During a
discussion about some of the items on the bid tabulation, Manager Williams
interjected that the water fund started the year with approximately $2.5
million due to the cash generated by last summer’s drought, so that this
project won’t impact other planned projects. He called attention to page 5 of
the agenda report, which states that it is the opinion of staff that there is
no other water project that can improve the reliability of Winnetka’s water
system by as much as this project can.
Mr. McInturff indicated that he thought the pricing was reasonable given the
sophisticated nature of the pump station and the high quality materials being
used. Manager Williams commended staff for their hard work investigating the
options and coming up with a plan, saying that other villages would have had to
hire a consultant to do the work for them.
The Trustees and President Pro Tem Eilers agreed that the project is one of
high priority and that the preparation work has been very thorough.
3)   Discussion: Transformer Purchases.
Acting Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla reported that the Village has
already used almost $55,000 of the $57,000 budgeted for new transformers this
year. He observed that since the budget was approved, four new homes requiring
three-phase service have been initiated, along with six new homes requiring 400
amp services, noting that the three-phase customers pay for the transformer and
400 amp customers pay a flat rate for service. He added that of the three
transformers that need replacing, one was damaged by a contractor and the
Village will be reimbursed for its cost, and the other two will not be
recovered, as one was damaged by a hit and run accident on Sheridan Road and
the other serves the Elm Street Metra Station and is in a state of disrepair.
Mr. Ciesla determined that the Electric Department will need to order about 8
more transformers just to take care of its supply, which usually amounts to
approximately 20 per year. He announced that most of the cost is recovered by
charging homeowners for the three-phase or the flat rate for the 400 amps, and
that transformers that are damaged in car accidents, like the one on Sheridan
Road, are paid for by the Village if the driver of the car is not found.
Manager Williams pointed out that the Village will be reimbursed eventually as
noted, and staff wanted the Council to be aware of the background when the
items come up on the next regular Council meeting agenda.
4)   Discussion: Generating Station Oil Pipe Lines.
Mr. Ciesla gave a brief description of the operation of the diesel oil tank
farm located at Tower Court, which holds the oil that is used to start the
diesel engines at the electric plant. He explained that the EPA did an
inspection last June and cited the Village for several violations, the first of
which was for piping under Tower Road bridge that had become kinked. He said
that the piping had been state-of-the-art when installed, but that the kinking
problem was a common one, and was experienced by many other users of the pipe.
Mr. Ciesla continued that the second violation came about because the
pressurized piping did not have automatic leak detection, noting that at the
time of installation in 1976, leak detection systems were not required, and the
Illinois State Fire Marshall has never brought up the requirement when
inspecting the pipes. He indicated that the third violation was for failure to
provide any release detection for piping from the tanks to the pump house, but
observed that these pipes are not under pressure, and annual testing has never
revealed any leaks.
He reported that the estimate to replace the 400 feet of kinked pipe was
$32,000; however, replacement was not currently under consideration because the
Village has no leak detection system in place, and the EPA only gave the
Village 60 days to take corrective measures, which is not enough time to put in
a leak detection system.
In response to a question about why the tanks are so far away from the plant,
Mr. McInturff replied that the Council at the time didn’t want to put oil tanks
so near to the lake due to environmental pressures.
Manager Williams observed that the diesel engines need the oil for fuel, and
that the plant also has some dual fuel engines that can use natural gas. He
recommended that some thought be given to converting the diesel engines to dual
fuel engines. He proposed that in the meantime, the plant use a tanker parked
near the facility, and to extract the oil from the tanker while a consultant is
hired to help with devising a strategy to solve the problems.
President Pro Tem Eilers then announced that Manager Williams had an additional
item for discussion that was not on the agenda.
Manager Williams reported that one of the plant’s diesel generators was in need
of repair after a rubber coupling connecting the cooling water to the engine’s
water jacket failed, causing the cylinder seals to also fail from overheating.
He said that the cost of repairs was estimated at $78,000.
He noted that the diesel engines are necessary to provide power to the pumps
for the water system and for black start conditions, which would only occur if
all ComEd delivery were lost in a blackout situation. He announced that the
Village gets credits from IMEA for having the diesel capacity, and therefore
the cost of repairs would be recouped in about ten months.
He concluded by saying that the item would be brought before the Council at its
next meeting, but that he wanted to introduce the matter at this time.
5)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 8:48 p.m.
August 15, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 5, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 15, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Trustees
Sandra Berger and Ken Behles. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael
D’Onofrio, Distribution Engineer Bryan McInturff and two persons in the
audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 6(f), Proposed Amendments to the Tree
Preservation Ordinance, had been removed from the Agenda. The Council’s
discussion of this item will take place following the Public Hearing on
September 5th. The Trustees approved the Agenda, as amended.
4. Quorum.
a)   September 5, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   September 12, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
None.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-16-2006 – Zoning Variations: 73 Locust St. - Adoption.
Saying that her concerns raised at the last meeting about setting precedent had
been satisfactorily addressed, Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Eilers,
moved to adopt the draft ordinance granting front yard and rear yard setback
variations to permit the construction of a new home on the subject property. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Berger and Behles.
b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2006 – Zoning Variations: 1258 Scott St. -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio again reviewed a request for three zoning
variations to permit the construction of a new roof structure over the entire
building which would allow for the expansion of the second floor. This project
would include the addition of a new bathroom, second floor laundry facility,
and walk-in closet, as well as the removal of an existing storage shed and bay
window. The matter was tabled at the August 1st meeting because neither the
applicants nor their representative was present.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that as proposed, the project would result in a net
decrease in the roofed lot coverage and a net increase in GFA and no change in
the sum of the side yard setbacks. He noted that the existing structure is
legal nonconforming with respect to all three elements. Mr. D’Onofrio reported
that after breaking the proposal into two parts, the Zoning Board of Appeals
had voted 6 to 0 to recommend approval of the roofed lot coverage and sum of
side yard setback variations and 4 to 2 to recommend in favor of the GFA
variation.
Remarking that he is concerned about the appearance of mass on the site,
Trustee Ritchell asked Mr. D’Onofrio to review how roof height is measured,
which he did, saying that the proposed height conforms to requirements.
Trustee Ritchell then asked about the third floor of the house. The architect,
John Robbins, explained that there is no functional third floor, nor is there
an opportunity for creating storage there.
Mr. Robbins went on to explain that the intent of the project is to stay within
the spirit of the zoning ordinance by getting rid of the shed that makes the
property seem more massive than it is and taking some of that storage space and
tucking it into the existing house, thereby creating a net visual reduction in
the appearance of bulk.
Trustee Tucker commented that she shares Trustee Ritchell’s concern about the
massive appearance of the structure because of raised roof height and increased
GFA. She said that in her opinion the house is already substantial and she has
trouble seeing the practical difficulties necessary to justify this variation
request. She asked Mr. Robbins to elaborate on the need for additional GFA and
whether any alternatives were explored.
Mr. Robbins reported that a variety of options had been considered but were
rejected because the applicants are trying to preserve the character of the
house and take advantage of a lot of work that was put into its interior a few
years ago. He indicated that no work has been put into the exterior of the home
in a long time and that at a minimum it must now be re-roofed and have new
gutters installed. Mr. Robbins explained that the proposed change to the roof
pitch is not, in his opinion, a major change and will not create an increase in
the visual mass of the building from a three-dimensional perspective.
Trustee Ritchell inquired as to why the existing roof pitch creates a problem.
Mr. Robbins responded that it creates an overall maintenance issue. He added
that the owners would also like to distinguish their home from its neighbor,
which is a mirror image.
In response to questions from the Council, Mr. D’Onofrio clarified some of the
GFA calculations. He also explained that the two members of the Zoning Board
who voted against this variation felt that the house is already large and
questioned the necessity for another bathroom and a walk-in closet.
Trustee Abell inquired whether in relation to its neighbor, the proposed new
roof would be about the same height. Mr. Robbins replied that the neighboring
house’s roof is slightly higher.
Attorney Janega asked about the ceiling height on the second floor. Mr. Robbins
said that it is eight (8) feet. She noted that because this house is
essentially a mirror of its neighbor, if the Council grants this variation, it
would be setting a precedent. President Woodbury commented that there are many
similar houses in Winnetka.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Robbins whether he saw the house as a potential tear
down if the requested improvements are not made. Mr. Robbins replied that it
had been discussed, but that the applicants are trying to make necessary
changes with minor interventions instead of starting from scratch.
Mr. Williams asked whether any comments had been received from neighbors. Mr.
D’Onofrio indicated that there have been none.
Trustee Eilers spoke in support of the requested variations. He said that since
this house was built much has changed in what the consumer demands and the
proposed design updates the existing structure to include those features
currently considered necessary. He added that the Village has been trying to
save its smaller, less expensive housing stock insofar as it can, so it is to
the Village’s advantage to preserve this home. He said that in his opinion the
requested variations are fully justified.
Trustee Abell concurred. He said that he is not troubled by the height or pitch
issues and that this is a good solution.
Trustee Ritchell pointed out that this home is not an historic property and
reiterated his concern about bulk. He expressed concern about the conclusion
that the property cannot yield a reasonable return.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce the draft
ordinance granting the requested variations. A tie resulted from the voice
vote. Ayes: Trustees Eilers and Abell. Nays: Trustees Ritchell and Tucker.
Absent: Trustees Berger and Behles. President Woodbury voted in favor of
introduction to break the tie.
c)   Ordinance No. M-18-2006 – Zoning Variations: Hubbard Woods School, 1110
Chatfield Rd. - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments included
in response to Council discussion at the last meeting regarding accessibility
issues and prior zoning relief for the property.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the draft as
proposed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the draft ordinance
granting two zoning variations to permit the construction of an outdoor
environmental learning area, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Berger and Behles.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2006 – Amending Village Code Pertaining to Appoint of
an Acting Village Manager – Introduction & Adoption. Attorney Janega explained
that the Council’s regular meeting schedule does not always allow for the
formal designation of another officer to perform the Manager’s duties in his
absence. Historically, the practice has been for the Manager to designate an
acting Manager and notify the Council before the designation is scheduled to go
into effect. However, this informal process does not clearly give the Acting
Manager all of the Manager’s powers and authority, which could leave that
individual without the ability to perform some routine actions. Therefore, the
proposed ordinance would specifically authorize the Village Manager to appoint
an acting manager with all of the powers and authority of the Village Manager
for up 30 days, with the Council appointing an acting Village Manager for
longer absences or disabilities.
Given Mr. Williams intention to be out of town on vacation beginning this
coming Friday, Trustee Tucker moved to waive introduction of this ordinance and
move directly to adoption. Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Berger and Behles.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the ordinance as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Berger and
Behles.
e)   Ordinance No. M-19-2006 – Disposal of Certain Personal Property Owned by
the Village - Introduction. Manager Williams explained that this ordinance
would cover certain specified vehicles and equipment owned by the Village that
are past their useful life and have been replaced. Attorney Janega added that
vehicles to be sold at a NWMC Auction were not covered by the earlier omnibus
surplus equipment disposal ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance
finding certain pieces of equipment to be surplus and authorizing their
disposal. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2006 – Amending Village Code Pertaining to the Tree
Preservation Ordinance – Standards for Heritage Trees, Tree Removals and
Appeals - Introduction. As announced earlier, this item was removed from the
Agenda, but President Woodbury called for any comments on the matter from
audience members.
Ramon Garcia, the Winnetka Caucus Chair, asked whether the Council wished any
input from the Caucus on this matter.
President Woodbury indicated that the Council has had significant discussion on
the matter because there are significant policy issues involved, but encouraged
the Caucus to bring any points that it wishes to pursue forward at the Public
Hearing on September 5th.
Mr. Garcia indicated that the Caucus might choose to ask a question on the
matter in its upcoming annual questionnaire.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1473 and 1474. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1473 in the amount of $767,036.04 and Warrant List No. 1474 in the
amount of $591,588.77. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Berger and Behles.
b)   Bid No. 06-023: Trenchless Lining of Sanitary Sewers. Mr. Williams
reviewed the bids for the trenchless relining of the sanitary sewers located
under Birch Street, from Willow to Hill Roads. He reported staff’s
recommendation that the bid be awarded to the low bidder, Insituform
Technologies, in the total amount of $48,987.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Berger and
Behles.
c)   Diesel Generator #9. Mr. Williams reviewed this item that was discussed at
the August 8th Study Session. He explained that the repair of the diesel
generator is critical to the operation of the Electric Plant and asked the
Council to authorize the issuance of a Purchase Order to cover materials and a
field engineer to perform the necessary work in an amount not to exceed
$80,000.
Noting the possible need to exceed the requested amount, Trustee Abell moved to
purchase materials and supply a field Engineer to repair the malfunctioning
diesel generator No. 9 at a total cost not to exceed $90,000. Trustee Eilers
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Berger and Behles.
d)   Transformer Purchases. Manager Williams noted that this item had also been
discussed at the last Study Session. He reported that staff recommended that
the Purchase Order for transformer purchases be increased by $50,000 to a total
of $107,000.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Abell, Tucker, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Berger and
Behles.
e)   Hibbard Road Water Main Replacement and Pump Station Project. Manager
Williams addressed a couple of issues that were raised when this item was
discussed at the last Study Session, noting that the matter would come up for
final action at the September 5th Council meeting. He said that completion of
this project would improve the Village’s water system to the point where it
should be able to handle virtually any contingency. Mr. Williams thanked Mr.
McInturff for his extensive engineering work on the project.
f)   Review: Post Office Committee Minutes. Mr. Williams briefly reviewed the
minutes of the Committee’s last meeting. He said that he expects the Committee
to meet with the Council later in the fall after the Village’s consultant has
reported on the viability of several of the general concepts under
consideration.
Trustee Eilers commented that ultimately the Council will have to make
decisions about trade-offs and that it is important to manage expectations. On
behalf of the Business Community Development Commission he expressed their deep
concern about surface parking.
10. Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers noted that the Business Community Development
Commission would be reporting soon to the Council on their recommendations
regarding a property maintenance code and the business overlay district.
c)   Village Attorney. Attorney Janega commented on an amendment to the Open
Meetings Act.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported on a third confirmed finding of Emerald
Ash Borers in neighboring communities.
11. Appointments.
None.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, board appointments, and possible
purchase of land pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Berger and
Behles. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:55 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent: Trustees Berger
and Behles. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Berger and Behles. The
meeting adjourned at 10:56 p.m.
