Recording Secretary
September 5, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 19, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 5, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Acting Director of Water & Electric
Rich Ciesla, Distribution Engineer Bryan McInturff and approximately 18 persons
in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4. Quorum.
a)   September 12, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Berger, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   September 19, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5. Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 1, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers moved to approve the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
b)   August 8, 2006, Study Session. Trustee Abell moved to approve the minutes
as drafted. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   August 15, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell moved to approve the
minutes as drafted. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-05-2006 – Amending the Tree Preservation Ordinance of the
Winnetka Village Code – Standards for Heritage Trees, Tree Removals and Appeals
– Public Hearing & Introduction. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing.
Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed the history of this item and explained that the
proposed draft reflects further work by the Forestry Commission and Village
staff to resolve outstanding policy issues not covered by prior amendments to
the Tree Preservation Code. Among other provisions, the draft contains proposed
amendments pertaining to providing for the designation of Heritage Trees,
establishing heightened protection standards for such trees, setting a minimum
mandatory fine for Tree Preservation Code violations, and imposing additional
sanctions for violations pertaining to Heritage Trees. It provides that
applications for removal of a Heritage Tree be heard by the Forestry Commission
instead of Village staff, and requires that notice of the request to remove a
Heritage Tree be given to neighbors before the building permit is issued. This
would allow adjacent property owners to discuss with the builder their concerns
about the possible impact such a tree removal will have with the builder with
an eye toward minimizing damage during the construction process. There are also
provisions to ensure the survivability of trees after construction. Mr.
Saunders pointed out that only the applicant could appeal the denial of any
tree removal permit, whether issued by Village Staff or the Forestry
Commission.
Referring to the provision requiring evidence that neighbors actually received
the notice about the removal of a Heritage Tree, Trustee Eilers asked whether
there is a similar requirement in any other section of the Village Code.
Attorney Janega replied that there is not. She explained that there are similar
requirements in other communities’ tree ordinances and added that there was a
consensus with the Commission that it seems to be warranted because of the
magnitude of the request and the need/desire to involve neighbors in the
process. In addition, it provides reassurance to the Forestry Commission that
notice has been given.
Trustee Eilers expressed concern about this provision because there are numbers
of residents who are gone for long periods of time.
Trustee Tucker commented that some properties are not occupied by the owner,
and Trustee Ritchell said that the language needs to be clear about what
constitutes service of notice.
Attorney Janega indicated that she would look into the service of process
rules.
Manager Williams commented that from staff’s perspective, the notice to the
neighbors is an important component because there can be significant impact on
the neighbors if a big tree is taken down on a 50’ lot. On the other hand, in
and of itself, the giving of notice and the public hearing process may create
false expectations. The intent is to shine a spotlight on what is happening to
see if there are alternatives that might save the tree.
Trustee Eilers also asked about the possibility of specifying the desirable
species of trees that are candidates for Heritage Trees instead of listing just
those that are undesirable. Mr. Saunders said that because there are so many
more desirable species than undesirable species that it was easier to list the
undesirable ones. Attorney Janega agreed, saying that this method leaves a
clearer picture for the general public and staff and that it is easier to
define by exclusion than inclusion.
Jim Gordon, 281 White Oak Lane, inquired as to how this process began and asked
for an explanation of the problem that is being solved by these amendments.
Mr. Saunders explained that the process started with the Forestry Commission a
little over three years ago because of concerns about the number of trees being
removed to accommodate new construction. This prompted the Forestry Commission
to review the existing language of the Tree Preservation Code, to which there
have been few changes since its adoption, as well as to examine how other
communities are handling the issue. Their intent was to revisit the ordinance
to make sure that the Village has in place the tools that it needs to protect
its natural resources, which are a part of its community character, and to
ensure that the ordinance reflects the current state of the industry.
Mr. Gordon asked how many Heritage Trees have been lost because they have not
been protected.
Mr. Saunders responded that in calendar 2004, approximately 60 trees that would
have qualified for heritage status were removed for construction projects, or
about 5 per month.
Mr. Gordon asked whether the draft provisions would have blocked any or all of
these sixty trees from being removed. Mr. Saunders replied that the Village
would have had more vigorous tools in place to protect them and would have
generated more conversation about the trees in question that might have caused
permit applicants to make different choices about removing a tree.
Mary Hill, Chairman of the Forestry Commission, voiced support for the proposed
draft, saying that committee members and staff have been working long and hard
to craft an ordinance that will help to protect the Village’s green heritage.
Todd Egan, 892 Oak Street, expressed opposition to the proposed amendments and
the Tree Preservation ordinance in its entirety. He said he doesn’t believe
that government should be telling individual property owners what they can and
cannot do on their property. He voiced concern about setting a precedent
whereby government usurps individual property rights. Mr. Egan stated his
belief that property owners preserve their trees through enlightened self-
interest; trees enhance the value of a lot; therefore, property owners do not
tear down trees unless necessary.
Matthew Derrick, 1096 Oak St., voiced the opinion that the drafted ordinance is
very arbitrary and rigid and leaves little room for flexibility. He opined that
developers will really not care and simply add the cost of any fine incurred to
the asking price, while it will be the homeowners who are really hurt. Mr.
Derrick urged the Council to be more flexible in deciding which trees may be
removed.
Bernie Hammer, 1455 Tower, a member of the Forestry Commission, pointed out
that there is regulation in almost all aspects of life for the protection of
the community. He said that the Commission worked very hard to come up with a
draft ordinance that will protect the rights of all parties while giving each
party the right to be heard.
Ron White, 434 Berkeley, suggested that an escape clause be included to protect
homeowners desiring to remove a Heritage Trees because it is in some way
damaging property.
Janet Poor, 595 Cedar, voiced support for the proposed ordinance.
Jim Gordon reiterated his concerns. He pointed out that in a recent Caucus
questionnaire a majority of respondents had indicated that they were satisfied
with the existing degree of tree regulation in the Village. He remarked that
the ordinance appears to be directly aimed at impeding new construction and
noted that no distinction is made between smaller lots and larger lots.
President Woodbury commented that the Council is frequently faced with inherent
conflicts and commended staff on their efforts to represent all interests. He
acknowledged the conflicting messages received from the public via the Caucus
questionnaire and the direction taken by the Forestry Commission and noted that
the problem is one of balancing private vs. public property rights as the
Council continues to struggle with land use issues. He said that the proposals
put forth by the Forestry Commission would elevate trees to the same level as
zoning.
Gertrude Niehoff, 557 Orchard Ln., urged the Council to take a long-term
perspective and, before adopting this ordinance, to consider first how it is
going to manage its trees over time within the context of change. She also
mentioned the need to provide for the removal of trees that present a safety
concern.
There being no further comments, President Woodbury closed the Public Hearing.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments and responded to questions from
the Trustees. Commenting on the Heritage Tree concept, she explained that it is
derived from the concept that when a tree reaches a particular size, it becomes
an asset to the community as well as to its owner. She pointed out that there
is no flat prohibition in the ordinance against removing any protected tree,
but a clear procedure for doing so is established. She also noted that any
denial of a tree removal application, whether by staff or the Forestry
Commission, can be appealed to the Village Council.
After a lengthy discussion of individual provisions, President Woodbury called
for comments from the Trustees.
Trustee Behles expressed some hesitation about transferring decision-making
authority to the Forestry Commission, saying that the way the ordinance has
been enforced in the past, with staff making the decisions, has worked very
well. He also mentioned that requiring every Heritage Tree removal application
to be heard in a public hearing by the Forestry Commission requires the
applicant to go through another public process and heightens expectations that
neighbors can effect change.
Trustees Berger and Tucker voiced concern about there being no distinction made
between lot sizes. Trustee Berger noted that the removal of a Heritage Tree on
an R-1 lot has an entirely different impact on the neighbors than the removal
of the same tree on an R-5 lot. She noted that the major problems seem to be
caused by new construction and suggested setting aside a separate section to
deal with that.
Trustee Eilers indicated that he believes that the Council needs to do
something, but said that, given the disparity of opinions in the community and
the overall ramifications of the policy decisions under consideration, the
Council should devote further study to the matter.
Trustee Abell said that although the Council needs to be hesitant about adding
regulations, he believes this is justified and would vote in favor of it.
Trustee Tucker agreed that there needs to be additional discussion and
clarification of language. She noted that a lot of questions have been raised
that need resolution, particularly the issues of flexibility, differentiation
by lot size, and focus on new construction rather than existing homes.
Trustee Ritchell concurred, saying that these are substantive and significant
changes that need to continue to be thoroughly explored, fleshed out, and
discussed.
Based on the comments, President Woodbury asked that staff come back to the
Trustees with revised suggestions at the next Study Session. He thanked all of
the participants for their attendance and their remarks.
Mr. Saunders suggested that the matter be slated for the November Study Session
in order to give staff time to meet again with the Forestry Commission.
b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2006 – Zoning Variations: 1258 Scott St. - Adoption.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that this is the third presentation of an ordinance
that would grant three zoning variations to permit the construction of a new
roof structure over the entire building which would allow for the expansion of
the second floor.
As requested by the Trustees at the last meeting, John Robbins, the applicants’
architect, presented a photo montage showing the existing and proposed
structures in relation to the neighbors along the street frontage. He pointed
out that some of the homes along the street appear to be higher than what is
proposed while others are lower.
Trustee Berger commented that height is not the issue; the question is whether
the overbuilt roof looks more massive than what is there now.
President Woodbury agreed, saying that the subject is the requested zoning
variations increasing gross floor area.
Trustee Behles noted that the ordinance mentions that the existing low-pitched
roof does not drain well and asked Mr. Robbins whether that is, in fact, a
problem. Mr. Robbins indicated that it is.
Trustee Berger inquired what could have been done differently to not increase
the GFA. Mr. Robbins responded that the applicant could have decided not to
increase the roof pitch, but said that in addition to the GFA considerations
there was an aesthetic call and a practical call in terms of increasing the
curb appeal and improving the overall maintainability of the house,
respectively, involved in the decision. He also mentioned that the house is
challenged by the existing ordinance because the front porch is included in the
GFA calculations because it is walled on three sides and covered.
Trustee Berger asked how the proposed increased roof pitch impacts the GFA. Mr.
D’Onofrio noted that it increases the GFA by approximately 250 sq. ft., but
pointed out that the attic bonus in the GFA calculations is intended to
encourage higher pitched roofs.
Trustee Abell remarked that this appears to be an unintended consequence. He
brought up the concern about setting a precedent raised at the last meeting.
Attorney Janega noted that the applicant has represented that the subject house
is a mirror image of its neighbor. That being the case, the neighbor could
request a similar variation and expect the same consideration, as could any
number of other homeowners in the Village who have homes with similar roof
pitches.
Trustee Tucker said that she cannot see the practical difficulty here to
distinguish it from so many other homes in the community to sufficiently
justify permitting it to further exceed the maximum allowable GFA.
Trustee Behles pointed out that the Village would not permit a new home of the
requested size to be built on this lot.
Manager Williams suggested that the Council consider the value in
rehabilitating and renovating the home with this relatively small variation as
opposed to tearing it down. He pointed out that the applicant has not proposed
demolishing the existing house.
Noting that preservation is a high priority for the Village, Trustee Eilers
cited the need for flexibility. He said although the house is not old in the
historical sense, it is dated in terms of features because times have changed
and consumer preferences along with them. He reasoned that the Village can’t
expect an owner to invest significantly in the exterior without permitting
improvements to the inside to meet modern demands.
Trustee Tucker said the question boils down to whether the Village lets
existing homes bulk up or allows them to be torn down.
Trustee Behles voiced concern about incremental creep on GFA, especially in
light of the fact that the house is already 451 feet over GFA. He indicated
that in the absence of a demonstrable pressing hardship or particular
difficulty, he sees no reason to put aside the ordinance for aesthetic
considerations and cannot support this variation.
President Woodbury commented that he believes that zoning deals with aesthetic
issues all the time and that given that the Village wants to encourage
renovations, this is a valid tradeoff.
Trustee Berger indicated that she was inclined to be in favor of the variation.
Trustee Ritchell said that he would not be able to support the variation
because of its precedent-setting nature.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt the ordinance
granting three zoning variations. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Eilers, Berger, and Abell. Nays: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Absent: None. President Woodbury voted in favor of the ordinance to
break the tie.
President Woodbury moved to Item 9(a), Petitions Regarding Teardowns.
King Poor, 735 Walden, commented on petitions presented to the Council asking
it to consider amending the Zoning Ordinance to curb teardowns by reducing the
height and size of new construction in order to ensure that light, air, and
green space be preserved for future generations. He stated his belief that
there is overwhelming sentiment among residents that the ongoing number of
teardowns is a problem that needs to be addressed. He urged the Council to look
further for creative, resourceful ways to address the issue.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. Poor for his comments, saying that dialogue
facilitates solutions. He pointed out that the Village has an Ad Hoc
Residential Design Committee that will be moving forward in the next few
months. In addition, a new ad hoc committee will be looking into historical and
residential preservation. Also, a report is expected from the Building Review
Committee on how to improve their processes, and work is being done to
revitalize the Landmark Preservation Commission. Mr. Woodbury reported that the
trend in demolitions is waning and that economic factors are expected to
continue to impact teardowns. He reminded the audience that it is necessary to
achieve a balance between property rights and community values and there are
advocates on both sides. He said that there needs to be a common consensus in
the community about what is right for Winnetka, and that this consensus will
come out of the discussions to come from these committees.
Susie Shabica, 326 Ridge, and Midge Powell, 693 Walden Rd., reiterated Mr.
Poor’s remarks.
The Council then returned to the regular agenda.
c)   Ordinance No. M-19-2006 – Disposal of Certain Personal Property Owned by
the Village - Adoption. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to
adopt this ordinance authorizing the disposal of certain specified vehicles and
equipment owned by the Village that are past their useful life and have been
replaced. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-21-2006 – Intergovernmental Agreement with the Village of
Glencoe Pertaining to “Split Lots” - Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that
this resolution pertains to certain parcels of land located partially in the
Village of Winnetka and partially in the Village of Glencoe. She noted that
there are 58 of these lots located along the north side of Scott Avenue between
Gordon Terrace and Grove Street. They have Winnetka mailing addresses and
receive all of their municipal services from the Village of Winnetka. The
subject negotiated agreement between Glencoe and Winnetka permits those
property owners who wish to do so to disconnect the relevant portion of their
property from Glencoe and annex it to the Village of Winnetka. To date, nearly
50 of the 58 Split Lots have indicated that they will apply for the boundary
change.
Ms. Janega reviewed the provisions of the resolution authorizing the execution
of the intergovernmental agreement, setting out both the negotiated terms of
the disconnection and the annexation and the language of the disconnection/
annexation ordinance. She noted that pursuant to the agreement, the tax
revenues currently received by Glencoe from any of the Split Lot territory
would be phased out over a 5-year period through annual reimbursements to be
paid by Winnetka after it begins receiving tax revenues from the newly annexed
properties.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt the Resolution
annexing land partially located in the Village of Glencoe. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7. Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8. Old Business.
None.
9. New Business.
a)   Petitions Regarding Teardowns. This item was discussed earlier.
b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1475 and 1476. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1475 in the amount of $1,525,873.23 and Warrant List No. 1476 in the
amount of $1,053,700.11. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Hibbard Road Water Main Replacement and Pump Station Project. Manager
Williams reported that the Village is making significant progress on gaining
the necessary easement to complete this project that is strategically important
to the improvement of the Village’s water system. He recommended that the bids
be awarded contingent upon acquisition of the easement.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize $1,300,000 for
the Hibbard Road water line replacement and the enhancement of the Wilmette
water interconnection. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the water line bid
to Lenny Hoffman for the installation of the 16’ water main in the amount of
$982,682, contingent upon the Village’s acquisition of the required easement.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the pump station bid
to Engineered Fluid, Inc. in the amount of $239,190, contingent upon the
Village’s acquiring the required easement. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Replacement of 1987 Skid Steer Loader & Trailer. Mr. Ciesla reviewed a
request to replace the existing skid steer loader and trailer that has reached
the end of its useful life. The equipment is used for various construction and
repair activities on both the electric and water systems and as a fork lift at
the Yards. He recommended the purchase be awarded to Atlas Bobcat, Inc. in the
amount of $31,247.
Attorney Janega commented that although the Village is proceeding without a
competitive bidding process, Atlas Bobcat is the sole source vendor in the area
and their price is less than GSA pricing.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the purchase as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10. Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the BCDC would soon be making
recommendations to the Village Council regarding a property maintenance code
and retail overlay district.
b)   Manager. Manager Williams introduced the Village’s new videographer. He
explained that Council meetings would soon be available via the internet from a
link on the Village’s home page. Comcast will continue to broadcast the
meetings on Channel 10.
Mr. Williams reported that an Ash tree on private property in Winnetka has been
found to have the Emerald Ash Borer. This is the fourth sighting confirmed in
the North Shore area. The Illinois Department of Agriculture has not yet
provided guidance as to what action will be taken, but in all likelihood a
quarantine will be imposed prohibiting the movement of firewood and ash trees
in and out of the area.
Finally, Mr. Williams announced that the State Legislature has realigned in-
person early and absentee voting for the upcoming election, and as a result
thereof residents will no longer be able to come to the Manager’s office to
vote. A list of early voting sites will be posted on the Village’s website.
11. Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Scott Freres to the Design
Review Board for a second full term beginning upon the expiration of his
current term. Trustee Eilers moved to appoint Mr. Freres. Trustee Behles
seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. Woodbury then suggested that an Ad Hoc Historic and Residential
Preservation Committee be established to be chaired by Keith Groebe with Jeff
Cohen, John Cook, Carter Howard, Posey Fisher, and Charlie Hoffman, as members.
Herb Ritchell will serve as the Council representative to this Committee.   
Trustee Abell so moved, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
12. Executive Session.
Trustee Abell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, board appointments, and possible
purchase of land pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Berger seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 11:17 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:50 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13. Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:51 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
September 12, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: October 3, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 12, 2006 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Sandra Berger. Also present: Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Chief
of Police Joseph DeLopez, Deputy Chief Patrick Kreis, Sergeants Florkow,
Harrison and Hornstein, and Officer Garofalo, Director of Public Works Steven
Saunders; Finance Director Ed McKee, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Acting Director of
Water & Electric Rich Ciesla and 1 person in the audience.
2)   Discussion: Police Department Comprehensive Strategic Review.
Chief of Police Joseph DeLopez presented the Council with an overview of his
department’s staffing, training, and equipment needs. He spoke of his vision
for the future of community policing, the pooling of resources for major
crimes, disasters, prisoner facilities and communications, the importance of
mutual aid with adjoining villages and the role of the department in complying
with Homeland Security mandates. Deputy Chief Kreis, Sergeants Florkow,
Harrison and Hornstein, and Officer Garofalo also demonstrated a variety of
equipment routinely supplied to patrol officers on routine patrol, and
equipment contained in the Police Department’s mobile office and used for
special purposes.
Discussion ensued about maintaining the effectiveness of the department under
the alternative scenarios of less funding and fewer employees, and of more
funds and additional employees. Chief DeLopez also answered questions from the
Council regarding programs to build positive relationships with the schools and
the community at large. President Woodbury asked that the Chief return to the
Council with a strategy for current and future Councils to use as a guideline
to advance the Police Department’s mission to maintain the safety of residents
and provide services to the community.
3)   Parking Analysis.
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders reviewed the results of the parking
study and presented options for the construction of a parking deck to mitigate
the deficit of parking spaces in the East and West Elm business districts.
After considering each of the options, the Council discussed the possibility of
a below ground two-level parking deck on Lincoln Avenue that could be expanded
if necessary. The Trustees also discussed aesthetics, funding and having a
design made of such a structure before a final decision is made.
President Woodbury directed the staff to place this item on the October 3rd
meeting agenda for further discussion and the possibility of developing a
design of the option selected.
4)   New Trier Parking Contract.
Finance Director Ed McKee updated the Council on the status of the contract to
lease parking spaces to New Trier High School at the Indian Hill commuter
parking lot. He reported that an agreement had been reached on the contract
amount and that the school had also agreed to dedicate staff members to oversee
parking. Mr. McKee added that the lot will be reconfigured slightly to make
parking more convenient for commuters.
The Trustees commented on the number of spaces allotted to the school, the term
of the contract, the benefit of providing parking in the community and the need
for a strategy for parking in this area.
5)   Audit update.
Mr. McKee informed the Council of the progress of the audit and after some
discussion, the Council asked Mr. McKee to invite a representative of the
auditing firm to the October study session to meet with them.
6)   Executive Session.
No executive session was held.
7)   Adjournment.
The Council returned to the open meeting and the meeting adjourned without
further business at 11:00 p.m.
September 19, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 3, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 19, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public
Works Steve Saunders, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Acting Director of Water &
Electric Rich Ciesla, Distribution Engineer Bryan McInturff, Village Forester
Jim Stier, and approximately 18 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
a)   Approval of the Agenda.
At the request of the Village President, Item 6(e), Ordinance MC-8-2006, was
added to the agenda to amend the Village Code to establish the administrative
rank of Commander in the Police Department. By the consent of the Council, the
agenda, as amended, was approved unanimously.
3)   Quorum.
a)   October 3, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Berger, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 10, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 5, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustees Eilers, Abell and President
Woodbury noted several corrections to the minutes. Trustee Ritchell moved to
approve the minutes as corrected, seconded by Trustee Abell. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
5)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-07-2006 – Hands-free Cellular Phone Ordinance – Public
Hearing & Introduction. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing at 7:38
p.m.
Chief DeLopez briefly reviewed the history of this item and explained that the
proposed draft reflects the Council’s instruction at the March 14, 2006 study
session to prepare an ordinance that would mirror the City of Chicago’s hand-
held cell phone ban.
The Trustees discussed whether the ordinance should contain further
restrictions for other types of electronic devices, commented upon the
enforcement aspects of the ordinance, and discussed delaying the effective date
or issuing warnings for a period of time and erecting signs throughout the
Village in order to educate drivers of the new ordinance before it is enforced.
Village Attorney Janega said that, in addition to signs, the Council could
choose to build an implementation schedule into the ordinance, such as a 6-
month warning period before beginning regular enforcement.
President Woodbury commented that if Winnetka wants to lead, it should, but the
issue is regional because people come from everywhere. He added that he had
written letters to the surrounding communities and to legislators at the county
and state level, but had received no response from Cook County or the state. He
then asked for comments from the public.
Bernard Hammer, 1455 Tower; John Dell, 1278 Scott, Dick Golden, 1203 Ash, and
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, spoke in favor of enacting a more restrictive local
ordinance that could include a cell phone ban or other electronic devices and a
regional or statewide law prohibiting the use of hand held cell phones while
driving.
Mr. Daggett Harvey, 596 Oak Street, spoke against creating an ordinance in
Winnetka that makes it an island from surrounding communities that do not have
an ordinance and described the difficulties of making calls in that situation.
He felt that a leadership effort should be directed toward statewide
legislation and added that if Winnetka does adopt an ordinance, he would like
it to be modeled after Highland Park’s.
Joni Johnson, 888 Tower, co-chair of the 2006 Caucus Council, reminded the
Council that it was the Caucus platform that had suggested this ordinance as a
result of the responses to the Caucus’ survey questionnaire. She commented that
it should not be an insurmountable problem to inform the community of the new
ordinance.
President Woodbury closed the public hearing at 8:15 pm and asked the Village
Attorney for her comments.
Attorney Janega reviewed the negligent driving provision of the Village Code,
which covers drivers endangering others by a variety of actions and is
ordinarily a secondary enforcement after an accident. She informed the Trustees
that the Council makes the decision on what the policy is and how it is
implemented and recommended that the ordinance be enforced if it is adopted.
She said that a warning period is not unusual and is certainly appropriate;
however, the longer it goes on, the less meaning the ordinance will have.
President Woodbury added that the Council can make the ordinance effective at a
future date and create some publicity before it becomes effective in
conjunction with a warning period to promote education first. He then asked for
comments from the Council.
The Trustees’ individual comments were in favor of the ordinance as drafted,
with recommendations for an educational campaign before the effective date, a
short warning period before regular enforcement begins, notices to residents
and signs to inform motorists of the new law.
Trustee Ritchell moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Berger.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury thanked the Caucus for bringing the issue to the forefront
and asked that residents complete the Caucus survey when it is received because
the results do have an effect on the community. He also thanked the Council for
taking a leadership position in enacting this ordinance and hoped that this
action would send a message to County and State officials to create a similar
law on a regional basis.
b)   Resolution R-22-2006 – Utility Easement Agreement with Evangelical Church
of Winnetka. - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed the resolution that would
authorize an easement agreement between the Village and the Evangelical
Covenant Church of Winnetka for a perpetual easement at the northwest corner of
the Church property to install and maintain water utility facilities. She
explained some of the key provisions of the agreement which also includes
restoration of landscaping and re-striping of the parking lot.
In response to questions from the Trustees, Acting Director of Water &
Electric, Rich Ciesla, explained that although construction on the water line
will begin next month, there will not be much disruption to the property until
the construction of the pump station begins next spring.
Manager Williams thanked Trustees Ritchell and Eilers for their help in
negotiating the agreement with the Church and acknowledged President Woodbury’s
vision to move the pump station so that the reliability of the Village’s water
system will be enhanced.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-22-
2006, authorizing the easement agreement with the Evangelical Covenant Church
of Winnetka substantially in the form submitted. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Ritchell moved to authorize the payment of $23,000 to the Evangelical
Covenant Church of Winnetka as provided in the easement agreement. The motion
was seconded by Trustee Eilers and passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution R-23-2006 – Amendment to Westmoor Commons Development
Agreement: 934-Westmoor - Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that this
resolution would approve an amendment to Section 7.B.2 of the Development
Agreement between the Village and Westmoor Commons which pertains to the
undergrounding of electric lines.
Following a short discussion, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved
to approve Resolution R-23-2006 and authorize the execution of an amendment to
the Development Agreement with Westmoor Commons. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell,
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-24-2006 – Mirani’s Restaurant - Adoption. Attorney Janega
explained that this resolution would authorize the issuance of a Class A-
1 Liquor License to Mirani’s Restaurant at a new location, 727 Elm Street,
conditioned upon the completion of final inspections and that the owner ceases
operation and surrenders the liquor license at the restaurant’s current
location.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the Resolution
authorizing a Class A-1 liquor license to Mirani’s Restaurant at 727 Elm
Street, subject to the conditions stated in the Resolution. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees: Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Ordinance MC-8-2006 – Amendment to Village Code to Establish the
Administrative Rank of Commander in the Winnetka Police Department –
Introduction. President Woodbury announced that this amendment to the Village
Code would establish the rank of police commander and has been added to the
agenda for introduction and adoption, if Council waives introduction.
Chief of Police Joe DeLopez briefly reviewed his plan for the restructuring of
the Police Department and stated that establishing the exempt rank of police
commander will allow the final phase of the reorganization. He explained that
the creation of this administrative rank will replace the rank of lieutenant,
will provide more continuity of supervision on a daily basis as well as when
the Chief and Deputy Chief are occupied elsewhere, and will allow more officers
to remain on patrol. He further explained that the position would be a change
in title as well as in duties for the officers appointed and that an exempt
rank does not earn overtime. He added that should the appointed officers not
perform well in the rank of commander, they would revert to the rank held
before appointment. The Chief asked that the Council waive introduction of this
ordinance so that the rank can be established before he leaves the country for
a vacation.
Attorney Janega explained that the Council had discussed establishing
administrative ranks at the time the Board of Fire and Police Commissioners’
chapter of the Village Code was amended last year. To further the
reorganization of the Police Department, this amendment to the Code provides
for the administrative rank of Police Commander below that of Deputy Chief and
gives the Chief power of appointment for the position subject to the approval
of the Village Manager. In response to concerns raised by Trustees Eilers and
Abell about the late notice of this agenda item, Attorney Janega stated that
the notice and addition of this item complies with the Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Eilers noted that he had spoken with the Village Manager earlier in the
day who had clarified that the proposed ordinance is a technical,
administrative clarification only and, in that way, he is in favor of it. He
further noted that approval of this Ordinance does not signify either the
Council’s approval or disapproval of Chief DeLopez’ restructuring of the Police
Department.
President Woodbury ascertained from the Council that there were no objections
to waiving the first reading of this amendment.
Trustee Abell moved to waive introduction of the ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Tucker. By the unanimous vote of the Council, the motion passed.
Trustee Tucker moved to adopt Ordinance MC-8-2006, establishing the
administrative rank of Commander in the Police Department, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees:
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Public Comment and Questions. None.
7)   Old Business.
a)   CBI Subdivision of 630 Pine Lane. [Note: Court reporter, Anna F. Hammer,
of Victoria Reporting Service, 312-443-1025, was in attendance at the request
of the applicant, CBI Builders.]
Assistant Director of Community Development, Brian Norkus, reviewed this
request to subdivide the parcel at 630 Pine Lane into three lots. The
application was discussed at the June 6th Council meeting, at which time no
action was taken because the Plan Commission’s findings detailed several
concerns resulting in a negative recommendation, and the applicant indicated it
was preparing a modified subdivision plan. Mr. Norkus explained that the
applicant submitted revised plans on September 11th, along with a narrative
describing the proposed alterations to the plans and other measures taken in
order to address the Plan Commission’s concerns.
After Mr. Norkus described the changes in the revised plan, the Trustees asked
him if the staff had good cooperation with the developers, if there has been
enough time to review the revisions and if the Plan Commission’s concerns had
all been addressed.
Mr. Norkus replied that the developers have shown an incredible amount of
cooperation and that the staff would always like more time to review plans, but
that he believes a thorough review has been done by the Village Engineer and
Forester, although the changes Mr. Stier has suggested have not yet been put
into the plans.
Mr. Norkus also said that the narrative supplied with the plans was not a point
by point response to the comments of the Plan Commission; however some of the
changes that were made, such as placing some compensatory storage underground,
will have a great impact, even though it was not listed in the narrative.
In response to further questions from the Trustees, Mr. Saunders explained the
revisions that were made to reduce the total stormwater storage requirements,
such as the placement of some stormwater detention underground, reduction of
the size and depth of the pond, reduction of the development on the lot that is
in the floodplain and eliminating the retaining walls. He also explained how
underground storage is accomplished and that it is the responsibility of the
homeowner to maintain the underground facility. He stated that this is the
third review of the grading plans and stormwater calculations and he is
satisfied with these revisions. He added that there will be a further review
for compliance with the Village’s rules and regulations for stormwater drainage
when the applications for building permits are received.
Trustee Tucker expressed concern about future owners who may alter the
stormwater storage facilities and asked Ms. Janega how the Village can retain
the permanency of the drainage.
Attorney Janega replied that this is done by recording it on the plat, but
there is no 100% guarantee that drainage will remain unaltered with future
owners. She said that the goal in the redevelopment of the property and in the
engineering is to make sure that the general rate of flow that goes to the
downstream properties is maintained and that is why there are detention areas.
President Woodbury ascertained from Village Forester Jim Stier that the
additional augering that Mr. Stier had asked for on Lot 1 and 2 is not yet
shown on the revised plans, but that all other areas of concern had been
addressed.
Mr. Stier also said, in response to Trustee Tucker’s comment about the Plan
Commission’s suggestion that an escrow account for trees might be appropriate,
that such an account could be considered, but that the developer’s tree
preservation efforts had been commendable.
President Woodbury then asked for comments from the applicant.
The developer’s attorneys, John Senica and Joann Angerola of McGuire Woods,
Chicago, and the developer’s architects, Fred Wilson and Trisha Charbonneaux,
spoke to the Council about their efforts to revise the plans to address the
concerns of the staff, the Plan Commission and the Council as well as the
homeowners of the neighboring properties.
Mr. Wilson cited the reduction in the size of the building on Lot 1 and the
footprint for Lot 2, and noted the drainage plan and the elimination of
retaining walls.
Ms. Charbonneaux addressed Mr. Stier’s request for additional boring and stated
that a specific tree inventory for the preservation of trees to the current
standard of the tree ordinance had been assembled. She added that the changes
in stormwater storage and the elimination of retaining walls had made a
significant improvement in the number of trees remaining on the property.
Trustee Behles asked if the building pads and footprints are clearly shown and
if they are part of the record for approval. He then asked if the house shape
is made part of the record.
Attorney Janega replied that normally, the record depicts buildable area and
the shape of the house is not part of the record. She said that if the Council
wants to limit it to this building footprint, then that would have to be
indicated on the subdivision plat.
Trustee Behles commented that he perceives that the applicant is suggesting
that the house shape is preferable to the neighbors and suggested that the
numbers that go with the revised footprint of this house as well as the other
footprints, be included in the approval.
Attorney Janega responded that this can be done with the footprint, but not the
building height and similar zoning standards.
Mr. Wilson stated that this is what had been intended and that there would be
no problem including this in the plans.
Trustee Berger commented that the reduction in size of the house on Lot 1 was
significant and asked Mr. Senica if the revised plan provides additional
comfort to the neighbors and if they have met with any of the neighbors to show
them the new plans.
Mr. Senica replied that the revisions were made from the comments received from
the Plan Commission meetings, but that the developer would be willing to meet
with the neighbors about the revised plans.
Trustee Ritchell asked about the condition of the existing house and if the
changes affect its landmark status.
Mr. Wilson presented drawings that showed the Council how they tried to
replicate the original architect’s intent.
Attorney Janega explained the landmark designation process and said the
Landmark Preservation Commission had recommended designation and indicated it
would approve the proposed changes to the house on Lot 2.
President Woodbury asked for comments from the audience.
George Covington, 500 Western Ave, Lake Forest, representing Mr. and Mrs.
Block, who own the neighboring property to the north, Mr. Terry Westbrook, 1160
Pelham, and Marcy Love, 1175 Pelham, noted that the developer had not met with
them about the revised plans, and expressed concerns about stormwater runoff,
the proximity of the new homes to their properties, the size and height of the
proposed houses, the loss of trees and the maintenance of the underground
stormwater storage system, and stated that the development is not consistent
with the R-2 zoning district and Comprehensive Plan.
Mr. Covington also commented that if the changes to the plans are significant
enough, the matter should be sent back to the Plan Commission for review in the
context of their letter of findings.
Mr. Daggert Harvey responded to Mr. Covington’s comment by saying that, as a
matter of procedure, if the Plan Commission and neighbors have said no to the
development, it doesn’t make sense to go back to the Plan Commission.
Andrea Korolis, 475 Provident, presented a list of complaints about her new
home that was built by CBI, saying that she wanted to give the Council her
perspective of the quality of CBI’s work. She asked that the Council pass an
ordinance to protect homeowners by requiring all builders to install battery
back-up systems for all pumps.
President Woodbury thanked her for her comments and expressed his hope that she
and CBI could come to a satisfactory resolution of the problems.
Mr. Marlowe, CBI Builders, addressed Ms. Korolis’ remarks.
Ms. Angerola stated that the subdivision plan meets the requirements of the
Subdivision Ordinance and that, although each person’s vision of a small estate
district may differ, they have complied with the rules and regulations of the
R-2 zoning district and in every respect with the Zoning Ordinance.
Attorney Janega addressed the procedural issues by first stating that the
application is for final subdivision approval. She then said that since the
original application has expired, the Village is not bound by that one and the
new application has gone before the Plan Commission. The applicant has also
submitted an application to the Landmark Preservation Commission. The Council
has received comments from the Plan Commission with its findings to support its
negative recommendation. Ms. Janega’s recommendation was to put this on the
Council’s agenda, not the Plan Commission’s, because the changes are within the
scope of the application considered by the Plan Commission. She explained that
the revised application is before the Council for policy guidance to allow the
Council time to consider the entire package, to give the neighbors an
opportunity to react to the revised plan and then to make a final decision. In
her opinion, the matter does not have to go back to the Plan Commission, since
the applicant has taken significant steps to address the Plan Commission
comments. She added that the differences between the original application and
this application explain why the provisions of the R-2 zoning district are
important. The zoning requirements haven’t changed, but there is now a
dramatically different subdivision plan that complies, has the same lot
configuration and, with the adjustment of the building pad and placing
stormwater storage underground, does not artificially create a drainage pond
and keeps it much more in line with neighboring properties than the previous
plan. She said that she thought a great deal of progress has been made, but
that the Council needs time to mull over the plan. She asked that the Council
keep in mind that Mr. Marlowe could build what he wants to on Lot 2 today, or
could demolish the house and build what he plans to for Lot 3 without a
subdivision or without any kind of action by the Council. She also observed
that Mr. Marlowe could build one massive home on this property without
subdividing but, by allowing three lots, the existing house can be preserved.
She concluded by saying that the Council has the option to direct that a
resolution be drafted authorizing the subdivision with certain recommended
conditions based on this discussion, and, assuming that this plan meets all
requirements, the Council would then have 60 days to take action.
Manager Williams interjected that since attorneys are present and the applicant
has a court reporter recording the proceedings, there is an implication of
litigation. He said the Council can also discuss this in executive session in
order to consult with the Village Attorney. He then ascertained that the staff
had no further requests of the developer.
President Woodbury suggested continuing the matter.
Trustee Abell questioned the Village Forester about the plan to add 141 new
trees and the likelihood that it is possible that they will grow to maturity
without being overcrowded.
Mr. Stier replied that there is a wide variety of trees planned by the
landscape architect and he thought they would have a good chance of survival.
He said the changes in the Tree Preservation Code during the last year have
helped to preserve more trees and that by working with the developer’s
arborist, he thought the new trees and the existing trees would work well
together.
Trustee Behles asked if the approval of this application was to contain
conditions, he would like to see the inclusion of the specific footprint and
the resulting area totals as part of the application and also, for the Council
to see documentation of the proposed additional augering suggested by Mr.
Stier.
Trustee Berger said that it is her opinion that this should go back to the Plan
Commission because of the extent of the changes and because the recommendations
of the Plan Commission are no longer in effect.
Trustee Eilers agreed that sending it back to the Plan Commission makes sense,
but since the Council has been told that the plans meet the Zoning and
Subdivision Ordinance requirements and based on Mr. Norkus’ comments, the
petitioner seems to have satisfied the Plan Commission’s thirteen points set
forth in Ms. Mitchel’s May 26, 2006 letter, he is not clear what flexibility
the Council has and would like to hear from Ms. Janega on that.
Trustee Tucker said that she would prefer that this go back to the Plan
Commission as well, because the Comprehensive Plan is important and that is why
there is Plan Commission review. She said that part of the problem for her is
that CBI has made representations in the past that they would work with the
staff, but what they came back with, was not what was discussed. She said that
since plans don’t have actual figures, and in light of past responses from CBI
and Mrs. Korolis’s comments, she would like some assurances that CBI will do as
they say.
Trustees Abell and Ritchell stated that they would like to continue the matter.
Ms. Janega said that she does not recommend sending the application back to the
Plan Commission. She said that the staff has not been able to review this new
plan as thoroughly as they need to and recommended that the matter be put on
the next Council meeting agenda. She added that depending on what staff
recommends, it may or may not include a resolution at that time.
President Woodbury announced that the matter would be continued to October 3rd.
8)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1477 and 1478. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1477 in the amount of $678,847.74 and Warrant List No. 1478 in the
amount of $457,460.08. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   ICMA Retirement Plan Amendment. Manager Williams reported that this
proposed amendment to the ICMA Administrative Services Agreement would reduce
plan fees and the mutual services fee pertaining to retirement savings accounts
and asked that the Council authorize that the agreement be signed. He said that
employees contribute to deferred compensation plans with their own money and
that assets have reached a point where the fees are discounted and this
amendment will lock in the rates.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the Finance
Director to sign an amendment to the ICMA Administrative Services Agreement. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Post Office Committee Minutes. Manager Williams reviewed the minutes of
the Post Office Committee, stating that the Committee continues to struggle
with parking and is beginning to discuss the economics of underground parking.
He suggested that the Council attend the meetings whenever they can. He also
said that the chair of the Post Office Committee agrees that it would be
helpful to have members of the Council at the meetings and he will attend the
Study Session in October.
9)   Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury asked that residents return the
Caucus questionnaire by September 30th, reminding viewers that the Council
seriously considers their comments.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that his meeting with METRA last week
was very positive and that there may be something to bring to the Design Review
Board sooner than had originally been thought, possibly in the spring.
Trustee Abell reported that he had attended the Northwest Municipal Conference
meeting last week and suggested that it might be helpful to bring up Winnetka
cell phone ordinance there to encourage other villages to consider a similar
ordinance. Manager Williams agreed that it may be of some help, although the
NWMC had declined to lobby for this in the past.
Trustee Ritchell reported that the Ad Hoc Historic Preservation Committee’s
first meeting will be on Monday September 25th.
c)   Attorney. None
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that there has been a lot of newspaper
coverage on ComEd’s rate increases, but that it will not have any effect in
Winnetka, where rates will not go up.
Mr. Williams also reported that Linda Walsh had informed him that the Chestnut
Mall had received a design award for the brickwork.
10)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointments of Hugh Brower to the Building
Review Committee for a full term replacing Karen Templeton; Posey Fisher to
serve as the Landmark Preservation Commission’s representative to the Plan
Commission; Julie Windsor to the Ad Hoc Residential Design Committee; and Laura
Good to a full term on the Landmark Preservation Commission to replace Bob
Zielinski.
a)   Trustee Abell moved to appoint Mr. Brower to the Building Review
Committee. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   Trustee Tucker moved to appoint Ms. Fisher to represent the Landmark
Preservation Commission at Plan Commission meetings. Trustee Ritchell seconded
the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Trustee Ritchell moved to appoint Julie Windsor to the Ad Hoc Residential
Design Committee, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
d)   Trustee Ritchell moved to appoint Laura Good to the Landmark Preservation
Commissions, seconded by Trustee Berger. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
11)   Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, board appointments, and possible
purchase of land pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(5),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council convened into Executive Session at 11:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 12:10 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 12:10 p.m.
______________________________
Deputy Clerk
October 3, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 17, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 3, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Deputy Police Chief Patrick Kreis, and approximately 30 persons in the
audience.
President Woodbury welcomed members of Boy Scout Troops 18 & 20 to the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   October 10, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 17, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 12, 2006, Study Session. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   September 19, 2006, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury referred the
Trustees to a revised draft reflecting changes submitted by Trustee Eilers.
Trustee Behles submitted an additional technical change. Then Trustee Ritchell,
seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to approve the minutes, as amended.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-07-2006 – Hands-free Cellular Phone Ordinance. Manager
Williams explained that at the last meeting, the Council asked staff to look at
how best to publicize the new ordinance to residents and surrounding villages.
He recommended that the Village publicize the new ordinance in the upcoming
issue of the Winnetka Report, insert a flyer in the Village’s utility bills,
and install appropriate signage at the entrances to the Village and along key
arterial streets. He said that given the upcoming holiday schedule, the two-
month utility billing cycle, and need for new signs, the ordinance should be
given an effective date of January 1, 2007. He added that staff also recommends
the removal of the provisions regarding fines for violations and that only
warning tickets be issued during the month of January.
Trustee Abell inquired as to approximately how many signs would be installed.
Mr. Williams indicated that he expected that signs would be posted on Willow,
Green Bay, Sheridan, Tower, and probably Hibbard and Ridge Roads.
Trustee Berger asked about the possibility of removing the signs regarding
truck traffic and stop sign violations to avoid a proliferation of signs
throughout the Village.
Trustee Eilers expressed concern about the effect this ordinance would have on
residents from neighboring communities who come to do business in the Village,
noting that they contribute to the Village’s vitality, would not get notice of
the ordinance, and might be angered by its enforcement causing them not to
frequent the local merchants. He urged that the Council adopt a six-month
warning period so as to permit people who are vital to the Village’s well being
to be educated. He pointed out that as proposed, the violation would not be a
secondary violation, as is the case in Highland Park, and that the first notice
that many might have is when they are issued a warning or ticket. He said that
he is in favor of the ordinance, but would like to see a longer warning period.
Deputy Chief Patrick Kreis noted that the offense would be written on moving
violation “Y” tickets, which would be reported to the Secretary of State if the
offender paid the fine or was found guilty in court. He said that he and Chief
DeLopez had discussed a longer warning period but felt that 30 days is
appropriate because a longer period would not cover repeat offenders and might,
in fact, aggravate the situation. He also commented on the removal of specified
fine amounts, saying that to do so would be in keeping with other Village
ordinances pertaining to moving vehicle violations. He explained that in
reality the court establishes the fine options on a moving violation and the
judge makes the final determination.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Deputy Chief Kreis that this would, in fact, be
treated as any other moving violation and would be reported to the Secretary of
State as such. Deputy Chief Kreis indicated that he believed that to be the
case. Trustee Eilers said that this reinforced his belief that the warning
period needs to be prolonged.
Trustee Abell asked staff to check to make sure that a violation would, in
fact, be reported to the Secretary of State, noting that if the ticket were to
be issued as a “LO” (local ordinance) violation, it would not be and would,
therefore, not be recorded on permanent driving records. He asked that staff
confirm how the City of Chicago enforces its ordinance.
Trustee Eilers commented that the “LO” approach would certainly ease the burden
on unknowing offenders and indicated that his opinion would change somewhat if
the violation was not going to be reported to the Secretary of State.
Trustee Ritchell indicated that he was in favor of the 30-day warning period
but urged an aggressive education program, including notices to the Park
District and School Districts and their staffs.
Trustee Behles said that he believes the Village should be able to complete the
necessary education program by February 1st and said that he was not in favor
of delaying the effective date beyond January 1, 2007.
Trustee Tucker said that she could go with a somewhat longer warning period of
45-60 days.
Manager Williams asked if the Trustees were comfortable with the taking out the
violations provision to make the ordinance consistent with other similar
violations.
Attorney Janega explained that the only reason this section had been included
in the draft ordinance was that staff was directed to base the Village’s
ordinance on the Chicago’s. She noted that the Village Code has a standard
range of fines for moving violations and the only other place it has specified
fines is for parking tickets. She said the section in question is not a
necessary provision within the structure of the Village’s Traffic Code.
Trustee Abell inquired as to whether officers could issue a “P” ticket in lieu
of a “Y” ticket and thereby forego reporting the offense to the Secretary of
State. Deputy Chief Kreis said that “P” tickets cannot be issued for moving
violations.
Trustee Berger asked whether it makes sense to make this violation a secondary
offense for the first year until people are well educated.
President Woodbury commented that the Police Department does not believe that
would be effective because it would mean that officers could not pull someone
over solely for using a hand-held cell phone. The point of the ordinance is to
prevent more serious accidents resulting from the use of hand-held phones.
Trustee Eilers commented that Winnetka is not an island and asked staff to
confirm whether Chicago was actually enforcing its ordinance as a primary
violation. He said that if he had the power to ban cell phones statewide, he
would do so, but given the small size of the community, a balanced and
effective approach needs to be found.
Aaron Wolff, a Highland Park resident, referred the Trustees to his letter in
the Agenda packet. He urged the Council to support the proposed ordinance and
to make the fine for an accident that occurs while talking on a cell phone a
substantial one.
President Woodbury remarked that, in keeping with Caucus survey results, he
contacted both neighboring communities and the State Legislature encouraging
them to pass some sort of regional legislation, but has thus far received no
response. This ordinance is a reasonable first step and will help to make
Winnetka a little bit safer.
John O’Malley, 1114 Merrill, spoke in support of the ordinance as drafted.
President Woodbury tabled the ordinance until the October 17th meeting in order
for staff to research some of the outstanding questions.
Ted Hocter, Assistant Scout Master of Boy Scout Troop 20, presented the Council
with letters from some of his scouts pertaining to the topic.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1479 and 1480. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items and answering questions from
the Trustees, he moved to approve Warrant List No. 1479 in the amount of
$738,810.08 and Warrant List No. 1480 in the amount of $809,959.93. Trustee
Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: 1249 Scott St. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for
zoning variations to permit construction of an attached garage and front
entrance on the existing home at 1249 Scott St. He noted that the existing
structure is currently nonconforming with respect to roofed lot coverage and
side yard setback, but conforms with respect to GFA, front yard lot coverage
and garage doors. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4 to 3
to recommend denial of the variation request.
Trustee Berger inquired about the combined side yard requirement on a 50-foot
lot and asked whether a new house could be constructed, if the existing house
was demolished. Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that it was possible.
Trustee Eilers commented that if the garage were to be located in the rear
yard, the requested variations would be minimal. Mr. D’Onofrio agreed, but
added that the topography makes that alternative very challenging because of a
steep drop off to the rear of the property.
Trustee Berger asked whether the house would still be over the GFA if the
garage were to be located in the rear quarter of the yard. Mr. D’Onofrio
responded that it would.
Todd Van Neck, the applicant, explained that to continue to use the car port
and the parking pad in front is no longer a functional solution. He reported
that his neighbors are aware of the requested variations required for the
proposed design and are generally in favor of it.
Nathan Kipnis, the applicant’s architect reviewed a number of the alternative
designs that had been considered and responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Tucker commented that there are many other houses on small lots in the
Village that lack a two-car garage and indicated that she could not consider
that a hardship.
The neighbors from 1243 Scott and 1238 Scott spoke in favor of the request.
President Woodbury remarked that there is a high standard for overturning the
Zoning Board’s recommendation.
Trustee Eilers said that while he is concerned about the proposed one-foot side
yard, the elimination of the car port and the parking pad is a fair tradeoff
and a benefit to the community.
Trustee Berger said that she thinks the plan is a good one aesthetically, but
echoed concerns about the requested one-foot side yard. She noted that the
house to the east could be replaced at some time in the future, further
decreasing the space between the two houses. She said she was inclined to vote
to support the Zoning Board’s recommendation.
Trustee Behles concurred. He said that the narrowness of the lot, the
projection of the chimney, and grade, do constitute hardships in terms of
putting the garage in the rear quarter, but the desire for a two-car garage
does not. He said that this is one of those lots where a two-car garage just
does not fit and a one-foot side yard would be an extreme variation.
Trustee Ritchell complimented the applicant and the architect on their efforts
but said that he could not vote to overturn the Zoning Board because the home
is already built to the maximum.
Trustee Tucker also supported denial, saying that the decision cannot be based
solely on aesthetics; the extent of the variations and the justification for
them has to be considered. This house is already nonconforming and to grant the
variations would make it more so.
Trustee Abell agreed. He said the lot is just too small for what the applicants
want to do and that the Council has to maintain the integrity of the Zoning
Ordinance.
Noting that the consensus of the Council is to uphold the Zoning Board’s
decision, President Woodbury called for a motion. He said that he hopes the
applicant can figure out some other solution to their problem.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell moved to concur with the
recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals and to deny the requested zoning
variations. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: Trustee Eilers. Absent: None.
c)   Bid No. 06-020: Janitorial Services for Public Facilities. Manager
Williams reviewed the bids for janitorial services at the Village Hall, Village
Yards, and the Elm Street and Hubbard Woods Train Stations. He reported staff’s
recommendation that the bid be awarded to Atlas Preferred Services, the current
service provider, for the amounts and charges specified in their bid response.
He noted that the bid reflects savings to the Village of $727.00 per month for
routine cleaning.
Trustee Behles asked how much of the Village’s annual costs come from special
services performed that are not included in the standard service fee. Manger
Williams replied that the two types of services are split about 50/50.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Trucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Feasibility Study for Possible Parking Structure – Lincoln Avenue Parking
Lot. Manager Williams explained that as discussed at the September 12th Study
Session, staff recommends hiring an engineering firm that specializes in
parking construction to confirm the cost and feasibility of erecting a parking
structure at the Lincoln Avenue Parking Lot. As conceived, the lot would
consist of two levels that would provide a net increase of 84 spaces but could
be expanded in the future to add a third level. He noted that such a lot would
not require the acquisition of any land and would provide flexibility for
future expansion. However, staff has gone as far as it can with the experience
and talent on hand, and the Village needs to confirm its expectations and the
estimate that building such a structure would cost in the neighborhood of
$6,000,000, in order to best understand its options when considering the three
major projects on the horizon: the parking deck, the Post Office redevelopment,
and streetscape. He noted that the Post Office Committee is pushing to put all
of the parking underground, which would be very expensive. Manager Williams
indicated that although not specifically provided for in the current budget,
there are sufficient funds available to cover the expense of the feasibility
study, estimated to be between $15,000 and $20,000.
Trustees Abell and Behles agreed that it is important to get accurate data in
order to keep the options open.
Trustee Ritchell suggested adding a condominium component above the proposed
parking garage just to see how that would alter the cost projections.
Referring to the Village’s parking study, Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, commented
that at its worst, the occupancy in east Elm is 88%, which he doesn’t believe
translates into a “shortage,” especially at the cost per space. He said that he
personally sees this project as questionable given the other demands on Village
funds and urged caution in moving forward.
President Woodbury remarked that with the impetus of the Post Office
redevelopment, the Village is engaging in a number of studies so that it can
better plan for the future. He indicated that future discussions about a
parking deck on Lincoln Avenue would have to include the Winnetka Community
House in terms of planning and financing because they would be co-beneficiaries
of such a deck. Mr. Woodbury said that the issue is getting to the point where
difficult and solid decisions have to be made, decisions that cannot be made
without complete information.
Mr. Kates commented that he hopes the Post Office redevelopment adds something
to the community above and beyond what it already has and reiterated that this
kind of expenditure demands careful consideration. He suggested stepped up
traffic and parking enforcement to alleviate some of the problems.
Manager Williams mentioned that in the next budget staff intends to recommend
the addition of one person tasked solely with parking enforcement.
Trustee Berger said that she believes that staff has done a good job of
providing options but questioned whether the Council ought to have a better
idea of whether it is convinced that a parking deck is needed and, if so, where
it would be.
Trustee Behles remarked that there is, in fact, no Council consensus that a
garage is the right decision, but it has agreed that if one is to be built,
this is the best location. He added that if the Council is going to consider
this as a viable option, it needs some hard information about the cost.
Trustee Abell commented that discussions about parking have been ongoing for
months and that the issue has been discussed repeatedly over the years, with
any decision always being put off. He added that his experience would indicate
that the Village is not going to make major changes with regard to parking
enforcement. The Village has always backed off from doing so and he doesn’t
believe that there will be much change this time. That said, however, when the
Post Office site is done, the incentive to do something about parking will be
gone, so he would like to address the issue now.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to authorize staff to solicit
engineering proposals to perform a cost and feasibility analysis for a possible
below-grade parking structure on the site of the Lincoln Avenue Parking Lot,
with and without a condominium component above. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Historical Society Request. Manager Williams reviewed a request from the
Winnetka Historical Society to conduct a walking tour of the Village Green and
set up tables for check-in and refreshments next to the cenotaph. He noted that
neither staff nor the Park District had voiced any concerns about the proposal.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. Mr. Woodbury reported that he and the Village Manager
had met with officials from the other Village governmental bodies to discuss
what is going on and how to work together. He said that all are involved in
strategic planning, especially the schools that are looking for ways in which
to deal with the demographic projections.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Ritchell reported that the Ad Hoc Historic and
Residential Preservation Committee had held its first meeting.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that the Council would be holding a
special study session on Saturday, November 18th, at 8:30 a.m. to do a
comprehensive strategic review of the Fire Department.
He also reported that the Village, in conjunction with SWANCC, had held a
document destruction event on September 23rd that resulted in 5,700 lbs. of
papers being shredded.
Mr. Williams noted that during the month of September there had been 437 hits
on the Village’s new video streaming of Council meetings.
Finally, he reported that the Village had fared well during the two recent
major storms, due in large measure to significant steps taken to improve
drainage. He noted that several motorists had been stranded by severe flooding
in the Ravines during the events and that the Police Chief and Fire Chief will
be sending a detailed summary of the incidents to both IDOT and Sen.
Schoenberg, asking that improvements be made to that stretch of roadway. He
mentioned that the Village’s repeated requests to do so have thus far gone
unanswered.
Trustee Abell commented that the reliability of the Village’s electrical system
has increased over time.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Edie Howell to the Ad Hoc
Residential Design Committee. Trustee Tucker moved to approve the appointment.
Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 9:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:27 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:28 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
October 10, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 5, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 10, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, ad hoc Post Office Committee Chairman Julian
D’Esposito, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Community
Development Director Brian Norkus, Village Forester Jim Stier, Finance Director
Ed McKee, Assistant Finance Director Hanna Sullivan, and approximately 12
persons in the audience.
2)   Briefing: Post Office Committee.
Post Office Committee Chairman Julian D’Esposito began his report by inviting
the Trustees to future meetings of the Post Office Committee and thanking the
Council for appointing members with a cross-section of talent, interests and
perspectives.
Mr. D’Esposito distributed a draft of the Use and Design Principles for the
Post Office site redevelopment, explaining that the Committee had retained the
services of S.B. Friedman to provide a financial analysis of several design
concepts presented by the Lakota Group as well as Village residents. He noted
that the Winnetka Garden Club had also drawn up some preliminary plans of their
own, which were not included in his handout as they are still in very rough
form. He reported that the committee was trying to get an understanding of how
the various uses and infrastructure inter-relate, rather than conducting just a
design exercise.
After briefly describing the concepts, Mr. D’Esposito observed that a broad
conclusion can be drawn that residential uses are the most remunerative,
followed by retail. He said the study concludes that underground parking is
enormously expensive, and the Committee is trying to come up with a design that
has little, if any, underground parking. He noted that the Committee also faces
the challenge of reconciling the Bennett Plan, which envisions an extension of
Moffat Mall at the center of the development site, with the concept of creating
four corners of economic activity at Elm and Chestnut Streets.
Mr. D’Esposito noted that the summary of plans is incomplete, because it does
not make an analysis of revenue production for the uses. He added that the
Committee is attempting to understand what kind of market exists for retail in
Winnetka in terms of quantity and types of uses, and is awaiting the results of
a marketing study to help answer those questions.
Mr. D’Esposito reported that the Committee’s progress has been slowed because a
number of meetings had been cancelled due to conflicting holidays, so Committee
will not be able to meet the end-of-year deadline making final recommendations
to the Council.
The Trustees asked how the issue of affordable housing will impact residential
development on the site. Mr. D’Esposito indicated that it is a significant
issue, but that the Plan Commission will make recommendations in that area.
Mr. Williams and Mr. Norkus added that the Plan Commission probably would not
make its recommendation before Spring of next year. Manager Williams conceded
that while that might be a late date, the community could have the option of
deciding what percentage of units should be affordable.
Mr. D’Esposito commented that the models being examined so far include only
market rate housing and suggested that the Committee could use a placeholder
number of affordable units to get a sense of their economic impact.
The Council thanked Mr. D’Esposito for his presentation and for all of his
work.
3)   CBI Subdivision, 630 Pine Lane.
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus reviewed the
procedural background for the CBI Subdivision of 630 Pine Lane.
Mr. Norkus noted that at the September 19 Council meeting, CBI presented
revised plans that met the engineering and tree protection requirements of the
Village Code. He stated that CBI has now submitted a narrative specifically
addressing the Plan Commission’s findings on the subdivision application, as
well as a table summarizing the aspects of the subdivision plans with regard to
stormwater drainage, tree protection and the Comprehensive Plan. He added that
CBI had held two meetings with surrounding neighbors, and that CBI indicated it
would explore some of their suggested modifications.
Attorney Janega observed that as platted, the building boxes shown on the
revised plat will restrict the bulk of the houses by restricting building
footprints. She recommended against using the exact amount of square footage or
gross floor area depicted on the plat as a size limitation because of
differences in how areas are calculated, and that the combined effect of the
limited building footprints and the Zoning Ordinance height limitations
necessarily limit the size and location of the houses.
The Trustees, Attorney Janega and Mr. Norkus discussed various aspects of the
subdivision approval, including the house on Lot 1, the slope ratio, lot
coverage, and bulk limitations, after which Attorney Janega and Mr. Norkus
expressed their belief that the revised plans and narrative provided enough
information to draft a subdivision resolution that would comply with the
Comprehensive Plan.
Invited to come forward with comments, CBI and its attorney stated they were
satisfied that the foregoing discussions had covered all the issues.
President Woodbury next invited the neighbors near the site to express their
thoughts and concerns.
Mr. & Mrs. Robert Baumgarten of 645 Hibbard asked about the location of the
poolhouse pump and air conditioner from their lot line, and about various
drainage issues. Attorney Janega responded that in the absence of particular
limitations, the pump and air conditioner would be located inside the building
box shown in the plans, and that underground storage and stormwater drainage is
subject to approval by the Village Engineer.
Attorney George Covington, 500 Western Ave, Lake Forest, representing Mr. and
Mrs. Block, 644 Pine Lane, stated that in their view the houses are still too
big for the vicinity, and urged the Council to include bulk limits in the plat
of subdivision.
Mr. Terry Westbrook, 1160 Pelham, said that he had asked for an assessment of
two-lot configuration, and if that’s not possible, for the house on Lot 3 to be
turned to the north or the west because of its proximity to his property.
One of the developer’s architects, Ms. Trisha Charbonneaux, responded, saying
that CBI feels strongly about the need for three lots and keeping the
landmarked house on Lot 2. She showed sketches of the current proposal for Lot
3 and a second plan with the house turned to the north, and noted that changing
the orientation of the home would move it significantly to the east to save
several large trees, but because of the front yard setback requirements, the
footprint can’t be rotated.
Mr. Westbrook remarked that he didn’t feel CBI looked at reducing the number of
lots from an economic standpoint.
President Woodbury invited CBI to elaborate on the economic impact of a two-lot
subdivision.
Mr. Marlowe, CBI Builders, said that he ran the two-lot configuration through a
computer several different ways, but the economics did not work.
President Woodbury observed that the subdivision application is based on three
lots, and the Council was charged with considering that application.
Manager Williams noted the many lawyers present on both sides and suggested
that the Council continue its discussion in Executive Session. President
Woodbury noted that the application would be placed on the Council’s November 7
meeting agenda.
4)   Discussion: Lou Karrison, Auditor.
Finance Director Ed McKee introduced the Village’s new auditors from Sikich
L.L.P., Lou Karrison and Janet Matthys. He stated that, following his report at
the last Council meeting that the Village’s audit was not yet completed, he had
invited the auditors to this meeting to give a status report directly to the
Council.
Asked about the consequence of the audit being late, Mr. McKee replied that
extensions have been granted and that the Police and Fire Pension reports had
been filed, so there is no harm to the Village as yet. He noted that the
extension expires at the end of October and that the audit would need to be
completed by then for the Village to keep its GFOA certificate.
Mr. McKee then invited the Council to address their questions and concerns
directly to the auditors. The discussion turned to the SAS-99 letters that had
been sent to each Council member. Ms. Matthys stated that it is a new standard
and that Sikich is one of the first firms to implement it. Trustee Berger
commented that it seemed a shallow effort to detect fraud, as it is very
unlikely the Trustees would have any pertinent information to contribute in
that area.
Asked by Manager Williams if Sikich had checked utility billing or employees’
salaries, as the previous auditor had done, Ms. Matthys replied that they had
not. She said that the auditors used a test to look at controls, examined
purchasing approvals, compared salaries with approved budget requests,
inspected budgetary lines and balance sheet asset accounts and confirmed all
revenues. Manager Williams commented that it is the first time in his career as
a Village Manager his salary had not been checked.
Trustee Tucker noted that her impression was that there is an undue reliance on
the questionnaire, and she had a concern that because of the fraud statement,
there might be areas that were not looked at because the auditors had the
questionnaire to rely upon.
Ms. Matthys answered that the firm follows a nationally recognized system of
audit procedures, and that their procedures have increased rather than
decreased in light of the questionnaire. She offered to take a look at the
wording of the questionnaire if the trustees take exception to particular
questions.
Trustee Eilers asked why the audit was delayed.
Mr. Karrison replied that the field work was scheduled for July but some
records were not yet ready, which complicated the process, throwing off the
timeline.
Trustee Eilers asked if these were material amounts that in were in question.
Mr. Karrison said the problems were due to the GASB 34 regulation that had been
passed 3 years ago. Ms. Matthys added that another part of the problem was that
the Village is a first-year client and everyone is making the adjustment to a
new system.
Manager Williams interjected that the auditors asked the Village to go back and
reconcile records with regard to capital assets that were quite old. Assistant
Finance Director Hanna Sullivan reported that she had been required to search
for records from as far back as the 1940’s and that she and other staff had
spent quite a lot of time searching for records for the auditors.
Asked about the intense focus on capital assets, Ms. Matthys replied that some
of the depreciation amounts had not seemed reasonable, and due to the effects
of GASB 34, they wanted assurance that the numbers were accurate for bonding
and credit decisions.
Manager Williams noted that the Village is AAA rated and that’s never been an
issue. He asked Ms. Sullivan how much all that investigation changed things,
and she responded that there was a slight increase of about 5% in the current
year depreciation for the proprietary funds.
Manager Williams observed that the Village had complied with the auditors’
every request, and staff had spent an inordinate amount of time tracking down
documentation.
Trustee Berger asked why they hadn’t gone to the prior auditors for that
information, since the Village had been with them ten years and had the AAA
rating.
Mr. Karrison replied that there is an art to auditing and this is Sikich’s
approach.
Asked if the October 31st deadline would be met, Mr. McKee said he believed it
would. Manager Williams asked if the auditors anticipated any other major
projects for next year in terms of chasing old records, and Mr. Karrison
replied that he thought they were in good shape.
Trustee Berger commented that, because the Village is a new client, the
auditors should have had more flexibility with regard to the allocation of time
and personnel to get the audit finished.
President Woodbury expressed the Council’s expectation that the audit be on
time next year, despite any problems that might crop up. Mr. Karrison gave
assurance that it would.
5)   Water Rate Study.
Manager Williams said that this item would be postponed for later discussion.
6)   Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Behles,
Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened
into Executive Session at 9:50 p.m.
7)   Adjournment.
the Council returned to the open meeting and the meeting adjourned without
further business at 10:35 p.m.
   ______________________________
   Recording Secretary
October 17, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 7, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 17, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Interim Director of Water
& Electric Rich Ciesla, Distribution Engineer Bryan McInturff and approximately
eight persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   November 7, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 14, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 3, 2006, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell commented that a report
attributed to him had really been made by Trustee Ritchell. Trustee Ritchell
then moved to approve the minutes as corrected. Trustee Behles seconded the
motion. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2006 – Hands-free Cellular Phone Ordinance - Adoption.
Attorney Janega responded to questions raised by the Trustees at the last
meeting. She reported the following:
   i)   Violations of the proposed ordinance would be reported to the Secretary
of State’s Office, but they would not be charged against an individual’s
driving record.
   ii)   The Chicago ordinance is enforced as a primary violation for purposes
of making a traffic stop. The City is, however, aware that the Secretary of
State does not record the violations on drivers’ records.
   iii)   The Police Department cannot enforce the proposed ordinance using “P”
or “LO” form tickets instead of the standard “Y” ticket form.
She explained that the Council can reconsider whether it wishes to make a
violation a primary offense, as it is in the City of Chicago and as originally
drafted, or whether it would prefer to treat it as a secondary offense. In
addition, the Council needs to decide whether to (1) make the Ordinance
effective immediately, but defer enforcement; (2) delay the effective date to
allow for publicity prior to enforcing the Ordinance; or (3) delay the
effective date and defer enforcement. She reported that staff recommends that
if the Council passes the drafted ordinance, the effective date should be
January 1, 2007, not a later date, and that if the Council makes the Ordinance
effective immediately, strict enforcement be postponed until January 1, 2007.
If the Council makes the Ordinance effective January 1, 2007, but wants to
delay enforcement, staff recommends that enforcement be delayed no more than
one month, so that officers could cite repeat offenders, even if the repeated
offenses occur during a warning period.
Ms. Janega added that other than the effective date, the implementation
alternatives should be handled administratively, rather than being included in
the Ordinance.
Finally, Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments included in the
revised Ordinance pursuant to Council’s discussions at the October 3rd meeting.
Trustee Eilers reiterated his desire to see a statewide ban, but said that in
the absence of one, he is not in favor of Winnetka’s setting itself up as
unique in the suburbs. He noted that the Village President and the Manager have
reported that there has been no support for a regional ban from the County or
neighboring villages. He reported that, as the Council’s representative to the
Chamber of Commerce and the Business Community Development Commission, he had
broached the subject in meetings of both groups. Both groups, in turn, voted
unanimously that the offense should be secondary rather than primary, because
to enforce it as primary could be detrimental to the business community. He
encouraged the Council to listen to these groups because they represent
important Village interests. He added that the issue is one of implementation,
not whether some sort of step should be taken. He urged the Council to set the
offense as secondary, as in Highland Park, and not to set itself apart, but to
move with Highland Park to build a North Shore coalition that, in turn, could
work to convince the County and State to take action.
Cicely Michalak and Bill Shiel, Chamber of Commerce Executive Director and
Chairman of the BCDC, respectively, confirmed Mr. Eilers’ comments about their
groups’ positions.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, spoke in opposition to the ordinance as proposed,
saying that, in his opinion, it is based on the false assumption that hand-held
cell phones present more of a danger than hands-free phones and that it will
hurt Winnetka’s reputation. He said studies show that both types of phones
create a nearly equal threat and if Winnetka enacts this ordinance it will, in
effect, be creating two classes of individuals, those with hand-held phones and
those with hands-free devices. He maintained that both groups would be
virtually equally distracted, but only one group would be subject to being
ticketed. He urged a ban on either all cell phones or none, and commented that
he believed the perception of widespread support throughout the community for
the proposed action was subject to interpretation. Mr. Liss added that the
Ordinance as proposed would set Winnetka apart, without support from
neighboring communities. He noted that statistics indicate only about 25% of
the traffic violators ticketed in the Village in the last year were from
Winnetka and that both the Police Chief and the Village Manager have said this
matter is best handled on a regional basis. He said that if the Ordinance makes
sense, then Winnetka ought to be able to convince its neighbors to take the
same step.
Heather Blackwell, 770 Heather Lane, spoke in support of the Ordinance on
behalf of the Hubbard Woods School PTO. She said that by using hands-free
systems, the parents of Winnetka school children will help to spread the word
and move the cause forward in other communities.
Melissa Mizel, 939 Tower Rd., also spoke in support of the Ordinance, saying
that, while banning the use of all cell phones would be much safer, that is not
an option at the present time. She said that to cite someone for using a hand-
held device in a community where they are banned is only holding them
responsible for their actions, and if an occasional person boycotts Winnetka
because of the ban, so be it. Speaking as a resident and Chamber of Commerce
member, she said that the value of business as usual is not worth jeopardizing
the safety of the community.
Joanie Johnson, 888 Tower Rd., co-chair of the 2006 Caucus Council, objected to
the Council’s reconsideration of this issue, noting that only one person spoke
in opposition to the Ordinance at the publicized public hearing on September
19th, after which the Council unanimously voted in favor of introducing the
Ordinance. She said that she believes the Caucus survey shows that there is a
resounding mandate in the community in support of the Ordinance, and that if
the Chamber and the BCDC objected to the plan, they should have appeared
earlier. She added that the issues of signage and publicizing the ban have been
addressed, and that if a violation is treated as secondary, the Village will be
waiting for an accident to occur instead of taking positive, proactive action
to prevent it in the first place. She agreed that a regional approach would be
preferable but said that the only new issue on the table is whether the
Ordinance would impact shopping, and that the answer to that question is purely
speculative.
Manager Williams pointed out that if enforcement were secondary, it would be
akin to the way in which seat belt regulation began, meaning that if an
individual was cited for one traffic offense while using a hand-held cell
phone, he would, in fact be cited for two.
Isabel Alejo, 739 Walden Rd., expressed support for the Ordinance. She
explained how seriously cell phone violations are treated in Spain, with high
fines and points on drivers’ licenses.
President Woodbury commented that the Trustees have given and continue to give
the matter careful and thorough consideration, and it is appropriate for the
Council to continue to listen to discussion and be open to residents’ opinions
throughout the legislative process. As requested by the Caucus, the issue has
been brought before the Council, but it is ultimately up to the Trustees to
make an informed decision using the best information available. He went on to
say that there are several policy issues for the Council to consider and
suggested beginning with whether or not enforcement should be primary or
secondary.
Chief DeLopez stated that this is a question upon which he and the Village
Manager disagree. He said that in the absence of a regional or statewide
ordinance, he would recommend that if the Council adopts a ban on cell phones,
the only effective way to implement it is to treat it as a primary offense
because this is a matter of public safety and not to do so sends the message
that the Village doesn’t really mean it. He said that appropriate education and
publication can be achieved.
Trustee Abell inquired whether a conscious decision has to be made about
primary vs. secondary enforcement. Chief DeLopez responded that the Council
must give direction.
Trustee Abell also asked whether the negligent driving ordinance could be
enhanced so as to cover cell phone offenses and whether fleshing out the
definitions of distracted driving, more in keeping with the Highland Park
ordinance might give the Police Department something that it could enforce as
primary or secondary, at the officer’s discretion. Chief DeLopez replied that
although negligent driving could, in fact, be a by-product of cell phone usage,
it is more difficult to establish that it is the basis for negligent driving.
He added that it is very difficult to articulate all of the evidence of
distracted driving because many examples are very subtle. He pointed out that
Highland Park could move forward at a later time to enforce its ordinance as
primary.
Manager Williams remarked that from a global perspective, he and the Chief
agree that any ban should be adopted statewide, but on the question of primary
vs. secondary, they disagree. He acknowledged the Chief’s comments about safety
and the strong support from the schools, saying that there is a tremendous
balancing act going on. He said that he believes enforcement should be
secondary because (1) just as was done with seatbelts, it is a good first step
in an educational process; (2) when a ticket as a secondary offense is issued,
it provides the officer with a “teachable moment” to the driver; and (3)
Winnetka would be out on an island because Chicago’s enforcement as a primary
offense is haphazard at best. Manager Williams emphasized, however, that both
he and the Chief would carry out any law enacted to the fullest extent
possible, regardless of which implementation approach is adopted.
Trustee Abell asked whether it would still be possible to post signs if the
offense is made a secondary one. Manager Williams said that it would be.
Trustee Abell indicated that he thought this was a significant point.
Trustee Tucker commented that the discussion has raised some questions in her
mind. She asked whether there is a way to enforce the Ordinance in particular
areas, particularly around schools. Attorney Janega indicated that the
ordinance could be drafted to create specific areas.
Chief DeLopez advised against creating zones of enforcement within the
community. He said that if it is going to be taken seriously, then it should be
enforced uniformly, He added that the Department could concentrate directed
enforcement in specific locations.
Trustee Tucker asked the Chief his thoughts about starting out as a secondary
offense and then reviewing the results before moving on to making it a primary
offense. The Chief indicated that he did not believe that to do so would
achieve much.
Trustee Ritchell expressed concern about backpedaling on the issue based on a
possible loss of sales over the safety of the community. He said he believes
that enforcement needs to be primary.
Trustee Tucker expressed some ambiguity on the issue saying that recent
discussions have just brought more information and data to light. She said,
however, that although she would prefer a regional approach, in the absence
thereof, she would support primary enforcement.
Trustee Abell disagreed, saying that he would prefer a broader approach
directed at distracted driving and a regional or statewide solution. He voiced
concern about setting Winnetka up as an island and indicated that he believes
the Village should take small steps, and that the more the matter is discussed
the more support for modifying behavior will be garnered. He said that he would
support secondary enforcement, particularly if there were some option to permit
officers to issue a primary ticket if there was something truly dangerous going
on. He added that he is persuaded that this is the best approach, because the
Village could still widely publicize its ordinance and post signs as a first
step in educating the public.
Trustee Eilers remarked on the fact that the City of Chicago enforces its
ordinance haphazardly, citing it as a weak example, whereas Highland Park’s
approach gets more to the problem. If Winnetka follows their lead, there is
reason to hope that a coalition can be built to further the cause throughout
the region. He said that in his best judgment the use of hand-held cell phones
should be a secondary offense.
Trustee Berger indicated that she would be in favor of secondary enforcement
because Winnetka is not an island and needs to work harder to figure out
creative, innovative ways to get our neighbors to join us. She added that she
would like to see the exception for hands-free devices eliminated from the
ordinance because there really is no difference between hands-free and hand-
held in terms of distraction.
Trustee Behles stated that the reason for enacting this ordinance is to enhance
public safety and a broader approach dealing with distracted driving is very
difficult to define. He said that he sees Highland Park’s approach as
relatively meaningless and predicted that it will have very little actual
impact. He indicated that he was in favor of primary enforcement and added that
if Winnetka is going to build a coalition, it should be based upon the Chicago
approach.
Trustee Berger inquired whether there would be support for deleting the hands-
free exception with secondary enforcement.
Trustee Behles said that he would not be in favor of banning all cell phones
because it would be too great a step.
Trustee Eilers indicated that he would consider banning all cell phones if
enforcement was secondary.
Trustee Abell agreed, saying that all cell phones should be treated in the same
manner.
Trustee Tucker indicated that if all cell phones were to be banned, she would
prefer secondary enforcement.
Trustee Ritchell said that he would not be in favor of striking the hands-free
exception provision.
President Woodbury commented that this ordinance is about safety and is a step
in the right direction. He said he was in favor of primary enforcement and
called for a motion to approve the ordinance as introduced.
Trustee Abell moved to delete Section 10.28.120(B)(2) excepting hands-free
devices and make the offense a secondary, and include staff’s recommendations
as to effective date, January 1, 2007, and omitting Section 10.28.120(C)
pertaining to fines. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion.
Manager Williams indicated that the Village Attorney would need to redraft the
ordinance to establish enforcement as secondary.
President Woodbury remarked that he believes that banning the use of all cell
phones would be moving way ahead of the community and what the Caucus wanted.
Trustee Abell suggested that more time is needed to consider all of the
information and the ramifications, and withdrew the motion. Trustee Eilers
agreed and withdrew his second.
Trustee Abell then moved to table the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Eilers. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, and
Tucker. Nays: Trustees Ritchell and Behles. Absent: None.
Attorney Janega indicated that she would bring back the proposed draft
ordinance, as well as two alternatives making the offense secondary, one
exempting hands-free devices and one encompassing all cell phones, for the
November 7th meeting.
Trustee Abell said that he would like to see an alternative dealing with
overall distracted driving.
Trustee Ritchell asked how many traffic violations over the last year resulted
in a citation. Chief DeLopez said that between October 1, 2005 and October 1,
2006, there were 4,092 violations that resulted in a traffic stop reported to
the State. He also reported that the City of Chicago issues approximately 23.5
tickets per district, per month for violations to their ordinance.
Trustee Eilers reiterated that even though the City’s offense is primary, it is
obvious that enforcement is haphazard at best.
President Woodbury remarked that this is a Winnetka matter, and it doesn’t
matter what Chicago does.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
a)   Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Ln., requested that the Trustees reconsider
the policy of holding Council meetings on the Tuesday of the General Election.
She read a letter she had written to the Pioneer Press and submitted letters of
support from local political party organizations.
President Woodbury explained that the Council had considered this request. He
observed that the polls are open from 6:00 a.m. to 7:00 p.m., so the Council
meeting doesn’t keep anyone from participating in the elections.
In the ensuing discussion, President Woodbury and other Council members stated
that Council members arrange their schedules to attend Council meetings on
Tuesdays, that some do not have the flexibility to reschedule them to meet on
Thursday, and that it is necessary for all Council members to attend meetings
to assure full discussion of the issues.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1481 and 1482. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items and answering questions from
the Trustees, he moved to approve Warrant List No. 1481 in the amount of
$1,300,019.99 and Warrant List No. 1482 in the amount of $509,835.39. Trustee
Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
b)   State Bid – Salt Purchase. Manager Williams explained that the Village
participates in the purchase of rock salt through the State of Illinois bid, a
joint purchasing program that provides municipalities the opportunity to take
advantage of joint purchasing power in order to realize cost savings. This year
the low bid for the program was submitted by North American Salt Company for
$40.68 per ton.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the purchase of
rock salt at $40.68 per ton from North American Salt through the State of
Illinois Cooperative purchasing program. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Water Rate Cost of Service Study. Manager Williams reviewed the results of
a recent cost of service study performed in response to a request from the Park
District to reduce its water rates for the golf course. He explained that the
purpose of the study was to determine whether each customer classification is
paying its fair share for water service as determined by calculating the
utility’s costs as compared to the rates paid by the customer class. This
calculation involves the application of accounting principles and making some
judgments regarding the assignment of various utility costs to the customer
classes. In general, the goal is to try to set the utility rates so that each
customer classification pays within 10% of its allocated costs.
Mr. Williams reported that the study concluded that the Indian Hill and Park
District golf courses are paying an appropriate amount for water service, but
identified three customer classifications that have a 10% difference between
the cost to serve the customer class and the amount they pay for service. These
classes are: (1) large residential, (2) commercial and (3) schools and
government. He reported that despite these findings, staff recommends that no
adjustments be made to the existing water rates because (1) the actual dollar
amount is relatively insignificant, (2) the Village would be required to modify
its flat rate design which would be contrary to the goals of water
conservation, and (3) our existing rate structure is easy to explain to
customers and easy to compare with rates in other villages.
It was the unanimous consensus of the Trustees to support staff’s
recommendation.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury reported on his attendance at a
recent Landmark Preservation Commission meeting at which he encouraged them to
come forward with new ideas to further their mission.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Tucker reported that she and her family had attended the
annual Fire Department Open House and found it to be a fun and informative
opportunity for her children to see the Public Safety Building, meet both fire
and police officers, and learn what they do.
She also reported on a recent meeting regarding the coastal management
initiative that might enable participating municipalities to obtain federal
funding for certain coastal projects within designated areas. She noted that
the Department of Natural Resources has recently joined with the Alliance for
the Great Lakes to form a new partnership to bring communities together to
discuss issues.
c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported to the Council on a recent request from
AT&T for an encroachment permit to allow it to place two large equipment boxes
on a concrete pad on the Elm Street parkway just west of Birch, to accommodate
installations for “Project LightSpeed,” a new communications package AT&T
marketing. She said that the Village staff has sent a letter denying the
request and added that staff would be meeting with AT&T to discuss the issues
involved.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Solid Waste Agency of Northern
Cook County, of which Winnetka is a member, is embarking on a comprehensive
strategic review of its operations with an eye toward driving costs down in
order to be competitive in the private marketplace. He said that eventually the
Council will need to decide whether the Village wants to continue to be a
member of SWANCC and under what conditions.
Trustee Abell said that he hopes the organization will come up with a better
system for disposing of household chemical waste.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, and personnel matters pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Abell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:26 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:27 p.m.
______________________________
Recording Secretary
November 7, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 5, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 7, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Assistant Director of
Community Development Brian Norkus and approximately 27 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury noted that an executive session would be held after item 6
(a), and the regular meeting would be reconvened at the conclusion of the
executive session. The Trustees approved the Agenda, as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   November 14, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 18, 2006, Special Study Session (Saturday). All of the Council
members present indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   November 21, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 17, 2006, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury asked the Council to
refer to a draft of the minutes showing suggested changes from Mr. Jeffrey Liss
and ascertained that the Council did not have any additional corrections or
additions. Trustee Tucker moved to approve the minutes as amended, seconded by
Trustee Abell. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2006 – Prohibiting Use of Hand-held Phones While
Driving - Adoption. Attorney Janega summarized the discussions held on this
issue at previous Council meetings and outlined several alternate drafts of the
ordinance, labeled Alternates A, B and C in the agenda materials, for the
Council to consider.
After hearing the opinions of the Chief of Police and several audience members,
President Woodbury asked for comments from the Trustees.
Trustees Ritchell, Tucker and Behles spoke in favor of the original amended
ordinance with primary enforcement, citing safety issues and overriding
evidence that the residents support an ordinance that deters cell phone use
while driving, with Trustee Behles also suggesting that primary enforcement is
the best way to educate the public.
Trustees Abell, Berger and Eilers spoke in favor of Alternate C, which would
prohibit the use of handheld and hands-free cell phones with secondary
enforcement, with Trustee Abell preferring to approach the issue as a public
education task, and with Trustee Berger commenting that passing a cell phone
ordinance without making it applicable to both hands-free and handheld is not
taking the issue sufficiently seriously and that she would agree to either
primary or secondary enforcement.
Trustee Eilers read a prepared statement which is attached to these minutes.
President Woodbury determined that three of the Trustees were in favor of the
original amended ordinance and three were in favor of Alternate C. He stated
that he would vote for the original ordinance as amended and that the Council
would revisit the ordinance in a year’s time.
Trustee Ritchell moved to amend the original ordinance, seconded by Trustee
Abell. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: Trustees Berger and Eilers.
Trustee Behles moved to adopt the amended ordinance as depicted in the agenda
materials, prohibiting the use of hand-held phones while driving with
enforcement as a primary offense, seconded by Trustee Abell. By roll call vote,
the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker and President
Woodbury. Nays: Trustees Abell, Berger, and Eilers.
Executive Session. Trustee Abell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for
the purpose of discussing pending and probable litigation pursuant to Section 2
(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:18 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine
Janega, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus.
6. Ordinances and Resolutions (Continued).
Court Reporter Martina Manzo, CSR, Victoria Court Reporting Service, was in
attendance at the request of the attorneys for CBI subdivision.
b)   Resolution No. R-25-2006 – Approval of CBI Subdivision at 630 Pine
Lane.  Note: Resolution number R-24-2006 corrected to R-25-2006 due to error in
numbering sequence.
Attorney Janega reviewed the resolution and each of the conditions incorporated
pursuant to the Plan Commission’s concerns and previous Council discussions. In
response to a question from Trustee Eilers concerning the approval of tree
removals, Ms. Janega stated that the Council could require the developer to
come before the Council for approval of the trees not depicted on the current
plans. In response to a question from Trustee Berger, she also said that draft
resolution requires the builder to complete repairs to the landmark house and
obtain a certificate of appropriateness before building permits will be issued.
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus responded to questions
from the Trustees regarding gross floor area calculations and structural
repairs to the existing house, and explained that many of the details of the
development will be addressed in the development agreement.
The attorney for CBI Builders, Joann Angarola, noted a correction to the
comparison tables in the agenda materials and asked when Mr. Marlowe, the
developer, would know when he can close up the house on Lot 2 to get the pipes
repaired.
Ms. Janega replied that Mr. Marlow only has to submit an application to
Community Development to be able to do plumbing work.
Mr. Westbrook, 1160 Pelham, asked the Council about a promised analysis of a
two-lot subdivision versus the proposed 3-lot subdivision, saying that he
thought the developer was just paying lip service to this and there had been no
good faith effort to make an analysis.
Ms. Janega replied that the developer had cited economic reasons as to why this
would not be done.
President Woodbury added that the Council has to deal with what is submitted
and the developer has applied for a 3-lot subdivision.
Mr. Westbrook then asked about the status of rotating the building on Lot 3.
President Woodbury replied that he recalled from previous discussions that the
conclusion was that rotating the house would cause the removal of more trees,
which would not be desirable.
Trustee Behles added that the developer had demonstrated that if the building
on Lot 3 was rotated, it would move significantly east and violate the setback,
so that it would not be in compliance with the Zoning Ordinance. He said that
this would also occur if the house was moved to the north.
Trustee Tucker commented that the Trustees felt that after the discussion on
this issue, there was nothing further to discuss since moving the house would
violate setbacks.
Mr. Westbrook said he thought this could have been given more attention and
asked that he be showed how the trees are depicted and the detail of the new
plantings.
Fred Wilson, architect for CBI, spent several minutes showing the neighbors the
drawings of the plantings to be used for screening. He also said that they had
talked to the neighbors about drainage and the rotation of the house and
explained that this would have removed six more trees and forced the
demolishing of the historic home. He then agreed to additional screening for
the neighbors at their request.
Mrs. Marlene Baumgarten, 645 Hibbard Road, also requested additional screening
for her property and said she was concerned that some of her trees would be
killed by the digging.
Ms. Janega offered to add the suggestions of the neighbors for additional
screening to the Development Agreement. She explained that there were no
screenings on the south side of the property because the area is for
compensatory storage and so trees cannot be planted on it.
President Woodbury suggested that the best way to deal with Mrs. Baumgarten’s
request would be for the developer to meet with her to try to improve the
screening for her property.
Andy Block, 644 Pine Lane, expressed concern about the size of development, its
proximity to his lot lines and the size of the addition to the landmark house.
He was also concerned about three structures on the property affecting the
character of the neighborhood, being exposed to construction work for the next
three years and construction vehicle access. He thought that the resolution
should hold the developer accountable for future damages caused by
construction. He hoped that the Council would implement protection for the
neighbors because of the history of irresponsibility with CBI.
Attorney Janega explained that the land subdivision and the actual development
are two different issues and that what is before the Council is the
subdivision. She said that the issues Mr. Block raised are addressed during
construction and in the development agreement, which is still to be negotiated.
The permitting process handles construction details and the development
agreement works through such issues as how the neighborhood will be protected
during construction, the wear and tear on Pine Lane from construction vehicles
and the protection of trees. She added that conditions will be put in place
that require the protection of the Clark house before any other development
occurs, so that the goal of preserving that house can actually be met. The goal
here is for the Council to focus on the particular development and the
resolution addresses the concerns of the subdivision and what can be done under
the subdivision ordinance.
Manager Williams commented that the staff and the Council are very aware of
these types of issues and they are handled by the development agreement.
President Woodbury added that development agreements on residential
developments are rare, but the Council has begun to require them in the larger
building areas for the protection of the neighborhood.
Mr. Block then asked about his request to limit the addition on the existing
home to the current footprint because the proposed addition is close to his
lot. He added that according to the Village web site, the Council approves the
Landmark Preservation Committee’s recommendations.
President Woodbury replied that when he had discussed this with Mr. Block, he
had said that the horse may already be out of the barn, and according to what
is in front of the Council today, the horse is out of the barn, unless the
developer decides to change it. He said that making the home a certified
landmark raises the bar.
Attorney Janega said that landmark designation does not prohibit additions and
that there are many changes that can be made to a landmarked house consistent
with Department of Interior standards and the Village Code, but there are
standards for appearance, etc. She said it is not the Village’s place to
anticipate the future and there is no basis in the record that allows the
Village to restrict the footprint of the house.
Mr. Norris Love, 1175 Pelham, asked what recourse the neighbors have if the
construction causes flooding and kills trees.
Attorney Janega observed that flooding at 1175 Pelham due to construction is
not likely because it is uphill from the property.
President Woodbury also said that he would answer that the grades and the
drainage are going away from Mr. Love’s house.
Mr. Norkus added that the Village requires as-built drawings for review and
approval of all improvements to make sure that water goes in a southwesterly
direction, although he couldn’t guarantee that there wouldn’t be a problem.
Manager Williams replied that, if the engineer’s recommendations don’t work,
then Mr. Love’s ultimate recourse is to go to court, but the Village would make
its best effort to correct problems. He said that the Village has excellent
engineers on staff.
Mr. Westbrook requested that the landscaping plan be corrected to include the
additional trees on the east side of the property that the architect agreed to
during the meeting.
President Woodbury assured Mr. Westbrook that it would be in the minutes.
Trustee Eilers asked that the Council revisit construction access because of
issues created by how the lots are situated. He expressed concern that
construction traffic off Pine Lane would be very disruptive to the neighbors
and that the police and fire department might prefer access during construction
off Hibbard. If Lot 1 were developed and sold off first, without a construction
easement remaining in place, then construction traffic for Lots 2 and 3 would
be forced onto Pine Lane, to the detriment of the neighbors. Acknowledging that
this is an issue that can be addressed in the construction agreement, he asked
if the developer could agree at this point that all construction traffic would
avoid Pine Lane, if that is the objective of the Village, so that this is not
an issue for negotiation during the construction easement process.
Attorney Janega said that she thought another concern is that should one of the
lots be sold off for development, the builder would need an access for that
development. She emphasized that construction access requirements will be
included in the development agreement, would cover all three lots and will not
only bind the initial parties, but their successors and assigns.
Trustee Behles said he would be reluctant to decide on construction access
before studying the trees that would be along the access route.
Mr. Marlowe said that he would agree to keep construction traffic off of Pine
Lane, depending on what is being traveled over and after consulting with the
Village Forester. He said that the house on the lot at Hibbard Road will be
developed last and that there will be a staging plan for parking vehicles.
Trustee Behles moved to adopt Resolution R-25-2006, as amended, accepting and
approving a Plat of Subdivision at 630 Pine Line, seconded by Trustee Eilers.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Woodbury called for a motion to introduce M-20-2006, establishing the
residence at 630 Pine Lane as a designated landmark, and explained that the
Council may also find it in the Village’s interests to waive introduction and
adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Abell moved to waive introduction, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By roll
call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adopt M-20-2006, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By roll
call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public comment and questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1483 and 1484. Trustee Ritchell reported there were no
unusual items on the first warrant list and moved to approve Warrant List 1483
in the amount of $970,285.78, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By roll call vote,
the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Ritchell then noted various items on the next warrant list and moved to
approve Warrant List 1484 in the amount of $799,472.38, seconded by Trustee
Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Capital budget status reports. Manager Williams reported to the Council on
the use of funds budgeted for capital improvements, commenting specifically on
public works, water & electric and post office committee items.
10)   Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the Streetscape Committee has had
its first meeting, but has nothing to report as yet.
Trustee Ritchell reported that the Ad Hoc Historic & Residential Preservation
Committee has met and is asking people to share experiences.
b)   Attorney. Ms. Janega reported that trial court’s judgment for the Village
in Bartnell v. Village of Winnetka was affirmed by the Appellate Court.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:35 p.m.
         
Deputy Clerk
November 14, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 5, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 14, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Village
Finance Director Ed McKee, Assistant Finance Director Hanna Sullivan, and
approximately 15 persons in the audience.
2)   Review Comprehensive Annual Financial Report.
Finance Director Ed McKee introduced the Village’s auditors, Lou Karrison and
Janet Matthys, from Sikich, LLC. Mr. Karrison gave an overview of the auditors’
annual report and answered questions from the Council. He reported that Sikich
gives the Village its highest level of assurance that the report contains
standards for the GFOA Certificate of Excellence.
Mr. Williams pointed out that the electric fund has made a significant
turnaround this fiscal year and that staff has worked hard to ensure that the
Village is reimbursed correctly. He noted that Sikich’s method for reporting
depreciation on assets brought important information to light which will impact
management’s decisions with regard to investment in the Village’s Capital
Improvements.
3)   Review Auditor’s Management Letter.
Mr. McKee asked the Council members if they had any concerns about issues
raised in the management letter.
In answer to a question raised about the custodial credit risks mentioned in
the letter, Mr. Karrison explained that the auditors recommend the Village
update its investment policy to include the Village’s position regarding
exposure where funds are held by a third party.
Mr. McKee observed that the Village is scheduled to take a look at its
investment policy in late 2007, but that recent legislation passed by the State
needs to be clarified before that discussion can take place. He noted that
Sikich had no concerns about the security of the investments, and that the
auditors would be consulted for input when the Village reviews its investment
policy.
In response to a request for elaboration on the future accounting
pronouncements, Mr. Karrison recommended that the Village hire an actuary to
help with compliance on the new GASB reporting requirements for post-employee
benefits. Mr. McKee said that he has already had preliminary discussions with
an actuary to help calculate the imputed value of the health insurance.
Mr. Karrison assured the Council that the other GASB standards mentioned in
Statements 46, 47, and 48 would have no impact on the Village.
4)   Tree Preservation Code Policy Issues.
Members of the Forestry Commission in attendance included Chair Mary Hill,
Susie Shabica, Anne Wilder and Bernie Hammer. Director of Public Works Steve
Saunders reviewed the background of the tree preservation code amendment
process. He reported that at the September 5, 2006, Council meeting, the matter
was continued to give staff a chance to clarify several questions with the
Forestry Commission. He said staff had prepared a detailed report for the
Commission that identified seven open issues, and also provided a detailed
discussion and recommendation on each issue. The Forestry Commission discussed
this information at its October 11, 2006, meeting.
Mr. Saunders indicated that with regard to heritage trees, both staff and the
Forestry Commission recommend that the current draft definition be used. He
observed that he had reviewed private tree removal permit applications for 2004
and that of 305 trees removed that year, possibly 35 to 45 would have qualified
as heritage trees.
Mr. Saunders elaborated on some of the other issues included in the draft
amendments, including fines related to heritage trees, notice to neighbors, the
tree preservation and protection plan, and adjusting the regulations depending
on lot size. Finally, he directed Council’s attention to the draft ordinance
contained in their agenda packets.
In response to a question about how much staff time would be taken up if the
proposed amendments pertaining to heritage trees were passed, Mr. Saunders
estimated that it could be 5 to 6 staff hours per tree removal application.
In answer to the question of whether the process would help to save many of the
proposed heritage trees, Mr. Saunders replied that there would not be an
absolute prohibition on removing heritage trees, and that in his opinion, it
was possible that only a few would actually have been saved. He indicated that
it would provide another level of scrutiny before a tree is removed, but if
there is a difficulty or a hardship, the tree would come down. He noted that at
best it could be a tool to use to work through issues with builders.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega directed the Council’s attention to a
discussion guide she had distributed, outlining the seven issues that had been
identified as a result of the heritage tree concept. She observed that the
bottom line policy decision involved whether or not the Council wished to
proceed with the heritage tree designation concept.
The Council discussed the application process and the issue of fines. Attorney
Janega assured the Council that there was sound legal basis to enact the tree
protection legislation, dating back to the Village’s 1869 charter and
Winnetka’s first tree protection ordinance, passed in 1923. She noted that such
ordinances have been upheld across the country and are not viewed as regulatory
takings.
Ms. Hill remarked that the proposed amended ordinance is similar to other tree
protection measures that have been enacted in other communities. She announced
that she had circulated petitions in favor of the proposed amendments to the
tree protection ordinance, and that the response had been very positive. In
answer to a question about how the fines compared to those in other
communities, Ms. Hill replied that they are in line with what has been done
elsewhere.
Ms. Wilder said that she had also circulated a petition urging the Council to
adopt the proposed tree preservation amendments, and estimated that a very
diverse group of people had signed. She handed over more petitions that had
been collected.
Todd Eagan, 892 Oak, spoke against the proposed amendments, saying that the
Caucus survey responses two years ago had favored leaving the ordinance as is.
He added that he felt it infringed on the owners’ property rights.
Crispin Hales, 1000 Sheridan Road, voiced his support for the proposed
amendments, saying that the people cutting down trees are more interested in
making money than in the character of the Village.
Ron White, 434 Berkeley, said he believed the proposed amendments are an
emotional response to the problem, and that the high fines would discourage
people from pruning so that the trees will become diseased.
Judy Oberman, 468 Ash, said that it seemed to her the goal was more about
trying to prevent new construction than saving heritage trees, and she was
currently more concerned with Ash trees. She observed that the builders could
afford the penalties, but smaller homeowners would be most impacted by the
delays and fees.
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce, agreed that the proposed amendments would cause delays
of between 4 ½ to 7 months in construction after time is factored in for the
Forestry Commission’s hearings, their appeals process and the maximum delay
from the Building Review Committee’s hearings. He also warned against the
notice provisions because it would cause tensions among neighbors, and added
that some lots are just not large enough to accommodate all of the replacement
trees.
Kay Hilarides, 1446 Edgewood, expressed support for the proposed amendments and
urged the Council not to let a few negative voices affect their decision.
Joyce Frank, 1092 Spruce, warned that if the proposed amendments were passed,
it would make it more difficult for people to sell their homes, and the extra
regulations would turn the Village Forester into a policeman.
In the ensuing discussion, the Trustees debated whether the extra time and
layer of process that both the homeowner and staff would go through would
result in saving many trees. There was no consensus for moving forward with the
proposed heritage tree designation, with Trustees Tucker and Ritchell in favor,
Trustees Behles and Abell against and Trustees Berger and Eilers undecided.
Manager Williams observed that the Council could further revise the proposed
amendments by changing size of the heritage tree, or removing the notice
provision. He noted that the Forestry Commission had taken an important step in
the past year in requiring chain-link fences to protect trees at construction
sites. In addition, the Village’s inspectors now issue stop work orders when
they see a tree being harmed at a construction site.
President Woodbury indicated that he thought the Council needed more input from
the Caucus. He suggested a question be put on the next Caucus survey to give
more perspective about what the community thinks with regard to tree
preservation.
In answer to a question about whether the other proposed amendments could be
implemented without the heritage tree designation, Attorney Janega replied in
the affirmative. All of the Trustees were in favor of moving ahead with the
other provisions in the amended ordinance.
Manager Williams acknowledged that there is a natural tension between property
rights and the community and the decision to go to the Caucus with the question
of heritage trees was a good one.
5)   Preliminary Discussion: Tax Levy.
Mr. McKee reported that the Village is in good fiscal health and that a modest
tax increase approximately equal to the rate of inflation should ensure that
operating costs and capital needs can be adequately funded. He indicated that
based on the inflation rate of 3.4%, staff recommends a total property tax
increase of between 3.4% and 4.9%.
Manager Williams pointed out the proposed increase would still keep the Village
under the non-home rule tax cap and that the rate is lower than the school
districts, due to diversified revenue sources and personnel cuts.
In discussing the raise in the rate, some Trustees favored the higher figure
because of ongoing projects such as streetscape, a possible new parking
structure, train station rehabilitation and the post office redevelopment.
Others favored the minimum amount. A consensus was reached that 4.25% would be
an agreeable figure.
Mr. McKee noted that the tax levy ordinance would come before the Council in
December.
6)   Discussion: Audit Contract.
Mr. McKee and the Council had a discussion about the Village’s recently
completed audit.
7)   Discussion: Review of Monthly Financial Statement.
Manager Williams reported that although the Council has considered the idea of
forming an Audit/Finance Committee, his recommendation is to assign two
Trustees to meet regularly with Finance Director Ed McKee to discuss the
financial statements. They would then report back to the Council to discuss the
statements as a committee of the whole, similar to what is done with the
warrant lists.
President Woodbury agreed, saying that it would be in keeping with the
Council’s responsibility. He observed that the Council would benefit from more
information from Mr. McKee.
Manager Williams suggested that the financial issues could be brought to a
Study Session every six months or so, to keep everyone informed.
8)    Manager’s Report.
None.
9)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
10)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:55 p.m.
   ______________________________
   Recording Secretary
November 18, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 5, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Fire Department on Saturday, November 18, 2006,
at 8:30 a.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order in the Conference Room at 8:30
a.m. Present: Trustees David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb
Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Deputy Fire Chief Steve Laureys, and no
persons in the audience.
After brief comments, the Chief and the Deputy Chief showed the Trustees the
training and certification board to give them an idea of the numerous training
programs, both required and elective, that firefighters must complete. Chief
Smith explained that because of program scheduling it can take up to 3.5 years
for a new recruit to be fully certified.
The group moved on to the equipment bay to view the Village’s vehicles and the
apparatus thereon. The Chief explained that the amount of equipment and the
number protocols with which a firemedic has to be familiar has increased
exponentially over the years.
The Trustees then traveled to the training tower at the Village Yards for a
live demonstration of a structure fire. The demonstration showed the deployment
of first response firefighters and equipment at the scene and their initial
responsibilities before automatic aid companies arrive. These responsibilities
include among other things establishing command, securing the water supply,
setting up engine pumping, activities, forcible entry, search and rescue,
ventilation. He explained that current staffing levels call for six
firefighter/paramedics to be on each shift, and the presentation illustrated
how all six individuals are required for the initial attack on a fire. Chief
Smith emphasized that training and experience are key to efficiency and safety.
2)   Comprehensive Strategic Budget Review.
Chief Smith presented a PowerPoint presentation reviewing the current staffing
levels, operations, and responsibilities in comparison to those in existence in
1976. He explained that the Fire Department has become the “all threat” default
responder in modern society and noted that the Winnetka Fire Department
responds to approximately 2,400 calls annually for incidents ranging from fires
and medical emergencies to lock-outs and lock-ins.
Chief Smith went on to present an overview of the Department’s staffing,
training, and equipment needs for the future and addressed challenges facing
the Department, such as changes in technology and impending retirements. He
discussed the Department’s strengths, weaknesses, and opportunities, as well as
potential alternative scenarios under more or less funding. Finally, the Chief
talked about the Department’s participation in consolidated fire efforts and
obstacles to possible future joint ventures.
Manager Williams explained that staffing levels drive the budget and are the
core decision that must be made about the future. He recommended that in view
of the approaching retirement of a number of existing senior officers, the
Council authorize the hiring of a transitional fire fighter to ensure that the
Village has sufficient trained personnel to maintain appropriate staffing
levels.
The Chief then responded to questions from the Trustees about programs,
activities, responsibilities and needs.
3)   Discussion: Sprinklers in Storage Occupancies.
Chief Smith reviewed a request to amend the Village Code to specify that
storage occupancies are required to be sprinklered. He emphasized that
sprinklers are the first line of defense against a fire spreading; they hold
the fire in check and control the extent of damage. He noted that this change
would have no impact on current businesses. There was a general consensus among
the Trustees to proceed with the proposed amendment.
A brief discussion ensued about the desirability of residential sprinklers in
some new construction, especially larger homes. Manager Williams indicated that
the matter would be brought back to the Council for consideration.
4)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 12:10 a.m.
   ______________________________
   Recording Secretary
November 21, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 5, 2006)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 19, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:41 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Acting Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, and 2
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
At the request of the Village President, Item 9(e), Auditor Discussion, was
added to the agenda so the Council could discuss the Village’s auditing firm.
The Council unanimously approved the agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   December 5, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Berger, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   December 12, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-21-2006 – Approving Withdrawal of Members from IMEA -
Introduction. Manager Williams reported that the Illinois Municipal Electric
Agency (IMEA) has decided that it would be in the best interests of the Agency
if the membership was made up of only those municipalities that are purchasing
power from the Agency. He said the idea is to avoid a potential conflict
between the members that have a financial stake in the IMEA and those that do
not.
Manager Williams explained that the process for withdrawing from the Agency
requires a majority of all members to pass an ordinance approving the
withdrawal of the specified members. Therefore, the Village is being asked to
pass an ordinance allowing the Villages of Albany, Allendale and Riverton, and
the Cities of Batavia, Geneseo, Geneva, McLeansboro, Rochelle and Springfield
to withdraw from the IMEA.
Trustee Berger moved to introduce the ordinance, seconded by Trustee Behles. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Resolution R-26-2006 – Project Shield, Phase II - Adoption. Attorney
Janega reported that on July 5, 2005, the Village Council adopted a resolution
authorizing the execution of an intergovernmental agreement with Cook County to
allow the Village to participate in “Project SHIELD,” funded by the Department
of Homeland Security. She noted that under that initial agreement, the Winnetka
Police Department was equipped with an interoperable communications system for
first responders.
Attorney Janega announced that the County has now embarked on what it has
designated as Phase II of Project SHIELD. She indicated that Phase II allocates
funds for the installation of inter-operable camera systems at the six suburban
Cook County Lake Michigan water plants. She observed that this First Amendment
to the original agreement adequately covers the Phase II project and does not
expose the Village to unknown and unidentified future expenses or liabilities.
Attorney Janega added that the agreement included a two year warranty and
maintenance period at no cost to the Village, and if the Village does not want
the equipment after that time, it can return it. In answer to a question about
maintenance after the period is up, Attorney Janega replied that there is no
way to gauge what those costs might be.
After a brief discussion, the Council decided that the only practical option
was to accept the equipment for the two-year period.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution R-22-2006,
authorizing execution of the First Amendment to the Cook County Mobile Video
and Data Communication Network Participation Agreement substantially in the
form submitted. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1485 and 1486. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1485 in the amount of $1,281,191.07 and Warrant List No. 1486 in the
amount of $312,907.99. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Village Hall Architectural Services. Public Works Director Steve Saunders
reported that the Village Capital Improvements Plan includes $60,000 for an
architectural study to determine the scope of work necessary for architectural
restoration improvements to Village Hall. He noted that Village Hall was
designed by Edwin Clark and is a designated local landmark.
Mr. Saunders observed that the facility remains essentially unchanged since its
construction in 1925, although a number of renovations have occurred over the
years. He added that the building does not meet the Village’s needs or modern
expectations in the areas of comfort, fire safety and technology, and is
suffering from a considerable amount of deferred maintenance.
Mr. Saunders indicated that Staff had developed a Request for Proposals
outlining the general scope of work which addresses all aspects of the current
building and which also requests the development of a program of recommended
improvements and their cost estimates.
Mr. Saunders announced that based upon the proposals and interview results, he
recommended YAS Architecture as the best-qualified firm to meet the Village’s
needs for this project. In answer to a question about why the Village chose the
highest bid of the three that were looked at, Mr. Saunders replied that the
firm was not only well-respected, but took the extra step of including a
nationally known historical architecture firm from Chicago as part of their
team. He added that their vision of the project seemed the most congruent with
what the Village hopes to accomplish, and they capture the modernization
process the best.
After some discussion about specific terms of the contract, the Trustees agreed
that the contract should be awarded.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize the proposed
contract with YAS Architecture of Evanston to complete a Building Modernization
and Restoration Plan for the Village Hall, for a fee amount not to exceed
$43,525, plus standard reimbursables. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   2009 Village Vehicle Sticker Design. Finance Director Ed McKee reported
that the Village has received a request from the Children’s Theatre of Winnetka
to be featured on the 2009 vehicle sticker to commemorate their 35th
anniversary. He noted that the Village has authorized other such designs on the
Village vehicle sticker for other special occasions. He observed that the
artwork will be supplied to the Village at no cost, and there are no additional
costs associated with the special designs. Mr. McKee noted that the vehicle
sticker design is a nice way to get community organizations a little publicity,
but cautioned that the Council should not go too far in advance with awarding
the designs.
Trustee Abell requested that the community be advised of the opportunity to be
recognized on the annual Village sticker and that organizations not be allowed
to lock up future designs which might inhibit wider participation. Manager
Williams said that this topic would be mentioned in the Winnetka Report and a
process developed to notify the community and handle applications in a fair
manner.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize the Children’s
Theatre of Winnetka logo as the design for the 2009 Village vehicle sticker. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Post Office Committee Minutes. Manager Williams reported that the Post
Office Committee has been reviewing the economic analyses of the different
development scenarios, to get a more thorough understanding of the factors
involved in redeveloping the Post Office site. He announced that the Committee
will continue to examine the various development scenarios at its December and
January meetings. He added that he anticipates that in February or March the
Committee expects to have a series of meetings inviting public input on the
scenarios, but the details are still being worked out. He predicted that in the
Spring the Committee would start writing their report, possibly to be finished
by early summer.
President Woodbury observed that he thought the Committee should make
recommendations at different budget levels. Manager Williams noted that he
would share President Woodbury’s comment with Chairman D’Esposito.
e)   Discussion – Auditor. Finance Director Ed McKee reported that the Village
has received a proposal from the accounting firm of Lauterbach & Amen, LLP. He
indicated that while the Village was pleased with the finished product of the
audit done by Sikich, there were some concerns about the process.
Mr. McKee observed that after talking with a partner at Lauterbach & Amen, he
feels a high level of confidence that the audit report next year will be
finished on deadline. In response to a question from Trustee Berger, he said
that, while it is not absolutely necessary to vote now, the Village should move
fairly quickly because Lauterbach could potentially get tied up with other
commitments.
The Trustees had a short discussion and, while acknowledging that the situation
was unusual, reached a consensus that the Village should change auditors.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to accept the proposal from
Lauterbach & Amen. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury thanked staff for the excellent Fire
Department review at the Village Yards on the previous Saturday, saying that
the presentation was very informational.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Abell announced he was proud of the fact that the New
Trier soccer team won the state championship. He also reported that he attended
the Northwest Municipal Conference meeting and they have hired an actuary who
is on call for municipalities in the event of more surprise legislation such as
the DROP program. He said that he would like to mention Winnetka’s cell phone
ban at the Conference’s next meeting and to ask for support from other towns in
order to develop a broader consensus. He asked for a memo outlining the
rationale for the hand-held ban, so he could bring it to the meeting with him.
c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that the State legislature had passed
an amendment to the Open Meetings Act, effective January 1, 2007, which allows
members of committees to participate by audio/video conferencing. She explained
that all of the subordinate bodies of the Village will have that option as
well, unless the Council amends the enabling ordinances that exercise that
power. She said that staff would be putting together a package of alternatives
and policy implications for the next Council meeting to help them to decide if
it would allow electronic attendance at Council meetings, and whether any of
the Council’s committees and any of the Village’s boards and commissions should
have that authority.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the DROP legislation will not be
introduced in the veto session. With regard to the Emerald Ash Borer invasion,
he indicated that the Department of Agriculture is considering ordering all of
the Ash trees in the quarantine zone to be taken down. He said the cost is
estimated to be in the area of $500,000 per half square mile, which would put
Winnetka’s share at around $750,000. He noted that next years’ budget will have
a contingency built in to cover the amount. In response to a question about
property owners who don’t have the means to take down their trees, Manager
Williams responded that perhaps the Village could share its volume discounts
with vendors or offer tax write-offs. He said the issue would need further
study.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council convened into Executive Session
at 8:36 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:00 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:02 p.m.
