Recording Secretary
December 5, 2006
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 16, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 5, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Public Works Director Steven Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee, Fire Chief
Scott Smith, Interim Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, Village Forester
Jim Stier and approximately seven persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 6(c), Approving Withdrawal of Members
from IMEA, has been tabled until the December 19th meeting. The Trustees
approved the Agenda, as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   December 12, 2006, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   December 19, 2006, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 10, 2006, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   November 7, 2006, Regular Meeting. Attorney Janega reviewed a red-lined
copy of the relevant pages showing changes suggested by Trustee Eilers. There
being no additional corrections, Trustee Eilers moved to approve the minutes as
amended, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   November 14, 2006, Study Session. President Woodbury referred the Trustees
to a red-lined copy of the relevant pages showing changes suggested by Trustee
Eilers. There being no additional corrections, Trustee Behles moved to approve
the minutes as amended, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
d)   November 18, 2006, Special Study Session. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by
Trustee Behles, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
e)   November 21, 2006, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury referred the
Trustees to a red-lined copy of the relevant pages showing changes suggested by
Trustee Abell. There being no additional corrections, Trustee Abell moved to
approve the minutes as amended, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-24-2006 – Zoning Variation: 127 Church Road -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a variation from the
Intensity of Use of Lot provisions of the Village Code to permit the
installation of a circular driveway in the front yard of a newly constructed
home. He explained that the proposed circular driveway would expand upon an
existing front yard parking area that has the capacity to park one car. The
proposed driveway would result in an increase in front yard lot coverage of
148.89 s.f., or 14.8%. He pointed out that pursuant to regulations adopted in
April, 2004, prior to the applicant’s purchase of the property, front yard lot
coverage calculations include all of the area between the outer edges of the
driveway. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the applicant’s plans call for the new
portion of the drive to be constructed using a grass paver system but added
that no bonuses are given for permeable surfaces in the required front yard. He
noted that there is another driveway on the property located along the south
side of the lot that attaches to the garage at the rear of the house.
Mr. D’Onofrio went on to explain that the original building permit for the new
home, issued in March, 2005, only called for the single driveway along the
south side of the property; it did not call for the front yard driveway or
parking pad. A separate driveway permit for a circular driveway was made in the
fall of 2005. This request was denied because it would have resulted in the
removal of a parkway tree. Revised plans were later submitted and approved
allowing for construction of a parking pad in the front yard.
Mr. D’Onofrio added that the applicant is also seeking relief from the Village
Code requiring that any driveway be a minimum of 10 feet away from any public
tree. The proposed circular driveway calls for a portion of the drive to be set
back six (6) feet from a 24-inch Oak tree located in the public right-of-way.
He stated that the Village Forester had commented that this placement would be
detrimental to the tree. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that a second large Oak tree on
the private property would also be impacted.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 4 to 2 to
recommend approval of the requested front yard lot coverage variation but
pointed out that the Zoning Board of Appeals has no jurisdiction over the Oak
tree, which is not a zoning issue.
Trustee Abell stated that the Zoning Board’s considerations appeared to focus
on safety, but noted that there are stop signs both north and south of the
subject property. He asked if there was a policy of trying to discourage curb
cuts.
Mr. D’Onofrio replied that the Village Code requires a minimum lot width for a
second curb cut.
Attorney Janega added that this requirement was intended to impose a
restriction on circular driveways on smaller lots, where they have a larger
impact.
President Woodbury commented that despite the stop signs, this is a highly
trafficked street.
Mr. D’Onofrio responded to questions about the proposed paver system.
Trustee Eilers indicated that he was sensitive to the traffic issue but pointed
out the property already appears to have two locations to turn a car around.
Mr. D’Onofrio confirmed this observation. He stated that the problem is
greatest for delivery trucks and individuals who are not familiar with the
configuration of the driveway.
Manager Williams remarked that there is a steep grade differential between the
front and the back.
Trustee Behles asked whether the front pavement near the property line permits
a three-point turn. Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that it does.
Trustee Tucker commented that the purpose of the April, 2004, amendment was to
address concerns raised by the community about front lot coverage. She asked
whether in Mr. D’Onofrio’s opinion the applicant could have developed a
conforming design to address their concerns in a different way. Mr. D’Onofrio
indicated that when a developer starts with a clean slate, it is nearly always
possible to design a conforming house.
President Woodbury asked the Village Forester to comment on the grass paver
system. Mr. Stier stated that even though the pavers are porous, installation
requires the area to be excavated. He explained that because most tree roots
are in the top 18” of soil and the goal is to protect the 10 ft. area of the
drip line, this system impacts the well-being of the tree. He said that in his
professional opinion, the installation of the circular driveway as designed
would be detrimental to the nearby Oak trees and could cause their failure to
thrive.
Attorney David Grossberg, 773 Prospect, and Eric Wefing, a representative of
the builder, spoke on behalf of the applicant, stressing safety concerns for
visitors and residents and the difficulty of finding a conforming design given
the slope of the yard. Mr. Grossberg distributed a photograph of the existing
house and a computer rendering of the site with the proposed circular driveway.
Trustee Tucker asked Mr. Grossberg what changed with respect to the property
between the time the original plans were submitted and the present, and why the
applicant didn’t take all of the site variables into account when originally
designing the home.
Mr. Grossberg indicated he was not certain that the architect was aware of the
April, 2004, changes to the Zoning Ordinance with regard to front yard lot
coverage and added that driveways are often a part of the landscape plans and
not part of the original house plans. He reiterated that the changes were made
for safety’s sake. He added that the only other option for providing adequate
turnaround space would have been paving the backyard, which is not generally
considered to be a desirable alternative.
Manager Williams asked whether the developer’s decision to request the
variation is based upon marketability. Mr. Grossberg responded that it does
have an impact on salability and that potential buyers had expressed concern.
The Trustees discussed possible ways in which the existing site could be
altered without adding the circular driveway.
President Woodbury pointed out that this request came to the Council from the
Zoning Board with a positive recommendation and expressed his opinion that in
circumstances such as this one, circular drives are frequently used. He noted
that this variation is demonstrative of the way in which concerns compete with
one another. While the fundamental issue is zoning, it impacts tree
preservation.
Trustee Eilers said that he was not certain the grass paver system would be
adequate to avoid damaging the Oak trees.
Trustee Behles voiced his belief that the matter has to be viewed as new
construction and had it come before the Zoning Board as such, the vote would
have been different. He indicated that he believed modifications could be made
to the existing site to meet the need for safe ingress and egress while still
conforming to the requirements of the Zoning Ordinance. He said he could not
support this request.
Trustees Berger, Eilers, Abell, Tucker, and Ritchell concurred.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to deny the requested
variation. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2006 – Amendments to Tree Preservation Code –
Introduction. Members of the Forestry Commission in attendance included Mary
Hill, Chair, Susie Shabica, Anne Wilder, and John Weber.
Mr. Saunders noted the lengthy legislative history of the proposed amendments,
then summarized the discussions at the November 14th Study Session, at which
time staff was directed to remove the proposed Heritage Tree provisions and to
proceed with the other proposed amendments.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amended language. She and Mr. Saunders then
responded to questions from the Trustees and the audience.
Trustee Abell inquired as to why the provisions regarding tree protection prior
to and following construction are limited to the construction site itself and
do not pertain to public or adjoining property.
Mr. Saunders responded that the matter had been discussed by the Forestry
Commission, but they concluded that to do so might further stress neighborly
relations that might already be strained because of the construction project
itself.
Trustee Ritchell asked who pays for the fine and removal of a public tree that
is harmed during construction.
Mr. Saunders replied that in practice the cost comes out of the permit bond.
Mary Hill, Chairperson of the Forestry Commission, thanked the Council for
their time and consideration and for the previously accepted amendments. She
indicated that the Commission will continue to challenge the Council to protect
the Village’s trees, which are so much a part of Winnetka’s heritage and
character.
President Woodbury commented that the issue of Heritage Trees had been tabled
with the recommendation that the Caucus include a question related thereto in
its annual questionnaire.
Trustee Eilers raised a question regarding the provision that a builder might
have to replace trees if the Director determines there is a street void in the
public parkway or sidewalk area within 250 feet of the subject property.
Mr. Saunders explained that the intent of this provision is to take advantage
of an opportunity to enhance the forestation of the area.
Attorney Janega added that this stipulation has been in the ordinance from the
beginning and that the language was amended to mirror language which has become
the norm for land use provisions, because the most immediate impact from
construction is within 250 feet of a particular property.
Manager Williams clarified that this only pertains if a developer is unable to
replace the required number of trees on the subject lot.
Mr. Eilers remarked that he has no issue with a developer being responsible for
the subject property, but expressed concern about the principle of making the
developer responsible for an area over which he has no control.
Trustee Behles commented that while this sounds like a potential liability for
the developer, it is typically a very small payment of tree inches but is not
appropriate.
Mr. Woodbury said that the intent is to fill an existing void after replacement
trees are planted.
Trustee Tucker indicated that she was not bothered by this provision. She
observed that this is just one more step to bring consistency to the Village
Code, allowing flexibility and strengthening protection. She noted that it
could be reviewed and revised at any time.
Trustee Berger raised a question about the provisions pertaining to the
notification of adjacent property owners.
Attorney Janega explained that the neighbors are presently notified of a
demolition but not a tree removal, or what the development plans are or how it
will impact their property. The proposed provisions provide for the
notification of anyone who has a protected tree within 15 feet of the subject
property line.
After further discussion of the proposed amendments, Attorney Janega said that
she would bring back revised language to address the Council’s concerns.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, then moved to introduce ordinance
MC-5-2006, amending various sections of the Tree Preservation Code. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Susie Shabica, 326 Ridge, a Forestry Commission member, stated that it was
never the intention of the Forestry Commission to punish or impose punitive
measures on thoughtful growth within the Village, but to encourage the care of
desired tree species and to give some assurance to neighbors that their trees
will not be damaged by careless construction methods. She noted that the
changes proposed by the Commission were written using parts of codes from other
communities similar to Winnetka, and had the blessing of the Village Forester
and approval of the Village Attorney. She urged the Council to reconsider the
heritage tree provisions and to consider assessments for tree removal similar
to those in place in the City of Chicago.
c)   Ordinance No. M-21-2006 – Approving Withdrawal of Members from IMEA –
Adoption. As noted previously, this item was tabled until the December 19th
meeting.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2006 – Open Meetings Policy – Attendance at Meetings –
Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that the Open Meetings Act has been
amended, effective January 1, 2007, to address the impact of electronic
communications on meetings. The amendments (1) clarify the definition of
“meeting,” erasing any question about whether e-mail exchanges or telephone
conversations among a majority of a quorum of a public body constitute a
meeting subject to the requirements of the Open Meetings Act; and (2) provide
for a majority of a public body to allow a member of that body to physically
attend a meeting by video or audio conference under certain circumstances. The
new provisions require that a quorum of the public body be physically present
at the location of the public meeting and require the public body to adopt
rules for remote attendance.
Ms. Janega reported that because all of the Village’s Boards, Commissions, and
Committees hold their meetings in accordance with the Open Meetings Act, each
of them will have the authority to adopt rules allowing remote attendance of
members, unless the Council limits their authority.
Attorney Janega went on to say that the Council had held one study session with
remote attendance by conference call, but that interaction among all of those
in attendance at a meeting was hampered by the inability to see one another, so
staff is recommending that the Council establish a Village-wide policy
requiring attendance at meetings to be by the physical presence of the member
and prohibiting participation via electronic means.
After a brief discussion that revealed a diversity of opinion among the
Trustees, Manager Williams suggested that the matter be slated for a future
Study Session agenda.
President Woodbury tabled the matter until a later time.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2006 – Automatic Fire Extinguishing Systems –
Introduction. Chief Smith reviewed his recommendation from the November 18th
Study Session regarding requirements for the installation of automatic fire
extinguishing systems in certain building occupancies. He explained that the
Village’s Fire Prevention and Life Safety Code does not clearly require the
installation of such systems when the principal use of a building, or a portion
it, is used for a storage occupancy. Chief Smith indicated that it has been the
Fire Department’s practice to require such sprinklers, but that because of the
lack of specificity in the Code, challenges have been received. He emphasized
that this is a technical amendment and would not change current practice.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce ordinance MC-10-
2006, amending the Village Code to require the use of automatic fire
extinguishing systems in storage occupancies. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
f)   Ordinance No. M-22-2006 – 2006 Tax Levy – Introduction. Mr. McKee
explained that pursuant to discussions at the November 14th Study Session, this
ordinance would increase the tax levy by a total of 4.25% for 2006. This would
mean that existing homeowners would see an increase of 2.65%, or approximately
$37 based on $10,000 total property tax bill, with new development contributing
the rest, or 1.6%. He noted that through careful management of expenses,
diversification of General Fund revenues, and increased building permit fees,
the Village’s share of a typical Winnetkan’s total property tax bill has
gradually decreased from 17.1% in 1993 to 13.9% in 2005. Mr. McKee pointed out
that despite the Village’s being home rule, the total increase of 4.25% is less
than the 5% increase that would be allowed under tax caps.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce ordinance M-
22-2006, setting the Village’s property tax levy for 2006 by increasing the tax
levy by a total of 4.25%. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Ordinance No. M-23-2006 – 2006 Bond Abatement – Introduction. Mr. McKee
explained that this ordinance would fulfill promises made by prior Councils to
abate the bonds issued for the construction of the Public Safety Building.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce ordinance M-
23-2006, abating the taxes levied to pay the principal and interest on the
General Obligation Refunding Bonds, Series 2003. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
h)   Resolution No. R-27-2006 – Health Reimbursement Account – Adoption.
Manager Williams reviewed the process by which the Village has moved to help
contain health care costs. As a part of that process, Village employees will be
consolidating from three health care plans to a single PPO plan in order to
spread the Village’s risk over a greater number of individuals. Among other
changes implemented, deductibles and out-of-pocket maximums have been increased
and a wellness program will be instituted. In addition, health reimbursement
accounts are to be established for employees in order to enable them to accrue
funds to pay for specified medical costs. The subject resolution authorizes the
establishment of those accounts. He noted that health insurance is a mandatory
subject of bargaining and that the global changes to the health insurance
program had been negotiated with the Village’s unions as a part of a total
settlement package.
A discussion ensued. Trustee Tucker questioned the cost of the HRA. Mr. McKee
reported that with 164 employees at $500 per employee, the HRA will cost the
Village $82,000.00 per year. This is cumulative over time and is available to
employees even if their employment with the Village is terminated. Trustee
Tucker expressed concern regarding this and the lack of options or flexibility
if the Village does not realize anticipated savings from the health insurance
consolidation.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-27-
2006, authorizing the establishment of Health Reimbursement Accounts for the
Village’s employees. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Abell. Nays: Trustee Tucker. Absent:
None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1487 and 1488. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1487 in the amount of $595,539.76 and Warrant List No. 1488 in the amount of
$300,523.21. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Electric System SCADA Master Station Upgrade. Mr. Ciesla explained that
the existing SCADA system is obsolete and needs to be upgraded in order to
maintain its integrity. He reviewed the reasons contained in the Agenda Report
detailing why staff recommends awarding the purchase to Survalent Technology
even though their cost is somewhat higher than that put forth in the second
proposal.
Manager Williams noted that in addition to the reasons cited in the Agenda
Report, the second proposal would require that a separate room complete with
two air conditioners be constructed at additional expense to accommodate the
equipment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award a purchase order to
Survalent Technology in the amount of $65,900 for a Master Station SCADA
upgrade. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Post Office Site – Scope of Services Contract Economic Analysis. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request to approve increased costs of services from S. B.
Friedman & Co. incurred by the Village in connection with the redevelopment of
the Post Office site. He reported that it is estimated an additional $25,000
would be required to complete the contract because the project tasks have
varied from the approved Scope of Services.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize execution of
the Memorandum of Understanding with S. B. Friedman & Co., dated November 22,
2006, to continue to provide services in connection with the redevelopment of
the Post Office site. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
A brief discussion ensued regarding the existing alternative designs and
parking.
President Woodbury remarked that the process, led by staff and the consultants,
is a good one and that ultimately the Village will get from the Post Office
Committee the product that it was asked to deliver.
10)   Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Behles announced that the winter edition of the Winnetka
Report would be delivered to homes soon. He urged residents to read their
copies for important updates on Village issues.
Trustee Ritchell reported that the Winnetka Historical Museum would be opening
shortly and that work on the Log House is progressing. He also commented that
the Ad Hoc Historic & Residential Preservation Committee has been meeting and
would be meeting again on Thursday to hear from the first of several
consultants.
b)   Manager. Manager Williams advised the Trustees that Winnetka was
approached by Highland Park and Lake Forest about establishing a North Shore
insurance pool for the intermediate layer of insurance. However, after
exploring the possibility, it was concluded that it would not benefit Winnetka
and no changes should be made to current policy. He indicated that the Village
is always looking to be more cost efficient and in the future would be looking
into the possibility of establishing a Workers’ Compensation insurance pool.
Mr. Williams announced that in the absence of unforeseen developments, the
January 2, 2007, Council meeting would be cancelled.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, no Executive Session was held.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:45 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
December 12, 2006
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: January 16, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 12, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Herb Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Deputy
Police Chief Patrick Kreis, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Public Works Director Steve
Saunders, Acting Director of Water and Electric Rich Ciesla, Finance Director
Ed McKee, and no persons in the audience.
President Woodbury announced that the agenda would be changed to remove item
#3, Manager’s Report.
2)   Discussion: Village Hall Architects.
Public Works Director Steve Saunders reported that the Village has hired YAS
Architecture to do an analysis and develop a rehabilitation and restoration
plan for the Village Hall. He indicated that the team needs direction from the
Council regarding the climate control system, incorporating modern technology
in the Council Chambers and whether the ivy on the building’s exterior can be
removed for masonry inspections.
Mr. Saunders explained that the project team had begun their work with walk-
throughs of the Village Hall and interviews with stakeholders of the facility.
He introduced Stephen Yas, president of YAS Architecture, and Michael Auf der
Heide, the project manager, who were attending the meeting to meet the Council
and gain insight into the function of the Council Chambers, as well as to
discuss opportunities for improvement both in Chambers and in the lobby.
Mr. Yas observed that whenever there is ivy growing on a masonry building there
is damage to the mortar, and that the vines also cover up any potential
problems. He noted that the ivy is starting to grow into the gutters and
downspouts.
Mr. Auf der Heide added that the team is sensitive to residents’ feelings about
the beauty of the ivy, but warned that the organic material on the face of the
masonry keeps it from ever drying out the way it should. As a result, it is
exposed to many more freeze/thaw cycles than it would be without the ivy, and
as the water expands it damages the masonry. He mentioned that the team has
seen several places where damage has occurred, especially over the heads of
windows. He explained that water gets into the steel and pushes the masonry
out, causing a pillow effect that is called spalling. He said that in addition,
there could be damage from efflorescence, where salts that are in the mortar
get pushed to the surface.
Mr. Auf der Heide continued that as much as 25% of the walls are concealed to
such a degree that the masonry underneath is not visible. He suggested at a
minimum to remove the vines from the cornice line and the gutters and
downspouts, and also suggested taking off the ivy from the north façade, since
it is the least visible. He indicated that these steps could help the team to
get a good idea of the wall condition without taking all the ivy off.
The Trustees discussed the issue of community reaction to removing the ivy from
the building, concluding that although the issue has never been raised
publicly, people are sensitive to change. They questioned the wisdom of keeping
the ivy on the building if it is causing damage to the masonry. The point was
also raised that the restoration work might have to be done in stages if the
cost is prohibitive.
Mr. Saunders observed that the immediate issue was how much ivy should be taken
down just to do the inspection, with the long-term issue being whether there
should be ivy on the walls at all. The first step is to formulate a strategy
for the first question so that the study can proceed.
President Woodbury suggested taking all the ivy off the north wall and
extrapolating the results for a worst-case scenario for the rest of the
building. Mr. Auf der Heide conceded that it was an approach the team could
work with, but that their recommendation is to take the vines off and leave
them off. He asked for and received the Council’s permission to remove the ivy
from the cornices and downspouts as well.
The next issue up for discussion was technology upgrades for the Chambers. Mr.
Yas had several suggestions for improving the method used for visual
presentations, which included the use of several cameras and a remote control
room. He confirmed that the system would also enhance the broadcasting of the
Council meetings that are currently taped for later viewing on the cable access
channel.
There was a discussion about the placement of the A/V screen, the performance
of the audio system, and the seating for the audience, as well as the central
hallway. Mr. Yas recommended restoring the first floor lobby to its original
condition, noting that it is not in bad shape, but just needs to be brought up
to ADA accessibility standards. He informed the group that the vestibule was
originally linear but was drywalled in 1984 to make storage closets on either
side of the main front doors. He suggested that there are other options for
storage and that the vestibule could be restored and highlighted with dramatic
lighting to showcase it.
Moving on to the building’s technical systems, Mr. Saunders explained that the
team was looking at a range of options, from retrofitting to scrapping
everything and starting over. He noted that in 2003 the wiring was brought up
to standard, but that the HVAC overhaul was deemed too expensive.
Manager Williams observed that the HVAC does not work on the second floor and
outlined the various strategies used to try to keep cool in the summer and warm
in the winter, none of which worked very satisfactorily.
In response to a question about when their report might be finished, Mr. Heide
replied that the final report could be finished in April, with a draft space
planning report ready earlier.
3)   Preliminary Review: 5-Year Capital Plan.
Village Manager Doug Williams reported that in response to Council’s wish to
take a closer look at the five-year plan in advance of the budget meetings, he
had prepared this special report.
Manager Williams started his presentation with a snapshot of the general fund,
and highlighted some major projects that would be coming up in the short-term.
He proposed transferring funds to the Post Office fund, which is a major
investment in infrastructure; earmarking other funds for the facilities fund
for another level of parking studies and also for the Village Hall restoration;
and finally, setting up a police and fire pension stabilization fund. He noted
that the numbers used in his report were preliminary, and some of them are just
placeholders until a more detailed picture.
Manager Williams highlighted other upcoming expenditures, such as Emerald Ash
Borer eradication efforts, laying the groundwork for joint computer-aided
dispatch with the Wilmette Police Department, a new ambulance for the Fire
Department, and in four or five years possibly a new ladder truck. He noted
that over the next three years, the Village is planning to do a number of
drainage projects, but is flexible on the schedule.
Manager Williams reported that money needs to be set aside for the streetscape
renovation, as well as for street resurfacing. He noted that the Elm Street
train station and the Scott Avenue parking deck are scheduled for
rehabilitation, and added that the Pine and Eldorado Street bridges are in line
for restoration.
Manager Williams outlined several other projects with regard to Willow and
Green Bay Roads, including drainage and a possible jurisdictional transfer in
future years. With regard to the electric plant, he pointed out that some
upcoming expenditures will include a new underground tank farm, an upgrade of
the tie lines with Wilmette, and a possible increase in the Northfield
substation capacity. He continued that the water plant would need some new
equipment, the water main installation at Illinois and Hibbard needs to be
completed, and a complete analysis of the plant should be scheduled to provide
a strategic overview of any new technologies that might need to be implemented
in the future.
The Trustees had a discussion about the foregoing information with staff, and
talked about the implications of such a convergence of projects. It was
understood that some of the dollar amounts being set aside as placeholders are
significantly less than the funds that will really needed for some of the
projects.
4)   Manager’s Report.
Removed from agenda
5)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing personnel matters pursuant to Section 2(c)(1) of the Open Meetings
Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The Council convened into Executive Session at
10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:30 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Eilers, Tucker, and Berger. Absent: Trustee
Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The meeting
adjourned at 11:31 p.m.
   ______________________________
   Recording Secretary
December 19, 2006
DRAFT MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 16, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 19, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:39 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee Tom Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steven Saunders,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Police Chief Joe DeLopez,
Village Forester Jim Stier and approximately three persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 9(c), Approval of Consultant Services
for the Affordable Housing Plan, has been removed from the Agenda. The matter
will be discussed at the January 9th Study Session. The Trustees approved the
Agenda, as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   January 2, 2007, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury announced that the
meeting has been cancelled. A Special Meeting will be held before the January
9th Study Session.
b)   January 9, 2007, Special Meeting & Study Session. All of the Council
members present indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   January 16, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2006 – Amendments to Tree Preservation Code – Adoption.
Anne Wilder, a member of the Forestry Commission was in attendance.
Attorney Janega explained that when the Ordinance was introduced at the
December 5th Council meeting, a question was raised regarding the expansion of
the enhancement tree requirement from adjacent streets and parkways to an area
250 feet from the subject property. At that time, the Council asked staff to
revise the language so as to link the required tree enhancement directly to the
subject property, with broader tree enhancement being an alternative when
planting a full complement of replacement trees would result in overcrowding.
She went on to review all of the proposed amendments made in response to
Council’s discussions, and pointed out again that the Ordinance has no
provisions for designating Heritage Trees.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend the Ordinance as
reflected in the revised draft. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved adopt Ordinance MC-5-2006, as
amended, to further amend the Tree Preservation Code of the Village Code. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
b)   Ordinance No. M-21-2006 – Approving Withdrawal of Members from IMEA –
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that the Illinois Municipal Electric
Agency, an intergovernmental agency made up of municipalities that own or
operate their own electric systems, is seeking to limit its membership to
municipalities that purchase power from the Agency. This Ordinance, which was
introduced at the November 21st meeting, was tabled at the December 5th meeting
because it was learned that IMEA had prepared a standardized ordinance that
approved both the withdrawal of the members and a corresponding supplement to
the intergovernmental agency agreement. She noted that the draft ordinance now
under consideration amends the introduced ordinance entirely, replacing the
text with the form preferred by IMEA.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the Ordinance as
reflected in the revised draft. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved adopt Ordinance M-21-2006,
as amended, approving the withdrawal of certain municipalities from membership
from the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency and authorizing execution of
Supplement 14 to the Agency Agreement forming the IMEA. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Berger, and
Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2006 – Automatic Fire Extinguishing Systems –
Adoption. In response to a question from Trustee Abell, Chief Smith commented
that this Ordinance would have no effect on one or two-family dwellings. There
being no further questions or comments, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to adopt ordinance MC-10-2006, amending the Village Code to
require the use of automatic fire extinguishing systems in storage occupancies.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
d)   Ordinance No. M-22-2006 – 2006 Tax Levy – Adoption. There being no further
questions or comments, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
adopt ordinance M-22-2006, setting the Village’s property tax levy for 2006 by
increasing the tax levy by a total of 4.25%. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Berger, and Abell.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
e)   Ordinance No. M-23-2006 – 2006 Bond Abatement – Adoption. President
Woodbury remarked that this fulfills a promise made to the Village to rebate
the levy for the bonds annually. There being no further questions or comments,
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce ordinance M-23-
2006, abating the taxes levied to pay the principal and interest on the General
Obligation Refunding Bonds, Series 2003. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
f)   Resolution No. R-28-2006 – Collective Bargaining Agreement with the
Metropolitan Alliance of Police, Representing Winnetka Police Officers –
Adoption. Chief DeLopez explained that Village has reached a negotiated
understanding regarding its collective bargaining agreement with the union
representing its police officers, and recommended its approval.
Attorney Janega referred the Trustees to a revised Resolution incorporating
language pertaining to the Village’s home rule authority.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution
No. R-28-2006, approving the Agreement with the Metropolitan Alliance of Police
for the period April 1, 2006 through March 31, 2009. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker,
and Abell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1489 and 1490. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1489 in the amount of $733,888.93 and Warrant List No. 1490 in the amount of
$395,075.29. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
b)   Appeal of Forestry Commission Decision – 510 Ash Street. Mr. Saunders
reviewed an appeal from the homeowner of the subject property to the Forestry
Commission’s denial of a request to remove a 34” DBH Oak tree from their rear
yard. He explained that the Village Forester’s evaluation of the tree is that
it appears to be healthy with no signs of any issues that would indicate that
it is either likely to die or that it constitutes a hazard to persons or
property and that, therefore, the tree removal application had been denied by
the Public Works Director. The applicant then appealed this decision to the
Forestry Commission, which upheld the denial because there was no evidence
presented to support that the tree is damaged or dangerous.
The applicant, Stan Schlitter, reported that two of four Oak trees that were on
their property when they purchased it have fallen down for no apparent reason,
damaging neighboring property. A third tree was removed with the Village’s
permission. He voiced concern that the remaining tree will also fall and cause
harm to persons or property.
Trustee Tucker asked whether the Village Forester’s opinion has changed since
October. Mr. Saunders replied that it has not.
President Woodbury inquired as to the approximate age of the tree. Mr. Stier,
the Forester, responded that it was likely at least 150 years old.
Mr. Schlitter pointed out that there were no apparent problems with either of
the other trees that fell down. He said that he would not want to remove the
tree if someone could confirm that it is healthy and safe, but no one seems to
be able to do that.
Trustee Tucker asked if any effort has been made to protect this tree. Mr.
Schlitter remarked that he is not aware of any recommended actions he could
take, especially since the roots extend well beyond his property line.
Mr. Stier commented on his inspection of the tree.
Trustee Tucker remarked that because the Forester says that the tree appears to
be healthy and safe, the applicant needs to show that it is not.
After further discussion, Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to
uphold the Forestry Commission’s denial of the requested tree removal. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
c)   Affordable Housing Plan – Approval of Consultant Services. As noted
earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda.
d)   Kenilworth Fire Contract. Fire Chief Scott Smith explained that for more
than 30 years the Village has provided fire protection and paramedic services
to Kenilworth, and the funds received for these services help to offset the
expenses of the Winnetka Fire Department. The formula for calculating the cost
to Kenilworth is based on EAV, population, and calls for service.
In response to a question from Trustee Behles, Manager Williams commented that
the formula has a fairness built into it that results in the residents in both
communities paying roughly the same for the services.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell moved to approve the extension of
the existing contract with Kenilworth under the same terms and conditions for
an additional five (5) years, until March 31, 2012. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Tucker, Berger, Behles, and
Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
e)   Post Office Committee Minutes. Manager Williams referred the Trustees to
the committee minutes in the Agenda packet.
10)   Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported on the recent meeting of the Ad Hoc
Streetscape Committee and indicated that the group is making good progress on
its mission.
b)   Attorney. Attorney Janega informed the Trustees that she authored the
chapter on utility systems in the most recent edition of the Illinois Institute
for Continuing Legal Education Municipal Law Series practice handbook.
c)   Manager. Manager Williams thanked Chief Smith and Mr. McKee for their
efforts to successfully renegotiate the Kenilworth Fire Contract. He also
distributed updated Five-year Capital Improvements Plans showing minor
adjustments to the copies handed out earlier, which he reviewed for the
Trustees. In addition, he announced that IMEA is on the cusp of bringing
Naperville into the agency. Additional generating resources are required to
accommodate Naperville, so IMEA is considering investment in a coal-fired plant
in Southern Illinois and is working to design this so that it is cost-neutral
for all existing members of IMEA. He said that in order to fully evaluate
IMEA’s proposal, Winnetka would be retaining the services of Gerald Keenan,
former Executive Director of the Illinois Commerce Commission, to perform an
independent analysis. Finally, Manager Williams asked Chief DeLopez to review
with the Council the steps the Village will be taking to notify drivers of the
new hand-held cell phone ban, which he did.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation and personnel matters pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays:
None. Trustee Eilers: None. The Council convened into Executive Session at 9:10
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:19 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent: Trustee
Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers. The meeting
adjourned at 10:20 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
2007
January 9, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: February 6, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 9, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee Tom Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Chief of Police Joe
DeLopez, Police Sergeant Rich Carlson, and 2 persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-1-2007 – Annexation of “Split Lots” – Introduction.
Village Attorney Janega reported that 58 parcels on the north side of Scott
Street are known as “split lots” because they are located partially in Winnetka
and partially in Glencoe. Of those, she said that 46 homeowners had signed
petitions to disconnect the Glencoe portions of their lots from the Village of
Glencoe.
Attorney Janega explained that Ordinance M-1-2007 finalizes the annexation of
the portions of these 46 split lots that Glencoe has agreed to disconnect. She
noted that the agreement with Glencoe to reimburse it for lost tax revenues
gradually reduces the reimbursement over a five-year period.
Attorney Janega indicated that the annexation of these 46 lots to Winnetka
effectively reverses Cook County’s decision to move the voters who resided
between Randolph and Grove Streets to Glencoe precincts for the April 2005
election. She added that she is working with the County Clerk’s office to have
them make an administrative change to move the registered voters of the 46
petitioners back into Winnetka’s Precinct 49.
Manager Williams observed that the school districts, based on Winnetka’s
precedent, are moving to straighten out the school boundaries as well. He
praised the project team for their hard work in helping to complete the
disconnection and annexation.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote the motion passed unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2007 – Rezoning of Annexed “Split Lots” – Introduction.
Attorney Janega reported that under Illinois law, property that is annexed
comes in under the most restrictive zoning classification, which in Winnetka is
R-1. Since the Winnetka portions of the split lots are in the R-5 Single Family
Zoning District, Ordinance MC-1-2007 rezones the newly annexed property to so
that the entire lots will be placed in the R-5 Zoning District.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote the motion passed unanimously
c)   Resolution No. R-6-2007 – Collective Bargaining Agreement with the
International Association of Firefighters, Local 2077 – Adoption. Manager
Williams reported that the Council has discussed the agreement with the IAFF in
Executive Sessions, as permitted by state law. He noted that the Council
packets contained all of the memos of understanding that have been reached. He
announced that both he and Fire Chief Scott Smith recommended that the contract
be ratified.
After a short discussion, Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell moved to
adopt Resolution R-6-2007 approving the Collective Bargaining Agreement with
the International Association of Firefighters, Local 2077. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell,
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
d)   Resolution No. R-5-2007 – Approving and Establishing Salary for Director
of Water & Electric – Adoption. Manager Williams reported that staff conducted
a nation-wide search to replace Bryan McInturff, who stepped down as the
Village’s Water & Electric Superintendent. He observed that a very qualified
candidate, Brian Keyes, has been offered the position, and he recommended an
annual salary of $115,000 per year.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger moved to adopt Resolution R-5-2007
establishing an annual salary of $115,000 for the Director of Water & Electric.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
5. New Business
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1491 and 1492. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1491 in the amount of $1,084,777.75 and Warrant List No. 1492 in the
amount of $367,385.59. Trustee tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, , Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
6. Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the special
meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Eilers.
The meeting adjourned at 8:12 p.m.
