                  Recording Secretary
January 9, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 6, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
January 9, 2007 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 8:15 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee Tom Eilers. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Police Sergeant
Rich Carlson, and 1 person in the audience.
President Woodbury announced that Item 2, Approval of Consultant Services for
the Affordable Housing Plan, has been removed from the Agenda.
2)   Affordable Housing Plan – Approval of Consultant Services.
As noted earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda.
3)   Discussion: Combined Purchase of a Computer Aided Dispatch (CAD) System
with the Wilmette Police Department.
Chief of Police Joe DeLopez introduced Rich Carlsen, the director of
Administrative Services at the Police Department, and a member of the CAD
selection team.
Chief DeLopez reported that currently the Police Department operates a very old
911 system with punch cards for reporting calls. He observed that it is a
cumbersome process, with Communications personnel first recording the calls on
an incident card and entering it into the computer as time permits. He
indicated that the records management system (RMS) is also very labor
intensive, and makes it difficult for the Communications Department to comply
with the unfunded state mandate of traffic data collection for racial
profiling.
Chief De Lopez noted that the police departments of Winnetka, Wilmette and
Northbrook had formed a technical committee to conduct a needs analysis for
each department, and sent out a detailed Request for Proposal for the joint
purchase of a CAD/mobile/RMS solution. He said the committee had narrowed down
the proposals that were received to three systems, and that after further
research, recommended New World Systems as the vendor that would best suite
each department’s needs. He observed that during this time, Northbrook dropped
out of the project.
Chief DeLopez indicated that the ultimate goal of both villages is to form a
consolidated dispatch center at a future date, and that the installation of the
new system would be a good first step towards that goal. He recounted some of
the systems’ advantages over the other bidders, including a Windows Server,
demographic profiling data collection, traffic crash reporting and redundant
servers, which make it the best candidate despite not being the lowest bidder.
Manager Williams concurred, saying that both he and the Wilmette Village
Manager recommend New World to their respective Councils, as they have the
strongest track record in Illinois. He added that another point in their favor
is the fact that the Glenview and Deerfield Police Departments also use the New
World system. He remarked that he has been in contact with Wilmette’s Manager
and a preliminary cost sharing agreement is being worked out.
Sergeant Carlson noted that the Winnetka Police Department works very closely
with Wilmette, both departments use the same channel and operate in a similar
manner, all of which will help to create a successful partnership.
The Trustees discussed different aspects of the system with Sergeant Carlson
and Chief DeLopez, and concluded that all were in favor of moving forward with
the implementation of the new CAD system in concert with the Wilmette Police
Department.
4)   Discussion: Amendments to Liquor Control Regulations, Winnetka Village
Code, Chapter 5.09.
Attorney Janega explained that several policy issues had surfaced recently
regarding the liquor control regulations.
Attorney Janega reported that a new state law permits one unsealed wine bottle
to be removed from a restaurant, provided it is put in a clear tamper-proof
package, the restaurant provides a receipt, and it was partially consumed along
with a meal. She said that notices about the new provision had been sent to all
liquor licensees when their renewal applications were mailed.
Attorney Janega next brought up the issues of “last call,” hours of service and
“BYOB” service in unlicensed restaurants. She noted that in order to tighten
perceived loopholes in the liquor ordinance, some changes to the language were
necessary. She described draft changes made to the liquor ordinance which
clarify the code to unequivocally prohibit “BYOB” service, remove language
referring to “last call,” and clearly define what service is permitted, and
when.
The Council briefly deliberated the issue of TV Riders for Class A and A-
1 Licensees and decided to leave the permitted number of televisions up to the
discretion of the Local Liquor Control Commissioner.
Attorney Janega mentioned that Trustee Abell had requested that the Council
consider the policy questions revolving around liquor code enforcement
procedures.
Trustee Abell observed that the last liquor violation hearing had used a lot of
time and resources, and he wondered if the process could be simplified
Attorney Janega noted that some of the procedures are statutory, but said that
she would do some research and come back with recommendations.
5)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn the meeting, seconded by Trustee Abell. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 10:10 p.m.
