Recording Secretary
January 16, 2007
DRAFT MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 6, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 16, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Acting Village Manager and Director of
Public Works Steve Saunders, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Interim Director of Water &
Electric Rich Ciesla, and approximately five persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   January 30, 2007, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   February 1, 2007, Budget Meeting. All of the Trustees present indicated
that they expected to attend.
c)   February 6, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 5, 2006, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury referred the
Trustees to changes suggested by Trustee Tucker. Trustee Tucker explained that
she wanted to clarify her reasons for voting against the Health Reimbursement
Account. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the
minutes, as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   December 12, 2006, Study Session. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   December 19, 2006, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury noted that Item 6
(a), MC-5-2006, had been adopted. Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Berger,
moved to approve the minutes, as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-1-2007 – Annexation of “Split Lots” – Adoption. Village
Attorney Janega reviewed this matter, explaining that Ordinance M-1-2007
finalizes the annexation of the portions of these 46 split lots that Glencoe
has agreed to disconnect.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers,
Berger, Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2007 – Rezoning of Annexed “Split Lots” – Public
Hearing and Adoption. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing, and
Attorney Janega reviewed the matter, noting that under Illinois law, property
that is annexed comes in under the most restrictive zoning classification,
which in Winnetka is R-1. Since the Winnetka portions of the lots are in the R-
5 Single Family Zoning District, Ordinance MC-1-2007 rezones the newly annexed
property to so that the entire lots will be placed in the R-5 Zoning District.
There being no comments or questions from the audience, President Woodbury
closed the Public Hearing.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt the ordinance. By
roll call vote the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Tucker expressed thanks to all who worked so hard to finally resolve
this long-standing issue.
c)   Ordinance No. M-2-2007 – Zoning Variation: 508 Birch Street -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot,
Front Setback, Side Yard Setback, and Garage variations to permit the
reconstruction of a one-story enclosed porch and construction of a detached
garage at the subject property. He explained that the property is existing
nonconforming and the proposed new porch would be built in the same location
and would have the same footprint as the existing one and that the proposed
garage would replace an existing one of the same size and would have the same
side yard setback. He noted that the variations would not result in an
expansion of any of the structures or an increase in the existing
nonconformity. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5 to 0 to
recommend approval of the requested variations.
After confirming with Mr. D’Onofrio that the applicant is otherwise in
compliance with the Zoning Ordinance, Trustee Abell said that he believes the
Zoning Board correctly analyzed the situation and that he is in favor of
granting the requested variations.
Trustee Behles asked whether the reason the garage cannot be built to be
conforming is to make accommodation for existing trees and shrubs. Mr.
D’Onofrio verified that that is what the testimony indicated. Trustee Behles
commented that although these are significant variations in terms of
percentages, they are due to the setting of the house on the property, as well
as the unique configuration of the lot. He agreed with Trustee Abell that the
Zoning Board’s decision was the right one.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the Ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Woodbury thanked the applicant for her efforts to upgrade the
property and for cooperating fully with the Village.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2007 – Amending Liquor Control Regulations –
Introduction. Attorney Janega briefly reviewed the policy issues relating to
liquor licenses which were discussed at the last study session. These include
(1) whether the liquor license hours apply to the service and consumption of
alcoholic beverages as well as to the sales transaction; (2) whether customers
should be permitted to remain on the premises to complete eating their meals
and drinking their alcoholic beverages after liquor sales end; (3) whether
unlicensed food establishments should be able to permit their customers to
bring their own alcoholic beverages when they come to eat; (4) whether the
Village should codify the “last call” practices followed by most restaurants;
(5) whether the Village should allow extended hours on New Years Eve; and (6)
whether the Liquor Control Commissioner (i.e., the Village President) should be
authorized to allow a licensee with a Television Rider to have more than one
television on the premises. Additionally, the Council considered whether the
Code should be amended to reflect a new State law that allows restaurants that
are licensed to sell alcoholic beverages to permit a patron to remove one
unsealed and partially consumed bottle of wine for consumption off the premises
under certain conditions, as well as whether the Village President should be
allowed to designate someone other than himself to serve as Liquor Control
Commissioner. She explained that in accordance with that discussion, the
proposed draft ordinance addresses all of the policy issues and incorporates
the provisions of the new State law but does not authorize the designation of
someone other than the Village President to serve as Local Liquor Commissioner.
Attorney Janega went on to review the proposed amendments and respond to
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce the Ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried
e)   Resolution No. R-1-2007 – MFT Transfer for Maintenance of Streets -
Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained that the State imposes a tax on the sale of
motor fuel and distributes some of those funds back to the municipalities. He
noted that historically the Village has used $125,000 of those funds to offset
the costs of street maintenance and recommended adopting this resolution in
keeping with past policy.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-1-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-2-2007 – NTHS Parking Lease - Adoption. Mr. McKee
reviewed the request for approval of a new intergovernmental agreement with New
Trier Township High School for the use of the Village’s parking resources in
the Indian Hill/New Trier area. He explained that it has taken some time to
negotiate a new agreement that is in keeping with Council’s previous
discussions. To do so some of the maintenance costs and some of the enforcement
responsibilities have been shifted to the School and the rate has been
increased to move more toward market value.
Trustee Eilers commented that the proposed new rate still reflects a gap
between what the Village gets and fair market value.
Mr. McKee responded that the proposal takes a reasonable step toward balancing
the benefit of having the School in the Village with trying to attain fair
market value. He pointed out that not all of the parking spaces are designated
for students; many are used by teachers and other School employees.
President Woodbury commented that the School determines the allocation of
spaces among its user groups and determines its own rates for their use.
Historically, the Village has had nothing to do with this process. He noted
that over time the Village has been taking incremental steps toward the goal of
reaching a more market-based price.
Chief DeLopez explained that as a part of the new agreement, the School’s para-
professionals would serve as the primary parking enforcement officers, with the
police serving in a secondary capacity. This would permit the Village to divert
its Community Service Officers to other duties. He indicated that if the School
does not uphold its commitments in this area, some costs would be passed back
to them.
Trustee Tucker echoed Trustee Eilers’ concern that the agreement does not reach
market value. She expressed her unease as a taxpayer about the subsidy to the
School and pointed out that subsidizing parking encourages driving, which fails
to contribute to Winnetka’s becoming a “greener” community. She suggested
authorizing the new agreement for a one-year period during which time the
matter can be reexamined.
Trustee Abell commented that the unit price per space is going down. Mr. McKee
explained that that is because all of the spaces being used by the School are
being covered in this Agreement, including a number that were not covered in
the former agreement and had been used by employees at no cost.
Trustee Ritchell voiced his discomfort with the proposal, particularly in light
of the soaring cost of parking and the other projects requiring significant
capital investment being discussed by the Council. He also expressed concern
about how the Village is going to measure the effectiveness of the School’s
enforcement and how it will proceed if the arrangement doesn’t work.
Trustee Eilers noted that the negotiated amount in the Agreement is in keeping
with Council’s direction, assuming the School’s satisfactory assumption of
enforcement and management responsibilities. However, he indicated that given
the significant capital cost decisions that this Council needs to make, he
cannot justify subsidizing the School to the extent proposed. He agreed with
Trustee Tucker that the whole package should be reexamined and said that the
Council should avoid entering into an agreement extending beyond one year.
Acting Manager Saunders pointed out that the Village regularly subsidizes
parking throughout the Village by imposing different rates for different user
groups based on various value judgments. He noted that New Trier, the Village’s
largest employer, is being asked to pay substantially more than the $10 per
space that other employers in town are required to pay for their employees.
After further discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees to table the
matter.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., advised the Council that the stationmaster at the
Hubbard Woods train station has been informing passengers that Metra plans to
remove the stationmaster from Hubbard Woods, which would likely result in the
station being locked at all times, which would make it impossible for commuters
to use it as they now do for shelter and bathroom facilities. He indicated that
he had checked with Metra and confirmed that such a move is being considered.
He urged the Council to officially state its objection to Metra before the
decision to implement this plan is made.
Trustee Eilers clarified with Mr. Kates that Metra is talking about closing the
building, not closing the station.
President Woodbury indicated that having a warming place is appropriate and
that the Village would voice its desire to keep the station building open. He
noted that the Village continues to actively work with Metra on the
rehabilitation of the Elm Street station but that there is no timetable for
renovation of the Hubbard Woods station. Mr. Woodbury pointed out, however,
that Metra/UP’s personnel issues are within the purview of their private
business plan and not under the Village’s control and mentioned that there is
considerable tension at the present time between UP and Metra.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1493 and 1494. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists
and commented on a number of the items. He then moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1493 in the amount of $753,580.80 and Warrant List No. 1494 in the amount
of $382,139.57.
Trustee Tucker noted that she and her husband had a $500 construction refund on
Warrant List No. 1494, so she would abstain from voting on that single item.
Trustee Ritchell amended his motion to first approve Warrant List No. 1493. The
motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and
Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Abell then moved to approve Warrant No. 034315 on Warrant List No.
1494, a deposit refund to Trustee Tucker and her husband, in the amount of
$500.00. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. Nays:
None. Absent: None. Trustee Tucker abstained.
Finally, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the
remaining items on Warrant List No. 1494. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and
Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Station Batteries. Mr. Ciesla reviewed the bids to purchase batteries to
supply power for circuit breakers, control systems, and protective relay
operations at the Village’s electric facilities. He explained that these are
necessary to ensure that power is always available for critical equipment
operations and that the batteries presently in service are at the end of their
useful life. Mr. Ciesla recommended the award of the bid to Lehman Electric
Services.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to authorize the purchase of
station batteries from Lehman Electric Services in the amount of $36,632.00. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Request for Proposal Award (RFP #06-007). Mr. Saunders reviewed the
process used to solicit and evaluate Requests for Proposals for preliminary
engineering services to address poor drainage conditions in the Alles-Birch-
Sunset drainage area where residents have been subjected to flooding during
heavy rainfall events because large portions of this area have either no or
minimal storm water conveyance system in place. He said that the proposal would
consist of developing a drainage and stormwater management study and
recommending improvements to the existing drainage system. He recommended that
the bid be awarded to Engineering Resource Associates for the not-to-exceed-
direct cost amount of $29,634.00.
Noting the public/private ownership of the existing drainage system, Trustee
Eilers asked how this would work. Mr. Saunders responded that historically the
Village has tried to separate out the components and to limit its investment to
those improvements that only affect public property.
Trustee Tucker asked why there is so little existing infrastructure. Mr.
Saunders explained that it was partly due to the age of the area and to mixed
ownership situation. He added that the low-lying topography also impacts the
situation.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to award the RFP as proposed.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker,
Abell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports.
a)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the Ad Hoc Streetscape Committee
has been making solid progress. They have looked at mapping the business
districts and Green Bay corridor and are moving toward a consensus on materials
and a timetable for the next steps and ranking of the first projects.
Trustee Ritchell reported that the Winnetka Historical Society had received a
good response to their Martin Luther King Day commemoration event. He also
mentioned that the Ad Hoc Historic and Residential Preservation Committee has
been meeting and has enlisted the support of outside advisors.
Trustee Abell reported that he and Trustee Berger had solicited support for the
Village’s cell phone ban at a recent Northwest Municipal Conference meeting and
that a copy of President Woodbury’s letter has been circulated to all mayors
and managers.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Business Community Development Commission
continues to review the C-2 Overlay District and that a report would be
forthcoming. He noted that a number of commercial property owners have joined
together to form an association.
b)   Village President. President Woodbury reported that he has sent a letter
regarding the implementation of the cell phone ban to the presidents of
Winnetka’s neighboring villages. He also mentioned that he and Mr. Saunders had
attended an informational meeting with representatives from other
municipalities and noted that there are several items which may eventually come
before the Council.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:20 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
