MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 20, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 6, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Interim Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla,
Trustee Candidate King Poor and approximately four persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 9(b), Hibbard Road Water Main, would be
discussed before the Warrant Lists, Item 9(a). The Trustees approved the Agenda
as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   February 8, 2007, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 13, 2007, Study Session/Budget Meeting. Trustee Abell indicated
that he would not be able to attend. All other members of the Council indicated
that they expected to attend.
c)   February 19, 2007, Budget Meeting. Trustee Abell indicated that he would
not be able to attend. All other members of the Council indicated that they
expected to attend.
d)   February 20, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 9, 2007, Special Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   January 9, 2007, Study Session Meeting. Trustee Tucker, seconded by
Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
c)   January 16, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-2-2007 – Zoning Variation: 508 Birch Street - Adoption.
President Woodbury called for any additional comments on the matter. There
being none, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the
Ordinance granting four zoning variations to permit the reconstruction of a
one-story enclosed porch and replacement of a detached garage. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2007 – Amending Liquor Control Regulations – Adoption.
President Woodbury called for any additional comments or questions. Trustee
Abell suggested that the draft be amended to permit the Liquor Commissioner to
determine the size of televisions allowed, as well as the number, and moved to
amend Code Section 5.09.100 H in Section 3 of the Ordinance accordingly.
Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-
2-2007, as amended, amending various provisions of the Liquor Ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-3-2007 – Zoning Variation: 1074 Laurel - Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for three zoning variations to permit the
remodeling of an existing attached garage, the addition of a front porch, and
the construction of a two-car detached garage. He noted that when all of the
improvements are made, the overall Gross Floor Area will be reduced by 8.34
feet and the roofed lot coverage will be increased by 651.17 sq. ft. Mr.
D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had recommended 7 to 0 to
recommend approval of the three requested variations.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
introduce the Ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Resolution No. R-3-2007 – Authorizing the Release of Executive Session
Minutes and Destruction of Verbatim Recordings - Adoption. Attorney Janega
explained that the Open Meetings Act requires the Council to examine and
approve Executive Session minutes twice a year and to release minutes for which
confidentiality is no longer required. The Act also requires the Village to
retain verbatim recordings of closed sessions for 18 months. Resolution No. R-
3-2007 covers the Executive Session minutes from June 6, 2006, through December
19, 2006, and authorizes the destruction of audio recordings of Executive
Session minutes from January 4, 2005, through June 21, 2005. Attorney Janega
noted that the Resolution specifically states that the Council is not
authorizing or consenting to the public disclosure of the verbatim recordings.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve Resolution No. R-3-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Hibbard Road 16” Water Main. Mr. Ciesla reviewed a change order for
additional work required in connection with the installation of the 16” water
main on Hibbard Road. He explained that the additional cost was incurred
largely because the contractor needed to use a trench box to ensure workers’
safety, which had not been anticipated. This increased the width of the
excavation and the amount of repaving materials required. Mr. Ciesla also
reported that the Village Engineer had also reviewed the change order and
confirmed that the trench box was necessary. There were also some other minor
changes that are typical of construction projects.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize Change Order
No. 1 in the amount of $116,434.63 for Lenny Hoffman for work on the Hibbard
Road Water Main. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Abell. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
President Woodbury noted that since the new Director of Water & Electric will
be starting work next week this would be Mr. Ciesla’s last appearance as
Interim Director of Water & Electric. He thanked Mr. Ciesla for his service and
professionalism during his tenure.
b)   Warrant Lists 1495 and 1496. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists
and commented on a number of the items. He then moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1495 in the amount of $1,496,826.13 and Warrant List No. 1496 in the amount
of $1,461,606.08. The motion was seconded by Trustee Tucker. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Public Safety Building Video Monitoring System. Chief DeLopez explained
that the existing video monitoring system in the Public Safety Building does
not cover all areas of the Police and Fire Departments, posing safety and
security concerns. He said that the current system also employs outdated
technology that is neither integrated, reliable, nor user friendly. He reviewed
the results of an RFI issued by the Police Department and the evaluation
process. Chief DeLopez reported staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded
to Secure Integrations because their system is comprehensive, reasonably
priced, and offers features not found on other systems. In addition, he noted
that Secure Integrations installed and maintains the video surveillance system
at New Trier High School and that access to this system is being installed at
the Village’s 9-1-1 Center, providing seamless integration with the NTHS
system.
After the Chief responded to questions from the Council, Trustee Berger,
seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the purchase of an integrated
video security monitoring system from Secure Integrations for a total amount of
$44,163.00. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Manager Williams asked Chief DeLopez to brief the Council on the status of the
Village’s enforcement of the new hand-held cell phone ban. The Chief reported
that a number of warning citations have been issued and that residents seem to
be well aware of the new law because the publicity was very good. He
complimented the Council on moving forward on this issue and responded to
questions.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury reported on two recent meetings, one
with the Ad Hoc Residential Design Committee and the other with State Senator
Schoenberg.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the Business Community Development
Commission’s discussions on the C-2 Overlay District will not be completed as
early as anticipated. He said that the C-2 Overlay District restricts property
owners’ rights to lease space to certain tenants. This is an exercise of
municipal authority which should be taken seriously. However, to remove those
restrictions could alter the complexion of the Village’s business districts. He
noted that a building owners’ group had formed to follow the process and
encouraged all interested parties to attend the next meeting to express their
views on this important matter.
Trustees Abell and Berger reported on their recent attendance at the NWMC
legislative brunch.
Trustee Tucker reported on a recent meeting pertaining to the Coastal
Management Program. She noted that the program to bring Federal dollars to
coastal communities is still under development but that the communities were
assured there will be no new State regulations that would override local zoning
regulations. She indicated that no action was required, but recommended that
the Village continue to monitor the program as it moves forward.
Trustee Behles repeated the invitation to residents to attend the next meeting
of the Streetscape Committee, where the Committee will begin to discuss the
palette of materials for the three business districts.
Trustee Ritchell reported that the Ad Hoc Historic and Residential Preservation
Committee continues to make progress.
c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that the last official act for
completing annexation of the Scott Avenue split lots has been done. She also
reported that there is a new piece of legislation has been submitted in
Springfield to prohibit the use of hand-held cell phones.
d)   Manager. Mr. Williams reported that last week IMEA had voted unanimously
to allow Naperville to become a member. He said that Winnetka would soon need
to make a decision about whether it wants to participate in Prairie State
Project and that there would be a presentation on the matter at the February
20th Council meeting.
Manager Williams also announced that the Ad Hoc Post Office Committee would be
hosting two public input gathering sessions in March and that residents would
soon receive a mailing inviting them to attend. He encouraged everyone who is
interested to attend.
Finally, Mr. Williams reported that the Village’s streaming of the Council
meetings appears to be well-received by the community, as indicated by the
number of hits on the web site.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Pending and Probable Litigation, Personnel Matters, and Board
Appointments pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(3) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 9:01 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:50 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:51 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
February 13, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 20, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 13, 2006, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:45 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee Abell. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Deputy Police
Chief Pat Kreis, Trustee Candidate King Poor, and 1 person in the audience.
2)   Police Department Budget.
Police Chief Joe DeLopez gave an overview of the Department’s organization,
noting that the reorganization of the Department’s structure has recently been
completed. He reviewed the Department’s activity for the past year, noting that
traffic accidents have decreased by 27% and that, due in part to a State grant,
traffic enforcement was boosted by 40%.
Chief De Lopez indicated that the budget would increase by $412,825, largely
because of higher salary and benefit costs. He reported that the budget
included the new position of Parking Enforcement Officer, partly to offset a
Community Service Officer who was recently called up to active duty in the
Army, and partly to increase parking enforcement in the business districts.
Asked for a recommendation about whether the Parking Enforcement Officer can
wait another year, Manager Williams said he would advise the Council at the
next budget meeting.
3)   Parking Fine Survey.
Chief DeLopez reported that Winnetka’s fines for late ticket payments are
somewhat lower than comparable communities, and recommended some adjustments.
Manager Williams then distributed an article containing some parking ideas for
the Council to consider.
4)   Social Worker Report.
Manager Williams reported that the Village’s arrangement of contracting out the
position of Social Worker with New Trier Township’s Arthur Sontag is working
well. He noted that the Social Services Annual Report contains information
about the quantity and types of cases that are handled.
5)   Police Computer Aided Dispatch Project.
Chief DeLopez informed the Council that he and Attorney Janega are working
together on an intergovernmental agreement with Wilmette as part of the CAD
project. He explained that while each village will purchase its own computer
equipment, the software will be shared and the cost split according to a
formula based on population. He outlined the different pieces of the agreement
and noted that some issues need to be clarified with the software vendor.
Attorney Janega reported that a Cy Pres fund had been created from some of the
PrimeCo settlement proceeds, with the funds intended for communities to use for
communications purposes. She indicated that the Village is eligible to apply
for a Cy Pres fund grant for the CAD project, and that the necessary
authorizing resolution would be placed on the Council’s next agenda. She noted
that the money would be used to offset the Village’s portion of the software
purchase with Wilmette.
Attorney Janega advised that she has been working with Wilmette’s corporation
counsel to finalize the intergovernmental agreement, which would likely come
before the Council at the first meeting in March. She added that the vendor has
confirmed in writing that it will hold the bid price until March 23.
6)   Discussion: 2006-07 Equipment Purchase.
Deputy Police Chief Pat Kreis presented the Police Department’s Taser proposal,
citing reduction of the risk of injury to suspects and officers as the main
objective of the request. He explained how Tasers work, and listed some
neighboring communities that use them.
Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry, spoke in support of the proposal, saying the Taser is a
less lethal alternative to a firearm.
In response to questioning from the Council, Chief DeLopez and Deputy Chief
Kreis acknowledged the need for additional research on the effects of the Taser
on certain specific segments of the population, explained the Department’s use
of force policy and where Tasers would fit in that policy, and confirmed that
officers would be extensively trained in the use of the device. Chief DeLopez
reiterated that the goal is to reduce risk and injury to both officers and
subjects.
In the ensuing discussion, Manager Williams explained that whether the Village
should include Tasers as a form of force available to Police officers should be
decided as a policy matter by the Council. After several Trustees expressed
concerns about the use of Tasers, and whether their use was warranted in
Winnetka, the Council determined that it would not authorize the use of Tasers.
7)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
8)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:00 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
February 20, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 6, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 20, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Trustee Candidate King Poor and approximately three
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 5(b), the February 8th Budget Meeting
minutes, has been removed from the Agenda and Item 10(a), the IMEA Prairie
State Generation Project, will be discussed after Item 6(a). The Trustees
approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   February 27, 2007, Budget Meeting. Mr. Woodbury announced that this
meeting has been cancelled.
b)   March 6, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 6, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers asked that the minutes
be amended to reflect his comments about the seriousness of the deliberations
by the Business Community Development Commission over the C-2 Overlay District.
There being no objections, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved
to approve the minutes, as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   February 8, 2007, Budget Meeting. As noted earlier, this item was removed
from the Agenda.
c)   February 13, 2007, Budget Meeting. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-3-2007 – Zoning Variation: 1074 Laurel - Adoption.
President Woodbury called for additional comments or questions regarding the
proposed ordinance granting three zoning variations to permit an existing
attached garage to be remodeled into a family room, and to permit the addition
of a front porch and the construction of a two-car detached garage. There being
none, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance No.
M-3-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Abell.
* * *
10)   Reports
a)   IMEA Prairie State Generation Project. As announced earlier by President
Woodbury, the Council moved to the discussion of this item.
The Village’s consultant for the project, Gerry Keenan of Palmer Bellevue, LLC,
responded to questions raised previously by the Trustees, and President
Woodbury encouraged the Trustees to continue to ask questions until they are
comfortable with the subject.
Trustee Behles noted that a number of questions exist regarding the mine
ownership and asked Mr. Keenan to comment.
Mr. Keenan explained that there currently is no mine, just proven but unmined
coal reserves that will be purchased by the project company, the entity in
which IMEA will be a shareholder to the extent of its 15.2% interest. He
enumerated what the project company will own. He said that the mine itself will
be operated by subsidiaries of the project company and that the precise legal
structure of those mining companies has not yet been defined but that the
intent is to build in liability shields so that any problems with the operation
of the mine fall back on the mining companies. He emphasized that while IMEA is
a shareholder in the mine, and the only relationship Winnetka would have with
the proposed plant would be through its power purchase agreement with IMEA.
Manager Williams pointed out that the IMEA will know approximately 91% of the
project costs when the Engineering, Procurement, and Construction contract is
completed in early March, but it is impossible to have perfect information. He
asked Mr. Keenan to comment on IMEA’s due diligence.
Mr. Keenan indicated that IMEA is well acquainted with the Prairie State
project because it had looked at it twice before when the timing was not right.
He said IMEA has thoroughly analyzed the project from a cost perspective, and
now with the participation of Naperville in IMEA, it makes sense for IMEA to be
a part of the project. IMEA has gotten Naperville to indemnify the Agency until
the Notice to Proceed as well as pay a premium. Ultimately, IMEA members will
have a new and better resource and will realize a price decrease, with more
diversity in its power sources and a better emissions profile.
He noted that historically, Winnetka’s philosophical approach has been to opt
for cost-based rather than market-based power to the extent possible; hence,
its participation in the Trimble County 1 and Trimble County 2 plants. If
Winnetka were to opt out of the Prairie State project, there would be no
alternative to purchasing some of its power on the open market, and no one can
foresee with accuracy what power costs will be in the future. Mr. Keenan
pointed out that this is one of the reasons Naperville is joining IMEA.
Manager Williams commented that Batavia, St. Charles, and Geneva have also
moved from a market-based to cost-based strategy and remarked on all the things
Naperville, a well-respected, well-run municipal utility, is willing to do to
become cost-based.
Mr. Williams asked Mr. Keenan for his recommendation based upon his review of
the materials, his professional background, and his expertise. Mr. Keenan
responded that in his opinion Prairie State has been thoroughly scrutinized by
numerous experts and Winnetka would be misguided if it did not move forward
with the project.
Trustee Eilers expressed some reservations about proceeding with the project,
but noted that his concerns were based on potential liability associated with
owning the mine and not on cost, because he believes that Winnetka is better
served with cost-based power.
Manager Williams reviewed the deadlines for the Village’s decision. He also
suggested that if the Village decides to proceed, it might wish to consider
paying off the debt over 30 years instead of 15 years as has been done in the
past.
It was agreed that Mr. Keenan would meet with a couple of the Trustees during
the next week to answer additional questions.
* * *
9)   New Business
b)   Affordable Housing Plan – Approval of Consultant. The Village President
then moved to the discussion of this item, to accommodate members of the
audience.
Mr. Norkus reviewed the Plan Commission’s efforts toward updating the Village’s
Affordable Housing Plan in keeping with Council’s directions. He reported that
staff had issued a Request for Proposals for services to include (1) a detailed
analysis of the Village’s housing stock and population; (2) an analysis of the
impact of teardowns on the relative affordability of housing and the resulting
shift in population, affordability and income levels; (3) facilitation of
meetings with residents and the Plan Commission to identify concerns and refine
objectives; and (4) drafting of a revised affordable housing plan. Mr. Norkus
said that thirteen proposals were received. These proposals were evaluated and
narrowed down to four. A subcommittee of the Plan Commission subsequently found
the Voorhees Center at UIC and Economic Research Associates to be the most
highly qualified firms to undertake the assignment. Ultimately, there was a
consensus that the project team at UIC’s Voorhees Center had the best
combination of skills, expertise, and project understanding. Consequently, the
Plan Commission voted 8-0 to recommend the selection of the Voorhees Center,
subject to their scope of services being modified to highlight and clarify the
project’s inclusion of defining a local standard of affordability as a specific
work task and project outcome. He noted that this has been done. In addition,
the Plan Commission communicated the fact that, upon conclusion of the Voorhees
plan, and depending on the outcome of that planning process, there may be a
need or desire to contract for additional services to assess the economic
impact and feasibility analysis of the recommendations. With this in mind, the
Voorhees Center also amended it scope of services to clarify and expand on the
fact that it will provide detailed recommendations for implementing the plan’s
ultimate recommendations. Mr. Norkus noted that the Voorhees Center anticipates
completing the project within eight months.
Maureen Mitchel, Chairperson of the Plan Commission, explained that although
the Commission’s process has taken longer than anticipated, the Plan Commission
is enthusiastic about the ultimate choice and looking forward to getting the
project underway. She said she expects this to be a vital process in which the
Plan Commission will be actively involved, not just a receiver of services.
To incorporate Trustee Tucker’s changes incorporated in the January 24 motion
passed by the Plan Commission, Trustee Eilers suggested the following two
amendments in the Voorhees contract: (1) to Section 1.7, add the word
“Winnetka’s” between “on” and “existing”; and (2) to Section 2.6, add the words
“Winnetka residents” between “including” and “local.” He then moved, seconded
by Trustee Ritchell, to execute a contract for services with the Voorhees
Center at the University of Illinois-Chicago, as amended, in the amount of
$43,012. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Eilers,
Ritchell, Behles, Tucker and Berger Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
* * *
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
The Council then returned to the scheduled agenda, in the order published.
b)   Resolution No. R-4-2007 – Joint Cy Pres Grant Application with Village of
Wilmette - Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that subject resolution would
authorize the joint application for a Cy Pres/IMF grant by the Village of
Winnetka and the Village of Wilmette. Cy Pres/IMF funds are the result of the
settlement of litigation over the imposition of the Telecommunications
Infrastructure Maintenance Fee and are available for municipal 9-1-
1 telecommunications programs. Any grant funds received would be applied in
their totality to help cover the costs associated with the joint purchase of
Computer Aided Dispatch system to be implemented by the Villages of Winnetka
and Wilmette.
Chief DeLopez responded to questions and added that the Village had been
informed that submitting a joint grant application would maximize the chances
of getting the maximum amount allowed.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve Resolution No. R-
4-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Abell.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1497 and 1498. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists
and commented on a number of the items. He then moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1497 in the amount of $1,140,745.04 and Warrant List No. 1498 in the amount
of $558,457.46. The motion was seconded by Trustee Tucker. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
b)   Affordable Housing Plan – Approval of Consultant Services. This item was
discussed earlier in the meeting. (See above.)
10)   Reports.
a)   IMEA Prairie State Generation Project. This item was discussed earlier in
the meeting. (See above.)
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the Streetscape Committee continues
to work diligently and is making progress. He invited anyone who is interested
in the program to attend the upcoming meetings scheduled for Thursday, February
22 and March 8 at 7:30 a.m. He said that the Committee hopes to have a report
to the Council in early summer.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Tucker moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments pursuant to Section 2(c)(3) of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Eilers seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The Council adjourned into Executive
Session at 9:23 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:50 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Abell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell. The meeting
adjourned at 9:51 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
March 6, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 20, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
6, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Fire Chief Scott
Smith, Trustee Candidate King Poor and approximately fifty persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
Mr. Woodbury welcomed members of the Washburne 8th grade class and their
parents. He explained that the Council would move the public comment section of
the Agenda forward after the Trustees got through some preliminary procedural
matters. The Trustees approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   March 13, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   March 20, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Eilers, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 30, 2007, Budget Meeting. Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
b)   February 1, 2007, Budget Meeting. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   February 8, 2007, Budget Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
d)   February 19, 2007, Budget Meeting. Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee
Eilers, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
e)   February 20, 2007, Regular Meeting. Mr. Woodbury referred the Trustees to
a previously distributed amendment suggested by Trustee Eilers. There being no
objections to this change, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved
to approve the minutes as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
* * *
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Ms. Ceci Gigiolio, 8th Grade Social Studies teacher at Carlton Washburne Middle
School, brought forth a proposal by her students to place a free-standing
plaque on the Village Green memorializing Dr. Martin Luther King’s 1965 speech
that took place there. Various class members spoke to the significance of the
event, its historical context and impact, and why it is worth acknowledging.
They presented the Trustees with letters from the Historical Society, Park
District, and Veterans of Foreign Wars supporting this endeavor, as well as
petitions of support signed by students and community members.
Manager Williams asked whether the group had a location on the Green in mind.
Ms. Gigiolio responded that they hoped to put it where it would be readily seen
but out of the way of events that take place on the Green. She suggested
someplace near the water fountain on the northwest corner.
President Woodbury and the Trustees thanked Ms. Gigiolio and the students for
their thoughtful and thorough presentation. Mr. Woodbury explained that there
are certain covenants governing the use of the land and suggested that the next
step in the approval-seeking process would be for the Village to invite
residents living in the neighborhood of the Village Green to discuss the
proposal and express their views. He indicated that the matter would be slated
for further consideration at an upcoming meeting.
* * *
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution No. R-7-2007 - 2007-2008 Village Budget –Public Hearing &
Introduction. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing.
Mr. McKee explained that the Council adopts a new budget resolution and new
utility and fee resolutions each year as a matter of practice, even when there
are no changes. He summarized the substantive changes made to the proposed
budget and reviewed proposed changes to the fees and rate resolutions resulting
from Council’s discussions during the budget meetings. He noted that a typical
homeowner would experience a $78, or 1.4%, increase in the cost of municipal
services over the current year. Residential customers will see the following
increases in their annual costs:
   1.   A 3% property tax increase
   2.   A 3% water rate increase
   3.   A 5% sewer rate increase
   4.   A $5.00 increase, from $20 to $25, for a second weekly refuse pick-up.
Trustee Eilers and Trustee Tucker mentioned that the final budget is the result
of many hours of study and work and that all of the issues had been thoroughly
vetted.
After a brief discussion, President Woodbury closed the Public Hearing, and
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce the 2007-2008
Budget Resolution, Resolution No. R-7-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   Updated Fee & Rate Resolutions. There being no additional comments or
questions, President Woodbury called for motions to introduce the fee and rate
resolutions.
   i)   Resolution No. R-8–2007 - Water Rates – Introduction.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Resolution No.
R-8-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-9–2007 - Electric Rates – Introduction.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Resolution No. R-
9-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-10–2007 – Sewer Rates – Introduction.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Resolution No. R-
10-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   iv)   Resolution No. R-11–2007 - Refuse Rates – Introduction.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Resolution No.
R-11-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. McKee and his staff for their extensive work
preparing the budget.
c)   Resolution No. R-14–2007 – State Highway Work – Adoption. Mr. Saunders
explained that several State roadways run through the Village. In order to
undertake repairs on any of the roadways under the jurisdiction of the Illinois
Department of Transportation, a permit requiring performance security must be
obtained. IDOT permits municipalities to provide this performance security in
the form of a resolution, rather than a bond. The subject resolution would
provide for such security for a two-year period.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-14-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-16 – 2007 – Traffic Signal Modernization at Green Bay
Road and Winnetka Avenue MFT Resolution, Engineering – Adoption. Mr. Saunders
explained that after extended negotiations, the Village has finally achieved
concurrence with IDOT on the design of this project to modernize the traffic
signal system at the subject intersection. The Village is now ready to proceed
with the necessary engineering. He noted that the budget contains $75,000 of
Motor Fuel Tax funds to cover the engineering costs associated with the
project, but that their expenditure must be approved via resolution.
Manager Williams suggested since this item relates to Item 9(d), that the
Council consider them simultaneously.
* * *
9(d)   Winnetka & Green Bay Traffic Signals – Design Engineering.
Mr. Saunders went on to explain that in May, 2004, the Village selected Clark-
Dietz, Inc., to perform the preliminary engineering work on the modernization
of the Winnetka Avenue and Green Bay Road traffic signals. However, since Green
Bay Road falls under the jurisdiction of the Illinois Department of
Transportation, their approval of the proposal was required, and it has taken
some time to work through numerous issues in order to obtain their concurrence
on the preliminary engineering design. As noted above, the Village is now ready
to proceed with a contract for the design engineering of the construction plans
and specifications. The preliminary engineering services agreement with Clark
Dietz, Inc. specifying the scope of work for the project estimates that it will
take 638 man-hours, for a total fee of $74,705.51, to complete the work.
* * *
   After Mr. Saunders responded to questions from the Trustees, Trustee
Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-16-2007
authorizing the expenditure of $75,000 of MFT funds for modernization and
improvements to the traffic signal at Green Bay Road and Winnetka Avenue. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Then Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award a contract for
design engineering for traffic signal improvements at the intersection of
Winnetka Avenue and Green Bay Road for an amount not to exceed $74,705.51,
using IDOT’s standard form of agreement. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-15 – 2007 – Combined Purchase of a Computer Aided
Dispatch (CAD) System with the Wilmette Police Department - Adoption. Attorney
Janega explained that the intergovernmental agreement authorized by this
resolution represents several years of work between the Village of Winnetka and
the Village of Wilmette. She went on to review the process used to select
vendors and compare cost proposals as well as to enumerate key provisions of
the agreements with Wilmette and with the selected vendor, New World Systems.
Manager Williams pointed out that by cooperating on this intergovernmental
project, the two villages will save approximately $350,000 over the initial
implementation of the system.
Ms. Janega and Chief DeLopez responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to adopt Resolution R-15-2007,
authorizing the execution of the Intergovernmental Agreement between the
Village of Wilmette and the Village of Winnetka for the Joint Acquisition,
Operation and Maintenance of Hardware and Software for a Computer Assisted
Police Dispatch System, substantially in the form presented. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger,
Behles, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Berger then moved to approve the joint purchase, with the Village of
Wilmette, of New World Systems’ CAD/Mobile/RMS product, at a total purchase
cost not to exceed $663,200, subject to the terms and conditions substantially
in the form set forth in the Standard Software License and Services Agreement
dated February 28, 2007. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Ordinance No. M-4-2007 – IMEA Prairie State Power Project – Introduction.
Attorney Janega reviewed the history of this issue, noting that the Council has
had several presentations on this transaction. She explained that the Illinois
Municipal Electric Agency is in the process of taking the final steps to become
a part of a power project in downstate Illinois with Peabody Coal and other
municipal bodies, and noted that the municipal bodies will own the lion’s share
of the plant. She explained that this transaction is similar to IMEA’s previous
power plant transactions, except that in this case the power plant will be a
“mine mouth” plant that will include the adjacent development of a new coal
mine to provide fuel for the power plant. Thus, IMEA’s ownership share will
include a corresponding ownership of the mine, as well as the plant. She
explained that pursuant to the Village’s 1990 power purchase agreement with
IMEA, the members have the option not to participate in the project, and
Winnetka now has to decide whether it will amend its purchase power agreement
with IMEA to participate in financing the project by purchasing power from the
new plant. She added that if the Village chooses to participate, it can select
the standard participation period of the life of the bonds, which is expected
to be 25 years, or can opt for a 15-year term as it has done in the past.
Because the Village is required by contract to make its decision by March 20th,
she recommended that the Council introduce the ordinance and complete its
discussion at the next meeting.
Manager Williams observed that Winnetka has done its own independent due
diligence in analyzing this project and should be proud of its process. He
explained that previous Councils have opted for 15-year debt schedules in order
to save interest and pay off the debt in a shorter period of time. He noted,
however, that IMEA is now a more mature, ongoing agency, particularly with the
addition of St. Charles and Naperville in its configuration. Therefore, the
situation is different this time, and Winnetka might want to consider extending
its debt schedule to the life of the bonds because, as the load growth of
member municipalities increases at a greater rate than Winnetka’s, more of the
debt will be pushed toward the larger, faster growing communities. He noted
that from a present value analysis, the yield curve is very flat. He added that
although the Village avoids the payment of interest if the debt is front-
loaded, from an equity standpoint the residents who live here after the shorter
debt service period would get the benefit of the improvements, without paying
for the debt. He also noted that extending the debt over a longer time lessens
the impact on electric rates.
Trustee Abell concurred with the arguments in favor of extending the 25-year
schedule.
Trustee Behles agreed, saying that with load growth changing, the Village is
likely to benefit from extending the debt schedule.
Trustee Ritchell indicated that he was not ready to vote one way or another on
this project, and Trustee Eilers indicated that he had questions relating to
confidential material. President Woodbury said that these would be discussed in
Executive Session.
President Woodbury asked if there was any disagreement with the Manager’s
recommendation to extend the debt schedule for the full length of the bond.
There being none, he called for a motion to introduce Ordinance No. M-4-2007
providing for a full term debt schedule. Trustee Abell so moved, seconded by
Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
This item was discussed earlier in the Agenda.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1499 and 1500. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commented on a number of the items and responded to questions. He then moved to
approve Warrant List No. 1499 in the amount of $664,695.71 and Warrant List No.
1500 in the amount of $248,275.18. The motion was seconded by Trustee Tucker.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell,
Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Post Office Redevelopment Site, Consultant Services. Mr. McKee explained
that the Post Office Project has evolved over time and its scope of work has
expanded necessitating adjustments in the approved contract amounts for both
consultants, The Lakota Group and S.B. Friedman & Co.
Manger Williams remarked that these are appropriate because both consultants
have had to produce more materials and attend more meetings than originally
anticipated because of the number of development concepts developed.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to increase the Post Office
redevelopment contract with Lakota Group by $73,261, to an amount not to exceed
$128,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to increase the Post Office
redevelopment contract with S. B. Friedman & Co., by $22,692, to an amount not
to exceed $147,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Update on Streetscape Master Planning Process – Lakota Contract. Mr.
Saunders provided an update on the status of the streetscape planning process.
He explained that the Committee is working through issues such as palettes and
geometrics, and that they hope to bring a draft master plan forward in the
spring for public discussion on implementation and funding. He noted that the
Committee has expended approximately $81,000 of the $114,579 authorized, and
that based upon the progress and the number of extra meetings projected beyond
the original scope of services, he anticipated that the final contract amount
might exceed the authorized sum.
Trustee Behles added that the Committee has moved to bi-monthly meetings in
order to complete their work in a timely fashion.
d)   Winnetka & Green Bay Traffic Signals – Design Engineering. This item was
discussed earlier in the Agenda.
e)   GIS Service Provider Agreement with Municipal GIS Partners. Mr. Saunders
explained that Winnetka is a member of Geographic Information Systems
Consortium (GISCon), a public entity that formed to help small and medium-sized
communities meet the challenges of developing effective information system
solutions. Since its inception, the Consortium’s membership has grown from 4
communities to 11. The Consortium permits members to avail themselves of staff
time and expertise without having to hire additional personnel. This means that
Winnetka has a full time specialist on site 3 days every 2 weeks. He
recommended approval of renewal of the Village’s service agreement with GISCon.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to enter into a service
agreement for GIS Services with Municipal GIS Partners, Inc., for a fee based
on the hourly rates set forth, not to exceed $449,570. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Chatfield Road Resurfacing Final Payment – Change Order. Mr. Saunders
explained that during last year’s construction season, staff found it
advantageous to complete resurfacing of Chatfield Road west of Hubbard Woods
School in conjunction with a sewer replacement project and that this Change
Order is the balancing of the Village’s two street reconstruction and
resurfacing contracts for last year. He asked the Council to authorize Change
Order #1 to the 2006 Street Resurfacing Contract awarded to Peter Baker & Son
by $32,307.32, to a contract amount of $209,203.12, noting that the total
expenditure for resurfacing between the two contracts is still less than the
amount authorized by the Council.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the Change Order
as requested. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   State Bid – Pickup Truck and Equipment Replacement. Mr. Saunders reviewed
a request to purchase a new pickup truck through the State of Illinois joint
purchasing program. The new truck would replace an existing vehicle that is
used for street repairs and maintenance and snow removal and has outlived its
useful life.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the purchase of a
truck through the State of Illinois joint purchasing program from Bob Ridings,
Inc., in the amount of $34,729.00. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and
Abell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Tree Trimming Contract. Mr. Saunders explained that the contractor to whom
the tree trimming and emergency time and material bid had been awarded has gone
out of business. They have only been paid for work performed. He noted that
because there remains a significant amount of work to be done under this
contract, staff contacted the second low bidder, Nels Johnson Tree Experts,
Inc., about completing the work. Nels Johnson has assured the Village that they
will hold to their original bid prices to perform the balance of the unfinished
work.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
i)   Replacement of 1994 Ford Road Rescue Ambulance. Chief Smith reviewed the
bid process for the purchase of a new ambulance to replace the Village’s 1994
reserve vehicle. He explained that the Village had participated in the
Northwest Municipal Conference vehicle purchasing program that identified two
vendors as providers. He noted that the bid documents are drawn so that the
Village is not obligated to purchase from the lowest bidder if there are valid
reasons to purchase from another vendor. If an alternate source is considered,
the Village must waive the competitive bidding process. He went on to explain
why the Fire Department recommends the purchase of a Foster Coach (Medtec)
ambulance over a Wheeled Coach ambulance, although the latter was the low
bidder identified in the NWMC purchasing program. The final vehicle would
consist of a Medtec modular box on a Freightliner chassis.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to waive the competitive
bids and award the contract for the chassis to Northwest Freightliner in the
amount of $66,930. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to waive the competitive bids
and award the contract for the ambulance module unit to Foster Coach Sales,
Inc., for the Medtec unit in the amount of $106,642, as outlined by price
quotation No. 10102-0003. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
6)   Reports.
a)   Village President. Mr. Woodbury presented Mr. McKee with a Certificate of
Achievement for Excellence in Financial Reporting awarded to the Village by the
Government Finance Officers Association, saying that it is the highest honor
achievable. He thanked Mr. McKee and his staff for their fine work.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles announced that the Streetscape Committee would be
meeting on Thursday morning at 7:30 a.m. and invited all interested persons to
attend.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Business Community Development Commission has
wrapped up its study of the C2 Overlay District and would soon be reporting to
the Council. He indicated that he has encouraged the property owners’ group
that has formed to continue to participate and speak on issues that are
important to them in order to be actively involved in formulating of vision of
what Winnetka’s business districts should look like in the future.
Trustee Ritchell invited all interested persons to attend an upcoming meeting
of the Ad Hoc Historic Residential Preservation Committee on Wednesday at 5:30
p.m. in the Public Safety Building.
c)   Village Manager. Mr. Williams reported on the first of the two Post Office
Committee public input meetings and encouraged residents to attend the upcoming
meeting on Saturday morning at 10:00 a.m. at North Shore Country Day School.
10)   Appointments.
Mr. Woodbury suggested the appointment of Anne Grubb to the Landmark
Preservation Commission for a full term to replace Stacy Tabachnik. Trustee
Tucker moved to appoint Ms. Grubb, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
He then suggested the appointment of Joan Evanich to the Design Review Board
for a full term to replace Lynn Osmond. Trustee Behles moved to appoint Ms.
Evanich, seconded by Trustee Tucker. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
11)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments, Possible Sale of Village Real Estate, and Power
Purchase Contracts pursuant to Section 2(c)(3), 2(c)(6), and 2(c)(23),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:55 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:56 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
March 13, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: April 3, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
13, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1. Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water &
Electric Brian Keys, Assistant Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla,
Distribution Engineer Bryan McInturff, Trustee Candidate King Poor, and 6
persons in the audience.
2. Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3. Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4. Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-4-2007 – IMEA Prairie State Power Project – Adoption.
Village President Ed Woodbury announced that the ordinance would authorize
Winnetka to participate in the financing of IMEA’s Prairie State Project by
purchasing power from the Project. He stated that this was the Council’s fourth
meeting on the subject and then invited anyone in the audience wishing to
comment on the matter to come forward.
Jennifer Walling, representing the Environmental Law and Policy Center, invited
Winnetka to join other municipal utilities that use clean, renewable energy
technology, saying that the possibilities for Winnetka are very exciting in the
area of sustainable energy. She stated that global warming is exacerbated by
coal fired plants, and noted that special bonds are available from the Federal
government, along with grants from the state, for local governments that want
to use renewable resources.
Anne Wedner, 3 Kent Road, said that it would seem that Winnetka should use new
coal technology so it can use Illinois coal, and should also choose
technologies other than coal. She urged the Council to work out a program to
use wind energy versus coal before committing to the Prairie State project.
Nancy Grant, 429 Provident, asked the Council to vote against participating in
the financing of the new coal plant, and questioned public involvement in the
decision. While acknowledging that the issue is a difficult one with energy
demands increasing, she reiterated her stance that the Council should not
approve the ordinance.
Jeff Cohen, 1028 Cherry, voiced his opinion that this is a situation of retro
thinking. He commented that in light of the overwhelming scientific evidence of
global warming, putting more particulates in the air to keep costs down is not
the way to go, and added that Winnetkans would probably be willing to pay up to
$100 a month for cleaner electricity.
Manager Williams responded that Winnetka is a member of the IMEA, which is the
Village’s wholesale power supplier. The Village has a contract to purchase all
of its power from IMEA through 2035. He explained that Prairie State will be
using state of the art technology that is 20% more efficient than other coal-
fired plants, and added that, unlike many other power plants. He noted that if
Winnetka chooses not to participate in the project, then the power Winnetka
would have purchased from Prairie State would come from old coal-fired
technology that is dirtier than Prairie State will be.
Mr. Williams also noted that President Woodbury has directed staff to pursue
other alternatives, including wind, and that the Village can acquire wind
generation credits through IMEA. He stated that he also plans to bring an
energy conservation program to the Council to expand on steps the Village has
already taken, such as converted street and traffic lights. He suggested that
through energy conservation and the purchase of wind credits through IMEA,
Winnetka can reduce what it buys from Prairie State. Prairie State will also
have room to add equipment to reduce greenhouse gases when the technology to
capture greenhouse gases becomes available. He observed that the Village’s
approach is similar to the one that the City of Springfield and the Sierra Club
agreed on, in that it calls for using new coal technology to reduce pollution
and identifying other renewable power sources.
President Woodbury observed that not all of the Village’s power will have to be
generated by Prairie State and that the Village will have options to have IMEA
buy some of its power for Winnetka from sustainable sources such as wind. He
pointed out that the plant will exist regardless of whether Winnetka signs the
contract, but that there are other environmental options to be investigated,
such as green buildings.
Manager Williams commented that the Village needs to have base load power that
operates around the clock, and that, while wind generated power is available to
meet intermediate load, it is not a base load power source.
Trustee Abell remarked that this is the fourth meeting at which the Council has
discussed Prairie State and that the Council is very transparent when it comes
to setting public policy, adding that information on all of the meetings was
available on the Village’s web site and notices have been published. He
observed that the trustees have gone through an extensive process in vetting
the project, and have asked the same kinds of questions as are being asked
tonight. He added that Winnetka is the only IMEA member that does its own due
diligence, which has included asking questions and giving feedback that IMEA
has then brought into its contract discussions. He noted that the technology at
Prairie State can be retrofitted to cut carbon emissions. He added that the
Council had gone through a lot of analysis and expressed the opinion that
participating in Prairie State operates to the Village economic advantage and
that the Village is doing the responsible thing with regard to long-term
stabilization of electric rates and moving away from older and dirtier coal
technology. He also observed that all of the trustees represent the Village as
a whole, and that all of them are responsive to calls from any resident.
Following additional comments from the audience, President Woodbury responded
that in addition to agenda packets being available on the Village’s website,
the meetings are televised and every meeting is open to the public.
Trustee Behles expressed the opinion that the Council is forward-thinking, and
agreed that the issue was debated at great length and discussed thoroughly. He
indicated that Prairie State will improve the environmental profile of the
totality of Winnetka’s power sources, and will be far cleaner than going to the
open market.
Mr. Williams observed that most renewable energy sources are not base load
sources and added that, if all the coal-fired plants in the U.S. met the
standards of TC2 and Prairie State, the U.S. would meet the standards of the
Kyoto protocol.
Trustee Berger acknowledged that it has been a challenge to meet the tight
schedule because of the March 19 deadline for signing the IMEA contract, but
that project will provide energy from a much cleaner source and is the right
thing to do.
Trustee Ritchell noted that this was the fourth meeting on Prairie State and
reminded residents to be proactive when it comes to keeping informed on the
issues, saying that the meetings are open and notices are posted on a regular
basis. He added that he stood by the advice of the Village’s advisors on the
project.
Trustee Tucker commented that many of the underlying decisions about the
Village’s purchase of electricity were made 15 to 20 years ago and she is
grateful that the topic of energy sources has been raised for discussion on a
going forward basis. She pointed out that the trustees ask the same questions
that the residents do, and that they have been doing that in this case. She
also expressed her appreciation to the audience members who have raised the
questions tonight.
Trustee Eilers said that he was voting in favor of the Prairie State ordinance,
but with concerns. He stated his belief that prudent stewardship for IMEA would
be a goal of energy diversification and not to cultivate a base load supported
by 100% coal, using the analogy of an investment portfolio that should be a
sensitive mixture of equities and bonds, even though one type of investment
might be overwhelmingly attractive at any particular time. He explained that
his economic concerns are: (1) that the process was flawed because there was a
short time frame for due diligence and what he considered to be key agreements
were not available; (2) that the transaction was contractor driven, so the
economics could be better for Winnetka and its IMEA partners; and (3) the
risks, especially of owning and operating a coal plant, are significant and
could be mitigated. He also stated that, since this is a take-it or leave-it
proposition, it is not sensible to let the best be the enemy of the good. He
commented that Chapman & Cutler is a superb choice for bond issues, but its
role is to opine on the corporate authority behind the bonds and the validity
of the transaction, but not on the economic concerns raised in the Village’s
review. He also expressed concern that Peabody’s owning 25% of the project does
not represent “skin in the game” if that equity position was purchased through
a land value write-up of property that had no prospective buyers other than the
Prairie State project. Noting that the Village Manager’s analysis of the worst
case scenario showed that Winnetka’s electric rates would increase by 10% if
Prairie State produced no power, he concluded that would be a very manageable
downside. In concluding, he stated that given IMEA’s track record and the
Village Manager’s deep involvement, proceeding to participate in the project
would be better for the Village than standing on the sidelines.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to approve the ordinance.
In discussing the motion, Trustee Abell reiterated that he favors the project,
stating his belief that it is in the Village’s long term interests. Trustee
Ritchell noted that he trusts the Village’s advisers. Trustee Behles again
noted that participating in the project would improve the Village’s
environmental profile while safeguarding the Village’s access to reasonably
priced power sources for years to come, and that the project provided a rare
opportunity that the Village should not miss.
By roll call vote the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Woodbury thanked those in the audience for attending and invited them
back to join in future Council discussions on related issues. He reiterated
that Winnetka is best off joining the project and using the information
gathered in the course of the Village’s due diligence process to influence IMEA
as to how the contracts are written and the plant is developed.
5. Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the special
meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The meeting adjourned at 8:20 p.m.
                  _________________________________
                  Recording Secretary
March 13, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 1, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, March
13, 2007 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:25 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water
& Electric Brian Keys, Assistant Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla,
Trustee Candidate King Poor, and 0 persons in the audience.
2)   Pension Funding Information.
Finance Director Ed McKee reported at the request of Trustee Tucker, he had
prepared a packet of material for Council’s review on the issue of pension
funding.
Mr. McKee noted that to some extent the pension funding is beyond the Village’s
control since the state sets the benefits, and the only recourse at the local
level is the autonomy to determine staffing levels. He observed that the Police
and Fire pension funds have been experiencing a decrease in the assumed
earnings rate. He added that even a small decrease has a big impact because of
the size of the assets, and subsequently impacts the tax levy.
Mr. McKee summarized the returns of the past six years, noting that a 6 1/2 %
return figure is reasonable, and answered questions from the Trustees. He
mentioned that he had not budgeted for any withdrawals from the pension
stabilization fund.
3)   Discussion: Resolution R-12-2007 – General Permit & License Fees.
Attorney Janega explained that the intent of adding a schedule for special
service fees is to establish by resolution some fixed rates for Village staff
and resources and to provide a touchstone for services on building and
construction fees. She then gave an overview of the additional charges that
were being added.
Discussion: Resolution R-13-2007 – Building, Zoning, Construction Fees.
Attorney Janega provided an explanation of the changes in the subject fees. The
Council discussed the changes with Attorney Janega, after which Mr. McKee
assured them that the fees could be adjusted at any time, if the need arises.
Electric Load Growth Analysis. Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys reviewed
the 2006 average annual residential use, explaining that for every month in
2006, the actual cost of power was higher than the base cost. He noted that the
Village’s electric consumption is on a slow upward trend, with the long-term
projection at a little over 2%. He observed that IMEA’s growth projections have
been accurate.
4)   Discussion: Cost of Wholesale Power Adjustment.
Manager Williams reported that staff has been discussing options for the cost
of power adjustment, and that several scenarios have surfaced.
Mr. Keys reported that the idea of the base cost is to smooth rates for
residents so they don’t see the fluctuations of the marketplace on every bill.
He explained that power costs vary over time due to seasonal factors, market
conditions and IMEA’s power contracts. He gave an overview of how the Power
Cost Adjustment (PCA) is calculated, noting that the existing formula, while
smoothing out fluctuations in energy costs for residents, is no longer
effectively collecting the balance owed.
Mr. Keys presented several options to change the PCA formula to make it more
effective. After a brief discussion with the Council about the various
scenarios, he recommended that the Village collect the outstanding balance over
a 24-month period, and revise the formula for calculating the PCA by using a
forecasting method, instead of looking back at previous months’ rates.
5)   Electric Rate Comparison.
Mr. Keys compared a standard ComEd residential electric bill with one from
Winnetka, which showed that the Village’s rates are comparable to the new ComEd
rates.
Manager Williams distributed a handout containing several snapshots of the
Village’s electric fund balance, noting that although the Electric Department
is having a good year, it is important to stay competitive with ComEd.
President Woodbury commented that revenue from the sale of electricity to
residents is used to keep property taxes down. The Trustees and Manager
Williams discussed the desirability of keeping rates consistent with the open
market, in order to transfer funds from the Electric Fund to the General Fund
each year.
6)   Discussion, Woodley Road Police Contract.
Manager Williams reported that the subject contract expires at the end of March
and that staff is preparing to re-negotiate the agreement for another term.
Police Chief Joe DeLopez reported that the Winnetka Police Department has been
providing contracted 24-hour police service to the Woodley Road Association
since January 1, 1992, and gave an overview of the types of services that are
provided to the Woodley Road area. He recommended a new three or four year
contract, and asked the Council for direction.
Mr. McKee and Manager Williams discussed the pricing options and answered
questions from the Trustees, after which a decision was reached to enter into a
new contract with the Woodley Road Association.
7)   Discussion: NTHS Parking Lease.
Mr. McKee reviewed the process whereby the New Trier High School parking lease
was negotiated. He said that since item was tabled at the January 16, 2007,
meeting staff has prepared additional information for Council’s review. He
asked for guidance on how best to proceed.
The Trustees discussed the issue of student and employee parking at New Trier,
with the consensus being that staff negotiated a contract that was in keeping
with Council direction in the spring of 2006. Chief DeLopez remarked that
commuters and neighbors were complaining less about parking problems since New
Trier took over enforcement of the area. It was decided to leave the contract
in place and renegotiate in two years’ time.
8)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
9)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:10 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
March 20, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 3, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
20, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent: Trustees
David Abell and Tom Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Fire Chief
Scott Smith, Trustee Candidate King Poor and approximately three persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   April 3, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   April 10, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 6, 2007, Regular Meeting. There being no corrections or additions,
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as
drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Budget and Fee & Rate Resolutions. Before calling for votes on the various
budget and fee and rate resolutions, President Woodbury remarked that the
Council had completed a comprehensive review of the Budget over numerous
meetings. He thanked staff for their efforts and time.
   i)   Resolution No. R-7-2007 - 2007-2008 Village Budget –Adoption. There
being no additional comments or questions, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to adopt the 2007-2008 Budget Resolution. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and
Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-8–2007 - Water Rates – Adoption. There being no
additional comments or questions, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker,
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-8-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-9–2007 - Electric Rates – Adoption. There being no
additional comments or questions, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-9-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
   iv)   Resolution No. R-10–2007 – Sewer Rates – Adoption. There being no
additional comments or questions, Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell,
moved to adopt Resolution R-10-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
   v)   Resolution No. R-11–2007 - Refuse Rates – Adoption. There being no
additional comments or questions, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell,
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-11-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Berger. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
   vi)   Resolution No. R-12-2007 – General Permit & License Fees – Adoption.
There being no comments or questions, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-12-2007. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Berger, and Behles: Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
   vii)   Resolution No. R-13 – 2007 – Building & Construction Fees – Adoption.
There being no comments or questions, Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-12-2007. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Berger, Behles, and Ritchell. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. McKee and staff for their hard work on the
Budget.
b)   Resolution No. R-17–2007 – Unincorporated Charges for Fire and Rescue
Services – Adoption. Chief Smith explained that the Kenilworth Fire Service
Agreement, which was approved by the Council in December 2006, establishes the
formula for service that is used each year to determine the annual fees. It is
based on population, equalized assessed valuation, and number of calls. The
subject resolution approves those charges for unincorporated New Trier
Township.
Trustee Berger confirmed with Chief Smith that the fee being charged Kenilworth
residents is similar to the cost to residents of Winnetka.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-17-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Eilers.
c)   Resolution No. R-2–2007 – NTHS Parking Lease – Adoption. Mr. McKee briefly
reviewed the history of this item. He explained that the subject resolution
would authorize the intergovernmental parking agreement negotiated with New
Trier High School, the terms of which are in keeping with the Council’s
discussions as to what would be acceptable for renewal. He noted that the
proposed contract reflects a significant move towards a more market-based
approach to parking costs and pointed out that the School has been successfully
performing the enforcement responsibilities that are a part of the new
contract.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-2-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Eilers.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2007 – Liquor Code Amendments - Introduction. Attorney
Janega noted that this Ordinance will be designated as Ordinance No. MC-4-2007
when it comes up for adoption. She explained that, by law, the Village
President is the Local Liquor Control Commissioner and the Village Code
designates the Village Trustees as the Liquor Advisory Board. The Village Code
also sets out the Village’s standards, procedures, and administrative structure
for the issuance, renewal, suspension and revocation of liquor licenses. She
related that recent experience showed that the current procedure of having the
Liquor Advisory Board conduct license suspension hearings can be costly for
both the Village and the licensee and can delay a final decision because of
scheduling difficulties, which led Trustee Abell to suggest streamlining the
process. She stated that Ordinance MC-4-2007 would amend the Liquor Control
regulations to give hearing authority to the Liquor Commissioner, who would
still be able to refer a matter to the Advisory Board, if needed. If the
Commissioner were considering revocation or a suspension of more than 15 days,
the matter would then be referred to the Advisory Board for its input and
recommendation. She added that the Ordinance would also amend the eligibility
standards for a liquor license to declare that anyone with a delinquent Village
account would not be eligible for a license. Attorney Janega then responded to
questions from the Trustees.
After remarking that the draft ordinance appears to address the issues raised
by Trustee Abell and provides the sought after flexibility, Trustee Tucker,
seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-4-2007. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2007 – Film Production and Special Event Permit Fees –
Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that in keeping with previous
discussions, Ordinance MC-3-2007 would amend the film production and special
event permit chapters of the Village Code to eliminate the rate formulas and to
provide for rates to be set by Council resolution. She noted that the new rates
were included as a part of the General Permit and License Fees Resolution, and
that the proposed amendment would make the fee resolution and the Village Code
consistent with each other.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-
3-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
John Vlahakis, 1018 Ash St., expressed some frustration over what appeared to
be a lack of cooperation between the Village and the Park District regarding
the possible use of the landfill area and the Village Yards to accommodate the
Park District’s proposed new maintenance facility. He asked the Council for
clarification of the Village’s position.
President Woodbury commented that the Village has been working, and will
continue to work, with the Park District to find a workable solution. He noted
that the Village has helped the Park District perform a needs analysis and
offered to cooperate on various pieces of land on and around the landfill for
which the Village sees no immediate use. He pointed out that the Park District
is a separate governmental body with a separate Board and a separate mission,
and one of the problems is that the Park District serves residents of
Northfield as well as those of Winnetka. He also mentioned that the Park Board
has issues of accessibility that it must consider when planning for their
maintenance center.
Manager Williams added that the Village has made a public inventory of existing
space that might be available for the Park District’s use and consistently
offered to share services, such as fueling facilities, and to facilitate the
construction of a Park District building on property near the landfill. He
noted, however, that there are significant issues inherent in building on the
landfill.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1501 and 1502. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1501 in the amount of $1,215,128.04 and Warrant
List No. 1502 in the amount of $268,802.11. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Eilers.
b)   Appeal: Driveway Removal – 1039 Ash Street. Mr. Saunders explained that
pursuant to an ordinance adopted in April 2004, any interior lot on a block
served by an alley is required to be accessed by a single driveway from the
alley. That ordinance also provides that if a new house is built on such a lot,
and the existing driveways do not conform to the new standards, the existing
non-conforming driveway shall be removed. However, in September of 2005, the
Village issued a building permit for a new residence on the subject property
where the previous house was accessed by a single driveway from Ash Street.
This driveway was on the approved plans, along with the proposed construction
of a new brick paver parking area in the front yard to be accessed from the
street. When the Village performed its final inspections prior to issuing the
Certificate of Occupancy, it was discovered that the driveway was erroneously
permitted, and the builder was informed that the Village Code requires removal
of the driveway. The builder is appealing this decision, asking that the
driveway be permitted to remain.
Manager Williams acknowledged staff’s mistake. He noted that one of the
features of this situation that sets it apart from the one that prompted the
enactment of the April 2004 ordinance is the fact that the driveway is off to
the side and that the impervious surface is less than the 30% maximum allowed
under the Zoning Ordinance. Mr. Williams added that the Village Attorney feels
that there are unique circumstances that apply in this case so as not to set a
precedent by letting the driveway remain and suggested that the practical
solution here is that the Council agrees to do so.
President Woodbury commented that it was the intent of the Council in 2004 that
people who have alley access should use it, and that garages should be off of
that alley in the rear of the property.
Trustee Berger said the design appears to make sense to her.
Trustee Behles mentioned that the Code requirement eliminates curb cuts and
facilitates parking more cars on the street. He said, however, that he believes
the Developer in this instance represented himself in good faith to the Village
and that it does not seem appropriate to penalize him for following the rules
as they were dictated to him.
Manager Williams pointed out that 95% of the Village’s streets don’t have
alleys, but suggested revisiting the issue at some time.
Trustee Ritchell said that, generally, he is not in favor of parking cars in
front yards and doesn’t want to encourage the practice.
Hearing a consensus to permit the driveway to remain, President Woodbury called
for a motion to ask the Village Attorney to prepare a resolution to that
effect. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, so moved. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell,
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
c)   Mirani’s Liquor License Issue. Ms. Janega announced that this matter has
been resolved by Mr. Mirani’s payment of the Village’s outstanding bills. She
said that the amendments to the Liquor Code introduced previously would help
prevent future similar occurrences.
d)   Electrical Line Clearance (Tree Trimming). Mr. Keys explained the Water &
Electric Department would like to renew for a third year the services of Nels
Johnson Tree Experts for electrical line tree trimming and clearance. This
contract was bid in 2005, and the contract provided for its extension for a
third year at the Village’s discretion. He said that the electric line
clearance contract will be rebid competitively in January of 2008. Mr. Keys
noted that Nels Johnson has been performing line clearance work for many years
and said they are well-trained in working around energized conductors.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize the extension
with Nels Johnson Tree Experts as recommended at a total cost not to exceed
$74,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Eilers.
e)   Parkway Tree Trimming, Removal, and Maintenance. Mr. Saunders reviewed the
bids for parkway tree trimming, removal, and maintenance, saying that given the
estimates it makes sense to award the bids in sections. He noted that the
budget contains $150,000 for parkway tree maintenance, trimming and removal and
$300,000 for the Emerald Ash Borer contingency. He recommended that they be
awarded to the lowest bidder for each contract, as follows:
   a.   Ash Tree and Stump Removal – Kinnucan Co.
   b.   Parkway Tree & Stump Removal – Nels Johnson Tree Experts, Inc.
   c.   Parkway Tree Trimming – Nels Johnson Tree Experts, Inc.
   d.   Emergency Time and Material Parkway Tree Maintenance – Asplundh
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the bids as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Eilers.
f)   Diesel Generator #8 – Vertical Drive Assembly Change Out. Mr. Keys
reviewed this request for a service technician and parts to support the
replacement of the vertical drive assembly on Diesel Generator #8. The vertical
assembly is critical to ensuring the continued reliability of the engine
component of Diesel #8, which is used as an emergency back-up for the Water
Plant and is required for the start-up of the Electric Plant boilers. He
explained that this project would be similar to the work done last fall on
Diesel #9, and recommended that the work be awarded to Fairbanks Morse Engine,
the original equipment manufacturer.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the issuance
of a purchase order to Fairbanks Morse Engine for a total cost not to exceed
$28,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and
Eilers.
g)   Outdoor Seating Permits and Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License Riders. Mr.
Williams explained that eleven local restaurants have submitted applications
for outdoor seating permits, along with the required certificates of insurance
and seating layouts, as follows:
1.   
Song O’Sixpence
Little Ricky’s
Corner Cooks
Crepes Cranny Café
Caffe Buon Giorno
Caribou Coffee
Panera
Starbucks Cafe
Sweet Shop
Café Aroma
Chocolate Box
He added that Song O’Sixpense, Little Ricky’s, and Corner Cooks had also
applied for Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License Riders.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the Outdoor
Seating Permits as requested, contingent upon the Village Engineer’s final
approval of table layouts, for the period April 1 through November 30, 2007. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the applications
for Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License Riders for the three restaurants. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that there will be another Streetscape
Committee meeting on Thursday morning, at which the Committee hopes to discuss
cost estimates and mapping. They hope to get their report to the Council by
early summer.
Trustee Berger reported that she had attended a recent NWMC meeting and
summarized some of the topics that were discussion, including new cable laws
and pension costs. Ms. Berger also mentioned that Wheeling is considering
joining Red Center, which would be good for Winnetka in that its costs would
decrease.
Trustee Ritchell announced the upcoming scheduled meeting of the Ad Hoc
Historic and Residential Preservation Committee.
c)   Village Manager. None.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
11)   Appointments.
Mr. Woodbury suggested the appointment of Mike Duhl to the Board of Fire &
Police Commissioners for a full term to replace Bill Meuer. Trustee Behles
moved to appoint Mr. Duhl, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
He then suggested the reappointment of John O’Malley to the Police Pension
Board. Trustee Berger moved to appoint Mr. O’Malley, seconded by Trustee
Behles. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Finally, he suggested that Molly Lien be appointed to replace Mark Crane as
Chairperson of the Zoning Board of Appeals. Trustee Tucker moved to appoint Ms.
Lien, seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Pending and Probable Litigation pursuant to
Section 2(c)(3) and 2(c)(11), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:00 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent: Trustees Abell
and Eilers. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Abell and Eilers. The meeting
adjourned at 10:01 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
April 3, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 1, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
3, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Trustee
Candidate King Poor and approximately two persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 6(a), Ordinance No. MC-4-2007 regarding
the Liquor Code Amendments has been removed from the Agenda. The Trustees
approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   April 10, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   April 17, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 13, 2007, Special Meeting. Trustee Behles suggested the removal of a
repetitive sentence. There being no other corrections or additions, Trustee
Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as amended. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   March 20, 2007, Regular Meeting. There being no corrections or additions,
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as
drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2007 – Liquor Code Amendments - Adoption. As noted
above, this item was removed from the Agenda for the evening.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2007 – Film Production and Special Event Permit Fees –
Adoption. Attorney Janega briefly reviewed the proposed amendments to the
Village Code pertaining to Film Production and Special Event Permit Fees,
noting that the changes reflect that fees are set from time to time by
resolution of the Village Council. There being no further comments or
questions, President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt Ordinance MC-3-2007
as introduced. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, so moved. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker,
Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-18-2007 – Permitting a Front Yard Driveway at 1039 Ash
St. – Introduction. Attorney Janega briefly reviewed this matter that was
discussed at the March 20th Council meeting, noting that the recitals reflect
the administrative history that sets this situation apart from any others so
that it doesn’t set a precedent. President Woodbury called for a motion to
adopt the Resolution authorizing the newly constructed driveway at 1039 Ash
Street to remain. Trustee Berger so moved. Trustee Behles seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2007 – Parking Ticket and Village Sticker Ticket Fine
Adjustments – Introduction. Ms. Janega explained that during the budget
meetings it was determined by the Council that fines should be modified for
parking and sticker violations, in part because the current fee structure has
become a disincentive for compliance. She noted that the new fees are more
likely to be enforced in the court system.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-5-
2007, adjusting ticket fines for parking and sticker violations. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-5-1007 – Zoning Variation: 1249 Scott Street –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for four zoning variations to
permit the construction of a new, tandem, attached two-car garage and one-
story, covered front entrance. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the applicants
previously requested relief for the construction of a two-car garage and were
denied. This application represents their efforts to address concerns raised
during the earlier process. He noted that the existing shed and carport would
be removed as a part of the improvements and explained that the side yard
setback variation is requested in order to have the east wall of the proposed
garage align with an existing chimney bump out. He added that the front yard
setback variation is required because the proposed front entrance would have a
setback of 31.86 feet, whereas the average setback of all other properties on
the block face is 38.28 feet. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 5-0 to recommend approval of the variations.
Trustee Berger inquired whether aligning the east wall would violate the
Village’s rules regarding required articulation. Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that
it would not.
Speaking from his own experience, Trustee Ritchell remarked that the proposed
10’ wide garage would be very narrow.
Trustee Tucker expressed concern because the proposed construction would be
adding nonconformity to an existing nonconforming lot. She noted that sometimes
lots are simply not large enough to accommodate all that is desired.
President Woodbury asked Mr. D’Onofrio to comment about the impact of the eaves
on the roofed lot coverage, which he did.
Trustee Behles concurred with Trustee Tucker’s comments about some lots being
unable to accommodate all of the desired development, but noted that in this
case there are mitigating factors. He noted that the design has been
sensitively executed to try to make the improvements more conforming and that
the final bulk will not be significantly greater than what already exists. He
said that he would support the Zoning Board’s recommendation as in his opinion
this design is both reasonable and feasible.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the garage cannot be added in
the back of the lot because of its slope and said that he views the topography
and the trees as presenting a hardship. He added that he believes that the
applicants have been very accommodating.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the Ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1503 and 1504. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1503 in the amount of $710,384.20 and Warrant List
No. 1504 in the amount of $339,271.55. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   2007 Parkway Elm Injection Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for
injecting parkway American Elm trees with a fungicide to protect against Dutch
Elm Disease, a fatal fungal infection. He explained that the Village has done
this in the past and that the process shows some success.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award a contract for
parkway Elm injections for FY2007-08 to Nels Johnson Tree Experts, for an
amount not to exceed $25,190. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   2007 Parkway Tree Planting. Mr. Saunders explained in that in 2006 the
Council awarded the contract for the 2006 Parkway Tree Planting program to St.
Aubin Nursery, pursuant to a public bidding process. He reported that the
contractor had agreed to hold the 2006 bid prices for an additional year, and
that staff recommends extending the contract for the 2007 Parkway Tree Planting
program.
President Woodbury pointed out that there is an opportunity for residents to
participate and to purchase trees and to plant them through this program.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award a one-year
extension to the 2006 Parkway Tree Planting contract to St. Aubin Nursery, for
an amount not to exceed $46,430. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, and
Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the new edition of the Winnetka
Report, including information on Clean-up Week, would be mailed shortly.
c)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported on a technical violation of the
EPA rules at the Water Plant regarding reporting procedures. He also announced
that Winnetka had been selected as a Tree City USA for the fourteenth year.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Abell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments, Personnel Matters, Pending and Probable
Litigation, Possible Sale of Village Real Estate, and Possible Purchase of Land
pursuant to Section 2(c)(3), 2(c)1, 2(c)11, 2(c) 6, and 2(c)(5), respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 8:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:55 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:56 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
April 10, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 1, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
10, 2007 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:41 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys,
Assistant Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, Trustee Candidate King
Poor, and approximately 65 persons in the audience.
2)   Proposal to Place Dr. King Plaque on Village Green.
President Woodbury started by inviting the residents who live near the Village
Green to speak first.
Jeff Johnson, 644 Oak, said he was impressed by the 8th graders’ hard work and
said he thought the project would be an enhancement to the Village Green.
President Woodbury said that several neighbors of the Village Green had emailed
their support of the plaque.
Ms. Ceci Gigiolio, a Social Studies teacher at Washburne Middle School, called
several students forward to give their reasons for requesting the commemorative
plaque.
Several students came forward and alternately spoke about the historical
context of Dr. King’s visit in 1965, the Fair Housing Movement and Summer
Project of the North Shore, and the Interfaith Housing Center, which came out
of the Fair Housing Movement. They submitted more petitions in support of the
project, and several preliminary designs for the plaque.
Several community members also came forward to endorse the project. Gail
Schecter, of the Interfaith Housing Center, commented that the event was Dr.
King’s first speech in a mostly white suburb and the largest crowd ever
gathered at the Village Green. Mary Garrison, Winnetka Park Board President,
expressed the Park District’s support for the project, and commended the class
and their teacher for their efforts.
Robin Grossberg, 773 Prospect, and Christy Southern, 1286 Forest Glen, who were
both present for Dr. King’s speech on the Village Green in 1965, also voiced
their strong support for the project.
David James, a Winnetka resident and veteran of the Tuskegee Airmen, was
invited by President Woodbury to give his comments. Mr. James said that he
thought the monument should also be to the citizens of Winnetka, since he
couldn’t think of another community that would have invited Dr. King to speak
in 1965. He recalled the riots that broke out in other Chicago suburbs when
people of color moved in. Mr. James also provided reminiscences about Dr. King
and his visit to Winnetka, and told of how the Fair Housing Movement and the
welcoming attitude of Winnetka residents gave him the courage to move his
family from Chicago’s south side to Winnetka the year after Dr. King spoke at
the Village Green.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Chamber of Commerce met earlier and gave the
project their unanimous support.
Trustee Berger voiced her support for the project, and said that she was struck
by Mr. James’ comment that the Village deserves to be honored for its
progressive attitude.
Trustee Ritchell commented that he was proud to be part of this particular
meeting, and suggested that the plaque be put on the spot where Dr. King spoke
in 1965.
Trustee Behles said he felt privileged to hear from Mr. James, and added that
the children in the community had taught a great civics lesson to the assembled
adults and the Village as a whole.
Trustee Abell noted that he was inspired to hear the comments, and reminded the
audience that the Village is about to embark on a discussion of affordable
housing, another difficult topic. He observed that economic discrimination
seems to be taking the place of the racial discrimination of 40 years ago, and
urged the students not to stop with the Dr. King plaque, but to lobby for other
things as well.
Trustee Tucker thanked the group for their presentation and said that although
the Council has been generally in favor of the idea, it was gratifying to see
all the different community groups such as neighbors of Green, the Historic
Society, Interfaith Housing, Park District, Chamber of Commerce and VFW come
together to support it.
President Woodbury thanked the students for bringing the issue forward and
having the courage to speak their minds. He said the lesson for the students is
that community service has a lot of value and he encouraged them to stay active
now and in the future. Noting the unanimous support for the project, he
directed the Village Attorney to draw up an appropriate resolution for the next
Council meeting. He also suggested that the students appoint a small committee
to work with Trustee Behles on the final design and placement of the plaque.
3)   Repair/Replace Lead Water Services.
Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys reported that the cost to replace
leaking lead service has historically been assumed by the customer. He noted
that in light of a recent incident involving a commercial customer in the East
Elm Business District, staff re-examined this practice, and has identified some
options for the Council to consider.
Mr. Keys reviewed the history of Village policy on this matter, explaining that
in 1998 the Village received a letter from the Illinois Department of Public
Health in response to an inquiry about repairing a lead service line. The
letter stated that leaking lead service should be replaced rather than
repaired. Consequently, the Village has required leaking lead services to be
replaced from the main to the meter, with the customer bearing the cost. In
some cases, this involves street excavation and pavement repair. He noted that
Section 13.04.100 of the Village Code provides that the Village will pay to
repair service leaks between the valve and the water main, but that when leaks
occur between the valve and the residence, the homeowner pays for repairs.
Mr. Keys went on to explain that Mr. Peter Skalski, owner of Phototronics on
Elm at Lincoln, had a leaking lead service last winter, and was required to pay
for replacement. The situation was unusual because the main is on the north
side of Elm Street and the store is on the south side, which necessitated an
extensive excavation.
Mr. Peter Skalski questioned the provision making it his responsibility to
replace the section of pipe from the valve from the main, when the Village
would pay if it only needed repairs. He said he felt the code is not very clear
on this point.
Manager Williams observed that the Village has always distinguished between
repair and replacement and that, while the Illinois Department of Public Health
is clear on the subject, most neighboring communities don’t require replacement
of lead pipe.
Further discussion centered around who should be responsible for the
replacement of the section from the valve to the main. Mr. Keys explained that
the service is owned by the property owner and the main is owned by the
Village, but the Village repairs the valve line because it’s located under the
street. He estimated the number of lead services left in the Village at between
1,000 and 2,000.
After deliberating the policy implications of offering compensation to
residents replacing lead water services, Council directed staff to come back
with some options for cost sharing from the valve to the main.
4)   Request that Council Not Meet on Day of General Election.
Manager Williams reported that the issue of rescheduling Council meetings that
fall on election days has been examined in the past and has been brought before
the Council again at the request of Mollybelle Berish, a Winnetka resident.
Manager Williams gave an overview of the agenda materials, explaining that Ms.
Berish’s concern is that a civic minded person can’t be an election judge and
also attend the Council meetings. He noted that an earlier attempt to move the
meeting days to Thursdays fell through.
Trustee Berger indicated that the general election day is very important and it
makes sense for the Council to be a bit more flexible to accommodate the date.
Trustee Behles noted that Winnetka is one of the few communities that conduct
meetings on election day and it would be nice to settle the issue once and for
all.
Trustee Abell agreed with Trustee Berger that the Village should be flexible
about general election day.
After a short discussion, the Trustees asked Attorney Janega to draft a
provision that would reflect the importance of election day by allowing the
rescheduling of Council meetings that fall on a general election day.
5)   Open Meetings Policy – Electronic Attendance.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega reported on recent amendments to the Open
Meetings Act that address the impact of electronic communications on meetings
in two key areas. First, she noted that the definition of “meeting” has been
amended to include any type of contemporaneous communication, including such
things as emails and instant messaging. She observed that although a one-way
communication, particularly with staff, would not qualify as a meeting,
problems have occurred in some communities because the law was silent on the
effect of such things as exchanging and forwarding emails, or replying to all
senders. She explained that the Village’s practice has always been to avoid
these situations, and that the amendment now clearly establishes that such
things can constitute meetings.
Attorney Janega explained that the second provision of the amendment allows
public bodies to establish rules permitting members to attend from a remote
location, subject to five conditions, which she briefly outlined. She indicated
that to take advantage of the electronic attendance provision, the Council
would have to amend its rules, which are set out in the Village Code. She added
that the Council also has the discretion to decide as a matter of policy,
whether its boards, commissions and committees should be allowed to adopt their
own rules on the subject.
After a brief exchange about the remote attendance issue, in which most
Trustees agreed that it is not feasible given the technology limitations of the
current Council Chambers, the Trustees instructed Attorney Janega to draft an
ordinance prohibiting the practice.
6)   Executive Session.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn into Executive
Session. Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:00 p.m.
7)   Adjournment.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:30 p.m. There being no
further business, the meeting adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
April 17, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 1, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
17, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of Water &
Electric Brian Keys, Trustee Candidate King Poor, and approximately 17 persons
in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   May 1, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   May 8, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
None.
6)   Village Auditors.
Finance Director Ed McKee introduced the Village’s new auditor, Ron Amen, from
the auditing firm of Lauderbach & Amen. Mr. Amen informed the Council that the
Village’s annual audit has been scheduled to start around the end of May. He
encouraged anyone with questions or concerns to contact him directly. In
response to a question about the timing of the finished product, he estimated
that the final audit should be delivered by early August.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2007 – Liquor Code Amendments - Adoption. President
Woodbury announced that the subject ordinance would amend the Village Code as
it pertains to standards and procedures for the renewal, revocation and
suspension of liquor licenses. In the absence of Attorney Janega, he noted that
Trustee David Abell would give an overview of the amendments.
Trustee Abell explained that the amendments were proposed to simplify the
extensive public hearing process that was formerly used to deal with a liquor
violation. He explained that the Village President would now be designated to
hold the hearings under his capacity as Liquor Commissioner.
Trustee Abell noted that several significant changes had been made since the
amendments were first proposed, including adding new subsections to Section
5.09.040 and Section 5.09.280. He observed that in essence the new amendments
give more discretion to the Liquor Commissioner to deal with liquor violations,
plus the ability to assign a designee to assist him.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-4-2007.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2007 – Parking Ticket and Village Sticker Ticket Fine
Adjustments – Adoption. President Woodbury announced that this ordinance would
change ticket fines for parking and sticker violations. He called for
additional comments, and there being none, called for a motion to adopt the
Ordinance.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-5-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-5-2007 – Zoning Variation: 1249 Scott St. – Adoption.
Noting that this matter had been discussed at the April 3rd Council meeting,
President Woodbury called for additional comments or questions.
There being none, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt
Ordinance M-5-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2007 –Emerald Ash Borer Amendments – Introduction.
Public Works Director Steve Saunders reported that the Illinois Department of
Agriculture has instituted a quarantine zone in response to the discovery of
the Emerald Ash Borer along the North Shore. He indicated that since Winnetka
falls within this quarantine area, a number of code amendments need to be made
in order to bring the Village into compliance with EAB regulations.
Mr. Saunders explained that the primary regulations apply during the flight
season of the beetle, from April 30 to September 1. The rules prohibit the
moving of any ash wood that hasn’t had the bark removed or that has not been
ground into shreds no larger than one inch in two dimensions, which is smaller
than a standard wood chipper can accomplish. Mr. Saunders observed that in
order to implement these provisions, the infested ash trees and wood will be
declared a nuisance under an amendment to the Village’s nuisance code, similar
to the provisions for dealing with elms that have Dutch elm disease.
The Council discussed the quarantine provisions with Mr. Saunders, after which
Manager Williams requested Mr. Saunders to clarify how the collection for brush
and yard waste will be affected this summer. Mr. Saunders explained that since
the landscape waste transfer point is outside the quarantine zone, Village
staff will have to open the bagged brush to make sure there is no wood in it,
then separate the brush and grind it to less than one inch. He noted that fines
for violating the quarantine regulations can be quite large.
President Woodbury asked if the ban on transporting non-coniferous cut wood
applied to firewood and Mr. Saunders replied in the affirmative.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-6-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-19-2007 – Extension of Cell Site License Agreement (US
Cellular) – Adoption. Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys reported that the
one-year extension of the March 26, 1996, agreement with U.S. Cellular to
install cell antennas on the Water & Electric smokestack has expired. He
indicated that the Village is in the process of negotiating new terms for the
agreement and that U.S. Cellular has requested a short extension to complete
the negotiations.
After a brief discussion in which the Council ascertained that this final
extension should be sufficient to complete the agreement, approval was given to
grant a six-month extension of the old cell cite license.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to extend the cell site
license agreement with U.S. Cellular by six months, in order to allow for the
completion of negotiations for a new license agreement. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-21-2007 – Plaque Commemorating Dr. Martin Luther King’s
Speech on the Village Green – Adoption. President Woodbury noted that the
commemorative plaque had been discussed at two previous meetings, and that the
Council is proud to sponsor this particular resolution.
Trustee Behles announced that he had tentatively scheduled a meeting with the
school committee to discuss the design and placement of the plaque.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve Resolution R-21-
2007, approving a plaque commemorating Dr. King’s speech on the Village Green.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1505 and 1506. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1505 in the amount of $863,803.44 and Warrant List
No. 1506 in the amount of $463,386.67. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid #07-001: Scott Avenue Parking Structure Repairs. Mr. Saunders reported
that the budget contains $200,000 to upgrade and repair the Scott Avenue
parking structure, noting that no major repairs have been undertaken since its
construction in 1990.
He indicated that the bid documents contained a base bid plus an additional
scope of work for alternate items that could be added if the budget permits.
Although the total bids came in significantly higher than the amount budgeted,
he observed that if painting is removed from the list of alternates, he could
make up all but $10,000 of the difference due to $25,000 that was budgeted but
not spent for snow guards to be installed on the roof at 1390 Willow.
There was a short discussion about some of the repair components, after which
President Woodbury observed that he thought the painting could be scheduled at
a future date, as it would improve the appearance of the building.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the bid in the
amount of $234,700 for the repair of the Scott Avenue parking structure. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Underground Connectors – Bid Extension. Mr. Keys reported that in 2006,
the Village solicited bids for the supply of secondary electrical connectors
manufactured by Homac. The purchase order was awarded for a one-year period,
with an option to extend the contract for one additional year at the same
terms, conditions, and pricing. He recommended Council approve a new purchase
order to HD Supply.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to extend the existing bid
agreement at present unit prices in an amount not to exceed $36,000. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Turbine #6 Inspection/Overhaul, Technical Field Engineer Reports. Mr. Keys
reported that an overhaul for Turbine #6, similar to the work that was
undertaken on Turbine #7 last year, is planned for in the fall. He noted that
the Village has a good relationship with the engineer at Siemens Technical, and
recommended that his services be retained again.
Mr. Keys observed that the overhaul is a preventative maintenance to ensure
sustainability of operation in order to secure the IMEA generating credits,
adding that costs would be recouped through the credits in less than six
months.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award a purchase order to
Siemens Demag Delaval Turbomachinery, Inc. in an amount not to exceed $97,630,
for the overhaul of Turbine #6. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Capital Budget Status Report. Manager Williams reported that staff had put
together this report as a result of previous Council discussions about projects
that are budgeted for but don’t get completed in the timeframe allotted. He
gave a brief overview of last year’s accomplishments, highlighting several
major projects and outlining a few that are slated for the coming months. He
observed that all in all, he was pleased with last fiscal year, and expected to
complete the projects slated for this year.
11)   Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the Chamber of Commerce had two
important events upcoming: the Recognition Luncheon on May 16, and the Sidewalk
Sale, scheduled for July 20 & 21. He encouraged residents to save the dates and
to spend locally at the sidewalk sale.
Trustee Abell reported that at the last NWMC meeting, the recommended
legislative positions previously discussed were approved. He noted that the
NWMC is tracking HB1500, the Cable and Video Competition Law 2007, because it
impacts municipalities with regard to cable TV franchises. He encouraged
residents to write their congressperson opposing the creation of a national
franchise, which AT&T is attempting to do with its Project Lightspeed.
President Woodbury commented that he and Manager Williams had met with state
senator Jeff Schoenberg earlier in the day, and informed him of the Village’s
opposition to the project in its current form. He stressed that there is no
problem with competition, but the control should be kept local.
Trustee Ritchell announced that the ad hoc HRPC had met recently and that
possibly in the next several months would have a report to put before the
Council. He said he was unable to attend the Historical Society meeting, as it
conflicted with the HRPC meeting.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that: (1) staff has made changes to the
warning signs about the hand-held cell phone ban, and new signs would soon be
made that will be significantly larger, in order to get the word out to the
larger community; and (2) on the issue of the electric cost adjustment, staff
continues to evaluate the options and is waiting until the books close on last
year before making any firm recommendations.
12)   Appointments.
President. Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Betty Skalski to the
Business Community Development Commission for a full term. Trustee Ritchell,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to appoint Ms. Skalski. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
He then suggested the reappointment of Steve Kardel to the Business Community
Development Commission for a full term. Trustee Abell moved to appoint Mr.
Kardel, seconded by Trustee Behles. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
He went on to suggest the reappointment of Peggy Swartchild to serve a full
term on the Business Community Development Commission. Trustee Abell, seconded
by Trustee Ritchell, moved to appoint Ms. Swartchild. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
He then suggested the appointment of Litt Clark to the Zoning Board of Appeals
to replace Jane Dowding, whose term has expired. Trustee Tucker moved to
appoint Mr. Clark, seconded by Trustee Berger. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Finally, he suggested the appointment of Hal Francke to serve on the Zoning
Board of Appeals to replace Mark Crane, whose term has expired. Trustee Behles,
seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to appoint Mr. Francke, By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
13)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Personnel Matters and Pending and Probable
Litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(3), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(11), respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 9:06 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:50 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:51 p.m.
         
