President Woodbury presented Mr. Abell with a copy of the Resolution.
a)   Ordinance No. M-6-2007 – Zoning Variations & Special Use Permit: 275
Fairview, Greeley School - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request from
Winnetka School District 36 for a Special Use Permit and three zoning
variations to permit the construction of an addition at Greeley School. The two
zoning variations would be Intensity of Use of Lot variations, increasing the
roofed lot coverage and impermeable lot coverage, and a Maximum Building Size
variation increasing the Gross Floor Area.
He explained that the proposed two-story addition would be located at the
northeast corner of the existing school building and would require the removal
of a portion of the existing structure. He noted that the building is legal
nonconforming with respect to roofed lot coverage, impermeable lot coverage,
and GFA, adding that the proposed improvements otherwise comply with all other
zoning regulations.
Mr. D’Onofrio mentioned that in connection with the Special Use Permit a
parking study had been conducted. He enumerated the consultant’s conclusions,
as well as the Village Engineer’s responses thereto.
Finally Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the proposed improvements had been reviewed
by the Zoning Board of Appeals, Plan Commission, and the Design Review Board
and received favorable recommendations.
Shelley Clark, Business Manager for School District 36, thanked the Council for
its consideration and Village staff for their assistance in moving the School’s
projects through the Village’s processes in a timely manner.
George Reigle, Green Associates, Inc., the School’s architect, and David
McCallum, the landscape architect, reviewed the plans and responded to
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Behles asked whether the School had investigated replacing some of the
asphalt with permeable surface in keeping with suggestions from the Design
Review Board. Mr. Reigle responded that the idea had been considered but
explained that the site is not excessive and the existing hard surface is fully
used, so it would be difficult to take any away.
Trustee Behles also inquired about the plans to relocate the entry during a
future phase of the redevelopment. Mr. Reigle explained that the plan is to
reorient the entrance to the northwest corner. Ms. Clark added that the student
drop-off and pick-up locations would not change.
President Woodbury encouraged the School to continue to look at the impermeable
areas and examine creative ways in to get less asphalt as the School continues
to grow.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
6-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-7-2007 – Zoning Variations & Special Use Permit: 515
Hibbard Rd., Carlton Washburne School - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a
request from Winnetka School District 36 for a Special Use Permit and two
zoning variations to permit the construction of an addition at Carlton
Washburne School. The two zoning variations would be Intensity of Use of Lot
variation, increasing the roofed lot coverage, and a Maximum Building Size
variation increasing the Gross Floor Area.
He explained that the proposed two-story addition would be located at the east
end of the school building and would require the removal of a portion of the
existing structure. He noted that the building is legal nonconforming with
respect to GFA but does conform with respect to roofed lot coverage. He added
that the proposed improvements otherwise comply with all other zoning
regulations. He pointed out that because the new addition is located in the
floodplain, it will be necessary to lower the grades to provide additional
compensatory storage. Mr. D’Onofrio distributed copies of a Preliminary
Engineering Plan Review prepared by the Village Engineer regarding the
preliminary stormwater detention calculations, site utility plans, and site
grading plans.
Mr. D’Onofrio mentioned that in connection with the Special Use Permit a
parking study had been conducted. He enumerated the consultant’s conclusions,
as well as the Village Engineer’s comments on the study’s recommendations and
conclusions.
Finally Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the proposed improvements had been reviewed
by the Zoning Board of Appeals, Plan Commission and the Design Review Board and
received favorable recommendations. However, the Design Review Board commented
that the landscaping was inadequate and suggested that funds from the green
roof be used “on the ground” instead, and that modifications to the façade be
explored. They also expressed concern about the impact of the compensatory
storage. The Plan Commission suggested that the Village consider a condition
requiring Village approval of a construction staging and access plan and a
recommendation that the School District work with the Park District on the
relocation of the basketball courts. Their most significant concerns related to
drainage and the inadequacy of the landscaping plan.
Trustee Behles asked Ms. Clark to clarify the ownership of the land between
Skokie School and Washburne School, in particular the land upon which the
basketball courts are located. Ms. Clark said that all of the land is owned by
the School District.
President Woodbury confirmed with Ms. Clark that the basketball courts would be
removed permanently. She indicated that they would be.
Mr. Saunders summarized the Preliminary Engineering Plan Review. He concluded
that the proposed plan provides improved drainage conditions for the playfields
and controlled release for the stormwater generated on the site, and the
proposed grading will not look out of character. He noted that, while the
magnitude of the addition does not trigger the Village’s stormwater detention
requirement, the engineering plans increase the stormwater detention. He added
that, assuming the calculations underlying the plans are correct, the plans
also provide the compensatory storage required by Village’s floodplain
ordinance, because the property is in the floodplain.
Trustee Eilers asked whether the drainage plans comply with all FEMA
requirements and the Village’s floodplain ordinance, as indicated by Mr. Reigle
at the Plan Commission. Mr. Saunders stated that, assuming the underlying
calculations are correct, the plans are in compliance and the School District
will be required to be bring the plans into compliance if corrections are
needed.
Responding to a question from Manager Williams, Mr. Saunders stated that the
combination of the regrading and the slower discharge rate will improve
stormwater drainage.
Trustee Behles agreed that adding detention and restricting outflow would be
positive steps. He asked whether the functionality of the playfield would be
marginalized by these moves. Mr. Saunders indicated that he thinks that it
actually will be somewhat improved in terms of usability.
Trustee Eilers inquired about the concerns raised by the Design Review Board
and the Plan Commission.
Attorney Janega indicated that the Council can plug in whatever conditions it
determines to be appropriate before the ordinance is adopted.
Trustee Abell asked about the status of the green roof, saying that he would
very much like to see it stay to make a firm statement about environmentally
conscious construction.
Trustee Behles strongly agreed.
Ms. Clark again thanked the Village staff for their help in walking the School
District through the processes and procedures. She then responded to the issues
raised by the boards, as well as to the green roof question. She said regarding
staging, the School would continue to work closely with the Village to ensure a
safe environment and as minimal an impact as possible. She indicated that the
School is aware of the concerns about the removal of the basketball courts and
has had conversations with the Park District to address those concerns. She
noted that the drainage and grading issues had already been addressed, and with
regard to the landscaping, she referred to the upgraded landscaping plan that
had been submitted. Finally, she reported that the School Board has made no
final decision about the green roof.
Mr. Reigle provided an overview of the site, reviewing the floor plans and
elevations, and Gary Weiss, the project’s civil engineer, explained the
grading, drainage and stormwater detention plans. Two new detention areas will
be created and the contour of the playfields will be changed. He said that when
the project is completed the storage of stormwater will be enhanced for the
neighborhood and the usability of the playfields will be enhanced for students.
Trustee Eilers asked if Washburne would be required to comply with Winnetka and
other regulatory agency flood plain requirements. Mr. Weiss replied yes.
President Woodbury asked Mr. Reigle to comment on the Design Review Board’s
concerns, which he did. He explained that one of the design objectives was to
minimize encroachment on the already small ball fields and that moving the
building to the east would have a detrimental effect. He said the upgraded
landscaping plans were intended to soften the impact of the building. He added
that there is no good way to incorporate columns or reflect the architecture of
Skokie School in the new addition to Washburne because the styles are so
different.
Trustee Behles remarked that unlike the Greeley project, where the
architectural solution is easy to absorb, this is a dramatic change to what is
a very significant open space, particularly given that the architectural
solution doesn’t have a lot of windows along the lower portion and presents as
a block along the open space. He said he hoped that if modifications to the
building are not feasible, that the landscaping plan would address the hulking
mass. He suggested for example, the addition of vertical green walls or
trellises to soften the mass of the building. Mr. Behles reiterated his earlier
comments about the importance of keeping the green roof.
Mr. McCallum indicated that the School would explore the possibility of a
living wall.
Commenting on the green roof concept, School Board President Anne Kelly said
that the Board had spent a lot of time discussing the sustainability of the
design elements, and that ultimately the decision boils down to a cost trade
off, approximately $300,000 for the green roof vs. $100,000 without. She
indicated that while they would like to include the green roof and plan to bid
the project both ways, it ultimately may come down to an economic decision.
Trustee Tucker commented that she believes it would be inappropriate to impose
the green roof as a condition of the special use permit because the School
Board must act in the best interests of the children of the community.
Trustee Abell asked whether it could be added at a later date. Ms. Kelly
replied that it could.
President Woodbury summarized the Council’s comments as follows: (1) He
encouraged the School Board to continue to work with the Park Board to find a
suitable place to relocate the basketball courts. (2) With regard to the
drainage and grading issues, the new plan appears to address the concerns
raised. (3) He suggested that the Council’s desire to see a green roof be
recorded in the recitals, but acknowledged that this must be left to the School
Board to decide. (4) He reinforced the importance of developing an effective
construction access and staging plan to minimize the impact of the construction
project on the neighborhood. (5) Finally, he strongly encouraged the School
Board to revisit the landscaping plans with an eye toward resolving some of the
architectural concerns.
Trustee Ritchell inquired about the timing of the project. Mr. Reigle indicated
that both projects would occur simultaneously and said they hope to break
ground this summer. Ms. Clark added that the projects would be completed in
phases and that the Board will be coming back to the Council when it moves into
the second phase, including the front terrace of Washburne and the entrance to
Greeley.
Manager Williams thanked Ms. Clark and the development team for their hard work
and cooperation.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
7-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury congratulated the School Board on the passage of its
referendum, saying that the Village voted with a strong voice to support this
valuable asset to the community.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2007 – Emerald Ash Borer Amendments – Adoption. There
being no additional comments or questions regarding this ordinance that was
introduced at the April 17th meeting, Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-6-2007 bringing the Village Code into
compliance with State regulations regarding the Emerald Ash Borer. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-6-2007 – Third Addendum to IMEA Power Supply Contract –
Introduction. Mr. Keys explained that in order to provide power for its
members, IMEA entered into long term purchase agreements with energy
wholesalers and purchased interest in two power plants. In order to finance its
participation in the two power plant projects, IMEA issued revenue bonds that
can only be retired through the purchase of power by IMEA’s members. In
electing to participate, member municipalities obtained the right to purchase
power from the IMEA project at rates set by IMEA’s Board. The debt service
component of those rates was arrived at by spreading the cost of the debt among
all of the purchasing users over either 15 years or the full life of the bonds.
Winnetka elected to participate in both projects with the 15-year debt
amortization in order to reduce the overall interest expense by retiring the
debt sooner, even though higher annual payments are required. Winnetka was the
only member to select the 15-year amortization schedule for the Trimble County
2 plant. He went on to explain that extending the debt service for the TC2
would reduce the impact on electric rates and distribute part of the cost of
the plant to customers residing in Winnetka through 2035 who would be receiving
power from that plant. In addition, the longer amortization schedule could
result in a savings to Winnetka by shifting a portion of future debt payments
to other IMEA members that may have higher load growth rates in the coming
years.
Manager Williams added that this do-over on TC2 is a follow-up to discussions
during the Prairie State considerations, saying that it is in the Village’s
strategic best interests to extend the amortization schedule.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Abell, moved to introduce Ordinance M-8-
2007, the Third Addendum to the Power Sales Contract between IMEA and the
Village, that would extend the termination date of the Power Sales Contract to
2035 and change the payment for Winnetka associated with the amortization of
the 2006 Bonds debt service. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2007 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to
Scheduling Village Council Meetings – Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed a
proposed amendment to the Village Code prepared in response to Council’s
discussion at the March 10th Study Session. The proposed language provides
flexibility by requiring meeting changes to be made in conjunction with the
posting of the annual meeting schedule that is required by the Open Meetings
Act. It allows the sitting Council to review the established, regularly
scheduled meeting dates before the end of each year to identify potential
conflicts in the upcoming year, including conflicts with a general election,
and to reschedule those meetings if necessary. She pointed out that the
Ordinance retains the existing automatic rescheduling for July 4th and January
1st. It also allows the Council to determine not only whether to reschedule a
particular meeting, but also to determine the best day for the rescheduled
meeting. In addition, it still permits the Council to set special meetings as
required.
There was a consensus that the draft language be amended to specify that it is
the intent that when regularly scheduled meetings fall on General Election Day,
they be rescheduled.
Trustee Eilers thanked Mollybelle Berish for bringing this matter to the
Council’s attention and moved to introduce Ordinance MC-7-2007, as amended.
Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
f)   Resolution No. R-20-2007 – MFT Authorization for Resurfacing of Various
Streets – Adoption. Mr. Saunders reviewed a request to authorize the
expenditure of MFT Funds for the resurfacing of various streets as a part of
the Village’s Capital Improvement Projects for 2007.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
20-2007, authorizing a $200,000 appropriation of MFT Funds for the resurfacing
of various streets. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
g)   Resolution No. R-22-2007 – Commending Trustee Abell – Adoption. This item
was discussed earlier on the Agenda.
h)   Resolution No. R-23-2007 – Authorizing the Appointment of a Representative
to the Board of Directors of the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency – Adoption.
Manager Williams explained that this resolution would designate Brian Keys, the
Village’s new Director of Water & Electric, as the Village’s representative to
the Board of Directors of the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency to replace
Bryan McInturff, who has served in this capacity for more than twenty years.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-23-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
i)   Resolution No. R-24-2007 – Department Heads’ Salaries – Adoption. Trustee
Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-24-2007
establishing the Department Heads’ salaries for FY 2007-2008. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Abell,
Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
j)   Resolution No. R-25-2007 – Village Manager’s Salary – Adoption.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-25-
2007 establishing the Village Manager’s salary for FY 2007-2008. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Berger, Behles,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Tucker mentioned that there was a consensus among the Trustees that an
independent consultant should review the compensation packages on a going-
forward basis. President Woodbury noted that he and the Village Manager had
agreed to move forward with this study.
2)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
3)   Old Business.
a)   Repair/Replace Lead Services. Manager Williams explained that this issue
had been discussed at the April 10th Study Session in response to a business
owner’s request for reconsideration of the Village’s policy.
Mr. Keys reviewed current policy and outlined four policy options for the
Village to offset or share expenses for replacement of service when a leaking
lead water service is replaced.
Following a discussion of the options, there was a consensus that the Village
would be responsible for the costs associated with replacing the service from
the main to the B-box, under the public right-of-way, with the assumption that
the B-box is located at or near the property line. In turn, the owner would be
responsible for replacing the service from the B-box to the building, under
private property. One approach discussed was to request that the owner obtain
multiple bids from qualified contractors to replace the service, the Village
would prorate the cost based on distances from the main to the B-box and from
the B-box to the building. The cost of the tap and the B-box would be waived.
The Trustees also agreed that the Village should move toward replacing lead
water services because it is the right thing to do and over time it will rid
the Village of the lead pipes. In further discussion, it was also acknowledged
that in some instances the B-Box is installed close to the building and not on
the property line or in the parkway. Trustee Abell suggested that Staff
consider placing the demarcation point (i) at the B-box, if the B-box is at the
customer’s property line or on the parkway, or (ii) at the customer’s property
line if the B-box is located on the customer’s property.
Trustee Abell noted the importance of replacing all lead services over time and
suggested that the Village start with a 50% cost-sharing arrangement with the
owners. President Woodbury suggested that the arrangement also include an
appropriate look-back period where the Village has required replacement of the
service.
In further discussion, the trustees confirmed that Libertyville and Winnetka
are the only two communities of the 23 surveyed that require replacement rather
than repair of leaks in lead services, except that while Libertyville does the
work from the B-box to the main, it does not require the owner to replace from
the B-box to the house if the repair is needed only on the Village’s portion of
the line.
The trustees also discussed how repair and replacement costs should be
allocated on a going-forward basis, and whether the Village or the owner should
cover 100% of the cost between the B-box and the building, if the Village
requires replacement of the entire service to the building when there is no
leak between the B-Box and the building.
Trustee Berger pointed out that in comparison to other communities Winnetka
would be assuming a far greater percentage of the costs.
After further discussion, President Woodbury asked staff to come back with a
reasonable process regarding cost sharing to reflect the Council’s consensus
that the full service line should be replaced when there is a leak any where on
the line, with the Village being responsible for 100% of the replacement costs
from the main to the B-box and/or property line, and the owner being
responsible from the B-box and/or property line to the building. He added that
staff should also propose a look-back period for a 50% sharing of costs.
4)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1507 and 1508. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1507 in the amount of $1,063,118.86 and Warrant
List No. 1508 in the amount of $670,807.32. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Abell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Berger reported on her recent attendance at Red Center’s
annual board meeting at which the budget was approved. She noted that Red
Center plans to hire two more dispatchers in anticipation of Wheeling joining
the organization and in hopes of attracting other new member communities. She
indicated that she believed this move would have minimal financial impact on
the Village.
President Woodbury commented that Trustee Berger had been elected Chair of the
Red Center Board. He remarked that this is good for Winnetka to have its
Trustees take on leadership positions in outside organizations because it
spreads the Village’s influence.
Trustee Abell announced the recent death of William Holland, husband of former
Village President Louise Holland.
Trustee Ritchell reported on the activities of the Ad Hoc Historic and
Residential Preservation Committee, saying that they hope to have a report to
the Council by the end of summer.
c)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that in accordance with
previous discussions about the disposal of unclaimed bicycles and accessories
that have been held by the Police Department for at least six months and for
which reasonable efforts to locate the owners of the property have been
unsuccessful, the Village donated ten bicycles and two scooters to the Winnetka
Community House.
He also reported that through a variety of different techniques that will not
compromise environmental standards, SWANCC expects to reduce its costs in the
range of 25% over the coming years, which will in turn reduce Winnetka’s costs.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
11)    Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Abell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Personnel Matters, pursuant to Section 2(c)
(3) and 2(c)1, respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Berger
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:13 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:25 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Abell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
