adjourned at 10:26 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
May 8, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: May 15, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
8, 2007 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
David Abell, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Herb Ritchell. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and approximately 20 persons in the audience.
2)   Analysis of Putting a Utility Grade Wind Turbine on Landfill.
resident Woodbury explained that the subject analysis was proposed by a
resident who suggested that the Village study the possibility of incorporating
wind power into its power portfolio and that the landfill might be a good
location for a wind turbine.. He noted that the Council asked staff to expand
on the idea, and the resulting discussion would be led by Water & Electric
Director Brian Keys and Village Manager Doug Williams. He indicated that after
the staff presentations, representatives from Skidmore, Owings & Merrill, LLP
would give a brief wind energy presentation, followed by Leslie McCain from
Community Energy, after which audience comments would be heard.
Mr. Keys began his presentation by giving an overview of the conditions that
would need to be met in order for a wind turbine to be considered at the
Village’s landfill. He explained that the IMEA established a Community Based
Wind Energy Program in December of 2006, as a way to expand the Agency’s
renewable resource portfolio without increasing wholesale energy costs. He
outlined the qualification criteria for participating in their wind energy
program, among them that the site would need to be ranked as at least a Class
3, or “fair” wind site. He noted that most of Cook County is considered Class
2, or “marginal,” but it is possible that Winnetka falls into a small pocket of
Class 3. Other conditions of participation in the program include: (i) the
turbine equipment be utility-grade, or capable of interfacing with the electric
infrastructure of the Village; (ii) all costs associated with the installation
and operation/maintenance of the turbine facility are borne by the Village;
(iii) local property owners or special interest groups have expressed no
serious objections to the proposal; (iv) the generating capacity is limited to
10% of the current peak load; and (v) at least 12 months of test data are
required to determine the wind classification of the site.
Mr. Keys went on to estimate the cost of the 12-month test at $30,000, adding
that more funds would be needed to conduct engineering studies to determine if
the turbine could be safely anchored without compromising the landfill.
There followed a brief question and answer session with the Trustees, during
which Mr. Keys clarified that the power generated by the turbine would offset
the Village’s consumption from the grid, and that IMEA buys power based
strictly on economics, and not because sources are clean or renewable. He
observed that the cost of wind power is roughly twice as much as the market
average. In response to a question from President Woodbury about other wind
energy in the region, Mr. Keys responded that there is some downstate and to
the west of Cook County.
3)   Renewable Energy Certificates.
Mr. Keys then gave a presentation explaining what Renewable Energy Certificates
(RECs) are and how they work. He noted that the RECs allow consumers the option
to support renewable energy that replaces non-renewable sources, typically by
purchasing the RECs from approved third-party suppliers, usually through their
electric utility.
Mr. Keys went on to explain that the Village could implement a certificate
program by working with a third party to provide the certificates after the
Electric Department bills it. He noted that the sale of RECs would have no
adverse impact on the Village’s power contract with IMEA. He described the City
of Naperville’s renewable energy program, launched in 2005. After an extensive
bid process, they selected Community Energy to provide the Certificates, that
give residents the option to buy kilowatt hours in increments of $5 per 200
kWh. He noted that the city does not profit from the program, and that
currently 2,700 customers have opted to participate.
When asked his opinion of whether such a program is feasible for Winnetka, Mr.
Keys replied that it is. He also recommended working with IMEA to craft a
strategy to include more renewable energy into their power portfolio. Mr. Keys
then confirmed for Trustee Behles that, because the Village has the billing
systems in place the program could be implemented fairly easily, adding that
there is not a big financial risk, even if participation in the program is very
low.
4)   List of Village’s “Green” Initiatives.
Village Manager Doug Williams outlined the Village’s programs and activities to
promote reduced energy use and conservation, both in the day-to-day operations
of its various departments, and also throughout the Village. He noted that in
1993 the Village converted from mercury vapor to high pressure sodium street
lights, significantly reducing energy consumption. Two years later, the Village
instituted a once-per-week garbage collection which resulted in increased
recycling among residents. In addition, by converting all of the Village’s
traffic signals from incandescent to LED bulbs, the Village uses roughly 85%
less energy to power its traffic signals. He mentioned that Winnetka’s other
green initiatives are numerous and extensive, and that Winnetka is in step with
Highland Park, which is considered to be a leader in energy conservation on the
North Shore.
Manager Williams observed that, while not as visible or exciting as
constructing a wind turbine, conservation is an important part of any green
strategy. When asked about the possibility of net metering, (selling back solar
energy at wholesale prices), Manager Williams replied that because about half
of each customer’s electric bill pays for the distribution system, it would
give an unfair advantage for those with solar capability, and that only the
incremental cost of generation should be credited.
5)   Proposed Going-Forward “Green” Strategy.
Manager Williams brought up the possibility of the Village giving compact
fluorescent light bulbs to residents as a way to put focus on the issue of
conservation, noting that if each Winnetka household changed one incandescent
bulb to compact fluorescent light, 431,156 kWh’s per year would be saved. He
listed various education campaigns that could be implemented, including utility
bill inserts, articles in the Winnetka Report, displays at Village Hall and the
library, and possibly hiring specialists to perform energy audits for
residents.
Manager Williams stressed that at present, renewable power sources can’t meet
base load power requirements, since electricity is consumed 24 hours a day,
seven days a week, every day of the year, even when the wind doesn’t blow and
the sun doesn’t shine; however, wind and solar can meet intermediate
requirements. He indicated that the Village will advocate for wind resources to
become a part of IMEA’s portfolio. For the long term, he suggested
investigating potential changes to building codes to increase energy
efficiency, and monitoring of fuel cell technology.
6)   Public Comment & Suggestions Regarding a Village “Green” Strategy.
Mr. Tom Kerwin, of the architecture firm Skidmore, Owings & Merrill and a
member of the Design Review Board, came forward and introduced senior planners
Peter VanVechten and Beth Erickson.
Mr. VanVechten observed that the firm fully supports the concept of introducing
renewable energy and listed the economic and environmental benefits of wind
energy. He observed that the new, smaller turbines are effective at lower wind
speeds, which makes sense for Winnetka. Ms. Erickson continued by presenting
case studies of Prairie Crossing in Grayslake, Illinois, and a wind-solar
combination project in Chicago’s Humboldt Park. She pointed out that grants are
available to help with funding from Illinois’ Renewable Energy Resources
Program.
Next, President Woodbury introduced Leslie McCain, Midwest Director of Business
Development with Community Energy. Ms. McCain identified Community Energy as
the only renewable energy company that both develops and supplies renewable
energy, citing over 60 municipalities and 100 colleges and universities who are
customers. She noted that the company currently has more than four billion kWh
of energy sales and is developing wind farms in 17 states. She explained that
ComEd buys power from Community Energy through an interconnection and purchase
agreement.
Ms. McCain said that Community Energy’s relationship with Naperville is working
very well, as there is no risk to the community and not much staff support is
needed to administer the certificate program. She indicated that Northbrook is
a charter member of the Illinois Municipal Wind Buyers Group, which is a
consortium that buys renewable wind certificates. She enumerated the
environmental benefits of membership, and added that a side benefit for
Northbrook has been very good public relations on the television news and in
the press.
Jennifer Walling, of the Environmental Law and Policy Center, asked the Council
to use Winnetka’s influence to persuade IMEA to include renewable energy
resources in its power portfolio. She urged staff to investigate the grant
opportunities offered by the Illinois Clean Energy Foundation that help fund
new wind projects.
Bruce Nellis, of the Sierra Club in Madison, Wisconsin, indicated that he
serves on two global warming task forces. He advised the Village to formulate a
goal for cutting carbon emissions and then devise a very clear plan to
accomplish the goal. He invited Winnetka to join the Sierra Club’s Cool Cities
Campaign and to take advantage of their educational initiatives for the
community.
Several Village residents in attendance expressed their support for what the
Village has done so far in the area of energy conservation, and urged the
Council to continue exploring ways to reduce carbon emissions, such as
encouraging residents to reduce their reliance on driving for short trips.
The Trustees agreed that the evening had been enlightening, and that green
options should be investigated further. President Woodbury asked Manager
Williams and Mr. Keys to investigate the Cool Cities and REC programs and also
to examine the cost to give away compact fluorescent lights to residents. He
noted that staff would work on reorienting IMEA as well.
7)   Discussion: Caucus Platform Regarding a Leash Law.
Police Chief Joe DeLopez reported that last year’s Winnetka Caucus survey found
that 59% of residents favor an ordinance that would require dogs be kept on
leashes at all times, and that 53% feel no dog should be left unattended. He
noted that the Winnetka Police Department surveyed neighboring communities and
the City of Chicago and Chicago Park District, and found that all those
surveyed have ordinances requiring dog owners to keep dogs on a leash, while
only two communities have a law prohibiting dogs from being unattended, even if
on a leash. He observed that the Village Code currently requires that a dog be
under the control of the owner but does not specifically state that a leash
must be used, leaving the option of voice command or electric collars, etc.
Chief DeLopez asked the Council for direction with regard to any changes to the
Village Code in response to the Caucus survey. He indicated that his agenda
report includes Police Department statistics pertaining to dog bites and stray
dog complaints, but that the data doesn’t show whether the dog bites resulted
from dogs running at large. He suggested the Council conduct a public hearing
on the issue.
Elise Covey, 1503 Edgewood Lane, said she favors a leash law as a public safety
issue. She noted that a loose dog can run out in the street and cause an
accident and also observed that people who are walking their dog on a leash
should not be bothered by dogs running loose.
Trustee Eilers observed that the provision in Paragraph (A)7 of Section
6.08.020 of the Village Code, that dog waste must be removed to an owners’
property, is apparently unknown to many residents since many use public
receptacles along their dog walks. He suggested that this restriction be
modified and observed that risks of waste being removed from public receptacles
is no different than that from home garbage cans.
The Council agreed that a public hearing should be held, and asked staff to
recommend a suitable date.
8)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
9)   Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 10:35
p.m..
____________________________
Recording Secretary
May 15, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 5, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
15, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee David Abell. Also present: Trustee-elect E. King Poor, Village
Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Water & Electric Rich
Ciesla, and approximately 11 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that at the request of the applicant, Item 11(b),
Policy Direction for 340 Poplar, would be removed from the agenda. The Council
approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   June 5, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Behles, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   June 12, 2007 Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee-elect Poor, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 1, 2007, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury referred the Trustees to
changes suggested by Trustee Eilers. Trustee Eilers explained that he wanted to
clarify some items pertaining to the Special Use application for 515 Hibbard.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the minutes as
amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   May 8, 2007, Study Session. President Woodbury referred the Trustees to
changes suggested by Trustee Eilers. Trustee Eilers explained that he wanted to
spell out some items pertaining to the leash law discussion. Trustee Eilers,
seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the minutes as amended. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-6-2007 – Zoning Variations & Special Use Permit: 275
Fairview, Greeley School. – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed a request from
Winnetka School District 36 for a Special Use Permit and three zoning
variations to permit the construction of an addition at Greeley School. She
explained that the Ordinance has been amended to reflect the Plan Commission’s
findings and recommendations, which had not been available for the May 1st
meeting. She added that new sections had also been inserted to reflect the
Council’s discussions, and then outlined the rest of the changes. She noted
that rather than making the incorporation of green space a condition of
approval, the ordinance encouraged the practice, which is consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan.
There being no further questions or comments, Trustee Tucker, seconded by
Trustee Behles, moved to amend Ordinance M-6-2007 as presented. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt, as amended,
Ordinance No. M-6-2007, granting a special use permit and variations to Greeley
School. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Behles, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
b)   Ordinance No. M-7-2007 – Zoning Variations & Special Use Permit: 515
Hibbard Rd., Carlton Washburne School - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a
request from Winnetka School District 36 for a Special Use Permit and two
zoning variations to permit the construction of an addition at Carlton
Washburne School. He observed that the architects for the project had some
changes to the original plans, which they would be distributing to the Council.
Attorney Janega referred the Trustees to amendments to the Ordinance. As with
the Greeley School ordinance, the amendments reflect both the findings and
recommendations from the Plan Commission and the Council’s discussions. She
noted the addition of new sections pertaining to the staging and access plan,
and incorporation of more green space to break up the mass of the building
addition.
Ms. Shelly Clark, Business Manager for School District 36, informed the Council
that the District had updated plans for the Council’s review, and asked the
project architect to come forward to present them.
School District architect George Reigle explained that the new plans contained
some modifications to the building in an effort to break up the mass of the
addition. The building footprint was altered slightly by bringing the center of
the east wall out eight feet and adding steps up to the wall. Windows were also
inserted at either end.
Mr. David McCallum, the landscape architect, explained that evergreen and shade
trees had been added to frame the south and north ends of the new fenestration.
Trustee Behles observed out that the ordinance was written to encourage various
green building technologies and urged the school district to add additional
screening and greenery.
Ms. Clark said the school district appreciated the Council’s comments and said
they would continue to look at the landscaping issue and determine what level
of funding could be used to enhance it.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend Ordinance M-7-2007
as presented, with further changes to incorporate the new plans. By voice vote,
the motion passed unanimously.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-7-
2007, granting a special use permit and variations to Washburne School. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
c)   Ordinance No. M-8-2007 – Authorizing Third Addendum to IMEA Power Supply
Contract – Adoption. This ordinance was introduced at the May 1st meeting.
There being no additional comments or questions, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by
Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-8-2007, extending the term of
the Power Sales Contract between IMEA and the Village to 2035 and making a
corresponding change to the amortization schedule for the Village’s share of
the TC2 debt service. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Berger, Behles, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Abell.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2007 –Amending the Village Code Pertaining to
Scheduling Village Council Meetings – Adoption. Attorney Janega pointed out
Section 2 of the Ordinance, where a specific reference to general elections had
been inserted as directed by the Council immediately prior to voting to
introduce the ordinance at the May 1st meeting.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-7-2007,
amending the Village Code as it pertains to scheduling Village Council
meetings. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Behles, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Abell.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   Seating of the New Village Council.
a)   Village Clerk’s Report: Election Results. There being no further
unfinished business for the sitting Council, President Woodbury asked Village
Clerk Doug Williams to report the certified results of the local election. Mr.
Williams read the results of the April 17, 2007, election in which Cook County
Clerk David Orr’s office declared Kenneth L. Behles and Sandra A. Berger duly
elected to a second term as Village Trustees, E. King Poor duly elected to a
first term as Village Trustee, and Edmund C. Woodbury duly elected for a second
term as Village President.
Mr. Williams then administered the Oath of Office to Trustees Poor, Behles, and
Berger and to President Woodbury.
b)   Call to Order. President Woodbury called the meeting of the new Council to
order. Present: Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, E. King Poor,
Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Water & Electric Rich
Ciesla, and several persons in the audience.
10)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2007 – Open Meetings Policy; Attendance at Meetings –
Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that Ordinance MC-8-2007 reflects the
Council’s discussion and policy determination following its discussion, at the
April 10th Study Session, of recent amendments to the Open Meetings Act. She
noted that the amendments added communicating by electronic means, such as e-
mails, text messaging and phone calls, to the definition of meetings, and that
they also authorize a majority of a public body to establish rules to permit a
member of that body to attend a meeting by video or audio conference, under
certain conditions.
Attorney Janega explained that Ordinance MC-8-2007 reflects the Council’s
conclusion that electronic participation at meetings is not currently feasible,
given the technological limitations of the Village’s Council Chambers and other
meeting sites. She pointed out that the ordinance amends the Village Code to
establish a policy requiring that members of the Council and the Village’s
various Boards and Commissions attend meetings in person. She also noted
related technical amendments pertaining to the Zoning Board of Appeals and the
Liquor Advisory Board.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-8-
2007, amending the Village Code with respect to Open Meetings Policy. By voice
vote the motion passed unanimously.
b)   Resolution No. R-26-2007 – EPA Signing Authority – Adoption. Attorney
Janega reported that the subject resolution gives authority to Village Manager
Doug Williams and the new Director of the Water & Electric Department, Brian
Keys, to sign and file permit applications and supporting documentation with
various governmental agencies in conjunction with the operations of the
Village’s water and electric utilities.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-26-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-27-2007 – Appointing Ed McKee as Village Treasurer –
Adoption. President Woodbury reported that the Village Council appoints the
Village Treasurer every two years, and the subject resolution will reappoint
Finance Director Ed McKee as Village Treasurer.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-27-2007.
By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-28-2007 – Appointing SWANCC Representatives – Adoption.
President Woodbury announced that the Village President and Village Manager
traditionally serve as Director and Alternate Director to the Solid Waste
Agency of Northern Cook County’s Board of Directors. Since the terms expired on
April 30, he explained that a resolution is needed to reappoint them.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-28-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion passed unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None
11)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1509 and 1510. Trustee Ritchell reviewed the Warrant Lists
