Recording Secretary
June 5, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 19, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
5, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker.
Absent: Trustee Ken Behles. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders,
Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Police Chief Joe DeLopez and
approximately 15 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   June 12, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Poor, indicated that they expected to attend. President Woodbury also
indicated that he would be unable to attend.
b)   June 19, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 15, 2007, Regular Meeting. There being no corrections or additions,
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Landmark Preservation Awards.
Posey Fisher, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission, presented
awards to this year’s recipients. She commented that each is an example of high
quality design and superior workmanship. The homes winning awards were as
follows:
 
510 Poplar    Restoration
920 Greenwood Rehabilitation
491 Orchard   Rehabilitation
211 Birch     Rehabilitation
1063 Cherry   New Construction
 
President Woodbury thanked all of the winners for their contributions toward
bettering the appearance of the Village and its housing stock. He then thanked
the Landmark Preservation Commission for its leadership and efforts to
encourage good design, rehabilitation of existing housing stock, and good new
construction when demolition is inevitable. He indicated that the Village would
be taking further steps in the future to promote the preservation and
construction of good architecture.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2007 – Open Meetings Policy; Attendance at Meetings -
Adoption. President Woodbury called for any additional comments regarding the
proposed amendment to the Village Code to establish a Village-wide policy
precluding members of the Village Council, committees, boards and commissions
from attending meetings through electronic means when they cannot be physically
present. There being none, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to
adopt Ordinance MC-8-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Behles.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2007 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to
Replacement of Lead Water Service Lines - Introduction. Attorney Janega
explained that the proposed amendments to the Village Code reflect the
Council’s previous discussions regarding the replacement of leaking lead water
services. The proposed language would change the existing policy pertaining to
the allocation of costs between the customer and the Village, making the
property owner responsible for the repair of any leaks on the owner’s property
instead of being required to bear the entire cost of replacement. Commercial
customers would be responsible from the building to the curb. Another goal of
the ordinance is to move the “B-Box” to the property line where necessary so as
to clarify responsibility and put everything where is it is more accessible to
the Village for maintenance. Therefore, for purposes of allocating costs, the
water service from the main to a customer will be viewed as two separate
pieces, rather than one.
The new language would change the allocation of costs as follows:
Proposed Allocation of Costs
Type of Service Location of Work Leak Lead Leak Non-Lead Service Upgrade or
                                 Service   Service       Replacement
                House to         Owner     Owner         Owner
Single-family   property line
                Property line to main      Village       Village Owner
                Relocate B-box             Village       N/A     Owner
Commercial,     Building to curb Customer  Customer      Customer
Multi-Family,   Curb to main               Village       Village Customer
Industrial      Relocate B-box             Village       N/A     Customer
 
Exceptions would be made if the Village causes damage to private property, in
which case the Village would be responsible; however, if a private party causes
damage to Village property, the party causing the damage would be responsible.
The Village would continue with its policy of not allowing any new lead lines
to be installed.
The cost for replacement of lead services due to upgrades or redevelopment
would continue to be borne by the customer.
Manager Williams noted that with regard to the look-back period for
reimbursement of expenses, the ordinance was drafted to be consistent with the
ordinance pertaining to sewer line replacements. However, the specified 120-day
period before its effective date would not cover some of the issues discussed.
In addition, he said, it needs to be clarified whether the look-back goes back
to the date of the leak or when the repairs were made. He advised the Council
that because these expenses were not budgeted for during the budgeting process,
staff may be recommending an adjustment in water rates to account for the
increase.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees agreed that it was reasonable to look
back to January 1, 2007, for purposes of reimbursing costs.
Trustee Eilers commented that he sees this policy as being consistent and
appropriate because a homeowner would not be required to replace a lead water
service until there is actually a leak on their property, while the Village
could choose to replace service from the main to the B-Box as it deems
necessary.
Peter Skalski, 740 Elm, and Ethan Holland, 510 Poplar, spoke in support of the
proposed ordinance and thanked the Trustees for considering the matter.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-9-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Resolution No. R-29-2007 – Prevailing Wages – Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that State law requires the Village to establish the prevailing wages
for workers employed in the construction of capital improvement projects in the
Village.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-29-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger,
Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9)   Old Business.
10)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1511 and 1512. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1511 in the amount of $882,611.12 and Warrant List
No. 1512 in the amount of $623,970.17. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Berger. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
b)   Chamber of Commerce Request for Sidewalk Sale. Manager Williams referred
the Trustees to a letter from the Chamber of Commerce outlining its plans and
requesting permission to hold its annual Sidewalk Sale on Friday, July 20 and
Saturday, July 21. He explained that the Village Code specifies that there be
no retail sales on the public right-of-way without specific authorization of
the Council.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the Chamber’s
request to hold its Sidewalk Sale as requested. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
c)   Woodley Road Association Agreement for Police Services. Chief DeLopez
explained that in accordance with Council’s direction, Staff negotiated an
Agreement with the Woodley Road Association for 24-hour police service that
closely reflects costs related to the Village’s Operating Budget and provides
for a 6.8% increase in fees for the term June 1, 2007, through May 31, 2008. He
reported that the Association agreed to let the Village Council establish each
subsequent annual renewal fee. He then responded to questions from the
Trustees.
Trustee Tucker remarked that it is an excellent contract, and Trustee Eilers
commented that the fact that the Association has left the renewal fee up to the
Village to determine is testimony to the high level of confidence that they
have in the Village.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve and authorize
the execution of the Agreement for Police Protection and Traffic Enforcement
Services with the Woodley Road Association. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Eilers, Berger, Poor, and Ritchell.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
d)   Cell Phone Quarterly Report. Chief DeLopez reported the results of Police
enforcement for violation of the cell phone prohibition as of May 30, 2007. He
noted that 81% of those cited resided outside of the Village limits.
President Woodbury noted that enforcement will continue, and the Village will
begin installing warning signs around schools as requested by the Caucus.
e)   Bid No. 07-008: 2007 Street Rehabilitation. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids
for the Village’s annual street Rehabilitation Project. He reported that
asphalt prices are continuing to rise and indicated that he does not expect
them to come back down in the future. He noted that if this proves to be the
case, the Council will have to consider increasing the budget for capital
funding on a going-forward basis if it wishes to continue to keep up the same
pace of activity. The streets to be rehabilitated as a part of this year’s
contract are as follows:
 
Berkeley Avenue         Willow Rd. to Oak Street
Glendale Avenue         Willow Rd. to Oak Street
Scott Avenue            Grove Street to Vernon Ave.
Walnut Street           Hawthorn Lane to Spruce St.
Indian Hill Parking Lot
 
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award Bid 07-008 to A
Lamp Concrete Contractors for the bid amount of $938,658.10. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers,
Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
f)   Bid No. 07-013: 2007 Sidewalk Program. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for
the Village’s annual Sidewalk Program. He noted again the significant price
increases for unit items, particularly concrete. He said that Staff believes
these increases reflect strong upward price pressure on construction prices
related to significant increases in the costs of concrete, fuel, and
construction labor.
He explained since the bids were above the budgeted amount, the Village has two
options for proceeding. First, because of the unit pricing, the Village could
manage the quantities by cutting back on the work to be done in order meet the
budgeted amount, or it can simply award the full value of the contract and
offset the budget variance through management of other capital construction
projects. Given that he does not expect to see costs decline in the foreseeable
future, he reported staff’s recommendation to follow the second course.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed with the work as planned.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award Bid No. 07-013 to
Schroeder & Schroeder for the unit prices as bid, for an amount not to exceed
$139,150. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
g)   Willow Road Improvement and Jurisdictional Transfer. Mr. Saunders
explained that Willow Road is under the jurisdiction of the Illinois Department
of Transportation from Provident to beyond the western corporate limits of the
Village. This means that IDOT controls virtually all issues related thereto. He
noted that this heavily traveled portion of roadway is in deteriorating
condition, and the Village has included in its Capital Plan a proposed
construction project to improve this stretch of roadway, with a targeted
construction date of 2010. He said that much of the funding will come from
Federal transportation funds, with the Village’s portion projected to be about
$900,000.
He reported that in the interim IDOT has postponed the resurfacing of the road
between the Edens Expressway and Provident Avenue that had been planned for
this year while more comprehensive improvements to Willow Road are being
studied in a cooperative effort by the Villages of Winnetka and Northfield. In
response to a request from the Village, IDOT has agreed conceptually to
participate financially in the Village’s project when it occurs and, as a part
of that participation, it makes sense for the Village and IDOT to consider
transferring jurisdiction of the road from IDOT to the Village. Thus, the
Village would control its own destiny over the future of Willow Road.
Manager Williams added that it is in the Village’s best strategic interests to
take over Willow Road because it increases long-term flexibility.
Trustee Eilers confirmed with Mr. Saunders that the purpose of this report was
informational and that the final decision whether to transfer jurisdiction
would be made sometime down the road. Mr. Saunders responded affirmatively.
h)   Water & Electric Plant Intake Maintenance Work. Mr. Keys explained that
inspections reveal that the water intakes at the Water & Electric Plant need
maintenance to replace stone displaced by wave action and leaky joints also
need repair. Noting that the Department was unsuccessful in obtaining
competitive bids due to the nature of the work, he recommended that the work be
awarded to Edward E. Gillian Company, which has performed similar work for the
Village in the past.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to award the contract for the
Water and Electric Plant maintenance work to Edward E. Gillen Company, for an
amount not to exceed $56,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
i)   Bid No. 07-011: Directional Boring Contract Bid. Mr. Keys reviewed the
bids for directional boring. He explained that the bid was structured with
fixed prices for units of work and the bid evaluation was based on estimated
quantities of work. He recommended that the bid be awarded to the lowest total
cost bidder, Directional Solutions.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award Bid 07-011 for
directional boring to Directional Solutions at the unit prices indicated in the
bid for an amount not to exceed $772,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Tucker, Eilers, Berger, Poor, and Ritchell. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
11)   Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury reported that he would be meeting on
Thursday morning with Northfield’s representatives regarding the joint Willow
Road planning process. He noted that Winnetka’s representatives to the
committee would be Trustee Behles, Mark Kurensky from the Plan Commission,
Becky Hurley from the School Board, Ian Sherman from the Park Board, and
Manager Williams and Steve Saunders on behalf of the Village.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers encouraged residents to attend and spend at the
Chambers upcoming Sidewalk Sale. He also noted that the Business Community
Development Commission will be meeting on Thursday to continue its discussion
on the Overlay District.
c)   Village Manager. None.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
12)   Appointments.
None.
13)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments, Possible lease of Village Land, and Pending and
Probable Litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(3), 2(c)(6), and 2(c)11,
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:21 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:11 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent: Trustee
Behles. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles. The meeting adjourned
at 10:12 p.m.
   Recording Secretary
June 12 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: July 17, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
12, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro tem Eilers called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: Trustee King Poor and Village President Edmund Woodbury. Also
in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine
Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, and approximately 10 persons
in the audience.
2)   Meet with Winnetka Caucus Officials.
President Pro Tem Eilers invited members of the Village Caucus to come forward
for a discussion of this year’s Caucus Questionnaire. Dan Streif, Chairman of
the Platform Committee, and Hugh Brower, Chairman of the Village Committee,
introduced the other Caucus members who were present.
Mr. Brower stated that the Caucus has been working to come up with issues to
address in the Questionnaire, and asked for the Council’s perspective. Mr.
Streif noted that the environment and intergovernmental cooperation are big
issues. He added that the Caucus would like to get a sense of the community’s
impressions on the cell phone ordinance, stop sign enforcement efforts, and
interactions they might have had with Village staff.
Mr. Brower mentioned that another possible topic is the gasoline-powered leaf-
blower ordinance, since the Caucus has learned that it is very difficult to
enforce. He indicated that the Forestry Commission would like a question about
Heritage Tree designation and removal procedures. There was a brief discussion
about the Heritage Tree issue, which Manager Williams explained is policy issue
involving balancing property rights against the preservation of the urban
forest.
Mr. Streif said the Caucus us also considering a question about the Post Office
site redevelopment, and Manager Williams suggested that they contact the Post
Office Committee directly, as they are working on their draft recommendations.
After further discussion, the Council suggested that the issue of revenue
sources for the redevelopment be separated from the basic use issue.
Discussion between the Caucus and Council focused on how to prioritize and pay
for such special capital projects as streetscape, a parking deck, Village Hall
rehabilitation and the Post Office site redevelopment.
3)   NERC Reliability Compliance.
Water & Electric Director Brian Keys reported that the North American Electric
Reliability Corporation (NERC) came into being as a response to the major power
blackout in the Midwest in 2003, that NERC monitors the bulk power grid, and
has issued reliability standards. He explained that the standards are now
mandatory and that the Village must either register with NERC or allow a Joint
Action Agency to act on its behalf.
Mr. Keys explained that IMEA has offered to register on behalf of the Village,
outlined the benefits of doing so, and explained the terms of IMEA’s proposed
joint responsibility agreement.
Manager Williams noted the increasing complexity of transmission issues and
recommended working with IMEA, since the Village must follow the standards
whether it acts alone or with the assistance of IMEA.
The Council concurred and directed staff to bring the agreement and authorizing
resolution before the Council at its next regular meeting.
4)   Lease Re-Negotiations for Wireless Service Providers.
Mr. Keys reported that the leases allowing wireless service providers to
install facilities at the Water & Electric Plant are up for renegotiation, and
that he surveyed comparable municipalities and learned that the average value
for cell site leases is $29,482 per year. He outlined possible terms for the
new lease arrangements and requested authorization to negotiate new contracts
accordingly.
After a brief discussion, the Council agreed that the proposed criteria for the
new lease arrangements were reasonable, and directed staff to proceed with
negotiations.
5)   Cool Cities.
Manager Williams reported that he had investigated the Sierra Club’s
Cool Cities initiative, and outlined the four-steps of the program. He said the
first, pledging to reduce carbon pollution to below 1990 levels, has been
treated as a symbolic step in most cases, since there is no real way to measure
what the actual 1990 levels were. He noted that the other three steps could
more reasonably be accomplished and that some of the items are already being
done in Winnetka.
Manager Williams explained that the first phase of the process would involve
passing a resolution endorsing the U.S. Mayors’ Climate Protection Agreement
and drew Council’s attention to a sample resolution in their agenda packets.
After a brief discussion with the Council, Manager Williams recommended
applying for the Cool Cities designation and implementing a formal program.
President Pro Tem Eilers asked for comments from the audience. Audience members
favored pursuing the Cool Cities program and establishing an official policy of
reducing carbon emissions within the Village. It was also suggested that
recognition be given to people who use strategies to reduce their carbon
footprints, and that the profile of the Village’s environmental initiatives be
raised.
The Council directed staff bring a climate protection resolution before them at
a subsequent meeting.
6)   Right-of-Way Ordinance.
Attorney Janega reported on pending state legislation that would give video
service providers such free and uncontrolled access to public rights-of-way
unless the municipality has a standard regulatory ordinance in place. She noted
that currently Winnetka does not have a specific right-of-way regulation
ordinance for public utilities, and suggested addressing the issue with two new
chapters to the Village Code, one to address the right-of-way regulations for
utilities, and the other to update and codify the 1988 cable television
franchising ordinance. She explained that she has been gathering input from the
affected Village departments in anticipation of the passage of the new law,
Senate Bill 678 as amended by the House, which passed the House on May 31st and
is expected to clear the Senate shortly.
Attorney Janega recommended passing a regulatory ordinance, whether or not the
new law takes effect and described key provisions of a possible Village
ordinance, including tree protection, prohibition of overhead lines,
limitations on the size and location of facilities that would be allowed above
ground, and compensation for sue of the rights-of-way.
Attorney Janega recommended bringing the proposed ordinance up for introduction
at the next Council meeting, and indicated that there was time to fine-tune the
ordinance and pass it at the July 10th Council meeting.
Following discussion, the Council directed that the ordinance be presented for
introduction at the next regular meeting.
7)   Executive Session..
No Executive Session was held.
8)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 10:28 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
June 19, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 10, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
19, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Sandra Berger, Ken Behles, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Brian
Keys and approximately 30 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   July 10, 2007, Special Meeting & Study Session. All of the Council members
present, except Trustees Behles and Tucker, indicated that they expected to
attend.
b)   July 17, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 5, 2007, Regular Meeting. There being no corrections or additions,
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2007 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to
Replacement of Lead Water Service Lines - Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed
proposed amendments to the draft ordinance that was introduced at the June 5th
meeting. She explained that the revisions clean up the language to make the
allocation clearer. The ordinance provides a clear frame of reference for the
replacement of lead service, whereby the basic responsibility is allocated
between the property owner and the Village based on the property line in single
family zoning districts and the curb line in other districts. In addition, the
proposed amendments clarify that up to 50% of reasonable costs incurred by
customers prior to enactment of this ordinance are eligible, if the leak was
identified and work completed on or after January 1, 2007.
The Trustees discussed whether paragraphs 1 and 2 of Subsection E should refer
to replacement as well as to repair and accepted Attorney Janega’s suggestion
that the phrase “shall be repaired” be changed to “shall be repaired or
replaced, as necessary,” to avoid the implication that replacement could be
required at the option of the owner.
With regard to the “look-back provisions,” Manager Williams reported that staff
estimates the expense for 50% reimbursement for those incidents that occurred
since January 1, 2007, to be approximately $35,000 to $40,000. On a going-
forward basis, the Village’s cost is likely to average $32,000 per year. He
indicated that because funding for such repairs has not been included in the
budgeting process, the options for funding are either to raise water rates or
draw down cash reserves. Therefore, he expects that staff will recommend a 2%
water rate increase to cover these expenses.
Trustee Tucker commented that she hopes staff will first explore other options.
Trustee Eilers asked whether the 50% look-back would be calculated as 50% of
the cost of replacement on the owner’s property. Mr. Keys advised that the
estimates provided the Council were calculated on 50% of the total cost, based
on staff’s understanding that the look back period would encompass the total
project cost. He explained that, up to now, homeowners were required to pay for
all of the work, and so were quoted a cost for the entire job, since they had
no reason to seek differentiation between the public and private portions. He
also noted that a review of recent lead service leaks indicated that
approximately half (54%) of the service connection length was installed in the
public right-of-way.
Peter Skalski once again thanked the Council for considering this matter, but
said he believes the 50% reimbursement to be arbitrary and not in keeping with
the language or intent of the existing Village Code. He asked that the Council
consider 100% reimbursement.
President Woodbury commented that the purpose of the proposed ordinance is to
reinforce the Village’s on-going policy on replacing leaking lead water
services and to codify the point at which the costs for replacement are divided
between the Village and the owner. He suggested that Mr. Skalski work with
staff to resolve his issue.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend Ordinance No. MC-
9-2007 as in the agenda materials and further amended as proposed by the
Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-
9-2007, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Eilers, Berger, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-9-2007 – Zoning Variation: 330 Willow Road – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Front and Corner Yard Setback variation
to permit construction of two one-story additions, explaining that the existing
setback is nonconforming. He reported that with the exception of the requested
setback variation, the proposed improvements comply with all other zoning
requirements and that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6 to 0 to recommend
approval of the variations.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
9-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-10-2007 – Zoning Variations: 938 Cherry Street –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot,
Maximum Building Size, Rear Yard Setback, and Front Setback variations to
permit the construction of a second floor addition. He explained that the
applicants wish to convert an existing legal nonconforming two-unit residential
building back to a single family home. In addition to the second floor
addition, the applicants are looking to add two bay windows to the front of the
home and a new chimney along the east side of the house. He reported that the
Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6 to 1 to recommend approval of the
variations.
Trustee Behles confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that the modification to remove a
proposed outdoor deck had been included in the zoning calculations and the
ordinance drafted to reflect that change.
President Woodbury remarked that the fact that this duplex, a nonconforming
use, is being converted back to a single family home is a positive aspect of
the request.
Trustee Tucker expressed some concern about the addition of a fourth bedroom.
The applicant’s architect explained that the house is small and has no official
family room or study to accommodate a single family residence. He mentioned
that there is little incentive to improve the property without the addition of
a fourth bedroom to add desired flexibility. Otherwise, the property produces a
good income as a rental property.
Trustee Poor said that ordinarily he would be reluctant to grant this variance,
but because of the very small size of the request, the significant benefit to
the neighborhood of having it converted into a single family home, and the
Zoning Board’s unanimous approval, he would be in support of the request.
Trustee Behles concurred, saying that he sees this addition as a natural infill
component that does not impinge in any way on adjacent homeowners and the
reduction to a single-family home is more than justification to be in favor of
the request.
Trustee Ritchell pointed out that the Village is on record as trying to save
older homes and that this is a relatively insignificant addition that will
achieve that end.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
10-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
d)   Ordinance No. M-11-2007 – Zoning Variations: 1243 Scott Street –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Intensity of Use of Lot and
Maximum Building Size variations to permit the construction of a one-story
kitchen addition to accommodate a large stove. He reported that the existing
house is legal nonconforming with respect to roofed lot coverage and gross
floor area, in that it exceeds the allowed maximum, and the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 6 to 0 to recommend approval of the variations.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M 11 2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury noted that this property is one of those split lots that did
not annex into the Village and encouraged the owner to consider taking
advantage of the opportunity to do so.
In order to accommodate a number of people waiting in the audience, President
Woodbury moved Items 8(a) Policy Direction: 340 Poplar, 9(b) Discussion: Zoning
Retail Overlay District, and 9(c) Petition: Ash Street Flood Relief, up in the
Agenda.
* * *
8)   Old Business.
a)   Policy Direction: 340 Poplar. Trustee Ritchell recused himself from the
discussion of this issue by stepping off the dais.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a rear yard variation to permit the
construction of a new detached garage as a part of the construction of a new
home. He noted that this item originally had been slated for discussion at the
May 15th Council meeting, but at the request of the petitioner, it was
continued in order to allow him to meet with his neighbors. Subsequently a
revised site plan was submitted calling for the garage side yard setback to be
increased by one foot. He observed, however, that this shifting of the garage
and drive has no impact on the variance request because it is, in fact, for a
variation on new construction.
Mr. D’Onofrio went on to explain that the proposed location of the garage
results in its being located in the rear quarter of the lot, which in turn
makes the home eligible for a GFA allowance of 209 sq. ft. He pointed out that
if the garage were to be located in a conforming location, that 209 sq. ft.
would be included in the GFA calculation, making the house over the maximum
allowable limit by 194 sq. ft. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had
voted 4 to 1 to recommend denial of the variation request.
In response to a request from Manager Williams, Mr. D’Onofrio addressed the
principles and practices associated with considering variations for new
construction. He explained that because it is new construction, the theory is
that the builder has a blank slate with which to begin. As a result, there are
very few instances in which a conforming alternative cannot be found. He
indicated that in his opinion there is nothing unique about this lot to warrant
a variation; conforming alternatives had been identified but discarded as a
matter of preference.
Trustee Behles remarked that the proposed plan is clearly based on the
applicant’s preference, not on the inability to find a conforming alternative,
as the garage and driveway could be shifted to the south side of the house.
The applicant, Harold Nations, thanked for Council for the opportunity to
discuss this matter and explained his reasons for asking for requesting the
variance. He discussed some of the alternative designs that had been
considered, enumerating the reasons they had been discarded. He indicated that
he and his wife are looking to downsize from their family home into a smaller,
centrally located home and in so doing to develop one of the Village’s smaller
lots into the kind of housing stock that the community needs.
Neighbors Barbara Warnecke and David Bender, 565 Orchard and 561 Orchard,
respectively, spoke in opposition to the request, noting that this is new
construction, the existence of conforming options, and the absence of unique
circumstances. They noted the smallness of the lots in the neighborhood and the
fact that others have been required to build onto their homes within the
confines of the Zoning Ordinance.
Herb Ritchell, 548 Willow, agreed, saying he believed the Zoning Board made the
correct decision.
In response to a question from Trustee Berger, Mr. D’Onofrio summarized the
circumstances surrounding other variations for new construction that have been
granted.
Mr. Nations pointed out that exactly the same size home could be built without
variation, if it were simply reoriented.
Trustee Poor expressed concern about the variation in light of the distress
throughout the Village about teardowns. He said he believes that variations for
new construction should be extremely rare. In this instance, it would set a
precedent that would take the Village in the wrong direction because there is
no unique, defining feature that prevents compliance with the Zoning Ordinance
as it is. In addition, objections from immediate neighbors should be
considered. He deferred to the members of the Zoning Board, who spent a lot of
time on this matter and came out loud and clear opposed to the proposal, and
indicated that he would vote to uphold their decision.
Trustees Berger, Tucker, and Eilers agreed.
Trustee Behles pointed out that the applicant testified to the existence of a
complying alternative, so there is no rationale for overturning the Zoning
Board’s decision.
Noting the Council’s unanimous sentiment, President Woodbury called for a
motion to uphold the recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals. Trustee
Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, so moved. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Tucker, Behles, and Eilers.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell (recused).
9)   New Business
b)   Discussion: Zoning Retail Overlay District.
President Woodbury noted that the Business Community Development Commission
spent many months studying this issue before bringing its recommendations
before the Council. He said tonight’s discussion would be the first in this
forum, with more to follow in the coming months.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the Retail Overlay District creates an additional
layer of zoning restrictions in the C-2 commercial districts designed to create
a retail hub in a confined area where one use generates use off of another use.
It was established in Winnetka approximately 20 years ago in reaction to the
proliferation of non-retail uses. He mentioned that one of the outcomes of the
Commission’s study has been the formation of the Winnetka Commercial Property
Owners Association whose members have attended and participated in BCDC
discussions. He went on to say that the BCDC looked at a number of factors
related to the Overlay District but focused on two components: (1) the
permitted and special uses allowed, and (2) the district boundaries. He
referred the Trustees to the Committee’s recommendations in the Agenda Report.
Manager Williams noted that if these recommendations were to move forward, text
and map amendments would be required. Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that normally the
next step would be to refer the matter to the Plan Commission to determine
compliance with the Comprehensive Plan and to make further recommendations.
Trustee Berger inquired as to why the BCDC had recommended the expansion of the
Overlay District into the Grand Foods site and Trustee Tucker asked why certain
areas had been omitted.
Trustee Eilers, who represented the Council on the BCDC, responded that the
Commission had spent a great deal of time on this matter because it impinges on
property owners’ rights. He said the Commission determined that there were key,
geographic “missing teeth” in the retail overlay districts that were
appropriate to include in the Overlay District, but that it was also necessary
to be mindful of transition areas.
Bill Shiel, Chairman of the Business Community Development Commission,
acknowledged that the Overlay District restricts the private property rights of
landowners insofar as it directs them to continue as retail; however, the
Commission unanimously felt that it has been a successful program and has
helped to maintain a vital retail area, and that permitting non-retail infill
into the area would be counterproductive. He noted that the Commission is
recommending the area within which a special use permit is required be reduced
from 100 feet from the public street to 60 feet. This would encourage small
shops without hurting the district and enable landowners to rent back spaces
for offices and other non-retail uses.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. Shiel and the Commission for their thorough
examination of the issue and thoughtful recommendations that will be a part of
ongoing community discussions about the vitality of the downtown.
David Humphrey, 1843 Winnetka Ave., Northfield, owner of the Grand Foods
property, spoke in opposition to the Commission’s recommendation to include
that site in the Overlay District. He pointed out that the Overlay District
affects the landowners’ ability to do business, thereby decreasing the value of
the land, and expressed his belief that the Commission has targeted his
property.
Jim Sayegh, of the Winnetka Galleria and a member of the Winnetka Commercial
Property Owners Association, distributed copies of his remarks presented on
behalf of that organization. He commented that in the owners’ opinion, the BCDC
is essentially recommending the status quo and offering nothing that promises
progress for the future. A copy of Mr. Sayegh’s comments may be found in the
Village’s public record of this meeting.
President Woodbury thanked Messrs. Humphrey and Sayegh for their comments and
responded to their remarks. He observed that whether to include the Grand Foods
property in the Overlay District is a core policy issue that ultimately the
Council will have to decide. He noted that from his professional experience, he
sees overlay districts being implemented throughout the country because they
work and will continue to work. He encouraged the property owners to continue
participating in the Commission’s meetings and to discuss what are core things
to do to attract businesses and create the vitality that is desired. He
suggested that the Council slate the Commission’s recommendations for further
discussion before sending the matter to the Plan Commission as the future
hearing body.
d)   Petition: Ash Street Flood Relief. Rosemary Swierk and Chris Baker, 1021
and 1010 Ash Street, respectively, reviewed the genesis of the petition for
relief.
Mr. Saunders explained that a significant amount of rain, accompanied by heavy
wind and hail, fell during the storms cited by the petitioners, just as the
leaves were beginning to fall from the trees. The storm knocked large numbers
of leaves off in a very short time, which clogged the grates on many of the
storm sewer inlets, making them unable to accommodate the runoff water and
causing the roadway and parkway to be inundated, which in part led to flooding
in a number of the Ash Street homes. He observed that flash flooding is likely
to have been a significant part of the problem, but it may not be the only
cause.
He noted that he had met with residents in response to the flooding and
committed to program an engineering study of the neighborhood in the Capital
Improvements Plan, but indicated that due to other capital improvements already
programmed and committed at that time for the current fiscal year, funding
would not be available until FY 2008. However, as explained by the petitioners,
affected residents are requesting that the Council allocate funds immediately
for this proposed engineering study. Mr. Saunders estimated that the cost of
such a study would be in the neighborhood of $25,000 to $50,000 and would be
worthwhile, but remarked that it might not be realistic to expect to complete
the study and take corrective action before the end of the this year.
After further comments from other Ash Street residents and a brief discussion
by the Council, the Trustees were unanimous in directing staff to implement an
engineering study as soon as possible.
* * *
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions continued
e)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2007 – Right-of-way Regulations – Introduction.
Attorney Janega reviewed the draft ordinance prepared in response to Council’s
discussion at the June 12th Study Session about amending the Village Code to
establish general standards for the use of public rights-of-way. She noted that
the ordinance would also update and codify the Village’s 1988 cable television
franchising ordinance. She explained that this ordinance was prompted by the
General Assembly’s recent actions, which had cleared the Senate late this
afternoon and would allow for a statewide certificate of authority for video
service providers. The legislative push is part of AT&T’s attempts to gain free
and uncontrolled access to public rights-of-way so they can launch their
“Project LightSpeed” to compete with incumbent cable providers in select
locations without having to make service uniformly available, as is required of
cable incumbents. The proposed legislation would pre-empt home rule and would
allow unrestricted access to public rights-of-way unless the municipality has a
standard regulatory ordinance in place. The proposed ordinance would maximize
the Village’s control over the use of its rights-of-way by cable and television
service providers and other public utilities, regardless of the ultimate
outcome of the pending legislation.
Trustee Eilersasked whether the Village’s interests would still be protected if
the ordinance is not adopted until after the new law goes into effect. Attorney
Janegaexpressed her belief that the Village’s interests would still be
protected, and explained that she some provisions of the ordinance would have
to be amended if it is adopted before the bill becomes law. She added that,
after further reviewing the pending bill, she had concluded it was not
necessary to go directly to adoption, because there are mandatory requirements
that apply even without a local ordinance and that, given the time frame for
Commerce Commission approval, she felt the Village could safely take the
additional 3 weeks and simply introduce the ordinance tonight.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to
introduce Ordinance No. MC-10-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Woodbury moved Item 9(c) forward in the Agenda to accommodate people
in the audience waiting to speak.
* * *
9)   New Business
c)   Discussion: Commercial Property Maintenance Code. Mr. D’Onofrio explained
that a Property Maintenance Study Group had been established by the Village and
the Chamber of Commerce to examine the possibility of creating a property
maintenance code. The group determined that there was a need for some type of
such code and concluded that it would be appropriate for the Village to adopt
the 2003 International Property Maintenance Code, published by the
International Code Council (ICC), as a model. He reported that most neighboring
communities have property maintenance codes and use a version of this code.
This would put Winnetka and its property owners on equal footing with its
neighbors. He said the draft code the group is proposing has been amended in
order to be more consistent with conditions in Winnetka and pointed out that
all references to residential properties have been eliminated.
Manager Williams asked if this would be a valuable tool for Mr. D’Onofrio in
his role as the Village’s professional Code Administrator. Mr. D’Onofrio
responded affirmatively.
Bill Shiel, Chairman of the Business Community Development Commission, reported
that the Commission had looked at this proposal and felt that it would be a
good way for the Village to deal with rogue property owners. Insofar as there
are rules that apply, such a code would provide a means for staff to hear from
tenants.
Manager Williams inquired whether existing staff could administer such a code.
Mr. D’Onofrio responded affirmatively.
Cicely Michalak, Director of the Chamber of Commerce, expressed the Chamber’s
support and thanks.
There was a unanimous consensus among the Trustees to slate the proposed
ordinance for introduction.
* * *
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions, continued.
f)   Resolution No. R-30-2007 – NERC Reliability Compliance – Adoption. There
being no additional comments or questions regarding this matter, which was
discussed at the June 12th Study Session, President Woodbury called for a
motion to adopt the resolution.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-30-
2007 authorizing the Village to enter into the “Agreement for Assignment of
Responsibility for Complying with NERC Reliability Standards” between the
Illinois Municipal Electric Agency and the Village of Winnetka. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Ritchell,
Tucker, Behles, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Resolution No. R-31-2007 – MFT Appropriation – Adoption. Mr. Saunders
explained that various Village streets that have been scheduled for resurfacing
as a part of the 2007 Capital Improvement Projects are funded by Motor Fuel Tax
funds. These streets include Rosewood and Burr Avenues. In May, the Village
Council adopted Resolution No. R-20-2007, authorizing the expenditure of
$200,000 for this project; however, the low bid for the total project was
$251,726, exceeding the amount authorized. Therefore, he recommended the
authorization of additional funds to cover the final bid amount.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-31-
2007 authorizing an additional $60,000 of Motor Fuel Tax funds for the
resurfacing of various streets, bringing the total authorization to $260,000.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
e)   Policy Direction: 340 Poplar. This item was moved forward on the Agenda
and discussed earlier in the evening.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1513 and 1514. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1513 in the amount of $741,310.18 and Warrant List
No. 1514 in the amount of $360,252.38. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Berger. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Discussion: Zoning Retail Overlay District. This item was moved forward on
the Agenda and discussed earlier in the evening.
c)   Discussion: Commercial Property Maintenance Code. This item was moved
forward on the Agenda and discussed earlier in the evening.
d)   Petition: Ash Street Flood Relief. This item was moved forward on the
Agenda and discussed earlier in the evening.
e)   Bid No. 07-012: Power Plant Window Replacement. Mr. Keys reviewed the bids
for the third phase of window replacements at the Electric Plant. He explained
that the existing windows have no screens and are not energy efficient. He
recommended that the bid be awarded to the low bidder, Winnova Windows & Doors,
Inc.
Trustee Behles reported that he had reviewed cross sections and specifications
on the proposed windows and that they are standard and acceptable, so he could
see no technical reason to move away from the low bidder.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the contract for
the replacement of windows on the south side of Boiler Room #8 at the Electric
Plant to Winnova Windows & Doors, Inc. in the amount of $41,347. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Behles,
Eilers, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid No. 07-09: 2007 Street Resurfacing. Mr. Saunders reviewed bids for the
2007 Street Resurfacing project that is solely funded by Motor Fuel Tax funds.
He said the streets scheduled for work this year are Rosewood Avenue from
Willow to Pine Street, and Burr Avenue from Westmoor to Laurel. Mr. Saunders
explained that although the lowest bid exceeded the engineer’s estimate for the
total project cost, staff’s opinion is that re-bidding the work will not
significantly improve the results. He therefore recommended awarding the bid to
the low bidder, Chicagoland Paving Contractors.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to award the contract for the
2007 MFT Street Resurfacing project to Chicagoland Paving Contractors for the
bid amount of $251,726.50. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
g)   Contract: Landfill Groundwater Monitoring Contract. Mr. Saunders reviewed
the bids for sample collection and chemical analysis of groundwater, leachate,
and sanitary sewer discharge at the landfill site and summarized the criteria
under which the bids were evaluated. He explained that although the Village
closed its municipal landfill in 1994, it must still comply with the applicable
regulations of the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency and the
Metropolitan Water Reclamation District of Greater Chicago. After going through
the bid process, he recommended that the bid for professional services be
awarded to Prairie Analytical Services.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the contract to
provide groundwater monitoring services for the Winnetka Municipal Landfill to
Prairie Analytical Services for an estimated annual amount of $50,577.80. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Eilers,
Berger, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reviewed the final design for the Martin Luther
King memorial. He noted that the construction should begin the end of July,
with completion anticipated by Labor Day, and added that the project has
received significant support in the form of contributions of labor and
services. He congratulated the students on their efforts thus far and indicated
that they would be continuing fund raising throughout the summer. He encouraged
all Winnetkans to support this project.
Trustee Poor remarked that the design is thoughtful and creative and preserves
history in a visual way. He thanked Trustee Behles for his efforts.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to endorse the design as
presented by Trustee Behles. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Finally, Trustee Behles announced that the new Winnetka Report would soon be
arriving at homes throughout the Village.
c)   Village Manager. None.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
8)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Lawson Whitesides to the
Forestry Commission to replace Bernie Hammer whose term has expired. Trustee
Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to appoint Mr. Whitesides. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
9)   Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, no Executive Session was held.
10)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 12:01 a.m.
   ______________________________________
   Recording Secretary
July 10, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: August 7, 2007)
A record of a legally convened Special Meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
10, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, and Herb Ritchell. Absent: Trustees Ken
Behles and Jessica Tucker. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Assistant
Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla and approximately four persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   July 17, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   July 19, 2007, Special Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 19, 2007, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury referred the Trustees
to changes submitted by Trustee Eilers. After reviewing the changes, Trustee
Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the minutes, as amended.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-9-2007 – Zoning Variation: 330 Willow Road – Adoption.
There being no additional comments or questions, President Woodbury called for
a motion to adopt the ordinance granting a zoning variation to permit the
construction of two additions along the north end of the existing home at 330
Willow Road. The motion to adopt was made by Trustee Poor and seconded by
Trustee Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Poor, Ritchell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Behles and Tucker.
b)   Ordinance No. M-10-2007 – Zoning Variations: 938 Cherry Street – Adoption.
There being no additional comments or questions, President Woodbury called for
a motion to adopt the ordinance granting four zoning variations to permit the
construction of a second floor addition at 938 Cherry Street. The motion to
adopt was made by Trustee Eilers and seconded by Trustee Poor. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Poor, Ritchell,
and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Behles and Tucker.
c)   Ordinance No. M-11-2007 – Zoning Variations: 1243 Scott Street – Adoption.
There being no additional comments or questions, President Woodbury called for
a motion to adopt the ordinance granting a zoning variation to permit the
construction of a kitchen addition at 1243 Scott Street. The motion to adopt
was made by Trustee Poor and seconded by Trustee Ritchell. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Ritchell, Eilers, and
Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Behles and Tucker.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2007 – Right-of-way Regulations – Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed the history of this matter and recent action by the State
legislature and explained additional revisions that were not in the agenda
packets, but were distributed to the Council this evening. She noted that she
is part of a subcommittee of the Illinois Municipal League’s Home Rule
Attorney’s Committee that is working to update the League’s model right-of way
ordinance in light of the new legislation. She added that the committee expects
to complete the updated model by the end of the summer and that she anticipates
some further amendments to the Village’s ordinance at that time. She said that
the ordinance before the Council has been customized specifically for Winnetka
and will enable Village staff to deal with the requests received so far from
AT&T, as well as with any similar requests that the Village might receive. She
noted that AT&T has not yet received the necessary Certificate from the
Illinois Commerce Commission.
After discussing the mechanics of how a request will be handled, Trustee Poor,
seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to amend the draft ordinance as presented in
the Agenda materials and in this evening’s discussion. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Berger then moved to adopt Ordinance MC-10-2007, as amended. Trustee
Eilers seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Ritchell, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Behles and Tucker.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1515 and 1516. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1515 in the amount of $944,898.09 and Warrant List
No. 1516 in the amount of $319,096.53. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Berger. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Ritchell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Behles and Tucker.
b)   701 Sheridan – 8-inch Water Main. Mr. Ciesla reviewed the bids for an
extension of this water main, which will serve the new home at 701 Sheridan and
will eliminate the dead-end main located on a private road that serves 691 and
693 Sheridan. He noted that the Village Forester requested that installation be
by augering in two areas to avoid damaging tree roots, which, along with the
necessary casing, added to the cost of the project. He recommended that the bid
be awarded to the low bidder, Elmwood Sewer and Water in the amount of
$99,305.00.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the contract as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Poor, Ritchell, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Behles and
Tucker.
c)   Bid No. 07-007 – Refuse Truck Purchase (PW-26). Mr. Saunders reviewed the
bid criteria, bids and bidding process for a new vehicle to replace an existing
refuse truck that has reached the end of its useful service life. He
recommended that the bid for the purchase of a new Freightliner M2-106 chassis
and a new Pak-Mor RHC225B body be awarded to R.N.O.W., Inc., in the amount of
$114,201.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Eilers, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Behles and
Tucker.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury reported that the Post Office
Committee had submitted its report to the Village Council and that the Council
would begin its discussions of the issue in September. He urged everyone to
familiarize themselves with the Committee’s report.
Mr. Woodbury also reported that the dedication of the plaque commemorating Dr.
Martin Luther King, Jr.’s speech on the Village Green would be held at the
Village Green on July 25th at 7:00 p.m.
Finally, President Woodbury announced that Village representatives would be
meeting with the Park District to discuss ways in which the Village can assist
them with their service center project.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers encouraged everyone to attend the Sidewalk Sale
planned for July 20 and 21.
Trustee Ritchell reported that the Ad Hoc Historic and Residential Preservation
Committee has been reviewing research and expects to deliver its report to the
Council in September.
Trustee Poor reported that he has spoken with other Trustees and Village staff
about ways Council meetings might be streamlined. He indicated a consensus to
explore ways to achieve this and expressed his hope to create efficiencies to
benefit everyone. He suggested considering raising the threshold for Council
approval of bids and using a consent agenda.
President Woodbury commented on the difference between the consent agenda
process and the Council’s current process and suggested discussing the proposal
at a study session.
c)   Village Manager. None.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Eilers,
Poor, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Behles and Tucker. The meeting
adjourned at 8:30 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
July 17, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 7, 2007)
A record of a legally convened Regular Meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
17, 2007, at 7:33 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, and Herb Ritchell. Absent:
Trustee Jessica Tucker. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and approximately three
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   July 19, 2007, Special Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend. It is anticipated that Trustee Tucker
will not attend.
b)   August 7, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Ritchell, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 12, 2007, Study Session. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger,
moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-12-2007 – Special Use Permit: 490 Hibbard Road –
Introduction. President Woodbury called for any comments or questions regarding
the Park District’s request for a special use permit to allow the construction
of two additional platform tennis courts at the existing four-court complex at
Skokie Playfield.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the information in the Agenda Report is up-to-date
and noted that the Design Review Board, Plan Commission and Zoning Board of
Appeals had commented favorably on the request.
Responding to questions from Trustee Berger, Park District Director Liza
McElroy responded that the paddle tennis courts are open for use from 8:00 a.m.
to 11:00 p.m., seven days a week. She said that about 75% of the courts’ users
are Winnetka residents, with the approximately 300 members of the Platform
Tennis Club being the main users, and 125 other people taking lessons. Like the
Winnetka Hockey Club, the Platform Tennis Club sells blocks of time for
individual use, and the remainder of the time is used by the Park District for
rentals and classes. She added that the Park District also rents out the Paddle
Hut.
Trustee Berger expressed concern about dedicating additional open space to a
special interest group and about how tight it appears the area will be. Ms.
McElroy replied that the two predominant uses of the open space, golf and
platform tennis, operate in different seasons, so there shouldn’t be any
conflicts.
Manager Williams asked Ms. McElroy if the Park Board had reviewed and approved
this addition and she confirmed that they had, by a vote of 7-0.
Trustee Behles inquired about impermeable surface and stormwater retention. Ms.
McElroy indicated that the Park Board intends to comply with any requirements
imposed by the Village and has the funds to do so.
President Woodbury commented that the 2004 special use ordinance for the paddle
hut required that parking be monitored for a year and asked if there should be
a similar condition, due to the additional use with the same parking.
Manager Williams recollected that the Park District reported on the parking and
no concerns about conflicting uses existed. Trustee Ritchell commented that he
parks there frequently and rarely sees a conflict, and that he believes the
existing parking is satisfactory.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
12-2007, as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-11-2007 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to Fines
for Parking in Handicapped Parking Zones – Introduction. President Woodbury
called for any comments or questions regarding the proposed ordinance, which
would increase the current $100 fine for violations of the handicapped parking
zones to $250, noting that State law authorizes the Village to set the fine as
high as $350.
In response to a question from Trustee Ritchell, Chief DeLopez stated that
there were about 23 violations of the State or local law over the last year-
and-a-half. He added that the proposed ordinance would serve clarify the law
and resolve confusion over the fine.
Trustee Poor remarked that he believes it is good policy to conform the local
ordinance with the State statute, and Trustee Behles noted that this is the
same approach used for building code enforcement.
Attorney Janega commented that the amendment will allow the Village to cite
violators for the local violation, which is not reported to the State.
President Woodbury confirmed a consensus among the Trustees to set the fine at
$250.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
11-2007, as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2007 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to the
Special Events Provisions – Introduction. President Woodbury called for any
comments or questions regarding the proposed ordinance, which would amend the
Village Code as it pertains to special events that require the provision of
special Village personnel, services, public ways, or Village property.
Attorney Janega explained that Chapter 5.66 of the Code was amended earlier
this year when fees were restructured, and these amendments reflect
developments in applicable case law. The draft ordinance incorporates the
legislative findings into the text of the chapter, in addition to stating them
in the preamble, as was done in the prior ordinances. She explained that the
amendments provide a clearer statement of Village’s policy to apply content-
neutral standards in assessing requests for special services. She pointed out
that the amendments clarify the definitions of special events by focusing on
requests for the exclusive use of Village resources, making those resources
unavailable for the Village’s normal operations. Attorney Janega also pointed
out amendments to the exemptions, and noted the exclusion of parades, which are
covered elsewhere in the Village Code. She explained the amendments to the
review process and the insurance and indemnification provisions, and noted that
the words “and risks related to” should be inserted in the insurance provision
in subsection H on page 6 of the draft, after the words “nature and extent of.”
She noted that the application review process has been clarified and that
failure to apply for a permit before the event will now be addressed as an
ordinance violation, rather than by assessing a double fee.
In response to a question from Trustee Poor, Chief DeLopez said about six
special events held on a regular basis each year, and that some events need
Police, Fire and Public Works details, while smaller events may require a
smaller special detail;
Responding to a question from Trustee Ritchell, Attorney Janega stated that the
application processing provision uses the term “promptly” because it is not
possible to fix a specific number of days for processing all applications. She
noted that, while most applications can probably be processed within a week,
more complex events can take weeks to process. She added that staff will
attempt to provide a more specific term when the ordinance comes up for final
action.
Chief DeLopez noted that annual, anticipated special events can be processed in
a short time frame, that the Department maintains contact with event organizers
and keeps them informed, and that no events have been delayed because the
Village was not able to process an application.
Trustee Eilers asked whether a visit by a national figure, such as the Majority
Leader of the U.S. Senate, to speak on a matter of national import would be
subject to the ordinance. Attorney Janega responded that it would depend on
whether the event required a special detail and whether the event is open to
the general public. She observed that the ordinance clearly exempts official
visits by certain elected officials who serve the area and are not asking for
exclusive use of resources for an event that excludes the general public. She
stressed that the standard has to be based on the nature of the event and not
the content of the speech, but that, generally, if a gathering is not open to
the general public it will be a special event. Ms. Janega added that she would
look at the issue more closely and provide a more definitive answer when the
ordinance is presented for adoption.
Asked by Trustee Eilers about block parties, Attorney Janega explained that a
block party would be considered a special event, but that the ordinance
authorizes the Village Manager to waive fees and the application process if the
cost of processing an application would outweigh the amount of resources needed
for the event.
Trustee Poor inquired about the distinction between public and private events,
noting that the Children’s Fair is open to the public. Attorney Janega pointed
out that the distinguishing factor is whether there’s an exclusive allocation
or use of Village resources over and above what is required for normal
operations, and that the Children’s Fair has expanded to the point that it
requires the Village to hire back personnel.
Chief DeLopez added that, often an event does not require the exclusive
assignment of police personnel, but if the organizers request that we leave
personnel in place and on the scene, then it’s necessary to hire back off-duty
personnel to provide that service.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance
MC-12-2007, as presented, with the additional amendment presented by the
Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1517 and 1518. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1517 in the amount of $779,202.10 and Warrant List
No. 1518 in the amount of $332,921.76. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Poor, Ritchell, Behles, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Tucker.
b)   Policy Direction: 910 Gordon Terrace. President Woodbury called for any
comments or questions regarding this request for three zoning variations to
permit the construction of a third floor addition that received a negative
recommendation from the Zoning Board of Appeals.
Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio explained the owner was
seeking variations from gross floor area and building height limits, as well as
from front yard setback requirements. Referring to agenda pages 84 and 87, he
explained that the variation would allow for a 3-story house, since the
existing basement is counted as a story because of its height above grade. He
also noted the house on flag lot behind the property, which would have a view
of the full 3 stories.
Responding to questions from Trustees Ritchell and Eilers, Mr. D’Onofrio said
the house is a split level, and that the proposal would add a floor to its
center section. He explained that the expanded house would not be considered as
a basement with two floors above, because a large portion of basement area is
exposed, with the exposed height ranging from 5 to 8 feet, depending on where
it’s measured.
Trustee Behles pointed out that the Zoning Ordinance says that if a basement
extends more than 4 feet above the ground, it is a full story, not a basement,.
President Woodbury added that there are two criteria for building height: the
vertical height and the number of stories, and that both are considered.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that gross floor area usually comes into play before the
number of stories is an issue, and that in this case the proposal is 155 square
feet over the GFA limits.
Seymour Turner, a representative of the owner’s architect, Airoom, said that
due to the sloping grade, the rear facade presents a greater elevation, but
pointed out that the maximum vertical height will be 29.5 feet, which is lower
than the allowable height to the ridge and will only be across a small portion
of the building. He asserted that a conforming new building would produce more
apparent bulk. He explained that they considered alternatives that would put
the addition above another part of the house where there is no basement, but
that created interior circulation problems and would have needed a side yard
variance.
Trustee Behles acknowledged that the only point the applicant could argue is
that the ridge of the roof is below the height limitation because the height of
the house doesn’t change as you raise the first floor. He stated he thinks the
question is about the appearance of bulk and the appearance of the house from
the two sides, noting that the house doesn’t appear to be 1.5 stories from the
front and appears very tall from the rear. He added that, although we have
allowed elevated basements to exceed GFA, we haven’t permitted the addition of
a 3rd story, which he views as a more significant request in this case.
Mr. Bill Pridmore, 906 Gordon Terrace, owner of the flag lot behind the Subject
Property, expressed his agreement with the Zoning Board’s recommendation. He
stated it was misleading to refer to what could be done with new construction
on the property and pointed out concerns about side yards as well. He observed
that granting the variation would change the way ridge height and stories are
defined and would set a bad precedent.
Responding to a question from Trustee Poor, Mr. Pridmore stated that the 3-
story west elevation on page 87 of the agenda depicts the view from the front
of his house.
Trustee Poor noted the ZBA’s efforts in considering the case and observed that
the ZBA’s comments were thoughtful and should be given deference unless the
Council believes the ZBA did something wrong. He expressed his belief that the
ZBA got it right, as the height increase is 7 feet and that the impact on the
neighboring property is significant, and stated he did not find any unique
circumstances sufficient to override the ZBA. He also expressed concern about
setting a precedent for all construction.
Trustee Berger agreed with Trustee Poor, adding that the petitioner hasn’t
proven a hardship, and expressing concern about the additional bulk.
Trustee Eilers noted the ZBA’s deliberations and stated that, although he is
inclined to preserve existing housing stock, he favors denial in this case
because of the bulk issue.
Trustee Ritchell expressed agreement with the other Trustees.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to uphold the finding of the
Zoning Board of Appeals and to deny the requested variation. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Behles, Eilers,
and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Tucker.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reported that the Streetscape Committee will meet
on Thursday at 7:30 a.m., for perhaps its final meeting. He reported the
committee as preparing a comprehensive package for the Council and that the
committee is developing a solid consensus in terms of mapping and street
furniture design, implementation and cost models. Trustee Behles also commented
that the Martin Luther King historic marker is now under construction and
invited everyone to the dedication ceremony at the Village Green on Wednesday,
July 25th at 7:00 p.m. The dedication will be followed by a reception at the
Woman’s Club. He noted the students are still trying to raise some funds.
Trustee Eilers invited everyone to the Chamber of Commerce Sidewalk Sale Days,
July 21st and 22nd.
c)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that the proposal for the
Sunset-Ash-Birch Street drainage study had come in slightly under $20,000, and
that the study will proceed.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury called for a motion to reappoint Janet Shen to the Design
Review Board for a full term. Trustee Ritchell so moved, seconded by Trustee
Behles. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury then called for a motion to appoint John Thomas as the Park
Board’s representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee Behles so moved,
seconded by Trustee Poor. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments, Possible Purchase of Land, and Pending and
Probable Litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(3), 2(c)(5), and 2(c)(11),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Poor seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Poor, Behles, Berger and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Tucker.
The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:30 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor and Ritchell. Absent: Trustee
Tucker. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Behles,
Eilers, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Tucker. The meeting adjourned
at 9:32 p.m.
   Recording Secretary
July 19, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: August 7, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday, July
19, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, King Poor, Tom Eilers, and Herb Ritchell. Absent:
Trustee Jessica Tucker. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike
D’Onofrio, and approximately 20 persons in the audience.
2)   Discussion of the BCDC Recommendations Regarding the C-2 Retail Overlay
District.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the Retail Overlay District was established in the
late 1980’s in an effort to stop the proliferation of real estate offices and
to provide a critical mass of clustered retail uses, so pedestrians could walk
to several retail destinations. He added that non-retail uses were permitted as
special uses in the overlay under certain conditions. He reported that the
Business Community Development Commission (BCDC) was charged with performing
studies and making recommendations regarding business development in the
Village, and that examining the overlay district was one of the ways it chose
to address the issue.
Mr. D’Onofrio said the BCDC looked at the history behind the creation of the
overlay district and the granting of special uses, and at nonconformities that
would be created if the overlay boundaries changed. He observed that an
outgrowth of the BCDC’s review was the creation of the Winnetka Commercial
Property Owners Association, which has actively participated in the BCDC’s
discussions.
Trustee Eilers commented that the BCDC was originally directed to look at the
boundaries of the overlay district, but also decided to look at the bigger
picture and study the overlay’s history and special use history in the district
as well.
Mr. D’Onofrio elaborated on the proposed changes to the overlay boundaries and
conditions requiring a special use permit. He noted that special uses are
required for the specified non-retail uses if they are located within 100 feet
of a public street, and gave an example of a first floor doctor’s office, which
would require a special use for a storefront location, but would be a permitted
use at the rear of a courtyard building, more than 100 feet from the street, or
on a second or third floor. In response to a question from President Woodbury,
he replied that the special use process is used as a tool to discourage non-
retail uses.
BCDC Chairman Bill Shiel observed that the Commission spent a lot of time
debating changing the distance requirement from 100 to 60 feet, which would
open up courtyard building uses, especially for medical offices on the first
floor. He added that the BCDC concluded that 60 feet is a generally accepted
retail depth and that reducing the distance wouldn’t hurt the intent of the
overlay district.
Mr. D’Onofrio next reported that the BCDC also considered whether the
boundaries of the overlay district should be contracted, expanded, or left
alone and that, after lengthy discussions, the BCDC recommended expanding the
West Elm Overlay by adding the two bank buildings at Green Bay Road and Elm
Street and the Grand Foods between Spruce and Pine, and expanding the Hubbard
Woods Overlay by adding the BP-Amoco property at the northeast corner of Tower
and Green Bay.
Manager Williams asked Mr. D’Onofrio to comment on his experience of how the
overlay district works and on the pros and cons of overlay districts generally.
Mr. D’Onofrio answered that the true test of an overlay district’s
effectiveness is how many special uses and variations are applied for, and how
many vacancies there are. He opined that Winnetka’s overlay has worked fairly
well from a zoning perspective, if the goal is to stop the proliferation on
non-retail uses in the area. He expressed concern about including the bank
properties in the overlay, stating it would create nonconformities, which will
then require variances to do any improvements. He added that the overlay should
be kept compact and noted that employees of non-retail uses activate a lot of
the retail uses.
Trustee Eilers explained that the BCDC’s reasoning for adding the banks to the
overlay had to do with land use succession issues and concern about what would
happen to the gateway of the West Elm business district if the banks were to
leave. He noted that the BP-Amoco property in Hubbard Woods also stands as a
gateway to the Hubbard Woods business district.
Jim Sayegh, co-chair and co-founder of the Winnetka Commercial Property Owners
Association (WCPOA) and owner of the Winnetka Galleria, reported that the
WCPOA’s membership believes the status quo should be maintained and that the
measures being discussed won’t create any more vibrancy in the business
districts. He stated that the overlay district is not in and of itself a bad
thing, if it is applied sensitively and correctly, but otherwise it could
create vacancies instead of vibrancy. He added that, in his view, the special
use process creates vacancies.
Mr. Sayegh stated that while the creation of the overlay district was a good
reaction to the proliferation of non-retail uses, WCPOA members feel that times
have changed and there are service uses that complement the retail industry and
would actually help to promote it. He urged the Council to form a panel that
includes property owners to study the overlay issue further. He noted that the
owners’ focus is on the use issue, and that the WCPOA’s position is that it is
unreasonable to allow a vet but not a doctor or dentist as a permitted use, and
that fitness or health clubs and physical therapists should also be permitted
uses. He asserted that the special use process is intimidating and that a lot
of people don’t want to deal with it.
Asked how many property owners are represented by the WCPOA, Mr. Sayegh replied
that it represents almost half the commercial square footage in the Village. He
noted that a WCPOA survey of Village merchants indicated “a groundswell of
support” to permit some of the service uses he mentioned in the business
districts. He suggested using a square footage concept for the overly, rather
than a fixed distance from the street, and used a floor plan of the Winnetka
Galleria to illustrate his suggestion.
Robert Dini, 1056 Gage Street, observed that he owns two buildings in Hubbard
Woods, and asked the Council to treat the commercial property owners as an
integral part of the community and to value their input. He echoed Mr. Sayegh’s
comment that the special use application process is cumbersome, noting that
according to Mr. D’Onofrio, it takes at least three months to process a special
use, and many applications take longer.
Bernard Hammer, 1455 Tower, opined that the overlay district creates a taking
of the use of property, and the Village ought to consider removing the sales
tax barrier. He said he believes owners should get the highest and best use of
their property.
Responding to Mr. Hammer, Attorney Janega expressed her professional opinion
that the overlay does not constitute a taking and added that the Council
articulates sustainable, detailed findings and conclusions on each of its
special use decisions.
Cicely Michalak, Executive Director of the Winnetka Chamber of Commerce and
BCDC member, noted that since its inception, the BCDC has welcomed landlords
and worked with them. She observed that resolving the issues has been a
struggle, but that overall the C-2 overlay has been seen as a positive thing.
She said that, as a Chamber executive, she hears from retailers who don’t like
the close proximity of medical uses because of parking problems, and indicated
that while the flow of people to medical offices might benefit restaurants, it
might not increase the vitality of retail businesses.
Mr. Shiel agreed that the BCDC has had open discussions with the property
owners. He noted the difference of opinion, but added that the BCDC has been
unbiased and has considered the property owners’ opinions in its deliberations.
Responding to an inquiry from the audience, Mr. Shiel stated that the BCDC
debated the fixed line vs. square footage issues, but concluded there are ways
to manipulate the use of square footage to circumvent retail uses, and there
can be difficulties if a building is poorly designed.
Dick Busscher, 1275 Asbury, indicated that certain locations that are now
included in the overlay district should not be, such as the north side of Gage
Street and the building on Tower at Green Bay Road, which faces single-family
residences and is difficult to rent because of a lack of foot traffic during
regular business hours.
Dan Schermerhorn, owner of Waterworks, 907 Green Bay Road, commented that the
special use process should be an integral part of the overlay discussion, and
that the BCDC should include a member from the WCPOA who has gone through the
special use process and can suggest how to streamline it.
David Humphrey, owner of the Moth Meyer property, between Spruce and Pine, on
the west side of Green Bay Road, stated that the BCDC failed to consider
leaving the overlay district alone and observed that it is highly unlikely that
the banks on Elm or the gas station in Hubbard Woods are going anywhere. He
noted that his property is in the transition area between the C-2 overlay and
B-1 zoning district, that it provides a transition, as it is out of the flow of
foot traffic and doesn’t rely on public parking spaces. He added that service
businesses have a customer base in the Village, and should be on the retail
outskirts, but not pushed completely out of town. He commended the BCDC for
evaluating the line of demarcation and recommending reducing it to 60 feet, but
added that the health of the retail districts would not be threatened if the
overlay were not expanded.
Rich Lalley, owner of DOTI, 568 Lincoln, said vibrancy can only be created by
attracting people into the business districts, and that people frequently stop
into his shop after an appointment with the orthodontist, doctor or other
medical professional. He agreed that the special use process takes too long and
costs small business owners valuable time and money which small “mom & pop”
businesses simply cannot afford. He asked the Council to consider the
importance of attracting these small business owners.
Josh Braun, of Braun Investment Group, 818 Elm and 906 Green Bay Road, said
businesses today are not conducted as they were 20 years ago when the overlay
was created. He discussed the question of the 60-foot break point for non-
retail uses, and pointed out that the issue is not simply black and white, and
that there is competition from Old Orchard and Northbrook Court. He noted that
retail rents are high and property owners would prefer the higher rent that
retail brings over the lower rent of a medical or other office, but that
sometimes non-retail is the only option to fill the space.
President Woodbury observed that reviewing the overlay district is just one
piece of a very large puzzle that the Village is undertaking to solve. He noted
that the subject is integral to the Post Office redevelopment, streetscape and
parking issues, and the Council does not want to overlook any detail in its
mission to provide for a fuller business district.
President Woodbury continued that two ancillary topics had been raised in the
discussion of expanding the overlay district, the first one being a voice on
the BCDC from the WCPOA. He asked Trustee Behles to discuss BCDC membership
with Mr. Shiel, and indicated that the Council would discuss the matter
further. On the issue of special uses, he pointed out that special use permit
processes are put in place intentionally so that there is review of a proposal.
He noted that a lot of municipalities around the country downzone properties so
they are forced into the special use process to do design review, but that
Winnetka does not use this tactic. He asked Manager Williams to work with his
staff to explore some options that might streamline some of the special use
processes. He said the expediency of the deal making process must be weighed
against the Village’s interest, and that he believed being Home Rule could help
the Village accomplish some changes in this area.
President Woodbury asked the Trustees for their thoughts on the proposal before
them, and listed the options as (1) do nothing, (2) continue to discuss the
matter, or (3) refer the proposed zoning changes to the Plan Commission for
consideration. He suggested if the Council desires to move forward on the
proposal, they could refer some of the questions to the Plan Commission for
their further review and study, after which time it would come back before the
Council.
Trustee Berger expressed appreciation for the BCDC’s work and the input of all
the people attending the meeting, and stated that doing nothing is not a good
option. She said the 60-foot demarcation should be studied further, as well as
the question of whether properties on side streets that don’t have frontage on
Green Bay Road should be in the overlay district.
Trustee Behles observed that the goals of the Council and audience are in sync,
even if the methodology for achieving them is not. He indicated that with a
diversity of commercial buildings in the Village, there needs to be a fairness
benchmark for deciding on a break point for retail. He added that the
discussion needs to continue, either with the Council or in the Plan
Commission, so details can be worked out in order to avoid any hint of
arbitrariness.
Trustee Poor commended the BCDC for the care that went into their
recommendation, and also commended the property owners for their constructive
and thoughtful dialogue. He agreed with Trustee Berger that doing nothing was
not a good option, and with Trustee Behles that more careful study is needed on
the issue of the break point. He noted that not all non-retail is alike, and
some non-retail can have a symbiotic relationship with retail. He said the need
for a transition area was articulated very well, as was the issue of Gage
Street, and he thought the Plan Commission could do some further study on all
the issues that were raised in the evening’s discussion.
Trustee Eilers indicated that the formation of the WCPOA was a wonderful
outcome of the BCDC’s deliberations, and urged them to continue speaking up on
issues in Winnetka. He said he favored forwarding the BCDC’s recommendations to
the Plan Commission.
Trustee Ritchell observed that the BCDC’s recommendations and the night’s
discussion were very informative, and added that further deliberation and study
was warranted. He agreed with President Woodbury that the special use process
should be streamlined, if possible.
President Woodbury observed that the Council unanimously favored moving the
process forward and suggested sending the BCDC’s proposal to the Plan
Commission for further study and recommendations. He noted that the idea is not
to redo what the BCDC had accomplished, but to determine if the recommendations
are in the Village’s best interests.
3)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion passed unanimously. The meeting adjourned at 9:50 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
August 7, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 21, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 7, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, and
approximately 4 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   August 14, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustees Tucker, Poor and Ritchell, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   August 21, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Berger, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 10, 2007, Special Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
b)   July 17, 2007, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury referred the Trustees
to changes submitted by Trustee Eilers. After reviewing the changes, Trustee
Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes, as amended.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   July 19, 2007, Special Meeting & Study Session. Trustee Poor, seconded by
Trustee Eilers, moved to approved the minutes. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-12-2007 –Special Use Permit: 490 Hibbard Rd. - Adoption.
President Woodbury reviewed the request from the Winnetka Park District for a
special use permit to construct two additional platform tennis courts at the
existing four-court complex at the Skokie Playfield.
There being no further comments or questions, Trustee Behles, seconded by
Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-12-2007, granting a special
use permit and variations to the Winnetka Park District. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-13-2007 –Zoning Variations: 800 Tower Rd. – Introduction.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio reviewed a request for four
zoning variations on the Subject Property, to permit construction of a new
detached garage, reconstruct the driveway, and add a front walk. He explained
that the applicants filed a previous application which included variations to
allow construction of a circular driveway, but the application was denied. He
noted that this application represents their efforts to address concerns raised
during the earlier process. The Zoning Board of Appeals voted to approved the
current application by a vote of 5 to 0.
Trustee Eilers confirmed that there is no fully conforming alternative for the
garage placement.
Trustee Ritchell confirmed that no neighbors have come forward with any
comments.
In answer to other questions from the Council, Mr. D’Onofrio responded that all
of the roofed lot coverage variation is being requested for the garage, and the
impermeable surface variation includes the garage and increases to the
driveway. He also established that the 1974 one-story addition to the residence
had never been a garage.
Trustee Tucker asked if there were any hardships that run with the land to
support the variation request. Attorney Janega noted that in granting a
variance, the Council needs findings of a practical difficulty or particular
hardship, and that since there are practical difficulties on this lot the
particular hardship finding could be struck and there would still be grounds
based on the record for granting the variance.
Trustee Behles pointed out that the garage could be more in compliance by
reducing its size, and if the driveway were gravel the entire impermeable
surface nonconformity would disappear.
Trustee Berger observed that it appears the garage is going to be used for
storage of items other than a car, in effect making it more like a big shed,
while the vehicles on the property remain parked outside the garage. She
questioned whether the variance is really being requested because the applicant
has too many things that need to be stored on the property.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the applicant was not in attendance at the meeting, at
which point President Woodbury suggested tabling the request until the
applicant could be present to answer questions.
Trustee Tucker indicated that she was ready to vote on the matter, and Trustees
Behles and Eilers indicated that they could vote now or wait for the applicant.
Trustees Poor, Ritchell and Berger favored waiting for the applicant to appear.
President Woodbury said he would rather give the applicant the courtesy of
being heard before a vote was taken.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to table the Ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried five to one.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-11-1007 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to Fines
for Parking in Handicapped Parking Zones – Adoption. President Woodbury briefly
reviewed the amendments to Section 10.24.010 of the Village Code to increase
the fine for improperly parking in spaces reserved for persons with
disabilities from $100 to $250.
There being no comments or questions, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-11-2007, increasing fines for
unauthorized use of parking places reserved for persons with disabilities. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees, Tucker, Poor,
Ritchell, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-33-2007 – Release of Executive Session Minutes. Attorney
Janega explained that the Open Meetings Act requires the Council to examine and
approve Executive Session minutes twice a year and to release minutes for which
confidentiality is no longer required. The Act also requires the Village to
retain verbatim recordings of closed sessions for 18 months. Resolution No. R-
33-2007 covers the Executive Session minutes from June 6, 2006, through June 5,
2007, and authorizes the destruction of audio recordings of Executive Session
minutes from July 7, 2005, through December 20, 2005. Attorney Janega noted
that the Resolution indicates the need to retain the confidentiality of ten
sets of minutes due to continuing litigation matters, and also states that the
Council is not authorizing or consenting to the public disclosure of the
verbatim recordings.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve Resolution No.
R-33-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Poor, Eilers, Berger, Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, expressed his dissatisfaction with the Willow Road
Enhancement process, and said he believed Northfield was intransigent on the
issue of the bottleneck problem. He remarked that the bottleneck on Willow Road
is not a local problem, but a regional one, and indicated that he thought
Glenview and Northbrook should have been included in the planning process. He
warned against ceding Winnetka’s interests to Northfield, and also mentioned
that the community meetings should have been scheduled for a location in
Winnetka, to make it more convenient for Winnetka residents to attend.
President Woodbury responded that he volunteered to get involved in the Willow
Road enhancement process to help break the deadlock between Northfield and
IDOT. He disagreed with Mr. Kates’ characterization of Northfield as
intransigent, and also indicated that Winnetka is not ceding anything to
Northfield, but rather is acting because of both communities’ shared interests.
He said IDOT has made it very clear that they are only concerned with
increasing capacity, and that the goals and objectives of both Winnetka and
Northfield need to be articulated to them. He pointed out that the issue is
about process and getting ideas on the table that weren’t evident in previous
discussions.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, indicated that he would like to see the underlying
issues addressed with regard to the Willow Road enhancement process, including
the bottleneck problem and also enhancing access to the Edens Expressway. He
noted that as a layperson, the technical aspects can be difficult to understand
and suggested that they be presented in advance of the community meetings, so
people will have a chance to digest them before attending.
President Woodbury responded that the growth projections along the Willow Road
corridor are all being taken into account and will be included whether Glenview
is sitting at the table or not. He noted that the main purpose of the study is
to explore different technical options, make decisions and act on them so the
problem doesn’t continue for the next 20 years.
Betina Carlisle, 150 Eddy Lane, Northfield, thanked President Woodbury for his
involvement and indicated that many Northfield residents felt that Glenview was
using undue influence with Senator Schoenberg to push their agenda through. She
expressed the hope that a solution could be found that benefits all the
interested communities.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1519 and 1520. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1519 in the amount of $1,084,466.82 and Warrant
List No. 1520 in the amount of $505,006.78. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Behles. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Poor, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid: PW-10 – Light Dump Truck/Plow. Manager Williams reported that the
Village’s Capital Improvement Plan includes the replacement of PW-10, a 1997
dump/plow truck which is at the end of its useful life. He explained that the
State of Illinois cooperative purchasing program contains the specified truck
chassis, as well as other required equipment.
There being no questions or comments, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve Bid PW-10, in the amount of $50,889, to purchase a
2008 Ford F450 chassis from Bob Ridings Ford of Taylorville, IL. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Eilers, Berger,
Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. President Woodbury indicated that the need has arisen
to schedule extra Study Sessions in September and October, in order to review
the Post Office Committee’s final report, the Lincoln Avenue parking deck
analysis, and the Village Hall renovation and Streetscape reports.
b)   Trustees. None.
c)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that in light of the national
attention that has been directed towards infrastructure, he was pleased to
announce that by the end of the summer, all of Winnetka’s bridges will have
been rebuilt in the last ten years, accomplished without bond issues or tax
increases. He also reported that before completing the Cool Cities application,
the Village will have to do an inventory of its 1990 carbon emissions. Finally,
he noted that he is attempting to resolve a drainage issue for some of the
residents on Hackberry.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments, Personnel Matters and Pending and Probable
Litigation pursuant to Section 2(c)(3), 2(c)1 and 2(c)11, respectively, of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned
into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:45 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Ritchell, Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:46 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
August 14, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: September 4, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday, August
14, 2007 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers. Absent: Trustees Herb Ritchell, King
Poor and Jessica Tucker. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys,
Assistant Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, Director of Community
Development Mike D’Onofrio, Planning Technician Jill Morgan and 8 persons in
the audience.
President Woodbury announced that Item #4, Juvenile Law & Procedures, had been
removed from the agenda.
2)   Power Plant Diesel Fuel Options
Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys reported that the two diesel fuel lines
that supply the Electric Plant are inoperable and at least one of them must be
replaced. He provided a detailed explanation of the necessity of diesel fuel to
allow the Village to generate power for critical Village facilities in the
event of a failure in the power transmission grid. Mr. Keys also described the
options for replacing the fuel lines, and reported that staff recommends
installing one tank in the coal bunkers at the plant, as it is the most cost-
effective solution; and allows the tanks at Tower & Green Bay Road to be
removed and the fuel lines in the Tower Road right-of-way to be abandoned in
place. He said that after the installation is complete, fuel would be delivered
through a line at the top of the bluff directly into the tank located in the
plant. Mr. Keys then answered questions about what environmental concerns there
might be and what steps would be taken to remediate them.
After weighing the need for providing services to critical facilities in the
event of power failure against the minimal risks and environmental issues, the
consensus of the Council members present was to proceed as recommended by
staff. Manager Williams noted that the staff will bring more information to the
Council as the project progresses.
3)   Winnetka Historical & Architectural Inventory. 
Posey Fisher, Chair of the Landmark Preservation Commission, introduced members
of the Ad Hoc Historical Preservation Committee and reported that in response
to the caucus platform, the Committee will be finishing its report to the
Council in October with a strong recommendation to hire a consultant to do an
inventory of all buildings in Winnetka. The Committee proposes the inventory be
done to create a database that can be used as a tool to research housing and
educate potential buyers about homes in the Village. In the ensuing discussion,
Mrs. Fisher explained that the bulk of the work would be done by the consultant
and that much of the information that would go into the database is already
available from the assessor’s office and in the Historical Society’s records.
She added that the Village staff could then be responsible for updating records
and keeping the database current.
Committee members Julie Windsor, Laura Good and Charles Hoffman spoke about the
importance of this project as part of an effort to reduce demolitions and to
preserve existing housing in Winnetka. They stressed that the inventory would
be a database for use by residents and prospective builders and homeowners.
The Trustees generally spoke in favor of the project; however, Trustee Berger
mentioned the number of projects the Council and staff have before them and
suggested the timing for the project may not be right.
To help minimize staff time, President Woodbury requested the Committee’s
assistance in developing criteria for the RFP for the consultant and asked the
Committee to have that ready when they present their report to the Council for
further discussion.
4)   Juvenile Law and Procedures.
Removed from agenda.
5)   Discussion – Special Use Permit Process for C2 Overlay District
Manager Williams explained that staff had discussed possibilities for
streamlining special use procedures, but after speaking with the Village
Attorney about the legislative process, staff had determined that there was not
much that could be done to reduce the timeframe, except to consider giving the
Zoning Administrator flexibility for some minor requests.
Village Attorney Janega further explained the legislative nature of the
Council’s actions and cautioned against departing from established zoning
process. She added that if the special use requests were to go directly to the
Council, the Council would have to allot time to consider whether the use is
consistent with the Comprehensive Plan and to conduct the public hearing and
hear testimony, work currently done by the Plan Commission and the Zoning
Board.
Jim Sayegh, 910 Green Bay Road, offered suggestions from his perspective as a
property owner, asking that the Council allow the special use process to
accommodate non-retail tenants, and to consider setting fees according to
square footage to ease the expense for smaller spaces. He also suggested the
Council consider expanding prohibited uses or that it allow owners to come
before the Council for preliminary guidance before filing an application, so
that owners would not have to wait for a decision as to whether a use will or
will not be allowed. He asserted that the process is hurting the small “mom and
pop” operations and should welcome as may different business as possible.
In response to questions from Trustee Berger, Ms. Janega advised against having
the Council give a pre-application indication of approval of a use in order for
a property owner to secure a tenant, because it effectively makes a zoning
change and could lead to litigation if the Council ultimately denies an
application after it gets a full record. However, she did say that some time
could be saved by waiving introducing and adopting a special use request in one
meeting.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio explained that, as a practical
matter, an applicant knows what the Council’s final action will be after
introduction, s and the process moves forward, except for issuance of permits,
so that an applicant would not gain much from a single appearance before the
Council.
The Council discussed examining the permitted uses rather than the process to
see if some flexibility could be provided there. The Trustees commented that:
1) the ordinance is strict on the uses of right, but if that is impractical,
then there must be a clear distinction on what uses are special; 2) it may be
possible to consider a different use for the rear of a space in the overlay
district; 3) the Council must also consider the effect of permitted uses on the
process; and 4) there has to be a process that is legally sound for both
winners and losers, and that applies to all, so that if challenged, the Village
will prevail, as has been the case in prior court challenges.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Plan Commission has been asked to study the
uses in the overlay district and hopes to have a report after their next
meeting.
6)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
7)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:10 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
August 21, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 4, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 21, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Sandra Berger. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Finance
Director Ed McKee, and approximately 4 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 6 (a), has been removed from the agenda,
and that the correct Resolution No. for Item 6 (c) is R-34-2007. The Trustees
approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   September 4, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   September 11, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 7, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2007 –Amending the Village Code Pertaining to the
Special Events Provisions – Adoption. As noted above, this item was removed
from the agenda. President Woodbury asked the audience members if anyone had
come to the meeting specifically to talk about the Special Event Ordinance.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce, said he was glad the ordinance had been removed from
the agenda because he considered the amendments expanding the exclusions for
public officials to be ill-advised and a radical departure from existing
Village law. He added that they weren’t discussed at the ordinance’s
introduction, questioned why they had been added, and who would pay for the
extra police protection for all the added exclusions. He noted that almost
every visit to Winnetka by an elected official would be a political event, and
not one for the general public.
President Woodbury indicated that the changes in question were made in response
to comments from Trustee Eilers, and that the original form limited the
exception to official visits, not fundraising visits, by elected officials who
represent Winnetkans. He noted that the changes would likely be discussed at
the first Council meeting in September.
b)   Ordinance No. M-14-2007 – Disposing of Surplus Equipment – Introduction.
Manager Williams reported that the subject ordinance would authorize the
Village to dispose of ten vehicles that are past their useful life and have
been or will be replaced. He observed that the equipment would be sold at the
NWMC’s September 22nd auction at the Winnetka Village Yards.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the Ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Resolution No. R-34-2007 – Joint Cy Pres Grant Application – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reported that the Winnetka and Wilmette Police Departments had
previously prepared a joint application for a Cy Pres fund grant to offset the
cost of the joint purchase of a computer aided dispatch (CAD) system. She
explained that Resolution R-4-2007 authorized the Chief of Police to make the
application, but that due to some administrative changes in the Cy Pres fund
grant rules, the Village Manager must now be designated as the person to sign
all the documents. She noted that in all other respects, the application is
identical to the one that was authorized by Resolution R-4-2007.
There being no comments or questions, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-34-2007, authorizing a joint application
for the Cy Pres/Infrastructure Maintenance Fee grant with the Village of
Wilmette. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees,
Tucker, Poor, Ritchell, Eilers, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1521 and 1522. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1521 in the amount of $1,713,923.62 and Warrant
List No. 1522 in the amount of $731,757.68. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Tucker. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Poor, Eilers, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger.
b)   Bid Authority Analysis. Mr. McKee reported that staff had studied the idea
of adjusting the purchasing authority of the Village manager, department heads
and purchasing agent. He observed that if the current figure of $25,000 were
adjusted for inflation, it would be around $37,000. He said staff would be
comfortable with raising it to $30,000, which would eliminate about half of the
bid items that currently appear on Council agendas. He noted that no other
towns in the area have a level of purchasing authority higher than $30,000 for
their Village Managers.
President Woodbury indicated that Council had been inclined to raise the amount
based on inflation to give more discretion to the manager and department heads,
rather than to lighten the Council’s agendas.
Manager Williams explained that, under the procedure in place when he came to
the Village in 1992, very few bid items were reviewed by the Council unless
they were extraordinary. He said he changed that policy because he prefers the
sunshine of accountability, and the $25,000 threshold has worked very well. He
added that the auditors like the Village’s process and would not recommend a
$40,000 threshold. He agreed with their recommendation, indicating that he
would advise keeping $25,000 or raising it to $30,000, but not going as high as
$40,000.
Trustee Poor commented that the two issues are the Manager’s threshold, and the
limit for department heads. He indicated that it seemed there would be some
benefit to raising the Manager’s limit to $30,000, and staff appears to be
comfortable with that change. He observed that raising the department heads’
limit could provide greater efficiencies for them, and he didn’t see a downside
to that, and suggested the possibility of using a consent agenda for some
purchase approvals.
Trustee Behles said he would prefer to err on the side of transparency over
efficiency, and agreed that using a consent agenda is something that should be
explored.
After a short discussion, Council directed staff to bring a resolution raising
department heads’ authority to $2,500.
c)   Quarterly Capital Budget Analysis. Manager Williams gave a review of the
status of capital projects as of July 31, 2007. He said most items are on
track, several large projects are temporarily in a holding pattern, and
feasibility studies are being completed before others are begun. He noted that
overall, 60% of budgeted projects are moving forward as planned.
d)   Cost of Underground Electric Services. Mr. Keys reported that at Council’s
request, the Water & Electric Department had reviewed the existing flat rate
charges for 200 and 400 amp underground electric service. After summarizing the
work scope for 2006-07 and applying knowledge of today’s higher prices, he
concluded that the Village is not recovering all of its costs for providing 200
and 400 amp service. He recommended a mid-year correction in the flat rates to
even things out somewhat.
At the request of Manager Williams, Mr. Keys explained how the average cost is
calculated to arrive at the flat rate charge, noting that otherwise the charges
could vary widely, because some properties are closer to existing
infrastructure than others.
Following a brief discussion, the Council expressed agreement that the policy
of using the average as a flat rate provides predictability for homeowners and
has cut down on the number of complaints from residents. Council directed staff
to bring a resolution for the adjusted rate to a future meeting.
e)   Payment Authorization – Hibbard Rd. Water Line Project. Mr. Keys reported
that the Hibbard Road Water Main project is 99% complete and that the Village’s
contractor, Lenny Hoffman, has submitted his final invoice for work performed
to complete the installation of water main, associated valves and the pumping
plant. He noted that a change order is being requested to the total project
scope to allow for higher materials cost and a change in how the connection was
eventually made, which increased the amount paid to Hoffman by $39,818.80.
Manager Williams commented that the Hibbard Road water main project is the most
important improvement the Village has completed in the last ten years, and that
it will pay big benefits when the Village eventually has to upgrade its water
plant.
Trustee Behles confirmed with Mr. Keys that staff had carefully analyzed the
cost increase.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve Change Order #2 to
increase the cost of the Hibbard Road water main project from $1,392,493.63 to
$1,425,064.44.00. By voice vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize a payment of
$52,697.70 to Lenny Hoffman for the balance of the work in their contract. By
voice vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the Plan Commission would be
discussing the BCDC’s recommendations for the overlay district at its next
meeting, and that in September it will conduct focus group interviews for
affordable housing.
Trustee Behles reported that the Streetscape Committee’s report is in final
revisions and will be forwarded to the Council for its October Study Session.
He also reported that a review meeting at Metra will determine their timeframe
and funding for the rehab of the train station, noting that the elevator
replacement will be included in this project.
c)   Village Manager. Manager Williams reported that several pockets of
mosquitoes with West Nile Virus had been found in Winnetka, and that the NSMAD
would be spraying on Wednesday night. He noted that the Village’s drainage
system had handled the recent rains well, and gave credit to the Village’s
ongoing efforts to improve and maintain the system. He also reported on the
Cool Cities application, noting that information from the Metropolitan Mayors
Conference outlines various strategies. He recommended starting on the process
right away to keep momentum going, rather than waiting for an environmental
committee to be formed.
d)   Village Attorney. None.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury announced that Susan Connor will be moving out of Winnetka
and has submitted her resignation from the Board of Fire and Police
Commissioners, effective September 15th. He then suggested the appointment of
Mark Crane to the Board of Fire and Police Commissioners, effective September
15th. Trustee Ritchell moved to appoint Mr. Crane, seconded by Trustee Behles.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Personnel Matters and Pending and Probable Litigation pursuant to
Sections 2(c)1 and 2(c)11, respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Tucker seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Berger. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at
8:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:33 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Ritchell, Poor, Behles, Eilers, and Tucker. Absent: Trustee
Berger. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger. The meeting
adjourned at 9:34 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
September 4, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 18, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 4, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
King Poor, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys,
and approximately 14 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that the agenda had been amended to add item 6(e),
Resolution R-37-2007, Commending Staff for Outstanding Performance in the
Aftermath of the August 23rd Storm.
4)   Quorum.
a)   September 11, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   September 18, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except President Woodbury, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   August 14, Study Session. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell,
moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   August 21, 2007, Regular Meeting. Attorney Janega reviewed several minor
corrections to the minutes, and Trustees Behles and Berger suggested minor
changes as well. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve
the minutes as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2007 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to the
Special Events Provisions – Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that Ordinance
MC-12-2007 had been amended slightly since it was introduced at the July 17th
meeting. She indicated that the main changes appear in Sections 5.66.040 and
5.66.50, where subsections were relocated so that all of the procedures for
processing permit applications appear in Section 5.66.040, and all fee and
charge provisions are consolidated in Section 5.66.050. She noted that the
remainder of the amendments were in the draft when it was first introduced.
Attorney Janega called the Council’s attention to the deletion of the alternate
language that had been added to Section 5.66.020 in the August 21st draft and
would have expanded the scope of the exclusions. That language was deleted
following comments made at the last Council meeting.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend Ordinance MC-12-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-12-2007
as amended, amending the Special Events provisions of Chapter 5.66 of the
Village Code. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Poor, Eilers, Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-13-2007 – Zoning Variations – 800 Tower Road –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the zoning variances being requested for
the Subject Property in order to construct a detached garage, reconstruct the
driveway, and add a front walk. He indicated that the matter had been tabled at
the last meeting to give the applicant a chance to appear before the Council.
Susan Connor, attorney for the applicant, said the original proposal has been
reduced, and remarked that she did not think the petitioner was asking for more
than was reasonable. She noted that safe ingress and egress from the Subject
Property continues to be a concern.
Trustee Tucker indicated that while she was sympathetic to the petitioner, she
was having difficulty finding hardships that run with the land, and she was
concerned about the amount of impermeable surface. She asked if the new
driveway and garage will lessen the safety concerns.
Ms. Connor expressed uncertainty that the safety issues are addressed with the
driveway and garage proposal. As for hardship that runs with the land, she said
denying the petitioner a walkway to the doors of the home is unreasonable, and
that it could be said that not having a garage is a hardship. She noted that
the neighbors are enthusiastically in favor of the proposal because the lack of
a facility to store material things contributes to the unsightly nature of the
property at present.
Trustee Behles agreed that some of the arguments had merit, but expressed
concerns about the amount of impermeable surface being added, noting that brick
pavers could have been used in place of asphalt for the driveway. He observed
that the property has an existing brick patio that is not shown on the site
plans. Mr. D’Onofrio confirmed that the patio is included in the impermeable
surface calculations.
The Council discussed with Ms. Connor the question of reasonable return without
the garage, as well as the issues of impermeable surface and roofed lot
coverage. Overall, the Trustees were bothered by the extent of the impermeable
surface variation being requested and felt that it could be reduced still
further. Trustees Tucker and Berger were also concerned with the size of the
roofed lot coverage variation.
Trustee Tucker indicated that she was not comfortable with negotiating the
application in the forum of a Council meeting, and that the applicant’s best
effort should have been prepared before the current discussion was to take
place.
President Woodbury said he agreed with Trustee Tucker that a Council meeting
was the wrong venue for negotiations to take place.
Ms. Connor acknowledged that it is the responsibility of the applicant to
address the concerns of the Council, and agreed to take the matter off the
table at present, and to bring an amended application to the September 18th
Council meeting.
c)   Ordinance M-14-2007 – Disposing of Surplus Equipment – Adoption. Manager
Williams reviewed the subject Ordinance, which was introduced at the August
21st Council meeting. There being no further comments or questions, Trustee
Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance M-14-2007,
authorizing the Village to dispose of 10 equipment items that are past their
useful life. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-35-2007 – Electric Rates – Introduction. Water & Electric
Director Brian Keys reported that the Village’s flat rate charge for the
installation of underground electric service had been re-examined in light of
increased material costs over the prior fiscal year. He indicated that staff
estimated the impact of increased costs and calculated the increase that is
necessary for the Village to recover its expenditures in installing single-
phase underground service.
In response to questions from the Council, Mr. Keys indicated that the increase
should cover the Village’s costs for the rest of the calendar year. He said the
issue would be revisited again, as costs are expected to keep increasing in the
future.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Resolution R-35-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Resolution No. R-37-2007 – Commending Staff for Outstanding Performance in
the Aftermath of the August 23rd Storm – Adoption. As noted previously, this
item was added to the agenda. President Woodbury asked Mr. D’Onofrio, Mr.
Saunders and Mr. Keys to give brief reports about what had been learned in the
aftermath of the storm.
Mr. D’Onofrio said he had learned just how valuable Village staff is, and that
the entire emergency operation was a team effort from top to bottom. He noted
that Code Red and Reverse 911 were projects that had proved their worth and
enhanced communications greatly, adding that the coordination and cooperation
of departments and staff was impressive.
Mr. Saunders announced on behalf of the 36 employees in the Public Works
Department and the Village’s tree service people, that they were proud to serve
the Village and were grateful for the opportunity to help residents return to
normalcy.
Mr. Keys indicated that as a relative newcomer to the Village, it was a
pleasure to see the teamwork that Manager Williams has assembled. He praised
the Village linemen’s dedication and also acknowledged the aid that came from
Naperville, Rantoul, Princeton and Springfield.
Manager Williams said the decision of Mr. D’Onofrio to call the Emergency
Operations Center into action almost instantly and Mr. Keys’ calling almost
immediately for help with the power lines were key decisions that paid off very
well. He observed that initially the Police and Fire Departments were heavily
involved, but that it soon became an electrical and cleanup event, and that
Messrs. D’Onofrio, Saunders and Keys were instrumental during the whole
process.
President Woodbury then read the resolution on behalf of the Council.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-37-2007,
commending staff for outstanding performance in the aftermath of the August
23rd storm. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
a)   A group of residents from Trapp Lane related problems surrounding the
construction of new homes, and asked for the Village’s assistance in dealing
with parking, drainage and wear and tear on their private street.
Dr. Ernie Weis, President of the Trapp Lane Association, gave a history of the
development of Trapp Lane and read a statement into the record that had been
distributed to the Council prior to the meeting.
Mr. Tom Barron, 1284 Trapp Lane, distributed his statement to the Council and
read it into the record.
Manager Williams read a letter into the record from Mr. Alfini, 1309 Trapp,
which was signed by approximately one dozen residents.
President Woodbury recalled that he was on the Council when the discussion
about drainage on Sunview, Trapp and Westmoor took place. He stated that the
Council refrained from acting on recommendations in a 2002 engineering study
because of the practical disruption to property owners, and noted that the
Village has since put stronger enforcement provisions in place to ensure
adequate supervision of construction work.
President Woodbury said the second issue past Councils have wrestled with is
that Trapp Lane, like a number of other Village streets, is a private road. He
observed that the private road associations exist informally and not always
strictly legally, which makes things even more difficult.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to give a short drainage analysis.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the Village’s stormwater permit review process,
explaining that all plans are reviewed by engineering staff and an updated plan
is also reviewed for compliance at the end of construction before a Certificate
of Occupancy is issued.
Mr. Saunders reported that staff has been researching Special Service Areas
(SSA’s) to determine if they can be used to finance private street
infrastructure repairs. If the SSA’s can be established for private roads and
sewers, then the Village could pay an engineer to implement the Stanley
recommendations and the cost would be added to the tax bills of the Trapp Lane
residents.
In reply to a question about where the runoff goes during the construction
phase, Mr. Saunders acknowledged that there are conditions where the sequencing
of the construction disrupts the lot before the stormwater infrastructure is
put in place. He said some tweaking of the process could be done, but that
there are limitations as to how effective that will be. He added that although
the foundation is put in before the storm retention is in place, most of the
time the connection to the storm sewer isn’t yet in place either.
Mr. D’Onofrio observed that earlier in the day he got a complaint about
contractors from Trapp parking on a neighboring street, and said this is a
typical scenario when enforcement is increased in a construction zone. He noted
there is an inspector who patrols the Village for construction violations, but
that because Trapp is a private road, only the Community Development Department
can enforce the rules there.
Mr. D’Onofrio described Trapp Lane as very narrow, only 17 feet wide, and noted
that when a nine-foot delivery truck is making deliveries, it will cause
problems. He said he couldn’t come up with a better way for deliveries to be
made, adding that more conditions placed on contractors draws out the duration
of the disruption.
Manager Williams noted that the Village has invested a substantial amount on
drainage in that area in the last 10 years, and that previous Councils have
declined public investment in private streets. He added he would press
consideration of the SSA as a priority. He suggested meeting with residents of
Trapp Lane over the next few months to formulate a process, noting that the
Village’s engineers currently have a full workload.
Mr. Greg Hartemayer, 1372 Trapp, asked for a timeframe when residents would get
the information on the SSA, and when the sequencing of the construction process
would be reviewed. He also asked if the Village would be able to do anything
further to discourage multiple construction projects on Trapp Lane, and if the
road repairs could be done retroactively.
President Woodbury suggested that residents meet with Village staff about these
questions, as Council meetings are not the proper forum for negotiating issues.
b)   Susan Connor, 1417 Tower Road, and outgoing member of the Board of Fire
and Police Commissioners, said it had been an honor and a privilege to serve on
the BFPC. She observed that the Police and Fire Chiefs are extraordinary
people, the hiring process is extensive, and suggested that somebody with a
good background in human resources or employment law is a plus for membership
in the Commission.
President Woodbury responded that he had the privilege of working with Ms.
Connor on the Zoning Board, and that the Village was fortunate to have her
working on the BFPC. He thanked Ms. Connor for her service and noted that
serving on the BFPC is a key position, one that is little recognized.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1523 and 1524. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1523 in the amount of $217,924.32 and Warrant List No. 1524 in the amount of
$461,284.35. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Poor, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid: Concrete Restoration at Water Plant. Mr. Keys reviewed the bids
received by the Water & Electric Department for Phase IV of the concrete
restoration at the water treatment plant, noting this is the fourth year this
type of budgeted work is being done, and that the job is in its final phase. He
recommended that the project be awarded to the second lowest bidder Schaefeges
Brothers, Inc., as the lowest bidder had no prior experience or references for
concrete restoration work.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to award phase IV of the
concrete restoration project at the water plant to Schaefges Brothers Inc., in
an amount not to exceed $75,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Poor, and Eilers.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. President Woodbury reported that the Post Office
Committee would give their report to the Council at its September 11th Study
Session, and invited public to attend and share their comments.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Poor reported that the Historical Society would be
marking their 75th anniversary on September 15, and that all are welcome to
attend the celebrations. He said more information was available at 847-501-
6025.
Trustee Behles reminded residents about the Willow Road Enhancement meeting on
September 19th at the North Shore Senior Center at 7:00 p.m. He reported that
at a recent METRA meeting, things were going well between the Village and the
train companies.
Trustee Tucker reported that the Chamber of Commerce Autumn Art Walk starts on
October 11. She noted that details are in the Winnetka Report and invited the
community to turn out and support local artists and the Chamber.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that two special meetings are scheduled
on the fourth Tuesday in October (Oct. 23rd) at 7:30 p.m., and the first
Saturday in November (Nov. 3rd) at 9:30 a.m.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Personnel Matters, Pending and Probable Litigation , and Possible
Purchase of Land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1), 2(c)(11), and 2(c)(5)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:06 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:45 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 10:46 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
September 11, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: November 6, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chamber on Tuesday,
September 11, 2007 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Herb Ritchell arrived at 8:30 p.m. Also in attendance: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Mike D’Onofrio and approximately 50 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance to the Flag.
President Woodbury asked for a moment of silence after the Pledge of Allegiance
to remember those killed in the September 11, 2001, terrorist attacks.
3)   Overview of Post Office Redevelopment – Michael D’Onofrio, Community
Development Director.
President Woodbury explained that the format and purpose of the meeting was to
allow Council to hear from the staff, the Post Office Committee and the public
about the redevelopment of the Post Office building site. He then listed a
number of decisions the Council must make that may relate to the Post Office
redevelopment, such as retail overlay district amendments, parking deck
options, streetscape design, affordable housing and the long-deferred
improvements to Village Hall.
Mr. D’Onofrio gave a history of the Post Office site, reviewing its past uses
beginning with the Horace Mann School in 1899 and describing the planning
efforts, including the creation of the ad hoc Committee, to study the possible
redevelopment of the site when the current Post Office lease expires in April,
2009. He noted public comments made when the 2020 Comprehensive Plan was being
developed in 1999, which indicated that there was widespread consensus that the
redevelopment of the Post Office site presents the Village with an opportunity
to establish a core aesthetic and social gathering place.
Mr. D’Onofrio shared his thoughts for a successful redevelopment based on his
expertise and previous experience in the development of downtown areas and made
the following points: 1) it will require public and private partnership
involvement; 2) the municipality must be the driving force and leader in the
development; 3) the more clearly the municipality defines the project, the more
smoothly it will go; 4) the municipality must be committed to providing
staffing and financial resources; 5) there must be decisiveness and
cohesiveness between all municipal staff and elected officials; 6) the services
of experts and consultants must be obtained to help with a project of this size
to ensure that taxpayers are getting a fair deal on the project; and, 7)the
municipality must have patience when unexpected things happen. He also
commented that when municipalities undertake this kind of development, it may
serve as a catalyst for other development in the community because it shows
that the community is committed to making improvements.
In Mr. D’Onofrio’s opinion, the site has served as an activity point for the
community and its best use would be a mix of retail, residential, parking and
some type of public use with attention to design and architectural
significance. He suggested extending Moffatt Mall to create a public place with
a purpose, linking Dwyer Park to Moffatt Mall so that it is integrated with the
West Elm Business District, and creating 4 corners of retail, with an amenity
at the northwest corner of Elm and Chestnut that draws people there. He said it
was also very important that this site provide not only parking for new
development but also to replace the 183 parking spaces that are currently
there.
Julian D’Esposito, chair of the Post Office Committee, described the aggressive
effort the Committee had made to involve the public throughout the process with
the hope that there would be a public consensus about the use of the site and
whether private or public tax dollars were to be used in this project. Mr.
D’Esposito explained that the Committee attempted to provide opportunities to
work with the public to develop and present their ideas at study sessions,
focus groups, and by establishing e-mail boxes to receive comments. He noted
that the Committee’s report was circulated widely and placed on the Village
website.
Mr. D’Esposito continued: After two plus years were spent, the Committee
determined that there was widespread public support for two equally important
goals: 1) to transform Elm Street into a magnet for people to gather year-
round, day and evening; and 2) to have the project fit into a larger Village
effort to spur investment in a larger West Elm District and to involve both
public and private interests. He stressed that the redevelopment must be more
than an interesting project, it must also become a central point for commercial
and leisure activities with mixed uses that will encourage others to improve
their properties. The Committee’s recommendation covers three options, mixed
retail/residential, Library, and open space, with the mixed use option the best
use. He described the Library option as an interesting use and the open space
option as being the last best use.
Mr. D’Esposito explained that a mixed use would allow 14,000 square feet of
retail space generating pedestrian traffic, 25-30 residential units on the 2nd
and 3rd floors and open space for a gathering place that incorporates Dwyer
Park. The Committee also realized that the site is a relatively small parcel
and the large objectives that have been set could create some tensions as the
Council considers redevelopment. There was some concern among the Committee
that there is no demand for retail, but the Committee felt a study of this
would have been premature, vacancy rates in West Elm are low and 14,000 square
feet is not a big retail space. The Committee suggested that the retail uses
should be in broad categories that serve a number of people rather than small
specialty stores. The Committee also suggested that it is important that there
be four corners of retail to connect retail businesses with the area north of
Elm Street. The Committee hopes the development will bring people looking for
smaller footprint housing to the area and that it may also provide an area for
affordable housing. He noted that the Plan Commission is studying the
affordable housing aspect and will report to the Council.
Mr. D’Esposito reported that the Committee had initially solicited other public
bodies to see if there was a need for any other civic space and at the time,
the Library did not express an interest in the site. However, about six months
ago, the Library District contacted the Committee about analyzing whether or
not relocating the Library would be financially feasible and publicly
supported. Mr. D’Esposito said that the Committee thought there was enough
interest in this proposal that it should be explored, but that it was
complicated in that it would need to be combined with other uses to meet the
goal of creating activity, it would have to be discussed intergovermentally and
it does not serve only Winnetka residents. Mr. D’Esposito also noted that the
Post Office lease expires in two years and the Library would have to be
prepared to break ground at that time.
Trustee Ritchell arrived at 8:30 p.m.
Mr. D’Esposito said that the Committee also spent about 6 months considering a
proposal made by a group of residents who wanted to create open space on the
site that would extend to Dwyer Park, with a single building approximately one-
half to two-thirds of the size of the current Post Office to serve a year-round
activity. The Committee felt this option would be the least likely to meet the
goals of the Village.
Mr. D’Esposito reported that the Committee had discussed design principles and
felt that it is essential to have four corners of retail at Elm and Chestnut,
that Moffatt Mall extend to Dwyer Park, and that the scale and appearance of
the project must respect adjacent properties, realizing that the street
frontages are quite different: residential, commercial, and the park. He said
it is conceivable that what the site looks like will be more important than
what it is and the design and materials for the project are highly important.
He said the buildings across the street have an interesting façade and detail
with variations in height and building materials. The Committee felt the
development should also have multiple buildings with variations in height and
scale, so they look like they were built at different times, and there should
be a gathering place that is pedestrian friendly. Mr. D’Esposito said the Dwyer
Park open space advocates opened the Committee’s eyes as to the importance of
considering Dwyer Park and he would like to move beyond the “leave it alone”
approach and work together to incorporate Dwyer Park into what happens on the
Village property. Leisure activities conducted in a limited amount of open
space can meet the gathering objective and the Committee felt that the
children’s playground should be preserved and expanded in some fashion. Mr.
D’Esposito said the project would be complex and would require a high level of
interaction between elected officials of the Park District and the Village to
achieve the objectives.
Mr. D’Esposito then addressed the need for parking to replace the existing 183
parking spaces and to support the new project, and said that the Committee felt
the site could accommodate the required parking and that, although expensive,
underground parking would work if directed to residents and employees with
surface parking designated for customers of the retail spaces.
Mr. D’Esposito concluded his comments by thanking former Village President Duhl
for his appointment to the Committee and for the opportunity to serve the
Village. He also thanked members of the public who attended the meetings and
for the diversity of their views.
Post Office Committee member Nan Greenough said that she had observed the five-
block downtown area in a small town in Michigan that has four corners of retail
and found it acts as a microcosm for people. She noted that, unlike a strip
mall where people come and go quickly, people gathered in the park, at the
coffee shop, the ice cream shop, the restaurant and the grocery. When community
events are held, the park is full of people. Mrs. Greenough expressed her
belief that people congregate where there is food and that the Post Office site
seems to be best served by something that will support the existing retail
businesses and residences in the location.
Post Office Committee member Pete Henderson stressed that the key to the
success of the project is that it must be a first class development with
architecture, landscaping and art that convey quality. He noted the importance
of the Library, the Park Board and the Village to work cooperatively, as well
as the West Elm property owners, the Chamber of Commerce and the Business
Community Development Commission, and said he was struck by the extraordinary
talents of the people in the Village, such as the restaurateurs. He noted that,
although the Committee had not reached a conclusion on affordable housing,
every member spoke in favor of it and since this is government land, there may
be fewer obstacles to overcome for providing affordable housing there.
The Trustees thanked Mr. D’Esposito and the Committee for their work.
Trustee Eilers commented that the Council had been invited to the Committee’s
meetings and so are acquainted with what has been said. He further commented
that West Elm could become a magnet and compared the size of a 14,000 square
foot building to that of Trader Joe’s without suggesting that as a use, since
it would not answer the other goals of the site.
In response to a question from Trustee Poor regarding the importance of four
corners of retail, Mr. D’Esposito answered that by having retail connected,
people will go from store to store without being disconnected by a parking lot,
and that having something going on at each corner will attract people and
generate as much activity as possible. Further, if the intersection is framed
with structures, a gathering spot can be created where people can participate
in leisure activities.
In response to questions from Trustee Tucker, Mr. D’Esposito stated that the
Committee had discussed open space at the corner of Oak and Chestnut and also
building to the property line, which would hold a sense of place, but decided
that there was no point in determining which was better because it is a design
issue to be considered further along in the process. He added that the
Committee avoided getting into such specifics of project design as building
height, but felt that the site should be a dense area for development and
should echo the varied roof lines of the existing street frontages. In his
opinion, the least desirable design would contain a uniform roof line, but that
this would be a subject of negotiation between the Village and the developer.
Mr. D’Esposito also clarified a comment he had made regarding the physical
challenge of integrating Dwyer Park with the development of the Post Office
site by agreeing with Trustee Behles that Dwyer Park has certain grade
differentials to overcome in order to connect the park with the new
development. He suggested using Dwyer Court as the access to underground
parking, but that there is very little clear entranceway to the park from the
back of the Post Office.
Trustee Berger asked Mr. D’Esposito’s opinion about whether the Committee’s
recommendation to improve the West Elm district by encouraging business should
be revisited, in light of the number of people who have requested open space
and a lower density building and also if he thought the Library would meet the
objectives.
Mr. D’Esposito replied that the Committee believes the site should be seen as
part of the business district rather than open space and used as a buffer for
residential areas. He felt that Winnetka has fallen behind other communities in
regenerating its business districts and not to do that would be missing an
opportunity. Mr. D’Esposito said that whether the Library would meet the
objectives would depend on how it is used, the hours, and what kinds of
activities there are to attract people.
In response to a question from Trustee Ritchell, Mr. D’Esposito stated that the
concept of four corners of retail is an urban planning notion. He noted that
the Bennett Plan is a 19th century classic design using Moffatt Mall as a
passageway between public uses with retail on street frontages. He said that it
is most desirable to have a focal point for the development, whether it is
Chestnut or Moffatt Mall, and a place where people can congregate or shop.
Trustee Poor expressed concern that the Committee’s report rejects open space
and asked why open space can’t play a role in opening arms to Dwyer Park and
contribute to attractiveness and a place to congregate.
Mr. D’Esposito explained that the term, “open space,” was used by the Committee
to define the alternative for people who didn’t want development to occur. The
Committee discussed proposals #4 and #10 which had a majority of the site
designated as open space. He stressed that the various designs for development
also incorporate open space as an essential element and the Committee does not
support either a “wall to wall” development or a large open space that depends
on community public events for its use. He added that parking requirements must
also be satisfied.
President Woodbury asked for comments from the audience and stated that
questions would be answered after everyone was given an opportunity to speak.
Betty Skalski, 611 Ash, a representative of the Chamber of Commerce Board and
business owner in the Village, stated that the Chamber strongly supports
development that creates a balance of public, private, business and green space
to make Winnetka a vibrant place to do business and shop with viable,
convenient surface parking that is crucial to businesses.
Bill Drakoff, 852 Oak, asked about holding the corner at Oak and Chestnut and
how far to the west development would be extended along Oak Street. He also
asked for an explanation of what is meant by respecting the single-family
structures and the south face of Dwyer Park.
Edith Howell, 277 Ridge Ave, advocated green space, saying that she feared a
plan that would remove two acres of open space and benefit only merchants. She
had collected signatures on a petition for open space and felt that persons who
have spoken for open space or a civic use have not been heard.
Suzanne Canfield, 956 Westmoor Road, President of the Garden Guild of Winnetka,
expressed that the Guild’s 126 members want to be actively involved in this
parcel and pointed out that the Guild had planted Dwyer Park, created the
butterfly garden there and had also landscaped the area around the Post Office,
which they believe it is the most important parcel in the Village. She said
that with the Post Office Committee’s recommendations, the Village will lose an
opportunity to connect the space with Village Hall as was contemplated in the
Bennett Plan and that development will block light and create darkness for the
existing park. Ms. Canfield suggested that if a public building such as a
Library were built, there would still be more open space than with the mixed
use development and that the Guild would support this civic use. She urged the
Council to reconsider condominium development and take an open land approach
and noted that the conservation of the land will enhance the Village.
Bob Horne, 340 Birch, a member of Business Community Development Commission and
a former Plan Commission member, believed that the Post Office Committee’s
recommendations are sound and that the business district needs the development.
He strongly urged that parking be a critical component and asked for a lower
density development, quality building materials and underground parking.
Becky Hurley, 1027 Spruce, said this is a rare opportunity for this site that
people have been thinking about since 1921. She supported the recommendation of
the Committee and believed that there should be a business district formulation
for the site. She added that open space is not a matter of square feet, but is
about function and a design that would make it possible to embrace Dwyer Park.
Alex MacDonald, 760 Cherry, questioned why the site needs four corners to bring
it to life, and stated that the Post Office is a handsome building that serves
the community. He did not support any building, and suggested that the Council
forget about Moffatt Mall and an idea from 100 years ago.
Debby Ross, 921 Tower Road, expressed disappointment in the Committee’s report,
saying she did not get a sense that the community wants such a dense
development, and that she favored a Library use with a restaurant and ice rink.
Joe Dooley, 849 Sheridan, said that as he reads the report, the Committee is
not against open space. He supported starting with green space with the ability
to further develop in the future and thought there should be space for a
restaurant, an ice cream shop, the Library and a small Post Office. He also
thought the Bennett Plan was good and that green all the way to Dwyer Park
would be beautiful. He asked the Council to look to future uses, instead of
looking at the immediate need of today and asked the Council to include the
green extension of Dwyer Park into the center of this development.
Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka Avenue, said she had caused the green space
movement, had attended all of the meetings and was appalled at the proposed
development of the site. She is opposed to any plan that proposes buildings on
all four streets and especially opposes a parking garage. She said she worked
on her own to create a plan for what she described as a flagship building with
parking that requires moving Dwyer Park. She said she was given access to the
Lakota Group, but could not get financial information, and came up with a
minimal plan that she believes is a compromise between development and green
space. She stated she has collected 250 signatures on a petition for that
concept and that, as she reads the notes taken at the focus group meetings, the
comments favored green space that would incorporate Dwyer Park. She said that
over the 30 months that people have gone to meetings, 750 people have said they
want more green space and less density. Mrs. Stevens said that she doesn’t
understand why the goal is to revitalize the business district because there
are vacant store fronts in all of the business districts. She believes that
every Winnetkan should benefit from this public land and that it should be kept
as open space or for a civic use and asked the Council to reconsider the
objectives.
Rob Abatoff, 730 Ardsley, said he attended meetings and heard broad
perspectives, but felt that the Committee’s report shows a clear lack of
reporting of the comments made at meetings. He said that what he heard is that
people want low density and that there is no need for more retail. He said he
is pro-development in the Village, but asked that the Council be realists in
what is going on in retail, adding that if millions of taxpayers’ money is
spent for retail development, it will take 50 years to recoup and the tax
payers will have to foot the bill. He suggested that the Council should work
with the Park District to come up with a central facility to save taxpayer
money.
Katherine Simone, 1087 Cherry, asked about postal services and whether the
Village is working with the Post Office to continue to provide services for the
residents.
Andy Bullock, President of the Chamber of Commerce, said the project represents
vitality and opportunity for the Village and is a chance to get a more balanced
use on the site and enhance the quality of life. He said that the Chamber
supports the Committee’s recommendation for a mixed use. He added that the
Chamber as an organization would also like to see adequate amounts of parking
provided and that the way this is done will be very important.
Cicely Michalak, 351 Ridge, Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce and a
member of the Business Community Development Commission, pointed out that
parking is critical to the success of the development. She said it would be
fabulous to have a consensus of all the people, but there are many different
ideas. She would like to see some sort of revenue producing element.
Jim Sayegh, of the Winnetka Commercial Property Owners Association, said that
all property owners have a stake in the community and that this site represents
Winnetka’s best opportunity to create an economic stimulus without diminishing
infrastructure. He would want the development to be Winnetka retail such as
Multiple Choices and The Book Stall, not the type of shopping that is on Willow
Road in Northbrook. He said the site is zoned for commerce and although it is
natural to want to preserve green space, it is not reflective of the site’s
context. Mr. Sayegh said that the Property Owners Association requests that the
site continue to perform essential functions for West Elm District with parking
and the presence of a traffic-generating use including foot traffic and agrees
with the recommendations of the Post Office Committee.
Terry Hutchens, 1170 Asbury, said she had learned of the Post Office issue when
she was asked to sign a petition, and did attend one Post Office Committee
meeting where Katie Stevens’ petition was discussed. She believes the
Committee’s recommendation seemed like a way for a business owner to get a new
building, and said she believes in open space.
Withrow Meeker, 338 White Oak Lane, spoke in favor of open space, but felt that
to put a development with a green strip down the center and four corners around
it is not open space. He would like the Council to consider the quality of the
development and favors low density and open space. He recalled the project to
put the railroad below grade, saying it was costly but was the right decision.
He suggested finding innovative people and using local resources for the
design.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, talked about a vibrant city center, such as has
been done in Highland Park, Evanston, and at the Glen. He said there may be a
silent majority of people who want a vibrant center, but who may not be voicing
their opinion. He said that given the economic conditions and climate we are
in, adopting a largely green plan may be best until the best alternative
presents itself. He said that the parking has to be preserved and where there
is parking, there must be continuity. He said there already is a Library and
felt it best to wait for the Caucus questionnaire results, but thought that the
community wanted to create something new that will bring in people and add
revenue.
Bill Powell, 693 Walden, commented that the Post Office block is publicly
owned, and it is for public use, not for private use or private subsidy. He
said “Winnetka” means beautiful place and should be kept that way. He added
that development will increase traffic congestion and supported enlarging Dwyer
Park instead.
There being no further comments from the audience, President Woodbury responded
to questions posed throughout the discussion. With regard to Mr. Drakoff’s
question about what the development would be along Oak St President Woodbury
stated that the Council does not know as yet what will happen along this
street. With regard to Ms. Simone’s question about working with the Post Office
President Woodbury stated that the Village and Post Office officials have held
meetings regarding services to the Village and it is clear that both parties
want a retail facility to remain, but that it is still to be determined whether
that will fit into a new development or at a different location. President
Woodbury reiterated that the Council’s purpose in tonight’s meeting is to
listen to everyone, and that the Council is not going to offer comments. He
said the Committee was appointed to help in the decision-making and the Council
will try to reach a consensus. He further stated that the Council will always
take comments from the public and is not bound by the process or by habit to
declare public meetings for their own sake. He said all of the Council’s
meetings are open and anyone can speak to the Council at any meeting. He added
that every Winnetkan is entitled to their opinion and although we will disagree
from time to time, the hallmark of our Village government is civility. He said
that no one’s integrity should be impugned for sharing their opinions and
President Woodbury asked that everyone remember that as we move forward our
views will be passionate because these are passionate issues. President
Woodbury referred to comments made about the highest and best use for the
property; he stated that we must also consider the lowest use, find a balance
and come to an agreement on what is the right use. President Woodbury announced
that there will be additional meetings on development of the Post Office site,
the first on Saturday, November 3, at 9:30 a.m. and another at the Council’s
January study session.
4)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Personnel Matters, Pending and Probable Litigation , and Possible
Purchase of Land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1), 2(c)(11), and 2(c)(5)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Tucker seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:14 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:15 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
September 18, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: October 2, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 18, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Eilers called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees King Poor, Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: President Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Michael D’Onofrio, Finance Director Ed McKee, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and
approximately 30 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Eilers led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   October 2, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 9, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 4, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6)   Public Hearing – Possible Leash Law.
President Pro Tem Eilers noted that the 2006 Caucus Survey indicated that 59%
of respondents favored a leash law in Winnetka. As a result, the Council is
investigating the adoption of an ordinance requiring that all dogs be
controlled on a leash while on public property. He then opened the public
hearing and invited Police Chief Joe DeLopez to give his report.
Chief DeLopez recapped the results of two surveys, one by the Winnetka Police
Department and one by the Northwest Municipal Conference, which disclose that
eight comparable communities all have leash laws, and two also ban dogs being
left tethered unattended in public places.
Chief DeLopez reported that Police Department records for 2004 through 2006
indicate there were 17 verified dog bite complaints and 132 verified stray dog
reports during that period.
In response to a question about how well electronic shock collars work, Chief
DeLopez replied that they can work if the dog is properly trained and the owner
is in close proximity, but that it is not a fail-safe method. He also observed
that retractable leashes can be a problem, as the dogs roam more freely and are
not as much under the owner’s control.
Regarding the tethering ban that was also brought up in the Caucus survey,
Chief DeLopez stated that most towns find that leaving a dog on a leash in
front of a business is not a problem. He noted animals left out for longer
durations or in bad weather are more of a rural problem than an urban one.
Chief DeLopez observed that the leash law will be no more difficult to enforce
than the Village’s existing law, and that no additional staff will be needed.
He noted that the current language is subjective, and the definition of “under
control” can be subject to interpretation.
President Pro Tem Eilers opened up the floor for audience members to make their
comments.
Thirteen residents in the audience voiced their opposition to a leash law: Bill
Pierce, 174 Woodland; Elaina Dreiske, 594 Elm; M. Jast, 567 Oak; Philip Haley,
485 Cherry; Lynn Jackson, 777 Rosewood; Sally Tomlinson, 1450 Asbury; Howard
and Sue Galler, 650 Sheridan; Keith Dipple, 1146 Cherry; Elizabeth Smith, 770
Rosewood; Mary Filice, 350 Linden; and Allen Welch, 890 Willow. Most commented
that the few irresponsible dog owners in the Village should be held accountable
for their dogs’ actions. Among their reasons for opposing a leash law: (a)
enacting a leash law is an overreaction to a small number of incidents; (b) a
leash law won’t solve the problem of runaway or stray dogs; (c) the Village
needs to provide space for dog owners to run their dogs off-leash safely; and
(d) most dog owners in the Village are responsible and try to anticipate
problems.
Six audience members spoke in support of a leash law: Craig Randall, 443
Chestnut; Linda Davis, 465 Sunset; Elise Covey, 1503 Edgewood Lane; Marcia
Baker, 476 Linden; Betty McNulty, 466 Linden; and Mollybelle Berish, 1274
Trapp. Most felt that safety, especially of children, should be the number one
concern. Other observations included: (a) the definition of “under control” is
too ambiguous; (b) dogs off-leash are intimidating to some people; (c) the
statistics on dog bites don’t include dogs biting other dogs; and (d) one
attack by an off-leash dog is one too many.
Other audience members who spoke were Sue Henderson, 555 Ash, who expressed
concern over current penalties, and Elisha Gray, 1125 Spruce, who submitted an
excerpt from a book about leash laws and urged the Council to consider the
purpose of the law and whether the proposed law would accomplish the desired
goals.
President Pro Tem Eilers closed the public hearing and thanked audience members
for coming forward. He indicated that the Council would discuss the matter and
either: (i) decide not to go forward with a leash law; (ii) instruct the
Village Attorney to draft an ordinance; or (iii) table the matter for more
discussion.
Trustee Poor commented that more problems are possibly being caused by dogs
running at large, which is a personal responsibility issue. He noted that the
vast majority of people he encounters on the sidewalks are walking their dogs
on leashes. He indicated that he was inclined to take a serious look at a leash
law because of the safety concerns involved, but that he could not support a
tether law. Finally, he remarked that he thought the Council should investigate
the option of taking dogs to the parks on a leash.
Trustee Berger said she thought the current law was too ambiguous and that
enacting a leash law would not cause an undue hardship. She indicated she would
be in favor of a leash law, but would be against a tethering law.
Trustee Behles observed that it only takes a handful of incidents to create
serious problems and that a few people being injured every year in dog attacks
is a significant issue. He said he would be in favor of discussing a leash law
but that a tethering ban would go too far.
Trustee Tucker acknowledged that there are very good arguments on both sides of
the issue, but the safety concerns were of primary importance. She said that
when weighing personal safety versus the burden of a leash, the weight comes
down on the side of the leash. She said she would support a leash law but not a
tethering ban, and suggested that any law that is enacted be reviewed in a year
or two to judge its effectiveness.
Trustee Ritchell said the big issue in his mind was the lack of definition of
what “under control” means. He noted that a clear majority of residents were
polled and said yes to a leash law, and that a clear majority of people do not
have dogs. He indicated that he would be in favor of a leash law, but would not
feel comfortable restricting the tethering of dogs.
President Pro Tem Eilers observed that because of the element of
unpredictability with dogs, he would be in favor of a leash law, but not in
favor of a tethering restriction. He summed up by noting that it seemed the
majority of Trustees were in favor of a leash ordinance, but pointed out that
the discussion doesn’t end if the Council directs the Village Attorney to draft
an ordinance for consideration.
Attorney Janega clarified with the Council that they wanted the ordinance
drafted as a leash law, and not amended to keep the same concept but with more
definition of “under control.”
Manager Williams observed that he was optimistic that conversations with the
Park District could provide a solution for the problem of the scarcity of
places for dogs to get exercise in the Village.
7)   Ronald Amen – Lauterbach and Amen – Village Auditor – Review 2006-07
Comprehensive Annual Financial Report.
The Village’s auditor, Ron Amen, gave an overview of the management letter
prepared for the Village for the fiscal year ended March 31, 2007. He pointed
out several small changes the Village could make to current internal controls,
including reviewing the deposits payable every few years, but noted that the
Village received an unqualified opinion, which is the highest level of opinion,
and the financial statements are a good quality.
Manager Williams thanked Mr. Amen for the timeliness and thoroughness of the
audit report.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-16-2007 –Special Use Permit – 906-08 Green Bay Road –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the owner of the Subject Property is
requesting a special use permit to allow for a non-retail office use within 73
feet of the street. He explained that the petitioners are requesting the
special use permit in order to redevelop the building for both retail and
office uses.
Mr. D’Onofrio said the special use application was originally submitted last
March. He observed that the plans depicted a proposed floor plan that would
maintain an area of retail tenant space measuring 50 feet from Green Bay Road,
and identified the remainder of the building to be used for unspecified non-
retail uses, and the Plan Commission and ZBA both recommended to deny the
application. He said a revised application was submitted in July that
identified a retail tenant which would occupy one of the two front retail
spaces.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Plan Commission expressed concern that a
particular non-retail tenant was not identified, and recommended denial of the
application. When the case appeared before the ZBA, the application had been
modified to identify “offices” as the non-retail special use, and the ZBA
recommended approval. Mr. D’Onofrio said although it is unusual not to have a
particular tenant in mind, staff is comfortable with their commitment to
leasing the space to an office use. He noted that a parking study was done that
concluded the effect on parking would be negligible, and that the Village
Engineer concurred with the study.
The Council discussed the unusual circumstances of the special use application
and clarified with Mr. D’Onofrio that approval of the special use would not
affect a change in the zoning. The Council then asked Attorney Janega to
comment on whether it was necessary for a particular use to be identified in
the application.
Attorney Janega observed that the Council can decide that a specific tenant
does not need to be identified as long as the use is specified. She noted that
the use runs with the land, regardless of who uses it, and said she did not
have a problem with the application being approved with a clearly defined use.
The applicant, Josh Braun, gave a brief presentation in which he stated that
the building has a natural break point at 73 feet from the street. He indicated
that the retail tenant which has been signed necessitated changing the
configuration of the building, and that the highest and best use of the space
involved devising a way to use the undesirable space at the rear of the
building.
Attorney Janega reviewed the draft ordinance, noting the factors that
distinguish this application from a request that the Council had previously
denied for anther property.
Trustee Behles recalled that the Council’s discussion of the BCDC’s
recommendations on the C-2 Overlay District included a reduction of the retail
depth requirement from 100 feet to 60 feet. He noted that the point will be a
non-issue if the Plan Commission eventually agrees with the BCDC’s
recommendation.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to introduce Ordinance M-16-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-15-2007 – Special Use & Variance – 910 Green Bay Road –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the owner of the Subject Property is
requesting a special use permit and variations in order to construct a two-
story addition to the Subject Property. Two special uses are being requested:
(1) a proposed 400 square-foot work/live unit on the first floor at 60 feet
from the street, whereas a retail use is required for the first 100 feet; and
(2) to keep the current at-grade parking for the proposed second story office
addition, whereas below-grade parking is required. The variations are being
requested to permit impermeable lot coverage that is 312 square feet over the
maximum allowed, and to provide no parking for the proposed work/live loft,
whereas 2.25 spaces are the requirement.
Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that the Plan Commission voted unanimously in favor of
recommending a special use for the at-grade parking, and voted to recommend
granting the special use for the work/live loft by a margin of six in favor and
three against. He said the ZBA voted unanimously in favor of recommending the
special use for at-grade parking and the variation for impermeable lot
coverage, and voted to recommend granting the special use for the work/live
loft and the variation for the residential parking by a margin of four in favor
and one against.
The applicant, Jim Sayegh, presented the Council with plans and elevations and
pointed out that only a half-story is actually being added to the existing
building. In response to questions from the Council, he said the north
elevation will be articulated so as to not block the air and light of the
building next door. He indicated that the work/live loft would be considered an
affordable housing unit.
Attorney Janega noted that the ordinance had a specific condition with regard
to pending Design Review approval. In response to questions from the Council,
Mr. D’Onofrio confirmed that a strictly residential dwelling unit is not a
permitted special use in the C-2 Overlay District.
In the Council’s discussion of various aspects of the redevelopment plan, most
commented that the work/live concept is an interesting alternative for the
space. The Trustees, with the exception of Trustee Ritchell, expressed approval
for all the special uses and variations. Trustee Ritchell said he did not want
to use the work/live loft as an example of affordable housing, and would not be
in favor of granting the parking variance for the work/live unit. He said he
would be in favor of approving the special use for at-grade parking and the
impermeable lot coverage variation.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to introduce Ordinance M-15-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried by a vote of five to one.
[NOTE: President Pro Tem Eilers passed over Item 8(c) to accommodate audience
members, and skipped to agenda Item 8(d), after which Item 11(a) was discussed.
The Council discussed the remaining items in order.]
c)   Ordinance MC-13-2007 – Commercial Property Maintenance Code –
Introduction: Mr. D’Onofrio reported that a study group consisting of members
from the BCDC and Chamber of Commerce studied the issue of a commercial
property maintenance code in 2006, and that the Council considered the proposal
at its June 19th meeting. Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that the proposal had the
support of the business community and the Chamber of Commerce.
Mr. Jim Sayegh, speaking as president of the Winnetka Commercial Property
Owners Association, said he had a problem with commercial properties being
singled out for the new property maintenance provisions and indicated that he
thought residential properties should also be included. He noted that
maintenance costs are deferred to tenants and asked the Council to table the
matter for the time being, until WCPOA members had time to review the ordinance
and give the Council feedback on it.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that WCPOA members were in attendance at every BCDC meeting
where the issue was discussed.
Trustee Behles indicated that the property maintenance code was discussed at
the last meeting of the BCDC, that businesses were in favor of it, and he
didn’t see a connection between applying the ordinance to businesses versus
residences.
Trustee Tucker agreed that the WCPOA had ample opportunity to weigh in on the
issue.
Trustee Poor noted that the discussions of the study group and the BCDC were
held openly, and that there was ample deliberation and notice.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-13-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried three to two.
d)   Resolution No. R-32-2007 – Walden Condominium Subdivision – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reported that the proposed condominium division was approved in
1992, but was never completed. She noted that the current proposal is unchanged
except that the number of parking spaces has been reduced by one space.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-32-
2007 approving the Walden Place Condominium Subdivision of 803-807 Walden Road.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
e)   Resolution No. R-35-2007 – Electric Rates – Adoption. Attorney Janega
reported that the subject resolution, introduced at the September 4th meeting,
would increase charges for 200 and 400 amp single-phase underground electric
service. She noted that the increase is being proposed because material costs
have risen significantly in the past year.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-35-2007
amending the Electric Rate schedules to increase the charges for 200 amp and
400 amp underground electric services. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: President Woodbury.
9)   Public Comment and Questions.
10)   Old Business.
None.
11)   New Business.
a)   Policy Direction – Request for Two Zoning Variations – 719 Foxdale. Mr.
D’Onofrio reported that the applicants are requesting side yard setback and
roofed lot coverage variations in order to construct a two-car detached garage
at the rear of the property, and a porte-cochere where the existing one-car
garage is located. He said the ZBA voted unanimously to recommend denial of the
variations at its August 13th meeting.
Mr. Daniel Kielson, a neighbor of the applicant, said his concern is with the
disproportionate amount of cement that will be on the property as the result of
building a two-car garage. He noted that although they are currently planning a
gravel driveway, he expects it will be paved over once snow season comes, since
gravel is extremely hard to shovel and plow. He added that so much new
impermeable surface would add to storm runoff.
Trustee Poor agreed with the ZBA that there are no hardships, and said the
proposal is far too intense and creates runoff issues. He indicated that he
would be in favor of affirming the ZBA’s recommendation.
Trustee Berger said she agreed with Trustee Poor.
Trustee Behles said he supported the findings of the ZBA, and noted that the
side yard setback problem is self-created.
Trustee Tucker echoed the comments of the other Trustees and said she didn’t
see any reason to overturn the ZBA’s recommendation.
Trustee Ritchell agreed with the rest of the Council and with Trustee Behles
about the side yard issue.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to accept the ZBA’s
recommendation and to deny the variations request. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: President Woodbury.
b)   Warrant Lists 1525 and 1526. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1525 in the amount of $1,532,696.49 and Warrant List No. 1526 in the amount of
$345,438.70. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Poor, Eilers,
Berger, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
c)   Bid #07-015: Kiosk. Manager Williams reported that the Village took bids
for the Hubbard Woods kiosk and staff recommends awarding the bid to the lowest
bidder, even though it is over budget. He observed that the Chamber of Commerce
has offered to offset some of the cost with a $10,000 contribution.
The Trustees had questions about the placement and lighting of the kiosk.
Manager Williams suggested that due to the lateness of the hour, the item be
tabled for a more thorough discussion at another meeting.
12)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles reminded everyone that on Wednesday, September
19, there would be a meeting on the Willow Road Enhancement project at 7:00
p.m. at the North Shore Senior Center.
Trustee Ritchell announced that the ad hoc Historic and Residential
Preservation Committee would be making its report in the near future.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village is in negotiations
with cell providers about increasing the lease amounts; however, staff is
having difficulty implementing the Council’s direction of CPI +1%, and may go
to a fixed percentage. Regarding increasing the Department Heads’ purchasing
authority, he announced that Attorney Janega had written an opinion that a
Council resolution is not required, since the change is administrative.
Finally, he noted some upcoming Study Session topics: (i) Streetscape will be
discussed at the regular October Study Session; (ii) a special Study Session
will be held the 4th Tuesday in October to discuss Village Hall renovation and
the parking deck; (iii) another special Study Session will be held on the first
Saturday in November to discuss the Post Office Committee’s report; and (iv) at
the Study Session in November, Kevin McCanna from Speer Financial will give a
presentation on TIF’s.
13)   Appointments.
None.
Executive Session.
None. Meetings
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
The meeting adjourned at 12:05 a.m.
         
Recording Secretary
October 2, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 6, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 2, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Brian
Keys, and approximately 10 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   October 9, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 16, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 18, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger suggested a few minor
changes to the minutes. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
approve the minutes, as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-16-2007 – Special Use Permit – 906-08 Green Bay Road –
Adoption. The applicant, Josh Braun, asked the Council to table the matter, as
he is currently in negotiations with a prospective tenant for the non-retail
space.
President Woodbury agreed to table Ordinance M-16-2007 until a later date.
b)   Ordinance No. M-15-2007 – Special Use and Variance – 910 Green Bay Road –
Adoption. President Woodbury announced that the subject ordinance, which was
introduced at the September 18th meeting, would grant two special use permits
and two zoning variations in conjunction with the construction of a two-story
addition to the subject property.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments made following the Council’s
discussions at the time of introduction. She noted that additional findings
were inserted to make clear that the work/live loft is being approved in
furtherance of the Village’s Affordable Housing Plan, and summarized the
conditions to be met by the applicant.
Trustee Ritchell indicated that he did not wish to set a precedent as to what
affordable housing would look like in the Village.
Trustee Eilers asked if the applicant was comfortable with the amendments.
The applicant, Mr. Jim Sayegh, said he hadn’t intended to officially label the
work/live loft as an affordable housing unit, and said he preferred to think of
it in terms of adding to the diversity of housing stock in the Village. He
indicated that he preferred the ordinance to be approved without the
amendments, but would agree to the changes if that was the only way for Council
to grant the special use permit for the work/live loft. He then asked the
Council to consider granting him 36 months to commence construction of the
unit.
Attorney Janega explained the rationale for the amendments and recommended
against a 36-month timeframe.
Trustee Behles indicated that he would be comfortable eliminating the special
use for the work/live loft, and approving the remainder of the application, if
the applicant so desired.
Trustee Tucker said she trusted Attorney Janega’s recommendations as legally
sound and upholding current regulations, and indicated that she would like to
keep the amendments in place. She said that if the applicant could not work
within that framework, then the work/live loft should be taken off the table.
Trustee Poor said he would be in favor of approving the project without the
affordable housing component if that was what the applicant wanted.
Trustee Ritchell said he could approve the commercial development but not the
work/live loft.
Trustee Eilers said he trusted the Village Attorney’s recommendation that
without the conditions the Council would be setting a questionable precedent,
and said he could approve the amended ordinance.
Trustee Berger said it was her understanding that the work/live loft was to be
considered an affordable housing unit.
Mr. Sayegh said he would accept the ordinance as amended, and asked again for a
longer timeframe to start construction on the unit.
Manager Williams said special use permits have a timeframe of 12 months and
that a significant precedent would be set if the Council granted the applicant
36 months to commence construction.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to amend Ordinance M-15-2007,
as proposed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote the motion carried
unanimously.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-15-
2007, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: Trustee Ritchell.
Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-13-2007 – Commercial Property Maintenance Code –
Adoption. President Woodbury announced that the subject ordinance was
introduced at the September 18th meeting and would create a property
maintenance code for commercial properties in the Village. He asked what the
intent would be for mixed use buildings in the commercial district.
Mr. D’Onofrio observed that the BCDC originally wanted the code to apply to
residential and non-residential but that Caucus survey comments indicated that
residents were not in favor of a residential code. He said he did not recall a
lot of discussion about mixed use buildings.
Attorney Janega noted that the model code was amended to delete all references
to dwelling units, and the ordinance would need substantial modification to
cover mixed use buildings that include residential units.
The Council agreed to table the ordinance to amend it for mixed use buildings.
d)   Resolution No. R-36-2007 – Water Meter Fees – Introduction. Mr. Keys
reported that staff is proposing a change in the Water Service fee resolution
to eliminate 3-inch and larger meters from the existing rate resolution. He
observed that the current price structure does not enable the Village to
recover its cost for the larger meters, and the proposed change allows the
Water & Electric Department determine the actual cost as larger meters are
requested.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to
introduce Resolution R-36-2007, amending the water service fees. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Resolution No. R-39-2007 – Extension of Cell Site License Agreement (U.S.
Cellular) – Adoption. Mr. Keys reported that the Village’s license agreement
with U.S. Cellular to install cell antennas on the smokestack of the Water &
Electric Plant expired at the end of March, 2006, and had been extended for one
year, at which time Village staff requested a six-month extension to continue
negotiations with U.S. Cellular. He indicated that the process has taken longer
than anticipated and asked the Council to approve another lease extension,
noting that any new terms will be retroactive to April 1, 2007.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-39-
2007, granting an extension of the U.S. Cellular cell site license to April 1,
2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1527 and 1528. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commented on a number of the items and moved to approve Warrant List No. 1527
in the amount of $1,148,919.65 and Warrant List No. 1528 in the amount of
$1,200,106.25. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor,
and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell – Abstained
b)   Policy Direction: Zoning Variations – 717 Locust. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed
the application for variations from front yard setback and front yard lot
coverage. He reported that the variations are being requested in order to
construct a new residence that will encroach into the front yard setback, which
in this case was calculated by averaging the other front yard setbacks on the
block face, rather than using the 40-foot setback required for the R-3 zoning
district. He indicated that the existing home has a 43.82 foot front yard
setback, and the applicant is proposing to use the same setback for the new
construction, as opposed to the 70.33 feet required by averaging the block
face.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the point of using the average is to create a pattern
so that if all the houses on a particular block are set back further than the
zoning ordinance requires, a new house would line up for consistency on the
block face. He said in this case, when the calculations are applied, the house
wound up being pushed further back than the other houses on the street, because
a couple of houses with deeper lots skewed the average. He added that requiring
the 70-foot setback would, in fact, create the very thing the zoning ordinance
is trying to avoid, which is a non-uniform block face.
Mr. D’Onofrio observed that the Zoning Board of Appeals voted to deny
recommending the variations by a vote of five to two.
Mr. D’Onofrio discussed the unique aspects of this particular block of Locust
Street under the Zoning Ordinance definition of block face. While responding to
questions from the Manager and Council, he expressed his opinion that granting
the variance in this instance is appropriate because of the established setback
pattern on the east side of the street.
Mr. Elliot Wiczer, the applicant’s attorney, pointed out that the existing
residence sits a little further into the front yard setback than the proposed
new home will, adding that the new house will not max out on floor area ratio
or impermeable surface. He observed that the proposal will not change the
character of the neighborhood and that if the entire block of Locust between
Dinsmore and Starr were taken into account, the average front yard setback
would be 40.05 feet.
Mr. Patrick Kenneally, 730 Walden, said he objected to the variations because
the Village Code provides protection for unusual situations, and stressed that
the subject property is the smallest lot on the east side of Locust Street. He
argued that no particular hardship had been established, that there is no
evidence of no reasonable return if the variations are not granted, and that
granting the variation will not accomplish the goal of diversity of housing
stock in the Village.
Mr. Joe Migley, 720 Walden, also objected to the request and said he hoped the
Council would uphold the ZBA’s denial of a recommendation to grant the
variations.
Mr. Brian Eustis, the son of the subject property’s owner, asked the Council to
grant the request, saying the current home is past its useful life, the lot is
problematic, and that the house could not yield a reasonable return without the
requested variations.
President Woodbury reminded those present that the matter is clearly a zoning
issue and not a real estate issue, and that one way to look at the question
would be to determine if enforcement of the code accomplishes the Zoning
Ordinance’s stated objectives.
Attorney Janega observed that, while the ZBA is expected to deny recommending a
variance for new construction, a negative recommendation does not mean it is
improper for the Council to grant the variance. She said the Council’s
standards for granting a variance hinge upon whether there is a practical
difficulty or particular hardship. She pointed out that the block is unique in
that the east side consists of one very long stretch punctuated by several “T”
intersections, which create several different block faces. She agreed with Mr.
D’Onofrio that denying the variance would discontinue the established land use
patterns, and added that this is a classic case of when a zoning variance is
appropriate.
Trustee Poor said he had a problem with the application because the net effect
of the new construction would permit a building that is substantially larger
than the current home, which would affect the neighborhood. He observed that
variations for new construction should be held to a high level of scrutiny and
that he favored giving deference to the ZBA’s denial of the recommendation.
Trustee Berger agreed with Trustee Poor, saying that granting the variance
would alter the essential character of the neighborhood.
Trustee Eilers said he believed the Zoning Ordinance in this case results in
unintended consequences and is contrary to maintaining the rhythm of the block
face and indicated that he was in favor of granting the variations.
Trustee Behles agreed with Trustee Eilers and added that the proposed new
construction would be the same height as the house next door and would have a
detached garage, and is therefore not out of character with the neighborhood.
Trustee Tucker said she agreed with Trustees Eilers and Behles.
Trustee Ritchell said good points were made all around, that he did not have a
problem with the position of the current home on the property and would be in
favor of granting the variance.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to instruct the Village
attorney to draft an ordinance approving the variation request. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: Trustees Berger and Poor. Absent: None.
c)   Policy Direction: Request for Third Driveway Entrance at 630 Blackthorn
Road. Mr. Saunders reported that the owners of the subject property have
applied for a building permit to construct an addition to the existing
residence, which currently has two curb cuts to accommodate a circular driveway
in front of the house. He indicated that a part of the proposed project
includes removing the existing detached garage and constructing an attached
garage that would be accessed by a new driveway, which would require a third
curb cut for the property.
Mr. Saunders indicated that there was no reason from a public safety standpoint
to deny the request, as long as landscaping at the parkway is tamed to maintain
sight lines, adding that no significant trees will need to be removed.
After ascertaining with Mr. Saunders that no bad precedent would be set by
approving the request, the Council reached a consensus to approve the request.
President Woodbury directed the Village Attorney to draft the required
resolution.
d)   Bid # 07-011: Directional Boring Contract. Mr. Keys reported that, in June
2007, the Village awarded a contract to Directional Solutions for directional
boring and wire pulling, but that on September 26, 2007, Directional Solutions
requested that the Village release it from the contract, citing difficulties in
hiring “qualified linemen” to pull the cable.
Mr. Keys noted that the second lowest bidder, Groundhog Utility Construction,
has indicated that it is willing to perform the directional boring and wire
pulling work in accordance with their original bid. He said staff recommends
terminating the original contract, accepting Groundhog Utility’s bid as the
most cost effective approach, and having the original contractor reimburse the
Village for the increased costs.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to terminate the contract with Directional Solutions and to execute a
purchase order and contract for directional boring to Groundhog Utility
Construction in an amount not to exceed $672,000. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Bid # 07-105: Kiosk Bid #07-015 – Sign Kiosk Fabrication and Installation
– Hubbard Woods. Mr. Saunders reviewed the two bids received in response to a
request for bids for the fabrication and installation of the Hubbard Woods
kiosk at Green Bay and Tower Roads. He indicated that concerns about lighting
have been taken into account, and that the Chamber of Commerce will be
contributing an additional $10,000 to help fund the project.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the contract for the
Hubbard Woods’ sign kiosk fabrication and installation to Western Remac, Inc.
for a base bid amount of $47,352. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Bid #07-017: Commercial District Holiday Lighting. Mr. Saunders reviewed
the bid summaries for this year’s holiday lighting and recommended awarding the
bid for holiday lighting to Landscape Concept Management. He said that, due to
complaints about last year’s drape lighting, this year’s program provides for
wrapping the trees for a better look.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the contract for
holiday lighting to Landscape Concept Management in the amount of $33,295. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Discussion: Consent Agenda. President Woodbury removed this item from the
agenda, to be rescheduled at a future Study Session.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the ad hoc Residential Design
Committee is close to choosing a consultant, and also encouraged everyone to
attend the Plan Commission meetings, as they are studying affordable housing
and the C-2 overlay district.
Trustee Behles reported that meetings are still ongoing between the Village and
the railroads and said he hoped if all goes well, construction could start in
the spring of 2008.
Trustee Tucker invited all residents to participate in the Chamber of Commerce
Autumn Art Walk, which begins on October 11.
Trustee Berger reported on some items from a recent Northwest Municipal
Conference legislative committee meeting. She then noted that other communities
have ongoing problems with Commonwealth Edison in the aftermath of the storm
last August, and remarked that Winnetka is fortunate to have its own electric
utility.
c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that she had been working with an IML
Home Rule subcommittee on a Rights-Of-Way ordinance. She indicated that the
process is now complete and that there are now three model ordinances, and she
would be coming back to the Council with updates to the Village Code to provide
greater consistency with the new model.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that the recent sale of 11 pieces of
surplus equipment netted $47,700.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to
Sections 2(C)(3) and 2(C)(11), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:55
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:12 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 11:13 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
October 9, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: October 16, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 9, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders,
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately 5 persons in
the audience.
2)   Master Streetscape and Wayfinding Plan.
Scott Freres, of the Lakota Group, gave a presentation of the streetscape
workbook, which he said was the culmination of the work the Streetscape
Committee has done in the past year and a half.
Mr. Freres started his presentation by identifying and analyzing the heavy and
moderate pedestrian traffic areas in the Village, and also pinpointed the
places with heavy vehicular traffic. He then gave an overview of several
different levels of intensity for streetscape improvements and described some
of the materials that could be used. He outlined the ways in which the plans
could be phased into operation, and reviewed some options for signage. Finally,
he summarized the cost options for the different phases, and reviewed an
alternate Phase 1, which consisted of scaled-down improvements contained in
“model blocks” in each of the three business districts.
Manager Williams informed the Council that the Study Session in November would
feature Kevin McCanna of Speer Financial, who will discuss the different
financial options to pay for the streetscape improvements. He observed that
identifying a revenue stream is the best option to pay for each phase as it is
implemented.
Trustee Behles stressed that it is important to think of the streetscape
project as an infrastructure improvement, and as an investment that will pay
dividends over time.
Trustees Eilers and Berger observed that there are several other capital
projects on the horizon and they all need to have funding identified.
There was a discussion about sign proliferation and where this fit into the
streetscape vision. Public Works Director Steve Saunders noted that signs
proliferate because people in the community ask for them, and the Village tries
to be responsive to the extent possible. He indicated that they could be
consolidated and made more attractive during the course of the streetscape
renovations.
Trustee Behles commented that the Comprehensive Plan was taken into account by
the Streetscape Committee when developing the workbook, and that several of the
elements contained in the plan are things that the Comprehensive Plan
specifically encourages.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. Freres and the Streetscape Committee and
indicated that the matter would likely come before the Council again in
January, after the funding issues have been presented.
3)   Discussion: Consent Agenda.
Attorney Janega reported that using a consent agenda would allow the Council to
pass any two or more ordinances, orders, resolutions or motions by placing them
together on the agenda and casting a single vote for all. She reviewed the
procedural requirements and outlined the items that are eligible to be placed
on the consent agenda.
Attorney Janega noted that any Council member can request an item be removed
from the consent agenda, and the item would not be tabled, but would be treated
as a regular agenda item at that meeting.
Manager Williams said he didn’t think a consent agenda would save very much
time, since the agenda items that take the longest are the zoning cases and
listening to public comment. He indicated that he preferred the transparency
that comes with open discussion of each agenda item.
The Council discussed the pros and cons of using a consent agenda and other
strategies to shorten the meetings. The point was raised that a consent agenda
is typically used in larger cities that have many agenda items to consider. A
decision was made to request staff to streamline their presentations, as their
reports are included in the agenda packets.
4)   Manager’s Report.
Manager Williams announced that an analysis of Trapp Lane drainage had revealed
that the cost to make improvements could cost approximately $500,000. He
proposed that Village staff schedule a neighborhood meeting to discuss funding
options with the residents.
Mr. Saunders reported that the resurfacing project at the Indian Hill parking
lots was nearly complete, but unexpected problems had occurred in the New Trier
faculty parking lot and a change order of $75,000 would be necessary to
complete the project.
Executive Session.
None.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:50 p.m.
