Recording Secretary
October 16, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 6, 2007)
A record of a legally convened Regular Meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 16, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Community
Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately 11 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that approval of the September 11th and October
2nd Council minutes would be postponed to a later date, pending clarification
of some points, and he also said Item 6(i) on the agenda would be heard after
item 6(b). The Trustees approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   October 23, 2007, Special Study Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 3, 2007, Special Study Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend this Saturday morning meeting.
c)   November 6, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 11, 2007, Study Session. As noted previously, this item was
removed from the Agenda.
b)   October 2, 2007, Regular Meeting. As noted previously, this item was
removed from the Agenda.
c)   October 9, 2007, Study Session. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-17-2007 – Zoning Variations: 150 Fuller Lane –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum Building Size, Side
Yard Setback, and Conforming Uses of Nonconforming Buildings zoning variations
to permit the construction of a second floor addition. He noted that the
addition would be a vertical extension of the existing side wall, which is
nonconforming with respect to minimum side yard setback and total side yard
requirements. In addition, he pointed out that the existing GFA is
nonconforming and mentioned that a variance is required to accommodate the
proposed window in the nonconforming north side wall.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5 to 2 to
recommend approval of the variations.
After a brief discussion, the Trustees agreed that although the lot is very
small by Village standards, the request is a modest one, and does not affect
neighboring light and air.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce Ordinance M-17-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. M-18-2007 – Zoning Variations: 1091 Sheridan Road –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Front Yard Setback, Side
Yard Setback, Rear Yard Setback, and Conforming Uses of Nonconforming Buildings
zoning variations to permit the construction of two one-story additions. He
explained that the homeowners wish to enlarge the existing attached one-car
garage and add a guest bedroom/den. He pointed out that the existing residence
is nonconforming with respect to the required front, side, and rear yard
setbacks and noted that a variation is also required for the proposed windows
in an existing nonconforming wall. He also mentioned that the proposed total
GFA and roofed lot coverage will comply with the Zoning Ordinance.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 7 to 0 to
recommend approval of the variations.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
18-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. M-19-2007 – Zoning Variations: 717 Locust Rd. –
Introduction. The Council skipped ahead to Item 6(i), after which time they
returned to Item 6(c) and followed the rest of the agenda in order.
President Woodbury called for any additional comments or questions regarding
this request for Intensity of Use of Lot and Front Yard Setback variations to
permit the construction of a new single family residence. He noted that the
matter had been discussed at the October 2nd Council meeting, at which time the
Village Attorney was directed to prepare an ordinance granting the requested
variances.
Mr. Joe Migley, 720 Walden, appealed to the Council to deny the requested
variation, saying the proposed new home will overshadow his back yard.
During the ensuing discussion, several Trustees felt that the request was not
in harmony with the Zoning Ordinance because the light and air of neighboring
properties would be encroached upon by the proposal, which is almost at maximum
allowable limits for gross floor area and roofed lot coverage. Others
disagreed, saying the setback average is skewed by the inclusion of the through
lot on the block face, and noting that a conforming alternative could be much
more intrusive with regard to neighboring light and air.
Attorney Janega pointed out that protection of light and air are bulk issues,
and that the proposal complies with bulk requirements, and added that it was
possible for a conforming building to diverge from the light and air principles
of the Zoning Ordinance. She observed that the purpose of the average setback
is to prevent extreme setbacks, and in this particular instance, because of the
singularity of the block face and the through lot skewing the average, the
result is a setback which is contrary to the intent of the Zoning Ordinance.
After more discussion, a majority of the Council indicated that the strict
application of the Zoning Ordinance was a departure from the spirit of the
code, and would not affect the bulk potential of the lot.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
19-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Eilers,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: Trustees Berger and Poor. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-36-2007 – Water Meter Fees - Adoption. President Woodbury
called for any additional comments or questions regarding this resolution
amending the Village’s fees for the purchase and installation of larger-sized
water meters that was introduced at the October 2nd Council meeting.
There being none, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt
Resolution No. R-36-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Resolution No. R-40-2007 – Permitting Third Driveway Entrance at 630
Blackthorn Road - Adoption. President Woodbury called for any additional
comments or questions regarding this resolution that would authorize the
construction of a third driveway entrance at the subject property, noting that
the item had been thoroughly reviewed at the October 2nd Council meeting.
There being none, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt
Resolution No. R-40-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-41-2007 – IMUA Emergency Aid Agreement - Adoption. Mr.
Keys reviewed the resolution authorizing the execution of the Illinois
Municipal Utilities Association’s Emergency Aid Agreement, noting that the
agreement has been revised to strengthen indemnification language and clarify
the pay structure.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
41-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Resolution No. R-42-2007 – The Winngate Condominium: 718 Green Bay Road -
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed the resolution approving the plat and
Condominium Declaration for the subject property, indicating that the
subdivision requirements have been met.
Mr. Dick Busscher, the developer of the Subject Property, said the address is
720 Green Bay Road. Attorney Janega said she would correct the address in the
resolution.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-41-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Resolution No. R-44-2007 – Extension of Cell Site License Agreement
(Verizon, Nextel & Cingular) – Adoption. Mr. Keys reported that the Village’s
license agreements with Verizon, Nextel, and Cingular expire on October 31,
2007. He indicated that the Village is negotiating new terms with all of its
cell site licensees, but that the process has taken longer than anticipated and
asked the Council to approve a lease extension until January 15, 2008.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-44-
2007, granting an extension of the cell site licenses to January 15, 2008. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
i)   Ordinance No. M-13-2007 – Zoning Variations: 800 Tower Road –
Introduction. This item was discussed after Item 6(b). Trustee Tucker, seconded
by Trustee Behles, moved to remove this matter from the table. By voice vote,
the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. D’Onofrio briefly reviewed the history of this request to construct a
detached garage in the rear of the property, reconstruct the driveway and add a
front walk. He reported that in response to comments made at previous meetings,
revised plans were submitted by the applicant showing a reduction in the size
of the garage, and the use of brick pavers instead of concrete. These changes
result in the reduction of impermeable lot coverage and bring the overall lot
coverage into conformance with the Zoning Ordinance. He noted that the front
yard lot coverage and rear yard setbacks remain the same.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5 to 0 to
recommend approval of the variations.
The applicant’s attorney, Susan Connor, said the applicant has done all he can
to make the variations as small as possible and added that the neighbors are in
support of the plan.
Attorney Janega observed that the Council has enough grounds to either grant or
deny the request, and pointed out that the applicant had bought the property
very recently, which went to his detriment.
After a short discussion, the Trustees indicated that the modified request more
clearly resembles the spirit of the Zoning Ordinance.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance M-13-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried five to one.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1529 and 1530. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists,
commenting on a number of the items and responding to questions. He then moved
to approve Warrant List No. 1529 in the amount of $783,799.51 and Warrant List
No. 1530 in the amount of $693,967.33. The motion was seconded by Trustee
Ritchell. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   State Bid – Salt Purchase. Manager Williams reported that the Village
purchases rock salt through a joint purchasing program that permits
municipalities to combine their purchasing power with the State of Illinois to
obtain lower prices. This year’s low bid of $42.71 per ton was submitted by
North American Salt Company. He reported that based upon prior years’
experience, staff estimates that the Village needs to purchase 1,016 tons of
rock salt for the coming year.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved authorize the purchase of
rock salt through the State of Illinois Cooperative Purchasing Program at
$42.71/ton. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports.
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Berger mentioned several items that were discussed at
the last NWMC board meeting, and announced that residents should be receiving
their property tax bills shortly.
Trustee Eilers encouraged everyone in the viewing audience and his fellow
Council members to attend upcoming Plan Commission meetings, as they discuss
affordable housing and changes to the C-2 Overlay District, and he also noted
that the Residential Design Guidelines Committee is close to approving terms
with its consultant.
Trustee Behles reminded everyone that there would be a Willow Road Enhancement
meeting on Wednesday, October 17, and said there would be another meeting in
Winnetka in November.
c)   Village Manager. Manager Williams called the Council’s attention to a copy
of the letter from Josh Braun formally withdrawing his application of a Special
Use Permit for 906-908 Green Bay Road. He then announced that SWANCC has begun
a compact fluorescent bulb recycling program, and Winnetka is among the first
towns to sign up for it.
d)   Village Attorney. Attorney Janega announced that she would be part of a
panel that is presenting the updated ROTW ordinance at an upcoming Illinois
Municipal League meeting.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury called for a motion to reappoint Mark Kurensky to a second
full term on the Plan Commission. Trustee Tucker so moved, seconded by Trustee
Behles. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments, Possible Purchase of Land, and Pending and
Probable Litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(3), 2(c)(5), and 2(c)(11),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Berger seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 9:14 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:55 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Tucker, and Ritchell. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 9:56 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
October 23, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 4, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 23, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Public
Works Director Steve Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee and approximately 10
persons in the audience.
2)   Village Hall Building Modernization and Restoration Plan.
Mr. Saunders said the renovation study of Village Hall is now complete, and
introduced the consultants, Stephen Yas and Mike Auf der Heide, of Yas
Architecture. He said the study was commissioned to look at historical
renovation possibilities with regard to the Council Chambers and the Finance
Department, and make the building more ADA compliant, as well as to get
recommendations for replacing the HVAC system.
Mr. Yas reviewed the recommendations contained in his report, starting with the
plan for the first floor. He said the Finance Department reorganization would
create a more efficient and pleasant working environment, and would allow more
natural light into the space. He made several recommendations for the main
lobby, including restoring the original vestibule doors.
Mr. Yas then reviewed the recommendations for the building’s exterior,
including windows, masonry, and other exterior elements. He noted that the ivy
would need to be taken down in order to do the restoration on the stonework,
adding that the ivy has a deleterious effect on the masonry overall.
Mr. Auf der Heide said that the HVAC equipment is at the end of its life
expectancy, and reviewed the new layouts and cost summaries contained in the
report. He indicated that the radiator system now in place is a superior method
for heating the building, and recommended against changing to an all-air
system.
Mr. Yas reviewed the technology changes for the Council Chambers, which include
upgrading the sound, video capabilities and projection screen, and creating an
audio/visual room in an adjacent storeroom. He indicated that the work would be
done so as to make the A/V elements as unobtrusive as possible, keeping in mind
the historically sensitive nature of the Chambers.
Mr. Yas said the Village could gain some points towards becoming a LEED-
certified building, and outlined some options or doing so. He indicated that
the cost estimates contained in the report do not take into account costs
associated with the LEED certification process.
Mr. Yas explained that the construction costs had been escalated in five-year
increments and divided into categories, and recommended putting aside a reserve
of 10% of the total cost for contingencies. He noted that fees not included in
the cost estimates include the premium for expenses related to LEED
certification, and design and engineering expenditures.
The Council discussed the recommendations and phasing of the different elements
with the consultants and Mr. Saunders. Louise Holland suggested that the
Council take some trips to neighboring town halls to view their council
chambers, to get a perspective on the uniqueness of Winnetka’s Chambers.
Manager Williams urged the Council to appoint a technical committee, which
would include a member of the Landmarks Preservation Committee, to assist with
the renovation process.
3)   Feasibility Analysis: Winnetka Lincoln Avenue Surface Lot.
Mr. Saunders introduced the Village’s consultant, Beth Kruger, of Walker
Parking Consultants, to give her report on a proposed Lincoln Avenue parking
deck.
Ms. Kruger presented several different concepts for a parking structure,
including one with a residential component; discussed the functionality of the
various options; and reviewed the cost estimates for each alternative.
The Council discussed the various plans, and several audience members also had
opportunity to ask questions and comment.
Manager Williams recalled that residents at the Galleria expressed a great deal
of concern about a possible parking structure when it was discussed several
years ago, adding that the Community House also has an interest.
Louise Holland, Chairman of the Board of Governors of the Community House Board
of Directors, said the Community House is a destination location and an anchor
for retail in the east Elm business district, and noted that it had just been
placed on the National Register of Historic Places. She observed that parking
is a top concern for users of the programs and that the Community House would
like to partner with the Village on the parking deck project.
Cicely Michalak, Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce, pointed out
that the vibrancy of the East Elm district depends upon parking demands being
met.
Trustee Behles indicated that several BCDC members have voiced their strong
support for a parking deck on Lincoln Avenue.
Mr. Saunders discussed the likelihood of qualifying for a Congestion Mitigation
and Air Quality (CMAQ) grant, saying chances are slim, since parking structures
tend to score poorly in the application process. Manager Williams agreed that
this avenue is a long shot. He said the Study Session in November will be a
discussion of financial alternatives available to fund the various projects
being considered by the Council.
4)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
5)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:00 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
November 3, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 4, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Saturday,
November 3, 2007, at 9:30 a.m.
3)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 9:40 a.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio and approximately 70
persons in the audience.
4)   Public Comment re: Future of Post Office Site.
President Woodbury announced that the purpose of the study session was to
continue the conversation about the potential development of the post office
site, beginning with public comment and then reports from the Caucus chair and
staff. He said the Council would then discuss key issues, but would not take
action at this meeting.
The following members of the audience indicated that they agree with the Post
Office Committee’s recommendation:
Pat Horne, 791 Bryant
Bob Horne, 162 Fuller
Mike Duhl, 660 Prospect
The following members of the audience indicated they were not in favor of the
Post Office Committee’s recommendation:
Pat Corrigan, 1139 Oakley
Steve Ryan, 720 Humboldt
Richard Matthias, 514 Oak
Edith Howell, 277 Ridge
Anne Montgomery, 945 Old Green Bay
Heather Higgins, retailer
James Fargo, 515 Provident
John Janssen, 199 Church
Vicky Apatoff, 730 Ardsley
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood
Barbara Fargo
John Cook, 765 Willow
Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka
Alternatives suggested were to use the site for a library or other public
building, a restaurant, a putting green, enhancement of the park and open
space, an annex for the library and community house, a band shelter, community
activities, parking and an open and friendly space for walking.
The following persons also addressed the Council:
Debbie Ross, 921 Tower Road
Suzanne Canfield, 956 Westmoor
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower
Janet Poor, 595 Cedar Street
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce
Those persons who offered their opinions asked the Council to:
   ·   retain a post office on the site;
   ·   incorporate the developments of other business district buildings as
well as the streetscape and parking garage projects into a Village-wide plan;
   ·   form a dialog between different points of view that would include
members of the Garden Guild, Park and Library Boards;
   ·   consider the opportunity for affordable housing;
   ·   consider the economy and its effects on retail uses, the sale of real
estate and vehicle fuel costs;
   ·   use permeable surfaces to aid in water runoff;
   ·   include meaningfully open space to capitalize on the assets of Moffat
Mall and Dwyer Park;
   ·   create a gathering place at Elm and Chestnut around a fountain or
sculpture;
   ·   retain ownership of the land;
   ·   retain the uniqueness and character of Winnetka;
   ·   create a high quality, but not too dense, development;
   ·   keep residents informed of the costs of the project;
   ·   conduct meetings on TV and in front of the public; and,
   ·   construct the project in phases.
There was some opposition to underground parking, multi-story parking,
buildings more than 3 stories high, an increase in vehicle traffic, and the
Village subsidizing the costs. There were also some opinions that the Caucus’
survey questions were flawed and therefore, the results of the survey should
not be taken as a mandate from the residents.
President Woodbury announced that because of time constraints, Item #4 on the
agenda, Council Discussion, will be re-scheduled either at a meeting to be
determined or at the January 8, 2008 study session.
President Woodbury then responded briefly to comments made by audience members,
referring issues raised about the Caucus’ survey questions to the more
appropriate forum of the Caucus’ town meeting and a decision about the library
to the Library Board. He reported that there have been active conversations and
meetings with the Post Office about occupying space in a new development and it
is high on the priority list, but the government moves slowly.
President Woodbury added that that the Council, as elected officials, residents
and taxpayers, have the Village’s interests at heart and are aware that they
are elected to serve the Village as a whole. He said the Council also realizes
that there are strong opinions and will continue to listen to the public,
review the tapes, strive to retain the character of the Village and find what
is right for Winnetka.
Trustee Ritchell left the meeting at 11:35 a.m.
3)   Caucus report – Harold Nations, Chair, 2007 Winnetka Caucus Council
Harold Nations, Chair of the 2007 Winnetka Caucus, presented the Caucus survey
results stating that generally, the survey reflects the opinions of residents
who have lived in the Village the longest and that he was aware of the
limitations of the Caucus survey questions about the Post Office site. He
reported that the survey was returned by approximately one third of the total
number of households in the Village, and the responses indicated that: 1) 68%
of people agreed with the Post Office Committee’s recommendation; 2) the
relocation of the library was split almost evenly with those who agreed and
disagreed and with 25% needing more information; and 3) 77% supported the
Committee’s recommendation to provide open space for public gatherings as part
of the redevelopment.
Mr. Nations announced that the town meeting would be held on November 15, 2007
in the Skokie School auditorium where residents could learn other results with
respect to the Village.
Mr. Hugh Brower commented that there had been considerable debate on the Caucus
Committee whether or not to include a question on the Post Office site in the
survey, but the decision was made to go ahead. He said he was struck by the
consensus that the Village should work with the Park District to retain Dwyer
Park and to look at the site as a 4-acre piece of property, not just 2 acres.
He said the Caucus survey results endorse a mixed use of retail with open space
and that placement of a new library on the site should be deferred for further
consideration pending the Library Board’s decisions. Mr. Brower also announced
that at 6:30 p.m. on Thursday, November 15th, prior to the town meeting, there
will be a focus session where residents will have an opportunity to question
the candidates.
4)   Strategic questions re the Future of the Post Office Site
Manager Williams stated that Village staff reviewed the Post Office Committee
report and deliberations, the public comments made to date and the tape of the
September 11th study session and identified 8 strategic questions:
1.   What are the Village’s objectives for the Post Office site?
2.   Would a “minimal development” and mostly open/green space meet the
Village’s objectives?
3.   Would a mixed use development with ground floor commercial space,
residential units on the upper floor, and some open space meet the Village’s
objectives?
4.   Would a new library on the PO site, potentially combined with some
complementary retail or community uses, meet the Village’s objectives?
5.   Should Village funds be invested in the redevelopment of the PO site?
6.   How much parking should the PO site accommodate?
7.   Should some “affordable housing,” as defined in the context of Winnetka,
be provided on the PO site?
8.   Should the Village own the land, sell it, lease it, or create a blended
ownership structure?
Mr. Williams noted that:
·   the history of the property and the comprehensive plans will be helpful in
determining the Village’s objectives for the Post Office site;
·    the public investment of funds will be driven by underground parking, but
also the streetscape and other projects;
·   the Council knows that there are four projects on the table and have
deliberately guided the process with this in mind so that the community can
decide how to spend key resources;
·   parking is essential to the business district and while the Post Office did
not raise the issue of future parking needs, it has been discussed;
·   the Council, as policy leaders, will need to think about the ownership of
the land; retaining it will lead down one path, blended ownership will lead to
another; and
·   Council discussions about a building plan will include a component about
affordable housing as defined in the context of Winnetka.
Mr. Williams added that there have been extensive discussions with the Post
Office who has said they will need 3000 square feet and the Village has made it
clear that a retail facility is wanted there.
President Woodbury concluded by saying that the next meeting will try to focus
on Council discussion and will try to schedule a meeting in December, but if
that is not possible, the next meeting about the Post Office site will be on
January 8th.
5)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
6)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 12:02 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
November 6, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: November 20, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 6, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Ritchell: Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant
Community Development Director Brian Norkus, Finance Director Ed McKee,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Deputy Police Chief Pat Kreis, and
approximately 6 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   November 13, 2007 Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   November 20, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 11, 2007, Study Session. Attorney Janega pointed out a minor
correction to page 8, which was not contained in the agenda packet. Trustee
Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the minutes as corrected.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)    October 2, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   October 16, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Poor commented on the third
paragraph of the 717 Locust variation discussion, and Trustee Eilers had a
question on the recording of a vote. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes as corrected. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-13-2007 – Zoning Variations: 800 Tower Road – Adoption .
President Woodbury called for any comments or questions regarding Ordinance M-
13-2007, which would grant two zoning variations to construct a detached
garage, reconstruct the driveway, and add a front walk.
Attorney Janega called attention to a minor change on page two of the ordinance
that adds a reference to the front walkway being constructed with brick pavers,
as well as the driveway.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend Ordinance No. M-13-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-13-
2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: Trustee Berger. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2007 – Zoning Variations: 150 Fuller Lane – Adoption.
President Woodbury called for any comments or questions regarding Ordinance M-
17-2007, which would grant three zoning variations to permit the construction
of a second floor addition.
There being none, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt
Ordinance No. M-17-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
c)   Ordinance No. M-18-2007 – Zoning Variations: 1091 Sheridan Road –
Adoption. President Woodbury called for any comments or questions regarding
Ordinance M-18-2007, which would grant four zoning variations to permit the
construction of one-story additions.
There being none, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt
Ordinance No. M-18-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Ritchell.
d)   Ordinance No. M-19-2007 – Zoning Variations: 717 Locust Road – Adoption.
President Woodbury called for any comments or questions regarding Ordinance M-
19-2007, which would grant two zoning variations to permit the construction of
a new single-family home.
Trustee Poor said he had problems with granting the variations from both policy
and procedural standpoints, and indicated that he thought the Zoning Ordinance
was in need of more scrutiny to further examine the issue of overbuilding in
the Village. He suggested amending the proposed ordinance to remove findings
relating to reasonable return and adequate light and air for neighboring
properties.
Attorney Janega commented on the proposed amendments. She then noted that the
affirmative vote of four Trustees was needed to pass the ordinance, and that
Trustee Ritchell, who was absent, was one of the four Trustees who supported
granting the variations.
The other Trustees, with the exception of Trustee Behles, said they could give
their support to the suggested amendments.
President Woodbury suggested tabling the ordinance while the amendments are
being made.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to table the ordinance. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
e)   Ordinance No. MC-14-2007 – Amending Village Code to Establish a Dog Leash
Law – Introduction. Deputy Police Chief Kreis reported that the 2006 Caucus
Survey recommended the Village study the adoption of a leash ordinance for dogs
on public property. He indicated that at the conclusion of the September 18th
public hearing on the issue, the Council directed staff to draft a proposed
leash law.
Deputy Chief Kreis said there had been some question about the use of the word
“non-retractable,” and explained that the concern had been that a long
retractable leash set past eight feet wouldn’t comply with the ordinance. He
indicated that the ordinance could be amended to take out the reference.
Trustee Poor suggested that the ordinance not be passed until the Village had
coordinated with the Park District to see if the restrictions on dogs in public
parks could be loosened in conjunction with the implementation of the leash
law. He suggested introducing the ordinance and tabling it until after
discussions with the Park Board are completed.
Manager Williams noted that he had received the impression in his discussions
with the Park District that they would review their policy if the Village
passed the leash law.
Trustee Berger indicated that she saw passage of the leash law as being
independent of any action that the Park Board would take, and said she
preferred not to delay passing the proposed ordinance.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-14-
2007, as amended, establishing a dog leash requirement in the Village Code. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to table adoption of the
ordinance until the Village has heard back from the Park District. By voice
vote the motion carried.
f)   Resolution No. R-43-2007 – Approval of Development Agreement between
Village, GBM Developers, and CBI Custom Homes, for 630 Pine Lane – Adoption.
Mr. Norkus reported that the Council approved the proposed CBI Subdivision of
630 Pine Lane last fall. He noted that the complexity of the conditions
necessitated a development agreement be entered into between the Village and
the developer to define the terms of Village approval. He reviewed the
agreement and commented upon the highlights, which include the priority given
to stabilizing the historic residence at 630 Pine Lane, so that Certificates of
Occupancy will not be issued for the new homes until the existing home is
rehabilitated.
Mr. Norkus answered several questions, discussed the various maintenance
conditions imposed by the agreement, and confirmed that the neighbors’ comments
have been incorporated.
Attorney Janega reviewed two technical amendments to the Resolution.
Manager Williams commented that development agreements have worked very well in
the past, and thanked Attorney Janega and Mr. Norkus for their hard work in
completing the project.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-43-
2007, approving a development agreement between the Village, GMB Developers and
CBI Custom Homes, for 630 Pine Lane. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Ritchell.
g)   Resolution No. R-48-2007 – Corner Cooks Liquor License – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reported that the owner of Corner Cooks had applied for a Class
A-1 liquor license, in order to expand her restaurant operation. She indicated
that Council action is required to authorize a new Class A-1 liquor license and
to reduce the number of authorized Class A licenses by one.
Attorney Janega noted that since the expansion is not complete at this time,
staff recommends that the Council’s approval be conditioned upon successful
completion of the Police Department’s inspection of the premises, and the
issuance of a Certificate of Occupancy by the Community Development department.
She noted that if the renovations are not completed as a result of pending
litigation against the property’s owner, then the license would not issue. She
confirmed that the process is the same that was followed with Mirani’s, when it
applied for a Class A-1 Liquor License in conjunction with relocating the
restaurant.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-48-
2007, approving a Class A-1 liquor license for Corner Cooks. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
h)   Resolution No. R-38-2007 – High Excess Liability Pool Renewal – Adoption.
Finance Director Ed McKee reported that although the Village is largely self-
insured, it is also part of a High Excess Liability Pool (HELP) that reduces
exposure to severe losses. He indicated that the pool has been a good
arrangement for the Village over the years, and that although alternatives have
been studied, none have proven suitable for the Village’s needs. He explained
how the pool works, and recommended that the Council continue the Village’s
HELP membership at an annual premium of $37,326.
There was a brief discussion about how the formula for member contributions
works, after which Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt
Resolution No. R-38-2007, authorizing the Village to continue HELP membership.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
i)   Three Ordinances re: New State Law: Cable & Video Competition. Attorney
Janega reported that she had recently been part of a subcommittee formed by the
Illinois Municipal League (IML) to update its model ordinance for
telecommunications carriers. She observed that the subcommittee had completed a
package of four model ordinances, which addressed provisions of video and cable
service under a new State of Illinois Public Act, passed in June of 2007, which
established the Cable and Video Competition Law of 2007, and the Cable and
Video Customer Protection Law. She indicated that the three ordinances up for
introduction are based on the IML’s model ordinances, but have been customized
for Winnetka.
   (i)   MC-15-2007 – R-O-W Amendments – Introduction. Attorney Janega
explained that the amendments contained in the subject ordinance incorporate
changes from the updated IML model ordinance. She indicated that the Village’s
prohibition against overhead utility facilities, its notice provisions, and its
tree preservation standards remained in effect.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-15-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   (ii)   MC-16-2007 – Cable/Video Provider and PEG Access Support Fees –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reported that Ordinance MC-16-2007 adds a new
Chapter 14.12 to the Village Code, establishing fees for video and cable
service and for PEG access, to cover the cost of getting the same services from
the other service providers.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-16-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   (iii)   MC-17-2007 – Cable/Video Service Customer Protection Law –
Introduction. Attorney Janega explained that the subject ordinance incorporates
the new State customer protection provisions into a new Chapter 14.16 of the
Village Code.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to introduce MC-17-2007. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
j)   Resolution No. R-47-2007 – Willow Road Rehabilitation – Phase I
Preliminary Engineering – Adoption. Mr. Saunders reported that the Village’s
capital plan includes a proposed construction project to improve Willow Road
between Provident and the Village’s western boundary. He indicated that
components of the proposal include pavement improvements, improving the
intersection of Willow and Hibbard, traffic calming near Crow Island School, a
bicycle path west of Hibbard Road, and installing a traffic signal at the
intersection of Willow and Forestway. He said federal funding will provide
approximately 70% of the estimated $3 million cost of the project, and that the
expenditure of MFT funds for the first phase of the project must be authorized
by resolution.
Manager Williams noted that the Village would be talking to policy leaders,
including the School Board, about implementing any rehabilitation plan near
Crow Island School.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-47-2007,
authorizing the expenditure of $200,000 of the Village’s Motor Fuel Tax funds
for completing Phase I Preliminary Engineering for the rehabilitation of Willow
Road. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Willow Road Rehabilitation – Phase I Preliminary Engineering Contract. Mr.
Saunders reported that staff had received and reviewed six proposals for the
Phase I Preliminary Engineering for the Willow Road improvement project between
Provident and the Village’s western boundary. He said the lowest bidder,
Civiltech Engineering, enjoyed a good reputation, demonstrated creativity in
their understanding of the project, and had experience successfully completing
similar projects. He added that the preliminary design, which will incorporate
the Willow Road Enhancement Study conclusions, would take about 16 months to
complete.
Mr. Saunders indicated that unforeseen requirements of the project, coupled
with a more complex and time-consuming scope of work involved, had led staff to
underestimate the cost of the project in its initial estimates. He noted that
the budgeted amount of $100,000 will be sufficient to pay for the portion of
the work that will take place in the current fiscal year, and that the
remainder of the cost will be budgeted in the next fiscal year.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the contract for
Preliminary Phase I Engineering Services for the Willow Road Rehabilitation to
Civiltech Engineering, Inc., for an amount not to exceed $196.085, to be paid
for with the Village’s Motor Fuel Tax Funds. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
b)   Warrant Lists 1531 and 1532. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1531 in the amount of $1,453,271.42 and Warrant List No. 1532 in the amount of
$660,588.06. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
10)   Reports
a)   Trustees. Trustee Behles announced that the Willow Road Enhancement
Committee would have its third workshop on Thursday, Nov. 29, in Winnetka at
the North Shore Country Day School, and encouraged all Winnetka residents to
attend.
Trustee Eilers reported that the Residential Design Committee would be bringing
approval of the consultant contracts before the Council at its next meeting.
b)   President. None.
c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega announced that she had recently attended the
IMLA’s annual conference in Nashville, where she also spoke on First Amendment
issues, and learned a good deal of useful information on Telecom laws.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams advised the Council that the Village had
received a violation from the Metropolitan Water Reclamation District for
leachate discharge into the sanitary sewer system. Mr. Saunders observed that
the fines would be about $500 for each violation, and that this is the first
time since the mid-90’s that the Village had had a problem.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The Council adjourned into
Executive Session at 9:27 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor and Tucker. Absent: Trustee
Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The meeting adjourned at 10:16
p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
November 13, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 4, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 13, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Community
Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately 14 persons in the
audience.
2)   Kevin McCanna – Speer Financial.
Manager Williams introduced Winnetka’s financial advisor, Kevin McCanna,
President of Speer Financial. Mr. McCanna gave an overview of the different
financing tools the Village could use to help pay for upcoming projects. After
explaining how a Tax Incremental Financing (TIF) District works, Mr. McCanna
reviewed Special Service Areas (SSA’s), Business Districts, and also different
types of bonds that could be used as funding vehicles, such as revenue bonds,
general obligation bonds, SSA bonds, Business District or sales tax bonds, and
alternate revenue bonds.
Mr. McCanna explained how an SSA would be created, and expanded on the criteria
necessary to qualify for a TIF district. He indicated that the dispute with TIF
bonds is whether the needed development would occur without the incentive
anyway, and he added that the other taxing bodies may lose a little revenue
early on, but that when the district expires and the value comes online, they
gain more. With regard to the school districts, he observed that although a
school district couldn’t stop the Village from implementing a TIF district, it
is easier to negotiate with the schools and reach an agreement with incentives
on both sides.
Mr. McCanna said the decision about which funding tool to use is made based on
who is at risk and who pays, and that developers prefer a TIF because no extra
money comes out of their pockets.
President Woodbury expressed concern that a TIF erodes the tax base somewhat,
but stressed that it doesn’t have to be the only financing option.
3)   PowerPoint Presentation Regarding Financing Options.
Manager Williams reported that staff recognized a few years ago that the
Village had several big projects on the horizon, and said the process was
managed so that the issues would converge at the same time, so the community
could prioritize them and decide whether to do all, some, or none of them. He
indicated that it is time to start thinking about funding tools, but stressed
that he is not making recommendations at this early stage, but rather is just
supplying brainstorming ideas to help facilitate the community discussion. He
stressed that the Council has not yet set policy direction on any of the
projects or their funding options.
Manager Williams then gave a Power Point presentation which sketched out
several ways in which the Village might fund some major projects, which
included the following key points:
   ·   No funding option will increase property taxes on single-family homes.
   ·   Each possible project is a stand-alone and funding one project does not
require funding another project.
   ·   The streetscape and Village Hall renovation are multi-phased, and each
phase is a stand-alone.
   ·   Funding options include using cash reserves; re-prioritizing the 5-year
Capital Plan; federal or state grants or private donations; or finding new
revenue sources.
Manager Williams then reviewed the various debt options. He also described how
a Home Rule sales tax would work, and explained that Winnetka is the only Home
Rule municipality on the North Shore that hasn’t implemented one.
Finally, Manager Williams observed that the course now was to determine which,
if any, of the four projects will be funded, and to determine the scope, budget
and source of funding for any selected project.
President Woodbury then called for questions from the audience.
Suggestions from the audience addressed aspects of funding, such as exploring
grants from non-profits or using private funding from the community or a local
foundation. The point was raised that the TIF district will have an impact on
homeowners’ taxes until the district expires.
The issue of a transfer tax was raised, and President Woodbury indicated that
the moment is not right, given the current real estate market. He said there
are other tools at the Village’s disposal at the current time, and indicated
that the issue could be brought up at another occasion if need arises.
After some further discussion, President Woodbury noted that the purpose of the
evening’s presentations was to give food for thought, and that more dialog was
not immediately required.
4)   Discussion: 2007 Property Tax Levy Analysis.
Mr. McKee reported that the Village remains fiscally strong, and that operating
costs and capital needs can be met with a modest property tax increase
approximately equal to the rate of inflation. He observed that the Village has
sufficient cash reserves and has a sound budget overall, but the challenge
remains to keep expense increases in line with revenue projections. He
indicated that implementation of most of the major capital projects being
contemplated would draw down reserves, and that the Village would need to
consider new revenue sources and explore forming partnerships with those
benefiting from the projects.
Mr. McKee went on to say that staff is recommending a tax levy increase for
existing residents of 2.5%, which equates to an increase of $70 for a resident
with a $20,000 total property tax bill. He estimated that new development would
add about 2.2%, resulting in a total property tax revenue increase to the
Village of about 4.7%, adding that if new development is greater or lesser than
2.2%, the existing residents would see a corresponding increase or decrease in
their portions.
Mr. McKee indicated that he used the Village’s scorecard system to rate
different aspects of the Village’s finances based on the change in the CPI for
2006, and noted that the Village is abiding by the non-Home Rule tax cap of
2.5%, even though it has not been bound by the cap since passage of the Home
Rule referendum.
There was a short discussion about some of the projections contained in the
memo, the current market situation and pension funding, after which it was
agreed that the proposed tax levy would keep Village finances on course.
Manager Williams announced that staff would prepare a tax levy ordinance for
consideration at the first meeting in December.
5)   Executive Session.
Trustee Eilers moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Pending and Probably Litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned
into Executive Session at 9:35 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:48 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:49 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
November 20, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 18, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 20, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus, Community Development
Plan Assistant Jill Morgan, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Police
Chief Joe DeLopez, Police Sgt. Marc Hornstein, Public Works Director Steve
Saunders, and approximately 13 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   December 4, 2007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   December 11, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 6, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Three Ordinances re: New State Law: Cable & Video Competition. Attorney
Janega reported that the three new code amendments were made necessary by the
enactment of the Cable and Video Competition Law and the Cable and Video
Customer Protection Law, both of which were enacted as part of Public Act 95-
0009 on June 30, 2007. She explained that the ordinances amend the Village’s
right-of-way regulations, establish service provider fees for cable and video
services, establish PEG access fees, and adopt statutory provisions of the
Cable and Video Customer protection law.  
   i)   Ordinance No. MC-15-2007 – R-O-W Amendments – Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed the key amendments that had been made since the time of introduction,
which included clarification of some of the fee provisions, and she also
explained the landscaping requirements.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to amend Ordinance MC-15-2007.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance
MC-15-2007, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   ii)   Ordinance No. MC-16-2007 – Cable/Video Provider and PEG Access Support
Fees – Adoption. There being no further comments or questions regarding the
subject ordinance, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt
Ordinance MC-16-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   iii)   Ordinance No. MC-17-2007 – Cable/Video Service Customer Protection
Law – Adoption. There being no further comments or questions regarding the
subject ordinance, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt
Ordinance MC-17-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-19-2007 – Zoning Variations: 717 Locust - Adoption.
President Woodbury announced that the subject ordinance would grant two
variations to permit the construction of a new single-family residence, and
called for a motion to remove the ordinance from the table.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to remove Ordinance M-19-
2007 from the table. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments, which were made based on suggestions
from Trustee Poor at the November 6th Council meeting.
Trustee Poor indicated that the amendments addressed his concerns.
President Woodbury observed that an initial discussion regarding the Zoning
Ordinance would take place at the December study session.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend Ordinance M-19-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance M-19-
2007, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: Trustees Berger and Poor. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-20-2007 – Zoning Variations: 510 Walnut – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the zoning variations application, which requests four
variations to permit the construction of a detached garage, to replace an
existing garage which was demolished in a storm. He noted that the original
garage was nonconforming and encroached into the neighbor’s property by two
feet, and that the new garage will be somewhat smaller than the original, and
will slightly decrease the net impermeable lot coverage, which is existing
nonconforming.
There being no questions or comments, President Woodbury suggested that the
Council consider waiving introduction of the ordinance because of the
extenuating circumstance of the severe storm.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance
M-20-2007. By voice vote the motion carried.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance M-20-2007.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-21-2007 – Local Landmark: 1140 Willow Road – Introduction.
Ms. Morgan reported that the Landmark Preservation Commission (LPC) has voted
to recommend the Log House in Crow Island Woods be designated as a Winnetka
Landmark. She outlined the criteria used for the recommendation, notably the
rarity of architectural type, style and period, and association with a
nationally significant historic figure, Anita Willets Burnham.
Trustee Behles indicated that since he is the Historical Society’s architect
for the Log House, he would abstain from voting.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
21-2007, granting local landmark status to the Log House in Crow Island Woods.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
e)   Ordinance No. M-22-2007 – Local Landmark: 662 Maple – Introduction. Ms.
Morgan reported that the LPC has recommended that the Koehn House at 662 Maple
be designated as a Winnetka Landmark. She described the home as a great example
of four-square architecture and added that, based upon the System for
Evaluation of Landmarks, the property received an overall score of 66 points,
resulting in a rating of “Significant.”
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
22-2007, granting local landmark status to 662 Maple. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
f)   Ordinance No. M-23-2007 – Local Landmark: 1345 Trapp Lane – Introduction.
Ms. Morgan indicated that the LPC had recommended that the Gordon House at 1345
Trapp Lane be designated as a Winnetka Landmark, noting that the house was
built in 1929 by a nationally-known architect and that it received an overall
score of 81.4 points, which gives it a “Unique” rating.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
23-2007, granting local landmark status for 1345 Trapp Lane. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
g)   Resolution No. R-45-2007 – Verizon – Lease Agreement – Adoption. Mr. Keys
reported that the license agreement with SMSA, formerly Verizon, has been re-
negotiated, with the new term retroactive to the expiration date of the old
license, October 31, 2007. He noted that this is the first of four license
agreements that the Village is re-negotiating with cellular communication
providers. He said key elements in the new terms include an annual lease
payment and an initial term of 10 years, with three one-year renewals.
Attorney Janega observed that now that Mr. Keys is finished with negotiations,
the resolution approves the agreement substantially in the form attached,
subject to her approval of the terms.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-45-2007,
approving the cell site lease agreement with Verizon, substantially in the form
attached. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Resolution No. R-46-2007 – U.S. Cellular – Lease Agreement – Adoption. Mr.
Keys presented the second of the four re-negotiated license agreements, noting
that the agreement is with Primeco, formerly U.S. Cellular, and that the new
agreement is retroactive to the expiration of the prior agreement, March 30,
2007..
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-46-2007,
approving the new license agreement with U.S. Cellular, substantially in the
form attached. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
i)   Resolution No. R-49-2007 – Traffic Signal Maintenance Agreement –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders reported that the Village has an ongoing agreement with
IDOT, under which the Village maintains a number of traffic signals on state
roads and bills IDOT for its share of eligible maintenance and repair costs. He
stated that the agreement expired last January and recommended entering into a
new agreement, which will run for ten more years.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-49-
2007, authorizing the Village to enter into an agreement with IDOT to maintain
certain traffic signals on streets under the jurisdiction of the State of
Illinois. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Police Chief Report: Juvenile Law and Procedures. Chief DeLopez reported
that a couple of recent incidents have raised concerns, and he would like
provide some clarifications about the arrest and processing of juveniles in
Winnetka.
Chief DeLopez gave an overview of Police Department procedures regarding
juveniles, noting that certain interactions are governed by State law. He
indicated that he had requested clarification from the State’s Attorney’s
office and had modified some department policies as a result. He then
introduced Winnetka’s Juvenile Group Supervisor, Sgt. Marc Hornstein.
Sgt. Hornstein said dealing with juveniles is one of the biggest challenges
facing the Police Department, since there is more grey area when dealing with
minors. He explained that the parents are contacted as soon as possible as a
matter of Department policy, and then outlined the various methods of dealing
with juvenile offenders: (i) issuing a citation; (ii) assigning the youth to
Peer Jury; (iii) doing a formal station adjustment, in which the parents of the
minor agree to terms. He indicated that officers are reluctant to involve the
juvenile in the court system, and often try to resolve the issue without
issuing a citation.
In the ensuing discussion about the procedures followed when dealing with
juveniles, it was ascertained that under the new policy, fingerprinting will
not always take place, and that Winnetka’s officers will now consult with Sgt.
Hornstein before making any decisions regarding a juvenile.
Susan Lifvendahl, 140 Westview Road, said she thinks the proposed new policy is
a good one, but may not go far enough, and disagreed that having a child go
through the courts is more detrimental than having him or her fingerprinted and
photographed, thus giving the child a criminal record. She observed that the
decision to arrest is a discretionary one, and asked that the same rules that
are followed when deciding to make an arrest also be followed when deciding
whether or not to fingerprint.
Stu Cohen, 505 Provident, expressed his concerns about law enforcement policies
in the Village, and said he believed the Council should oversee the Police
Department in juvenile matters.
Eric Lifvendahl, 140 Westview Road., said juveniles need to be dealt with in a
sensitive way, with a lot of discretion and common sense used by the officer
involved, and expressed the hope that there is a middle ground that could be
achieved.
President Woodbury observed that community-specific standards are an essential
part of law enforcement in Winnetka, and called on the Police Chief to review
department policies relative to community-specific goals and to make the
necessary modifications. He acknowledged that Winnetka’s police officers deal
with the complete range of public safety issues, and that it’s a tough job,
adding that the police are by and large sensitive to the issues that apply.
b)   Cell Phone Quarterly Report. Chief DeLopez reported the enforcement
results for the Village’s hand-held cell phone ordinance from February 1, 2007,
to October 31, 2007, as follows:
   ·   Warning notices:   411
   ·   Citations      111
   ·   Total         522
He observed that of the total number of motorists affected, 16.3% were
residents of Winnetka, and 83.7% resided elsewhere, which indicates that, for
the most part, Winnetkans know about the law.
There was a short discussion about how the citations are handled in court,
after which Chief DeLopez observed that the challenge remains with regard to
communicating the law to people residing outside of Winnetka.
c)   Authorization the Purchase of Squad Cars. Chief DeLopez reported that the
Police Department had obtained one of three needed police patrol cars from a
now-expired State bid, but there currently is no State bid or assigned supplier
available to supply the other two squad cars. He asked the Council to waive the
formal bid process in order to purchase the vehicles from Thomas Dodge of
Orland Park, IL, which can accommodate the Police Department’s immediate need
for two 2008 Dodge Charger Police sedans.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to authorize the purchase of
two squad cars at Thomas Dodge for an amount not to exceed $48,000. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Consultant Services – Residential Design Guidelines. Mr. Norkus reported
that the ad hoc Residential Design Committee had recently evaluated the
qualifications of consultants to help the Committee complete its mission of
developing a series of non-binding design guidelines for the Village. He stated
that after interviewing three of the firms, the Committee selected Winter &
Company of Boulder, CO, as having the best combination of project approach,
relevant experience and personnel.
Mr. Norkus briefly outlined the scope of work and reviewed some of the
highlights contained in the consultant’s proposal, noting that, because of the
nonbinding nature of the guidelines, the final report will be an educational
tool with several different audiences in mind.
Trustee Eilers indicated that Mr. Winter has outstanding communications skills,
which will be important in presenting the concepts to the community.
Manager Williams observed that Winter & Co. could prove helpful with the review
of the Zoning Ordinance, to which Mr. Norkus added that it is nearly impossible
to have a discussion about design and character without bringing zoning issues
into play.
Charles Paige, an architect and Winnetka resident, said he is interested in the
design guidelines with regard to teardowns, saying he thought that the
guidelines would be subjective and difficult to enforce and that zoning is the
best control tool.
President Woodbury observed that the Design Guidelines Committee meetings are
open to the public, and all are welcome to attend.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve a contract for
services with Winter & Company in the amount of $99,845. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Change Order #1: Indian Hill Parking Lot. Mr. Saunders reported that a
routine rehabilitation project at the Indian Hill parking lot had run into
problems when it was discovered that the lot was constructed with a gravel base
of insufficient thickness over subgrade soil. To correct the deficiencies, the
scope of work was expanded at an additional cost of $81,000, necessitating the
approval of a change order by the Council.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve Change Order #1 to
the 2007 Street Rehabilitation Contract in the amount of $81,000 to accommodate
additional needed work at the Indian Hill parking lot. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Warrant Lists 1533 and 1534. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1533 in the amount of $782,096.70 and Warrant List No. 1534 in the amount of
$297,333.96. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Bid: Green Bay Trail Fence Replacement. Mr. Saunders reported that a fence
along the railroad right-of-way in Winnetka’s portion of the Green Bay Trial is
in poor condition, and that it would be wise to coordinate replacement of the
fence with an upcoming drainage project planned by the Union Pacific Railroad.
He recommended waiving the bidding process, due to time constraints, and
allowing the lowest of three competitive price proposals to be awarded the job.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to waive bids and award a
purchase order to All American Fence for an amount not to exceed $37,000. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. President Woodbury announced that the Post Office site
redevelopment will come before the Council for discussion on Monday, December
17, and again on January 8th.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Behles invited all interested residents to attend the
Willow Road Enhancement Project workshop on Thursday, November 29, at 7:30 p.m.
at the North Shore Country Day School.
Trustee Ritchell reported that the ad hoc Historic Residential Preservation
Committee is reviewing an outline of their presentation, which should be
completed shortly.
Trustee Berger announced that Wilmette and Wheeling have officially joined the
RED Center, which is good for Winnetka.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that tours have been scheduled on
Saturday, December 1, and Saturday, December 8, for the Council to look at
various redevelopment projects, and invited all interested parties to attend.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11), of
the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into
Executive Session at 10:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:48 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:49 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
December 1, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 18, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was convened at Village Hall on Saturday, December 1, 2007, at
9:30 a.m.
1)   Call to Order.
The meeting was called to order at 9:30 a.m. Present: Trustees Jessica Tucker,
Tom Eilers, and Herb Ritchell. Absent: Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger and
King Poor. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Mike D’Onofrio and approximately 4 other persons.
2)   Bus Tour of re-developments in surrounding downtown areas.
Mr. D’Onofrio conducted a tour of various developments in the surrounding
suburbs of Park Ridge, Des Plaines, Mt. Prospect and Arlington Heights, to
examine those downtown projects in relation to the development of the post
office site. Staff suggested eight points to consider when viewing the
developments:
   ·   Architecture and design;
   ·   The relationship of the new projects to the existing downtown
architecture, building mass and streetscape;
   ·   Parking;
   ·   Traffic circulation;
   ·   The treatment of four sides of a building when all are exposed – service
and refuse areas, access to parking;
   ·   Overall cost of the redevelopment projects and percentage of public
investment;
   ·   Integration of streetscape, open and common space; and,
   ·   Signage.
3)   Adjournment.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion passed. The meeting adjourned at 12:30 p.m.
