Deputy Clerk
December 4, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: December 18, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 4, 2007, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Public Works Steve
Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, and one person in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   December 11, 2007, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   December 17, 1007, Special Study Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   December 18, 1007, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 23, 2007, Special Study Session. Trustee Berger, seconded by
Trustee Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
b)   November 3, 2007, Special Study Session. Trustee Eilers, seconded by
Trustee Berger, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
c)   November 13, 2007, Study Session. Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-21-2007 – Local Landmark: 1140 Willow Road – Adoption.
President Woodbury announced that the subject ordinance would designate the
Schmidt-Burnham Log House in Crow Island Woods as a Winnetka Landmark. There
being no further questions or comments, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to adopt Ordinance M-21-2007. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker (Trustee Behles
abstained). Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
b)   Ordinance No. M-23-2007 – Local Landmark: 1345 Trapp Lane – Adoption.
President Woodbury announced that the subject ordinance would designate the
Gordon House at 1345 Trapp Lane as a Winnetka Landmark. There being no further
questions or comments, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to
adopt Ordinance M-23-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Ritchell.
c)   Ordinance No. M-24-2007 – 2007 Tax Levy – Introduction. Mr. McKee reported
that pursuant to discussions at the November 13th Study Session, this ordinance
would increase the total tax levy for the Village by a total of 4.7% for 2007.
He noted that the Village continues to stay below the state-mandated caps for
non-home rule municipalities.
Trustee Ritchell arrived at 7:42 p.m.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance M-24-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-25-2007 – 2007 Tax Abatement – Introduction. Mr. McKee
reported that the subject ordinance would abate the tax levy for the Village’s
Series 2003 Bonds, which were issued for the Public Safety Building. He
explained that the bonds were backed by the Village’s property taxes so the
Village could get the lowest interest rate possible, but that the alternate
funding source is sales taxes. Because the sales tax revenues have been
sufficient to make the bond payments each year, the tax levy is abated each
year.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce Ordinance M-25-
2007. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
a)   Withdrawal by Owners – Local Landmark: 662 Maple. Attorney Janega
explained that the owners of the subject property had formally withdrawn their
application for local landmark designation for their home, and that no further
Council action is required.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1535 and 1536. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1535 in the amount of $1,136,267.96 and Warrant List No. 1536 in the amount of
$562,404.39. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Change Order #1: Lakota Contract. Mr. Saunders reported that the Village
entered into a contract with the Lakota Group in December, 2005, to develop a
master plan for streetscape improvements in the Village’s business districts.
He explained that the scope of the original project was unexpectedly expanded
and that Lakota met with the committee a total of 12 times, resulting in an
increase of $22,049.90 over the initial contract amount.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to approve Change Order #1
to the Streetscape Master Plan contract for the Lakota Group, increasing the
contract amount by $22,049.90 for a contract maximum of $136.628.90. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Village Council Meeting Schedule for 2008. Manager Williams reviewed the
schedule of 2008 Council meetings, noting that several holidays or election
days conflicted with Council meetings and that those meetings could be
rescheduled, as long as notices comply with Open Meetings Act requirements. He
recommended canceling the first meeting of the year, scheduled for New Year’s
Day, and rescheduling the two meetings that coincide with the Primary and
General Elections.
After a short discussion, it was the sense of the Council that the January 1
meeting should be cancelled and that the meetings set for election days should
be rescheduled for the following Thursdays, to maximize residents’ opportunity
to vote and participate in the Council meetings.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the modified
2008 Council meeting schedule as discussed. By voice vote the motion carried
unanimously.
d)   Turbine # 6 Inspection and Overhaul. Mr. Keys reported that staff had
completed the disassembly and inspection of Turbine #6, and areas for
significant repair had been identified. He added that the two competing vendors
had done an extensive on-site inspection of the turbine components, and
recommended awarding the repair work to Power Plant Services Inc., based on
favorable feedback from other sites. He observed that additional items may be
identified as needing repair as the work progresses, and requested a
contingency of $15,000 to cover any unforeseen event.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to issue a purchase order to
Power Plant Services, Inc. in an amount not to exceed $66,200, with an
additional $15,000 that may be spent on other identified repair work authorized
by the Village. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Consultant Services for Department Head Compensation Review. Trustee
Tucker reported that in furtherance of the Council’s decision to review current
compensation methodology, practices and amounts for Department Heads, she and
Trustee Poor had interviewed several compensation consulting firms and had
determined that Hewitt and Associate had the best combination of project
approach, relevant experience, community understanding and available resources.
After a brief discussion about some proposed modifications to the letter of
engagement, billing procedures and the target date for the completion of the
report, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve a contract
with Hewitt and Associates, substantially in the form as modified by the
Village Attorney, for an amount not to exceed $8,000. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers reported that the Plan Commission would give a
status report on Affordable Housing at the study session in January and the
Commission is also hard at work on studying the Overlay District.
Trustee Behles announced that the Willow Road Enhancement Committee had
conducted a final workshop the previous Thursday, and that the consultant’s
report may be ready sometime in January.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams made a minor correction to his Power Point
presentation of November 13th.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing board appointments and pending and probable litigation, pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(3) and 2(c)(11) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 8:26 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:09 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:10
p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
December 8, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 18, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was convened at Village Hall on Saturday, December 8, 2007, at
9:30 a.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 9:30 a.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, King Poor, Absent: Trustee Jessica Tucker, Tom
Eilers, and Herb Ritchell. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately 3 other
persons.
2)   Bus Tour of Re-developments in surrounding downtown areas.
Mr. D’Onofrio conducted a tour of various developments in the surrounding
suburbs of Park Ridge, Des Plaines, Mt. Prospect and Arlington Heights, to
examine those downtown projects in relation to the development of the post
office site. Staff suggested eight points to consider when viewing the
developments:
   ·   Architecture and design;
   ·   The relationship of the new projects to the existing downtown
architecture, building mass and streetscape;
   ·   Parking;
   ·   Traffic circulation;
   ·   The treatment of four sides of a building when all are exposed – service
and refuse areas, access to parking;
   ·   Overall cost of the redevelopment projects and percentage of public
investment;
   ·   Integration of streetscape, open and common space; and,
   ·   Signage.
3)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion passed. The meeting adjourned at 12:30 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
December 11, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: December 18, 2007)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 11, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Ritchell. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant
Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and approximately 4 persons in
the audience.
2)   Power Point Presentation re: Renewable Energy Certificates.
Mr. Keys explained that purchasing Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs) does
not ensure that renewable power will be specifically delivered to Winnetka, but
rather helps to get more “green” power onto the power grid. He said the REC
program works by offsetting the cost differential between renewable energy and
fossil power, with funds from the sale of the certificates closing the
operating gap of the green suppliers.
Mr. Keys reported that a difficulty with RECs is that there is no real way to
determine exactly where the power supply comes from. He noted that there is
currently a non-profit organization that audits the renewable energy suppliers
to ensure that RECs are actually used to purchase green power, and that buying
RECs from a supplier that is certified will ensure that green power was
actually procured.
Mr. Keys outlined two types of REC programs that could be implemented, using
the Naperville and Northbrook REC programs as examples of each type, and
outlining the environmental benefits and funding options of each plan.
There was a discussion about whether it is best to purchase RECs that only
support renewable power generated in Illinois, or whether to buy from a
regional or national supplier as well. The consensus of the Council was that
more study was needed, but that Winnetka should take a leadership role in
educating residents about the benefits of a REC program and that, after further
examination, the Village could choose a plan that would work well for the
Village. Mr. Keys indicated that he would do more research and come before the
Council in a few months to discuss implementation of a REC program in more
detail.
3)   Proposed Zoning Modifications.
Mr. D’Onofrio said he understood the concerns about bulk that were raised in
Trustee Tucker’s memo, and that he hears from people on both sides of the issue
on a regular basis. He indicated that the residential design guidelines
consultant would be looking at zoning as part of their process, and said a
change in zoning at this time would impact the residential design guidelines
discussion. He noted that while building height was determined by zoning
district, the side yard setbacks were calculated using lot size.
Attorney Janega observed that the gross floor area (GFA) is also based on lot
size and is inversely proportionate, with GFA decreasing as lot area increases.
Trustee Tucker reiterated her concerns about the bulk of houses being
constructed on small lots.
Trustee Poor agreed with Trustee Tucker that there was a problem with
overbuilding, and that it affects neighbors with regard to light, air and
drainage, and has an environmental impact as well. He suggested creating a
greater differential in terms of floor area ratio (FAR) between existing houses
and new houses to create a greater incentive to renovate vs. tearing down.
Mr. Norkus said his perception was that design and materials, massing and scale
were the issue, not side yard setbacks, and that moving the side yard setback
doesn’t do much in terms of scaling and mass.
Mr. D’Onofrio said he agreed with Trustee Poor in theory, but in practice he
didn’t believe it would drive the decision to renovate a house, because if the
house has small bedrooms and a small kitchen it doesn’t make sense to add onto
it.
Trustee Behles noted that zoning limits and restrictions are very interactive,
and that if the side yards are squeezed there would be unintended consequences
of bulk pushing out somewhere else in the building envelope, or garages being
brought to the front of the house, as well as badly designed houses. He agreed
with Trustee Poor that tweaking the FAR limit would be the best functional
approach.
President Woodbury commented that the eclectic mix of houses was an important
feature of Winnetka character, and that zoning that is too restrictive can
force designers into a box.
Attorney Janega commented that determining whether the changes will be the
right vehicle to accomplish the community’s goals requires a great deal of time
and intense study on the part of staff, and she related some of the history of
the last round of zoning amendments, which included an extensive study to see
where the Village’s zoning regulations stood in relation to other comparable
communities.
Trustee Eilers suggested using the residential design guidelines consultant,
Mr. Winter, as a resource since he has national experience with zoning and
could bring a new perspective to the Village.
Mr. D’Onofrio and Mr. Norkus agreed, and said Mr. Winter would be discussing
zoning issues throughout the residential design guidelines process, to see if
there are any barriers to good design contained in the Zoning Ordinance.
After more discussion, it was decided that reviewing the Zoning Ordinance is a
priority, and that the right path to having that conversation was through the
residential design guidelines process, as well as having Mr. Winter come before
the Council in the near future for a discussion about zoning. Manager Williams
indicated that a Study Session possibly in spring or early summer would be an
appropriate time to invite Mr. Winter to talk to the Council.
4)   Part-time Economic Development Coordinator.
Trustee Tucker explained her proposal for hiring a part-time economic
development coordinator who could help not only to market the Village to
prospective businesses, but who also could act as liaison to the Chamber,
Winnetka Property Owners Association, BCDC and other Village boards.
Trustee Behles indicated that the BCDC has been discussing such a position
lately, in conjunction with their C-2 overlay recommendations, and that they
would be interested in the Council’s decision on the matter.
Trustee Eilers expressed an interest in the concept, but noted that he saw the
marketing aspect as the responsibility of building owners. He observed that the
job description, as outlined by Trustee Tucker, involves varied disciplines,
which could make it a difficult position to fill.
President Woodbury commented that there could be a need for someone to collect
data on the business districts, to help with fixing potential problems, and
that this was an issue for the BCDC to wrestle with.
Manager Williams agreed with Trustee Eilers about the marketing aspect, and
noted that the commercial vacancy rate is only 5%. He indicated that he
believes it is important to keep the Chamber engaged and involved, and that the
Village must be careful not to absorb too many of the Chamber’s functions.
5)   Draft: 2008-09 Capital Improvements Plan.
Mr. McKee summarized the outlook of the various Village funds, noting that
projects have now been categorized into those that cost less or more than
$50,000, based on the Auditor’s recommendation to set $50,000 as the dollar
amount threshold for capital project designation.
Mr. McKee proposed creating a separate Streetscape Fund to be funded by cash
transfers from the General Fund, so as to better evaluate potential street
improvement costs and financing options. He did not recommend an increase in
the electric rates but said an increase in the water rates would be necessary
to build up cash reserves due to inflation and lead service replacements.
Manager Williams said he would like the Council to give serious thought to
creating a Special Service Area to make the streetscape project sustainable,
and noted that the capital plan report was for planning purposes, to prepare
for budget meetings in February.
President Woodbury said the numbers contained in the agenda report are
placeholders, since the Council will decide at budget time which projects to
fund.
6)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing pending and probable litigation pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The Council adjourned into
Executive Session at 10:16 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:42 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers and Tucker. Absent: Trustee
Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
7)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell. The meeting adjourned at 10:43
p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
December 17, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 7, 2008 )
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 17, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:39 p.m. Present: Trustees
Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Behles. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately 35 persons in the
audience.
2)   Village President Remarks.
Village President Ed Woodbury recapped the meetings held to date on the
possible redevelopment of the post office site, noting the public input
received, the work of the Post Office Committee, and the Council’s goal to hear
all voices, find the core issues and determine whether or not the site should
be developed. He also noted that the Council’s challenge will be to build on
the foundation and legacy of Winnetka and to move the community forward so that
it is greater and more beautiful than when the Trustees found it. President
Woodbury suggested that the continuing dialog would review the comprehensive
plans, the Bennett Plan, parking studies dating back 50 years, the public input
that is the hallmark of Winnetka’s process, the vitality of the business
districts, parking, streetscape, overlay districts and affordable housing.
3)   Village Council Review of Staff Report.
President Woodbury announced that the Council would skip this item and proceed
to the Council’s questions, comments and discussion.
4)   Village Council Questions, Comments, and Discussion.
Trustee Eilers commented on the Post Office Committee’s report, the Village
Council’s role, what it may mean to revitalize the West Elm business district
and suggested the following policy guidelines for the Council’s consideration
[Note: his written comments are appended to the minutes.]:
∙   develop an 8,000 to 12,000 square foot retail building;
∙   the Village should continue to own the site and should develop and own the
retail building;
∙   the balance of the site could be open space and surface parking for now;
∙   no direct or indirect Village subsidy is justified for market rate housing,
and;
∙   Village subsidies may be appropriate only if necessary to achieve broader
community objectives.
Trustee Poor offered seven core principles to consider and expressed hope that
the Council could build on the principles, and move beyond the study and
discussion stage to an agreement:
∙   Village ownership is a critical part of any development, so the Village
will have the ability shape the future of the site, which is a key part of the
downtown area.
∙   The site should have a building that is no more than 2.5 stories high and
8,000 – 10,000 square feet, and that allows for a post office and some retail,
perhaps residential on the second floor, but also civic or other uses, such as
a restaurant.
∙   The development should open arms to Dwyer Park and allow for open space
similar to the Bennett Plan, which is as a wonderful aesthetic. Four corners of
retail detracts from openness.
∙   Maintain the existing surface level parking. Any underground parking should
be very limited, as its huge cost would have an impact on other Village
commitments.
∙   Design should be high quality and should blend with existing architecture.
∙   Use this development as an opportunity for environmental leadership in
design, energy efficiency and green building. Consider environmental
incentives, such as a bike depot to encourage bicycling.
∙   Encourage community gatherings, events and public uses, such as a
children’s library.
Trustee Tucker observed that the Council’s perspective is broad in scope and
what concerns her is quality of life. She added that the Council must be
fiscally responsible and noted there are many projects to be done. She said
that on the tours of developments in other, larger communities, she saw
examples of things Winnetka doesn’t want to do, such as little foot traffic, a
lot of unused retail space, and bulky buildings with a lot of impermeable
surface. She said she loved coming home to Winnetka’s unique pedestrian scale
and architectural image, and noted the timeless principle that less is more.
She proposed that development of the site offer the following:
∙   Set an example for other commercial buildings of the high quality of
design, scale and architectural style that we value in the community;
∙   Create shopping, a retail postal facility and a non-competing business
development that complements and focuses on existing local merchants;
∙   Village ownership of the land;
∙   Develop small, but well, and as an extension of Moffat Mall;
∙   A restaurant above, overlooking four corners of retail below;
∙   Maintain financial resources to phase in streetscape and parking in East
Elm;
∙   Develop in phases, build on the corner, sign up the post office for a new
facility now and go from there;
∙   Consider civic uses, so that if a building built at the northeast corner of
the site doesn’t work it can someday be converted to a library.
Trustee Ritchell stressed viewing development from a financially neutral
position, stating that he does not want to spend taxpayers’ money for two
levels of parking and that the development should minimize financial impact by
generating sales tax and, if condos are built, property tax. He also favored
retaining ownership of the land and agreed that the process should be taken in
steps. He said the post office should remain in business while something new is
built and saw an immediate need to negotiate with the post office. He added
that the development should be in small parcels, to retain the post office and
to provide new retail uses that would benefit residents, fill current gaps and
complement the Village, such as a drug, hardware, ice cream, or stationery
store. He does not view the concept of four corners of retail as either a
mantra or an objective, and added that parking spaces need to be retained and
considered throughout all phases of the development. He believed more open
space is fine as well, but would leave the amount of open space to the
architects.
Trustee Berger expressed agreement with her colleagues about retaining
ownership of the land and with the concept of less is more in a building that
would include a post office facility. She added that she embraced Trustee
Poor’s suggestions about an environmentally friendly development with a
community gathering space that is open to Dwyer Park, which she felt could be
accomplished with a library on the site.
During further discussion, the Council made the following comments and
suggestions, but did not make any final determinations:
∙   Ownership and development control: (1) ownership of the land was a
consistent theme; (2) Village should retain control of the land and
development, but reach out to builders for construction; (3) Village ownership
would allow Village to develop the site and lease spaces to create an asset
that would offset other capital costs and diversify housing; and (4) further
discussion is necessary about staff that would be needed to manage the
development and find tenants.
∙   Size: (1) clarification is needed on the size of the development and the
residential component; (2) post office’s requirements will drive the size of
the building.
∙   Uses: (1) retail on the first floor may increase foot traffic; (2) uses
other than condominiums could be discussed for a second floor; (3) consider a
library or a civic building and post office with adequate parking for the
business district; (4) post office has requested a 15-year lease with options
to renew for a total of 30 years; (5) parking could be placed on the south end
of the site to transition into the neighborhood.
∙   Library: (1) support and assist the Library Board if it determines that
further discussion into the possibility of a library on the site is desirable;
(2) consider a children’s library section on the site, instead of moving the
entire library; (3) there is interest in receiving a proposal from the library,
but there’s a short time frame because of the Post Office lease.
∙   Affordable Housing: (1) should be discussed if there is a residential
component; (2) could justify a subsidy for underground parking.
∙   Financial: (1) Village subsidy for the cost of underground parking for
residential development would be justified only if there were an affordable
housing component or other public uses.
∙   Timing and phased development: (1) rather than slowing down the process
waiting for a decision about the library, retain open space, with Moffatt Mall
expansion and parking, for other possibilities in the future; (2) because of
the short time frame with the post office, consider alternate uses that add to
the community.
∙   Environment: (1) make environmental concerns a goal and aim high; (2)
provide environmental leadership in terms of energy efficiency and
conservation;
∙   Set an example with this development for other developers so that they can
see the type of construction Winnetka wants for the community.
Members of the audience then offered their comments:
∙   Ann Wilder, 1096 Spruce, said she didn’t understand what the rush was. She
expressed hope that the post office doesn’t drive the whole project and asked
about other locations for the post office.
∙   Paul Dunn, 988 Hill Road, member of Ad Hoc Post Office Committee commented
that it was interesting to hear the Council’s opinions. He pointed out that by
the time the development is completed, every building will be built
environmentally well. He said he was surprised by the Council’s desire to
retain ownership, and cautioned against playing the role of developer, adding
that the Village should own properties integral to conducting its business, and
that being a landlord is a very difficult thing to do and is not something
Village employees should be engaged in. He added that the Village can control
the development through development agreements and other contracts, so it
doesn’t need to retain ownership to maintain development control.
∙   Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce, asked how the Council came to the conclusion that
revitalization of the West Elm business district is necessary and what has been
the premise on which so much time has been spent?
Responding to Ms. Wilder’s comments, President Woodbury and the Trustees stated
that other locations for the post office are a possibility, but a decision must
be made before the current lease expires and other locations must include space
for parking and drop off boxes. They added that discussions have been held for
30 months, and that the Council has had 6 or 7 hours of discussion and would
like to set a direction before three Trustees retire in May.
∙   John Janssen, 199 Church Road, asked the Village to provide up to $100,000
in financial support to form what he described as a citizen consensus group
made up of people representing 12 to 14 interest groups, such as the Garden
Club, Village, Library, etc, which would use volunteers as resource persons. He
said he envisions a mixed use building that would contain the library, coffee
shop, and retail for a civic use, possibly named the Winnetka Commons. He
suggested it could be a showplace for green architecture, gathering places, art
shows and lectures, and would tie Village Hall and Dwyer Park together. He said
the consensus group would report regularly to the Council and to the citizens,
working together with the Library Board and Village. He said that once the
consensus group is authorized, it should be able to come up with an idea
quickly after the first of the year. In answer to Trustees’ questions, Mr.
Janssen said the group had not yet been formed, but that he had identified
people who may want to participate. He added that he wants to build on the
process begun by the Post Office Committee.
Responding to Mr. Janssen, Trustee Tucker said she was hesitant, particularly
with the requested funding, and thought the most productive discussion would be
with the Council, since the Council can take action. She said she was not sure
another group of residents would accomplish more and expressed concern that the
Council may set a precedent by funding another group, especially where there is
opposition, and asked whether the Council would then be giving funds to every
group who didn’t agree with the recommendations.
Trustee Eilers asked if the library is doing a long range vision of what
libraries will be in the future.
Mr. Janssen (a member of the Library Board) said that the Library Board is not
actively looking at this question and does not feel that there is public
support for going forward with such groundwork as consultants, specifications.
He explained that another issue is that the library district serves Winnetka
and Northfield and that residents of the two towns don’t want to pay for each
other’s buildings, but suggested that Northfield wouldn’t have to be asked for
money if Winnetka paid for the building.
President Woodbury responded that the consensus of this Council is to keep the
library building discussions alive, but that those discussions would be with
the Library Board and the Village Council would not presume to tell the Library
Board what to do.
∙   Pat Horne, 791 Bryant, expressed concern about the hard feelings the issue
has generated, asked that everyone try to do the best for Winnetka because
that’s the important issue, noted that 8,000 to 10,000 square feet would not
cover the 2-acre site, and added that whatever the Council concludes would be
fine.
∙   Edith Howell, 277 Ridge, agreed with all of Mr. Janssen’s comments, adding
that a consensus group would allow time for the Village to have all of the
pieces in place before beginning anything. She felt that the residents have not
completely understood the mixed use development, its consequences and cost, and
said she wants to keep the character of Winnetka and avoid the urbanization
seen in other communities.
∙   Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, expressed concern over the ultimate
development, stating that we will be building to suit the post office, which we
know wants 3000 square feet, and that further discussion will give us more
input about their needs. He said it might be useful to issue an RFP seeking
development proposals for the 2-acre parcel, with 15,000 square feet for
development and the parking we require, and that maybe someone will come up
with something new.
∙   Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka Avenue, asserted that there are 25 vacant
stores or offices in the business districts and that Hubbard Woods is “dying on
the vine.” She thought there could be a civic building that incorporates the
park, but the Park District, Garden Club, and Library were not part of the 34
month process. She thought that no group was put together to come up with the
best use for the land and didn’t think retail and office space was the best
use.
Trustee Poor informed Mrs. Stevens that a consensus was being formed around a
lot of what she had just described and what had been said during the meeting.
President Woodbury concluded the discussion by saying that the most important
thing is the process and creating a dialogue. He stated he has long thought
that the Bennett Plan was right and that a modest development at the corner of
Elm and Chestnut is the right thing to do. He expressed agreement with the
comments about 2.5 stories and the rest of it, but added his main thought is of
the catalytic effect the development could have, not only for properties,
landlords and development, but also for the downtown. He added his belief that
the strength in Winnetka is the ability to talk about issues and hash them out
in forums such as this, knowing that we can’t agree on all points, but still
have that open dialogue and try to keep going forward. He said he would like to
see a modest development that incorporates Dwyer Park, is consistent with what
the Village is trying to do, and is in the right context for the legacy of
Winnetka.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles. The meeting adjourned
at 9:45 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
December 18, 2007
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: January 15, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 18, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Behles. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, and approximately 8
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   January 1, 2008, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury announced that this
meeting was cancelled and that warrant list approval would be considered at a
special meeting on January 8 th .
b)   January 8, 2008, Special Meeting and Study Session. All of the Council
members present indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   January 15, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 20, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   December 1, 2007, Special Tour. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   December 4, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   December 8, 2007, Special Tour. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
e)   December 11, 2007, Study Session. Trustee Poor proposed amending the
minutes to reflect that the zoning review discussion ended with the
understanding that Mr. Winter would not simply evaluate the Zoning Ordinance as
part of the design guidelines process, but he would also initiate a zoning
assessment before the completion of his design guidelines report. Manager
Williams indicated that staff was hoping to bring Mr. Winter before the Council
in February, if possible. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to
approve the minutes, as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-27-2007 – 981 Gordon Terrace – Zoning Variations –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for four zoning variations to
permit the expansion of the existing nonconforming detached garage. He
described the Subject Property as a corner lot with a unique trapezoidal shape,
a narrow north front yard which is adjacent to a public right-of-way and
parking lot, and an east rear yard which abuts the commercial properties that
front on Green Bay Road.
Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that the Zoning Board of Appeals had recommended
approval of the variations request by a vote of four to two, with one
abstention because a member of the board came in late and missed part of the
hearing.
After a short discussion, the Council agreed to defer to the ZBA’s
recommendation. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to introduce
Ordinance M-27-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   Ordinance M-26-2007 – Disposal of Surplus Electrical Equipment –
Introduction. Mr. Keys reported that there are currently two surplus volt
capacitor cans and miscellaneous support racks in inventory, as well as three
damaged electrical transformers. He observed that the surplus material will
probably never be utilized, due to the Village’s current electrical
configuration, and that the damaged transformers are no longer operable. He
suggested that the Council authorize the Village Manager to dispose of the
items as he determines is best, due to the small net worth of the items.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
26-2007. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance M-24-2007 – 2007 Tax Levy – Adoption. There being no further
questions or comments, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to
adopt ordinance
M-24-2007, increasing the Village’s property tax levy for 2007 by a total of
4.7%. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
d)   Ordinance M-25-2007 – 2007 Tax Abatement – Adoption. There being no
further questions or comments, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell,
moved to adopt ordinance M-25-2007, abating the taxes levied to pay the
principal and interest on the General Obligation Refunding Bonds, Series 2003.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
e)   Resolution R-50-2007 – Nextel, Cell Site Lease Agreement – Adoption. Mr.
Keys reported that the Village and Nextel have tentatively agreed to terms for
a new license agreement for cellular communication antennas on the smokestack
of the Water & Electric Plant, and noted that this is the third of four cell
site license agreements that staff has been renegotiating.
There being no further comments, Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell,
moved to adopt Resolution R-50-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Behles.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Kim Chatain, 205 Forest and a business owner in East Elm, voiced her concerns
about possible redevelopment in East Elm, commercial vacancies in Hubbard
Woods, and the scale of redevelopment for the Post Office site. She said she
thought unique boutiques are what draw people to the Village and asked the
Council to consider a balanced development of the Post Office site, and to keep
it as green and open as possible.
Peggy Martay, 635 Blackthorn, expressed concern about including retail space in
the redevelopment of the Post Office site in light of the current vacancy rate.
Theresa Hutchins, 1170 Asbury, expressed a preference for a civic building at
the Post Office site, rather than retail development, and offered to help raise
money for it.
President Woodbury indicated that the consensus at the Post Office meeting the
previous night was to do a scaled-back development, adding that the Council’s
aim is not to select a particular plan but to identify goals and objectives. He
observed that after taking all matters under consideration and trying to
balance the competing and sometimes conflicting input, the Council will try to
pinpoint the important objectives at its January 8 th Study Session. He
indicated that common ground has been identified, namely a large interest in
open space and a modest development of some sort, and encouraged all interested
residents to attend the January 8 th meeting to hear the Council’s discussions
as they work through the issues in more detail.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1537 and 1538. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1537 in the amount of $764,111.66 and Warrant List No. 1538 in the amount of
$803,504.49. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Behles.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Eilers invited all interested people to attend the next
night’s Plan Commission meeting to hear the discussion on the Overlay District.
[Drafter’s Note: The meeting was subsequently cancelled.]
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams indicated that the Winnetka Report was delivered
a little late to get the word out about the December 17 th Post Office meeting,
but did make it out in time to publicize the Post Office Study Session in
January.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Charles Page to the Design
Review Board for a full term. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell
moved to approve the appointment. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Behles.
Executive Session. None.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Ayes: Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles. The meeting adjourned
at 8:35 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
2008
January 8, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL MEETING & STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 7, 2008)
A record of a legally convened special meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 8, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, and approximately 45 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1539 and 1540. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1539 in the amount of $1,173,559.94 and Warrant List No. 1540 in the amount of
$614,676.28. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Adjournment of Special Meeting.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The special meeting adjourned at 7:40 p.m.
6)   Convene Study Session.
Call to Order. President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:41 p.m.
Present: Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb
Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director
of Community Development Brian Norkus, and approximately 45 persons in the
audience.
a)   Post Office
   i)   Draft Planning & Development Principles for PO Site. President Woodbury
announced that tonight’s discussion was a continuation of the dialog that was
started at the December 17 th meeting. He indicated that the Trustees’ comments
from the December meeting had been put together into a draft document of core
principles.
Trustee Behles, who was absent from the December 17 th meeting, read a
statement into the record. The statement is appended to the minutes.
The Council had an in-depth conversation about the core principles, in which
the size and scale of a possible structure were further clarified, with
President Woodbury cautioning against trying to design the site at this early
juncture. Affordable housing, retaining a retail post office facility and the
issue of a civic use for the site were also discussed.
It was agreed that Trustee Behles’ comments about providing affordable housing
options and including Dwyer Park and the Park District in the planning process
would be added to the draft of core principles as points #9 and #10,
respectively.
President Woodbury announced that there would be a short break before the
public comment portion of the evening began.
   ii)   Council Discussion: Next Steps. President Woodbury observed that the
core principles would be further refined, with the intent of using them to
formulate a resolution at a future date.
   iii)   Council General Discussion. Deferred until after Public Comment. See
below.
   iv)   Public Comment.
      Several persons in the audience commented on the proposed redevelopment,
as follows:
      Suzanne Canfield, 956 Westmoor: Garden Guild prefers a building centered
on the block rather than a building on one corner; if development begins in the
NE corner, it might not stop there; a central, flagship civic building is more
likely to preserve the beauty of the parcel.
      Rob Apatoff, 730 Ardsley: PowerPoint presentation; do not “chop up” the
land; consider a civic complex alongside a small mixed-use space; Council
should take an additional 90-120 days to consider the issues.
      Rob Schoder, 330 Locust: He hears concern and confusion about the right
way to execute a strategy with so many disparate elements; recommends
revisiting the plan.
      Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka: Disagrees with Community Development’s
report of number of retail vacancies; displayed a map she created showing
retail spaces she believes are vacant; consider moving the Post Office to the
East Elm business district, which is more in need of revitalization.
      Kim Chatain, 205 Forest: Opposes raising taxes for creating retail space;
slow down the process; consider more options for green space.
      Tony Mars, 736 Green Bay Road: Winnetka library facility is substandard
when compared with other communities, such as Evanston and Glenview; a new
library on the site will draw people to Winnetka and keep residents from
spending elsewhere.
      Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce: there’s substantial feeling in the Village
opposing a retail structure on the site; table the planning and development
principles and give matter further consideration.
      Susie Shabica, 326 Ridge: Supports library option and a design
competition for a green library model.
      Julie Windsor, 1479 Tower Road: Village needs a commercial recycling
program; wants Village to do “something special” on the Post Office site.
      Cindy Dillon, 649 Locust: Wants to know about commercial vacancies in
relation to other North Shore communities; opposes housing on the site; favors
non-retail use.
      Carole Smith, 503 Hawthorn, President of Winnetka/Northfield Library
Board: Library is a separate entity from the Village and encompasses Winnetka
and Northfield; residents of both villages opposed a new library, even if built
with donated funds, because of increased operating costs; new library facility
would need to be 34,000 to 46,000 square feet and could cost $20 million; new
library would require more taxes just to pay for the larger staff and
collection.
      Susan Boatman, 636 Garland, member of North Suburban Library District
Board: Agrees with Ms. Smith; Library Board is constrained by Caucus plank and
by opposition from Northfield residents; current library will be obsolete in
ten years if it can’t accommodate new technology and add teen space.
      John Jansson, 199 Church Road, Vice-President of Library Board, but
speaking as a private citizen: Reads a statement into record; is forming a
citizen “consensus” group representing 12 to 14 interest groups in the Village
to produce a plan for the block by fall 2008; asks Council to support citizen
group instead of pursuing mixed-use/retail; library needs more space; consensus
group will propose mixed used plan that includes library, post office and
architecturally significant civic center; asks Village to authorize financial
and staff resources for citizens group.
      Dick Kates, 1326 Tower: Is disappointed with the consultant reports;
recommends new consultants with different perspectives; fourth corner of retail
at Elm and Chestnut won’t solve retail problem; agrees with Library Board
members that bond issue is needed for a new library but won’t be approved; no
need for new civic facility; Park District and the Community House already
serve that need.
      Sherry Molitor, 1133 Scott: Hopes Village Board and Library District will
take a step back and consider a new library.
      Vickie Apatoff, 730 Ardsley: Comprehensive Plan recognized the library as
a critical anchor for the West Elm business district, and recommended that the
library district’s needs be considered when converting any institutional
property that may become available; notes Post Office Committee report
statement that relocating public library to the site offers an opportunity to
realize a central feature of the original Bennett Plan; encourages the Library
Board and Village to explore this alternative together.
      Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood: Library can expand without moving; agrees
with Garden Club on the question of retail; suggests renting current space to
the post office facility on a year-to-year basis to give more time to the
process.
   iii)   Council General Discussion. President Woodbury responded to the
various points that were raised. He noted that the Draft Core Principles state
that the Village should not provide any subsidy for market-based development.
Regarding retail vacancies, he stated that the Village doesn’t have comparison
data, but that the Chamber of Commerce could do a survey and added that,
whatever the correct number, retail businesses go through cycles, market and
physical conditions come into play, as well as the quality of the business and
competition. Mr. Woodbury expressed agreement with Mr. Kates’ observations
about bringing in fresh consultants. Regarding size of development, he noted
that what is being proposed is clearly less than the 36,000 square feet the
report says would fit on the site. Finally, he observed that issues related to
how Winnetka markets itself are best discussed with the BCDC and Chamber of
Commerce.
Trustee Eilers said his recollection of the December 17 th discussion was not
that adding more commercial space would necessarily revitalize West Elm, but
that a retail post office facility is necessary for West Elm and would be good
for traffic volume. He observed that a limited retail facility alongside the
retail post office is acceptable, but there was no consensus that it would be
on the corner. He added that he did not wish to lose the post office while
waiting for the use of a civic center, and that the strategy was devised to
preserve the retail post office location. Regarding the library, he said only
the Library Board can decide to relocate and that as a citizen, he expects that
Board to provide a vision of what libraries will be in the future, and how a
new building would meet that vision.
Trustee Ritchell commented on the vacancy issue, noting the number can change
daily, that the Village doesn’t control the many contributing factors and the
current rate did not shock him. He agreed that a retail post office would be a
retail anchor.
President Woodbury commented at length about the library issue, noting that the
Council has heard and listened to the PO Committee’s recommendations. He
stressed that the Library Board is a separate taxing body and that the Council
would encourage and support discussion about a new library in the proper venue,
i.e., in the Library Boardroom. He commented on the role of the Caucus in the
process, but observed that its recommendations are one piece of the process,
and not necessarily the final or correct answer. He added that, although he is
a citizen, he has to be very cautious when commenting on a public matter
because he is a Board member. He urged the Library Board not to be constrained
by citizens or the Caucus in pursuing its goals.
Trustees Tucker and Berger agreed with President Woodbury’s assessment. Trustee
Berger added that the Council should expect the Library Board to do its job,
address the concerns that have been raised, and report back to the citizens of
Winnetka.
Trustee Poor also agreed with the foregoing comments. He commented that it
would be counterproductive to put the project on hold for an indefinite period
of time, noting progress since the December 17 th meeting in setting some good
benchmarks and adding that the $20 million bond issue a new library could
require might not pass.
Trustee Behles agreed that the Council can’t wait indefinitely, that the
library option is out of the Council’s control, and said the process needs to
move forward.
President Woodbury announced that the Council will likely next consider the
Post Office redevelopment in March and notice would be posted on the Village’s
website.
b)   Affordable Housing.
   i)   Status Report. Mr. Norkus provided the following status report:
      ∙   The Plan Commission hired an Affordable Housing consultant in May
2007 and assigned a 3-member working group to help steer the process.
      ∙   The Affordable Housing Subcommittee held a series of focus groups
with institutions, residents, non-profit groups and faith-based organizations
to help determine community concerns.
      ∙   The Consultants provided the Plan Commission with a series of
strategies and tools Winnetka could employ to address its affordable housing
needs.
      ∙   A survey was sent to the Village’s public sector employees, to gather
information on existing employee housing/commuting patterns, and to gauge
interest in housing affordability in the Village.
      ∙   Two identical public workshops are scheduled at Village Hall to
garner public participation in the process: Saturday morning, January 26 th and
Tuesday evening, January 29 th .
      ∙   Estimated completion of the Plan Commission’s draft plan is in March.
Trustee Tucker stated that she believed in the concept of affordable housing
but was concerned that the standards need to be looked at from a Winnetka
perspective, with consideration given to underlying property values. She
expressed her hope that the final plan would reflect a customized Winnetka
standard, and added that preserving smaller houses on smaller lots would
maintain housing diversity and affordability. She expressed her hope that the
new Council will consider zoning, overbuilding and teardowns when it prepares
the Village’s Affordable Housing Plan.
Mr. Norkus observed that the Plan Commission’s efforts are focused on the
multifamily and commercial areas, but that it is difficult to look at the
affordable housing situation without taking a look at single family houses.
Attorney Janega confirmed that the focus of the current Affordable Housing Plan
is on multifamily and mixed use buildings in commercial areas. She also
explained that the definition of affordability under State law is based on
income levels and monthly cash outlay for housing costs.In response to a
question from a resident, Manager Williams explained that, because the Village
is home rule unit, it is not bound by the statutory definition of affordability
and can adopt a definition of affordability that reflects the Village's unique
characteristics.
President Woodbury encouraged residents and the Trustees to attend one of the
Affordable Housing workshops.
7)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
8)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Study Session adjourned at
11:05 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
January 15, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 19, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 15, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Public Works
Director Steve Saunders, and approximately 9 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   February 7, 2008 Regular Meeting & Budget Meeting. President Woodbury
noted that the February 5 th meeting was moved to the 7 th due to the election.
All of the Council members present indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 12, 2008, Study Session & Budget Meeting. All of the Council
members present indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   Feb 19, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend
d)   February 26, 2008, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
except Trustee Behles, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 18, 2007, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-26-2007 – Disposal of Surplus Electrical Equipment – Adoption.
President Woodbury reported that the Subject Ordinance, introduced at the
December 17 th Council meeting, would authorize the Village Manager to dispose
of surplus electrical equipment.
There being no further comments or questions, Trustee Ritchell, seconded by
Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance M-26-2007. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance M-27-2007 – 981 Gordon Terrace – Zoning Variations – Adoption.
President Woodbury reviewed the request for four zoning variations to permit
the expansion of the existing nonconforming detached garage on the Subject
Property.
There being no further comments, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger,
moved to adopt Ordinance M-27-2007. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance MC-14-2007 – Leash Law – Adoption. President Woodbury reviewed
the Council’s discussion of the Leash Law Ordinance when it was introduced at
the November 6 th Council meeting, noting that there have been subsequent
conversations with the Park District to try to coordinate efforts in Winnetka’s
parks.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to remove Ordinance MC-14-
2007 from the table. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Attorney Janega explained the 5 different options the Council could consider,
including setting no maximum length, or maximum leash lengths of from 8 to 10
feet, and allowing or prohibiting retractable leashes.
There was a discussion about the various lengths of retractable leashes.
Trustee Poor commented that they are very popular and outlawing them would be
difficult to enforce.
Trustee Berger favored prohibiting retractable leashes and limiting leash
length to 10 feet, for safety and also to show the Park District that the
animals would be under control in the Village’s parks.
Trustee Tucker agreed with Trustee Berger and asked Chief DeLopez for his
perspective.
Chief DeLopez responded that the goal is to increase safety and not impose
hardships, and observed that an animal on a 20-foot retractable leash is not
firmly under control. He added that the NWMC survey showed no consistency on
retractable leashes and leash lengths, but said he supported Trustee Berger’s
view, and urged the Council to limit the length of retractable leashes.
President Woodbury suggested passing a leash law that is silent on the notion
of retractable and maximum length, and letting people use their judgment, and
if it doesn’t work, the Police Chief can report those findings to the Council.
Trustee Ritchell agreed with President Woodbury, and added that the Police
Department should not be in the position of having to measure leashes.
There was further discussion about leash length and types of leashes, with no
clear consensus emerging on the options that were presented.
Angie DeMars, 1216 Willow and Noah’s Ark Pet Supply owner, said that she has
seen retractable leashes used irresponsibly, but favors passing a leash law
without prohibiting longer or retractable leashes, as Winnetka’s lots are small
and dogs need some freedom of movement.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend Ordinance MC-14-
2007, to include Option E, allowing retractable leashes and no maximum length
specified. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: President Woodbury,
Trustees Behles, Eilers and Ritchell. Nays: Trustees Tucker, Berger and Poor.
Absent: None.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-14-
2007 as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Eilers, and Ritchell. Nays: Trustees Tucker, Berger
and Poor. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance M-1-2008 – Special Use Permit: ––––906-908 Green Bay Road–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the request for a special use permit to
allow a health club facility to operate on the Subject Property. He indicated
that the proposed tenant, Pilates Barre, would be located at the rear of the
building, while the front 71.5 feet will be occupied by two retail tenants. He
reported that the petitioner had filed two previous special use permit
applications. One was withdrawn before it came before the Council, and the
second was withdrawn after an ordinance was introduced in September, 2007.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the results of the KLOA parking study, which found that
there was adequate on-street parking for the facility, and observed that both
the Plan Commission and ZBA voted unanimously to recommend in favor of granting
the special use permit.
Trustee Ritchell voiced concerns about the facility possibly taking parking
spaces away from commuters in the morning hours, and Mr. Saunders explained
that the lower level of the two-level Scott Avenue parking deck is assigned to
commuters and the upper level is set aside for employees and customers of the
business district.
Mr. D’Onofrio and the Council discussed parking and aspects of the interior
layout, and Mr. D’Onofrio introduced Josh Braun, the applicant.
Mr. Braun observed that one of the retail tenants, Ann Sacks, anticipates
opening in a few weeks, and the other retail space will be rented to E Street
Denim, which should bring in a lot of foot traffic. He noted that the Pilates
Barre is currently operating across the street, and wants to expand its
business.
Attorney Janega indicated that the ordinance was a preliminary draft and would
be fine-tuned for adoption.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance M-1-
2008. By voice vote, the motion carried
e)   Ordinance M-2-2008 – Special Use Permit: 564 ––––Lincoln–– – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the request for a special use permit to allow a medical
office to occupy a portion of the first floor of the Subject Property beginning
at 35.67 feet from the public street. He indicated that the space has been
vacant for 18 months and that the medical offices of the applicant, Dr. Roma
Franzia are currently located across the street, outside of the retail overlay
district. He noted that the front portion of the building will contain a
medical spa, which is a permitted use in the overlay district, and that the
entrance into the medical office will be a separate entrance off the courtyard
of the Subject Property.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the medical spa is not the subject of the special use
application, as it is considered a beauty parlor-type use, which is allowed in
the C-2 overlay district, and does generate sales tax revenue.
Trustee Behles commented on the doorway connecting the medical spa and doctor’s
office, and asked if there was any concern that the spa could be co-opted as an
extension of the medical office.
Mr. D’Onofrio said there is no concern, because the two businesses are very
different and staff feels the special use application stands on its own merits.
The applicant, Dr. Roma Franzia, indicated that she was a solo practitioner,
and needed more space and better access for handicapped persons and parents
with strollers, as well as a more professional and modern building.
Richard Lally, owner of Designs of the Interior on the other side of the
Galleria courtyard, urged approval of the special use permit, noting that the
vacant space is bad for business, and that Dr. Franzia’s office would bring
people to the East Elm retail district.
Greer Braun, 850 Bell Lane, also expressed support, to keep the business
district viable.
Jim Sayegh, owner of the Subject Property, report that the residents of the
building are support the application and offered that the requested special use
is a creative way to use a difficult space.
Trustee Eilers observed the consistency of the request with what the BCDC is
trying to achieve in the retail overlay district, and added that the building’s
configuration lends itself to the special use.
Trustee Behles agreed with Trustee Eilers, and said the fact that a medical
office is a permitted special use and is consistent with the goals of the
business district helps him to overcome the difficulty of the 35-foot distance
from the street frontage.
Trustee Tucker asked Attorney Janega if she thought the request was an erosion
of the retail space point and if a precedent was being set, especially in light
of a similar request in June of 2006 which was denied.
Attorney Janega distinguished the current application from the prior one for
557 Chestnut Street, noting that the medical office is a permitted special use,
the space has been vacant for a long time, only the interior and not structural
walls will be modified, access to the office is not on the street front, the
front of the space will be a retail use, and the medical office will generate
traffic that will benefit the retail businesses in the area. She indicated that
she was comfortable with providing findings that meet the special use
objectives.
Manager Williams pointed out that 557 Chestnut is in the heart of the business
district, and this location is more remote, which is also a deciding factor.
Responding to questions about whether the special use permit should run with
the property or revert back to the retail overlay if the applicant vacates the
premises, Attorney Janega stated that typically medical office uses run with
the land, but that the Council could make the special use expire if the tenant
moves out.
As there was no immediate consensus on that issue, Manager Williams suggested
that Attorney Janega present options to the Council when the ordinance comes up
for adoption.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance M-2-
2008. By voice vote, the motion carried
f)   Resolution R-1-2008 – 2008 MFT Transfer for Maintenance of Streets –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders reported that the State imposes a tax on the sale of
motor fuel and distributes some of the funds back to the municipalities. He
said it has been Village policy in past years to allocate $125,000 from the
Motor Fuel Tax funds towards maintaining streets, and recommended doing so
again this year.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-1-
2008, authorizing the transfer of $125,000 from the MFT Fund to the General
Fund to offset costs for street maintenance. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Resolution R-2-2008 – Westmoor Commons: Townhome Condominium Association
(932/934/936 Westmoor Road) – Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that all
condominium developments have to comply with the Illinois Condominium Property
Act in order to sell the units, and indicated that staff has reviewed the
bylaws and made sure the Village’s Code requirements are being met.
There being no questions or comments, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-2-2008. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
h)   Resolution R-3-2008 – High Excess Liability Pool Renewal – Adoption.
Manager Williams reported that at its November 6, 2007, meeting, the Council
approved Resolution R-38-2007, approving membership in the High Excess
Liability Pool (HELP), which reduces exposure to severe losses. He indicated
that the pool attorney recently informed members that he had made an error
while drafting the resolutions passed by the various municipalities. He
reviewed the wording that had been erroneously included in the first
resolution, and recommended passing a revised resolution to incorporate the
necessary changes.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-3-2008.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
i)   Resolution R-4-2008 – Cell Site Agreement with New Cingular Wireless PCS –
Adoption. Mr. Keys reported that the current license agreement with New
Cingular Wireless PCS had expired on October 31, 2007, and that the lease
extension is due to expire on January 15, 2008. He indicated that staff and New
Cingular Wireless PCS have tentatively agreed to terms, which are similar to
the three cell site license agreements which staff has renegotiated in recent
months, but that there were several items proposed by Cingular which required
direction from the Council.
Attorney Janega indicated that most of the changes Cingular was requesting were
things that affect the Village’s rights of ownership of the property, including
a softening of the language that imposes a flat requirement of the licensees to
refrain from interfering with Village operations. She said such a provision
would invite contention and make the contract difficult to administer, and that
staff recommends the Council not accept the proposed changes and instead move
forward with the Village’s standardized approach to license administration.
Manager Williams observed that municipal use of communications have
consistently trumped any other uses, and recommended against deviating from
that policy.
The Trustees agreed with Attorney Janega, and President Woodbury indicated that
the agreement would be approved substantially in the form provided, with staff
having latitude to negotiate to protect the Village’s interests.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-4-2008
substantially in the form presented, subject to changes incorporated by the
Village Attorney. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1541 and 1542. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1541 in the amount of $713,626.22 and Warrant List No. 1542 in the amount of
$338,283.27. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Cenotaph War Memorial Restoration Study & Plans/Specifications. Mr.
Saunders reported that the War Memorial, or Cenotaph, in the Village Green was
completed in 1928, and that the Winnetka post of the American Legion originally
committed to maintaining the structure, but that the post is no longer
operative in the Village.
Mr. Saunders observed that given the unique historical perspective of the
structure, staff believes that an expert in historic restoration should be
retained to assess the current condition of the Cenotaph and to develop plans
to restore it. He said after discussions with two firms that specialize in this
type of work, US Heritage Group submitted a joint proposal for assessment and
design services with Bailey Edward Design.
Trustee Berger indicated that, even though the Cenotaph is an important part of
Winnetka history, she would like to see a moratorium on additional studies
until some of the many projects currently before the Council are completed.
Trustee Eilers said he was supportive of Trustee Berger’s comments, but noted
that the structure is deteriorating rapidly and the Council needs to proceed
with the process of restoration.
Trustee Behles commented that the Cenotaph is a complex arrangement, that it
would be a costly and difficult process to restore the memorial, and
recommended moving forward cautiously.
Trustee Tucker agreed with Trustee Berger and asked if the project can wait.
Mr. Saunders said it was up to the Council to decide on funding the study, but
that nothing is gained by postponing the assessment, and that barring
unforeseen complications, the scope of the project could probably be completed
in the upcoming fiscal year.
Mr. Phil Hosa, 605 Cherry, distributed a handout showing pictures of the
deteriorating condition of the Cenotaph, and said he supported the Council in
whatever it decided to do and would do what he could to help with fundraising
efforts to help offset the cost of rehabilitation.
Trustee Poor said he was in favor of moving forward with the study, as a way to
honor what the Cenotaph means to the Village, and added the details of the
costs and fundraising efforts could be worked out at a later date.
Trustee Behles thanked Mr. Hosa for championing the issue of the Cenotaph and
credited him with getting the matter before the Council.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award a contract to US
Heritage group to perform a condition study and develop design specifications
for the restoration of the Cenotaph, in an amount not to exceed $62,520. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Refuse Truck Bid Revision: Bid #07-007. Mr. Saunders reported that in July
of 2007, the Council awarded a bid to RNOW, Inc. to purchase a new Freightliner
M2 chassis and Pak-Mor 225B refuse body. He indicated that RNOW recently
informed the Village that although the chassis is manufactured, Pak-Mor has not
yet programmed the body on their assembly line, and indicated that RNOW has
severed their relationship with Pak-Mor because delivery time and warranty
service have fallen to unacceptable levels.
Mr. Saunders said RNOW will honor their alternate bid for a different packer
body, which, although it has some alternate specifications, they would not be a
problem for Winnetka’s refuse trucks, and some features might even be superior
to the Pak-Mor body. He noted that the truck is necessary to replace a model
that is nearing the end of its useful life and recommended that the bid be
changed to the alternate, in order to expedite delivery of the new truck.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to void Purchase Order
#42485, pursuant to Bid 07-007, for $114,201. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize the purchase
of a Freightliner M-2-106 chassis and LoadMaster Excel refuse packer to RNOW,
Inc. pursuant to Bid 07-007, Alternate #2, for $110, 720. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Cable Bid, Bid #07-018. Mr. Keys reported that a request for bids was
issued for the purchase and delivery of cable for April 1, 2008, through March
31, 2009, and that four vendors responded. He observed that prices are subject
to change because of fluctuations in metal prices, and recommended ordering
both primary and secondary cable at this time from Okonite.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to award bid 07-018 to
Okonite for the purchase of 600-volt secondary cable and 15-kv primary cable at
the unit prices for a total amount not to exceed $783,000, subject to all of
the conditions stated in the request for bid. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Transformer Bid, Bid #07-019. Mr. Keys explained that unit prices for
transformers are evaluated differently from cable, using the material cost plus
the life cycle cost of the unit, and that the Village assesses the life cycle
cost to determine the best prices. He noted that prices are subject to
escalation, again due to metal costs, and recommended awarding the bid for
single-phase transformers to Resco (Ermco) and the bid for three-phase
transformers to Wesco (ABB).
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award bid 07-019 to Resco
(Ermco) at the unit prices bid for single-phase transformers in an amount not
to exceed $50,000 and to Wesco (ABB) at the unit prices bid for three-phase
transformers in an amount not to exceed $100,000. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Change Order #1 – Willow Road–– Study. Manager Williams reported that a
proposal is contained in the agenda packet from the Willow Road consultant, The
Lakota Group, in response to a request by the Willow Road Steering Committee to
expand their scope of work to include five additional meetings before preparing
their report. He indicated that the State is expected to reimburse the Village
for the additional costs.
Trustee Behles noted that the meetings have been scheduled, and will conclude
the work of the Steering Committee, and said that both communities have
unanimously agreed to move forward.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to sign the proposal from Lakota for additional services on the Willow
Road study, for an amount not to exceed $7,600. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Eilers announced that the Plan Commission will hold two
community workshops on Affordable housing on January 26 th and January 29 th
and invited all interested residents to attend.
   ii)   Trustee Berger reported that the NWMC had received an update from
Commonwealth Edison relating to the August storm, and added that their
legislative brunch, scheduled for February 9, will focus on public safety
pension reforms.
   iii)   Trustee Behles observed that the work of the Willow Road Steering
Committee will be completed in March.
c)   Attorney. None
d)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that the Council will hold budget
sessions starting in late February and concluding by mid-March, and the notices
would be posted on the Village’s website, and added that a preliminary budget
is available at the library or at the Manager’s office. He reported that SWANCC
has developed a compact fluorescent light bulb recycling program, which will be
implemented soon. Lastly, he noted that the Finance Director has estimated the
August storm costs to be close to $600,000, and that the Village should get a
75% reimbursement from FEMA.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing board appointments, the sale or lease of real estate, and pending
and probable litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(3), 2(c)(6), and 2(c)(11)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Berger seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 10:10 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:02 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
11:03 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
February 7, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: February 19, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
February 7, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, and approximately 10 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   February 12, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   Feb 19, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend
c)   February 26, 2008, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Behles, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 17, 2007, Special Study Session. Trustee Berger, seconded by
Trustee Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
b)   January 8, 2008, Special Meeting and Study Session. President Woodbury
referred the Trustees to amended language submitted by Trustee Tucker, who
explained her corrections to the Trustees. President Woodbury suggested an
additional amendment. There being no further comments, Trustee Behles, seconded
by Trustee Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-1-2008 – Special Use Permit: 906-908 Green Bay Road –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the draft ordinance
granting a special use permit to allow a health club facility to operate on the
Subject Property. The amendments reflect Council’s discussion at the last
meeting.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to amend Ordinance M-1-2008
as recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Ordinance M-1-
2008, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance M-2-2008 – Special Use Permit: 564 Lincoln – Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed the amended ordinance granting a special use permit to allow a
medical office to occupy a portion of the first floor of the Subject Property
beginning at 35.67 feet from the public street. She explained that the
amendments reflect Council’s discussion at the time of introduction, flesh out
details of the proposed use and give a more detailed description of the
building. She noted that the provision stating that the special use runs with
the land was not changed, because that is consistent with what has been done in
other instances. Since medical offices are a permitted use in the C-2 Overlay
District, no new special use permit would be required for a subsequent tenant
as long as the space is continuously used for the same purpose.
Trustee Tucker expressed concern about granting the ordinance as written,
describing the use as a substantial change in the Village’s policy regarding
the use of prime, first floor retail space in the Overlay District, even though
the subject property is on the edge of the district. She said that she fears
setting a precedent and is reluctant to let go of this space for use in this
manner for subsequent tenants.
Attorney Janega acknowledged that this is a policy issue for Council’s
consideration, but said that she does not recommend limiting the special use
permit so that it would revert back to retail space if this particular doctor
decided to leave the Village or sell her practice for whatever reason, and
added that any non-retail use other than a medical office would still require
an application for a new special use permit. Addressing the issue of possible
precedent, she noted that every special use permit is carefully drafted to
reflect the unique circumstances of the specific application so that the
Council’s hands are not tied in the future.
Manager Williams pointed out that in an analogous circumstance, the Council
granted a special use permit for the Private Bank on the edge of the retail
overlay district, but subsequently denied a similar application for the same
use that encroached to a greater extent into the district. He indicated that he
does not see this special use as binding for future Councils.
President Woodbury acknowledged that limiting the use to this applicant would
be a substantial deviation from what the Council has done in the past.
Attorney Janega suggested the possibility of changing the phrase “medical use”
to “medical practice,” so that the special use would be for a single medical
user and could not be broker up into smaller medical uses.
Trustee Eilers asked whether a new special use would be required if the
applicant chose to sell this practice and her successor required significant
interior alterations.
Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that question comes down to the intensity of use and
expansion. Generally, if the new user intends to occupy the same footprint for
the same use, a new special use permit would not be required.
Trustee Behles pointed out, however, that if there were any change or increase
in floor space in any way, the new occupant would need to apply for a new
special use permit.
Trustee Ritchell commented that while this policy makes sense for the present,
he would like to give future Councils the authority to revisit and reaffirm the
use of the space as a doctor’s office, should there be a change of occupants.
Attorney Janega remarked that to include such a provision would be a first for
a special use permit and put an additional burden on both the property owner
and the tenant should the doctor decide to sell her practice. She noted that
from a land use standpoint, the use would not be changing, and adding such a
clause is the same as saying that the special use permit expires when the
tenancy expires. The only instance in which the Village has included such a
provision was the Historical Museum.
Trustee Tucker agreed with Trustee Ritchell and inquired as to the possibility
of providing some sort of safety valve to check this down the road.
Ms. Janega noted that the Zoning Ordinance itself provides the check and
balance, because if the Council determines that certain uses are eroding the
Overlay District, then it can amend the Zoning Ordinance to eliminate those
uses as permitted uses.
Trustee Behles pointed out that it is important to remember that medical
offices are permitted special uses in the C-2 Overlay District. Therefore, this
request is not an erosion of the overlay district, but it does constitute an
erosion of the provision limiting the first 100 feet of street footage to
retail uses.
Responding to questions from the Village Manager, Ms. Janega suggested that the
Council adjourn into Executive Session to discuss probable litigation.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn into Executive
Session at 8:05 p.m. to discuss probable litigation pursuant to Section 2(c)
(11) of the Open Meetings Act. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Tucker, Behles, Eilers, Berger, Poor and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
At 8:22 p.m., the Council reconvened into the Regular Council meeting. All
Council members were still present.
The applicant, Dr. Roma Franzia, explained her reasons for moving her offices
in the proposed site, stating that her practice would bring in both patients
and their families into the area and that she needs appropriate space and
amenities to accommodate her handicapped patients. Dr. Franzia added that she
does not intend to take on a partner or to break up the space, but would like
the special use to run with the land because it would better enable her to sell
her practice were she to retire some time down the road, noting that requiring
a prospective buyer of the practice to first obtain a special use would be an
impediment to its sale.
Trustee Berger reminded the Council that when last this matter was discussed,
two of the leading reasons cited for granting this special use permit were (1)
the foot traffic that the practice would generate, and (2) the fact that this
particular space has been vacant for a good part of its history. She remarked
that given the unique character of this space and the configuration of the
building, the 100-foot requirement may not be appropriate for this particular
building, whereas a doctor’s office could adapt itself to the uniqueness of the
building.
Trustee Tucker commented upon the importance of thoroughly exploring the
ramifications of this decision in recognition of the value of the retail
overlay district to the Village.
Trustee Poor indicated that he does not believe that the proposed use will
erode the retail overlay district and said that in his opinion the special use
should run with the land as has been done in other cases.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to amend Ordinance M-2-2008
as recommended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance M-2-2008,
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers,
Berger, Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-3-2008 – Zoning Variation: 681 Garland – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for variations from the Intensity of Use of Lot,
Maximum Building Size, and Side Yard Setback requirements to permit a one-story
addition and basement stairs on the east side of the subject residence and a
chimney on the west wall of an existing family room, explaining that the
proposed addition increases existing nonconformities in impermeable lot
coverage, gross floor area and side yard encroachment. He pointed to testimony
regarding the need for external stairs and a number of alternatives that were
considered. Mr. D’Onofrio noted that after appearing before the Zoning Board
the first time, the applicant reduced the size of the overall addition in
response to the Board’s comments. He reported that the Zoning Board had voted 7
to 0 to recommend in favor of the variation request.
Trustee Tucker expressed concern about increasing the overall size of a home
that is already nonconforming, as well as the side yard encroachment.
Trustee Behles asked to hear firsthand the testimony as to particular design
alternatives, stating that he does not see a problem with leaving the stairs in
the existing position to avoid encroaching further into the side yard.
The applicant, Colette LeBrun, explained that the proposed stairs would only
intrude into what is a “dead area” between their property and the neighbor’s.
President Woodbury observed that from a zoning standpoint, a “dead area” serves
as a buffer between two properties and provides an element of safety for the
individual properties. He explained that in terms of the Zoning Ordinance this
request constitutes an extreme variation and, therefore, the Council must
consider the impact of its decision not only on similar cases but also on the
character of the Village.
Trustee Behles stated that he is looking for some compelling need to relocate
the stairs, noting that the applicant cannot control the location of the
neighbor’s house, which could change in the future. He added that this house is
already over every limit and that it is hard for him to find any argument for
granting this variation.
In the absence of the applicant’s architect, President Woodbury suggested that
the matter be continued until such time as he can be present to respond to
specific questions raised by Mr. Behles and the other Trustees.
d)   Resolution R-5-2008 – Metropolitan Mayors Caucus Greenest Region Compact
of Metropolitan Chicago – Adoption. Mr. Keys explained that the Metropolitan
Mayors Caucus approved a motion to recommend that the region’s nine Councils of
Government and the City of Chicago approve adoption of the Caucus’s Greenest
Region Compact. The Compact is a voluntary initiative to improve the region’s
air, water and land, reduce greenhouse gases, minimize waste, and reduce energy
consumption through a series of environmental actions. By signing the Compact,
the Village would be pledging to make environmental considerations a priority
in its operations and within the community. Mr. Keys went on to review the
strategies identified in the Compact for achieving these goals. He noted that
the Compact is voluntary and municipalities are not penalized if the goals of
the program are not met but noted that the Village has already undertaken many
of the initiatives outlined. Mr. Keys mentioned that municipalities who sign
the Compact before February 15, 2008, are eligible to participate in the MMC’s
CFL distribution program.
Reiterating that the Village already engages in many of conservation
strategies, Manager Williams remarked that given the nature of the Compact and
the availability of free CFL’s, it makes sense for the Village to move forward
before February 15 th .
Trustee Poor commended Manager Williams for bringing this to the Council’s
attention, saying that he wholeheartedly supports the resolution because
Winnetka wants to set an example on attacking environmental and conservation
issues.
Trustee Behles concurred, saying that the Village should not wait for the
formation of an Environmental Commission to take action.
Trustees Berger and Eilers confirmed with Mr. Keys and Attorney Janega that
there were no potential downsides to approving participation in the Compact.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-5-2008,
authorizing the Village’s participation in the Metropolitan Mayors Caucus
Greenest Region Compact of Metropolitan Chicago. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Kaveh Mirani and Patrick O’Neil, restaurateurs in the Village, requested that
the Council amend the Liquor Ordinance to extend the hours of business and
clarify the definition of “service.” Mr. Mirani cited both a demand for later
service and the owners’ need to be able to compete effectively with other area
restaurants as reasons for the request. He asked that the weekend hours be
extended until 1:00 AM, as is done in Glencoe.
Mr. O’Neil agreed with Mr. Mirani’s comments, saying that a one hour extension
for both indoor and sidewalk service would probably be sufficient.
President Woodbury commented on the history of liquor service in the Village
and suggested scheduling that the Council discuss the issues raised at a study
session.
Responding to a question from Trustee Poor about the inclusion of the term
consumption in the Liquor Ordinance, Attorney Janega commented that the
Village’s ordinance is unique in that regard, as consumption is generally
considered to be an implicit aspect of liquor service, and that the distinction
was added in direct response to questions about whether consumption is part of
liquor service.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1543 and 1544. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1543 in the amount of $1,684,445.66 and Warrant List No. 1544 in the amount of
$563,521.64. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Water Plant RFP – Long Term Improvement Plan. Mr. Keys reviewed proposals
to initiate an engineering study of the water plant facility and its ability to
meet future water treatment standards. The intent is to develop a long-term
plant improvement plan which includes an evaluation of membrane filtration
(filtering technology) feasibility and recommendations toward meeting all
applicable regulations and future finished water quality goals. He reported
staff’s recommendation that the contract be awarded to Camp Dresser & McKee,
Inc.
Manager Williams remarked that the Village has never done a long-range
assessment of the current operation and plant. He said that this was important
in light of changes that must be made which could be very expensive, and noted
that he does not expect that any improvements or changes will be made for the
next couple of years.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award a contract to Camp
Dresser & McKee, Inc., for the long term water plant improvement plan study and
membrane filtration feasibility evaluation for an amount not to exceed $29,891.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Ritchell, Tucker,
Behles, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   GIS Service Provider Agreement with Municipal GIS Partners. Mr. Williams
explained that this is the same contract for the same provider that the Village
has used for some time for updating and populating its GIS system. The service
provider is Municipal GIS Partners (MGP), who provided estimates of the labor
hours and associated rates needed to perform these services for the FY 08-09 at
an increase in cost of approximately 3% over the existing rates. He recommended
approval of the contract.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize a service
agreement for GIS services with Municipal GIS Partners, Inc., for a fee based
on the hourly rates set forth in the agenda report, not to exceed $51,050.00.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Eilers, Berger,
Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Recycling Analysis. Mr. Williams reviewed a report prepared by the
Director of Public Works illustrating that recycling collections and
residential refuse collections from 2001-2007 that show that Winnetkans have
increased the amount of material recycled as a percent of the total waste
stream from 24.4% in 2001 to 29.2% in 2007. He said that this is among the
highest recycling rates in the area. He added that starting in 2009, the
Village will be paid approximately $60,000 per year for its recycling material
and there will be no additional cost.
Trustee Poor remarked that this is an excellent trend and represents real
progress. He asked about the possibility of implementing a commercial recycling
program.
Manager Williams indicated that there may be some strategies to put into
service in 2008-2009. He cautioned, however, that the Village tried a
commercial recycling program a number of years ago but participation was not
sufficient to keep it going.
e)   Capital Budget Status Report. Mr. Williams reviewed the final capital
projects status report for items budgeted at $50,000 or more.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Behles reported on the recent Willow Road Steering Committee
meeting. He said the committee has made great progress sorting through details,
mapping out Willow Road, and working out plan. Once these tasks are completed
the Committee will draft a report for the respective Village Councils to
consider, hopefully sometime in March.
   ii)   Trustee Eilers reported on the Plan Commission’s Affordable Housing
Workshops. He encouraged public participation and attendance in the process.
Mr. Eilers also reported on three recent meetings of the Ad Hoc Residential
Design Committee, including a workshop involving eight Winnetka architects.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams referred the Trustees to a report from Steve
Saunders on snow removal operations in February, saying that it has been one of
the snowiest in quite some time with 19” of snow falling in the Village in
three storms.
Mr. Williams also reported that the Village would be undertaking an electronics
recycling program run by a local not-for-profit organization. There will be no
cost to the Village, donations will be tax deductible, and the program meets
ISO standards.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Joe Adams to the Zoning Board
of Appeals for a full five-year term. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to so appoint Mr. Adams. By voice vote, the motion carried.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing board appointments, the sale or lease of real estate, and pending
and probable litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(3), 2(c)(6), and 2(c)(11)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 9:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:32 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:33 p.m.
         
   Recording Secretary
February 12, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: March 4, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 12, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro tem Eilers called the meeting to order at 7:45 p.m. Present:
Trustees Sandra Berger, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
President Woodbury and Trustee Ken Behles. Also in attendance: Village Manager
Doug Williams, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Finance Director Ed McKee, Public Works
Director Steve Saunders, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and approximately 16 persons
in the audience.
2)   Financing Alternatives for Streetscape Program, Village Hall Renovation,
and Parking Structure.
Manager Williams reported that four major capital projects, Post Office
redevelopment, Streetscape, a Lincoln Ave. parking structure, and Village Hall
renovation, are being considered by the Council, and that financing strategies
have been developed by Staff, keeping in mind Council’s direction not to
increase homeowners’ property taxes, and future demands that might be placed on
the Village’s cash reserves by the Post Office redevelopment.
[President Woodbury arrived at 7:50 p.m.]
Manager Williams gave a brief outline of strategies that Staff has devised to
keep the various projects moving forward:
∙   Post Office Redevelopment: There is no specific vision for the Post Office
site, but staff feels maximum flexibility must be maintained in terms of the
redevelopment and how the Village might finance it.
∙   Streetscape: Staff could develop a specific 5-year plan for Streetscape and
the Council could then decide whether or not to move forward with the process.
It is possible to fund the project from a 0.5% home rule sales tax, which would
provide approximately $400,000 per year. Creation of a Special Service Area
(SSA) would be extremely complex, because State law mandates that the area be
contiguous, which would require the four separate SSA’s.
∙   Parking Structure: Building a parking structure on Lincoln Avenue would
require a massive investment of cash reserves. Staff recommends putting the
project on the back burner, while continuing conversations with stakeholders
such as Community House, Fell property owners, East Elm businesses; and
exploring alternative funding sources, such as a CMAQ grant.
∙   Village Hall Renovation: Staff recommends hiring an architect and
appointing a technical committee to keep the project moving forward. With
detailed drawings on hand, the project could be planned in stages and paid for
with a debt wrap on outstanding bonds, and possibly some cash reserves.
Trustee Eilers read a statement into the record, which is attached to these
minutes.
An in-depth discussion of the recommended strategies ensued, in which there was
general agreement with Staff’s suggestions of preserving flexibility on
spending; moving forward to the next level on Streetscape and Village Hall
renovation; exploring the various possibilities for the East Elm parking deck;
and also continuing to zero in on core principles and policy direction for the
PO Redevelopment.
Mr. Dick Kates 1326 Tower, had the following comments:
∙   The Village Hall renovation needs to be undertaken;
∙   Encouraged the Council to continue working with the Library Board to
determine whether relocating the library to the PO site is feasible;
∙   Noted that the Caucus survey revealed the community does not want a new
parking structure in East Elm; questioned the results of the parking study and
suggested forming a committee to study the issue; and
∙   Objected to the Streetscape project on the grounds that the public has not
been engaged enough in the process, and questioned its effectiveness in
revitalizing the business districts.
Mr. Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce, recommended that the Council pass a resolution
outlining what direction the PO redevelopment should take.
Mr. Bob Horne, 162 Fuller, suggested using bonds to help pay for Streetscape
improvements, urged action on consultant’s recommendations of consultants
instead of shelving studies after they’re done, and expressed preference for a
parking structure over Village Hall renovations.
Manager Williams said the possibility of issuing bonds for Streetscape could be
investigated, and indicated that with the projected $400,000 home rule sales
tax revenue stream, the Village could issue $4 million worth of bonds.
Ms. Cicely Michalak, BCDC member and director of the Chamber of Commerce, said
she felt it was time to validate the existence of the studies on the commercial
districts and to take action on the BCDC’s recommendations. She disagreed with
statements that the public was not engaged in the Streetscape planning, and
noted that BCDC meetings are always open to the public.
President Woodbury made the following points:
∙   The next draft of the Council’s core principles for the Post Office site
will address intergovernmental cooperation and will lead to a Resolution that
formally states the principles;
∙   Discussions with the Community House and other stakeholders on the issue of
the Lincoln Ave. parking structure should continue;
∙   The Streetscape project was studied in open meetings for a year, articles
have been in the newspaper, and the issue has been discussed in Council
meetings; as yet, no individual decisions have been made on the
recommendations, and community input will be solicited before any plans are
made final;
∙   The Council is willing to help the Library Board in any way possible, but
can’t tell them what to do and can’t conduct a needs analysis for them.
Manager Williams indicated that an RFP will be issued for architectural
services to draw up plans for the Village Hall renovation, and Staff will
create a proposed five-year plan for Streetscape implementation. He said
discussions with stakeholders about the feasibility of a Lincoln Avenue parking
structure will continue.
3)   IML Public Safety Pension Packet.
[This item was discussed after Item # 4, after which the rest of the agenda was
discussed in its original order.]
Manager Williams reported that the Illinois Municipal League has asked its
members to adopt a resolution urging the Illinois General Assembly to stop
passing additional costly benefit improvements, and to adopt reforms to protect
Police and Fire pensions. He indicated that he thought it was important for the
Village to pass a resolution similar to a sample provided by the IML, although
the resolution should be tailored to fit Winnetka’s situation.
Manager Williams reviewed the sample Resolution contained in his agenda report,
after which there was a short discussion about the need for municipalities to
be protected from the State’s legislative actions where additional public
safety pension benefits are concerned. President Woodbury asked the Police
Chief if he would like to comment.
Chief DeLopez indicated that while Winnetka’s public safety officers appreciate
the fiscal problems presented by additional benefit legislation that is passed
in Springfield, it is important to keep in mind that, unlike other public
pension funds, they contribute 9.5% of their pay to their pension. He noted
that actuarial studies have shown that the life expectancy for a police officer
or firefighter is shortened due to the nature of their work, that they
willingly work nights, weekends and holidays, and that every day on the job
they are willing to risk their lives for strangers. Finally, he commented that
many of the statistics and issues contained in the IML documents supporting a
resolution are generalizations, and do not apply equally in all municipalities,
and he asked the Council to consider information specific to Winnetka when
making their decisions.
Manager Williams indicated that a resolution would be brought before the
Council at the next regular meeting, amended to fit Winnetka’s needs.
4)   Relocation of Park District Service Center to 1390 Willow Road.
[This item was discussed after Item #2 on the agenda.]
Mr. Saunders reported that the Park District has been undertaking a needs
analysis for improving their service center, and that one of the options being
looked at is moving a big portion of the operation to the closed landfill at
1390 Willow. He observed that an alternative version of the plan was discussed,
in which the existing north end of the southern Public Works building (Building
B) would be divided between the Village and Park District, and a less intensive
vehicle storage building would be constructed on the landfill, also to be
shared between the Village and Park District.
Mr. Saunders said he did not think the alternate plan was in the Village’s best
interests, gave an overview of his reasons, and reviewed his adjacency study,
all of which are contained in his agenda report. He concluded by saying that he
was not convinced there would be savings realized in the plan, and that from an
operational standpoint, it is a definite negative.
The Council briefly discussed the pros and cons of sharing space at the Public
Works Yards.
Mr. Ian Sherman, Vice President of the Park Board, thanked the Village for
cooperating with the Park District, and said the Park has the proposed new
service center narrowed down to three sites, one of them being the Willow Road
site, and they were open to the possibility of a shared parking structure on
the landfill if it would provide efficiencies and save money.
Mr. Chuck Dowding, 968 Elm, said he hoped the Village and Park Board would
continue to study the possibility of sharing Building B and a parking structure
on the second tier of the landfill and offered his engineering students to do
further studies on the issue of space-sharing.
Manager Williams commented that the Village has told the Park District that
they are welcome to share fueling facilities and the mechanic bays on a split
shift basis, providing that the Village has the day shift. He observed that
having the Park District right next door might provide additional synergies,
but noted that the recommendation of Staff that the idea of sharing Building B
does not make sense for the Village.
After further discussion, it was the consensus of the Council to deny the
request of the Park District to entertain further discussions about renovating
and sharing Building B, but that a dialog will continue about exploring other
efficiencies.
5)   Annual Report: Police Social Services Worker.
Chief DeLopez reported that since 2003, the Police Department has contracted
with New Trier Township to provide social service crisis intervention. He
indicated that the arrangement is working well, and that Mr. Art Sontag, the
social worker employed by the Township to work with the Police Department has
done an outstanding job and is a real asset to the Police Department. He gave a
summary of cases referred to Mr. Sontag for the past year, and also reviewed
the number of cases referred each year since the arrangement was put in place
in 2004.
Manager Williams complimented Chief DeLopez for recommending the contract with
New Trier Township, noting the efficiency and high quality of the social
services provided.
President Woodbury indicated that the program is working so well, that there
does not seem to be a need for an annual report, but that instead, Chief
DeLopez only need report if there are problems, and that the matter can be
discussed at budget time as well.
6)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
7)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles. The meeting adjourned
at 10:15 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
February 19, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: March 18, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 19, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Herb Ritchell. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Community Development Director
Mike D’Onofrio, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and approximately nine
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   February 26, 2008, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   March 3, 2008, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend. President Woodbury reminded everyone
that the meeting would be held in the Public Safety Building.
c)   March 4, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 15, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Poor, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
b)   February 7, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-3-2008 – Zoning Variation: 681 ––––Garland–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the applicant is requesting
Intensity of Use of Lot, Maximum Building Size, and Side Yard Setback
requirements to permit a one-story addition and basement stairs on the east
side of the subject residence and a chimney on the west wall of an existing
family room. This matter was continued from the February 7 th meeting because
the architect had not been present to answer questions regarding conforming
alternatives for the exterior stairs on the east side of the building that had
been considered. The proposed outside basement stair with a 2.12’ side setback
would encroach 5.38’ into the minimum required side setback of 7.5’. Mr.
D’Onofrio introduced the architect, Tom Lindsay, Lindsay Associates, to answer
the Trustees’ questions.
Mr. Lindsay distributed photos of the existing exterior stairs and the front of
the house to the Council members, noting that the stairs where they are
proposed to be located would not be visible from the front of the house. He
explained that it is necessary for the home’s occupant to have the exterior
stairs because the interior stairs are a challenge to negotiate for certain
purposes, such as moving in/out large appliances. The interior stairs’ tread
and height are nonconforming, as is the head clearance, which is a more
critical shortcoming.
In response to a question from Trustee Behles, Mr. Lindsay explained why, in
his opinion, rebuilding the stairs where they are currently located is not a
viable option. He said the family room adjacent to the present location has no
foundation underneath it to act as underpinning, therefore, it is better to put
the stairs alongside the full basement underneath the kitchen and dining room
where there is a foundation. In addition to which, to rebuild in the present
location would be considerably more expensive.
Trustee Poor inquired as to whether the neighbors have voiced any objections.
The applicant, Colette LeBrun, indicated that they have not, saying that their
view will remain very much the same as it is now.
Trustee Poor then asked whether the proposed construction would pose an
impediment to backyard access in the event of a fire emergency. Mr. Lindsay
responded that there is sufficient access on the other side of the house and
that the proposed stairs have relatively no mass to navigate.
Trustee Eilers mentioned that there is a large tree to the east of the house
which might impede access for an emergency vehicle.
Trustee Tucker expressed her concerns about increasing the degree of
nonconformity on an already nonconforming structure, explaining that side yards
have a purpose and need to be protected. She raised the issue of potential
flooding.
Mr. Lindsay responded that the Village has an extensive grading review that
requires a property to be able to contain its problems on its own property. He
said that as designed there is almost the same amount of impermeable surface
and that there will be a drain at the bottom of the stairs as a matter of good
practice.
President Woodbury and Trustees Berger and Poor inquired about structural
issues and possible conforming alternatives that had been and might be
considered. Mr. Lindsay responded.
Trustee Behles commented that the original point is that this house is over the
maximum required limits, and there are clear conforming alternatives. Whether
or not these place an additional financial burden on the applicant does not
warrant moving the stairs to encroach further into the side yard and does not
meet the standard for a variation.
President Woodbury asked Mr. D’Onofrio whether the proposed construction could
take place within the property line. Mr. D’Onofrio replied that it would be
close.
Mrs. LeBrun indicated that the neighbors were aware of the possibility of an
overdig and were unopposed.
Trustee Berger pointed out that there could be a different neighbor in six
months who might, in fact, be opposed. She said that her biggest concern is the
2-foot side yard setback and she hasn’t heard compelling testimony that there
really is no other alternative design.
Trustee Eilers indicated that he would be in favor of the request if the
conclusion is reached that the hardship is the small kitchen and that can only
addressed by the addition of the breakfast room as designed.
Trustee Poor said that although the applicants have made a big effort to
minimize the effect, he remains concerned with the encroachment into the side
yard. He added that he is not convinced that there really is no alternative,
although it might be very expensive. He indicated that he could not support the
variation.
Noting that there was a consensus among the Trustees to deny the requested side
yard variation, President Woodbury and Attorney Janega explained the
applicant’s alternatives.
Thereafter, Mr. Lindsay and Mrs. LeBrun asked that the case be treated as a
continuance. Mr. Lindsay then asked whether the Trustees had any concerns about
the requested one-story addition.
Trustee Berger said that this was a minor increase, and she had no objections.
Trustee Behles agreed, saying that the side yard encroachment was the problem.
b)   Resolution R-7-2008 – Approval of 897 Tower Rd./900 Private Rd.
Subdivision – Adoption. Mr. Norkus reviewed a request to subdivide the existing
single parcel at 900 Private Road into two lots. He explained that the parcel
is unique in that it is a “through lot” extending nearly 500 feet from Private
Road to Tower Road. The proposed division would result in lots of 59,077 sq. ft
(Lot 1 to the north, upon which the existing home is located) and 30,041 sq. ft
(Lot 2 to the south). Mr. Norkus reported that the proposed subdivision
complies with basic quantitative measures, including minimum lot area, lot
depth, and lot width. He added that the subject property has an existing
nonconformity whereby the total side yard requirement (55.82 ft.) is not met.
(53.59 feet is provided, a deficiency of 2.23 feet.) The resulting subdivision
would result in a total side yard requirement of 63.75 ft, which would increase
the degree of deficiency, or nonconformity, to 10.16 feet.
Mr. Norkus then reviewed staff analysis and recommendations as to what measures
are advisable to assure that the proposed subdivision complies with various
Village codes and regulations regarding general lot layout, utility easements,
and drainage easements.
Mr. Norkus reported that after considering whether the proposed subdivision
created any adverse impact, the Plan Commission had voted unanimously to
recommend approval subject to the conditions identified by staff and presented
in the Agenda Report, which have been agreed to by the applicant.
Mr. Norkus went on to review the conditions agreed to by the applicants. He
said that the most notable is that there will be an offset rear lot line, which
is generally discouraged by staff, but for which the applicants expressed a
strong preference. Therefore, the applicants have agreed to an unusually large
utility easement in the rear. Otherwise, drainage easements are provided for to
continue necessary access to a creek on the subject property for other
neighboring properties.
Trustee Behles asked whether the applicants are trying to maintain as large a
lot as possible for the existing home and asked whether the existing house is
to remain. Mr. Norkus indicated that that appears to be the case at this time.
Trustee Behles then asked whether Lot 1 would be a candidate for further
subdivision in the future. Mr. Norkus responded that the possibility could not
be ruled out, but that it would be difficult to do.
Trustee Eilers asked whether the large easement in the rear addresses the
majority of the concerns about subdivision. Mr. Norkus replied affirmatively,
noting that the smaller lot to the south also means a smaller house.
Trustee Poor inquired as to the potential impact of the subdivision on
drainage. Mr. Norkus again noted that neighboring properties will have
continued access to the creek for drainage purposes. He added that at the
present time, no plans have been submitted for any house on the south property,
so no impact can be determined; hence, the drainage easements.
Mr. Saunders indicated that the proposal has been carefully reviewed and he is
comfortable with them
Saying that it is difficult to evaluate the request without redevelopment plans
for the south lot, Trustee Tucker asked how utility easements impact lot size
for zoning purposes. Mr. Norkus explained that they are not deducted from lot
size in determining relevant lot calculations.
Trustee Tucker went on to express concern about potential redevelopment on the
neighborhood. She asked whether there are plans to landmark the existing home
on Lot 1.
The applicant’s attorney, David Grossberg said that at present there are no
plans for the development of Lot 2 or to landmark the home on Lot 1. He
indicated that the applicants’ intent is to preserve the existing, old home.
Mr. Grossberg explained that there is an existing pool and patio adjacent to
that home and the offset rear lot line is requested in order to allow existing
landscaping and trees to continue to serve as a buffer between the two
properties. He pointed out that Lot 2 will still be well above the minimum lot
size for the zoning district and, therefore, sufficient to accommodate a
graceful home.
The applicants’ architect, Paul Konstant, noted that most of the existing house
has already been improved, thereby attesting to the fact that it is not a
candidate for demolition.
Trustee Behles commented that although he wishes there was some way to
guarantee that the existing home would not be demolished, that is not a part of
the process here. He said that in his opinion the requests are not unreasonable
and, therefore, he would support the subdivision request.
The remaining Trustees concurred.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-7-2008.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
c)   Resolution R-8-2008 – Urging the Illinois General Assembly to Protect
Police and Fire Pensions – Adoption. Mr. Williams explained that pursuant to
the Council’s direction at the last Study Session, this is the IML draft
resolution regarding fire pension enhancements refined to reflect ’
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-7-2008.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1545 and 1546. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1545 in the amount of $772,741.31 and Warrant List No. 1546 in the amount of
$248,451.83. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
b)   Amended Federal Aid Agreement – ––––Pine Street–– and ––––Eldorado–
––Street Bridge Construction and Construction Engineering. Mr. Saunders
explained that the construction contract awarded by IDOT in November 2006 for
the costs associated with the rehabilitation of the Pine Street and Eldorado
Street bridges, including construction engineering, was significantly above the
engineer’s estimate for the project. This, in addition to IDOT-approved change
orders, resulted in a final project cost of approximately $2,761,832, of which
the Village’s 20% is $552,342, or $139,960 more than the original anticipated
match of $412,382, approved by the Council in April 2006. The Federal
government will be funding the remaining 80%. He recommended that the Council
authorize the proposed Local Agency Amendment #1 authorizing Federal funding
for the Phase III Engineering and Construction reflecting the increase in
Winnetka’s participation amount.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to authorize Local Agency
Amendment #1 authorizing Federal funding for Phase III Engineering and
Construction for the Pine Street and Eldorado Street Bridge improvements. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Eilers, Berger,
Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ritchell.
Manager Williams thanked Mr. Saunders and the Public Works Department for their
excellent job in overseeing the rebuilding of Winnetka’s infrastructure,
emphasizing that the bridge projects have been 80% funded by the Federal
government, while the Village has paid only 20%.
c)   Upgrade of the Police 9-1-1 Communications System. Chief DeLopez explained
that the Police Department is currently operating its 9-1-1 system with out-of-
date equipment, much of which has reached the end of its useful life and needs
to be replaced. In addition, the existing equipment and furniture is not
suitable for being relocated or expanded to accommodate a future combined
dispatch facility. He noted that conversations continue with the Village of
Wilmette regarding a combined dispatch facility. He went on to briefly review
the responses to a recent Request for Information put forth by the Department
to potential equipment vendors and to explain why J&L/ModuCom is the
Department’s preferred provider.
Sgt. Rich Carlson added that the project, as designed, would allow for any of
several scenarios for transition to a Combined Dispatch, should this be
undertaken during the design life of the system.
President Woodbury commented that this matter would be back before the Council
for continued discussion at a later time.
d)   Budget Overview. Mr. McKee presented an overview of the coming year’s
proposed budget. He noted that the budget is balanced, generates positive cash
flow before capital investment, and controls costs within the Village’s
constraints.
In response to a question from President Woodbury about possible concerns, Mr.
McKee noted that the cash balances in the Water and Electric funds are
declining, which makes it hard to invest in capital while keeping the rates
low.
Mr. McKee went on to explain the format of the budget and Village funds to
discuss the Village’s revenues and expenditures. In doing so, he pointed out
that revenues are very property tax driven while expenses are very wage and
benefit dependent, with personnel costs amounting to 35% of the budget despite
the fact that staffing levels remain relatively constant. He pointed out that
the impact of State mandated increases to police and fire pensions can be seen
reverberating throughout the budget.
Mr. McKee summarized the status of each of the Village funds and the budget as
a whole, noting that the Village’s philosophy is to (1) balance the operating
budgets and generate sufficient cash for capital investments program; (2)
utilize a pay-as-you-go philosophy to encourage fiscal discipline; (3) comply
with property tax caps, even though home rule status obviates the necessity to
do so; and (4) set utility rates based on cost of service.
Mr. McKee concluded by saying that the budget is fiscally sound and meets the
Village’s mission by maintaining Village services, rebuilding infrastructure,
and keeping tax and fee increases to a minimum. He noted, however, that the
General Fund budget will continue to be under pressure as municipal expenses
are likely to increase faster than revenue, so it is possible that some sort of
intervention may be needed to balance future budgets. He said that the Village
will continue to review services to become more efficient and cost effective
without diminishing desired services while continuing to make significant
capital investments in the Village infrastructure.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. The Village President reported that the Village has
received word from IDOT that the section of Willow Road from Forestway to
Hibbard will be repaved this spring, something that many residents have been
requesting for a long time.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Tucker announced that the Chamber of Commerce would be
sponsoring a Spring Art Walk from March 13-23. She encouraged residents to turn
out to support the North Shore Art League and local merchants.
   ii)   Trustee Berger reported that she, Trustee Eilers, and President
Woodbury had attended the recent NWMC administrative brunch, at which keeping
public safety funds solvent was a key topic.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that the Village has started an
electronics waste recycling program, the details of which can be found on the
Village’s website. There is no cost to the Village and the recyclables will not
be sent overseas.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Herb Ritchell. The meeting adjourned at 10:
16 p.m.
            
   Recording Secretary
February 26, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
Budget Review Meeting
(Approved: April 1, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 26, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community
Development Mike D’Onofrio, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Deputy Fire Chief Steve
Laureys, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Deputy Police Chief Pat Kries, Trustees-
elect Chris Rintz and Gene Greable, and no persons in the audience.
2)   Police Department.
Police Chief Joe DeLopez reviewed the Police Department’s mission and
organization structure, and gave an overview of activity in 2007, including
annual events and special details. He indicated that the department’s
reorganization was completed in 2007, and the Computer Automated Dispatch
system has been fully implemented, and noted that four personnel losses in 2007
impacted overtime costs, but resulted in cost savings for compensation for the
younger, newer recruits.
Chief DeLopez reviewed the planned capital projects for the upcoming year,
which include a communications systems upgrade, improvements to the firearms
range, and roof rehabilitation for the Public Safety Building, and observed
that a combined dispatch and lockup project with Wilmette is in the planning
stage. He said he plans to give periodic reports to the Council throughout the
year to give an overview of community interactions, crime prevention efforts,
personnel development, significant investigations, and technical enhancements.
Chief DeLopez reported that the increase in this year’s budget is 3.6%, which
is less than the 3.75% wage increase for employees, citing less tenured
employees receiving longevity pay and completion of the department’s
reorganization as factors in holding the increase down.
3)   Fire Department.
Fire Chief Scott Smith reviewed the accomplishments made by the Fire Department
this year, which include completing New Trier High School internal
communications, purchasing a new ambulance, participation in the Cook County
Pharmaceutical Plan and NIMS Training. He summarized the number of calls for
assistance, the total number of fires and the estimated losses vs. saved
property values, and the amount of training completed by department staff. He
indicated that the Department’s goals for next year are to continue to provide
high-quality training and mentoring of future officers and new recruits,
certify the firemedics in Advanced Cardiac Life Support (ACLS), complete the
promotion process, replace a staff vehicle and prepare for and begin union
contract negotiations.
Chief Smith indicated that he and Deputy Chief Steve Laureys covered 880 hours
in 2007 to save on overtime costs and that the transitional firemedic has also
reduced overtime expenses. He observed that the increase over last year’s
budget is 4.4%, but that when the capital projects are separated out, it’s
actually a .2% reduction.
4)   Community Development.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio gave an overview of the
Department’s functions, which include approving building permits, performing
plan and zoning reviews, conducting electrical, plumbing and mechanical
inspections, managing code enforcement and food service sanitation, and also
staffing for 6 standing committees and 4 ad hoc committees.
Mr. D’Onofrio summarized the number of demolition permits, building permits,
and zoning cases that have been processed and gave an overview of the building
permit process to give an idea of the amount of time and work involved to
complete the process. He indicated that the number of demolition permits and
related fees had decreased significantly, but on the plus side, the new permit
clerk has increased efficiency in the department, so much so that he did not
hire the Plan Reviewer Assistant approved for last year’s budget.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the budget will increase 2.9% over last year, that
revenue is expected to stay flat throughout the next fiscal year, and that he
will be meeting with Finance Director Ed McKee to discuss increases in some
building permit fees.
5)   Administration.
Due to time constraints, this item was removed from the agenda.
6)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
7)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:20 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
