Tuesday, March 11, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: April 1, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
11, 2008 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, and Jessica Tucker. Absent:
Trustee Ritchell. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus, Director of Water &
Electric Brian Keys, Finance Director Ed McKee, Trustees-Elect Chris Rintz,
Bill Johnson, Gene Greable, and 0 persons in the audience.
2)   Discussion – Nore Winter, Residential Design & Zoning Consultant.
Assistant Community development Director Brian Norkus reviewed the background
of the Residential Design Guidelines project, and introduced Mr. Nore Winter,
the consultant who is assisting the Community Development Department with the
process.
Mr. Winter gave a presentation that included examples of steps taken by similar
communities similar to try to manage the rate of change taking place within
their borders, and explained how he would go about creating a design handbook
for Winnetka. He noted that the handbook would be highly visual, and that
community participation and focus groups are used to help create the final
product, which would be tailored to fit specific user groups such as
homeowners, laypeople, builders, architects, and neighborhood advocates.
Trustee Herb Ritchell arrived at 7:54 p.m.
Mr. Winter gave some examples of possible amendments that could be made to the
zoning ordinance in an effort to preserve the light and air of neighbors, and
also to maintain the unique character of Winnetka. He indicated that zoning
restrictions can often have unintended consequences, which can be better
understood through the 3-D computer modeling techniques that Winter & Company
uses to illustrate the concept. He described his approach as a two-track
system, with Track 1 being the design concepts contained in the handbook, and
Track 2 consisting of code refinements.
Trustee-Elect Rintz, a member of the Ad Hoc Residential Design Guidelines
Committee, commented that enlisting the expertise of Winter & Company is an
important step, and noted that the upcoming public meetings in April will
enable the Council to gauge the community’s enthusiasm for design guidelines.
Trustee Tucker stated that she was glad to be having a discussion about how
issues with design and zoning overlap, but that she saw them as separate
issues, and added that many people want to address the overall bulk of new
homes in the context of lot size. She said that design guidelines can help to
influence the look of a home, but the overall goal should be to cut back on
size, especially on small lots, and to keep new development context-sensitive.
Mr. Winter indicated that Track 2 will look at mass and scale, as there is a
limit to what design guidelines can do, with the important thing being to
strike a balance between when something should be codified, and when design
guidelines are called for. He noted that a voluntary design guidelines process
brings more people to the table to talk about good design, instead of fighting
over how it will be used. He remarked that the discussion needs to start with
precisely defining community character, examining values, and clarifying the
issues before any solutions are crafted.
There was a lengthy discussion about the current problems facing Winnetka, how
to deal with them effectively, and the various design and zoning tools
available to the Council. There was general agreement that using the two track
system would be something different and potentially very helpful for the
Village.
Manager Williams indicated that many Villages see Winnetka’s zoning ordinance
as setting the standard, and that the two-track discussion will carry the
Village forward even further. He noted the need for the Council to set
priorities in order to accomplish all the projects currently before it, as
there are many projects awaiting completion.
Mr. Winter indicated that he would come back in six to eight weeks with a draft
of the design guidelines handbook and some tools to address bulk issues.
It was decided that the work of the Residential Design Guidelines Committee
should be allowed to unfold, including gathering community input from the
public workshops, before any amendments are made to the current zoning
ordinance.
Manager Williams suggested that a resolution be drafted to formalize the
outgoing Council’s intentions to reduce bulk in the zoning ordinance. President
Woodbury directed the Village Attorney to prepare such a resolution for
consideration at the April 15 th Council meeting.
3)   Budget Review:
a)   Power Cost Adjustment. Mr. Keys reported that the proposed increase in the
base rates will include the cost of purchasing power and explained that the
Power Cost Adjustment will be reset after the close of each fiscal year by
determining the actual cost of power from the prior year, and then either
collecting or rebating the difference through a fixed power cost adjustment
rider over the next 12 months. He reviewed the percentage increases for one-,
two- and three-year increments to collect the outstanding power cost adjustment
balance and noted that commercial rates will see larger increases on a per-rate
basis.
There was a short discussion about which of the three catch-up plans to choose
from, with the final consensus being to recoup the outstanding shortage in the
power cost adjustment over a three-year period. Manager Williams indicated that
staff would prepare the resolutions for the electric rates and the power cost
adjustment and bring them before the Council in April.
b)   Various Fees. Manager Williams suggested a raise in the demolition fee to
$13,500, and after a brief discussion, the Council agreed. Mr. McKee
distributed a handout containing the changes to the rate fees, and said the
final resolutions would be brought to the Council for introduction at the next
regular meeting.
4)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
5)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:18 p.m.
