A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
18, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell, and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus, Finance Director Ed
McKee, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Water & Electric Director Brian
Keys, and approximately 14 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that item 9(g), Outdoor Seating Permits and
Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License Riders, was removed from the Agenda. The
matter will be considered at the April 1 st Council meeting. The Trustees then
approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
April 1, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
April 8, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated that
they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 19, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
b)   March 3, 2008, Budget Meeting. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger,
moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   March 4, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
d)   March 4, 2008, Budget Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-3-2008 – Zoning Variation: 681 ––––Garland––– Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio explained that the applicant is requesting Intensity of Use of Lot,
Maximum Building Size, and Side Yard Setback requirements to permit a one-story
addition on the east side of the subject residence and a bay window on the west
wall of an existing family room. This matter was discussed at the February 7 th
and 19 th Council meetings and the applicants have since revised their plans to
relocate the originally proposed outside stairs to a conforming location north
of the proposed breakfast room, in approximately the same location as the
existing exterior stairs. This change results in the stairs having a conforming
side yard setback of 8.7 ft. and reduces the GFA by 2.49 s.f., RLC by 0.04
s.f., and impermeable surface by 106.06 s.f. over the original plans.
President Woodbury confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that these changes address all
of the concerns raised by the Trustees at previous meetings. President Woodbury
then suggested waiving introduction on this ordinance since the matter has been
thoroughly discussed.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to waive introduction and move
directly to adoption. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt ordinance M-3-2008. By
roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Ritchell,
Tucker, Behles, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
a)   Ordinance No. M-4-2008 – Landmark Designation: 592 Cherry Street –
Introduction. Jill Morgan, Village Planning Technician and liaison to the
Landmark Preservation Commission, reviewed the proposal to designate the Joseph
R. Gay House at 592 Cherry as a local landmark. She reported that the Landmark
Preservation Commission has found that the Gay House satisfies the criteria for
the designation based upon a variety of factors, most significantly the home’s
unique Queen Anne style architecture and the association with the local war
hero Dinsmore Ely.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-4-
2008. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Resolution R-17-2008 – 701-705 Sheridan Rd.: Approval of Bluhm’s Second
Resubdivision – Adoption. Mr. Norkus reviewed a proposal to consolidate two
existing parcels at 701 and 705 Sheridan Road into a single lot that would
measure 115,493 square feet (2.65 acres). He explained that the home currently
under construction at 701 Sheridan is located on a single parcel of land that
itself was the product of a consolidation. The owner acquired the vacant lot
immediately north and has applied to consolidate the additional parcel into the
original lot area.
Mr. Norkus went on to say that proposed improvements identified by the
applicant at this time consist primarily of a reconfigured driveway on the
additional lot area. He noted that the existing vacant lot has a nonconforming
width and that the proposed consolidation would eliminate that existing
nonconformity and create a lot that wholly conforms to all zoning requirements,
including minimum lot area and width.
Mr. Norkus explained that the existing property at 701 Sheridan was the result
of a two-lot consolidation in 2004-2005. At that time, the Village Council
imposed a restrictive covenant to maintain side yards previously required of
the lakefront lot of 43.07 feet. In considering the current application, the
Plan Commission found that the small difference between the existing
requirements and the standard zoning requirements eliminated the need to
maintain the restrictive covenant. As a result, the current resubdivision
ordinance includes language which effectively vacates the 2005 side yard
restrictive covenant. Additionally, it provides for easements that were
requested by staff for the purpose of serving the subject lots and adjoining
parcels with Village utilities.
Mr. Norkus reported that the Plan Commission had voted unanimously to recommend
approval of the proposed Bluhm’s Second Resubdivision, subject to the
conditions outlined in the Agenda Report.
Trustee Behles confirmed with Mr. Norkus that there is no difference of opinion
between the owner and the Village about the setbacks. Mr. Norkus agreed.
Trustee Ritchell inquired as to whether this consolidation could be subdivided
at some time in the future. Mr. Norkus said that it would be difficult to rule
out that possibility, but that it becomes less likely as the Zoning Ordinance
becomes more restrictive.
Trustee Behles commented that he sees taking a nonconforming lot out of the
neighborhood as a positive.
Attorney Janega noted that as a part of the first resubdivision, there was a
requirement that the primary access be from Sheridan Road and suggested rolling
the access covenants into the new plat to avoid possible confusion in the
future about whether they remain in effect.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Poor, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-17-2008 as
drafted with the addition of language specifying that the access covenant
continues to be in effect. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, and Behles. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-9-2008 - 2008-2009 Village Budget –Adoption. Mr. McKee
reviewed two proposed amendments to the draft budget that was introduced at the
March 4 th Council meeting after the Public Hearing on the matter. These
include a transfer of $580,000 to the Streetscape Fund out of the General Fund,
and a reduction in staffing in the Community Development Department.
Manager Williams responded to a couple of questions from the audience regarding
the funding of certain accounts.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend Resolution No. R-9-
2008 as outlined by Mr. McKee. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-9-2008,
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor,
Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Updated Fee & Rate Resolutions.
   i)   Resolution No. R-10-2008 - Water Rates – Adoption. Trustee Behles,
seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-10-2008. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Eilers, Berger, Poor,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-11-2008 - Electric Rates – Adoption. Manager Williams
noted that the Electric rate resolution has been amended to include changes to
the purchase power cost adjustment formula and purchase power cost adjustment
catch-up that were discussed at the March 11 th Study Session. He indicated
that the specific numbers would be brought to the Council in April.
      Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to amend the Resolution
as outlined by Mr. Williams.
      Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-11-
2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor,
Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   iii)   Resolution No. R-12-2008 – Sewer Rates – Adoption. Trustee Ritchell,
seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-12-2008. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers,
Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   iv)   Resolution No. R-13-2008 - Refuse Rates – Adoption. Trustee Ritchell,
seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt Resolution R-13-2008. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers,
Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   v)   Resolution No. R-14-2007 – General Permit & License Fees – Adoption.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-14-
2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger,
Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   vi)   Resolution No. R-15-2007 – Building & Construction Fees – Adoption.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-15-
2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Ritchell,
Tucker, Behles, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   vii)   Resolution No. R-16-2007 – Fire Service Fees – Non-resident –
Adoption. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to adopt Resolution
R-16-2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Eilers, Berger, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
      President Woodbury thanked Mr. McKee and his staff for their efforts in
putting the Budget together.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Jeffrey Liss, 1354 Edgewood, suggested that the Village schedule a couple of
informational meetings each year to help new residents understand how the
Caucus and Council work.
8)   Old Business.
a)   Post Office Devlopment Principles. President Woodbury indicated that the
document in the Agenda materials, a redraft of the Planning and Development
Principles for the Post Office Site, dated March 11, 2008, was prepared by
Trustees Berger and Tucker as a “working document” to assist the Council in its
policy discussions. He added that it is a base document that will be discussed
and possibly modified based on this evening’s discussion, with the goal of
bringing a resolution formally stating the principles before the Council at its
April 1 st meeting.
Trustee Behles distributed copies of his recommended edits to that draft and
reviewed his comments, and Trustee Berger distributed copies of suggested
additional statements, or action points, to be included.
A lengthy discussion ensued regarding the draft documents and comments.
Trustee Tucker remarked that she had originally felt that most, if not all, of
the competing uses for the site could be accommodated. However, after all of
the discussions, she now believes that such development would be too dense,
overwhelming the site. She indicated that she believes it is now more
appropriate to prioritize uses, particularly since the Library option is still
in flux.
Trustee Ritchell said that he believes the new draft is right on target.
Trustee Poor stated that in his opinion it is important not to rule out any
uses because no one knows where the Library option will end up.
Trustee Behles encouraged the Trustees not to draft a document that would shut
the door on any alternative plan and to leave a document that would lead in
some direction for a future Council should the Library option fail. He urged
that the basic planning goals of the Post Office Committee’s recommendations be
included in the final resolution.
Trustee Berger commented that she had not included those points in the March 11
th draft because she did not think there was consensus on the all of the goals
and objectives.
Trustee Eilers remarked that the goals and objectives mentioned in the Post
Office Committee (“POC”) report might raise unrealistic expectations for the
potential of the site.
Trustee Poor added that while the concepts included in the POC report are not
objectionable, they may detract from the purpose of what the Council is
attempting to do, which is to set out a set of core principles upon which there
is a consensus.
President Woodbury said that what the Council is trying to do is to distill
everything that has been heard down to a finite number of points, but it does
not mean that there are no other ideas out there just because they aren’t
specifically mentioned.
Trustee Behles stated that the Council needs to set forth some conceptual basis
for what the Village is trying to accomplish in the downtown with the site’s
redevelopment so that these are articulated and understood. He indicated that
he believed there is clear consensus as to the goals and objectives set forth
in the POC report, and added that he can see the development principles
changing, but not the goals and objectives.
President Woodbury commented that the Village is now at the point of allowing
the Library to move forward with its exploration. He said regardless of the
development path taken, everyone intends for it to serve many of the goals
stated in the POC report.
In further discussion of potential development options, Trustee Behles said
that he does not want to adopt any kind of language that would close the door
on either a mixed use development with a residential element or an affordable
housing option. He opined that if the Village adopts an affordable housing
policy, it would be difficult to rule such a component out of any residential
development on the Post Office site, if the Village intends to apply the
concept to private development.
Trustee Berger noted that the Village needs to carefully consider any
residential use on the site because it drives the cost of the development so
high due to the necessity to provide underground parking.
Trustee Eilers concurred with Trustee Behles, but commented that the Council
should not jump ahead of the Plan Commission on this issue. He said that if the
final affordable housing plan specifies that a certain percentage should be
affordable, then it should apply to land that is owned by the Village.
Trustee Ritchell said when the Council finally gets to the matter of affordable
housing, this and every other project will be considered.
President Woodbury noted that should a residential component ultimately be
included in the development plan, affordable housing should be included within
the context of any affordable housing plan adopted by the Village but, for the
moment, the development of that affordable housing plan is in the hands of the
Plan Commission.
With regard to the proposed action points, Trustee Eilers supported the
inclusion of language saying that the Council would not take any action on the
Site’s redevelopment until some specific date so that the Library Board can
proceed with its analysis and planning. He said that to do so would send a
message to the community that the Council is not rushing the Library Board and
that that Board was going to have some time to proceed without thought that the
Council was taking off in a different direction. He expressed the belief that
allowing the Library’s process to mature without any perceived pressure would
eliminate a lot of rancor in the community if a future Council ultimately
decides to go with a mixed use development. He emphasized that any date
specified would not be a timetable within which the job would be done, just a
minimum timeframe that could be extended if need be.
Mr. Eilers also opined that the Caucus might be dealing with this issue at some
level in the future, even if just in the interviewing of candidates, and added
that given the current economy, the Village doesn’t want to push the
development, even if it could do so.
Mr. Eilers went on to say that to set the Post Office matter aside for the time
being would allow the Council to clearly get on with other business on the
table, such as streetscape, Village Hall renovation, and a possible parking
deck in the East Elm business district.
Trustee Poor voiced reluctance to identify any specific date because there are
as yet too many unknowns and the Council can achieve what it wants and allow
the process to unfold without inserting a date.
There was a general consensus among the Trustees not to specify a date.
The Trustees went on to discuss the inclusion of language encouraging the
continued existence of a retail Post Office on the site.
Trustee Eilers remarked that the retailers rely on the Post Office to bring
people into the neighborhood. He pointed out, however, that this Council cannot
ensure that a Post Office will remain.
Both President Woodbury and Manager Williams noted that ongoing conversations
with the Post Office are of a positive, cooperative nature.
President Woodbury then opened the floor for comments from the audience.
Ann Wilder and Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce Street, Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Road, and
Jeffery Liss, 1364 Edgewood, shared their thoughts on points noted the draft
document.
Susan Boatman, President of the Library Board, 636 Garland, commended the
Council on its core principles and reported on the current status of her
Board’s activity. She indicated that they hope to have their needs assessment
completed by early fall and thanked the Council for stepping back to allow this
process to move forward.
Julian D’Esposito, 640 Blackthorn, Chairman of the Ad Hoc Post Office
Committee, remarked that he is pleased that the Library is going forward to
investigate this alternative since the Committee strove so hard to get this
issue on the table, even though some people may not like the consequence of the
delay. He went on to comment on the proposed draft principles, saying that he
believes some to be inconsistent with the direction in which the Council is
headed. He voiced approval for the direction the Council is headed, but he
encouraged the Trustees not to adopt principles that might be inappropriate in
the context of developing a Library on the site, i.e., ownership of
improvements on the site and limiting development to a “modest” size.
Lastly, Mr. D’Esposito recommended that while the Library moves ahead with its
investigation of a possible new Library, if the goal is to combine the Post
Office Site and Dwyer Park, then the three governmental bodies, i.e., Village,
Park District, and Library that would necessarily be involved, should get
together at the earliest opportunity to determine whether this option is really
feasible, and if so, how it would work. He pointed out that the details of
substantial real estate transactions amongst the parties must be hammered out.
Whether or not those details can be worked out has a significant impact on
whether or not a Library is even possible. He added that if the Library option
is not going to be possible, then the Village needs to know so it can proceed
with another alternative, and noted that the proposed principles are limiting
if this turns out to be the case.
Nan Greenough, 500 Maple, a member of the Ad Hoc Post Office Committee,
concurred that the proposed principles may not be relevant based on changed
circumstances. She voiced concern that the implied definition of a public use
is very narrow. She urged the Council not to consider the Library in a vacuum,
but to remember that the site is in a business district and that whatever is
ultimately developed needs to address existing conditions and uses and improve
the synergies thereof.
Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka Ave., thanked the Council for keeping the Post
Office planning process transparent, respecting peoples’ opinions, and keeping
the land for the public.
After further discussion, President Woodbury asked the Village Attorney to
prepare a draft resolution which would formally set out the proposed Post
Office planning and development principles as modified by the Council’s
discussions for consideration at the April 1 st meeting.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1549 and 1550. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1549 in the amount of $1,269,254.15 and Warrant List No. 1550 in the amount of
$295,772.89. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Certificate of Appropriateness for Installation of Public Art – First Bank
& Trust, ––––100 Green Bay Road. Mr. Norkus reviewed a request to install an
outdoor sculpture on the property of the First Bank and Trust at 100 Green Bay
Road. The Bank is proposing to install a “Cool Globe” similar to ones that can
be seen in downtown Chicago. He reported that the Design Review Board had voted
unanimously to recommend issuance of a Certificate of Appropriateness.
Mr. Norkus explained that the only reason this matter is before the Council is
that the property is in the C-1 zoning district. He went on to note that the
installation of the globe on the Bank’s property would require the redesign of
a low brick wall, and he suggested that the Design Review Board be authorized
to grant final approval of the necessary redesign.
Noting the applicant is a client of his firm, Trustee Behles recused himself
from the discussion and vote on this matter.
Trustee Tucker asked if there had been any objections raised at the Design
Review meeting. Mr. Norkus replied that there had been none.
Trustee Poor remarked that similar globes can be seen throughout Chicago and
that he finds them tasteful and has no objections to the proposed installation.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to authorize the issuance of
a Certificate of Appropriateness for the installation of a “Cool Globes”
sculpture, provided it is located entirely on the private property at 100 Green
Bay Road and to authorize the Design Review Board to issue final approval of
the necessary redesign of the low brick wall. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
c)   Bid 08-009: Landscape Maintenance. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for
landscape maintenance of various public properties and Village facilities. He
recommended that the bid be awarded to Anthony Scopelliti for the amounts
quoted in Bid #08-009. He noted that although the low bid was received from
KGI, the Village’s most recent vendor, the Village was not satisfied with their
performance and some of their bid prices appear to be unsustainably low.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid for
landscape maintenance of various public properties and Village facilities to
Anthony Scopelliti Landscaping, Inc. as quoted in Bid #08-009. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Behles,
Eilers, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Parkway Tree Trimming, Removal and Maintenance. Mr. Saunders reviewed the
bids for parkway tree trimming, removal and maintenance. He recommended that
the bid be awarded to Nels Johnson Tree Experts, Inc., for the unit rates
contained in their bid proposal.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Electrical Line Clearance (Tree Trimming). Mr. Keys reviewed the bids for
utility line clearance, parkway tree trimming, and emergency services. He
recommended that the bid be awarded to Nels Johnson Tree Experts, Inc., at the
bid rates, with a total cost not to exceed $90,000.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Purchase of Replacement Truck – PW 11. Mr. Saunders reviewed a request by
the Public Works Department to replace a 1999 drive dump/plow truck that is in
poor condition and beyond its useful life. He recommended that the Village
purchase a replacement vehicle through the State of Illinois Cooperative
Purchasing Program from Bob Ridings Ford for $58,714.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to authorize the purchase as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Ritchell, Tucker, Eilers, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Annual Outdoor Seating Permits & Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License
Riders. As noted earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. President Woodbury reported that a generous donation
from Arthur Nielsen had been received, kicking off the fundraising campaign for
the renovation of the Cenotaph.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Tucker encouraged everyone to enjoy the Chamber of Commerce’s
annual 2008 Art Walk between from now until March 22 nd and support local
merchants.
   ii)   Trustee Behles reported that the Willow Road Steering Committee has
completed its formal meetings, and its findings are being reviewed. The
Committee will reconvene on April 15 th for ratification.
   iii)   Trustee Berger reported on a recent NWMC Board meeting.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that as a result of the Village’s
participation in the Metropolitan Mayors Caucus Greenest Region Compact of
Metropolitan Chicago, Winnetka would be receiving 3,100 free CFLs. He said that
Staff is currently working on a distribution plan.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing board appointments, personnel matters, and pending and probable
litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(3), 2(c)(1), and 2(c)(11) respectively,
of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into
Executive Session at 10:58 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:15 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Ritchell, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
