____________________________
   Recording Secretary
April 1, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: April 18, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
1, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Public Works
Director Steve Saunders, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Finance Director Ed McKee,
and approximately 15 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 5(c), the March 18 th Regular Meeting
Minutes, and Item 6(d), Resolution No. R-18-2008, Release of Executive Session
Minutes, have been removed from the Agenda. The Trustees approved the Agenda as
amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   April 8, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   April 15, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   February 26, 2008, Budget Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   March 11, 2008, Study Session. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Ritchell,
moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   March 18, 2008, Regular Meeting. As noted earlier, this item was removed
from the Agenda.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-4-2008 – Landmark Designation: ––––592 Cherry Street–– –
Adoption. President Woodbury called for additional comments regarding the
ordinance granting local landmark status to the Joseph R. Gay House at 592
Cherry. There being none, he called for a motion to adopt the ordinance that
was introduced at the March 18 th Council meeting.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Ordinance M-4-2008,
designating the Gay house as a Winnetka Landmark. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers, Berger,
and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2008 – Amending Purchase Power Cost Adjustment –
Introduction. Manager Williams explained that this ordinance and the next
resolution would complete the process of implementing new purchase power cost
adjustment recovery methods that were discussed during budget sessions.
Ordinance No. MC-1-2008 amends the Village Code to set up the new structure,
and Resolution No. R-21-2008 puts the new rates in place.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Eilers, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-1-
2008, amending Section 13.08.160 of the Village Code. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
c)   Resolution No. R-21-2008 – Purchase Power Cost Adjustment for 2008-2009 –
Introduction. Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce
Resolution No. R-21-2008, setting the Wholesale Power Purchase Cost Adjustment
for 2008-09. By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Resolution No. R-18-2008 – Authorizing the Release of Executive Session
Minutes and the Destruction of Verbatim Recordings – Adoption. As noted
previously, this item was removed from the Agenda.
e)   Resolution No. R-20-2008 – MFT Resurfacing Various Streets – Adoption. Mr.
Saunders reported that various streets have been scheduled for resurfacing as
part of the Village’s Capital Improvement Projects for 2008, that the projects
will be funded solely with MFT funds, and indicated that expenditures for the
projects are not anticipated to exceed $200,000. This is, however, in addition
to the $1.1M that is budgeted from the General Fund for street refurbishing.
Mr. Saunders noted that a complete list of those streets to be refurbished
during this year’s construction season could be found in the new edition of the
Winnetka Report.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-20-2008.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None
8)   Old Business.
a)   Draft Resolution No. R-22-2008 – Post Office Principles – Discussion Only.
President Woodbury provided a brief review of the history of this matter. He
explained that at the last meeting the Village Attorney had been directed to
prepare, for discussion purposes only, the proposed draft resolution for the
purpose of clearly setting out this Council’s intentions with regard to the
redevelopment of the Post Office.
Manager Williams noted that the absolute deadline for this Council to adopt any
such resolution would be May 6 th .
President Woodbury pointed out that everything that has taken place thus far is
part of the record for the future and will be taken into account for any
decision-making process that ensues. He referred to a handout suggesting one
possible recital omitted from the proposed draft referring to the Comprehensive
Plan, recognizing that the Plan is a guide only.
Trustee Tucker supported the inclusion of such a recital, saying that the
Comprehensive Plan is an important land use document and it therefore makes
sense to include some reference to it for background purposes.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to include the proposed recital.
Trustee Behles remarked that although he would prefer to see specific reference
made to the goals and objectives identified by the Post Office Committee, the
draft resolution is, in fact, a reflection of the consensus of this Council and
serves as an excellent policy statement. He opined that as a planning
development document, he believes it to be lacking because it does not
articulate anything about alternate uses or about what the Village is trying to
achieve on the Site. He expressed concern that as planning principles, some of
the items may not apply because they do not necessarily relate to the stated
policy. He said, however, that it is important that the Council adopt this as a
policy statement to move the matter forward so that the next Council has this
Council’s input.
Trustee Eilers agreed with Mr. Behles, saying that although the document is not
worded exactly as he would have stated it, in the spirit of moving forward, he
is in support of both the resolution and the principles as drafted.
Trustee Berger remarked that this resolution and the principles attached
thereto are intended as a continuing enhancement of stated principles; it is
not intended to rewrite the Post Office Committee’s report, nor has it been
diluted to obtain consensus. She said the Post Office Committee report still
stands and is important, as it reflects a great deal of time, energy and money
expended by the Village on this project.
Trustee Ritchell indicated that he is comfortable with the draft as written,
saying that it does not eliminate anything that has gone before; it simply
states where this Council thinks the Village is at this point.
After a brief discussion, there was agreement to delete the language referring
to owning any improvement constructed on the Site.
Responding to Trustee Behles’ comments, Trustee Tucker remarked that there
clearly has to be some inherent flexibility assumed because there are so many
unknowns.
Trustee Eilers indicated that he believes the document reflects a basic
understanding, and is clearly only intended as a guide.
Trustee Poor agreed.
President Woodbury commented that the document reflects a balance and has a
dual purpose; it allows time for the Library to pursue its analysis and sets
out principles that will still be applicable as time passes. He then called for
comments from the audience.
Ann Wilder, 1096 Spruce Street, said that she doesn’t think the Village should
necessarily rule out owning the Library and reiterated her hope that any
meetings among members of the three governing boards will be open to the
public.
President Woodbury responded that the Village does not want to create another,
quasi-shadow board. As previously noted, the Council will appoint a Trustee
liaison who will attend each of the other governmental board’s meetings and
report back on an informal basis, just as the liaison to the NWMC or the
Historical Society does.
9)   New Business.
a)   Annual Outdoor Seating Permits & Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License
Riders. Manager Williams reported that the Village has historically approved
outdoor seating permits and sidewalk restaurant liquor license riders for one
row of tables relatively near the building, immediately in front of the
restaurants, so as not to cause difficulty for pedestrians. However, in the
last few years, some restaurateurs have begun placing a second row of tables on
the sidewalk, resulting in complaints from residents. He indicated that while
Staff recommends granting both the requested outdoor seating permits and the
sidewalk restaurant liquor license riders, Staff recommends against allowing
the second row of tables on the sidewalks. He asked the Council to provide
policy direction.
Mr. Saunders explained that some of the sidewalks are only ten feet wide,
making it very difficult to maintain the five-foot pedestrian clearance
specified by the Village, and creating challenges if there are two rows of
tables.
Mr. McKee reported that there have been instances in which a pedestrian is
forced to walk on the tree grates, resulting in the filing of a claim against
the Village.
Trustee Behles mentioned that in other municipalities, some appropriate
demarcation line from the curb is established and barriers are set up forming a
corral of sorts. Restaurateurs are permitted to put as many tables as possible
between their establishment and the barrier.
Trustee Poor remarked that table creep is a reality that has to be recognized.
The matter is one of allowing the proper movement of people.
Manager Williams noted that historically, the Village has attempted to work
cooperatively with the cafes without any formal regulation to keep acceptable
passageways open. He added that while sidewalk liquor rider licensees are
already required by Code to provide some sort of barriers, they do encroach on
the available space, making it even tighter.
Mitra Ryndak, proprietor of Café Aroma, objected to limiting tables to one row,
noting that to do so would particularly impact her situation and business. She
pointed out that the season for outdoor seating is short, demand is high, and
outdoor cafes bring life and vitality to the downtown.
President Woodbury said that it is the Village’s intent to administer each
application consistently.
Kaveh Mirani, proprietor of Mirani’s, also spoke in opposition to regulating
outdoor seating.
Trustee Eilers suggested that Ms. Ryndak might contact her neighbors to see if
they would agree to let her expand her service to the outside of the immediate
sidewalk in front of her establishment so as to avoid the necessity for a
second row of tables, or perhaps, just put up some sort of barrier as
recommended by Trustee Behles.
Trustee Behles agreed that the Village wants to promote sidewalk activity and
doesn’t want to arbitrarily limit the number of tables, so long as there is
adequate passageway.
Trustee Poor concurred, saying that we want to provide outdoor seating in
addition to making sure that the sidewalks are safe.
Trustee Berger indicated that she believes it would be a good idea for Ms.
Ryndak to contact her neighbors.
Trustee Ritchell said that the safety of citizens needs to be the first
concern. Restaurants must maintain at least a five foot passageway, regardless
of the width of their sidewalks.
There was a general consensus that applicants can approach their neighbors
about placing tables in front of their businesses and that if two rows of
seating are to be permitted, the applicant must set up barriers to maintain the
required passageway.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the nine
applications for outdoor seating permits for the period April 1 through
November 30, 2008, contingent upon the Village Engineer’s final approval of
table layouts after the tables are placed, with the understanding that a five
foot passageway must be maintained and if there are two proposed rows of
seating, a barrier between the tables and the passageway must be in place. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the four
applications for sidewalk liquor license riders. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Tucker. Nays: None.
Absent: None. Trustee Ritchell abstained.
b)   Warrant Lists 1551 and 1552. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
15551 in the amount of $635,754.45 and Warrant List No. 1552 in the amount of
$424,093.86. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   2008 Parkway Tree Planting Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bidding process
and bids for the 2008 Parkway Tree Planting Program and reviewed the Village’s
options for awarding the project. He recommended that the bid be awarded
jointly to ’’St.’ Aubins and Alaniz, splitting the bid between the two low
overall bidders.
Manager Williams confirmed with Mr. Saunders that these bid prices would be
available to residents who wish to participate in the Village’s private tree
planting program, and President Woodbury encouraged residents to do so.
Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to award the contract for
the 2008 Tree Planting Program jointly to St. Aubins and Alaniz, based on the
lower price per tree species, as submitted in their respective unit price bid
proposals. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Tucker,
Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, and Ritchell. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Local and Long Distance Phone Service. Mr. McKee reviewed his summary
report of the renewal process and recommended that the Council authorize him to
negotiate phone carrier services for the Village, with the assistance of
Indaplex, the Village’s phone consultant. He noted that Indaplex is compensated
on an hourly basis.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to accept Staff’s
recommendation and to authorize the Finance Director to enter into negotiations
for phone carrier service for the Village. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, Tucker and Behles.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Copier Leases. Mr. McKee reported that the Village currently leases copier
machines in various departments, as the practice is more cost-effective than
purchasing them. He reviewed his summary of several Xerox copiers currently in
use that are nearing the expiration of their existing five-year leases, and
outlined the expected changes in cost for the new lease period. He recommended
that the Council authorize him to execute a contract with Xerox to implement
the copier changes proposed.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to accept Staff’s
recommendation and to authorize the Finance Director to enter into a contract
with Xerox for new leases of copier machines. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Berger, and Eilers.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Woodley Road Association Contract. Mr. McKee reported that the Village
provides limited police services to the Woodley Road Association, which is an
unincorporated area south of the Village, and that a new contract was approved
last year that requires the Council to set the price annually. He indicated
that staff had been directed to use the same percentage increase as that of the
Police Department Operating Budget in calculating cost adjustments, so the
amount for this fiscal year is 3.6%.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to increase the contract
for the Woodley Road Association police service by 3.6%. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Berger, Eilers, and
Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
g)   Upgrade the Police 9-1-1 Communications System. Chief DeLopez briefly
reviewed his previous report to the Council regarding this matter. That report
explained that the Police Department’s current 9-1-1 communications system is
out of date, and the existing equipment and furniture is not suitable for being
relocated or expanded to accommodate a future Combined Dispatch facility. He
also reviewed the RFP process and the responses thereto and reiterated his
recommendation that the Village accept the proposal of J&L/ModuCom. He then
responded to questions from the Trustees, noting that he anticipates at least
two years for the project to be completed.
Manager Williams reported that he had recently conferred with the Village of
Wilmette and their technical committee and been assured that these plans are in
harmony with what is going on in Wilmette and that this equipment will be
compatible as the Villages move forward.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to award the contract to J&L/
ModuCom in the amount of $270,207.20, plus an additional $25,000 for
contingency purposes. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Berger, and Eilers. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. Mr. Woodbury announced that residents would be
receiving a mailing about upcoming community meetings regarding the
recommendations of the Ad-hoc Historic & Residential preservation Committee. He
noted that this is an important residential land use discussion for the Council
and the community arising out of issues identified in Caucus questionnaires.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Berger reported that a recent meeting sponsored by the NWMC
regarding public safety pension funding had been well attended.
   ii)   Trustee Eilers announced that the Ad-hoc Residential Design Committee
would be seeking community input at upcoming community workshops. He encouraged
residents to attend and share their views.
      Trustee Eilers also reported that the Hazelton Clinic offers programs for
families experiencing drug and/or alcohol related problems and urged anyone who
has such problems to attend to help avoid tragic outcomes.
c)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that for the fifteenth year in a row
Winnetka has once again been designated a Tree City by the Arbor Day
Foundation.
11)   Appointments.
a)   President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Mark Crane to the Board
of Police and Fire Commissioners for a full term. Trustee Berger, seconded by
Trustee Ritchell, so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Chuck Page as the
Design Review Board’s representative to the Forestry Commission. Trustee
Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, so moved. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Ritchell moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Personnel Matters and the Purchase or Lease of Village Real Estate,
pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) and 2(c)(5) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker.
Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:20
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers,
Poor, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:16 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
April 8, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: April 15, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
8, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike
D’Onofrio, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric
Brian Keys, Trustees-elect Chris Rintz, Gene Greable and Bill Johnson, and
approximately 16 persons in the audience.
2)   Discussion: Formation of Environmental & Forestry Commission.
President Woodbury gave an overview of the Council’s reasons for exploring the
formation of an environmental committee. He noted that Trustees Behles and Poor
were given the task of investigating the creation of the potential new
commission and providing recommendations to the Council, and called on them to
describe their findings.
Trustee Behles reported that he and Trustee Poor proposed forming a new
commission from the Forestry Commission, which already deals with environmental
issues. He reviewed their report, which included a draft mission statement for
the proposed Environmental and Forestry Commission, proposed duties and
functions, and recommendations for the composition and qualifications of the
new commission.
Trustee Poor observed that most neighboring towns already have environmental
committees, and noted that there is a real need in Winnetka to provide focus
and expertise to environmental initiatives, given growing public awareness of
global warming, the need to protect the environment has arisen, and the
leadership role local governments have been taking in this area.
The Trustees discussed the ramifications of adding to the Forestry Commission’s
workload, and reached consensus on combining the functions into one committee
due to the overlap in the forestry and environmental areas. They also agreed
that Winnetka should take a leadership position in this area, given the
Village’s excellent record on environmental issues.
President Woodbury noted the need to amend the Village Code and add two members
to form the new Commission. He then asked for comment from the members of the
Forestry Commission who were present.
Ms. Mary Hill, Chairman of the Forestry Commission, stated that the Forestry
Commission has not had a chance to meet and discuss the proposal, but observed
that the current Commission members consider themselves environmentalists by
nature. She agreed that the Forestry Commission is the logical body to take on
the added environmental mission, but noted that more staff support would be
needed, and expressed the hope that the commission could get many things
accomplished.
Manager Williams acknowledged the need for more staff support, and said that in
addition to Mr. Saunders and Mr. Keys, he has proposed hiring an assistant
public works director to be a liaison to the new Environmental & Forestry
Commission.
The remaining Forestry Commission members also supported creating a combined
Forestry & Environmental Commission, but expressed concern over the potential
impact of the additional workload and noted the probable need for additional
staff support.
Manager Williams and Attorney Janega indicated that the necessary ordinance
could be brought before the Council in May.
President Woodbury observed that the new commission would provide a forum for
discussing and raising policy issues that ultimately would be addressed by the
Council. He added that the Council would proceed and that an ordinance would be
introduced in May.
3)   Discussion: Proposed Streetscape Program.
Mr. Saunders gave a brief history of the Streetscape project, and reviewed the
recommendations of the Ad Hoc Streetscape Committee. He reported that the
estimated cost of the improvements outlined in the three-phase Master Plan is
$21.3 million in 2007 dollars, and noted that, to advance the project within
the Village’s funding limitations, Staff was directed to develop a program to
implement portions of Phase I of the Master Plan over a 5-year period.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the various implementation options, their proposed
timelines and several funding strategies, and recommended identifying a revenue
stream so future phases of the project could be undertaken.
Discussion ensued about the possibility of taking over jurisdiction of Green
Bay Road in Hubbard Woods, which would make implementation of the lighting
standards easier to accomplish. Mr. Saunders indicated that a jurisdictional
transfer would involve lengthy negotiations with IDOT and could take several
years to complete.
Different funding alternatives were also discussed, and Manager Williams noted
that although a portion of the cash reserves could be used, other revenue will
be needed to supplement them. Manager Williams observed that an important issue
to consider when planning future streetscape implementation projects is the
perception in Hubbard Woods that the Village is more concerned with the Elm
Street business districts than with Hubbard Woods.
Trustee Behles stated that Mr. Saunders’ report does an excellent job of trying
to find pieces of the Streetscape Plan that can be implemented, and suggested
that the Village focus on projects that have the highest impact on the business
districts and that are on roadways that the Village controls. He noted that
once a project is approved, it takes about a year to go through design,
engineering and bidding, and expressed concern that involving IDOT in the
process will cause it to drag on for another few years. He added that he favors
funding the first project as soon as possible.
Trustee Eilers agreed that some projects could be done right away and shouldn’t
be delayed while the Village deals with IDOT. He also noted the need for
streetscape improvements.
Mr. Greg Byers, 1479 Asbury, urged the Council to take action on Streetscape to
enhance the vibrancy of the community and to start jurisdictional transfer
negotiations with IDOT right away.
Mr. Bill Shiel, of 566 Willow and chairman of the BCDC, observed that the BCDC
has been talking about a streetscape project for several years and believes it
is the most important way to improve the business districts. He urged the
Council to investment in the business districts right away, not in years to
come.
Ms. Pat Horne, 791 Bryant, expressed disappointment with the Village’s
commitment to the business districts and commented that the Metra stations and
downtown areas are a disgrace, with litter on the streets and orange cones
covering the stumps of trees that have been removed.
Ms. Roberta Rubin, 811 Elm Street, expressed disillusionment with the level of
support the Village provides to the business community and said something needs
to be done as soon as possible.
Ms. Linda Pedian, 510 Sheridan, also thought immediate action was called for,
and said the Village needs to be inviting for new businesses to come in and
fill the vacancies, and noted that safety is a concern at intersections. She
suggested that amenities like benches, planters and even light poles be funded
by donations.
Mr. Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, said he felt that Winnetkans would not be in favor
of spending funds on Streetscape improvements, and complained that there was
not enough community involvement in the process leading up to the
recommendations. He said he felt that the economy and big box stores have more
to do with the vacancy rate than the condition of the business districts, and
commented that if something is done in the near future, to make sure it will
have a positive effect before embarking on the project.
Ms. Cicely Michalak, Director of the Chamber of Commerce, said she felt that
support from the Village for the business districts is mandatory, and that a
project could be done using cash reserves and without impacting Green Bay Road.
Several more audience members spoke up in favor of choosing a streetscape
project to implement right away, and all felt that aesthetics are crucial to
the success of the business districts.
President Woodbury said the question in his mind was to get the most value out
of its investment, said he views streetscape improvements as an investment in
infrastructure and not just beautification. He stated that there is a money
issue, regardless of the project size, and outlined four parallel paths that he
thought were important for consideration:
   ∙   He encouraged the Council to authorize a pilot, demonstration project.
   ∙   He proposed reforming the Streetscape Committee to work with Staff to
hone in on details, especially on the first project.
   ∙   He urged the Trustees to give serious thought about how to fund the
projects.
   ∙   He suggested holding detailed public meetings in each business district
before embarking on the first major project.
Trustee Behles stated that he felt the Streetscape Committee had already honed
in on the particulars and that the committee doesn’t have the expertise to
parse out the details without an engineering plan, but that he had no problem
reconvening the committee to determine a first project. He reiterated that it
takes a year to do the engineering, get bids and complete the paperwork, and
that the Council needs to think about when to decide on a course of action.
Trustees Tucker and Ritchell agreed with President Woodbury that reforming the
Streetscape Committee was a good idea.
Trustee Berger also agreed that recalling the Streetscape Committee was a good
idea, added that the plan as it stands now is almost designed to fail because
it is so expensive, and observed that some of the smaller concerns, such as
garbage on the streets, the orange cones, unattractive posters and signage,
etc. could be addressed.
Trustee Poor observed that the Village needs to support the business districts
and seconded Trustee Berger’s comments, and also spoke in favor of doing the
pilot program right away.
Trustee Eilers also agreed that reforming the Streetscape Committee was a good
idea, and noted that the business districts need the streetscape program and
that time is of the essence.
Manager Williams suggested that the Council give the Streetscape Committee a
sense of the amount that the Village can expend out of its cash reserves, and
let them figure out how to spend it. He agreed with Mr. Byers’ comments that it
is time to start negotiating with IDOT on a jurisdictional transfer of Green
Bay Road in Hubbard Woods. He indicated that if the Streetscape Committee can
get its report to the Council in August or September, the Village should be in
a position to let bids by the end of this year or early next year.
President Woodbury observed that the pilot project could be completed in 2008,
and said it would make a good example to refer to when the public meetings are
held.
4)   Manager’s Report:
a)   Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to report on the status of the Hubbard
Woods pedestrian bridge. Mr. Saunders answered that Union Pacific Railroad has
scheduled structural repairs on the bridge to commence early next week, and
noted that the railroad’s engineers did not feel that the bridge needs to be
closed in the interim.
b)   Next, Mr. Saunders reported that IDOT has extra grant money for
communities that maintain state roads because the effects of last winter were
particularly harsh. He said he requested $112,000, and that it looks like the
Village will receive the full amount.
c)   Manager Williams asked Attorney Janega to review a preliminary draft
resolution concerning the intentions of the outgoing Council with regard to
bulk and mass in the Zoning Ordinance. Attorney Janega reviewed several major
points, asked the Council to provide her with comments and said the draft
resolution will likely be introduced at the next Council meeting.
d)   Manager Williams observed that the ivy had been removed from Village Hall,
based on the architect’s report and Council’s direction, and that the outside
of the building is clearly in need of repair.
5)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Personnel Matters, pursuant to Section 2(c)(1) of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 10:23 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at xx p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Behles, Berger,
Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
xx p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
April 15, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: May 6, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
15, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Tom Eilers, King Poor, Herb Ritchell and Jessica
Tucker. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Director of
Community Development Michael D’Onofrio, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of
Water & Electric Brian Keys and approximately 16 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   May 6, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
b)   May 13, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustees Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend,
as did the three Trustees-elect.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   March 18, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee
Tucker, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   April 1, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   April 8, 2008, Study Session. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Ritchell, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution No. R-22-2008 – Post Office Principles – Adoption. President
Woodbury noted the one additional clause referencing the Comprehensive Plan
added to the preamble reflecting Council’s discussion at the last meeting. He
then called for additional comments from the audience and the Council.
Trustee Ritchell remarked that the Resolution represents a good record of the
Council’s thoughts and provides a good guide for future Councils.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, asked whether the Council intends to put the Post
Office project on hold as indicated in the Resolution, and urged the Council to
move forward with gathering data and investigating a mixed use alternative, to
avoid additional delay in the final redevelopment.
President Woodbury responded that the Council wants to give the Library time to
move forward with their process. He explained that, regardless of the Library’s
final determination, the Resolution sets forth this Council’s thinking about
core development principles, but added that this Council cannot bind a future
Council. He noted that the Resolution does not call for an indefinite pause in
its consideration of other alternatives, and pointed out that this is a
transitional phase that gives the Library Board time while allowing for the
issue to be passed from the current Council to the next.
Trustee Eilers commented that the Council is aware that it could simultaneously
explore both the Library and mixed use tracks, but has decided as a policy
matter to hold off for now so the Library can conduct its analysis and the
Village can explore other land use issues, such as combining the Post Office
parcel with Dwyer Park.
Manager Williams remarked that the Village is not standing idly by, but is
continuing its negotiations with the Post Office for retaining a retail
facility, regardless of which development option is chosen in the end.
Trustee Behles added that the Village has already studied a great deal of
information on the mixed use option, but has not yet thoroughly investigated
the Library alternative, so as a matter of policy, this Council is putting the
matter aside for awhile, so the Library option can be fully considered and has
the opportunity to become as viable an option as the mixed use option.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-22-2008.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Woodbury expressed thanks to the Post Office Committee for their many
hours of hard work, to staff for their support and assistance in sorting out
policy matters, to the public for raising their voices to be heard, and lastly,
to the Council for their careful consideration of the issues. He said that
whichever option the Village ultimately pursues, the direction provided by this
Council will be valuable.
b)   Ordinance No. M-6-2008 – Zoning Variation: 616 Garland Ave. –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for Maximum Building Size, Front
(Corner) Yard Setback, and Garage variations to permit the construction of a
one-story addition. In addition, the applicants are requesting the variations
to expand the existing one-car attached garage at the basement level into a
two-car attached garage with front-facing garage doors below the first floor of
the principal building. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted
7 to 0 to recommend in favor of the requested variations.
The applicants’ architect, Healy Rice, explained the need for the variation and
responded to questions from the Trustees.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Poor remarked that although he does not
usually see the lack of a two-car garage in and of itself as a hardship, given
the location near North Shore Country Day School and the fact that the proposal
does not have an adverse impact on neighbors’ light and green space, he would
support the variation.
Trustee Behles concurred, noting that a significant portion of the addition is
on a sloping grade, which minimizes its impact.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to introduce Ordinance M-6-
2008. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Resolution No. R-19-2008 – Extension of Sidewalk Replacement Contract –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders recommended that the Council award a one-year extension
of the June 2007 contract with Schroeder & Schroeder, Inc. for sidewalk
replacement services, explaining that the contract sets terms for the extension
and makes the rate increase equivalent to the increase in the Chicagoland CPI
between January 2007 and January 2008, or +4.7%.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adopt Resolution R-19-
2008 authorizing an extension of the Village’s contract as recommended by the
Director of Public Works. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Eilers, Berger, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2008 – Village Code Amendments Regarding Floodplain
Regulation – Introduction. Mr. Saunders explained that the subject resolution
would revise map and elevation references in the Village Code in accordance
with FEMA’s recent map modernization program. He emphasized that these
revisions will not change the current boundaries of the floodplain in Winnetka.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-2-2008. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Resolution No. R-18-2008 – Authorizing the Release of Executive Session
Minutes and the Destruction of Verbatim Recordings – Adoption. Manager Williams
explained that the Open Meetings Act requires the Council to examine and
approve Executive Session minutes and to release minutes for which
confidentiality is no longer required. He also explained that the Act also
requires the Village to retain verbatim recordings of closed sessions for 18
months, noting that the Act makes the recordings available only to a judge, not
to the general public.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-18-
2008 releasing the Executive Session minutes from July 17, 2007 through March
18, 2008 that are no longer required for reasons of confidentiality, and
permitting the destruction of audio recordings of closed meetings from August
16, 2005 through September 19, 2006. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Eilers, Berger, Poor, Ritchell, Tucker, and Behles.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Resolution No. R-23-2008 – Policy Statement on Single-family Zoning
Regulations – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed resolution
reflecting comments she received from the Trustees on the preliminary draft.
She explained that she has attempted to reflect the Council’s discussion from
the March 11 th Study Session and to set forth this Council’s position on
several issues not ready for final determination: over-building, bulk
regulations and contextual building design. She noted that the Resolution is
framed as this Council’s “desire and request” that the incoming Council
considers related zoning amendments at the earliest reasonable opportunity. She
cautioned against adding too much detail to the requested action and emphasized
that, as with the Post Office resolution adopted earlier, this Council can
convey its thinking on a matter but cannot bind a future Council.
Trustee Tucker noted that design and zoning issues overlap and said the
resolution was drafted with the hope of articulating the frustration that many
people have voiced with overbuilding and bulk. She expressed the hope that the
Village’s design consultant, Nore Winter & Associates, would pursue a review of
the impact of the current provisions of the Zoning Ordinance in conjunction
with the review of design issues.
Manager Williams reported that the Council should be discussing a proposal from
Mr. Winter in the near future.
Trustee Behles voiced his opinion that the subject resolution represents a
strong and clear statement of this Council’s view of the issue without trying
to make recommendations on specific ordinance components.
Trustee Eilers agreed that the general direction is appropriate, but also
voiced some concern that a number of the statements are opinion, not
necessarily fact.
Trustee Poor stated that he sees the resolution as a very constructive and
positive document that represents the Council’s thinking after countless hours
of discussion and consideration and harvests the collective wisdom of the
retiring Trustees. He noted that the issue comes up on many levels, and this is
the first concrete step toward addressing the problem of overbuilding and
teardowns that has been prevalent throughout the community for some time.
Trustee Ritchell agreed with the spirit of the resolution and expressed
satisfaction with the draft.
Mr. Liss inquired as to whether the wording would exclude modern architecture
and the use of solar panels or new building techniques in the future.
President Woodbury responded that it would not.
In response to further discussion, Attorney Janega suggested deleting
everything after the word “run-off” in the sixth recital of the preamble, and
deleting the reference to preserving open space in the eighth recital of the
preamble.
Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Tucker, moved to adopt the resolution
with the modifications suggested by the Village Attorney. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Behles,
Eilers, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Woodbury congratulated the Council for making this move, saying it is
not often that Councils adopt resolutions indicating their intent.
****
President Woodbury then moved to the discussion of Item 9(a).
9)   a)   Policy Direction –––––455 Sheridan Road.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a front yard setback zoning variation to
permit construction of a new single-family residence at 455 Sheridan Road. The
subject property is a lake-front flag lot, and the front yard setback for such
properties has consistently been interpreted to be the west lot line, excluding
the “flagpole” portion that adjoins the public street. The proposed new
construction would not meet the minimum required 50-ft front yard setback from
that line, but rather would have a front yard set back of 10.21 feet for the
proposed attached garage. He noted that the existing residence currently
provides a front yard setback of approximately 7.47 feet, and that except for
the requested setback variation, the proposed residence complies with all other
zoning requirements. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals
had voted 6 to 1 to recommend against the variation request.
The applicant, James Findlay, and his attorney, Susan Connor, explained that
the main reasons for requesting the variation are to save mature trees on the
bluff side of the house and protect neighbors’ lake views and property values.
In response to questions from the Trustees about a conforming alternative, Ms.
Connor argued that a conforming alternative represents the worst possible
scenario because it pushes the house to the bluff edge, compromising both the
bluff and the adjacent neighbors’ views. She maintained that moving the new
construction closer to the water would encourage other new construction to be
built closer to the water’s edge. Ms. Connor pointed out that the applicant is
proposing a house that is significantly smaller than the permitted FAR, and
asserted that the proposed setback would be consistent with the front setbacks
of its neighbors. She then distributed drawings of an alternative design that
could be built on the property, stating that the proposed design is relatively
small by comparison and was carefully designed to balance the interests of the
applicants with those of the neighbors.
In response to a question from Trustee Poor about the neighbors’ positions, Ms.
Connor pointed to letters of support in the Agenda packet.
Trustee Poor also inquired about the trees that would have to be removed to
build a conforming home.
Responding to a question from Trustee Poor about the effect of a conforming
home on existing trees, Mr. Findlay stated that three large trees would have to
be removed. He added that the proposed plan was designed only after the Village
Forester visited the site, and it has received his approval.
After a brief discussion about the possibility of reducing the GFA to achieve a
greater setback, Trustee Behles commented that the proposed front setback is
being driven by the desire to avoid front-facing garage doors, which, in turn,
requires that the garage extend far enough away from the body of the house to
allow the garage to be approached from the north. He noted that pushing the
garage back would push the whole house back, and that one of the arguments for
not pushing the garage back was that it would result in front-facing garage
doors that would need a variation. Noting that the lot’s configuration causes
its front yard to face the back yard of the adjoining property, not a street,
Trustee Behles defined the question as follows: whether it is more unacceptable
to have a big house close to the lot line without the front-facing garage
doors, or to have front-facing garage doors facing the back yard of the
adjacent property? He opined that given the unusual circumstances, the front-
facing garage doors are less offensive than a three-car garage close to
someone’s yard, and to him represents a better use of the site. He added,
however, that he does think the proposed house is very big, that he prefers the
proffered alternative in terms of how it is placed on the parcel, and that he
thinks there are probably other acceptable design options.
President Woodbury indicated that the Council could only consider the variation
application that has been presented, but must consider whether there are
conforming alternatives.
Trustee Poor distinguished this application from another involving an unusual
front yard setback variation, stating that this request has no effect on the
neighbors, who are supportive of the request. He acknowledged that the house is
large, but well within permitted limits. He opined that forcing a conforming
structure here would result in the loss of some grand trees, to the detriment
of the environment and possibly to the integrity of the bluff itself, and would
also adversely impact the neighbors’ views. Saying that he usually agrees with
the Zoning Board’s recommendations, he stated that he found the arguments in
this instance compelling and would therefore support the variation.
Trustee Berger expressed concern about the size of the variation being
requested and the overall bulk of the house. She indicated that she could not
see any practical difficulty or particular hardship, and thus would agree with
the Zoning Board.
Trustee Eilers agreed that the proposed house is large, but noted that it is
within the maximum limits. He added that he does not see forcing a conforming
alternative in this case as within anyone’s interest, and thus sees the
petitioner’s solution as a good one.
Trustee Behles indicated that he believes a variation is warranted in this
case, but not this particular variation.
Trustees Tucker and Ritchell agreed with Trustees Behles and Berger.
In response to a question from Ms. Connor, Trustee Behles suggested that a
proposal requiring a variation that might be looked upon more favorably by the
Council would be one that respects articulated site planning principles, but
does not necessarily go all the way to conforming.
Trustee Poor added that he believes the applicant has made a good case for not
building in a conforming location.
Trustee Ritchell commented that saving the trees and having the neighbors’
support are in the applicant’s favor.
Ms. Connor then requested that the variation request be withdrawn.
President Woodbury suggested that when the applicant comes back for
reconsideration, he present a tree survey.
*****
6)   President Woodbury then returned to the normal agenda order, at Item 6(g).
g)   Purchase Power Cost Adjustment. Attorney Janega explained that this
ordinance and the next resolution would complete the process of implementing
the new purchase power cost adjustment recovery methods that were discussed
during previous meetings. Ordinance MC-1-2008 amends the Village Code to set up
the new structure, and Resolution R-21-2008 puts the new rates in place. Both
documents have been amended slightly since introduction to assure consistent
terminology and incorporate actual end-of-year figures from IMEA. She said the
new rates would go into effect on May 1, 2008.
Mr. Keys summarized how the final calculations were determined and estimated
the increase for a residential customer at between $.50 and $1.85 per year. In
response to a question from Trustee Ritchell, Mr. Keys indicated that staff
will provide the Council with an annual update to ensure the new process is
working as expected.
   i)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2008 – Amending Purchase Power Cost Adjustment –
Adoption. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to amend
Ordinance MC-1-2008 in accordance with the Village Attorney’s revisions. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
      Trustee Ritchell, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt, as amended,
Ordinance MC-1-2008, amending Section 13.08.160 of the Village Code. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Ritchell, Tucker, Behles, Eilers,
Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-21-2008 – Purchase Power Cost Adjustment for 2008-
2009 – Adoption. Trustee Tucker, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to amend
Resolution R-21-2008 in accordance with the Village Attorney’s revisions. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
      Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt, as amended,
Resolution R-21-2008, setting the Wholesale Power Purchase Cost Adjustment for
2008-09. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Eilers,
Berger, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker.
h)   Salary Resolutions. President Woodbury remarked that these recommendations
and resolutions recognize the continued excellence of the staff and thanked
them all for their fine work during the past year.
   i)   Resolution No. R-27-2008 – Department Head Salaries – Adoption. Trustee
Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-27-2008. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Berger, Eilers, Behles,
Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   ii)   Resolution No. R-28-2008 – Village Manager Salary – Adoption. Trustee
Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-28-2008. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees, Berger, Eilers, Poor,
Behles, Ritchell and Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Road, suggested that in order to determine the real
reasons behind businesses leaving Winnetka, the Village conduct an exit survey
when business owners cut off their utility billing.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Policy Direction – ––––455 Sheridan Road. As noted above, this item was
discussed earlier in the meeting.
b)   Warrant Lists 1553 and 1554. Trustee Poor reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1553 in the amount of $757,669.64 and Warrant List No. 1554 in the amount of
$353,340.84. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Outdoor Seating Permit – Little Lan’s. Manager Williams reviewed a request
by Little Lan’s for an Outdoor Seating Permit.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the Permit. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. Mr. Woodbury reported that Senator Schoenberg had
announced plans to move forward to widen Willow Road to four lanes. He thanked
the members of the Willow Road Steering Committee for their many hours of study
and said the parties have understood from the beginning that the State was
ultimately in control of the process. He urged the Committee to finalize its
report and bring it to the Council.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Eilers reported that the Plan Commission’s discussion of the
Overlay District may conclude at next week’s meeting.
   ii)   Trustee Berger called residents’ attention to the SWANCC enclosure
regarding recycling that appeared in Sunday’s newspaper.
      Trustee Behles reported that the Streetscape Committee had reconvened and
hopes to move forward to bring a revised recommendation to the Council early
this summer.
      Trustee Ritchell reported on the recent public meetings regarding
historic preservation, saying that a second meeting will be held tomorrow
evening. He said there had been a good discussion and many ideas exchanged.
President Woodbury added that while passionate, the discussion had been civil
and balanced.
c)   Attorney: Attorney Janega reported that she was one of the speakers at the
recent IML Attorneys’ Conference.
d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Tucker moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Personnel Matters and the Purchase or Lease of Village Real Estate,
pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) and 2(c)(5) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Ritchell seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and
Tucker. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 10:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:59 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, and Tucker. Absent:
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Eilers, seconded by Trustee Ritchell, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
