   Recording Secretary
May 13, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: June 3, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
13, 2008 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Police
Chief Joe DeLopez, Assistant to the Village Manager Liz Rosenthal, and
approximately 8 persons in the audience.
2)   Discussion: Request for Liquor Code Amendments.
Attorney Janega reviewed Liquor Ordinance amendments made in 2007, and reported
that a couple of local restaurateurs have requested further amendments to
extend the hours for liquor service and further clarify the definition of
“service.” She indicated that a survey of neighboring communities revealed that
each has its own customized standard for liquor classifications and hours of
sale.
Chief DeLopez observed that the 2007 amendments were meant to make clear the
absolute cutoff time for the dispensing of alcohol, but that some confusion
remains with respect to service with and without food.
Assistant to the Village Manager Liz Rosenthal distributed a handout
summarizing the liquor codes from neighboring towns, and pointed out that each
community has its own way of communicating the hours of service, with some
villages having 25 or more classes of license, and others using business
districts or core areas to regulate liquor sales.
President Woodbury noted that the uncertainty seems to stem from the disparity
between hours of operation and hours of liquor service, and that the Council
could consider aligning them to alleviate confusion.
Chief DeLopez agreed with President Woodbury, and reiterated that the issue of
service with food needs clarification. He then reviewed a report of noise
disturbances from the business districts which seems to show that the sidewalk
restaurants have not been a contributing factor in noise complaints.
Trustee Behles said he favors extending the sidewalk liquor hours, to promote
activity and interest in the downtown areas.
Attorney Janega explained that the limitations on liquor-only sales resulted
from earlier interpretations of the Liquor Ordinance’s definition of
“restaurant,” which was drawn from State statutes. Manager Williams noted that
the Village ensures that the service of food is the primary business of the
establishment by requiring that the bar not be more than 10% of the total floor
area.
Mr. Kaveh Mirani, Mirani’s – 727 Elm, thanked the Council for their time and
indicated that the issue of “consumption” is complicated because the hours of
liquor sales are so short. He asked the Council to consider increasing the
hours, at least on weekends, so that patrons who order a drink close to the
sales cutoff time don’t have to consume the drink in a hurry. He also said he
was in favor of increasing the sidewalk hours of operation and liquor sales.
Mr. Patrick O’Neil, Little Ricky’s – 540 Lincoln, said the sidewalk is the big
issue in his mind because people like to go outside and smoke while they have a
drink, and that he would like the hours extended on weekends.
Betty Skalski, Phototronics; Roberta Rubin, The Book Stall; and Cicely Michalak
and Andy Bullock of the Chamber of Commerce, all expressed support for
expanding the hours of liquor service, both inside and on the sidewalk, to keep
the business districts thriving and vibrant.
The Council discussed the extension of hours, both indoors and outside, and
agreed to extend the sidewalk liquor hours to 10:00 PM on weekdays and 11:00 PM
on weekends. After ascertaining that Mr. Mirani and Mr. O’Neil had no
objections, they also agreed to align the hours for indoor restaurant liquor
sales and hours of business at 11:00 PM on weekdays and 12:00 AM on weekends.
3)   Draft Report of Recent Streetscape Committee Activities.
Trustee Behles reported that the Streetscape Committee had been reconvened for
the purpose of establishing an implementation program for Streetscape
improvements. He reviewed the objectives of establishing such a program and
outlined the Committee’s preliminary recommendations, which include:
   ∙   Officially adopt the Village of Winnetka Commercial District Streetscape
Report as the Village’s master plan for future streetscape redevelopment in the
three business districts for use as a roadmap by the Design Review Board, Plan
Commission, Zoning Board of Appeals and the Village Council.
   ∙   Commence a 5-year series of annual Streetscape projects that will
provide the most impact and value for the community.
   ∙   Undertake a “demonstration” project in 2008 to showcase all streetscape
elements and materials and give residents a chance to give their feedback.
Trustee Behles stated that the Committee recommends Hubbard Woods as the
location of the demonstration project, at the site of the new information
kiosk, adjacent to the Village parking lot on Tower and Green Bay Roads. He
added that the Streetscape Committee would rather see more intensive projects
completed in limited areas versus watered-down versions over a wider area. He
indicated that the strategy for the five-year plan will be to garner the most
value for the business community, which in the Committee’s view means
concentrating on the Hubbard Woods and Elm Street business districts.
President Woodbury noted that by adopting the Streetscape recommendations, the
Village will have input with developers in the redevelopment areas, so that
their work will fit into the Village’s streetscape plans.
Mr. Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, objected to officially adopting the Streetscape
Report until after public hearings have been held, but agreed with the concept
of a demonstration project, so the public can see what will be done and the
prices involved.
President Woodbury agreed that public hearings will have to be held before the
Streetscape Plan is adopted, and added that the demonstration project will help
people conceptualize the Streetscape Plan.
Mr. Saunders commented that there has been a reorganization at IDOT, and there
is a new bureau chief in the electric department who might be easier to work
with on the question of lighting issues.
After further discussion, the Council gave approval for the demonstration
project to move forward.
4)   Winnetka Cenotaph Condition Study and Proposed Repairs.
Mr. Saunders reported that the analysis of the condition of the Cenotaph has
been completed, and he reviewed the consultant’s report and cost estimates for
the project. He noted the formation of a private fundraising committee chaired
by Phil Hosa, 605 Cherry, to set goals and strategies for private fundraising
efforts to help pay for the restoration work.
Manager Williams recommended moving forward with the task of preparing final
designs and specifications, in the event that the Council wishes to complete
the work in 2008, adding that each freeze-thaw cycle will do further damage and
escalate restoration costs.
Trustee Behles voiced concern about the limited timeframe to collect donations
if the work were to commence after the Fourth of July, and Trustee Johnson
suggested waiting a year to let the fundraising run its course.
President Woodbury observed that the fundraising aspect is meant to build
community spirit and make a community event out of the restoration, but that
the overall issue is about preserving a local treasure.
Trustee Berger noted that the Council needs to be very careful about the money
it spends beyond delivering services, given the demands on Village resources,
and added that she felt waiting one more year for contributions to come in
would not do great harm to the 81-year old monument.
After a short discussion, the Council agreed to approve spending approximately
$20,000 on the final designs and specifications and to discuss the timing of
the restoration project when more was known about the fundraising efforts.
5)   Cell Phone Quarterly Report.
Chief DeLopez gave an overview of the enforcement results for the Village’s
hand-held cell phone prohibition as of March 1, 2008, one year after the
Winnetka Police Department started issuing citations for violations. He
reported that 15.7% of those cited were Village residents, while 84.3% resided
elsewhere, and he noted that hand-held cell phone violations are the second
highest traffic enforcement activity in the Village.
Chief DeLopez observed that the courts don’t take the cell-phone infractions
seriously, and dismiss or give supervision to most motorists who are ticketed.
He noted that there is not a strong desire in other municipalities to enact a
ban similar to Winnetka’s and that a state-wide ban would increase
effectiveness.
President Woodbury thanked Chief DeLopez, and indicated that future reports
could be delivered to the Council with their agenda packets, since it was no
longer necessary to continue doing formal reports at Council meetings.
6)   Executive Session.
None.
7)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee King, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
25 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
May 20, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 17, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
20, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, King Poor, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, and Chris Rintz. Absent:
Trustee Johnson. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Director of Community
Development Michael D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development
Brian Norkus, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys and approximately 20
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   June 3, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present, except
President Woodbury, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   June 10, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Rintz, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 6, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-7-2008 – Special Use Permit & Zoning Variations: 515
Hibbard Rd., Carlton Washburne School - Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a
request for a Special Use Permit and Intensity of Use of Lot and Maximum
Building Size variations from School District 36 to construct an addition on
the west side of the Carlton Washburne School. He noted that in 2007 when the
School District made a similar request for an addition on the east side of the
building, they indicated that they would be coming back to the Village for
these improvements to the west side. He also explained that although requested
increases in roofed lot coverage and gross floor area seem significant despite
the institutional use, the property is located in a single-family residential
zoning district, meaning that the standards for that district apply.
Manager Williams remarked that in the past the Council has discussed creating
institutional districts but decided to keep all zoning districts residential.
He mentioned that this, however, makes the requested variances seem large
relative to residential uses.
Mr. D’Onofrio went on to review a traffic/parking study submitted by the
applicant, noting that the Village’s Traffic Engineer agreed with the
consultant’s conclusions. He also reported that the Plan Commission had voted
unanimously to recommend approval of the Special Use Permit, and the Zoning
Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 in favor of the Special Use Permit and the two
variations. He added that the Design Review Board had considered the design of
the improvements and suggested some modifications to the landscape design as
well as to the size and appearance of the lobby addition.
Penny Lanphier, President of the School Board, explained that this is Phase II
of the School’s building project, the completion of a plan that was approved by
the voters last spring. She introduced the project’s architect, George Reigle,
who walked the Trustees through the specifics of the planned improvements.
Referring to the traffic study and conclusions, President Woodbury confirmed
with Ms. Lanphier that the School has formed a safety committee to help address
some of the traffic and safety issues. Ms. Lanphier responded that the District
is taking steps to understand transportation issues throughout the District and
has already met with Mr. Saunders and Mr. DeLopez to begin to tackle some of
the problems.
In response to a question from Trustee Behles about the Design Review Board’s
comments, Mr. Reigle indicated that the School hopes to keep as many trees as
possible and where necessary will come back with life-size replacements in
addition to supplemental trees.
Trustee Greable asked whether Phase I of the project will be completed before
Phase II begins. Mr. Reigle responded that the expectation is that any overlap
between phases will occur during the summer months.
After further discussion, Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
introduce Ordinance M-8-2008. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   Ordinance No. M-8-2008 – Special Use Permit: 813 Oak St. – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a Special Use Permit to permit a health
club facility in the subject property, which is located in the C-2 Retail
Overlay District. The existing tenant, Spynergy, proposes to expand their
current space in order to provide additional customer comfort and relief from
current overcrowded conditions. Mr. D’Onofrio went on to review a traffic/
parking study submitted by the applicant, noting that the Village’s Traffic
Engineer concurred with the consultant’s conclusions. He also reported that the
Plan Commission had voted unanimously to recommend approval of the Special Use
Permit, and that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend in
favor of the Special Use, subject to limiting the expansion to accommodate a
maximum of 40 operable bikes in the facility.
Jennifer Solberg, the applicant, responded to questions from the Trustees.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
introduce Ordinance M-8-2008. By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance MC-3-2008 – Establishing Environmental & Forestry Commission -
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed the draft of the ordinance that would
amend the Village Code to establish an Environmental and Forestry Commission
pursuant to the Council’s discussion at the April 8 th Study Session and in
keeping 2007 Caucus Platform. She explained that she incorporated the substance
of the recommendations made by Trustees Behles and Poor in their report to the
Council and made the language consistent with that throughout the chapter
pertaining to boards and commissions.
Trustee Behles inquired whether it is important that the student member be a
Winnetka resident. There was a consensus that it was.
He also inquired about the omission of a general mission statement for the
commission.
Ms. Janega explained that there are no general mission statements for the other
boards and commissions. Their mission is defined by their duties and
responsibilities and that such a statement was deliberately omitted for the
sake of consistency.
Trustee Poor remarked that this is a very exciting step and a great opportunity
to get ways of being good stewards of the environment in front of the public.
He said that he and Trustee Behles had met with the Forestry Commission and
they are very enthusiastic to begin.
Trustee Greable suggested that in keeping with the Caucus recommendation,
language be included to the effect that the Committee will explore ways of
funding that will not increase taxes.
Manager Williams pointed out that only the Village Council can commit taxpayer
dollars. Attorney Janega concurred, noting that the Council has many funding
options, and that it is important to avoid any phrasing that would imply that
the Commission has independent policy-making and spending authority.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance M-8-
2008. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
Trustee Johnson arrived at 9:15 p.m.
a)   Zoning Review – Winter and Company Proposal. Mr. Norkus explained that at
the March 11 th Study Session the Village Council discussed various lingering
concerns about the Zoning Ordinance as it relates to new, single-family homes.
At that meeting several options for possible zoning modifications, as well as
the relationship of such an effort to the item already under discussion,
voluntary residential design guidelines, were discussed. Recognizing that the
objectives of zoning and design guidelines frequently overlap and that numerous
zoning modifications that have been made throughout the years encourage good
design, the Council had requested that Winter and Company consider its concerns
and submit a proposed scope of services to evaluate the Village’s zoning
regulations on a parallel path with the ongoing design guidelines project.
Mr. Winter reviewed his Memorandum to the Council, included in the Agenda
Packet, outlining possible courses of study that might be undertaken as a part
of a review of current zoning regulations as they relate to building mass,
scale, and community character (Track 2), separate and apart from the ongoing
residential design guidelines project (Track 1). He also enumerated potential
products that could come from Track 2, saying that he sees this project
following a step or two behind the Design Guidelines Handbook effort. He
emphasized that this Track 2 scope of work is not associated in any way with
discussions about potential designation of historic districts in Winnetka that
have been suggested by the Ad Hoc Historic and Residential Preservation
Committee.
After Mr. Winter completed his presentation, he responded to questions from the
Trustees, and a lengthy discussion ensued.
Saying that the issues under discussion are not unique to Winnetka, Trustee
Poor suggested that Mr. Winter’s final report include a comparison of
Winnetka’s approach to those of neighboring, comparable communities.
Billy Kampenga, 609 Sheridan Rd., expressed concerns about how this matter came
to be on the Council’s agenda, costs associated with the proposed studies, and
possible implications for Winnetkans’ property rights.
Responding to Mr. Kampenga’s remarks, Trustee Poor stated that the issues of
mass and bulk are under consideration by the Council because there has been
widespread concern voiced among Winnetkans about overbuilding and teardowns.
Joan Esposito, 952 Pine St., commented that many teardowns have been small,
old, poorly maintained homes and/or those that are out-of-date with today’s
lifestyles and standards. She indicated that she does not believe that this is
necessarily bad for the community and suggested taking an historical look at
houses that have been demolished to determine whether they are really ones that
we wanted to keep and comparing them to what has been built in their stead.
Carry Buck, 609 Sheridan Rd., questioned the necessity of proceeding with these
projects and possibly implementing new restrictions, given that the number of
demolitions is decreasing. She noted that Mr. Winter is reputed to be a
nationally recognized preservationist specializing in districting and voiced
concern that Tracks 1 and 2 are steps toward creating preservation districts in
Winnetka. She objected to any action by the Council that might bring State or
Federal regulation into the Village.
President Woodbury explained that the issue at hand is the engagement of Mr.
Winter to take on the task of analyzing the Village’s Zoning Ordinance, in
addition to his already-approved work on the voluntary residential design
guidelines project. Both of the projects have been undertaken in response to
issues raised by the Caucus and residents and have nothing to do with
demolitions or establishing historical districts.
Mr. Woodbury went on to say that there are many in the community who think that
the loss of any home is a loss, while others view redevelopment as a sign of a
vital community. Both sides are present in the community and have been before
the Council. He said that the strength of Winnetka is its ability to have a
dialogue about such controversial matters and sort out what is right for the
Village. He emphasized that at the present time the only matter before the
Council is the question of whether or not to pursue a zoning study to address
concerns raised by the previous Council.
Commenting on Mr. Winter’s proposal, Trustee Behles said that he believes that
the proposed study is precisely what the previous Council asked for in order to
better understand the current zoning before making any decisions to modify the
existing Zoning Ordinance. He indicated that he believes this study will
provide a solid basis of information and should be pursued.
Trustee Berger said that although she had some reservations about the original
contract on design guidelines, she is in favor of proceeding with this study
because she believes the real crux of the matter to be zoning regulations. The
purpose of the study is to review the existing regulations, understand their
impact, make the Zoning Ordinance easier to understand, and resolve some of the
issues that come before the Council.
Trustee Rintz agreed that having Mr. Winter proceed with a complete analysis of
the Zoning Ordinance is a positive step so that the Council will fully
understand any potential outcomes as it moves forward with any changes to the
zoning regulations.
Trustee Poor stated that the Council has identified an area of concern where
people feel strongly on both sides of the issue and this study is the
appropriate next step in the process. He said that there are certainly houses
that deserve to be torn down, and no one advocates stopping teardowns
completely, but the concern is about what goes up in their place. This study is
designed to help provide the tools necessary to address concerns about new
development and preserving the character of the community. The process has
been, and will continue to be, a deliberate and open one. There will be no rush
to a decision. The Council is looking for balanced, precision tools that
respect property rights, as well as rights of neighbors.
Trustees Greable and Johnson agreed that this was a positive way to move
forward with identifying tools to help preserve the special nature of Winnetka
without disadvantaging the property rights of owners.
There being a consensus among the Trustees, President Woodbury directed staff
to prepare a final proposal. Then in deference to people waiting in the
audience, he turned to Item 9(g) on the Agenda.
9(g)Willow Road Steering Committee Report – President Woodbury provided a brief
background of the issue before introducing the Committee’s Chairperson, Becky
Hurley, to comment on the Willow Road Context-Sensitive Enhancement Plan, which
was distributed to the Council.
Ms. Hurley thanked Mr. Woodbury for the opportunity to chair this Committee,
whose mission it was to come up with a consensus road plan for the stretch of
Willow Road from Sunset Ridge to Provident through the communities of
Northfield and Winnetka. She said that although there have been a lot of
previous discussions about improvements to this stretch of roadway, three new
elements were injected into the approach this time: (1) it was a partnership
between the two villages; (2) the committee sought a context-sensitive
solution; and (3) the parties had assistance from The Lakota Group. She said
she was struck by the commitment of the villages and the participants to the
project. Although agreement was not reached on the number of lanes, the process
was a positive one and the Committee reached a consensus on an enormous number
of other issues that strengthened the relationship between the two villages in
the end.
Scott Freres, The Lakota Group, briefly reviewed the process, pointing out that
it was a planning exercise, as opposed to an engineering study, aimed at
putting together a plan to serve as a jumping off point from which the two
communities can move forward. He went on to address some of the details about
the Winnetka portion of the roadway. He mentioned, in particular, crossing and
circulation at Crow Island School, the intersections at Hibbard and Forestway,
and paths to link Winnetka to a multipurpose path to the forest preserve.
Mr. Saunders distributed a memorandum and directed the Trustees’ attention to a
diagram depicting Option 1A for the configuration of the intersection of Willow
and Hibbard. He suggested that drawing 1A be considered as an addendum to the
final report.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., spoke to the importance of accepting the report
subject to the inclusion of Option 1A.
Mr. Freres and Mr. Saunders responded to questions from the Trustees and the
audience.
Trustee Behles emphasized that the final report is a planning document that is
subject to engineering, and changes will inevitably be made.
Manager Williams remarked that there will be further opportunity for the
Village to refine the improvements as a detailed analysis of the
recommendations and engineering plans evolve.
Penny Lanphier, 123 Birch, former Trustee and President of the School Board,
complimented the Committee on their process and their courage to address the
matter. She thanked the Council and the Committee for integrating all elements
of the communities into the process and addressing the issues of quality of
life and character of the villages as a part of that process.
President Woodbury and Manager Williams thanked Ms. Hurley and Winnetka’s
representatives on the committee for their efforts.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to accept the Plan with
inclusion of Option 1A as discussed by Mr. Saunders. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1557 and 1558. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1557 in the amount of $1,262,374.45 and Warrant List No. 1558 in the amount of
$316,103.10. The motion was seconded by Trustee Poor. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Behles, Berger, Poor, Rintz, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid No. 08-010 – Directional Boring Contract. Mr. Keys reviewed a bid for
directional boring and associated wire pulling primarily associated with the
revision and/or installation of new underground electric services. He
recommended that the bid be awarded to Archon Construction Company in an amount
not to exceed $645,000.
President Woodbury confirmed with Mr. Keys that the second year is optional.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Poor, Johnson, Greable, Behles, and Rintz.
c)   Bid No. 08-001 – 2008 Street Rehabilitation Program. Mr. Saunders reviewed
the bids for the 2008 Street Rehabilitation Program. He recommended that the
bid be awarded to Chicagoland Paving for the bid amount of $719,604.50.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Johnson, Behles, Rintz, Berger, Poor, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid No. 08-011 – MFT 2008 Street Rehabilitation Program. Mr. Saunders
explained that this is the companion bid to Item 9(c) for this year’s street
rehabilitation program, for the use of MFT funds. He recommended that the bid
be awarded to Peter Baker & Sons Company for the bid amount of $184,406.07.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Bid No. 08-012 – Post Office Roof Repairs. Mr. Saunders reviewed options
and bids to repair the existing roof on the Winnetka Post Office which leaks
excessively throughout the building. He explained that as owner of the building
and the landlord, the responsibility for repair or replacement falls to the
Village. He recommended that E. F. Bassing be awarded the bid for a 2-year
solution, at the bid price of $29,600.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Rintz, Berger, Poor, Johnson, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Chamber of Commerce Request: Sidewalk Sale. Mr. Williams reviewed a
request from the Chamber of Commerce to hold the annual Sidewalk Sale on Friday
and Saturday, July 18 th and 19 th .
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the request. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
g)   Willow Road Steering Committee Report. As noted above, this item was
discussed earlier in the meeting.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Johnson reported that the Chamber of Commerce has
welcomed him at its most recent meeting as the new Trustee liaison to that
group.
Trustee Poor reminded the audience that this coming Monday would be the 81 st
Memorial Day celebration in the Village and encouraged all to attend to hear
Ross Ritchell, recently returned from Iraq, speak and to honor those who gave
their lives in defense of our freedoms. He added that the Cenotaph, which is
badly in need of repair, will turn 80 in November. He asked for the public’s
help in funding badly-needed repairs to this part of ’’’’Winnetka’’’s heritage
by making a tax deductible contribution to the Cenotaph Restoration Fund.
Trustee Berger reported on her recent attendance at a NWMC board meeting with
Trustee Greable. She also remarked that the Library was making progress with
its due diligence study about relocating to the Post Office site.
Trustee Behles reported that the Streetscape Committee met at the demonstration
site to solidify the extent and the scope of the project. He noted that they
are very sensitive to the summer construction season timeframe.
c)   Attorney: None.
d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury called for a motion to appoint Louise Holland to replace
Posey Fisher as Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission. Trustee
Berger so moved, seconded by Trustee Poor. By voice vote, the motion carried.
12)   Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, no Executive Session was held.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Poor,
Greable, Johnson, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:55 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
June 3, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 17, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
3, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Ken Behles called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present:
Trustees Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris Rintz.
Absent: Village President Ed Woodbury. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director
Mike D’Onofrio, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
Finance Director Ed McKee, Water & Electric Director Brian Keys, and
approximately 16 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Behles led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   June 10, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee Rintz, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   June 17, 2008 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 13, 2008, Study Session. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz,
moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Landmark Preservation Awards.
Posey Fisher, Chairman of the Landmark Preservation Commission, presented the
annual Preservation awards and congratulated the winning owners and architects.
The following properties received awards:
   ∙   72 Fox Lane, Restoration
   ∙   1183 Tower, Rehabilitation
   ∙   1229 Ash, Rehabilitation
   ∙   1093 Spruce, New Construction
   ∙   Winnetka Paddle Hut, New Construction
President Pro Tem Behles thanked Ms. Fisher for her service, and observed that
the Council looks forward to the Preservation Awards each year.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-7-2008 – Special Use Permit & Zoning Variations: ––––515
Hibbard Road–, ––Carlton–– Washburne School – Adoption. President Pro Tem
Behles stated that Ordinance M-7-2008 would grant a Special Use permit and two
zoning variations for an addition on the west side of Carlton Washburne School
at 515 Hibbard Road.
Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to reflect the Plan Commission’s
findings, which were not available when the ordinance was introduced.
There being no further questions or comments, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to amend the ordinance as recommended by the Village Attorney. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adopt Ordinance M-7-2008
granting a Special Use Permit and zoning variations to the Carlton Washburne
School. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
Ordinance No. M-8-2008 – Special Use Permit: ––––813 Oak Street–– -- Adoption.
President Pro Tem Behles stated that Ordinance M-8-2008 would grant a Special
Use Permit for the expansion of an existing health club facility at 813 Oak.
Attorney Janega explained that, as with Ordinance M-7-2008, this ordinance had
been amended to reflect the Plan Commission’s findings.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to
amend the ordinance as recommended. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Ordinance M-8-2008
granting a Special Use Permit to an existing health club facility at 813 Oak
Street. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2008 – Establishing Environmental & Forestry Commission
– Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that amendments had been made since
introduction to clarify several minor items, and asked if there were any
questions or comments regarding the amended ordinance.
There being none, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to amend the
ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-3-2008
establishing an Environmental & Forestry Commission. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays:
None. Absent: President Woodbury.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2008 – Liquor Code Amendments Pertaining to Permitted
Hours for the ––––Sale–– and Service of Food and Alcoholic Beverages –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the Liquor
Ordinance, and noted that the BASSET Training and the separate hours for fast
food restaurants, food stores and standard restaurants had not been discussed
at the May 13 th Study Session. She observed that upon further consideration,
she recommended against changing the hours of operation for fast food
restaurants and food stores at the current time, as notice had not been given
to the current licensees, but noted that the Council could discuss the issue at
a future Study Session, as well as hold public hearings, in advance of the next
licensing year.
There was an extensive discussion regarding bifurcating the hours for standard
restaurants and fast food restaurants; about the service of food with alcoholic
beverages; and the practicality of requiring BASSET training for all liquor
licensees’ employees, after which President Pro Tem Behles called for audience
comments.
Mr. Kaveh Mirani, Mirani’s Restaurant, 727 Elm, expressed his belief that it is
impractical for a restaurant to comply with the BASSET training provision for
all employees because of employee turnover. He indicated that training for the
bartender and managers would be more realistic.
Chief DeLopez remarked that part of the reason the Illinois Liquor Control
Commission established the BASSET training was to avoid the inappropriate
service of alcohol, and he added that the selling of alcohol is a high risk
business, and he didn’t think it was an undue burden to impose on the Village’s
liquor licensees.
Mr. Scott Hallerman, 839 Locust; and Mr. Creed Tucker, 850 Locust; both
expressed support for scheduling a discussion on the topic of shortening the
hours of fast food establishment as soon as possible.
The Council decided to proceed with aligning the hours of alcohol service and
hours of business, and extending hours on weekends and on the sidewalk, but to
strike the provisions for BASSET Training and the hours of operation for fast
food restaurants for the time being, to allow time for further Council
discussion and public participation.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-4-2008
amending the Village’s Liquor Ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-9-2008 – Harris Bank Lease – Introduction. Finance
Director Ed McKee reported that the Village leases a small portion of the
street to Harris Bank to allow better access to its drive-up banking facility.
He explained the method used to arrive at the new lease price, indicating that
it was based on inflation, and recommended renewing the lease as amended.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to introduce Ordinance M-9-
2008. By voice vote, the motion carried.
e)   Resolution No. R-30-2008 – ––––Cook– ––County–– Data Sharing Agreement –
Adoption. Public Works Director Steve Saunders reported that the Village uses
data from the Cook County Assessor’s office to maintain its GIS system, which
is used by all departments in the Village. He indicated that the Assessor’s
office has recently changed procedures and now requires municipalities to enter
into an intergovernmental agreement in exchange for providing their free
services.
After a brief discussion about the kind of information received and how it is
used by the Village, the Council authorized Staff to sign the intergovernmental
agreement allowing the Village access to Cook County’s digital GIS information.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-30-2008.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1559 and 1560. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1559 in the amount of $719,344.78 and Warrant List No. 1560 in the amount of
$405,618.09. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays:
None. Absent: President Woodbury.
b)   Turbine #6 Inspection/Overhaul. Water & Electric Director Brian Keys
reported that the last of three turbine overhauls had been successfully
completed within the expected budget amount, but a change order was required
for Technical Field Advisor work that had exceeded the original purchase order
by $34,618.84 due to an unanticipated increase in the scope of work.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to
approve Change Order #1 in the amount of $34,618.84 for the completion of the
Technical Field Advisor work for the Turbine #6 overhaul. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz.
Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury
c)   Copier Leases. Mr. McKee reported that the leases for the Manager’s Office
copy machines were expired, that a new contract had been negotiated with Xerox
for new machines, and he recommended approval of the new lease terms.
There being no comments or questions, Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to authorize the Finance Director to execute a contract with
Xerox to implement the copier changes proposed. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: President Woodbury.
d)   Capital Budget Status Report. Mr. McKee reviewed the status of the
Village’s Capital spending for items budgeted at $50,000 or more, and noted the
projects that were not yet complete.
Manager Williams observed that the plan for replacing the diesel fuel line at
the power plant had changed, but that Staff hoped to complete the project this
year.
11)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Berger reported that the Winnetka/Northfield Library
Board had selected a needs analysis consultant. She also remarked that the New
Trier Township Prevention Council has asked the Village to adopt a resolution
banning underage possession of tobacco, and expressed her support for such a
resolution.
c)   Village President Pro Tem. None
d)   Attorney. None
e)   Manager. None
12)   Appointments.
None.
13)   Executive Session.
Trustee Greable moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Pending and Probable Litigation pursuant to
Sections 2(c) (3) and 2(c) (11), respectively, of the Open Meetings Act .
Trustee Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:27 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:28 p.m.
