   Recording Secretary
June 10, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: July 15, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
10, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, and King Poor. Absent:
Trustee Chris Rintz. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Assistant
Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
and approximately 14 persons in the audience.
2)   Meet with Caucus Platform Committee.
President Woodbury welcomed several members of the Caucus Platform Committee,
and the committee Chair, Blanche Romey. Lauren Taslitz,
Ms. Romey indicated that the Caucus is in the process of identifying key issues
for the Caucus Questionnaire, and asked the Council for their thoughts on which
issues they would like feedback on from the community.
The issue of term limits was discussed, after which President Woodbury
suggested that the Caucus meet with the Residential Design Guidelines Committee
to get ideas for a question. It was decided that the Library Board needs time
to finish their needs analysis before any more questions are asked about the
issue of the Post Office redevelopment. Other issues that were discussed
included affordable housing, curb-side refuse collection, and Streetscape.
Trustee Behles indicated that the initiatives that are currently before the
Council came directly out of the 2005 Caucus Platform, and recommended
revisiting those questions to see if any would be relevant for this year. He
noted that if last year was the year of the Post Office redevelopment, this
year is the year of residential development issues.
Trustee Greable observed that there seems to be some confusion in the community
about the difference between historic preservation, design guidelines, and
zoning, and suggested putting more focus on the issue of historic preservation.
Trustee Poor noted that the Council continues to be concerned about the
question of residential redevelopment as it pertains to the light and air of
neighbors and the environment.
3)   Lead Water Service Line Policy.
Water & Electric Director Brian Keys reported that in June of 2007, the Village
Code was amended so that the Village would pay for the replacement of leaking
lead water service from the water main to the property line; and the property
owner would be responsible for the portion from the property line to the water
meter.
Mr. Keys indicated that a survey of eight neighboring communities showed that
Winnetka’s policy is in line with a majority of the communities, with the
exception that all eight allow repairs to lead service and ’’’’Winnetka’’ does
not, as a result of a letter from the Illinois Department of Public Health. He
said Mr. Mike D’Onofrio, Director of the Community Development Department,
recently contacted the Illinois Department of Public Health’s Chief Engineer,
who indicated that the 1998 letter from IDPH is still in effect.
Mr. Keys observed that for safety of the drinking water, the Village adds
polyorthophosphates to the potable water, which adheres in a microscopic
coating to the inside of lead pipes and prevents the leeching of the lead into
the water. He indicated that water tests of the Village’s water supply
performed at multiple sites over the years have never turned up lead levels
anywhere near the required action level of 15ppb.
Mr. Keys noted that the Illinois Plumbing Code contains a provision for
requesting a variance, in the event the Village and/or homeowners wish to
repair an existing lead service leak, and added that he did not see a safety
concern with repairing a lead water service.
There was a discussion about the possibility of continuing to replace the
service line, but allowing the homeowners choose to repair their portion, due
to the financial burden placed on residents who need to replace lead service.
Manager Williams brought up the question of where the division of ownership
occurs on private roads, and indicated that current Village policy is that the
homeowner pays to replace the entire water service.
Attorney Janega said she would do some research on the repair of lead water
service and prepare a memo of her findings for the Council.
4)   Assistant Public Works Director Position.
Public Works Director Steve Saunders reported that the Public Works Department
would benefit considerably from the addition of an Assistant Public Works
Director position. He noted that there had been such a position until 2004,
when it was left vacant in favor of hiring an Engineering/Forestry Inspector to
expedite plan review and field inspection duties. He said while that move has
worked well in filling the operational need in the engineering area, it has
left the department in a weaker position with regard to administration, direct
management of large capital projects, and the ability to research and develop
new programs and improvements in Public Works services.
Mr. Saunders indicated that adding an Assistant Director would be beneficial
for: (i) direct management of large capital projects; (ii) analysis, research,
improvements, especially in Department administration; (iii) budget analysis;
(iv) environmental coordination; and (v) succession planning.
Mr. Saunders noted that the position has been funded in the budget but not yet
authorized, and gave a breakdown of the estimated salary, pension and
healthcare costs associated with adding the position.
Trustee Behles pointed out that the Village does not save money or solve the
succession problem by hiring outside project managers.
Trustee Berger said she felt the organization chart was heavy on managerial/
supervisor positions.
Manager Williams noted that the supervisors in the department are working
supervisors who are out on the street with their subordinates working alongside
them, as well as supervising the activities of work crews.
After further discussion, the Council agreed that a compelling need exists for
an Assistant Director of Public Works, and authorized filling the position.
5)   2010 & 2011 Village Sticker Design.
Mr. McKee reported that since 2001, the Village vehicle sticker design has been
used to commemorate anniversaries of various community institutions. He
indicated that there have been five requests for the 2010 sticker design, and
the Community House has requested 2011 for its 100 th anniversary. He asked the
Council for direction in selecting the 2010 vehicle sticker designs from the
following applicants: Hadley School for the Blind; Winnetka Community Nursery
School; North Shore Art League; Willow Wood Preschool; and the Junior League of
Evanston-North Shore.
Mr. Chuck Young, President of the Hadley School for the Blind, indicated that
the school is the largest school for the blind in the world, and that to
commemorate its 90 th anniversary in 2010 they would like to be considered for
the vehicle sticker design.
Pam Schultz, President of the Winnetka Community Nursery School, also requested
consideration of the 2010 Vehicle Sticker design, to honor the accomplishments
of the organization over the past 60-plus years.
The Council discussed an equitable way to choose from among community
organizations for the honor of being on the Village’s vehicle sticker, and
ultimately decided that a policy of using major anniversaries as the criteria
for being selected. The Hadley School for the Blind was chosen for 2010, for
its 90 th anniversary, and the Community House in 2011 for its Centennial.
6)   NTHS Parking Lot Lease.
Mr. McKee reported that in 2007 the Village entered into a new parking lease
with New Trier High School which is in effect until September 1, 2009, at which
point it automatically renews for one-year periods with a 5% increase until one
party decides to terminate. He indicated that if the Council wishes to
renegotiate the contract, NTHS should be notified by August 31, 2008, and asked
for direction on the question of allowing the lease to automatically renew at
the 5% increase.
After some discussion, the Council agreed to let the lease renew automatically
at the 5% increase for the next year.
7)   Manager’s Report.
None.
8)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(3) and 2(c)(11) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Poor seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Rintz. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:16 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:00 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson and Poor. Absent: Trustee
Rintz. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
9)   Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson and Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz. The meeting
adjourned at 11:01 p.m.
