Recording Secretary
June 17, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: July 15, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
17, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Fire Chief Scott Smith,,
Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately six persons
in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   July 1, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   July 8, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   May 20, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles noted a small correction on
page 7 of the minutes. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
approve the minutes as corrected. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   June 3, 2008 Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee poor,
moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2008 – Liquor Code Amendments Pertaining to Permitted
Hours for ––––Sale–– and Service of Food and Alcoholic Beverages -- Adoption.
Village Attorney Katherine Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the
Village’s Liquor Code which align the hours of liquor service with business
hours, expand the hours during which liquor may be served on weekends, and also
provide for expanded hours of liquor service at sidewalk restaurants.
Attorney Janega noted that provisions which were included at the time of
introduction that would have added a BASSET training requirement had been
deleted, and she reviewed a survey of neighboring communities’ BASSET training
requirements. She indicated that a discussion about the BASSET training, as
well as hours of business for fast food restaurants and food stores, would take
place at a later date.
President Woodbury pointed out that Winnetka currently uses BASSET training as
a sanction when liquor violations occur, and added that he thought training at
the time of licensing could be useful in preventing violations.
Manager Williams agreed, and observed that Winnetka’s Police Chief, Joe DeLopez
would also concur that an ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure, but he
added that the issue of staff turnover was a valid one, and further discussion
on the matter was called for.
The Trustees agreed that more discussion on the BASSET training requirement was
necessary, and it was decided to bring the issue to a Study Session later in
the summer.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to amend Ordinance MC-4-2008.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-4-2008
amending the Village’s Liquor Code. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-9-2008 – ––––Harris– ––Bank– ––Land–– Lease – Adoption.
President Woodbury called for any comments or questions regarding renewal of
the Village’s lease of a small portion of property to Harris Bank, for better
access to the drive-up banking facility.
There being none, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt
Ordinance M-9-2008, granting a lease to Harris Bank. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. M-10-2008 – Zoning Variations: ––––455 Sheridan Road–– –
Introduction. Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio reported that a
previous application had been submitted for a front yard setback variation, but
was withdrawn in order to redesign the attached garage. He indicated that the
new plans bring the garage in line with the main portion of the house and
reduce the amount of front yard variation, but they necessitate variations for
three front-facing garage doors.
Mr. D’Onofrio noted that the Subject Property is a through lot, and therefore
has two front yards, one to the east along the bluff above Lake Michigan, and
one on the west side of the lot, where the variation is being requested, and he
added that the ZBA recommended granting the variations.
Susan Connor, the applicant’s attorney, said in addition to having two front
yards, the property is an irregularly shaped flag lot, and that the conforming
location is not a good use of the property, in that it would destroy three
large trees and severely diminish the enjoyment of the neighbors’ riparian
views, as well as potentially damage the bluff.
Trustees Greable and Behles indicated that the revised plans are far superior
to the original application.
Trustee Poor observed that variations for new construction are rare, but that
this case shows a compelling need in order to save the trees and spare the
fragile bluff area.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance
M-10-2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance M-10-
2008, granting three zoning variations to 455 Sheridan Road. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Resolution No. R-31-2008 – Prevailing Wages – Adoption. Attorney Janega
explained that State law requires the Village to establish the prevailing wages
for workers employed in the construction of capital improvement projects in the
Village.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-31-
2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Park District Proposed Open Field Burn. Ms. Liza McElroy, Park District
Director, introduced Mr. Robert Smith, also of the Park District, and Ms.
Carolyn Kurtz, the Park Board President.
Mr. Smith described the recent restoration of Crow Island Woods, and said the
project would benefit greatly from a controlled burn to take out invasive
species and deadfall, and he added that Bell Woods was also in need of a burn
to clear out its understory. He noted that fire is the most effective tool in
controlling invasive species, as opposed to the more labor-intensive clearing
by hand, and added that Pizzo & Associates, the firm that would do the burn,
has an excellent safety record.
Ms. McElroy said the goal of the project is to restore the wildflowers and
return the woods to their natural state, and to keep non-native species at bay.
She observed that the Park District applied for burn permits in 2006, and the
objections did not have to do with safety, but rather with environmental
sensitivity and the medical conditions of people near the parks. She noted that
a permit was received for a controlled burn in Bell Woods, but that the burn
was not done at the time, because the Park District would like to do both parks
in one package.
Fire Chief Scott Smith noted that the Fire Department had never been
comfortable with a controlled burn in the Village, that the health of citizens
was the primary concern, and he added that he was against giving permission for
the burns.
Ms. Kurtz observed that Pizzo & Assoc. is a remarkable organization, that they
have sensitivity to the proximity of homes, and that the Park District will
offer consideration to residents who have health issues.
President Woodbury suggested that the Park District contact the affected
residents, have their public meetings to gather input, and then come back
before the Council with the results for a more thorough and informed
discussion.
b)   Warrant Lists 1561 and 1562. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1561 in the amount of $1,121,565.12 and Warrant List No. 1562 in the amount of
$574,232.96. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Berger reported that the Library Board met on Monday night and
they were moving forward on their needs analysis.
   ii)   Trustee Greable announced that the Historical Society’s benefit was
being held on Saturday, and encouraged the viewing audience to attend.
   iii)   Trustee Johnson reported that at the recent Chamber meeting, they
discussed their upcoming retreat.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the village has been getting
between 16,000-19,000 monthly hits on its website, which is a substantial
increase over past usage. He also indicated that over the summer, IDOT will
resurface Willow Road from the Edens Expressway to Hibbard. Finally, he said
the reimbursement from the state for the Willow Road Study had been delivered
to Northfield and that Winnetka would get its portion shortly.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Greable moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(3) and 2(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:13 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:08 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:09 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
July 8, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING & STUDY SESSION
(Approved: August 5, 2008)
A record of a legally convened special meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
8, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Chris Rintz, and King
Poor. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director
of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Assistant Director of Water & Electric Rich
Ciesla and Water Plant Superintendent Pat Freely. There were no persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1563 and 1564. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1563 in the amount of $922,023.77 and Warrant List No. 1564 in the amount of
$806,709.03. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Poor, Greable, Johnson, and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Adjournment of Special Meeting.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The special meeting adjourned at 7:41 p.m.
6)   Convene Study Session.
a)   Call to Order. President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:42 p.m.
Present: Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Chris
Rintz, and King Poor. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Assistant Director of Water
& Electric Rich Ciesla and Water Plant Superintendent, Pat Freely. There were
no persons in the audience.
b)   Winnetka Water Filtration Plant; Long Term Improvement Plan and Membrane
Filtration Feasibility Evaluation. Mr. Keys explained that in February the
Village Council awarded a contract to Camp Dresser & McKee, Inc., to perform a
long term water plant improvement plan study, which would include an evaluation
of membrane filtration feasibility and recommendations toward meeting all
application regulations and future finished water quality goals. He noted that
CDM has extensive experience working with both Lake Michigan water plants and
with membrane filtration systems. Mr. Keys went on to introduce Len Rago and
Theresa Brooks from Camp Dresser & McKee who gave a PowerPoint presentation
reviewing their report and recommendations and responded to questions from the
Trustees.
Mr. Rago and Ms. Brooks provided an overview of applicable water treatment
regulations, discussed required Plant capacity, reviewed their evaluation of
existing Water Plant conditions, explained the differences between the membrane
and conventional treatment alternatives, and outlined improvement
recommendations and prioritization, and costs associated therewith.
Mr. Rago explained that the existing Water Plant was originally constructed in
1921 and expanded in 1931. Historic water demands have required that the Plant
produce significantly more water than originally anticipated by the Plant
design. He noted that Staff continually faces new challenges in reliably
providing a safe source of drinking water, such as aging facilities;
increasingly more stringent treated water quality requirements; and increasing
costs in operating the facilities. The Plant was originally designed and is
currently rated for 6 mgd, but historic peak demand has hit 12 mgd and
consistently peaks over 6 mgd on an annual basis. Therefore, the Plant should
be rated to deliver 12 mgd. The size of the plant impacts what regulations
apply to the Village, and water quality is driven by the regulations. He
emphasized, however, that considering the age and conditions of the existing
facilities, the Village does a very good job of meeting existing regulations.
Mr. Rago compared conventional treatment, which is currently used, to membrane
treatment, which is new technology but significantly more costly. He reported
that for a variety of reasons both staff and CDM recommend that the Village
continue with conventional treatment methods at this time. He went on to review
recommended improvements and priorities.
Trustee Berger asked whether the projected bottom line is being driven by
trying to increase the Plant’s capacity. Mr. Rago responded that the Plant is
quite old and improvements over the years have been stop-gap, so there is a lot
that is needed to do. Ms. Brooks added that only one of the immediate priority
improvements is driven by regulations.
Manager Williams commented that he believes the improvements to the Water Plant
should be the top priority for capital improvements. He also remarked that it
is highly unlikely that the Plant’s service would change substantially over
time. Mr. Williams said that whether to proceed with the conventional or
membrane alternative is the core issue to be decided. Once that determination
is made, Staff will then be able to better understand how to fit the
improvements into the capital plan and come back to the Council with a specific
plan.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed with the conventional
treatment alternative.
Trustee Poor commented that the membrane alternative is too much of an
undertaking for the present time, given that the Village is doing all right in
terms of quantity and quality.
Trustee Behles observed that the driver for membrane technology would be the
need to increase capacity beyond the 12 mgd, but that there does not appear to
be any such need now or in the foreseeable future.
Trustee Rintz remarked on the need to improve the working conditions at the
Plant.
Trustee Greable said that he was in favor of moving forward as quickly as
possible, saying that this is stewardship of the highest level and the Village
has an obligation to the residents to provide safe water and adequate supply on
an ongoing basis.
7)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(3) and 2(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:15 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:50 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
8)   Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Poor,
Greable, Johnson, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Study Session
adjourned at 10:51 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
July 15, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: September 2, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
15, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Community
Development Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric
Brian Keys and approximately 18 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 9(a), Zoning Regulations – Track 2
Evaluation by Winter and Company, had been removed from the Agenda, but
audience comments would be taken during the Public Comment portion of the
Agenda. He also noted that the issue of handguns would be raised in the Public
Comment portion of the meeting, and the item had been added to the Agenda to
allow for Council discussion; however, no formal action is scheduled for this
meeting. The Trustees approved the Agenda, as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   August 5, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   August 12, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
President Woodbury and Trustee Johnson, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   June 10, 2008, Study Session. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   June 17, 2008 Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
6)   Village Auditor: Update on Annual Audit.
Mr. McKee introduced Ron Amen, a partner of the Village’s current auditing firm
of Lauterbach and Amen. He noted that although the Auditor works closely with
staff to ensure that the audit is materially correct, it does not get into the
day-to-day operations of the Village. He emphasized that ultimately the Auditor
works for the Council.
Mr. Amen then updated the Council on the status of the audit and responded to
questions from the Trustees. He mentioned that the final CAFR and Management
Letter will be distributed later this summer. Mr. Amen assured the Council that
the audit is progressing in a timely fashion and the Village’s financial
records meet all applicable standards.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-11-2008 – Zoning Variations: ––––635 Hill Rd.–– -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a side yard setback
variation to permit the construction of an addition to a detached garage. He
explained that because the garage is not located in the rear quarter of the lot
depth, the garage must provide an 8 ft. minimum side setback. Were it to be
located in the rear quarter, it would require only a 3 ft. setback. He noted
that the existing garage is nonconforming with respect to the east setback in
that it provides a setback of 0.98 ft., whereas the addition would provide an
east side setback of 2.13 ft. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that in all other respects
the construction complies with the Zoning Ordinance, and the Zoning Board of
Appeals had voted 4 to 1 to recommend in favor of the variation.
Trustee Berger confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that with respect to the impact on
the neighbors, the difference between the proposed location and the rear
quarter is the same and relatively negligible. She also asked the applicant to
comment on the conforming alternative.
Healy Rice, the architect, explained that as proposed there is less impact on
the existing trees and shrubbery and that it is more effective to do it this
way than to have two structures.
Trustee Poor said he believed that this is a classic case for a variation; to
force a conforming location would result in greater tree loss.
Attorney Janega explained that while the Zoning Board of Appeals is required to
find practical difficulty and particular hardship, the Council is not.
Therefore, the ordinance has been drafted to cite only practical difficulty.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce Ordinance M-11-
2008. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   Ordinance No. M-12-2008 – Zoning Variations: 1004 Pine Tree Lane -
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a front yard setback
variation to permit the construction of a new single-family residence. The
proposed new residence results in a north front yard setback of 25 ft., whereas
a minimum of 50 ft. is required. He noted that the subject property is
irregularly shaped with street frontage on two streets that do not intersect.
Mr. D’Onofrio pointed out that the request otherwise conforms with the Zoning
Ordinance and that with respect to roofed lot coverage, gross floor area, and
impermeable surface, the proposed construction does not come close to the
maximum bulk regulations. Finally, he mentioned that a similar variation was
granted in 2001 for the adjacent property with the same setback requirements.
The applicants, Joseph and Patricia Albian and their architect, responded to
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Behles commented that although the variation is for new construction,
the situation meets all the standards for a variation and that he would
therefore be in support of the variation request.
Trustee Berger concurred.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce Ordinance M-12-
2008. By voice vote, the motion carried.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
Peter Gelderman, 896 Tower Rd., requested that the Village carefully control
the number of construction projects on any given block to ensure neighborhood
peace and tranquility and the safety of children.
President Woodbury remarked that there are such regulations in place, and Mr.
D’Onofrio explained that the Community Development Department works closely
with Public Works and the Police Department to minimize impact on neighbors.
9)   Old Business.
a)   Zoning Regulations – Track 2 Evaluation by Winter and Company. As noted
earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda.
b)   Ash Street Drainage. Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed the history of this
matter. He explained that after receiving a petition from a group of residents
requesting that the Village address stormwater flooding problems in the subject
neighborhood, the Council directed staff to undertake a drainage study to
confirm whether or not a flooding problem exists and what improvements might be
undertaken to improve conditions. The Village hired a consultant, who
determined that the neighborhood does flood due to surcharging of the storm
sewer system. Mr. Saunders briefly summarized the consultant’s three proposed
improvements and their projected costs.
He went on to explain that after discussing the consultant’s recommended
alternative at the March budget study session, the Council asked staff to
develop a less costly alternative that would provide appropriate improvements.
He reviewed the alternative improvement plan prepared by staff and funding
alternatives therefor. He indicated that if approved as proposed in the Agenda
Report, the project could begin this year and be completed next year, and
responded to questions from the Trustees.
Responding to questions from Trustee Berger, Mr. Saunders noted that the area
in question is low and flat, and some of it lies in the flood plain. In
addition, the storm sewers are small, and as the Village has developed over
time, there is more runoff water. Although the Village takes steps to mitigate
the impact of new development, it is not the same as if no development was
taking place.
Trustee Berger then asked whether there are other areas with similar problems.
Mr. Saunders replied that some other areas have been identified, particularly
in western Winnetka, and that the Village’s capital plan includes plans to
address these areas.
President Woodbury remarked that a previous Council determined that a problem
exists, but the question is one of severity, timing, and budget. He asked what
the Village’s normal design standard for sewers is. Mr. Saunders replied that
there is no specific design standard because there are some places where the
Village can’t get what it would like to have, and there are some neighborhoods
where the Village is lucky to get two year protection. He noted that there was
a period of some 40 years that the Village did not build up its stormwater
sewer system but, over the last 15 years or so, most of the improvement
recommendations identified in a Village-wide study have been implemented. The
backbone of the Village’s drainage system has been improved, and attention is
now being focused on specific neighborhoods.
Trustee Johnson expressed concern that there will be requests for similar
extensive improvements from other neighborhoods.
Manager Williams commented that the Village has made a distinction between
public and private property. If there is a problem on public property, the
Village will address it, but the Village will never be able to solve all of the
problems because of system constraints. He expressed strong confidence in the
Village’s engineering staff and their recommendations.
Trustee Rintz said that in walking the area, he observed that about 50% of the
homes have been newly developed, but he was not ready to point at new
construction as the cause of the problem. He voiced concern that the plan as
proposed would only serve to solve the problem as it exists today and that if
the majority of the remaining homes were to be renovated or rebuilt, the
situation would change. He suggested it might be worthwhile to project forward
to avoid problems in the longer term.
Mr. Saunders replied that over time the Village has refined its regulations and
has come a long way. It now requires new construction to hold detention for the
maximum amount of impervious surface that is permitted on the lot, and there
are very restrictive release rates. He said he believes the proposed 10 year
protection is appropriate and pointed out that the Village doesn’t have the
option to provide detention on a broad scale, only what is required with
redevelopment. In response to a question from Mr. Rintz, he indicated that he
does not believe the Village will be shortchanging itself by proceeding with
the proposed alternative plan.
Trustee Poor complimented staff on their efforts to provide an alternative that
represents a balanced approach and provides more than minimal protection but is
not the most expensive. He said this is not a panacea but it will provide
significant improvement for the neighborhood. He indicated that he would
support proceeding this year.
Trustee Behles concurred, saying that staff has shown the right course in a
prudent way, and to delay action would not be a positive step.
Mr. Saunders noted that because of the timing of the petitions, the contract to
pave Ash Street had already been let, so it will be necessary to dig up
portions of the newly-laid pavement.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to direct staff to obtain bids
based on staff’s recommended solution at an estimated cost of $210,000. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
In order to accommodate members of the audience, President Woodbury moved to
the discussion of Item 10(f), the Village’s handgun ordinance. He reiterated
his earlier comment that the matter would be discussed by the Council in
Executive Session but no action would be taken at this meeting.
* * *
10(f)   Handgun Ordinance Discussion. Attorney Janega briefed the Trustees on
the recent Supreme Court opinion in District of Columbia v. Heller and its
current implications for the Village of Winnetka.
Jeanne Bishop and Jennifer Bishop-Jenkins strongly urged the Council to refrain
from taking any immediate action in reaction to the recent court decision. They
asked the Trustees to wait until such time as a comprehensive plan that is
being worked out by the Legal Community Against Violence has been completed. It
is hoped that this plan will allow for positive coordination between
communities with a similar desire to amend their local gun regulations.
President Woodbury acknowledged this is an extremely sensitive matter for the
Village. He voiced the intent to have a measured discussion about the issue and
what is appropriate for this community, but said the Trustees must be mindful
of court actions that might be impending. He said for the present, everything
would remain status quo.
The Council then returned to the original agenda.
* * *
10)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1565 and 1566. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1565 in the amount of $873,867.06 and Warrant List No. 1566 in the amount of
$285,953.54. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Bid 08-014: Replacement of 1997 Freightliner FL80 Hook-lift/Plow Truck (PW
15). Mr. Saunders reviewed bids for a truck used as a front-line dump truck and
plow truck, for leaf collection, and for sewer cleaning. He reported staff’s
recommendation that the bid be awarded to the lowest complying bidder that
meets all specifications, Trans Chicago Truck Group (Bid #1), for a
Freightliner M2 106V chassis, for $151.928.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger,
Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Street Rehabilitation Program – Change Order ––––#1, Green Bay Road––
Repairs. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village authorized its paving
contractor for the 2008 Street Rehabilitation Program to perform additional
surface patching on Green Bay Road, a State road that is maintained by the
Village. This work, which is expected to cost about $90,000, was not included
in the contract awarded under the 2008 Street Rehabilitation Program, but was
performed because the State made $71,000 available in reimbursable funds for
these repairs.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to authorize Change Order #1
to the 2008 Street Rehabilitation Program, authorizing the expenditure of an
additional $90,000 for repairs to Green Bay Road, $71,000 of which is to be
reimbursed by IDOT. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Rintz, Berger, Poor, Greable, and Johnson. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid: Diesel Fuel Tank Engineering. Mr. Keys explained that the buried fuel
lines that supply the Electric Plant with long duration supply of diesel fuel
were deemed inoperable due to regulatory requirements and briefly reviewed
actions taken by the Village’s thus far to resolve the problem. He reported
that the Office of the State Fire Marshall has approved the Village’s plans to
install a diesel tank in one of the unused coal bunkers at the Electric Plant
and long term plans to remove the existing tank farm, and issued a permit
allowing the Village to proceed with detailed engineering. Mr. Keys reviewed
the bids and bidding process for the project and reported staff’s
recommendation that Strand Associates be awarded the contract for engineering
services in an amount not to exceed $59,000.
Trustee Behles remarked that this is an elegant solution to a nasty problem and
asked Mr. Keys what he expected the construction costs to be. Mr. Keys said he
anticipated those costs to be somewhat over $500,000, not including long term
abandonment plans and clean up of the existing fuel line and boiler tank. He
noted that staff is not asking for approval of construction related services or
bidding services until the complexity of the project has been determined.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid as
recommended, subject to the Village Attorney’s approval of the indemnification
provision. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor,
Greable, Johnson, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Bid: Water Plant Intake Maintenance Work. Mr. Keys reviewed the bids for
periodic dive inspections and stone work that are required to maintain the
intake pipes laid along the bottom of Lake Michigan to the Water Plant. He
reported staff’s recommendation that the bid be awarded to Edward E. Gillen
Company in the amount of $71,100.
Trustee Berger inquired about how frequently stone replacement is expected to
be required. Mr. Keys responded that staff expects this to be an annual project
on an ongoing basis.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger,
Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
f)   Handgun Ordinance Discussion. Attorney Janega briefed the Trustees on the
recent Supreme Court opinion in District of Columbia v. Heller and its current
implications for the Village of Winnetka.
As noted earlier, this item was added to the Agenda to allow for Council
discussion. President explained that there will be further discussion of the
matter in Executive Session, after which the Council may return to open
session; however, no formal action is scheduled for this meeting.
11)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Berger reminded residents to post their addresses in a
clearly visible location on the outside of their residences. She also reported
that the Library Board has met with its consultant, who has started its needs
analysis. A preliminary status report should be delivered shortly. She noted
that the Library Board is taking steps to assure that the tax levy is done
correctly in the future.
President Woodbury asked when residents might expect public hearings on the
matter.
John Jansson, a member of the Library Board, indicated that they were looking
at the end of September and early October to hold public input sessions.
Trustee Behles reported on two recent meetings of the Ad Hoc Streetscape
Committee. He encouraged residents to look for the demonstration project on the
south side of Tower Road to be completed in the fall.
Trustee Johnson reported on the recent Chamber of Commerce meeting.
Trustee Rintz announced that the Plan Commission would hold a hearing on the
Fell development project on July 23 rd and encouraged interested persons to
attend.
On behalf of the Business Community Development Commission, Trustee Poor
encouraged everyone to attend the upcoming Sidewalk Sale and to “Shop
Winnetka!”
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that a traffic accident at the corner
of Willow and Hibbard Roads had resulted in the loss of the traffic controller
for the corner. He said the Water & Electric Department was working to expedite
the repair and return conditions to normal, and indicated that he had
authorized the purchase of a new controller for an amount slightly in excess of
$25,000.
12)   Appointments.
President Woodbury called for a motion to reappoint Richard Glenn to the Police
Pension Board for a full term. Trustee Berger so moved, seconded by Trustee
Rintz. By voice vote, the motion carried.
He then called for a motion to appoint David Bender to the Fire Pension Board
for a full term. Trustee Johnson so moved, seconded by Trustee Behles. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
Next he asked for a motion to appoint Scott Myers to the Zoning Board of
Appeals for a full term. Trustee Poor so moved, seconded by Trustee Behles. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
He next requested a motion to appoint John Iberle to the Plan Commission for a
full term. Trustee Rintz so moved, seconded by Trustee Johnson. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
Finally, President Woodbury called for a motion to appoint David Abell as Chair
of the new Environmental and Forestry Commission, effective September 1, 2008.
Trustee Behles so moved, seconded by Trustee Berger. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
13)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(3) and 2(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:15 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:16 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
August 5, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: August 19, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 5, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Director of
Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys and
approximately three persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda, as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
a)   August 12, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
President Woodbury and Trustee Johnson, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   August 19, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   July 8, 2008, Special Meeting and Study Session. Trustee Berger, seconded
by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-11-2008 – Zoning Variations: ––––635 Hill Rd.–– -
Adoption. President Woodbury called for further comments regarding proposed
ordinance granting a side yard setback variation to permit the construction of
a detached garage.
Trustee Behles explained that he was opposed to this request because there is
nothing unique or unusual about the site; it is large, rectangular, and a
conforming alternative can be identified. He added that he does not consider a
four-car garage to be a necessity, nor is it something that is required to
achieve a reasonable return. He voiced concern about making an exception to the
Zoning Ordinance for something like this, even though this particular variation
would really harm no one.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-11-
2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Johnson, Rintz, Berger, and Poor. Nays: Trustee Behles. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-12-2008 – Zoning Variations: 1004 Pine Tree Lane -
Adoption. President Woodbury called for further comments regarding proposed
ordinance granting a front yard setback variation to permit the construction of
a new single-family residence. There being none, Trustee Poor, seconded by
Trustee Greable, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-12-2008. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Greable, Behles, Rintz, and
Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1567 and 1568. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1567 in the amount of $1,696,827.88 and Warrant List No. 1568 in the amount of
$1,225,757.48. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Request to Place Flags on the Village Green. Manager Williams reported
that there has been a proposal by a local student to place 2, 977 American
flags on the Village Green to honor each person who lost their life in the
terrorist attacks on September 11 ,  2001. He provided an historical
perspective on the use of the Village Green, explaining that the Village owns
the property, while the Park District maintains it. It is, therefore, necessary
for the Village Council to review and approve every request to do something
unique on the property. He reported that neither the Park District nor staff
had expressed any problems with the plan.
President Woodbury recognized Genevieve Nielsen, 425 Linden, who explained that
her plan is to place 2,977 4” x 6” American flags on the Village Green on the
evening of September 10 th and remove them late on September 11 th .
President Woodbury remarked that the Council must consider each request for the
Village Green’s use very carefully as it is a prominent place in the Village
and a center of focus in the community. It must it separate this very positive
use from those that might be considered a negative use.
Ms. Nielsen responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Johnson voiced his support for the project and commended Ms. Nielsen
for her efforts.
Trustee Behles concurred, saying that this is a temporary, 24-hour expression
of sentiment, not a permanent structure, and being the Village’s place of
remembrance, there is no more appropriate place for such a thing to take place.
The other Trustees agreed.
Phil Hoza, Quartermaster of the VFW, also expressed his support.
President Woodbury explained to Ms. Nielsen that based on the non-permanent,
non-political nature of her request, one-time approval is granted. He suggested
that the flags be accompanied by a poster or notice explaining the memorial
purpose of the display and asked Ms. Nielsen to work with Mr. Saunders to
determine the best location on the Village Green for the project.
c)   Concrete Restoration, Winnetka Water Treatment Plant. Mr. Keys reviewed
bids for the fifth year of concrete restoration at the Water Treatment Plant
including surface treatment and deck and basin repairs. He reported that after
a review of the bids by both staff and the Village’s consulting engineer, the
recommended contractor is the low bidder, Fibrwrap Construction Inc. He
explained while the repairs are well specified, the required quantities are
uncertain until the work is actually underway and may, in fact, exceed the base
units used in the bid scope, particularly with regard to the repairs to the
walls of the north settling basin which could not be inspected prior to the
bidding. Therefore, he requested that the Trustees award the bid to Fibrwrap
Construction for $62,000 with an additional amount not to exceed $20,000 for
additional work beyond the estimated units.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to
award the bid as recommended to Fibrwrap Construction, Inc., for an amount not
to exceed $82,000, including the $20,000 contingency. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Greable, Johnson,
Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Bid 08-016: Replacement of 1991 Water Truck (WE 65). Mr. Keys reviewed
bids for a truck used to perform maintenance and repair activities on the
Village’s water distribution system. He reported staff’s recommendation that
the bid be awarded to the lowest complying bidder that meets all
specifications, Northwest Trucks Inc., in the amount of $186,679.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Johnson, Greable, Behles, Rintz, Berger, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Cenotaph Construction Bid Authorization. Mr. Saunders explained that in
May the Village Council authorized staff to proceed with developing design and
bidding documents for the possible restoration of the Cenotaph structure on the
Village Green. At that time, it was decided that the timing of the project
would be discussed when those documents were completed and more was known about
the status of fundraising efforts. He reported that design drawings and plans
are now 95% complete and once final comments are incorporated will be ready to
be sent out for bid. Overall, he said it is unlikely that the project cost
estimate will vary substantially from the total amount of $435,789.88 presented
in May. He went on to enumerate areas that need further revision and to report
on current fundraising efforts amounting to approximately $38,000 to date. Mr.
Saunders then outlined two options, and their benefits and drawbacks, for
moving forward with the project: (1) proceeding to solicit competitive bids for
the restoration work to begin in the fall, and (2) defer the project (and
bidding) until next spring. He then responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Poor asked how long the bidding process might take and whether the
project might be broken down into pieces. Mr. Saunders responded that the
process might be completed in time to award the bid at the second meeting in
September and to begin construction soon thereafter. He explained that the
masonry and tuckpointing work has to be done all at once. The flagpole and
bronzes, however, might be taken out and done at a later date, but they are not
the big driver of the cost.
Trustee Behles asked whether the intent is to put gravel around the monument or
to replace the pavers. Mr. Saunders replied that while the detail on the
original plans shows landscape quality gravel, he has yet to determine whether
that would meet the requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act.
President Woodbury asked how long Mr. Saunders expected the construction period
to last. Mr. Saunders said he anticipated it to be around ninety days.
Mr. Woodbury went on to say that if construction starts in the spring, it is
unlikely that it would be done in time for Memorial Day, and inquired as to the
possibility of doing half in the fall and half in the spring.
Trustee Behles remarked that to do so might leave the Cenotaph vulnerable to
the elements over the winter.
Trustee Johnson observed that it would be nice to be certain that the project
would be completed in time for next year’s Memorial Day and the Fourth of July
celebrations, but indicated that he finds the idea of getting alternative bids
for both fall and spring start dates appealing.
Trustee Greable raised a question as to whether fundraising efforts might
become less productive if the Village steps in to start the project now and
agrees to use tax dollars to subsidize the difference in cost.
Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry, Chairman of the fundraising committee, reported on the
Committee’s efforts and thanked everyone for their support. He said that the
Committee will continue to actively solicit contributions and that he expects
additional funds to flow in over time.
Nan Greenough, 500 Maple, enumerated the Committee’s next steps. She urged the
Council to begin construction on the project as soon as possible to avoid
further damage that might occur as a result of the freeze/thaw cycle over the
coming winter.
Trustee Poor thanked the Committee for its efforts thus far, as did President
Woodbury, saying that what has been raised thus far is remarkable.
Trustee Behles said he was in favor of proceeding with the bid documents as
soon as possible with an eye toward starting construction in the fall, which is
the best time for the Village Green to be “down.” He observed that to defer the
project until spring with the expectation of receiving significant additional
funds would not overcome the wintering problems and would not allow for a full
season of use.
Trustee Berger expressed concern about moving ahead immediately, saying that
while everyone agrees that the Cenotaph should be preserved, it is a question
of timing, expense and who pays for it. She pointed out that the monument was
originally erected by 3,800 private citizens with the promise to maintaining
it; for the Village to take it over now is passing the responsibility of the
public to the Village itself. She added that the question is not one of
importance, but one of priorities. She noted that there are many projects
competing for public funds. She said that although she feels some
responsibility to proceed up to the $300,000 put into the budget as a
placeholder, she is not comfortable exceeding that amount.
Trustee Rintz strongly recommended going out for bid now. He commented that the
Cenotaph is the center of civic pride around which Winnetka’s residents gather
for community events. He opined that for a community of Winnetka’s stature to
not be willing or able to maintain one of its central gathering points makes no
sense. He added that this is a good economy in which to buy construction trades
and he expects to see aggressive bidding before the end of the year.
Fundraising efforts can continue and potentially help to maintain the site in
the future.
Trustee Poor concurred with Trustees Behles and Rintz.
Trustee Greable voiced no objection to moving forward immediately and putting
fundraising off to the side as part of the decision-making process. He,
however, wanted it to be clear to all that when the item was included in the
budget, the Council had the expectation of receiving substantial funds from
private parties.
Trustee Johnson agreed.
Trustee Berger urged all to contribute to the fundraising efforts so that
public funds can be spent for other purposes.
President Woodbury acknowledged that this community asset has gone unattended
to for too many years. He said it is time to stop deferring maintenance and
start investing in those assets, saying that to wait further will likely result
only increased deterioration and costs.
Therefore, it was the consensus of the Trustees to authorize staff to solicit
competitive bidding proposals for repairing the Cenotaph with a base bid for
fall 2008 construction and an alternate option for a 2009 start date.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Berger reported on the recent activities of the Library
Board. She said that the first draft of the needs analysis has been presented
to the Board and will be finalized soon. The Board will then hold workshops and
public meetings. She noted that there will be significant changes in that
Board’s composition in the coming months due to the expiration of members’
terms.
Trustee Rintz reported that the Plan Commission continues to discuss the
redevelopment of the Fell property. Another meeting is scheduled for the end of
August.
Trustee Behles provided an update on the status of the streetscape
demonstration project and encouraged everyone to visit the site when it is
completed. The Committee will be presenting its proposal to the Council later
in the fall.
President Woodbury added that the goal of the demonstration project is to
solicit the public’s reaction to the physical design aspects. He noted that
after the Committee makes its recommendations, there will be an opportunity for
the public to comment and hearings will be held.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the copy of Comprehensive Annual
Financial Report for the fiscal year 2007-2008 was now available. He said that
the Village’s Auditor would attend the September Study Session to the review
the Report and respond to questions. He indicated that the Village had had a
good fiscal year overall.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury called for a motion to appoint Louise Holland as the
Landmark Preservation Commission representative to the Plan Commission,
effective immediately. Trustee Behles so moved, seconded by Trustee Rintz. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
He then called for a motion to appoint Susan Curry to the Landmark Preservation
Commission for a full term, effective immediately. Trustee Berger so moved,
seconded by Trustee Johnson. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Finally he asked for a motion to appoint Debbie Ross to the Environmental and
Forestry Commission, effective September 1st. Trustee Poor so moved, seconded
by Trustee Behles. By voice vote, the motion carried.
12)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 9:
00 p.m.
____________________________
   Recording Secretary
August 12, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: August 19, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 12, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Behles called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Chris Rintz, and King Poor. Absent:
President Edmund Woodbury and Trustee Bill Johnson. Also in attendance: Village
Manager Doug Williams, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys and Assistant
Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla. There were no people in the audience.
2)   Net Metering.
Mr. Keys led the Trustees through a PowerPoint presentation. He explained that
net metering serves as an incentive for consumer investment in renewable
energy. It enables customers to use their own generation to offset their energy
consumption over a billing period. When the consumer generates electricity in
excess of their demand, the meter turns “backwards,” meaning that electricity
flows to the grid, and the customer is credited or paid for the amount
generated. He noted that in the absence of a defined policy with regard to net
metering, municipalities may face regulation in the future. Mr. Keys went on to
review different scenarios for net metering, reviewed existing policies in
place in other agencies, and outlined a proposed policy for Winnetka. For the
purposes of this discussion he identified $0.04/kWh as the base power rate, or
purchased power cost, and $0.10/kWh as the retail rate, or true cost of
service. He also enumerated the key decisions that need to be made should the
Council decide to allow net metering interconnections, such as (1) what type of
sources would be permitted? (2) what capacity/sizes? (3) will the Village
permit customers to send power onto the grid, and if so, what is the customer’s
earned credit? and (4) will insurance be required?
Trustee Poor inquired as to the credit being offered to Ameren and ComEd
customers. Mr. Keys replied that they are offering credit for the full retail
cost.
Manager Williams noted that from a practical point of view, net metering is not
likely to have a big impact in Winnetka because the Village is not in a good
wind location, photovoltaic costs are enormous, and there are too many trees.
He said that historically Winnetka has set its electric rates based on cost of
service with no special classes of customers receiving subsidies. Using the
present example, the Village’s avoided cost would be $0.04/kWh, but if the
Council decides to grant a $0.10/kWh credit, there will be a subsidy in place
benefiting those with net metering. He mentioned that staff would be bringing a
number of renewable energy options before the Council in the fall, some of
which will involve subsidies of one sort or another. He recommended that the
Council proceed gradually and mentioned that when the Council considered the
same issue a couple of years ago, they talked about crediting avoided costs
instead of retail costs. He also noted that the Village’s load growth continues
to grow at about 2% per year, in keeping with the national average.
Trustee Behles commented that even though there is not much renewable potential
in Winnetka, the Village is part of a larger whole and should set an example.
After further discussion, there was a consensus that staff should move forward
with drafting a policy permitting net metering with credits calculated using
the base power rate. It was agreed that the policy should limit the credit bank
and require the homeowner to pay for the associated utility costs, have
insurance in excess of $1M and provide for some sort of hold harmless
agreement.
Trustee Berger remarked that although this will have minimal impact overall, it
is a good step in the right direction.
3)   Executive Session.
None was held.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Rintz,
and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: President Woodbury and Trustee Johnson. The
meeting adjourned at 8:36 p.m.
