   Recording Secretary
September 9, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: September 16, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 9, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Chris Rintz, and King
Poor. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Finance Director Ed
McKee, Assistant Finance Director Hanna Sullivan, and Deputy Police Chief Pat
Kries. There were six people in the audience.
2)   Zoning Regulations: Track 2 Evaluation by Winter & Company.
Mr. D’Onofrio briefly summarized the Council’s actions to date pertaining to
concerns raised about zoning standards as they relate to new single family
homes in single family zoning districts. As proposed by Winter & Company, this
Track 2 path, with Track 1 being their ongoing work on the voluntary
Residential Design Guidelines project, would result in specific and detailed
evaluation of zoning standards related to scale and bulk issues. It would be
accompanied by detailed computer modeling of existing zoning standards to
investigate their appropriateness. The project would culminate with a report
summarizing findings and recommending specific Code amendments where necessary.
As proposed, the consultant would conduct a series of public workshops as a
part of the process. Depending upon the number of community workshops
conducted, as determined by the Council, the consultant provided two
alternative budget figures, $76,679 or $91,149, with project completion in
eight to ten months.
Mr. D’Onofrio referred the Trustees to a comparison of Winnetka’s zoning
ordinance bulk regulations with those of Glencoe, Highland Park, Kenilworth,
Lake Forest, and Wilmette. He pointed out that a precise comparison cannot be
made because there are differences in the comparable zoning districts among the
six communities. He also cautioned against looking at individual regulations,
because they are all a part of a larger whole; and nuances in each ordinance
play a significant role in determining the size, shape, bulk and location of a
building on a lot.
President Woodbury remarked that as originally conceived, the two-track process
would be somewhat overlapping, whereas they are now proposed to be more
parallel. He inquired of staff as to how they envision the two being
integrated.
Mr. Norkus responded that the two projects could be commingled successfully,
particularly because much of the field work has already been completed;
however, to do so would extend the process because of the time required for
community workshops. He said that the Ad Hoc Residential Design Committee would
be charged with keeping the process moving forward on a timely basis.
Trustee Rintz, Chairman of the Ad Hoc Residential Design Committee, indicated
that although he had originally thought that commingling the two projects would
be beneficial, he is now concerned that proceeding in that direction would
delay finalizing his committee’s work. He said that adding another eight-plus
months to the process would extend it unduly and was not what the previous
Council envisioned.
In response to a question from President Woodbury, Mr. Rintz suggested that Mr.
Winter’s proposal could be trimmed down and shortened by omitting much of the
additional public process, and it would still generate the work product needed
to make good decisions.
Trustee Poor recalled that at the time the previous Council passed Resolution
No. R-23-2008 pertaining to zoning regulations in the single-family residential
zoning district, the Council wanted to do something concrete to address
concerns about overbuilding and encourage renovations of existing homes, as
well as harvest the experience of the remaining trustees. However, rather than
enacting a blanket 2% reduction in GFA as proposed, it deferred action in order
to do a more comprehensive zoning review. He voiced concern about postponing
action another eight months or more.
Trustee Poor went on to say that he would like to see a way to simplify the
process and compress the timeframe and suggested that eliminating the public
workshops would both expedite the process and trim the costs. This, he said,
would move the process forward in a slimmed down fashion but still result in
the Village identifying some tools and recommendations to address overbuilding
in a way that will not take another nine months. He pointed out that there will
be significant turnover in the Council in the coming year and encouraged his
colleagues to take action before it loses the institutional memory and
understanding of the sitting Trustees.
Trustee Behles remarked that the Village does not need six more workshops on
top of the already-completed design guidelines process to move forward. He
opined that this work needs to be scaled back and synchronized with the work of
the ad hoc committee in order to avoid further public confusion. He noted that
the issues of design guidelines and zoning are integrated and need to be
addressed as such. He pointed out that design guidelines function within the
context of zoning, which, in turn, feed into community character and
architectural recommendations. He opined that what the Village needs is the
modeling of the existing ordinance and potential modifications thereto, which
are the most desirable components of the subject proposal. He said that what is
missing is this technical analysis, which should be completed fairly quickly,
to piggyback onto the already-completed work of the ad hoc committee. He
cautioned against letting this become an issue that dominates the Council’s
attention during 2009.
Trustee Rintz commented that the design guidelines track is working to identify
Winnetka-style architecture; zoning is numerically how you get Design A instead
of Design B.
Trustee Berger agreed with Trustee Behles, saying that the timeframe for this
proposal is too long and the cost too high. She said that in her opinion this
is one of the most critical issues facing the Village, and that therefore, it
needs to move forward as quickly as it can.
Trustees Greable and Johnson concurred with the others’ comments regarding
expense and timing.
Manager Williams pointed out that if there are to be zoning amendments, there
will be additional public process when the matter comes before the Council for
consideration. He suggested that staff work with Mr. Winter on the technical
analysis, modeling, and synchronizing those results with the ad hoc committee’s
report, so that both presentations can be made to the Trustees no later than
January 1, 2009.
3)   Business Hours for Restaurants.
Attorney Janega explained that during the course of recent Council discussions
about the hours for liquor service and restaurant hours, some neighbors of the
McDonald’s in Hubbard Woods requested that the Council consider reducing the
hours of operation of fast food restaurants. The neighbors suggested that the
hours be limited to between 6:30 a.m. and 11:00 p.m. on weekends to alleviate
activities they perceived to impact the peaceful enjoyment of the adjacent
single family residential neighborhood. At that time, the discussion of the
matter was deferred until summer, with the intent that if any change was deemed
warranted, the change could become effective with the 2009 calendar year
license cycle. (Although the Village Code permits fast food restaurants to be
open from 6:00 a.m. until midnight daily, McDonalds is currently open from 6:00
a.m. to 11:00 p.m. on Sunday through Thursday and 6:00 a.m. to midnight on
Friday and Saturday.)
Ms. Janega referred the Trustees to a comparison of restaurant and liquor
service hours for neighboring communities, and Deputy Police Chief Pat Kries
commented on Police incident reports for McDonalds and other similar locales.
He reported that the Police Department averages one nuisance call per month for
the subject location and that most incidents involve local teens. He also noted
that the restaurant owners have been active participants with the Police
Department in crime prevention matters.
Scott Hallerman, 839 Locust, and Creed Tucker, 850 Locust, spoke on behalf of
the neighbors, saying that the establishment’s hours have increased over time
and that early morning and late evening activities on the site disrupt the
peace and quiet of the neighborhood. They encouraged the Council to curtail
McDonald’s hours of operation to 9:00 p.m. or 10:00 p.m. during the week and
11:00 p.m. on weekend, saying that bad behavior tends to correlate with later
hours.
President Woodbury pointed that any action taken by the Council must be
community-wide and cannot pertain to only one restaurant.
Mark Freeman, McDonald’s owner, and his attorney, Darren Miller, spoke against
restricting the hours of operation. Mr. Miller expressed concern about the
potential punitive nature of any change to the existing Code that would impact
only one set of existing businesses. He pointed out that the restaurant has
been operating within the law in the same location since 1981 and that the
owners, old and new, have consistently worked with the Police Department and
the neighbors to resolve issues. Mr. Freeman reviewed his plans to invest
significantly in a renovation of the store and stated that any restriction
reducing his hours of operation would impact his business and result in the
loss of sales.
In response to a question from Trustee Berger, Mr. Freeman confirmed that the
extra hour of operation is a popular hour in the restaurant.
After further discussion, Manager Williams suggested that the Village arrange a
meeting with both the owners and the neighbors to talk through some of the
problems to see if they can be resolved satisfactorily.
Trustee Behles stated that he would not be in favor of a Code change adding
restrictions on this one business, saying that nothing has changed over time
and there is no history to suggest the need to make a punitive judgment against
this one establishment. Any such Code change would have larger implications for
the entire Village and could discourage, instead of encourage, business
development in the community.
There was a consensus to facilitate a dialogue among the parties before
considering any changes to the Village Code.
4)   Review Comprehensive Annual Financial Report.
Ron Amen, Lauterbach and Amen, reported on the audit process and the
Comprehensive Annual Financial Report for the fiscal year ended March 31, 2008.
He commended the Finance Department and Village staff for their assistance
during the audit and noted the Village’s receipt of a Certificate of
Achievement for Excellence in Financial Reporting presented by the Government
Finance Officers Association to government units whose comprehensive annual
financial reports achieve the highest standards in government accounting and
financial reporting. Mr. Amen reported that the Village’s overall financial
position remains strong, and operations are generally in line with the budget.
Mr. Amen, Mr. McKee and Ms. Sullivan then responded to questions from the
Trustees.
President Woodbury thanked Mr. Amen and his firm for their thorough reports.
5)   Executive Session.
Trustee Rintz moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing personnel matter, and pending, probable or imminent litigation
pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) and 2(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Rintz and Poor.
Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:21
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:08 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Rintz, and Poor. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Rintz, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:09 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
September 16, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(October 7, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 16, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor, and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, and four
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   October 7, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 14, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend, although Trustee Poor said he might be
unavailable.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 2, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   September 9, 2008, Study Session. Trustee Berger suggested the addition of
the specific date that the Council expects to hear the reports from the zoning
consultant and the Ad Hoc Residential Design Committee. Trustee Johnson,
seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the minutes as amended. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-05-2008 – Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to
Cost Allocation for Replacing Lead Water Services - Introduction. Attorney
Janega briefly reviewed the history of this issue and referred the Trustees to
a revised draft of the proposed ordinance. She explained that the proposed
language would correct an existing inconsistency between residential and non-
residential properties, in order to be certain that the Code accurately
reflects the Village’s practice. In summary, she said that the Village will be
responsible for repairs or replacement between the main and the property line
for residential property and between the main and the curb line for commercial
property. The Ordinance also clarifies the repair responsibilities for damages
to the Village’s water system and provides for the Village’s professional staff
to determine the procedural steps for administering the Ordinance.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-05-2008, including the revised text recommended by the Village Attorney. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-06-2008 – Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to the
Foreign Fire Insurance Board Deposit of Funds - Introduction. Attorney Janega
reviewed the draft Ordinance amending the Village Code to bring the Village
process into line with recently-adopted amendments to the statutory framework
regarding Foreign Fire Insurance Board monies. She explained that the funds
come through the State from a tax imposed on insurance companies operating
within the Village, and are controlled by the Foreign Fire Insurance Board,
which allocates the funds for uses that directly benefit the firefighters.
However, as drafted, the Ordinance would continue to allow the Village
Treasurer to handle the bookkeeping.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce the ordinance.
By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Lane, raised a commercial property maintenance
issue. Manager Williams responded that the Council has been studying the
property maintenance code issue. She repeated her request that the Trustees
formally amend the Village Code to state that no Council meetings may be held
on an election night.
President Woodbury noted that there would be no Council meeting this coming
November 4 th , and that the Code had been modified last year to provide that
the Council would publish its Meeting Calendar annually, taking into account
scheduled upcoming holidays and events.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce Street, expressed concern about the effectiveness of
the Village’s ordinance against driving while using a hand-held cell phone. He
urged the Village to increase enforcement and take steps to heighten awareness
and strengthen penalties and suggested ways in which this might be done.
President Woodbury noted that citations for cell phone violations rank as the
#2 ticket issued and indicated that the Village would consider some of Mr.
Hecht’s suggestions.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1573 and 1574. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1573 in the amount of $1,048,171.82 and Warrant List No. 1574 in the amount of
$438,123.86. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Rintz, Johnson, Berger, Greable, Behles, and
Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Lease of Public Works Site to Winnetka Park District. Attorney Janega
reviewed the draft of a Letter of Intent for the Village to enter into a long-
term lease with the Winnetka Park District for their use of a portion of the
Village’s Public Works Yards for new operational facilities. She explained that
the Park District has requested such written assurance from the Village and
went on to enumerate the key terms of the agreement between the parties. She
noted that the final documents would include a platted survey showing the
location of easements and a development agreement and that these would all be
presented to the Council before being finalized.
Manager Williams remarked that there is a long tradition of cooperation between
the Village and the Park District and pointed out the parties’ exchange of
property in 1994 that enabled the Village to build its Winnetka Avenue pump
station.
Trustee Greable mentioned that cooperation between the governmental entities in
the use of property and services is in the best interest of all residents and
said he hoped to see more of it in the future. He suggested that given the
lengthy duration of the lease, an escalator clause be included in the insurance
provisions. The other Trustees concurred.
In response to comments made by Trustee Johnson and President Woodbury,
respectively, Attorney Janega indicated that the final lease would also include
provisions addressing abandonment of the lease and disposition of the site
should construction not begin within a predetermined period of time.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the Village
President to sign a Letter of Intent to the Winnetka Park District
substantially in the form attached to the Agenda Report, incorporating comments
made at the meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, Rintz, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Poor reported that at its first meeting the
Environmental and Forestry Commission heard presentations from the head of
Highland Park’s Environmental Commission and from a representative of an
Illinois environmental advocacy group. He also indicated that the heritage tree
issue would be back on their agenda for further discussion in the near future.
Trustee Berger reported that the Library Board was moving forward with
continuing its needs analysis and planning visits to notable libraries in the
area.
Trustee Rintz reported that the Plan Commission would continue its discussion
on the Fell redevelopment project at its September 24 th meeting. He also
reported that Community Development staff had contacted Winter & Company to
plot a presentation course for the zoning code analysis and report of the Ad
Hoc Residential Design Committee to coincide in January.
Trustee Behles urged all residents to visit the streetscape demonstration
project so that they can offer their comments during public hearings to be held
in October.
Trustee Johnson reported on his attendance at the most recent meeting of the
Chamber of Commerce.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that Jeffrey Liss had reiterated a
suggestion that the Council and the Caucus sponsor an annual event for new
residents to explain the Caucus process and the duties and responsibilities of
the various Village boards and committees. President Woodbury indicated that he
would raise the matter with the Caucus and other governmental bodies.
Mr. Williams then reviewed a preliminary report on the past weekend’s rainfall
and flash flooding event prepared by Steve Saunders, Director of Public Works/
Village Engineer. He reported that overall the Village’s stormwater facilities
worked well in response to the storm, and the Village’s infrastructure
weathered the event well, thanks to significant investments in improvements
over the last fifteen years. He said that the event was a severe test of the
system and staff would be evaluating possible additional improvements that
might be made.
President Woodbury thanked staff for their dedicated service and ongoing
cleanup efforts.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Stephen Presser to the Board
of Police and Fire Commissioners for a second full term. Trustee Berger,
seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to appoint Mr. Presser. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Pending and Probable Litigation and Board Appointments pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Poor seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Rintz, Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Poor.
Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:10
p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:06 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Poor. Absent: Trustee
Rintz. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz. The meeting adjourned at
10:07 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
October 7, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(October 21, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 7, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:42 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor, and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Director of
Public Works Steve Saunders, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, and approximately 9 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   October 14, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   October 21, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend, with the exception of Trustee Poor, who
indicated that he may not be present.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   September 16, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Greable, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2008 – Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to Cost
Allocation for Replacing Lead Water Services - Adoption. Attorney Janega
reviewed proposed amendments made to the ordinance since introduction, and
stated that the subject ordinance is the final step in clarifying the
provisions for water repair and replacement of lead services.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to amend Ordinance MC-5-2008,
as indicated in the revised draft. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-5-
2008 as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2008 – Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to the
Foreign Fire Insurance Board Deposit of Funds - Adoption. There being no
comments or amendments, President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt this
ordinance.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-6-
2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Resolution No. R-35-2008 – Authorizing the Release of Executive Session
Minutes and the Destruction of Verbatim Recordings – Adoption. Attorney Janega
explained that the Open Meetings Act requires the Council to examine and
approve Executive Session minutes and to release those which no longer require
confidentiality. She indicated that the Act also requires the Village to retain
verbatim recordings of closed sessions for 18 months, noting that the Act makes
the recordings available only to a judge, not to the general public.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
35-2008 releasing the Executive Session minutes no longer required for reasons
of confidentiality, and permitting the destruction of audio recordings of
closed meetings from October 10, 2006 through March 20, 2007. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor
and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
President Woodbury invited members of the audience who wished to address the
Council to do so at this time.
Dr. James Feld, 260 White Oak Lane, requested the Village’s help with extreme
flooding in his neighborhood during heavy rain storms. He indicated that the
storm of September 13 th  filled his basement with three to four feet of water,
and he distributed photos to the Council showing some of the damage that
resulted. He added that his basement has severely flooded on two other
occasions throughout the years, and that consultations with landscapers,
plumbers and the Village to try to mitigate the flooding problem have been to
no avail. He asked for help in finding a solution to the problem of flooding in
the Sunset/DeWindt/White Oak neighborhood.
Mr. Bill Doyle, 251 white Oak Lane, said he experienced flooding that same
weekend, despite the fact that he had recently installed new sump pumps,
generators, and trenches. He said he would be interested in hearing how the
Village would address the problem.
Mr. Jim Gordon, 281 White Oak Lane, said he saw the issue in larger terms, and
described the flooding that occurs on streets and in yards throughout the
neighborhood on a regular basis. He suggested that a detention basin be built
to contain the excess water during a heavy storm.
Mr. Judd Porter, 970 Sunset, said the severe flooding that occurs in the area
impacts property values, and asked if there was a way to funnel the water
somewhere else when heavy rains fall.
Public Works Director Steve Saunders thanked the residents for their
perspective, and observed that there are only two ways to address storm water:
store it or move it. He indicated that the Skokie River holds a finite amount
of water, and that it impacts other towns downstream, so storage is a better
option, but added that open space is an issue that requires a community
discussion. He described a number of stormwater improvements that could be
undertaken to help improve drainage, and estimated their cost.
Manager Williams cautioned that the Village can only relieve drainage problems,
not solve them. He indicated that the key priority is to add additional
drainage capacity in the neighborhood, and described such other possible
measures as pump station improvements, re-grading the forest preserve drainage
ditch and adding a new storm sewer system roughly from Birch to Sunset.
President Woodbury indicated that water in basements is not acceptable, but
water being detained in yards and streets for a period of time is tolerable,
and is an element of the drainage system. After some discussion, the Council
determined that Mr. Saunders should proceed with engineering studies to
determine how best to add more detention and pipe capacity.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1575 and 1576. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1575 in the amount of $1,182,381.76 and Warrant List No. 1576 in the amount of
$636,921.62. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Policy Direction: Subdivision and Zoning Variations - 596 Arbor Vitae. Mr.
Norkus reviewed a request to subdivide the existing single lot at 596 Arbor
Vitae into three lots. He noted that the parcel is unique among neighboring
parcels because it is a “through lot” extending 300 feet from Arbor Vitae to
Lincoln Avenue, and he added that the proposed division occurs along the
boundary between the R-4 and R-5 zoning districts. He said the applicant wishes
to further divide the Lincoln Ave. frontage into two 50-foot wide lots, which
would not conform with R-5 zoning district requirements for lot area and width.
He indicated, however, that the proposed new lots would be consistent with the
size of adjacent residential parcels and would preserve the harmony of the
block face.
Mr. Norkus reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 5-2 to recommend
denial of the requested zoning variations, and the Plan Commission had voted 8-
0 to recommend approval of the proposed subdivision, subject to staff’s
recommended modifications. He recalled that a similar subdivision creating 50-
foot wide lots was approved on Ash Street a couple of years ago.
The applicant, Mr. Dave Webster, gave a history of the lot going back to 1954,
and noted that when the property was bought, 50-foot lot widths were allowed.
He noted the absence of objections from neighbors, and added that that a 100-
foot wide lot would be out of character with the surrounding neighborhood.
The applicant’s attorney, Mr. David Schrauth, asserted that the standards for a
zoning variation had been met, and that it was no surprise the Plan Commission
recommended approval, as the proposed lots would be more in keeping with the
other lots on the block face.
Ms. Nan Greenough, 500 Maple, gave her perspective in support of the requested
variations in light of a decision some years ago to grant a variation at 1031
Ash to permit two 50-foot wide lots, in keeping with the surrounding
neighborhood. She noted that the proposed subdivision is in keeping with the
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan recommendation to scale down density from the
commercial districts to the larger lots in residential areas.
Trustee Poor said he usually defers to the ZBA, but would support this
variation request because it maintains the character of the block face, and
also is an opportunity to provide a diversity of housing stock, which the
Village encourages.
Trustee Berger said while she respected the argument that the proposal is in
keeping with the other lots on the block face, she had a concern about creating
substandard lots and indicated that she would like to hear from the rest of the
Council before making up her mind.
Trustee Rintz noted that he is on the Plan Commission and supports the request,
that a large lot with a large house in that neighborhood would destroy the
rhythm of the block face, and that he agreed with Trustee Poor on the issue of
housing diversity.
Trustee Behles said the ZBA made the right decision from a purely zoning
perspective, and the Plan Commission had made a sound recommendation from a
planning perspective, to unify the block face and to keep mass, scale and
height relative to the other homes on the block. He said he would support the
variations, but urged the Council to be resolute in its support for conforming
houses to be built on the small lots that will be created.
Trustee Johnson agreed with Trustee Behles that the 50-foot lots keep a sense
of conformity and help create diverse housing stock, and supported the request
with the caveat that zoning variations not be granted to any future houses on
the site.
Trustee Greable complimented the ZBA and Plan Commission for an outstanding
job, and said he would support the request to create two 50-foot lots.
Trustee Berger then said she would support the consensus of the Council,
providing no zoning variations would be granted to future houses on the site.
President Woodbury directed Attorney Janega to draft an ordinance for the
Council and use language she deemed appropriate with respect to future houses
on the site being built in compliance with the Zoning Ordinance.
c)   Roof Replacement/Repair, Winnetka Electric Plant. Water & Electric
Director Brian Keys reviewed the bids to replace two sections of roof at the
Electric Plant, and indicated that the bid amount is over budget and an offset
would be taken from the Building Account to address the cost difference.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid for roof
replacement at the Electric Plant to Eurocon Enterprises, Inc., in the amount
of $37,700, with an additional amount not to exceed $3,500 to cover additional
work beyond the specified units. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
d)   Bid 08-018: PW-25 Truck Chassis and Body Replacement. Mr. Saunders
reviewed the bids for replacement of a dump/snow removal truck, and recommended
awarding the bid to Northwest Trucks, Inc.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the bid for a
replacement truck chassis and body, plow, and hook lift system, excluding the
Epoke application unit, to Northwest Trucks, Inc., for $162,655. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor
and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
e)   Bid #08-020: Winnetka Cenotaph Restoration. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids
received for the Cenotaph restoration project, and recommended Sebastian
Contracting, as there was a significant price advantage with their Spring start
price. He noted that there is currently $53,000 in the Cenotaph Restoration
Fund.
Manager Williams asked the Council to table awarding the bid until he could
have staff complete some further research.
f)   Bid 08-023: 2008 Holiday Lighting. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for the
Village’s 2008 Holiday Lighting, and recommended awarding the bid to Landscape
Concepts Management, as they were the low bidder, and completed the Holiday
Lights in 2007 in a highly satisfactory manner.
After a short discussion, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to
award the bid for the 2008 holiday lighting to Landscape Concepts Management
for $42,008.20. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Greable announced that he had attended his first NWMC meeting
and the Legislative Committee meeting.
   ii)   Trustee Behles announced that the ad hoc Streetscape Committee would
be holding two public meetings, on Monday, October 20 th at 7:30 PM, and
Saturday, October 25 th at 10:00 AM and invited residents to attend.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that he had authorized a study to
explore the impact of the Willow Road dam improvements, and noted that the NWMC
auction netted the Village $35,000.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Joe Adams as Chairman of the
Zoning Board of Appeals, effective immediately. Trustee Rintz, seconded by
Trustee Greable, moved to appoint Mr. Adams. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Joni Johnson to the Zoning
Board of Appeals for a full term, effective immediately, to replace Molly Lien.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to appoint Ms. Johnson. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury called for the appointment of Zandra Vlahakis as the Student
Member of the Environmental and Forestry Commission, effective immediately.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to appoint Ms. Vlahakis. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing   Pending or Probable Litigation, Board Appointments and possible
lease of Village land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11,) 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(6) of
the Open Meetings Act.   Trustee Poor seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into
Executive Session at 10:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:15 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
16 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
October 14, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(November 6, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 14, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Public Works Director
Steve Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee, and 4 persons in the audience.
2)   Zoning Regulations: Track 2 Evaluation by Winter & Co.
Mr. Norkus reviewed a report from the Village’s Residential Design Guidelines
consultant, Winter & Co., that detailed their proposal to evaluate Winnetka’s
zoning regulations. He stated that Winter & Co. anticipates coming to the
December Study Session with a strategy paper for addressing mass and scale
issues, and that two subsequent study sessions are planned for more discussion
about possible Zoning Ordinance modifications. He noted that the Residential
Design Guidelines project would take a low profile in the interim, until after
any proposed zoning modifications are finalized and approved.
Trustee Rintz commented that the Residential Design Guidelines project will not
suffer from being put on a back burner while the consultant devotes his full
attention to the zoning issues.
There was a short discussion in which it was agreed that the project is moving
in the right direction, and the timing is right for it to be completed before a
new Council is seated in May of 2009.
Manager Williams said the contract for Winter & Co. would be brought to the
Council at the next meeting for approval.
3)   Request by Trapp Lane Homeowners to Dedicate Trapp Lane as a Public
Street.
Mr. Saunders summarized the issue of private streets in Winnetka, and reviewed
a request from the Trapp Lane homeowners that the Village take over Trapp Lane
as a public street. He described the improvements that needed to be completed
before the transfer could occur, and outlined the funding options available to
the residents.
Mr. Saunders reported that the residents on Trapp Lane were requesting the
Village’s help to defray construction costs, on the grounds that, as residents
of a private street they receive fewer Village services, without a
corresponding reduction in real estate taxes. He said the homeowners feel that
the demolition fees collected from Trapp lane properties in the last five years
should also be taken into consideration, as part of those fees go into the
Village’s street repair fund. He indicated that the homeowners would like to
know what amount, if any, the Village is willing to provide towards the
upgrades before they can take a final vote on whether to formally request the
Village to take over Trapp Lane.
Mr. D’Onofrio said teardowns and new construction cause some of the biggest
problems on private streets, and that Trapp Lane is especially tough because it
is only 17 feet wide. He noted that private streets don’t have police
enforcement for parking and other issues, and that the Community Development
Department receives a high volume of requests for enforcement of construction
parking rules. He indicated that a lot of repaving done on private streets
after utilities are hooked in is substandard, resulting in a patchwork quilt
effect, and added that staff spends a lot of time trying to sort out the
problems, which is frustrating for both residents and staff.
Manager Williams observed that drainage is another thorny issue on private
roads, and although the Village recently put in a new drainage system for the
area west of Hibbard Road, not one private road has hooked into it. He said the
Village has been dealing with the drainage problems for the past 35 years, and
recommended that the Council consider some changes in its policy regarding the
dedication of private streets. He suggested that evenly splitting the cost of
bringing the private roads up to Village standards could be a good compromise,
and that though this would be a paradigm shift, it would enable Village staff
to solve problems that are currently out of their control.
President Woodbury agreed with Manager Williams and indicated that a change in
the policy would create a positive framework for homeowners on private roads to
partner with the Village.
There was a discussion about the ongoing burden of more roads to maintain
without new revenue to pay for it, as well as the question of setting a
precedent that might affect the Village’s discretion to decline certain
streets.
Mr. Saunders indicated that taking over more private roads would not require
more staff or equipment.
President Woodbury observed that if the Council is interested in modifying its
policy, a new one could be crafted that leaves flexibility for the Village in
light of its financial obligations.
Trustee Rintz suggested that the Village develop a system of recapturing the
Village’s costs, if and when it helps to fund upgrades on a private street
prior to dedication to the Village.
Trustee Greable indicated that he could find no fault with the current policy
and likened the tax equity complaint to the issue of school choice in that
people pay property taxes whether their children attend public school or not.
Manager Williams observed that private roads were put in by developers at the
time of the subdivision’s construction, and that many are substandard.
Mollybelle Berish, 1274 Trapp Lane, said she bought her house because they
liked the house, and did not really consider the implications of living on a
private road at that time. She noted that the construction activity takes a big
toll on the road.
Tom Barron, 1284 Trapp Lane, observed that the drainage issues are a regional
problem, and that one neighboring street does not even have a storm sewer. He
said he believed residents were only asking for what is fair in light of the
taxes they pay, and that the Village is charged with protecting the interests
of all residents regardless of where they live.
Manager Williams agreed that drainage is a regional issue and said he was
interested in helping to solve the problem.
After a lengthy discussion, the Council agreed that the policy should be
updated, but that it should maintain flexibility so as not to create a big
burden on cash resources, and directed Staff to craft a new policy for Council
discussion.
4)   August 2008 Financial Statements.
Mr. McKee gave an overview of Winnetka’s financial position. He noted that t,
but stated that the Village has strong cash balances and that the major
operating funds are not experiencing any large difficulties. He reviewed each
of the Village’s funds and answered questions from the Council.
5)   Identity Theft Prevention Policy.
Mr. McKee reported that the Village is considered a creditor under the Fair and
Accurate Credit Transactions Act, and is therefore required to implement an
identity theft prevention program. He described the his proposed plan to meet
the November 1 st compliance deadline. After a brief discussion, the Council
directed Mr. McKee to proceed and bring a draft of the policy to its next
meeting.
6)   Village Investments.
Mr. McKee reported that the Village’s conservative investment policies and
practices have kept risk exposure low, and he described the various vehicles
for Village investments. After some discussion, the Council indicated they were
satisfied that the funds are properly diversified and risk is low.
7)   Financial Planning.
This item was removed from the Agenda.
8)   Executive Session.
None.
9)   Adjournment.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
35 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
October 21, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(November 6, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 21, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, and Chris Rintz. Absent:
Trustee King Poor. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Police
Chief Joe DeLopez, Deputy Police Chief Patrick Kreis, and approximately 29
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 6(e), a Subdivision and Zoning
Variations request for 596 Arbor Vitae, had been removed from the Agenda at the
request of the applicant. The Trustees approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
a)   November 6, 2008, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury reminded the
audience that, the next regular Council meeting has been moved to Thursday
November 6 th because the regular Tuesday night schedule coincides with the
presidential election. All of the Council members present indicated that they
expected to attend.
b)   November 11, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   October 7, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2008 – Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to
Weapons – Introduction/Adoption. President Woodbury explained that due to
pending litigation, the Council would first have a brief executive session
before proceeding with the public discussion. He added that public comments
would be limited to Winnetka residents only.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn into Executive
Session to discuss pending litigation pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11) of the Open
Meetings Act. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee King
Poor. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 7:37 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 7:58 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson and Rintz. Absent: Trustee
King Poor. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
At the request of President Woodbury, Attorney Janega summarized recent
developments regarding local handgun control regulations, reviewed options
available to the Council and responded to questions about the possible
consequences of those options. She explained that draft ordinance MC-9-2008
would repeal only the possession and penalty portions of the existing ordinance
and restructure the remaining provisions.
The following individuals spoke in support of retaining a ban on handguns:
         Jim Nowacki, 708 Prospect
         Marcia Adelman, 1141 Chatfield
         Brad McLane, 593 Provident Ave.
         Joyce Bishop, 722 Oak St.
         Tobi Heekin, 575 Orchard Ln.
         Jeanne Bishop, 832 Cherry St.
         Sara Kirkpatrick, 1103 Cherry St.
         Jeff Hoch, 1320 Westmoor Trail
         Brooke Bechtold, 897 Cherry St.
President Woodbury thanked the speakers for sharing their stories, and the
audience for their time and patience. He said the Trustees understand that this
is an extremely emotional issue, and asked residents to understand the
difficulty of the choice before the Council. He then called for a motion to
adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of discussing pending litigation
pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the Open Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson so
moved, seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Johnson, Behles, Rintz, Berger, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee King Poor. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:55 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Rintz. Absent: Trustee
King Poor. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
President Woodbury announced that the Council would be voting on two motions.
First, he called for a motion to introduce Ordinance No. MC-9-2008, amending
Chapter 9.12 of the Village Code. Trustee Berger so moved, seconded by Trustee
Johnson. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Berger, Greable, Johnson, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee King
Poor.
President Woodbury then called for a motion to table this Ordinance until the
November 18 th meeting, with the Village Attorney being directed to draft
further amendments to strengthen the Ordinance within the framework of current
Second Amendment law. Trustee Rintz so moved, seconded by Berger. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Rintz, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee King Poor.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2008 – Amending the Village Code to Implement Federal
Identity Theft Protection - Introduction. Noting that this item had been
discussed at the October 14 th Study Session, Attorney Janega reviewed the
draft ordinance amending the Village Code to implement policies and procedures
to detect, prevent, and mitigate the impact of identity theft to bring the
Village into compliance with the Fair and Accurate Credit Transactions (FACT)
Act of 2003 by the November 1, 2008 deadline. The ordinance also provides for
the Finance Director to develop and administer implementation procedures.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-7-2008. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2008 – Amending the Village Code to Add Net Metering
Provisions - Introduction. Attorney Janega reviewed the draft ordinance, which
would add a net metering provision to the Village Code’s electric utility
provisions. The amendment would set standards under which an electric utility
customer could install a renewable energy source and deliver excess power into
the Village’s electric distribution system, subject to protections for the grid
and other customers.
Mr. Keys noted that the Village has received one request for net metering for a
home under construction at 261 Sheridan Rd. The proposed ordinance and the
companion rate resolution (R-36-2008) implement the Council’s directive from
the August 12 th Study Session to provide a credit to a net metering customer
at the avoided costs of the Village’s wholesale purchase of power.
Manager Williams referred the Trustees to a letter from David Abell, Chairman
of the Environmental and Forestry Commission endorsing the ordinance.
President Woodbury asked about the necessity of requiring the customer to
provide $5,000,000 in insurance to protect the Village should the customer’s
equipment fail to operate correctly.
Claud Ricard, the applicant, and his attorney Richard Shopiro, maintained that
the Ordinance as written eliminates the incentive for individuals to install
net metering systems because the costs shifted to the individual exceed any
benefit from the credits. They urged the Council to reconsider its policy and
credit individuals with the retail power cost.
Manager Williams summarized the Village’s energy charges and reviewed the
evolution of the renewable energy credit calculations.
Mr. Keys pointed out that net metering adds some level of billing complexity
and that bi-directional meter costs are greater than those of a standard meter.
President Woodbury remarked that the draft ordinance appears to be in keeping
with the Study Session discussion and other organizations’ policies.
Trustee Greable agreed and commented that he is a believer in cost-based rates.
Trustee Behles added that he views using the wholesale rate as a good first
step toward implementing a net metering policy.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-8-2008. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
President Woodbury asked staff to re-examine the insurance provisions before
the matter is up for adoption.
d)   Resolution No. R-36-2008 – Amending Electric Rates to Provide for
Renewable Energy Credits – Introduction. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to introduce Resolution No. R-36-2008 amending the Village’s
electric rates to provide for the purchase of renewable energy credits. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
e)   Ordinance No. M-14-2008-2008 – Subdivision and Lot Width Variation: 596
Arbor Vitae – Introduction. As noted earlier, this item was removed from the
Agenda at the applicant’s request.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
President Woodbury invited members of the audience who wished to address the
Council on any matter not on the Agenda to do so at this time.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., raised questions about the revised Streetscape
Master Plan and the proposed Implementation Plan. In particular, he voiced
concern about the possibility of reversing the traffic flow on Gage Street and
suggested the importance of hearing from the public before adopting any such
plan. He also expressed concern about the availability of funding for the
Streetscape Plan and asked Manager Williams several questions about the status
of the Village’s cash reserves. Mr. Kates emphasized the importance of
educating the public fully about the current cost estimates for implementing
the Plan, as well as the projected costs for other capital projects competing
for Village funding.
President Woodbury commented that the Village’s process is very public and that
the intent is for the Committee to makes its recommendations to the Council and
for the Council to adopt the Master Plan so as to have guidelines in place for
developers in the future.
Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry Street, spoke in support of moving ahead with the
Streetscape project.
8)   Old Business.
a)   Zoning Regulations: Track 2 Evaluation by Winter & Company. Mr. Williams
briefly reviewed the history of this item, noting that it had been discussed at
the October 14 th Study Session. He reported Staff’s recommendation that the
Village Manager be authorized to execute a contract with Winter & Company to
amend the current agreement to increase the total project budget from $99,845
to $134,425, with an allowance for an additional $12,000 on an as-needed basis,
for a total of $146,425.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the authorization
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Rintz, Berger, Greable, and Johnson. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee King Poor.
b)   Bid No. 08-020: Winnetka Cenotaph Restoration. Manager Williams reported
Staff’s recommendation that the bid for the Cenotaph Restoration project be
awarded to Sebastian Contracting for the alternate bid for spring construction
in the amount of $323,950. He noted that it is anticipated that the project
will be completed in time for the Memorial Day activities on the Village Green
and announced that donations to the project now amounted to $56,450 .
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Rintz, Berger, Behles, and Johnson. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee King
Poor.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1577 and 1578. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1577 in the amount of $1,311,620.01 and Warrant List No. 1578 in the amount of
$1,099,193.56. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Rintz.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee King Poor.
b)   Bid 08-022: Electric Wiring, Water Plant. Manager Williams reviewed the
bids for electrical wiring work within the Water Plant, including the
replacement of receptacles, light fixtures, and low voltage electric circuits.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to award the bid for wiring
work at the Water Plant to Kordick Electric Company, in the amount of
$23,934.00, with an additional amount not to exceed $3,500 to cover additional
work beyond the specified units. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson, Behles and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee King Poor.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Behles reminded the audience about the Streetscape public
meeting scheduled for 10:00 a.m. Saturday, October 25, at Village Hall.
   ii)   Trustee Johnson reported that he had attended the recent meeting of
the Chamber of Commerce.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury announced that Trustee Johnson would be replacing Trustee
Poor as the Council’s representative to the Business Community Development
Commission. He then suggested the appointment of Trustee. Johnson as Chairman
of that Committee, effective immediately. Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to appoint Trustee Johnson. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
Next, President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Paul Dunn to the Business
Community Development Commission for a full term, effective immediately.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to appoint Mr. Dunn. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Then President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Arthur Braun to the
Business Community Development Commission for a full term, effective
immediately. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to appoint Mr.
Braun. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Finally, President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Louise Holland as the
Plan Commission’s representative to the Business Community Development
Commission, effective immediately. Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to appoint Mr. Dunn. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
None, other than the two executive sessions noted in 6a, above.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee King Poor. The meeting
adjourned at 10:55 p.m.
