November 6, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
November 6, 2008
(Approved: December 2, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
November 6, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:43 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, and King Poor. Absent:
Trustee Chris Rintz. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Fire Chief Scott Smith, and approximately 12 persons
in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
November 11, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
November 18, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
October 14, 2008, Study Session. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
October 21, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson,
moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2008 – Amending the Village Code to Implement Federal
Identity Theft Protection - Adoption. There being no further questions or
comments, President Woodbury called for a motion to adopt Ordinance MC-7-2008
amending the Village Code to implement policies and procedures to detect,
prevent, and mitigate the impact of identity theft to bring the Village into
compliance with the Fair and Accurate Credit Transactions (FACT) Act of 2003.
Trustee Greable so moved, seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Behles, Berger,
and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
President Woodbury invited members of the audience who wished to address the
Council on any matter not on the Agenda to do so at this time.
Carry Buck, 609 Sheridan Rd., commented on the results of the latest Caucus
survey and recent election, and urged the Council to respect the wishes of the
community and not revamp the Zoning Ordinance.
8)   Old Business.
a)   Bid 08-024: Ash Street Drainage Improvements. Mr. Williams reviewed the
bids for the construction and installation of a new storm sewer, pavement
removal and replacement, and all related collateral work, as discussed at the
July 15 th Council meeting. He mentioned that because this work is slated to
begin in late fall, it is possible that there may be a temporary asphalt patch
until next year. He also noted that this work would involve a portion of
Rosewood Avenue that was recently repaved.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the bid to A. Lamp
Concrete Contractors for the bid amount of $183,665.00. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Poor, Greable, and
Johnson. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1579 and 1580. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1579 in the amount of $647,086.61 and Warrant List No. 1580 in the amount of
$633,454.55. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Poor.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
b)   Park District Burn Request. Chief Smith reviewed a request from the Park
District to perform a “prairie burn” in Crow Island Park and Bell Woods. He
noted that this request was similar to one made in 2006, and that the permit
for Crow Island Woods was not granted, and the Bell Woods permit was granted,
although the work was not performed. He reported that the Fire Department is
opposed to all open burns in the Village because of environmental, medical, and
convenience issues, but emphasized that he has no concerns about the contractor
the Park District had selected.
Chief Smith then responded to several questions from the Trustees.
Joe Raimondi, 870 Bell Lane, expressed some concern for his property, which
borders the proposed burn area.
Suzanne Canfield, President of the Garden Guild of Winnetka, encouraged the
Council to permit the burn, saying that the environmental benefits, which she
enumerated, exceed the concerns.
President Woodbury asked the Park District to review its notification process,
and also asked the contractor, Jack Pizzo, to explain how the fire would be
controlled, and elaborate on other safety precautions, which they did. Mr.
Pizzo also reviewed staffing and communications procedures and responded to
questions from the Trustees.
Liza McElroy, Park District Director, explained that after evaluating the
woods, the Park District believes that this is the most efficient way to bring
out all of the potential that the sites have to offer. She said that to try to
accomplish the same end with manual labor would be impossible.
Carolyn Kurtz, Park Board President, added that the Board believes this is the
right thing to do to enhance the beauty of these parks for the benefit of all
Winnetkans.
Manager Williams pointed out that this is a one-time permit only.
Trustees Greable, Johnson, Behles, and Poor expressed support for the request.
Trustee Berger disagreed.
Based on the discussion, President Woodbury advised the Park District that they
had one-time permission to conduct a controlled burn in Crow Island and Bell
Woods, provided that appropriate insurance certificates are provided to the
Village, the procedures for the burn are completely spelled out in writing and
approved by the Fire Department, and letters advising neighbors of the burn are
hand-delivered to each household.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
i)   Trustees.
Trustee Berger reported that the Library was moving forward with a report on
the tax levy and would be conducting an all-day tour of neighboring facilities
on November 15 th . She also reported that RED Center had recently adopted a
comprehensive plan addressing staffing and planning for the next 5-8 years, and
noted Chief Smith’s significant contribution to that endeavor.
Trustee Behles reported that the Streetscape Committee had concluded its public
meetings and redrafted portions of the master plan and the implementation plan,
and would be presenting these to the Council at the upcoming Study Session.
Trustee Johnson reported on the most recent meeting of the Business Community
Development Commission.
b)   Attorney. None.
c)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the Village has thus far been very
successful in controlling costs associated with the maintenance of its website.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing   Pending or Probable Litigation, Board Appointments and Possible
Lease of Village Land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11,) 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(6) of
the Open Meetings Act.   Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz. The Council adjourned
into Executive Session at 8:55 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:58 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Poor. Absent: Trustee
Rintz. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz. The meeting adjourned at
9:59 p.m.
            
Recording Secretary
November 11, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
November 11, 2008
(Approved: December 2, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 11, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:45 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, and Chris Rintz. Absent:
Trustee King Poor. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Water & Electric Director Brian Keys, and approximately 55
persons in the audience.
2)   Streetscape.
Mr. Saunders reported that the Streetscape Committee was formed in December,
2006, and that after the Committee submitted its final report in October, 2007,
the Council referred the plan to Staff to develop implementation strategies. He
added that the Council reviewed the implementation strategies in April, 2008,
set six criteria for implementation, and directed the Streetscape Committee to
reconvene and establish an implementation program for streetscape improvements.
He reported that following several meetings, including two public input
sessions, the Committee was now submitting its revised Streetscape Master Plan
and Implementation Plan.
Trustee Behles introduced the members of the Streetscape Committee who were in
attendance and thanked them for their hard work over the last few years. He
also thanked Village staff and all the department heads for giving technical
assistance on the project, and then introduced Scott Freres, of The Lakota
Group.
Mr. Freres’ made a Power Point presentation that covered what streetscape is
and why it is needed; reviewed the design process used to formulate the plans;
described several sample streetscape plans; illustrated signage and wayfinding;
and explained the implementation strategy.
Trustee Behles explained that the revised implementation plan proposed starting
with a single project, phased over two years, in the West and East Elm and
Hubbard Woods business districts. He reviewed the cost estimates, and noted
that the plan was purely conceptual, and that details would be worked out as
the projects proceed through design and engineering. He urged the Village to
expand its vision of the business districts, and to put some money behind that
vision.
President Woodbury invited the attendees in the audience to come forward and
voice their opinions.
Julie Windsor, 1479 Tower and owner of Beat Street in Hubbard Woods, spoke in
opposition to the project, stating that establishing commercial recycling
should be the first priority, and expressing concern that the project could
result in higher taxes and rents; that features would encroach on the already
narrow sidewalks in Hubbard Woods; and that the proposed lighting is
inadequate.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, said he was against adopting the plan at this time,
citing the current economic climate, and competition for the streetscape
dollars in the form of employee pension obligations, Village Hall renovation,
the water plant upgrade, potential post office site redevelopment, and a
parking structure on Lincoln Avenue. He questioned the need for some of the
street furniture elements and voiced opposition to recommendations for
reconfiguring Gage Street, and suggested putting the issue to a referendum. He
said he would favor elements that improve safety, as well as cosmetic
improvements, if the cost is kept down, and added that the important thing is
to get shoppers into the stores, possibly by bringing in public events.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce, said the Village’s first priority was to figure out
how to make up the increase in unfunded pension liability that has resulted
from the recent turmoil in the stock markets.
David Humphrey, owner of Moth Meyer Properties, said he liked the look of the
plan but expressed concern about the proposed Spruce Street improvements which
remove one of the driveways on his property, on the corner of Green Bay and
Spruce.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, said he saw the issue in four parts: 1) does the
Council like the look and feel of the plan; 2) is there anything that can be
eliminated to reduce the cost; 3) how is it adopted and to what extent is it
locked in; and 4) where does it fit in when looked at in terms of the other
capital projects the Village is contemplating?
The remaining speakers included:
Peter & Betty Skalski, 611 Ash and owners of Phototronics
Mike Klein, 955 Sunset
Nan Greenough, 500 Maple
Peter Horne, 791 Bryant
Jim Sayegh, co-chair of the Winnetka Property Owners Assoc.
Joan Evanich, 779 Bryant
Joan Fragen, 1230 Lindenwood
Pat Horne, 791 Bryant & owner of Multiple Choices
Roberta Rubin, owner of The Book Stall
Jean Wright, 555 Sheridan and owner of Jean Wright Reality
Leslie Stevens, 142 Church
Cicely Michalak, 351 Ridge and Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce
Mike Miller, 428 Linden
Peg Swartchild, owner of Material Possessions
The speakers were in favor of proceeding with Phase 1, citing the following
benefits: it is a good marketing tool for Winnetka; it will improve property
values for all of Winnetka; the business districts are tired and need
improvements; lighting and signage need improvement, and it may be a good time
to go out to bid, as contractors are currently worried and hungry for work. The
speakers advised the Council to implement the plan as recommended by the
Streetscape Committee, and expressed concern that partial implementation would
not have the desired impact.
President Woodbury observed that competition for funds and priorities would be
addressed during the discussion of Item #5, Financial Planning for Capital
Improvements. He then asked Mr. Saunders to address the concerns about
commercial recycling, lighting levels and the reversal of Gage Street.
Mr. Saunders stated that a commercial recycling provision would be added to the
next recycling contract after the current one expires in ten months and
suggested drop-off locations and placing recycling containers next to refuse
containers in the meantime. He explained that the spacing and lamping of the
new light fixtures would provide more intense light and direct it downward. He
said the Gage Street reversal was explored primarily because of concerns about
traffic at Merrill and Green Bay Rd., and the thought was that Gage would be a
safer exit onto Green Bay because it has a traffic light, but that it needs
further study. He observed that the intent for the intersection of Spruce and
Green Bay is to maximize pedestrian safety and to make it more of a 90 degree
intersection, which could be accomplished without eliminating any driveways on
Mr. Humphrey’s property.
Trustee Behles noted that the master plan is a conceptual vision, and all of
the elements would be vetted with private property owners and details would be
worked out during the design and engineering phases.
President Woodbury then asked the Council for their thoughts concerning the
adoption of the plan by Resolution.
Trustee Greable said regarding the Gage Street portion of the plan, that he
wished to make it clear the Council would take residents’ concerns into
consideration, possibly by making an addendum to the Master Plan. He noted that
the Streetscape Committee had made a supreme effort to address all the issues
and indicated that he favored formally adopting the plan.
Trustee Berger also agreed that the Streetscape Committee had done a phenomenal
job, and gave her support to adopting the plan, adding that investment in the
Village’s business districts is long overdue. She said when the time came to
implement the recommendations, she was in favor of revisiting what could be
done in Hubbard Woods, as that particular business district has been left
behind due to the complications of working with IDOT on the Green Bay corridor.
Trustee Rintz congratulated the Streetscape Committee on doing a fantastic job,
said the plan looks to the future, noted that the downtowns are tired and need
revitalization, and that the plan points in the right direction. He indicated
that he knew from personal experience that streetscape implementation changes
how people think about their community and also how they use it, and he added
that he supports adopting the Master Plan.
Trustee Johnson thanked the Streetscape Committee for their recommendations and
the audience for their comments, and he observed that the Council should seize
the moment and look to the future, adopt the Master Plan and then discuss the
implementation aspects further when the time comes.
Trustee Behles emphasized that the Master Plan is a vision, which would change
over time and could be modified as it gets implemented, but asked the Council
to avoid the temptation to cherry-pick elements in or out at this time.
President Woodbury said Trustee Poor had emailed his comments, which gave his
support to moving forward with the first phase of the plan as a whole, with no
major deletions, in order to provide a noticeable impact to the business
districts.
President Woodbury observed that 16 years had passed since the original
streetscape program had been implemented in Winnetka, and stated that the
consensus of the Council was to move forward towards adoption by Resolution of
the Master Plan as a guide for future development. Saying that funding was key
to the Village’s ability to execute the plan, he reordered the Agenda so that
Item #5, Financial Planning – Major Capital Improvements, would be discussed
next, after which the rest of the items would be taken in their regular order.
[DRAFTER’S NOTE: This item was discussed after Item #5, after which the Agenda
resumed its regular order.]
3.   2008 Property Tax Analysis.
Finance Director Ed McKee reported that the Village remains fiscally strong,
despite the current problems with the economy, and that operating costs and
capital needs can be met with a modest property tax increase. He observed that
the Village has sufficient cash reserves and has a sound budget overall, but
the challenge remains to keep expense increases in line with revenue
projections. He recommended a modest tax levy increase for existing residents
of 3.4%, which would add approximately $90 to a typical $20,000 tax bill, with
an overall increase including new development of 4.7%.
President Woodbury confirmed that the amount was below the State’s tax caps.
Mr. McKee observed that the Village’s share of the property tax pays for most
municipal services, and that staff would be available at the next Council
Meeting to discuss the suggested increase and make whatever modifications are
called for.
4)   Winnetka Water Plant Improvements.
Mr. Keys explained several proposed changes to the Water Plant improvements
that were presented at the July Study Session. He noted that the costs for the
proposed pipe gallery were higher than originally estimated, that improvements
were added to minimize shut-downs, and that a chlorine scrubber that the
consultant had listed as an option was added. He asked the Council to consider
approving $60,000 to proceed with design and engineering for the water pipe
extension to clear well #3.
Trustee Rintz expressed concern over the number of changes and the increased
costs and noted that the late hour did not leave enough time for substantive
discussion.
President Woodbury suggested bringing the matter back at the next meeting, and
Manager Williams said the issue would be discussed in more detail at the
November 18 th Council Meeting.
[DRAFTER’S NOTE: This item was discussed after Item #2, after which the Agenda
resumed its regular order, starting with Item #3.]
5)   Financial Planning – Major Capital Improvements.
Manager Williams reviewed the following major capital improvement projects:
·   Post Office Redevelopment
·   Streetscape Projects for the West & East Elm Business Districts
·   Underground Parking Structure on Lincoln Avenue
·   Renovation of Village Hall
·   Renovation of the Water Plant
·   Three Drainage Projects
·   Fire Department Ladder Truck
Manager Williams indicated that the majority of the projects had been discussed
for several years and that no decisions had to be made immediately, as there
would be detailed discussions at the upcoming budget meetings. He noted that
the drainage projects had been re-evaluated in light of September’s 9-inch
rainstorm, and that three other significant projects had been identified. He
then recommended four core projects: 1) Renovation to Village Hall’s exterior
walls and roof, which are essential, and in the longer term, replacing the HVAC
system; 2) begin the Water Plant renovations to ensure that it functions
optimally for the long term future; 3) begin the drainage projects, starting
with the Sunset and White Oak area; 4) purchase a new ladder truck for the Fire
Department, which is critical for public safety.
Manager Williams reported that the pension funds have taken a big hit on the
equity side, resulting in a current unfunded liability across all the funds of
$14 million, and explained that pensions are looked at over the long term, and
that pension contributions could be expected to increase approximately $600,000
- $700,000 per year.
Manager Williams reported that the Village has about $20.3 million in cash
reserves, and due to the current economic uncertainty, Staff recommends keeping
between $10 and $12 million on hand, leaving approximately $9 million to spend
on capital improvements. He outlined possible options for funding Streetscape,
Village Hall renovation and the drainage projects, including Special Service
Areas and creating a stormwater utility. He added that the Post Office
redevelopment will not happen in the near future, and the Lincoln Avenue
parking garage would cost $10 million, thus wiping out all reserves.
Manager Williams went on to say that sales tax and building permit revenues
have fallen somewhat, and that Staff was therefore being conservative with
estimates. He indicated that if the Parking Deck is taken off the table and
Village Hall renovation is limited to the essentials, that left a tension
between Streetscape and the Water Plant, which could be eased with a referendum
or bond issue for either project, or a water fund revenue bond, which would
increase rates by approximately 24%. He noted that there is an equity issue
with the Village’s largest water customer, New Trier High School, and suggested
several ways to ameliorate that problem.
Trustee Greable observed that the Water Fund should be paying its own way. He
commended Staff for their excellent financial planning, and he added that the
Village is in very sound shape.
Manager Williams noted that no decisions needed to made immediately, that those
discussions would take place at budget time.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, said the analysis and approach were good, and that all
projects on the horizon would need to be kept in mind when deciding on spending
the reserves.
Mike Klein, 955 Sunset, said the drainage work needed to be done as soon as
possible, and strongly urged the Council to make the commitment.
Ann Wilder, 1096 Spruce, raised equity concerns in setting up a stormwater
utility.
Manager Williams said drainage is a Village-wide problem, and that drainage
problems can be managed but not solved, since the reality is that the southwest
portion of Winnetka is built on a swamp.
Bill Doyle, 251 White Oak Lane, asked the Council to fast-track the drainage
project, saying he felt that the situation is a crisis that calls out for
immediate attention.
President Woodbury explained that engineering had to be undertaken before any
construction could be started, and that there would be complications of public
vs. private interests when the project has to move through residents’ back
yards.
Mr. Saunders cautioned that it could take a year or more for even a fairly
uncomplicated project to move from engineering to the bidding process, and
indicated that Staff would move as quickly as possible.
Kim Hack, 1099 Sunset, said she was thrilled to hear the issue of drainage
being confronted, but that the real issue is fixing the cause of the backup as
well as the symptoms of flooding in the neighborhood.
Jim Feld, 260 White Oak, asked about discussing detention with the Park
District.
Manager Williams recalled that it was brought up years ago and the public
outcry against it was fierce.
President Woodbury commented that the issue of using Crow Island or other open
space has been brought to the attention of the Park District.
Bill Shelton, 956 Sunset, said he supports Streetscape but was primarily
concerned with drainage, and urged the Village to work to improve the drainage
system.
6)   Manager’s Report – Update on Zoning Study.
Manager Williams distributed a memo from Mike D’Onofrio, Director of Community
Development, regarding his recent visit to Colorado to brief the Village’s
consultant, Nore Winter, on the particulars of Winnetka’s Zoning Ordinance. He
observed that good progress was being made on the final report.
7)   Executive Session.
None.
8)   Adjournment.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Poor. The meeting adjourned at
11:40 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
November 18, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
November 18, 2008
(Approved: December 16, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
November 18, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor, and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of
Water & Electric Brian Keys, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Deputy Police Chief
Patrick Kreis, and approximately 30 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Woodbury announced that Item 6(d), a subdivision and lot width
variation for 596 Arbor Vitae, has been removed from the Agenda at the request
of the applicant. The Trustees approved the Agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
December 2, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
December 9, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
None
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2008 – Amending the Village Code Pertaining to Weapons
– Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that the Ordinance, as introduced on
October 21 st , would repeal the possession and penalty sections of Section
9.12 of the Village Code and restructure the remaining provisions. She briefly
reviewed the history of this matter and the lawsuits filed against Winnetka’s
hand gun ban and similar ordinances in other municipalities. She went on to
review the specific provisions of the existing Code and how they would be
affected by the proposed amendments. She then referred the Trustees to a table
in the Agenda Report summarizing Illinois state weapons laws and penalties,
noting that the absence of a hand gun ordinance in the Village does not mean
that the citizens will be without protection, because the scope of the State
laws is greater and their penalties, many of which include extensive jail
terms, exceed the fines that the Village could impose. She then noted a
proposed amendment to the introduced ordinance that would retain the provision
permitting firearms to be discharged under the supervision of the Police
Department at a parade or other civic ceremony.
President Woodbury invited Village residents in the audience to share their
comments.
Those individuals speaking against repealing the hand gun ordinance included:
Brooke Bechtold, 897 Cherry
Paul Crnkovich, 195 Ridge
Tawny Beechum, 1356 Asbury
Janice McVicker, 1 N. Indian Hill
Penny Lanphier, 250 Birch
Amanda Hanley, 1310 Scott
Jeanne Bishop, 832 Cherry
Mary Talbot, 896 Spruce
Joseph Nesler, 491 Hill Road, spoke in support of repeal.
President Woodbury closed the public discussion and called for comments from
the Trustees.
Noting that he spent many hours wrestling with this issue, Trustee Poor said he
felt compelled to support repeal of the ordinance, despite his personal belief
that the handgun ban reflects the values of the community, because the risk to
the Village is too great should the plaintiffs in the lawsuit prevail.
Trustee Behles agreed, pointing out that the Council has the responsibility to
balance community values with community prudence.
Trustees Johnson and Greable concurred.
President Woodbury reiterated that this has been a difficult decision for the
Council and noted that, although the Trustees believe that the Village is
better off because of the handgun ban, the Village’s decision is impacted by
events outside of its boundaries.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to amend Ordinance MC-9-2008,
in accordance with the revised draft as reviewed by the Village Attorney. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Then Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-
9-2008, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, Rintz, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
b)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2008 – Amending the Village Code to Add Net Metering
Provisions – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments to the
Ordinance, introduced on October 21, that would add a net metering provision to
the Village Code’s electric utility provisions, setting standards under which a
customer of the Village’s electric utility customer could install a renewable
energy source and deliver excess power into the Village’s electric distribution
system, subject to protections for the grid and other customers. She noted that
the accompanying rate resolution, Resolution No. R-36-2008, clarifies that the
reimbursement formula utilizes the Village’s budgeted purchase power cost
(i.e., the avoided cost) and amends the carry-forward provision to permit
excess credits to be carried forward from one billing period to the next, but
not past the end of the calendar year.
Mr. Keys addressed some of the technical aspects of the ordinance and commented
on questions about potential risks to the system, and Staff’s rationale for
assessing a $50 billing charge. He then responded to questions from the
Trustees.
Trustee Behles opined that providing credits at a discounted value and charging
extra fees serves as a disincentive to individuals looking to install
alternative energy sources, and noted that other Illinois utilities credit
customers on a one-to-one basis. He added that if the Village’s goal is to
encourage renewable energy, this ordinance does not do that.
Manager Williams explained that the Village has a consistent policy of setting
its rates at the cost of service and he said the proposed ordinance maintains
that principle and avoids having one class of customers subsidize another. He
suggested other ways in which the Village might provide credit for renewable
energy in a transparent manner.
Trustee Berger stated that she sees this as something new and that the
ordinance gives the Village an opportunity to try it out and observed that any
investment by an individual is made with an eye toward achieving a net gain in
the future.
Trustee Greable agreed with Trustee Berger.
David Abell, 740 Oak Street, agreed that it is in the Village’s best interests
to take some action on this issue, but opposed adoption of the ordinance as
drafted because it provides no economic incentive to a homeowner to generate
energy. He suggested (1) eliminating the $50 billing fee, and (2) permitting
the Finance Director to waive the insurance requirements upon determining that
adequate coverage is in place. He said he believes that the Village should be
willing to accept some risk and absorb some of the administrative costs
associated with renewable energy.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, agreed with Mr. Abell’s comments.
Sandy Rau, 1235 Scott Ave., and Amanda Hanley, 1310 Scott, each voiced their
opinion that there are residents who want to incorporate green energy sources
into their homes and urged the Council to make the ordinance more encouraging
for homeowners to do so.
Trustee Poor observed that net metering is a good concept and that the
Village’s considering it is an important first step in the right direction,
taken with the understanding that there is more work to be done. He added that
using the avoided cost is in keeping with past Village policy and provides an
identifiable measure, but indicated support for deleting the $50 billing fee
and insurance requirement and moving forward with something that will encourage
green electricity.
Trustee Berger indicated that she favored allowing the Finance Director to
waive the insurance requirement rather than eliminating it from the ordinance
altogether.
Trustee Rintz added that whatever insurance is required, it should be
commercially available to the homeowner. He suggested that having the Village
absorb the cost of equipment and installation expenses might prove to be an
incentive, as well as being transparent.
Village Attorney Janega then suggested wording to further amend the ordinance
to eliminate the $50 billing fee and give the Finance Director authority to
waive the insurance requirements.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to amend Ordinance MC-8-
2008, as reflected in the agenda materials, with the further amendments
suggested by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-8-
2008, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, Rintz, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
c)   Resolution No. R-36-2008 – Amending Electric Rates to Provide for
Renewable Energy Credits – Adoption. Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to amend Ordinance R-36-2008, in accordance with the revised
draft as reviewed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
36-2008 amending the Village’s electric rates to establish a net metering rate
structure to deal with costs and credits for renewable energy systems that
interconnect with the village’s electrical system. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
d)   Ordinance No. M-14-2008 – Subdivision and Lot Width Variation: 596 Arbor
Vitae – Introduction. As noted earlier, this item was removed from the Agenda
at the applicant’s request.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
President Woodbury invited members of the audience who wished to address the
Council on any matter not on the Agenda to do so at this time. There being
none, the Council moved on to the next item on the Agenda.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1581 and 1582. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1581 in the amount of $1,008,715.72 and Warrant List No. 1582 in the amount of
$479,210.57. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to review the status of the Village’s
winter salt supply.
Mr. Saunders indicated that the Village has now received its entire salt order
for the year, about 1,250 tons. He stated that the Village was able to secure
about 20% of its salt order at about $106 per ton, but that the balance of the
order was at the higher prices anticipated in September, about $155 per ton.
This means that the Village will spend roughly $180,000 on salt this year, or
about triple the anticipated budget amount. He added that the entire order has
been delivered, so the Village will not face the situation that occurred last
year when it could not get salt delivered .
b)   Private Road/Public Road Policy. Mr. Saunders briefly reviewed the
Council’s discussion of the Trapp Lane neighbors’ request at the October 14 th
Study Session, which resulted in staff being was asked to draft an updated 1994
policy. Mr. Saunders reviewed key draft provisions that would revise that
policy to address the equity concerns raised at the October meeting and provide
some structure to the process. He mentioned that, of the approximately 25
private streets in the Village, 14 are specifically identified in the draft
policy, since they serve as “de facto” public streets, meaning that they: (1)
provide an important link between two public streets, or (2) serve 10 or more
homes. He noted that the suggested procedural guidelines outlined in the draft
are patterned after the Village’s policy on constructing sidewalks, which has
been successful in advancing several sidewalk projects in recent years.
Mr. Saunders then responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Rintz inquired about funding methods, asking whether residents could be
given a choice between a special assessment or a one-time payment.
Mr. Saunders said this was probably not feasible because of the complexities of
the special assessment process, which covers a defined by area.
Trustee Behles remarked that working these projects into the capital plan will
be very challenging, because of the many variables and the potential for costs
to be much greater than those associated with a sidewalk. He expressed concern
about setting an expectation that the Village will do the work if the criteria
are met.
Speaking on behalf of the Trapp Lane neighbors, Ernie Weis, President of the
Trapp Lane Neighborhood Association, reviewed the neighborhood’s reasons for
making this request. He noted that they have been trying to come to an
agreement for many years and have finally reached the point where every current
resident is in favor of the project, although there remains some discussion
about how best to finance the residents’ portion.
In response to a question from Manager Williams, Mr. Weis indicated that all of
the residents were comfortable with the rough draft policy.
Trustee Berger commented that the issues advanced by the Trapp Lane homeowners
apply equally to all private streets. She expressed concern that the draft
policy sets up a situation where private roads that are not adequately
maintained by homeowners will end being the responsibility of the Village,
while well maintained private roads will receive no benefit from the Village.
She added that the policy places a lot of responsibility on the sitting Council
to decide whether or not to undertake any particular project, resulting in the
possibility that some requests might be granted while others will not,
depending upon the availability of funds at the time of the request. She
suggested that, given the significant cost associated with addressing all of
the private roads, budgeting for the total estimated cost should begin so that
all private roads can be treated equally and that this is as a more global
issue that needs to be addressed before adopting the draft policy.
Trustee Rintz concurred with Trustee. Berger, noting that by the time a request
gets to the Council, the expectation that the Village will ante up is already
there. He said if the Council commits to cost sharing, then doing so should not
be based on other budgetary considerations. He agreed that there are equity
issues since some private roads have already expended substantial amounts on
their own to improve their roads. He suggested that the Village explore means
of recapturing its portion of the cost sharing.
Trustee Behles endorsed the concept of adopting a policy, but said he is
struggling with its structure. He cautioned against creating an incentive for
private roads to move to the public side and adding a new financial burden on
the Village. He said although there’s benefit to the Village in terms of
operation in taking over the private streets, given the magnitude of the
associated costs and unpredictability of timing and extent of the requests,
budgeting becomes problematic. He added that he was uncertain that a 50/50
split was appropriate.
Trustees Rintz and Poor agreed, and Trustee Berger suggested reaching out to
the other private roads before adopting any policy.
Hearing a consensus to tentatively move forward with some sort of change in the
current policy, President Woodbury asked staff to put together a proposal for
further consideration that would incorporate a strategy for recapturing Village
costs and a methodology for handling the potential expenses in five-year
increments.
c)   Semi-annual Capital Report. Manager Williams reviewed the status of
capital projects for items budgeted at $50,000 or more.
d)   Purchase of Replacement Mobile Computers. Chief DeLopez introduced Sgt.
Rich Carlson, who explained that the existing equipment is outdated and will
not be adequate to run the new CAD system software that allows officers in the
field to receive and review calls in their squad cars.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the purchase of
six (6) mobile computers for an amount not to exceed $26,850. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Greable,
Johnson, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees. Trustee Berger reported on the recent Library Board meeting and
thanked President Woodbury for showing his support by joining in the Library’s
bus tour of other area libraries.
Trustee Poor reported that the Environmental and Forestry Commission had heard
a presentation on the Village’s current green initiatives.
Trustee Greable reported that he had attended a recent NWMC meeting at which
the speaker addressed financial planning for tough financial times and added
that he would be meeting with the Legislative Committee to work on key issues.
He also said he would be meeting with the Executive Director of the Winnetka
Historical Society to get an update on their activities.
Trustee Behles reported that Metra would be presenting its latest version of
station and platform improvements for the Elm Street station at the upcoming
meeting of the Design Review Board, with an eye toward beginning construction
in the spring.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury called for the appointment of Carl Lane to the Zoning Board
of Appeals for a full term. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to
approve the appointment of Mr. Lane. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Behles moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing   Pending or Probable Litigation, Board Appointments and Possible
Lease of Village Land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11,) 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(6) of
the Open Meetings Act.   Trustee Berger seconded the motion. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into
Executive Session at 10:24 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:15 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
11:16 p.m.
            
Recording Secretary
December 2, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
December 2, 2008
(Approved: January 6, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 2, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor, and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Police Chief Joe
DeLopez, and approximately 18 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the Agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
   a)   December 9, 2008, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   December 16, 2008, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   a)   November 6, 2008, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
   b)   November 11, 2008, Study Session. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution No. R-37-2008 - Winnetka Commercial Districts Master
Streetscape and Wayfinding Plan and Village of Winnetka Commercial Districts
Streetscape and Wayfinding Implementation Plan - Adoption. President Woodbury
referred the Trustees to a memorandum prepared by the Village Attorney in
response to a suggestion from him that a reference to the Design Guidelines be
included in the Streetscape Resolution. He commented that the additional
recital would officially record that the Village's commercial Design Guidelines
are one of the precursors to the Streetscape and Wayfinding Master Plan and
proposed adding the recital as drafted by Ms. Janega. Mr. Woodbury then invited
members of the audience to speak.
Bob Horne, 340 Birch Street, a former member of the Business Community
Development Commission and the Plan Commission, urged the Trustees to vote in
favor of the Plan, saying that streetscape supports property values and is an
integral part of bringing vitality to the community.
Commercial property owners Jim Sayegh, Josh Braun and Arthur Braun also spoke
in support of the Plan.
Other audience members who spoke in support of adopting the Resolution included
Cicely Michalak, 351 Ridge, Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce and a
member of the Business Community Development Commission, Robert Dini, 1056 Gage
St., Michael Klein, 955 Sunset Road. Ms. Michalak pointed out that the
Streetscape Committee has been holding open public meetings for the last three
years, there have been Village-wide mailings regarding the project, and it has
been featured in articles in the Winnetka Report.
Robert Mucci, 1040 Tower Rd., and Steve Miller, 603 Provident, members of the
Caucus Platform Committee, advocated withholding action on the Plan to allow
residents an opportunity to express their opinions via the annual Caucus
survey, citing the long-standing practice of using the Caucus platform process
to ascertain residents' opinions on significant Village issues. Mr. Mucci
voiced his belief that the majority of the Village's taxpayers are not fully
informed on the issue, are unaware of the magnitude of the project's costs, and
have not been polled about whether it is an appropriate project for the Village
to undertake at this time.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, also objected to the adoption of the Resolution, saying
that the Plan has not been thoroughly vetted by the community and that it has
numerous components that may or may not be deemed necessary and desirable. He
noted the struggling economy, limited resources, and the need to prioritize the
Village's outstanding capital projects, and he strongly urged the Council not
to approve the plan until after the issue is presented in the Caucus
questionnaire and other options have been thoroughly explored. Mr. Kates also
related information about the Village of Glencoe's relatively recent
streetscape improvements.
Julie Windsor, 1479 Tower Road, spoke against adopting the Plan as well, saying
that not enough is allocated to Hubbard Woods and that, in her opinion,
streetscape will do little to bring money back to the shops.
President Woodbury thanked everyone for participating in the process and went
on to respond to some of the comments. He noted the thoroughness of the
Streetscape study process, adding that the Village went to extraordinary
lengths to solicit input from residents and businesses. He explained that the
Resolution explicitly reserves the Council's right to allocate or expend funds
to implement the Plan and to determine the timing of any implementation.
Trustee Behles explained that the Streetscape Master Plan is a long-range
vision and is the kind of long-range planning that residents should expect from
the Council, just as they expect for streets, sewers, bridges, and water mains.
He added that streetscape is a legitimate infrastructure that has been ignored
for many years, and pointed out that the implementation is worth $5.5 million
spread over 3-1/2 years. Mr. Behles also addressed objections based on claims
of inadequate public awareness.
Trustee Berger agreed, stressing the importance of understanding that the Plan
is a guide and that there will be further opportunity to comment as the Village
moves forward. She reiterated her hope that more of the total expense will be
allocated to Hubbard Woods.
Trustee Poor also concurred, noting the great effort made to publicize this
effort of three years and expressing satisfaction that it has been given wide
publicity. He added that the Trustees have considered this matter carefully and
determined that the Village can afford to undertake this project at this time,
noting that it has been in line for a long time and is important to the entire
Village, not just the merchants.
Trustee Rintz agreed, saying the streetscape has a qualitative aspect to it
that can't be put down in numbers and that you can't put a price tag on public
pride. He observed that the Plan speaks to the quality of the overall downtown
experience and voiced his opinion that the downtown is in need of a facelift.
Trustees Johnson and Greable were in accord with the other Trustees. Trustee
Johnson noted that the money will be spent over time and that there are
stopping places along the way and safeguards built in to the process. Trustee
Greable added that the documents give credence to the idea that the Council is
concerned about the Village's streetscape in its totality, noting the Plans
will be living documents and will be subject to review by future Councils as
the project moves forward.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to include the additional recital
drafted by the Village Attorney.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Resolution R-37-2008,
including the new recital. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Rintz, Berger, Poor, Greable, and Johnson. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   b)   2008 Tax Levy & Tax Abatement
      i)   Ordinance No. M-15-2008 - 2008 Tax Levy Ordinance - Adoption.
Manager Williams provided a historical perspective, noting that one of the key
commitments to residents when the Village elected to become a home rule unit
was to make every effort to keep taxes low and below tax caps, which the
Village has been able to do thus far. He then asked Mr. McKee to review the
agenda material.
Mr. McKee explained that the Village doesn't take money from residents unless
it needs to do so, but pointed out that property taxes constitute the largest
and most stable revenue source for the Village's General Fund, which pays for
most of the traditional municipal services. He enumerated some of the items
considered when setting the tax levy, including the strength of the budget,
cash balances, projected capital needs, and the status of the pension funds. He
emphasized Mr. Williams' comment that the Village continues to stay below the
levy allowed by the tax cap law for non-home rule municipalities while meeting
the goal of keeping property taxes low over the long term without compromising
the ability to complete capital projects on a pay-as-you-go basis. He then
provided a tax rate analysis and reviewed some of the worksheets used in
preparing the proposed levy. Summarizing, Mr. McKee stated that this year's tax
levy ordinance would increase the total tax levy for the Village by a total of
4.7% for 2008, but that existing homeowners would see an increase of 3.4%, with
new development contributing the remaining 1.3%. Mr. McKee concluded by
responding to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-15-2008. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
      ii)   Ordinance No. M-16-2008 - 2008 Tax Abatement Ordinance - Adoption.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M
16-2008 abating the tax levy for the Village's Series 2003 Bonds which were
issued for the Public Safety Building. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., objected to Village committees holding 7:30 a.m.
meetings on the grounds that there are many residents who might like to attend
but cannot due to their work and commuting schedules. President Woodbury
indicated that the Village would keep that in mind for the future.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Warrant Lists 1583 and 1584. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1583 in the amount of $648,210.71 and Warrant List No. 1584 in the
amount of $268,460.35. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Purchase of Police 2009 Ford Expedition. Chief DeLopez reviewed a
request to replace an existing Ford Expedition that has reached the end of its
life cycle. In response to a request from Manager Williams, he explained that
the Department cycles vehicles out of service when they reach 75,000 miles and
generally replaces like vehicle for like vehicle. He reported that the
Expedition has proved to be a valuable vehicle for the Department, as it is
especially effective for a variety of purposes.
Manager Williams remarked that the useful life of vehicles has been extended
from 65,000 miles to 75,000 miles without a problem and added that the Village
would check with other departments before auctioning the old vehicle, to see if
they could use it.
Chief DeLopez added that Sutton Ford is presently holding an SSV that meets all
of the Village's specifications, that it is the only one of its kind available
in the area and that that the money for the vehicle has already been budgeted.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the purchase of a
Ford Expedition Special Service Vehicle 4x4 through the NWMC/Suburban
Purchasing Cooperative Bid Award program from Sutton Ford in the amount of
$26,334. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, Rintz, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Bid 08-025: Replacement of 1994 Bucket Truck. Mr. Keys reviewed the
bids to replace an existing bucket truck that is used to perform maintenance
and repair activities on the Village's electric distribution system. He
reported that of the six bids received, only two met all of the Village's
specifications, the lower of which came from Dueco, Inc.
Trustee Greable asked whether the same vehicle might be purchased through a
cooperative program, in the same way as the Police SSV discussed previously.
Manager Williams responded that he would confirm that staff has fully
investigated that possibility before he signs the purchase order.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the purchase of a
new bucket truck from Dueco, Inc., the lowest bidder that met all
specifications, in the amount of $146,055. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Greable, Johnson, Behles, and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Winnetka Water Plant Improvements. Manager Williams explained that
although there is no crisis, the Water Plant is in need of significant
investment as there have been no improvements made to this Village
infrastructure in a very long time. He said he believes this to be the next
major project that the Village needs to address in order to ensure that the
Village has a top-notch facility for the next 30 years. He went on to review
the history of this issue, noting that earlier this year the Council awarded a
contract to Camp Dresser & McKee Inc., for a long term improvement plan study,
including an evaluation of the feasibility of membrane filtration. The
consultant's recommendations were presented during the summer, and it was
determined that the water plant would continue to utilize conventional
filtration technology. Part of the CDM plan identified other improvements that
are currently needed or may be necessary in the future. Since that time, staff
has identified additional improvements as optional and/or potentially required
by future regulations and has developed a plan for the next few years. He
emphasized that while there is a logical sequencing to the projects of any
single year, each year's plan stands on its own.
Manager Williams referred the Trustees to a document distributed this evening
that reconciles staff's updated plan with the original CDM report. He
emphasized that the Trustees need not decide on staff's water improvement plan
this evening as there will be an opportunity to review it again during the
upcoming budget discussions and that there is plenty of time to discuss funding
alternatives. At this point, staff is only requesting permission to move
forward with obtaining engineering services for the first of the improvements
so that the Village will be in the position to begin to make modifications
during the September - December 2009 period.
Mr. Williams then asked Mr. Keys to review his Agenda Report and walk the
Council through the reconciliation document, which he did. Afterwards, he
responded to questions from the Trustees.
After a brief discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees to advance
$60,000 of capital expenditures to begin the engineering work, permit
applications, and bid document preparation associated with the water pipe
extension into Clear Well #3
   e)   Village Council Meeting Schedule for 2009. President Woodbury called
for a motion to approve the 2009 Meeting Schedule, as proposed. Trustee Berger
so moved, seconded by Trustee Poor. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. None.
   b)   Trustees
      i)   Trustee Berger reported on the recent RED Center meeting, saying
they are moving forward with their Comprehensive Plan and will be hiring an
assistant to the director in the near future. She also reported on the recent
presentation 8th graders from Washburne School made to representatives of local
governmental units regarding their vision for redeveloping the Post Office
site, noting that the students were equally divided between open space and
mixed use in their opinions of the site's best use.
      ii)   Trustee Behles reported that METRA had appeared before the Design
Review Board regarding their upcoming rehabilitation of the Elm Street station
and that, generally speaking, their presentation was well received. He added
that the DRB's comments will be coming to the Council for decisions on options
as to material modifications and upgrades. He indicated that they appear to be
on track to begin construction in 2009.
   c)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Board Appointments and Possible Lease of Village Land, pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(6) of the Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:22 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:50 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:51 p.m.
            
   Recording Secretary
December 9, 2008
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
December 9, 2008
(Approved: December 16, 2008)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 9, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Community Development
Director Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus,
and approximately 25 persons in the audience.
2)   Zoning Ordinance Analysis and Options to Encourage Renovation of Existing
Homes.
President Woodbury noted that the purpose of tonight’s zoning discussion was to
review a preliminary report from the Village’s consultant, ask questions,
welcome comments from the public, and then further consider the issue of bulk
and mass as it pertains to the Village’s zoning regulations. He indicated that
no conclusions would be drawn at this juncture, and that the matter would be
scheduled for further discussion at a future meeting.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio reviewed the history of the
residential zoning analysis project, which was begun after some Village
residents expressed concern about bulk and mass regulations. He noted that this
year’s Winnetka Caucus Questionnaire raised a question about the Village’s
policy with regard to renovation of existing homes, but that the focus for the
evening would be on the zoning analysis. He then introduced the Village’s
consultant, Mr. Nore Winter, of Winter & Company, who had prepared a
comprehensive review of the effects of the Village’s Zoning Ordinance with
regard to perceived mass and scale.
Mr. Winter gave a presentation summarizing the findings of his zoning review,
which was developed by gathering comments from surveys and workshops,
interviewing Staff, and doing computer modeling of the effects of the current
Zoning Ordinance. He illustrated how the relationship of mass and scale varies
depending on zoning district, noting that the R-1 and R-2 Zoning Districts do
not experience the perceived mass and scale issues that are common in the R-
4 and R-5 Zoning Districts.
Mr. Winter reviewed the computer models that were developed to show how the
existing exemptions and incentives affect new construction; reviewed computer
models of alternative tools that could be considered; and summarized trends in
new building construction.
The Trustees discussed several technical aspects of the report with Mr. Winter,
who answered questions and elaborated on some of his recommendations. Mr.
Norkus and Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that they saw a need to recalibrate some of
the current incentives to avoid unintended consequences like repetitive
architectural styles, but added that other incentives are working well.
President Woodbury called for comments from the audience.
Mike Klein, of 955 Sunset and New Trier Partners, said good architecture
requires flexibility, that too many rules prevent good design in the attempt to
maximize square footage, and suggested some modeling from the inside out,
rather than from the outside in, as presented in the Winter & Co. report. He
also suggested better landscaping requirements, as well as encouraging window
wells to allow lower level development, and voiced the opinion that the issue
is more about design than mass and scale.
Carry Buck, 609 Sheridan Road, said she thought the discussion was supposed to
be about zoning and not design, and suggested that the Council retain the
current regulations, since the Caucus Questionnaire did not indicate a clear
consensus in the community about zoning restrictions.
Gary Frank, 1092 Spruce, complimented the analysis, commented that the proposed
options were appealing, and opined that the Zoning Ordinance is now very
complicated and should be simplified, as giving architects more latitude would
result in better designs.
Jean Brainard, 910 Willow, cautioned that in today’s real estate market, the
Village shouldn’t do anything to discourage people from building in
’’’’Winnetka’’, and indicated that she thought the Council was doing a good
job.
Steve Eisen, of Heritage Builders and the Coalition for Property Rights, said
more regulations and restrictions will reduce property rights and values,
especially in the current economic climate. He advised the Council to consider
how Winnetka’s standards compare with other nearby towns, and noted that the
Caucus Survey offered no mandate for zoning changes.
President Woodbury observed that residents regularly give feedback to the
Village on the issue of redevelopment and that the current discussion stems
from those conversations. He said this is a complex issue, that there is no
rush to make a decision, and that the end result could be that there is no
change. He indicated that the Council is mindful of the current economic
situation and changes in the housing market in recent months, and he added that
the issue may be up for more discussions as early as January.
3)   House Bill 5088 – Public Safety Pension Reform Bill.
Finance Director Ed McKee reviewed the requirements of the recent public safety
pension reform law and explained that the Village has begun preparing reports
as required by the new law, adding that the pensions would be discussed at
length at the upcoming budget meetings.
After a brief discussion, President Woodbury suggested possibly scheduling a
Council Study Session to meet with the pension boards, to increase transparency
and inform the Council.
Trustee Berger suggested holding a public pension forum to communicate to the
public and to shine a light on the excellent work the pension boards have done,
adding that they are very talented individuals and their actuary is one of the
best.
4)   Review Planned Development Ordinance.
Attorney Janega reported that the first planned development under the Village’s
Planned Development Ordinance, an application by New Trier Partners at Elm and
Lincoln, will be on the Council’s December 16 th agenda. She and Mr. D’Onofrio
gave a PowerPoint presentation, to provide the Council with an overview of
basic land use concepts, the planned development ordinance, and the procedural
and substantive framework for its consideration of the upcoming application.
5)   Possible Additional Council Meeting Dates.
Manager Williams reviewed tentative additional meeting dates for the 2009-2010
budget reviews and for possible additional study sessions for other significant
policy issues.
6)   Executive Session.
None.
7)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
34 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
December 16, 2008
DRAFT MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
December 16, 2008
(Approved: January 20, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 16, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Behles announced that President Woodbury would be delayed and
called the meeting to order at 7:41 p.m. Present: Trustees Sandra Berger, Gene
Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris Rintz. Absent: President Woodbury.
Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega,
Community Development Director Mike D'Onofrio, Deputy Police Chief Patrick
Kreis, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and
approximately 30 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Behles led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   January 6, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   January 13, 2009 Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   November 18, 2008 Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   December 9, 2008, Study Session. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   2008 Tax Levy & Tax Abatement.
   i)   Ordinance No. M-15-2008 - Tax Levy Ordinance - Adoption. There being no
substantive questions or comments, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Berger,
moved to adopt ordinance M-15-2008, increasing the Village's property tax levy
for 2008 by a total of 4.7%. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   ii)   Ordinance No. M-16-2008 - Tax Abatement Ordinance - Adoption. There
being no further questions or comments, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Rintz, moved to adopt ordinance M-16-2008, abating the taxes levied to pay the
principal and interest on the General Obligation Refunding Bonds, Series 2003.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2008 - Zoning Variation: 1345 Tower Road -
Introduction/ Adoption. Mr. D'Onofrio reviewed a request for side yard setback
and total side yard setback variations to allow construction of pitched roofs
to replace existing flat roofs over the study and porte-cochere of the
residence. He explained that the current roofs, which are legally
nonconforming, do not shed water and snow effectively, and he added that the
new construction will not enlarge the encroachment into the side yard setbacks.
He reported that in all other respects the construction complies with the
Zoning Ordinance, and the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6-0 to recommend in
favor of the variation.
Trustee Berger confirmed with Mr. D'Onofrio that the matter was before the
Council because of the percentage of the variation, and the Trustees discussed
ways in which a straightforward case such as the one before them could be
decided by the ZBA or Zoning Administrator. The decision was made to waive
introduction of the ordinance in order to eliminate some of the time the
homeowner would have to wait before making the improvements.
Trustee Poor, seconded by trustee Berger, moved to waive introduction of the
ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance M-17-
2008. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2009 - Opt Out of Historic Preservation Tax Assessment
Freeze - Introduction. Attorney Janega reported that the Village's Landmark
Preservation Ordinance allows property owners to apply for designation of their
property as a certified Village landmark, and that under Illinois property tax
laws, certain of these properties may become eligible for an eight-year
property tax assessment freeze. She enumerated the circumstances under which a
tax assessment freeze can become available, and explained how the process
works.
Attorney Janega indicated that in last November's general election, an advisory
referendum to opt out of the property tax assessment freeze program was passed
by a majority of Winnetka voters. She explained that the Illinois Assessment
Freeze Law contains a procedure by which a taxing body can opt out of the tax
assessment freeze program, but it requires action within the first 30 days of
the calendar year, and the opt-out provision is valid only for the year in
which it is passed; therefore, a new ordinance would have to be adopted
annually.
Attorney Janega confirmed that the subject ordinance would only apply to
Winnetka's portion of the property tax, and would not affect properties that
already qualify for the freeze. She added that Staff has not calculated the
amount of tax money that has been frozen, but only ten properties have
qualified for it. She indicated that the rationale for the property tax
assessment freeze is to provide an incentive for people to renovate or repair
an historically significant owner-occupied residence.
Kathy Almond, 1225 Hill Rd., provided a handout listing the ten properties in
Winnetka that have qualified for the property tax assessment freeze and
estimating the extra amount that an average taxpayer would have paid as a
result of the assessment freeze. She questioned why Winnetka's taxpayers should
have to pay more in property taxes to offset the properties that qualify for
the freeze.
Trustee Berger pointed out that the Village's portion is only 13% of that
total, which is an extremely small amount. Trustee Behles agreed, and noted
that the Village does not have the authority to abate the taxes of Winnetka's
other taxing bodies.
Nan Greenough, 550 Maple, observed that the Village may find submitting the
opt-out paperwork every year to be more trouble than it's worth when weighed
against the money being saved. She noted that the process has saved some
important homes, and after the freeze period ends the homes are worth more, so
taxes to the Village actually increase. She asserted that the recently passed
referendum was misleading and confusing and that she did not believe it
reflected the will of the majority of Winnetka residents.
Carry Buck, 609 Sheridan, said 61% of residents voted for the opt-out provision
of the property tax assessment freeze and asked the Council to vote with the
residents.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak and Chair of the Landmark Preservation Commission, said
a vote to opt out of the historic preservation property tax assessment freeze
is a vote to abandon the efforts of the LPC. She asked how the Council could
consider opting out without knowing the impact on the Village and taxpayers,
and she added that since no information was put out by the Village prior to the
election, voters only saw one side of the referendum question.
Joni Johnson, 888 Tower, strongly encouraged the Council not to opt out of the
property tax assessment freeze program, because it saves houses, benefits
overall property values and preserves the architectural heritage of the
Village. She said the few dollars it costs per household is more than
outweighed by the huge symbolic significance for the Village, and asked the
Council to continue to honor the ten households who have successfully met the
state's standards to participate in the property tax assessment freeze program.
Trustee Berger agreed that the referendum question was not clearly worded, and
suggested a public hearing be held to help the Council make an informed
decision on the opt-out question.
Manager Williams indicated that the issue was put on the agenda because of the
State's January deadline for adopting an opt-out provision, and the Council's
January meetings are already booked with other items.
During the ensuing discussion, the Trustees agreed that they were in no
position to make an informed decision, because information from the Finance
Department was necessary before proceeding, and a public hearing needed to be
held to give the public a chance to comment. They also decided that meeting the
January 2009 deadline was not crucial. President Pro Tem Behles suggested that
the discussion take place after the Council reviews the upcoming report from
the ad hoc Historic and Residential Preservation Committee.
* * * * * *
President Woodbury arrived at 8:50 p.m.
* * * * * * *
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
a)   Stormwater Drainage Engineering Study. Mr. Saunders reported that after
severe storms last September caused severe flooding in several areas of the
Village, the Council directed Staff to begin planning drainage improvements in
the areas most affected by the deluge. He reviewed the process used to choose a
qualified engineering firm to undertake the first step of the project, which
includes hydrologic and hydraulic modeling and analyses of the drainage basins
affected by the flooding, and of the systems draining the water away from the
neighborhoods.
Mr. Saunders reported that staff has evaluated all of the proposals received,
and recommends entering into a contract with Christopher B. Burke Engineering
Ltd., to perform a Stormwater Management Study for the western Winnetka
drainage area, for a fee not to exceed $64,085.
Manager Williams indicated that there would be a public presentation of the
study's findings and the preliminary plans.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Rintz moved to
approve the contract as recommended with Christopher B. Burke Engineering, for
a fee not to exceed $64,085. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1585 and 1586. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1585 in the amount of $1,051,727.01 and Warrant List No. 1586 in the amount of
$457,336.84. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Upgrade and Modification of Police Firing Range Bullet Trap. Deputy Chief
Kreis reported that the Police Department's firing range has not been
significantly updated since its initial construction 40 years ago. He indicated
that funds have been budgeted for replacing the bullet trap to improve rifle
training, enhance safety and reduce annual maintenance costs for the range. He
reviewed the lowest bid received, which was from the firm that currently
maintains the range targeting system in a satisfactory manner, and asked the
Council to approve the expenditure.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to approve expenditure of
$54,840 to Meggitt Training Systems, Inc., in order to replace the Police
firing range bullet trap and perform additional minor improvements. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Fell Site Planned Development, Consideration of Preliminary Draft.
President Woodbury explained that this is the first Planned Development
application to be considered by the Council and, to ensure a complete and
thorough process, this will be the first of three meetings to consider the
application. He indicated that the Council has copies of the full record from
the Plan Commission, Zoning Board of Appeals and Design Review Board, as well
as all correspondence received, and he noted that the record is at the core of
the Council's consideration. He said Village staff would give a short
introduction, followed by a presentation by New Trier Partners describing the
proposal, and finally, the attorney representing the neighbors who have filed
written protests would explain their views.
Mr. D'Onofrio reported that New Trier Partners, LLC, ("NTP") the owners of 511
Lincoln, 718-732 Elm and 513-515 Lincoln, have applied for a Planned
Development for the site, known as the Fell Site Planned Development. He gave a
background of the application process to-date, and described NTP's proposed
mixed use building. He indicated that zoning exceptions have been requested for
height and intensity of use, and noted that the Village's Planned Development
Ordinance provides for exceptions from zoning regulations. He noted that the
plan had been recommended by the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Design Review
Board, but recommendation had been denied by the Plan Commission.
Mr. Steve Elrod, attorney for NTP, said he thought that the proposed
development was a better product as a result of going through Winnetka's zoning
process and from working with neighbors and staff to improve the plan. He
indicated that as a result of the vetting process with the ZBA, Plan Commission
and DRB, New Trier Partners has volunteered to set aside units for affordable
housing, achieve LEED certification for the new building, contribute to a new 8
to10-inch water main in the Elm Street right-of-way, implement the Streetscape
plan along Lincoln and Elm, create and post wayfinding signs for the Elm Street
Business District, and add three new on-street parking spaces on Elm Street.
Mr. Elrod observed that the Plan Commission's concern regarding height was not
substantial, as it only related to the Elm Street portion of the proposed
building. He added that the Plan Commission supported a mixed use development,
and that many residents are also supportive of the plan.
Mr. John Tulty, OKW Architects, described the plan modifications generated by
comments from Village boards, neighbors and residents. He commented that the
proposed development is viable and appropriate for the community, provides
continuity of residency for empty nesters and sets the bar for a level of
quality to be followed by other redevelopment projects in Winnetka.
Mr. Mike Klein, NTP's managing partner, said he believed Winnetka's downtown
should look as good or better than most downtowns and that it does not
currently reflect the Village's demographics. He noted that East Elm has been
described as "tired" and observed that the proposed new development would
provide a fresh and vibrant retail environment, act as a catalyst for other
development and would increase the Village's real estate and retail sales tax
bases. He indicated that some objections had been withdrawn after modifications
were made, and compared the height of the proposed plan to the Winnetka Mews
and other 4-story buildings in Winnetka.
During the ensuing discussion about the proposal, Mr. Klein used a scale model
to show the Council various aspects of the proposed development from all sides.
President Woodbury announced that the Council had received written protests
from a number of residents located within 250 feet of the Fell property. He
explained that as a result, the plan would now have to be passed by a super
majority of the Council, and gave the timeframe for the applicant to file any
objections to the protests.
Mr. Frank Petrek, 711 Oak and attorney for the protestors, said the protestors
feel the proposed development is too high, too large, has too much intensity of
use and the mass is out of scale with the character of the neighborhood, and
cited several objections based on the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan.
President Woodbury encouraged the applicant to look at ways to treat the east
elevation to make it look more pleasing to the eye, and announced that the next
discussion of the Fell Redevelopment would take place at the regular Study
Session on January 13, followed by a Special Study Session on January 27, both
at 7:30 p.m. in the Council Chambers.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None
b)   Trustees. Trustee Poor reported that the Environmental and Forestry
Commission heard a report from Mary Allen of SWANCC at its last meeting, which
was very informative and contained ideas for enhancing the Village's recycling
efforts.
c)   Attorney. None
d)   Manager. None
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Jan Bawden to the Plan
Commission for a full term, effective immediately. Trustee Greable, seconded by
Trustee Johnson, moved to appoint Ms. Bawden. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Woodbury then suggested the appointment of Janet Shen as the Design
Review Board's representative to the Environmental & Forestry Commission,
effective immediately. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to
appoint Ms. Shen. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
58 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
