Recording Secretary
January 13, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
January 13, 2009
(Approved: February 3, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 13, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Behles announced that President Woodbury would be delayed and
called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present: Trustees Sandra Berger, Gene
Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris Rintz. Absent: None. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community
Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately 45 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Old Business..
a)   Fell Site Redevelopment, Consideration of Preliminary Approval. Mr.
D’Onofrio reported that some additional information had been provided for the
Council, including new correspondence and renderings of the revised plans. He
reviewed the changes that had been made to the proposal, which included a
reduction in the size of the fourth floor along Elm Street, and an improved
east facade.
Mike Klein, New Trier Partners, said he had made the modifications in response
to comments received at the last meeting, and that architect Mike Fitzgerald
would present the modifications.
Mike Fitzgerald, OKW Architects, gave a presentation of the changes, which
included:
∙   Pulling back the fourth story to give the building “shoulders” and to
reduce visibility from the street
∙   Eliminating some balconies on the south elevation, and moving others, so as
to further increase the side yard setback
∙   Improving the architecture of the east elevation
∙   Reducing the glass on the gables on Elm Street
∙   Adding architectural detail to the wall behind Conney’s Pharmacy
∙   Reducing overall bulk by approximately 1,000 square feet
After a brief Council discussion about the modifications, President Woodbury
invited Frank Petrek, the attorney for the objectors, to make his statement.
Frank Petrek, 711 Oak and attorney for the objectors, stated that the neighbors
are opposed to the height and intensity of the proposed building, and that even
though the architecture is thoughtful and good, the building is still out of
proportion with the neighborhood. He indicated that notwithstanding the
modifications to the fourth story, the position of the objecting neighbors has
not changed.
Audience members who commented that they were in favor of the proposed project
included:
∙   David Cooper, 939 Ash
∙   Bill Silverstein, Winnetka commercial property owner
∙   Ted Widen, 389 Ridge
∙   Kathy Roeser, 787 Sunset
∙   Dan Gill, 116 Church
∙   Randy Baumgarten, 261 Linden
∙   Margaret Lamason, 962 Pine
∙   Ian Larkin, 988 Oak
∙   Corey Minturn, 1427 Asbury
∙   Marcario Lullo, 1010 Oak
∙   Mark Dziersk, 680 Elder
∙   Charlie Thompson, 808 sunset
∙   John Palmer, 650 Blackthorn
∙   Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood
∙   Jim Sayegh, owner of Winnetka Galleria
∙   Doug Kiersey, 475 Elder Lane
∙   Tor Solberg, 672 Maple
Attendees who gave comment opposing the proposal were:
∙   Edith Howell, 277 Ridge
∙   Anne Wilder and Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce
∙   Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka
∙   Susie Hilbrant, 160 Westview
∙   Bob Dearborn, 585 Arbor Vitae
President Woodbury observed that the goal of the Planned Development Ordinance
is to yield the best design for the particular location of the project, while
the job of the Council is to balance the competing interests of neighbors. He
noted that the project would have an impact on the vitality of the East Elm
business district, even though there is a debate about the functional
obsolescence of the current properties on the Elm Street side. He added that
the Council would consider the application in light of what is in the best
interest for the Village at this specific location, and that concerns about
precedent are not applicable, as there are things that are appropriate for East
Elm that aren’t appropriate elsewhere in Winnetka.
Trustee Behles complimented New Trier Partners on their efforts to refine the
plan, and said the obvious solution to the height and bulk issues would be to
remove the fourth floor altogether. He observed that the site slopes four feet
from Lincoln to Elm on the east, but while the floors step down the top of the
building does not, which adds to the bulk. He said further improvements are
needed at the corner of Lincoln and Elm facing Phototronics, and he noted that
any release from height and intensity of use regulations would have to meet the
standards in the Planned Development Ordinance.
Trustee Rintz indicated that the architectural changes on the east elevation
were a huge improvement, as were the “shoulders” added to the building, but
that he was still struggling with the overall mass. He suggested that an
updated model of the new plan be created to help with visualizing the most
recent modifications.
Trustee Berger said there was no question in her mind that something needs to
go on the site, but added that she remained concerned about the bulk and mass
issues. She agreed that a new scale model would be helpful and expressed
concern about the integration of Conney’s and Phototronics.
Trustee Greable thanked staff and New Trier Partners for their efforts and
complimented the three Village committees that had addressed the development
and fleshed out the issues. He said he was concerned with what is best for the
community in the long term, and that the height and mass were the biggest
negatives to the proposal. He indicated that he is open to options, and
expressed optimism that something could be built on the site.
Trustee Johnson said he liked the new changes but was still concerned about
setting a precedent with the height, mass and density, and said he wished more
negotiations would take place between New Trier Partners and Conney’s and
Phototronics so the corner of that block could be integrated some way into the
site.
Trustee Poor said he would echo the foregoing comments and noted that there is
no dispute that it should be a mixed use development, but that the height and
bulk are the issues that give pause.
Mike Klein, New Trier Partners, said every possible change has been made, and
noted that he said from the onset that the project could not move forward with
a reduction in height or the number of floors. He commented that the additional
few feet is imperceptible, and focusing on the many positives of the plan was
more productive than focusing on the perceived negatives.
President Woodbury reiterated that the Council has to balance interests on both
sides, and he urged the applicant to take the evening’s comments into
consideration when refining the plan. He also encouraged bringing a scale model
that reflects the changes. He announced that a special meeting would be held on
January 27 th to continue deliberations on the planned development application.
5)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Pending and
Probable Litigation pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings
Act. Trustee Rintz seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:38 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:18 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
19 p.m.
