Recording Secretary
January 19, 2009
DRAFT MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
January 19, 2009
(Approved: February 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Monday,
January 19, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Public Works Director
Steven Saunders, and approximately 25 persons in the audience.
2)   Private Road Dedication Policy Update.
Mr. Saunders reported on developments since the November 18, 2008, Study
Session, when the Council expressed concerns over the proposed new cost-sharing
formula and how to effectively budget for the potential cost of upgrading
private streets. The Council asked staff to revisit cost sharing for bringing
private roads up to Code for dedication to the Village and how the Village
might recapture some of its costs.
Mr. Saunders related that the issue before the Council resulted from the
request of the Trapp Lane Homeowners’ Association that the Village take over
Trapp Lane as a public street. He explained that Winnetkans living on private
streets are responsible for the maintenance of the both the private roadway and
private storm sewer system underneath it. He summarized the Village’s long-
standing policy as being that, if the homeowners bring the roadway and storm
sewer systems up to village standards at their expense and the appropriate
right-of-way or right-of-way plus easements to the Village, then the Village
will take over the road as a public roadway. He mentioned that one of the
objections homeowners on private streets raise is that they pay the same taxes
as Winnetkans living on public streets maintained by the Village, but they do
not receive the same services.
Mr. Saunders then reviewed an alternative cost sharing methodology in which the
Village’s cost-sharing could be based on the cost of stormwater improvements
rather than on 50% of the total improvement cost. He explained that this would
recognize that the source and scope of stormwater runoff and the benefits of
drainage improvements often extend beyond the neighborhood in which they are
located. He added that this method would also clearly connect the Village’s
expenditure to both the public benefit and to addressing problems beyond a
limited neighborhood, although it could also cause the Village to wrestle with
the issue of how to fund and construct drainage improvements that provide
public benefit, but that also drain, and are located under, private roadways.
He pointed out that the likely solution to the White Oak Lane flooding would
probably include drainage improvements on private streets.
Mr. Saunders identified the following four specific policy issues before the
Council and commented briefly on each: (1) Village’s cost share, (2) recapture
of Village costs, (3) budgeting for projects, and (4) funding mechanism for
private share.
Mr. Saunders summarized the three cost sharing options: (1) to continue with
the current policy, (2) adopt a policy whereby the Village would share in 50%
of the total cost, or (3) adopt a policy where the Village would share in 100%
of the cost of associated drainage improvements. He stated that if the Village
decides to implement some kind of recapture provision, it will need to
determine if there is public benefit and whether the recapture provision is
appropriate and consistent with what is done on public streets. He noted that
there is significant benefit to the Village’s owning the right-of-way because
Village utilities are underneath the streets, so utility projects would be
easier to accomplish. It would also help the Village address regional drainage
problems and aid the Village in dealing with the negative and disruptive
effects of construction projects.
Mr. Saunders suggested that one way to address budgeting for possible private
road projects would be to allocate a portion of the capital funding for roadway
or drainage improvements. Funding could be accomplished either via cash up
front or through a special service area.
Responding to questions from Trustee Poor, Mr. Saunders stated that
approximately 25 private streets that might choose to become public, with some
being more desirable from a public policy standpoint, and estimated total costs
at approximately $6,000,000. He reported that Glencoe’s policy is similar to
’’’’Winnetka’’’s current policy in that it will help the homeowners’
associations, but does not enter into any kind of cost sharing arrangement with
them.
In response to a question from Trustee Berger, Mr. Saunders suggested adopting
a policy in which the village would pay 100% of the stormwater drainage
improvement costs up front and then recapture 50% of that expense over time.
Manager Williams pointed out that the recapture would only be triggered by
demolitions and rebuilds.
Trustee Berger remarked that it would then take a very long time to recover the
costs.
Trustee Rintz indicated that he would be comfortable with a policy in which the
Village covers the drainage portion of the work with the neighborhood picking
up the road work portion because the Village gets a dedicated road back as the
payoff for its investment. He pointed out that in some circumstances, i.e.,
White Oak/DeWindt, there is no tradeoff that benefits the Village, and that any
change in policy should be implemented consistently.
President Woodbury pointed out that to spend public funds, there must be a
public benefit.
Mr. Saunders commented that from a practical standpoint, it will take 100%
agreement among the property owners to get a street dedication, making it
unlikely that many private streets will opt to dedicate their streets to the
Village, as many of the affected homeowners feel strongly about maintaining
their ownership.
Among the Trapp Lane homeowners who then spoke in support of the amending the
existing policy so as facilitate moving ahead with the Trapp Lane project were
Ernie Weis, 1279 Trapp; Tom Barron, 1284 Trapp; Joe Bauer, 1294 Trapp; Bob
Jacob, 1350 Trapp; Mike Hills, 1324 Trapp; and Carol Fessler, 1314 Trapp. All
agreed that this would be a good public/private partnership and now is the time
to get this problem solved and done well.
President Woodbury concurred, saying that now is the time for the Village to
move forward. The questions are (1) which policy path is appropriate, and (2)
what is the financial split?
Trustee Behles indicated support for the Village entering into some sort of
cost sharing arrangement, but expressed concern about raising a level of
expectation that the Village will be able to rectify the flooding in this part
of town, which it can’t. He said that without a regional solution, certain
portions of the Village will be subject to flooding from time to time and added
that he views the need to have 100% agreement among the homeowners to be a very
limiting factor, so the chance of many more neighborhoods seeking to dedicate
their streets is low.
Manager Williams supported Mr. Behles’ remark, saying that even if the Village
had owned the street with new stormwater improvements in place, flooding would
still have occurred during last fall’s severe storms.
President Woodbury likened the policy’s evolutionary process to that of the
sidewalk policy, which thus far has worked well and was designed to address a
particular set of circumstances. In this case, the Trapp Lane neighbors, after
many years of negotiating with one another, have come together as one to ask
the Village to help them solve an existing problem.
Trustee Poor said the neighborhood has put forth a compelling case for the
Village to address a long-standing problem that stems from a policy that
existed when the neighborhood was first developed. He noted the long term
problem of precedent remains and said he is not convinced that what the Council
does for Trapp Lane has to become a long term policy that could affect years of
expenditures. He said he would be in favor proceeding on Trapp Lane with a 50/
50 split, but making it clear that this is not necessarily the policy that will
apply on a going forward basis. He suggested sending notices to all private
road homeowners to get a better sense of how big this issue is likely to be
over the long term.
Trustees Greable and Johnson expressed some reservations about either cost
sharing option based on the long term expenses and implications of any change
in policy.
Trustee Berger voiced concerns about how the Village’s participation in the
project costs impacts the same issues for all other homeowners on private
streets. She indicated that she could not vote in favor of the 50/50 policy as
written without holding a public hearing to which all residents of private
roads were invited to participate.
Trustee Rintz voiced the opinion that whatever the Village does for Trapp Lane,
it should be consistent with what it intends to do elsewhere in the future. He
said he could support either the 50/50 or stormwater only model, so long as
there is ultimately a dedication of the street to the Village, because the key
is the public benefit.
Manager Williams indicated that staff would bring a redrafted policy back
before the Council in the spring.
3)   Willow Road Reconstruction – Preliminary Design.
Mr. Saunders updated the Trustees on the status of the preliminary roadway
design for the reconstruction of Willow Road from Forestway Drive to Provident
Avenue, as prepared by Civiltech Engineering. He reviewed the preliminary plans
and explained where they contain some modifications to the Willow Road Steering
Committee’s recommendations which had been provided to Civiltech for inclusion
in the design. He enumerated the next steps in the process and indicated that
he expected construction to begin in 2011.
4)   Investment Policy – Non-Pension Assets.
Mr. McKee explained that the Village’s investment policy hasn’t been revised
since 1999 and suggested, in light of the current market environment, that
changes be made to the Village’s investment policy to give staff flexibility in
purchasing collateralized CD’s as dictated by existing market conditions.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to proceed, and Mr. McKee was asked to
come back to the Council with a revised policy.
5)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held due to the lateness of the hour..
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Rintz, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:00 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
January 20, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
January 20, 2009
(Approved: February 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 20, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, and King Poor. Absent:
Trustee Chris Rintz. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, and
approximately 25 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   January 27, 2009, Special Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 2, 2009, Special Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
c)   February 3, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
d)   February 5, 2009, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   December 16, 2008, Regular Meeting. President Woodbury indicated that
several minor corrections had been suggested by Staff, and Trustee Johnson,
seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the minutes as amended. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
b)   January 6, 2009, Regular Meeting. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-1-2009 – Zoning Variation: 1069 Spruce – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed the request for a variation from the Maximum Building Size
limitations of the Zoning Ordinance to allow the replacement of a sunroom on
the Subject Property. He explained that the current residence is legally
nonconforming with regard to gross floor area, and that the proposed new
sunroom will decrease the nonconformity by 2.1 square feet. He indicated that
the Zoning Board of Appeals had unanimously approved the request at its
December 8 th meeting.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the matter is before the Council because the
residence is considered a “post-FAR” building under the Zoning Ordinance, and
explained the meaning of that term. He described the request as de minimis, and
noted the reduction in the degree of nonconformity.
Mr. Dan Faierstein, the applicant’s attorney, asked the Council to waive
introduction and adopt the ordinance to avoid further delays for the applicant.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance
M-1-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance M-1-2009.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
b)   Ordinance M-2-2009 – Zoning Variation: 1509 Scott – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed the request for zoning variations for gross floor area, east
side yard setback and west building wall articulation, to construct additions
at the front and rear of the residence, on the first and second floors. He
described the proposed construction, and observed that the Zoning Board of
Appeals had approved the request by a vote of 6 to 1.
Mr. Seymour Turner, Airoom Architects, described the east side yard setback
request as vertically extending the existing nonconformity, explained that the
floor area ratio request was necessary because the applicant cannot take
advantage of the detached garage credit, and he noted that the wall
articulation was required because of an overhang condition from the second
floor.
Trustee Behles asked Mr. Turner to sum up how the applicant meets the standards
of practical difficulty or particular hardship.
Mr. Turner asserted that the overhangs are a significant difficulty because it
is not practical to jog the perimeter walls over or restructure the roof, and
that the GFA variation is necessary because of the inability to have a detached
garage and get the related GFA bonus. He added that the front bedroom is very
small and doesn’t have a closet, and commented that reducing the plan by 375
square feet to eliminate the gross floor area variation would do away with the
master bath, which is a common amenity in modern homes.
Mr. Turner observed that the hardship is based on the construction of the
existing home and its location on the lot, because to get the garage bonus,
they would have to tear down the attached garage and eliminate the existing
sitting room to build a garage in the rear yard.
Trustee Berger observed that the small size of the lot is an additional
challenge, and that it is difficult to design a house to meet today’s living
standards on such a small lot. She indicated that she wanted to go on record as
saying that the proposal contained a lot of bulk for such a small lot and the
Council has been looking for ways to find a balance between reasonable return
and keeping bulk down.
Mr. Turner responded that the neighbors are supportive, that the additions give
the home a better presence, and that most of the new construction can’t be seen
from the street.
Trustee Behles observed that he saw nothing in the record that supports a claim
of practical difficulty or particular hardship, and that he had not seen any
effort to create a conforming alternative.
Attorney Janega noted that she had not included a recital for practical
difficulties when she drafted the ordinance because the record didn’t contain
factual findings on that issue.
Trustee Poor agreed with Trustee Behles and said there was a lack of evidence
of hardship and that given the magnitude of the GFA variation request, he would
be disinclined to approve.
Trustee Berger also said she would be disinclined to approve the request, given
the concerns over the bulk issue.
Trustee Greable indicated that he thought the ZBA did an adequate job of
talking about the various additions, and that though there was a consensus that
there should be a reduction in the request, in the end they voted to approve
the variations. He said he felt there was enough in the record to justify the
ZBA’s recommendation and he would vote to approve the request.
Trustee Johnson passed.
President Woodbury noted that a motion to introduce would not carry, and that
the applicant’s options are to table the ordinance so revisions can be made, or
to withdraw it and start the process over with the ZBA.
Mr. Turner asked the Council to table the ordinance.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to table Ordinance M-2-2009.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
c)   Ordinance M-3-2009 – Zoning Variation: 941 Tower – Introduction. President
Woodbury commented preliminarily that the material in the agenda packet was
extensive, and that while there continues to be a difference of opinion between
the neighbors, it is not the Council’s job to arbitrate disputes but to hear a
zoning case. He indicated that the Council would hear from Mr. D’Onofrio, from
the applicant’s attorney, and from audience members, and he requested that the
neighbors not repeat what had already been said in front of the ZBA.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the request for setback variations for the east front
corner yard and north rear yard, in order to construct a new home on the
Subject Property, which is bisected by a driveway easement that is considered a
private road by the Zoning Ordinance because it serves four other properties in
the Wild Cherry Subdivision. He explained that the easement imposes three
corner yard setbacks on the Subject Property along Tower Road and the driveway,
resulting in a very small, triangular buildable area.
Mr. D’Onofrio said the proposed new home complies with all other applicable
zoning requirements, and noted that the existing residence is nonconforming
with regard to front, rear and side yard setbacks. He pointed out that the
setbacks for the four other properties along the horseshoe-shaped driveway
range from 15 feet to two feet, and the rear yard setbacks range from 13.2 feet
to 2.91 feet, with the front yard at 939 Tower encroaching 2 feet into the
driveway easement.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that after considering the case at three separate
meetings, the Zoning Board of Appeals had recommended the variation request by
a vote of 6 to 1.
In the course of the discussion, Mr. D’Onofrio confirmed that the impervious
surface would roughly double, that the impervious surface calculation includes
the road pavement, that the easement is wider in some places than the actual
road and that the homes at 929 and 939 Tower have 110% front yard variances.
Mr. David Schrauth, the applicant’s attorney, stated that the applicant, Anne
McGoey, intends to live in the house with her daughter and her family. He
asserted that the record contained all the facts needed to make a
determination, and that the history of the conflict between the neighbors and
the applicant was not relevant or helpful. He stressed that the issue is the
easement running through the property, and that the landmark status of 939
Tower cannot be used to limit development of neighboring property.
Trustee Berger asked why the neighbors objected to moving the existing
driveway, and Mr. Schrauth replied that they liked the meandering nature of it,
and confirmed that the north side of the proposed new home would be less bulky
if the driveway were moved.
Melissa Mizel, 939 Tower, said she was concerned about the blocking of sunlight
to her property, and introduced Peter Land of IIT, who had done a shade study.
Mr. Peter Land gave his shadow study presentation and said the proposed new
home would cast shadows at 939 Tower.
Christine O’Reilly, 1116 Tower; Susan Benjamin, Benjamin Historic
Certifications LLC; Julie Windsor, 1479 Tower; and Crispin Hales, 1000
Sheridan; all spoke in opposition to the variation request.
Barbara Johnson, 935 Tower, supported the applicant’s request.
Philip Torf, attorney for the Mizels distributed a handout and asked the
Council to reject the ZBA’s recommendation.
Michael Edwards, 939 Tower, distributed a handout to the Council, asserted that
some things in the record are false and swayed the judgment of the ZBA, and
said the eight point test for granting a zoning variation had not been met.
John Zachar, the applicant’s architect, presented a shadow study that showed
the same day of each month of the year and also showed shadows cast by two
large homes to the west of the subdivision.
Mr. Schrauth said the home at 939 Tower has a 100% variation on the easement,
so the size of the front yard variation request is not the issue, and that at
3,200 square feet, the size and scale of the proposed new home is not out of
character for Winnetka.
The Council questioned the architect and discussed the request, after which
Attorney Janega explained that their focus should be on Mr. D’Onofrio’s
presentation of the record rather than claimed errors in the ZBA minutes. She
explained that, at a minimum, the Council has to find a practical difficulty or
particular hardship and that all the other standards are components of the
first, and are used to help to determine how to find the balance between what
is being requested and the impact on other properties. She noted that if the
Council finds a practical difficulty or particular hardship in this case, it
need not enter findings in the other areas.
Trustee Poor said he believed a good case had been made for some variation
because the driveway situation is unusual and creates a practical difficulty,
but he added that he had a problem with this particular variation because of
the size of the new home, which affects issues of light and air for the
neighbors, and character of the subdivision. He suggested the neighbors
consider mediation to work out their differences.
Trustee Berger said that the proposed new home complies in all other respects
to the Zoning Ordinance and the variation that’s being requested is less than
what some of the neighbors have, and that she would approve the variation
request.
Trustee Behles indicated that this was a zoning variation, not a demolition
hearing, and although he echoed Trustee Poor’s comments about this being the
wrong house for this particular site, the design was forced by the unusual
condition of the easement. He said he believed the standards for a zoning
variation had been met and he would approve the request.
Trustee Greable said there was do doubt that a new house needs to be built on
the site, but that he would like to see a better option created and so would be
against this request.
Trustee Johnson expressed agreement with Trustee Poor, saying he thought the
parties should work out their differences and that he would vote against
introducing the ordinance.
President Woodbury said if the neighbors want a better house, and the applicant
wants a new house, then compromise is in order, and asked Mr. Schrauth if he
would like the ordinance tabled.
Mr. Schrauth noted that the dispute with the neighbors was mediated by a
professional before and after the litigation proceeded, and that the hearing
before the Council was the applicant’s last resort. He then asked that the
ordinance be tabled.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to table Ordinance M-3-2009.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1589 and 1590. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1589 in the amount of $1,107,633.45 and Warrant List No. 1590 in the amount of
$950,470.91. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Poor.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz:
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees.
i)   Trustee Greable announced that he had recently attended the NWMC meeting,
where the financial stress of local communities was discussed. He said the
coming year or two will be tough as far as cutting costs and providing
services, and he noted that Highland Park’s 2009 budget contains no raises for
employees.
ii)   Trustee Johnson reported that Multiple Choices in West Elm is changing
hands, and the Chamber of Commerce is urging people to shop locally to help
retailers weather the economic downturn.
iii)   Trustee Poor reported that the Village has its first net metering
customer, and that Winnetka is one of the few municipalities to offer a net
metering option. He also said the Environmental and Forestry Commission will be
studying the concept further, as well as putting together a list of priorities
and action items for projects to be considered.
c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that she would be in Springfield on
Thursday to moderate the Professional Responsibility panel for the IICLE
Municipal Law Update program and that she has been appointed Chair of the
Illinois Municipal League’s Home Rule Committee.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that in all probability, the Fell
Redevelopment would not be considered at the January 27 th meeting and that one
of the budget review sessions would be moved up and Fell Redevelopment
rescheduled to February. He added that the next zoning discussion would take
place at a special meeting on February 2, and there would be a budget review
meeting on February 5, in addition to the regular meeting on February 3.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz. The meeting adjourned at 10:28
p.m.
               
Recording Secretary
January 27, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
Budget Review Meeting
January 27, 2009
(Approved: February 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 27, 2008, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Chris Rintz, and King
Poor. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Finance
Director Ed McKee, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Fire Chief Scott Smith,
Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Deputy Police Chief Pat Kreis, and 4 persons in the
audience.
2)   Budget Overview.
Mr. McKee commented on the budget within the context of the current unstable
economic environment. He noted that the State is experiencing serious financial
problems, which in the future could put revenues that the Village receives from
the State at risk, and he mentioned that the State continues to push unfunded
mandates on municipal entities. He reported that the Village has healthy cash
reserves due to the conservative way the Village’s finances are managed,
including: balancing the operating budget annually; generating significant cash
to fund capital improvements; limiting employee head count; funding pensions at
the recommended amount; and carrying very limited debt obligations.
Mr. McKee indicated that the proposed budget is balanced, generates $8.6
million of operating cash-flow, and controls costs, to the extent possible. He
remarked that under the proposed budget the average resident will pay 3.0% or
$173 more for services in the next year. He went on to explain how to navigate
through the budget document, broke down the relationships between funds. He
then reviewed the Village’s fiscal situation, starting with an overview of
revenue sources and a recap of expenses and moving on to a review of major
capital expenses and large projects. He said he expects the Village to be cash
neutral at the end of the year.
With regard to the capital projects, Manager Williams commented that 2009 is
the year of design. In 2010 the Council will be reevaluating the Village’s
financial position and deciding where and how to move forward and with which
projects. He noted the necessity to move forward with putting on a new roof on
Village Hall, as the building has experienced significant leakage and damage in
recent rain storms.
Manager Williams went on to discuss employee head count and recent steps and
recommendations for the future to control personnel costs as best as possible
while maintaining services. He focused on the strategic need to plan for
replacing long-tenured employees who are eligible for retirement, particularly
in the Water & Electric and Fire Departments where it takes considerable time
to train new recruits. He proposed adding one apprentice lineman and shifting
some resources from the Police Department to the Fire Department when an
upcoming retirement occurs in order to be able to add another transitional
firefighter without increasing headcount, until such time as the Fire
Department works through the upcoming wave of retirements. He mentioned that
the Village employed a transitional officer in the Police Department when it
went through a similar experience.
Mr. McKee’s continued with a summary of each individual fund, and which time
the Council discussed the budget in further detail, including: (i) balancing
current budget pressures against maintaining services; (ii) possible
consequences to the Village of the pension losses; and (iii) keeping personnel
and health costs under control. He concluded by saying that the Village is in a
strong position because of the conservative policies and cash reserves.
3)   Fire Department.
Fire Chief Scott Smith gave a PowerPoint presentation reviewing cost saving
measures that have been implemented, recent accomplishments of the Department,
and summarizing the proposed budget. He mentioned the proposed purchase of a
new 75-foot ladder/pumper truck, at a cost of $800,000 to replace the existing
truck that is nearing the end of its useful life.
Chief Smith then responded to questions from the Trustees about the impact of
the impending retirements on the Department and desirability of maintaining
current staffing levels.
There was a consensus among the Trustees to move forward as soon as possible
with the purchase of the new ladder truck.
4)   Police Department.
Police Chief Joe DeLopez reviewed the Police Department’s mission,
organizational structure, activity overview and staffing levels, emphasizing
that not all Department personnel are interchangeable due to the uniqueness of
responsibilities and levels of authority for different positions. He indicated
that since the Department is comprised of younger personnel, there has been and
will continue to be increased use of family medical and military leave. In
light thereof, he recommended against any permanent reduction in current
staffing levels.
Manager Williams said Staff would prefer to fill the vacant position in the
Police Department, but that in the interest of adding another transitional
firemedic, this was the only way to accomplish that without increasing the
employee head count. He observed that there could be an increase in overtime as
a result of the unfilled position and that time would tell how it is going to
work out. Therefore, there would need to be a year-by-year evaluation to
determine the success of this proposal.
There was a discussion about the possibility of consolidating dispatch centers
with other communities, particularly Wilmette. Deputy Chief Kreis noted that
Winnetka is strongly in favor of this, but that attempts to work with logical
partners have been unsuccessful thus far. He indicated that he and Chief
DeLopez would continue to pursue the matter.
5)   Administration.
Due to the lateness of the hour, this item was postponed to a later date.
6)   Executive Session.
None.
7)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:35 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
February 2, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
February 2, 2009
(Approved: February 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Monday,
February 2, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and approximately 12
persons in the audience.
2)   Zoning Regulations – Phase 2 Evaluation.
President Woodbury asked Mr. D’Onofrio to begin his presentation by reviewing
methods to make renovation of existing homes more attractive, since previous
Councils have made encouraging renovation a priority.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the residential zoning analysis process to-date, and
gave an overview of the project’s focus, which was partially shaped by results
from last year’s Caucus Questionnaire, and noted that Staff had been directed
to develop a list of potential measures for relaxing the regulations governing
renovation of existing homes.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that renovation permits have increased in the last five
years, and in 2008 they comprised 84% of all major building permits in the
Village, although the increase is largely due to a reduction in applications
for new home permits.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained how the two-tiered treatment of existing zoning
regulations differs for existing homes and new construction with regard to
gross floor area (GFA) and floor area ratio (FAR). He outlined the core issues
that went into Staff’s consideration of potential incentives for renovation and
listed some zoning relief items for consideration to promote renovation:
·   Increase the GFA, RLC and attic allowances for older homes.
·   Allow for vertical articulation, different building materials and different
roof lines, in addition to the existing requirement.
·   Eliminate zoning variation requirements for one-for-one replacement of
subordinate structures such as porches, detached garages and sheds, or portions
of the principal structure where a new addition would decrease an existing
nonconformity.
President Woodbury questioned an observation in Mr. D’Onofrio’s agenda report
that asserts he is doubtful whether a homeowner would remodel a home based on a
GFA increase of 2%, since that has been the Village’s practice for many years.
Trustee Behles indicated that he has never viewed the extra GFA as an
incentive, but rather sees it as a compensation for renovating around the
inefficiencies of an older home.
President Woodbury agreed with Trustee Behles’ assessment, and asked if an
allowance of 2% is big enough.
Attorney Janega explained that the rationale for the two-tiers of standards
came about because it became clear that, due to design elements like steeper
roofs and smaller rooms and interior layout, older homes could not build to the
maximum GFA, whereas new construction could take full advantage. She indicated
that this made it possible for the Village to articulate a rationale for the
two-tiered approach, and added that the same rationale could probably support
increasing the difference between the two tiers, either by increasing GFA for
older homes or reducing GFA for newer ones.
Trustee Behles observed that he was not comfortable with raising the limit of
FAR, but that other things in the report could help, and he would also like to
add an automatic allowance for 18-inch eaves, which the Council wants to
encourage, but for which the homeowner is penalized. He also suggested that
raising the roofed lot coverage (RLC) might be a better avenue for providing
relief to homeowners who wish to renovate.
There was a discussion about the benefits of increasing the RLC, as well as the
other zoning relief items that were listed as incentives for renovation, after
which Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that measures other than zoning relief could be
considered, including:
·   Reduce renovation costs by potentially amending the Village’s regulations
for undergrounding of electric services.
·   Consider adopting the ICC International Existing Building Code, which is
tailored to existing buildings and uses more performance-related standards than
the code currently in use.
·   Streamline the zoning relief process.
·   Adjust fees for renovation projects.
·   Give priority to permit processing for renovation permits.
·   Provide waivers from certain forestry and engineering requirements when the
scope of the renovation project will have little or no impact on forestry or
engineering issues.
After more discussion about the proposals from Staff, Manager Williams asked
the Council for some preliminary direction.
Trustee Rintz said he would accept any of the suggestions after some more
discussion and refinements, as he thought saving a home and keeping the
integrity of the block face are well worth considering.
Trustee Johnson agreed with Trustee Rintz but said the Council should try to
think through the unintended consequences of any changes before a decision is
made.
Trustee Greable indicated that he thought it is important to give existing
homes a chance to survive, so tools should definitely be considered to achieve
that aim. He also commented that the current economic situation is causing home
values to slide and he wanted to reassure citizens that the Council would be
doing everything it can to help the situation.
Trustee Berger said she was not convinced that increasing GFA would make a big
difference, and noted that the Caucus has asked the Council to address bulk and
mass issues. She agreed that streamlining the permit process and adjusting fees
are good steps, but cautioned that the Council should proceed carefully before
making any zoning changes.
Trustee Poor said he approved of streamlining the permit process, agreed with
the concerns about raising the GFA allowance, and said the 52% of respondents
to the Caucus Survey who are against changing current restrictions need to be
considered.
Trustee Behles said he would also be against increasing GFA either in the attic
or by going above the current 40% FAR threshold, and wanted to keep promoting
undergrounding of utilities. He did see benefits to streamlining the zoning and
permit processes and suggested giving relief to homeowners with 18-inch eaves.
President Woodbury asked for audience comments.
Nan Greenough, 500 Maple, said the last time the zoning process was
streamlined, the Zoning Administrator position was created, which seems to have
been a tremendous success, and said more simplification would be effective. She
also endorsed the allowance for 18-inch eaves and indicated that anything that
could be done to help make the Code more friendly to an older house would be a
good step.
Steve Aisin, Heritage Builders, said the building permit fees should be
commensurate with the services provided. He agreed with honoring the Caucus
Survey results indicating that no changes should be enacted.
Joe Nash, Koenig & Strey Realtors, said he encouraged anything that could be
done to make renovation more attractive and help property values.
President Woodbury observed that the night’s discussions are preliminary and
that with the assistance of Staff, the streamlining of the zoning process can
move forward. The focus of the discussion then changed to issues of new
construction in the Village.
Mr. Norkus reported that Staff has been working with Winter & Co. to develop a
wider range of alternative options for addressing bulk and mass in new
construction, using computer modeling to illustrate the results. He summarized
the results of the residential zoning analysis, including:
·   Reducing GFA by 2% or 5%, which would not make much difference in bulk or
mass
·   Creating first floor minimums or second floor limits to sculpt the bulk of
new houses, which could alleviate light and air concerns of neighbors and would
be more effective if RLC is increased
·   Front wall building articulation, which Mr. Norkus concluded could get
clunky, as illustrated by the modeling
·   Side wall articulation, which the models show does not reduce bulk very
much and could squeeze the building envelope to create a long building
·   A correction in determining the first floor height
·   Adjustments in determining the average front setback
Mr. Norkus observed that no judgments have been formed by Staff as to which
alternatives would work best, as the first step of the exercise is to use the
computer models to see what happens in three dimensions when changes are
applied and to see how potential unintended consequences would play out.
President Woodbury pointed out the conflict between light and air vs. compact
development that retains open space, and questioned if light and air are more
precious than impervious surface, which also raises the issue of flooding.
Mr. Norkus said Staff had lively discussions about the trade-offs between one
story elements and compactness, and observed that the modeling is illustrative
of how difficult it is to achieve contextual design using the Zoning Ordinance.
Trustee Behles said the designer needs to have freedom to achieve good design
and the Council should be looking for methods that keep the options flexible
and not to prescribe too many elements.
President Woodbury recalled that the discussion in December revolved around
creating more flexibility, not more regulation, and the tone should be to
create better design, more flexibility and less regulation.
There was a discussion about how to encourage better design and less perceived
bulk in the R-5 Zoning District using a carrot rather than a stick approach,
and whether the side yard setbacks need to be looked at, or if that is even
practical on a 50-foot wide lot.
Manager Williams asked Mr. Norkus for this thoughts, given that this is his
fifth in-depth analysis of Winnetka’s Zoning Ordinance.
Mr. Norkus observed that the problem has more to do with design than with GFA,
that the 3-dimension modeling reinforced that observation, and that he is not
comfortable with changing the Zoning Ordinance. He indicated that the Council
should preserve options, since trying to achieve contextual design through
zoning is difficult at best, and he added that the Design Guidelines should
meet regulations in the middle. He commented that understanding the RLC
limitations as a restraining factor on design was an “aha” moment, and agreed
that loosening restrictions in that area might help to achieve design goals.
Mr. D’Onofrio added that zoning is a “one size fits all” solution, while design
guidelines are more flexible.
Trustee Berger said that, other than relaxing the RLC restrictions, none of the
options seem to solve the problem of bulk and mass. She added that the design
guidelines are voluntary and are likely to be ignored by most builders.
Trustee Poor said he thought performance-based RLC could introduce flexibility
and could reduce perceived mass and bulk.
Mr. Nash warned that property values are deteriorating because of the economy,
and expressed concern that builders prefer to build elsewhere because the
Zoning Ordinance is too restrictive, citing the tree deposits and underground
storage requirements as examples.
President Woodbury asked Trustee Poor to explore the issue of tree permits with
the Environmental & Forestry Committee, and noted that the Council has to
balance the interests of people who think teardowns are terrible and those who
prefer redevelopment.
Melissa Mizel, 939 Tower, said she thought the Zoning Ordinance should define
concepts like neighborhood character, reasonable use and rate of return, and
that non-economic trends such as environmental awareness could be looked at.
She also suggested having neighbors sign off on new construction as a way to
encourage neighborhood architecture, since 75% of new homes are built by people
who don’t live in ’’’’Winnetka.
Charlie Hoffman, 303 Sheridan, said he thought zoning regulations have gone
almost as far as they can to achieve the Council’s goals, that there are
limitations to encouraging good architecture and design, but that there are
still small improvements that can be made in all the zoning districts, not just
the R-5.
Manager Williams suggested that Staff digest all the comments and bring back
refined options to the March Study Session, after which the Council could move
forward with public hearings in April to try to wrap up any zoning changes
before the new Council is seated in May.
3)   Executive Session.
None.
4)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 10:30 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
February 3, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
BUDGET REVIEW MEETING
February 3, 2009
(Approved: February 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 3, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 8:11 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Community
Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately 4 persons in the
audience.
2)   Budget Review – Public Works.
Mr. Saunders presented a budget summary and an overview of the Department’s
activities, organization and funding sources, and he also provided some details
of snow and ice control methods and practices. He reported that the Village’s
current recycling contract will expire at the end of September, and that he
will investigate ways to add a commercial recycling component to the new
contract. He noted that the Village’s recycling rates are increasing, while the
amount of refuse being collected is trending downward.
Mr. Saunders reviewed plans to improve the drainage in west Winnetka, discussed
where improvements might be made and also listed some possible sources of
revenue to help fund stormwater projects.
Manager Williams pointed out that the engineering study will need to be
completed and several months of in-depth discussions may be necessary, after
which drainage projects can be scheduled.
Mr. Saunders concluded his presentation by reviewing the projects slated to be
completed in 2009, which include the stormwater drainage study, Cenotaph
reconstruction, Village Hall improvements, Streetscape engineering and
preparations for Phase 1 construction.
3)   Budget Review –Community Development.
Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio gave an overview of the
Department’s functions, which include approving building permits, performing
plan and zoning reviews, conducting electrical, plumbing and mechanical
inspections, managing code enforcement and food service sanitation, and also
staffing for 6 standing committees and 2 ad hoc committees. He reported that,
in addition to its everyday activities, the Department oversaw a residential
zoning analysis with Nore Winter & Co., assisted the Plan Commission with a
review of affordable housing, and processed the preliminary Planned Development
of New Trier Partners for The Fell Property, while also providing staff support
to the DRB, ZBA, Plan Commission and Council for the Fell Redevelopment
proposal.
Mr. D’Onofrio summarized the number of demolition permits, building permits,
and zoning cases that were processed in 2008, and noted that the budget will
decrease by 5.9% from last year, largely because a budgeted position for Plan
Reviewer Assistant was never filled, and also because the funds allocated for
consultants were significantly reduced. He indicated that the code enforcement
program has been very successful and there are plans to expand inspections to
include large remodeling projects.
4)   Budget Review – Administration.
This item will be discussed at a later meeting.
5)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Pending &
Probable Litigation, Board Appointments and Personnel Matters, pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(1) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays:
None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:40 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:41 p.m.
               
Recording Secretary
February 3, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
February 3, 2009
(Approved: February 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 3, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Finance
Director Ed McKee, and approximately four persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
a)   February 5, 2009, Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   February 10, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
c)    February 11, 2009, Special Study Session. All of the Council members
present, except Trustee Berger, indicated that they expected to attend. Manager
Williams announced that the Fell Site proposal was scheduled to be discussed at
this meeting.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
a)   January 13, 2009, Study Session. Trustee Johnson made some corrections to
the minutes pertaining to his comments on the Fell Site Planned Development.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the minutes as
amended. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Resolution No. R-1-2009 – 2009 MFT Transfer for Maintenance of Streets –
Adoption. Mr. Saunders reported that it has been Village policy in the past to
allocate $125,000 from the Motor Fuel Tax funds towards maintaining streets,
but that recent direction from the Council has been to reduce the amount of
this transfer over time to reserve some MFT funds for capital expenses. He
recommended authorizing $95,000 in MFT funds for street maintenance.
Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-1-
2009, authorizing the expenditure of $95,000 in MFT funds for street
maintenance. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, urged the Council to carefully consider the budget
within the broader context of the economic times, which he opined are
undoubtedly the most difficult the country has faced in a lifetime. In
particular he asked the Trustees to reconsider the costs associated with the
Streetscape project in light of the reported pension plan losses and diminished
sales tax revenue. He asked the Council to postpone any work on Streetscape
until the economy settles down and the economic stimulus bill is passed, as
there may be possible matching funds included for local government projects.
President Woodbury responded saying that the Council has been, and continues to
be, very mindful of the economic environment. He recalled that after 9/11 the
Village weathered the tough economic times by freezing capital spending and may
have to do so again, but he noted that projects may need to be ready to
commence in order to qualify for any stimulus funding that might become
available, so the Council is on the right track in budgeting for Streetscape,
Village Hall renovation and the Water Plant upgrades.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce, also commented on the proposed expenditure on
Streetscape.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1591 and 1592. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1591 in the amount of $1, 684,706.84 and Warrant List No. 1592 in the amount of
$399,143.36. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees.
i)   Trustee Greable announced that the NWMC will host a legislative brunch on
February 21 st and invited his colleagues to attend. He observed that the topic
of the pension funding shortfall was being addressed and that there could be an
increase in employer contributions in 2010.
ii)   Trustee Johnson said the BCDC had held two meetings in January and that
he would have a lot to report to the Council at a later date.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams said he would contact New Trier Partners and to
switch the meeting date to the regular Tuesday Study Session, as all of the
Council would be present. He also reported that the extreme cold of the past
weekend had caused several neighboring communities to come within several hours
of having their water intake pipes completely freezing up. He said the
Village’s electric plant puts the Village in a unique situation, because a
boiler can be fired up to flush hot water back out the pipe to thaw any
freezing, and that Winnetka is in a position to help nearby municipalities in
the event of an emergency. Lastly, he reported that he would add a pension
component to Thursday’s budget meeting to discuss what is currently being
discussed in the area of extra contributions in 2010.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Joni Johnson as the ZBA’s
representative to the Plan Commission. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Rintz, moved to appoint Ms. Johnson as the ZBA rep to the Plan Commission. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
President Woodbury then suggested that John O’Malley be reappointed to the
Police Pension Board for another full term. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to so appoint Mr. O’Malley. By voice vote, the motion carried.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 8:
10 p.m.
               
Recording Secretary
February 5, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL BUDGET REVIEW
February 5, 2009
(Approved: February 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 5, 2009 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Assistant Director of Water & Electric
Rich Ciesla, Finance Director Ed McKee, and one person in the audience.
2)   Budget Review – Water & Electric Department.
Manager Williams reviewed the Village’s fiscal situation in light of possible
budget shortfalls for the next few years and summarized the projects that are
currently under consideration. Mr. Williams also suggested that three projects
be deferred until a financial review for the next three years is completed: 1)
Winnetka Ave. water main ($500,000, water fund), 2) Winnetka and Green Bay
traffic signal upgrade ($450,000, motor fuel tax fund), and 3) effluent clear
well #3 extension ($305,000, water fund).
Mr. Keys reviewed the Electric Department’s revenue and expenses, along with
some routine expenditures and capital improvements. He reported that the
Department has several linemen who are approaching 40 years’ employment with
the Village and cautioned that any sudden departures would leave a big gap in
knowledge of the Village’s infrastructure. He recommended budgeting for an
apprentice lineman in this year’s budget, as it takes four to six years to
develop a full-fledged lineman.
Mr. Keys indicated that a portion of the Power Cost Adjustment deficit had been
recouped as a result of last year’s change in the rate, but said the amount was
still being under-collected, due mainly to a decrease in sales. He observed
that the wholesale cost of power would increase by 5% in the coming year and
recommended a 1.9% increase in residential rates.
Mr. Keys said the Water Department’s sales were lower than projected, as last
summer was not overly hot, and he proposed an 8% rate increase for residents
and 10% for unincorporated areas. He reviewed the distribution of income and
capital expenditures, as well as the water plant improvement program.
There was a discussion about the financial challenges that the Village may face
next year and beyond, and the Council examined ways to contain costs.
3)   Executive Session.
None.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 9:50 p.m.
