Recording Secretary
February 10, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
February 10, 2009
(Approved: March 3, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 10, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio,
Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and approximately 40
persons in the audience.
2)   Fell Site Planned Development, Consideration of Preliminary Plat.
President Woodbury announced that the most recent modifications to the proposed
Fell Site Planned Development were included in the Council’s agenda packet and
the applicant would review them, after which Mr. Norkus would report on the
changes made since the Plan Commission issued its findings.
Mike Klein, New Trier Partners (NTP), said he had endeavored to address the
Council’s comments from the January 13 th meeting, including addressing the
sloping of the site on Elm Street, the “bookend” gables at the corner behind
Conney’s and Phototronics, and more tweaking of the height and mass elements.
He pointed to the new model NTP been created to illustrate the latest
modifications.
Mike Fitzgerald, OKW Architects, described the latest changes to the plan,
including:
·   Overall building height reduced by one foot, to a height of 48.5 feet
·   4 th story gable along Lincoln Avenue eliminated
·   Architectural elements added to the north wall behind Conney’s and
Phototronics for consistency with the Lincoln and Elm Street elevations
Mr. Fitzgerald stated he was unable to step the building down along the slope
of Elm Street because it would require adding ramps or steps in the residential
hallways and would also hinder access to the second floor courtyard for the
units on Elm Street. He described an alternative lower level plan, that
addresses concerns about the viability of below grade retail by eliminating the
first lower level, which would have contained retail and related parking.
Mr. Lee Brown, Teska Associates, who had testified on behalf of NTP before the
ZBA and Plan Commission, asserted that the proposal met the Comprehensive
Plan’s goal of promoting a healthy commercial tax base. He estimated the
development would generate $194,000 in real estate taxes and $60,000 sales in
taxes annually, and predicted that the building’s residents would buy goods and
services locally. He asserted that the proximity of the Metra station satisfies
the Comprehensive Plan’s standard to minimize congestion, and said the
community would benefit from having a LEED-certified building, streetscape
improvements and a water main upgrade.
Trustee Greable expressed concern about the financial viability of the project
considering the unstable economic and lending environment.
Mr. Klein answered that NTP does not anticipate starting construction until
mid-2010, when he hopes times will be better, and noted that the Winnetka
location is an excellent draw for investors, and that the proximity to the
train will attract buyers.
The Council discussed the plan with Mr. Klein and Mr. Fitzgerald in light of
the most recent modifications, after which President Woodbury asked Mr. Norkus
to make his comments.
Mr. Norkus reviewed a Staff report that identified the elements of the proposal
that the Plan Commission found inconsistent with the Comprehensive Plan, and
that explained how the recent modifications affect those elements.
Mr. Frank Petrek, the objectors’ attorney, said the neighbors’ objections had
not been addressed because there was still no data on overall intensity of use
and the second floor courtyard open space is not accessible to the general
public. He observed that the height is still 48 ½ feet, and the eaves on the
south side are 2 ½ feet taller than the ridge line. He said the neighbors feel
no consideration is being given to the south side and asked that no variations
be granted.
President Woodbury asked Attorney Janega to define open space and public space.
Attorney Janega explained that the reference to “common open space” in Section
17.58.070(d)(4) is part of the required documentation for the planned
development application and that Community Development staff makes the initial
determination of whether the application is complete. She clarified that the
Zoning Ordinance uses the term “public” to denote the general public and uses
“common” as an element of ownership, so that common space is not necessarily
open to the public. She added that questions about the completeness of the
application are raised early on in the process and that, at this point, the
question before the Council is whether the substantive standards have been met.
President Woodbury stated that he cannot vote on this matter because of the
formal objections, and said the core question is whether this is the right
development at the right time at this location. He called for comments from the
Council and asked them to indicate whether or not they are inclined to approve
the application.
Trustee Behles indicated that the first order of business is to see that the
building fits into the community, and directed his comments to that aspect. He
said the plan before the Council is not the same proposal the Plan Commission
rejected last fall, citing NTP’s efforts to reduce the perceived mass and scale
of the development by: (i) creating fourth floor setbacks; (ii) removing the
bookend gables; (iii) adding architectural detail on the east elevation to
match the rest of the building; and (iv) reducing the overall building height
by one foot. He added that, while the project was not perfect, he believes that
the modified development proposal is now compatible with the surrounding
community and he would be able to vote to approve it.
Trustee Rintz stressed the need to distinguish between a zoning case and a
planned development application, as zoning is prescribed in hard numbers and
planned development ordinances are adopted because of the flexibility they give
to town boards, developers and staff for infill redevelopment. He noted that
the numbers are a framework, and can be exceeded in some cases, since the
approval bodies take into account soft issues that can’t be quantified in the
zoning process. He stated that he felt the project is now more consistent with
what he would like to see in downtown development than what was before the Plan
Commission, and that a pedestrian on Elm or Lincoln will have the perception of
a three story building. He said the applicant has made every attempt to adjust
his plan to accommodate the Council’s concerns, which gives the impression that
they do have the best interests of downtown at heart. He said he could support
the application with the caveat that he would like to see two levels of parking
instead of the alternate scheme.
Trustee Berger echoed Trustee Behles’ comments, said she was unsure about the
alternate plan, and asked if the intensity of the condominium development
correlated to the affordable housing goals. She indicated that she would be
willing to view the application favorably.
Trustee Poor said he thought the Plan Commission’s decision was entitled to be
given a lot of weight and that the recent changes to the plan provided
incremental benefits, but he did not think they materially alter the Plan
Commission’s fundamental concerns. He commented that while there is no doubt
the East Elm business district needs to be improved and mixed use is
appropriate, he was not inclined to approve the application because of height
and intensity of use considerations.
Trustee Johnson said the building is very pleasing, the setbacks are a
reasonable compromise between the developers and the community, and the
modified plan is a great improvement over what is currently on Elm Street. He
commended the applicant for showing good faith in responding to the Council’s
concerns, and said he would be inclined to approve the project, which would
bring vibrancy to the downtown.
Trustee Greable stated that there are benefits to the community and the
downtown area in moving forward with the development. He observed that the Plan
Commission might not have voted against the current plan, and noted that the
planned development ordinance encourages creative thinking and that he would
favor moving forward.
President Woodbury observed that five Trustees were inclined to approve the
application, but that before a vote could be taken the public benefits needed
to be discussed.
Manager Williams observed that Trustee Rintz and President Woodbury are
uniquely suited to participate in and enhance the negotiations, and he asked
them to take the time to attend the meetings between the developer and Staff.
Edith Howell, 277 Ridge, stated she supports a mixed use development on the
Fell Site, but agrees with Trustee Poor that this is not the right project
because the height and bulk are too intense. She recommended leaving the site
as is until the right development comes along.
Susie Hilbrand, 160 Westview, inquired about the development process if other
buildings in the area became available for sale. She suggested that, to
demonstrate a desire to be neighborly, the applicant should do a better job of
maintaining the site.
President Woodbury responded that the Council can’t determine hypothetical
situations about other buildings, and said upkeep of existing buildings would
be discussed in the meetings about public benefits.
Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka, stated that the plan has come a long way but
concerns of the neighbors to the south have not been addressed. She recommended
stepping back the south elevation and adding architectural detail on the fourth
story so it doesn’t look like a box was dropped on top of the building. She
disagreed with granting the height exception, noting that she didn’t see any
public benefits or open space considerations.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce, disagreed with the Village Attorney’s opinion and
asserted that the planned development ordinance grants a density bonus in
return for the public benefit of open space.
Debbie Ross, 921 Tower, expressed concern over the precedent the Council was
setting and added that the character of Winnetka needed to be considered.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, expressed agreement with the Village Attorney’s
interpretation of the open space provision, said that the Council has taken the
spirit of Winnetka into account, and added that open space is not mandated in a
commercial district development. He stated that the height issue is de minimis,
that other buildings in town are over 45 feet tall and that the NTP proposal is
a vast improvement over the current, unsightly view on Elm Street.
Arthur Brown, 850 Bell and a member of the BCDC, read the memo from the BCDC
contained in the agenda packet. He added that as a private citizen, he feels
that there is much to be gained by going forward with the application and much
to be lost by not doing it, and that given the economy there won’t be any other
developers coming along soon.
Paul Dunn, 988 Hill and a member of the BCDC, read his letter to the Council in
which he stated he is 100% in favor of the applicant’s plans for the Fell Site.
Nan Greenough, 500 Maple, disclosed that she had been the chief editor of the
Comprehensive Plan, and that the allowable height in the commercial districts
was reduced to 2 ½ stories in response to an inappropriate project that was
being envisioned at the time. She explained that the architects of the
Comprehensive Plan were aware that a planned development ordinance could grant
an exception to the height restrictions if certain conditions were met. She
observed that Winnetka has two distinct portions, a residential suburban
section, and a commercial section. She noted that, in the suburbs, the
commercial area is either a strip mall or an urban-looking enclave with a dense
and concentrated commercial area built up to the sidewalk, which Winnetka has
always preferred over a strip mall. She added that the NTP proposal is an
environmentally conscious strategy, that maximizes density in close proximity
to the train station. She noted that the presence of a
3-½ acre park a block away from the development site nullifies the open space
argument.
President Woodbury indicated that the Council is acutely aware of the public
dialog about the proposal on both sides and that all the comments will be taken
into account as the process moves forward.
3)   Executive Session.
None.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
10 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
February 17, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
February 17, 2009
(Approved: March 3, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 17, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris
Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Director of
Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community
Development Brian Norkus, and approximately 10 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
March 3 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members indicated that they
expected to attend.
March 10, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members indicated that they
expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
President Woodbury called for any corrections or additions to the draft minutes
enumerated below:
January 19, 2009, Special Study Session.
January 20, 2009, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greable made a correction to the
last page of the minutes under “Trustee Reports.”
January 27, 2009, Budget Meeting. Trustee Behles corrected a typo.
February 2, 2009, Special Study Session.
February 3, 2009, Regular Meeting. Trustee Greable made a correction to the
wording of his report on the NWMC meeting.
February 3, 2009, Budget Meeting.
February 5, 2009, Budget Meeting.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve all of the minutes
as amended. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-2-2009 – Zoning Variation: ––––1509 Scott St.–– -
Introduction. This item was tabled at the January 20 th meeting at Petitioners’
request. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to remove the item
from the table. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the revised plans which reduce the requested gross floor
area variation request by 114.22 square feet, and he noted that that no changes
had been made to the building line articulation or side yard setback variation
requests.
Mr. Seymour Turner, Airroom Architects, reviewed the changes made to the plans
since the January 20 th meeting, and maintained that a hardship is caused by:
(i) the cantilevered portions of the second floor contribute 55 to possibly 80
square feet to the GFA calculation, even though it is not useable living space;
and (ii) the side yard setbacks and articulation are existing nonconformities
and no changes can be made without requesting the variations. He observed that
the essential character of the neighborhood will not be changed, the neighbors
applaud the efforts to improve the front elevation, and that the other
standards for zoning variations have been met.
Trustee Berger commented that it seems the practical difficulties are related
to the existing structure and not the property itself, and asked if it is
reasonable to use that as a justification for granting the variation.
Attorney Janega observed that in terms of the relief being sought, there is a
reasonable connection between the sideyard setback and articulation variations
and the house’s layout and location on the lot, but the GFA argument seems to
be that the owner can’t take advantage of the detached garage floor area bonus
because of where the house is built. She said ultimately the Council must use
its discretion in making the call.
Trustee Poor said he could appreciate the point about the existing
nonconformities but that the GFA is still 8% over the allowed amount and asked
if there was a different reason for the GFA variation.
Mr. Turner replied that it comes down to amenities that are consistent with
expectations for a modern Winnetka home, and the revisions tried to get as
close as possible to trading out the existing inadequate areas of the home
while still adding the desired improvements.
President Woodbury pointed out that it is the Council’s longstanding policy to
encourage renovation, and to use discretionary allowances as a way to keep the
Village’s existing housing stock modernized, and that while this case may be
different, it was important to keep policy precedent in mind.
The applicant, John Stiffler, said his family has lived in the house for 11
years and they love the location but are looking for an opportunity to make the
home more livable for his family with three growing children.
Trustee Rintz said he had wondered what would happen if a new house were to be
built on the lot and he calculated that new construction could have a GFA of
approximately 3,671 square feet. He said he felt the directive of encouraging
renovation by granting the variations would result in a home that had less GFA
than a newly constructed home on the lot would have if it took advantage of all
the allowable GFA bonuses.
Trustee Berger agreed with the point Trustee Rintz was making, but said she
would argue that because of the lot’s length, new construction with a detached
garage would result in a different kind of bulk on the site. She said her other
concern was the practical difficulty standard, and she was hesitant to make a
decision to allow more bulk to be created, as renovation should address bulk
and mass concerns.
Trustee Poor said his concern was that a GFA variation was a different issue
than a setback variation, and he was hearing that the applicant would like more
space for optimum use of the house, which is a common situation for many older
homes in the Village. He indicated that he could see granting the side yard
setback variation, but not the variation request for more GFA, as he didn’t
think the practical difficulty standard was met.
Trustee Behles agreed with Trustee Poor, and said anything over the 55 square
feet of GFA hardship created by the cantilevers is not a practical difficulty
but is the option of the homeowner. He observed that the Zoning Ordinance is
clear that unless a lot is configured uniquely, there is no hardship or
difficulty that runs with the land.
Trustee Greable concluded that the current location is a hardship, because the
lot is small and also because it is not practical to move the garage to the
rear quarter. He noted that the proposed extra bulk is in the rear of the home
where it won’t be seen from the street and said he would be in favor of voting
for the variations.
Trustee Johnson said he could vote for the variances.
President Woodbury said he would cast the tie-breaking vote, and noted that
although there were good opinions about too much bulk on the site, he would
vote to introduce the ordinance in harmony with the Zoning Ordinance’s spirit
of encouraging renovation of existing homes.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to introduce Ordinance M-2-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
b)   Resolution No. R-3-2009 – Authorization of Two New Liquor Licenses –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that Resolution R-3-2009 would authorize two
new liquor licenses: a Class A license for Marco Roma, an existing restaurant;
and a Class D license for Acute Angle Wines, a new internet wine retailer. She
noted that both applications have been reviewed, required background checks
have cleared, and the Police Chief is satisfied that both applicants are
eligible for the license requested.
Mr. Ben Kaehler answered several questions about some particulars of his
internet business, after which Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-3-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
[Drafter’s Note: At this point in the meeting, the Council discussed Item 9(b),
after which it returned to the agenda and proceeded with the remaining items,
in order.]
c)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2009 – Prohibiting Third Party (Electric) Demand
Response Programs – Introduction. Mr. Keys reported that the Federal Energy
Regulatory Commission (FERC) has issued a ruling mandating the development of
Demand Response Programs (DRP) by regional transmission organizations (RTOs).
He explained that a DRP allows third parties to contract with customers of an
electric system who shed load, sell the corresponding demand capacity reduction
into the RTO, and share the revenue with the customer. He observed that the
utility could be required to continue purchasing capacity for all loads, even
capacity that has been sold into the RTO under the DRP, resulting in increased
costs to the community. He said the FERC ruling contains a provision allowing
municipal utilities to pass an ordinance blocking implementation of a DRP, and
that the IMEA has suggested that its members do so.
Manager Williams observed that if the Village were to explore decreasing load
demand, Winnetka, not a third party, should be in charge of the program.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-2-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
d)   Ordinance No. M-4-2009 – Disposition of Surplus Electrical Equipment –
Introduction. Mr. Keys reported that the Electric Department has 43
transformers that are deteriorated and need to be disposed of. He estimated
that the transformers will net less than $3,000, and requested the Council to
authorize the Village Manager to dispose of the equipment in the best manner,
either with or without going out for bids.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance M-4-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
a)   Warrant Lists 1593 and 1594. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant List No.
1593 in the amount of $832,912.56 and Warrant List No. 1594 in the amount of
$472,486.37. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
[Drafter’s Note: The following item was discussed after Item 6(b).]
b)   METRA Elm Street Station Renovations. Mr. Norkus reported that METRA staff
were present, to request approval of the final phase of the plans for the
reconstruction of the Elm Street train station, and to request policy direction
regarding the Village’s share of the cost of several design upgrades. He
related that in 2006, the Council approved the expenditure of $12,565 to
upgrade the roof material for the depot building from asphalt shingles to
slate. He reviewed the scope of the proposed renovations, gave the cost
estimates for upgrades on the elevator tower, roof and light fixtures for the
pedestrian bridge, and indicated that METRA has incorporated the Village’s
Streetscape-standard trash receptacles, benches and bicycle racks at no extra
cost.
Discussion followed as to various details of the renovation work, especially
the light fixtures, elevators and how to protect the stairs against corrosion
from winter salting.
Trustee Berger took issue with using streetscape elements before they have been
discussed by the Council, as the Streetscape Plan has only been approved in
concept. Trustee Poor asked when the work would begin.
Glen Peters, METRA, responded that METRA is ready to go out for bids, and
estimated that work will start within 60-90 days and should take approximately
18 months to complete. He stated that the use of salt is a safety issue and
METRA is looking for a vinyl product to put at the base of the metal to see if
it will reduce rust.
Trustee Behles observed that he has been working on the Elm Street station
improvement project for almost four years. He noted the support of METRA staff
for what the Village wants to do, and he commended them for their efforts.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, inquired about parking during the construction
period, the Zengeler Cleaner lease, and visibility and security in the
elevators. He also asked if an alarm could be put in the depot to alert
passengers that a train is pulling in, encouraged using the look-alike
alternate to the Village’s standard lighting to save money, and questioned
whether there is enough shelter space on the platform
Trustee Behles said the alternate lighting was not chosen by the DRB because
there was no sample available for them to view, and he added that the
manufacturer has worked for the Village in the past so they already have the
lighting specifications on hand. He noted that the elevators will have security
cameras, which should take care of the security and visibility problem for
elevator users.
Mr. Peters responded that the shelters are sized based on peak train boarding
and that METRA feels they are adequate to handle the peak amount of passengers.
He added that the elevators will be equipped with an emergency phone. He noted
that METRA will give the Village an opportunity to accept the pricing for the
upgrades when bids are in, and upon completion of the project METRA will bill
the Village for the extra costs.
President Woodbury indicated that METRA should proceed with the bid process and
report back to the Village once the bids are in place.
c)   Bid No. 09-002: Transformers. Mr. Keys reviewed the bid results for the
purchase and delivery of pad mount transformers for the coming year. He
explained that transformers are purchased based on their life cycle cost, which
is arrived at by evaluating the purchase cost against the amount of estimated
electrical loss for each transformer. He noted that funds for the transformer
purchase have been included in the 2009-10 budget, but he is asking the Council
to authorize a Purchase Order for $125,000 prior to actual adoption of the
budget, since the lead time before shipping is 8 to 14 weeks.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to authorize the purchase of
transformers at unit prices bid from Resco (Ermco) for single phase
transformers and from Resco (Pauwels) for three phase transformers in an amount
not to exceed $125,000. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. None.
b)   Trustees.
i)   Trustee Greable reported that: IMRF pension funding was the main topic at
the last NWMC meeting; and the Legislative Brunch will be held on Saturday
February 21 st .
ii)   Trustee Johnson reported that he attended a Chamber of Commerce meeting
and that their annual luncheon is scheduled for April 29 th .
iii)   Trustee Poor reported that Public Works Director Steve Saunders gave a
stormwater management presentation at the last Environmental & Forestry
Commission meeting and said they also discussed some ways in which Winnetkans
can support Earth Day.
c)   Attorney. None.
d)   Manager. Manager Williams said since budget presentations are completed,
the next steps for adoption of the budget would be to hold a public hearing the
first meeting in March and then adopting the budget at the second March
meeting. He indicated that the March Study Session could be used as a forum to
discuss any remaining budget issues the Council would like to focus on, and he
suggested quarterly budget reviews going forward, in case adjustments need to
be made in response to changing revenues in the current unstable economic
climate. He recommended holding off on moving forward with improvements at the
intersection of Green Bay Rd. and Winnetka Ave. and the water plant
improvements for the time being, and said Staff is moving forward with an RFP
for Streetscape design.
11)   Appointments.
President Woodbury suggested the reappointment of Mark Fuller to the Fire
Pension Board for another full term. Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to reappoint Mr. Fuller. By voice vote, the motion carried.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn into Executive
Session to discuss Pending and Probable Litigation, Board Appointments, and
Collective Bargaining pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3) and 2(c)(2) of the
Open Meetings Act. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
council adjourned into Executive session at 10:02 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:06 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:07 p.m.
            
Recording Secretary
March 3, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
March 3, 2009
(Approved: March 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
3, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and
Chris Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water &
Electric Brian Keys, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and approximately 5
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
   a)   March 10, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   March 17, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
with the exception of Trustee Johnson, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   a)   February 10, 2009, Study Session. Trustee Greable questioned figures
for sales and property tax revenues given on page 1 of the minutes. Manager
Williams indicated that Staff would watch the meeting on webcast to verify the
numbers. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the
minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   February 17, 2009, Regular Meeting. Trustee Johnson, seconded by
Trustee Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance M-2-2009 – Zoning Variation: 1509 Scott – Adoption. Attorney
Janega reviewed amendments made since introduction to the subject ordinance,
which grants zoning variations for maximum building size, side yard setback,
and building line articulation to allow construction of a pair of two-story
additions at the front and rear of the residence. She indicated that additional
recitals were inserted for findings of practical difficulty and reasonable
return to sustain granting the variance if the Council so chooses.
      There being no further comments or questions, Trustee Rintz, seconded by
Trustee Greable, moved to amend Ordinance M-2-2009 as recommended by the
Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
      Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Ordinance M-2-
2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson,
Rintz and President Woodbury. Nays: Trustees Behles, Berger and Poor. Absent:
None.
   b)   Ordinance MC-2-2009 – Prohibiting Third-Party (Electric) Demand
Response Programs – Adoption. President Woodbury announced that the subject
ordinance, introduced at the February 17 th Council Meeting, would prohibit
customers from diverting system demand to third-party demand response
aggregators.
      There being no further questions or comments, Trustee Berger, seconded by
Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-2-2009. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Ordinance M-4-2009 – Disposition of Surplus Electrical Equipment –
Adoption. President Woodbury indicated that the subject ordinance, introduced
at the February 17 th Council Meeting, authorizes the Village Manager to
dispose of surplus electrical transformers that are deteriorated or otherwise
unsuitable for further use.
      There being no further questions or comments, Trustee Johnson, seconded
by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance M-4-2009. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Resolution R-4-2009 – FY 2009-2010 Village Budget – Public Hearing &
Introduction. President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing at 7:50 p.m. Manager
Williams swore in Finance Director Ed McKee and those in the audience who
wished to testify.
      Mr. McKee summarized the substantive changes made to the proposed budget
and noted that a typical homeowner would experience an increase of $173, or 3%,
in the cost of municipal services over the previous year. He indicated that
capital projects would be discussed at next week’s Study Session, prior to
adopting the budget.
      The Council discussed the impact of the recent pension losses on future
budgets, with Trustee Greable noting that wages and benefits are 46.5% of the
budget, with pensions being 5.8% of the total. He added that 21% of the
Village’s tax levy is allocated to pensions and said it was necessary to
address the issue with the State legislature.
      Ed Woodbury commented that the Village, working with the NWMC and IML,
have been successful in pressing legislators not to pass additional funding
requirements or pension enhancements without due consideration of the
implications for municipalities, but that vigilance is needed to ensure pension
enhancements are not increased.
      After further discussion on pension funding, Trustee Berger suggested
that the Village host a pension forum and invite legislators and hold them
accountable.
      Mr. Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, said that the $500,000 budgeted for
streetscape engineering should be deferred until the economic climate improves,
noting that in previous bad times the Village delayed spending on capital
projects. He also had some questions and comments about the pension plan
situation and asked how the Village factors interest income into the budget
projections.
      President Woodbury acknowledged that in 2001 capital projects were put on
hold in response to bad markets, but that current revenues are on track and
there has not been a large shortfall from the amount budgeted. He noted that
budgeting $500,000 for streetscape is not the same thing as approving the
expenditure, and he added that if revenues fall off, the Village could
reconsider what actions to take.
      Mr. McKee explained how he estimated the interest income and pension
assumptions and reported that the investments are in a mix of vehicles so that
interest rate changes affect the funds gradually over long periods of time.
      After another brief discussion about the pensions, President Woodbury
closed the Public Hearing at 8:15 p.m.. He announced that the Council will hold
a final budget workshop at its March 10 th Study Session, and the budget will
then be considered for adoption at the March 17 th Council Meeting.
      Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce the 2009-
2010 Budget Resolution, Resolution R-4-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   e)   Updated Fee & Rate Resolutions. Mr. McKee reported that the utility
rate resolutions required a formal introduction and could be changed prior to
adoption. He indicated that the general permit fees, building and construction
fees and fire services for unincorporated areas did not require introduction,
but were on the agenda for the Council’s review prior to adoption at the next
Council meeting.
      After a brief discussion, President Woodbury reiterated that the
resolutions were subject to change before adoption, and called for a motion to
introduce the following fee and rate resolutions.
      i)   Resolution R-5-2009 – Water Rates – Introduction.
      ii)   Resolution R-6-2009 – Electric Rates – Introduction.
      iii)   Resolution R-7-2009 – Sewer Rates – Introduction.
      iv)   Resolution R-8-2009 – Refuse Rates – Introduction.
      Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce rate
Resolutions
R-5-2009 (water), R-6-2009 (electric), R-7-2009 (sewer) and R-8-2009 (refuse
collection). By voice vote, the motion carried.
      President Woodbury announced that the following three resolutions would
be adopted at the next meeting without introduction, but were on the agenda for
discussion purposes.
      v)   Resolution R-9-2009 – General Permit & License Fees – Review.
      vi)   Resolution R-10-2009 – Building, Zoning & Construction Fees –
Review. .
      vii)   Resolution R-11-2009 – Fire Service Fees – Review.
      A brief discussion followed, with some questions on R-2-2009, but no
comments on fire service fees or building, zoning and construction fees
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
   None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Warrant Lists 1595 and 1596. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1595 in the amount of $1,197,684.21 and Warrant List No. 1596 in the
amount of $361,276.48. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor
and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Private/Public Road Policy. Mr. Saunders reported that the issue of
Village participation in funding private road improvements was raised by the
residents on Trapp Lane, after the neighborhood reached unanimous agreement to
dedicate Trapp Lane to the Village. He observed that revisions to the Village’s
private street dedication policy were discussed at the November 18, 2008, Study
Session, and again at the January 19, 2009, Council Meeting.
      Mr. Saunders reviewed the current private street dedication policy and
then outlined changes that were proposed at the prior meetings. He indicated
that dedication of private streets to the public domain creates benefits that
include uniform appearance of streets, facilitating completion of
infrastructure projects, and providing a better equity of service levels
throughout the Village. He said Staff had devised amendments to the private
street policy based on Council’s concerns of focusing on streets that provide
the most public benefit and on funding considerations.
      Mr. Saunders explained three possible options for the Council’s
consideration: a) affirm the current policy; b) modify the current policy for
stormwater projects with public benefit; and c) modify the current policy to
permit 50% cost participation in dedication projects.
      Trustees Behles and Rintz commented that the third option was the most
clear and understandable, and didn’t include a lot of subjective
considerations. They asked for clarification on the issue of public benefit.
      Mr. Saunders indicated that in areas like White Oak/DeWindt, where
private and public drainage systems commingle, there is a public benefit to
pursuing drainage projects even if the streets are going to remain private,
because the larger watershed is affected. He noted that the Burke engineering
study will give a better understanding of what can be done to help with
drainage problems in the White Oak/DeWindt neighborhood.
      President Woodbury observed that the amendments to the current policy
were crafted at the direction of Council to deal with Trapp Lane so as to limit
concerns about setting precedent, and said he would like to limit the
discussion to Trapp Lane.
      Manager Williams indicated that the figures chosen for Option C were
arrived at based on Staff’s best estimate of the costs of bringing all the
private streets in Winnetka up to standard and assuming a 50/50 funding ratio
over a ten year period. He noted that the cost estimate is approximately 9% of
the street resurfacing budget, and since 10% of Winnetka’s roads are private,
the numbers dovetail.
      Trustee Poor noted the patience of the residents on Trapp Lane and said
the Village should respond to their situation with Option C.
      Trustee Behles said he thought Option C gave the most protection against
setting precedent, and it seemed a fair way for homeowners to have a discussion
about street dedication, as the terms will be understood ahead of time.
      Trustee Berger recommended holding a public hearing so the remaining
homeowners on private roads could give their views, and cautioned against
setting precedent. She remarked that if incentive is offered for maintenance of
private roads, there should also be a benefit with regard to stormwater
maintenance, and she added that she was concerned that putting in a cap on the
Village’s participation would not be fair for everyone throughout the Village.
      Trustee Johnson agreed with Trustee Berger.
      Trustee Greable indicated that he preferred to wait until the Burke study
is concluded to get an overall picture of the situation but added that he could
support Option C as a way to move forward at this time.
      After further discussion, President Woodbury said he thought the Village
owed it to the Trapp Lane residents to move forward, that the public hearing
option is not dropped, but a consensus is needed to make a decision in this
instance. He noted that the Burke study will raise many difficult issues beyond
private road dedication. He asked for a motion to authorize Staff to commence
engineering and design work on a 50/50 partnership with Trapp Lane for the
eventual dedication of Trapp Lane of a public street.
      Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to grant approval to
Staff to proceed with design and engineering to bring the roadway and storm
sewer system of Trapp Lane up to Village standards, splitting costs evenly with
Trapp Lane residents, in order to dedicate Trapp Lane as a public street. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Greable, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: Trustees Berger and Johnson. Absent: None.
      President Woodbury encouraged the next Council to continue the
discussions about private streets and to keep moving the process forward.
   c)   Electrical Line Clearance (Tree Trimming). Mr. Keys reviewed the
electric line clearance bid received last February from Nels Johnson Tree
Experts, and he explained that the bid was structured so that rates were given
for three years, with annual renewals at the Village’s discretion. He observed
that Nels Johnson has been performing the line clearance for many years and is
trained in working around energized conductors. He noted that the firm has
never experienced a safety accident while working for the Water & Electric
Department, and recommended awarding the second-year contract to Nels Johnson
for an amount not to exceed $100,000.
      There being no further questions or comments, Trustee Berger, seconded by
Trustee Behles, moved to award the contract for electric line clearance to Nels
Johnson Tree Experts in an amount not to exceed $100,000 . By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. None.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Berger reported that the Winnetka/Northfield Library Board
is dealing with a potential increase of $94,000 in IMRF funding and that the
Board is organizing a long-range planning committee to continue with their
needs analysis. She announced that she had attended the NWMC legislative brunch
with President Woodbury and Jessica Tucker, candidate for Village President.
She observed that the Village is very fortunate not to be in a deficit
position, as many of Winnetka’s neighbors are facing huge budget shortfalls,
and she said the attendees stressed to the State legislators that it is
important not to increase costs for municipalities.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams asked Mr. Saunders to report on the progress
of the Cenotaph renovation. Mr. Saunders indicated that the contractor is ready
to start when the weather breaks, possibly next week, and that neighbors are
being notified about the project. He added that the work is almost certainly
not going to be completed by Memorial Day, but the Village will work with the
Memorial Day Committee and the Park District with regard to Memorial Day
ceremonies.
11)   Appointments.
   None.
12)   Executive Session.
   Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Pending
and Probable Litigation, Board Appointments, and Collective Bargaining,
pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3) and 2(c)(2) respectively, of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor
and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive
Session at 9:40 p.m.
      The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:03 p.m. Present:
President Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz.
Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
04 p.m.
            
   Recording Secretary
March 10, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
March 10, 2009
(Approved: April 7, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
10, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:36 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and
Chris Rintz. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Finance
Director Ed McKee, and approximately 14 persons in the audience.
2)   Zoning Regulations – Phase 2 Evaluation.
   Mr. D’Onofrio reported that two previous meetings have been held on the
topic of zoning regulations and how they relate to residential renovation
projects, with the goal of making rehabilitation projects more attractive. He
said Staff had refined some of the proposals gathered at those meetings, and at
the Council’s request, had further analyzed the recommendations made by the
Village’s consultant on zoning and residential design guidelines. Mr. D’Onofrio
reviewed the recommendations in his agenda report, and the Council discussed
the recommendations either individually or in related groups.
   The Council agreed with Staff’s recommendations for Items 1, 3, 4, 9, 11, 13
and 14 in Agenda Report:
      ·   No change to GFA, attic allowance, articulation requirements, and
first floor height;
      ·   no priority given to the processing of renovation permits;
      ·   no changes made in building permit fee structures.
A majority of the Council agreed to move forward with the recommended changes
for Items 2, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 12 in the Agenda Report:
      ·   Increase RLC;
      ·   eliminate zoning relief requirements for one-for-one replacement of
legally nonconforming subordinate structures and for improvements which reduce
nonconformities;
      ·   expand the 18-inch eave maximum for calculation of RLC;
      ·   adjust side yard setbacks on properties located on improved alleys;
      ·   expand the authority of the Zoning Administrator;
      ·   and revise how average front yard setbacks are determined.
   The Council also reached consensus that Item 10 – granting waivers for
certain forestry requirements, should be referred to the Environmental &
Forestry Commission for their review and recommendation.
   Melissa Mizel, 939 Tower, said she thought some inconsistencies in the
Zoning Ordinance were overlooked in the February Study Session discussion about
the Residential Zoning Analysis. She commented that they create a “loophole”
that can result in incompatible scale and mass on substandard lots in the R-
1 and R-2 Zoning Districts and she asked the Council to include them in their
review of possible amendments to the Zoning Ordinance.
   President Woodbury responded that Staff and the Residential Design
Guidelines Committee would deal with the issue of new construction on
nonconforming lots. He said the proposed changes to the Zoning Ordinance which
had been discussed are designed to give flexibility to those who wish to
renovate their homes. He noted that the Public Hearing regarding the zoning
amendments would take place at the April 14 th Council Meeting, followed by
introduction of the code amendment.
[Drafter’s Note: At this point in the meeting, the Council discussed Item 4,
after which it returned to the agenda and proceeded with the remaining items in
order.]
3)   Preliminary Budget – Fiscal Year 2009-10.
   Mr. McKee reviewed the significant capital projects contained in the FY 2009
Budget, which includes design work for Streetscape, flood control in the Sunset
neighborhood, and rehabilitation of Village Hall. He observed that nothing is
being approved by authorizing the amounts in the budget, as Council approval is
still needed for expenditures over $25,000.
   Trustee Berger pointed out that once the money is budgeted, Staff moves
forward with plans with the understanding that the projects are agreed upon,
and that budget time is the right time for any Trustee to speak up if he or she
feels strongly that something shouldn’t be included.
   Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, said he thought the Council should reconsider
transferring money into the Streetscape fund, in light of the pension fund
problems, the uncertain outcome of the drainage study, and the future possible
redevelopment of the Post Office site.
   Linda Pedian, 510 Sheridan & Trustee candidate, said economic times have
changed since the Streetscape Committee made its recommendations two years ago,
and that she was not sure it was a pressing issue at the moment. She
recommended adding a full-time commercial liaison to the Village staff.
   Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce, asked whether the Winnetka Streetscape plan could
be done for less money, and asked why the Village’s plan costs so much more per
square foot than Glencoe’s streetscape did.
   President Woodbury responded that Glencoe took items off their Streetscape
budget and put them in other budget areas, making the total amount look
smaller, whereas the Village has all of its Streetscape elements included in
one budget account. He noted that the costs of Glencoe’s Streetscape are
comparable to Winnetka’s, but that the two can’t be fairly compared.
[Drafter’s Note: The following item was discussed after Item 2.]
4)   Zoning: Retail Overlay District.
   Mr. Norkus reviewed the intent of the C-2 Retail Overlay District,
explaining that it was created to encourage a concentration of retail uses in
the business districts while exposing them to maximum foot traffic, and also
discouraging office and other non-retail uses. He noted that the BCDC and Plan
Commission had made recommendations for revisions to the C-2 Overlay boundaries
and that the permitted list of uses was updated so as to eliminate obsolete
uses and add contemporary uses that weren’t in existence 20 years ago. He
observed that both the BCDC and Plan Commission found the 100-foot first floor
retail depth requirement to be too deep; he reviewed the changes to the uses
list; and described the proposed new boundaries to the Overlay District.
   In the ensuing discussion about the boundary changes, several Trustees
commented that it was difficult to get a sense of why the changes were
recommended, since the most recent Plan Commission minutes weren’t available
for inclusion in the agenda packet.
   Glen Weaver, 574 Lincoln, said historically a property was only eligible for
inclusion in the Overlay District if it were located across the street from
other retail uses and commented that less regulation is better for the business
community.
   Jim Sayegh, owner of Winnetka Galleria and Co-Chair of the Winnetka
Commercial Property Owners Association, observed that when the Overlay District
is applied correctly it has a positive impact and contributes to a healthy and
vibrant retail community. He indicated, however, that technical difficulties in
applying the rules uniformly have the unintended consequence of hurting retail.
He said the commercial property owners did not feel invited into the process of
examining the Overlay District, listed his concerns with the permitted uses,
and used a chart to illustrate his problem with the 100-foot retail depth
requirement on the first floor. He said he would be in favor of using square
feet for a measure of the retail on first floors, rather than a rigid
requirement for a certain depth.
   Josh Braun, owner of 818 Elm and 906-08 Green Bay Road, said the internet
has changed the business landscape and that other drastic changes have appeared
in the economy since the overlay ordinance was passed. He observed that the
special use process is time-consuming and asked the Council to take into
account the speed with which change takes place, and to make provisions to
review the ordinance on a periodic basis to keep it current with the times. He
commented that since every store is configured differently, the square footage
concept seems like a good one, and he advised keeping the ordinance flexible.
   David Humphrey, Moth Meyer, Inc., pointed out that the Overlay District’s
purpose is to concentrate foot traffic in a central location and to move
service uses into the mixed use areas at the periphery of the business
districts. He questioned the wisdom of adding properties on Green Bay Road,
since a busy main artery is not the place to be adding pedestrian traffic.
   Bob Dini, owner of 1056 Gage, observed that no owners of commercial real
estate were members of the BCDC or Plan Commission and that they did not feel
heard throughout the Overlay discussion process.
   President Woodbury said the question is whether to move forward on the issue
or to do more study.
   Trustee Poor said he supported the amendments to the list of permitted uses,
but he was not ready to make a decision on the concept of flexible square
footage vs. a 50-foot depth requirement for retail, and that without the
benefit of the Plan Commission findings, the same would hold true for the
changes to the boundaries.
   Trustee Berger said a one size fits all formula doesn’t work for everyone,
and that the issue needed to be treated thoughtfully, especially in light of
the current economic climate. She indicated that she did not think it would be
possible to make a decision on the boundaries or the retail depth provision
before the retirement of three of the Council members in May, and she added
that more could be done to clean up the list of permitted uses.
   Trustee Rintz commended Mr. Humphrey for his comments, agreed with Trustee
Berger that one size doesn’t fit all, and acknowledged that he did not
particularly like overlay districts, as the outcome frequently is not what was
hoped for. He said he did not think increasing the boundaries would have the
desired effect, and he added that a vibrant core and flexibility for property
owners are what is needed. He said the retail depth requirement is a stumbling
block, and recommended a formula for three dimensions, including square footage
or a minimum footprint, to give the property owners wider options.
   Trustee Behles said he would like to see the issue wrapped up before the new
Council is seated, that it cannot be done without the findings of the Plan
Commission, that he leans towards less regulation, and the original intent of
the ordinance was to concentrate pedestrian activities and retail uses near
each other. He observed that the retail depth question is abstract and that
small tenants need to be supported, since the Village won’t attract national
tenants.
   Trustee Johnson said he would like some more conversation on the retail
depth requirement and square footage and that he’d also like more information
on what other communities are doing.
   Trustee Greable said he could agree on the uses list but that the boundaries
discussion was confusing, and he did not understand why the Moth Meyer site was
brought in, as it’s on the periphery. He said the issue of square footage vs.
retail depth requirement needs more airing and that what was viable in 1987 is
not practical in 2009.
   President Woodbury indicated that more discussion would take place on the
issue of depth, square footage and boundaries at the council’s April 14 th
meeting.
5)   Executive Session.
   Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to Section 2(c)(11) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Rintz seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor
and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive
Session at 11:30 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:53 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
6)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
54 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
March 17, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
March 17, 2009
(Approved: April 7, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
17, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, King Poor and Chris Rintz.
Absent: Trustee Bill Johnson. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike
D’Onofrio, Finance Director Ed McKee, Water & Electric Director Brian Keys, and
approximately 22 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Quorum.
   a)   April 7, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   April 14, 2009, Special Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   a)   March 3, 2009, Regular Meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution No. R-4-2009 – 2009-10 Village Budget – Adoption. President
Woodbury observed that the Council has completed its review of the 2009-10
Budget and held a public hearing on the budget at its March 3 rd meeting. He
pointed out that the Winnetka Talk did not give the correct figures in their
March 12 th issue, and he clarified that in 2009 the average resident will pay
an increase of $86 in property taxes, $41 in electric rates and $46 in water
rates, for a total increase of $173 paid to the Village. He then called for any
questions or comments prior to adopting the FY 2009-10 Budget.
   There being none, President Woodbury announced that Finance Director Ed
McKee had won an award from the Government Finance Officers Association for
excellence in financial reporting, and he commended Mr. McKee for his hard work
in preparing the budget.
   Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adopt Resolution R-4-
2009, approving the FY 2009-10 Budget. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Johnson.
   b)   Updated Fee & Rate Resolutions. President Woodbury called for any
additional comments or questions about the various fee and rate resolutions,
which were introduced at the March 3 rd Council Meeting.
      i)   Resolution No. R-5-2009 – Water Rates – Adoption.
         Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution
R-5-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
      ii)   Resolution No. R-6-2009 – Electric Rates – Adoption. President
Woodbury announced that a technical amendment had been made to this Resolution
and he called for a motion to amend.
         Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend Resolution
R-6-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
         Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adopt Resolution
R-6-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
      iii)   Resolution No. R-7-2009 – Sewer Rates – Adoption.
         Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution
R-7-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
      iv)   Resolution No. R-8-2009 – Refuse Rates – Adoption.
         Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Resolution
R-8-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
      v)   Resolution No. R-9-2009 – General Permit & License Fees – Adoption.
         Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution
R-9-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
      vi)   Resolution No. R-10-2009 – Building, Zoning & Construction Fees –
Adoption.
         Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution
R-10-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
      vii)   Resolution No. R-11-2009 – Fire Service Fees – Adoption.
         Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-
11-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
   c)   Ordinance M-3-2009 – 941 Tower: Zoning Variation – Introduction.
President Woodbury observed that Ordinance M-3-2009 would grant front and rear
yard setback variations to allow construction of a new home on the subject
property. He noted that the request had been tabled at the January 20 th
Council meeting, and called for a motion to remove the ordinance from the
table.
   Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to remove M-3-2009 from
the table. By voice vote the motion passed.
   Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the request was tabled so the neighbors could
try to mediate their differences and the applicant could revise the plans. He
said no mediation had taken place but the plans had been revised significantly,
and included a reduction in the GFA by 244.29 square feet and the elimination
of a half story by reducing the building height by 3.24 feet.
   David Schrauth, the applicant’s attorney, said although attempts at
gathering the neighbors together for mediation had failed, the applicant did
accomplish the goal of addressing the height concerns and revising the plans so
that the new home will not cast any shadows in the windows of 939 Tower.
   John Zachar, the applicant’s architect, reviewed the new plan and details of
the exterior materials being used.
   Melissa Mizel, 939 Tower, thanked the applicant for the amendments to the
plan, but said she still felt the house is not correct for approval by the
Council, and read from a handout she had previously distributed to Council
which pointed out what she perceived to be a loophole in the Zoning Ordinance
that creates unwanted bulk in new construction on small lots.
   Philip Torf, Ms. Mizel’s attorney, reviewed his handout, distributed to the
Council, in which he listed what he believed were errors in procedure.
   James Blum, 933 Tower, said the attempt at mediation would have made it
impossible for emergency vehicles to pass down the private road.
   Julie Windsor, 1479 Tower, said the group of houses in the subdivision is
important to Winnetka, the landmark status of 939 Tower must be considered, the
impact of the demolition on the neighborhood should be discussed, and she asked
the Council to give the matter more time.
   Barbara Johnson, 935 Tower, said the real issue is the private road running
through the front yard of 941 Tower, and that the reason the current house is
not kept up is that the applicant has been trying to rebuild for several years
now. She noted that a homeowner buys a property, not a neighborhood; therefore,
considerations about neighborhood character only go so far, and she disputed
that emergency vehicles wouldn’t get through if the road were re-routed.
   Mr. Schrauth observed that the plight of the owner relates to the property,
that the applicant has shown ample evidence of hardship, and the proposed new
home complies to all zoning requirements except the two requested setback
variations.
   Attorney Janega pointed out that the Council Meeting forum is not an
administrative proceeding, but a legislative process. She noted that Illinois
law requires an ordinance be enacted when granting a zoning variation, and she
stated that the standard of review is for the objector to prove that the
legislative body erred in granting the zoning relief.
   Attorney Janega observed that the Landmark Preservation Commission is not
involved in variance applications made by properties that are not designated
landmarks, and that the Village’s voluntary landmark designation ordinance does
not provide for landmark districts, but only covers individual properties. She
indicated that there is no evidence of a restrictive covenant protecting the
neighborhood against redevelopment in the future, so that while 939 Tower is a
designated landmark, it does not control what happens on the other parcels in
the neighborhood.
   Attorney Janega said the record in this case provides plenty of proof that
the applicant has met the standards of reasonable return, practical difficulty
and particular hardship, and she added that the requirement of three front
setbacks for the subject property makes this a textbook example of unique
circumstances. She noted that the standards for building size are set by a
formula based on lot size, not zoning district, and that the Village has not
adopted any design guidelines to-date, and so the issue has no bearing on this
case. With regard to due process issues and vested rights, she said land use
regulation is the balancing of property owners’ rights, and in this case the
impact on the owner outweighs the other interests, as there is no conforming
buildable area and the current house is not habitable.
   President Woodbury commented that the matter before the Council is a zoning
matter, notwithstanding that it is a hotly contested issue, and that the ZBA
took extra care to gather all the facts and the Council has done the same. He
asked the Council for their thoughts on the application.
   Trustee Poor said he was impressed by the applicant’s efforts to address the
issues of height and bulk and he commended her attempts to compromise with the
neighbors. He noted that he couldn’t recall another case where there had been
more process for a zoning variation request, and indicated that he could
approve the amended request.
   Trustee Berger agreed with Trustee Poor’s comments and said she also has
never seen so much attention given to a zoning case before, that the standards
have been met, and she would be in favor of granting the variations.
   Trustee Rintz agreed with the preceding comments relative to mitigating the
impact of the new building on the neighborhood, and said the proposal is
compliant in all other respects, although he would have liked to have seen
better design. He pointed out that the whole process would not have been
necessary except for the loophole in the Zoning Ordinance that views this
particular driveway as a private road, and said he was inclined to support the
request.
   Trustee Behles sympathized with the historic argument and the uniqueness of
the neighborhood, but he noted that he had never seen a piece of property more
deserving of a zoning variation and therefore would vote in favor of the
request.
   Trustee Greable said his concerns with the original height and mass of the
proposal had been addressed, and that since the hardship test has been met, he
would vote in favor of the request.
   President Woodbury announced that the ordinance would be up for adoption at
the April 7 th Council meeting. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles,
moved to introduce Ordinance M-3-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   d)   Ordinance M-5-2009 – Approving Riverton as an IMEA Member –
Introduction. Mr. Keys reported that the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency is
requesting its members to approve the petition of the Village of Riverton to
become a member of the IMEA, as approval of a majority of the existing members
is required to grant the application.
   Manager Williams indicated that most Illinois municipalities with electric
systems belong to the IMEA because there are huge economies of scale in joining
together to purchase power, and that although Riverton is very small, it is a
good thing for IMEA members to approve its membership application.
   Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce Ordinance M-5-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
   None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Wine Bar. Attorney Janega reported that the Village has received a
request from a property owner in Hubbard Woods about opening a wine bar which
would serve light food such as appetizers and desserts, but that currently the
Village does not have a classification for this type of liquor license. She
observed that the Police Chief had stated he had no concerns about approving a
wine bar, and commented that it might revitalized the business districts. She
indicated that sidewalk service is also requested along with the wine bar
classification, and asked for direction from the Council about whether it would
like to amend the Liquor Ordinance.
   Manager Williams observed that the issue of liquor without food service has
been evolving since the 1980’s when the Village approved its first liquor
ordinance. He said the service of food is required to prevent something like a
sports bar from opening, and that the issue is the challenges faced by business
owners in the current economic climate.
   Trustee Greable said when he was Chair of the Winnetka Caucus in 2005, a
question was drafted for the survey about amending the Liquor Ordinance to
serve alcohol without full meals, which the community rejected by a 2-to-
1 margin.
   Katherine Dellin, owner of 930 Green Bay, said wine bars are generally more
attractive than bars, clarified that the food would not be prepared on the
premises, and added that the menu would likely consist of appetizers, desserts,
wine and coffee.
   Irma Villarreal, 1045 Westmore, said she recently moved to Winnetka from San
Francisco, where wine bars are quite popular, and she indicated that they are
typically attractive and draw a more upscale clientele than regular bars do.
She added that they don’t usually have meals, but serve snacks to accompany the
wine.
   President Woodbury said no decision would be made at this point without an
actual application to review, but asked the Council for their thoughts on the
matter.
   Trustee Berger said the idea of a “bar” signifies a particular idea that
doesn’t apply to wine bars and that she thought it would be a good fit for
Winnetka.
   Trustee Poor said he would be reluctant to take this step without more input
from the Caucus, since there seems to be significant sentiment on the part of
residents on the issue.
   Trustee Behles agreed with Trustee Berger and said the Caucus question did
not speak to the issue as the word “bar” doesn’t bring to mind a $10 glass of
wine, and that not only is a wine bar not in the same league as a road house,
wine bars exist in many other municipalities and add vitality to the business
districts.
   Trustee Rintz agreed with Trustees Behles and Berger, and said the use
described in the agenda report is not the bar he voted “no” to in the 2005
Caucus Survey. He commented that the Council has an obligation to be visionary,
look to the future, and keep an open mind to new uses when they present
themselves. He observed that while the Caucus is helpful in wholesale changes
in policy, this just a minor step and he’d hate to lose the use to ’’Wilmette’
or Glencoe while waiting for a two year Caucus Questionnaire process to play
out.
   Trustee Greable reiterated his thoughts about the 2005 Caucus Survey and
said Winnetka is a family-oriented environment and the issue of alcohol
expansion is one the community has deep feelings about, and he would want more
community input.
   President Woodbury said with three Trustees in favor, two disinclined and
one absent, if a tie resulted he would cast the tiebreaking vote, and he
suggested moving forward with an application for the Council to consider.
   b)   Warrant Lists 1597 and 1598. Trustee Greable reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1597 in the amount of $809,917.51 and Warrant List No. 1598 in the
amount of $396,200.96. The motion was seconded by Trustee Berger. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
   c)   Annual Outdoor Seating Permits & Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License
Riders. Manager Williams reported that ten restaurants had applied for outdoor
seating permits from the Village, and four restaurants had also applied for
outdoor liquor license riders. He indicated that the completed applications
included insurance certificates, which were approved by Finance Director Ed
McKee, and also a sketch of the table layouts, approved by Public Works
Director Steve Saunders
   Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to approve the 2009
Outdoor Seating Permits. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Berger, Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to approve the four
applications for outdoor liquor license riders. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Johnson.
   d)   Cable Bid. The subject bid was tabled for further review by Staff.
   e)   Lead Service Replacement Bid. Mr. Keys reported that during the last
year the Village replaced approximately 20 lead water services, using multiple
contractors via competing bids on a project-by-project basis. He said since the
work is recurring, Staff has prepared a bid document to secure a contractor on
an annual basis. After reviewing the bid results, he recommended authorizing a
purchase order to Biagi Plumbing at the proposed unit rates in an amount not to
exceed $80,000.
   After a brief discussion, Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved
to authorize the Village Manager to execute a Purchase Order to Biagi Plumbing
at the proposed rates in an amount not to exceed $80,000. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Poor and Rintz.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Woodbury announced that Section 17.72.040
(C) of the Zoning Ordinance provides for public hearings on Zoning Ordinance
amendments, held by a public body designated by the Village Council. He called
for a motion to designate the Village Council, sitting as a Committee of the
Whole, to hear the proposed amendments to the single family residential bulk
regulations and setting the hearing for Tuesday, April 7, 2009, during the
regular Village Council Meeting.
   Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to so designate the
Council as Committee of the Whole and to set the hearing date for April 7 th .
By voice vote, the motion carried.
   President Woodbury also announced that the proposed amendments to the C-
2 Overlay District will be studied further before the Council determines the
extent of any amendments or sets a hearing date.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Poor reported that Village Forester Jim Stier gave a
presentation to the Environmental & Forestry Commission about the functions of
the forestry department and also updated them about the Emerald Ash Borer
situation. He said the Commission is working on a brochure called Going Green
Winnetka, which gives pointers on how to recycle almost anything. He encouraged
Winnetkans to observe Earth Hour on March 28 th by turning off their lights for
one hour beginning at 8:30 p.m.
      ii)   Trustee Greable said he attended the Historical Society meeting on
March 9 th , and announced that their gala is scheduled for June, and he added
that the Society is in the process of creating a DVD about Winnetka’s history.
         Trustee Greable also announced that the NWMC will hold its Legislative
Days in Springfield on April 21 & 22, and encouraged his colleagues to attend
and express their concerns to legislators.
      iii)   Trustee Berger said the Red Center subcommittee has met twice in
the last two months, and that the budget will be presented to the Board on
April 1 st .
         Trustee Berger also reported that at the Library Board’s last meeting
a Long Range Planning Committee was formed, with the aim of finishing up the
work of the needs analysis and deciding how to move forward from there.
   c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega announced that Friday, March 27 th , she
would be moderating a seminar for municipal attorneys.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams gave an update on the efforts Staff is making
to apply for stimulus funds.
11)   Appointments.
   President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Chuck Dowding to the
Environmental & Forestry Commission for a full term, effective May 1 st .
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to so appoint Mr. Dowding. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
12)   Executive Session.
   Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Pending
and Probable Litigation and Board Appointments and setting the price for the
lease of Village Property, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(C)(3) and 2(c)(6),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 10:04 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:22 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Poor, and Rintz. Absent: Trustee
Johnson. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Johnson. The meeting adjourned at
10:23 p.m.
            
   Recording Secretary
March 31, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
March 31, 2009
(Approved: April 28, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
31, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present:
Trustees Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris Rintz.
Absent: Trustee Behles. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike
D’Onofrio, Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus, and
approximately 24 persons in the audience.
2)   Zoning: C-2 Retail Overlay District.
   Mr. D’Onofrio reported that at its March 10 th Study Session discussion
about the C-2 overlay district, the Council directed that staff return, at a
later meeting after the February Plan Commission minutes are released, with an
analysis of overlay district regulations in neighboring communities.
   Mr. D’Onofrio discussed how the overlay regulations of Highland Park,
Wilmette and Lake Forest compare with Winnetka’s overlay district guidelines,
and observed that Winnetka’s overlay is the least restrictive of the other
communities that use them. He indicated that most communities limit the
businesses that can be in the overlay, either through capping non-retail uses
at a 10% maximum of all commercial space, or through outright prohibition of
uses. He commented that the Lake Forest 10% maximum has an equity problem, with
the first 10% of non-retail getting in and every other non-retail needing a
special use permit after that, and he also noted that the program is difficult
to administer.
   Mr. D’Onofrio said the Winnetka Commercial Property Owner’s Association
(WCPOA) proposes the use of a retail standard based on square footage and
minimum width. He noted that they recommend using a width of 20 feet and a
minimum GFA of 1,200 square feet, instead of the requirement that retail be
placed in the first 50 or 60 feet from the street, as recommended by the Plan
Commission and BCDC, respectively. He indicated that an unintended consequence
of the square foot standard could be problems with parking, and the likelihood
that ground floor spaces will get divided up into small, shallow retail spaces,
which would be hard to reconvert to larger spaces in the future.
   President Woodbury said the issues to be discussed are: (i) uses; (ii) the
Overlay’s boundaries; and (iii) the first-floor depth vs. the square footage
proposal. He suggested trying to create consensus by discussing each issue
separately, with each Trustee specifying whether he or she agreed with the BCDC
and Plan Commission recommendations, while also raising any questions or
concerns. He suggested reviewing the uses first.
   After a brief discussion, the Council agreed that the Plan Commission’s
recommended amendments to the uses in the C-2 Overlay were acceptable.
   President Woodbury suggested a discussion about the boundaries next,
starting with Hubbard Woods, and reminded the Trustees that Trustee Behles had
written a memo with his thoughts.
   Trustee Berger agreed with the Plan Commission’s recommendations for the
Amoco/Tower Rd. corner, and also agreed with Trustee Behles’ written comment
that he did not endorse the Plan Commission’s recommendation for Gage Street,
saying she also preferred to remove Gage Street from the overlay, as it does
not seem to fit in with the original intent of the overlay concept.
   Trustees Greable, Poor and Johnson felt that Gage Street should be kept in
the C-2 Overlay, as the area has a natural pedestrian feel to it.
   Trustee Rintz said he did not feel strongly either way and could go with the
majority on the issue.
   President Woodbury said the issue did not have to be settled right away, and
encouraged the Trustees to visit the area and continue the discussion at a
later date.
   Attorney Janega verified that the Council agreed with the Plan Commission on
the Amoco/Tower Road area and also the Merrill Street recommendations, and that
there was a split on the Gage Street recommendation.
   After a discussion of the proposals for the Elm Street business districts,
the consensus was to accept the recommendations to extend the boundaries to
include the southwest and northwest corners of Elm and Green Bay and the north
side of Spruce from Green Bay on the west to the edge of the Humphrey property
on the east. There was not full agreement about the need to include an area
north of Elm along Green Bay Road, and also whether to wrap the overlay around
on Oak west of Chestnut, and the Council was split on the issue of whether to
accept the Plan Commission’s recommendations for the Grand Foods site, with
four Trustees, including Trustee Behles in his written comments, rejecting the
Plan Commission’s recommendation, and two agreeing with it.
   When the discussion turned to depth there was agreement that the Plan
Commission’s recommendation for a 50-foot retail depth should be accepted, and
Trustee Greable agreed with the majority, even though he expressed reservations
with the reduction from 100 feet. The Trustees voiced concern with the square
footage concept because it could lead to creation of unusual building
configurations, potentially add to parking problems, and would be difficult to
administer. Trustee Rintz suggested working out an alternate rule for courtyard
buildings, as they have a natural break point at the halfway mark.
   Jim Sayegh, WCPOA co-chair and 570 Lincoln, said economics is the driving
factor for what will fill a commercial space and illustrated his support for
the idea of a square footage component to the overlay regulations. He
distributed a handout with his comments, which are attached to these minutes.
   Robert Dini, WCPOA co-chair, asked the Council to consider excluding east
and west Gage Street from the overlay, as he did not think the demand for
retail existed in those areas, and he also commented that the WCPOA was not
represented at the meetings dealing with the C-2 Overlay
   Glen Weaver, 574 Lincoln, said he believed the overlay is an obstruction and
reduces economic value in the Village.
   David Humphrey, Mothmeyer and 434 Willow, agreed that his property is a
transitional space and if the north half is added to the overlay, the
transition space is lost, and winds up dispersing the foot traffic instead of
concentrating it.
   Nan Greenough, 500 Maple, agreed with the Plan Commission’s recommendation
for the Grand Foods property to improve the synergy on Spruce Street and
commented that the square footage concept would lead to chaos.
   Maureen Mitchell, 460 Elder and Chair of the Plan Commission, assured the
Council that the Plan Commission takes seriously the importance of a thorough
process that allows members of the public to participate and articulate their
views, and that those views are then taken into consideration. She said the
recommendations were made after a lengthy process had been thoroughly
exhausted, and that no member of the community was shortchanged.
   Louise Holland, 545 Oak and Plan Commission member, said the overlay is
important to the vitality of the Village because it maintains the ambiance of
the community. She noted that the Plan Commission is well aware of the
pressures landlords feel in the face of changing times and that steps were
recommended to make renting commercial space easier. She indicated that the
Hubbard Woods merchants felt that eliminating Gage Street from the overlay
would make Green Bay Road a tunnel, and that the already problematic parking on
Gage will only be made worse if it is taken out of the overlay.
   Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry, after announcing that the Cenotaph repairs are under
way and that Meakin Poor will be the Memorial Day speaker, said Bratschi
Plumbing came to Winnetka in 1939 and is not currently in the overlay and he
would like to keep it that way. He said the business people are struggling and
anything the Village can do to support them would be in the best interest of
the community.
   Arthur Braun, 850 Bell Lane and BCDC member, said the people who own the
commercial buildings are speaking about what their needs are, and that they are
asking for flexibility in these difficult economic times.
   Cicely Michalak, 351 Ridge, Executive Director of the Winnetka Chamber of
Commerce and BCDC member, observed that there were commercial property owners,
as well as business owners, who were very involved in the overlay review,
explained the rational for the recommendation to include the Grand Food site in
the overlay and encouraged the Council to keep Gage Street in the overlay’s
boundary.
   President Woodbury observed that the overlay assessment is made more
difficult because it has been 20 years since it was last looked at, and he
encouraged subsequent Councils not to let a decade or more elapse before
undertaking another examination of the overlay. He said one of the goals of the
review is to emphasize flexibility, that keeping the status quo is not a viable
option, and there would be a public hearing on the suggested revisions at the
April 21 st Council meeting.
3)   Executive Session.
   None.
4)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Behles. The meeting adjourned at
10:10 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
April 7, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
April 7, 2009
(Approved: April 28, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
7, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and
Chris Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Acting Village Manager Steve Saunders,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike
D’Onofrio, Finance Director ’’Ed McKee’, Water & Electric Director ’’Brian
Keys’, and approximately 37 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   President Woodbury announced that Item 6(a), the zoning variation for 941
Tower, has been deferred to the April 21st agenda. He noted that this
rescheduling is solely to allow ample time tonight for public comment and
discussion on the Fell Property redevelopment ordinance. He stated clearly that
it does not indicate that the Council is thinking of not granting the variance.
4)   Quorum.
   a)   April 14, 2009, Special Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   April 21, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Berger, indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   April 28, 2009, Special Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   a)   March 10, 2009, Study Session. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   March 17, 2009, Regular Meeting. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Berger, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-3-2009 – Zoning Variations: 941 Tower – Adoption. As
noted earlier, this item was removed from tonight’s Agenda and has been
rescheduled for the April 21 st meeting.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-6-2009 – Fell Site Planned Development, Consideration
of Preliminary Approval – Introduction. President Woodbury announced that the
Council would hear first from the Village Attorney and Mr. D’Onofrio, who would
review the draft ordinance and respond to questions from the Council. Then New
Trier Partners would have an opportunity to speak, followed by the neighbors
who have filed an objection, and the audience. Finally, the discussion would
return to the Council to provide further comment and direction.
   Attorney Janega explained that the proposed ordinance, which will govern the
preliminary approval of the Fell Site Planned Development, can been divided
into three categories. Section 1 incorporates the recitals, which will contain
the procedural history and the Council’s view of the factual record. Section 2
contains the Council’s specific findings necessary to support the modifications
and exceptions. Sections 3 to 13 grant the preliminary approval and set out the
terms and conditions for final approval.
   She reviewed the ordinance and pointed out that it is still in draft form
and is therefore subject to further modification to reflect the Council’s
discussions. She noted that it is common in complex zoning matters for an
ordinance to be introduced with some matters unresolved. Ms. Janega emphasized
that this ordinance is not the final granting of anything and that the scope of
the approval does not go beyond what was specifically put before the Council.
   Attorney Janega added that certain provisions, in particular Section 6
enumerating the conditions for final approval, such as streetscape
improvements, parking, first floor commercial space, and affordable housing ,
reflect agreements reached with the Developer as the result of extensive
negotiations. These conditions for final approval are locked in with the
preliminary approval. She thanked President Woodbury and Trustee Rintz for
lending their expertise to these negotiations, and also thanked Mr. Elrod and
his client for their cooperation.
   In reviewing Section 6, Ms. Janega noted the three substantive provisions
that remain as key issues for New Trier Partners: (1) the limit on the size of
first floor retail space; (2) the terms of the affordable housing requirement;
and (3) the performance security requirement.
   After an extended discussion about the various conditions, President
Woodbury reiterated that these conditions, particularly the retail space, had
been arrived at through a lengthy negotiation process during which the Village
sought to provide some flexibility to the Developer while retaining some level
of control over the development.
   Steve Elrod, attorney for New Trier Partners, then spoke on behalf of his
client. He pointed out that NTP has not made any changes to the plans since the
Council was polled three weeks ago at which time there was a favorable
consensus. He added that the proposed project is a planned development in
accordance with the Planned Development Ordinance that allows for special
procedures, modifications, and exceptions to strict enforcement of the Zoning
Code. Mr. Elrod went on to note that NTP began to meet with staff and
commissions more than a year ago and commented that during this time, this
project has undergone more intense public scrutiny than any other of which he
is aware. Finally, he remarked that the ordinance before the Council is
extremely protective of the Village and includes all of the substantive
provisions that were required by the Village and acquiesced to by NTP. He
expressed his hope that the Council would introduce the ordinance tonight and
approve it in its final form as soon as possible.
   Following Mr. Elrod’s remarks, Frank Petrek spoke on behalf of the objectors
at 711 Oak St., citing deficiencies and non-compliance issues that he believes
should preclude the ordinance from being introduced.
   Others who spoke against introduction of the ordinance included: Edie
Howell, 277 Ridge; Ann Wilder and Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce Street; Cindy Brady,
615 Spruce Street; Bill Patterson and Nancy Jaffee, 511 Cedar Street; Xerxes
Bhote, 599 Maple Street; Michael Levitin, 507 Cedar; Kevin O’Hara, 520 Maple;
Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka Ave.; Bob Dearborn, 585 Arbor Vitae, Marcia Oley,
363 Sunset; Rebecca Petrek, 711 Oak; and Vicki Apatoff, 730 Ardsley Rd.
   Jim Sayegh, Winnetka Galleria, and Nan Greenough, 500 Maple St., spoke in
favor of moving forward with the ordinance.
   Mr. Walter Sobel, the architect of the existing Fell building, and his son,
Richard Sobel, spoke in support of preserving, renovating, and greening the
existing Fell Store building and volunteered their services toward such an
endeavor.
   Before turning to the Trustees for their comments, President Woodbury
responded to some of the questions and ideas raised by the audience. He opined
that although the community is averse to change, sometimes change is a good
thing, and said that after more than a year of process, it is time for the
Council to do something to move forward.
   Trustee Behles indicated his support for the draft ordinance, saying that it
contains all of the substantive issues and does what the Village wants it to
do. He promised to do everything possible to ensure that it is as perfect as
possible before it is adopted, but reminded the audience that this is just the
preliminary approval stage. He voiced his belief that the project fits Winnetka
and will result in a development of which Winnetka will be proud.
   Trustee Poor disagreed. He indicated that while he is in favor of
revitalizing East Elm, he is not in favor of achieving that goal by approving
the tallest building in Winnetka. He asked the Council to pause and reconsider
and not approve a plan that includes the fourth floor.
   Trustee Berger stated that she believes the Village should move forward with
preliminary approval.
   Trustee Rintz agreed, saying that the public benefit items are exactly as
they have always been discussed and the Village is getting significant public
benefit. He said he believes this to be the right project for Winnetka at the
right time. He pointed out that whether the building is three stories or four
stories, development of the site will still be making one building out of three
existing buildings.
   Trustees Johnson and Greable concurred. Trustee Johnson pointed out that
although no taxpayer money will be contributed to the project, the downtown and
the community as a whole will ultimately benefit substantially from the
developer’s investment. Trustee Greable added that in his view the process has
been thorough and complete and resulted in a development that is contextually
correct where it is located.
   In his concluding remarks, President Woodbury expressed the hope that
everyone would recognize what a difficult issue this has been for the Trustees,
who have the best interests of Winnetka at heart, and have given careful
consideration to the matter. He added that consensus does not always mean
unanimity.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-6-2009, granting preliminary approval for the Fell Site Planned Development.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Behles, and Rintz. Nays: Trustee Poor. Absent: None.
   President Woodbury advised the audience that the final draft of the
ordinance would be published on the website in the next ten days and will be up
for adoption on April 28 th .
   The Council took a brief break at 11:30 p.m.
   After the break, President Woodbury announced that with the exception of the
Warrant Lists, all of the remaining Agenda items were procedural in nature.
Therefore, given the lateness of the hour, should there be any questions from
the Council, discussion would be postponed to another meeting.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-5-2009 – Approving Riverton as an IMEA Member –
Adoption. There being no additional comments or questions from the Council
regarding this matter, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to
adopt Ordinance No. M-5-2009, approving the petition of the Village of Riverton
to become a member of the Illinois Municipal Electric Agency. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Greable, Johnson,
Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Resolution No. R-13-2009 – Release of Executive Session Minutes –
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that this is a routine housekeeping item.
The ordinance covers the Executive Session minutes from October 7, 2008 through
March 17, 2009, and noted that twelve sets of minutes are not being released
due to continuing litigation matters.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-13-2009,
authorizing the release of Executive Session minutes no longer required for
reasons of confidentiality and permit the destruction of certain audio
recordings of closed meetings from April 3, 2007 through September 11, 2007. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Rintz, Berger, Poor,
Greable, Johnson, and Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   e)   Resolution No. R-15-2009 – American Recovery and Reinvestment Act
(Federal Stimulus) Funding - ––––Tower Road–– Resurfacing – Adoption. Mr.
Saunders explained that Staff has identified one project likely to qualify for
stimulus funding, the resurfacing of Tower Road between Hibbard and Sheridan,
omitting the Green Bay intersection and the Tower Road Bridge. In order to
secure this funding, the Village is required to pass a resolution authorizing
expenditure of the local match for the project.
   Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Resolution R-15-
2009, authorizing the expenditure of $105,000 for the local match to resurface
Tower Road from Hibbard Road to Sheridan Road using federal stimulus funding.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Rintz, Berger,
Poor, Greable, and Johnson. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
   None.
8)   Old Business.
   None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1599 and 1600. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1599 in the amount of $1,194,281.02 and Warrant List No. 1600 in the
amount of $517,571.14. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor
and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Bid No. 09-004: Cable Bids. Mr. Keys review the bids for cable during
the timeframe of April 1, 2009 through March 31, 2010.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award Bid #09-004 to
Okonite for the purchase of 15kV primary cable at the unit prices bid in an
amount not to exceed $387,000 and to Wesco for the purchase of 600V secondary
cable at the unit prices bid in an amount not to exceed $137,000, subject to
all of the conditions stated in the request for bid. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Rintz, Berger, Poor, Greable, Johnson, and
Behles. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   2009 Parkway Tree Trimming, Removal, and Maintenance Bids. Mr. Saunders
reviewed the bids for parkway tree trimming, removal, and maintenance.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles moved to award tree trimming and
maintenance contracts at the unit rates contained in bid proposals received
February 10, 2009, as follows:
      (1)   Parkway Tree & Stump Removal to Trees “R” Us
      (2)   Parkway Tree Trimming to R.W. Hendrickson
      (3)   Emergency Time & Material Work to Landscape Concepts Management
   By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Greable,
Johnson, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   2009 Parkway Tree Planting Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for
parkway tree planting.
      Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to award the bid for the
2009 Parkway Tree Planting Program jointly to St. Aubin’s, Fox Ridge, and
Alaniz based on the lower price per tree species as submitted in their
respective unit price bid proposals, for an estimated amount of $41,363. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Greable, Johnson,
Behles, Rintz, and Berger. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Woodbury reminded the audience of the
public hearing to be held on general zoning changes next Tuesday, April 14 th
and the public hearing on C2 Overlay on April 21st.
   b)   Trustees. None.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None
11)   Appointments.
   None.
12)   Executive Session.
   Due to the lateness of the hour, no Executive Session was held.
13)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
54 p.m.
            
   Recording Secretary
April 14, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
April 14, 2009
(Approved: April 28, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
14, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and
Chris Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Planning Assistant Jill Morgan, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and
approximately 20 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   The Trustees approved the agenda.
4)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance MC-3-2009 – Zoning Regulations: Renovation of Existing Homes
– Public Hearing and Introduction . President Woodbury announced that the
subject ordinance would amend portions of the Zoning Ordinance to promote
renovation of residential properties. He indicated that if the ordinance is
introduced, it will be up for adoption at the April 21 st Council meeting.
   Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the history of the zoning review process, and
presented an analysis of the following proposed amendments to the Zoning
Ordinance:
      ·   Amendments to Section 17.30.030, “Intensity of Use of Lot,”
increasing the maximum RLC for homes constructed prior to February 7, 1989, in
the R-4 and R-5 single family zoning districts and establishing a 200 square-
foot RLC allowance for detached garages located in the rear quarter;
      ·   Amendment to Section 17.60, “Variations,” to expand the authority of
the Zoning Administrator to include jurisdiction over: (i) one-for-one
replacement of legal non-conforming accessory structures or principle
structures where the new construction will reduce the nonconformity or have the
same footprint; and (ii) over garage door width for attached and front-facing
doors;
      ·   Amendment to Section 17.30.130, “Obstructions in Required Yards or
Courts,” to increase the portion of eaves and other ornamental elements that
can intrude into any required yard setback, from 18 to 24 inches;
      ·   Amendment to Section 17.30.060, “Side Yard Setbacks,” to allow
flexibility in cases where properties are located on an improved alley, and
adjusting the minimum required side yard setback for lots with a width of more
than 60 and less than 100 feet;
      ·   Amendment to Section 17.30.050; Front and Corner Yard Setbacks,” to
prevent exceedingly disparate front setbacks between adjoining properties by
reducing the maximum average setback requirement by five feet.
   Attorney Janega explained that the ordinance would need some revisions
before adoption, and the revised ordinance would be distributed to the Council.
   The Trustees discussed the zoning amendments with Mr. D’Onofrio and Attorney
Janega and clarified several points in the draft ordinance.
   Michael Levitin, 507 Cedar, commended the Council for making the effort to
assist people who want to remodel their homes.
   President Woodbury observed that the outstanding zoning questions for the
next Council to deal with are articulation, forestry requirements, and fee
waivers; and then he closed the public hearing.
   Trustee Poor said he thought the changes were positive overall, but he was
not convinced that a 2% increase in RLC will tilt the decision towards
renovating and away from tearing down. He indicated that he couldn’t support
such a speculative incentive, as it means less green space and probably more
impermeable surface, and he added that it is contrary to what the 2007 Caucus
platform had in mind.
   Trustee Berger indicated that she thought all the amendments were good, and
commented that the change in RLC with regard to eaves is only for pre-FAR
houses, not new construction. She noted that it will relieve some of the time
and expense to go through a variance request, as such requests almost always
get granted.
   Trustee Rintz agreed with Trustee Berger and said it is aesthetically more
sensitive to have more RLC to work with when remodeling an older home, as it
gives people the flexibility they need to do the job right, from a design
perspective.
   Trustee Behles said he thought encouraging people to take a look at their
existing property and create better design would be accomplished with the
proposed amendments, and that they create a bigger distinction between new
construction and rehabilitation of existing housing stock.
   Trustee Johnson said he agreed with the proposed amendments, and commented
that it was a positive approach for renovating older homes, without being
negative towards new construction
   Trustee Greable indicated that he was in favor of the amendments overall,
although he would have liked to see more than a 2% increase in RLC, as it would
have a greater impact on the R4 and R5 Zoning Districts, which are the
districts most impacted by the teardown issue. He asked when the Winter & Co.
final report will be finished.
   Trustee Rintz said the Residential Design Guidelines Committee had reviewed
a draft of the report at their last meeting, and the text was fairly complete
but that more graphics were needed.
   President Woodbury observed that the consensus is to move forward to create
flexibility for existing homes, and even though the changes seem small, they
will have a good effect. He indicated that he shared Trustee Poor’s concerns
about RLC relative to new construction, as there is a tradeoff with green space
and possible stormwater issues, and he announced that the ordinance would be up
for adoption on April 21 st .
   Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to introduce MC-3-2009,
amending the Zoning Ordinance. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Ordinance M-7-2009 – Special Use Permit: ––––492 Hill Rd.––
(––––Indian– ––Hill– ––Park––) – Introduction. President Woodbury noted that
the Park District has asked the Council to waive introduction of the subject
ordinance and proceed directly to adoption.
   Mr. D’Onofrio read a petition received from neighbors who are against the
requested special use permit. He then reviewed the request for a special use
permit to renovate the playground, and noting that although the proposed
improvements would enlarge the existing playground, the current use of the
playground would not be changed. He observed that the proposal complies with
all zoning bulk, lot coverage and setback requirements, and that it has been
approved by the ZBA, Plan Commission and DRB.
   Liza McElroy, Winnetka Park District Director, introduced Bob Smith, the
Superintendent of Parks, Rich Schram, the District’s landscape architect, and
Carolyn Kurtz, Park Board President. She said the District is upgrading its
play areas, as playground design has changed significantly since the last
improvements were made ten years ago. She described the meetings that had been
held throughout the course of the special use application process, and observed
that the proposal had been given approval by the ZBA, DRB and Plan Commission.
   Mr. Smith reviewed the Park District’s criteria for replacing playgrounds,
which include the age and condition of the area, ADA and Consumer Safety
Commission guidelines, and play opportunities appropriate for each age group.
   Mr. Schram reviewed the current conditions at the park and reviewed the
proposed new design.
   Ms. Kurtz maintained that the Park Board had been thorough and prudent in
approving the proposal, and had listened carefully to neighbors who supported
the plan, as well as those who opposed it. She said an alternate proposal put
forth by a neighbor had been considered, but the Board’s risk management firm
had preferred the original plan, and she stood by the Board’s decision to
proceed with the proposal currently before the Council.
   The Council discussed the proposal with the applicants, including the effect
the expansion will have on the ice skating rink, security concerns, and tree
replacement.
   Terry Duffy, 159 Bertling, said he attended the meetings about the proposed
renovation and expansion of the playground, and he commented that the meeting
announcements had not made any mention of an expansion, only that the park was
to be renovated. He said a renovation would be wonderful, but that the new
playlot should be relocated so as not to interfere with the ice rink and
playing fields, and he asked the Council to delay approving the application so
as to give the neighbors more time for discussions with the Park Board.
   Greg Robitaille, 145 Bertling, said the neighbors are asking for
alternatives to be considered, echoed Mr. Duffy’s concerns and also asked that
approval be delayed.
   Robin Vandermolen, 553 Elder, said she had also attended several meetings
about the plan, and that she was in favor of the proposal because it
accommodates the parents who have young children as well as older ones. She
commented that the proposed placement gives parents a good view of, and easy
access to, all the playlots, and she felt the proposal did not take anything
away from the playfield.
   Patty Buck, 477 Elder, agreed with Ms. Vandermolen, and said the
neighborhood around the park has a large population of small children, whose
families approve the proposal as is. She said she attended the Park District’s
meetings and disagreed that alternatives were not considered, and she commented
that the Board was diligent in following due process.
   Kim Melancon, 255 Poplar, said the Park District did consider alternative
options, and commented that the ice skating season is only 4-6 weeks out of the
year.
   Kelly Smith, 552 Hawthorn Lane, agreed with the comments made by the
previous three speakers.
   Ms. Kurtz said she appreciated all the comments and the interaction with
neighbors and that the Park Board listened and did the best it could. She asked
the Council to waive introduction of the ordinance so that work could get
started when the weather warms up.
   President Woodbury asked why the new tot lot can’t be moved to the north,
keeping the same triangular shape, in order to make everyone happy?
   Mr. Smith said the idea was considered, but there is a large grade change,
and it moves the tot lot closer to the street.
   President Woodbury informed the Council that their options are to approve
the plan as proposed, table it as the residents urged, or ask for amendments to
the ordinance.
   After a short discussion, the Trustees concluded that it would do no harm to
pause and go back and try to work out a compromise with the dissenting
neighbors.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to table introduction of
Ordinance No. M-7-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   c)   Resolution R-2-2009 – Intergovernmental Agreement Regarding the Martin
Luther King, Jr. Monument – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed the Resolution
authorizing the execution of an intergovernmental agreement, included in the
agenda packet, that would divide the balance due on the Martin Luther King,
Jr., monument on the Village Green, and allocating the ongoing maintenance
costs among the Village, the Park District, and School District 36.
   Trustee Behles said he wanted to publicly recognize the contractor on the
project, Ray Sanchez, as he has been waiting patiently for two years to be paid
the remainder of the fees on the project.
   Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-2-
2009, authorizing the intergovernmental agreement between the Village, Park
District and School Dist. 36 for ongoing maintenance of the Martin Luther King,
Jr., monument on the Village Green, substantially in the form attached. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Ordinance M-8-2009 – Landmark Designation: ––––507 Cedar Street–– –
Introduction. Ms. Morgan summarized the Landmark Preservation Commission’s
recommendation to designate 507 Cedar as a local landmark, noting that its
prairie style architecture and prominent location on the Village Green earned
the house a rating of “Unique.”
   Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to introduce Ordinance M-8-
2009, granting local landmark status to 507 Cedar Street. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
   e)   Ordinance M-9-2009 – Zoning Variations: ––––1161 Spruce Street–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the request for corner yard setback and
front-facing garage variations in order to construct an addition to the
attached garage, which would convert the south part of the existing garage into
finished interior space and to add a one-car addition to the north end,
resulting in a corner setback variation of 3.56 feet, and a front-facing garage
variation of 2.58 feet. He indicated that the ZBA had approved the request at
its March 9 th meeting.
   Mr. D’Onofrio described the proposed addition and verified for the Trustees
that under the proposed zoning amendments discussed earlier, a variation
request such as this would fall under the jurisdiction of the Zoning
Administrator.
   The applicant, James Ingle, and his architect, Mark Verbryck, explained the
difficulties they had faced when trying to design a conforming alternative, and
described the proposed design as minimizing the degree of nonconformity.
   Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to waive introduction of
the ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Ordinance M-9-2009.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. . Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   f)   Resolution R-12-2009 – MFT Resurfacing of Various Streets – Adoption.
Mr. Saunders reported that various streets have been scheduled for resurfacing
in 2009, and that MFT funds would be used to fund the improvements. He noted
that the expenditure must be authorized by Resolution, and that the cost was
not expected to exceed $200,000.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-12-
2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   g)   Resolution R-14-2009 – State Highway Work – Adoption. Mr. Saunders
reported that the Village occasionally needs to repair roadways under the
jurisdiction of IDOT, and that permits for such work require performance
security, which municipalities are permitted to provide in the form of a
resolution rather than a bond. He asked the Council to consider adopting the
subject resolution providing the State of Illinois performance security in
order to secure a Highway Permit for 2009 and 2010.
   Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Resolution R-14-
2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   h)   Resolution R-16-2009 – Extension of Sidewalk Replacement Contract with
Schroeder & Schroeder, Inc., – Adoption. Mr. Saunders reported that in 2007 the
Village entered into a sidewalk replacement contract with Schroeder &
Schroeder, Inc. containing a provision for three one-year extensions, with the
rates tied to the Chicagoland CPI of each year. He observed that the contract
was extended in 2008 at an increase of 4.7%, and he recommended extending the
contract for an additional year at the current prices.
   Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Resolution R-16-
2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger,
Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   New Business.
   a)   One Year Extension of Yard Waste Composting Contract with Thelen Sand &
Gravel. Mr. Saunders reported that Illinois law requires yard waste to be
composted rather than disposed of in landfills, and therefore the Village has
contracted with Thelen to haul yard waste from a transfer site on the Village
landfill. He observed that the operation has gone very will over the life of
the current contract, and recommended extending the contract for another year
at the current pricing.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to extend the contract
with Thelen Sand & Gravel. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
None
   b)   Outdoor Seating Permit: Love Always Cafe. Manager Williams reported
that a new business at 745 Elm Street has applied for an outdoor seating permit
and that the Village Engineer has revised the applicant’s seating plan to allow
enough room for pedestrians to pass.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the
application for outdoor seating for Love Always Cafe. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Poor gave a brief report about the Earth Day brochure that the
Forestry & Environmental Committee has put together, which will be distributed
at various Village events.
   Trustee Johnson reported that the Winnetka Chamber of Commerce awards
luncheon will take place on April 29 th and that Trustee Ken Behles is the Man
of the Year. He also announced that the dates of the Sidewalk Sale will be July
17 and 18.
6)   Executive Session.
   Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Pending
and Probable Litigation, Board Appointments, Lease of Real Estate and
collective bargaining, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(3), 2(c)(6) and 2(c)
(2) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Behles seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 10:06 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:40 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
7)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
41 p.m.
            
Recording Secretary
April 21, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
April 21, 2009
(Approved: May 5, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
21, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and Chris Rintz.
Absent: Trustee Sandra Berger. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and
approximately 30 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   The Trustees approved the agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
   a)   April 28, 2009, Special Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   May 5, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2009 – C2 Overlay – Public Hearing & Introduction.
President Woodbury opened the Public Hearing on the ordinance that would amend
portions of the Village Code as it pertains to the C-2 Retail Overlay District.
This matter was discussed at the March 10th and March 31st Study Sessions.
   Mr. D’Onofrio provided background on this matter. He reviewed the historical
basis for adoption of the Overlay District in 1987, its purpose and goals, the
rationale for it, how the various elements of the District’s regulations work
together to achieve its goals, and the process that brought the Council to the
current discussion.
   Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the Overlay District was created to preserve
the retail character of the business districts by encouraging the retailing of
goods in a comparison shopping environment, to provide a wide variety of retail
shops, and to expose such shops to a maximum of foot traffic in concentrated
channels or areas. He noted that the effectiveness of the District as a means
of encouraging a healthy and vital business district is dictated by the
boundaries of the district, the uses permitted “by right,” and the underlying
standards for compliance. He then explained these components.
   Mr. D’Onofrio then reported staff’s finding that ’’’’Winnetka’’’s retail
overlay district is the least restrictive of neighboring communities studied.
   Mr. D’Onofrio went on to review the proposed modifications to allowed uses,
overlay district boundaries, and other standards, noting where the
recommendations of the Business Community Development Commission and the Plan
Commission differed.
   Mr. D’Onofrio responded to Council’s request at the March 31 st meeting that
staff study the question of whether it might be advisable to have a specific
retail depth criteria for courtyard buildings. He also reported staff’s
evaluation of the Winnetka Commercial Property Owners Association’s (WCPOA)
suggested alternative to the proposed 50-foot overlay depth and its request for
relief from the Special Use Permit process. Regarding the latter, he expressed
concern that the WCPOA proposal could shift the primary focus of the District
and diminish its retail identity, and observed it could also leave the
commercial business districts with only a veneer of retail uses. Regarding
special use procedures, Mr. D’Onofrio recommended that staff begin to examine
ways in which the process might be streamlined to expedite requests for certain
non-retail uses, rather than going through the current Special Use process
which can take three to six months.
   Trustee Behles remarked that he believes that the lengthy processing time
issue is the real burden that the Council has heard from the property owners,
adding that in this regard, what is good for the property owners is ultimately
good for the Village.
   President Woodbury then moved to public comment.
   Julie Windsor, 911 Green Bay Road, spoke in favor of keeping Gage Street in
the Overlay District in order to make small store fronts available for emerging
businesses.
   Robert Dini, 1056 Gage, who owns property on the south side of Gage Street,
east of Green Bay, strongly urged the Council to remove that area from the C2
Overlay, except for the required depth from the Green Bay corner.
   David Humphrey, Moth Meyer, Inc., argued strongly against including any
portion of the Grand Foods property in the Overlay District, saying that the
property meets none of the criteria for inclusion and appropriately belongs in
the transition zone and mixed use area. He pointed out that given the size of
the Grand Foods property, any future redevelopment would fall under the Planned
Development Ordinance.
   Jim Sayegh, 570 Lincoln, spoke on behalf of the WCPOA and clarified their
proposal.
   Terry Deason, 1436 Scott, read a statement from Cicely Michalak, Executive
Director of the Winnetka Chamber of Commerce and member of the BCDC, and
encouraged the Council to make a decision tonight.
   President Woodbury closed the public hearing and remarked that this process
has taken three years of study, in part because it has been 20 years since the
overlay was last considered. He responded to Mr. Sayegh’s comments and offered
to facilitate a meeting with staff to better understand the WCPOA proposals. He
went on to explain some of the limitations and constraints within which the
Council must operate. Mr. Woodbury pointed out that the ordinance as drafted is
only up for introduction at this meeting and may be amended before adoption. He
then polled the Trustees for their positions on the proposed changes reflected
in the draft ordinance.
   There was unanimous agreement among the Trustees to accept all of the
proposed modifications to permitted uses.
   Regarding the map and overlay boundaries, Mr. Woodbury noted that the
Council has been split over the inclusion of Gage Street and the Grand Foods
block in the Overlay District and stated that unless there is a clear consensus
among the Trustees present, these matters will remain open until all six
Trustees can be heard.
   Trustee Johnson read a statement from the Business Community Development
Commission supporting retaining Gage Street in the Overlay District, adding the
two bank properties at the corner of Elm and Green Bay Roads, extending the
Overlay District to the north side of Spruce Street and up the west side of
Green Bay for a distance of approximately 186 ft., going north.
   Trustee Poor concurred with the conclusions of the BCDC, Plan Commission and
Chamber of Commerce that Gage Street is part of an overall cluster of natural
retail and should remain within the overlay. He agreed with Mr. Humphrey that
the Grand Foods property should not be part of the retail Overlay District. He
observed that there was a reason it was originally excluded and said he hasn’t
heard any compelling reasons to change that. He indicated that he would vote
against its inclusion.
   Trustee Rintz agreed with Trustee Poor on both matters.
   Trustee Behles reiterated his comments that he believes that Gage Street
beyond the Green Bay Road overlay setback should be pulled out of the Overlay
District, stating that he views this space as transitional. He pointed out that
not being in the Overlay District does not mean that the space cannot be used
for retail purposes. Pertaining to Grand Foods, Mr. Behles indicated that he
agrees with Mr. Humphrey but that a case could also be made for including the
Spruce Street edge as part of a retail hub in the future, albeit not in its
present form. He indicated that he could support either removing the property
entirely from the Overlay District or including just the southern setback along
Spruce Street, without extending it north along Green Bay. He also remarked
that he sees no reason to extend the Overlay to include the bank frontages
along Green Bay Rd. beyond 50 feet from the corner.
   Trustee Johnson said that he favors keeping Gage Street in the Overlay
District and thinks that it is a reasonable compromise to extend the Overlay to
include the Spruce Street frontage of the Grand Foods property, extending north
along Green Bay Rd.
   Trustee Greable supported leaving Gage Street in the Overlay District and
including the Spruce Street edge of the Grand Foods property. He said he could
go either way on coming north along Green Bay.
   President Woodbury recapped the Trustees’ various comments on outstanding
map and boundary questions and directed the Village Attorney to prepare a
revised draft with alternative provisions for the Council to review. He then
called for a motion to introduce the ordinance. Trustee Behles, seconded by
Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-4-2009. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2009 –Zoning Regulations: Renovation of Existing
Homes – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed proposed amendments drafted in
response to Council’s discussion at the April 14th meeting. After a brief
discussion, Trustee Behles suggested further revision to the minimum side yard
setback provisions. It was agreed to bring the ordinance back for adoption at
the April 28th meeting after the suggested revisions were incorporated into the
draft.
   c)   Ordinance M-3-2009 – 941 Tower: Zoning Variation – Adoption. President
Woodbury called for any additional comments regarding Ordinance M-3-2009 that
would grant front and rear yard setback variations to allow construction of a
new home on the subject property.
   Melissa Mizel and Michael Edwards, 939 Tower Rd., each argued against
adoption of the ordinance, citing precedent and perceived process
improprieties.
   Julie Windsor, 1479 Tower Rd., and Ann Hosbein, 1026 Fisher Crescent, also
urged the Council not to grant the ordinance.
   Mr. Woodbury responded to remarks made by the objectors before turning to
the Trustees for their comments.
   Trustee Poor briefly repeated his previous observation that he cannot
remember any zoning case that has had as much process as this one. He remarked
that as a direct result of the Council’s decision not to approve the proposed
project in January, the proposal was significantly reduced in scope to the
benefit of the neighbors and noted that the Council had encouraged them to seek
mediation to resolve the issue. He indicated that he would vote to adopt the
ordinance as it now stands.
   Trustee Johnson expressed his preference for the five neighbors working this
out among themselves, but stated that since they have not done this, the
Council would have to decide. He then commented on the uniqueness of the
property and voiced his support for the ordinance. Next he moved to adopt
Ordinance M-3-2009 granting front and rear setback variations to allow
construction of a new home at 941 Tower Road. Trustee Behles seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Behles,
Rintz, Poor, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger.
   d)   Ordinance No. M-8-2009 - Landmark Designation: 507 Cedar St. –
Adoption. There being no further comments on this matter from the audience or
the Trustees, Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt
Ordinance M-8-2009, designating 507 Cedar Street as a local landmark. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Rintz, Poor,
Greable, and Johnson. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger.
   e)   Ordinance No. M-11-2009 – Post Office Lease – Introduction. Trustee
Poor reviewed the tentative terms of the new lease negotiated with the U. S.
Postal Service for the lease of the Post Office site. He summarized significant
provisions and noted that it extends the Post Office’s ability to stay in the
current space while providing flexibility and economic benefit to the Village.
He recommended approval.
   Trustees Greable and Johnson voiced their support for the proposed lease and
expressed their thanks to Trustee Poor and staff for their efforts to negotiate
the agreement.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
11-2009, approving and authorizing the execution of a lease with the United
States Postal Service. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
   Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry Street, reported on the status of the Cenotaph
project.
8)   Old Business.
   None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Warrant Lists 1601 and 1602. Trustee Johnson reviewed the Warrant
Lists. After commenting on a number of the items, he moved to approve Warrant
List No. 1601 in the amount of $1,152,883.96 and Warrant List No. 1602 in the
amount of $273,940.56. The motion was seconded by Trustee Behles. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Greable, Johnson, Poor and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger.
   b)   Landmark Preservation Commission and Building Review Committee
Analysis. Manager Williams reviewed staff’s evaluation of the advantages and
disadvantages of combining the duties of the Landmark Preservation Commission
(LPC) and the Building Review Committee (BRC) into one committee, and reported
staff’s opinion that the advantages outweigh the disadvantages. He added that
merging two closely related, but yet separate functions, into one committee is
not new for the Village and emphasized that there would be no change in the
scope of responsibilities, but rather a combining of the two missions.
   Attorney Janega observed that this could be achieved by expanding the duties
of the LPC to absorb those currently performed by the BRC. She emphasized that
this would not impact the existing Landmark Preservation Ordinance.
   Trustee Behles expressed support for combining the functions and that he
believes the appropriate forum for building review is the Landmark Preservation
Committee where the expertise resides.
   Trustee Rintz concurred, saying that when serving the Village it is
important to have some relevance and meaning to the work that one is doing.
   There was a consensus to direct the Village Attorney to prepare an ordinance
for review at the next meeting.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Woodbury announced that he and President-
elect Tucker had attended a Mayors Summit with Representative Mark Kirk and had
a good conversation about the 10 th Congressional District. He also reported
that he had a private meeting with State Senator Schoenberg and they had a
productive conversation about State pensions and other legislation affecting
the Village. He encouraged all Winnetkans to pay attention to what is happening
beyond the Village’s borders and to understand how it impacts Winnetka.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Poor reported on the recent successful distribution of the
Environmental and Forestry Commission recycling brochure and announced that
copies can be obtained in Village Hall. He also commented on the Park
District’s successful open burn in Bell Woods and Crow Island parks and
observed that this would benefit the restoration of both areas.
      ii)   Trustee Behles encouraged the audience to visit the Village Green
to observe the reconstruction of the Cenotaph. He announced that the Village
hopes to have the project completed by the Fourth of July.
   c)   Attorney. Ms. Janega advised the Council that the Village is
participating in an amicus brief in support of Chicago and Oak Park on appeal
in the National Rifle Association’s suit challenging local regulation of
handguns.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams reminded Winnetkans that next week the
Village will be doing annual spring clean up. He urged residents to have their
materials for pick up out over the weekend. He also reported that a recent
study shows that Winnetka has the third lowest water rate in relation to our
neighbors, and that our sewer rates are 47% below the average.
11)   Appointments.
   None.
12)   Executive Session.
   Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Pending
and Probable Litigation and Personnel Matters, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11),
and 2(c)(1), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greable
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Behles, Johnson, Greable, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger.
The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:33 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:33 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Johnson, Greable, Poor, and Rintz. Absent: Trustee
Berger. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Johnson, Greable,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Berger. The meeting adjourned at
11:34 p.m.
               
   Recording Secretary
April 28, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
April 28, 2009
(Approved: May 5, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
28, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and
Chris Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and approximately 40 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   The Trustees approved the agenda. President Woodbury welcomed Boy Scout
Troop 20 to the meeting.
4)   Village Council Minutes.
   a)   March 31, 2009, Special Study Session. Trustee Johnson, seconded by
Trustee Berger, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
   b)   April 7, 2009, Regular Council Meeting. Trustee Poor, seconded by
Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
   c)   April 14, 2009, Special Meeting. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to approve the minutes. By voice vote, the motion carried.
5)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance M-6-2009 – Fell Site Planned Development: Consideration of
Preliminary Approval – Adoption. President Woodbury announced that the Fell
Site Planned Development discussion would start with Village Attorney Katherine
Janega’s review of the amendments that have been made to the ordinance since
introduction at the April 7 th Council meeting, followed by comments from the
applicant, the objectors’ attorney, audience comments, and finally, discussion
by the Council.
   Attorney Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the subject ordinance,
and reported that a letter had arrived late in the afternoon from the
objectors’ attorney, stating their reasons for opposing the ordinance.
   Attorney Janega answered questions from the Council about the draft
ordinance, including the financing provisions, streetscape improvements, height
issue, concerns with the length of the construction timeline, underground
parking access for future redevelopment on the corner properties, and timing
and contents of the development agreement. She also confirmed the overall
reduction in the size, and that the four zoning exceptions originally requested
had been reduced to two. At the end of the discussion, a majority felt that the
ordinance left the Village well protected.
   Steven Elrod, the applicant’s attorney, commented that the process is a
zoning process, not a judicial proceeding, and that although his clients
haven’t always agreed with decisions by Staff, the Council, and the subordinate
boards, they had respected their decisions and abided by them. He pointed out
that 28 of the 33 Plan Commission findings had been in favor of the
development, and that NTP had taken the five opposing findings and addressed
them through reductions in size, height, and scaling back the fourth floor. He
expressed the applicant’s hope that the Council will make a positive
determination, he agreed that the Council is the final arbiter of the process,
and disputed the points raised by the objectors. He indicated that he was
authorized by his clients to accept all of the conditions that have been
imposed in the ordinance, and that NTP will build a development that will make
Winnetka proud. He then introduced Bernie Sergesketter, a former Plan
Commission member, who wished to endorse the proposed redevelopment.
   Frank Petrek, attorney for the objectors, objected to Mr. Sergesketter
speaking out of order.
   Bernie Sergesketter, 475 Ash, said he was a past chairman of the Plan
Commission and that the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, was approved during
his tenure. He expressed support for the Fell Site Redevelopment proposal,
citing the importance of maintaining a vital, healthy tax base and convenient
shopping for residents while also providing an opportunity for long-time
residents to downsize and stay in the Village. He indicated that the guidelines
put in place by the Plan Commission were intended to be applied with
flexibility, so that residents would get the design elements they desire.
   President Woodbury then asked the applicant to address the concerns about
the construction timeline.
   Mr. Elrod stated that this question is not usually dealt with until the
final stages of negotiating a development agreement and that NTP has attempted
to create a realistic schedule based on the time needed to obtain financing,
construction loans and proceed with the actual construction.
   Michael Klein, a New Trier Partners principal, said the 30-month duration of
the building permit was arrived at based upon the projected 6 to 12 months
needed to complete the final development agreement and a 24-month construction
period. He indicated that NTP does not expect to break ground until late 2010.
   Frank Petrek, 711 Oak and attorney for the objectors, asserted that Chapter
17.58 of the Village Code controls the proceedings, and that he thought the
matter needed to be referred back to the Plan Commission to consider the
amendments that have been made to the plan since it’s been before the Council.
He also objected to the finding in the amended Ordinance that states the
Objectors did not offer any evidence to support their objection, saying that
they relied on the findings of the Plan Commission.
   The following audience members spoke in support of the project: Robin
Doerge, 1 Golf Lane; Catherine Embree, 475 Rosewood; Mack Hines; Bill Ryan,
1174 Spruce; Ted Widen, 389 Ridge; Margaret Lamason, 962 Pine; Michelle
McKenna, 148 DeWindt; Angie Campolo, 419 Chestnut; Tracy Lullo, 1099 Merrill;
Cicely Clarke Michalak, 351 Ridge and executive director of the Chamber of
Commerce; Richard Kates 1326 Tower; Peter Skalski, 611 Ash; Joe Szokol, 976
Sunset; Charles Thompson, 808 Sunset; Mark Dziersk, 680 Elder; Emery Koller 597
Provident; Paul Sheridan, 69 Abbottsford; Bill Lytton, 642 Ash; and Bill
Lamotte, 596 Locust.
   Those speaking against the proposal were: Michael Levitin, 507 Cedar;
Richard Sobol, Walter Sobol, the architect of the Fell building; Jane Dearborn
585 Arbor Vitae; Nancy Jaffe, 511 Cedar; Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce; Rebecca
Petrek, 711 Oak, also spoke in opposition and asked that the fourth floor on
the south side be set back as it is on the other three sides.
   Trustee-elect Jeni Spinney, 280 Sheridan, thanked the Council for their time
and the younger audience members for attending and participating, and said she
looked forward to serving on the Council for the next two years.
   Louise Holland, 545 Oak and Board president of the Hadley School on Elm,
said she hoped the development agreement would preserve access to the Hadley
School during construction, since non-sighted people use Elm Street to commute
from the train and bus.
   President Woodbury said the Council has carefully considered all of the
issues, including architecture, height, bulk, public benefits and public
comments. He noted that comments for and against have been about even and that
the Council has tried to integrate the public’s concerns into the ordinance. He
then asked Attorney Janega to give her opinion on the question of whether the
Council has the authority to adopt the ordinance granting preliminary approval
of the planned development.
   Attorney Janega answered unequivocally, yes.
   President Woodbury called for a motion to amend the ordinance as set out in
the agenda packet, so the Council would have a clear draft to work with.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to amend Ordinance M-6-
2009, as proposed. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   Trustee Poor proposed two amendments to the draft: (i) to strike a recital
on p. 21 of the draft ordinance that states the Objectors did not offer any
expert testimony or evidence in support of their opinions, as he felt it
diluted their objections; and (ii) to strike a recital on p. 24 of the draft
ordinance stating that the changes made to the Applicant’s proposal adequately
address and resolve the concerns that led the Plan Commission to recommend
denial of preliminary approval.
   President Woodbury suggested discussing each proposed amendment separately,
and he called for a motion on the first amendment proposed by Trustee Poor.
   Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend the ordinance to
strike the third to last recital on p. 21 of the draft ordinance pertaining to
the Objector’s lack of evidence or testimony.
   Trustee Rintz asked whether the recital isn’t merely a statement of fact
which did not diminish the Objectors’ testimony.
   Attorney Janega said the recital was inserted because of case law that
revealed issues with public participation in a special use permit request. She
indicated that the purpose of the finding was to provide a record in the
Council’s legislative process to indicate what the records of the subordinate
bodies contain, and she added that it is simply a statement of fact that there
was no expert evidence presented in support of the Objectors’ opinions. She
noted that striking the recital could imply that the Objectors may not have
been given an opportunity to present evidence, whereas they were given
opportunity at every level in the planned development application process.
   President Woodbury called for a vote on the motion to strike the third to
last recital on p. 21 of the draft ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion
failed. Ayes: Trustee Poor. Nays: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson and
Rintz. Absent: None.
   Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to strike the second to last
recital on p. 24 of the draft ordinance stating that the amended plan addresses
the Plan Commission’s concerns.
   Trustee Behles noted that the recital is important, but to avoid
misinterpretation the Council needs to have clear authorship of the finding.
After a brief discussion, a majority agreed to retain the finding, but to
modify it slightly in order to clarify that the Council has the authority to
determine that the amended plan addresses the Plan Commission’s stated concerns
and the amended plan is sufficient to overrule the Plan Commission’s
recommendation.
   President Woodbury called for a vote on the motion to strike the second to
last recital on p. 24 of the draft ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion
failed. Ayes: Trustee Poor. Nays: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson and
Rintz. Absent: None.
   Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend the second to
last recital, deleting the words “that led the Plan Commission to recommend
that preliminary approval be denied.” By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson and Rintz. Nays: Trustee Poor.
Absent: None.
   President Woodbury said it was now time for Council discussion of the
amended ordinance up for adoption.
   Trustee Berger indicated that she did not see the need for any further
amendments, and that she was in favor of moving forward.
   Trustee Poor said he was against the ordinance for the reasons he stated on
April 7 th , at introduction.
   Trustee Rintz pointed out that the application process is still ongoing, and
that he was hopeful the applicant would continue to refine his plan during the
development agreement negotiations, particularly with respect to the fourth
floor setback along the southern elevation. He noted that the significant
public benefit of affordable housing helped him to accept the mass and height
of the proposal.
   Trustee Behles indicated that not only has the building changed
significantly since the matter arrived before the Council, but the public
benefits have grown as well, and he would vote for preliminary approval.
   Trustee Johnson noted that the building on the north side of Elm is 42 feet
high, while the ridge line of the proposed new building is 41.8. He indicated
that the new structure will have the feel of a three story building and said he
was in favor of granting preliminary approval.
   Trustee Greable observed that the affordable housing component was worthy of
support, especially for older residents who want to downsize and stay in town.
He said he loved Winnetka and would never make a decision that he didn’t feel
was right for the whole Village, and that he supported the project.
   President Woodbury thanked the Trustees for the time they have given to the
application and called for a motion to adopt Ordinance M-6-2009.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Ordinance M-6-2009
as amended, granting preliminary approval to the Fell Site Planned Development.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, and Rintz. Nays: Trustee Poor. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance MC-3-2009 – Zoning Regulations: Renovation of Existing Homes
– Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that the approval of the subject ordinance
was continued at the Council’s April 21 st meeting, in order to clarify the
language regarding minimum side yard setbacks on lots of 60 feet or less that
are adjacent to an improved alley.
   Trustee Berger said she would like to go on record as saying that the
Council hasn’t yet gotten to where it hoped to go, which was to look at what is
causing excess bulk, particularly with new construction.
   President Woodbury observed that the Residential Design Guidelines were a
necessary first step in that regard.
   Trustee Poor said he was in favor of all the proposed zoning changes
contained in the ordinance, with the exception of the 2% increase in roofed lot
coverage, because he saw it as a speculative measure that may not encourage
renovation, but will surely decrease green space and increase impermeable
surface.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to amend Ordinance MC-3-
2009, as proposed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote the motion carried.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-3-
2009 as revised, amending the Zoning Ordinance as it pertains to renovation of
existing homes. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Johnson, and Rintz. Nays: Trustee Poor. Absent: None.
   c)   Ordinance M-11-2009 – Post Office Lease – Adoption. President Woodbury
thanked Trustee Poor for his work in negotiating the new lease with the U.S.
Post Office. Trustee Poor noted that the rent will increase from $7,000 per
year to $191,000 per year and gives the parties the right to terminate the
lease on one year’s notice beginning in 2010.
   Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adopt Ordinance M-11-
2009, authorizing a new lease with the USPS at 512 Chestnut Street. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson,
Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Ordinance MC-5-2009 – Combining Landmark Preservation Commission &
Building Review Committee – Introduction. Attorney Janega reported that, at
President Woodbury’s request, Staff has evaluated the pros and cons of
combining the duties of the Landmark Preservation Commission and the Building
Review Committee. She reviewed the draft ordinance and explained that while it
does not change the Landmark Preservation ordinance, it transfers the
demolition review process from the Building Review Committee (BRC) to the
Landmark Preservation Commission (LPC). She added that the Zoning Board of
Appeals would take over appeals of building permit decisions.
   President Woodbury suggested that the terms of members be shortened from
five years to three, and informed Louise Holland, the chair of the LPC, that
the change could be effective as of July 1 st .
   Manager Williams confirmed that the term of service will be amended, and
that July 1 will be the effective date.
   Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance
MC-5-2009, amending the Village Code to provide for eliminating the BRC and
transferring its duties to the LPC. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   New Business.
   a)   New Backhoe/Loader – PW 47 Replacement. Mr. Saunders explained that the
budget contains funds for the replacement of a backhoe to replace a current
machine that is reaching the end of its useful life. He indicated that a
suitable replacement has been located with pricing from both the State of
Illinois joint purchasing program and the GSA. After reviewing the comparative
pricing under both purchasing programs, he recommended awarding a purchase
order to Westside Tractor Sales of Wauconda for the purchase of a John Deere
410J Backhoe/Loader for $115.607, pursuant to the State of Illinois Joint
Purchasing Program, Contract #401556JD.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to award the purchase order
as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles,
Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Streetscape Engineering Services Contract. Mr. Saunders reported that,
at Council’s direction, staff had issued an RFP for engineering services to
develop Streetscape final construction plans, specifications and estimates for
the improvements identified in the Implementation Plan which the Council
approved last December. He observed that two responses were received from
Ciorba Group and SpaceCo, both of which have a demonstrated track record of
successfully completing municipal streetscape projects, and of working on
projects for the Village in the past.
   After reviewing the two responses received and the criteria used to evaluate
them, he recommended awarding a contract to Ciorba Group, Inc., for Streetscape
Engineering Services in an amount not to exceed $234,114.
   Mr. Saunders observed that the contract does not cover the construction
phase, which will require additional field engineering work, and that $500,000
is budgeted for engineering through the end of construction. He noted that
approval of the engineering contract does not authorize construction funding,
and assured the Council that if the Council does not approve construction next
year, the engineering report will still be useful for several years to come.
   Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, said it was his opinion that the approval for
engineering should be delayed until next year, and he also questioned why the
RFP was not advertised, but was sent to only four firms, with only two
responses to choose from. He also commented on the prior use of The Lakota
Group for the master plan design work, stating that a more qualified and less
expensive firm had been available.
   Mr. Saunders replied that it is common practice to pre-qualify firms,
especially for engineering work, and that the Village needs the job done
correctly and with minimal disruption to the business community. He observed
that the engineering firms were free to pick their design partner, and he noted
that both firms had successfully completed work in Winnetka in the past and
that their knowledge of the Village’s culture was necessary in this instance.
   After a brief discussion, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved
to award the contract as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None
7)   Reports.
   None.
8)   Appointments.
   President Woodbury suggested the appointment of Dean Egerter to the BCDC for
a full term, effective immediately. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Behles, moved to appoint Mr. Egerter to the BCDC for a full term. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
9)   Executive Session.
   Trustee Berger moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters pursuant to Section 2(c)(1) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Behles seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:08 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:28 p.m. Present: President
Woodbury, Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor, and Rintz. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
10)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Berger, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Behles, Berger, Greable,
Johnson, Poor and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
30 p.m.
               
   Recording Secretary
May 5, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
May 5, 2009
(Approved: June 16, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
5, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Woodbury called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present:
Trustees Ken Behles, Sandra Berger, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor and
Chris Rintz. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Water & Electric Director ’’Brian Keys’,
President-elect Jessica Tucker, Trustees-elect Jennifer Spinney and Linda
Pedian and approximately 40 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Woodbury led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   The Trustees approved the Amended Agenda as distributed on the dais.
4)   Quorum.
   a)   May 12, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
Trustee-elect Pedian, indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   May 19, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   a)   April 21, 2009, Regular Meeting. Trustees Johnson and Behles suggested
corrections. Trustee Behles, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the
minutes, as corrected. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   April 28, 2009, Special Meeting. Trustees Greable and Rintz suggested
corrections. Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the
minutes as corrected. By voice vote, the motion carried.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2009 – Zoning Retail Overlay District – Adoption.
Attorney Janega reviewed revisions pointing out that the amended Zoning
Ordinance will define the Overlay District as the first 50 feet of lot depth..
She noted that the Council must determine the boundaries of the Overlay
tonight.
   Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the two areas left to be defined are the two
bank properties at Green Bay and Elm and the Grand Foods property, and guided
the Trustees in discussing the various options.
   Bob Dini, 1056 Gage Street, and Jim Sayegh, President of the Winnetka
Commercial Property Owners Association, thanked the Council for their time and
effort devoted to considering this matter. Mr. Sayegh urged the Council to
continue to explore ways to expedite the special use permit process.
   Bob Humphrey, owner of Grand Foods, also thanked the Council for the time
they have devoted to this problem and reiterated his position that the property
should be left out of the Overlay District.
   President Woodbury commented that staff had met with Mr. Sayegh and
concluded that the changes submitted by the WCPOA are beyond the scope of the
public notice for the pending amendments and therefore cannot be acted upon
tonight. He added that he directed staff to study the square footage approach
suggested by Mr. Sayegh, test the proposal out in various configurations
throughout the business districts, and report back to the new Council for
further discussion. He also asked staff to come back to the Council with some
suggestions for shortening the Village’s special use permit process. He then
turned to discussion of the two outstanding boundary questions outlined by Mr.
D’Onofrio.
   With regard to the two bank properties at Green Bay and Elm, Trustees
Behles, Greable, Rintz, and Berger indicated they would not support extending
the Overlay District north on Green Bay beyond the fifty foot depth along Elm
Street.
   Trustees Johnson and Poor disagreed.
   President Woodbury noted the consensus for excluding those areas.
   Trustees Poor and Berger voiced support for leaving the Grand Foods property
in its entirety out of the Overlay District, based upon the belief that this
property does not satisfy the criteria for inclusion in the Overlay District.
   Trustees Rintz and Behles expressed a preference for excluding the existing
buildings and those portions of the property facing Green Bay Road, but favored
including the north side of Spruce, which would preserve the current operation,
but send a message about what is desired to future developers.
   After further discussion, Trustee Greable also supported this option, saying
that it would hold the corner of Green Bay and Spruce for future retail
development. He commented on the thoroughness of the Council’s Overlay District
discussions and urged the Council to decide.
   Trustee Johnson favored including both the Spruce Street and the Green Bay
Road frontage of the Grand Food property in the Overlay District, but indicated
that he would support the Spruce Street frontage, with current buildings
excluded.
   President Woodbury asked the Village Attorney to repeat the boundaries
agreed upon and then called for a motion to amend Ordinance No. MC-4-2009
accordingly. Trustee Behles moved to amend the Ordinance to reflect the Overlay
District as depicted in Option 6 of the agenda report and as noted by the
Village Attorney, and to make other amendments as outlined by the Village
Attorney in her presentation. Trustee Johnson seconded the motion. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
   Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-4-
2009, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Johnson, Behles, Rintz, and Berger. Nays: Trustee Poor. Absent: None.
   President Woodbury thanked the Business Community Development Commission and
the Plan Commission for their efforts, and added that keeping the overlay
current is important to the vitality of the Village’s business districts.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-7-2009 – Special Use Permit: ––––492 Hill Road––
(––––Indian– ––Hill– ––Park––) – Introduction/Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio briefly
reviewed this request for a modification of a Special Use Permit to renovate
the playground at Indian Hill Park, explaining that the matter had been tabled
at the last meeting in order to allow for further discussions between the Park
District and the neighbors.
   Carolyn Kurtz, Park Board President, reviewed the steps that the Board took
to gather additional neighborhood input and reported the Board is confident
that all aspects of due diligence have been performed. She reported that the
Board had reaffirmed its original location of the playground, but decided to
eliminate the proposed new plantings.
   President Woodbury invited members of the audience to comment.
   Terry Duffy, 159 Bertling, Michelle Hedge, 139 Bertling, Judy Meikle, 443
Hill Rd., Alexandra Perraud, 5 Sheridan Road, and Hector Hernandez, 451 Hill
Rd., objected to the proposal based on concerns about the impact on the ice
rink and the loss of green space.
   Responding to questions from Trustee Berger, Ms. Kurtz commented on concerns
about the loss of green space, and she and Rick Schram, the Park Board’s
landscape architect, reviewed illustrations of the proposed renovations. Ms.
Kurtz explained that there is currently only one play circle for younger
children, whereas current design guidelines separate young and older children
into different play areas. Acknowledging divergent opinions about how the park
space should be used, she said the Board believes that all of the interest
groups are best served by the proposed plans.
   President Woodbury commented that the issue is a difficult one, noting that
the Park Board is a duly elected board with the right to set its own policies
and direction as they deem appropriate, but adding that the Council has
consistently provided an additional forum for public process. He added that
because this is the last meeting of the current Council, if this Council wishes
to act on the matter, introduction of the ordinance must be waived.
   Trustee Poor remarked that his previous reservations about the request were
based on his belief that the neighbors had not had sufficient opportunity to be
heard. He noted that given the differences of opinion, there is never going to
be unanimous agreement, but the process has been fully addressed in a way that
is sensitive to all sides of the issue. He added that the Council must consider
the unanimous favorable recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals and give
deference to the Park Board’s decision, as the Council does not have the right
to redesign their project, and indicated that he would support waiving
introduction.
   Trustees Berger, Rintz, Behles, Johnson, and Greable concurred.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-7-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
7-2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor,
Greable, Johnson, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2009 – Combining Landmark Preservation Commission
and Building Review Committee – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed the
amendments made to the ordinance since its introduction in response to
discussion at that time. She also responded to questions from the Trustees.
   Manager Williams reiterated that the duties of the committees would simply
be combined and that the whole demolition process will remain exactly as it is.
   Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, and George Conbeer, 780 Prospect, urged the Council
to postpone a final decision on the matter to permit further input from
residents.
   President Woodbury explained that the only purpose of this ordinance is to
streamline Village processes to make the committee structure work better. He
remarked that while the Building Review Committee was set up with good intent,
it has not worked well, and putting their duties into the Landmark Preservation
Commission is appropriate.
   Trustees Greable and Johnson agreed that the matter should be set aside for
further study.
   Trustees Behles, Rintz, Berger, and Poor disagreed, saying that this is
purely an administrative move aimed at increasing efficiency and that it should
not be held up.
   Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to amend the ordinance as
discussed by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   Then, Trustee Berger, seconded by Trustee Behles, moved to adopt Ordinance
No. MC-5-2009 combining the duties of the Landmark Preservation Commission and
the Building Review Committee, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Berger, Poor, Behles, and Rintz. Nays: Trustees Greable
and Johnson. Absent: None.
   d)   Salary Resolutions. Manager Williams reported that the salary
