May 12, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
May 12, 2009
(Approved: June 16, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
12, 2009 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer Spinney.
Absent: Trustee Linda Pedian. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, and two persons in the audience.
2)   Consent Agenda & Meeting Time Adjustment.
   In response to a request from the Village President, Ms. Janega reported on
the use of consent agendas and the possibility of moving Council meeting times
up by a half hour with an eye toward bringing Council meetings to a close at an
earlier time and increasing efficiency. She explained that a consent agenda is
a vehicle for the Council to consolidate various items and vote for them with a
single vote. She noted that the process varies from community to community, but
in order for an item to be placed on a consent agenda, there must be unanimous
consent among the Trustees. She added that a move to implement a consent agenda
or change the start time would require an amendment to the Village Code.
   President Tucker suggested that a consent agenda might be implemented for
routine items that don’t require discussion, such as minutes, adoptions,
warrants and some bids.
   Trustee Greable asked why this hasn’t been done before.
   Manager Williams responded that he has historically recommended against the
move because (1) a former Village President specifically requested that the
warrants be examined by a Trustee; (2) the current process provides an extra
layer of review, particularly for bid items; and (3) in his opinion, most of
the time is consumed by audience comments.
   Trustees Poor, Greable, Johnson, Spinney and Rintz all expressed support for
trying a consent agenda, observing that: (i) it would be a step toward
controlling and simplifying the agendas; (ii) it would allow the Council to
focus attention on the important policy issues; (iii) it would be sensitive to
everyone’s time by streamlining the process to get everyone out earlier.
   Trustee Spinney also remarked that while audience participation is good and
to be encouraged, there needs to be some means to control repetitive and
lengthy comments.
   Trustee Rintz also expressed his belief that no Trustee would be shy about
removing an item from a consent agenda if that Trustee had any concerns about
that item.
   Audience members Jeffrey Liss and Bob Sanfilippo commented on the matter.
   President Tucker noted a consensus to try something in a limited, flexible
fashion and agreement that heavy-lifting items like ordinances and policy
issues are going to take time and are not appropriate for a consent agenda.
   A brief discussion about how to control meeting length and what items could
be included in a consent agenda followed.
   Manager Williams remarked that from a practical point of view the Village
Manager and the Village President would work together to set the consent
agenda.
[Drafter’s Note: At this point in the meeting, the Council discussed Items 4
and 5, after which it returned to the agenda and proceeded with the remaining
items, in order.]
3)   Council Orientation and Handbook Review.
   Attorney Janega gave a PowerPoint presentation and reviewed the new edition
of the Council Handbook with the Trustees, highlighting key points and some of
the changes that have been incorporated in the latest edition.
[Drafter’s Note: The following two items were discussed after Item 2.]
4)   Email Options.
   Manager Williams explained the current practice for distributing email
correspondence to Trustees, whereby all communications and material intended
for the Council is directed to the Manager’s Office, which, in turn,
distributes it to the Council in an appropriate manner. Noting that there have
been several requests for direct email contact with the Trustees, he reviewed
alternative procedures that might be implemented by the Village and their
advantages and disadvantages.
   After a brief discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees that the
current procedure works well and should be continued.
5)   Videotaping of Study Sessions.
   Manager Williams explained that while the Village videotapes all of the
regular Council meetings, the study sessions generally are not recorded,
although there have been recent requests from residents to record them as a
matter of course. He went on to outline the problems associated with
videotaping the study sessions given the current Council room set up and
facilities; in particular, the historical seating arrangement, awkward
projection angles, and the inadequate sound system. He pointed out that if a
significant a policy issue has been slated for discussion, arrangements have
been made to videotape the relevant study session.
   After a brief discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees to
continue the current process.
   In response to a question from President Tucker, Manager Williams commented
on the advisability of establishing a Streetscape Technical Committee to assist
the Public Works Director as that project moves forward.
6)   Executive Session.
   None.
7)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Poor,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Pedian. The meeting adjourned at
10:10 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
May 19, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
May 19, 2009
(Approved: June 16, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
19, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Public Works Director
Steve Saunders, Planning Tech Jill Morgan, and approximately 13 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   President Tucker announced that Item 9(e), MFT Street Rehab Bids, had been
added to the Agenda. The Trustees approved the agenda as amended.
4)   Quorum.
   a)   June 2, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   June 9, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present, except
President Tucker and Trustee Poor, indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-12-2009 – Landmark Designation: ––––660 Pine Street –
Introduction. Ms. Morgan reviewed the Landmark Preservation Commission’s
findings in support of their recommendation that the Council designate the
Subject Property as a Winnetka Landmark.
   There being no questions or comments, Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance M-12-2009, granting landmark designation
to 660 Pine Street. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Resolution No. R-24-2009 – Avli Restaurant – Class A Liquor License
with TV Rider & Takeout Rider – Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that the
Village has received an application for a new Class A-1 Liquor License, with TV
and Takeout Riders, from Avli Restaurant, Inc. The new restaurant would be
located in the Laundry Mall building, in the space previously occupied by
Harbour House. She explained that the licensing requirements have been met, but
that renovations to the space have not yet been completed, so the authorization
of the new license subject would be to passage of final inspections by the
Police and Community Development Departments, and issuance of a Certificate of
Occupancy.
   The applicant, Louis Alexakis, 1082 Elm, said he has lived in Winnetka for
the past nine years and looks forward to having a business close to home. He
noted that he has always been in the food business and recently was general
manager of the Greek Islands restaurant in Chicago.
   Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Resolution R-24-
2009, authorizing a Class A Liquor License with TV and Takeout Riders for Avli
Restaurant. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Resolution No. R-25-2009 – Policy Statement Re: Police and Fire
Pensions – Adoption. President Tucker announced that the subject Resolution is
a policy statement that urges the General Assembly to stop enacting pension
enhancements; to protect Police & Fire pension benefits; and to adopt
meaningful pension reform. She welcomed the police officers and firefighters
who were in attendance and stated there is no doubt that the Council and
community have the utmost regard and respect for the state’s public safety
employees, and value their work and the protection they provide.
   President Tucker noted that the legislators in Springfield need to hear from
the Council as representatives of the taxpayers, and that putting their
concerns in a resolution is one way of communicating with state
representatives. She observed that the Village diligently covers its pension
obligations, but that it is an unprecedented time in history, with substantial
market and job losses, home foreclosures, plummeting 401k’s, and pressures on
taxpayers creating a financial storm. She pointed out that the choice for
municipal governments is either to raise taxes or cut services to cover rising
pension obligations, and that the message in the subject Resolution is as much
about protecting current pensions and public employee jobs as it is about
reform across the board, including municipal employee pensions.
   Manager Williams observed that the discussion is only in the beginning
stages, and that he would like to review some points raised by the Firefighters
Local 2077 in response to Resolution R-25-2009. He noted that the Resolution
had been amended earlier in the day in response to some points in the union’s
memo, and he discussed key issues that union members had brought up, including:
resetting the amortization table; binding arbitration issues; a two-tier
benefits system; and pension asset base allocation. He indicated that the
Village’s unfunded pension liability is currently $39 million in all three
pension funds, which will result in service cuts or tax hikes in the future if
it continues to rise unabated.
   Manager Williams reviewed the amendments made to the Resolution since the
draft was first circulated, and he reiterated that the conversation on pensions
is just beginning, and was a difficult one to initiate, but that it would be
continued.
   Patrick Colleran, Police union president, thanked Local 2077 for putting the
counterpoint memo together, and said on behalf of his membership that the
subject has struck a nerve. He indicated that he was interested in following
where the discussion will lead from here and that he looked forward to working
with the Council on the pension issue.
   Ron Schimean, 1157 Willow, said he would like to see the Council vote no on
adopting the resolution as currently written, and present it to police and
firefighters to discuss it before taking any action. He indicated that even
though he didn’t believe the resolution was written to single out any
particular entity, the perception is that it does single out public safety
employees, and he added that lumping Winnetka’s employees with the employees of
other entities in Illinois and other states, is a disservice to the high
caliber of every Village employee.
   John Ripka, Vice President of Local 2077 and Firefighter Pension Board
member, said input from, and discussion with, police and fire representatives
is a necessary part of the pension reform process. He noted that many pension
funds have been poorly managed and now that legislation mandates proper funding
levels, those municipalities are finding it difficult to catch up on funding
that should have been allocated all along. He observed that a public safety
career is not a typical nine-to-five job, listed the hazards that are everyday
occurrences on the job, and stated that public safety employees expect their
families to be taken care of upon retirement from an honorable career.
Former Trustee Sandy Berger, 650 Ardsley, encouraged the Council to adopt the
resolution, said pension funding has been an issue for a long time, and
observed that although there were public safety employees represented at the
meeting, there weren’t many taxpayers on hand, and the Council represents the
interests of the taxpayers of Winnetka. She noted that the community is not
trying to dishonor its police and firefighters, that their contribution is
greatly valued, but economic realities have to be dealt with.
   Trustee Pedian said she respects the employees, complimented Mr. Schimean
and Mr. Ripka, and indicated that she saw a need for negotiations of
stakeholders and a dialog about the future, adding that she supported passing
the resolution.
   Trustee Poor noted that the process is improved because of the participation
of the public safety unions and their members. He observed that no changes will
be made in the short term, but the problem with pension funding has to be
recognized, solutions need to be explored, and a constructive dialog has to
take place, and therefore he supported the resolution.
   Trustee Rintz said it was important to note that the title of the resolution
has the words “to protect police and fire pensions” to make sure a structure is
maintained that is consistent with the promises made in the past, and that the
important thing is to send a message downstate to let legislators know the
enhancements have to stop. He indicated that he therefore supported passing the
resolution.
   Trustee Johnson thanked the union representatives, seconded what had been
said by his colleagues, and said the Council is tasked with trying to represent
the citizens of Winnetka and deal with economic realities. He suggested
amending the resolution to delete the provision in Section 2(d) broadening
investment authority to invest up to 60% of assets in common stock.
   Trustee Spinney said the Council clearly wants to protect the public safety
employees, but that the Village has a problem, and the time has come to do
something about it and look for solutions in a constructive way. She said while
no changes are being made immediately, the prospect is daunting, and indicated
that she supported the resolution.
   Trustee Greable thanked the public safety employees for the outstanding job
they do, and said the discussion is not about individuals but pension costs,
and about communicating to legislators that they need to be careful before
mandating additional benefits that don’t have state funds behind them. He said
overall, he could support he resolution.
   After further discussion, in which a reference to communities in California
was deleted, Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt the
revised Resolution R-25-2009, adopting a policy statement re Police and Fire
pensions.
   Trustee Johnson proposed an amendment to take out a provision in Section 2
(d) that would broaden local investment authority to allow up to 60% of assets
to be allocated in common stock, saying that last Fall’s stock market crash
would have affected the pensions much worse if they hadn’t had the 45% cap.
   Attorney Janega suggested softening the language of the provision by adding
the word “consider” broadening investment authority, rather than deleting the
provision altogether.
   Trustee Johnson said he would like the statement deleted, and made a motion
to strike the provision. There being no second, the motion was defeated.
   Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to amend the language per
Attorney Janega’s suggestion. By roll call vote, the motion to adopt the
amended Resolution R-25-2009 carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
   None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Warrant Lists 1605 and 1606. Trustee Pedian reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1605 in the amount of $1,012,285.05 and Warrant List No. 1606 in the amount
of $624,962.66. The motion was seconded by Trustee Johnson. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Chamber of Commerce Request: Sidewalk Sale. Manager Williams announced
that the Winnetka Chamber of Commerce had applied for a permit to use the
Village’s sidewalks for its annual Sidewalk Sale event and it also requested
permission to close off a portion of Lincoln Avenue south of Elm Street. He
reported that this year’s event will be held on Friday and Saturday, July 17 &
18.
   There being no questions or comments, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to authorize the Chamber’s annual sidewalk sale for July 17-18.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
   c)   2010 Illinois Municipal Retirement Fund Contribution Rate. Mr. McKee
reported that the Illinois Municipal Retirement Fund suffered serious losses in
2008, and the pension contribution rate will be increased in January of 2010.
He explained that communities will have the option of capping their rate
increases at 10%, which, while appealing in the short-term, has a long-term
negative consequence and shifts some of the costs to future taxpayers.
   After reviewing the Village’s options for the 2010 pension rate, Mr. McKee
recommended funding the full amount of 14.1%, as the cost difference between
paying the 10% limit and the full cost is $140,000. He noted that while the
figure is not a small amount in the context of the projected Village budget for
next year, he believed it was reasonable to fund the full amount.
   Ms. Berger said she was in agreement with Mr. McKee’s opinion to fund the
full 14.1%, as paying the smaller percentage pushes the cost to future
taxpayers.
   After a brief discussion, the Council also agreed with Mr. McKee’s
recommendation to fund the IMRF pension at the full 14.1%
   d)   Capital Budget Status Report. Mr. McKee reviewed the Capital Budget
Status Report, summarizing projects over $50,000. He noted that it’s important
for staff to have the flexibility to defer projects such as delaying a vehicle
purchase because the vehicle can be used for awhile longer. He added that the
Village should be proud of its philosophy not to spend money just because it’s
in the budget. There were no questions or comments from the Council.
   e)   Bid No. 09-010 – MFT Street–– Rehab. Mr. Saunders reviewed the six bids
that were received for the 2009 street rehabilitation program, all of which
were below his estimate of $191,721.50. He recommended awarding the bid to
Schroeder Asphalt Services, Inc. He explained how unit pricing works, and noted
that the actual cost of the work can be a little over or under the original
estimate, but unit pricing ensures that the Village only pays for what is
actually constructed.
   There being no questions or comments, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Spinney, moved to award Bid No. 09-010 to Schroeder Asphalt Services, for an
evaluated cost of $147,317.50, based on unit prices. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker announced that she and Trustee
Greable had attended the last regular NWMC meeting until September.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported that the Historical Society has its annual
Gala scheduled for June 20 th and invited all interested residents to call the
Historical Society to purchase tickets.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney said the Library Board had sworn in three new
Trustees and one returning one, as well as new Board President Bob Sanfilippo,
making four new members, and that on June 3 rd they would hold a combined
meeting of the Long Range Planning and Finance committees.
      iii)   Trustee Johnson reported that at the Chamber’s last meeting he had
informed them that Trustee Greable would be their new liaison.
      iv)   Trustee Rintz reported that the Residential & Design Guidelines
Committee felt that high quality visual references were needed to illustrate
the text of the report from Winter & Co, and that Brian Norkus has completed
taking photographs to go accompany the report. He said he hoped the Council be
able to review the report within the next month.
      v)   Trustee Poor said the 82 nd annual Memorial Day ceremony will be
held to honor all those who have served over the last 140 years, he noted that
the Cenotaph Restoration Fund is up to about $70,000, and asked those who
haven’t yet donated to please contribute.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
   President Tucker suggested the following appointments:
      ·   Katie Stevens to the Environmental & Forestry Commission for a second
full term.
      ·   Becky Hurley to be Chair of the Plan Commission for a full term,
replacing Chair Maureen Mitchel, whose term has expired.
      ·   Terry Dason to the BCDC, upon assuming the role of Executive Director
of the Chamber of Commerce, to replace Cicely Michalak.
      ·   Cicely Michalak to the BCDC for a full term effective with her
resignation as Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce.
      ·   Susan Whitcomb to be the school district’s representative to the Plan
Commission replacing Becky Hurley.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to approve the appointments
as recommended by President Tucker. By voice vote, the motion carried.
12)   Executive Session.
   None.
13)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 9:
45 p.m.
            
   Recording Secretary
June 2, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
June 2, 2009
(Approved: June 16, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
2, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike D’Onofrio,
Public Works Director ’’Steve Saunders’, Assistant Public Works Director Steve
Auth, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Fire Chief Scott Smith, and
approximately 6 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
   President Tucker announced that Item 9(b), Policy Direction: 255 Poplar, was
removed from the agenda at the request of the applicant. The Trustees approved
the agenda as amended
4)   Quorum.
   a)   June 9, 2009 Study Session. All of the Council members present, with
the exception of President Tucker, indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   June 16, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance M-12-2009 – Landmark Designation: ––––660 Pine St.–– –
Adoption . President Tucker announced that Ordinance M-12-2009, introduced at
the May 19 th meeting, would designate 660 Pine Street as a local landmark, and
called for any questions or comments.
   Trustee Greable commented that he had visited the home, and that it is a
unique house, and he was glad to see it designated as a local landmark.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Ordinance M-12-
2009, designating 660 Pine Street a local landmark. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance M-13-2009 – Zoning Variation: ––––594 Elm Street–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the applicant’s request for a variation to
construct a one-story attached garage addition below the first floor of the
residence, with the garage doors being part of the front building elevation
facing Walnut Street. He noted that the Zoning Ordinance permits attached
garages below the first floor, provided the doors are not part of the front
building elevation. He indicated that the ZBA had recommended in favor of the
variation request at its May 11 th meeting. He noted that since the home is a
designated local landmark, the Landmark Preservation Commission had reviewed
the proposal, and recommended that the plan be approved.
   The applicant, Elena Dreiske, 594 Elm, thanked the Council for considering
the request. She said conforming alternatives were considered, but that the
attached front-facing garage is the most practical solution for the home.
   Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance
M-13-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
[Drafter’s Note: At this point in the meeting, the Council discussed Item 9(c),
after which it proceeded with the remaining items in their scheduled order.]
   c)   Ordinance MC-6-2009 – Amending Village Code: Council Procedures and
Consent Agenda – Introduction. Attorney Janega reported that the subject
ordinance had come out of the May 12 th Study Session, in which the Council
discussed the use of a consent agenda as a tool to bring meetings to a close at
an earlier time. She explained the rules governing consent agendas set forth in
the Illinois Municipal Code, and reviewed the changes that would be made to the
Village Code to outline the order of business at Council meetings, including
the use of a consent agenda.
   The Council discussed the logistics of using the consent agenda. Attorney
Janega pointed out that it only takes one Council member to request removing an
item, and that no reason is required. Any items remaining on the consent agenda
would be passed without discussion.
   Trustee Rintz said he wanted to make clear for the record that the consent
agenda approach was thoroughly discussed at great length at the May 12 th Study
Session, and nothing is being passed in haste.
   Trustee Poor agreed, and observed that the consent agenda vehicle could pave
the way for meetings to be streamlined so that there will be more time to
discuss policy matters.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to introduce MC-6-2009.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
   d)   Resolution R-28-2009 – Establishing Rules of Procedure of the Village
Council – Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that the subject Resolution was
before the Council for discussion purposes only, since it cannot be adopted
until Ordinance MC-6-2009, amending the Village Code to provide for a consent
agenda, has been adopted. She reviewed the items that could be placed on the
Consent Agenda, including: (i) approval of minutes; (ii) adoption of ordinances
with no amendments since introduction; (iii) resolutions other than the budget
resolution; (iv) approval of warrants; (v) bid awards; and (vi) contract
approvals. After reviewing the rules and procedures, Attorney Janega answered
questions from the Council.
   Trustee Greable expressed concern with putting resolutions on the consent
agenda. Manager Williams observed that some resolutions need more vetting and
would be on the regular agenda, but that routine and straightforward matters
like the Motor Fuel Tax resolutions can be put on the consent agenda. He
reminded the Council that any one Council member can request to have any item
removed from the consent agenda, with no reason required.
   Trustee Tucker noted that when there is doubt, the Council will err on the
side of sunshine and caution.
   Trustee Greable asked why the public comment is in the middle of the agenda,
where people sometimes have to wait a long time before they can comment.
Manager Williams explained that the rationale behind the current placement is
to allow people who go through the process and get on the agenda to be heard
first, and he added that it has been moved from first to last item over the
years, and was finally settled in the middle at the request of the last Village
President.
   After a brief discussion, President Tucker said it would be best to keep
public comment where it is for now, do some thinking about it, and place it
somewhere else on the agenda if the Councils decides it wants to move it.
   There was a conversation about whether the warrant list should be placed on
the consent agenda, as Manager Williams had recommended against it. The
majority of the Trustees, with the exception of Trustee Johnson, felt that the
warrant officer is well informed and charged with due diligence in that area,
and they agreed to keep it on the consent agenda.
   Attorney Janega explained that no action would be taken at this time, and
the resolution would be up for adoption at the June 16 th meeting.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
   None.
8)   Old Business.
   None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Warrant Lists 1607 and 1608. Trustee Pedian reviewed the Warrant Lists.
After commenting on a number of the items, she moved to approve Warrant List
No. 1607 in the amount of $832,011.51 and Warrant List No. 1608 in the amount
of $557,011.38. The motion was seconded by Trustee Greable. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Policy Direction 255 Poplar Street. As noted earlier, this item was
removed from the agenda at the applicant’s request.
[Drafter’s Note: The following item was discussed after Item 6(b).]
   c)   Corner Cooks Sidewalk Grilling Request. President Tucker said this item
was on the agenda because the Village needs to articulate a coherent policy,
and that while Winnetka is supportive of its businesses and is happy to have
Corner Cooks, a consistent policy should be applied across the board to all
commercial establishments.
   Attorney Janega reported that the Village received a request from Corner
Cooks to place several grills on the sidewalk adjacent to the restaurant for a
barbecue grilling class on Father’s Day. She observed that the Village Manager
does not have the authority to grant such a request, and it was denied.
   Attorney Janega related that the Fire Chief has expressed safety concerns
about placing the grills on the sidewalk, and that the only time grilling is
done on the sidewalk in Winnetka is at the Sidewalk Sale, where the grills are
in an alley and no alcohol is served. She mentioned that the Post Office
parking lot could be an alternative site for the grilling event, and said the
matter is within the Council’s discretion to decide.
   Trustee Rintz pointed out that a restaurant sold hot dogs on the sidewalk at
last year’s 4 th of July parade and asked if they had a permit. Attorney Janega
replied that they did not.
   Trustee Poor asked if there is a precedent for fees paid to the Village for
this type of event, and why the request hadn’t come under the special events
ordinance.
   Attorney Janega replied that the special event provisions could provide
guidance and a fee structure if the event is moved to the Post Office parking
lot, but that the original request for blocking the sidewalk didn’t fall under
the Special Events Ordinance.
   Chief Smith observed that his issue is with enforcement of the ban on
grilling within 15 feet of a commercial building, and he noted that the Fire
Department has ongoing problems with trying to enforce that provision of the
Village Code. He said he would have no problem with grilling in the Post Office
parking lot, but that grilling on the sidewalk is unsafe and a violation of
Village Code.
   Betsy Simson, owner of Corner Cooks, said she made the request in order to
conduct a grilling class on Father’s Day, as a creative response to the
overwhelming pressure on businesses to stimulate new business and keep
recreational dollars in the Village. She answered questions from the Council
about the particulars of the event, and indicated that she would be happy to
have the class in the Post Office parking lot.
   Darrel Graham, 530 Laurel, said the Village should approve the event in the
parking lot, as a way to promote a local business.
   Cicely Michalak, Chamber of Commerce, BCDC and 351 Ridge, said she supports
the request and offered to have the BCDC assist in some way, if necessary.
   The Council discussed the issue and concluded that the Post Office parking
lot is a good compromise location, although some concern was expressed that
there will be numerous other similar requests. Staff was directed to follow the
special use process and waive the fees.
   Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to allow Corner Cooks to
use the Post Office parking lot on Father’s Day for a grilling cooking class
with details to be worked out with Staff. By voice vote, the motion passed.
   d)   Change Order for Primary Cable: The Okonite Company. Mr. Keys reported
that last April, two purchase orders were authorized to procure primary and
secondary cable for the next fiscal year, at a cost of $387,000 for the primary
and $137,000 for the secondary cable. He indicated that based on several recent
new business projects, an additional amount of primary cable is required, and
he requested authorization to purchase an additional 9,000 feet of primary
cable, for a change order amount of $56,000.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to approve the change
order for Bid #09-004, in the amount of $56,000 for the purchase of 15kV
primary cable at unit prices. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
   e)   Street Resurfacing and Rehab Bids. Mr. Saunders reported that bids for
the 2009 street rehabilitation program were received in May, with six bidders
responding. He reviewed the bid results, and recommended rejecting the bid of
the low bidder, Chicagoland Paving, as they had two jobs with the Village last
year that were not completed satisfactorily. He recommended awarding the
contract to the next-lowest bidder, A. Lamp Concrete for $370.006.30, a
difference of $4,243.60.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to award Bid #09-009 to A
Lamp Concrete Contractors, Inc. for the 2009 Street Rehabilitation Program. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. No report.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Rintz reported that the Plan Commission met last week and
introduced the new Chair, Becky Hurley, and also discussed a special use permit
which will soon come before the Council.
   c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that the Village filed an amicus
brief in support of the City of Chicago and Oak Park in the NRA lawsuit filed
against those two communities, and that the Seventh Court of Appeals had just
issued a decision affirming the dismissal of the cases, finding that the Second
Amendment is not applicable to local communities. She observed that the case is
destined for the Supreme Court, as another federal decision goes the other way.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams gave a brief report and asked Public Works
Director Steve Saunders to introduce his guest.
   Mr. Saunders introduced the new Assistant Director of Public Works, Steve
Auth, who began his tenure with the Village on the previous day, and noted that
Mr. Auth recently worked for the Village of Lindenhurst as the Superintendent
of Public Works. He also indicated that two weeks ago the Village was informed
of a new source of funding from the state called the IDOT Emergency repair
fund, and that the Village submitted an application for all of Green Bay Road
from Winnetka to Scott Avenues, and that the decision should be made around the
end of June.
   Manager Williams thanked Mr. Saunders for getting the application turned in
on time, as there was a lot of work involved in filling it out, and he observed
that having an in-house engineer was a benefit.
11)   Appointments.
   None.
12)   Executive Session.
   Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Pending &
Probable Litigation, Collective Bargaining and Board Appointments pursuant to
Sections 2(c)11, 2(c)(2) and 2(c)(3) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Pedian seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:50
p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:59 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
00 p.m.
               
   Recording Secretary
June 9, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
June 9, 2009
(Approved: June 16, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
9, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Pro Tem Poor called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present:
Trustees Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, and Jennifer Spinney.
Absent: Trustee Chris Rintz and President Jessica Tucker. Also in attendance:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance
Director Ed McKee, and no persons in the audience.
2)   Village of Winnetka Investment Policy – Non Pension Assets.
   Mr. McKee reported that while the prime concern for Village investments is
to preserve asset values in the current market environment, it is also
important to earn a fair rate of return. He said he recommends adding two
additional banks to the list of financial institutions the Village does
business with (Any Wintrust Financial Corporation Bank, and First Bank and
Trust). He observed that both banks are willing to provide collateral in the
form of U.S. Government Securities with full faith and credit backing, to
secure Village deposits.
   Mr. McKee observed that he chose these two banks because he knows of other
government entities that are using them and getting higher rates for their CDs.
He indicated that he has reviewed the existing investment policy with the
Village’s auditor, and that some housekeeping changes are recommended.
   Mr. McKee answered questions from the Trustees regarding the recommended
addition of the two new financial institutions.
   Trustee Rintz arrived at 7:45 p.m.
   The Council discussed the minor changes to the investment policy with Mr.
McKee, and decided to increase the bond amount for the Village Manager and
Finance Director.
   Attorney Janega said she would prepare the documents necessary to add the
two new institutions to the Village’s banking list and to amend the investment
policy, for Council consideration at its next meeting.
3)   NTHS Parking Lease.
   Mr. McKee reported that in February, 2007, the Village entered into a new
parking lease with New Trier High School (NTHS), which is up for automatic
renewal at a 5% increase on September 1, 2009, unless notice is given to NTHS
by August 31, 2009. He recalled that the contract negotiations had been lengthy
and the new terms had not been well received by NTHS, as the value of the
original contract was increased substantially. He asked the Council for
direction on allowing the NTHS lease to automatically renew at the 5% increase.
   After a short discussion, the Council agreed that the contract should be
renewed for another year, rather than renegotiating a new one, and that Mr.
McKee should bring the matter up again next year at renewal time.
4)   Reports.
   a)   Council
      i)   Trustee Johnson said the BCDC is working on a press release and
brochure about how to open a business in Winnetka, and that they would have a
Mission Statement ready for the next Council meeting, but nothing else was
ready at the moment.
      ii)   Trustee Pedian reported that she will continue to work with the
Plan Commission on the Affordable Housing plan, noting that the drafting is in
the final phase and is a high priority now that the Fell Property has an
affordable housing component.
      iii)   Trustee Spinney said she had attended the Library Board’s Long
Range Planning Committee meeting and that the new Board is very impressive and
off to a good start.
   b)   Manager. Manager Williams said the Village’s cable TV franchise has
been expired for six years, and is on a month-to-month basis, as negotiations
with Comcast are stalled. He said the arrangement works well, the fees are paid
and wires undergrounded, and that the State and Federal governments have pretty
much pre-empted local authority with regard to cable franchises. He reported
that as of January 1, 2010, Comcast will stop doing community broadcasting from
Highland Park, and that Winnetka can either pay for a fiber optic line to
Highland Park to broadcast its meetings, leave them on the website only, or
talk to New Trier High School about broadcasting with them. He said he would
like the Council to consider how important broadcasting on TV is versus using
the internet and that the matter would be discussed at a later date.
   Manager Williams also shared a list of pending agenda items with the Council
at President Tucker’s request, so they would have an idea of upcoming matters.
5)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: President Jessica Tucker. The
meeting adjourned at 8:37 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
June 16, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
June 16, 2009
(Approved: July 7, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
16, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Public Works Director
Steve Saunders, Planning Technician Jill Morgan, and approximately 22 persons
in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Approval of the Agenda.
The Trustees approved the agenda as drafted.
4)   Quorum.
   a)   July 7, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   July 14, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
5)   Village Council Minutes.
   i)   May 5, 2009, Regular Meeting
   ii)   May 12, 2009   , Study Session
   iii)   May 19, 2009   , Regular Meeting
   iv)   June 2, 2009   , Regular Meeting
   v)   June 9, 2009, Study Session
President Tucker called for any additions or corrections to any of the draft
minutes. There being none, Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to
approve the minutes as drafted. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
6)   Landmark Preservation Awards.
Ms. Morgan introduced Louise Holland, Chairperson of the Landmark Preservation
Commission, to present this year’s awards for the following projects:
Restoration
Christ Church Dovecot Chapel
Rehabilitation
790 Bryant                   Peter & Lisa Beebe
1089 Spruce                  Colin & Robyn Hall
507 Cedar                    Michael Levitin & Meredith Mendes
500 Maple                    Wally & Nan Greenough
564 Meadow                   Richard & Marilee Redmond
175 Sheridan                 Pat & Lorill Haynes
New Construction
785 Walden                   Chris & Laura Janssen
 
Ms. Holland thanked the Council for the opportunity to present these awards and
remarked at how gratifying it was to the Commission to have the number of
applications received this year. She said the Commission strives to make the
public aware that tasteful restoration, rehabilitation and new construction can
be achieved in a way that fits contextually into the neighborhoods.
7)   Business Community Development Commission: Statement of Purpose.
Attorney Janega explained that the proposed statement is the culmination of
extended work by the Commission. It is intended to flesh out the powers and
duties of the Committee that are set forth in the Village Code, giving them
some practical impact by guiding the Committee’s activities related to economic
development.
Trustee Johnson, Acting Chair of the Committee, and Committee members Arthur
Braun, Louise Holland, Betty Skalski, and Cicely Michalak commented on the
Committee’s activities and the proposed Statement of Purpose and responded to
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved that the Statement of
Purpose be approved by the Council as an operating guide. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-13-2009 – Zoning Variation: ––––594 Elm St.–– –
Adoption. There being no further comments or questions, Trustee Johnson,
seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-13-2009 granting a
variation from the below grade front facing garage door requirement in order to
permit construction of a below grade garage. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Rintz, Pedian, Poor, Greable, and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2009 – Amending Village Code: Council Procedures and
Consent Agenda – Adoption. There being no further comments or questions
regarding this Ordinance, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to
adopt Ordinance No. MC-6-2009 amending the Village Code as it pertains to
Council rules and procedures. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Greable, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, and Pedian.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Resolution No. R-28-2009 – Establishing Rules of Procedure of the
Village Council – Adoption. President Tucker referred the Trustees to comments
distributed on the dais from Jeffrey Liss, 1324 Edgewood, questioning the need
for formal rules and urging the Council not to specify a time limit for
speakers. Mr. Liss reiterated his remarks in person.
Addressing some of Mr. Liss’s comments, Attorney Janega explained that the
rules implement the Council’s procedures which have now been restructured. She
noted that rules, per se, need to be stated firmly, while guidelines, which
serve as a tool for informing the public, can be stated more softly. She
emphasized that at all times, the Village President has the discretion to
control the meeting as he/she deems appropriate for the circumstances.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Poor concurred with Attorney Janega’s
approach, saying that establishing rules creates an evenhanded approach, sets a
goal, and encourages compliance. He indicated that he would support the
Resolution as drafted.
Trustee Pedian remarked that she has heard people say that they would like
guidelines as to how to approach Council meetings, and that these rules help
the public know what to expect.
Trustee Greable, Spinney, Rintz, and Johnson agreed on the need to articulate
rules and procedures and indicated that they were comfortable with the three-
minute time limit. There was a consensus, however, to replace the word
“maximum” in Rule 13.7.1 with “up to.”
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-28-
2009 establishing rules of order and procedure for the Village Council, as
amended above. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Pedian, Poor, Greable, Johnson, Spinney, and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   d)   Resolution No. R-26-2009 – Prevailing Wages – Adoption. Attorney Janega
explained that State law requires the Village to establish the prevailing wages
annually for workers employed in the construction of capital improvement
projects in the Village, and that the Village’s practice is to adopt the rates
as determined by the Illinois Department of Labor as of June 2009.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
26-2009 establishing the prevailing rate of wages for construction work in the
Village of Winnetka. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes:
Trustees Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, Poor, and Greable. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
   e)   Resolution No. R-27-2009 – Marco Roma Packaged Meal Takeout Rider –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed this request from one of the Village’s Class
A liquor licensees that would allow patrons to order a full meal for carry-out,
as well as an alcoholic beverage to go along with it. She emphasized that the
sale of the alcohol must be incidental and complementary to the sale of a
complete meal, as provided in the Village Code.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
27-2009 authorizing the creation of an additional Takeout Rider License to be
issued to Marco Roma Pizzeria & Italian Eatery, located at 14 Green Bay Road.
By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   f)   Resolution No. R-29-2009 - Village of Winnetka Investment Policy – Non-
Pension Assets – Adoption. Mr. McKee briefly reviewed this item and the next,
noting that the first matter had been discussed by the Council at three
previous meetings and the Resolution had been discussed at the June 9 th Study
Session. Both documents are consistent with those discussions.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-29-
2009 amending the Village’s existing investment policy. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Greable, Johnson, Spinney,
Rintz, and Pedian. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   g)   Ordinance No. M-15-2009 - Designating Authorized Investment Advisors,
Money Managers and Depository Institutions for Village Funds And Money –
Introduction. Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce
