   Recording Secretary
July 14, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
July 14, 2009
(Approved: August 18, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
14, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, Planning
Technician Jill Morgan, and approximately 13 persons in the audience.
2)   Zoning Retail Overlay District.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the history of the recent revisions to the C-2 Overlay
District zoning provisions, and reported that, as directed by the Council,
staff has been investigating ways to streamline the special use permit process.
Mr. Norkus described the evolution of the special use process in Winnetka and
explained the difference between special use permits and zoning variations. He
reported that one possible change that staff has developed would be to add a
new type of approval procedure for uses that are deemed to be “retail
exceptions.” These requests could come before a proposed hybrid committee
composed of members from the Plan Commission, ZBA, DRB and/or BCDC. He
explained that one of the key differences in process for retail exceptions is
that they wouldn’t be required to complete the same level of parking study that
a more significant use like a bank or a realtor would entail, since the impact
of the retail exceptions would be minimal. He said staff had assumed that the
same standards for granting special uses would still be applied by the hybrid
committee, but with a shortened timeframe, and that there had been no
discussion about whether there should be different standards for the retail
exceptions.
Mr. D’Onofrio and Mr. Norkus discussed how the retail exceptions might be
classified, and elaborated upon those uses.
Manager Williams observed that a core policy decision for the Council to
consider is whether it wishes to split the special use permit process into two
paths, with significant special uses staying in the current process and smaller
exceptions taking the streamlined course.
An in-depth discussion of the special use process followed, with most Trustees
in general agreement that there should be a faster process for certain special
use requests for commercial properties than the current method provides. There
was no consensus about the composition of the committee that would hear the
exception requests.
President Tucker called for comments from the audience.
Jim Sayegh, Winnetka Commercial Property Owners Assoc.; Dan Schermerhorn, 907
Green Bay; and Robert Dini, 1056 Gage, all expressed support for staff’s
proposals to streamline the process for retail exceptions, and Mr. Dini also
requested that the special use permit fees be reduced.
Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce asked why a tenant would need a special use permit to
use less space, and Mr. D’Onofrio and Mr. Norkus clarified that it relates to
the 50-foot retail requirement in the overlay district.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak and BCDC member, pointed out that a hybrid committee
that does not have regular meeting dates will do a disservice to the Village in
terms of transparency, since people may have difficulty finding out when the
committee meets. She said the special use process protects Winnetka’s retail
spaces and asked that no retail exceptions be created.
Betty Skalski, 611 Ash, Phototronics, and BCDC member, agreed that the
integrity of the
C-2 overlay district should be preserved, although she sympathized with the
building owners’ position.
There was further Council discussion on the points raised by the audience.
Trustees Rintz and Pedian suggested having the retail exception come straight
to the Council, for the sake of transparency and to expedite the timeframe
required.
President Tucker noted support for keeping the current process for major
special use requests, and directed staff to further explore the issue of retail
exception requests, taking into account the transparency issue, overall
integrity of the retail overlay, parking considerations and notice
requirements. She requested that the permit application fees also be examined
for fairness.
3)   Ethics Ordinances and Conflicts of Interest.
Attorney Janega reported that the Winnetka Caucus has raised a question about a
possible conflict of interest in connection with a member of the Design Review
Board, and it is asking the Council to expand the Village’s ethics ordinance to
include all members of Village boards and commissions. She explained direct and
indirect conflicts, actual and apparent conflicts, and statutes that govern
interests for Village officials. She added that the Village Code puts further
limits on interests for Village officials and employees, but note that the
Village’s ordinances do not apply to members of boards and commissions.
Attorney Janega observed that the current ethics provisions have served the
Village well, and indicated that the current situation is more about
appearances than with an actual conflict of interest.
Attorney Janega gave some examples of circumstances which would create a
conflict, described other scenarios where no conflict would exist, and answered
questions from the Council.
President Tucker called for comments from the audience.
Anne Wilder, 1096 Spruce, remarked that the Village’s advisory bodies are
significant and carry a lot of weight, and that an apparent conflict could
easily be hidden if the participant with the interest just didn’t attend the
pertinent meeting.
Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce, said the ethics rules for the Council and Village
officials should be extended to include every member of every board and
commission, and raised the issue of fairness with regard to resident contact
with Council members on a given issue.
Irma Villarreal, 1045 Westmoor, recommended changing the Village Code to expand
the scope of the regulations to include boards and commissions, and asked that
existing committee members be grandfathered in.
Vicki Apatoff, 730 Ardsley, said the Village Code should be revised so it does
not appear that anyone is getting special favors, and added that she had a
statement from Edie Howell, 277 Ridge, who was unable to attend the meeting, to
the effect that members of the audience who offer public comment should be
asked whether they live in Winnetka and if they have a financial interest in
the matter being discussed.
Attorney Janega said there are two separate issues to think about: i) members
of boards and commissions who are issued contracts with the Village, which
could be addressed by extending the provisions of Section 2.4 to include all
members of boards and commissions; and ii) a member of a board or commission
who has an interest in a matter being brought before that committee, which, for
the Council, is currently covered by state statute.
Manager Williams asked Attorney Janega to comment on the appearance of
fairness.
Attorney Janega explained that Illinois law treats special use requests as
legislative determinations, so the Council’s process is legislative process,
while the proceedings of subordinate bodies that make their recommendations to
the Council after hearings are quasi-judicial. She noted that the Council has
traditionally allowed citizens to talk to their elected officials both at
formal meetings and outside of the Chambers in less formal, casual encounters.
She observed that the specific issue of contacts between the Council and
applicants and citizens was not raised prior to tonight’s meeting and would not
be addressed at this time, but is a future policy call for the Council to make.
Manager Williams said in his experience, land use issues being treated as
judicial proceedings is a frustrating state of affairs, as the matter cannot be
discussed outside the Council Chambers. He indicated that the Council should be
available to communicate with the public it represents.
Mr. Hecht pointed out that he did not intend for his comments to be limited to
land use issues.
President Tucker said the issue would be discussed again at a future meeting,
and directed Attorney Janega to research what kinds of ethics laws comparable
communities have enacted, and to bring that to the next discussion.
4)   Membership Analysis: EFC & PC.
Due to the lateness of the hour, this item was postponed until the next regular
Council meeting.
5)   Reports.
   a)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported that he attended the Chamber of Commerce
meeting that morning, and that the Sidewalk Sale will be held this weekend. He
also reported that the Chamber is developing a new website, and has signed up
eight new members.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney said she had enjoyed a tour of the RED Center with
Trustee Tucker earlier in the day.
   b)   Village President. No report.
   c)   Village Attorney. No report.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams distributed a list of upcoming agenda items
and also announced that the Village is in discussions with the Woodley Road
Association about improving Woodley Road and dedicating it to the Village.
Executive Session. None
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
55 p.m.
