items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
[DRAFTER’S NOTE: This item was discussed after Item 6(d), after which Item 9(e)
was discussed.]
   a)   Ordinance No. M-14-2009 – ––––Winnetka– ––Park– ––District– ––Service–
––Center–– Lease – Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that the subject
ordinance authorizes the execution of a long-term lease between the Village and
the Winnetka Park District for its new maintenance facility and service center
at the Village’s Public Works Yards. She noted that the three changes made by
the Council at the time of introduction at the July 21 st Council meeting are
included as Exhibit A to the lease and that no amendment is required.
There being no further questions or comments, Trustee Rintz, seconded by
Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt M-14-2009 approving the Park District’s Lease of
Village Property for its new ’’’’Service’ ’’Center. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
[DRAFTER'S NOTE:  The next item was discussed after Item 9(b), after which Item
9(d) was discussed.]
   b)   Ordinance No. M-17-2009 – Disposing of Certain Personal Property Owned
by the Village – Introduction. Mr. Saunders reported that six pieces of Village
equipment are past their useful life, and the subject ordinance authorizes
their sale at the NWMC auction in September, and he reviewed the list of items
to be disposed of.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce M-17-2009. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
[DRAFTER'S NOTE: The next item was discussed after Item 9(d) after which
Reports were given (Item 10).]
   c)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2009 – Board & Commission Members – Introduction.
Attorney Janega reported that at its July 21 st meeting, the Council directed
her to make an amendment to Chapter 3.04 deleting the requirement that one of
the members of the Environmental & Forestry Commission also be a member of the
DRB, and she noted that a technical amendment to Chapter 3.08 has also been
made.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to introduce MC-8-2009. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
[DRAFTER'S NOTE: This item was discussed after Item 9(a), after which Item 6(a)
was discussed.]
   d)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2009 – Ethics Code Amendment – Introduction.
Attorney Janega reported that at its July 14 th Study Session, the Council
directed her to review ethics ordinances from other municipalities in the area
and that, after sharing the preliminary results of that research with the
Village President, President Tucker directed her to prepare an ordinance
expanding the scope of the Village Code’s current ethics provisions contained
in Chapter 2.40.
Attorney Janega reviewed a study guide she had prepared for the Council
identifying the general policy issues to be decided, and gave a summary of the
provisions contained in Ordinance MC-7-2009.
Trustee Greable asked why the issue of ethics is being addressed at the present
time, and asked if there were any examples that would help to give context to
the discussion.
President Tucker indicated that the Caucus had brought it to the Council’s
attention, citing instances over the years where questions have arisen. She
observed that the Council has the prerogative to keep the current ethics
provisions as they are, or to make any changes they feel are necessary.
Manager Williams said the only problem he could recall was during the recent
approval process for the Fell Development project, where a committee member had
done some work for the developers, and was also working on some projects for
the Village. He noted that while nothing illegal occurred in either instance, a
ban on appointees having contracts with the Village should not have many
unintended consequences.
Attorney Janega elaborated upon that particular case, and also described one
other time when a conflict was alleged but that at trial and on appeal, the
courts agreed there had been no conflict. She said from time to time she gets
an inquiry from a Trustee or appointee, but that problems have been rare.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, said the examination of the ethics issue has been
useful, and suggested that cases which are de minimis should be distinguished;
spouses should be included in the prohibited interest category; and he
disagreed with the idea that the public shouldn’t be allowed to talk to Village
officials ex parte.
Attorney Janega clarified that ex parte communications should be interpreted to
mean communication with one party in a proceeding outside the presence of the
other party, meaning that the average citizen could have contact if they
weren’t directly involved, and she noted that the provision needs to be make
that clearer.
Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka, supported tightening the ethics code and indicated
that there is an appearance of impropriety when appointees of boards and
committees have contracts with the Village. She expressed support for a
prohibition on appointees doing business with the Village for the period that
they are serving, and agreed that spouses should be included in the prohibited
interest category.
Anne Wilder, 1096 Spruce, agreed with Ms. Stevens’ points and stated that the
issue is one of the most important the Council has to deal with. She observed
that Winnetka was in the forefront in passing a hand-held cell phone ban, and
is a leader on environmental issues, and she urged the Council to make Winnetka
a leader in ethics reform as well.
Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce, said he feels the draft ordinance is premature, and
the new Chapter 2.41 unnecessarily complicates the statutory provisions and
should be abandoned. He was in favor of simply including the Village’s
appointees in the current ethics code provisions, and said he supports a ban on
any communications with the Council or appointees on any matter that is pending
or going to be pending before any of the Village’s bodies.
Irma Villarreal, 1045 Westmoor, said she thought the provisions in the draft
ordinance were very good and she indicated that she liked how the issue of
conflicts was dealt with separately in the new Chapter 2.41. She urged the
Council to select an Ethics Commission that is made up of a separate group of
residents, rather than using the Council in that capacity.
Vicki Apatoff, 730 Ardsley, observed that ad hoc committees should be added to
the definitions for appointees and included in the prohibited interests, citing
the ad hoc Streetscape Technical Committee as an example of an advisory body
that has an impact on Village spending. She also said the Caucus is interested
in expansion of the financial definitions.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, pointed out that the Village shouldn’t be
prevented from getting the best people for their input and expertise, and said
that a conflict with one particular issue can be cured with recusal. He
indicated that he was against the prohibition on ex parte communications with
Village staff, Council and appointees, unless a conflict was disclosed.
The Trustees agreed that the public comments had been very informative, and
after a discussion about the proposals contained in the draft ordinance, the
consensus was reached that more time, conversation and public input was needed
before they could make an informed decision.
President Tucker said the issue would be discussed again at the October Study
Session, and encouraged the audience members who had made comments to put them
in writing for the Trustees.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
[DRAFTER'S NOTE: The next item was discussed first, after which Item 6(d) was
discussed.]
   a)   Request to Place Flags on the Village Green. Manager Williams reviewed
a request by local student Genevieve Nielsen, identical to last year’s request,
to honor those who died on September 11, 2001, by placing 2,977 American flags
on the Village Green.
Genevieve Nielsen, 425 Linden, said she would like to repeat her 9/11 tribute
of last year by planting one flag for each person who died on 9/11. She said
the flags had been bought last year by local businesses, and that she intends
to have residents help her plant the flags this year.
The Council unanimously approved the request.
[DRAFTER'S NOTE: The next item was discussed after Item 9(c), after which Item
6(b) was discussed.]
   b)   Elm Street Railroad Station Renovation. Mr. Saunders announced that the
construction bids for the Elm Street Station renovation have been obtained by
Metra and the project is scheduled to start soon. He reviewed the scope of the
rehabilitation, which includes the renovation of all existing exterior
passenger facilities.
Mr. Saunders noted that Metra’s standard details such as roofing materials and
lighting fixtures are not consistent with the Village’s appearance standards,
and he reviewed a list of alternates, along with their pricing. He then
reviewed a memo with the recommendations from former Trustee Ken Behles, who
had been active in the negotiations with Metra during his tenure on the
Council.
Glen Peters, Metra, 545 W. Jackson, Chicago, informed the Council that Metra
would work with the Village on the Streetscape project in any way, as long as
costs are comparable. He said once approval is given, the project is ready to
be started in September, and noted that elements such as benches and waste
receptacles would be the last items to be installed, so the Village has extra
time to consider their specifications.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, pointed out that the Village’s lease with Metra
requires Metra to repair the Hubbard Woods train station, and asked when Metra
plans to honor its lease with the Village make the necessary repairs. He
suggested an engineer from Metra inspect the pedestrian bridge and stairs, and
report the results of the inspection to the Village Council.
Crispin Hales, 1000 Sheridan, said after he had inspected the bridge in March
of 2008, he wrote a letter to Metra warning that the bridge was unsound. He
noted that Metra responded quickly and did the necessary repairs, but he
recommended that the components be painted before they’re installed to prevent
corrosion, and also called for annual inspections.
Mr. Peters replied that the bridge had been inspected and repaired immediately
after Metra had received Mr. Hales’ letter, and that it is sound and
structurally safe. He observed that renovations to the Hubbard Woods station
are in Metra’s plan but that funds are short and therefore it has been pushed
out beyond the five-year plan period.
Trustee Poor requested that Metra schedule immediate inspections on the bridge
at Hubbard Woods coordinated with the Public Works Department and report back
to the Council, and he asked Attorney Janega to review the Village’s lease with
Metra for compliance issues.
President Tucker directed Manager Williams and Mr. Saunders to follow up with
the bridge inspection. She asked the Council for their thoughts on the
alternatives that were up for consideration.
The Council’s consensus as to follow the recommendations in Mr. Behles’ memo
and to accept Alternates 1, 3 and 4 and to decline Alternates 2 and 5.
[DRAFTER'S NOTE:  The next item was discussed after Item 9(e), after which Item
9(b) was discussed.]
   c)   Policy Direction: Request for Third Driveway Entrance at 700 Locust
Street. Mr. Saunders reviewed a request for a third curb cut for a driveway at
the subject property, noting that he had denied the application, as the Village
Code limits him to approving only a second curb cut.
The applicants, Tim & Julie Clary, said their request stems from a desire to
have access to the front door of the home, as there is no access to the front
door from the current driveway on Dinsmoor. They maintained that the traffic
impact would be minimal and that the driveway they are proposing would make
access to the front door more practical and functional for the home.
After a Council discussion about the request, several Trustees indicated that a
single entrance on Locust could be designed to give adequate access to the
front door, and the consensus of the Council was to deny the request for a
third curb cut.
[DRAFTER'S NOTE:  The next item was discussed after item 6(b), after which Item
6(c) was discussed.]
   d)   Bid 09-014: FD 75’ Minimum Quint Type Aerial Fire Apparatus. Chief
Smith reported that $800,000 has been budgeted for the replacement of the Fire
Departments’ ladder truck, and gave some background information about the
specific type of truck being purchased. He reviewed the bids that were received
in response to the specifications that were prepared, and indicated that the
lowest bidder, Smeal Fire Apparatus, also complied with all but minor or no
longer necessary specifications, and recommended awarding them the bid in the
amount of $674,406.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to
award the contract for a new Quint Type Aerial Fire Apparatus to Smeal Fire
Apparatus, in the amount of $674,406. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
[DRAFTER'S NOTE: The next item was discussed after Item 6(a), after which Item
9(c) was discussed.]
   e)   Park District Proposed Open Field Burn. Chief Smith reviewed a request
by the Park District to conduct controlled burns at Crow Island and Bell Woods,
similar to burns conducted last April. He reported that previous open burns
conducted by the Park District’s contractor, Pizzo & Associates, were completed
in a professional manner with minimal disruption to the surrounding community,
and that they have agreed to comply with the required submittals and community
notifications required for the current burn application.
Terry Schwartz, Director of the Winnetka Park District, echoed Chief Smith’s
comments and asked Bob Smith, Superintendent of Parks, to comment on the
results of the first burn.
Mr. Smith said dramatic changes had been observed at both sites last spring,
and that a lot of invasive species had been eradicated, and he noted that it
will take several burns to achieve the final results, as it is an ongoing
process.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to allow the Park District to
conduct controlled burns at Crow Island and Bell Woods. By roll call vote, the
motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   f)   CATV Franchise – Meeting Video Playback Options. Due to the lateness of
the hour, this item was postponed to a later meeting date.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported that she had received a
request from a resident asking for the Village’s help in persuading the U.S.
Post Office to keep the Hubbard Woods retail post office facility. She noted
that she had sent letters to ’’Winnetka’’s elected officials in
’’’’Washington’’ asking for their help with the matter. She encouraged
