   Recording Secretary
September 1, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
September 1, 2009
(Approved: September 15, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 1, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Assistant Finance
Director Hanna Sullivan, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Public Works Director Steve
Saunders, and three persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   September 8, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   September 15, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to approve the Agenda as
drafted. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson,
Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda.
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   August 18, 2009, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1619 & 1620. Approving Warrant List No. 1619 in the
amount of $1,059,892.11, and Warrant List No. 1620 in the amount of
$246,228.15.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-17-2009 – Disposing of Certain Personal Property Owned
by the Village –Adoption. Approving the disposal of 11 items of equipment owned
by the Village.
   d)   Ordinance No. MC-8-2009 – Board and Commission Members – Adoption.
Amending the Village Code as it pertains to certain members of the Design
Review Board, Environmental and Forestry Commission and Plan Commission.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Village Auditor: Comprehensive Annual Financial Report (CAFR).
Mr. McKee introduced Ron Amen, Lauterback & Amen, LLP, the Village’s Auditor.
Mr. Amen began his remarks by thanking Mr. McKee and his staff for their
assistance, courtesy, and professionalism in preparing the CAFR, which he
explained is the final official record of the fiscal year, ending on March 31,
2009. He reviewed the Auditor’s comments and suggestions contained in the
Management Letter and commented on several pages in the CAFR. Mr. Amen pointed
out that the Village had once again received the prestigious GFOA Certificate
of Achievement for Excellence in Financial Reporting, as well as the Auditor’s
“unqualified” opinion, which is the highest achievable. Mr. Amen then responded
to questions from the Council.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-10-2009 – Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to
the Foreign Fire Insurance Board – Introduction. Attorney Janega briefly
reviewed the history and responsibilities of the Foreign Fire Insurance Board.
She explained that this ordinance would amend the Village Code to reflect
requirements of recent legislation pertaining to the membership size of that
board, as well as to provide for an election mechanism to bring the terms of
two new members into line with those of existing members, and then transition
to staggered two-year terms. Finally, the ordinance completes the
reorganization of the relevant chapter that was begun last year. Ms. Janega
then responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-10-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
a)   Request for New Liquor License Category - The Wine Shop. Attorney Janega
reviewed a request for the establishment of a new liquor license category to
permit the opening of a new retail business involving the sale of certain kinds
of packaged alcoholic beverages. She explained that at present the proposed
operation does not fall within any of the Village’s existing categories. Ms.
Janega noted that both Glencoe and Wilmette have similar businesses operating.
She reported that Chief DeLopez had inquired of his colleagues in these
communities as to enforcement issues and received no negative comments.
The applicant, Emily Link, fleshed out her concept in greater detail,
clarifying questions about food and space. She indicated that she would be
providing limited food products, wrapped sandwiches and prepackaged salads
prepared by A la Carte in Wilmette, from a cooler in the store, but the
purchase of such products would be incidental to the purchase of the packaged
alcoholic beverages. She said that at this time her business model is to have a
small tasting bar, but not a wine bar where customers could purchase glasses of
wine for consumption in the store. She also responded to questions about space
requirements and proposed hours of operation.
Trustee Spinney said she thinks it is a wonderful concept and thanked Ms. Link
for presenting it to Winnetka.
Noting that the Council had previously discussed the wine bar concept and
generally been favorably disposed toward it, Trustee Pedian asked whether Ms.
Link had considered adding such a bar to her proposal.
Mr. Link indicated that she had but said that at this time she is not prepared
to expand her proposed operation. Perhaps in a year or so, such an expansion
might be appropriate.
Ms. Pedian then asked Attorney Janega about the possibility of adding the wine
bar concept to the required Code amendment.
Ms. Janega recommended a two-step process addressing Ms. Link’s proposal
initially and then adding a wine bar classification at a later time after
further discussion and consideration.
In response to a question from Trustee Pedian, Chief DeLopez indicated that he
believes The Wine Shop represents a good economic opportunity for the community
and reported that he has no concerns, particularly in light of the fact that
neither Glencoe nor Wilmette has experienced any enforcement problems with
their similar stores.
Trustee Poor commented that while he had been skeptical about the wine bar
concept when last discussed, he is persuaded by this presentation and is in
favor of this proposal as it is in keeping with the character of the Village.
He concurred that the wine bar concept needs further consideration before
establishing such a license classification.
President Tucker polled the Trustees, and there was a consensus to move forward
with establishing a new liquor license category to fit the wine shop concept
and to further discuss the wine bar concept at some later time.
   b)   Video Gaming Law. Attorney Janega explained that a new Act recently
signed by the Governor allows video gambling in any restaurant that has a
license to serve alcoholic beverages. Under this new Act, the income from each
terminal is taxed and a percentage of that tax goes to the State and a
percentage goes to the municipality in which the terminal is located. The bill
is one of several designed to provide additional funding for State capital
projects. She added that pursuant to the Act, any municipality may pass an
ordinance prohibiting the video gambling within its corporate limits, and she
went on to enumerate some nearby communities that have already done so. In
doing so, she noted that the Winnetka Village Code does not address video
gaming machines or games of chance, although it does regulate amusement
devices.
Ms. Janega commented that although early news reports implied that
municipalities that prohibit video gaming machines would not receive any road
funds from the State, nothing in the Act makes such a connection. She went on
to explain that the trade-off for prohibiting video gaming is the loss of the
possible tax revenue stream. She referred the Trustees to materials in the
Agenda Packet estimating the potential net income from such machines and
remarked that it is impossible to test the validity of these estimates in the
context of Winnetka as numerous other circumstances would have to be factored
into the equation.
Chief DeLopez stated his strong objections to permitting video gambling in
Winnetka.
President Tucker polled the Trustees, and there was a unanimous consensus to
opt-out of video gaming. Attorney Janega indicated that she would prepare the
necessary ordinance for consideration at the next meeting.
   c)   CATV Franchise – Meeting Video Playback Options. Manager Williams
reported that Comcast has advised the Village that they will be shutting down
their Highland Park local access studio on January 1, 2010. He explained that
currently, content on the Village’s local access channel, Channel 10, including
the rebroadcast of Village Council meetings, is managed by this studio and its
staff, as the Village does not have the equipment necessary to handle this
process. He then reviewed three options identified by staff to address this
situation: (1) invest approximately $50,000 in the equipment to maintain the
status quo operation from Village Hall; (2) explore a partnership with New
Trier High School to handle the playback of Council meetings on their Channel
19; or (3) rely solely on internet for the rebroadcast of Council meetings.
After a brief discussion, there was agreement that in view of the associated
costs, the best option is to proceed with exploring a partnership with New
Trier High School, at least for the near future.
   d)   Bid Number 09-015: Street Rehabilitation Fall 2009 Program. Mr.
Saunders summarized the bids and bidding process for the Street Rehabilitation
Fall 2009 Program consisting of the milling and resurfacing of various streets
throughout the Village. This work includes spot curb and gutter removal and
replacement, sidewalk removal and replacement, and all related collateral work.
He recommended awarding the bid to the low bidder, A Lamp, in the amount of
$385,585.75.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to award the bid as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Pedian, Poor, Greable, Johnson, Spinney, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported that the Village Hall
Technical Committee had met with staff and the architect to review the proposed
interior renovations and cost estimates. The matter will come before the
Council for discussion at a Study Session later this fall.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Johnson mentioned that the Business Community Development
Commission would be holding its monthly meeting this coming Thursday morning at
7:30 a.m.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney reported on the recent meeting of the Library
Board.
   c)   Attorney. In response to a question raised at the last meeting, Ms.
Janega reported that after reviewing the Village’s lease with Union Pacific for
the Hubbard Woods train station, she has concluded that the lease does not
cover the pedestrian bridge, stairs, platforms and walkways and does not give
the Village any enforceable rights over them.
Mr. Saunders reported that Metra and Union Pacific had inspected the questioned
structural issues last Thursday and determined that there are no severe
structural concerns. A written report to that effect is expected soon. He
pointed out that the pedestrian bridge, stairs, etc., are owned and maintained
by Union Pacific, and they inspect them in accordance with Federal law.
   d)   Manager. None.
10)   Appointments.
None.
