information is be available.
The applicant voiced no objection to this course of action.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to table Resolution No. R-32-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   d)   Ordinance No. MC-9-2009 – Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to
the Emerald Ash Borer Infestations – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed
proposed amendments to this ordinance amending the Village Code regarding the
removal of ash trees infested by Emerald Ash Borers. The Ordinance was
introduced at the September 15th Council meeting.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to amend the ordinance as
presented in the amended draft. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-9-
2009, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Greable, Spinney, Johnson, and Pedian. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
   e)   Ordinance MC-13-2009 – Reducing the Speed Limit on ––––Walden Road–– –
Introduction. Attorney Janega recounted the Council’s September 15 th
discussion regarding the speed limit on Walden Road and presented an ordinance
that would reduce the speed limit to 25 mph.
Mr. Saunders explained the State’s standards for establishing speed limits and
reported that data from a speed study of Walden Road supports the reduction of
the speed limit.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-13-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
None.
   a)   Curbside Recycling Contract. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village’s
current curbside recycling contract with Veolia Environmental Services has
expired. He outlined three options the Village has explored for providing this
service : (1) seek to extend the contract with Veolia; (2) seek competitive
bids for a new contract; or (3) award a contract to Groot Industries pursuant
to the terms of an agreement negotiated by SWANCC for member municipalities. He
gave staff’s recommendation to pursue the third option, noting it shares
revenue based on the weight of materials recycled and thus is the most
economically advantageous to the Village. He noted that residents would
experience almost identical service, but that service routes would be adjusted
to match the Village’s refuse collection routes. He added that Groot has also
agreed to incorporate the CNG trucks into the operation.
Mr. Saunders introduced Brooke Beal, SWANCC’s Executive Director, who briefed
the Council on SWANCC’s revenue sharing program and the CNG component of the
contract with Groot. Then he and Frank Hillegonds, Groot Industries, responded
to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Rintz arrived at 9:17 p.m.
Trustee Greable inquired about Groot’s proposed pricing reopener provision with
mandatory mediation. Attorney Janega responded that she does not recommend
accepting that provision unless it is in SWANCC’s mast agreement and that she
would verify that before signing off on the form of the contract.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee poor, moved to award a contract for
curbside recycling services to Groot Industries pursuant to terms negotiated by
SWANCC for its member municipalities, substantially in the form presented in
the Agenda. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson,
Rintz, Pedian, Poor, Greable, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Manager Williams commented on the important role SWANCC has played in driving
down the Village’s garbage disposal costs and on SWANCC’s ongoing efforts to be
on the cutting edge of recycling, carbon footprint reduction, and
sustainability.
   b)   Stack Repairs, Winnetka Electric Plant . Mr. Keys reviewed the bids for
repairs to the exhaust stack at the Electric Plant, including railing repair,
painting, expansion joint repair, and gunite replacement. He commented on the
wide diversity of bids and recommended that the bid be awarded to Gerard
Chimney Company in the amount of $53,715. He then responded to questions from
the Trustees.
After a thorough discussion, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney,
moved to award Bid 09-018 as recommended. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Rintz, Pedian, Poor, Greable, and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Water Plant Intake Maintenance Work. Mr. Keys reviewed the bids for
periodic dive inspections and placement of stone around the Water Plant’s
intake pipe, necessitated by the displacement of stone and sand around the pipe
due to wave action.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to award the bid for the
underwater video inspection and stone work required on the 20” intake at the
Water Plant to the Edward E. Gillen Company in an amount of $74,656. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Rintz, Pedian, Poor,
Greable, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Repair Options - Green Bay Road. Mr. Saunders explained that the
Village has been notified that it has received State of Illinois Emergency
Repair Funds amounting to $730,000, to resurface portions of Green Bay Road. He
summarized key points about how funding will be administered, as well as the
three broad strategies evaluated by staff and outlined in the agenda report
regarding the use of the funds to improve the conditions of the roadway, as
follows:
      (1)   Resurface all four lanes, with minimal curb repair and pavement
base repair, for as long a stretch as the funding will allow.
      (2)   Resurface the outer two lanes, with significant curb repair and
pavement base repair, for as long a stretch as the funding will allow.
      (3)   Do new curb, pavement base repair, and complete resurfacing in the
Hubbard Woods business district, and negotiate with IDOT towards a
jurisdictional transfer of this portion of Green Bay Road.
Mr. Saunders then explained staff’s assessment of each alternative. He
concluded by saying that, if the Village Council feels that the long-term
strategic benefits of being able to control Green Bay Road through Hubbard
Woods outweigh the long-term costs of all future maintenance of that roadway,
staff recommends Option 3. However, if the Village Council is not of that
opinion, then staff recommends proceeding with Option 2.
Manager Williams remarked that, while taking over a roadway is never
economically feasible, if there are strategic reasons for doing so, such as
pedestrian safety, curb bump outs, streetscape, etc., Option 3 opens up
opportunities.
Mr. Saunders responded to questions from the Trustees.
Trustees Greable, Spinney, and Pedian commented that, before making a decision,
they would like to have more information about the economic impact of taking
over the roadway now and in the future.
Trustee Johnson said he favors taking over Green Bay Road in Hubbard Woods,
noting that the streetscape implementation plan for Hubbard Woods involves only
east/west streets because of the need to engage with IDOT in order to make
changes to Green Bay Road.
Ken Behles, 426 Maple, a former Trustee and Chair of the ad hoc Streetscape
Technical Committee, spoke in support of choosing the third option, as Green
Bay Road is so long that it makes sense to take jurisdiction in incremental
steps when the opportunity occurs. He noted that these funds present a rare
opportunity to take a small bite out of a huge project and pointed out that
roadway improvements in Hubbard Woods are very important to the Hubbard Woods
merchants.
Terry Dason, Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce, echoed Mr. Behles’
remarks.
Noting that there was no consensus among the Trustees as to one particular
course of action, President Tucker announced that for the time being the
Council would keep all options open for further research and discussion.
   e)   Holiday Lighting Bids. Mr. Saunders reviewed the bids for holiday
lighting of public trees throughout the Village for the upcoming holiday
season, noting that, this year, at the urging of the business community, the
Village has expanded the number of trees to be lit in the Hubbard Woods
business district.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to award Bid 08-023, 2008
Holiday Lighting, to The Care of Trees for $38,385.00. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Poor, Greable, Spinney, Johnson, and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker thanked the Trustees and public for
attending the Elm Street Train Station groundbreaking event. She reported that
she had met with State Sen. Jeffrey Schoenberg to discuss pension reform and
mentioned the upcoming Special Study Session on the topic. She added that she
would be meeting with other local board presidents to discuss topics of mutual
interest. Finally she announced an upcoming showing of the documentary
The History of Winnetka, by John Newcomb, at the Community House.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Pedian reported on continuing work by the Plan Commission’s
subcommittee on Affordable Housing .
      ii)   Trustee Johnson reported that the BCDC had received a presentation
on brand imaging and noted some steps the BCDC is taking to promote the
business districts.
      iii)   Trustee Spinney reported that the Library Board special meeting
had been cancelled.
      iv)   Trustee Greable reported that he attended a recent NWMC legislative
meeting held to discuss the NWMC’s legislative agenda for the coming year, and
noted that pension reform continues to be the primary focus.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams asked Mr. Keys to brief the Council on the
CFL coupon that residents will find in their utility bill as a part of the IMEA
program to promote the use of CFL light bulbs.
11)   Appointments.
   a)   President Tucker asked for a motion to reappoint Gary Frank to the
Design Review Board for a full term and to serve as Chairman of the Committee,
as of November 1st. Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to so
appoint Mr. Frank. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   b)   President Tucker asked for a motion to appoint John Swierk to the
Design Review Board for a full term, beginning November 1st, to replace John
McLinden upon the expiration of his term. Trustee Spinney, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to appoint Mr. Swierk. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Pedian moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Board
Appointments, Personnel, Pending and Probable Litigation, and Collective
Bargaining pursuant to Sections 2(c)(3), 2(c)(1), 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(2),
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson,
Pedian, Poor, Rintz, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 10:50 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:35 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
36 p.m.
         
   Recording Secretary
October 13, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
October 13, 2009
(Approved: November 3, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 13, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Assistant Public Works Director Stephen
Auth, and 4 people in the audience.
2)   WEFC Strategic Plan and 2009-2010 Implementation Plan.
President Tucker welcomed the Chair of WEFC, David Abell.
Mr. Abell presented the WEFC’s strategic plan and said the WEFC would like
guidance from the Council about some initiatives the Commission would like to
pursue. He noted that the last Caucus Questionnaire revealed strong community
support for environmental issues, with most residents favoring an emphasis on
education rather than regulation, and commented that behavior can be strongly
influenced by economic incentives.
Mr. Abell then explained the four focus areas of the WEFC’s strategic plan: a)
energy conservation; b) forestry and green space; c) green buildings; and d)
water, air and solid waste. He stated that the WEFC is interested in
collaborating with neighboring municipalities, regional government bodies and
environmental and conservation organizations, and asked the Council to support
the WEFC’s recommendations, and to provide funding for the WEFC’s educational
tasks.
Following some suggestions and discussion with the Council, the public was
invited to comment.
George Conbeer, 780 Prospect and Caucus Chair, asked if the WEFC has thought
about communicating and collaborating with any of the Village’s other local
governing bodies.
Mr. Abell replied that some WEFC members prefer to let Village staff coordinate
communications with local governing boards, and noted that the Park District
and New Trier High School have representatives on the Commission.
Vicki Apatoff, 730 Ardsley, and Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce, voiced concern about
dwindling green space and the loss of trees in residential areas.
Mr. Abell explained the WEFC’s previous efforts to have the Council pass a
Heritage Tree Ordinance, but recalled that it was opposed by a majority of the
public at the time and that the issue would not be revisited in the near
future.
Mr. Abell then distributed and discussed a list of the WEFC’s current projects
and asked if for Council support mandatory recycling of construction debris and
prohibiting synthetic phosphorous in fertilizer. The Council agreed on the need
to educate the public, responded favorably to the construction recycling
proposal, and asked the WEFC to do more research and come back with scientific
and measurable reasons on the need for the phosphorous ban.
3)   Draft Ethics Ordinance.
Attorney Janega reviewed a PowerPoint presentation summarizing the fundamental
policy issues involved in amending the Village’s ethics ordinance. She noted
that normally conflict of interest prohibitions require some link between the
interest of the official in question and the subject matter over which the
official must make some decision, and pointed out that none of the communities
she had surveyed had an ethics ordinance with as broad a prohibition as the
draft that she had been directed to prepare.
Trustee Poor remarked that the presentation was a good informational exercise
and that there had recently been a situation in the Plan Commission which the
Council would like to avoid in the future, while at the same time steering
clear of wholesale disqualifications. He indicated support for an ordinance
similar to the Northfield’s, which provides for Council review of certain
situations, as opposed to being overly restrictive across the board.
There was an extensive discussion about how restrictive the ordinance should be
in order to avoid future problems, with the Council generally in favor of
having all board and committee members complete an annual disclosure statement,
and using the Northfield ordinance as a model for what Winnetka is trying to
accomplish. Attorney Janega remarked that Glencoe cites examples of violations
in its ordinance, which could be a helpful thing to include in the draft under
consideration in Winnetka.
Mr. Conbeer said the Caucus thinks the Village’s entire ethics code is too weak
and that it should apply to appointed boards. He added that the Caucus platform
would likely have an ethics plank.
Mr. Hecht said that apparent conflicts are very real and should not be
tolerated, and that rules should err on the side of caution to eliminate any
possible conflict. He expressed support for making blue-ribbon panels subject
to the ethics rules, in addition to the regular other boards and commissions.
Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka, agreed with Mr. Hecht’s comments, and said
standards need to be more stringent to avoid having Village consultants serving
on the Village’s boards.
Ms. Apatoff said the concern about narrowing the pool of candidates is valid
and noted that the real concern is preventing someone who serves on a Village
board from being given a competitive advantage when bidding on a Village
contract.
The Council weighed in with their views on the matter, and reached a consensus
to have Attorney Janega return at some later date with a draft ordinance that
is similar to Northfield’s. The draft will also include blue-ribbon panels and
families of board members, and give examples of conflicts of interest.
4)   Village President’s Core Objectives.
President Tucker asked the Council for their thoughts on the policy initiatives
and concerns contained on her list of Core Objectives. After a short
discussion, it was agreed that drainage issues should be discussed at a future
Study Session, and that the Police Chief will give his recommendations on
developing an administrative hearing procedure once he has completed his review
of the process.
5)   Reports.
None.
6)   Executive Session.
Trustee Pedian moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Personnel Matters, Board Appointments, Pending Litigation and
Collective Bargaining, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1), 2(c)(3), 2(c)(11) and 2(c)
(2) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Spinney seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 11:00 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:48 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
7)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
49 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
October 20, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
October 20, 2009
(Approved: November 3, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 20, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz, and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio,
Police Chief ’’Joe DeLopez, and four persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   October 27, 2009, Special Study Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   November 3, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker read the list of items on the Consent Agenda. Trustee Poor,
seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the Agenda as presented. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian,
Rintz, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda.
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   October 6, 2009, Regular Meeting
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1625 & 1626. Approving Warrant List No. 1625 in the
amount of $1,255,561.18, and Warrant List No. 1626 in the amount of
$433,698.34.
   c)   Ordinance MC-13-2009 – Reducing the Speed Limit on ––––Walden Road–– –
Adoption. This Ordinance would reduce the speed limit on Walden Road to 25 mph.
It was introduced at the October 6th Council meeting.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Greable, Rintz, and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-18-2009 – Zoning Variation: ––––933 Tower Rd.–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for two zoning variations to
permit the construction of an addition to an attached garage at 933 Tower Road.
The proposed plan is to turn the existing one-car garage with a front facing
door ninety degrees and construct an addition to accommodate a second car. The
addition would result in a roofed lot coverage 5.04% over the permitted maximum
and a front yard setback of 21.34 ft, whereas a minimum of 50 ft. is required.
He noted that the front yard setback is an existing nonconformity and that two
previous zoning cases were approved, but neither project was ever completed. He
added that the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended approval by a 4 to 0 vote.
The applicant, James Blum, explained that the main motivation behind the
request is to solve significant water problems. It is hoped that changing the
orientation of the garage and installing new pumps will change where the water
pools on the property, in addition to providing the homeowners with a second
garage bay.
Trustee Poor voiced support for granting the variation but commented that the
absence of a two-car garage does not constitute a hardship or significantly
impact reasonable return as there are numerous properties throughout the
Village that lack that feature.
Trustee Greable agreed and suggested the removal of language in the draft
ordinance stating that a two-car garage is considered a standard amenity for
homes throughout the Village.
The other Trustees concurred.
Trustee Spinney pointed out that at best this variation will not solve the
water issues, but will only facilitate the implementation of other water
mitigation efforts.
In response to a question from Trustee Pedian, who expressed concern about the
impact of increased impermeable surface on neighbors, Mr. Blum went on to
briefly explain his other plans for addressing the water problem.
Attorney Janega remarked that the Village Code requires engineering approval of
all plans. She added that the reference to the recital on economic return will
be revised as discussed by the Council.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to introduce Ordinance M-18-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2009 – Video Poker – Introduction. Attorney Janega
explained the draft ordinance follows the Council’s discussions on September 1
st and would amend the Village Code to prohibit video gaming machines and other
electronic gaming devices. She summarized the State’s new Video Gaming Act,
noting that it allows municipalities to pass an ordinance prohibiting video
gambling. She observed that no restaurant has expressed an interest in video
gaming, and that the State Gaming Board hasn’t yet enacted rules. She added
that the proposed ordinance would also clarify distinctions between different
types of gaming and amusement devices and how they are regulated by the
Village, and would update the current code provisions.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-12-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
None.
   a)   Policy Direction: Request for New Liquor License Category. Chief
DeLopez reviewed a request for a new liquor license category from the owner of
D’s Haute Dogs, 551 Lincoln Avenue. The applicant has requested the creation of
a license category that would permit him to sell canned beer with the purchase
of a hot dog. Chief DeLopez referred the Trustees to the information provided
by the applicant, as outlined in the Agenda Report and pointed out that the
beer sales would be in cans only, would be incidental to the purchase of food,
and that there would be no direct customer access to the beer cooler and no
sale of beer to go.
Trustee Rintz asked Chief DeLopez his opinion of the request. In response,
Chief DeLopez stated that this specific request does not greatly disturb him,
but that canned beer is easily portable, and he is also concerned about a
proliferation of requests to modify the Village’s ordinances.
Responding to a question from Manager Williams, Mr. Boyar indicated that the
beer could be served in glasses.
Trustee Pedian pointed to the casual nature of the operation and the fact that
it caters to families as possible causes for concern, and asked if the sale of
beer would have a significant positive impact on the applicant’s business, or
if it was merely “nice to have.”
Chief DeLopez replied that the applicant reports frequent requests for beer
service.
Trustee Spinney indicated that she was struggling with this request. She
expressed concern about alcohol abuse in the community and about whether this
would just be one more step down a “slippery slope.”
Trustee Johnson referred to the 2005 Village Caucus Platform, noting the
community’s opposition to liquor code revisions, particularly anything that
could resemble a “bar.” He stated that, while he hopes applicant’s business
succeeds, he cannot support this proposal.
The applicant, Jared Boyar, spoke in support of his request and responded to
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Greable commented on the 2005 Caucus survey question and Platform. He
observed that this is a fast food restaurant, which historically has been
omitted from being considered an acceptable place for liquor service, and that
until he is satisfied that the community wants to change its values relating to
alcohol, he cannot support this or any similar request.
Trustees Rintz, Pedian and Poor also indicated that they would not vote in
favor of this expansion to the liquor code at this time.
Noting the lack of support among the Trustees, President Tucker voiced her own
concerns and commented that there needs to be further discussion in the
community about expanding the parameters of liquor service in the Village.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported on her attendance at the
NWMC monthly meeting last week where information regarding the H1N1 flu
outbreak was presented. She added that pension reform continues to be a topic
of discussion among the members.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported that he had attended the recent meeting of
the Historical Society’s Board of Directors, and there was great excitement
over the success of the showing of The Winnetka Story. He also reported on the
Chamber of Commerce meeting, where the discussion focused on ways to generate
support for holiday sales.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney reported on the recent RED Center activities as
well as the Streetscape Technical Committee meeting and the Library Board
meeting, which was attended by former Trustee Berger in her stead.
      iii)   Trustee Johnson invited interested residents to attend the
upcoming meeting of the BCDC, where there will be a presentation and discussion
regarding branding.
      iv)   Trustee Poor reported on last week’s Environmental and Forestry
Commission meeting.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that New Trier high School would
take over the rebroadcast of Village Council meetings beginning on November 18
th on Channel 18. He also reported that the Village expects to implement an
electronic sign-up for the e-mail distribution of news and information in the
very near future. In response to a question from Trustee Pedian, Mr. Williams
stated that the Village would be sticking notices on garbage cans to advise
residents of the upcoming changes in the recycling schedule.
11)   Appointments.
   a)   President Tucker asked for a motion to appoint Lawson Whitesides as the
Environmental and Forestry Commission’s representative to the Plan Commission,
effective immediately. Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to so
appoint Mr. Whitesides. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel,
Pending and Probable Litigation, and Collective Bargaining pursuant to Sections
2(c)(1), 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(2), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Pedian seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz, and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 8:57 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:45 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
46 p.m.
         
   Recording Secretary
October 27, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
October 27, 2009
(Approved: November 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 27, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, and Jennifer Spinney.
Absent: Trustee Chris Rintz. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, and approximately
23 persons in the audience.
2)   Pension Education Session.
Finance Director Ed McKee noted that at the last budget process, the Council
indicated that it would like to see in-depth exploration of how the pensions
work and their impact on the Village budget. He introduced the actuary for the
Police and Fire pension funds, Tim Sharpe, and also their investment advisor,
Richard Potter of Becker, Burke Assoc.
Mr. McKee reported that the most pressing pension issues facing many government
bodies are not problems for Winnetka, due to the Village’s conservative
approach to managing the pension funds. He then gave an extended presentation,
that covered (i) why pensions are offered, (ii) how pensions work, (iii) State
statutes on public safety pensions; and (iv) an overview of Winnetka’s three
pension funds (Police, Fire and IMRF). He concluded with a review of the
funding status and investment return history of the three pension funds, and
then invited Tim Sharpe, the actuary for the Village’s Police and Fire pension
boards, to give a summary of his actuarial assumptions.
[Trustee Rintz arrived at 8:05 p.m.]
Mr. Sharpe reported that Winnetka is in a very strong position because its
Police and Fire pension accounts are well funded, with an estimated 15 years of
reserves on hand, because Winnetka has always made the full funding
requirement. He explained that the current problems have arisen because
investment returns have plunged twice in the last decade, drastically reducing
the funding ratios. He noted that the Village has time to address the
shortfalls thanks to its 15-year cushion of reserves, but advised that even if
the markets were to rebound and give back all the lost earnings, there is still
a lost year to be factored in. He briefly described how some communities are
dealing with their pension obligations, and answered a few questions from the
Council.
Rick Potter, the investment advisor for the Village’s Police and Fire pension
funds, explained that his firm does quarterly performance analyses and makes
investment recommendations to the pension boards, based on the statutory
requirements. He reported that there is a fair amount of diversification in
both funds, and that the investments have been conservative, which has been
very beneficial in the turbulent markets. He reviewed a recent quarterly
report, which showed that the equity funds did well despite the difficult
market conditions, and that the bonds outperformed the bond index and the major
market investments.
After Mr. Potter had answered the Council’s questions, the public was invited
to comment.
Bernie Hammer, 1455 Tower, said he thought the pensions are too generous and
are a burden to future generations of residents. He gave some suggestions for
saving money, including raising the retirement age, and questioned the salaries
of the Department Heads.
Winnetka Deputy Fire Chief Steve Laureys pointed out that the Village’s portion
of the Cook County tax bill is just under 14% of the entire bill, meaning that
the Village’s portion of a $10,000 tax bill is approximately $1,400. He noted
that this year’s pension levy is the largest in recent history, but is less
than 1%, which works out to an extra $100 on the $10,000 tax bill, or less than
$5 per firefighter employed by the Village, and he questioned whether this is
an area to examine for significant cost savings.
Winnetka Police Sgt. Marc Hornstein, secretary of the Police Pension Fund,
commented that the Village has a very competent Manager and department heads,
who by virtue of the fact that they are in the risk management business earn
generous compensation. He noted that while creation of a second tier pension
system was called for in Resolution R-25-2009, he pointed to information that
shows those plans ultimately cost more to administer.
Winnetka Fire Chief Scott Smith said the job of firefighter is a young person’s
job, due to the heavy gear that is worn and the strenuous nature of the job,
and that there are cumulative effects to the exposures to smoke and other
toxins that make 30 years on the job adequate.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, asked whether the figure that was budgeted last year
for pension funding was too low. Mr. McKee replied that the figure was close
but a little low on the IMRF.
Marc Hecht asked how the pension funds are constituted and who picks the
trustees. Manager Williams explained that State statutes require five members
each on the Police and Fire Pension Boards, each with two active employees, one
retired employee and two residents. He noted that the boards work very well
together.
Sandy Berger, 650 Ardsley, commended the Council and Staff for discussing such
a difficult topic in a public meeting, adding that there are no easy answers
for meeting the pension obligations going forward. She acknowledged the pension
board members in attendance, John O’Malley, Richard Glenn, Marc Hornstein and
John Ripka, and thanked them for the work they do.
Peter Sweeney, 827 Prospect, questioned the need for pension benefits to
attract and retain quality workers and that the Village could hire contractors
rather than employees to work for the Village. He suggested outsourcing the
Police Department, indicated that he was in favor of a volunteer fire
department, and added that in a community where he had lived the local scout
troop provided ambulance service.
John O’Malley, 1114 Merrill and vice president of the Police Pension Board,
said the Board began a program in March to put more funds into equity, and that
currently 44% of the funds are invested that way. He stated that, based on
preliminary research he has done, he supports keeping the defined benefit
pension program as he believes a defined contribution plan offers only phantom
savings. He observed that the members on the pension boards have no material
disagreements, and noted that residents’ interests are well-represented.
Winnetka firefighter John Ripka, President of the Firefighters Pension Board,
reiterated that only $5 of a $10,000 tax bill funds firefighter pensions, noted
that firefighters do not get Social Security, and questioned the assumption
that the benefits are overly generous.
President Tucker thanked everyone who had attended the meeting, noting that the
discussion had been educational.
3)   Reports.
None.
4)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters, Board Appointments, Pending Litigation and Collective Bargaining,
pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1), 2(c)(3), 2(c)11, and 2(c)2, respectively, of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive
Session at 9:48 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:59 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
00 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
November 3, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
November 3, 2009
(Approved: November 17, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 3, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz, and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Fire Chief Scott Smith and
two persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   November 10, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Poor, indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   November 17, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that the Agenda had been amended to add
consideration of approval of the Collective Bargaining Agreement with the
Metropolitan Alliance of Police. She read the list of the items on the Consent
Agenda and asked if any Trustee wished to remove any item before voting.
Trustee Greable requested that Item 5(a), the monitor defibrillator purchase,
be removed from the Consent Agenda. Then Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Spinney, moved to approve the Agenda as amended. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian, Rintz, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda (as amended).
   i)   Village Council Minutes.
      (1)   October 13, 2009, Study Session
      (2)   October 20, 2009, Regular Meeting
   ii)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1627 and 1628. Approving Warrant List No. 1627 in
the amount of $906,789.91, and Warrant List No. 1628 in the amount of
$317,805.23.
   iii)   Ordinance No. M-18-2009 – Zoning Variation: ––––933 Tower Rd.–– –
Adoption. This Ordinance would grant two zoning variations to permit the
construction of an addition to an attached garage at 933 Tower Road. It was
introduced at the October 20th Council meeting.
   iv)   Ordinance No. MC-12-2009 – Video Poker – Adoption. This Ordinance
would amend the Village Code to prohibit video gaming machines and other
electronic gaming devices. This matter was discussed at the September 1st
Council meeting and introduced at the October 20th Council meeting.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Greable, Rintz, and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-14-2009 – Amending the Village Code as it pertains to
Student Membership on the Environmental & Forestry Commission – Introduction.
Attorney Janega reviewed this Ordinance, which would amend the Village Code as
it pertains to student membership on the Winnetka Environmental and Forestry
Commission. She explained that the proposed changes would allow for the
appointment of one or more student members and permit those students to either
reside or attend school in Winnetka to qualify for the positions. These changes
would add flexibility and broaden the pool of available candidates. She noted
that as the student(s) have non-voting status, these changes do not alter the
voting procedures.
Trustee Poor, speaking as the Trustee liaison to the Environmental and Forestry
Commission, remarked that these proposed changes came about because the
students recommended by the environmental club advisor at New Trier this year
both live in Glencoe. He said these amendments take a more expansive view of
participation while maintaining a solid connection to Winnetka.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to introduce Ordinance No. MC-
14-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   b)   Resolution No. R-32-2009 - Collective Bargaining Agreement with the
Metropolitan Alliance of Police, Representing Winnetka Police Officers –
Adoption. Manager Williams summarized the significant economic provisions of a
tentative agreement reached with the union representing Winnetka’s police
officers regarding wages, hours, and working conditions. He noted that the
current contract expired on March 31, 2009, and the proposed new agreement
would cover the period from April 1, 2009 to March 31, 2011. He explained that
Illinois law is very strict in the governance of negotiations with public
safety employees, requiring binding arbitration in the event of an impasse. He
reported that the terms agreed to were reached after exhaustive study of
Winnetka’s comparable communities and lengthy negotiations between the parties.
Trustee Johnson complimented Winnetka’s public safety officers for their
dedication and professionalism, but went on to note that the ever-increasing
salaries and attendant pension benefits, which are mandated by the State, are,
in his opinion, unsustainable in the long term. He urged residents to
communicate their concerns about the escalating costs and the strictures of the
State-imposed bargaining system to their State legislators. Remarking on public
comments made at last week’s study session about restructuring the fire and
police departments to cut benefit costs, Mr. Johnson stated that he strongly
believes that such a move would not be desirable or appropriate for this
community and he would not support such action.
Trustee Greable commented that during the upcoming budget study sessions the
Trustees would be evaluating the impact that these contract changes will have
throughout the budget and the cost of all the services that the Village
provides.
Trustee Pedian added that Council worked diligently to be fair and respectful
to all stakeholders and to reach a mutually acceptable agreement recognizing
(a) how the Village’s employees are paid in comparison to our comparable
communities, (b) the impact of the downturn in the economy, and (c) State-
mandated rules and regulations.
President Tucker agreed that the Village needs to thoroughly explore ways in
which to cut the budget.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-32-
2009. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
None.
   a)   Policy Direction: Chamber of Commerce Banner Request. Manager Williams
reviewed a request from the Chamber of Commerce to hang a single banner across
Green Bay Road in the Hubbard Woods business district, to encourage holiday
shopping. It is anticipated that this banner would be hung on an annual basis
as well. He noted that in the past other organizations have requested, and may
again request, permission to hang similar banners. Heretofore, those requests
have been denied and the organizations have been directed to use the sign
kiosks that were recently constructed at considerable expense to the Village.
He said that from a technical standpoint, hanging the banner would not be a
problem, but he cautioned the Council to think about the implications of a
change in policy.
Trustee Johnson reported that the Chamber’s request had the full support of the
Business Community Development Commission.
Following further discussion, the unanimous consensus of the Council was to
approve the Chamber’s proposal for the Hubbard Woods banner and to direct staff
to work with the Chamber to find a suitable location for a banner in the Elm
Street business district.
   b)   Monitor Defibrillator Purchase. (This item was removed from the Consent
Agenda at the request of Trustee Greable.) Chief Smith reviewed the bids for
two monitor defibrillators and two professional automatic external
defibrillators. He explained that this emergency medical equipment is carried
on the Village’s fire engines and ambulances, is vital to the paramedics in
assessing cardiac patients, and must meet certain standards, including Illinois
Department of Public Health requirements, as well as field medic equipment
needs. He said that two of the four existing monitors have exceeded their life
expectancy and were scheduled for replacement in 2009; and that replacement of
the other two units is being considered because of increasing annual
maintenance costs. He recommended awarding the bid to be awarded to the low
bidder, Philips, in the amount of $48,816.20.
Trustee Greable explained that he had asked that this item be removed from the
Consent Agenda because services and equipment that are vital to the health and
safety of Winnetka’s residents are very important and should be thoroughly
examined. He asked Chief Smith a series of questions about the Department’s
equipment in general and the defibrillators in particular, the frequency of
their use, and the skills and training required to operate equipment designed
for paramedic use.
Chief Smith responded to Mr. Greable’s questions.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to award the bid as
recommended to Philips for two monitor defibrillators and two professional
automatic external defibrillators in the amount of $48,816.20. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Spinney, Johnson,
Rintz, Pedian, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker thanked staff and the participants
in last week’s study session for putting together an informative presentation
on public pensions. She encouraged residents who were unable to attend to watch
the webcast of the meeting on the Village’s website. She also announced that
she had asked Nan Greenough to join the ad hoc Village Hall Technical Committee
in evaluating the best way to proceed with the upcoming renovation of Village
Hall.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Rintz reported that the Plan Commission had heard from the
special subcommittee on the status of discussions on an updated affordable
housing plan.
      ii)   Trustee Greable reported on a recent meeting of the NWMC’s
Legislative Committee and that the focus for 2010 continues to be protecting
the health of local governments.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
President Tucker proposed the appointment of Becky Hurley as an alternate
Winnetka representative to the Community Advisory Group for the Willow Road CSS
process. Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to so appoint Ms.
Hurley. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel,
and Pending and Probable Litigation, and Board Appointments pursuant to
Sections 2(c)(1), 2(c)(11) and 2(c)(3), respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Spinney seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz, and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 8:45 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:15 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
16 p.m.
         
   Recording Secretary
November 10, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
November 10, 2009
(Approved: November 17, 2009 )
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 10, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Linda Pedian, Jennifer Spinney, Bill Johnson, and Chris Rintz. Absent: Trustees
Greable and Poor. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, and 3 persons in the
audience.
2)   2009 Property Tax Analysis.
Finance Director Ed McKee provided a background on the Village’s spending and
tax levy policies, explaining how the Village has managed to reduce its share
of the property tax bill. He reported that the Village’s conservative financial
policies have allowed the Village to weather the financial stresses of 2008 and
2009 well when compared to other municipalities, and that operating costs and
capital needs can be met with a modest property tax increase. He observed that
the Village has sufficient cash reserves and a sound budget overall, but the
challenge remains to keep expense increases in line with revenue projections.
He explained how the proposed tax levy is determined and recommended setting
the tax levy at $12,748,403, which would be a tax levy increase of 0.1% on the
existing tax base, and an overall tax increase, including new development, of
1.7%, which would add approximately $3 to a typical $20,000 tax bill.
Village Manager Williams referred the Council to the history of tax levy
changes on page 8 in the agenda materials, which shows that the Village has
kept its home rule pledge to stay within the tax caps, with a cumulative under
tax cap savings of nearly $1 million.
The Council discussion centered around reducing operating expenses and lowering
taxes, and the Council expressed their desire to hear the opinions of Trustees
Greable and Poor.
Mr. McKee informed the Council that the next steps were to introduce and adopt
an ordinance setting the tax levy by the second regular Council meeting in
December so the statutory deadlines can be met. It was the consensus of the
Council that Mr. McKee prepare the levy as recommended.
3)   Fidelity Bonds.
Finance Director Ed McKee explained the Village’s current fidelity bond
coverage and reported that, as directed, he evaluated the cost of purchasing
additional coverage for individuals who have responsibility for electronic
transfers and investing. He introduced the Village’s insurance adviser, Michael
Kelly, from Lamb, Little & Co., who answered questions from the Council.
After a short discussion, the Council agreed that no changes to the Village’s
current coverage were necessary.
4)   New State FOIA and Open Meetings Laws.
Village Attorney Janega summarized the key points to the changes in the Open
Meetings Act and Freedom of Information Act under P.A. 96-542 and explained
their potential impact on Village operations and expenses, including the
designation of a FOI officer, shorter response times, reduced copying fees, and
mandatory training. She reported that staff has already begun implementing some
of the procedural changes in advance of the January 1 effective date.
Manager Williams asked the Council to consider amending the 1994 Council
resolution setting the Village’s policies on FOIA requests, so that it reflects
the changes to the law.
Ms. Janega agreed and added that the fee resolution should also be updated to
reflect changes in allowable copying charges.
The Council directed Ms. Janega to prepare a resolution to update the Village’s
policy regarding responses to FOIA requests.
5)   Identity Theft Prevention Policy.
Finance Director Ed McKee provided the annual report, as required by the
Identity Theft Prevention Policy passed in 2008, noting two minor incidents
that had been investigated and resolved.
There being no comment or discussion, President Tucker thanked Mr. McKee for
his report.
6)   Reports.
Trustee Johnson reported that the Business Community Development Committee met
and has engaged pro bono advice to help the community prosper in these
troubling times.
Trustee Pedian reported that the Plan Commission is well on its way to refining
an affordable housing plan and that she has given her final recommendations to
them.
Manager Williams reported that, as directed when the Council decided to place a
banner in the Hubbard Woods business district, he has been working with the
Chamber of Commerce to identify possible locations in the Elm Street business
districts as well. He provided photos depicting a proposed location on the east
side of the intersection at Elm and Chestnut and another at the base of the Elm
Street bridge at Lincoln, and reported that the Chamber does not favor the Elm
and Chestnut location.
After discussing the two sites, the Council determined that neither location
was preferable and that more input from the Chamber was needed.
Trustee Pedian asked about placing signs on Green Bay Road near Elm and Manager
Williams responded that Illinois Department of Transportation approval would be
required.
7)   Executive .
None.
8)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion passed. Meeting adjourned at 9:49 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
November 17, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
November 17, 2009
(Approved: December 15, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 17, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz, Gene Greable, and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders and five
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   December 1, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   December 8, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Spinney, indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker read the list of items on the Consent Agenda and asked if any
Trustee wished to remove any item before voting. Then Trustee Johnson, seconded
by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda as drafted. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian, and Rintz,
and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda.
   i)   Village Council Minutes.
      (1)   October 27, 2009, Special Study Session
      (2)   November 3, 2009, Regular Meeting
      (3)   November 10, 2009, Study Session
   ii)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1629 and 1630. Approving Warrant List No. 1629 in
the amount of $859,205.12 and Warrant List No. 1630 in the amount of
$429,112.36.
   iii)   Ordinance No. MC-14-2009 – Adoption. This Ordinance would amend the
Village Code as it pertains to student membership on the Winnetka Environmental
and Forestry Commission. It was introduced at the November 3rd Council meeting.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Greable, Rintz, and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution No. R-33-2009 – Authorizing the Issuance of a Class E-
2 Liquor License to Good Grapes – Adoption. Attorney Janega reviewed a request
for issuance of a new Class E-2 liquor license. Under the E-2 liquor license
classification that was recently approved by the Council, a licensee would be
permitted to sell bottled wines and have limited wine tastings. She explained
that the subject Resolution would make a Class E-2 license available for
issuance to this applicant once the applicant is deemed to meet all of the
eligibility standards. She noted that this applicant has a similar business in
Glencoe that was, in fact, used as one of the models in the creation of
ordinance establishing the E-2 category.
Trustee Poor expressed some concerns, saying that he would like to see specific
details such as hours of operation incorporated into the Resolution.
Trustee Pedian inquired as to the status of individual who initially came to
the Council seeking this license category. Attorney Janega said that individual
has not yet settled on a location; therefore, no application has been submitted
for consideration.
Trustee Greable remarked that he would like to see more detailed information on
the application, and said that he could not support the application as
presented.
Ms. Janega explained that the liquor license application is a one-size-fits-all
form for use by the licensees of all of established licensed categories.
Trustee Johnson confirmed that the applicant is, in fact, planning to provide
wine tastings and that there will be no food service.
Attorney Janega explained that while the classification does not allow the sale
of drinks by the glass, it does permit tastings in connection with the retail
sale of the bottled wines as defined by the Village Code. She noted that while
food is permitted by the ordinance, it is not a requirement for the license.
The applicant, Teresa Lucas, explained how the wine tastings would be handled
and indicated that tastings would be limited to ¼ to ½ oz. each. She said she
had completed the application in keeping with the information in the Village
Code and planned to comply with the hours of operation specified therein,
although they are somewhat different than those in Glencoe. She went on to say
that she plans to provide reasonably-priced wines from specialized small
vineyards, as well as a very few boutique beers, although they will not be the
focus of the business.
Attorney Janega again reviewed the licensing process and the evolution of the
E-2 license category.
Noting that the applicant has completed the application as presented to her,
Trustee Rintz said he was opposed to penalizing her by delaying her opening
because the Council is asking for information that the application does not
require.
Trustee Spinney concurred, particularly given that delay would postpone the
applicant’s opening until after the Thanksgiving holiday.
Trustee Pedian indicated that she was inclined to vote in favor as well.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to authorize the issuance of a
Class E-2 liquor license to Good Grapes, 809 Oak Street, subject to the
conditions stated in the Resolution and discussed at this meeting. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Rintz, Pedian, and Spinney. Nays:
Trustees Greable and Johnson. Trustee Poor voted “Present.” Absent: None.
President Tucker welcomed Boy Scout Troop 18 to the meeting, noting that they
are working on their Communication Badge.
   b)   Resolution R-35-2009: Winnetka Avenue/Green Bay Road Traffic Signal
Engineering – Contract Supplement – Adoption. Mr. Saunders updated the Council
on the status of the project to modernize the traffic signal equipment at the
intersection of Green Bay Road and Winnetka Avenue, which is under the
jurisdiction of IDOT and subject to IDOT’s review and approval. He explained
that during their review processes, IDOT has repeatedly raised the number of
requirements to be met before the Village could proceed with the improvements,
significantly expanding the scope of the project. As a result of these
unforeseen increases in the scope causing additional time and work for the
design engineers, Clark-Dietz Engineering. a second contract amendment has been
requested to finalize the design plans and specifications. The subject
Resolution would authorize the expenditure of MFT funds to complete plans and
specifications for the project. Mr. Saunders mentioned that the State, through
Senator Schoenberg’s office, has committed $100,000 toward the construction of
this project as part of the State’s capital funding program. He then responded
to questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Resolution R-35-2009,
authorizing the expenditure of $33,800 of additional Motor Fuel Tax funds for
design engineering to complete plans and specifications for the Winnetka Avenue
and Green Bay Road Traffic Signal Improvements. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Poor, Greable, Spinney, Johnson, and Rintz.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Johnson then moved to authorize a contract extension not to exceed
$33,800 to Clark-Dietz, Inc., to complete design engineering for the Winnetka
Avenue and Green Bay Road Traffic Signal Improvements. Trustee Pedian seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson,
Rintz, Pedian, Poor, Greable, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
None.
   a)   Policy Direction: BCDC Branding Project. Mr. D’Onofrio explained that
the Business Community Development Commission, Village Officials and staff had
met recently with resident Mark Dziersk of Brandimage, to discuss a possible
branding project for the Village. As a result of these meetings, Mr. Dziersk
prepared the scope of services for the project that was included in the Agenda
packet. The community imaging program would revolve around the creation of a
logo and tagline which could be used in any number of ways. He noted that at
this point, the cost to the Village would be minimal as Mr. and Mrs. Dziersk
would be providing their services pro bono. The BCDC asked that the proposal be
reviewed by the Village Council before proceeding with the project.
Trustee Johnson, Chair of the Business Community Development Commission,
mentioned the names of some of the firms for whom the Dziersks have worked. He
added that the Commission doesn’t see this as a project to brand the entire
Village, because of all the entities and stakeholders that would need to be
involved, but rather as an imaging project for the business districts to create
a way to help Winnetka market itself to the outside world as a place to live
and do business. It is not intended to replace the familiar images that the
Village uses to identify its corporate bodies.
Mr. Johnson mentioned that Ken Behles, the Chair of the ad hoc Streetscape
Technical Committee, had been approached about the project and responded
positively to the suggestion that any design product might possibly be
incorporated into the streetscape elements.
Terry Dason, Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce, relayed the
Chamber’s enthusiastic support for the project.
There was unanimous consensus among the Trustees to move forward with the
project.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker thanked all of the participants for
their contributions to the Veterans Day celebration and Cenotaph rededication
ceremony. She also thanked the Police Department for the informative crime
prevention videos that can now be viewed on the Village’s website.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported on the Winnetka Historical Society meeting
on November 9 th and the Chamber of Commerce meeting on November 10 th .
      ii)   Trustee Spinney reported that former Trustee Berger had attended
last night’s Library Board meeting in her stead and reported that the Board is
moving forward with the Strategic Planning Committee.
      iii)   Trustee Pedian reported that a new feature focusing on “someone
you should know in the Village family” would appear in the upcoming edition of
the Winnetka Report.
      iv)   Trustee Poor reported on the most recent meeting of the
Environmental & Forestry Commission at which Mr. Saunders announced that the
long-awaited commercial recycling program is scheduled to begin in January.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the due to Comcast’s closing of
its Highland Park studio, rebroadcast of the Council meetings and the Bulletin
Board will be suspended on November 30 th until sometime in mid-December, at
which time it is hoped that rebroadcast will resume on Channel 10 pursuant to
an intergovernmental agreement being worked out with the City of Highland Park
who will be taking over the operation of the studio. In the meantime,
arrangements are already in place for the rebroadcast of Council meetings
through New Trier High School, and webcasts of the meetings are available
through the Village’s website.
Manager Williams then asked Mr. Saunders to update the Council on a draft
ordinance proposed by the Metropolitan Water Reclamation District that would
impact the Village if enacted.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Metropolitan Water Reclamation District of
Greater Chicago (MWRD) is responsible for collecting and treating wastewater
from Chicago and suburban Cook County. In 2004, it was given authority by the
State to regulate and manage stormwater within its service area, and to
establish rules and regulations for accomplishing this.
Recently, the MWRD released a draft of their proposed countywide Watershed
Management Ordinance (WMO) establishing the MWRD as the stormwater permitting
authority for Cook County and requiring landowners and developers to obtain
permits from the MWRD for residential and commercial development that takes
place in or adjacent to sensitive areas such as floodplains, riparian areas, or
wetlands. The draft ordinance establishes regulations for stormwater
management; soil erosion and sediment control; floodplain, riparian and wetland
development.
Mr. Saunders went on to review the major provisions of the draft ordinance and
their impact on Winnetkans, particularly those Winnetka properties within the
floodplain. He said Village staff has met with technical staff from other
affected communities in the area to develop a joint set of comments to submit
to the MWRD concerning the draft ordinance. Staff has also brought this issue
to the attention of the Northwest Municipal Conference so that other
communities are notified of the potential impacts of this sweeping new set of
regulations.
He indicated that the MWRD would be taking public comment on the proposed
regulations and urged Winnetka’s citizens to be aware of the pending new
legislation.
There was agreement among the Trustees that the community needs to be well
informed about the potential implications of these proposed regulations.
Manager Williams confirmed that this is a major issue, one that could have
significant implications in terms of property values. He added that it is very
important that the Village collaborate with other municipalities.
11)   Appointments.
President Tucker proposed the appointment of Bob Dearborn to the Design Review
Board to replace Scott Freres who resigned, effective immediately. Trustee
Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to so appoint Mr. Dearborn. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Pedian moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters, and Pending and Probable Litigation, Board Appointments, and
Collective Bargaining pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1), 2(c)(11) 2(c)(3), and 2(c)
(2), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson seconded
the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:47 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:37 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
38 p.m.
         
   Recording Secretary
