3)   Quorum.
   a)   December 8, 2009, Study Session. All of the Council members present,
except Trustee Spinney, indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   December 15, 2009, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney,
Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1631 and 1632. Approving Warrant List No. 1631 in
the amount of $998,994.18, and Warrant List No. 1632 in the amount of
$427,552.18.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing
item on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-19-2009 – 2009 Property Tax Levy -- Introduction . Mr.
McKee explained that the Village’s share of a typical Winnetkan’s total
property bill has declined by 22.2%, from the high point of 17.1% in 1993, to
its current 13.3%, through careful management of expenses, diversification of
General Fund revenues, and a reduction in the number of full-time employees
from 178 in 1989 to 162 in 2009.
Mr. McKee reported that the State is suffering significant financial stress in
the current bad economic conditions, which has resulted in a sizeable reduction
in pension asset values for municipalities. He also noted that utility sales
were weak last summer, but he pointed out that because of the Village’s
conservative financial policies, it has weathered the financial stresses well
compared to other municipalities. He stated that this year’s tax levy ordinance
would increase the total tax levy for the Village by a total of 1.7% for 2009,
amounting to an increase of approximately $3 on a typical $20,000 property tax
bill.
Bernie Hammer, 1455 Tower Road, expressed concern about the unfunded pension
obligations and questioned some benefits that are provided to the Village’s
employees.
Manager Williams pointed out that the Villages of Winnetka and Skokie were
instrumental in defeating a major pension enhancement proposed by the State
legislature, effectively saving taxpayers millions of dollars, and he noted
that President Tucker continues to participate with other communities and the
NWMC to address the issue. He observed that Winnetka has a track record of
saving money and the Village Councils over the years have been extremely
fiscally conservative.
After a short discussion, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved
to introduce Ordinance M-19-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-20-2009 – 2009 Tax Abatement – Introduction. There
being no comments, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to
introduce Ordinance M-20-2009 abating the tax levy for the Village’s Series
2003 Bonds which were issued for the Public Safety Building. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Mr. McKee responded to a question from Mr. Hammer about itemized contractual
services, and President Tucker welcomed Boy Scout Troop 18 to the meeting.
8)   Old Business.
   None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Set Council Meeting Agenda for 2010. Manager Williams reported that the
Council’s meeting schedule is set in advance, in order to take into account
holidays and election day conflicts, and that any Tuesday meeting that is
rescheduled because of a conflict would be either held the following Thursday,
or cancelled and wrapped into the following Study Session.
After a brief discussion the Trustees agreed that it would be in the best
interests of the community to continue rescheduling meetings that have holiday
or election day conflicts.
Attorney Janega noted that in 2010, the Tuesdays that would need to be
rescheduled would be the election days of February 4 th and November 4 th .
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian moved to approve the 2010 Meeting
Schedule. By voice vote, the motion carried.
