December 8, 2009
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
December 8, 2009
(Approved: December 15, 2009)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
December 8, 2009, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Convene Study Session.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:47 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, and Chris Rintz . Absent:
Trustee Jennifer Spinney. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike
D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus, and
approximately 5 persons in the audience.
   a)   Draft Ethics Ordinance -- Discussion. Attorney Janega reported resident
Jeffrey Liss had emailed that some questions to the Council. She said the
questions were helpful in identifying policy issues and circulated her memo
commenting on his questions. She observed that the core policy issue is whether
the Council wants to define conflict of interest in the classic sense based on
the link between an official’s or appointee’s interest in a matter and their
decision-making authority over that matter, or whether to define a conflict
based on any contact with the Village, even if that person has no authority on
the matter in question. She commented that taking the latter approach would
make Winnetka’s ordinance unlike any others in the area.
Attorney Janega then reviewed the revised draft, which attempts to use the
Northfield approach as a model, as directed by the Council. She explained the
amended definitions and other revisions, and elaborated on some unintended
consequences that could occur with some of the provisions in the draft
ordinance.
She explained that the Council had the choice to take a zero-tolerance
approach, which would treat every conflict as a disqualification for serving;
or instead choose to disqualify a person by having them recuse themselves,
which would retain the Council’s discretion and take circumstances into account
in each instance. She clarified that under the recusal option, once a conflict
is disclosed the person would remove themselves from any participation or
voting on the matter, but noted that certain Statutes define when a recusal
will not cure a prohibited interest and would prohibit a transaction. She added
that if there is an appearance of conflict, recusal isn’t required by Statute,
but recusal could be required if the Council desires.
There was a discussion about the issues involved with zero-tolerance, and then
the focus moved to the question of whether the prohibited interests should
include the interests of all family members and whether conflicts should also
include past relationships. It was the sense of the Council that the ethics
rules shouldn’t be so overly broad that they would disqualify a large pool of
potential committee appointees, and that the Council should retain a level of
discretion so as to strike a balance between recusal and dismissal.
President Tucker then asked if there were any audience comments.
Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka, said bringing the Village’s appointees under the
ethics regulations addressed 90% of the Caucus concerns, and asked a few
questions.
George Conbeer, 780 Prospect, speaking as the Caucus Chair, said the draft
ordinance addressed Caucus concerns, but added that, as a private citizen, he
thought the regulations needed to be shorter and less complicated.
Responding to questions about the disclosure statements, Attorney Janega
suggested that the Council could simply require copies of the statutory ethics
statements to be submitted to the Village.
The Council concurred with that suggestion, and directed the Village Attorney
to further revise the ordinance as discussed and to bring it before the Council
for introduction.
   b)   Retail Overlay District – Discussion. Mr. D’Onofrio summarized his
agenda report, and noted that the proposed revisions to the Special Use
application process were intended to help strike a balance between expediting
the Special Use approval process and protecting the desired retail activity in
the overlay district, while preserving the transparency of the special use
permit process. He reviewed the refinements staff had made to the original
proposals from the July Study Session, estimating that if the proposal to carve
out a one-stop exception process were put into effect, approval time for the
exceptions to retail uses could be cut roughly in half.
The Council discussed the proposal and debated the merits of having the ZBA or
Plan Commission review the retail exception applications before sending them to
the Council, after which President Tucker asked for audience comments.
Joanie Johnson, 888 Tower, said she thought the Plan Commission was the
appropriate body to review the exception applications and questioned why some
of the proposed additional standards were necessary.
Jim Sayegh, 570 Lincoln, suggested that streamlining the Special Use
application process should include his proposal to make a retail exception of
any use under 1,000 square feet, and he asked that the streamlining process not
be slowed down by further review from other committees.
Robert Dini, 1056 Gage, said a one-step approval process for the exceptions was
important and asked if fees could be reduced further.
Arthur Braun, 850 Bell Lane, said speeding up the special use process would
help to reduce vacancies, and suggested that the BCDC have some involvement in
the process.
After further Council discussion, there was agreement that the Council should
continue consideration of the proposed streamlining of the special use process;
fees should be examined one more time; legal notice requirements should also be
streamlined, and that both the one-step and two-step approval process should be
considered further. They also agreed that the BCDC, Plan Commission and ZBA
should review the proposals and make recommendations to the Council, and that
the issue would be discussed again, perhaps as early as the February Study
Session.
2)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing Personnel, pursuant to Section 2(c)(1) of the Illinois Open Meetings
Act. Trustee Rintz seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Spinney. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 11:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:45 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, and Rintz. Absent: Trustee
Spinney. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
3)   Adjournment.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Spinney. The meeting adjourned at
11:46 p.m.
