on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greable, Spinney, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Property Tax Levy .
      i)   Ordinance M-19-2009 – 2009 Property Tax Levy – Adoption. There being
no substantive questions or comments, Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to adopt Ordinance M-19-2009, setting the Village’s property tax
levy for 2009, an increase of 1.7% over last year. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Spinney, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
      ii)   Ordinance M-20-2009 – 2009 Tax Abatement – Adoption. There being no
further questions or comments, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney,
moved to adopt ordinance M-20-2009, abating the taxes levied to pay the
principal and interest on the General Obligation Refunding Bonds, Series 2003.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Spinney,
Johnson, Pedian, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-21-2009 – Landmark Designation: ––––790 Bryant Ave.–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed an application to designate ’’’’790 Bryant
Avenue’’ as a local landmark. He noted that the 1903 home, owned by Peter &
Lisa Bebe, is an excellent example of the Arts & Crafts style, is associated
with several individuals of historical prominence, and received a Landmark
Preservation Award earlier this year.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-21-2009. By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Tucker thanked the applicants for their efforts to rehabilitate this
part of Winnetka’s history, saying that preserving the past is as important as
redevelopment for our future.
   c)   Ordinance No. MC-7-2009 – Amended Ethics Regulations – Introduction.
Noting that this matter had been discussed at four previous meetings, Attorney
Janega reviewed the key changes to the draft ordinance amending the Village
Code to revise and expand ethics regulations for all Village officers,
employees and members of boards, commissions and committees. The ordinance
divides the Village’s ethics regulations into two chapters and expands the
conflict of interest provisions from a single paragraph to a comprehensive
chapter containing multiple sections. She pointed out that further clean-up
needs to be incorporated before adoption and called attention to two points
warranting further consideration by the Council: (1) whether to incorporate
additional language based on the Section 2.41.080 and (2) whether to include a
savings provision that would protect the Village against having its own actions
invalidated by the improper acts of an officer, employee or member of a board
or commission.
After an extended discussion, there was a consensus not to add the additional
provisions from Northfield and to replace the text of Section 2.41.100 with the
proposed savings clause.
George Conbeer, 780 Prospect, and Chair of the 2009 Caucus Council, said that
the critical element of the Caucus concern had been addressed, in that
appointees have been brought under the same umbrella as elected officials.
Mark Hecht, 1096 Spruce Street, commented that in his opinion, it was
inadvisable to include a “savings clause.”
Trustee Greable remarked that Attorney Janega has done an exceptional job of
drafting an ordinance improving the Village’s ethics regulations and
transparency.
The other Trustees added similar comments.
President Tucker thanked everyone for their thoughtful participation in this
important matter.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-7-2009, incorporating the evening’s discussion points. By voice vote, the
motion carried unanimously.
   d)   Resolution No. R-36-2009 – Ratifying the Collective Bargaining
Agreement between the Village and the International Association of Firefighters
– Adoption. Discussion of this matter took place after consideration of Item 9
(b).
Manager Williams reviewed the key provisions of a tentative agreement reached
with the union representing Winnetka’s firefighters, saying that it is
consistent with the terms of the agreement recently approved with the
’’’’Winnetka’’ police officers.
Chief Smith explained some of the operational provisions and responded to
questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve Resolution No. R-36-
2009. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Greable,
Spinney, Rintz, and Pedian. Nays: Trustee Johnson. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Policy Direction: Special Use Permit – ––––572 Lincoln Ave. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed a request for approval of a special use permit that would
permit a real estate office to occupy the first floor in the Winnetka Galleria
building at 572 Lincoln Avenue. He explained that the Petitioner, Anitfield
Finance Co., LLC, proposes to lease the space to Coldwell Banker, which would
consolidate its two current locations, 586 Lincoln, which is outside the
overlay, and 554 Lincoln, in the overlay to the south of the Galleria, into 572
Lincoln, where it would occupy 4,791 square feet of space, with roughly half of
that being within 50 feet of the front property line. Mr. D’Onofrio briefly
reviewed the history and purpose of the C-2 Overlay District, as well as the
Village Engineer’s comments on the parking study submitted by the applicant. He
noted that the matter is before the Council for policy direction because the
Plan Commission found the proposal was not consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan, and the Zoning Board of Appeals, had a tied vote of 3 to 3 and thus did
not recommend approval of the special use permit.
Bernard Citron, attorney for the applicant, presented his arguments in favor of
granting the special use permit, and his client, Jim Sayegh, added his
perspective.
Carry Buck, 609 Sheridan Road, voiced support for the request.
Patrick O’Rourke, Regional Vice President for Coldwell Banker, the firm that
plans to consolidate its offices, explained the organization’s business reasons
for wanting to move. He mentioned that similar requests had been approved in
Lake Forest and Wilmette and went on to respond to questions from the Trustees.
He pointed out that the location in the Galleria offers Coldwell Banker
employees the opportunity to use available parking in the Lincoln Avenue lot
via the back entrance to the building, thereby taking their parking off of
Lincoln Avenue.
Heidi Prassas, 834 Westwood Lane., Wilmette, spoke on behalf of the owners of
the building in which the Coldwell Banker South office is presently located,
saying that to permit the relocation would seriously damage their property’s
value.
John Myefski, 867 Grove, Glencoe, the Galleria’s architect, presented his
perspective on its design and limitations and spoke in favor of granting the
request.
Richard and Betsy Rutledge, the owners of 586 Lincoln, spoke in opposition to
the special use permit, saying that it would impair the value of their property
as well as violate the intent of the C-2 Overlay District.
Nan Greenough, 500 Maple Street, also spoke against granting the special use
permit, pointing out that once the space is gone as a retail space, the Village
is unlikely to ever get it back.
After an extended discussion, President Tucker polled the Trustees.
Trustee Poor stated that he could not support the request because it would be a
major displacement and interruption to the retail use on Lincoln Avenue in a
prime location. He pointed out that the Council had taken an exhaustive look
the Overlay District and determined in the spring to make some revisions and
major concessions to it but, at the same time, reaffirmed the basic principle
that the Village wants to encourage the clustering of retail in the District,
not erode its presence.
Trustee Pedian echoed Mr. Poor’s comments.
Trustee Rintz agreed, adding that because the proposal would add additional
non-retail street frontage in the heart of the District, which would be
contrary to all of the decisions already made about the Overlay District, he
could not vote in favor of the proposal.
Trustees Johnson and Spinney urged the applicants to consider some creative
solutions to the problem.
Trustee Greable also indicated that he could not support the request because
residents have indicated that they do not want more non-retail uses in the
District, which exists for the purpose of protecting the Village’s retail mix
and providing sales tax dollars.
Noting the consensus to deny the request, President Tucker offered the
applicant the opportunity to take time to go over possible options with Village
staff and his architect and the applicant replied affirmatively.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to table the matter. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   b)   House Bill 5088 – Public Safety Pension Reform Bill. Mr. McKee reported
that last fall, the State legislature implemented public safety pension
reforms. He categorized the purpose of the law as: i) improving dissemination
of pension information; ii) encouraging good business practices; and iii)
strengthening ethics and enforcement of ethics infractions. He noted that both
public safety pension boards have directed the Village Treasurer to meet the
annual reporting requirement to comply with the law, and that both he and the
Village’s auditor agree that a separate report is preferable.
Mr. McKee then responded to questions from the Trustees.
Also, in response to a question from Manager Williams regarding an issue raised
by a resident regarding Social Security payments for Village employees, Mr.
McKee confirmed that employees do, in fact, pay into Social Security and,
therefore, receive a normal Social Security benefit.
Attorney Janega explained that the resident had been referring to the Social
Security Windfall Elimination Provision, which does not apply to Village
employees because they contribute to both a public pension and Social Security,
and she distributed informational materials from the Social Security
Administration’s website.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker announced that an RFP had been
issued for an executive search firm to assist the Council in its efforts to
find a successor to Manager Williams, who will retire on June 1 st .
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported on the most recent NWMC meeting and noted
the NWMC’s proactive position on the draft MWRD ordinance.
      ii)   Trustee Johnson reported that the BCDC had met with the branding
consultants and is looking forward to seeing their work product as early as
February.
      iii)   Trustee Spinney reported on the most recent meetings of RED Center
and the Library Board.
      iv)   Trustee Poor reported that the WEFC hopes to have its own website
up during the next year and will be working closely with SWANCC on a new
electronics recycling program.
   c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported on new Supreme Court rules of
professional conduct for attorneys who appear before administrative bodies and
said she will be advising the Village’s various boards and commissions about
how it may affect their proceedings.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams announced that rebroadcast of the Council
meetings is expected to resume in early January on Channel 10. He also reported
that as a result of arduous negotiating on the part of Mr. Saunders, a
controversial clause was removed from the new contract with Groot. Lastly, he
congratulated Mr. Saunders on spearheading the efforts of the NWMC regarding
the MWRD ordinance.
11)   Appointments.
President Tucker suggested the appointment of Mary Hickey to the Zoning Board
of Appeals for a full term, effective immediately. Trustee Poor, seconded by
Trustee Pedian, moved to appoint Ms. Hickey. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
President Tucker then suggested the appointment of Marilyn Garcia to the
Landmark Preservation Commission for a full term, effective immediately.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to appoint Ms. Garcia. By
voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Pedian moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters, Board Appointments, and Pending Litigation pursuant to Sections 2(c)
(1), 2(c)(3), and 2(c)(11 ) , of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Spinney and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 11:02 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:33 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Spinney and Rintz. Nays: None.
Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Spinney and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
34 p.m.
            
