Recording Secretary
January 12, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
January 12, 2010
(Approved: February 4, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 12, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, King Poor, and Chris Rintz. Absent: Trustees Linda
Pedian and Jennifer Spinney. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Public Works
Director Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, and
approximately five persons in the audience.
2)   Project Update: Village Hall Restoration and Renovation.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the progress of the Village Hall restoration/renovation
project, which began in 2007 with a needs analysis that identified a scope of
work estimated at $2.9 million. He reported that the exterior improvements were
brought to the Council for discussion in June, 2009, and the Council’s
consensus was to proceed with the roof and exterior repairs recommended by the
Village’s consultant, Holabird & Root (H&R), although no definitive conclusion
was reached about the windows.
Mr. Saunders stated that the DRB and LPC have strongly recommended restoring
the existing windows and replacing the storm windows, and that the consultants
are working with an ad hoc technical committee to evaluate the window options
and interior improvements. He noted that the exterior of the building is
suffering from deferred maintenance, the roof leaks, and there are climate
control issues throughout the building. He recommended proceeding with the
project, as the Village Hall is a beautiful landmark which is in need of
stewardship to prevent further decay and restore it to its original grandeur.
Mr. Saunders summarized the major components of the project, which include
exterior repairs; window renovation; a new HVAC system; updating of the Council
Chambers to accommodate modern technology; modernizing and improving
functionality in the Finance Department offices; installing fire sprinklers
throughout the building; and renovating the basement.
Mr. Saunders introduced the H&R project team: Ernie Wagner, project manager;
Mary Brush, preservation expert; and Amy Milobowski, the project architect.
Ms. Milobowski reviewed the renderings of the interior renovations, discussed
the proposed technical upgrades, and answered questions from the Council.
Ms. Brush reviewed the process for repairing the roof, clock tower, and outside
stairs; described the cleaning methods for the limestone exterior walls; and
reviewed the restoration of the windows, which she described as in remarkably
good condition.
Mr. Saunders next reviewed the agenda information about the process and project
costs associated with LEED certification, estimated the cost of LEED
certification at approximately $150,000 and asked the Council if they would
like to commission the consultants who are needed to verify compliance with
LEED standards.
Mr. Wagner noted that the only additional costs are the “soft” costs associated
with getting the official certification, as the base project is already
committed to using the most energy efficient materials and construction.
A discussion about the question of LEED certification and the project cost
elements followed, during which Mr. Saunders explained that cost projections
were formulated using staff’s best knowledge of the current market and that the
actual project cost will only be revealed when bids are received.
Manager Williams pointed out that the Village is in a very strong financial
position, with $20 million in reserves, $10 million of which can be spent on
capital improvements, which is an enviable position to be in, given the state
of the economy. He noted that if the Village Hall renovation is completed at a
cost of $3.5 million, that still leaves $6.5 million for other capital
projects.
Ken Behles, 426 Maple, an architect and former trustee, said the building needs
the work and strongly recommended in favor of spending the extra funds to
obtain LEED certification, as it’s important for public entities to set an
example and to be leaders in the area of green and sustainable design. He added
that he has never seen a better bidding environment, and urged the Council to
approve sending the project out for bids.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, said the public is smart enough to believe that the
construction is energy efficient without spending the extra money for the
certification.
After further deliberation, the Council was in nearly unanimous agreement and
directed the consultants to prepare bid documents and specifications with
alternates.
Mr. Saunders indicated that the bid request could be ready to send out around
March 1 st , and could be back for Council consideration of bids towards the
end of April.
3)   Capital Plan for 2010/2011 Village Budget.
Manager Williams explained that this is a preliminary review of proposed
capital projects, and the Council will have several more chances for discussion
and decision-making at budget time.
Mr. McKee reported that he has gone into more detail in his agenda report than
in previous years, and has included a breakdown by fund to provide background
for the Council. He noted that the Village has enough cash overall to fund
routine capital expenses, but that financing needs to be examined for the
proposed storm water drainage project.
Following a lengthy discussion about drainage projects, Trustee Greable said he
thought the Council should act on the Burke engineering study while they can
still rely on Manager Williams’ expertise. Mr. Saunders explained that the most
serious flooding problem in the Village is in the study area in southwest
’’’’Winnetka’’, as the water has been getting into people’s homes on a routine
basis, but pointed out that there will always be some flooding on the streets
and in yards, as the area is in the flood plain.
Mr. McKee continued his review of the funds and discussed several upcoming
projects, including a possible second transformer in Northfield if the electric
load continues to increase, and improvements at the water plant. He added that
more detailed presentations and discussions will take place at budget time, but
this was a snapshot of what is upcoming.
Justin Nolan, 159 DeWindt, expressed support for the drainage improvements at
Hill Road, as flooding occurs in the area every summer.
4)   Reports.
None.
Executive Session. None.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Present: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Poor,
and Rintz. Absent: Trustees Pedian and Spinney. The meeting adjourned at 10:10
p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
January 19, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
January 19, 2010
(Approved: February 4, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 19, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, and Jennifer Spinney.
Absent: Trustee Chris Rintz. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders,
Water & Electric Director Brian Keys, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, Assistant to
the Village Manager Liz Rosenthal, and approximately three persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   January 23, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present, except Trustee Greable, indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   January 26, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present, except Trustee Poor, indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   January 28, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present, except Trustee Poor, indicated that they expected to attend.
   d)   February 4, 2010 (Thursday), Regular Meeting. All of the Council
members present, except Trustee Poor, indicated that they expected to attend.
   e)   February 6, 2010 (Saturday), Budget Meeting. After a brief discussion
about holding meetings on Saturdays, President Tucker decided to cancel and
reschedule this Budget Meeting.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that Item 9(b), the Rapid Mix Bids for the Water
Plant, had been added to the Consent Agenda. Trustee Johnson, seconded by
Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda, as amended. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1637 and 1638. Approving Warrant List No. 1637 in
the amount of $1,015,001.63, and Warrant List No. 1638 in the amount of
$363,975.95.
   b)   Village Council Minutes
      i)   December 15, 2009, Regular Meeting
      ii)   January 5, 2010, Regular Meeting
   c)   Rapid Mix Bids, Water Plant. – Awarding the bid for the purchase of one
chemical induction (rapid mix) unit for the Water Plant to treat incoming lake
water at the beginning point of the water treatment process to the low bidder,
Siemens Water Technologies, in the amount of $82,988.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian and Greable. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-1-2010 – Zoning Variation: ––––1004 Pine Tree Ln.–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for a front yard setback
variation to permit the construction of a new, single-family residence on the
subject property, resulting in a setback of 23.5 ft, whereas a minimum of 50
ft. is required. He noted that the property is irregularly shaped and has
street frontage on two streets, impacting the setback requirements. He reported
that the Village Council had approved a similar variation for this property in
2008, adding that some minor design modifications were made and, because
construction did not begin within 12 months of the prior approval, the permit
has now lapsed and a new variation is required. He added that, in 2001, an
adjacent property with the same setback requirements was granted a variance for
25-foot front setback for new construction. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the
Zoning Board of Appeals had voted 6 to 0 to recommend granting the variation.
Trustee Poor confirmed with Mr. D’Onofrio that this variation is essentially
the same as the one approved in 2008.
Responding to a question from Trustee Pedian, Mr. D’Onofrio stated that the
Zoning Board had not made any recommendations as to whether a fence or new
landscaping should be installed along the north lot line.
Attorney Janega explained that the draft ordinance incorporates the language
from the original variance and noted the addition of a provision containing the
standard 12-month limitation and correction of references to the dimensions of
the original and current requests.
The applicant, Pat Albion, asked if the location of the air conditioners needs
to be specified in the ordinance and the Village Attorney confirmed with Mr.
D’Onofrio that it was not necessary.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance M-1-
2010 as amended by the Village Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Resolution No. R-4-2010 - Willow Road Federal Aid Rehabilitation
Amendment to Phase I Engineering Contract – Adoption. Mr. Saunders explained
that in 2007, the Council awarded a contract to Civiltech Engineering to
provide preliminary engineering services for a proposed Federal-aid
rehabilitation of Willow Road. He related that three issues beyond the scope of
the original contract arose in the course of performance and that the Village
also asked Civiltech to perform extra pavement investigations, for a total
additional cost of $53,823. Mr. Saunders noted that the work is being done with
Motor Fuel Tax funds, under IDOT’s standard form of agreement for such
projects, and that resolution R-4-2010 would authorize the expenditure of
$53,900 of additional Motor Fuel Tax funds for design engineering to complete
Phase I Engineering for the Willow Road Federal-aid Rehabilitation.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Road, expressed concern about plans to eliminate the
split phasing of the light at Hibbard and Willow Roads.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-4-
2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Greable, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve a contract
extension not to exceed $53,823 to Civiltech Engineering to complete Phase I
Engineering for the Willow Road Federal-aid Rehabilitation. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Poor, Greable, Johnson and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
   c)   Resolution No. R-5-2010 – Power Plant Fuel Tank Storage – Adoption.
President Tucker announced that the Council would consider this resolution
after it considers awarding the Fuel Tank bid, Item 9(c).
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, expressed concern about the direction Council gave to
staff at its last study session regarding LEED certification for the Village
Hall renovation and rehabilitation. He voiced strong reservations about
pursuing LEED certification at the estimated cost of $150,000, saying that the
Village would be setting an example by its actions and does not need a plaque
to prove that it has done the responsible thing. He asked that the items
pertaining to LEED certification be removed from the bid package or that the
matter be put on the agenda for a formal vote by the Council.
Trustee Poor remarked that this issue already has been extensively debated
extensively in public, and the decision should not be revisited. He agreed with
earlier comments made by Trustee Rintz to the effect that there is more at
stake than just a plaque, stating that this is an opportunity for the Village
to set an example for all who do work subject to the Village’s ordinance, which
specifically encourages LEED certification.
After further discussion, President Tucker denied Mr. Kates’ request.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Winnetka Cenotaph Restoration – Change Order #2. Mr. Saunders reviewed
additional items that arose during the restoration process, giving rise to a
second change order request to complete the project. Mr. Saunders responded to
questions from the Trustees and reported that the total cost of construction
was $376,064, with over $67,000 being provided by private donations.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved   to authorize Change Order
#2 for the Winnetka Cenotaph Restoration in the amount of $11,058.04. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Poor, Greable, Johnson,
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
Manager Williams thanked Mr. Saunders for personally shepherding this project
through to completion.
   b)   Rapid Mix Bids, Water Plant. [See Consent Agenda, Item (5)(c), above.]
   c)   Bid 09-013A: Diesel Fuel Tank Project. Mr. Keys summarized the history
of the Village’s efforts to relocate the diesel fuel tanks necessary for the
operation of the Electric Plant. He reviewed the bids for the installation of a
new concrete structure at the lower rear elevation of the Electric Plant to
store the Village’s supply of diesel fuel. He recommended the project be
awarded to Keno & Sons Construction Company, the lowest qualified bidder, in
the amount of $309,571.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to award Bid No. 09-013A
for the installation of a diesel fuel tank at the Electric Plant as
recommended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Spinney, Johnson, Pedian, and Poor. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
President Tucker announced that the Council would now consider Resolution R-5,
which is Item 6(c) under Ordinances and Resolutions.
* * * * * *
   6(c)    Resolution No. R-5-2010 – Power Plant Fuel Tank Storage – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that the Village Code’s fire safety provisions set
capacity limits for above-ground fuel storage tanks and that this Resolution
would grant an exception to those limitations, which were intended to address
residential applications and minimize hazards in residential neighborhoods, to
allow the installation of a 20,000 gallon diesel fuel storage tank in a new,
confined concrete structure at the Power Plant.
Mr. Keys responded to questions from the Trustees, after which Trustee Poor,
seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-5-2010. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Greable, Spinney, Johnson,
and Pedian. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
* * * * * *
   d)   Construction Services for Diesel Fuel Tank Project. Mr. Keys reviewed a
request for a change order for additional funds for construction services
associated with the installation of the diesel fuel tank.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve a Change Order to
Strand Associates, Inc., in the amount of $9,500 to perform construction
services required to complete the diesel tank project at the Electric Plant. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Poor, Greable,
Spinney, and Johnson. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
   e)   Administrative Hearing Process. Chief DeLopez summarized his Agenda
Report regarding the establishment of a local, quasi-judicial administrative
hearing process, as permitted by State law, to adjudicate municipal ordinance
violations, thereby removing these violations from the circuit court system. He
mentioned several advantages, noting that it would not preclude the Village
from taking a matter to the circuit court. He commented that he had not
initially supported this process, but has become a supporter given the problems
in the circuit court system. He explained that the goal is to increase
efficiency and effectiveness of the enforcement process, and to change behavior
to make the community safer. He added that the proposed hearing process could
achieve better compliance with local ordinances and create a more convenient
adjudication process. Chief DeLopez noted that he had observed and spoken to
other towns with administrative hearings and received favorable reports. He
opined that the administrative hearing process would permit the Village to
better address property maintenance issues and nuisance complaints that are
frequently thrown out of court for the sake of expediency. He commented that,
although he had no definite cost analysis at this time, the income generated by
the process generally underwrites the operating costs, but that there are
significant up-front costs to set up the process. He urged the Council to move
ahead by issuing an RFP to consolidate existing ticketing processes and
recordkeeping, and an RFP for an administrative law judge, and to begin work on
amending the Village Code to incorporate an administrative hearing process.
President Tucker thanked the Chief for his report and a lengthy dialogue
ensued.
Manager Williams commented that it is frustrating to hear complaints about
parking in the Village and have the court not see the issue as a problem. He
also recommended that the adjudicator be a direct appointee of the Village
Council and urged the Council to move forward to finish its analysis and
consideration of the process.
Sgt. Rich Carlson reviewed the current ticketing and appeal process, noting
that it is time-consuming and labor-intensive. He commented that what is
important to Winnetka is sometimes considered trivial to the court system.
Responding to a question from Trustee Spinney, Chief DeLopez said that the new
process would be an adjunct to peer jury, because some of the matters can be
handled without a criminal arrest.
Trustee Greable commented that the proposed process would have a significant
impact on the community and needed more study and community input before
implementation, and suggested putting the matter on a study session agenda.
Trustee Poor thanked the Chief for bringing this discussion to the Council and
remarked that the new process would give more effect to Village laws such as
the ban on cell phones, because infractions would be treated more in the manner
that was originally intended. He added that it could also keep juveniles out of
the court system.
Trustee Pedian agreed on the need for more community dialogue before
implementation.
Noting a consensus among the Trustees to keep this discussion alive, President
Tucker announced that the matter would be slated for a study session after the
budget season.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker announced that due to the press of
time and other issues, Trustee Pedian would take her place on the ad hoc
Village Hall technical committee.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Poor reported on the Village Attorney’s recent presentation
to the Environmental and Forestry Commission. He also reported that the
Commission is moving forward with demolition and construction recycling
provisions and hopes to have recommendations before the Council in the near
future.
      ii)   Trustee Johnson reported on the most recent BCDC meeting, at which
the BCDC discussed parking and the North Shore Convention and Visitors Bureau
made a presentation and encouraged the community to join the organization. He
said the consensus among BCDC members is to set the matter on a Council agenda.
      iii)   Trustee Spinney reported that the Library’s Strategic Planning
Committee had had a successful first meeting.
      iv)   Trustee Greable reported on recent Historical Society, Chamber of
Commerce and NWMC meetings.
   c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that she had provided an orientation
session for the newest member of the ZBA, had met with the Environmental and
Forestry Commission as reported by Trustee Poor, and would be meeting next week
with the Plan Commission. She also reported that the new FOIA amendments are
now in effect and that Mary Ivins, Deputy Clerk, will be serving as the
Village’s FOIA Officer. She also announced that she is serving on the planning
committee for the Illinois Institute of Legal Education’s Municipal Law Update
Program. Finally, she reported that on January 6 th , Winnetka, Skokie, the
IML, and the International Municipal Lawyers’ Association had filed an amicus
brief in the U.S. Supreme Court in the Chicago and Oak Park handgun ordinance
cases. She noted the brief supports local handgun regulations, and that the
Village’s lead role was part of the commitment the Village Council made to the
public after amending the Village’s handgun ordinance in the face of the
National Rifle Association’s suit.
   d)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that the preliminary 2010-11 Budget
would be available for inspection at the Library on Friday. He also reported
that the rebroadcast of Council meetings has resumed on Channel 10 and that it
continues to be available on Channel 18 and on the Village’s website.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Spinney seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Rintz. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:12 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor and Spinney. Absent: Trustee
Rintz. Also present: Village Attorney Katherine Janega and Assistant to the
Village Manager Liz Rosenthal.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz. The meeting adjourned at
11:13 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
February 4, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
February 4, 2010
(Approved: February 16, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
February 4, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, Chris Rintz and Jennifer Spinney.
Absent: Trustee Poor. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Community
Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys,
and approximately 30 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Greable took a moment to thank the community, neighbors, friends, the
trustees and the fire and police chiefs for the comfort and support given to
his family at the time of his son’s death.
President Tucker acknowledged the troop leader and members of Boy Scout Troop
20 who were in attendance to accomplish advanced requirements for merit badges
in citizenship and communications.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   February 9 Study Session. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
   b)   Thursday, February 11 Budget Meeting . All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   February 16 Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   d)   Thursday, February 18 Budget Meeting.  All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   e)   Thursday, February 25 Budget Meeting.  All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that the special use permit request for 572 Lincoln,
Item 8(a), had been removed from the agenda and would be considered at the
February 16 th meeting. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to
approve the Agenda as amended. By roll call vote. the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Poor.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   January 12, 2010, Study Session.
      ii)   January 19, 2010 Regular Meeting
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1639 and 1640. Approving Warrant List No. 1639 in
the amount of $1,543,243.17, and Warrant List No. 1640 in the amount of
$903,085.84.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-1-2010 – Zoning Variation: ––––1004 Pine Tree Ln.–– –
Adoption. Approving a request for a front yard setback variation in order to
construct a new home on the Subject Property.
   d)   Electric Plant Intake Dredging Bid #09-022. Awards a bid for the
dredging of the intake and cooling ponds at the Electric Plant.
   e)   Lead Service Replacements; Biagi Plumbing Corporation. Extends the
contract for lead service replacements to the lowest qualified bidder, Biagi
Plumbing, for the evaluated amount of $70,000 to March 31, 2011.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Poor.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-2-2010 – Zoning Variation: ––––655 Sheridan Road–– -
- Introduction .
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a front yard setback variation on the
Subject Property, which is being sought because a private road easement creates
a hardship for the property owners, who wish to build a home on the current
vacant lot. He noted that the proposed construction meets with all other zoning
requirements, is sized modestly for the lot, and the ZBA voted unanimously at
its December, 2009 meeting to recommend approval for the variation.
Trustees Greable and Pedian raised questions about granting a variation to new
construction that Mr. D’Onofrio answered with several examples of circumstances
where variations had been granted.
Steve Friedman, attorney for the applicant, and Paul Konstant, architect,
explained the hardship created by the private road easement, and added that the
variation was also requested to preserve table land, an historic fence and
mature trees on the lot. Mr. Konstant noted that the home to be built reflects
the character of older houses in town and the scale was maintained in
consideration of the trees.
Trustee Rintz commented that if it were not for the phantom easement, the house
would be conforming and that is what is driving the need for the variance.
There were no comments from the audience and no further discussion.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to introduce Ordinance M-2-
2010. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2010 – Amending the Village Code to Authorize IMEA
to Operate as a Curtailment Service Provider – Introduction/Adoption.
Mr. Keys reported that a recent ruling by the Federal Energy Regulatory
Commission (FERC) changed the way power is bought and sold which necessitated a
change in the Village Code. He explained that the Village has been asked by its
wholesale electric provider, IMEA, to adopt an ordinance authorizing IMEA to
operate as a curtailment service provider to Village-owned generation
facilities. He said the ordinance would ensure that the IMEA is able to secure
funds to offset costs that otherwise would impact the wholesale price of power
for the Village. He added that there is a time constraint on this action and
asked that the Council introduce and adopt the ordinance this evening.
There was no further discussion.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to waive introduction. By
voice vote, the motion passed.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-2-
2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson,
Pedian, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Poor.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
   a)   Policy Direction: Special Use Permit 572 Lincoln Ave. This item was
removed from the agenda and will be considered on Feb 16 th .
9)   New Business.
   a)   Policy Direction: Special Use Permit – ––––310 Green Bay Road––
(––––North– ––Shore– ––Country– ––Day School––).
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the request of North Shore Country Day School for an
amendment to the school’s special use permit and two zoning variations to
permit the construction of additions that will add approximately 7,000 square
feet to the Upper School at 310 Green Bay Road and improve accessibility and
security by way of elevators and stair enclosures. He noted that the Zoning
Board, Plan Commission and Design Review Board all recommended in favor of the
request. Mr. Norkus distributed the final plans to the Council which had been
received this afternoon.
Tom Doar, head of North Shore Country Day School, and Mr. Trung Le, design
principal with the architectural firm of OWP, explained both the reason for the
improvements and additions to the school and the concept of the final plan. Mr.
Le also spoke about the process that led to the modern design for the additions
and the features that use green technology. He noted that the lateness of the
plan submittal was so that the comments of the Design Review Board could be
incorporated.
In response to a question from Trustee Johnson about building height, Mr.
D’Onofrio stated that the maximum height allowed is 60 feet because of the
unique nature of institutions in a residential area. He said the height of the
upper school with the additions will be within the limits at 45 feet. He noted
that the school currently has 3 stories and there will be 4 stories on the
south side, but because of the change in elevation, the ridge line remains the
same.
Manager Williams clarified that the height of the parapet did not change from
the first drawings submitted to the revised drawing.
The Council discussed the traffic and circulation plan, the south elevation
plan to pull the roof slate down the east facade, LEEDS certification and the
overall contemporary look of the additions.
Trustees Greable and Spinney expressed their support of the project.
Trustee Rintz commented that he appreciated the juxtaposition of original and
contemporary architectures, but would change the east elevation to stucco
rather than the slate as shown.
Mr. Steve Hardy, 642 Elder, said that it was his first opportunity to see the
drawings and thought the south end of the school was aesthetically
unattractive. He asked if there had been an opportunity for the neighbors to
consider the final design.
Mr. Doar replied that the neighbors were notified in October and then received
a follow up letter, but that the design had just been finalized after receiving
the Design Review Board’s comments. He hoped that the Council would move
forward and that the neighbors would come to the Council’s meetings.
Trustee Pedian was respectful of the school’s goals and strategic plan, but was
not in favor of the design elements on the south side of the school. She also
felt it important that the neighbors have an opportunity to comment on the
final design.
After further discussion, the Trustees reached a consensus to send the final
plan to the Design Review Board for its comments and schedule the matter for
the first Council meeting in March. In the intervening time, the Council asked
the school to reach out to the neighbors and directed the Village Attorney to
draft an ordinance for introduction.
Trustee Greable moved to direct the Village Attorney to draft an ordinance with
the understanding that the final plan will go back to the Design Review Board
for its comments and also comments from neighbors. The motion was seconded by
Trustee Spinney and by voice vote, the motion passed.
   b)   BCDC – ––––North– ––Shore–– Convention Bureau.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Business Community Development Commission had
met to discuss the merits of ’’Winnetka’’s joining the North Shore Convention
and Visitors Bureau, which is the Official Destination Marketing Organization
for the ’’North’ ’’Shore’ region of ’’’’Chicago’’, as designated by the
Illinois Department of Commerce and Economic Opportunity’s Bureau of Tourism.
Mr. D’Onofrio said that the source of half of the Bureau’s annual budget is the
Illinois Bureau of Tourism and the other half comes from local member
communities and private businesses. Once the municipality joins, then
businesses in the community may also join by paying membership and advertising
fees. He said that the cost of the Village joining the Bureau is $6500 per
year. He reported that the members of the BCDC see this as an opportunity to
support local businesses and reached a consensus to recommend to the Council
that the Village consider joining the Bureau. The BCDC also recommended that
membership be evaluated after a year or two to see what benefits have been
realized by the businesses and the Village.
Gina Speckman, Executive Director of the North Shore Convention Bureau, spoke
specifically about the Bureau’s programs that include guides for visitors,
meeting and event resources, public transportation, and a regional and
Northwestern football map and guide. The Village’s membership fee of $6500 and
inclusion in the guides is worth about $1 million in advertising. Ms. Speckman
also reported in response to the Trustee’s questions that membership fees are
based on sales taxes for restaurants and retailers and so, are higher for
larger communities and lower for others, such as Glencoe or Kenilworth. She
added that municipalities with hotels are charged on a different scale and
typically, a membership would be about $100,000.
Trustee Johnson reported that the BCDC does think membership is worthwhile and
that after a year, the Village would have a good measure of membership
interest.
The Trustees ascertained from Chamber of Commerce Director Terry Dason that a
few of the merchants in town had expressed interest in joining, such as the
Community House, Michael Restaurant and Roberta Rubin, but that the Bureau’s
presentation to each business would not be done until the Village becomes a
member.
President Tucker asked for comments from the audience and there were none.
Trustee Pedian stated that, for her, the discussion has to be about helping the
businesses. She expressed her opinion that the brochures are perfectly aligned
with the convention and hotel business, but do not work in a diverse community
of businesses and are not the way decisions are made when a person is
travelling. She said that it has been her experience that the largest sources
of sales taxes in the Village are from Land Rover and the gas stations. She was
not sure if membership is right for our business community and was not
convinced that the Village should spend $6,500 to take this risk without
knowing that more businesses are interested.
After further discussion about the level of interest in the business community,
the cost to the Village and support of local businesses, the Trustees agreed to
a membership for one year.
Trustee Greable requested that the Council be advised of the number of Winnetka
businesses that become members by May 1 st .
Trustee Johnson moved to authorize an expenditure of $6,500 to join the North
Shore Convention and Visitor’s Bureau for one year, seconded by Trustee
Greable. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson,
Pedian, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Poor.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported that she had attended an
intergovernmental meeting with representatives from the Park District, New
Trier High School and other local schools to discuss strategic planning and
create an Ad Hoc committee to study efficiencies.
   b)   Trustees. Trustee Johnson reported that the Business Community
Development Commission hopes to meet with imaging people at its February 15 th
meeting at 7:00 a.m. He also reported that the majority of the former Fell
property has been leased.
   c)   Manager. Manager Williams reported that METRA is set to begin the
rehabilitation of the train station at Elm Street and will be replacing,
instead of repairing, the pedestrian walkway on this project.
In response to a question from Trustee Johnson, Mr. Norkus reported that the
cost to the Village on this $5 million project that will renovate the station,
platform and pedestrian bridge is just $1,000 due to suggestions from the
Design Review Board that saved some money and federal stimulus funds.
President Tucker added that the Hubbard Woods station is in the cue as well.
11)   Appointments.
President Tucker suggested the appointment of Charles Page to the Plan
Commission as the Design Review Board representative for a full term, effective
immediately. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to appoint
Charles Page. By voice vote, the motion carried.
12)   Executive Session.
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Poor. The meeting adjourned at
10:29 p.m.
         
Deputy Clerk
February 9, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
February 9, 2010
(Approved: March 2, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 9, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Convene Study Session.
President Tucker called the Study Session to order at 7:31 p.m. Present:
Trustees Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and
Jennifer Spinney. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director
Mike D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, and approximately 52
persons in the audience.
President Tucker announced that the Special Meeting that had been scheduled
before the Study Session was cancelled, and approval of the contract for the
Village Manager search firm would be on the next regular meeting agenda.
   a)   Report of Ad Hoc Streetscape Technical Committee. President Tucker
noted that the Council would hear the report of the ad hoc Streetscape
Technical Committee as part of its open consideration of the Streetscape
project, and that the discussion will be held with the results of the advisory
referendum in mind. She clarified that the cost of the project being discussed
is an estimated $5.34 million, not the $21 million stated in the referendum,
with the cost divided between the next two years. She noted that funding for
the proposed project is contained in the Village’s cash reserves, and will not
come from new taxes or a bond issue. She invited the audience to attend the
Council’s budget deliberations, set to begin Thursday, February 11, to hear
more about the Village’s proposed Capital budget. After noting that the only
decision that will be made this evening is whether to send the project out for
bids, President Tucker welcomed Ken Behles, Chair of the ad hoc Streetscape
Technical Committee.
Mr. Behles summarized the activities of the Streetscape Technical Committee,
reiterating that the project under consideration is estimated to cost $5.34
million over the next two fiscal years. He reviewed a power point presentation
describing the street furniture elements that were chosen, and said the
Committee recommends that the Council direct staff to solicit bids for the
Priority One Implementation Plan for the 2010-11 fiscal year.
Mr. Saunders then reviewed the material selections, signs, and sample layout
plans, as well as details of the construction phasing and cost estimates.
The Trustees discussed the proposal with Mr. Saunders, and Trustee Greable
suggested that the financial benefits of the project to the Village should be
part of the conversation. Trustee Rintz asked how deferred maintenance figures
into the project’s cost.
Mr. Saunders said the deferred maintenance for the project area is
approximately $820,000, which would be spread out over several years if the
Council did not approve the Streetscape project.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower; Mitch Wywiorski, 1042 Westmoor; Douglas Uhlinger & Irma
Villarreal, 1045 Westmoor; Jean Pierre Rachmaninoff, 132 Bertling; Chatford
Podromos, 143 Sheridan; David Mulligan, 1080 Pelham; Steve Miller, 603
Provident; Julie Windsor, 1479 Tower; Tom Hensel, 615 Hill; Vicki Apatoff, 733
Ardsley; Katie Stevens, 211 Winnetka; Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce; and Jeffrey
Liss, 1364 Edgewood, spoke in opposition to the project and were against
directing staff to send out requests for bids.
Terry Dason, Director of the Winnetka Chamber of Commerce; Nan Greenough, 500
Maple; Phil Hosa, 605 Cherry; and Sandy Berger, 650 Ardsley, spoke in favor of
moving forward with asking for bids.
Trustee Poor said reasonable minds can differ on the issues and noted that a
majority of respondents of the Caucus Survey thought the business districts
needed revitalization. He observed that the referendum referenced a $21 million
project cost, not the actual $5.34 million project under consideration, and he
added that doing nothing would result in the Village looking increasingly worn
out and dated. He indicated that he was in favor of sending out requests for
bids, to get a clear picture of the exact cost of the proposed project.
Trustee Pedian said the comments from residents had caused a shift in her
thinking on the matter, and that given the referendum results and the economic
downturn, the timing was not right to undertake a streetscape project. She
suggested that owners and landlords could give feedback on how the Village can
best support its business community, and said she was not in favor of going out
for bids.
Trustee Rintz expressed concern over the tone of the public dialogue, which
included over 100 emails, regarding the streetscape issue. He observed that to
have a true discussion on policy he needed to have all the facts gathered first
and the actual cost of the project is a critical piece of information that is
lacking. He voiced support for requesting bids in order to have the whole
picture in making a well-informed policy decision.
Trustee Johnson said he was taken aback by the strength of feeling on the issue
and that adults should be able to thoughtfully disagree. He noted that the
money is in the budget if the project goes forward, and that the Council is
always very frugal with the taxpayers’ money. He said he would like to see the
actual pricing information, and voted in favor of requesting bids.
Trustee Spinney also expressed concern at the tone of public comments, and said
her concern was primarily safety and security, noting that the current tree
grates are a hazard and the intersection of Green Bay and Spruce Street is very
dangerous. She indicated that she wanted to see the pricing numbers and voted
in favor of getting bid results.
Trustee Greable said he thought the reference to $21 million in the referendum
was misleading, and urged people in the community to become involved in the
upcoming budget process to see a review of capital expenditures for the next
five years. He said he was in favor of requesting bids to get a clear picture
of the actual cost of the project, but he was not committed to spending the
money, and would like to see the community come to agreement on the issue.
President Tucker noted that the vote was five to one in support of going out to
bid for some hard numbers, and directed staff to prepare RFP’s.
2)   Executive Session.
None.
3)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 11:00 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
February 11, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
Budget Review Meeting
February 11, 2010
(Approved: March 2, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
February 11, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:40 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, Christopher Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: Trustee E. King Poor. Also present: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Finance Director Ed McKee, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Police
Chief Joe DeLopez, Deputy Police Chief Pat Kreis, and 4 persons in the
audience.
2)   Budget Overview.
Mr. McKee reported that the budget is balanced annually, with $49.8 million in
revenue and $48.3 million in expenses resulting in a $1.5 million excess of
revenues over expenses. When depreciation is added in, the cash flow from
operations is $3.59 million, up about .55 million. He noted that an average
homeowner would pay about $193 or 3.3% more in municipal services as a result
of the proposed budget. The cost of electric power and financing improvements
at the water plant are primarily responsible for these increases.
Mr. McKee summarized the individual funds of the budget interspersed with
discussion from the Council that included: (i) balancing revenue with current
expenses and reviewing services to become more cost effective; (ii) planned
capital improvement projects; (iii) pension funding; and, (iv) control of
personnel and health insurance costs. Mr. McKee listed several factors that may
affect future budgets, such as the condition of the State’s budget and sales
tax revenue, rates and fees for the general fund, funding for drainage projects
and repairs that may be needed at the landfill due to leachate. He concluded by
saying that the Village is currently in a good position because of conservative
policies and $21 million in cash reserves.
Manager Williams made several comments directed at future needs of the Village.
He, (i) urged the Council to become actively involved and focus on the
strategic need to plan for the end of the SWANCC contract in 2017; (ii) noted
the substantial improvements during the last 10 years to the drainage problems
in the Village and hoped that the Council would go forward with the
improvements proposed for the remaining portions of the Village; (iii) reported
on the reduction in employees over the years while at the same time reducing
overtime costs thanks to the efforts of department heads; (iv) cautioned that
departments are at the point where further reductions of employees may
compromise services such as snowplowing or water and electric repairs for
emergencies; (v) noted that the Village has saved substantially on legal
services by having an in-house attorney but that within the next five years,
the Council may have to address this; (vi) added that one of the consequences
of the changes in the FOIA law is that it requires more staff time for
compliance; and, (vii) suggested that the Council may want to consider a
communications director for the website and cable TV as pressure for these
services increases in the future.
Mr. McKee answered several questions from the audience regarding the liability
fund, health insurance figures, pension funding, last year’s increase in tax
bills due to a change in homeowner’s exemption and costs associated with the
landfill should leachate occur.
Trustee Greable complimented Manager Williams on how well the financial
philosophy of “pay as you go” has worked to balance the operating budget and
personally thanked him for making the finances in the community number one
through his management and leadership.
3)   Police Department.
Chief DeLopez gave an overview of the proposed Police Department budget that
includes just two capital improvement projects for the year for roof repair and
a consolidated dispatch center. Operational costs increased only $40,307 or
0.7% if you exclude the increase in costs related to the police pension expense
which increased $275,480.
Chief DeLopez explained that a change in organizational structure and having
supervisors cover shifts has made it possible to eliminate one position
subsequent to the retirement of an officer this year. He said it is a challenge
to continue a high level of service, cover unplanned vacancies and mandated
training with one less officer. He believes sufficient staff is necessary to be
proactive in safeguarding the community with important activities such as
preventive patrol, school and business district visits, traffic enforcement and
other opportunities for community engagement.
Chief DeLopez reviewed how the nature of policing our community has changed
over the past 5 years, the number of investigative hours spent by officers on
domestic violence, property crimes and identity theft and how Department
personnel must function to support the overlapping activities of all units. He
also described how State grants have been used for traffic safety and how
resources are shared with other communities,
In response to a question from the audience, Deputy Chief Kreis explained how
the roof of the public safety building was leaking and needs repair. He said
they are pursuing a green roof and will have more explanation for the Council
later in the year.
Chief DeLopez provided information on parking in the downtown areas in response
to questions from Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Road, who commented that allocating
money to the Police Department to accomplish parking enforcement is less
expensive than the cost of a new parking lot.
4)   Administration.
President Tucker announced that this item would be rescheduled.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Poor. The meeting adjourned at
10:22 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
February 16, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
February 16, 2010
(Approved: March 2, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 16, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Michael D’Onofrio
and approximately 13 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   Thursday, February 18, 2010 Budget Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   Thursday, February 25, 2010 Budget Meeting. All of the Council members
present, except Trustee Spinney, indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   March 2, 2010, Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
President Tucker read an open letter she sent to the editor of the Winnetka
Talk regarding the ongoing community discussion about Streetscape, noting that
the Village Council will implement a process that invites all members of the
community to discuss how to revitalize the shopping districts. She said the bid
process has been placed on hold and that the Council will discuss a process and
timeline going forward. She emphasized that the Council was never considering a
$21 million Streetscape, only the $5.5 million first phase, and that the first
phase is the only plan the Council intends to address in its discussions.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that the Agenda had been amended by removing Item 5
(a), Resolution R-6-2010, at the applicant’s request, and by adding Item 9(a),
a discussion about Pension Fairness for Illinois Communities Coalition. Trustee
Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to approve the Agenda as amended. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney,
Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   February 4, 2010, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1641 and 1642. Approving Warrant List No. 1641 in
the amount of $1,226,231.63, and Warrant List No. 1642 in the amount of
$463,901.92.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-2-2010 – Zoning Variation: 655 Sheridan – Adoption.
Introduced at the February 4 th Council Meeting and unanimously approved by the
ZBA, this Ordinance grants a front yard setback variation to allow construction
of a new single-family residence.
   d)   Switchgear Bids. Awards Bid #10-001 for the purchase of two pieces of
switchgear and associated fuse holders to Intermountain Electronics, Inc. in
the amount of $31,404.93.
   e)   Dehumidification Unit Installation Bids, Water Plant. Awards Bid #010-
003 for the installation of a dehumidification unit at the Water Plant to Amber
Mechanical Contractors, in the amount of $53,000.
   f)   Executive Search Firm Services for New Village Manager. Approves an
agreement substantially in the form provided in the Agenda Report with
Springsted, Inc., for executive search services in connection with the hiring
of a new Village Manager, in the amount of $15,700, plus expenses.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution No. R-6-2010 – Authorizing an Addendum to the Development
Agreement for the CBI Subdivision at ––––630 Pine Lane–– – Adoption. (Item
removed from the agenda at the applicant’s request.)
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
George Conbeer, 780 Prospect, and Marc Hecht, 1096 Spruce, voiced concerns
about the choice of Springsted, Inc. to perform the executive search for the
new Village Manager, speculating on possible future conflicts of interest
because, in their opinion, Springsted might want future work from the Village.
Attorney Janega described the Council’s process. She explained that the Council
carefully reviewed the qualifications and methodology of each firm that
responded to the request for proposals, before narrowing the field to five
firms for interviews, and finally authorizing contract negotiations with
Springsted. She noted there have been no conversations about future business
and there is no basis for speculation about what might or might not happen in
the future.
Trustee Rintz reiterated that the process had been very deliberative, with the
Trustees selecting the firm after more than sixteen hours of debate and
discussion in closed session.
Mr. Hecht raised questions about the status of the streetscape project, which
the Village President answered.
8)   Old Business.
   a)   Policy Direction: Special Use Permit – ––––572 Lincoln Avenue. This
matter was tabled at the December 15,   2009, Council meeting when the matter
was up for policy direction, in order to give the petitioner the opportunity to
consider alternative floor plans. Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Poor,
moved to remove the item from the table. By voice vote the motion carried.
Mr. D’Onofrio summarized the background of this proposal to consolidate the two
current Coldwell Banker Real Estate offices, located at 586 and 554 Lincoln
Avenue, by vacating the existing locations and relocating into new space at 572
Lincoln Avenue, which is within the C-2 Retail Overlay District. The proposed
occupancy by Coldwell Banker, which is a non-retail use and requires a special
use permit, occurs on the ground floor at the front property line.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the three revised floor plans submitted by the
applicant, explaining that the original plan had Coldwell Banker occupying the
entire 4,791 s.f. and the entire Lincoln Street frontage of the first floor to
the north of the Galleria building’s courtyard, and that each of the revised
plans calls for a portion of that space and street frontage to be used for
retail. He noted also that the applicant and Coldwell Banker have agreed to
certain measures, as outlined in their Voluntary Parking Action Plan, designed
to mitigate potential on-street parking problems by real estate agents
associated with Coldwell Banker.
Manager Williams remarked that the parking plan is an attempt by the applicant
to nudge their real estate agents to park in the Lincoln Avenue lot instead of
on the street where spaces are better used by customers. He commented that
because of the Galleria’s immediate proximity to the ’’’’Lincoln Avenue’’ lot,
there will be less demand for parking on the street.
Jim Sayegh, the applicant, thanked the Council for the opportunity to present
revised plans, and staff for spending so much time trying to come up with
suggestions for a win/win solution for the landlord, Village and tenants in
east Elm. He went on to comment on each of the revised floor plans, saying that
he does not see option 1A as being a viable retail space but believes that
either option 1 or 2 would be marketable and could be made to work.
Mr. Sayegh urged the Council to grant the special use permit on the grounds
that it would not set a precedent, because the situation is unique. He added
that the benefits of the parking concessions outweigh the de minimus loss of
retail space.
Peter O’Rourke, Coldwell Banker, stated that Coldwell Banker needs a decision
so it can move forward with its planning. He said that the existing south
building is not up to the standards that they are hoping to achieve and pointed
out that Wilmette and Lake Forest had approved similar requests.
Mr. Sayegh and Mr. O’Rourke responded to questions and comments from the
Trustees.
John Rutledge spoke on behalf of the property owners of 586 Lincoln, saying
that if this request is granted, the two existing Coldwell Banker landlords
will be hurt, forever impeding the relative value of the existing landlords’
property.
Attorney Janega opined that should 586 Lincoln become vacant, any use could
occupy that space, because it is outside of the C-2 Overlay, but that 554
Lincoln is within the Overlay District, so should Coldwell Banker vacate that
space, the special use permit would expire and any successor non-retail use
would be required to seek a new special use permit.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., spoke in support of the proposed consolidation,
citing the parking benefit and the impact on potential renters who may perceive
a new, vacant building as an indication that there is a problem with the
viability of the retail district. He pointed out that if the Council denies the
application, there is no guarantee that Coldwell Banker will not eventually
vacate both of their existing spaces anyway.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak and a member of the BCDC and Plan Commission, remarked
that the Overlay District was studied for over two years and major concessions
were made to landlords by cutting the front setback requirement from 100 ft to
50 ft. She expressed concern that granting this request would set a precedent
for all landlords having problems renting their space. She opined that service
office spaces should be located in Indian Hill and added that parking
compliance should have been required in the past and the enforcement part of
plan should still be enacted. She urged the Council to deny the request.
Eve Bremen, Manager of Coldwell Banker South, spoke in support of the request
and added an historical perspective of the firm’s current space.
Midge Powell, 693 Walden Road and a member of the Plan Commission, also urged
the Council to deny the request.
Bernie Citron, attorney for the applicant, argued in favor of granting the
request, saying that non-retail uses are not prohibited uses, but rather
special uses, which are permitted but for review of their impact. He
represented that the applicant has met all of the 11 applicable standards and
that, therefore, the Village must grant the special use permit. He noted that
the subject property is not in the middle of the Overlay District and applicant
is not creating a new real estate office. There is nothing to prevent Coldwell
Banker South from leaving its current space, and Coldwell Banker North can
retool and re-rent.
Asked to comment, Attorney Janega confirmed Mr. Citron’s statement that the
proposed use is not a prohibited use and that special use permits do require
compliance with specific standards, but the Council has the authority to either
grant or deny the request before it. She suggested that, if the Council is
inclined to grant the request, it should require the applicant to provide a
hold harmless agreement indemnifying the Village from any claims by the
landlords.
Ms. Janega also observed that there are no market studies showing the viability
of the carved out retail space and voiced concern that if that space proves not
to be viable retail space, then it may be only a matter of time until the
existing special use tenant comes before the Council to ask to take it over.
Mr. Sayegh indicated the applicant’s willingness to stipulate in whatever form
necessary that they will not in the future pursue a special use permit for the
proposed retail space.
After further discussion, President Tucker polled the Trustees.
Saying that this has been a difficult issue, Trustee Spinney said that if this
were a request for a new real estate office, she would not consider it, but
given that it is just a relocation and consolidation of a business that has
been a good neighbor and is trying to work within the Village’s constraints,
she does not find it that offensive. She said that while she is reluctant to
give up retail space and would find it difficult to grant any similar requests
for special use permits in the Overlay District, the amount of space here is
minimal and the request reasonable, so she would support granting the special
use permit, but did not express a strong preference for either Option 1 or
Option 2.
Trustee Rintz agreed that this is a difficult issue to balance and that it
isn’t precedent setting because each special use permit stands on its own
merits. He explained that when this was before the Council in December, his
main issue was frontage; he did not have a problem with moving an existing use
in the district. With the new proposal, the applicant has presented an
alternative that conforms to the standards and has submitted an impressive and
innovative plan to control parking, demonstrating a willingness on the part of
Coldwell Banker to “put their money where their mouth is.” He said he feels
good about the fact that the Village would be losing an unattractive frontage
with a big, blue awning in favor of an elegant small sign.
Trustee Rintz indicated that he believes in the C-2 overlay when it comes to
restricting new uses, but does not believe that it was intended to eliminate
existing uses. He does not see the overlay district as a tool to chase
businesses out of town, but to keep others from coming in. He went on to say
that it is not the job of the Council or the C-2 overlay to manipulate the
market and decide which spaces get rented and which don’t, or which landlord is
more worthy of having a tenant. Mr. Rintz then said he would support granting
the special use permit and said his preference would be for Option 2.
Trustee Johnson expressed appreciation for the applicant’s struggles but said
he was not moved by the parking plan, which he views as having de minimus
impact, and he could not support the request.
Trustee Pedian agreed with Trustee Johnson’s assessment of the impact of the
parking plan. She remarked that the Council has to be careful to create healthy
retail districts for the present and the future, not create more vacancies. She
expressed the belief that there are other visible locations in Winnetka,
outside of the C-2 Overlay District, that may offer Coldwell Banker the
opportunity to consolidate, reduce staff, and improve revenues, and where
parking is less of an issue. Therefore, she could not support the applicant’s
request at this time.
After summarizing all of the recent deliberations on the C-2 Overlay District,
Trustee Greable said the guiding principle of the District is to ensure a
viable retail presence for the entire Winnetka community, now and in the
future. As a matter of public policy, if the Council believes in the overlay
district, then it must support it. He said he is not concerned with who owns or
leases the buildings but in the future of Winnetka, and he sees no need for
more of the Village’s prime retail space to be occupied by service businesses.
Therefore, he would not support the request.
Trustee Poor agreed that this has been a difficult issue and said he respects
both points of view. He stated that he believes the overlay district is
important to supporting retail in Winnetka. He explained that in December he
had voted against the request because he was concerned about the long term
effect of the proposal on the overlay district. However, the law does permit
special uses, and the amended application is sufficiently different from the
original to cause him to change his mind. He said an important distinction is
that when Coldwell Banker South is vacated it will revert to retail. This is an
unusual situation in that one retail use is being substituted for another, with
a net loss of approximately 500 s. f., which in his opinion is not a major
shift. There is the benefit being offered the Village of improved parking, and
the applicant has made a concerted effort to address and mitigate the Council’s
articulated concerns. Finally, he pointed out that the applicant believes so
much in the viability of the proposed retail space that they are willing to
accept a covenant running with the land stipulating that the space will remain
retail, which he noted as a new benefit to the Village. He said he believes
that this is going to improve the overall frontage of Lincoln Avenue and
benefit the Village; therefore, he would support the amended application as it
has been described.
Noting that the Council is split on the matter, President Tucker inquired as to
whether a supermajority was needed in this instance, because the application
came before the Council without a favorable recommendation. The Village
Attorney explained that the Village President would need to vote to break the
tie in the absence of written objections from neighboring owners, whereupon the
Village President asked to defer the decision to the next Council meeting,
stating she was not fully prepared to vote and that it made sense to see what
sort of ordinance and conditions would be fashioned from the current
discussion.
After a brief discussion, there was agreement that Attorney Janega should
prepare a draft ordinance.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Pension Fairness for Illinois Communities Coalition. Manager Williams
reviewed a request from the Northwest Municipal Conference to become a member
of the Pension Fairness for Illinois Communities Coalition, formed to provide a
voice for stakeholders who are impacted by the public safety pension crisis, as
well as a forum for them to work cooperatively on a legislative solution to
achieve pension fairness. He explained that the decision is whether or not to
become a member of this coalition with the suggested contribution level of
$1,000.
Trustee Greable voiced support for joining the Coalition, saying that this is
an important issue for all taxpayers, and it is important to take steps to hold
State officials accountable for their actions.
There was consensus among the Trustees to join the Coalition.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. None.
   b)   Trustees. Trustee Spinney reported on her recent attendance at the
Strategic Planning Committee for the library, as well as the Library Board and
the RED Center meetings.
Trustee Johnson reported that the Business Community Development Commission was
very excited about the first product from branding consultants and were looking
forward to presenting it to the Council in the future.
Trustee Greable reported that the Winnetka Historical Society is working on
their strategic plan and planning their annual gala, scheduled for June 19 th .
He also reported on a recent Chamber of Commerce meeting, saying that the
Chamber is working on financing and raising membership.
Trustee Pedian remarked that she had attended the recent BCDC meeting at which
their branding ideas had been unveiled. She also reminded the audience that
although the warrants are approved on the consent agenda, they are carefully
reviewed in advance.
Trustee Poor reported that Environmental and Forestry Commission has been hard
at work researching and putting together the elements for a demolition
recycling ordinance that they hope to bring before the Council in the spring.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
No Executive Session was held.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
15 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
February 18, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL BUDGET MEETING
February 18, 2010
(Approved: March 2, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 18, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Fire
Chief Scott Smith, Deputy Fire Chief Steve Laureys, Fire Lt. John Ripka,
Director of Public Works Steve Saunders, Assistant Director of Public Works
Steve Auth, Finance Director Ed McKee and 2 persons in the audience.
President Tucker announced that the Public Works presentation would be given
first, followed by the Fire Department.
2)   Fire Department.
This item was discussed after the Public Works discussion.
Chief Smith reported that 2009 was a challenging year for personnel issues,
with five staffers dealing with illnesses and injuries. He said overtime costs
had been contained by using the Fire Chief, Deputy Fire Chief and
Administrative Lieutenant to cover 43 24-hour shifts. He summarized some of the
Department’s accomplishments, including:
·   Responding to 2,276 calls for assistance.
·   Responding to 50 fires, including 20 structure fires. (Estimated total
property value saved in 2009 – $35,039,900)
·   Installation and integration of computer dispatch program with RED center.
·   524 hours of Fire Department personnel training. He noted that the quality
of training for Winnetka’s Fire Department is second to none, and the Village
has a highly professional and well-trained Fire Department staff.
Chief Smith said a couple of the goals he would like to pursue for next year
include changing how the Village monitors fire alarms, and putting the new
Quint ladder truck, which was approved last year, into service. After reviewing
his budget summary, he observed that there was a reduction in health insurance
costs, and he added that the Department produced $760,305 in revenues last
year.
Chief Smith answered questions from the trustees, and Trustee Spinney thanked
him for his efforts on behalf of RED Center.
Manager Williams noted that Winnetka is unique in its use of high ranking Fire
Department officers to cover day shifts in order to save on overtime costs.
   a)   Fire Alarm Monitoring. Due to the lateness of the hour, discussion of
this item was postponed until a later date.
3)   Public Works.
This item was moved up the agenda and discussed first.
Manager Williams reported that, at the request of Trustee Greable, some of the
drainage information had been updated.
Mr. Saunders described the activities of the Public Works Department in both
operational and capital improvement areas, and said the funding for the
department's activities come from the General Fund, Sewer Fund, Refuse Fund,
MFT Fund and Fleet Services Fund. He said there have been staff reductions
attained through attrition and a reduction in the refuse collection routes,
which was made possible as recycling rates have increased and solid waste
collection has decreased over the past several years. He indicated that
recycling collection has been improved with the new Groot recycling contract,
which provides for the Village to get some revenues from recycling, and he
added that a commercial recycling program is being run with existing staff,
with a resulting reduction in waste that is removed from the business
districts.
Mr. Saunders summarized the items he anticipates may need policy direction in
2010, including: (i) the upcoming Trapp Lane dedication to the Village; (ii) a
sewer relocation project at 10 Indian Hill Road; (iii) rehabilitation of
Chestnut Street and Alles Road; and (iv) an evaluation of the current policy
for paving alleys.
Mr. Saunders summarized the stormwater drainage improvements that had been
undertaken over the last 15 years, and reviewed the stormwater drainage
projects contained in his Capital Improvements Plan. He discussed at length two
proposed major stormwater improvement projects: improvements to the Birch/Hill
diversion, and improvements in the area of Duke Child Fields and Hibbard Rd. He
gave an overview of financing options for the drainage projects, including: (i)
implementing a stormwater utility; (ii) creating a special service area; (iii)
issuing debt or wrapping the Village’s current debt; (iv) using cash reserves;
or any combination of the previous options.
Manager Williams pointed out that the entire western portion of the Village is
interrelated with regard to stormwater drainage, as the higher ground drains
onto the lower ground, causing the choke points and overwhelming the pumps.
There was a thorough discussion about the need to mitigate flooding in the
Village, with several Trustees in favor of moving forward quickly and others in
favor of gathering more community feedback on the issue before making a final
decision.
Trustee Greable said the Council needs to take control of the project and
address drainage in this budget, as the improvements would be good for the
Village as a whole.
Dick Kates, 1326 Tower, said the analysis of the Council and involvement of the
community is required on such a large project, and noted that the demands of
future water plant improvements need to be kept in mind as well. He suggested
holding community forums and involving the Caucus in the public process.
4)   Adjournment.
President Tucker adjourned the meeting at 10:06 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
February 25, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
Budget Review Meeting
February 25, 2010
(Approved: March 16, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
February 25, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, E. King Poor and Christopher Rintz,
who arrived at 7:38 p.m. Absent: Trustee Jennifer Spinney. Also present:
Village Manager Doug Williams, Finance Director Ed McKee, Village Attorney
Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Michael D’Onofrio, Water &
Electric Director Brian Keys, Assistant Water & Electric Director Richard
Ciesla, and 4 persons in the audience.
2)   Community Development.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the functions of the Community Development Department,
noting an increase of 0.4% overall in the department’s budget that was mostly
due to an increase in wages and benefits. A reduction in revenue due to less
building activities was discussed, as well as opportunities to reduce costs and
increase revenue. Mr. D’Onofrio stated that although building permit revenues
were down last year, the department’s budget reflects the amount estimated last
year and he has noticed an upward trend in remodeling and room addition permits
for this year.
There was also discussion about the amount of staff time required for regular
duties and preparation and attendance at board and committee meetings as well
as Freedom of Information Act compliance.
At Trustee Pedian’s request, Village Attorney Janega clarified the photocopying
charges permitted by the FOIA law.
Mrs. Anne Wilder, 1096 Spruce, suggested that information requested repeatedly
could be publicized.
Trustee Greable requested that a report be made to the Council about FOIA
requests during the first 5 months of the year including the amount of staff
time allotted. Trustee Greable also initiated discussion about addressing the
department’s budget deficit this year.
Trustee Rintz commented that some of Community Development’s services are fee
driven, but not all, and that even if there were no permit fees received for a
year, it would still be necessary to have the department.
Manager Williams added that, years ago, the Trustees felt there was value to
having a Community Development Department supported by fees and taxes.
3)   Permit fees.
President Tucker announced that Mr. Howard Handler, North Shore Barrington
Association of Realtors, had submitted information to the Council on demolition
fees and referred to an analysis completed by Mr. D’Onofrio. She then
ascertained that the Council had no comments and asked Mr. Handler to speak.
Mr. Handler commented on the points raised in the findings he submitted to the
Council and asked that the Council review the fees associated with demolitions
such as police services or utility fees, to determine if they can be reduced.
President Tucker thanked Mr. Handler and told him the Council would take his
remarks under advisement.
4)   Water & Electric Department.
Mr. Keys gave an overview of staffing levels in the department, the impact of
weather on sales, projected power cost, and revenue and expenditures. Mr. Keys
then reviewed the purchase power cost adjustment figures and the plan for
capital improvements over the next few years.
The Trustees discussed comparison of electric and water rates, wind power,
renewable energy credits and plans for maintenance and improvements. Trustee
Poor asked that the Council be updated on the electric rate comparisons once
Commonwealth Edison announced its increase in rates.
Mr. McKee summarized the modest rate adjustments planned to help fund the water
and electric plant improvements, noting that staff is cognizant of protecting
the assets of the community and plans to do things gradually over time. He
added that the 5-year strategic plan may also include the possibility of bond
financing or an interfund loan for substantial improvements.
5)   Adjournment.
President Tucker adjourned the meeting at 10:08 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
March 2, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
March 2, 2010
(Approved: March 16, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
2, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:31 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Community Development
Director Mike D’Onofrio, and approximately 50 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   March 9, 2010 Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   March 11, 2010 Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present, with the possible exception of Trustee Rintz, indicated that they
expected to attend.
   c)   March 16, 2010 Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that budget matters are taking up much of the
Council’s time at the moment, and that it could be the end of April before
community discussions about downtown revitalization can begin. Trustee Rintz,
seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to approve the Agenda. By roll call vote the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   February 9, 2010, Study Session.
      ii)   February 11, 2010 Budget Meeting.
      iii)   February 16, 2010 Regular Council Meeting.
      iv)   February 18, 2010 Budget Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1643 and 1644. Approves Warrant List No. 1643 in the
amount of $647,830.69, and Warrant List No. 1644 in the amount of $350,738.47.
   c)   GIS Service Provider Agreement with Municipal GIS Partners. Approves a
service agreement for GIS services with Municipal GIS Partners, Inc., for a fee
set forth in the agenda report, not to exceed $52,583.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-3-2010 – 310 Green Bay Road: North Shore Country Day
School Special Use Permit – Introduction/Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this
request for a special use permit and variations to permit the construction of
two additions on the north and south ends of the North Shore Country Day School
(NSCDS) Upper School building. He indicated that approval of the request would
result in a total GFA of 213,857.12 square feet and would allow a building
height of 3 stories for the north addition and 4 stories for the south
addition. He noted that the matter was before the Council for policy direction
at its February 4 th meeting, after receiving approval from the ZBA and Plan
Commission, and that the DRB had subsequently recommended approval after
revised plans were submitted at its February 18 th meeting. He summarized the
proceedings of the February 18 th DRB meeting, as the minutes had not been
finalized in time to include them in the agenda packet.
Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that the school had reached out to neighbors in writing
and the ZBA had notified 120 property owners about its public hearing on the
matter, but there is still some neighborhood concern about the school’s
proposal. He distributed a packet to the Council containing the letters of
invitation from NSCDS to the neighbors concerning the proposed additions and
then introduced Tom Doer, head of the NSCDS.
Mr. Doer reviewed the school’s efforts to communicate with neighbors throughout
the application process, and said that NSCDS has worked to maintain a good
relationship with its neighbors over the years. He noted that some confusion
had arisen after a recent neighborhood meeting where it had been represented
that NSCDS would not appear at the DRB’s February meeting, and at the last
minute the school decided to attend that meeting, where it obtained approval
for its updated plans. He asked the Council to approve their request so that
construction could begin over the summer when the building will be empty.
Mr. Kerry Leonard, OWP/P - the school’s architect, said the proposed project is
largely an interior renovation of an outdated building, which will enable the
facilities to meet modern safety and fire codes and at the same time address
sustainability issues. He noted that the design is consistent with what has
evolved over the years at NSCDS and he assured the Council that the mechanical
units on the roof of the south addition would meet noise requirements of the
Village and the Illinois EPA. He stated that there have been seven public
meetings to discuss the application; five letters sent from NSCDS to the
neighbors; and three meetings held with the neighborhood. He added that he
thought the DRB had been thorough in addressing the concerns of residents in
the vicinity and had pushed the applicant to revise the plans and make them
more acceptable to the neighborhood.
Jane McCarthy, Board of Trustees of NSCDS and 1026 Pine, said the school’s
board is vested in having a thoughtful, friendly relationship with the
surrounding neighborhood, and that she felt the school had earnestly tried to
respond to concerns from the lower Boards and the neighbors. She said a delay
would be very disruptive to the project, as there is a short window of time to
get the construction completed, and reiterated that the school has a long
history of working in a thoughtful and cooperative manner with the neighbors.
Steve and Jody Hardy of 642 Elder; Gene Ullrich and Moira Fahey-Ullrich of 648
Elder, and Robin Thomas of 638 Elder said not enough effort had been made to
inform neighbors of the plan, asked the Council to delay approval of the
application.
James Colley, 302 Forest, disagreed with the characterization of the school’s
attempts to communicate with the neighbors as inadequate. He said he has been
involved since the beginning of the process and had made himself available as a
liaison between the school and the neighbors, but had not been contacted about
the issue until the previous evening. He voiced concern that a delay in
approval will negatively impact the students of NSCDS.
Tom Fromolo, Chairman of the Board of NSCDS, said the board is committed to
delivering world-class education, and is equally committed to being a good
neighbor. He said he couldn’t explain why the letters from the school to the
neighbors had not been acknowledged, nor why the neighbors had not taken
advantage of their right to be involved in the process in front of the
Village’s lower Boards. He noted that NSCDS had followed the public process
faithfully and had met four times with the DRB to earn its approval.
Attorney Janega reviewed the draft of Ordinance M-3-2010, prepared at the
Council’s direction after discussion at the February 4 th Council meeting,
noting that concerns raised about traffic were addressed in Section 4(B) and
that Section 6 of the Ordinance reserves the right of the Village to modify
those conditions.
Trustees Greable, Spinney and Johnson expressed satisfaction with the work of
the lower boards and said they favor introducing the ordinance, but also
expressed sympathy for the concerns of the neighbors, and requested that NSCDS
take the time after introduction of the ordinance to have further meetings with
the neighbors to discuss their issues.
Trustee Rintz said the concerns are boiling down to aesthetics and
architecture, which are very subjective issues. He suggested that the most
pressing concerns could be dealt with following introduction of the ordinance:
(i) the final draft of the ordinance should include a provision that the DRB
must approve the landscaping plan before a building permit is issued by the
Village; however the permit for the trailers is not included in the
requirement; (ii) concurrent with the submission of the building permit
application, the applicant will provide a design for a mechanical screen which
has been reviewed and approved by an acoustical engineer for compliance with
the requirements of the building code; (iii) prior to issuance of a Certificate
of Occupancy, the applicant shall provide a report by the acoustical engineer
certifying that the installation of the mechanical screen is in accordance with
the approved design and that field testing has confirmed that the requirements
of the building code have been met; and (iv) observance of the parking and sign
recommendations included in the draft ordinance should alleviate concerns about
traffic and parking.
Trustees Pedian and Poor said they could not vote for introduction of the
ordinance at this time, given that the neighbors feel they haven’t had adequate
input, and recommended taking more time to meet with neighbors to resolve the
issues.
Attorney Janega indicated that Trustee Rintz’ suggestions could be incorporated
into the final draft of the ordinance.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to introduce Ordinance M-3-
2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: Trustees Pedian and Poor. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance M-4-2010 – 572 ––––Lincoln–– Ave.: Galleria/Coldwell Banker
Non-Retail Special Use Permit – Introduction. President Tucker noted that at
its last meeting, the Council’s vote had been split about whether to support
approval of the special use application and that Attorney Janega had been
instructed to prepare a draft ordinance for consideration at tonight’s meeting.
Trustee Greable read a statement disclosing that he has been working with a
friend who is a real estate agent with Coldwell Banker, as part of the process
of buying a condominium at the Winnetka Mews. He noted for the record that he
has not contracted with the agent, neither he nor his wife have any financial
interest in Coldwell Banker, that their potential purchase of the condominium
is unrelated to the Coldwell Banker leases, and that he and his wife do not
have a direct or indirect interest in the subject matter before the Council.
Attorney Janega indicated that Trustee Greable does not have an actual or
apparent conflict in the matter, and that he can cast a vote.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a special use permit to allow a non-
retail use to occupy the first floor within 50 feet of the property line in the
C-2 Retail Overlay District. He summarized the two alternative floor plans
which create retail spaces on the street frontage and noted that some
additional correspondence had been received and placed on the dais for the
Council.
The applicant, Jim Sayegh, said the only new item he had to add was that he had
a signed letter of intent for the retail space, substantiated by Mr. D’Onofrio,
and that he was prepared to accept the ordinance as drafted.
John Rutledge, representing 554 Lincoln, said granting the special use would go
against the intent of the C-2 Retail Overlay and urged the Council to reject
the application.
Arthur Braun, 850 Bell Lane, said there is no issue for the Council about a
business moving from one area to another in the retail overlay district, and
said he was in favor of the request.
Nan Greenough, 500 Maple, said the purpose of the retail overlay is to maintain
the Village’s sales tax revenue and that if this property is taken out of the
sales tax revenue base, it would not come back in again, and she asked the
Council to deny the application.
Attorney Janega reviewed elements of the draft ordinance, answered questions
from the Council, and confirmed that, as the ordinance is drafted, the
applicant would not be permitted to pursue a special use permit for the vacant
space to the south of the Subject Property, nor would they be permitted to
market the retail space for a special use.
Attorney Janega then reported that the Village had received signed letters from
eight property owners stating that they object to the application under the
provisions of Section 17.56.010(G) of the Village Code, which provides that if
20% of the property owners within 250 feet of the proposed special use file
written and acknowledged objections, then a supermajority of the Council is
needed to approve the request. She noted that the letters had arrived only a
few hours before the Council meeting was scheduled to begin, that none of the
letters was notarized as required, and it was therefore her opinion that the
letters did not trigger the supermajority vote requirement.
There was a discussion about the provisions of Section 17.56.010(G), and the
Council concluded that the ordinance is unclear about whether the signatures
have to be notarized, and also that no deadline is imposed for turning in the
objections. Manager Williams noted that historically, the objections are
received at the ZBA level and are submitted to the Council with the rest of the
record.
Mr. Bernie Citron, the applicant’s attorney, pointed out that one of the
signatures appeared to be invalid, since the property is owned by a trust and
an individual had signed the protest. He asked for more time to investigate
whether the number of protests constitutes 20% of the property owners within
250 feet.
After a discussion about fairness in the process for all sides, it was decided
to give the applicant time to investigate whether 8 people constituted 20% of
the property owners within 250 feet, and to verify whether the signatures were
valid.
After further discussion, the Council decided to take the eight protests on
file and give those objectors until Monday, March 8 th , to get the signatures
notarized, and to give the applicants until Thursday, March 11 th , to submit
their arguments.
The Village President also directed the Village Attorney to initiate
proceedings to clarify the provisions of Section 17.56.010(G) for future
applications.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance M-4-
2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Rintz and
Spinney, and President Tucker. Nays: Trustees Greable, Johnson and Pedian.
Absent: None.
   c)   Resolution No. R-7-2010 – 2010-11 Village Budget – Public Hearing &
Introduction. The Village President opened the public hearing on the budget at
10:30 p.m.
Mr. McKee summarized the substantive changes made to the proposed budget as a
result of the study session discussions, including eliminating six employee
positions, and noted that a typical homeowner would experience an increase of
$193, or 3.3%, in the cost of municipal services over last year.
Trustee Poor left the meeting at 10:35 PM.
There was a discussion about whether to keep the funds for Streetscape in the
budget, as there were safety concerns that were addressed by the proposal.
Manager Williams observed that Streetscape and other expenditures under Tab 13
would be discussed at next week’s Study Session, along with the issue of
deferred maintenance, and that revisions can be made before the budget is
adopted at the next Council meeting.
Mr. McKee explained that the increases in water and electric rates were
necessary to make future improvements to the water plant facility so the
community will continue to have a safe and healthy water supply, and that the
electric power is purchased wholesale from the IMEA, which has projected
increased energy costs. Manager Williams observed that the Water & Electric
Plant has a lot of fixed costs, and that when sales are down significantly, as
they have been the past two summers due to the weather, the Village has to
recoup those losses.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, suggested compiling police reports of the most
hazardous intersections in Winnetka to see if there is a serious problem at
Green Bay and Spruce that needs to be addressed. He expressed concern about the
pension funds, as the charts in the budget show there has been a steady
deterioration of funds since 2002, partly caused by changes to pension benefits
passed by the State legislature. He also expressed concern over the flooding
problem and urged the Council to have Village staff do a study instead of
paying a consultant to do it.
There being no further substantive comments, President Tucker closed the public
hearing at 11:05 p.m.
Trustee Pedian commented on the streetscape category and Trustee Greable
commented on downtown redevelopment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Resolution R-
7-2010, adopting the Village’s 2010-11 budget. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
   d)   Updated Fee & Rate Resolutions. President Tucker called for a motion to
introduce the following fee and rate resolutions. Mr. McKee said the Council
can contact him with questions, as his report is self-explanatory.
      i)   Resolution R-8-2010 – Water Rates – Introduction.
      ii)   Resolution R-9-2010 – Electric Rates – Introduction.
      iii)   Resolution R-10-2010 – Sewer Rates – Introduction.
      iv)   Resolution R-11-2010 – Refuse Rates – Introduction.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce rate Resolutions
R-8-2010 (water), R-9-2010 (electric), R-10-2010 (sewer) and R-11-2010 (refuse
collection). By voice vote, the motion carried.
President Tucker announced that the following three resolutions would be
adopted at the next meeting without introduction, but were on the agenda for
discussion purposes.
      v)   Resolution R-12-2010 – General Permit & License Fees – Review.
      vi)   Resolution R-13-2010 – Building, Zoning & Construction Fees –
Review.
      vii)   Resolution R-14-2010 – Fire Services Fees – Review.
There were no comments or questions from the Council.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported that she attended the NWMC
Legislative Brunch with Trustee Johnson on February 20 th and that pensions
were the hot topic, and that pensions would again be discussed Springfield’s
Legislative Days. She also reported that she had attended IPELRA’s Employment
Law Seminar and a press conference in Chicago at which Winnetka showed
solidarity for upholding municipal handgun bans.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Johnson invited all in the viewing audience to attend the
BCDC’s meetings and hear about their branding project.
      ii)   Trustee Rintz reported that the Plan Commission had met and
discussed general policy matters and concluded that lines of communication with
the Council need to be more open. He indicated that the Chair, Becky Hurley,
plans on coming to a Study Session about every six months to report to the
Council, and will also update the Plan Commission about what issues the Council
is addressing.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
Executive Session. None.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Poor. The meeting adjourned at
11:17 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
March 9, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
March 9, 2010
(Approved: April 20, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
9, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Linda Pedian, Jennifer Spinney, Bill Johnson, and Chris Rintz, Gene Greable,
King Poor. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of
Public Works Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys and
approximately 8 persons in the audience.
2)   Budget Follow Up.
Mr. McKee summarized the General Fund revenues and expenses over the next few
years, projecting that the fund will generate enough cash for operating and
capital needs and that operations will be cash neutral in 2013 and 2014. He
noted that the projections assume that there are no major reductions in revenue
from the State and that things will continue as forecasted today. He added that
the Village is in good shape relative to other communities and suggested
evaluating fees and taxes next year at budget time as this year most items were
unchanged. He also mentioned that expenses need to be monitored and adjusted to
stay in balance with the revenues available.
The Trustees discussed the effect that a reduction in State revenues would have
on the General Fund, and reviewed permit, sales tax and utility revenues.
Health insurance, pension increases, monthly revenue trend analyses, and
increasing the General Fund minimum reserve balance to$13 million from the
current $11 million was also discussed.
In response to a question from Trustee Rintz, Mr. McKee explained that the
budget is balanced in that operating revenues exceed operating expenses. If all
capital improvements are done, reserve funds would decline to pay for capital
improvements, which is typical as the Village saves up money over several years
to pay for these types of expenses.
Trustee Poor noted that the Council has abided by the tax cap and this year’s
increase in taxes of 0.1% has also had an effect on the budget since it’s been
a long time since the increase was so small.
The Council then discussed the funds originally planned for streetscape and
determined that the balance would go into the General Fund with $750,000
allotted for deferred maintenance, such as sidewalk repairs and replacement of
deteriorating pavers. Mr. McKee was directed to make this transfer prior to
April 1, 2010.
Manager Williams explained a six-point strategy for drainage throughout the
Village. His proposal recommended that the Village: (1) not invest any
additional resources into the southern drainage project, unless a licensing
solution was found to enhance the capacity of the storm water pump station at
Winnetka Avenue; (2) analyze the consequences of not storing water on Duke
Child’s field for the northern drainage basin improvements and new pump station
in the vicinity of Hibbard and Willow as well as the feasibility of obtaining a
permit to discharge storm water into the river at Willow Road; (3) verify the
justification of the proposed drainage improvements in the 5-year plan for
areas other than southwest Winnetka and determine if other drainage
improvements are necessary; (4) conduct a detailed study of financing options;
(5) seek public input with public hearings and a citizen survey; and, (6)
provide a comprehensive policy on drainage improvements in 2011 after
completing the foregoing.
The Council discussed financing possibilities, using the caucus survey for
public input and the website for public education, and the current budgeted
amount of $266,000 which Mr. Saunders confirmed. Mr. Saunders added that he
would not expect to spend the entire amount because the survey will show
specific areas that need improvement.
Trustee Greable asked that Manager Williams reformat the steps as a policy
guideline and include timelines in order to facilitate the process.
There were no comments from the audience and no further discussion.
Manager Williams reported that he and the Council had received several e-mails
in which opinion was split as a result of a mailing sent to residents by the
North Shore – Barrington Association of Realtors regarding demolition fees.
Trustee Rintz was troubled by the amount of information the Council gets that
is half truths and from people who are promoting themselves, such as the
statements of the advocacy group for the realtors. He provided information
about the fees Lake Forest charges: (1) a $10,000 demolition tax instead of a
fee, because substance does not have to be proved to charge a tax and the tax
doesn’t get factored into the permit cost; (2) a $2300 demolition permit fee,
(3) a $2500 demolition review fee; (4) a $4,000 impact fee, in all totaling
over $18,000; (5) in addition, it costs $36,000 to build a home on a lot; and
(6) another $30,000 in building permit fees. He felt that the Village had been
transparent by putting the costs into one demolition fee. Trustee Rintz
concluded by saying that based on Mr. D’Onofrio’s analysis of costs, the
demolition fee is not enough, since we do not charge impact fees, yet watch
developers ruin infrastructure.
President Tucker remarked that it was very offensive to have this mailing go
out with misinformation.
Trustee Spinney was respectful of people who want to sell their homes to
builders and was horrified that this propaganda was sent to all Winnetka
residents. It was her opinion that the Council needs to do public relations to
make our citizens aware of why the fees are in place and why they need to go
up. She felt that the piece was out of line, very aggressive and very
offensive.
President Tucker ascertained that Trustee Rintz and Trustee Spinney were in
favor of a fee of $16,070 for demolitions.
Trustee Pedian commented on the misinformation going out in uncontrolled
environments is a problem globally for the nation, from health care to
demolition fees. She emphasized that the Village Council is very conservative
so as to keep the character of the Village and to be fair to everyone, but the
Council only has its Council meetings to educate the citizens, not mailings or
going door to door. She referred to Trustee Rintz’s example of the total costs
for demolition which include other costs that she felt should be born by the
purchaser. She thought Mr. D’Onofrio’s analysis was reasonable and would vote
in favor of it.
Trustee Poor agreed with his colleagues. He asked Village Attorney Janega what
legal standard applies if the Village raises the fee.
Ms. Janega replied that the standard is if there is a reasonable relationship
between the cost and recouping the cost, that it does not have to be dollar for
dollar and that there is documentation that would sustain an increase in the
fee. She said that Trustee Rintz is right about the tax vs. fee and a challenge
would have to establish there was no basis for the fee. She added that the
group that did the study doesn’t have legal standing to file a challenge.
Trustee Greable asked who the North Shore – Barrington Association of Realtors
is and what Winnetka businesses are members of the organization and are
accountable for the slander in the mailing. He supports increasing the
demolition fee to $16,030 and wondered if that was high enough.
Trustee Johnson suggested a compromise in light of the current economic
conditions by splitting the increase instead of raising it to the full $16,030.
President Tucker replied that since there is a clear consensus of the Council,
there would not be a split of an increase in the fee. She also asked what
Winnetka businesses are members of the organization and if they had signed off
on the mailing.
Howard Handler, North Shore-Barrington Association of Realtors, replied that
his Board had decided on the mailing and he could not say for sure, but there
may be about 200 businesses. He expressed his opinion that there has to be a
hearing on the fees and it may be that other communities have higher fees after
going through the standards to do that. Mr. Handler questioned charges for
staff reimbursement, police calls, the Historical Society, and missing utility
meters.
President Tucker thanked him for his comments.
Mr. Kates commented about the destruction caused by demolition companies and
asked if it was possible to require those companies to pay for the problems
they cause. He felt that some people have their whole wealth in their house and
may ultimately have to accept a lower price because of demolition and that the
buyer should take on the burden.
President Tucker commented that one of the things talked about when home rule
was established was licensing contractors to help mitigate these damages.
Mr. Handler said that he was told that the staff was not recommending a fee
increase.
Manager Williams replied that staff did not make any recommendations for an
increase, but did ask for policy direction.
Joan Zietlow, 870 Foxdale, said she has a neighbor whose home will probably be
demolished eventually and her concern was that the total cost for demolition
comes off the purchase price, so the homeowner doesn’t realize as much profit
when they sell. If the costs are correct and if the Village is being totally
transparent about what the cost is, then she would be comfortable with the
amount being charged.
Jean Beckman, 840 Foxdale, felt that it was very difficult to make decisions
about her home because of rule changes. She asked that the Council put
information point by point on the website and have a dialogue online with
frequently asked questions.
President Tucker assured Mrs. Beckman that this had been discussed several
times, and that there was a wealth of information on the website.
President Tucker ascertained the consensus of the trustees was to raise the fee
to $16,000 and there was no further discussion.
3)   Green Bay Road Parking Lane Resurfacing Options.
Director of Public Works, Steve Saunders, informed the Council that IDOT will
be resurfacing Green Bay Road throughout the Village and has received a letter
from the State asking if the Village is interested in more than the minimum
amount of asphalt for a cost of $28,000 to the Village. Mr. Saunders
recommended that the Village make the contribution because the resurfacing will
last longer, but needs authorization from the Council. Mr. Saunders added that
the money allocated for Green Bay Road by Representative Hamos and Senator
Schoenberg, will be used for another location.
Manager Williams noted that the ravines are a priority due to flooding at the
location that has required the rescue of people.
President Tucker ascertained that the consensus of the Council supports staff’s
recommendation.
4)   Discussion: New Appointment Procedure.
Village Attorney Janega explained the need to review the process for appointing
people to committees and commissions.
In response to a question from Trustee Greable, Attorney Janega listed the
boards and commissions and stated that it is her opinion as well as the
Attorney General’s, that appointments to the Plan Commission, Zoning Board, and
Board of Fire and Police Commissioners may be considered in executive session,
but that advisory boards, such as the Business Community Development Commission
and the Forestry and Environmental Commission, may have to be done in an open
meeting. She thought the focus of the discussion should be on a process for
identifying people to become possible candidates and a method of obtaining
information from them so that the Council can review their qualifications and
interest plus the potential for conflicts of interest.
Trustee Poor requested time to review the matter before making a decision and
to develop clear, unambiguous policy guidelines.
After some discussion, the Trustees agreed that a formal process was necessary,
but required time for review. Manager Williams will schedule this item for the
April study session.
5)   Discussion: Reschedule April Study Session.
President Tucker announced that the Northwest Municipal Conference’s
legislative days are from April 13 to April 15 in Springfield. She requested
that the April study session be rescheduled to April 15 th , so that she and
other interested Trustees could attend. The Trustees agreed to reschedule the
April 13 th study session to April 15 th .
6)   Reports: 
Trustee Spinney reported that at a special session of the Library Board, the
new lease with Northfield was being negotiated.
Trustee Greable reported that the Historical Society had approved a strategic
plan and is moving ahead with its gala.
Trustee Johnson reported that the Business Community Development Commission is
writing a statement of purpose for branding centered on the business community.
The Commission would like the Council to meet with the consultants to get the
Council’s response to what has been done.
Trustee Poor noted that March 10 th is Winnetka’s 144 th birthday.
President Tucker reported that the cable channel 10 is showing the Highland
Park Board meetings, instead of Winnetka and we are trying to resolve the
problem.
7)   Executive Session.
Trustee Greable moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters and Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) and 2
(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Pedian seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into
Executive Session at 10:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:05 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz, and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Attorney Katherine Janega and Assistant to the
Village Manager Liz Rosenthal.
8)   Adjournment.
President Tucker adjourned the meeting at 11:05 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
March 16, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
March 16, 2010
(Approved: April 20, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
16, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village
Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Community Development
Director Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian
Norkus, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys and approximately 74 persons in
the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   April 6, 2010 Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   April 15, 2010 Study Session. President Tucker reminded the audience
that due to a scheduling conflict this meeting will be held on Thursday
evening, instead of Tuesday evening. All of the Council members present
indicated that they hoped to attend, although Trustee Rintz was not certain
that he would be able to do so.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney,
Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   February 25, 2010 Budget Meeting.
      ii)   March 2, 2010 Regular Council Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1643 and 1644. Approves Warrant List No. 1645 in the
amount of $708,422.76, and Warrant List No. 1646 in the amount of $380,189.74.
   c)   Annual Outdoor Seating Permits and Sidewalk Restaurant Liquor License
Riders approving the permits and riders for local restaurants for the coming
summer season.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-3-2010 – 310 Green Bay Road: North Shore Country Day
School Special Use Permit –Adoption. President Tucker indicated that she
understood that based on meetings held between the parties since the Ordinance
was introduced on March 2 nd , a consensus solution has been found. She then
recognized the School’s representative, Rory Finlay, 1000 Hill Rd., a School
parent and Trustee, who reviewed the School’s process and dialogue with the
neighbors. He reported that as a result of meetings between the School and
neighbors the School had agreed to commit significant additional resources to
address the neighbors’ concerns.
Tom Formolo, 115 De Windt Rd., Chairman of the NSCDS Board, also commented on
the negotiations between the School and the neighbors, saying that as a result
the final product will be better than before. The south addition will be
modified to accommodate a more traditional design, and parking will be reduced.
He reported that the School Board is committed to keeping to their proposed
schedule, has already approved the new design and associated increased costs,
and is ready to move forward.
Kerry Leonard, OWP/P, the School’s architect, briefly reviewed the proposed
changes in design, noting that the most important change is to the roof line,
which is needed to create a more traditional appearance but extends an
additional 12 feet.
Attorney Janega reviewed the revisions to the draft ordinance and commented
that she had also just received a proposed draft from Hal Franke on behalf of
NSCDS, but noted that staff has not had time to consider it. She added that the
proposed modifications require consideration by the Zoning Board.
Trustee Pedian remarked that the School has clearly made a significant effort
to address neighbors’ concerns. She congratulated the School on reaching out to
the neighborhood and expressed the desire to help the School meet their
deadlines.
Attorney Janega suggested that since the new proposal which requires further
ZBA consideration appears to have some traction, it would be best not to act
tonight. She recommended allowing the School to continue with installing their
mobile classrooms, while the School comes in with its revised plans and
drawings for ZBA review. Staff could then come back to the Council with a
clearer ordinance, that could be amended and adopted at a later meeting, since
it has already been introduced. She said this would allow the School to move
forward and get it all back to the Council as expeditiously as possible.
Manager Williams remarked that staff concurs with this approach.
Mr. Formolo indicated the School’s willingness to pursue this parallel track so
long as the School can be assured that they can stay on track with their
building schedule. He said that they don’t want to start and then not get
approval.
Steve Hardy, 640 Elder, Gene Ullrich, 648 Elder, and Jim Lahti, 660 Elder,
commented on the new proposal.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to table the matter at this
time. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Johnson, Rintz, Pedian, Poor, Greable, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to direct the Zoning Board of
Appeals to hold a special meeting during the month of April to consider the
modified south elevation plans. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Rintz, Pedian, Johnson, Greable, and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to confirm permission for
NSCDS to complete installation of its mobile classrooms at this time. By roll
call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Rintz,
Johnson, Poor, Greable, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance M-4-2010 – 572 ––––Lincoln–– Ave.: Galleria/Coldwell Banker
Non-Retail Special Use Permit – Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio briefly reviewed this
request for approval of a Special Use Permit that would permit a real estate
office to occupy the first floor in the Winnetka Galleria building at 572
Lincoln Avenue, noting that the matter was discussed at the December 15th and
February 16th Council Meetings, and Ordinance M-4-2010 was introduced at the
March 2nd Council Meeting. He pointed out that the Village Attorney has drafted
two versions of the ordinance because each relates to a different exhibit
depicting alternate floor plans provided by the applicant.
Jim Sayegh, the applicant, expressed a preference for Option #1 but indicated
that both he and Coldwell Banker would be open to either option.
Speaking on behalf of the owners of 552-554 Lincoln, Phil Couri objected to
staff’s dismissal of the eight signatures protesting the special use permit.
Attorney Janega explained her legal opinion that the signatures do not meet the
requirements of the Notary Public Act..
Bernard Citron, attorney for the applicant, concurred with Ms. Janega’s
assessment and commented that all of the parties have been held to the same
standards and the same laws equally.
In response to a question from Trustee Greable regarding the disparity between
staff’s number and the number submitted by the parties, Mr. D’Onofrio explained
how staff had determined the relevant number of parcels within 250 ft. of the
property in question to be thirty-eight.
President Tucker polled the Trustees to determine which floor plan was
preferred, and there was a consensus in favor of Exhibit 1.
President Tucker then called for a motion to amend Ordinance No. M-4-2010 as
presented in Alternate 1, which depicts Exhibit 1. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Poor, so moved. Ayes:
Trustees Rintz, Poor, and Spinney. Nays: Trustees, Pedian, Greable, and
Johnson. There being a tie vote, President Tucker voted affirmatively.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-4-
2010, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Rintz,
Poor, and Spinney. Nays: Trustees, Pedian, Greable, and Johnson. There being a
tie vote, President Tucker voted affirmatively.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-5-2010 – Special Use Permit: ––––26-30 Green Bay Road––
– Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed a request for   a Special Use Permit for
a real estate office at 26-30 Green Bay Road which is located in the C-
1 Limited Retail Commercial Zoning District, commonly known as Indian Hill. He
reported that both the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Plan Commission had
voted favorably on this matter.
The applicant, Mike Golden, 975 Pine Street, commented on his proposal. In
response to a question from Trustee Greable, he indicated that he preferred the
C-1 District to the C-2 Overlay District because he felt there is higher
visibility for drive-by traffic.
Trustee Pedian remarked that the applicant needs to concentrate their parking
on the east side of Green Bay Road and complimented them for taking a over a
building in need of attention and restoration.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
5-2010. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   d)   Resolution No. R-7-2010 – 2010-11 Village Budget – Adoption. President
Tucker announced that the Council would begin with consideration of the Budget
and move on to the fee and rate resolutions.
Mr. McKee summarized the substantive changes made to the original draft budget
as a result of the study session discussions. He projected that a typical
homeowner would experience an increase of $193, or 3.3%, in the cost of
municipal services over last year, noting that most of the increase is the
result of an increase in the wholesale cost of electricity. Mr. McKee also
reviewed changes to the draft capital plan and the transfer of fund to close
out the Streetscape Fund and establish a Business District Revitalization Fund.
In response to a question from Manager Williams, Mr. McKee commented that he
expects that the Village’s electric rates will be fairly comparable to
Commonwealth Edison’s over the next year.
Attorney Janega responded to an inquiry from Bernie Hammer, 1455 Tower, as to
the status of attempts to get AT&T to put its lines underground along Tower Rd.
Manager Williams added that this has been an ongoing issue and that the Village
continues to search for a successful resolution.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower Rd., expressed the hope that with the transfer of the
Streetscape funds, outstanding maintenance issues will not be deferred, as a
failure to maintain can do more harm and lead to public safety issues. He also
sought confirmation that the Village will be moving forward with the six steps
recently outlined by the Village Manager regarding flood control.
President Tucker indicated that in the near future the Council will set forth a
timeline and process for a community-wide dialogue regarding steps that need to
be taken to spend funds to maintain and revitalize our business districts.
Manager Williams added that he believes the Budget has been modified to allow
the Village to move forward in the next year with the flood control steps.
John Jansson, 199 Church Rd., urged the Village to keep all of the funds
currently allocated to the Streetscape Fund in the Business District
Revitalization Fund in light of the potential future redevelopment of the Post
Office site.
Tim Foley, 165 De Windt, thanked the Council for allocating some funds to
address the flooding in south Winnetka, and Joe Nash, 1025 Oak, asked whether
the Village has applied for any Federal funding to address flooding in the
Village.
Manager Williams responded that the Village has been successful on some
stimulus funding and staff is always on the lookout for funding/grant
opportunities, but said that he is not aware of any grant programs for drainage
issues.
Trustee Greable remarked that he hopes staff will be able to bring back the
Manager’s six-point plan for formal adoption by the Council before Manager
Williams retires on June 1 st .
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to amend Resolution R-7-2010
by replacing Exhibit A with the revised Exhibit A in the agenda packet, as
outlined by the Finance Director. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Poor, Greable, Spinney, Johnson, and Rintz.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Resolution R-7-
2010, as amended, to approve the 2010-11 Budget. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Poor, Greable, Spinney, Pedian,
and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to authorize the fiscal
2009/2010 transfers to close out the Streetscape Fund. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Poor, Greable, Spinney,
Pedian, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to establish the Business
District Revitalization Fund, effective immediately. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Poor, Greable, Spinney,
Pedian, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   e)   Updated Fee & Rate Resolutions. President Tucker announced that
consideration of Resolution No. R-13-2010 containing the demolition fees would
be put off until after the consideration of the other fee resolutions. At that
time staff will review the methodology for calculating these fees and audience
comments will be heard.
      i)   Resolution R-8-2010 – Water Rates – Adoption. Trustee Poor, seconded
by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Resolution R-8-2010, establishing the water
rates for the next fiscal year. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Greable, Spinney, Pedian, and Rintz.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
      ii)   Resolution R-9-2010 – Electric Rates – Adoption. Trustee Johnson,
seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Resolution R-9-2010, establishing the
electric rates for the next fiscal year. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees, Johnson, Poor, Greable, Spinney, Pedian, and
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
      iii)   Resolution R-10-2010 – Sewer Rates – Adoption. Trustee Pedian,
seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Resolution R-10-2010, establishing
the sewer rates for the next fiscal year. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Poor, Johnson, Greable, Spinney, and Rintz.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
      iv)   Resolution R-11-2010 – Refuse Rates – Adoption. Trustee Johnson,
seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Resolution R-11-2010, establishing
the refuse rates for the next fiscal year. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Greable, Spinney, Pedian, Poor,
and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
      v)   Resolution R-12-2010 – General Permit & License Fees – Adoption.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adopt Resolution R-12-
2010, establishing the general permit and license fees for the next fiscal
year. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Johnson, Greable, Spinney, Pedian, Poor, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
      vi)   Resolution R-14-2010 – Fire Services Fees – Adoption. Trustee
Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Resolution R-14-2010,
establishing the fire service fees for the next fiscal year. By roll call vote,
the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Pedian, Poor, Johnson, Greable,
Spinney, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
      vii)   Resolution R-13-2010 – Building, Zoning & Construction Fees –
Adoption. Mr. D’Onofrio summarized the evolution of the Village’s demolition
fee and reviewed the methodology used to determine the fee amount, currently
$13,350 and proposed to be increased to $16,070. He explained that it has been
the Village’s philosophy to have the demolition permit fee recover staff time
associated with demolitions and defray the costs related to the overall impact
of demolitions on Village infrastructure. He also shared comparable fee
information from nearby communities, noting that what is really important to a
developer is the total cost of construction, which in many communities is not a
single fee, but the total of a number of different charges. For example, he
compared the actual cost for the same builder to build approximately the same
size house in both Glencoe and Winnetka, pointing out that while Glencoe
charges no demolition permit fee, the final cost of all permits and fees paid
to the Village of Glencoe for a comparable house are approximately 20% higher
per square foot of construction than in Winnetka.
Manager Williams referred the Council to a Memorandum prepared by the Community
Development Department comparing demolition permit fees.
In response to a question from Trustee Pedian, Mr. D’Onofrio indicated that he
expects the number of demolitions in the Village this year will be in the low
20’s, whereas the high water mark several years ago was in the mid 50’s.
Trustee Greable asked why Winnetka has chosen to impose a demolition fee, while
Glencoe has not.
Manager Williams replied that the decision goes back to the high water mark of
demolitions and their impact on the community. At that time the Council
directed staff to make it a core philosophy that taxpayers do not subsidize
demolitions, and thereby, developers.
Trustee Pedian remarked that this is a complex issue and that some of the
public statements made have been unfair because they don’t accurately represent
the true picture.
Commenting on the philosophy established by prior Councils to see that the
Village is made whole and reimbursed for all opportunity costs related to time
spent on demolitions, Mr. McKee explained how the Village prices labor costs as
reflected in the demolition fee. He stated that in his opinion the numbers
reflected in Mr. D’Onofrio’s calculations of the demolition fee are a
reasonable representation of the opportunity cost to the Village of diverting
staff time to demolition related matters, noting that Mr. D’Onofrio’s analysis
assigns no opportunity cost to time spent by the Council and others, like
himself, on demolition related activities.
President Tucker remarked that the community has clearly established a policy
that the costs associated with demolition activities are more appropriately
borne by those that take up the activity than by the majority of taxpayers in
the community.
Joe Nash, 1025 Oak Street, and Steve Strange, 887 Ash Street, spoke against
increasing the demolition fee, citing some unique, extra costs involved in
building in Winnetka.
David Bartels, 265 Poplar, said that he doesn’t understand the logic of
increasing the demolition fee when a lot of the costs are involved with
building rather than demolishing, which particularly burdens the owners of
smaller homes. He suggested that the Council consider raising the building
permit fees instead of the demolition fee.
Howard Handler, representing the North Shore-Barrington Association of Realtors
(NSBAR) objected to the Village’s proposal to increase demolition fees and
disputed the Village’s position that it is comparable to other communities.
Trustee Pedian remarked that the Council had previously asked Mr. Handler for a
list of members of his organization, which has not yet been received. She said
that the Trustees have been researching other communities in an effort to
thoroughly understand the organization’s objections and observed the NSBAR has
approached several neighboring communities with its concerns.
Mr. Handler responded that NSBAR has about 200 members, among them many of the
real estate agents in the community. He represented that the people most
affected by the high demolition fee are people that own older homes that are
likely to be demolished.
Bernard Hammer, 1455 Tower, opined that fees have to be commensurate with the
costs incurred and questioned lumping them together because not everybody needs
or uses all of the components.
Jeff Higgins, 329 Woodland Avenue, commented that despite the full court press
by NSBAR objecting to the Village’s demolition fee, he wholeheartedly supports
raising the demolition fee.
Melissa Mizel, 939 Tower Rd., also supported the demolition fee.
Barbara West, 310 White Oak Ln., urged the Council not to take any action that
would make it more difficult or more expensive to sell older homes.
John Alfini, 1309 Trapp Ln., and Andy Cavallari, 150 Westview, agreed with Ms.
West.
Eric Barton, a Highland Park resident, said he was speaking on behalf of the
Home Builders Association of Chicago. He stated that the home building industry
is in terrible shape right now and that from a home builder’s perspective, all
fees are high. He said that he has not heard any justification for why the
demolition fee should be increased.
Trustee Rintz responded at length to Mr. Handler’s and Mr. Barton’s comments.
He said he is one of the founding members of the City of Chicago Chapter of the
Home Builders Association. To the best of his knowledge, he was unaware of any
authorization by the current board to speak on this issue. Mr. Bartels
certainly was not speaking on behalf of Mr. Rintz or his firm and was not
authorized to represent his interest on the issue. Speaking as a builder
himself, he said that builders do not offer less when the demolition fee is
high, but look at the overall total cost of construction when calculating what
to offer for a property. He expressed dismay at the newspaper ads and robo
calls to residents on this issue, the potential for such tactics to tear the
community apart, and the amount of time that has already been spent by the
Council and staff on this topic. He concluded by saying that he would support
an increase in the demolition fee.
Trustee Pedian concurred, saying that the Council has spent an enormous amount
of time to ensure that it is not missing critical information in order to asses
the proposed increase.
Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, and Greable agreed, as did President Tucker.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adopt Resolution R-13-
2010, establishing the building, zoning, and construction fees for the next
fiscal year. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Pedian, Poor, Greable, Spinney, Johnson, and Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   f)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2010 – Village Code Amendment: Designating an ADA
Coordinator and establishing an ADA Grievance Procedure – Introduction/
Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that this ordinance would amend the Village
Code as required by State law regarding ADA notification and an ADA grievance
procedure. Because the compliance date for adhering to this new law is the end
of March, she suggested that the Council waive introduction and move directly
to adoption.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. MC-2-2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously.
Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Greable, Spinney, Rintz, and Pedian. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-
2-2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Johnson, Greable, Spinney, Rintz, and Pedian. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Request for New Liquor License Category: D’s Haute Dogs. Chief DeLopez
reviewed a request from Jared Boyar, owner of D’s Haute Dogs, for
reconsideration of his October request for the creation of a new liquor license
category that would permit his establishment to sell beer and wine.
Manager Williams reported that he had received two emails in support of this
request.
Chief DeLopez and Sgt. Marc Hornstein addressed issues of liquor service and
display as well as the process for background investigations and on-site
inspections to determine compliance with Village laws.
Mr. Boyar spoke in support of his request.
Trustee Greable suggested that the Council re-examine the question of expanding
liquor service again at a study session.
Attorney Janega explained some of the differences between various existing
license categories, saying that D’s is a type of restaurant, but not as defined
in the liquor ordinance. She said that a license category could be created to
accommodate D’s business model, but that kind of service and characteristics
must be carefully delineated so that the Village doesn’t inadvertently provide
for liquor to be served at a fast food restaurant.
After a lengthy discussion, there was a consensus among the Trustees not to
create a new license category at this time.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. None.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Spinney reported on recent Library Board and its Strategic
Planning Committee meetings.
      ii)   Trustee Poor reported that the WEFC has advanced two of its major
initiatives.
   c)   Attorney. Ms. Janega announced that she is moderating the IML Municipal
Attorneys Conference in Bloomington on March 19 and also will be a featured
speaker on the new FOIA laws.
   d)   Manager. Mr. Williams advised the audience that Ms. Janega had been
instrumental in trying to make the new FOIA laws better for municipalities than
originally proposed.
11)   Appointments.
President Tucker called for a motion to reappoint Mike Duhl to the Board of
Fire and Police Commissioners for a full term. Trustee Johnson, seconded by
Trustee Pedian so moved. By voice vote, the motion carried unanimously.
She then called for a motion to appoint Debbie Ross to replace David Abell as
Chair of the Environmental & Forestry Commission, effective April 1 st .
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, so moved. By voice vote, the motion
carried unanimously.
Trustee Poor thanked Mr. Abell for his leadership on the Environmental &
Forestry Commission.
12)   Executive Session.
Due to the lateness of the hour, no Executive Session was held.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 12:
15 a.m.
            
Recording Secretary
April 6, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
April 6, 2010
(Approved: April 20, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
6, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor and Jennifer Spinney.
Absent: Trustee Chris Rintz. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Village Forester Jim Stier, and
approximately 4 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
a)   Thursday, April 15, 2010 Study Session. All of the Council members
present, except Trustee Johnson, indicated that they expected to attend.
b)   April 20, 2010, Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the Agenda. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian
and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
5)   Consent Agenda
a)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1647 and 1648. Approving Warrant List No. 1647 in the
amount of $1,342,384.96, and Warrant List No. 1648 in the amount of
$441,558.82.
b)   Ordinance M-5-2010 – Special Use Permit: ––––26-30 Green Bay Road–– –
Adoption. Grants a Special Use Permit for a real estate office at 26-30 Green
Bay Road in the C-1 Limited Retail Commercial Zoning District.
c)   Transformer Bid, Bid #10-007. Authorizes the purchase of five 100-KVA
single-phase transformers from Border States in an amount not to exceed
$14,649.50, and the purchase of one 500-KVA three-phase transformer from Wesco
in an amount not to exceed $29,739.00.
d)   Cable Bid, Bid #10-006. Authorizes the purchase of primary cable from
Okonite at unit prices in an amount not to exceed $440,020 and the purchase of
secondary cable from Resco at unit prices in a amount not to exceed $139,000.
e)   Directional Boring Contract Extension, Bid # 08-010. Authorizes the award
of directional boring and wire pulling work from June 1, 2010 through May 31,
2011 to Archon Construction Co., in an amount not to exceed $690,000.
f)   High Lift Pump Replacement, Water Plant. Awards the contract to replace
the Water Plant’s #4 high lift pump to Clark Dietz in the amount of $30,060.
g)   Electrical Line Clearance (Tree Trimming). Authorizes Nels Johnson Tree
Experts to perform electrical line clearance work at the third-year hourly
rates contained in Bid #08-022 in an amount not to exceed $108,000.
h)   Parkway Tree Trimming, Removal and Maintenance. This item awards tree
trimming and maintenance contracts at the unit rates contained in bid proposals
as follows:
   i)   Parkway Tree & Stump Removal: Landscape Concepts Management
   ii)   Parkway Tree Trimming: Trees “R” Us
   iii)   Emergency Time & Material Work: Landscape Concepts Management
i)   2010 Parkway Tree Planting Bids. Awards the bid for the 2010 Parkway Tree
Planting Program jointly to St. Aubin’s, Fox Ridge, A. Weiler, and Goodmark
Nurseries, based on the lower price per tree species as submitted in their
respective unit price bid proposals, for an estimated total amount of $34,744.
j)   Landscape Maintenance Contract Extension. Extends the terms of the 2008
and 2009 Landscape Maintenance Contract with Anthony Scopelliti Landscaping, as
modified, for an estimated amount not to exceed $103,280.
k)   One-Year Extension of Yard Waste Composting Contract with Thelen Sand &
Gravel. Authorizes a one-year extension of the current composting contract with
Thelen Sand & Gravel for $7.00 per cubic yard.
l)   Resolution R-15-2010 MFT Resolution for Improvements by Municipality Under
the Illinois Highway Code – ––––Chestnut Street–– Reconstruction. Authorizes
the use of MFT funds for the Chestnut Street Reconstruction Project in an
amount not to exceed $200,000.
m)   Resolution R-16-2010: Extension of Sidewalk Replacement Contract with
Schroeder & Schroeder, Inc. Authorizes an extension of the contract with
Schroeder & Schroeder, Inc., for sidewalk replacement for 2010, with unit
prices increased 2.2% to reflect the CPI, for an amount not to exceed $130.00.
n)   Resolution R-17-2010: State Highway Work. Provides the State of Illinois
performance security as required for Highway Permits, for calendar 2010 & 2011.
o)   Resolution R-18-2010: Cook County Data Sharing Agreement. Authorizes the
Village to sign an intergovernmental agreement allowing it access to Cook
County’s digital GIS information.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian and Greable. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Rintz.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance M-6-2010: Establishing Special Service Area #3, Providing for
Pavement and Stormwater Improvements to Trapp Lane for the Purpose of
Dedicating Trapp Lane as a Public Street .
Mr. Saunders reported that at the Council’s direction, Staff had been meeting
with Trapp Lane homeowners to develop an engineering design to bring the street
up to standards so that it can be dedicated to the Village. He indicated that
all 20 of the property owners have signed agreements regarding the
reconstruction and public dedication of Trapp Lane, and propose to pay for 50%
of the project cost using a Special Service Area (SSA) to capture the funds.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the following improvements planned for Trapp Lane: (i)
retaining the existing 30-foot easement reaching the required 50-foot roadway
width by using dedicated utility easements; (ii) excavating and replacing the
existing pavement of the roadway to a width of 20 feet; (iii) stormwater
drainage improvements contained in a 2002 drainage study of the area; and (iv)
hiring a consultant to provide detailed plans and specifications for the
project.
Mr. Saunders said the projected cost of the improvements is $505,516, and under
the SSA, the property tax bills of the Trapp Lane homeowners would be assessed
for their half of the cost. He reviewed the steps necessary to establish the
SSA, which include introducing an ordinance establishing the SSA, holding a
public hearing, waiting 60 days for any objections to be filed, and finally,
adopting the ordinance establishing the SSA. He reviewed a preliminary schedule
of activities for SSA #3 and proposed setting the public hearing date for May
4, 2010, in the Council Chambers.
Mr. Saunders indicated that while the project is not specified in the 2010-11
budget, it is anticipated that that since the Village’s portion is so small,
the funds could be taken from the general street construction fund, or could be
borrowed from a bank. He indicated that the Village’s financial analyst
recommended using a figure that is 125% of the engineer’s estimate, as there
are no bid figures to go by. Lastly, he noted that the Trapp Lane homeowners
worked very hard to come to agreement on how to finance the project, and that
Trapp Lane Association director Ernie Weiss was instrumental at keeping
everyone on task during the arduous process.
Attorney Janega recommended waiving introduction and passing the ordinance,
since passage of the ordinance is what triggers the process for the public
hearing and objections. She noted that in order to go straight to adoption, the
figure of 125% of the estimate needs to be changed to $631,895.
Mr. Saunders answered questions from several of the Trustees, after which
Manager Williams commended him on his hard work getting the 20 neighbors to
agree on the project.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance M-6-2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Johnson, Spinney, Pedian and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Ordinance M-6-2010
with the corrected amount of $631,895 for the cost estimate in Section 4 of the
Ordinance. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to set a Public Hearing for
the Proposed Special Service Area #3 at 7:30 p.m. on Tuesday, May 4, 2010 in
the Winnetka Council Chambers. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Pedian and Greable. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
