April 15, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
Thursday, April 15, 2010
(Approved: May 4, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
April 15, 2010 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem King Poor called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present:
Trustees Gene Greable, Linda Pedian, Chris Rintz and Jennifer Spinney. Absent:
Village President Jessica Tucker and Trustee William Johnson. Also in
attendance: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega,
Fire Chief Scott Smith, Deputy Fire Chief Steve Laureys, Fire Lt. John Ripka,
and one person in the audience.
2)   Direct Connect Fire Alarm (Radio System).
Chief Smith described the Village’s current fire alarm detection systems and
said the contract with Alarm Detection Systems, Inc. (ADS) to service and
control the direct connection alarms in the Village has expired. He reported
that the technology has changed in the six years since the contract was signed
and he is now comfortable with radio alarm systems, as they are reliable and
less expensive than phone lines. He indicated that the proposal will benefit
the Village’s commercial establishments, since many of them have phone line
issues.
Lt. Ripka gave a Power Point presentation explaining how the different alarm
systems work and how the current Village system is set up, and described the
benefits of a radio alarm system, which include: (i) lower costs to
subscribers; (ii) potential revenues- for the Village; (iii) fewer false alarm
calls; (iv) quicker repair time; and (v) less out-of-service time.
Lt. Ripka also presented the relative pros and cons of vendor-owned radio
equipment and Village-owned equipment and said the main advantages of owning
the equipment would be that the Village would also own the FCC license for the
radio frequency, and would control subscriber charges.
Chief Smith recommended that the Council direct him to issue an RFP for a radio
alarm system and to discuss at a later date whether the Village or a vendor
owns the equipment.
The Council discussed the issue, asked questions, and ascertained that a
Village-owned system would not require additional personnel to administer it.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, said he found the current central station alarm
system’s gaps in response time to be troubling.
President Pro Tem Poor commented favorably on the case for switching to radio
system owned by the Village, and he directed the Chief to examine the costs and
pricing and to report back to the Council when he is ready.
3)   Board and Commission Appointments.
Attorney Janega reviewed the Village’s process for building a pool of
candidates for boards and commissions, observing that over the years the
process has evolved into one in which it is largely the Council members who
suggest persons for appointment.
Attorney Janega described a possible a two-step process: 1) information
gathering to build a pool of interested people; and 2) screening serious
candidates for conflicts. She noted that the second step wouldn’t be necessary
until someone was being considered for a particular role. She reviewed sample
talent bank applications from Kenilworth and Wilmette, and suggested doing
something similar to the Kenilworth process in Winnetka.
The Council discussed possible procedures for obtaining talent for the boards
and committees and for discussing the candidates’ qualifications.
There followed a discussion about the application of the Village’s ethics
ordinance for bringing potential conflicts to light, and about how to
communicate concerns about a given candidate in an open meeting without
embarrassing that person, violating the provisions of the Open Meetings Act and
opening up the process to politicking.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, said the candidates for a board should be prepared
to take on some controversial subjects and should have a certain tolerance for
being in that position, as part of the qualifications to serve.
Attorney Janega suggested giving the matter some more thought, and added that
she would gather information from other municipal attorneys about how their
clients deal with the appointment process. She also asked the Council members
to send her their comments on the draft information sheet contained in the
agenda materials.
4)   Discussion: Council Communication Procedures.
Manager Williams said since e-mails are becoming the preferred method for
residents to communicate with the Council, printing out last-minute e-mails for
distribution to the Council can be burdensome. He suggested setting a deadline
of 5 PM the day before a meeting, to ensure that Tuesday afternoons are not
devoted to printing out emails which the Council is then expected to read when
they arrive at the meeting.
Manager Williams also said that the public needs to be aware that anything they
send in becomes part of the public record and can be subject to FOIA requests,
and that a notice to that effect will be posted on the agendas from now on.
Finally, he strongly recommended that all Council members use a Winnetka email
account for Village business.
5)   Reports.
a)   Trustee Poor reviewed some highlights from the last EFC meeting.
Executive Session. None.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:35 p.m.
