Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
a)   Ordinance No. M-6-2010 – Trapp Lane SSA #3 – Reconsider & Adoption . Mr.
Saunders reported that Ordinance M-6-2010, adopted at the Council’s April 6 th
meeting, establishes a Special Service Area to fund stormwater and pavement
improvements to Trapp Lane as part of the process of dedicating it to the
Village as a public street. He noted that the publication deadlines did not
allow adequate notice to meet the requirements for the hearing date of May 4,
2010, and that the ordinance therefore needed to be reconsidered and amended to
change the hearing date to May 18, 2010.
Trustee Greable asked if the Village had the option to join in the bond issue
if it wished and Mr. Saunders replied that it could.
Attorney Janega noted that the public hearing will give everyone a chance to
ask questions.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to reconsider Ordinance
M-6-2010. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to amend Section 3 of
Ordinance
M-6-2010 to indicate a public hearing date of May 18, 2010. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Ordinance M-6-2010
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
b)   Resolution No. R-20-2010 – Plat of Consolidation – ––––781 Locust Street––
(Schmetterling Subdivision – Adoption. Mr. Norkus reported that a plat of
consolidation is being requested by the property owners at 781 Locust and 788
Walden, in order to divide a land-locked plot of land between them. He noted
that the consolidation will make 781 Locust a “lot of record” as required by
the Building Code and will give the Village utility easements for the
construction of the new home at 781 Locust.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Resolution R-20-
2010 approving the Schmetterling Subdivision. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
c)   Proposed Healy Resubdivision of 9 Indian Hill Road / 3 Golf Lane.
   i)   Ordinance No. M-7-2010 – Authorizing the Plat of Annexation for the
Southerly Portion of ––––9 Indian Hill Road–– into the ––––Village– of
––Winnetka–– – Introduction. Mr. Norkus reviewed this request for a
resubdivision request to reconfigure four existing parcels into two lots that
will each greatly exceed the minimum lot area for the R-2 Zoning District, and
will eliminate a nonconforming flag lot. He observed that although the two
properties at 9 Indian Hill and 3 Golf Lane had nonconformities on them, the
Plan Commission has determined that there will be no adverse impact from
approving the resubdivision.
Mr. Norkus noted, however, that a portion of 9 Indian Hill currently lies in
unincorporated Cook County, and that the petitioner has therefore filed an
application for annexation of the land into the Village. He indicated that
introduction of tonight’s ordinance approving the plat of annexation plat would
be followed by passage of the ordinance at the next Council meeting, along with
adoption of a Resolution approving the plat of resubdivision.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance M-7-
2010 authorizing the Plat of Annexation for 9 Indian Hill. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Boy Scout David Jaggard, 137 Glenwood, commented that he was working on a merit
badge and asked what the Village was doing to help clear out invasive buckthorn
on the Green Bay Trail. Mr. Saunders replied that the Village works with the
Park District to clear the trail, but that efforts stop at the railroad
embankment. David offered help from the youth at Winnetka Bible Church.
8)   Old Business.
None.
a)   Petition for Reconsideration of Ordinance M-3-2009, Zoning Variance at 941
Tower. Attorney Janega reviewed this request, which asks for the repeal of
ordinance M-3-2009, granted on April 21, 2009. She said the key point for the
Council to bear in mind is that the Illinois Statutes provide an exclusive
remedy for overturning a zoning variation, which is an action in the Illinois
courts. She observed that all of the issues brought up in the petition are
issues for judicial determination, and she recommended that, since no action is
required, the Council advise the parties to talk to their attorneys.
Trustee Poor asked if there was a remedy for the water pipe situation.
Attorney Janega replied that staff can take steps administratively to protect
the pipes and that Mr. D’Onofrio has talked to the parties and is addressing
the situation so that construction can go forward without an interruption in
water service to 939 Tower, and without harm to neighboring properties.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that Village staff is aware of the water service
situation at 941 Tower, and he has informed the Public Works and Water &
Electric Departments. He said he told the owner of 941 Tower that if the water
line is damaged they will have to repair it, as the Village’s policy is that
the party doing the work is responsible for repairs.
Melissa Mizel, 939 Tower, said she believed the Council had the prerogative to
repeal the Ordinance.
David Schrauth, attorney for the owner of 941 Tower, agreed with Attorney
Janega’s assessment of the situation, and the remedy must be sought in court.
Michael Edwards, 939 Tower, said the Village should be firmer in its
application of its powers with regard to tree trimming.
Attorney Janega said the Village’s role in protecting against tree trimming is
codified in the Tree Preservation Ordinance, and that a permit is not required
for trimming trees. She observed that the Village Code relies on neighbors to
be people of good will, and that absent that condition, the parties should seek
legal counsel.
The Trustees agreed to defer to Attorney Janega’s legal opinion on the matter,
and denied the petition to repeal Ordinance M-3-2009.
9)   New Business.
a)   Policy Direction: Improving Unpaved Alleys. Mr. Saunders reported that
there are currently four unpaved alleys in the Village, which have been unpaved
since the neighborhoods in which they are located were originally developed. He
observed that over the years, many alleys in the Village have been paved, using
a 40+ year-old policy to allocate the costs at 15% to the Village and 85% to
the adjacent residents. He noted that the Village pays 100% of storm sewer
costs associated with the alley upgrading and pays for maintenance and repairs
of alleys.
Mr. Saunders said while property owners agree on the need to pave the alleys,
the Village cannot get them to develop a consensus on the cost-sharing aspect
of the project. He reviewed an evaluation of the current policy, which
projected the costs to pave and upgrade each of the four unpaved alleys, and
also included alternative funding ratios for the Village and residents. He
noted that a survey of other area towns that have gravel alleys revealed that
all of them pay more than 15% of the cost to upgrade them. He recommended that
Winnetka increase its funding to 75% of the projected cost and create Special
Service Areas for the residents to pay their part of the expenses.
Mr. Saunders described the main advantages of amending the policy as: (i) only
a simple majority of the property owners and registered voters in the SSA
territory is required to establish it, as opposed to the unanimous approval
currently required; (ii) the Village reaps a benefit from the paved alleys in
the form of a reduction of maintenance costs, and (iii) the drainage and
function of the alleys would be improved.
The Council asked questions of Mr. Saunders and discussed the proposal.
Trustee Rintz observed that the Village paying less than 75% of the costs won’t
make a difference in bringing the homeowners on board, remarked that the alleys
are public property much like sidewalks are, and said it is a disgrace for a
town of Winnetka’s caliber to have gravel alleys
After some more discussion, a majority of the Trustees agreed with Mr.
Saunders’ recommendation that the Village pay 75% of the cost of upgrading the
alleys, with an SSA established for the residents’ repayment of costs.
b)   Additional Outdoor Seating Permits. Manager Williams reported that two
Winnetka restaurants, Mirani’s and Love’s Winnetka, had missed the deadline for
turning in outdoor seating permit requests and that Love’s Winnetka was asking
to put additional tables in front of a neighboring vacant storefront and
computer store. He indicated that historically, the Village has denied requests
for extra tables in front of vacant properties, but that in this situation
there is common ownership of all the properties and the property owner and the
other store both support the idea. He recommended approving the request on an
experimental basis for one year only, to make sure there are no unintended
consequences.
The Council agreed with Manager Williams’ recommendation and Trustee Pedian,
seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to approve the outdoor seating permits for
Mirani’s, and Love's Winnetka with the proposed expanded table layout for
Love’s for a one-year trial period. By voice vote, the motion passed
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve the Sidewalk
Restaurant rider for Mirani's. By voice vote, the motion passed.
10)   Reports
a)   Village President. President Tucker commented that the Council may want to
consider passing another resolution on issues important to Winnetka, to send a
message to legislators in Springfield.
b)   Trustees.
   i)   Trustee Rintz announced that there would be a special Plan Commission
meeting on Thursday April 22 nd .
   ii)   Trustee Spinney reported that the Library Board is having thorough
discussions about strategic planning; announced that RED Center voted to
