President Tucker announced that this request was introduced at the April 20 th
Council Meeting to approve the annexation of the southerly portion of 9 Indian
Hill from Cook County into the Village of Winnetka, as part of the lot
reconfiguration for the proposed Healy resubdivision.
There being no comments or discussion, President Tucker called for a motion to
adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Ordinance M-7-2010
approving the Plat of Annexation for a portion of 9 Indian Hill Road. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
      ii)   Resolution R-19-2010: Healy Resubdivision of 9 Indian Hill Road and
3 Golf Lane – Adoption. Ms. Janega reported that the substance of this
Resolution was discussed at the April 20 th Council meeting and that it would
approve the consolidation of four separate lots, comprising six parcels, into
two lots of record and at the same time would also cure a number of zoning
nonconformities. She added that the final plat contains the recommended
modifications.
President Tucker ascertained from the audience and the Trustees that there were
no further comments or need for discussion and called for a motion to adopt the
resolution.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adopt Resolution R-19-
2010 approving the Healy Resubdivision. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
President Tucker acknowledged the Boy Scouts in attendance.
8)   Old Business.
   a)   Proposed Multifaceted Stormwater Management Strategy. Mr. Saunders
reviewed staff’s recommendations and a timeline for a Village-wide stormwater
management strategy, which would include:
      ·   Skokie River pump station expansion feasibility study: Staff would
obtain a technical feasibility report from Christopher B. Burke Engineering,
Ltd. (CBBEL) and research the Forest Preserve’s licensing requirements.
Estimated cost: $16,000.
      ·   Further evaluation of northern improvements (without detention).
Staff proposes to hire CBBEL to analyze the consequences of not storing water
on Duke Child’s Field in conjunction with proposed Willow road drainage
improvements in that area. Staff would also evaluate the feasibility of
obtaining a discharge permit at Willow Road and the river from the IDNR. Any
future drainage work should be coordinated with the Willow Road project to
avoid tearing up Willow Road twice in a short time period. Estimated cost:
$50,000
      ·   Drainage Survey for areas not surveyed in the 2009 study. CBBEL would
be asked to submit a proposal to survey the remaining drainage areas of the
Village. The proposal would be presented to the Council in June of this year.
Initial survey work could be completed by the end of September at an estimated
cost of $30,000. After analyzing the survey results with CBBEL, staff would
return to the Council in October of 2010 with a report recommending additional
areas for modeling of proposed improvements, including cost estimates.
Estimated cost for the full study would be up to $165,000, including modeling.
      ·   Financing Study. Mr. Saunders recommended that the Council hire a
consultant to study financing options, including a stormwater utility, cash
reserves and special service areas, while the other steps are being taken. An
RFP could be issued and proposals submitted to the Council in June, 2010, with
a financing option being presented by the end of August and a report on a
stormwater utility study being presented in October. Estimated cost: $50,000
      ·   Public Input/Education. Once the reports have been completed, the
Council should hold public hearings and possibly survey residents in order to
gather public input.
      ·   Provide Policy Direction. The last step in the cycle would be for the
Council to provide policy direction on a comprehensive drainage improvement
policy in 2011.
Council discussion centered around costs of the project, the necessity for
professional help with financing options, surveying residents to discover
additional areas that may have flooding problems, and the feasibility of
expanding the pump station which would be critical to the southern set of
improvements. Trustee Pedian suggested the possibility of reducing costs by
hiring an intern or using current staff to do the survey work. Trustee Poor
requested that the study include an analysis of the impact that replacing green
space with impervious surface over the years has had on flooding issues.
Mr. Richard Kates, 1326 Tower Road, suggested simplifying the survey to
encourage greater response and reduce costs.
Mr. Mitch Wywiorski, 1042 Westmoor, stated his support of the pump station
expansion and was of the opinion that it should be at the top of the list
because it is a time-consuming process.
Mr. Tim Foley, 165 DeWindt, speaking on behalf of the DeWindt Homeowners
Association, expressed support for the plan and asked that it be expedited to
provide help to areas that currently known to have flooding problems.
The Council reached a consensus to proceed with the plan as outlined in the 6-
step stormwater management strategy developed by staff, directing staff to
reduce costs wherever possible.
9)   Seating of the New Village Council.
   a)   Village Clerk’s Report: Election Results. Village Clerk Doug Williams
reported the certified results of the local election. Mr. Williams read the
results of the February 2, 2010, election in which Cook County Clerk David
Orr’s office declared Gene Greable, Bill Johnson and Chris Rintz duly elected
to second terms as Village Trustees.
      i)   Administration of Oath of Office to Trustees-elect E. Gene Greable,
William Johnson, and Christopher Rintz. Mr. Williams administered the Oath of
Office to Trustees Greable, Johnson and Rintz.
   b)   Call to Order of the New Council. President Tucker called the meeting
of the new Council to order at 9:15 p.m. Present: Trustees Gene Greable, Bill
Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Village Attorney Katherine
Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders and one person in the audience.
10)   Ordinances and Resolutions
   a)   Ordinance MC-3-2010: Amending the Village Code to Address the
Appointment of an Interim Village Manager – Introduction and Adoption. Attorney
Janega reported that with the pending retirement of the Village Manager, the
Council will need to appoint someone to perform the duties of Village Manager
until a new Manager is appointed. She indicated that some minor amendments need
to be made to the Village Code pertaining to Village Clerk, Deputy Clerk and
acting Village Manager.
Ms. Janega explained that Ordinance MC-3-2010 would allow the appointment of a
non-resident interim appointee, which would also expand the pool of qualified
candidates for that position. Because of the need to get signature forms in
place, Ms. Janega recommended that introduction be waived and that the Council
proceed to adoption.
Trustee Greable suggested a revision to provide that the interim village
manager could be an administrative officer or other highly qualified individual
rather than someone with prior experience as a village manager.
Ms. Janega concurred.
President Tucker determined that there were no further questions, comments or
discussion on this item.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance MC-3-2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Johnson seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-3-2010
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance MC-4-2010: Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to
Residency Requirements for the Village Clerk – Introduction and Adoption.
Attorney Janega reported that Ordinance MC-4-2010 removes the residency
requirement for the Village Clerk and allows the appointment of one or more
Deputy Clerks. She recommended that the Council waive introduction and proceed
to adoption.
There being no further comment or discussion, President Tucker called for a
motion to introduce and then adopt the ordinance.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance MC-4-2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-4-
2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson,
Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
11)   New Business.
   a)   10 Indian Hill Road / 3 Golf Lane Sanitary Sewer Relocation and
Easement Agreements. Removed from agenda.
12)   Reports.
a)   Village President. President Tucker referred to the salary resolutions
that are approved at this time of year and thanked the department heads,
village manager, village attorney and other staff members for their diligence,
dedication and professionalism and for stepping up during the transition to the
new village manager. She also thanked Trustee Johnson for chairing the BCDC
until a new chair person was found.
b)   Trustees.
i)   Trustee Johnson reported that he had attended a class on incident command
overview at the Center for Domestic Preparedness in connection with Homeland
Security and found it helpful and interesting.
c)   Village Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that she would be in
Springfield Thursday and Friday for the Illinois Institute of Continuing Legal
Education’s Municipal Law update program. She noted that she was part of the
planning committee for the program, that she will be moderating a panel on the
Freedom of Information Act, Open Meetings Act, and will be speaking on ethics.
Ms. Janega then reported that she will not be able to attend the Village
Manager’s last Council meeting on May 18 th , and so expressed her deep
gratitude and admiration for Mr. Williams over the nearly 17 years they’ve
worked together, and wished him well in his retirement.
d)   Village Manager. Mr. Williams proudly reported that the Village had
slightly less than 2000 hours of overtime this year which is a significantly
low amount historically. He thanked the department heads for their efforts in
this area.
Mr. Williams also recognized Trustee Rintz’s dog, Bailey, who discovered a
sinkhole at a Village intersection, enabling the Village to take immediate
steps to avoid what could have become a serious hazard. He presented Trustee
Rintz with a citizenship award for Bailey.
