Deputy Clerk
May 11, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
May 11, 2010
(Approved: June 1, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
11, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Jennifer Spinney, Bill Johnson, and Chris Rintz, Greable, Poor. Trustee Pedian
arrived at 8:13 p.m. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Doug
Williams, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works, Steve
Saunders, Assistant Public Works Director Steve Auth, Director of Community
Development Michael D'Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development
Brian Norkus, Chief of Police Joe DeLopez, Deputy Chief of Police Patrick
Kreis, Sgt. Richard Carlson and approximately 5 persons in the audience.
President Tucker announced that item (2) on the agenda, Administrative Hearing
Process, would be discussed first at the request of the Environmental and
Forestry Commission.
2)   Administrative Hearing Process.
Chief DeLopez explained the administrative hearing process that would establish
a local court-like forum for the adjudication of certain municipal ordinance
violations. After providing the Council with information from other communities
who are using the process, such as Glencoe and Lake Forest, Chief DeLopez
introduced Mr. David Eterno, an administrative law judge for several suburban
communities and the City of Chicago.
Mr. Eterno described his background, the procedure for a typical hearing for a
municipal citation and staff required.
[Trustee Pedian arrived in the course of this discussion.]
The Council discussed the types of ordinance violations that could be heard in
this forum, the Village Prosecutor’s role in monitoring the process, the
collection of fines and possible costs of the program. After obtaining a
consensus from the Trustees to explore this further, President Tucker requested
that Chief DeLopez and Attorney Janega determine what offenses would be
appropriate and develop a model for the Council to consider.
3)   Construction and Demolition Debris Recycling Ordinance Proposal.
David Abell and Jan Bawden, members of the Winnetka Environmental and Forestry
Commission, presented the Commission’s proposal for a program to increase the
amount of recycling of non-hazardous construction and demolition debris. Dubbed
“Dollars in your Dumpster,” the program would focus on educating contractors
and would require contractors to file a recycling plan at the beginning of a
project, identifying at least 50% of construction or demolition material for
recycling. The Commission also suggests a $250 fee to cover administrative
costs and a $2000 deposit that would be refunded upon compliance or forfeited
if the recycling target is not met. Mr. Abell commented that most large
contractors are already recycling and that the proposed program would encourage
smaller contractors to participate. The Commission believes the program would
be attractive to contractors as well as helpful to the environment because
nearly all recycled materials are used in new construction and the cost to
recycle is almost half that of unloading materials at a transfer station.
The Council commented on the opportunity presented by having a recycling
facility in Northbrook, the inclusion of small and medium sized construction
projects as well as larger ones, and whether or not the $2000 deposit was a
necessary or effective method of encouraging recycling by contractors. The
consensus was to direct staff to work with the Commission to obtain further
information and determine what steps are necessary to implement the program.
4)   Reports.
Trustee Rintz reported that the Plan Commission would have a special meeting
May 12th.
Trustee Johnson reported that he had attended the BCDC meeting.
Manager Williams distributed a summary of the financial condition of the
Village over the past 10 years reporting that revenue was stronger than
predicted allowing the Village to add $1.2 million to the $22.6 million of
reserves in the general fund. He was optimistic about the next fiscal year as a
result.
5)   Executive Session.
None
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion passed. Meeting adjourned at 10:27 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
May 18, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
May 18, 2010
(Approved: June 15, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
18, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Doug Williams, Community
Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders,
Water & Electric Director Brian Keys, and approximately 50 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   May 20, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present except Trustee Poor indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   June 1, 2010, Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote. the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney,
Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   May 4, 2010, Regular Council Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1653 and 1654. Approving Warrant List No. 1653 in
the amount of $901,232.39, and Warrant List No. 1654 in the amount of
$331,960.33.
   c)   Resolution R-23-2010: MFT Resurfacing of Various Streets. Approves the
use of MFT funds to resurface various Village streets.
   d)   Resolution R-25-2010: SWANCC Board Appointment. Appoints Interim
Village Manager Liz Rosenthal as Winnetka’s alternate SWANCC director.
   e)   Bid No. 10-015: Metal Clad Switchgear. Authorizes a purchase order to
Power Line Supply in the amount of $47,030.00 for the purchase of metal clad
switchgear, per Bid #10-015.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution R-24-2010: Commending Doug Williams, Village Manager –
Adoption. President Tucker announced that she was honored to present this
resolution commending Manager Williams on behalf of the Council, adding that
his legacy will never be forgotten.
After President Tucker read the commendation, the Trustees each expressed their
appreciation and gratitude for Manager Williams’ service and dedication to the
Village. Next, members of the audience shared their comments honoring Manager
Williams, after which Manager Williams gave his farewell comments.
Manager Williams began by noting that the Winnetka Village Council is made up
of residents who volunteer their efforts and contribute many hours of their
time towards making decisions that serve the best interests of the entire
community. He stressed that these talented, good-hearted and intelligent
volunteers are an absolute necessity for Winnetka to remain a shining example
of good government.
Manager Williams continued that he is concerned about what he perceives to be a
rising tide of incivility in Winnetka, including personal attacks and unfounded
conspiracy theories, which threaten to dry up the pool of civic-minded
volunteers willing to serve in such a climate. While urging residents to
continue to be actively engaged in the public policy process, he advocated a
respectful approach, which will enrich the Village’s participatory democracy
and will ultimately improve the policy decisions that are made.
Manager Williams reiterated his point by recommending that Winnetkans be hard
on the issues but soft on the people, in order to ensure that the Village’s
most valuable resource – its talented and capable volunteers on the Council and
Village boards and commissions – will continue to step forward to serve.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adopt Resolution R-24-2010,
commending Village Manager Doug Williams. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance M-3-2010 – Special Use Permit and Zoning Variations: ––––310
Green Bay Road, ––––North––– ––Shore– ––Country– ––Day School–– – Adoption.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to remove Ordinance M-3-2010
from the table. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the special use permit is being requested by North
Shore Country Day School (NSCDS) in order to construct additions the ’’’’Upper’
’’School’’ building. He reviewed the history of the matter, which was first
heard by the Council at its February 4, 2010, meeting and was discussed again
at its March 2 nd and March 16 th meetings, with presentations to the DRB and
ZBA in March and April.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the revised design from NSCDS and indicated that the ZBA
had recommended approval of the zoning variations that are being requested in
connection with the revisions. He observed that the DRB had voted to recommend
approval conditional upon further review of the landscaping and trash enclosure
plans.
Tom Doer, Headmaster of NSCDS, urged the Council to approve the request so the
school could stay on track with its construction schedule and have students
back in the building for the Fall, 2011 semester.
Jim Lahti, 660 Elder, said that the neighbors who had originally been opposed
to the plan have had good communications with the school, they felt listened
to, their changes were incorporated and they now approve of the request.
The Trustees complimented the school and the neighbors on being able to work
together to move the project forward.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to amend Ordinance M-3-2010.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Ordinance M-3-2010 as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Ordinance M-9-2010 – Zoning Variation: 1108 Merrill – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed this request for rear yard setback and building height
variations to permit two additions to the existing legal, nonconforming garage.
He noted that the neighborhood is unique in that the property is bounded by the
commercial district to the east, Sacred Heart school to the south, multifamily
residences to the north and single family homes to the west.
Susan Connor, the applicant’s attorney, described the project and asked the
Council to approve the request.
After a brief Council discussion, Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson,
moved to introduce Ordinance M-9-2010. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   d)   Ordinance M-8-2010 – Zoning Variation: 310 ––––Woodland–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a side yard setback
variation to allow construction of a new detached garage to replace an existing
nonconforming garage, which is substandard and dilapidated.
The applicants, Jeff and Carol Comeaux, said they planned on staying in the
house and were trying to improve it by replacing the old garage, which would
look better and would be able to accommodate a typical modern car.
There being no questions or comments, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Rintz, moved to introduce Ordinance M-8-2010. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, commented that the Village needs to do a simple
drainage survey to gather information for the Council that pinpoints where the
problem areas are. Manager Williams said Public Works Director Steve Saunders
would discuss the issue with Mr. Kates and report back to the Council in the
near future.
8)   Old Business.
   a)   Public Hearing: Special Service Area #3 – Trapp Lane Improvements.
President Tucker convened the public hearing at 9:40 p.m. There being no
audience members who stepped forward to participate, Manager Williams swore in
Mr. Saunders for his testimony.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the details of the proposed improvements to the special
service area (SSA #3), which is being put forward so that Trapp Lane can be
improved and dedicated to the Village as a public street. He reported that SSA
#3 is intended to improve stormwater drainage to the Territory and to upgrade
Trapp Lane to Village standards. He said the services proposed in SSA #3 are
unique and in addition to the general municipal services provided to the
Village as a whole and will be for the common interests and specific benefit of
the Territory.
Mr. Saunders observed that the purpose of the hearing is to consider borrowing
an amount not to exceed $631,985.00 (125% of the engineer’s estimate of project
costs) which could be accomplished by issuing general obligation bonds, to pay
part of the costs of improvements to SSA #3. He noted that the bonds, if
issued, will be retired by the levy of a direct tax of all taxable property in
SSA #3 over a period of no more than ten years, at a rate not to exceed 5%
annually or the maximum allowable rate.
Mr. Saunders indicated that all persons affected by SSA #3 will be given an
opportunity to file objections to the formation of the special service area
over a 60-day waiting period. He then entered into the record the Certificate
of Publication announcing the public hearing.
There being no further testimony or other evidence, President Tucker closed the
public hearing at 9:50 p.m. She announced that no further action would be taken
at this time, that there is now a 60-day waiting period, and if no petitions or
objections are received from Trapp Lane owners and/or voters during that time,
the Village Attorney will prepare an ordinance formally establishing the
Special Service Area #3.
   b)   Anitfield Request for Extension of M-7-2010 Timeline. President Tucker
announced that the Council prefers to wait until Attorney Janega can be present
to provide legal counsel, and asked for a motion to give an extension of the
timeline until the June 2 nd Council meeting.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to extend the timeline
provided in Ordinance M-7-2010 to June 2. By voice vote, the motion carried.
9)   New Business.
   a)   10 Indian Hill Road / 3 Golf Lane Sanitary Sewer Relocation and
Easement Agreements. Mr. Saunders reported that since their construction, the
properties at 8, 9, 10 and 11 Indian Hill Road and 3 Golf Lane have been served
by a privately installed, undocumented sanitary sewer line, which runs directly
under the garage/coach house at 10 Indian Hill Rd. He noted that in order to
bring the situation into compliance with the Village Code and with Metropolitan
Water Reclamation District (MWRD) requirements, the sanitary sewer must be
reconstructed and maintained by the Village, for public safety reasons.
Mr. Saunders observed that staff has encountered similar situations in the past
with sufficient frequency to have developed a policy whereby the Village funds
the cost of constructing a new, relocated sanitary sewer line and the affected
property owners have paid the cost of constructing new sewer service lines to
connect to the new public sewer. He reviewed several instances in the last two
years where this policy was implemented, noting that both involved homes
located on public roadways. He said in this instance, the properties are on
private streets, and that staff and the property owners have sought a solution
that would satisfy all parties and merit approval from the MWRD.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the resulting plan, which estimates cost of constructing
the relocated sanitary sewer at $61,037, and he indicated that standard utility
easements have been negotiated with the property owners at 10 Indian Hill and 3
Golf Lane. Finally, he noted that the homeowner at 10 Indian Hill has spent
$15, 939.50 on design and engineering work for the sanitary sewer reroute,
$11,649.00 of which would have been incurred by the Village to reroute the
public lines, and he recommended reimbursement from the Village for this
expense.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to award a contract for
construction of public sanitary sewer improvements at 10 Indian Hill and 3 Golf
Lane to SCSI Inc. for an amount not to exceed $61,037. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve the easement
agreements between the Village and the property owners of 10 Indian Hill Rd.
and 3 Golf Lane, to locate a public sanitary sewer beneath these two
properties, substantially in the form attached to the agenda report. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council discussed the $11,649 expense which was recommended to be
reimbursed to the homeowner, and ascertained that an extra amount of $4,290.50
was spent to reroute the sewer line for the neighbors. The majority of the
Trustees felt that since the extra amount was paid for the benefit of the
neighbors, it has public value, and it was decided that the entire amount would
be reimbursed.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to reimburse the owner of 10
Indian Hill Rd. for engineering costs not to exceed $15,939.50, which were
incurred for design and technical engineering services associated with
relocation of the sanitary sewer at 10 Indian Hill Rd. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays:
Trustee Johnson. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported on the proceedings of the
NWMC meeting and announced that the Annual Banquet was scheduled for June 23.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported on the NWMC general session, noting that
public pensions were still a big topic.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney reported that the Library Board is still deciding
on a consultant for their strategic plan process.
      iii)   Trustee Johnson gave a report on the most recent BCDC meeting, and
noted that the deadline for the final Fell Redevelopment plan to be submitted
is in October.
      iv)   Trustee Rintz reported that the Plan Commission had completed its
work on Appendix 6 of the Comprehensive Plan and would be sending a report to
the Council soon.
      v)   Trustee Poor urged residents to attend the Memorial Day ceremony at
the newly renovated Cenotaph.
   c)   Attorney. None
   d)   Manager. None.
11)   Appointments.
President Tucker suggested the appointment of John Iberle as the Plan
Commissions representative to the Business Community Development Commission, to
replace Louise Holland, effective immediately. Trustee Johnson, seconded by
Trustee Greable, moved to so appoint Mr. Iberle. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
President Tucker called for a motion to appoint Liz Rosenthal to the position
of Interim Village Manager, effective June 1. Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee
Johnson, moved to appoint Ms. Rosenthal Interim Village Manager. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Tucker called for a motion to appoint Liz Rosenthal to the position
of Interim Village Clerk, effective June 1. Trustee Johnson, seconded by
Trustee Spinney moved to appoint Ms. Rosenthal Interim Village Clerk. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
12)   Executive Session.
None
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
54 p.m.
         
