MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
Tuesday, June 1, 2010
(Approved: June 15, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
1, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:33 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Public Works
Director Steve Saunders, Director of Community Development Mike D'Onofrio, and
approximately 4 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   June 8,2010, Special Executive Session (There will be NO Study Session
this week). All of the Council members present indicated that they expected to
attend.
   b)   June 10, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   June 15, 2010 Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney,
Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   May 11, 2010 Study Session.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1654 and 1655. Approving Warrant List No. 1654 in
the amount of $995,731.39, and Warrant List No. 1655 in the amount of
$208,505.36.
   c)   Ordinance M-8-2010 – Zoning Variation: 310 ––––Woodland–– – Adoption.
Grants a zoning variation to permit the construction of a detached garage at
310 Woodland Avenue. Introduced at May 18 th Council Meeting.
   d)   Ordinance M-9-2010 – Zoning Variation: 1108 Merrill – Adoption. Grants
two zoning variations to allow construction of an addition to an existing,
legally nonconforming detached garage. Introduced at the May 18 th Council
Meeting
   e)   Resolution R-26-2010 – EPA/NERC Signature Authority – Adoption. Updates
the designation of the Village Manager and Director of Water & Electric as
authorized to sign certain environmental and electric compliance documents on
behalf of the Village.
   f)   Resolution R-27-2010 – Prevailing Wages – Adoption. Establishes
prevailing wage rates for laborers, workers and mechanics engaged in the
construction of public works by, or on behalf of, the Village of Winnetka.
   g)   New Trier Parking Lease. Renews the parking lease with New Trier High
School at the 5% increase provided in the lease.
   h)   Amended Federal Aid Agreement for Pine Street & Eldorado Street Bridge
Construction and Construction Engineering: Final Project Closeout. Authorizes
the Village President to sign the Local Agency Amendment #1 and Supplement #1
for the Phase III engineering and construction and associated engineering
services for the Pine and Eldorado Street bridge improvements
   i)   Change Order to Complete the Streetscape Engineering Contract. Approves
Supplement #1 to the Streetscape engineering contract, increasing the
contractual maximum by $11,374.90
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, inquired about the change order on the Streetscape
engineering contract and also asked about the status of the drainage
questionnaire.
Mr. Saunders replied that he had met with the Village’s drainage consultant,
CCBEL, and had shared Mr. Kates’ questionnaire suggestions, but he has not yet
heard from them.
Mr. Saunders said the Streetscape engineering consultant was concerned that
some of the work on the project might be out of scope, but the decision was
made to work through the process to see how it fell into the contractual
maximum. He noted that the extra work was determined to be out of scope, but
the additional cost was less than the consultant had originally estimated.
President Tucker welcomed two Cub Scouts who were attending the meeting as a
part of their merit badge requirements.
8)   Old Business.
   a)   Anitfield Request for Extension of M-7-2010 Timeline. Attorney Janega
reported that Mr. Sayegh had called her late last week and indicated he had not
been successful in getting the indemnifications required for the special use
and, when he inquired about what to do next, that she advised him to consult
his attorney wh. Ms. Janega related that Mr. Sayegh said he did not see any
point in pursuing a further extension, that she requested written confirmation
of his intent to withdraw the request, and that she had not received any
further communications from him. She suggested that his absence is indicative
of his intent not to proceed.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson moved to
deny Anitfield’s request for an extension of the M-7-2010 timeline. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz,
Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9)   New Business.
None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. None
   b)   Trustees. No reports
