June 15, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
June 15, 2010
(Approved: July 6, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June 15, 2010,
at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of
Community Development Mike D’Onofrio, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Deputy Fire Chief
Steve Laureys, Police Chief Joe DeLopez, and approximately 40 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   June 22, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   June 29, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   July 6, 2010, Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   d)   July 7, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to approve the Agenda. By roll
call vote. the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz,
Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   May 18, 2010, Regular Meeting.
      ii)   June 1, 2010, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1657 and 1658. Approving Warrant List No. 1657 in
the amount of $754,501.40, and Warrant List No. 1658 in the amount of
$468,585.22.
   c)   Valve Bids. Authorizes Village Manager to issue a purchase order to
Henry Pratt Company in the amount of $128,251 for the purchase of valves and
operators used in the water treatment process.
   d)   Resolution R-28-2010: Amending the 2008 License Agreement with New
Cingular Wireless – Adoption. Approves the First Amendment to the 2008 Cellular
Antenna License Agreement with New Cingular Wireless, PCS.
   e)   Chamber Request for Sidewalk Sale. Approval for the Winnetka Chamber of
Commerce to use Village sidewalks during the annual sidewalk sale on July 16 &
17.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance M-10-2010 – Zoning Variation: ––––879 Ash Street–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a side yard setback
variation to permit a window well to encroach into the side yard setback, as
part of a basement remodeling project to an existing single family home. He
indicated that the ZBA had unanimously approved the request, and added that the
applicant is asking the Council to waive introduction and approve the ordinance
at this time.
Attorney Janega noted that the Village Code provides for waiver of introduction
when an emergency exists or when the procedures do not serve the best interests
of the Village. After a short discussion, the Council agreed that the variance
was small and that the interests of the Village would be served by waiving
introduction.
After the project architect, Chip Hackley, briefly described the project,
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance M-10-2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Ordinance M-10-2010,
granting a variation from the minimum side yard setback requirement. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance M-11-2010 – Zoning Variation: ––––606 Green Bay Road–– –
Introduction. Trustee Greable recused himself from this matter, due to the
recent purchase of a condominium at the Winnetka Mews, which is directly north
of the Subject Property.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed the request for variations from impermeable lot coverage
restrictions and front yard setback requirements for Grand Foods, to allow
construction of an addition that would increase impermeable surface by 2,713
square feet, and also result in front yard setbacks that are 5.26 feet above
the required maximum along Pine Street and 9.72 feet above the required maximum
on the Green Bay frontage. He said the proposed addition would be used to
accommodate expanded floral and produce departments.
Mr. D’Onofrio explained that the setback requirement is different in the
commercial districts, as the intent is to preserve the building face and to
prevent strip malls or similar uses. He reviewed the process before the Design
Review Board, which included three meetings, after which the DRB voted to
recommend approval with the following conditions: (i) 4-inch caliper trees in
the Green Bay Rd. parkway; (ii) a planting strip on the west side of Green Bay
Rd.; (iii) further study of the optimum sidewalk size at Pine and Green Bay;
(iv) a preference for the garbage enclosure to be moved to the west into the
Village parking lot, so larger delivery trucks can enter off Green Bay Road
instead of Pine Street.
Mr. D’Onofrio said the plans were reviewed by Village Engineer Steve Saunders
in light of the DRB recommendations. He reviewed Mr. Saunders’ memo indicating
that he has no significant concerns about traffic or pedestrian safety
resulting from of approval of the variations.
Mr. D’Onofrio referred to a letter in the agenda materials from one of the
store’s owners listing steps Grand Foods will take in order to reduce not only
the impact of the proposed addition, but also the current operations of the
store. The operational changes include: (i) replacing the 6-foot fence with an
8-foot fence on the west property line; (ii) working with Staff to move the
existing garbage container to the Village parking lot to the west of the
current location; (iii) rerouting the early morning delivery trucks off Pine
Street; (iv) move the ice machine next to the front entrance of the store; (v)
installation of a thermostat on the louvers in the compressor room to reduce
compressor noise.
Mr. D’Onofrio answered questions from the Council, after which the project
architect, Dave Gillespie of Gillespie Design Group, gave a presentation
showing the proposed expansion. He said grocery stores have evolved over the
years as consumer trends have changed, and that a larger produce department is
necessary for the store to remain a viable destination spot.
The Council expressed support for Grand Foods, but several Trustees had
concerns about the amount of impermeable surface and also the long unbroken
expanse of brick wall along Pine Street, and encouraged the applicants to come
up with creative solutions to these issues.
Attorney Janega commented that moving the garbage dumpsters onto Village
property can be accomplished, and she recalled that a similar arrangement had
been created for Panera in Hubbard Woods when they installed a handicap
accessible ramp. Mr. Saunders said moving the dumpsters would result in the
loss of one parking space and the removal of a sick tree, and would serve the
needs of the applicant as well as the Village.
The audience was invited to comment, starting with Al Zembler, 640 Winnetka
Mews and president of the Winnetka Mews Condo Association. Mr. Zembler said he
appreciated Grand Foods’ working with residents of the Mews.
Ted Reed, 640 Winnetka Mews, read a statement favoring the expansion subject to
the condition that the operational changes mentioned above become part of the
ordinance.
Bill Gallagher, 640 Winnetka Mews, said Mr. Zembler speaks for the Mews, as he
is president of the condo association and added that he thought most of the
residents are very happy to have such a good neighbor in Grand Foods.
Jill Wendell, 865 Pine, asked if the sidewalk at Green Bay and Pine could be
rounded to make it safer for small children; suggested a sign to remind the
refuse truck drivers to keep noise to a minimum; and asked the Village to paint
a white stripe at the sidewalk at the parking lot exit onto Pine St. so cars
would know to stop before proceeding onto the street.
Attorney Janega observed that the Drafter’s Note she had inserted into the
proposed ordinance was put there to leave room for conditions that came out of
the DRB suggestions. She noted that the DRB acts in an advisory role, and many
of their suggestions did not pertain to the application for zoning variations.
She indicated that many of the conditions Grand Foods has agreed to can be
incorporated into the ordinance as conditions, including: (i) replacing the
fence from the south end of the Village lot to Pine St.; (ii) routing of
delivery trucks, which will be worked out with Staff; and (iii) rounding the
corner of the sidewalk at Pine and Green Bay. She said the matter of moving the
dumpster to the southeast corner of the Village parking lot could be treated as
a separate item as it requires a license agreement, and would be worked out
between Staff and Grand Foods.
Attorney Janega noted that concerns about the north wall of the store and
impermeable surface could be incorporated into conditions once the applicant
has explored some options, and she added that the DRB could be called upon to
comment on any proposed treatment of the brick wall.
Mr. Saunders indicated that the request for pavement striping and a larger
corner on Pine Street are addressed in his agenda report.
Attorney Janega assured the Council that the conditions could be drafted so the
work is done to the approval of the Village Engineer, and that engineering
standards would provide enough guidance as the expansion work progresses. She
said she would work with Mr. D’Onofrio and Mr. Saunders to make sure all the
elements are addressed.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to introduce Ordinance M-11-
2010. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   c)   Ordinance M-12-2010 – Zoning Variation: ––––903 Private Road–– –
Introduction. Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for variations from the
garages prohibitions in Section 17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance to allow
three front-facing garage doors to face towards Ravine Drive on a new, single
family home currently under construction. He noted that the approved plans call
for a three-car garage with two doors facing towards Private Road and one
facing Ravine Drive.
Mr. D’Onofrio observed that shortly after the permit was issued the applicants
decided to change the garage configuration and they filed an appeal of the
Zoning Administrator’s determination that the Subject Property is a corner lot.
He said the appeal was heard by the ZBA, which upheld the decision that the
parcel is a corner lot, and the applicant subsequently filed a request for
variations. He noted that the footprint of the home will stay the same, with
all three garage doors facing Ravine Road, and the ZBA voted 4-3 to recommend
approval of the request.
Trustee Poor pointed out that approving the request would result in less
impervious surface as only one driveway would be needed, and would also save a
tree.
The applicant, Troy Muniz, said the lot is uniquely situated between the end of
a driveway that extends off Private Road and a pathway to Ravine Drive. He
maintained that the spirit of the prohibition on front-facing garage doors was
to address large garages on front elevations of properties, and that the
requested variation in this case would allow the garage doors to face the most
discreet location on the side of the home.
Victoria Birov, the applicants’ attorney, and Steve Aisen, the applicants’
contractor, discussed the request with the Council, and gave the chronology of
the process, which started with the submission of conforming plans before the
request for variations was made mid-way through construction.
Sarah Bennett, 887 Private Road, asked the Council to grant the variations to
reduce driveway space, save the tree and to minimize her view of the garage.
The matter was discussed by the Council, and the Trustees unanimously felt that
granting the variations would result in a better house and an improved view for
the neighbors; however, several Trustees were concerned with the fact that the
house was well under construction before the application for variations was
made.
Trustee Spinney, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to introduce Ordinance M-12-
2010. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Marco Roma Request. Chief DeLopez said in March, 2010, it was brought
to his attention that Marco Roma may not meet the Village Code’s definition of
a restaurant under the Liquor Ordinance. He reviewed Marco Roma’s original
liquor license application, which stated that a hostess would be on the
premises. He said he had met with Mr. Mr. D’Ascenzi to discuss the options
available to allow him to keep his liquor license.
Joseph Urso, a Marco Roma employee, said the restaurant has not violated any
state laws, and the decision to apply for a liquor license stemmed from
customer requests.
Attorney Janega said the Council has three options: (i) amend the Code’s
definition of a full-service restaurant; (ii) create a separate license
category for this type of restaurant; or (iii) tighten the definition of full-
service restaurant so there is no question as to what that is. She said if the
Council wants to alter the concept of what qualifies as a full-service
restaurant she would need to meet with the police chief to have a closer
examination of where the lines are drawn, and she asked the Council for
direction on how it wishes to treat the issue.
Trustee Rintz said the liquor standards are written to fit the Village and are
reflective of the community, and that Marco Roma should comply with the license
requirements or stop serving liquor.
President Tucker agreed, and observed that as Liquor Commissioner it was her
job to make sure the liquor laws are obeyed. The Trustees agreed with President
Tucker that the restaurant needs to comply with the current liquor laws by July
1 st .
   b)   Susan Kroger Fire Code Appeal. Richard Kates, attorney for the
petitioner, distributed exhibits and photos to the Council, and he disputed the
Winnetka Fire Chief’s interpretation of the Building Code. After reviewing the
Fire Safety and Life Safety Codes, he took issue with the Fire Chief’s
interpretation of the word “building,” posited that the site is grandfathered
in, and asked why the Village can’t simply be satisfied with the automatic
alarm system.
Chief Smith said each space in a multi-use structure is independently
evaluated, and the requirements are not applied capriciously or arbitrarily. He
said he stood by his opinion and his experience as a fire official that there
is a hazard that needs to be remedied in this instance. He noted that many
people sleep through fire alarms, especially children, and he remarked that the
building was constructed in 1926 and is not equipped with modern fire
suppression features.
Deputy Chief Laureys observed that buildings are not viewed from a zoning
perspective by fire departments, and he added that people have a 70% better
chance of survival when there is a sprinkler system in place. He pointed out
that there is no shortage of empty rental space in the Village, and questioned
the necessity of a residential use being converted to a business.
Attorney Janega asked Deputy Chief Laureys if he was certified by the State
Fire Marshall and if he took continuing education courses. Deputy Chief Laureys
replied in the affirmative. Deputy Chief Laureys also confirmed that the
certifications he earns are based on a standardized interpretation of the
building codes.
Attorney Janega then confirmed with Chief Smith that the record of the appeal
is contained in pp. 213-235 of the agenda packet, which includes the Chief’s
agenda report with attachments, and the petition for appeal and four exhibits.
Trustee Poor said it would be helpful to have a memo from Mr. Kates summarizing
his position and that the Council should take the matter under advisement while
it examines the facts.
Attorney Janega stated that the whole issue would become moot if the owner of
the building, Richard Busscher, installs a sprinkler system. She suggested that
the Village Prosecutor, Barb Mangler, draft a response brief for Chief Smith
and that the parties’ briefs both be filed on Friday, June 25, with no
responses. She indicated that she would prepare a memo with her findings for
Council consideration at the July 6 th Council Meeting.
   c)   Capital Budget Status Report. Mr. McKee said the report is for the
Council’s information and doesn’t require a discussion. He invited anyone with
questions to contact him.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker read from a letter promoting the
Library District’s Summer Reading Program; announced that the NWMC Banquet is
coming up next week; and she invited the audience to read the public pension
article in the last issue of the Winnetka Talk.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Johnson announced the upcoming BCDC meeting.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney reported that she attended the RED Center Board of
Directors meeting, and the Library’s Finance Committee meeting, where the
budget was discussed.
      iii)   Trustee Poor announced that the EFC’s web page is up and running.
   c)   Attorney. No Report.
   d)   Manager. Manager Rosenthal reported that the Green Bay Road resurfacing
project was scheduled to commence June 21 st and is expected to be completed by
Labor Day. She said the road will be open to traffic, albeit with periodic lane
closures throughout, and the Village is working with the contractor to mitigate
problems for the Sidewalk Sale in Hubbard Woods. She also announced that since
the $4,640 CBBEL proposal to evaluate the stormwater drainage surveys for the
rest of the Village is well below Staff’s estimates and within Staff’s
purchasing authority, the survey will be done immediately, since the survey
must be completed before the overall drainage strategy endorsed by the Council
at its May 4 th meeting can be implemented.
11)   Appointments.
President Tucker suggested the appointment of Scott Madlener to the
Environmental & Forestry Commission effective immediately, to complete the term
of David Abell, who is retiring from the Commission. Trustee Poor, seconded by
Trustee Johnson, moved to so appoint Mr. Madlener. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
46 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
July 6, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
July 6, 2010
(Approved: July 20, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July 6, 2010,
at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, and Jennifer Spinney.
Absent: Trustee Rintz. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Ed McKee, Steve Saunders, Joe DeLopez, Mike
D'Onofrio and approximately 27 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   July 7, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   July 13, 2010, Study Session. Cancelled.
   c)   July 20, 2010, Regular Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that Item 5(l), the Driver Training Instructor
Agreement with NIPSTA, had been removed from the agenda. Trustee Johnson
requested that item 5(e), Commercial District Infrastructure Repairs, be
removed from the consent agenda. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian,
moved to approve the Agenda as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Poor, Pedian, Johnson, Spinney, Greable. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Rintz.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   June 15, 2010, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1659 and 1660. Approves Warrant List No. 1659 in the
amount of $1,941,702.19 and Warrant List No. 1660 in the amount of $592,216.41.
   c)   Underground Fuel Line Cleaning and Closure Bids. Authorizes purchase
