Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian moved to approve the items on the
consent agenda, as amended, by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Pedian, Johnson, Spinney, Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance M-11-2010 – Zoning Variation: ––––606 Green Bay Road–– –
Adoption. Trustee Greable recused himself from this matter, due to the recent
purchase of a condominium at the Winnetka Mews, which is directly north of the
Subject Property.
Ms. Janega reviewed the proposed amendments to the ordinance which was
introduced at the June 15 th Council meeting. The ordinance would grant
variations from the zoning requirements subject to certain conditions, listed
in Section 3 of the ordinance, to allow an increase in impermeable surface of
2,713 square feet, and also result in front yard setbacks that are 5.26 feet
above the required maximum along Pine Street and 9.72 feet above the required
maximum on the Green Bay Road frontage for an addition that would expand the
floral and produce departments.
Public Works Director Steve Saunders reported that discussions had begun with
the architect to find alternative materials for the impermeable surface area.
At Trustee Poor’s request, he will report back to the Council when final
recommendations have been developed.
Mr. Dave Gillespie, the applicant’s architect, will submit the revised plan to
the Design Review Board and confirmed that he is speaking with Village staff to
reduce impermeable surface and find alternative materials, including the
walkways.
Jill Wendall, 865 Pine Street asked for additional signage to be erected soon
for the safety of her child and had suggestions for landscaping.
President Tucker instructed staff to begin working on the signage and to notify
Mrs. Wendall when the DRB discusses the north elevation of the addition.
Mr. Ted Reed thanked the staff, the members of the Zoning Board and Design
Review Board, and the property owners for meeting with the neighbors and
incorporating many of their suggestions.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Poor moved to amend Ordinance M-11-2010 in
accordance with the Village Attorney's recommendations. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Ordinance M-11-2010 as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Poor, Pedian, Johnson and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Greable
(recused) and Rintz.
   b)   Ordinance M-12-2010 – Zoning Variation: ––––903 Private Road–– –
Adoption. Village Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments to Ordinance M-12-
2010, introduced at the Council’s June 15 th meeting, which would grant
variations subject to certain conditions from the garage door limitations of
Section 17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance for a new, single family home
currently under construction to have a three-car, attached garage with all
three doors facing Ravine Drive.
Trustee Greable requested that the condition requiring a landscape plan for the
maintenance of fencing and installation of shrubbery be subject to review by
the Community Development Department prior to the issuance of a certificate of
occupancy.
Trustee Spinney moved to amend the ordinance in accordance with the Village
Attorney’s recommendations and to include additional language requiring the
review of the landscape plan prior to issuance of the certificate of occupancy.
Trustee Greable seconded, and by voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adopt Ordinance M-12-
2010 as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor,
Spinney, Johnson, Greable. Nays: Trustee Pedian. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
None.
8)   Old Business.
   a)   Policy Direction - Susan Kroeger Fire Code Appeal. At its June 15 th
Council meeting, the Council requested that the Village Prosecutor and Ms.
Kroeger’s attorney file briefs stating their positions in regard to Ms.
Kroeger’s appeal of the Fire Chief’s decision that requires the installation of
a sprinkler system in a leased apartment space currently being used as an
office.
Village Attorney Janega advised that the Council’s options under the Village
Code are to uphold, reverse or modify the decision of the Fire Chief and that
it is appropriate to conduct the discussion in Executive Session, if desired.
President Tucker stated that having received the briefs submitted and heard
extensive argument at the June 15 th Council meeting, she would like to discuss
the matter as a group without further comment by the parties.
Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney and Greable agreed that as a quasi-judicial
matter, the discussion should be held in executive session.
Ms. Janega advised that the next court date is scheduled before the Council
next meets and that a continuance would be requested if necessary.
Trustee Pedian moved to take the matter under advisement and issue a ruling at
the next meeting, seconded by Trustee Poor. By voice vote, the motion carried
unanimously.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Chestnut Street Resurfacing Project. Director of Public Works, Steve
Saunders, reported that the capital improvements plan for this fiscal year
included funding for rehabilitation of streets and the main project under
consideration was the reconstruction of Chestnut between Willow and Hill, but
also Alles Road to the west of Chestnut and Birch. He stated that the project
includes reconstruction, stormwater detention, sewer, and related landscaping
repairs, and that bids were very competitive with the low bid of $916,900
nearly $300,000 below the Village’s estimate.
Mr. Saunders reported that the Village's standard since 1985 has been to
install curb and gutter upon reconstruction and is in keeping with the Winnetka
2020 Comprehensive Plan. However, after a number of discussions with Chestnut
Street residents who expressed a strong interest in retaining the unique nature
of the street that does not currently have curb and gutter, Mr. Saunders and
his staff modified the original design by proposing a narrower roadway and a
lower profile curb which would not compromise public safety or the integrity of
the roadway. Staff’s recommendation is to install the modified curb and gutter
with a narrower pavement section, although a number of residents continue to
request the elimination of curbs. Mr. Saunders detailed the merits of curb and
gutter construction as it relates to water run off, traffic safety, road
integrity and maintenance. He described other options illustrated in his agenda
report, including recreating the road as it is now with shoulders and no
ditches. He said this would require additional drains in the parkways to
collect water, gravel shoulders to prevent driving off the sides of the road,
and increased maintenance due to heavy rainstorms, plowing and street cleaning
and that this option would not provide longevity or structure at the edges of
the pavement or for containment of water. In response to a question from
Trustee Poor, he explained that reconstruction of a road is typically every 50
years or so, but may be sooner as evidenced at Tower and Forestway which was
done just 10 years ago and is already showing wear.
Trustee Pedian confirmed with Mr. Saunders and Ms. Janega that a special
assessment of the homeowners for the maintenance of the road in the second
option would not be appropriate in this instance because a special service area
is designed for municipal services that are not provided for the general public
and to retire debt, not for ongoing maintenance.
Trustee Greable commented that this project would be the last major street in
the Village to install curb and gutters and confirmed with Mr. Saunders that
when the rest of the streets were done, the standard they applied the roadway
standards that were in the comprehensive plan prior to Winnetka 2020. He also
confirmed that Mr. Saunders had attempted to work with the neighbors and the
result of that is his recommendation as Village engineer to narrow the road by
one foot and reduce the curb profile. Trustee Greable also raised the
possibility of installing new sidewalks along the entire street.
Todd Kroll, 269 Chestnut, presented a petition with the signatures of 14 other
residents who are requesting alternatives to the proposed plan and a continuing
dialogue.
Monica Carroll, 190 Chestnut spoke in favor of the road’s reconstruction and
reported the support of Rob and Lynn Martin, who had also written a letter to
the Council.
Neil Stearns, 260 Chestnut, also spoke in favor of the plan and urged that the
Village standards be imposed on public streets.
William Hickey, 180 Chestnut, also expressed support for the Village plan for
public roads, but urged the Council to spend funds prudently.
TC Wooster, 263 Chestnut, asked whether the plan improves the water conditions.
Mr. Saunders replied that the plan is an improvement for stormwater runoff, but
will not solve the drainage problems.
The Council commented on the necessity of improving the condition of the road,
the value of dialogue between the residents and the Village engineer, safety
issues and sidewalk repair. The consensus among the Council was generally in
support of the staff’s recommendations, but also to allow residents another
opportunity to meet with the Village staff to discuss the proposal, the
installation of sidewalks and to hear from those who have not yet expressed
their opinions. The Council also recognized the need to come to a decision soon
and continued the matter to the July 20 th meeting for decision.
   b)   Village Hall Restoration & Renovation – Bid Results. Public Works
Director Saunders summarized the facility needs analysis completed in January
for the restoration of Village Hall and reported that the bids received for the
project cost were much higher than expected. After meeting with the three low
bidders and receiving input from other bidders, he concluded that the bids
could be structured more advantageously. The Technical Committee then met to
talk through the issues associated with the bids and concluded that because
there was a lack of competition for the bids for roofing, masonry and windows,
these should be re-bid to a broader number of contractors. Mr. Saunders
suggested redesigning the roofing system c to save money, restoring the windows
in place instead of completely removing them and separating the roof and the
HVAC from the bidding package. He said that the Council could award the project
to the lowest bidder or renegotiate with the lowest bidder, but the lowest bid
does not appear to be a fair market price and the lowest bidder is also the
masonry bidder, so it seems unrealistic to expect a better price. His
recommendation was to reject the bids received in May and re-bid the project
separately. He thought the savings could possibly be $600,000, but that there
was no way to be certain of the total cost reduction.
Trustee Pedian commented that she felt it best to unbundle the package and re-
bid the most extensive projects to increase competition, reduce cost and get
the best quality and price for the rest of the work. Trustees Spinney, Johnson,
Poor and Greable concurred.
Trustee Johnson moved to reject all bids received, seconded by Trustee Spinney.
By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Pedian, Johnson,
Spinney and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
   c)   Commercial District Infrastructure Repairs. Public Works Director
Saunders reported that the 2010 budget included $750,000 for necessary repairs
of sidewalks, curbs and streets not completed due to the deferment of the
Streetscape Plan. Staff has identified areas in need of repair in the east and
west Elm and Hubbard Woods business districts for a total estimated cost of
$415,000. Mr. Saunders said repairs to sidewalks can be added to the existing
sidewalk contract and bids will be solicited for resurfacing projects and brick
paver repairs. He asked the Council to authorize the sidewalk and tree grate
repairs as an addition to the sidewalk and curb replacement contract awarded to
Schroeder & Schroeder, Inc. in April.
Trustee Greable moved to authorize staff to solicit bids as recommended,
seconded by Trustee Johnson. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Poor moved to authorize the expenditure of up to $30,000 on sidewalk
and tree grate repair and $20,000 on spot curb repairs as an addition to the
sidewalk and curb replacement contract awarded to Schroeder & Schroeder, Inc.
on April 6, 2010. The motion was seconded by Trustee Spinney and by roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Pedian, Johnson, Spinney,
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker congratulated the 4 th of July
organizers on a successful event and acknowledged the work of the Public Works
Department. She announced that Winnetka Parks is hosting a party on the pier at
Tower Road beach with proceeds going to the foundation to enhance park district
resources.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported that he and President Tucker had attended
the NWMC banquet on June 23 rd when new officers were sworn in.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney reported she had attended the Library board meeting
and that the Strategic Planning Committee has had to begin the process of
selecting a strategic planner again.
      iii)   Trustee Johnson announced that the BCDC would be meeting on
Thursday at 7:15 a.m.
      iv)   Trustee Pedian announced that she and Trustee Greable are meeting
with the Village treasurer to study employee compensation for the purpose of
assuring fair and competitive staff compensation and benefits.
   c)   Attorney. No report.
   d)   Manager. No report.
11)   Appointments.
None
12)   Trustee Pedian moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss
deliberations of quasi-adjudicative bodies, setting the price for the lease of
Village property and pending and probable litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)
(4), 2(c)(6) and 2(c)(11) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Rintz. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 10:16 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at ________ p.m. Present: President
