MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
July 20, 2010
(Approved: August 17, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
20, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
Community Development Director Mike D'Onofrio, Assistant Community Development
Director Brian Norkus, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Deputy Fire Chief Steve Laureys,
and approximately 27 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   July 21, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   July 22, 2010, Special Executive Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   August 2, 2010, Special Executive Session. President Tucker announced
that this meeting would be rescheduled.
   d)   August 3, 2010, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that Resolution No. R-29-2010, approving Isabel’s
Subdivision, had been moved from the regular agenda to the consent agenda.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the Agenda as
amended. By roll call vote. the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson,
Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   July 6, 2010, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1661 and 1662. Approving Warrant List No. 1661 in
the amount of $808,355.62, and Warrant List No. 1662 in the amount of
$227,693.06.
   c)   Rock Salt Purchase Bid. Approves the purchase of rock salt at $67.39/
ton from Cargill Salt, through the State of Illinois Cooperative purchasing
program.
   d)   MFT Street Resurfacing Bid Awards.
   e)   Isabel’s Subdivision (1236 Asbury): Resolution No. R-29-2010 –
Adoption.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and
Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-13-2010: Trapp Lane SSA – Introduction & Adoption. Mr.
Saunders gave a review of the process leading up to the proposed establishment
of the Trapp Lane Special Service Area (SSA), which is being created to fund
the homeowners’ portion of the costs associated with dedicating Trapp Lane to
the Village. He indicated that the final step in establishing the SSA is the
adoption of Ordinance M-13-2010.
Mr. Saunders said since the engineering for the project has been approved,
staff recommends waiving introduction of the ordinance and proceeding to
adoption at this time.
After a short discussion, Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to
waive introduction of Ordinance M-13-2010. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Ordinance M-13-2010,
establishing the Trapp Lane SSA. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Anthony DeFalco, 738 Lincoln, said his car had been damaged last summer during
a resurfacing project on Pine Street. He said neither the Village’s contractor
nor its insurance company would assume responsibility for the damage to his
car, and he asked the Council if they could provide any help. President Tucker
explained that the Village does not discuss pending claims in public session
and added that, if the Village changes its position on his claim, he would be
notified in writing.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, asked if the Village has considered applying for a
grant in connection with the Village Hall renovation. He also noted that Metra
is planning to make changes in its train schedules that will affect Winnetkans
and he asked the Council to take action.
Sarah Fay, 811 Elm, introduced herself to the Council as the new editor of the
Winnetka/Glencoe Patch, an online news and business directory affiliated with
AOL.
Beth Ann Papoutsis, 720 Prospect, said she spoke for several neighbors who are
concerned about the replacement of vintage homes in Winnetka. She distributed a
letter summarizing their concerns, along with a proposed ordinance that she
said would place requirements on homes purchased by developers.
Jim Nowacki, 708 Prospect, said he favors Ms. Papoutsis’ proposed ordinance
because of his concerns that the quality and diversity of architecture in the
Village is declining.
8)   Old Business.
   a)   Chestnut Street Resurfacing Project. Mr. Saunders reported that, as
directed by the Council, staff had met with 11 Chestnut Street residents on
July 14 th to hear their concerns about the design of the Chestnut Street
Reconstruction Project, especially the installation of curbs and gutters and
the removal of parking bays. He reviewed the results of the meeting, including
steps staff will take to mitigate such issues as traffic enforcement and
signage. He said he recommends awarding the bid for the reconstruction project,
and asked for the Council’s direction regarding: (i) the design and
installation of curbs and gutters; (ii) whether to retain or remove the parking
bays; and (iii) how to proceed with the expansion of the sidewalk. He noted
that the project’s timeline allows for continuing discussion on the sidewalk,
in keeping with the Sidewalk Guidelines, and that if the bids are approved
there will still be time to resolve the issue.
Todd Kroll, 269 Chestnut, said that installing curbs and gutters will change
the aesthetic of the street, questioned the plans for stormwater drainage, and
expressed concerns about increased traffic and the project cost, but added that
he favored the sidewalk expansion.
Bill Hickey, 180 Chestnut, said there are neighbors who support the plan, as
the road is in desperate need of reconstruction. He added that Chestnut Street
is a public road, so the best interests of the community need to be considered.
Lynn Martin, 193 Chestnut, urged the Council to award the contract and said she
agreed with Mr. Hickey.
Mr. Saunders reported that the bids had come in 25% below estimates, so now is
the time to award the contract. He provided more detail about the stormwater
drainage improvements, and said there would be some benefit for Alles and
Birch, which have persistent flooding issues.
The Council then discussed the project at length. The Trustees expressed
unanimous support for proceeding with the project with curbs and gutters, as
recommended by Mr. Saunders, and also supported eliminating the parking bays.
They expressed sensitivity to the feelings expressed by the residents about the
aesthetics of the project and agreed that sidewalks could be discussed further.
President Tucker asked Mr. Saunders to report back to the Council about the
results of the sidewalk discussions.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to award Bid 10-13 to Lenny
Hoffman Excavating in the amount of $916,900. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ruling: Susan Kroeger Fire Code Appeal. Attorney Janega reported that
she had received a letter from Ms. Kroeger’s attorney hours before the Council
meeting and recommended continuing the matter until the next Council Meeting,
so she could discuss it with the Council in Executive Session, as there was a
concern about probable litigation.
The Council agreed with Attorney Janega, and President Tucker continued the
matter until the August 3 rd Council Meeting.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Glenn Weaver Fire Code Appeal. Glenn Weaver, 574 Lincoln, said he was
asking for an immediate exception from the sprinkler requirements for his
building and urged that the sprinkler provisions be removed from the Fire Code.
He cited the cost of installing a system and asserted that the regulation was
keeping him from leasing the space to a retail merchant that had occupied the
space at some time in the past. He reviewed his correspondence and distributed
a survey of available commercial spaces.
Chief Smith said Mr. Weaver is asking for a variance from provisions that have
been fairly and evenly applied since the requirements were added to the Village
Code in the 1970’s. He noted that Mr. Weaver’s fire suppression system is an
old one that is out of date, and the Fire Code requirements were triggered
because Mr. weaver contemplated a change of use in his building.
After a lengthy Council discussion, President Tucker indicated that the Council
would take the matter under advisement so as to have time to review Mr.
Weaver’s survey.
   b)   Landmark Preservation Commission Study Request. Louise Holland, Chair
of the Landmark Preservation Commission, requested that the Council consider a
proposal to compile information on each building in the Village. The
information, which would be limited to: i) date of construction; ii) type of
building; iii) style of building; iv) architect; v) builder; vi) whether or not
the building has been torn down; and vii) a photograph, would be compiled at no
cost to the Village by student interns in the Public History program at Loyola
University Chicago. She added that the primary source of information will be
the Village’s building files, and that the information would be compiled into a
data base that could be used by a wide array of users, including potential
homebuyers, the Historical Society, the BCDC, and the Plan Commission.
Ms. Holland answered questions from the Council, after which the Trustees
discussed the request. Attorney Janega explained that the powers and duties of
the Landmark Preservation Commission, as provided in the Village Code, include
such projects.
Margaret Herman, 832 Bryant, and John Jansson, 199 Church, both expressed
support for the project.
The Council reached unanimous agreement that the LPC should proceed with the
project as proposed.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported on resident inquiries
about police solicitations and announced that the Winnetka Police Department
does not solicit for funds and that anyone receiving a call for money on behalf
of the Winnetka Police Department should call the police non-emergency number.
She also announced that the library would be celebrating its 100 th birthday on
July 28 th .
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported on the Historical Society and Chamber
meetings.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney reported on the Library Board meeting.
      iii)   Trustee Johnson reported on the BCDC meeting.
      iv)   Trustee Pedian asked residents to please complete the drainage
survey that had been sent, in order to help the Village with its stormwater
management study.
      v)   Trustee Poor reported on the EFC meeting.
   c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that the dismissal of the suit
challenging the Village’s special events ordinance, Brandt v. Winnetka, was
affirmed on appeal.
   d)   Manager. Interim Manager Rosenthal reported that the construction
strike has been settled and crews will return to Winnetka for directional
boring and road construction work.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters and Appointments to Public Office, Council Deliberations as a Quasi-
Adjudicative Body and Pending and Probable Litigation, pursuant to Sections 2
(c)(1), 2(c)(3), 2(c)(4) and 2(c)(11) respectively, of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 10:52 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:16 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
17 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
August 3, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
August 3, 2010
(Approved: August 17, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 3, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, and approximately
seven persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   August 10, 2010 Study Session. President Tucker announced that this
meeting is cancelled, and noted that a special executive session would be
called for August 16 th  . All of the Council members present expected to
attend the special executive session.
   b)   August 17, 2010. All of the Council members present indicated that they
expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker noted that Item 5(a), the Grand Foods Parking Lot License
Agreement, had been removed from the Consent Agenda to allow Trustee Greable to
recuse himself from voting on this item. Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda as amended. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes. None.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1663 and 1664. Approving Warrant List No. 1663 in
the amount of $1,334,569.00 and Warrant List No. 1664 in the amount of
$513,275.91.
   c)   Bid # 10-021 – 2010 Commercial District Resurfacing Program. Awards the
2010 commercial district resurfacing program to J.A. Johnson Paving Co., in the
amount of $291,428.67.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Village Auditor: Comprehensive Annual Financial Report (CAFR).
Finance Director Ed McKee introduced the Village’s auditor, Ron Amen of
Lauterbach & Amen, to present the annual financial report, and noted that the
auditor serves in an important role as a check and balance on the Village’s
administrative staff.
Mr. Amen thanked Mr. McKee and Assistant Finance Director Hanna Sullivan for
their assistance and professionalism, and announced that the Village has again
received the prestigious Government Finance Officers Association (GFOA)
Certificate of Achievement Award. He reviewed the CAFR, noting that the
Village’s auditor has issued an unqualified opinion, which is the highest level
of opinion that can be issued, and described the year-end fund balance of just
over $26 million as very healthy. He noted that the auditor is retained by the
Council and stressed his availability to the members of the Council. Mr. Amen
then answered questions from the Trustees.
Trustee Greable noted that the CAFR shows the Village’s finances are excellent,
that the cash balances are healthy, and that the Village’s careful stewardship
has allowed the Village to continue to provide all services to the community,
without needing to implement cuts as other communities have.
Trustee Spinney expressed thanks to retired Village Manager Doug Williams for
his stewardship and for keeping the Village’s credit rating Triple A. Other
Council members concurred.
Mr. Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, asked about post-employment fund liabilities for
health care and also about how the Prairie State overruns reported by the
Chicago Tribune would affect the Village.
In response, Mr. McKee explained how retiree health care costs are accounted
for, noting the difference between direct and indirect costs.
Responding to the Prairie State question, Attorney Janega corrected some of the
statements in the Chicago Tribune article. She stressed that there are no
Winnetka tax dollars at risk and that the cost of Prairie State would be only
one of the factors in the Village’s power purchase costs. She explained that
the Village purchases all of its power from the IMEA and that the power from
Prairie State will be a small portion of IMEA’s power portfolio, so the
reported cost overruns will have less of an impact on Winnetka’s electric
rates.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution R-30-2010: Grand Food Parking Lot License Agreement –
Adoption. Trustee Greable recused himself from this matter, as he is an
adjacent neighbor of Grand Foods.
Attorney Janega reviewed the provisions of the license agreement, which is a
condition of the ordinance granting zoning variations to Grand Foods in order
to construct an addition.
There being no questions, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to
adopt Resolution R-30-2010 authorizing the execution of a license agreement
between the Village and Grand Foods substantially in the form attached. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. Abstain: Trustee Greable.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
Laura Michaels, 22 nd Century Media, introduced herself to the Council as the
editor of the Winnetka Current, a new weekly newspaper that will launch on
September 8 th and would be delivered free to every home and business in the
60093 ZIP code.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, said he was concerned about the changes that Metra
is planning to make to the morning train schedule, asked what the Village is
doing to get the changes reversed, and he suggested contacting state
legislators.
Interim Manager Rosenthal said she has been in contact with Metra to convey
resident concern and she encouraged residents to give their comments to Metra
at one of several community meetings.
Mr. Kates echoed Mr. Liss’ call for the Village to take action.
9)   Old Business.
   a)   Ruling: Susan Kroeger Fire Code Appeal. President Tucker announced that
the record on this matter is closed. She suggested that the Council adjourn for
a brief Executive Session to discuss their ruling and then reconvene the
regular meeting.
Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session for Council
