                  )
From a decision of             )
Winnetka Fire Chief Scott T. Smith,       )
Respondent,                )
requiring the installation of an       )
automated fire suppression system       )
in a third floor unit of the building      )
at 888 Green Bay Road         )
                  )
FINDINGS, CONCLUSIONS AND ORDER
OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
ON THE APPEAL OF SUSAN KROEGER, LTD.
   The Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”), having reviewed
and considered the record in the above referenced matter, including the
documents and legal memoranda submitted by the attorney for Petitioner Susan
Kroeger, Ltd., and by the Village Prosecutor on behalf of Respondent Winnetka
Fire Chief Scott T. Smith, and having heard and considered the presentations
and legal arguments made by and on behalf of the Petitioner and Respondent, and
being otherwise fully advised, enters the following Findings, Conclusions and
Order:
I.   FINDINGS OF FACT
   A.   Jurisdiction and Parties
      1.   This is an appeal to the Village Council pursuant to Section
15.16.090 Winnetka Village Code (“WVC”).
      2.    Petitioner Susan Kroeger, Ltd., (“Petitioner”) operates a business
on the first floor of a building having the common street address of 886 Green
Bay Road in the Village of Winnetka.
      3.   Petitioner also leases a third floor unit at 888 Green Bay Road,
which is used as Petitioner’s office (the “Leased Unit”).
      4.   886 and 888 Green Bay Road are part of a building (the “Subject
Property”) that is located at the northwest corner of Tower Road and Green Bay
Road in the Village of Winnetka and is owned by Richard Busscher (“Owner”).
      5.   Respondent Fire Chief Scott T. Smith (the “Fire Chief”) is the Fire
Chief of the Village of Winnetka Fire Department
      6.   Petitioner appeals from a determination of the Fire Chief, stated in
a letter dated March 30, 2010, requiring the installation of an automated fire
suppression system (commonly referred to as a “sprinkler system”) in the Leased
Unit (the “Order”) within 30 days if Petitioner wished to continue occupying
that space for her business use.
      7.   A copy of the March 30, 2010 letter was also sent to the Owner.
      8.   The Fire Chief reiterated the Order in a letter to the Owner dated
April 12, 2010.
      9.   The Owner has not appealed from either the initial Order or the
April 12, 2010 letter and is not a party to this appeal.
   B.   Procedural Background
      10.   The Village Council heard the appeal at its regular Council meeting
on Tuesday, June 15, 2010.
      11.   At the close of the appeal hearing on June 15, 2010, the Council
directed the parties to submit written statements of their positions by June
25, 2010.
      12.   On June 25, 2010, Petitioner’s counsel filed the Brief of Susan
Kroeger, Ltd.
      13.   On June 25, 2010, the Village Prosecutor submitted a Memorandum to
the Village Council, with a subject line of “Rationale and support for the Fire
Chief’s determination requiring an automatic fire extinguishment system for the
third floor unit at 888 Green Bay Road occupied by Susan Kroeger, Ltd.”
      14.   The record consists of the following:
         a.   “Appeal of Decision of Scott T. Smith, Village Fire Chief
Pursuant to Winnetka Village Code Section 15.16.090,” filed by Petitioner on
April 29, 2010, including the following exhibits:
            i.   Kroeger Ex. A: March 30, 2010, letter from Fire Chief Scott T.
Smith to Ms. Susan Kroeger.
            ii.   Kroeger Ex. B: April 12, 2010, letter from Fire Chief Scott
T. Smith to Mr. Richard Buscher [sic].
            iii.   Kroeger Ex. C: Pages 27 through 30 of Title 15 of the
Winnetka Village Code, containing 15.16, “Fire Prevention and Life Safety
Codes.”
         b.   Agenda Report prepared by Fire Chief Scott T. Smith, dated June
9, 2010, including:
            i.   WFD Attachment 1:   Table of Existing Buildings That Required
Sprinkler Systems Installed Due To A Change Of Use.
            ii.   WFD Attachment 2:   Excerpts of Winnetka Village Code
(“WFC”):
               (1)   WFC § 8.04.010: “Automatic alarm and fire detection
systems requirements – Telephone alarms – Smoke detectors”
               (2)   WFC § 15.16.050: “Automatic fire extinguishing systems”
               (3)   WFC § 15.36.050: Criteria for change of use or occupancy”
         c.   Exhibits submitted by Respondent at the June 15, 2010, Council
meeting:
            i.   Kroeger Ex. 1: Excerpt of Chapter 3, Definitions, of NFPA 101,
Life Safety Code, 2000 Edition
            ii.   Kroeger Ex. 2: Excerpt of Chapter 2, Definitions, of 2003
International Fire Code
            iii.   Kroeger Ex. 3: Two-sided print-out of 11 photos, 9 on side
1, 2 on side 2
         d.   Brief of Susan Kroeger, Ltd., submitted June 25, 2010.
         e.   Memorandum from Village Prosecutor, submitted June 25, 2010,
captioned “Rationale and support for the Fire Chief’s determination requiring
an automatic fire extinguishment system for the third floor unit at 88 Green
Bay Road occupied by Susan Kroeger, Ltd.,” including the following exhibits:
            i.   WFD Ex. A: Excerpt of WVC Chapter 15.16, containing §
15.16.010 and portion of § 15.16.020
            ii.   WFD Ex. B: Excerpt of WVC Chapter 15.36, containing §
15.36.050
            iii.   WFD Ex. C: Excerpt of Section 4.6, General Requirements,
NFPA 101, Life Safety Code, 2000 Edition, containing §§ 4.6.1 through 4.6.12.3
            iv.   WFD Ex. D: Excerpt of WVC Chapter 15.16, containing §
15.16.050
            v.   WFD Ex. E: Excerpt of Chapter 38, New Business Occupancies,
NFPA 101, Life Safety Code, 2000 Edition, containing §§ 38.1.1 through
38.2.2.3.2
            vi.   WFD Ex. F: Document captioned “Attachment 1, Existing
Buildings That Required Sprinkler Systems Installed Due To A Change Of Use”
         f.   Letter dated July 20, 2010, from Petitioner’s attorney to the
Village Attorney.
   C.   Factual Record
      13.   Petitioner has operated a design business in the first floor space
of 886 Green Bay Road since 2003, with the business’ office space being located
on the first floor of a courtyard building north of the Subject Property.
      14.   Petitioner relocated her office spaces into the Leased Unit after
the cost of the other space increased.
      15.   The Leased Unit was not inspected prior to the time Petitioner
began occupying the Leased Unit.
      16.   Petitioner did not obtain a certificate of occupancy before
occupying the Leased Unit.
      17.   On February 23, 2010, in the course of a routine inspection, the
Winnetka Fire Department’s inspector learned that Petitioner had relocated her
office to the Leased Unit.
      18.   Based on Petitioner’s statements and photos submitted for the
record, the Leased Unit contains various furnishings, including: a couch; a
table and chairs; assorted work surfaces, including five work stations with
computers; a desk-top copier; books and material samples stored on shelving,
cabinet tops and window ledges and sills; a window unit air conditioner; a
refrigerator; a stove with oven; a microwave oven; a toaster oven; a front
door; and a rear door.
      19.   According to the Petitioner, the gas stove in the Leased Unit has
been turned off.
      20.   The building on the Subject Property was built in 1926.
      21.   The rear stairway of the building on the Subject Property is wood,
which is a combustible material.
      22.   The building on the Subject Property does not meet current safety
standards for emergency egress.
      23.   The building on the Subject Property is not equipped with a
sprinkler system.
      24.   It is the opinion of the Fire Chief that, for purposes of
administering WVC Chapter 15.16, the Subject Property is a mixture of
mercantile occupancies, business occupancies and multifamily residential
occupancies.
      25.   It is the opinion of the Fire Chief that, for purposes of
administering WVC Chapter 15.16, the Leased Unit was a residential occupancy
prior to its occupation by Petitioner.
      26.   It is the opinion of the Fire Chief that, for purposes of
administering WVC Chapter 15.16, Petitioner’s use of the Leased Unit as an
office is a change of occupancy from residential occupancy to business
occupancy.
      27.   The Fire Chief has determined that placing a business use in the
midst of residential units increases the hazard to life and property.
      28.   The Fire Chief has been a firefighter since 1977 and has been a
certified Fire Inspector since 1979.
      29.   Deputy Fire Chief Laureys is also a member of the Winnetka Fire
Department and is a certified Fire Inspector I, Fire Inspector II, and Plans
Examiner I.
      30.   The Fire Inspector and Plans Examiners certifications are issued by
the Office of the State Marshal after taking various courses, which include
courses in the application of Life Safety Code and NFPA standards.
      31.   The Fire Chief and Deputy Fire Chief follow a standardized
interpretation when they apply the Life Safety Code and NFPA standards.
      32.   According to the Fire Chief and Deputy Fire Chief, fire
departments, including the Winnetka Fire Department, evaluate multi-use
structures by considering the use or occupancy of each individual space in the
building or structure.
      33.   The Fire Chief followed the established practice of evaluating each
individual tenant space according to its own use group.
      34.   In determining that the change from residential occupancy to
business occupancy in the Leased Unit created a hazard, the Fire Chief relied
on his experience as a firefighter and his knowledge of and experience with the
Life Safety Code and NFPA standards.
      35.   The Fire Chief and Deputy Fire Chief described the increased
hazards from the change of occupancy as including the following:
         a.   Fire alarms inside units aren’t heard throughout the building,
and people hear things differently, as evidenced by studies that children are
not awakened by fire alarms.
         b.   An unchecked fire doubles in size every minute.
         c.   If a fire were to occur in the Leased Unit during the night and
the fire alarm malfunctioned, it would create a hazard, because there would be
no one to alert persons in the other units.
         d.   Sprinklers can put a fire out or hold a fire in check until
firefighters arrive.
         e.   With sprinklers in place, there is a 70% better chance of
surviving a fire.
         f.   The existing alarm system in the building is not up to current
NFPA.
         g.   In an emergency, persons in the building would have to leave
through a stairwell that is not up to current NFPA standards.
      30.   Petitioner disagrees with the Fire Chief’s conclusions.
      31.   Neither Petitioner nor her legal counsel claims to be a certified
firefighter, fire inspector or plan examiner, or to have experience in
firefighting practices, in assessing fire hazards, or in the application of
fire and life safety codes.
      32.   The Fire Chief’s March 30, 2010, letter to Petitioner is an order
requiring that a safety-to-life measure be taken, as provided in WVC §
15.16.090.
      33.   The Owner of the Subject Property was also served with a copy of
the Fire Chief’s March 30, 2010 letter.
      34.   Petitioner filed a timely appeal on April 29, 2010, within 30 days
from the date of the Fire Chief’s March 30, 2010, letter.
      35.   The Fire Chief’s April 12, 2010, letter to the Owner of the Subject
Property is also an order requiring that a safety-to-life measure be taken, as
provided in WVC § 15.16.090.
      36.   The Owner has not filed an appeal from either of the Fire Chief’s
letters.
   D.   Petitioner’s Position
      37.   Petitioner admits that an automatic fire alarm and fire detection
system is required as provided in WVC § 8.04.010.
      38.   Petitioner is not seeking a variance from the provisions of the
Village’s fire sprinkler system requirements pursuant to WVC § 15.16.080, but
contends instead that the requirement does not apply in this instance.
      39.   Petitioner contends that Attachment 1 submitted by the Fire Chief,
which lists buildings in which a sprinkler system was required due to a change
in use inside the building, is irrelevant to the issue of whether the Fire
Chief properly exercised his discretion in issuing the March 30, 2010, letter.
      40.   Petitioner contends that the model codes referred to in WVC Chapter
15.16 have not been adopted by reference, because the beginning of WVC §
15.16.010 (A) refers to paragraphs A through D, while the paragraphs preceding
each code are number 1 through 4.
      41.   Petitioner contends that the Life Safety Code adopted pursuant to
WVC § 15.16.030 does not apply.
      42.   Petitioner admits that there has been a change of use in the Lease
Unit, but contends that a sprinkler is not required for the unit because the
reference in WVC § 15.16.050 (B) to a change in use or occupancy refers to the
building as a whole, and not to individual units within the building.
      43.   Petitioner contends that the exception to the fire sprinkler
requirement in WVC § 15.16.050 (C) applies because:
         a.   the building was a mixed use building in 1977 and remains a mixed
use building;
         b.   the use of the Leased Unit as an office is not a hazard; and
         c.   the Fire Chief has failed to establish that the change
constitutes “a distinct hazard to life or property.”
      44.   Petitioner contends that the Fire Chief abused his discretion and
acted arbitrarily in issuing the March 30, 2010, letter, because he did not
state the facts on which he based his conclusion that the change in use in the
Leased Unit constitutes an increased hazard and because the Fire Chief did not
specifically state that the change in use “constitutes a distinct hazard to
life or property.”
      45.   Petitioner contends that the Fire Chief’s decision is arbitrary and
that the Fire Chief has not established that the Leased Unit is “a distinct
threat to life or property.”
      46.   Petitioner contends that the use of the Leased Unit is less
hazardous than the residential apartments and that the alarm in the Leased
Unit, if activated, would be heard throughout the building.
      47.   Petitioner’s counsel represents that it would cost Petitioner
$10,000 to relocate her office.
      48.   Petitioner’s counsel represents that Petitioner would not be able
to install the sprinkler system because she is a tenant and does not own the
building, and that the cost of putting a sprinkler into the Lease Unit alone is
$100,000.
II.   STATEMENT OF ISSUES
      49.   Whether the model codes listed in WVC § 15.16.010 have been adopted
by reference.
      50.   Whether the various Village Code provisions contain sufficient
detail and standards to delegate certain decision-making authority to the Fire
Chief.
      51.   Whether the Fire Chief was required to state a detailed factual
basis for the conclusions stated in his letter of March 30, 2010.
      52.   Whether, in order to issue a valid compliance order, the Fire Chief
was required to make a specific finding on all three of the standard for
eligibility for an exception to the sprinkler alarm requirement, when his
finding that the change in the Leased Unit from a residential occupancy to a
business occupancy constitutes an increased hazard meant that all three
eligibility standards could not be met.
      53.   Whether the change in the Leased Unit from a residential apartment
to an office is a change in use or occupancy that triggers the automated fire
suppression system requirement of WVC § 15.16.050.
      54.   Whether Petitioner established that the Fire Chief arbitrarily
concluded that the change in the Leased Unit from a residential occupancy to a
business occupancy created a particular hazard.
      55.   Whether the Fire Chief properly concluded that the change in the
Leased Unit from a residential occupancy to a business occupancy creates a
particular hazard for the residential occupants of the building.
      56.   Whether the Fire Chief properly ordered Petitioner to vacate the
Leased Unit within 30 days if a sprinkler system was not installed by that
time.
      57.   Whether the Life Safety Code, NFPA 101, applies to this case.
      58.   Whether the cost of moving or of installing a sprinkler system is
relevant to the issue of compliance.
III.   RELEVANT PROVISIONS OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE
      59.   Pursuant to WVC § 2.52.020 of the Village Code, the Winnetka Fire
Department is responsible for providing “firefighting, fire prevention and life
safety services in the Village.”
      60.   Pursuant to WVC § 2.52.030, the Fire Chief is the head of the Fire
Department, whose powers and duties include being a “building officer,” as
defined in WVC Chapter 15.04, and “administering and enforcing the provision of
[the Village] Code relating to fire prevention and public safety.”
      61.   Pursuant to WVC § 2.52.020(A) the functions and authority of the
Winnetka Fire Department include conducting “a minimum of one regularly
scheduled annual inspection of all structures in the village other than single
and two-family residential structures, and to conduct such additional
reinspections as necessary to assure correction of any violations.”
      62.   WVC § 15.36.050 provides, in relevant part:
No change shall be made in the use or occupancy of any land, structure or
portion of such land or structure used wholly or partially for nonresidential
purposes, unless the Director has first issued a certificate of occupancy for
such change. No certificate of occupancy shall be issued . . . unless . . . (2)
the structural, electrical mechanical, health and sanitation, plumbing, HVAC
and fire safety elements in the subject unit and in all common interior and
exterior areas are inspected and all health and life safety requirements are
approved, as required by this code.
      63.   WVC § 15.16.010 provides as follows:
Section 15.16.010   Adoption of codes by reference.
   A.   Adoption of code by reference. The model codes described in the
following subsections A through D are each adopted by reference pursuant to the
home rule authority of the Village of Winnetka under Article VII, Section 6 of
the State of Illinois Constitution of 1970, and further pursuant to applicable
provisions of the Illinois Municipal Code and the Municipal Adoption of Codes
and Records Act, 50 ILCS 220/1 through 220/7, except as modified by the
exclusions, amendments and additional provisions set forth in this chapter.
      1.   International Fire Code, 2003 Edition.
      2.   National Fire Protection Association (NFPA) 101 Life Safety Code,
2000 Edition, specifically including all appendices and the standards referred
to in the code.
      3.   Standards for the Installation of Automatic Sprinkler Systems,
National Fire Protection Association (NFPA) Publication 13, 1999 Edition.
      4.   Standards for the Installation of Standpipe and Hose Systems,
National Fire Protection Association (NFPA) Publication 14, 2000 Edition.
   B.   Rules of construction. In the event any provision of the codes adopted
by reference in this chapter conflict with state law, with any other ordinance
of the Village, or with any other provision of this code, the provision
requiring the highest standard for protection of life or property, as
determined by the Fire Chief, shall prevail.
      64.   WVC § 15.16.030 provides as follows:
Section 15.16.030   Life Safety Code
   A.   Title.   The NFPA 101 Life Safety Code, 2000 Edition, shall be known as
the Life Safety Code of the Village of Winnetka.
   B.   Applicability of Life Safety Code.
      1.   Building alterations or construction. All additions and alterations
to existing buildings, and all construction of new buildings, shall comply with
the provisions of the Life Safety Code.
      2.   Restoration of damaged buildings. Whenever any existing building is
damaged by fire or other cause to the extent that the cost of restoration to
its condition immediately prior to the occurrence shall exceed fifty (50)
percent of the cost of the restoration of the entire building new or whenever
alterations or additions are made to any existing building which are equivalent
to more than fifty (50) percent of the value of such building, the construction
or alteration shall include all changes necessary to bring the building into
conformity with the Life Safety Code. Costs determined under this paragraph
shall be determined in the same manner such costs are determined for
nonconforming buildings under chapter 15.64 of this code.
   C.   Elevators. Any elevator installation shall be designed to accommodate
an ambulance litter, in horizontal position, plus two attendants.
   D.   Exit Doors. Exit doors shall be installed to swing with exit travel,
irrespective on [sic] the number of persons served, in any building used for an
occupancy described in subsection B of Section 15.16.050 of this chapter.
      65.   WVC § 15.16.050 provides as follows:
Section 15.16.050   Automatic fire extinguishing systems
   A.   The Standards for the Installation of Sprinkler Systems, 1999 Edition,
also known NFPA Publication 13, shall be known as Automatic Sprinkler
Regulations of the Village of Winnetka.
   B.   Automatic fire extinguishing systems required. Except as provided in
subsection D of this section, automatic fire extinguishing systems, installed
in accordance with the standards set forth in NFPA Publication 13, Standard for
the Installation of Sprinkler Systems, 1999 Edition, or alternate similar fire
suppression systems as approved by the Fire Chief, shall be installed in all
buildings used for any one or more of the following occupancies:
      1.   Assembly occupancy used for gathering together six or more persons;
      2.   Any occupancy where there is an activity involving the use of
flammable liquids or gases or where flammable or combustible finishes are
applied;
      3.   Mercantile occupancy;
      4.   Institutional occupancy;
      5.   Multifamily residential occupancy;
      6.   Educational occupancy;
      7.   Business occupancy; and
      8.   Storage occupancy.
   C.   Exceptions. The requirements of subsection B of this section shall not
apply where the use or occupancy: (1) is the same as it was prior to the
amendment of this section effective on February 15, 1977; (2) has continued
without change or, if there has been a change, the change does not increase the
hazard to life or property; and (3) does not constitute a distinct hazard to
life or property as determined by the Fire Chief.
   D.   Terms. The terms used in this section shall have the same meanings as
those terms have in the Fire Prevention Code and the Life Safety Code adopted
by this chapter.
IV.   DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSIONS
      66.   Subsection (A) of WVC § 15.16.010 expresses a clear intent to
adopt, by reference, the four model codes listed in Subsection (A), “except as
modified by the exclusions, amendments and additional provisions set forth in
this chapter.”
         a.   The opening sentence of Subsection (A) of WVC § 15.16.010 is
followed by four numbered paragraphs, each listing a model code.
         b.   WVC § 15.16.020 is captioned “Fire Code,” and states exclusions
from and amendments to “International Fire Code, 2003 Edition,” which is the
model code listed in paragraph 1 of WVC § 15.16.010(A).
         c.   WVC § 15.16.030 is captioned “Life Safety Code,” and states
exclusions from and amendments to the “NFPA Life Safety Code, 2000 Edition,”
which is the model code listed in paragraph 2 of WVC § 15.16.010(A).
         d.   WVC § 15.16.050 is captioned “Automatic fire extinguishing
systems,” and sets out provisions modifying the “
         e.   WVC § 15.16.040 is captioned “Standpipe and hose systems,” and
sets out provisions pertaining to the “Standards for the Installation of
Standpipe and Hose Systems, 2000 Edition, also known as NFPA Publication 14,”
which is the model code listed in paragraph 4 of WVC § 15.16.010(A).
         f.   The clear and repeated references throughout Chapter 15.16 to the
codes listed in WVC § 15.16.010 (A) confirms the intent to adopt those codes by
reference and the discrepancy in the numbering of the subsections of WVC §
15.16.010 must therefore be seen as a scrivener’s error, as argued by the
Village Prosecutor.
      67.   The provisions of the Village Code, read as a whole, contain
sufficient detail and standards to delegate decision-making authority to the
Fire Chief.
         a.   Petitioner’s letter of July 20, 2010, mischaracterizes the
argument of the Village Prosecutor as asserting that the Fire Chief can
arbitrarily “pick which of the several codes and laws he would apply.”
         b.   Based on the highlighted language in the attachment to the letter
of July 20, 2010, Petitioner believes that the Village Council has improperly
delegated to the Fire Chief, an administrative officer, the Village Council’s
“inherent function of determining what the law shall be and to whom it should
be applied.”
         c.   The statement in WVC § 15.16.010 (B) directs the Fire Chief to
resolve conflicts between provisions in WVC Chapter 15.16 and State law, other
Village ordinances or other provisions of the Village Code by applying “the
highest standard for protection of life or property, as determined by the Fire
Chief.”
         d.   The directive does not tell the Fire Chief to “determine what the
law shall be and to whom it should be applied.” Nor does it authorize the Fire
chief to make decisions on personal preference. Rather, the Fire Chief is
directed to make a choice only when there is a conflict among laws that have
already been enacted by the State or the Village, and to make that choice based
on what, in his professional judgment provides “the highest standard for
protection of life or property.”
         e.   The clearly defined policy of the Village Council to choose the
highest standards for public safety is not undone by the directive to the Fire
Chief, who is a trained firefighting and life safety professional, particularly
when WVC § 15.16.090 allows the Fire Chief’s decisions to be appealed to the
Village Council.
      68.   That the decision of the Fire Chief, as stated in his letter of
March 30, 2010, did not include extensive factual findings does not render his
decision improper or arbitrary.
         a.   The letter of March 30, 2010, was a straightforward
administrative compliance directive from the Fire Chief.
         b.   The letter of March 30, 2010, was not the product of an
evidentiary hearing and did not require evidentiary findings.
         c.   Nothing in the Village Code prescribes the form or requirements
for an order of the Fire Chief.
         d.   Nothing in the Village Code requires the Fire Chief to enter
detailed factual findings in support of every administrative act he takes in
seeking compliance with the Village’s fire and life safety codes.
         e.   WVC § 15.16.050 (B) states the basic requirement that all
buildings and all occupancies within buildings shall be equipped with automatic
sprinkler systems.
         f.   WVC § 15.16.050 (C) defines three standards for eligibility for
an exception to the sprinkler requirement, all of which standards must be met
to be eligible. Because eligibility for an exception under WVC § 15.16.050 (C)
requires a finding that all of the standards have been met, rather than a
finding that any of the standards have been met, it does not require the Fire
Chief to make findings negating all of the standards of eligibility in order to
issue a valid compliance order.
         g.   Once the Fire Chief concluded that the change in occupancy from
residential to business is an increase in hazard, the eligibility standards for
an exception could not be met, and it was not necessary to make a finding on
the third eligibility standard to issue the compliance order.
         h.   Petitioner’s claim that the Fire Chief must affirmatively
establish that the change in occupancy creates a “distinct hazard” and that the
Fire Chief has failed to do so conflates the first and second points of Section
15.160.050(C), rather than giving them each effect, which is required if the
exception is to apply.
         i.   The essence of Petitioner’s claim is that the Fire Chief cannot
require compliance with the basic requirement for sprinkler systems in WVC
§15.16.050 (B) unless he first makes specific findings on each of the three
eligibility standards and establishes that the exception in WVC § 15.16.050 (C)
does not apply. This would change WVC § 15.16.050 from a provision that states
an unequivocal mandate and then allows limited exceptions into a provision in
which the exception is presumed to exist and the right to the exception must be
disproved before the sprinkler mandate can be enforced. WVC § 15.16.050 (B)
contains no such equivocation.
         j.   The letter of March 30, 2010, provided adequate notice to
Petitioner and the Owner of the Fire Chief’s determination and what was
required to comply.
         k.   The appeal to the Village Council pursuant to WVC § 15.16.080
provides a forum and a procedural mechanism to challenge a compliance order, to
obtain the factual basis for the order and to make a factual case for an
exception.
      69.   The Fire Chief’s interpretation of WVC § 15.16.050 as requiring the
installation of a sprinkler system when there is a change in use or occupancy
within an occupancy unit of a building, even if the overall use of the building
does not change, is consistent with the plain language of WVC § 15.16.050 and
has been consistently applied by the Fire Chief.
         a.   WVC § 15.16.050 (B) requires sprinkler systems “in all buildings
used for any one or more of the [listed] occupancies.”
         b.   WVC § 15.16.050 (B) uses the term “used” in reference to the
building as a whole, and all parties consider the building on the Subject
Property to be a “mixed use building.”
         c.   The eight paragraphs of WVC § 15.16.050 (B) list occupancies in
the singular and treat “occupancies” as parts of the whole, so that it may take
one or more “occupancies” to determine the overall use of the building.
         d.   WVC § 15.16.050 (C) applies to the “use or occupancy.” The use of
the disjunctive “or,” rather than conjunctive “and” is further evidence of the
intent to treat the two concepts as different rather than as synonymous.
         e.   Petitioner’s argument is, in essence, an “all or nothing”
interpretation: according to Petitioner, if the change in occupancy does not
change the use category of the building as a whole, then sprinklers cannot be
required.
         f.   Although Petitioner contends that WVC § 15.16.050 (C) pertains to
the “building” as a whole, accepting that argument would either render the
phrase “or occupancy” meaningless surplusage or alter its meaning to “and
occupancy,” and it would require reading the word “building” into a provision
where it has been omitted, all of which is contrary to the plain language of
WVC § 15.16.050 (C) and contrary to accepted principles of statutory
construction.
      70.   The definitions in the Life Safety Code, NFPA 101, 2000 Edition,
confirm the Fire Chief’s understanding and application of the term “occupancy”
to individual units in a building as well as to the overall occupancy of the
building as a whole.
         a.   WVC § 15.16.050 (D) states that the terms in WVC § 15.16.050 have
the same meanings as they have in the Life Safety Code and the Fire Prevention
Code.
         b.   Section 3.3.25 of the Life Safety Code defines “building” as “any
structure used for or intended for supporting or sheltering any use or
occupancy.”
         c.   Section 3.3.134 of the Life Safety Code defines “occupancy” as
“the purpose for which a building or portion thereof is used or intended to be
used.” [Emphasis added.]
         d.   The Fire Chief’s determination that the Leased Unit is an
occupancy and that the change in occupancy from residential to business trigger
the sprinkler requirement is therefore supported by the definition incorporated
into the Village Code and specifically incorporated into WVC § 15.16.050.
      71.   The second condition to be met to be entitled to the exception in
WVC § 15.16.050 (C) is that the change “does not increase the hazard to life or
property.”
         a.   In the proceedings before the Council, the Fire Chief and Deputy
Fire Chief presented ample evidence to support the Fire Chief’s opinion that
the change of use increased the hazard to life and property.
         b.   As trained firefighters with relevant certifications from the
Office of the State Fire Marshal and lengthy experience as both firefighters
and fire inspectors, the Fire Chief and Deputy Fire Chief are qualified to
express their expert, professional opinions as to when circumstances will
constitute a hazard or increase a hazard.
         c.   If the Leased Unit were not equipped with fire sprinklers and a
fire broke out at night, when the Leased Unit is unoccupied, the fire would
spread unchecked until firefighters arrived.
         d.   If an alarm in the Leased Unit were to malfunction, it would put
the sleeping residents in jeopardy, for there would be no alarm to hear and no
one would be present to alert neighboring residents of danger.
         e.   That Petitioner’s office may not be particularly hazardous, in
and of itself, is not dispositive, for WVC § 15.16.050 (C) addresses the
impact, not the nature of use or occupancy.
      72.   Petitioner has not established that she met the third prong of the
test for the exception stated in WVC § 15.16.050 (C).
         a.   It was Petitioner’s burden to persuade the Fire Chief that the
change in the Leased Premises “does not constitute a distinct hazard to life or
property.”
         b.   It is clear from the Fire Chief’s letter of March 30, 2010, that
the Petitioner had not persuaded him that the change “does not constitute a
distinct hazard to life or property,” for if he had determined that Petitioner
had met the test, he would not have issued the compliance order.
         c.   While Petitioner has argued that the Fire Chief’s decision was
unfounded, Petitioner has not pointed to any evidence to support that
conclusion.
         d.   As noted above, there is ample evidence to support the Fire
Chief’s expert opinion.
         e.   Petitioner’s argument is based on the self-serving layman’s
opinions of Petitioner and her counsel, rather than on credible evidence.
      73.   Attachment 1 to the Fire Chief’s initial Agenda Report to the
Village Council shows the consistency with which the Fire Chief has applied the
fire sprinkler requirement.
      74.   Petitioner’s assertions that moving her business office from the
Leased Unit would cost $10,000, that installing sprinklers would cost $100,000
and that Petitioner could not install sprinklers because she is not the owner
of the Subject Property, are irrelevant to determining the validity of the Fire
Chief’s order of March 30, 2010.
         a.   The exceptions to the sprinkler requirement do not include an
exception based on the cost of compliance.
         b.   The cost of compliance is relevant, if at all, only in a request
for a modification of regulations under WVC § 15.16.080, which requires the
establishment of “practical difficulties” in carrying out the strict provisions
of WVC Chapter 15.16.
         c.   Petitioner’s claim is only that the sprinkler requirement does
not apply and Petitioner has stated that she is not seeking a variance from the
Village Code’s requirements.
         d.   Petitioner is familiar with the Village’s pre-occupancy
inspection and sprinkler requirements, having previously been required to
comply with them when moving into 866 Green Bay in 2003.
         e.   Petitioner took a calculated risk in ignoring the Village Code’s
pre-occupancy and sprinkler requirements when moving into the Leased Unit and
Petitioner cannot credibly complain that the imposition of those requirements
after the fact is unreasonably expensive or impossible, since the requirements
could have been avoided all together by choosing other space.
         f.   In any event, the Fire Chief’s order of March 30, 2010, does not
order Petitioner to install the sprinklers. Rather, it orders the Petitioner to
move from the Leased Unit and for the Leased Unit to be returned to its prior
residential occupancy if sprinklers have not been installed within 30 days.
Thus, the Fire Chief’s order of March 30, 2010, gave Petitioner alternative
routes to compliance.
V.   ULTIMATE CONCLUSIONS AND ORDER
      75.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Village Council finds that the
model codes listed in WVC § 15.16.010 have been duly adopted by reference.
      76.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Village Council finds that the
change in the Leased Unit from a residential apartment to a business occupancy
is a change in use or occupancy and thus triggered the automated fire
suppression system requirement of WVC § 15.16.050.
      77.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Village Council finds that
Chapter 15.16 properly delegates the Village Council’s discretion to the Fire
Chief for purposes of administering the codes stated in that chapter.
      78.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Village Council finds that the
Fire Chief properly concluded that the change of use of the Leased Unit from a
residential apartment to an office creates a hazard to life and property.
      79.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Village Council finds that the
Fire Chief did not abuse his discretion or act arbitrarily in issuing the
compliance order contained in his letter of March 30, 3010.
      80.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Village Council finds that the
Petitioner has not established that she is entitled to the exception to the
sprinkler mandate provided in WVC § 15.16.050 (C).
      81.   Nothing in this Order shall be construed as expressing an opinion
as to the relative rights and responsibilities of the Owner and Petitioner
under Petitioner’s lease for the Leased Unit.
FOR ALL OF THE ABOVE AND FOREGOING REASONS, it is hereby ordered:
   A.   That the order issued by the Fire Chief in his letter of March 30, 2010
is affirmed.
   B.   That Petitioner is hereby ordered to comply with the compliance order
stated in the Fire Chief’s March 30, 2010, letter forthwith, and to either (i)
cease using the Leased Unit as a business occupancy and to vacate the Leased
Unit so it can revert to a residential occupancy, or (ii) proceed with the
installation of sprinklers in the Lease Unit.
   ADOPTED and ENTERED this 3 rd day of August, 2010, by the following roll
call vote of the Council of the Village of Winnetka.
   AYES:         Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz, Spinney     
   NAYS:      None     
   ABSENT      None     
