August 17, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
August 17, 2010
(Approved: September 21, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 17, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:37 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike
D’Onofrio, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Finance Director Ed McKee,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and approximately nine persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
President Tucker announced that there would be a Special Executive Session on
Tuesday, August 24 at 8:00 p.m. All of the Council members present indicated
that they expected to attend.
   a)   September 7, 2010, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
with the exception of Trustee Rintz who was unsure, indicated that they
expected to attend.
   b)   September 14, 2010 Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda as
amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson,
Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   July 20, 2010, Regular Meeting.
      ii)   August 3, 2010 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1665 and 1666. Approving Warrant List No. 1665 in
the amount of $783,741.82, and Warrant List No. 1666 in the amount of
$311,983.15.
   c)   Design Bids, Diesel Engine Catalyst Units. Awards a contract for the
professional services required to install emission controls on the diesel
generators to Barnes, Henry, Meisenhiemer & Gende, Inc., in an amount not to
exceed $68,982.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance M-14-2010 – Zoning Variation: 389 Ridge – Introduction. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a three-foot variation from the rear yard
setback requirement, in order to replace the existing legally nonconforming
garage, which is dilapidated.
Mr. D’Onofrio reported that the Zoning Board had unanimously approved the
request, but added that an adjacent neighbor had written a letter asking the
Council to deny the variation based on the increased height of the proposed new
garage.
Ted Widen, the applicant, said moving the garage up to a conforming location
would make entering the garage difficult and could disturb the roots of a
nearby tree. He noted that the house is set back significantly from the street
and has a very small back yard, and that the proposed new garage would provide
needed storage space.
Mr. Robert Cody, 712 Ash, asked the Council to reject the request as the new
garage roof will impair the light and air to his back yard. He added that
nonconforming uses should be eliminated, hardship notwithstanding.
The Council discussed the request with both Mr. Widen and Mr. Cody.
Mrs. Widen appealed to the Council to grant the variation and asserted that,
from an aesthetic perspective, the new garage would have a nominal impact.
Trustee Greable said he thought the ZBA made the right call, and that a new
garage will improve not only the Subject Property but the surrounding
neighborhood. He noted that the footprint of the new garage is almost the same
as the old one and said he could approve the request.
Trustee Spinney said she had toured the Subject Property and she did not see
how it created a hardship for Mr. Cody.
Trustees Pedian and Poor said they thought the height of the new garage would
negatively affect Mr. Cody’s yard and that they could not support the request.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance M-
14-2010. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Johnson,
Spinney, Rintz, and Greable. Nays: Trustees Pedian and Poor. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
   a)   Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, noted that, as a result of public input,
METRA had modified the schedule changes that were made due to Union Pacific’s
bridge construction project, and thanked President Tucker and Trustee Johnson
for their efforts.
   b)   Anna Lothson, Assistant Editor of the Winnetka Current, introduced
herself to the Council and said she would be reporting on Council meetings and
community events.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Request to Place Flags on Village Green. Taylor Tucker, 850 Locust,
said she was honored that Genevieve Nielsen had asked her to take over this 9/
11 observance and asked the Council for permission to once again plant flags on
the Village Green. She also invited Winnetkans to help plant the flags on
September 10 th .
The Council commended Miss Tucker for her community service and unanimously
approved the request.
   b)   Corner Cooks Street Use Request. Attorney Janega reviewed this request
to use two parking spaces to accommodate a smoker for a planned pig roast at
Corner Cooks on Friday, October 15, 2010. She explained that although a special
event application has been filed, the Council’s approval is necessary since
Village Code prohibits the use of public streets and sidewalks for any business
purpose without the Council’s explicit consent. She noted that Fire Chief Scott
Smith has reviewed the request and finds the proposed location acceptable,
provided that it is sufficiently blocked off and monitored to prevent curious
onlookers from touching the smoker.
The Council approved the request with the condition that the roaster be
attended at all times.
   c)   Bid 10-016a: Village Hall Roof Restoration. Mr. Saunders reviewed the
bid responses for the restoration and replacement of the Village Hall roof. He
stated that the lowest bidder, Mortenson Roofing, appears to be a qualified
contractor, was recommended by the architect of the Hubbard Woods School roof
project, and that its bid is much lower than any of the roof repair bids from
the overall Village Hall restoration project bids that had been received in
May, 2010. He recommended awarding the bid to Mortenson so the work can begin
by September 1 st and be completed by the end of the year.
The Council commended Mr. Saunders on his work preparing new bid documents to
save the Village money, after the earlier bids for the full project exceeded
estimated costs.
After discussing some particulars of the roof project with the Council, Mr.
Saunders announced that the masonry bids were opened the previous week, and it
appeared that the Village would once again save money when compared to the
original bid numbers.
Mr. Saunders added that staff had also researched applying for funding in
connection with LEED certification for the restoration project, and that the
Illinois Clean Energy Foundation is evaluating whether the Village will earn
enough points to qualify for a grant. He noted that the Park District’s
recently completed service center had qualified for funding as new construction
and so was able to earn points for Silver certification. He pointed out that
the grant is capped at $75,000, which is about half of the estimated cost for
LEED certification for the Village Hall renovation.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to award the bid for the
Village Hall Roof Restoration to Mortenson Roofing Company in the amount of
$632,654. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees
Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Audit Contract. Mr. McKee reported that the Village’s auditor,
Lauterbach & Amen, is in the last year of a 4-year contract and reviewed the
Village’s recent history regarding retaining an audit firm. He asked the
Council for guidance on whether to seek a multi-year contract extension with
Lauterbach & Amen or to issue an RFP for a new firm, adding that he recommends
staying with the current firm, in light of other administrative changes
currently taking place in the Village.
After a brief discussion, the Council reached general consensus to do a 3-year
contract extension with the current auditor, and Mr. McKee said he would
proceed accordingly.
   e)   Turbine/Generator #6 Emergency Repairs. Mr. Keys reported that the #6
generator at the Power Plant had failed on August 4 th while coming online for
a scheduled test of generation capability for the IMEA and the regional
transmission authority. He said no Winnetka customers experienced a power
outage as a result of this equipment failure but noted that there is a
financial incentive for returning the unit to service, as the IMEA pays the
Village capacity credits in exchange for the right to take the output of the
generating units. He reported that IMEA was deducting capacity credits from the
Village at the rate of $400 per day until August 14 th , when the amount
doubled to $800 per day.
Mr. Keys said it had been difficult to get pricing on the repairs, as the
problem can only be diagnosed after the generator is taken apart in the repair
shop; therefore, all of the bidders specified that the repairs will be
performed on a time and material basis, due to the unknown nature of the
breakdown. He recommended authorizing the Interim Village Manager to sign a
contract with Keystone Specialty Services Company in an amount not to exceed
$65,000 for emergency repairs to the #6 generator.
Trustee Pedian commented that it would be wise to add contingency funds to the
base amount in case a bigger problem was found, in order to expedite the
process and get the generator back into operation as quickly as possible.
Mr. Keys said the extra latitude could make the work go more smoothly, and he
noted that he would get the necessary approval from the Interim Village Manager
if the extra funds are needed.
After a brief discussion, the Council agreed to authorize the Interim Village
Manager to approve an extra $35,000 for the repairs if necessary.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to authorize the Village
Manager to sign a contract with Keystone Specialty Services Company for
emergency repairs to the #6 generator as outlined on p. 68 of the Agenda in a
base amount of $65,000 with authorization for the Interim Village Manager to
approver a further $35,000 if it is deemed necessary. By roll call vote, the
motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz,
Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President.
      i)   The Hubbard Woods Post Office will close on September 25 and the
Village will post a consumer affairs phone number for resident comment on its
website. President Tucker added that she would urge the Post Office to
reconsider closing the facility and perhaps reduce the hours instead.
      ii)   Regarding the issue of the Village government’s accomplishments,
President Tucker stated that public input is an important and highly valued
component of the Council’s process and that the Council engages in a very
deliberate and thoughtful process and working with residents on the issues
historically takes time. She also pointed out that the Council rarely engages
in significant policy discussions in the summer months, as many residents are
gone during the summer, and that the Council has held many extra meetings in
recent months as part of their manager search process. Finally, she said the
Village’s dedicated staff is always on hand to assist the Council and noted
that she is happy to talk with residents about their concerns.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Spinney reported on the RED center budget subcommittee
meeting and also on the latest meeting of the Library District’s Strategic
Planning Committee.
      ii)   Trustee Rintz said the Plan Commission will meet on August 25 th to
discuss affordable housing.
      iii)   Trustee Poor reported that the EFC is hopeful that it will have a
demolition recycling ordinance drafted for the Council in the fall.
   c)   Attorney. No report.
   d)   Manager. Interim Manager Rosenthal reported that Winnetka has
experienced higher electrical loads than normal due to the unusually hot summer
weather. She said although the purchases from IMEA in June were typical, the
purchases in July set an all time record high. She indicated that these strong
sales in July have put the Electric Fund back on track for the current fiscal
year, and thus far the August sales appear to be above average as well.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Poor moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters and Appointments to Public Office, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) and 2
(c)(3) respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:25 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:07 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Poor, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 10:
08 p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
September 7, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
September 7, 2010
(Approved: September 21, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 7, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Community
Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, Fire Chief Scott Smith, Deputy Fire Chief
Steve Laureys, Fire Lt. John Ripka, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and
approximately 12 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   September 14, 2010, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   September 21, 2010, Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
except Trustee Spinney indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that Ordinance M-14-2010 had been removed from the
consent agenda for consideration on the regular agenda. Trustee Johnson,
seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the Agenda as amended. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz,
Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda.
   a)   Village Council Minutes. None.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1667 and 1668. Approving Warrant List No. 1667 in
the amount of $1,448,798.39, and Warrant List No. 1668 in the amount of
$826,216.13.
   c)   High Lift Pump Replacement, Water Plant. Awards the replacement of the
Water Plant’s high lift pump #4 to Mechanical Incorporated in the amount of
$332,300 in accordance with the terms and conditions of Bid #0250050.
   d)   Bid 10-023: Holiday Lighting. Awards the bid for the 2010 Holiday
Lighting to Landscape Concepts Management for $36,068.50
   e)   Change Order: Primary Cable – The Okonite Company. Awards a change
order to the Okonite Company in the amount of $71,500 for the purchase of 15kV
1/0 primary cable at the unit prices bid, subject to the contract conditions.
   f)   Turbine/Generator #6 Emergency Repairs. Ratifies the issuance of a
change order to Keystone Specialty Services in an amount not to exceed $118,099
for a rotor rewind of the #6 generator.
   g)   Audit Contract. Approves a three-year contract with the audit firm of
Lauterbach and Amen.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance M-14-2010 – Zoning Variation: 389 Ridge – Adoption. Mr.
D’Onofrio reported that this ordinance, introduced at the August 17 th Council
Meeting, grants a 3-foot rear yard setback variance to permit the replacement
of a dilapidated detached garage on the Subject Property. He noted that a
letter from Robert Cody, a neighbor at 712 Ash who opposes the variation
request, had been included in the Council’s agenda packets, and he added that a
copy of a letter received this morning from Mr. Cody’s Attorney, David Meeks,
had been placed in front of each Trustee on the dais.
President Tucker asked the parties to give their comments.
Mr. Meeks said Mr. Cody objects to the variation because he believes granting
the variation will be detrimental to his property, and the garage could
reasonably be sited within the standard setbacks and still provide adequate
vehicle maneuvering. He disputed several facts that had been brought forward at
the ZBA hearing and asserted that no practical difficulty or hardship had been
proven by the applicant.
Mrs. Widen, 389 Ridge and the applicant, objected to Mr. Cody’s coming forth
with objections at this stage, as he had not attended the ZBA hearing and did
not file an objection until the matter came before the Council. She noted that
the property has an extraordinarily large front yard setback, which moves the
house back on the lot and limits the back yard space. She noted that she and
her husband had made concessions on the height, width and design of the
proposed new garage in order to gain Mr. Cody’s initial approval of the
project, and she asked the Council to uphold the ZBA’s recommendation.
Attorney Janega explained that, under the Zoning Ordinance, the Council has
greater latitude to grant variations than the ZBA Ordinance. She reviewed the
Ordinance she had prepared granting the variation, and pointed out that the
ordinance contains a finding as to practical difficulties and that the Council
does not have to enter additional findings as to a particular hardship and
reasonable return. She explained the basis of the evidentiary record and
provided the historical framework for the requirement for a larger setback when
a rear yard abuts a side yard was established. She added that the Council has
the option to grant or deny the variation, or to send the matter back to the
ZBA, but she recommended that the Council act on the record that was before it.
Chip Hardt, 395 Ridge, asked if the concept of hardship applies to the
neighbors, as the garage is rapidly becoming a safety hazard.
Attorney Janega replied that safety is not a compelling zoning argument because
an unsafe garage can be removed.
After a thorough Council discussion, Trustees Greable, Spinney, Johnson and
Rintz said they could see the practical difficulties and could support the
variation, although they would like to see the neighbors work out their
differences.
Trustees Pedian and Poor were against granting the variance, as they considered
it detrimental to Mr. Cody’s yard.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Ordinance 14-2010.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Rintz
and Spinney. Nays: Trustees Pedian and Poor. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance M-15-2010 – Disposing of Certain Personal Property Owned by
the Village of Winnetka – Introduction. Mr. Saunders reviewed his agenda
report, which described the Village equipment that is past its useful life and
is being replaced. He reported that the vehicles would be disposed of at the
NWMC auction on October 9.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance M-15-
2010. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, commented that while the Village’s cash reserves are
healthy and the Village should be proud of the auditor’s good report, the
Council needs to keep pension funding in mind and also consider using the
reserves for drainage improvements, instead of discussing a stormwater drainage
fee.
Trustee Greable thanked Mr. Kates for his diligence in following the issues,
and he observed that the Trustees are well aware of the state of the pension
funds and the need for careful consideration of capital projects. Trustees
Johnson and Pedian agreed, noting that the Council puts in many hours of hard
work.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Radio Fire Alarm Systems – Direct Connects. Deputy Chief Laureys
reviewed the results of an RFP that was issued after the April Study Session
discussion about direct connect fire alarm systems. He explained the advantages
of a radio system over the current dedicated copper phone lines currently in
use, and asked the Council for direction on owning vs. renting the system, as
well as an amount for the monthly fee charged to subscribers. He recommended
owning the system and all of its components, as well as charging a user fee to
offset some of the Village’s costs for dispatching and also for future
equipment purchases.
Lt. Ripka distributed a draft flyer to notify businesses and building owners of
the switch to the radio alarm system, and also handed out a comparison of what
fees other communities charge for direct connect radio systems.
The Council discussed the radio system, and Deputy Chief Laureys confirmed that
the new system will be a positive change for the businesses, especially in
light of the fact that phone companies are moving towards phasing out the
dedicated copper line system. He said residences are welcome to connect to the
new system, and that the more radios that are put into service, the more
revenue the Village would receive.
After more discussion, the Council reached consensus in favor of moving forward
with the radio alarm system and keeping it Village-owned, charging a monthly
fee of $55.
Attorney Janega stated that the rates would be set by resolution and that the
Village can reserve the right to adjust rates, and noted that rates can be
revisited when the annual rate resolution is considered.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to award a contract to install
all equipment and services as provided by RFP 010-010 to Fox Valley. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Bid 10-016b: Village Hall Masonry Restoration. Mr. Saunders reported on
the bid results for the Village Hall masonry restoration, and recommended
against awarding the project to the lowest bidder, since a reference check
revealed concerns with their subcontractor and supporting financial statements.
He recommended awarding the bid to the next lowest bidder, GC Masonry, as their
references indicated that the company is conscientious and does good work.
Reporting on his investigation of LEED Certification, Mr. Saunders reported
that grant money is only available on a major renovation, for a project at a
level of Silver or better, and that the maximum grant amount is $40,000-
$50,000. He indicated that the interior renovation could possibly qualify if
enough points can be generated to reach Silver certification.
Trustee Rintz commented that the Village’s costs would have been much higher if
the bids for the Village Hall restoration project had not been split up, and he
thanked Mr. Saunders for his efforts overseeing multiple contractors, which
will entail extra work.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to award bid for the
Village Hall Masonry Restoration to GC Masonry & Construction, Inc., for the
amount of $347,800. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Multi-Purpose Refuse Scooter Vehicle Purchase and Request to Waive
Formal Bid Requirements. Mr. Saunders requested a waiver of formal bid
requirements, as it is extremely difficult to write an exact specification for
the vehicle. He said Staff had worked with suppliers and vendors to get a truck
that will work with the Village’s other equipment, and that due diligence had
been exercised to get a low price.
There being no questions or discussion, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee
Pedian, moved to waive the formal bid requirements and award the purchase of a
Cushman Turf Truckster Multi-Purpose Refuse Scooter Vehicle to Burris Equipment
for $24,275. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable,
Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Policy Direction: Village Vehicle Sticker Design. Mr. McKee said each
year since 2004 the Village has awarded the design of the Village vehicle
sticker to various Winnetka organizations to commemorate their anniversaries or
other important dates. He noted that in the past few years the Village has been
receiving 5 to 7 applications from organizations wanting to be placed on the
sticker, and that it is getting more difficult to choose which worthy entity
will get the honor each year.
Mr. McKee said he had surveyed surrounding communities, and reported a
preference among finance directors to either use the same design every year or
to work with schools to create a design. He asked the Council for their
thoughts about having a contest in a Winnetka school for 2013 and beyond.
Several Trustees were in favor of asking the BCDC or the Chamber of Commerce
for their input before using schoolchildren. Noting a variety of opinions on
the Council, President Tucker asked Mr. McKee to come back with a list of
organizations that have applied for the 2013 sticker, and the Council will
narrow it down.
   e)   Policy Direction: Caucus Request for Study of Government Efficiencies.
Attorney Janega reported that the 2009 Caucus Platform called for creating an
ad hoc committee to study government efficiency. She noted that the Village
President has scheduled an intergovernmental meeting for November 1 st , which
could be a catalyst for discussion and study of the issue. She pointed out that
her agenda report included a list from each Village department detailing
examples of intergovernmental cooperation and the economies of scale that have
been achieved. Referring to the Caucus survey’s comments about the separate
Village and Park District facilities on Willow Road, she explained that the
Park District declined the Village’s invitation to participate in a joint
project at the time the Public Works facility was being constructed at 1390
Willow, but that the Village later provided the Park District with land south
of the Village’s building for the Park District’s own facility.
Attorney Janega explained that all of the Village’s taxing bodies have specific
statutory functions and each has its respective budget, along with its own
specific responsibility to the taxpayers. She noted that the different
government bodies work together to achieve efficiencies when they can, but
consolidating the functions of the bodies is not limited under Illinois law to
areas where public bodies provide comparable services or use comparable
facilities or equipment.
Trustee Greable commented that if the Council is doing its job right, it
focuses on efficiencies at budget time.
President Tucker said the list in the packet describes a lot of cost savings
that she had been unaware of.
The Trustees were generally in favor of letting the intergovernmental group
discuss the issue and perhaps identify some areas for improvement.
Tim Cawley, 700 Blackthorn & Caucus Council, criticized the separate Park
District and Village facilities, said he was not impressed by the agenda report
illustrating cost-saving measures already taken, and recommended hiring an
impartial consultant to assess what could be done.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported that the Council has spent
the summer working on the manager search. She noted that the NWMC will resume
meetings this fall and that she and Trustee Greable are the liaisons.
President Tucker announced that Flags would be planted on Friday (September 10)
at the Village Green to commemorate 9/11 this Saturday.
Finally, President Tucker reported that Metra has updated its new train
schedule to make additional stops for New Trier students. She added that she
had written to the U.S. Post Office about the closing of the Hubbard Woods
postal station, but has not received a response, and the station is due to
close later this month.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Spinney reported that she had attended the Board meeting at
Red Center with Chief Smith, and she thanked him for his help orienting her to
the Red Center.
      ii)   Trustee Johnson reported that the BCDC would meet Thursday at 7:
15AM.
      iii)   Trustee Rintz reported that the Plan Commission is continuing its
discussions on Affordable Housing.
      iv)   Trustee Pedian reminded the public that she and Trustee Greable are
studying compensation for Village staff in light of future efficiencies. She
noted that a benefit of term limits was that new eyes were looking at issues
every four years.
      v)   Trustee Poor reported that the EFC would meet Wednesday, and that a
draft demolition recycling ordinance should be ready soon for Council
discussion.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Interim Manager Rosenthal reported that since the new Freedom
of Information Act rules went into effect on January 1, 2010, the Village has
received 258 FOIA requests, and the Village’s primary FOIA officer estimates
that she spends approximately 50% of her time responding to these requests.
This does not include the time spent by other staff members, including the
department heads and Village Attorney.
Manager Rosenthal also announced that the Village has been awarded a $100,000
Energy Efficiency and Conservation Block Grant, which was applied for by the
Water & Electric Department. She said the grant money will allow the Village to
perform energy efficiency audits of all municipal owned buildings and retrofit
approximately 50 streetlights with energy efficient lighting technologies.
Lastly, Manager Rosenthal reported that Fire Chief Scott Smith is retiring
after 33 years of serving the Village, and that a search is being conducted for
a new Fire Chief.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters and Appointments to Public Office, pursuant to Section 2(c)(3) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Pedian seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive
Session at 10:40 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 11:58 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adjourn the meeting. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:59
p.m.
         
Recording Secretary
September 14, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
September 14, 2010
(Approved: October 5, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 14, 2020, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:34 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Interim Village Manager Liz
Rosenthal, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development
Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director of Community Development Brian Norkus,
Planning Assistant Jill Morgan and approximately 16 persons in the audience.
2)    Appendix Six of Winnetka’s 2020 Comprehensive Plan – Annual Update of
Plan Recommendations.
President Tucker welcomed members of the Plan Commission, BCDC, and other
Committees who were in attendance, and she invited Becky Hurley, Chair of the
Plan Commission, to address the Council.
Ms. Hurley thanked the Council for their time and asked Mr. Norkus to give his
remarks first.
Mr. Norkus said the drafters of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan had devised the
review of Appendix 6 as a strategy to keep the plan current and fresh, and to
keep the recommendations from becoming dated. He observed that the Plan
Commission had made the decision to change how Appendix 6 has traditionally
been reviewed by deconstructing the entire process and building a new one, with
the result that there is now a more comprehensive list of issues facing the
Village, along with the Commission’s recommendations.
Mr. Norkus indicated that during the course of the review, the high priority
recommendations were falling increasingly in the area of improving the downtown
and business districts, which is not surprising given the state of the national
economy. He pointed out that issues related to the business districts have
surfaced in recent years, such as the need to enhance the pedestrian
environment, creating solutions to parking issues and rehabbing the Winnetka
Metra station.
Mr. Norkus observed that the BCDC was invited to join in the review after it
was found that many of the Appendix 6 issues seemed to converge with the
interests of the BCDC. He listed the Plan Commission’s three recommendations
for the Council: i) engage in a strategic planning process for the downtown
business districts; ii) completion of the voluntary residential design
guidelines; and iii) complete amendments to the Affordable Housing Plan.
Mr. Norkus noted that an executive summary from the Village of Glen Ellyn had
been included in the agenda materials, since it has similarities to Winnetka,
and it is hoped that the Village can learn from their process. He said it is
too early to get into detail about how a strategic plan might be created, and
that the first order of business is to conduct an analysis of Winnetka’s trade
area in order to reassess where ’’’’Winnetka’’ stands in the retail, commercial
and office marketplace.
Ms. Hurley explained that in the years since the Comprehensive Plan was
drafted, Appendix 6 had become large, unwieldy, and difficult to comprehend;
therefore, the Commission had revamped the entire document for ease of use. She
said three key objectives had surfaced, and since a majority of the issues
related to the business districts, the downtown strategic plans had been placed
first. She noted that the Plan Commission wants to focus on assisting the
Council and keeping the lines of communication open, and she asked for their
guidance on the direction they were taking.
The Council discussed the concept of a business district strategic plan with
input from members of the Plan Commission, BCDC and EFC who were in the
audience, including Jason Harris, Scott Madlener, John Jansson, Midge Powell,
Mark Kurensky, Louise Holland and Arthur Braun. The major points brought up for
consideration were: i) the timing is right for an examination of the commercial
districts, so that as the economy improves, there could be some projects that
are ready to be started; ii) although the Comprehensive Plan needs updating,
the document is flexible and is a vision for the future and should be used a
such; iii) the seismic shifts in technology, the economy, the environment and
energy all need to be taken into account when examining how to help the
business community; and iv) the strategic plan is not going to be a rehash of
the Streetscape process.
The Council had a discussion about the need for a strategic plan for the
business districts, and it was agreed that better communication with the
community will be a crucial piece of the process, in order to help people
understand how all the pieces fit together. The Council generally agreed with
the direction the Plan Commission was going in with its recommendations, and it
was pointed out that a strategic plan needs to be thought of in the context of
infrastructure and revenue streams. It was suggested that the Council use the
expertise and talent of its boards and commissions to do the deep conversations
on the issues and to attend their meetings whenever practical.
Mr. Norkus said the Glen Ellyn process needs more study, and he added that the
Village has collected a lot of data and has identified specific concerns
already, so that a strategic planning process need not be started from the very
beginning.
Mr. D’Onofrio said the community’s values must be identified and the objectives
agreed upon before an action plan can be developed, and he noted that the
process needs to involve all the stakeholders, as the route is multi-layered
and interactive.
The Council agreed that the Plan Commission was off to a great start, but that
the information will need to be assimilated, and the Council will need the new
Village Manager in place to help guide the process.
Ms. Hurley suggested that the Plan Commission pull together resources such as
the consultant reports that have been written about the business districts,
clarify its recommendations, and come back for more discussion. She noted that
not all of the recommendations will involve budgeting funds, and that some
policy decisions may have an impact at no cost to the Village.
Attorney Janega observed that there is a difference between a strategic plan
and comprehensive plan, as the latter is a vision statement that helps guide a
strategic plan, and she noted that the Council deals with a mini strategic plan
every year at budget time.
3)   Executive Session.
Trustee Pedian moved to adjourn into Executive Session for the purpose of
discussing matters pertaining to the selection and qualifications of a new
Village Manager, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) and 2(c)(3), of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and
Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 9:41 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:59 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 11:
00 p.m.
