Recording Secretary
September 21, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
September 21, 2010
(Approved: October 19, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 21, 2010, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, Chris Rintz and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Community Development Mike
D'Onofrio, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant Public Works
Director Steve Auth, Planning Assistant Ann Klaassen, and approximately 19
persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   September 29, 2010, Special Council Meeting. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   October 5, 2010 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present,
with the exception of Trustee Poor, indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   October 12, 2010, Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
President Tucker announced that Item 10(a), a discussion about the Special Use
Process, has been removed from the Agenda and will be considered at a later
date after the Plan Commission, ZBA and BCDC have reviewed the matter.
President Tucker also extended her sympathy to the family of Robert Dini, who
recently passed away, and she added that he was active and interested in
Village civic matters and his participation in Council meetings will be missed.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote. the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney,
Rintz, Pedian, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   August 17, 2010, Regular Meeting.
      ii)   September 7, 2010, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1669 and 1670. Approving Warrant List No. 1669 in
the amount of $2,347,330.76, and Warrant List No. 1670 in the amount of
$533,594.62.
   c)   Ordinance M-15-2010: Disposing of Certain Personal Property Owned by
the Village of Winnetka – Adoption. Approves the disposal of four pieces of
equipment owned by the Village.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Landmark Preservation Awards.
Louise Holland, Chair of the LPC, gave a presentation describing this year’s
award winners, noting that out of 15 applications received last spring, eight
awards were given. The eight award recipients were:
·   735 Ardsley Road (Rehabilitation); Owners: Greg and Sandy Welch; Architect:
Chip Hackley, Hackley & Associates Architects, Kenilworth
·   657 Ash Street (Rehabilitation); Owners: Chuck and Mary Happ; Architect:
Behles + Behles, Evanston
·   594 Elm Street (Rehabilitation); Owner: Elena Dreiske; Architect: Benvenuti
& Stein
·   804 Lincoln Avenue (Rehabilitation); Owners: Steve and Kathleen Patterson;
Architect: Mark Ver Bryck, Ver Bryck Architects, Northfield
·   1173 Tower Road (Rehabilitation); Owners: Kevin and Maria Lynch; Architect:
Chip Hackley, Hackley & Associates Architects, Kenilworth
·   1014 Dinsmore Road (New Construction); Owners: Bill Coe and Theresa Malin;
Architect: Doug Reynolds, The Reynolds Group, Northfield
·   518 Hawthorn Lane (New Construction); Owner: Mary Jo LaViolette; Architect:
Behles + Behles, Evanston
·   245 Linden Street (New Construction); Owners: Mark and Mary Nomellini;
Architect: Diana Melichar, Melichar Architects, Lake Forest
Ms. Holland congratulated the homeowners and thanked Ann Klaassen for her hard
work as the LPC’s staff representative. She also thanked the Council for the
opportunity to appear before them and present the work of the Commission.
The Trustees commended the homeowners for their efforts to preserve Winnetka’s
character.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance MC-5-2010: Construction & Demolition Debris Recycling –
Introduction. Mr. Saunders reported that, at the urging of the Winnetka Caucus,
the Village’s Environmental and Forestry Commission (EFC) identified recycling
of construction debris as a priority during their strategic planning process.
He observed that after many months of diligent effort by the EFC, Village staff
and various stakeholders, a proposed program has been developed for the
Council’s consideration.
Mr. Saunders described the key components of the Construction Demolition Debris
(CDD) program contained in his agenda report, and he noted that staff has
identified open issues that need the Council’s direction: i) Should there be a
permit fee, and if so, how much? and ii) should a deposit fee be charged, and
if so, how much? He recommended the requirement of a $250 permit fee to cover
staff time and also the requirement of a $2,000 deposit to guarantee
compliance.
Mr. Saunders observed that the program’s initial target of a recycling plan
that recycles 50% of construction debris can be increased over the years as
developers become familiar with and accustomed to the provisions of the
ordinance.
Attorney Janega reviewed the provisions of the ordinance with the Council and
noted that not only would she review it once again before adoption, but it
would also be reviewed by Community Development and possibly the EFC.
The Council discussed how best to increase the threshold for recycling from 50%
to a higher amount, and Mr. Saunders recommended keeping it on a calendar year,
with the ordinance becoming effective on January 1, 2011, and increasing the
recycling amount a year later, as that should ensure time for staff to devise
their method for calculating the debris weights.
The Council were generally in agreement with giving contractors a chance to
adjust to the requirements, especially smaller contractors who may not
currently be recycling construction materials.
Debbie Ross, 921 Tower; and Richard Kates, 1326 Tower; spoke in favor of the
ordinance.
The Council agreed with Mr. Saunders’ recommendations with regard to the
deposit, permit fee, and the effective date of the ordinance, as well as the
date to increase the amount required to be recycled.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-5-
2010. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Attorney Janega indicated that the ordinance would possibly be reviewed by the
EFC one last time, and that a draft may be shown to local contractors, as a way
to capture any further refinements.
   b)   Ordinance MC-6-2010: Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to “Fire
Prevention and Life Safety Codes,” and “Security and Fire Detection Systems” –
Introduction. Attorney Janega reported that the subject ordinance remedies a
scrivener’s error contained in the Village’s Fire Prevention and Life Safety
Code. She noted that the Ordinance also clarifies several provisions in Chapter
15.16 and amends the definition of “direct connection alarm” contained in
Chapter 8.04, in furtherance of the Council’s authorization of a contract to
install a Village-owned radio fire alarm system.
Attorney Janega reviewed the amendments, and said the most significant change
is in Section 15.16.050, where language was inserted to make clear that the
provisions apply to a building or any part thereof, so as to be in agreement
with the State Fire Marshall’s definition of “building.” She said on behalf of
the absent Winnetka’s Fire Chief that the requirement to bring sprinkler
systems into commercial buildings has been in the Village Code since the
1970’s, and the buildings that are not up to date are the ones that have not
been renovated or rehabbed since then. After noting that most owners have
complied over time, Attorney Janega pointed out that it is the Council’s
prerogative to keep the provision in the code or remove it.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower, said he thought there were economic consequences
with regard to the requirement of sprinkler systems upon a change of use in a
commercial property, asked the Village to explore ways to help with the cost of
bringing the water pipes into the building from the street, and recommended
letting the BCDC or Chamber have some input on the issue.
After the Council asked questions and made their comments, Trustee Johnson,
seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-6-2010. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
   c)   Ordinance MC-7-2010: Amending the Village Code as it Pertains to
Certain Temporary Signs – Introduction. Attorney Janega reported that in
response to some over-sized election signs that were observed during the
primary election season last spring, some clarifications were made to Chapter
15.64 to eliminate ambiguity with regard to temporary signs. She reviewed the
amendments to Section 15.64.090 of the Sign Code that would provide a temporary
fix for the upcoming election season, prior to a new state law that will become
effective on January 1, 2011, that will affect the Village’s sign code.
During the ensuing discussion, several Trustees expressed concerns about the
ordinance being perceived as limiting residents’ free speech so close to the
election. A consensus was reached to table the ordinance until after the first
of the year.
   d)   Resolution R-31-2010: Café Aroma Class A Liquor License – Adoption.
Attorney Janega said this is a straightforward application to sell beer and
wine with meals at Café Aroma, currently located at 561 ½ Lincoln Ave. She
noted that Café Aroma plans to relocate this fall to a larger space at 749 Elm,
which is currently under construction, and that since the new space is not
ready for the required inspections, it is too early to deal with issues such as
the dates for the occupancy of the new space, the surrender of the Lincoln
Avenue license and the issuance of the license for 749 Elm. She also noted that
the applicant has not applied for a sidewalk liquor service rider; therefore
the Resolution contains a condition prohibiting the sale of beer and wine at
Café Aroma’s sidewalk tables.
After the Council confirmed with the applicant, Mitra Ryndak, that the code’s
requirements regarding a hostess and waitstaff will be met, Trustee Poor,
seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adopt Resolution R-31-2010. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
Beth Ann Papoutsis, 720 Prospect, asked what can be done to get an ordinance
passed that would place requirements on property owners who sell to developers.
Attorney Janega indicated that in her opinion the Village cannot legally
require someone to sell their property a certain way, and said a future Study
Session is a better forum for discussing the issue of demolitions.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
   a)   Policy Direction: Zoning Retail Overlay District. [No action: removed
from the agenda.]
   b)   Policy Direction: 250-Foot Notification. Mr. D’Onofrio reported that
the issue of meeting notification was raised earlier in the year during the
North Shore Country Day School special use application process, when some of
the neighbors were concerned that no notices were sent when the matter went
before the Plan Commission and Design Review Board. After reviewing a matrix of
current notification requirements from his agenda report, he observed that the
biggest issues surface with special use applications, because of the need for
multiple meetings by several boards. He reviewed several suggested changes from
his agenda report for the Council to consider.
After a thorough Council discussion, it was agreed that all the meetings
involved in a special use application, as well as large DRB cases, should be
formally noticed in the same way that zoning variation requests are noticed by
the ZBA, and perhaps a device could be used that would draw attention to the
envelope to heighten awareness of the issue.
   c)   Policy Direction: Signature Requirements for Special Use Objections.
Attorney Janega reported that some confusion arose during the consideration of
a special use application by Anitfield, LLC as to the meaning of the words
“signed and acknowledged” for written protests, and also about the timing of
submitting such protests. She reviewed the provisions of the Notary Public Act
from her agenda report, and recommended using the same guidelines for the
definition of “signed and acknowledged” for official protests in the Village.
She advised the Council to set the timeframe to turn in objections at the ZBA
level, instead of having protests come in at the 11 th hour when a matter is
before the Council.
After a brief Council discussion, it was suggested that staff come back with
recommendations for how to notify residents about the timeframe for making a
formal protest.
Attorney Janega said she would discuss the matter with the Director of
Community Development and come back to the Council with some recommendations.
11)   Reports
   a)   Village President. Tucker reported that she had attended the House
Pension Subcommittee's hearing on public safety pension reform to discuss
Police & Firefighter Pension Funds. She said she would distribute a
presentation from the Coalition for Pension Fairness to the Council and make it
available on the Village’s website as well.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported on the recent Chamber of Commerce and NWMC
meetings.
      ii)   Trustee Johnson reported on the last BCDC meeting.
      iii)   Trustee Pedian reported that there is a new retail business in
town and welcomed them to Winnetka.
   c)   Attorney. Attorney Janega reported that on Friday she would be
moderating the attorneys CLE program at the Illinois Municipal League, and also
making presentations on the Rules of Professional Conduct as they apply to
municipal practitioners and on dealing the new FOIA Public Access Counselor.
   d)   Manager. Manager Rosenthal reported that the Village is resurfacing
portions of Elm, Oak, and Spruce Streets, and that the project may
significantly disrupt normal business in the West Elm business district for a
two to three week period. She noted that the Public Works Director has worked
with the Village’s contractor, Johnson Paving, to develop a plan which should
minimize disruption to the businesses.
Mr. Saunders reviewed a plan that has been devised to commence night
construction in order to minimize disruption to business owners. He noted that
the work cannot safely be accomplished under traffic and that the Village Code
authorizes the Village Manager to authorize night construction for public
projects. “He observed that the plan should shave about a week off the
completion time, will save the business owners from losing large amounts of
business, and the premium for night construction should only add about $5,500
to the cost of the project.”  
12)   Appointments.
None.
13)   Executive Session.
Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters and Appointments to Public Office pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) and 2(c)
(3) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Pedian seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The Council adjourned into
Executive Session at 10:49 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 12:05 a.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Absent:
None. Also present: Interim Village Manager Liz Rosenthal and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 12:
06 a.m.
            
