considered: removed from agenda.]
   e)   Change Order for Three-Phase Transformer, Wesco. [Not considered:
removed from agenda.]
   f)   Resolution R-32-2010: Cook County Energy Efficiency and Conservation
Block Grant Program Subrecipient Agreement – Adoption. Authorizes the execution
of the Energy Efficiency and Conservation Block Grant Program Subrecipient
Agreement, substantially in the form attached.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Poor and Rintz.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
None
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
Glenn Weaver, 574 Lincoln, gave some information that he said he had received
from the Illinois State Fire Marshall regarding mandatory sprinkler systems.
President Tucker asked him to send a copy of his remarks and any correspondence
from the Fire Marshall’s office.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   2010 Property Tax Levy Analysis. Mr. McKee reported that the Village’s
share of the total tax bill sent to residents has declined 22.2% since 1993,
and that Winnetka’s share of a typical property tax bill is currently 13.3% of
the total. He noted that income taxes from the state have declined and are not
expected to rise in the near future, and interest rates are at a low point not
seen in a generation, but the Village has nevertheless kept many of its fees
unchanged over the years in an effort to keep homeowners’ costs down.
Mr. McKee indicated that the property tax bills, which are usually sent out by
Cook County around August, may not be mailed until December or later, making it
impossible to know what new development will contribute to property tax
increases for the 2009 tax year. He observed that analysis of building permits
issued by the Village leads him to predict that the Village should still
receive a new development property tax revenue increase in the 1.3 % – 1.6%
range. After reviewing the framework for evaluating property tax increase
requests from his agenda report, Mr. McKee recommended that the Village capture
all of the CPI increase plus the increase for the total amount of new
development allowed for 2010, for an overall levy increase of 4.0% to 4.3%.
Mr. McKee remarked that the delayed tax bills would cause a cash flow issue, as
opposed to a revenues lost issue, but since investment returns are so low the
lost investment income would not be substantial.
Trustee Greable asked Mr. McKee to explain for the public how the unfunded
amounts are arrived at.
Mr. McKee explained that he uses extremely conservative assumptions to predict
the amount of investment return on the pension funds; therefore, the funded
amount looks smaller than it would if a larger investment return assumption was
used, as most other municipalities do. He noted that Winnetka’s pension funding
is in much better shape than most other localities and that, if the Village
used generally accepted actuarial assumptions as is done in other
municipalities, the reported funded amounts in the pension would be higher.
Trustee Greable observed that Mr. McKee’s reports would be much easier for
citizens to understand if he were to include an example of a real tax bill
which was broken down showing the amount of the total bill that is received by
each of the Village’s taxing bodies. He said his tax bill of $17,500 showed
roughly $2,300 going to the Village and approximately $11,375 going to the
schools. He made the point that the Village’s portion of the property tax bill
is 13%, while District 36 and New Trier H.S. take about 65%, and he added that
the Village provides all services and capital improvements out of that 13%. He
noted that the Council is very careful with the Village’s revenues and does a
fair job of holding down property taxes, especially when Winnetka’s share is
put in context of the entire property tax bill.
The Council continued to discuss the levy and ask questions, after which Mr.
McKee indicated that he had given a range for the tax levy increase for the
Council to consider, and that the levy would be introduced at the first Council
meeting in December.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to increase the 2010
property tax levy at an amount from $13,258,339 to $13,296,584, a projected
increase of 2.7%, or $72 for a taxpayer with an annual $20,000 total property
tax bill, with the overall percentage increase in the levy and new development
projected to be 4.0 – 4.3% . By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees
Poor and Rintz.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported the following: (i) the
Village has won a Best of the Best Award in the category of intergovernmental
cooperation for its efforts in cooperating with the Park District for its new
Service Center at the Public Works Yards on Willow Road; (ii) asked residents
to participate in the strategic planning surveys being sent out by the Park
District and the Library; and (iii) invited residents to attend the Police and
Fire Open House on Saturday, October 2.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Spinney reported that the Library Board and its Strategic
Planning Committee are working to finalize their community survey, and she
encouraged residents to complete the survey, which will come in the mail.
      ii)   Trustee Johnson reported that at the BCDC’s special meeting on
Sept. 30 th Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio gave a presentation
that showed that while the rate of unoccupied commercial spaces has risen since
the economic collapse of 2008, the vacancy rate has declined from 9.8% in May
2009 to 7.3% as of August 2010.
      iii)   Trustee Pedian reported on the latest meeting of the Village Hall
restoration committee and on the progress of the compensation study, and she
announced that the Park District is meeting this week to discuss final plans
for the playground renovation on the Village Green.
   c)   Attorney. No report.
   d)   Manager. Manager Rosenthal reminded the audience that the Council
Meeting scheduled for Election Day, November 2, is rescheduled for Thursday,
November 4.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Johnson moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Pending and
Probable Litigation, Leasing of Village Property and Discussion of Closed
Session Minutes, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(4), 2(c)(11)and 2(c)(21)
respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Pedian seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson,
Pedian, and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Poor and Rintz. The Council
adjourned into Executive Session at 8:30 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 9:25 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, and Jeni Spinney.
Absent: Trustees, Chris Rintz and King Poor. Also present: Interim Village
Manager Liz Rosenthal and Village Attorney Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Poor and Rintz. The meeting adjourned
at 9:26 p.m.
            
   Recording Secretary
October 12, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
October 12, 2010
(Approved: November 4, 2010)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 12, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Convene Study Session.
President Tucker called the Study Session to order at 7:34 p.m. Present:
Trustees Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Linda Pedian, King Poor, and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: Trustee Chris Rintz. Also in attendance: Acting Village
Manager Liz Rosenthal, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed
McKee, Public Works Director Steve Saunders and approximately 10 persons in the
audience.
President Tucker welcomed Rob Bahan, incoming Village Manager, who was present
to hear the discussion.
2)   Update: Proposed Multi-Faceted Stormwater Management Strategy.
Mr. Saunders reported that, following serious flooding in September of 2008,
when 8.1 inches of rain fell over a 40-hour period, Council directed staff to
make stormwater management strategy a priority. He reviewed: (i)
recommendations from the 2009 flood risk reduction study of southwest Winnetka
prepared by Christopher B. Burke Engineering, Ltd. (CBBEL); (ii) the results of
CBBEL’s 2010 drainage survey of the remaining areas of the Village; (iii) the
Council’s May 2010 Stormwater Management Strategy; and (iv) the results of the
Skokie River pump station feasibility study.
Mr. Saunders explained that the pump station project would increase the pump’s
capacity by approximately 75%. He described the process to upgrade the facility
and estimated the project cost at approximately $700,000, plus another $35,000
for electrical improvements at the site.
Mr. Saunders discussed the feasibility of improvements at Duke Child Field and
Hibbard Road and concluded that detention is a necessary part of any project in
that area, because permits to dump more outfall to the river are almost
impossible to obtain. He reviewed the results of the new 2010 drainage survey,
which the Council can use to determine additional areas that may need further
study and possible drainage improvements.
Mr. Saunders recommended that the Council: (i) proceed with the pump expansion
at the Skokie River; (ii) start joint discussions with New Trier High School
and the Park District about detention in Duke Child Field; (iii) do further
study and modeling of six additional problem areas identified in the 2010 CBBEL
survey; (iv) and delay a financing study until all possible additional
improvements have been identified.
Responding to a question from Trustee Poor, Mr. Saunders said that the extra
pump capacity would keep the water level in the forest preserve drainage ditch
lower, allowing more water to drain into that ditch from the approximately 40%
of Village storm sewers, which drain into that ditch.
Trustee Greable was in favor of starting the pump improvements immediately,
noting that the Village has the money for such a capital project and that the
Comprehensive Plan places a priority on adding to the pumping capabilities at
the Skokie River.
In answer to a question from Trustee Johnson about the impact of expanded
pumping capacity on downstream communities, Mr. Saunders stated that one of the
permit conditions for the pump station is that the Village shut off the pumps
if the river level exceeds a certain elevation.
Trustee Pedian asked about the drainage ditch at Sunset and DeWindt. Mr.
Saunders explained that although the ditch flows across private property, it is
a significant portion of the Village’s public storm sewer system and takes in
water from Indian Hill and everything south of Willow and north of Hill.
Trustee Spinney was in favor going forward with Mr. Saunders’ recommendations,
and starting the pump expansion project as soon as possible.
Trustee Poor asked if the effect of covering more and more green space with
impermeable surface could be measured. Mr. Saunders replied that conducting
such a survey would be a monumental project, but that comparing GIS maps could
show some of the progression of impermeable surface in the Village.
President Tucker asked if the extra pump capacity would have been helpful
during the September 2008 storm. Mr. Saunders said there is very little that
can be done with a storm of that magnitude, as it was a 100-year storm and the
Village is working on improvements for a 5-10 year storm. He said it is
possible that the duration of the flooding could have been shorter once the
rain stopped.
President Tucker cautioned that expectations have to be kept in line with what
any drainage improvements can actually deliver.
Mitch Wywiorski 1052 Westmoor; Jud Porter, 970 Sunset; Dr. Jim Feld, 260 White
Oak; Ann Wilder, 1096 Spruce; and Richard Kates, 1326 Tower; had questions and
comments. It was suggested that impermeable surface restrictions be examined to
make sure the Village is doing all it can to mitigate runoff problems which
contribute to flooding.
There was a brief discussion about the efficacy of refining zoning regulations,
in an effort to combat flooding problems with every tool at the Village’s
disposal. Trustee Poor suggested that the EFC examine creative ways for
individuals to address the drainage issue, with rain barrels and rain gardens,
for example.
The Council generally agreed that expansion of the Skokie River pump should
begin as soon as possible, and the six additional problem areas identified in
the most recent CBBEL survey should be studied further. Trustee Spinney
requested a special Study Session to discuss the avenues for financing drainage
improvements.
President Tucker directed Mr. Saunders to commence working on his
recommendations, as there was a consensus of Council approval.
3)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:45 p.m.
October 19, 2010
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
October 19, 2010
