$622,674.09.
   c)   Change Order: Lead Service Replacements – Biagi Plumbing. Authorizes
the execution of a change order with Biagi Plumbing in the amount of $30,000
for the replacement of lead water services through March 31, 2011, at the unit
prices contained in Bid #09-005.
   d)   Resolution R-33-2010: ILWARN Emergency Mutual Aid Agreement – Adoption.
Authorizes the execution of a Mutual Aid Agreement with the Illinois Water &
Wastewater Agency Response Network.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried
unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Poor, Johnson, Spinney, Rintz, Pedian, and Greable.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance MC-5-2010: Construction & Demolition Debris Recycling –
Adoption. Attorney Janega reported that the ordinance has been amended to
incorporate comments from the Council, EFC and staff since introduction at the
September 21 st Council Meeting. She reviewed the substantive amendments, as
well as technical modifications that were made to clarify the language and keep
it in agreement with the rest of the Village Code.
Trustee Poor thanked the Environmental and Forestry Commission for their work
in advancing an idea raised by the Caucus, and especially thanked EFC member
Jan Bawden and Village staff for their work in taking a good idea from the EFC
and making it a reality. He noted that Winnetka is one of few Illinois
municipalities to have a demolition debris recycling ordinance, which shows the
progressive spirit of the Village.
President Tucker thanked Trustee Poor for his championing of environmental
causes.
There being no further comments or questions, Trustee Pedian, seconded by
Trustee Johnson, moved to amend Ordinance MC-5-2010 as presented by the Village
Attorney. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt, as amended,
Ordinance MC-5-2010 adding a new Chapter 15.54 to the Village Code and amending
other provisions of the Building Code to establish requirements for the
recycling of construction and demolition debris. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Resolution R-36-2010: Amending Construction Fee Schedule – Adoption.
Attorney Janega explained that this resolution is a companion to Ordinance MC-
5-2010 and amends the Village’s Fee Resolution to set the permit fee and
compliance deposit authorized by new Village Code Chapter 15.54.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Pedian, moved to adopt Resolution R-36-
2010 amending the Construction Fee Schedule to provide for a $250 permit fee
and a $2,000 compliance deposit for the construction and demolition debris
recycling program. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
   a)   Procedures for Special Use Permit Objections. Attorney Janega reported
that she had met with Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio in order to
come up with amendments to the Zoning Ordinance pertaining to special use
applications. She indicated that the phrase “signed and acknowledged” had been
included in the definitions section in Chapter 17.56, and she added that
provisions for submitting written protests had been laid out in a new, separate
section. She then reviewed her recommendations for amending the Zoning
Ordinance to clarify the formal protest procedures for special use
applications.
Trustee Greable suggested that Village staff create a protest form, to make it
easier for property owners to file their protest. Attorney Janega responded
that the form could then be put on the Village’s website.
The Council discussed the suggested amendments with Attorney Janega, who
explained: (i) what is meant by 20% of property owners; (ii) how the 250-foot
distance for notice is measured; (iii) the formal procedures for making a
record in front of the ZBA; and (iv) the reasons for suggesting that the
deadline for written protests be the first meeting at which the Council
considers the special use.
The Trustees directed that the procedures for lodging a formal complaint be
clearly stated and that staff consider ways that the notice envelope could be
marked so that people would know it contained important information.
Attorney Janega said she would discuss the Council’s concerns with Mr.
D’Onofrio and then address them in the draft zoning amendments and the public
hearing on the proposed amendments.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to direct the Village
Attorney to prepare an ordinance amending the special use procedures as
recommended by the Village Attorney. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Trustee Pedian, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to designate the Village
Council, sitting as a Committee of the Whole, to hold a public hearing on the
amendments to the special use procedures contained in the Zoning Ordinance. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian,
Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Poor, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to set the public hearing on
the proposed zoning amendment for Tuesday, December 7, 2010, and to direct
staff to issue the required notices. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Capital Budget Status Report. Mr. McKee reported on the updated
spreadsheet summarizing capital expenditures for the Village and stated there
was nothing unusual or outstanding to report. He commented that the Village
tends to budget for all potential capital projects, even though wherever
possible, staff defers capital projects that have already been budgeted, if no
harm will be done by waiting.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported that she recently attended
a NWMC presentation about the Illinois State Toll Highway Authority’s potential
future tollway projects. She said the Legislative Committee is encouraging
members to attend the Springfield veto session to provide support for
legislative priorities. She also announced that on November 1 st at 7:30PM
there will be an intergovernmental forum to discuss cooperation between
governmental bodies to reduce costs.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Greable reported that he attended the Chamber of Commerce
meeting, that the Chamber has started planning for the end-of-year sale, and
that the businesses are happy that night construction was used for the street
improvements in the West Elm district.
      ii)   Trustee Spinney reported that the Library District’s surveys would
be mailed around the end of the month and urged residents to complete them.
      iii)   Trustee Johnson reported that he had recently attended an
interesting presentation about the use of tasers.
      iv)   Trustee Poor reported that the EFC would begin working on other
projects now that the demolition debris recycling ordinance had been passed,
and distributed a sample of the new water and electric bills, which will graph
usage amounts to help people become aware of their energy footprint.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Manager Rosenthal had several reports: (i) the Village
Council’s first meeting in November would be held on Thursday, November 4 th so
as not to conflict with Election Day; (ii) electric sales are up approximately
8% from the four-year average, thanks to the hot summer; and water sales are
about 95% of the four-year average – about even with last year; and (iii)
although the bidding at the October 9 th NWMC auction was low, the Village’s
total exceed its minimum of $42,000 by $5,800.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Pedian moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss minutes of
closed session, pending and probable litigation and employee salary and
