respectively.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adopt Resolution R-20-
2011 appointing President Tucker and Mr. Bahan SWANCC representatives. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Greable, Johnson, Kates,
Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Resolution R-21-2011: Appointing Ed McKee as Village Treasurer –
Adoption. President Tucker explained that the Village Code requires the Village
Treasurer to be appointed every two years, and historically this position has
been filled by the Finance Director.
   Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adopt Resolution R-21-
2011 appointing Ed McKee Village Treasurer. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Greable, Johnson, Kates, Rintz and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Ordinance M-5-2011: Disposition of Surplus Electrical Equipment –
Introduction. Mr. Keys explained the Village’s policy regarding the destruction
or disposal of surplus Village property, that the portable 2,500 gallon storage
tank has been removed from service, and that authorization is needed to dispose
of it and other surplus electrical equipment.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Ordinance
M-5-2011 authorizing the disposal of surplus electrical equipment. By voice
vote, the motion carried unanimously.
   e)   Resolution R-16-2011: Preliminary Approval of Consolidation – 769 & 777
Locust – Adoption. Trustee Rintz recused himself from consideration of this
matter and left the dais.
   Mr. Norkus reviewed this request for preliminary approval of a proposed
conceptual plan for consolidation of 769 Locust and 777 Locust into a single
lot. Both lots are located in the R-4 zoning district, and the proposed
consolidation would make the resulting lot the largest and the widest on this
block of Locust. He added that the applicants propose to demolish the residence
and garage now on 769 Locust and expand the Applicants’ home across what is now
the property line. Because the proposed consolidation of the two lots creates a
zoning nonconformity, the applicants submitted an accompanying zoning variation
request. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals had voted in favor of
granting the variation.
   Mr. Norkus then reviewed applicable zoning and development trends and
regulations, and noted the corresponding increase in required side yard and
front yard setbacks as lots increase to assure that the scale of larger homes
is offset by larger setbacks and greater amounts of open space. He pointed out
that the increased lot area resulting from the proposed consolidation would
increase the maximum permitted gross floor area for 777 Locust from 3,840 sq.
ft., to 6,115 sq. ft., and that the proposed addition of 1,405 sq. ft., would
increase the size of the residence from 3,799 sq. ft to 5,193 sq. ft., and
would allow an additional 922 sq. ft. of GFA in the future without requiring
any zoning relief. In this instance, where a larger lot would be created within
a zoning district, the front setbacks for the underlying zoning district
continue to apply, so the enlarged property would continue to be subject to the
existing 30-ft front setback requirement.
   Mr. Norkus reported that Plan Commission evaluated the proposed
consolidation, noted that the proposed lot is larger than the neighboring lots,
and recommended preliminary approval of the consolidation be subject to
restrictive covenants to mitigate the creation of the nonconforming north side
yard as well as the imposition of a restrictive covenant to require a larger
front yard setback on future construction on the property. He noted that the
Commission also expressed concern that the consolidated lot would be closer in
size to lots in the R-2 zoning district and that it could be developed with the
bulk and density of the larger lot zoning district, while having smaller front
and side setbacks that correspond to the smaller R-4 and R-5 zoning districts.
   After an exchange of questions and answers between the Council and Mr.
Norkus and hearing from the applicants’ architects, Trustee Johnson, seconded
by Trustee Braun, moved to adopt Resolution R-16-2011 granting preliminary
approval of the proposed consolidation of 769 and ’’’’777 Locust Street’’,
subject to the restrictive covenants stated in the Resolution. By roll call
vote, the motion carried unanimously. Ayes: Trustees Johnson, Braun, Kates,
Spinney, and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Rintz [recused].
