MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
May 17, 2011
(Approved: June 21, 2011)
   A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
17, 2011, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:38 p.m. Present: Trustees
Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Richard Kates, and Jennifer Spinney. Absent:
Trustees Arthur Braun and Chris Rintz. Also present: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Mike
D’Onofrio, and approximately 8 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   Thursday, May 19, 2011, Special Executive Session. All of the Council
members present indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   May 24, 2011, Rescheduled Study Session. All of the Council members
present indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Greable, moved to approve the Agenda.
By roll call vote. the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Kates, Johnson,
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Rintz and Braun.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   April 12, 2011, Study Session.
      ii)   April 26, 2011, Rescheduled Council Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1701 and 1702. Approving Warrant List No. 1701 in
the amount of $937,987.23, and Warrant List No. 1702 in the amount of
$319,243.97.
   c)   Bid #11-017: Trenchless Lining of Sanitary Sewers – Municipal
Partnering Bid. Awards Winnetka’s portion of a shared bid for trenchless lining
of existing sanitary sewers to Insituform Technologies in the total amount of
$151,389.20.
   d)   Bid #11-013: 2011 Street Rehabilitation Program. Awards a contract to A
Lamp Concrete Contractors for the 2011 Street Rehabilitation Program, in the
amount of $809,579.76.
   e)   Bid #11-015: Traffic Signal Painting. Authorizes the Village Manager to
award a purchase order to Certa Pro Painters, in the amount of $29,570, to
paint traffic signals and streetlight poles in accordance with the terms and
conditions of Bid #11-015.
   f)   Ordinance M-5-2011: Disposition of Surplus Electrical Equipment –
Adoption. Authorizes the Village Manager to dispose of surplus electrical
equipment.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Kates, Johnson, and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Braun and Rintz.
6)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance M-6-2011: Zoning Variation – Village Green 525 Maple -
- Introduction.
   Mr. D’Onofrio explained that this Special Use Permit request from the
Winnetka Park District would allow the renovation of the Village Green
playground, including the repair, cleaning and repainting of the 1998 play
equipment, as well as a new layout to separate the play areas for older and
younger children. He noted that the renovations will bring the playground into
compliance with ADA regulations, and that the ZBA, DRB and Plan Commission had
all voted to recommend approval.
   Mr. Bob Smith, Superintendent of Parks, and Rick Schramm, the Park
District’s landscape architect, reviewed the plans, described the extensive
public meeting process that had been followed, explained that comments from
residents were taken into account, and noted the support from the Village’s
lower boards.
   After Council discussion of the project, it was suggested that introduction
of the ordinance could be waived to expedite the construction process.
   Attorney Janega explained that, given the already extensive public input on
the project, the Council has grounds to find that the additional time needed
for the second reading does not serve the public interest in this instance.
   Trustee Kates, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance M-6-2011. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adopt Ordinance M-6-
2011. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Kates, Johnson,
Spinney and Greable. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Braun and Rintz.
   b)   Ordinance M-7-2011: Zoning Variation – 12 Indian Hill – Introduction.
Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a zoning variation to allow
construction of a second detached 2-car garage on the Subject Property, which
will result in an east front yard setback variation of 60.84%. He noted that
the proposed new garage will be similar in size and design to the existing
garage, both will be served by a common driveway, and he added that the ZBA
voted to recommend approval.
   After a brief discussion, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney,
moved to introduce Ordinance M-7-2011. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Public Comment and Questions.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Policy Direction: Zoning Variation Request – ––––1034 Ash Street. Mr.
D’Onofrio reviewed this request for a gross floor area variation to construct a
one-story mudroom addition. He explained that the home has a legal
nonconforming gross floor area of 4,667.27 square feet, which is 1304.24 square
feet above the allowed maximum for this parcel in the R-5 Zoning District.
   Mr. D’Onofrio reported that, at its January 10, 2011, meeting, the ZBA had
previously recommended denial of an application for gross floor area and roofed
lot coverage variations. That application was subsequently withdrawn and the
current request reduced the size of the original proposal by 271.81 square
feet. The ZBA again recommended denial at its April 11, 2011, meeting. He
reviewed zoning amendments made in 2002 which changed how basements are
calculated into the gross floor area (GFA) based on the height of the first
floor above grade, explaining that the Subject Property became legally
nonconforming with respect to GFA at that time.
   The Council asked Mr. D’Onofrio a series of questions, and then the
applicant, William Franklin, was asked to give his comments.
   Mr. Franklin commented on the unfairness of being required to comply with
the 2002 zoning amendments, when they do not apply to buildings built before
the cutoff date in the ordinance. He added that the impermeable surface would
be reduced if the variation request is granted, as the existing concrete deck
will be replaced and a floodable crawlspace would be installed. He stressed the
importance of a mudroom for a home with a detached garage, and distributed
pictures of several other homes on Ash Street with nonconforming basements to
illustrate that his request would not change the character of the neighborhood.
   There was a discussion about how flood plain regulations help to control the
rate of storm runoff into the Village’s storm sewer system.
   Attorney Janega explained that the Zoning Ordinance was amended in response
to the construction of box-like homes that were maximizing the use of GFA, and
that a distinction was made for homes built before the GFA regulations in 1989
because those earlier homes generally had a steeper roof pitch that limited the
GFA, even if the home had a larger footprint. She pointed out that the
standards for hardship in a zoning variation case have to run with the land and
not with the Zoning Ordinance itself. She added that the Council makes the
determination about what constitutes reasonable return and that his application
requires the favorable vote of four Trustees because of the negative
recommendation by the ZBA.
   Trustee Greable commented that the size of the proposal is more in line with
a room addition than a mudroom, and added that he could not see a practical
difficulty or hardship for the applicants. Trustees Spinney, Johnson and Kates
agreed.
   Attorney Janega explained that the applicants are typically given the option
to withdraw their application at this time, as there is a required 12-month
waiting period before another request can be filed once the Council denies a
zoning variation.
   Mr. Franklin thanked the Council for their time and withdrew his request.
   b)   D’s Haute Dogs Liquor License Request. Attorney Janega reviewed this
renewed request from the owner of D’s Haute Dogs for a liquor license to serve
beer and wine at his establishment. She explained that this is a limited food
establishment and is not eligible for a liquor license under the Village Code,
and she cautioned the Council to look for unintended consequences if an
amendment is made to the liquor ordinance to allow a limited food establishment
to serve alcohol.
   The applicant, Jared Boyar, said he built his establishment to be a
restaurant, and that the current space does not allow for more indoor seating
or a hostess and waitstaff. He presented the Council with a petition from his
customers supporting his request.
   The Council discussed the request, and although the Trustees expressed
support for the business, there was consensus that the current format is not
appropriate for the service of alcohol.
   President Tucker encouraged residents to visit D’s Haute Dogs for a hot dog
and wished Mr. Boyar the best of luck.
10)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported that she had attended last
week’s NWMC meeting with Manager Bahan and Trustee Kates, and that the Caucus
Spring Town Meeting will be held tomorrow night.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Kates reported that he had attended a Firefighters Pension
Board meeting, that the Board expressed concern about the 6.25% assumed rate of
return for their investments, and that they would like more input with the
Council in the future.
      ii)   Trustee Greable reported that the Historical Society will host
their annual benefit at the “Home Alone” house on June 4th. He also reported on
the most recent Chamber of Commerce meeting and urged residents to support the
LGDF funds being distributed to municipalities, since a disruption in the
funding would impact services and local property taxes.
      iii)   Trustee Spinney reported that she attended her first BCDC meeting
as Council liaison.
      iv)   Trustee Johnson reported that he attended the EFC meeting as their
liaison, replacing retired Trustee King Poor.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Manager Bahan reported that information about the LGDF has
been put on the Village’s website, that an email blast was sent to residents,
and that the Village is also keeping pressure on legislators.
11)   Appointments.
   President Tucker suggested the reappointment of David Bender to the Fire
Pension Board for a second full term, effective immediately. Trustee Kates,
seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to so appoint Mr. Bender. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
12)   Executive Session.
   a)   Trustee Johnson made a motion to hold a special executive session on
Thursday, May 19, 2011, to discuss collective bargaining, pursuant to Section 2
(c)(2) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Greable seconded the motion.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Kates, Johnson
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Rintz and Braun.
   b)   Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn into
Executive Session to discuss Pending and probable Litigation, pursuant to
Section 2(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Kates, Johnson and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Rintz and Braun. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 10:12 p.m.
   The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:30 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Greable, Kates, Johnson and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Rintz and Braun. Also present: Village Manager Rob Bahan and Village
Attorney Katherine Janega.
13)   Adjournment.
   Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Greable, Kates, Johnson
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Rintz and Braun. The meeting
adjourned at 10:31 p.m.
      
   Recording Secretary
