February 7, 2012
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
February 7, 2012
(Approved: March 8, 2012)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Police Department, 410 Green Bay Road, on
Tuesday, February 7, 2012, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:30 p.m. Present: Trustees
Arthur Braun, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Richard Kates, Chris Rintz and
Jennifer Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky, Deputy Fire Chief
John Ripka, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, Assistant Director
of Community Development Brian Norkus, Director of Public Works Steve Saunders,
and approximately 10 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   February 14, 2012, Special Executive Session. All Council members
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   February 14, 2012, Study Session/Budget Meeting. All Council members,
with the exception of Chris Rintz, indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   February 16, 2012 Budget Meeting. All Council members indicated that
they expected to attend.
   d)   February 21, 2012, Council Meeting/Budget Meeting. All Council members
indicated that they expected to attend.
President Tucker asked the Trustees about rescheduling the March 6 th Council
Meeting for Thursday, March 8 th , to accommodate Trustees attending the
Northwest Municipal Conference Legislative Days in Springfield on March 6 & 7.
All Council members indicated that they expected to attend a rescheduled
Council Meeting on Thursday, March 8.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Braun, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Greable, Kates,
Johnson, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   December 20, 2012, Regular Meeting.
      ii)   January 17, 2012, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1735 and 1736. Approving Warrant List No. 1735 in
the amount of $1,433,119.07, and Warrant List No. 1736 in the amount of
$506,331.23.
   c)   Resolution R-3-2012: Cook County GIS Data Sharing Resolution –
Adoption. A resolution authorizing Village staff to sign an intergovernmental
agreement allowing the Village access to Cook County’s digital GIS information.
Trustee Spinney, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Greable, Kates, Johnson, Rintz and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Stormwater Update.
Mr. Saunders reported that, at the next regular Council meeting, staff will
present its recommendations for (i) the Sanitary Sewer Evaluation contract and
(ii) a coastal engineering consultant for the proposed tunnel project. He added
that the Village, in cooperation with the MWRD, is working on a project with
students from UIC to identify methods of infiltrating or storing 2,000 gallons
of rain water per property from roof and sump pump drainage.
Addressing the question of green infrastructure, Mr. Saunders explained that it
is more helpful in a smaller rain storm, as the benefits are mainly in water
quality, and that it is only a small component of a complete stormwater system.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution R-4-2012: Kenilworth Fire Contract – Adoption. Chief
Berkowsky reported that Winnetka’s partnership with Kenilworth dates to the
1930’s for fire service and the 1970’s for paramedic services, and that
Kenilworth accounts for about 8% of Winnetka’s call volume. He added that the
Fire Department also does hydrant testing and public education, and
participates in Kenilworth’s events.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved
to adopt Resolution R-4-2012. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Braun, Greable, Kates, Johnson, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
   b)   Resolution R-5-2012: Supporting NWMC 2012 Legislative Program –
Adoption. President Tucker explained that each year the Northwest Municipal
Conference (NWMC) creates a legislative platform to alert lawmakers to the
concerns of local governments. She said the NWMC 2012 Legislative Program
recommends solutions to challenges facing local governments, including the
diversion of local revenues and passing teachers’ pension costs to local
government units.
President Tucker urged residents to contact their legislators about the State’s
structural deficit problem and called for more pension reform.
Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adopt Resolution R-5-2012.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Greable, Kates,
Johnson, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Ordinance MC-1-2012: Coach House Amendments – Introduction. Attorney
Janega reported that the issue of coach houses was last discussed in September,
when it was put on hold to bring it before the Council as a separate discussion
from Affordable Housing. She reviewed the zoning history of coach houses in
Winnetka, and then gave a detailed explanation of the proposed Zoning Ordinance
amendments in MC-1-2012, including:
·   Coach houses will no longer be treated like nonconforming two-family and
multi-family dwellings.
·   Coach houses will not be subject to the Commercial Property Maintenance
Code, as they are in the residential zoning districts.
·   No limitations or requirements pertaining to tenancy or rent are imposed.
·   Existing coach houses will be a permitted accessory use in single family
zoning district.
·   A one-time inspection process for a certificate of occupancy will replace
the annual registration requirement.
Attorney Janega explained that a public hearing is required before the zoning
amendments can be adopted and that typically, and that the Village Council
typically designates itself, sitting as Committee of the Whole, as the hearing
body.
After Attorney Janega answered questions from the Council, they had a thorough
discussion about the proposed zoning amendments.
Trustee Kates suggested striking the language in Section 15.36.030(B) that
provides for automatic denial of a certificate of occupancy if the Village does
not give the owner written notice of inspection results within two weeks.
Mr. Norkus explained that the provision is included to place the onus on the
coach house owner to bring the building up to code and to have it inspected, as
there may be a tendency not to follow up on corrections that need to be made.
Trustee Kates also asked that the language in Section 15.36.030(B) be
reexamined to make sure that those who are currently not in compliance would
not be considered ineligible.
Attorney Janega said she understood Trustee Kates’ points, and said Staff would
consider his suggestion further to ensure that there would be no unintended
consequences or ambiguities in the process.
Trustee Spinney said she was in favor of letting property owners use their
coach houses as they see fit.
Trustee Braun said he believed coach house units are a significant way to
increase the Village’s housing stock if they are kept under the control of the
property owner and are maintained in good condition.
Trustee Rintz said Attorney Janega’s agenda report laid out the issues in an
up-front, easy to understand fashion, and he agreed with Trustee Kates’
suggestion to remove the language about an automatic denial of the certificate
of occupancy application if no notice is received. He added that future
Councils might want to have a discussion about letting new coach houses be
constructed in the R1 and R2 single family zoning districts.
Trustee Kates said many of the Village’s coach houses are treasures, and it
would be a waste not to allow them to be used.
Kimberly Harold, owner of a coach house in Winnetka, said she had been unaware
of the need to register accessory buildings, and added that she thought there
were a lot of coach house owners who are in the same predicament. She noted
that there are built-in protections about renters, as the owner of the property
would be very careful when placing a tenant in such close proximity to their
own residence.
Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to introduce Ordinance MC-1-
2012. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Greable, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to designate the Council,
acting as a Committee of the Whole, as the body that will hold the required
public hearing. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Johnson, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to set the public hearing
on the daft Zoning Ordinance amendments for March 8, 2012. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
Trustee Rintz commented that he had recently walked by 718 Hibbard, and said
that the decision to grant the variations to replace the garage was a good one
and that, more importantly, the attractive house behind the garage is now
visible. Trustee Johnson added that North Shore Country Day School also did a
nice job with their new addition after listening to input from the Council and
the school’s neighbors.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
None.
11)   Reports
   a)   Village President. President Tucker reported that she had recently
participated in a conference call with Congressman Dold, where concerns of
local governments were shared.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Kates reported on the Park District meeting on the bike
path, where signage was discussed.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None.
12)   Appointments.
None.
13)   Executive Session.
   a)   Trustee Johnson moved to hold a Special Executive Session on Tuesday,
February 14, 2012, at 5:00 p.m., to discuss Personnel and Collective
Bargaining, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1) and 2(c)(2) respectively, of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Braun seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Greable, Kates, Johnson, Rintz
and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Trustee Braun moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss
Pending and Probable Litigation, Personnel, Collective Bargaining, and Review
of Executive Session Minutes, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(11), 2(c)(1), 2(c)(2)
and 2(c)(21), respectively, of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Johnson
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Braun, Greable, Kates, Johnson, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 9:05 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:34 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Braun, Greable, Kates, Johnson, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Rob Bahan and Village Attorney
Katherine Janega.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Greable, Kates,
Johnson, Rintz and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at
10:35 p.m.
      
   Recording Secretary
February 14, 2012
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL BUDGET MEETING
February 14, 2012
(Approved: March 8, 2012)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 14, 2012, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:35 p.m. Present: Trustees
Arthur Braun, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Richard Kates, and Jennifer Spinney.
Absent: Trustee Chris Rintz. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Chief of Police
Patrick Kreis, Deputy Police Chief Joe Pellus, Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky,
Deputy Fire Chief John Ripka and approximately 3 persons in the audience.
2)   Budget Overview.
Manager Bahan thanked Finance Director Ed McKee for his hard work putting the
budget together, and he also thanked the Department Heads for their diligence
at budget time.
Mr. McKee began his presentation with a comprehensive overview of the Village’s
key revenues and core financial principles, along with a review of the
enterprise funds and cash reserves. He reported that the budget impact on a
typical resident would be an increase in total taxes and fees of $120 over last
year, or 1.9%, and he showed a breakdown of the allocation of property taxes
between Winnetka’s other taxing bodies. He reviewed a comparison of property
taxes paid in 2012 vs. 1997, which showed that increases have consistently
stayed slightly under the rate of inflation.
President Tucker pointed out that different taxing bodies have different needs
and that a referendum was passed for the nearly $50 million in school
improvements. She asked if a more fair comparison might be with other nearby
municipalities.
Trustee Greable said the community complains about high property taxes, and he
thought it was educational to highlight that the Village’s share of the
property tax bill is only 13% of the total.
Manager Bahan identified some strategic planning priority issues that will come
before the Council in the coming year, including stormwater management,
downtown revitalization and technology upgrades. He said a priority for the
Council will be the need to decide how to pay for stormwater improvements and
establish a timeframe for establishing a revenue stream to retire any debt that
is issued.
Manager Bahan next listed some additional strategic initiatives for the
Council’s consideration, including:
·   Stewardship of infrastructure - electric utility; water/sanitary systems;
and sanitary sewer evaluation
·   Technology/Communication – software and website upgrades; new phone and
internet contracts
·   Downtown Redevelopment – encourage private sector investment; policy
determination about replacing deteriorated pavers in downtowns; consider a
professional evaluation of the three business districts
Mr. McKee said there is incentive to make a fiscal year changeover so that it
coincides with the calendar year, but he advised that it would save on costs to
defer the changeover until the necessary software upgrades are completed. He
reviewed personnel costs and he pointed out that headcount has been trending
downward since 1989. He explained that it will be more difficult going forward
to continue to operate and provide services that people are accustomed to while
still reducing staffing levels.
In reviewing pension costs, Mr. McKee reported that the public safety expenses
are roughly double what the IMRF pension costs are. He explained that the
Village funds what the actuary says is necessary and that the lowest
assumptions on return are used, so that although the pension liability is
significant, the plans are funded appropriately.
Trustee Kates said there are no actuarial recommendations per se, but that the
actuary has not disagreed with the pension boards’ handling of the pension
funds. He said although the pension boards are working very hard, the public
safety pension liabilities are an area of concern for the Village
Mr. McKee said the concerns are valid, and he explained that the actuary uses a
set of assumptions to make projections about how much investment the funds will
earn, how long retirees will live, etc., and determines an amount to be levied
in property taxes. He noted that the actuary does not set the amount used for
assumed investment returns, but instead provides a range of acceptable options
and leaves it to the discretion of the pension boards and the Village to
determine.
Mr. McKee reviewed the major capital expenses provided for in the budget, which
total $11.22 million and include: (i) electrical and stormwater improvements;
(ii) rehab of streets, parking lots, sidewalks and traffic signals; (iii) the
continuing renovation of Village Hall; (iv) water plant and water main
improvements; and (v) expenses for equipment, etc.
Mr. McKee explained that the biggest portion of the Village’s operating
expenses are wages for employees – which is typical for a service organization,
since people are the key resource needed to provide the services. He explained
that the 2% increase in the electric fee will be offset by a reduction in the
power cost adjustment of 2.1%, and he predicted that water sales could be flat
or slightly decreasing, which will have a significant budget impact.
In conclusion, Mr. McKee said the new budget is fiscally sound and austere,
keeping tax and fee increases to a minimum while still maintaining the
Village’s core services, rebuilding infrastructure, and establishing a
stormwater improvement fund to track costs associated with stormwater projects.
President Tucker thanked Mr. McKee for finding ways to provide savings and
still supply services that residents have come to expect, and she noted that
Winnetka is in great shape compared to many other area municipalities.
3)   Police Department.
Chief Kreis reviewed the department’s mission statement and organizational
chart, along with highlights of 2011. He reported that in 2011 there were 5,600
vacancy hours, which he explained are hours lost when an employee is injured,
retires, or is called up to serve in the military. He said approximately 25% of
the department’s training was deferred because the volume of vacancy hours was
very high last year, and he explained that a review is presently being
conducted to plan and implement a recovery of the training hours.
In comparing past budgets to the current one, Chief Kreis noted that increases
are largely driven by benefit costs, as no wage increases other than
contractual step increases are factored into they new budget. He reviewed key
goals and objectives for the coming year: (i) implement a new policy manual
system with daily training bulletins; (ii) administrative adjudication process;
(iii) combined dispatch initiative; (iv) roof rehabilitation work on the police
station; and (v) acquisition and upgrades of equipment.
Trustee Greable asked why a placeholder amount for combined dispatch was in the
budget.
Chief Kreis explained that he is still committed to exploring the possibilities
to build that partnership with neighboring communities.
Manager Bahan said he has had detailed discussions with the Managers of
Kenilworth and Northfield and that it is possible that the Northfield Board’s
concerns can be addressed in the next six months or so, or possibly other
configurations for combined dispatch can be worked out.
4)   Fire Department.
Chief Berkowsky began his presentation with an explanation of the
organizational chart of the department followed, by a review of daily
responsibilities covered by personnel. He noted that administration personnel
cover on shifts when the need arises due to injuries, illness or retirement,
and that this reallocation of personnel saved $3,795 in potential overtime last
year, or $176,088. He reviewed calls by response areas, fire losses/saves and
listed the outside agencies that the Winnetka Fire Department cooperates with
to receive and provide mutual to neighboring communities.
Chief Berkowsky listed some 2011 accomplishments, including: (i) Radio Direct
Connect Alarm Network exceeded its goal of 100 connections by 30%; (ii)
additional response grid added to the grid map system; (iii) replacement of an
incident command vehicle; (iv) update of MABAS box cards to enhance
responsiveness; and (v) management training and paramedics certified in
Pediatric Advanced Life Support Skills. He said revenue received for providing
service to Kenilworth and unincorporated areas, and for ambulance and fire
alarm radio fees totaled $875,360.
Trustee Kates asked what the Village is doing to reduce the number of false
alarm calls.
Chief Berkowsky explained that the old technology causes a lot of the false
alarms and as more Direct Connect alarms are installed, the incidence should
diminish.
5)   Executive Session.
None.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee Johnson, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Greable, Kates,
Johnson and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Rintz. The meeting adjourned
at 9:25 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
February 21, 2012
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
February 21, 2012
(Approved: April 3, 2012)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held at the Police Department on Tuesday, February 21,
2012, at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:32 p.m. Present: Trustees
Arthur Braun, Gene Greable, Bill Johnson, Richard Kates, Chris Rintz and
Jennifer Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Director of Water
& Electric Brian Keys, Assistant Director of Water & Electric Rich Ciesla,
Community Development Director Mike D'Onofrio, Assistant Community Development
Director Brian Norkus, and approximately 8 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
