Recording Secretary
January 8, 2013 - STUDY SESSION
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
January 8, 2013
(Approved: February 5, 2013)
A record of a legally convened study session meeting of the Council of the
Village of Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on
Tuesday, January 8, 2013 at 7:30 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:41 p.m. Present: Trustees
Arthur Braun, Patrick Corrigan, Richard Kates, Stuart McCrary and Jennifer
Spinney. Absent: Trustee Jack Buck. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Director of Public Works Steve
Saunders, Finance Director Ed McKee, Community Development Director Mike
D’Onofrio, Assistant to the Village Manager Megan Pierce and approximately 10
persons in the audience.
2)   Stormwater Utility Feasibility Study Workshop #1.
Mr. Saunders explained that Municipal and Financial Services Group (MFSG) has
prepared a presentation to assist the Council in evaluating possible flood risk
reduction improvements in the 14 identified drainage areas. He summarized the
elements that affect the way a stormwater utility is created:
·   Level of service and service scenarios. This includes the elements of the
stormwater management program, such as ongoing maintenance expenses, capital
improvements, planning and funding for the replacement of existing
infrastructure and possibly replenishing General Fund reserves that have been
committed to the initial set of planned capital improvements. MFSG has explored
funding scenarios for full implementation, phased implementation and a base
program.
·   Funding options. MFSG has identified the following funding alternatives:
(1) Stormwater Fee; (2) General Fund; (3) a combination of alternatives 1 and
2.
·   Rates. How will rates be assessed to different properties?
Mr. Saunders explained that each of the elements must be determined before
there can be an understanding of how specific properties will be affected by a
stormwater utility, and that the purpose of tonight’s workshop was to update
the Council on progress to-date and to get policy direction going forward. He
noted that Workshop #2 will provide information about methodologies that other
locales use, along with successful approaches that have been identified by
other communities; and Workshop #3 will entail a discussion of rate
methodologies, levels of service and funding mechanisms. Public comment will be
helpful at Workshop #3, as the concepts will be developed enough for the
implications to be understood by the public.
Mr. David Hyder, MFSG, presented a study timeline, identified cost categories,
and then provided a detailed explanation of those categories, including: (i) a
detailed breakdown of stormwater operating expenses up to 2017, and general
forecast to 2023; (ii) expenses for current planned capital projects; (iii)
expense scenarios for possible future projects; (iv) costs of infrastructure
replacements; and (v) annual projected payments for repaying the General Fund.
Lastly, he presented a slide that illustrated the total annual revenue
requirements and identified each scenario and cost category.
Mr. Hyder next reviewed three funding options, based on revenue sources: (i)
100% from stormwater fee; (ii) 100% from property tax; and (iii) a combination
of property taxes and stormwater fees. He then reviewed the projected impacts
on a base sample property owner for each of the funding options for full,
phased and base program scenarios, and explained that the 100% property tax
funding scenario does not draw from tax-exempt properties, so there is a higher
cost to residential users under that scenario.
Mr. Hyder then recapped the study timeline, and Council questions and
discussion followed.
Responding to Trustee questions about operations and maintenance (O&M) costs
and about guidelines for replacement, Mr. Saunders explained that the current
O&M costs (Slide #7) include the stormwater maintenance crew, repairs and
cleaning, with a portion of the costs split between the General Fund and the
Sanitary Sewer Fund. He said that maintenance repair work for both storm and
sanitary sewers is scheduled in conjunction with the Village’s street
resurfacing program.
There was a discussion about the stormwater construction schedule as it relates
to people paying for benefits that they might never enjoy using. Asked if the
project schedule could be compressed, Mr. Saunders explained that some of the
proposed projects can’t be constructed until the proposed tunnel is completed,
as they rely on the tunnel to accept their discharge. He added that the tunnel
schedule is very aggressive and could not reasonably be compressed further.
There was a discussion about equity issues when structuring payment for the
proposed improvements. Responding to comments about the wisdom of completing
projects in drainage areas where only a few homes reported flooding, Mr.
Saunders observed that this does not mean that flooding in those areas is not
important, but that it is a smaller magnitude. He added that Staff hopes to
have cost/benefit information for those areas at the February workshop.
There was general agreement that all areas of the Village should be protected
against flooding, but there was no consensus on the question of repaying the
General Fund for expenditures related to stormwater projects.
Mr. Saunders reiterated that the Council is not being asked to make any policy
decisions at this time, but rather to start thinking about some of the elements
it may want to include or omit, which will help in developing a rate philosophy
for funding stormwater improvements.
Trustee Spinney said the Council needs to communicate to residents that
stormwater management is a community-wide problem, and the reasons this is so.
Mr. McKee distributed a memo to the Council that contained context about the
issue of repaying the General Fund for stormwater expenses incurred, noting
that the memo was for information purposes and did not need to be discussed.
President Tucker then called for public comment.
Jeffry Liss, 1364 Edgewood, said he having more detail about affected
properties in the six optional drainage areas at the next workshop, would help
with the cost/benefit discussion.
Manager Bahan said the Village cannot share confidential information about
specific addresses that responded to the survey.
Mr. Saunders said his sense from the discussion was that the Council would like
to see information with cost implications for repaying the General Fund and
further information for funding O&M, and that full funding from property taxes
should still be examined as an option for the time being. He explained that the
next workshop would focus on rate methodologies and how they would be applied
to different classes of properties.
3)   2013 Budget Schedule – Strategic Items and Input.
a)   Business Community Development Commission (BCDC) Update. Jason Harris,
Chair of the BCDC, reported that the BCDC was active in 2012 going out into the
community to assess the health of business and retail activity. He explained
that a strategic framework had been developed from ideas gathered from local
merchants, property owners and residents, which was further distilled into six
Strategic Themes: (i) business support; (ii) communications; (iii)
associations/events; (iv) branding/physical appearance; (v) Village
regulations/policies and (vi) innovations. He noted that the first three themes
are relatively easy to implement and the last three are more difficult to
accomplish.
Mr. Harris reviewed three principles that guide the BCDC: (i) that their
proposal is a work in progress that needs input from the Council and other key
community stakeholders; (ii) that the BCDC’s efforts are aligned with the
upcoming ULI study; and (iii) that the BCDC team is a cross-functional team of
diverse members of the community and Village staff.
Mr. Harris summarized the following BCDC recommendations to the Council: (i)
fully communicate the Village’s permit process to illustrate that Winnetka is a
good place to do business; (ii) increase communication between the Village and
its business districts and among the four business districts; (iii) create a
strong business development web presence on the redesigned Village; (iv)
consider hiring a public relations firm to promote events and activities in
Winnetka; (v) invest $25,000 to implement a floral program along the Green Bay
Road corridor for the summer shopping season; and (vi) align BCDC findings with
regard to signage, wayfinding and branding with recommendations that come out
of the ULI process.
Mr. Harris asked the Council how it envisions the role of the BCDC going
forward, and he asked for guidance about community development issues the
Council would like the BCDC to explore further.
Responding to a question from Trustee Braun, Mr. Harris said he believed the
Village needs to articulate a vision and provide the BCDC a strategic framework
with an overriding direction, and that the ULI study, especially the second
phase, would provide much-needed guidance. A brief discussion about the
vacancies in the commercial districts followed.
Manager Bahan explained that the ULI process will begin in February, and that
their data collection and recommendations will be the basis from which the
Council will make decisions about the strategies it wishes to implement.
President Tucker recalled that when she first joined the Council as a Trustee
in 2004, the business districts were a top priority, with parking, streetscape
and redevelopment of the Post Office (PO) site being studied. She explained
that the PO redevelopment was put on hold to give the library time to do a
needs assessment, and that the floods of 2008 and 2011 shifted the Village’s
focus to stormwater management. She said the ULI process will jump-start the
business district conversation and provide a renewed focus.
Trustee Kates said that while the BCDC recommendations are reasonable, the
Council needs a specific plan before it can allocate money. He observed that
the BCDC’s early morning meeting time results in little public attendance and
input from the community, and not enough meeting time to accomplish the BCDC’s
goals. He added that he expects the ULI study to provide much-needed context
and some novel ideas for the Village to pursue.
Mr. Harris said the business community wants to be heard and that it is the
goal of the BCDC to rally the Council.
Trustee Spinney commented favorably on the BCDC’s progress and added that the
commercial vacancy rate is very good when viewed in light of the stagnant
economy.
Trustee Braun encouraged the Council to start thinking about the PO site and
added that he would like to see the BCDC heavily involved with the ULI project.
Trustee McCrary said he would like to see questions of the PO redevelopment and
commercial revitalization placed on future agendas so progress can be made.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood, suggested (i) contacting Wilmette to find out
about their downtown redevelopment process; (ii) re-examining the sprinkler
system requirements in the Village’s fire code; (iii) considering increasing
allowable building height; and (iv) approving the BCDC’s blueprint and asking
them to refine the proposal and bring a specific plan back to the Council for
approval.
Patrick O’Neil, a member of the BCDC, disagreed that the BCDC’s proposals were
not specific enough, and thanked Mr. Harris for his work as chair.
Terry Dason, Executive Director of the Chamber of Commerce, also thanked Mr.
Harris, and commented that he has a lot of creative ideas that, if put into
action, would be advantageous for the community.
Trustee Kates suggested fleshing out the ideas and following up on the “Listen
and Learn” sessions.
Manager Bahan said the proposals would be incorporated into the budget once the
particulars were received from the BCDC.
b)   Proposed Budget Schedule and Major Budget Initiatives. Mr. McKee reviewed
the budget meeting schedule and explained how the fiscal year will be shortened
to accommodate a second budget process in August, which is necessitated by the
move to a calendar fiscal year. He said he is in the process of putting a draft
budget document together for the Council.
Trustees Braun and McCrary that the budget meeting dates be well publicized,
and Manager Bahan responded that information would be put on the Village’s
website, cable access channel, in a press release and in a special E-Winnetka
newsletter.
Trustee Kates noted that policy is made through the allocation of budget
funding, and encouraged the Council to take use the budget process as an
opportunity to get things accomplished, and not get overwhelmed with the data.
Mr. McKee said Staff tries to put flexibility in the budget, and if the Council
feels its needs are not being met, to please make the desired changes.
Manager Bahan commented that the Council will have the unique opportunity to go
through the budget process two times this year, and he added that the Capital
Plan is an area the Council can dive into to impact residents’ lives.
4)   Public Comment.
None.
5)   Executive Session.
None.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Kates, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 10:15 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
