March 5, 2013
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
March 5, 2013
(Approved: April 4, 2013)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Council Chambers on Tuesday, March 4, 2013, at
7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Tucker called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Arthur Braun, Jack Buck, Patrick Corrigan, Richard Kates, Stuart McCrary and
Jennifer Spinney. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Community Development Director Michael
D’Onofrio, Finance Director Edward McKee, Public Works Director Steven
Saunders, Water & Electric Director Brian Keys, Assistant to the Village
Manager Megan Pierce and approximately ten persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Tucker led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   March 12, 2013 Study Session. All Council members indicated they expect
to attend. Manager Bahan will check with Council members for their availability
to start the meeting at 6:00 p.m. due to the amount of material to cover.
   b)   March 19, 2013 Regular Meeting & Study Session. All Council members
indicated they expect to attend. The Urban Land Institute (ULI) is scheduled
present its preliminary report from the first Technical Assistance Panel (TAP).
c)   Thursday, April 4, 2013 Rescheduled Regular Meeting. All Council members
indicated they expect to attend. (Meeting rescheduled due to    Passover.)
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Buck, Corrigan, Kates,
McCrary and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   February 5, 2013, Regular Meeting.
      ii)   February 6, 2013 Budget Meeting.
   b)   Warrant Lists Nos. 1787 and 1788. Approving Warrant List No. 1787 in
the amount of $787,512.88, and Warrant List No. 1788 in the amount of
$757,330.54.
   c)   Resolution R-16-2013: Green Bay-Winnetka Avenue Traffic Signal –
Adoption. A Resolution authorizing the expenditure of $500,000 in Motor Fuel
Tax funds for the Traffic Signal Modernization Project for Green Bay Road and
Winnetka Avenue .
   d)   Water Plant, Low Lift Pump Repairs. Ratifying the Village Manager’s
approval of a change order to Illinois Pump for emergency repairs to Low Lift
Pump #1 in the amount of $12,300, for a total contract award of $31,886.
   e)   State of Illinois Cooperative Salt Purchasing Program 2012-2013.
Approving the purchase of rock salt through the State of Illinois Cooperative
purchasing program from the low bidder, Morton Salt, at $52.74 per ton.
Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Buck, Corrigan, Kates, McCrary and Spinney.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Stormwater Update.
   a)   Sanitary Sewer Anti-Backflow Program – Additional Program Data.
Director of Public Works Steven Saunders reviewed the proposed funding for the
Sanitary Sewer Anti-Backflow Program in the FY 2013-14 Budget. He presented his
analysis of the reimbursement requests since the program’s inception in 2006,
explaining there have been 39 reimbursements under the program, with 13 of
those properties correcting some sort of illegal stormwater connection to the
sanitary sewer. Initially budgeted at $30,000 in FY 2012-2013, the amount was
later increased to $70,000. Mr. Saunders has proposed $50,000 for FY 2013-2014,
and also recommended additional eligibility conditions for program
participants.
Responding to a series of questions from Trustee Kates, Mr. Saunders stated
that, during the last year, two out of 15 program applicants were found to have
illegal stormwater connections. He explained that the Village might find such
connections if the homeowner invited the Village to inspect the property, but
that the Department’s sanitary sewer program does not include conducting random
in-home compliance inspections. Mr. Saunders noted that, under the Village
Code’s enforcement powers, the Village can issue a ticket to homeowners who
have illicit connections.
Trustee Corrigan stated that he discovered he had prohibited connections when
he had a backflow preventer installed, that he believes many people are not
aware that their stormwater connections to the sanitary sewer are improper, and
that the money could instead be spent on public education. Trustee McCrary
suggested that the Village might be able to require inspections of homes that
have not participated in this program and whose homes have not been otherwise
inspected because of recent construction. Through the inspection, homeowners
would be educated and given time to rectify the problems. Mr. Saunders said it
may be fruitful to do inspections in targeted areas and that the Metropolitan
Water Reclamation District (MWRD) may again require inspections.
Trustee Braun favored fixed start and end dates for benefit programs such as
this one, and suggested that Mr. Saunders could outline ways to achieve the
goals discussed by the Council and then determine an ending date. Trustee Buck
favored eliminating the program and not funding improvements to individual
homes. Trustee Spinney said she believes there are better ways to spend
$50,000. Mr. Saunders clarified that the program does not fund the cost of
eliminating prohibited connections; it applies only to the cost of the backflow
devices that are installed after prohibited connections are eliminated. He
added that there are six applicants currently in queue who have reached the
inspection stage and will likely apply for reimbursement.
The consensus of the Council was to have staff estimate the amount needed to
cover the residents currently in queue for potential reimbursement, and for
staff to consider how to bring the program to an end. Manager Bahan commented
that the policy change would be communicated to residents through such methods
as the Village website and the Winnetka Report, and confirmed that eliminating
the Sanitary Sewer Anti-Backflow Program does not require a formal amendment to
the proposed budget.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Public Hearing: 2013-14 Village Budget. President Tucker declared the
Public Hearing open. Finance Director Edward McKee reviewed the proposed budget
and the metrics considered in compiling it, saying that the Village looks at
the bottom-line impact on residents, and that the proposed budget reflects a
1.7% property tax increase for a typical residence. He added that there are
proposed water and sewer rate increases to fund capital improvements, and noted
that the Village’s rates are competitive in the market, compared to surrounding
increases. He said the total impact of the budget on the average household
would be approximately a 1.8% increase. He also explained that the Village
makes up 13.36% of the total property tax bill for a Winnetka resident and that
the Village’s total expenses in the proposed budget, minus stormwater costs,
are down 2.1%.
With no comments from the audience, President Tucker declared the Public
Hearing closed.
      i)   Resolution R-8-2013: Village Budget – Introduction.
Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Resolution R-8-
2013. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Updated Fee and Rate Resolutions.
      i)   Resolution R-9-2013: Water Rates – Introduction.   
      ii)   Resolution R-10-2013: Electric Rates – Introduction.
      iii)   Resolution R-11-2013: Sewer Rates – Introduction.
      iv)   Resolution R-12-2013: Refuse Rates – Introduction.
President Tucker explained that Resolutions R-9 through R-12 can be introduced
in a single motion, but would need to be separately adopted at the March 19
meeting. She added that Resolutions R-13 through R-15 are provided for
information only, as they do not require introduction. Mr. McKee clarified the
application of the proposed rates on the average residence.
Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee Spinney, moved to introduce Resolutions
R-9-2013, R-10-2013, R-11-2013, and R-12-2013. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
      v)   Resolution R-13-2013: General Permit & License Fees (Informational
only).
      vi)   Resolution R-14-2013: Building, Zoning & Construction Fees
(Informational only).
      vii)   Resolution R-15-2013: Fire Service Fees (Informational only).
Trustee Buck asked about reviewing the individual fees presented in the budget.
Manager Bahan noted that staff is performing an analysis of comparable fees in
the area and would have that information prepared for the FY 2014-15 budget
later this year.
There were no other questions about Resolutions R-13 through R-15.
   c)   Ordinance M-3-2013: 630 Pine Lane Landmark Rescission – Adoption.
Ordinance M-3-2013, which was introduced at the February 19, 2013, Council
meeting, would rescind the landmark designation for 630 Pine Lane, due to the
deteriorated condition of the home. Village Attorney Janega explained that,
although the rescission of landmark status is a fairly simple process, this
case is more complex because it needs to be accomplished without changing the
underlying subdivision. She reviewed the proposed conditions outlined in the
amended Ordinance. She noted that GBM had already provided proof of ownership
and a certificate of good standing, confirming that it was the proper party to
seek rescission. She added that, while demolition is a foregone conclusion of
these proceedings, the owner not yet filed a demolition application. She
explained that the Ordinance contains conditions for expediting the demolition
process, reserves the Village’s right to impose an administrative delay if
necessary, provides for an abbreviated Historical and Architectural Impact
Study (HAIS) process before the Landmark Preservation Commission, but prohibits
a preservation delay. The amendments made since introduction requires a
construction easement over the west lot (Lot 1) to access 630 Pine Lane, so as
not to further disturb the subdivision property neighbors along Pine Lane.
Attorney Janega also noted the restrictive zoning covenants that ensure that
the future development of 630 Pine Lane is consistent with the previously
approved and recorded Subdivision Plat and Development Agreement.
Attorney Janega explained that an HAIS is normally required before a demolition
permit can be issued, but due to the condition of this property, the amended
ordinance allows a parallel process, although the HAIS would need to be
completed before the owner could obtain a building permit, and no deadline is
set for demolition. Trustee Kates favored eliminating the HAIS requirement, and
the Council discussed the merits of the HAIS in this case and the need to
protect against setting a precedent for future demolition applications. The
viability of placing a lien on the property was also discussed.
Todd Stevens, Attorney, spoke on behalf of the applicants. He explained that he
would proceed with the Cook County environmental application and inspection
requirement as soon as he applies for demolition, and said he thought the
demolition could be completed in 90 days. Mr. Stevens and the Council discussed
the requirements for construction conditions, especially as related to the sale
of Lot 1, and easements that are generally acceptable. Mr. Stevens expressed
his client’s concern that the easements could negatively impact the
marketability of Lot 1. The Council discussed allowing access off Pine Lane,
with a bond being required to ensure adequate repair and completion of private
roads, although this may not be satisfactory to the neighbors who were present
at the first meeting. Attorney Janega advised Mr. Stevens that a demolition
permit could be applied for and that Staff would look to add a 90 day timeline.
Trustee McCrary said he preferred the Ordinance as written, saying that trying
to accommodate GBM so that the Lot 1 (along Hibbard) could sell first seems
unrealistic. Responding to a question from Trustee Buck about why an ordinance
is required if landmark status is voluntary, Attorney Janega explained only the
owner can apply for a landmark designation and for the rescission of the
landmark, and that because the landmark is designated by ordinance, an
ordinance is required to rescind. She added that the conditions added to the
ordinance related to the underlying subdivision, rather than the landmark
itself.
Trustee Braun moved to amend Ordinance M-3-2013. The motion failed for lack of
a second.
8)   Public Comment and Questions.
Resident Gertrude Niehoff, 557 Orchard. She related an incident in which the
gas company damaged a clay sanitary sewer pipe that serves her property, and
requested the Council’s support of a complete GIS system for the Public Works
Department, including an inventory of both the Village’s utilities and
connections to other agencies so there would be complete information about all
underground infrastructure.
Responding to a question from Trustee Braun, Mr. Saunders noted that the
Village has been able to map much of the old infrastructure from the Village’s
own records, that outside agencies do not like to share proprietary
information, that the Village does not have good historic information on
sanitary sewer locations, and that programs to locate them are not reliable He
added that companies are required to call JULIE before performing excavations.
.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
   a)   Business District Floral Program Proposal. The Business Community
Development Commission (BCDC) asked the Council to consider implementing a
Business District Floral Program to beautify the three individual business
districts. Using initial information from the BCDC, Village Staff evaluated the
capacity of the existing poles to support two baskets and related hardware,
explored practices in other communities, and determined options for contracting
out the maintenance of the floral program. Manager Bahan noted that the Staff
Report estimated the first year investment would be $81,161 for 60 pole
locations (120 baskets) or $40,580 for 30 poles (60 baskets). Going forward,
there would be annual maintenance and basket replacement costs.
Trustee Kates stated that Indian Hill is a different type of district where the
baskets may not be as necessary, and that eliminating baskets there would
reduce the pole locations by 9 poles (18 baskets). He asked about the timeline
for the recommendations from the Urban Land Institute (ULI) study and expressed
concern about going forward at this time. Manager Bahan said that ULI’s draft
report will be presented on March 19, but the full recommendations won’t be in
until after the time the Village would need to move forward to install baskets
in July, 2013. Manager Bahan said going forward will not be detrimental but
stressed that this is a matter of policy and prioritization for the Council.
Trustee Buck suggested that no more staff time be spent on the project, that
the community and businesses could support some program, but that the Village
has other, larger priorities. Trustee Braun said that the business districts
have had no beautification efforts and wants to do something for the downtown
area. The short timeline for Village staff to obtain bids and to move forward
with installation this year was reviewed, after which Trustee Corrigan
commented that the business districts look shabby and that the amount in
question is not very much money to invest to add visual excitement to the area.
Business owner Patrick O’Neil and Winnetka-Northfield Chamber of Commerce
Executive Director Terry Dason spoke on behalf of the BCDC’s program
recommendations and the desirability of the improvements, and Ms. Dason noted
that local businesses are struggling and she did not believe they would fund a
floral program.
The Council directed Staff to proceed with inclusion of the program in the FY
2013-14 budget, for 60 pole locations.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Reports.
   a)   Village President. No report.
   b)   Trustees.
   Trustee McCrary: The Historical Society’s Gala will be held June 8 at a
historical home on Sheridan Road.
   Trustee Kates: The BCDC discussed various topics at its last meeting, but
made no decisions that concern the Council.
   c)   Attorney. No report.
   d)   Manager. No report.
13)   Executive Session.
Trustee Braun moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss Personnel
Matters, pursuant to Section 2(c)(1) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee
Spinney seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Braun, Buck, Corrigan, Kates, McCrary and Spinney. Nays: None. Absent:
None. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 8:57 p.m.
The Council reconvened into Regular Session at 10:13 p.m. Present: President
Tucker, Trustees Braun, Buck, Corrigan, Kates, McCrary and Spinney. Nays: None.
Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Rob Bahan.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee Corrigan, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 10:15 p.m.
            
Recording Secretary
March 12, 2013
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
March 12, 2013
(Approved: April 4, 2013)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
12, 2013 at 6:15 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
Trustee Spinney called the meeting to order at 6:22 p.m. Present: Trustees
Arthur Braun, Jack Buck, Patrick Corrigan, Richard Kates, and Stuart McCrary.
Absent: President Jessica Tucker. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Village Attorney Katherine Janega, Finance Director Ed McKee, Public
Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant to the Village Manager Megan Pierce,
and approximately 12 persons in the audience.
President Tucker arrived at 6:25 p.m.
2)   Municipal Financial Services Group (MFSG) Stormwater Utility Workshop #3.
Public Works Director Steven Saunders provided background on the Stormwater
Utility Workshops. Stormwater financing is being discussed separately from the
technical engineering and construction of proposed stormwater improvements,
with the stormwater financing workshops focusing on how to pay for the
stormwater program. Workshop #1 looked at the level of service. Workshop #2
focused on how a billing system would work and how rates could be set-up.
Based on the policy direction from the Village Council, the analysis in
Workshop #3 focuses on financing only the currently planned capital improvement
projects, and looks at two alternative funding approaches: (i) stormwater
utility fees fund 100% of program, or (ii) funding is split 50/50 between a
utility and property taxes. While a differential rate structure based on
property location was previously analyzed, the Council opted to look at a rate
base that is spread consistently across the Village.
David Hyder of MFSG summarized the policy issues considered through the prior
stormwater workshops: (i) the analysis includes only current planned capital
projects (which total $41,024,000), and does not include repaying reserves to
the general fund; (ii) the analysis looks at funding through a stormwater fee
and a through a combination of fees and taxes; (iii) the analysis uses a rate
base of impervious area, and a fee structured on a uniform base; (iv)
stormwater operating expenses from 2013 to 2018 will continue to be recovered
through the General Fund.
Mr. Hyder said MFSG recommends issuing bonds in 2014 and 2015 for a total of
$34,205,500, and then provided models for debt service payments based on 20-
year bonds and on 30-year bonds. The unfunded stormwater revenue requirements
were then projected out for a five-year period, using both the 100% stormwater
fee funding and the 50/50 stormwater fee/property tax funding model.
MFSG again recommended that Winnetka use impervious area as its rate base,
based on analysis showing that the average impervious area for a single family
home was 3,400 square feet, so that the unit measurement, called an equivalent
runoff unit (ERU), would be 3.400 square feet, rounded to the nearest tenth of
a unit.
Mr. Hyder briefly described two alternate approaches to the ERU calculation
that are not widely used and that MFSG does not recommend. The Intensity of
Development (ID) approach creates a rate based on a ratio of impervious area to
pervious area. This approach tends to benefit those that have more pervious
area, but lacks correlation between the amount of stormwater discharge and the
stormwater bill, so that it is not likely to encourage people to reduce their
impervious area. The second alternative is the Equivalent Hydraulic Acreage
(EHA) approach, which uses impervious and pervious area, as well as a runoff
coefficient, to determine the number of EHAs per parcel. In addition to
shifting the costs more to parcels with the most pervious area, this method is
also difficult to administer, as it also requires the Village to manage
pervious and impervious area calculations for every parcel.
Mr. Hyder then presented funding scenarios for both the 100% stormwater fee
funding model and the combined (50/50) funding model, with each model being
displayed with a 20-year bond assumption and 30-year bond assumption. Using the
same Village parcels as in previous fee analysis, Hyder presented calculations
of property owner impacts. Residential, commercial, and tax-exempt properties
with various impervious areas, equalized assessed values, and ERU’s were
described with comparable bills.
MFSG’s presentation then turned to a discussion of credits and incentives. A
credit reduces the fee charged to a property, based on actual on-site
qualifying stormwater management. Credits recognize potential reductions in the
Village’s stormwater costs and encourage property owners to proactively manage
their impact. A credit program structure would need to determine eligibility,
qualifying activities, standards, the amount of fee reduction, and maximum
credit offered. Mr. Hyder illustrated a sample credit program, showing examples
of control activities (rain gardens, pervious pavement, green roods, and
parking lot retention) and the range of credits that could be offered. He added
that credits are usually only available to non-residential parcels that exceed
Village standards and that an application process, fee, and reporting controls
would need to be established. Mr. Hyder explained that incentives are one-time
reimbursements for the installation of stormwater controls, and are typically
offered where credits are not available to residential parcels, to encourage
public participation in stormwater management. He described an incentive
program, which could be offered on a first-come, first-served basis to
residents that install items such as rain barrels or rain gardens.
Lastly, MFSG presented implementation issues for the Village to consider. Mr.
Hyder first described three common approaches to billing a stormwater fee: the
utility “water” bill, the property tax bill, or a separate stormwater bill. He
then noted that the Village would need to manage a database that accounts for
the impervious area throughout the entire Village (the Village currently
updates this information every five years). Finally, he said the Village would
need a process to handle property owner appeals to the stormwater utility
process.
MFSG’s ultimate recommendations, based on their experience and analysis were as
follows: 1) It is feasible for the Village to fund at least a portion of the
stormwater program through a stormwater utility. 2) A level of service should
be provided by the Village that funds both operation and maintenance, as well
as current planned capital. 3) The Village should consider using 30-year bonds
due to the projected life of the capital assets. 4) A uniform fee structure
should be used for all parcels in the Village, based on actual impervious area
and calculated based on multiples of 3,400 square feet. 5) Subject to changes
in the capital program or credits program, the Village should consider the
magnitude of a bi-monthly fee of $2.87 starting in fiscal year 2014 and
increasing to $30.53 in fiscal year 2018.
Mr. Saunders noted that the credits and incentives program is a purely a policy
decision, and that it is a way to mitigate the impact to some of the larger
tax-exempt properties that may face hefty fees. Regarding level of service, he
said the presentation assumes the construction of the currently planned capital
projects, though there is still a need to determine the level of protection for
the specific areas.
Trustee Kates asked whether there is any reduction in the Village’s expenses if
credits were given. Mr. Hyder responded that there would not be a reduction in
the short-term. He added that credit programs generally do not have a large
impact, because of the standards that must be met to receive the credit, and he
noted that if a tax-exempt organization received a large credit, it would
reduce that organization’s total contribution to stormwater improvements.
Trustee Buck favored including operation and maintenance costs in the fee,
rather than in the General Fund, and that the replenishment of the General Fund
reserves is still an open issue. Responding to a question from Trustee Buck,
Manager Bahan and Mr. Saunders described the additional $700,000 in revenues
that staff proposes could be allocated to stormwater from the General Fund, by
freeing up funds that had historically been used for street repairs, and using
Motor Fuel Tax (MFT) funds for those repairs, rather than setting MFT funds
aside for major street projects.
In discussing equity with the Council, Mr. Hyder said is not uncommon for
Illinois localities to use blended funding, due to the magnitude of expenses,
and that, rather than doing away with the fee in 20 or 30 years when the
capital financing is paid off, the Village could add additional program
elements to be funded by the fee over time. It was noted that the $500,000 in
the level of service funding that stems from the upcoming retirement of current
bonds has already been anticipated in the FY 2013-14 Budget and will be
allocated toward capital. The importance of being transparent about the full
impact of project costs on taxes and fees as part of the community engagement
process was emphasized by the Council. Trustee McCrary noted the focus on cash
flow analogies and that issuing 30-year debt will spread the cost over more
years and won’t necessarily be cheaper for any particular household. Trustee
Kates reiterated the importance of transparency, as well as the need to reduce
concern over sticker shock, saying he does not think people will agree to repay
reserves that were accumulated to pay for large projects.
President Tucker urged the Council to educate the community on the various
funding options so that residents, commercial property owners and non-profits
will know what the impact will be - depending upon the option - and come
together on a consensus solution. Trustee Corrigan stated that if a utility is
formed, it should reimburse the General Fund for the property tax revenues used
for stormwater-related work. He also stated that, while shifting the $700,000
to pay stormwater expenses was a creative idea for decreasing annual expenses,
he opposed doing so, as reserves have been saved for a rainy day.
The Council discussed the need to justify using taxpayer money, as well as
which people are being served by stormwater improvements under consideration.
Mr. Saunders stated that, while it was agreed that the six additional areas
studied by Baxter & Woodman had merit, they will be evaluated at a later time.
He also recounted the current capital projects that remain in the analysis and
the set of improvements that resulted from the 25-, 50- and 100-year studies
conducted by Christopher B. Burke Engineering, Ltd. Discussion continued about
including future projects in the current analysis, which may help with
understanding the full cost and benefit at different stages of work. Trustee
Kates reiterated his view that there needs to be a cost/benefit analysis of the
projects in the six-additional drainage areas and that he opposes committing to
them. Manager Bahan and Mr. Saunders said that items can be considered as part
of a plan when the funding is not yet determined.
Trustee Braun stated, the goal has been to cure the stormwater issues
experienced in 2011, that there are funding considerations, but that the
reality is that the improvements will cost $41 million, and said that the
Council needs to hear from the residents before further narrowing the decisions
and determining the solution. Responding to a question from Trustee Kates, Mr.
Hyder addressed the difference between the $41 million capital cost reference
and the $74 million recently noted in the news. He described that in Workshop
#2, the 30-year spending model included $48.2 million in capital projects, but
that tonight, the 30-year spending model only includes $33.7 million in
capital, as reserves have been excluded and there were minor changes to some
project costs.
The Council discussed cost projections and other items the Village will be
considering in the five-year capital improvement program. Responding to a
comment that the sanitary sewer program had not yet been discussed, Mr.
Saunders said that because of the Village’s significant sanitary program in the
mid-1980’s, he does not anticipate a large investment in public inflow/
infiltration, and that $1 million has been allocated in the sewer fund to
address those issues.
Mr. Saunders described the National Pollutant Discharge Elimination System
(NPDES) permitting process, explained how “first flush” water would not go to
the lake, and that the Village would control contaminants from entering Lake
Michigan. Responding to a comment about seeking grants for the project, Manager
Bahan said staff is working with Representative Schakowsky’s office to
understand funding opportunities and is actively seeking funds through a
jurisdictional transfer of Willow Road.
Following further discussion, Mr. Hyder summarized his understanding of what
the Council wanted in the final report: (i) a breakdown of each of the
components into a cost per ERU; (ii) spreading costs of currently planned
capital projects over a 30-year period; and (iii) layer in the other
considerations as increments on that base. Trustee McCrary suggested
replenishing reserves on a pay-as-you-go basis so that more debt is not issued
until the reserves have been repaid. Trustee Corrigan suggested that more
examples of homeowner impacts be provided, using specific addresses and actual
costs, rather than averages.
The discussion concluded with a summary of the stormwater projects planned for
the upcoming fiscal year (Winnetka Avenue Pump Station and three northern
projects). Mr. Saunders said he anticipates construction of those projects this
year, but they all would come back to Council to get authorization to bid and
that the April Stormwater Update will offer a more detailed discussion about
moving forward on those projects.
3)   Public Comment.
Alan Orschel, 1258 Pine Street: Opposes excluding any homeowners from the
improvements, saying it would make it hard to sell property and could run the
risk of a lawsuit.
John Durbin, 815 Ash Street: Discussed the location of his home and a drain
installed in the neighbor’s back yard, questioned the incentive to remove a
clay tennis court on his property, and inquired about the credit program and
how one could be compensated for storing water.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood: Agrees with Trustee Kates’ opposition to credits,
since the money is still required for the program; asked if there can be
different rates for public and private tax-exempt properties; asked if houses
that previously flooded would have lower insurance premiums if the program was
implemented. Village Attorney Katherine Janega noted that there must be a
reasonable basis on which to build rate categories, and that the Village cannot
estimate impact on premiums, as floodplain insurance is uniform, with premiums
set by FEMA. Manager Bahan added that FEMA requires homes in the floodplain to
have flood insurance, and this requirement would remain even if the Tunnel is
constructed.
Steve Knaus, 905 Green Avenue: Expressed concern over project costs, especially
the “worst case scenario” in Table 11 of MFSG’s narrative report.
Ted Wynnychenko, 1086 Oak Street: Believes credits are important, and that the
Village should encourage residents to mitigate stormwater; opposes conditions
for credits to educational institutions, saying the Village should not dictate
the curriculum of the schools.
Lori Morse, Glencoe resident: Expressed a regional interest and asserted that
changes in Winnetka will affect taxpayers in neighboring communities; inquired
about changes to the Village’s NPDES permit and potential impacts to Lake
Michigan beaches and drinking water.
4)   Executive Session.
None.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Spinney, seconded by Trustee Buck, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:56 p.m.
