roll call vote the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Adams, Corrigan, Kates and
McCrary. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Braun and Buck.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   February 11, 2014 Study Session. (as corrected, p. 4)
      ii)   February 18, 2014 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated March 4, 2014 in the
amount of $511,605.04.
   c)   Bid #014-001: Hubbard Woods Parking Structure Maintenance & Repair. A
bid award of $214,645 to J. Gill & Company, for the maintenance and repair of
the Hubbard Woods Parking Deck. The project scope also includes painting
concrete and structural elements, as recommended in the Urban Land Institute
Study.
   d)   Fire Station Kitchen Renovation. A bid award of $79,900 to Prairie
Forge Group, for the Fire Station Kitchen Renovation Project.
      Trustee Adams, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to approve the
foregoing items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Adams, Corrigan, Kates and McCrary. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Braun and Buck.
6)   Stormwater Update.
None.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution R-5-2014: Northfield Intergovernmental Agreement for Fire
Prevention Services. Chief Berkowsky explained that after their fire inspector
retired, the Village of Northfield approached him with a request to provide
fire prevention services. He conducted a cost-benefit analysis to determine
feasibility of providing these services to Northfield, which revealed that it
would be possible if the hours of Winnetka’s two part-time fire inspectors are
increased.
      Chief Berkowsky recommended approval of an intergovernmental agreement
with Northfield, charging an annual fee of $21,238.60. The contract will
benefit Northfield, as it will not need to hire and train an employee to
provide fire prevention services. The benefits to Winnetka are: (i) it builds
on current regionalization of services between municipalities; (ii) it will
provide for 45% of the annual budgeted salaries of Winnetka’s two part-time
fire inspectors; and (iii) Winnetka’s Fire Prevention Bureau will now be
staffed on a more frequent basis.
      There were no questions from the Council or the audience. Trustee Kates
commended the Chief for his leadership of the Fire Department.
      Trustee McCrary, seconded by Trustee Adams, moved to adopt Resolution R-
5-2014. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Adams, Corrigan,
Kates and McCrary. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Braun and Buck.
   b)   Ordinance MC-2-2014: Adding Chapter 13.16 and Establishing a Stormwater
Utility – Adoption. Attorney Janega explained that the subject Ordinance had
been amended at the Council’s last meeting, and she presented the Ordinance in
its final form, with the amendments incorporated. There have been no further
amendments.
      Laurie Morse, 271 Hawthorne Avenue, Glencoe. Ms. Morse said Winnetka’s
stormwater management plan has no measurable goals for water quality or flood
control, and in her opinion these goals should be included.
      Attorney Janega pointed out that Ordinance MC-2-2014 does not adopt the
Stormwater Master Plan, but rather amends the Village Code to establish the
statutory basis for creating a stormwater utility.
      Trustee Kates, seconded by Trustee Corrigan, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-
2-2014. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Adams, Corrigan,
Kates and McCrary. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Braun and Buck.
   c)   Resolution R-4-2014: Establishing Stormwater Utility Fees and Charges –
Adoption. Attorney Janega said the subject Resolution sets the rate for
stormwater utility service following the same process used by the other Village
utilities. She noted that the issue has been discussed several times by the
Council in open meetings.
      There being no questions or comments, Trustee Adams, seconded by Trustee
McCrary, moved to adopt Resolution R-4-2014. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Adams, Corrigan, Kates and McCrary. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Braun and Buck.
   d)   Ordinance MC-3-2014: Adding Code Chapter 4.23 – Natural Gas Use Tax.
Attorney Janega reviewed the final draft of the subject Ordinance, which
establishes a new gross receipts tax for natural gas customers who buy from
out-of-state providers. She explained that she discussed the collection of the
new tax with attorneys for North Shore Gas and NICOR and made the final
amendments based on those discussions.
      i)   Amend and Adopt Ordinance MC-3-2014.
         Trustee Adams, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to amend Ordinance
MC-3-2014 as presented by the Village Attorney. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Adams, Corrigan, Kates and McCrary. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Braun and Buck.
      Trustee Adams, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to adopt Ordinance MC-
3-2014. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Adams, Corrigan,
Kates and McCrary. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Braun and Buck.
      ii)   Natural Gas Use Tax Collection Agreements – Authorization.
         Trustee Adams, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to authorize the
Village Manager or his designee to enter into natural gas tax collection
agreements with North Shore Gas and NICOR, substantially in conformance with
the terms presented and subject to approval of the final form by the Village
Attorney. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Adams,
Corrigan, Kates and McCrary. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Braun and Buck.
8)   Public Comment.
None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Reports.
   a)   Village President. President Greable announced that the Council would
discuss the ULI recommendations again at its next Study Session.
   b)   Trustees.
