MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
June 3, 2014
(Approved: June 24, 2014)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
3, 2014, at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Greable called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Arthur Braun, Carol Fessler, Richard Kates, William Krucks, Stuart McCrary and
Marilyn Prodromos. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant to the Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Katherine
Janega, Community Development Director Mike D'Onofrio, Public Works Director
Steve Saunders, Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky, Deputy Fire Chief John Ripka, and
approximately 33 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Greable led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   June 10, 2014 Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   June 17, 2014 Regular Meeting. President Greable announced that this
meeting would be rescheduled for June 24. All of the Council members present,
with the possible exception of Trustee Braun, indicated that they expected to
attend.
   c)   July 1, 2014 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Fessler, Kates,
Krucks, McCrary and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   May 20, 2014 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List for the period ending May 29,
2014, in the amount of $636,686.22.
   c)   Resolution R-19-2014: Prevailing Wage Resolution - Adopt. An annual
Resolution establishing the prevailing wage rates for the Village of Winnetka
public works and maintenance projects, as required by the Prevailing Wage Act.
   d)   Traffic Signal Modernization at Green Bay Road and Oak Street. Awarding
the contract for the traffic signal modernization project at Green Bay Road at
Oak Street to Home Towne Electric, Inc. in the amount of $208,745.86.
   e)   Outdoor Seating Permit for Trifecta Grill. Approving an outdoor seating
permit for Trifecta Grill, effective immediately, subject to Village approval
of the table layout.
   f)   Village Hall Standby Generator, Equipment Purchase Bid #014-007. An
authorization for the Village Manager to award a purchase order to Zonatherm
Products LLC, in the amount of $43,850 in accordance with the terms of Bid
#014-007.
   g)   Bid Award - 2004 Street Rehabilitation Program. Awarding a contract for
the 2014 Street Rehabilitation to A Lamp Concrete Contractors, in the total
amount of $776,252.38.
   Trustee McCrary, seconded by Trustee Fessler, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Fessler, Kates, Krucks, McCrary and Prodromos.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Stormwater Monthly Summary Report.
Mr. Saunders reviewed this monthly report from the Village's Stormwater Project
Manager on status, cost and schedule information for each of the Village's
stormwater projects. The Spruce Outlet project is a little behind schedule due
to wet weather in May, but is scheduled for completion by June 30. The Lloyd
Outlet project was completed under budget, and performed as expected during
recent heavy rain storms. The Winnetka Avenue Pump Station project is also
scheduled for completion by the end of June, and the new trash rack system
operated as designed during those same storms.
Mr. Saunders explained that work on the Northwest Winnetka project will proceed
once the final permit is issued by the Cook County Forest Preserve District
(CCFPD), and Governor Quinn signs a bill approving grant funding for the
project. The CCFPD referred the permit approval to the Real Estate Committee in
May, and Staff intends to schedule a meeting with Commissioner Suffredin before
the Board's June 17 meeting.
Finally, Mr. Saunders said the concept review report for the Willow Road Tunnel
project will be presented by MWH at the Council's rescheduled June 24 meeting.
Mr. Saunders answered a few questions from the Council pertaining the CCFPD
permit and the IKE Grant projects. He noted that the Northwest Winnetka project
will likely be constructed over two phases, fall of 2014 and spring of 2015,
due to the delays in permit and grant funding approvals.
Kim Knaus, 905 Greenwood, asked if constituents who are in favor of the
Northwest Winnetka project should contact Commissioner Suffredin, since it
seems that he is entertaining concerns from residents who oppose the project.
Mr. Saunders replied in the affirmative.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions. None.
8)   Public Comment. None.
9)   Old Business. None.
10)   New Business.
   a)   Proclamation: Thomas Fritts Day. Manager Bahan explained that Mr.
Fritts is approaching his 50th year with the Boy Scouts, and this is an
opportunity to honor his many years of service to the Scouts and Winnetka
community.
   President Greable read a proclamation honoring Thomas Fritts for his 50
years of service as Scoutmaster to Winnetka Boy Scout Troop 20.
   Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee Braun, moved to proclaim June 8, 2014,
Thomas Fritts Day. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Proclamation: Presence St. Francis Hospital Medical Services Day. Fire
Chief Alan Berkowsky said St. Francis Hospital has taught the majority of
paramedics in the area, and also provides monthly continuing education. He
thanked the organization for providing 40 years of training expertise in
emergency medical services.
   President Greable read a proclamation recognizing the 40th anniversary of
Presence St. Francis Hospital Emergency Medical Services System.
   Trustee Braun, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to proclaim June 3, 2014,
Presence St. Francis Hospital Emergency Medical Services Day. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
   c)   925-931 Green Bay Road - Request to Extend Special Use & Variation. Mr.
D'Onofrio reviewed the request from the owners of the Subject Property to
extend the date of construction associated with the special use permit and
variation that was granted when the Council approved Ordinance M-7-2013 on June
18, 2013. The permit requires construction to commence within one year of the
passage of the Ordinance. The applicant is requesting an extension to April 30,
2015, to enable more time to negotiate with the prospective tenant.
   Mr. D'Onofrio explained that the originally approved plan has not changed,
and the applicant is working closely with the prospective tenant. He said he
could not recall the Village previously receiving a request for an extension,
but noted that the situation is unique, and the applicant is working to get a
tenant that will be an asset to the community.
   Hal Francke, attorney for the applicant, Packer Associates, LLC (Packer).
Mr. Francke explained that the Subject Property has been owned by his client
for over 20 years, and Packer wants to do the right thing with the property.
The prospective tenant is the same user that was discussed last year at the
Plan Commission meetings, and has signed a fully negotiated letter of intent.
He explained that the delay is being caused by some changes in management at
the prospective tenant's corporate offices, and extra time is needed to finish
the deal. He noted that, regardless of who ultimately signs a lease for the
building, the construction of the parking lot according to the original plan
will go forward.
   The Council was in full agreement that the extension should be granted,
given the importance of the site in the Hubbard Woods business district, and
the fact that the plan for the property has not changed. Trustee Krucks
cautioned that he would not be comfortable granting a second extension in ten
months, but would recommend sending the applicant back before the Plan
Commission.
   Trustee Kates, seconded by Trustee Braun, moved to direct the Village
Attorney to prepare an ordinance to extend the Special Use Permit and variation
granted in Ordinance M-7-2013, until April 15, 2015. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Braun, Fessler, Kates, Krucks, McCrary and
Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
11)   Appointments.
   a)   President Greable announced the appointment of Jon Talty as Chair of
the Business Community Development Commission for a full term, effective
immediately. Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee Braun, moved to approve the
appointment. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   President Greable announced the re-appointment of Susan Curry to a full
term on the Landmark Preservation Commission, effective immediately. Trustee
