   Deputy Clerk
June 30, 2015
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL RESCHEDULED STUDY SESSION
June 30, 2015
(Approved: July 21, 2015)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
30, 2015 at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Greable called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Carol Fessler, William Krucks, Stuart McCrary, Scott Myers and
Marilyn Prodromos. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Village Attorney Karl Camillucci, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
Assistant Public Works Director Jim Bernahl and approximately 13 persons in the
audience.
2)   Stormwater Program Alternative Options.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the Village's actions on flood risk mitigation following
a violent storm in 2008 that caused severe flooding in parts of Winnetka.
Christopher B. Burke Engineering, Ltd. (CBBEL) was hired to study areas in
western Winnetka and provide flood mitigation recommendations, using the
standard 10-year protection level and providing overland flow routes for storms
beyond that intensity.
Mr. Saunders said CBBEL's study results were presented in 2009, along with
recommendations for a project that would provide 10-year level protection at a
cost of $8.1 million. Before taking action on any stormwater recommendations,
in 2010, the Council requested CBBEL to expand the study area to include
additional drainage areas throughout Winnetka for the same protection level.
The report and recommendations for the additional areas were presented in 2011,
just weeks before a severe storm caused serious widespread flooding; it was
later revealed that stormwater improvements designed for 10-year level
protection would have done little to alleviate the severe flooding from that
storm.
Mr. Saunders explained that the magnitude of the 2011 storm prompted the
Council to evaluate alternatives for 25-, 50-, and 100-year level storms. After
researching and modeling the higher caliber storms and exploring various
detention options, CBBEL presented separate recommendations for each of the
eight study areas, with a total estimated cost of $41.5 million; the Tunnel
option (a project comprised of five drainage areas) was estimated at $32.5
million in 2011 dollars. He said based upon the extreme difficulty in meeting
detention requirements in west Winnetka, the Council made the decision to
explore the feasibility of the Tunnel Project.
Mr. Saunders said the Village engaged engineering firms to conduct the
feasibility analysis for the Tunnel Project. In September 2012, the cost
estimate was increased to $34.6 million; and last April, with 30% of the Tunnel
design completed, the cost estimate was again increased, to $58.5 million.
Mr. Saunders reviewed possible next steps to further the goal of re-evaluating
previously rejected alternatives:
   ∙   Engage an engineering firm with expertise in evaluating serious large-
scale flooding issues and applying a variety of solutions. The consultant could
also be tasked with examining other solutions not previously brought to the
Village, and flesh them out with details about feasibility, as well as cost
projects.
   ∙   Re-examine the 100-year protection level the Village has established.
   ∙   Seek out a community with distinctive topography similar to that of
western Winnetka, to see if a creative solution was found to move storm water.
   ∙   Re-approach other government agencies regarding land use for detention.
   ∙   Identify acceptable methodologies to quantify benefits and cost for
STADI and non-STADI alternatives.
Mr. Saunders explained that the timeline for any of the above projects is
dependent upon engaging the qualified specialists and identifying other
alternatives; however, he recommended issuing a Request for Proposals (RFP) as
soon as possible so a bid could be awarded in late summer or early fall. He
suggested working on community outreach and communication in the meantime,
using the Village's stormwater website and easily-digestible fact sheets, as
well as providing opportunities to gather public feedback.  
Trustee Cripe asked if there is any way to expedite the review process, and
also inquired about constructing the western Winnetka stormwater project in
phases.
Mr. Saunders noted that Winnetka's drainage basins have been extensively
modeled and the Village owns the results of these studies; therefore, once an
engineering firm is selected, it just needs to study and understand the
collected data - not reinvent it - which will save time. He added that the
western Winnetka project could be broken into phases while the Village
negotiates with other agencies and stakeholders.
Trustee Prodromos asked how the Village expects to bypass the constraints on
detention possibilities based on policies of the Forest Preserve District and
local school districts.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village had specific projects to discuss in its
first round of talks with the various land owners in the Village; however,
there could be other ways of achieving flood reduction goals. He said, for
example, that the Forest Preserve District could be interested in a project if
it also furthers their mission.
Trustee Myers agreed that all of the possibilities need to be examined with
fresh eyes, and a consulting firm should be chosen that can mix various
elements and devise a creative solution. He suggested a cost-benefit analysis
also be applied to the Lake, and encouraged continued communication with the
community.
President Greable called for audience questions.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo said she was happy to hear that
communication and outreach are being planned, and she asked where the
stormwater utility estimating tool might be found.
Mr. Saunders explained that a stormwater estimator had been placed on the
Village's stormwater website prior to implementing the utility to facilitate
calculation of individual utility fees. Now that the fee is in place, the
estimator is no longer necessary, and has been removed.
King Poor, 735 Walden Road Mr. Poor asked if the Village is aware of green
infrastructure projects in other communities such as Glenview and Hinsdale, who
the design engineers of such projects are, and whether those firms would
receive a copy of the Village's RFP.
Mr. Saunders said other communities are undertaking a variety of solutions,
including green infrastructure on private properties; however, he noted that
green infrastructure can assist with handling stormwater runoff, but cannot
cope with a large-scale storm. He added, however, that firms utilizing Best
Management Practices (BMPs) will receive a copy of the RFP.
Jim Gordon, 281 White Oak Lane. Mr. Gordon asked if the Village had considered
spending more money for the new engineering studies, given that the Village
will want estimates for BMPs, detention, pumping and other methods.
Mr. Saunders explained that he devised the cost estimate for the studies by
reviewing what the Village has paid for previous studies, examining hourly
rates taken from recent RFPs, and calculating about five months' of work. He
felt comfortable that his total projected cost of $150,000 is reasonable to
take pre-existing information and develop alternatives for western Winnetka.
Baird Smart, 112 Church Road. Mr. Smart asked how much of the original
projected $41 million in stormwater projects was earmarked for acquisition of
land for detention, and also asked if detention could be built under the
streets in western Winnetka.
Mr. Saunders said there has been no estimation on costs to acquire land from
other agencies; furthermore, using the area under public streets for detention
is the most expensive option available, as other utilities also need that
space. He explained that the cost per acre foot for detention from permeable
pavers is expensive compared to traditional detention ponds.
President Greable called for comments from the audience.
Ms. Balsamo said she thinks Mr. Saunders' reports are thorough and concise, and
she urged the Council to take up the public relations efforts that Mr. Saunders
has been recommending all along, as a strategy to inform the populace and
reduce rancor in the community.
Debbie Ross, 921 Tower Road. Ms. Ross encouraged the Council to study BMPs for
a solution, and to partner with non-governmental agencies which can help
individual homeowners mitigate flooding on their own property.
After a further brief discussion, the Council reached consensus to issue an RFP
to engage an engineering firm to review and evaluate previous alternatives, as
well as recommend other possibilities to provide flood risk reduction to
western Winnetka.
President Greable encouraged residents to voice their opinions by sending
emails to the Village's dedicated stormwater email address:
Stormwatercomments@winnetka.org.
3)   Public Comment.
None.
12)   Executive Session.
Trustee Krucks, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to adjourn into Executive
Session to discuss Collective Bargaining, pursuant to Section 2(c)(2) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee McCrary seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler, Krucks, McCrary, Myers
and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Greable announced that the Council would not return to the open
meeting after Executive Session. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 8:55 p.m.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:56 p.m.
