July 14, 2015
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
July14, 2015
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
14, 2015 at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Greable called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, William Krucks, Stuart McCrary, and Marilyn Prodromos. Absent:
Trustees Carol Fessler and Scott Myers. Also in attendance: Assistant to the
Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Karl Camillucci, Director of
Water & Electric Brian Keys, and 1 person in the audience.
2)   Transformer Yard Fire Protection Assessment.
Mr. Keys explained that in 2014, the Village engaged Strand Associates to
examine fire protection options for the Electric Plant and adjacent Transformer
Yard (Yard). In March, Strand presented their findings, along with two
recommended options for protection at the Yard: (A) installation of a sprinkler
system and blast walls between the four transformers; (B) replacement of the
existing transformers with new ones that are filled with fluid that has a
higher flash point. After much deliberation, the Council did not reach a
consensus on an option for the Yard and requested staff to research other
alternatives.
Mr. Keys described the location of the Yard and the placement of the
transformers, which are spaced close together and filled with mineral oil. He
noted that three of the transformers were manufactured in 1970, and one in
1995, and he explained that the expected life of a transformer is 40-50 years.
It is nearly impossible to pinpoint when a transformer will fail, since the
lifespan is dependent upon its insulation, which deteriorates over time from
the effects of temperature, moisture and air.
Mr. Keys explained that a recommended option from the previous Strand report
called for replacement of all four transformers. However, the 1995 unit has 20
or more years of useful life, and could be retrofilled with a higher flash-
point lubricant. He said Staff has developed a hybrid option (Option C) that
combines replacing three older transformers and retrofilling the newer one,
along with the other protection measures recommended in the Strand report.
Mr. Keys noted that cost estimates for the construction of new transformers has
been updated and is higher than originally projected by Strand. Option C would
cost more than installation of a deluge fire protection system (Option A), but
less than replacing all four transformers (Option B). He said it represents a
middle ground between the two recommendations presented by Strand.
The Council discussed the options, noting that spending $213,000 on a deluge
system for equipment that will likely require replacement in a few years is not
financially prudent. Mr. Keys explained that moving the equipment farther apart
is not feasible, as it would it be very costly and the space constraints of the
Yard make expansion nearly impossible.
President Greable called for public comment.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood. Mr. Liss asked if a barrier wall would provide
enough protection or if replacing three transformers at once would provide
economies of scale.
Mr. Keys explained the barrier walls are not sufficient to halt a fire, which
is why Strand recommended a deluge system if the old transformers are retained.
On the other hand, the deluge protection system is not needed if the older
transformers are replaced and the newer one is retrofilled. Mr. Keys said he
could research the issue of saving money by ordering three new transformers at
the same time; however, Budget constraints would dictate such a purchase.
Trustee McCrary preferred the recommended hybrid Option C, but not necessarily
replacing all of the older transformers at once. The other Trustees were in
agreement that the hybrid Option C was the best choice, as installing a deluge
system to protect the older transformers was not a financially viable option.
President Greable said he was struggling to support Option A because it is the
cheapest one, and he enquired about purchasing a spare transformer in case one
of the older transformers failed.
Mr. Keys explained that if Option A is chosen, the old transformers will have
to be run longer to justify the expense of a deluge system. He said any of the
options will work, and that doing nothing is the only non-viable choice.
The Council discussed buying a spare transformer, or using one of the older
ones as a backup; however, the consensus was that spending money on Option A
was a waste of taxpayer dollars. It was suggested that Option C be implemented
in phases, to keep the project within budget constraints, and Mr. Keys agreed
that design could be done in the current budget cycle, with construction in the
next two budget years.
Mr. Keys said the fire protection measures could be discussed again at budget
time, where a preferred option and possible phasing could be finalized.
Trustee McCrary said the budget analysis needs to communicate that Option A
also would include the cost of replacement a few years later - which will add
significantly to its cost.
Ms. Pierce explained that Staff is working on the Budget calendar, and the
matter will be scheduled for more discussion in October.
3)   Public Comment.
Jeffrey Liss, Edgewood Avenue. Mr. Liss commended the contractor of the
Northwest Winnetka Stormwater Project for keeping traffic flowing and residents
informed during the difficult construction period. He also lauded the Park
District for the July 4th events and turnout, but suggested more emphasis on
the celebration of Independence Day instead of the 4th of July.
4)   Executive Session.
None.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Krucks, seconded by Trustee Prodromos, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:24 p.m.
