September 1, 2015
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
September 1, 2015
(Approved: September 15, 2015)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 1, 2015, at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Greable called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Carol Fessler, William Krucks, Stuart McCrary, Scott Myers and
Marilyn Prodromos. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant to the Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter M.
Friedman, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Director of Community
Development Mike D'Onofrio, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Police
Chief Patrick Kreis , and approximately 23 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Greable led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   September 8, 2015 Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   September 15, 2015 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   October 6, 2015 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee McCrary, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
5)   Consent Agenda.
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   August 18, 2015 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated August 14-27, 2015 in
the amount of $531,625.38.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-16-2015: Request for Village to Vacate Alley Right-of-
Way Adjacent to 319 Fairview - Adoption. An Ordinance approving the vacation
and sale of a portion of an unimproved alley adjacent to the Subject Property.
   d)   Ordinance No. M-17-2015: Special Use Permit for Yoga and Fitness Studio
in C-2 Retail Overlay District, 549 Lincoln Avenue - Adoption. An Ordinance
granting a special use permit to allow the operation of Yoga Barre, a boutique
fitness studio, in the Retail Overlay District.
   e)   Resolution No. R-26-2015: Third Amendment to a License Agreement with
New Cingular Wireless PCS, LLC - Adoption. Approval of the Third Amendment to
the 1996 license agreement with New Cingular Wireless, substantially in the
form presented.
   Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler, Krucks, McCrary, Myers and Prodromos.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Stormwater.
   a)   Willow Road STADI Project: Independent Engineer's Cost Estimate Review.
Mr. Saunders summarized developments from the MWH Review Point #2 presentation
and discussion in April. The significant increase in the cost estimates
resulted in the Council authorizing a third-party review of the cost
projections. He reviewed the process used by V3 Companies, the engineering firm
hired to perform the cost evaluation, and introduced Mr. Greg Wolterstorff,
Vice President of V3, to give the report.
   Mr. Wolterstorff explained that he used the same cost estimating approach
for the open-cut construction that a contractor would use; and then he brought
in expertise from Hatch Mott MacDonald (HMM) to estimate the tunneling costs
based on their proprietary database. Significant drivers of the increased cost
estimates include:
   ∙   Winnetka's. narrow roadways restrict construction, resulting in the
addition of 10% more installation time.
   ∙   Construction recycling requirements add the burden of a 2 ½ hour round-
trip and payment of tipping fees to dispose of the waste, which added
significantly to the cost.
   ∙   Utility relocation added $600,000.
   Mr. Wolterstorff reported that HMM projected the tunneling costs would be
higher than MWH projected, based on the following:
   ∙   Two shifts would be required to accommodate MWH's progression estimates.
   ∙   The cost per foot of concrete for the 96-inch pipe was deemed to be more
expensive.
   ∙   The contingency was raised to 30% based on (i) a gravel layer east of
Sheridan Road that could cause unforeseen tunneling problems; (ii) the design
is only 30% finalized; (iii) the outfall system needs significantly more
design; and (iv) requirements for crossing under the railroad could impact
final plans.
   Mr. Saunders said the Council could choose to perform a cost estimate
reconciliation by engaging MWH and V3 to work together to achieve a single best
estimate for the STADI project. The result would be a cost estimate the Village
could have a fair degree of confidence in, if the Council is interested in
attaining one. He added that the Council also has the option to commission a
value engineering project to develop the best and most cost-effective version
of the STADI project; it could also approve finalizing the permit applications
and submitting them to the permitting agencies. He noted that there is almost
no chance a project can be designed that will meet the original 2012 cost
projections; therefore a value engineering exercise would have limited, if any,
worth. He explained that the permit approval process is lengthy and intensive,
and would require a lot of staff time. He recommended holding off on the permit
application submittals if the Council is entertaining serious doubts about
moving forward with the STADI project.
   Finally, Mr. Saunders explained that the Village may want to program some
additional water quality programs into the 2016 Budget, with the goal of
targeting pollutants the Village can take actions to control, such as bacteria,
oil, grease and phosphorus. He noted that mercury comes from the atmosphere,
and is not under the purview of the Village to reduce or eliminate.
   Mr. Saunders said the Request for Proposals (RFP) to re-evaluate potential
non-tunnel alternatives for significant flood relief has garnered 14 responses,
which staff is reviewing with the anticipation of bringing a preliminary bid
award recommendation to the Council later in September or in October.
   The Council asked questions at length, and confirmed with Mr. Wolterstorff
that his 30% contingency recommendation would not change with further analysis;
he also verified that a final project cost would very likely come in above $60
million, even after a trueing up process with MWH. Mr. Saunders reiterated that
staff would need to spend significant amounts of time answering questions about
the STADI project if the permit applications were submitted.
   The Council asked Mr. Saunders what lessons had been learned from the STADI
process. Mr. Saunders explained that in the future, initial cost estimates will
be considered as a range since there are so many unknowns at the conceptual
stage. Another lesson is that once a few conceptual alternatives are chosen,
some money must be spent up front to develop enough detail to make an informed
decision. And finally, the Village has learned that a major infrastructure
project requires a thorough community process in order to achieve buy-in from
the public.
   President Greable called for audience comment.
   Howard Jessen, 225 Ridge Road. Mr. Jessen asked if the Council would still
consider pursuing the STADI project in spite of the fact that it would cost
upwards of $58 million.
   Richard Kates, 1326 Tower Road. Mr. Kates said the Council needs more facts,
and recommended moving forward with the permit submittals and the cost
reconciliation; however, he was against performing value engineering. He added
that any western solutions will center around acquiring land for detention.
   Ann Wilder, Winnetka. Ms. Wilder pointed out that the STADI cost projections
do not include the financing costs; and noted that the 14 respondents to the
RFP must believe there is a viable alternative solution, or they wouldn't
submit proposals.
   Debbie Ross, 921 Tower Road. Ms. Ross requested the Council to halt all
spending of taxpayer money on the STADI project until the alternaties are
evaluated.
   Crispin Hales, 1000 Sheridan Road. Mr. Hales said the stormwater utility fee
is based upon the original STADI cost estimate, and that both his utility fee
and the tunnel cost will likely wind up being double the original projections.
He urged the Council to stop the STADI project and look at other options.
   The Council discussed the Village's options thoroughly, and a near-unanimous
consensus was reached to halt any further work on the STADI project in favor of
exploring alternative approaches that may include a combination of green
infrastructure and detention. An appeal was made to the community that fought
so hard to protect Lake Michigan, to be equally committed to protecting their
neighbors' homes and the schools by supporting a solution to the chronic
flooding in southwest Winnetka.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-6-2015: Amending Winnetka Village Code Regarding Water
Conservation - Amend & Adopt. Mr. Keys reviewed the background of the subject
Code amendment, which was introduced in July. As a result of concerns raised at
that time, the Ordinance has been revised to change the odd/even requirement to
non-consecutive watering days, and a provision was inserted that temporarily
exempts new landscaping and grass from the watering restrictions. In addition,
some minor language was added at the request of the Illinois Department of
Natural Resources (IDNR).
   Mr. D'Onofrio added that there would be no impact on the plumbing code for
residents and builders, as the majority of fixtures sold now have water
conservation devices built in.
   After a brief discussion, Trustee McCrary, seconded by Trustee Fessler,
moved to amend Ordinance No. MC-6-2015 as described in the agenda materials. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler, Krucks,
McCrary, Myers and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Prodromos, moved to adopt No. MC-6-2015
as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Fessler, Krucks, McCrary, Myers and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-18-2015: 525 Elm Street, Variations for the
Construction and Use of a Detached Garage -Introduction / Adoption. Mr.
D'Onofrio reviewed this request for a zoning variation to allow the replacement
of a detached garage.
   There being no audience or Council discussion, Trustee Fessler, seconded by
Trustee Krucks, moved to waive introduction of Ordinance No. M-18-2015. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler, Krucks, McCrary,
Myers and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee Krucks, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
18-2015. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler,
Krucks, McCrary, Myers and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8)   Public Comment.
Eleanor Prince, Kenilworth. Ms. Prince opined that the One Winnetka Planned
Development is too large and should not be approved, and she submitted written
comments to the Council.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
   a)   Downtown Master Plan Steering Committee. Manager Bahan reviewed the
membership of the Steering Committee, contained in the agenda packet materials.
He explained that the meetings will be noticed to the public to comply with the
Illinois Open Meetings Act, maintain transparency, and maximize community
input. The working group membership is still being refined and will be provided
at a later date.
   There being no questions or comments, Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee
Fessler, moved to approve the selection of the members of the Downtown Master
Plan Steering Committee. By voice vote, the motion carried.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Reports.
   a)   Village President. None.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Prodromos reported on the last Business Community
Development Commission meeting and added that in 2015 fifteen new businesses
have opened in Winnetka.
      ii)   Trustee Fessler reported on the last Plan Commission meeting.
   c)   Attorney. None,
   d)   Manager. None.
13)   Executive Session.
None.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Prodromos, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
