September 8, 2015
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
September 8, 2015
(Approved: October 6, 2015)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 8, 2015, 2015 at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Greable called the meeting to order at 7:03 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Carol Fessler, William Krucks, Stuart McCrary, Scott Myers and
Marilyn Prodromos. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Community Development Director Mike D'Onofrio, Village Attorney Karl
Camillucci, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, and approximately 11
persons in the audience.
2)   Board and Commission Annual Update.
President Greable thanked the Village's advisory boards for their hard work and
dedication, and invited the Chair of each committee to deliver their annual
progress report, which lists policy issues, significant accomplishments, and
recommendations to the Council.
   a)   Plan Commission: Tina Dalman, Chair.
      Policy Issues: Commercial zoning amendments; One Winnetka Planned
Development; New Trier High School special use permit; and Hubbard Woods Park
special use permit.
      Projects: Completed review of commercial zoning regulations and issued
recommendations last fall. Considered the One Winnetka Planned Development over
six meetings, beginning last March.
      Recommendations: Increase the cooperation and coordination with other
local governmental bodies on major initiatives and projects; adopt formal
processes for public hearings for all advisory bodies; and review standards for
approval of development and zoning applications, to ensure consistency or
process.
   b)   Business Community Development Commission (BCDC): Jon Talty, Chair.
      Policy Issues: One Winnetka Planned Development; assisting Economic
Development Coordinator; Downtown Master Planning process; and promoting the
Winnetka brand.
      Projects: Participation in Downtown Master Planning process;
communicating the business community's perspective on the One Winnetka Planned
Development application; and invite businesses to the October BCDC meeting to
discuss the state of the current local marketplace.
      Recommendations: Continue implementing ULI recommendations and reinforce
the BCDC as a stakeholder in the Downtown Master Plan process, keep the
Commission relevant.
   b)   Design Review Board (DRB): John Swierk, Chair.
      Projects: Reviewed 14 applications, including New Trier High School's
expansion and the Hubbard Woods Park renovation.
      Recommendations: Please include an option for larger projects to receive
non-binding comments at an earlier stage; particularly early review and
cooperation between the Village and other government units, including School
and Park Districts. Doing so will give more leverage to the Village over design
issues.
   c)   Landmark Preservation Commission (LPC): Louise Holland, Chair.
      Policy Issues: Demolition applications; Historic & Architectural Impact
studies; 60-day delay periods.
      Projects: Reviewed 24 demolition applications; hosted a Landmark Trolley
Tours, which were very successful.
      Recommendations: Consider appointing Paul Weaver to the LPC; consider
increasing the delay for demolition permits of historic homes to six months
from two months; please include the list of Winnetka landmarks and National
historic places in the fall issue of the Winnetka Report; request a budget
allowance not to exceed $1,500 for a second year of the Trolley Tours in the
spring of 2016; and also requests a budget of $600 for the annual Landmark
Preservation Awards.
   d)   Zoning Board of Appeals (ZBA): Joni Johnson, Chair.
      Policy Issues: Considered 16 zoning variation cases; one mixed special
use/variation case; three special use applications.
      Projects: The ZBA will start consideration of the One Winnetka
application once the Plan Commission review is concluded.
      Recommendations: Draft revisions to the ZBA's 1996 Rules and Regulations,
which are very antiquated (drafted before the PUD Ordinance was passed).
   e)   Environmental & Forestry Commission (EFC): Chuck Dowding, Chair.
      Policy Issues: Net Metering.
      Projects: Monitor effectiveness of: downtown recycling receptacles; coal
tar ban; construction recycling ordinance. Incentivize residents to increase
their rate of recycling. Also of interest: studying the efficiency of small
refuse haulers on long driveways throughout the Village.
      Recommendations: Provide guidance on preferences for proposed activities.
      After hearing all of the reports and thorough Council discussion about
their recommendations with the committee Chairs, President Greable encouraged
the Trustees to give their comments to Manager Bahan.
      Manager Bahan said Staff would synthesize feedback from tonight's meeting
and develop some recommended action items for the Council.
3)   Public Comment.
Ted Wynnychenko, Oak Street. Mr. Wynnychenko expressed frustration that the
Village will not consider a net metering program.
Richard Kates, 1326 Tower Road. Mr. Kates said he did not approve of net
metering and furthermore it is a small issue concerning a small amount of money
and not worth the Council's time. He also asked what the Village is doing to
educate the community about the coal tar ban.
Trustee Cripe pointed out that the Council was not considering the issue of net
metering at this meeting. The time was scheduled for presentations by the
various advisory bodies of the Village and not for policy discussions and
decisions.
4)   Executive Session. None.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee Krucks, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:55 p.m.
