(Approved: October 6, 2015)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 15, 2015, at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
   President Greable called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Carol Fessler, William Krucks, Stuart McCrary, Scott Myers and
Marilyn Prodromos. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant to the Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter M.
Friedman, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and approximately 12 persons in
the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
   President Greable led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   October 4, 2015 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   b)   October 13, 2015 Study Session. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   c)   October 19, 2015 Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
   d)   October 20, 2015 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expected to attend.
4)   Approval of the Agenda.
   Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.  
      i)   September 1, 2015 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated August 27 – September
10, 2015 in the amount of $1,248,778.63.
      Trustee McCrary, seconded by Trustee Fessler, moved to approve the
foregoing items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler, Krucks, McCrary, Myers and
Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
6)   Stormwater.
   a)   Stormwater Monthly Summary Report. Mr. Saunders reported that the
Northwest Winnetka stormwater project is nearing completion, with pond
restoration and pavement work finished. He added that the Forest Glen area is
currently undergoing storm sewer work; the project is expected to be finished
ahead of schedule if the weather holds. Next up for stormwater: evaluation of
non-STADI stormwater projects to bring relief to the southwest Winnetka study
area.
   b)   RFP #015-013 – Evaluation of Non-STADI Alternatives for Western and
Southwestern Winnetka. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village wishes to re-
evaluate stormwater projects for this area of the Village, now that the STADI
project has been put on hiatus. In addition, it is hoped that other creative
and cost effective non-tunnel solutions can be identified in tandem with the
assessment of previously studied flood reduction measures.
   After reviewing the Request for Proposals (RFP) process, Mr. Saunders
explained that the recommended firm was chosen by a team of Village officials
following interviews of the top four respondents. He said the fee estimates
were submitted in a separate, sealed envelope, which was not opened until the
RFP reviews were concluded, to keep the focus on qualifications and experience.
He noted that the core aspects of the evaluation project are: (i) ensure the
technical work is reliable and accurate; and (ii) facilitate significant public
participation in order to achieve consensus to move forward with what will
likely be a major infrastructure project. The end result is expected to be a
series of three to five alternatives for Winnetka as a community to evaluate
based on cost, proposed effectiveness and duration of implementation phase(s).
   Mr. Saunders said Strand Associates did not submit the lowest fee proposal,
and he does not lightly recommend passing over the lowest bidder; however, the
value their team will bring to the project is significant and worth the cost.
He introduced the project team leaders: John Lyons and Mark Shubak of Strand,
and Gary Wolnitzek of Human Nature, a specialist in community engagement.
   Mr. Lyons described Strand's approach, which includes:
   ∙   Verifying the existing stormwater modeling;
   ∙   Examining information from the area's natural systems, like topography
and soil conditions; and from manmade elements, such as infrastructure and
property boundaries;
   ∙   Taking into account demographic information and comprehensive planning
tools;
   ∙   Examining the entire watershed to understand how upstream areas may
contribute to problems in the affected areas; and
   ∙   Creating partnerships whenever possible with other entities such as the
park district, schools and forest preserve to perhaps advance the Village's
flood risk reduction goals, while at the same time helping to further the
mission of these other local units.
   Using this wide array of information, Strand will recommend holistic and
appropriate solutions that will include both traditional and innovative new
technologies. Mr. Lyons noted that the Village Council and community will need
to have a conversation about the level of protection during this process; and
Strand will provide the data so an informed decision can be made.
   Next, Mr. Shubak reviewed Strand's technical methodologies, including:
   ∙   Using a state-of-the art modeling platform that provides accurate
results and demonstrates performance;
   ∙   Connecting the use of green technology with more traditional engineering
solutions;
   ∙   Leveraging past investments through fresh review and data collection;
and
   ∙   Reviewing construction costs using value engineering principles, recent
bid tabulations and unit prices, risk analysis, and detailed contingency
allowances.
   He stressed that green infrastructure alone cannot solve Southwest
Winnetka's flooding problems; however, it can be used effectively to augment
and reduce the scale of more traditional mitigation approaches.
   Finally, Mr. Wolnitzek described the approach to public engagement:
   ∙   Raise awareness in the community through the use of public meetings;
   ∙   Use community input to explore alternatives; and
   ∙   Build consensus by creating a supported vision.
   Responding to a request for clarification about the bid process, Mr.
Saunders explained that the proposals were reviewed separately from their cost
information, to gauge the bidders' understanding of the project and to
establish that they were qualified to perform the work. When the fees were
opened, the understanding of the project was commensurate with the proposed
fees. He noted, for example, the lowest bid of $38,000 displayed a complete
lack of understanding of the project scope.
   Next, Mr. Saunders was asked to elaborate on the project timeline. He said
the proposed six-month schedule begins with the notice to proceed and ends with
a presentation to the Council and community about the various mitigation
alternatives that were devised. After that discussion, there will be additional
public process to narrow the field of options, which will begin at the end of
this project's six-month timeframe.
   When requested to give an approximation of how much of the existing
engineering could be extracted and used for alternative project formation, Mr.
Shubak estimated that a substantial amount could be used for the modeling
verification; however, any new and innovative measures will be started from the
conceptual stage. Mr. Lyons assured the Council that Strand will capitalize on
as much of the prior work as possible.
   When asked what form the community engagement would take, Mr. Wolnitzek
explained that there may be open houses, or there could be community meetings
with presentations. The best approach for Winnetka has not yet been formulated.
   The Council expressed concern about accurate cost approximations, given past
experience with the STADI project.
   Mr. Saunders explained that one of the strongest elements of Strand's
proposal is their risk register. In tandem with identifying and evaluating
alternative solutions, areas of risk that could impact a potential project are
also pinpointed and further investigated. He noted that in some cases,
engineering on risk areas may be advanced, to gain understanding that could
limit the risk of a cost projection escalating precipitously.
   After the Council discussion, Mr. Saunders advised that the project is not
designed to offer the community a single, ultimate solution. Rather, the
purpose of the public participation is to examine a number of possible
solutions, and to reach consensus on the best one.
   President Greable called for audience questions and comments.
   Richard Kates, 1326 Tower Road. Mr. Kates asked if Strand had ever dealt
with an area like Winnetka in the past, and what types of solutions had been
recommended.
   Mr. Saunders explained that a lot of the firms responding to the Village's
RFP had similar approaches, that the solutions are scalable to fit the
particular community. He added that outcomes are driven by the specific
conditions each community faces.
   Mr. Lyons noted that Strand has applied its methodology to urban and semi-
rural areas and everything in between; and the spectrum of solutions varies
according to each community's circumstances.
   Mike Benoit, 1288 Sunview Lane. Mr. Benoit asked where he could find details
of the study area, and he recommended storing water upstream in the Botanic
Gardens.
   Mr. Saunders said the map of the study area is on page 20 of the agenda
packet; Mr. Lyons added that he couldn't yet determine the upper boundary of
the project area.
   Ann Wilder, Winnetka. Ms. Wilder asked if unincorporated Winnetka is going
to be studied, and stated she didn't feel they were community stakeholders as
they don't pay Winnetka taxes.
   Mr. Shubak explained that Strand will not be trying to "solve" any drainage
problems in unincorporated Winnetka; however, the Indian Hill golf course may
provide an opportunity for detention, which would provide a benefit to the
Village.
   Doug, Northbrook. Doug was interested in finding out if landscaping with
certain shrubbery could help with solving the flooding problems.
   Mr. Saunders said the modeling has to make careful assumptions, and can't
rely on things homeowners may or may not do with their landscaping.
   Bill Johnson, 214 Essex. Mr. Johnson asked if Strand has ever encountered a
situation where they could not craft a solution.
   Mr. Shubak explained that he has seen a few instances where physical
constraints simply did not allow for the desired level of flood control. He
noted that infrastructure is the preferred solution; however, in those cases,
buyouts of the worst hit areas were recommended. He said there is no way of
telling at this point whether that could happen in Winnetka.
   Mr. Saunders added that it may be revealed that the objective of 100-year
protection against structural flooding cannot be met. He stressed that this
doesn't mean the Village couldn't get most of its goals met. It is possible
that there could be alternative mitigation solutions that are more cost-
effective than achieving the 100-year protection.
   Mr. Kates criticized the recommendation to form partnerships with other
units of government at the mid-point of the project, as he felt this aspect
will take a longer time. He therefore urged Strand to start negotiations with
the other local entities at the beginning of the project. He also pointed out
that some of the community's flooding has been caused by sump pump failures and
sanitary sewer backups, and Strand needs to determine where the stormwater
runoff problems occur.
   Mr. Benoit asked the Council to consider a holiday of the stormwater utility
fee until the Village has a project and cost estimates that will determine what
the fee will ultimately be.
   Manager Bahan explained that the initial utility fee structure is funding
approximately $500,000 of operations and maintenance, and that approximately
$11 million of the $16.5 million in bonds that were issued will be spent on
stormwater projects by the end of 2015. He said the projects for the remaining
target areas will be costly, and the Village is collecting proportionately.
   Mr. Johnson recommended revisiting the financing issue to identify the
households that will benefit the most from stormwater projects. Manager Bahan
noted that the fee rationale chosen by the Council was to spread costs evenly,
as it is a community problem. In addition, it would be extremely complex to
bill for different amounts.
   Matt Wendt, 607 Willow Road. Mr. Wendt urged the Council to offer New Trier
High School new baseball netting in exchange for the use of some land for
detention; and he also recommended sending proposals to the Forest Preserve and
golf course. He said the solution should be regional and could very well
include the Botanic Gardens
   Ms. Wilder said she was hopeful a solution that uses the latest technology,
unifies the Village, and is the right choice for all of Winnetka and the region
can be identified, and she cautioned against communication that is only public
relations in nature.
   Trustee Krucks, seconded by Trustee Fessler, moved to authorize Staff to
negotiate a contract with Strand Associates to complete a stormwater management
study, subject to Council approval at a Council meeting in October. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler, Krucks, McCrary,
Myers and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions:
None.
8)   Public Comment.
None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business:
None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Reports.
   a)   Village President. None.
   b)   Trustees. None.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None.
13)   Executive Session.
Trustee Myers moved to adjourn into Executive Session to discuss personnel
matters, collective bargaining, appointment of a person to a public office, and
the lease or purchase of land, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1), 2(c)(2), 2(c)(3),
and 2(c)(5) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Krucks seconded the
motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler,
Krucks, McCrary, Myers and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Greable announced that the Council would not return to the open
meeting after Executive Session. The Council adjourned into Executive Session
at 9:55 p.m.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Prodromos, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 10:46 p.m.
