   c)   Ordinance No. M-25-2015: Special Use Permit for Architectural Office in
C-2 Retail Overlay District, 523 Chestnut Street – Introduction / Adoption. Mr.
D'Onofrio reviewed this request to locate an architectural office in vacant
ground floor space previously occupied by a retail store. He explained that
professional offices are permitted only as a special use in the C-2 Retail
Overlay District; and the request was reviewed and approved by the Plan
Commission. He noted that recent amendments to the special use application
process reduced approval time for the request by approximately three to five
weeks. He said the Council could waive introduction if it votes unanimously to
do so.
   Trustee McCrary requested clarification that the change of use from a salon
to an office would not trigger the Village's fire sprinkler requirement for
commercial buildings.
   Arthur Braun, the owner of the building, explained the building is fully
outfitted with fire sprinklers, and is therefore in compliance with Fire Codes.
   During a brief discussion about parking requirements, Mr. D'Onofrio noted
that the applicant received a waiver from the parking study requirement from
the Plan Commission. During testimony before the Plan Commission, the applicant
said clients park in the Village lot across the street from the Subject
Property.
   Trustee Fessler, seconded by Trustee Prodromos, moved to waive introduction
of Ordinance M-25-2015. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Fessler, Krucks, McCrary, Myers and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Krucks, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance M-25-
2015. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler,
Krucks, McCrary, Myers and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Resolution No. R-43-2015: Approving an Agreement with Burns & McDonnell
Engineering Company, Inc. for Professional Services – Adoption. Mr. Keys noted
that during the 2016 Budget meetings in October, the water distribution system
was discussed. With 72 miles of water main in Winnetka, the Village needs to
identify a replacement program, as well as sources of funding. Staff issued a
Request for Proposals (RFP) for a contractor to develop a water main
replacement plan, including prioritization; complete a rate study; and a
recommended fee structure to fund the replacement program.
   Mr. Keys explained that although three other firms bid lower, staff selected
Burns & McDonnell based on the talent they displayed, along with positive
references from previous employers. He added that a similar project completed
for the City of DeKalb garnered very positive feedback.
   Trustee Cripe expressed concern that so many mains will be recommended for
replacement that field studies will also need to be undertaken to get an extra
layer of data for prioritization purposes.
   Mr. Keys cautioned that a water main replacement program should not be
thought of as a short-term fix; rather, it is a decades-long initiative. The
study will give a level or priority and the Council will be asked to provide
direction on how to fund the program.
   Responding to a question about what qualifications made Burns & McDonnell
stand out, Mr. Keys explained they have more technical experience than any of
the other responding firms, and they have extensive experience doing rate
studies.
   Trustee Myers said he was concerned about the uncertainty factor when it
comes to the quality of the water mains and which ones should be replaced
first.
   Mr. Keys said there will be some level of uncertainty about which pipes may
fail first. He explained the replacement program will set a framework to work
with; then each year it can be evaluated and re-prioritized based on new
conditions. He added that the Council's decision on funding will determine the
plan that is ultimately put in place.
   Mr. Keys noted that after the recommendations are heard in the spring, there
will be follow-up required and the consultants may be brought back for further
discussion. He said he would like to wrap up discussions before the next budget
cycle, in order to develop the plan and implementation strategy in 2017. It was
noted that community discussions will also be required in 2017, prior to plan
development.
   Trustee Fessler asked what sources Burns & McDonnell will use for costing
the implementation plan. Mr. Keys said previous projects will be used, along
with benchmarks gained from similar projects the Village has completed. Manager
Bahan explained that cost uncertainty was far greater for the tabled Willow
Road Tunnel project, as it was so unique. Costing of water mains is readily
available, as it is a typical core capital project.
   Responding to a question about the Water Plant, Mr. Keys explained an
assessment of the Plant has been completed, and the Plant will be included in
the rate study. He noted that technical aspects of the Water Plant assessment
will not be re-done by Burns & McDonnell. He added that other rate classes may
be proposed, as the Village currently only has classes for "residential" and
"commercial."
   There being no audience comment or further discussion, Trustee Fessler,
seconded by Trustee McCrary, moved to adopt Resolution R-43-2015. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler, Krucks, McCrary, Myers
and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
