March 8, 2016
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
March 8, 2016
(Approved: April 5, 2016)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
8, 2016 at 7:00p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Greable called the meeting to order at 7:01p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Carol Fessler, William Krucks, Stuart McCrary, Scott Myers and
Marilyn Prodromos. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Assistant to the Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter M.
Friedman, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Director of Community
Development Mike D’Onofrio, and 3 persons in the audience.
2)   Stormwater Regulation Review – Impermeable Surface Limits and Detached
Garage Regulation.
Mr. Saunders explained the Village’s Stormwater Master Plan recommends
reviewing the Zoning Ordinance to determine whether any provisions potentially
impacting stormwater runoff should be revised. He said the Village aims to
strike the right balance between excessive stormwater runoff, property values,
design appearance and tree protection.
Mr. Saunders said he examined zoning regulations that encourage detached
garages in the rear quarter of the lot. The study of new construction revealed
that the smaller the lot size, the higher the percentage of homes with detached
garages, in order to reap the Floor Area Ratio (FAR) bonus. He illustrated that
a typical block in the R-5 Zoning District has mostly rear, detached garages,
with a long driveway to access the garage. He noted the impermeable surface
very quickly adds up when viewed on a large scale; other impacts of the
detached garages include tree removal and structures built very near property
lines. Additionally, homes with no alley access have an almost 10% higher
concentration of detached garages.
Mr. Saunders explained that smaller lots are not wide enough to construct an
attached front- facing garage; and the Zoning Ordinance limits the width of
front-facing garage doors. The homes built on these smaller lots are almost all
at or near the 50% maximum lot coverage allowed by the Code.
The Council briefly discussed the aesthetics of attached garages, increased
storm runoff caused by the longer driveways, and storage of rainwater as
required by the Village’s Design Guidelines. Responding to a question about
permeable pavers, Mr. Saunders explained that the published runoff figure for
design permeable pavers is 80% permeable.
After reviewing several designs of front-facing attached garages, Mr. Saunders
explained it would not be prudent to focus solely on impermeable surface
coverage without also examining tree health and design issues. He suggested
three methods of dealing with impermeable surface coverage:
   Reduce impermeable surface coverage limits on a sliding scale only for lots
larger than 5,000 square feet.
   Modify garage regulations to reduce requirements/incentives for detached
garages.
   Explore ways to mitigate potential negative aesthetic impact of attached
garages on narrow lots.
During the ensuing discussion, several suggestions were raised:
   Keep the incentive/allowance for detached garages in the rear quarter, but
add a provision requiring design permeable surfaces on the driveway
   Be mindful to treat blocks with no alley access fairly
   Gather feedback from homeowners before making any changes
   Get input from real estate professionals to gauge the impact on property
values
   Find out if the proposed amendments would negatively affect older homes
Mr. Saunders explained the estimated reduction of one acre-foot of water going
into the storm sewer system was calculated based on his review of building
permits, which showed approximately one-third of the homes would have been
required to build smaller under the proposed new regulations. He noted that
increasing detention capacity would slow the stormwater release rate, but it
does not reduce the volume of runoff, whereas impervious surface affects the
total volume the stormwater system has to take in.
Mr. D’Onofrio commented that it is not typical to see a zoning variation
request for lot coverage; most requests are for gross floor area or setback
variations, usually related to an existing nonconforming home.
It was suggested that permeable pavement be installed in the alleys; and a
proposal was put forth to differentiate new construction from building
additions/remodels so as not to encourage teardowns. Mr. Saunders explained
that zoning modifications are complicated precisely because there are so many
factors at play.
Once Council discussion was finished, President Greable called for public
comment.
Ron White, Berkeley Avenue. Mr. White asked what the impact proposed new
regulations would have if he were to tear down his current garage and rebuild
it, given that it is an exceptionally deep two-car garage. Mr. Saunders
consulted the GIS system and said there would probably be very little impact,
as there is only 19% lot coverage on Mr. White’s parcel.
Jeffrey Liss, 1364 Edgewood. Mr. Liss suggested taking into account what size
garage people are likely to build; and he suggested raising building height
allowances as an approach to reducing impermeability.
The Council was in agreement that, while it is valuable to consider zoning
amendments, discussions should be deferred for the time being, given the number
of major projects coming before the Council in the near future.
1)   Public Comment.
None.
2)   Closed Session.
Trustee Fessler moved to adjourn into Closed Session to discuss purchase or
lease of real property, and for setting a price for sale or lease of property
owned by the Village of Winnetka, pursuant to Sections 2c(5) and 2c(6) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Krucks seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Fessler, Krucks, McCrary, Myers
and Prodromos. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Greable announced that the Council would not return to the open
meeting after Closed Session. The Council adjourned into Closed Session at 8:06
p.m.
1)   Adjournment.
Trustee Prodromos, seconded by Trustee Fessler, moved to adjourn the Closed
Session. By roll call vote, the motion carried unanimously. The Closed Session
ended at 9:35 p.m.
