May 10, 2016
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
May 10, 2016
(Approved: June 7, 2016)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
10, 2016 at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Krucks called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present:
Trustees Andrew Cripe, William Krucks, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, Christopher
Rintz and Kristin Ziv. Absent: President Gene Greable. Also in attendance:
Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant to the Village Manager Megan Pierce,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant Public Works Director Jim
Bernahl and approximately 40 persons in the audience.
President Pro Tem Krucks explained tonight’s meeting is a Study Session which
is an informal meeting of the Village Council. No action can or will be taken
tonight.
2)   Stormwater Alternatives Evaluation – Council Update.
Director Saunders stated the purpose of tonight’s meeting is not to discuss the
details but rather the outcomes of the public engagement process. Mr. Saunders
covered the scope of the study as well as the three parts of the public
participation phase—awareness, exploration and vision. He continued that having
completed three significant phases of public participation, Strand outlined a
Concept Stormwater Vision that meets the Village’s stated flood-control
objectives. Strand’s cost estimate for the four-phase Vision is $57.7 million.
Mr. Saunders stated that with the motto of leaving “no stone unturned,” there
are parts of the Vision that need refinement, further discussion with
stakeholders and landholders, and more definition before decisions can be made.
He reiterated the Village’s goal is to build as much consensus as possible
around the Vision. He then addressed the current status of stormwater
alternatives and summarized some of the key issues.
Mr. Saunders noted further engagement with the Winnetka Park District and
community is necessary to assess the use of Crow Island Woods as a recreated
wetland for the Skokie River.
Winnetka Park District Executive Director Bob Smith extended his support for
this issue and reiterated the need for the process to be publically vetted. Mr.
Smith commented that there are more meetings in which the public can continue
to be involved.
Mr. Saunders stated discussions with the Cook County Forest Preserve District
will continue. These discussions will address their land use policy and the
Village maintaining native tree species among other items. Currently, the
Forest Preserve District’s land use policy prohibits its land to be used for
stormwater management with the exception of the Skokie Lagoons which were built
for stormwater management in the 1930s.
New Trier High School is willing to be a partner and consider the use of Duke
Childs Field if they can effectively swap land and not loose athletic field
space. District 36 and the Park District have been very cooperative recognizing
this is not only a Village issue, but a community-wide one. Discussion points
have included the possibility of relocating stormwater under the existing field
site as well as relocating the fields to the current landfill site. Filtration
for the water needs to be addressed and the Winnetka Park District Golf Course
was suggested as a potential site with natural filtration. Mr. Saunders
reported the field area between Skokie and Washburn Schools is being explored,
as is a large scale conveyance storm sewer in the area of Sunset Road.
President Pro Tem Krucks commended Director Saunders on his report and thanked
both the Park District and School Board for their cooperation.
The Council then directed questions to Mr. Saunders concerning the proposed
alternatives.
Mark Shubak from Strand Associates noted he is pleased with the feedback he has
received from the Cook County Forest Preserve District. He continued that a
plan involving Crow Island Woods would need to include treating the water that
is redirected there to improve the habitat and water quality on the property.
Trustee Rintz asked if the ‘100 year storm’ engineering standard could be
further probed and perhaps a different standard with associated risks and costs
entertained.
Mr. Shubak replied that Strand can explore the tradeoffs from that standard to
assist the Council in making a decision.
President Pro Tem Krucks called on members of the public in attendance to come
forward and address the Council.
Jeff Franzen, 290 Euclid Avenue. Mr. Franzen stated his opposition to using the
Crow Island Woods as an above ground wetland and requested a matrix of
solutions be created.
Richard Kates, Winnetka. Mr. Kates noted the importance of community members
verbalizing their concerns and then allowing experts to craft a solution.
Peter Mone, 1035 Sunset Road. Mr. Mone expressed his desire for Crow Island
Woods to remain a forest.
Bill Fiedler, 111 Sunset Road. Mr. Fiedler expressed his passion for Crow
Island Woods and noted his belief that a matrix of alternatives will be helpful
for public information.
Christy Morris, Winnetka. Ms. Morris noted her children attend Crow Island
School and utilize the Woods as an outdoor learning area.
Debbie Ross, 929 Tower Road. Ms. Ross remarked that Crow Island Woods is not
being taken away, it is being improved. She also stated it is defeatist to
build homes in flood plains and expect them not to flood.
A resident on Euclid Avenue expressed his displeasure with Village leadership.
He continued that he found the proposed solutions to be flawed and believes
residents were not appropriately informed that Crow Island Woods is being
considered as a possible stormwater site.
Trustee Myers responded to this comment detailing the many mediums used to
notify and engage residents about the stormwater management study process, and
he reminded residents of their responsibility to stay informed.
A resident on Apple Tree Road thanked Village staff and Council members for
their work on this important issue. He discouraged engaging the Lakota group at
a cost of $72,000 to study Crow Island Woods when there is a number of the
population that is opposed to the use of this land.
Harold Nations, 181 Apple Tree Road. Mr. Nations asked if there are other
beneficiaries of Winnetka’s stormwater management that may wish to be a party
to the planning process. He also requested Cook County President Toni
Preckwinkle be engaged in the discussion with the Forest Preserve District.
Steve Lesnik, 1122 Sunset Road. Mr. Lesnik remarked on the cost of an event
that may occur once in a hundred years versus the number of people who use Crow
Island Woods daily. He noted many golf courses accept stormwater for their
municipalities and serve as a natural filtration system. He continued that
Winnetka may be obliged to maintain the Woods in perpetuity if bargaining with
the Cook County Forest Preserve District and may need to consider how much the
Village is willing to bargain.
Kim Hack, 1099 Sunset Road. Ms. Hack noted that due to its narrowness, digging
box culverts for a conveyance system would be very difficult on Sunset Road.
President Pro Tem Krucks asked Director Saunders to clarify why the 100 year
storm has been established as the engineering standard for this project.
Director Saunders explained he can’t speak to the impetus of 100 years as the
engineering standard but continued there is a 1% chance of this type of storm
occurring. He equated the probability of these storm occurrences to that of
rolling Yahtzee in the dice game. Yahtzee has a probability score of 1/512. He
continued that Winnetka has had three storms of 100 year scale since 2008.
Mark Shubak of Strand Associates added that many more homes flood from storms
of smaller scale.
Chris Crone, 1205 Sunset Road. Mr. Crone noted creative landscaping and tree
planting has helped mitigate stormwaters as have requiring newly constructed
homes to have a tank built underneath in other cities.
Gwen Trindel, Winnetka. Ms. Trindel discouraged the use of the landfill as a
replacement area for Duke Childs Field.
Bill Savino, 1098 Mount Pleasant. Mr. Savino noted the Forest Preserve is
formed to preserve the forest.
Trustee Cripe clarified that Council members are learning about this process at
the same time as members of the public are. He reassured that Crow Island Woods
will be thoughtfully addressed.
Trustee Myers reaffirmed Trustee Cripe’s comments and reinforced the
transparency of the process.
President Pro Tem Krucks thanked members of the public for speaking and asked
Council members for their thoughts on the proposed next steps.
The Council agreed that Strand should continue to engage with the Cook County
Forest Preserve District, and that they should prepare a scope for further
analysis with New Trier High School concerning Duke Childs Field stopping short
of preparing a proposal. With respect to Crow Island Woods, the Council decided
against asking Strand to prepare a scope and proposal for further analysis or
consideration at this time, and also not to prepare a scope and proposal to
perform additional detail engineering concerning stormwater conveyance along
Sunset Road. The Council supported Strand’s further engagement with School
District 36 as well as tasked them with the development of a matrix of
conceptual options including estimated costs and flood reduction performance.
President Pro Tem Krucks explained the June 7th Council Meeting will either
address One Winnetka or stormwater but not both due to the significance of both
items.
Village Manager Robert Bahan directed Director Saunders and Strand to proceed
with preparations to present at the June 7th meeting.
3)   Public Comment:  
None.
4)   Closed Session.
None
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 10:30 p.m.
 
