Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Krucks, Lanphier, Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
6)   Stormwater Report.
None.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution No. R-70-2016: Approving a Work Program for Downtown Master
Plan Implementation (Adoption). This item was deferred to a later meeting.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-19-2016: 521 Birch Street, Winnetka Park District,
Special Use Permit and Variation (Introduction/Adoption). Mr. D’Onofrio
reviewed this request to allow expansion and renovation of the playground and
modifications to landscaping and pathways at Dwyer Park. Since the park is
located in a single-family residential district, a special use permit is
required, and a zoning variation is being requested for a front yard setback.
The original application was revised in response to concerns expressed by the
community, and the revised request was recommended for approval by the Plan
Commission, Zoning Board of Appeals and Design Review Board.
   After Mr. D’Onofrio described the proposed improvements to Dwyer Park,
Robert Smith, Director of Winnetka Park District, described the planning and
engagement process that led to the development of the park renovation plan. He
explained that the Park District and Park Board try their best to achieve
unanimous approval from the community; however, some residents have expressed
reservations about aspects of the plan, including the gazebo and changes in the
landscaping.
   Mr. Smith explained that the Park District conducted a community survey and
held numerous public meetings about the proposal. Costa Kutulas, Parks
Superintendent, reviewed the site plans in greater detail, and both Mr. Smith
and Mr. Kutulas answered questions from the Council.
   Next, President Greable called for public comment.
   Stacy Burgoon, 856 Oak Street. Ms. Burgoon opposed the busier programming
and added impervious surface aspects of the plan and posited that the park is
mainly used by younger children and therefore “tween” programming will go
unused.
   Cyrus Subawalla, 897 Spruce Street. Mr. Subawalla suggested moving the
gazebo to separate it from the playground and asked for a softer ground cover
than concrete.
   Trey Thomas, 511 Ash Street. Mr. Thomas expressed concern about removing
green space for ping pong and baggo, as these sites might not get used.
   Dave Bornhoeft, 850 Cherry Street. Mr. Bornhoeft expressed approval of the
plan, as it will attract a broader segment of the community and will serve
older kids as well as younger ones.
   Richard Laible, 978 Elm. Mr. Laible expressed strong support for the plan
and said attracting older kids and more users to the park is a good thing.
   Tom Font, 972 Elm Street. Mr. Font said he supported the proposed new layout
and urged the Council to approve the request.
   Doug Crimmins, 1208 Ash Street. Mr. Crimmins said he strongly supports the
plan, as the park will have something for everyone and will bring the community
together.
   Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo asked what, if any, coordination
will occur with the Park District for Downtown Master Plan implementation, and
she asked where the drinking fountains will be.
   Trustee Myers explained that the Post Office site was discussed during the
development of the Downtown Master Plan, and any redevelopment there will
integrate into Dwyer Park.
   John Thomas, 525 Ash Street and Park District Trustee. Mr. Thomas said the
Park Board is satisfied that the Dwyer Park proposal is the best plan for the
community, the planning process was transparent and thorough, and he urged the
Council to approve the request.
   The Council discussed the application, and all were in favor or approving
the request as well as waiving introduction, with the exception of Trustee
Lanphier, who was in favor of introducing the Ordinance and adopting it at the
January 3, 2017 Regular Council meeting. There was a suggestion made to monitor
the usage of the ping pong table and baggo courts, and to remove them in the
event they are not being used.
   Brad McLane, Sheridan Road and Park District Trustee. Mr. McLane said the
Park District had a long and thorough public process, which has already pushed
back the start date of the park renovation. He noted that if the bid process is
not started immediately, construction will be delayed still further, and he
urged the Council to waive introduction and adopt the Ordinance immediately.
   Trustee Krucks pointed out that there was no evidence in the record that
anyone in the community had been denied an opportunity to express their opinion
at the eight public meetings that were held to discuss the proposal. He added
that the Park Board has been responsive to public comment every step of the way
and that waiting until January will not change his mind about voting
affirmatively on the special use and variation application.
   Trustee Lanphier said she raised the question of waiving introduction out of
concern that there is a public expectation that there will be two discussions
about the Ordinance, and she agreed that there have been ample opportunities
for public comment.
   Trustee Krucks, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-19-2016. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Krucks, Lanphier, Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Krucks, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-19-
2016. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Krucks,
Lanphier, Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-20-2016: Authorizing the Disposition of Surplus
Personal Property Owned by the Village of Winnetka (Introduction). Mr. Keys
explained that the purpose of the Subject Ordinance is to dispose of routine
items during the course of the year, and that this request also contains the
replacement of two vehicles which are past their useful life.
   There being no questions or comments, Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee
Ziv, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-20-2016. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
