   Deputy Clerk
January 10, 2017
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 7, 2017)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 10, 2017, at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Greable called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, Christopher Rintz and Kristin Ziv.
Absent: Trustee Bill Krucks. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Community Development Director Mike
D’Onofrio, Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus, and four
persons in the audience.
2)   Downtown Master Plan Implementation Work Program.
Mike D’Onofrio reviewed the process that led to the adoption of Winnetka’s
Downtown Master Plan (DMP), adding that priority implementation items were
included in the 2017 Budget. He explained that a proposed plan calls for
establishment of a Task Force to oversee the implementation phase, with
assistance from Teska Associates. The process would also include further
community engagement, examination of policy issues and issuance of
recommendations to the Council.
Mr. D’Onofrio said it is hoped the Task Force will continue building on the
momentum of the DMP development process, with support from staff and Teska. The
Teska proposal includes delivering assistance on zoning implementation, parking
allocation and regulatory signage, as well as designing a wayfinding program
and street parklet prototype for the Council’s consideration.
Trustee Lanphier thought the plan was very ambitious, and felt the land use and
zoning examination should be a separate process, since it is such a significant
undertaking. She also suggested the Village design a training program for
members of boards and commissions so they can properly perform the tasks the
Council gives them. She noted the Council needs to take a broader look at the
commercial districts: for instance, determine the barriers to businesses
opening in Winnetka, and what the Village can do to support business
development. She also recommended reaching out to the community to find out
what it would like to see, and asked how streetscape fits into the overall
plan.
Trustee Rintz commented that before a facilitator is engaged to help execute
specific pieces of the DMP, the Council needs to develop an implementation
blueprint. He recalled how the Streetscape process was derailed by an advisory
referendum in 2009, explaining that the Streetscape Master Plan is designed in
detail and shovel-ready. He suggested the Task Force begin with an examination
of the Streetscape Plan for recommendations on signage, parking bump-outs and
other street elements like lighting. He said he could not see starting to put
in improvements in the absence of a complete plan, and questioned hiring a
consultant to develop recommendations on signage that are already contained in
the Streetscape Plan.
Trustee Rintz also expressed concern about paying for DMP implementation in the
long term, and he suggested the Task Force examine financing opportunities such
as a tax increment financing (TIF) district, instead of going immediately to
taxpayers. He noted that he is in favor of a Task Force and using Teska’s
expertise; however, an overall plan should be developed before beginning
implementation.
Trustee Myers agreed with the idea of using the Streetscape Plan as a starting
point for setting priorities. He thought the Council should prioritize several
goals in the areas of land use and zoning, and then assign Teska the task of
developing options to consider. He said he saw the Task Force as the Council’s
arms and legs to facilitate a timely implementation of the DMP, since it would
have the time to collect data, engage the community and study the issues.
Trustee Lanphier said she thought the Task Force is being charged with too much
work and suggested breaking it down between two groups. Manager Bahan advised
that Village staff cannot provide support for two different committees. He
noted that the DMP acknowledges the Streetscape Plan is sound, and contains
recommendations that signage be developed based on that plan.
Trustee Ziv commented that the talent and capabilities of the DMP Steering
Committee members were impressive and she did not see a need to add more
members to the proposed Task Force.
Trustee Cripe was in favor of validating the Streetscape Plan and then
prioritizing the areas for improvement. He suggested the Task Force gather
facts about the Retail Overlay District and Special Use Permit process so the
Council has sufficient information to weigh the options on land use.
The Council continued discussing the options for DMP implementation, finally
reaching general consensus to accept the recommendation to form a Task Force,
with one or two at-large members. The first chore of the Task Force would be to
review the Streetscape Plan. While that work is going on, the Council will
review zoning and land use, the Overlay District, and the Planned Development
process. After the first two pieces are accomplished, an examination of
financing options will take place.
President Greable called for audience comment.
Jeffrey Liss, 1464 Edgewood. Mr. Liss suggested: (i) looking at the recommended
bike racks, as designs have changed since 2008, (ii) enlisting the Plan
Commission to help with the land use research; and (iii) rethinking the
realignment of Merrill Street, as there could be a conflict with PACE buses.
Terry Dason, Director of the Winnetka-Northfield Chamber. Ms. Dason expressed
support for using the Streetscape Plan and said she looks to forward to helping
with DMP implementation in any way possible.
Debbie Guillod, Winnetka. Ms. Guillod suggested burying the power lines in the
business districts to update the look of the Village.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo urged the Council to include residents
in the implementation process wherever possible, expressed support for a
thorough financing discussion, and said the League of Women Voters is ready to
help in the process.
3)   Public Comment.
None.
4)   Closed Session.
None.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:53 p.m.
 
