Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to approve the Agenda. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
5)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   December 20, 2016 Regular Meeting.
      ii)   January 3, 2017 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated December 30, 2016 –
January 12, 2017 in the amount of $1,265,703.80.
   c)   Resolution No. R-4-2017: Letter of Understanding for Auxiliary
Telecommunicator Staffing (Adoption). An authorization for the Village Manager
to execute a Letter of Understanding with the Village of Glenview for the
staffing of dispatch services to the Village of Winnetka.
   d)   Resolution No. R-5-2017: Information Technology Consultant Renewal
(Adoption). An approval to extend the original December, 2011 contract with
Prescient Solutions in the amount of $53,500 for Year 1; $54,570 for Year 2;
and $55,600 for Year 3 of the extension.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Krucks, Lanphier, Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
6)   Stormwater Report.
None.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2017: An Ordinance Prohibiting Phosphorus-Containing
Fertilizers (Introduction). Mr. Saunders explained that water quality is an
important piece of the Village’s strategy to mitigate stormwater flooding, and
stormwater sampling in 2014 revealed the presence of phosphorus, a common lawn
fertilizer. He noted that excess phosphorus contributes to algae blooms on
local waterways, and both Lake Michigan and the Skokie River are classified by
the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency as impaired due to phosphorus. He
said last summer the Council tasked the Environmental & Forestry Commission
(EFC) with researching local prohibitions on phosphorus and providing
recommendations for Winnetka, adding that the State of Illinois has a ban in
place for commercial applications.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the EFC’s evaluation process, and said their
recommendations for a phosphorus ban are modeled on other local ordinances
which prohibit individual property owners from applying phosphorus fertilizer.
He noted that Winnetka’s proposed Ordinance contains exceptions for new lawns,
vegetable gardens and areas where soil testing shows an insufficiency of
natural phosphorus. He explained that a robust public education effort would be
undertaken using the Village’s communication channels, along with requiring
informational signs at the point of purchase. It is hoped that retailers
outside of Winnetka will also agree to place information about Winnetka’s ban
in their stores.
Trustee Lanphier commented that she took part in the research and
recommendation process as Council liaison to the EFC. She noted that there are
other harmful compounds in stormwater, and the EFC plans to take a more
comprehensive look at them now that the phosphorus issue has been dealt with.
Mr. Saunders answered questions from the Council, assuring them that the ban
will apply to the Park District and golf course, and that there will be follow-
up monitoring of Winnetka’s stormwater to determine the effects of the ban. He
described a two-fold enforcement process: making sure retailers post the
required signs, and ticketing violators with a standard Municipal Code
violation.
President Greable called for public comment.
Chris Crone, Winnetka resident. Mr. Crone said he fully supports the phosphorus
ban.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. MC-1-2017. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-1-2017 and Resolution No. R-1-2017: Granting
Preliminary Approval of the One Winnetka Planned Development and the
Development Agreement, including 511-515 Lincoln Avenue, 714-740 Elm Street,
and Portion of Lincoln Avenue Right-of-Way (Adoption). Mr. D’Onofrio noted that
this is the sixth time the Council has discussed the One Winnetka planned
development preliminary application, and that no changes have occurred since
the Ordinance was introduced in December.
Trustee Lanphier requested that the concerns of the Hadley School and 711 Oak
Street neighbors continue to be addressed, and she suggested the new
development include a memorial to the Fell Building.
President Greable called for public comment.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak Street. Ms. Holland thanked the Council and Developer
for the safety measures in the development agreement to protect visually
impaired employees at the Hadley School. She noted that the stretch of sidewalk
in front of the nearly vacant building on the south side of Elm Street is a
serious ice hazard, and she requested that the Village salt the sidewalk when
ice and snow conditions occur.
Several Trustees briefly reiterated their reasons for approving the preliminary
application, and there was a call to ensure the construction phase provides a
minimum of disturbance to the area.
Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-1-2017.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Krucks, Lanphier,
Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-1-
2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Krucks,
Lanphier, Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Greable asked that the construction commence as soon as possible.
David Trandel, the Developer, thanked the Village for a thorough process which
resulted in a terrific end product and noted that he takes the issue of
stewardship very seriously.
8)   Public Comment.
None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Reports.
   a)   Village President. None
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Ziv reported on the Community Conversation event the
previous Saturday with Trustee Bill Krucks.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Manager Bahan said the Village continues to monitor events in
Springfield through the NWMC and IML.
13)   Closed Session.
None.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adjourn the meeting. By voice
vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 7:42 p.m.
