   e)   Resolution No. R-19-2017: Approving a Class A-2 Liquor License for
Grateful Bites Pizza Shopper, LLC (Adoption). Approval of a Resolution to
Authorize a Liquor License for Grateful Bites Pizza Shoppe at 899 Green Bay
Road.
   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Krucks, Myers, Rintz, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Lanphier.
6)   Stormwater Report.
None.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2017: An Ordinance Prohibiting Phosphorus-Containing
Fertilizers (Amendment & Adoption). Public Works Director/Village Engineer
Steve Saunders reviewed the January introduction of the agenda item and the
subsequent conversations with the Winnetka Park District. Staff members from
the Village and Park District evaluated the golf course maintenance practices,
and how those might be impacted by the Village ordinance. Mr. Saunders noted
that golf courses are currently exempt from the State ban on phosphorus in
fertilizers. Since the golf course is the single largest piece of land where
fertilizer would be applied within Village limits, Staff recommended a separate
prohibition specific to the course maintenance. Mr. Saunders highlighted the
amended portions of the Ordinance for the Council, and reiterated that the ban
would assist the Village with its water quality goals.
   Trustee Krucks asked why the term “waterway” was not defined in the Subject
Ordinance and suggested that it be clarified by the Village Attorney.
   Trustee Myers inquired about the process for the annual water sampling the
Park District would be required to perform, as outlined in the amended portion
of the Ordinance. He and Mr. Saunders discussed how a strategy would be
developed and how the entities would cooperate in stewardship of these assets.
Trustee Myers wished to emphasize efforts that are performed at the lowest
possible impact to the environment.
   [Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:22 p.m.]
   Trustee Rintz suggested that the language in Section B.5 limit phosphorous
application to areas in need to prevent excessive use. Trustee Cripe noted that
technical, professional consultation might be of benefit to creating the best
process for Park District soil sampling.
   Trustee Ziv expressed concern about exempting the Park District from the ban
and not applying it to all in the community equally.
   Trustee Cripe said it would be inappropriate to exempt the Park District
simply because they are a sister agency to the Village, and asserted that in
some cases the application could be warranted and beneficial.
   Trustee Lanphier promoted the benefit of educating all agencies and
applicators and said through restricted and appropriate use, the phosphorous
applied can be naturally absorbed.
   The Trustees discussed the difference between the State ban and local
regulations.
   Mr. Saunders reviewed two suggested additional amendments to the Ordinance,
including the definition of waterway and a change to Section B.5.A.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to incorporate two
aforementioned changes into Ordinance No. MC-1-2017. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to defer the adoption of
the Ordinance until the changes could be discussed with the Park District. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2017: Amending Liquor Code (Introduction). Deputy
Police Chief Marc Hornstein presented two changes to the Village’s Liquor Code
being proposed to support local businesses and organizations. He explained that
the current Code does not allow the Police Department to permit an existing
license holder to host off-site events which involve sale of liquor, such as a
wine walk. The permit would be issued on a temporary basis—creating a “special
use license.”
   Further, the Class C license for non-profit organizations is currently
limited to seven licenses per year. Mr. Hornstein said the Police Department
has found this limit to be prohibitive to some community organizations.
   Trustee Lanphier inquired about the current limit on the Class C and when it
was put in place. Mr. Hornstein explained the Police Department’s due-diligence
process and the applicable insurance requirements.
   Trustee Rintz asked about the two-day restriction referenced in the
Executive Summary, and said he did not see the limitation in the Code Amendment
itself. The Council discussed whether a two day limit on special events was
appropriate. They suggested the event timing should be approved as part of the
application with the Police Department.
   Terry Dason, Executive Director of the Winnetka-Northfield Chamber of
Commerce. Ms. Dason concurred with the broadened timing for special events
seeking a liquor license and said the change would be appreciated by
businesses.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-3-2017, including an amendment for special event permit duration. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
   c)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2017: Amending the Village Code to Adopt Small Cell
Regulations (Introduction). Water & Electric Director Brian Keys explained the
regulation proposed for small cell locations as well as the review process that
is contained in proposed new Chapter 14.20. He outlined the locations at which
small cells would be prohibited and standards that would govern aesthetics of
the cells and regulatory compliance. Small cells requests would be considered
via an application process, and would be confirmed through a lease agreement.
   Based on a question from Trustee Cripe, Mr. Keys reviewed the prior Council
direction to administer small cell regulations at the administrative level. Mr.
Keys noted that the Village does not have the ability to outright prohibit
small cells. Attorney Friedman noted these regulations were fashioned based on
comparable communities; however, the process was tailored to Winnetka’s
specific desires.
   Attorney Friedman and Mr. Keys explained small cell technology and the
capacity the telecommunicators hope to create.
   Trustee Rintz clarified that the minimum $4,000 rent would be per small cell
site, per year, and Mr. Keys explained the logic of that fee based on Village
rent received at the macro cell locations. Trustee Ziv noted that the Village
should not make it prohibitive, since the facilities would be beneficial to
residents.
   Trustee Krucks, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-4-2017. By voice vote, the motion carried.
8)   Public Comment.
None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
   a)   Downtown Master Plan Implementation. Manager Bahan introduced the
opportunity for a public/private partnership to implement targeted streetscape
improvements related to the redevelopment of Fred’s Garage (gateway corner of
Spruce and Chestnut Streets). He explained the optional levels of improvement,
as well as the developer contribution, and how the Village arrived at an
estimated project cost of $482,887. Mr. Bahan also outlined the recommended
items the Village could defer from the 2017 Business District Revitalization
Fund in order to create capacity for this project.
   The Council asked a number of questions regarding the level of developer
contribution and the recommendation to defer projects that were specific
outcomes of the recent Downtown Master Plan (DMP) process. There was also a
lengthy discussion around what specific items would be part of the improvement
and whether the corner and portion of Chestnut was the most appropriate
location.
   Based on a suggestion from Trustee Cripe, the Council debated seeking
funding from private landowners who stand to benefit from streetscape
improvements around their business.
   Staff continued with an explanation of financing methods available to public
entities, so the Council could consider future streetscape phasing and budget
appropriations. After a brief background on Tax Increment Financing (TIF), the
Council directed the Village Manager to seek out qualified consultants who
could perform an eligibility analysis related to the Elm Business District and
the One Winnetka redevelopment.
   Finally, Assistant Village Manager Pierce presented the revised
implementation schedule for other DMP projects, including installation of a
parklet, review of parking signage and allocations, board and commissioner
training, and zoning evaluation. The Council again expressed concern and varied
opinions about which projects should be prioritized. They discussed the value
of the zoning recommendations from the DMP and the need to hire a planning
expert and process facilitator such as Teska Associates. Staff indicated that
the zoning was targeted for later in the year, so the Council could further
consider the desired scope of work, as well as the role of the Implementation
Task Force.
   [Trustee Lanphier left the meeting at 9:50 p.m.]
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Reports.
   a)   Village President. None.
   b)   Trustees. None.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None.
13)   Closed Session.
None.
14)   Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Rintz, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:58 p.m.
