   Deputy Clerk
March 14, 2017
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: April 6, 2017)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
14, at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Krucks called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present:
Trustees Andrew Cripe, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, Christopher Rintz and
Kristin Ziv. Absent: President Gene Greable. Also in attendance: Village
Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Water & Electric
Director Brian Keys, Finance Director Timothy Sloth, Assistant Finance Director
Nick Mostardo, and no persons in the audience.
2)   Water Rate Study.
Mr. Keys introduced David Naumann and Claus Dunkelberg of Burns & McDonnell
Engineering Company, who the Village engaged to develop a prioritization and
financial plan for replacement of Winnetka’s water distribution system. Mr.
Naumann led the presentation with a review of the two prior Council
discussions. The study targets a 100-year water main replacement cycle, which
is a common target for communities.
By way of the prior financial scenario summaries, Mr. Naumann presented an
updated Scenario 1, which factors in cost-savings by coordinating water main
replacement with the street rehabilitation program. He also reviewed the impact
of the 4% rate increase which the Council adopted for FY 2017; previous
scenarios included an 8.5% increase assumption. The average age of water mains
in Winnetka is high compared to national averages; much of it is beyond its
useful life and the system has a high failure rate.
Before reviewing water rate structures used in the region, Mr. Naumann stated
that Winnetka’s existing rate structure is entirely volumetric. While the
structure is easy to explain, it does not provide fixed cost recovery and is
more susceptible to weather variances. Mr. Naumann showed the rate design
options in both the existing rate structure as well as a rate design which
includes a service charge—to shift the Village’s reliance on volumetric rates
over time.
He then answered questions about how service charges were determined—clarifying
that the volumetric rate covers the cost of distribution and the service charge
is more focused on administrative costs, billing, etc. The Council and Mr.
Naumann discussed the potential fluctuation in revenues under the service
charge rate design.
Lastly, Mr. Naumann presented an alternate scenario, which extends the
financial plan an additional five years. The alternate maintains the 100-year
replacement cycle, but extends the timing to reach that target—essentially
taking the Village 15 years of rate increases rather than 10, but lessening the
rate increases on an annual basis. Mr. Keys explained that both scenarios
include the fund receiving a $1 million load in 2017; the Scenario 1 includes
two additional $1 million loans. Mr. Bahan noted that the primary the vehicle
the Village has considered are low-interest IEPA loans. Mr. Keys and the
Council discussed how he would collaborate large replacement projects with
future roadwork and stormwater improvements.
Trustee Cripe asked specifically how the replacement cycle would sync with
improvements such as the Downtown Master Plan and stormwater engineering. Mr.
Keys noted the fund is still restrained by the amount of reserves available
when other projects come to fruition. Trustee Rintz suggested small debt
issuances might serve the Village well when opportunities arose to accomplish
additional projects together. However, there is a limit on how much of the
distribution system could be worked on in a single year.
The Council discussed the differences between the 6.5% and 8.5% increases in
the scenarios and how the compounding of rates might impact or be perceived by
residents. Mr. Naumann said the 8.5% increase provides a bit more flexibility
in terms of advancing maintenance and dealing with breaks that may occur
without impacting the overall replacement program. They also considered the
price sensitivity to the increase in rates vs. in the service charge and
whether spreading the impact out over time with debt would be fairer.
The Trustees expressed concern about a structure that locked-in multiple debt
issuances, when other major improvements might require debt in the future. They
felt an initial $1 million loan made sense and that future issuances could be
evaluated later.
President Pro Tem indicated he would support raising the user charge by $1
higher than estimated in the scenario. Mr. Naumann said a potential negative of
this choice would be the impact on the smaller users.
Mr. Bahan confirmed there was consensus to allocate a $1 million loan to the
water fund in 2017, as well as to proceed with the 8.5% increase. He stated the
future debt would be re-evaluated during the Budget process. Mr. Keys asked for
clarity about the potential change to the fixed service charge. Mr. Naumann
said he would look at some variations on the charge. Trustee Myers also
suggested grouping smaller customer categories for the charge, such as 1”
meters and below, which Mr. Naumann concurred was reasonable.
Lastly, Mr. Keys presented information on metrics, which was requested by the
Council during the last session. Trustee Myers asked to add the cost of
replacement per lineal foot on an annual basis. The metrics will appear in
future Budget books for the water fund.
3)   Public Comment.
None.
4)   Closed Session.
None.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:26 p.m.
