None.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-1-2017: An Ordinance Prohibiting Phosphorus-Containing
Fertilizers (Adoption). Mr. Saunders reviewed the amendments that the Council
had adopted at the March 7 Regular Council meeting. He said he has discussed
the final Ordinance with the Park District and the amendments met with their
approval.
   The Council briefly discussed the Ordinance; all were in favor of the final
form.
   Trustee Cripe moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-1-2017. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Lanphier, Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Krucks.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2017: An Ordinance Amending the Winnetka Village
Code to Adopt Small Cell Regulations (Amendment & Adoption). Mr. Keys reviewed
three amendments to the Ordinance dealing with mounting strength, guy wires and
signs/advertising, made at the request of a wireless carrier and after the
Village Attorney reviewed and approved the changes.
   There being no questions or comments from the Council or the audience,
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to amend Ordinance No. MC-4-
2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Lanphier,
Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Krucks.
   Trustee Rintz, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-4-
2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Lanphier,
Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Krucks.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-2-2017: Approving a Contract With Fox Valley Fire &
Safety and Disposing of Surplus Equipment (Introduction). Chief Berkowsky gave
background on the Village’s fire alarm network system. Calls are transmitted
from individual radio alarms to an antenna on the Public Safety Building, where
they bypass the Village’s emergency dispatch system and go directly to RED
Center for dispatching. He explained that the current system provides that if
the RED Center facility should experience any problems, the calls would go to
the Village emergency dispatch center. With the upcoming emergency dispatch
center consolidation, a suitable backup system must be put in place. The Chief
recommended engaging Emergency 24 monitoring agency as the new backup system in
the event RED Center becomes unable to receive or dispatch the fire alarm
calls. He added that the service will cost of $6.00 per alarm per month plus an
installation fee of $2,000 will be absorbed by the Village for the time being,
and a rate adjustment for 2018 could be discussed at budget time.
   After a brief discussion, Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to
introduce Ordinance No. M-2-2017. By voice vote, the motion carried.
8)   Public Comment.
None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.  
   a)   Resolution No. R-21-2017: Highway Authority Agreements for 574 Green
Bay Road (Adoption). Mr. Saunders explained that the former site of Fred’s
Garage is being redeveloped. When the underground fuel tanks were removed, they
not only leaked oil at the site, but some of the contamination also migrated
under the adjacent public right of way. The developer completed soil
remediation at the project site and is proposing to allow the contaminated soil
to remain in place under the public street, as permitted by the Illinois
Environmental Protection Agency (IEPA) and with the permission of the Village.
The highway authority agreements provide reimbursement to the Village in the
event expenses or liability occur from the continued presence of the
contaminated soil under the public street.
   Mr. Saunders reassured the Council that the area in question contains low
concentrations of contaminants, which naturally degrade over time, and he
recommended entering into the highway authority agreements to avoid the high
level of disruption to the area if excavation and removal of the soil were to
take place. He explained that two agreements are required for the same area, as
two incident numbers were reported to the IEPA.
   The Council discussed the recommendation, and a request was made to record
the agreements against the property, and not just the corporation, for extra
security.
   Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-21-
2017, with the agreements amended to bind the Subject Property. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Lanphier, Myers, Rintz and Ziv.
Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Krucks.
   b)   Resolution No. R-22-2017: Amendment to Village's FOIA Policy
(Adoption). Attorney Friedman explained that the Village’s current FOIA Policy
is inconsistent with State Statute, which has been amended. To bring Winnetka’s
FOIA Policy into compliance with the State Statute, a change is required to
remove the provision that automatically deems as “personal information” the
identity of a person who transmits correspondence (opinion, complaint, etc.) to
the Village. He noted that the Village will still have discretion on a case-by-
case basis to redact a person’s identity if it is deemed necessary.
   In response to a question from the Council, Attorney Friedman confirmed that
the change is being made to be consistent with State Statute.
   There being no further comments or questions, Trustee Myers, seconded by
Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-22-2017. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Lanphier, Myers, Rintz and Ziv. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Krucks.
