MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 6, 2017)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
16, 2017, at 7:00 p.m.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, and Kristin Ziv.
Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Village Attorney
Peter M. Friedman, Community Development Director Mike D’Onofrio, Public Works
Director Steve Saunders, and approximately 6 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Rintz led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   June 6, 2017 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expect to attend.
   b)   June 13, 2017 Study Session. All of the Council members present except
President Rintz indicated that they expect to attend.
   c)   June 20, 2017 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
indicated that they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo reported that she had received a call
purportedly from the WPD soliciting funds, and she asked that the Village’s
communication vehicles be used alert residents about the calls. She also
announced that the Landmark Preservation Commission’s landmark trolley tour is
coming up on Sunday, and she invited the Council and community to attend.
5)   Reports.
   a)   Trustees:
      i)   Trustee Myers reported on the Chamber of Commerce board meeting and
the upcoming Winnetka Music Festival.
   b)   Village President: President Rintz thanked the Council for their hard
work at the May Study Session, and explained that the assignments would be
sequenced and this information will be shared with the Council and community.
He also thanked Community Development for overseeing the repaving of an alley
in the East Elm Business District and ensuring the installation of gates for
the trash containers, which will improve the area for the Music Festival.
Lastly, he commented that a building on Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue being
rehabbed pursuant to comment from the Design Review Board is an asset to the
area.
   c)   Attorney: None.
   d)   Manager: None.
6)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to approve the Agenda. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
7)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   May 2, 2017 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated April 28 – May 11, 2017
in the amount of $1,410,701.28.
   c)   Resolution No. R-37-2017: Approving a Master Intergovernmental
Agreement to Borrow Emergency Vehicles (Adoption). Approval of an
intergovernmental borrowing agreement.
   d)   Resolution No. R-41-2017: SWANCC Board Appointments (Adoption).
Appointing the Village’s representatives to the SWANCC Board of Directors for a
two-year term.
   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-4-2017: 275 Fairview Avenue, Greeley School, Special
Use Permit and Variation (Introduction/Adoption). Mr. D’Onofrio reviewed this
request by Winnetka Public School District 36 for a special use permit and
zoning variation to replace kindergarten playground equipment at Greeley
School. The request received unanimous recommendations for approval from the
Plan Commission, Design Review Board and Zoning Board of Appeals.
   Trustee Lanphier ascertained with Mr. D’Onofrio that there were no
objections to the request from neighbors.
   After the Chief Financial Officer for the school briefly described their
plans, and there being no further questions and comments from the Council,
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-4-2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
4-2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-5-2017: 1110 Chatfield Road, Hubbard Woods School,
Special Use Permit and Variation (Introduction/Adoption). Mr. D’Onofrio
reviewed another request from District 36 for a special use permit and zoning
variation, this time for Hubbard Woods School, also for a playground
renovation. This request also received unanimous recommendations for approval
from the Plan Commission, Design Review Board and Zoning Board of Appeals.
   Trustee Lanphier again confirmed that there were no objections from
neighbors.
   There being no further comments or questions from the Council or audience,
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-5-2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-5-
2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Resolution No. R-39-2017: Engineering Services for Stormwater
Regulations Evaluation (Adoption). Mr. Saunders explained that the 2017 Budget
contains funding for a review of Winnetka’s stormwater management regulations,
to ensure they are compliant with regulations of Cook County and the
Metropolitan Water Reclamation District of Greater Chicago. It will also
examine whether the Village is doing all it can to use and encourage Best
Management Practices.
   After reviewing the responses to Winnetka’s Request for Proposals, Mr.
Saunders recommended Engineering Resource Associates, as the firm demonstrated
the best understanding of the study goals.
   After a brief discussion, Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved
to adopt Resolution No. R-39-2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   d)   Resolution No. R-40-2017: Health Services Professional Agreement
(Adoption). Mr. D’Onofrio explained that Winnetka has contracted for sanitarian
services for the last decade through an intergovernmental health services
agreement with the Village of Wilmette. Earlier this year, Wilmette informed
the Village that it would terminate the agreement effective July 1, 2017, due
to the retirement of its full-time Sanitarian, Lynn Hoette. Ms. Hoette proposes
to provide her services to Winnetka through her private company, Health
Inspection Professional, Inc. Since Ms. Hoette is known by Winnetka’s food
business owners and has done a satisfactory job for the last ten years, Mr.
D’Onofrio recommended entering into a contract for sanitation services with her
new private firm.
   The Council briefly discussed the request and Ms. Hoette assured President
Rintz that she would be Winnetka’s main service provider.
   Ms. Balsamo asked about the qualifications to become a licensed sanitarian,
and Ms. Hoette explained the process.
   Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-40-
2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   e)   Old Business. None.
9)   New Business.
   a)   Environmental and Forestry Commission Green Awards. Mr. Saunders
explained that the Environmental and Forestry Commission (EFC) has developed
its first annual Green Awards program to recognize those in the community that
demonstrate exceptional commitment to sustainability and environmental
awareness. As Mr. Saunders was introducing the two recipients for the 2017
Green Awards, the Chair of the EFC, Chuck Dowding, arrived.
   Mr. Dowding presented an award to Liz Kunkle in the category of Recycling/
Waste Reduction for her efforts to increase recycling through work with
Winnetka’s schools, the EFC, Village vendors and others.
   Next, Mr. Dowding presented an award to Theodore Wynnychenko and Laura Hill
in the category of Green Building for their construction of a LEED certified
home in the area of water-energy interconnection, use of geothermal heating and
cooling, closed cell insulation, countertop composting, two rain gardens, a
rainwater harvesting system, use of LED bulbs and installation of permeable
paving.
11)   Appointments:
   a)   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to re-appoint Meg Benson
as Chair of the Board of Fire and Police Commissioners. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
   b)   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to appoint Brad McLane
to the Zoning Board of Appeals to fill Bob Dearborn’s unexpired term. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
   c)   Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to appoint Gene Greable
to the Zoning Board of Appeals for a full term. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
   d)   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to appoint the
following Downtown Master Plan Task Force members: Chair John Talty, Meg
Benson, Bob Winter, Steve Hudson, Becky Hurley and John Swierk. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
