June 20, 2017
(Approved: July 11, 2017)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
20, 2017, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, and Kristin Ziv.
Absent: Village Manager Robert Bahan. Also present: Assistant Village Manager
Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Benjamin Schuster, Director of Water & Electric
Brian Keys, Interim Police Chief Marc Hornstein, and approximately 6 persons in
the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Myers led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
July 6, 2017 Rescheduled Regular Meeting. Assistant Manager Pierce announced
that this meeting is cancelled.
July 11, 2017 Regular Meeting / Study Session. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
July 18, 2017 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Julian Solotorousky, Asbury Avenue, and Steve Gerbel, Oakdale Avenue. Mr.
Gerbel distributed a handout containing photographs of the conditions at the
Hubbard Woods Metra station while Mr. Solotorousky described conditions at the
station as not only an eyesore, but also dangerous. He urged the Village to use
money from its Capital budget to improve the crumbling platforms and make them
safe for elderly or visually impaired commuters.
President Rintz said that Metra has told the Village that a rehabilitation
project for the Hubbard Woods station is on their Capital Plan, and he added
that he could not see spending Village funds to improve their property.
Assistant Manager Pierce said Metra scoped the engineering and station
improvements as early as 2015, however, funds are held up in the State bond
program. She noted that the Village hired an engineering company to inspect the
station and after the East stairway was deemed unsafe, Metra made an
appointment to visit the site in the next week or so.
The Council discussed different options for dealing with the situation.
President Rintz said the Village will engage directly with Metra about the
physical deterioration of the station, but he advised caution, as Metra has the
authority to close the station rather than rehabilitate it. Residents were
encouraged to attend the Metra Board meetings to make their concerns about the
station known.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo thanked the Council for moving Public
Comment to the beginning of its meetings.
Reports.
Village President.
President Rintz invited the community to two upcoming events: Art in the Park
and the Winnetka Music Festival, and he thanked Trustee Myers for donating his
time and energy to run the music festival. He added that he had done some
research on a parcel of Village-owned land in the Indian Hill business
district, and had concluded that the Village’s return on its investment is very
generous, since the parcel has brought in substantial revenues.
Trustees.
Trustee Myers reported on the last Chamber meeting.
Trustee Lanphier reported on the last Community Conversations.
Attorney.
None.
Manager.
None.
Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the Agenda. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda.
Village Council Minutes.
June 6, 2017 Regular Meeting.
Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated June 2 – 15, 2017 in the amount
of $934,378.11.
Resolution No. R-48-2017: Annual Prevailing Wage Resolution (Adoption). A
Resolution adopting the 2017 prevailing wages.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to approve the foregoing items on
the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-49-2017: Approving Motorola STARCOM Lease Agreement
(Adoption). Interim Police Chief Hornstein explained that Federal legislation
has mandated the vacation of existing radio frequencies used for public safety
communications. The Village currently participates in a radio communication
network formed in 1975 with 13 other North Shore communities; and this
infrastructure has not been significantly improved since 2000.
The option of building a new network to support a new radio frequency was
studied and the cost was estimated at between $10 and $15 million. A better
option is to join a larger, existing network currently being used by Glenview,
Northbrook, Niles and Morton Grove. This new system is digital and does not
require the use of telephone lines, which keeps subscriber costs down. The
Village recommends joining this network, called STARCOM-21, and entering into a
lease agreement with Motorola to use the Water & Electric plant smoke stack for
a communications site.
After a short discussion, Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to
adopt Resolution No. R-49-2017 approving the lease agreement with Motorola. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier,
Myers, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
None.
Appointments.
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 7:50 p.m.
