MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
September 5, 2017
(Approved: September 19, 2017)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 5, 2017, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Kristin Ziv. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Director
of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Public Works Director, Steve Saunders, Finance
Director Tim Sloth, Community Development Director David Schoon, Village
Attorney Brooke Lenneman, and approximately 6 persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Cripe led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
September 12, 2017 Study Session. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
September 19, 2017 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
October 3, 2017 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Swierk reported that he had begun his tours of the Village departments.
Trustee Cripe invited the community to the annual 9/11 flag planting ceremony
on Village Green on September 10.
Attorney.
None.
Manager.
None.
Village President.
President Rintz congratulated the Park District on opening the newly
rehabilitated Dwyer Park; he reported on the progress of the Hubbard Woods
train station rehabilitation work; updated the Council on One Winnetka final
application progress; noted that the Village had met with Hoffman Group to get
a sense of their plans for the various buildings it owns in the Village; and
finally, he announced that the Lincoln Avenue parking lot rehabilitation
project is complete and the lot is now open.
Approval of the Agenda.
After Trustee Lanphier asked a question about the third curb cut at 220 Birch,
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the Agenda. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
August 15, 2017 Regular Meeting.
Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated August 11-31, 2017 in the amount
of $713,906.93.
Resolution No. R-61-2017; Approving A Purchase of Spare Circuit Breaker For The
Plant Load Center From Eaton Corp. (Adoption). A Resolution approving the
purchase of a spare circuit breaker from Eaton Corp. for the Plant Load Center.
Resolution R-62-2017: Third Curb Cut for Driveway at 220 Birch Street
(Adoption). A Resolution granting approval for a third curb cut to allow a
second driveway at 220 Birch.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-6-2017: 1121 Merrill Street, Variations (Introduction /
Adoption).
Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for zoning variations in order to construct a
new detached garage on the Subject Property, which is a through lot and does
not have a rear yard under the Zoning Ordinance. He noted that the applicant
has requested a waiver of introduction and immediate adoption of the Ordinance.
The Council asked a few questions, and President Rintz asked why the request
had been reduced by two feet. Mr . Schoon explained that it was an attempt by
the Zoning Board of Appeals to reduce the amount of the requested setback
variation.
Trustee Swierk questioned whether the setback was measured properly from Gordon
Terrace at 18 feet.
The applicant’s architect, Andrew Venamore, clarified that the reduction in
size was done to reduce the intensity of the requested setback variation and
the setback from Gordon Terrace was 16 feet. Mr. Schoon concurred, explaining
that the notice for the Zoning Board of Appeals had the correct 16-foot
measurement.
Trustee Lanphier said she would like time to visit the property before voting
to approve the request. The other Trustees were in favor of waiving
introduction.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to introduce Ordinance M-6-
2017 granting zoning variations from GFA, front yard lot coverage, front yard
setback, and garage regulations, to allow construction of a detached garage at
1121 Merrill. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Resolution No. R-59-2017, Waiving Formal Bidding and Approving Change Order No.
2 to the Agreement with ALamp Concrete Contractors (Adoption). Mr. Keys
explained that there have been four water main breaks on Forest Glen Drive in
the past month, with two of the breaks flooding a residence at 1245 Forest Glen
Drive. He said the main was installed in 1901, and that staff is requesting
expediting replacement of 1,330 feet of water main to avoid impacting the
residence at 1245 Forest Glen Drive further.
Mr. Keys noted that design of the project has been expedited and a permit
applied for from the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency (IEPA). He
explained that a transfer of funds from the General Fund to the Water Fund, as
approved in the 2017 Budget, could fund the expedited water main replacement,
and would be repaid in 2019 with an IEPA loan. In addition, ALamp Concrete
Contractors, Inc. was the lowest qualified bidder for the project, at the same
unit prices for the 2017 Street Rehabilitation Project. Finally, temporary
patching would be used on Forest Glen at the conclusion of the water main
replacement, since this road is scheduled for work in the 2019 Street
Rehabilitation Project.
Mr. Sloth distributed a spreadsheet showing the impact on the water main
replacement plan if the Water Fund receives the $1 million loan from the
General Fund, noting that it would reduce the amount of funding for water main
projects in 2019 and 2020. A loan from the IEPA will be applied for, to repay
the loan.
The Council asked questions and discussed the request. Trustee Dearborn said he
thought the Water Fund should pay interest on a loan from the General Fund.
Manager Bahan said since the loan will be very short-term until an IEPA loan is
secured, interest was not discussed, but that the Council could do so if it
wished.
Mr. Keys noted that the Village needs more experience with the replacement plan
before it can know whether the rankings need to be adjusted to account for
something like significant property damage during main breaks. He explained
that to keep the Water Fund whole, scheduled 2018 water main work will be held
back until 2019 and 2020, barring emergencies.
After more discussion, the Council directed staff to add interest to the $1
million General Fund loan to the Water Fund, equivalent to that charged by an
IEPA loan.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-59-
2017 approving Change Order #2 to the agreement with ALamp Concrete
Contractors, Inc. for the 2017 Street Rehabilitation Contract, for the
replacement of the Forest Glen East water main. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-60-2017: Approving a Contract with Fairbanks Morse Engine for
Repairs to Diesel Generator #9 (Adoption). Mr. Keys explained that a crucial
generator at the Electric Plant has a mechanical issue, and has been taken out
of service until repairs can be made. The original manufacturer of the
generator, Fairbanks Morse Engine, inspected the unit, and recommended it be
used only in the event of emergency until it is repaired. Mr. Keys said he is
requesting authorization to repair the generator using parts and labor from
Fairbanks Morse, and he explained the extent of the work and how it would be
accomplished. He noted that the lead time for parts is 155 days; therefore,
time is of the essence.
After a short discussion, the Council agreed to authorize the repair.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-60-
2017 approving a contract with Fairbanks Morse Engine to repair Diesel
Generator #9. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution R-63-2017: Stormwater Management Preliminary Engineering (Adoption).
Mr. Saunders explained that after the Council approved a Memorandum of
Understanding with Cook County for a stormwater detention project on land owned
by the Forest Preserves, staff was directed to obtain a proposal from Strand
Associates for engineering work to flesh out the detention portions of the
Stormwater Management Plan. This is being done to satisfy a major requirement
of the Forest Preserves that Winnetka use land within its boundaries for other
stormwater storage projects.
Mr. Saunders said he envisions going through a process similar to the one with
the Forest Preserves to reach agreements with the other owners of open space in
Winnetka. He added that refinement of the concepts during engagement with their
boards will be necessary. In addition, the Village needs a permit from the Army
Corps of Engineers to mitigate the wetlands on the Forest Preserve site, and
Strand will help with that application process.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the proposed scope of work and the schedule, adding it is
hoped that the work will be complete in March, 2018. He explained that this
next phase of the planning will mainly require meetings with the park and
school boards to gain preliminary approval of the project concepts, and that
allocating funds for more robust community engagement was not necessary until a
later stage.
President Rintz explained that the Forest Preserve plan was revised to cut
costs, and it is expected that plans for the other storage projects will be
similarly revised during the negotiation process with the other landowners. He
cautioned against spending more money on consulting fees for public engagement
so soon in the negotiation process.
Several Trustees advocated for continued community engagement during
negotiations with other agencies. In addition, it was suggested that a
flowchart of the different pieces and their timing could be developed for the
Council and public to understand how the different phases of the overall
Stormwater Vision would come together.
Ultimately, there was consensus to move forward with engaging Strand to perform
the next phase of preliminary engineering for the Stormwater Vision project.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Chris Crone, Winnetka. Mr. Crone said he favored early community engagement, so
public input could be considered during project revisions, and to keep the
public up to speed with developments during this next phase of negotiations.
Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-63-2017
authorizing Strand Associates to perform preliminary engineering and permit
applications for Phase 1 and Phase 2 of the Stormwater Management Vision for
Western and Southwestern Winnetka, for a fee not to exceed $107,800. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers,
Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
None.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:51 p.m.
Deputy Clerk
September 12, 2017
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
September 12, 2017
(Approved: October 17, 2017)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 12, 2017, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Kristin Ziv. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky, Interim Police Chief
Marc Hornstein, Finance Director Tim Sloth, Assistant Finance Director Nick
Mostardo, and approximately 4 persons in the audience.
Public Comment.
None.
2017 Winnetka Public Safety Pension Update.
President Rintz commented that a similar meeting was held in 2009 to discuss
the public safety pensions, and not much has changed in that time. The
conclusion then was that there was not much Winnetka could do to change
downstate pension issues; however, it is important for all Councils to
thoroughly understand the pensions and funding.
Mr. Sloth introduced Mark Fuller, President of the Fire Pension Board, John
O’Malley, President of the Police Pension Board; Mary Nye from AndCo., the
pension funds’ investment advisor; Jason Franken, the funds’ actuary; and Kevin
McCanna from Speer Financial, the Village’s investment advisor.
After Mr. Sloth gave an overview of the public safety pension funds’
performance, Ms. Nye explained her role as general investment consultant to the
pension funds, and reviewed asset allocation provisions mandated by State
Statute. She recapped investment performance for each fund, noting that
AndCo.’s focus for pension funds is on a 50-year horizon, not short-term
results.
Next, Mr. Franken explained basic funding concepts, and noted that Winnetka’s
public safety pension funds have achieved positive returns for most of the past
decade, even performing above the median in 2009, when returns were generally
low. He noted that the actuary’s role is to smooth asset gains/losses over a
five-year period to create a stable contribution path – which is difficult to
accomplish in a volatile market. He said amortization of unfunded liabilities
will decrease slowly over time, as Tier 2 employees start to become the
majority.
Finally, Mr. McCanna explained that Moody’s overemphasizes pensions in its bond
ratings, and now uses a corporate bond rate to restate liabilities. Even so,
Winnetka retains its Aaa rating. He said given that Winnetka updated its
mortality tables and exceeds State funding requirements, the Village is in good
shape and should stay on this path.
A few Trustees asked questions and discussed the issue of pension funding over
the past 10 years. Trustee Ziv raised concern about the performance of the
Village’s pension funds and said they should be looking at market-based
benchmarks. President Rintz noted that raising taxes to further increase
pension contributions does not create an appreciable increase in property
values, and he asked how Winnetka compares to its neighbors.
Mr. Franken replied that the statewide average funding ratio is 57-59%, with a
lot of the plans using higher return assumptions and older mortality tables. If
everyone used Winnetka’s funding methodology, their funding ratios would
decrease. Conversely, if Winnetka used the methods of other towns, its funding
ratio would increase. He said compared to other municipalities, Winnetka’s
pensions are funded significantly above average.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Mark Fuller, Chair of the Fire Pension Fund. Mr. Fuller noted that interest
rates are historically low, which affects compliance with the Village’s fund
balance policy. He said the pension funds declined by relatively small amounts
in 2008 because Winnetka uses a lower than average assumption on returns, and
he explained that the goal is to plot a course that smooths out volatility in
the markets.
Gene Greable, former Village President. Mr. Greable noted that the
presentations were very informative, and he advised the Council to continue to
work with its actuaries and financial advisors, as they are all top notch
consultants.
President Rintz noted that the pension board meetings are open the public, and
he urged those interested in the pension funding issue to attend.
The Council was in general agreement that Winnetka is following a good path.
Trustee Ziv expressed some concerns and said the funds’ performance should be
reviewed by the Council annually. It was suggested that a short presentation be
made at budget time each year to ensure the goals are being met, and an annual
presentation given to the Council by the Village’s investment advisors.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:27 p.m.
