community events.
Attorney.
None.
Manager.
Manager Bahan said he would keep the Council informed of developments in the
State Legislature’s fall veto session.
Village President.
President Rintz gave the following report:
Village officials and a team from Strand Associates took a walking tour of Duke
Childs Field and Crow Island Woods to become familiar with the sites, and
investigate stormwater mitigation opportunities. Strand is working on some
engineering concepts based on those walking tours.
President Rintz attended a meeting with the board presidents of New Trier High
School, District 36, and the Winnetka Park District. He updated those
colleagues on the Village’s stormwater progress and learned their preferences
for presentations at their board meetings. He gained insight from New Trier
High School about areas where they believed costs could be reduced during the
design process. During a conversation about Tax Increment Financing (TIF), it
became evident that the schools were not aware of the Village’s preliminary
discussions, or of the Council’s desire to be extremely cautious with funding
amounts. President Rintz called on the Trustees to help people in the community
to understand what the Council is trying to do with its TIF approach to
Downtown Master Plan implementation. He suggested these Boards should be
invited to the October Study Session devoted to TIF discussion, and they also
be sent a recent Illinois Municipal Review article on TIF.
The Village is still waiting for documents from the One Winnetka developer
before the Council can consider final approval for the planned development.
Fred’s Garage is open and bringing energy and a great atmosphere to the Elm
Business District; and Minos in Hubbard Woods is preparing a soft opening next
week.
The Hubbard Woods train station repairs are complete – the stairs and platforms
are safer, but not looking great. He called on Village staff to keep pushing
Metra to move forward on the station renovations.
The Downtown Master Plan Task Force will meet later in October, and the
community is urged to attend and participate in this important initiative.
The Forest Preserves are seeking help from the Winnetka community as they hold
a series of design workshops for planned improvements at the Skokie Lagoons
boat launch. They would like a citizens group to give feedback and work with
the Forest Preserves on fundraising efforts to fund the improvements.
Interested parties should contact the Village Manager’s office or President
Rintz.
