vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
September 19, 2017 Regular Meeting.
Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated September 15-28, 2017 in the
amount of $555,157.17.
Resolution No. R-66-2017: Approving an Intergovernmental Agreement to Borrow
Emergency Vehicles with the Village of Northbrook (Adoption).
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to approve the foregoing items on
the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-67-2017: 1236 – 1240 Asbury – Subdivision (Adoption). Mr.
Schoon reviewed this request for approval of a plat of resubdivision for two
existing parcels at 1236 and 1240 Asbury. The Subject Property currently
consists of two lots, one that is improved with a single family home which
complies with lot area requirements, and a vacant lot which has a legal,
nonconforming lot size. The proposed new subdivision would move the lot line
between the existing two lots to create two conforming buildable lots.
Mr. Schoon explained that the applicant proposes to demolish the single family
home, garage and swimming pool at 1236 Asbury and to construct two new homes,
one on each new lot. The Plan Commission voted unanimously to recommend
approval of the resubdivision, subject to two restrictive covenants.
After the Trustees asked a few questions, President Rintz asked the applicant
if he would like to comment.
Glenn Gutnayer, Glen Gutnayer Custom Homes and purchaser of the lots. Mr.
Gutnayer said he would apply for construction permits as soon as the demolition
is complete, and that both houses would be built simultaneously to minimize
disruption on the block.
Doug Gurley, current owner of the lots. Mr. Gurley explained that he spent two
years drawing up plans to remodel the home, but it became apparent that that
the cost of doing so outweighed the value of the home.
Several Trustees raised concerns about stormwater runoff from the two proposed
new homes, and asked whether they could put restrictions on the proposed
subdivision. Some were in favor of requiring that all of the runoff generated
from the new construction be detained.
Attorney Friedman explained that the subdivision request meets the Village’s
standards for subdivisions, and the applicant is not asking for zoning relief
at this time.
Trustee Cripe read from Section 15.26.100(e) of the Village Code, which
provides that: “No new building, other structure or addition shall be
constructed which will result in the surface water run-off, during and
following construction of any such improvement, at a rate greater than the
water run-off immediately prior to such construction…”.
It was understood that during the building permit approval process, the Village
has authority to require sufficient stormwater detention to prevent adverse
impacts of new development on neighboring properties. This could be
accomplished by treating the two new lots as previously undeveloped for
Engineering Guidelines review purposes, so that all new stormwater runoff would
be detained.
With the question of stormwater impacts settled, there was consensus to approve
the resubdivision request.
Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-67-
2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
