Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 7:58 p.m.
Deputy Clerk
October 10, 2017
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
October 10, 2017
(Approved: November 7, 2017)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 10, 2017, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Kristin Ziv. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Ben Schuster,
Community Development Director David Schoon, Economic Development Coordinator
Mike Martella, and approximately 19 persons in the audience.
Public Comment.
None.
Tax Increment Financing (TIF) Phase II Update.
President Rintz gave a comprehensive review of the downtown revitalization
process during the last 12 years. In 2007, a plan developed by of a special
task force and its consultants was unveiled, but the public had significant
concerns about the possibility of spending Winnetka’s reserves. In 2009 the
plan was tabled, and the Village’s focus shifted to stormwater control after a
storm in 2011 caused extensive flooding.
President Rintz noted that the One Winnetka Planned Development offers an
opportunity to invest in a large streetscape project. He explained that the
creation of a Tax Increment Financing (TIF) district could help to fund
revitalization opportunities, especially in light of the fact that the downtown
Equalized Assessed Values (EAV) declined in the past three years. He said the
Village will be very cautious and conservative in its approach to a TIF, and
does not plan to reap more funds than are needed to do specific projects. The
other taxing districts in Winnetka have been invited to join the conversation,
and to share their concerns about the impact of a TIF on their budgets.
Ms. Pierce explained that Phase II of the TIF evaluation being performed by
Ehlers & Associates includes drafting a Redevelopment Plan containing project
costs and goals, as well as identification of a budget. Council direction is
needed in these areas before Phase II can be completed and a determination made
on whether to begin work on Phase III. She introduced Maureen Barry and
Jennifer Tammen, advisors from Ehlers & Associates, to give a presentation
clarifying the difference between Redevelopment Project Costs and a TIF Budget.
After Ms. Barry reviewed the scope of the Phase II project, she explained the
differences between the Redevelopment Project Costs (RPC) and the TIF Budget.
The RPC is a designation of the maximum TIF dollars that could be spent over
the life of the TIF; the TIF Budget is developed every year as part of the
annual Budget process. She defined the rules that govern categories for TIF
expenses, which impact what the Village could spend. Ehlers developed the
incremental revenue estimates using growth from the One Winnetka Planned
development and inflation, projected out 23 years, which is when the TIF would
expire. The net value over the life of the TIF is projected to be approximately
$34 million, based on the growth, the Village’s tax rate, and collection
estimates. If the Council’s goal is not to spend the total amount that the TIF
would accrue, it would set the RPC lower.
After Ms. Barry reviewed a list of TIF-eligible expenses, Ms. Tammen
distributed a handout of comparable communities’ TIF programs.
Trustee Myers asked how the Council could calculate its return on investment,
since enhancing community vibrancy and economic vitality are difficult to
quantify. Ms. Tammen explained there is no way to tell in the planning stage
what the return will be, as this stage is broad assumptions and conceptual
costs. Later studies can measure the effects of the enhancements, and the TIF
Annual Report could contain this type of analysis.
The Council thoroughly discussed the concept of using a TIF district to improve
the Elm business district. Concern was expressed about choosing an amount for
the RPC without any data about specific projects and their estimated costs.
Trustee Ziv expressed doubt about whether a TIF should be used as a funding
source. There was unanimity in the desire to be cautious and conservative in
the approach to using TIF funds, and to ensure the schools and other taxing
bodies don’t suffer undue hardship as a result of the EAV freeze. It was
generally agreed that finding a way to accelerate investment in the downtowns
is a viable objective. The role of the Downtown Master Plan Task Force was also
discussed as a way to help prioritize improvements and refine costs.
Ms. Pierce explained that the rationale for adopting a TIF before the next tax
year begins was to capture the base EAV before One Winnetka begins development.
She also reviewed the scope of work for the Downtown Master Plan Task Force,
and projected timeline.
President Rintz requested development of a broad list of projects and general
cost estimates for the Council to examine at another meeting in the near
future, to help them arrive at an RPC threshold. Next, he called for public
comment.
Trish Kocanda, Superintendent of Winnetka Public Schools District 36. Ms.
Kocanda said the schools share the Village’s interest in a thriving downtown,
and she expressed an interest in learning more about TIF and what the tax
impact would be on the District 36 budget, especially in light of aging
infrastructure and burdens on district taxpayers.
Paul Sally, Superintendent of New Trier High School District 203. Mr. Sally
explained that the District 203 tax levy is spread over a number of
communities, so the freeze in one EAV would result in increased levies for
those other taxpayers. He noted that an intergovernmental agreement to address
the secondary effects of a Winnetka TIF district could be a way to potentially
alleviate concerns of the school board.
Rebecca Wolf, Winnetka-Northfield Library Director. Ms. Wolf noted that the
Library District works very well with the Village, and she expressed a desire
to be part of the conversations about the potential TIF district.
President Rintz explained that the Village is looking at relatively small
amounts compared to what a TIF could potentially deliver, and that the
structure would be carefully planned.
Manager Bahan said staff will develop some proposed goals and cost estimates to
bring back to the Council, and continue to stay engaged with the Village’s
other taxing districts.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:09 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
