Public Comment.
None.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Lanphier reported that she enjoyed the Public Safety Open House the
previous Saturday.
Trustee Cripe reported on the Illinois Solar Open House a few weeks ago.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. President Rintz reported on the New Trier Open House
showcasing the school’s recent renovations, and he commented that the project
was a big success.
Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
September 12, 2017 Study Session.
October 3, 2017 Regular Meeting.
Warrant List. Approving the Warrant List dated September 29 – October 12, 2017
in the amount of $1,319,210.54.
Resolution No. R-69-2017: Green Bay Road & Elm Street Traffic Signal
(Adoption). Awarding a contract to Home Towne Electric, Inc. for traffic signal
modernization at Green Bay Road and Elm Street, in the amount of $276,689.04.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Myers.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-68-2017: Easement Agreements for 824 Boal Parkway (Adoption).
Mr. Saunders explained that stormwater improvements on Boal Parkway will
provide a connection between two existing storm sewers to alleviate standing
water during large rain storms. Before completion of the project, a new
easement is required at the Subject Property, along with a payment of $2,600
from the Village to the homeowner to provide for restoration of the property
once construction is completed.
The Council briefly discussed the request.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-68-
2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Myers.
Ordinance No. M-7-2017: 191 Sheridan Road, Variation (Introduction/Adoption).
Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for variations from front yard setback
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to allow construction of a walkway, artist
studio and pool in the front yard of the Subject Property. The proposed
improvements would replace an existing legal, nonconforming cabana that
currently sits in the front yard setback. Lots on Sheridan Road are through
lots, and as such, have two front yards. The east front yard setback is
measured from Lake Michigan.
Mr. Schoon said the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended approval of the request
at its September 11 meeting; however, since the variation is a reduction
greater than 50% of the setback requirement, the Village Council has final
approval of the request. A neighbor to the north of the Subject Property has
raised concerns about building on the bluff and is requesting a delay in
approving the request.
The Council discussed the issue of building on the lakefront bluff, and Trustee
Cripe requested that the applicant provide details on the proposal.
Zibby Miechowicki, the applicant’s contractor, explained that the existing pool
house at the lakefront is deteriorating and needs to be replaced. In an effort
to shore up the bluff and design a structure that matched the house, a proposal
was developed that included retaining walls, pool, patio and an artist studio.
Mr. Miechowicki said the proposed improvements were more desirable than simply
shoring up the bluff with a concrete wall.
There was an extensive discussion about the how the construction of the
retaining walls might disturb the bluff, and Mr. Miechowicki said he is being
advised by lakefront structural engineers and Public Works Director Steve
Saunders.
The Trustees requested a visual contrasting the current encroachment into the
setback vs. the new proposed encroachment, as there was nothing in the agenda
packet that clearly illustrated the desired comparison.
President Rintz noted that an application for such a significant variance
should provide all the documentation the Council needs to make an informed
decision. He suggested the matter could be deferred until the applicant can
supply better exhibits, and Mr. Miechowicki agreed to postpone the matter until
the next Council meeting.
Trustee Swierk said it was important to preserve as much of the required
setback as possible, to avoid setting a precedent for more such requests. He
asked Mr. Miechowicki to find out how far from the water the proposed structure
could be moved and still satisfy the homeowner. Trustee Dearborn asked how many
other lakefront properties have similar structures and whether they encroach
into the required setback.
Mitchell Ruchim, on behalf of an adjacent neighbor, Leo Birov. Mr. Ruchim
thanked the Council for deferring the matter, as Mr. Birov has grave concerns
about the variation request was unable to attend this meeting because he is out
of the country.
With no objections from the Trustees, President Rintz said the matter will be
continued until the November 7 Council meeting.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
None.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:07 p.m.
            
Deputy Clerk
October 26, 2017
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL BUDGET MEETING
October 26, 2017
(Approved: November 7, 2017)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
October 26, 2017, at 6:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 6:04 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, and Kristin Ziv. Absent:
Trustees Scott Myers and John Swierk. Also in attendance: Village Manager
Robert Bahan, Finance Director Tim Sloth, Assistant Finance Director Nick
Mostardo, Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky, Deputy Fire Chief John Ripka, Public
Safety Analyst Martin Sellers, and 2 persons in the audience.
Public Comment.
None.
Budget Review Topic:
Budget Overview/2017 Tax Levy. Mr. Sloth reviewed the timeline of the Budget
process, which begins in July with setting goals, and ends in September with
preparing the draft Budget. He explained that the Budget is scheduled to be
introduced at a Public Hearing in November, and will be adopted, along with the
Rate Resolutions, in early December. He noted that the draft 2018 Budget is
balanced – meaning it is financed by operating revenues, and does not use fund
balances to pay for ongoing expenses. The Village has consistently retained its
Aaa bond rating due to careful stewardship of the Village’s finances and
funding the public safety pension funds as recommended by the Village’s
actuary.
Mr. Sloth said the Budget proposes a flat property tax for the fourth
consecutive year, meaning no property tax increase for existing homeowners, and
a slight 0.8% increase for new growth. The 2018 increase in fees for a typical
resident will be $173 (2.3%).
Next, Mr. Sloth reviewed General Fund revenues and transfers, along with
operating expenditures. He noted that the Village stays below State-mandated
property tax caps, even though under Home Rule it could levy more. Increased
revenue from the 2017 tax levy will go directly to public safety pension
funding.
Capital Improvement Plan Overview. Mr. Sloth reviewed the Five-Year Capital
Improvement Plan (CIP), noting that only 51% of the CIP is funded and that
discussions will be held in 2018 about the unfunded projects. The 2018 Budget
continues to address aging infrastructure, with funds from the Motor Fuel Tax
helping to fund road and parking lot repair. Money is also set aside for
downtown revitalization; fire protection at the transformer yard; water system
improvements; and refuse equipment and vehicles.
As the Council discussed the Budget overview, Trustee Dearborn expressed
concern about transfers from the General Fund, and he recommended the Council
think about deferral of some initiatives.
President Rintz explained that the General Fund transfer to the Refuse Fund has
been ongoing for the past few decades, and is discussed every year at Budget
time. He noted that much discussion is needed to decide the future funding
structure for the Refuse Fund and how that would be accomplished; the community
would also need to be surveyed before any big changes can be implemented.
Trustee Ziv expressed reservations about the size of the Village’s reserves,
and urged the Council to spend some of the funds on CIP projects.
President Rintz commented that Winnetka is one of few communities in Illinois
to have a Aaa bond rating in large part due to its sizeable reserves and
careful financial planning. The bond rating is critical for execution of large
infrastructure projects, and the Council needs to be cautious about spending
down the reserves.
[Assistant Village Manager Pierce arrived at 6:45 PM.]
Finance Department.
Mr. Mostardo reviewed the functions of the Finance Department, as well as its
divisions and staff numbers. He noted that 2017 was a year of innovation and
collaboration, and he recapped the Department’s significant accomplishments:
(i) winning the Distinguished Budget Presentation award and receiving a
Certificate of Excellence in Financial Reporting from the Government Finance
Officers Association; (ii) a redesign of the utility bills to provide more
information to customers; (iii) a redesign of the Village’s financial
statements for greater transparency; (iv) the launch of online payments for
vehicle stickers and dog licenses; (v) providing assistance with rate studies;
and (vi) completion of an internal control review.
Mr. Mostardo said the 2017 Budget is running at or slightly below projections,
and the proposed 2018 Budget is essentially flat due to a decrease in staffing
levels. Goals for 2018 include: (i) implement revised internal control
procedures; (ii) continue enhancing cyber security measures; (iii) win budget
and audit awards; (iv) apply for the Tyler Technologies Excellence award; and
(v) continue support for utility funds, treasury management and investment
strategies.
Fire Department.
Chief Berkowsky reviewed the Department’s personnel, administrative duties of
officers, and demographics. He noted that 25% of his personnel have 20 or more
years of service, which could result in retirements at any time. In addition,
there have been some challenges due to injuries and a Firemedic on military
leave. He explained that a request is being made to hire a transitional
Firemedic in 2018 to supplement current and future staffing shortfalls.
Chief Berkowsky said overtime costs are managed by using the Chief, Deputy
Chief and Administrative Lieutenants to cover shifts wherever possible; the
overtime cost savings in 2017 were approximately $198,000.
Deputy Chief Ripka reviewed a request to adjust fees for the radio alarm
network to recoup costs incurred when the Village transitioned to the Combined
Dispatch system earlier this year.
Chief Berkowsky explained that the Fire Department budget increase of 6.8%
includes the cost of a transitional Firemedic as well as a new HVAC system to
replace the current one, which is over 20 years old and at the end of its life.
Goals for 2018 include: (i) install Customer Relationship Management software;
(ii) provide advanced cardiac life-support training to all Department
paramedics; (iii) implement a rescue drone program; and (iv) adopt the 2015
International Fire Codes.
Assistant Manager Pierce explained that hiring a transitional firefighter would
allow the Village to pull from its current eligibility list, which will expire
in 2018.
Public Affairs/Village Manager’s Office.
Ms. Pierce explained the functions of the Public Affairs Office vs. the Village
Manager’s Office, noting that all staffing comes out of the Manager’s Office,
which is separated into three divisions: Administration, Human Resources and
Economic Development. Objectives for the Manager’s Office in 2018 include: (i)
conduct a communications assessment and to develop a new Communications Plan;
(ii) complete the Tax Increment Finance evaluation; (iii) implement Customer
Relationship Management software; and (iv) revise the 2016 Business Plan.
Ms. Pierce next reviewed objectives for the Economic Development Division: (i)
conduct a business climate survey; (ii) continue sponsorship and facilitation
of local special events; (iii) conduct a marketing and branding assessment; and
(iv) continue fostering of co-advertising opportunities for Winnetka.
Finally, Ms. Pierce reviewed the goals for the Human Resources Division: (i)
conduct a compensation study; (ii) administer entry-level testing for the Fire
and Police Departments, along with Police promotional testing; (iii) begin
collective bargaining negotiations with Police and Fire unions; and (iv)
facilitate Village-wide customer service training.
President Rintz commented that the Village’s website needs upgrading as soon as
possible, as it is the Village’s most important communications and customer
service tool.
After a brief discussion, President Rintz called for public comment.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo said the proposed Budget reflects
careful planning and is very thorough, which is what the community wants to
see.
Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:04 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
November 2, 2017
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL BUDGET MEETING
November 2, 2017
(Approved: November 21, 2017)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
November 2, 2017, at 6:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 6:03 p.m. Present: Trustees
Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers John Swierk, and Kristin Ziv.
Absent: Trustee Cripe. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Finance Director Tim Sloth, Assistant Finance Director Nick Mostardo, Director
of Community Development David Schoon, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
Assistant Public Works Director Jim Bernahl, and no persons in the audience.
Public Comment.
None; no audience members were present.
Budget Review Topic:
Community Development Department.
After Mr. Schoon reviewed the functions of the Community Development
Department, he listed his 2018 objectives: (i) Downtown Master Plan
implementation; (ii) assistance with the One Winnetka Development; (iii)
adoption of the 2015 building code series; (iv) processing building permits and
conducting inspections; (v) Board/Commission support; and (vi) economic
development activities.
Next, Mr. Schoon compared the budgets of 2016 – 2018, noting that increases are
largely driven by increased supply costs and third-party services. He reviewed
a building permit revenue comparison from 2017 vs. 2018, noting that it is very
difficult to predict construction activity in the Village year to year.
After a brief discussion, there were no changes to the proposed 2018 Community
Development budget.
Public Works Department.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the Public Works Department’s functions along with an
organizational chart, noting that the Department has downsized through
attrition over the past few years. The proposed 2018 budget contains funding
for a full-time Refuse Collector and a transitional Forester, in anticipation
of potential retirements, as the Forester is an integral part of Winnetka’s
urban forest maintenance program.
Motor Fuel Tax Fund.
Mr. Saunders explained that investments in equipment and street rehabilitation
make up the bulk of the 2018 Capital Improvement budget, and he reviewed how
the streets are chosen for rehabilitation using a an annual roadway evaluation.
The streets are programmed for rehabilitation in conjunction with the water
main replacement program, to achieve efficiencies in street repaving and
patching.
During a discussion about equipment replacement costs, Mr. Saunders explained
that the two leaf-vacuum machines are beyond repair, and dump truck which is
also a front-line snow plow has failing hydraulics, and the Village’s mechanics
cannot repair them.
[Trustee Cripe arrived at 7:00 PM.]
Trustee Myers asked if Winnetka could develop a shared plowing service with
other area municipalities to save on capital costs. Mr. Saunders said it would
be interesting to try and achieve economies of scale on this area; however,
problems would likely arise when trying to decide who would get to use which
truck and when. Manager Bahan said staff would keep examining the concept.
Sanitary Sewer Fund.
Mr. Saunders reviewed areas where repairs are planned in 2018, and he noted
that the cash flow in the fund will go negative by 2022 if no action is taken
to stabilize it. He explained that continuation of rate increases are offset by
a corresponding reduction in water usage in recent years, and reviewed other
revenue options for consideration: (i) implementing a base monthly fee; (ii)
reducing or eliminating the backflow reimbursement program; and (iii) loans or
other debt (not recommended).
The Council discussed the options, and the majority were in favor of a base $2
monthly sanitary sewer fee for 2018, and also encouraging participation in the
backflow prevention reimbursement program over the next few years, with an eye
towards eliminating the program once significant stormwater improvement
projects have been completed.
Refuse Fund.
Trustee Lanphier announced that she had to leave the meeting early, and said
she was in favor of Alternate Cash Flow #2 to keep the Refuse Fund solvent. She
exited the meeting at 7:50 PM.
Mr. Saunders reviewed staffing for refuse collection, and explained that the
2018 Refuse Fund budget is fairly flat, with the exception of: (i) an
additional employee to cover lost manpower due to vacations, sick time and
injuries; and (ii) capital outlay for two new refuse trucks and three refuse
scooters. He said policy discussions in 2018 will resolve the question of
continued funding of the refuse program (property taxes vs. service fees) and
that public discussion and surveys will help the Council determine the level of
collection service the community desires (rear-door vs. curbside).
Mr. Saunders reviewed the refuse collection fleet, noting that two refuse truck
replacements which have been deferred since 2015 are being proposed for 2018,
along with three refuse scooters. He also reviewed the long-term outlook for
fleet replacement, along with current cost estimates.
Mr. Saunders noted that addition to the purchase of new trucks, long-term
stabilization of the fund is needed, since current billing rates cannot sustain
the equipment investment. He presented three alternate cash flow scenarios for
consideration:
Alternate #1: Increase the refuse fee to $20 per month, and keep $200,000
annual transfer from the General Fund.
Alternate #2: Increase the monthly refuse fee to $25 per month and eliminate
the annual General Fund transfer.
Alternate #3: Increase the monthly refuse fee to $50 per month and eliminate
the annual General Fund transfer and the portion of the tax levy for refuse
collection.
Mr. Saunders recommended increasing the monthly refuse fee to $25 per month,
which would eliminate the need for the annual $500,000 General Fund transfer to
the Refuse Fund. In addition, he said fleet replacements should not be sent out
to bid until the policy discussions have concluded. Looking forward to the 2019
Refuse budget, he recommended public engagement to gauge the community’s
preferences for curbside vs. rear-door collection, and funding refuse services
through property taxes or a separate fee.
Responding to a question about potential cost-savings for curbside collection,
Mr. Saunders explained that since some or even most residents will still opt to
pay for back door collection, the Village will have to offer it, minimizing
cost savings for curb-side service.
Trustee Myers suggested a usage-based system, to account for residents who
winter in other locales and smaller families who produce less waste, in the
interest of fairness and also to incentivize recycling.
The Council thoroughly discussed the Refuse Fund cash flow options, service
levels and equipment replacement. Five Trustees were in favor of Alternate #2’s
increased service fees; however, Trustee Dearborn suggested eliminating the
annual $500,000 General Fund transfer and earmarking the necessary amount of
the tax levy for Refuse Collection at this time. He also opposed funding the
purchase of two new refuse trucks.
Responding to a question about whether outsourcing would provide significant
cost savings, Mr. Saunders said the Village of Glencoe’s monthly service fee
for refuse/recycling, fall leaf collection, and spring cleanup is $43.40 per
month, which is fairly close to Winnetka’s costs of $40.92 per household. He
was unsure of whether Glencoe’s contract includes tipping fees to the landfill.
President Rintz commented there is a market to sell used refuse trucks to other
municipalities – so if new ones are purchased and down the road the Village
outsources refuse collection, those assets could be sold.
Stormwater Fund.
Mr. Saunders next reviewed the Stormwater Fund, which contains an estimated
$6,240,000 in capital projects, including several neighborhood stormwater
projects, and engineering for Stormwater Vision projects. Like the previously
reviewed funds, the Stormwater Fund will experience cash flow problems in the
near future, due to capital project funding needs. With an estimated $57
million in stormwater projects in the Stormwater Vision program, additional
funding will be needed by 2019.
Several Trustees discussed the option of using the Village’s reserves to fund
engineering for the upcoming Stormwater Vision projects, in order to have more
reliable cost estimates for the discussions about borrowing funds or issuing
bonds.
President Rintz agreed that the reserves should be used for the stormwater
project funding, but he cautioned that spending down the reserves prior to a
potential bond issue could hurt the Village’s bond rating. He suggested using
money from the Stormwater Fund to support current projects, and leave the
reserves in the general Fund for the time being, until they are needed.
There was a discussion about how equity issues will come into play, especially
in the area south of Willow Road.
Manager Bahan said he would contact the Village’s financial advisor, Kevin
McCanna, in early 2018 to meet with the Council about a funding schedule.
Fleet Services Fund.
Mr. Saunders noted that increases in 2018 relate mainly to salary increases and
a small increase in services and supplies. He said a contingency amount was
built into the fund in the event that fuel prices rise in 2018.
Motor Fuel Tax Fund.
Mr. Saunders reviewed next year’s projects from the MFT Fund: (i) $300,000 for
the Elm Street-Green Bay Road traffic signal modernization; and (ii) $400,000
for Phase I and II engineering for the Cheery Street and Oak Street Bridge
rehabilitation projects. The bridge projects are eligible for Federal aid in
the amount of 80% of the project cost.
Business District Revitalization Fund.
Due to the lateness of the hour, the Council agreed that discussion of this
fund should be deferred to the next Budget meeting.
Adjournment.
Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By voice
vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:15 p.m.
November 7, 2017
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
November 7, 2017
(Approved: November 21, 2017)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
November 7, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Kristin Ziv. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter M. Friedman,
Community Development Director David Schoon, Director of Water & Electric Brian
Keys, and approximately 45 persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Myers led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
November 9, 2017 Budget Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
November 14, 2017 Study Session. All of the Council members present except
Trustee Dearborn said they expect to attend.
November 21, 2017 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
None.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Lanphier reported that Trustee Myers would be joining her at Community
Conversations the following Saturday.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. President Rintz reported on a meeting held the previous week
with the Cook County Forest Preserve Foundation, as well as on a recent meeting
with the One Winnetka project team to discuss outstanding items for the Planned
Development’s final approval with the Council.
Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the Agenda. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
October 10, 2017 Study Session.
October 17, 2017 Regular Meeting.
October 26, 2017 Budget Meeting.
Warrant List. Approval of Warrant List dated October 13 – November 2, 2017, in
the amount of $1,850,273.74.
Resolution No. R-71-2017: SAFEbuilt Contract Renewal (Adoption). A resolution
approving a renewal contract with SAFEbuilt, Illinois, Inc. for building
inspection and plan review services for 2018.
Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
2017 Preservation Awards Presentation. Louise Holland, Chair of the Landmark
Preservation Commission, thanked the Council for its assistance honoring the
award winners. She introduced three of the Commissioners who were in
attendance: Paul Weaver, Laura Good and Beth Ann Papoutsis; and she expressed
appreciation for their efforts to keep vintage homes in Winnetka.
Ms. Holland described the winning properties and presented the 2017 awards to
the following Winnetka residents:
Emilee & Brian Wolf: 864 Bryant Avenue, Rehabilitation award
Alan Gordon & Julia Ramos Roth: 188 Church Road, Rehabilitation award
Dave & Elisa Bartels: 265 Poplar Street, Rehabilitation award
Kevan & Catherine Comstock: 854 Prospect Avenue, Rehabilitation award
Nan & Wally Greenough: 500 Maple Street, Restoration award
Carter Ruehrdanz & Rose Selker: 635 Maple Street, Restoration award
Ms. Holland commended the 2017 Preservation Award recipients, and thanked them
for their efforts to retain original housing stock in the Village.
President Rintz thanked the awardees for their passion and commitment to
support the architectural heritage of Winnetka.
Trustee Dearborn said he enjoyed serving as the Council’s liaison to the
Landmark Preservation Commission, and he commended the commissioners for their
dedication to preserving the architecture in Winnetka.
The other Trustees thanked the homeowners for working to keep the character of
Winnetka and for investing in their homes and the community.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-70-2017: Approving A Contract With Great Lakes Plumbing And
Heating Co. For Construction Of A Fire Protection System (Adoption). Mr. Keys
reviewed the 2015 Electric Plant fire system assessment process, completed by
Strand Associates. At that time, Strand estimated the cost for a fire
protection system to be $814,000. In 2016, Clark Dietz Engineers was engaged to
design the system, and estimated the project cost at $600,000. The 2017 Budget
had the estimated amount allocated for the project; however, the lowest
qualified bid to complete the work, received in 2017, was $768,000. Based on
that amount, the revised figure will be placed in the 2018 Budget. The low
bidder, Great Lakes Plumbing and Heating Co., is recommended to construct the
system.
The Council briefly discussed the project, and received assurances from Mr.
Keys that the low bidder has been thoroughly vetted and has the qualifications
and references to construct the fire suppression system.
President Rintz noted that he is very comfortable with Great Lakes Plumbing and
Heating, as they have extensive experience with public works projects and enjoy
a reputation as the best pipe fitters in the region.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adopt Resolution. No.
R-70-2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-7-2017: 191 Sheridan Road, Variation (Introduction/Adoption).
Mr. Schoon explained that this request for a zoning variation was first
discussed at the October 17 Council meeting. Action on the request was deferred
so the contractor could provide updated drawings; and the Council would also
have a chance to hear the concerns of a neighboring property owner.
Mr. Schoon reviewed new overlays of the proposed improvements, which would be
set 13.58 feet back from the water’s edge, necessitating a variation of 36.42
feet from the 50-foot setback requirement in the Zoning Ordinance. He also
reviewed aerial photos of lakefront properties, which the Council requested in
order to gauge the extent of other similar developments in Winnetka. He
explained that it is difficult to distinguish which structures are terraces,
and which are buildings, from the GIS photos.
Jeff Harting, GTH Architects, and the project architect. Mr. Harting described
the Subject Property as a large lot improved with a small home, which the
owners do not wish to demolish. He said the existing cabana and patio are
deteriorated and the bluff is eroding; therefore, the owners needed a project
to maintain the bluff, while preserving the views from the neighboring
properties.
There was a discussion about the proposed structure’s proximity to the Lake,
and how it would weather pounding waves. Mr. Harting explained that there will
be steel doors to the studio which have been tested to withstand waves and
weather. In addition, the sea walls will protect the structure from the waves.
Charlie Shabica, Village resident. Mr. Shabica, who identified himself as an
engineer, explained that Lake Michigan has stayed in a six-foot elevation range
for the last 2,000 years, and that the current elevation is not expected to
increase based on that data.
The Council discussed the request further, assessing issues of reasonable
return, and the possibility of setting precedent for other zoning variations
along the lakefront. They also heard a description of the plantings from the
project’s landscape architect.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Marina Birov, 195 Sheridan Road. Ms. Birov said her home is directly north of
the Subject Property, and claimed that she was never notified about the
variation request or the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing. She expressed concern
with the proposed structure, and said she believed the setback variation is too
large.
Mitchell Ruchim, attorney for the Birovs. Mr. Ruchim noted that a suggestion
was made to reduce the variance at the October 17 Council meeting, and he said
that granting such a large variation would set a precedent.
Nancy and Scott Santi, owners of 191 Sheridan Road. Mrs. Santi said the couple
bought the home because they love historical houses and that they want to
preserve the residence and apply for landmark status. Their problem relates to
the bluff erosion, which washes mud into the existing cabana and renders it
unusable. She explained that the proposed outbuilding will be constructed with
stone and brick that matches the home, and that they were careful not to ruin
the sight lines of their neighbors. Mr. Santi said they could alternatively
place a jetty in the water and create a beach and then no setback variation
would be required; however, the Santi’s prefer not to use that tactic.
President Rintz called the matter back to the Council for discussion.
Trustee Swierk said he would not be in favor of the current request as he did
not see a unique circumstance or reasonable return issue; but he would be
agreeable to granting a variance for a revised structure with the existing
front yard setback.
Trustee Cripe expressed support for the variance request, explaining that as a
past member of the Zoning Board of Appeals, he understood zoning variations to
be a tool for achieving the goal of preserving older homes. He noted that this
home is an historic structure which was constructed before enactment of the
Zoning Ordinance, and therefore flexibility is called for.
Trustee Ziv agreed with Trustee Cripe’s comments. She added that she supported
the Zoning Board’s recommendation, and believed the project will shore up the
bluff with an aesthetically pleasing and carefully engineered improvement.
Trustee Dearborn said he was against approving such a large variation request;
however, he expressed admiration for the owners’ efforts at preserving the
home.
Trustee Myers favored granting the variation and noted that most homes on
Sheridan Road have reached maximum lot coverage so precedent won’t be an issue.
He added that a reasonable return is achieved by allowing the home to enjoy
amenities commensurate to other homes on Sheridan Road.
Trustee Lanphier said she was very reluctant to approve the request out of
concern about the structure’s proximity to the Lake, and the possibility that
the action of the waves could prove detrimental to it.
President Rintz asked if there was a motion to introduce the Subject Ordinance.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-8-2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-8-2017: 984 Green Bay Road – Special Use Permit for Dental
Office Within the Commercial Overlay District (Introduction). Mr. Schoon
reviewed this request for a Special Use Permit to allow the expansion of an
existing pediatric dental practice in the C2 Commercial Overlay District. The
Plan Commission voted unanimously to recommend approval of the request.
Dr. Mary Tierney, DDS, said she would like to relocate her practice in order to
expand and continue to serve children in the community, as most of her young
patients are from Winnetka.
After a brief discussion, the Council was in unanimous support of granting the
Special Use Permit.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
8-2017. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. M-9-2017: 725 Elm Street – Special Use Permit for Real Estate
Sales, Rental & Management Office Within the Commercial Overlay District
(Introduction). Mr. Schoon reviewed this application for a Special Use Permit
(SUP) to allow a real estate sales office in the C2 Commercial Overlay
District. The applicant proposes using the space to show various models of
units in the One Winnetka development, and use the space as a management office
once the development is finished. The Plan Commission agreed that it makes
sense to use the space for One Winnetka sales and leasing; however, there was
concern about the long-term use as a management office. The SUP was recommended
for approval, subject to a five-year expiration. The applicant requests that
the SUP stay in effect for as long as the lease on the space is held by One
Winnetka or an affiliate.
Skeet Walker, Vice President of Property Management at Springbank, explained
the preference is to keep the office until One Winnetka no longer owns the
development, as it is typical to show models of the units for sale or rent.
After a brief discussion about the five-year sunset provision, Trustee Myers,
Seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-9-2017.
President Rintz asked the Council if they would like to discuss amending the
Ordinance to eliminate the five-year limit on the SUP.
The Trustees discussed the issue of the sunset provision at length, and the
majority was in favor of keeping it in the Subject Ordinance. Mr. Walker
confirmed for Trustee Lanphier that the office would only be for the sale and
leasing of One Winnetka units, and not for other property leased by Springbank.
Trustee Myers recommended the Council have a discussion about the future of the
Retail Overlay District before removing retail space on a long-term basis.
Trustee Swierk was in favor of the time limit on the basis that if the office
doesn’t generate a lot of foot traffic, the space will revert to retail in five
years. Trustee Lanphier felt the space should be freed up for retail once One
Winnetka construction is completed. Trustee Dearborn said he did not have
strong feelings either way, and the applicant could re-apply for another SUP in
five years.
Trustee Ziv was not in favor of the restriction, and suggested letting the
market determine what would be leased at the space. Trustee Cripe commented
that it was a small and somewhat odd location, and he added that the landlord
should be able to lease the space as they see fit.
President Rintz urged the Council to think about precedent, as there are other
large landowners in the Village, and he asked if any Trustee had a proposal to
amend the Subject Ordinance.
Trustee Ziv moved to amend the ordinance to eliminate the five-year sunset
provision. The motion was seconded by Trustee Cripe. By roll call vote, the
motion failed. Ayes: Trustees Cripe and Ziv. Nays: Trustees Dearborn, Lanphier,
Myers and Swierk. Absent: None.
Next the Council voted on Trustee Myers’ motion to introduce Ordinance No. M-9-
2017 as presented in the agenda materials. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. M-10-2017: 564 Lincoln Avenue – Special Use Permit for Expansion
of Real Estate Office Within the Commercial Overlay District (Introduction).
Mr. Schoon reviewed the third SUP request of the evening, to allow expansion of
existing Coldwell Banker offices in the Galleria building to an area within the
C2 Commercial Overlay District. The applicant received approval for an SUP in
2010 to expand into adjacent office space in the Galleria, but did not complete
the expansion within 18 months, and the SUP expired.
Karen Aronson, Coldwell Banker, explained that her office promotes the Village
of Winnetka. Her staff patronizes local restaurants and recommends local
services to their clients. She noted that her office is the only real estate
office in the Village without street frontage, and they need the visibility.
The Council asked a few questions, and then President Rintz called for public
comment.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak and Landmarks Preservation Commission Chair. Ms.
Holland said allowing an office use at the subject location depresses the
retail shops across the street and also works against the Downtown Master Plan
and a potential Tax Increment Financing district for downtown revitalization.
Josh Keating, owner of Stacked and Folded. Mr. Keating said he supports the SUP
request, as Coldwell Banker brings a lot of business to the area.
Terry Dason, Winnetka-Northfield Chamber of Commerce Director. Ms. Dason spoke
in support of the SUP, as Coldwell Banker supports and promotes the Village,
and serves on the Chamber’s Board of Directors.
The Council discussed the request. Trustees Cripe, Swierk and Ziv were in favor
of granting the SUP, as it would be an expansion of an existing office; and the
request is a small one on the edge of the Overlay District. Trustees Dearborn,
Lanphier and Myers did not agree, as they felt it would bring little foot
traffic to the area, and the wider discussion about the C2 Commercial Overlay
District should take place before this particular exception is granted.
President Rintz explained that he would be the tie-breaking vote in this
instance, and he was conflicted about the request due to the 2010 SUP process
and subsequent abandonment of the permit by Coldwell Banker. Nevertheless, he
said he was inclined to vote in favor, given the general ambivalence about the
Overlay District in the community.
Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-10-
2017. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Swierk, Ziv
and President Rintz. Nays: Trustees Dearborn, Lanphier and Myers. Absent: None.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
None.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:52 p.m.
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
November 9, 2017
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL BUDGET MEETING
November 9, 2017
(Approved: December 5, 2017)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
November 9, 2017, at 6:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 6:05 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers John Swierk, and
Kristin Ziv. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Finance Director Tim Sloth, Assistant
Finance Director Nick Mostardo, Interim Police Chief Marc Hornstein, Public
Safety Analyst Keri Kaup, Director of Community Development David Schoon,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys,
Assistant Director of Water & Electric Giovanni McLean, and one person in the
audience.
Public Comment.
None.
President Rintz announced that the evening’s agenda had been changed to include
a review of the Business District Revitalization Fund.
Budget Review Topic:
Police Department.
Interim Chief Hornstein reviewed the proposed 2018 Department Budget, noting
that it is slightly below the amount budgeted in 2017. Expenditure highlights
for next year include: (i) replacement of three vehicles; (ii) installation of
new software for citation and crash reporting; and (iii) renovation of the
former communications center. He explained that the old communications room
will be expanded and used by the Investigations and Records Departments. He
added that the savings in 2018 from participating in consolidated dispatching
is estimated to be $94,867.00.
The Council briefly discussed the project for electronic citation and crash
reporting, as well as the community’s reaction to the new consolidated dispatch
model. Manager Bahan offered to schedule tours of the consolidated dispatch
center for any interested Council members.
Business District Revitalization Fund.
Mr. Schoon explained that this fund provides for investment in the business
districts, and is managed through the coordination of Public Works, Community
Development, and Village Manager’s Office. After reviewing proposed
streetscape, signage, and maintenance projects for 2018, Mr. Schoon explained
that the Village needs to identify a funding source for streetscape
improvements.
The Council discussed the issue of funding for business district improvement
projects. Trustees Ziv and Dearborn expressed a desire to determine the scope
of the projects, get their cost estimates, and identify funding sources before
moving forward with any projects. Trustee Ziv also voiced opposition to
implementing a Tax Increment Financing district unless all other possibilities
are exhausted.
Trustees Myers and Lanphier explained that the Budget is a planning tool that
sets funds aside for proposed projects based on the priorities set by the
Council. The actual projects will be reviewed and approved by the Council once
the Downtown Master Plan (DMP) Task Force makes its recommendations to the
Council.
President Rintz noted that previous Councils have determined that small and
incremental projects do not provide the desired impact for the business
districts. He explained that a gateway project is a meaningful change to inform
the public about what a large-scale streetscape plan would look like, and he
added that there will be more discussion when the projects come up for bid
approvals.
Trustee Myers said the DMP Task Force will develop a plan for the Council to
discuss and approve before any money gets spent. In tandem with these efforts,
the Council will make the bigger decision on whether to use a TIF for
financing, or to do the projects piecemeal over the years, when funds are
available.
Manager Bahan observed that 2018 is a year where planning is not yet complete,
so a good option is to preserve capacity in the absence of a Budget that
doesn’t line up perfectly with the planning timeline.
Water & Electric Department:
Water Fund. Mr. Keys explained that water consumption is on the decline due to
conservation efforts in the community, putting pressure on the Water Fund. Very
little capital work is planned for 2018, since the Forest Glen water main
project was moved up to 2017. The fund will regroup next year, and get back
into compliance with the fund balance policy.
Mr. Keys explained the Village is implementing last year’s rate study
recommendations by adding a usage fee and a service charge based on water meter
size. He reviewed water rates of other area communities, showing Winnetka’s
charges are on the low end of the spectrum at this time.
Mr. Keys reviewed key 2018 operating expenditures, which include maintenance
programs, consulting services, vehicles/equipment, and others. Capital projects
include the lining for a water main that crosses the railroad right of way at
Willow Road, and replacement of a truck which was purchased in 2003.
Electric Fund. Mr. Keys reviewed the electric sales, coming in slightly under
the forecast, along with operating revenues and expenses. The fund balance
policy is being met, and the rate increase of 2.4% for 2018 is on track with
findings from the 2016 rate study, with an additional 1.6% increase potentially
needed for the Purchase Power Cost Adjustment.
Mr. Keys explained that since the current rate structure is not equitably
balanced across the customer classes, the rate study recommended a “bandwith,”
which is a parameter for moving certain rates incrementally either higher or
lower. This method gradually moves the customer classes into equity, instead of
shocking users with a sudden adjustment.
Mr. Keys said Winnetka’s rates are still very competitive with ComEd. He noted
that if the long-range cost of power forecast is on target, a slight increase
for the Power Cost Adjustment will need to be added next year.
Mr. Keys reviewed the operating expenditures and proposed capital projects for
2018, which includes a project to overhaul three steam turbines over the next
four years. He explained that the benefits to continuing to run the electric
utility continue outweigh the costs; the utility currently contributes a net of
$654,824 to the General Fund.
President Rintz noted that the electric utility reduces the tax levy, and is a
critical piece of the Village’s financial stability. Trustee Lanphier added
that it also results in extremely reliable electric service.
Trustee Myers suggested that some modeling be done about the impact of a
potential property tax freeze being considered by the State Legislature, along
with projections of the impact on property values if the property tax deduction
is eliminated on Federal tax returns.
Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:31 p.m.
