   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
8) Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a) Ordinance No. M-1-2018: 800 Tower Road - Zoning Variations
(Introduction).
   Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for zoning variations to allow the
construction of a second-story addition and detached garage on the Subject
Property. Four variations are being requested from the following provisions of
the Zoning Ordinance: (i) Roofed Lot Coverage; (ii) Front Yard Lot Coverage;
(iii) Front Yard Setback; and (iv) Garages.
   After Mr. Schoon reviewed the proposed construction, he explained that the
original application was amended to address concerns addressed at the September
11, 2017 Zoning Board of Appeals (ZBA) meeting. The amended request was
discussed at the December 11, 2017 ZBA meeting, and it was recommended for
approval, conditioned upon a modification to the chimney. The recommended
change has been made, and is included in the agenda materials.
   During the Council question period, Mr. Schoon confirmed that the
construction will be required to conform to Village requirements for grading,
to prevent stormwater runoff problems for adjacent neighbors. He also explained
that the impermeable surface area will not exceed zoning limitations, and that
no neighbors have raised objections to the proposed construction.
   Hristo Hristov, the applicant, explained that he is trying to improve the
home’s live-ability and he thanked the Council for its consideration.
   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-1-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-1-
2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b) Ordinance No. M-2-2018: 700 Elm Street - Special Use Permit and Zoning
Variations (Introduction/Adoption).
   Mr. Schoon explained that the request for a Special Use Permit and zoning
variations is being made by the Hadley Institute for a proposed expansion of
its facilities. He described the renovations, and noted that the Zoning Board
of Appeals, Plan commission and Design Review Board had all recommended
approval of the requests.
   There being no questions from the Council, Julie Tye, President of Hadley
Institute, explained that the project will update the building’s mechanical
systems and reconfigure the building to be compliant with the Americans with
Disabilities Act, as well as provide technology upgrades that 21 st Century
learners require.
   The Council discussed impacts the One Winnetka construction might have on
the Institute, and Manager Bahan assured them that the Village will work with
all the parties to ensure the construction goes as smoothly as possible.
   Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-2-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-2-
2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c) Ordinance M-3-2018: 1075 Gage Street - C-2 Overlay District Special Use
Permit (Introduction).
   Mr. Schoon described the request, and explained that the request is being
made for the relocation and expansion of an existing orthodontist practice into
the Commercial Overlay District. The Plan Commission recommended approval of
the request, as it was felt that the office would bring people to the Hubbard
Woods business district and increase traffic to local restaurants and shops.
   The applicant, Alex Figueroa, said leasing problems at the current office
forced his practice to look at new locations, and that he has existing
practices in Naperville and downtown Chicago. He explained that the presence of
his practice at this location would bolster other retail uses in the area.
   Several Trustees expressed concern about granting this particular exception,
as it does not fit into the current retail overlay model, and the Council has
yet to hold comprehensive discussions about the future of the Commercial
Overlay District. Others felt the use would increase pedestrian traffic to the
area.
   Louise Holland, 545 Oak Street and Plan Commission member. Ms. Holland said
she voted against the request at the Plan Commission meeting, because the space
is too large; parking for park users and area retail will be diminished; and
continual encroachment of Special Uses in the overlay district is at odds with
the Downtown Master Plan (DMP). She added that the Indian Hill business
district is a better location for this office use.
   Trustee Dearborn expressed understanding for Ms. Holland’s viewpoint, and he
suggested accelerating the timeframe for Council discussions on the Retail
Overlay District.
   Trustee Myers said he struggles with the request because it is right in the
middle of the Overlay District, and it could be several years before any
appreciable increase in foot traffic would result from granting the request. He
felt if the Council is making policy decisions that put the Village
incrementally on a path towards reducing the Overlay District’s effectiveness,
the policy discussion must come as soon as possible.
   Trustee Lanphier supported keeping the practice in Winnetka, but had
reservations about approving this request prior to hearing results of the
Downtown Master Plan Task Force examination of the Overlay District.
   Trustee Ziv explained that at the Plan Commission hearing on this request,
the leasing agent for the location said great effort was made to find a retail
tenant for the space, but none were interested.
   Trustee Cripe noted that the Overlay District is not helping to fill vacant
spaces in the Hubbard Woods business district, which has a 17% vacancy rate. He
explained that Green Bay Road does not help the situation, as it is slices
through the heart of the district and presents a challenge to pedestrians. He
agreed that the Council needs to have a thorough discussion about the Overlay
Districts, but he couldn’t see a benefit in denying this application because
that discussion has not yet taken place. He commended the landlord for making a
sustained effort to lease the property to a retailer, and posited that the
dental office would be better than another empty storefront. Finally, he
pointed out that medical devices are subject to sales tax, which creates
another point in favor of granting the request.
   Trustee Swierk was in favor of approving the request, as the office could be
vacant for a long time before a retail tenant steps forward.
   President Rintz explained that the Overlay District would come to the
Council soon and hopefully after the DMP Task Force makes its report. In the
meantime, the Village has no control of market forces or when a potential user
will apply for a Special Use Permit, and the Council must deal with them as
they come up.
   Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
3-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Swierk,
Dearborn, Ziv. Nays: Trustees Lanphier and Myers. Absent: None.
9) Old Business.
None.
10) New Business.
None.
11) Appointments:
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to appoint Margaret Lamason
to the ad hoc Downtown Master Plan Task Force. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
12) Closed Session.
None.
13) Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:03 p.m.
                                                 
Deputy Clerk
January 16, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
January 16, 2018
(Approved: February 6, 2018)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
January 16, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
1) Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Myers called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present:
Trustees Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, John Swierk, and
Kristin Ziv. Absent: President Chris Rintz. Also present: Village Manager
Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter M.
Friedman, Assistant Public Works Director Jim Bernahl, Director of Water &
Electric Brian Keys, Director of Community Development David Schoon, and
approximately 5 persons in the audience.
2) Pledge of Allegiance.
President Pro Tem Myers led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3) Quorum.
   a) February 6, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   All of the Council members present said they expect to attend.
   b) February 13, 2018 Study Session.
   All of the Council members present said they expect to attend.
   c) February 20, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   All of the Council members present said they expect to attend.
4) Public Comment.
Brian Schmisek, 932 Ash Street. Mr. Schmisek advised the Council to invest more
money in the Village’s utility lines to move them underground for improved
reliability and aesthetics. Mr. Keys explained that the Village owns the public
rights of way jointly with AT&T. Undergrounding utilities is a considerable
expense, and would be even more expensive in Winnetka, since AT&T requires
payment for the Village to put infrastructure underground. Currently, he said
all new lines do get put underground. President Pro Tem Myers added that that
Downtown Master Plan calls for overhead lines in the downtown areas to be put
underground as those areas are improved with streetscape.
5) Reports:
   a) Trustees.
      i) Trustee Ziv reported on a League of Women Voters presentation on Tax
Increment Financing.
      ii) Trustee Dearborn reported on the latest Landmark Presentation
Commission meeting.
      iii) Trustee Lanphier reported on the most recent Community Conversations
event.
   b) Attorney.
   None.
   c) Manager.
   None.
   d) Village President.
   President Pro Tem Myers reported on the recent Downtown Master Plan Task
Force meeting.
6) Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to approve the Agenda. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
7) Consent Agenda
   a) Village Council Minutes.
      i) December 19, 2017 Regular Meeting.
      ii) January 2, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   b) Approval of Warrant List dated December 29, 2017 – January 11, 2018 in
the amount of $1,480,474.14.
   c) Resolution No. R-4-2018: Approval and Release of Closed Session Minutes
(Adoption).
   d) A Resolution approving minutes of Closed Sessions, determining which
minutes still require confidential treatment, and authorizing destruction of
audio recordings of Closed Sessions held before July 16, 2016. Resolution No.
R-5-2018: Extending Tree Trimming, Maintenance, and Removal Contracts
(Adoption). A one-year extension of contracts for (i) parkway tree/stump
removal and parkway tree trimming to Advanced Tree Care Company; and (ii)
emergency time and material work to Nels Johnson Tree Experts.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv.
Nays: None. Absent: President Rintz.
8) Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a) Resolution No. R-6-2018: 2017 Street Rehabilitation and Public
Improvement Program; Change Order No. 3 (Adoption).
   Mr. Bernahl reviewed previous change orders to ALamp for the 2017 Street
Rehabilitation Project, and then explained that a third change is needed for
additional work that was required for the Cherry Street rehabilitation project.
He noted that a detailed list was included in the agenda materials.
   Trustee Cripe commended the Village for its handling of the Cherry Street
project, which had many unforeseen issues.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-6-
2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: President Rintz.
   b) Ordinance No. M-3-2018: 1075 Gage Street - C-2 Overlay District Special
Use Permit (Adoption).
   Mr. Schoon said there was nothing new to report since the Ordinance was
introduced at the last regular Council meeting.
   Trustee Lanphier asked the applicant what he plans for the street frontage
windows to retain visual interest. The applicant, Alex Figueroa, said the plans
are under review to determine what can be done to add interest to the windows
area, including putting the waiting area in the front window locations, and
having shades that only cover half the windows.
   President Pro Tem Myers noted that this application is a test-case for
future uses that might be allowed in the Retail Overlay, and he urged the
applicant to be mindful of the issues.
   A resident on Whitebridge Hill Road commented that children coming and going
to the office would bring vibrancy to the area.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
3-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: President Rintz.
   c) Resolution No. R-7-2018: Resolution Rejecting Bids, Waiving Bidding, and
Awarding A Contract For The Purchase Of Primary Cable (Adoption).
   Mr. Keys explained that two companies, Prysmian Group and The Okonite
Company, responded to the Village’s request for bids for primary cable. Both
bids were deemed non-responsive, as both companies elected not to accept
fundamental terms and conditions of the contract. Since Prysmian Group was not
interested in working with the Village on the bid issues, he recommended
rejecting both of the bids, waiving the bid requirement, and awarding a
contract to The Okonite Company based on unit prices in its bid.
   After Mr. Keys answered several questions from the Council, Attorney
Schuster explained Prysmian Group was not interested in any kind of contract
negotiation with the Village – and they were also not willing to hold their
pricing firm for the year. He added that traditional bidding might not be the
most effective way to get the best results for cable purchases, since there are
so few players in the cable marketplace, and strict bidding rules prevent the
Village from communicating with vendors before a bid is submitted. Instead, a
Request for Proposals process would allow more one-on-one negotiation with
vendors.
   After a brief discussion, Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to
adopt Resolution No. R-7-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent:
President Rintz.
9) Old Business.
None.
10) New Business.
None.
11) Appointments:
None.
12) Closed Session.
None.
13) Adjournment.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 7:46 p.m.
      
