REGULAR MEETING
February 20, 2018
(Approved: March 6, 2018)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
February 20, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
1) Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and Kristin Ziv. Absent: Trustees
Robert Dearborn and Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter M.
Friedman, Community Development Director David Schoon, Assistant Public Works
Director Jim Bernahl, and approximately 4 persons in the audience.
2) Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Myers led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3) Quorum.
   a) March 6, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   All of the Council members present, except Trustee Myers, said they expect
to attend.
   b) March 13, 2018 Study Session.
   All of the Council members present said they expect to attend.
   c) March 22, 2018 Rescheduled Regular Meeting.
   All of the Council members present, except Trustees Cripe and Myers, said
they expect to attend.
4) Public Comment.
Gabriel Hostalet from KJI Landscaping. Mr. Hostalet inquired as to why the 2018
floral basket program was not awarded to his company, since it was the lower
bidder, and in light of the fact that his company was the first vendor for the
floral program. Manager Bahan explained that Public Works Director Steve
Saunders had previously spoken to Mr. Hostalet and explained to him that his
original contract was not renewed because of a performance issue. The 2018
floral program will be completed by the current vendor, with a one-year
contract extension. The Village will bid out the floral basket program in 2019,
and Manager Bahan encouraged Mr. Hostalet to bid on the project at that time.
5) Reports:
   a) Trustees.
   None.
   b) Attorney.
   None.
   c) Manager.
   None.
      i) Village President. President Rintz extended his appreciation to the
Public Works staff, who put in many hours to keep the streets and sidewalks
clear and safe during the recent snow event. Next, he reported on a recent
meeting with the Village President of Wilmette, which was scheduled in order to
open a line of communication to facilitate working on items of mutual benefit
in the future.
6) Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Ziv requested that item 7(e) be removed from the Consent Agenda for
discussion under New Business. Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved
to approve the final form of the Agenda. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7) Consent Agenda
   a) Village Council Minutes.
      i) February 6, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   b) Approval of Warrant List dated February 2 – 15, 2018 in the amount of
$316,814.35.
   c) Resolution No. R-12-2018: Approving One-Year Renewal of the Contract with
Asplundh Tree Expert Company for Utility Line Clearance (Adoption).
   d) Resolution No. R-13-2018: Eighth Extension of Landscape Waste Hauling
Contract (Adoption).
   e) Resolution No. R-14-2018: Purchase of Fleet Vehicle for Engineering
Division (Adoption). This item was removed from the Consent Agenda at the
request of Trustee Ziv and discussed under New Business.
   f) Resolution No. R-16-2018: Approving In-Lieu Opt-Out Payments as
Reportable Earnings Under the Illinois Municipal Retirement Fund (IMRF) Pension
(Adoption). Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the
foregoing items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Dearborn and Lanphier.
8) Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a) Ordinance No. M-4-2018: 67 Brier Street- Preliminary Plat Approval
Gilchrist Subdivision and Variations (Introduction).
   Mr. Schoon explained that based on Council direction at the previous Regular
Council meeting, an approval Ordinance has been prepared for the Subject
Property. There were no questions from the Council or audience.
   Trustee Swierk stated that he felt the variations were too large and too
numerous, and he did not support the request. The other Trustees reiterated
their support of the request in the interest of keeping modest homes in
Winnetka and alleviating the property tax burden the larger lot would bring.
   Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
4-2018. By voice vote, the motion carried.
   b) Ordinance No. M-5-2018: 860 Lamson Drive- Preliminary Plat Approval
Sandra K. Crown Subdivision and Variations (Introduction).
   Mr. Schoon explained that this item had also been prepared for consideration
based on Council direction at the prior meeting. Mr. Christopher Canning noted
he was here representing the applicant, but that he had nothing new to present.
There were no questions from the Council or audience.
   Trustee Swierk asked if there had been any discussion about not granting the
variation for gross floor area, as he did not see a reason to grant that
particular variation request. President Rintz explained that there had been a
lengthy conversation about this request, and once again the preservation of
existing housing stock and alleviation of property tax burdens were prevailing
reasons the Council directed an approval ordinance be drafted.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
5-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Myers,
Ziv, and President Rintz. Nays: Trustee Swierk. Absent: Trustees Dearborn and
Lanphier.
   c) Resolution No. R-15-2018: Boal Parkway Pump Station Project Engineering
Change Order No. 1 (Adoption).
   Mr. Bernahl reviewed the Village’s contract with HR Green as well as the
work done to-date. An alternative location for the pump station was determined
to be in the best interests of the surrounding area, and this work added to the
cost of the engineering contract with HR Green. He noted that the new location
will result in an overall decrease in the total project cost.
   After Mr. Bernahl explained how the project cost would be reduced with the
new pump station design, Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to
adopt Resolution No. R-15-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Cripe, Myers, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Dearborn
and Lanphier.
9) Old Business.
None.
10) New Business.
   a) Resolution No. R-14-2018: Purchase of Fleet Vehicle for Engineering
Division (Adoption).
   Mr. Bernahl provided background on the condition of the current vehicle,
including existing maintenance issues. He explained that staff proposes to
purchase a replacement vehicle through the Suburban Purchasing Cooperative to
get the best pricing. Trustee Ziv opined that 12 years was not a very old
vehicle, and that the Village’s in-house mechanics can do repairs on it so why
would it be replaced. Mr. Bernahl said parts are getting difficult to come by,
and that 10 years is the standard replacement timeframe for fleet vehicles so
the Village will not be without the vehicle in the event it breaks down and
cannot be repaired.
   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
14-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Myers,
Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Dearborn and Lanphier.
11) Appointments:
None.
12) Closed Session.
None.
13) Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adjourn the meeting. By voice
vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 7:27 p.m.
      
