MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
March 6, 2018
(Approved: March 19, 2018)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
6, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
1) Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, John Swierk, and Kristin Ziv.
Absent: Trustee Scott Myers. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter M. Friedman,
Community Development Director David Schoon, and approximately five persons in
the audience.
2) Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Dearborn led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3) Quorum.
   a) March 13, 2018 Study Session.
   All of the Council members present said they expect to attend.
   b) March 22, 2018 Rescheduled Regular Meeting.
   All of the Council members present except Trustees Cripe and Ziv said they
expect to attend.
   c) April 3, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   a) All of the Council members present said they expect to attend.
4) Public Comment.
Pat Balsamo 1037 Cherry. Ms. Balsamo asked about the status of the One Winnetka
Planned Development final application.
Mr. Schoon explained that the developer, David Trandel, has been working with
staff on the submittal of required documents. Mr. Trandel expects to have the
final application ready in the near future.
President Rintz added that there have been several meetings between the
developer and staff, and the Village has made clear that a complete package
must be submitted so that an efficient process can ensue.
John Thomas, 525 Ash. Mr. Thomas commented on the upcoming discussion about the
Plan Commission membership.
5) Reports:
   a) Trustees.
      i) Trustee Lanphier announced that Trustee Swierk would join her at the
next Community Conversations.
      ii) Trustee Cripe gave an update on recent stormwater meetings with the
boards of New Trier High School and the Park District.
   b) Attorney.
   None.
   C) Manager.
   None.
   d) Village President.
   President Rintz reported on a recent meeting he had with Congressperson Jan
Schakowsky, where potential grant funds to assist the Village with the
stormwater project on forest preserve land was discussed.
6) Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
7) Consent Agenda
   a) Village Council Minutes.
      i) February 13, 2018 Study Session.
      ii) February 20, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   b) Approval of Warrant List dated February 16 – March 1, 2018 in the amount
of $381,255.29.
   c) Resolution No. R-17-2018: Purchase of Hook Lift Truck Replacement
(Adoption).
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Myers.
8) Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a) Ordinance No. M-4-2018: 67 Brier Street – Preliminary Plat Approval
Gilchrist Subdivision and Variations (Adoption).
   Mr. Schoon explained that he had no new information to present on the
matter, which has been discussed at two prior meetings.
   There being no comments or questions from the Council or audience, Trustee
Cripe, seconded by Trustee Ziv, moved to adopt Ordinance M-4-2018. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Ziv.
Nays: Trustee Swierk. Absent: Trustee Myers.
   b) Ordinance No. MC-1-2018: Plan Commission Composition (Introduction).
   President Rintz explained that the idea of streamlining the membership of
the Plan Commission by removing representatives of the Library, District 36,
and Park District, occurred to him at the February Study Session, during a
discussion about updating Winnetka’s Comprehensive Plan. When he asked the
Village Attorney if a 13-member Plan Commission is unusual, the response was an
unqualified yes. A review of area municipalities revealed that all of
Winnetka’s neighbors have either seven or nine members on their Plan
Commissions. President Rintz opined that Winnetka’s large Plan Commission
membership could lead to inefficiencies just by its sheer size, despite the
dedicated efforts of the group. He noted that the Plan Commission’s main duty
under the Village Code is the development and periodic updating of the
Village’s Comprehensive Plan, along with assisting the Village in ensuring the
goals of the Plan are met.
   Trustee Lanphier said she was reluctant to remove that channel of
communication from the other taxing agencies.
   John Thomas, 525 Ash. Mr. Thomas commented that the brevity of discussions
depends largely on the Chair of the Commission, and that some are more
effective than others.
   Pat Balsamo, 1037 Cherry. Ms. Balsamo questioned whether the Plan
Commissioners realize what their mission is as expressed by the Village Code,
and recommended thorough vetting of members before they are appointed.
   Trustee Ziv noted that a streamlined Planned Development approval process
would be helpful in keeping the Plan Commission meetings to a minimum. She was
against removing the three members of the Commission who are elected officials,
as she felt that more intergovernmental cooperation is needed in the Village,
not less. She also recommended additional Council discussion before the
drafting of a new Comprehensive Plan is scheduled.
   Trustee Swierk said most of the Plan Commissions he comes into contact with
have seven members, and agreed that Winnetka could reduce its Plan Commission
membership. He added that representatives of the park district, library and
schools are free to attend meetings and voice their opinions at any time.
   Trustee Cripe said he liked the proposal and that he would make the group
even smaller, citing the “Rule of Seven” in the study of group dynamics as
evidence that a smaller group gets more accomplished. He pointed out that
providing intergovernmental cooperation is not the stated duty of the Plan
Commission, and recommended that coordination of that effort take place
elsewhere. He agreed that streamlining the Planned Development approval process
is needed.
   Trustee Dearborn agreed that more efficiency in the Plan Commission’s
processes would be helpful. He agreed with Trustee Ziv that the Council needs
to have more discussion about the drafting of a new Comprehensive Plan and what
that would entail. He said the Council needs to clearly frame the mission of
the Plan Commission, and possibly remove any conflict that results from the
intergovernmental membership. He asked Attorney Friedman if it is appropriate
for the Park District, Library District and District 36 to have liaisons on the
Plan Commission
   Attorney Friedman explained that the question is difficult to answer, as
there are no examples of similar situations in nearby communities. He
speculated that the idea of intergovernmental membership may have originated in
an obsolete Illinois Statute, and that over the years most towns have dropped
the requirement. He noted that the Village Code is silent on the question of
whether the government liaisons are on the Commission representing the interest
of their boards, which would seem to create some conflict.
   After a short conversation, the majority of the Trustees, with the exception
of Trustee Ziv, were in favor of introducing the Subject Ordinance,
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce Ordinance
MC-1-2018. By voice vote, the motion carried.
9) Old Business.
None.
10) New Business.
None.
11) Appointments.
None.
12) Closed Session.
Trustee Dearborn moved to adjourn into Closed Session to discuss Personnel
Matters and Pending or Probable Litigation, pursuant to Sections 2c(1) and 2c
(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Swierk seconded the motion. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier,
Swierk, and Ziv. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Myers.
President Rintz announced that the Council would not return to the open meeting
after Executive Session. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 8:35
p.m.
13) Adjournment.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:24 p.m.
      
    Deputy Clerk
March 13, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
13, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
1) Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Kristin Ziv. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Community Development Director David Schoon,
Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus, and approximately 4
persons in the audience.
2) Public Comment.
Ted Wynnychenko, Village Resident. Mr. Wynnychenko advocated for a change in
the Village’s electric rate structure to allow net metering for residents who
have installed solar panels. He opined that more residents would use solar if
they received a retail credit, rather than a wholesale credit, on the excess
electricity they generate.
Trustee Cripe said he was in favor of discussing the issue of net metering
again; Trustee Lanphier agreed. President Rintz said there could be potential
to find time to address this issue again.
Pat Balsamo, League of Women Voters. Ms. Balsamo, on behalf of a former Village
President, suggested considering regulations for Airbnb arrangements. She said
the League of Women Voters supports a healthy business climate, and it is
gratifying to see the Council addressing the Retail Overlay.
3) C-2 Commercial Overlay District.
President Rintz said the issue before the Trustees is whether to work on the
Overlay as a Council, or relegate it to another body for its study and
recommendations.
Mr. Schoon thanked Mr. Norkus for his work on the agenda report, and for doing
most of the research that went into it. Then Messrs. Schoon and Norkus gave a
thorough presentation providing background information on the Commercial
Overlay District that included:
   ∙    Zoning Overview & Commercial Districts
   ∙    Overlay District Origins & Modifications
   ∙    Overlay District Current Allowed Land Uses
   ∙    Current Ground Floor Business Use Mix
   ∙    Overlay District Special Uses
   ∙    Comparable Communities’ Efforts to Promote Ground Floor Retail
   ∙    Comparison of Uses in Relation to Neighboring Communities
   ∙    National Practices to Promote Ground Floor Retail
After the presentation, Mr. Schoon explained that Staff is looking for
direction from the Council: specifically:
   ∙    Does the Council need additional information and analysis? If yes:
      *   What information would be helpful?
      *   Should the matter be referred to the Plan Commission, DMP Task Force
or other advisory body for further evaluation and recommendations?
   ∙    If no additional information is desired, is the Council prepared to
direct staff to draft any Code amendments?
President Rintz thanked Messrs. Schoon and Norkus for a thoughtful, organized,
and readable agenda report and presentation. Next, he called for Council
questions.
Responding to a question about what the original goal of promoting retail had
been, Mr. Schoon said the concern had been raised about other uses impacting
the shopping experience in Winnetka. Mr. Norkus added that providing amenities
that generate foot traffic was also a goal, so residents could shop and find
personal services locally. He said sales tax revenue was not the sole goal.
Responding to a point raised about criteria for granting a Special Use Permit
being subjective, Mr. Schoon explained that the general standards have a degree
of subjectivity, and it is generally the applicant’s responsibility to prove an
application meets the standards. Attorney Schuster cautioned against thinking
of subjective vs. objective standards, and recommended operating on the premise
that special uses are wanted and welcomed, but that the Village does not want a
proliferation in any one location that could have an adverse effect.
Responding to a question about how many special uses are too many, Attorney
Schuster said negative impacts can be minimized by taking each application
case-by-case.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Mike Noonan, attorney for the Dyson family, owners of 844 Spruce Street. Mr.
Noonan asked for 844 Spruce Street to be removed from the Overlay District so
the Dyson family can move its business offices to the site. President Rintz
explained that applying for a Special Use Permit would be the fastest way to
get into the space, as the Council will take some time deciding whether and how
much to modify the Overlay District.
Terry Dason, Director of the Chamber of Commerce. Ms. Dason spoke in favor of
the Overlay District as a way to retain vibrancy and foot traffic in the
downtowns. She expressed concern that without the Overlay, banks and real
estate offices will proliferate. She also said the many nail salons in the
commercial districts do not create foot traffic.
Next, the Council discussed their options at length. It was generally agreed
that the Overlay District is not trying to address the vacancy rate in the
business districts, but rather aims to create a vibrant retail cluster with
engaging community spaces. There was a suggestion to modify the list of uses
permitted by right, and possibly change the parameters of the Overlay District
to a percentage-based system.
There was a discussion about the purview of examining the Overlay District, and
the consensus was reached that the Council would deal with the issue, possibly
by adding extra meetings to its schedule. Mr. Schoon was directed to get input
from the Plan Commission at its next meeting, as to whether it would recommend
any modifications in the permitted uses, and then give the feedback to the
Council for its future deliberations.
4) Closed Session.
None.
5) Adjournment.
Trustee Ziv, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By voice
vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:49 p.m.
 
____________________________
Deputy Clerk
March 19, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Monday, March
19, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
1) Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, and John Swierk.
Absent: Trustee Kristin Ziv. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Ben Schuster,
Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus, Public Works Director
Steve Saunders, and approximately 5 persons in the audience.
2) Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Cripe led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3) Quorum.
   a) April 3, 2018 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   b) April 10, 2018 Study Session. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   c) April 17, 2018 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present except
President Rintz said they expect to attend.
4) Public Comment.
George Gilmore, 631 Blackthorn. Mr. Gilmore expressed concern about
neighborhood impacts of a construction project taking place next door to his
home, citing safety concerns about unsafe conditions and debris piles.
Manager Bahan said Village staff will take enforcement actions immediately on
Tuesday, and make an appointment to speak with the builder.
Trustee Myers expressed concern about the practices of this particular builder
in other situations, and several Trustees agreed that they hear many complaints
about this builder.
5) Reports:
   a) Trustees. None.
   b) Attorney. None.
   c) Manager. Manager Bahan said the Village is keeping an eye on lead water
service legislation taking shape in Springfield, which would adversely impact
the Village’s budget.
   d) Village President. None.
6) Approval of the Agenda.
President Rintz noted that Item 7(c) was removed from the Consent Agenda for
discussion under New Business. Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved
to approve the Agenda. By voice vote, the motion carried.
7) Consent Agenda
   a) Village Council Minutes.
      i) March 6, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   b) Approval of Warrant List dated March 2-15, 2018 in the amount of
$845,401.18.
   c) Resolution No. R-18-2018: Purchase of Two Refuse Trucks (Adoption). This
item was removed from the Consent Agenda and discussed under New Business.
   d) Resolution No. R-19-2018: Purchase of Police Patrol Vehicle (Adoption).
   e) Resolution No. R-20-2018: Approving an Easement Agreement for the
Operation
and Maintenance of a Water Main (Adoption).
   f) Resolution No. R-21-2018: 2018 Street Rehabilitation and Public
Improvement
Project (Adoption).
   g) Annual Outdoor Seating Permits.
   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda, with the exception of Item 7(c), by omnibus vote.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ziv.
8) Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a) Ordinance No. MC-1-2018: Plan Commission Composition (Adoption).
President Rintz noted that letters received from the Boards of the Park
District and Library have been distributed to the Council. He explained that
bringing the Plan Commission structure into line with other area municipalities
was simply an effort to modernize and streamline the Plan Commission’s
processes. Then he called for public comment.
   John Thomas, Winnetka resident and Park Board Commissioner. Mr. Thomas said
the Park Board does not feel that a 13-member Plan Commission is unwieldy, and
he added that the elected officials on the Plan Commission recuse themselves
from voting on matters that affect their particular agency. He also disputed
that the “rule of seven” deals with the number of members on a committee.
   The Council discussed the measure, and it was noted that most of the issues
addressed by the Plan Commission do not impact the other taxing bodies;
therefore, their expertise is rarely necessary. Their input would, however, be
sought when a new Comprehensive Plan is drafted. It was also felt that a
potential conflict of interest could be posed by other agencies having
representation on the Commission; but that possibly their voices could be heard
as non-voting or ex-officio members. Ultimately, the efficacy of a smaller body
was agreed upon.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-
1-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ziv.
   b) Ordinance No. M-6-2018: 615 Cedar Street – Variations (Introduction). Mr.
Norkus reviewed this request for zoning variations to allow the construction of
an addition to the home on the Subject Property. He descried the variations
from Gross Floor Area and Side Yard Setback provisions which are being sought.
He explained that two neighbors have expressed opposition to the project, and
one adjacent neighbor spoke in support of the proposal. The Zoning Board of
Appeals (ZBA) discussed modifying the request, and also acknowledged that an
unfinished attic space counts to gross floor area even though it is unusable.
The ZBA ultimately voted to recommend approval of the request.
   Chip Hackley, the project architect, noted that the existing attached garage
could be used for the addition if a legal detached garage is built, but doing
so would reduce the yard and remove a large oak tree. He explained that the
applicant felt the current project is a better option.
   President Rintz asked why a previous application in 2014 didn’t go through.
Corinne Evans, the home’s owner, explained that she and her husband decided to
think long and hard about doing a remodeling project. Her husband, Andrew,
added that upon reflection, they chose a more modest plan, in the interest of
preserving the home.
   Linda Pedian, 595 Cedar Street. Ms. Pedian said this portion of Cedar Street
is made up of odd lots, and zoning constraints make preservation efforts
difficult. She urged the Council to grant the request.
   After a brief discussion, the Council agreed to grant the request, and to
waive introduction.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-6-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ziv.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
6-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ziv.
9) Old Business.
None.
10) New Business.
   a) Metropolitan Mayors Caucus Greenest Region Compact 2. Liz Kunkle,
Environmental & Forestry (EFC) Commissioner, gave the Council background
information on the Metropolitan Mayors Caucus (MMC), and explained that the
original Greenest Region Compact (GRC) endeavored to provide sustainable goals
for communities and regions. The Village of Winnetka adopted the first GRC; and
the GRC2 is an update which the EFC recommends the Village also adopt. The
Village can pick and choose which projects to implement, as well as their
timing. Adoption of the GRC2 would also give the Village access to grant
funding for its environmental initiatives. She recommended the Council adopt
the initiative and announce the measure on Earth Day, April 22.
   The Council discussed the measure and asked about next steps if the Village
adopts the GRC2. Chuck Dowding, EFC Chair, said the commission could
investigate which things in the compact Winnetka is already doing and report
that to the MMC to demonstrate progress.
   During public comment, Ms. Pedian recommended the Village adopt the GRC2.
   The Council was in favor of adopting the GRC2.
   President Rintz suggested the EFC come back to the Council in the fall and
present its initial implementation recommendations to the Council.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to direct the Village
Attorney to draft a Resolution adopting the Greenest Region Compact 2. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers,
and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ziv.
   b) Resolution No. R-18-2018: Purchase of Two Refuse Trucks (Adoption). Mr.
Saunders reviewed the Public Works Department truck replacement program, noting
that the Council funded the purchase of two refuse trucks during the 2018
Budget process. He explained that a previous Council deferred vehicle
replacement, as it wanted to do a refuse collection study; however, at this
time the Refuse Program needs the replacement trucks to continue providing the
desired level of service.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt Resolution No.
R-18-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Ziv.
11) Appointments.
None.
12) Closed Session.
None.
13) Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:05 p.m.
 
