    Deputy Clerk
May 8, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
(Approved: June 5, 2018)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, May
8, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
1) Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Finance
Director Tim Sloth, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and approximately 5
persons in the audience.
2) Public Comment.
None.
3) Refuse Collection Survey Results.
President Rintz outlined the key points for the evening’s discussion:
   ∙    Does the Council prefer to fund refuse collection through fees or with
property taxes?
   ∙    Would the Council like Staff to perform a cost study for a curbside
pickup program?
   ∙    Does the Council wish to consider implementing a composting program?
Mr. Saunders explained that the Council has discussed refuse funding during the
last few budget cycles, and set a goal to survey the community in 2018 to gauge
attitudes on point-of-service options and preferences for funding (primarily
through property taxes or with user fees). He gave an overview of the survey
results, which garnered a 25% response rate. The results revealed that the
community is nearly evenly divided on the question of property tax vs. fee to
pay for the service. A slight inclination was shown for curbside collection;
however, a large minority was very opposed to the idea of eliminating back door
service. The community was generally uninterested in a composting program, with
58% responding that they would be somewhat or very unlikely to subscribe;
however, the Village may be able to assist interested residents with
identifying a private composting service.
Mr. Saunders noted that customer satisfaction with refuse collection was very
high, with 97.2% of respondents rating the service good or excellent, and he
added that refuse crews deserve public recognition for their hard work in this
area.
The Council asked questions relating to cost estimates for back door vs.
curbside service, and whether there is a Village Code provision mandating back
door pickups. Mr. Saunders explained that cost estimates would need to be
further refined and evaluated before the full impact of changing the service
options could be known, and he added that there is no Code provision that
mandates back door refuse pickup. He noted that respondents answered nearly all
questions on the survey.
Attorney Schuster explained that while there are no legal obstacles to
requiring curbside collection, the Americans with Disabilities Act mandates
that a waiver system be in place for individuals with disabilities. He noted
that not everyone who applies may qualify for a waiver; however, virtually
every community has a waiver system.
Next, President Rintz called for audience comment.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo asked how people with alley refuse
pickup would be dealt with in a curbside scenario; Mr. Saunders explained that
alleys would be treated as the curbside for residents with alley pickup.
Jeffrey Liss, Winnetka resident. Mr. Liss asked if the Council’s goal in
considering a service fee model is to cut costs or raise revenue; and whether
staff would be cut if the service is moved to the curbside. He opined it is
more convenient to have back door collection; that residents feel the amenity
is a perk of living in Winnetka; and that it may be difficult for elderly
residents to put their refuse bins at the curb.
President Rintz explained the goal in contemplating a fee for service model is
equity in charging for refuse collection, since those with higher property
taxes pay a bigger percentage of the overall cost for the same service. He
noted that there is a demographic divide between older residents and younger
ones on the issue of back door vs. curbside service, which will make discussion
of the issue difficult.
Mr. Saunders said calculations about staffing impacts can’t be done until the
Village determines which levels of service to offer.
Chuck Dowding, 968 Elm and Chair of the Environmental & Forestry Commission
(EFC). Mr. Dowding urged the Council to consider information from a 2016 EFC
study about increasing recycling to save costs on refuse collection. He noted
that the Greenest Region Compact 2, which the Council recently adopted,
promotes reducing waste by increasing recycling, and it calls for those who
throw away more to pay more. He explained that the Council has an opportunity
to demonstrate that doing the right thing is also financially advantageous. He
recalled that when the Winnetka landfill was closed 25 years ago, there was
resistance to implementing a recycling program, but that over the years,
community recycling has greatly improved.
Kiz Kunkle, 1303 Holly Lane and EFC commissioner. Ms. Kunkle explained that
promoting the importance of keeping refuse out of landfills has shifted popular
opinion fairly quickly in the past few years. She said food scraps are a large
subset of waste creation that needs to be addressed, and she encouraged the
Council to put Winnetka at the forefront of the composting movement. She noted
that the EFC will study recycling and composting as part of the Village’s
participation in the Greenest Region Compact 2, which guides member communities
to educate the public and increase awareness of the environmental consequences
of its actions. She recommended in lieu of a Village-wide composting program,
perhaps interested residents could be given information about private companies
that offer subscription programs; and she encouraged the Village to hire zero
waste managers for public events in Winnetka.
Trustee Myers noted that he was the Council liaison to the EFC when refuse
collection was studied in 2016, and three issues were examined: (i) property
tax vs. fee for funding; (ii) “pay as you throw” fee schedules; and (iii)
backdoor vs. curbside pickup location. He explained that the funding issue was
examined in light of equity, and it was felt that paying with property taxes
only makes sense if usage is uniform around the community – which is not the
case with refuse pickup. “Pay as you throw” fee scheduling is a concept to
encourage residents to reduce their waste. Waste volume is directly tied to
tipping fees for refuse hauling; tipping fees will keep increasing as landfills
become full and new ones are located farther away. Efficiencies for curbside
collection would only be realized with a high rate of participation, and
wouldn’t make sense until that can be achieved. Finally, composting was only
discussed with an eye towards educating the community and providing a vehicle
for interested residents to compost food scraps.
Trustee Wedner thought the back door collection model might inadvertently
incentivize people to throw away recyclables as the path of least resistance.
She added that even relatively small cost-saving measures should be considered,
as they would provide some relief to taxpayers.
Trustee Lanphier said the tax environment has changed with the new limits on
deductibility, and she added that implementing a fee system would give
residents a measure of control over how much they pay for refuse services.
There was a discussion about the history of subsidizing the Refuse Fund with
transfers from the General Fund with Mr. Saunders explaining that the practice
dates back to the early 1980’s when the Village joined the Solid Waste Agency
of Northern Cook County. The Council agreed that if refuse is to be funded
purely through a service fee, it is important to communicate to residents where
the tax savings from that measure would go.
After more discussion about funding, the Council reached consensus to have
Village staff develop a financial analysis for fee-based refuse collection
service for the 2019 Budget discussions.
During a conversation about whether to change to curbside refuse collections,
Mr. Saunders noted that it would be prudent to continue offering the back door
option, as 40% of survey respondents opposed curbside collections. He explained
that curbside collection allows for a greater degree of automation, which is
helpful in reducing worker injuries, and he added that curbside containers use
smart technology that facilitate a “pay as you throw” fee schedule.
There was general consensus to ultimately implement curbside collection, with
continued back door subscriptions for those that prefer it; however, the
Council’s workload would not permit all the analyses to be completed in time
for the 2019 Budget sessions. It was agreed that Village staff would develop
implementation scenarios and bring the matter back for further discussion in
2019, with the goal of including curbside collection in the 2020 Budget.
The discussion then turned to the issue of composting. Mr. Saunders cautioned
that the Village’s transfer hauler does not currently have a permit to haul
food scraps; therefore, he would be more interested in partnering with a
private service for food scrap recycling. The Council generally favored
educating the public about the practice of composting food scraps, and pointing
them to a reliable private contractor.
President Rintz suggested the EFC vet the food scrap composting issue and
report back to the Council with recommendations.
Mr. Liss suggested making back door pick-up the baseline service for refuse
collection, and offering a savings if curbside is chosen; and he recommended
putting information for private composting company on the Village’s website.
President Rintz cautioned against advertising a private enterprise on the
Village website.
4) Closed Session.
None.
5) Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:49 p.m.
