    Deputy Clerk
June 5, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
(Approved: June 19, 2018)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
5, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
1) Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Village
Attorney Peter Friedman, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, Community
Development Director David Schoon, Assistant Community Development Director
Brian Norkus, and approximately 13 persons in the audience.
2) Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Lanphier led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3) Quorum.
   a) June 12, 2018 Study Session. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
   b) June 19, 2018 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   c) July 3, 2018 Regular Meeting. This meeting is cancelled due to its
proximity to the Fourth of July holiday.
4) Public Comment.
Jeremy Moreno, 839 Cherry. Mr. Moreno asked for better enforcement of the
Village’s seasonal ban on gas-powered leaf blowers.
Manager Bahan said he would forward Mr. Moreno’s comments to the Police
Department. He recommended Mr. Moreno call the Village when he sees a problem,
as police officers cannot always be at the scene of an infraction when it
occurs.
Trustee Lanphier suggested homeowners talk to their landscapers about using
electric or battery-powered equipment.
5) Reports:
   a) Trustees.
      i) Trustee Myers invited the community to the Winnetka Music Festival on
Friday and Saturday, June 15-16.
      ii) Trustee Lanphier invited residents to attend the upcoming Community
Conversations at Peet’s Coffee.
   b) Attorney. None.
   c) Manager. Manager Bahan noted that the State legislature had passed its
Budget; of note was the Local Government Distributive Fund, which was cut by
5%, instead of last year’s 10%. He added that the Chamber of Commerce is
bringing a Farmer’s Market to the Village Hall parking lot every Saturday
throughout the summer.
   d) Village President. President Rintz read aloud some letters he had
received from Crow Island School first-graders as part of an assignment on
writing persuasive letters.
6) Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
7) Consent Agenda
   a) Village Council Minutes.
      i) May 8, 2018 Study Session.
      ii) May 15, 2018 Regular Meeting.
   b) Approval of Warrant List dated May 11-31, 2018 in the amount of
$1,280,179.46.
   c) Resolution No. R-32-2018: Purchase of Leaf Vacuum Machines (Adoption).
   d) Resolution No. R-33-2018: Authorization to Use Motor Fuel Tax Funds
(Adoption).
   e) Resolution No. R-34-2018: Authorizing the Purchase of Primary Cable Under
a Contract with Okonite (Adoption).
   f) Resolution No. R-35-2018: Approving a Class A Liquor License for Kyoto
Sushi Plus, Inc. (Adoption).
   g) Resolution No. R-35-2018: Approving a Class A Liquor License for Kyoto
Sushi Plus, Inc. (Adoption).
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
8) Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a) Ordinance No. MC-4-2018: Amending Chapter 14.20 of the Winnetka Village
Code to Revise Small Wireless Facilities Regulation (Introduction & Adoption).
Mr. Keys reviewed Winnetka’s current regulations governing the installation of
small cell facilities. He explained that the State passed legislation in April
that overrides local ordinances pertaining to these facilities. The new
legislation gives municipalities a two-month period for enactment of
application procedures, and it strictly limits local control over permit fees,
locations of the facilities, and timeframe for permit processing.
   Mr. Keys said Village staff recommends revising Winnetka’s Village Code to
bring it in compliance with the new State law. He noted that the agenda packet
contains proposed language that conforms to the State requirements, and he
advised waiving introduction of the Subject Ordinance, as several small cell
site applications will be submitted by wireless carriers.
   The Council briefly discussed the request, and concurred with the
recommended Code amendment, as the new legislation leaves the Village very
little latitude in regulating small cell site applications.
   Trustee Swierk suggested working with wireless carriers to limit equipment
on utility poles where possible, and instead request the facilities be placed
on the ground.
   There being no further discussion or public comment, Trustee Myers, seconded
by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of Ordinance No. MC-4-2018. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier,
Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-
4-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Wedner. Nays: Trustee Swierk. Absent: None.
9) Old Business.
None.
10) New Business.
   Downtown Master Plan Task Force – Draft Downtown Streetscape and Signage
Plan Report. President Rintz explained the focus of the night’s discussion is
on whether the Council wishes to adopt the proposed Streetscape and Signage
Plan (Plan); and if yes, whether a request for Phase I engineering proposals
should be issued.
   Trustee Myers reviewed the history of Winnetka’s downtown revitalization
efforts, which began with a streetscape study in 2006, continued with a 2013
with the Urban Land Institute (ULI) study, as well as a Business Community
Development Commission follow-up of the ULI study, and culminated in the
formation of a Downtown Master Plan (DMP), adopted by the Village in the fall
of 2016. He explained that the DMP Task Force has worked over the past year to
develop a set of streetscape improvements in conformance with DMP
recommendations. He added that if the proposed Plan is adopted, the issues of
funding and phasing still remain to be determined by the Council.
   Jon Talty, Task Force Chair, noted that retail habits are changing both
locally and nationally, and Winnetka’s peers are investing in their downtowns
in an effort to attract visitors, and drive foot traffic to the local
retailers. He explained that the Plan is focused on downtown walkability and
reinvigorating and activating the areas by encouraging community involvement in
the commercial districts. He said balancing parking concerns is a primary
concern, and he reviewed a proposed reallocation of parking in West Elm which
would add customer parking spaces to the northern Post Office parking lot to
offset on-street parking removals. The proposed Plan would break the Elm
Business District into four quadrants, two on each side of Green Bay Road, and
stagger preliminary implementation of the plan into three phases.
   Trustee Myers reviewed the budget for each phase of work, for a total
estimated preliminary implementation cost in the Elm Business District of $8.5
million. He noted that some of the funding would come from the One Winnetka
developer, as well as grant awards from other agencies.
   Mr. Talty explained that adopting the Plan and starting implementation would
help the Village to qualify for potential grant funds, and he asked for the
Council’s help in demonstrating to the community that Winnetka can grow and
evolve in a healthy and responsible way.
   President Rintz called for public comment.
   Shawn Scott, Doyle Opticians, 565 Chestnut Street. Mr. Scott asked about
parking impacts of the Plan and he cautioned that street parking is very
important to customers, especially the elderly. He also recommended the Village
ensure that employees park in their designated spaces.
   Stephanie Hochschild, The Book Stall, 811 Elm Street. Ms. Hochschild
expressed concern about dislocation of parking in front of stores, and she read
comments expressing opposition to the Plan by nearby business owners: Ellen
Holthaus, Ellen’s on Elm; Elizabeth Berwanger, Village Toy Shop; Mark Kemerer,
Kaehler’s; Angela DeMars, Noah’s Ark Pet Supply; Deb McMahon, J. McLaughlin.
   Penny, Bliss Salon, 818 Elm Street. Penny expressed concern about losing
employee parking from the Post Office lot.
   Manager Bahan and Trustee Myers explained that there are ample spaces for
employees on Birch Street, as well as additional capacity in the Post Office
parking lot once the loading dock area is factored in.
   Mr. Talty noted that Winnetka’s business district parking does not meet
current codes for size or handicapped accessibility. He explained that the
Village cannot solve a large issue by considering the parking needs of each
individual case-by-case. He added that the Plan makes the streets safer and
more walkable for shoppers, which promotes activity for the businesses.
   Theresa Lucas, Good Grapes, 821 Chestnut Court. Ms. Lucas said she cared
more about purchases than creating gathering spaces or community experiences,
and added that she felt her area was written off by the Plan, as it is not
scheduled for Phase I implementation. She called for a project that could be
done immediately, and asked the following questions: (i) What return will the
Village get on its streetscape investment? (ii) How does reallocating employee
parking help the businesses? (iii) Where is a survey of Chamber members on the
topic? (iv) How did other towns revitalize their downtowns without spending
millions of dollars?
   President Rintz pointed out that adding community attractions near shopping
areas is a national trend, and that many regional malls, including Old Orchard,
are putting in attractions because they bring people out of their homes and
into a location where they also have an opportunity to shop and spend money.
   Terry Dason, Chamber of Commerce Director. Ms. Dason expressed support for
the Plan and voiced concern that Winnetka’s businesses will lose customers to
other more vital towns if the Council does not act soon to revitalize the
business districts. She added that the Task Force heard the parking concerns of
the community and worked very hard to develop a solution.
   Next, President Rintz asked the Trustees for their comments.
   Trustee Swierk said he would support the Plan on condition that the Village
continue with its efforts to expand employee parking at the post office loading
area. He noted that it is unrealistic to expect to find parking in front of
every store, and added that walking a block from the parking lot would expose
shoppers to other stores along the way.
   Trustee Cripe expressed support for the Plan, but he was concerned about the
mid-block bumpouts displacing street parking, and said he would be comfortable
eliminating them unless and until there is more acceptance of the new parking
accommodations.
   President Rintz explained that until the Plan moves to the design phase, it
is impossible to know where specific on-street parking spaces will be
eliminated. He noted that the Plan is a framework which will eventually be
fleshed out and brought back in more detail for Council approval.
   Trustee Cripe agreed the infrastructure and sidewalks need to be dealt with,
and that it makes sense to proceed to the design phase of the Plan to get a
picture of what on-street parking will look like.
   Trustee Wedner said she did not feel the Council knew enough about the Plan
to adopt it, asked what the economic payoff is for the investment, and called
for data about how increased foot traffic leads to increased retail sales. She
added that she did not feel a sense of urgency about the Plan from the business
community and suggested more thinking about how the Village can entice
businesses compatible with Winnetka’s demographics, and less effort creating
outdoor gathering areas, especially in light of the local climate.
   Trustee Dearborn said he liked the Plan and agreed with Trustee Cripe that
the mid-block bumpouts could be eliminated. He explained that the Plan needs to
be examined from a long-term perspective as something which will be phased in
over several years in a way that is least disruptive for the businesses. He
called for financing that takes into account other priorities like stormwater
management, and he expressed support for Council approval at each step in the
implementation process.
   Trustee Lanphier said she supported the Plan for implementation of necessary
capital improvements, since the quality of Winnetka’s infrastructure will add
to the attractiveness of the shopping districts. She expressed support for the
concept of gathering spaces and commerce as complementary elements, and called
for the Village to continue to work closely with business owners to address
their needs. She asked for more details about how the gathering element will be
used in the cold weather months and also called more comprehensive feedback on
parking concerns, especially from elderly residents.
   Trustee Myers explained that the process of developing the Plan included
extensive community outreach, with the goal of balancing maximum convenience
vs. enhancing the shopping experience. He noted that ensuring parking for every
customer in front of every store limits the Village’s ability to enhance the
shopping experience, and that parking is just one element of a successful
downtown revitalization effort. Other critical elements are devising a plan to
activate the commercial areas, and being expedient with the financing
discussions.
   President Rintz thanked the Task Force for its hard work, and called for a
motion to accept the Downtown Streetscape and Signage Plan, as well as a motion
to authorize design of Phase I of the Plan.
   Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to direct staff to draft a
Resolution accepting the Downtown Streetscape and Signage Plan. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and
Swierk. Nays: Trustee Wedner. Absent: None.
   Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to authorize staff to issue
a request for proposals for engineering design services for Phase I (West 1)
implementation of the Plan. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
11) Appointments:
None.
12) Closed Session.
None.
13) Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:33 p.m.
