    Deputy Clerk
June 12, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
June 12, 2018
(Approved: July 17, 2018)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
12, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Cripe called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present:
Trustees Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and Anne
Wedner. Absent: President Chris Rintz. Also in attendance: Village Manager
Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter
Friedman, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant Public Works Director
Jim Bernahl, and 2 persons in the audience.
Public Comment.
Liz Kunkle, 1303 Holly Lane, EFC Commissioner. Ms. Kunkle said she would like
to ensure the Greenest Region Compact 2 (GRC2) initiatives are considered in
light of all the Village’s operations, and she urged the Village to work on
GRC2 goals within its departments, as well as with local agencies.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street and League of Women Voters board member. Ms. Balsamo
said the League of Women Voters Board was pleased to learn of the Council’s
decision to adopt and implement the Streetscape and Signage Plan.
Evaluation of Stormwater Management Regulations.
Mr. Saunders explained that Winnetka’s Stormwater Master Plan recommends an
evaluation of the Village’s stormwater regulations. In 2017, Engineering
Research Associates (ERA) was hired to perform a thorough study of Winnetka’s
stormwater regulations, including: (i) ensuring compliance with Federal, State,
and County regulations; (ii) ensuring that Winnetka’s regulations reflect
current best stormwater management practices; and (iii) comparing Winnetka’s
regulations with those of other local area municipalities and counties.
Erin Pande, ERA Project Manager, explained that Winnetka’s standards generally
meet or exceed those of other counties; are in line with surrounding
municipalities; and are more restrictive than the Metropolitan Water
Reclamation District’s (MWRD) Watershed Management Ordinance (WMO). She noted
that members of the Village’s boards and commissions contributed to the
process, raising concerns about general flooding issues, impermeable surface
allowances, and Village oversight levels. The boards and commissions also
recommend continuing to enforce flood plain regulations and encouraging
residents to use best practices in small-scale projects that benefit individual
properties.
Next, Ms. Pande reviewed ERA’s recommendations:
Set a more restrictive detention threshold for existing developments
Increase detention requirements for redeveloments
Make no change in the Village’s detention requirements for deep basements
Develop a hierarchy of best management practices (BMPs)
Require maintenance on BMP projects, to ensure continued effectiveness
Reduce the required restrictor size and mandate that they be self-cleaning if
smaller than three inches
Repeal waivers granted by an amended WMO for flood protection elevation, base
flood elevation setbacks, and construction of basements in the flood plain.
The Council asked questions about the WMO waivers vs. Winnetka’s historical
practices in the flood plain. They also discussed how to provide incentives for
residents to utilize BMPs, and problems associated with restrictors.
During a discussion of the study’s recommendations, the Council agreed to lower
the threshold requirement for providing detention for new and existing
development. There was also consensus to reinstate floodplain protections that
were eliminated by the recent WMO amendments.
Finally, the Council asked for more information, such as storage capacity and
maintenance requirements, along with a list of potential BMPs, before it
sanctions a system for encouraging the use of BMPs for individual development
or redevelopment. No decision was made regarding an offer of financial
incentives to residents who install BMP projects on their property.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:45 p.m.
