Pat Balsamo, League of Women Voters. Ms. Balsamo commended the Village on the
success of the Winnetka Music Festival, and adoption of the Streetscape and
Signage Master Plan.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Lanphier reported that the last Community Conversations was held at the
Winnetka Farmers Market, which was a great venue to meet with residents.
Trustee Myers reported that the Winnetka Music Festival was a great success,
with a combined total attendance estimated at 17,000 – double the attendance
from 2017. He thanked the 300 volunteers from the Village, and praised the
hospitality, coordination and support from the Village, Park District, and
Community House.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. President Rintz thanked Trustee Myers for putting on a great
festival the previous weekend. Next, he announced that on June 27, there will
be a joint public hearing on the revised One Winnetka development. He gave a
quick review on status of the development and outlined the procedures which
will be followed. Finally, he read a letter from the Metropolitan Water
Reclamation District (MWRD) announcing the selection of Winnetka’s stormwater
projects at the Forest Preserves and Duke Childs Field for MWRD funding
assistance. He thanked Public Works Director Steve Saunders and Village Manager
Rob Bahan for pursuing the grant opportunity, and added that the MWRD has
authority to fund up to half the cost of the projects, which would greatly
relieve the taxpayers of Winnetka.
Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
June 5, 2018 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated June 1-14, 2018 in the amount of $300,341.43.
Resolution No. R-37-2018: Annual Prevailing Wage Resolution (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-39-2018: Authorizing the Purchase of Secondary Cable Under a
Contract with Wesco (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-40-2018: Holiday Lighting Program (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-41-2018: Trenchless Relining of Sanitary Sewers (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-42-2018: Purchase of Police Vehicle (Adoption).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Wedner.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-9-2018: 319 & 321 Sheridan Road - Godin Preliminary Plat and
Variation (Introduction & Adoption). Mr. Schoon explained that the Subject
Property had been a single lot until about 30 years ago, when it was divided
into two buildable lots. The new property owner desires to re-combine them into
one lot and demolish the home at 321 Sheridan Road, which will change lot width
calculations and cause the remaining home to become nonconforming with regard
to its side yard setback. He reported that the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan
Commission voted to recommend approval of the subdivision request and zoning
variation.
The Council briefly discussed the request, and Trustee Swierk suggested waiving
introduction so the Ordinance could be adopted immediately.
The Council agreed, and Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to
waive introduction of Ordinance No. M-9-2018. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Wedner.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-9-
2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Wedner.
Resolution No. R-38-2018: Village Internet and Phone Service Contract
(Adoption). Mr. Mostardo explained that the Village’s three-year contract with
Comcast for internet and phone service is expiring this summer. Staff is very
satisfied with Comcast’s performance and customer service, and recommends
waiving the bid requirements and approving a new five-year contract with
Comcast.
Mr. Mostardo noted that pricing for the new contract includes a savings of 9%
from the current contract, due to the longer five year term. In addition,
Comcast will convert an existing coaxial circuit at Village Hall to fiber
optic, which provides backup in an emergency, and improves the Village’s
overall service level. Since Village operations depend on reliable phone and
network service, the new contract would also provide for a response time within
four hours in the event of a system failure.
The Council briefly discussed the contract renewal recommendation, and agreed
it is in the Village’s best interests to waive bidding and take advantage of
the benefits offered by the new contract.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
38-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Wedner.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
Resolution No. R-43-2018: Approving Downtown Streetscape and Signage Master
Plan (Adoption). President Rintz explained that at the June 5 th Council
meeting, the Downtown Master Plan Task Force presented its final Streetscape
and Signage Master Plan (Plan), and the Council passed a motion directing Staff
to draft a resolution accepting the plan. He noted that the Plan is a
comprehensive design guide for the pedestrian-oriented Elm Street Business
District.
Mr. Schoon said he had nothing to add to President Rintz’ summary, and
explained August would be the soonest that the request for engineering bids
could be issued.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Pat Balsamo, 1037 Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo asked if the concerns of the
business community had been addressed.
President Rintz said the Council has a clear understanding of the merchants’
concerns, and will ensure the business owners are welcomed as participants in
every step of the implementation process.
Trustee Myers explained that until engineering design work is completed, the
Council can’t know the quantity or location of parking spots that will be
displaced. He recommended the parking needs be prioritized as follows: (i)
customers; (ii) employees; and (iii) commuters. He noted the Plan demonstrates
that enough parking capacity exists in the downtown area, and balancing the
needs of competing users can be accomplished once engineering design is
completed.
Meg Benson, Task Force member. Ms. Benson pointed out that business district
parking will be improved under the Plan, since both regular and handicapped
spaces will be brought up to code.
President Rintz explained that the Council can now turn its attention to the
financing discussions, which will begin at the July 24 special meeting.
The Council discussed a possible timeline for implementing Phase I of the
project. It was estimated that there could be an engineering design bid
recommendation for the Council’s consideration in the fall, followed by
discussions on design plans and funding estimates over the winter, and possibly
bidding the work in 2019.
Trustees Lanphier and Dearborn commented that the public needs to understand
that the Plan is much more than a beautification effort for the downtowns, and
is a significant and necessary infrastructure upgrade.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adopt Resolution No.
R-43-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Wedner.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 7:45 p.m.
            
    Deputy Clerk
July 10, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
July 10, 2018
(Approved: August 7, 2018)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
10, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, and Scott Myers. Absent:
Trustees John Swierk and Anne Wedner. Also in attendance: Village Manager
Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Ben
Schuster, Community Development Director David Schoon, Assistant Community
Development Director Brian Norkus, and approximately 4 persons in the audience.
Public Comment.
Eric Powers invited President Rintz to a reception in August for a high-ranking
associate of the Dalai Lama.
C-2 Commercial Overlay District.
Mr. Schoon reviewed the results of an informal poll of the Plan Commission
about the Overlay District that sought input on allowed uses, boundaries of the
Overlay District, and special use permit standards.
In most instances, the Commission was evenly divided between keeping the status
quo for special uses, and making revisions. Concerns were expressed about
overconcentration of permitted uses such as personal services (beauty/nail
salons, fitness/yoga, and dry cleaners/tailors/shoe repair). A suggestion was
made to add a window transparency requirement as a condition for approval of
special uses such as educational/tutoring or artistic/cultural instruction, to
give the appearance of a store and thus ensure an active block face for
pedestrians.
Some commissioners felt that medical offices, which are currently a special use
in the Overlay District, could be permitted if the space occupies less than
2,000 square feet, or possibly only allowing certain medical offices that
generate foot traffic. Two commissioners felt that medical offices larger than
2,000 square feet should not be allowed in the Overlay District.
A majority of the commissioners favored allowing a limited service restaurant
(without a drive-through window) as a permitted use; however, several felt
there should be size and location limitations. Brew pubs were preferred as a
special use by a majority of commissioners, with further limitations on such
uses suggested by several members. The full Plan Commission felt strongly that
financial services such as investment/security/commodity brokers should remain
as special uses.
After a few questions from the Council, President Rintz called for public
comment.
Louise Holland, Plan Commissioner and Chair of the Landmark Preservation
Commission. Ms. Holland urged the Council to preserve the Overlay District. She
objected to special use permits being granted in the heart of the business
districts; she asked the Council to give the Downtown Master Plan time to
succeed; and she opined that retail uses will find Winnetka once streetscape
projects have been completed.
Next, President Rintz read an email from Trustee Wedner, who could not attend
the meeting. Trustee Wedner generally agreed with the goal of encouraging
retail in Winnetka, and she was not in favor of changing the boundaries. She
also listed her preferences for permitted uses and special uses.
President Rintz explained the Council had been given a copy of the Lake Forest
Retail Overlay District regulations at the request of Trustee Dearborn. There
was a brief discussion about the Lake Forest methods for dealing with special
uses, which limit them to 10% of ground floor street frontage and 10% of ground
floor square footage.
President Rintz suggested that in cases where a supermajority of the Plan
Commission approves a special use application, the Council could defer to their
decision, in the interest of streamlining the approval process.
Trustee Lanphier said instead of framing the question as retail vs other uses,
the Council needs to envision downtowns where the windows entice pedestrians
and encourage interaction. While Winnetka’s downtowns provide services that the
community finds valuable, it is possible that retail opportunities might be
found within their storefronts to encourage people stopping in, like they would
a store or a restaurant. She urged consideration of traffic patterns and
parking availability, as some of the special uses have high volumes of traffic
for short time periods of time. The Village needs to match parking demand with
infrastructure limitations, so as not to inadvertently create a problem with
parking demand.
Trustee Myers noted that retail sales are not as simple as they were when the
Overlay District was created, thanks to the proliferation of online shopping.
With the advent of E-commerce, services have come to be viewed as desirable and
residents want them nearby. He explained he struggles with the standards for
granting special uses, as he feels they are ambiguous and subjective. He
described certain special uses, such as fitness studios, as high value/high
volume; and others, like financial services, as high value/low volume. He
suggested that maybe the low volume-type special uses could be pushed to the
edges of the Overlay Districts.
Trustee Cripe urged the Council to focus on updated uses that engage
pedestrians, like a “makerspace,” and steer clear of ones that undermine that
goal, such as a tutoring center where children are just dropped off for
appointments. He noted that both of those examples, however, are more engaging
than something like a medical or financial service office.
Trustee Dearborn commented that he felt the special use process was too
subjective, which is why he was interested in a percentage method like Lake
Forest uses, as a way to place limits on special uses while trying to remove
some subjectivity from the process.
Trustee Lanphier cautioned against allowing too many uses by right, as the
Village still needs to exercise oversight of parking and traffic.
There was more discussion on topics such as: pushing some uses to outer edges
of the Overlay District and onto second floors; how to activate storefronts
that are approved for a special use; flexibility for requiring parking studies;
how to tighten up the special use approval standards; and how to relieve
burdens on small business owners and landlords.
Attorney Schuster weighed in on how the Council could allow some desired
special uses and also maintain the desired activity and control over parking
and traffic in the retail districts. He cautioned that if the special use
regulations become too specific, the Council’s discretion to deny a particular
special use application could be reduced.
President Rintz suggested the Council generate a list of uses it wants
permitted by right.
Trustee Myers suggested a framework: (i) uses permitted by right, adding brew
pubs and some level of quick-serve restaurants; (ii) special uses by right with
a percentage cap – such as yoga studios, nail salons, medical offices, and
tutoring services; and (iii) special uses that would include realtors,
architects, and other similar office uses. Once a special use by right cap is
met, an applicant would have to meet a higher bar with a special use permit
process.
Mr. Schoon explained that Lake Forest has a frontage percentage cap and also a
square footage cap for special uses.
President Rintz said the uses could be examined individually, and a certain
percentage of allowed space assigned to each one. The Village would come up
with a net number of what it will accept for special uses and divide it up by
specific uses.
After more discussion, the Council agreed to explore adding limited service
restaurants to the list of permitted uses and tweaking the definition. The
permitted special uses capped by percentage would be: medical offices; arts/
cultural uses (except a makerspace); fitness/yoga; tutoring/test prep; beauty
salons, barber shops, nail salons; and dry cleaners/tailors/shoe repair.
Special uses that would need to go through the full application process:
professional offices like financial services and realtors. It was agreed that
brew pubs could be a permitted use in Hubbard Woods since the Village’s parking
deck provides ample parking and the amount of available space; but would be a
special use in the Elm Business District, since parking is at more of a premium
in this district.
Finally, the Council did not express interest in modifying the Overlay District
boundaries at this time. Some tightening of the language for special use
standards was requested, as well as data regarding square footage of current
uses occupying space in each business district, which such uses would become
“capped” uses.
Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:04 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
