Public Comment.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak Street, and Elise Covey, former Winnetka resident. Ms.
Holland and Ms. Covey thanked the Village for installing railings on the
sidewalk in front of Christ Church at Sheridan Road and Humboldt Avenue. Ms.
Holland recalled that the area had been hazardous before the railings were
installed.
Reports:
Trustees. Trustee Lanphier reported on the success of the Community
Conversations event, being held at the Farmer’s Market over the summer. She
also thanked the Village for being a sponsor of the Nielsen tennis tournament
and making such a wonderful event possible.
Trustee Dearborn reported on a recent visit he made to RED Center, commenting
that it is a high tech and interesting facility.
Trustee Cripe reported on the last Environmental & Forestry Commission meeting,
where the GRC2 initiatives were evaluated.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. None.
Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
June 12, 2018 Study Session.
June 19, 2018 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated June 15 – July 12, 2018 in the amount of
$758,084.24.
Resolution No. R-44-2018: Approving an Agreement with Phoenix Fire System, Inc.
for Plant Load Center Fire Protection Design-Build (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-46-2018: Approving a Contract with Master Project Inc. for
Electric Plant Roof Replacement (Adoption).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Myers and Wedner.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-45-2018: 860 Lamson Drive - Sandra K. Crown Subdivision Final
Plat Approval (Adoption). President Rintz explained that in April, the Council
adopted Ordinance No. M-5-2018 approving the preliminary plat of subdivision
for the Subject Property. The subject Resolution grants approval of the final
subdivision plat.
Mr. Schoon noted that the final plat is in substantial compliance with the
preliminary plat, and the applicant is asking for final approval.
There being no questions or comments from the audience or Council, Trustee
Cripe, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-45-2018.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Swierk
and President Rintz. Nays: Trustee Lanphier. Absent: Trustees Myers and Wedner.
Ordinance No. M-10-2018: Disposition of a Surplus Vehicle (Introduction). Mr.
Saunders explained that the existing vehicle is in poor condition and he asked
for the Council’s permission to dispose of the vehicle. He explained that
ordinarily, the purchase agreement for the replacement vehicle would contain
provisions for disposal of the old one, but it had inadvertently been left out
of Resolution No. R-14-2018.
There were no comments or questions from the audience or Council.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-10-2018. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. MC-5-2018: Updating Flood Hazard Protection Regulations
(Introduction). President Rintz noted that this matter had been thoroughly
discussed at the June Study Session, where the Council approved amending the
Village’s flood plain regulations to restore provisions that were removed by
the Metropolitan Water Reclamation District (MWRD) in February.
Mr. Saunders explained that the MWRD stripped buffer protections from its
Watershed Management Ordinance (WMO) during a recent revision process. Since
the Village has incroporated the WMO into the Village Code by reference, the
buffer protections need to be restored to Winnetka’s flood protection
regulations.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the previously existing flood plain buffer regulations
that will be restored to the Village Code, as well as several minor technical
amendments to update FEMA Map Panel numbers, WMO reference citations, and
formula corrections.
There being no questions or comments from the audience or Council, Trustee
Cripe, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to introduce Ordinance No. MC-5-2018.
By voice vote, the motion carried.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
Forest Preserves Next Century Conservation Plan. Beth Dunn, Director of
Governmental Affairs at the Botanic Gardens, said the Forest Preserves of Cook
County (FPCC) has over 70,000 acres of land, with one of the highest
concentrations of rare, threatened, and endangered plants in the state. She
explained that the Botanic Gardens receives annual operating support from the
Forest Preserves, and partners with it on important initiatives. She introduced
Anthony Tindall, FPCC Policy Manager for Special Projects, and noted that Mr.
Tindall would present Cook County’s vision for the next 100 years of Forest
Preserves upgrades, restoration, and oversight of this valuable resource.
Mr. Tindall noted that the FPCC hopes to increase its acreage and restore
woodlands, savannahs, wetlands and prairies; and also ensure that all
communities are welcome in the Forest Preserves. He gave a presentation
outlining the FPCC’s Next Century Preservation Plan, which will ensure thriving
native landscapes, as well as safeguard and expand the assets of the Forest
Preserves.
The presentation was well received by the Council, as the Forest Preserves are
used and valued by the Winnetka community.
President Rintz thanked the FPCC for its vision in partnering with Winnetka on
its proposed wetlands solution to the Village’s stormwater issues, and he
commended the vision and leadership of the organization.
Comprehensive Annual Financial Report (CAFR). Mr. Sloth explained that the
annual audit is intended to show that the Village’s financial statements are
accurate and complete. He noted that the Village’s position is very strong and
the General Fund continues to generate adequate cash flow for operating and
capital needs.
Mr. Sloth said the General Fund posted a $357,000 surplus for 2017, which was
attributed to cost-cutting measures that reduced operating costs, adding that
most of the capital projects budgeted for 2017 were completed. He explained
that the Village’s financial consultant, Kevin McCanna, recommended the Village
consider dedicating future General Fund surplus funds for a project such as
downtown revitalization, or paying down pension obligations; or even assigning
it to a general Capital account with no specific designation.
Mr. Sloth noted that the enterprise funds increased their cash positions, with
the exception of the Electric Fund; and capital spending totaled nearly $6
million. The only budgeted capital projects that were not completed were
stormwater related, which are being deferred until all intergovernmental
negotiations are complete. Lastly, he said pension funding increased
significantly in 2017.
Ron Amen, Lauterback & Amen, the Village’s Auditor. Mr. Amen explained that the
audit process involves a significant assessment of the Village’s internal
controls as they relate to fraud and risk. He said the Village earned the
opinion of “Unqualified,” which is the highest that can be achieved. After
reviewing the audit process, he elaborated upon the comments in the Management
Letter.
President Rintz said he was not prepared to have a discussion about allocation
of excess funds until a full Council was present; and he asked Mr. Amen why it
is recommended to move the funds out of the General Fund.
Mr. Amen said it is not the auditor’s recommendation, and he explained that the
practice stems from the manner in which the Bond Council examines a
municipality’s General Fund as part of its ratings exercise. He cautioned that
if a fund receiving property tax revenue becomes too financially healthy,
questions could arise. He noted that no two towns are the same in their fund
policies, so comparisons are not helpful.
The Council discussed the audit, fund balances, and pension funding.
There were no comments or questions from the audience.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
Trustee Cripe moved to adjourn into Closed Session to discuss Litigation and
Closed Session Minutes, pursuant to Sections 2c(11) and 2c(21) of the Illinois
Open Meetings Act. Trustee Lanphier seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Myers and Wedner.
President Rintz announced that the Council would not return to the open meeting
after Executive Session. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 8:05
p.m.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:26 p.m.
            
    Recording Secretary
July 24, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
July 24, 2018
(Approved: August 21, 2018)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
24, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and Anne Wedner.
Absent: Trustee Penny Lanphier. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Finance Director Tim Sloth, Assistant
Finance Director Nick Mostardo, Community Development Director David Schoon,
Economic Development Coordinator Mike Martella, and approximately 9 persons in
the audience.
Public Comment.
None.
Discussion of Downtown Financing Options.
President Rintz explained that the evening’s discussion would round out
conversations about tools for funding downtown revitalization projects. Once
all the options have been presented, the Council will have the information it
needs to develop a financing methodology. The final funding discussion will be
held with an eye towards balancing downtown revitalization needs against flood
risk reduction costs.
Mr. Sloth reviewed funding options for the Council’s consideration:
Home Rule sales tax
Property tax increase
Vehicle sticker increase
Real estate transfer tax
Food & beverage tax
Special Service Area
The revenue capacity of each option was identified for both a “pay-as-you-go”
financing amount, as well as its potential value as collateral for a bond
issuance. After the presentation, President Rintz called for public comment.
There being none, the Council discussed the options.
There was general distaste for a tax increase. Trustee Myers suggested the
Village fund streetscape from a combination of grants, operating efficiencies
that result in a surplus, and other means. He noted that gathering a pool of
funds in this manner would mean a lengthier improvement process, but could have
a smaller adverse impact on businesses and residents.
Several Trustees were in favor of allocating budget surpluses from the General
Fund and budgeting an additional amount for the streetscape fund each year.
Trustee Wedner commended Village staff on doing an incredible job managing the
Village finances. She expressed discomfort about discussing tax increases
without knowing how residents feel about the downtown improvements. She
suggested going to a referendum to get community input; and she added that
perhaps the Village could partner with the Park District for financing on a
portion of the streetscape costs.
President Rintz noted that Staff had been charged with collecting data on all
the potential revenue mechanisms available to the Council, even though a
discussion about raising taxes is unpalatable. He commented the establishment
of a tax increment financing (TIF) fund is appealing in that it does not raise
anyone’s taxes.
Some Trustees were in favor of adjusting the Village’s vehicle sticker charges;
the issue may be discussed further at Budget time.
Manager Bahan noted that the Village is always looking for grant opportunities.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Roger Hochschild, owner of The Book Stall. Mr. Hochschild said the Council
needs to hear the perspectives of retailers and restaurant operators before
enacting a sales tax. He also expressed concern about the tree removal
proposals and parking changes in the Downtown Streetscape & Signage Master Plan
(Plan), as he supposed it would have negative impacts on retail and
restaurants.
President Rintz explained that the Plan is a framework that needs to be fleshed
out and discussed with the community, including businesses, before any action
is taken.
Use of the Public Sidewalks – Private Art Structures.
President Rintz explained that in 2017, five private art structures were
installed on public sidewalks without prior Village approval. Several of the
installations were relocated to private property at the Village’s request, and
all were taken down for the winter. In April 2018, the art returned and was
placed either wholly or partially on the public sidewalk. The Village has
received comments and concerns from the community about the art, and it also
needs to consider liability issues associated with the placement of objects on
the public sidewalks. President Rintz noted that he has sought to discuss the
issue with The Hoffmannn Group, to no avail. Now, the Council must have a
discussion about whether it is necessary to enact legislation to govern not
only art installations, but planters and sandwich signs that reside on the
public sidewalks.
Next, President Rintz called for public comment.
Mary Jo LaViolette, Winnetka resident. Ms. LaViolette said the art is
encroaching on the public sidewalk and should not have been put down without
the Village’s permission.
President Rintz asked Ms. LaViolette what she would have the Council do about
other things on the sidewalk, such as planters and small signs, since the
principle of using the public sidewalk applies to these things too.
Ms. LaViolette explained that she did not see planters and small signs as
encroachments and asked if the Village gets complaints about them. She
recommended that the Village appoint a board to approve any art objects on the
public sidewalks, and she asked the Council to enact an ordinance governing
such items.
Theresa Lucas, owner of Good Grapes. Ms. Lucas said her customers generally
seem to like the Hoffmannn statues; she added that beauty is in the eye of the
beholder, and art is not meant to be liked by all. She also expressed concern
about an expansion of the art to the point that it blocks signs and tables of
business owners.
Maggie Wilson, Winnetka resident. Ms. Wilson said the store where she works has
a sandwich sign out front to attract customers and she expressed support for
keeping it.
Dan Burns, The Hoffmann Group. Mr. Burns said other towns where Hoffmann is a
large real estate holder have a public art program, and it has increased sales
and foot traffic. He added that there is no art that 100% of people will like.
He explained that Hoffmann has done improvements to make its buildings more
attractive, and would like to expand the art program with a focus on local
artists, as a way to single out Winnetka from other towns.
The Council discussed with Mr. Burns the art programs in other towns where
Hoffmann owns real estate, and he explained there is no public process for the
art in the other towns.
Trustee Cripe explained that in Winnetka, things usually get done by people
working together in good faith and he expressed dislike for writing new
legislation governing public art. He said he especially does not like getting
emails from the community about the big statue on the corner Elm Street and
Lincoln Avenue, and he called upon Mr. Burns to simply be a good neighbor and
remove it.
Trustee Wedner commented that it is in The Hoffmann Group’s best interests not
to create animus in the community.
Other Trustees were in favor of having some kind of Village control in place
for public art, even it’s only to designate spaces for it.
After more conversation, Attorney Schuster explained that the Village has
control over its rights of way. The Council could appoint an art review board
for a Village public art program; however, it does not have authority to
regulate the content of private art in public spaces. The Village does have
power to designate areas where art can be placed and where it cannot, as long
as it is not based on the art’s content.
The Council discussed its options at length, and Attorney Schuster explained
how a content-neutral public art policy could be enacted, basing regulations on
location, sight lines, size of objects, proximity to buildings, etc.
Ultimately, the Council agreed that if the controversial statue on the corner
of Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue is not removed by August 24, the Village
Attorney should draft an ordinance legislating placement of public art on the
Village rights of way.
President Rintz added that the Village is simply looking for neighborliness and
civil discourse from The Hoffmann Group and an acknowledgment that the art is
on Village property, and the issue should be considered in that light.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:35 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
August 7, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
August 7, 2018
(Approved: August 21, 2018)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
August 7, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, and Penfield Lanphier. Absent: Trustees Scott
Myers, John Swierk, and Anne Wedner. Also present: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Assistant Finance Director Nick Mostardo, Police Chief Marc Hornstein,
Community Development Director David Schoon, and approximately five people in
the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Cripe led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
August 14, 2018 Study Session – Cancelled.
August 21, 2018 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present except
Trustee Lanphier said they expect to attend.
September 4, 2018 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Thursday, September 13, 2018 Study Session. All of the Council members present
except Trustee Dearborn said they expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Eva, Manager of Bliss Salon. Eva commented that parking is more important to
her than streetscape bump-outs that would reduce on-street parking; and she
also asked that the 90-minute parking limit be raised to 2 hours. She confirmed
with President Rintz that two-hour parking in the Post Office parking lot would
be more beneficial than the four-hour commuter parking that is currently
available there.
Jane Hayes, owner of the Classic Stitch. Ms. Hayes also expressed concerns
about losing parking spaces in the Elm Business District, and she echoed the
call to increase the time allotment for customer parking. President Rintz
explained that the store owners will be consulted in the development of a
Streetscape Plan before a final version of the plan is adopted.
Carin Adelman, Laurel Avenue. Ms. Adelman said neighborhood dog owners are
leaving their dog droppings in her yard and bushes, and she felt under attack
because she put out a sign telling dog owners to clean up after their pets. She
asked the Council to help her resolve the problem and to find a way to restore
a feeling of pride in the community.
President Rintz expressed grave concern about Ms. Adelman’s problem; Trustee
Lanphier commented that the larger issue is civility in the Village and she
suggested the Council schedule a conversation about how to restore it. Trustee
Cripe concurred, noting that he frequently sees dog droppings left behind by on
the parkways, adding that the behavior is not only rude – it contributes to
pollution in the Village waterways.
Susie Shabica, Winnetka resident. Ms. Shabica sympathasized with Ms. Adelman’s
plight, and echoed the previous comments about parking reductions created by
the Streetscape Plan implementation. She opined that the corner bumpouts would
only be used part of the year when the weather is warm. She asked how many
trees would be removed, and how many and what types would be replanted. She
asked for further information about the lighting of the trees; and finally, she
asked what was going to be done to redevelop the Post Office site. She posited
that there are too many realtors in the business districts taking up valuable
parking, and other communities deal with the issue of realtors differently than
Winnetka.
President Rintz said the Streetscape Plan is still in the conceptual stage. He
explained that there was consensus during the Plan’s development that the trees
on the north-south oriented streets do not have healthy trees vs. the better
health of the trees on streets that run east-west. He added that a reduction in
the number of trees will affect the mood of holiday lighting, and will have to
be an important component of conversations about tree removal. Ms. Shabica
noted that she would prefer lighting on poles instead of trees, for the health
of the trees.
Reports:
Trustees. None.
Attorney. None.
Manager. Manager Bahan reported that the private art on the corner of Lincoln
Avenue and Elm Street has not been removed, and the hold harmless still
agreement has not been received from Hoffmann Group.
Village President. None.
Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
July 10, 2018 Study Session.
July 17, 2018 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated July 13 – August 2, 2018 in the amount of
$1,937,816.28.
Ordinance No. M-10-2018: Disposition of a Surplus Vehicle (Adoption).
Ordinance No. MC-5-2018: Updating Flood Hazard Protection Regulations
(Adoption).
Resolution No. R-48-2018: Approval & Release of Closed Session Minuets
(Adoption).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and President Rintz. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Myers, Swierk, and Wedner.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-47-2018: Approving Amended NIPAS Agreement (Adoption). Chief
Hornstein explained that the Northern Illinois Police Alarm System (NIPAS) is a
mutual aid organization that provides assistance in the event of a police
emergency that exceeds the response capacity of a member community. He reviewed
the history of the organization, and noted that the original bylaws and
membership agreement have not been updated since 1983.
Trustee Dearborn asked what the changes were from the old agreement to the new
one.
Attorney Friedman explained that the old agreement did not specify who would
pay for aid that was received; and language has been updated for dealing with
intergovernmental liabilities. He recommended the Village join into the new
agreement, as it protects NIPAS as a unit.
There being no further questions or public comment, Trustee Cripe, seconded by
Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-47-2018. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and President
Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Myers, Swierk, and Wedner.
Resolution No. R-49-2018: BMO Harris Lease Renewal (Adoption). Mr. Mostardo
explained that Harris Bank has leased a small parcel to the north of Village
Hall for its drive-through facility since 1968. The bank does not have
exclusive use of the area, and the Village keeps the rent low; however, the
rent was increased slightly for the next ten-year lease term.
There being no comments or questions from the Council or audience, Trustee
Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-49-
2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, and President Rintz. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Myers, Swierk, and
Wedner.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
Request for Code Amendments to Allow Amusement Arcades. Mr. Schoon reviewed a
request by Hoffmann Commercial Real Estate for a Village Code amendment to
permit coin-operated amusement devices, and a Zoning Amendment to permit an
arcade business at 552-554 Lincoln Avenue.
Mr. Schoon said the issue immediately before the Council is to designate a body
to conduct a public hearing on the Zoning Amendment and consider the requests.
He explained that the Council could send the matter to an advisory body, or
keep the hearing and discussion at the Council level.
The Council discussed the history of legislating coin-operated amusement
devices in Winnetka, which stems from community concerns when the Liquor Code
was relaxed nearly a decade ago. At that time, some residents expressed concern
that bars and games would proliferate in Winnetka.
Dan Burns, Hoffmann Real Estate representative. Mr. Burns elaborated on the
concept of an arcade at the Subject Property, explaining that an ice cream
store and toy store would occupy adjacent space, creating energy between the
businesses and enticing young families to the downtown area.
After a few Council questions, President Rintz called for public comment.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo said she has discussed the arcade with
some friends and they think the concept sounds interesting; she added that this
type of thing is being done in other locales, and it gives children a chance to
socialize.
The Council had a thorough discussion about whether it would prefer to send the
request to an advisory board or keep the matter at the Council level. In the
end, there was consensus to keep the request with the Council, to show the
business community that Winnetka can move quickly to benefit new businesses.
Mr. Schoon was directed to gather detailed information relating to the
requested Code and Zoning Ordinance amendments, and to bring draft amendment
language for the Council to consider.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:03 p.m.
            
    Recording Secretary
