September 4, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
September 4, 2018
(Approved: October 2, 2018)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 4, 2018, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Village
Attorney Peter Friedman, Police Chief Marc Hornstein, Commander Brian
O’Connell, Finance Director Tim Sloth, Assistant Finance Director Nick
Mostardo, and approximately 4 persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Swierk led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
September 13, 2018 Rescheduled Study Session. All of the Council members
present except Trustee Dearborn and possibly Trustee Wedner said they expect to
attend.
September 18, 2018 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
October 2, 2018 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present with the
possible exception of Trustee Swierk said they expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Leeann Rechtin 123 Fuller Lane. Ms. Rechtin commended Village services and
pointed out the value of retaining good employees and rewarding them by funding
their pensions and not cutting their jobs in the name of efficiencies. Next,
she commented on problems she has with her neighbors on issues such as
lighting, fencing, and flooding, and she asked for a review and possible
amendment of sections 9.08.060 and 9.16.020 of the Village Code dealing with
nuisances.
President Rintz explained that the Village is updating its building codes, and
Ms. Rechtin’s comments will help with the ongoing review. He noted that many
communities have legislation governing lighting, whereas Winnetka does not,
adding that the Village tries to be objective and not get too stringent with
its regulations.
Pat Balsamo, 1037 Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo commented that the League of Women
Voters has ongoing concerns about the statue in the Elm Business District and
asked when an Ordinance might be in place.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Lanphier invited the community to the next Community Conversations
event at the Farmer’s Market.
Trustee Myers said the Chamber is very happy with the new location of the
Farmer’s Market.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. President Rintz announced that – pending complete submittal
of documents from the applicant – it appears the Council will review the final
One Winnetka application at its next Regular Meeting on September 18. He
invited the community to an open house on September 8 th at the Tower Road boat
launch, being held to gather public comments on a proposed boathouse at the
location.
Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
August 21, 2018 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated August 17-30, 2018 in the amount of
$1,185,880.21.
Ordinance No. MC-6-2018: Amending Liquor Code for Class D License (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-53-2018: Approving a Class D Liquor License for Albatross Wine
Company, LLC (Adoption).
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-52-2018: Approving the Purhcase of Police Conducted Electrical
Weapons (Tasers) (Adoption). Chief Hornstein explained that Tasers are an
effective tool for the Police Department. He said the current models were
purchased in 2012-2013, and their warranties are now expired. He reviewed
purchasing options for new Tasers and recommended the most cost-effective
procedure: a purchase of 28 new devices with a five-year warranty, with payment
spread over the next four years. The total cost of the units would be
$41,564.00, which includes a reduction in cost for purchasing the units in this
way.
The Council discussed the request for a short time, reaching consensus to
approve the recommendation.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Ms. Rechtin asked if a better alternative is to stagger the purchases so they
don’t all have to be re-purchased at the same time every five years.
Chief Hornstein explained that this option was explored; however, the Village
would spend more money doing it that way, as there would be no discount.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-52-
2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
Public Safety Pension Update. President Rintz explained that following a 2017
Study Session to discuss the Village’s public safety pension funds, the Council
requested an annual update from the pension boards. He said the presidents of
the Police and Fire Pension Boards were in attendance to discuss investment
strategies and performance, following a presentation by Finance Director Tim
Sloth.
Mr. Sloth briefly explained how pensions work, and reviewed statutory
requirements for structuring and funding public safety pension funds. Next he
outlined the pension boards’ responsibilities, and gave detailed summaries of
the Police and Fire pension funds. Finally, he introduced Mark Fuller,
president of the Fire Pension Board; and John O’Malley, president of the Police
Pension Board.
Mr. Fuller stressed that the Village is doing the right thing by erring on the
conservative side with its assumptions on rates of investment return, and fully
funding the pensions each year. He explained that the conservative assumptions
make Winnetka’s funding numbers look worse than they may actually be, and he
gave a thorough discourse on asset allocation. He concluded that the Fire
Pension Fund is performing very credibly, and returns are coming in above the
projections.
Mr. O’Malley agreed with all Mr. Fuller said, noting that the Police and Fire
pension boards function independently. He gave a long-term history of the
Police Pension Fund’s funding ratio, and explained that in recent years the
Village has lowered its assumptions on rate of investment return, as well as
updated its mortality tables – both of which reduced the funding ratio on
paper. He added that Winnetka targets 100% funding by 2035, which is a higher
bar than State Statute sets, which only mandates 90% funding by 2040.
The Trustees discussed the pensions briefly and thanked Messrs. Fuller and
O’Malley for their stewardship.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:15 p.m.
            
    Recording Secretary
September 13, 2018
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
RESCHEDULED STUDY SESSION
September 13, 2018
(Approved: October 2, 2018)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
September 13, 2018 at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and Anne Wedner. Absent: Trustees Bob
Dearborn and Penny Lanphier. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Community Development Director David Schoon,
Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant Public Works Director Jim
Bernahl, Finance Director Tim Sloth, and approximately 3 persons in the
audience.
Public Comment.
None.
Request for Code Amendments to Allow Amusement Arcades.
Mr. Schoon reviewed a request for a zoning amendment from Hoffman Commercial
Real Estate (HCRE) to allow coin-operated amusement devices. HCRE is proposing
to open an arcade and other child-friendly businesses in one of its Lincoln
Avenue properties. He noted that a public hearing has been noticed for the
October 2 Council meeting, in case the Council wishes to move forward with
approving the plan. He reviewed the draft approval Ordinance for the arcade,
and answered questions from the Council.
The Council discussed the request and made the following suggestions:
Ensure there is adequate supervision for young children
Ensure management undergoes a criminal background check
Ensure some kind of video surveillance for the area
Ensure the space is kept clean and sanitary
Place limits on internet connectivity to the proposed games
Remove limits on size and location of future arcades
Hours of operation for coin-operated devices should end at 10:00 PM for
arcades, 12:00 AM for restaurants
Attorney Schuster recommended separating the zoning issues from licensing
provisions, which can cover hours, supervision and other issues. Since the
license is issued on an annual basis, the Village has the freedom to make
changes on licensing each year. He cautioned against trying to legislate the
content of the games, since the Zoning Ordinance is a land use vehicle, not a
moral tool.
The Trustees agreed to let the market dictate which games will be popular, and
also to leave it to parents to police their children’s gaming.
Dan Burns, HCRE. Mr. Burns explained the games are not intended to connect to
the internet. He noted that surveillance cameras are planned for safety
purposes; and also that the sanitary aspect is very important to HCRE. He gave
the hours of operation as: Wednesday-Thursday from 3:00 PM – 8:00 PM; Friday
from 3:00 PM – 9:30 PM; and Saturday-Sunday from 11:00 AM – 9:30 PM.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Julie Windsor, Village resident and owner of Beat Street. Ms. Windsor opined
that the arcade concept is a very good offering to families in the area, and
she agreed that supervision and safety are key factors. She noted that high
school students make great employees: they can clean the arcade and help
supervise the kids, as well as work at Beat Street and the gelato store.
Lastly, she recommended a bike rack be installed at the back of the arcade
building.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo said residents will want a very clean
arcade and recommended a full-time cleaner be employed on-site.
Mr. Schoon said he would work with the Village Attorney to make the recommended
changes to the draft Ordinance to eliminate language limiting the size and
location of arcades; and include provisions in the license application for: (i)
hours of operation; (ii) video surveillance; (iii) criminal background checks
for managers/operators; (iv) adequate supervision of children; (v) sanitary/
cleanliness requirements; and (vi) limiting internet connectivity of the games.
2017 Sanitary Sewer Evaluation Final Report.
Mr. Saunders explained that over the past several years the Village has been
working to identify and correct sources of Inflow/Infiltration (I/I) into the
public sanitary sewer system. A followup study by RJN Group, Inc. (RJN) was
approved by the Council in the spring of 2017 for further field studies of
sewer basins previously identified as problematic. RJN presented findings and
cost estimates for repairs in advance of 2019 Budget discussions, to give the
Council adequate background to consider funding alternatives.
Shilpa Ghia, RJN project manager. Ms. Ghia presented an overview of the types
of inspections performed, along with the results for each: (i) smoke testing;
(ii) manhole inspections; (iii) sewer televising; (iv) dyed water testing; (v)
private property inspections; and (vi) advanced inspections.
Next, Ms. Ghia summarized the identified defects in both public and private
sector systems, and she reviewed cost estimates to repair the public sector
defects, totaling approximately $3 million. Finally, she reviewed the
Metropolitan Water Reclamation District (MWRD) I/I reduction program, which
calls for development of a long-term public operations & maintenance program,
as well as a private sector remediation program.
Mr. Saunders said Village staff is already researching methods to finance this
latest set of improvements, and he explained that the Illinois Environmental
Protection Agency (IEPA) loan program is an attractive option. The Village is
already using IEPA loans to help finance its water main replacement program.
The Council briefly discussed the next steps in the I/I reduction program, and
President Rintz commented that strategic planning lays out a long-term plan for
infrastructure improvements. He noted that the Village’s “pay as you go” policy
has historically focused on short-term solutions, leaving future Councils to
deal with major infrastructure needs.
Manager Bahan asked Mr. Saunders to elaborate on the private sanitary sewer
system.
Mr. Saunders explained that private sewer laterals running from each home to
the public sewer in the street make up almost the same amount of pipe as the
public system. The Village has not inspected private laterals; however, there
are almost certainly private connections that need repairs. He noted that
another big source of I/I into the public sanitary sewer is improperly
connected sump pumps, but the Village cannot inspect without being intrusive to
the homeowners. It is the responsibility of the homeowner to correct any
deficiencies in their private sewer connections.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:17 p.m.
____________________________
