Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner.
Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-15-2018: 723 Elm Street - Definition Fitness Special Use Permit
(Introduction & Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for a special use
permit to allow a personal training studio in the C-2 Commercial District,
which does not allow fitness uses by right. The owner of the proposed new
business and a representative of the building’s owner, BJB, assured the Council
the business is ready to be up and running.
After a brief discussion, the Council was in agreement to waive introduction
and proceed to adoption of the approval Ordinance.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-15-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-15-
2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
One Winnetka Approval Documents. President Rintz explained that the evening’s
discussion of the One Winnetka Planned Development will begin with a review
from Community Development Director David Schoon; then Village Attorney
Friedman would review the approval documents; the developer would give a
presentation; public comment would be heard; and finally, the Council would ask
questions and provide comments.
Mr. Schoon reviewed the history of the One Winnetka Planned Development
approval process since modifications were made to the plan after its
preliminary approval in January, 2017. He noted that the Council sent the
amended final plan to the Village’s advisory bodies -- the Zoning Board of
Appeals (ZBA); Plan Commission (PC); and Design Review Board (DRB) -- for their
review and recommendations after it was presented to the Village Council in
December, 2017. He reviewed the changes made in the final plan application; the
recommendations of the advisory bodies; the zoning relief required for
approval; and estimated the public benefit of the development to be a net gain
of $4,739,000. He explained that the Council’s options include continuing the
case for further discussion; introduction of the approval Ordinances; or denial
of the request.
Attorney Friedman explained that there are five documents that must be passed
by the Council in conjunction with approval of the final application: (i) an
Ordinance rezoning the parcel at 710 Elm Street; (ii) a Resolution approving a
final plat of subdivision; (iii) an Ordinance granting a Special Use Permit,
zoning variations and Certificate of Appropriateness; (iv) an Ordinance
granting final approval of a Planned Development; and (v) a Resolution
approving an amended and restated Development Agreement. He noted that all of
the approval documents have been provided to the Developer and his legal team
for their approval.
Next, Attorney Friedman reviewed the protections to the Village contained in
the approval documents, and he explained that there will be no subdivision or
zoning relief granted until the Developer closes on the Conney’s Pharmacy
property. The deadline for that closing is December 31, 2018 (with two 90-day
extensions). Only after the Conney’s closing occurs will the plat of
subdivision be recorded and the zoning approvals be triggered. The Developer is
required to convey the Village’s parking level to the Village within five days
of the Conney’s closing. If the Conney’s closing does not happen within the
prescribed timeframe, the Village has the right to declare the zoning approvals
null and void.
Attorney Friedman noted that before any building permits will be issued to the
Developer, the following must take place: (i) the final plat of subdivision be
recorded; (ii) the vertical lot for parking conveyed to the Village; (iii) all
required forms and approvals from other government agencies be issued to the
Developer; (iv) a reciprocal easement agreement recorded between Village and
Developer for maintenance and access to the parking structure; (v) a letter of
credit equal to 125% of the public improvements be posted; (vi) an escrow
account established to reimburse the village for third-party expenses,
indemnification, and hold harmless agreements; (vii) a third-party guarantor be
appointed; and (viii) written detailed documentation of all necessary financing
received from the Developer.
Attorney Friedman continued that the Village will not convey any property to
the Developer until all of the Certificates of Occupancy for the development’s
new units have been issued. The Developer has 90 days to submit building permit
applications (with two 90-day extensions); and the Village is entitled to hire
an on-site plan compliance inspector, with the Developer paying the first
$30,000 of that expense. Construction is required to commence within 60 days of
the issuance of building permits and must be substantially complete within 24
months after issuance. Once construction begins, the development must comply
with all approval documents, plans, construction schedules, staging, and
seismic monitoring of adjacent buildings. If the development lags, or if any
schedules or deadlines are not met, the Village may draw the letter of credit,
and call upon the third-party guarantor.
David Trandel, Project Developer, Stonestreet Partners, LLC. Mr. Trandel
thanked the Village for the very professional approval process for the One
Winnetka Planned Development application. Next, he introduced George Kisiel,
the Planning and Zoning manager for One Winnetka.
Mr. Kisiel presented the overall project changes, along with adjustments made
after the meetings over the summer with the ZBA, PC, and DRB. He explained that
the proposed development will now contain 58 residential units: 15 private
condominiums, 7 townhomes, 36 rental apartments, 29,895 square feet of retail
space, and 91 additional public parking spaces. He estimated the total
contribution of public benefits to be $7.3 million. He reviewed the development
site and subdivision plans, streetscape improvement plan, traffic impact, site
access, and elevation plans. He said the final amended plan would have a less
disruptive construction process, provide improved housing choices in Winnetka
and will revitalize the downtown with updated retail facilities.
President Rintz called for public comment, and he reminded the audience to be
respectful in its discourse; and to refrain from making personal attacks or
repeating rumors or innuendo. He also asked speakers that had any financial
interest in the proposed development to disclose that information.
Harvey Bundy, 740 Ardsley Road. Mr. Bundy said he had put a deposit on a
townhome, and commented that One Winnetka is ideal for empty nesters looking to
stay in Winnetka.
Wally Greenough, 500 Maple Street and ZBA member. Mr. Greenough said the
constraints put on the ZBA for the final approval process resulted in an
affirmative vote on the final amended project. He urged the Council to deny the
final application, as the delays have been caused by the developer, and
moreover the public benefit is reduced in the amended final plan.
Jack Coladarci, 568 Cherry Street. Mr. Coladarci questioned whether the parking
widths would accommodate larger vehicles like sport utility vehicles.
Peter Tyor, 711 Oak Street Condo Board President. Mr. Tyor asked the Council to
remember the interests of the families living at 711 Oak Street, and he urged
them to vote against the development.
Michael Levitin, 507 Cedar Street. Mr. Levitan questioned the parking valuation
and plans and asked the Council to conduct an independent evaluation and
appraisal of the portion of the Village parking lot that is being given to the
development.
Ewa Weir, Senior Vice President of JLL, on behalf of the Hadley Institute. Ms.
Weir requested that items from her October 2, 2018 letter pertaining to
landscaping; the parking lot retaining wall; sidewalk snowmelt system;
insurance certificate; and construction scheduling; to be codified in the
Development Agreement.
Phil Hoza, 605 Cherry Street. Mr. Hoza urged the Council to approve the project
as the application has been in process for three years.
Walter Sobol, For Fells Future. Mr. Sobol urged the Council to preserve the
Fell Building, which his father designed and which won an architecture award,
and he asked how historical requirements will be dealt with in the demolition
permit application.
Richard Metzler, 120 Woodley Road. Mr. Metzler urged approval of the project,
as it will benefit the Village as a whole, and added that he would like to buy
a unit there.
Rocky Flintermann, President of the Winnetka-Northfield Chamber of Commerce.
Mr. Flintermann expressed support of the One Winnetka development, as it will
bring new residents to the Village and provide updated commercial space.
Ann Wilder, Winnetka resident. Ms. Wilder posited that if restaurants in the
One Winnetka development have outdoor cafes on the public sidewalk that the
Developer is effectively getting more free space for its tenants.
Marc Hecht, Winnetka resident. Mr. Hecht called for Trustee Cripe to recuse
himself from the One Winnetka final approval process..
Vicki Hoffstetter, owner of Oui Madame. Ms. Hoffstetter urged approval of the
development, as it will bring new life to the Elm Business District.
There being no more public comment, President Rintz adjourned the meeting for a
short recess at 8:55 PM.
President Rintz called the meeting to order again at 9:05 PM, and called for
questions and comments from the Trustees.
Trustee Swierk commented that Village Staff and the ZBA, PC and DRB had
thoroughly reviewed the application and he only needed a clarification on the
building materials recommended by the DRB. He added that tighter parking spaces
are the new norm, and he had no reservations about the size of the
development’s parking spots.
Trustee Cripe explained that his law firm is a very large organization, and he
would refrain from commenting until he had investigated Mr. Hecht’s claim. He
noted he would recuse himself if there is even an appearance of conflict, to
best serve the interests of the community.
Trustee Wedner explained that when she ran for Trustee of the Village Council,
there was a huge desire expressed in the community to approve One Winnetka,
especially among the younger families.
President Rintz asked the project architect about the materials for the
balconies, as well as for the bottom expanse of the east elevation.
Lucien Lagrange, Project Architect. Mr. LaGrange explained that the ground
floor is stone, the second and third floors are brick, and the fourth level is
a different color brick. The balconies will be a precast material that looks
like stone and will have detailed railings, and the east elevation will be
concrete poured and painted to look like stone.
Trustee Dearborn explained that he did not feel that Winnetka is anti-
development; the project is taking a long to approve because the developer
amended that plan and is asking the Village for concessions. He wondered if the
parcel is being too heavily used and that space and functionality might not
work out. He also questioned the Developer’s public benefit estimates.
Mr. Trandel said the project does have mixed uses and competing interests, and
will have a building manager to oversee all the elements well. Mr. Kiesel
explained that his public benefit projections include not only streetscape, but
real estate and sales tax projections. He added that he would defer to Village
staff for the public benefit figures.
Trustee Dearborn said the DRB should have another look at the plans and define
the building materials and details; and he asked what the Hadley Institute
concerns are.
Louise Holland, Board Chair of the Hadley Institute. Ms. Holland said the east
façade of the proposed One Winnetka development looks out over the most
historic portion of the Village, and said Hadley’s concern is that concrete is
not similar to limestone, which was in the original application; she also said
Hadley would like to be considered and named in the development documents.
Mr. Schoon explained that materials would be addressed at the building permit
level once the development is approved.
President Rintz expressed concern about poured concrete for the east façade, as
it can be done wrong and have very negative effects. He noted that he would
like to see a material at that elevation that is respectful to the neighborhood
to the east, and he added that any motion to introduce the approval ordinance
should include that provision.
Trustee Myers commented that he has never worked with someone more ethical than
Trustee Cripe, and he expressed disappointment that something that could be
taken care of with a simple phone call would be handled in an open meeting. He
also expressed reservations about the east elevation, noted the development
needs to focus on improving the downtown atmosphere with quality design and
materials, and endorsed having the DRB approve modified materials.
After a discussion about the amended final plan sketches, the Developer
explained how the materials have changed as a result of an extensive value
engineering process. He noted that the focus was on changing materials below
ground and remaining true to the project drawings above-ground.
Trustee Lanphier had questions about the width of the parking spaces. Mr.
Schoon confirmed that the width of the spaces is the same in the final amended
plan as in the original application; and that the Village Engineer reviewed the
plans and removed some spaces where appropriate.
Trustee Lanphier asked about the new transformer location; whether garbage
collection is resolved; the traffic flow; deliveries to the businesses; and
asked that the east elevation be visually improved. She said all the details
must be set to the satisfaction of the Council before the development can be
approved.
Trustee Myers called for verification of materials and architectural details of
the amended design. He said the proposal is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan; and that density must be weighed against the need for downtown vibrancy
and the tax relief provided by adding more residents to the Village. He
explained that it is important the building be made with quality materials, and
if the DRB is involved in reviewing the changes, he would be in favor of
approval.
Trustee Dearborn felt that the burden of the amended final plan’s concessions
is falling on the shoulders of the Village and its residents, and that the
amended proposal is not as good as the original plan. He said the impact on the
Hadley Institute and area residents, as well as the loss of a portion of the
Village parking lot, is too much to ask for and the burden on the Village is
too large; and he would not be in favor of approval.
Trustee Wedner cautioned against seeking perfection in the final plan, as it is
impossible to achieve. She said Winnetka needs this type of housing, along with
modern retail facilities. She commented that the architecture is pleasing, and
is replacing a major eyesore. She felt confidence in the protections offered in
the approval Ordinance and Development Agreement, and said she supports the
final amended plan as a positive thing for Winnetka.
Trustee Swierk said the One Winnetka application has been through a rigorous
process with the ZBA, PC and DRB, and that the amount of real estate taxes the
development will generate will be substantial. He agreed that work on the east
elevation should be addressed by the DRB as the project moves forward, and he
expressed support for the project.
Trustee Lanphier said there is no question that the parcel needs to be
developed; however, she could not support the amended final plan, as she did
not feel it met the goals of the Village. She urged the Council to think about
changes to the planned development process for the future.
President Rintz agreed that the process took too long, largely due to the
difficulty of getting the necessary information from the applicant. He said it
is important to revitalize the downtown area, even if some residents don’t see
the value of it. Next he suggested that the motion to introduce the development
agreement contain the modifications discussed https://
deputyregistrar.cookcountyclerk.com/ by the Trustees.
Ordinance No. M-11-2018: An Ordinance Rezoning the Property Located at 710 Elm
Street to the C2 General Retail Commercial District and Portions to the C2
Commercial Overlay District (Introduction).
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
