Trustee Dearborn led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   February 12, 2019 Study Session. All of the Council members present
except Trustee Swierk said they expect to attend.
   b)   February 19, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
except Trustee Dearborn said they expect to attend.
   c)   March 5, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
Mr. Kaveh Mirani, owner of Mirani’s at Home, 567B Lincoln Avenue. Mr. Mirani
expressed concern about the disruption to the Lincoln Avenue business district
during the Winnetka Music Festival. He explained that during the event in 2018
his business suffered, since his delivery drivers did not have access to his
store. He asked the Council to find a way to avoid closing Lincoln Avenue
during this year’s festival.
President Rintz offered to arrange a meeting with the festival organizers and
Mr. Mirani, in anticipation of working out a compromise.
5)   Reports:
   a)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustees Lanphier and Cripe thanked Village staff for all their work
on snow removal and water main breaks during the extreme cold snap.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager. None.
   d)   Village President. President Rintz commended the Public Works
Department for its snow removal efforts in the commercial districts.
6)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   January 15, 2019 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated January 11 – 31, 2019 in the amount of
$1,175,529.28.
   c)   Resolution No. R-11-2019: Security for Construction on State Highways
(Adoption).
   d)   Resolution No. R-13-2019: Parkway Tree Trimming, Removal, and
Maintenance Contract (Adoption).
      Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the
foregoing items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustees Myers and Wedner.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-1-2019: Authorizing the Disposition of Surplus Personal
Property Owned by the Village of Winnetka (Introduction/Adoption). Chief
Hornstein explained that the majority of items in the disposal Ordinance are
standard obsolete equipment. However, he wished to explain the proposed
disposal of six Police Department firearms that are past their useful life. He
noted that the weapons can only be sold to a licensed firearms dealer, and will
not be purchased by a private citizen, as only another law enforcement agency
is qualified to purchase the weapons.
      There being no questions or comments from the audience or Council,
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-1-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Myers and
Wedner.
      Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No.
M-1-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Myers and Wedner.
   b)   Public Hearing: Rezoning of 1311 Scott Avenue.
      i)   Ordinance No. M-2-2019: 1311 Scott Avenue Annexation & Map Amendment
(Introduction/Adoption).
         President Rintz opened the public hearing at 7:21 p.m.
         Mr. Schoon briefly reviewed this matter, which was thoroughly
discussed at two previous Council meetings. The Council has also approved an
intergovernmental agreement with Glencoe pertaining to “split lots.”
         John Gurley, 1293 Scott. Mr. Gurley explained that he was in the
original group of residents that disconnected from Glencoe in 2006; however, he
still gets two tax bills, still has two PIN numbers for his property, and is
still paying taxes for the Glencoe Park District. He urged the Council to do
everything it can to get the situation straightened out.
         Trustee Lanphier noted that each taxing district has to act separately
to disconnect or annex the split lots on Scott Avenue.
         Manager Bahan said the Village will ensure the plat consolidations for
the lots have been filed, and will investigate the situation with the park
districts.
         Mr. Schoon explained that a letter sent to the Scott Avenue residents
in 2006 informed them that each taxing body would have to be dealt with
separately; this would account for the separate PIN numbers.
         Mr. Gurley offered to pay any associated legal fees if the Village
would take up the issue of the separate park district taxes and PINs.
         President Rintz closed the public hearing at 7:33 p.m.
         The Council discussed a resolution to the ongoing split lot problem,
and Manager Bahan said Village staff will work on a resolution.
         Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive
introduction of Ordinance No. M-2-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustees Myers and Wedner.
         Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt Ordinance
No. M-2- 2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Myers and Wedner.
   c)   Resolution No. R-12-2019: Approving an Agreement with L. Marshall Inc.
for Water Plant Roof Replacement (Adoption). Mr. Keys explained that the roof
replacement is part of the 2019 Capital Plan and Budget. He recommended the low
bidder to complete the project, as the Village has previous positive experience
with the vendor, L. Marshall Inc.
      There being no questions or comments from the audience or Council,
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
12-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Myers and Wedner.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
None.
