MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
March 12, 2019
(Approved: April 4, 2019)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
12, 2019, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Finance
Director Tim Sloth, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant Public
Works Director Jim Bernahl, Community Development Director David Schoon, and
approximately 13 persons in the audience.
Public Comment.
Pat Balsamo, 1037 Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo commended the Park District on the
public roll-out of its bluff restoration project; thanked the Village for a
fantastic Founder’s Day celebration on March 10; and stated the League of Women
Voters position regarding the District 36 referendum on the upcoming April 2
ballot.
Private Road Policy Discussion.
President Rintz explained that this would be a preliminary discussion to find
out what the Village’s legal obligations and limitations are, and to hear the
concerns of residents. He noted that the Ardsley request, which was received
last October, will not be addressed at this time, as the Council needs to put
the issue into historical and legal context before it can discuss next steps.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the request from the Ardsley/Pelham Road Association to
modify the policy on private streets in the Village. The Ardsley/Pelham
residents feel that as taxpayers they should receive the same services related
to roads that the rest of Winnetka’s residents do, and they also have questions
about the upgrading of private streets prior to dedication to the Village.
Next, Mr. Saunders reviewed the history of private roads in Winnetka, along
with the existing policy for dedicating private streets to the Village, which
was adopted in 1994. He said the issue of private roads was discussed by the
Village Council in 2008-09, but no action was taken. He noted that a major
obstacle to dedicating private streets is the requirement for unanimity among
the homeowners on the private road to do so.
The Council discussed some of the reasons why homeowners would want to remain
living on a private street: control over the street, discretion over design
standards, expenses related to upgrading for dedication. There was also
discussion about easements, costs of services per mile of street, cost per mile
to convert private roads to public ones, connections to storm sewers, and the
potentialy of creating zoning nonconformities on lots adjacent to private roads
when they are dedicated to the Village as public streets.
President Rintz asked Attorney Schuster to explain what the limitations are for
the Village to provide services to private roads.
Attorney Schuster explained that the Illinois Constitution prohibits public
bodies from spending public funds on private facilities, although incidental
private benefit is permissible – as in the case of a public/private
partnership. The Village would need to define a public benefit in order to
spend public funds on a private road; this could only be determined on a case-
by-case basis.
Next, President Rintz invited Finance Director Tim Sloth to give an example of
what portion of a private road resident’s property tax bill is related to the
segment of the private road they own.
Mr. Sloth explained that the private road portion would be approximately 2-3%
of the property’s Equalized Assessed Value, and that the portion ultimately
being paid to the Village is quite small when compared to the overall tax bill.
Trustee Wedner thought the more relevant number is the total tax payment to the
Village, since those funds are used on public roads throughout Winnetka. She
added that private road residents pay for maintenance on their own street as
well as the public streets around them, which should be recognized as an injury
of sorts.
Trustee Myers observed that everyone pays to maintain State roads, whether they
drive on them or not; and residents of private roads do drive on public roads
in theVillage.
Trustee Cripe thought the amount of money spent to maintain the piece of street
in front of each house is too small and granular to be used as a basis for
making policy decisions about private roads.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Rob Apatoff, 730 Ardsley Road. Mr. Apatoff explained that he only spoke for the
Ardsley/Pelham Road Association. He outlined their problem as one of paying
more taxes and not enjoying services such as snow plowing and street repair/
maintenance. He said private streets add charm and attractiveness to the
Village, and his association does not wish to dedicate its street to the
Village if it means destroying this charm in the process of bringing it up to a
public standard. He said the association’s first choice would be to remain
private but to receive the same street services other homeowners do.
Trustee Dearborn said the idea of keeping the roads as they are is a good one;
however, the question is how the Village would justify taking on the
maintenance of private streets. President Rintz added that this was not
included in the 2019 Annual Budget.
The Council discussed the concept of amending the private road policy, and it
was agreed that the Council needs to gather more information about maintenance
costs and tax dollar amounts before an in-depth conversation can take place.
President Rintz called for additional public comment.
Steve Parker, resident of a private road. Mr. Parker explained that, more than
dollar figures, the issue was representation of residents on private roads. He
noted that demolitions and new home construction are very hard on roads;
however, the private roads do not receive a reimbursement from any of the
demolition permit funds specifically earmarked for road maintenance. He agreed
with Mr. Apatoff that most private road residents do not want the charm of
their roads destroyed by bringing them up to the public standards. He added
that some help with snow plowing and the ability to get favorable pricing when
the Village awards road maintenance bids would be very helpful to those on
private roads.
[Trustee Cripe left the meeting at 8:30 PM)
President Rintz noted that speed bumps make snow plowing very difficult. Mr.
Parker responded that at least half of private roads do not have speed bumps.
In addition, there are several public streets which are not at the required
width the Village specifies for upgrading private roads, and some of them do
not have sidewalks and curbs.
Attorney Schuster explained that the Village would have to be very careful when
spending public funds on private property, no matter how small the amount; it
is necessary to show a public benefit in order to meet the constitutionality
test. He said he could research the issue and develop guidelines on exactly
what can and cannot be done relative to helping private roads with Village
money.
Nancy Eisensen, Hoyt Lane. Ms. Eisensen said there are five homes on her
private road and they contract for private snow plowing services. She asked the
Village to help them with plowing and putting down road salt.
Liz Kunkle, Holly Lane. Ms. Kunkle said her private road has four homes, and
she was unaware it was a private road when she bought her house. She said she
would like some collaboration from the Village and equitable treatment.
Michael Cannman, DeWindt Road Association President. Mr. Cannman said
stormwater is the issue for his association, since the existing storm sewer
system is 100 years old. He said his association has put in stormwater
improvements and a pumping system; however, they need a place to pump the water
to.
Debbie Gersh, Private Road. Ms. Gersh said the goal should be cooperation,
compromise and equity. She encouraged the Council to think about the benefits
to the community that private roads provide. She said there are a lot of
drawbacks for private road residents, such as no parking/accident enforcement.
Trustee Myers said the two themes heard were: (i) residents on private roads
would like to participate in the buying power of the Village to achieve
economies on road paving and other maintenance; and (ii) these residents also
want the Village to maintain the private roads if it is legal to do so. He
added that he thought there was value to maintaining the character of the
private roads; and maybe the private road policy could be amended to maintain
some aspects of that character when a road goes public. He noted that some
private roads are used more like public roads, but others are really private
roads; and that distinction needs to be taken into consideration.
Trustee Lanphier agreed that many private roads are used by the general public,
and wondered aloud how that could be factored as a public benefit. She also
brought up the question of liability on a private road if the Village takes on
maintenance responsibilities.
President Rintz noted that issues get solved when they are discussed out in the
open and differing points of view are heard. He said the State Constitution
limits the Village’s options as to what it can do on private roads; and he
added that it might be worthwhile to schedule another conversation after staff
has had some time to gather more information and provide deeper context for the
next discussion.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:02 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
March 19, 2019
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
March 19, 2019
(Approved: April 4, 2019)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, March
19, 2019, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees
Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and Anne Wedner.
Absent: Trustee Andrew Cripe. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Community Development Director David Schoon, Director of Water & Electric Brian
Keys, and approximately 15 persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Wedner led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
April 4, 2019 Rescheduled Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
said they expect to attend.
April 9, 2019 Study Session. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
April 16, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Connie Henry, 1111 Merrill. Ms. Henry wished to comment on the District 36
school funding referendum. She also asked the Village to coordinate with the
other taxing bodies to reign in property taxes for residents.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Swierk thanked the Winetka Police Department for sending a
representative to the funeral of McHenry County Sheriff's Deputy Jacob Keltner,
who was murdered on March 7.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. President Rintz referred to an article in Crain’s Chicago
Business about ComEd’s request to raise electricity rates, and he noted that
Winnetka’s power plant gives residents freedom from such rate hikes.
Approval of the Agenda. Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to
approve the Agenda. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
March 5, 2019 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated March 1-14, 2019 in the amount of $678,207.57.
Annual Outdoor Seating Permits.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Cripe.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Public Hearing: Zoning Amendments.
Code Amendments for Proposed Banquet Facility at 915 Green Bay Road (to be
continued to April 16). Attorney Friedman explained that this Public Hearing
would be continued to April 16, 2019.
Public Hearing: Zoning Amendments.
Ordinance No. MC-1-2019: Commercial District Zoning Ordinance Amendments
(Public Hearing & Introduction). President Rintz opened the Public Hearing at
7:15 PM.
Mr. Schoon summarized the 12-month process of discussing the Retail Overlay
District, and reviewed amendments proposed by the Council in its final
deliberations on January 15, 2019. The revisions include changes to the
boundaries, uses and some minor text changes to improve readability.
President Rintz called for public comment. There being none, the Council
discussed the proposed amendments. At issue was a requirement for special uses
to provide a display or other feature that would be compatible with the
adjacent retail windows. There was concern that once a special use is granted,
the applicant does not always deliver on promised concessions like active
display windows or pop-up retail stores.
The discussion next turned to the problem of a building owner signing a lease
with a tenant for a use that is not allowed by right. Mr. Schoon explained that
property owners and businesses are informed of new zoning rules when they are
adopted. Attorney Friedman noted that the landlord is liable for abiding by the
Village’s Zoning Ordinance.
President Rintz suggested that every business be required to obtain a business
license from the Village before opening. This would give the Community
Development Department a chance to engage with them and catch any nonconforming
uses.
Manager Bahan explained that the Village’s current process typically catches
nonconforming uses before they go in. He noted that business licensing was
discussed several years ago, and it was decided not to put another regulatory
hurdle in the way of new businesses. He added that the Village could send
annual or semi-annual reminders to the landlords.
Next, there was a discussion about fast food vs. fast casual and co-working
spaces. Mr. Schoon was requested to tweak the definitions to clarify and ensure
no fast food establishments would be permitted by right; and to make it clear
that co-working spaces are collaborative, temporary workspaces.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-1-2019. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Resolution No. R-28-2019: 1025 & 1035 Sheridan Road - Amendment to CS Lakeside
Consolidation Final Plat (Adoption). Mr. Schoon explained that after the plat
for the Subject Property was approved last December, the applicant worked with
neighbors to improve easements for ingress/egress and utilities. Since the
final plat was never recorded, it can be revised if the Council approves doing
so.
There being no Council discussion or public comment, Trustee Myers, seconded by
Trustee Swierk, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-28-2019. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Cripe.
Resolution No. R-29-2019: Approving a Change Order to the Contract with The
Progress Group for the Repair of Unit #6 Turbine and Generator (Adoption). Mr.
Keys reviewed the results of an inspection of Turbine #6. The cost overrun is
the result of two significant unplanned issues: (i) a problem with the rotor
winding; and (ii) replacement of three rows of turbine blades. The total cost
of the overhaul would now be $763,830 – which exceeds the authorized and
budgeted amounts for this project.
Mr. Keys warned there is a possibility, albeit low, that further problems could
be discovered once the unit is disassembled. He explained the unit is scheduled
to be out of service until May 1; after that time, the Village will experience
a reduction in capacity credits from IMEA. He added that the capacity credits
help the Village to keep its wholesale electric rates very competitive with
ComEd.
President Rintz observed that the Electric Fund’s current reserve exceeds the
fund balance by several hundred thousand dollars. Mr. Keys agreed the fund
balance will not be harmed with the extra drawdown for the unforeseen repairs.
There being no audience comment or further discussion, Trustee Wedner, seconded
by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-29-2019. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Cripe.
Resolution No. R-31-2019: Rejecting Bids, Waiving Bidding, and Awarding a
Contract for the Northfield Load Center Fire Protection Design-Build
(Adoption). Mr. Keys explained that the Village issued a Request for Bids for
the design and engineering of improvements to the fire protection system at the
Northfield Load Center. Two responses were received; however, neither bid
contained favorable pricing.
Mr. Keys said staff was able to negotiate with one of the firms, Orr Protection
Systems, to complete the project for an amount not to exceed $59,195. Staff
recommends rejecting the bids, and approving a contract with Orr Protection
Systems for the design and engineering of a fire protection system at the
Northfield Load Center.
There being no audience comment or Council discussion, Trustee Lanphier,
seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-31-2019. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers,
Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Cripe.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
None.
Appointments.
None.
Closed Session.
Trustee Dearborn moved to adjourn into Closed Session to discuss Collective
Bargaining, pursuant to Section 2c(2) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Myers seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Cripe.
President Rintz announced that the Council would not return to the open meeting
after Executive Session. The Council adjourned into Executive Session at 8:25
p.m.
Adjournment.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:58 p.m.
______________________________
    Recording Secretary
April 4, 2019
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
RESCHEDULED REGULAR MEETING
April 4, 2019
(Approved: April 16, 2019)
A record of a legally convened rescheduled regular meeting of the Council of
the Village of Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on
Thursday, April 4, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Community Development Director David Schoon, Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky, Deputy
Fire Chief John Ripka, and approximately 13 persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Myers led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
April 9, 2019 Study Session. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
April 16, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present with the
exception of President Rintz said they expect to attend.
May 7, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Jessie Butler, 960 Old Green Bay Road. Ms. Butler expressed concerns with a
proposed banquet facility at 915 Green Bay Road allowed by right in the
business district. She noted that there could be noise issues for the
surrounding residents, as well as safety problems with the valet parking. She
asked that it be designated as a special use and undergo thorough review by the
required advisory bodies.
Pat Balsamo, League of Women Voters. Ms. Balsamo said the League’s board has
voted to recommend the Village of Winnetka offer a composting service to
residents to decrease the amount of refuse going to the landfill.
Reports:
Trustees. None.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. None.
Approval of the Agenda. President Rintz suggested moving Item 10, New Business,
to be discussed after Item 7, Consent Agenda. Trustee Wedner, seconded by
Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the Agenda as amended. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
March 12, 2019 Study Session.
March 19, 2019 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated March 15-29, 2019 in the amount of $911,254.72.
Resolution No. R-30-19 2019: Sidewalk and Curb Replacement Program Contract
Award (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-33-2019: Approving a Professional Services Agreement with
Strand & Associates for Specification of Switchgear and Breakers at Northfield
Substation (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-34-2019: Approving a Class A-1 Liquor License for FFMM, Inc.
d/b/a George Trois & Aboyer (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-35-2019: Approving 2019 - 2022 Agreement between the Village
of Winnetka and the International Association of Firefighters (Adoption).
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
[These items were discussed after Item 10, New Business.]
Ordinance No. MC-1-2019: Commercial District Zoning Ordinance Amendments
(Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed amendments to the Subject Ordinance, pursuant
to Council discussion during introduction at the March 19 Regular Meeting:
The definition of co-working spaces was clarified, and reference to fabrication
tools has been eliminated.
A standard for special uses which deals with interior display windows was
amended to include the word “active.” Staff recommends setting more specific
conditions for display windows on a case-by-case basis, since each special use
application is unique.
At the request of a Winnetka commercial building owner, the effective date of
the Subject Ordinance would be 90 days from its passage, to allow building
owners to finalize ongoing prospective tenant negotiations under the current
zoning regulations.
Leslie Stevens, owner of 950 Green Bay Road. Ms. Stevens explained that she has
owned a building in the commercial district for many years, and that changes to
the Retail Commercial Overlay make it confusing for owners. She noted that she
had asked for a 90-day period before the new zoning amendments take effect, as
she was in the process of renting to a nail salon; the use will be affected by
the new Ordinance. She also suggested contacting building owners via certified
mail, since it is hard to track some of them down.
Trustee Cripe asked her if 90 days would be enough time for her tenant, and
does the business have to be open in that timeframe. Attorney Friedman
explained that the submittal of a building permit application within 90 days
would be sufficient.
President Rintz expressed frustration with Special Use permit holders who do
not follow through with the conditions of their permit. Trustee Myers said his
understanding is the Council has authority to impose sanctions or revoke a
Special Use permit, but that it has never been employed.
Attorney Friedman explained that each Special Use Permit approval Ordinance
gives the Council authority to revoke the permit if the conditions are not met,
or to impose fines if the Council doesn’t wish such drastic action as revoking
a permit. In addition, each permit holder signs an agreement, which is an
exhibit to the adoption ordinance, to abide by the conditions.
The Council discussed sanction options, with a general preference to notify
permit holders of these conditions, and to begin to use its enforcement
options. President Rintz requested Village staff to serve notice on Coldwell
Banker that they are in violation of their Special Use Permit, and must come
before the Council to cure their deficiency. Mr. Schoon explained that the
Village will develop a program to follow up with special uses, both immediately
afterwards, and over the length of the permit.
Next, the Council discussed personal fitness facilities as a permitted use in
the C-1 Commercial Districts.
Trustee Lanphier suggested refining the category of “personal fitness facility”
since although one-on-one training wouldn’t be a big problem with area traffic,
a fitness facility with larger classes could impact the area’s traffic and
parking patterns.
Mr. Schoon explained that personal fitness facilities had been split from
health clubs (larger than 2500 square feet) in previous Council discussions;
and although the Council didn’t specifically discuss how to treat the C-
1 Commercial District, staff made the call not to allow personal fitness
facilities since health clubs are already prohibited.
After a brief discussion, the Council agreed to allow personal fitness
facilities as a special use in the C-1 Commercial District.
Trustee Swierk suggested sending the adopted Ordinance to the commercial
property owners. Mr. Schoon noted that plans are underway to send new notices
to owners.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-1-
2019.
Trustee Myers moved to amend Ordinance No. MC-1-2019: (i) to take effect 90
days from its date of passage, so a building permit application submittal
within the 90-day window is considered within the deadline for operating under
the current commercial zoning provisions; and (ii) to allow personal fitness
studios as a special use in the C-1 Commercial District. Trustee Cripe seconded
motion to amend. By roll call vote, the motion passed. Ayes: Trustees Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Wedner amended her motion to adopt Ordinance No. MC-1-2019 to include
the two foregoing amendments; Trustee Cripe seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers,
Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-2-2019: Planned Development Amendments (Public Hearing &
Introduction). President Rintz opened the Public Hearing at 8:17 PM. Mr. Schoon
reviewed the Council process on Planned Development (PD) amendments, after
which President Rintz called for public comment.
Jack Coladarci, Cherry Street. Mr. Coladarci asked what would happen when a
term of one of the appointed Planned Development commissioners expired?
Attorney Friedman explained that a new commissioner would be appointed from the
Zoning Board of Appeals (ZBA) or Plan Commission (PC).
There being no further public comment, President Rintz closed the public
hearing at 8:25 PM.
Next, President Rintz called for Council questions.
Trustee Myers asked how the Council could ensure the training for each PD
commissioner.
Manager Bahan explained that it would be an administrative procedure, and
Village staff will work with Attorney Friedman to develop the training.
President Rintz asked why a 3D model is required for a PD application
submittal. Mr. Schoon explained that it could be electronic, not necessarily a
full-blown model. Trustee Swierk asked that the word “model” be changed to
“rendering.”
Trustee Swierk asked if the quorum of the PD Commission (PDC) would be five
members; Attorney Friedman responded that it would. President Rintz suggested
changing the quorum to seven members, as five seemed to low; the Council agreed
to change it to seven.
Trustee Lanphier said she was not comfortable with a nine-member PDC, and
recommended a larger group. She suggested continuing the public hearing on the
PD Ordinance amendments until the Council has studied the issues with the
current Ordinance more thoroughly. She also called for more public engagement
on the review of the PD process.
The Council discussed these suggestions; however, it was generally agreed that
interested residents are aware of the Council’s PD review through E-Winnetka,
watching meeting videos on the Village’s website, and contacting their Trustees
individually. It was also mentioned that the Council is elected to represent
the community based upon expertise that residents may not possess. In this case
the Council is establishing a process with the aim of providing more public
engagement, not less.
President Rintz said a compromise might be to re-open the public hearing and
continue it until the April 16 Council meeting, to provide extra time for
public comment. The Ordinance would, however, still be up for adoption at the
April 16 meeting.
Trustee Dearborn felt that creating a joint PDC removed input from the members
of the ZBA and PC who are not on the PDC. He added that he was not sure the
current PD process is broken, as developers expect some redundancy in the
application process. In addition, Trustee Dearborn objected to certain language
in Section 3.22.030(#3) of the proposed PD ordinance limiting membership
turnover on the PDC.
Trustee Wedner explained that, from a resident perspective, there had been
fatigue with the length of the One Winnetka process, and many people simply
dropped out of participating in the meetings. She said having fewer meetings
would provide opportunity for more impactful public participation, as there
would be less superfluous information being presented.
Trustee Cripe said the process to amend the PD Ordinance has been very well-
informed. In addition, residents trust the Council to use its expertise and
best judgement where this particular policy is concerned. He noted that the
process is not broken; however, it needs improvement. The ZBA and PC have
different standards, which makes review of a PD application very confusing. The
overall goal is to determine whether a development is good for Winnetka, and to
create an improved process that gets to that goal in a logical and expedient
fashion. He cautioned against striving for perfection in making policy, and to
instead achieve a viable result, since policy can be revisited any time the
need arises.
Trustee Myers said in his experience on both the ZBA and PC, the conversation
about PDs is whether they are fundamentally right for the community. He
explained that the necessary interplay of the ZBA and PC standards will come to
fruition on the PDC, where experts from both boards can deliberate in the same
room.
President Rintz recalled that the discussions about amending the PD Ordinance
started in 2013 with the Urban Land Institute study and recommendations; and
the Downtown Master Plan also recommended furthering that goal. The present
conversation is intended to mitigate the perception that Winnetka is a
difficult place to do business. He explained that the idea behind amending the
PD process is to make it more accessible and efficient; however, that does not
mean standards will be eased or public conversation restricted. He noted that a
forum for intelligent goal-directed conversations needs to be created in order
to attract the best developers to the Village. Finally, he said he would defer
to Trustee Lanphier’s request and re-open the public hearing for one more round
of public engagement on the issue.
President Rintz re-opened the Public Hearing at 9:05 PM, and then continued the
hearing to the April 16 Council meeting.
Trustee Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. MC-2-2019, and to continue the public hearing until April 16. By voice
vote, the motion carried.
Resolution No. R-32-2019: Approving an IGA with the Village of Glencoe for Fire
Inspection Services (Adoption). Chief Berkowsky explained that the Subject
Resolution approves an agreement Between the Villages of Winnetka and Glencoe
for the Winnetka Fire Department to provide fire inspection services to
Glencoe. The contract is similar to an existing arrangement with the Village of
Northfield, where Winnetka also provides fire inspection services.
There being no further Council or public comment, Trustee Myers, seconded by
Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-32-2019. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and
Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
[This item was discussed after approval of the Consent Agenda.]
1309 Trapp Lane - Variation (Policy Direction). Mr. Schoon explained that the
Subject Property is a through-lot that fronts on both Holly and Trapp Lanes,
and as such is bound under the Zoning Ordinance to observe two front yard
setbacks. In addition, an average minimum setback is required, which is
calculated by averaging the lot frontage of the lots on Trapp Lane. The
applicant proposes to construct an in-ground swimming pool which will meet the
minimum front yard setback, but not the average minimum setback. The request
for a zoning variation was heard by the Zoning Board of Appeals (ZBA); however,
the Board could not come to a consensus favor of the variation.
Mark Gent, the owner of the Subject Property, explained that the home was built
in 2012, and he was not aware at that time of the average minimum setback
requirement. He noted that 75% of the lot space is covered by setbacks, and he
explained that re-orienting the pool to a conforming location would kill a
large honey locust tree, which he wishes to avoid.
Responding to questions, Mr. Gent next explained that the proposed length of
his pool was calculated based upon a goal of having a shallow end at 2 ½ feet
since his children are currently very young; and a deep end which will be safe
for diving when the children are older. The standards for the pool slope based
upon these considerations add to the length of the pool. He said a neighbor on
his street has a legally nonconforming setback that is six feet to the north of
his proposed setback.
Trustee Swierk noted that the neighbor who commented at the ZBA hearing was
concerned about stormwater impacts of the proposed pool.
President Rintz called for audience comment.
Liz Kunkle, 1303 Holly Lane. Ms. Kunkle said she represented the residents on
Holly Lane; stormwater is their major concern, as all private roads bear
responsibility for their own stormwater systems. She explained that the
homeowners want to ensure that the Village’s stormwater guidelines and
standards are observed by the proposed pool development. The homeowners’
concerns will be alleviated if the Council and Village are comfortable with the
plan and the drainage requirements.
Mr. Schoon explained that the Village will review Mr. Gent’s plans to make sure
they comply with drainage and stormwater requirements. He noted that the home
does not come close to meeting the maximum impervious surface limit, even with
the proposed pool improvement.
Trustee Lanphier asked if there were any other areas in the Village that would
be comparable to this neighborhood; and she commented that the average setback
calculation leaves a very small building envelope on this parcel. Mr. Schoon
was unsure of other areas that might be comparable. He said a through lot
usually has a predominant street frontage line; and the homes are generally in
alignment.
Trustees Wedner, Cripe, Myers and Swierk were in favor of granting the
variation, as the unusually small buildable area of the lot constitutes a
unique circumstance.
Trustee Dearborn was in favor of the applicant removing the deck at the north
end of the pool, which the applicant was amenable to doing, in order to have
the entire pool facility further removed from Holly Lane. Most Trustees did not
see the purpose in this, as the pool deck is not included in the variance
request as the proposed deck is allowed in the required setback. .
Trustee Lanphier said she would encourage the applicant to shorten the pool
since the home is in the flood plain, although she generally supported the
variation request.
Since five of the Council members were in favor of granting the request as it
is, Attorney Friedman was directed to prepare an approval ordinance.
Appointments:
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to approve Lynn Hanley to a
full term on the Zoning Board of Appeals to replace Mark Naumann, effective
immediately. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:15 p.m.
___________________________________
    Recording Secretary
April 9, 2019
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
April 9, 2019
(Approved: May 7, 2019)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
9, 2019, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Public
Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant Public Works Director Jim Bernahl,
Community Development Director David Schoon, and approximately three persons in
the audience.
Public Comment.
None.
Elm Phase I Streetscape Update.
President Rintz reviewed the public process leading up to adoption of the
Downtown Streetscape and Signage Master Plan, and the Council’s approval to
implement Phase I of the Plan, scheduled for late summer 2019. He explained
that he preferred a mid-point review of the engineering plans, since it is
easier to revise them at this stage if necessary.
Jodi Mariano, Teska Associates. Ms. Mariano reviewed the streetscape elements,
which are designed to provide more space for pedestrians and make the downtown
more inviting. She reviewed proposed plantings, lighting, tree grates,
planters, bike racks, benches, signs, and the directory kiosks.
Dwayne O’Laughlin, Ciorba. Mr. O’Laughlin reviewed the turning radius for
vehicles at intersections and parking stalls, explaining that a widely-used
software, Autoturn, was used for the engineering plans. He noted that the
length and width of the parking stalls exceed most of the commonly accepted
criteria for parking geometrics. After he reviewed the lighting plan, he said
the next step in the process will be to hold a construction phasing open house
to inform the public and get their feedback.
The Council discussed the parking plan in detail, and asked about solar powered
lighting. Mr. O’Laughlin explained that the technology for commercial solar is
not yet up to par, since there is no way to deal with extended days when it is
cloudy. He confirmed that new sewers and water mains will be installed, and
Chestnut Street will be resurfaced.
The Council discussed the various streetscape elements; more bike racks were
requested, along with trees that do not drop pods and seeds.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Ted Wynnychenko, Oak Street. Mr. Wynnychenko had the following comments: (i) he
approved of the parking bumpout on the north end of Chestnut street in front of
Fred’s Garage, as it will slow drivers down and improve pedestrian safety; (ii)
the standard 9-foot parking stall is too small for people who have difficulty
parking their large SUVs, and who will therefore take two parking spaces; (iii)
he was disappointed that the plan is not more focused on stormwater control
using such best practices as permeable pavement; and (iv) he recommended
getting rid of the festoon lights and poles as an unnecessary expense.
President Rintz explained that the new parking stalls will be larger than the
current ones, which are not up to standard. He added that the Post Office
parking lot is being dealt with on a temporary basis, as the parcel will
eventually be redeveloped, making the expense of a temporary permeable system
financially unsound.
Mr. Saunders added that installing permeable pavers in the business districts
would be intensely disruptive to the businesses; however, an alternate estimate
for the cost of permeable pavers in the intersections could be sought. He noted
that the Village of Kenilworth is not entirely pleased with the permeable
pavers it has installed on some public streets, and he is monitoring the
development of the permeable paver technology for signs of improvement in wide-
scale use.
The issue of stormwater remediation in conjunction with the streetscape project
was discussed. Trustee Myers explained that the Downtown Master Plan Task Force
discussed the issue; however, it was determined that the negative impact of a
long-term street construction project puts too large a burden on the affected
businesses.
President Rintz noted that the Council must walk a line between experimenting
with public dollars on untested infrastructure, and making wise investments
that will last.
Jack Coladarci, Cherry Street. Mr. Coladarci said he favored the yellow
spectrum of lighting for the festoon lights since it is warmer, and he wondered
if there would be a scale problem between the overhead street lighting and the
festoon poles. Lastly, he said he had researched pavers, as the Village would
not want to repeat the problem of crumbling pavers that occurred with its last
street paver installation.
Ms. Mariano confirmed that the engineering team is working with lighting
manufacturers to get the low end of the lighting scale, which is a warm yellow
light. She added that the overhead light will not in a position to overhang the
festoon lights.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village has learned that clay pavers last; the
previously used pavers were concrete, which fall apart over time.
Cary Michael, Fred’s Garage. Mr. Michael expressed concern about construction
scheduling.
Mr. Saunders said the open house on April 24 will allow business owners to give
feedback about construction phasing; the aim is not to affect any one business
for the entire construction period.
After a final discussion about the streetscape elements, a request was made to
find a way to measure the effectiveness of the streetscape project, either with
a parking count or using sales tax information. Manager Bahan explained that
sales tax information is only transmitted in broad aggregate and a single
business district cannot be separated out. He noted that the infrastructure in
the business districts, which is the bulk of the project cost, is at the end of
its lifespan and must be replaced.
Ms. Mariano said she would try to find studies about the efficacy of
streetscape improvements.
President Rintz suggested one more look at the plans on May 7 to confirm the
implementation plan and provide one last look before moving forward.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:48 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
April 16, 2019
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
April 16, 2019
(Approved: May 7, 2019)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, April
16, 2019, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Cripe called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present:
Trustees Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Scott Myers, and Anne Wedner.
Absent: President Chris Rintz and Trustee John Swierk. Also present: Village
Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney
Peter Friedman, Community Development Director David Schoon, Director of Water
& Electric Brian Keys, Police Chief Marc Hornstein, and approximately 30
persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Lanphier led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
April 25, 2019 Special Closed Session (5:30 PM). All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
April 25, 2019 Special Study Session (7:00 PM). All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
April 30, 2019 Special Closed Session. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
May 7, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Ted Wynnychenko, Oak Street. Mr. Wynnychenko expressed disappointment about the
lack of best stormwater practices in the Phase I streetscape improvements, and
he disagreed with the Village’s characterization of Kenilworth’s
dissatisfaction with its permeable paver system. He said he contacted
Kenilworth officials, who said they were happy with the permeable pavers
installed, although the systems were not without their challenges. He also
pointed to the Village of Oak Park as a municipality that has installed green
alleys which are working as expected.
Composting Presentation. Mary Baillos and Allison Elli, students at New Trier
High School, explained that they had been researching composting and
environmental issues. They encouraged the Village to implement a composting
program, as food waste is the third largest contributor of greenhouse gasses.
Next, they presented three alternatives for a Village-wide composting program:
(i) a seasonal ride-along curbside program in conjunction with the collection
of yard waste; (ii) a year-round bucket pickup program from Collective
Resource, a private composting service based in Evanston; and (iii) a community
compost program, with collection bins at public facilities around the Village.
The two students also urged the Village to consider zero waste programs for
community events like the Music Festival, Fourth of July, etc. Finally, Ms.
Elli said they had gathered a petition with 240 signatures in support of a
Village-wide composting program, and she asked the Council to commit to
researching and implementing a composting plan for Winnetka.
Trustee Myers asked Ms. Baillos and Ms. Elli if they would be interested in
working on a zero waste program for the Winnetka Music festival, and the
Council discussed the three composting options presented.
Melissa Mizel, Tower Road and Liz Kunkle, Holly Lane. Ms. Mizel and Ms. Kunkle
both spoke in support of a Village-wide composting program.
Reports:
Trustees. None.
Attorney. None.
Manager. Manager Bahan reported that the Village is keeping an eye on pending
legislation in Springfield impacting lead service lines and legal recreational
cannabis.
Village President. None.
Approval of the Agenda. Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to
approve the Agenda. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
April 4, 2019 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated March 29 – April 11, 2019 in the amount of
$415,415.53.
Resolution No. R-36-2019: Approving the Purchase of a Horton Ambulance and
Stryker Powerload System (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-40-2019: Purchase of Police Patrol Vehicle (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-41-2019: Waiving Bidding and Approving Change Order No. 6 to
the Contract with B-Max Inc. for Electric Distribution System Work (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-42-2019: Approving an Easement Agreement for the Maintenance
of Utilities at Indian Hill Club (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-44-2019: Purchase of Televising Truck Software Updates
(Adoption).
Resolution No. R-45-2019: Approving an Easement Agreement for the Maintenance
of a Water Main at 70 & 77 Indian Hill Road (Adoption).
Outdoor Seating Permit: Adelheidi's of Winnetka, LLC.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, and Wedner. Nays:
None. Absent: President Rintz and Trustee Swierk.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. MC-2-2019: Planned Development Amendments (Public Hearing
Continuation). President Pro Tem Cripe explained that this public hearing would
again be continued, since President Rintz and Trustee Swierk are both absent.
Public Hearing:
Resolution No. R-37-2019: Approving a Banquet and Reception Hall at 915 Green
Bay Road (Public Hearing & Discussion). Mr. Schoon reviewed the request to
allow a banquet/reception hall, catering kitchen, café/specialty food store,
along with valet parking, in the Hubbard Woods business district. He said
several Village regulations must be amended in order to approve the use. Based
upon resident concerns about parking, the Village conducted a survey of the
Hubbard Woods parking deck, which showed that evenings and weekends have much
more open parking capacity than on weekdays.
Mr. Schoon went through the applicant’s parking plan, which would require the
use of valet parking for evening events; on-street/public lot parking for the
food store/café; on-street/public lot and possible valet parking for daytime
weekend events. He said the Village also has the option of creating a one-day
parking pass for patrons.
Mr. Schoon explained that the Council has discretion to allow the facility by
right on a one-time basis. Village staff has prepared the following Ordinances/
Resolutions based upon the one-time approval option: (i) Resolution No. R-37-
2019: Approving a Banquet and Reception Hall at 915 Green Bay Road; (ii)
Ordinance No. MC-3-2019: Adopting Parking Regulations; (iii) Resolution No. R-
38-2019: Establishing Valet Parking Permit Fees and Daily Banquet Parking
Permit Fees; (iv) Ordinance No. MC-4-2019: Amending Liquor Code to Create Class
Y; and (v) Resolution No. R-39-2019: Approving a Class Y Liquor License for
Boutique Bites.
The Ordinances are offered for introduction; the Resolutions are presented for
information purposes only, and will be submitted for adoption at the May 7,
2019 Council meeting, along with the two Ordinances. After Mr. Schoon reviewed
the provisions of the approval documents, President Pro Tem Cripe opened the
Public Hearing at 7:57 PM.
Elaina Vasquez, owner/applicant. Ms. Vasquez said she is working with an
architect to address noise concerns and pledged to be a good addition to the
Hubbard Woods business community. She explained that she has a very high-end
clientele, and there is a gap in the market on the North Shore. She described
her Hubbard Woods proposal as very similar to her current business in the West
Loop, where a mainstay of the business is bridal/baby showers and weddings. She
said she owns the tables, chairs, plates and silverware, and the only vendors
loading in the evenings would be the band/DJ and the florist, who are very
efficient and quiet.
Julie Erst, 983 Vine. Ms. Erst expressed concerns about valet and on-street
parking in her neighborhood, and noise from guests exiting an event late in the
evening.
Ed Gjertson, 900 Old Green Bay Road. Mr. Gjertson felt the notification to area
residents needs to be increased beyond 250 feet from the Subject Property;
expressed concerns about guest parking; and said noise is an important
consideration, since his home is directly behind the venue. He added that the
business would be a great addition to Hubbard Woods but urged the Council to
use the special use process to make sure more voices are heard.
Peter Butler, Old Green Bay Road. Mr. Butler opposed approving the application
on a one-time basis; expressed concerns about noise; and called for more public
input through the special use process.
Jessie Butler, Old Green Bay Road. Ms. Butler supported the café concept, but
cautioned against approving a banquet venue that operates until midnight. She
asked the Council to consider the neighbors, especially residents in apartments
above and across the street from the facility. She said Hubbard Woods is very
crowded in the summer and is not the right area for such a business.
Maureen Black, 814 Prospect. Ms. Black expressed concerns about crowds of
people in the area after an event, and intoxicated guests getting into their
cars to drive home.
Ted Wynnychenko. Mr. Wynnychenko expressed support for the business as good for
the community, and he commented that the fear of consequences always surfaces
when something new is proposed.
Joanie Vitzel, property manager of 925 Green Bay Road. Ms. Vitzel opposed the
request on the grounds that tenants in adjacent apartments will be negatively
affected by noise from the banquet venue, and commented there is not enough
parking in the area to support the business model.
Mead Montgomery, 945 Green Bay Road. Mr. Montgomery said notification of area
residents needs to be expanded and he posited that the number of ordinances and
resolutions needed to approve the application is a red flag. He expressed
concern about potential noise and parking issues.
Jack Coladarci, Cherry Street. Mr. Coladarci said it is possible that too many
businesses in Hubbard Woods rely on the parking deck, which could send
customers out into the neighborhoods to park. He asked if there is a way to
soundproof the banquet space to cut down on the noise for tenants in the
neighboring apartments.
David and Susan Schwartz, owners of 913 Green Bay Road. Mr. Schwartz said he
shares many of the previously expressed concerns. He noted that the 915 Green
Bay Road building has a skylight that will make it impossible to soundproof the
space, and the noise will affect his tenants. He also expressed concerns about
parking, intoxicated guests exiting from events, and people smoking outside the
venue.
Terry Dason, Chamber of Commerce Director. Ms. Dason urged approval of the
application. She said the Village is fortunate to have a business like Boutique
Bites interested in moving in, and Ms. Vasquez has a stellar reputation. She
said the North Shore has a dearth of facilities for wedding/bridal showers and
other events, and residents wind up spending their money in other communities.
She said Minos is one of the busiest restaurants in the area and there has
never been a noise complaint from their large outdoor space. She noted that
food businesses add vibrancy and bring other new businesses to a community, and
she said a lesson learned from the Winnetka Music Festival is that there is no
issue with intoxicated people roving the streets.
Jeffrey Liss, Winnetka resident. Mr. Liss said the soundproofing will help
solve noise issues in the venue. He added that although there was a big
community controversy over allowing a nearby McDonald’s to open, it has turned
out to be an asset to the community. He urged a swift approval process for
people looking to invest in Winnetka.
Trustee Wedner thanked the residents for coming out to speak their minds, as
the Council welcomes community participation. She noted that the Village needs
to maintain a vibrant community in the face of empty storefronts and revenue
losses. With proper care, the issues of noise and parking can be resolved and
the area needs the vitality this new business will provide. She said it is
natural to worry about a new use; however, this facility fills a need in
Winnetka for people who do not belong to private clubs for showers, weddings,
and other parties.
Trustee Dearborn asked what was being done to address noise concerns.
Ms. Vasquez explained that since the Letter of Intent process was protracted,
an architect has only recently been engaged. They are working on a plan for
noise elimination, and the skylight is being investigated. She pointed out that
there are no windows in the building except for the front window of the café,
which will be insulated; and that the building is made of brick, which is not a
good conductor of sound. She noted that concerns about 150 guests exiting a
wedding reception at midnight are unfounded. She described how typically by the
end of dinner around 9:00 PM, people start drifting out, and by midnight there
are rarely more than 30 guests still at the celebration. In addition, her
practice at her current business is to staff two doormen at wedding receptions
to control guests and keep order.
Trustee Lanphier suggested the music could stop before midnight, and the valet
parking be structured to not begin before 5:00 PM. She also called for specific
noise standards above what the general nuisance standards provide.
Trustee Myers explained that the Council has been working for several years to
recognize the impact that changes in shopping habits have on Winnetka and what
to do about it. The empty storefronts are testament to these changes; and
services are now a vital component of a vibrant business district. He expressed
concerns about parking and noise; however, he agreed that at a typical North
Shore wedding most of the guests have left by 10:00 PM and furthermore, most
guests will use ride-sharing services. He called for more specific noise
standards to buffer apartments that are very close to the venue, and rules for
valet parking that ensure residential streets are not used. He added that a
penalty should be devised if the rules aren’t followed. Finally, he expressed
confidence the business will work if the few issues are worked out.
Trustee Dearborn said Ms. Vasquez has a good reputation as proprietor of her
West Loop business, and that her business in Hubbard Woods would be well
received. After noting that the site has been empty for six years, he added
that there is a void for this type of business in Winnetka. The Village has
responsibility to hear the concerns of residents, and to monitor and enforce
conditions associated with approval of the business. He said the noise issues
need to be worked out, and oversight will be needed to ensure that neighbors
are not being imposed on.
Trustee Lanphier suggested the request should be going through the special use
process, and not being dealt with by the Council. She agreed that more notice
needs to be given to residents, and she requested that the valet parking comes
back for further review before it is approved. She called for a parking plan,
to ensure that guest parking does not spill over into the neighborhoods.
Manager Bahan explained that if the applicant submits a noise plan from her
architect, the Boutique Bites request will be on the Council’s May 7 agenda for
discussion and potential approval.
Ordinance No. MC-3-2019, Adopting Parking Regulations (Introduction) and
Resolution No. R-38-2019, Establishing Valet Parking Permit Fees and Daily
Banquet Parking Permit Fees (Information Only).
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to introduce Ordinance No. MC-
3-2019. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. MC-4-2019: Amending Liquor Code to Create Class Y (Introduction).
Chief Hornstein reviewed the amendments required to the Liquor Code in order to
approve the Boutique Bites request. A new license classification was created to
cover all three components of the business: banquet/reception hall, specialty
food store/café and catering. He explained that if the Code amendment is
adopted, the approval of a potential liquor license would be contingent upon
completion of an applicant background check.
Maureen Black, 814 Prospect. Ms. Black asked if there were other liquor
licenses that allowed service of alcohol until midnight on New Year’s Eve.
Chief Hornstein said the Class A liquor licensees are permitted to serve until
midnight on weekends and until 1:00 AM on New Year’s Eve.
There being no further questions or comments, Trustee Dearborn, seconded by
Trustee Myers, moved to introduce Ordinance No. MC-4-2019. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
Resolution No. R-39-2019: Approving a Class Y Liquor License for Boutique Bites
(Information Only). President Pro Tem Cripe reiterated that this Resolution was
on the agenda for informational purposes and would be presented for adoption on
May 7.
Resolution No. R-43-2019: Authorizing the Reimbursement of the Village of
Glencoe for a Joint Water Study (Adoption). Manager Bahan explained that
Village staff is always looking for operational efficiencies; and the idea of a
joint water distribution system has been discussed informally with Glencoe over
the past 18 months.
Mr. Keys explained that the proposal to combine the two water systems needs a
professional feasibility study. He reviewed the scope of the study: (i)
evaluation of water plants; (ii) conceptual arrangement of water plants; (iii)
hydraulic analysis of the proposed combined system; (iv) evaluation of the
emergency interconnect; (v) opinion of probable costs to construct; and (vi)
final report and presentations to both villages.
Mr. Keys said Glencoe is taking the lead to contract with Strand, and Winnetka
will reimburse them for up to 50% of the study fee, not to exceed $34,955.
The Council was in favor of exploring a potential cost-saving measure for
Winnetka and thanked Village staff for their work.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-43-
2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Myers, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: President Rintz and Trustee
Swierk.
Old Business.
None.
New Business.
Metropolitan Mayors Caucus Greenest Region Compact 2. Liz Kunkle, Environmental
& Forestry (EFC) Commissioner, thanked her fellow EFC members and Public Works
Analyst Matt Havlik for their hard work in the review of the Greenest Region
Compact 2 (GRC2) goals and objectives. She said the three priorities that
emerged from the review of the GRC2 goals are: Waste, Water, and Energy. The
recommendations for each issue include:
Waste: (i) Adopt a pay-as-you-throw refuse program; (ii) support a composting
program and discourage use of single-use unrecyclable items; and (iii) reinvest
cost savings into sustainable initiatives and infrastructure.
Water: (i) Reduce stormwater runoff/contamination and encourage residents to
use best management practices on their properties; (ii) collaborate with other
agencies to enhance wetlands; and (iii) adopt Village codes to enable rainwater
harvesting and eliminate barriers to sustainable landscaping.
Energy: (i) Adopt energy efficiency measures for Village facilities where
feasible; and (ii) support adoption of renewable energy technologies and access
to alternative energy options for residents.
The EFC also recommends incorporating elements of the GRC2 into the Village’s
Comprehensive Plan to document Winnetka’s sustainability goals, and further
implementation plans.
The Trustees were enthusiastic about the hard work that went into the EFC’s
review process, and thanked the Commission for its efforts. It was mentioned
that the Village would need to know costs and return on investment before
implementing the recommendations.
Trustee Myers said the Village is already studying a “pay-as-you-throw” refuse
collection program, and zero waste events and rain barrels are within the realm
of feasibility. However, other initiatives - like reinvesting savings from
environmental programs back into the environment, and more renewable energy
sources - need more study.
President Pro Tem Cripe said each of the recommendations deserves its own Study
Session, since there is a lot involved in implementation. He said a dedicated
team of people will need to get items teed up for the Council, and there could
also be discussion about investing in environmental initiatives during Budget
review time each year.
Trustee Lanphier suggested the EFC focus on initiatives the Council is already
working on, like flood reduction and refuse collection; she added that Energy
is a long-range issue, and the focus for now should be on things that could be
done in the next few years.
President Pro Tem Cripe called for public comment.
Julie Erst, 983 Vine. Ms. Erst asked if the Village belongs to an energy
consortium that burns coal, and whether the Village has considered renewable
sources when the contract expires.
Manager Bahan explained that the current energy supply contract expires in
2040; he noted that the portfolio is evolving and that coal is not a part of
it; there are some renewable sources in the portfolio.
Jeffrey Liss, Winnetka Mews. Mr. Liss said all of the EFC’s recommendations are
good ones, and possibly the Council could rank them.
President Pro Tem Cripe explained that the EFC has already ranked them; now the
Village needs to find a way forward. He commended the EFC for the incredibly
hard work it has done on the GRC2 framework. Next, he polled the Council on the
EFC Proposed Work Plan:
Incorporate elements of the GRC2 into the Comprehensive Plan. All in favor,
with help from the EFC and without overwhelming staff.
Incorporate elements of the GRC2 into the EFC’s strategic plan. All in favor.
Pursue the three recommended environmental priorities, Waste, Water, Energy.
All in favor; the Council reordered them: #1 Water; #2 Waste; #3 Energy – and
the EFC should work with the Council on these priorities.
EFC to provide regular status reports towards meeting its goals. All in favor.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Myers, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 10:00 p.m.
