    Recording Secretary
April 25, 2019
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL SPECIAL MEETING
April 25, 2019
(Approved: May 7, 2019)
A record of a legally convened Special Meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Thursday,
April 25, 2019, at 6:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 6:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan and
0 persons in the audience.
Trustee Myers moved to adjourn into Closed Session to discuss Personnel,
pursuant to Section 2c(1) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Cripe
seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Rintz announced that the Council would return to the open meeting
after Closed Session. The Council adjourned into Closed Session at 6:02 p.m
Adjournment of Closed Session.
Trustee Myers, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to adjourn Closed Session. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The closed session adjourned at 6:55 p.m.
Reconvene to Open Session.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees
Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, Scott Myers, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Finance Director Tim Sloth, Assistant Finance Director Tony Vazquez, Community
Development Director David Schoon, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
Assistant Public Works Director Jim Bernahl, Communications Manager Josie
Clark, and approximately 9 persons in the audience
Trustee Myers led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Public Comment.
None.
Ordinance No. MC-2-2019: Planned Development Amendments (Public Hearing &
Adoption). President Rintz explained that on April 4, a Public Hearing held on
the Subject Ordinance was continued to ensure more time for public input.
Mr. Schoon reviewed amendments to the Subject Ordinance since introduction: (i)
the quorum for the proposed Planned Development Commission is increased from
five to seven members; (ii) the preliminary application must include a three-
dimensional model or rendering of the proposed planned development; and (iii)
to recommend approval, the affirmative vote of a majority of a quorum of the
entire Commission is required, rather than the members present.
Trustee Swierk suggested a minor adjustment in Section 17.58.070.H.7.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Wally Greenough, 500 Maple Street. Mr. Greenough commented that adding the step
of a preliminary conceptual review is a good amendment to the Planned
Development process, but that overall he did not feel major changes are needed.
He said the One Winnetka process was dragged out by the developer, and not the
process itself. The Special Use Process (SUP) requires applications to be heard
by both the Zoning Board of Appeals (ZBA) and Plan Commission (PC) - even minor
requests which are not controversial. He urged the Council not to pass the
Subject Ordinance, as he felt it would limit community input.
Louise Holland, 545 Oak Street. Ms. Holland recalled that during her tenure as
Village President, two planned developments were approved through a series of
meetings between the Village Manager, Village President and Developer, since
there was no PD Ordinance at the time. She said she felt the Subject Ordinance
limits community input. She pointed out that the Developer was the problem with
the One Winnetka application, as he was unprepared and kept making changes to
his proposal, and changing the PD process will not prevent bad developers from
pitching projects.
There being no further public comment, the Council discussed the Subject
Ordinance.
Trustee Myers explained that the proposed PD amendments came about not because
of the length of the One Winnetka process, but because flaws in the process
became apparent as it played out. He agreed that public input is very
important; and the proposed PD amendments do not put limits on public comment.
He said the most important consideration is the interplay of zoning and
planning, and having the two bodies consider an application separately does not
provide the best input. A PD Commission made up of members of both bodies will
understand all sides of the equation, will meet in public, and take the
community’s comments into consideration.
Next, Trustee Myers suggested a change to Section 3.22.030.B.3 to omit a phrase
for clarity, and eliminate the perception that the makeup of the Commission
should never change.
Trustee Lanphier felt the Council needs to do more research and hear more
public comment before enacting any substantial changes to the PD Ordinance.
While there are good elements in the Subject Ordinance, she felt the SUP and PD
processes should be considered in tandem, and the roles of the ZBA and PC
further clarified before making such a significant policy change. She suggested
tabling the section of the Subject Ordinance creating a PD Commission for
further study.
Trustee Swierk agreed that it was the developer who slowed down the One
Winnetka process; however, he disagreed that the proposed new PD process would
be more streamlined than the SUP process. He noted the PD Commission has a two
meeting minimum, and the developer is required to attend a pre-application
meeting with the Zoning Administrator and to give a concept review to the
Council. He supported combining members of the ZBA and PC on one PD commission.
Trustee Cripe said bad or challenging developers will always be a problem with
or without changes to the PD Ordinance; it is not the intent to see a bad
project fly through the PD process, and making a policy change based on the One
Winnetka application would be a bad idea. The objective is to improve a
procedure that does not clearly articulate the roles of the ZBA and PC and has
approval standards that are murky and conflicting. He said a combined PD
Commission will bring improved clarity to the process; and he noted that no
policy is written in stone, and can be tweaked at any time it is deemed
necessary.
Trustee Wedner agreed with the comments of Trustees Myers, Swierk, and Cripe in
support of the Subject Ordinance.
Trustee Dearborn said he could not support the creation of PD Commission, as he
felt input from the ZBA and PC is the most important part of the PD process. He
said without a guarantee that the PD Commission is a better system, he could
not support it. He suggested that a good development would flow through the
current process fairly quickly, and added that he did not mind some duplication
between the ZBA and PC, as it provides a more rigorous debate and fuller input
for the Council’s recommendation.
President Rintz said there is a misperception that the impact of the One
Winnetka application is driving this discussion; in fact, the issue of the PD
process was raised by the Urban Land Institute study and the Downtown Master
Plan Steering Committee. In addition, the current PD process worked poorly with
the New Trier Partners PD application. The Council is not trying to limit
zoning and planning input – rather the proposed PD amendments preserve that
participation and includes public engagement as very valuable and necessary. He
indicated that there is nothing in Subject Ordinance that precludes members of
the ZBA and PC coming to meetings and voicing their opinion, and that he
encouraged that.
President Rintz closed the Public Hearing at 7:44 PM.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-2-
2019 as amended by Trustees Myers and Swierk. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Myers, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: Trustees
Dearborn and Lanphier. Absent: None.
Refuse Collection Evaluation.
Mr. Saunders explained that the goal of the refuse collection study is to
provide more choices in fee and service levels. After showing an overview of
the 2019 Refuse budget and summarizing the residential and commercial refuse
and recycling services, he presented two videos: one of back-door refuse
collections and the other of curbside recycling service, which is less labor-
intensive and more efficient. Possible changes to the refuse program could
include outsourcing commercial/institutional refuse collection, and offering
different residential service levels, including curbside pickups; a “pay-as-
you-throw” refuse collection; and a subscription composting service.
The Council discussed the loss of Village control over commercial operations if
they are outsourced, and it was suggested the perhaps the Chamber of Commerce
could start a consortium of business owners who would choose the same vendor
for refuse collection in the business districts. It was pointed out, however,
that not all businesses are Chamber members. Mr. Saunders explained that
divesting of the commercial/institutional collections would allow a more normal
work week for the refuse collector staff, and in conjunction with offering
curbside service, would save on costs.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Louise Holland, Oak Street. Ms. Holland noted that she prefers back door
collection because of a raccoon problem; the refuse collectors are the face of
the Village for many residents; and the service is wonderful just the way it
is.
Jack Coladarci, Cherry Street. Mr. Coladarci said it might be unsightly to have
cans at the curbside for refuse as well as recycling and yard waste; he asked
what the cost to replace all the refuse cans in the Village would be; and he
suggested the Chamber of Commerce poll its members about their views on
commercial refuse collections.
Joan Fragen, 1230 Lindenwood Avenue; Meg Ziel, 989 Elm Street; Street; Nancy
Fox, 1036 Elm Street; Liz Kunkle, 1303 Holly Lane; Jessica Balver, 538 Meadow
Road; and Christine Donnelly, 7 Kent Road. All of these commenters urged the
Council to prioritize offering a composting program, as Winnetka has always
prided itself on being a progressive community, and is behind in this regard.
Josh Connell, Lake Shore Recycling Systems. Mr. Connell said he was available
to Council and staff for discussion, in the event the Village wants to pursue a
composting program.
President Rintz said he had not expected to hear so many comments about
composting from the audience, and clearly there was a lot of interest in the
community about the issue.
Trustee Wedner asked about the impact on the Village’s refuse collection
program if more recycling and composting is encouraged. Mr. Saunders explained
that eventually, there would be less need for staffing and equipment and lower
tipping fees. He added that offering a menu of services that people are
interested in should be a priority.
Trustee Myers noted that the current landfill is expected to fill up by 2030,
at which point tipping fees will increase as the waste is hauled farther away.
Trustee Swierk asked where waste from garbage disposals goes. Mr. Saunders said
it gets caught on screens at the water filtration plant, gets mixed with the
sludge and winds up as compost. He noted that it is an expensive way to compost
recyclable food items, and he added that items that pass through the screens
wind up back in the water supply.
Trustee Lanphier suggested getting input from businesses before changing the
commercial collections. Trustee Myers explained that the Village has tried to
get input from commercial customers, and that the Refuse Survey did provide
good insight. The next priority is to think about the future in terms of
reducing waste output and reducing Winnetka’s costs.
Trustee Dearborn noted that outsourcing commercial collections provides only
minimal savings on funding the service; but a curbside collection with a “pay-
as-you-throw” approach would incentivize waste reduction, as would a composting
program.
Trustee Wedner advocated for modernization of the Village’s refuse program, as
the work conditions for back-door service are very challenging, and it is
getting difficult to recruit employees to staff refuse collection.
Trustee Cripe felt the overall goal should be to reduce as much garbage as
possible and rethink the whole refuse system, starting with offering curbside
collection; a pay-as-you-throw option; and a composting program; and then about
phase out commercial collection through attrition in the future.
The Council discussed how to incentivize generating less refuse and recycling
more, and the general consensus was that the community is happy with Village-
run refuse collection, but the system needs rethinking. Staff was asked to
prepare a menu of refuse service options that includes curbside collection and
pay-as-you-throw, and composting; and to research a franchise option for
commercial collection.
Adjournment.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:22 p.m.
