MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
June 4, 2019
(Approved: June 18, 2019)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
4, 2019, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees Jack
Coladarci, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, John Swierk, and
Anne Wedner. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Community Development Director David Schoon, Assistant Community Development
Director Brian Norkus, Police Chief Marc Hornstein, Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky,
Deputy Fire Chief John Ripka, Economic Development Coordinator Cindy Plante,
and approximately 35 persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Swierk led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
June 11, 2019 Study Session. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
June 18, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
July 2, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present except
Trustees Swierk and Lanphier said they expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Pat Balsamo, Cherry Street. Ms. Balsamo noted that June 4, 2019 is the 100 th
anniversary of Congress passing the 19 th Amendment granting women the right to
vote. She explained that Illinois was the first state to ratify the 19 th
Amendment on June 10, 1919 - edging out Wisconsin by one hour. She urged the
Village to consider June 10, 2019 as a day to celebrate women’s right to vote,
along with Illinois’ substantial role in accomplishing that goal.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Lanphier reported that Community Conversations would meet at the
Farmers’ Market through October.
Trustee Wedner reported on the progress of the Skokie Lagoons boat house
project at Tower Road and Forest Way Drive; and she invited interested
residents to join in the effort.
Trustee Swierk thanked the organizers of the Memorial Day observance on the
Village Green.
Trustee Cripe reported that a stormwater meeting with District 36 took place on
Monday; good progress is being made with both New Trier High School and
District 36.
Trustee Coladarci reported on the most recent Landmark Preservation Commission
meeting.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. None.
Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
April 30, 2019 Special Meeting.
May 14, 2019 Study Session.
May 21, 2019 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated May 17-30, 2019 in the amount of $1,661,737.00.
Ordinance No. M-7-2019: 976 Green Bay Road - Robbins Architecture - Special Use
Permit (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-54-2019: Approving a Class A-1 Liquor License for Wild Thing,
LLC d/b/a Spirit Elephant (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-55-2019: Establishing a Retiree Health Savings (RHS) Program
for Members of the International Association of Firefighters (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-56-2019: Community Rating System Program - Repetitive Loss
Area No. 5 (Adoption).
Trustee Coladarci, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and
Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Public Hearing.
Resolution No. R-37-2019: Approving a Banquet and Reception Hall at 915 Green
Bay Road (Public Hearing & Adoption).
Mr. Schoon reviewed the regulations associated with approval of the reception
hall, including adopting parking regulations and liquor code amendments, along
with approval of a liquor license and valet parking fees. The applicant has
submitted a refined proposal for the banquet/catering/café facility; and plans
for noise remediation and valet/daytime parking have been strengthened.
Next, Mr. Schoon reviewed amendments to the approval documents since they were
first introduced/reviewed, including the frontage of the café, noise standards,
parking provisions and penalties, and liquor regulations.
David Ash, Aria Group and project architect. Mr. Ash reviewed the plans for the
café and event space entrances, as well as sound control measures that include
a new roof and the services of Steiner Acoustics to ensure low noise
transmission. He explained that most deliveries would take place during the
day, and very little of that noise would transmit beyond the Metra tracks.
Michael from VIP Valet Services. Michael said he is requesting four parking
spaces in front of the venue to greet and ticket guests. The cars will be
parked at the Hubbard Woods parking garage top level, where there will be an
attendant on duty at all times; in addition, the vehicles will be identified as
valet-parked. For guests leaving an event, the parking out front can be reduced
to two spaces. VIP staffs one driver per every 5-7 cars to expedite the traffic
flow, and staffing can be quickly pulled from nearby locations in the event
more personnel are needed.
Elaina Vazquez, owner of Boutique Bites, and Jennifer Jambrosic, Director of
Operations. Ms. Vazquez explained that she has owned her event/catering
business for 11 years and operated in the West Loop for the past five years.
She said she has experience with venue operations and dealing with late-night
traffic at her West Loop location; and she works exclusively with VIP Valet.
She expressed excitement at joining the Winnetka community and reviewed her
plans: (i) a café providing prepared lunch and dinner options well as party
food for intimate gatherings; (ii) an event venue that preserves the unique
traits of the space and creates an option in the area to host celebrations; and
(iii) a catering business. Ms. Vazquez noted that she has a proven track record
of cultivating and maintaining high end clientele.
Ms. Jambrosic added that Boutique Bites has received a warm welcome from the
community, and she expressed a desire to have a positive impact on the
community.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Katie Cory, owner of Skandal; Nancy Grant, Winnetka resident and business
owner; Cynthia Burr, Vivid Art Gallery; Kelly Appleton, Winnetka resident;
Connie Henry, resident; Celeste Robbins, Robbins Architecture; Laurie Rubin,
Scott Avenue; Gina Speckman, Director of Chicago North Shore Convention and
Visitor’s Bureau; Julie Kennedy, Winnetka resident; Sally Tomlinson, Winnetka
resident; Lynn Jackson, 777 Rosewood; Alice Campbell, Winnetka resident; Tom
Kramer, Glenview resident; Terry Dason, Director of the Winnetka-Northfield
Chamber of Commerce; and Enil Pasik, 915 Green Bay Road landlord
representative; all spoke in favor of Boutique Bites and urged the Council to
approve its operation in Hubbards.
Joanie Vitzel, property manager of 925 Green Bay Road. Ms. Vitzel expressed
concerns about parking, designating spaces for guest drop-off and pick-up at
events, sound-proofing, and timing of deliveries.
President Rintz closed the public hearing at 8:05 PM.
Trustee Cripe thanked Ms. Vazquez for so thoroughly addressing the community’s
concerns; noted that the new business will bring vibrancy to the Hubbard Woods
business district; and added that the Council will remain sensitive to
potential issues that might crop up.
Trustee Swierk said his concerns about noise problems were alleviated by Ms.
Vazquez’ presentation, and he asked what attendance level triggers a valet
parking requirement. Ms. Vazquez said any event above 75 guests is required to
have the valet parking as part of their package.
Trustee Wedner said the business concept is a great one, and added that she was
thrilled to see so many residents at the meeting to express their support.
Trustee Dearborn said he was excited about the new business coming to Winnetka;
however, the Village needs to follow up on any ordinance violations that may
occur, and he asked how noise and parking issues will be monitored.
Mr. Schoon explained that the Police Department typically responds to noise
complaints; a parking agreement will be developed for Boutique Bites to help
the Village monitor parking compliance.
Chief Hornstein said the Police Department works with the offending party to
remediate a noise issue. He noted that if an issue cannot be resolved, a
citation is issued and the matter is resolved through the Administrative
Adjudication process.
Trustee Lanphier thanked Ms. Vazquez for working through the issues and
expressed appreciation for the café, as it fills a void in the area. She said
the Village and applicant will work together to assure success on an ongoing
basis.
Trustee Coladarci said the applicant’s proposal addresses concerns raised by
the neighbors, and added that if sound does not become an issue, the business
is perfect for Hubbard Woods.
President Rintz noted that historically, change is a difficult prospect in
Winnetka and sometimes issues can be over-analyzed. He asked the community to
trust that the Council and Staff have done their diligence to work through the
issues, and he added that the business is a great addition to the Village.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
37-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-3-2019: Adopting Parking Regulations (Adoption).
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-3-
2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-38-2019: Establishing the Fees for the Processing of a Valet
Parking Permit Application and Banquet Parking Permits (Adoption).
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
38-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-4-2019: Amending Liquor Code to Create Class Y (Adoption).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to adopt Ordinance No. MC-
4-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-39-2019: Approving a Class Y Liquor License for Boutique Bites
(Adoption).
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
39-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. MC-5-2019: Adoption of Model Building Codes & Local Amendments
(Adoption). Mr. Schoon explained that after concern was expressed with the
proposed residential property maintenance code at the May 21 st Council
meeting, staff had prepared a memo addressing those concerns and others raised
