    Recording Secretary
June 11, 2019
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
June 11, 2019
(Approved: June 18, 2019)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, June
11, 2019, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 p.m. Present: Trustees Jack
Coladarci, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penny Lanphier, John Swierk, and Anne
Wedner. Absent: None. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Public Works Director Steve Saunders,
Finance Director Tim Sloth, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Community
Development Director David Schoon, and approximately four persons in the
audience.
Public Comment.
None.
Intergovernmental Agreement with New Trier High School for Stormwater
Improvements at Duke Childs Field.
Mr. Saunders reviewed the Stormwater Vision process, which began in 2015 with
the engagement of Strand Associates. The project area drains to the Skokie
River; a system of levees that keeps the river from overflowing its banks into
the Village requires pumping to get the area’s stormwater to the river.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Stormwater Vision hinges on a wetlands storage
project on Cook County Forest Preserve land west of Hibbard Road. Permission to
use the land was negotiated with the County and culminated in a Memorandum of
Understanding that requires the Village to enter into agreements with other
local agencies to develop additional stormwater detention projects upstream of
the wetlands storage project.
To comply with the Memorandum of Understanding requirements for additional
detention projects, the Village has been negotiating with New Trier District
203, School District 36 and the Park District. An agreement has been reached
with District 203 for an underground storage project at Duke Childs Field/
Skokie Playfields that will provide approximately 14.2 acre-feet of stormwater
detention. The agreement also calls for maintaining the existing grades of the
current playfields; provides 50-year flood protection for Duke Childs Field;
and allows District 203 to improve and modernize its current playfield
facilities.
After Mr. Saunders reviewed the proposed athletic field improvements and
project benefits, Attorney Friedman reviewed key points from the
Intergovernmental Agreement (IGA). Key elements of the agreement include: (i)
the Village will construct compensatory stormwater storage to protect against a
50-year storm event; (ii) the current Declaration of Covenants will be replaced
with an easement agreement to allow the stormwater utility under a portion of
the playfields; (iii) the property will continue to be used for athletic
purposes; and (iv) open space will be preserved at the site. Preliminary plans
are approved by the agreement; if the final plans do not substantially differ
from the preliminary plans, each body is required to approve the final plans. A
text amendment to the Zoning Ordinance will be adopted to provide the necessary
zoning variations to allow the Duke Childs Field improvement project. In the
event the project is unable to proceed for a reason other than a breach of the
IGA, the Village and District 203 have agreed in a Letter of Understanding to
negotiate a new easement agreement.
Mr. Schoon reviewed explained that a Public Hearing is scheduled on June 18 for
the required Zoning amendments associated with the athletic field improvements:
(i) a special use permit; (ii) several setback variations; and (iii) a
Certificate of Appropriateness for design elements.
Trustee Cripe thanked New Trier High School for its partnership with the
Village, and also thanked President Rintz, Manager Bahan and Mr. Saunders for
the tremendous amount of effort that went into the negotiation of the IGA.
Trustee Swierk asked for clarification about who will pay for the athletic
field improvements. Mr. Saunders explained that the Village will pay for the
detention construction costs and provide a gravel base for the new parking lot;
and District 203 will pay for the athletic field improvements.
The Council discussed the IGA, and there being no public comments, there was
consensus to bring the IGA and other approval documents to the next Regular
Council for a final discussion and adoption.
Closed Session.
Trustee Cripe moved to adjourn into Closed Session to discuss the Sale or Lease
of Property Owned by the Village of Winnetka, pursuant to Section 2c(6) of the
Illinois Open Meetings Act. Trustee Wedner seconded the motion. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier,
Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
President Rintz announced that the Council would not return to the open meeting
after Closed Session. The Council adjourned into Closed Session at 7:54 p.m.
Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:15 p.m.
