Recording Secretary
July 9, 2019
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL MEETING
July 9, 2019
(Approved: August 6, 2019)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
9, 2019, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 p.m. Present: Trustees Jack
Coladarci, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, and John Swierk.
Absent: Trustee Anne Wedner. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Public
Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant Public Works Director Jim Bernahl,
Finance Director Tim Sloth, and approximately 13 persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Lanphier led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
July 16, 2019. All of the Council members present said they expect to attend.
August 6, 2019. All of the Council members present except Trustee Lanphier said
they expect to attend.
August 20, 2019. All of the Council members present except President Rintz and
Trustee Dearborn said they expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Scott Myers, Val Haller, and Mark Haller from the Winnetka Music Festival
Committee. Mr. Myers thanked the Village Council for its sponsorship, and
Village staff for helping to plan, set up and execute the Winnetka Music
Festival. He explained that the cost of the festival is nearly $400,000, most
of which is raised from private sponsors and beer/wine sales; and he noted that
the event organizers are discussing ways to keep the festival sustainable in
future years. Mr. Haller added that the festival draws outsiders to Winnetka
and showcases the Village in a positive light.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Coladarci reported on items of interest on the Warrant List and the
most recent Landmark Preservation Commission meeting.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. President Rintz thanked the Winnetka Music Festival
Committee for putting on another great event. He added that the One Winnetka
development would be discussed at the next Council meeting.
Approval of the Agenda.
President Rintz said Item No. 7(c) would be removed from the Consent Agenda and
discussed under New Business. Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe,
moved to approve the final form of the Agenda. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
June 18, 2019 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated June 14 – July 4, 2019 in the amount of
$896,581.73.
Reject Bids: 2019 Water Main Improvement Project. This item was removed for
discussion under New Business.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Wedner.
Ordinances and Resolutions: None.
New Business.
Reject Bids: 2019 Water Main Improvement Project. Mr. Saunders explained that
bids received for the Subject Project were much higher than anticipated, due to
the lateness of the construction season, higher cost of materials, and the fact
that it is a stand-alone project. He said staff recommends rejecting these bids
and rebidding the project in early 2020 when market conditions are expected to
be more favorable, and the project can be bundled with other street
rehabilitation work for cost savings.
Trustee Dearborn commended the decision as an example of Village staff adhering
to important fiscal standards and budgetary constraints.
There being no public or further Council comment, Trustee Dearborn, seconded by
Trustee Cripe, moved to authorize the Village Manager to reject the bids
received pursuant to RFB #019-002: 2019 Water Main Improvement Project. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Wedner.
Petition Request – Sidewalk on Locust Road Between Hill Road and Indian Hill
Road.
Mr. Bernahl explained that a petition for a sidewalk has been received for the
east side of Locust Road between Hill and Indian Hill Roads. He described the
area where the sidewalk has been requested and reviewed Village communications
with the affected residents. He said IDOT and Cook County standards must be
followed that would affect the width of the proposed sidewalk, as well as
whether a curb must be placed. He also identified utilities and landscaping
which would be affected by a sidewalk.
Mr. Bernahl said Village staff had worked on preliminary design elements of the
sidewalk, including its width and replacement of disturbed driveway aprons. He
explained the Council could opt to fund the sidewalk project in the 2020 Budget
and pursue negotiations with Cook County; or it could consider denial of the
request. Preliminary cost estimates are $150,000 – $200,000.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Jim Orth, 79 Locust. Mr. Orth said safety is the concern for pedestrians, dog
walkers, and cyclists, and he urged approval for the sidewalk.
Jennifer Frant, 47 Indian Hill. Ms. Frant expressed drainage concerns if the
sidewalk prevented water from running into the ground near her property. Mr.
Bernahl described the area in more detail and explained that putting in a
culvert could be an option to help with drainage.
Mr. and Mrs. Boudewyns, 988 Hill Road. Mr. Boudewyns said the proposed sidewalk
would run along the side of their yard, and he expressed concern about the
security of their property since a sidewalk would draw more people to the east
side of Locust Road. He added that they did not want to lose landscaping to a
sidewalk, and that the money could be better spent elsewhere. Mrs. Boudewyns
noted that she did not see a lot of pedestrian/cycle traffic on Locust Road,
and Mr. Boudewyns asked for a pedestrian count of the area.
Mike Michaelson, 77 Locust. Mr. Michaelson said he did not see the benefit of a
sidewalk if no curb is also installed. He said the gravel is a natural buffer
for vehicle traffic and placing a sidewalk without a curb would increase his
safety concerns. He added that he spent a lot of money putting in landscaping
along the roadside, which would be removed if a sidewalk is put in.
Trustee Lanphier said although it is a Village goal to complete a sidewalk
network, the views of people who have been maintaining the rights-of-way must
be taken into consideration. The safety concerns are very important, however,
and she would approve a sidewalk if the Village could keep it in the right-of-
way. She called for further talks with Cook County to see if a sidewalk project
is feasible.
Trustee Coladarci asked why the County doesn’t put in the sidewalk if Locust is
a County road. Mr. Bernahl explained that the County does not have a sidewalk
replacement program, and when the road was resurfaced recently, residents
communicated they did not want curbs put in.
There was a discussion about the history of the Village’s sidewalk program, and
how to move forward with completion of the sidewalk network. It was suggested
the gravel path could be enclosed or otherwise made more secure to enhance the
safety of pedestrians while still keeping the Road’s rural charm and preserving
the landscaping along the road. Mr. Bernahl said if the County approves, the
gravel walkway could possibly be better defined.
Michael Callahan, 76 Locust Road. Mr. Callahan said the road was very unsafe,
and it is the only main artery coming through Winnetka that does not have a
sidewalk. He noted there is a lot of walking and biking traffic, and he urged
negotiating with the County about the condition of the road as well.
The Council discussed the request, agreeing that sidewalks are desirable for
safety purposes, and asked staff bring back additional information after
further talks with Cook County about a possible sidewalk project. It was also
suggested that a report of how many areas lack sidewalks be provided so a long
term plan can be developed, as well as a survey of the use of this stretch of
Locust Road by pedestrians and cyclists.
Old Business.
Refuse Collection Evaluation Follow-up. Mr. Saunders explained that he had
follow-up information to present based on Council discussion at its April 25
Special Meeting, along with recommendations that will impact the 2020 Budget
discussion.
Composting
Mr. Saunders said Staff had met with three food waste composting vendors, and
recommends the Village promote each of them as an option for residents to
contract with directly. He added that franchising a single vendor is not very
feasible, since the Village’s landscape hauling contractor does not provide
food waste services, and the Village’s landfill permit does not allow food
scrap waste.
After a brief discussion, the Council agreed with the recommendation; and
endorsed marketing the vendors through Village communication vehicles, Go Green
Winnetka, Community Conversations and possibly the Coldwell Banker popup space.
Commercial Refuse Collection
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village would like to better focus on its core
residential refuse program by outsourcing commercial collections. Doing so will
have a positive impact on the refuse collection workforce, as most injuries
occur during commercial collections. In addition the work hours are very
demanding. He said there are two options for the Village to choose from: (i)
franchise a single hauler; and (ii) license several haulers for commercial
users to choose from. He said the franchise process is long and difficult, and
Staff recommends the license-based approach. This would allow the Village to
set standards for the haulers including: hours of operation; size, appearance
and location of dumpsters; and maintenance of dumpsters and trucks. He
estimated that a commercial refuse licensing program could be undertaken by the
middle of next year.
The Council discussed the recommendation, confirming that the price for
commercial district customers would be in line with current Village fees. The
toll on the refuse collection workforce was also brought to light, as the
Village has difficulty retaining refuse employees. Commercial recycling was
examined; along with Village oversight options of any potential licensed
refuse/recycling haulers. A consensus was reached to outsource the commercial
refuse/recycling collections, using the licensing option. The Council will see
and discuss the specifications of a refuse hauling license before it goes into
effect.
Pay-as-you-Throw Refuse Collections
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village’s financial software is not compatible
with RFID chips. Staff could, however, use several sizes of containers to
differentiate rates for amount and frequency of pickups. Although such a
program would require further vetting, the 2020 Budget proposal could contain
an item for purchasing new refuse containers to go along with implementation of
a pay-as-you-throw refuse program. He also recommended altering the base level
of refuse service to place containers in a location visible from the street so
refuse collectors don’t have to hunt for them.
Mr. Saunders said there have been a number of complaints about the refuse fee
from people who only generate a bag of garbage every month or so, and he would
not only appreciate having an option for these residents, but also would like
to provide a financial incentive to generate less material for the landfill.
The Council discussed the option of curbside collections and using different
sized bins for a pay as you throw option. President Rintz said the Village
would hold one more Study Session on the issues, and urged the Council to be
ready to make a decision at that time. He also authorized Mr. Saunders to put
the expense for new refuse bin in his proposed 2020 Budget.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:15 p.m.
                                            
    Recording Secretary
July 16, 2019
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
July 16, 2019
(Approved: August 6, 2019)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday, July
16, 2019, at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 p.m. Present: Trustees Jack
Coladarci, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, and Anne Wedner.
Absent: Trustee John Swierk. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Community Development Director David Schoon,
Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky, Deputy Fire Chief John Ripka, and approximately 50
persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Dearborn led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
August 6, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present except
Trustee Lanphier said they expect to attend.
August 20, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present except
Trustees Dearborn, Cripe, and Wedner said they expect to attend.
September 3, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Ted Wynnychenko, Oak Street. Mr. Wynnychenko urged the Council to adopt a net
metering program to encourage solar use in the Village. President Rintz said he
hopes to have a net metering discussion at a fall Study Session if the
Council’s schedule allows.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Wedner reported on the most recent Environmental & Forestry Commission
meeting.
Trustee Lanphier invited residents to stop by and share their thoughts at
Community Conversations, being held at the Farmers’ Market during the summer
months. She reported that she had met with Coldwell Banker, along with Trustee
Wedner and Economic Development Coordinator Cindy Plante, to explore a pop up
store at the realtor’s Lincoln Avenue location.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. President Rintz reported on the formal signing of the
stormwater agreement between the Village and New Trier High School District
203.
Approval of the Agenda.
President Rintz announced that Items 8(a) and 8(b) would be removed from the
Agenda for discussion at a later Council meeting. He also suggested moving the
discussion of Item 9(a) ahead of Item 8. Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee
Swierk, moved to approve the final form of the Agenda. By voice vote, the
motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Approval of Warrant List dated June 28 – July 11, 2019 in the amount of
$445,016.64.
Resolution No. R-64-2019: Approving Letter of Agreement Renewal with AT&T for
Village Kiosk at 794 Oak Street (Adoption).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Wedner. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Swierk.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
[The following items were discussed after Item 9(a).]
Ordinance No. M-8-2019: 949 Fischer Landmark Designation (Introduction). This
item was removed from the Agenda for discussion at a later Council meeting.
Ordinance No. M-9-2019: 779 Bryant Landmark Designation (Introduction). This
item was removed from the Agenda for discussion at a later Council meeting.
Resolution No. R-63-2019: Approving Rejecting Formal Bids and Entering Into a
Contract with Epoxy Flooring US, LLC (Adoption). Chief Berkowsky explained that
the original apparatus floor dates to the 1960’s, and is at the end of its
useful life.
Deputy Chief Ripka said only a single bid was submitted for the floor
replacement project, and it is over budget. The Village Attorney has advised
rejecting the bid, getting three quotes directly from reputable vendors, and
contracting directly with an approved vendor.
The Council briefly discussed the recommendation, and Attorney Friedman
explained the Village has the discretion to reject bids as well as to determine
that the bidding process is not appropriate for this project.
There being no public comment or further Council discussion, Trustee Wedner,
seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-63-2019 rejecting
the formal bids and contracting with Epoxy Flooring US, LLC. By roll call vote,
the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier,
Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-65-2019: Regarding Zoning, Regulation, Licensing, and Possible
Prohibition of Recreational Cannabis Businesses (Adoption). Attorney Friedman
explained that in light of the State’s legalization of recreational cannabis
products, he recommends the Village initiate a process to consider Zoning
amendments relating to cannabis businesses. Since nothing in the Village Code
addresses cannabis operations, it is desirable to close any perceived loopholes
until the Council has a chance to discuss the issue and amend the Zoning
Ordinance.
Attorney Friedman reviewed the Village’s options, since the new Act pre-empts
Home Rule. The Village may enact zoning regulations for cannabis businesses if
it wishes to allow them, or it can ban them outright. At issue is whether the
Council wishes to hold the public hearing for the Zoning amendments, or whether
it would send the matter to the Plan Commission for a public hearing and
recommendations.
Trustee Wedner said the Council should find out what residents want before
taking any action to ban the facilities outright, as it is a potential source
of tax revenue for the community.
Trustee Swierk agreed with Trustee Wedner, but expressed concern about tax
revenues being affected by the Federal prohibition on cannabis.
Attorney Friedman said banks are very hesitant to get involved with aspects of
cannabis businesses that deal with the product, but he has not heard of any
Federal implications for imposing state and local taxes.
Trustee Cripe said it would be irresponsible to impose taxes on cannabis
products and businesses until the conflict between Federal and State law gets
worked out. He added that until cannabis is Federally legal, the Village should
comply with Federal law, regardless of what residents prefer.
President Rintz suggested that a public hearing would be the forum to gauge the
opinions of the community. After another brief Council discussion, during which
it was generally agreed the Council should hold the public hearing, he called
for public comment.
Ted Wynnychenko, Oak Street. Mr. Wynnychenko said residents could plant their
own cannabis plants.
Dana Hoffnagle, Ridge Avenue. Ms. Hoffnagle said many cannabis businesses in
California are high-end facilities and are carefully closed off to the general
public. She opined that cannabis is a safer alternative to alcohol and drugs
and cannabis businesses are a realistic option for the Winnetka community.
Ron White, Wilmette. Mr. White said he is opposed to businesses selling
cannabis in Winnetka.
Derek Culetta, Winnetka resident. Mr. Culetta said he moved to Winnetka for a
small town experience, and urged the Council not to allow cannabis
dispensaries; he agreed that Federal clarification is needed.
Mead Montgomery, Old Green Bay Road. Mr. Montgomery expressed concern about the
conflict with Federal law, and urged the Council not to be in a hurry to roll
out cannabis dispensaries.
Attorney Friedman noted that only registered medical cannabis patients can grow
plants at home, with a limit of five plants.
The Council was in agreement to adopt the Subject Resolution and to hold a
public hearing on potential Zoning amendments in the near future.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe moved to amend Resolution No. R-65-
2019 to provide that the Village Council will hold a public hearing on the
cannabis issues instead of the Plan Commission. By voice vote, the motion
carried.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt amended Resolution
No. R-65-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
New Business.
[This item was discussed after Item No. 7 and before item No. 8.]
688 & 694 Green Bay Road – Willow Trace Residences Planned Development –
Concept Plan Review. Mr. Schoon reviewed the new Planned Development
application process, noting that the concept plan review is simply a
preliminary review of the proposed development, and is not binding on either
side. After he reviewed the current zoning of the Subject Properties, he
explained that subdivision relief would be needed to combine the two parcels
into one buildable lot. In addition, zoning variations for gross floor area,
height, roof form and façade articulation would also be sought in connection
with the proposed development.
Rick Swanson, Swanson Development, LLC. Mr. Swanson presented his proposal,
which calls for demolition of two existing vacant structures at the Subject
Property, and constructing a single replacement building which will include six
2,200 square-foot luxury residences. Also included in the proposal: 12
underground parking spaces, two outdoor parking spaces, a rooftop storage area,
and rooftop deck.
Mr. Swanson explained that his proposal complies with zoning density and
setback provisions and all of the units will be privately owned and maintained.
He would request zoning variations for increased height to accommodate higher
ceilings in the units, and from gross floor area (GFA) limits to allow for the
underground garage, which will add 5,176 square feet to the GFA calculations.
[Trustee Swierk arrived at 7:21 PM.]
There were no comments from the audience.
The Council discussed the proposal, and asked Mr. Swanson to address the
following issues in his final plan: (i) the impact on the homes immediately to
the west of the development; (ii) height of the building; (iii) stormwater
control; (iv) parking and driveway access; and (v) access and siting of the
trash enclosure.
The Council was generally favorably inclined towards the proposed development,
and Mr. Swanson was asked to communicate with the neighbors to hear their
concerns.
Mr. Swanson said engagement with the neighbors is an important part of his
process. In addition, his research reveals a need for this type of housing in
Winnetka.
Old Business.
Status of One Winnetka Development and Related Compliance and Property
Maintenance Issues. President Rintz explained that the following discussion
could have been held in Closed Session since it involves the sale of public
property as well as potential litigation; however, the Trustees all agreed that
the public should be included in this important conversation. He reminded the
audience that issues such as the look, height, scale and other elements of the
development have already been extensively litigated. The current discussion
should focus solely on the defaults with the Development Agreement, and
property maintenance issues.
Trustee Cripe said he would continue to abstain from the One Winnetka
discussion because of the possible appearance of a conflict of interest, and he
stepped down from the dais.
Attorney Friedman explained that the One Winnetka development is out of
compliance with several provisions of the Development Agreement: (i) it has
missed the deadline to provide a third-party guaranty; (ii) it has missed the
deadline to close on the Conney’s property and record a subdivision plat for
the development parcel; and (iii) the properties are not being maintained and
Code violations exist relating to public health and safety.
Attorney Friedman said the Village has filed a complaint against the Developer
in Cook County Circuit Court. Tonight’s discussion will provide direction from
the Council about the best next steps. He added that while there has been
intermittent communication with the Developer and his lawyers, there have been
no guarantees given about when the defaults will be cured. If the Council
decides to terminate the Development Agreement, all of the approvals will be
revoked and the property will revert to its original zoning. A future developer
would have to start all over again.
President Rintz noted that the developer, Mr. Trandel, along with a
representative from Conney’s, had been invited to present their side of the
story. However, neither of the interested parties responded and they are not in
attendance. Next, he called for public comment.
Richard Messer, 120 Woodley. Mr. Messer said he was a contract purchaser of a
unit at One Winnetka, and that he had received a text from Mr. Trandel that the
contract with Conney’s was signed this afternoon.
Robert Orroyo, Kenilworth. Mr. Orroyo said he was also a contract purchaser of
One Winnetka, and although he would love to see the development built, it would
be better to cancel the agreement sooner rather than later so he could move on
and plan his future.
Glen Udell, representative of the Developer. Mr. Udell blamed the trouble with
signing a contract with Conney’s on outside factors, and said there is now a
firm agreement to finish construction on the proposed new Conney’s site.
Dave Humphrey, Moth Myer; Bob Humphrey, Winnetka resident; Brian Van Cleve, Elm
Street; Wally Greenough, Maple Street; George Wolper, Prospect Avenue; Barbara
Aquilino, Winnetka resident; Frank Petrek, 711 Oak Street; and Meade
Montgomery, Winnetka resident. All of the foregoing recommended cancelling the
Development Agreement as, Mr. Trandel does not appear able to get his
development built, and the property is unsafe and unsanitary in its current
state.
Terry Dason, Director of the Winnetka-Northfield Chamber of Commerce. Ms. Dason
expressed concern for Conney’s and asked the Council to take their plight into
consideration.
President Rintz asked the Council for their thoughts.
The Council discussed the option to terminate the Development Agreement, and
reached consensus to do so. While the Trustees were sympathetic to Conney’s
situation, the Council did not desire to get involved in a private business
deal.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Wedner, directed Attorney Friedman to draft
a resolution to nullify the One Winnetka Development Agreement. By voice vote,
the motion carried.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:39 p.m.
 
