September 3, 2019
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
September 3, 2019
(Approved: September 17, 2019)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 3, 2019, at 7:03 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:05 p.m. Present: Trustees Jack
Coladarci, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, John Swierk, and Anne Wedner. Absent:
Trustee Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Stewart Weiss, Finance
Director Tim Sloth, Community Development Director David Schoon, Senior Planner
Ann Klaassen, Communications Manager Josie Clark, and approximately 20 persons
in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Cripe led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
September 10, 2019 Study Session. All of the Council members present except
Trustees Swierk and Wedner said they expect to attend.
September 17, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
October 1, 2019 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Greer Braun, Bell Lane. Ms. Braun requested better lighting and signage, along
with reflector road striping, on Tower Road between Forest Way Drive and Green
Bay Road.
Arthur Braun, Bell Lane. Mr. Braun commended Winnetka’s Firemedics, who
recently evacuated him from his home to the hospital, on their professionalism.
Elisha Gray, Spruce Street. Mr. Gray asked the Village to work with the Park
District to develop a maintenance plan for the Green Bay Trail, as this
community asset is in need of stewardship.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Swierk reported on the last Plan Commission meeting.
Trustee Cripe gave an update regarding the District 36 and Park District
stormwater negotiations.
Trustee Coladarci reported on a recent Community Conversations at the Farmers’
Market.
Attorney. None.
Manager. None.
Village President. None.
Approval of the Agenda.
President Rintz removed Item 7(d) from the Consent Agenda for a separate vote.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to approve the final form of
the Agenda. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
August 20, 2019 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated August 16-29, 2019 in the amount of
$1,279,982.97.
Resolution No. R-72-2019: Amended GIS Consortium Member Agreement (Adoption).
Village Green Flag Request. This item was removed from the consent agenda for a
separate vote under Item #8.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the foregoing items
on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Village Green Flag Request. Manager Bahan explained that the 9/11 flag planting
tradition takes place every year to honor victims of the terrorist attack on
September 11, 2001.
Charlie, Henry, William, and Julia Cripe, who are organizing the event, invited
the community to participate.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to approve the placement of
flags on the Village Green to commemorate the casualties of the 9/11 terrorist
attacks. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. MC-8-2019: Submission & Approval of Plats of Subdivisions for
Planned Developments (Introduction). Mr. Schoon reviewed several technical
amendments to the Village Code pertaining to Planned Developments.
There being no comments or questions from the Council or public, Trustee Cripe,
seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to introduce Ordinance No.
MC-8-2019. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. M-12-2019: Repealing Ordinance No. M-21-2018 (Introduction/
Adoption). Mr. Sloth explained that the 2017 Water Rate Study recommended the
Village borrow $1 million from the Illinois Environmental Protection Agency
(IEPA) to help finance water main replacements. However, a recent staff
analysis revealed that the Water Fund has performed more favorably than
projections had indicated. In light of this new information, the loan is no
longer deemed necessary and Staff is recommending repeal of Ordinance No. M-21-
2018, which authorized the low-interest IEPA loan.
Trustee Dearborn asked if the water rate increases should be re-examined.
Mr. Sloth explained that the surplus in the Water Fund is due to: (i)
unanticipated lower operating expenses; and (ii) non-completion of several
capital projects. The Village still needs $2.5 million in annual revenue to
maintain its 100-year replacement cycle.
Manager Bahan added that additional revenue also came from the finalization of
the Northfield water contract, and the economies of scale achieved by syncing
water main replacements with street repair projects. He said the Council would
discuss the water rates more thoroughly at the Budget workshop.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-21-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Lanphier.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Ordinance
No. M-21-2018. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Ordinance No. M-14-2019: 901-905 Green Bay Road - Jeannie Balsam Interiors -
Special Use Permit (Introduction/Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed this request to
approve a special use permit to allow an existing business in Hubbard Woods to
expand and move into first-floor space in the Commercial Retail Overlay
district.
Jeannie Balsam, owner of Jeannie Balsam Interiors. Ms. Balsam explained that
the proposed new space is perfect to expand and evolve her current business,
and that other locations in Hubbard Woods won’t work for her. She noted that
she is committed to staying in Winnetka and will be a good neighbor in the
Overlay District.
President Rintz called for public comment.
The following audience members recommended approval of the special use permit:
Greer Braun, Bell Lane; Dorothy Bickmore, Winnetka; Cynthia Burr, Vivid Art
Gallery; Kevin Maloney, owner of 901-05 Green Bay Road; and Sarah Dippold,
Hubbard Woods business owner.
The Trustees briefly discussed the request; all were in favor of approving the
special use permit and the Council commended Ms. Balsam on her business
expansion.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-14-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Trustees
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Lanphier.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-14-
2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe,
Dearborn, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Resolution No. R-70-2019: Authorizing Agreement with CivicPlus for Website
Redesign (Adoption). Ms. Pierce explained that the Village’s website is out of
date, does not provide optimal functioning, and is difficult to update and keep
current. A Request for Proposals for a website redesign was issued in June, and
nine firms responded.
Ms. Pierce said after staff review of the proposals and interviews with three
of the vendors, CivicPlus was selected as the preferred vendor, due to their
experience in municipal website design.
Brad Berkel, Regional Director of Sales, CivicPlus. Mr. Berkel explained that
CivicPlus develops integrated technology platforms for local governments and
has extensive experience working with municipalities.
The Council discussed the development of a new website for the Village, and
there was consensus to hire CivicPlus to design a new Village website.
There being no public comment, Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Swierk,
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-70-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Lanphier.
Ordinance No. M-8-2019: 949 Fisher Lane - Landmark Designation (Introduction).
Mr. Schoon explained that the Village’s local landmark process includes
consideration of the historical and architectural significance of each proposed
property.
Ms. Klassen reviewed the landmark process, noting that only the owners of the
property can initiate a landmark review, and explaining that the Landmark
Preservation Commission (LPC) uses a scoring system to determine the level of
significance for each property. Next, she reviewed the three properties under
consideration for local landmark status, giving their rating by the LPC.
Mr. Schoon then reviewed the State’s property tax assessment freeze program for
local landmarks, noting that the qualifications are stringent and not all local
landmark awardees apply for it. The program lasts for 12 years, and offers a
freeze on assessment increases for the first eight years, followed by a series
of escalating assessments for the next four years that ends in current market
assessment at year 12. He reviewed a chart showing that the impact of reduced
tax dollar revenue is minimal.
Trustee Wedner said the Council needs to discuss the policy question of
sanctioning an increased burden on the rest of Winnetka’s taxpayers, no matter
how small the scale. She noted that the decision to bestow local landmark
status also affects the area’s other taxing agencies, and the program did not
feel fair to her.
Trustee Swierk said the property tax assessment freeze offsets the State’s
requirement that qualifying homes must first undergo a rehabilitation project
in excess of 25% of the property’s fair market value; the program encourages
renovation by helping the homeowner to recoup that cost.
Trustee Dearborn said the dollar impact from the assessment freeze is
deminimis, and the landmark program encourages investment in structures that
might otherwise be demolished.
Trustee Coladarci said residents routinely appeal their property taxes, which
also affects the overall tax base.
Trustee Cripe explained that it is important to think about taxpayer fairness,
given the extremely high property taxes in Winnetka; however, an unintended
consequence of that tax burden might be that residents hesitate to improve
their homes, knowing their assessment will then go even higher. For a minimal
impact on the tax base at large, the landmark program is a reasonable incentive
for homeowners to preserve and improve their homes and get a short break from
the ensuing higher property tax assessment.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Louise Holland, Chair of the LPC. Ms. Holland explained that only properties
that are local landmarks or listed on the National Register of Historic Places
can participate in the State’s assessment freeze program. She noted that the
three cases up for consideration tonight are the first landmark requests the
Village has received since 2012, since the 1991 Landmark Ordinance prohibits
the LPC from soliciting residents to apply for local landmark status. Finally,
she urged the Council to approve the local landmark designation of the three
Subject Properties.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
8-2019. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. M-9-2019: 779 Bryant Avenue - Landmark Designation
(Introduction).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. M-9-2019. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Ordinance No. M-13-2019: 1094 Fisher Crescent Lane Landmark Designation
(Introduction).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. M-13-2019. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Old Business.
None.
New Business:
None.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
None.
Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:23 p.m.
____________________________
Recording Secretary
 
September 10, 2019
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL STUDY SESSION
September 10, 2019
(Approved: October 1, 2019)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
September 10, 2019 at 7:00 p.m.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 p.m. Present: Trustees Jack
Coladarci, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, and Penny Lanphier. Absent: Trustees
John Swierk and Anne Wedner. Also in attendance: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Megan Pierce, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Public
Works Director Steve Saunders, Assistant Public Works Director Jim Bernahl,
Finance Director Tim Sloth, and approximately 6 persons in the audience.
Public Comment.
None.
Residential Refuse Collection Evaluation Follow-Up.
Mr. Saunders recapped previous Council discussions on the Village’s refuse
collection operations; tonight’s discussion will concern “Pay-as-you-Throw”
(PAYT) and curbside collections. The main goal of a PAYT program is to
encourage customers to generate less trash and ultimately opt for curbside
collections. Since the Village’s financial billing system is not currently
compatible with RFID chips, the use of several sizes of containers to
differentiate levels of service was discussed at the July 10 Study Session.
Mr. Saunders proposed funding the purchase of new refuse containers in the 2020
Budget, with an option to implement a modified PAYT program by July 1, 2020. He
noted that developing a rate design that incentivizes waste reduction and
curbside collection is a sensitive project, as there are annual revenue goals
the Refuse Fund must meet.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Steve Juliasson, Hubbard Woods. Mr. Juliasson asked: (i) what happens if a
refuse container breaks; and (ii) if curbside collection is the preferred
option.
Mr. Saunders explained that: (i) the Village replaces broken or worn refuse
containers; and (ii) the Village would still provide backdoor service, but will
offer curbside at a lower fee for those residents who desire to pay less.
Cecilia Milotti, Rosewood Avenue. Ms. Milotti asked if she could pay for only
twice monthly collection. President Rintz said that is not an option that could
be offered, but the smaller cans will reduce her refuse costs.
Joan von Krogh, Winnetka resident. Ms. Von Krogh said she doesn’t generate
refuse and only puts out recycling once per month and that it would be most
equitable to charge based upon the size of each household. President Rintz
explained that the Village is trying to be as fair as possible while still
needing to pay the expenses of running refuse service; perfect fairness is not
achievable without an inordinate amount of administration.
Trustee Cripe pointed out that there are large families in the Village that do
not generate large amounts of refuse because they recycle and compost, as well
as buy items in bulk with less packaging.
Mr. Juliasson asked about RFID technology for the Village. Mr. Saunders
reiterated that the current billing system does not support it; however, he
would purchase containers with the chips in the event the technology becomes
supported at some future point.
Chuck Dowding, 968 Elm Street and Chair of the Environmental & Forestry
Commission. Mr. Dowding thanked the Council for making progress on the refuse
issue, and commended the proposed new system.
Jeffrey Liss, 640 Winnetka Mews. Mr. Liss said the backdoor refuse collections
are what makes Winnetka a special place to live in many people’s opinion. He
asked if there are real cost savings and reduced worker injury with PAYT and
called for documentation.
Pat Balsamo asked what happened with the composting issue. Mr. Saunders
explained that the Village is partnering with three composting vendors for
residents to choose from, and that the information has been disseminated on the
Village’s website, in E-Winnetka and in the Winnetka Report.
The Council discussed the PAYT and curbside collection proposals.
Trustee Cripe said he thought the proposal has been well thought-out and
accomplishes the Village’s goals in a straightforward and comprehensible
manner. He added that encouraging residents to reduce their garbage is a
responsible aim as landfills are nearing capacity.
Trustee Dearborn pointed out that 90% of residents expressed satisfaction with
refuse service, and he cautioned against transferring cost burdens to those who
opt for back door collection. He urged the Village to address the issue of
residents who do not put their containers at the top of the driveway, and to
consider the aesthetics of curbside collection (i.e.,animals, litter). He
expressed a preference for offering the smaller bins at a lower fee, but
keeping the refuse collection strictly back door.
Trustee Lanphier said she thought the Village’s goals are not clearly
delineated. She wondered if the problems with back door service might revolve
around the Village being too lenient with residents who don’t follow the rule
of leaving the container in plain sight; and she urged more enforcement for
those customers.
Trustee Coladarci expressed concern with unsightliness of trash containers in
front of houses; he added that the back door collection is a special perk of
living in Winnetka and makes the financial burden of living in the Village seem
more worthwhile.
Trustee Dearborn added that reducing waste means composting more and reducing
the overall amount of garbage.
Trustee Cripe explained that the status quo is being retained, with the option
of curbside collections in two different sizes being added; the program would
be an interim step towards being able to automate collections in the future. He
added that another compelling argument is the negative impact of back door
collection on refuse workers, which drives up injury costs.
Trustee Lanphier suggested moving gradually with a curbside program.
President Rintz noted that younger residents don’t think twice about curbside
refuse collection. He added that notwithstanding the high taxes residents pay,
back door service results in a high risk of injury for the Village’s refuse
staff; curbside collection makes their job more humane and extends their
careers.
After more discussion, the Trustees agreed they could support the recommended
new program:
1x/week curbside, 35 gal. cart: $22-27/month
1x/week curbside, 65 gal. cart: $33-38/month
1x/week backdoor: $45-55/month
2x/week backdoor: $100-110/month
Closed Session.
Trustee Dearborn moved to adjourn into Closed Session to discuss the Purchase
or Lease of Property Owned by the Village of Winnetka, and Review of Closed
Session Minutes, pursuant to Sections 2c(5) and 2c(21) of the Illinois Open
Meetings Act. Trustee Cripe seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, and Lanphier. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustees Swierk and Wedner.
President Rintz announced that the Council would not return to the open meeting
after Closed Session. The Council adjourned into Closed Session at 8:45 p.m.
Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:35 p.m.
 
