Village Council Minutes.
September 3, 2019 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated August 30 - September 12, 2019 in the amount of
$1,751,820.87.
Ordinance No. MC-8-2019: Submission & Approval of Plats of Subdivisions for
Planned Developments (Adoption).
Ordinance No. M-8-2019: 949 Fisher Lane - Landmark Designation (Adoption) .
This item was removed from the Consent Agenda for discussion under Ordinances &
Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-9-2019: 779 Bryant Avenue - Landmark Designation (Adoption) .
This item was removed from the Consent Agenda for discussion under Ordinances &
Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-13-2019: 1094 Fisher Crescent Lane Landmark Designation
(Adoption) . This item was removed from the Consent Agenda for discussion under
Ordinances & Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-73-2019: Authorizing Agreement with GovHR for Compensation and
Classification Evaluation (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-74-2019: Approval and Release of Closed Session Minutes
(Adoption).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and
Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
New Business
North Shore Country Day School Day Proclamation.
President Rintz invited North Shore Country Day School (NSCDS) Headmaster Tom
Flemma to comment on the 100 th anniversary of the NSCDS. Mr. Flemma thanked
the Council for its strong partnership with the school, adding that the school
is honored to celebrate its Centennial the same year the Village celebrates its
Sesquicentennial.
President Rintz read the Proclamation aloud, and presented it to Headmaster
Flemma.
Pioneer Day Proclamation.
President Rintz invited Ann Eriksson, Human Resources Generalist, to tell the
audience about the upcoming Pioneer Day event. Ms. Eriksson invited the
community to the final celebration of Winnetka’s Sesquicentennial: Pioneer Day,
explaining it would be a day of fun and education to close out the events of
Winnetka’s 150 th birthday year.
President Rintz thanked Ms. Eriksson for her hard work organizing the
Sesquicentennial events in 2019, and read aloud a Proclamation proclaiming
September 22 as Pioneer Day.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-8-2019: 949 Fisher Lane - Landmark Designation (Adoption).
Trustee Lanphier said she was thrilled to see the three houses nominated for
landmark status.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-8-
2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: Trustee Wedner. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-9-2019: 779 Bryant Avenue - Landmark Designation (Adoption).
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-9-
2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: Trustee Wedner. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-13-2019: 1094 Fisher Crescent Lane Landmark Designation
(Adoption).
Trustee Coladarci, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adopt Ordinance
No. M-13-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: Trustee Wedner. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-15-2019: 1083 Gage Street - North Shore Barber - Special Use
Permit (Introduction/Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed this request to locate a
barber shop on the ground floor in the Commercial Retail Overlay district.
Failouni Ghassan, the applicant, said he is looking forward to opening his
business in Winnetka.
Trustee Lanphier clarified that the Zoning Ordinance had been changed to
prevent the Village from being overrun by barber shops and beauty salons, but
that this was a welcome use for the area.
There being no further public or Council comment, Trustee Swierk, seconded by
Trustee Dearborn, moved to waive introduction of Ordinance No. M-15-2019. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Wedner, moved to adopt Ordinance No.
M-15-2019. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Old Business.
Recreational Cannabis Businesses (Public Hearing). President Rintz explained
that the Village has not received an application to open a cannabis business in
Winnetka. He noted that the issue is being discussed because the Council wants
to consider the policy question of whether or not to allow such businesses
prior to the January 1, 2020 effective date of legalized recreational cannabis
in Illinois.
Trustee Swierk recused himself due to an appearance of a conflict of interest
with his current clients; he stepped down from the dais.
President Rintz opened the public hearing at 7:30 p.m.
Mr. Schoon explained that the Illinois Cannabis Regulation Tax Act gives
authority to municipalities to regulate the zoning of cannabis businesses,
including prohibiting such businesses; permit on-premises consumption of
cannabis in the event a business is approved; and impost a local tax of up to
3% on the retail sale of cannabis. Tonight’s public hearing will provide
feedback to the Council on the policy question of whether or not to allow
cannabis businesses in Winnetka. Next, Mr. Schoon reviewed the zoning issues
related to any potential approval of cannabis businesses.
President Rintz, after commenting that the vast majority of emails the Council
had received were against allowing cannabis businesses in Winnetka, called for
public comment.
Cecilia Milotti, 422 Rosewood Avenue; Elizabeth Parkinson, 321 Linden Avenue;
Jessica Tucker, 850 Locust Street; all spoke against allowing cannabis
businesses in Winnetka.
Maggie, local student. Maggie asked what measures the Council would take to
prevent marketing to minors if cannabis businesses are approved in Winnetka.
Jennifer Coker, 800 Oak Street; and Rickard Liable; both expressed support for
a cannabis dispensary in Winnetka for the revenue benefits, as well as to fill
vacant storefronts in Winnetka.
President Rintz announced that the Village of Glencoe’s Zoning Commission is
holding a public hearing on October 7 to discuss its proposal to open a
cannabis dispensary in a shopping center on Winnetka’s northern border. He
encouraged Winnetka residents to attend that meeting to express their views.
Trustee Lanphier asked if the Smoke Free Illinois Act would also affect the
smoking of cannabis. Attorney Friedman confirmed that all facilities affected
by the Smoke Free Act will also prohibit smoking of cannabis, except for
locations where onsite consumption is permitted.
President Rintz said the first question to be answered is whether the Council
wishes to allow the sale of recreational cannabis in the Village, as a “no”
would make all the other zoning questions moot.
Trustee Cripe said although he has no problem with cannabis use or
decriminalization, he could not support permitting cannabis businesses while
Federal law makes cannabis illegal.
Trustee Wedner said she would like more input from the community, especially
new voices; she declined to impose her perspective on the issue until more
residents have come forward with their views.
Trustee Lanphier said she could not support a cannabis business in Winnetka, as
there are too many practical problems to be sorted out - including the
disparity between Federal and State laws.
Trustee Dearborn said the issue is the message Winnetka would send to the world
by allowing cannabis businesses; especially the signal to the Village’s youth
in light of data about dangers associated with cannabis use in youngsters. He
supported Winnetka being a cannabis-free municipality.
Trustee Coladarci did not see a benefit for the Village in permitting a
cannabis dispensary or lounge; he said the issue is similar to Winnetka’s
stance with regard to liquor stores and bars, which are also prohibited.
President Rintz said the issue for him is what it would say about Winnetka,
even in the face of the financial benefits of allowing a retail cannabis
business. He agreed that the disparity with Federal law is a solid
consideration; and the vast majority of communication from residents has been
to prohibit cannabis businesses in the Village.
There was general agreement to direct Attorney Friedman to draft an Ordinance
prohibiting all cannabis businesses in Winnetka, including craft growers,
cultivation centers, dispensaries, infusers, processors, or shippers.
President Rintz continued the public hearing to the October 1, 2019 Regular
Council meeting.
Trustee Swierk returned to the dais.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session.
Trustee Cripe moved to adjourn into Closed Session to discuss Collective
Bargaining, pursuant to Section 2c(2) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Swierk seconded the motion. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, Swierk, and Wedner. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
President Rintz announced that the Council would not return to the open meeting
after Closed Session. The Council adjourned into Closed Session at 8:15 p.m.
Adjournment.
Trustee Wedner, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:43 p.m.
      
