Recording Secretary
December 8, 2020
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: January 5, 2021)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Tuesday, December 8, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Jack Coladarci, Andrew Cripe,
Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, and John Swierk. Absent: None. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Economic Development Coordinator Liz
Dechant, Public Works Director Steve Saunders, and approximately 9 persons in
the audience.
2)   Public Comment.
Ms. Kazenas read an email from Liz Kunkle, Winnetka resident. Ms. Kunkle urged
the Council to monitor the 2018 Streetscape Plan to ensure it is current and
sustainable and she suggested that the Village’s trees be regarded as
infrastructure that merits regular maintenance and improvement.
3)   Parkway Planting Permit Review.
Mr. Saunders explained that the Village’s parkway planting policy prohibits
encroachments on the public parkways without a permit. Last fall, a resident
placed planting boxes in the parkway in front of his home and has asked for
Council review of the policy so his planters can remain on the parkway. He
reviewed the current parkway Code provisions in Section 12.04.040 along with
suggested changes to the language to clarify the Village’s intent.
Mr. Saunders noted that the Public Works Director has discretion to approve
certain encroachments such as rain gardens on a case-by-case basis. Most other
encroachments involve planting ground cover around parkway trees or are in
areas where there is no sidewalk to delineate a parkway from a front yard and
where low-growing plantings are allowed. He explained that he works to
accommodate homeowners where possible in their efforts to beautify the Village.
Mr. Saunders reviewed a list of clarifications to the policy in Section
12.28.010 that would: (i) encourage green technologies such as rain gardens and
bioswales; (ii) allow the Environmental & Forestry Commission (EFC) to be
included as part of the permit application process; (iii) prohibit certain
obstructions in public rights of way; (iv) provide guidance for height and
location of plantings; and (v) absolve the Village from any responsibility to
maintain plantings installed under a parkway planting permit.
Mr. Saunders said Staff is seeking Council approval to: (i) draft a Code
amendment ordinance to clarify the provisions of Section 12.04.040 as
explained; (ii) amend the permit process in Section 12.28.010 as outlined
above; and (iii) direct the EFC to develop guidelines for green infrastructure
projects on parkways.
Trustee Cripe said the real impact of the evening’s discussion should be to
encourage green infrastructure improvements and he suggested the Council defer
amending the permit process until after the EFC has made its recommendation. He
proposed five principles to consider during the permit approval process: (i)
interference with utility lines or access to infrastructure; (ii) interference
with snow removal and banking; (iii) interference with motorist visibility;
(iv) consistency with neighborhood aesthetics; and (v) encourage plantings that
have benefits to the Village including its stormwater plan. He cautioned
against language in the Code that is too restrictive to allow for routine
maintenance of parkways and planting.
Trustee Lanphier expressed concerns about safe pedestrian passage on streets
where there are no sidewalks as well as safe egress for cars parked along such
parkways. She asked if the Village keeps in mind where a sidewalk might be
placed in the future and considers those factors in the permit approval process
and she also asked if watering systems are dealt with. Mr. Saunders said the
Village does take large plantings like trees into consideration on parkways
where a sidewalk may be placed in the future and reserves the right to remove
the plantings if it is deemed necessary. He added that there is a separate
permit required for sprinkler systems, and the Village retains the right to
remove them if a sidewalk placement or utility work requires. Lastly, Trustee
Lanphier suggested cross-referencing provisions for trimming trees and shrubs
that overhang the sidewalks with the parkway planting provisions.
Trustee Swierk asked if vegetable gardens should be specifically prohibited in
the interest of aesthetics; Mr. Saunders said the issue could be dealt with by
staff while still providing flexibility for cases where a garden would not be
disruptive. Trustee Swierk also asked for a provision stipulating that any
vegetation removed by the Village for utility work or other Village purposes
would be replaced at the homeowner’s expense.
Trustee Dearborn observed that the current policy, while some cleanup is
warranted, is working fairly well and he expressed support for the amendments
suggested by Staff. Mr. Saunders agreed that with some clarification the
Village can continue to work with homeowners on parkway planting schemes.
Trustee Coladarci asked if further clarification is needed to clearly define
what a “structure” is. Mr. Saunders cautioned against undue specificity in the
Code, as it removes discretion from Staff; there are extenuating circumstances
with each application, and what might be approved at one site would not be
appropriate for another.
Attorney Friedman explained that the parkways belong to the Village, and all
improvements thereon must be approved by the Village. He said the Code sets out
the Village’s expectations, and also delegates authority to Staff so every
single application does not have to be approved by the Council. Therefore, an
open-ended policy can achieve the Council’s aims while still allowing
homeowners leeway to improve their parkways.
President Rintz called for public comment.
Chuck Dowding, EFC Chair. Mr. Dowding said the EFC would be happy to research
and recommend appropriate plantings for parkways.
Craig Witty, Winnetka resident. Mr. Witty said he routinely trims parkway trees
and he asked for clarification that this practice is allowed by the Village.
Scarlett Harper, Winnetka resident. Ms. Harper expressed support for rain
garden installations throughout the Village.
Teri Cross, Winnetka resident. Ms. Cross commended Council and Staff for all
the hard work that goes into crafting policy.
Liz Kunkle, EFC Commissioner. Ms. Kunkle also expressed support for distributed
green infrastructure improvements to alleviate stormwater runoff problems and
she commended the Council for dealing with the issue on a timely basis. She
suggested the permit guidelines include recommendations for planting native
species along with rain gardens; and she asked for safety of bicyclists to be
kept in mind when sight lines are being evaluated.
Jeremy Marino, Winnetka resident. Mr. Marino said his hope is that the Village
will take this as an opportunity to provide guidance vs. prohibiting a lot of
measures, especially in light of climate change and stormwater problems. He
stressed that the community should be enabled to be creative in its solutions
and he asked why “structures” are singled out for regulation.
Kelly Conway, Winnetka resident. Ms. Conway noted that turf lawns do not help
with runoff or environmental problems and she expressed support for rain
gardens and other green infrastructure improvements which improve runoff and
help boost bird and insect populations.
Next, Ms. Kazenas read the following email comments:
•   Laura and Sid Glen: The Glens were in support of keeping restrictions in
place for Village parkways as they felt raised beds in parkways are not
appropriate.
•   Cherry Street residents: These residents expressed opposition to allowing
raised beds on Village parkways.
•   Cherry Street residents: These residents asked that the raised beds on the
parkway on Cherry Street be removed for aesthetic reasons.
President Rintz noted that the last two emails were unsigned but will be duly
noted.
The Council discussed its options on the parkway planting policy, and it
generally agreed to use Trustee Cripe’s five principles for public rights-of-
way as a framework, with the addition of bicycle safety considerations. Trustee
Swierk suggested the Village make clear that parkway improvements will only be
approved on a parkway in front of the homeowner applying for a permit. The EFC
was directed to research and develop guidance on green infrastructure on
Village rights-of-way.
President Rintz said the Council would provide final policy direction after
hearing from the EFC.
Ms. Conway noted that there are already Code provisions regarding a homeowner’s
responsibility to care for the parkway in front of their home.
4)   Sales Tax Rebate Economic Relief Program Review & Restaurant Relief Grant
Proposal.
Ms. Dechant summarized the results of the Sales Tax Rebate Program, which was a
big success - $297,500 was disbursed to 76 businesses (about one-third of
Winnetka businesses). She noted that the relief program was a lifeline for
Winnetka businesses, helping them keep their doors open, and that Winnetka’s
Economic Development program is a model for neighboring communities.
Next, Ms. Kazenas reviewed a proposed relief grant program for restaurants,
which are very negatively impacted by the Tier 3 pandemic restrictions. There
are 25 eligible restaurants in Winnetka, including new restaurants that opened
in 2020, that would qualify for $4,000 in grant funding, which will be paid
from the remaining balance in the Sales Tax Rebate Program, plus $22,500 from
the Economic Development operating budget. No application will be required to
participate in the grant program, so there will be no forms to review and no
staff time used for processing applications. If the Council approves the
program, the funds will be disbursed by the end of the year and will be hand-
delivered to the restaurants.
Trustee Apatoff said the Village needs to also assist businesses to apply for
Federal relief funding since the Village has limited funds for grant programs.
Ms. Dechant explained that she has been helping connect local businesses to
State and Federal relief funding; the Chamber of Commerce is helping businesses
with their applications as well. Ms. Kazenas added that the Village’s website
contains helpful links for businesses to apply for aid monies, and the E-
Development newsletter has been providing weekly updates on assistance
programs.
After discussing the proposed program, including a flat grant amount vs. a
tiered system and the need for financial aid to restaurants, the Council was
unanimously in favor of moving forward with the program.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting
adjourned at 8:50 PM.
 
________________________________
