January 5, 2021
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR VIRTUAL MEETING
January 5, 2021
(Approved: January 19, 2021)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Tuesday, January 5, 2021 at 7:00 PM.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Jack Coladarci, Andrew Cripe,
Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, and John Swierk. Absent: None. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Water & Electric Director Brian Keys,
Community Development Director David Schoon, and 15 persons in the audience.
Pledge of Allegiance.
President Rintz led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
Quorum.
January 12, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
January 19, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
February 2, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Public Comment.
Mickey Archambault, Winnetka resident. Mr. Archambault thanked the Village for
the Winter Winnetka Report, and pointed out a typographical error on page 2.
Reports:
Trustees.
Trustee Apatoff commended Trustee Coladarci on chairing the Landmark
Preservation Commission meeting the previous evening.
Attorney. Attorney Friedman explained that the Village President’s Emergency
Declaration is due to expire at the end of tonight’s meeting; he recommended
the Council extend the Declaration until the end of the February 16 th Council
meeting.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to extend the Village
President’s Emergency Declaration until the end of the February 16, 2021
Village Council Regular Meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Manager. None.
Village President. President Rintz reminded homeowners that it is their
responsibility to shovel the sidewalk in front of their home for snowfalls
under two or three inches; and he encouraged the Trustees to sign up for the
Distinctly Winnetka program.
Approval of the Agenda.
President Rintz requested the removal of Item 7(f) from the Consent Agenda for
a separate vote. Trustee Coladarci, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve
the Agenda as amended by President Rintz. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and
Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Consent Agenda
Village Council Minutes.
December 1, 2020 Regular Meeting.
December 8, 2020 Study Session.
December 15, 2020 Regular Meeting.
Approval of Warrant List dated dated December 11 – December 30 in the amount of
$1,366,442.18.
Resolution No. R-1-2021: Approving the Purchase of SCBA Equipment (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-2-2021: Approving Contract with Survalent for SCADA Support
(Adoption).
Resolution No. R-3-2021: Change Order No. 1 to Contract with Asplundh Tree
Expert Co. (Adoption).
Resolution No. R-4-2021: Approving Contract Purchase of Low Lift Pump
(Adoption) . This item was removed from the Consent Agenda and discussed under
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-5-2021: Roof Replacement over Reaction Basin & Chlorine Room
at Water Plant (Adoption).
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the foregoing
items a, b, c, d, e, and g on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-4-2021: Approving Contract Purchase of Low Lift Pump
(Adoption). President Rintz explained that he wanted to vote on this item
separately because one of the bids was deemed “non-responsive” and he wished to
ensure maximum transparency on the matter.
Mr. Keys explained that the low-lift pumps bring water from the Lake into the
Water Plant for treatment. The parts are submerged, and repairs are time-
consuming; therefore, the 2021 Budget contains funding for a spare pump in the
event a unit is taken out of service for a prolonged time. The new pump’s
specifications must match the specifications of the existing pumps and bidders
were requested to identify a repair facility for their proposed unit in the
Chicago area. The bidder that was rejected responded with a repair facility
that has no experience with the pump they offered. In light of this issue,
their bid was deemed non-responsive. The remaining two bidders are known to the
Village and responded with qualifying bids, prompting staff to recommend
awarding the bid to the lower of the two qualified bidders.
After a brief discussion, Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to
adopt Resolution No. R-4-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-1-2021: 1180 Oakley Avenue Zoning Variations (Introduction &
Adoption). Manager Bahan explained the next four Ordinances are eligible for a
potential waiver of introduction because it has been the Council’s preference
that land use items recommended for approval by the advisory boards with no
dissentions or public opposition are noticed on the agenda for waiver of
introduction and adoption to give the Council the prerogative of streamlining
the approval process. There is no requirement, however, that the Council waive
introduction.
Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for zoning variations at the Subject Property
which, if granted, would allow the homeowners to improve an existing older home
rather than demolish it. The Zoning Board of Appeals voted unanimously to
recommend approval of the variations, since the front elevation of the home
would be preserved.
The applicant, Krzysztof Marzec, explained that he is moving to Winnetka from
the city; and it was his original intention to demolish the home and replace it
with a new structure. During discussion of the demolition permit application at
the Landmark Preservation Commission (LPC) meeting, it became clear that the
home had historic significance, and he modified his plans. Since the home is
structurally sound, an addition is feasible; however, in order to modernize the
home and make it functional for modern standards, the gross floor area will be
exceeded, and a variation is being sought.
All of the Trustees expressed appreciation for Mr. Marzec’s efforts to preserve
the home; Trustee Swierk suggested matching the new architecture with the look
of the older home. Trustee Dearborn commended the LPC Commissioners for helping
to achieve a good outcome for the home.
There being no public comment, Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Coladarci,
moved to waive introduction of Ordinance No. M-1-2021. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier,
and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Ordinance
No. M-1-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-2-2021: 839-841 Elm – Berkshire Hathaway Special Use Permit
(Introduction/Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for a special use
permit to allow a real estate office to relocate to a peripheral location in
the Retail Overlay District. The proposed new office space was previously
occupied by Ellen’s on Elm and has been vacant for the past year. If the
request is approved, the space currently occupied by Berkshire Hathaway, which
is located on a high-profile corner in the Retail Overlay, will be required to
conform with the Zoning Ordinance.
Jim Nash, the applicant, explained that the storefront currently occupied by
Berkshire Hathaway is activated with seasonal displays; and the real estate
firm is a good neighbor in the Elm Business District. The business model has
changed and fewer people meet at the office, so parking in the area will not be
significantly impacted by the granting of the special use permit.
Terry Dason, Director of the Chamber of Commerce, agreed that Berkshire
Hathaway is a very good neighbor and she expressed excitement about the soon to
be vacated corner of Elm and Chestnut Streets.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Coladarci, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-2-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried Ayes: Trustees
Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Ordinance
No. M-2-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-3-2021: 930 Green Bay – KMK Luxury Consignment Special Use
Permit (Introduction/Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for a luxury
consignment store to permanently occupy a storefront where it had a successful
run as a pop-up shop. He explained that it is considered a second-hand store
under the Zoning Ordinance and is therefore a special use. The Plan Commission
unanimously recommended approval of the special use permit with no conditions.
Ms. Dason encouraged the Council to approve the special use permit, as the
store fits in well with the Hubbard Woods Design District.
The Council was in unanimous agreement to approve the request.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-3-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-3-
2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Ordinance No. M-4-2021: 1415-1423 Asbury Subdivision and Variations
(Introduction/Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed this request to relocate the lot
line between the two Subject Lots, which would allow the owners of the Subject
Properties to demolish the home on 1423 Asbury, keep the home on 1415 Asbury,
and construct a one-car garage.
The Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan Commission both voted unanimously to
recommend approval of the request with no conditions; and the Landmark
Preservation Commission approved the demolition of the home at 1423 Asbury with
no delays.
Cal Bernstein, attorney for the property owners. Mr. Bernstein gave background
on the two lots and explained the rationale for the request.
After the Trustees briefly discussed the request with Mr. Bernstein, there was
consensus to waive introduction and approve the request.
There being no public comment, Trustee Coladarci, seconded by Trustee Swierk,
