Recording Secretary
March 2, 2021
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR VIRTUAL MEETING
March 2, 2021
(Approved: April 8, 2021)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Tuesday, March 21, 2021, at 7:00PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Jack Coladarci, Andrew Cripe,
Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, and John Swierk. Absent: None. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Deputy Fire Chief John Ripka,
Community Development Director David Schoon, and approximately 37 persons in
the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Coladarci led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   March 9, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend. Manager Bahan explained that this meeting would be
scheduled as a special meeting to review The Walden planned development.
   b)   March 16, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   c)   Thursday, April 8, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
Rick Kasten, Winnetka resident. Mr. Kasten asked if the Village can do anything
about coyotes in Winnetka. Manager Bahan explained that because Winnetka is so
close to the Forest Preserves, there is little the Village can do to contain
the animals. He said the Village does not have a program to cull Coyotes; and
he offered to call Mr. Kasten and discuss the issue with him further.
5)   Reports:
   a)   Trustees. None.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager. Manager Bahan reported that on Wednesday, February 24 Winnetka
participated in a Covid-19 vaccine clinic in conjunction with the Cook County
Department of Public Health and in partnership with other area municipalities.
Nearly 500 people were vaccinated that day, and Assistant Village Manager
Kristin Kazenas was the lead Winnetka official helping to run the clinic.
   d)   Village President. President Rintz commended Manager Bahan, Fire Chief
Berkowsky, and the Wilmette Village Manager for persisting with Cook County to
get the vaccine doses and administer them to essential personnel in the MABAS
jurisdiction.
6)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
7)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   February 2, 2021 Regular Virtual Meeting.
      ii)   February 9, 2021 Virtual Study Session.
      iii)   February 16, 2021 Regular Virtual Meeting.
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated February 12 - 25, 2021 in the amount of
$1,399,495.07.
   c)   Resolution No. R-30-2021: Refuse Containers and RFID Equipment Purchase
(Adoption).
   d)   Resolution No. R-31-2021: Landscape Maintenance Services Contract
(Adoption).
   e)   Resolution No. R-32-2021: Use of MFT Funding for Street Rehabilitation
(Adoption).
   f)   Resolution No. R-33-2021: Security for Construction on State Highways
(Adoption).
   g)   Resolution No. R-34-2021: Purchase of 600 Volt Underground Cable
(Adoption).
   h)   Resolution No. R-36-2021: 566 Chestnut Street Easement Agreement
(Adoption).
   i)   Resolution No. R-37-2021: 852 Spruce Street Easement Agreement
(Adoption).
   j)   Resolution No. R-38-2021: Withdrawal from Intergovernmental Personnel
Benefit Cooperative Sub-Pool and Authorizing Petition for Full Membership
(Adoption).
Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and
Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution No. R-35-2021: Creating a Regional Fire Training Tower
Cooperative (Adoption). Deputy Chief Ripka explained that Winnetka’s fire
training tower needs significant repairs; hoping to save costs for Winnetka,
the Fire Department approached three neighboring municipalities to form a
regional training cooperative. The cooperative would share costs associated
with upkeep and maintenance of the training tower facility in exchange for use
of the tower to train their personnel. Training officers from each department
will meet regularly to ensure smooth operation of the facility.
   There was no public comment and no questions or comments from the Council.
This matter was discussed by the Council at the 2021 Budget meeting.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Resolution No.
R-35-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2021: Demolition Delay & Building Size Bonus for
Historic Homes (Public Hearing and Introduction). President Rintz opened the
Public Hearing at 7:21 PM. He explained that public comment would be taken
immediately after
   Mr. Schoon reviewed the Council’s discussion leading to the drafting of a
Code amendment Ordinance to extend the current demolition delay and add a
provision for a gross floor area (GFA) bonus for those homeowners who choose to
preserve their historic home rather than demolish it. Pursuant to thorough
discussion at previous Council meetings, the following provisions have been
added to the draft Ordinance:
   •   The 270-day demolition delay period would begin on the date the LPC
holds its preliminary review of a demolition application. Currently, a typical
delay period for a home found to be significant is 150-180 days from the
submittal of a demolition application; under the new amendment, a typical delay
period would be 300-330 days from the date of application.
   •   Applicants going through the demolition process who receive a
determination from the LPC that their home is historically or architecturally
significant have the option to change their plans and pursue renovation of the
home using the GFA bonus. In addition, homeowners who believe their home is
significant may apply to the LPC for the GFA bonus.
   •   The Ordinance provisions will apply to current demolition permits
already under consideration by the Landmark Preservation Commission (LPC).
Given that these demolition applications are already in process, a demolition
delay issued in these instances for a home found to be significant would begin
from the date the application was submitted and would be 270 days.
   •   Criteria has been added to better define critical exterior historical/
architectural features that must be preserved once a GFA bonus is received.
   •   Properties previously found to be historically/architecturally
significant which have been preserved are eligible to apply for the GFA bonus.
Landmarks would automatically qualify for the GFA bonus if they have not been
altered inappropriately as determined by the LPC.
   Mr. Schoon explained that his department conducted research in response to a
question about how the GFA bonus would affect other zoning provisions for the
property. He stressed that any zoning relief sought under these conditions will
need to be evaluated on a case-by-case basis since each parcel is unique. In
most instances, conforming lots should be able to accommodate the additional
GFA without needing additional zoning relief; however, smaller lots may need
setback relief, depending upon the layout and configuration of the home.
   Ms. Kazenas began polling the audience for comments.
   Scott Myers, Winnetka resident and former Village Trustee. Mr. Myers noted
that the balance between the public good and individual rights has been skewed
in recent years with the result that too many significant homes worth
preserving have been demolished. However, he cautioned against finding too many
homes to be architecturally or historically significant and called for a
Council appeals process to provide a check on the LPC determinations.
   Beth Ann Papoutsis, LPC Commissioner. Ms. Papoutsis said not every vintage
property reviewed by the LPC is deemed significant and in need of an HAIS. The
LPC carefully evaluates each demolition application using input from the
Winnetka Historical Society, Landmarks Illinois, and neighbor testimony. She
expressed support for preserving homes that are truly historically or
architecturally significant.
   Scott from Brightleaf urged the Council to apply the 270-day demolition
delay on the date a demolition application is submitted, since the completion
of an HAIS can take 60- 90 days. Attorney Friedman clarified that the start of
the delay period for applications already underway when the Ordinance is
potentially passed is the date the application was submitted. Mr. Schoon
further clarified that going forward, the 270-day delay will begin on the date
the LPC holds it preliminary review of the demolition application, not the date
an HAIS is received.
   Leo Birov, Winnetka resident. Mr. Birov said a longer demolition delay
affects the value of the homes and that the current economic outlook is too
uncertain to make changes to it. In addition, he called for input from the
Caucus and a referendum from the community before enacting the Subject
Ordinance.
   Nan Greenough, Winnetka resident and former Chair of the LPC. Ms. Greenough
noted studies show that landmarked properties do not suffer in property values;
the standards are very well defined and national standards are used in
Winnetka’s landmark ordinance; and the numerical values developed for historic
characteristics are applied in a consistent manner.
   Jeff Jaymont, Winnetka resident. Mr. Jaymont expressed his concern that the
Council was overreaching its authority by considering the Subject Ordinance and
could lead to complications that pit neighbor against neighbor. He agreed that
a community referendum should decide the issue.
   Betsy Owens, 228 Myrtle Street. Ms. Owens asked how many homes are likely to
go through the demolition review process. President Rintz said this would be
discussed during Council deliberation.
   Laura Good, LPC Commissioner. Ms. Good commended the Council’s efforts in
trying to preserve Winnetka’s significant properties. She recommended the
community listen to a presentation called “The Power of Uniqueness” from April
25, 2017 on YouTube. She said preservation of significant properties
contributes to Winnetka’s beauty, history, and culture.
   Mickey Archambault, Winnetka resident. Mr. Archambault said the sellers of
homes that are potentially significant should be required to inform potential
buyers that a demolition might be a difficult endeavor. He called for the
creation of a list of significant homes in Winnetka and agreed that a community
referendum is called for.
   Chris Enck, LPC Commissioner. Mr. Enck said the LPC sees many demolition
applications in relation to the size of the Village and he expressed support
for the Subject Ordinance as it will bring Winnetka in line with neighboring
communities.
   Dina Sporel, Maple Street. Ms. Sporel expressed support for the Subject
Ordinance as the diversity of the housing stock was one of the reasons she
moved to the Village. She added that too many Winnetka homes that should have
been preserved have been demolished.
   Katie Comstock, 854 Prospect Avenue. Ms. Comstock expressed support for the
Subject Ordinance, as Winnetka’s architectural heritage needs to be preserved.
   Ms. Kazenas read emails from the following people in favor of the Subject
Ordinance: Dan Waters, 463 Willow Road; Nancy & Bill Yurek, 647 Lincoln Avenue;
Sandy & Greg Welch, Winnetka residents; Patti VanCleave, 1047 Elm Street; Mindy
Turitz, Winnetka resident; Mary Trieschmann, Director of the Winnetka
Historical Society; Melissa Schaefer, 985 Spruce Street; Debbie & Jeff Ross,
Winnetka residents; Mike & Nancy Ritter, 255 Chestnut Street; Julie Pagliaro,
848 Lincoln Avenue; J.A.Offerle, 112 Fuller Lane; Cathy Nowacki, 708 Prospect
Avenue; Lori Nielsen, 567 Oak Street; Dave Murdoch, Pine Street; Maggie
Meiners, 480 Willow Road; Suzy & Tom Lyman, Winnetka residents; Joe Long, 700
Ardsley; Justine Hourihane, 491 Hawthorn Lane; Sara Ann & Joel Harris, 516
Orchard Lane; Elisha & Nina Gray, 1125 Spruce Street; Jennifer Felderman, 530
Willow Road; Joan Evanich, 779 Bryant Avenue; Lisa & Cary Cochrane, 270
Fairview Avenue; Mary-Audrey Atteberry, 769 Foxdale; Linda & Peter Arts,
Winnetka residents; Bethany Crocker, 559 Oak Street; Susan Curry, 375 Hawthorn
Lane; Nancy Davis, 637 Hill Road; and James & Penny DeYoung, Winnetka
residents.
   Ms. Kazenas read emails from the following people against passage of the
Subject Ordinance: Chris Veech, Winnetka resident; John Phillips, Kenilworth
resident; Bill Phillips, 885 Hill Road; George Pappas, 1024 Cherry Street; Mark
Mitchell, 224 Sheridan Road; Sarah Elder Lyons, Winnetka resident; Josh,
Winnetka resident; and Steve Aisen, Newgard Homes.
   President Rintz reminded the Trustees that the preservation issue has been
extensively discussed in prior meetings, and much public comment has been
heard. Therefore, tonight’s discussion should be limited to the amendments to
the draft Ordinance. He asked if the Subject Ordinance provides an appeal in
the event a homeowner does not agree with an LPC decision. Attorney Friedman
explained that there is an appeal for a determination about renovations to a
significant home. Any further appeals should be discussed by the Council.
   Trustee Cripe agreed that an appeal for property owners unhappy with either
a demolition delay determination or a denial of the GFA bonus should be
provided. He asked if the application fee is refunded in the event a demolition
permit application is withdrawn. Mr. Schoon explained that a small portion is
kept for Village processing expenses, but the bulk of the fee is returned to
the applicant if the demolition does not go forward.
   Trustee Swierk urged the Council to continue the discussion to another
night, as property rights are involved, and it makes sense to involve the
Caucus or call for a community referendum. He suggested the Village create a
review board to review plans for new homes and renovations to older homes.
   Trustee Lanphier expressed surprise that not much comment touched on the GFA
preservation bonus incentive; and she disagreed that major changes are being
made to the preservation ordinance that would merit a community referendum. She
was supportive of the Subject Ordinance as it gives room to allow a demolition
applicant to rethink their decision and perhaps preserve a significant home.
   Trustee Apatoff said the community is largely in favor of pausing the
demolition of significant homes and that some of the people against the Subject
Ordinance are people who benefit financially from teardowns that do not live in
Winnetka. He refuted assertions that preservation efforts harm home values or
that they scare young families away from Winnetka.
   Trustee Swierk asked how many homes would be affected by the Subject
Ordinance.
   Mr. Schoon explained that in the last five years there have been 106
demolition applications; of that total the LPC asked for 21 HAIS reports, and
ultimately 7 properties were issued demolition delays.
   Trustee Dearborn reiterated that the Subject Ordinance is an interim step
until the Comprehensive Plan is updated in the next 18 months or so; the
process is not being changed and the incentive in the form of a GFA bonus for
preserving significant homes needs to be given as much attention as the
demolition delay. In addition, he agreed that an appeal process should be
provided for those homeowners who disagree with an LPC determination.
   Trustee Coladarci noted that seven homes were given demolition delays over a
five-year period, so the new delay provision will not affect a significant
number of applicants seeking a demolition permit. He explained that the HAIS
reports are professionally done and provide very detailed information
architecturally or historically significant homes. He added that most
applications for demolition permits are approved without delays.
   Trustee Cripe expressed support for the Subject Ordinance, as it is not
extreme and deals with the problem of significant homes being demolished in
Winnetka. He thanked the community for its interest and comments, and cautioned
that there will never be 100% consensus either for or against preservation
efforts.
   Attorney Friedman said a determination of historic or architectural
significance could be made appealable to the Council and an appeal provision
added to provide a homeowner who is denied the GFA bonus.
   President Rintz explained that Winnetka Councils do not historically govern
by referendum, preferring to make the hard choices after public participation
and thoughtful deliberation.
   Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. MC-2-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, and Lanphier. Nays: Trustee Swierk. Absent: None.
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
None.
11)   Appointments.
None.
12)   Closed Session.
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Coladarci, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned
at 9:30PM.
 
