Recording Secretary
March 9, 2021
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL VIRTUAL MEETING
March 9, 2021
(Approved: April 8, 2021)
A record of a legally convened special meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Tuesday, March 9, 2021 at 7:00PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll. Present: Trustees Jack Coladarci, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn,
Penfield Lanphier, and John Swierk. Absent: Trustee Robert Apatoff. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Community Development Director David Schoon, Village Attorney Ben
Schuster, and approximately 11 persons in the audience.
2)   Public Comment.
None.
3)   Ordinance No. M-6-2021: 688-694 Green Bay Road – The Walden Preliminary
Planned Development (Introduction).
President Rintz explained that the evening’s discussion would begin with a
presentation by the Community Development Director, followed by a presentation
by the developer, then public comment, and lastly Council questions and
comments.
[Trustee Apatoff arrived at 7:05 PM.]
   Mr. Schoon explained that the site of the proposed Planned Development (PD)
at 688-694 Green Bay Road is approximately 1/3 of an acre and consists of two
parcels, one vacant and one containing a single-family home. The property is
located in the B1 Multi-family Residential Zoning District and is adjacent to
B1 Multi-family, B2 Multi-family, and R3 Single Family Residential districts
and is situated across from the Metra tracks.
   Mr. Schoon said the planned development has gone through the pre-application
meeting and concept plan review, and tonight will complete the preliminary plan
review before the PD potentially undergoes a final plan review in the future.
During its concept plan review, the Village Council was generally favorably
inclined to the proposed development; however, several issues were recommended
for consideration: (i) impact of the development on homes immediately to the
west; (ii) impact of building height on neighbors; (iii) stormwater mitigation;
(iv) parking and driveway access; and (v) site and access of refuse bins.
   The formal PD review process began with the Design Review Board (DRB)
reviewing the original design: a four-story building with six units. During the
DRB’s review, neighbors expressed concern with the height and bulk of the
building; stormwater impacts; construction excavation; noise and light from the
driveway; and screening of the building. The DRB approved the design subject to
a 1.7-foot height reduction.
   Next, the Planned Development Commission (PDC) reviewed the proposal on
October 19, 2020. Public comments similar to those heard at the DRB review were
expressed, along with concerns about the impact on property values and public
benefit; after deliberation, PDC recommended denial of the proposal because the
exceptions for building size and impermeable lot coverage were too large; the
two members in support of approval pointed out that the development is in line
with multi-family zoning, and they did not think the project would unreasonably
impede the use or development of adjacent properties.
   Next Mr. Schoon compared the original design with the current Amended Plan.
The building footprint remains the same, although the orientation in the
Amended Plan has been rotated to facilitate site access. The building has been
shifted ten feet to the south, and two parking spaces moved from the rear of
the building to the front yard. To better shield the property from the
neighbors to the west, a four-foot retaining wall to create a berm is proposed
in the Amended Plan, with the top planted with Norway Spruce trees and the
lower level lined with Arbor Vitae. Other changes made in the Amended Plan: (i)
the stormwater vault will be located underneath the berm and stormwater will
release to Green Bay Road; (ii) the building height is reduced to three
stories; (iii) the number of units is reduced from six to five; (iv)
underground parking spaces have been reduced and storage units relocated in the
parking area; (v) the western side of the building contains two stories rather
than three; the former sixth unit has been replaced with an outdoor garden
space; and (vi) the outdoor roof deck will only be used by the third floor unit
owners. Parking access has been improved with an ADA accessible space and the
applicant proposes to heat the driveway.
   The Amended Plan requests approval of a plat of consolidation to combine the
two parcels into one lot, and also requires variations from the following
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance: (i) maximum building size; (ii) maximum
building height; (iii) required principal roof form; (iv) exterior wall
articulation for the south façade; and (v) front yard setback. An $18,000
donation will be made to the Village for an EV charging station as a public
benefit.
   On February 3, 2021 the PDC reviewed the Amended Plan, and after its
deliberation voted unanimously to recommend approval of the development and the
associated zoning relief. As a result, the Council will consider introducing an
Ordinance approving the preliminary plat of subdivision and zoning variations
subject to the following conditions: (i) the outdoor roof deck is limited to
the use of the third floor residents only; (ii) an $18,000 contribution will be
made to the Village for an EV charging station; (iii) the development will have
on-site EV charging stations; (iv) additional landscaping will be installed and
maintained; and (v) the Final Plan must be submitted to the Village within 18
months from approval of the Preliminary Plan.
   Mr. Schoon noted that one written protest from a neighbor has been received;
this is not sufficient to require a supermajority of the Council to approve the
Preliminary Plan.
   Rick Swanson, the project developer, said he had worked with the neighbors
at the request of the Council during the Concept Plan Review stage, as well
Village staff and the PDC, to arrive at a preliminary plan that most neighbors
are comfortable with. He noted that the landscaping will be maintained through
the use of a homeowner’s association, the PD will share the cost of a new fence
with the neighbor to the north to alleviate privacy concerns, and a heated
driveway will be installed to enhance safety. He added that he will continue to
work with the neighbors throughout the construction phase.
President Rintz called public comment.
   Mary Jo Bushell resident to the west. Ms. Bushell objected to the proposed
development on the grounds that stormwater would run onto her property and
asked for a guarantee that the stormwater will be detained. She also stated the
balconies of the proposed development would be intrusive when she is using her
yard and she urged the Village Council to visit her yard to gauge the
invasiveness of the balconies.
   Mary Ellen Stanfield, 680 Green Bay Road. Ms. Stanfield said she thought the
proposed building is too big for the neighborhood.
   Brandie Siavelis, 415 Cedar Street. Ms. Siavelis urged the Council to
approve the project in the interest of progress for the Village, as the
proposal will be beautiful and appeal to empty nesters in Winnetka.
   Ruud Coster, 696 Green Bay Road. Mr. Coster expressed concern that the
development will lead to other large buildings in the Green Bay Corridor, and
he asked the Council to keep these concerns in mind.
   Trustee Swierk asked about parking requirements and the EV charging stations
and stated that he thought enough public benefit was provided by improving the
property and the associated increased tax base for the Village.
   Trustee Dearborn commended the developer for making adjustments in response
to neighbor concerns and he noted that the neighbors deserve assurances that
stormwater runoff will not impact neighboring properties. Mr. Schoon explained
that the site currently has no detention even though there is impermeable
surface from existing development. The project will provide underground
detention designed to handle the additional impermeable surface run- off from a
100-year storm which will provide overflow capacity to keep the water onsite
until it is discharged to Green Bay Road. The Village Engineer has approved the
preliminary design of the system and will also review and approve the final
design at the Final Plan approval phase.
   President Rintz added that a modern storage system will be installed whereas
there is no storage currently, which should provide comfort to the neighbors on
the stormwater issues.
   Asked about the safety of the parking ramp, Mr. Swanson explained that
safety of pedestrian on the sidewalk, as well as vehicular traffic on Green Bay
Road, was taken into consideration in designing the parking. He added that the
drainage will be significantly improved with the installation of an underground
stormwater detention vault.
   Trustee Apatoff asked about the location of the boiler for the heated
driveway, as it could provide noise issues for the neighbors. Mr. Swanson said
the system has not yet been finalized; however, if a boiler is used, it would
be placed in the garage. He added that the residential heating and cooling
units will be on the roof and screened; and the electric panels will be placed
in the garage.
   Trustee Lanphier asked if there was any option other than the sloped
driveway on Green Bay Road. Mr. Swanson explained that the space constraints on
the lot limit the options for driveway access and after exploring all the
possibilities, the current plan is the safest.
   Trustee Cripe asked where the proposal stands with neighbor objections. Mr.
Schoon said the neighbors find the building attractive, and improvements have
been made, but the neighbors who have been coming to the PDC and DRB meetings
still have objections to the height and size of the proposed development. Only
one written objection has been received; the Village would need to receive
written objections from 20% of the residents within 250 feet of the proposed
development to trigger a supermajority vote of the Village Council.
   The Council discussed the proposal and it was agreed that this type of
development is the right choice for this portion of the Green Bay Road
corridor; the developer has done an excellent job of working with the
neighbors; the advisory boards thoroughly vetted the proposal; the design is
pleasing; the stormwater detention is a benefit to the area; and the
development fills a need in Winnetka.
   Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Cripe moved to introduce Ordinance No.
M-6-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:18PM.
 
