Recording Secretary
March 16, 2021
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR VIRTUAL MEETING
March 16, 2021
(Approved: April 8, 2021)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Tuesday, March 16, 2021 at 7:00PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:07 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn,
Penfield Lanphier, and John Swierk. Absent: Trustee Jack Coladarci. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Community Development Director David
Schoon, Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus, Village Attorney
Peter Friedman, and approximately 12 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Dearborn led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   Thursday, April 8, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
   b)   April 13, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   c)   April 20, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
Richard Kates, Tower Road. Mr. Kates said his concern that public comment could
not be heard on a Zoom meeting have been alleviated and he would comment later
in the meeting on an agenda item.
Theresa Lukas, owner of Good Grapes. Ms. Lukas asked if the Village would
modify its position on special events in 2021 in light of announcements that
Governor Pritzker would soon announce Phase 5 of Restore Illinois Plan;
President Rintz responded in the negative.
5)   Reports:
   a)   Trustees. None.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager. None.
   d)   Village President. None.
6)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes. None.
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated February 26 – March 11, 2021 in the
amount of $382,082.66.
   c)   Resolution No. R-40-2021: Repair of Groundwater Monitoring Wells
Repairs at Municipal Landfill (Adoption).
   d)   Resolution No. R-41-2021: Uniform Service Contract(Adoption).
   e)   Resolution No. R-42-2021: Maintenance of Water Plan Raw Water Intake
(Adoption).
   f)   Resolution No. R-43-2021: Allowing Outdoor Dining and Liquor Service
During COVID-19 Emergency (Adoption).
   g)   Outdoor Seating Permits.
   Ms. Lukas said the agenda report on outdoor seating had inaccurate numbers
for her seating chart; and she asked if street closures are also approved with
the outdoor seating. Manager Bahan responded in the affirmative. She requested
that in the future, the Council consider Outdoor Seating in February instead of
March to help businesses better plan their outdoor events for the year.
President Rintz said he would discuss a scheduling change for next year with
Manager Bahan.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to approve the
foregoing items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier,
and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-7-2021: 1081-1083 Gage – North Shore Barber Special Use
Permit (Introduction & Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for a
special use permit to allow the expansion of an existing barber shop. The Plan
Commission unanimously approved the request at its February meeting.
   There being no public comment or discussion by the Council, Trustee Swierk,
seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to waive introduction of Ordinance No. M-7-
2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Coladarci.
   Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
7-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Coladarci.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-2-2021: Demolition Delay & Building Size Bonus for
Historic Homes (Adoption). President Rintz explained that the Council has
thoroughly discussed the Subject Ordinance, heard considerable comment at the
Public Hearing at introduction, and made revisions based on public feedback and
Council discussion.
   Mr. Schoon confirmed that the Ordinance has been amended to provide three
opportunities for applicants to appeal Landmark Preservation Commission (LPC)
determinations: (i) if a home is found by the LPC to be architecturally/
historically significant and a demolition delay is issued: (ii) if during a
process to apply for a GFA bonus the LPC rules that a home is not eligible
because it is not architecturally/historically significant; and (iii) if the
LPC determines a home is not significant during a demolition application
process and the owner disagrees.
   Trustee Lanphier asked if the appeal process is similar to a zoning appeal
process; Attorney Friedman explained that it is similar. However, in zoning
cases there are instances where the Zoning Board of Appeals (ZBA) has final
authority over minor types of zoning relief. Since the LPC is an advisory body
and not a quasi-judicial one like the ZBA, the appeal process provides that an
LPC final decision may be appealed to the Village Council.
President Rintz called for public comment.
   Louise Holland, former Chair of the LPC. Ms. Holland commended the Council
for addressing the issue of preservation and she thanked the Council, the LPC
members, Village staff and Attorney Friedman for helping to develop the Subject
Ordinance.
   Beth Ann Papoutsis, LPC Commissioner. Ms. Papoutsis thanked the Council for
its effort in amending the Preservation Ordinance; she commented that all
vintage properties in Winnetka are not considered significant, and demolition
applications are very carefully evaluated by the LPC. The HAIS is only required
by the LPC for a select few properties that have been deemed significant by
experts and neighbors, leaving ample room for new construction in Winnetka. The
Subject Ordinance will help preserve the beauty and character of the Village.
   Richard Kates, Tower Road. Mr. Kates said the owners of potentially
significant properties should have been notified of the Council’s discussion of
the Preservation Ordinance amendments. He recommended an emergency provision be
included in the Subject Ordinance for owners to appeal directly to the Village
Council if an emergency need arises.
[Trustee Coladarci arrived at 7:50 PM.]
Next, President Rintz called for any last Council discussion.
   Trustee Swierk reiterated his call for the creation of a list of potentially
historic homes in Winnetka; and he said the Council appears to be acting in
contrast to community opinion in the 2019 Winnetka Caucus survey. He did not
feel the Council should adopt the Subject Ordinance.
   Trustee Cripe clarified that the 2019 Caucus survey did not show consensus
one way or another on the issue of preservation, and that the Council is tasked
with making a decision for the community based on its best information and
public engagement.
   Trustee Apatoff said the Council has taken a thorough review and that most
public comment received about the Subject Ordinance has been strongly in favor
of preserving historic properties if possible.
   Trustee Lanphier noted that the Caucus survey is a limited tool for gauging
public opinion and should be taken as a starting point, not a final community
determination. She added that the function of the survey is to inform Village
boards about the community’s preliminary thoughts on the issues; however, it is
the board’s decision to gather information and take final action. She supported
adoption of the Subject Ordinance.
   Trustee Coladarci reiterated his previous comments on the preservation issue
and said he would vote in favor of the Subject Ordinance. The Comprehensive
Plan update will provide long term and detailed guidance on the preservation
issue.
   President Rintz said he does not confer a negative bias on a comment based
upon someone’s profession or any other aspect of their status; disagreement
does not make anyone wrong; rather, it just means the issue is controversial.
He commended the Trustees for representing the community to the best of their
abilities.
   Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Ordinance No.
MC-2-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, and Lanphier. Nays: Trustee Swierk. Absent: None.
   c)   Resolution No. R-39-2021: Historic Maximum Building Size Application
Fees & Escrow (Adoption). Mr. Schoon explained that the Fee Resolution needs to
be amended to cover Village expenses associated with the passage of Ordinance
No. MC-2-2021. The fees being amended are: (i) preliminary property history
study; (ii) maximum building bonus size application fee; (iii) escrow amount
for the administrative review of an addition or renovation to a historic
property granted the GFA bonus; and (iv) a fee to appeal an LPC determination.
There was no public comment.
   Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No.
R-39-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier. Nays: Trustee Swierk. Absent: None. and
9)   Old Business.
None.
10)   New Business.
None.
11)   Appointments:
None.
12)   Closed Session.
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:07PM.
 
