Recording Secretary
April 8, 2021
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR VIRTUAL MEETING
April 8, 2021
(Approved: April 20, 2021)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Thursday, April 8, at 7:00PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Manager Bahan Called
the roll Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn,
Penfield Lanphier, and John Swierk. Absent: Trustee Jack Coladarci. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Community Development Director David
Schoon, Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky, and approximately 21 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Lanphier led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   April 13, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present except
Trustee Swierk said they expect to attend.
   b)   April 20, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   c)   May 4, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
   Mark Cison, 988 Elm Street. Mr. Cison urged the Village Council to lift mask
mandates in public spaces in Winnetka.
   Crow Island School third grade class video comment. Ms. Cook’s class of
third graders asked the Village Council to consider approving another dog park
in Winnetka. President Rintz explained that talks have been held with the Park
District about construction of a potential new dog park in conjunction with
stormwater improvements in West Winnetka.
5)   Presentation – Cook County Board President Toni Preckwinkle.
   President Rintz said Winnetka owes a debt of gratitude to President
Preckwinkle, Forest Preserve Superintendent Arnold Randall, and Cook County
Commissioner Larry Suffredin for their support of the Village’s stormwater
wetlands detention project. He also extended his personal thanks to Ms.
Preckwinkle and the Cook County Department of Public Health for allowing the
establishment of the MABAS 3 Covid-19 vaccination site at New Trier West High
School, where over 6,300 vaccinations have already been administered to
essential governmental employees under the supervision of Winnetka and Wilmette
staff, and where vaccines will soon be offered to community members at large.
   President Preckwinkle said the 2021 Cook County budget is balanced and
contains no new property tax increases. Funding is included for criminal
justice reform, a vaccination program, small business aid, and public
transportation. She said the MABAS 3 site is the only MABAS site in Cook County
and she thanked Winnetka for its support; other County mass vaccination sites
are in Des Plaines, Tinley Park, Forest Park, River Forest, and South Holland.
She encouraged all in the community over 16 years of age to make a vaccine
appointment after April 12 and urged the use of masking and other precautions
to contain the spread of Covid variants, which are more easily spread and
potentially more lethal.
6)   Reports:
   a)   Trustees. None.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager. Manager Bahan said on April 13, area municipalities will host
a Jewel-Osco vaccine clinic at New Trier West for service workers in the area.
New doses of Moderna are scheduled to be administered in the near future.
   d)   Village President. President Rintz said a new clothing shop opened in
Hubbard Woods last week; he reminded residents and businesses that gas powered
leaf blowers are not permitted during the spring and summer months and he will
be encouraging enforcement of Winnetka’s leaf blower law; and he reported on a
meeting he attended with Village engineering staff to interview for grant
funding for improving the Green Bay Trail.
7)   Proclamation Honoring Retiring Fire Chief Alan Berkowsky.
   President Rintz lost his computer connection at 7:27 PM. President Pro Tem
Cripe read aloud a proclamation honoring the accomplishments of Winnetka’s Fire
Chief, Alan Berkowsky, who is retiring after ten years of service to Winnetka.
[President Rintz regained connectivity at 7:31 PM.]
   The Council members and Manager Bahan each expressed their appreciation for
everything Chief Berkowsky has done for the Winnetka community and for his
leadership in the Fire Department, and all agreed that he will be missed.
   Chief Berkowsky said it was an honor to work in Winnetka, and he commended
the professionalism of the Village Council. He noted that the Winnetka Fire
Department has excellent staff and the Village is in good hands.
   Scott Myers, former Village Trustee. Mr. Myers also thanked Chief Berkowsky
for his leadership of the Fire Department and especially for his assistance
during the Winnetka Music Festival to help keep the event running smoothly and
safely.
   Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adopt the honorary
Proclamation for Chief Berkowsky. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent:
Trustee Coladarci.
8)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the Agenda By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Coladarci.
9)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   March 2, 2021 Regular Meeting.
      ii)   March 9, 2021 Special Meeting.
      iii)   March 16, 2021 Regular Meeting.
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated March 12 – April 1, 2021 in the amount
of $1,266,053.71.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-6-2021: 688-694 Green Bay Road – The Walden Preliminary
Planned Development (Adoption).
   d)   Resolution No. R-40-2021: Repair Groundwater Monitoring Wells at
Municipal Landfill (Adoption).
   e)   Resolution No. R-45-2021: Extending Temporary Tent Approval for Schools
(Adoption).
   f)   Resolution No. R-46-2021: Purchase of Leaf Collector (Adoption).
   g)   Resolution No. R-47-2021: Curbside Collection Refuse Rates (Adoption).
   h)   Resolution No. R-48-2021: Purchase of Pad-Mount Metering Gear
(Adoption).
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Coladarci.
10)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-8-2021: 651 Elm Street Variation (Introduction /
Adoption). Mr.Schoon reviewed this request for a front yard setback variation
for new construction which was recommended for approval by the Zoning Board of
Appeals (ZBA). The Village Council must approve all zoning variations for new
construction. He explained that the site has been vacant since 2007; the
applicant proposes constructing a new single-family home that complies with all
zoning requirements except the front yard setback. New is required construction
to observe the average of the front yard setbacks on the block face; however,
since two of the three neighboring properties have a much larger setback than
is the norm for this zoning district, the Subject Property would be required to
provide a 60-foot front yard setback under strict observance of the Zoning
Ordinance. The applicant proposes a setback of 40+ feet; the ZBA considered the
request including hearing from three neighbors in support of the variation; and
it voted unanimously to recommend approval of the variation.
   Trustee Lanphier asked if there is any reason that the property shouldn’t be
permitted to observe the 30-foot front yard for the R-4 Zoning District, since
she was not certain the standards the Subject Property are being asked to meet
make sense in this situation.
   Trustee Cripe explained that he wouldn’t like to see the home any closer to
the street, as it would affect the views of the neighbors and bisect the block
face.
   Trustee Lanphier asked where stormwater detention would be. Mr. Schoon
explained that the construction will have to meet Village stormwater
requirements but that plans are not yet finalized.
   Elliot Wiczer, attorney for the applicants. Mr. Wiczer explained that the
setback variation would help the new owners preserve several large trees at the
rear of the property. Matt Burdeen, the applicant, noted that stormwater
detention requirements for the Subject Property may affect how many of the
trees can be saved, and he added that he is working with Village engineers on
stormwater detention.
There was no public comment.
   The Trustees were in favor of approving the request and waiving introduction
of the Ordinance.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-8-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Apatoff, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Coladarci.
   Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Ordinance No.
M-8-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Coladarci.
11)   Old Business.
None.
12)   New Business.
   a)   880 Willow Zoning Variations (Policy Direction). Mr. Schoon explained
that the owners of the Subject Property were granted gross floor area and front
and side yard setback variations in 2019 to allow construction of a new home.
At that time, no garage was proposed for the site. Tonight, the Council will
discuss a request to approve variations from impermeable lot coverage and
roofed lot coverage limitations to allow construction of a detached garage in
the rear quarter of the property. Mr. Schoon noted that it appears the property
owner added additional impermeable lot coverage that was not approved in the
construction plans in 2019; therefore, the current variation request includes
this additional lot coverage.
   The ZBA recommended denial of the variation request, given that the home was
so recently granted significant zoning relief. It was felt that the garage
should have been planned at that time. Since the request was recommended for
denial, Council approval requires a favorable vote of four Village Trustees.
President Rintz asked the Trustees for their questions.
   Trustee Swierk asked what the stormwater management situation is. Mr. Schoon
said the property meets current requirements but would have to provide
additional stormwater management for any additional impermeable surface.
   Trustee Lanphier asked if an existing paved patio could be converted to a
wooden deck to alleviate the amount of impermeable surface. Mr. Schoon
explained if the surface underneath was permeable, a wood deck would reduce the
amount impermeable lot coverage on the site.
   Trustee Cripe asked why there was no request for a garage when the home was
being constructed.
President Rintz called for public comment.
   Tiffany Vitiello and Steven Fowler, neighbors to 880 Willow. Mr. Fowler
explained that he shares a driveway with the Subject Property and it is
difficult to function with the current situation of too many cars parked in
close proximity. He appealed to the Council to consider approving the request
so the shared driveway is more accessible.
   Jeffrey Liss, Winnetka resident. Mr. Liss asked to what extent the Council’s
decision will depend on the absence of a garage request when the home was being
constructed.
   Mark and Julie Swislow, neighbors to 880 Willow. The Swislows said they were
in favor of a garage simply to make the site more appealing visually with
regard to trash cans and parked cars.
Next, President Rintz called on the applicants.
   Todd Stephens, the applicant’s attorney, explained that this particular
block of Willow road has very small nonconforming lots, and the adjacent
neighbors have all submitted letters asking the Council to grant approval of
the requested variations to allow the garage to be constructed. He said the
applicants would either remove the nonconforming impervious surface that had
been added or compensate with more stormwater storage.
   Trustee Cripe asked again about the garage that had been on the Subject
Property previously and why the applicants didn’t include a request for a
garage when they were constructing their home. Attorney Stephens said it had
been more like a shed that was 100 years old and was taken down by the
applicants’ contractors. Mrs. Cazacu explained that they did not think a garage
was important at the time; it is only after living in the home and seeing the
problems with the shared driveway that they realize a longer driveway and
garage is needed.
   President Rintz asked the applicants to explain what other vehicles would be
housed in the garage. Mrs. Cazacu said the couple has a jet ski that they would
like to store in the garage. President Rintz asked what happens to the current
cars if they will not be parked in the garage. Mrs. Cazacu said having a longer
driveway will help with the car parking and there will only be two cars parked
in the driveway.
   Trustee Swierk asked for clarification that the driveway is not “shared.”
Mr. Schoon explained the driveways are two separate narrow driveways. Trustee
Swierk said he could not be in favor of this belated request for a garage, as
he felt it should have been included in the original construction plans for the
home.
   Trustee Dearborn said there are a number of cars in the two adjacent
driveways. He noted that the ZBA indicated it would have required the home to
be smaller during its consideration of the original variation request in 2019
if it had included a garage in the plans. He said he could not see granting
approval to a situation that could potentially exacerbate stormwater runoff
problems in this area.
   Trustee Cripe agreed with Trustees Swierk and Dearborn, but he expressed
frustration that a negative decision by the Council is bad for the neighbors
who have all expressed support for the request. He commented that the site has
legitimate safety problems which may complicate strict adherence to the Zoning
Ordinance.
   President Rintz suggested approval of the request might include a limitation
on the number of cars that could be parked on the Subject Property.
   Trustee Apatoff expressed discomfort with how the applicants went about the
original demolition and construction, and he was also not enthusiastic about
going against the unanimous recommendation of the ZBA.
   Trustee Lanphier noted that the original home on the site did not have a
garage in the rear quarter but did have a shed near the house. She said the
issues seem to be storage of items like garbage cans and a jet ski, and parking
on the driveway. She said she was reluctant to grant the requested variation to
build a garage in light of the original home plan leaving out the garage
entirely. She suggested the applicants revise their plan to extend the driveway
and install a storage shed, rather than a garage, to reduce the impermeable
surface variation being requested.
   Mr. Stephens explained that his clients had been sick with Covid-19 and did
not appear for the ZBA hearing. He said they would be willing to revise their
plans and appear before the ZBA again if the Council would table its decision
on the matter tonight.
   President Rintz said it does not look like the applicants have the approval
of four Trustees on this appeal, and a negative determination would require
them to wait 12 months before coming back with another request.
   Attorney Friedman explained that if the matter is continued to a Council
meeting, it must be given a date; however, if the request is sent back to the
ZBA, public notice will be given for the public hearing date on the matter.
President Rintz asked the Council if it would like to keep the matter in its
purview or if it would prefer to send the issue back to the ZBA.
   Trustee Swierk said double driveways are not uncommon in Winnetka; and he
would not consider changing his mind for a garage given the way the request
made its way to the Village.
   Attorney Stephens said the applicants made a mistake and should not be
punished.
   President Rintz suggested tabling the request for 60 days to give the
applicants time to work out a better design.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to defer the request
until the first Council meeting in June. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Coladarci.
13)   Appointments:
None.
14)   Closed Session.
None.
15)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dearborn,
Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Coladarci. The meeting
adjourned at 8:44PM.
 
