Recording Secretary
April 20, 2021
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
April 20, 2021
(Approved: May 18, 2021)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Tuesday, April 20, 2021 at 7:00PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Andrew Cripe, Jack Coladarci,
Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, and John Swierk. Absent: None. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Community Development Director David
Schoon, Director of Water & Electric Brian Keys, and approximately 12 persons
in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Rintz led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   May 4, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   b)   May 11, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present except
Trustee Dearborn said they expect to attend.
   c)   May 18, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
   Julie Windsor, owner of Beat Street on Lincoln Avenue. Ms. Windsor warned of
hazards associated with the Lincoln Avenue Streetscape project and she asked
for better barricades to keep people out of the construction area, since
several pedestrians have fallen.
   Scarlett Harper, 711 Sheridan Road and student member of the Environmental &
Forestry Commission (EFC). Ms. Harper said she has been working on State
legislation to ban subscription mosquito spraying operations in Illinois to
protect bees and other pollinators, and that the bill has been introduced in
Springfield. Trustee Lanphier commended Ms.Harper for her work on the
legislation and she urged the Village to plant more trees than it removes for
projects such as Streetscape.
   Liz Kunkle, 1303 Holly Lane. Ms. Kunkle also commended Ms. Harper for
helping to get the pesticide legislation passed. She explained that she has
been working with Senator Laura Fine to pass a State proclamation that
designates the first week in May as composting awareness month.
   Mickey Archambault, Winnetka resident. Mr. Archambault asked what
celebrations the Village will allow on the 4th of July. Manager Bahan said the
Village is working with the Park District and at this time the fireworks are
planned to be postponed until perhaps the fall based on Covid-19 infection
rates; no determination has been made about other celebrations such as a
parade.
5)   Reports:
   a)   Motion to Extend Village President’s Declaration of Emergency. Attorney
Friedman explained that the Emergency Declaration has been extended from its
original date of passage last November. The provisions of the declaration have
remained the same, and this extension would last until the June 22 Village
Council meeting.
   Trustee Dearborn asked if there is any reason to keep extending the
declaration.
   Manager Bahan explained the declaration provides for emergency staffing,
which is still an issue for the Village during the pandemic; in addition,
purchasing is also affected on occasion.
   Trustee Coladarci, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to extend the Village
President’s November 23, 2020 Declaration of Emergency until the end of the
June 22, 2021 Village Council meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: None.
   b)   Trustees. None.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. Manager Bahan announced that the New Trier Township vaccine
clinic will now offer appointments to the general public aged 16 and over.
   e)   Village President. President Rintz expressed admiration for Ms.
Harper’s efforts on behalf of bees and the environment.
6)   Arbor Day Proclamation.
   President Rintz read a proclamation setting aside April 30 as Arbor Day in
Winnetka to encourage the planting of trees.
   Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to proclaim April 30,
2021 as Arbor Day in Winnetka. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None.
Absent: None.
7)   Monarch Butterfly Proclamation.
   President Rintz read a proclamation that designates April 22 as Monarch
Remembrance Day in Winnetka. Trustee Lanphier said Chicago is an important area
for the Monarch migration and she urged the community to plant milkweed and to
limit pesticide use to help the Monarch population rebound.
   Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to proclaim April 22,
2021 as Monarch Remembrance Day in Winnetka. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and
Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8)   Environmental and Forestry Commission 2021 Green Award.
   Liz Kunkle, EFC Commissioner, said Spirit Elephant restaurant in Hubbard
Woods received a 2021 Green Award. She explained that the restaurant is vegan,
which is very beneficial for carbon emission reductions and is very nearly a
zero-waste operation. In addition, appliances are energy efficient and takeout
containers are compostable. The Sustainable Community award was presented to
the business owner last week.
   Trustee Apatoff commended Spirit Elephant for its efforts and the EFC for
publicizing its Green Award program.
9)   Approval of the Agenda.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the Agenda. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe,
Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
10)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   April 8, 2021 Regular Meeting.
      ii)   April 13, 2021 Study Session.
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated April 2 – April 15, 2021 in the amount
of $1,040,170.41.
   c)   Resolution No. R-49-2021: Class P Liquor License for Open Kitchens Inc.
(Adoption).
   d)   Outdoor Seating Permit: Peet’s Coffee Inc.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and
Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
11)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-9-2021: 630 Sheridan Road Zoning Variations
(Introduction/ Adoption). Mr. Schoon reviewed this request for height and
setback zoning variations for an existing playhouse on the Subject Property.
The request was recommended for approval by the Zoning Board of Appeals (ZBA)
and several neighbors, although there was concern with the standard for
reasonable return.
   There was a discussion about having the Council review zoning for accessory
structures such as children’s playhouses. Trustee Cripe said it was a burden on
the homeowners during a pandemic and could be handled by the ZBA. President
Rintz noted that some Village Code cleanup can be done after the Comprehensive
Plan is updated.
   Brian Sullivan, Winnetka resident. Mr. Sullivan explained that the intent
was to have a place for the children to play with their friends; he added that
the structure is well built and safe.
   The Council being in agreement to waive introduction, Trustee Lanphier,
seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to waive introduction of Ordinance No. M-9-
2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci,
Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Ordinance No.
M-9-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   b)   Ordinance No. MC-3-2021: Sign Code Amendment - Illuminated Signs
(Introduction). Mr. Schoon explained that the Design Review Board (DRB) was
tasked by the Council to study the issue of illuminated signs and has completed
its report and recommendations. The DRB has concluded that it can only
recommend allowing backlit halo signs; it did not recommend approval of an
illuminated “open” sign as some members felt these signs do not fit in with the
character of the community.
   The Trustees discussed the issue of illuminated signs to clarify what the
different types of electronic signs look like; the amendment being contemplated
in the Subject Ordinance would clarify that the Village does allow “halo”
President Rintz called for public comment.
   Julie Windsor, owner of Beat Street on Lincoln Avenue. Ms. Windsor said her
illuminated “open” sign is small and unobtrusive; however, there are more
tasteful varieties to be found. She suggested the Village approve a discreet,
uniform “open” sign that businesses could use during the pandemic, as the
current environment is very challenging.
   Theresa Lukas, owner of Good Grapes on Chestnut Court. Ms. Lukas said some
stores look closed during the day since their lighting is low. She suggested a
very simple “open” sign to help customers identify that the store is open for
business.
   Liz Kunkle, Winnetka resident. Ms. Kunkle supported the idea of a uniform
sign for all businesses and asked if a temporary measure could be put in place
under the President’s Emergency Declaration during the pandemic.
   Trustee Swierk suggested a moratorium on enforcing the sign code until the
Comprehensive Plan update is completed, and then updating the entire sign code.
   Trustee Apatoff asked if new business owners are informed of the provisions
of the sign code. Manager Bahan noted that in the absence of business licensing
in Winnetka, the opportunity to engage is sometimes lost. If a new business
does a build-out, staff has opportunities to engage with the new owner. He
noted that the sign code is enforced on a complaint basis only.
   Trustee Swierk said implementing a business license process would give the
Village a chance to inform new businesses of Village requirements. Trustee
Dearborn explained that the DRB does help businesses identify types of approved
signs; the issue at hand tonight is the DRB recommendation to allow the halo
signs.
   Trustee Cripe said it seems the Council has no problem approving the Subject
Ordinance; and he suggested the issue of nonconforming signs be dealt with
through a waiver process, so the unique circumstances of each business needs to
be considered. The DRB has set a good standard for the Village as a whole;
however, exceptions should be considered.
   President Rintz said he would discuss the issue of further study with the
Chair of the DRB, as setting standards for exceptions is a different assignment
than was first tasked to them. He also suggested a soft enforcement approach on
nonconforming signs while the issue is being studied by the DRB.
   Trustee Dearborn noted that the DRB makes exceptions for unique
circumstances for permitted signs, and that illuminated signs could be added to
their purview. Mr. Schoon clarified that the DRB has the discretion to permit
variations on permitted signs, but that a prohibited sign cannot be granted an
exception. The DRB in its recent deliberations on internally illuminated signs
chose not to recommend adding them to the list of allowed variations. President
Rintz said he would discuss the Council’s goals with the DRB Chair.
   The Council was generally in favor of having the DRB further study the sign
issue, and of moving forward to approve the recommendations in the Subject
Ordinance.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to introduce Ordinance
No. MC-3-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier,
and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   c)   Resolution No. R-44-2021: Purchase of Northfield Substation Transformer
(Adoption). Mr. Keys explained that the purchase of the transformer is a
significant milestone in the expansion of the Northfield Substation to provide
contingency power for Winnetka residents. Contracts for the installation of a
firewall, switchgear, and a ComEd tie-in have already been awarded. The new
transformer must be able to fit in the transformer yard and to align with the
existing conduits of the Village’s distribution system. An optional alternate
bid was also sought for pricing of a load-tap changer (LTC) beyond base bid
specifications. The LTC is used to regulate output voltage.
   Mr. Keys reviewed specifications of the new transformer and noted that each
bid was evaluated using calculations to determine the cost impact of the
transformer’s electrical losses. Although all bids received were deemed non-
responsive, a bid analysis completed Strand Associates recommended negotiating
with WEG Transformers. The negotiations were successful, and WEG Transformers
is the recommended vendor. Since the original bids were deemed non-responsive,
the Village Attorney recommends rejecting all bids, waiving bidding, and
awarding the contract to WEG Transformers.
   Mr. Keys noted that the contract contains progress payments, as the project
is significant in size, and he reviewed the schedule contained in his Agenda
Report. The next step in the process is an installation contract for the new
transformer, to take place in early 2022.
   After a brief discussion of the total budget impact for the project over two
budget years, several Trustees commended Mr. Keys for working to secure the
favorable contract, and the Council agreed to adopt the Subject Resolution.
   There being no public comment, Trustee Coladarci, seconded by Trustee Cripe,
moved to adopt Resolution No. R-44-2021 awarding a contract to WEG Transformers
for a 138kV to 12.47kV substation transformer in an amount not to exceed
$860,500.. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
12)   Old Business.
None.
13)   New Business.
None.
14)   Appointments:
None.
15)   Closed Session.
None.
16)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Coladarci, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: None.. The meeting adjourned at 9:04PM.
 
