(Approved: July 20, 2021)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held at Village Hall, 510 Green Bay Road, on Tuesday, June
29, 2021, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 PM. Present: Trustees
Robert Apatoff, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Kim Mancini, and John
Swierk. Absent: Trustee Andrew Cripe. Also present: Village Manager Robert
Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter
Friedman, and approximately eight persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Rintz led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   July 20, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   b)   August 3, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   c)   August 10, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
None.
5)   Reports:
   a)   Village Attorney Report - Remote Participation. Attorney Friedman
explained that the Open Meetings Act (OMA) only allows remote participation at
a public meeting when there is an in-person quorum for the meeting. This rule
was amended during the Covid 19 pandemic to allow virtual public meetings in
the interest of public safety. After the emergency provisions lapse, State
regulations will only allow remote participation by a Council member in three
instances: (i) family emergency; (ii) being away for business or health
reasons, and (iii) being away on the business of the Village. Since the OMA
permits a public body to be more strict in its application of the law, Winnetka
further only allows remote participation a maximum of four times per term.
The Council briefly discussed the process of remote meeting participation.
   b)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Lanphier said the 2021 Farmers’ Market is up and running and
she encouraged the Council and community to attend.
      ii)   Trustee Swierk reported on the most recent Plan Commission meeting.
      iii)   Trustee Dearborn noted that the Streetscape project in East Elm is
finished and the result looks very nice.
      iv)   Trustee Apatoff said he attended his first ribbon cutting as
Chamber liaison, and he noted that the community is starting to gather in the
downtowns again.
   c)   Attorney. None.
   d)   Manager. None.
   e)   Village President. President Rintz echoed Trustee Lanphier’s comments
about the Farmers’ Market, and he reported on a meeting with Rep. Jan
Schakowsky about the Forest Preserve stormwater project.
6)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Consent Agenda
