MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
July 20, 2021
(Approved: August 3, 2021)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Tuesday, July 20, 2021 at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Present: Trustees
Robert Apatoff, Robert Dearborn, Kim Mancini, and John Swierk. Absent: Trustees
Andrew Cripe and Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Community Development Director David Schoon, Finance Director Tim Sloth,
Accounting Manager Dell Duckworth, Ron Amen of Lauterbach and Amen, and
approximately seven persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Rintz led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   August 3, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   b)   August 10, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present said
they expect to attend.
   c)   August 17, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
said they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
Elisha Gray, 1125 Spruce Street. Mr. Gray thanked the Village for setting aside
funds to improve the Green Bay Trail and he made suggestions to facilitate the
growth of native plants after Metra re-aligns the track at the Hubbard Woods
train station.
[Trustee Lanphier arrived at 7:05.]
5)   Reports:
   a)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Mancini reported on the last meeting of the Environmental &
Forestry Commission.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager. None.
   d)   Village President. President Rintz commented that the Village rendered
assistance to nearly every business that asked for help during the Covid-19
restrictions, and that no business received special treatment. He added that
the Covid relief for sidewalk cafes will be in force until the end of the
summer dining season. He also updated the community about kick-off meetings
with the Park District and District 203 for the design and implementation
phases of stormwater projects
6)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   May 18, 2021 Regular Meeting.
      ii)   June 29, 2021 Special Meeting.   
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated June 25 – July 15, 2021 in the amount of
$1,089,357.82.
   c)   Reolution No. R-24-2021: Approving a Class A-1 Liquor License for
Little Honeycomb Inc. (Adoption).
   d)   Resolution No. R-64-2021: Approving a Class A-1 Liquor License for
Spruce Operations LLC (Adoption).
   e)   Outdoor Seating Permit: At Properties.
   Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Dearborn, Lanphier, Mancini, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Cripe.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution No. R-65-2021: 800-808-812 Oak Subdivision – Final Plat
Approval (Adoption). Mr. Schoon explained that the vacant 808 Oak Street
property was sold in the mid-1990’s without ever being subdivided from the two
adjacent parcels. He reviewed the zoning for the Subject Properties and
presented photos of the three parcels. Resolution No. R-65-2021 would cure any
irregularities with the three lots, which have not been properly subdivided. He
noted that no proposals for developing the 808 Oak parcel have been submitted
to the Village, and the Plan Commission has unanimously recommended approval of
the subdivision request.
   After the Trustees asked several questions about the vacant parcel,
President Rintz called for public comment.
   John Sheppard, 812 Oak Street and President of the812 Oak Street Condominium
Board. Mr. Sheppard said his Board had no objection to the subdivision request;
however, the Board would have concerns about any new construction at the 808
Oak site, especially with regard to large trees in the courtyard of 812 Oak,
and periodic repairs that need to take place along the west wall of the
building.
   After a brief discussion, the Trustees were in agreement to approve the
subdivision request.
   Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to adopt Resolution
No. R-65-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Dearborn, Lanphier, Mancini, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Cripe.
9)   Old Business.
   a)   880 Willow Road Zoning Variations (Policy Direction). President Rintz
explained that this appeal of a Zoning Board of Appeals (ZBA) negative
recommendation was first heard and discussed by the Council on April 8. At that
time, the case was continued to give the applicants time to amend their
proposal in hopes of gaining Council approval for their request.
   Mr. Schoon explained that the applicants’ attorney had been under the
impression that tonight’s meeting would be held on Zoom; and the applicants
would like their attorney present during the discussion of their amended plan.
Therefore, they are asking for another continuance for an August or September
meeting. After polling the Council, President Rintz called for a motion to
continue the Subject Request until the September 9 Regular Council meeting.
   Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to continue
deliberation of the Subject Request until the September 9, 2021 Council
meeting. By voice vote, the motion carried.
10)   New Business.
   a)   Comprehensive Annual Financial Report (CAFR). Mr. Sloth explained that
the 2020 Audit provides reasonable assurance that the Village’s financial
reports are complete and accurate. A few items of note for 2020 include: (i)
the audit received a Certificate of Achievement from the Government Finance
Officers Association (GFOA) for meeting and exceeding the high standards for
financial reporting set by the GFOA; and (ii) a Management Letter from the
auditors that provides notes on internal controls found no significant
deficiencies or weaknesses in the Village’s internal financial controls.
   Mr. Sloth reviewed the Village’s major funds, noting that in 2020 the
General Fund posted a $1.95 million surplus; revenues came in at 99% of
projections; and expenditures were approximately 91% of budget forecasts. Last
spring, it was estimated that nearly $1.7 million would be lost in revenue due
to the Covid-19 pandemic downturn, and corresponding budget cuts were
identified to offset this amount. This explains why expenditures were slightly
lower than budget projections, since Village staff adhered to the budget cuts
while revenue came in better than anticipated. The General Fund total
unassigned fund balance is $25.85 million – or 98% of operating expenditures;
this exceeds the fund balance requirement of 50% of operating expenses. After
reviewing the status of the utility funds, public safety pension funds, and the
IMRF pension fund, he thanked Accounting Manager Dell Duckworth for her help in
preparing the audit.
   Mr. Amen thanked Village staff for being trustworthy and accommodating with
requests from the auditors. He explained that the Village’s General Fund and
business-type accounts had positive increases in fund balances in 2020; he
noted that no reportable conditions or material weaknesses were found for
Winnetka’s financial reporting. However, two items considered “cleanup” items
had been identified: (i) the Information Technology fund (an internal service
fund) is slightly below the fund balance policy by just approximately $24,000;
and (ii) three funds were over their budgeted expenditure amount for 2020.
   After a brief discussion about pension funding and deferred projects,
President Rintz thanked Mr. Amen for his expertise through the years and
Manager Bahan commended Mr. Sloth and Ms. Duckworth staff for their proficiency
in maintaining Winnetka’s financial reporting systems.
11)   Appointments: None.
12)   Closed Session. None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to adjourn the meeting.
By voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:02 PM.
 
