WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
August 3, 2021
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Council Chambers at Village Hall on Tuesday,
August 3, 2021, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Present: Trustees
Robert Apatoff, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Kim Mancini, and John Swierk.
Absent: Penfield Lanphier. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, and
approximately 8 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Rintz led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   Tuesday, August 17, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
   b)   Thursday, September 9, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
   c)   Tuesday, September 14, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment. None
5)   Reports: None.
   a)   Trustees. 
      i)   Trustee Swierk announces that he was hired by the Winnetka Platform
Tennis Club to assist with the installation of two new paddle courts and to
assist with the expansion plans of the hut. Trustee Swierk reports that he will
follow the rules and recuse himself during the entire process.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager. None.
   d)   Village President. None.
6)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to approve the Agenda. By
vote, the motion carried.
7)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   July 20, 2021, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated July 16 - July 29, 2021 in the amount of
$1,402,951.64.
   c)   Resolution No. R-66-2021: Boiler Blowdown Tank Contract (Adoption).
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Apatoff, Swierk, Dearborn, and Mancini,. Nays:
None. Absent: Lanphier.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-4-2021: Flood Plain Maps Update (Introduction/
Adoption).
   FEMA, the Federal Emergency Management Agency, has recently updated the
Flood Insurance Rate Map. Emily Grimm, the Assistant Village Engineer, speaks
on the update that FEMA has recently released. Prior to the update, the
lakefront areas have been marked as AE-zones whereas the lakefront will now be
changed to a V-zone coastal area. The maps and the language in the Village
ordinance reflecting the classification of the zone change must be updated as
these changes will be effective September 10 th . The Flood Insurance Rate Map
update will only affect properties along the lakefront, however, most of the
properties are observed to be outside of the flood plain. The update by FEMA
may impact accessory structures that are lower along the lakefront such as any
boat houses. Trustee Mancini questions if current properties alongside Lake
Michigan, that are not part of the flood zone, will have to adhere to the new
changes. According to Emily Grimm, the update would not affect any current
structures, just any new structures. Reflecting that the main change in the
update is that structures would not be able to be elevated on fill but rather
would have to be elevated by pilings or columns.
   The Council was in unanimous agreement to approve the request.
   Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. MC-4-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Swierk, Dearborn, Mancini, Cripe, Apatoff. Nays: None. Absent: Lanphier.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adopt MC-4-2021. By roll
call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Swierk, Apatoff, Mancini,
and Dearborn. Nays: None. Absent: Lanphier.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-13-2021: 747 Walden Zoning Variation (Introduction/
Adoption).
   This zoning variation request for new construction was unanimously approved
by the Zoning Board of Appeals. Brian Norkus, the Assistant Community
Development Director briefs the Council on the Ordinance presented. The matter
is presented before the Council because the zoning variation procedure in the
zoning code requires Council approval for any variation request associated with
new construction. As previously mentioned, the Zoning Board of Appeals has
already approved of the request variation and found that the request has met
the standards of zoning relief considering the nonconforming use. The request
for relief by the homeowners, to allow for new construction of a new single-
family residence, for the minimum front yard setback of 10.55 feet from Walden
Rd whereas a minimum of 30 feet is required. The property is located on the S/
E corner of Westmoor Rd and Walden Rd and is zoned R-5 single family
residential. The district provides for the smallest residential lot sizes
requiring a minimum lot area of 8900 sq ft in area for corner lots and the
subject property is slightly non-conforming with respect to the lot area
minimum. The current residents express that they would like to rebuild their
home due to the home being outdated. They inform the Council on some current
issues being encountered with the structure such as the sewer lines not being
up to code standards since some of the structuring is over 100 years old.
According to the homeowners, they feel that the approval of their request will
also help the home look a little more linear in appearance with the surrounding
homes.
   The Council was in unanimous agreement to approve the request.
   Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-13-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried Ayes: Trustees
Swierk, Mancini, Dearborn, Apatoff, and CripeNays: None. Absent: Lanphier.
   Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
13-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Swierk, Apatoff,
Cripe, Mancini, Dearborn. Nays: None. Absent: Lanphier.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-14-2021: Transfer of Property and Dedication of Essex
Road as a Public Right-of-Way (Introduction/Adoption).
   Essex Rd, a presumed public street owned by New Trier High School, is
presented to the Council as a transfer of property. Essex Rd, between Winnetka
Ave and Sunset Rd, has served as part of the Villages Street system and the
Village and High School have understood that it has served as the Village
street right-of-way. During a routine title search conducted by New Trier, it
was discovered that that portion of Essex Rd is in fact owned by New Trier. The
High School claims to have no interest in owning part of the Villages street
system, however, the Village is interested in acquiring the road as it is part
of the Villages circulation system and is improved with water and sewer
utilities. New Trier has prepared a Plat of Dedication, that would be recorded
and transfer the property to a publicly dedicated right-of-way. The transaction
would include but not be limited to preparation of a quickclaim deed which
would be recorded along with the plat of dedication to confirm Village
ownership.
   There being no public comment, Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Dearborn,
moved to waive introduction of Ordinance No. M-14-2021. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Dearborn, Swierk, Apatoff and Mancini.
Nays: None. Absent: Lanphier.
   Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt M-14-2021. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Mancini,
Dearborn, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Lanphier.
   d)   Resolution No. R-68-2021: Easement Amendment for Thorn Tree Subdivision
(Adoption)
   The request presented to the Council is to modify particular elements of the
Thorn Tree subdivision. As part of the approval of the subdivision, the
developers of the subdivision were required to design and construct stormwater
detention facilities to accommodate the anticipated amount of stormwater runoff
that would be generated by the development of the ten lot subdivision. To
assure the continued function and operation of the stormwater detention
facilities that were constructed as part of the approved subdivision, there is
a stormwater detention easement that is located within the approximate location
of the stormwater basin. The subject property, 130 Evergreen Ln, is looking to
incorporate a pool in the backyard of the property, however, the pool location
conflicts with the location of the stormwater detention basin as well as the
location of the easement itself. The applicant has requested adjusting the
boundaries of the stormwater easement on the property and to accommodate the
proposed pool, the applicant proposed to regrade the backyard and effectively
provide additional stormwater volume in order to allow for the pool
construction. The plans submitted by the applicant detail how they will propose
to maintain existing stormwater volumes. Village engineering staff have
reviewed and determined that the revised plan will accommodate the stormwater
detention and floodplain compensatory storage requirements.
   The Council was in unanimous agreement to approve the request.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to adopt R-68-2021. By
roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Cripe, Mancini, Dearborn,
Swierk and Apatoff. Nays: None. Absent: Lanphier.
8)   Old Business. None.
9)   New Business. None.
10)   Appointments: None.
11)   Closed Session. None.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 7:45 PM.
 
