A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Council Chambers at Village Hall on Tuesday,
August 17, 2021 at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order. 
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 PM. Present: Trustees
Robert Apatoff, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Penfield Lanphier, Kim Mancini,
and John Swierk. Absent: None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Community Development Director David Schoon, Director of Engineering Jim
Bernahl, and approximately 14 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Lanphier led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   Thursday, September 9, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
   b)   September 14, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present
said they expect to attend.
   c)   September 21, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present
said they expect to attend except Trustee Mancini who will not be present.
4)   Public Comment.
None.
5)   Reports:
Village Green September 11 Memorial Flags Request. President Rintz explained
the annual tradition that began 2008 honoring the victims of the September 11,
2001 terrorist attacks by planting American flags on the Village Green.
President Rintz notes that this year’s tradition marks the 20th anniversary of
the attacks. Trustee Cripe’s children Charlie, William and Julia step up to the
podium to address the Council on the tradition. The Cripe family thanks the
Council for allowing them to honor those who lost their lives by placing 2,977
American flags each year. The flags are planted on September 10 and are removed
at sundown on September 11. Trustee Lanphier thanks the Cripe family for
dedicating their time and effort into this tradition and emphasizes the
importance this brings. President Rintz also thanks Trustee Cripe and the
family for the efforts put forth in this tradition.
Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Lanphier, moved to approve the Village
Green September 11 Memorial Flags Request.
By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Lanphier,
Swierk, Dearborn, Cripe, and Mancini. Nays: None. Absent: None.
   a)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Lanphier announces her resignation to end her term early as
she will be moving across the country to be with her family. Elaborating on
serving the Winnetka community, Trustee Lanphier emphasizes what an honor it
has been working alongside the members of the Council, the community at whole
and the residents of Winnetka. While Trustee Lanphier expresses her
disappointment in leaving, she stresses how fortunate Winnetka is for having
such a dedicated and amazing staff that is serving the community. Trustee
Lanphier emphasizes what a privilege it has been working with the stormwater
project and the benefits as well as the great progress the project has made
since she started. She also speaks on how important Comprehensive plan has been
to her as well as she has been working with the project for twenty years.
Trustee Lanphier speaks on how fortunate Winnetka is to have such competent and
dedicated staff and Trustees.
      Trustee Dearborn expresses how lucky the Council and Winnetka has been to
have Trustee Lanphier serve the community. He emphasizes what a privilege it
has been working with her. Trustee Cripe, Trustee Swierk and Trustee Apatoff
convey what a joy it has been working alongside Trustee Lanphier and recognize
the commitment she has had to the community and wish her well Trustee Mancini
thanks Trustee Lanphier for her words of advice while working alongside her and
believes that she has been a great influence on the Council. Village Manager
Rob Bahan thanks Trustee Lanphier for her contributions and support she has
given to the community. President Rintz thanks Trustee Lanphier for her service
to the community and how accessible she has been to Winnetka as a whole. He
believes that this type of service to the community should carry on and wishes
Trustee Lanphier the best of luck. Village resident Becky Hurley steps up to
the podium to recognize Trustee Lanphier’s compassion with serving the
community and speak on how wonderful it has been working with her.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager. None.
   d)   Village President. President Rintz and Village Manager Rob met with
State Senator Laura Fine and State Representative Robyn Gabel in  regard to the
stormwater concept. President Rintz believes that it is important to engage our
elected officials due to the help and assistance they can provide for the
Village.
   President Rintz and Community Development Director David Schoon toured the
93 Green Bay Rd site before meeting the finalists and shared some comments the
Trustees had with respect to public spaces as well as answering technical
questions The engagement of the finalists will be interesting to see as they
all want to work with the community.
   Village Engineer Jim Bernahl, Village Manager Rob Bahan and President Rintz
met with IDOT regarding jurisdictional transfers and the priority of Willow Rd
with the stormwater project coming up. President Rintz believes that it is
important for staff to have control over those aspects as well as touching base
on 93 Green Bay Rd. Rob Bahan states that any financial support from IDOT does
not seem likely due to funding reasons.
