Mancini. Nays: None. Absent: None.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Resolution No. R-71-2021: Comprehensive Plan Contract Addendum
(Adoption).
   President Rintz states that this Resolution would approve an addendum to the
agreement with The Lakota Group for the preparation of the Comprehensive Plan.
Work on the plan began in late 2019 and was placed on hold in mid-March of 2020
due to the pandemic which also halted in person meetings.
   Community Development Director David Schoon informs the Council of the three
major phases of the plan that were set in place with the Lakota Group. Prior to
the shutdown, the Village was in the analyzing phase of the Comprehensive Plan
at the time the work stopped. There were still a number of steps that needed to
be completed which will need to be revisited prior to the work resuming. The
Community Development staff would like to engage the community in order to get
new ideas and feedback prior to re-starting the work which would require
additional time from the consultant team that had not initially been planned.
With Council approval, the Comprehensive Plan can move forward with the
“analyze phase” in order to move into the “visioning” and “draft and final
plan” that would take place in 2022.
   Becky Hurley and Scott Freres, from the Laokota Group update the Council on
the proposed next steps.
   Scott Freres elaborates on the opportunity to rebuild a community given the
current situation and emphasizes the importance of knowing what makes a
community a community. He advocates for the engagement with the community on a
more personal level and to refreshen the community on the Comprehensive Plan.
   Becky Hurley states that the pandemic has opened the door to think more
deeply into basic community engagement. She believes that the community needs
to be reminded of the concept of the Comprehensive Plan and find out what they
believe changed as a result of the pandemic.
   Trustee Apatoff expresses his concern about re-engaging and making decisions
based on the current state when it may change and believes that things can
quickly go back to how they use to be.
   Trustee Lanphier also expresses her thoughts on re-engaging with the plan
based on the current state of the community. She feels as though there can be
positivity taken away from the situation as to what the community has really
enjoyed about Winnetka through these times. Trustee Lanphier questions if there
may be Village wide questionnaire that may be given to the community and to see
the data that is received back in terms of a real estate standpoint and
economic standpoint. She believes the time and money spent will make this
community cutting edge.
   Trustee Dearborn questions if there is a way to continue the Comprehensive
Plan without any future halts on the plan due to the interruptions of COVID.
Scott confirms that there are certain aspects within the Comprehensive Plan
that will not change moving forward regardless of COVID. Given the
circumstances, the plan will still have an anticipated end date of 2022.
   Trustee Swierk expresses his concern on the financial aspect for the restart
of the Comprehensive Plan given that the request requires 50% more in expenses.
Scott indicated that the expenses were totaled based on itemizing the tasks,
hours, staff involved, Scott and his team members. These hours were compared
and resulted to the same hours spent on the project prior to the Comprehensive
Plan being halted due to the pandemic. Trustee Swierk and Trustee Apatoff
suggest that rather than doing a total restart of the plan, a streamline
restart may be more appropriate and may save more money. Trustee Cripe agrees
that there being more work with the restart accurately reflects the higher
expenses for the plan.
   Teresa Lucas, a business owner within Winnetka, suggests that if the Village
is leaning towards a survey, that using the businesses to promote the surveys
in their weekly emails.
   Trustee Apatoff expresses that he doesn't see an issue with providing money
for the Comprehensive Plan restart if the plan is streamlined in order to move
forward quickly, as previously stated in the discussion.
   Trustee Lanphier notes that the pause caused a cultural change and believes
that some things are permanently changed. She believes that some of the data
needs to be revisited due to the change and that the investment is worth it.
   Trustee Dearborn believes that this initiative is critical and although the
money plays a significant role in the restart plan, he puts his trust in the
Lakota Group and staff working on the Comprehensive Plan.
   Trustee Lanphier, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adopt Resolution
No. R-71-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Lanphier,
Dearborn, Manicini, Cripe, and Apatoff. Nays: Trustee Swierk. Absent: None.
9)   Old Business. None.
10)   New Business.
   a)   Phase 4 Streetscape Concepts (Policy Direction).
   Community Development Director David Schoon presents phase 4 of the
streetscape concepts to the Council confirming that the third phase of the
streetscape has nearly been completed. Staff has identified some other
streetscape elements to be completed at the time that other redevelopment
projects occur including areas around the post office site and areas around the
One Winnetka site. Staff has identified some projects that have the potential
to move forward with independent of the redevelopment of the two sites. Staff
has also identified some infrastructure projects that staff would like to
implement. This includes some water main work and relocated some underground
electric transformers along with the planning of the Village Hall parking lot.
Staff has coordinated these projects along with the streetscape project.
   Jodi Mariano, from Teska Associates, confirms that phases I, II and III are
nearly completed with phase III needing only a few more elements to be
finalized.
   Moving forward with phase IV, this phase will fill in where the previous
phases left off and will complete the work needed to be addressed such as
sidewalk cracks, pavement repair, ADA detectable warnings, and trees in poor
condition. Some work that is planned in phase IV includes the water main work
previously mentioned, electrical work, and the streetscape work that has been
planned. Work that is planned on the parking lots will be more along the lines
of landscape work, however, the Village Hall parking lot will include some
reconstruction in addition to the landscape work.
   Trustee Cripe suggests that in terms of the landscape work, trees that may
provide shade will be beneficial on days with high temperatures especially
considering the Farmers Market that takes place on the parking lot.
   David Schoon speaks on the cost estimate of the project and indicates that
the numbers have been adjusted to reflect the market rate prices noting that
this being a concept plan, Council approval will permit staff develop
construction documents to present a more detailed proposal.
   Theresa Lucas, who is a business owner, expresses her concern about the
length of time for the construction that will take place. She mentioned that
the previous phases presented difficulty for businesses being accessible. She
mentioned that if Chestnut St and Chestnut Ct are closed, it allows no access
to her business. Teresa suggests that the antique benches that are currently in
place can be stained due to them being so worn out if the Village already plans
on upgrading the area. She would like for the Village to consider improving the
lighting on the streets for pedestrians at night. She also requests that the
two parking spots in the post office north parking lot be designated to her
business during the phase IV construction. She believes that the construction
will impact the amount of traffic going down the streets and would like signs
put up indicating that businesses are operating during the construction. Teresa
thinks that a gathering to celebrate the completion of phase IV will bring the
businesses and community together in a positive light.
   President Rintz does not see an issue with designating parking spots for
businesses upon request, however, the other issues presented may be up for
discussion.
   Trustee Cripe presents his concern in regards to the new trash cans
attracting bees due to the food disposed of and questions if lids may help with
this issue as businesses are forced to keep their doors closed.
   Trustee Lanphier mentions that there have been a few complaints with the
pile up of garbage. She questions if there can be trash bins that are placed on
two ends to help lessen the amount of disposal piling up.
   With respect to the Comprehensive Plan, implementing crosswalks that will
allow for safer crossing for pedestrians and slow traffic which may be
beneficial for the businesses. The streetscape plan will improve the pedestrian
traffic as well as vehicular traffic in the downtown area.
   Discussion of the location of the transformers looking to be relocated will
be in question based on the location. President Rintz expresses the challenge
of keeping the transformers more discreet rather than in plain sight.
   There was consensus to move forward with phase IV streetscape improvements
for the purpose of proceeding with the design contract as presented.
11)   Appointments: None
12)   Closed Session. None
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn , seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:15 PM.
 
