Recording Secretary
September 14, 2021
MINUTES
Joint Virtual Study Session Meeting of Winnetka Village Council Plan Commission
Village Hall
September 14, 2021
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka and the Plan Commission, which was held in the Village Hall Council
Chambers on Tuesday, September 14, 2021, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:04 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Andrew
Cripe, Tina Dalman, Bob Dearborn, John Swierk, Kim Mancini and President Rintz.
Absent: None. Assistant Community Development Director Brian Norkus called the
roll of the Plan Commission. Present: Trustees Jonathan Alt, Mamie Case, Layla
Danley, Jon Golan, and Chair Bridget Orsic. Absent: None. Also present: Village
Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas, Village
Attorney Ben Schuster, Deputy Village Clerk Berina Gradjan, Assistant Community
Development Director Brian Norkus, and approximately 4 persons in the audience.
2)   Public Comment. None.
3)   Comprehensive Plan Working Session Discussion.
The Comprehensive Plan had been put on hold in the Spring of 2020 due to the
COVID-19 pandemic. Members of the Lakota Group, Scott Freres, Becky Hurley and
Siraj Asfahani, Village Council and the Plan Commission, gather to discuss the
restart of the Comprehensive Plan and re-engagement with the project.
Scott Freres, of the Lakota Group, directs Council and the Plan Commission to
inform the Lakota Group on what type of information they are seeking to be
obtained as they begin re-engaging regarding the re-start of the Comprehensive
Plan. Mr. Freres asks that Council and the Plan Commission convey their
expectations of the different tasks they feel the Lakota Group needs to
accomplish as well as the various data they seek to gather from surrounding
communities.
Scott Freres informs members of the Council and the Plan Commission about how
important it is for them to engage with the Winnetka community and to make sure
that they can not only be distinguished out in public, but also approachable by
carrying a flag, “Winnetka Futures”. Mr. Freres states that part of their goal
with engaging amongst the community is to hears their thoughts, concerns, ideas
and make members of the community feel heard. Mr. Freres notes that the
Comprehensive Plan allows for the establishment of direction, initiatives,
protocols and programs to move the community forward and make good decisions.
He states that part of good decision-making is immediately being attentive to
the current problems and changes that are going on within Winnetka in order to
avoid future problems. Mr. Freres stresses that while the Comprehensive Plan
instils successful strategies, it is not a design plan.
Scott Freres describes the Lakota Groups three phases for the Comprehensive
Plan consisting of the analyze phase, visioning phase and plan making phase.
The “analyze phase” is the front-end phase striving to have a better
understanding of where the Comprehensive Plan is at today such as the number of
vacancies, building permits, zoning code, sustainability, housing, etc... While
the analyzing phase consists of knowing where the Village stands regarding the
topics mentioned, it also reflects on “community voices”, also known as the
state of the community, and is the front chapter for the Comprehensive Plan.
Mr. Freres indicates that one way they have been able to engage with residents
is by having an open conversation and allowing the residents to express their
thoughts and opinions on what community means to them. Scott Freres indicates
the Lakota Groups interest in planning a BBQ in hopes of bringing more of the
community together and engaging in open conversation.
After deliberation on how the future of Winnetka will be regarding the
pandemic, members of both the Council and Plan commission agree that there will
have to be new ways of implementing procedures, potential changes to the zoning
code and adjustments to the way things have been done within the community in
past years. While Trustee Apatoff and Trustee Cripe state that any changes
implemented, or release of information should be done with caution as there is
still a high level of uncertainty and unfamiliarity with the pandemic. Because
of the changes that have taken place due to COVID since the initial start of
the Comprehensive Plan, Trustee Dalman and Trustee Swierk are in agreement that
one helpful way to start restart the project, is by going back to those that
were already interviewed and seeing what has changed in their opinion to
distinguish the difference between the “now and then”.
Layla Danley, of the Plan Commission, expressed her observation on how the
community has been much more engaged with outdoor activities and a healthier
lifestyle with respect to the increase in foot traffic around town. Many more
children are observed riding their bikes, pedestrians walking and engagement of
movement rather than solely relying on driving. Trustee Swierk states that
after living in Winnetka for 18 years, summers have become much more lively
rather than empty prior to the pandemic starting. Discussion regarding all of
the increase in foot traffic and bicycles, led to members of the Council
suggesting a potential change within the zoning code for bike lanes and cross
walks. Becky Hurley, of the Lakota Group, agrees that there is in fact an
increase in foot traffic, however, that this may be an opportunity for the
Village nudge change along and move towards active transportation. This would
not only help decrease vehicle dependability, but would also be beneficial in
terms of safety, congestion, and sustainability.
Regarding the restart of the Comprehensive Plan, the Lakota Group assures
Council and the Plan Commission that they are not restarting from the beginning
as they have all of the information from the initial start of the project. They
reflect on the engagement of the community, working sessions and meetings with
staff from January of 2020. However, the Lakota Group states that they provided
staff a timeline of the project which was under review and would be finalized
once the open house, hosted by the Lakota Group, took place. Trustee Mancini
questions what information has been gathered regarding the Lakota Groups
pillars to aid in the restart of the Comprehensive Plan. Becky Hurley states
that they have been structuring their foundation based on the communities’
feedback and data they have gathered.
An issue that the Plan Commission, Council and Lakota Group surface is how to
engage members of the community that are not fully participating with their
feedback and how to nudge those members to get active. Part of the thought
process was brought up by Chair Orsic to promote positivity with those that
volunteer. While sending surveys engaging the community and asking a broader
questions, “what they would like in the Village”, the Lakota Group suggests
that surveys can be more direct to narrow massive requests. This would show
more of a shared value and needs for the community as a whole rather than
individual wants and needs. Mr. Freres informs Council and the Plan Commission
that they have been in contact with the Park District to get a list of new
residents that have moved to Winnetka within the past year in order to engage
with them on why they moved to Winnetka.
While there is a high level of engagement with the residents and community
members, discussion of reaching out to those that have moved out of Winnetka
may also be helpful and provide more insight on what to incorporate in the
Comprehensive Plan. Council members feel that reaching out to those residents
to obtain a better understanding as to why they moved out of Winnetka can
surface different points of view that can encourage positive change in the
community.
(Trustee Swierk left the meeting at 8:29pm.)
Scott Freres informs Council of their engagement with the business owners of
the Winnetka business districts. Mr. Freres stresses the various factors that
can help bring in more customers from surrounding communities and how that can
benefit Winnetka businesses. Siraj Asfahani references Spirit Elephant, in
Winnetka, and how it is the hotspot for a diverse group of people ranging from
age and race diversity, but also accommodating to vegan and non-vegan
customers. Trustee Dalman also states how great the business is with their
social media and have influencers posting on social media that promote the
business around the Chicagoland area. Trustee Dalman and Mr. Asfahani both
agree that social media and marketing help benefit businesses to be recognized
and promote visits from surrounding communities. Scott Freres touches on the
surrounding communities such as Glencoe, Wilmette and Kenilworth being a
regional economy with a lot of dynamic between the Villages. Residents that
move to surrounding communities still feel that they are engaged with one
another and can commute freely with the convenience of options and location.
Trustee Cripe states that it would be interesting to get input out of
surrounding community businesses to see what it would take for them to open in
Winnetka. While Trustee Cripe thinks that Winnetka does not have enough density
to support things that Winnetka residents want, he would like to see more
density emerging.
Scott Freres states that, from a developer’s perspective, there are various
buildings within Winnetka that can be converted into different operational
spaces. By doing so, this can increase the liveliness within Winnetka by either
using these spaces as resident housing or businesses to attract customers from
outside of town.
(Trustee Swierk returned at 8:53pm.)
Scott Freres informs Council and members of the Plan Commission that any ideas
and suggestions they may have for the Lakota Group regarding the Comprehensive
Plan can be directed to Becky Hurley, Siraj Asfahani or Scott Feres himself.
The Lakota Group also assures Council and the Plan Commission that they are
open to reaching out to people and will be working on updating the project
website. Promotion of the project and a survey will be presented at various
events within the Village and platforms on various social media.
4)   Closed Session. None.
5)   Adjournment.
Chair Bridget Orsic, seconded by Layla Danley moved to adjourn the Plan
Commission meeting. Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to
adjourn the Village Council meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried.
Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, Mancini, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: None. The meeting adjourned at 8:59 PM.
 
