MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
September 21, 2021
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Council Chambers at Village Hall on Tuesday,
September 21, 2021, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 PM. Present: Trustees
Robert Apatoff, Andrew Cripe, Robert Dearborn, Tina Dalman and John Swierk.
Absent: Kim Mancini. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant
Village Manager Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, and
approximately 4 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Apatoff led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   Tuesday, October 5, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend. Trustee Swierk to be absent.
   b)   Tuesday, October 12, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
   c)   Tuesday, October 19, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
   d)   Friday, October 29, 2021 Budget Review Meeting. All of the Council
members present said they expect to attend. Trustee Dalman to be absent.
4)   Public Comment.
   a)   Lynn L. Doranski of 786 Foxdale. Resident would like to voice support
for a Village Ordinance that would allow residents to have native plant gardens
on the parkway of their residence. The resident’s parkway is in need of a
makeover and they would prefer to use native plant rather than grass. The
resident states that grass cannot hold water or store carbon the way native
plants can and is willing to be a test site if the Village is seeking to do so.
The resident visited Michigan City, IN and noticed that the city has installed
native gardens at the corners of their business districts so that water can
filter through the garden and through the sewer drain. The resident believes
that as a community, this would be a great step forward for the natural
environment.
   Village Manager Rob Bahan confirms that Jim Bernahl, Engineering Director,
will call to follow up with the resident. The Village has a parkway planting
permit process and guidance with the Environmental & Forestry Commission.
5)   Reports: None.
   a)   Trustees. 
      i)   Trustee Apatoff states that the past two weekends have been big
weekends for Winnetka and would like to credit the public/private partnership
on the Music Fest. Trustee Apatoff believes that the amount of work that has
been done is amazing and thanks staff and the Park District.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager. None.
   d)   Village President. None.
6)   Appointments:
   a)   Next, Manager Bahan read a list of advisory board appointments
recommended by Trustee Rintz:
      i)   Re-appoint Layla Danley to a three-year term on the Plan Commission
– her term will expire 9/21/2024;
      Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to re-appoint Layla
Danley to the Plan Commission. Her term will expire on 9/21/2024. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, and
Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Mancini.
      ii)   Appoint Katie Moor to a three-year term on the Design Review Board
– her term will expire 11/15/2024. Katie is filling a vacancy from Brad
McLane’s resignation;
      Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to appoint Katie Moor
to Design Review Board to fill the vacancy from Brad McLane’s resignation. Her
term will expire on 11/15/2024. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes:
Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent:
Mancini.
      iii)   Appoint Patrick Hanley to a three-year term on the Environmental
and Forestry Commission – his term will start on 10/21/2021 and will expire on
10/22/2024. Patrick is filling a vacancy from Chris Foley’s resignation as of
10/20/2021;
      Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to appoint Patrick
Hanley to the Environmental and Forestry Commission to fill a vacancy from
Chris Foley’s resignation. His term will expire on 10/22/2024. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, and
Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Mancini.
7)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to approve the Agenda. By
vote, the motion carried.
8)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i)   August 17, 2021, Regular Meeting.
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated August 27 – September 9, 2021 in the
amount of $4,118,969.59.
   c)   Ordinance No. M-15-2021: 1184 Tower Road - Variations (Adoption).
   d)   Resolution No. R-79-2021: Appoint Village Treasurer (Adoption). 
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Dalman, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, and Swierk. Nays:
None. Absent: Mancini.
9)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. MC-5-2021: Amending Liquor Code (Introduction).
   Marc Hornstein, Chief of Police, informs Council that businesses will come
to Police to apply for a liquor license and Police will then review the
business model to distinguish which of the 18 variations of licenses, in the
Village code, the business will fall under. When Hometown Coffee & Juice
applied, Police did not find that the business fit into the liquor license
categories under restaurants and decided that they best fit under the Class F
license with a recommendation of making two changes in the Village code. One
change is to allow for the sale of beer, wine and liquor as their business
model calls for the sale of cocktails. The second change would adjust the hours
of the sales of alcohol. Chief Hornstein requests the start of liquor sales at
9am and with these particular changes, will then allow Hometown Coffee & Juice
to fit under the Class F liquor license and accommodate their request.
   Members of Council address Chief Hornstein regarding other restaurants
within the Village and how their sales and hours compare to the request that
Hometown Coffee & Juice is proposing. A few of the Council members question why
the hours are as early as they are being requested. Chief Hornstein confirms
that majority of the restaurants in Winnetka are Class A-1 liquor license
holders and that the Village code allows for the sale of alcohol during their
hours of operation. Mr. Hornstein confirms that Police is proposing the hours
to be adjusted to 9am in order to keep the hours consistent with the
restaurants.
   After some conversation between Council members, members agree that there
may be a level of concern regarding potential problems that may occur and how
the Village would deal with those situations. President Rintz states that he is
not comfortable with the idea of selling alcohol at such an early hour and
suggests that they should reach out to the restaurant. President Rintz would
like to have the owners present to answer questions for Council and to confirm
their intentions with the proposed request prior to voting on the Ordinance.
   Without any objection, Council agrees to defer the Ordinance No. MC-5-2021:
Amending Liquor Code (Introduction) to the October 5, 2021 Council meeting.
   b)   Ordinance No. M-16-2021 – 582 Provident Avenue – Variations 
(Introduction/Adoption).
   Community Development Director, David Schoon, briefs Council that the
proposed request is for an approval of four variations to allow construction of
additions to the existing structure on the subject property. The Zoning Board
of Appeals approved the request by a vote of 4-2, however, one variation could
not be supported which was the corner yard setback. The subject property is
located on the N/W on Provident and Spruce St and is zoned R-5 Single Family
Residential. The variations requested are to replace the detached garage with
an attached garage. The variation request would also include a proposed family
room, mud room and work area room with a master suite above. In addition, the
subject owners are also requesting a front porch expansion, creating a
wraparound porch, and proposed 2 story additions to the home.
   Subject property owner, Brian Donohoe, informs Council of the gap between
the space of the neighbor’s house which gives the property an awkward look. The
proposal would allow to utilize the extra space to have more room for the
family inside their home while the wrap around porch would allow them to
maximize the outdoor space. Mr. Donohoe believes that the turnaround of the
property is 5 years and believes that the addition would be nice for the
neighborhood and the family.
   Katie Donohoe addresses Council and expresses their willingness to keep the
charm of the town intact with their home. The changes they are looking to make
will be for the fact that they would like to stay in Winnetka and preserve the
home long term. Ms. Donohoe thanks Council for their time and consideration.
   President Rintz questions if the additions being added to the home will stay
consistent with the current material in place. Architect, Chip Hacklia,
confirms that the material will be the original and will be consistent with
what the home already has integrated.
   Members of Council question if there will be any issues with construction
regarding stormwater issues, variation requests, and if there will be any
internal space issues. Mr. Schoon as well as Mr. Hacklia inform Council that
there are no requests for impermeable lot coverage or roof lot coverage
variations and there will be no issues regarding the interior construction of
the home.
   After some discussion regarding the home, Council is in agreement that they
are in favor of the project moving forward and agree that the home has a
beautiful design. Council is in favor for the subject property owners to uphold
the home and preserve the charm of the property.
   The Council was in unanimous agreement to approve the request.
   Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-16-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried Ayes: Trustees
Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Mancini.
   Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-
16-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe,
Dalman, Dearborn, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Mancini.
10)   Old Business. None.
11)   New Business. None.
12)   Closed Session.
Closed Session for the purpose of discussing specific personnel, disposition of
and setting of the price for Village property, and probable or imminent
litigation, pursuant to Sections 2(c)(1), 2(c)(6), and 2(c)(11) of the Illinois
Open Meetings Act  
Trustee Cripe moved to adjourn into Closed Session for the purpose of
discussing specific personnel, disposition of and setting of the price for
Village property, and probable or imminent litigation, pursuant to Sections 2
(c)(1), 2(c)(6), and 2(c)(11) of the Illinois Open Meetings Act. By roll call
vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, and
Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Mancini.
President Rintz announced that the Council meeting would adjourn automatically
and immediately upon the conclusion of the Closed Session without the conduct
of any further business. The Council adjourned into Closed Session at 8:52 PM.
13)   Adjournment.
The meeting adjourned at 9:48 PM.
 
