Recording Secretary
October 12, 2021
MINUTES
Study Session Meeting of Winnetka Village Council
Village Hall
October 12, 2021
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on Tuesday,
October 12, 2021, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:04 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Andrew
Cripe, Tina Dalman, Bob Dearborn, John Swierk and Kim Mancini. Absent: None.
Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Deputy Village Clerk Berina Gradjan,
and approximately 10 persons in the audience.
2)   Public Comment. None.
3)   93 Green Bay Road Proposals – Review of Revised Proposals.
President Rintz informs Council and the three finalists, Tala Coffee Roasters,
Firecakes Craft Donuts and Left Coast, that each presenter will have 20 minutes
to present their proposed plans and will be allowed 20 minutes to answer any
questions from Council and hear public comment.
Community Development Director David Schoon introduces the Founder and CEO of
Tala Coffee Roasters, Ryan Hickman.
Ryan Hickman, Founder and CEO of Tala Coffee Roasters, introduces himself and
his background in the food industry to Council. Mr. Hickman states that he has
spent 25 years in the food industry and was a franchisee of Rosati’s Pizza
operating two locations; Vernon Hills and Libertyville. In 2016, Mr. Hickman
sold his restaurants to start Tala Coffee Roasters and launched his business in
2017. He began with a roasting and production facility in Libertyville and
before opening his Highwood location in 2018. Mr. Hickman informs Council that
Tala Coffee Roasters selects and sources their coffee from around the world,
roasts the coffee, packages and delivers to their cafes as well providing
wholesale and accommodating online customers. He also introduces the team,
Keith Lilja, Head Roaster and Graphic Designer, Stefan Tong, Café Manager and
Educational Director and Joanna Tong, Director of Marketing and Operations.
Joanna Tong addresses Council with their concept for the 93 Green Bay Rd site.
She states that they would like to use the site as their second space and would
love the opportunity to be successful within Winnetka. Ms. Tong briefs Council
that part of the reason as to why they were so drawn to the site is due to the
charm and the history of the old gas station and believes that they can bring
their own characteristic to the site.
(Trustee Cripe arrived at 7:11PM.)
She informs Council that part of their proposal would be to improve parking and
circulation at the site as well as generating a more efficient way to allow
traffic to flow. This would require the removal of barriers between the 93
Green Bay Rd lot and Metra lot. Regarding their interior floor plan proposal,
Tala Coffee Roasters presented Council a realistic floor plan for the space,
previewing how the table and seating layout will look as one of their goals is
to accommodate as much seating as possible. Ms. Tong also displays their
concept of the entrance and exit outlets at the site allowing for a more
efficient way to allow foot traffic to flow.
Tala Coffee Roasters are looking to return the site to its former glory and go
beyond with the image of the 93 Green Bay Road site. Ms. Tong states that they
are looking to build on an outdoor atmosphere that would be welcoming to the
community and allow for gatherings due to the seating opportunities that they
plan on incorporating. Ms. Tong informs Council that her team is very hands on
and is very determined to achieve a level of success. She assures council that
the team is very much ready to get going with their goals and believes that
Winnetka is a perfect fit for their business model they are looking to bring to
life.
Ms. Tong stresses that everything on the menu is made in house to bring a
unique menu and ingredients that are not just added sugars. They bring a
variety of food items to the menu and provide quality and accessibility stating
that these are two of Tala’s core values. Ms. Tong speaks on Tala’s consistency
and repeatability that comes from their craft and determination to bring the
best coffee to their customers. Tala Coffee Roasters not only provides the
sales of food and coffee but various merchandise as well. They allow for the
sales of clothing that has been successful with the business model and state
that they would like to expand the service and offer coffee delivery within
Winnetka. They believe that this will draw in local teens, young adults,
surrounding adults and surrounding neighborhoods to the location.
During COVID, Tala Coffee Roasters did not lay off any of their employees and
state that they grew 40% during the pandemic. The business also provided
promotional opportunities, raises for their employees and hired even more
workers. They believe this displays their confidence in wanting to be
successful and that they are capable of staying as a lively business.
Members of Council question how Tala would perform during various hours of the
day and when they believe they would peak during the day. Ms. Tong believes
that given how close they are to New Trier, they believe that their peaks would
be early in the morning and a heavy number of students coming in after school
to do homework. Staff of Tala Coffee Roasters also state that they believe
customers are more susceptible to “grab-and-go” services in the morning rather
than later in the day when customers tend to sit in for some duration of time.
Tala staff also confirms that these projections depend on Winnetka’s resident
and community tendencies of when people get up and get moving can certainly
change based on the Village’s pattern.
Trustee Mancini questions how the business was able to uphold their sales in
recent times. Mr. Hickman elaborates that by selling merchandise and apparel to
their customers, experimenting with grab-and-go, delivery services and
providing long term stocking with coffee bags located at the Tala storefront,
these services helped with Tala’s sales and their success.
Members of Council address the Tala team on their concern regarding the
surrounding competitors and what will set them apart. Ms. Tong states that
because they believe that they are so unique and have a very convenient
location to build, their staff is very confident that they will have a
successful rate with their sales and business. Ms. Tong also states that Tala
works with coffee importers that ensure they are obtaining quality coffee. Mr.
Lilja also informs Council that Tala has a special way of roasting the coffee
in order to preserve the quality and ensure that customers can taste their
signature coffee specialties.
Members of Council thank the Tala Coffee Roasters team for their time and
presentation.
Community Development Director David Schoon introduces Firecakes Craft Donuts
to Council.
Jonathan Fox thanks Council for their time and briefly introduces the original
Firecakes Donuts location, established in 2013 in River North. Mr. Fox informs
Council that he is in fact a Winnetka resident and is looking to bring his
business to help support and add another small businesses within the Village.
Mr. Fox’s team, Mitch Goltz and Shai Wolkowicki, inform Council that they
believe Firecakes Donuts will in fact be successful and bring a great feel to
Winnetka. Mr. Fox briefs Council on his success within the River North location
and by crafting a perfect donut, as well as bringing ice cream and coffee, they
have been extremely successful and believes that he can achieve the same
success in Winnetka. He believes that while passion drives product, partnering
with local companies who share the same core values to bring organic
ingredients and quality products, it will ultimately bring a unique staple to
his business and drive success.
Mr. Fox informs Council on the social aspect of Firecakes Craft Donuts. He
states that his storefront allows for customers to have social engagement while
enjoying the various desserts on the menu and coffee and that this will draw a
lot of community residents from Winnetka and surrounding communities. Mr. Fox
shares that his business model also includes catering services to various
events and that the catering setup is unique offering a donut wall, sweet
tables and delivery services. While COVID restrictions forced his business
models to change, he adapted by installing walk up locations at his
storefronts, touchless payment options and pre-order options. Mr. Fox also
states that Firecakes also has a food truck that provides mobile food sales for
the business which increases sales and adds to his business’s success.
Mr. Goltz briefs Council that the Firecakes team plans to incorporate a covered
heated outdoor space that will allow for an increase in gatherings even during
the colder seasons. The location offers an open feel with larger windows,
interior walk-up windows to show the various donuts and be more eye appealing.
Mr. Fox believes that being located near New Trier High School will contribute
to certain hours peaking especially considering the menu items that will
attract social engagement for not only the New Trier students, but the
community as a whole.
Council questions if there is anything else other than donuts for breakfast.
Mr. Fox informs Council that the business also provides homemade fruit
parfaits, yogurts and fresh squeezed orange and lemon juice. Mr. Fox states
that they use local farmers to improve the quality of ingredients and strive to
accommodate various customers. Firecakes offers vegan ice cream and various
products on the menu which is available all of the time to accommodate a range
of customers.
Members of Council question the traffic flow and how Firecakes will manage
their sales and productions. Mr. Goltz briefs Council that they are looking to
designate time restricted parking spots and traffic studies to learn the
traffic patterns to allow for functionality in the long-term when they get more
familiar with how the business traffic will be. Mr. Fox also confirms that
Firecakes has a software that determines the types of sales and consumer needs
based on times and sales of the business and the products. Mr. Fox assures
Council that the sales are monitored to track the product sales and aid in
keeping the business running efficiently.
Community Development Director David Schoon introduces Micheal Madden, owner of
Left Coast to Council.
Michael Madden addresses Council on his beliefs as to what Left Coast can bring
for the Village. Mr. Madden believes that his business can bring a variety of
healthy, delicious and convenient options to a local neighborhood café. He
ensures that the business model is a chef driven menu and a full restaurant
serving high quality organic foods made from scratch. The business began in
Lincoln Park in 2016 and offers a variety of healthy menu options ranging from
cold pressed juices, smoothies, various different coffees and breakfast and
lunch options. Mr. Madden believes that his unique variety of options will
attract a wide spectrum of social groups allowing for social gatherings that
people will be attracted to.
Mr. Madden states that they will eventually look to incorporate turf to the
front patio giving Left Coast a special feel to the site that would be unique
to the business as well as allowing for more seating by incorporating benches
expanding the seating. Mr. Madden believes that by increasing parking spaces,
it will allow for an increase in traffic flow and can be done so by removing
the fence which would carve out about 30 spots. Increasing parking will allow
for the convenience of customers to be able to sit in and enjoy their
experience at Left Coast.
Mr. Madden informs Council that he ready to go in terms of kicking off the
planning process and working with the Village to ensure proper construction and
build outs of the site. He has much of the equipment and staff developed as
well as the plan coordination. While he is aware that the project will be a
financial investment, he sees a huge amount of potential and believes it would
be an honor to operate Left Coast at 93 Green Bay Road site.
Trustee Cripe questions Mr. Madden on what the staffing situation will look
like at the 93 Green Bay Road location. Mr. Madden informs Council that they
are planning on transferring various staff members and while there is a
challenge presented, he believes that promoting the hiring process and Left
Coast’s grand opening, he will attract applicants from Winnetka and surrounding
communities. He confirms that staffing will not be an issue, however, he
emphasizes that he will be focused on finding the right chefs to be able to
uphold the quality of the food.
Members of Council address the business model sales and the takeout stance for
Left Coast. Council wonders if Mr. Madden believes that the projected sales for
takeout will be similar to his other locations. Mr. Madden confirms that he
doesn’t think it will be much different and that the rate of sales will be
leveled equally. During COVID, Mr. Madden came out with an app that allows for
customers to place orders from Left Coast and for returning customers, allows
for the app to track certain food restriction and past orders. The app has
reached about 10,000 local users and completes about 500 transactions a week.
The app also allows for customers to pay ahead of time and pick their food out.
Mr. Madden states that this helps his business success and is also convenient
for his customers.
Mr. Madden stresses on Left Coasts efforst to really take an extra step with
their customers and make the location be a special place. He informs Council
that staff from Left Coast takes extra measures to familiarize themselves with
their customers names, returning customers and to create an atmosphere that is
very welcoming.
Presidents Rintz stresses on the diversity and growth of Winnetka and thanks
the finalists for taking the time to come out and present their willingness to
conduct their business in Winnetka. President Rintz informs Council and the
finalists that within a few weeks, Council will have an answer on a finalist
4)   Closed Session. None.
5)   Adjournment.
Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adjourn the Village
Council meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, Mancini, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 9:23 PM.
 
_____________________________
