November 2, 2021
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
November 2, 2021
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Council Chambers at Village Hall on Tuesday,
November 2, 2021, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Present: Trustees
Robert Apatoff, Tina Dalman, Robert Dearborn, and Kim Mancini. Absent: Trustees
Andy Cripe and John Swierk. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Community Development Director David Schoon and approximately 7 persons in the
audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Rintz led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
   a)   Tuesday, November 9, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
   b)   Tuesday, November 16, 2021 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members
present said they expect to attend.
4)   Public Comment.
   a)   Jeffrey Liss. Resident informs Council of his understanding that both
the Winnetka and Glencoe Water Plants are nearing the end of their life and
suggests that one water plant is built to serve both Villages for economic
purposes. Mr. Liss states that the Glencoe leadership team is receptive to
exploring the idea of having one water plant for the two Villages and asks that
President Rintz reach out to his counterpart for consideration.
   Mr. Liss also informs Council of his observation that on rainier days, the
water takes some time before it drains down to the sewers. Resident suggests
that by providing more openings for sewer access down the streets will allow
for the water to drain at a faster rate.
   President Rintz confirms that he has had several discussions with Larry
Levin, former Village President of Glencoe, regarding the Water Plant, however,
it did not seem as though Winnetka matched what Glencoe was seeking. President
Rintz informs Mr. Liss that he provided Glencoe an opportunity to purchase
water from Winnetka, however, Glencoe did not seem too interested in accepting
the offer.
   Village Manager Rob Bahan confirms that the water plant is not nearing the
end of its useful life. Mr. Bahan informs the resident that the Village had
reinvested in the water plant about 10 years ago as well as making capital
improvements. However, the Village is focused on improving the water main
replacements at this time.
   b)   Teri Cross. Resident would like to voice their disappointment regarding
polystyrene not being accepted at the Public Works drop off site from November
29, 2021 – April 1, 2022. The resident believes that there will be an increased
amount of polystyrene at residential homes for the holiday season, and without
a source that will recycle the material, the plastic will end up in landfills.
Resident is asking for the Village to consider an alternative that would allow
for this service to continue.
   Public Works Director Gio McLean will follow up with the resident and
provide more information.
5)   Reports: None.
   a)   Trustees.
      i)   Trustee Apatoff reports that he was present at the opening of
Hometown Coffee & Juice and states that not only was it a nice moment to
experience, he was also pleased to see the neighboring retailers and
restaurants coming over to congratulate the owner and providing support.
   b)   Attorney. None.
   c)   Manager.
      i)   Village Manager Rob Bahan thanks Council and staff for participating
in the all day budget review meeting and announces that Village staff will be
introducing the budget on November 16 th . A budget message will be prepared on
behalf of Council and will be distributed to members of Council for review.
   d)   Village President.
      i)   President Rintz briefly recaps how magnificent it is that for the 8
th year in a row, the Village will not increase the tax levy. Mr. Rintz thanks
Village Manager Rob Bahan and the Village staff for all of their hard work and
believes that the tax payers will be very appreciative to know there will be no
tax increase.
6)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
7)   Consent Agenda
   a)   Village Council Minutes.
      i. September 14, 2021, Joint Study Session
      ii. September 21, 2021, Regular Meeting
      iii. October 5, 2021, Regular Meeting
      iv. October 12, 2021, Study Session
      v. October 19, 2021, Regular Meeting
   b)   Approval of Warrant List dated October 8 – October 21, 2021 in the
amount of $1,297,791.65.
   c)   Resolution No. R-86-2021: CRS Program – Repetitive Loss Area Analysis
Update (Adoption)
   d)   Resolution No. R-87-2021: SAFEbuilt Contract Renewal (Adoption)
   Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Dalman, Dearborn, and Mancini. Nays: None.
Absent: Trustee Cripe and Swierk.
8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
   a)   Ordinance No. M-18-2021: Amending a Special Use Permit for Platform
Tennis Courts at 490 Hibbard Road (Introduction/Adoption).
   President Rintz states that since Trustee Swierk is providing professional
services on the project, he has recused himself from the Council meeting.
   Community Development Director David Schoon briefly states that the subject
property is located on Park District property at 490 Hibbard Road. The
representatives of both the applicant, Winnetka Platform Tennis Club, and the
Winnetka Park District are present to further brief Council and answer any
questions regarding the project. Mr. Schoon informs Council that the applicant
is requesting an approval of an amendment to an existing special use and
proposes to make the improvements in two phases. The first phase would be the
courts themselves, occurring within the next year or so. The second phase would
consist of the expansion of the paddle tennis hut, occurring within the next 2-
5 years depending on fundraising activities. The applicant’s request has been
reviewed and approved of by the Zoning Board of Appeals (4-0), Plan Commission
(5-0) and Design Review Board (5-0).
   Colin Cross, President of Winnetka Platform Tennis Club, informs Council of
the history of the club and the progress of the project so far. Mr. Cross
states that they have completed three phases so far, building four courts to
begin with, construction of the new hut and an additional two courts before
looking to add the proposed additions. Mr. Cross informs Council that they have
fund raised $390,000.00 to construct the requested two additional courts due to
the overall growth and interest in platform tennis.
   Costa Kutulas, Director of Parks and Maintenance for the Winnetka Park
District, addresses Council on how excited they are for the development of the
project and increase interest in platform tennis. Mr. Kutulas states that they
are looking to improve on the facility with the expansion of the tennis courts
and believe that it will benefit Winnetka as well as the surrounding
communities as well.
   President Rintz questions Mr. Kutulas on how they are dealing with
stormwater retention/detention. Mr. Kutulas informs Council that whatever
requirement are proposed by Village staff, will be met and addressed by the
Winnetka Platform Tennis Club and Winnetka Park District.
   President Rintz addresses how the big oak tree, on site, will be preserved
from dying. Mr. Kutulas states that the tree is not in the layout boundaries of
where the paddle court expansion will occur, so they are confident that the oak
tree will not be affected. Soil treatment and root aeration will also be
provided to help maintain the species.
   Trustee Dalman questions what the plan is for the expansion of the hut,
wondering if this is for more gathering space. Mr. Kutulas states that the
expansion would be to allow for more food activities while the other expansion
will increase the gathering space for indoor and outdoor use depending on the
weather/season.
   Trustee Dearborn expresses how great he believes the project and tennis club
is, allowing for great activity and allowing for the activity to continue in
winter months.
   Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-18-2021. By voice call vote, the motion carried
   Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to adopt Ordinance No.
M-18-2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Dalman, Dearborn, and Mancini. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Cripe and Swierk.
9)   Old Business. None.
10)   New Business. None.
11)   Appointments: None.
12)   Closed Session. None.
12)   Adjournment.
Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 7:26 PM.
 
