December 7, 2021
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
REGULAR MEETING
December 7, 2021
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held in the Council Chambers at Village Hall on Tuesday,
December 7, 2021, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 PM. Present: Trustees
Robert Apatoff, Andy Cripe, Tina Dalman, Robert Dearborn and Kim Mancini.
Absent: Trustee John Swierk. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan,
Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman,
Deputy Village Clerk Berina Gradjan, Police Chief Marc Hornstein, Community
Development Director David Schoon, Assistant Village Engineer Emily Grimm and
approximately 11 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
Trustee Mancini led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
Tuesday, December 14, 2021 Study Session. All of the Council members present
said they expect to attend.
Tuesday, December 21, 2021 Regular Meeting Cancelled
4)   Public Comment.
Jim King: A 40-year resident of Winnetka, states that there have been numerous
power outages and damage caused by overhead power lines from rain fall and
storms that have occurred overtime. Mr. King believes that there is a need for
Council to consider long-term plans regarding power lines that will decrease
the number of outages and damage resulting from storms. Jim King also believes
the Village Council can consider a thoughtful revamp of the ordinance.
President Rintz confirms that the Village will be working with AT&T regarding
shared ownership on the power lines early next year and that the Village
Council will utilize study sessions in 2022 to work on undergrounded utilities.
Liz Kunkle: Founder of Go Green Winnetka, Environmental & Forestry
Commissioner, and Plan Commission EFC Representative Liz Kunkle expresses her
gratitude on the Village incorporating food scraps and organics collection for
residential recycling services in early 2022. She encourages curbside hauler
chosen to be one part of composting options the Village offers. Ms. Kunkle also
advises Village staff to consider revising the language around the composting
program on the Villages website to clarify that food scrap reduction, landfill
diversion, and composting are priorities for the Village.
4)   Reports:
None.
Trustees. 
None.
Attorney.
None.
Manager.
None.
Village President.
President Rintz reports he and Village Manager Rob Bahan virtually attended the
annual Christmas Tea for the League of Women Voters to present the State of the
Village address along with Northfield and Kenilworth. Mr. Rintz commends the
Village on what has transpired in the last year on all fronts as well as the
League of Women Voters adequately informing the public on what is going on with
their local government.
President Rintz had the great honor to attend the Christmas tree lighting and
informs Council and staff that he is in awe with the turnout of families and
children that were present. Mr. Rintz looks forward to the event in the coming
years and hopes that the annual lighting of the tree will grow in popularity.
5)   Approval of the Agenda.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to approve the Agenda. By
voice vote, the motion carried.
Consent Agenda
6)   Village Council Minutes.
      i. October 29, 2021, Budget Review Meeting
      ii. November 16, 2021, Regular Meeting
Approval of Warrant List dated November 5 – November 18, 2021 in the amount of
$1,257,616.12.
Ordinance No. MC-6-2021: Fire Sprinkler Fee Rebate Renewal (Introduction/
Adoption)
Property Tax Levy and Abatement Ordinances (Adoption)
