Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to approve the foregoing
items on the Consent Agenda by omnibus vote. By roll call vote, the motion
carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, and Mancini. Nays:
None. Absent: Trustee Swierk.
7)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-23-2021: Authorizing the Disposition of Surplus Personal
Property Owned by the Village of Winnetka (Introduction/Adoption).
Police Chief Marc Hornstein informs Council that the disposal of surplus
property is an opportunity for the Village to recoup some expenditures for
property purchased in recent years. Specifically, Mr. Hornstein indicates that
for the duty pistols, purchased 10 years ago, was a planned scheduled
replacement.
Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-23-2021. By voice call vote, the motion carried
Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-23-
2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe,
Dalman, Dearborn, and Mancini.  Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Swierk.
Ordinance No. M-24-2021: Mathnasium - 1054 Gage Street (Introduction/Adoption)
Community Development Director David Schoon confirms that the applicant is
requesting to expand its existing tutoring facility. The Plan Commission has
previously reviewed the request and unanimously given approval as they believe
it is a great use for the area attracting young families. Following guidelines
set forth by the Village, the applicant has also agreed to secure parking
passes for its employees in the A lot.
Mathnasium owner, Javier Santacruz, conveys his excitement to expand the
overall space allowing for the growth of his business.
Council expresses their gratitude for the business presence in town.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-24-2021. By voice call vote, the motion carried
Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Mancini, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-24-
2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Cripe,
Dalman, Dearborn, and Mancini.  Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Swierk.
Resolution No. R-98-2021: Purchase of Underground Stormwater Vaults (Adoption)
President Rintz states what a big step forward this purchase is for the
Village’s stormwater relief and is thrilled that after 10 years of discussion,
the project is beginning to move forward.
Assistant Village Engineer Emily Grimm confirms that the Village is proceeding
to install two stormwater vaults under Duke Childs Field and Little Duke
Playing Fields. Ms. Grimm informs Council that the chosen vendor is StormTrap,
due to having the necessary manufacturing capacity to build the large vaults as
well as the capacity to store the vaults on site until the installation takes
place in the summer. Staff has requested the $4.2 million purchase of these
vaults by the Village to ensure that the two needed vaults will be ready in
time for the installation.
Trustee Mancini, liaison for the Environmental & Forestry Commission, requests
further clarification on the purity of the water. Ms. Grimm informs Council
that the vaults are designed to have water quality components; sumps that are
designed to slow the flow of the water aiding with filtration and are equipped
with concrete basins limiting any toxins or lead that may be present.
Council deliberates the long-term planning and upkeeping of the vaults, Mike
Waldren, from Strand Associates, briefs Council on the yearly maintenance and
the underground system that will be of contribution.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Dalman, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-98-
2021. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Dalman,
Dearborn, and Mancini.  Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Swierk. Abstain: None.
8)   Old Business.
None.
9)   New Business.
714-740 Elm Street & 511-515 Lincoln – Stonestreet Partners – Planned
Development – Concept Plan Review
President Rintz reports that this is a concept review of a new proposed
physical building and indicates that Council is not here to comment or
disparage the developer and the team. President Rintz indicates that public
comments are required to be limited toward the building and any comments
related to development team are not permitted.
Community Development Director David Schoon briefly elaborates on the
development process, confirming that currently, the project is at the concept
plan review stage. Mr. Schoon states that the planned development process
includes three major public review components, which is currently in the first
component, and that Council will be able to provide any comments and
suggestions on the proposed development. Comments given by Council are
preliminary and Council will not be voting on any action.
David Schoon indicates that the comprehensive plan designates the subject
property appropriate for mixed use land uses, zoned as C-2 General Retail
Commercial consisting of 74 rental units, ranging from 1-3 bedrooms, first
floor commercial space of about 18,000 sq ft and 158 parking spaces.
Jon Talty, of OKW Architects, confirms that CA Ventures is not part of the
development plan, as requested to be spoken into record per Village Council.
Mr. Talty addresses Council, not only as a representative of the applicant, but
also as a resident of Winnetka and former chairman of the Board of Fire &
Police Commissioners. Mr. Talty states that he brings a dense knowledge of the
community that he feels is particularly unique given that his firm has also
been a great contributor to the Village and has an understanding of the
community needs and expectations.
Reiterating Mr. Schoon’s project briefing, Mr. Talty states that the property
will be a five-story mixed use planned development site plan, a cross between
commercial and residential use. The development will incorporate lower-level
parking, grade level commercial space, and upper-level luxury rental
properties. The number of units will be determined by the market demand.
Providing further detail of the site plan, Mr. Talty provides descriptive units
of measure including recommended zoning variations per Village Staff, structure
materials of the site, proposed amenity spaces provided for the residents such
as terraces and a courtyard, and the overall impact the site will have on the
community based on both the residential and commercial ends.
Mr. Talty believes that luxury rental within the community is important,
offering continuity for the residents that have lived in Winnetka and grown
weary of owning large homes. Believing that rentals come with an easier
lifestyle rather than maintaining the responsibilities of home ownership, Mr.
Talty strongly encourages Council to consider the benefits of such a
development.
 
10)   Public Comment:
Frank Petrik: Mr. Petrik appreciates the communication Village staff has had
with the surrounding property owners of the proposed development site and
informs Council of his observations regarding the released plans that may
propose potential violations or concerns.
John Phalen: President of 7-Eleven Oak Street Corporation requests that Council
encourages the developers to reach out so that their needs and wants can be
heard.
Marc Hecht: Mr. Hecht, a resident of Winnetka, informs Council that he has seen
no attempt to comply with Winnetka zoning and that the proposed development
does not meet zoning requirements. Mr. Hecht states that such a proposal will
wreck the West Elm business district. While parking has already been a factor,
Mr. Hecht believes that there will be congestion concerns with parking. He
encourages Council to require that any developer must oblige with strict
compliance on zoning laws particularly height and set back restrictions,
reflecting on the previous failure of the development due to compliance
violations.
President Rintz addresses Mr. Hecht stating that the property owner, due to
property rights, has a right to petition, however, that does not guarantee
approval as the Village Council will make decisions they feel would be best for
the community.
Deliberation between Village Council and Jon Talty further describes the
details of the design construction of the proposed development elaborating on
the number of parking spots per unit, construction plans including various set
back requirements, and commercial plans including construction of retail
spacing.
Trustee Cripe addresses the developers stating that the proposed development is
too large in mass, violating the zoning code. Trustee Cripe indicates that the
plans need to be sensitive to the community’s needs and that the current
scaling is too large in height and overall size for the Village. However, if
the developers can redesign the development to comply with Winnetka’s zoning
and minimize the size, the developers can potentially proceed with the overall
planning process. While the Council unanimously agrees that the sizing of the
structure is too large in size, members do acknowledge that the facade of the
development is particularly well planned.
President Rintz encourages the development team to reach out to Hadley School,
7-Eleven, Oak Street residents, and the residents along Maple Avenue, that will
be impacted by the development to provide an opportunity for positive
communication and feedback.
Members of Council indicate that with amending the design plans and decreasing
the sizing of the proposed development, the development may still be eligible
to move forward in the design process when it comes before Council at a future
date.
11)   Appointments:
None.
12)   Closed Session:
None.
13)   Adjournment.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:25 PM.
 
      
    Recording Secretary
December 14, 2021
MINUTES
Study Session
Winnetka Village Council
Village Hall
December 14, 2021
 
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened study session meeting of the Council of the
Village of Winnetka, which was held in the Village Hall Council Chambers on
Tuesday, December 14, 2021, at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:02 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Andrew
Cripe, Tina Dalman, and Bob Dearborn. Absent: Trustee Kim Mancini and John
Swierk. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager
Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Deputy Village Clerk Berina
Gradjan, Community Development Director David Schoon, Senior Planner Ann
Klaassen and approximately 6 persons in the audience.
2)   Public Comment.
Liz Kunkle, a Winnetka resident, founder of Go Green Winnetka, Environmental
and Forestry Commissioner and Plan Commissioner. Ms. Kunkle indicates that she
does not believe the Village should allow for a five-story building or that the
Village should repeat its mistake from the previous development that was
approved, referencing the One Winnetka Concept Plan overview at the Village
Council meeting on December 7, 2021. Liz Kunkle believes that Winnetka needs
lower end housing and diversified housing from a sustainability standpoint.
Referencing the 2040 Plan, Ms. Kunkle urges the Village to continue looking at
ways to enhance its refuse and recycling. Liz Kunkle also urges Council to
consider a budget item for a sustainability or climate coordinator that will
work on sustainability issues. 
Ted Wynnychenko, Winnetka resident, questions what the Village has received in
exchange for land held in trust by the State to private individuals.
Jessica Bollhoefer. Requests that the One Winnetka team incorporates
sustainability aspects that will be incorporated into the design and
construction, adding community solar power within Village and various sites.
Fan Fruit, a Winnetka resident, encourages Council to consider community solar
power that would provide for various buildings within the Village.   
Comprehensive Plan Existing Conditions Report Discussion.
The Comprehensive Plan consulting team from the Lakota Group reviews the draft
existing conditions report in preparation for the visioning phase. The report
will provide a summary on the consulting teams observations of the community
from studying the data they have collected and their engagement with community
members. Scott Freres, of the Lakota Group, informs Council that the plan has
since consisted of engaging with members of the community; listening to their
wants, needs, and some of the concerns, before developing ideas and solutions.
Mr. Freres believes that given the pandemic, the community in 2040 will be
affected by the changing and developing ways that Winnetka is evolving. While
change is inevitable, Scott Freres informs Council that he believes that
culture and heritage of the community has sustained and will continue to be
protected as the plan develops.
Confirming that the visioning plan will begin in January, Mr. Freres states
that the team will kick off the plan with developing a “brand mission
statement”; a statement that defines what Winnetka is about and where the plan
is headed. The Lakota Group will partner with the Plan Commission in order to
set pillars and themes that will move the plan into its final planning phase.
Becky Hurley, of the Lakota Group and Winnetka resident, emphasizes that
Winnetka requires and deserves thorough community engagement and ensures
Council that The Lakota Group works hard to engage with and understand what the
community wants. Ms. Hurley informs Council that after reengaging with the
community, the team has found that the community has remained consistent with
defining the importance of its schools, Winnetka’s lakefront, parks, and a
sense of community. Ms. Hurley addresses Council on the demographics of
Winnetka regarding business needs and improvements, the rising number of young
families moving to Winnetka and how members of the community have expressed
that Winnetka has a lack of housing options.
Fran Rood, Senior Vice President at SB Friedman Development Advisors, a real
estate, and economic development consulting firm, advises Council on the teams
observations and studies regarding the demographic and census data collected
overtime. Ms. Rood elaborates on the initial analysis that was concluded pre-
pandemic; indicating a noticeable flat or declining population, lengthy sales
time with single-family home, and an increase in vacancy within the business
districts. Ms. Rood provides an overview for Council on how the trajectory has
changed overtime and ideas that Council may consider that can potentially
improve and add to the business and residential aspects for the Village.
Caren Kay, Project Manager at SB Friedman, provides information for Council
regarding the three key elements of the real estate market analysis: trajectory
in single family homes, insight in the retail market and commercial adaptive
reuse, and mixed-use development along Green Bay Road.
Stacey Meekins, an SB Friedman associate, elaborates on community mobility
within the Village. She states that although Winnetka has options for
transportation via the Metra and Pace, incorporating more choices to safely get
around is a key factor for members of the community.
Scott Ruhland, Senior Associate of the Lakota Group, emphasizes the importance
of preserving the historic and rich heritage in Winnetka including the cultural
and architectural influences. While moving forward with planning, the Lakota
Group is looking to maintain the authenticity and charm of Winnetka while
improving the development plans. Mr. Ruhland shares what members of the
community have expressed they feel would be beneficial for the Village
considering the need to adapt to the changes occurring due to the pandemic. Mr.
Ruhland also covers the teams approach regarding sustainability,
infrastructure, utilities, internet activity, and solar power energy.
 
Public Comment.
Charles Downing commends the thoroughness of the report. Mr. Downing addresses
Council on his observations regarding the report and study session agenda
packet.
King Poor inquired how the Village may become a leader in the transition to
clean energy.
Greg Aiton. Implies that change needs to occur in order to avoid the impacts of
climate change. Provided that education on the matter be emphasized along with
public support. Mr. Aiton presents various steps that can be taken locally to
improve on sustainability within the Village.
Liz Kunkle. Commends the presentation of the Lakota Group. Ms. Kunkle shares
her unique take away from the report, expressing her feedback and ideas that
may be beneficial for the Village.
Elisha Gray. Regarding the Green Bay Trail, Mr. Gray expresses his concerns and
views on the upkeeping and improvements of the trail moving into the visioning
phase of the plan. Mr. Gray also hopes that the Village will apply allocated
funds toward matters pertaining to the Green Bay Tail.
 
Deliberation between Council members encourages planning to be mindful
regarding implementing diversity for homeowners. Trustee Cripe believes that
while there may be ways to increase the diversity with housing and residents in
Winnetka, a potential challenge is whether or not the current residents will be
accepting of the concept.
 
Trustee Apatoff suggests that there be sensitivity regarding the timing of the
project and that while the community has adapted to the pandemic, planning
should not be based on the change that has been implemented because of COVID.
Trustee Apatoff commends the focus on innovation and sustainability, however,
encourages seeking a balance between upholding Winnetka’s tradition and
improving its tradition.
 
President Rintz requests not to rely on statistical analysis and spend time
looking qualitatively at the reality of the Village. Indicating that while
Winnetka is expensive, surrounding communities provide affordable and quality
living, urging the team to be mindful of how Winnetka is presented.
 
Village Council commends the Lakota Group and their counterpart, SB Friedman,
for their thorough work and looks forward to their future engagement.
3)   Closed Session.
None.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Apatoff, seconded by Trustee Dearborn, moved to adjourn the Village
Council meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Cripe, Dalman, and Dearborn. Nays: None. Absent: Trustees Mancini and Swierk.
The meeting adjourned at 8:59 PM.
 
      
