January 11, 2022
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
VIRTUAL STUDY SESSION
January 11, 2022
(Approved: xx)
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held virtually on the Zoom videoconference platform on
Tuesday, January 11, 2022 at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Robert Apatoff, Andy Cripe,
Tina Dalman, Bob Dearborn, Kim Mancini, and John Swierk. Absent: None. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Deputy Village Clerk Berina Gradjan,
and Community Development Director David Schoon, Director of Engineering Jim
Bernahl, and approximately 6 persons in the audience.
2)   Public Comment.
None.
3)   Streetscape Phase 4 – Final Design Presentation.
Jim Bernahl, Director of Engineering, briefly recaps the milestones of the
streetscape project regarding phases 1, 2, and 3. Mr. Bernahl confirms that the
final phase will begin in March and is anticipated to be completed by June.
Jillian Troiani, Associate Landscape Architect at Teska Associates, informs
Council on the proposed streetscape plans for phase 4; water main work,
electrical work, public parking lot enhancements, and streetscape tree
preservation and replacement, landscape plantings, hardscape and furnishing,
and lighting infrastructure. Ms. Troiani confirms that businesses will continue
to have access to all utility services and will be able to continue operating
without disruption while construction is being completed.
Mr. Bernahl indicates that Community Development Director David Schoon had met
with the Gardening Club to discuss a preliminary concept plan discussing the
plantings on the site to ensure partnership and involvement.
Jim Bernahl provides Council a budget summary for phase 4 streetscape, totaling
$2,957,736, and the funding sources for the streetscape phase. Mr. Bernahl
provides a breakdown of the total costs; transformer relocation; $91,000, water
main work; $535,731, subtotal phase 4 streetscape; $1,898,484, mobilization and
traffic control contingencies; $180,000, general contingency; $252,251.
Deliberation between Council members and Mr. Bernahl discuss the financial
aspects of the Phase IV Streetscape plan regarding various project costs, cost
increases, and costs coinciding with the bidding process with ALamp Concrete
Contractors, Inc.
Jim Bernahl addresses Council regarding plans of the Village hosting a joint
meeting with the surrounding businesses and residents, that will be affected by
the Streetscape project, to provide information for the overall construction
schedule, sequence of activities, and information regarding adequate parking
spaces for access to businesses during construction.
 
4)   Public Comment.
Theresa Lucas, owner of Good Grapes, shares her comments and concerns regarding
the Streetscape construction planning, project schedule, and public safety
concerns.
 
President Rintz thanks Ms. Lucas for her time and comments, and emphasizes the
importance of business owners attending the open house hosted by the Village.
5)   Closed Session.
None.
6)   Adjournment.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adjourn the Village
Council meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, Mancini, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None. The
meeting adjourned at 8:16 PM.
      
    Recording Secretary
