A record of a legally convened regular meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held at Council Chambers on Tuesday, June 7, 2022, at 7:00
PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Pro Tem Bob Dearborn called the meeting to order at 7:01 PM.
Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas called the roll. Present: Trustees
Rob Apatoff, Andy Cripe, Tine Dalman, Bob Dearborn, and Kim Mancini. Absent:
President Christopher Rintz and John Swierk. Also present: Village Clerk Berina
Gradjan, Community Development Director David Schoon, Water & Electric Director
Brian Keys, Engineering Director Jim Bernahl, Fire Chief John Ripka, Village
Attorney Peter Friedman, and approximately 47 persons in the audience.
2)   Pledge of Allegiance.
President Rintz led the group in the Pledge of Allegiance.
3)   Quorum.
June 14, 2022 Study Session. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
June 21, 2022 Regular Meeting. All of the Council members present said they
expect to attend.
Life Saving Awards Presentation
Police Chief Marc Hornstein recognizes and presents the Life Saving Award to
Officers Dan Timmers, Logan Jernigan, and Firemedic Katie Schmidly for their
life saving actions on March 18, 2022, rescuing two drowning teens at the Tower
Road pier.
4)   Public Comment.
Stephanie Heraty informs Council of a petition created around the Winnetka Park
District issue, indicates that the residents are not opposed to Elder and
Centennial improvement plan, however, they are opposed to the deviated plans
including a steel walls that will restrict shoreline access and lakefront.
Randy Winchurch requests a withdrawal of the letter of support on behalf of the
Village indicating that the letter is a breaking barrier in trust between
Village officials and the Village community.
Judy Rau thanks the Council for their service and President Rintz’s letter to
the Winnetka community. However, Ms. Val indicates that the lack of
transparency and altered plans is devolving into a lack of trust and
restricting the community of access to the shoreline.
Jim Rau commends the elegance of the initial designs, however, indicates that
he opposed to the final plans.
Lori Peterson expresses her disappointment in proposed final plans and believes
that the steel walls will shed national negative light on the Village and the
mistreatment of the shoreline. Ms. Peterson urges Council not to support the
plans.
Paul Constant is concerned regarding the forces of nature, the maintenance of
the walls, and urges Council to reconsider their stance on the plans moving
forward.
Chris Ailas reflects on a previous owners request for a privacy screen that was
brought before the Village and suggests Council consider a similar approach.
Elaiza Earls expresses her support regarding the initial plans, however, is
opposed to the steel walls indicating that they will restrict the natural
beauty of the shoreline.
Beth Kisha believes that the steel walls will set a bad precedent along the
shoreline.
Ron White reflects on historic decisions that the Park District Board has made
and indicates that he is opposed to the plans regarding the steel walls.
Tom Brown believes that the Village Council serves as the community guardians
that preserve the history, spirit, and community of Winnetka and believes that
the steel walls will provide a cage-like feeling.
Colleen Root commends the number of community members that have taken a stance
against the plans. Ms. Root confirms that as a Park District Board
Commissioner, she was astounded by the plans when provided for review and urges
Council to consider an ordinance that will regulate the height of walls along
the lakefront.
Park District Board of Commissioners President Warren James confirms that there
is no unwritten agreement only an exchange agreement that is posted for the
public to access. While Mr. Warren confirms that there has been extensive
negotiations, the Park District Board is seeking the best possible outcome and
encourages members of the community to join the Park District meetings.
Trustee Cripe enters the meeting at 7:41 pm.
Katie Stevens suggests that due to the proposed changes in the plans, the Park
District Board has the ability to terminate the deal and indicates that she is
not in favor of the steel walls to be place along the shoreline.
Irene Smith indicates that she does not believe that the letter sent by
President Rintz reflects the support of the Village Council. Ms. Smith believes
that the plan sets a terrible precedent and urges Council to preserve and
protect the shorelines.
John Nickels reflects on the trust that the community has instilled in the
Council and commends the vision and unity that is stood for and urges Council
to stand up for what the community believes is the right thing.
John Ruth suggests that Council review the Village ordinance and jurisdictions
related to the matter.
Suzy Schriber, former Park District Board Commissioner, believes that there
should be a thoughtful and respectful approach to the issue and states that the
waters of the great lakes are federal property and financial spendings and
grants received have to represent the taxpayers of the United States.
Vicky Apatoff believes that there has been a lack of transparency between the
Park District Board and members of the community, indicating that she is
opposed to the plans that were released.
Warren James invites and urges members of the public to the Park District
meeting.
Theresa Lucas, owner of Good Grapes, expresses her observations regarding the
ongoing Streetscape construction, indicating that she has been frustrated with
the progress and the revenue loss experienced in combination with Village wide
events. Ms. Lucas suggests that Council dedicate a day to chestnut court
businesses to celebrate the completion of the project and requests the closure
of chestnut court on July 14 th .
The Village Trustees confirm their individual letters of submissions to the
Illinois Department of Natural Resources and Army Corps of Engineers stating
their opposition to the revised plans proposing steel walls.
