A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held at Council Chambers on Tuesday, June 14, 2022, at 7:00
PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:03 PM. Assistant Village
Manager Kazenas called the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Rob
Apatoff, Andy Cripe, Bob Dearborn, Kim Mancini, and John Swierk. Absent:
Trustee Tina Dalman. Also present: Assistant Village Manager Kristin Kazenas,
Village Attorney Braeden Llord, Deputy Village Clerk Berina Gradjan, Deputy
Police Chief Brian O’Connell, and Police Chief Marc Hornstein, and 0 persons in
the audience.
2)   Public Comment.
Trustee Apatoff, liaison to the Chamber of Commerce, addressed matters
regarding the Bike ordinance and informs Council that businesses have strong
concerns that young bikers are not attentive to signage and feel that there is
a lack of education regarding the ordinance.
Automated License Plate Readers.
Police Chief Marc Hornstein states that the goals for the Automated License
Plate Reader are ultimately used for crime prevention, alerting officers of
missing persons, endangered elderly, and AMBER alerts.
Chief Hornstein identifies the various technological features of the Automated
License Plate Reader and informs Council on how these features can benefit
crime prevention measures by the Winnetka Police Department while assisting
surrounding municipalities in crime identification. Chief Hornstein briefs
Council on crime rates in the northern and western suburbs as well as the types
of crimes committed within the Village.
Marc Hornstein reviews the process of data storage that is obtained, how
surrounding municipalities that use the system reference the data, and what the
regional approach is with Automated License Plate Reader.
Flock Safety representative and Council review costs related matters, the
installation process, software diagnostics, and expenditure.
Council discusses the overall success rate of the Automated License Plate
Reader and privacy concerns related to the general public. President Rintz
believes that while the Automated License Plate Reader is beneficial, the
written policy should be revised and reviewed with the Village Attorney before
the policy is implemented. Council consensus was Village staff should proceed
with the purchase of the Automated License Plate Reader and work with the
Village Attorney to draft an updated policy which addresses the privacy
concerns placing clean restrictions on the use of the data and ensuring that
the data collected by Flock is deleted every 30 days.
3)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adjourn the Village
Council meeting. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Cripe, Dearborn, Mancini, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Tina Dalman.
The meeting adjourned at 8:16 PM.
 
   /s/Berina Gradjan, Deputy Village Clerk   
