15)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
Ordinance No. M-11-2022: 564 Green Bay Road – Mixed Use Building Special Use &
Variations (Introduction/Adoption)
Community Development Director David Schoon informs Council of a new three-
story mixed-use development building requesting a Special Use Permit and zoning
variations. Engel & Volkers will be set to occupy the first-floor commercial
use space, while two residential units will be located on top floor. The Design
Review Board, Plan Commission, Zoning Board of Appeals, and the Landmark
Preservation Commission have recommended approval of the requests.
Community Development Director David Schoon and Council review parking related
matters, materials regarding the façade of the development, and overall
building structure details.
Karen Erikson, co-owner of Engel & Volkers, expresses her involvement in the
community and is optimistic about the future contributions to the Village.
Trustee Handler commends the aesthetic of the development and looks forward to
the project.
Trustee Apatoff commends the work of Ms. Erikson regarding the building design
and believes that this is an excellent development for the community that will
provide upscale rental in the Village.
Trustee Swierk, seconded by Trustee Dalman, moved to waive introduction of
Ordinance No. M-11-2022. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Apatoff, Dalman, Dearborn, Handler, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee
Andy Cripe.
Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-11-
2022. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff, Dalman,
Dearborn, Handler, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Andy Cripe.
Resolution No. R-90-2022: Police HVAC Contract (Adoption)
Deputy Police Chief Brian O’Connell briefs Council regarding the Police
Department’s existing HVAC system consisting of five roof top units (RTUs).
While only one RTU has been replaced 11 years ago, the remaining RTUs have
reached the end of their useful lives. Deputy Chief O’Connell states that
repairs are extremely costly and often unexpected.
Deputy Chief O’Connell confirms that replacing the RTUs will not suffice and
that a complete upgrade of the HVAC system is needed. Trane U.S. Inc.
representatives provided a thorough evaluation of the system and suggested
replacing the existing RTUs and Variable Volume and Temperature system with
fewer, higher efficiency RTUs and a conversion to a true Variable Air Volume
system.
Council discusses concerns regarding the current versus new number of tons of
AC within the building, various specs and efficiency of the upgraded system,
contract terms and fees, as well as budget related matters.
 
Trane U.S. Inc. representatives and Council discuss the infrastructure of the
HVAC system, various market costs, and concerns related to the one year
warranty offered by Trane U.S. Inc..
President Rintz commends the upgrade to the HVAC system, however, suggests that
a one-year warranty for $500,000 should be reconsidered for a longer term and
reflected in the contract terms. Trane U.S. Inc. representatives agree to
reconsider the warranty terms before finalizing the agreement.
Consideration of Resolution No. R-90-2022 is deferred to the August 16 th
meeting.
16)   Appointments.
None.
Closed Session for the Purpose of Discussing the Sale or Lease and Related
Compensation of Village Property Pursuant to Section 2(c)(6) of the Open
Meetings.
 
