August 9, 2022
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
August 9, 2022
 
(Approved: September 6, 2022)
 
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held at Council Chambers on Tuesday, August 9, 2022, at 7:
00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:00 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Rob Apatoff, Andy Cripe,
Tina Dalman, Bob Dearborn, Kim Handler, and John Swierk. Absent: None. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Deputy Village Clerk Berina Gradjan,
and Police Chief Marc Hornstein, and approximately 33 persons in the audience.
2)   Public Comment.
Chuck Dowding requests that Council does not sign letter of support for
rejuvenation of Elder and Centennial Park because it may set a precedent for
other planter boxes along the shorelines.
Listening Session – Firearm Safety.
President Rintz indicates that this is a listening session for Council, stating
that the Highland Park shooting has influenced the conversation.
Village Attorney Peter Friedman briefs Council and members of the public on the
Village Code pertaining to firearm safety and the regulations in place on a
State and Federal level. Peter Friedman indicates some of the challenges that
are presented under the second amendment and various ordinances pertaining to
firearms in surrounding municipalities such as the Village of Deerfield and
Village of Highland Park.
Police Chief Marc Hornstein informs Council and members of the public regarding
the Firearm Owner’s Identification Card process and the Police Department’s
regulations pertaining to firearms in the Village. Chief Hornstein confirms the
various steps taken prior to a citizen being permitted to conceal and carry
such as background checks that are completed in search of domestic violence
incidents, mental health issues recorded with the State, screenings etc.. Chief
Hornstein indicated how the Winnetka Police Department works with the State
Police on matters related to firearms, the process of destruction regarding
firearms, and the Police Department’s plans to provide free gun locks to the
public in an effort to enhance safety.
Council discusses matters related to mental health issues and firearm safety,
Village records pertaining to FOID card holders, and partnership between the
Police Department and New Trier High School, as well as partnership on a State
level.
 
Public Comment.
Peter Gelderman briefs Council on the history regarding the AK47 and states
that he does not believe citizens or Police Officers should carry the AK47.
Caryn Rosen Adelman states that she was present at the 1988 Winnetka school
shooting and encourages Council to make positive decisions.
Stephanie Jacobs states that she was present at the Highland Park parade and
that she does not believe that this is a political issue, but a public health
issue. Ms. Jacobs believes that citizens should have the freedom to be in
public spaces without the fear or someone in the general public carrying a
firearm. Ms. Jacobson encourages Council to stand up and make a change.
Catherine Hurly, a student at the time of the 1988 Hubbard Woods School
shooting, indicates that she is committed to addressing that the gun violence
issue that is a public health crisis. She encourages Council to begin making a
change.
Lauren Harper, a member of the Moms Demand Change group consisting of North
Shore residents, states that she is working on issues related to gun safety and
preventing gun violence. Ms. Hupper briefs Council regarding statistics of the
youth and resources that may be utilized to address the firearm issues.
Speaker name not stated, commends the Village on their outreach to the
community regarding Village signage and general information that may assist the
public and encourages the Village to take action on keeping the community safe.
Kaity Brandtor supports the ban on assault weapons and encourages Council to
take a stand.
Sara Gries briefs Council on her experience as a teacher with school shootings
and hopes that Council can make a difference with gun violence.
Layla Danley hopes that the Village can send out a message for safe gun storage
and provide statistics related to gun violence. Ms. Danley encourages Council’s
leadership.
Jonah Spazito encourages Council to take a stand and hopes that Council is
reaching out to State representatives and State senators on the issue.
Meg Siel challenges Council to “break out of the box” and make a difference and
redefine what is possible.
Jenna Daugherty states that she moved to the Village because of the safety in
the community and encourages Council to take leadership.
Joanna Raimo helped lead the student walkout at New Trier High School after the
Stoneman Douglas school shooting. Joanna Raimo feels that while there was
dialogue regarding gun violence, she is disappointed that there has not been
much change. She encourages the community and Council to take leadership and
continue the conversation surrounding gun violence.
Jean Bishop, a resident whose family members were killed in a home shooting due
to lack of safe firearm storage, is hopeful that Council will take action and
make change regarding gun violence.
Jackie Raimo believes that there should be requirements for firearm safety and
consequences for firearm incidents. She encourages Council to take action.
Lyla Kelber states that she does not feel safe at school and does not believe
that metal detectors and armed teachers are the solution, but that a ban of
assault weapons is implemented. She encourages education regarding firearm
storage, warning signs, funding towards organizations for firearm safety, and
encourages Council to take action.
Caroline Betso informs Council on United Kingdom’s process for gun owners and
the difference of regulations in place compared to the United States. Ms. Betso
encourages actions to be taken.
Therese Peach states that her child was impacted by a bullet that came through
her window while residing in New York, and recognizes that the Village’s
calmness is the strength that is needed. She encourages the community to work
together towards peace.
President Rintz appreciates the comments made by members of the public and
states that Council will convene on the issue in the near future.
 
3)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:00 PM.
 
   /s/Berina Gradjan, Deputy Village Clerk   
   Recording Secretary
