8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-100-2022: A Resolution Calling for Federal and State Action on
Gun Safety and Violence (Adoption)
President Rintz indicates that Council will be considering a Resolution calling
for Federal and State action on gun safety and violence. The Village Attorney
has advised Council that there is a State preemption and a recent Supreme Court
ruling that limits the Council’s ability to expand any requirements. President
Rintz defers the discussion to a later date in order to ensure that Village
Attorney Peter Friedman will be in attendance.
Public Comment.
Charl Capria, on behalf of Kaity Brandtor, thanks Council for their time and
consideration reflecting on the devastating events at the Highland Park
Shooting.
Lauren Harper thanks Council for approaching gun related issues and urges
Council to come together to stand for change while also encouraging New Trier
Township High School to engage in conversation of safe gun storage measures.
Speaker name not stated, thanks Council for their service and reflects on the
lack of education behind secure gun storage of gun owners.
Jean Bishop reflects on the beautiful nature of Lake Michigan. Ms. Bishop
shares her story regarding gun violence in the community and thanks Council for
their civic duty.
Natalie Ernest reflects on a letter signed on behalf of the members of the
community regarding matters related to safe gun storage and encourages Council
to take action on gun control matters.
Lizzy Kelly thanks Council for the discussion related to gun violence issues.
Ms. Kelly reflects on the Hubbard Woods school shooting and encourages Council
to consider making changes.
Jackie Raimo expresses her disappointment regarding the State’s preemption and
indicates her interest in working towards gun related safety measures.
Kate McNally thanks Council for their service, commitment, thoughtfulness, and
courage.
Speaker name not stated, reflects on personal experiences related to gun
violence and indicates the importance of presence to make change.
President Rintz commends the value of Winnetka being a safe place and is
disheartened to hear the community question safety in the Village. President
Rintz expresses hope in the State, Federal, and local representatives standing
for change.
Members of Council unanimously express their disappointment in the immense
amount of gun violence related issues at a local and national level and express
their disappointment at the State and Federal levels lack of attention and
effort to work towards resolving gun related issues.
Council deliberates various matters pertaining to gun related issues such as
the process to obtaining a FOID card, manufactures and sellers, negligence
related matters, and implementation measures related to firearm safety.
Council members discuss changes to certain language within the Resolution
presented in order to finalize a more structured and thorough docume